if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-110"]="The main portion of the following story appeared—with slight modifications—in the _Graphic_ newspaper; other chapters, more especially addressed to adult readers, in the _Fortnightly Review_ and the _National Observer_, as episodic sketches. My thanks are tendered to the editors and proprietors of those periodicals for enabling me now to piece the trunk and limbs of the novel together, and print it complete, as originally written two years ago.\n\nI will just add that the story is sent out in all sincerity of purpose, as an attempt to give artistic form to a true sequence of things; and in respect of the book’s opinions and sentiments, I would ask any too genteel reader, who cannot endure to have said what everybody nowadays thinks and feels, to remember a well-worn sentence of St. Jerome’s: If an offense come out of the truth, better it is that the offense come than that the truth be concealed.\n\n_November_ 1891.\n\nAuthor’s Preface to the Fifth and Later Editions\nThis novel being one wherein the great campaign of the heroine begins after an event in her experience which has usually been treated as fatal to her part of protagonist, or at least as the virtual ending of her enterprises and hopes, it was quite contrary to avowed conventions that the public should welcome the book and agree with me in holding that there was something more to be said in fiction than had been said about the shaded side of a well-known catastrophe. But the responsive spirit in which _Tess of the d’Urbervilles_ has been received by the readers of England and America would seem to prove that the plan of laying down a story on the lines of tacit opinion, instead of making it to square with the merely vocal formulae of society, is not altogether a wrong one, even when exemplified in so unequal and partial an achievement as the present. For this responsiveness I cannot refrain from expressing my thanks; and my regret is that, in a world where one so often hungers in vain for friendship, where even not to be wilfully misunderstood is felt as a kindness, I shall never meet in person these appreciative readers, male and female, and shake them by the hand.\n\nI include amongst them the reviewers—by far the majority—who have so generously welcomed the tale. Their words show that they, like the others, have only too largely repaired my defects of narration by their own imaginative intuition.\n\nNevertheless, though the novel was intended to be neither didactic nor aggressive, but in the scenic parts to be representative simply and in the contemplative to be oftener charged with impressions than with convictions, there have been objectors both to the matter and to the rendering.\n\nThe more austere of these maintain a conscientious difference of opinion concerning, among other things, subjects fit for art, and reveal an inability to associate the idea of the sub-title adjective with any but the artificial and derivative meaning which has resulted to it from the ordinances of civilization. They ignore the meaning of the word in Nature, together with all aesthetic claims upon it, not to mention the spiritual interpretation afforded by the finest side of their own Christianity. Others dissent on grounds which are intrinsically no more than an assertion that the novel embodies the views of life prevalent at the end of the nineteenth century, and not those of an earlier and simpler generation—an assertion which I can only hope may be well founded. Let me repeat that a novel is an impression, not an argument; and there the matter must rest; as one is reminded by a passage which occurs in the letters of Schiller to Goethe on judges of this class: “They are those who seek only their own ideas in a representation, and prize that which should be as higher than what is. The cause of the dispute, therefore, lies in the very first principles, and it would be utterly impossible to come to an understanding with them.” And again: “As soon as I observe that any one, when judging of poetical representations, considers anything more important than the inner Necessity and Truth, I have done with him.”\n\nIn the introductory words to the first edition I suggested the possible advent of the genteel person who would not be able to endure something or other in these pages. That person duly appeared among the aforesaid objectors. In one case he felt upset that it was not possible for him to read the book through three times, owing to my not having made that critical effort which “alone can prove the salvation of such an one.” In another, he objected to such vulgar articles as the Devil’s pitchfork, a lodging-house carving-knife, and a shame-bought parasol, appearing in a respectable story. In another place he was a gentleman who turned Christian for half-an-hour the better to express his grief that a disrespectful phrase about the Immortals should have been used; though the same innate gentility compelled him to excuse the author in words of pity that one cannot be too thankful for: “He does but give us of his best.” I can assure this great critic that to exclaim illogically against the gods, singular or plural, is not such an original sin of mine as he seems to imagine. True, it may have some local originality; though if Shakespeare were an authority on history, which perhaps he is not, I could show that the sin was introduced into Wessex as early as the Heptarchy itself. Says Glo’ster in _Lear_, otherwise Ina, king of that country:\n\nAs flies to wanton boys are we to the gods; They kill us for their sport.\n\nThe remaining two or three manipulators of Tess were of the predetermined sort whom most writers and readers would gladly forget; professed literary boxers, who put on their convictions for the occasion; modern “Hammers of Heretics”; sworn Discouragers, ever on the watch to prevent the tentative half-success from becoming the whole success later on; who pervert plain meanings, and grow personal under the name of practising the great historical method. However, they may have causes to advance, privileges to guard, traditions to keep going; some of which a mere tale-teller, who writes down how the things of the world strike him, without any ulterior intentions whatever, has overlooked, and may by pure inadvertence have run foul of when in the least aggressive mood. Perhaps some passing perception, the outcome of a dream hour, would, if generally acted on, cause such an assailant considerable inconvenience with respect to position, interests, family, servant, ox, ass, neighbour, or neighbour’s wife. He therefore valiantly hides his personality behind a publisher’s shutters, and cries “Shame!” So densely is the world with any shifting of positions, even the best warranted advance, galls somebody’s kibe. Such shiftings often begin in sentiment, and such sentiment sometimes begins in a novel.\n\n_July_ 1892.\n\nThe foregoing remarks were written during the early career of this story, when a spirited public and private criticism of its points was still fresh to the feelings. The pages are allowed to stand for what they are worth, as something once said; but probably they would not have been written now. Even in the first short time which has elapsed since the book was first published, some of the critics who have provoked the reply have “gone down into silence,” as if to remind one of the infinite unimportance of both their say and mine.\n\n_January_ 1895.\n\nThe present edition of this novel contains a few pages that have never appeared in any previous edition. When the detached episodes were collected as stated in the preface of 1891, these pages were overlooked, though they were in the original manuscript. They occur in Chapter X.\n\nRespecting the sub-title, to which allusion was made above, I may add that it was appended at the last moment, after reading the final proofs, as being the estimate left in a candid mind of the heroine’s character—an estimate that nobody would be likely to dispute. It was disputed more than anything else in the book. _Melius fuerat non scibere._ But there it stands.\n\nThe novel was first published complete, in three volumes, in November, 1891.\n\n_March_ 1912.\n\nPhase the First:\n\nThe Maiden\n\n# I\n\nOn an evening in the latter part of May a middle-aged man was walking homeward from Shaston to the village of Marlott, in the adjoining Vale of Blakemore, or Blackmoor. The pair of legs that carried him were rickety, and there was a bias in his gait which inclined him somewhat to the left of a straight line. He occasionally gave a smart nod, as if in confirmation of some opinion, though he was not thinking of anything in particular. An empty egg-basket was slung upon his arm, the nap of his hat was ruffled, a patch being quite worn away at its brim where his thumb came in taking it off. Presently he was met by an elderly parson astride on a gray mare, who, as he rode, hummed a wandering tune.\n\n“Good night t’ee,” said the man with the basket.\n\n“Good night, Sir John,” said the parson.\n\nThe pedestrian, after another pace or two, halted, and turned round.\n\n“Now, sir, begging your pardon; we met last market-day on this road about this time, and I said ‘Good night,’ and you made reply ‘_Good night, Sir John_,’ as now.”\n\n“I did,” said the parson.\n\n“And once before that—near a month ago.”\n\n“I may have.”\n\n“Then what might your meaning be in calling me ‘Sir John’ these different times, when I be plain Jack Durbeyfield, the haggler?”\n\nThe parson rode a step or two nearer.\n\n“It was only my whim,” he said; and, after a moment’s hesitation: “It was on account of a discovery I made some little time ago, whilst I was hunting up pedigrees for the new county history. I am Parson Tringham, the antiquary, of Stagfoot Lane. Don’t you really know, Durbeyfield, that you are the lineal representative of the ancient and knightly family of the d’Urbervilles, who derive their descent from Sir Pagan d’Urberville, that renowned knight who came from Normandy with William the Conqueror, as appears by Battle Abbey Roll?”\n\n“Never heard it before, sir!”\n\n“Well it’s true. Throw up your chin a moment, so that I may catch the profile of your face better. Yes, that’s the d’Urberville nose and chin—a little debased. Your ancestor was one of the twelve knights who assisted the Lord of Estremavilla in Normandy in his conquest of Glamorganshire. Branches of your family held manors over all this part of England; their names appear in the Pipe Rolls in the time of King Stephen. In the reign of King John one of them was rich enough to give a manor to the Knights Hospitallers; and in Edward the Second’s time your forefather Brian was summoned to Westminster to attend the great Council there. You declined a little in Oliver Cromwell’s time, but to no serious extent, and in Charles the Second’s reign you were made Knights of the Royal Oak for your loyalty. Aye, there have been generations of Sir Johns among you, and if knighthood were hereditary, like a baronetcy, as it practically was in old times, when men were knighted from father to son, you would be Sir John now.”\n\n“Ye don’t say so!”\n\n“In short,” concluded the parson, decisively smacking his leg with his switch, “there’s hardly such another family in England.”\n\n“Daze my eyes, and isn’t there?” said Durbeyfield. “And here have I been knocking about, year after year, from pillar to post, as if I was no more than the commonest feller in the parish.... And how long hev this news about me been knowed, Pa’son Tringham?”\n\nThe clergyman explained that, as far as he was aware, it had quite died out of knowledge, and could hardly be said to be known at all. His own investigations had begun on a day in the preceding spring when, having been engaged in tracing the vicissitudes of the d’Urberville family, he had observed Durbeyfield’s name on his waggon, and had thereupon been led to make inquiries about his father and grandfather till he had no doubt on the subject.\n\n“At first I resolved not to disturb you with such a useless piece of information,” said he. “However, our impulses are too strong for our judgement sometimes. I thought you might perhaps know something of it all the while.”\n\n“Well, I have heard once or twice, ’tis true, that my family had seen better days afore they came to Blackmoor. But I took no notice o’t, thinking it to mean that we had once kept two horses where we now keep only one. I’ve got a wold silver spoon, and a wold graven seal at home, too; but, Lord, what’s a spoon and seal?... And to think that I and these noble d’Urbervilles were one flesh all the time. ’Twas said that my gr’t-granfer had secrets, and didn’t care to talk of where he came from.... And where do we raise our smoke, now, parson, if I may make so bold; I mean, where do we d’Urbervilles live?”\n\n“You don’t live anywhere. You are extinct—as a county family.”\n\n“That’s bad.”\n\n“Yes—what the mendacious family chronicles call extinct in the male line—that is, gone down—gone under.”\n\n“Then where do we lie?”\n\n“At Kingsbere-sub-Greenhill: rows and rows of you in your vaults, with your effigies under Purbeck-marble canopies.”\n\n“And where be our family mansions and estates?”\n\n“You haven’t any.”\n\n“Oh? No lands neither?”\n\n“None; though you once had ’em in abundance, as I said, for your family consisted of numerous branches. In this county there was a seat of yours at Kingsbere, and another at Sherton, and another in Millpond, and another at Lullstead, and another at Wellbridge.”\n\n“And shall we ever come into our own again?”\n\n“Ah—that I can’t tell!”\n\n“And what had I better do about it, sir?” asked Durbeyfield, after a pause.\n\n“Oh—nothing, nothing; except chasten yourself with the thought of ‘how are the mighty fallen.’ It is a fact of some interest to the local historian and genealogist, nothing more. There are several families among the cottagers of this county of almost equal lustre. Good night.”\n\n“But you’ll turn back and have a quart of beer wi’ me on the strength o’t, Pa’son Tringham? There’s a very pretty brew in tap at The Pure Drop—though, to be sure, not so good as at Rolliver’s.”\n\n“No, thank you—not this evening, Durbeyfield. You’ve had enough already.” Concluding thus, the parson rode on his way, with doubts as to his discretion in retailing this curious bit of lore.\n\nWhen he was gone, Durbeyfield walked a few steps in a profound reverie, and then sat down upon the grassy bank by the roadside, depositing his basket before him. In a few minutes a youth appeared in the distance, walking in the same direction as that which had been pursued by Durbeyfield. The latter, on seeing him, held up his hand, and the lad quickened his pace and came near.\n\n“Boy, take up that basket! I want ’ee to go on an errand for me.”\n\nThe lath-like stripling frowned. “Who be you, then, John Durbeyfield, to order me about and call me ‘boy’? You know my name as well as I know yours!”\n\n“Do you, do you? That’s the secret—that’s the secret! Now obey my orders, and take the message I’m going to charge ’ee wi’... Well, Fred, I don’t mind telling you that the secret is that I’m one of a noble race—it has been just found out by me this present afternoon, p.m.” And as he made the announcement, Durbeyfield, declining from his sitting position, luxuriously stretched himself out upon the bank among the daisies.\n\nThe lad stood before Durbeyfield, and contemplated his length from crown to toe.\n\n“Sir John d’Urberville—that’s who I am,” continued the prostrate man. “That is if knights were baronets—which they be. ’Tis recorded in history all about me. Dost know of such a place, lad, as Kingsbere-sub-Greenhill?”\n\n“Ees. I’ve been there to Greenhill Fair.”\n\n“Well, under the church of that city there lie—”\n\n“’Tisn’t a city, the place I mean; leastwise ’twaddn’ when I was there—’twas a little one-eyed, blinking sort o’ place.”\n\n“Never you mind the place, boy, that’s not the question before us. Under the church of that there parish lie my ancestors—hundreds of ’em—in coats of mail and jewels, in gr’t lead coffins weighing tons and tons. There’s not a man in the county o’ South-Wessex that’s got grander and nobler skillentons in his family than I.”\n\n“Oh?”\n\n“Now take up that basket, and goo on to Marlott, and when you’ve come to The Pure Drop Inn, tell ’em to send a horse and carriage to me immed’ately, to carry me hwome. And in the bottom o’ the carriage they be to put a noggin o’ rum in a small bottle, and chalk it up to my account. And when you’ve done that goo on to my house with the basket, and tell my wife to put away that washing, because she needn’t finish it, and wait till I come hwome, as I’ve news to tell her.”\n\nAs the lad stood in a dubious attitude, Durbeyfield put his hand in his pocket, and produced a shilling, one of the chronically few that he possessed.\n\n“Here’s for your labour, lad.”\n\nThis made a difference in the young man’s estimate of the position.\n\n“Yes, Sir John. Thank ’ee. Anything else I can do for ’ee, Sir John?”\n\n“Tell ’em at hwome that I should like for supper,—well, lamb’s fry if they can get it; and if they can’t, black-pot; and if they can’t get that, well chitterlings will do.”\n\n“Yes, Sir John.”\n\nThe boy took up the basket, and as he set out the notes of a brass band were heard from the direction of the village.\n\n“What’s that?” said Durbeyfield. “Not on account o’ I?”\n\n“’Tis the women’s club-walking, Sir John. Why, your da’ter is one o’ the members.”\n\n“To be sure—I’d quite forgot it in my thoughts of greater things! Well, vamp on to Marlott, will ye, and order that carriage, and maybe I’ll drive round and inspect the club.”\n\nThe lad departed, and Durbeyfield lay waiting on the grass and daisies in the evening sun. Not a soul passed that way for a long while, and the faint notes of the band were the only human sounds audible within the rim of blue hills.\n\n# II\n\nThe village of Marlott lay amid the north-eastern undulations of the beautiful Vale of Blakemore, or Blackmoor, aforesaid, an engirdled and secluded region, for the most part untrodden as yet by tourist or landscape-painter, though within a four hours’ journey from London.\n\nIt is a vale whose acquaintance is best made by viewing it from the summits of the hills that surround it—except perhaps during the droughts of summer. An unguided ramble into its recesses in bad weather is apt to engender dissatisfaction with its narrow, tortuous, and miry ways.\n\nThis fertile and sheltered tract of country, in which the fields are never brown and the springs never dry, is bounded on the south by the bold chalk ridge that embraces the prominences of Hambledon Hill, Bulbarrow, Nettlecombe-Tout, Dogbury, High Stoy, and Bubb Down. The traveller from the coast, who, after plodding northward for a score of miles over calcareous downs and corn-lands, suddenly reaches the verge of one of these escarpments, is surprised and delighted to behold, extended like a map beneath him, a country differing absolutely from that which he has passed through. Behind him the hills are open, the sun blazes down upon fields so large as to give an unenclosed character to the landscape, the lanes are white, the hedges low and plashed, the atmosphere colourless. Here, in the valley, the world seems to be constructed upon a smaller and more delicate scale; the fields are mere paddocks, so reduced that from this height their hedgerows appear a network of dark green threads overspreading the paler green of the grass. The atmosphere beneath is languorous, and is so tinged with azure that what artists call the middle distance partakes also of that hue, while the horizon beyond is of the deepest ultramarine. Arable lands are few and limited; with but slight exceptions the prospect is a broad rich mass of grass and trees, mantling minor hills and dales within the major. Such is the Vale of Blackmoor.\n\nThe district is of historic, no less than of topographical interest. The Vale was known in former times as the Forest of White Hart, from a curious legend of King Henry III’s reign, in which the killing by a certain Thomas de la Lynd of a beautiful white hart which the king had run down and spared, was made the occasion of a heavy fine. In those days, and till comparatively recent times, the country was densely wooded. Even now, traces of its earlier condition are to be found in the old oak copses and irregular belts of timber that yet survive upon its slopes, and the hollow-trunked trees that shade so many of its pastures.\n\nThe forests have departed, but some old customs of their shades remain. Many, however, linger only in a metamorphosed or disguised form. The May-Day dance, for instance, was to be discerned on the afternoon under notice, in the guise of the club revel, or “club-walking,” as it was there called.\n\nIt was an interesting event to the younger inhabitants of Marlott, though its real interest was not observed by the participators in the ceremony. Its singularity lay less in the retention of a custom of walking in procession and dancing on each anniversary than in the members being solely women. In men’s clubs such celebrations were, though expiring, less uncommon; but either the natural shyness of the softer sex, or a sarcastic attitude on the part of male relatives, had denuded such women’s clubs as remained (if any other did) of this their glory and consummation. The club of Marlott alone lived to uphold the local Cerealia. It had walked for hundreds of years, if not as benefit-club, as votive sisterhood of some sort; and it walked still.\n\nThe banded ones were all dressed in white gowns—a gay survival from Old Style days, when cheerfulness and May-time were synonyms—days before the habit of taking long views had reduced emotions to a monotonous average. Their first exhibition of themselves was in a processional march of two and two round the parish. Ideal and real clashed slightly as the sun lit up their figures against the green hedges and creeper-laced house-fronts; for, though the whole troop wore white garments, no two whites were alike among them. Some approached pure blanching; some had a bluish pallor; some worn by the older characters (which had possibly lain by folded for many a year) inclined to a cadaverous tint, and to a Georgian style.\n\nIn addition to the distinction of a white frock, every woman and girl carried in her right hand a peeled willow wand, and in her left a bunch of white flowers. The peeling of the former, and the selection of the latter, had been an operation of personal care.\n\nThere were a few middle-aged and even elderly women in the train, their silver-wiry hair and wrinkled faces, scourged by time and trouble, having almost a grotesque, certainly a pathetic, appearance in such a jaunty situation. In a true view, perhaps, there was more to be gathered and told of each anxious and experienced one, to whom the years were drawing nigh when she should say, “I have no pleasure in them,” than of her juvenile comrades. But let the elder be passed over here for those under whose bodices the life throbbed quick and warm.\n\nThe young girls formed, indeed, the majority of the band, and their heads of luxuriant hair reflected in the sunshine every tone of gold, and black, and brown. Some had beautiful eyes, others a beautiful nose, others a beautiful mouth and figure: few, if any, had all. A difficulty of arranging their lips in this crude exposure to public scrutiny, an inability to balance their heads, and to dissociate self-consciousness from their features, was apparent in them, and showed that they were genuine country girls, unaccustomed to many eyes.\n\nAnd as each and all of them were warmed without by the sun, so each had a private little sun for her soul to bask in; some dream, some affection, some hobby, at least some remote and distant hope which, though perhaps starving to nothing, still lived on, as hopes will. They were all cheerful, and many of them merry.\n\nThey came round by The Pure Drop Inn, and were turning out of the high road to pass through a wicket-gate into the meadows, when one of the women said—\n\n“The Load-a-Lord! Why, Tess Durbeyfield, if there isn’t thy father riding hwome in a carriage!”\n\nA young member of the band turned her head at the exclamation. She was a fine and handsome girl—not handsomer than some others, possibly—but her mobile peony mouth and large innocent eyes added eloquence to colour and shape. She wore a red ribbon in her hair, and was the only one of the white company who could boast of such a pronounced adornment. As she looked round Durbeyfield was seen moving along the road in a chaise belonging to The Pure Drop, driven by a frizzle-headed brawny damsel with her gown-sleeves rolled above her elbows. This was the cheerful servant of that establishment, who, in her part of factotum, turned groom and ostler at times. Durbeyfield, leaning back, and with his eyes closed luxuriously, was waving his hand above his head, and singing in a slow recitative—\n\n“I’ve-got-a-gr’t-family-vault-at-Kingsbere—and knighted-forefathers-in-lead-coffins-there!”\n\nThe clubbists tittered, except the girl called Tess—in whom a slow heat seemed to rise at the sense that her father was making himself foolish in their eyes.\n\n“He’s tired, that’s all,” she said hastily, “and he has got a lift home, because our own horse has to rest to-day.”\n\n“Bless thy simplicity, Tess,” said her companions. “He’s got his market-nitch. Haw-haw!”\n\n“Look here; I won’t walk another inch with you, if you say any jokes about him!” Tess cried, and the colour upon her cheeks spread over her face and neck. In a moment her eyes grew moist, and her glance drooped to the ground. Perceiving that they had really pained her they said no more, and order again prevailed. Tess’s pride would not allow her to turn her head again, to learn what her father’s meaning was, if he had any; and thus she moved on with the whole body to the enclosure where there was to be dancing on the green. By the time the spot was reached she had recovered her equanimity, and tapped her neighbour with her wand and talked as usual.\n\nTess Durbeyfield at this time of her life was a mere vessel of emotion untinctured by experience. The dialect was on her tongue to some extent, despite the village school: the characteristic intonation of that dialect for this district being the voicing approximately rendered by the syllable UR, probably as rich an utterance as any to be found in human speech. The pouted-up deep red mouth to which this syllable was native had hardly as yet settled into its definite shape, and her lower lip had a way of thrusting the middle of her top one upward, when they closed together after a word.\n\nPhases of her childhood lurked in her aspect still. As she walked along to-day, for all her bouncing handsome womanliness, you could sometimes see her twelfth year in her cheeks, or her ninth sparkling from her eyes; and even her fifth would flit over the curves of her mouth now and then.\n\nYet few knew, and still fewer considered this. A small minority, mainly strangers, would look long at her in casually passing by, and grow momentarily fascinated by her freshness, and wonder if they would ever see her again: but to almost everybody she was a fine and picturesque country girl, and no more.\n\nNothing was seen or heard further of Durbeyfield in his triumphal chariot under the conduct of the ostleress, and the club having entered the allotted space, dancing began. As there were no men in the company, the girls danced at first with each other, but when the hour for the close of labour drew on, the masculine inhabitants of the village, together with other idlers and pedestrians, gathered round the spot, and appeared inclined to negotiate for a partner.\n\nAmong these on-lookers were three young men of a superior class, carrying small knapsacks strapped to their shoulders, and stout sticks in their hands. Their general likeness to each other, and their consecutive ages, would almost have suggested that they might be, what in fact they were, brothers. The eldest wore the white tie, high waistcoat, and thin-brimmed hat of the regulation curate; the second was the normal undergraduate; the appearance of the third and youngest would hardly have been sufficient to characterize him; there was an uncribbed, uncabined aspect in his eyes and attire, implying that he had hardly as yet found the entrance to his professional groove. That he was a desultory tentative student of something and everything might only have been predicted of him.\n\nThese three brethren told casual acquaintance that they were spending their Whitsun holidays in a walking tour through the Vale of Blackmoor, their course being south-westerly from the town of Shaston on the north-east.\n\nThey leant over the gate by the highway, and inquired as to the meaning of the dance and the white-frocked maids. The two elder of the brothers were plainly not intending to linger more than a moment, but the spectacle of a bevy of girls dancing without male partners seemed to amuse the third, and make him in no hurry to move on. He unstrapped his knapsack, put it, with his stick, on the hedge-bank, and opened the gate.\n\n“What are you going to do, Angel?” asked the eldest.\n\n“I am inclined to go and have a fling with them. Why not all of us—just for a minute or two—it will not detain us long?”\n\n“No—no; nonsense!” said the first. “Dancing in public with a troop of country hoydens—suppose we should be seen! Come along, or it will be dark before we get to Stourcastle, and there’s no place we can sleep at nearer than that; besides, we must get through another chapter of _A Counterblast to Agnosticism_ before we turn in, now I have taken the trouble to bring the book.”\n\n“All right—I’ll overtake you and Cuthbert in five minutes; don’t stop; I give my word that I will, Felix.”\n\nThe two elder reluctantly left him and walked on, taking their brother’s knapsack to relieve him in following, and the youngest entered the field.\n\n“This is a thousand pities,” he said gallantly, to two or three of the girls nearest him, as soon as there was a pause in the dance. “Where are your partners, my dears?”\n\n“They’ve not left off work yet,” answered one of the boldest. “They’ll be here by and by. Till then, will you be one, sir?”\n\n“Certainly. But what’s one among so many!”\n\n“Better than none. ’Tis melancholy work facing and footing it to one of your own sort, and no clipsing and colling at all. Now, pick and choose.”\n\n“’Ssh—don’t be so for’ard!” said a shyer girl.\n\nThe young man, thus invited, glanced them over, and attempted some discrimination; but, as the group were all so new to him, he could not very well exercise it. He took almost the first that came to hand, which was not the speaker, as she had expected; nor did it happen to be Tess Durbeyfield. Pedigree, ancestral skeletons, monumental record, the d’Urberville lineaments, did not help Tess in her life’s battle as yet, even to the extent of attracting to her a dancing-partner over the heads of the commonest peasantry. So much for Norman blood unaided by Victorian lucre.\n\nThe name of the eclipsing girl, whatever it was, has not been handed down; but she was envied by all as the first who enjoyed the luxury of a masculine partner that evening. Yet such was the force of example that the village young men, who had not hastened to enter the gate while no intruder was in the way, now dropped in quickly, and soon the couples became leavened with rustic youth to a marked extent, till at length the plainest woman in the club was no longer compelled to foot it on the masculine side of the figure.\n\nThe church clock struck, when suddenly the student said that he must leave—he had been forgetting himself—he had to join his companions. As he fell out of the dance his eyes lighted on Tess Durbeyfield, whose own large orbs wore, to tell the truth, the faintest aspect of reproach that he had not chosen her. He, too, was sorry then that, owing to her backwardness, he had not observed her; and with that in his mind he left the pasture.\n\nOn account of his long delay he started in a flying-run down the lane westward, and had soon passed the hollow and mounted the next rise. He had not yet overtaken his brothers, but he paused to get breath, and looked back. He could see the white figures of the girls in the green enclosure whirling about as they had whirled when he was among them. They seemed to have quite forgotten him already.\n\nAll of them, except, perhaps, one. This white shape stood apart by the hedge alone. From her position he knew it to be the pretty maiden with whom he had not danced. Trifling as the matter was, he yet instinctively felt that she was hurt by his oversight. He wished that he had asked her; he wished that he had inquired her name. She was so modest, so expressive, she had looked so soft in her thin white gown that he felt he had acted stupidly.\n\nHowever, it could not be helped, and turning, and bending himself to a rapid walk, he dismissed the subject from his mind.\n\n# III\n\nAs for Tess Durbeyfield, she did not so easily dislodge the incident from her consideration. She had no spirit to dance again for a long time, though she might have had plenty of partners; but ah! they did not speak so nicely as the strange young man had done. It was not till the rays of the sun had absorbed the young stranger’s retreating figure on the hill that she shook off her temporary sadness and answered her would-be partner in the affirmative.\n\nShe remained with her comrades till dusk, and participated with a certain zest in the dancing; though, being heart-whole as yet, she enjoyed treading a measure purely for its own sake; little divining when she saw “the soft torments, the bitter sweets, the pleasing pains, and the agreeable distresses” of those girls who had been wooed and won, what she herself was capable of in that kind. The struggles and wrangles of the lads for her hand in a jig were an amusement to her—no more; and when they became fierce she rebuked them.\n\nShe might have stayed even later, but the incident of her father’s odd appearance and manner returned upon the girl’s mind to make her anxious, and wondering what had become of him she dropped away from the dancers and bent her steps towards the end of the village at which the parental cottage lay.\n\nWhile yet many score yards off, other rhythmic sounds than those she had quitted became audible to her; sounds that she knew well—so well. They were a regular series of thumpings from the interior of the house, occasioned by the violent rocking of a cradle upon a stone floor, to which movement a feminine voice kept time by singing, in a vigorous gallopade, the favourite ditty of “The Spotted Cow”—\n\nI saw her lie do′-own in yon′-der green gro′-ove; Come, love!′ and I'll tell′ you where!′\n\nThe cradle-rocking and the song would cease simultaneously for a moment, and an exclamation at highest vocal pitch would take the place of the melody.\n\n“God bless thy diment eyes! And thy waxen cheeks! And thy cherry mouth! And thy Cubit’s thighs! And every bit o’ thy blessed body!”\n\nAfter this invocation the rocking and the singing would recommence, and the “Spotted Cow” proceed as before. So matters stood when Tess opened the door and paused upon the mat within it, surveying the scene.\n\nThe interior, in spite of the melody, struck upon the girl’s senses with an unspeakable dreariness. From the holiday gaieties of the field—the white gowns, the nosegays, the willow-wands, the whirling movements on the green, the flash of gentle sentiment towards the stranger—to the yellow melancholy of this one-candled spectacle, what a step! Besides the jar of contrast there came to her a chill self-reproach that she had not returned sooner, to help her mother in these domesticities, instead of indulging herself out-of-doors.\n\nThere stood her mother amid the group of children, as Tess had left her, hanging over the Monday washing-tub, which had now, as always, lingered on to the end of the week. Out of that tub had come the day before—Tess felt it with a dreadful sting of remorse—the very white frock upon her back which she had so carelessly greened about the skirt on the damping grass—which had been wrung up and ironed by her mother’s own hands.\n\nAs usual, Mrs Durbeyfield was balanced on one foot beside the tub, the other being engaged in the aforesaid business of rocking her youngest child. The cradle-rockers had done hard duty for so many years, under the weight of so many children, on that flagstone floor, that they were worn nearly flat, in consequence of which a huge jerk accompanied each swing of the cot, flinging the baby from side to side like a weaver’s shuttle, as Mrs Durbeyfield, excited by her song, trod the rocker with all the spring that was left in her after a long day’s seething in the suds.\n\nNick-knock, nick-knock, went the cradle; the candle-flame stretched itself tall, and began jigging up and down; the water dribbled from the matron’s elbows, and the song galloped on to the end of the verse, Mrs Durbeyfield regarding her daughter the while. Even now, when burdened with a young family, Joan Durbeyfield was a passionate lover of tune. No ditty floated into Blackmoor Vale from the outer world but Tess’s mother caught up its notation in a week.\n\nThere still faintly beamed from the woman’s features something of the freshness, and even the prettiness, of her youth; rendering it probable that the personal charms which Tess could boast of were in main part her mother’s gift, and therefore unknightly, unhistorical.\n\n“I’ll rock the cradle for ’ee, mother,” said the daughter gently. “Or I’ll take off my best frock and help you wring up? I thought you had finished long ago.”\n\nHer mother bore Tess no ill-will for leaving the housework to her single-handed efforts for so long; indeed, Joan seldom upbraided her thereon at any time, feeling but slightly the lack of Tess’s assistance whilst her instinctive plan for relieving herself of her labours lay in postponing them. To-night, however, she was even in a blither mood than usual. There was a dreaminess, a pre-occupation, an exaltation, in the maternal look which the girl could not understand.\n\n“Well, I’m glad you’ve come,” her mother said, as soon as the last note had passed out of her. “I want to go and fetch your father; but what’s more’n that, I want to tell ’ee what have happened. Y’ll be fess enough, my poppet, when th’st know!” (Mrs Durbeyfield habitually spoke the dialect; her daughter, who had passed the Sixth Standard in the National School under a London-trained mistress, spoke two languages: the dialect at home, more or less; ordinary English abroad and to persons of quality.)\n\n“Since I’ve been away?” Tess asked.\n“Ay!”\n\n“Had it anything to do with father’s making such a mommet of himself in thik carriage this afternoon? Why did ’er? I felt inclined to sink into the ground with shame!”\n\n“That wer all a part of the larry! We’ve been found to be the greatest gentlefolk in the whole county—reaching all back long before Oliver Grumble’s time—to the days of the Pagan Turks—with monuments, and vaults, and crests, and ’scutcheons, and the Lord knows what all. In Saint Charles’s days we was made Knights o’ the Royal Oak, our real name being d’Urberville!... Don’t that make your bosom plim? ’Twas on this account that your father rode home in the vlee; not because he’d been drinking, as people supposed.”\n\n“I’m glad of that. Will it do us any good, mother?”\n\n“O yes! ’Tis thoughted that great things may come o’t. No doubt a mampus of volk of our own rank will be down here in their carriages as soon as ’tis known. Your father learnt it on his way hwome from Shaston, and he has been telling me the whole pedigree of the matter.”\n\n“Where is father now?” asked Tess suddenly.\n\nHer mother gave irrelevant information by way of answer: “He called to see the doctor to-day in Shaston. It is not consumption at all, it seems. It is fat round his heart, ’a says. There, it is like this.” Joan Durbeyfield, as she spoke, curved a sodden thumb and forefinger to the shape of the letter C, and used the other forefinger as a pointer. “‘At the present moment,’ he says to your father, ‘your heart is enclosed all round there, and all round there; this space is still open,’ ’a says. ‘As soon as it do meet, so,’”—Mrs Durbeyfield closed her fingers into a circle complete—“‘off you will go like a shadder, Mr Durbeyfield,’ ’a says. ‘You mid last ten years; you mid go off in ten months, or ten days.’”\n\nTess looked alarmed. Her father possibly to go behind the eternal cloud so soon, notwithstanding this sudden greatness!\n\n“But where _is_ father?” she asked again.\n\nHer mother put on a deprecating look. “Now don’t you be bursting out angry! The poor man—he felt so rafted after his uplifting by the pa’son’s news—that he went up to Rolliver’s half an hour ago. He do want to get up his strength for his journey to-morrow with that load of beehives, which must be delivered, family or no. He’ll have to start shortly after twelve to-night, as the distance is so long.”\n\n“Get up his strength!” said Tess impetuously, the tears welling to her eyes. “O my God! Go to a public-house to get up his strength! And you as well agreed as he, mother!”\n\nHer rebuke and her mood seemed to fill the whole room, and to impart a cowed look to the furniture, and candle, and children playing about, and to her mother’s face.\n\n“No,” said the latter touchily, “I be not agreed. I have been waiting for ’ee to bide and keep house while I go fetch him.”\n\n“I’ll go.”\n\n“O no, Tess. You see, it would be no use.”\n\nTess did not expostulate. She knew what her mother’s objection meant. Mrs Durbeyfield’s jacket and bonnet were already hanging slily upon a chair by her side, in readiness for this contemplated jaunt, the reason for which the matron deplored more than its necessity.\n\n“And take the _Compleat Fortune-Teller_ to the outhouse,” Joan continued, rapidly wiping her hands, and donning the garments.\n\nThe _Compleat Fortune-Teller_ was an old thick volume, which lay on a table at her elbow, so worn by pocketing that the margins had reached the edge of the type. Tess took it up, and her mother started.\n\nThis going to hunt up her shiftless husband at the inn was one of Mrs Durbeyfield’s still extant enjoyments in the muck and muddle of rearing children. To discover him at Rolliver’s, to sit there for an hour or two by his side and dismiss all thought and care of the children during the interval, made her happy. A sort of halo, an occidental glow, came over life then. Troubles and other realities took on themselves a metaphysical impalpability, sinking to mere mental phenomena for serene contemplation, and no longer stood as pressing concretions which chafed body and soul. The youngsters, not immediately within sight, seemed rather bright and desirable appurtenances than otherwise; the incidents of daily life were not without humorousness and jollity in their aspect there. She felt a little as she had used to feel when she sat by her now wedded husband in the same spot during his wooing, shutting her eyes to his defects of character, and regarding him only in his ideal presentation as lover.\n\nTess, being left alone with the younger children, went first to the outhouse with the fortune-telling book, and stuffed it into the thatch. A curious fetishistic fear of this grimy volume on the part of her mother prevented her ever allowing it to stay in the house all night, and hither it was brought back whenever it had been consulted. Between the mother, with her fast-perishing lumber of superstitions, folk-lore, dialect, and orally transmitted ballads, and the daughter, with her trained National teachings and Standard knowledge under an infinitely Revised Code, there was a gap of two hundred years as ordinarily understood. When they were together the Jacobean and the Victorian ages were juxtaposed.\n\nReturning along the garden path Tess mused on what the mother could have wished to ascertain from the book on this particular day. She guessed the recent ancestral discovery to bear upon it, but did not divine that it solely concerned herself. Dismissing this, however, she busied herself with sprinkling the linen dried during the day-time, in company with her nine-year-old brother Abraham, and her sister Eliza-Louisa of twelve and a half, called “’Liza-Lu,” the youngest ones being put to bed. There was an interval of four years and more between Tess and the next of the family, the two who had filled the gap having died in their infancy, and this lent her a deputy-maternal attitude when she was alone with her juniors. Next in juvenility to Abraham came two more girls, Hope and Modesty; then a boy of three, and then the baby, who had just completed his first year.\n\nAll these young souls were passengers in the Durbeyfield ship—entirely dependent on the judgement of the two Durbeyfield adults for their pleasures, their necessities, their health, even their existence. If the heads of the Durbeyfield household chose to sail into difficulty, disaster, starvation, disease, degradation, death, thither were these half-dozen little captives under hatches compelled to sail with them—six helpless creatures, who had never been asked if they wished for life on any terms, much less if they wished for it on such hard conditions as were involved in being of the shiftless house of Durbeyfield. Some people would like to know whence the poet whose philosophy is in these days deemed as profound and trustworthy as his song is breezy and pure, gets his authority for speaking of “Nature’s holy plan.”\n\nIt grew later, and neither father nor mother reappeared. Tess looked out of the door, and took a mental journey through Marlott. The village was shutting its eyes. Candles and lamps were being put out everywhere: she could inwardly behold the extinguisher and the extended hand.\n\nHer mother’s fetching simply meant one more to fetch. Tess began to perceive that a man in indifferent health, who proposed to start on a journey before one in the morning, ought not to be at an inn at this late hour celebrating his ancient blood.\n\n“Abraham,” she said to her little brother, “do you put on your hat—you bain’t afraid?—and go up to Rolliver’s, and see what has gone wi’ father and mother.”\n\nThe boy jumped promptly from his seat, and opened the door, and the night swallowed him up. Half an hour passed yet again; neither man, woman, nor child returned. Abraham, like his parents, seemed to have been limed and caught by the ensnaring inn.\n\n“I must go myself,” she said.\n\n’Liza-Lu then went to bed, and Tess, locking them all in, started on her way up the dark and crooked lane or street not made for hasty progress; a street laid out before inches of land had value, and when one-handed clocks sufficiently subdivided the day.\n\n# IV\n\nRolliver’s inn, the single alehouse at this end of the long and broken village, could only boast of an off-licence; hence, as nobody could legally drink on the premises, the amount of overt accommodation for consumers was strictly limited to a little board about six inches wide and two yards long, fixed to the garden palings by pieces of wire, so as to form a ledge. On this board thirsty strangers deposited their cups as they stood in the road and drank, and threw the dregs on the dusty ground to the pattern of Polynesia, and wished they could have a restful seat inside.\n\nThus the strangers. But there were also local customers who felt the same wish; and where there’s a will there’s a way.\n\nIn a large bedroom upstairs, the window of which was thickly curtained with a great woollen shawl lately discarded by the landlady, Mrs Rolliver, were gathered on this evening nearly a dozen persons, all seeking beatitude; all old inhabitants of the nearer end of Marlott, and frequenters of this retreat. Not only did the distance to the The Pure Drop, the fully-licensed tavern at the further part of the dispersed village, render its accommodation practically unavailable for dwellers at this end; but the far more serious question, the quality of the liquor, confirmed the prevalent opinion that it was better to drink with Rolliver in a corner of the housetop than with the other landlord in a wide house.\n\nA gaunt four-post bedstead which stood in the room afforded sitting-space for several persons gathered round three of its sides; a couple more men had elevated themselves on a chest of drawers; another rested on the oak-carved “cwoffer”; two on the wash-stand; another on the stool; and thus all were, somehow, seated at their ease. The stage of mental comfort to which they had arrived at this hour was one wherein their souls expanded beyond their skins, and spread their personalities warmly through the room. In this process the chamber and its furniture grew more and more dignified and luxurious; the shawl hanging at the window took upon itself the richness of tapestry; the brass handles of the chest of drawers were as golden knockers; and the carved bedposts seemed to have some kinship with the magnificent pillars of Solomon’s temple.\n\nMrs Durbeyfield, having quickly walked hitherward after parting from Tess, opened the front door, crossed the downstairs room, which was in deep gloom, and then unfastened the stair-door like one whose fingers knew the tricks of the latches well. Her ascent of the crooked staircase was a slower process, and her face, as it rose into the light above the last stair, encountered the gaze of all the party assembled in the bedroom.\n\n“—Being a few private friends I’ve asked in to keep up club-walking at my own expense,” the landlady exclaimed at the sound of footsteps, as glibly as a child repeating the Catechism, while she peered over the stairs. “Oh, ’tis you, Mrs Durbeyfield—Lard—how you frightened me!—I thought it might be some gaffer sent by Gover’ment.”\n\nMrs Durbeyfield was welcomed with glances and nods by the remainder of the conclave, and turned to where her husband sat. He was humming absently to himself, in a low tone: “I be as good as some folks here and there! I’ve got a great family vault at Kingsbere-sub-Greenhill, and finer skillentons than any man in Wessex!”\n\n“I’ve something to tell ’ee that’s come into my head about that—a grand projick!” whispered his cheerful wife. “Here, John, don’t ’ee see me?” She nudged him, while he, looking through her as through a window-pane, went on with his recitative.\n\n“Hush! Don’t ’ee sing so loud, my good man,” said the landlady; “in case any member of the Gover’ment should be passing, and take away my licends.”\n\n“He’s told ’ee what’s happened to us, I suppose?” asked Mrs Durbeyfield.\n\n“Yes—in a way. D’ye think there’s any money hanging by it?”\n\n“Ah, that’s the secret,” said Joan Durbeyfield sagely. “However, ’tis well to be kin to a coach, even if you don’t ride in ’en.” She dropped her public voice, and continued in a low tone to her husband: “I’ve been thinking since you brought the news that there’s a great rich lady out by Trantridge, on the edge o’ The Chase, of the name of d’Urberville.”\n\n“Hey—what’s that?” said Sir John.\n\nShe repeated the information. “That lady must be our relation,” she said. “And my projick is to send Tess to claim kin.”\n\n“There _is_ a lady of the name, now you mention it,” said Durbeyfield. “Pa’son Tringham didn’t think of that. But she’s nothing beside we—a junior branch of us, no doubt, hailing long since King Norman’s day.”\n\nWhile this question was being discussed neither of the pair noticed, in their preoccupation, that little Abraham had crept into the room, and was awaiting an opportunity of asking them to return.\n\n“She is rich, and she’d be sure to take notice o’ the maid,” continued Mrs Durbeyfield; “and ’twill be a very good thing. I don’t see why two branches o’ one family should not be on visiting terms.”\n\n“Yes; and we’ll all claim kin!” said Abraham brightly from under the bedstead. “And we’ll all go and see her when Tess has gone to live with her; and we’ll ride in her coach and wear black clothes!”\n\n“How do you come here, child? What nonsense be ye talking! Go away, and play on the stairs till father and mother be ready!... Well, Tess ought to go to this other member of our family. She’d be sure to win the lady—Tess would; and likely enough ’twould lead to some noble gentleman marrying her. In short, I know it.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“I tried her fate in the _Fortune-Teller_, and it brought out that very thing!... You should ha’ seen how pretty she looked to-day; her skin is as sumple as a duchess’.”\n\n“What says the maid herself to going?”\n\n“I’ve not asked her. She don’t know there is any such lady-relation yet. But it would certainly put her in the way of a grand marriage, and she won’t say nay to going.”\n\n“Tess is queer.”\n\n“But she’s tractable at bottom. Leave her to me.”\n\nThough this conversation had been private, sufficient of its import reached the understandings of those around to suggest to them that the Durbeyfields had weightier concerns to talk of now than common folks had, and that Tess, their pretty eldest daughter, had fine prospects in store.\n\n“Tess is a fine figure o’ fun, as I said to myself to-day when I zeed her vamping round parish with the rest,” observed one of the elderly boozers in an undertone. “But Joan Durbeyfield must mind that she don’t get green malt in floor.” It was a local phrase which had a peculiar meaning, and there was no reply.\n\nThe conversation became inclusive, and presently other footsteps were heard crossing the room below.\n\n“—Being a few private friends asked in to-night to keep up club-walking at my own expense.” The landlady had rapidly re-used the formula she kept on hand for intruders before she recognized that the newcomer was Tess.\n\nEven to her mother’s gaze the girl’s young features looked sadly out of place amid the alcoholic vapours which floated here as no unsuitable medium for wrinkled middle-age; and hardly was a reproachful flash from Tess’s dark eyes needed to make her father and mother rise from their seats, hastily finish their ale, and descend the stairs behind her, Mrs Rolliver’s caution following their footsteps.\n\n“No noise, please, if ye’ll be so good, my dears; or I mid lose my licends, and be summons’d, and I don’t know what all! ’Night t’ye!”\n\nThey went home together, Tess holding one arm of her father, and Mrs Durbeyfield the other. He had, in truth, drunk very little—not a fourth of the quantity which a systematic tippler could carry to church on a Sunday afternoon without a hitch in his eastings or genuflections; but the weakness of Sir John’s constitution made mountains of his petty sins in this kind. On reaching the fresh air he was sufficiently unsteady to incline the row of three at one moment as if they were marching to London, and at another as if they were marching to Bath—which produced a comical effect, frequent enough in families on nocturnal homegoings; and, like most comical effects, not quite so comic after all. The two women valiantly disguised these forced excursions and countermarches as well as they could from Durbeyfield, their cause, and from Abraham, and from themselves; and so they approached by degrees their own door, the head of the family bursting suddenly into his former refrain as he drew near, as if to fortify his soul at sight of the smallness of his present residence—\n\n“I’ve got a fam—ily vault at Kingsbere!”\n\n“Hush—don’t be so silly, Jacky,” said his wife. “Yours is not the only family that was of ’count in wold days. Look at the Anktells, and Horseys, and the Tringhams themselves—gone to seed a’most as much as you—though you was bigger folks than they, that’s true. Thank God, I was never of no family, and have nothing to be ashamed of in that way!”\n\n“Don’t you be so sure o’ that. From your nater ’tis my belief you’ve disgraced yourselves more than any o’ us, and was kings and queens outright at one time.”\n\nTess turned the subject by saying what was far more prominent in her own mind at the moment than thoughts of her ancestry—“I am afraid father won’t be able to take the journey with the beehives to-morrow so early.”\n\n“I? I shall be all right in an hour or two,” said Durbeyfield.\n\nIt was eleven o’clock before the family were all in bed, and two o’clock next morning was the latest hour for starting with the beehives if they were to be delivered to the retailers in Casterbridge before the Saturday market began, the way thither lying by bad roads over a distance of between twenty and thirty miles, and the horse and waggon being of the slowest. At half-past one Mrs Durbeyfield came into the large bedroom where Tess and all her little brothers and sisters slept.\n\n“The poor man can’t go,” she said to her eldest daughter, whose great eyes had opened the moment her mother’s hand touched the door.\n\nTess sat up in bed, lost in a vague interspace between a dream and this information.\n\n“But somebody must go,” she replied. “It is late for the hives already. Swarming will soon be over for the year; and it we put off taking ’em till next week’s market the call for ’em will be past, and they’ll be thrown on our hands.”\n\nMrs Durbeyfield looked unequal to the emergency. “Some young feller, perhaps, would go? One of them who were so much after dancing with ’ee yesterday,” she presently suggested.\n\n“O no—I wouldn’t have it for the world!” declared Tess proudly. “And letting everybody know the reason—such a thing to be ashamed of! I think _I_ could go if Abraham could go with me to kip me company.”\n\nHer mother at length agreed to this arrangement. Little Abraham was aroused from his deep sleep in a corner of the same apartment, and made to put on his clothes while still mentally in the other world. Meanwhile Tess had hastily dressed herself; and the twain, lighting a lantern, went out to the stable. The rickety little waggon was already laden, and the girl led out the horse, Prince, only a degree less rickety than the vehicle.\n\nThe poor creature looked wonderingly round at the night, at the lantern, at their two figures, as if he could not believe that at that hour, when every living thing was intended to be in shelter and at rest, he was called upon to go out and labour. They put a stock of candle-ends into the lantern, hung the latter to the off-side of the load, and directed the horse onward, walking at his shoulder at first during the uphill parts of the way, in order not to overload an animal of so little vigour. To cheer themselves as well as they could, they made an artificial morning with the lantern, some bread and butter, and their own conversation, the real morning being far from come. Abraham, as he more fully awoke (for he had moved in a sort of trance so far), began to talk of the strange shapes assumed by the various dark objects against the sky; of this tree that looked like a raging tiger springing from a lair; of that which resembled a giant’s head.\n\nWhen they had passed the little town of Stourcastle, dumbly somnolent under its thick brown thatch, they reached higher ground. Still higher, on their left, the elevation called Bulbarrow, or Bealbarrow, well-nigh the highest in South Wessex, swelled into the sky, engirdled by its earthen trenches. From hereabout the long road was fairly level for some distance onward. They mounted in front of the waggon, and Abraham grew reflective.\n\n“Tess!” he said in a preparatory tone, after a silence.\n\n“Yes, Abraham.”\n\n“Bain’t you glad that we’ve become gentlefolk?”\n\n“Not particular glad.”\n\n“But you be glad that you ’m going to marry a gentleman?”\n\n“What?” said Tess, lifting her face.\n\n“That our great relation will help ’ee to marry a gentleman.”\n\n“I? Our great relation? We have no such relation. What has put that into your head?”\n\n“I heard ’em talking about it up at Rolliver’s when I went to find father. There’s a rich lady of our family out at Trantridge, and mother said that if you claimed kin with the lady, she’d put ’ee in the way of marrying a gentleman.”\n\nHis sister became abruptly still, and lapsed into a pondering silence. Abraham talked on, rather for the pleasure of utterance than for audition, so that his sister’s abstraction was of no account. He leant back against the hives, and with upturned face made observations on the stars, whose cold pulses were beating amid the black hollows above, in serene dissociation from these two wisps of human life. He asked how far away those twinklers were, and whether God was on the other side of them. But ever and anon his childish prattle recurred to what impressed his imagination even more deeply than the wonders of creation. If Tess were made rich by marrying a gentleman, would she have money enough to buy a spyglass so large that it would draw the stars as near to her as Nettlecombe-Tout?\n\nThe renewed subject, which seemed to have impregnated the whole family, filled Tess with impatience.\n\n“Never mind that now!” she exclaimed.\n\n“Did you say the stars were worlds, Tess?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“All like ours?”\n\n“I don’t know; but I think so. They sometimes seem to be like the apples on our stubbard-tree. Most of them splendid and sound—a few blighted.”\n\n“Which do we live on—a splendid one or a blighted one?”\n\n“A blighted one.”\n\n“’Tis very unlucky that we didn’t pitch on a sound one, when there were so many more of ’em!”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Is it like that _really_, Tess?” said Abraham, turning to her much impressed, on reconsideration of this rare information. “How would it have been if we had pitched on a sound one?”\n\n“Well, father wouldn’t have coughed and creeped about as he does, and wouldn’t have got too tipsy to go on this journey; and mother wouldn’t have been always washing, and never getting finished.”\n\n“And you would have been a rich lady ready-made, and not have had to be made rich by marrying a gentleman?”\n\n“O Aby, don’t—don’t talk of that any more!”\n\nLeft to his reflections Abraham soon grew drowsy. Tess was not skilful in the management of a horse, but she thought that she could take upon herself the entire conduct of the load for the present and allow Abraham to go to sleep if he wished to do so. She made him a sort of nest in front of the hives, in such a manner that he could not fall, and, taking the reins into her own hands, jogged on as before.\n\nPrince required but slight attention, lacking energy for superfluous movements of any sort. With no longer a companion to distract her, Tess fell more deeply into reverie than ever, her back leaning against the hives. The mute procession past her shoulders of trees and hedges became attached to fantastic scenes outside reality, and the occasional heave of the wind became the sigh of some immense sad soul, conterminous with the universe in space, and with history in time.\nThen, examining the mesh of events in her own life, she seemed to see the vanity of her father’s pride; the gentlemanly suitor awaiting herself in her mother’s fancy; to see him as a grimacing personage, laughing at her poverty and her shrouded knightly ancestry. Everything grew more and more extravagant, and she no longer knew how time passed. A sudden jerk shook her in her seat, and Tess awoke from the sleep into which she, too, had fallen.\n\nThey were a long way further on than when she had lost consciousness, and the waggon had stopped. A hollow groan, unlike anything she had ever heard in her life, came from the front, followed by a shout of “Hoi there!”\n\nThe lantern hanging at her waggon had gone out, but another was shining in her face—much brighter than her own had been. Something terrible had happened. The harness was entangled with an object which blocked the way.\n\nIn consternation Tess jumped down, and discovered the dreadful truth. The groan had proceeded from her father’s poor horse Prince. The morning mail-cart, with its two noiseless wheels, speeding along these lanes like an arrow, as it always did, had driven into her slow and unlighted equipage. The pointed shaft of the cart had entered the breast of the unhappy Prince like a sword, and from the wound his life’s blood was spouting in a stream, and falling with a hiss into the road.\n\nIn her despair Tess sprang forward and put her hand upon the hole, with the only result that she became splashed from face to skirt with the crimson drops. Then she stood helplessly looking on. Prince also stood firm and motionless as long as he could; till he suddenly sank down in a heap.\n\nBy this time the mail-cart man had joined her, and began dragging and unharnessing the hot form of Prince. But he was already dead, and, seeing that nothing more could be done immediately, the mail-cart man returned to his own animal, which was uninjured.\n\n“You was on the wrong side,” he said. “I am bound to go on with the mail-bags, so that the best thing for you to do is bide here with your load. I’ll send somebody to help you as soon as I can. It is getting daylight, and you have nothing to fear.”\n\nHe mounted and sped on his way; while Tess stood and waited. The atmosphere turned pale, the birds shook themselves in the hedges, arose, and twittered; the lane showed all its white features, and Tess showed hers, still whiter. The huge pool of blood in front of her was already assuming the iridescence of coagulation; and when the sun rose a hundred prismatic hues were reflected from it. Prince lay alongside, still and stark; his eyes half open, the hole in his chest looking scarcely large enough to have let out all that had animated him.\n\n“’Tis all my doing—all mine!” the girl cried, gazing at the spectacle. “No excuse for me—none. What will mother and father live on now? Aby, Aby!” She shook the child, who had slept soundly through the whole disaster. “We can’t go on with our load—Prince is killed!”\n\nWhen Abraham realized all, the furrows of fifty years were extemporized on his young face.\n\n“Why, I danced and laughed only yesterday!” she went on to herself. “To think that I was such a fool!”\n\n“’Tis because we be on a blighted star, and not a sound one, isn’t it, Tess?” murmured Abraham through his tears.\n\nIn silence they waited through an interval which seemed endless. At length a sound, and an approaching object, proved to them that the driver of the mail-car had been as good as his word. A farmer’s man from near Stourcastle came up, leading a strong cob. He was harnessed to the waggon of beehives in the place of Prince, and the load taken on towards Casterbridge.\n\nThe evening of the same day saw the empty waggon reach again the spot of the accident. Prince had lain there in the ditch since the morning; but the place of the blood-pool was still visible in the middle of the road, though scratched and scraped over by passing vehicles. All that was left of Prince was now hoisted into the waggon he had formerly hauled, and with his hoofs in the air, and his shoes shining in the setting sunlight, he retraced the eight or nine miles to Marlott.\n\nTess had gone back earlier. How to break the news was more than she could think. It was a relief to her tongue to find from the faces of her parents that they already knew of their loss, though this did not lessen the self-reproach which she continued to heap upon herself for her negligence.\n\nBut the very shiftlessness of the household rendered the misfortune a less terrifying one to them than it would have been to a thriving family, though in the present case it meant ruin, and in the other it would only have meant inconvenience. In the Durbeyfield countenances there was nothing of the red wrath that would have burnt upon the girl from parents more ambitious for her welfare. Nobody blamed Tess as she blamed herself.\n\nWhen it was discovered that the knacker and tanner would give only a very few shillings for Prince’s carcase because of his decrepitude, Durbeyfield rose to the occasion.\n\n“No,” said he stoically, “I won’t sell his old body. When we d’Urbervilles was knights in the land, we didn’t sell our chargers for cat’s meat. Let ’em keep their shillings! He’ve served me well in his lifetime, and I won’t part from him now.”\n\nHe worked harder the next day in digging a grave for Prince in the garden than he had worked for months to grow a crop for his family. When the hole was ready, Durbeyfield and his wife tied a rope round the horse and dragged him up the path towards it, the children following in funeral train. Abraham and ’Liza-Lu sobbed, Hope and Modesty discharged their griefs in loud blares which echoed from the walls; and when Prince was tumbled in they gathered round the grave. The bread-winner had been taken away from them; what would they do?\n\n“Is he gone to heaven?” asked Abraham, between the sobs.\n\nThen Durbeyfield began to shovel in the earth, and the children cried anew. All except Tess. Her face was dry and pale, as though she regarded herself in the light of a murderess.\n\n# V\n\nThe haggling business, which had mainly depended on the horse, became disorganized forthwith. Distress, if not penury, loomed in the distance. Durbeyfield was what was locally called a slack-twisted fellow; he had good strength to work at times; but the times could not be relied on to coincide with the hours of requirement; and, having been unaccustomed to the regular toil of the day-labourer, he was not particularly persistent when they did so coincide.\n\nTess, meanwhile, as the one who had dragged her parents into this quagmire, was silently wondering what she could do to help them out of it; and then her mother broached her scheme.\n\n“We must take the ups wi’ the downs, Tess,” said she; “and never could your high blood have been found out at a more called-for moment. You must try your friends. Do ye know that there is a very rich Mrs d’Urberville living on the outskirts o’ The Chase, who must be our relation? You must go to her and claim kin, and ask for some help in our trouble.”\n\n“I shouldn’t care to do that,” says Tess. “If there is such a lady, ’twould be enough for us if she were friendly—not to expect her to give us help.”\n\n“You could win her round to do anything, my dear. Besides, perhaps there’s more in it than you know of. I’ve heard what I’ve heard, good-now.”\n\nThe oppressive sense of the harm she had done led Tess to be more deferential than she might otherwise have been to the maternal wish; but she could not understand why her mother should find such satisfaction in contemplating an enterprise of, to her, such doubtful profit. Her mother might have made inquiries, and have discovered that this Mrs d’Urberville was a lady of unequalled virtues and charity. But Tess’s pride made the part of poor relation one of particular distaste to her.\n\n“I’d rather try to get work,” she murmured.\n\n“Durbeyfield, you can settle it,” said his wife, turning to where he sat in the background. “If you say she ought to go, she will go.”\n\n“I don’t like my children going and making themselves beholden to strange kin,” murmured he. “I’m the head of the noblest branch o’ the family, and I ought to live up to it.”\n\nHis reasons for staying away were worse to Tess than her own objections to going. “Well, as I killed the horse, mother,” she said mournfully, “I suppose I ought to do something. I don’t mind going and seeing her, but you must leave it to me about asking for help. And don’t go thinking about her making a match for me—it is silly.”\n\n“Very well said, Tess!” observed her father sententiously.\n\n“Who said I had such a thought?” asked Joan.\n\n“I fancy it is in your mind, mother. But I’ll go.”\n\nRising early next day she walked to the hill-town called Shaston, and there took advantage of a van which twice in the week ran from Shaston eastward to Chaseborough, passing near Trantridge, the parish in which the vague and mysterious Mrs d’Urberville had her residence.\n\nTess Durbeyfield’s route on this memorable morning lay amid the north-eastern undulations of the Vale in which she had been born, and in which her life had unfolded. The Vale of Blackmoor was to her the world, and its inhabitants the races thereof. From the gates and stiles of Marlott she had looked down its length in the wondering days of infancy, and what had been mystery to her then was not much less than mystery to her now. She had seen daily from her chamber-window towers, villages, faint white mansions; above all, the town of Shaston standing majestically on its height; its windows shining like lamps in the evening sun. She had hardly ever visited the place, only a small tract even of the Vale and its environs being known to her by close inspection. Much less had she been far outside the valley. Every contour of the surrounding hills was as personal to her as that of her relatives’ faces; but for what lay beyond, her judgment was dependent on the teaching of the village school, where she had held a leading place at the time of her leaving, a year or two before this date.\n\nIn those early days she had been much loved by others of her own sex and age, and had used to be seen about the village as one of three—all nearly of the same year—walking home from school side by side; Tess the middle one—in a pink print pinafore, of a finely reticulated pattern, worn over a stuff frock that had lost its original colour for a nondescript tertiary—marching on upon long stalky legs, in tight stockings which had little ladder-like holes at the knees, torn by kneeling in the roads and banks in search of vegetable and mineral treasures; her then earth-coloured hair hanging like pot-hooks; the arms of the two outside girls resting round the waist of Tess; her arms on the shoulders of the two supporters.\n\nAs Tess grew older, and began to see how matters stood, she felt quite a Malthusian towards her mother for thoughtlessly giving her so many little sisters and brothers, when it was such a trouble to nurse and provide for them. Her mother’s intelligence was that of a happy child: Joan Durbeyfield was simply an additional one, and that not the eldest, to her own long family of waiters on Providence.\n\nHowever, Tess became humanely beneficent towards the small ones, and to help them as much as possible she used, as soon as she left school, to lend a hand at haymaking or harvesting on neighbouring farms; or, by preference, at milking or butter-making processes, which she had learnt when her father had owned cows; and being deft-fingered it was a kind of work in which she excelled.\n\nEvery day seemed to throw upon her young shoulders more of the family burdens, and that Tess should be the representative of the Durbeyfields at the d’Urberville mansion came as a thing of course. In this instance it must be admitted that the Durbeyfields were putting their fairest side outward.\n\nShe alighted from the van at Trantridge Cross, and ascended on foot a hill in the direction of the district known as The Chase, on the borders of which, as she had been informed, Mrs d’Urberville’s seat, The Slopes, would be found. It was not a manorial home in the ordinary sense, with fields, and pastures, and a grumbling farmer, out of whom the owner had to squeeze an income for himself and his family by hook or by crook. It was more, far more; a country-house built for enjoyment pure and simple, with not an acre of troublesome land attached to it beyond what was required for residential purposes, and for a little fancy farm kept in hand by the owner, and tended by a bailiff.\n\nThe crimson brick lodge came first in sight, up to its eaves in dense evergreens. Tess thought this was the mansion itself till, passing through the side wicket with some trepidation, and onward to a point at which the drive took a turn, the house proper stood in full view. It was of recent erection—indeed almost new—and of the same rich red colour that formed such a contrast with the evergreens of the lodge. Far behind the corner of the house—which rose like a geranium bloom against the subdued colours around—stretched the soft azure landscape of The Chase—a truly venerable tract of forest land, one of the few remaining woodlands in England of undoubted primaeval date, wherein Druidical mistletoe was still found on aged oaks, and where enormous yew-trees, not planted by the hand of man grew as they had grown when they were pollarded for bows. All this sylvan antiquity, however, though visible from The Slopes, was outside the immediate boundaries of the estate.\n\nEverything on this snug property was bright, thriving, and well kept; acres of glass-houses stretched down the inclines to the copses at their feet. Everything looked like money—like the last coin issued from the Mint. The stables, partly screened by Austrian pines and evergreen oaks, and fitted with every late appliance, were as dignified as Chapels-of-Ease. On the extensive lawn stood an ornamental tent, its door being towards her.\n\nSimple Tess Durbeyfield stood at gaze, in a half-alarmed attitude, on the edge of the gravel sweep. Her feet had brought her onward to this point before she had quite realized where she was; and now all was contrary to her expectation.\n\n“I thought we were an old family; but this is all new!” she said, in her artlessness. She wished that she had not fallen in so readily with her mother’s plans for “claiming kin,” and had endeavoured to gain assistance nearer home.\n\nThe d’Urbervilles—or Stoke-d’Urbervilles, as they at first called themselves—who owned all this, were a somewhat unusual family to find in such an old-fashioned part of the country. Parson Tringham had spoken truly when he said that our shambling John Durbeyfield was the only really lineal representative of the old d’Urberville family existing in the county, or near it; he might have added, what he knew very well, that the Stoke-d’Urbervilles were no more d’Urbervilles of the true tree then he was himself. Yet it must be admitted that this family formed a very good stock whereon to regraft a name which sadly wanted such renovation.\n\nWhen old Mr Simon Stoke, latterly deceased, had made his fortune as an honest merchant (some said money-lender) in the North, he decided to settle as a county man in the South of England, out of hail of his business district; and in doing this he felt the necessity of recommencing with a name that would not too readily identify him with the smart tradesman of the past, and that would be less commonplace than the original bald, stark words. Conning for an hour in the British Museum the pages of works devoted to extinct, half-extinct, obscured, and ruined families appertaining to the quarter of England in which he proposed to settle, he considered that _d’Urberville_ looked and sounded as well as any of them: and d’Urberville accordingly was annexed to his own name for himself and his heirs eternally. Yet he was not an extravagant-minded man in this, and in constructing his family tree on the new basis was duly reasonable in framing his inter-marriages and aristocratic links, never inserting a single title above a rank of strict moderation.\n\nOf this work of imagination poor Tess and her parents were naturally in ignorance—much to their discomfiture; indeed, the very possibility of such annexations was unknown to them; who supposed that, though to be well-favoured might be the gift of fortune, a family name came by nature.\n\nTess still stood hesitating like a bather about to make his plunge, hardly knowing whether to retreat or to persevere, when a figure came forth from the dark triangular door of the tent. It was that of a tall young man, smoking.\n\nHe had an almost swarthy complexion, with full lips, badly moulded, though red and smooth, above which was a well-groomed black moustache with curled points, though his age could not be more than three- or four-and-twenty. Despite the touches of barbarism in his contours, there was a singular force in the gentleman’s face, and in his bold rolling eye.\n\n“Well, my Beauty, what can I do for you?” said he, coming forward. And perceiving that she stood quite confounded: “Never mind me. I am Mr d’Urberville. Have you come to see me or my mother?”\n\nThis embodiment of a d’Urberville and a namesake differed even more from what Tess had expected than the house and grounds had differed. She had dreamed of an aged and dignified face, the sublimation of all the d’Urberville lineaments, furrowed with incarnate memories representing in hieroglyphic the centuries of her family’s and England’s history. But she screwed herself up to the work in hand, since she could not get out of it, and answered—\n\n“I came to see your mother, sir.”\n\n“I am afraid you cannot see her—she is an invalid,” replied the present representative of the spurious house; for this was Mr Alec, the only son of the lately deceased gentleman. “Cannot I answer your purpose? What is the business you wish to see her about?”\n\n“It isn’t business—it is—I can hardly say what!”\n\n“Pleasure?”\n\n“Oh no. Why, sir, if I tell you, it will seem—”\n\nTess’s sense of a certain ludicrousness in her errand was now so strong that, notwithstanding her awe of him, and her general discomfort at being here, her rosy lips curved towards a smile, much to the attraction of the swarthy Alexander.\n\n“It is so very foolish,” she stammered; “I fear I can’t tell you!”\n\n“Never mind; I like foolish things. Try again, my dear,” said he kindly.\n\n“Mother asked me to come,” Tess continued; “and, indeed, I was in the mind to do so myself likewise. But I did not think it would be like this. I came, sir, to tell you that we are of the same family as you.”\n\n“Ho! Poor relations?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Stokes?”\n\n“No; d’Urbervilles.”\n\n“Ay, ay; I mean d’Urbervilles.”\n\n“Our names are worn away to Durbeyfield; but we have several proofs that we are d’Urbervilles. Antiquarians hold we are,—and—and we have an old seal, marked with a ramping lion on a shield, and a castle over him. And we have a very old silver spoon, round in the bowl like a little ladle, and marked with the same castle. But it is so worn that mother uses it to stir the pea-soup.”\n\n“A castle argent is certainly my crest,” said he blandly. “And my arms a lion rampant.”\n\n“And so mother said we ought to make ourselves beknown to you—as we’ve lost our horse by a bad accident, and are the oldest branch o’ the family.”\n\n“Very kind of your mother, I’m sure. And I, for one, don’t regret her step.” Alec looked at Tess as he spoke, in a way that made her blush a little. “And so, my pretty girl, you’ve come on a friendly visit to us, as relations?”\n\n“I suppose I have,” faltered Tess, looking uncomfortable again.\n\n“Well—there’s no harm in it. Where do you live? What are you?”\n\nShe gave him brief particulars; and responding to further inquiries told him that she was intending to go back by the same carrier who had brought her.\n\n“It is a long while before he returns past Trantridge Cross. Supposing we walk round the grounds to pass the time, my pretty Coz?”\n\nTess wished to abridge her visit as much as possible; but the young man was pressing, and she consented to accompany him. He conducted her about the lawns, and flower-beds, and conservatories; and thence to the fruit-garden and greenhouses, where he asked her if she liked strawberries.\n\n“Yes,” said Tess, “when they come.”\n\n“They are already here.” D’Urberville began gathering specimens of the fruit for her, handing them back to her as he stooped; and, presently, selecting a specially fine product of the “British Queen” variety, he stood up and held it by the stem to her mouth.\n\n“No—no!” she said quickly, putting her fingers between his hand and her lips. “I would rather take it in my own hand.”\n\n“Nonsense!” he insisted; and in a slight distress she parted her lips and took it in.\n\nThey had spent some time wandering desultorily thus, Tess eating in a half-pleased, half-reluctant state whatever d’Urberville offered her. When she could consume no more of the strawberries he filled her little basket with them; and then the two passed round to the rose-trees, whence he gathered blossoms and gave her to put in her bosom. She obeyed like one in a dream, and when she could affix no more he himself tucked a bud or two into her hat, and heaped her basket with others in the prodigality of his bounty. At last, looking at his watch, he said, “Now, by the time you have had something to eat, it will be time for you to leave, if you want to catch the carrier to Shaston. Come here, and I’ll see what grub I can find.”\n\nStoke d’Urberville took her back to the lawn and into the tent, where he left her, soon reappearing with a basket of light luncheon, which he put before her himself. It was evidently the gentleman’s wish not to be disturbed in this pleasant _tête-à-tête_ by the servantry.\n\n“Do you mind my smoking?” he asked.\n\n“Oh, not at all, sir.”\n\nHe watched her pretty and unconscious munching through the skeins of smoke that pervaded the tent, and Tess Durbeyfield did not divine, as she innocently looked down at the roses in her bosom, that there behind the blue narcotic haze was potentially the “tragic mischief” of her drama—one who stood fair to be the blood-red ray in the spectrum of her young life. She had an attribute which amounted to a disadvantage just now; and it was this that caused Alec d’Urberville’s eyes to rivet themselves upon her. It was a luxuriance of aspect, a fulness of growth, which made her appear more of a woman than she really was. She had inherited the feature from her mother without the quality it denoted. It had troubled her mind occasionally, till her companions had said that it was a fault which time would cure.\n\nShe soon had finished her lunch. “Now I am going home, sir,” she said, rising.\n\n“And what do they call you?” he asked, as he accompanied her along the drive till they were out of sight of the house.\n\n“Tess Durbeyfield, down at Marlott.”\n\n“And you say your people have lost their horse?”\n\n“I—killed him!” she answered, her eyes filling with tears as she gave particulars of Prince’s death. “And I don’t know what to do for father on account of it!”\n\n“I must think if I cannot do something. My mother must find a berth for you. But, Tess, no nonsense about ‘d’Urberville’;—‘Durbeyfield’ only, you know—quite another name.”\n\n“I wish for no better, sir,” said she with something of dignity.\n\nFor a moment—only for a moment—when they were in the turning of the drive, between the tall rhododendrons and conifers, before the lodge became visible, he inclined his face towards her as if—but, no: he thought better of it, and let her go.\n\nThus the thing began. Had she perceived this meeting’s import she might have asked why she was doomed to be seen and coveted that day by the wrong man, and not by some other man, the right and desired one in all respects—as nearly as humanity can supply the right and desired; yet to him who amongst her acquaintance might have approximated to this kind, she was but a transient impression, half forgotten.\n\nIn the ill-judged execution of the well-judged plan of things the call seldom produces the comer, the man to love rarely coincides with the hour for loving. Nature does not often say “See!” to her poor creature at a time when seeing can lead to happy doing; or reply “Here!” to a body’s cry of “Where?” till the hide-and-seek has become an irksome, outworn game. We may wonder whether at the acme and summit of the human progress these anachronisms will be corrected by a finer intuition, a closer interaction of the social machinery than that which now jolts us round and along; but such completeness is not to be prophesied, or even conceived as possible. Enough that in the present case, as in millions, it was not the two halves of a perfect whole that confronted each other at the perfect moment; a missing counterpart wandered independently about the earth waiting in crass obtuseness till the late time came. Out of which maladroit delay sprang anxieties, disappointments, shocks, catastrophes, and passing-strange destinies.\n\nWhen d’Urberville got back to the tent he sat down astride on a chair, reflecting, with a pleased gleam in his face. Then he broke into a loud laugh.\n\n“Well, I’m damned! What a funny thing! Ha-ha-ha! And what a crumby girl!”\n\n# VI\n\nTess went down the hill to Trantridge Cross, and inattentively waited to take her seat in the van returning from Chaseborough to Shaston. She did not know what the other occupants said to her as she entered, though she answered them; and when they had started anew she rode along with an inward and not an outward eye.\n\nOne among her fellow-travellers addressed her more pointedly than any had spoken before: “Why, you be quite a posy! And such roses in early June!”\n\nThen she became aware of the spectacle she presented to their surprised vision: roses at her breasts; roses in her hat; roses and strawberries in her basket to the brim. She blushed, and said confusedly that the flowers had been given to her. When the passengers were not looking she stealthily removed the more prominent blooms from her hat and placed them in the basket, where she covered them with her handkerchief. Then she fell to reflecting again, and in looking downwards a thorn of the rose remaining in her breast accidentally pricked her chin. Like all the cottagers in Blackmoor Vale, Tess was steeped in fancies and prefigurative superstitions; she thought this an ill omen—the first she had noticed that day.\n\nThe van travelled only so far as Shaston, and there were several miles of pedestrian descent from that mountain-town into the vale to Marlott. Her mother had advised her to stay here for the night, at the house of a cottage-woman they knew, if she should feel too tired to come on; and this Tess did, not descending to her home till the following afternoon.\nWhen she entered the house she perceived in a moment from her mother’s triumphant manner that something had occurred in the interim.\n\n“Oh yes; I know all about it! I told ’ee it would be all right, and now ’tis proved!”\n\n“Since I’ve been away? What has?” said Tess rather wearily.\n\nHer mother surveyed the girl up and down with arch approval, and went on banteringly: “So you’ve brought ’em round!”\n\n“How do you know, mother?”\n\n“I’ve had a letter.”\n\nTess then remembered that there would have been time for this.\n\n“They say—Mrs d’Urberville says—that she wants you to look after a little fowl-farm which is her hobby. But this is only her artful way of getting ’ee there without raising your hopes. She’s going to own ’ee as kin—that’s the meaning o’t.”\n\n“But I didn’t see her.”\n\n“You zid somebody, I suppose?”\n\n“I saw her son.”\n“And did he own ’ee?”\n\n“Well—he called me Coz.”\n\n“An’ I knew it! Jacky—he called her Coz!” cried Joan to her husband. “Well, he spoke to his mother, of course, and she do want ’ee there.”\n\n“But I don’t know that I am apt at tending fowls,” said the dubious Tess.\n\n“Then I don’t know who is apt. You’ve be’n born in the business, and brought up in it. They that be born in a business always know more about it than any ’prentice. Besides, that’s only just a show of something for you to do, that you midn’t feel beholden.”\n\n“I don’t altogether think I ought to go,” said Tess thoughtfully. “Who wrote the letter? Will you let me look at it?”\n\n“Mrs d’Urberville wrote it. Here it is.”\n\nThe letter was in the third person, and briefly informed Mrs Durbeyfield that her daughter’s services would be useful to that lady in the management of her poultry-farm, that a comfortable room would be provided for her if she could come, and that the wages would be on a liberal scale if they liked her.\n\n“Oh—that’s all!” said Tess.\n\n“You couldn’t expect her to throw her arms round ’ee, an’ to kiss and to coll ’ee all at once.”\n\nTess looked out of the window.\n\n“I would rather stay here with father and you,” she said.\n\n“But why?”\n\n“I’d rather not tell you why, mother; indeed, I don’t quite know why.”\n\nA week afterwards she came in one evening from an unavailing search for some light occupation in the immediate neighbourhood. Her idea had been to get together sufficient money during the summer to purchase another horse. Hardly had she crossed the threshold before one of the children danced across the room, saying, “The gentleman’s been here!”\n\nHer mother hastened to explain, smiles breaking from every inch of her person. Mrs d’Urberville’s son had called on horseback, having been riding by chance in the direction of Marlott. He had wished to know, finally, in the name of his mother, if Tess could really come to manage the old lady’s fowl-farm or not; the lad who had hitherto superintended the birds having proved untrustworthy. “Mr d’Urberville says you must be a good girl if you are at all as you appear; he knows you must be worth your weight in gold. He is very much interested in ’ee—truth to tell.”\n\nTess seemed for the moment really pleased to hear that she had won such high opinion from a stranger when, in her own esteem, she had sunk so low.\n\n“It is very good of him to think that,” she murmured; “and if I was quite sure how it would be living there, I would go any-when.”\n\n“He is a mighty handsome man!”\n\n“I don’t think so,” said Tess coldly.\n\n“Well, there’s your chance, whether or no; and I’m sure he wears a beautiful diamond ring!”\n\n“Yes,” said little Abraham, brightly, from the window-bench; “and I seed it! and it did twinkle when he put his hand up to his mistarshers. Mother, why did our grand relation keep on putting his hand up to his mistarshers?”\n\n“Hark at that child!” cried Mrs Durbeyfield, with parenthetic admiration.\n\n“Perhaps to show his diamond ring,” murmured Sir John, dreamily, from his chair.\n\n“I’ll think it over,” said Tess, leaving the room.\n\n“Well, she’s made a conquest o’ the younger branch of us, straight off,” continued the matron to her husband, “and she’s a fool if she don’t follow it up.”\n\n“I don’t quite like my children going away from home,” said the haggler. “As the head of the family, the rest ought to come to me.”\n\n“But do let her go, Jacky,” coaxed his poor witless wife. “He’s struck wi’ her—you can see that. He called her Coz! He’ll marry her, most likely, and make a lady of her; and then she’ll be what her forefathers was.”\n\nJohn Durbeyfield had more conceit than energy or health, and this supposition was pleasant to him.\n\n“Well, perhaps that’s what young Mr d’Urberville means,” he admitted; “and sure enough he mid have serious thoughts about improving his blood by linking on to the old line. Tess, the little rogue! And have she really paid ’em a visit to such an end as this?”\n\nMeanwhile Tess was walking thoughtfully among the gooseberry-bushes in the garden, and over Prince’s grave. When she came in her mother pursued her advantage.\n\n“Well, what be you going to do?” she asked.\n\n“I wish I had seen Mrs d’Urberville,” said Tess.\n\n“I think you mid as well settle it. Then you’ll see her soon enough.”\n\nHer father coughed in his chair.\n\n“I don’t know what to say!” answered the girl restlessly. “It is for you to decide. I killed the old horse, and I suppose I ought to do something to get ye a new one. But—but—I don’t quite like Mr d’Urberville being there!”\n\nThe children, who had made use of this idea of Tess being taken up by their wealthy kinsfolk (which they imagined the other family to be) as a species of dolorifuge after the death of the horse, began to cry at Tess’s reluctance, and teased and reproached her for hesitating.\n\n“Tess won’t go-o-o and be made a la-a-dy of!—no, she says she wo-o-on’t!” they wailed, with square mouths. “And we shan’t have a nice new horse, and lots o’ golden money to buy fairlings! And Tess won’t look pretty in her best cloze no mo-o-ore!”\n\nHer mother chimed in to the same tune: a certain way she had of making her labours in the house seem heavier than they were by prolonging them indefinitely, also weighed in the argument. Her father alone preserved an attitude of neutrality.\n\n“I will go,” said Tess at last.\n\nHer mother could not repress her consciousness of the nuptial vision conjured up by the girl’s consent.\n\n“That’s right! For such a pretty maid as ’tis, this is a fine chance!”\n\nTess smiled crossly.\n\n“I hope it is a chance for earning money. It is no other kind of chance. You had better say nothing of that silly sort about parish.”\n\nMrs Durbeyfield did not promise. She was not quite sure that she did not feel proud enough, after the visitor’s remarks, to say a good deal.\nThus it was arranged; and the young girl wrote, agreeing to be ready to set out on any day on which she might be required. She was duly informed that Mrs d’Urberville was glad of her decision, and that a spring-cart should be sent to meet her and her luggage at the top of the Vale on the day after the morrow, when she must hold herself prepared to start. Mrs d’Urberville’s handwriting seemed rather masculine.\n\n“A cart?” murmured Joan Durbeyfield doubtingly. “It might have been a carriage for her own kin!”\n\nHaving at last taken her course Tess was less restless and abstracted, going about her business with some self-assurance in the thought of acquiring another horse for her father by an occupation which would not be onerous. She had hoped to be a teacher at the school, but the fates seemed to decide otherwise. Being mentally older than her mother she did not regard Mrs Durbeyfield’s matrimonial hopes for her in a serious aspect for a moment. The light-minded woman had been discovering good matches for her daughter almost from the year of her birth.\n\n# VII\n\nOn the morning appointed for her departure Tess was awake before dawn—at the marginal minute of the dark when the grove is still mute, save for one prophetic bird who sings with a clear-voiced conviction that he at least knows the correct time of day, the rest preserving silence as if equally convinced that he is mistaken. She remained upstairs packing till breakfast-time, and then came down in her ordinary week-day clothes, her Sunday apparel being carefully folded in her box.\n\nHer mother expostulated. “You will never set out to see your folks without dressing up more the dand than that?”\n\n“But I am going to work!” said Tess.\n\n“Well, yes,” said Mrs Durbeyfield; and in a private tone, “at first there mid be a little pretence o’t.... But I think it will be wiser of ’ee to put your best side outward,” she added.\n\n“Very well; I suppose you know best,” replied Tess with calm abandonment.\n\nAnd to please her parent the girl put herself quite in Joan’s hands, saying serenely—“Do what you like with me, mother.”\n\nMrs Durbeyfield was only too delighted at this tractability. First she fetched a great basin, and washed Tess’s hair with such thoroughness that when dried and brushed it looked twice as much as at other times. She tied it with a broader pink ribbon than usual. Then she put upon her the white frock that Tess had worn at the club-walking, the airy fulness of which, supplementing her enlarged _coiffure_, imparted to her developing figure an amplitude which belied her age, and might cause her to be estimated as a woman when she was not much more than a child.\n\n“I declare there’s a hole in my stocking-heel!” said Tess.\n\n“Never mind holes in your stockings—they don’t speak! When I was a maid, so long as I had a pretty bonnet the devil might ha’ found me in heels.”\n\nHer mother’s pride in the girl’s appearance led her to step back, like a painter from his easel, and survey her work as a whole.\n\n“You must zee yourself!” she cried. “It is much better than you was t’other day.”\n\nAs the looking-glass was only large enough to reflect a very small portion of Tess’s person at one time, Mrs Durbeyfield hung a black cloak outside the casement, and so made a large reflector of the panes, as it is the wont of bedecking cottagers to do. After this she went downstairs to her husband, who was sitting in the lower room.\n\n“I’ll tell ’ee what ’tis, Durbeyfield,” said she exultingly; “he’ll never have the heart not to love her. But whatever you do, don’t zay too much to Tess of his fancy for her, and this chance she has got. She is such an odd maid that it mid zet her against him, or against going there, even now. If all goes well, I shall certainly be for making some return to pa’son at Stagfoot Lane for telling us—dear, good man!”\n\nHowever, as the moment for the girl’s setting out drew nigh, when the first excitement of the dressing had passed off, a slight misgiving found place in Joan Durbeyfield’s mind. It prompted the matron to say that she would walk a little way—as far as to the point where the acclivity from the valley began its first steep ascent to the outer world. At the top Tess was going to be met with the spring-cart sent by the Stoke-d’Urbervilles, and her box had already been wheeled ahead towards this summit by a lad with trucks, to be in readiness.\n\nSeeing their mother put on her bonnet, the younger children clamoured to go with her.\n\n“I do want to walk a little-ways wi’ Sissy, now she’s going to marry our gentleman-cousin, and wear fine cloze!”\n\n“Now,” said Tess, flushing and turning quickly, “I’ll hear no more o’ that! Mother, how could you ever put such stuff into their heads?”\n\n“Going to work, my dears, for our rich relation, and help get enough money for a new horse,” said Mrs Durbeyfield pacifically.\n\n“Goodbye, father,” said Tess, with a lumpy throat.\n\n“Goodbye, my maid,” said Sir John, raising his head from his breast as he suspended his nap, induced by a slight excess this morning in honour of the occasion. “Well, I hope my young friend will like such a comely sample of his own blood. And tell’n, Tess, that being sunk, quite, from our former grandeur, I’ll sell him the title—yes, sell it—and at no onreasonable figure.”\n\n“Not for less than a thousand pound!” cried Lady Durbeyfield.\n\n“Tell’n—I’ll take a thousand pound. Well, I’ll take less, when I come to think o’t. He’ll adorn it better than a poor lammicken feller like myself can. Tell’n he shall hae it for a hundred. But I won’t stand upon trifles—tell’n he shall hae it for fifty—for twenty pound! Yes, twenty pound—that’s the lowest. Dammy, family honour is family honour, and I won’t take a penny less!”\n\nTess’s eyes were too full and her voice too choked to utter the sentiments that were in her. She turned quickly, and went out.\n\nSo the girls and their mother all walked together, a child on each side of Tess, holding her hand and looking at her meditatively from time to time, as at one who was about to do great things; her mother just behind with the smallest; the group forming a picture of honest beauty flanked by innocence, and backed by simple-souled vanity. They followed the way till they reached the beginning of the ascent, on the crest of which the vehicle from Trantridge was to receive her, this limit having been fixed to save the horse the labour of the last slope. Far away behind the first hills the cliff-like dwellings of Shaston broke the line of the ridge. Nobody was visible in the elevated road which skirted the ascent save the lad whom they had sent on before them, sitting on the handle of the barrow that contained all Tess’s worldly possessions.\n\n“Bide here a bit, and the cart will soon come, no doubt,” said Mrs Durbeyfield. “Yes, I see it yonder!”\n\nIt had come—appearing suddenly from behind the forehead of the nearest upland, and stopping beside the boy with the barrow. Her mother and the children thereupon decided to go no farther, and bidding them a hasty goodbye, Tess bent her steps up the hill.\n\nThey saw her white shape draw near to the spring-cart, on which her box was already placed. But before she had quite reached it another vehicle shot out from a clump of trees on the summit, came round the bend of the road there, passed the luggage-cart, and halted beside Tess, who looked up as if in great surprise.\n\nHer mother perceived, for the first time, that the second vehicle was not a humble conveyance like the first, but a spick-and-span gig or dog-cart, highly varnished and equipped. The driver was a young man of three- or four-and-twenty, with a cigar between his teeth; wearing a dandy cap, drab jacket, breeches of the same hue, white neckcloth, stick-up collar, and brown driving-gloves—in short, he was the handsome, horsey young buck who had visited Joan a week or two before to get her answer about Tess.\n\nMrs Durbeyfield clapped her hands like a child. Then she looked down, then stared again. Could she be deceived as to the meaning of this?\n\n“Is dat the gentleman-kinsman who’ll make Sissy a lady?” asked the youngest child.\n\nMeanwhile the muslined form of Tess could be seen standing still, undecided, beside this turn-out, whose owner was talking to her. Her seeming indecision was, in fact, more than indecision: it was misgiving. She would have preferred the humble cart. The young man dismounted, and appeared to urge her to ascend. She turned her face down the hill to her relatives, and regarded the little group. Something seemed to quicken her to a determination; possibly the thought that she had killed Prince. She suddenly stepped up; he mounted beside her, and immediately whipped on the horse. In a moment they had passed the slow cart with the box, and disappeared behind the shoulder of the hill.\n\nDirectly Tess was out of sight, and the interest of the matter as a drama was at an end, the little ones’ eyes filled with tears. The youngest child said, “I wish poor, poor Tess wasn’t gone away to be a lady!” and, lowering the corners of his lips, burst out crying. The new point of view was infectious, and the next child did likewise, and then the next, till the whole three of them wailed loud.\n\nThere were tears also in Joan Durbeyfield’s eyes as she turned to go home. But by the time she had got back to the village she was passively trusting to the favour of accident. However, in bed that night she sighed, and her husband asked her what was the matter.\n\n“Oh, I don’t know exactly,” she said. “I was thinking that perhaps it would ha’ been better if Tess had not gone.”\n\n“Oughtn’t ye to have thought of that before?”\n\n“Well, ’tis a chance for the maid—Still, if ’twere the doing again, I wouldn’t let her go till I had found out whether the gentleman is really a good-hearted young man and choice over her as his kinswoman.”\n\n“Yes, you ought, perhaps, to ha’ done that,” snored Sir John.\n\nJoan Durbeyfield always managed to find consolation somewhere: “Well, as one of the genuine stock, she ought to make her way with ’en, if she plays her trump card aright. And if he don’t marry her afore he will after. For that he’s all afire wi’ love for her any eye can see.”\n\n“What’s her trump card? Her d’Urberville blood, you mean?”\n\n“No, stupid; her face—as ’twas mine.”\n\n# VIII\n\nHaving mounted beside her, Alec d’Urberville drove rapidly along the crest of the first hill, chatting compliments to Tess as they went, the cart with her box being left far behind. Rising still, an immense landscape stretched around them on every side; behind, the green valley of her birth, before, a gray country of which she knew nothing except from her first brief visit to Trantridge. Thus they reached the verge of an incline down which the road stretched in a long straight descent of nearly a mile.\n\nEver since the accident with her father’s horse Tess Durbeyfield, courageous as she naturally was, had been exceedingly timid on wheels; the least irregularity of motion startled her. She began to get uneasy at a certain recklessness in her conductor’s driving.\n\n“You will go down slow, sir, I suppose?” she said with attempted unconcern.\n\nD’Urberville looked round upon her, nipped his cigar with the tips of his large white centre-teeth, and allowed his lips to smile slowly of themselves.\n\n“Why, Tess,” he answered, after another whiff or two, “it isn’t a brave bouncing girl like you who asks that? Why, I always go down at full gallop. There’s nothing like it for raising your spirits.”\n\n“But perhaps you need not now?”\n\n“Ah,” he said, shaking his head, “there are two to be reckoned with. It is not me alone. Tib has to be considered, and she has a very queer temper.”\n\n“Who?”\n\n“Why, this mare. I fancy she looked round at me in a very grim way just then. Didn’t you notice it?”\n\n“Don’t try to frighten me, sir,” said Tess stiffly.\n\n“Well, I don’t. If any living man can manage this horse I can: I won’t say any living man can do it—but if such has the power, I am he.”\n\n“Why do you have such a horse?”\n\n“Ah, well may you ask it! It was my fate, I suppose. Tib has killed one chap; and just after I bought her she nearly killed me. And then, take my word for it, I nearly killed her. But she’s touchy still, very touchy; and one’s life is hardly safe behind her sometimes.”\n\nThey were just beginning to descend; and it was evident that the horse, whether of her own will or of his (the latter being the more likely), knew so well the reckless performance expected of her that she hardly required a hint from behind.\n\nDown, down, they sped, the wheels humming like a top, the dog-cart rocking right and left, its axis acquiring a slightly oblique set in relation to the line of progress; the figure of the horse rising and falling in undulations before them. Sometimes a wheel was off the ground, it seemed, for many yards; sometimes a stone was sent spinning over the hedge, and flinty sparks from the horse’s hoofs outshone the daylight. The aspect of the straight road enlarged with their advance, the two banks dividing like a splitting stick; one rushing past at each shoulder.\n\nThe wind blew through Tess’s white muslin to her very skin, and her washed hair flew out behind. She was determined to show no open fear, but she clutched d’Urberville’s rein-arm.\n\n“Don’t touch my arm! We shall be thrown out if you do! Hold on round my waist!”\n\nShe grasped his waist, and so they reached the bottom.\n\n“Safe, thank God, in spite of your fooling!” said she, her face on fire.\n\n“Tess—fie! that’s temper!” said d’Urberville.\n\n“’Tis truth.”\n\n“Well, you need not let go your hold of me so thanklessly the moment you feel yourself out of danger.”\n\nShe had not considered what she had been doing; whether he were man or woman, stick or stone, in her involuntary hold on him. Recovering her reserve, she sat without replying, and thus they reached the summit of another declivity.\n\n“Now then, again!” said d’Urberville.\n\n“No, no!” said Tess. “Show more sense, do, please.”\n\n“But when people find themselves on one of the highest points in the county, they must get down again,” he retorted.\n\nHe loosened rein, and away they went a second time. D’Urberville turned his face to her as they rocked, and said, in playful raillery: “Now then, put your arms round my waist again, as you did before, my Beauty.”\n\n“Never!” said Tess independently, holding on as well as she could without touching him.\n\n“Let me put one little kiss on those holmberry lips, Tess, or even on that warmed cheek, and I’ll stop—on my honour, I will!”\n\nTess, surprised beyond measure, slid farther back still on her seat, at which he urged the horse anew, and rocked her the more.\n\n“Will nothing else do?” she cried at length, in desperation, her large eyes staring at him like those of a wild animal. This dressing her up so prettily by her mother had apparently been to lamentable purpose.\n\n“Nothing, dear Tess,” he replied.\n\n“Oh, I don’t know—very well; I don’t mind!” she panted miserably.\n\nHe drew rein, and as they slowed he was on the point of imprinting the desired salute, when, as if hardly yet aware of her own modesty, she dodged aside. His arms being occupied with the reins there was left him no power to prevent her manœuvre.\n\n“Now, damn it—I’ll break both our necks!” swore her capriciously passionate companion. “So you can go from your word like that, you young witch, can you?”\n\n“Very well,” said Tess, “I’ll not move since you be so determined! But I—thought you would be kind to me, and protect me, as my kinsman!”\n\n“Kinsman be hanged! Now!”\n\n“But I don’t want anybody to kiss me, sir!” she implored, a big tear beginning to roll down her face, and the corners of her mouth trembling in her attempts not to cry. “And I wouldn’t ha’ come if I had known!”\n\nHe was inexorable, and she sat still, and d’Urberville gave her the kiss of mastery. No sooner had he done so than she flushed with shame, took out her handkerchief, and wiped the spot on her cheek that had been touched by his lips. His ardour was nettled at the sight, for the act on her part had been unconsciously done.\n\n“You are mighty sensitive for a cottage girl!” said the young man.\n\nTess made no reply to this remark, of which, indeed, she did not quite comprehend the drift, unheeding the snub she had administered by her instinctive rub upon her cheek. She had, in fact, undone the kiss, as far as such a thing was physically possible. With a dim sense that he was vexed she looked steadily ahead as they trotted on near Melbury Down and Wingreen, till she saw, to her consternation, that there was yet another descent to be undergone.\n\n“You shall be made sorry for that!” he resumed, his injured tone still remaining, as he flourished the whip anew. “Unless, that is, you agree willingly to let me do it again, and no handkerchief.”\n\nShe sighed. “Very well, sir!” she said. “Oh—let me get my hat!”\n\nAt the moment of speaking her hat had blown off into the road, their present speed on the upland being by no means slow. D’Urberville pulled up, and said he would get it for her, but Tess was down on the other side.\n\nShe turned back and picked up the article.\n\n“You look prettier with it off, upon my soul, if that’s possible,” he said, contemplating her over the back of the vehicle. “Now then, up again! What’s the matter?”\n\nThe hat was in place and tied, but Tess had not stepped forward.\n\n“No, sir,” she said, revealing the red and ivory of her mouth as her eye lit in defiant triumph; “not again, if I know it!”\n\n“What—you won’t get up beside me?”\n\n“No; I shall walk.”\n\n“’Tis five or six miles yet to Trantridge.”\n\n“I don’t care if ’tis dozens. Besides, the cart is behind.”\n\n“You artful hussy! Now, tell me—didn’t you make that hat blow off on purpose? I’ll swear you did!”\n\nHer strategic silence confirmed his suspicion.\n\nThen d’Urberville cursed and swore at her, and called her everything he could think of for the trick. Turning the horse suddenly he tried to drive back upon her, and so hem her in between the gig and the hedge. But he could not do this short of injuring her.\n\n“You ought to be ashamed of yourself for using such wicked words!” cried Tess with spirit, from the top of the hedge into which she had scrambled. “I don’t like ’ee at all! I hate and detest you! I’ll go back to mother, I will!”\n\nD’Urberville’s bad temper cleared up at sight of hers; and he laughed heartily.\n\n“Well, I like you all the better,” he said. “Come, let there be peace. I’ll never do it any more against your will. My life upon it now!”\n\nStill Tess could not be induced to remount. She did not, however, object to his keeping his gig alongside her; and in this manner, at a slow pace, they advanced towards the village of Trantridge. From time to time d’Urberville exhibited a sort of fierce distress at the sight of the tramping he had driven her to undertake by his misdemeanour. She might in truth have safely trusted him now; but he had forfeited her confidence for the time, and she kept on the ground progressing thoughtfully, as if wondering whether it would be wiser to return home. Her resolve, however, had been taken, and it seemed vacillating even to childishness to abandon it now, unless for graver reasons. How could she face her parents, get back her box, and disconcert the whole scheme for the rehabilitation of her family on such sentimental grounds?\n\nA few minutes later the chimneys of The Slopes appeared in view, and in a snug nook to the right the poultry-farm and cottage of Tess’s destination.\n\n# IX\n\nThe community of fowls to which Tess had been appointed as supervisor, purveyor, nurse, surgeon, and friend made its headquarters in an old thatched cottage standing in an enclosure that had once been a garden, but was now a trampled and sanded square. The house was overrun with ivy, its chimney being enlarged by the boughs of the parasite to the aspect of a ruined tower. The lower rooms were entirely given over to the birds, who walked about them with a proprietary air, as though the place had been built by themselves, and not by certain dusty copyholders who now lay east and west in the churchyard. The descendants of these bygone owners felt it almost as a slight to their family when the house which had so much of their affection, had cost so much of their forefathers’ money, and had been in their possession for several generations before the d’Urbervilles came and built here, was indifferently turned into a fowl-house by Mrs Stoke-d’Urberville as soon as the property fell into hand according to law. “’Twas good enough for Christians in grandfather’s time,” they said.\n\nThe rooms wherein dozens of infants had wailed at their nursing now resounded with the tapping of nascent chicks. Distracted hens in coops occupied spots where formerly stood chairs supporting sedate agriculturists. The chimney-corner and once-blazing hearth was now filled with inverted beehives, in which the hens laid their eggs; while out of doors the plots that each succeeding householder had carefully shaped with his spade were torn by the cocks in wildest fashion.\n\nThe garden in which the cottage stood was surrounded by a wall, and could only be entered through a door.\n\nWhen Tess had occupied herself about an hour the next morning in altering and improving the arrangements, according to her skilled ideas as the daughter of a professed poulterer, the door in the wall opened and a servant in white cap and apron entered. She had come from the manor-house.\n\n“Mrs d’Urberville wants the fowls as usual,” she said; but perceiving that Tess did not quite understand, she explained, “Mis’ess is a old lady, and blind.”\n“Blind!” said Tess.\n\nAlmost before her misgiving at the news could find time to shape itself she took, under her companion’s direction, two of the most beautiful of the Hamburghs in her arms, and followed the maid-servant, who had likewise taken two, to the adjacent mansion, which, though ornate and imposing, showed traces everywhere on this side that some occupant of its chambers could bend to the love of dumb creatures—feathers floating within view of the front, and hen-coops standing on the grass.\n\nIn a sitting-room on the ground-floor, ensconced in an armchair with her back to the light, was the owner and mistress of the estate, a white-haired woman of not more than sixty, or even less, wearing a large cap. She had the mobile face frequent in those whose sight has decayed by stages, has been laboriously striven after, and reluctantly let go, rather than the stagnant mien apparent in persons long sightless or born blind. Tess walked up to this lady with her feathered charges—one sitting on each arm.\n\n“Ah, you are the young woman come to look after my birds?” said Mrs d’Urberville, recognizing a new footstep. “I hope you will be kind to them. My bailiff tells me you are quite the proper person. Well, where are they? Ah, this is Strut! But he is hardly so lively to-day, is he? He is alarmed at being handled by a stranger, I suppose. And Phena too—yes, they are a little frightened—aren’t you, dears? But they will soon get used to you.”\n\nWhile the old lady had been speaking Tess and the other maid, in obedience to her gestures, had placed the fowls severally in her lap, and she had felt them over from head to tail, examining their beaks, their combs, the manes of the cocks, their wings, and their claws. Her touch enabled her to recognize them in a moment, and to discover if a single feather were crippled or draggled. She handled their crops, and knew what they had eaten, and if too little or too much; her face enacting a vivid pantomime of the criticisms passing in her mind.\n\nThe birds that the two girls had brought in were duly returned to the yard, and the process was repeated till all the pet cocks and hens had been submitted to the old woman—Hamburghs, Bantams, Cochins, Brahmas, Dorkings, and such other sorts as were in fashion just then—her perception of each visitor being seldom at fault as she received the bird upon her knees.\n\nIt reminded Tess of a Confirmation, in which Mrs d’Urberville was the bishop, the fowls the young people presented, and herself and the maid-servant the parson and curate of the parish bringing them up. At the end of the ceremony Mrs d’Urberville abruptly asked Tess, wrinkling and twitching her face into undulations, “Can you whistle?”\n“Whistle, Ma’am?”\n\n“Yes, whistle tunes.”\n\nTess could whistle like most other country-girls, though the accomplishment was one which she did not care to profess in genteel company. However, she blandly admitted that such was the fact.\n\n“Then you will have to practise it every day. I had a lad who did it very well, but he has left. I want you to whistle to my bullfinches; as I cannot see them, I like to hear them, and we teach ’em airs that way. Tell her where the cages are, Elizabeth. You must begin to-morrow, or they will go back in their piping. They have been neglected these several days.”\n\n“Mr d’Urberville whistled to ’em this morning, ma’am,” said Elizabeth.\n“He! Pooh!”\n\nThe old lady’s face creased into furrows of repugnance, and she made no further reply.\n\nThus the reception of Tess by her fancied kinswoman terminated, and the birds were taken back to their quarters. The girl’s surprise at Mrs d’Urberville’s manner was not great; for since seeing the size of the house she had expected no more. But she was far from being aware that the old lady had never heard a word of the so-called kinship. She gathered that no great affection flowed between the blind woman and her son. But in that, too, she was mistaken. Mrs d’Urberville was not the first mother compelled to love her offspring resentfully, and to be bitterly fond.\n\nIn spite of the unpleasant initiation of the day before, Tess inclined to the freedom and novelty of her new position in the morning when the sun shone, now that she was once installed there; and she was curious to test her powers in the unexpected direction asked of her, so as to ascertain her chance of retaining her post. As soon as she was alone within the walled garden she sat herself down on a coop, and seriously screwed up her mouth for the long-neglected practice. She found her former ability to have degenerated to the production of a hollow rush of wind through the lips, and no clear note at all.\n\nShe remained fruitlessly blowing and blowing, wondering how she could have so grown out of the art which had come by nature, till she became aware of a movement among the ivy-boughs which cloaked the garden-wall no less then the cottage. Looking that way she beheld a form springing from the coping to the plot. It was Alec d’Urberville, whom she had not set eyes on since he had conducted her the day before to the door of the gardener’s cottage where she had lodgings.\n\n“Upon my honour!” cried he, “there was never before such a beautiful thing in Nature or Art as you look, ‘Cousin’ Tess (‘Cousin’ had a faint ring of mockery). I have been watching you from over the wall—sitting like _Im_-patience on a monument, and pouting up that pretty red mouth to whistling shape, and whooing and whooing, and privately swearing, and never being able to produce a note. Why, you are quite cross because you can’t do it.”\n\n“I may be cross, but I didn’t swear.”\n\n“Ah! I understand why you are trying—those bullies! My mother wants you to carry on their musical education. How selfish of her! As if attending to these curst cocks and hens here were not enough work for any girl. I would flatly refuse, if I were you.”\n\n“But she wants me particularly to do it, and to be ready by to-morrow morning.”\n\n“Does she? Well then—I’ll give you a lesson or two.”\n\n“Oh no, you won’t!” said Tess, withdrawing towards the door.\n\n“Nonsense; I don’t want to touch you. See—I’ll stand on this side of the wire-netting, and you can keep on the other; so you may feel quite safe. Now, look here; you screw up your lips too harshly. There ’tis—so.”\n\nHe suited the action to the word, and whistled a line of “Take, O take those lips away.” But the allusion was lost upon Tess.\n\n“Now try,” said d’Urberville.\n\nShe attempted to look reserved; her face put on a sculptural severity. But he persisted in his demand, and at last, to get rid of him, she did put up her lips as directed for producing a clear note; laughing distressfully, however, and then blushing with vexation that she had laughed.\n\nHe encouraged her with “Try again!”\n\nTess was quite serious, painfully serious by this time; and she tried—ultimately and unexpectedly emitting a real round sound. The momentary pleasure of success got the better of her; her eyes enlarged, and she involuntarily smiled in his face.\n\n“That’s it! Now I have started you—you’ll go on beautifully. There—I said I would not come near you; and, in spite of such temptation as never before fell to mortal man, I’ll keep my word.... Tess, do you think my mother a queer old soul?”\n\n“I don’t know much of her yet, sir.”\n\n“You’ll find her so; she must be, to make you learn to whistle to her bullfinches. I am rather out of her books just now, but you will be quite in favour if you treat her live-stock well. Good morning. If you meet with any difficulties and want help here, don’t go to the bailiff, come to me.”\n\nIt was in the economy of this _régime_ that Tess Durbeyfield had undertaken to fill a place. Her first day’s experiences were fairly typical of those which followed through many succeeding days. A familiarity with Alec d’Urberville’s presence—which that young man carefully cultivated in her by playful dialogue, and by jestingly calling her his cousin when they were alone—removed much of her original shyness of him, without, however, implanting any feeling which could engender shyness of a new and tenderer kind. But she was more pliable under his hands than a mere companionship would have made her, owing to her unavoidable dependence upon his mother, and, through that lady’s comparative helplessness, upon him.\n\nShe soon found that whistling to the bullfinches in Mrs d’Urberville’s room was no such onerous business when she had regained the art, for she had caught from her musical mother numerous airs that suited those songsters admirably. A far more satisfactory time than when she practised in the garden was this whistling by the cages each morning. Unrestrained by the young man’s presence she threw up her mouth, put her lips near the bars, and piped away in easeful grace to the attentive listeners.\n\nMrs d’Urberville slept in a large four-post bedstead hung with heavy damask curtains, and the bullfinches occupied the same apartment, where they flitted about freely at certain hours, and made little white spots on the furniture and upholstery. Once while Tess was at the window where the cages were ranged, giving her lesson as usual, she thought she heard a rustling behind the bed. The old lady was not present, and turning round the girl had an impression that the toes of a pair of boots were visible below the fringe of the curtains. Thereupon her whistling became so disjointed that the listener, if such there were, must have discovered her suspicion of his presence. She searched the curtains every morning after that, but never found anybody within them. Alec d’Urberville had evidently thought better of his freak to terrify her by an ambush of that kind.\n\n# X\n\nEvery village has its idiosyncrasy, its constitution, often its own code of morality. The levity of some of the younger women in and about Trantridge was marked, and was perhaps symptomatic of the choice spirit who ruled The Slopes in that vicinity. The place had also a more abiding defect; it drank hard. The staple conversation on the farms around was on the uselessness of saving money; and smock-frocked arithmeticians, leaning on their ploughs or hoes, would enter into calculations of great nicety to prove that parish relief was a fuller provision for a man in his old age than any which could result from savings out of their wages during a whole lifetime.\n\nThe chief pleasure of these philosophers lay in going every Saturday night, when work was done, to Chaseborough, a decayed market-town two or three miles distant; and, returning in the small hours of the next morning, to spend Sunday in sleeping off the dyspeptic effects of the curious compounds sold to them as beer by the monopolizers of the once-independent inns.\n\nFor a long time Tess did not join in the weekly pilgrimages. But under pressure from matrons not much older than herself—for a field-man’s wages being as high at twenty-one as at forty, marriage was early here—Tess at length consented to go. Her first experience of the journey afforded her more enjoyment than she had expected, the hilariousness of the others being quite contagious after her monotonous attention to the poultry-farm all the week. She went again and again. Being graceful and interesting, standing moreover on the momentary threshold of womanhood, her appearance drew down upon her some sly regards from loungers in the streets of Chaseborough; hence, though sometimes her journey to the town was made independently, she always searched for her fellows at nightfall, to have the protection of their companionship homeward.\n\nThis had gone on for a month or two when there came a Saturday in September, on which a fair and a market coincided; and the pilgrims from Trantridge sought double delights at the inns on that account. Tess’s occupations made her late in setting out, so that her comrades reached the town long before her. It was a fine September evening, just before sunset, when yellow lights struggle with blue shades in hairlike lines, and the atmosphere itself forms a prospect without aid from more solid objects, except the innumerable winged insects that dance in it. Through this low-lit mistiness Tess walked leisurely along.\n\nShe did not discover the coincidence of the market with the fair till she had reached the place, by which time it was close upon dusk. Her limited marketing was soon completed; and then as usual she began to look about for some of the Trantridge cottagers.\n\nAt first she could not find them, and she was informed that most of them had gone to what they called a private little jig at the house of a hay-trusser and peat-dealer who had transactions with their farm. He lived in an out-of-the-way nook of the townlet, and in trying to find her course thither her eyes fell upon Mr d’Urberville standing at a street corner.\n\n“What—my Beauty? You here so late?” he said.\n\nShe told him that she was simply waiting for company homeward.\n\n“I’ll see you again,” said he over her shoulder as she went on down the back lane.\n\nApproaching the hay-trussers, she could hear the fiddled notes of a reel proceeding from some building in the rear; but no sound of dancing was audible—an exceptional state of things for these parts, where as a rule the stamping drowned the music. The front door being open she could see straight through the house into the garden at the back as far as the shades of night would allow; and nobody appearing to her knock, she traversed the dwelling and went up the path to the outhouse whence the sound had attracted her.\n\nIt was a windowless erection used for storage, and from the open door there floated into the obscurity a mist of yellow radiance, which at first Tess thought to be illuminated smoke. But on drawing nearer she perceived that it was a cloud of dust, lit by candles within the outhouse, whose beams upon the haze carried forward the outline of the doorway into the wide night of the garden.\n\nWhen she came close and looked in she beheld indistinct forms racing up and down to the figure of the dance, the silence of their footfalls arising from their being overshoe in “scroff”—that is to say, the powdery residuum from the storage of peat and other products, the stirring of which by their turbulent feet created the nebulosity that involved the scene. Through this floating, fusty _débris_ of peat and hay, mixed with the perspirations and warmth of the dancers, and forming together a sort of vegeto-human pollen, the muted fiddles feebly pushed their notes, in marked contrast to the spirit with which the measure was trodden out. They coughed as they danced, and laughed as they coughed. Of the rushing couples there could barely be discerned more than the high lights—the indistinctness shaping them to satyrs clasping nymphs—a multiplicity of Pans whirling a multiplicity of Syrinxes; Lotis attempting to elude Priapus, and always failing.\n\nAt intervals a couple would approach the doorway for air, and the haze no longer veiling their features, the demigods resolved themselves into the homely personalities of her own next-door neighbours. Could Trantridge in two or three short hours have metamorphosed itself thus madly!\n\nSome Sileni of the throng sat on benches and hay-trusses by the wall; and one of them recognized her.\n\n“The maids don’t think it respectable to dance at The Flower-de-Luce,” he explained. “They don’t like to let everybody see which be their fancy-men. Besides, the house sometimes shuts up just when their jints begin to get greased. So we come here and send out for liquor.”\n\n“But when be any of you going home?” asked Tess with some anxiety.\n\n“Now—a’most directly. This is all but the last jig.”\n\nShe waited. The reel drew to a close, and some of the party were in the mind of starting. But others would not, and another dance was formed. This surely would end it, thought Tess. But it merged in yet another. She became restless and uneasy; yet, having waited so long, it was necessary to wait longer; on account of the fair the roads were dotted with roving characters of possibly ill intent; and, though not fearful of measurable dangers, she feared the unknown. Had she been near Marlott she would have had less dread.\n\n“Don’t ye be nervous, my dear good soul,” expostulated, between his coughs, a young man with a wet face and his straw hat so far back upon his head that the brim encircled it like the nimbus of a saint. “What’s yer hurry? To-morrow is Sunday, thank God, and we can sleep it off in church-time. Now, have a turn with me?”\n\nShe did not abhor dancing, but she was not going to dance here. The movement grew more passionate: the fiddlers behind the luminous pillar of cloud now and then varied the air by playing on the wrong side of the bridge or with the back of the bow. But it did not matter; the panting shapes spun onwards.\n\nThey did not vary their partners if their inclination were to stick to previous ones. Changing partners simply meant that a satisfactory choice had not as yet been arrived at by one or other of the pair, and by this time every couple had been suitably matched. It was then that the ecstasy and the dream began, in which emotion was the matter of the universe, and matter but an adventitious intrusion likely to hinder you from spinning where you wanted to spin.\n\nSuddenly there was a dull thump on the ground: a couple had fallen, and lay in a mixed heap. The next couple, unable to check its progress, came toppling over the obstacle. An inner cloud of dust rose around the prostrate figures amid the general one of the room, in which a twitching entanglement of arms and legs was discernible.\n\n“You shall catch it for this, my gentleman, when you get home!” burst in female accents from the human heap—those of the unhappy partner of the man whose clumsiness had caused the mishap; she happened also to be his recently married wife, in which assortment there was nothing unusual at Trantridge as long as any affection remained between wedded couples; and, indeed, it was not uncustomary in their later lives, to avoid making odd lots of the single people between whom there might be a warm understanding.\n\nA loud laugh from behind Tess’s back, in the shade of the garden, united with the titter within the room. She looked round, and saw the red coal of a cigar: Alec d’Urberville was standing there alone. He beckoned to her, and she reluctantly retreated towards him.\n\n“Well, my Beauty, what are you doing here?”\n\nShe was so tired after her long day and her walk that she confided her trouble to him—that she had been waiting ever since he saw her to have their company home, because the road at night was strange to her. “But it seems they will never leave off, and I really think I will wait no longer.”\n\n“Certainly do not. I have only a saddle-horse here to-day; but come to The Flower-de-Luce, and I’ll hire a trap, and drive you home with me.”\n\nTess, though flattered, had never quite got over her original mistrust of him, and, despite their tardiness, she preferred to walk home with the work-folk. So she answered that she was much obliged to him, but would not trouble him. “I have said that I will wait for ’em, and they will expect me to now.”\n\n“Very well, Miss Independence. Please yourself... Then I shall not hurry... My good Lord, what a kick-up they are having there!”\n\nHe had not put himself forward into the light, but some of them had perceived him, and his presence led to a slight pause and a consideration of how the time was flying. As soon as he had re-lit a cigar and walked away the Trantridge people began to collect themselves from amid those who had come in from other farms, and prepared to leave in a body. Their bundles and baskets were gathered up, and half an hour later, when the clock-chime sounded a quarter past eleven, they were straggling along the lane which led up the hill towards their homes.\n\nIt was a three-mile walk, along a dry white road, made whiter to-night by the light of the moon.\n\nTess soon perceived as she walked in the flock, sometimes with this one, sometimes with that, that the fresh night air was producing staggerings and serpentine courses among the men who had partaken too freely; some of the more careless women also were wandering in their gait—to wit, a dark virago, Car Darch, dubbed Queen of Spades, till lately a favourite of d’Urberville’s; Nancy, her sister, nicknamed the Queen of Diamonds; and the young married woman who had already tumbled down. Yet however terrestrial and lumpy their appearance just now to the mean unglamoured eye, to themselves the case was different. They followed the road with a sensation that they were soaring along in a supporting medium, possessed of original and profound thoughts, themselves and surrounding nature forming an organism of which all the parts harmoniously and joyously interpenetrated each other. They were as sublime as the moon and stars above them, and the moon and stars were as ardent as they.\n\nTess, however, had undergone such painful experiences of this kind in her father’s house that the discovery of their condition spoilt the pleasure she was beginning to feel in the moonlight journey. Yet she stuck to the party, for reasons above given.\n\nIn the open highway they had progressed in scattered order; but now their route was through a field-gate, and the foremost finding a difficulty in opening it, they closed up together.\n\nThis leading pedestrian was Car the Queen of Spades, who carried a wicker-basket containing her mother’s groceries, her own draperies, and other purchases for the week. The basket being large and heavy, Car had placed it for convenience of porterage on the top of her head, where it rode on in jeopardized balance as she walked with arms akimbo.\n\n“Well—whatever is that a-creeping down thy back, Car Darch?” said one of the group suddenly.\n\nAll looked at Car. Her gown was a light cotton print, and from the back of her head a kind of rope could be seen descending to some distance below her waist, like a Chinaman’s queue.\n\n“’Tis her hair falling down,” said another.\n\nNo; it was not her hair: it was a black stream of something oozing from her basket, and it glistened like a slimy snake in the cold still rays of the moon.\n\n“’Tis treacle,” said an observant matron.\n\nTreacle it was. Car’s poor old grandmother had a weakness for the sweet stuff. Honey she had in plenty out of her own hives, but treacle was what her soul desired, and Car had been about to give her a treat of surprise. Hastily lowering the basket the dark girl found that the vessel containing the syrup had been smashed within.\n\nBy this time there had arisen a shout of laughter at the extraordinary appearance of Car’s back, which irritated the dark queen into getting rid of the disfigurement by the first sudden means available, and independently of the help of the scoffers. She rushed excitedly into the field they were about to cross, and flinging herself flat on her back upon the grass, began to wipe her gown as well as she could by spinning horizontally on the herbage and dragging herself over it upon her elbows.\n\nThe laughter rang louder; they clung to the gate, to the posts, rested on their staves, in the weakness engendered by their convulsions at the spectacle of Car. Our heroine, who had hitherto held her peace, at this wild moment could not help joining in with the rest.\n\nIt was a misfortune—in more ways than one. No sooner did the dark queen hear the soberer richer note of Tess among those of the other work-people than a long-smouldering sense of rivalry inflamed her to madness. She sprang to her feet and closely faced the object of her dislike.\n\n“How darest th’ laugh at me, hussy!” she cried.\n\n“I couldn’t really help it when t’others did,” apologized Tess, still tittering.\n\n“Ah, th’st think th’ beest everybody, dostn’t, because th’ beest first favourite with He just now! But stop a bit, my lady, stop a bit! I’m as good as two of such! Look here—here’s at ’ee!”\n\nTo Tess’s horror the dark queen began stripping off the bodice of her gown—which for the added reason of its ridiculed condition she was only too glad to be free of—till she had bared her plump neck, shoulders, and arms to the moonshine, under which they looked as luminous and beautiful as some Praxitelean creation, in their possession of the faultless rotundities of a lusty country-girl. She closed her fists and squared up at Tess.\n\n“Indeed, then, I shall not fight!” said the latter majestically; “and if I had known you was of that sort, I wouldn’t have so let myself down as to come with such a whorage as this is!”\n\nThe rather too inclusive speech brought down a torrent of vituperation from other quarters upon fair Tess’s unlucky head, particularly from the Queen of Diamonds, who having stood in the relations to d’Urberville that Car had also been suspected of, united with the latter against the common enemy. Several other women also chimed in, with an animus which none of them would have been so fatuous as to show but for the rollicking evening they had passed. Thereupon, finding Tess unfairly browbeaten, the husbands and lovers tried to make peace by defending her; but the result of that attempt was directly to increase the war.\n\nTess was indignant and ashamed. She no longer minded the loneliness of the way and the lateness of the hour; her one object was to get away from the whole crew as soon as possible. She knew well enough that the better among them would repent of their passion next day. They were all now inside the field, and she was edging back to rush off alone when a horseman emerged almost silently from the corner of the hedge that screened the road, and Alec d’Urberville looked round upon them.\n\n“What the devil is all this row about, work-folk?” he asked.\n\nThe explanation was not readily forthcoming; and, in truth, he did not require any. Having heard their voices while yet some way off he had ridden creepingly forward, and learnt enough to satisfy himself.\n\nTess was standing apart from the rest, near the gate. He bent over towards her. “Jump up behind me,” he whispered, “and we’ll get shot of the screaming cats in a jiffy!”\n\nShe felt almost ready to faint, so vivid was her sense of the crisis. At almost any other moment of her life she would have refused such proffered aid and company, as she had refused them several times before; and now the loneliness would not of itself have forced her to do otherwise. But coming as the invitation did at the particular juncture when fear and indignation at these adversaries could be transformed by a spring of the foot into a triumph over them, she abandoned herself to her impulse, climbed the gate, put her toe upon his instep, and scrambled into the saddle behind him. The pair were speeding away into the distant gray by the time that the contentious revellers became aware of what had happened.\n\nThe Queen of Spades forgot the stain on her bodice, and stood beside the Queen of Diamonds and the new-married, staggering young woman—all with a gaze of fixity in the direction in which the horse’s tramp was diminishing into silence on the road.\n\n“What be ye looking at?” asked a man who had not observed the incident.\n\n“Ho-ho-ho!” laughed dark Car.\n\n“Hee-hee-hee!” laughed the tippling bride, as she steadied herself on the arm of her fond husband.\n\n“Heu-heu-heu!” laughed dark Car’s mother, stroking her moustache as she explained laconically: “Out of the frying-pan into the fire!”\n\nThen these children of the open air, whom even excess of alcohol could scarce injure permanently, betook themselves to the field-path; and as they went there moved onward with them, around the shadow of each one’s head, a circle of opalized light, formed by the moon’s rays upon the glistening sheet of dew. Each pedestrian could see no halo but his or her own, which never deserted the head-shadow, whatever its vulgar unsteadiness might be; but adhered to it, and persistently beautified it; till the erratic motions seemed an inherent part of the irradiation, and the fumes of their breathing a component of the night’s mist; and the spirit of the scene, and of the moonlight, and of Nature, seemed harmoniously to mingle with the spirit of wine.\n\n# XI\n\nThe twain cantered along for some time without speech, Tess as she clung to him still panting in her triumph, yet in other respects dubious. She had perceived that the horse was not the spirited one he sometimes rose, and felt no alarm on that score, though her seat was precarious enough despite her tight hold of him. She begged him to slow the animal to a walk, which Alec accordingly did.\n\n“Neatly done, was it not, dear Tess?” he said by and by.\n\n“Yes!” said she. “I am sure I ought to be much obliged to you.”\n\n“And are you?”\n\nShe did not reply.\n\n“Tess, why do you always dislike my kissing you?”\n\n“I suppose—because I don’t love you.”\n\n“You are quite sure?”\n\n“I am angry with you sometimes!”\n\n“Ah, I half feared as much.” Nevertheless, Alec did not object to that confession. He knew that anything was better then frigidity. “Why haven’t you told me when I have made you angry?”\n\n“You know very well why. Because I cannot help myself here.”\n\n“I haven’t offended you often by love-making?”\n\n“You have sometimes.”\n\n“How many times?”\n\n“You know as well as I—too many times.”\n\n“Every time I have tried?”\n\nShe was silent, and the horse ambled along for a considerable distance, till a faint luminous fog, which had hung in the hollows all the evening, became general and enveloped them. It seemed to hold the moonlight in suspension, rendering it more pervasive than in clear air. Whether on this account, or from absent-mindedness, or from sleepiness, she did not perceive that they had long ago passed the point at which the lane to Trantridge branched from the highway, and that her conductor had not taken the Trantridge track.\n\nShe was inexpressibly weary. She had risen at five o’clock every morning of that week, had been on foot the whole of each day, and on this evening had in addition walked the three miles to Chaseborough, waited three hours for her neighbours without eating or drinking, her impatience to start them preventing either; she had then walked a mile of the way home, and had undergone the excitement of the quarrel, till, with the slow progress of their steed, it was now nearly one o’clock. Only once, however, was she overcome by actual drowsiness. In that moment of oblivion her head sank gently against him.\n\nD’Urberville stopped the horse, withdrew his feet from the stirrups, turned sideways on the saddle, and enclosed her waist with his arm to support her.\n\nThis immediately put her on the defensive, and with one of those sudden impulses of reprisal to which she was liable she gave him a little push from her. In his ticklish position he nearly lost his balance and only just avoided rolling over into the road, the horse, though a powerful one, being fortunately the quietest he rode.\n\n“That is devilish unkind!” he said. “I mean no harm—only to keep you from falling.”\n\nShe pondered suspiciously, till, thinking that this might after all be true, she relented, and said quite humbly, “I beg your pardon, sir.”\n\n“I won’t pardon you unless you show some confidence in me. Good God!” he burst out, “what am I, to be repulsed so by a mere chit like you? For near three mortal months have you trifled with my feelings, eluded me, and snubbed me; and I won’t stand it!”\n\n“I’ll leave you to-morrow, sir.”\n\n“No, you will not leave me to-morrow! Will you, I ask once more, show your belief in me by letting me clasp you with my arm? Come, between us two and nobody else, now. We know each other well; and you know that I love you, and think you the prettiest girl in the world, which you are. Mayn’t I treat you as a lover?”\n\nShe drew a quick pettish breath of objection, writhing uneasily on her seat, looked far ahead, and murmured, “I don’t know—I wish—how can I say yes or no when—”\n\nHe settled the matter by clasping his arm round her as he desired, and Tess expressed no further negative. Thus they sidled slowly onward till it struck her they had been advancing for an unconscionable time—far longer than was usually occupied by the short journey from Chaseborough, even at this walking pace, and that they were no longer on hard road, but in a mere trackway.\n\n“Why, where be we?” she exclaimed.\n\n“Passing by a wood.”\n\n“A wood—what wood? Surely we are quite out of the road?”\n\n“A bit of The Chase—the oldest wood in England. It is a lovely night, and why should we not prolong our ride a little?”\n\n“How could you be so treacherous!” said Tess, between archness and real dismay, and getting rid of his arm by pulling open his fingers one by one, though at the risk of slipping off herself. “Just when I’ve been putting such trust in you, and obliging you to please you, because I thought I had wronged you by that push! Please set me down, and let me walk home.”\n\n“You cannot walk home, darling, even if the air were clear. We are miles away from Trantridge, if I must tell you, and in this growing fog you might wander for hours among these trees.”\n\n“Never mind that,” she coaxed. “Put me down, I beg you. I don’t mind where it is; only let me get down, sir, please!”\n\n“Very well, then, I will—on one condition. Having brought you here to this out-of-the-way place, I feel myself responsible for your safe-conduct home, whatever you may yourself feel about it. As to your getting to Trantridge without assistance, it is quite impossible; for, to tell the truth, dear, owing to this fog, which so disguises everything, I don’t quite know where we are myself. Now, if you will promise to wait beside the horse while I walk through the bushes till I come to some road or house, and ascertain exactly our whereabouts, I’ll deposit you here willingly. When I come back I’ll give you full directions, and if you insist upon walking you may; or you may ride—at your pleasure.”\n\nShe accepted these terms, and slid off on the near side, though not till he had stolen a cursory kiss. He sprang down on the other side.\n\n“I suppose I must hold the horse?” said she.\n\n“Oh no; it’s not necessary,” replied Alec, patting the panting creature. “He’s had enough of it for to-night.”\n\nHe turned the horse’s head into the bushes, hitched him on to a bough, and made a sort of couch or nest for her in the deep mass of dead leaves.\n\n“Now, you sit there,” he said. “The leaves have not got damp as yet. Just give an eye to the horse—it will be quite sufficient.”\n\nHe took a few steps away from her, but, returning, said, “By the bye, Tess, your father has a new cob to-day. Somebody gave it to him.”\n\n“Somebody? You!”\n\nD’Urberville nodded.\n\n“O how very good of you that is!” she exclaimed, with a painful sense of the awkwardness of having to thank him just then.\n\n“And the children have some toys.”\n\n“I didn’t know—you ever sent them anything!” she murmured, much moved. “I almost wish you had not—yes, I almost wish it!”\n\n“Why, dear?”\n\n“It—hampers me so.”\n\n“Tessy—don’t you love me ever so little now?”\n\n“I’m grateful,” she reluctantly admitted. “But I fear I do not—” The sudden vision of his passion for herself as a factor in this result so distressed her that, beginning with one slow tear, and then following with another, she wept outright.\n\n“Don’t cry, dear, dear one! Now sit down here, and wait till I come.” She passively sat down amid the leaves he had heaped, and shivered slightly. “Are you cold?” he asked.\n\n“Not very—a little.”\n\nHe touched her with his fingers, which sank into her as into down. “You have only that puffy muslin dress on—how’s that?”\n\n“It’s my best summer one. ’Twas very warm when I started, and I didn’t know I was going to ride, and that it would be night.”\n\n“Nights grow chilly in September. Let me see.” He pulled off a light overcoat that he had worn, and put it round her tenderly. “That’s it—now you’ll feel warmer,” he continued. “Now, my pretty, rest there; I shall soon be back again.”\n\nHaving buttoned the overcoat round her shoulders he plunged into the webs of vapour which by this time formed veils between the trees. She could hear the rustling of the branches as he ascended the adjoining slope, till his movements were no louder than the hopping of a bird, and finally died away. With the setting of the moon the pale light lessened, and Tess became invisible as she fell into reverie upon the leaves where he had left her.\n\nIn the meantime Alec d’Urberville had pushed on up the slope to clear his genuine doubt as to the quarter of The Chase they were in. He had, in fact, ridden quite at random for over an hour, taking any turning that came to hand in order to prolong companionship with her, and giving far more attention to Tess’s moonlit person than to any wayside object. A little rest for the jaded animal being desirable, he did not hasten his search for landmarks. A clamber over the hill into the adjoining vale brought him to the fence of a highway whose contours he recognized, which settled the question of their whereabouts. D’Urberville thereupon turned back; but by this time the moon had quite gone down, and partly on account of the fog The Chase was wrapped in thick darkness, although morning was not far off. He was obliged to advance with outstretched hands to avoid contact with the boughs, and discovered that to hit the exact spot from which he had started was at first entirely beyond him. Roaming up and down, round and round, he at length heard a slight movement of the horse close at hand; and the sleeve of his overcoat unexpectedly caught his foot.\n\n“Tess!” said d’Urberville.\n\nThere was no answer. The obscurity was now so great that he could see absolutely nothing but a pale nebulousness at his feet, which represented the white muslin figure he had left upon the dead leaves. Everything else was blackness alike. D’Urberville stooped; and heard a gentle regular breathing. He knelt and bent lower, till her breath warmed his face, and in a moment his cheek was in contact with hers. She was sleeping soundly, and upon her eyelashes there lingered tears.\n\nDarkness and silence ruled everywhere around. Above them rose the primaeval yews and oaks of The Chase, in which there poised gentle roosting birds in their last nap; and about them stole the hopping rabbits and hares. But, might some say, where was Tess’s guardian angel? where was the providence of her simple faith? Perhaps, like that other god of whom the ironical Tishbite spoke, he was talking, or he was pursuing, or he was in a journey, or he was sleeping and not to be awaked.\n\nWhy it was that upon this beautiful feminine tissue, sensitive as gossamer, and practically blank as snow as yet, there should have been traced such a coarse pattern as it was doomed to receive; why so often the coarse appropriates the finer thus, the wrong man the woman, the wrong woman the man, many thousand years of analytical philosophy have failed to explain to our sense of order. One may, indeed, admit the possibility of a retribution lurking in the present catastrophe. Doubtless some of Tess d’Urberville’s mailed ancestors rollicking home from a fray had dealt the same measure even more ruthlessly towards peasant girls of their time. But though to visit the sins of the fathers upon the children may be a morality good enough for divinities, it is scorned by average human nature; and it therefore does not mend the matter.\n\nAs Tess’s own people down in those retreats are never tired of saying among each other in their fatalistic way: “It was to be.” There lay the pity of it. An immeasurable social chasm was to divide our heroine’s personality thereafter from that previous self of hers who stepped from her mother’s door to try her fortune at Trantridge poultry-farm.\nEnd of Phase the First\n\n# XII\n\nMaiden No More\nThe basket was heavy and the bundle was large, but she lugged them along like a person who did not find her especial burden in material things. Occasionally she stopped to rest in a mechanical way by some gate or post; and then, giving the baggage another hitch upon her full round arm, went steadily on again.\n\nIt was a Sunday morning in late October, about four months after Tess Durbeyfield’s arrival at Trantridge, and some few weeks subsequent to the night ride in The Chase. The time was not long past daybreak, and the yellow luminosity upon the horizon behind her back lighted the ridge towards which her face was set—the barrier of the vale wherein she had of late been a stranger—which she would have to climb over to reach her birthplace. The ascent was gradual on this side, and the soil and scenery differed much from those within Blakemore Vale. Even the character and accent of the two peoples had shades of difference, despite the amalgamating effects of a roundabout railway; so that, though less than twenty miles from the place of her sojourn at Trantridge, her native village had seemed a far-away spot. The field-folk shut in there traded northward and westward, travelled, courted, and married northward and westward, thought northward and westward; those on this side mainly directed their energies and attention to the east and south.\n\nThe incline was the same down which d’Urberville had driven her so wildly on that day in June. Tess went up the remainder of its length without stopping, and on reaching the edge of the escarpment gazed over the familiar green world beyond, now half-veiled in mist. It was always beautiful from here; it was terribly beautiful to Tess to-day, for since her eyes last fell upon it she had learnt that the serpent hisses where the sweet birds sing, and her views of life had been totally changed for her by the lesson. Verily another girl than the simple one she had been at home was she who, bowed by thought, stood still here, and turned to look behind her. She could not bear to look forward into the Vale.\n\nAscending by the long white road that Tess herself had just laboured up, she saw a two-wheeled vehicle, beside which walked a man, who held up his hand to attract her attention.\n\nShe obeyed the signal to wait for him with unspeculative repose, and in a few minutes man and horse stopped beside her.\n\n“Why did you slip away by stealth like this?” said d’Urberville, with upbraiding breathlessness; “on a Sunday morning, too, when people were all in bed! I only discovered it by accident, and I have been driving like the deuce to overtake you. Just look at the mare. Why go off like this? You know that nobody wished to hinder your going. And how unnecessary it has been for you to toil along on foot, and encumber yourself with this heavy load! I have followed like a madman, simply to drive you the rest of the distance, if you won’t come back.”\n\n“I shan’t come back,” said she.\n\n“I thought you wouldn’t—I said so! Well, then, put up your basket, and let me help you on.”\n\nShe listlessly placed her basket and bundle within the dog-cart, and stepped up, and they sat side by side. She had no fear of him now, and in the cause of her confidence her sorrow lay.\n\nD’Urberville mechanically lit a cigar, and the journey was continued with broken unemotional conversation on the commonplace objects by the wayside. He had quite forgotten his struggle to kiss her when, in the early summer, they had driven in the opposite direction along the same road. But she had not, and she sat now, like a puppet, replying to his remarks in monosyllables. After some miles they came in view of the clump of trees beyond which the village of Marlott stood. It was only then that her still face showed the least emotion, a tear or two beginning to trickle down.\n\n“What are you crying for?” he coldly asked.\n\n“I was only thinking that I was born over there,” murmured Tess.\n\n“Well—we must all be born somewhere.”\n\n“I wish I had never been born—there or anywhere else!”\n\n“Pooh! Well, if you didn’t wish to come to Trantridge why did you come?”\n\nShe did not reply.\n\n“You didn’t come for love of me, that I’ll swear.”\n\n“’Tis quite true. If I had gone for love o’ you, if I had ever sincerely loved you, if I loved you still, I should not so loathe and hate myself for my weakness as I do now!... My eyes were dazed by you for a little, and that was all.”\n\nHe shrugged his shoulders. She resumed—\n\n“I didn’t understand your meaning till it was too late.”\n\n“That’s what every woman says.”\n\n“How can you dare to use such words!” she cried, turning impetuously upon him, her eyes flashing as the latent spirit (of which he was to see more some day) awoke in her. “My God! I could knock you out of the gig! Did it never strike your mind that what every woman says some women may feel?”\n\n“Very well,” he said, laughing; “I am sorry to wound you. I did wrong—I admit it.” He dropped into some little bitterness as he continued: “Only you needn’t be so everlastingly flinging it in my face. I am ready to pay to the uttermost farthing. You know you need not work in the fields or the dairies again. You know you may clothe yourself with the best, instead of in the bald plain way you have lately affected, as if you couldn’t get a ribbon more than you earn.”\n\nHer lip lifted slightly, though there was little scorn, as a rule, in her large and impulsive nature.\n\n“I have said I will not take anything more from you, and I will not—I cannot! I _should_ be your creature to go on doing that, and I won’t!”\n\n“One would think you were a princess from your manner, in addition to a true and original d’Urberville—ha! ha! Well, Tess, dear, I can say no more. I suppose I am a bad fellow—a damn bad fellow. I was born bad, and I have lived bad, and I shall die bad in all probability. But, upon my lost soul, I won’t be bad towards you again, Tess. And if certain circumstances should arise—you understand—in which you are in the least need, the least difficulty, send me one line, and you shall have by return whatever you require. I may not be at Trantridge—I am going to London for a time—I can’t stand the old woman. But all letters will be forwarded.”\n\nShe said that she did not wish him to drive her further, and they stopped just under the clump of trees. D’Urberville alighted, and lifted her down bodily in his arms, afterwards placing her articles on the ground beside her. She bowed to him slightly, her eye just lingering in his; and then she turned to take the parcels for departure.\n\nAlec d’Urberville removed his cigar, bent towards her, and said—\n\n“You are not going to turn away like that, dear! Come!”\n\n“If you wish,” she answered indifferently. “See how you’ve mastered me!”\n\nShe thereupon turned round and lifted her face to his, and remained like a marble term while he imprinted a kiss upon her cheek—half perfunctorily, half as if zest had not yet quite died out. Her eyes vaguely rested upon the remotest trees in the lane while the kiss was given, as though she were nearly unconscious of what he did.\n\n“Now the other side, for old acquaintance’ sake.”\n\nShe turned her head in the same passive way, as one might turn at the request of a sketcher or hairdresser, and he kissed the other side, his lips touching cheeks that were damp and smoothly chill as the skin of the mushrooms in the fields around.\n\n“You don’t give me your mouth and kiss me back. You never willingly do that—you’ll never love me, I fear.”\n\n“I have said so, often. It is true. I have never really and truly loved you, and I think I never can.” She added mournfully, “Perhaps, of all things, a lie on this thing would do the most good to me now; but I have honour enough left, little as ’tis, not to tell that lie. If I did love you, I may have the best o’ causes for letting you know it. But I don’t.”\n\nHe emitted a laboured breath, as if the scene were getting rather oppressive to his heart, or to his conscience, or to his gentility.\n\n“Well, you are absurdly melancholy, Tess. I have no reason for flattering you now, and I can say plainly that you need not be so sad. You can hold your own for beauty against any woman of these parts, gentle or simple; I say it to you as a practical man and well-wisher. If you are wise you will show it to the world more than you do before it fades... And yet, Tess, will you come back to me! Upon my soul, I don’t like to let you go like this!”\n\n“Never, never! I made up my mind as soon as I saw—what I ought to have seen sooner; and I won’t come.”\n\n“Then good morning, my four months’ cousin—good-bye!”\n\nHe leapt up lightly, arranged the reins, and was gone between the tall red-berried hedges.\n\nTess did not look after him, but slowly wound along the crooked lane. It was still early, and though the sun’s lower limb was just free of the hill, his rays, ungenial and peering, addressed the eye rather than the touch as yet. There was not a human soul near. Sad October and her sadder self seemed the only two existences haunting that lane.\n\nAs she walked, however, some footsteps approached behind her, the footsteps of a man; and owing to the briskness of his advance he was close at her heels and had said “Good morning” before she had been long aware of his propinquity. He appeared to be an artisan of some sort, and carried a tin pot of red paint in his hand. He asked in a business-like manner if he should take her basket, which she permitted him to do, walking beside him.\n\n“It is early to be astir this Sabbath morn!” he said cheerfully.\n\n“Yes,” said Tess.\n\n“When most people are at rest from their week’s work.”\n\nShe also assented to this.\n\n“Though I do more real work to-day than all the week besides.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“All the week I work for the glory of man, and on Sunday for the glory of God. That’s more real than the other—hey? I have a little to do here at this stile.” The man turned, as he spoke, to an opening at the roadside leading into a pasture. “If you’ll wait a moment,” he added, “I shall not be long.”\n\nAs he had her basket she could not well do otherwise; and she waited, observing him. He set down her basket and the tin pot, and stirring the paint with the brush that was in it began painting large square letters on the middle board of the three composing the stile, placing a comma after each word, as if to give pause while that word was driven well home to the reader’s heart—\n\nAgainst the peaceful landscape, the pale, decaying tints of the copses, the blue air of the horizon, and the lichened stile-boards, these staring vermilion words shone forth. They seemed to shout themselves out and make the atmosphere ring. Some people might have cried “Alas, poor Theology!” at the hideous defacement—the last grotesque phase of a creed which had served mankind well in its time. But the words entered Tess with accusatory horror. It was as if this man had known her recent history; yet he was a total stranger.\n\nHaving finished his text he picked up her basket, and she mechanically resumed her walk beside him.\n\n“Do you believe what you paint?” she asked in low tones.\n\n“Believe that tex? Do I believe in my own existence!”\n\n“But,” said she tremulously, “suppose your sin was not of your own seeking?”\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n“I cannot split hairs on that burning query,” he said. “I have walked hundreds of miles this past summer, painting these texes on every wall, gate, and stile the length and breadth of this district. I leave their application to the hearts of the people who read ’em.”\n\n“I think they are horrible,” said Tess. “Crushing! Killing!”\n\n“That’s what they are meant to be!” he replied in a trade voice. “But you should read my hottest ones—them I kips for slums and seaports. They’d make ye wriggle! Not but what this is a very good tex for rural districts.... Ah—there’s a nice bit of blank wall up by that barn standing to waste. I must put one there—one that it will be good for dangerous young females like yerself to heed. Will ye wait, missy?”\n\n“No,” said she; and taking her basket Tess trudged on. A little way forward she turned her head. The old gray wall began to advertise a similar fiery lettering to the first, with a strange and unwonted mien, as if distressed at duties it had never before been called upon to perform. It was with a sudden flush that she read and realized what was to be the inscription he was now halfway through—\n\n# THOU, SHALT, NOT, COMMIT—\n\nHer cheerful friend saw her looking, stopped his brush, and shouted—\n\n“If you want to ask for edification on these things of moment, there’s a very earnest good man going to preach a charity-sermon to-day in the parish you are going to—Mr Clare of Emminster. I’m not of his persuasion now, but he’s a good man, and he’ll expound as well as any parson I know. ’Twas he began the work in me.”\n\nBut Tess did not answer; she throbbingly resumed her walk, her eyes fixed on the ground. “Pooh—I don’t believe God said such things!” she murmured contemptuously when her flush had died away.\n\nA plume of smoke soared up suddenly from her father’s chimney, the sight of which made her heart ache. The aspect of the interior, when she reached it, made her heart ache more. Her mother, who had just come downstairs, turned to greet her from the fireplace, where she was kindling barked-oak twigs under the breakfast kettle. The young children were still above, as was also her father, it being Sunday morning, when he felt justified in lying an additional half-hour.\n\n“Well!—my dear Tess!” exclaimed her surprised mother, jumping up and kissing the girl. “How be ye? I didn’t see you till you was in upon me! Have you come home to be married?”\n\n“No, I have not come for that, mother.”\n\n“Then for a holiday?”\n\n“Yes—for a holiday; for a long holiday,” said Tess.\n\n“What, isn’t your cousin going to do the handsome thing?”\n\n“He’s not my cousin, and he’s not going to marry me.”\n\nHer mother eyed her narrowly.\n\n“Come, you have not told me all,” she said.\n\nThen Tess went up to her mother, put her face upon Joan’s neck, and told.\n\n“And yet th’st not got him to marry ’ee!” reiterated her mother. “Any woman would have done it but you, after that!”\n\n“Perhaps any woman would except me.”\n\n“It would have been something like a story to come back with, if you had!” continued Mrs Durbeyfield, ready to burst into tears of vexation. “After all the talk about you and him which has reached us here, who would have expected it to end like this! Why didn’t ye think of doing some good for your family instead o’ thinking only of yourself? See how I’ve got to teave and slave, and your poor weak father with his heart clogged like a dripping-pan. I did hope for something to come out o’ this! To see what a pretty pair you and he made that day when you drove away together four months ago! See what he has given us—all, as we thought, because we were his kin. But if he’s not, it must have been done because of his love for ’ee. And yet you’ve not got him to marry!”\n\nGet Alec d’Urberville in the mind to marry her! He marry _her_! On matrimony he had never once said a word. And what if he had? How a convulsive snatching at social salvation might have impelled her to answer him she could not say. But her poor foolish mother little knew her present feeling towards this man. Perhaps it was unusual in the circumstances, unlucky, unaccountable; but there it was; and this, as she had said, was what made her detest herself. She had never wholly cared for him; she did not at all care for him now. She had dreaded him, winced before him, succumbed to adroit advantages he took of her helplessness; then, temporarily blinded by his ardent manners, had been stirred to confused surrender awhile: had suddenly despised and disliked him, and had run away. That was all. Hate him she did not quite; but he was dust and ashes to her, and even for her name’s sake she scarcely wished to marry him.\n\n“You ought to have been more careful if you didn’t mean to get him to make you his wife!”\n\n“O mother, my mother!” cried the agonized girl, turning passionately upon her parent as if her poor heart would break. “How could I be expected to know? I was a child when I left this house four months ago. Why didn’t you tell me there was danger in men-folk? Why didn’t you warn me? Ladies know what to fend hands against, because they read novels that tell them of these tricks; but I never had the chance o’ learning in that way, and you did not help me!”\n\nHer mother was subdued.\n\n“I thought if I spoke of his fond feelings and what they might lead to, you would be hontish wi’ him and lose your chance,” she murmured, wiping her eyes with her apron. “Well, we must make the best of it, I suppose. ’Tis nater, after all, and what do please God!”\n\n# XIII\n\nThe event of Tess Durbeyfield’s return from the manor of her bogus kinsfolk was rumoured abroad, if rumour be not too large a word for a space of a square mile. In the afternoon several young girls of Marlott, former schoolfellows and acquaintances of Tess, called to see her, arriving dressed in their best starched and ironed, as became visitors to a person who had made a transcendent conquest (as they supposed), and sat round the room looking at her with great curiosity. For the fact that it was this said thirty-first cousin, Mr d’Urberville, who had fallen in love with her, a gentleman not altogether local, whose reputation as a reckless gallant and heartbreaker was beginning to spread beyond the immediate boundaries of Trantridge, lent Tess’s supposed position, by its fearsomeness, a far higher fascination that it would have exercised if unhazardous.\n\nTheir interest was so deep that the younger ones whispered when her back was turned—\n\n“How pretty she is; and how that best frock do set her off! I believe it cost an immense deal, and that it was a gift from him.”\n\nTess, who was reaching up to get the tea-things from the corner-cupboard, did not hear these commentaries. If she had heard them, she might soon have set her friends right on the matter. But her mother heard, and Joan’s simple vanity, having been denied the hope of a dashing marriage, fed itself as well as it could upon the sensation of a dashing flirtation. Upon the whole she felt gratified, even though such a limited and evanescent triumph should involve her daughter’s reputation; it might end in marriage yet, and in the warmth of her responsiveness to their admiration she invited her visitors to stay to tea.\n\nTheir chatter, their laughter, their good-humoured innuendoes, above all, their flashes and flickerings of envy, revived Tess’s spirits also; and, as the evening wore on, she caught the infection of their excitement, and grew almost gay. The marble hardness left her face, she moved with something of her old bounding step, and flushed in all her young beauty.\n\nAt moments, in spite of thought, she would reply to their inquiries with a manner of superiority, as if recognizing that her experiences in the field of courtship had, indeed, been slightly enviable. But so far was she from being, in the words of Robert South, “in love with her own ruin,” that the illusion was transient as lightning; cold reason came back to mock her spasmodic weakness; the ghastliness of her momentary pride would convict her, and recall her to reserved listlessness again.\n\nAnd the despondency of the next morning’s dawn, when it was no longer Sunday, but Monday; and no best clothes; and the laughing visitors were gone, and she awoke alone in her old bed, the innocent younger children breathing softly around her. In place of the excitement of her return, and the interest it had inspired, she saw before her a long and stony highway which she had to tread, without aid, and with little sympathy. Her depression was then terrible, and she could have hidden herself in a tomb.\n\nIn the course of a few weeks Tess revived sufficiently to show herself so far as was necessary to get to church one Sunday morning. She liked to hear the chanting—such as it was—and the old Psalms, and to join in the Morning Hymn. That innate love of melody, which she had inherited from her ballad-singing mother, gave the simplest music a power over her which could well-nigh drag her heart out of her bosom at times.\n\nTo be as much out of observation as possible for reasons of her own, and to escape the gallantries of the young men, she set out before the chiming began, and took a back seat under the gallery, close to the lumber, where only old men and women came, and where the bier stood on end among the churchyard tools.\n\nParishioners dropped in by twos and threes, deposited themselves in rows before her, rested three-quarters of a minute on their foreheads as if they were praying, though they were not; then sat up, and looked around. When the chants came on, one of her favourites happened to be chosen among the rest—the old double chant “Langdon”—but she did not know what it was called, though she would much have liked to know. She thought, without exactly wording the thought, how strange and god-like was a composer’s power, who from the grave could lead through sequences of emotion, which he alone had felt at first, a girl like her who had never heard of his name, and never would have a clue to his personality.\n\nThe people who had turned their heads turned them again as the service proceeded; and at last observing her, they whispered to each other. She knew what their whispers were about, grew sick at heart, and felt that she could come to church no more.\n\nThe bedroom which she shared with some of the children formed her retreat more continually than ever. Here, under her few square yards of thatch, she watched winds, and snows, and rains, gorgeous sunsets, and successive moons at their full. So close kept she that at length almost everybody thought she had gone away.\n\nThe only exercise that Tess took at this time was after dark; and it was then, when out in the woods, that she seemed least solitary. She knew how to hit to a hair’s-breadth that moment of evening when the light and the darkness are so evenly balanced that the constraint of day and the suspense of night neutralize each other, leaving absolute mental liberty. It is then that the plight of being alive becomes attenuated to its least possible dimensions. She had no fear of the shadows; her sole idea seemed to be to shun mankind—or rather that cold accretion called the world, which, so terrible in the mass, is so unformidable, even pitiable, in its units.\n\nOn these lonely hills and dales her quiescent glide was of a piece with the element she moved in. Her flexuous and stealthy figure became an integral part of the scene. At times her whimsical fancy would intensify natural processes around her till they seemed a part of her own story. Rather they became a part of it; for the world is only a psychological phenomenon, and what they seemed they were. The midnight airs and gusts, moaning amongst the tightly-wrapped buds and bark of the winter twigs, were formulae of bitter reproach. A wet day was the expression of irremediable grief at her weakness in the mind of some vague ethical being whom she could not class definitely as the God of her childhood, and could not comprehend as any other.\n\nBut this encompassment of her own characterization, based on shreds of convention, peopled by phantoms and voices antipathetic to her, was a sorry and mistaken creation of Tess’s fancy—a cloud of moral hobgoblins by which she was terrified without reason. It was they that were out of harmony with the actual world, not she. Walking among the sleeping birds in the hedges, watching the skipping rabbits on a moonlit warren, or standing under a pheasant-laden bough, she looked upon herself as a figure of Guilt intruding into the haunts of Innocence. But all the while she was making a distinction where there was no difference. Feeling herself in antagonism, she was quite in accord. She had been made to break an accepted social law, but no law known to the environment in which she fancied herself such an anomaly.\n\n# XIV\n\nIt was a hazy sunrise in August. The denser nocturnal vapours, attacked by the warm beams, were dividing and shrinking into isolated fleeces within hollows and coverts, where they waited till they should be dried away to nothing.\n\nThe sun, on account of the mist, had a curious sentient, personal look, demanding the masculine pronoun for its adequate expression. His present aspect, coupled with the lack of all human forms in the scene, explained the old-time heliolatries in a moment. One could feel that a saner religion had never prevailed under the sky. The luminary was a golden-haired, beaming, mild-eyed, God-like creature, gazing down in the vigour and intentness of youth upon an earth that was brimming with interest for him.\n\nHis light, a little later, broke though chinks of cottage shutters, throwing stripes like red-hot pokers upon cupboards, chests of drawers, and other furniture within; and awakening harvesters who were not already astir.\n\nBut of all ruddy things that morning the brightest were two broad arms of painted wood, which rose from the margin of yellow cornfield hard by Marlott village. They, with two others below, formed the revolving Maltese cross of the reaping-machine, which had been brought to the field on the previous evening to be ready for operations this day. The paint with which they were smeared, intensified in hue by the sunlight, imparted to them a look of having been dipped in liquid fire.\n\nThe field had already been “opened”; that is to say, a lane a few feet wide had been hand-cut through the wheat along the whole circumference of the field for the first passage of the horses and machine.\n\nTwo groups, one of men and lads, the other of women, had come down the lane just at the hour when the shadows of the eastern hedge-top struck the west hedge midway, so that the heads of the groups were enjoying sunrise while their feet were still in the dawn. They disappeared from the lane between the two stone posts which flanked the nearest field-gate.\n\nPresently there arose from within a ticking like the love-making of the grasshopper. The machine had begun, and a moving concatenation of three horses and the aforesaid long rickety machine was visible over the gate, a driver sitting upon one of the hauling horses, and an attendant on the seat of the implement. Along one side of the field the whole wain went, the arms of the mechanical reaper revolving slowly, till it passed down the hill quite out of sight. In a minute it came up on the other side of the field at the same equable pace; the glistening brass star in the forehead of the fore horse first catching the eye as it rose into view over the stubble, then the bright arms, and then the whole machine.\n\nThe narrow lane of stubble encompassing the field grew wider with each circuit, and the standing corn was reduced to a smaller area as the morning wore on. Rabbits, hares, snakes, rats, mice, retreated inwards as into a fastness, unaware of the ephemeral nature of their refuge, and of the doom that awaited them later in the day when, their covert shrinking to a more and more horrible narrowness, they were huddled together, friends and foes, till the last few yards of upright wheat fell also under the teeth of the unerring reaper, and they were every one put to death by the sticks and stones of the harvesters.\n\nThe reaping-machine left the fallen corn behind it in little heaps, each heap being of the quantity for a sheaf; and upon these the active binders in the rear laid their hands—mainly women, but some of them men in print shirts, and trousers supported round their waists by leather straps, rendering useless the two buttons behind, which twinkled and bristled with sunbeams at every movement of each wearer, as if they were a pair of eyes in the small of his back.\n\nBut those of the other sex were the most interesting of this company of binders, by reason of the charm which is acquired by woman when she becomes part and parcel of outdoor nature, and is not merely an object set down therein as at ordinary times. A field-man is a personality afield; a field-woman is a portion of the field; she had somehow lost her own margin, imbibed the essence of her surrounding, and assimilated herself with it.\n\nThe women—or rather girls, for they were mostly young—wore drawn cotton bonnets with great flapping curtains to keep off the sun, and gloves to prevent their hands being wounded by the stubble. There was one wearing a pale pink jacket, another in a cream-coloured tight-sleeved gown, another in a petticoat as red as the arms of the reaping-machine; and others, older, in the brown-rough “wropper” or over-all—the old-established and most appropriate dress of the field-woman, which the young ones were abandoning. This morning the eye returns involuntarily to the girl in the pink cotton jacket, she being the most flexuous and finely-drawn figure of them all. But her bonnet is pulled so far over her brow that none of her face is disclosed while she binds, though her complexion may be guessed from a stray twine or two of dark brown hair which extends below the curtain of her bonnet. Perhaps one reason why she seduces casual attention is that she never courts it, though the other women often gaze around them.\n\nHer binding proceeds with clock-like monotony. From the sheaf last finished she draws a handful of ears, patting their tips with her left palm to bring them even. Then, stooping low, she moves forward, gathering the corn with both hands against her knees, and pushing her left gloved hand under the bundle to meet the right on the other side, holding the corn in an embrace like that of a lover. She brings the ends of the bond together, and kneels on the sheaf while she ties it, beating back her skirts now and then when lifted by the breeze. A bit of her naked arm is visible between the buff leather of the gauntlet and the sleeve of her gown; and as the day wears on its feminine smoothness becomes scarified by the stubble and bleeds.\n\nAt intervals she stands up to rest, and to retie her disarranged apron, or to pull her bonnet straight. Then one can see the oval face of a handsome young woman with deep dark eyes and long heavy clinging tresses, which seem to clasp in a beseeching way anything they fall against. The cheeks are paler, the teeth more regular, the red lips thinner than is usual in a country-bred girl.\n\nIt is Tess Durbeyfield, otherwise d’Urberville, somewhat changed—the same, but not the same; at the present stage of her existence living as a stranger and an alien here, though it was no strange land that she was in. After a long seclusion she had come to a resolve to undertake outdoor work in her native village, the busiest season of the year in the agricultural world having arrived, and nothing that she could do within the house being so remunerative for the time as harvesting in the fields.\n\nThe movements of the other women were more or less similar to Tess’s, the whole bevy of them drawing together like dancers in a quadrille at the completion of a sheaf by each, every one placing her sheaf on end against those of the rest, till a shock, or “stitch” as it was here called, of ten or a dozen was formed.\n\nThey went to breakfast, and came again, and the work proceeded as before. As the hour of eleven drew near a person watching her might have noticed that every now and then Tess’s glance flitted wistfully to the brow of the hill, though she did not pause in her sheafing. On the verge of the hour the heads of a group of children, of ages ranging from six to fourteen, rose over the stubbly convexity of the hill.\n\nThe face of Tess flushed slightly, but still she did not pause.\n\nThe eldest of the comers, a girl who wore a triangular shawl, its corner draggling on the stubble, carried in her arms what at first sight seemed to be a doll, but proved to be an infant in long clothes. Another brought some lunch. The harvesters ceased working, took their provisions, and sat down against one of the shocks. Here they fell to, the men plying a stone jar freely, and passing round a cup.\n\nTess Durbeyfield had been one of the last to suspend her labours. She sat down at the end of the shock, her face turned somewhat away from her companions. When she had deposited herself a man in a rabbit-skin cap, and with a red handkerchief tucked into his belt, held the cup of ale over the top of the shock for her to drink. But she did not accept his offer. As soon as her lunch was spread she called up the big girl, her sister, and took the baby of her, who, glad to be relieved of the burden, went away to the next shock and joined the other children playing there. Tess, with a curiously stealthy yet courageous movement, and with a still rising colour, unfastened her frock and began suckling the child.\n\nThe men who sat nearest considerately turned their faces towards the other end of the field, some of them beginning to smoke; one, with absent-minded fondness, regretfully stroking the jar that would no longer yield a stream. All the women but Tess fell into animated talk, and adjusted the disarranged knots of their hair.\n\nWhen the infant had taken its fill, the young mother sat it upright in her lap, and looking into the far distance, dandled it with a gloomy indifference that was almost dislike; then all of a sudden she fell to violently kissing it some dozens of times, as if she could never leave off, the child crying at the vehemence of an onset which strangely combined passionateness with contempt.\n\n“She’s fond of that there child, though she mid pretend to hate en, and say she wishes the baby and her too were in the churchyard,” observed the woman in the red petticoat.\n\n“She’ll soon leave off saying that,” replied the one in buff. “Lord, ’tis wonderful what a body can get used to o’ that sort in time!”\n\n“A little more than persuading had to do wi’ the coming o’t, I reckon. There were they that heard a sobbing one night last year in The Chase; and it mid ha’ gone hard wi’ a certain party if folks had come along.”\n\n“Well, a little more, or a little less, ’twas a thousand pities that it should have happened to she, of all others. But ’tis always the comeliest! The plain ones be as safe as churches—hey, Jenny?” The speaker turned to one of the group who certainly was not ill-defined as plain.\n\nIt was a thousand pities, indeed; it was impossible for even an enemy to feel otherwise on looking at Tess as she sat there, with her flower-like mouth and large tender eyes, neither black nor blue nor grey nor violet; rather all those shades together, and a hundred others, which could be seen if one looked into their irises—shade behind shade—tint beyond tint—around pupils that had no bottom; an almost standard woman, but for the slight incautiousness of character inherited from her race.\n\nA resolution which had surprised herself had brought her into the fields this week for the first time during many months. After wearing and wasting her palpitating heart with every engine of regret that lonely inexperience could devise, common sense had illuminated her. She felt that she would do well to be useful again—to taste anew sweet independence at any price. The past was past; whatever it had been, it was no more at hand. Whatever its consequences, time would close over them; they would all in a few years be as if they had never been, and she herself grassed down and forgotten. Meanwhile the trees were just as green as before; the birds sang and the sun shone as clearly now as ever. The familiar surroundings had not darkened because of her grief, nor sickened because of her pain.\n\nShe might have seen that what had bowed her head so profoundly—the thought of the world’s concern at her situation—was founded on an illusion. She was not an existence, an experience, a passion, a structure of sensations, to anybody but herself. To all humankind besides, Tess was only a passing thought. Even to friends she was no more than a frequently passing thought. If she made herself miserable the livelong night and day it was only this much to them—“Ah, she makes herself unhappy.” If she tried to be cheerful, to dismiss all care, to take pleasure in the daylight, the flowers, the baby, she could only be this idea to them—“Ah, she bears it very well.” Moreover, alone in a desert island would she have been wretched at what had happened to her? Not greatly. If she could have been but just created, to discover herself as a spouseless mother, with no experience of life except as the parent of a nameless child, would the position have caused her to despair? No, she would have taken it calmly, and found pleasure therein. Most of the misery had been generated by her conventional aspect, and not by her innate sensations.\n\nWhatever Tess’s reasoning, some spirit had induced her to dress herself up neatly as she had formerly done, and come out into the fields, harvest-hands being greatly in demand just then. This was why she had borne herself with dignity, and had looked people calmly in the face at times, even when holding the baby in her arms.\n\nThe harvest-men rose from the shock of corn, and stretched their limbs, and extinguished their pipes. The horses, which had been unharnessed and fed, were again attached to the scarlet machine. Tess, having quickly eaten her own meal, beckoned to her eldest sister to come and take away the baby, fastened her dress, put on the buff gloves again, and stooped anew to draw a bond from the last completed sheaf for the tying of the next.\n\nIn the afternoon and evening the proceedings of the morning were continued, Tess staying on till dusk with the body of harvesters. Then they all rode home in one of the largest wagons, in the company of a broad tarnished moon that had risen from the ground to the eastwards, its face resembling the outworn gold-leaf halo of some worm-eaten Tuscan saint. Tess’s female companions sang songs, and showed themselves very sympathetic and glad at her reappearance out of doors, though they could not refrain from mischievously throwing in a few verses of the ballad about the maid who went to the merry green wood and came back a changed state. There are counterpoises and compensations in life; and the event which had made of her a social warning had also for the moment made her the most interesting personage in the village to many. Their friendliness won her still farther away from herself, their lively spirits were contagious, and she became almost gay.\n\nBut now that her moral sorrows were passing away a fresh one arose on the natural side of her which knew no social law. When she reached home it was to learn to her grief that the baby had been suddenly taken ill since the afternoon. Some such collapse had been probable, so tender and puny was its frame; but the event came as a shock nevertheless.\n\nThe baby’s offence against society in coming into the world was forgotten by the girl-mother; her soul’s desire was to continue that offence by preserving the life of the child. However, it soon grew clear that the hour of emancipation for that little prisoner of the flesh was to arrive earlier than her worst misgiving had conjectured. And when she had discovered this she was plunged into a misery which transcended that of the child’s simple loss. Her baby had not been baptized.\n\nTess had drifted into a frame of mind which accepted passively the consideration that if she should have to burn for what she had done, burn she must, and there was an end of it. Like all village girls, she was well grounded in the Holy Scriptures, and had dutifully studied the histories of Aholah and Aholibah, and knew the inferences to be drawn therefrom. But when the same question arose with regard to the baby, it had a very different colour. Her darling was about to die, and no salvation.\n\nIt was nearly bedtime, but she rushed downstairs and asked if she might send for the parson. The moment happened to be one at which her father’s sense of the antique nobility of his family was highest, and his sensitiveness to the smudge which Tess had set upon that nobility most pronounced, for he had just returned from his weekly booze at Rolliver’s Inn. No parson should come inside his door, he declared, prying into his affairs, just then, when, by her shame, it had become more necessary than ever to hide them. He locked the door and put the key in his pocket.\n\nThe household went to bed, and, distressed beyond measure, Tess retired also. She was continually waking as she lay, and in the middle of the night found that the baby was still worse. It was obviously dying—quietly and painlessly, but none the less surely.\n\nIn her misery she rocked herself upon the bed. The clock struck the solemn hour of one, that hour when fancy stalks outside reason, and malignant possibilities stand rock-firm as facts. She thought of the child consigned to the nethermost corner of hell, as its double doom for lack of baptism and lack of legitimacy; saw the arch-fiend tossing it with his three-pronged fork, like the one they used for heating the oven on baking days; to which picture she added many other quaint and curious details of torment sometimes taught the young in this Christian country. The lurid presentment so powerfully affected her imagination in the silence of the sleeping house that her nightgown became damp with perspiration, and the bedstead shook with each throb of her heart.\n\nThe infant’s breathing grew more difficult, and the mother’s mental tension increased. It was useless to devour the little thing with kisses; she could stay in bed no longer, and walked feverishly about the room.\n\n“O merciful God, have pity; have pity upon my poor baby!” she cried. “Heap as much anger as you want to upon me, and welcome; but pity the child!”\n\nShe leant against the chest of drawers, and murmured incoherent supplications for a long while, till she suddenly started up.\n\n“Ah! perhaps baby can be saved! Perhaps it will be just the same!”\n\nShe spoke so brightly that it seemed as though her face might have shone in the gloom surrounding her. She lit a candle, and went to a second and a third bed under the wall, where she awoke her young sisters and brothers, all of whom occupied the same room. Pulling out the washing-stand so that she could get behind it, she poured some water from a jug, and made them kneel around, putting their hands together with fingers exactly vertical. While the children, scarcely awake, awe-stricken at her manner, their eyes growing larger and larger, remained in this position, she took the baby from her bed—a child’s child—so immature as scarce to seem a sufficient personality to endow its producer with the maternal title. Tess then stood erect with the infant on her arm beside the basin; the next sister held the Prayer-Book open before her, as the clerk at church held it before the parson; and thus the girl set about baptizing her child.\n\nHer figure looked singularly tall and imposing as she stood in her long white nightgown, a thick cable of twisted dark hair hanging straight down her back to her waist. The kindly dimness of the weak candle abstracted from her form and features the little blemishes which sunlight might have revealed—the stubble scratches upon her wrists, and the weariness of her eyes—her high enthusiasm having a transfiguring effect upon the face which had been her undoing, showing it as a thing of immaculate beauty, with a touch of dignity which was almost regal. The little ones kneeling round, their sleepy eyes blinking and red, awaited her preparations full of a suspended wonder which their physical heaviness at that hour would not allow to become active.\n\nThe most impressed of them said:\n\n“Be you really going to christen him, Tess?”\n\nThe girl-mother replied in a grave affirmative.\n\n“What’s his name going to be?”\n\nShe had not thought of that, but a name suggested by a phrase in the book of Genesis came into her head as she proceeded with the baptismal service, and now she pronounced it:\n\n“SORROW, I baptize thee in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.”\n\nShe sprinkled the water, and there was silence.\n\n“Say ‘Amen,’ children.”\n\nThe tiny voices piped in obedient response, “Amen!”\n\nTess went on:\n\n“We receive this child”—and so forth—“and do sign him with the sign of the Cross.”\n\nHere she dipped her hand into the basin, and fervently drew an immense cross upon the baby with her forefinger, continuing with the customary sentences as to his manfully fighting against sin, the world, and the devil, and being a faithful soldier and servant unto his life’s end. She duly went on with the Lord’s Prayer, the children lisping it after her in a thin gnat-like wail, till, at the conclusion, raising their voices to clerk’s pitch, they again piped into silence, “Amen!”\n\nThen their sister, with much augmented confidence in the efficacy of the sacrament, poured forth from the bottom of her heart the thanksgiving that follows, uttering it boldly and triumphantly in the stopt-diapason note which her voice acquired when her heart was in her speech, and which will never be forgotten by those who knew her. The ecstasy of faith almost apotheosized her; it set upon her face a glowing irradiation, and brought a red spot into the middle of each cheek; while the miniature candle-flame inverted in her eye-pupils shone like a diamond. The children gazed up at her with more and more reverence, and no longer had a will for questioning. She did not look like Sissy to them now, but as a being large, towering, and awful—a divine personage with whom they had nothing in common.\n\nPoor Sorrow’s campaign against sin, the world, and the devil was doomed to be of limited brilliancy—luckily perhaps for himself, considering his beginnings. In the blue of the morning that fragile soldier and servant breathed his last, and when the other children awoke they cried bitterly, and begged Sissy to have another pretty baby.\n\nThe calmness which had possessed Tess since the christening remained with her in the infant’s loss. In the daylight, indeed, she felt her terrors about his soul to have been somewhat exaggerated; whether well founded or not, she had no uneasiness now, reasoning that if Providence would not ratify such an act of approximation she, for one, did not value the kind of heaven lost by the irregularity—either for herself or for her child.\n\nSo passed away Sorrow the Undesired—that intrusive creature, that bastard gift of shameless Nature, who respects not the social law; a waif to whom eternal Time had been a matter of days merely, who knew not that such things as years and centuries ever were; to whom the cottage interior was the universe, the week’s weather climate, new-born babyhood human existence, and the instinct to suck human knowledge.\n\nTess, who mused on the christening a good deal, wondered if it were doctrinally sufficient to secure a Christian burial for the child. Nobody could tell this but the parson of the parish, and he was a new-comer, and did not know her. She went to his house after dusk, and stood by the gate, but could not summon courage to go in. The enterprise would have been abandoned if she had not by accident met him coming homeward as she turned away. In the gloom she did not mind speaking freely.\n\n“I should like to ask you something, sir.”\n\nHe expressed his willingness to listen, and she told the story of the baby’s illness and the extemporized ordinance. “And now, sir,” she added earnestly, “can you tell me this—will it be just the same for him as if you had baptized him?”\n\nHaving the natural feelings of a tradesman at finding that a job he should have been called in for had been unskilfully botched by his customers among themselves, he was disposed to say no. Yet the dignity of the girl, the strange tenderness in her voice, combined to affect his nobler impulses—or rather those that he had left in him after ten years of endeavour to graft technical belief on actual scepticism. The man and the ecclesiastic fought within him, and the victory fell to the man.\n\n“My dear girl,” he said, “it will be just the same.”\n\n“Then will you give him a Christian burial?” she asked quickly.\n\nThe Vicar felt himself cornered. Hearing of the baby’s illness, he had conscientiously gone to the house after nightfall to perform the rite, and, unaware that the refusal to admit him had come from Tess’s father and not from Tess, he could not allow the plea of necessity for its irregular administration.\n\n“Ah—that’s another matter,” he said.\n\n“Another matter—why?” asked Tess, rather warmly.\n\n“Well—I would willingly do so if only we two were concerned. But I must not—for certain reasons.”\n\n“Just for once, sir!”\n\n“Really I must not.”\n\n“O sir!” She seized his hand as she spoke.\n\nHe withdrew it, shaking his head.\n\n“Then I don’t like you!” she burst out, “and I’ll never come to your church no more!”\n\n“Don’t talk so rashly.”\n\n“Perhaps it will be just the same to him if you don’t?... Will it be just the same? Don’t for God’s sake speak as saint to sinner, but as you yourself to me myself—poor me!”\n\nHow the Vicar reconciled his answer with the strict notions he supposed himself to hold on these subjects it is beyond a layman’s power to tell, though not to excuse. Somewhat moved, he said in this case also—\n\n“It will be just the same.”\n\nSo the baby was carried in a small deal box, under an ancient woman’s shawl, to the churchyard that night, and buried by lantern-light, at the cost of a shilling and a pint of beer to the sexton, in that shabby corner of God’s allotment where He lets the nettles grow, and where all unbaptized infants, notorious drunkards, suicides, and others of the conjecturally damned are laid. In spite of the untoward surroundings, however, Tess bravely made a little cross of two laths and a piece of string, and having bound it with flowers, she stuck it up at the head of the grave one evening when she could enter the churchyard without being seen, putting at the foot also a bunch of the same flowers in a little jar of water to keep them alive. What matter was it that on the outside of the jar the eye of mere observation noted the words “Keelwell’s Marmalade”? The eye of maternal affection did not see them in its vision of higher things.\n\n# XV\n\n“By experience,” says Roger Ascham, “we find out a short way by a long wandering.” Not seldom that long wandering unfits us for further travel, and of what use is our experience to us then? Tess Durbeyfield’s experience was of this incapacitating kind. At last she had learned what to do; but who would now accept her doing?\n\nIf before going to the d’Urbervilles’ she had vigorously moved under the guidance of sundry gnomic texts and phrases known to her and to the world in general, no doubt she would never have been imposed on. But it had not been in Tess’s power—nor is it in anybody’s power—to feel the whole truth of golden opinions while it is possible to profit by them. She—and how many more—might have ironically said to God with Saint Augustine: “Thou hast counselled a better course than Thou hast permitted.”\n\nShe remained at her father’s house during the winter months, plucking fowls, or cramming turkeys and geese, or making clothes for her sisters and brothers out of some finery which d’Urberville had given her, and she had put by with contempt. Apply to him she would not. But she would often clasp her hands behind her head and muse when she was supposed to be working hard.\n\nShe philosophically noted dates as they came past in the revolution of the year; the disastrous night of her undoing at Trantridge with its dark background of The Chase; also the dates of the baby’s birth and death; also her own birthday; and every other day individualized by incidents in which she had taken some share. She suddenly thought one afternoon, when looking in the glass at her fairness, that there was yet another date, of greater importance to her than those; that of her own death, when all these charms would have disappeared; a day which lay sly and unseen among all the other days of the year, giving no sign or sound when she annually passed over it; but not the less surely there. When was it? Why did she not feel the chill of each yearly encounter with such a cold relation? She had Jeremy Taylor’s thought that some time in the future those who had known her would say: “It is the ——th, the day that poor Tess Durbeyfield died”; and there would be nothing singular to their minds in the statement. Of that day, doomed to be her terminus in time through all the ages, she did not know the place in month, week, season or year.\n\nAlmost at a leap Tess thus changed from simple girl to complex woman. Symbols of reflectiveness passed into her face, and a note of tragedy at times into her voice. Her eyes grew larger and more eloquent. She became what would have been called a fine creature; her aspect was fair and arresting; her soul that of a woman whom the turbulent experiences of the last year or two had quite failed to demoralize. But for the world’s opinion those experiences would have been simply a liberal education.\n\nShe had held so aloof of late that her trouble, never generally known, was nearly forgotten in Marlott. But it became evident to her that she could never be really comfortable again in a place which had seen the collapse of her family’s attempt to “claim kin”—and, through her, even closer union—with the rich d’Urbervilles. At least she could not be comfortable there till long years should have obliterated her keen consciousness of it. Yet even now Tess felt the pulse of hopeful life still warm within her; she might be happy in some nook which had no memories. To escape the past and all that appertained thereto was to annihilate it, and to do that she would have to get away.\n\nWas once lost always lost really true of chastity? she would ask herself. She might prove it false if she could veil bygones. The recuperative power which pervaded organic nature was surely not denied to maidenhood alone.\n\nShe waited a long time without finding opportunity for a new departure. A particularly fine spring came round, and the stir of germination was almost audible in the buds; it moved her, as it moved the wild animals, and made her passionate to go. At last, one day in early May, a letter reached her from a former friend of her mother’s, to whom she had addressed inquiries long before—a person whom she had never seen—that a skilful milkmaid was required at a dairy-house many miles to the southward, and that the dairyman would be glad to have her for the summer months.\n\nIt was not quite so far off as could have been wished; but it was probably far enough, her radius of movement and repute having been so small. To persons of limited spheres, miles are as geographical degrees, parishes as counties, counties as provinces and kingdoms.\n\nOn one point she was resolved: there should be no more d’Urberville air-castles in the dreams and deeds of her new life. She would be the dairymaid Tess, and nothing more. Her mother knew Tess’s feeling on this point so well, though no words had passed between them on the subject, that she never alluded to the knightly ancestry now.\n\nYet such is human inconsistency that one of the interests of the new place to her was the accidental virtues of its lying near her forefathers’ country (for they were not Blakemore men, though her mother was Blakemore to the bone). The dairy called Talbothays, for which she was bound, stood not remotely from some of the former estates of the d’Urbervilles, near the great family vaults of her granddames and their powerful husbands. She would be able to look at them, and think not only that d’Urberville, like Babylon, had fallen, but that the individual innocence of a humble descendant could lapse as silently. All the while she wondered if any strange good thing might come of her being in her ancestral land; and some spirit within her rose automatically as the sap in the twigs. It was unexpected youth, surging up anew after its temporary check, and bringing with it hope, and the invincible instinct towards self-delight.\nEnd of Phase the Second\n\nThe Rally\n\n# XVI\n\nOn a thyme-scented, bird-hatching morning in May, between two and three years after the return from Trantridge—silent, reconstructive years for Tess Durbeyfield—she left her home for the second time.\n\nHaving packed up her luggage so that it could be sent to her later, she started in a hired trap for the little town of Stourcastle, through which it was necessary to pass on her journey, now in a direction almost opposite to that of her first adventuring. On the curve of the nearest hill she looked back regretfully at Marlott and her father’s house, although she had been so anxious to get away.\n\nHer kindred dwelling there would probably continue their daily lives as heretofore, with no great diminution of pleasure in their consciousness, although she would be far off, and they deprived of her smile. In a few days the children would engage in their games as merrily as ever, without the sense of any gap left by her departure. This leaving of the younger children she had decided to be for the best; were she to remain they would probably gain less good by her precepts than harm by her example.\n\nShe went through Stourcastle without pausing and onward to a junction of highways, where she could await a carrier’s van that ran to the south-west; for the railways which engirdled this interior tract of country had never yet struck across it. While waiting, however, there came along a farmer in his spring cart, driving approximately in the direction that she wished to pursue. Though he was a stranger to her she accepted his offer of a seat beside him, ignoring that its motive was a mere tribute to her countenance. He was going to Weatherbury, and by accompanying him thither she could walk the remainder of the distance instead of travelling in the van by way of Casterbridge.\n\nTess did not stop at Weatherbury, after this long drive, further than to make a slight nondescript meal at noon at a cottage to which the farmer recommended her. Thence she started on foot, basket in hand, to reach the wide upland of heath dividing this district from the low-lying meads of a further valley in which the dairy stood that was the aim and end of her day’s pilgrimage.\n\nTess had never before visited this part of the country, and yet she felt akin to the landscape. Not so very far to the left of her she could discern a dark patch in the scenery, which inquiry confirmed her in supposing to be trees marking the environs of Kingsbere—in the church of which parish the bones of her ancestors—her useless ancestors—lay entombed.\n\nShe had no admiration for them now; she almost hated them for the dance they had led her; not a thing of all that had been theirs did she retain but the old seal and spoon. “Pooh—I have as much of mother as father in me!” she said. “All my prettiness comes from her, and she was only a dairymaid.”\n\nThe journey over the intervening uplands and lowlands of Egdon, when she reached them, was a more troublesome walk than she had anticipated, the distance being actually but a few miles. It was two hours, owing to sundry wrong turnings, ere she found herself on a summit commanding the long-sought-for vale, the Valley of the Great Dairies, the valley in which milk and butter grew to rankness, and were produced more profusely, if less delicately, than at her home—the verdant plain so well watered by the river Var or Froom.\n\nIt was intrinsically different from the Vale of Little Dairies, Blackmoor Vale, which, save during her disastrous sojourn at Trantridge, she had exclusively known till now. The world was drawn to a larger pattern here. The enclosures numbered fifty acres instead of ten, the farmsteads were more extended, the groups of cattle formed tribes hereabout; there only families. These myriads of cows stretching under her eyes from the far east to the far west outnumbered any she had ever seen at one glance before. The green lea was speckled as thickly with them as a canvas by Van Alsloot or Sallaert with burghers. The ripe hue of the red and dun kine absorbed the evening sunlight, which the white-coated animals returned to the eye in rays almost dazzling, even at the distant elevation on which she stood.\n\nThe bird’s-eye perspective before her was not so luxuriantly beautiful, perhaps, as that other one which she knew so well; yet it was more cheering. It lacked the intensely blue atmosphere of the rival vale, and its heavy soils and scents; the new air was clear, bracing, ethereal. The river itself, which nourished the grass and cows of these renowned dairies, flowed not like the streams in Blackmoor. Those were slow, silent, often turbid; flowing over beds of mud into which the incautious wader might sink and vanish unawares. The Froom waters were clear as the pure River of Life shown to the Evangelist, rapid as the shadow of a cloud, with pebbly shallows that prattled to the sky all day long. There the water-flower was the lily; the crow-foot here.\n\nEither the change in the quality of the air from heavy to light, or the sense of being amid new scenes where there were no invidious eyes upon her, sent up her spirits wonderfully. Her hopes mingled with the sunshine in an ideal photosphere which surrounded her as she bounded along against the soft south wind. She heard a pleasant voice in every breeze, and in every bird’s note seemed to lurk a joy.\n\nHer face had latterly changed with changing states of mind, continually fluctuating between beauty and ordinariness, according as the thoughts were gay or grave. One day she was pink and flawless; another pale and tragical. When she was pink she was feeling less than when pale; her more perfect beauty accorded with her less elevated mood; her more intense mood with her less perfect beauty. It was her best face physically that was now set against the south wind.\n\nThe irresistible, universal, automatic tendency to find sweet pleasure somewhere, which pervades all life, from the meanest to the highest, had at length mastered Tess. Being even now only a young woman of twenty, one who mentally and sentimentally had not finished growing, it was impossible that any event should have left upon her an impression that was not in time capable of transmutation.\n\nAnd thus her spirits, and her thankfulness, and her hopes, rose higher and higher. She tried several ballads, but found them inadequate; till, recollecting the psalter that her eyes had so often wandered over of a Sunday morning before she had eaten of the tree of knowledge, she chanted: “O ye Sun and Moon ... O ye Stars ... ye Green Things upon the Earth ... ye Fowls of the Air ... Beasts and Cattle ... Children of Men ... bless ye the Lord, praise Him and magnify Him forever!”\n\nShe suddenly stopped and murmured: “But perhaps I don’t quite know the Lord as yet.”\n\nAnd probably the half-unconscious rhapsody was a Fetishistic utterance in a Monotheistic setting; women whose chief companions are the forms and forces of outdoor Nature retain in their souls far more of the Pagan fantasy of their remote forefathers than of the systematized religion taught their race at later date. However, Tess found at least approximate expression for her feelings in the old _Benedicite_ that she had lisped from infancy; and it was enough. Such high contentment with such a slight initial performance as that of having started towards a means of independent living was a part of the Durbeyfield temperament. Tess really wished to walk uprightly, while her father did nothing of the kind; but she resembled him in being content with immediate and small achievements, and in having no mind for laborious effort towards such petty social advancement as could alone be effected by a family so heavily handicapped as the once powerful d’Urbervilles were now.\n\nThere was, it might be said, the energy of her mother’s unexpended family, as well as the natural energy of Tess’s years, rekindled after the experience which had so overwhelmed her for the time. Let the truth be told—women do as a rule live through such humiliations, and regain their spirits, and again look about them with an interested eye. While there’s life there’s hope is a conviction not so entirely unknown to the “betrayed” as some amiable theorists would have us believe.\n\nTess Durbeyfield, then, in good heart, and full of zest for life, descended the Egdon slopes lower and lower towards the dairy of her pilgrimage.\n\nThe marked difference, in the final particular, between the rival vales now showed itself. The secret of Blackmoor was best discovered from the heights around; to read aright the valley before her it was necessary to descend into its midst. When Tess had accomplished this feat she found herself to be standing on a carpeted level, which stretched to the east and west as far as the eye could reach.\n\nThe river had stolen from the higher tracts and brought in particles to the vale all this horizontal land; and now, exhausted, aged, and attenuated, lay serpentining along through the midst of its former spoils.\n\nNot quite sure of her direction, Tess stood still upon the hemmed expanse of verdant flatness, like a fly on a billiard-table of indefinite length, and of no more consequence to the surroundings than that fly. The sole effect of her presence upon the placid valley so far had been to excite the mind of a solitary heron, which, after descending to the ground not far from her path, stood with neck erect, looking at her.\n\nSuddenly there arose from all parts of the lowland a prolonged and repeated call—“Waow! waow! waow!”\n\nFrom the furthest east to the furthest west the cries spread as if by contagion, accompanied in some cases by the barking of a dog. It was not the expression of the valley’s consciousness that beautiful Tess had arrived, but the ordinary announcement of milking-time—half-past four o’clock, when the dairymen set about getting in the cows.\n\nThe red and white herd nearest at hand, which had been phlegmatically waiting for the call, now trooped towards the steading in the background, their great bags of milk swinging under them as they walked. Tess followed slowly in their rear, and entered the barton by the open gate through which they had entered before her. Long thatched sheds stretched round the enclosure, their slopes encrusted with vivid green moss, and their eaves supported by wooden posts rubbed to a glossy smoothness by the flanks of infinite cows and calves of bygone years, now passed to an oblivion almost inconceivable in its profundity. Between the post were ranged the milchers, each exhibiting herself at the present moment to a whimsical eye in the rear as a circle on two stalks, down the centre of which a switch moved pendulum-wise; while the sun, lowering itself behind this patient row, threw their shadows accurately inwards upon the wall. Thus it threw shadows of these obscure and homely figures every evening with as much care over each contour as if it had been the profile of a court beauty on a palace wall; copied them as diligently as it had copied Olympian shapes on marble _façades_ long ago, or the outline of Alexander, Caesar, and the Pharaohs.\n\nThey were the less restful cows that were stalled. Those that would stand still of their own will were milked in the middle of the yard, where many of such better behaved ones stood waiting now—all prime milchers, such as were seldom seen out of this valley, and not always within it; nourished by the succulent feed which the water-meads supplied at this prime season of the year. Those of them that were spotted with white reflected the sunshine in dazzling brilliancy, and the polished brass knobs of their horns glittered with something of military display. Their large-veined udders hung ponderous as sandbags, the teats sticking out like the legs of a gipsy’s crock; and as each animal lingered for her turn to arrive the milk oozed forth and fell in drops to the ground.\n\n# XVII\n\nThe dairymaids and men had flocked down from their cottages and out of the dairy-house with the arrival of the cows from the meads; the maids walking in pattens, not on account of the weather, but to keep their shoes above the mulch of the barton. Each girl sat down on her three-legged stool, her face sideways, her right cheek resting against the cow, and looked musingly along the animal’s flank at Tess as she approached. The male milkers, with hat-brims turned down, resting flat on their foreheads and gazing on the ground, did not observe her.\n\nOne of these was a sturdy middle-aged man—whose long white “pinner” was somewhat finer and cleaner than the wraps of the others, and whose jacket underneath had a presentable marketing aspect—the master-dairyman, of whom she was in quest, his double character as a working milker and butter maker here during six days, and on the seventh as a man in shining broad-cloth in his family pew at church, being so marked as to have inspired a rhyme:\n\nDairyman Dick All the week: On Sundays Mister Richard Crick.\n\nSeeing Tess standing at gaze he went across to her.\n\nThe majority of dairymen have a cross manner at milking time, but it happened that Mr Crick was glad to get a new hand—for the days were busy ones now—and he received her warmly; inquiring for her mother and the rest of the family—(though this as a matter of form merely, for in reality he had not been aware of Mrs Durbeyfield’s existence till apprised of the fact by a brief business-letter about Tess).\n\n“Oh—ay, as a lad I knowed your part o’ the country very well,” he said terminatively. “Though I’ve never been there since. And a aged woman of ninety that use to live nigh here, but is dead and gone long ago, told me that a family of some such name as yours in Blackmoor Vale came originally from these parts, and that ’twere a old ancient race that had all but perished off the earth—though the new generations didn’t know it. But, Lord, I took no notice of the old woman’s ramblings, not I.”\n\n“Oh no—it is nothing,” said Tess.\n\nThen the talk was of business only.\n\n“You can milk ’em clean, my maidy? I don’t want my cows going azew at this time o’ year.”\n\nShe reassured him on that point, and he surveyed her up and down. She had been staying indoors a good deal, and her complexion had grown delicate.\n\n“Quite sure you can stand it? ’Tis comfortable enough here for rough folk; but we don’t live in a cowcumber frame.”\n\nShe declared that she could stand it, and her zest and willingness seemed to win him over.\n\n“Well, I suppose you’ll want a dish o’ tay, or victuals of some sort, hey? Not yet? Well, do as ye like about it. But faith, if ’twas I, I should be as dry as a kex wi’ travelling so far.”\n\n“I’ll begin milking now, to get my hand in,” said Tess.\n\nShe drank a little milk as temporary refreshment—to the surprise—indeed, slight contempt—of Dairyman Crick, to whose mind it had apparently never occurred that milk was good as a beverage.\n\n“Oh, if ye can swaller that, be it so,” he said indifferently, while holding up the pail that she sipped from. “’Tis what I hain’t touched for years—not I. Rot the stuff; it would lie in my innerds like lead. You can try your hand upon she,” he pursued, nodding to the nearest cow. “Not but what she do milk rather hard. We’ve hard ones and we’ve easy ones, like other folks. However, you’ll find out that soon enough.”\n\nWhen Tess had changed her bonnet for a hood, and was really on her stool under the cow, and the milk was squirting from her fists into the pail, she appeared to feel that she really had laid a new foundation for her future. The conviction bred serenity, her pulse slowed, and she was able to look about her.\n\nThe milkers formed quite a little battalion of men and maids, the men operating on the hard-teated animals, the maids on the kindlier natures. It was a large dairy. There were nearly a hundred milchers under Crick’s management, all told; and of the herd the master-dairyman milked six or eight with his own hands, unless away from home. These were the cows that milked hardest of all; for his journey-milkmen being more or less casually hired, he would not entrust this half-dozen to their treatment, lest, from indifference, they should not milk them fully; nor to the maids, lest they should fail in the same way for lack of finger-grip; with the result that in course of time the cows would “go azew”—that is, dry up. It was not the loss for the moment that made slack milking so serious, but that with the decline of demand there came decline, and ultimately cessation, of supply.\n\nAfter Tess had settled down to her cow there was for a time no talk in the barton, and not a sound interfered with the purr of the milk-jets into the numerous pails, except a momentary exclamation to one or other of the beasts requesting her to turn round or stand still. The only movements were those of the milkers’ hands up and down, and the swing of the cows’ tails. Thus they all worked on, encompassed by the vast flat mead which extended to either slope of the valley—a level landscape compounded of old landscapes long forgotten, and, no doubt, differing in character very greatly from the landscape they composed now.\n\n“To my thinking,” said the dairyman, rising suddenly from a cow he had just finished off, snatching up his three-legged stool in one hand and the pail in the other, and moving on to the next hard-yielder in his vicinity, “to my thinking, the cows don’t gie down their milk to-day as usual. Upon my life, if Winker do begin keeping back like this, she’ll not be worth going under by midsummer.”\n\n“’Tis because there’s a new hand come among us,” said Jonathan Kail. “I’ve noticed such things afore.”\n\n“To be sure. It may be so. I didn’t think o’t.”\n\n“I’ve been told that it goes up into their horns at such times,” said a dairymaid.\n\n“Well, as to going up into their horns,” replied Dairyman Crick dubiously, as though even witchcraft might be limited by anatomical possibilities, “I couldn’t say; I certainly could not. But as nott cows will keep it back as well as the horned ones, I don’t quite agree to it. Do ye know that riddle about the nott cows, Jonathan? Why do nott cows give less milk in a year than horned?”\n\n“I don’t!” interposed the milkmaid, “Why do they?”\n\n“Because there bain’t so many of ’em,” said the dairyman. “Howsomever, these gam’sters do certainly keep back their milk to-day. Folks, we must lift up a stave or two—that’s the only cure for’t.”\n\nSongs were often resorted to in dairies hereabout as an enticement to the cows when they showed signs of withholding their usual yield; and the band of milkers at this request burst into melody—in purely business-like tones, it is true, and with no great spontaneity; the result, according to their own belief, being a decided improvement during the song’s continuance. When they had gone through fourteen or fifteen verses of a cheerful ballad about a murderer who was afraid to go to bed in the dark because he saw certain brimstone flames around him, one of the male milkers said—\n\n“I wish singing on the stoop didn’t use up so much of a man’s wind! You should get your harp, sir; not but what a fiddle is best.”\n\nTess, who had given ear to this, thought the words were addressed to the dairyman, but she was wrong. A reply, in the shape of “Why?” came as it were out of the belly of a dun cow in the stalls; it had been spoken by a milker behind the animal, whom she had not hitherto perceived.\n\n“Oh yes; there’s nothing like a fiddle,” said the dairyman. “Though I do think that bulls are more moved by a tune than cows—at least that’s my experience. Once there was an old aged man over at Mellstock—William Dewy by name—one of the family that used to do a good deal of business as tranters over there—Jonathan, do ye mind?—I knowed the man by sight as well as I know my own brother, in a manner of speaking. Well, this man was a coming home along from a wedding, where he had been playing his fiddle, one fine moonlight night, and for shortness’ sake he took a cut across Forty-acres, a field lying that way, where a bull was out to grass. The bull seed William, and took after him, horns aground, begad; and though William runned his best, and hadn’t _much_ drink in him (considering ’twas a wedding, and the folks well off), he found he’d never reach the fence and get over in time to save himself. Well, as a last thought, he pulled out his fiddle as he runned, and struck up a jig, turning to the bull, and backing towards the corner. The bull softened down, and stood still, looking hard at William Dewy, who fiddled on and on; till a sort of a smile stole over the bull’s face. But no sooner did William stop his playing and turn to get over hedge than the bull would stop his smiling and lower his horns towards the seat of William’s breeches. Well, William had to turn about and play on, willy-nilly; and ’twas only three o’clock in the world, and ’a knowed that nobody would come that way for hours, and he so leery and tired that ’a didn’t know what to do. When he had scraped till about four o’clock he felt that he verily would have to give over soon, and he said to himself, ‘There’s only this last tune between me and eternal welfare! Heaven save me, or I’m a done man.’ Well, then he called to mind how he’d seen the cattle kneel o’ Christmas Eves in the dead o’ night. It was not Christmas Eve then, but it came into his head to play a trick upon the bull. So he broke into the ’Tivity Hymm, just as at Christmas carol-singing; when, lo and behold, down went the bull on his bended knees, in his ignorance, just as if ’twere the true ’Tivity night and hour. As soon as his horned friend were down, William turned, clinked off like a long-dog, and jumped safe over hedge, before the praying bull had got on his feet again to take after him. William used to say that he’d seen a man look a fool a good many times, but never such a fool as that bull looked when he found his pious feelings had been played upon, and ’twas not Christmas Eve. Yes, William Dewy, that was the man’s name; and I can tell you to a foot where’s he a-lying in Mellstock Churchyard at this very moment—just between the second yew-tree and the north aisle.”\n\n“It’s a curious story; it carries us back to medieval times, when faith was a living thing!”\n\nThe remark, singular for a dairy-yard, was murmured by the voice behind the dun cow; but as nobody understood the reference, no notice was taken, except that the narrator seemed to think it might imply scepticism as to his tale.\n\n“Well, ’tis quite true, sir, whether or no. I knowed the man well.”\n\n“Oh yes; I have no doubt of it,” said the person behind the dun cow.\n\nTess’s attention was thus attracted to the dairyman’s interlocutor, of whom she could see but the merest patch, owing to his burying his head so persistently in the flank of the milcher. She could not understand why he should be addressed as “sir” even by the dairyman himself. But no explanation was discernible; he remained under the cow long enough to have milked three, uttering a private ejaculation now and then, as if he could not get on.\n\n“Take it gentle, sir; take it gentle,” said the dairyman. “’Tis knack, not strength, that does it.”\n\n“So I find,” said the other, standing up at last and stretching his arms. “I think I have finished her, however, though she made my fingers ache.”\n\nTess could then see him at full length. He wore the ordinary white pinner and leather leggings of a dairy-farmer when milking, and his boots were clogged with the mulch of the yard; but this was all his local livery. Beneath it was something educated, reserved, subtle, sad, differing.\n\nBut the details of his aspect were temporarily thrust aside by the discovery that he was one whom she had seen before. Such vicissitudes had Tess passed through since that time that for a moment she could not remember where she had met him; and then it flashed upon her that he was the pedestrian who had joined in the club-dance at Marlott—the passing stranger who had come she knew not whence, had danced with others but not with her, and slightingly left her, and gone on his way with his friends.\n\nThe flood of memories brought back by this revival of an incident anterior to her troubles produced a momentary dismay lest, recognizing her also, he should by some means discover her story. But it passed away when she found no sign of remembrance in him. She saw by degrees that since their first and only encounter his mobile face had grown more thoughtful, and had acquired a young man’s shapely moustache and beard—the latter of the palest straw colour where it began upon his cheeks, and deepening to a warm brown farther from its root. Under his linen milking-pinner he wore a dark velveteen jacket, cord breeches and gaiters, and a starched white shirt. Without the milking-gear nobody could have guessed what he was. He might with equal probability have been an eccentric landowner or a gentlemanly ploughman. That he was but a novice at dairy work she had realized in a moment, from the time he had spent upon the milking of one cow.\n\nMeanwhile many of the milkmaids had said to one another of the newcomer, “How pretty she is!” with something of real generosity and admiration, though with a half hope that the auditors would qualify the assertion—which, strictly speaking, they might have done, prettiness being an inexact definition of what struck the eye in Tess. When the milking was finished for the evening they straggled indoors, where Mrs Crick, the dairyman’s wife—who was too respectable to go out milking herself, and wore a hot stuff gown in warm weather because the dairymaids wore prints—was giving an eye to the leads and things.\n\nOnly two or three of the maids, Tess learnt, slept in the dairy-house besides herself, most of the helpers going to their homes. She saw nothing at supper-time of the superior milker who had commented on the story, and asked no questions about him, the remainder of the evening being occupied in arranging her place in the bed-chamber. It was a large room over the milk-house, some thirty feet long; the sleeping-cots of the other three indoor milkmaids being in the same apartment. They were blooming young women, and, except one, rather older than herself. By bedtime Tess was thoroughly tired, and fell asleep immediately.\n\nBut one of the girls, who occupied an adjoining bed, was more wakeful than Tess, and would insist upon relating to the latter various particulars of the homestead into which she had just entered. The girl’s whispered words mingled with the shades, and, to Tess’s drowsy mind, they seemed to be generated by the darkness in which they floated.\n\n“Mr Angel Clare—he that is learning milking, and that plays the harp—never says much to us. He is a pa’son’s son, and is too much taken up wi’ his own thoughts to notice girls. He is the dairyman’s pupil—learning farming in all its branches. He has learnt sheep-farming at another place, and he’s now mastering dairy-work.... Yes, he is quite the gentleman-born. His father is the Reverend Mr Clare at Emminster—a good many miles from here.”\n\n“Oh—I have heard of him,” said her companion, now awake. “A very earnest clergyman, is he not?”\n\n“Yes—that he is—the earnestest man in all Wessex, they say—the last of the old Low Church sort, they tell me—for all about here be what they call High. All his sons, except our Mr Clare, be made pa’sons too.”\n\nTess had not at this hour the curiosity to ask why the present Mr Clare was not made a parson like his brethren, and gradually fell asleep again, the words of her informant coming to her along with the smell of the cheeses in the adjoining cheeseloft, and the measured dripping of the whey from the wrings downstairs.\n\n# XVIII\n\nAngel Clare rises out of the past not altogether as a distinct figure, but as an appreciative voice, a long regard of fixed, abstracted eyes, and a mobility of mouth somewhat too small and delicately lined for a man’s, though with an unexpectedly firm close of the lower lip now and then; enough to do away with any inference of indecision. Nevertheless, something nebulous, preoccupied, vague, in his bearing and regard, marked him as one who probably had no very definite aim or concern about his material future. Yet as a lad people had said of him that he was one who might do anything if he tried.\n\nHe was the youngest son of his father, a poor parson at the other end of the county, and had arrived at Talbothays Dairy as a six months’ pupil, after going the round of some other farms, his object being to acquire a practical skill in the various processes of farming, with a view either to the Colonies or the tenure of a home-farm, as circumstances might decide.\n\nHis entry into the ranks of the agriculturists and breeders was a step in the young man’s career which had been anticipated neither by himself nor by others.\n\nMr Clare the elder, whose first wife had died and left him a daughter, married a second late in life. This lady had somewhat unexpectedly brought him three sons, so that between Angel, the youngest, and his father the Vicar there seemed to be almost a missing generation. Of these boys the aforesaid Angel, the child of his old age, was the only son who had not taken a University degree, though he was the single one of them whose early promise might have done full justice to an academical training.\n\nSome two or three years before Angel’s appearance at the Marlott dance, on a day when he had left school and was pursuing his studies at home, a parcel came to the Vicarage from the local bookseller’s, directed to the Reverend James Clare. The Vicar having opened it and found it to contain a book, read a few pages; whereupon he jumped up from his seat and went straight to the shop with the book under his arm.\n\n“Why has this been sent to my house?” he asked peremptorily, holding up the volume.\n\n“It was ordered, sir.”\n\n“Not by me, or any one belonging to me, I am happy to say.”\n\nThe shopkeeper looked into his order-book.\n\n“Oh, it has been misdirected, sir,” he said. “It was ordered by Mr Angel Clare, and should have been sent to him.”\n\nMr Clare winced as if he had been struck. He went home pale and dejected, and called Angel into his study.\n\n“Look into this book, my boy,” he said. “What do you know about it?”\n\n“I ordered it,” said Angel simply.\n\n“What for?”\n\n“To read.”\n\n“How can you think of reading it?”\n\n“How can I? Why—it is a system of philosophy. There is no more moral, or even religious, work published.”\n\n“Yes—moral enough; I don’t deny that. But religious!—and for _you_, who intend to be a minister of the Gospel!”\n\n“Since you have alluded to the matter, father,” said the son, with anxious thought upon his face, “I should like to say, once for all, that I should prefer not to take Orders. I fear I could not conscientiously do so. I love the Church as one loves a parent. I shall always have the warmest affection for her. There is no institution for whose history I have a deeper admiration; but I cannot honestly be ordained her minister, as my brothers are, while she refuses to liberate her mind from an untenable redemptive theolatry.”\n\nIt had never occurred to the straightforward and simple-minded Vicar that one of his own flesh and blood could come to this! He was stultified, shocked, paralysed. And if Angel were not going to enter the Church, what was the use of sending him to Cambridge? The University as a step to anything but ordination seemed, to this man of fixed ideas, a preface without a volume. He was a man not merely religious, but devout; a firm believer—not as the phrase is now elusively construed by theological thimble-riggers in the Church and out of it, but in the old and ardent sense of the Evangelical school: one who could\n\nIndeed opine That the Eternal and Divine Did, eighteen centuries ago In very truth...\n\nAngel’s father tried argument, persuasion, entreaty.\n\n“No, father; I cannot underwrite Article Four (leave alone the rest), taking it ‘in the literal and grammatical sense’ as required by the Declaration; and, therefore, I can’t be a parson in the present state of affairs,” said Angel. “My whole instinct in matters of religion is towards reconstruction; to quote your favorite Epistle to the Hebrews, ‘the removing of those things that are shaken, as of things that are made, that those things which cannot be shaken may remain.’”\n\nHis father grieved so deeply that it made Angel quite ill to see him.\n\n“What is the good of your mother and me economizing and stinting ourselves to give you a University education, if it is not to be used for the honour and glory of God?” his father repeated.\n\n“Why, that it may be used for the honour and glory of man, father.”\n\nPerhaps if Angel had persevered he might have gone to Cambridge like his brothers. But the Vicar’s view of that seat of learning as a stepping-stone to Orders alone was quite a family tradition; and so rooted was the idea in his mind that perseverance began to appear to the sensitive son akin to an intent to misappropriate a trust, and wrong the pious heads of the household, who had been and were, as his father had hinted, compelled to exercise much thrift to carry out this uniform plan of education for the three young men.\n\n“I will do without Cambridge,” said Angel at last. “I feel that I have no right to go there in the circumstances.”\n\nThe effects of this decisive debate were not long in showing themselves. He spent years and years in desultory studies, undertakings, and meditations; he began to evince considerable indifference to social forms and observances. The material distinctions of rank and wealth he increasingly despised. Even the “good old family” (to use a favourite phrase of a late local worthy) had no aroma for him unless there were good new resolutions in its representatives. As a balance to these austerities, when he went to live in London to see what the world was like, and with a view to practising a profession or business there, he was carried off his head, and nearly entrapped by a woman much older than himself, though luckily he escaped not greatly the worse for the experience.\n\nEarly association with country solitudes had bred in him an unconquerable, and almost unreasonable, aversion to modern town life, and shut him out from such success as he might have aspired to by following a mundane calling in the impracticability of the spiritual one. But something had to be done; he had wasted many valuable years; and having an acquaintance who was starting on a thriving life as a Colonial farmer, it occurred to Angel that this might be a lead in the right direction. Farming, either in the Colonies, America, or at home—farming, at any rate, after becoming well qualified for the business by a careful apprenticeship—that was a vocation which would probably afford an independence without the sacrifice of what he valued even more than a competency—intellectual liberty.\n\nSo we find Angel Clare at six-and-twenty here at Talbothays as a student of kine, and, as there were no houses near at hand in which he could get a comfortable lodging, a boarder at the dairyman’s.\n\nHis room was an immense attic which ran the whole length of the dairy-house. It could only be reached by a ladder from the cheese-loft, and had been closed up for a long time till he arrived and selected it as his retreat. Here Clare had plenty of space, and could often be heard by the dairy-folk pacing up and down when the household had gone to rest. A portion was divided off at one end by a curtain, behind which was his bed, the outer part being furnished as a homely sitting-room.\n\nAt first he lived up above entirely, reading a good deal, and strumming upon an old harp which he had bought at a sale, saying when in a bitter humour that he might have to get his living by it in the streets some day. But he soon preferred to read human nature by taking his meals downstairs in the general dining-kitchen, with the dairyman and his wife, and the maids and men, who all together formed a lively assembly; for though but few milking hands slept in the house, several joined the family at meals. The longer Clare resided here the less objection had he to his company, and the more did he like to share quarters with them in common.\n\nMuch to his surprise he took, indeed, a real delight in their companionship. The conventional farm-folk of his imagination—personified in the newspaper-press by the pitiable dummy known as Hodge—were obliterated after a few days’ residence. At close quarters no Hodge was to be seen. At first, it is true, when Clare’s intelligence was fresh from a contrasting society, these friends with whom he now hobnobbed seemed a little strange. Sitting down as a level member of the dairyman’s household seemed at the outset an undignified proceeding. The ideas, the modes, the surroundings, appeared retrogressive and unmeaning. But with living on there, day after day, the acute sojourner became conscious of a new aspect in the spectacle. Without any objective change whatever, variety had taken the place of monotonousness. His host and his host’s household, his men and his maids, as they became intimately known to Clare, began to differentiate themselves as in a chemical process. The thought of Pascal’s was brought home to him: “_A mesure qu’on a plus d’esprit, on trouve qu’il y a plus d’hommes originaux. Les gens du commun ne trouvent pas de différence entre les hommes_.” The typical and unvarying Hodge ceased to exist. He had been disintegrated into a number of varied fellow-creatures—beings of many minds, beings infinite in difference; some happy, many serene, a few depressed, one here and there bright even to genius, some stupid, others wanton, others austere; some mutely Miltonic, some potentially Cromwellian—into men who had private views of each other, as he had of his friends; who could applaud or condemn each other, amuse or sadden themselves by the contemplation of each other’s foibles or vices; men every one of whom walked in his own individual way the road to dusty death.\n\nUnexpectedly he began to like the outdoor life for its own sake, and for what it brought, apart from its bearing on his own proposed career. Considering his position he became wonderfully free from the chronic melancholy which is taking hold of the civilized races with the decline of belief in a beneficent Power. For the first time of late years he could read as his musings inclined him, without any eye to cramming for a profession, since the few farming handbooks which he deemed it desirable to master occupied him but little time.\n\nHe grew away from old associations, and saw something new in life and humanity. Secondarily, he made close acquaintance with phenomena which he had before known but darkly—the seasons in their moods, morning and evening, night and noon, winds in their different tempers, trees, waters and mists, shades and silences, and the voices of inanimate things.\n\nThe early mornings were still sufficiently cool to render a fire acceptable in the large room wherein they breakfasted; and, by Mrs Crick’s orders, who held that he was too genteel to mess at their table, it was Angel Clare’s custom to sit in the yawning chimney-corner during the meal, his cup-and-saucer and plate being placed on a hinged flap at his elbow. The light from the long, wide, mullioned window opposite shone in upon his nook, and, assisted by a secondary light of cold blue quality which shone down the chimney, enabled him to read there easily whenever disposed to do so. Between Clare and the window was the table at which his companions sat, their munching profiles rising sharp against the panes; while to the side was the milk-house door, through which were visible the rectangular leads in rows, full to the brim with the morning’s milk. At the further end the great churn could be seen revolving, and its slip-slopping heard—the moving power being discernible through the window in the form of a spiritless horse walking in a circle and driven by a boy.\n\nFor several days after Tess’s arrival Clare, sitting abstractedly reading from some book, periodical, or piece of music just come by post, hardly noticed that she was present at table. She talked so little, and the other maids talked so much, that the babble did not strike him as possessing a new note, and he was ever in the habit of neglecting the particulars of an outward scene for the general impression. One day, however, when he had been conning one of his music-scores, and by force of imagination was hearing the tune in his head, he lapsed into listlessness, and the music-sheet rolled to the hearth. He looked at the fire of logs, with its one flame pirouetting on the top in a dying dance after the breakfast-cooking and boiling, and it seemed to jig to his inward tune; also at the two chimney crooks dangling down from the cotterel, or cross-bar, plumed with soot, which quivered to the same melody; also at the half-empty kettle whining an accompaniment. The conversation at the table mixed in with his phantasmal orchestra till he thought: “What a fluty voice one of those milkmaids has! I suppose it is the new one.”\n\nClare looked round upon her, seated with the others.\n\nShe was not looking towards him. Indeed, owing to his long silence, his presence in the room was almost forgotten.\n\n“I don’t know about ghosts,” she was saying; “but I do know that our souls can be made to go outside our bodies when we are alive.”\n\nThe dairyman turned to her with his mouth full, his eyes charged with serious inquiry, and his great knife and fork (breakfasts were breakfasts here) planted erect on the table, like the beginning of a gallows.\n\n“What—really now? And is it so, maidy?” he said.\n\n“A very easy way to feel ’em go,” continued Tess, “is to lie on the grass at night and look straight up at some big bright star; and, by fixing your mind upon it, you will soon find that you are hundreds and hundreds o’ miles away from your body, which you don’t seem to want at all.”\n\nThe dairyman removed his hard gaze from Tess, and fixed it on his wife.\n\n“Now that’s a rum thing, Christianer—hey? To think o’ the miles I’ve vamped o’ starlight nights these last thirty year, courting, or trading, or for doctor, or for nurse, and yet never had the least notion o’ that till now, or feeled my soul rise so much as an inch above my shirt-collar.”\n\nThe general attention being drawn to her, including that of the dairyman’s pupil, Tess flushed, and remarking evasively that it was only a fancy, resumed her breakfast.\n\nClare continued to observe her. She soon finished her eating, and having a consciousness that Clare was regarding her, began to trace imaginary patterns on the tablecloth with her forefinger with the constraint of a domestic animal that perceives itself to be watched.\n\n“What a fresh and virginal daughter of Nature that milkmaid is!” he said to himself.\n\nAnd then he seemed to discern in her something that was familiar, something which carried him back into a joyous and unforeseeing past, before the necessity of taking thought had made the heavens gray. He concluded that he had beheld her before; where he could not tell. A casual encounter during some country ramble it certainly had been, and he was not greatly curious about it. But the circumstance was sufficient to lead him to select Tess in preference to the other pretty milkmaids when he wished to contemplate contiguous womankind.\n\n# XIX\n\nIn general the cows were milked as they presented themselves, without fancy or choice. But certain cows will show a fondness for a particular pair of hands, sometimes carrying this predilection so far as to refuse to stand at all except to their favourite, the pail of a stranger being unceremoniously kicked over.\n\nIt was Dairyman Crick’s rule to insist on breaking down these partialities and aversions by constant interchange, since otherwise, in the event of a milkman or maid going away from the dairy, he was placed in a difficulty. The maids’ private aims, however, were the reverse of the dairyman’s rule, the daily selection by each damsel of the eight or ten cows to which she had grown accustomed rendering the operation on their willing udders surprisingly easy and effortless.\n\nTess, like her compeers, soon discovered which of the cows had a preference for her style of manipulation, and her fingers having become delicate from the long domiciliary imprisonments to which she had subjected herself at intervals during the last two or three years, she would have been glad to meet the milchers’ views in this respect. Out of the whole ninety-five there were eight in particular—Dumpling, Fancy, Lofty, Mist, Old Pretty, Young Pretty, Tidy, and Loud—who, though the teats of one or two were as hard as carrots, gave down to her with a readiness that made her work on them a mere touch of the fingers. Knowing, however, the dairyman’s wish, she endeavoured conscientiously to take the animals just as they came, excepting the very hard yielders which she could not yet manage.\n\nBut she soon found a curious correspondence between the ostensibly chance position of the cows and her wishes in this matter, till she felt that their order could not be the result of accident. The dairyman’s pupil had lent a hand in getting the cows together of late, and at the fifth or sixth time she turned her eyes, as she rested against the cow, full of sly inquiry upon him.\n\n“Mr Clare, you have ranged the cows!” she said, blushing; and in making the accusation, symptoms of a smile gently lifted her upper lip in spite of her, so as to show the tips of her teeth, the lower lip remaining severely still.\n\n“Well, it makes no difference,” said he. “You will always be here to milk them.”\n\n“Do you think so? I _hope_ I shall! But I don’t _know_.”\n\nShe was angry with herself afterwards, thinking that he, unaware of her grave reasons for liking this seclusion, might have mistaken her meaning. She had spoken so earnestly to him, as if his presence were somehow a factor in her wish. Her misgiving was such that at dusk, when the milking was over, she walked in the garden alone, to continue her regrets that she had disclosed to him her discovery of his considerateness.\n\nIt was a typical summer evening in June, the atmosphere being in such delicate equilibrium and so transmissive that inanimate objects seemed endowed with two or three senses, if not five. There was no distinction between the near and the far, and an auditor felt close to everything within the horizon. The soundlessness impressed her as a positive entity rather than as the mere negation of noise. It was broken by the strumming of strings.\n\nTess had heard those notes in the attic above her head. Dim, flattened, constrained by their confinement, they had never appealed to her as now, when they wandered in the still air with a stark quality like that of nudity. To speak absolutely, both instrument and execution were poor; but the relative is all, and as she listened Tess, like a fascinated bird, could not leave the spot. Far from leaving she drew up towards the performer, keeping behind the hedge that he might not guess her presence.\n\nThe outskirt of the garden in which Tess found herself had been left uncultivated for some years, and was now damp and rank with juicy grass which sent up mists of pollen at a touch; and with tall blooming weeds emitting offensive smells—weeds whose red and yellow and purple hues formed a polychrome as dazzling as that of cultivated flowers. She went stealthily as a cat through this profusion of growth, gathering cuckoo-spittle on her skirts, cracking snails that were underfoot, staining her hands with thistle-milk and slug-slime, and rubbing off upon her naked arms sticky blights which, though snow-white on the apple-tree trunks, made madder stains on her skin; thus she drew quite near to Clare, still unobserved of him.\n\nTess was conscious of neither time nor space. The exaltation which she had described as being producible at will by gazing at a star came now without any determination of hers; she undulated upon the thin notes of the second-hand harp, and their harmonies passed like breezes through her, bringing tears into her eyes. The floating pollen seemed to be his notes made visible, and the dampness of the garden the weeping of the garden’s sensibility. Though near nightfall, the rank-smelling weed-flowers glowed as if they would not close for intentness, and the waves of colour mixed with the waves of sound.\n\nThe light which still shone was derived mainly from a large hole in the western bank of cloud; it was like a piece of day left behind by accident, dusk having closed in elsewhere. He concluded his plaintive melody, a very simple performance, demanding no great skill; and she waited, thinking another might be begun. But, tired of playing, he had desultorily come round the fence, and was rambling up behind her. Tess, her cheeks on fire, moved away furtively, as if hardly moving at all.\nAngel, however, saw her light summer gown, and he spoke; his low tones reaching her, though he was some distance off.\n\n“What makes you draw off in that way, Tess?” said he. “Are you afraid?”\n\n“Oh no, sir—not of outdoor things; especially just now when the apple-blooth is falling, and everything is so green.”\n\n“But you have your indoor fears—eh?”\n\n“Well—yes, sir.”\n\n“What of?”\n\n“I couldn’t quite say.”\n\n“The milk turning sour?”\n\n“No.”\n“Life in general?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Ah—so have I, very often. This hobble of being alive is rather serious, don’t you think so?”\n\n“It is—now you put it that way.”\n\n“All the same, I shouldn’t have expected a young girl like you to see it so just yet. How is it you do?”\n\nShe maintained a hesitating silence.\n\n“Come, Tess, tell me in confidence.”\n\nShe thought that he meant what were the aspects of things to her, and replied shyly—\n\n“The trees have inquisitive eyes, haven’t they?—that is, seem as if they had. And the river says,—‘Why do ye trouble me with your looks?’ And you seem to see numbers of to-morrows just all in a line, the first of them the biggest and clearest, the others getting smaller and smaller as they stand farther away; but they all seem very fierce and cruel and as if they said, ‘I’m coming! Beware of me! Beware of me!’ ... But _you_, sir, can raise up dreams with your music, and drive all such horrid fancies away!”\n\nHe was surprised to find this young woman—who though but a milkmaid had just that touch of rarity about her which might make her the envied of her housemates—shaping such sad imaginings. She was expressing in her own native phrases—assisted a little by her Sixth Standard training—feelings which might almost have been called those of the age—the ache of modernism. The perception arrested him less when he reflected that what are called advanced ideas are really in great part but the latest fashion in definition—a more accurate expression, by words in _logy_ and _ism_, of sensations which men and women have vaguely grasped for centuries.\n\nStill, it was strange that they should have come to her while yet so young; more than strange; it was impressive, interesting, pathetic. Not guessing the cause, there was nothing to remind him that experience is as to intensity, and not as to duration. Tess’s passing corporeal blight had been her mental harvest.\n\nTess, on her part, could not understand why a man of clerical family and good education, and above physical want, should look upon it as a mishap to be alive. For the unhappy pilgrim herself there was very good reason. But how could this admirable and poetic man ever have descended into the Valley of Humiliation, have felt with the man of Uz—as she herself had felt two or three years ago—“My soul chooseth strangling and death rather than my life. I loathe it; I would not live alway.”\n\nIt was true that he was at present out of his class. But she knew that was only because, like Peter the Great in a shipwright’s yard, he was studying what he wanted to know. He did not milk cows because he was obliged to milk cows, but because he was learning to be a rich and prosperous dairyman, landowner, agriculturist, and breeder of cattle. He would become an American or Australian Abraham, commanding like a monarch his flocks and his herds, his spotted and his ring-straked, his men-servants and his maids. At times, nevertheless, it did seem unaccountable to her that a decidedly bookish, musical, thinking young man should have chosen deliberately to be a farmer, and not a clergyman, like his father and brothers.\n\nThus, neither having the clue to the other’s secret, they were respectively puzzled at what each revealed, and awaited new knowledge of each other’s character and mood without attempting to pry into each other’s history.\n\nEvery day, every hour, brought to him one more little stroke of her nature, and to her one more of his. Tess was trying to lead a repressed life, but she little divined the strength of her own vitality.\n\nAt first Tess seemed to regard Angel Clare as an intelligence rather than as a man. As such she compared him with herself; and at every discovery of the abundance of his illuminations, of the distance between her own modest mental standpoint and the unmeasurable, Andean altitude of his, she became quite dejected, disheartened from all further effort on her own part whatever.\n\nHe observed her dejection one day, when he had casually mentioned something to her about pastoral life in ancient Greece. She was gathering the buds called “lords and ladies” from the bank while he spoke.\n\n“Why do you look so woebegone all of a sudden?” he asked.\n\n“Oh, ’tis only—about my own self,” she said, with a frail laugh of sadness, fitfully beginning to peel “a lady” meanwhile. “Just a sense of what might have been with me! My life looks as if it had been wasted for want of chances! When I see what you know, what you have read, and seen, and thought, I feel what a nothing I am! I’m like the poor Queen of Sheba who lived in the Bible. There is no more spirit in me.”\n\n“Bless my soul, don’t go troubling about that! Why,” he said with some enthusiasm, “I should be only too glad, my dear Tess, to help you to anything in the way of history, or any line of reading you would like to take up—”\n\n“It is a lady again,” interrupted she, holding out the bud she had peeled.\n\n“What?”\n\n“I meant that there are always more ladies than lords when you come to peel them.”\n\n“Never mind about the lords and ladies. Would you like to take up any course of study—history, for example?”\n\n“Sometimes I feel I don’t want to know anything more about it than I know already.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because what’s the use of learning that I am one of a long row only—finding out that there is set down in some old book somebody just like me, and to know that I shall only act her part; making me sad, that’s all. The best is not to remember that your nature and your past doings have been just like thousands’ and thousands’, and that your coming life and doings ’ll be like thousands’ and thousands’.”\n\n“What, really, then, you don’t want to learn anything?”\n\n“I shouldn’t mind learning why—why the sun do shine on the just and the unjust alike,” she answered, with a slight quaver in her voice. “But that’s what books will not tell me.”\n\n“Tess, fie for such bitterness!” Of course he spoke with a conventional sense of duty only, for that sort of wondering had not been unknown to himself in bygone days. And as he looked at the unpracticed mouth and lips, he thought that such a daughter of the soil could only have caught up the sentiment by rote. She went on peeling the lords and ladies till Clare, regarding for a moment the wave-like curl of her lashes as they dropped with her bent gaze on her soft cheek, lingeringly went away. When he was gone she stood awhile, thoughtfully peeling the last bud; and then, awakening from her reverie, flung it and all the crowd of floral nobility impatiently on the ground, in an ebullition of displeasure with herself for her _niaiseries_, and with a quickening warmth in her heart of hearts.\n\nHow stupid he must think her! In an access of hunger for his good opinion she bethought herself of what she had latterly endeavoured to forget, so unpleasant had been its issues—the identity of her family with that of the knightly d’Urbervilles. Barren attribute as it was, disastrous as its discovery had been in many ways to her, perhaps Mr Clare, as a gentleman and a student of history, would respect her sufficiently to forget her childish conduct with the lords and ladies if he knew that those Purbeck-marble and alabaster people in Kingsbere Church really represented her own lineal forefathers; that she was no spurious d’Urberville, compounded of money and ambition like those at Trantridge, but true d’Urberville to the bone.\n\nBut, before venturing to make the revelation, dubious Tess indirectly sounded the dairyman as to its possible effect upon Mr Clare, by asking the former if Mr Clare had any great respect for old county families when they had lost all their money and land.\n\n“Mr Clare,” said the dairyman emphatically, “is one of the most rebellest rozums you ever knowed—not a bit like the rest of his family; and if there’s one thing that he do hate more than another ’tis the notion of what’s called a’ old family. He says that it stands to reason that old families have done their spurt of work in past days, and can’t have anything left in ’em now. There’s the Billets and the Drenkhards and the Greys and the St Quintins and the Hardys and the Goulds, who used to own the lands for miles down this valley; you could buy ’em all up now for an old song a’most. Why, our little Retty Priddle here, you know, is one of the Paridelles—the old family that used to own lots o’ the lands out by King’s Hintock, now owned by the Earl o’ Wessex, afore even he or his was heard of. Well, Mr Clare found this out, and spoke quite scornful to the poor girl for days. ‘Ah!’ he says to her, ‘you’ll never make a good dairymaid! All your skill was used up ages ago in Palestine, and you must lie fallow for a thousand years to git strength for more deeds!’ A boy came here t’other day asking for a job, and said his name was Matt, and when we asked him his surname he said he’d never heard that ’a had any surname, and when we asked why, he said he supposed his folks hadn’t been ’stablished long enough. ‘Ah! you’re the very boy I want!’ says Mr Clare, jumping up and shaking hands wi’en; ‘I’ve great hopes of you;’ and gave him half-a-crown. O no! he can’t stomach old families!”\n\nAfter hearing this caricature of Clare’s opinion poor Tess was glad that she had not said a word in a weak moment about her family—even though it was so unusually old almost to have gone round the circle and become a new one. Besides, another diary-girl was as good as she, it seemed, in that respect. She held her tongue about the d’Urberville vault and the Knight of the Conqueror whose name she bore. The insight afforded into Clare’s character suggested to her that it was largely owing to her supposed untraditional newness that she had won interest in his eyes.\n\n# XX\n\nThe season developed and matured. Another year’s instalment of flowers, leaves, nightingales, thrushes, finches, and such ephemeral creatures, took up their positions where only a year ago others had stood in their place when these were nothing more than germs and inorganic particles. Rays from the sunrise drew forth the buds and stretched them into long stalks, lifted up sap in noiseless streams, opened petals, and sucked out scents in invisible jets and breathings.\n\nDairyman Crick’s household of maids and men lived on comfortably, placidly, even merrily. Their position was perhaps the happiest of all positions in the social scale, being above the line at which neediness ends, and below the line at which the _convenances_ begin to cramp natural feelings, and the stress of threadbare modishness makes too little of enough.\n\nThus passed the leafy time when arborescence seems to be the one thing aimed at out of doors. Tess and Clare unconsciously studied each other, ever balanced on the edge of a passion, yet apparently keeping out of it. All the while they were converging, under an irresistible law, as surely as two streams in one vale.\n\nTess had never in her recent life been so happy as she was now, possibly never would be so happy again. She was, for one thing, physically and mentally suited among these new surroundings. The sapling which had rooted down to a poisonous stratum on the spot of its sowing had been transplanted to a deeper soil. Moreover she, and Clare also, stood as yet on the debatable land between predilection and love; where no profundities have been reached; no reflections have set in, awkwardly inquiring, “Whither does this new current tend to carry me? What does it mean to my future? How does it stand towards my past?”\nTess was the merest stray phenomenon to Angel Clare as yet—a rosy, warming apparition which had only just acquired the attribute of persistence in his consciousness. So he allowed his mind to be occupied with her, deeming his preoccupation to be no more than a philosopher’s regard of an exceedingly novel, fresh, and interesting specimen of womankind.\n\nThey met continually; they could not help it. They met daily in that strange and solemn interval, the twilight of the morning, in the violet or pink dawn; for it was necessary to rise early, so very early, here. Milking was done betimes; and before the milking came the skimming, which began at a little past three. It usually fell to the lot of some one or other of them to wake the rest, the first being aroused by an alarm-clock; and, as Tess was the latest arrival, and they soon discovered that she could be depended upon not to sleep through the alarm as others did, this task was thrust most frequently upon her. No sooner had the hour of three struck and whizzed, than she left her room and ran to the dairyman’s door; then up the ladder to Angel’s, calling him in a loud whisper; then woke her fellow-milkmaids. By the time that Tess was dressed Clare was downstairs and out in the humid air. The remaining maids and the dairyman usually gave themselves another turn on the pillow, and did not appear till a quarter of an hour later.\n\nThe gray half-tones of daybreak are not the gray half-tones of the day’s close, though the degree of their shade may be the same. In the twilight of the morning, light seems active, darkness passive; in the twilight of evening it is the darkness which is active and crescent, and the light which is the drowsy reverse.\n\nBeing so often—possibly not always by chance—the first two persons to get up at the dairy-house, they seemed to themselves the first persons up of all the world. In these early days of her residence here Tess did not skim, but went out of doors at once after rising, where he was generally awaiting her. The spectral, half-compounded, aqueous light which pervaded the open mead impressed them with a feeling of isolation, as if they were Adam and Eve. At this dim inceptive stage of the day Tess seemed to Clare to exhibit a dignified largeness both of disposition and physique, an almost regnant power, possibly because he knew that at that preternatural time hardly any woman so well endowed in person as she was likely to be walking in the open air within the boundaries of his horizon; very few in all England. Fair women are usually asleep at mid-summer dawns. She was close at hand, and the rest were nowhere.\n\nThe mixed, singular, luminous gloom in which they walked along together to the spot where the cows lay often made him think of the Resurrection hour. He little thought that the Magdalen might be at his side. Whilst all the landscape was in neutral shade his companion’s face, which was the focus of his eyes, rising above the mist stratum, seemed to have a sort of phosphorescence upon it. She looked ghostly, as if she were merely a soul at large. In reality her face, without appearing to do so, had caught the cold gleam of day from the north-east; his own face, though he did not think of it, wore the same aspect to her.\n\nIt was then, as has been said, that she impressed him most deeply. She was no longer the milkmaid, but a visionary essence of woman—a whole sex condensed into one typical form. He called her Artemis, Demeter, and other fanciful names half teasingly, which she did not like because she did not understand them.\n\n“Call me Tess,” she would say askance; and he did.\n\nThen it would grow lighter, and her features would become simply feminine; they had changed from those of a divinity who could confer bliss to those of a being who craved it.\n\nAt these non-human hours they could get quite close to the waterfowl. Herons came, with a great bold noise as of opening doors and shutters, out of the boughs of a plantation which they frequented at the side of the mead; or, if already on the spot, hardily maintained their standing in the water as the pair walked by, watching them by moving their heads round in a slow, horizontal, passionless wheel, like the turn of puppets by clockwork.\n\nThey could then see the faint summer fogs in layers, woolly, level, and apparently no thicker than counterpanes, spread about the meadows in detached remnants of small extent. On the gray moisture of the grass were marks where the cows had lain through the night—dark-green islands of dry herbage the size of their carcasses, in the general sea of dew. From each island proceeded a serpentine trail, by which the cow had rambled away to feed after getting up, at the end of which trail they found her; the snoring puff from her nostrils, when she recognized them, making an intenser little fog of her own amid the prevailing one. Then they drove the animals back to the barton, or sat down to milk them on the spot, as the case might require.\n\nOr perhaps the summer fog was more general, and the meadows lay like a white sea, out of which the scattered trees rose like dangerous rocks. Birds would soar through it into the upper radiance, and hang on the wing sunning themselves, or alight on the wet rails subdividing the mead, which now shone like glass rods. Minute diamonds of moisture from the mist hung, too, upon Tess’s eyelashes, and drops upon her hair, like seed pearls. When the day grew quite strong and commonplace these dried off her; moreover, Tess then lost her strange and ethereal beauty; her teeth, lips, and eyes scintillated in the sunbeams and she was again the dazzlingly fair dairymaid only, who had to hold her own against the other women of the world.\n\nAbout this time they would hear Dairyman Crick’s voice, lecturing the non-resident milkers for arriving late, and speaking sharply to old Deborah Fyander for not washing her hands.\n\n“For Heaven’s sake, pop thy hands under the pump, Deb! Upon my soul, if the London folk only knowed of thee and thy slovenly ways, they’d swaller their milk and butter more mincing than they do a’ready; and that’s saying a good deal.”\n\nThe milking progressed, till towards the end Tess and Clare, in common with the rest, could hear the heavy breakfast table dragged out from the wall in the kitchen by Mrs Crick, this being the invariable preliminary to each meal; the same horrible scrape accompanying its return journey when the table had been cleared.\n\n# XXI\n\nThere was a great stir in the milk-house just after breakfast. The churn revolved as usual, but the butter would not come. Whenever this happened the dairy was paralyzed. Squish, squash echoed the milk in the great cylinder, but never arose the sound they waited for.\n\nDairyman Crick and his wife, the milkmaids Tess, Marian, Retty Priddle, Izz Huett, and the married ones from the cottages; also Mr Clare, Jonathan Kail, old Deborah, and the rest, stood gazing hopelessly at the churn; and the boy who kept the horse going outside put on moon-like eyes to show his sense of the situation. Even the melancholy horse himself seemed to look in at the window in inquiring despair at each walk round.\n\n“’Tis years since I went to Conjuror Trendle’s son in Egdon—years!” said the dairyman bitterly. “And he was nothing to what his father had been. I have said fifty times, if I have said once, that I _don’t_ believe in en; though ’a do cast folks’ waters very true. But I shall have to go to ’n if he’s alive. O yes, I shall have to go to ’n, if this sort of thing continnys!”\n\nEven Mr Clare began to feel tragical at the dairyman’s desperation.\n\n“Conjuror Fall, t’other side of Casterbridge, that they used to call ‘Wide-O’, was a very good man when I was a boy,” said Jonathan Kail. “But he’s rotten as touchwood by now.”\n\n“My grandfather used to go to Conjuror Mynterne, out at Owlscombe, and a clever man a’ were, so I’ve heard grandf’er say,” continued Mr Crick. “But there’s no such genuine folk about nowadays!”\n\nMrs Crick’s mind kept nearer to the matter in hand.\n\n“Perhaps somebody in the house is in love,” she said tentatively. “I’ve heard tell in my younger days that that will cause it. Why, Crick—that maid we had years ago, do ye mind, and how the butter didn’t come then—”\n\n“Ah yes, yes!—but that isn’t the rights o’t. It had nothing to do with the love-making. I can mind all about it—’twas the damage to the churn.”\n\nHe turned to Clare.\n\n“Jack Dollop, a ’hore’s-bird of a fellow we had here as milker at one time, sir, courted a young woman over at Mellstock, and deceived her as he had deceived many afore. But he had another sort o’ woman to reckon wi’ this time, and it was not the girl herself. One Holy Thursday of all days in the almanack, we was here as we mid be now, only there was no churning in hand, when we zid the girl’s mother coming up to the door, wi’ a great brass-mounted umbrella in her hand that would ha’ felled an ox, and saying ‘Do Jack Dollop work here?—because I want him! I have a big bone to pick with he, I can assure ’n!’ And some way behind her mother walked Jack’s young woman, crying bitterly into her handkercher. ‘O Lard, here’s a time!’ said Jack, looking out o’ winder at ’em. ‘She’ll murder me! Where shall I get—where shall I—? Don’t tell her where I be!’ And with that he scrambled into the churn through the trap-door, and shut himself inside, just as the young woman’s mother busted into the milk-house. ‘The villain—where is he?’ says she. ‘I’ll claw his face for’n, let me only catch him!’ Well, she hunted about everywhere, ballyragging Jack by side and by seam, Jack lying a’most stifled inside the churn, and the poor maid—or young woman rather—standing at the door crying her eyes out. I shall never forget it, never! ’Twould have melted a marble stone! But she couldn’t find him nowhere at all.”\n\nThe dairyman paused, and one or two words of comment came from the listeners.\n\nDairyman Crick’s stories often seemed to be ended when they were not really so, and strangers were betrayed into premature interjections of finality; though old friends knew better. The narrator went on—\n\n“Well, how the old woman should have had the wit to guess it I could never tell, but she found out that he was inside that there churn. Without saying a word she took hold of the winch (it was turned by handpower then), and round she swung him, and Jack began to flop about inside. ‘O Lard! stop the churn! let me out!’ says he, popping out his head. ‘I shall be churned into a pummy!’ (He was a cowardly chap in his heart, as such men mostly be). ‘Not till ye make amends for ravaging her virgin innocence!’ says the old woman. ‘Stop the churn you old witch!’ screams he. ‘You call me old witch, do ye, you deceiver!’ says she, ‘when ye ought to ha’ been calling me mother-law these last five months!’ And on went the churn, and Jack’s bones rattled round again. Well, none of us ventured to interfere; and at last ’a promised to make it right wi’ her. ‘Yes—I’ll be as good as my word!’ he said. And so it ended that day.”\n\nWhile the listeners were smiling their comments there was a quick movement behind their backs, and they looked round. Tess, pale-faced, had gone to the door.\n\n“How warm ’tis to-day!” she said, almost inaudibly.\n\nIt was warm, and none of them connected her withdrawal with the reminiscences of the dairyman. He went forward and opened the door for her, saying with tender raillery—\n\n“Why, maidy” (he frequently, with unconscious irony, gave her this pet name), “the prettiest milker I’ve got in my dairy; you mustn’t get so fagged as this at the first breath of summer weather, or we shall be finely put to for want of ’ee by dog-days, shan’t we, Mr Clare?”\n\n“I was faint—and—I think I am better out o’ doors,” she said mechanically; and disappeared outside.\n\nFortunately for her the milk in the revolving churn at that moment changed its squashing for a decided flick-flack.\n\n“’Tis coming!” cried Mrs Crick, and the attention of all was called off from Tess.\n\nThat fair sufferer soon recovered herself externally; but she remained much depressed all the afternoon. When the evening milking was done she did not care to be with the rest of them, and went out of doors, wandering along she knew not whither. She was wretched—O so wretched—at the perception that to her companions the dairyman’s story had been rather a humorous narration than otherwise; none of them but herself seemed to see the sorrow of it; to a certainty, not one knew how cruelly it touched the tender place in her experience. The evening sun was now ugly to her, like a great inflamed wound in the sky. Only a solitary cracked-voice reed-sparrow greeted her from the bushes by the river, in a sad, machine-made tone, resembling that of a past friend whose friendship she had outworn.\n\nIn these long June days the milkmaids, and, indeed, most of the household, went to bed at sunset or sooner, the morning work before milking being so early and heavy at a time of full pails. Tess usually accompanied her fellows upstairs. To-night, however, she was the first to go to their common chamber; and she had dozed when the other girls came in. She saw them undressing in the orange light of the vanished sun, which flushed their forms with its colour; she dozed again, but she was reawakened by their voices, and quietly turned her eyes towards them.\n\nNeither of her three chamber-companions had got into bed. They were standing in a group, in their nightgowns, barefooted, at the window, the last red rays of the west still warming their faces and necks and the walls around them. All were watching somebody in the garden with deep interest, their three faces close together: a jovial and round one, a pale one with dark hair, and a fair one whose tresses were auburn.\n\n“Don’t push! You can see as well as I,” said Retty, the auburn-haired and youngest girl, without removing her eyes from the window.\n\n“’Tis no use for you to be in love with him any more than me, Retty Priddle,” said jolly-faced Marian, the eldest, slily. “His thoughts be of other cheeks than thine!”\n\nRetty Priddle still looked, and the others looked again.\n\n“There he is again!” cried Izz Huett, the pale girl with dark damp hair and keenly cut lips.\n\n“You needn’t say anything, Izz,” answered Retty. “For I zid you kissing his shade.”\n\n_”What_ did you see her doing?” asked Marian.\n\n“Why—he was standing over the whey-tub to let off the whey, and the shade of his face came upon the wall behind, close to Izz, who was standing there filling a vat. She put her mouth against the wall and kissed the shade of his mouth; I zid her, though he didn’t.”\n\n“O Izz Huett!” said Marian.\n\nA rosy spot came into the middle of Izz Huett’s cheek.\n\n“Well, there was no harm in it,” she declared, with attempted coolness. “And if I be in love wi’en, so is Retty, too; and so be you, Marian, come to that.”\n\nMarian’s full face could not blush past its chronic pinkness.\n\n“I!” she said. “What a tale! Ah, there he is again! Dear eyes—dear face—dear Mr Clare!”\n\n“There—you’ve owned it!”\n\n“So have you—so have we all,” said Marian, with the dry frankness of complete indifference to opinion. “It is silly to pretend otherwise amongst ourselves, though we need not own it to other folks. I would just marry ’n to-morrow!”\n\n“So would I—and more,” murmured Izz Huett.\n\n“And I too,” whispered the more timid Retty.\n\nThe listener grew warm.\n\n“We can’t all marry him,” said Izz.\n\n“We shan’t, either of us; which is worse still,” said the eldest. “There he is again!”\n\nThey all three blew him a silent kiss.\n\n“Why?” asked Retty quickly.\n\n“Because he likes Tess Durbeyfield best,” said Marian, lowering her voice. “I have watched him every day, and have found it out.”\n\nThere was a reflective silence.\n\n“But she don’t care anything for ’n?” at length breathed Retty.\n\n“Well—I sometimes think that too.”\n\n“But how silly all this is!” said Izz Huett impatiently. “Of course he won’t marry any one of us, or Tess either—a gentleman’s son, who’s going to be a great landowner and farmer abroad! More likely to ask us to come wi’en as farm-hands at so much a year!”\n\nOne sighed, and another sighed, and Marian’s plump figure sighed biggest of all. Somebody in bed hard by sighed too. Tears came into the eyes of Retty Priddle, the pretty red-haired youngest—the last bud of the Paridelles, so important in the county annals. They watched silently a little longer, their three faces still close together as before, and the triple hues of their hair mingling. But the unconscious Mr Clare had gone indoors, and they saw him no more; and, the shades beginning to deepen, they crept into their beds. In a few minutes they heard him ascend the ladder to his own room. Marian was soon snoring, but Izz did not drop into forgetfulness for a long time. Retty Priddle cried herself to sleep.\n\nThe deeper-passioned Tess was very far from sleeping even then. This conversation was another of the bitter pills she had been obliged to swallow that day. Scarce the least feeling of jealousy arose in her breast. For that matter she knew herself to have the preference. Being more finely formed, better educated, and, though the youngest except Retty, more woman than either, she perceived that only the slightest ordinary care was necessary for holding her own in Angel Clare’s heart against these her candid friends. But the grave question was, ought she to do this? There was, to be sure, hardly a ghost of a chance for either of them, in a serious sense; but there was, or had been, a chance of one or the other inspiring him with a passing fancy for her, and enjoying the pleasure of his attentions while he stayed here. Such unequal attachments had led to marriage; and she had heard from Mrs Crick that Mr Clare had one day asked, in a laughing way, what would be the use of his marrying a fine lady, and all the while ten thousand acres of Colonial pasture to feed, and cattle to rear, and corn to reap. A farm-woman would be the only sensible kind of wife for him. But whether Mr Clare had spoken seriously or not, why should she, who could never conscientiously allow any man to marry her now, and who had religiously determined that she never would be tempted to do so, draw off Mr Clare’s attention from other women, for the brief happiness of sunning herself in his eyes while he remained at Talbothays?\n\n# XXII\n\nThey came downstairs yawning next morning; but skimming and milking were proceeded with as usual, and they went indoors to breakfast. Dairyman Crick was discovered stamping about the house. He had received a letter, in which a customer had complained that the butter had a twang.\n\n“And begad, so ’t have!” said the dairyman, who held in his left hand a wooden slice on which a lump of butter was stuck. “Yes—taste for yourself!”\n\nSeveral of them gathered round him; and Mr Clare tasted, Tess tasted, also the other indoor milkmaids, one or two of the milking-men, and last of all Mrs Crick, who came out from the waiting breakfast-table. There certainly was a twang.\n\nThe dairyman, who had thrown himself into abstraction to better realize the taste, and so divine the particular species of noxious weed to which it appertained, suddenly exclaimed—\n\n“’Tis garlic! and I thought there wasn’t a blade left in that mead!”\n\nThen all the old hands remembered that a certain dry mead, into which a few of the cows had been admitted of late, had, in years gone by, spoilt the butter in the same way. The dairyman had not recognized the taste at that time, and thought the butter bewitched.\n\n“We must overhaul that mead,” he resumed; “this mustn’t continny!”\n\nAll having armed themselves with old pointed knives, they went out together. As the inimical plant could only be present in very microscopic dimensions to have escaped ordinary observation, to find it seemed rather a hopeless attempt in the stretch of rich grass before them. However, they formed themselves into line, all assisting, owing to the importance of the search; the dairyman at the upper end with Mr Clare, who had volunteered to help; then Tess, Marian, Izz Huett, and Retty; then Bill Lewell, Jonathan, and the married dairywomen—Beck Knibbs, with her wooly black hair and rolling eyes; and flaxen Frances, consumptive from the winter damps of the water-meads—who lived in their respective cottages.\n\nWith eyes fixed upon the ground they crept slowly across a strip of the field, returning a little further down in such a manner that, when they should have finished, not a single inch of the pasture but would have fallen under the eye of some one of them. It was a most tedious business, not more than half a dozen shoots of garlic being discoverable in the whole field; yet such was the herb’s pungency that probably one bite of it by one cow had been sufficient to season the whole dairy’s produce for the day.\n\nDiffering one from another in natures and moods so greatly as they did, they yet formed, bending, a curiously uniform row—automatic, noiseless; and an alien observer passing down the neighbouring lane might well have been excused for massing them as “Hodge”. As they crept along, stooping low to discern the plant, a soft yellow gleam was reflected from the buttercups into their shaded faces, giving them an elfish, moonlit aspect, though the sun was pouring upon their backs in all the strength of noon.\n\nAngel Clare, who communistically stuck to his rule of taking part with the rest in everything, glanced up now and then. It was not, of course, by accident that he walked next to Tess.\n\n“Well, how are you?” he murmured.\n\n“Very well, thank you, sir,” she replied demurely.\n\nAs they had been discussing a score of personal matters only half-an-hour before, the introductory style seemed a little superfluous. But they got no further in speech just then. They crept and crept, the hem of her petticoat just touching his gaiter, and his elbow sometimes brushing hers. At last the dairyman, who came next, could stand it no longer.\n\n“Upon my soul and body, this here stooping do fairly make my back open and shut!” he exclaimed, straightening himself slowly with an excruciated look till quite upright. “And you, maidy Tess, you wasn’t well a day or two ago—this will make your head ache finely! Don’t do any more, if you feel fainty; leave the rest to finish it.”\n\nDairyman Crick withdrew, and Tess dropped behind. Mr Clare also stepped out of line, and began privateering about for the weed. When she found him near her, her very tension at what she had heard the night before made her the first to speak.\n\n“Don’t they look pretty?” she said.\n\n“Who?”\n\n“Izzy Huett and Retty.”\n\nTess had moodily decided that either of these maidens would make a good farmer’s wife, and that she ought to recommend them, and obscure her own wretched charms.\n\n“Pretty? Well, yes—they are pretty girls—fresh looking. I have often thought so.”\n\n“Though, poor dears, prettiness won’t last long!”\n\n“O no, unfortunately.”\n\n“They are excellent dairywomen.”\n\n“Yes: though not better than you.”\n\n“They skim better than I.”\n\n“Do they?”\n\nClare remained observing them—not without their observing him.\n\n“She is colouring up,” continued Tess heroically.\n“Who?”\n\n“Retty Priddle.”\n\n“Oh! Why it that?”\n\n“Because you are looking at her.”\n\nSelf-sacrificing as her mood might be, Tess could not well go further and cry, “Marry one of them, if you really do want a dairywoman and not a lady; and don’t think of marrying me!” She followed Dairyman Crick, and had the mournful satisfaction of seeing that Clare remained behind.\n\nFrom this day she forced herself to take pains to avoid him—never allowing herself, as formerly, to remain long in his company, even if their juxtaposition were purely accidental. She gave the other three every chance.\n\nTess was woman enough to realize from their avowals to herself that Angel Clare had the honour of all the dairymaids in his keeping, and her perception of his care to avoid compromising the happiness of either in the least degree bred a tender respect in Tess for what she deemed, rightly or wrongly, the self-controlling sense of duty shown by him, a quality which she had never expected to find in one of the opposite sex, and in the absence of which more than one of the simple hearts who were his house-mates might have gone weeping on her pilgrimage.\n\n# XXIII\n\nThe hot weather of July had crept upon them unawares, and the atmosphere of the flat vale hung heavy as an opiate over the dairy-folk, the cows, and the trees. Hot steaming rains fell frequently, making the grass where the cows fed yet more rank, and hindering the late hay-making in the other meads.\n\nIt was Sunday morning; the milking was done; the outdoor milkers had gone home. Tess and the other three were dressing themselves rapidly, the whole bevy having agreed to go together to Mellstock Church, which lay some three or four miles distant from the dairy-house. She had now been two months at Talbothays, and this was her first excursion.\n\nAll the preceding afternoon and night heavy thunderstorms had hissed down upon the meads, and washed some of the hay into the river; but this morning the sun shone out all the more brilliantly for the deluge, and the air was balmy and clear.\n\nThe crooked lane leading from their own parish to Mellstock ran along the lowest levels in a portion of its length, and when the girls reached the most depressed spot they found that the result of the rain had been to flood the lane over-shoe to a distance of some fifty yards. This would have been no serious hindrance on a week-day; they would have clicked through it in their high pattens and boots quite unconcerned; but on this day of vanity, this Sun’s-day, when flesh went forth to coquet with flesh while hypocritically affecting business with spiritual things; on this occasion for wearing their white stockings and thin shoes, and their pink, white, and lilac gowns, on which every mud spot would be visible, the pool was an awkward impediment. They could hear the church-bell calling—as yet nearly a mile off.\n\n“Who would have expected such a rise in the river in summer-time!” said Marian, from the top of the roadside bank on which they had climbed, and were maintaining a precarious footing in the hope of creeping along its slope till they were past the pool.\n\n“We can’t get there anyhow, without walking right through it, or else going round the Turnpike way; and that would make us so very late!” said Retty, pausing hopelessly.\n\n“And I do colour up so hot, walking into church late, and all the people staring round,” said Marian, “that I hardly cool down again till we get into the That-it-may-please-Thees.”\n\nWhile they stood clinging to the bank they heard a splashing round the bend of the road, and presently appeared Angel Clare, advancing along the lane towards them through the water.\n\nFour hearts gave a big throb simultaneously.\n\nHis aspect was probably as un-Sabbatarian a one as a dogmatic parson’s son often presented; his attire being his dairy clothes, long wading boots, a cabbage-leaf inside his hat to keep his head cool, with a thistle-spud to finish him off. “He’s not going to church,” said Marian.\n\n“No—I wish he was!” murmured Tess.\n\nAngel, in fact, rightly or wrongly (to adopt the safe phrase of evasive controversialists), preferred sermons in stones to sermons in churches and chapels on fine summer days. This morning, moreover, he had gone out to see if the damage to the hay by the flood was considerable or not. On his walk he observed the girls from a long distance, though they had been so occupied with their difficulties of passage as not to notice him. He knew that the water had risen at that spot, and that it would quite check their progress. So he had hastened on, with a dim idea of how he could help them—one of them in particular.\n\nThe rosy-cheeked, bright-eyed quartet looked so charming in their light summer attire, clinging to the roadside bank like pigeons on a roof-slope, that he stopped a moment to regard them before coming close. Their gauzy skirts had brushed up from the grass innumerable flies and butterflies which, unable to escape, remained caged in the transparent tissue as in an aviary. Angel’s eye at last fell upon Tess, the hindmost of the four; she, being full of suppressed laughter at their dilemma, could not help meeting his glance radiantly.\n\nHe came beneath them in the water, which did not rise over his long boots; and stood looking at the entrapped flies and butterflies.\n\n“Are you trying to get to church?” he said to Marian, who was in front, including the next two in his remark, but avoiding Tess.\n\n“Yes, sir; and ’tis getting late; and my colour do come up so—”\n\n“I’ll carry you through the pool—every Jill of you.”\n\nThe whole four flushed as if one heart beat through them.\n\n“I think you can’t, sir,” said Marian.\n\n“It is the only way for you to get past. Stand still. Nonsense—you are not too heavy! I’d carry you all four together. Now, Marian, attend,” he continued, “and put your arms round my shoulders, so. Now! Hold on. That’s well done.”\n\nMarian had lowered herself upon his arm and shoulder as directed, and Angel strode off with her, his slim figure, as viewed from behind, looking like the mere stem to the great nosegay suggested by hers. They disappeared round the curve of the road, and only his sousing footsteps and the top ribbon of Marian’s bonnet told where they were. In a few minutes he reappeared. Izz Huett was the next in order upon the bank.\n\n“Here he comes,” she murmured, and they could hear that her lips were dry with emotion. “And I have to put my arms round his neck and look into his face as Marian did.”\n\n“There’s nothing in that,” said Tess quickly.\n\n“There’s a time for everything,” continued Izz, unheeding. “A time to embrace, and a time to refrain from embracing; the first is now going to be mine.”\n\n“Fie—it is Scripture, Izz!”\n\n“Yes,” said Izz, “I’ve always a’ ear at church for pretty verses.”\n\nAngel Clare, to whom three-quarters of this performance was a commonplace act of kindness, now approached Izz. She quietly and dreamily lowered herself into his arms, and Angel methodically marched off with her. When he was heard returning for the third time Retty’s throbbing heart could be almost seen to shake her. He went up to the red-haired girl, and while he was seizing her he glanced at Tess. His lips could not have pronounced more plainly, “It will soon be you and I.” Her comprehension appeared in her face; she could not help it. There was an understanding between them.\n\nPoor little Retty, though by far the lightest weight, was the most troublesome of Clare’s burdens. Marian had been like a sack of meal, a dead weight of plumpness under which he has literally staggered. Izz had ridden sensibly and calmly. Retty was a bunch of hysterics.\nHowever, he got through with the disquieted creature, deposited her, and returned. Tess could see over the hedge the distant three in a group, standing as he had placed them on the next rising ground. It was now her turn. She was embarrassed to discover that excitement at the proximity of Mr Clare’s breath and eyes, which she had contemned in her companions, was intensified in herself; and as if fearful of betraying her secret, she paltered with him at the last moment.\n\n“I may be able to clim’ along the bank perhaps—I can clim’ better than they. You must be so tired, Mr Clare!”\n\n“No, no, Tess,” said he quickly. And almost before she was aware, she was seated in his arms and resting against his shoulder.\n\n“Three Leahs to get one Rachel,” he whispered.\n\n“They are better women than I,” she replied, magnanimously sticking to her resolve.\n\n“Not to me,” said Angel.\n\nHe saw her grow warm at this; and they went some steps in silence.\n\n“I hope I am not too heavy?” she said timidly.\n\n“O no. You should lift Marian! Such a lump. You are like an undulating billow warmed by the sun. And all this fluff of muslin about you is the froth.”\n\n“It is very pretty—if I seem like that to you.”\n\n“Do you know that I have undergone three-quarters of this labour entirely for the sake of the fourth quarter?”\n\n“No.”\n“I did not expect such an event to-day.”\n\n“Nor I... The water came up so sudden.”\n\nThat the rise in the water was what she understood him to refer to, the state of breathing belied. Clare stood still and inclinced his face towards hers.\n\n“O Tessy!” he exclaimed.\n\nThe girl’s cheeks burned to the breeze, and she could not look into his eyes for her emotion. It reminded Angel that he was somewhat unfairly taking advantage of an accidental position; and he went no further with it. No definite words of love had crossed their lips as yet, and suspension at this point was desirable now. However, he walked slowly, to make the remainder of the distance as long as possible; but at last they came to the bend, and the rest of their progress was in full view of the other three. The dry land was reached, and he set her down.\n\nHer friends were looking with round thoughtful eyes at her and him, and she could see that they had been talking of her. He hastily bade them farewell, and splashed back along the stretch of submerged road.\n\nThe four moved on together as before, till Marian broke the silence by saying—\n\n“No—in all truth; we have no chance against her!” She looked joylessly at Tess.\n\n“What do you mean?” asked the latter.\n\n“He likes ’ee best—the very best! We could see it as he brought ’ee. He would have kissed ’ee, if you had encouraged him to do it, ever so little.”\n\n“No, no,” said she.\n\nThe gaiety with which they had set out had somehow vanished; and yet there was no enmity or malice between them. They were generous young souls; they had been reared in the lonely country nooks where fatalism is a strong sentiment, and they did not blame her. Such supplanting was to be.\n\nTess’s heart ached. There was no concealing from herself the fact that she loved Angel Clare, perhaps all the more passionately from knowing that the others had also lost their hearts to him. There is contagion in this sentiment, especially among women. And yet that same hungry nature had fought against this, but too feebly, and the natural result had followed.\n\n“I will never stand in your way, nor in the way of either of you!” she declared to Retty that night in the bedroom (her tears running down). “I can’t help this, my dear! I don’t think marrying is in his mind at all; but if he were ever to ask me I should refuse him, as I should refuse any man.”\n\n“Oh! would you? Why?” said wondering Retty.\n\n“It cannot be! But I will be plain. Putting myself quite on one side, I don’t think he will choose either of you.”\n\n“I have never expected it—thought of it!” moaned Retty. “But O! I wish I was dead!”\n\nThe poor child, torn by a feeling which she hardly understood, turned to the other two girls who came upstairs just then.\n\n“We be friends with her again,” she said to them. “She thinks no more of his choosing her than we do.”\n\nSo the reserve went off, and they were confiding and warm.\n\n“I don’t seem to care what I do now,” said Marian, whose mood was turned to its lowest bass. “I was going to marry a dairyman at Stickleford, who’s asked me twice; but—my soul—I would put an end to myself rather’n be his wife now! Why don’t ye speak, Izz?”\n\n“To confess, then,” murmured Izz, “I made sure to-day that he was going to kiss me as he held me; and I lay still against his breast, hoping and hoping, and never moved at all. But he did not. I don’t like biding here at Talbothays any longer! I shall go hwome.”\n\nThe air of the sleeping-chamber seemed to palpitate with the hopeless passion of the girls. They writhed feverishly under the oppressiveness of an emotion thrust on them by cruel Nature’s law—an emotion which they had neither expected nor desired. The incident of the day had fanned the flame that was burning the inside of their hearts out, and the torture was almost more than they could endure. The differences which distinguished them as individuals were abstracted by this passion, and each was but portion of one organism called sex. There was so much frankness and so little jealousy because there was no hope. Each one was a girl of fair common sense, and she did not delude herself with any vain conceits, or deny her love, or give herself airs, in the idea of outshining the others. The full recognition of the futility of their infatuation, from a social point of view; its purposeless beginning; its self-bounded outlook; its lack of everything to justify its existence in the eye of civilization (while lacking nothing in the eye of Nature); the one fact that it did exist, ecstasizing them to a killing joy—all this imparted to them a resignation, a dignity, which a practical and sordid expectation of winning him as a husband would have destroyed.\n\nThey tossed and turned on their little beds, and the cheese-wring dripped monotonously downstairs.\n\n“B’ you awake, Tess?” whispered one, half-an-hour later.\n\nIt was Izz Huett’s voice.\n\nTess replied in the affirmative, whereupon also Retty and Marian suddenly flung the bedclothes off them, and sighed—\n\n“So be we!”\n\n“I wonder what she is like—the lady they say his family have looked out for him!”\n\n“I wonder,” said Izz.\n\n“Some lady looked out for him?” gasped Tess, starting. “I have never heard o’ that!”\n\n“O yes—’tis whispered; a young lady of his own rank, chosen by his family; a Doctor of Divinity’s daughter near his father’s parish of Emminster; he don’t much care for her, they say. But he is sure to marry her.”\n\nThey had heard so very little of this; yet it was enough to build up wretched dolorous dreams upon, there in the shade of the night. They pictured all the details of his being won round to consent, of the wedding preparations, of the bride’s happiness, of her dress and veil, of her blissful home with him, when oblivion would have fallen upon themselves as far as he and their love were concerned. Thus they talked, and ached, and wept till sleep charmed their sorrow away.\n\nAfter this disclosure Tess nourished no further foolish thought that there lurked any grave and deliberate import in Clare’s attentions to her. It was a passing summer love of her face, for love’s own temporary sake—nothing more. And the thorny crown of this sad conception was that she whom he really did prefer in a cursory way to the rest, she who knew herself to be more impassioned in nature, cleverer, more beautiful than they, was in the eyes of propriety far less worthy of him than the homelier ones whom he ignored.\n\n# XXIV\n\nAmid the oozing fatness and warm ferments of the Froom Vale, at a season when the rush of juices could almost be heard below the hiss of fertilization, it was impossible that the most fanciful love should not grow passionate. The ready bosoms existing there were impregnated by their surroundings.\n\nJuly passed over their heads, and the Thermidorean weather which came in its wake seemed an effort on the part of Nature to match the state of hearts at Talbothays Dairy. The air of the place, so fresh in the spring and early summer, was stagnant and enervating now. Its heavy scents weighed upon them, and at mid-day the landscape seemed lying in a swoon. Ethiopic scorchings browned the upper slopes of the pastures, but there was still bright green herbage here where the watercourses purled. And as Clare was oppressed by the outward heats, so was he burdened inwardly by waxing fervour of passion for the soft and silent Tess.\n\nThe rains having passed, the uplands were dry. The wheels of the dairyman’s spring-cart, as he sped home from market, licked up the pulverized surface of the highway, and were followed by white ribands of dust, as if they had set a thin powder-train on fire. The cows jumped wildly over the five-barred barton-gate, maddened by the gad-fly; Dairyman Crick kept his shirt-sleeves permanently rolled up from Monday to Saturday; open windows had no effect in ventilation without open doors, and in the dairy-garden the blackbirds and thrushes crept about under the currant-bushes, rather in the manner of quadrupeds than of winged creatures. The flies in the kitchen were lazy, teasing, and familiar, crawling about in the unwonted places, on the floors, into drawers, and over the backs of the milkmaids’ hands. Conversations were concerning sunstroke; while butter-making, and still more butter-keeping, was a despair.\n\nThey milked entirely in the meads for coolness and convenience, without driving in the cows. During the day the animals obsequiously followed the shadow of the smallest tree as it moved round the stem with the diurnal roll; and when the milkers came they could hardly stand still for the flies.\n\nOn one of these afternoons four or five unmilked cows chanced to stand apart from the general herd, behind the corner of a hedge, among them being Dumpling and Old Pretty, who loved Tess’s hands above those of any other maid. When she rose from her stool under a finished cow, Angel Clare, who had been observing her for some time, asked her if she would take the aforesaid creatures next. She silently assented, and with her stool at arm’s length, and the pail against her knee, went round to where they stood. Soon the sound of Old Pretty’s milk fizzing into the pail came through the hedge, and then Angel felt inclined to go round the corner also, to finish off a hard-yielding milcher who had strayed there, he being now as capable of this as the dairyman himself.\n\nAll the men, and some of the women, when milking, dug their foreheads into the cows and gazed into the pail. But a few—mainly the younger ones—rested their heads sideways. This was Tess Durbeyfield’s habit, her temple pressing the milcher’s flank, her eyes fixed on the far end of the meadow with the quiet of one lost in meditation. She was milking Old Pretty thus, and the sun chancing to be on the milking-side, it shone flat upon her pink-gowned form and her white curtain-bonnet, and upon her profile, rendering it keen as a cameo cut from the dun background of the cow.\n\nShe did not know that Clare had followed her round, and that he sat under his cow watching her. The stillness of her head and features was remarkable: she might have been in a trance, her eyes open, yet unseeing. Nothing in the picture moved but Old Pretty’s tail and Tess’s pink hands, the latter so gently as to be a rhythmic pulsation only, as if they were obeying a reflex stimulus, like a beating heart.\n\nHow very lovable her face was to him. Yet there was nothing ethereal about it; all was real vitality, real warmth, real incarnation. And it was in her mouth that this culminated. Eyes almost as deep and speaking he had seen before, and cheeks perhaps as fair; brows as arched, a chin and throat almost as shapely; her mouth he had seen nothing to equal on the face of the earth. To a young man with the least fire in him that little upward lift in the middle of her red top lip was distracting, infatuating, maddening. He had never before seen a woman’s lips and teeth which forced upon his mind with such persistent iteration the old Elizabethan simile of roses filled with snow. Perfect, he, as a lover, might have called them off-hand. But no—they were not perfect. And it was the touch of the imperfect upon the would-be perfect that gave the sweetness, because it was that which gave the humanity.\n\nClare had studied the curves of those lips so many times that he could reproduce them mentally with ease: and now, as they again confronted him, clothed with colour and life, they sent an _aura_ over his flesh, a breeze through his nerves, which well nigh produced a qualm; and actually produced, by some mysterious physiological process, a prosaic sneeze.\n\nShe then became conscious that he was observing her; but she would not show it by any change of position, though the curious dream-like fixity disappeared, and a close eye might easily have discerned that the rosiness of her face deepened, and then faded till only a tinge of it was left.\n\nThe influence that had passed into Clare like an excitation from the sky did not die down. Resolutions, reticences, prudences, fears, fell back like a defeated battalion. He jumped up from his seat, and, leaving his pail to be kicked over if the milcher had such a mind, went quickly towards the desire of his eyes, and, kneeling down beside her, clasped her in his arms.\n\nTess was taken completely by surprise, and she yielded to his embrace with unreflecting inevitableness. Having seen that it was really her lover who had advanced, and no one else, her lips parted, and she sank upon him in her momentary joy, with something very like an ecstatic cry.\n\nHe had been on the point of kissing that too tempting mouth, but he checked himself, for tender conscience’ sake.\n\n“Forgive me, Tess dear!” he whispered. “I ought to have asked. I—did not know what I was doing. I do not mean it as a liberty. I am devoted to you, Tessy, dearest, in all sincerity!”\n\nOld Pretty by this time had looked round, puzzled; and seeing two people crouching under her where, by immemorial custom, there should have been only one, lifted her hind leg crossly.\n\n“She is angry—she doesn’t know what we mean—she’ll kick over the milk!” exclaimed Tess, gently striving to free herself, her eyes concerned with the quadruped’s actions, her heart more deeply concerned with herself and Clare.\n\nShe slipped up from her seat, and they stood together, his arm still encircling her. Tess’s eyes, fixed on distance, began to fill.\n\n“Why do you cry, my darling?” he said.\n\n“O—I don’t know!” she murmured.\n\nAs she saw and felt more clearly the position she was in she became agitated and tried to withdraw.\n\n“Well, I have betrayed my feeling, Tess, at last,” said he, with a curious sigh of desperation, signifying unconsciously that his heart had outrun his judgement. “That I—love you dearly and truly I need not say. But I—it shall go no further now—it distresses you—I am as surprised as you are. You will not think I have presumed upon your defencelessness—been too quick and unreflecting, will you?”\n\n“N’—I can’t tell.”\n\nHe had allowed her to free herself; and in a minute or two the milking of each was resumed. Nobody had beheld the gravitation of the two into one; and when the dairyman came round by that screened nook a few minutes later, there was not a sign to reveal that the markedly sundered pair were more to each other than mere acquaintance. Yet in the interval since Crick’s last view of them something had occurred which changed the pivot of the universe for their two natures; something which, had he known its quality, the dairyman would have despised, as a practical man; yet which was based upon a more stubborn and resistless tendency than a whole heap of so-called practicalities. A veil had been whisked aside; the tract of each one’s outlook was to have a new horizon thenceforward—for a short time or for a long.\nEnd of Phase the Third\n\n# XXV\n\nThe Consequence\nClare, restless, went out into the dusk when evening drew on, she who had won him having retired to her chamber.\n\nThe night was as sultry as the day. There was no coolness after dark unless on the grass. Roads, garden-paths, the house-fronts, the barton-walls were warm as hearths, and reflected the noontime temperature into the noctambulist’s face.\n\nHe sat on the east gate of the dairy-yard, and knew not what to think of himself. Feeling had indeed smothered judgement that day.\n\nSince the sudden embrace, three hours before, the twain had kept apart. She seemed stilled, almost alarmed, at what had occurred, while the novelty, unpremeditation, mastery of circumstance disquieted him—palpitating, contemplative being that he was. He could hardly realize their true relations to each other as yet, and what their mutual bearing should be before third parties thenceforward.\n\nAngel had come as pupil to this dairy in the idea that his temporary existence here was to be the merest episode in his life, soon passed through and early forgotten; he had come as to a place from which as from a screened alcove he could calmly view the absorbing world without, and, apostrophizing it with Walt Whitman—\n\nCrowds of men and women attired in the usual costumes, How curious you are to me!—\n\nresolve upon a plan for plunging into that world anew. But behold, the absorbing scene had been imported hither. What had been the engrossing world had dissolved into an uninteresting outer dumb-show; while here, in this apparently dim and unimpassioned place, novelty had volcanically started up, as it had never, for him, started up elsewhere.\n\nEvery window of the house being open, Clare could hear across the yard each trivial sound of the retiring household. The dairy-house, so humble, so insignificant, so purely to him a place of constrained sojourn that he had never hitherto deemed it of sufficient importance to be reconnoitred as an object of any quality whatever in the landscape; what was it now? The aged and lichened brick gables breathed forth “Stay!” The windows smiled, the door coaxed and beckoned, the creeper blushed confederacy. A personality within it was so far-reaching in her influence as to spread into and make the bricks, mortar, and whole overhanging sky throb with a burning sensibility. Whose was this mighty personality? A milkmaid’s.\n\nIt was amazing, indeed, to find how great a matter the life of the obscure dairy had become to him. And though new love was to be held partly responsible for this, it was not solely so. Many besides Angel have learnt that the magnitude of lives is not as to their external displacements, but as to their subjective experiences. The impressionable peasant leads a larger, fuller, more dramatic life than the pachydermatous king. Looking at it thus, he found that life was to be seen of the same magnitude here as elsewhere.\n\nDespite his heterodoxy, faults, and weaknesses, Clare was a man with a conscience. Tess was no insignificant creature to toy with and dismiss; but a woman living her precious life—a life which, to herself who endured or enjoyed it, possessed as great a dimension as the life of the mightiest to himself. Upon her sensations the whole world depended to Tess; through her existence all her fellow-creatures existed, to her. The universe itself only came into being for Tess on the particular day in the particular year in which she was born.\n\nThis consciousness upon which he had intruded was the single opportunity of existence ever vouchsafed to Tess by an unsympathetic First Cause—her all; her every and only chance. How then should he look upon her as of less consequence than himself; as a pretty trifle to caress and grow weary of; and not deal in the greatest seriousness with the affection which he knew that he had awakened in her—so fervid and so impressionable as she was under her reserve—in order that it might not agonize and wreck her?\n\nTo encounter her daily in the accustomed manner would be to develop what had begun. Living in such close relations, to meet meant to fall into endearment; flesh and blood could not resist it; and, having arrived at no conclusion as to the issue of such a tendency, he decided to hold aloof for the present from occupations in which they would be mutually engaged. As yet the harm done was small.\n\nBut it was not easy to carry out the resolution never to approach her. He was driven towards her by every heave of his pulse.\n\nHe thought he would go and see his friends. It might be possible to sound them upon this. In less than five months his term here would have ended, and after a few additional months spent upon other farms he would be fully equipped in agricultural knowledge and in a position to start on his own account. Would not a farmer want a wife, and should a farmer’s wife be a drawing-room wax-figure, or a woman who understood farming? Notwithstanding the pleasing answer returned to him by the silence, he resolved to go his journey.\n\nOne morning when they sat down to breakfast at Talbothays Dairy some maid observed that she had not seen anything of Mr Clare that day.\n\n“O no,” said Dairyman Crick. “Mr Clare has gone hwome to Emminster to spend a few days wi’ his kinsfolk.”\n\nFor four impassioned ones around that table the sunshine of the morning went out at a stroke, and the birds muffled their song. But neither girl by word or gesture revealed her blankness. “He’s getting on towards the end of his time wi’ me,” added the dairyman, with a phlegm which unconsciously was brutal; “and so I suppose he is beginning to see about his plans elsewhere.”\n\n“How much longer is he to bide here?” asked Izz Huett, the only one of the gloom-stricken bevy who could trust her voice with the question.\n\nThe others waited for the dairyman’s answer as if their lives hung upon it; Retty, with parted lips, gazing on the tablecloth, Marian with heat added to her redness, Tess throbbing and looking out at the meads.\n\n“Well, I can’t mind the exact day without looking at my memorandum-book,” replied Crick, with the same intolerable unconcern. “And even that may be altered a bit. He’ll bide to get a little practice in the calving out at the straw-yard, for certain. He’ll hang on till the end of the year I should say.”\n\nFour months or so of torturing ecstasy in his society—of “pleasure girdled about with pain”. After that the blackness of unutterable night.\n\nAt this moment of the morning Angel Clare was riding along a narrow lane ten miles distant from the breakfasters, in the direction of his father’s Vicarage at Emminster, carrying, as well as he could, a little basket which contained some black-puddings and a bottle of mead, sent by Mrs Crick, with her kind respects, to his parents. The white lane stretched before him, and his eyes were upon it; but they were staring into next year, and not at the lane. He loved her; ought he to marry her? Dared he to marry her? What would his mother and his brothers say? What would he himself say a couple of years after the event? That would depend upon whether the germs of staunch comradeship underlay the temporary emotion, or whether it were a sensuous joy in her form only, with no substratum of everlastingness.\n\nHis father’s hill-surrounded little town, the Tudor church-tower of red stone, the clump of trees near the Vicarage, came at last into view beneath him, and he rode down towards the well-known gate. Casting a glance in the direction of the church before entering his home, he beheld standing by the vestry-door a group of girls, of ages between twelve and sixteen, apparently awaiting the arrival of some other one, who in a moment became visible; a figure somewhat older than the school-girls, wearing a broad-brimmed hat and highly-starched cambric morning-gown, with a couple of books in her hand.\n\nClare knew her well. He could not be sure that she observed him; he hoped she did not, so as to render it unnecessary that he should go and speak to her, blameless creature that she was. An overpowering reluctance to greet her made him decide that she had not seen him. The young lady was Miss Mercy Chant, the only daughter of his father’s neighbour and friend, whom it was his parents’ quiet hope that he might wed some day. She was great at Antinomianism and Bible-classes, and was plainly going to hold a class now. Clare’s mind flew to the impassioned, summer-steeped heathens in the Var Vale, their rosy faces court-patched with cow-droppings; and to one the most impassioned of them all.\n\nIt was on the impulse of the moment that he had resolved to trot over to Emminster, and hence had not written to apprise his mother and father, aiming, however, to arrive about the breakfast hour, before they should have gone out to their parish duties. He was a little late, and they had already sat down to the morning meal. The group at the table jumped up to welcome him as soon as he entered. They were his father and mother, his brother the Reverend Felix—curate at a town in the adjoining county, home for the inside of a fortnight—and his other brother, the Reverend Cuthbert, the classical scholar, and Fellow and Dean of his College, down from Cambridge for the long vacation. His mother appeared in a cap and silver spectacles, and his father looked what in fact he was—an earnest, God-fearing man, somewhat gaunt, in years about sixty-five, his pale face lined with thought and purpose. Over their heads hung the picture of Angel’s sister, the eldest of the family, sixteen years his senior, who had married a missionary and gone out to Africa.\n\nOld Mr Clare was a clergyman of a type which, within the last twenty years, has well nigh dropped out of contemporary life. A spiritual descendant in the direct line from Wycliff, Huss, Luther, Calvin; an Evangelical of the Evangelicals, a Conversionist, a man of Apostolic simplicity in life and thought, he had in his raw youth made up his mind once for all in the deeper questions of existence, and admitted no further reasoning on them thenceforward. He was regarded even by those of his own date and school of thinking as extreme; while, on the other hand, those totally opposed to him were unwillingly won to admiration for his thoroughness, and for the remarkable power he showed in dismissing all question as to principles in his energy for applying them. He loved Paul of Tarsus, liked St John, hated St James as much as he dared, and regarded with mixed feelings Timothy, Titus, and Philemon. The New Testament was less a Christiad then a Pauliad to his intelligence—less an argument than an intoxication. His creed of determinism was such that it almost amounted to a vice, and quite amounted, on its negative side, to a renunciative philosophy which had cousinship with that of Schopenhauer and Leopardi. He despised the Canons and Rubric, swore by the Articles, and deemed himself consistent through the whole category—which in a way he might have been. One thing he certainly was—sincere.\n\nTo the aesthetic, sensuous, pagan pleasure in natural life and lush womanhood which his son Angel had lately been experiencing in Var Vale, his temper would have been antipathetic in a high degree, had he either by inquiry or imagination been able to apprehend it. Once upon a time Angel had been so unlucky as to say to his father, in a moment of irritation, that it might have resulted far better for mankind if Greece had been the source of the religion of modern civilization, and not Palestine; and his father’s grief was of that blank description which could not realize that there might lurk a thousandth part of a truth, much less a half truth or a whole truth, in such a proposition. He had simply preached austerely at Angel for some time after. But the kindness of his heart was such that he never resented anything for long, and welcomed his son to-day with a smile which was as candidly sweet as a child’s.\n\nAngel sat down, and the place felt like home; yet he did not so much as formerly feel himself one of the family gathered there. Every time that he returned hither he was conscious of this divergence, and since he had last shared in the Vicarage life it had grown even more distinctly foreign to his own than usual. Its transcendental aspirations—still unconsciously based on the geocentric view of things, a zenithal paradise, a nadiral hell—were as foreign to his own as if they had been the dreams of people on another planet. Latterly he had seen only Life, felt only the great passionate pulse of existence, unwarped, uncontorted, untrammelled by those creeds which futilely attempt to check what wisdom would be content to regulate.\n\nOn their part they saw a great difference in him, a growing divergence from the Angel Clare of former times. It was chiefly a difference in his manner that they noticed just now, particularly his brothers. He was getting to behave like a farmer; he flung his legs about; the muscles of his face had grown more expressive; his eyes looked as much information as his tongue spoke, and more. The manner of the scholar had nearly disappeared; still more the manner of the drawing-room young man. A prig would have said that he had lost culture, and a prude that he had become coarse. Such was the contagion of domiciliary fellowship with the Talbothays nymphs and swains.\n\nAfter breakfast he walked with his two brothers, non-evangelical, well-educated, hall-marked young men, correct to their remotest fibre, such unimpeachable models as are turned out yearly by the lathe of a systematic tuition. They were both somewhat short-sighted, and when it was the custom to wear a single eyeglass and string they wore a single eyeglass and string; when it was the custom to wear a double glass they wore a double glass; when it was the custom to wear spectacles they wore spectacles straightway, all without reference to the particular variety of defect in their own vision. When Wordsworth was enthroned they carried pocket copies; and when Shelley was belittled they allowed him to grow dusty on their shelves. When Correggio’s Holy Families were admired, they admired Correggio’s Holy Families; when he was decried in favour of Velasquez, they sedulously followed suit without any personal objection.\n\nIf these two noticed Angel’s growing social ineptness, he noticed their growing mental limitations. Felix seemed to him all Church; Cuthbert all College. His Diocesan Synod and Visitations were the mainsprings of the world to the one; Cambridge to the other. Each brother candidly recognized that there were a few unimportant score of millions of outsiders in civilized society, persons who were neither University men nor churchmen; but they were to be tolerated rather than reckoned with and respected.\n\nThey were both dutiful and attentive sons, and were regular in their visits to their parents. Felix, though an offshoot from a far more recent point in the devolution of theology than his father, was less self-sacrificing and disinterested. More tolerant than his father of a contradictory opinion, in its aspect as a danger to its holder, he was less ready than his father to pardon it as a slight to his own teaching. Cuthbert was, upon the whole, the more liberal-minded, though, with greater subtlety, he had not so much heart.\n\nAs they walked along the hillside Angel’s former feeling revived in him—that whatever their advantages by comparison with himself, neither saw or set forth life as it really was lived. Perhaps, as with many men, their opportunities of observation were not so good as their opportunities of expression. Neither had an adequate conception of the complicated forces at work outside the smooth and gentle current in which they and their associates floated. Neither saw the difference between local truth and universal truth; that what the inner world said in their clerical and academic hearing was quite a different thing from what the outer world was thinking.\n\n“I suppose it is farming or nothing for you now, my dear fellow,” Felix was saying, among other things, to his youngest brother, as he looked through his spectacles at the distant fields with sad austerity. “And, therefore, we must make the best of it. But I do entreat you to endeavour to keep as much as possible in touch with moral ideals. Farming, of course, means roughing it externally; but high thinking may go with plain living, nevertheless.”\n\n“Of course it may,” said Angel. “Was it not proved nineteen hundred years ago—if I may trespass upon your domain a little? Why should you think, Felix, that I am likely to drop my high thinking and my moral ideals?”\n\n“Well, I fancied, from the tone of your letters and our conversation—it may be fancy only—that you were somehow losing intellectual grasp. Hasn’t it struck you, Cuthbert?”\n\n“Now, Felix,” said Angel drily, “we are very good friends, you know; each of us treading our allotted circles; but if it comes to intellectual grasp, I think you, as a contented dogmatist, had better leave mine alone, and inquire what has become of yours.”\n\nThey returned down the hill to dinner, which was fixed at any time at which their father’s and mother’s morning work in the parish usually concluded. Convenience as regarded afternoon callers was the last thing to enter into the consideration of unselfish Mr and Mrs Clare; though the three sons were sufficiently in unison on this matter to wish that their parents would conform a little to modern notions.\n\nThe walk had made them hungry, Angel in particular, who was now an outdoor man, accustomed to the profuse _dapes inemptae_ of the dairyman’s somewhat coarsely-laden table. But neither of the old people had arrived, and it was not till the sons were almost tired of waiting that their parents entered. The self-denying pair had been occupied in coaxing the appetites of some of their sick parishioners, whom they, somewhat inconsistently, tried to keep imprisoned in the flesh, their own appetites being quite forgotten.\n\nThe family sat down to table, and a frugal meal of cold viands was deposited before them. Angel looked round for Mrs Crick’s black-puddings, which he had directed to be nicely grilled as they did them at the dairy, and of which he wished his father and mother to appreciate the marvellous herbal savours as highly as he did himself.\n\n“Ah! you are looking for the black-puddings, my dear boy,” observed Clare’s mother. “But I am sure you will not mind doing without them as I am sure your father and I shall not, when you know the reason. I suggested to him that we should take Mrs Crick’s kind present to the children of the man who can earn nothing just now because of his attacks of delirium tremens; and he agreed that it would be a great pleasure to them; so we did.”\n\n“Of course,” said Angel cheerfully, looking round for the mead.\n\n“I found the mead so extremely alcoholic,” continued his mother, “that it was quite unfit for use as a beverage, but as valuable as rum or brandy in an emergency; so I have put it in my medicine-closet.”\n\n“We never drink spirits at this table, on principle,” added his father.\n\n“But what shall I tell the dairyman’s wife?” said Angel.\n\n“The truth, of course,” said his father.\n\n“I rather wanted to say we enjoyed the mead and the black-puddings very much. She is a kind, jolly sort of body, and is sure to ask me directly I return.”\n\n“You cannot, if we did not,” Mr Clare answered lucidly.\n\n“Ah—no; though that mead was a drop of pretty tipple.”\n\n“A what?” said Cuthbert and Felix both.\n\n“Oh—’tis an expression they use down at Talbothays,” replied Angel, blushing. He felt that his parents were right in their practice if wrong in their want of sentiment, and said no more.\n\n# XXVI\n\nIt was not till the evening, after family prayers, that Angel found opportunity of broaching to his father one or two subjects near his heart. He had strung himself up to the purpose while kneeling behind his brothers on the carpet, studying the little nails in the heels of their walking boots. When the service was over they went out of the room with their mother, and Mr Clare and himself were left alone.\n\nThe young man first discussed with the elder his plans for the attainment of his position as a farmer on an extensive scale—either in England or in the Colonies. His father then told him that, as he had not been put to the expense of sending Angel up to Cambridge, he had felt it his duty to set by a sum of money every year towards the purchase or lease of land for him some day, that he might not feel himself unduly slighted.\n\n“As far as worldly wealth goes,” continued his father, “you will no doubt stand far superior to your brothers in a few years.”\n\nThis considerateness on old Mr Clare’s part led Angel onward to the other and dearer subject. He observed to his father that he was then six-and-twenty, and that when he should start in the farming business he would require eyes in the back of his head to see to all matters—some one would be necessary to superintend the domestic labours of his establishment whilst he was afield. Would it not be well, therefore, for him to marry?\n\nHis father seemed to think this idea not unreasonable; and then Angel put the question—\n\n“What kind of wife do you think would be best for me as a thrifty hard-working farmer?”\n\n“A truly Christian woman, who will be a help and a comfort to you in your goings-out and your comings-in. Beyond that, it really matters little. Such an one can be found; indeed, my earnest-minded friend and neighbour, Dr Chant—”\n\n“But ought she not primarily to be able to milk cows, churn good butter, make immense cheeses; know how to sit hens and turkeys and rear chickens, to direct a field of labourers in an emergency, and estimate the value of sheep and calves?”\n\n“Yes; a farmer’s wife; yes, certainly. It would be desirable.” Mr Clare, the elder, had plainly never thought of these points before. “I was going to add,” he said, “that for a pure and saintly woman you will not find one more to your true advantage, and certainly not more to your mother’s mind and my own, than your friend Mercy, whom you used to show a certain interest in. It is true that my neighbour Chant’s daughter had lately caught up the fashion of the younger clergy round about us for decorating the Communion-table—alter, as I was shocked to hear her call it one day—with flowers and other stuff on festival occasions. But her father, who is quite as opposed to such flummery as I, says that can be cured. It is a mere girlish outbreak which, I am sure, will not be permanent.”\n\n“Yes, yes; Mercy is good and devout, I know. But, father, don’t you think that a young woman equally pure and virtuous as Miss Chant, but one who, in place of that lady’s ecclesiastical accomplishments, understands the duties of farm life as well as a farmer himself, would suit me infinitely better?”\n\nHis father persisted in his conviction that a knowledge of a farmer’s wife’s duties came second to a Pauline view of humanity; and the impulsive Angel, wishing to honour his father’s feelings and to advance the cause of his heart at the same time, grew specious. He said that fate or Providence had thrown in his way a woman who possessed every qualification to be the helpmate of an agriculturist, and was decidedly of a serious turn of mind. He would not say whether or not she had attached herself to the sound Low Church School of his father; but she would probably be open to conviction on that point; she was a regular church-goer of simple faith; honest-hearted, receptive, intelligent, graceful to a degree, chaste as a vestal, and, in personal appearance, exceptionally beautiful.\n\n“Is she of a family such as you would care to marry into—a lady, in short?” asked his startled mother, who had come softly into the study during the conversation.\n\n“She is not what in common parlance is called a lady,” said Angel, unflinchingly, “for she is a cottager’s daughter, as I am proud to say. But she _is_ a lady, nevertheless—in feeling and nature.”\n\n“Mercy Chant is of a very good family.”\n\n“Pooh!—what’s the advantage of that, mother?” said Angel quickly. “How is family to avail the wife of a man who has to rough it as I have, and shall have to do?”\n\n“Mercy is accomplished. And accomplishments have their charm,” returned his mother, looking at him through her silver spectacles.\n\n“As to external accomplishments, what will be the use of them in the life I am going to lead?—while as to her reading, I can take that in hand. She’ll be apt pupil enough, as you would say if you knew her. She’s brim full of poetry—actualized poetry, if I may use the expression. She _lives_ what paper-poets only write... And she is an unimpeachable Christian, I am sure; perhaps of the very tribe, genus, and species you desire to propagate.”\n\n“O Angel, you are mocking!”\n\n“Mother, I beg pardon. But as she really does attend Church almost every Sunday morning, and is a good Christian girl, I am sure you will tolerate any social shortcomings for the sake of that quality, and feel that I may do worse than choose her.” Angel waxed quite earnest on that rather automatic orthodoxy in his beloved Tess which (never dreaming that it might stand him in such good stead) he had been prone to slight when observing it practised by her and the other milkmaids, because of its obvious unreality amid beliefs essentially naturalistic.\n\nIn their sad doubts as to whether their son had himself any right whatever to the title he claimed for the unknown young woman, Mr and Mrs Clare began to feel it as an advantage not to be overlooked that she at least was sound in her views; especially as the conjunction of the pair must have arisen by an act of Providence; for Angel never would have made orthodoxy a condition of his choice. They said finally that it was better not to act in a hurry, but that they would not object to see her.\n\nAngel therefore refrained from declaring more particulars now. He felt that, single-minded and self-sacrificing as his parents were, there yet existed certain latent prejudices of theirs, as middle-class people, which it would require some tact to overcome. For though legally at liberty to do as he chose, and though their daughter-in-law’s qualifications could make no practical difference to their lives, in the probability of her living far away from them, he wished for affection’s sake not to wound their sentiment in the most important decision of his life.\n\nHe observed his own inconsistencies in dwelling upon accidents in Tess’s life as if they were vital features. It was for herself that he loved Tess; her soul, her heart, her substance—not for her skill in the dairy, her aptness as his scholar, and certainly not for her simple formal faith-professions. Her unsophisticated open-air existence required no varnish of conventionality to make it palatable to him. He held that education had as yet but little affected the beats of emotion and impulse on which domestic happiness depends. It was probable that, in the lapse of ages, improved systems of moral and intellectual training would appreciably, perhaps considerably, elevate the involuntary and even the unconscious instincts of human nature; but up to the present day, culture, as far as he could see, might be said to have affected only the mental epiderm of those lives which had been brought under its influence. This belief was confirmed by his experience of women, which, having latterly been extended from the cultivated middle-class into the rural community, had taught him how much less was the intrinsic difference between the good and wise woman of one social stratum and the good and wise woman of another social stratum, than between the good and bad, the wise and the foolish, of the same stratum or class.\n\nIt was the morning of his departure. His brothers had already left the Vicarage to proceed on a walking tour in the north, whence one was to return to his college, and the other to his curacy. Angel might have accompanied them, but preferred to rejoin his sweetheart at Talbothays. He would have been an awkward member of the party; for, though the most appreciative humanist, the most ideal religionist, even the best-versed Christologist of the three, there was alienation in the standing consciousness that his squareness would not fit the round hole that had been prepared for him. To neither Felix nor Cuthbert had he ventured to mention Tess.\n\nHis mother made him sandwiches, and his father accompanied him, on his own mare, a little way along the road. Having fairly well advanced his own affairs, Angel listened in a willing silence, as they jogged on together through the shady lanes, to his father’s account of his parish difficulties, and the coldness of brother clergymen whom he loved, because of his strict interpretations of the New Testament by the light of what they deemed a pernicious Calvinistic doctrine.\n\n“Pernicious!” said Mr Clare, with genial scorn; and he proceeded to recount experiences which would show the absurdity of that idea. He told of wondrous conversions of evil livers of which he had been the instrument, not only amongst the poor, but amongst the rich and well-to-do; and he also candidly admitted many failures.\n\nAs an instance of the latter, he mentioned the case of a young upstart squire named d’Urberville, living some forty miles off, in the neighbourhood of Trantridge.\n\n“Not one of the ancient d’Urbervilles of Kingsbere and other places?” asked his son. “That curiously historic worn-out family with its ghostly legend of the coach-and-four?”\n\n“O no. The original d’Urbervilles decayed and disappeared sixty or eighty years ago—at least, I believe so. This seems to be a new family which had taken the name; for the credit of the former knightly line I hope they are spurious, I’m sure. But it is odd to hear you express interest in old families. I thought you set less store by them even than I.”\n\n“You misapprehend me, father; you often do,” said Angel with a little impatience. “Politically I am sceptical as to the virtue of their being old. Some of the wise even among themselves ‘exclaim against their own succession,’ as Hamlet puts it; but lyrically, dramatically, and even historically, I am tenderly attached to them.”\n\nThis distinction, though by no means a subtle one, was yet too subtle for Mr Clare the elder, and he went on with the story he had been about to relate; which was that after the death of the senior so-called d’Urberville, the young man developed the most culpable passions, though he had a blind mother, whose condition should have made him know better. A knowledge of his career having come to the ears of Mr Clare, when he was in that part of the country preaching missionary sermons, he boldly took occasion to speak to the delinquent on his spiritual state. Though he was a stranger, occupying another’s pulpit, he had felt this to be his duty, and took for his text the words from St Luke: “Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee!” The young man much resented this directness of attack, and in the war of words which followed when they met he did not scruple publicly to insult Mr Clare, without respect for his gray hairs.\n\nAngel flushed with distress.\n\n“Dear father,” he said sadly, “I wish you would not expose yourself to such gratuitous pain from scoundrels!”\n\n“Pain?” said his father, his rugged face shining in the ardour of self-abnegation. “The only pain to me was pain on his account, poor, foolish young man. Do you suppose his incensed words could give me any pain, or even his blows? ‘Being reviled we bless; being persecuted we suffer it; being defamed we entreat; we are made as the filth of the world, and as the offscouring of all things unto this day.’ Those ancient and noble words to the Corinthians are strictly true at this present hour.”\n\n“Not blows, father? He did not proceed to blows?”\n\n“No, he did not. Though I have borne blows from men in a mad state of intoxication.”\n“No!”\n\n“A dozen times, my boy. What then? I have saved them from the guilt of murdering their own flesh and blood thereby; and they have lived to thank me, and praise God.”\n\n“May this young man do the same!” said Angel fervently. “But I fear otherwise, from what you say.”\n\n“We’ll hope, nevertheless,” said Mr Clare. “And I continue to pray for him, though on this side of the grave we shall probably never meet again. But, after all, one of those poor words of mine may spring up in his heart as a good seed some day.”\n\nNow, as always, Clare’s father was sanguine as a child; and though the younger could not accept his parent’s narrow dogma, he revered his practice and recognized the hero under the pietist. Perhaps he revered his father’s practice even more now than ever, seeing that, in the question of making Tessy his wife, his father had not once thought of inquiring whether she were well provided or penniless. The same unworldliness was what had necessitated Angel’s getting a living as a farmer, and would probably keep his brothers in the position of poor parsons for the term of their activities; yet Angel admired it none the less. Indeed, despite his own heterodoxy, Angel often felt that he was nearer to his father on the human side than was either of his brethren.\n\n# XXVII\n\nAn up-hill and down-hill ride of twenty-odd miles through a garish mid-day atmosphere brought him in the afternoon to a detached knoll a mile or two west of Talbothays, whence he again looked into that green trough of sappiness and humidity, the valley of the Var or Froom. Immediately he began to descend from the upland to the fat alluvial soil below, the atmosphere grew heavier; the languid perfume of the summer fruits, the mists, the hay, the flowers, formed therein a vast pool of odour which at this hour seemed to make the animals, the very bees and butterflies drowsy. Clare was now so familiar with the spot that he knew the individual cows by their names when, a long distance off, he saw them dotted about the meads. It was with a sense of luxury that he recognized his power of viewing life here from its inner side, in a way that had been quite foreign to him in his student-days; and, much as he loved his parents, he could not help being aware that to come here, as now, after an experience of home-life, affected him like throwing off splints and bandages; even the one customary curb on the humours of English rural societies being absent in this place, Talbothays having no resident landlord.\n\nNot a human being was out of doors at the dairy. The denizens were all enjoying the usual afternoon nap of an hour or so which the exceedingly early hours kept in summer-time rendered a necessity. At the door the wood-hooped pails, sodden and bleached by infinite scrubbings, hung like hats on a stand upon the forked and peeled limb of an oak fixed there for that purpose; all of them ready and dry for the evening milking. Angel entered, and went through the silent passages of the house to the back quarters, where he listened for a moment. Sustained snores came from the cart-house, where some of the men were lying down; the grunt and squeal of sweltering pigs arose from the still further distance. The large-leaved rhubarb and cabbage plants slept too, their broad limp surfaces hanging in the sun like half-closed umbrellas.\n\nHe unbridled and fed his horse, and as he re-entered the house the clock struck three. Three was the afternoon skimming-hour; and, with the stroke, Clare heard the creaking of the floor-boards above, and then the touch of a descending foot on the stairs. It was Tess’s, who in another moment came down before his eyes.\n\nShe had not heard him enter, and hardly realized his presence there. She was yawning, and he saw the red interior of her mouth as if it had been a snake’s. She had stretched one arm so high above her coiled-up cable of hair that he could see its satin delicacy above the sunburn; her face was flushed with sleep, and her eyelids hung heavy over their pupils. The brim-fulness of her nature breathed from her. It was a moment when a woman’s soul is more incarnate than at any other time; when the most spiritual beauty bespeaks itself flesh; and sex takes the outside place in the presentation.\n\nThen those eyes flashed brightly through their filmy heaviness, before the remainder of her face was well awake. With an oddly compounded look of gladness, shyness, and surprise, she exclaimed—“O Mr Clare! How you frightened me—I—”\n\nThere had not at first been time for her to think of the changed relations which his declaration had introduced; but the full sense of the matter rose up in her face when she encountered Clare’s tender look as he stepped forward to the bottom stair.\n\n“Dear, darling Tessy!” he whispered, putting his arm round her, and his face to her flushed cheek. “Don’t, for Heaven’s sake, Mister me any more. I have hastened back so soon because of you!”\n\nTess’s excitable heart beat against his by way of reply; and there they stood upon the red-brick floor of the entry, the sun slanting in by the window upon his back, as he held her tightly to his breast; upon her inclining face, upon the blue veins of her temple, upon her naked arm, and her neck, and into the depths of her hair. Having been lying down in her clothes she was warm as a sunned cat. At first she would not look straight up at him, but her eyes soon lifted, and his plumbed the deepness of the ever-varying pupils, with their radiating fibrils of blue, and black, and gray, and violet, while she regarded him as Eve at her second waking might have regarded Adam.\n\n“I’ve got to go a-skimming,” she pleaded, “and I have on’y old Deb to help me to-day. Mrs Crick is gone to market with Mr Crick, and Retty is not well, and the others are gone out somewhere, and won’t be home till milking.”\n\nAs they retreated to the milk-house Deborah Fyander appeared on the stairs.\n\n“I have come back, Deborah,” said Mr Clare, upwards. “So I can help Tess with the skimming; and, as you are very tired, I am sure, you needn’t come down till milking-time.”\n\nPossibly the Talbothays milk was not very thoroughly skimmed that afternoon. Tess was in a dream wherein familiar objects appeared as having light and shade and position, but no particular outline. Every time she held the skimmer under the pump to cool it for the work her hand trembled, the ardour of his affection being so palpable that she seemed to flinch under it like a plant in too burning a sun.\n\nThen he pressed her again to his side, and when she had done running her forefinger round the leads to cut off the cream-edge, he cleaned it in nature’s way; for the unconstrained manners of Talbothays dairy came convenient now.\n\n“I may as well say it now as later, dearest,” he resumed gently. “I wish to ask you something of a very practical nature, which I have been thinking of ever since that day last week in the meads. I shall soon want to marry, and, being a farmer, you see I shall require for my wife a woman who knows all about the management of farms. Will you be that woman, Tessy?”\n\nHe put it that way that she might not think he had yielded to an impulse of which his head would disapprove.\n\nShe turned quite careworn. She had bowed to the inevitable result of proximity, the necessity of loving him; but she had not calculated upon this sudden corollary, which, indeed, Clare had put before her without quite meaning himself to do it so soon. With pain that was like the bitterness of dissolution she murmured the words of her indispensable and sworn answer as an honourable woman.\n\n“O Mr Clare—I cannot be your wife—I cannot be!”\n\nThe sound of her own decision seemed to break Tess’s very heart, and she bowed her face in her grief.\n\n“But, Tess!” he said, amazed at her reply, and holding her still more greedily close. “Do you say no? Surely you love me?”\n\n“O yes, yes! And I would rather be yours than anybody’s in the world,” returned the sweet and honest voice of the distressed girl. “But I _cannot_ marry you!”\n\n“Tess,” he said, holding her at arm’s length, “you are engaged to marry some one else!”\n\n“No, no!”\n“Then why do you refuse me?”\n\n“I don’t want to marry! I have not thought of doing it. I cannot! I only want to love you.”\n\n“But why?”\n\nDriven to subterfuge, she stammered—\n\n“Your father is a parson, and your mother wouldn’ like you to marry such as me. She will want you to marry a lady.”\n\n“Nonsense—I have spoken to them both. That was partly why I went home.”\n\n“I feel I cannot—never, never!” she echoed.\n\n“Is it too sudden to be asked thus, my Pretty?”\n\n“Yes—I did not expect it.”\n\n“If you will let it pass, please, Tessy, I will give you time,” he said. “It was very abrupt to come home and speak to you all at once. I’ll not allude to it again for a while.”\n\nShe again took up the shining skimmer, held it beneath the pump, and began anew. But she could not, as at other times, hit the exact under-surface of the cream with the delicate dexterity required, try as she might; sometimes she was cutting down into the milk, sometimes in the air. She could hardly see, her eyes having filled with two blurring tears drawn forth by a grief which, to this her best friend and dear advocate, she could never explain.\n\n“I can’t skim—I can’t!” she said, turning away from him.\n\nNot to agitate and hinder her longer, the considerate Clare began talking in a more general way:\n\n“You quite misapprehend my parents. They are the most simple-mannered people alive, and quite unambitious. They are two of the few remaining Evangelical school. Tessy, are you an Evangelical?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“You go to church very regularly, and our parson here is not very High, they tell me.”\n\nTess’s ideas on the views of the parish clergyman, whom she heard every week, seemed to be rather more vague than Clare’s, who had never heard him at all.\n\n“I wish I could fix my mind on what I hear there more firmly than I do,” she remarked as a safe generality. “It is often a great sorrow to me.”\n\nShe spoke so unaffectedly that Angel was sure in his heart that his father could not object to her on religious grounds, even though she did not know whether her principles were High, Low or Broad. He himself knew that, in reality, the confused beliefs which she held, apparently imbibed in childhood, were, if anything, Tractarian as to phraseology, and Pantheistic as to essence. Confused or otherwise, to disturb them was his last desire:\n\nLeave thou thy sister, when she prays, Her early Heaven, her happy views; Nor thou with shadow’d hint confuse A life that leads melodious days.\n\nHe had occasionally thought the counsel less honest than musical; but he gladly conformed to it now.\n\nHe spoke further of the incidents of his visit, of his father’s mode of life, of his zeal for his principles; she grew serener, and the undulations disappeared from her skimming; as she finished one lead after another he followed her, and drew the plugs for letting down the milk.\n\n“I fancied you looked a little downcast when you came in,” she ventured to observe, anxious to keep away from the subject of herself.\n\n“Yes—well, my father had been talking a good deal to me of his troubles and difficulties, and the subject always tends to depress me. He is so zealous that he gets many snubs and buffetings from people of a different way of thinking from himself, and I don’t like to hear of such humiliations to a man of his age, the more particularly as I don’t think earnestness does any good when carried so far. He has been telling me of a very unpleasant scene in which he took part quite recently. He went as the deputy of some missionary society to preach in the neighbourhood of Trantridge, a place forty miles from here, and made it his business to expostulate with a lax young cynic he met with somewhere about there—son of some landowner up that way—and who has a mother afflicted with blindness. My father addressed himself to the gentleman point-blank, and there was quite a disturbance. It was very foolish of my father, I must say, to intrude his conversation upon a stranger when the probabilities were so obvious that it would be useless. But whatever he thinks to be his duty, that he’ll do, in season or out of season; and, of course, he makes many enemies, not only among the absolutely vicious, but among the easy-going, who hate being bothered. He says he glories in what happened, and that good may be done indirectly; but I wish he would not wear himself out now he is getting old, and would leave such pigs to their wallowing.”\n\nTess’s look had grown hard and worn, and her ripe mouth tragical; but she no longer showed any tremulousness. Clare’s revived thoughts of his father prevented his noticing her particularly; and so they went on down the white row of liquid rectangles till they had finished and drained them off, when the other maids returned, and took their pails, and Deb came to scald out the leads for the new milk. As Tess withdrew to go afield to the cows he said to her softly—\n\n“And my question, Tessy?”\n\n“O no—no!” replied she with grave hopelessness, as one who had heard anew the turmoil of her own past in the allusion to Alec d’Urberville. “It _can’t_ be!”\n\nShe went out towards the mead, joining the other milkmaids with a bound, as if trying to make the open air drive away her sad constraint. All the girls drew onward to the spot where the cows were grazing in the farther mead, the bevy advancing with the bold grace of wild animals—the reckless, unchastened motion of women accustomed to unlimited space—in which they abandoned themselves to the air as a swimmer to the wave. It seemed natural enough to him now that Tess was again in sight to choose a mate from unconstrained Nature, and not from the abodes of Art.\n\n# XXVIII\n\nHer refusal, though unexpected, did not permanently daunt Clare. His experience of women was great enough for him to be aware that the negative often meant nothing more than the preface to the affirmative; and it was little enough for him not to know that in the manner of the present negative there lay a great exception to the dallyings of coyness. That she had already permitted him to make love to her he read as an additional assurance, not fully trowing that in the fields and pastures to “sigh gratis” is by no means deemed waste; love-making being here more often accepted inconsiderately and for its own sweet sake than in the carking, anxious homes of the ambitious, where a girl’s craving for an establishment paralyzes her healthy thought of a passion as an end.\n\n“Tess, why did you say ‘no’ in such a positive way?” he asked her in the course of a few days.\n\nShe started.\n\n“Don’t ask me. I told you why—partly. I am not good enough—not worthy enough.”\n\n“How? Not fine lady enough?”\n\n“Yes—something like that,” murmured she. “Your friends would scorn me.”\n\n“Indeed, you mistake them—my father and mother. As for my brothers, I don’t care—” He clasped his fingers behind her back to keep her from slipping away. “Now—you did not mean it, sweet?—I am sure you did not! You have made me so restless that I cannot read, or play, or do anything. I am in no hurry, Tess, but I want to know—to hear from your own warm lips—that you will some day be mine—any time you may choose; but some day?”\n\nShe could only shake her head and look away from him.\n\nClare regarded her attentively, conned the characters of her face as if they had been hieroglyphics. The denial seemed real.\n\n“Then I ought not to hold you in this way—ought I? I have no right to you—no right to seek out where you are, or walk with you! Honestly, Tess, do you love any other man?”\n\n“How can you ask?” she said, with continued self-suppression.\n\n“I almost know that you do not. But then, why do you repulse me?”\n\n“I don’t repulse you. I like you to—tell me you love me; and you may always tell me so as you go about with me—and never offend me.”\n\n“But you will not accept me as a husband?”\n\n“Ah—that’s different—it is for your good, indeed, my dearest! O, believe me, it is only for your sake! I don’t like to give myself the great happiness o’ promising to be yours in that way—because—because I am _sure_ I ought not to do it.”\n\n“But you will make me happy!”\n\n“Ah—you think so, but you don’t know!”\n\nAt such times as this, apprehending the grounds of her refusal to be her modest sense of incompetence in matters social and polite, he would say that she was wonderfully well-informed and versatile—which was certainly true, her natural quickness and her admiration for him having led her to pick up his vocabulary, his accent, and fragments of his knowledge, to a surprising extent. After these tender contests and her victory she would go away by herself under the remotest cow, if at milking-time, or into the sedge or into her room, if at a leisure interval, and mourn silently, not a minute after an apparently phlegmatic negative.\n\nThe struggle was so fearful; her own heart was so strongly on the side of his—two ardent hearts against one poor little conscience—that she tried to fortify her resolution by every means in her power. She had come to Talbothays with a made-up mind. On no account could she agree to a step which might afterwards cause bitter rueing to her husband for his blindness in wedding her. And she held that what her conscience had decided for her when her mind was unbiassed ought not to be overruled now.\n\n“Why don’t somebody tell him all about me?” she said. “It was only forty miles off—why hasn’t it reached here? Somebody must know!”\n\nYet nobody seemed to know; nobody told him.\n\nFor two or three days no more was said. She guessed from the sad countenances of her chamber companions that they regarded her not only as the favourite, but as the chosen; but they could see for themselves that she did not put herself in his way.\n\nTess had never before known a time in which the thread of her life was so distinctly twisted of two strands, positive pleasure and positive pain. At the next cheese-making the pair were again left alone together. The dairyman himself had been lending a hand; but Mr Crick, as well as his wife, seemed latterly to have acquired a suspicion of mutual interest between these two; though they walked so circumspectly that suspicion was but of the faintest. Anyhow, the dairyman left them to themselves.\n\nThey were breaking up the masses of curd before putting them into the vats. The operation resembled the act of crumbling bread on a large scale; and amid the immaculate whiteness of the curds Tess Durbeyfield’s hands showed themselves of the pinkness of the rose. Angel, who was filling the vats with his handful, suddenly ceased, and laid his hands flat upon hers. Her sleeves were rolled far above the elbow, and bending lower he kissed the inside vein of her soft arm.\nAlthough the early September weather was sultry, her arm, from her dabbling in the curds, was as cold and damp to his mouth as a new-gathered mushroom, and tasted of the whey. But she was such a sheaf of susceptibilities that her pulse was accelerated by the touch, her blood driven to her finger-ends, and the cool arms flushed hot. Then, as though her heart had said, “Is coyness longer necessary? Truth is truth between man and woman, as between man and man,” she lifted her eyes and they beamed devotedly into his, as her lip rose in a tender half-smile.\n\n“Do you know why I did that, Tess?” he said.\n\n“Because you love me very much!”\n\n“Yes, and as a preliminary to a new entreaty.”\n\n“Not _again_!”\n\nShe looked a sudden fear that her resistance might break down under her own desire.\n\n“O, Tessy!” he went on, “I _cannot_ think why you are so tantalizing. Why do you disappoint me so? You seem almost like a coquette, upon my life you do—a coquette of the first urban water! They blow hot and blow cold, just as you do, and it is the very last sort of thing to expect to find in a retreat like Talbothays.... And yet, dearest,” he quickly added, observing how the remark had cut her, “I know you to be the most honest, spotless creature that ever lived. So how can I suppose you a flirt? Tess, why don’t you like the idea of being my wife, if you love me as you seem to do?”\n\n“I have never said I don’t like the idea, and I never could say it; because—it isn’t true!”\n\nThe stress now getting beyond endurance, her lip quivered, and she was obliged to go away. Clare was so pained and perplexed that he ran after and caught her in the passage.\n\n“Tell me, tell me!” he said, passionately clasping her, in forgetfulness of his curdy hands: “do tell me that you won’t belong to anybody but me!”\n\n“I will, I will tell you!” she exclaimed. “And I will give you a complete answer, if you will let me go now. I will tell you my experiences—all about myself—all!”\n\n“Your experiences, dear; yes, certainly; any number.” He expressed assent in loving satire, looking into her face. “My Tess, no doubt, almost as many experiences as that wild convolvulus out there on the garden hedge, that opened itself this morning for the first time. Tell me anything, but don’t use that wretched expression any more about not being worthy of me.”\n\n“I will try—not! And I’ll give you my reasons to-morrow—next week.”\n\n“Say on Sunday?”\n\n“Yes, on Sunday.”\n\nAt last she got away, and did not stop in her retreat till she was in the thicket of pollard willows at the lower side of the barton, where she could be quite unseen. Here Tess flung herself down upon the rustling undergrowth of spear-grass, as upon a bed, and remained crouching in palpitating misery broken by momentary shoots of joy, which her fears about the ending could not altogether suppress.\n\nIn reality, she was drifting into acquiescence. Every see-saw of her breath, every wave of her blood, every pulse singing in her ears, was a voice that joined with nature in revolt against her scrupulousness. Reckless, inconsiderate acceptance of him; to close with him at the altar, revealing nothing, and chancing discovery; to snatch ripe pleasure before the iron teeth of pain could have time to shut upon her: that was what love counselled; and in almost a terror of ecstasy Tess divined that, despite her many months of lonely self-chastisement, wrestlings, communings, schemes to lead a future of austere isolation, love’s counsel would prevail.\n\nThe afternoon advanced, and still she remained among the willows. She heard the rattle of taking down the pails from the forked stands; the “waow-waow!” which accompanied the getting together of the cows. But she did not go to the milking. They would see her agitation; and the dairyman, thinking the cause to be love alone, would good-naturedly tease her; and that harassment could not be borne.\n\nHer lover must have guessed her overwrought state, and invented some excuse for her non-appearance, for no inquiries were made or calls given. At half-past six the sun settled down upon the levels with the aspect of a great forge in the heavens; and presently a monstrous pumpkin-like moon arose on the other hand. The pollard willows, tortured out of their natural shape by incessant choppings, became spiny-haired monsters as they stood up against it. She went in and upstairs without a light.\n\nIt was now Wednesday. Thursday came, and Angel looked thoughtfully at her from a distance, but intruded in no way upon her. The indoor milkmaids, Marian and the rest, seemed to guess that something definite was afoot, for they did not force any remarks upon her in the bedchamber. Friday passed; Saturday. To-morrow was the day.\n\n“I shall give way—I shall say yes—I shall let myself marry him—I cannot help it!” she jealously panted, with her hot face to the pillow that night, on hearing one of the other girls sigh his name in her sleep. “I can’t bear to let anybody have him but me! Yet it is a wrong to him, and may kill him when he knows! O my heart—O—O—O!”\n\n# XXIX\n\n“Now, who mid ye think I’ve heard news o’ this morning?” said Dairyman Crick, as he sat down to breakfast next day, with a riddling gaze round upon the munching men and maids. “Now, just who mid ye think?”\n\nOne guessed, and another guessed. Mrs Crick did not guess, because she knew already.\n\n“Well,” said the dairyman, “’tis that slack-twisted ’hore’s-bird of a feller, Jack Dollop. He’s lately got married to a widow-woman.”\n\n“Not Jack Dollop? A villain—to think o’ that!” said a milker.\n\nThe name entered quickly into Tess Durbeyfield’s consciousness, for it was the name of the lover who had wronged his sweetheart, and had afterwards been so roughly used by the young woman’s mother in the butter-churn.\n\n“And had he married the valiant matron’s daughter, as he promised?” asked Angel Clare absently, as he turned over the newspaper he was reading at the little table to which he was always banished by Mrs Crick, in her sense of his gentility.\n\n“Not he, sir. Never meant to,” replied the dairyman. “As I say, ’tis a widow-woman, and she had money, it seems—fifty poun’ a year or so; and that was all he was after. They were married in a great hurry; and then she told him that by marrying she had lost her fifty poun’ a year. Just fancy the state o’ my gentleman’s mind at that news! Never such a cat-and-dog life as they’ve been leading ever since! Serves him well beright. But onluckily the poor woman gets the worst o’t.”\n\n“Well, the silly body should have told en sooner that the ghost of her first man would trouble him,” said Mrs Crick.\n\n“Ay, ay,” responded the dairyman indecisively. “Still, you can see exactly how ’twas. She wanted a home, and didn’t like to run the risk of losing him. Don’t ye think that was something like it, maidens?”\n\nHe glanced towards the row of girls.\n\n“She ought to ha’ told him just before they went to church, when he could hardly have backed out,” exclaimed Marian.\n\n“Yes, she ought,” agreed Izz.\n\n“She must have seen what he was after, and should ha’ refused him,” cried Retty spasmodically.\n\n“And what do you say, my dear?” asked the dairyman of Tess.\n\n“I think she ought—to have told him the true state of things—or else refused him—I don’t know,” replied Tess, the bread-and-butter choking her.\n\n“Be cust if I’d have done either o’t,” said Beck Knibbs, a married helper from one of the cottages. “All’s fair in love and war. I’d ha’ married en just as she did, and if he’d said two words to me about not telling him beforehand anything whatsomdever about my first chap that I hadn’t chose to tell, I’d ha’ knocked him down wi’ the rolling-pin—a scram little feller like he! Any woman could do it.”\n\nThe laughter which followed this sally was supplemented only by a sorry smile, for form’s sake, from Tess. What was comedy to them was tragedy to her; and she could hardly bear their mirth. She soon rose from table, and, with an impression that Clare would soon follow her, went along a little wriggling path, now stepping to one side of the irrigating channels, and now to the other, till she stood by the main stream of the Var. Men had been cutting the water-weeds higher up the river, and masses of them were floating past her—moving islands of green crow-foot, whereon she might almost have ridden; long locks of which weed had lodged against the piles driven to keep the cows from crossing.\n\nYes, there was the pain of it. This question of a woman telling her story—the heaviest of crosses to herself—seemed but amusement to others. It was as if people should laugh at martyrdom.\n\n“Tessy!” came from behind her, and Clare sprang across the gully, alighting beside her feet. “My wife—soon!”\n\n“No, no; I cannot. For your sake, O Mr Clare; for your sake, I say no!”\n\n“Tess!”\n\n“Still I say no!” she repeated.\n\nNot expecting this, he had put his arm lightly round her waist the moment after speaking, beneath her hanging tail of hair. (The younger dairymaids, including Tess, breakfasted with their hair loose on Sunday mornings before building it up extra high for attending church, a style they could not adopt when milking with their heads against the cows.) If she had said “Yes” instead of “No” he would have kissed her; it had evidently been his intention; but her determined negative deterred his scrupulous heart. Their condition of domiciliary comradeship put her, as the woman, to such disadvantage by its enforced intercourse, that he felt it unfair to her to exercise any pressure of blandishment which he might have honestly employed had she been better able to avoid him. He released her momentarily-imprisoned waist, and withheld the kiss.\n\nIt all turned on that release. What had given her strength to refuse him this time was solely the tale of the widow told by the dairyman; and that would have been overcome in another moment. But Angel said no more; his face was perplexed; he went away.\n\nDay after day they met—somewhat less constantly than before; and thus two or three weeks went by. The end of September drew near, and she could see in his eye that he might ask her again.\n\nHis plan of procedure was different now—as though he had made up his mind that her negatives were, after all, only coyness and youth startled by the novelty of the proposal. The fitful evasiveness of her manner when the subject was under discussion countenanced the idea. So he played a more coaxing game; and while never going beyond words, or attempting the renewal of caresses, he did his utmost orally.\n\nIn this way Clare persistently wooed her in undertones like that of the purling milk—at the cow’s side, at skimmings, at butter-makings, at cheese-makings, among broody poultry, and among farrowing pigs—as no milkmaid was ever wooed before by such a man.\n\nTess knew that she must break down. Neither a religious sense of a certain moral validity in the previous union nor a conscientious wish for candour could hold out against it much longer. She loved him so passionately, and he was so godlike in her eyes; and being, though untrained, instinctively refined, her nature cried for his tutelary guidance. And thus, though Tess kept repeating to herself, “I can never be his wife,” the words were vain. A proof of her weakness lay in the very utterance of what calm strength would not have taken the trouble to formulate. Every sound of his voice beginning on the old subject stirred her with a terrifying bliss, and she coveted the recantation she feared.\n\nHis manner was—what man’s is not?—so much that of one who would love and cherish and defend her under any conditions, changes, charges, or revelations, that her gloom lessened as she basked in it. The season meanwhile was drawing onward to the equinox, and though it was still fine, the days were much shorter. The dairy had again worked by morning candlelight for a long time; and a fresh renewal of Clare’s pleading occurred one morning between three and four.\n\nShe had run up in her bedgown to his door to call him as usual; then had gone back to dress and call the others; and in ten minutes was walking to the head of the stairs with the candle in her hand. At the same moment he came down his steps from above in his shirt-sleeves and put his arm across the stairway.\n\n“Now, Miss Flirt, before you go down,” he said peremptorily. “It is a fortnight since I spoke, and this won’t do any longer. You _must_ tell me what you mean, or I shall have to leave this house. My door was ajar just now, and I saw you. For your own safety I must go. You don’t know. Well? Is it to be yes at last?”\n\n“I am only just up, Mr Clare, and it is too early to take me to task!” she pouted. “You need not call me Flirt. ’Tis cruel and untrue. Wait till by and by. Please wait till by and by! I will really think seriously about it between now and then. Let me go downstairs!”\n\nShe looked a little like what he said she was as, holding the candle sideways, she tried to smile away the seriousness of her words.\n\n“Call me Angel, then, and not Mr Clare.”\n\n“Angel.”\n\n“Angel dearest—why not?”\n\n“’Twould mean that I agree, wouldn’t it?”\n\n“It would only mean that you love me, even if you cannot marry me; and you were so good as to own that long ago.”\n\n“Very well, then, ‘Angel dearest’, if I _must_,” she murmured, looking at her candle, a roguish curl coming upon her mouth, notwithstanding her suspense.\n\nClare had resolved never to kiss her until he had obtained her promise; but somehow, as Tess stood there in her prettily tucked-up milking gown, her hair carelessly heaped upon her head till there should be leisure to arrange it when skimming and milking were done, he broke his resolve, and brought his lips to her cheek for one moment. She passed downstairs very quickly, never looking back at him or saying another word. The other maids were already down, and the subject was not pursued. Except Marian, they all looked wistfully and suspiciously at the pair, in the sad yellow rays which the morning candles emitted in contrast with the first cold signals of the dawn without.\n\nWhen skimming was done—which, as the milk diminished with the approach of autumn, was a lessening process day by day—Retty and the rest went out. The lovers followed them.\n\n“Our tremulous lives are so different from theirs, are they not?” he musingly observed to her, as he regarded the three figures tripping before him through the frigid pallor of opening day.\n\n“Not so very different, I think,” she said.\n\n“Why do you think that?”\n\n“There are very few women’s lives that are not—tremulous,” Tess replied, pausing over the new word as if it impressed her. “There’s more in those three than you think.”\n\n“What is in them?”\n\n“Almost either of ’em,” she began, “would make—perhaps would make—a properer wife than I. And perhaps they love you as well as I—almost.”\n\n“O, Tessy!”\n\nThere were signs that it was an exquisite relief to her to hear the impatient exclamation, though she had resolved so intrepidly to let generosity make one bid against herself. That was now done, and she had not the power to attempt self-immolation a second time then. They were joined by a milker from one of the cottages, and no more was said on that which concerned them so deeply. But Tess knew that this day would decide it.\n\nIn the afternoon several of the dairyman’s household and assistants went down to the meads as usual, a long way from the dairy, where many of the cows were milked without being driven home. The supply was getting less as the animals advanced in calf, and the supernumerary milkers of the lush green season had been dismissed.\n\nThe work progressed leisurely. Each pailful was poured into tall cans that stood in a large spring-waggon which had been brought upon the scene; and when they were milked, the cows trailed away. Dairyman Crick, who was there with the rest, his wrapper gleaming miraculously white against a leaden evening sky, suddenly looked at his heavy watch.\n\n“Why, ’tis later than I thought,” he said. “Begad! We shan’t be soon enough with this milk at the station, if we don’t mind. There’s no time to-day to take it home and mix it with the bulk afore sending off. It must go to station straight from here. Who’ll drive it across?”\n\nMr Clare volunteered to do so, though it was none of his business, asking Tess to accompany him. The evening, though sunless, had been warm and muggy for the season, and Tess had come out with her milking-hood only, naked-armed and jacketless; certainly not dressed for a drive. She therefore replied by glancing over her scant habiliments; but Clare gently urged her. She assented by relinquishing her pail and stool to the dairyman to take home, and mounted the spring-waggon beside Clare.\n\n# XXX\n\nIn the diminishing daylight they went along the level roadway through the meads, which stretched away into gray miles, and were backed in the extreme edge of distance by the swarthy and abrupt slopes of Egdon Heath. On its summit stood clumps and stretches of fir-trees, whose notched tips appeared like battlemented towers crowning black-fronted castles of enchantment.\n\nThey were so absorbed in the sense of being close to each other that they did not begin talking for a long while, the silence being broken only by the clucking of the milk in the tall cans behind them. The lane they followed was so solitary that the hazel nuts had remained on the boughs till they slipped from their shells, and the blackberries hung in heavy clusters. Every now and then Angel would fling the lash of his whip round one of these, pluck it off, and give it to his companion.\n\nThe dull sky soon began to tell its meaning by sending down herald-drops of rain, and the stagnant air of the day changed into a fitful breeze which played about their faces. The quick-silvery glaze on the rivers and pools vanished; from broad mirrors of light they changed to lustreless sheets of lead, with a surface like a rasp. But that spectacle did not affect her preoccupation. Her countenance, a natural carnation slightly embrowned by the season, had deepened its tinge with the beating of the rain-drops; and her hair, which the pressure of the cows’ flanks had, as usual, caused to tumble down from its fastenings and stray beyond the curtain of her calico bonnet, was made clammy by the moisture till it hardly was better than seaweed.\n\n“I ought not to have come, I suppose,” she murmured, looking at the sky.\n\n“I am sorry for the rain,” said he. “But how glad I am to have you here!”\n\nRemote Egdon disappeared by degree behind the liquid gauze. The evening grew darker, and the roads being crossed by gates, it was not safe to drive faster than at a walking pace. The air was rather chill.\n\n“I am so afraid you will get cold, with nothing upon your arms and shoulders,” he said. “Creep close to me, and perhaps the drizzle won’t hurt you much. I should be sorrier still if I did not think that the rain might be helping me.”\n\nShe imperceptibly crept closer, and he wrapped round them both a large piece of sail-cloth, which was sometimes used to keep the sun off the milk-cans. Tess held it from slipping off him as well as herself, Clare’s hands being occupied.\n\n“Now we are all right again. Ah—no we are not! It runs down into my neck a little, and it must still more into yours. That’s better. Your arms are like wet marble, Tess. Wipe them in the cloth. Now, if you stay quiet, you will not get another drop. Well, dear—about that question of mine—that long-standing question?”\n\nThe only reply that he could hear for a little while was the smack of the horse’s hoofs on the moistening road, and the cluck of the milk in the cans behind them.\n\n“Do you remember what you said?”\n\n“I do,” she replied.\n\n“Before we get home, mind.”\n\n“I’ll try.”\n\nHe said no more then. As they drove on, the fragment of an old manor house of Caroline date rose against the sky, and was in due course passed and left behind.\n\n“That,” he observed, to entertain her, “is an interesting old place—one of the several seats which belonged to an ancient Norman family formerly of great influence in this county, the d’Urbervilles. I never pass one of their residences without thinking of them. There is something very sad in the extinction of a family of renown, even if it was fierce, domineering, feudal renown.”\n\n“Yes,” said Tess.\n\nThey crept along towards a point in the expanse of shade just at hand at which a feeble light was beginning to assert its presence, a spot where, by day, a fitful white streak of steam at intervals upon the dark green background denoted intermittent moments of contact between their secluded world and modern life. Modern life stretched out its steam feeler to this point three or four times a day, touched the native existences, and quickly withdrew its feeler again, as if what it touched had been uncongenial.\n\nThey reached the feeble light, which came from the smoky lamp of a little railway station; a poor enough terrestrial star, yet in one sense of more importance to Talbothays Dairy and mankind than the celestial ones to which it stood in such humiliating contrast. The cans of new milk were unladen in the rain, Tess getting a little shelter from a neighbouring holly tree.\n\nThen there was the hissing of a train, which drew up almost silently upon the wet rails, and the milk was rapidly swung can by can into the truck. The light of the engine flashed for a second upon Tess Durbeyfield’s figure, motionless under the great holly tree. No object could have looked more foreign to the gleaming cranks and wheels than this unsophisticated girl, with the round bare arms, the rainy face and hair, the suspended attitude of a friendly leopard at pause, the print gown of no date or fashion, and the cotton bonnet drooping on her brow.\n\nShe mounted again beside her lover, with a mute obedience characteristic of impassioned natures at times, and when they had wrapped themselves up over head and ears in the sailcloth again, they plunged back into the now thick night. Tess was so receptive that the few minutes of contact with the whirl of material progress lingered in her thought.\n\n“Londoners will drink it at their breakfasts to-morrow, won’t they?” she asked. “Strange people that we have never seen.”\n\n“Yes—I suppose they will. Though not as we send it. When its strength has been lowered, so that it may not get up into their heads.”\n\n“Noble men and noble women, ambassadors and centurions, ladies and tradeswomen, and babies who have never seen a cow.”\n\n“Well, yes; perhaps; particularly centurions.”\n\n“Who don’t know anything of us, and where it comes from; or think how we two drove miles across the moor to-night in the rain that it might reach ’em in time?”\n\n“We did not drive entirely on account of these precious Londoners; we drove a little on our own—on account of that anxious matter which you will, I am sure, set at rest, dear Tess. Now, permit me to put it in this way. You belong to me already, you know; your heart, I mean. Does it not?”\n\n“You know as well as I. O yes—yes!”\n\n“Then, if your heart does, why not your hand?”\n\n“My only reason was on account of you—on account of a question. I have something to tell you—”\n\n“But suppose it to be entirely for my happiness, and my worldly convenience also?”\n\n“O yes; if it is for your happiness and worldly convenience. But my life before I came here—I want—”\n\n“Well, it is for my convenience as well as my happiness. If I have a very large farm, either English or colonial, you will be invaluable as a wife to me; better than a woman out of the largest mansion in the country. So please—please, dear Tessy, disabuse your mind of the feeling that you will stand in my way.”\n\n“But my history. I want you to know it—you must let me tell you—you will not like me so well!”\n\n“Tell it if you wish to, dearest. This precious history then. Yes, I was born at so and so, Anno Domini—”\n\n“I was born at Marlott,” she said, catching at his words as a help, lightly as they were spoken. “And I grew up there. And I was in the Sixth Standard when I left school, and they said I had great aptness, and should make a good teacher, so it was settled that I should be one. But there was trouble in my family; father was not very industrious, and he drank a little.”\n\n“Yes, yes. Poor child! Nothing new.” He pressed her more closely to his side.\n\n“And then—there is something very unusual about it—about me. I—I was—”\n\nTess’s breath quickened.\n\n“Yes, dearest. Never mind.”\n\n“I—I—am not a Durbeyfield, but a d’Urberville—a descendant of the same family as those that owned the old house we passed. And—we are all gone to nothing!”\n\n“A d’Urberville!—Indeed! And is that all the trouble, dear Tess?”\n\n“Yes,” she answered faintly.\n\n“Well—why should I love you less after knowing this?”\n\n“I was told by the dairyman that you hated old families.”\n\nHe laughed.\n\n“Well, it is true, in one sense. I do hate the aristocratic principle of blood before everything, and do think that as reasoners the only pedigrees we ought to respect are those spiritual ones of the wise and virtuous, without regard to corporal paternity. But I am extremely interested in this news—you can have no idea how interested I am! Are you not interested yourself in being one of that well-known line?”\n\n“No. I have thought it sad—especially since coming here, and knowing that many of the hills and fields I see once belonged to my father’s people. But other hills and fields belonged to Retty’s people, and perhaps others to Marian’s, so that I don’t value it particularly.”\n\n“Yes—it is surprising how many of the present tillers of the soil were once owners of it, and I sometimes wonder that a certain school of politicians don’t make capital of the circumstance; but they don’t seem to know it... I wonder that I did not see the resemblance of your name to d’Urberville, and trace the manifest corruption. And this was the carking secret!”\n\nShe had not told. At the last moment her courage had failed her; she feared his blame for not telling him sooner; and her instinct of self-preservation was stronger than her candour.\n\n“Of course,” continued the unwitting Clare, “I should have been glad to know you to be descended exclusively from the long-suffering, dumb, unrecorded rank and file of the English nation, and not from the self-seeking few who made themselves powerful at the expense of the rest. But I am corrupted away from that by my affection for you, Tess (he laughed as he spoke), and made selfish likewise. For your own sake I rejoice in your descent. Society is hopelessly snobbish, and this fact of your extraction may make an appreciable difference to its acceptance of you as my wife, after I have made you the well-read woman that I mean to make you. My mother too, poor soul, will think so much better of you on account of it. Tess, you must spell your name correctly—d’Urberville—from this very day.”\n\n“I like the other way rather best.”\n\n“But you _must_, dearest! Good heavens, why dozens of mushroom millionaires would jump at such a possession! By the bye, there’s one of that kidney who has taken the name—where have I heard of him?—Up in the neighbourhood of The Chase, I think. Why, he is the very man who had that rumpus with my father I told you of. What an odd coincidence!”\n\n“Angel, I think I would rather not take the name! It is unlucky, perhaps!”\n\nShe was agitated.\n\n“Now then, Mistress Teresa d’Urberville, I have you. Take my name, and so you will escape yours! The secret is out, so why should you any longer refuse me?”\n\n“If it is _sure_ to make you happy to have me as your wife, and you feel that you do wish to marry me, _very, very_ much—”\n\n“I do, dearest, of course!”\n\n“I mean, that it is only your wanting me very much, and being hardly able to keep alive without me, whatever my offences, that would make me feel I ought to say I will.”\n\n“You will—you do say it, I know! You will be mine for ever and ever.”\nHe clasped her close and kissed her.\n\n“Yes!”\n\nShe had no sooner said it than she burst into a dry hard sobbing, so violent that it seemed to rend her. Tess was not a hysterical girl by any means, and he was surprised.\n\n“Why do you cry, dearest?”\n\n“I can’t tell—quite!—I am so glad to think—of being yours, and making you happy!”\n\n“But this does not seem very much like gladness, my Tessy!”\n\n“I mean—I cry because I have broken down in my vow! I said I would die unmarried!”\n\n“But, if you love me you would like me to be your husband?”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes! But O, I sometimes wish I had never been born!”\n\n“Now, my dear Tess, if I did not know that you are very much excited, and very inexperienced, I should say that remark was not very complimentary. How came you to wish that if you care for me? Do you care for me? I wish you would prove it in some way.”\n\n“How can I prove it more than I have done?” she cried, in a distraction of tenderness. “Will this prove it more?”\n\nShe clasped his neck, and for the first time Clare learnt what an impassioned woman’s kisses were like upon the lips of one whom she loved with all her heart and soul, as Tess loved him.\n\n“There—now do you believe?” she asked, flushed, and wiping her eyes.\n\n“Yes. I never really doubted—never, never!”\n\nSo they drove on through the gloom, forming one bundle inside the sail-cloth, the horse going as he would, and the rain driving against them. She had consented. She might as well have agreed at first. The “appetite for joy” which pervades all creation, that tremendous force which sways humanity to its purpose, as the tide sways the helpless weed, was not to be controlled by vague lucubrations over the social rubric.\n\n“I must write to my mother,” she said. “You don’t mind my doing that?”\n\n“Of course not, dear child. You are a child to me, Tess, not to know how very proper it is to write to your mother at such a time, and how wrong it would be in me to object. Where does she live?”\n\n“At the same place—Marlott. On the further side of Blackmoor Vale.”\n\n“Ah, then I _have_ seen you before this summer—”\n\n“Yes; at that dance on the green; but you would not dance with me. O, I hope that is of no ill-omen for us now!”\n\n# XXXI\n\nTess wrote a most touching and urgent letter to her mother the very next day, and by the end of the week a response to her communication arrived in Joan Durbeyfield’s wandering last-century hand.\n\nDear Tess,\n\nJ write these few lines Hoping they will find you well, as they leave me at Present, thank God for it. Dear Tess, we are all glad to Hear that you are going really to be married soon. But with respect to your question, Tess, J say between ourselves, quite private but very strong, that on no account do you say a word of your Bygone Trouble to him. J did not tell everything to your Father, he being so Proud on account of his Respectability, which, perhaps, your Intended is the same. Many a woman—some of the Highest in the Land—have had a Trouble in their time; and why should you Trumpet yours when others don’t Trumpet theirs? No girl would be such a Fool, specially as it is so long ago, and not your Fault at all. J shall answer the same if you ask me fifty times. Besides, you must bear in mind that, knowing it to be your Childish Nature to tell all that’s in your heart—so simple!—J made you promise me never to let it out by Word or Deed, having your Welfare in my Mind; and you most solemnly did promise it going from this Door. J have not named either that Question or your coming marriage to your Father, as he would blab it everywhere, poor Simple Man.\nDear Tess, keep up your Spirits, and we mean to send you a Hogshead of Cyder for you Wedding, knowing there is not much in your parts, and thin Sour Stuff what there is. So no more at present, and with kind love to your Young Man.—From your affectte. Mother,\n\nJ. Durbeyfield\n\n“O mother, mother!” murmured Tess.\n\nShe was recognizing how light was the touch of events the most oppressive upon Mrs Durbeyfield’s elastic spirit. Her mother did not see life as Tess saw it. That haunting episode of bygone days was to her mother but a passing accident. But perhaps her mother was right as to the course to be followed, whatever she might be in her reasons. Silence seemed, on the face of it, best for her adored one’s happiness: silence it should be.\n\nThus steadied by a command from the only person in the world who had any shadow of right to control her action, Tess grew calmer. The responsibility was shifted, and her heart was lighter than it had been for weeks. The days of declining autumn which followed her assent, beginning with the month of October, formed a season through which she lived in spiritual altitudes more nearly approaching ecstasy than any other period of her life.\n\nThere was hardly a touch of earth in her love for Clare. To her sublime trustfulness he was all that goodness could be—knew all that a guide, philosopher, and friend should know. She thought every line in the contour of his person the perfection of masculine beauty, his soul the soul of a saint, his intellect that of a seer. The wisdom of her love for him, as love, sustained her dignity; she seemed to be wearing a crown. The compassion of his love for her, as she saw it, made her lift up her heart to him in devotion. He would sometimes catch her large, worshipful eyes, that had no bottom to them looking at him from their depths, as if she saw something immortal before her.\n\nShe dismissed the past—trod upon it and put it out, as one treads on a coal that is smouldering and dangerous.\n\nShe had not known that men could be so disinterested, chivalrous, protective, in their love for women as he. Angel Clare was far from all that she thought him in this respect; absurdly far, indeed; but he was, in truth, more spiritual than animal; he had himself well in hand, and was singularly free from grossness. Though not cold-natured, he was rather bright than hot—less Byronic than Shelleyan; could love desperately, but with a love more especially inclined to the imaginative and ethereal; it was a fastidious emotion which could jealously guard the loved one against his very self. This amazed and enraptured Tess, whose slight experiences had been so infelicitous till now; and in her reaction from indignation against the male sex she swerved to excess of honour for Clare.\n\nThey unaffectedly sought each other’s company; in her honest faith she did not disguise her desire to be with him. The sum of her instincts on this matter, if clearly stated, would have been that the elusive quality of her sex which attracts men in general might be distasteful to so perfect a man after an avowal of love, since it must in its very nature carry with it a suspicion of art.\n\nThe country custom of unreserved comradeship out of doors during betrothal was the only custom she knew, and to her it had no strangeness; though it seemed oddly anticipative to Clare till he saw how normal a thing she, in common with all the other dairy-folk, regarded it. Thus, during this October month of wonderful afternoons they roved along the meads by creeping paths which followed the brinks of trickling tributary brooks, hopping across by little wooden bridges to the other side, and back again. They were never out of the sound of some purling weir, whose buzz accompanied their own murmuring, while the beams of the sun, almost as horizontal as the mead itself, formed a pollen of radiance over the landscape. They saw tiny blue fogs in the shadows of trees and hedges, all the time that there was bright sunshine elsewhere. The sun was so near the ground, and the sward so flat, that the shadows of Clare and Tess would stretch a quarter of a mile ahead of them, like two long fingers pointing afar to where the green alluvial reaches abutted against the sloping sides of the vale.\n\nMen were at work here and there—for it was the season for “taking up” the meadows, or digging the little waterways clear for the winter irrigation, and mending their banks where trodden down by the cows. The shovelfuls of loam, black as jet, brought there by the river when it was as wide as the whole valley, were an essence of soils, pounded champaigns of the past, steeped, refined, and subtilized to extraordinary richness, out of which came all the fertility of the mead, and of the cattle grazing there.\n\nClare hardily kept his arm round her waist in sight of these watermen, with the air of a man who was accustomed to public dalliance, though actually as shy as she who, with lips parted and eyes askance on the labourers, wore the look of a wary animal the while.\n\n“You are not ashamed of owning me as yours before them!” she said gladly.\n“O no!”\n\n“But if it should reach the ears of your friends at Emminster that you are walking about like this with me, a milkmaid—”\n\n“The most bewitching milkmaid ever seen.”\n\n“They might feel it a hurt to their dignity.”\n\n“My dear girl—a d’Urberville hurt the dignity of a Clare! It is a grand card to play—that of your belonging to such a family, and I am reserving it for a grand effect when we are married, and have the proofs of your descent from Parson Tringham. Apart from that, my future is to be totally foreign to my family—it will not affect even the surface of their lives. We shall leave this part of England—perhaps England itself—and what does it matter how people regard us here? You will like going, will you not?”\n\nShe could answer no more than a bare affirmative, so great was the emotion aroused in her at the thought of going through the world with him as his own familiar friend. Her feelings almost filled her ears like a babble of waves, and surged up to her eyes. She put her hand in his, and thus they went on, to a place where the reflected sun glared up from the river, under a bridge, with a molten-metallic glow that dazzled their eyes, though the sun itself was hidden by the bridge. They stood still, whereupon little furred and feathered heads popped up from the smooth surface of the water; but, finding that the disturbing presences had paused, and not passed by, they disappeared again. Upon this river-brink they lingered till the fog began to close round them—which was very early in the evening at this time of the year—settling on the lashes of her eyes, where it rested like crystals, and on his brows and hair.\n\nThey walked later on Sundays, when it was quite dark. Some of the dairy-people, who were also out of doors on the first Sunday evening after their engagement, heard her impulsive speeches, ecstasized to fragments, though they were too far off to hear the words discoursed; noted the spasmodic catch in her remarks, broken into syllables by the leapings of her heart, as she walked leaning on his arm; her contented pauses, the occasional little laugh upon which her soul seemed to ride—the laugh of a woman in company with the man she loves and has won from all other women—unlike anything else in nature. They marked the buoyancy of her tread, like the skim of a bird which has not quite alighted.\n\nHer affection for him was now the breath and life of Tess’s being; it enveloped her as a photosphere, irradiated her into forgetfulness of her past sorrows, keeping back the gloomy spectres that would persist in their attempts to touch her—doubt, fear, moodiness, care, shame. She knew that they were waiting like wolves just outside the circumscribing light, but she had long spells of power to keep them in hungry subjection there.\n\nA spiritual forgetfulness co-existed with an intellectual remembrance. She walked in brightness, but she knew that in the background those shapes of darkness were always spread. They might be receding, or they might be approaching, one or the other, a little every day.\n\nOne evening Tess and Clare were obliged to sit indoors keeping house, all the other occupants of the domicile being away. As they talked she looked thoughtfully up at him, and met his two appreciative eyes.\n\n“I am not worthy of you—no, I am not!” she burst out, jumping up from her low stool as though appalled at his homage, and the fulness of her own joy thereat.\n\nClare, deeming the whole basis of her excitement to be that which was only the smaller part of it, said—\n\n“I won’t have you speak like it, dear Tess! Distinction does not consist in the facile use of a contemptible set of conventions, but in being numbered among those who are true, and honest, and just, and pure, and lovely, and of good report—as you are, my Tess.”\n\nShe struggled with the sob in her throat. How often had that string of excellences made her young heart ache in church of late years, and how strange that he should have cited them now.\n\n“Why didn’t you stay and love me when I—was sixteen; living with my little sisters and brothers, and you danced on the green? O, why didn’t you, why didn’t you!” she said, impetuously clasping her hands.\n\nAngel began to comfort and reassure her, thinking to himself, truly enough, what a creature of moods she was, and how careful he would have to be of her when she depended for her happiness entirely on him.\n\n“Ah—why didn’t I stay!” he said. “That is just what I feel. If I had only known! But you must not be so bitter in your regret—why should you be?”\n\nWith the woman’s instinct to hide she diverged hastily—\n\n“I should have had four years more of your heart than I can ever have now. Then I should not have wasted my time as I have done—I should have had so much longer happiness!”\n\nIt was no mature woman with a long dark vista of intrigue behind her who was tormented thus, but a girl of simple life, not yet one-and twenty, who had been caught during her days of immaturity like a bird in a springe. To calm herself the more completely, she rose from her little stool and left the room, overturning the stool with her skirts as she went.\n\nHe sat on by the cheerful firelight thrown from a bundle of green ash-sticks laid across the dogs; the sticks snapped pleasantly, and hissed out bubbles of sap from their ends. When she came back she was herself again.\n\n“Do you not think you are just a wee bit capricious, fitful, Tess?” he said, good-humouredly, as he spread a cushion for her on the stool, and seated himself in the settle beside her. “I wanted to ask you something, and just then you ran away.”\n\n“Yes, perhaps I am capricious,” she murmured. She suddenly approached him, and put a hand upon each of his arms. “No, Angel, I am not really so—by nature, I mean!” The more particularly to assure him that she was not, she placed herself close to him in the settle, and allowed her head to find a resting-place against Clare’s shoulder. “What did you want to ask me—I am sure I will answer it,” she continued humbly.\n\n“Well, you love me, and have agreed to marry me, and hence there follows a thirdly, ‘When shall the day be?’”\n\n“I like living like this.”\n\n“But I must think of starting in business on my own hook with the new year, or a little later. And before I get involved in the multifarious details of my new position, I should like to have secured my partner.”\n\n“But,” she timidly answered, “to talk quite practically, wouldn’t it be best not to marry till after all that?—Though I can’t bear the thought o’ your going away and leaving me here!”\n\n“Of course you cannot—and it is not best in this case. I want you to help me in many ways in making my start. When shall it be? Why not a fortnight from now?”\n\n“No,” she said, becoming grave: “I have so many things to think of first.”\n\n“But—”\nHe drew her gently nearer to him.\n\nThe reality of marriage was startling when it loomed so near. Before discussion of the question had proceeded further there walked round the corner of the settle into the full firelight of the apartment Mr Dairyman Crick, Mrs Crick, and two of the milkmaids.\n\nTess sprang like an elastic ball from his side to her feet, while her face flushed and her eyes shone in the firelight.\n\n“I knew how it would be if I sat so close to him!” she cried, with vexation. “I said to myself, they are sure to come and catch us! But I wasn’t really sitting on his knee, though it might ha’ seemed as if I was almost!”\n\n“Well—if so be you hadn’t told us, I am sure we shouldn’t ha’ noticed that ye had been sitting anywhere at all in this light,” replied the dairyman. He continued to his wife, with the stolid mien of a man who understood nothing of the emotions relating to matrimony—“Now, Christianer, that shows that folks should never fancy other folks be supposing things when they bain’t. O no, I should never ha’ thought a word of where she was a sitting to, if she hadn’t told me—not I.”\n\n“We are going to be married soon,” said Clare, with improvised phlegm.\n\n“Ah—and be ye! Well, I am truly glad to hear it, sir. I’ve thought you mid do such a thing for some time. She’s too good for a dairymaid—I said so the very first day I zid her—and a prize for any man; and what’s more, a wonderful woman for a gentleman-farmer’s wife; he won’t be at the mercy of his baily wi’ her at his side.”\n\nSomehow Tess disappeared. She had been even more struck with the look of the girls who followed Crick than abashed by Crick’s blunt praise.\nAfter supper, when she reached her bedroom, they were all present. A light was burning, and each damsel was sitting up whitely in her bed, awaiting Tess, the whole like a row of avenging ghosts.\n\nBut she saw in a few moments that there was no malice in their mood. They could scarcely feel as a loss what they had never expected to have. Their condition was objective, contemplative.\n\n“He’s going to marry her!” murmured Retty, never taking eyes off Tess. “How her face do show it!”\n\n“You _be_ going to marry him?” asked Marian.\n\n“Yes,” said Tess.\n\n“When?”\n\n“Some day.”\n\nThey thought that this was evasiveness only.\n\n“_Yes_—going to _marry_ him—a gentleman!” repeated Izz Huett.\n\nAnd by a sort of fascination the three girls, one after another, crept out of their beds, and came and stood barefooted round Tess. Retty put her hands upon Tess’s shoulders, as if to realize her friend’s corporeality after such a miracle, and the other two laid their arms round her waist, all looking into her face.\n\n“How it do seem! Almost more than I can think of!” said Izz Huett.\n\nMarian kissed Tess. “Yes,” she murmured as she withdrew her lips.\n\n“Was that because of love for her, or because other lips have touched there by now?” continued Izz drily to Marian.\n\n“I wasn’t thinking o’ that,” said Marian simply. “I was on’y feeling all the strangeness o’t—that she is to be his wife, and nobody else. I don’t say nay to it, nor either of us, because we did not think of it—only loved him. Still, nobody else is to marry’n in the world—no fine lady, nobody in silks and satins; but she who do live like we.”\n\n“Are you sure you don’t dislike me for it?” said Tess in a low voice.\n\nThey hung about her in their white nightgowns before replying, as if they considered their answer might lie in her look.\n\n“I don’t know—I don’t know,” murmured Retty Priddle. “I want to hate ’ee; but I cannot!”\n\n“That’s how I feel,” echoed Izz and Marian. “I can’t hate her. Somehow she hinders me!”\n\n“He ought to marry one of you,” murmured Tess.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“You are all better than I.”\n\n“We better than you?” said the girls in a low, slow whisper. “No, no, dear Tess!”\n\n“You are!” she contradicted impetuously. And suddenly tearing away from their clinging arms she burst into a hysterical fit of tears, bowing herself on the chest of drawers and repeating incessantly, “O yes, yes, yes!”\n\nHaving once given way she could not stop her weeping.\n\n“He ought to have had one of you!” she cried. “I think I ought to make him even now! You would be better for him than—I don’t know what I’m saying! O! O!”\n\nThey went up to her and clasped her round, but still her sobs tore her.\n\n“Get some water,” said Marian, “She’s upset by us, poor thing, poor thing!”\n\nThey gently led her back to the side of her bed, where they kissed her warmly.\n\n“You are best for’n,” said Marian. “More ladylike, and a better scholar than we, especially since he had taught ’ee so much. But even you ought to be proud. You _be_ proud, I’m sure!”\n\n“Yes, I am,” she said; “and I am ashamed at so breaking down.”\n\nWhen they were all in bed, and the light was out, Marian whispered across to her—\n\n“You will think of us when you be his wife, Tess, and of how we told ’ee that we loved him, and how we tried not to hate you, and did not hate you, and could not hate you, because you were his choice, and we never hoped to be chose by him.”\n\nThey were not aware that, at these words, salt, stinging tears trickled down upon Tess’s pillow anew, and how she resolved, with a bursting heart, to tell all her history to Angel Clare, despite her mother’s command—to let him for whom she lived and breathed despise her if he would, and her mother regard her as a fool, rather then preserve a silence which might be deemed a treachery to him, and which somehow seemed a wrong to these.\n\n# XXXII\n\nThis penitential mood kept her from naming the wedding-day. The beginning of November found its date still in abeyance, though he asked her at the most tempting times. But Tess’s desire seemed to be for a perpetual betrothal in which everything should remain as it was then.\n\nThe meads were changing now; but it was still warm enough in early afternoons before milking to idle there awhile, and the state of dairy-work at this time of year allowed a spare hour for idling. Looking over the damp sod in the direction of the sun, a glistening ripple of gossamer webs was visible to their eyes under the luminary, like the track of moonlight on the sea. Gnats, knowing nothing of their brief glorification, wandered across the shimmer of this pathway, irradiated as if they bore fire within them, then passed out of its line, and were quite extinct. In the presence of these things he would remind her that the date was still the question.\n\nOr he would ask her at night, when he accompanied her on some mission invented by Mrs Crick to give him the opportunity. This was mostly a journey to the farmhouse on the slopes above the vale, to inquire how the advanced cows were getting on in the straw-barton to which they were relegated. For it was a time of the year that brought great changes to the world of kine. Batches of the animals were sent away daily to this lying-in hospital, where they lived on straw till their calves were born, after which event, and as soon as the calf could walk, mother and offspring were driven back to the dairy. In the interval which elapsed before the calves were sold there was, of course, little milking to be done, but as soon as the calf had been taken away the milkmaids would have to set to work as usual.\n\nReturning from one of these dark walks they reached a great gravel-cliff immediately over the levels, where they stood still and listened. The water was now high in the streams, squirting through the weirs, and tinkling under culverts; the smallest gullies were all full; there was no taking short cuts anywhere, and foot-passengers were compelled to follow the permanent ways. From the whole extent of the invisible vale came a multitudinous intonation; it forced upon their fancy that a great city lay below them, and that the murmur was the vociferation of its populace.\n\n“It seems like tens of thousands of them,” said Tess; “holding public-meetings in their market-places, arguing, preaching, quarrelling, sobbing, groaning, praying, and cursing.”\n\nClare was not particularly heeding.\n\n“Did Crick speak to you to-day, dear, about his not wanting much assistance during the winter months?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“The cows are going dry rapidly.”\n\n“Yes. Six or seven went to the straw-barton yesterday, and three the day before, making nearly twenty in the straw already. Ah—is it that the farmer don’t want my help for the calving? O, I am not wanted here any more! And I have tried so hard to—”\n\n“Crick didn’t exactly say that he would no longer require you. But, knowing what our relations were, he said in the most good-natured and respectful manner possible that he supposed on my leaving at Christmas I should take you with me, and on my asking what he would do without you he merely observed that, as a matter of fact, it was a time of year when he could do with a very little female help. I am afraid I was sinner enough to feel rather glad that he was in this way forcing your hand.”\n\n“I don’t think you ought to have felt glad, Angel. Because ’tis always mournful not to be wanted, even if at the same time ’tis convenient.”\n\n“Well, it is convenient—you have admitted that.” He put his finger upon her cheek. “Ah!” he said.\n\n“What?”\n\n“I feel the red rising up at her having been caught! But why should I trifle so! We will not trifle—life is too serious.”\n\n“It is. Perhaps I saw that before you did.”\n\nShe was seeing it then. To decline to marry him after all—in obedience to her emotion of last night—and leave the dairy, meant to go to some strange place, not a dairy; for milkmaids were not in request now calving-time was coming on; to go to some arable farm where no divine being like Angel Clare was. She hated the thought, and she hated more the thought of going home.\n\n“So that, seriously, dearest Tess,” he continued, “since you will probably have to leave at Christmas, it is in every way desirable and convenient that I should carry you off then as my property. Besides, if you were not the most uncalculating girl in the world you would know that we could not go on like this for ever.”\n\n“I wish we could. That it would always be summer and autumn, and you always courting me, and always thinking as much of me as you have done through the past summer-time!”\n\n“I always shall.”\n\n“O, I know you will!” she cried, with a sudden fervour of faith in him. “Angel, I will fix the day when I will become yours for always!”\n\nThus at last it was arranged between them, during that dark walk home, amid the myriads of liquid voices on the right and left.\n\nWhen they reached the dairy Mr and Mrs Crick were promptly told—with injunctions of secrecy; for each of the lovers was desirous that the marriage should be kept as private as possible. The dairyman, though he had thought of dismissing her soon, now made a great concern about losing her. What should he do about his skimming? Who would make the ornamental butter-pats for the Anglebury and Sandbourne ladies? Mrs Crick congratulated Tess on the shilly-shallying having at last come to an end, and said that directly she set eyes on Tess she divined that she was to be the chosen one of somebody who was no common outdoor man; Tess had looked so superior as she walked across the barton on that afternoon of her arrival; that she was of a good family she could have sworn. In point of fact Mrs Crick did remember thinking that Tess was graceful and good-looking as she approached; but the superiority might have been a growth of the imagination aided by subsequent knowledge.\n\nTess was now carried along upon the wings of the hours, without the sense of a will. The word had been given; the number of the day written down. Her naturally bright intelligence had begun to admit the fatalistic convictions common to field-folk and those who associate more extensively with natural phenomena than with their fellow-creatures; and she accordingly drifted into that passive responsiveness to all things her lover suggested, characteristic of the frame of mind.\n\nBut she wrote anew to her mother, ostensibly to notify the wedding-day; really to again implore her advice. It was a gentleman who had chosen her, which perhaps her mother had not sufficiently considered. A post-nuptial explanation, which might be accepted with a light heart by a rougher man, might not be received with the same feeling by him. But this communication brought no reply from Mrs Durbeyfield.\n\nDespite Angel Clare’s plausible representation to himself and to Tess of the practical need for their immediate marriage, there was in truth an element of precipitancy in the step, as became apparent at a later date. He loved her dearly, though perhaps rather ideally and fancifully than with the impassioned thoroughness of her feeling for him. He had entertained no notion, when doomed as he had thought to an unintellectual bucolic life, that such charms as he beheld in this idyllic creature would be found behind the scenes. Unsophistication was a thing to talk of; but he had not known how it really struck one until he came here. Yet he was very far from seeing his future track clearly, and it might be a year or two before he would be able to consider himself fairly started in life. The secret lay in the tinge of recklessness imparted to his career and character by the sense that he had been made to miss his true destiny through the prejudices of his family.\n\n“Don’t you think ’twould have been better for us to wait till you were quite settled in your midland farm?” she once asked timidly. (A midland farm was the idea just then.)\n\n“To tell the truth, my Tess, I don’t like you to be left anywhere away from my protection and sympathy.”\n\nThe reason was a good one, so far as it went. His influence over her had been so marked that she had caught his manner and habits, his speech and phrases, his likings and his aversions. And to leave her in farmland would be to let her slip back again out of accord with him. He wished to have her under his charge for another reason. His parents had naturally desired to see her once at least before he carried her off to a distant settlement, English or colonial; and as no opinion of theirs was to be allowed to change his intention, he judged that a couple of months’ life with him in lodgings whilst seeking for an advantageous opening would be of some social assistance to her at what she might feel to be a trying ordeal—her presentation to his mother at the Vicarage.\n\nNext, he wished to see a little of the working of a flour-mill, having an idea that he might combine the use of one with corn-growing. The proprietor of a large old water-mill at Wellbridge—once the mill of an Abbey—had offered him the inspection of his time-honoured mode of procedure, and a hand in the operations for a few days, whenever he should choose to come. Clare paid a visit to the place, some few miles distant, one day at this time, to inquire particulars, and returned to Talbothays in the evening. She found him determined to spend a short time at the Wellbridge flour-mills. And what had determined him? Less the opportunity of an insight into grinding and bolting than the casual fact that lodgings were to be obtained in that very farmhouse which, before its mutilation, had been the mansion of a branch of the d’Urberville family. This was always how Clare settled practical questions; by a sentiment which had nothing to do with them. They decided to go immediately after the wedding, and remain for a fortnight, instead of journeying to towns and inns.\n\n“Then we will start off to examine some farms on the other side of London that I have heard of,” he said, “and by March or April we will pay a visit to my father and mother.”\n\nQuestions of procedure such as these arose and passed, and the day, the incredible day, on which she was to become his, loomed large in the near future. The thirty-first of December, New Year’s Eve, was the date. His wife, she said to herself. Could it ever be? Their two selves together, nothing to divide them, every incident shared by them; why not? And yet why?\n\nOne Sunday morning Izz Huett returned from church, and spoke privately to Tess.\n\n“You was not called home this morning.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“It should ha’ been the first time of asking to-day,” she answered, looking quietly at Tess. “You meant to be married New Year’s Eve, deary?”\n\nThe other returned a quick affirmative.\n\n“And there must be three times of asking. And now there be only two Sundays left between.”\n\nTess felt her cheek paling; Izz was right; of course there must be three. Perhaps he had forgotten! If so, there must be a week’s postponement, and that was unlucky. How could she remind her lover? She who had been so backward was suddenly fired with impatience and alarm lest she should lose her dear prize.\n\nA natural incident relieved her anxiety. Izz mentioned the omission of the banns to Mrs Crick, and Mrs Crick assumed a matron’s privilege of speaking to Angel on the point.\n\n“Have ye forgot ’em, Mr Clare? The banns, I mean.”\n\n“No, I have not forgot ’em,” says Clare.\n\nAs soon as he caught Tess alone he assured her:\n\n“Don’t let them tease you about the banns. A licence will be quieter for us, and I have decided on a licence without consulting you. So if you go to church on Sunday morning you will not hear your own name, if you wished to.”\n\n“I didn’t wish to hear it, dearest,” she said proudly.\n\nBut to know that things were in train was an immense relief to Tess notwithstanding, who had well-nigh feared that somebody would stand up and forbid the banns on the ground of her history. How events were favouring her!\n\n“I don’t quite feel easy,” she said to herself. “All this good fortune may be scourged out of me afterwards by a lot of ill. That’s how Heaven mostly does. I wish I could have had common banns!”\n\nBut everything went smoothly. She wondered whether he would like her to be married in her present best white frock, or if she ought to buy a new one. The question was set at rest by his forethought, disclosed by the arrival of some large packages addressed to her. Inside them she found a whole stock of clothing, from bonnet to shoes, including a perfect morning costume, such as would well suit the simple wedding they planned. He entered the house shortly after the arrival of the packages, and heard her upstairs undoing them.\n\nA minute later she came down with a flush on her face and tears in her eyes.\n\n“How thoughtful you’ve been!” she murmured, her cheek upon his shoulder. “Even to the gloves and handkerchief! My own love—how good, how kind!”\n\n“No, no, Tess; just an order to a tradeswoman in London—nothing more.”\nAnd to divert her from thinking too highly of him, he told her to go upstairs, and take her time, and see if it all fitted; and, if not, to get the village sempstress to make a few alterations.\n\nShe did return upstairs, and put on the gown. Alone, she stood for a moment before the glass looking at the effect of her silk attire; and then there came into her head her mother’s ballad of the mystic robe—\n\nThat never would become that wife That had once done amiss,\n\nwhich Mrs Durbeyfield had used to sing to her as a child, so blithely and so archly, her foot on the cradle, which she rocked to the tune. Suppose this robe should betray her by changing colour, as her robe had betrayed Queen Guinevere. Since she had been at the dairy she had not once thought of the lines till now.\n\n# XXXIII\n\nAngel felt that he would like to spend a day with her before the wedding, somewhere away from the dairy, as a last jaunt in her company while there were yet mere lover and mistress; a romantic day, in circumstances that would never be repeated; with that other and greater day beaming close ahead of them. During the preceding week, therefore, he suggested making a few purchases in the nearest town, and they started together.\n\nClare’s life at the dairy had been that of a recluse in respect the world of his own class. For months he had never gone near a town, and, requiring no vehicle, had never kept one, hiring the dairyman’s cob or gig if he rode or drove. They went in the gig that day.\n\nAnd then for the first time in their lives they shopped as partners in one concern. It was Christmas Eve, with its loads a holly and mistletoe, and the town was very full of strangers who had come in from all parts of the country on account of the day. Tess paid the penalty of walking about with happiness superadded to beauty on her countenance by being much stared at as she moved amid them on his arm.\n\nIn the evening they returned to the inn at which they had put up, and Tess waited in the entry while Angel went to see the horse and gig brought to the door. The general sitting-room was full of guests, who were continually going in and out. As the door opened and shut each time for the passage of these, the light within the parlour fell full upon Tess’s face. Two men came out and passed by her among the rest. One of them had stared her up and down in surprise, and she fancied he was a Trantridge man, though that village lay so many miles off that Trantridge folk were rarities here.\n\n“A comely maid that,” said the other.\n\n“True, comely enough. But unless I make a great mistake—” And he negatived the remainder of the definition forthwith.\n\nClare had just returned from the stable-yard, and, confronting the man on the threshold, heard the words, and saw the shrinking of Tess. The insult to her stung him to the quick, and before he had considered anything at all he struck the man on the chin with the full force of his fist, sending him staggering backwards into the passage.\n\nThe man recovered himself, and seemed inclined to come on, and Clare, stepping outside the door, put himself in a posture of defence. But his opponent began to think better of the matter. He looked anew at Tess as he passed her, and said to Clare—\n\n“I beg pardon, sir; ’twas a complete mistake. I thought she was another woman, forty miles from here.”\n\nClare, feeling then that he had been too hasty, and that he was, moreover, to blame for leaving her standing in an inn-passage, did what he usually did in such cases, gave the man five shillings to plaster the blow; and thus they parted, bidding each other a pacific good night. As soon as Clare had taken the reins from the ostler, and the young couple had driven off, the two men went in the other direction.\n\n“And was it a mistake?” said the second one.\n\n“Not a bit of it. But I didn’t want to hurt the gentleman’s feelings—not I.”\n\nIn the meantime the lovers were driving onward.\n\n“Could we put off our wedding till a little later?” Tess asked in a dry dull voice. “I mean if we wished?”\n\n“No, my love. Calm yourself. Do you mean that the fellow may have time to summon me for assault?” he asked good-humouredly.\n\n“No—I only meant—if it should have to be put off.”\n\nWhat she meant was not very clear, and he directed her to dismiss such fancies from her mind, which she obediently did as well as she could. But she was grave, very grave, all the way home; till she thought, “We shall go away, a very long distance, hundreds of miles from these parts, and such as this can never happen again, and no ghost of the past reach there.”\n\nThey parted tenderly that night on the landing, and Clare ascended to his attic. Tess sat up getting on with some little requisites, lest the few remaining days should not afford sufficient time. While she sat she heard a noise in Angel’s room overhead, a sound of thumping and struggling. Everybody else in the house was asleep, and in her anxiety lest Clare should be ill she ran up and knocked at his door, and asked him what was the matter.\n\n“Oh, nothing, dear,” he said from within. “I am so sorry I disturbed you! But the reason is rather an amusing one: I fell asleep and dreamt that I was fighting that fellow again who insulted you, and the noise you heard was my pummelling away with my fists at my portmanteau, which I pulled out to-day for packing. I am occasionally liable to these freaks in my sleep. Go to bed and think of it no more.”\n\nThis was the last drachm required to turn the scale of her indecision. Declare the past to him by word of mouth she could not; but there was another way. She sat down and wrote on the four pages of a note-sheet a succinct narrative of those events of three or four years ago, put it into an envelope, and directed it to Clare. Then, lest the flesh should again be weak, she crept upstairs without any shoes and slipped the note under his door.\n\nHer night was a broken one, as it well might be, and she listened for the first faint noise overhead. It came, as usual; he descended, as usual. She descended. He met her at the bottom of the stairs and kissed her. Surely it was as warmly as ever!\n\nHe looked a little disturbed and worn, she thought. But he said not a word to her about her revelation, even when they were alone. Could he have had it? Unless he began the subject she felt that she could say nothing. So the day passed, and it was evident that whatever he thought he meant to keep to himself. Yet he was frank and affectionate as before. Could it be that her doubts were childish? that he forgave her; that he loved her for what she was, just as she was, and smiled at her disquiet as at a foolish nightmare? Had he really received her note? She glanced into his room, and could see nothing of it. It might be that he forgave her. But even if he had not received it she had a sudden enthusiastic trust that he surely would forgive her.\n\nEvery morning and night he was the same, and thus New Year’s Eve broke—the wedding day.\n\nThe lovers did not rise at milking-time, having through the whole of this last week of their sojourn at the dairy been accorded something of the position of guests, Tess being honoured with a room of her own. When they arrived downstairs at breakfast-time they were surprised to see what effects had been produced in the large kitchen for their glory since they had last beheld it. At some unnatural hour of the morning the dairyman had caused the yawning chimney-corner to be whitened, and the brick hearth reddened, and a blazing yellow damask blower to be hung across the arch in place of the old grimy blue cotton one with a black sprig pattern which had formerly done duty there. This renovated aspect of what was the focus indeed of the room on a full winter morning threw a smiling demeanour over the whole apartment.\n\n“I was determined to do summat in honour o’t”, said the dairyman. “And as you wouldn’t hear of my gieing a rattling good randy wi’ fiddles and bass-viols complete, as we should ha’ done in old times, this was all I could think o’ as a noiseless thing.”\n\nTess’s friends lived so far off that none could conveniently have been present at the ceremony, even had any been asked; but as a fact nobody was invited from Marlott. As for Angel’s family, he had written and duly informed them of the time, and assured them that he would be glad to see one at least of them there for the day if he would like to come. His brothers had not replied at all, seeming to be indignant with him; while his father and mother had written a rather sad letter, deploring his precipitancy in rushing into marriage, but making the best of the matter by saying that, though a dairywoman was the last daughter-in-law they could have expected, their son had arrived at an age which he might be supposed to be the best judge.\n\nThis coolness in his relations distressed Clare less than it would have done had he been without the grand card with which he meant to surprise them ere long. To produce Tess, fresh from the dairy, as a d’Urberville and a lady, he had felt to be temerarious and risky; hence he had concealed her lineage till such time as, familiarized with worldly ways by a few months’ travel and reading with him, he could take her on a visit to his parents and impart the knowledge while triumphantly producing her as worthy of such an ancient line. It was a pretty lover’s dream, if no more. Perhaps Tess’s lineage had more value for himself than for anybody in the world beside.\n\nHer perception that Angel’s bearing towards her still remained in no whit altered by her own communication rendered Tess guiltily doubtful if he could have received it. She rose from breakfast before he had finished, and hastened upstairs. It had occurred to her to look once more into the queer gaunt room which had been Clare’s den, or rather eyrie, for so long, and climbing the ladder she stood at the open door of the apartment, regarding and pondering. She stooped to the threshold of the doorway, where she had pushed in the note two or three days earlier in such excitement. The carpet reached close to the sill, and under the edge of the carpet she discerned the faint white margin of the envelope containing her letter to him, which he obviously had never seen, owing to her having in her haste thrust it beneath the carpet as well as beneath the door.\n\nWith a feeling of faintness she withdrew the letter. There it was—sealed up, just as it had left her hands. The mountain had not yet been removed. She could not let him read it now, the house being in full bustle of preparation; and descending to her own room she destroyed the letter there.\n\nShe was so pale when he saw her again that he felt quite anxious. The incident of the misplaced letter she had jumped at as if it prevented a confession; but she knew in her conscience that it need not; there was still time. Yet everything was in a stir; there was coming and going; all had to dress, the dairyman and Mrs Crick having been asked to accompany them as witnesses; and reflection or deliberate talk was well-nigh impossible. The only minute Tess could get to be alone with Clare was when they met upon the landing.\n\n“I am so anxious to talk to you—I want to confess all my faults and blunders!” she said with attempted lightness.\n\n“No, no—we can’t have faults talked of—you must be deemed perfect to-day at least, my Sweet!” he cried. “We shall have plenty of time, hereafter, I hope, to talk over our failings. I will confess mine at the same time.”\n\n“But it would be better for me to do it now, I think, so that you could not say—”\n\n“Well, my quixotic one, you shall tell me anything—say, as soon as we are settled in our lodging; not now. I, too, will tell you my faults then. But do not let us spoil the day with them; they will be excellent matter for a dull time.”\n\n“Then you don’t wish me to, dearest?”\n\n“I do not, Tessy, really.”\n\nThe hurry of dressing and starting left no time for more than this. Those words of his seemed to reassure her on further reflection. She was whirled onward through the next couple of critical hours by the mastering tide of her devotion to him, which closed up further meditation. Her one desire, so long resisted, to make herself his, to call him her lord, her own—then, if necessary, to die—had at last lifted her up from her plodding reflective pathway. In dressing, she moved about in a mental cloud of many-coloured idealities, which eclipsed all sinister contingencies by its brightness.\n\nThe church was a long way off, and they were obliged to drive, particularly as it was winter. A closed carriage was ordered from a roadside inn, a vehicle which had been kept there ever since the old days of post-chaise travelling. It had stout wheel-spokes and heavy felloes, a great curved bed, immense straps and springs, and a pole like a battering-ram. The postilion was a venerable “boy” of sixty—a martyr to rheumatic gout, the result of excessive exposure in youth, counter-acted by strong liquors—who had stood at inn-doors doing nothing for the whole five-and-twenty years that had elapsed since he had no longer been required to ride professionally, as if expecting the old times to come back again. He had a permanent running wound on the outside of his right leg, originated by the constant bruisings of aristocratic carriage-poles during the many years that he had been in regular employ at the King’s Arms, Casterbridge.\n\nInside this cumbrous and creaking structure, and behind this decayed conductor, the _partie carrée_ took their seats—the bride and bridegroom and Mr and Mrs Crick. Angel would have liked one at least of his brothers to be present as groomsman, but their silence after his gentle hint to that effect by letter had signified that they did not care to come. They disapproved of the marriage, and could not be expected to countenance it. Perhaps it was as well that they could not be present. They were not worldly young fellows, but fraternizing with dairy-folk would have struck unpleasantly upon their biased niceness, apart from their views of the match.\n\nUpheld by the momentum of the time, Tess knew nothing of this, did not see anything, did not know the road they were taking to the church. She knew that Angel was close to her; all the rest was a luminous mist. She was a sort of celestial person, who owed her being to poetry—one of those classical divinities Clare was accustomed to talk to her about when they took their walks together.\n\nThe marriage being by licence there were only a dozen or so of people in the church; had there been a thousand they would have produced no more effect upon her. They were at stellar distances from her present world. In the ecstatic solemnity with which she swore her faith to him the ordinary sensibilities of sex seemed a flippancy. At a pause in the service, while they were kneeling together, she unconsciously inclined herself towards him, so that her shoulder touched his arm; she had been frightened by a passing thought, and the movement had been automatic, to assure herself that he was really there, and to fortify her belief that his fidelity would be proof against all things.\n\nClare knew that she loved him—every curve of her form showed that—but he did not know at that time the full depth of her devotion, its single-mindedness, its meekness; what long-suffering it guaranteed, what honesty, what endurance, what good faith.\n\nAs they came out of church the ringers swung the bells off their rests, and a modest peal of three notes broke forth—that limited amount of expression having been deemed sufficient by the church builders for the joys of such a small parish. Passing by the tower with her husband on the path to the gate she could feel the vibrant air humming round them from the louvred belfry in the circle of sound, and it matched the highly-charged mental atmosphere in which she was living.\n\nThis condition of mind, wherein she felt glorified by an irradiation not her own, like the angel whom St John saw in the sun, lasted till the sound of the church bells had died away, and the emotions of the wedding-service had calmed down. Her eyes could dwell upon details more clearly now, and Mr and Mrs Crick having directed their own gig to be sent for them, to leave the carriage to the young couple, she observed the build and character of that conveyance for the first time. Sitting in silence she regarded it long.\n\n“I fancy you seem oppressed, Tessy,” said Clare.\n\n“Yes,” she answered, putting her hand to her brow. “I tremble at many things. It is all so serious, Angel. Among other things I seem to have seen this carriage before, to be very well acquainted with it. It is very odd—I must have seen it in a dream.”\n\n“Oh—you have heard the legend of the d’Urberville Coach—that well-known superstition of this county about your family when they were very popular here; and this lumbering old thing reminds you of it.”\n\n“I have never heard of it to my knowledge,” said she. “What is the legend—may I know it?”\n\n“Well—I would rather not tell it in detail just now. A certain d’Urberville of the sixteenth or seventeenth century committed a dreadful crime in his family coach; and since that time members of the family see or hear the old coach whenever—— But I’ll tell you another day—it is rather gloomy. Evidently some dim knowledge of it has been brought back to your mind by the sight of this venerable caravan.”\n\n“I don’t remember hearing it before,” she murmured. “Is it when we are going to die, Angel, that members of my family see it, or is it when we have committed a crime?”\n\n“Now, Tess!”\n\nHe silenced her by a kiss.\n\nBy the time they reached home she was contrite and spiritless. She was Mrs Angel Clare, indeed, but had she any moral right to the name? Was she not more truly Mrs Alexander d’Urberville? Could intensity of love justify what might be considered in upright souls as culpable reticence? She knew not what was expected of women in such cases; and she had no counsellor.\n\nHowever, when she found herself alone in her room for a few minutes—the last day this on which she was ever to enter it—she knelt down and prayed. She tried to pray to God, but it was her husband who really had her supplication. Her idolatry of this man was such that she herself almost feared it to be ill-omened. She was conscious of the notion expressed by Friar Laurence: “These violent delights have violent ends.” It might be too desperate for human conditions—too rank, to wild, too deadly.\n\n“O my love, why do I love you so!” she whispered there alone; “for she you love is not my real self, but one in my image; the one I might have been!”\n\nAfternoon came, and with it the hour for departure. They had decided to fulfil the plan of going for a few days to the lodgings in the old farmhouse near Wellbridge Mill, at which he meant to reside during his investigation of flour processes. At two o’clock there was nothing left to do but to start. All the servantry of the dairy were standing in the red-brick entry to see them go out, the dairyman and his wife following to the door. Tess saw her three chamber-mates in a row against the wall, pensively inclining their heads. She had much questioned if they would appear at the parting moment; but there they were, stoical and staunch to the last. She knew why the delicate Retty looked so fragile, and Izz so tragically sorrowful, and Marian so blank; and she forgot her own dogging shadow for a moment in contemplating theirs.\n\nShe impulsively whispered to him—\n\n“Will you kiss ’em all, once, poor things, for the first and last time?”\n\nClare had not the least objection to such a farewell formality—which was all that it was to him—and as he passed them he kissed them in succession where they stood, saying “Goodbye” to each as he did so. When they reached the door Tess femininely glanced back to discern the effect of that kiss of charity; there was no triumph in her glance, as there might have been. If there had it would have disappeared when she saw how moved the girls all were. The kiss had obviously done harm by awakening feelings they were trying to subdue.\n\nOf all this Clare was unconscious. Passing on to the wicket-gate he shook hands with the dairyman and his wife, and expressed his last thanks to them for their attentions; after which there was a moment of silence before they had moved off. It was interrupted by the crowing of a cock. The white one with the rose comb had come and settled on the palings in front of the house, within a few yards of them, and his notes thrilled their ears through, dwindling away like echoes down a valley of rocks.\n\n“Oh?” said Mrs Crick. “An afternoon crow!”\n\nTwo men were standing by the yard gate, holding it open.\n\n“That’s bad,” one murmured to the other, not thinking that the words could be heard by the group at the door-wicket.\n\nThe cock crew again—straight towards Clare.\n\n“Well!” said the dairyman.\n\n“I don’t like to hear him!” said Tess to her husband. “Tell the man to drive on. Goodbye, goodbye!”\n\nThe cock crew again.\n\n“Hoosh! Just you be off, sir, or I’ll twist your neck!” said the dairyman with some irritation, turning to the bird and driving him away. And to his wife as they went indoors: “Now, to think o’ that just to-day! I’ve not heard his crow of an afternoon all the year afore.”\n\n“It only means a change in the weather,” said she; “not what you think: ’tis impossible!”\n\n# XXXIV\n\nThey drove by the level road along the valley to a distance of a few miles, and, reaching Wellbridge, turned away from the village to the left, and over the great Elizabethan bridge which gives the place half its name. Immediately behind it stood the house wherein they had engaged lodgings, whose exterior features are so well known to all travellers through the Froom Valley; once portion of a fine manorial residence, and the property and seat of a d’Urberville, but since its partial demolition a farmhouse.\n\n“Welcome to one of your ancestral mansions!” said Clare as he handed her down. But he regretted the pleasantry; it was too near a satire.\n\nOn entering they found that, though they had only engaged a couple of rooms, the farmer had taken advantage of their proposed presence during the coming days to pay a New Year’s visit to some friends, leaving a woman from a neighbouring cottage to minister to their few wants. The absoluteness of possession pleased them, and they realized it as the first moment of their experience under their own exclusive roof-tree.\nBut he found that the mouldy old habitation somewhat depressed his bride. When the carriage was gone they ascended the stairs to wash their hands, the charwoman showing the way. On the landing Tess stopped and started.\n\n“What’s the matter?” said he.\n\n“Those horrid women!” she answered with a smile. “How they frightened me.”\n\nHe looked up, and perceived two life-size portraits on panels built into the masonry. As all visitors to the mansion are aware, these paintings represent women of middle age, of a date some two hundred years ago, whose lineaments once seen can never be forgotten. The long pointed features, narrow eye, and smirk of the one, so suggestive of merciless treachery; the bill-hook nose, large teeth, and bold eye of the other suggesting arrogance to the point of ferocity, haunt the beholder afterwards in his dreams.\n\n“Whose portraits are those?” asked Clare of the charwoman.\n\n“I have been told by old folk that they were ladies of the d’Urberville family, the ancient lords of this manor,” she said, “Owing to their being builded into the wall they can’t be moved away.”\n\nThe unpleasantness of the matter was that, in addition to their effect upon Tess, her fine features were unquestionably traceable in these exaggerated forms. He said nothing of this, however, and, regretting that he had gone out of his way to choose the house for their bridal time, went on into the adjoining room. The place having been rather hastily prepared for them, they washed their hands in one basin. Clare touched hers under the water.\n\n“Which are my fingers and which are yours?” he said, looking up. “They are very much mixed.”\n\n“They are all yours,” said she, very prettily, and endeavoured to be gayer than she was. He had not been displeased with her thoughtfulness on such an occasion; it was what every sensible woman would show: but Tess knew that she had been thoughtful to excess, and struggled against it.\n\nThe sun was so low on that short last afternoon of the year that it shone in through a small opening and formed a golden staff which stretched across to her skirt, where it made a spot like a paint-mark set upon her. They went into the ancient parlour to tea, and here they shared their first common meal alone. Such was their childishness, or rather his, that he found it interesting to use the same bread-and-butter plate as herself, and to brush crumbs from her lips with his own. He wondered a little that she did not enter into these frivolities with his own zest.\n\nLooking at her silently for a long time; “She is a dear dear Tess,” he thought to himself, as one deciding on the true construction of a difficult passage. “Do I realize solemnly enough how utterly and irretrievably this little womanly thing is the creature of my good or bad faith and fortune? I think not. I think I could not, unless I were a woman myself. What I am in worldly estate, she is. What I become, she must become. What I cannot be, she cannot be. And shall I ever neglect her, or hurt her, or even forget to consider her? God forbid such a crime!”\n\nThey sat on over the tea-table waiting for their luggage, which the dairyman had promised to send before it grew dark. But evening began to close in, and the luggage did not arrive, and they had brought nothing more than they stood in. With the departure of the sun the calm mood of the winter day changed. Out of doors there began noises as of silk smartly rubbed; the restful dead leaves of the preceding autumn were stirred to irritated resurrection, and whirled about unwillingly, and tapped against the shutters. It soon began to rain.\n\n“That cock knew the weather was going to change,” said Clare.\n\nThe woman who had attended upon them had gone home for the night, but she had placed candles upon the table, and now they lit them. Each candle-flame drew towards the fireplace.\n\n“These old houses are so draughty,” continued Angel, looking at the flames, and at the grease guttering down the sides. “I wonder where that luggage is. We haven’t even a brush and comb.”\n\n“I don’t know,” she answered, absent-minded.\n\n“Tess, you are not a bit cheerful this evening—not at all as you used to be. Those harridans on the panels upstairs have unsettled you. I am sorry I brought you here. I wonder if you really love me, after all?”\n\nHe knew that she did, and the words had no serious intent; but she was surcharged with emotion, and winced like a wounded animal. Though she tried not to shed tears, she could not help showing one or two.\n\n“I did not mean it!” said he, sorry. “You are worried at not having your things, I know. I cannot think why old Jonathan has not come with them. Why, it is seven o’clock? Ah, there he is!”\n\nA knock had come to the door, and, there being nobody else to answer it, Clare went out. He returned to the room with a small package in his hand.\n\n“It is not Jonathan, after all,” he said.\n\n“How vexing!” said Tess.\nThe packet had been brought by a special messenger, who had arrived at Talbothays from Emminster Vicarage immediately after the departure of the married couple, and had followed them hither, being under injunction to deliver it into nobody’s hands but theirs. Clare brought it to the light. It was less than a foot long, sewed up in canvas, sealed in red wax with his father’s seal, and directed in his father’s hand to “Mrs Angel Clare.”\n\n“It is a little wedding-present for you, Tess,” said he, handing it to her. “How thoughtful they are!”\n\nTess looked a little flustered as she took it.\n\n“I think I would rather have you open it, dearest,” said she, turning over the parcel. “I don’t like to break those great seals; they look so serious. Please open it for me!”\n\nHe undid the parcel. Inside was a case of morocco leather, on the top of which lay a note and a key.\n\nThe note was for Clare, in the following words:\n\nMy dear son,—\nPossibly you have forgotten that on the death of your godmother, Mrs Pitney, when you were a lad, she—vain, kind woman that she was—left to me a portion of the contents of her jewel-case in trust for your wife, if you should ever have one, as a mark of her affection for you and whomsoever you should choose. This trust I have fulfilled, and the diamonds have been locked up at my banker’s ever since. Though I feel it to be a somewhat incongruous act in the circumstances, I am, as you will see, bound to hand over the articles to the woman to whom the use of them for her lifetime will now rightly belong, and they are therefore promptly sent. They become, I believe, heirlooms, strictly speaking, according to the terms of your godmother’s will. The precise words of the clause that refers to this matter are enclosed.\n\n“I do remember,” said Clare; “but I had quite forgotten.”\n\nUnlocking the case, they found it to contain a necklace, with pendant, bracelets, and ear-rings; and also some other small ornaments.\n\nTess seemed afraid to touch them at first, but her eyes sparkled for a moment as much as the stones when Clare spread out the set.\n\n“Are they mine?” she asked incredulously.\n\n“They are, certainly,” said he.\n\nHe looked into the fire. He remembered how, when he was a lad of fifteen, his godmother, the Squire’s wife—the only rich person with whom he had ever come in contact—had pinned her faith to his success; had prophesied a wondrous career for him. There had seemed nothing at all out of keeping with such a conjectured career in the storing up of these showy ornaments for his wife and the wives of her descendants. They gleamed somewhat ironically now. “Yet why?” he asked himself. It was but a question of vanity throughout; and if that were admitted into one side of the equation it should be admitted into the other. His wife was a d’Urberville: whom could they become better than her?\n\nSuddenly he said with enthusiasm—\n\n“Tess, put them on—put them on!” And he turned from the fire to help her.\n\nBut as if by magic she had already donned them—necklace, ear-rings, bracelets, and all.\n\n“But the gown isn’t right, Tess,” said Clare. “It ought to be a low one for a set of brilliants like that.”\n\n“Ought it?” said Tess.\n\n“Yes,” said he.\n\nHe suggested to her how to tuck in the upper edge of her bodice, so as to make it roughly approximate to the cut for evening wear; and when she had done this, and the pendant to the necklace hung isolated amid the whiteness of her throat, as it was designed to do, he stepped back to survey her.\n\n“My heavens,” said Clare, “how beautiful you are!”\n\nAs everybody knows, fine feathers make fine birds; a peasant girl but very moderately prepossessing to the casual observer in her simple condition and attire will bloom as an amazing beauty if clothed as a woman of fashion with the aids that Art can render; while the beauty of the midnight crush would often cut but a sorry figure if placed inside the field-woman’s wrapper upon a monotonous acreage of turnips on a dull day. He had never till now estimated the artistic excellence of Tess’s limbs and features.\n\n“If you were only to appear in a ball-room!” he said. “But no—no, dearest; I think I love you best in the wing-bonnet and cotton-frock—yes, better than in this, well as you support these dignities.”\n\nTess’s sense of her striking appearance had given her a flush of excitement, which was yet not happiness.\n\n“I’ll take them off,” she said, “in case Jonathan should see me. They are not fit for me, are they? They must be sold, I suppose?”\n\n“Let them stay a few minutes longer. Sell them? Never. It would be a breach of faith.”\n\nInfluenced by a second thought she readily obeyed. She had something to tell, and there might be help in these. She sat down with the jewels upon her; and they again indulged in conjectures as to where Jonathan could possibly be with their baggage. The ale they had poured out for his consumption when he came had gone flat with long standing.\n\nShortly after this they began supper, which was already laid on a side-table. Ere they had finished there was a jerk in the fire-smoke, the rising skein of which bulged out into the room, as if some giant had laid his hand on the chimney-top for a moment. It had been caused by the opening of the outer door. A heavy step was now heard in the passage, and Angel went out.\n\n“I couldn’ make nobody hear at all by knocking,” apologized Jonathan Kail, for it was he at last; “and as’t was raining out I opened the door. I’ve brought the things, sir.”\n\n“I am very glad to see them. But you are very late.”\n\n“Well, yes, sir.”\n\nThere was something subdued in Jonathan Kail’s tone which had not been there in the day, and lines of concern were ploughed upon his forehead in addition to the lines of years. He continued—\n\n“We’ve all been gallied at the dairy at what might ha’ been a most terrible affliction since you and your Mis’ess—so to name her now—left us this a’ternoon. Perhaps you ha’nt forgot the cock’s afternoon crow?”\n\n“Dear me;—what—”\n\n“Well, some says it do mane one thing, and some another; but what’s happened is that poor little Retty Priddle hev tried to drown herself.”\n\n“No! Really! Why, she bade us goodbye with the rest—”\n\n“Yes. Well, sir, when you and your Mis’ess—so to name what she lawful is—when you two drove away, as I say, Retty and Marian put on their bonnets and went out; and as there is not much doing now, being New Year’s Eve, and folks mops and brooms from what’s inside ’em, nobody took much notice. They went on to Lew-Everard, where they had summut to drink, and then on they vamped to Dree-armed Cross, and there they seemed to have parted, Retty striking across the water-meads as if for home, and Marian going on to the next village, where there’s another public-house. Nothing more was zeed or heard o’ Retty till the waterman, on his way home, noticed something by the Great Pool; ’twas her bonnet and shawl packed up. In the water he found her. He and another man brought her home, thinking ’a was dead; but she fetched round by degrees.”\n\nAngel, suddenly recollecting that Tess was overhearing this gloomy tale, went to shut the door between the passage and the ante-room to the inner parlour where she was; but his wife, flinging a shawl round her, had come to the outer room and was listening to the man’s narrative, her eyes resting absently on the luggage and the drops of rain glistening upon it.\n\n“And, more than this, there’s Marian; she’s been found dead drunk by the withy-bed—a girl who hev never been known to touch anything before except shilling ale; though, to be sure, ’a was always a good trencher-woman, as her face showed. It seems as if the maids had all gone out o’ their minds!”\n\n“And Izz?” asked Tess.\n\n“Izz is about house as usual; but ’a do say ’a can guess how it happened; and she seems to be very low in mind about it, poor maid, as well she mid be. And so you see, sir, as all this happened just when we was packing your few traps and your Mis’ess’s night-rail and dressing things into the cart, why, it belated me.”\n\n“Yes. Well, Jonathan, will you get the trunks upstairs, and drink a cup of ale, and hasten back as soon as you can, in case you should be wanted?”\n\nTess had gone back to the inner parlour, and sat down by the fire, looking wistfully into it. She heard Jonathan Kail’s heavy footsteps up and down the stairs till he had done placing the luggage, and heard him express his thanks for the ale her husband took out to him, and for the gratuity he received. Jonathan’s footsteps then died from the door, and his cart creaked away.\n\nAngel slid forward the massive oak bar which secured the door, and coming in to where she sat over the hearth, pressed her cheeks between his hands from behind. He expected her to jump up gaily and unpack the toilet-gear that she had been so anxious about, but as she did not rise he sat down with her in the firelight, the candles on the supper-table being too thin and glimmering to interfere with its glow.\n\n“I am so sorry you should have heard this sad story about the girls,” he said. “Still, don’t let it depress you. Retty was naturally morbid, you know.”\n\n“Without the least cause,” said Tess. “While they who have cause to be, hide it, and pretend they are not.”\n\nThis incident had turned the scale for her. They were simple and innocent girls on whom the unhappiness of unrequited love had fallen; they had deserved better at the hands of Fate. She had deserved worse—yet she was the chosen one. It was wicked of her to take all without paying. She would pay to the uttermost farthing; she would tell, there and then. This final determination she came to when she looked into the fire, he holding her hand.\n\nA steady glare from the now flameless embers painted the sides and back of the fireplace with its colour, and the well-polished andirons, and the old brass tongs that would not meet. The underside of the mantel-shelf was flushed with the high-coloured light, and the legs of the table nearest the fire. Tess’s face and neck reflected the same warmth, which each gem turned into an Aldebaran or a Sirius—a constellation of white, red, and green flashes, that interchanged their hues with her every pulsation.\n\n“Do you remember what we said to each other this morning about telling our faults?” he asked abruptly, finding that she still remained immovable. “We spoke lightly perhaps, and you may well have done so. But for me it was no light promise. I want to make a confession to you, Love.”\n\nThis, from him, so unexpectedly apposite, had the effect upon her of a Providential interposition.\n\n“You have to confess something?” she said quickly, and even with gladness and relief.\n\n“You did not expect it? Ah—you thought too highly of me. Now listen. Put your head there, because I want you to forgive me, and not to be indignant with me for not telling you before, as perhaps I ought to have done.”\n\nHow strange it was! He seemed to be her double. She did not speak, and Clare went on—\n\n“I did not mention it because I was afraid of endangering my chance of you, darling, the great prize of my life—my Fellowship I call you. My brother’s Fellowship was won at his college, mine at Talbothays Dairy. Well, I would not risk it. I was going to tell you a month ago—at the time you agreed to be mine, but I could not; I thought it might frighten you away from me. I put it off; then I thought I would tell you yesterday, to give you a chance at least of escaping me. But I did not. And I did not this morning, when you proposed our confessing our faults on the landing—the sinner that I was! But I must, now I see you sitting there so solemnly. I wonder if you will forgive me?”\n\n“O yes! I am sure that—”\n\n“Well, I hope so. But wait a minute. You don’t know. To begin at the beginning. Though I imagine my poor father fears that I am one of the eternally lost for my doctrines, I am of course, a believer in good morals, Tess, as much as you. I used to wish to be a teacher of men, and it was a great disappointment to me when I found I could not enter the Church. I admired spotlessness, even though I could lay no claim to it, and hated impurity, as I hope I do now. Whatever one may think of plenary inspiration, one must heartily subscribe to these words of Paul: ‘Be thou an example—in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity.’ It is the only safeguard for us poor human beings. ‘_Integer vitae_,’ says a Roman poet, who is strange company for St Paul—\n\nThe man of upright life, from frailties free, Stands not in need of Moorish spear or bow.\n\n“Well, a certain place is paved with good intentions, and having felt all that so strongly, you will see what a terrible remorse it bred in me when, in the midst of my fine aims for other people, I myself fell.”\n\nHe then told her of that time of his life to which allusion has been made when, tossed about by doubts and difficulties in London, like a cork on the waves, he plunged into eight-and-forty hours’ dissipation with a stranger.\n\n“Happily I awoke almost immediately to a sense of my folly,” he continued. “I would have no more to say to her, and I came home. I have never repeated the offence. But I felt I should like to treat you with perfect frankness and honour, and I could not do so without telling this. Do you forgive me?”\n\nShe pressed his hand tightly for an answer.\n\n“Then we will dismiss it at once and for ever!—too painful as it is for the occasion—and talk of something lighter.”\n\n“O, Angel—I am almost glad—because now _you_ can forgive _me_! I have not made my confession. I have a confession, too—remember, I said so.”\n\n“Ah, to be sure! Now then for it, wicked little one.”\n\n“Perhaps, although you smile, it is as serious as yours, or more so.”\n\n“It can hardly be more serious, dearest.”\n\n“It cannot—O no, it cannot!” She jumped up joyfully at the hope. “No, it cannot be more serious, certainly,” she cried, “because ’tis just the same! I will tell you now.”\n\nShe sat down again.\n\nTheir hands were still joined. The ashes under the grate were lit by the fire vertically, like a torrid waste. Imagination might have beheld a Last Day luridness in this red-coaled glow, which fell on his face and hand, and on hers, peering into the loose hair about her brow, and firing the delicate skin underneath. A large shadow of her shape rose upon the wall and ceiling. She bent forward, at which each diamond on her neck gave a sinister wink like a toad’s; and pressing her forehead against his temple she entered on her story of her acquaintance with Alec d’Urberville and its results, murmuring the words without flinching, and with her eyelids drooping down.\nEnd of Phase the Fourth\n\n# XXXV\n\nThe Woman Pays\nHer narrative ended; even its re-assertions and secondary explanations were done. Tess’s voice throughout had hardly risen higher than its opening tone; there had been no exculpatory phrase of any kind, and she had not wept.\n\nBut the complexion even of external things seemed to suffer transmutation as her announcement progressed. The fire in the grate looked impish—demoniacally funny, as if it did not care in the least about her strait. The fender grinned idly, as if it too did not care. The light from the water-bottle was merely engaged in a chromatic problem. All material objects around announced their irresponsibility with terrible iteration. And yet nothing had changed since the moments when he had been kissing her; or rather, nothing in the substance of things. But the essence of things had changed.\n\nWhen she ceased, the auricular impressions from their previous endearments seemed to hustle away into the corner of their brains, repeating themselves as echoes from a time of supremely purblind foolishness.\n\nClare performed the irrelevant act of stirring the fire; the intelligence had not even yet got to the bottom of him. After stirring the embers he rose to his feet; all the force of her disclosure had imparted itself now. His face had withered. In the strenuousness of his concentration he treadled fitfully on the floor. He could not, by any contrivance, think closely enough; that was the meaning of his vague movement. When he spoke it was in the most inadequate, commonplace voice of the many varied tones she had heard from him.\n\n“Tess!”\n\n“Yes, dearest.”\n\n“Am I to believe this? From your manner I am to take it as true. O you cannot be out of your mind! You ought to be! Yet you are not... My wife, my Tess—nothing in you warrants such a supposition as that?”\n\n“I am not out of my mind,” she said.\n\n“And yet—” He looked vacantly at her, to resume with dazed senses: “Why didn’t you tell me before? Ah, yes, you would have told me, in a way—but I hindered you, I remember!”\n\nThese and other of his words were nothing but the perfunctory babble of the surface while the depths remained paralyzed. He turned away, and bent over a chair. Tess followed him to the middle of the room, where he was, and stood there staring at him with eyes that did not weep. Presently she slid down upon her knees beside his foot, and from this position she crouched in a heap.\n\n“In the name of our love, forgive me!” she whispered with a dry mouth. “I have forgiven you for the same!”\n\nAnd, as he did not answer, she said again—\n\n“Forgive me as you are forgiven! _I_ forgive _you_, Angel.”\n“You—yes, you do.”\n\n“But you do not forgive me?”\n\n“O Tess, forgiveness does not apply to the case! You were one person; now you are another. My God—how can forgiveness meet such a grotesque—prestidigitation as that!”\n\nHe paused, contemplating this definition; then suddenly broke into horrible laughter—as unnatural and ghastly as a laugh in hell.\n\n“Don’t—don’t! It kills me quite, that!” she shrieked. “O have mercy upon me—have mercy!”\n\nHe did not answer; and, sickly white, she jumped up.\n\n“Angel, Angel! what do you mean by that laugh?” she cried out. “Do you know what this is to me?”\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n“I have been hoping, longing, praying, to make you happy! I have thought what joy it will be to do it, what an unworthy wife I shall be if I do not! That’s what I have felt, Angel!”\n\n“I know that.”\n\n“I thought, Angel, that you loved me—me, my very self! If it is I you do love, O how can it be that you look and speak so? It frightens me! Having begun to love you, I love you for ever—in all changes, in all disgraces, because you are yourself. I ask no more. Then how can you, O my own husband, stop loving me?”\n\n“I repeat, the woman I have been loving is not you.”\n\n“But who?”\n\n“Another woman in your shape.”\n\nShe perceived in his words the realization of her own apprehensive foreboding in former times. He looked upon her as a species of imposter; a guilty woman in the guise of an innocent one. Terror was upon her white face as she saw it; her cheek was flaccid, and her mouth had almost the aspect of a round little hole. The horrible sense of his view of her so deadened her that she staggered, and he stepped forward, thinking she was going to fall.\n\n“Sit down, sit down,” he said gently. “You are ill; and it is natural that you should be.”\n\nShe did sit down, without knowing where she was, that strained look still upon her face, and her eyes such as to make his flesh creep.\n\n“I don’t belong to you any more, then; do I, Angel?” she asked helplessly. “It is not me, but another woman like me that he loved, he says.”\n\nThe image raised caused her to take pity upon herself as one who was ill-used. Her eyes filled as she regarded her position further; she turned round and burst into a flood of self-sympathetic tears.\n\nClare was relieved at this change, for the effect on her of what had happened was beginning to be a trouble to him only less than the woe of the disclosure itself. He waited patiently, apathetically, till the violence of her grief had worn itself out, and her rush of weeping had lessened to a catching gasp at intervals.\n\n“Angel,” she said suddenly, in her natural tones, the insane, dry voice of terror having left her now. “Angel, am I too wicked for you and me to live together?”\n\n“I have not been able to think what we can do.”\n\n“I shan’t ask you to let me live with you, Angel, because I have no right to! I shall not write to mother and sisters to say we be married, as I said I would do; and I shan’t finish the good hussif I cut out and meant to make while we were in lodgings.”\n\n“Shan’t you?”\n\n“No, I shan’t do anything, unless you order me to; and if you go away from me I shall not follow ’ee; and if you never speak to me any more I shall not ask why, unless you tell me I may.”\n\n“And if I order you to do anything?”\n\n“I will obey you like your wretched slave, even if it is to lie down and die.”\n\n“You are very good. But it strikes me that there is a want of harmony between your present mood of self-sacrifice and your past mood of self-preservation.”\n\nThese were the first words of antagonism. To fling elaborate sarcasms at Tess, however, was much like flinging them at a dog or cat. The charms of their subtlety passed by her unappreciated, and she only received them as inimical sounds which meant that anger ruled. She remained mute, not knowing that he was smothering his affection for her. She hardly observed that a tear descended slowly upon his cheek, a tear so large that it magnified the pores of the skin over which it rolled, like the object lens of a microscope. Meanwhile reillumination as to the terrible and total change that her confession had wrought in his life, in his universe, returned to him, and he tried desperately to advance among the new conditions in which he stood. Some consequent action was necessary; yet what?\n\n“Tess,” he said, as gently as he could speak, “I cannot stay—in this room—just now. I will walk out a little way.”\n\nHe quietly left the room, and the two glasses of wine that he had poured out for their supper—one for her, one for him—remained on the table untasted. This was what their _Agape_ had come to. At tea, two or three hours earlier, they had, in the freakishness of affection, drunk from one cup.\n\nThe closing of the door behind him, gently as it had been pulled to, roused Tess from her stupor. He was gone; she could not stay. Hastily flinging her cloak around her she opened the door and followed, putting out the candles as if she were never coming back. The rain was over and the night was now clear.\n\nShe was soon close at his heels, for Clare walked slowly and without purpose. His form beside her light gray figure looked black, sinister, and forbidding, and she felt as sarcasm the touch of the jewels of which she had been momentarily so proud. Clare turned at hearing her footsteps, but his recognition of her presence seemed to make no difference to him, and he went on over the five yawning arches of the great bridge in front of the house.\n\nThe cow and horse tracks in the road were full of water, the rain having been enough to charge them, but not enough to wash them away. Across these minute pools the reflected stars flitted in a quick transit as she passed; she would not have known they were shining overhead if she had not seen them there—the vastest things of the universe imaged in objects so mean.\n\nThe place to which they had travelled to-day was in the same valley as Talbothays, but some miles lower down the river; and the surroundings being open, she kept easily in sight of him. Away from the house the road wound through the meads, and along these she followed Clare without any attempt to come up with him or to attract him, but with dumb and vacant fidelity.\n\nAt last, however, her listless walk brought her up alongside him, and still he said nothing. The cruelty of fooled honesty is often great after enlightenment, and it was mighty in Clare now. The outdoor air had apparently taken away from him all tendency to act on impulse; she knew that he saw her without irradiation—in all her bareness; that Time was chanting his satiric psalm at her then—\n\nBehold, when thy face is made bare, he that loved thee shall hate; Thy face shall be no more fair at the fall of thy fate. For thy life shall fall as a leaf and be shed as the rain; And the veil of thine head shall be grief, and the crown shall be pain.\n\nHe was still intently thinking, and her companionship had now insufficient power to break or divert the strain of thought. What a weak thing her presence must have become to him! She could not help addressing Clare.\n\n“What have I done—what _have_ I done! I have not told of anything that interferes with or belies my love for you. You don’t think I planned it, do you? It is in your own mind what you are angry at, Angel; it is not in me. O, it is not in me, and I am not that deceitful woman you think me!”\n\n“H’m—well. Not deceitful, my wife; but not the same. No, not the same. But do not make me reproach you. I have sworn that I will not; and I will do everything to avoid it.”\n\nBut she went on pleading in her distraction; and perhaps said things that would have been better left to silence.\n\n“Angel!—Angel! I was a child—a child when it happened! I knew nothing of men.”\n\n“You were more sinned against than sinning, that I admit.”\n\n“Then will you not forgive me?”\n\n“I do forgive you, but forgiveness is not all.”\n\n“And love me?”\n\nTo this question he did not answer.\n\n“O Angel—my mother says that it sometimes happens so!—she knows several cases where they were worse than I, and the husband has not minded it much—has got over it at least. And yet the woman had not loved him as I do you!”\n\n“Don’t, Tess; don’t argue. Different societies, different manners. You almost make me say you are an unapprehending peasant woman, who have never been initiated into the proportions of social things. You don’t know what you say.”\n\n“I am only a peasant by position, not by nature!”\n\nShe spoke with an impulse to anger, but it went as it came.\n\n“So much the worse for you. I think that parson who unearthed your pedigree would have done better if he had held his tongue. I cannot help associating your decline as a family with this other fact—of your want of firmness. Decrepit families imply decrepit wills, decrepit conduct. Heaven, why did you give me a handle for despising you more by informing me of your descent! Here was I thinking you a new-sprung child of nature; there were you, the belated seedling of an effete aristocracy!”\n\n“Lots of families are as bad as mine in that! Retty’s family were once large landowners, and so were Dairyman Billett’s. And the Debbyhouses, who now are carters, were once the De Bayeux family. You find such as I everywhere; ’tis a feature of our county, and I can’t help it.”\n\n“So much the worse for the county.”\n\nShe took these reproaches in their bulk simply, not in their particulars; he did not love her as he had loved her hitherto, and to all else she was indifferent.\n\nThey wandered on again in silence. It was said afterwards that a cottager of Wellbridge, who went out late that night for a doctor, met two lovers in the pastures, walking very slowly, without converse, one behind the other, as in a funeral procession, and the glimpse that he obtained of their faces seemed to denote that they were anxious and sad. Returning later, he passed them again in the same field, progressing just as slowly, and as regardless of the hour and of the cheerless night as before. It was only on account of his preoccupation with his own affairs, and the illness in his house, that he did not bear in mind the curious incident, which, however, he recalled a long while after.\n\nDuring the interval of the cottager’s going and coming, she had said to her husband—\n\n“I don’t see how I can help being the cause of much misery to you all your life. The river is down there. I can put an end to myself in it. I am not afraid.”\n\n“I don’t wish to add murder to my other follies,” he said.\n\n“I will leave something to show that I did it myself—on account of my shame. They will not blame you then.”\n\n“Don’t speak so absurdly—I wish not to hear it. It is nonsense to have such thoughts in this kind of case, which is rather one for satirical laughter than for tragedy. You don’t in the least understand the quality of the mishap. It would be viewed in the light of a joke by nine-tenths of the world if it were known. Please oblige me by returning to the house, and going to bed.”\n\n“I will,” said she dutifully.\n\nThey had rambled round by a road which led to the well-known ruins of the Cistercian abbey behind the mill, the latter having, in centuries past, been attached to the monastic establishment. The mill still worked on, food being a perennial necessity; the abbey had perished, creeds being transient. One continually sees the ministration of the temporary outlasting the ministration of the eternal. Their walk having been circuitous, they were still not far from the house, and in obeying his direction she only had to reach the large stone bridge across the main river and follow the road for a few yards. When she got back, everything remained as she had left it, the fire being still burning. She did not stay downstairs for more than a minute, but proceeded to her chamber, whither the luggage had been taken. Here she sat down on the edge of the bed, looking blankly around, and presently began to undress. In removing the light towards the bedstead its rays fell upon the tester of white dimity; something was hanging beneath it, and she lifted the candle to see what it was. A bough of mistletoe. Angel had put it there; she knew that in an instant. This was the explanation of that mysterious parcel which it had been so difficult to pack and bring; whose contents he would not explain to her, saying that time would soon show her the purpose thereof. In his zest and his gaiety he had hung it there. How foolish and inopportune that mistletoe looked now.\n\nHaving nothing more to fear, having scarce anything to hope, for that he would relent there seemed no promise whatever, she lay down dully. When sorrow ceases to be speculative, sleep sees her opportunity. Among so many happier moods which forbid repose this was a mood which welcomed it, and in a few minutes the lonely Tess forgot existence, surrounded by the aromatic stillness of the chamber that had once, possibly, been the bride-chamber of her own ancestry.\n\nLater on that night Clare also retraced his steps to the house. Entering softly to the sitting-room he obtained a light, and with the manner of one who had considered his course he spread his rugs upon the old horse-hair sofa which stood there, and roughly shaped it to a sleeping-couch. Before lying down he crept shoeless upstairs, and listened at the door of her apartment. Her measured breathing told that she was sleeping profoundly.\n\n“Thank God!” murmured Clare; and yet he was conscious of a pang of bitterness at the thought—approximately true, though not wholly so—that having shifted the burden of her life to his shoulders, she was now reposing without care.\n\nHe turned away to descend; then, irresolute, faced round to her door again. In the act he caught sight of one of the d’Urberville dames, whose portrait was immediately over the entrance to Tess’s bedchamber. In the candlelight the painting was more than unpleasant. Sinister design lurked in the woman’s features, a concentrated purpose of revenge on the other sex—so it seemed to him then. The Caroline bodice of the portrait was low—precisely as Tess’s had been when he tucked it in to show the necklace; and again he experienced the distressing sensation of a resemblance between them.\n\nThe check was sufficient. He resumed his retreat and descended.\n\nHis air remained calm and cold, his small compressed mouth indexing his powers of self-control; his face wearing still that terrible sterile expression which had spread thereon since her disclosure. It was the face of a man who was no longer passion’s slave, yet who found no advantage in his enfranchisement. He was simply regarding the harrowing contingencies of human experience, the unexpectedness of things. Nothing so pure, so sweet, so virginal as Tess had seemed possible all the long while that he had adored her, up to an hour ago; but\n\nThe little less, and what worlds away!\n\nHe argued erroneously when he said to himself that her heart was not indexed in the honest freshness of her face; but Tess had no advocate to set him right. Could it be possible, he continued, that eyes which as they gazed never expressed any divergence from what the tongue was telling, were yet ever seeing another world behind her ostensible one, discordant and contrasting?\n\nHe reclined on his couch in the sitting-room, and extinguished the light. The night came in, and took up its place there, unconcerned and indifferent; the night which had already swallowed up his happiness, and was now digesting it listlessly; and was ready to swallow up the happiness of a thousand other people with as little disturbance or change of mien.\n\n# XXXVI\n\nClare arose in the light of a dawn that was ashy and furtive, as though associated with crime. The fireplace confronted him with its extinct embers; the spread supper-table, whereon stood the two full glasses of untasted wine, now flat and filmy; her vacated seat and his own; the other articles of furniture, with their eternal look of not being able to help it, their intolerable inquiry what was to be done? From above there was no sound; but in a few minutes there came a knock at the door. He remembered that it would be the neighbouring cottager’s wife, who was to minister to their wants while they remained here.\n\nThe presence of a third person in the house would be extremely awkward just now, and, being already dressed, he opened the window and informed her that they could manage to shift for themselves that morning. She had a milk-can in her hand, which he told her to leave at the door. When the dame had gone away he searched in the back quarters of the house for fuel, and speedily lit a fire. There was plenty of eggs, butter, bread, and so on in the larder, and Clare soon had breakfast laid, his experiences at the dairy having rendered him facile in domestic preparations. The smoke of the kindled wood rose from the chimney without like a lotus-headed column; local people who were passing by saw it, and thought of the newly-married couple, and envied their happiness.\n\nAngel cast a final glance round, and then going to the foot of the stairs, called in a conventional voice—\n\n“Breakfast is ready!”\n\nHe opened the front door, and took a few steps in the morning air. When, after a short space, he came back she was already in the sitting-room mechanically readjusting the breakfast things. As she was fully attired, and the interval since his calling her had been but two or three minutes, she must have been dressed or nearly so before he went to summon her. Her hair was twisted up in a large round mass at the back of her head, and she had put on one of the new frocks—a pale blue woollen garment with neck-frillings of white. Her hands and face appeared to be cold, and she had possibly been sitting dressed in the bedroom a long time without any fire. The marked civility of Clare’s tone in calling her seemed to have inspired her, for the moment, with a new glimmer of hope. But it soon died when she looked at him.\n\nThe pair were, in truth, but the ashes of their former fires. To the hot sorrow of the previous night had succeeded heaviness; it seemed as if nothing could kindle either of them to fervour of sensation any more.\n\nHe spoke gently to her, and she replied with a like undemonstrativeness. At last she came up to him, looking in his sharply-defined face as one who had no consciousness that her own formed a visible object also.\n\n“Angel!” she said, and paused, touching him with her fingers lightly as a breeze, as though she could hardly believe to be there in the flesh the man who was once her lover. Her eyes were bright, her pale cheek still showed its wonted roundness, though half-dried tears had left glistening traces thereon; and the usually ripe red mouth was almost as pale as her cheek. Throbbingly alive as she was still, under the stress of her mental grief the life beat so brokenly that a little further pull upon it would cause real illness, dull her characteristic eyes, and make her mouth thin.\n\nShe looked absolutely pure. Nature, in her fantastic trickery, had set such a seal of maidenhood upon Tess’s countenance that he gazed at her with a stupefied air.\n\n“Tess! Say it is not true! No, it is not true!”\n\n“It is true.”\n\n“Every word?”\n\n“Every word.”\n\nHe looked at her imploringly, as if he would willingly have taken a lie from her lips, knowing it to be one, and have made of it, by some sort of sophistry, a valid denial. However, she only repeated—\n\n“It is true.”\n\n“Is he living?” Angel then asked.\n\n“The baby died.”\n\n“But the man?”\n\n“He is alive.”\n\nA last despair passed over Clare’s face.\n\n“Is he in England?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe took a few vague steps.\n\n“My position—is this,” he said abruptly. “I thought—any man would have thought—that by giving up all ambition to win a wife with social standing, with fortune, with knowledge of the world, I should secure rustic innocence as surely as I should secure pink cheeks; but—However, I am no man to reproach you, and I will not.”\n\nTess felt his position so entirely that the remainder had not been needed. Therein lay just the distress of it; she saw that he had lost all round.\n\n“Angel—I should not have let it go on to marriage with you if I had not known that, after all, there was a last way out of it for you; though I hoped you would never—”\n\nHer voice grew husky.\n\n“A last way?”\n\n“I mean, to get rid of me. You _can_ get rid of me.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“By divorcing me.”\n\n“Good heavens—how can you be so simple! How can I divorce you?”\n\n“Can’t you—now I have told you? I thought my confession would give you grounds for that.”\n\n“O Tess—you are too, too—childish—unformed—crude, I suppose! I don’t know what you are. You don’t understand the law—you don’t understand!”\n\n“What—you cannot?”\n\n“Indeed I cannot.”\n\nA quick shame mixed with the misery upon his listener’s face.\n\n“I thought—I thought,” she whispered. “O, now I see how wicked I seem to you! Believe me—believe me, on my soul, I never thought but that you could! I hoped you would not; yet I believed, without a doubt, that you could cast me off if you were determined, and didn’t love me at—at—all!”\n\n“You were mistaken,” he said.\n\n“O, then I ought to have done it, to have done it last night! But I hadn’t the courage. That’s just like me!”\n\n“The courage to do what?”\n\nAs she did not answer he took her by the hand.\n\n“What were you thinking of doing?” he inquired.\n\n“Of putting an end to myself.”\n\n“When?”\n\nShe writhed under this inquisitorial manner of his. “Last night,” she answered.\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Under your mistletoe.”\n\n“My good—! How?” he asked sternly.\n\n“I’ll tell you, if you won’t be angry with me!” she said, shrinking. “It was with the cord of my box. But I could not—do the last thing! I was afraid that it might cause a scandal to your name.”\n\nThe unexpected quality of this confession, wrung from her, and not volunteered, shook him perceptibly. But he still held her, and, letting his glance fall from her face downwards, he said, “Now, listen to this. You must not dare to think of such a horrible thing! How could you! You will promise me as your husband to attempt that no more.”\n\n“I am ready to promise. I saw how wicked it was.”\n\n“Wicked! The idea was unworthy of you beyond description.”\n\n“But, Angel,” she pleaded, enlarging her eyes in calm unconcern upon him, “it was thought of entirely on your account—to set you free without the scandal of the divorce that I thought you would have to get. I should never have dreamt of doing it on mine. However, to do it with my own hand is too good for me, after all. It is you, my ruined husband, who ought to strike the blow. I think I should love you more, if that were possible, if you could bring yourself to do it, since there’s no other way of escape for ’ee. I feel I am so utterly worthless! So very greatly in the way!”\n\n“Ssh!”\n\n“Well, since you say no, I won’t. I have no wish opposed to yours.”\n\nHe knew this to be true enough. Since the desperation of the night her activities had dropped to zero, and there was no further rashness to be feared.\n\nTess tried to busy herself again over the breakfast-table with more or less success, and they sat down both on the same side, so that their glances did not meet. There was at first something awkward in hearing each other eat and drink, but this could not be escaped; moreover, the amount of eating done was small on both sides. Breakfast over, he rose, and telling her the hour at which he might be expected to dinner, went off to the miller’s in a mechanical pursuance of the plan of studying that business, which had been his only practical reason for coming here.\n\nWhen he was gone Tess stood at the window, and presently saw his form crossing the great stone bridge which conducted to the mill premises. He sank behind it, crossed the railway beyond, and disappeared. Then, without a sigh, she turned her attention to the room, and began clearing the table and setting it in order.\n\nThe charwoman soon came. Her presence was at first a strain upon Tess, but afterwards an alleviation. At half-past twelve she left her assistant alone in the kitchen, and, returning to the sitting-room, waited for the reappearance of Angel’s form behind the bridge.\n\nAbout one he showed himself. Her face flushed, although he was a quarter of a mile off. She ran to the kitchen to get the dinner served by the time he should enter. He went first to the room where they had washed their hands together the day before, and as he entered the sitting-room the dish-covers rose from the dishes as if by his own motion.\n\n“Yes. I saw you coming over the bridge,” said she.\n\nThe meal was passed in commonplace talk of what he had been doing during the morning at the Abbey Mill, of the methods of bolting and the old-fashioned machinery, which he feared would not enlighten him greatly on modern improved methods, some of it seeming to have been in use ever since the days it ground for the monks in the adjoining conventual buildings—now a heap of ruins. He left the house again in the course of an hour, coming home at dusk, and occupying himself through the evening with his papers. She feared she was in the way and, when the old woman was gone, retired to the kitchen, where she made herself busy as well as she could for more than an hour.\n\nClare’s shape appeared at the door. “You must not work like this,” he said. “You are not my servant; you are my wife.”\n\nShe raised her eyes, and brightened somewhat. “I may think myself that—indeed?” she murmured, in piteous raillery. “You mean in name! Well, I don’t want to be anything more.”\n\n“You _may_ think so, Tess! You are. What do you mean?”\n\n“I don’t know,” she said hastily, with tears in her accents. “I thought I—because I am not respectable, I mean. I told you I thought I was not respectable enough long ago—and on that account I didn’t want to marry you, only—only you urged me!”\n\nShe broke into sobs, and turned her back to him. It would almost have won round any man but Angel Clare. Within the remote depths of his constitution, so gentle and affectionate as he was in general, there lay hidden a hard logical deposit, like a vein of metal in a soft loam, which turned the edge of everything that attempted to traverse it. It had blocked his acceptance of the Church; it blocked his acceptance of Tess. Moreover, his affection itself was less fire than radiance, and, with regard to the other sex, when he ceased to believe he ceased to follow: contrasting in this with many impressionable natures, who remain sensuously infatuated with what they intellectually despise. He waited till her sobbing ceased.\n\n“I wish half the women in England were as respectable as you,” he said, in an ebullition of bitterness against womankind in general. “It isn’t a question of respectability, but one of principle!”\n\nHe spoke such things as these and more of a kindred sort to her, being still swayed by the antipathetic wave which warps direct souls with such persistence when once their vision finds itself mocked by appearances. There was, it is true, underneath, a back current of sympathy through which a woman of the world might have conquered him. But Tess did not think of this; she took everything as her deserts, and hardly opened her mouth. The firmness of her devotion to him was indeed almost pitiful; quick-tempered as she naturally was, nothing that he could say made her unseemly; she sought not her own; was not provoked; thought no evil of his treatment of her. She might just now have been Apostolic Charity herself returned to a self-seeking modern world.\n\nThis evening, night, and morning were passed precisely as the preceding ones had been passed. On one, and only one, occasion did she—the formerly free and independent Tess—venture to make any advances. It was on the third occasion of his starting after a meal to go out to the flour-mill. As he was leaving the table he said “Goodbye,” and she replied in the same words, at the same time inclining her mouth in the way of his. He did not avail himself of the invitation, saying, as he turned hastily aside—\n\n“I shall be home punctually.”\n\nTess shrank into herself as if she had been struck. Often enough had he tried to reach those lips against her consent—often had he said gaily that her mouth and breath tasted of the butter and eggs and milk and honey on which she mainly lived, that he drew sustenance from them, and other follies of that sort. But he did not care for them now. He observed her sudden shrinking, and said gently—\n\n“You know, I have to think of a course. It was imperative that we should stay together a little while, to avoid the scandal to you that would have resulted from our immediate parting. But you must see it is only for form’s sake.”\n\n“Yes,” said Tess absently.\n\nHe went out, and on his way to the mill stood still, and wished for a moment that he had responded yet more kindly, and kissed her once at least.\n\nThus they lived through this despairing day or two; in the same house, truly; but more widely apart than before they were lovers. It was evident to her that he was, as he had said, living with paralyzed activities in his endeavour to think of a plan of procedure. She was awe-stricken to discover such determination under such apparent flexibility. His consistency was, indeed, too cruel. She no longer expected forgiveness now. More than once she thought of going away from him during his absence at the mill; but she feared that this, instead of benefiting him, might be the means of hampering and humiliating him yet more if it should become known.\n\nMeanwhile Clare was meditating, verily. His thought had been unsuspended; he was becoming ill with thinking; eaten out with thinking, withered by thinking; scourged out of all his former pulsating, flexuous domesticity. He walked about saying to himself, “What’s to be done—what’s to be done?” and by chance she overheard him. It caused her to break the reserve about their future which had hitherto prevailed.\n\n“I suppose—you are not going to live with me—long, are you, Angel?” she asked, the sunk corners of her mouth betraying how purely mechanical were the means by which she retained that expression of chastened calm upon her face.\n\n“I cannot” he said, “without despising myself, and what is worse, perhaps, despising you. I mean, of course, cannot live with you in the ordinary sense. At present, whatever I feel, I do not despise you. And, let me speak plainly, or you may not see all my difficulties. How can we live together while that man lives?—he being your husband in nature, and not I. If he were dead it might be different... Besides, that’s not all the difficulty; it lies in another consideration—one bearing upon the future of other people than ourselves. Think of years to come, and children being born to us, and this past matter getting known—for it must get known. There is not an uttermost part of the earth but somebody comes from it or goes to it from elsewhere. Well, think of wretches of our flesh and blood growing up under a taunt which they will gradually get to feel the full force of with their expanding years. What an awakening for them! What a prospect! Can you honestly say ‘Remain’ after contemplating this contingency? Don’t you think we had better endure the ills we have than fly to others?”\n\nHer eyelids, weighted with trouble, continued drooping as before.\n\n“I cannot say ‘Remain,’” she answered, “I cannot; I had not thought so far.”\n\nTess’s feminine hope—shall we confess it?—had been so obstinately recuperative as to revive in her surreptitious visions of a domiciliary intimacy continued long enough to break down his coldness even against his judgement. Though unsophisticated in the usual sense, she was not incomplete; and it would have denoted deficiency of womanhood if she had not instinctively known what an argument lies in propinquity. Nothing else would serve her, she knew, if this failed. It was wrong to hope in what was of the nature of strategy, she said to herself: yet that sort of hope she could not extinguish. His last representation had now been made, and it was, as she said, a new view. She had truly never thought so far as that, and his lucid picture of possible offspring who would scorn her was one that brought deadly convictions to an honest heart which was humanitarian to its centre. Sheer experience had already taught her that in some circumstances there was one thing better than to lead a good life, and that was to be saved from leading any life whatever. Like all who have been previsioned by suffering, she could, in the words of M. Sully-Prudhomme, hear a penal sentence in the fiat, “You shall be born,” particularly if addressed to potential issue of hers.\n\nYet such is the vulpine slyness of Dame Nature, that, till now, Tess had been hoodwinked by her love for Clare into forgetting it might result in vitalizations that would inflict upon others what she had bewailed as misfortune to herself.\n\nShe therefore could not withstand his argument. But with the self-combating proclivity of the supersensitive, an answer thereto arose in Clare’s own mind, and he almost feared it. It was based on her exceptional physical nature; and she might have used it promisingly. She might have added besides: “On an Australian upland or Texan plain, who is to know or care about my misfortunes, or to reproach me or you?” Yet, like the majority of women, she accepted the momentary presentment as if it were the inevitable. And she may have been right. The intuitive heart of woman knoweth not only its own bitterness, but its husband’s, and even if these assumed reproaches were not likely to be addressed to him or to his by strangers, they might have reached his ears from his own fastidious brain.\n\nIt was the third day of the estrangement. Some might risk the odd paradox that with more animalism he would have been the nobler man. We do not say it. Yet Clare’s love was doubtless ethereal to a fault, imaginative to impracticability. With these natures, corporal presence is something less appealing than corporal absence; the latter creating an ideal presence that conveniently drops the defects of the real. She found that her personality did not plead her cause so forcibly as she had anticipated. The figurative phrase was true: she was another woman than the one who had excited his desire.\n\n“I have thought over what you say,” she remarked to him, moving her forefinger over the tablecloth, her other hand, which bore the ring that mocked them both, supporting her forehead. “It is quite true, all of it; it must be. You must go away from me.”\n\n“But what can you do?”\n\n“I can go home.”\n\nClare had not thought of that.\n\n“Are you sure?” he inquired.\n\n“Quite sure. We ought to part, and we may as well get it past and done. You once said that I was apt to win men against their better judgement; and if I am constantly before your eyes I may cause you to change your plans in opposition to your reason and wish; and afterwards your repentance and my sorrow will be terrible.”\n\n“And you would like to go home?” he asked.\n\n“I want to leave you, and go home.”\n\n“Then it shall be so.”\n\nThough she did not look up at him, she started. There was a difference between the proposition and the covenant, which she had felt only too quickly.\n\n“I feared it would come to this,” she murmured, her countenance meekly fixed. “I don’t complain, Angel, I—I think it best. What you said has quite convinced me. Yes, though nobody else should reproach me if we should stay together, yet somewhen, years hence, you might get angry with me for any ordinary matter, and knowing what you do of my bygones, you yourself might be tempted to say words, and they might be overheard, perhaps by my own children. O, what only hurts me now would torture and kill me then! I will go—to-morrow.”\n\n“And I shall not stay here. Though I didn’t like to initiate it, I have seen that it was advisable we should part—at least for a while, till I can better see the shape that things have taken, and can write to you.”\n\nTess stole a glance at her husband. He was pale, even tremulous; but, as before, she was appalled by the determination revealed in the depths of this gentle being she had married—the will to subdue the grosser to the subtler emotion, the substance to the conception, the flesh to the spirit. Propensities, tendencies, habits, were as dead leaves upon the tyrannous wind of his imaginative ascendency.\n\nHe may have observed her look, for he explained—\n\n“I think of people more kindly when I am away from them”; adding cynically, “God knows; perhaps we will shake down together some day, for weariness; thousands have done it!”\n\nThat day he began to pack up, and she went upstairs and began to pack also. Both knew that it was in their two minds that they might part the next morning for ever, despite the gloss of assuaging conjectures thrown over their proceeding because they were of the sort to whom any parting which has an air of finality is a torture. He knew, and she knew, that, though the fascination which each had exercised over the other—on her part independently of accomplishments—would probably in the first days of their separation be even more potent than ever, time must attenuate that effect; the practical arguments against accepting her as a housemate might pronounce themselves more strongly in the boreal light of a remoter view. Moreover, when two people are once parted—have abandoned a common domicile and a common environment—new growths insensibly bud upward to fill each vacated place; unforeseen accidents hinder intentions, and old plans are forgotten.\n\n# XXXVII\n\nMidnight came and passed silently, for there was nothing to announce it in the Valley of the Froom.\n\nNot long after one o’clock there was a slight creak in the darkened farmhouse once the mansion of the d’Urbervilles. Tess, who used the upper chamber, heard it and awoke. It had come from the corner step of the staircase, which, as usual, was loosely nailed. She saw the door of her bedroom open, and the figure of her husband crossed the stream of moonlight with a curiously careful tread. He was in his shirt and trousers only, and her first flush of joy died when she perceived that his eyes were fixed in an unnatural stare on vacancy. When he reached the middle of the room he stood still and murmured in tones of indescribable sadness—\n\n“Dead! dead! dead!”\n\nUnder the influence of any strongly-disturbing force, Clare would occasionally walk in his sleep, and even perform strange feats, such as he had done on the night of their return from market just before their marriage, when he re-enacted in his bedroom his combat with the man who had insulted her. Tess saw that continued mental distress had wrought him into that somnambulistic state now.\n\nHer loyal confidence in him lay so deep down in her heart, that, awake or asleep, he inspired her with no sort of personal fear. If he had entered with a pistol in his hand he would scarcely have disturbed her trust in his protectiveness.\n\nClare came close, and bent over her. “Dead, dead, dead!” he murmured.\nAfter fixedly regarding her for some moments with the same gaze of unmeasurable woe, he bent lower, enclosed her in his arms, and rolled her in the sheet as in a shroud. Then lifting her from the bed with as much respect as one would show to a dead body, he carried her across the room, murmuring—\n\n“My poor, poor Tess—my dearest, darling Tess! So sweet, so good, so true!”\n\nThe words of endearment, withheld so severely in his waking hours, were inexpressibly sweet to her forlorn and hungry heart. If it had been to save her weary life she would not, by moving or struggling, have put an end to the position she found herself in. Thus she lay in absolute stillness, scarcely venturing to breathe, and, wondering what he was going to do with her, suffered herself to be borne out upon the landing.\n\n“My wife—dead, dead!” he said.\n\nHe paused in his labours for a moment to lean with her against the banister. Was he going to throw her down? Self-solicitude was near extinction in her, and in the knowledge that he had planned to depart on the morrow, possibly for always, she lay in his arms in this precarious position with a sense rather of luxury than of terror. If they could only fall together, and both be dashed to pieces, how fit, how desirable.\n\nHowever, he did not let her fall, but took advantage of the support of the handrail to imprint a kiss upon her lips—lips in the day-time scorned. Then he clasped her with a renewed firmness of hold, and descended the staircase. The creak of the loose stair did not awaken him, and they reached the ground-floor safely. Freeing one of his hands from his grasp of her for a moment, he slid back the door-bar and passed out, slightly striking his stockinged toe against the edge of the door. But this he seemed not to mind, and, having room for extension in the open air, he lifted her against his shoulder, so that he could carry her with ease, the absence of clothes taking much from his burden. Thus he bore her off the premises in the direction of the river a few yards distant.\n\nHis ultimate intention, if he had any, she had not yet divined; and she found herself conjecturing on the matter as a third person might have done. So easefully had she delivered her whole being up to him that it pleased her to think he was regarding her as his absolute possession, to dispose of as he should choose. It was consoling, under the hovering terror of to-morrow’s separation, to feel that he really recognized her now as his wife Tess, and did not cast her off, even if in that recognition he went so far as to arrogate to himself the right of harming her.\n\nAh! now she knew what he was dreaming of—that Sunday morning when he had borne her along through the water with the other dairymaids, who had loved him nearly as much as she, if that were possible, which Tess could hardly admit. Clare did not cross the bridge with her, but proceeding several paces on the same side towards the adjoining mill, at length stood still on the brink of the river.\n\nIts waters, in creeping down these miles of meadowland, frequently divided, serpentining in purposeless curves, looping themselves around little islands that had no name, returning and re-embodying themselves as a broad main stream further on. Opposite the spot to which he had brought her was such a general confluence, and the river was proportionately voluminous and deep. Across it was a narrow foot-bridge; but now the autumn flood had washed the handrail away, leaving the bare plank only, which, lying a few inches above the speeding current, formed a giddy pathway for even steady heads; and Tess had noticed from the window of the house in the day-time young men walking across upon it as a feat in balancing. Her husband had possibly observed the same performance; anyhow, he now mounted the plank, and, sliding one foot forward, advanced along it.\n\nWas he going to drown her? Probably he was. The spot was lonely, the river deep and wide enough to make such a purpose easy of accomplishment. He might drown her if he would; it would be better than parting to-morrow to lead severed lives.\n\nThe swift stream raced and gyrated under them, tossing, distorting, and splitting the moon’s reflected face. Spots of froth travelled past, and intercepted weeds waved behind the piles. If they could both fall together into the current now, their arms would be so tightly clasped together that they could not be saved; they would go out of the world almost painlessly, and there would be no more reproach to her, or to him for marrying her. His last half-hour with her would have been a loving one, while if they lived till he awoke, his day-time aversion would return, and this hour would remain to be contemplated only as a transient dream.\n\nThe impulse stirred in her, yet she dared not indulge it, to make a movement that would have precipitated them both into the gulf. How she valued her own life had been proved; but his—she had no right to tamper with it. He reached the other side with her in safety.\n\nHere they were within a plantation which formed the Abbey grounds, and taking a new hold of her he went onward a few steps till they reached the ruined choir of the Abbey-church. Against the north wall was the empty stone coffin of an abbot, in which every tourist with a turn for grim humour was accustomed to stretch himself. In this Clare carefully laid Tess. Having kissed her lips a second time he breathed deeply, as if a greatly desired end were attained. Clare then lay down on the ground alongside, when he immediately fell into the deep dead slumber of exhaustion, and remained motionless as a log. The spurt of mental excitement which had produced the effort was now over.\n\nTess sat up in the coffin. The night, though dry and mild for the season, was more than sufficiently cold to make it dangerous for him to remain here long, in his half-clothed state. If he were left to himself he would in all probability stay there till the morning, and be chilled to certain death. She had heard of such deaths after sleep-walking. But how could she dare to awaken him, and let him know what he had been doing, when it would mortify him to discover his folly in respect of her? Tess, however, stepping out of her stone confine, shook him slightly, but was unable to arouse him without being violent. It was indispensable to do something, for she was beginning to shiver, the sheet being but a poor protection. Her excitement had in a measure kept her warm during the few minutes’ adventure; but that beatific interval was over.\n\nIt suddenly occurred to her to try persuasion; and accordingly she whispered in his ear, with as much firmness and decision as she could summon—\n\n“Let us walk on, darling,” at the same time taking him suggestively by the arm. To her relief, he unresistingly acquiesced; her words had apparently thrown him back into his dream, which thenceforward seemed to enter on a new phase, wherein he fancied she had risen as a spirit, and was leading him to Heaven. Thus she conducted him by the arm to the stone bridge in front of their residence, crossing which they stood at the manor-house door. Tess’s feet were quite bare, and the stones hurt her, and chilled her to the bone; but Clare was in his woollen stockings and appeared to feel no discomfort.\n\nThere was no further difficulty. She induced him to lie down on his own sofa bed, and covered him up warmly, lighting a temporary fire of wood, to dry any dampness out of him. The noise of these attentions she thought might awaken him, and secretly wished that they might. But the exhaustion of his mind and body was such that he remained undisturbed.\n\nAs soon as they met the next morning Tess divined that Angel knew little or nothing of how far she had been concerned in the night’s excursion, though, as regarded himself, he may have been aware that he had not lain still. In truth, he had awakened that morning from a sleep deep as annihilation; and during those first few moments in which the brain, like a Samson shaking himself, is trying its strength, he had some dim notion of an unusual nocturnal proceeding. But the realities of his situation soon displaced conjecture on the other subject.\n\nHe waited in expectancy to discern some mental pointing; he knew that if any intention of his, concluded over-night, did not vanish in the light of morning, it stood on a basis approximating to one of pure reason, even if initiated by impulse of feeling; that it was so far, therefore, to be trusted. He thus beheld in the pale morning light the resolve to separate from her; not as a hot and indignant instinct, but denuded of the passionateness which had made it scorch and burn; standing in its bones; nothing but a skeleton, but none the less there. Clare no longer hesitated.\n\nAt breakfast, and while they were packing the few remaining articles, he showed his weariness from the night’s effort so unmistakeably that Tess was on the point of revealing all that had happened; but the reflection that it would anger him, grieve him, stultify him, to know that he had instinctively manifested a fondness for her of which his common-sense did not approve, that his inclination had compromised his dignity when reason slept, again deterred her. It was too much like laughing at a man when sober for his erratic deeds during intoxication.\nIt just crossed her mind, too, that he might have a faint recollection of his tender vagary, and was disinclined to allude to it from a conviction that she would take amatory advantage of the opportunity it gave her of appealing to him anew not to go.\n\nHe had ordered by letter a vehicle from the nearest town, and soon after breakfast it arrived. She saw in it the beginning of the end—the temporary end, at least, for the revelation of his tenderness by the incident of the night raised dreams of a possible future with him. The luggage was put on the top, and the man drove them off, the miller and the old waiting-woman expressing some surprise at their precipitate departure, which Clare attributed to his discovery that the mill-work was not of the modern kind which he wished to investigate, a statement that was true so far as it went. Beyond this there was nothing in the manner of their leaving to suggest a _fiasco_, or that they were not going together to visit friends.\n\nTheir route lay near the dairy from which they had started with such solemn joy in each other a few days back, and as Clare wished to wind up his business with Mr Crick, Tess could hardly avoid paying Mrs Crick a call at the same time, unless she would excite suspicion of their unhappy state.\n\nTo make the call as unobtrusive as possible, they left the carriage by the wicket leading down from the high road to the dairy-house, and descended the track on foot, side by side. The withy-bed had been cut, and they could see over the stumps the spot to which Clare had followed her when he pressed her to be his wife; to the left the enclosure in which she had been fascinated by his harp; and far away behind the cow-stalls the mead which had been the scene of their first embrace. The gold of the summer picture was now gray, the colours mean, the rich soil mud, and the river cold.\n\nOver the barton-gate the dairyman saw them, and came forward, throwing into his face the kind of jocularity deemed appropriate in Talbothays and its vicinity on the re-appearance of the newly-married. Then Mrs Crick emerged from the house, and several others of their old acquaintance, though Marian and Retty did not seem to be there.\n\nTess valiantly bore their sly attacks and friendly humours, which affected her far otherwise than they supposed. In the tacit agreement of husband and wife to keep their estrangement a secret they behaved as would have been ordinary. And then, although she would rather there had been no word spoken on the subject, Tess had to hear in detail the story of Marian and Retty. The later had gone home to her father’s, and Marian had left to look for employment elsewhere. They feared she would come to no good.\n\nTo dissipate the sadness of this recital Tess went and bade all her favourite cows goodbye, touching each of them with her hand, and as she and Clare stood side by side at leaving, as if united body and soul, there would have been something peculiarly sorry in their aspect to one who should have seen it truly; two limbs of one life, as they outwardly were, his arm touching hers, her skirts touching him, facing one way, as against all the dairy facing the other, speaking in their adieux as “we”, and yet sundered like the poles. Perhaps something unusually stiff and embarrassed in their attitude, some awkwardness in acting up to their profession of unity, different from the natural shyness of young couples, may have been apparent, for when they were gone Mrs Crick said to her husband—\n\n“How onnatural the brightness of her eyes did seem, and how they stood like waxen images and talked as if they were in a dream! Didn’t it strike ’ee that ’twas so? Tess had always sommat strange in her, and she’s not now quite like the proud young bride of a well-be-doing man.”\n\nThey re-entered the vehicle, and were driven along the roads towards Weatherbury and Stagfoot Lane, till they reached the Lane inn, where Clare dismissed the fly and man. They rested here a while, and entering the Vale were next driven onward towards her home by a stranger who did not know their relations. At a midway point, when Nuttlebury had been passed, and where there were cross-roads, Clare stopped the conveyance and said to Tess that if she meant to return to her mother’s house it was here that he would leave her. As they could not talk with freedom in the driver’s presence he asked her to accompany him for a few steps on foot along one of the branch roads; she assented, and directing the man to wait a few minutes they strolled away.\n\n“Now, let us understand each other,” he said gently. “There is no anger between us, though there is that which I cannot endure at present. I will try to bring myself to endure it. I will let you know where I go to as soon as I know myself. And if I can bring myself to bear it—if it is desirable, possible—I will come to you. But until I come to you it will be better that you should not try to come to me.”\n\nThe severity of the decree seemed deadly to Tess; she saw his view of her clearly enough; he could regard her in no other light than that of one who had practised gross deceit upon him. Yet could a woman who had done even what she had done deserve all this? But she could contest the point with him no further. She simply repeated after him his own words.\n\n“Until you come to me I must not try to come to you?”\n\n“Just so.”\n\n“May I write to you?”\n\n“O yes—if you are ill, or want anything at all. I hope that will not be the case; so that it may happen that I write first to you.”\n\n“I agree to the conditions, Angel; because you know best what my punishment ought to be; only—only—don’t make it more than I can bear!”\n\nThat was all she said on the matter. If Tess had been artful, had she made a scene, fainted, wept hysterically, in that lonely lane, notwithstanding the fury of fastidiousness with which he was possessed, he would probably not have withstood her. But her mood of long-suffering made his way easy for him, and she herself was his best advocate. Pride, too, entered into her submission—which perhaps was a symptom of that reckless acquiescence in chance too apparent in the whole d’Urberville family—and the many effective chords which she could have stirred by an appeal were left untouched.\n\nThe remainder of their discourse was on practical matters only. He now handed her a packet containing a fairly good sum of money, which he had obtained from his bankers for the purpose. The brilliants, the interest in which seemed to be Tess’s for her life only (if he understood the wording of the will), he advised her to let him send to a bank for safety; and to this she readily agreed.\n\nThese things arranged, he walked with Tess back to the carriage, and handed her in. The coachman was paid and told where to drive her. Taking next his own bag and umbrella—the sole articles he had brought with him hitherwards—he bade her goodbye; and they parted there and then.\n\nThe fly moved creepingly up a hill, and Clare watched it go with an unpremeditated hope that Tess would look out of the window for one moment. But that she never thought of doing, would not have ventured to do, lying in a half-dead faint inside. Thus he beheld her recede, and in the anguish of his heart quoted a line from a poet, with peculiar emendations of his own—\n\nGod’s _not_ in his heaven: All’s _wrong_ with the world!\n\nWhen Tess had passed over the crest of the hill he turned to go his own way, and hardly knew that he loved her still.\n\n# XXXVIII\n\nAs she drove on through Blackmoor Vale, and the landscape of her youth began to open around her, Tess aroused herself from her stupor. Her first thought was how would she be able to face her parents?\n\nShe reached a turnpike-gate which stood upon the highway to the village. It was thrown open by a stranger, not by the old man who had kept it for many years, and to whom she had been known; he had probably left on New Year’s Day, the date when such changes were made. Having received no intelligence lately from her home, she asked the turnpike-keeper for news.\n\n“Oh—nothing, miss,” he answered. “Marlott is Marlott still. Folks have died and that. John Durbeyfield, too, hev had a daughter married this week to a gentleman-farmer; not from John’s own house, you know; they was married elsewhere; the gentleman being of that high standing that John’s own folk was not considered well-be-doing enough to have any part in it, the bridegroom seeming not to know how’t have been discovered that John is a old and ancient nobleman himself by blood, with family skillentons in their own vaults to this day, but done out of his property in the time o’ the Romans. However, Sir John, as we call ’n now, kept up the wedding-day as well as he could, and stood treat to everybody in the parish; and John’s wife sung songs at The Pure Drop till past eleven o’clock.”\n\nHearing this, Tess felt so sick at heart that she could not decide to go home publicly in the fly with her luggage and belongings. She asked the turnpike-keeper if she might deposit her things at his house for a while, and, on his offering no objection, she dismissed her carriage, and went on to the village alone by a back lane.\n\nAt sight of her father’s chimney she asked herself how she could possibly enter the house? Inside that cottage her relations were calmly supposing her far away on a wedding-tour with a comparatively rich man, who was to conduct her to bouncing prosperity; while here she was, friendless, creeping up to the old door quite by herself, with no better place to go to in the world.\n\nShe did not reach the house unobserved. Just by the garden-hedge she was met by a girl who knew her—one of the two or three with whom she had been intimate at school. After making a few inquiries as to how Tess came there, her friend, unheeding her tragic look, interrupted with—\n\n“But where’s thy gentleman, Tess?”\n\nTess hastily explained that he had been called away on business, and, leaving her interlocutor, clambered over the garden-hedge, and thus made her way to the house.\n\nAs she went up the garden-path she heard her mother singing by the back door, coming in sight of which she perceived Mrs Durbeyfield on the doorstep in the act of wringing a sheet. Having performed this without observing Tess, she went indoors, and her daughter followed her.\n\nThe washing-tub stood in the same old place on the same old quarter-hogshead, and her mother, having thrown the sheet aside, was about to plunge her arms in anew.\n\n“Why—Tess!—my chil’—I thought you was married!—married really and truly this time—we sent the cider—”\n\n“Yes, mother; so I am.”\n\n“Going to be?”\n\n“No—I am married.”\n\n“Married! Then where’s thy husband?”\n\n“Oh, he’s gone away for a time.”\n\n“Gone away! When was you married, then? The day you said?”\n\n“Yes, Tuesday, mother.”\n\n“And now ’tis on’y Saturday, and he gone away?”\n\n“Yes, he’s gone.”\n\n“What’s the meaning o’ that? ’Nation seize such husbands as you seem to get, say I!”\n\n“Mother!” Tess went across to Joan Durbeyfield, laid her face upon the matron’s bosom, and burst into sobs. “I don’t know how to tell ’ee, mother! You said to me, and wrote to me, that I was not to tell him. But I did tell him—I couldn’t help it—and he went away!”\n\n“O you little fool—you little fool!” burst out Mrs Durbeyfield, splashing Tess and herself in her agitation. “My good God! that ever I should ha’ lived to say it, but I say it again, you little fool!”\n\nTess was convulsed with weeping, the tension of so many days having relaxed at last.\n\n“I know it—I know—I know!” she gasped through her sobs. “But, O my mother, I could not help it! He was so good—and I felt the wickedness of trying to blind him as to what had happened! If—if—it were to be done again—I should do the same. I could not—I dared not—so sin—against him!”\n\n“But you sinned enough to marry him first!”\n\n“Yes, yes; that’s where my misery do lie! But I thought he could get rid o’ me by law if he were determined not to overlook it. And O, if you knew—if you could only half know how I loved him—how anxious I was to have him—and how wrung I was between caring so much for him and my wish to be fair to him!”\n\nTess was so shaken that she could get no further, and sank, a helpless thing, into a chair.\n\n“Well, well; what’s done can’t be undone! I’m sure I don’t know why children o’ my bringing forth should all be bigger simpletons than other people’s—not to know better than to blab such a thing as that, when he couldn’t ha’ found it out till too late!” Here Mrs Durbeyfield began shedding tears on her own account as a mother to be pitied. “What your father will say I don’t know,” she continued; “for he’s been talking about the wedding up at Rolliver’s and The Pure Drop every day since, and about his family getting back to their rightful position through you—poor silly man!—and now you’ve made this mess of it! The Lord-a-Lord!”\n\nAs if to bring matters to a focus, Tess’s father was heard approaching at that moment. He did not, however, enter immediately, and Mrs Durbeyfield said that she would break the bad news to him herself, Tess keeping out of sight for the present. After her first burst of disappointment Joan began to take the mishap as she had taken Tess’s original trouble, as she would have taken a wet holiday or failure in the potato-crop; as a thing which had come upon them irrespective of desert or folly; a chance external impingement to be borne with; not a lesson.\n\nTess retreated upstairs and beheld casually that the beds had been shifted, and new arrangements made. Her old bed had been adapted for two younger children. There was no place here for her now.\n\nThe room below being unceiled she could hear most of what went on there. Presently her father entered, apparently carrying in a live hen. He was a foot-haggler now, having been obliged to sell his second horse, and he travelled with his basket on his arm. The hen had been carried about this morning as it was often carried, to show people that he was in his work, though it had lain, with its legs tied, under the table at Rolliver’s for more than an hour.\n\n“We’ve just had up a story about—” Durbeyfield began, and thereupon related in detail to his wife a discussion which had arisen at the inn about the clergy, originated by the fact of his daughter having married into a clerical family. “They was formerly styled ‘sir’, like my own ancestry,” he said, “though nowadays their true style, strictly speaking, is ‘clerk’ only.” As Tess had wished that no great publicity should be given to the event, he had mentioned no particulars. He hoped she would remove that prohibition soon. He proposed that the couple should take Tess’s own name, d’Urberville, as uncorrupted. It was better than her husbands’s. He asked if any letter had come from her that day.\n\nThen Mrs Durbeyfield informed him that no letter had come, but Tess unfortunately had come herself.\n\nWhen at length the collapse was explained to him, a sullen mortification, not usual with Durbeyfield, overpowered the influence of the cheering glass. Yet the intrinsic quality of the event moved his touchy sensitiveness less than its conjectured effect upon the minds of others.\n\n“To think, now, that this was to be the end o’t!” said Sir John. “And I with a family vault under that there church of Kingsbere as big as Squire Jollard’s ale-cellar, and my folk lying there in sixes and sevens, as genuine county bones and marrow as any recorded in history. And now to be sure what they fellers at Rolliver’s and The Pure Drop will say to me! How they’ll squint and glane, and say, ‘This is yer mighty match is it; this is yer getting back to the true level of yer forefathers in King Norman’s time!’ I feel this is too much, Joan; I shall put an end to myself, title and all—I can bear it no longer!... But she can make him keep her if he’s married her?”\n\n“Why, yes. But she won’t think o’ doing that.”\n\n“D’ye think he really have married her?—or is it like the first—”\n\nPoor Tess, who had heard as far as this, could not bear to hear more. The perception that her word could be doubted even here, in her own parental house, set her mind against the spot as nothing else could have done. How unexpected were the attacks of destiny! And if her father doubted her a little, would not neighbours and acquaintance doubt her much? O, she could not live long at home!\n\nA few days, accordingly, were all that she allowed herself here, at the end of which time she received a short note from Clare, informing her that he had gone to the North of England to look at a farm. In her craving for the lustre of her true position as his wife, and to hide from her parents the vast extent of the division between them, she made use of this letter as her reason for again departing, leaving them under the impression that she was setting out to join him. Still further to screen her husband from any imputation of unkindness to her, she took twenty-five of the fifty pounds Clare had given her, and handed the sum over to her mother, as if the wife of a man like Angel Clare could well afford it, saying that it was a slight return for the trouble and humiliation she had brought upon them in years past. With this assertion of her dignity she bade them farewell; and after that there were lively doings in the Durbeyfield household for some time on the strength of Tess’s bounty, her mother saying, and, indeed, believing, that the rupture which had arisen between the young husband and wife had adjusted itself under their strong feeling that they could not live apart from each other.\n\n# XXXIX\n\nIt was three weeks after the marriage that Clare found himself descending the hill which led to the well-known parsonage of his father. With his downward course the tower of the church rose into the evening sky in a manner of inquiry as to why he had come; and no living person in the twilighted town seemed to notice him, still less to expect him. He was arriving like a ghost, and the sound of his own footsteps was almost an encumbrance to be got rid of.\n\nThe picture of life had changed for him. Before this time he had known it but speculatively; now he thought he knew it as a practical man; though perhaps he did not, even yet. Nevertheless humanity stood before him no longer in the pensive sweetness of Italian art, but in the staring and ghastly attitudes of a Wiertz Museum, and with the leer of a study by Van Beers.\n\nHis conduct during these first weeks had been desultory beyond description. After mechanically attempting to pursue his agricultural plans as though nothing unusual had happened, in the manner recommended by the great and wise men of all ages, he concluded that very few of those great and wise men had ever gone so far outside themselves as to test the feasibility of their counsel. “This is the chief thing: be not perturbed,” said the Pagan moralist. That was just Clare’s own opinion. But he was perturbed. “Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid,” said the Nazarene. Clare chimed in cordially; but his heart was troubled all the same. How he would have liked to confront those two great thinkers, and earnestly appeal to them as fellow-man to fellow-men, and ask them to tell him their method!\n\nHis mood transmuted itself into a dogged indifference till at length he fancied he was looking on his own existence with the passive interest of an outsider.\n\nHe was embittered by the conviction that all this desolation had been brought about by the accident of her being a d’Urberville. When he found that Tess came of that exhausted ancient line, and was not of the new tribes from below, as he had fondly dreamed, why had he not stoically abandoned her in fidelity to his principles? This was what he had got by apostasy, and his punishment was deserved.\n\nThen he became weary and anxious, and his anxiety increased. He wondered if he had treated her unfairly. He ate without knowing that he ate, and drank without tasting. As the hours dropped past, as the motive of each act in the long series of bygone days presented itself to his view, he perceived how intimately the notion of having Tess as a dear possession was mixed up with all his schemes and words and ways.\n\nIn going hither and thither he observed in the outskirts of a small town a red-and-blue placard setting forth the great advantages of the Empire of Brazil as a field for the emigrating agriculturist. Land was offered there on exceptionally advantageous terms. Brazil somewhat attracted him as a new idea. Tess could eventually join him there, and perhaps in that country of contrasting scenes and notions and habits the conventions would not be so operative which made life with her seem impracticable to him here. In brief he was strongly inclined to try Brazil, especially as the season for going thither was just at hand.\n\nWith this view he was returning to Emminster to disclose his plan to his parents, and to make the best explanation he could make of arriving without Tess, short of revealing what had actually separated them. As he reached the door the new moon shone upon his face, just as the old one had done in the small hours of that morning when he had carried his wife in his arms across the river to the graveyard of the monks; but his face was thinner now.\n\nClare had given his parents no warning of his visit, and his arrival stirred the atmosphere of the Vicarage as the dive of the kingfisher stirs a quiet pool. His father and mother were both in the drawing-room, but neither of his brothers was now at home. Angel entered, and closed the door quietly behind him.\n\n“But—where’s your wife, dear Angel?” cried his mother. “How you surprise us!”\n\n“She is at her mother’s—temporarily. I have come home rather in a hurry because I’ve decided to go to Brazil.”\n\n“Brazil! Why they are all Roman Catholics there surely!”\n\n“Are they? I hadn’t thought of that.”\n\nBut even the novelty and painfulness of his going to a Papistical land could not displace for long Mr and Mrs Clare’s natural interest in their son’s marriage.\n\n“We had your brief note three weeks ago announcing that it had taken place,” said Mrs Clare, “and your father sent your godmother’s gift to her, as you know. Of course it was best that none of us should be present, especially as you preferred to marry her from the dairy, and not at her home, wherever that may be. It would have embarrassed you, and given us no pleasure. Your bothers felt that very strongly. Now it is done we do not complain, particularly if she suits you for the business you have chosen to follow instead of the ministry of the Gospel.... Yet I wish I could have seen her first, Angel, or have known a little more about her. We sent her no present of our own, not knowing what would best give her pleasure, but you must suppose it only delayed. Angel, there is no irritation in my mind or your father’s against you for this marriage; but we have thought it much better to reserve our liking for your wife till we could see her. And now you have not brought her. It seems strange. What has happened?”\n\nHe replied that it had been thought best by them that she should go to her parents’ home for the present, whilst he came there.\n\n“I don’t mind telling you, dear mother,” he said, “that I always meant to keep her away from this house till I should feel she could come with credit to you. But this idea of Brazil is quite a recent one. If I do go it will be unadvisable for me to take her on this my first journey. She will remain at her mother’s till I come back.”\n\n“And I shall not see her before you start?”\n\nHe was afraid they would not. His original plan had been, as he had said, to refrain from bringing her there for some little while—not to wound their prejudices—feelings—in any way; and for other reasons he had adhered to it. He would have to visit home in the course of a year, if he went out at once; and it would be possible for them to see her before he started a second time—with her.\n\nA hastily prepared supper was brought in, and Clare made further exposition of his plans. His mother’s disappointment at not seeing the bride still remained with her. Clare’s late enthusiasm for Tess had infected her through her maternal sympathies, till she had almost fancied that a good thing could come out of Nazareth—a charming woman out of Talbothays Dairy. She watched her son as he ate.\n\n“Cannot you describe her? I am sure she is very pretty, Angel.”\n\n“Of that there can be no question!” he said, with a zest which covered its bitterness.\n\n“And that she is pure and virtuous goes without question?”\n\n“Pure and virtuous, of course, she is.”\n\n“I can see her quite distinctly. You said the other day that she was fine in figure; roundly built; had deep red lips like Cupid’s bow; dark eyelashes and brows, an immense rope of hair like a ship’s cable; and large eyes violety-bluey-blackish.”\n\n“I did, mother.”\n\n“I quite see her. And living in such seclusion she naturally had scarce ever seen any young man from the world without till she saw you.”\n\n“Scarcely.”\n\n“You were her first love?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“There are worse wives than these simple, rosy-mouthed, robust girls of the farm. Certainly I could have wished—well, since my son is to be an agriculturist, it is perhaps but proper that his wife should have been accustomed to an outdoor life.”\n\nHis father was less inquisitive; but when the time came for the chapter from the Bible which was always read before evening prayers, the Vicar observed to Mrs Clare—\n\n“I think, since Angel has come, that it will be more appropriate to read the thirty-first of Proverbs than the chapter which we should have had in the usual course of our reading?”\n\n“Yes, certainly,” said Mrs Clare. “The words of King Lemuel” (she could cite chapter and verse as well as her husband). “My dear son, your father has decided to read us the chapter in Proverbs in praise of a virtuous wife. We shall not need to be reminded to apply the words to the absent one. May Heaven shield her in all her ways!”\n\nA lump rose in Clare’s throat. The portable lectern was taken out from the corner and set in the middle of the fireplace, the two old servants came in, and Angel’s father began to read at the tenth verse of the aforesaid chapter—\n\n“Who can find a virtuous woman? for her price is far above rubies. She riseth while it is yet night, and giveth meat to her household. She girdeth her loins with strength and strengtheneth her arms. She perceiveth that her merchandise is good; her candle goeth not out by night. She looketh well to the ways of her household, and eateth not the bread of idleness. Her children arise up and call her blessed; her husband also, and he praiseth her. Many daughters have done virtuously, but thou excellest them all.”\n\nWhen prayers were over, his mother said—\n\n“I could not help thinking how very aptly that chapter your dear father read applied, in some of its particulars, to the woman you have chosen. The perfect woman, you see, was a working woman; not an idler; not a fine lady; but one who used her hands and her head and her heart for the good of others. ‘Her children arise up and call her blessed; her husband also, and he praiseth her. Many daughters have done virtuously, but she excelleth them all.’ Well, I wish I could have seen her, Angel. Since she is pure and chaste, she would have been refined enough for me.”\n\nClare could bear this no longer. His eyes were full of tears, which seemed like drops of molten lead. He bade a quick good night to these sincere and simple souls whom he loved so well; who knew neither the world, the flesh, nor the devil in their own hearts, only as something vague and external to themselves. He went to his own chamber.\n\nHis mother followed him, and tapped at his door. Clare opened it to discover her standing without, with anxious eyes.\n\n“Angel,” she asked, “is there something wrong that you go away so soon? I am quite sure you are not yourself.”\n\n“I am not, quite, mother,” said he.\n\n“About her? Now, my son, I know it is that—I know it is about her! Have you quarrelled in these three weeks?”\n\n“We have not exactly quarrelled,” he said. “But we have had a difference—”\n\n“Angel—is she a young woman whose history will bear investigation?”\n\nWith a mother’s instinct Mrs Clare had put her finger on the kind of trouble that would cause such a disquiet as seemed to agitate her son.\n\n“She is spotless!” he replied; and felt that if it had sent him to eternal hell there and then he would have told that lie.\n\n“Then never mind the rest. After all, there are few purer things in nature then an unsullied country maid. Any crudeness of manner which may offend your more educated sense at first, will, I am sure, disappear under the influence or your companionship and tuition.”\n\nSuch terrible sarcasm of blind magnanimity brought home to Clare the secondary perception that he had utterly wrecked his career by this marriage, which had not been among his early thoughts after the disclosure. True, on his own account he cared very little about his career; but he had wished to make it at least a respectable one on account of his parents and brothers. And now as he looked into the candle its flame dumbly expressed to him that it was made to shine on sensible people, and that it abhorred lighting the face of a dupe and a failure.\n\nWhen his agitation had cooled he would be at moments incensed with his poor wife for causing a situation in which he was obliged to practise deception on his parents. He almost talked to her in his anger, as if she had been in the room. And then her cooing voice, plaintive in expostulation, disturbed the darkness, the velvet touch of her lips passed over his brow, and he could distinguish in the air the warmth of her breath.\n\nThis night the woman of his belittling deprecations was thinking how great and good her husband was. But over them both there hung a deeper shade than the shade which Angel Clare perceived, namely, the shade of his own limitations. With all his attempted independence of judgement this advanced and well-meaning young man, a sample product of the last five-and-twenty years, was yet the slave to custom and conventionality when surprised back into his early teachings. No prophet had told him, and he was not prophet enough to tell himself, that essentially this young wife of his was as deserving of the praise of King Lemuel as any other woman endowed with the same dislike of evil, her moral value having to be reckoned not by achievement but by tendency. Moreover, the figure near at hand suffers on such occasion, because it shows up its sorriness without shade; while vague figures afar off are honoured, in that their distance makes artistic virtues of their stains. In considering what Tess was not, he overlooked what she was, and forgot that the defective can be more than the entire.\n\n# XL\n\nAt breakfast Brazil was the topic, and all endeavoured to take a hopeful view of Clare’s proposed experiment with that country’s soil, notwithstanding the discouraging reports of some farm-labourers who had emigrated thither and returned home within the twelve months. After breakfast Clare went into the little town to wind up such trifling matters as he was concerned with there, and to get from the local bank all the money he possessed. On his way back he encountered Miss Mercy Chant by the church, from whose walls she seemed to be a sort of emanation. She was carrying an armful of Bibles for her class, and such was her view of life that events which produced heartache in others wrought beatific smiles upon her—an enviable result, although, in the opinion of Angel, it was obtained by a curiously unnatural sacrifice of humanity to mysticism.\n\nShe had learnt that he was about to leave England, and observed what an excellent and promising scheme it seemed to be.\n\n“Yes; it is a likely scheme enough in a commercial sense, no doubt,” he replied. “But, my dear Mercy, it snaps the continuity of existence. Perhaps a cloister would be preferable.”\n\n“A cloister! O, Angel Clare!”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Why, you wicked man, a cloister implies a monk, and a monk Roman Catholicism.”\n\n“And Roman Catholicism sin, and sin damnation. Thou art in a parlous state, Angel Clare.”\n\n“_I_ glory in my Protestantism!” she said severely.\n\nThen Clare, thrown by sheer misery into one of the demoniacal moods in which a man does despite to his true principles, called her close to him, and fiendishly whispered in her ear the most heterodox ideas he could think of. His momentary laughter at the horror which appeared on her fair face ceased when it merged in pain and anxiety for his welfare.\n\n“Dear Mercy,” he said, “you must forgive me. I think I am going crazy!”\n\nShe thought that he was; and thus the interview ended, and Clare re-entered the Vicarage. With the local banker he deposited the jewels till happier days should arise. He also paid into the bank thirty pounds—to be sent to Tess in a few months, as she might require; and wrote to her at her parents’ home in Blackmoor Vale to inform her of what he had done. This amount, with the sum he had already placed in her hands—about fifty pounds—he hoped would be amply sufficient for her wants just at present, particularly as in an emergency she had been directed to apply to his father.\n\nHe deemed it best not to put his parents into communication with her by informing them of her address; and, being unaware of what had really happened to estrange the two, neither his father nor his mother suggested that he should do so. During the day he left the parsonage, for what he had to complete he wished to get done quickly.\n\nAs the last duty before leaving this part of England it was necessary for him to call at the Wellbridge farmhouse, in which he had spent with Tess the first three days of their marriage, the trifle of rent having to be paid, the key given up of the rooms they had occupied, and two or three small articles fetched away that they had left behind. It was under this roof that the deepest shadow ever thrown upon his life had stretched its gloom over him. Yet when he had unlocked the door of the sitting-room and looked into it, the memory which returned first upon him was that of their happy arrival on a similar afternoon, the first fresh sense of sharing a habitation conjointly, the first meal together, the chatting by the fire with joined hands.\n\nThe farmer and his wife were in the field at the moment of his visit, and Clare was in the rooms alone for some time. Inwardly swollen with a renewal of sentiment that he had not quite reckoned with, he went upstairs to her chamber, which had never been his. The bed was smooth as she had made it with her own hands on the morning of leaving. The mistletoe hung under the tester just as he had placed it. Having been there three or four weeks it was turning colour, and the leaves and berries were wrinkled. Angel took it down and crushed it into the grate. Standing there, he for the first time doubted whether his course in this conjecture had been a wise, much less a generous, one. But had he not been cruelly blinded? In the incoherent multitude of his emotions he knelt down at the bedside wet-eyed. “O Tess! If you had only told me sooner, I would have forgiven you!” he mourned.\n\nHearing a footstep below, he rose and went to the top of the stairs. At the bottom of the flight he saw a woman standing, and on her turning up her face recognized the pale, dark-eyed Izz Huett.\n\n“Mr Clare,” she said, “I’ve called to see you and Mrs Clare, and to inquire if ye be well. I thought you might be back here again.”\n\nThis was a girl whose secret he had guessed, but who had not yet guessed his; an honest girl who loved him—one who would have made as good, or nearly as good, a practical farmer’s wife as Tess.\n\n“I am here alone,” he said; “we are not living here now.” Explaining why he had come, he asked, “Which way are you going home, Izz?”\n\n“I have no home at Talbothays Dairy now, sir,” she said.\n\n“Why is that?”\n\nIzz looked down.\n\n“It was so dismal there that I left! I am staying out this way.” She pointed in a contrary direction, the direction in which he was journeying.\n\n“Well—are you going there now? I can take you if you wish for a lift.”\n\nHer olive complexion grew richer in hue.\n\n“Thank ’ee, Mr Clare,” she said.\n\nHe soon found the farmer, and settled the account for his rent and the few other items which had to be considered by reason of the sudden abandonment of the lodgings. On Clare’s return to his horse and gig, Izz jumped up beside him.\n\n“I am going to leave England, Izz,” he said, as they drove on. “Going to Brazil.”\n\n“And do Mrs Clare like the notion of such a journey?” she asked.\n\n“She is not going at present—say for a year or so. I am going out to reconnoitre—to see what life there is like.”\n\nThey sped along eastward for some considerable distance, Izz making no observation.\n\n“How are the others?” he inquired. “How is Retty?”\n\n“She was in a sort of nervous state when I zid her last; and so thin and hollow-cheeked that ’a do seem in a decline. Nobody will ever fall in love wi’ her any more,” said Izz absently.\n\n“And Marian?”\n\nIzz lowered her voice.\n\n“Marian drinks.”\n\n“Indeed!”\n\n“Yes. The dairyman has got rid of her.”\n\n“And you!”\n\n“I don’t drink, and I bain’t in a decline. But—I am no great things at singing afore breakfast now!”\n\n“How is that? Do you remember how neatly you used to turn ‘’Twas down in Cupid’s Gardens’ and ‘The Tailor’s Breeches’ at morning milking?”\n\n“Ah, yes! When you first came, sir, that was. Not when you had been there a bit.”\n\n“Why was that falling-off?”\n\nHer black eyes flashed up to his face for one moment by way of answer.\n\n“Izz!—how weak of you—for such as I!” he said, and fell into reverie. “Then—suppose I had asked _you_ to marry me?”\n\n“If you had I should have said ‘Yes’, and you would have married a woman who loved ’ee!”\n\n“Really!”\n\n“Down to the ground!” she whispered vehemently. “O my God! did you never guess it till now!”\n\nBy-and-by they reached a branch road to a village.\n\n“I must get down. I live out there,” said Izz abruptly, never having spoken since her avowal.\n\nClare slowed the horse. He was incensed against his fate, bitterly disposed towards social ordinances; for they had cooped him up in a corner, out of which there was no legitimate pathway. Why not be revenged on society by shaping his future domesticities loosely, instead of kissing the pedagogic rod of convention in this ensnaring manner?\n\n“I am going to Brazil alone, Izz,” said he. “I have separated from my wife for personal, not voyaging, reasons. I may never live with her again. I may not be able to love you; but—will you go with me instead of her?”\n\n“You truly wish me to go?”\n\n“I do. I have been badly used enough to wish for relief. And you at least love me disinterestedly.”\n\n“Yes—I will go,” said Izz, after a pause.\n\n“You will? You know what it means, Izz?”\n\n“It means that I shall live with you for the time you are over there—that’s good enough for me.”\n\n“Remember, you are not to trust me in morals now. But I ought to remind you that it will be wrong-doing in the eyes of civilization—Western civilization, that is to say.”\n\n“I don’t mind that; no woman do when it comes to agony-point, and there’s no other way!”\n\n“Then don’t get down, but sit where you are.”\n\nHe drove past the cross-roads, one mile, two miles, without showing any signs of affection.\n\n“You love me very, very much, Izz?” he suddenly asked.\n\n“I do—I have said I do! I loved you all the time we was at the dairy together!”\n\n“More than Tess?”\n\nShe shook her head.\n\n“No,” she murmured, “not more than she.”\n\n“How’s that?”\n\n“Because nobody could love ’ee more than Tess did!... She would have laid down her life for ’ee. I could do no more.”\n\nLike the prophet on the top of Peor, Izz Huett would fain have spoken perversely at such a moment, but the fascination exercised over her rougher nature by Tess’s character compelled her to grace.\n\nClare was silent; his heart had risen at these straightforward words from such an unexpected unimpeachable quarter. In his throat was something as if a sob had solidified there. His ears repeated, “_She would have laid down her life for ’ee. I could do no more!_”\n\n“Forget our idle talk, Izz,” he said, turning the horse’s head suddenly. “I don’t know what I’ve been saying! I will now drive you back to where your lane branches off.”\n\n“So much for honesty towards ’ee! O—how can I bear it—how can I—how can I!”\n\nIzz Huett burst into wild tears, and beat her forehead as she saw what she had done.\n\n“Do you regret that poor little act of justice to an absent one? O, Izz, don’t spoil it by regret!”\n\nShe stilled herself by degrees.\n\n“Very well, sir. Perhaps I didn’t know what I was saying, either, wh—when I agreed to go! I wish—what cannot be!”\n\n“Because I have a loving wife already.”\n\n“Yes, yes! You have!”\n\nThey reached the corner of the lane which they had passed half an hour earlier, and she hopped down.\n\n“Izz—please, please forget my momentary levity!” he cried. “It was so ill-considered, so ill-advised!”\n\n“Forget it? Never, never! O, it was no levity to me!”\n\nHe felt how richly he deserved the reproach that the wounded cry conveyed, and, in a sorrow that was inexpressible, leapt down and took her hand.\n\n“Well, but, Izz, we’ll part friends, anyhow? You don’t know what I’ve had to bear!”\n\nShe was a really generous girl, and allowed no further bitterness to mar their adieux.\n\n“I forgive ’ee, sir!” she said.\n\n“Now, Izz,” he said, while she stood beside him there, forcing himself to the mentor’s part he was far from feeling; “I want you to tell Marian when you see her that she is to be a good woman, and not to give way to folly. Promise that, and tell Retty that there are more worthy men than I in the world, that for my sake she is to act wisely and well—remember the words—wisely and well—for my sake. I send this message to them as a dying man to the dying; for I shall never see them again. And you, Izzy, you have saved me by your honest words about my wife from an incredible impulse towards folly and treachery. Women may be bad, but they are not so bad as men in these things! On that one account I can never forget you. Be always the good and sincere girl you have hitherto been; and think of me as a worthless lover, but a faithful friend. Promise.”\n\nShe gave the promise.\n\n“Heaven bless and keep you, sir. Goodbye!”\n\nHe drove on; but no sooner had Izz turned into the lane, and Clare was out of sight, than she flung herself down on the bank in a fit of racking anguish; and it was with a strained unnatural face that she entered her mother’s cottage late that night. Nobody ever was told how Izz spent the dark hours that intervened between Angel Clare’s parting from her and her arrival home.\n\nClare, too, after bidding the girl farewell, was wrought to aching thoughts and quivering lips. But his sorrow was not for Izz. That evening he was within a feather-weight’s turn of abandoning his road to the nearest station, and driving across that elevated dorsal line of South Wessex which divided him from his Tess’s home. It was neither a contempt for her nature, nor the probable state of her heart, which deterred him.\n\nNo; it was a sense that, despite her love, as corroborated by Izz’s admission, the facts had not changed. If he was right at first, he was right now. And the momentum of the course on which he had embarked tended to keep him going in it, unless diverted by a stronger, more sustained force than had played upon him this afternoon. He could soon come back to her. He took the train that night for London, and five days after shook hands in farewell of his brothers at the port of embarkation.\n\n# XLI\n\nFrom the foregoing events of the winter-time let us press on to an October day, more than eight months subsequent to the parting of Clare and Tess. We discover the latter in changed conditions; instead of a bride with boxes and trunks which others bore, we see her a lonely woman with a basket and a bundle in her own porterage, as at an earlier time when she was no bride; instead of the ample means that were projected by her husband for her comfort through this probationary period, she can produce only a flattened purse.\n\nAfter again leaving Marlott, her home, she had got through the spring and summer without any great stress upon her physical powers, the time being mainly spent in rendering light irregular service at dairy-work near Port-Bredy to the west of the Blackmoor Valley, equally remote from her native place and from Talbothays. She preferred this to living on his allowance. Mentally she remained in utter stagnation, a condition which the mechanical occupation rather fostered than checked. Her consciousness was at that other dairy, at that other season, in the presence of the tender lover who had confronted her there—he who, the moment she had grasped him to keep for her own, had disappeared like a shape in a vision.\n\nThe dairy-work lasted only till the milk began to lessen, for she had not met with a second regular engagement as at Talbothays, but had done duty as a supernumerary only. However, as harvest was now beginning, she had simply to remove from the pasture to the stubble to find plenty of further occupation, and this continued till harvest was done.\n\nOf the five-and-twenty pounds which had remained to her of Clare’s allowance, after deducting the other half of the fifty as a contribution to her parents for the trouble and expense to which she had put them, she had as yet spent but little. But there now followed an unfortunate interval of wet weather, during which she was obliged to fall back upon her sovereigns.\n\nShe could not bear to let them go. Angel had put them into her hand, had obtained them bright and new from his bank for her; his touch had consecrated them to souvenirs of himself—they appeared to have had as yet no other history than such as was created by his and her own experiences—and to disperse them was like giving away relics. But she had to do it, and one by one they left her hands.\n\nShe had been compelled to send her mother her address from time to time, but she concealed her circumstances. When her money had almost gone a letter from her mother reached her. Joan stated that they were in dreadful difficulty; the autumn rains had gone through the thatch of the house, which required entire renewal; but this could not be done because the previous thatching had never been paid for. New rafters and a new ceiling upstairs also were required, which, with the previous bill, would amount to a sum of twenty pounds. As her husband was a man of means, and had doubtless returned by this time, could she not send them the money?\n\nTess had thirty pounds coming to her almost immediately from Angel’s bankers, and, the case being so deplorable, as soon as the sum was received she sent the twenty as requested. Part of the remainder she was obliged to expend in winter clothing, leaving only a nominal sum for the whole inclement season at hand. When the last pound had gone, a remark of Angel’s that whenever she required further resources she was to apply to his father, remained to be considered.\n\nBut the more Tess thought of the step, the more reluctant was she to take it. The same delicacy, pride, false shame, whatever it may be called, on Clare’s account, which had led her to hide from her own parents the prolongation of the estrangement, hindered her owning to his that she was in want after the fair allowance he had left her. They probably despised her already; how much more they would despise her in the character of a mendicant! The consequence was that by no effort could the parson’s daughter-in-law bring herself to let him know her state.\n\nHer reluctance to communicate with her husband’s parents might, she thought, lessen with the lapse of time; but with her own the reverse obtained. On her leaving their house after the short visit subsequent to her marriage they were under the impression that she was ultimately going to join her husband; and from that time to the present she had done nothing to disturb their belief that she was awaiting his return in comfort, hoping against hope that his journey to Brazil would result in a short stay only, after which he would come to fetch her, or that he would write for her to join him; in any case that they would soon present a united front to their families and the world. This hope she still fostered. To let her parents know that she was a deserted wife, dependent, now that she had relieved their necessities, on her own hands for a living, after the _éclat_ of a marriage which was to nullify the collapse of the first attempt, would be too much indeed.\n\nThe set of brilliants returned to her mind. Where Clare had deposited them she did not know, and it mattered little, if it were true that she could only use and not sell them. Even were they absolutely hers it would be passing mean to enrich herself by a legal title to them which was not essentially hers at all.\n\nMeanwhile her husband’s days had been by no means free from trial. At this moment he was lying ill of fever in the clay lands near Curitiba in Brazil, having been drenched with thunder-storms and persecuted by other hardships, in common with all the English farmers and farm-labourers who, just at this time, were deluded into going thither by the promises of the Brazilian Government, and by the baseless assumption that those frames which, ploughing and sowing on English uplands, had resisted all the weathers to whose moods they had been born, could resist equally well all the weathers by which they were surprised on Brazilian plains.\n\nTo return. Thus it happened that when the last of Tess’s sovereigns had been spent she was unprovided with others to take their place, while on account of the season she found it increasingly difficult to get employment. Not being aware of the rarity of intelligence, energy, health, and willingness in any sphere of life, she refrained from seeking an indoor occupation; fearing towns, large houses, people of means and social sophistication, and of manners other than rural. From that direction of gentility Black Care had come. Society might be better than she supposed from her slight experience of it. But she had no proof of this, and her instinct in the circumstances was to avoid its purlieus.\n\nThe small dairies to the west, beyond Port-Bredy, in which she had served as supernumerary milkmaid during the spring and summer required no further aid. Room would probably have been made for her at Talbothays, if only out of sheer compassion; but comfortable as her life had been there, she could not go back. The anti-climax would be too intolerable; and her return might bring reproach upon her idolized husband. She could not have borne their pity, and their whispered remarks to one another upon her strange situation; though she would almost have faced a knowledge of her circumstances by every individual there, so long as her story had remained isolated in the mind of each. It was the interchange of ideas about her that made her sensitiveness wince. Tess could not account for this distinction; she simply knew that she felt it.\n\nShe was now on her way to an upland farm in the centre of the county, to which she had been recommended by a wandering letter which had reached her from Marian. Marian had somehow heard that Tess was separated from her husband—probably through Izz Huett—and the good-natured and now tippling girl, deeming Tess in trouble, had hastened to notify to her former friend that she herself had gone to this upland spot after leaving the dairy, and would like to see her there, where there was room for other hands, if it was really true that she worked again as of old.\n\nWith the shortening of the days all hope of obtaining her husband’s forgiveness began to leave her; and there was something of the habitude of the wild animal in the unreflecting instinct with which she rambled on—disconnecting herself by littles from her eventful past at every step, obliterating her identity, giving no thought to accidents or contingencies which might make a quick discovery of her whereabouts by others of importance to her own happiness, if not to theirs.\n\nAmong the difficulties of her lonely position not the least was the attention she excited by her appearance, a certain bearing of distinction, which she had caught from Clare, being superadded to her natural attractiveness. Whilst the clothes lasted which had been prepared for her marriage, these casual glances of interest caused her no inconvenience, but as soon as she was compelled to don the wrapper of a fieldwoman, rude words were addressed to her more than once; but nothing occurred to cause her bodily fear till a particular November afternoon.\n\nShe had preferred the country west of the River Brit to the upland farm for which she was now bound, because, for one thing, it was nearer to the home of her husband’s father; and to hover about that region unrecognized, with the notion that she might decide to call at the Vicarage some day, gave her pleasure. But having once decided to try the higher and drier levels, she pressed back eastward, marching afoot towards the village of Chalk-Newton, where she meant to pass the night.\n\nThe lane was long and unvaried, and, owing to the rapid shortening of the days, dusk came upon her before she was aware. She had reached the top of a hill down which the lane stretched its serpentine length in glimpses, when she heard footsteps behind her back, and in a few moments she was overtaken by a man. He stepped up alongside Tess and said—\n\n“Good night, my pretty maid”: to which she civilly replied.\n\nThe light still remaining in the sky lit up her face, though the landscape was nearly dark. The man turned and stared hard at her.\n\n“Why, surely, it is the young wench who was at Trantridge awhile—young Squire d’Urberville’s friend? I was there at that time, though I don’t live there now.”\n\nShe recognized in him the well-to-do boor whom Angel had knocked down at the inn for addressing her coarsely. A spasm of anguish shot through her, and she returned him no answer.\n\n“Be honest enough to own it, and that what I said in the town was true, though your fancy-man was so up about it—hey, my sly one? You ought to beg my pardon for that blow of his, considering.”\n\nStill no answer came from Tess. There seemed only one escape for her hunted soul. She suddenly took to her heels with the speed of the wind, and, without looking behind her, ran along the road till she came to a gate which opened directly into a plantation. Into this she plunged, and did not pause till she was deep enough in its shade to be safe against any possibility of discovery.\n\nUnder foot the leaves were dry, and the foliage of some holly bushes which grew among the deciduous trees was dense enough to keep off draughts. She scraped together the dead leaves till she had formed them into a large heap, making a sort of nest in the middle. Into this Tess crept.\n\nSuch sleep as she got was naturally fitful; she fancied she heard strange noises, but persuaded herself that they were caused by the breeze. She thought of her husband in some vague warm clime on the other side of the globe, while she was here in the cold. Was there another such a wretched being as she in the world? Tess asked herself; and, thinking of her wasted life, said, “All is vanity.” She repeated the words mechanically, till she reflected that this was a most inadequate thought for modern days. Solomon had thought as far as that more than two thousand years ago; she herself, though not in the van of thinkers, had got much further. If all were only vanity, who would mind it? All was, alas, worse than vanity—injustice, punishment, exaction, death. The wife of Angel Clare put her hand to her brow, and felt its curve, and the edges of her eye-sockets perceptible under the soft skin, and thought as she did so that a time would come when that bone would be bare. “I wish it were now,” she said.\n\nIn the midst of these whimsical fancies she heard a new strange sound among the leaves. It might be the wind; yet there was scarcely any wind. Sometimes it was a palpitation, sometimes a flutter; sometimes it was a sort of gasp or gurgle. Soon she was certain that the noises came from wild creatures of some kind, the more so when, originating in the boughs overhead, they were followed by the fall of a heavy body upon the ground. Had she been ensconced here under other and more pleasant conditions she would have become alarmed; but, outside humanity, she had at present no fear.\n\nDay at length broke in the sky. When it had been day aloft for some little while it became day in the wood.\n\nDirectly the assuring and prosaic light of the world’s active hours had grown strong, she crept from under her hillock of leaves, and looked around boldly. Then she perceived what had been going on to disturb her. The plantation wherein she had taken shelter ran down at this spot into a peak, which ended it hitherward, outside the hedge being arable ground. Under the trees several pheasants lay about, their rich plumage dabbled with blood; some were dead, some feebly twitching a wing, some staring up at the sky, some pulsating quickly, some contorted, some stretched out—all of them writhing in agony, except the fortunate ones whose tortures had ended during the night by the inability of nature to bear more.\n\nTess guessed at once the meaning of this. The birds had been driven down into this corner the day before by some shooting-party; and while those that had dropped dead under the shot, or had died before nightfall, had been searched for and carried off, many badly wounded birds had escaped and hidden themselves away, or risen among the thick boughs, where they had maintained their position till they grew weaker with loss of blood in the night-time, when they had fallen one by one as she had heard them.\n\nShe had occasionally caught glimpses of these men in girlhood, looking over hedges, or peeping through bushes, and pointing their guns, strangely accoutred, a bloodthirsty light in their eyes. She had been told that, rough and brutal as they seemed just then, they were not like this all the year round, but were, in fact, quite civil persons save during certain weeks of autumn and winter, when, like the inhabitants of the Malay Peninsula, they ran amuck, and made it their purpose to destroy life—in this case harmless feathered creatures, brought into being by artificial means solely to gratify these propensities—at once so unmannerly and so unchivalrous towards their weaker fellows in Nature’s teeming family.\n\nWith the impulse of a soul who could feel for kindred sufferers as much as for herself, Tess’s first thought was to put the still living birds out of their torture, and to this end with her own hands she broke the necks of as many as she could find, leaving them to lie where she had found them till the game-keepers should come—as they probably would come—to look for them a second time.\n\n“Poor darlings—to suppose myself the most miserable being on earth in the sight o’ such misery as yours!” she exclaimed, her tears running down as she killed the birds tenderly. “And not a twinge of bodily pain about me! I be not mangled, and I be not bleeding, and I have two hands to feed and clothe me.” She was ashamed of herself for her gloom of the night, based on nothing more tangible than a sense of condemnation under an arbitrary law of society which had no foundation in Nature.\n\n# XLII\n\nIt was now broad day, and she started again, emerging cautiously upon the highway. But there was no need for caution; not a soul was at hand, and Tess went onward with fortitude, her recollection of the birds’ silent endurance of their night of agony impressing upon her the relativity of sorrows and the tolerable nature of her own, if she could once rise high enough to despise opinion. But that she could not do so long as it was held by Clare.\n\nShe reached Chalk-Newton, and breakfasted at an inn, where several young men were troublesomely complimentary to her good looks. Somehow she felt hopeful, for was it not possible that her husband also might say these same things to her even yet? She was bound to take care of herself on the chance of it, and keep off these casual lovers. To this end Tess resolved to run no further risks from her appearance. As soon as she got out of the village she entered a thicket and took from her basket one of the oldest field-gowns, which she had never put on even at the dairy—never since she had worked among the stubble at Marlott. She also, by a felicitous thought, took a handkerchief from her bundle and tied it round her face under her bonnet, covering her chin and half her cheeks and temples, as if she were suffering from toothache. Then with her little scissors, by the aid of a pocket looking-glass, she mercilessly nipped her eyebrows off, and thus insured against aggressive admiration, she went on her uneven way.\n\n“What a mommet of a maid!” said the next man who met her to a companion.\n\nTears came into her eyes for very pity of herself as she heard him.\n“But I don’t care!” she said. “O no—I don’t care! I’ll always be ugly now, because Angel is not here, and I have nobody to take care of me. My husband that was is gone away, and never will love me any more; but I love him just the same, and hate all other men, and like to make ’em think scornfully of me!”\n\nThus Tess walks on; a figure which is part of the landscape; a fieldwoman pure and simple, in winter guise; a gray serge cape, a red woollen cravat, a stuff skirt covered by a whitey-brown rough wrapper, and buff-leather gloves. Every thread of that old attire has become faded and thin under the stroke of raindrops, the burn of sunbeams, and the stress of winds. There is no sign of young passion in her now—\n\nThe maiden’s mouth is cold . . . . . Fold over simple fold Binding her head.\n\nInside this exterior, over which the eye might have roved as over a thing scarcely percipient, almost inorganic, there was the record of a pulsing life which had learnt too well, for its years, of the dust and ashes of things, of the cruelty of lust and the fragility of love.\n\nNext day the weather was bad, but she trudged on, the honesty, directness, and impartiality of elemental enmity disconcerting her but little. Her object being a winter’s occupation and a winter’s home, there was no time to lose. Her experience of short hirings had been such that she was determined to accept no more.\n\nThus she went forward from farm to farm in the direction of the place whence Marian had written to her, which she determined to make use of as a last shift only, its rumoured stringencies being the reverse of tempting. First she inquired for the lighter kinds of employment, and, as acceptance in any variety of these grew hopeless, applied next for the less light, till, beginning with the dairy and poultry tendance that she liked best, she ended with the heavy and course pursuits which she liked least—work on arable land: work of such roughness, indeed, as she would never have deliberately voluteered for.\n\nTowards the second evening she reached the irregular chalk table-land or plateau, bosomed with semi-globular tumuli—as if Cybele the Many-breasted were supinely extended there—which stretched between the valley of her birth and the valley of her love.\n\nHere the air was dry and cold, and the long cart-roads were blown white and dusty within a few hours after rain. There were few trees, or none, those that would have grown in the hedges being mercilessly plashed down with the quickset by the tenant-farmers, the natural enemies of tree, bush, and brake. In the middle distance ahead of her she could see the summits of Bulbarrow and of Nettlecombe Tout, and they seemed friendly. They had a low and unassuming aspect from this upland, though as approached on the other side from Blackmoor in her childhood they were as lofty bastions against the sky. Southerly, at many miles’ distance, and over the hills and ridges coastward, she could discern a surface like polished steel: it was the English Channel at a point far out towards France.\n\nBefore her, in a slight depression, were the remains of a village. She had, in fact, reached Flintcomb-Ash, the place of Marian’s sojourn. There seemed to be no help for it; hither she was doomed to come. The stubborn soil around her showed plainly enough that the kind of labour in demand here was of the roughest kind; but it was time to rest from searching, and she resolved to stay, particularly as it began to rain. At the entrance to the village was a cottage whose gable jutted into the road, and before applying for a lodging she stood under its shelter, and watched the evening close in.\n\n“Who would think I was Mrs Angel Clare!” she said.\n\nThe wall felt warm to her back and shoulders, and she found that immediately within the gable was the cottage fireplace, the heat of which came through the bricks. She warmed her hands upon them, and also put her cheek—red and moist with the drizzle—against their comforting surface. The wall seemed to be the only friend she had. She had so little wish to leave it that she could have stayed there all night.\n\nTess could hear the occupants of the cottage—gathered together after their day’s labour—talking to each other within, and the rattle of their supper-plates was also audible. But in the village-street she had seen no soul as yet. The solitude was at last broken by the approach of one feminine figure, who, though the evening was cold, wore the print gown and the tilt-bonnet of summer time. Tess instinctively thought it might be Marian, and when she came near enough to be distinguishable in the gloom, surely enough it was she. Marian was even stouter and redder in the face than formerly, and decidedly shabbier in attire. At any previous period of her existence Tess would hardly have cared to renew the acquaintance in such conditions; but her loneliness was excessive, and she responded readily to Marian’s greeting.\n\nMarian was quite respectful in her inquiries, but seemed much moved by the fact that Tess should still continue in no better condition than at first; though she had dimly heard of the separation.\n\n“Tess—Mrs Clare—the dear wife of dear he! And is it really so bad as this, my child? Why is your cwomely face tied up in such a way? Anybody been beating ’ee? Not _he_?”\n\n“No, no, no! I merely did it not to be clipsed or colled, Marian.”\n\nShe pulled off in disgust a bandage which could suggest such wild thoughts.\n\n“And you’ve got no collar on” (Tess had been accustomed to wear a little white collar at the dairy).\n\n“I know it, Marian.”\n\n“You’ve lost it travelling.”\n\n“I’ve not lost it. The truth is, I don’t care anything about my looks; and so I didn’t put it on.”\n\n“And you don’t wear your wedding-ring?”\n\n“Yes, I do; but not in public. I wear it round my neck on a ribbon. I don’t wish people to think who I am by marriage, or that I am married at all; it would be so awkward while I lead my present life.”\nMarian paused.\n\n“But you _be_ a gentleman’s wife; and it seems hardly fair that you should live like this!”\n\n“O yes it is, quite fair; though I am very unhappy.”\n\n“Well, well. _He_ married you—and you can be unhappy!”\n\n“Wives are unhappy sometimes; from no fault of their husbands—from their own.”\n\n“You’ve no faults, deary; that I’m sure of. And he’s none. So it must be something outside ye both.”\n\n“Marian, dear Marian, will you do me a good turn without asking questions? My husband has gone abroad, and somehow I have overrun my allowance, so that I have to fall back upon my old work for a time. Do not call me Mrs Clare, but Tess, as before. Do they want a hand here?”\n\n“O yes; they’ll take one always, because few care to come. ’Tis a starve-acre place. Corn and swedes are all they grow. Though I be here myself, I feel ’tis a pity for such as you to come.”\n\n“But you used to be as good a dairywoman as I.”\n\n“Yes; but I’ve got out o’ that since I took to drink. Lord, that’s the only comfort I’ve got now! If you engage, you’ll be set swede-hacking. That’s what I be doing; but you won’t like it.”\n\n“O—anything! Will you speak for me?”\n\n“You will do better by speaking for yourself.”\n\n“Very well. Now, Marian, remember—nothing about _him_ if I get the place. I don’t wish to bring his name down to the dirt.”\n\nMarian, who was really a trustworthy girl though of coarser grain than Tess, promised anything she asked.\n\n“This is pay-night,” she said, “and if you were to come with me you would know at once. I be real sorry that you are not happy; but ’tis because he’s away, I know. You couldn’t be unhappy if he were here, even if he gie’d ye no money—even if he used you like a drudge.”\n\n“That’s true; I could not!”\n\nThey walked on together and soon reached the farmhouse, which was almost sublime in its dreariness. There was not a tree within sight; there was not, at this season, a green pasture—nothing but fallow and turnips everywhere, in large fields divided by hedges plashed to unrelieved levels.\n\nTess waited outside the door of the farmhouse till the group of workfolk had received their wages, and then Marian introduced her. The farmer himself, it appeared, was not at home, but his wife, who represented him this evening, made no objection to hiring Tess, on her agreeing to remain till Old Lady-Day. Female field-labour was seldom offered now, and its cheapness made it profitable for tasks which women could perform as readily as men.\n\nHaving signed the agreement, there was nothing more for Tess to do at present than to get a lodging, and she found one in the house at whose gable-wall she had warmed herself. It was a poor subsistence that she had ensured, but it would afford a shelter for the winter at any rate.\nThat night she wrote to inform her parents of her new address, in case a letter should arrive at Marlott from her husband. But she did not tell them of the sorriness of her situation: it might have brought reproach upon him.\n\n# XLIII\n\nThere was no exaggeration in Marian’s definition of Flintcomb-Ash farm as a starve-acre place. The single fat thing on the soil was Marian herself; and she was an importation. Of the three classes of village, the village cared for by its lord, the village cared for by itself, and the village uncared for either by itself or by its lord (in other words, the village of a resident squire’s tenantry, the village of free or copy-holders, and the absentee-owner’s village, farmed with the land) this place, Flintcomb-Ash, was the third.\n\nBut Tess set to work. Patience, that blending of moral courage with physical timidity, was now no longer a minor feature in Mrs Angel Clare; and it sustained her.\n\nThe swede-field in which she and her companion were set hacking was a stretch of a hundred odd acres in one patch, on the highest ground of the farm, rising above stony lanchets or lynchets—the outcrop of siliceous veins in the chalk formation, composed of myriads of loose white flints in bulbous, cusped, and phallic shapes. The upper half of each turnip had been eaten off by the live-stock, and it was the business of the two women to grub up the lower or earthy half of the root with a hooked fork called a hacker, that it might be eaten also. Every leaf of the vegetable having already been consumed, the whole field was in colour a desolate drab; it was a complexion without features, as if a face, from chin to brow, should be only an expanse of skin. The sky wore, in another colour, the same likeness; a white vacuity of countenance with the lineaments gone. So these two upper and nether visages confronted each other all day long, the white face looking down on the brown face, and the brown face looking up at the white face, without anything standing between them but the two girls crawling over the surface of the former like flies.\n\nNobody came near them, and their movements showed a mechanical regularity; their forms standing enshrouded in Hessian “wroppers”—sleeved brown pinafores, tied behind to the bottom, to keep their gowns from blowing about—scant skirts revealing boots that reached high up the ankles, and yellow sheepskin gloves with gauntlets. The pensive character which the curtained hood lent to their bent heads would have reminded the observer of some early Italian conception of the two Marys.\n\nThey worked on hour after hour, unconscious of the forlorn aspect they bore in the landscape, not thinking of the justice or injustice of their lot. Even in such a position as theirs it was possible to exist in a dream. In the afternoon the rain came on again, and Marian said that they need not work any more. But if they did not work they would not be paid; so they worked on. It was so high a situation, this field, that the rain had no occasion to fall, but raced along horizontally upon the yelling wind, sticking into them like glass splinters till they were wet through. Tess had not known till now what was really meant by that. There are degrees of dampness, and a very little is called being wet through in common talk. But to stand working slowly in a field, and feel the creep of rain-water, first in legs and shoulders, then on hips and head, then at back, front, and sides, and yet to work on till the leaden light diminishes and marks that the sun is down, demands a distinct modicum of stoicism, even of valour.\n\nYet they did not feel the wetness so much as might be supposed. They were both young, and they were talking of the time when they lived and loved together at Talbothays Dairy, that happy green tract of land where summer had been liberal in her gifts; in substance to all, emotionally to these. Tess would fain not have conversed with Marian of the man who was legally, if not actually, her husband; but the irresistible fascination of the subject betrayed her into reciprocating Marian’s remarks. And thus, as has been said, though the damp curtains of their bonnets flapped smartly into their faces, and their wrappers clung about them to wearisomeness, they lived all this afternoon in memories of green, sunny, romantic Talbothays.\n\n“You can see a gleam of a hill within a few miles o’ Froom Valley from here when ’tis fine,” said Marian.\n\n“Ah! Can you?” said Tess, awake to the new value of this locality.\n\nSo the two forces were at work here as everywhere, the inherent will to enjoy, and the circumstantial will against enjoyment. Marian’s will had a method of assisting itself by taking from her pocket as the afternoon wore on a pint bottle corked with white rag, from which she invited Tess to drink. Tess’s unassisted power of dreaming, however, being enough for her sublimation at present, she declined except the merest sip, and then Marian took a pull from the spirits.\n\n“I’ve got used to it,” she said, “and can’t leave it off now. ’Tis my only comfort—You see I lost him: you didn’t; and you can do without it perhaps.”\n\nTess thought her loss as great as Marian’s, but upheld by the dignity of being Angel’s wife, in the letter at least, she accepted Marian’s differentiation.\n\nAmid this scene Tess slaved in the morning frosts and in the afternoon rains. When it was not swede-grubbing it was swede-trimming, in which process they sliced off the earth and the fibres with a bill-hook before storing the roots for future use. At this occupation they could shelter themselves by a thatched hurdle if it rained; but if it was frosty even their thick leather gloves could not prevent the frozen masses they handled from biting their fingers. Still Tess hoped. She had a conviction that sooner or later the magnanimity which she persisted in reckoning as a chief ingredient of Clare’s character would lead him to rejoin her.\n\nMarian, primed to a humorous mood, would discover the queer-shaped flints aforesaid, and shriek with laughter, Tess remaining severely obtuse. They often looked across the country to where the Var or Froom was known to stretch, even though they might not be able to see it; and, fixing their eyes on the cloaking gray mist, imagined the old times they had spent out there.\n\n“Ah,” said Marian, “how I should like another or two of our old set to come here! Then we could bring up Talbothays every day here afield, and talk of he, and of what nice times we had there, and o’ the old things we used to know, and make it all come back a’most, in seeming!” Marian’s eyes softened, and her voice grew vague as the visions returned. “I’ll write to Izz Huett,” she said. “She’s biding at home doing nothing now, I know, and I’ll tell her we be here, and ask her to come; and perhaps Retty is well enough now.”\n\nTess had nothing to say against the proposal, and the next she heard of this plan for importing old Talbothays’ joys was two or three days later, when Marian informed her that Izz had replied to her inquiry, and had promised to come if she could.\n\nThere had not been such a winter for years. It came on in stealthy and measured glides, like the moves of a chess-player. One morning the few lonely trees and the thorns of the hedgerows appeared as if they had put off a vegetable for an animal integument. Every twig was covered with a white nap as of fur grown from the rind during the night, giving it four times its usual stoutness; the whole bush or tree forming a staring sketch in white lines on the mournful gray of the sky and horizon. Cobwebs revealed their presence on sheds and walls where none had ever been observed till brought out into visibility by the crystallizing atmosphere, hanging like loops of white worsted from salient points of the out-houses, posts, and gates.\n\nAfter this season of congealed dampness came a spell of dry frost, when strange birds from behind the North Pole began to arrive silently on the upland of Flintcomb-Ash; gaunt spectral creatures with tragical eyes—eyes which had witnessed scenes of cataclysmal horror in inaccessible polar regions of a magnitude such as no human being had ever conceived, in curdling temperatures that no man could endure; which had beheld the crash of icebergs and the slide of snow-hills by the shooting light of the Aurora; been half blinded by the whirl of colossal storms and terraqueous distortions; and retained the expression of feature that such scenes had engendered. These nameless birds came quite near to Tess and Marian, but of all they had seen which humanity would never see, they brought no account. The traveller’s ambition to tell was not theirs, and, with dumb impassivity, they dismissed experiences which they did not value for the immediate incidents of this homely upland—the trivial movements of the two girls in disturbing the clods with their hackers so as to uncover something or other that these visitants relished as food.\n\nThen one day a peculiar quality invaded the air of this open country. There came a moisture which was not of rain, and a cold which was not of frost. It chilled the eyeballs of the twain, made their brows ache, penetrated to their skeletons, affecting the surface of the body less than its core. They knew that it meant snow, and in the night the snow came. Tess, who continued to live at the cottage with the warm gable that cheered any lonely pedestrian who paused beside it, awoke in the night, and heard above the thatch noises which seemed to signify that the roof had turned itself into a gymnasium of all the winds. When she lit her lamp to get up in the morning she found that the snow had blown through a chink in the casement, forming a white cone of the finest powder against the inside, and had also come down the chimney, so that it lay sole-deep upon the floor, on which her shoes left tracks when she moved about. Without, the storm drove so fast as to create a snow-mist in the kitchen; but as yet it was too dark out-of-doors to see anything.\n\nTess knew that it was impossible to go on with the swedes; and by the time she had finished breakfast beside the solitary little lamp, Marian arrived to tell her that they were to join the rest of the women at reed-drawing in the barn till the weather changed. As soon, therefore, as the uniform cloak of darkness without began to turn to a disordered medley of grays, they blew out the lamp, wrapped themselves up in their thickest pinners, tied their woollen cravats round their necks and across their chests, and started for the barn. The snow had followed the birds from the polar basin as a white pillar of a cloud, and individual flakes could not be seen. The blast smelt of icebergs, arctic seas, whales, and white bears, carrying the snow so that it licked the land but did not deepen on it. They trudged onwards with slanted bodies through the flossy fields, keeping as well as they could in the shelter of hedges, which, however, acted as strainers rather than screens. The air, afflicted to pallor with the hoary multitudes that infested it, twisted and spun them eccentrically, suggesting an achromatic chaos of things. But both the young women were fairly cheerful; such weather on a dry upland is not in itself dispiriting.\n\n“Ha-ha! the cunning northern birds knew this was coming,” said Marian. “Depend upon’t, they keep just in front o’t all the way from the North Star. Your husband, my dear, is, I make no doubt, having scorching weather all this time. Lord, if he could only see his pretty wife now! Not that this weather hurts your beauty at all—in fact, it rather does it good.”\n\n“You mustn’t talk about him to me, Marian,” said Tess severely.\n\n“Well, but—surely you care for ’n! Do you?”\n\nInstead of answering, Tess, with tears in her eyes, impulsively faced in the direction in which she imagined South America to lie, and, putting up her lips, blew out a passionate kiss upon the snowy wind.\n\n“Well, well, I know you do. But ’pon my body, it is a rum life for a married couple! There—I won’t say another word! Well, as for the weather, it won’t hurt us in the wheat-barn; but reed-drawing is fearful hard work—worse than swede-hacking. I can stand it because I’m stout; but you be slimmer than I. I can’t think why maister should have set ’ee at it.”\n\nThey reached the wheat-barn and entered it. One end of the long structure was full of corn; the middle was where the reed-drawing was carried on, and there had already been placed in the reed-press the evening before as many sheaves of wheat as would be sufficient for the women to draw from during the day.\n\n“Why, here’s Izz!” said Marian.\n\nIzz it was, and she came forward. She had walked all the way from her mother’s home on the previous afternoon, and, not deeming the distance so great, had been belated, arriving, however, just before the snow began, and sleeping at the alehouse. The farmer had agreed with her mother at market to take her on if she came to-day, and she had been afraid to disappoint him by delay.\n\nIn addition to Tess, Marian, and Izz, there were two women from a neighbouring village; two Amazonian sisters, whom Tess with a start remembered as Dark Car, the Queen of Spades, and her junior, the Queen of Diamonds—those who had tried to fight with her in the midnight quarrel at Trantridge. They showed no recognition of her, and possibly had none, for they had been under the influence of liquor on that occasion, and were only temporary sojourners there as here. They did all kinds of men’s work by preference, including well-sinking, hedging, ditching, and excavating, without any sense of fatigue. Noted reed-drawers were they too, and looked round upon the other three with some superciliousness.\n\nPutting on their gloves, all set to work in a row in front of the press, an erection formed of two posts connected by a cross-beam, under which the sheaves to be drawn from were laid ears outward, the beam being pegged down by pins in the uprights, and lowered as the sheaves diminished.\n\nThe day hardened in colour, the light coming in at the barndoors upwards from the snow instead of downwards from the sky. The girls pulled handful after handful from the press; but by reason of the presence of the strange women, who were recounting scandals, Marian and Izz could not at first talk of old times as they wished to do. Presently they heard the muffled tread of a horse, and the farmer rode up to the barndoor. When he had dismounted he came close to Tess, and remained looking musingly at the side of her face. She had not turned at first, but his fixed attitude led her to look round, when she perceived that her employer was the native of Trantridge from whom she had taken flight on the high-road because of his allusion to her history.\n\nHe waited till she had carried the drawn bundles to the pile outside, when he said, “So you be the young woman who took my civility in such ill part? Be drowned if I didn’t think you might be as soon as I heard of your being hired! Well, you thought you had got the better of me the first time at the inn with your fancy-man, and the second time on the road, when you bolted; but now I think I’ve got the better of you.” He concluded with a hard laugh.\n\nTess, between the Amazons and the farmer, like a bird caught in a clap-net, returned no answer, continuing to pull the straw. She could read character sufficiently well to know by this time that she had nothing to fear from her employer’s gallantry; it was rather the tyranny induced by his mortification at Clare’s treatment of him. Upon the whole she preferred that sentiment in man and felt brave enough to endure it.\n\n“You thought I was in love with ’ee I suppose? Some women are such fools, to take every look as serious earnest. But there’s nothing like a winter afield for taking that nonsense out o’ young wenches’ heads; and you’ve signed and agreed till Lady-Day. Now, are you going to beg my pardon?”\n\n“I think you ought to beg mine.”\n\n“Very well—as you like. But we’ll see which is master here. Be they all the sheaves you’ve done to-day?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“’Tis a very poor show. Just see what they’ve done over there” (pointing to the two stalwart women). “The rest, too, have done better than you.”\n\n“They’ve all practised it before, and I have not. And I thought it made no difference to you as it is task work, and we are only paid for what we do.”\n\n“Oh, but it does. I want the barn cleared.”\n\n“I am going to work all the afternoon instead of leaving at two as the others will do.”\n\nHe looked sullenly at her and went away. Tess felt that she could not have come to a much worse place; but anything was better than gallantry. When two o’clock arrived the professional reed-drawers tossed off the last half-pint in their flagon, put down their hooks, tied their last sheaves, and went away. Marian and Izz would have done likewise, but on hearing that Tess meant to stay, to make up by longer hours for her lack of skill, they would not leave her. Looking out at the snow, which still fell, Marian exclaimed, “Now, we’ve got it all to ourselves.” And so at last the conversation turned to their old experiences at the dairy; and, of course, the incidents of their affection for Angel Clare.\n\n“Izz and Marian,” said Mrs Angel Clare, with a dignity which was extremely touching, seeing how very little of a wife she was: “I can’t join in talk with you now, as I used to do, about Mr Clare; you will see that I cannot; because, although he is gone away from me for the present, he is my husband.”\n\nIzz was by nature the sauciest and most caustic of all the four girls who had loved Clare. “He was a very splendid lover, no doubt,” she said; “but I don’t think he is a too fond husband to go away from you so soon.”\n\n“He had to go—he was obliged to go, to see about the land over there!” pleaded Tess.\n\n“He might have tided ’ee over the winter.”\n\n“Ah—that’s owing to an accident—a misunderstanding; and we won’t argue it,” Tess answered, with tearfulness in her words. “Perhaps there’s a good deal to be said for him! He did not go away, like some husbands, without telling me; and I can always find out where he is.”\n\nAfter this they continued for some long time in a reverie, as they went on seizing the ears of corn, drawing out the straw, gathering it under their arms, and cutting off the ears with their bill-hooks, nothing sounding in the barn but the swish of the straw and the crunch of the hook. Then Tess suddenly flagged, and sank down upon the heap of wheat-ears at her feet.\n\n“I knew you wouldn’t be able to stand it!” cried Marian. “It wants harder flesh than yours for this work.”\n\nJust then the farmer entered. “Oh, that’s how you get on when I am away,” he said to her.\n\n“But it is my own loss,” she pleaded. “Not yours.”\n\n“I want it finished,” he said doggedly, as he crossed the barn and went out at the other door.\n\n“Don’t ’ee mind him, there’s a dear,” said Marian. “I’ve worked here before. Now you go and lie down there, and Izz and I will make up your number.”\n\n“I don’t like to let you do that. I’m taller than you, too.”\n\nHowever, she was so overcome that she consented to lie down awhile, and reclined on a heap of pull-tails—the refuse after the straight straw had been drawn—thrown up at the further side of the barn. Her succumbing had been as largely owning to agitation at the re-opening the subject of her separation from her husband as to the hard work. She lay in a state of percipience without volition, and the rustle of the straw and the cutting of the ears by the others had the weight of bodily touches.\n\nShe could hear from her corner, in addition to these noises, the murmur of their voices. She felt certain that they were continuing the subject already broached, but their voices were so low that she could not catch the words. At last Tess grew more and more anxious to know what they were saying, and, persuading herself that she felt better, she got up and resumed work.\n\nThen Izz Huett broke down. She had walked more than a dozen miles the previous evening, had gone to bed at midnight, and had risen again at five o’clock. Marian alone, thanks to her bottle of liquor and her stoutness of build, stood the strain upon back and arms without suffering. Tess urged Izz to leave off, agreeing, as she felt better, to finish the day without her, and make equal division of the number of sheaves.\n\nIzz accepted the offer gratefully, and disappeared through the great door into the snowy track to her lodging. Marian, as was the case every afternoon at this time on account of the bottle, began to feel in a romantic vein.\n\n“I should not have thought it of him—never!” she said in a dreamy tone. “And I loved him so! I didn’t mind his having _you_. But this about Izz is too bad!”\n\nTess, in her start at the words, narrowly missed cutting off a finger with the bill-hook.\n\n“Is it about my husband?” she stammered.\n\n“Well, yes. Izz said, ‘Don’t ’ee tell her’; but I am sure I can’t help it! It was what he wanted Izz to do. He wanted her to go off to Brazil with him.”\n\nTess’s face faded as white as the scene without, and its curves straightened. “And did Izz refuse to go?” she asked.\n\n“I don’t know. Anyhow he changed his mind.”\n\n“Pooh—then he didn’t mean it! ’Twas just a man’s jest!”\n\n“Yes he did; for he drove her a good-ways towards the station.”\n\n“He didn’t take her!”\n\nThey pulled on in silence till Tess, without any premonitory symptoms, burst out crying.\n\n“There!” said Marian. “Now I wish I hadn’t told ’ee!”\n\n“No. It is a very good thing that you have done! I have been living on in a thirtover, lackaday way, and have not seen what it may lead to! I ought to have sent him a letter oftener. He said I could not go to him, but he didn’t say I was not to write as often as I liked. I won’t dally like this any longer! I have been very wrong and neglectful in leaving everything to be done by him!”\n\nThe dim light in the barn grew dimmer, and they could see to work no longer. When Tess had reached home that evening, and had entered into the privacy of her little white-washed chamber, she began impetuously writing a letter to Clare. But falling into doubt, she could not finish it. Afterwards she took the ring from the ribbon on which she wore it next her heart, and retained it on her finger all night, as if to fortify herself in the sensation that she was really the wife of this elusive lover of hers, who could propose that Izz should go with him abroad, so shortly after he had left her. Knowing that, how could she write entreaties to him, or show that she cared for him any more?\n\n# XLIV\n\nBy the disclosure in the barn her thoughts were led anew in the direction which they had taken more than once of late—to the distant Emminster Vicarage. It was through her husband’s parents that she had been charged to send a letter to Clare if she desired; and to write to them direct if in difficulty. But that sense of her having morally no claim upon him had always led Tess to suspend her impulse to send these notes; and to the family at the Vicarage, therefore, as to her own parents since her marriage, she was virtually non-existent. This self-effacement in both directions had been quite in consonance with her independent character of desiring nothing by way of favour or pity to which she was not entitled on a fair consideration of her deserts. She had set herself to stand or fall by her qualities, and to waive such merely technical claims upon a strange family as had been established for her by the flimsy fact of a member of that family, in a season of impulse, writing his name in a church-book beside hers.\n\nBut now that she was stung to a fever by Izz’s tale, there was a limit to her powers of renunciation. Why had her husband not written to her? He had distinctly implied that he would at least let her know of the locality to which he had journeyed; but he had not sent a line to notify his address. Was he really indifferent? But was he ill? Was it for her to make some advance? Surely she might summon the courage of solicitude, call at the Vicarage for intelligence, and express her grief at his silence. If Angel’s father were the good man she had heard him represented to be, he would be able to enter into her heart-starved situation. Her social hardships she could conceal.\n\nTo leave the farm on a week-day was not in her power; Sunday was the only possible opportunity. Flintcomb-Ash being in the middle of the cretaceous tableland over which no railway had climbed as yet, it would be necessary to walk. And the distance being fifteen miles each way she would have to allow herself a long day for the undertaking by rising early.\n\nA fortnight later, when the snow had gone, and had been followed by a hard black frost, she took advantage of the state of the roads to try the experiment. At four o’clock that Sunday morning she came downstairs and stepped out into the starlight. The weather was still favourable, the ground ringing under her feet like an anvil.\n\nMarian and Izz were much interested in her excursion, knowing that the journey concerned her husband. Their lodgings were in a cottage a little further along the lane, but they came and assisted Tess in her departure, and argued that she should dress up in her very prettiest guise to captivate the hearts of her parents-in-law; though she, knowing of the austere and Calvinistic tenets of old Mr Clare, was indifferent, and even doubtful. A year had now elapsed since her sad marriage, but she had preserved sufficient draperies from the wreck of her then full wardrobe to clothe her very charmingly as a simple country girl with no pretensions to recent fashion; a soft gray woollen gown, with white crape quilling against the pink skin of her face and neck, and a black velvet jacket and hat.\n\n“’Tis a thousand pities your husband can’t see ’ee now—you do look a real beauty!” said Izz Huett, regarding Tess as she stood on the threshold between the steely starlight without and the yellow candlelight within. Izz spoke with a magnanimous abandonment of herself to the situation; she could not be—no woman with a heart bigger than a hazel-nut could be—antagonistic to Tess in her presence, the influence which she exercised over those of her own sex being of a warmth and strength quite unusual, curiously overpowering the less worthy feminine feelings of spite and rivalry.\n\nWith a final tug and touch here, and a slight brush there, they let her go; and she was absorbed into the pearly air of the fore-dawn. They heard her footsteps tap along the hard road as she stepped out to her full pace. Even Izz hoped she would win, and, though without any particular respect for her own virtue, felt glad that she had been prevented wronging her friend when momentarily tempted by Clare.\n\nIt was a year ago, all but a day, that Clare had married Tess, and only a few days less than a year that he had been absent from her. Still, to start on a brisk walk, and on such an errand as hers, on a dry clear wintry morning, through the rarefied air of these chalky hogs’-backs, was not depressing; and there is no doubt that her dream at starting was to win the heart of her mother-in-law, tell her whole history to that lady, enlist her on her side, and so gain back the truant.\n\nIn time she reached the edge of the vast escarpment below which stretched the loamy Vale of Blackmoor, now lying misty and still in the dawn. Instead of the colourless air of the uplands, the atmosphere down there was a deep blue. Instead of the great enclosures of a hundred acres in which she was now accustomed to toil, there were little fields below her of less than half-a-dozen acres, so numerous that they looked from this height like the meshes of a net. Here the landscape was whitey-brown; down there, as in Froom Valley, it was always green. Yet it was in that vale that her sorrow had taken shape, and she did not love it as formerly. Beauty to her, as to all who have felt, lay not in the thing, but in what the thing symbolized.\n\nKeeping the Vale on her right, she steered steadily westward; passing above the Hintocks, crossing at right-angles the high-road from Sherton-Abbas to Casterbridge, and skirting Dogbury Hill and High-Stoy, with the dell between them called “The Devil’s Kitchen”. Still following the elevated way she reached Cross-in-Hand, where the stone pillar stands desolate and silent, to mark the site of a miracle, or murder, or both. Three miles further she cut across the straight and deserted Roman road called Long-Ash Lane; leaving which as soon as she reached it she dipped down a hill by a transverse lane into the small town or village of Evershead, being now about halfway over the distance. She made a halt here, and breakfasted a second time, heartily enough—not at the Sow-and-Acorn, for she avoided inns, but at a cottage by the church.\n\nThe second half of her journey was through a more gentle country, by way of Benvill Lane. But as the mileage lessened between her and the spot of her pilgrimage, so did Tess’s confidence decrease, and her enterprise loom out more formidably. She saw her purpose in such staring lines, and the landscape so faintly, that she was sometimes in danger of losing her way. However, about noon she paused by a gate on the edge of the basin in which Emminster and its Vicarage lay.\n\nThe square tower, beneath which she knew that at that moment the Vicar and his congregation were gathered, had a severe look in her eyes. She wished that she had somehow contrived to come on a week-day. Such a good man might be prejudiced against a woman who had chosen Sunday, never realizing the necessities of her case. But it was incumbent upon her to go on now. She took off the thick boots in which she had walked thus far, put on her pretty thin ones of patent leather, and, stuffing the former into the hedge by the gatepost where she might readily find them again, descended the hill; the freshness of colour she had derived from the keen air thinning away in spite of her as she drew near the parsonage.\n\nTess hoped for some accident that might favour her, but nothing favoured her. The shrubs on the Vicarage lawn rustled uncomfortably in the frosty breeze; she could not feel by any stretch of imagination, dressed to her highest as she was, that the house was the residence of near relations; and yet nothing essential, in nature or emotion, divided her from them: in pains, pleasures, thoughts, birth, death, and after-death, they were the same.\n\nShe nerved herself by an effort, entered the swing-gate, and rang the door-bell. The thing was done; there could be no retreat. No; the thing was not done. Nobody answered to her ringing. The effort had to be risen to and made again. She rang a second time, and the agitation of the act, coupled with her weariness after the fifteen miles’ walk, led her to support herself while she waited by resting her hand on her hip and her elbow against the wall of the porch. The wind was so nipping that the ivy-leaves had become wizened and gray, each tapping incessantly upon its neighbour with a disquieting stir of her nerves. A piece of blood-stained paper, caught up from some meat-buyer’s dust-heap, beat up and down the road without the gate; too flimsy to rest, too heavy to fly away; and a few straws kept it company.\n\nThe second peal had been louder, and still nobody came. Then she walked out of the porch, opened the gate, and passed through. And though she looked dubiously at the house-front as if inclined to return, it was with a breath of relief that she closed the gate. A feeling haunted her that she might have been recognized (though how she could not tell), and orders been given not to admit her.\n\nTess went as far as the corner. She had done all she could do; but determined not to escape present trepidation at the expense of future distress, she walked back again quite past the house, looking up at all the windows.\n\nAh—the explanation was that they were all at church, every one. She remembered her husband saying that his father always insisted upon the household, servants included, going to morning-service, and, as a consequence, eating cold food when they came home. It was, therefore, only necessary to wait till the service was over. She would not make herself conspicuous by waiting on the spot, and she started to get past the church into the lane. But as she reached the churchyard-gate the people began pouring out, and Tess found herself in the midst of them.\n\nThe Emminster congregation looked at her as only a congregation of small country-townsfolk walking home at its leisure can look at a woman out of the common whom it perceives to be a stranger. She quickened her pace, and ascended the road by which she had come, to find a retreat between its hedges till the Vicar’s family should have lunched, and it might be convenient for them to receive her. She soon distanced the churchgoers, except two youngish men, who, linked arm-in-arm, were beating up behind her at a quick step.\n\nAs they drew nearer she could hear their voices engaged in earnest discourse, and, with the natural quickness of a woman in her situation, did not fail to recognize in those noises the quality of her husband’s tones. The pedestrians were his two brothers. Forgetting all her plans, Tess’s one dread was lest they should overtake her now, in her disorganized condition, before she was prepared to confront them; for though she felt that they could not identify her, she instinctively dreaded their scrutiny. The more briskly they walked, the more briskly walked she. They were plainly bent upon taking a short quick stroll before going indoors to lunch or dinner, to restore warmth to limbs chilled with sitting through a long service.\n\nOnly one person had preceded Tess up the hill—a ladylike young woman, somewhat interesting, though, perhaps, a trifle _guindée_ and prudish. Tess had nearly overtaken her when the speed of her brothers-in-law brought them so nearly behind her back that she could hear every word of their conversation. They said nothing, however, which particularly interested her till, observing the young lady still further in front, one of them remarked, “There is Mercy Chant. Let us overtake her.”\n\nTess knew the name. It was the woman who had been destined for Angel’s life-companion by his and her parents, and whom he probably would have married but for her intrusive self. She would have known as much without previous information if she had waited a moment, for one of the brothers proceeded to say: “Ah! poor Angel, poor Angel! I never see that nice girl without more and more regretting his precipitancy in throwing himself away upon a dairymaid, or whatever she may be. It is a queer business, apparently. Whether she has joined him yet or not I don’t know; but she had not done so some months ago when I heard from him.”\n\n“I can’t say. He never tells me anything nowadays. His ill-considered marriage seems to have completed that estrangement from me which was begun by his extraordinary opinions.”\n\nTess beat up the long hill still faster; but she could not outwalk them without exciting notice. At last they outsped her altogether, and passed her by. The young lady still further ahead heard their footsteps and turned. Then there was a greeting and a shaking of hands, and the three went on together.\n\nThey soon reached the summit of the hill, and, evidently intending this point to be the limit of their promenade, slackened pace and turned all three aside to the gate whereat Tess had paused an hour before that time to reconnoitre the town before descending into it. During their discourse one of the clerical brothers probed the hedge carefully with his umbrella, and dragged something to light.\n\n“Here’s a pair of old boots,” he said. “Thrown away, I suppose, by some tramp or other.”\n\n“Some imposter who wished to come into the town barefoot, perhaps, and so excite our sympathies,” said Miss Chant. “Yes, it must have been, for they are excellent walking-boots—by no means worn out. What a wicked thing to do! I’ll carry them home for some poor person.”\n\nCuthbert Clare, who had been the one to find them, picked them up for her with the crook of his stick; and Tess’s boots were appropriated.\nShe, who had heard this, walked past under the screen of her woollen veil till, presently looking back, she perceived that the church party had left the gate with her boots and retreated down the hill.\n\nThereupon our heroine resumed her walk. Tears, blinding tears, were running down her face. She knew that it was all sentiment, all baseless impressibility, which had caused her to read the scene as her own condemnation; nevertheless she could not get over it; she could not contravene in her own defenceless person all those untoward omens. It was impossible to think of returning to the Vicarage. Angel’s wife felt almost as if she had been hounded up that hill like a scorned thing by those—to her—superfine clerics. Innocently as the slight had been inflicted, it was somewhat unfortunate that she had encountered the sons and not the father, who, despite his narrowness, was far less starched and ironed than they, and had to the full the gift of charity. As she again thought of her dusty boots she almost pitied those habiliments for the quizzing to which they had been subjected, and felt how hopeless life was for their owner.\n\n“Ah!” she said, still sighing in pity of herself, “_they_ didn’t know that I wore those over the roughest part of the road to save these pretty ones _he_ bought for me—no—they did not know it! And they didn’t think that _he_ chose the colour o’ my pretty frock—no—how could they? If they had known perhaps they would not have cared, for they don’t care much for him, poor thing!”\n\nThen she grieved for the beloved man whose conventional standard of judgement had caused her all these latter sorrows; and she went her way without knowing that the greatest misfortune of her life was this feminine loss of courage at the last and critical moment through her estimating her father-in-law by his sons. Her present condition was precisely one which would have enlisted the sympathies of old Mr and Mrs Clare. Their hearts went out of them at a bound towards extreme cases, when the subtle mental troubles of the less desperate among mankind failed to win their interest or regard. In jumping at Publicans and Sinners they would forget that a word might be said for the worries of Scribes and Pharisees; and this defect or limitation might have recommended their own daughter-in-law to them at this moment as a fairly choice sort of lost person for their love.\n\nThereupon she began to plod back along the road by which she had come not altogether full of hope, but full of a conviction that a crisis in her life was approaching. No crisis, apparently, had supervened; and there was nothing left for her to do but to continue upon that starve-acre farm till she could again summon courage to face the Vicarage. She did, indeed, take sufficient interest in herself to throw up her veil on this return journey, as if to let the world see that she could at least exhibit a face such as Mercy Chant could not show. But it was done with a sorry shake of the head. “It is nothing—it is nothing!” she said. “Nobody loves it; nobody sees it. Who cares about the looks of a castaway like me!”\n\nHer journey back was rather a meander than a march. It had no sprightliness, no purpose; only a tendency. Along the tedious length of Benvill Lane she began to grow tired, and she leant upon gates and paused by milestones.\n\nShe did not enter any house till, at the seventh or eighth mile, she descended the steep long hill below which lay the village or townlet of Evershead, where in the morning she had breakfasted with such contrasting expectations. The cottage by the church, in which she again sat down, was almost the first at that end of the village, and while the woman fetched her some milk from the pantry, Tess, looking down the street, perceived that the place seemed quite deserted.\n\n“The people are gone to afternoon service, I suppose?” she said.\n\n“No, my dear,” said the old woman. “’Tis too soon for that; the bells hain’t strook out yet. They be all gone to hear the preaching in yonder barn. A ranter preaches there between the services—an excellent, fiery, Christian man, they say. But, Lord, I don’t go to hear’n! What comes in the regular way over the pulpit is hot enough for I.”\n\nTess soon went onward into the village, her footsteps echoing against the houses as though it were a place of the dead. Nearing the central part, her echoes were intruded on by other sounds; and seeing the barn not far off the road, she guessed these to be the utterances of the preacher.\n\nHis voice became so distinct in the still clear air that she could soon catch his sentences, though she was on the closed side of the barn. The sermon, as might be expected, was of the extremest antinomian type; on justification by faith, as expounded in the theology of St Paul. This fixed idea of the rhapsodist was delivered with animated enthusiasm, in a manner entirely declamatory, for he had plainly no skill as a dialectician. Although Tess had not heard the beginning of the address, she learnt what the text had been from its constant iteration—\n\n“O foolish Galatians, who hath bewitched you, that ye should not obey the truth, before whose eyes Jesus Christ hath been evidently set forth, crucified among you?”\n\nTess was all the more interested, as she stood listening behind, in finding that the preacher’s doctrine was a vehement form of the view of Angel’s father, and her interest intensified when the speaker began to detail his own spiritual experiences of how he had come by those views. He had, he said, been the greatest of sinners. He had scoffed; he had wantonly associated with the reckless and the lewd. But a day of awakening had come, and, in a human sense, it had been brought about mainly by the influence of a certain clergyman, whom he had at first grossly insulted; but whose parting words had sunk into his heart, and had remained there, till by the grace of Heaven they had worked this change in him, and made him what they saw him.\n\nBut more startling to Tess than the doctrine had been the voice, which, impossible as it seemed, was precisely that of Alec d’Urberville. Her face fixed in painful suspense, she came round to the front of the barn, and passed before it. The low winter sun beamed directly upon the great double-doored entrance on this side; one of the doors being open, so that the rays stretched far in over the threshing-floor to the preacher and his audience, all snugly sheltered from the northern breeze. The listeners were entirely villagers, among them being the man whom she had seen carrying the red paint-pot on a former memorable occasion. But her attention was given to the central figure, who stood upon some sacks of corn, facing the people and the door. The three o’clock sun shone full upon him, and the strange enervating conviction that her seducer confronted her, which had been gaining ground in Tess ever since she had heard his words distinctly, was at last established as a fact indeed.\nEnd of Phase the Fifth\n\nThe Convert\n\n# XLV\n\nTill this moment she had never seen or heard from d’Urberville since her departure from Trantridge.\n\nThe rencounter came at a heavy moment, one of all moments calculated to permit its impact with the least emotional shock. But such was unreasoning memory that, though he stood there openly and palpably a converted man, who was sorrowing for his past irregularities, a fear overcame her, paralyzing her movement so that she neither retreated nor advanced.\n\nTo think of what emanated from that countenance when she saw it last, and to behold it now!... There was the same handsome unpleasantness of mien, but now he wore neatly trimmed, old-fashioned whiskers, the sable moustache having disappeared; and his dress was half-clerical, a modification which had changed his expression sufficiently to abstract the dandyism from his features, and to hinder for a second her belief in his identity.\n\nTo Tess’s sense there was, just at first, a ghastly _bizarrerie_, a grim incongruity, in the march of these solemn words of Scripture out of such a mouth. This too familiar intonation, less than four years earlier, had brought to her ears expressions of such divergent purpose that her heart became quite sick at the irony of the contrast.\n\nIt was less a reform than a transfiguration. The former curves of sensuousness were now modulated to lines of devotional passion. The lip-shapes that had meant seductiveness were now made to express supplication; the glow on the cheek that yesterday could be translated as riotousness was evangelized to-day into the splendour of pious rhetoric; animalism had become fanaticism; Paganism, Paulinism; the bold rolling eye that had flashed upon her form in the old time with such mastery now beamed with the rude energy of a theolatry that was almost ferocious. Those black angularities which his face had used to put on when his wishes were thwarted now did duty in picturing the incorrigible backslider who would insist upon turning again to his wallowing in the mire.\n\nThe lineaments, as such, seemed to complain. They had been diverted from their hereditary connotation to signify impressions for which Nature did not intend them. Strange that their very elevation was a misapplication, that to raise seemed to falsify.\n\nYet could it be so? She would admit the ungenerous sentiment no longer. D’Urberville was not the first wicked man who had turned away from his wickedness to save his soul alive, and why should she deem it unnatural in him? It was but the usage of thought which had been jarred in her at hearing good new words in bad old notes. The greater the sinner, the greater the saint; it was not necessary to dive far into Christian history to discover that.\n\nSuch impressions as these moved her vaguely, and without strict definiteness. As soon as the nerveless pause of her surprise would allow her to stir, her impulse was to pass on out of his sight. He had obviously not discerned her yet in her position against the sun.\n\nBut the moment that she moved again he recognized her. The effect upon her old lover was electric, far stronger than the effect of his presence upon her. His fire, the tumultuous ring of his eloquence, seemed to go out of him. His lip struggled and trembled under the words that lay upon it; but deliver them it could not as long as she faced him. His eyes, after their first glance upon her face, hung confusedly in every other direction but hers, but came back in a desperate leap every few seconds. This paralysis lasted, however, but a short time; for Tess’s energies returned with the atrophy of his, and she walked as fast as she was able past the barn and onward.\n\nAs soon as she could reflect, it appalled her, this change in their relative platforms. He who had wrought her undoing was now on the side of the Spirit, while she remained unregenerate. And, as in the legend, it had resulted that her Cyprian image had suddenly appeared upon his altar, whereby the fire of the priest had been well nigh extinguished.\n\nShe went on without turning her head. Her back seemed to be endowed with a sensitiveness to ocular beams—even her clothing—so alive was she to a fancied gaze which might be resting upon her from the outside of that barn. All the way along to this point her heart had been heavy with an inactive sorrow; now there was a change in the quality of its trouble. That hunger for affection too long withheld was for the time displaced by an almost physical sense of an implacable past which still engirdled her. It intensified her consciousness of error to a practical despair; the break of continuity between her earlier and present existence, which she had hoped for, had not, after all, taken place. Bygones would never be complete bygones till she was a bygone herself.\n\nThus absorbed, she recrossed the northern part of Long-Ash Lane at right angles, and presently saw before her the road ascending whitely to the upland along whose margin the remainder of her journey lay. Its dry pale surface stretched severely onward, unbroken by a single figure, vehicle, or mark, save some occasional brown horse-droppings which dotted its cold aridity here and there. While slowly breasting this ascent Tess became conscious of footsteps behind her, and turning she saw approaching that well-known form—so strangely accoutred as the Methodist—the one personage in all the world she wished not to encounter alone on this side of the grave.\n\nThere was not much time, however, for thought or elusion, and she yielded as calmly as she could to the necessity of letting him overtake her. She saw that he was excited, less by the speed of his walk than by the feelings within him.\n\n“Tess!” he said.\n\nShe slackened speed without looking round.\n\n“Tess!” he repeated. “It is I—Alec d’Urberville.”\n\nShe then looked back at him, and he came up.\n\n“I see it is,” she answered coldly.\n\n“Well—is that all? Yet I deserve no more! Of course,” he added, with a slight laugh, “there is something of the ridiculous to your eyes in seeing me like this. But—I must put up with that.... I heard you had gone away; nobody knew where. Tess, you wonder why I have followed you?”\n\n“I do, rather; and I would that you had not, with all my heart!”\n\n“Yes—you may well say it,” he returned grimly, as they moved onward together, she with unwilling tread. “But don’t mistake me; I beg this because you may have been led to do so in noticing—if you did notice it—how your sudden appearance unnerved me down there. It was but a momentary faltering; and considering what you have been to me, it was natural enough. But will helped me through it—though perhaps you think me a humbug for saying it—and immediately afterwards I felt that of all persons in the world whom it was my duty and desire to save from the wrath to come—sneer if you like—the woman whom I had so grievously wronged was that person. I have come with that sole purpose in view—nothing more.”\n\nThere was the smallest vein of scorn in her words of rejoinder: “Have you saved yourself? Charity begins at home, they say.”\n\n“_I_ have done nothing!” said he indifferently. “Heaven, as I have been telling my hearers, has done all. No amount of contempt that you can pour upon me, Tess, will equal what I have poured upon myself—the old Adam of my former years! Well, it is a strange story; believe it or not; but I can tell you the means by which my conversion was brought about, and I hope you will be interested enough at least to listen. Have you ever heard the name of the parson of Emminster—you must have done do?—old Mr Clare; one of the most earnest of his school; one of the few intense men left in the Church; not so intense as the extreme wing of Christian believers with which I have thrown in my lot, but quite an exception among the Established clergy, the younger of whom are gradually attenuating the true doctrines by their sophistries, till they are but the shadow of what they were. I only differ from him on the question of Church and State—the interpretation of the text, ‘Come out from among them and be ye separate, saith the Lord’—that’s all. He is one who, I firmly believe, has been the humble means of saving more souls in this country than any other man you can name. You have heard of him?”\n\n“I have,” she said.\n\n“He came to Trantridge two or three years ago to preach on behalf of some missionary society; and I, wretched fellow that I was, insulted him when, in his disinterestedness, he tried to reason with me and show me the way. He did not resent my conduct, he simply said that some day I should receive the first-fruits of the Spirit—that those who came to scoff sometimes remained to pray. There was a strange magic in his words. They sank into my mind. But the loss of my mother hit me most; and by degrees I was brought to see daylight. Since then my one desire has been to hand on the true view to others, and that is what I was trying to do to-day; though it is only lately that I have preached hereabout. The first months of my ministry have been spent in the North of England among strangers, where I preferred to make my earliest clumsy attempts, so as to acquire courage before undergoing that severest of all tests of one’s sincerity, addressing those who have known one, and have been one’s companions in the days of darkness. If you could only know, Tess, the pleasure of having a good slap at yourself, I am sure—”\n\n“Don’t go on with it!” she cried passionately, as she turned away from him to a stile by the wayside, on which she bent herself. “I can’t believe in such sudden things! I feel indignant with you for talking to me like this, when you know—when you know what harm you’ve done me! You, and those like you, take your fill of pleasure on earth by making the life of such as me bitter and black with sorrow; and then it is a fine thing, when you have had enough of that, to think of securing your pleasure in heaven by becoming converted! Out upon such—I don’t believe in you—I hate it!”\n\n“Tess,” he insisted; “don’t speak so! It came to me like a jolly new idea! And you don’t believe me? What don’t you believe?”\n\n“Your conversion. Your scheme of religion.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nShe dropped her voice. “Because a better man than you does not believe in such.”\n\n“What a woman’s reason! Who is this better man?”\n\n“I cannot tell you.”\n\n“Well,” he declared, a resentment beneath his words seeming ready to spring out at a moment’s notice, “God forbid that I should say I am a good man—and you know I don’t say any such thing. I am new to goodness, truly; but newcomers see furthest sometimes.”\n\n“Yes,” she replied sadly. “But I cannot believe in your conversion to a new spirit. Such flashes as you feel, Alec, I fear don’t last!”\n\nThus speaking she turned from the stile over which she had been leaning, and faced him; whereupon his eyes, falling casually upon the familiar countenance and form, remained contemplating her. The inferior man was quiet in him now; but it was surely not extracted, nor even entirely subdued.\n\n“Don’t look at me like that!” he said abruptly.\n\nTess, who had been quite unconscious of her action and mien, instantly withdrew the large dark gaze of her eyes, stammering with a flush, “I beg your pardon!” And there was revived in her the wretched sentiment which had often come to her before, that in inhabiting the fleshly tabernacle with which Nature had endowed her she was somehow doing wrong.\n\n“No, no! Don’t beg my pardon. But since you wear a veil to hide your good looks, why don’t you keep it down?”\n\nShe pulled down the veil, saying hastily, “It was mostly to keep off the wind.”\n\n“It may seem harsh of me to dictate like this,” he went on; “but it is better that I should not look too often on you. It might be dangerous.”\n\n“Ssh!” said Tess.\n\n“Well, women’s faces have had too much power over me already for me not to fear them! An evangelist has nothing to do with such as they; and it reminds me of the old times that I would forget!”\n\nAfter this their conversation dwindled to a casual remark now and then as they rambled onward, Tess inwardly wondering how far he was going with her, and not liking to send him back by positive mandate. Frequently when they came to a gate or stile they found painted thereon in red or blue letters some text of Scripture, and she asked him if he knew who had been at the pains to blazon these announcements. He told her that the man was employed by himself and others who were working with him in that district, to paint these reminders that no means might be left untried which might move the hearts of a wicked generation.\n\nAt length the road touched the spot called “Cross-in-Hand.” Of all spots on the bleached and desolate upland this was the most forlorn. It was so far removed from the charm which is sought in landscape by artists and view-lovers as to reach a new kind of beauty, a negative beauty of tragic tone. The place took its name from a stone pillar which stood there, a strange rude monolith, from a stratum unknown in any local quarry, on which was roughly carved a human hand. Differing accounts were given of its history and purport. Some authorities stated that a devotional cross had once formed the complete erection thereon, of which the present relic was but the stump; others that the stone as it stood was entire, and that it had been fixed there to mark a boundary or place of meeting. Anyhow, whatever the origin of the relic, there was and is something sinister, or solemn, according to mood, in the scene amid which it stands; something tending to impress the most phlegmatic passer-by.\n\n“I think I must leave you now,” he remarked, as they drew near to this spot. “I have to preach at Abbot’s-Cernel at six this evening, and my way lies across to the right from here. And you upset me somewhat too, Tessy—I cannot, will not, say why. I must go away and get strength.... How is it that you speak so fluently now? Who has taught you such good English?”\n\n“I have learnt things in my troubles,” she said evasively.\n\n“What troubles have you had?”\n\nShe told him of the first one—the only one that related to him.\n\nD’Urberville was struck mute. “I knew nothing of this till now!” he next murmured. “Why didn’t you write to me when you felt your trouble coming on?”\n\nShe did not reply; and he broke the silence by adding: “Well—you will see me again.”\n\n“No,” she answered. “Do not again come near me!”\n\n“I will think. But before we part come here.” He stepped up to the pillar. “This was once a Holy Cross. Relics are not in my creed; but I fear you at moments—far more than you need fear me at present; and to lessen my fear, put your hand upon that stone hand, and swear that you will never tempt me—by your charms or ways.”\n\n“Good God—how can you ask what is so unnecessary! All that is furthest from my thought!”\n\n“Yes—but swear it.”\n\nTess, half frightened, gave way to his importunity; placed her hand upon the stone and swore.\n\n“I am sorry you are not a believer,” he continued; “that some unbeliever should have got hold of you and unsettled your mind. But no more now. At home at least I can pray for you; and I will; and who knows what may not happen? I’m off. Goodbye!”\n\nHe turned to a hunting-gate in the hedge and, without letting his eyes again rest upon her, leapt over and struck out across the down in the direction of Abbot’s-Cernel. As he walked his pace showed perturbation, and by-and-by, as if instigated by a former thought, he drew from his pocket a small book, between the leaves of which was folded a letter, worn and soiled, as from much re-reading. D’Urberville opened the letter. It was dated several months before this time, and was signed by Parson Clare.\n\nThe letter began by expressing the writer’s unfeigned joy at d’Urberville’s conversion, and thanked him for his kindness in communicating with the parson on the subject. It expressed Mr Clare’s warm assurance of forgiveness for d’Urberville’s former conduct and his interest in the young man’s plans for the future. He, Mr Clare, would much have liked to see d’Urberville in the Church to whose ministry he had devoted so many years of his own life, and would have helped him to enter a theological college to that end; but since his correspondent had possibly not cared to do this on account of the delay it would have entailed, he was not the man to insist upon its paramount importance. Every man must work as he could best work, and in the method towards which he felt impelled by the Spirit.\n\nD’Urberville read and re-read this letter, and seemed to quiz himself cynically. He also read some passages from memoranda as he walked till his face assumed a calm, and apparently the image of Tess no longer troubled his mind.\n\nShe meanwhile had kept along the edge of the hill by which lay her nearest way home. Within the distance of a mile she met a solitary shepherd.\n\n“What is the meaning of that old stone I have passed?” she asked of him. “Was it ever a Holy Cross?”\n\n“Cross—no; ’twer not a cross! ’Tis a thing of ill-omen, Miss. It was put up in wuld times by the relations of a malefactor who was tortured there by nailing his hand to a post and afterwards hung. The bones lie underneath. They say he sold his soul to the devil, and that he walks at times.”\n\nShe felt the _petite mort_ at this unexpectedly gruesome information, and left the solitary man behind her. It was dusk when she drew near to Flintcomb-Ash, and in the lane at the entrance to the hamlet she approached a girl and her lover without their observing her. They were talking no secrets, and the clear unconcerned voice of the young woman, in response to the warmer accents of the man, spread into the chilly air as the one soothing thing within the dusky horizon, full of a stagnant obscurity upon which nothing else intruded. For a moment the voices cheered the heart of Tess, till she reasoned that this interview had its origin, on one side or the other, in the same attraction which had been the prelude to her own tribulation. When she came close, the girl turned serenely and recognized her, the young man walking off in embarrassment. The woman was Izz Huett, whose interest in Tess’s excursion immediately superseded her own proceedings. Tess did not explain very clearly its results, and Izz, who was a girl of tact, began to speak of her own little affair, a phase of which Tess had just witnessed.\n\n“He is Amby Seedling, the chap who used to sometimes come and help at Talbothays,” she explained indifferently. “He actually inquired and found out that I had come here, and has followed me. He says he’s been in love wi’ me these two years. But I’ve hardly answered him.”\n\n# XLVI\n\nSeveral days had passed since her futile journey, and Tess was afield. The dry winter wind still blew, but a screen of thatched hurdles erected in the eye of the blast kept its force away from her. On the sheltered side was a turnip-slicing machine, whose bright blue hue of new paint seemed almost vocal in the otherwise subdued scene. Opposite its front was a long mound or “grave”, in which the roots had been preserved since early winter. Tess was standing at the uncovered end, chopping off with a bill-hook the fibres and earth from each root, and throwing it after the operation into the slicer. A man was turning the handle of the machine, and from its trough came the newly-cut swedes, the fresh smell of whose yellow chips was accompanied by the sounds of the snuffling wind, the smart swish of the slicing-blades, and the choppings of the hook in Tess’s leather-gloved hand.\n\nThe wide acreage of blank agricultural brownness, apparent where the swedes had been pulled, was beginning to be striped in wales of darker brown, gradually broadening to ribands. Along the edge of each of these something crept upon ten legs, moving without haste and without rest up and down the whole length of the field; it was two horses and a man, the plough going between them, turning up the cleared ground for a spring sowing.\n\nFor hours nothing relieved the joyless monotony of things. Then, far beyond the ploughing-teams, a black speck was seen. It had come from the corner of a fence, where there was a gap, and its tendency was up the incline, towards the swede-cutters. From the proportions of a mere point it advanced to the shape of a ninepin, and was soon perceived to be a man in black, arriving from the direction of Flintcomb-Ash. The man at the slicer, having nothing else to do with his eyes, continually observed the comer, but Tess, who was occupied, did not perceive him till her companion directed her attention to his approach.\n\nIt was not her hard taskmaster, Farmer Groby; it was one in a semi-clerical costume, who now represented what had once been the free-and-easy Alec d’Urberville. Not being hot at his preaching there was less enthusiasm about him now, and the presence of the grinder seemed to embarrass him. A pale distress was already on Tess’s face, and she pulled her curtained hood further over it.\n\nD’Urberville came up and said quietly—\n\n“I want to speak to you, Tess.”\n\n“You have refused my last request, not to come near me!” said she.\n\n“Yes, but I have a good reason.”\n\n“Well, tell it.”\n\n“It is more serious than you may think.”\n\nHe glanced round to see if he were overheard. They were at some distance from the man who turned the slicer, and the movement of the machine, too, sufficiently prevented Alec’s words reaching other ears. D’Urberville placed himself so as to screen Tess from the labourer, turning his back to the latter.\n\n“It is this,” he continued, with capricious compunction. “In thinking of your soul and mine when we last met, I neglected to inquire as to your worldly condition. You were well dressed, and I did not think of it. But I see now that it is hard—harder than it used to be when I—knew you—harder than you deserve. Perhaps a good deal of it is owning to me!”\n\nShe did not answer, and he watched her inquiringly, as, with bent head, her face completely screened by the hood, she resumed her trimming of the swedes. By going on with her work she felt better able to keep him outside her emotions.\n\n“Tess,” he added, with a sigh of discontent,—“yours was the very worst case I ever was concerned in! I had no idea of what had resulted till you told me. Scamp that I was to foul that innocent life! The whole blame was mine—the whole unconventional business of our time at Trantridge. You, too, the real blood of which I am but the base imitation, what a blind young thing you were as to possibilities! I say in all earnestness that it is a shame for parents to bring up their girls in such dangerous ignorance of the gins and nets that the wicked may set for them, whether their motive be a good one or the result of simple indifference.”\n\nTess still did no more than listen, throwing down one globular root and taking up another with automatic regularity, the pensive contour of the mere fieldwoman alone marking her.\n\n“But it is not that I came to say,” d’Urberville went on. “My circumstances are these. I have lost my mother since you were at Trantridge, and the place is my own. But I intend to sell it, and devote myself to missionary work in Africa. A devil of a poor hand I shall make at the trade, no doubt. However, what I want to ask you is, will you put it in my power to do my duty—to make the only reparation I can make for the trick played you: that is, will you be my wife, and go with me?... I have already obtained this precious document. It was my old mother’s dying wish.”\n\nHe drew a piece of parchment from his pocket, with a slight fumbling of embarrassment.\n\n“What is it?” said she.\n\n“A marriage licence.”\n\n“O no, sir—no!” she said quickly, starting back.\n\n“You will not? Why is that?”\n\nAnd as he asked the question a disappointment which was not entirely the disappointment of thwarted duty crossed d’Urberville’s face. It was unmistakably a symptom that something of his old passion for her had been revived; duty and desire ran hand-in-hand.\n\n“Surely,” he began again, in more impetuous tones, and then looked round at the labourer who turned the slicer.\n\nTess, too, felt that the argument could not be ended there. Informing the man that a gentleman had come to see her, with whom she wished to walk a little way, she moved off with d’Urberville across the zebra-striped field. When they reached the first newly-ploughed section he held out his hand to help her over it; but she stepped forward on the summits of the earth-rolls as if she did not see him.\n\n“You will not marry me, Tess, and make me a self-respecting man?” he repeated, as soon as they were over the furrows.\n\n“I cannot.”\n\n“But why?”\n“You know I have no affection for you.”\n\n“But you would get to feel that in time, perhaps—as soon as you really could forgive me?”\n\n“Never!”\n\n“Why so positive?”\n\n“I love somebody else.”\n\nThe words seemed to astonish him.\n\n“You do?” he cried. “Somebody else? But has not a sense of what is morally right and proper any weight with you?”\n\n“No, no, no—don’t say that!”\n\n“Anyhow, then, your love for this other man may be only a passing feeling which you will overcome—”\n\n“No—no.”\n“Yes, yes! Why not?”\n\n“I cannot tell you.”\n\n“You must in honour!”\n\n“Well then.... I have married him.”\n\n“Ah!” he exclaimed; and he stopped dead and gazed at her.\n\n“I did not wish to tell—I did not mean to!” she pleaded. “It is a secret here, or at any rate but dimly known. So will you, _please_ will you, keep from questioning me? You must remember that we are now strangers.”\n\n“Strangers—are we? Strangers!”\n\nFor a moment a flash of his old irony marked his face; but he determinedly chastened it down.\n\n“Is that man your husband?” he asked mechanically, denoting by a sign the labourer who turned the machine.\n\n“That man!” she said proudly. “I should think not!”\n\n“Who, then?”\n\n“Do not ask what I do not wish to tell!” she begged, and flashed her appeal to him from her upturned face and lash-shadowed eyes.\n\nD’Urberville was disturbed.\n\n“But I only asked for your sake!” he retorted hotly. “Angels of heaven!—God forgive me for such an expression—I came here, I swear, as I thought for your good. Tess—don’t look at me so—I cannot stand your looks! There never were such eyes, surely, before Christianity or since! There—I won’t lose my head; I dare not. I own that the sight of you had waked up my love for you, which, I believed, was extinguished with all such feelings. But I thought that our marriage might be a sanctification for us both. ‘The unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the husband,’ I said to myself. But my plan is dashed from me; and I must bear the disappointment!”\n\nHe moodily reflected with his eyes on the ground.\n\n“Married. Married!... Well, that being so,” he added, quite calmly, tearing the licence slowly into halves and putting them in his pocket; “that being prevented, I should like to do some good to you and your husband, whoever he may be. There are many questions that I am tempted to ask, but I will not do so, of course, in opposition to your wishes. Though, if I could know your husband, I might more easily benefit him and you. Is he on this farm?”\n\n“No,” she murmured. “He is far away.”\n\n“Far away? From _you_? What sort of husband can he be?”\n\n“O, do not speak against him! It was through you! He found out—”\n\n“Ah, is it so!... That’s sad, Tess!”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But to stay away from you—to leave you to work like this!”\n\n“He does not leave me to work!” she cried, springing to the defence of the absent one with all her fervour. “He don’t know it! It is by my own arrangement.”\n\n“Then, does he write?”\n\n“I—I cannot tell you. There are things which are private to ourselves.”\n\n“Of course that means that he does not. You are a deserted wife, my fair Tess—”\n\nIn an impulse he turned suddenly to take her hand; the buff-glove was on it, and he seized only the rough leather fingers which did not express the life or shape of those within.\n\n“You must not—you must not!” she cried fearfully, slipping her hand from the glove as from a pocket, and leaving it in his grasp. “O, will you go away—for the sake of me and my husband—go, in the name of your own Christianity!”\n\n“Yes, yes; I will,” he said abruptly, and thrusting the glove back to her he turned to leave. Facing round, however, he said, “Tess, as God is my judge, I meant no humbug in taking your hand!”\n\nA pattering of hoofs on the soil of the field, which they had not noticed in their preoccupation, ceased close behind them; and a voice reached her ear:\n\n“What the devil are you doing away from your work at this time o’ day?”\n\nFarmer Groby had espied the two figures from the distance, and had inquisitively ridden across, to learn what was their business in his field.\n\n“Don’t speak like that to her!” said d’Urberville, his face blackening with something that was not Christianity.\n\n“Indeed, Mister! And what mid Methodist pa’sons have to do with she?”\n\n“Who is the fellow?” asked d’Urberville, turning to Tess.\n\nShe went close up to him.\n\n“Go—I do beg you!” she said.\n\n“What! And leave you to that tyrant? I can see in his face what a churl he is.”\n\n“He won’t hurt me. _He’s_ not in love with me. I can leave at Lady-Day.”\n\n“Well, I have no right but to obey, I suppose. But—well, goodbye!”\n\nHer defender, whom she dreaded more than her assailant, having reluctantly disappeared, the farmer continued his reprimand, which Tess took with the greatest coolness, that sort of attack being independent of sex. To have as a master this man of stone, who would have cuffed her if he had dared, was almost a relief after her former experiences. She silently walked back towards the summit of the field that was the scene of her labour, so absorbed in the interview which had just taken place that she was hardly aware that the nose of Groby’s horse almost touched her shoulders.\n\n“If so be you make an agreement to work for me till Lady-Day, I’ll see that you carry it out,” he growled. “’Od rot the women—now ’tis one thing, and then ’tis another. But I’ll put up with it no longer!”\n\nKnowing very well that he did not harass the other women of the farm as he harassed her out of spite for the flooring he had once received, she did for one moment picture what might have been the result if she had been free to accept the offer just made her of being the monied Alec’s wife. It would have lifted her completely out of subjection, not only to her present oppressive employer, but to a whole world who seemed to despise her. “But no, no!” she said breathlessly; “I could not have married him now! He is so unpleasant to me.”\n\nThat very night she began an appealing letter to Clare, concealing from him her hardships, and assuring him of her undying affection. Any one who had been in a position to read between the lines would have seen that at the back of her great love was some monstrous fear—almost a desperation—as to some secret contingencies which were not disclosed. But again she did not finish her effusion; he had asked Izz to go with him, and perhaps he did not care for her at all. She put the letter in her box, and wondered if it would ever reach Angel’s hands.\n\nAfter this her daily tasks were gone through heavily enough, and brought on the day which was of great import to agriculturists—the day of the Candlemas Fair. It was at this fair that new engagements were entered into for the twelve months following the ensuing Lady-Day, and those of the farming population who thought of changing their places duly attended at the county-town where the fair was held. Nearly all the labourers on Flintcomb-Ash farm intended flight, and early in the morning there was a general exodus in the direction of the town, which lay at a distance of from ten to a dozen miles over hilly country. Though Tess also meant to leave at the quarter-day, she was one of the few who did not go to the fair, having a vaguely-shaped hope that something would happen to render another outdoor engagement unnecessary.\n\nIt was a peaceful February day, of wonderful softness for the time, and one would almost have thought that winter was over. She had hardly finished her dinner when d’Urberville’s figure darkened the window of the cottage wherein she was a lodger, which she had all to herself to-day.\n\nTess jumped up, but her visitor had knocked at the door, and she could hardly in reason run away. D’Urberville’s knock, his walk up to the door, had some indescribable quality of difference from his air when she last saw him. They seemed to be acts of which the doer was ashamed. She thought that she would not open the door; but, as there was no sense in that either, she arose, and having lifted the latch stepped back quickly. He came in, saw her, and flung himself down into a chair before speaking.\n\n“Tess—I couldn’t help it!” he began desperately, as he wiped his heated face, which had also a superimposed flush of excitement. “I felt that I must call at least to ask how you are. I assure you I had not been thinking of you at all till I saw you that Sunday; now I cannot get rid of your image, try how I may! It is hard that a good woman should do harm to a bad man; yet so it is. If you would only pray for me, Tess!”\n\nThe suppressed discontent of his manner was almost pitiable, and yet Tess did not pity him.\n\n“How can I pray for you,” she said, “when I am forbidden to believe that the great Power who moves the world would alter His plans on my account?”\n\n“You really think that?”\n\n“Yes. I have been cured of the presumption of thinking otherwise.”\n\n“Cured? By whom?”\n\n“By my husband, if I must tell.”\n\n“Ah—your husband—your husband! How strange it seems! I remember you hinted something of the sort the other day. What do you really believe in these matters, Tess?” he asked. “You seem to have no religion—perhaps owing to me.”\n\n“But I have. Though I don’t believe in anything supernatural.”\n\nD’Urberville looked at her with misgiving.\n\n“Then do you think that the line I take is all wrong?”\n\n“A good deal of it.”\n\n“H’m—and yet I’ve felt so sure about it,” he said uneasily.\n\n“I believe in the _spirit_ of the Sermon on the Mount, and so did my dear husband.... But I don’t believe—”\n\nHere she gave her negations.\n\n“The fact is,” said d’Urberville drily, “whatever your dear husband believed you accept, and whatever he rejected you reject, without the least inquiry or reasoning on your own part. That’s just like you women. Your mind is enslaved to his.”\n\n“Ah, because he knew everything!” said she, with a triumphant simplicity of faith in Angel Clare that the most perfect man could hardly have deserved, much less her husband.\n\n“Yes, but you should not take negative opinions wholesale from another person like that. A pretty fellow he must be to teach you such scepticism!”\n\n“He never forced my judgement! He would never argue on the subject with me! But I looked at it in this way; what he believed, after inquiring deep into doctrines, was much more likely to be right than what I might believe, who hadn’t looked into doctrines at all.”\n\n“What used he to say? He must have said something?”\n\nShe reflected; and with her acute memory for the letter of Angel Clare’s remarks, even when she did not comprehend their spirit, she recalled a merciless polemical syllogism that she had heard him use when, as it occasionally happened, he indulged in a species of thinking aloud with her at his side. In delivering it she gave also Clare’s accent and manner with reverential faithfulness.\n\n“Say that again,” asked d’Urberville, who had listened with the greatest attention.\n\nShe repeated the argument, and d’Urberville thoughtfully murmured the words after her.\n\n“Anything else?” he presently asked.\n\n“He said at another time something like this”; and she gave another, which might possibly have been paralleled in many a work of the pedigree ranging from the _Dictionnaire Philosophique_ to Huxley’s _Essays_.\n\n“Ah—ha! How do you remember them?”\n\n“I wanted to believe what he believed, though he didn’t wish me to; and I managed to coax him to tell me a few of his thoughts. I can’t say I quite understand that one; but I know it is right.”\n\n“H’m. Fancy your being able to teach me what you don’t know yourself!”\n\nHe fell into thought.\n\n“And so I threw in my spiritual lot with his,” she resumed. “I didn’t wish it to be different. What’s good enough for him is good enough for me.”\n\n“Does he know that you are as big an infidel as he?”\n\n“No—I never told him—if I am an infidel.”\n\n“Well—you are better off to-day that I am, Tess, after all! You don’t believe that you ought to preach my doctrine, and, therefore, do no despite to your conscience in abstaining. I do believe I ought to preach it, but, like the devils, I believe and tremble, for I suddenly leave off preaching it, and give way to my passion for you.”\n“How?”\n\n“Why,” he said aridly; “I have come all the way here to see you to-day! But I started from home to go to Casterbridge Fair, where I have undertaken to preach the Word from a waggon at half-past two this afternoon, and where all the brethren are expecting me this minute. Here’s the announcement.”\n\nHe drew from his breast-pocket a poster whereon was printed the day, hour, and place of meeting, at which he, d’Urberville, would preach the Gospel as aforesaid.\n\n“But how can you get there?” said Tess, looking at the clock.\n\n“I cannot get there! I have come here.”\n\n“What, you have really arranged to preach, and—”\n\n“I have arranged to preach, and I shall not be there—by reason of my burning desire to see a woman whom I once despised!—No, by my word and truth, I never despised you; if I had I should not love you now! Why I did not despise you was on account of your being unsmirched in spite of all; you withdrew yourself from me so quickly and resolutely when you saw the situation; you did not remain at my pleasure; so there was one petticoat in the world for whom I had no contempt, and you are she. But you may well despise me now! I thought I worshipped on the mountains, but I find I still serve in the groves! Ha! ha!”\n\n“O Alec d’Urberville! what does this mean? What have I done!”\n\n“Done?” he said, with a soulless sneer in the word. “Nothing intentionally. But you have been the means—the innocent means—of my backsliding, as they call it. I ask myself, am I, indeed, one of those ‘servants of corruption’ who, ‘after they have escaped the pollutions of the world, are again entangled therein and overcome’—whose latter end is worse than their beginning?” He laid his hand on her shoulder. “Tess, my girl, I was on the way to, at least, social salvation till I saw you again!” he said freakishly shaking her, as if she were a child. “And why then have you tempted me? I was firm as a man could be till I saw those eyes and that mouth again—surely there never was such a maddening mouth since Eve’s!” His voice sank, and a hot archness shot from his own black eyes. “You temptress, Tess; you dear damned witch of Babylon—I could not resist you as soon as I met you again!”\n\n“I couldn’t help your seeing me again!” said Tess, recoiling.\n\n“I know it—I repeat that I do not blame you. But the fact remains. When I saw you ill-used on the farm that day I was nearly mad to think that I had no legal right to protect you—that I could not have it; whilst he who has it seems to neglect you utterly!”\n\n“Don’t speak against him—he is absent!” she cried in much excitement. “Treat him honourably—he has never wronged you! O leave his wife before any scandal spreads that may do harm to his honest name!”\n\n“I will—I will,” he said, like a man awakening from a luring dream. “I have broken my engagement to preach to those poor drunken boobies at the fair—it is the first time I have played such a practical joke. A month ago I should have been horrified at such a possibility. I’ll go away—to swear—and—ah, can I! to keep away.” Then, suddenly: “One clasp, Tessy—one! Only for old friendship—”\n\n“I am without defence. Alec! A good man’s honour is in my keeping—think—be ashamed!”\n\n“Pooh! Well, yes—yes!”\n\nHe clenched his lips, mortified with himself for his weakness. His eyes were equally barren of worldly and religious faith. The corpses of those old fitful passions which had lain inanimate amid the lines of his face ever since his reformation seemed to wake and come together as in a resurrection. He went out indeterminately.\n\nThough d’Urberville had declared that this breach of his engagement to-day was the simple backsliding of a believer, Tess’s words, as echoed from Angel Clare, had made a deep impression upon him, and continued to do so after he had left her. He moved on in silence, as if his energies were benumbed by the hitherto undreamt-of possibility that his position was untenable. Reason had had nothing to do with his whimsical conversion, which was perhaps the mere freak of a careless man in search of a new sensation, and temporarily impressed by his mother’s death.\n\nThe drops of logic Tess had let fall into the sea of his enthusiasm served to chill its effervescence to stagnation. He said to himself, as he pondered again and again over the crystallized phrases that she had handed on to him, “That clever fellow little thought that, by telling her those things, he might be paving my way back to her!”\n\n# XLVII\n\nIt is the threshing of the last wheat-rick at Flintcomb-Ash farm. The dawn of the March morning is singularly inexpressive, and there is nothing to show where the eastern horizon lies. Against the twilight rises the trapezoidal top of the stack, which has stood forlornly here through the washing and bleaching of the wintry weather.\n\nWhen Izz Huett and Tess arrived at the scene of operations only a rustling denoted that others had preceded them; to which, as the light increased, there were presently added the silhouettes of two men on the summit. They were busily “unhaling” the rick, that is, stripping off the thatch before beginning to throw down the sheaves; and while this was in progress Izz and Tess, with the other women-workers, in their whitey-brown pinners, stood waiting and shivering, Farmer Groby having insisted upon their being on the spot thus early to get the job over if possible by the end of the day. Close under the eaves of the stack, and as yet barely visible, was the red tyrant that the women had come to serve—a timber-framed construction, with straps and wheels appertaining—the threshing-machine which, whilst it was going, kept up a despotic demand upon the endurance of their muscles and nerves.\n\nA little way off there was another indistinct figure; this one black, with a sustained hiss that spoke of strength very much in reserve. The long chimney running up beside an ash-tree, and the warmth which radiated from the spot, explained without the necessity of much daylight that here was the engine which was to act as the _primum mobile_ of this little world. By the engine stood a dark, motionless being, a sooty and grimy embodiment of tallness, in a sort of trance, with a heap of coals by his side: it was the engine-man. The isolation of his manner and colour lent him the appearance of a creature from Tophet, who had strayed into the pellucid smokelessness of this region of yellow grain and pale soil, with which he had nothing in common, to amaze and to discompose its aborigines.\n\nWhat he looked he felt. He was in the agricultural world, but not of it. He served fire and smoke; these denizens of the fields served vegetation, weather, frost, and sun. He travelled with his engine from farm to farm, from county to county, for as yet the steam threshing-machine was itinerant in this part of Wessex. He spoke in a strange northern accent; his thoughts being turned inwards upon himself, his eye on his iron charge, hardly perceiving the scenes around him, and caring for them not at all: holding only strictly necessary intercourse with the natives, as if some ancient doom compelled him to wander here against his will in the service of his Plutonic master. The long strap which ran from the driving-wheel of his engine to the red thresher under the rick was the sole tie-line between agriculture and him.\n\nWhile they uncovered the sheaves he stood apathetic beside his portable repository of force, round whose hot blackness the morning air quivered. He had nothing to do with preparatory labour. His fire was waiting incandescent, his steam was at high pressure, in a few seconds he could make the long strap move at an invisible velocity. Beyond its extent the environment might be corn, straw, or chaos; it was all the same to him. If any of the autochthonous idlers asked him what he called himself, he replied shortly, “an engineer.”\n\nThe rick was unhaled by full daylight; the men then took their places, the women mounted, and the work began. Farmer Groby—or, as they called him, “he”—had arrived ere this, and by his orders Tess was placed on the platform of the machine, close to the man who fed it, her business being to untie every sheaf of corn handed on to her by Izz Huett, who stood next, but on the rick; so that the feeder could seize it and spread it over the revolving drum, which whisked out every grain in one moment.\n\nThey were soon in full progress, after a preparatory hitch or two, which rejoiced the hearts of those who hated machinery. The work sped on till breakfast time, when the thresher was stopped for half an hour; and on starting again after the meal the whole supplementary strength of the farm was thrown into the labour of constructing the straw-rick, which began to grow beside the stack of corn. A hasty lunch was eaten as they stood, without leaving their positions, and then another couple of hours brought them near to dinner-time; the inexorable wheel continuing to spin, and the penetrating hum of the thresher to thrill to the very marrow all who were near the revolving wire-cage.\n\nThe old men on the rising straw-rick talked of the past days when they had been accustomed to thresh with flails on the oaken barn-floor; when everything, even to winnowing, was effected by hand-labour, which, to their thinking, though slow, produced better results. Those, too, on the corn-rick talked a little; but the perspiring ones at the machine, including Tess, could not lighten their duties by the exchange of many words. It was the ceaselessness of the work which tried her so severely, and began to make her wish that she had never come to Flintcomb-Ash. The women on the corn-rick—Marian, who was one of them, in particular—could stop to drink ale or cold tea from the flagon now and then, or to exchange a few gossiping remarks while they wiped their faces or cleared the fragments of straw and husk from their clothing; but for Tess there was no respite; for, as the drum never stopped, the man who fed it could not stop, and she, who had to supply the man with untied sheaves, could not stop either, unless Marian changed places with her, which she sometimes did for half an hour in spite of Groby’s objections that she was too slow-handed for a feeder.\n\nFor some probably economical reason it was usually a woman who was chosen for this particular duty, and Groby gave as his motive in selecting Tess that she was one of those who best combined strength with quickness in untying, and both with staying power, and this may have been true. The hum of the thresher, which prevented speech, increased to a raving whenever the supply of corn fell short of the regular quantity. As Tess and the man who fed could never turn their heads she did not know that just before the dinner-hour a person had come silently into the field by the gate, and had been standing under a second rick watching the scene and Tess in particular. He was dressed in a tweed suit of fashionable pattern, and he twirled a gay walking-cane.\n\n“Who is that?” said Izz Huett to Marian. She had at first addressed the inquiry to Tess, but the latter could not hear it.\n\n“Somebody’s fancy-man, I s’pose,” said Marian laconically.\n\n“I’ll lay a guinea he’s after Tess.”\n\n“O no. ’Tis a ranter pa’son who’s been sniffing after her lately; not a dandy like this.”\n\n“Well—this is the same man.”\n\n“The same man as the preacher? But he’s quite different!”\n\n“He hev left off his black coat and white neckercher, and hev cut off his whiskers; but he’s the same man for all that.”\n\n“D’ye really think so? Then I’ll tell her,” said Marian.\n\n“Don’t. She’ll see him soon enough, good-now.”\n\n“Well, I don’t think it at all right for him to join his preaching to courting a married woman, even though her husband mid be abroad, and she, in a sense, a widow.”\n\n“Oh—he can do her no harm,” said Izz drily. “Her mind can no more be heaved from that one place where it do bide than a stooded waggon from the hole he’s in. Lord love ’ee, neither court-paying, nor preaching, nor the seven thunders themselves, can wean a woman when ’twould be better for her that she should be weaned.”\n\nDinner-time came, and the whirling ceased; whereupon Tess left her post, her knees trembling so wretchedly with the shaking of the machine that she could scarcely walk.\n\n“You ought to het a quart o’ drink into ’ee, as I’ve done,” said Marian. “You wouldn’t look so white then. Why, souls above us, your face is as if you’d been hagrode!”\n\nIt occurred to the good-natured Marian that, as Tess was so tired, her discovery of her visitor’s presence might have the bad effect of taking away her appetite; and Marian was thinking of inducing Tess to descend by a ladder on the further side of the stack when the gentleman came forward and looked up.\n\nTess uttered a short little “Oh!” And a moment after she said, quickly, “I shall eat my dinner here—right on the rick.”\n\nSometimes, when they were so far from their cottages, they all did this; but as there was rather a keen wind going to-day, Marian and the rest descended, and sat under the straw-stack.\n\nThe newcomer was, indeed, Alec d’Urberville, the late Evangelist, despite his changed attire and aspect. It was obvious at a glance that the original _Weltlust_ had come back; that he had restored himself, as nearly as a man could do who had grown three or four years older, to the old jaunty, slapdash guise under which Tess had first known her admirer, and cousin so-called. Having decided to remain where she was, Tess sat down among the bundles, out of sight of the ground, and began her meal; till, by-and-by, she heard footsteps on the ladder, and immediately after Alec appeared upon the stack—now an oblong and level platform of sheaves. He strode across them, and sat down opposite of her without a word.\n\nTess continued to eat her modest dinner, a slice of thick pancake which she had brought with her. The other workfolk were by this time all gathered under the rick, where the loose straw formed a comfortable retreat.\n\n“I am here again, as you see,” said d’Urberville.\n\n“Why do you trouble me so!” she cried, reproach flashing from her very finger-ends.\n\n“_I_ trouble _you_? I think I may ask, why do you trouble me?”\n\n“Sure, I don’t trouble you any-when!”\n\n“You say you don’t? But you do! You haunt me. Those very eyes that you turned upon me with such a bitter flash a moment ago, they come to me just as you showed them then, in the night and in the day! Tess, ever since you told me of that child of ours, it is just as if my feelings, which have been flowing in a strong puritanical stream, had suddenly found a way open in the direction of you, and had all at once gushed through. The religious channel is left dry forthwith; and it is you who have done it!”\n\nShe gazed in silence.\n\n“What—you have given up your preaching entirely?” she asked. She had gathered from Angel sufficient of the incredulity of modern thought to despise flash enthusiasm; but, as a woman, she was somewhat appalled.\n\nIn affected severity d’Urberville continued—\n\n“Entirely. I have broken every engagement since that afternoon I was to address the drunkards at Casterbridge Fair. The deuce only knows what I am thought of by the brethren. Ah-ha! The brethren! No doubt they pray for me—weep for me; for they are kind people in their way. But what do I care? How could I go on with the thing when I had lost my faith in it?—it would have been hypocrisy of the basest kind! Among them I should have stood like Hymenaeus and Alexander, who were delivered over to Satan that they might learn not to blaspheme. What a grand revenge you have taken! I saw you innocent, and I deceived you. Four years after, you find me a Christian enthusiast; you then work upon me, perhaps to my complete perdition! But Tess, my coz, as I used to call you, this is only my way of talking, and you must not look so horribly concerned. Of course you have done nothing except retain your pretty face and shapely figure. I saw it on the rick before you saw me—that tight pinafore-thing sets it off, and that wing-bonnet—you field-girls should never wear those bonnets if you wish to keep out of danger.” He regarded her silently for a few moments, and with a short cynical laugh resumed: “I believe that if the bachelor-apostle, whose deputy I thought I was, had been tempted by such a pretty face, he would have let go the plough for her sake as I do!”\n\nTess attempted to expostulate, but at this juncture all her fluency failed her, and without heeding he added:\n\n“Well, this paradise that you supply is perhaps as good as any other, after all. But to speak seriously, Tess.” D’Urberville rose and came nearer, reclining sideways amid the sheaves, and resting upon his elbow. “Since I last saw you, I have been thinking of what you said that _he_ said. I have come to the conclusion that there does seem rather a want of common-sense in these threadbare old propositions; how I could have been so fired by poor Parson Clare’s enthusiasm, and have gone so madly to work, transcending even him, I cannot make out! As for what you said last time, on the strength of your wonderful husband’s intelligence—whose name you have never told me—about having what they call an ethical system without any dogma, I don’t see my way to that at all.”\n\n“Why, you can have the religion of loving-kindness and purity at least, if you can’t have—what do you call it—dogma.”\n\n“O no! I’m a different sort of fellow from that! If there’s nobody to say, ‘Do this, and it will be a good thing for you after you are dead; do that, and it will be a bad thing for you,’ I can’t warm up. Hang it, I am not going to feel responsible for my deeds and passions if there’s nobody to be responsible to; and if I were you, my dear, I wouldn’t either!”\n\nShe tried to argue, and tell him that he had mixed in his dull brain two matters, theology and morals, which in the primitive days of mankind had been quite distinct. But owing to Angel Clare’s reticence, to her absolute want of training, and to her being a vessel of emotions rather than reasons, she could not get on.\n\n“Well, never mind,” he resumed. “Here I am, my love, as in the old times!”\n\n“Not as then—never as then—’tis different!” she entreated. “And there was never warmth with me! O why didn’t you keep your faith, if the loss of it has brought you to speak to me like this!”\n\n“Because you’ve knocked it out of me; so the evil be upon your sweet head! Your husband little thought how his teaching would recoil upon him! Ha-ha—I’m awfully glad you have made an apostate of me all the same! Tess, I am more taken with you than ever, and I pity you too. For all your closeness, I see you are in a bad way—neglected by one who ought to cherish you.”\n\nShe could not get her morsels of food down her throat; her lips were dry, and she was ready to choke. The voices and laughs of the workfolk eating and drinking under the rick came to her as if they were a quarter of a mile off.\n\n“It is cruelty to me!” she said. “How—how can you treat me to this talk, if you care ever so little for me?”\n\n“True, true,” he said, wincing a little. “I did not come to reproach you for my deeds. I came Tess, to say that I don’t like you to be working like this, and I have come on purpose for you. You say you have a husband who is not I. Well, perhaps you have; but I’ve never seen him, and you’ve not told me his name; and altogether he seems rather a mythological personage. However, even if you have one, I think I am nearer to you than he is. I, at any rate, try to help you out of trouble, but he does not, bless his invisible face! The words of the stern prophet Hosea that I used to read come back to me. Don’t you know them, Tess?—‘And she shall follow after her lover, but she shall not overtake him; and she shall seek him, but shall not find him; then shall she say, I will go and return to my first husband; for then was it better with me than now!’ ... Tess, my trap is waiting just under the hill, and—darling mine, not his!—you know the rest.”\n\nHer face had been rising to a dull crimson fire while he spoke; but she did not answer.\n\n“You have been the cause of my backsliding,” he continued, stretching his arm towards her waist; “you should be willing to share it, and leave that mule you call husband for ever.”\n\nOne of her leather gloves, which she had taken off to eat her skimmer-cake, lay in her lap, and without the slightest warning she passionately swung the glove by the gauntlet directly in his face. It was heavy and thick as a warrior’s, and it struck him flat on the mouth. Fancy might have regarded the act as the recrudescence of a trick in which her armed progenitors were not unpractised. Alec fiercely started up from his reclining position. A scarlet oozing appeared where her blow had alighted, and in a moment the blood began dropping from his mouth upon the straw. But he soon controlled himself, calmly drew his handkerchief from his pocket, and mopped his bleeding lips.\n\nShe too had sprung up, but she sank down again. “Now, punish me!” she said, turning up her eyes to him with the hopeless defiance of the sparrow’s gaze before its captor twists its neck. “Whip me, crush me; you need not mind those people under the rick! I shall not cry out. Once victim, always victim—that’s the law!”\n\n“O no, no, Tess,” he said blandly. “I can make full allowance for this. Yet you most unjustly forget one thing, that I would have married you if you had not put it out of my power to do so. Did I not ask you flatly to be my wife—hey? Answer me.”\n\n“You did.”\n\n“And you cannot be. But remember one thing!” His voice hardened as his temper got the better of him with the recollection of his sincerity in asking her and her present ingratitude, and he stepped across to her side and held her by the shoulders, so that she shook under his grasp. “Remember, my lady, I was your master once! I will be your master again. If you are any man’s wife you are mine!”\n\nThe threshers now began to stir below.\n\n“So much for our quarrel,” he said, letting her go. “Now I shall leave you, and shall come again for your answer during the afternoon. You don’t know me yet! But I know you.”\n\nShe had not spoken again, remaining as if stunned. D’Urberville retreated over the sheaves, and descended the ladder, while the workers below rose and stretched their arms, and shook down the beer they had drunk. Then the threshing-machine started afresh; and amid the renewed rustle of the straw Tess resumed her position by the buzzing drum as one in a dream, untying sheaf after sheaf in endless succession.\n\n# XLVIII\n\nIn the afternoon the farmer made it known that the rick was to be finished that night, since there was a moon by which they could see to work, and the man with the engine was engaged for another farm on the morrow. Hence the twanging and humming and rustling proceeded with even less intermission than usual.\n\nIt was not till “nammet”-time, about three o’clock, that Tess raised her eyes and gave a momentary glance round. She felt but little surprise at seeing that Alec d’Urberville had come back, and was standing under the hedge by the gate. He had seen her lift her eyes, and waved his hand urbanely to her, while he blew her a kiss. It meant that their quarrel was over. Tess looked down again, and carefully abstained from gazing in that direction.\n\nThus the afternoon dragged on. The wheat-rick shrank lower, and the straw-rick grew higher, and the corn-sacks were carted away. At six o’clock the wheat-rick was about shoulder-high from the ground. But the unthreshed sheaves remaining untouched seemed countless still, notwithstanding the enormous numbers that had been gulped down by the insatiable swallower, fed by the man and Tess, through whose two young hands the greater part of them had passed. And the immense stack of straw where in the morning there had been nothing, appeared as the _faeces_ of the same buzzing red glutton. From the west sky a wrathful shine—all that wild March could afford in the way of sunset—had burst forth after the cloudy day, flooding the tired and sticky faces of the threshers, and dyeing them with a coppery light, as also the flapping garments of the women, which clung to them like dull flames.\n\nA panting ache ran through the rick. The man who fed was weary, and Tess could see that the red nape of his neck was encrusted with dirt and husks. She still stood at her post, her flushed and perspiring face coated with the corndust, and her white bonnet embrowned by it. She was the only woman whose place was upon the machine so as to be shaken bodily by its spinning, and the decrease of the stack now separated her from Marian and Izz, and prevented their changing duties with her as they had done. The incessant quivering, in which every fibre of her frame participated, had thrown her into a stupefied reverie in which her arms worked on independently of her consciousness. She hardly knew where she was, and did not hear Izz Huett tell her from below that her hair was tumbling down.\n\nBy degrees the freshest among them began to grow cadaverous and saucer-eyed. Whenever Tess lifted her head she beheld always the great upgrown straw-stack, with the men in shirt-sleeves upon it, against the gray north sky; in front of it the long red elevator like a Jacob’s ladder, on which a perpetual stream of threshed straw ascended, a yellow river running uphill, and spouting out on the top of the rick.\nShe knew that Alec d’Urberville was still on the scene, observing her from some point or other, though she could not say where. There was an excuse for his remaining, for when the threshed rick drew near its final sheaves a little ratting was always done, and men unconnected with the threshing sometimes dropped in for that performance—sporting characters of all descriptions, gents with terriers and facetious pipes, roughs with sticks and stones.\n\nBut there was another hour’s work before the layer of live rats at the base of the stack would be reached; and as the evening light in the direction of the Giant’s Hill by Abbot’s-Cernel dissolved away, the white-faced moon of the season arose from the horizon that lay towards Middleton Abbey and Shottsford on the other side. For the last hour or two Marian had felt uneasy about Tess, whom she could not get near enough to speak to, the other women having kept up their strength by drinking ale, and Tess having done without it through traditionary dread, owing to its results at her home in childhood. But Tess still kept going: if she could not fill her part she would have to leave; and this contingency, which she would have regarded with equanimity and even with relief a month or two earlier, had become a terror since d’Urberville had begun to hover round her.\n\nThe sheaf-pitchers and feeders had now worked the rick so low that people on the ground could talk to them. To Tess’s surprise Farmer Groby came up on the machine to her, and said that if she desired to join her friend he did not wish her to keep on any longer, and would send somebody else to take her place. The “friend” was d’Urberville, she knew, and also that this concession had been granted in obedience to the request of that friend, or enemy. She shook her head and toiled on.\n\nThe time for the rat-catching arrived at last, and the hunt began. The creatures had crept downwards with the subsidence of the rick till they were all together at the bottom, and being now uncovered from their last refuge, they ran across the open ground in all directions, a loud shriek from the by-this-time half-tipsy Marian informing her companions that one of the rats had invaded her person—a terror which the rest of the women had guarded against by various schemes of skirt-tucking and self-elevation. The rat was at last dislodged, and, amid the barking of dogs, masculine shouts, feminine screams, oaths, stampings, and confusion as of Pandemonium, Tess untied her last sheaf; the drum slowed, the whizzing ceased, and she stepped from the machine to the ground.\n\nHer lover, who had only looked on at the rat-catching, was promptly at her side.\n\n“What—after all—my insulting slap, too!” said she in an underbreath. She was so utterly exhausted that she had not strength to speak louder.\n\n“I should indeed be foolish to feel offended at anything you say or do,” he answered, in the seductive voice of the Trantridge time. “How the little limbs tremble! You are as weak as a bled calf, you know you are; and yet you need have done nothing since I arrived. How could you be so obstinate? However, I have told the farmer that he has no right to employ women at steam-threshing. It is not proper work for them; and on all the better class of farms it has been given up, as he knows very well. I will walk with you as far as your home.”\n\n“O yes,” she answered with a jaded gait. “Walk wi’ me if you will! I do bear in mind that you came to marry me before you knew o’ my state. Perhaps—perhaps you are a little better and kinder than I have been thinking you were. Whatever is meant as kindness I am grateful for; whatever is meant in any other way I am angered at. I cannot sense your meaning sometimes.”\n\n“If I cannot legitimize our former relations at least I can assist you. And I will do it with much more regard for your feelings than I formerly showed. My religious mania, or whatever it was, is over. But I retain a little good nature; I hope I do. Now, Tess, by all that’s tender and strong between man and woman, trust me! I have enough and more than enough to put you out of anxiety, both for yourself and your parents and sisters. I can make them all comfortable if you will only show confidence in me.”\n\n“Have you seen ’em lately?” she quickly inquired.\n\n“Yes. They didn’t know where you were. It was only by chance that I found you here.”\n\nThe cold moon looked aslant upon Tess’s fagged face between the twigs of the garden-hedge as she paused outside the cottage which was her temporary home, d’Urberville pausing beside her.\n\n“Don’t mention my little brothers and sisters—don’t make me break down quite!” she said. “If you want to help them—God knows they need it—do it without telling me. But no, no!” she cried. “I will take nothing from you, either for them or for me!”\n\nHe did not accompany her further, since, as she lived with the household, all was public indoors. No sooner had she herself entered, laved herself in a washing-tub, and shared supper with the family than she fell into thought, and withdrawing to the table under the wall, by the light of her own little lamp wrote in a passionate mood—\n\nMy own Husband,\n\nLet me call you so—I must—even if it makes you angry to think of such an unworthy wife as I. I must cry to you in my trouble—I have no one else! I am so exposed to temptation, Angel. I fear to say who it is, and I do not like to write about it at all. But I cling to you in a way you cannot think! Can you not come to me now, at once, before anything terrible happens? O, I know you cannot, because you are so far away! I think I must die if you do not come soon, or tell me to come to you. The punishment you have measured out to me is deserved—I do know that—well deserved—and you are right and just to be angry with me. But, Angel, please, please, not to be just—only a little kind to me, even if I do not deserve it, and come to me! If you would come, I could die in your arms! I would be well content to do that if so be you had forgiven me!\nAngel, I live entirely for you. I love you too much to blame you for going away, and I know it was necessary you should find a farm. Do not think I shall say a word of sting or bitterness. Only come back to me. I am desolate without you, my darling, O, so desolate! I do not mind having to work: but if you will send me one little line, and say, “_I am coming soon_”, I will bide on, Angel—O, so cheerfully!\n\nIt has been so much my religion ever since we were married to be faithful to you in every thought and look, that even when a man speaks a compliment to me before I am aware, it seems wronging you. Have you never felt one little bit of what you used to feel when we were at the dairy? If you have, how can you keep away from me? I am the same woman, Angel, as you fell in love with; yes, the very same!—not the one you disliked but never saw. What was the past to me as soon as I met you? It was a dead thing altogether. I became another woman, filled full of new life from you. How could I be the early one? Why do you not see this? Dear, if you would only be a little more conceited, and believe in yourself so far as to see that you were strong enough to work this change in me, you would perhaps be in a mind to come to me, your poor wife.\n\nHow silly I was in my happiness when I thought I could trust you always to love me! I ought to have known that such as that was not for poor me. But I am sick at heart, not only for old times, but for the present. Think—think how it do hurt my heart not to see you ever—ever! Ah, if I could only make your dear heart ache one little minute of each day as mine does every day and all day long, it might lead you to show pity to your poor lonely one.\n\nPeople still say that I am rather pretty, Angel (handsome is the word they use, since I wish to be truthful). Perhaps I am what they say. But I do not value my good looks; I only like to have them because they belong to you, my dear, and that there may be at least one thing about me worth your having. So much have I felt this, that when I met with annoyance on account of the same, I tied up my face in a bandage as long as people would believe in it. O Angel, I tell you all this not from vanity—you will certainly know I do not—but only that you may come to me!\n\nIf you really cannot come to me, will you let me come to you? I am, as I say, worried, pressed to do what I will not do. It cannot be that I shall yield one inch, yet I am in terror as to what an accident might lead to, and I so defenceless on account of my first error. I cannot say more about this—it makes me too miserable. But if I break down by falling into some fearful snare, my last state will be worse than my first. O God, I cannot think of it! Let me come at once, or at once come to me!\n\nI would be content, ay, glad, to live with you as your servant, if I may not as your wife; so that I could only be near you, and get glimpses of you, and think of you as mine.\n\nThe daylight has nothing to show me, since you are not here, and I don’t like to see the rooks and starlings in the field, because I grieve and grieve to miss you who used to see them with me. I long for only one thing in heaven or earth or under the earth, to meet you, my own dear! Come to me—come to me, and save me from what threatens me!—\n\nYour faithful heartbroken Tess\n\n# XLIX\n\nThe appeal duly found its way to the breakfast-table of the quiet Vicarage to the westward, in that valley where the air is so soft and the soil so rich that the effort of growth requires but superficial aid by comparison with the tillage at Flintcomb-Ash, and where to Tess the human world seemed so different (though it was much the same). It was purely for security that she had been requested by Angel to send her communications through his father, whom he kept pretty well informed of his changing addresses in the country he had gone to exploit for himself with a heavy heart.\n\n“Now,” said old Mr Clare to his wife, when he had read the envelope, “if Angel proposes leaving Rio for a visit home at the end of next month, as he told us that he hoped to do, I think this may hasten his plans; for I believe it to be from his wife.” He breathed deeply at the thought of her; and the letter was redirected to be promptly sent on to Angel.\n\n“Dear fellow, I hope he will get home safely,” murmured Mrs Clare. “To my dying day I shall feel that he has been ill-used. You should have sent him to Cambridge in spite of his want of faith and given him the same chance as the other boys had. He would have grown out of it under proper influence, and perhaps would have taken Orders after all. Church or no Church, it would have been fairer to him.”\n\nThis was the only wail with which Mrs Clare ever disturbed her husband’s peace in respect to their sons. And she did not vent this often; for she was as considerate as she was devout, and knew that his mind too was troubled by doubts as to his justice in this matter. Only too often had she heard him lying awake at night, stifling sighs for Angel with prayers. But the uncompromising Evangelical did not even now hold that he would have been justified in giving his son, an unbeliever, the same academic advantages that he had given to the two others, when it was possible, if not probable, that those very advantages might have been used to decry the doctrines which he had made it his life’s mission and desire to propagate, and the mission of his ordained sons likewise. To put with one hand a pedestal under the feet of the two faithful ones, and with the other to exalt the unfaithful by the same artificial means, he deemed to be alike inconsistent with his convictions, his position, and his hopes. Nevertheless, he loved his misnamed Angel, and in secret mourned over this treatment of him as Abraham might have mourned over the doomed Isaac while they went up the hill together. His silent self-generated regrets were far bitterer than the reproaches which his wife rendered audible.\n\nThey blamed themselves for this unlucky marriage. If Angel had never been destined for a farmer he would never have been thrown with agricultural girls. They did not distinctly know what had separated him and his wife, nor the date on which the separation had taken place. At first they had supposed it must be something of the nature of a serious aversion. But in his later letters he occasionally alluded to the intention of coming home to fetch her; from which expressions they hoped the division might not owe its origin to anything so hopelessly permanent as that. He had told them that she was with her relatives, and in their doubts they had decided not to intrude into a situation which they knew no way of bettering.\n\nThe eyes for which Tess’s letter was intended were gazing at this time on a limitless expanse of country from the back of a mule which was bearing him from the interior of the South-American Continent towards the coast. His experiences of this strange land had been sad. The severe illness from which he had suffered shortly after his arrival had never wholly left him, and he had by degrees almost decided to relinquish his hope of farming here, though, as long as the bare possibility existed of his remaining, he kept this change of view a secret from his parents.\n\nThe crowds of agricultural labourers who had come out to the country in his wake, dazzled by representations of easy independence, had suffered, died, and wasted away. He would see mothers from English farms trudging along with their infants in their arms, when the child would be stricken with fever and would die; the mother would pause to dig a hole in the loose earth with her bare hands, would bury the babe therein with the same natural grave-tools, shed one tear, and again trudge on.\n\nAngel’s original intention had not been emigration to Brazil but a northern or eastern farm in his own country. He had come to this place in a fit of desperation, the Brazil movement among the English agriculturists having by chance coincided with his desire to escape from his past existence.\n\nDuring this time of absence he had mentally aged a dozen years. What arrested him now as of value in life was less its beauty than its pathos. Having long discredited the old systems of mysticism, he now began to discredit the old appraisements of morality. He thought they wanted readjusting. Who was the moral man? Still more pertinently, who was the moral woman? The beauty or ugliness of a character lay not only in its achievements, but in its aims and impulses; its true history lay, not among things done, but among things willed.\n\nHow, then, about Tess?\n\nViewing her in these lights, a regret for his hasty judgement began to oppress him. Did he reject her eternally, or did he not? He could no longer say that he would always reject her, and not to say that was in spirit to accept her now.\n\nThis growing fondness for her memory coincided in point of time with her residence at Flintcomb-Ash, but it was before she had felt herself at liberty to trouble him with a word about her circumstances or her feelings. He was greatly perplexed; and in his perplexity as to her motives in withholding intelligence, he did not inquire. Thus her silence of docility was misinterpreted. How much it really said if he had understood!—that she adhered with literal exactness to orders which he had given and forgotten; that despite her natural fearlessness she asserted no rights, admitted his judgement to be in every respect the true one, and bent her head dumbly thereto.\n\nIn the before-mentioned journey by mules through the interior of the country, another man rode beside him. Angel’s companion was also an Englishman, bent on the same errand, though he came from another part of the island. They were both in a state of mental depression, and they spoke of home affairs. Confidence begat confidence. With that curious tendency evinced by men, more especially when in distant lands, to entrust to strangers details of their lives which they would on no account mention to friends, Angel admitted to this man as they rode along the sorrowful facts of his marriage.\n\nThe stranger had sojourned in many more lands and among many more peoples than Angel; to his cosmopolitan mind such deviations from the social norm, so immense to domesticity, were no more than are the irregularities of vale and mountain-chain to the whole terrestrial curve. He viewed the matter in quite a different light from Angel; thought that what Tess had been was of no importance beside what she would be, and plainly told Clare that he was wrong in coming away from her.\n\nThe next day they were drenched in a thunder-storm. Angel’s companion was struck down with fever, and died by the week’s end. Clare waited a few hours to bury him, and then went on his way.\n\nThe cursory remarks of the large-minded stranger, of whom he knew absolutely nothing beyond a commonplace name, were sublimed by his death, and influenced Clare more than all the reasoned ethics of the philosophers. His own parochialism made him ashamed by its contrast. His inconsistencies rushed upon him in a flood. He had persistently elevated Hellenic Paganism at the expense of Christianity; yet in that civilization an illegal surrender was not certain disesteem. Surely then he might have regarded that abhorrence of the un-intact state, which he had inherited with the creed of mysticism, as at least open to correction when the result was due to treachery. A remorse struck into him. The words of Izz Huett, never quite stilled in his memory, came back to him. He had asked Izz if she loved him, and she had replied in the affirmative. Did she love him more than Tess did? No, she had replied; Tess would lay down her life for him, and she herself could do no more.\n\nHe thought of Tess as she had appeared on the day of the wedding. How her eyes had lingered upon him; how she had hung upon his words as if they were a god’s! And during the terrible evening over the hearth, when her simple soul uncovered itself to his, how pitiful her face had looked by the rays of the fire, in her inability to realize that his love and protection could possibly be withdrawn.\n\nThus from being her critic he grew to be her advocate. Cynical things he had uttered to himself about her; but no man can be always a cynic and live; and he withdrew them. The mistake of expressing them had arisen from his allowing himself to be influenced by general principles to the disregard of the particular instance.\n\nBut the reasoning is somewhat musty; lovers and husbands have gone over the ground before to-day. Clare had been harsh towards her; there is no doubt of it. Men are too often harsh with women they love or have loved; women with men. And yet these harshnesses are tenderness itself when compared with the universal harshness out of which they grow; the harshness of the position towards the temperament, of the means towards the aims, of to-day towards yesterday, of hereafter towards to-day.\n\nThe historic interest of her family—that masterful line of d’Urbervilles—whom he had despised as a spent force, touched his sentiments now. Why had he not known the difference between the political value and the imaginative value of these things? In the latter aspect her d’Urberville descent was a fact of great dimensions; worthless to economics, it was a most useful ingredient to the dreamer, to the moralizer on declines and falls. It was a fact that would soon be forgotten—that bit of distinction in poor Tess’s blood and name, and oblivion would fall upon her hereditary link with the marble monuments and leaded skeletons at Kingsbere. So does Time ruthlessly destroy his own romances. In recalling her face again and again, he thought now that he could see therein a flash of the dignity which must have graced her grand-dames; and the vision sent that _aura_ through his veins which he had formerly felt, and which left behind it a sense of sickness.\n\nDespite her not inviolate past, what still abode in such a woman as Tess outvalued the freshness of her fellows. Was not the gleaning of the grapes of Ephraim better than the vintage of Abi-ezer?\n\nSo spoke love renascent, preparing the way for Tess’s devoted outpouring, which was then just being forwarded to him by his father; though owing to his distance inland it was to be a long time in reaching him.\n\nMeanwhile the writer’s expectation that Angel would come in response to the entreaty was alternately great and small. What lessened it was that the facts of her life which had led to the parting had not changed—could never change; and that, if her presence had not attenuated them, her absence could not. Nevertheless she addressed her mind to the tender question of what she could do to please him best if he should arrive. Sighs were expended on the wish that she had taken more notice of the tunes he played on his harp, that she had inquired more curiously of him which were his favourite ballads among those the country-girls sang. She indirectly inquired of Amby Seedling, who had followed Izz from Talbothays, and by chance Amby remembered that, amongst the snatches of melody in which they had indulged at the dairyman’s, to induce the cows to let down their milk, Clare had seemed to like “Cupid’s Gardens”, “I have parks, I have hounds”, and “The break o’ the day”; and had seemed not to care for “The Tailor’s Breeches” and “Such a beauty I did grow”, excellent ditties as they were.\n\nTo perfect the ballads was now her whimsical desire. She practised them privately at odd moments, especially “The break o’ the day”:\n\nArise, arise, arise! And pick your love a posy, All o’ the sweetest flowers That in the garden grow. The turtle doves and sma’ birds In every bough a-building, So early in the May-time At the break o’ the day!\n\nIt would have melted the heart of a stone to hear her singing these ditties whenever she worked apart from the rest of the girls in this cold dry time; the tears running down her cheeks all the while at the thought that perhaps he would not, after all, come to hear her, and the simple silly words of the songs resounding in painful mockery of the aching heart of the singer.\n\nTess was so wrapt up in this fanciful dream that she seemed not to know how the season was advancing; that the days had lengthened, that Lady-Day was at hand, and would soon be followed by Old Lady-Day, the end of her term here.\n\nBut before the quarter-day had quite come, something happened which made Tess think of far different matters. She was at her lodging as usual one evening, sitting in the downstairs room with the rest of the family, when somebody knocked at the door and inquired for Tess. Through the doorway she saw against the declining light a figure with the height of a woman and the breadth of a child, a tall, thin, girlish creature whom she did not recognize in the twilight till the girl said “Tess!”\n\n“What—is it ’Liza-Lu?” asked Tess, in startled accents. Her sister, whom a little over a year ago she had left at home as a child, had sprung up by a sudden shoot to a form of this presentation, of which as yet Lu seemed herself scarce able to understand the meaning. Her thin legs, visible below her once-long frock, now short by her growing, and her uncomfortable hands and arms revealed her youth and inexperience.\n\n“Yes, I have been traipsing about all day, Tess,” said Lu, with unemotional gravity, “a-trying to find ’ee; and I’m very tired.”\n\n“What is the matter at home?”\n\n“Mother is took very bad, and the doctor says she’s dying, and as father is not very well neither, and says ’tis wrong for a man of such a high family as his to slave and drave at common labouring work, we don’t know what to do.”\n\nTess stood in reverie a long time before she thought of asking ’Liza-Lu to come in and sit down. When she had done so, and ’Liza-Lu was having some tea, she came to a decision. It was imperative that she should go home. Her agreement did not end till Old Lady-Day, the sixth of April, but as the interval thereto was not a long one she resolved to run the risk of starting at once.\n\nTo go that night would be a gain of twelve-hours; but her sister was too tired to undertake such a distance till the morrow. Tess ran down to where Marian and Izz lived, informed them of what had happened, and begged them to make the best of her case to the farmer. Returning, she got Lu a supper, and after that, having tucked the younger into her own bed, packed up as many of her belongings as would go into a withy basket, and started, directing Lu to follow her next morning.\n\n# L\n\nShe plunged into the chilly equinoctial darkness as the clock struck ten, for her fifteen miles’ walk under the steely stars. In lonely districts night is a protection rather than a danger to a noiseless pedestrian, and knowing this, Tess pursued the nearest course along by-lanes that she would almost have feared in the day-time; but marauders were wanting now, and spectral fears were driven out of her mind by thoughts of her mother. Thus she proceeded mile after mile, ascending and descending till she came to Bulbarrow, and about midnight looked from that height into the abyss of chaotic shade which was all that revealed itself of the vale on whose further side she was born. Having already traversed about five miles on the upland, she had now some ten or eleven in the lowland before her journey would be finished. The winding road downwards became just visible to her under the wan starlight as she followed it, and soon she paced a soil so contrasting with that above it that the difference was perceptible to the tread and to the smell. It was the heavy clay land of Blackmoor Vale, and a part of the Vale to which turnpike-roads had never penetrated. Superstitions linger longest on these heavy soils. Having once been forest, at this shadowy time it seemed to assert something of its old character, the far and the near being blended, and every tree and tall hedge making the most of its presence. The harts that had been hunted here, the witches that had been pricked and ducked, the green-spangled fairies that “whickered” at you as you passed;—the place teemed with beliefs in them still, and they formed an impish multitude now.\n\nAt Nuttlebury she passed the village inn, whose sign creaked in response to the greeting of her footsteps, which not a human soul heard but herself. Under the thatched roofs her mind’s eye beheld relaxed tendons and flaccid muscles, spread out in the darkness beneath coverlets made of little purple patchwork squares, and undergoing a bracing process at the hands of sleep for renewed labour on the morrow, as soon as a hint of pink nebulosity appeared on Hambledon Hill.\n\nAt three she turned the last corner of the maze of lanes she had threaded, and entered Marlott, passing the field in which as a club-girl she had first seen Angel Clare, when he had not danced with her; the sense of disappointment remained with her yet. In the direction of her mother’s house she saw a light. It came from the bedroom window, and a branch waved in front of it and made it wink at her. As soon as she could discern the outline of the house—newly thatched with her money—it had all its old effect upon Tess’s imagination. Part of her body and life it ever seemed to be; the slope of its dormers, the finish of its gables, the broken courses of brick which topped the chimney, all had something in common with her personal character. A stupefaction had come into these features, to her regard; it meant the illness of her mother.\n\nShe opened the door so softly as to disturb nobody; the lower room was vacant, but the neighbour who was sitting up with her mother came to the top of the stairs, and whispered that Mrs Durbeyfield was no better, though she was sleeping just then. Tess prepared herself a breakfast, and then took her place as nurse in her mother’s chamber.\n\nIn the morning, when she contemplated the children, they had all a curiously elongated look; although she had been away little more than a year, their growth was astounding; and the necessity of applying herself heart and soul to their needs took her out of her own cares.\nHer father’s ill-health was the same indefinite kind, and he sat in his chair as usual. But the day after her arrival he was unusually bright. He had a rational scheme for living, and Tess asked him what it was.\n\n“I’m thinking of sending round to all the old antiqueerians in this part of England,” he said, “asking them to subscribe to a fund to maintain me. I’m sure they’d see it as a romantical, artistical, and proper thing to do. They spend lots o’ money in keeping up old ruins, and finding the bones o’ things, and such like; and living remains must be more interesting to ’em still, if they only knowed of me. Would that somebody would go round and tell ’em what there is living among ’em, and they thinking nothing of him! If Pa’son Tringham, who discovered me, had lived, he’d ha’ done it, I’m sure.”\n\nTess postponed her arguments on this high project till she had grappled with pressing matters in hand, which seemed little improved by her remittances. When indoor necessities had been eased, she turned her attention to external things. It was now the season for planting and sowing; many gardens and allotments of the villagers had already received their spring tillage; but the garden and the allotment of the Durbeyfields were behindhand. She found, to her dismay, that this was owing to their having eaten all the seed potatoes,—that last lapse of the improvident. At the earliest moment she obtained what others she could procure, and in a few days her father was well enough to see to the garden, under Tess’s persuasive efforts: while she herself undertook the allotment-plot which they rented in a field a couple of hundred yards out of the village.\n\nShe liked doing it after the confinement of the sick chamber, where she was not now required by reason of her mother’s improvement. Violent motion relieved thought. The plot of ground was in a high, dry, open enclosure, where there were forty or fifty such pieces, and where labour was at its briskest when the hired labour of the day had ended. Digging began usually at six o’clock and extended indefinitely into the dusk or moonlight. Just now heaps of dead weeds and refuse were burning on many of the plots, the dry weather favouring their combustion.\n\nOne fine day Tess and ’Liza-Lu worked on here with their neighbours till the last rays of the sun smote flat upon the white pegs that divided the plots. As soon as twilight succeeded to sunset the flare of the couch-grass and cabbage-stalk fires began to light up the allotments fitfully, their outlines appearing and disappearing under the dense smoke as wafted by the wind. When a fire glowed, banks of smoke, blown level along the ground, would themselves become illuminated to an opaque lustre, screening the workpeople from one another; and the meaning of the “pillar of a cloud”, which was a wall by day and a light by night, could be understood.\n\nAs evening thickened, some of the gardening men and women gave over for the night, but the greater number remained to get their planting done, Tess being among them, though she sent her sister home. It was on one of the couch-burning plots that she laboured with her fork, its four shining prongs resounding against the stones and dry clods in little clicks. Sometimes she was completely involved in the smoke of her fire; then it would leave her figure free, irradiated by the brassy glare from the heap. She was oddly dressed to-night, and presented a somewhat staring aspect, her attire being a gown bleached by many washings, with a short black jacket over it, the effect of the whole being that of a wedding and funeral guest in one. The women further back wore white aprons, which, with their pale faces, were all that could be seen of them in the gloom, except when at moments they caught a flash from the flames.\n\nWestward, the wiry boughs of the bare thorn hedge which formed the boundary of the field rose against the pale opalescence of the lower sky. Above, Jupiter hung like a full-blown jonquil, so bright as almost to throw a shade. A few small nondescript stars were appearing elsewhere. In the distance a dog barked, and wheels occasionally rattled along the dry road.\n\nStill the prongs continued to click assiduously, for it was not late; and though the air was fresh and keen there was a whisper of spring in it that cheered the workers on. Something in the place, the hours, the crackling fires, the fantastic mysteries of light and shade, made others as well as Tess enjoy being there. Nightfall, which in the frost of winter comes as a fiend and in the warmth of summer as a lover, came as a tranquillizer on this March day.\n\nNobody looked at his or her companions. The eyes of all were on the soil as its turned surface was revealed by the fires. Hence as Tess stirred the clods and sang her foolish little songs with scarce now a hope that Clare would ever hear them, she did not for a long time notice the person who worked nearest to her—a man in a long smockfrock who, she found, was forking the same plot as herself, and whom she supposed her father had sent there to advance the work. She became more conscious of him when the direction of his digging brought him closer. Sometimes the smoke divided them; then it swerved, and the two were visible to each other but divided from all the rest.\n\nTess did not speak to her fellow-worker, nor did he speak to her. Nor did she think of him further than to recollect that he had not been there when it was broad daylight, and that she did not know him as any one of the Marlott labourers, which was no wonder, her absences having been so long and frequent of late years. By-and-by he dug so close to her that the fire-beams were reflected as distinctly from the steel prongs of his fork as from her own. On going up to the fire to throw a pitch of dead weeds upon it, she found that he did the same on the other side. The fire flared up, and she beheld the face of d’Urberville.\n\nThe unexpectedness of his presence, the grotesqueness of his appearance in a gathered smockfrock, such as was now worn only by the most old-fashioned of the labourers, had a ghastly comicality that chilled her as to its bearing. D’Urberville emitted a low, long laugh.\n\n“If I were inclined to joke, I should say, How much this seems like Paradise!” he remarked whimsically, looking at her with an inclined head.\n\n“What do you say?” she weakly asked.\n\n“A jester might say this is just like Paradise. You are Eve, and I am the old Other One come to tempt you in the disguise of an inferior animal. I used to be quite up in that scene of Milton’s when I was theological. Some of it goes—\n\n‘Empress, the way is ready, and not long, Beyond a row of myrtles.... ... If thou accept My conduct, I can bring thee thither soon.’ ‘Lead then,’ said Eve.\n\n“And so on. My dear Tess, I am only putting this to you as a thing that you might have supposed or said quite untruly, because you think so badly of me.”\n\n“I never said you were Satan, or thought it. I don’t think of you in that way at all. My thoughts of you are quite cold, except when you affront me. What, did you come digging here entirely because of me?”\n\n“Entirely. To see you; nothing more. The smockfrock, which I saw hanging for sale as I came along, was an afterthought, that I mightn’t be noticed. I come to protest against your working like this.”\n\n“But I like doing it—it is for my father.”\n\n“Your engagement at the other place is ended?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Where are you going to next? To join your dear husband?”\n\nShe could not bear the humiliating reminder.\n\n“O—I don’t know!” she said bitterly. “I have no husband!”\n\n“It is quite true—in the sense you mean. But you have a friend, and I have determined that you shall be comfortable in spite of yourself. When you get down to your house you will see what I have sent there for you.”\n\n“O, Alec, I wish you wouldn’t give me anything at all! I cannot take it from you! I don’t like—it is not right!”\n\n“It _is_ right!” he cried lightly. “I am not going to see a woman whom I feel so tenderly for as I do for you in trouble without trying to help her.”\n\n“But I am very well off! I am only in trouble about—about—not about living at all!”\n\nShe turned, and desperately resumed her digging, tears dripping upon the fork-handle and upon the clods.\n\n“About the children—your brothers and sisters,” he resumed. “I’ve been thinking of them.”\n\nTess’s heart quivered—he was touching her in a weak place. He had divined her chief anxiety. Since returning home her soul had gone out to those children with an affection that was passionate.\n\n“If your mother does not recover, somebody ought to do something for them; since your father will not be able to do much, I suppose?”\n\n“He can with my assistance. He must!”\n\n“And with mine.”\n\n“No, sir!”\n\n“How damned foolish this is!” burst out d’Urberville. “Why, he thinks we are the same family; and will be quite satisfied!”\n\n“He don’t. I’ve undeceived him.”\n\n“The more fool you!”\n\nD’Urberville in anger retreated from her to the hedge, where he pulled off the long smockfrock which had disguised him; and rolling it up and pushing it into the couch-fire, went away.\n\nTess could not get on with her digging after this; she felt restless; she wondered if he had gone back to her father’s house; and taking the fork in her hand proceeded homewards.\n\nSome twenty yards from the house she was met by one of her sisters.\n\n“O, Tessy—what do you think! ’Liza-Lu is a-crying, and there’s a lot of folk in the house, and mother is a good deal better, but they think father is dead!”\n\nThe child realized the grandeur of the news; but not as yet its sadness, and stood looking at Tess with round-eyed importance till, beholding the effect produced upon her, she said—\n\n“What, Tess, shan’t we talk to father never no more?”\n\n“But father was only a little bit ill!” exclaimed Tess distractedly.\n\n’Liza-Lu came up.\n\n“He dropped down just now, and the doctor who was there for mother said there was no chance for him, because his heart was growed in.”\n\nYes; the Durbeyfield couple had changed places; the dying one was out of danger, and the indisposed one was dead. The news meant even more than it sounded. Her father’s life had a value apart from his personal achievements, or perhaps it would not have had much. It was the last of the three lives for whose duration the house and premises were held under a lease; and it had long been coveted by the tenant-farmer for his regular labourers, who were stinted in cottage accommodation. Moreover, “liviers” were disapproved of in villages almost as much as little freeholders, because of their independence of manner, and when a lease determined it was never renewed.\n\nThus the Durbeyfields, once d’Urbervilles, saw descending upon them the destiny which, no doubt, when they were among the Olympians of the county, they had caused to descend many a time, and severely enough, upon the heads of such landless ones as they themselves were now. So do flux and reflux—the rhythm of change—alternate and persist in everything under the sky.\n\n# LI\n\nAt length it was the eve of Old Lady-Day, and the agricultural world was in a fever of mobility such as only occurs at that particular date of the year. It is a day of fulfilment; agreements for outdoor service during the ensuing year, entered into at Candlemas, are to be now carried out. The labourers—or “work-folk”, as they used to call themselves immemorially till the other word was introduced from without—who wish to remain no longer in old places are removing to the new farms.\n\nThese annual migrations from farm to farm were on the increase here. When Tess’s mother was a child the majority of the field-folk about Marlott had remained all their lives on one farm, which had been the home also of their fathers and grandfathers; but latterly the desire for yearly removal had risen to a high pitch. With the younger families it was a pleasant excitement which might possibly be an advantage. The Egypt of one family was the Land of Promise to the family who saw it from a distance, till by residence there it became in turn their Egypt also; and so they changed and changed.\n\nHowever, all the mutations so increasingly discernible in village life did not originate entirely in the agricultural unrest. A depopulation was also going on. The village had formerly contained, side by side with the argicultural labourers, an interesting and better-informed class, ranking distinctly above the former—the class to which Tess’s father and mother had belonged—and including the carpenter, the smith, the shoemaker, the huckster, together with nondescript workers other than farm-labourers; a set of people who owed a certain stability of aim and conduct to the fact of their being lifeholders like Tess’s father, or copyholders, or occasionally, small freeholders. But as the long holdings fell in, they were seldom again let to similar tenants, and were mostly pulled down, if not absolutely required by the farmer for his hands. Cottagers who were not directly employed on the land were looked upon with disfavour, and the banishment of some starved the trade of others, who were thus obliged to follow. These families, who had formed the backbone of the village life in the past, who were the depositaries of the village traditions, had to seek refuge in the large centres; the process, humorously designated by statisticians as “the tendency of the rural population towards the large towns”, being really the tendency of water to flow uphill when forced by machinery.\n\nThe cottage accommodation at Marlott having been in this manner considerably curtailed by demolitions, every house which remained standing was required by the agriculturist for his work-people. Ever since the occurrence of the event which had cast such a shadow over Tess’s life, the Durbeyfield family (whose descent was not credited) had been tacitly looked on as one which would have to go when their lease ended, if only in the interests of morality. It was, indeed, quite true that the household had not been shining examples either of temperance, soberness, or chastity. The father, and even the mother, had got drunk at times, the younger children seldom had gone to church, and the eldest daughter had made queer unions. By some means the village had to be kept pure. So on this, the first Lady-Day on which the Durbeyfields were expellable, the house, being roomy, was required for a carter with a large family; and Widow Joan, her daughters Tess and ’Liza-Lu, the boy Abraham, and the younger children had to go elsewhere.\n\nOn the evening preceding their removal it was getting dark betimes by reason of a drizzling rain which blurred the sky. As it was the last night they would spend in the village which had been their home and birthplace, Mrs Durbeyfield, ’Liza-Lu, and Abraham had gone out to bid some friends goodbye, and Tess was keeping house till they should return.\n\nShe was kneeling in the window-bench, her face close to the casement, where an outer pane of rain-water was sliding down the inner pane of glass. Her eyes rested on the web of a spider, probably starved long ago, which had been mistakenly placed in a corner where no flies ever came, and shivered in the slight draught through the casement. Tess was reflecting on the position of the household, in which she perceived her own evil influence. Had she not come home, her mother and the children might probably have been allowed to stay on as weekly tenants. But she had been observed almost immediately on her return by some people of scrupulous character and great influence: they had seen her idling in the churchyard, restoring as well as she could with a little trowel a baby’s obliterated grave. By this means they had found that she was living here again; her mother was scolded for “harbouring” her; sharp retorts had ensued from Joan, who had independently offered to leave at once; she had been taken at her word; and here was the result.\n\n“I ought never to have come home,” said Tess to herself, bitterly.\n\nShe was so intent upon these thoughts that she hardly at first took note of a man in a white mackintosh whom she saw riding down the street. Possibly it was owing to her face being near to the pane that he saw her so quickly, and directed his horse so close to the cottage-front that his hoofs were almost upon the narrow border for plants growing under the wall. It was not till he touched the window with his riding-crop that she observed him. The rain had nearly ceased, and she opened the casement in obedience to his gesture.\n\n“Didn’t you see me?” asked d’Urberville.\n\n“I was not attending,” she said. “I heard you, I believe, though I fancied it was a carriage and horses. I was in a sort of dream.”\n\n“Ah! you heard the d’Urberville Coach, perhaps. You know the legend, I suppose?”\n\n“No. My—somebody was going to tell it me once, but didn’t.”\n\n“If you are a genuine d’Urberville I ought not to tell you either, I suppose. As for me, I’m a sham one, so it doesn’t matter. It is rather dismal. It is that this sound of a non-existent coach can only be heard by one of d’Urberville blood, and it is held to be of ill-omen to the one who hears it. It has to do with a murder, committed by one of the family, centuries ago.”\n\n“Now you have begun it, finish it.”\n\n“Very well. One of the family is said to have abducted some beautiful woman, who tried to escape from the coach in which he was carrying her off, and in the struggle he killed her—or she killed him—I forget which. Such is one version of the tale... I see that your tubs and buckets are packed. Going away, aren’t you?”\n\n“Yes, to-morrow—Old Lady Day.”\n\n“I heard you were, but could hardly believe it; it seems so sudden. Why is it?”\n\n“Father’s was the last life on the property, and when that dropped we had no further right to stay. Though we might, perhaps, have stayed as weekly tenants—if it had not been for me.”\n\n“What about you?”\n\n“I am not a—proper woman.”\n\nD’Urberville’s face flushed.\n\n“What a blasted shame! Miserable snobs! May their dirty souls be burnt to cinders!” he exclaimed in tones of ironic resentment. “That’s why you are going, is it? Turned out?”\n\n“We are not turned out exactly; but as they said we should have to go soon, it was best to go now everybody was moving, because there are better chances.”\n\n“Where are you going to?”\n\n“Kingsbere. We have taken rooms there. Mother is so foolish about father’s people that she will go there.”\n\n“But your mother’s family are not fit for lodgings, and in a little hole of a town like that. Now why not come to my garden-house at Trantridge? There are hardly any poultry now, since my mother’s death; but there’s the house, as you know it, and the garden. It can be whitewashed in a day, and your mother can live there quite comfortably; and I will put the children to a good school. Really I ought to do something for you!”\n\n“But we have already taken the rooms at Kingsbere!” she declared. “And we can wait there—”\n\n“Wait—what for? For that nice husband, no doubt. Now look here, Tess, I know what men are, and, bearing in mind the _grounds_ of your separation, I am quite positive he will never make it up with you. Now, though I have been your enemy, I am your friend, even if you won’t believe it. Come to this cottage of mine. We’ll get up a regular colony of fowls, and your mother can attend to them excellently; and the children can go to school.”\n\nTess breathed more and more quickly, and at length she said—\n\n“How do I know that you would do all this? Your views may change—and then—we should be—my mother would be—homeless again.”\n\n“O no—no. I would guarantee you against such as that in writing, if necessary. Think it over.”\n\nTess shook her head. But d’Urberville persisted; she had seldom seen him so determined; he would not take a negative.\n\n“Please just tell your mother,” he said, in emphatic tones. “It is her business to judge—not yours. I shall get the house swept out and whitened to-morrow morning, and fires lit; and it will be dry by the evening, so that you can come straight there. Now mind, I shall expect you.”\n\nTess again shook her head, her throat swelling with complicated emotion. She could not look up at d’Urberville.\n\n“I owe you something for the past, you know,” he resumed. “And you cured me, too, of that craze; so I am glad—”\n\n“I would rather you had kept the craze, so that you had kept the practice which went with it!”\n\n“I am glad of this opportunity of repaying you a little. To-morrow I shall expect to hear your mother’s goods unloading... Give me your hand on it now—dear, beautiful Tess!”\n\nWith the last sentence he had dropped his voice to a murmur, and put his hand in at the half-open casement. With stormy eyes she pulled the stay-bar quickly, and, in doing so, caught his arm between the casement and the stone mullion.\n\n“Damnation—you are very cruel!” he said, snatching out his arm. “No, no!—I know you didn’t do it on purpose. Well, I shall expect you, or your mother and children at least.”\n\n“I shall not come—I have plenty of money!” she cried.\n\n“Where?”\n\n“At my father-in-law’s, if I ask for it.”\n\n“_If_ you ask for it. But you won’t, Tess; I know you; you’ll never ask for it—you’ll starve first!”\n\nWith these words he rode off. Just at the corner of the street he met the man with the paint-pot, who asked him if he had deserted the brethren.\n\n“You go to the devil!” said d’Urberville.\n\nTess remained where she was a long while, till a sudden rebellious sense of injustice caused the region of her eyes to swell with the rush of hot tears thither. Her husband, Angel Clare himself, had, like others, dealt out hard measure to her; surely he had! She had never before admitted such a thought; but he had surely! Never in her life—she could swear it from the bottom of her soul—had she ever intended to do wrong; yet these hard judgements had come. Whatever her sins, they were not sins of intention, but of inadvertence, and why should she have been punished so persistently?\n\nShe passionately seized the first piece of paper that came to hand, and scribbled the following lines:\n\nO why have you treated me so monstrously, Angel! I do not deserve it. I have thought it all over carefully, and I can never, never forgive you! You know that I did not intend to wrong you—why have you so wronged me? You are cruel, cruel indeed! I will try to forget you. It is all injustice I have received at your hands!\nT.\n\nShe watched till the postman passed by, ran out to him with her epistle, and then again took her listless place inside the window-panes.\n\nIt was just as well to write like that as to write tenderly. How could he give way to entreaty? The facts had not changed: there was no new event to alter his opinion.\n\nIt grew darker, the fire-light shining over the room. The two biggest of the younger children had gone out with their mother; the four smallest, their ages ranging from three-and-a-half years to eleven, all in black frocks, were gathered round the hearth babbling their own little subjects. Tess at length joined them, without lighting a candle.\n\n“This is the last night that we shall sleep here, dears, in the house where we were born,” she said quickly. “We ought to think of it, oughtn’t we?”\n\nThey all became silent; with the impressibility of their age they were ready to burst into tears at the picture of finality she had conjured up, though all the day hitherto they had been rejoicing in the idea of a new place. Tess changed the subject.\n\n“Sing to me, dears,” she said.\n\n“What shall we sing?”\n\n“Anything you know; I don’t mind.”\n\nThere was a momentary pause; it was broken, first, in one little tentative note; then a second voice strengthened it, and a third and a fourth chimed in unison, with words they had learnt at the Sunday-school—\n\nHere we suffer grief and pain, Here we meet to part again; In Heaven we part no more.\n\nThe four sang on with the phlegmatic passivity of persons who had long ago settled the question, and there being no mistake about it, felt that further thought was not required. With features strained hard to enunciate the syllables they continued to regard the centre of the flickering fire, the notes of the youngest straying over into the pauses of the rest.\n\nTess turned from them, and went to the window again. Darkness had now fallen without, but she put her face to the pane as though to peer into the gloom. It was really to hide her tears. If she could only believe what the children were singing; if she were only sure, how different all would now be; how confidently she would leave them to Providence and their future kingdom! But, in default of that, it behoved her to do something; to be their Providence; for to Tess, as to not a few millions of others, there was ghastly satire in the poet’s lines—\n\nNot in utter nakedness But trailing clouds of glory do we come.\n\nTo her and her like, birth itself was an ordeal of degrading personal compulsion, whose gratuitousness nothing in the result seemed to justify, and at best could only palliate.\n\nIn the shades of the wet road she soon discerned her mother with tall ’Liza-Lu and Abraham. Mrs Durbeyfield’s pattens clicked up to the door, and Tess opened it.\n\n“I see the tracks of a horse outside the window,” said Joan. “Hev somebody called?”\n\n“No,” said Tess.\n\nThe children by the fire looked gravely at her, and one murmured—\n\n“Why, Tess, the gentleman a-horseback!”\n\n“He didn’t call,” said Tess. “He spoke to me in passing.”\n\n“Who was the gentleman?” asked the mother. “Your husband?”\n\n“No. He’ll never, never come,” answered Tess in stony hopelessness.\n\n“Then who was it?”\n\n“Oh, you needn’t ask. You’ve seen him before, and so have I.”\n\n“Ah! What did he say?” said Joan curiously.\n\n“I will tell you when we are settled in our lodging at Kingsbere to-morrow—every word.”\n\nIt was not her husband, she had said. Yet a consciousness that in a physical sense this man alone was her husband seemed to weigh on her more and more.\n\n# LII\n\nDuring the small hours of the next morning, while it was still dark, dwellers near the highways were conscious of a disturbance of their night’s rest by rumbling noises, intermittently continuing till daylight—noises as certain to recur in this particular first week of the month as the voice of the cuckoo in the third week of the same. They were the preliminaries of the general removal, the passing of the empty waggons and teams to fetch the goods of the migrating families; for it was always by the vehicle of the farmer who required his services that the hired man was conveyed to his destination. That this might be accomplished within the day was the explanation of the reverberation occurring so soon after midnight, the aim of the carters being to reach the door of the outgoing households by six o’clock, when the loading of their movables at once began.\n\nBut to Tess and her mother’s household no such anxious farmer sent his team. They were only women; they were not regular labourers; they were not particularly required anywhere; hence they had to hire a waggon at their own expense, and got nothing sent gratuitously.\n\nIt was a relief to Tess, when she looked out of the window that morning, to find that though the weather was windy and louring, it did not rain, and that the waggon had come. A wet Lady-Day was a spectre which removing families never forgot; damp furniture, damp bedding, damp clothing accompanied it, and left a train of ills.\n\nHer mother, ’Liza-Lu, and Abraham were also awake, but the younger children were let sleep on. The four breakfasted by the thin light, and the “house-ridding” was taken in hand.\n\nIt proceeded with some cheerfulness, a friendly neighbour or two assisting. When the large articles of furniture had been packed in position, a circular nest was made of the beds and bedding, in which Joan Durbeyfield and the young children were to sit through the journey. After loading there was a long delay before the horses were brought, these having been unharnessed during the ridding; but at length, about two o’clock, the whole was under way, the cooking-pot swinging from the axle of the waggon, Mrs Durbeyfield and family at the top, the matron having in her lap, to prevent injury to its works, the head of the clock, which, at any exceptional lurch of the waggon, struck one, or one-and-a-half, in hurt tones. Tess and the next eldest girl walked alongside till they were out of the village.\n\nThey had called on a few neighbours that morning and the previous evening, and some came to see them off, all wishing them well, though, in their secret hearts, hardly expecting welfare possible to such a family, harmless as the Durbeyfields were to all except themselves. Soon the equipage began to ascend to higher ground, and the wind grew keener with the change of level and soil.\n\nThe day being the sixth of April, the Durbeyfield waggon met many other waggons with families on the summit of the load, which was built on a wellnigh unvarying principle, as peculiar, probably, to the rural labourer as the hexagon to the bee. The groundwork of the arrangement was the family dresser, which, with its shining handles, and finger-marks, and domestic evidences thick upon it, stood importantly in front, over the tails of the shaft-horses, in its erect and natural position, like some Ark of the Covenant that they were bound to carry reverently.\n\nSome of the households were lively, some mournful; some were stopping at the doors of wayside inns; where, in due time, the Durbeyfield menagerie also drew up to bait horses and refresh the travellers.\nDuring the halt Tess’s eyes fell upon a three-pint blue mug, which was ascending and descending through the air to and from the feminine section of a household, sitting on the summit of a load that had also drawn up at a little distance from the same inn. She followed one of the mug’s journeys upward, and perceived it to be clasped by hands whose owner she well knew. Tess went towards the waggon.\n\n“Marian and Izz!” she cried to the girls, for it was they, sitting with the moving family at whose house they had lodged. “Are you house-ridding to-day, like everybody else?”\n\nThey were, they said. It had been too rough a life for them at Flintcomb-Ash, and they had come away, almost without notice, leaving Groby to prosecute them if he chose. They told Tess their destination, and Tess told them hers.\n\nMarian leant over the load, and lowered her voice. “Do you know that the gentleman who follows ’ee—you’ll guess who I mean—came to ask for ’ee at Flintcomb after you had gone? We didn’t tell’n where you was, knowing you wouldn’t wish to see him.”\n\n“Ah—but I did see him!” Tess murmured. “He found me.”\n\n“And do he know where you be going?”\n\n“I think so.”\n\n“Husband come back?”\n\n“No.”\n\nShe bade her acquaintance goodbye—for the respective carters had now come out from the inn—and the two waggons resumed their journey in opposite directions; the vehicle whereon sat Marian, Izz, and the ploughman’s family with whom they had thrown in their lot, being brightly painted, and drawn by three powerful horses with shining brass ornaments on their harness; while the waggon on which Mrs Durbeyfield and her family rode was a creaking erection that would scarcely bear the weight of the superincumbent load; one which had known no paint since it was made, and drawn by two horses only. The contrast well marked the difference between being fetched by a thriving farmer and conveying oneself whither no hirer waited one’s coming.\n\nThe distance was great—too great for a day’s journey—and it was with the utmost difficulty that the horses performed it. Though they had started so early, it was quite late in the afternoon when they turned the flank of an eminence which formed part of the upland called Greenhill. While the horses stood to stale and breathe themselves Tess looked around. Under the hill, and just ahead of them, was the half-dead townlet of their pilgrimage, Kingsbere, where lay those ancestors of whom her father had spoken and sung to painfulness: Kingsbere, the spot of all spots in the world which could be considered the d’Urbervilles’ home, since they had resided there for full five hundred years.\n\nA man could be seen advancing from the outskirts towards them, and when he beheld the nature of their waggon-load he quickened his steps.\n\n“You be the woman they call Mrs Durbeyfield, I reckon?” he said to Tess’s mother, who had descended to walk the remainder of the way.\n\nShe nodded. “Though widow of the late Sir John d’Urberville, poor nobleman, if I cared for my rights; and returning to the domain of his forefathers.”\n\n“Oh? Well, I know nothing about that; but if you be Mrs Durbeyfield, I am sent to tell ’ee that the rooms you wanted be let. We didn’t know that you was coming till we got your letter this morning—when ’twas too late. But no doubt you can get other lodgings somewhere.”\n\nThe man had noticed the face of Tess, which had become ash-pale at his intelligence. Her mother looked hopelessly at fault. “What shall we do now, Tess?” she said bitterly. “Here’s a welcome to your ancestors’ lands! However, let’s try further.”\n\nThey moved on into the town, and tried with all their might, Tess remaining with the waggon to take care of the children whilst her mother and ’Liza-Lu made inquiries. At the last return of Joan to the vehicle, an hour later, when her search for accommodation had still been fruitless, the driver of the waggon said the goods must be unloaded, as the horses were half-dead, and he was bound to return part of the way at least that night.\n\n“Very well—unload it here,” said Joan recklessly. “I’ll get shelter somewhere.”\n\nThe waggon had drawn up under the churchyard wall, in a spot screened from view, and the driver, nothing loth, soon hauled down the poor heap of household goods. This done, she paid him, reducing herself to almost her last shilling thereby, and he moved off and left them, only too glad to get out of further dealings with such a family. It was a dry night, and he guessed that they would come to no harm.\n\nTess gazed desperately at the pile of furniture. The cold sunlight of this spring evening peered invidiously upon the crocks and kettles, upon the bunches of dried herbs shivering in the breeze, upon the brass handles of the dresser, upon the wicker-cradle they had all been rocked in, and upon the well-rubbed clock-case, all of which gave out the reproachful gleam of indoor articles abandoned to the vicissitudes of a roofless exposure for which they were never made. Round about were deparked hills and slopes—now cut up into little paddocks—and the green foundations that showed where the d’Urberville mansion once had stood; also an outlying stretch of Egdon Heath that had always belonged to the estate. Hard by, the aisle of the church called the d’Urberville Aisle looked on imperturbably.\n\n“Isn’t your family vault your own freehold?” said Tess’s mother, as she returned from a reconnoitre of the church and graveyard. “Why, of course ’tis, and that’s where we will camp, girls, till the place of your ancestors finds us a roof! Now, Tess and ’Liza and Abraham, you help me. We’ll make a nest for these children, and then we’ll have another look round.”\n\nTess listlessly lent a hand, and in a quarter of an hour the old four-post bedstead was dissociated from the heap of goods, and erected under the south wall of the church, the part of the building known as the d’Urberville Aisle, beneath which the huge vaults lay. Over the tester of the bedstead was a beautiful traceried window, of many lights, its date being the fifteenth century. It was called the d’Urberville Window, and in the upper part could be discerned heraldic emblems like those on Durbeyfield’s old seal and spoon.\n\nJoan drew the curtains round the bed so as to make an excellent tent of it, and put the smaller children inside. “If it comes to the worst we can sleep there too, for one night,” she said. “But let us try further on, and get something for the dears to eat! O, Tess, what’s the use of your playing at marrying gentlemen, if it leaves us like this!”\n\nAccompanied by ’Liza-Lu and the boy, she again ascended the little lane which secluded the church from the townlet. As soon as they got into the street they beheld a man on horseback gazing up and down. “Ah—I’m looking for you!” he said, riding up to them. “This is indeed a family gathering on the historic spot!”\n\nIt was Alec d’Urberville. “Where is Tess?” he asked.\n\nPersonally Joan had no liking for Alec. She cursorily signified the direction of the church, and went on, d’Urberville saying that he would see them again, in case they should be still unsuccessful in their search for shelter, of which he had just heard. When they had gone, d’Urberville rode to the inn, and shortly after came out on foot.\n\nIn the interim Tess, left with the children inside the bedstead, remained talking with them awhile, till, seeing that no more could be done to make them comfortable just then, she walked about the churchyard, now beginning to be embrowned by the shades of nightfall. The door of the church was unfastened, and she entered it for the first time in her life.\n\nWithin the window under which the bedstead stood were the tombs of the family, covering in their dates several centuries. They were canopied, altar-shaped, and plain; their carvings being defaced and broken; their brasses torn from the matrices, the rivet-holes remaining like martin-holes in a sandcliff. Of all the reminders that she had ever received that her people were socially extinct, there was none so forcible as this spoliation.\n\nShe drew near to a dark stone on which was inscribed:\n\n# OSTIUM SEPULCHRI ANTIQUAE FAMILIAE D’URBERVILLE\n\nTess did not read Church-Latin like a Cardinal, but she knew that this was the door of her ancestral sepulchre, and that the tall knights of whom her father had chanted in his cups lay inside.\n\nShe musingly turned to withdraw, passing near an altar-tomb, the oldest of them all, on which was a recumbent figure. In the dusk she had not noticed it before, and would hardly have noticed it now but for an odd fancy that the effigy moved. As soon as she drew close to it she discovered all in a moment that the figure was a living person; and the shock to her sense of not having been alone was so violent that she was quite overcome, and sank down nigh to fainting, not, however, till she had recognized Alec d’Urberville in the form.\n\nHe leapt off the slab and supported her.\n\n“I saw you come in,” he said smiling, “and got up there not to interrupt your meditations. A family gathering, is it not, with these old fellows under us here? Listen.”\n\nHe stamped with his heel heavily on the floor; whereupon there arose a hollow echo from below.\n\n“That shook them a bit, I’ll warrant!” he continued. “And you thought I was the mere stone reproduction of one of them. But no. The old order changeth. The little finger of the sham d’Urberville can do more for you than the whole dynasty of the real underneath.... Now command me. What shall I do?”\n\n“Go away!” she murmured.\n\n“I will—I’ll look for your mother,” said he blandly. But in passing her he whispered: “Mind this; you’ll be civil yet!”\n\nWhen he was gone she bent down upon the entrance to the vaults, and said—\n\n“Why am I on the wrong side of this door!”\n\nIn the meantime Marian and Izz Huett had journeyed onward with the chattels of the ploughman in the direction of their land of Canaan—the Egypt of some other family who had left it only that morning. But the girls did not for a long time think of where they were going. Their talk was of Angel Clare and Tess, and Tess’s persistent lover, whose connection with her previous history they had partly heard and partly guessed ere this.\n\n“’Tisn’t as though she had never known him afore,” said Marian. “His having won her once makes all the difference in the world. ’Twould be a thousand pities if he were to tole her away again. Mr Clare can never be anything to us, Izz; and why should we grudge him to her, and not try to mend this quarrel? If he could on’y know what straits she’s put to, and what’s hovering round, he might come to take care of his own.”\n\n“Could we let him know?”\n\nThey thought of this all the way to their destination; but the bustle of re-establishment in their new place took up all their attention then. But when they were settled, a month later, they heard of Clare’s approaching return, though they had learnt nothing more of Tess. Upon that, agitated anew by their attachment to him, yet honourably disposed to her, Marian uncorked the penny ink-bottle they shared, and a few lines were concocted between the two girls.\nHonour’d Sir,\n\nLook to your Wife if you do love her as much as she do love you. For she is sore put to by an Enemy in the shape of a Friend. Sir, there is one near her who ought to be Away. A woman should not be try’d beyond her Strength, and continual dropping will wear away a Stone—ay, more—a Diamond.\nFrom Two Well-Wishers\n\nThis was addressed to Angel Clare at the only place they had ever heard him to be connected with, Emminster Vicarage; after which they continued in a mood of emotional exaltation at their own generosity, which made them sing in hysterical snatches and weep at the same time.\nEnd of Phase the Sixth\n\n# LIII\n\nFulfilment\nIt was evening at Emminster Vicarage. The two customary candles were burning under their green shades in the Vicar’s study, but he had not been sitting there. Occasionally he came in, stirred the small fire which sufficed for the increasing mildness of the spring, and went out again; sometimes pausing at the front door, going on to the drawing-room, then returning again to the front door.\n\nIt faced westward, and though gloom prevailed inside, there was still light enough without to see with distinctness. Mrs Clare, who had been sitting in the drawing-room, followed him hither.\n\n“Plenty of time yet,” said the Vicar. “He doesn’t reach Chalk-Newton till six, even if the train should be punctual, and ten miles of country-road, five of them in Crimmercrock Lane, are not jogged over in a hurry by our old horse.”\n\n“But he has done it in an hour with us, my dear.”\n\n“Years ago.”\n\nThus they passed the minutes, each well knowing that this was only waste of breath, the one essential being simply to wait.\n\nAt length there was a slight noise in the lane, and the old pony-chaise appeared indeed outside the railings. They saw alight therefrom a form which they affected to recognize, but would actually have passed by in the street without identifying had he not got out of their carriage at the particular moment when a particular person was due.\n\nMrs Clare rushed through the dark passage to the door, and her husband came more slowly after her.\n\nThe new arrival, who was just about to enter, saw their anxious faces in the doorway and the gleam of the west in their spectacles because they confronted the last rays of day; but they could only see his shape against the light.\n\n“O, my boy, my boy—home again at last!” cried Mrs Clare, who cared no more at that moment for the stains of heterodoxy which had caused all this separation than for the dust upon his clothes. What woman, indeed, among the most faithful adherents of the truth, believes the promises and threats of the Word in the sense in which she believes in her own children, or would not throw her theology to the wind if weighed against their happiness? As soon as they reached the room where the candles were lighted she looked at his face.\n\n“O, it is not Angel—not my son—the Angel who went away!” she cried in all the irony of sorrow, as she turned herself aside.\n\nHis father, too, was shocked to see him, so reduced was that figure from its former contours by worry and the bad season that Clare had experienced, in the climate to which he had so rashly hurried in his first aversion to the mockery of events at home. You could see the skeleton behind the man, and almost the ghost behind the skeleton. He matched Crivelli’s dead _Christus_. His sunken eye-pits were of morbid hue, and the light in his eyes had waned. The angular hollows and lines of his aged ancestors had succeeded to their reign in his face twenty years before their time.\n\n“I was ill over there, you know,” he said. “I am all right now.”\n\nAs if, however, to falsify this assertion, his legs seemed to give way, and he suddenly sat down to save himself from falling. It was only a slight attack of faintness, resulting from the tedious day’s journey, and the excitement of arrival.\n\n“Has any letter come for me lately?” he asked. “I received the last you sent on by the merest chance, and after considerable delay through being inland; or I might have come sooner.”\n\n“It was from your wife, we supposed?”\n\n“It was.”\n\nOnly one other had recently come. They had not sent it on to him, knowing he would start for home so soon.\n\nHe hastily opened the letter produced, and was much disturbed to read in Tess’s handwriting the sentiments expressed in her last hurried scrawl to him.\n\nO why have you treated me so monstrously, Angel! I do not deserve it. I have thought it all over carefully, and I can never, never forgive you! You know that I did not intend to wrong you—why have you so wronged me? You are cruel, cruel indeed! I will try to forget you. It is all injustice I have received at your hands!\n\nT.\n“It is quite true!” said Angel, throwing down the letter. “Perhaps she will never be reconciled to me!”\n\n“Don’t, Angel, be so anxious about a mere child of the soil!” said his mother.\n\n“Child of the soil! Well, we all are children of the soil. I wish she were so in the sense you mean; but let me now explain to you what I have never explained before, that her father is a descendant in the male line of one of the oldest Norman houses, like a good many others who lead obscure agricultural lives in our villages, and are dubbed ‘sons of the soil.’”\n\nHe soon retired to bed; and the next morning, feeling exceedingly unwell, he remained in his room pondering. The circumstances amid which he had left Tess were such that though, while on the south of the Equator and just in receipt of her loving epistle, it had seemed the easiest thing in the world to rush back into her arms the moment he chose to forgive her, now that he had arrived it was not so easy as it had seemed. She was passionate, and her present letter, showing that her estimate of him had changed under his delay—too justly changed, he sadly owned,—made him ask himself if it would be wise to confront her unannounced in the presence of her parents. Supposing that her love had indeed turned to dislike during the last weeks of separation, a sudden meeting might lead to bitter words.\n\nClare therefore thought it would be best to prepare Tess and her family by sending a line to Marlott announcing his return, and his hope that she was still living with them there, as he had arranged for her to do when he left England. He despatched the inquiry that very day, and before the week was out there came a short reply from Mrs Durbeyfield which did not remove his embarrassment, for it bore no address, though to his surprise it was not written from Marlott.\nSir,\n\nJ write these few lines to say that my Daughter is away from me at present, and J am not sure when she will return, but J will let you know as Soon as she do. J do not feel at liberty to tell you Where she is temperly biding. J should say that me and my Family have left Marlott for some Time.—\nYours,\n\nJ. Durbeyfield\nIt was such a relief to Clare to learn that Tess was at least apparently well that her mother’s stiff reticence as to her whereabouts did not long distress him. They were all angry with him, evidently. He would wait till Mrs Durbeyfield could inform him of Tess’s return, which her letter implied to be soon. He deserved no more. His had been a love “which alters when it alteration finds”. He had undergone some strange experiences in his absence; he had seen the virtual Faustina in the literal Cornelia, a spiritual Lucretia in a corporeal Phryne; he had thought of the woman taken and set in the midst as one deserving to be stoned, and of the wife of Uriah being made a queen; and he had asked himself why he had not judged Tess constructively rather than biographically, by the will rather than by the deed?\n\nA day or two passed while he waited at his father’s house for the promised second note from Joan Durbeyfield, and indirectly to recover a little more strength. The strength showed signs of coming back, but there was no sign of Joan’s letter. Then he hunted up the old letter sent on to him in Brazil, which Tess had written from Flintcomb-Ash, and re-read it. The sentences touched him now as much as when he had first perused them.\n\n...I must cry to you in my trouble—I have no one else!... I think I must die if you do not come soon, or tell me to come to you... please, please, not to be just—only a little kind to me.... If you would come, I could die in your arms! I would be well content to do that if so be you had forgiven me!... if you will send me one little line, and say, _I am coming soon_, I will bide on, Angel—O, so cheerfully!... think how it do hurt my heart not to see you ever—ever! Ah, if I could only make your dear heart ache one little minute of each day as mine does every day and all day long, it might lead you to show pity to your poor lonely one.... I would be content, ay, glad, to live with you as your servant, if I may not as your wife; so that I could only be near you, and get glimpses of you, and think of you as mine.... I long for only one thing in heaven or earth or under the earth, to meet you, my own dear! Come to me—come to me, and save me from what threatens me!\n\nClare determined that he would no longer believe in her more recent and severer regard of him, but would go and find her immediately. He asked his father if she had applied for any money during his absence. His father returned a negative, and then for the first time it occurred to Angel that her pride had stood in her way, and that she had suffered privation. From his remarks his parents now gathered the real reason of the separation; and their Christianity was such that, reprobates being their especial care, the tenderness towards Tess which her blood, her simplicity, even her poverty, had not engendered, was instantly excited by her sin.\n\nWhilst he was hastily packing together a few articles for his journey he glanced over a poor plain missive also lately come to hand—the one from Marian and Izz Huett, beginning—\n\n“Honour’d Sir, Look to your Wife if you do love her as much as she do love you,” and signed, “From Two Well-Wishers.”\n\n# LIV\n\nIn a quarter of an hour Clare was leaving the house, whence his mother watched his thin figure as it disappeared into the street. He had declined to borrow his father’s old mare, well knowing of its necessity to the household. He went to the inn, where he hired a trap, and could hardly wait during the harnessing. In a very few minutes after, he was driving up the hill out of the town which, three or four months earlier in the year, Tess had descended with such hopes and ascended with such shattered purposes.\n\nBenvill Lane soon stretched before him, its hedges and trees purple with buds; but he was looking at other things, and only recalled himself to the scene sufficiently to enable him to keep the way. In something less than an hour-and-a-half he had skirted the south of the King’s Hintock estates and ascended to the untoward solitude of Cross-in-Hand, the unholy stone whereon Tess had been compelled by Alec d’Urberville, in his whim of reformation, to swear the strange oath that she would never wilfully tempt him again. The pale and blasted nettle-stems of the preceding year even now lingered nakedly in the banks, young green nettles of the present spring growing from their roots.\n\nThence he went along the verge of the upland overhanging the other Hintocks, and, turning to the right, plunged into the bracing calcareous region of Flintcomb-Ash, the address from which she had written to him in one of the letters, and which he supposed to be the place of sojourn referred to by her mother. Here, of course, he did not find her; and what added to his depression was the discovery that no “Mrs Clare” had ever been heard of by the cottagers or by the farmer himself, though Tess was remembered well enough by her Christian name. His name she had obviously never used during their separation, and her dignified sense of their total severance was shown not much less by this abstention than by the hardships she had chosen to undergo (of which he now learnt for the first time) rather than apply to his father for more funds.\n\nFrom this place they told him Tess Durbeyfield had gone, without due notice, to the home of her parents on the other side of Blackmoor, and it therefore became necessary to find Mrs Durbeyfield. She had told him she was not now at Marlott, but had been curiously reticent as to her actual address, and the only course was to go to Marlott and inquire for it. The farmer who had been so churlish with Tess was quite smooth-tongued to Clare, and lent him a horse and man to drive him towards Marlott, the gig he had arrived in being sent back to Emminster; for the limit of a day’s journey with that horse was reached.\n\nClare would not accept the loan of the farmer’s vehicle for a further distance than to the outskirts of the Vale, and, sending it back with the man who had driven him, he put up at an inn, and next day entered on foot the region wherein was the spot of his dear Tess’s birth. It was as yet too early in the year for much colour to appear in the gardens and foliage; the so-called spring was but winter overlaid with a thin coat of greenness, and it was of a parcel with his expectations.\n\nThe house in which Tess had passed the years of her childhood was now inhabited by another family who had never known her. The new residents were in the garden, taking as much interest in their own doings as if the homestead had never passed its primal time in conjunction with the histories of others, beside which the histories of these were but as a tale told by an idiot. They walked about the garden paths with thoughts of their own concerns entirely uppermost, bringing their actions at every moment in jarring collision with the dim ghosts behind them, talking as though the time when Tess lived there were not one whit intenser in story than now. Even the spring birds sang over their heads as if they thought there was nobody missing in particular.\n\nOn inquiry of these precious innocents, to whom even the name of their predecessors was a failing memory, Clare learned that John Durbeyfield was dead; that his widow and children had left Marlott, declaring that they were going to live at Kingsbere, but instead of doing so had gone on to another place they mentioned. By this time Clare abhorred the house for ceasing to contain Tess, and hastened away from its hated presence without once looking back.\n\nHis way was by the field in which he had first beheld her at the dance. It was as bad as the house—even worse. He passed on through the churchyard, where, amongst the new headstones, he saw one of a somewhat superior design to the rest. The inscription ran thus:\n\nIn memory of John Durbeyfield, rightly d’Urberville, of the once powerful family of that Name, and Direct Descendant through an illustrious Line from Sir Pagan d’Urberville, one of the Knights of the Conqueror. Died March 10th, 18—\nHow are the Mighty Fallen.\n\nSome man, apparently the sexton, had observed Clare standing there, and drew nigh. “Ah, sir, now that’s a man who didn’t want to lie here, but wished to be carried to Kingsbere, where his ancestors be.”\n\n“And why didn’t they respect his wish?”\n\n“Oh—no money. Bless your soul, sir, why—there, I wouldn’t wish to say it everywhere, but—even this headstone, for all the flourish wrote upon en, is not paid for.”\n\n“Ah, who put it up?”\n\nThe man told the name of a mason in the village, and, on leaving the churchyard, Clare called at the mason’s house. He found that the statement was true, and paid the bill. This done, he turned in the direction of the migrants.\n\nThe distance was too long for a walk, but Clare felt such a strong desire for isolation that at first he would neither hire a conveyance nor go to a circuitous line of railway by which he might eventually reach the place. At Shaston, however, he found he must hire; but the way was such that he did not enter Joan’s place till about seven o’clock in the evening, having traversed a distance of over twenty miles since leaving Marlott.\n\nThe village being small he had little difficulty in finding Mrs Durbeyfield’s tenement, which was a house in a walled garden, remote from the main road, where she had stowed away her clumsy old furniture as best she could. It was plain that for some reason or other she had not wished him to visit her, and he felt his call to be somewhat of an intrusion. She came to the door herself, and the light from the evening sky fell upon her face.\n\nThis was the first time that Clare had ever met her, but he was too preoccupied to observe more than that she was still a handsome woman, in the garb of a respectable widow. He was obliged to explain that he was Tess’s husband, and his object in coming there, and he did it awkwardly enough. “I want to see her at once,” he added. “You said you would write to me again, but you have not done so.”\n\n“Because she’ve not come home,” said Joan.\n\n“Do you know if she is well?”\n\n“I don’t. But you ought to, sir,” said she.\n\n“I admit it. Where is she staying?”\n\nFrom the beginning of the interview Joan had disclosed her embarrassment by keeping her hand to the side of her cheek.\n\n“I—don’t know exactly where she is staying,” she answered. “She was—but—”\n“Where was she?”\n\n“Well, she is not there now.”\n\nIn her evasiveness she paused again, and the younger children had by this time crept to the door, where, pulling at his mother’s skirts, the youngest murmured—\n\n“Is this the gentleman who is going to marry Tess?”\n\n“He has married her,” Joan whispered. “Go inside.”\n\nClare saw her efforts for reticence, and asked—\n\n“Do you think Tess would wish me to try and find her? If not, of course—”\n\n“I don’t think she would.”\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\n“I am sure she wouldn’t.”\n\nHe was turning away; and then he thought of Tess’s tender letter.\n\n“I am sure she would!” he retorted passionately. “I know her better than you do.”\n\n“That’s very likely, sir; for I have never really known her.”\n\n“Please tell me her address, Mrs Durbeyfield, in kindness to a lonely wretched man!” Tess’s mother again restlessly swept her cheek with her vertical hand, and seeing that he suffered, she at last said, is a low voice—\n\n“She is at Sandbourne.”\n\n“Ah—where there? Sandbourne has become a large place, they say.”\n\n“I don’t know more particularly than I have said—Sandbourne. For myself, I was never there.”\n\nIt was apparent that Joan spoke the truth in this, and he pressed her no further.\n\n“Are you in want of anything?” he said gently.\n\n“No, sir,” she replied. “We are fairly well provided for.”\n\nWithout entering the house Clare turned away. There was a station three miles ahead, and paying off his coachman, he walked thither. The last train to Sandbourne left shortly after, and it bore Clare on its wheels.\n\n# LV\n\nAt eleven o’clock that night, having secured a bed at one of the hotels and telegraphed his address to his father immediately on his arrival, he walked out into the streets of Sandbourne. It was too late to call on or inquire for any one, and he reluctantly postponed his purpose till the morning. But he could not retire to rest just yet.\n\nThis fashionable watering-place, with its eastern and its western stations, its piers, its groves of pines, its promenades, and its covered gardens, was, to Angel Clare, like a fairy place suddenly created by the stroke of a wand, and allowed to get a little dusty. An outlying eastern tract of the enormous Egdon Waste was close at hand, yet on the very verge of that tawny piece of antiquity such a glittering novelty as this pleasure city had chosen to spring up. Within the space of a mile from its outskirts every irregularity of the soil was prehistoric, every channel an undisturbed British trackway; not a sod having been turned there since the days of the Cæsars. Yet the exotic had grown here, suddenly as the prophet’s gourd; and had drawn hither Tess.\n\nBy the midnight lamps he went up and down the winding way of this new world in an old one, and could discern between the trees and against the stars the lofty roofs, chimneys, gazebos, and towers of the numerous fanciful residences of which the place was composed. It was a city of detached mansions; a Mediterranean lounging-place on the English Channel; and as seen now by night it seemed even more imposing than it was.\n\nThe sea was near at hand, but not intrusive; it murmured, and he thought it was the pines; the pines murmured in precisely the same tones, and he thought they were the sea.\n\nWhere could Tess possibly be, a cottage-girl, his young wife, amidst all this wealth and fashion? The more he pondered, the more was he puzzled. Were there any cows to milk here? There certainly were no fields to till. She was most probably engaged to do something in one of these large houses; and he sauntered along, looking at the chamber-windows and their lights going out one by one, and wondered which of them might be hers.\n\nConjecture was useless, and just after twelve o’clock he entered and went to bed. Before putting out his light he re-read Tess’s impassioned letter. Sleep, however, he could not—so near her, yet so far from her—and he continually lifted the window-blind and regarded the backs of the opposite houses, and wondered behind which of the sashes she reposed at that moment.\n\nHe might almost as well have sat up all night. In the morning he arose at seven, and shortly after went out, taking the direction of the chief post-office. At the door he met an intelligent postman coming out with letters for the morning delivery.\n\n“Do you know the address of a Mrs Clare?” asked Angel. The postman shook his head.\n\nThen, remembering that she would have been likely to continue the use of her maiden name, Clare said—\n\n“Of a Miss Durbeyfield?”\n\n“Durbeyfield?”\n\nThis also was strange to the postman addressed.\n\n“There’s visitors coming and going every day, as you know, sir,” he said; “and without the name of the house ’tis impossible to find ’em.”\n\nOne of his comrades hastening out at that moment, the name was repeated to him.\n\n“I know no name of Durbeyfield; but there is the name of d’Urberville at The Herons,” said the second.\n\n“That’s it!” cried Clare, pleased to think that she had reverted to the real pronunciation. “What place is The Herons?”\n\n“A stylish lodging-house. ’Tis all lodging-houses here, bless ’ee.”\n\nClare received directions how to find the house, and hastened thither, arriving with the milkman. The Herons, though an ordinary villa, stood in its own grounds, and was certainly the last place in which one would have expected to find lodgings, so private was its appearance. If poor Tess was a servant here, as he feared, she would go to the back-door to that milkman, and he was inclined to go thither also. However, in his doubts he turned to the front, and rang.\n\nThe hour being early, the landlady herself opened the door. Clare inquired for Teresa d’Urberville or Durbeyfield.\n\n“Mrs d’Urberville?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nTess, then, passed as a married woman, and he felt glad, even though she had not adopted his name.\n\n“Will you kindly tell her that a relative is anxious to see her?”\n\n“It is rather early. What name shall I give, sir?”\n\n“Angel.”\n“Mr Angel?”\n\n“No; Angel. It is my Christian name. She’ll understand.”\n\n“I’ll see if she is awake.”\n\nHe was shown into the front room—the dining-room—and looked out through the spring curtains at the little lawn, and the rhododendrons and other shrubs upon it. Obviously her position was by no means so bad as he had feared, and it crossed his mind that she must somehow have claimed and sold the jewels to attain it. He did not blame her for one moment. Soon his sharpened ear detected footsteps upon the stairs, at which his heart thumped so painfully that he could hardly stand firm. “Dear me! what will she think of me, so altered as I am!” he said to himself; and the door opened.\n\nTess appeared on the threshold—not at all as he had expected to see her—bewilderingly otherwise, indeed. Her great natural beauty was, if not heightened, rendered more obvious by her attire. She was loosely wrapped in a cashmere dressing-gown of gray-white, embroidered in half-mourning tints, and she wore slippers of the same hue. Her neck rose out of a frill of down, and her well-remembered cable of dark-brown hair was partially coiled up in a mass at the back of her head and partly hanging on her shoulder—the evident result of haste.\nHe had held out his arms, but they had fallen again to his side; for she had not come forward, remaining still in the opening of the doorway. Mere yellow skeleton that he was now, he felt the contrast between them, and thought his appearance distasteful to her.\n\n“Tess!” he said huskily, “can you forgive me for going away? Can’t you—come to me? How do you get to be—like this?”\n\n“It is too late,” said she, her voice sounding hard through the room, her eyes shining unnaturally.\n\n“I did not think rightly of you—I did not see you as you were!” he continued to plead. “I have learnt to since, dearest Tessy mine!”\n\n“Too late, too late!” she said, waving her hand in the impatience of a person whose tortures cause every instant to seem an hour. “Don’t come close to me, Angel! No—you must not. Keep away.”\n\n“But don’t you love me, my dear wife, because I have been so pulled down by illness? You are not so fickle—I am come on purpose for you—my mother and father will welcome you now!”\n\n“Yes—O, yes, yes! But I say, I say it is too late.”\n\nShe seemed to feel like a fugitive in a dream, who tries to move away, but cannot. “Don’t you know all—don’t you know it? Yet how do you come here if you do not know?”\n\n“I inquired here and there, and I found the way.”\n\n“I waited and waited for you,” she went on, her tones suddenly resuming their old fluty pathos. “But you did not come! And I wrote to you, and you did not come! He kept on saying you would never come any more, and that I was a foolish woman. He was very kind to me, and to mother, and to all of us after father’s death. He—”\n\n“I don’t understand.”\n\n“He has won me back to him.”\n\nClare looked at her keenly, then, gathering her meaning, flagged like one plague-stricken, and his glance sank; it fell on her hands, which, once rosy, were now white and more delicate.\n\nShe continued—\n\n“He is upstairs. I hate him now, because he told me a lie—that you would not come again; and you _have_ come! These clothes are what he’s put upon me: I didn’t care what he did wi’ me! But—will you go away, Angel, please, and never come any more?”\n\nThey stood fixed, their baffled hearts looking out of their eyes with a joylessness pitiful to see. Both seemed to implore something to shelter them from reality.\n\n“Ah—it is my fault!” said Clare.\n\nBut he could not get on. Speech was as inexpressive as silence. But he had a vague consciousness of one thing, though it was not clear to him till later; that his original Tess had spiritually ceased to recognize the body before him as hers—allowing it to drift, like a corpse upon the current, in a direction dissociated from its living will.\n\nA few instants passed, and he found that Tess was gone. His face grew colder and more shrunken as he stood concentrated on the moment, and a minute or two after, he found himself in the street, walking along he did not know whither.\n\n# LVI\n\nMrs Brooks, the lady who was the householder at The Herons and owner of all the handsome furniture, was not a person of an unusually curious turn of mind. She was too deeply materialized, poor woman, by her long and enforced bondage to that arithmetical demon Profit-and-Loss, to retain much curiousity for its own sake, and apart from possible lodgers’ pockets. Nevertheless, the visit of Angel Clare to her well-paying tenants, Mr and Mrs d’Urberville, as she deemed them, was sufficiently exceptional in point of time and manner to reinvigorate the feminine proclivity which had been stifled down as useless save in its bearings to the letting trade.\n\nTess had spoken to her husband from the doorway, without entering the dining-room, and Mrs Brooks, who stood within the partly-closed door of her own sitting-room at the back of the passage, could hear fragments of the conversation—if conversation it could be called—between those two wretched souls. She heard Tess re-ascend the stairs to the first floor, and the departure of Clare, and the closing of the front door behind him. Then the door of the room above was shut, and Mrs Brooks knew that Tess had re-entered her apartment. As the young lady was not fully dressed, Mrs Brooks knew that she would not emerge again for some time.\n\nShe accordingly ascended the stairs softly, and stood at the door of the front room—a drawing-room, connected with the room immediately behind it (which was a bedroom) by folding-doors in the common manner. This first floor, containing Mrs Brooks’s best apartments, had been taken by the week by the d’Urbervilles. The back room was now in silence; but from the drawing-room there came sounds.\n\nAll that she could at first distinguish of them was one syllable, continually repeated in a low note of moaning, as if it came from a soul bound to some Ixionian wheel—\n\nThen a silence, then a heavy sigh, and again—\n\nThe landlady looked through the keyhole. Only a small space of the room inside was visible, but within that space came a corner of the breakfast table, which was already spread for the meal, and also a chair beside. Over the seat of the chair Tess’s face was bowed, her posture being a kneeling one in front of it; her hands were clasped over her head, the skirts of her dressing-gown and the embroidery of her night-gown flowed upon the floor behind her, and her stockingless feet, from which the slippers had fallen, protruded upon the carpet. It was from her lips that came the murmur of unspeakable despair.\n\nThen a man’s voice from the adjoining bedroom—\n\n“What’s the matter?”\n\nShe did not answer, but went on, in a tone which was a soliloquy rather than an exclamation, and a dirge rather than a soliloquy. Mrs Brooks could only catch a portion:\n\n“And then my dear, dear husband came home to me ... and I did not know it!... And you had used your cruel persuasion upon me ... you did not stop using it—no—you did not stop! My little sisters and brothers and my mother’s needs—they were the things you moved me by ... and you said my husband would never come back—never; and you taunted me, and said what a simpleton I was to expect him!... And at last I believed you and gave way!... And then he came back! Now he is gone. Gone a second time, and I have lost him now for ever ... and he will not love me the littlest bit ever any more—only hate me!... O yes, I have lost him now—again because of—you!” In writhing, with her head on the chair, she turned her face towards the door, and Mrs Brooks could see the pain upon it, and that her lips were bleeding from the clench of her teeth upon them, and that the long lashes of her closed eyes stuck in wet tags to her cheeks. She continued: “And he is dying—he looks as if he is dying!... And my sin will kill him and not kill me!... O, you have torn my life all to pieces ... made me be what I prayed you in pity not to make me be again!... My own true husband will never, never—O God—I can’t bear this!—I cannot!”\n\nThere were more and sharper words from the man; then a sudden rustle; she had sprung to her feet. Mrs Brooks, thinking that the speaker was coming to rush out of the door, hastily retreated down the stairs.\n\nShe need not have done so, however, for the door of the sitting-room was not opened. But Mrs Brooks felt it unsafe to watch on the landing again, and entered her own parlour below.\n\nShe could hear nothing through the floor, although she listened intently, and thereupon went to the kitchen to finish her interrupted breakfast. Coming up presently to the front room on the ground floor she took up some sewing, waiting for her lodgers to ring that she might take away the breakfast, which she meant to do herself, to discover what was the matter if possible. Overhead, as she sat, she could now hear the floorboards slightly creak, as if some one were walking about, and presently the movement was explained by the rustle of garments against the banisters, the opening and the closing of the front door, and the form of Tess passing to the gate on her way into the street. She was fully dressed now in the walking costume of a well-to-do young lady in which she had arrived, with the sole addition that over her hat and black feathers a veil was drawn.\n\nMrs Brooks had not been able to catch any word of farewell, temporary or otherwise, between her tenants at the door above. They might have quarrelled, or Mr d’Urberville might still be asleep, for he was not an early riser.\n\nShe went into the back room, which was more especially her own apartment, and continued her sewing there. The lady lodger did not return, nor did the gentleman ring his bell. Mrs Brooks pondered on the delay, and on what probable relation the visitor who had called so early bore to the couple upstairs. In reflecting she leant back in her chair.\n\nAs she did so her eyes glanced casually over the ceiling till they were arrested by a spot in the middle of its white surface which she had never noticed there before. It was about the size of a wafer when she first observed it, but it speedily grew as large as the palm of her hand, and then she could perceive that it was red. The oblong white ceiling, with this scarlet blot in the midst, had the appearance of a gigantic ace of hearts.\n\nMrs Brooks had strange qualms of misgiving. She got upon the table, and touched the spot in the ceiling with her fingers. It was damp, and she fancied that it was a blood stain.\n\nDescending from the table, she left the parlour, and went upstairs, intending to enter the room overhead, which was the bedchamber at the back of the drawing-room. But, nerveless woman as she had now become, she could not bring herself to attempt the handle. She listened. The dead silence within was broken only by a regular beat.\n\nDrip, drip, drip.\n\nMrs Brooks hastened downstairs, opened the front door, and ran into the street. A man she knew, one of the workmen employed at an adjoining villa, was passing by, and she begged him to come in and go upstairs with her; she feared something had happened to one of her lodgers. The workman assented, and followed her to the landing.\n\nShe opened the door of the drawing-room, and stood back for him to pass in, entering herself behind him. The room was empty; the breakfast—a substantial repast of coffee, eggs, and a cold ham—lay spread upon the table untouched, as when she had taken it up, excepting that the carving-knife was missing. She asked the man to go through the folding-doors into the adjoining room.\n\nHe opened the doors, entered a step or two, and came back almost instantly with a rigid face. “My good God, the gentleman in bed is dead! I think he has been hurt with a knife—a lot of blood has run down upon the floor!”\n\nThe alarm was soon given, and the house which had lately been so quiet resounded with the tramp of many footsteps, a surgeon among the rest. The wound was small, but the point of the blade had touched the heart of the victim, who lay on his back, pale, fixed, dead, as if he had scarcely moved after the infliction of the blow. In a quarter of an hour the news that a gentleman who was a temporary visitor to the town had been stabbed in his bed, spread through every street and villa of the popular watering-place.\n\n# LVII\n\nMeanwhile Angel Clare had walked automatically along the way by which he had come, and, entering his hotel, sat down over the breakfast, staring at nothingness. He went on eating and drinking unconsciously till on a sudden he demanded his bill; having paid which, he took his dressing-bag in his hand, the only luggage he had brought with him, and went out.\n\nAt the moment of his departure a telegram was handed to him—a few words from his mother, stating that they were glad to know his address, and informing him that his brother Cuthbert had proposed to and been accepted by Mercy Chant.\n\nClare crumpled up the paper and followed the route to the station; reaching it, he found that there would be no train leaving for an hour and more. He sat down to wait, and having waited a quarter of an hour felt that he could wait there no longer. Broken in heart and numbed, he had nothing to hurry for; but he wished to get out of a town which had been the scene of such an experience, and turned to walk to the first station onward, and let the train pick him up there.\n\nThe highway that he followed was open, and at a little distance dipped into a valley, across which it could be seen running from edge to edge. He had traversed the greater part of this depression, and was climbing the western acclivity when, pausing for breath, he unconsciously looked back. Why he did so he could not say, but something seemed to impel him to the act. The tape-like surface of the road diminished in his rear as far as he could see, and as he gazed a moving spot intruded on the white vacuity of its perspective.\n\nIt was a human figure running. Clare waited, with a dim sense that somebody was trying to overtake him.\n\nThe form descending the incline was a woman’s, yet so entirely was his mind blinded to the idea of his wife’s following him that even when she came nearer he did not recognize her under the totally changed attire in which he now beheld her. It was not till she was quite close that he could believe her to be Tess.\n\n“I saw you—turn away from the station—just before I got there—and I have been following you all this way!”\n\nShe was so pale, so breathless, so quivering in every muscle, that he did not ask her a single question, but seizing her hand, and pulling it within his arm, he led her along. To avoid meeting any possible wayfarers he left the high road and took a footpath under some fir-trees. When they were deep among the moaning boughs he stopped and looked at her inquiringly.\n\n“Angel,” she said, as if waiting for this, “do you know what I have been running after you for? To tell you that I have killed him!” A pitiful white smile lit her face as she spoke.\n\n“What!” said he, thinking from the strangeness of her manner that she was in some delirium.\n\n“I have done it—I don’t know how,” she continued. “Still, I owed it to you, and to myself, Angel. I feared long ago, when I struck him on the mouth with my glove, that I might do it some day for the trap he set for me in my simple youth, and his wrong to you through me. He has come between us and ruined us, and now he can never do it any more. I never loved him at all, Angel, as I loved you. You know it, don’t you? You believe it? You didn’t come back to me, and I was obliged to go back to him. Why did you go away—why did you—when I loved you so? I can’t think why you did it. But I don’t blame you; only, Angel, will you forgive me my sin against you, now I have killed him? I thought as I ran along that you would be sure to forgive me now I have done that. It came to me as a shining light that I should get you back that way. I could not bear the loss of you any longer—you don’t know how entirely I was unable to bear your not loving me! Say you do now, dear, dear husband; say you do, now I have killed him!”\n\n“I do love you, Tess—O, I do—it is all come back!” he said, tightening his arms round her with fervid pressure. “But how do you mean—you have killed him?”\n\n“I mean that I have,” she murmured in a reverie.\n\n“What, bodily? Is he dead?”\n\n“Yes. He heard me crying about you, and he bitterly taunted me; and called you by a foul name; and then I did it. My heart could not bear it. He had nagged me about you before. And then I dressed myself and came away to find you.”\n\nBy degrees he was inclined to believe that she had faintly attempted, at least, what she said she had done; and his horror at her impulse was mixed with amazement at the strength of her affection for himself, and at the strangeness of its quality, which had apparently extinguished her moral sense altogether. Unable to realize the gravity of her conduct, she seemed at last content; and he looked at her as she lay upon his shoulder, weeping with happiness, and wondered what obscure strain in the d’Urberville blood had led to this aberration—if it were an aberration. There momentarily flashed through his mind that the family tradition of the coach and murder might have arisen because the d’Urbervilles had been known to do these things. As well as his confused and excited ideas could reason, he supposed that in the moment of mad grief of which she spoke, her mind had lost its balance, and plunged her into this abyss.\n\nIt was very terrible if true; if a temporary hallucination, sad. But, anyhow, here was this deserted wife of his, this passionately-fond woman, clinging to him without a suspicion that he would be anything to her but a protector. He saw that for him to be otherwise was not, in her mind, within the region of the possible. Tenderness was absolutely dominant in Clare at last. He kissed her endlessly with his white lips, and held her hand, and said—\n\n“I will not desert you! I will protect you by every means in my power, dearest love, whatever you may have done or not have done!”\n\nThey then walked on under the trees, Tess turning her head every now and then to look at him. Worn and unhandsome as he had become, it was plain that she did not discern the least fault in his appearance. To her he was, as of old, all that was perfection, personally and mentally. He was still her Antinous, her Apollo even; his sickly face was beautiful as the morning to her affectionate regard on this day no less than when she first beheld him; for was it not the face of the one man on earth who had loved her purely, and who had believed in her as pure!\n\nWith an instinct as to possibilities, he did not now, as he had intended, make for the first station beyond the town, but plunged still farther under the firs, which here abounded for miles. Each clasping the other round the waist they promenaded over the dry bed of fir-needles, thrown into a vague intoxicating atmosphere at the consciousness of being together at last, with no living soul between them; ignoring that there was a corpse. Thus they proceeded for several miles till Tess, arousing herself, looked about her, and said, timidly—\n\n“Are we going anywhere in particular?”\n\n“I don’t know, dearest. Why?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Well, we might walk a few miles further, and when it is evening find lodgings somewhere or other—in a lonely cottage, perhaps. Can you walk well, Tessy?”\n\n“O yes! I could walk for ever and ever with your arm round me!”\n\nUpon the whole it seemed a good thing to do. Thereupon they quickened their pace, avoiding high roads, and following obscure paths tending more or less northward. But there was an unpractical vagueness in their movements throughout the day; neither one of them seemed to consider any question of effectual escape, disguise, or long concealment. Their every idea was temporary and unforefending, like the plans of two children.\n\nAt mid-day they drew near to a roadside inn, and Tess would have entered it with him to get something to eat, but he persuaded her to remain among the trees and bushes of this half-woodland, half-moorland part of the country till he should come back. Her clothes were of recent fashion; even the ivory-handled parasol that she carried was of a shape unknown in the retired spot to which they had now wandered; and the cut of such articles would have attracted attention in the settle of a tavern. He soon returned, with food enough for half-a-dozen people and two bottles of wine—enough to last them for a day or more, should any emergency arise.\n\nThey sat down upon some dead boughs and shared their meal. Between one and two o’clock they packed up the remainder and went on again.\n\n“I feel strong enough to walk any distance,” said she.\n\n“I think we may as well steer in a general way towards the interior of the country, where we can hide for a time, and are less likely to be looked for than anywhere near the coast,” Clare remarked. “Later on, when they have forgotten us, we can make for some port.”\n\nShe made no reply to this beyond that of grasping him more tightly, and straight inland they went. Though the season was an English May, the weather was serenely bright, and during the afternoon it was quite warm. Through the latter miles of their walk their footpath had taken them into the depths of the New Forest, and towards evening, turning the corner of a lane, they perceived behind a brook and bridge a large board on which was painted in white letters, “This desirable Mansion to be Let Furnished”; particulars following, with directions to apply to some London agents. Passing through the gate they could see the house, an old brick building of regular design and large accommodation.\n\n“I know it,” said Clare. “It is Bramshurst Court. You can see that it is shut up, and grass is growing on the drive.”\n\n“Some of the windows are open,” said Tess.\n\n“Just to air the rooms, I suppose.”\n\n“All these rooms empty, and we without a roof to our heads!”\n\n“You are getting tired, my Tess!” he said. “We’ll stop soon.” And kissing her sad mouth, he again led her onwards.\n\nHe was growing weary likewise, for they had wandered a dozen or fifteen miles, and it became necessary to consider what they should do for rest. They looked from afar at isolated cottages and little inns, and were inclined to approach one of the latter, when their hearts failed them, and they sheered off. At length their gait dragged, and they stood still.\n\n“Could we sleep under the trees?” she asked.\n\nHe thought the season insufficiently advanced.\n\n“I have been thinking of that empty mansion we passed,” he said. “Let us go back towards it again.”\n\nThey retraced their steps, but it was half an hour before they stood without the entrance-gate as earlier. He then requested her to stay where she was, whilst he went to see who was within.\n\nShe sat down among the bushes within the gate, and Clare crept towards the house. His absence lasted some considerable time, and when he returned Tess was wildly anxious, not for herself, but for him. He had found out from a boy that there was only an old woman in charge as caretaker, and she only came there on fine days, from the hamlet near, to open and shut the windows. She would come to shut them at sunset. “Now, we can get in through one of the lower windows, and rest there,” said he.\n\nUnder his escort she went tardily forward to the main front, whose shuttered windows, like sightless eyeballs, excluded the possibility of watchers. The door was reached a few steps further, and one of the windows beside it was open. Clare clambered in, and pulled Tess in after him.\n\nExcept the hall, the rooms were all in darkness, and they ascended the staircase. Up here also the shutters were tightly closed, the ventilation being perfunctorily done, for this day at least, by opening the hall-window in front and an upper window behind. Clare unlatched the door of a large chamber, felt his way across it, and parted the shutters to the width of two or three inches. A shaft of dazzling sunlight glanced into the room, revealing heavy, old-fashioned furniture, crimson damask hangings, and an enormous four-post bedstead, along the head of which were carved running figures, apparently Atalanta’s race.\n\n“Rest at last!” said he, setting down his bag and the parcel of viands.\n\nThey remained in great quietness till the caretaker should have come to shut the windows: as a precaution, putting themselves in total darkness by barring the shutters as before, lest the woman should open the door of their chamber for any casual reason. Between six and seven o’clock she came, but did not approach the wing they were in. They heard her close the windows, fasten them, lock the door, and go away. Then Clare again stole a chink of light from the window, and they shared another meal, till by-and-by they were enveloped in the shades of night which they had no candle to disperse.\n\n# LVIII\n\nThe night was strangely solemn and still. In the small hours she whispered to him the whole story of how he had walked in his sleep with her in his arms across the Froom stream, at the imminent risk of both their lives, and laid her down in the stone coffin at the ruined abbey. He had never known of that till now.\n\n“Why didn’t you tell me next day?” he said. “It might have prevented much misunderstanding and woe.”\n\n“Don’t think of what’s past!” said she. “I am not going to think outside of now. Why should we! Who knows what to-morrow has in store?”\n\nBut it apparently had no sorrow. The morning was wet and foggy, and Clare, rightly informed that the caretaker only opened the windows on fine days, ventured to creep out of their chamber and explore the house, leaving Tess asleep. There was no food on the premises, but there was water, and he took advantage of the fog to emerge from the mansion and fetch tea, bread, and butter from a shop in a little place two miles beyond, as also a small tin kettle and spirit-lamp, that they might get fire without smoke. His re-entry awoke her; and they breakfasted on what he had brought.\n\nThey were indisposed to stir abroad, and the day passed, and the night following, and the next, and next; till, almost without their being aware, five days had slipped by in absolute seclusion, not a sight or sound of a human being disturbing their peacefulness, such as it was. The changes of the weather were their only events, the birds of the New Forest their only company. By tacit consent they hardly once spoke of any incident of the past subsequent to their wedding-day. The gloomy intervening time seemed to sink into chaos, over which the present and prior times closed as if it never had been. Whenever he suggested that they should leave their shelter, and go forwards towards Southampton or London, she showed a strange unwillingness to move.\n\n“Why should we put an end to all that’s sweet and lovely!” she deprecated. “What must come will come.” And, looking through the shutter-chink: “All is trouble outside there; inside here content.”\n\nHe peeped out also. It was quite true; within was affection, union, error forgiven: outside was the inexorable.\n\n“And—and,” she said, pressing her cheek against his, “I fear that what you think of me now may not last. I do not wish to outlive your present feeling for me. I would rather not. I would rather be dead and buried when the time comes for you to despise me, so that it may never be known to me that you despised me.”\n\n“I cannot ever despise you.”\n\n“I also hope that. But considering what my life has been, I cannot see why any man should, sooner or later, be able to help despising me.... How wickedly mad I was! Yet formerly I never could bear to hurt a fly or a worm, and the sight of a bird in a cage used often to make me cry.”\n\nThey remained yet another day. In the night the dull sky cleared, and the result was that the old caretaker at the cottage awoke early. The brilliant sunrise made her unusually brisk; she decided to open the contiguous mansion immediately, and to air it thoroughly on such a day. Thus it occurred that, having arrived and opened the lower rooms before six o’clock, she ascended to the bedchambers, and was about to turn the handle of the one wherein they lay. At that moment she fancied she could hear the breathing of persons within. Her slippers and her antiquity had rendered her progress a noiseless one so far, and she made for instant retreat; then, deeming that her hearing might have deceived her, she turned anew to the door and softly tried the handle. The lock was out of order, but a piece of furniture had been moved forward on the inside, which prevented her opening the door more than an inch or two. A stream of morning light through the shutter-chink fell upon the faces of the pair, wrapped in profound slumber, Tess’s lips being parted like a half-opened flower near his cheek. The caretaker was so struck with their innocent appearance, and with the elegance of Tess’s gown hanging across a chair, her silk stockings beside it, the pretty parasol, and the other habits in which she had arrived because she had none else, that her first indignation at the effrontery of tramps and vagabonds gave way to a momentary sentimentality over this genteel elopement, as it seemed. She closed the door, and withdrew as softly as she had come, to go and consult with her neighbours on the odd discovery.\n\nNot more than a minute had elapsed after her withdrawal when Tess woke, and then Clare. Both had a sense that something had disturbed them, though they could not say what; and the uneasy feeling which it engendered grew stronger. As soon as he was dressed he narrowly scanned the lawn through the two or three inches of shutter-chink.\n\n“I think we will leave at once,” said he. “It is a fine day. And I cannot help fancying somebody is about the house. At any rate, the woman will be sure to come to-day.”\n\nShe passively assented, and putting the room in order, they took up the few articles that belonged to them, and departed noiselessly. When they had got into the Forest she turned to take a last look at the house.\n\n“Ah, happy house—goodbye!” she said. “My life can only be a question of a few weeks. Why should we not have stayed there?”\n\n“Don’t say it, Tess! We shall soon get out of this district altogether. We’ll continue our course as we’ve begun it, and keep straight north. Nobody will think of looking for us there. We shall be looked for at the Wessex ports if we are sought at all. When we are in the north we will get to a port and away.”\n\nHaving thus persuaded her, the plan was pursued, and they kept a bee-line northward. Their long repose at the manor-house lent them walking power now; and towards mid-day they found that they were approaching the steepled city of Melchester, which lay directly in their way. He decided to rest her in a clump of trees during the afternoon, and push onward under cover of darkness. At dusk Clare purchased food as usual, and their night march began, the boundary between Upper and Mid-Wessex being crossed about eight o’clock.\n\nTo walk across country without much regard to roads was not new to Tess, and she showed her old agility in the performance. The intercepting city, ancient Melchester, they were obliged to pass through in order to take advantage of the town bridge for crossing a large river that obstructed them. It was about midnight when they went along the deserted streets, lighted fitfully by the few lamps, keeping off the pavement that it might not echo their footsteps. The graceful pile of cathedral architecture rose dimly on their left hand, but it was lost upon them now. Once out of the town they followed the turnpike-road, which after a few miles plunged across an open plain.\nThough the sky was dense with cloud, a diffused light from some fragment of a moon had hitherto helped them a little. But the moon had now sunk, the clouds seemed to settle almost on their heads, and the night grew as dark as a cave. However, they found their way along, keeping as much on the turf as possible that their tread might not resound, which it was easy to do, there being no hedge or fence of any kind. All around was open loneliness and black solitude, over which a stiff breeze blew.\n\nThey had proceeded thus gropingly two or three miles further when on a sudden Clare became conscious of some vast erection close in his front, rising sheer from the grass. They had almost struck themselves against it.\n\n“What monstrous place is this?” said Angel.\n\n“It hums,” said she. “Hearken!”\n\nHe listened. The wind, playing upon the edifice, produced a booming tune, like the note of some gigantic one-stringed harp. No other sound came from it, and lifting his hand and advancing a step or two, Clare felt the vertical surface of the structure. It seemed to be of solid stone, without joint or moulding. Carrying his fingers onward he found that what he had come in contact with was a colossal rectangular pillar; by stretching out his left hand he could feel a similar one adjoining. At an indefinite height overhead something made the black sky blacker, which had the semblance of a vast architrave uniting the pillars horizontally. They carefully entered beneath and between; the surfaces echoed their soft rustle; but they seemed to be still out of doors. The place was roofless. Tess drew her breath fearfully, and Angel, perplexed, said—\n\n“What can it be?”\n\nFeeling sideways they encountered another tower-like pillar, square and uncompromising as the first; beyond it another and another. The place was all doors and pillars, some connected above by continuous architraves.\n\n“A very Temple of the Winds,” he said.\n\nThe next pillar was isolated; others composed a trilithon; others were prostrate, their flanks forming a causeway wide enough for a carriage; and it was soon obvious that they made up a forest of monoliths grouped upon the grassy expanse of the plain. The couple advanced further into this pavilion of the night till they stood in its midst.\n\n“It is Stonehenge!” said Clare.\n\n“The heathen temple, you mean?”\n\n“Yes. Older than the centuries; older than the d’Urbervilles! Well, what shall we do, darling? We may find shelter further on.”\n\nBut Tess, really tired by this time, flung herself upon an oblong slab that lay close at hand, and was sheltered from the wind by a pillar. Owing to the action of the sun during the preceding day, the stone was warm and dry, in comforting contrast to the rough and chill grass around, which had damped her skirts and shoes.\n\n“I don’t want to go any further, Angel,” she said, stretching out her hand for his. “Can’t we bide here?”\n\n“I fear not. This spot is visible for miles by day, although it does not seem so now.”\n\n“One of my mother’s people was a shepherd hereabouts, now I think of it. And you used to say at Talbothays that I was a heathen. So now I am at home.”\n\nHe knelt down beside her outstretched form, and put his lips upon hers.\n\n“Sleepy are you, dear? I think you are lying on an altar.”\n\n“I like very much to be here,” she murmured. “It is so solemn and lonely—after my great happiness—with nothing but the sky above my face. It seems as if there were no folk in the world but we two; and I wish there were not—except ’Liza-Lu.”\n\nClare thought she might as well rest here till it should get a little lighter, and he flung his overcoat upon her, and sat down by her side.\n\n“Angel, if anything happens to me, will you watch over ’Liza-Lu for my sake?” she asked, when they had listened a long time to the wind among the pillars.\n\n“I will.”\n\n“She is so good and simple and pure. O, Angel—I wish you would marry her if you lose me, as you will do shortly. O, if you would!”\n\n“If I lose you I lose all! And she is my sister-in-law.”\n\n“That’s nothing, dearest. People marry sister-laws continually about Marlott; and ’Liza-Lu is so gentle and sweet, and she is growing so beautiful. O, I could share you with her willingly when we are spirits! If you would train her and teach her, Angel, and bring her up for your own self!... She had all the best of me without the bad of me; and if she were to become yours it would almost seem as if death had not divided us... Well, I have said it. I won’t mention it again.”\n\nShe ceased, and he fell into thought. In the far north-east sky he could see between the pillars a level streak of light. The uniform concavity of black cloud was lifting bodily like the lid of a pot, letting in at the earth’s edge the coming day, against which the towering monoliths and trilithons began to be blackly defined.\n\n“Did they sacrifice to God here?” asked she.\n\n“No,” said he.\n“Who to?”\n\n“I believe to the sun. That lofty stone set away by itself is in the direction of the sun, which will presently rise behind it.”\n\n“This reminds me, dear,” she said. “You remember you never would interfere with any belief of mine before we were married? But I knew your mind all the same, and I thought as you thought—not from any reasons of my own, but because you thought so. Tell me now, Angel, do you think we shall meet again after we are dead? I want to know.”\n\nHe kissed her to avoid a reply at such a time.\n\n“O, Angel—I fear that means no!” said she, with a suppressed sob. “And I wanted so to see you again—so much, so much! What—not even you and I, Angel, who love each other so well?”\n\nLike a greater than himself, to the critical question at the critical time he did not answer; and they were again silent. In a minute or two her breathing became more regular, her clasp of his hand relaxed, and she fell asleep. The band of silver paleness along the east horizon made even the distant parts of the Great Plain appear dark and near; and the whole enormous landscape bore that impress of reserve, taciturnity, and hesitation which is usual just before day. The eastward pillars and their architraves stood up blackly against the light, and the great flame-shaped Sun-stone beyond them; and the Stone of Sacrifice midway. Presently the night wind died out, and the quivering little pools in the cup-like hollows of the stones lay still. At the same time something seemed to move on the verge of the dip eastward—a mere dot. It was the head of a man approaching them from the hollow beyond the Sun-stone. Clare wished they had gone onward, but in the circumstances decided to remain quiet. The figure came straight towards the circle of pillars in which they were.\n\nHe heard something behind him, the brush of feet. Turning, he saw over the prostrate columns another figure; then before he was aware, another was at hand on the right, under a trilithon, and another on the left. The dawn shone full on the front of the man westward, and Clare could discern from this that he was tall, and walked as if trained. They all closed in with evident purpose. Her story then was true! Springing to his feet, he looked around for a weapon, loose stone, means of escape, anything. By this time the nearest man was upon him.\n\n“It is no use, sir,” he said. “There are sixteen of us on the Plain, and the whole country is reared.”\n\n“Let her finish her sleep!” he implored in a whisper of the men as they gathered round.\n\nWhen they saw where she lay, which they had not done till then, they showed no objection, and stood watching her, as still as the pillars around. He went to the stone and bent over her, holding one poor little hand; her breathing now was quick and small, like that of a lesser creature than a woman. All waited in the growing light, their faces and hands as if they were silvered, the remainder of their figures dark, the stones glistening green-gray, the Plain still a mass of shade. Soon the light was strong, and a ray shone upon her unconscious form, peering under her eyelids and waking her.\n\n“What is it, Angel?” she said, starting up. “Have they come for me?”\n\n“Yes, dearest,” he said. “They have come.”\n\n“It is as it should be,” she murmured. “Angel, I am almost glad—yes, glad! This happiness could not have lasted. It was too much. I have had enough; and now I shall not live for you to despise me!”\n\nShe stood up, shook herself, and went forward, neither of the men having moved.\n\n“I am ready,” she said quietly.\n\n# LIX\n\nThe city of Wintoncester, that fine old city, aforetime capital of Wessex, lay amidst its convex and concave downlands in all the brightness and warmth of a July morning. The gabled brick, tile, and freestone houses had almost dried off for the season their integument of lichen, the streams in the meadows were low, and in the sloping High Street, from the West Gateway to the mediæval cross, and from the mediæval cross to the bridge, that leisurely dusting and sweeping was in progress which usually ushers in an old-fashioned market-day.\n\nFrom the western gate aforesaid the highway, as every Wintoncestrian knows, ascends a long and regular incline of the exact length of a measured mile, leaving the houses gradually behind. Up this road from the precincts of the city two persons were walking rapidly, as if unconscious of the trying ascent—unconscious through preoccupation and not through buoyancy. They had emerged upon this road through a narrow, barred wicket in a high wall a little lower down. They seemed anxious to get out of the sight of the houses and of their kind, and this road appeared to offer the quickest means of doing so. Though they were young, they walked with bowed heads, which gait of grief the sun’s rays smiled on pitilessly.\n\nOne of the pair was Angel Clare, the other a tall budding creature—half girl, half woman—a spiritualized image of Tess, slighter than she, but with the same beautiful eyes—Clare’s sister-in-law, ’Liza-Lu. Their pale faces seemed to have shrunk to half their natural size. They moved on hand in hand, and never spoke a word, the drooping of their heads being that of Giotto’s “Two Apostles”.\n\nWhen they had nearly reached the top of the great West Hill the clocks in the town struck eight. Each gave a start at the notes, and, walking onward yet a few steps, they reached the first milestone, standing whitely on the green margin of the grass, and backed by the down, which here was open to the road. They entered upon the turf, and, impelled by a force that seemed to overrule their will, suddenly stood still, turned, and waited in paralyzed suspense beside the stone.\n\nThe prospect from this summit was almost unlimited. In the valley beneath lay the city they had just left, its more prominent buildings showing as in an isometric drawing—among them the broad cathedral tower, with its Norman windows and immense length of aisle and nave, the spires of St Thomas’s, the pinnacled tower of the College, and, more to the right, the tower and gables of the ancient hospice, where to this day the pilgrim may receive his dole of bread and ale. Behind the city swept the rotund upland of St Catherine’s Hill; further off, landscape beyond landscape, till the horizon was lost in the radiance of the sun hanging above it.\n\nAgainst these far stretches of country rose, in front of the other city edifices, a large red-brick building, with level gray roofs, and rows of short barred windows bespeaking captivity, the whole contrasting greatly by its formalism with the quaint irregularities of the Gothic erections. It was somewhat disguised from the road in passing it by yews and evergreen oaks, but it was visible enough up here. The wicket from which the pair had lately emerged was in the wall of this structure. From the middle of the building an ugly flat-topped octagonal tower ascended against the east horizon, and viewed from this spot, on its shady side and against the light, it seemed the one blot on the city’s beauty. Yet it was with this blot, and not with the beauty, that the two gazers were concerned.\n\nUpon the cornice of the tower a tall staff was fixed. Their eyes were riveted on it. A few minutes after the hour had struck something moved slowly up the staff, and extended itself upon the breeze. It was a black flag.\n\n“Justice” was done, and the President of the Immortals, in Æschylean phrase, had ended his sport with Tess. And the d’Urberville knights and dames slept on in their tombs unknowing. The two speechless gazers bent themselves down to the earth, as if in prayer, and remained thus a long time, absolutely motionless: the flag continued to wave silently. As soon as they had strength, they arose, joined hands again, and went on.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-110"]="다음 이야기의 주요 부분은—약간의 수정을 거쳐—『그래픽』 신문에 실렸고, 다른 장들은, 특히 성인 독자를 겨냥한 부분은, 삽화적 소품의 형태로 『포트나이틀리 리뷰』와 『내셔널 옵서버』에 실렸다. 이제 소설의 몸통과 사지를 한데 이어붙여, 이 년 전 처음 쓴 그대로 완전한 형태로 인쇄할 수 있게 해 준 이 정기간행물들의 편집자와 발행인 들에게 감사를 전한다.\n\n다만 한마디 덧붙이자면, 이 이야기는 사물의 참된 흐름에 예술적 형식을 부여하려는 시도로서 온전히 진실한 목적으로 세상에 내놓는 것이다. 그리고 이 책의 견해와 정서에 관해서는, 요즘 누구나 생각하고 느끼는 바를 말로 옮긴 것을 견딜 수 없어 하는 지나치게 점잖은 독자가 있다면, 성 히에로니무스의 닳고 닳은 한 문장을 떠올려 주기를 청한다. 진리에서 어떤 거슬림이 나온다면, 진리를 감추는 것보다 그 거슬림이 나오는 편이 낫다.\nT.H.\n\n1891년 11월.\n\n이 소설은 여주인공의 위대한 분투가, 보통 그녀의 주인공 역할에 치명적이라고, 적어도 그녀의 기획과 희망의 사실상 종말이라고 여겨져 온 한 사건 이후에 비로소 시작되는 작품이다. 그러니 잘 알려진 어떤 파국의 그늘진 면에 대해 소설에서 일찍이 말해진 것보다 더 말할 거리가 남아 있다고 보는 나의 견해에 대중이 동의하고 이 책을 환영해 준 것은, 공언된 관습에는 자못 어긋나는 일이었다. 그러나 『더버빌가의 테스』가 영국과 미국의 독자들에게 받은 호응의 정신을 보면, 사회의 한낱 입에 발린 정식에 들어맞도록 이야기를 짓는 대신 암묵적 여론의 선을 따라 이야기를 펼치는 방식이, 이번처럼 고르지 못하고 불완전한 성취로 예시된 경우에조차, 아주 그릇된 것만은 아님이 입증되는 듯하다. 이 호응에 대해 나는 감사의 뜻을 표하지 않을 수 없다. 그리고 나의 유감은, 우정을 그토록 자주 헛되이 갈구하는 세상에서, 일부러 오해받지 않는 것만으로도 친절로 느껴지는 이 세상에서, 이 고마운 독자들을, 남자든 여자든, 직접 만나 손을 맞잡을 일은 결코 없으리라는 점이다.\n\n그들 가운데 나는 비평가들도—압도적 다수를—포함시킨다. 이 이야기를 그토록 너그러이 환영해 준 이들 말이다. 그들의 말은, 그들 역시 다른 독자들처럼, 자신의 상상적 직관으로 나의 서술의 결함을 너무도 크게 메워 주었음을 보여 준다.\n\n그럼에도, 이 소설은 교훈적이지도 공격적이지도 않게, 다만 장면을 그린 부분에서는 단순히 재현적으로, 사색하는 부분에서는 확신보다 인상을 더 많이 담도록 의도되었으나, 내용에 대해서도 표현에 대해서도 반대하는 이들이 있었다.\n\n이들 가운데 더 준엄한 자들은, 여러 가지 중에서도 예술에 적합한 소재에 관해 양심에 따른 견해 차이를 고수하며, 부제에 붙은 형용사의 관념을 문명의 규범이 그 말에 부여한 인위적이고 파생적인 의미 말고는 어떤 것과도 결부시키지 못하는 무능을 드러낸다. 그들은 자연 안에서 그 말이 지니는 의미를, 그에 대한 모든 미학적 권리와 더불어 무시하며, 자기네 기독교의 가장 고결한 측면이 제공하는 영적 해석은 말할 것도 없다. 또 다른 이들은, 본질적으로 따지자면 이 소설이 더 이르고 단순한 세대의 인생관이 아니라 십구 세기 말에 만연한 인생관을 구현하고 있다는 주장에 지나지 않는 근거로 반대한다—나로서는 그 주장이 근거가 충분하기를 바랄 따름인 주장이다. 다시 한번 말하건대, 소설은 인상이지 논증이 아니다. 그러니 이 문제는 거기서 멈추어야 한다. 이 부류의 심판자들에 관해 실러가 괴테에게 보낸 편지에 나오는 한 구절이 떠오른다. \"그들은 재현 속에서 오직 자기 자신의 관념만을 찾는 자들이며, 있는 것보다 있어야 할 것을 더 높이 친다. 그러므로 분쟁의 원인은 바로 그 첫 원리에 있으니, 그들과 합의에 이르기란 도무지 불가능하다.\" 그리고 또 이렇게 말한다. \"시적 재현을 판단하면서 누군가가 내적 필연성과 진리보다 무언가를 더 중하게 여기는 것을 보는 순간, 나는 그자와 끝이다.\"\n\n초판 서문의 머리말에서 나는 이 책장들 속의 무언가를 견디지 못할 점잖은 인물이 나타날 가능성을 시사한 바 있다. 그 인물은 앞서 말한 반대자들 가운데 어김없이 나타났다. 한 경우에 그는, \"오직 그것만이 그런 자의 구원을 입증할 수 있는\" 비평적 노력을 내가 기울이지 않은 탓에 이 책을 세 번 통독하기란 불가능하다고 속상해했다. 또 다른 경우에는, 점잖은 이야기 속에 악마의 쇠스랑이라든가 하숙집 고기칼, 수치스럽게 사들인 양산 따위의 천박한 물건들이 등장하는 데 반대했다. 또 다른 대목에서 그는, 불멸의 신들에 관해 불경한 표현이 쓰인 데 대한 슬픔을 더 잘 드러내려고 반시간 동안 기독교도가 된 신사였다. 다만 바로 그 타고난 점잖음이, 그로 하여금 아무리 고마워해도 지나치지 않을 연민의 말로 저자를 용서하게 만들었으니, \"그저 자기 최선을 우리에게 줄 뿐인 것을.\" 이 위대한 비평가에게 나는 이렇게 장담할 수 있다. 신들을, 단수든 복수든, 논리에 맞지 않게 향해 외치는 것은 그가 상상하는 만큼 나만의 독창적인 원죄가 아니라고. 사실 거기에 약간의 국지적 독창성은 있을지 모른다. 다만 셰익스피어가 역사의 권위자라면—아마 아닐 테지만—나는 그 죄가 칠왕국 시대만큼이나 일찍이 웨식스에 들어왔음을 보여 줄 수 있다. 『리어왕』에서 글로스터가, 달리 말하면 그 나라의 왕 이나가 이렇게 말한다.\n\n장난꾸러기 소년들에게 파리가 그러하듯 우리는 신들에게 그러하니, 그들은 재미 삼아 우리를 죽인다.\n\n테스를 주물러 댄 나머지 두세 명은, 대다수 작가와 독자가 기꺼이 잊어버리고 싶어 할 미리 작정한 부류였다. 자칭 문학의 권투 선수들, 그때그때 필요에 따라 자기 신념을 걸치는 자들, 현대판 \"이단자를 두드리는 망치들\", 잠정적인 절반의 성공이 훗날 온전한 성공으로 자라지 못하게 늘 망을 보는 맹세한 낙담 조장자들이었다. 그들은 명백한 의미를 왜곡하고, 위대한 역사적 방법을 실천한다는 명목 아래 인신공격을 일삼는다. 하지만 그들에게도 내세울 명분이, 지킬 특권이, 이어 갈 전통이 있을지 모른다. 세상의 일들이 자기에게 어떻게 비치는지를, 그 어떤 숨은 의도도 없이 받아 적을 뿐인 한낱 이야기꾼은 그중 더러를 못 보고 지나쳤을 수도 있고, 조금도 공격할 뜻이 없을 때조차 순전한 부주의로 거기에 부딪쳤을 수도 있다. 어쩌면 어느 꿈결 같은 한때의 산물인 지나가는 한 가닥 통찰이, 널리 실행에 옮겨진다면, 그런 공격자에게 지위와 이해관계와 가족, 하인, 소, 나귀, 이웃, 또는 이웃의 아내와 관련해 적잖은 불편을 끼칠지도 모른다. 그래서 그는 출판사의 덧문 뒤에 자기 정체를 용감하게 숨긴 채 \"수치다!\" 하고 외치는 것이다. 세상은 어찌나 빽빽한지, 지위가 조금이라도 옮겨지면, 아무리 정당한 진전이라도, 누군가의 발뒤꿈치 부르튼 자리를 쓰리게 한다. 그런 옮겨짐은 흔히 정서에서 비롯되고, 그런 정서는 때로 소설 한 편에서 비롯된다.\n\n1892년 7월.\n\n앞의 말들은 이 이야기의 초기 행로에서, 그 논점들에 대한 공적·사적 비평이 아직 감정에 생생하던 무렵에 쓴 것이다. 한때 했던 말로서, 그 나름의 값어치만큼 그대로 두기로 한다. 다만 아마 지금이라면 쓰지 않았을 것이다. 책이 처음 출간된 이래 흘러간 짧은 시간 동안에도, 그 응답을 불러온 비평가들 가운데 일부는 \"침묵 속으로 내려갔으니\", 마치 그들의 말이나 나의 말이나 무한히 하찮음을 일깨우려는 듯하다.\n\n1895년 1월.\n\n이 소설의 이번 판에는 이전 어느 판에도 실린 적 없는 몇 쪽이 들어 있다. 1891년 서문에 적은 대로 따로 떨어진 삽화들을 한데 모을 때, 이 쪽들은 원래 원고에 있었음에도 빠뜨려졌다. 이 부분은 10장에 나온다.\n\n부제에 관해서는, 앞서 언급한 그 부제 말인데, 한마디 덧붙이자면 그것은 최종 교정쇄를 읽은 뒤 마지막 순간에 붙인 것으로, 여주인공의 성격에 대해 공정한 마음이 남긴 평가, 그러니까 누구도 이의를 달지 않으리라 여겨질 평가였다. 그런데 그것이 이 책에서 무엇보다도 가장 큰 이의를 받았다. 차라리 쓰지 않았던 편이 나았을 것이다. 그러나 그것은 그대로 거기에 있다.\n\n이 소설은 1891년 11월, 세 권짜리 완전판으로 처음 출간되었다.\n\nT.H.\n\n1912년 3월.\n\n처녀\nI\n\n# I\n\n오월 하순 어느 저녁, 한 중년 남자가 섀스턴에서 이웃한 블레이크모어, 또는 블랙무어 골짜기에 있는 말럿 마을로 걸어 돌아가고 있었다. 그를 떠받친 두 다리는 후들거렸고, 걸음걸이에는 한쪽으로 쏠리는 버릇이 있어 그를 직선에서 다소 왼쪽으로 기울게 했다. 그는 무언가 딱히 생각하는 것도 아니면서, 마치 어떤 의견을 확인이라도 하듯 이따금 고개를 야무지게 끄덕였다. 빈 달걀 바구니가 그의 팔에 걸려 있었고, 모자의 보풀은 헝클어졌으며, 챙에서 모자를 벗을 때 엄지손가락이 닿는 자리는 천이 완전히 닳아 떨어져 있었다. 이윽고 회색 암말에 올라탄 나이 든 목사가 그를 마주쳤는데, 목사는 말을 타고 가며 두서없는 가락을 흥얼거리고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오.\" 바구니를 든 남자가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 존 경.\" 목사가 말했다.\n\n걸어가던 남자는 한두 걸음 더 가다가 멈춰 서서 돌아섰다.\n\n\"이보시오, 실례지만 말이오. 지난 장날에도 이 길에서 이맘때 만났을 때, 내가 '안녕하시오' 했더니, 당신이 지금처럼 '안녕하시오, 존 경' 하고 답했소.\"\n\n\"그랬지요.\" 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"그 전에도 한 번, 한 달쯤 전에 그랬고.\"\n\n\"그랬을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 여러 번이나 나를 '존 경'이라 부르는 게 무슨 뜻이오? 나는 그저 평범한 잭 더비필드, 행상꾼일 뿐인데?\"\n\n목사가 한두 걸음 더 가까이 다가왔다.\n\n\"그저 내 변덕이었소.\" 그가 말하고는, 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말을 이었다. \"얼마 전에 새 주(州) 역사책에 쓸 가계도를 뒤지다가 알아낸 사실 때문이었소. 나는 스태그풋 레인의 골동품 연구가, 트링엄 목사라오. 더비필드, 당신이 정말 모른단 말이오? 당신이 더버빌가의 오래되고 기사다운 가문의 직계 후손이라는 것을. 그 가문은 정복왕 윌리엄과 함께 노르망디에서 건너온 그 이름난 기사, 페이건 더버빌 경에게서 그 혈통을 잇고 있으니, 배틀 수도원 명부에도 나와 있소.\"\n\n\"그런 얘긴 처음 듣소!\"\n\n\"음, 사실이오. 잠깐 턱을 들어 보시오, 당신 옆얼굴 윤곽을 좀 더 잘 보게. 그렇지, 그게 바로 더버빌가의 코와 턱이오. 조금 격이 떨어지긴 했지만. 당신 선조는 노르망디의 에스트르마빌라 영주가 글래모건셔를 정복하는 것을 도운 열두 기사 중 하나였소. 당신 가문의 분가들은 잉글랜드의 이 지역 전역에 걸쳐 영지를 가지고 있었고, 그들의 이름은 스티븐 왕 시대의 회계 장부에도 등장하오. 존 왕의 치세에는 그중 하나가 성요한기사단에 영지를 내줄 만큼 부유했고, 에드워드 이세 시대에는 당신의 선조 브라이언이 웨스트민스터로 소환되어 대평의회에 참석했소. 올리버 크롬웰 시대에는 조금 기울었으나 심각한 정도는 아니었고, 찰스 이세의 치세에는 충성심으로 왕립 떡갈나무 기사 작위를 받았소. 그래, 당신 가문에는 대대로 존 경이 이어져 왔으니, 옛 시절처럼 기사 작위가 준남작 작위처럼 세습된다면, 즉 아버지에게서 아들로 기사 작위가 내려진다면, 당신은 지금 존 경이 되었을 거요.\"\n\n\"설마 그럴 리가!\"\n\n\"한마디로,\" 목사가 회초리로 자기 다리를 단호하게 탁 치며 결론지었다. \"잉글랜드에 이만한 가문은 또 없을 거요.\"\n\n\"세상에, 그렇단 말이오?\" 더비필드가 말했다. \"그런데 이렇게 나는 해마다 이리저리 떠밀려 다니며, 마치 이 교구에서 제일 흔해 빠진 놈이라도 되는 양 굴러먹었구려…… 그런데 나에 관한 이 소식이 알려진 지는 얼마나 됐소, 트링엄 목사?\"\n\n목사는, 자기가 아는 한, 그것은 세상의 기억에서 완전히 사라졌으며 알려져 있다고 하기도 어렵다고 설명했다. 그 자신의 조사는 지난봄 어느 날 시작되었는데, 더버빌가의 흥망성쇠를 추적하던 중 더비필드의 짐마차에 적힌 이름을 보고는, 거기서부터 그의 아버지와 할아버지에 관해 알아보게 되었고, 마침내 그 문제에 의심의 여지가 없게 되었다는 것이다.\n\n\"처음에는 이런 쓸데없는 이야기로 당신을 성가시게 하지 않으리라 마음먹었소.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 우리의 충동은 때로 판단보다 너무 강한 법이지요. 그래도 당신이 어쩌면 이미 그 일을 조금쯤 알고 있을지도 모른다고 생각했소.\"\n\n\"음, 우리 집안이 블랙무어로 오기 전에 더 나은 시절을 보냈다는 얘긴 한두 번 들은 적이 있긴 하오. 하지만 나는 그게 우리가 지금은 말 한 마리만 두지만 한때는 두 마리를 두었다는 뜻쯤으로 여기고 신경 쓰지 않았소. 집에 오래된 은수저 하나랑, 오래된 문장이 새겨진 인장도 하나 있긴 하오. 하지만 원, 수저랑 인장이 다 뭐란 말이오?…… 그런데 나하고 그 고귀한 더버빌가가 줄곧 한 핏줄이었다니. 우리 증조부가 비밀을 가지고 있었고, 어디서 왔는지 말하기 싫어했다고 하더이다…… 그런데 목사 양반, 외람되지만 우리가 지금 어디서 연기를 피우고 사오? 그러니까, 우리 더버빌가가 어디서 사오?\"\n\n\"당신들은 아무 데서도 살지 않소. 당신들은—주(州)의 명문 가문으로서는—멸절되었소.\"\n\n\"그거 안됐구려.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그 허울 좋은 가문 연대기에서 말하는 대로, 남계가 끊겼소. 그러니까 가라앉았고, 몰락한 것이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 어디 누워 있소?\"\n\n\"킹스비어서브그린힐에, 줄줄이 늘어선 당신네 납골당에, 퍼벡 대리석 닫집 아래 조각상들과 함께.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리 가문의 저택과 영지는 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"하나도 없소.\"\n\n\"오? 땅도 없단 말이오?\"\n\n\"하나도. 비록 앞서 말했듯 한때는 차고 넘치게 가지고 있었지만 말이오, 당신네 가문은 수많은 분가로 이루어져 있었으니까. 이 주(州)에도 킹스비어에 당신네 영지가 하나 있었고, 셔턴에 또 하나, 밀폰드에 또 하나, 럴스테드에 또 하나, 웰브리지에 또 하나가 있었소.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리가 다시 우리 것을 되찾게 될까요?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 나도 모르오!\"\n\n\"그럼 그 일에 대해 내가 뭘 하는 게 좋겠소, 목사 양반?\" 잠시 뒤에 더비필드가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아무것도, 아무것도 하지 마시오. '강하던 자들이 어찌하여 엎드러졌는고' 하는 생각으로 스스로를 다스리는 것 말고는. 그건 지방 사학자와 계보학자에게나 다소 흥미로운 사실일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니오. 이 주(州)의 오두막살이 농민들 중에도 거의 같은 광채를 지닌 집안이 여럿 있소. 잘 가시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 기념으로 발길을 돌려 나와 함께 맥주나 한 쿼트 하지 않겠소, 트링엄 목사? '순수한 한 방울' 주막에 아주 맛 좋은 술이 한 통 들어와 있소. 물론, 정말이지 롤리버 주막만큼 좋진 않지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"아니, 고맙소. 오늘 저녁은 됐소, 더비필드. 당신은 이미 충분히 마셨소.\" 그렇게 끝맺고는, 목사는 이 진기한 옛이야기 한 토막을 늘어놓은 것이 분별 있는 짓이었나 의심하며 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n목사가 가버리자, 더비필드는 깊은 몽상에 잠겨 몇 걸음 걷다가, 길가 풀 둑에 앉아 바구니를 앞에 내려놓았다. 몇 분 뒤 저 멀리서 한 젊은이가 더비필드가 걸어온 것과 같은 방향으로 걸어오는 것이 보였다. 더비필드는 그를 보고 손을 들어 올렸고, 소년은 걸음을 재촉하여 가까이 다가왔다.\n\n\"얘야, 그 바구니 좀 들어라! 너한테 심부름을 시켜야겠다.\"\n\n가느다란 막대기 같은 풋내기는 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. \"당신이 누군데, 존 더비필드, 나한테 이래라저래라 하고 '얘야' 따위로 부르는 거요? 내가 당신 이름 아는 만큼 당신도 내 이름 잘 알면서!\"\n\n\"안다고, 안다 이거지? 그게 바로 비밀이야, 그게 비밀이라고! 자, 내 명령대로 하고, 내가 시킬 전갈을 받아라…… 그래, 프레드, 너한테 말해 줘도 상관없겠지, 그 비밀이란 내가 고귀한 가문의 후손이라는 거다. 바로 오늘 오후에, 정오 지나서 내가 막 알아낸 일이지.\" 그렇게 선언하면서 더비필드는 앉은 자세에서 몸을 눕혀, 데이지꽃 사이 둑 위에 호사스럽게 길게 뻗었다.\n\n소년은 더비필드 앞에 서서, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 길쭉한 몸을 찬찬히 바라보았다.\n\n\"더버빌가의 존 경, 그게 바로 나다.\" 드러누운 남자가 말을 이었다. \"기사가 곧 준남작이라면 말이지. 그리고 실제로 그렇거든. 나에 관한 게 죄다 역사책에 적혀 있어. 얘야, 킹스비어서브그린힐이라는 데를 아느냐?\"\n\n\"네. 그린힐 장(場)에 가본 적 있어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 도시의 교회 아래에 누워 있는 게……\"\n\n\"제가 말하는 데는 도시가 아니에요. 적어도 제가 갔을 땐 아니었어요. 외눈박이처럼 깜박거리는 자그마한 동네였는걸요.\"\n\n\"그 동네야 어떻든 상관없다, 얘야, 그게 지금 우리가 따질 문제가 아니야. 바로 그 교구의 교회 아래에 내 선조들이 누워 있어, 수백 명이나, 갑옷을 입고 보석을 두른 채, 수 톤씩이나 나가는 커다란 납관 속에 말이야. 사우스웨식스 주(州) 어디에도 나만큼 웅장하고 고귀한 해골을 집안에 둔 사람은 없어.\"\n\n\"오?\"\n\n\"자, 이제 그 바구니를 들고 말럿으로 가서, '순수한 한 방울' 주막에 도착하거든, 즉시 말과 마차를 보내 나를 집으로 데려가라고 해라. 그리고 마차 바닥에는 작은 병에 럼주 한 모금 담아 넣고, 내 외상으로 달아 두라고 해. 그걸 마치거든 바구니를 들고 우리 집으로 가서, 내 아내한테 그 빨래는 치워 두라고 해라, 끝낼 필요 없다고, 내가 집에 올 때까지 기다리라고 해, 들려줄 소식이 있으니까.\"\n\n소년이 망설이는 태도로 서 있자, 더비필드는 주머니에 손을 넣어 만성적으로 몇 개 안 되는 그의 동전 중 하나인 일 실링을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"수고비다, 얘야.\"\n\n이것이 그 상황에 대한 젊은이의 평가를 바꿔 놓았다.\n\n\"네, 존 경. 감사합니다. 또 해드릴 일이 있나요, 존 경?\"\n\n\"집에 가서 내가 저녁으로—그래, 구할 수 있으면 양 내장 튀김이 좋겠다고 전해라. 그게 안 되면 선지순대, 그것도 안 되면 뭐, 곱창이라도 괜찮다고.\"\n\n\"네, 존 경.\"\n\n소년이 바구니를 들었고, 그가 출발할 때 마을 쪽에서 금관악대의 가락이 들려왔다.\n\n\"저게 뭐냐?\" 더비필드가 말했다. \"설마 나 때문은 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"여자들 모임 행진이에요, 존 경. 아니, 당신 따님도 회원 중 하나잖아요.\"\n\n\"아 그래, 더 큰 일들을 생각하느라 까맣게 잊고 있었구나! 그럼 어서 말럿으로 가서 그 마차를 시켜 두어라, 그러면 나도 마차를 몰고 가서 그 모임을 한번 둘러볼지도 모르겠다.\"\n\n소년은 떠났고, 더비필드는 저녁 햇살 속 풀과 데이지꽃 위에 누워 기다렸다. 한참 동안 그 길로는 사람 하나 지나가지 않았고, 푸른 언덕들이 두른 가장자리 안에서 들리는 사람 소리라곤 희미한 악대 가락뿐이었다.\nII\n\n# II\n\n말럿 마을은, 앞서 말한 아름다운 블레이크모어, 또는 블랙무어 골짜기의 북동쪽 굽이굽이 사이에 자리 잡고 있었으니, 빙 둘러싸여 외진 고장으로, 런던에서 네 시간이면 닿는 거리인데도 대부분 아직 관광객이나 풍경화가의 발길이 닿지 않은 곳이었다.\n\n이 골짜기는, 어쩌면 한여름 가뭄철을 빼고는, 그것을 둘러싼 언덕 꼭대기에서 내려다볼 때 가장 잘 알게 되는 골짜기다. 궂은 날씨에 안내자도 없이 그 깊은 곳으로 무작정 거니노라면, 좁고 구불구불하고 진창인 길에 불만을 품기 십상이다.\n\n들판이 결코 시들지 않고 샘이 결코 마르지 않는 이 비옥하고 아늑한 고장은, 남쪽으로 햄블던 힐, 불배로, 네틀콤투트, 도그버리, 하이 스토이, 버브 다운의 봉우리들을 품은 험준한 백악질 산등성이로 경계 지어진다. 해안에서부터 석회질 구릉지와 곡창지대를 따라 북쪽으로 스무 마일을 터벅터벅 걸어온 나그네가, 문득 이 가파른 절벽 가장자리 하나에 다다르면, 발아래 지도처럼 펼쳐진, 자기가 지나온 곳과는 완전히 다른 고장을 보고 놀라며 기뻐한다. 그의 등 뒤로는 언덕들이 탁 트여 있고, 햇볕은 들판이 어찌나 넓은지 울타리 없는 풍경처럼 보이는 그 위로 이글거리며 내리쬐며, 오솔길들은 희고, 산울타리는 낮고 가지가 엮여 있으며, 대기는 빛깔이 없다. 그런데 여기 이 골짜기에서는, 세상이 더 작고 더 섬세한 규모로 지어진 듯하다. 들판은 자그마한 목초지에 지나지 않아, 어찌나 작은지 이 높이에서 보면 그 산울타리들이 더 옅은 초록빛 풀밭을 뒤덮은 짙은 초록 실그물처럼 보인다. 아래쪽 대기는 나른하고, 어찌나 쪽빛으로 물들었는지 화가들이 중경(中景)이라 부르는 부분조차 그 빛깔을 띠며, 그 너머 지평선은 가장 짙은 군청색이다. 경작지는 적고 좁으니, 약간의 예외를 빼면 눈앞에 펼쳐지는 것은 큰 언덕과 골짜기 안의 작은 언덕과 골짜기를 외투처럼 덮은, 풀과 나무의 넓고 풍요로운 덩어리다. 이러한 곳이 바로 블랙무어 골짜기다.\n\n이 고장은 지형적으로 못지않게 역사적으로도 흥미롭다. 이 골짜기는 옛날에 백사슴 숲이라 불렸는데, 헨리 삼세 치세의 진기한 전설에서 비롯된 이름이다. 그 전설에서 토머스 드 라 린드라는 자가, 왕이 몰다가 살려 준 아름다운 백사슴을 죽인 것이 무거운 벌금의 빌미가 되었다. 그 시절에, 그리고 비교적 최근까지도, 이 고장은 빽빽이 숲으로 덮여 있었다. 지금도 그 비탈에 아직 살아남은 오래된 떡갈나무 숲과 불규칙한 수목 띠에서, 그리고 그 많은 목초지에 그늘을 드리우는 속 빈 나무들에서, 옛 모습의 자취를 찾아볼 수 있다.\n\n숲은 사라졌으나, 그 그늘에서 비롯된 오랜 풍습은 더러 남아 있다. 하지만 많은 것이 모습을 바꾸거나 위장한 형태로만 남아 있을 뿐이다. 가령 오월제 춤은, 이날 오후에, 그곳에서 모임 잔치, 또는 '모임 행진'이라 불리는 모습으로 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n이는 말럿의 젊은 주민들에게는 흥미로운 행사였으나, 정작 의식에 참여하는 이들은 그 참된 의미를 알아채지 못했다. 그 특이함은 해마다 줄지어 걷고 춤추는 풍습을 이어 온 데 있다기보다, 회원이 오로지 여자들뿐이라는 데 있었다. 남자들의 모임에서는 그런 행사가, 비록 사그라드는 중이긴 해도, 그리 드물지 않았다. 그러나 연약한 성(性)의 타고난 수줍음 때문인지, 아니면 남자 친척들의 빈정대는 태도 때문인지, 아직 남아 있는 여자들의 모임은 (만일 다른 모임이 있었다 한들) 이 영광이자 절정을 다 빼앗기고 말았다. 오로지 말럿의 모임만이 살아남아 그 고장의 곡물 축제를 떠받쳤다. 그것은 공제조합으로서는 아니더라도 어떤 종류의 봉헌 자매회로서, 수백 년 동안 행진해 왔다. 그리고 지금도 행진하고 있었다.\n\n이 무리는 모두 흰 가운을 입고 있었으니, 명랑함과 오월이 한뜻이던 옛 시절, 멀리 내다보는 습관이 감정을 단조로운 평균으로 깎아내리기 전의 시절에서 살아남은 흥겨운 유물이었다. 그들이 처음으로 제 모습을 드러낸 것은 둘씩 짝지어 교구를 도는 행렬 행진에서였다. 햇빛이 초록빛 산울타리와 덩굴이 얽힌 집들의 정면을 배경으로 그들의 자태를 비추자, 이상과 현실이 살짝 부딪쳤다. 무리 전체가 흰옷을 입었다지만, 그들 가운데 똑같은 흰빛은 둘도 없었으니까. 어떤 것은 순백에 가까웠고, 어떤 것은 푸르스름하게 창백했으며, 나이 든 이들이 입은 어떤 것은 (아마도 여러 해 접힌 채 묵혀 두었던 것이리라) 송장 같은 빛깔에, 조지 왕조풍으로 기울어 있었다.\n\n흰 드레스라는 특징 말고도, 여자와 처녀 들은 저마다 오른손에는 껍질 벗긴 버드나무 가지를, 왼손에는 흰 꽃 한 다발을 들고 있었다. 앞엣것의 껍질을 벗기는 일과 뒤엣것을 고르는 일에는 저마다의 정성이 들어 있었다.\n\n행렬 속에는 중년의, 심지어 노년의 여자도 몇 있었으니, 세월과 고생에 시달려 은백색으로 곱슬거리는 머리칼과 주름진 얼굴은, 이런 발랄한 자리에서 거의 기괴한, 분명 애처로운 모습이었다. 어쩌면 참되게 보자면, \"내게는 그것들에서 아무런 즐거움이 없노라\" 하고 말해야 할 세월이 다가오는 이 근심 많고 산전수전 겪은 이들 하나하나에 대해, 그 젊은 동무들에 대해서보다 더 많은 것을 헤아리고 들려줄 수 있었으리라. 하지만 여기서는 나이 든 이들은 지나치고, 그 보디스 아래 생명이 빠르고 따뜻하게 고동치는 이들에게로 가자.\n\n과연 젊은 처녀들이 이 무리의 다수를 이루었으니, 무성한 머리칼은 햇빛 속에서 금빛과 검은빛과 갈색의 온갖 색조를 비추었다. 어떤 이는 아름다운 눈을, 어떤 이는 아름다운 코를, 어떤 이는 아름다운 입과 자태를 지녔으되, 그 모두를 지닌 이는 거의, 아니 아예 없었다. 이렇게 거칠게 대중의 눈길에 드러난 자리에서 입술을 어찌 두어야 할지 난감해하는 모습, 고개를 가누지 못하고 얼굴에서 자의식을 떼어 내지 못하는 모습이 그들에게 뚜렷이 보였으니, 그들이 여러 눈길에 익숙지 않은 진짜 시골 처녀들임을 드러냈다.\n\n그리고 그들 하나하나가 바깥으로 햇볕에 데워졌듯, 저마다 제 영혼이 쪼일 작은 사사로운 해 하나씩을 품고 있었다. 어떤 꿈, 어떤 애정, 어떤 취미, 적어도 어떤 아득하고 먼 희망 하나를. 그 희망은 비록 굶주려 사그라들지언정, 희망이란 으레 그렇듯, 여전히 살아남아 있었다. 그들은 모두 명랑했고, 그중 많은 이가 들떠 있었다.\n\n그들이 '순수한 한 방울' 주막을 돌아, 큰길에서 벗어나 작은 쪽문을 지나 목초지로 들어서려 할 때, 한 여자가 말했다.\n\n\"하느님 맙소사! 아니, 테스 더비필드, 저기 네 아버지가 마차를 타고 집에 가시는 거 아냐!\"\n\n이 외침에 무리 중 한 젊은 회원이 고개를 돌렸다. 그녀는 곱고 아름다운 처녀였으니—어쩌면 다른 몇몇보다 더 아름답진 않았을지 모르나—작약 같은 도톰한 입과 크고 순진한 눈이 빛깔과 모양에 웅변을 더해 주었다. 그녀는 머리에 빨간 리본을 매고 있었으니, 그 흰옷 무리 중 그토록 두드러진 장식을 뽐낼 수 있는 이는 그녀뿐이었다. 그녀가 둘러보니, 더비필드가 '순수한 한 방울'의 이륜마차를 타고 길을 따라 가는 것이 보였는데, 마차는 가운 소매를 팔꿈치 위로 걷어붙인, 머리가 곱슬곱슬한 다부진 처녀가 몰고 있었다. 그녀는 그 주막의 명랑한 하녀로, 잡일꾼 노릇을 하다가 때때로 마부와 마구간지기 일도 맡았다. 더비필드는 등을 기댄 채 호사스레 눈을 감고, 머리 위로 손을 흔들며, 느릿한 영창조로 노래하고 있었다.\n\n\"나는—킹스비어에—커다란—가문—납골당이—있다네—그리고—거기—납관—속에—기사—작위—받은—선조들이—있다네!\"\n\n모임 사람들이 키득거렸으나, 테스라 불린 처녀만은 아니었다. 아버지가 사람들 눈에 바보짓을 하고 있다는 생각에 그녀 안에서 뜨거운 기운이 서서히 차오르는 듯했다.\n\n\"피곤하셔서 그래.\" 그녀가 황급히 말했다. \"오늘은 우리 집 말이 쉬어야 해서, 그래서 마차를 얻어 타고 집에 가시는 거야.\"\n\n\"테스, 네 순진함에 복이나 받아라.\" 동무들이 말했다. \"장 보고 거나하게 한잔 걸치신 거지. 하하하!\"\n\n\"이봐, 그이에 대해 농담을 한마디라도 하면, 나 너희랑 한 발짝도 더 안 걸을 거야!\" 테스가 외쳤고, 양 볼에 오른 핏기가 얼굴과 목까지 번졌다. 이내 그녀의 눈에 물기가 차오르고, 시선이 땅으로 떨어졌다. 자기들이 정말로 그녀를 아프게 했음을 알아챈 그들은 더는 말하지 않았고, 다시 질서가 잡혔다. 테스의 자존심은 다시 고개를 돌려, 아버지에게 무슨 뜻이 있었다면 그게 무엇인지 알아보는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 그렇게 그녀는 무리 전체와 함께 풀밭에서 춤을 추기로 되어 있는 마당으로 나아갔다. 그곳에 다다를 무렵 그녀는 평정을 되찾아, 가지로 옆 사람을 톡 치며 여느 때처럼 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n테스 더비필드는 이 시기 그녀의 삶에서, 경험에 물들지 않은 한낱 감정의 그릇이었다. 마을 학교를 다녔는데도 그녀의 혀에는 어느 정도 사투리가 남아 있었으니, 이 지역 사투리의 특징적인 억양은 대략 UR이라는 음절로 옮길 수 있는 발성이었는데, 아마 인간의 언어에서 찾을 수 있는 그 어떤 발음 못지않게 풍부한 소리였다. 이 음절을 타고난, 도톰하게 오므린 짙은 붉은 입은 아직 그 분명한 모양으로 자리 잡지 않았고, 그녀의 아랫입술은 한마디 한 뒤 두 입술이 맞닿을 때면 윗입술 가운데를 위로 밀어 올리는 버릇이 있었다.\n\n그녀의 모습에는 아직 어린 시절의 단계들이 어른거렸다. 오늘 이렇게 걸어가는 동안에도, 그 발랄하고 아름다운 여인다움에도 불구하고, 때때로 그녀의 볼에서 열두 살의 그녀를, 눈에서 반짝이는 아홉 살의 그녀를 볼 수 있었고, 심지어 다섯 살의 그녀가 이따금 입가의 곡선 위로 스쳐 지나가기도 했다.\n\n그러나 이것을 아는 이는 적었고, 헤아리는 이는 더더욱 적었다. 소수의, 주로 낯선 이들이 무심코 지나치며 그녀를 한참 바라보다가, 그 싱그러움에 잠시 매혹되어, 과연 그녀를 다시 볼 수 있을까 궁금해하곤 했다. 하지만 거의 모든 이에게 그녀는 곱고 그림 같은 시골 처녀, 그 이상은 아니었다.\n\n마구간지기 처녀가 모는 개선마차에 탄 더비필드는 더 보이지도 들리지도 않았고, 모임이 정해진 자리로 들어서자 춤이 시작되었다. 무리에 남자가 없었으므로 처녀들은 처음에는 서로 짝지어 춤을 추었으나, 일을 마칠 시각이 다가오자 마을의 남자 주민들이, 다른 한량들과 길손들과 더불어 그 자리로 모여들어, 짝을 흥정해 보려는 기색을 보였다.\n\n이 구경꾼들 가운데 신분이 좀 더 높은 세 젊은이가 있었으니, 어깨에 작은 배낭을 멘 끈으로 묶고 손에는 튼튼한 지팡이를 들고 있었다. 서로 전반적으로 닮은 점과 연이은 나이로 보아, 그들이—실제로 그러했듯이—형제라는 것을 거의 짐작할 수 있었다. 맏이는 정식 부목사의 흰 넥타이와 높은 조끼, 챙이 얇은 모자 차림이었고, 둘째는 평범한 대학생 차림이었다. 셋째이자 막내의 행색은 그를 무어라 규정하기에 좀처럼 충분치 않았다. 그의 눈과 옷차림에는 어디에도 매이지 않고 갇히지 않은 데가 있어, 그가 아직 제 직업의 홈으로 들어가는 입구를 찾지 못했음을 내비쳤다. 그에 대해서는 그가 무언가를, 그리고 모든 것을 두서없이 시험 삼아 공부하는 학생이라는 것 정도밖에 점칠 수 없었으리라.\n\n이 세 형제는 우연히 마주친 이들에게, 자기들이 성령강림절 휴가를 블랙무어 골짜기를 가로지르는 도보 여행으로 보내는 중이며, 북동쪽의 섀스턴 마을에서 남서쪽으로 길을 잡았다고 말했다.\n\n그들은 큰길가 문에 기대어, 이 춤과 흰 드레스를 입은 처녀들이 무슨 뜻인지 물었다. 형 둘은 잠깐 이상은 머무를 생각이 없는 게 분명했으나, 남자 짝도 없이 춤추는 한 무리의 처녀들이라는 광경이 셋째에게는 흥미로워 보였는지, 좀처럼 발걸음을 옮기려 들지 않았다. 그는 배낭의 끈을 풀어 지팡이와 함께 산울타리 둑에 내려놓고 문을 열었다.\n\n\"뭐 하려고 그래, 에인절?\" 맏이가 물었다.\n\n\"가서 저들이랑 한바탕 춤추고 싶은데. 우리 다 같이 추면 어때—일이 분만—그리 오래 붙들리진 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"안 돼, 안 돼, 말도 안 되는 소리!\" 첫째가 말했다. \"한 무리 촌뜨기 계집애들과 대놓고 춤을 춘다니—누가 보기라도 하면 어쩌려고! 어서 가자, 안 그러면 스타우어캐슬에 닿기도 전에 날이 저물 거야, 거기보다 더 가까운 데는 묵을 데도 없어. 게다가 내가 일부러 책까지 챙겨 왔으니, 자기 전에 『불가지론에 대한 반박』을 한 장은 더 읽어야 한다고.\"\n\n\"알았어—오 분 안에 너랑 커스버트를 따라잡을게, 멈추지 마. 약속할게, 꼭 그럴게, 펠릭스.\"\n\n형 둘은 마지못해 그를 두고 걸어갔으니, 동생을 뒤따라오기 쉽게 그의 배낭까지 들어 주었고, 막내는 들판으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"이거 천 번 아까운 일인걸요.\" 춤이 잠시 멎자마자 그가 가장 가까이 있는 두세 처녀에게 점잖게 말했다. \"그대들의 짝은 어디 있나요, 아가씨들?\"\n\n\"아직 일을 안 마쳤어요.\" 그중 가장 대담한 처녀가 답했다. \"곧 여기로 올 거예요. 그때까지, 나리께서 짝이 되어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"그럼요. 하지만 이 많은 분들 중 나 하나가 무슨 소용이겠소!\"\n\n\"없는 것보다야 낫지요. 자기랑 같은 부류끼리 마주 보고 발이나 맞추면서, 껴안고 어우러지는 것도 하나 없으면 참 서글픈 일인걸요. 자, 골라 보세요.\"\n\n\"쉿, 그렇게 나서지 좀 마!\" 더 수줍은 처녀가 말했다.\n\n이렇게 청을 받은 젊은이는 그들을 훑어보며 좀 가려 보려 했으나, 무리가 죄다 처음 보는 이들이라 좀처럼 제대로 가려낼 수가 없었다. 그는 거의 손에 닿는 대로 첫 번째 처녀를 골랐는데, 그것은 말을 건넨 처녀가 기대한 것과 달리 그녀가 아니었고, 또한 공교롭게도 테스 더비필드도 아니었다. 가문이며, 조상의 해골이며, 기념비의 기록이며, 더버빌가의 이목구비도, 아직은 테스의 삶의 싸움에서, 흔해 빠진 시골 처녀들을 제치고 춤 상대 하나 끌어당겨 줄 만큼조차 그녀에게 도움이 되지 못했다. 빅토리아 시대의 돈이 받쳐 주지 않는 노르만 혈통이란 그 정도였다.\n\n다른 처녀들을 무색하게 만든 그 처녀의 이름은, 그게 무엇이었든, 전해 내려오지 않는다. 그러나 그녀는 그날 저녁 남자 짝이라는 호사를 처음 누린 이로서 모두의 부러움을 샀다. 그러나 본보기의 힘이 어찌나 컸던지, 훼방꾼이 끼어들지 않았을 때는 문 안으로 들어서기를 서두르지 않던 마을 젊은이들이, 이제는 재빨리 끼어들어, 이내 짝들 사이에 시골 청년들이 눈에 띄게 섞여 들었으니, 마침내 모임에서 가장 못생긴 여자조차 더는 짝춤에서 남자 쪽 자리에 서서 발을 맞출 필요가 없게 되었다.\n\n교회 시계가 종을 쳤다. 그 순간 학생은 가봐야겠다고 말했다. 그동안 자기를 잊고 있었던 것이다. 일행과 합류해야 했다. 그가 춤에서 빠져나올 때 그의 눈길이 테스 더비필드에게 닿았는데, 사실대로 말하자면 그녀의 그 큰 눈동자에는 그가 자기를 고르지 않은 데 대한 희미한 원망의 기색이 어려 있었다. 그 역시 그제야, 그녀가 뒤로 물러서 있던 탓에 그녀를 알아보지 못한 것이 안타까웠다. 그런 생각을 품은 채 그는 목초지를 떠났다.\n\n너무 오래 지체한 탓에 그는 서쪽으로 난 오솔길을 쏜살같이 달려 내려가, 이내 움푹한 곳을 지나 다음 언덕을 올랐다. 아직 형들을 따라잡지는 못했으나, 숨을 고르려고 잠시 멈춰 뒤를 돌아보았다. 초록 마당에서 처녀들의 흰 자태가, 자기가 그들 틈에 있을 때 빙빙 돌던 그대로 빙빙 도는 것이 보였다. 그들은 벌써 그를 까맣게 잊은 듯했다.\n\n어쩌면 한 사람만 빼고, 모두가. 이 흰 형체는 산울타리 옆에 홀로 떨어져 서 있었다. 그 자리로 보아 그는 그것이 자기가 춤추지 않은 예쁜 처녀임을 알았다. 사소한 일이었으나, 그래도 그는 그녀가 자기의 무심함에 상처받았음을 본능적으로 느꼈다. 그녀에게 청했더라면 좋았을 것을, 그녀의 이름이라도 물어보았더라면 좋았을 것을, 하고 그는 바랐다. 그녀는 어찌나 다소곳하고, 어찌나 표정이 풍부하던지, 얇은 흰 가운 차림이 어찌나 부드러워 보이던지, 그는 자기가 어리석게 굴었다고 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 어쩔 수 없는 일이었고, 그는 몸을 돌려 빠른 걸음으로 옮기며 그 일을 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버렸다.\nIII\n\n# III\n\n테스 더비필드로 말하자면, 그녀는 그 일을 그렇게 쉽사리 마음에서 몰아내지 못했다. 짝이야 얼마든지 있었을 테지만, 그녀는 오랫동안 다시 춤출 마음이 나지 않았다. 하지만 아아! 그들은 그 낯선 젊은이만큼 곱게 말하지 못했다. 그 젊은 이방인의 멀어져 가는 모습이 언덕 위에서 햇살에 삼켜지고 나서야, 그녀는 잠시의 슬픔을 떨치고 짝이 되고자 하는 이에게 그러마고 답했다.\n\n그녀는 땅거미가 질 때까지 동무들과 함께 남아, 어느 정도 흥겹게 춤에 어울렸다. 다만 아직 마음이 온전했던 터라, 그녀는 순전히 춤 그 자체를 위해 한 가락 밟는 것을 즐겼을 뿐, 구애를 받고 마음을 얻은 처녀들의 그 \"달콤한 괴로움, 쓰디쓴 단맛, 기분 좋은 아픔, 즐거운 시름\"을 보면서도, 정작 자기 자신이 그런 일에서 어떤 일을 겪을 수 있는지는 거의 헤아리지 못했다. 자기 손을 잡고 지그를 추려는 청년들의 다툼과 옥신각신은 그녀에게 그저 재밋거리였을 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었다. 그리고 그들이 사나워지면 그녀는 그들을 나무랐다.\n\n그녀는 더 늦게까지 남아 있을 수도 있었겠지만, 아버지의 이상한 행색과 거동이 떠올라 처녀의 마음을 불안하게 했고, 아버지가 어찌 되었을까 궁금해하며 그녀는 춤추는 무리에서 슬며시 빠져나와, 부모의 오두막이 있는 마을 끝자락으로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n아직 수십 야드나 떨어져 있는데도, 그녀가 두고 온 것과는 다른 가락의 소리가 들려왔다. 그녀가 잘 아는, 너무도 잘 아는 소리였다. 그것은 집 안에서 들려오는 일정한 쿵쿵 소리였으니, 돌바닥 위에서 요람을 거칠게 흔들어 대는 데서 나는 것이었고, 그 움직임에 박자를 맞추어 여자 목소리 하나가 활기찬 갤로파드 가락으로 즐겨 부르는 노래 「점박이 암소」를 부르고 있었다.\n\n나는 보았네 그녀가 저—기 푸—른 숲에 누—워 있는 걸, 오라, 내 사랑! 내가 어디인지 일러—줄 테니!\n\n요람 흔드는 소리와 노래가 잠시 동시에 그치고, 그 가락 대신 가장 높은 목청의 외침이 들려오곤 했다.\n\n\"하느님이 네 다이아몬드 같은 눈에 복을 주시기를! 네 밀랍 같은 볼에도! 네 앵두 같은 입에도! 네 큐피드 같은 허벅지에도! 네 복된 몸뚱이 구석구석에도!\"\n\n이 기원이 끝나면 다시 요람 흔드는 소리와 노래가 시작되고, 「점박이 암소」가 전처럼 이어졌다. 테스가 문을 열고 안쪽 발판 위에 멈춰 서서 그 광경을 살필 때, 사정은 이러했다.\n\n가락이 울려 퍼지는데도, 집 안은 처녀의 감각에 이루 말할 수 없는 황량함으로 다가왔다. 들판의 잔칫날 흥겨움에서—흰 가운, 꽃다발, 버드나무 가지, 풀밭 위에서 빙빙 돌던 움직임, 낯선 이를 향한 은은한 감정의 번뜩임에서—초 한 자루 켜진 이 누런 우울의 광경에 이르기까지, 이 무슨 낙차란 말인가! 이 대비의 충격에 더해, 그녀에게는 자기가 더 일찍 돌아와 이 집안일에서 어머니를 돕지 않고 바깥에서 제 흥에 겨워 놀았다는 차가운 자책이 밀려왔다.\n\n거기, 테스가 두고 갔던 그대로, 어머니가 아이들 무리 한가운데서, 월요일에 시작한 빨래 통 위에 구부정히 서 있었으니, 그 빨래는 늘 그렇듯 이제 주말까지 질질 끌려와 있었다. 바로 그 통에서, 어제—테스는 끔찍한 회한의 가책을 느끼며 깨달았다—지금 자기 등에 걸친 바로 그 흰 드레스가 나왔으니, 그녀가 축축한 풀밭에서 치마 자락에 풀물을 그토록 함부로 들인 그 옷이, 어머니가 손수 짜서 다림질해 준 것이었다.\n\n여느 때처럼 더비필드 부인은 통 옆에서 한 발로 균형을 잡고 있었고, 다른 발은 앞서 말한 막내를 어르는 일에 매여 있었다. 요람의 흔들개는 그 돌바닥 위에서 그토록 많은 해 동안, 그토록 많은 아이들의 무게를 받아 가며 고된 임무를 다한 끝에 거의 평평하게 닳아 버렸고, 그 탓에 요람이 한 번 흔들릴 때마다 엄청난 덜컹임이 따라붙어, 아기를 베틀의 북처럼 이쪽저쪽으로 내던졌으니, 더비필드 부인이 제 노래에 흥이 올라, 비눗물에 종일 부대낀 끝에 남은 온 힘을 다해 흔들개를 밟아 댔기 때문이다.\n\n요람은 딸깍딸깍, 딸깍딸깍 흔들렸고, 촛불은 키를 한껏 늘이며 위아래로 너울너울 춤추기 시작했으며, 부인의 팔꿈치에서는 물이 뚝뚝 떨어졌고, 노래는 한 절의 끝을 향해 내달렸으니, 그동안 더비필드 부인은 딸을 바라보았다. 어린 자식들의 짐을 진 지금까지도, 조앤 더비필드는 가락을 열렬히 사랑하는 사람이었다. 바깥세상에서 블랙무어 골짜기로 흘러 들어온 노래 한 가락도, 테스의 어머니는 일주일이면 그 곡조를 익혀 버렸다.\n\n그 여인의 얼굴에는 아직도 젊은 날의 싱그러움이, 심지어 예쁨까지도 희미하게 비쳐 나왔으니, 테스가 뽐낼 수 있는 그 미모도 대부분 어머니에게서 물려받은 것이며, 따라서 기사답지도 역사적이지도 않은 것임을 짐작케 했다.\n\n\"제가 요람을 흔들게요, 엄마.\" 딸이 부드럽게 말했다. \"아니면 좋은 옷 벗고 짜는 거 도와드릴까요? 진작 다 끝내신 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n어머니는 그토록 오래 집안일을 자기 혼자 떠맡게 했다고 테스를 원망하지 않았다. 실은 조앤은 어느 때든 그 일로 딸을 나무라는 일이 거의 없었으니, 자기 일의 짐을 더는 본능적인 방법이 그 일을 미루어 두는 데 있었던 터라, 테스의 도움이 없는 것을 별로 아쉬워하지 않았던 것이다. 그런데 오늘 밤은 여느 때보다도 한결 들떠 있었다. 그 어머니의 눈빛에는 처녀로서는 알 수 없는 몽롱함과 골똘함과 들뜸이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"음, 네가 와서 다행이다.\" 마지막 음이 입에서 빠져나가자마자 어머니가 말했다. \"네 아버지를 데리러 가야 하는데, 그보다 더한 건, 너한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려주고 싶구나. 알고 나면 너도 어지간히 기뻐할 거다, 우리 귀염둥아!\" (더비필드 부인은 늘 사투리로 말했다. 런던에서 교육받은 여선생 밑에서 국립학교 육 학년 과정을 마친 그녀의 딸은 두 가지 언어를 썼으니, 집에서는 어느 정도 사투리를, 밖에서나 지체 높은 이들에게는 표준 영어를 썼다.)\n\n\"제가 나가 있던 사이에요?\" 테스가 물었다.\n\n\"그래!\"\n\"오늘 오후에 아버지가 저 마차에서 그렇게 허수아비 꼴을 하신 거랑 무슨 상관이 있어요? 왜 그러신 거예요? 부끄러워서 땅속으로 꺼져 버리고 싶었다고요!\"\n\n\"그게 다 그 일의 한 부분이었지! 우리가 이 주(州) 전체에서 제일가는 양반 가문으로 밝혀졌단다—올리버 그럼블 시대보다도 한참 전, 이교도 투르크인들 시절까지 거슬러 올라가는—기념비며, 납골당이며, 가문(家紋)이며, 문장(紋章)이며, 그 밖에 별의별 것까지 다 있는 가문 말이다. 성(聖) 찰스 시대에는 우리가 왕립 떡갈나무 기사 작위까지 받았고, 우리 진짜 이름은 더버빌이야!…… 어때, 가슴이 부풀지 않니? 네 아버지가 삯마차를 타고 집에 온 게 다 그 때문이지, 사람들 짐작처럼 술을 마셔서가 아니란다.\"\n\n\"그건 다행이네요. 그게 우리한테 무슨 득이 될까요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼! 거기서 큰일이 생길지도 모른다고들 하더라. 이 일이 알려지면 우리와 같은 신분의 양반들이 한 무더기 마차를 타고 이리로 내려올 게 틀림없어. 네 아버지가 섀스턴에서 집에 오는 길에 그걸 알게 됐고, 그 일의 가계도를 죄다 나한테 들려줬단다.\"\n\n\"아버지는 지금 어디 계세요?\" 테스가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n어머니는 동문서답으로 답했다. \"오늘 섀스턴에서 의사를 만나러 갔지. 폐병 같은 건 전혀 아니라더라. 심장 둘레에 지방이 꼈다더구나. 자, 이런 모양이래.\" 조앤 더비필드는 말하면서, 물에 분 엄지와 검지를 구부려 알파벳 C자 모양을 만들고, 다른 검지를 가리키는 막대처럼 썼다. \"'지금으로서는,' 의사가 네 아버지한테 그러더래, '당신 심장이 여기랑 여기 둘레로 빙 둘러 막혀 있고, 이 틈만 아직 열려 있소,' 하더란다. '이게 다 맞물리면, 이렇게,'\"—더비필드 부인은 손가락을 오므려 완전한 동그라미를 만들었다—\"'당신은 그림자처럼 훅 가버리는 거요, 더비필드 씨,' 하더래. '십 년을 더 살 수도 있고, 십 개월 만에, 아니 열흘 만에 갈 수도 있소,' 하고 말이야.\"\n\n테스는 놀란 기색이었다. 이렇게 갑작스러운 영광을 누리면서도, 아버지가 그토록 빨리 영원한 구름 너머로 가버릴지도 모른다니!\n\n\"그런데 아버지는 어디 계시냐고요?\" 그녀가 다시 물었다.\n\n어머니는 변명하는 표정을 지었다. \"자, 화부터 버럭 내지는 말아라! 가엾은 양반이—목사님 소식에 잔뜩 들뜬 나머지 너무 기운이 솟구쳐서—삼십 분 전에 롤리버 주막으로 올라갔단다. 내일 그 벌통 짐을 싣고 떠나야 하니, 가문이고 뭐고 그건 꼭 배달해야 하는 거라, 그 여정에 쓸 기운을 차려야 한다잖니. 거리가 워낙 멀어서 오늘 밤 열두 시 조금 지나서는 출발해야 한다는구나.\"\n\n\"기운을 차린다고요!\" 테스가 발끈하며 말했고, 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. \"오, 하느님! 기운을 차린다고 술집에 가다니! 그리고 엄마도 아버지랑 똑같이 그러자고 했다니요, 엄마!\"\n\n그녀의 나무람과 그 기색이 온 방 안을 채우는 듯했고, 가구며, 촛불이며, 이리저리 뛰노는 아이들이며, 어머니의 얼굴에까지 주눅 든 표정을 입히는 듯했다.\n\n\"아니다.\" 후자가 발끈하며 말했다. \"나는 그러자고 안 했어. 내가 그이를 데리러 가는 동안 네가 남아 집을 봐 주길 기다리고 있던 참이다.\"\n\n\"제가 갈게요.\"\n\n\"오, 안 돼, 테스. 너도 알다시피, 그래 봐야 소용없을 거다.\"\n\n테스는 따지지 않았다. 어머니의 반대가 무슨 뜻인지 알았던 것이다. 더비필드 부인의 윗옷과 보닛은 이미 그녀 옆 의자에 슬그머니 걸쳐져, 이 작정한 나들이를 위한 채비를 갖추고 있었으니, 그 이유를, 정작 부인은 그 필요성보다도 더 한탄하는 것이었다.\n\n\"그리고 『완벽한 점술서』는 헛간에 갖다 두렴.\" 조앤이 손을 부랴부랴 닦고 옷을 걸치며 말을 이었다.\n\n『완벽한 점술서』는 그녀의 팔꿈치 옆 탁자에 놓인 오래되고 두툼한 책이었는데, 주머니에 어찌나 넣고 다녔던지 닳아서 여백이 활자 가장자리까지 줄어들어 있었다. 테스가 그것을 집어 들자, 어머니가 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n이렇게 주막으로 게으른 남편을 찾으러 가는 일은, 아이들을 기르는 진창과 혼란 속에서도 더비필드 부인에게 아직 남아 있는 즐거움 가운데 하나였다. 롤리버 주막에서 그를 찾아내, 한두 시간 그의 곁에 앉아, 그동안 아이들에 대한 온갖 생각과 근심을 떨쳐 버리는 일이 그녀를 행복하게 했다. 그럴 때면 삶 위로 일종의 후광이, 서쪽 노을 같은 빛이 어렸다. 근심이며 다른 현실들이 형이상학적으로 잡히지 않는 것이 되어, 차분히 바라볼 한낱 마음속 현상으로 가라앉았고, 더는 몸과 마음을 쓸어 대는 절박한 응어리로 버티고 서 있지 않았다. 당장 눈앞에 보이지 않는 아이들은 차라리 밝고 바람직한 부속물처럼 여겨졌고, 일상의 자질구레한 일들도 그곳에서는 그 면모에 우스움과 즐거움이 없지 않았다. 그녀는 지금은 혼인한 남편이 구애하던 시절, 바로 그 자리에서 그의 곁에 앉아, 그의 성격적 결함에는 눈을 감고 오로지 연인이라는 이상적인 모습으로만 그를 바라보던 때 느끼던 기분을 조금쯤 다시 느꼈다.\n\n테스는 어린 동생들과 남겨진 채, 먼저 점술서를 들고 헛간으로 가서 그것을 초가지붕 짚 속에 쑤셔 넣었다. 이 꾀죄죄한 책에 대한 어머니의 묘한 미신적 두려움 때문에, 어머니는 그것을 결코 밤새 집 안에 두는 법이 없었고, 그래서 그것을 들춰 본 뒤에는 늘 이리로 도로 가져왔다. 빠르게 스러져 가는 미신과 민간전승과 사투리와 입에서 입으로 전해진 옛 노래의 잡동사니를 지닌 어머니와, 무한히 개정된 학습 규정 아래서 국립학교의 정규 교육과 표준 지식을 익힌 딸 사이에는, 흔히들 말하듯 이백 년의 간극이 있었다. 그 둘이 함께 있을 때면 제임스 왕조 시대와 빅토리아 시대가 나란히 놓이는 셈이었다.\n\n정원 길을 따라 돌아오면서 테스는 하필 오늘 같은 날 어머니가 그 책에서 무엇을 알아내고 싶어 했을까 곰곰이 생각했다. 그녀는 최근의 가문 발견이 그것과 관련 있으리라 짐작했으나, 그것이 오로지 자기 자신에 관한 일임은 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리고, 그녀는 낮 동안 말려 둔 빨래에 물을 뿌리는 일에 분주했으니, 아홉 살배기 남동생 에이브러햄과, '리자루'라 불리는 열두 살 반의 여동생 일라이자루이자가 함께였고, 더 어린 아이들은 잠자리에 들었다. 테스와 그다음 아이 사이에는 사 년 남짓의 간격이 있었으니, 그 틈을 메웠던 두 아이는 갓난 시절에 죽었고, 이 때문에 그녀는 동생들과 단둘이 있을 때면 어머니 대리 같은 태도를 띠게 되었다. 에이브러햄 다음으로 어린 축에는 호프와 모디스티라는 두 여자아이가 더 있었고, 그다음에 세 살배기 사내아이, 그다음에 막 첫돌을 채운 갓난아기가 있었다.\n\n이 어린 영혼들은 모두 더비필드라는 배에 탄 승객이었으니—그들의 즐거움도, 필요도, 건강도, 심지어 그 목숨까지도 전적으로 더비필드 집안 두 어른의 판단에 매여 있었다. 더비필드 집안의 가장들이 곤경으로, 재난으로, 굶주림으로, 질병으로, 타락으로, 죽음으로 배를 몰아가기로 한다면, 갑판 아래 갇힌 이 여섯 어린 포로들도 그들과 함께 그리로 떠밀려 갈 수밖에 없었다—여섯의 가엾은 것들, 어떤 조건으로든 삶을 원하느냐는 물음조차 받은 적 없었거늘, 하물며 게으른 더비필드 집안의 자식이 된다는 데 따르는 그토록 모진 조건으로 삶을 원하느냐는 물음이야 더더욱. 요즘 들어 그 시(詩)가 산뜻하고 순결한 만큼이나 그 철학이 심오하고 미덥다고 여겨지는 어느 시인이, 무슨 권위로 \"자연의 거룩한 섭리\"를 운운하는지, 더러는 알고 싶어 할지도 모른다.\n\n밤이 깊어 갔으나, 아버지도 어머니도 다시 나타나지 않았다. 테스는 문밖을 내다보며, 마음속으로 말럿을 한 바퀴 돌았다. 마을이 눈을 감고 있었다. 사방에서 촛불과 등불이 꺼져 가고 있었다. 그녀는 불 끄는 도구와 뻗은 손길을 마음속으로 그려 볼 수 있었다.\n\n어머니가 데리러 간 것은 결국 데려와야 할 사람이 하나 더 늘었다는 뜻일 뿐이었다. 테스는, 새벽 한 시 전에 길을 떠나려는, 건강도 시원찮은 사람이 이런 늦은 시각에 주막에서 제 가문의 핏줄이나 기리고 있어서는 안 된다는 것을 차츰 깨닫기 시작했다.\n\n\"에이브러햄,\" 그녀가 어린 남동생에게 말했다. \"모자 쓰고—무섭진 않지?—롤리버 주막에 올라가서, 아버지랑 어머니가 어찌 됐는지 좀 보고 오렴.\"\n\n소년은 자리에서 냉큼 뛰어내려 문을 열었고, 밤이 그를 삼켜 버렸다. 또 삼십 분이 지났다. 남자도, 여자도, 아이도 돌아오지 않았다. 에이브러햄도 제 부모처럼 그 덫 같은 주막에 끈끈이로 붙들려 잡힌 모양이었다.\n\n\"내가 직접 가봐야겠어.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그래서 리자루가 잠자리에 들었고, 테스는 그들을 모두 안에 가두어 잠그고, 어둡고 구불구불한, 급히 나아가라고 난 것이 아닌 골목길, 아니 거리를 따라 길을 나섰다. 그 거리는 손바닥만 한 땅에도 값이 매겨지기 전에, 시침 하나짜리 시계가 하루를 충분히 잘게 나누던 시절에 닦인 길이었다.\n\n# IV\n\nIV\n롤리버 주막은, 길고 끊긴 이 마을 이쪽 끝의 유일한 술집으로, 점포 밖 판매 면허밖에 자랑할 수 없었다. 그래서 누구도 가게 안에서는 합법적으로 술을 마실 수 없었으므로, 손님들을 위해 드러내 놓고 마련된 자리라곤, 정원 울타리 말뚝에 철사 조각으로 붙들어 매어 선반처럼 만든, 폭 여섯 인치에 길이 두 야드쯤 되는 작은 판자 하나뿐이었다. 이 판자 위에 목마른 길손들은 길에 선 채로 술을 마시며 잔을 내려놓았고, 찌꺼기를 폴리네시아 군도 모양으로 먼지투성이 땅에 내던지며, 안에 편안히 앉을 자리가 있었으면 하고 바랐다.\n\n길손들은 그러했다. 그러나 똑같은 바람을 품은 동네 단골들도 있었다. 그리고 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있는 법이다.\n\n이층의 어느 큰 침실에는, 그 창문이 여주인 롤리버 부인이 얼마 전에 내버린 커다란 모직 숄로 두껍게 가려져 있었는데, 이날 저녁 열 명 남짓한 사람들이 모여 있었으니, 죄다 더없는 행복을 좇는 이들이요, 죄다 말럿 이쪽 끝의 오랜 주민이자 이 은신처의 단골이었다. 흩어진 마을의 더 먼 쪽에 있는, 정식 면허를 갖춘 술집 '순수한 한 방울'까지의 거리가 이쪽 끝에 사는 이들에게는 그 시설을 사실상 이용할 수 없게 만든 데다, 그보다 훨씬 더 심각한 문제, 곧 술의 품질이, 다른 주막 주인과 함께 너른 집에서 마시느니 롤리버와 함께 지붕 밑 한구석에서 마시는 편이 낫다는 널리 퍼진 의견을 굳혔던 것이다.\n\n방 안에 놓인 앙상한 네 기둥 침대는 그 세 면을 둘러앉은 여러 사람에게 앉을 자리를 내주었고, 두엇은 서랍장 위에 몸을 올렸으며, 또 한 사람은 떡갈나무를 깎아 만든 \"괴짝\" 위에, 둘은 세면대 위에, 또 한 사람은 걸상 위에 자리 잡았으니, 이렇게 해서 다들 어떻게든 편히 앉아 있었다. 이 시각에 이른 그들의 마음의 편안함이란, 그 영혼이 살갗 밖으로 부풀어 올라, 제 인격을 방 안 가득 따뜻하게 펼쳐 놓는 그런 경지였다. 이 과정에서 방과 그 가구는 점점 더 위엄 있고 호사스러워졌으니, 창에 걸린 숄은 태피스트리의 풍요로움을 띠었고, 서랍장의 놋쇠 손잡이는 황금 문고리 같았으며, 깎아 만든 침대 기둥들은 솔로몬 성전의 장엄한 기둥들과 어딘가 친척 사이라도 되는 듯했다.\n\n더비필드 부인은 테스와 헤어진 뒤 이리로 잰걸음으로 걸어와, 앞문을 열고, 짙은 어둠에 잠긴 아래층 방을 가로지른 다음, 빗장의 요령을 잘 아는 손가락을 가진 사람답게 계단 문을 열었다. 구불구불한 계단을 오르는 것은 더 더딘 일이었고, 마지막 계단 위로 빛 속에 떠오른 그녀의 얼굴은 침실에 모인 일행 전부의 시선과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"—제 사비를 털어 모임 행진 뒤풀이를 하려고 사사로이 청한 몇몇 벗들이 와 있는 거랍니다.\" 여주인이 발소리에 외쳤으니, 계단 너머를 빠끔히 내다보며, 교리문답을 외는 아이처럼 술술 늘어놓았다. \"오, 더비필드 부인, 당신이로군요—원 세상에—깜짝 놀랐잖아요!—정부에서 보낸 무슨 영감님인 줄 알았네.\"\n\n더비필드 부인은 나머지 모인 이들에게 눈짓과 끄덕임으로 맞아들여졌고, 남편이 앉아 있는 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그는 멍하니 혼자 나직한 목소리로 흥얼거리고 있었다. \"나는 여기저기 어떤 사람들 못지않게 훌륭하다네! 나는 킹스비어서브그린힐에 커다란 가문 납골당이 있고, 웨식스의 그 어떤 사람보다 훌륭한 해골들이 있다네!\"\n\n\"그 일에 대해 내 머릿속에 떠오른 게 있는데, 들려줄 게 있어요—굉장한 계획이라고요!\" 그의 명랑한 아내가 속삭였다. \"여보, 존, 나 안 보여요?\" 그녀가 그를 쿡 찔렀으나, 그는 그녀를 마치 유리창 너머를 보듯 꿰뚫어 보며 제 영창을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"쉿! 그렇게 큰 소리로 노래하지 마세요, 양반님.\" 여주인이 말했다. \"정부 사람이라도 지나가다가 제 면허를 빼앗아 가면 어쩌려고요.\"\n\n\"우리한테 무슨 일이 생겼는지, 이이가 당신한테 다 말했겠죠?\" 더비필드 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그런 셈이죠. 거기서 돈푼이라도 나올 것 같아요?\"\n\n\"아, 그게 비밀이지요.\" 조앤 더비필드가 슬기롭게 말했다. \"하지만 마차에 타진 못해도, 마차와 친척이라는 것만으로도 좋은 일 아니겠어요.\" 그녀는 남들 들으라는 목소리를 거두고, 남편에게 나직이 말을 이었다. \"당신이 그 소식을 가져온 뒤로 줄곧 생각해 봤는데, 트랜트리지 바깥, 체이스 숲 가장자리에, 더버빌이라는 이름의 굉장한 부잣집 마님이 산대요.\"\n\n\"어이—그게 무슨 소리야?\" 존 경이 말했다.\n\n그녀가 그 소식을 되풀이했다. \"그 마님이 우리 친척이 틀림없어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래서 내 계획은 테스를 보내 친척임을 내세우게 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"이제 당신이 말하니 그런 이름의 마님이 정말 있긴 하구먼.\" 더비필드가 말했다. \"트링엄 목사는 그 생각을 못 했지. 하지만 그 마님이야 우리에 비하면 아무것도 아니지—틀림없이 우리 집안의 방계일 거고, 노르만 왕 시절 한참 뒤에야 갈라져 나온 가지일 테니까.\"\n\n이 문제가 논의되는 동안, 두 사람 다 골똘한 나머지, 어린 에이브러햄이 방 안으로 슬그머니 기어들어 와, 자기들에게 돌아가자고 청할 틈을 엿보고 있는 것을 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"그 마님은 부자고, 우리 딸을 분명히 눈여겨봐 줄 거예요.\" 더비필드 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그러면 아주 좋은 일이 될 테고. 한 집안의 두 가지가 왕래하지 못할 까닭이 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"네, 그러면 우리도 다 친척이라고 내세우는 거예요!\" 침대 밑에서 에이브러햄이 환하게 말했다. \"그리고 테스가 그 마님이랑 같이 살러 가면, 우리도 다 같이 그 마님을 보러 가는 거고, 그 마님 마차를 타고, 검은 옷을 입는 거예요!\"\n\n\"얘야, 네가 어떻게 여기 왔니? 무슨 헛소리를 하는 거야! 저리 가서, 아버지랑 어머니가 채비될 때까지 계단에서 놀고 있어!…… 그래, 테스가 이 우리 집안의 다른 식구를 찾아가는 게 좋겠어. 그 애라면 분명히 마님의 마음을 살 거야—테스라면 말이지. 그러면 아마 어느 고귀한 신사가 그 애와 혼인하는 데까지 이를 게 틀림없어. 한마디로, 난 그렇게 될 줄 알아.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"내가 『점술서』로 그 애 운수를 봤는데, 바로 그게 나왔다니까요!…… 오늘 그 애가 얼마나 예뻐 보였는지 당신도 봤어야 하는데. 살결이 공작부인처럼 매끈하다니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 본인은 가겠다고 하던가?\"\n\n\"아직 그 애한테 안 물어봤어요. 그런 친척 마님이 있다는 것도 아직 몰라요. 하지만 그건 분명히 그 애를 굉장한 혼사 길로 이끌어 줄 테고, 그 애도 가는 걸 싫다곤 안 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"테스는 별나단 말이야.\"\n\n\"하지만 속은 고분고분해요. 그 애는 나한테 맡겨 둬요.\"\n\n이 대화는 사사로운 것이었으나, 그 요지가 주위 사람들의 머리에 충분히 전해져, 더비필드 집안이 이제 여느 사람들보다 더 묵직한 일거리를 입에 올리고 있으며, 그들의 예쁜 맏딸 테스에게 좋은 앞날이 기다리고 있다는 것을 짐작케 했다.\n\n\"테스는 참 보기 좋은 처녀라니까, 오늘 그 애가 다른 애들이랑 교구를 돌아다니는 걸 봤을 때 나도 속으로 그랬지.\" 술꾼 노인 중 하나가 나직이 말했다. \"하지만 조앤 더비필드는 마룻바닥에서 보리 싹이 트지 않도록 조심해야 할 거야.\" 이것은 묘한 뜻이 담긴 동네 말이었는데, 아무도 대꾸하지 않았다.\n\n대화는 모두가 어울리는 것이 되었고, 이윽고 아래층 방을 가로지르는 또 다른 발소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"—오늘 밤 제 사비로 모임 행진 뒤풀이를 하려고 사사로이 청한 몇몇 벗들이…….\" 여주인은, 새로 들어온 이가 테스임을 알아채기도 전에, 침입자들을 위해 늘 준비해 두는 그 상투적인 말을 잽싸게 다시 써먹었다.\n\n어머니의 눈으로 보기에도, 주름진 중년에게는 그리 어울리지 않을 것 없는 매개로서 이곳에 떠도는 술기운 속에서, 처녀의 앳된 얼굴은 슬프도록 어울리지 않아 보였다. 그리고 테스의 검은 눈에서 번뜩이는 나무람의 빛까지 갈 것도 없이, 그 아버지와 어머니는 자리에서 일어나, 황급히 맥주를 마저 비우고, 그녀 뒤를 따라 계단을 내려왔으니, 롤리버 부인의 당부가 그 발소리를 뒤쫓았다.\n\n\"제발 소리 좀 내지 말아 주세요, 그래 주시면 고맙겠어요, 여러분. 안 그러면 면허를 잃고, 소환장을 받고, 그 밖에 무슨 일이 닥칠지 몰라요! 안녕히들 가세요!\"\n\n그들은 함께 집으로 갔으니, 테스가 아버지의 한쪽 팔을, 더비필드 부인이 다른 쪽 팔을 잡았다. 그는 사실 술을 거의 마시지 않았으니—습관적으로 술을 즐기는 사람이라면 일요일 오후에 동쪽을 향한 절이나 무릎 꿇는 예배에 한 치 흔들림 없이 교회까지 지고 갈 수 있을 양의 사분의 일도 안 되었다. 하지만 존 경의 허약한 체질은 이 방면의 사소한 죄를 산더미로 부풀려 놓았다. 신선한 바깥 공기에 나서자 그는 꽤나 비틀거려, 세 사람의 줄을 어느 순간에는 마치 런던으로 행군하는 듯이, 또 어느 순간에는 마치 배스로 행군하는 듯이 기우뚱하게 만들었으니—이는 우스운 효과를 자아냈으니, 밤늦게 집으로 돌아가는 가족들에게는 흔한 일이었다. 그리고 대부분의 우스운 효과가 그렇듯, 따지고 보면 그리 우습지만은 않은 것이었다. 두 여자는 이 본의 아닌 옆걸음질과 갈지자 행군을 할 수 있는 한 용감히 감추었으니, 그 원인인 더비필드에게서도, 에이브러햄에게서도, 그리고 자기들 자신에게서도 숨겼다. 그리하여 그들은 차츰차츰 제 집 문에 다가갔으니, 집안의 가장은 가까워지자 문득 아까 그 후렴을 터뜨렸다. 마치 지금 사는 거처가 얼마나 작은지를 보고 제 영혼을 다잡으려는 듯이—\n\n\"나는 킹스비어에 가—문 납골당이 있다네!\"\n\n\"쉿—그렇게 바보같이 굴지 마세요, 재키.\" 그의 아내가 말했다. \"옛날에 이름깨나 날린 게 당신네 집안만은 아니라고요. 앵크텔 집안이랑, 호시 집안이랑, 트링엄 집안 자기들만 봐도, 당신만큼이나 거의 다 영락했잖아요—하긴 당신네가 그들보다 더 대단한 집안이긴 했지요, 그건 사실이고. 다행히도 나는 무슨 집안 출신도 아니라서, 그런 걸로 부끄러워할 게 하나도 없으니!\"\n\n\"그건 그렇게 장담하지 마. 당신 천성으로 보면, 내 생각엔 당신네야말로 우리 누구보다도 더 망신살이 뻗쳤을 게 분명하고, 한때는 어엿한 왕이며 왕비였을 거야.\"\n\n테스는, 자기 조상에 대한 생각보다 그 순간 자기 마음속에서 훨씬 더 두드러진 것을 말함으로써 화제를 돌렸다—\"아버지가 내일 그렇게 일찍 벌통을 싣고 길을 떠나지 못하실까 봐 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"내가? 나는 한두 시간이면 멀쩡해질 거다.\" 더비필드가 말했다.\n\n가족이 모두 잠자리에 든 것은 열한 시였고, 토요일 장이 서기 전에 벌통을 캐스터브리지의 소매상들에게 배달하려면 다음 날 아침 두 시가 출발할 수 있는 가장 늦은 시각이었으니, 그리로 가는 길은 험한 길을 따라 이십 마일에서 삼십 마일이나 되는 데다, 말과 짐마차도 더없이 느렸다. 한 시 반에 더비필드 부인이 테스와 그 어린 동생들이 모두 자는 큰 침실로 들어왔다.\n\n\"가엾은 양반이 못 가겠구나.\" 그녀가 맏딸에게 말했고, 딸의 큰 눈은 어머니의 손이 문에 닿는 순간 떠졌다.\n\n테스는 침대에서 일어나 앉았으니, 꿈과 이 소식 사이의 어렴풋한 틈새에 빠진 채였다.\n\n\"하지만 누군가는 가야 해요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"벌통도 이미 늦었어요. 올해 분봉도 곧 끝날 테고, 다음 주 장까지 미뤘다간 사겠다는 데도 없어져서, 우리한테 고스란히 떠안게 될 거예요.\"\n\n더비필드 부인은 이 위급한 상황을 감당할 만해 보이지 않았다. \"어쩌면 어느 젊은이가 가주려나? 어제 너랑 그렇게 춤추고 싶어 안달하던 애들 중 하나가 말이다.\" 그녀가 이윽고 넌지시 말했다.\n\n\"오, 안 돼요—절대로 그렇겐 못 해요!\" 테스가 자존심 있게 잘라 말했다. \"그리고 그 까닭을 모두에게 알리다니요—그런 부끄러운 일이! 에이브러햄이 같이 가서 말동무가 되어 준다면 저 혼자라도 갈 수 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n어머니는 마침내 이 안에 동의했다. 어린 에이브러햄은 같은 방 한구석에서 깊은 잠에서 깨워져, 아직 마음은 저세상에 둔 채로 옷을 입혀졌다. 그동안 테스는 황급히 옷을 차려입었다. 그렇게 둘은 등불을 켜고 마구간으로 나갔다. 후들거리는 작은 짐마차에는 이미 짐이 실려 있었고, 처녀는 말 프린스를 끌어냈으니, 그 말은 마차보다 딱 한 단계만 덜 후들거릴 뿐이었다.\n\n그 가엾은 짐승은, 모든 살아 있는 것이 잠자리에 들어 쉬어야 할 이 시각에 자기가 밖에 나가 일하라는 부름을 받았다는 것을 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯, 밤을, 등불을, 그리고 둘의 모습을 의아하게 둘러보았다. 그들은 등불에 양초 토막을 잔뜩 채워 넣고, 그것을 짐의 바깥쪽에 매단 다음, 말을 앞으로 몰았으니, 처음에는 오르막길에서 기운이라곤 거의 없는 짐승에게 짐을 너무 지우지 않으려고 말의 어깨 옆에서 걸었다. 진짜 아침은 아직 멀었으나, 그들은 할 수 있는 한 기운을 내려고, 등불과 약간의 빵과 버터, 그리고 그들 자신의 대화로 인공의 아침을 만들었다. 에이브러햄은 (지금까지 일종의 비몽사몽 속에서 움직여 온 터라) 좀 더 온전히 잠에서 깨어나면서, 하늘을 배경으로 한 갖가지 어두운 물체들이 띠는 이상한 모양들에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다. 굴에서 뛰쳐나오는 사나운 호랑이처럼 보이는 이 나무에 대해, 거인의 머리를 닮은 저것에 대해.\n\n스타우어캐슬이라는 작은 마을을 지났을 때, 그곳은 두꺼운 갈색 초가지붕 아래 말없이 잠들어 있었는데, 그들은 더 높은 지대에 이르렀다. 왼쪽으로 더 높이, 사우스웨식스에서 거의 가장 높다 할 불배로, 또는 빌배로라 불리는 솟아오른 땅이, 흙으로 판 도랑들에 둘러싸인 채 하늘로 부풀어 올라 있었다. 이 언저리부터 긴 길은 한동안 꽤 평탄하게 이어졌다. 그들은 짐마차 앞쪽에 올라탔고, 에이브러햄은 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"테스 누나!\" 침묵 끝에 그가 무언가를 꺼내려는 듯한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"응, 에이브러햄.\"\n\n\"우리가 양반이 됐다니 기쁘지 않아?\"\n\n\"딱히 기쁘진 않아.\"\n\n\"하지만 누나가 신사랑 혼인하게 된다니 기쁘지?\"\n\n\"뭐?\" 테스가 얼굴을 들며 말했다.\n\n\"우리 굉장한 친척이 누나가 신사랑 혼인하게 도와줄 거라고.\"\n\n\"내가? 우리 굉장한 친척? 우리한테 그런 친척은 없어. 누가 그런 걸 네 머릿속에 넣었니?\"\n\n\"아버지 찾으러 갔을 때 롤리버 주막에서 그 얘기 하는 걸 들었어. 트랜트리지 바깥에 우리 집안의 부잣집 마님이 산대, 그리고 누나가 그 마님이랑 친척임을 내세우면, 마님이 누나를 신사랑 혼인하는 길로 이끌어 줄 거라고 엄마가 그랬어.\"\n\n누나는 문득 잠잠해지더니, 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 침묵 속으로 빠져들었다. 에이브러햄은, 듣는 것보다는 말하는 즐거움 때문에 계속 떠들어 댔으므로, 누나가 넋을 놓고 있는 것은 아무 상관이 없었다. 그는 벌통에 등을 기대고, 얼굴을 위로 쳐든 채 별들에 대해 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 그 차가운 맥박이 머리 위 검은 허공 속에서, 이 두 가닥 인간의 삶과는 차분히 무관하게 고동치고 있었다. 그는 저 반짝이는 것들이 얼마나 멀리 있는지, 그리고 그 너머에 하느님이 계신지 물었다. 그러나 그의 어린애다운 재잘거림은 이따금, 천지창조의 경이보다도 그의 상상에 한층 더 깊이 새겨진 것으로 되돌아왔다. 만일 테스가 신사와 혼인해서 부자가 된다면, 누나는 별들을 네틀콤투트만큼 가까이 끌어당겨 줄 만큼 커다란 망원경을 살 돈이 생기는 걸까?\n\n온 집안에 스며든 듯한 이 거듭되는 화제가 테스를 조바심으로 가득 채웠다.\n\n\"이제 그 얘긴 그만해!\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n\"누나가 별들이 세계라고 했지, 테스 누나?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n\"다 우리 세계 같은 거야?\"\n\n“잘 모르겠어. 그래도 그런 것 같아. 더러는 우리 집 텃밭 사과나무에 달린 사과 같달까. 대부분은 멀쩡하고 튼실한데—몇 개는 병들어 있지.”\n\n“우리가 사는 건 어느 쪽이야—멀쩡한 거야, 병든 거야?”\n\n“병든 거지.”\n\n“멀쩡한 게 그렇게나 많은데, 하필 멀쩡한 걸 못 고른 게 참 운도 없네!”\n\n“그래.”\n\n“정말 그런 거야, 테스 누나?” 에이브러햄이 이 드문 이야기를 곱씹다가 잔뜩 감명받은 얼굴로 누나를 돌아보며 말했다. “멀쩡한 걸 골랐으면 어땠을까?”\n\n“음, 아버지가 지금처럼 기침하고 비실대지도 않았을 거고, 이번 길에 나서지 못할 만큼 취하지도 않았겠지. 그리고 어머니도 늘 빨래만 하면서 도무지 끝을 못 보지는 않았을 거야.”\n\n“그리고 누나는 처음부터 부잣집 마님이라, 신사하고 결혼해서 부자가 될 필요도 없었겠네?”\n\n“오, 에이비, 그만—그런 얘기는 더는 하지 마!”\n\n혼자 생각에 잠긴 에이브러햄은 곧 졸음에 겨워졌다. 테스는 말을 다루는 데 능숙하지 않았으나, 당분간은 짐수레 운전을 자기가 도맡고, 에이브러햄이 원한다면 잠들게 둘 수 있겠다고 생각했다. 그녀는 동생이 떨어지지 않도록 벌통 앞에 일종의 둥지 같은 자리를 만들어 주고, 고삐를 자기 손에 쥔 채 전처럼 천천히 나아갔다.\n\n프린스는 손이 거의 가지 않아도 됐다. 군더더기 동작이라곤 할 기운조차 없었던 것이다. 곁에서 정신을 흩어 놓는 동무가 없어지자, 테스는 벌통에 등을 기댄 채 그 어느 때보다 깊이 몽상에 빠져들었다. 어깨 너머로 말없이 지나가는 나무와 산울타리의 행렬은 현실 밖 환상의 장면들과 이어졌고, 이따금 일렁이는 바람은 어떤 거대하고 슬픈 영혼의 한숨이 되었으니, 공간으로는 우주와 맞닿고 시간으로는 역사와 맞닿은 영혼이었다. 그러다 그녀는 제 삶에 얽힌 사건의 그물을 살펴보며, 아버지의 자부심이 얼마나 헛된 것인지를, 어머니의 공상 속에서 자기를 기다리는 신사다운 구혼자가 어떤 자인지를 보는 듯했다—그자를 보았으니, 얼굴을 잔뜩 찡그린 인물이 그녀의 가난과, 수의에 싸인 기사 가문의 혈통을 비웃고 있었다. 모든 것이 점점 더 터무니없어졌고, 그녀는 더 이상 시간이 어떻게 흐르는지 알 수 없었다. 갑작스러운 충격이 자리에 앉은 그녀를 흔들었고, 테스 역시 빠져들었던 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n정신을 잃었을 때보다 한참을 더 와 있었고, 짐수레는 멈춰 서 있었다. 평생 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 텅 빈 신음 소리가 앞쪽에서 들려왔고, 뒤이어 “이봐, 거기!” 하는 외침이 따랐다.\n\n짐수레에 걸려 있던 등불은 꺼져 있었으나, 다른 등불 하나가 그녀의 얼굴을 비추고 있었다—제 것보다 훨씬 밝은 빛이었다. 끔찍한 일이 벌어진 것이다. 마구가 길을 막은 무언가에 뒤엉켜 있었다.\n\n기겁한 테스는 뛰어내렸고, 무서운 진실을 발견했다. 그 신음은 아버지의 가엾은 말 프린스에게서 난 것이었다. 소리 없는 두 바퀴로 늘 그러하듯 화살처럼 이 좁은 길을 내달리던 아침 우편마차가, 느릿느릿 불도 켜지 않은 그녀의 수레를 들이받은 것이었다. 마차의 뾰족한 끌채가 칼처럼 불행한 프린스의 가슴을 파고들었고, 그 상처에서 생명의 피가 줄기를 이루며 솟구쳐, 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 길바닥으로 떨어지고 있었다.\n\n절망에 빠진 테스는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 그 구멍에 손을 갖다 댔으나, 얻은 것이라곤 얼굴에서 치마까지 진홍빛 핏방울이 튀어 얼룩진 것뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 어쩔 줄 모르고 서서 지켜보았다. 프린스 역시 버틸 수 있는 한 굳건히, 움직임 없이 서 있었다. 그러다 갑자기 풀썩 주저앉아 버렸다.\n\n이때쯤 우편마차꾼이 그녀에게 다가와, 아직 온기가 남은 프린스의 몸을 끌어내고 마구를 풀기 시작했다. 그러나 프린스는 이미 죽어 있었고, 당장은 더 어찌할 도리가 없음을 본 우편마차꾼은 다친 데 없는 제 짐승에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n“댁이 길을 잘못 들었소.” 그가 말했다. “나는 우편 자루를 가지고 계속 가야 하니, 댁은 짐을 지키며 여기 있는 게 상책이오. 되도록 빨리 사람을 보내 도와주리다. 날이 밝아 오니, 무서워할 것 없소.”\n\n그는 말에 올라 제 길을 서둘러 갔고, 테스는 우두커니 서서 기다렸다. 대기는 창백해졌고, 새들은 산울타리에서 몸을 털고 날아올라 지저귀었다. 좁은 길이 제 흰 모습을 온통 드러냈고, 테스도 제 모습을 드러냈으니, 길보다 더 희었다. 그녀 앞에 고인 거대한 핏덩이는 이미 응고하며 무지갯빛을 띠기 시작했고, 해가 떠오르자 거기서 백 가지 프리즘의 색조가 반사되었다. 프린스는 그 곁에 뻣뻣하게 누워 꼼짝하지 않았다. 두 눈은 반쯤 뜨여 있었고, 가슴에 난 구멍은 그를 살아 있게 하던 모든 것이 거기서 빠져나갔다기에는 도무지 충분히 커 보이지 않았다.\n\n“이게 다 내 탓이야—전부 내 탓!” 소녀가 그 광경을 바라보며 울부짖었다. “변명할 거리도 없어—하나도. 이제 어머니랑 아버지는 뭘로 먹고살아? 에이비, 에이비!” 그녀는 이 참사가 다 벌어지는 동안 곤히 잠들어 있던 아이를 흔들었다. “이제 짐을 못 가지고 가—프린스가 죽었어!”\n\n에이브러햄이 사태를 다 알아차리자, 오십 년 세월의 주름이 그 어린 얼굴 위에 즉석에서 패었다.\n\n“아니, 난 어제만 해도 춤추고 웃었는데!” 그녀는 혼잣말을 이어 갔다. “내가 그런 바보였다니!”\n\n“그건 우리가 멀쩡한 별이 아니라 병든 별에 살고 있어서 그래, 그렇지, 누나?” 에이브러햄이 눈물 사이로 중얼거렸다.\n\n그들은 끝없이 이어지는 듯한 시간 동안 말없이 기다렸다. 마침내 어떤 소리와, 다가오는 어떤 물체가, 우편마차꾼이 약속을 지켰음을 그들에게 알려 주었다. 스토어캐슬 근방에서 온 어느 농부의 일꾼이 튼튼한 조랑말을 끌고 다가왔다. 조랑말은 프린스 대신 벌통 짐수레에 매였고, 짐은 캐스터브리지를 향해 옮겨졌다.\n\n같은 날 저녁, 빈 짐수레가 사고 장소에 다시 이르렀다. 프린스는 아침부터 그 도랑에 누워 있었다. 그러나 핏덩이가 고였던 자리는, 지나가는 수레들에 긁히고 쓸렸어도 길 한복판에 여전히 보였다. 이제 프린스에게서 남은 전부가 그가 예전에 끌던 그 짐수레에 들어 올려졌고, 발굽을 허공에 든 채, 편자가 지는 햇빛에 번쩍이며, 말럿까지 팔구 마일 길을 되짚어 갔다.\n\n테스는 더 일찍 돌아가 있었다. 어떻게 소식을 전해야 할지 도무지 떠오르지 않았다. 부모의 얼굴에서 이미 그들의 손실을 알고 있음을 알아챘을 때, 그녀의 혀는 한시름 놓았다. 그렇다고 그녀가 제 부주의를 두고 스스로에게 쌓아 올리던 자책이 덜어진 것은 아니었지만.\n\n그러나 바로 그 무계획한 살림살이 덕분에, 이 불행은 잘나가는 집안에 닥쳤을 때보다 그들에게 덜 끔찍한 일이 되었다. 다만 이번 경우엔 그것이 파멸을 뜻했고, 저쪽 경우엔 그저 불편을 뜻했을 뿐이지만. 더비필드 집안의 얼굴에는, 딸의 안위를 더 욕심내는 부모라면 소녀에게 불사르듯 쏟아 냈을 시뻘건 분노 따위는 조금도 없었다. 테스가 스스로를 탓한 만큼 그녀를 탓하는 이는 아무도 없었다.\n\n말 가죽 장수와 무두장이가 프린스의 사체를 두고 늙어 빠진 짐승이라며 고작 몇 실링밖에 쳐 주지 않으리란 게 밝혀지자, 더비필드가 그 자리에서 떨치고 일어섰다.\n\n“안 돼.” 그는 의연하게 말했다. “녀석의 늙은 몸뚱이를 팔지는 않겠어. 우리 더버빌가가 이 땅의 기사였을 적엔, 군마를 고양이 먹이로 팔아넘기지 않았다고. 그까짓 실링은 자기네나 가지라고 해! 녀석은 살아생전 나를 잘 섬겼으니, 이제 와서 헤어지진 않겠어.”\n\n이튿날 그는 텃밭에 프린스의 무덤을 파느라, 지난 몇 달간 가족 먹일 작물을 길러 낼 때보다 더 열심히 일했다. 구덩이가 다 파이자, 더비필드와 그의 아내는 말에 밧줄을 묶어 그것을 향해 길을 따라 끌어 올렸고, 아이들이 장례 행렬처럼 뒤를 따랐다. 에이브러햄과 라이자루는 흐느꼈고, 호프와 모데스티는 벽에 메아리치는 큰 울음소리로 슬픔을 토해 냈다. 프린스가 굴러 떨어지듯 묻히자 아이들은 무덤가에 둘러섰다. 밥벌이가 그들에게서 떠나갔으니, 이제 어찌하나?\n\n“프린스는 천국에 갔어요?” 에이브러햄이 흐느낌 사이로 물었다.\n\n이윽고 더비필드가 흙을 삽으로 퍼 넣기 시작했고, 아이들은 다시 울음을 터뜨렸다. 테스만 빼고 모두가. 그녀의 얼굴은 마르고 창백했으니, 마치 스스로를 살인자로 여기는 듯했다.\n\n# V\n\n말에 주로 기대고 있던 흥정 장사가 곧장 엉망이 되었다. 궁핍까지는 아니어도, 곤경이 멀리서 어른거렸다. 더비필드는 이 고장 말로 ‘느슨하게 꼬인 사내’라 불릴 만한 위인이었다. 이따금은 일할 기운이 넉넉했으나, 그 ‘이따금’이 일손이 필요한 때와 맞아떨어지리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 게다가 날품팔이의 규칙적인 노역에 익숙지 않았던 터라, 어쩌다 그 둘이 맞아떨어질 때조차 그리 끈기 있게 버티지 못했다.\n\n한편 테스는, 제 부모를 이 수렁에 빠뜨린 장본인으로서, 그들을 거기서 건져 내려면 무엇을 할 수 있을지 말없이 궁리하고 있었다. 그러던 차에 어머니가 제 계획을 꺼냈다.\n\n“좋을 때가 있으면 나쁠 때도 있는 법이지, 테스.” 그녀가 말했다. “네 고귀한 핏줄이 이보다 더 절실한 때에 드러난 적도 없었다. 친척을 찾아가 봐야 해. 더 체이스 변두리에 사는, 틀림없이 우리 친척일 아주 부유한 더버빌 마님이 있단다. 너는 그분께 가서 친척이라 밝히고, 우리 곤경에 좀 도와 달라고 청해야 해.”\n\n“그러고 싶지 않아요.” 테스가 말했다. “그런 마님이 정말 계신다 해도, 우리한테 친절하게 대해 주시는 것만으로도 충분하죠—도움을 주실 거라 기대할 게 아니라요.”\n\n“넌 그분을 잘 구슬려서 뭐든 하게 할 수 있어, 얘야. 게다가, 네가 아는 것보다 더한 게 있을지도 모른다. 나는 들을 만큼 들었다니까, 정말로.”\n\n자기가 저지른 해악에 대한 짓누르는 듯한 자책감 탓에, 테스는 평소보다 어머니의 뜻을 더 고분고분 따르게 되었다. 그러나 그녀로서는 이득이 그토록 의심스러운 일을 떠올리며 어머니가 어찌 그런 흡족함을 느끼는지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 어머니가 알아보았더라면, 이 더버빌 마님이 견줄 데 없는 미덕과 자비를 갖춘 분임을 알아냈을지도 모른다. 그러나 테스의 자존심은 가난한 친척 노릇을 유난히 꺼리게 만들었다.\n\n“차라리 일자리를 구하는 게 낫겠어요.” 그녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n“더비필드, 당신이 결정해요.” 아내가 뒤편에 앉은 그를 돌아보며 말했다. “당신이 가야 한다고 하면, 얘는 갈 거예요.”\n\n“내 자식들이 가서 낯선 친척한테 신세 지는 건 마뜩잖아.” 그가 중얼거렸다. “나는 이 가문에서 가장 고귀한 갈래의 가장이니, 그에 걸맞게 살아야지.”\n\n가지 않으려는 그의 이유가, 테스 자신이 가기를 꺼리는 이유보다 그녀에게는 더 못마땅했다. “음, 내가 말을 죽였으니, 어머니.” 그녀가 서글프게 말했다. “뭐든 해야 할 것 같긴 해요. 가서 그분을 뵙는 거야 상관없지만, 도움을 청하는 일은 제게 맡겨 주세요. 그리고 그분이 저한테 혼처를 맺어 줄 거라느니 하는 생각은 마세요—그건 바보 같은 짓이에요.”\n\n“참 옳은 말이다, 테스!” 아버지가 점잖게 한마디 했다.\n\n“내가 언제 그런 생각을 했다고?” 조앤이 물었다.\n\n“어머니 마음속에 있는 것 같아서요. 어쨌든 갈게요.”\n\n이튿날 일찍 일어난 그녀는 섀스턴이라는 언덕 마을까지 걸어가, 일주일에 두 번 섀스턴에서 동쪽 체이스버러로 가는 합승마차를 이용했다. 그 마차는, 어렴풋하고 알 수 없는 더버빌 마님이 거처를 둔 교구인 트랜트리지 근처를 지나갔다.\n\n그 잊지 못할 아침, 테스 더비필드의 길은 그녀가 태어나, 그녀의 삶이 펼쳐져 온 골짜기의 북동쪽 굽이굽이 사이로 나 있었다. 블랙무어 골짜기는 그녀에게 세상이었고, 그 주민들은 그 세상의 종족이었다. 말럿의 문과 층계 디딤대에서 그녀는 어린 시절의 경이로운 나날에 그 골짜기를 내려다보았고, 그때 그녀에게 신비였던 것은 지금도 그에 못지않게 신비였다. 그녀는 침실 창에서 날마다 탑들과 마을들과, 어렴풋한 흰 저택들을 보았다. 무엇보다 그 높은 곳에 위풍당당하게 선 섀스턴 마을을 보았으니, 그 창들이 저녁 햇빛 속에서 등불처럼 빛났다. 그녀는 그곳에 거의 가 본 적이 없었고, 골짜기와 그 주변 가운데서도 작은 구역만을 가까이 들여다보아 알고 있을 뿐이었다. 골짜기 바깥 먼 곳은 더더욱 가 본 적이 없었다. 둘러싼 언덕들의 윤곽 하나하나는 친척들의 얼굴만큼이나 그녀에게 친근했다. 그러나 그 너머에 있는 것에 대해서는, 그녀의 판단이 마을 학교에서 배운 가르침에 기댈 뿐이었으니, 그녀는 이 일이 있기 한두 해 전 그곳을 떠날 무렵 그 학교에서 으뜸가는 자리를 차지하고 있었다.\n\n그 어린 시절 그녀는 또래의 같은 성별 아이들에게 무척 사랑받았고, 셋이 한 무리를 이루어—모두 나이가 거의 같았다—나란히 학교에서 집으로 걸어가는 모습으로 마을에서 종종 눈에 띄곤 했다. 테스가 한가운데였다—고운 그물코 무늬가 든 분홍 무명 앞치마를, 본래 빛깔을 잃고 정체 모를 삼차색이 되어 버린 두툼한 원피스 위에 걸치고서—길고 가느다란 다리로 성큼성큼 걸었으니, 채소와 광물 보물을 찾느라 길과 둑에 무릎 꿇다가 찢어진, 무릎께에 작은 사다리꼴 구멍들이 난 꽉 끼는 스타킹을 신고 있었다. 그때 그녀의 흙빛 머리카락은 솥걸이 갈고리처럼 늘어져 있었다. 양옆 두 소녀의 팔은 테스의 허리를 감았고, 그녀의 팔은 두 동무의 어깨에 얹혀 있었다.\n\n테스는 자라면서 형편이 어떤지를 알게 되자, 어머니에게 자못 맬서스주의자가 된 심정을 느꼈다. 어린 동생을 그토록 많이, 생각 없이 안겨 준 탓이었으니, 그들을 돌보고 먹여 살리는 일이 이만저만한 골칫거리가 아니었기 때문이다. 어머니의 지력은 행복한 아이의 그것이었다. 조앤 더비필드는, 섭리에 기대 사는 그녀 자신의 그 긴 가족 명단에 더해진 또 하나의 아이였을 뿐이며, 그것도 맏이는 아니었다.\n\n그래도 테스는 어린 동생들에게 인정 많은 자선을 베풀었고, 그들을 되도록 돕고자, 학교를 그만두자마자 이웃 농장에서 건초 만들기나 추수 일을 거들곤 했다. 또는 더 즐겨, 우유 짜기나 버터 만드는 일을 거들었으니, 이는 아버지가 소를 가지고 있을 적에 배운 것이었다. 손재주가 야물었던 터라, 그것은 그녀가 솜씨를 발휘하는 종류의 일이었다.\n\n날마다 그녀의 어린 어깨에 집안의 짐이 더 얹히는 듯했고, 테스가 더버빌 저택에서 더비필드 가문을 대표하는 일은 당연한 것처럼 다가왔다. 이번 경우만큼은, 더비필드 집안이 자기네 가장 그럴듯한 면을 바깥으로 내세우고 있었음을 인정해야겠다.\n\n그녀는 트랜트리지 크로스에서 합승마차를 내려, 더 체이스라 알려진 지역 쪽으로 난 언덕을 걸어 올랐으니, 들은 바로는 그 경계에 더버빌 마님의 저택 더 슬로프스가 있다고 했다. 그것은 흔히 말하는 영주의 저택, 즉 밭과 목초지와, 주인이 제 식구를 먹여 살릴 수입을 어떻게든 쥐어짜 내야 하는 투덜대는 소작농이 딸린 그런 집이 아니었다. 그보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더한 것이었으니, 오로지 순수한 향락을 위해 지은 시골 저택으로, 거주에 필요한 만큼과, 주인이 직접 손에 쥐고 마름에게 돌보게 하는 작은 취미 농장에 드는 만큼을 넘어서는, 골칫거리가 될 땅이라곤 한 에이커도 딸려 있지 않았다.\n\n진홍빛 벽돌 수위실이 먼저 눈에 들어왔으니, 처마까지 빽빽한 상록수에 파묻혀 있었다. 테스는 이것이 저택 본채인 줄 알았으나, 약간 떨리는 마음으로 옆쪽 작은 문을 지나 안으로 나아가, 진입로가 굽어지는 지점에 이르자 본채가 온전히 시야에 들어왔다. 그것은 최근에 세운 것이었으니—실로 거의 새것이었다—수위실의 상록수와 그토록 대비를 이루던 바로 그 짙은 붉은빛이었다. 집 모퉁이 너머 멀리로—둘레의 차분한 빛깔에 맞서 제라늄 꽃송이처럼 솟아오른 그 모퉁이 너머로—더 체이스의 부드러운 하늘빛 풍경이 펼쳐져 있었으니, 그것은 참으로 유서 깊은 삼림지로, 의심할 바 없는 태곳적 연원을 지닌, 영국에 몇 남지 않은 숲 가운데 하나였다. 거기서는 늙은 떡갈나무에 아직도 드루이드교의 겨우살이가 발견되었고, 사람 손으로 심지 않은 거대한 주목들이, 활을 만들려고 가지를 쳐 내던 그 옛날 자라던 그대로 자라고 있었다. 그러나 이 모든 숲의 태고는, 더 슬로프스에서 보이기는 했어도, 영지의 코앞 경계 바깥이었다.\n\n이 아늑한 저택의 모든 것이 환하고, 번창하고, 잘 손질되어 있었다. 몇 에이커에 걸친 온실들이 비탈을 따라 그 기슭의 잡목숲까지 뻗어 내려갔다. 모든 것이 돈처럼 보였으니—조폐국에서 막 찍어 낸 마지막 동전처럼. 오스트리아소나무와 상록 떡갈나무로 일부 가려진 마구간은 온갖 최신 설비를 갖추고서, 부속 예배당만큼이나 위엄 있어 보였다. 드넓은 잔디밭 위에는 장식용 천막이 서 있었고, 그 문이 그녀 쪽을 향해 있었다.\n\n순박한 테스 더비필드는 자갈 깔린 마당 가장자리에 서서, 반쯤 겁먹은 자세로 멍하니 바라보았다. 자기가 어디 있는지 채 깨닫기도 전에 발이 그녀를 이 지점까지 데려왔던 것인데, 이제 모든 것이 그녀의 예상과는 딴판이었다.\n\n“우리가 오래된 집안인 줄 알았는데, 여긴 죄다 새것이잖아!” 그녀가 천진하게 말했다. 그녀는 ‘친척 찾기’를 두고 그토록 선뜻 어머니의 계획에 응한 것이, 그러느니 집 가까이에서 도움을 얻으려 애써 보았어야 했던 것이 못내 후회스러웠다.\n\n이 모든 것을 소유한 더버빌가—처음엔 스스로를 스토크더버빌가라 불렀던 이들—는, 이런 고풍스러운 시골 지역에서 마주치기엔 다소 별난 집안이었다. 트링엄 목사가, 우리의 어슬렁대는 존 더비필드가 이 주(州) 안이나 그 근처에 현존하는, 옛 더버빌 가문의 진정 유일한 직계 후손이라 말했을 때 그는 참된 말을 했던 것이다. 그는 거기에, 자기가 익히 알고 있던 한 가지를 덧붙일 수도 있었을 것이다. 곧 스토크더버빌가도 그 자신과 마찬가지로 진짜 혈통의 더버빌가가 아니라는 것을. 그래도 이 집안이, 그토록 쇄신이 절실했던 한 가문 이름을 다시 접붙이기에 자못 훌륭한 바탕이 되었음은 인정해야겠다.\n\n근래에 작고한 노(老) 사이먼 스토크 씨가 북부에서 정직한 상인으로(혹자는 돈놀이꾼이라 했다) 재산을 일구었을 때, 그는 자기 사업 구역의 부름이 닿지 않는 잉글랜드 남부에서 향사(鄕士)로 자리 잡기로 마음먹었다. 그러면서 그는, 과거의 약삭빠른 장사치와 자신을 너무 쉽게 결부 짓지 않을 이름, 본래의 밋밋하고 무뚝뚝한 글자보다 덜 흔해 빠진 이름으로 다시 시작할 필요를 느꼈다. 그는 대영박물관에서 한 시간 동안, 자기가 자리 잡으려는 잉글랜드의 그 지역과 관련된 멸절했거나, 반쯤 멸절했거나, 가려졌거나, 몰락한 가문들을 다룬 책장을 들여다본 끝에, ‘더버빌d’Urberville’이라는 이름이 그 어느 것 못지않게 보기에도 듣기에도 좋다고 여겼다. 그리하여 더버빌은 그 자신과 그 영원한 후손을 위해 그의 이름에 덧붙여졌다. 그래도 그는 이 일에서 분에 넘치게 구는 사람은 아니었으니, 새로운 바탕 위에 가계도를 짜면서도 혼인 관계와 귀족 연줄을 엮는 데 마땅히 분별이 있었고, 엄격히 절제된 지위 위로 작위를 단 하나도 끼워 넣지 않았다.\n\n이 상상의 작업을 가엾은 테스와 그 부모는 당연히 몰랐다—그들로서는 자못 곤혹스럽게도. 실로 그런 덧붙임이 가능하다는 것조차 그들은 알지 못했으니, 잘 타고난 용모야 운수의 선물일지 몰라도 가문 이름은 천성으로 오는 것이라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n테스가 물에 뛰어들려는 사람처럼, 물러설지 밀고 나갈지 좀체 가늠하지 못한 채 여전히 머뭇거리며 서 있을 때, 한 형체가 천막의 어두운 삼각형 문에서 걸어 나왔다. 담배를 피우는 키 큰 젊은이였다.\n\n그는 거의 거무스름한 안색에, 비록 붉고 매끄럽긴 했어도 모양새가 영 어설픈 도톰한 입술을 지녔고, 그 위로는 끝을 말아 올린, 잘 손질된 검은 콧수염이 있었다. 그러나 그의 나이는 스물서넛을 넘지 않았을 것이다. 그 윤곽에 야만적인 데가 묻어 있긴 했어도, 그 신사의 얼굴에는, 그리고 대담하게 굴리는 눈에는 남다른 힘이 있었다.\n\n“자, 어여쁜 아가씨, 무얼 도와 드릴까요?” 그가 앞으로 나오며 말했다. 그러다 그녀가 잔뜩 어쩔 줄 몰라 서 있는 것을 알아채고는, “나는 신경 쓰지 마요. 나는 더버빌이오. 나를 보러 왔소, 아니면 우리 어머니를 보러?”\n\n이 더버빌가의 화신이자 같은 이름을 지닌 자는, 집과 정원이 그러했던 것보다도 더, 테스가 예상했던 바와 달랐다. 그녀는 나이 들고 위엄 있는 얼굴을, 모든 더버빌가 용모의 정수를, 그녀 가문과 잉글랜드 역사의 여러 세기를 상형문자로 새겨 담은 듯 화신된 기억들로 깊이 주름진 얼굴을 꿈꾸어 왔다. 그러나 그녀는 빠져나갈 수 없는 일이었으므로, 눈앞의 용무에 마음을 다잡고 대답했다—\n\n“어머님을 뵈러 왔어요, 선생님.”\n\n“유감이지만 만나실 수 없을 거요—병약하신 분이라.” 가짜 가문의 현 대표자가 대답했다. 이자가 곧 최근 작고한 신사의 외아들 알렉 씨였던 것이다. “내가 그 용무를 대신할 수는 없겠소? 어머니를 뵙고자 하는 용건이 무엇이오?”\n\n“용건이라기보다—그게—뭐라 말해야 할지 모르겠어요!”\n\n“기쁜 일이오?”\n\n“오, 아뇨. 저, 선생님, 말씀드리면 좀—”\n\n이제 자기 심부름이 어딘가 우스꽝스럽다는 느낌이 테스에게 어찌나 강했던지, 그에 대한 경외감과 이곳에 있는 데서 오는 전반적인 거북함에도 불구하고, 그녀의 발그레한 입술이 미소를 향해 휘었고, 이는 거무스름한 알렉산더의 마음을 자못 끌었다.\n\n“너무나 바보 같은 일이라.” 그녀가 더듬거렸다. “말씀드릴 수 없을 것 같아요!”\n\n“상관없소. 나는 바보 같은 것을 좋아하니. 다시 해 봐요, 아가씨.” 그가 다정하게 말했다.\n\n“어머니가 가 보라고 하셨어요.” 테스가 말을 이었다. “게다가, 정말이지, 저 스스로도 그러려던 참이었고요. 하지만 이럴 줄은 몰랐어요. 저는 선생님께, 우리가 선생님과 같은 가문이라는 걸 말씀드리러 왔어요.”\n\n“호! 가난한 친척이라?”\n\n“네.”\n\n“스토크가?”\n\n“아뇨, 더버빌가요.”\n\n“그래, 그래. 더버빌가 말이오.”\n\n“우리 이름은 닳고 닳아 더비필드가 됐지만, 우리가 더버빌가라는 증거가 여럿 있어요. 고고학자들이 그렇다고 하고요—그리고—그리고 우리한테는 오래된 인장이 있는데, 방패 위에 앞발을 치켜든 사자가, 그 위에 성이 새겨져 있어요. 그리고 아주 오래된 은수저도 있는데, 작은 국자처럼 우묵하고, 똑같은 성이 새겨져 있어요. 다만 어찌나 닳았는지 어머니가 완두콩 수프 젓는 데 쓰지만요.”\n\n“은빛 성은 분명 내 문장(紋章)이오.” 그가 부드럽게 말했다. “그리고 내 문장(紋章)은 앞발을 치켜든 사자고.”\n\n“그래서 어머니가, 우리가 선생님께 알리는 게 좋겠다고 하셨어요—우리가 안 좋은 사고로 말을 잃었고, 또 이 가문에서 가장 오래된 갈래라서요.”\n\n“참으로 친절하신 어머님이로군. 그리고 나로서는, 그분이 그러신 걸 후회하지 않소.” 알렉은 말하면서, 테스가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히게 하는 그런 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다. “그래서, 어여쁜 아가씨, 친척으로서 우리에게 다정한 방문을 온 거요?”\n\n“그런 셈이에요.” 테스가, 다시금 불편한 기색으로 우물거렸다.\n\n“음—나쁠 거 없지. 어디 사오? 무얼 하는 사람이오?”\n\n그녀는 그에게 간략히 사정을 말해 주었다. 그리고 이어진 물음들에 답하며, 자기를 데려다준 바로 그 마부 편으로 돌아갈 작정이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n“그자가 트랜트리지 크로스를 다시 지나가려면 한참 멀었소. 시간이나 보낼 겸 정원이나 한 바퀴 둘러볼까, 어여쁜 사촌 누이?”\n\n테스는 방문을 되도록 짧게 줄이고 싶었으나, 젊은이가 졸라 대는 통에 그를 따라가기로 했다. 그는 그녀를 잔디밭과 화단과 온실로 이끌었고, 거기서 다시 과수원과 유리 온실로 데려가, 딸기를 좋아하느냐고 물었다.\n\n“네.” 테스가 말했다. “철이 되면요.”\n\n“벌써 났소.” 더버빌은 그녀를 위해 열매 표본을 따기 시작했고, 몸을 굽히며 그것을 그녀에게 건네주었다. 이윽고 ‘브리티시 퀸’ 품종 가운데 유난히 좋은 것을 하나 골라, 똑바로 서서 줄기를 잡고 그녀의 입가에 들이밀었다.\n\n“아뇨—아니에요!” 그녀가 재빨리, 그의 손과 제 입술 사이에 손가락을 집어넣으며 말했다. “제 손으로 받는 게 좋겠어요.”\n\n“쓸데없는 소리!” 그가 우겼다. 그래서 그녀는 약간 곤혹스러운 채로 입술을 벌려 그것을 받아 물었다.\n\n그들은 이런 식으로 한동안 정처 없이 거닐었고, 테스는 더버빌이 건네는 것은 무엇이든 반쯤 즐거운, 반쯤 내키지 않는 상태로 먹었다. 그녀가 더는 딸기를 먹지 못하게 되자 그는 그녀의 작은 바구니를 딸기로 가득 채웠다. 그러고 나서 둘은 장미나무 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 그는 꽃을 따 그녀에게 가슴에 꽂으라고 주었다. 그녀는 꿈속에 있는 사람처럼 따랐고, 더는 꽂을 데가 없게 되자 그가 직접 그녀의 모자에 꽃봉오리 한두 개를 꽂아 주고, 넘쳐흐르는 인심으로 다른 꽃들을 그녀의 바구니에 수북이 쌓았다. 마침내 그는 시계를 보며 말했다. “자, 뭐 좀 먹고 나면, 섀스턴 가는 마부를 잡으려거든 떠날 시간이 될 거요. 이리 와요, 무슨 먹을거리가 있나 보고 올 테니.”\n\n스토크더버빌은 그녀를 잔디밭으로, 그리고 천막 안으로 데려가, 거기 두고 갔다가 곧 가벼운 점심거리가 든 바구니를 들고 다시 나타나, 손수 그녀 앞에 놓아 주었다. 이 즐거운 단둘만의 자리를 하인들에게 방해받고 싶지 않다는 것이 분명 그 신사의 바람이었다.\n\n“담배 좀 피워도 괜찮겠소?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“오, 전혀요, 선생님.”\n\n그는 천막 안에 자욱이 퍼진 연기의 타래 사이로, 그녀가 예쁘게, 또 무심하게 우물거리는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그리고 테스 더비필드는, 제 가슴의 장미를 천진하게 내려다보면서도, 저 푸른 마취의 안개 뒤에 자기 극(劇)의 ‘비극적 화근’이 될 자가, 그녀의 젊은 삶의 스펙트럼에서 핏빛 붉은 광선이 되기 십상인 자가 잠재해 있음을 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 바로 지금, 단점이나 다름없는 한 가지 속성을 지니고 있었다. 알렉 더버빌의 눈을 그녀에게 못 박히게 한 것이 바로 그것이었다. 그것은 외양의 무성함, 성숙의 충만함이었으니, 그것이 그녀를 실제보다 더 여인답게 보이게 했다. 그녀는 그 이목구비를, 그것이 가리키는 성질 없이, 어머니에게서 물려받았다. 그것은 이따금 그녀의 마음을 괴롭혀 왔으나, 동무들이 그건 세월이 고쳐 줄 흠이라고 말해 주곤 했다.\n\n그녀는 곧 점심을 다 먹었다. “이제 집에 갈게요, 선생님.” 그녀가 일어서며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 이름이 뭐라고 했소?” 그가, 집이 보이지 않는 데까지 진입로를 따라 그녀를 배웅하며 물었다.\n\n“테스 더비필드예요, 저 아래 말럿의.”\n\n“그래, 당신네 식구가 말을 잃었다고 했지?”\n\n“제가—그 애를 죽였어요!” 그녀가 프린스의 죽음을 자세히 일러 주며, 두 눈에 눈물이 차올라 대답했다. “그래서 그 일로 아버지께 무얼 어찌해 드려야 할지 모르겠어요!”\n\n“내가 무어라도 할 수 있을지 생각해 봐야겠소. 우리 어머니가 당신한테 일자리를 마련해 주셔야지. 하지만, 테스, ‘더버빌’이니 하는 헛소리는 마요—그냥 ‘더비필드’요, 알겠소—아주 다른 이름이지.”\n\n“저는 그보다 나은 걸 바라지 않아요, 선생님.” 그녀가 다소 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n한순간—오직 한순간—그들이 진입로 굽이에서, 키 큰 진달래와 침엽수 사이, 수위실이 보이기 전에 이르렀을 때, 그는 마치 그러려는 듯 그녀에게 얼굴을 기울였다—그러나, 아니다. 그는 마음을 고쳐먹고 그녀를 보내 주었다.\n\n이렇게 그 일이 시작되었다. 이 만남의 의미를 알아챘더라면, 그녀는 어찌하여 자기가 그날 엉뚱한 남자에게 눈에 띄고 탐내어질 운명이었던가를, 모든 면에서—인간이 옳고 바라는 것을 빚어낼 수 있는 한도껏 옳고 바라는—그 다른 남자에게가 아니었던가를 물었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀의 지인들 가운데 그런 부류에 가까웠을 자에게, 그녀는 반쯤 잊힌, 한때 스쳐 간 인상에 지나지 않았다.\n\n사물의 잘 짜인 계획이 서툴게 실행되는 가운데, 부름이 정작 올 사람을 불러내는 일은 드물고, 사랑할 사람이 사랑할 때와 맞아떨어지는 일도 좀처럼 없다. 자연은 가엾은 제 피조물에게, 봄이 행복한 행함으로 이어질 수 있는 그때에 “보라!” 하고 일러 주는 일이 흔치 않다. 또 “어디?” 하는 어떤 몸의 외침에 “여기!” 하고 응답해 주는 일도, 그 숨바꼭질이 지긋지긋하고 닳아 빠진 놀이가 되어 버린 뒤에야 비로소 있을 뿐이다. 우리는 인류 진보의 절정이자 정점에 이르면, 지금 우리를 이리저리 덜컹대며 굴리는 것보다 더 섬세한 직관과, 더 긴밀한 사회 기구의 상호작용에 의해 이 시대착오들이 바로잡힐지 궁금해할 법도 하다. 그러나 그런 완전함은 예언할 수도, 가능하다고 떠올릴 수조차 없다. 이번 경우에도, 수백만의 경우와 마찬가지로, 완전한 전체의 두 반쪽이 완전한 순간에 마주한 것이 아니었음을 아는 것으로 족하다. 사라진 짝은 제멋대로 땅 위를 헤매며, 어리석을 만큼 둔하게, 그 뒤늦은 때가 올 때까지 기다리고 있었던 것이다. 이 서투른 지체에서 불안과, 실망과, 충격과, 파국과, 야릇하기 그지없는 운명들이 솟아났다.\n\n더버빌은 천막으로 돌아와, 얼굴에 흡족한 빛을 띤 채 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 의자에 걸터앉았다. 그러더니 그는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“이런, 빌어먹을! 거 참 우스운 일이군! 하하하! 그리고 어찌나 풋풋한 계집인지!”\n\n# VI\n\n테스는 언덕을 내려와 트랜트리지 크로스로 갔고, 체이스버러에서 섀스턴으로 돌아가는 합승마차에 자리를 잡으려 멍하니 기다렸다. 그녀는 마차에 오르며 다른 승객들이 자기에게 무어라 했는지 알지 못했다. 그들에게 대답은 했어도 말이다. 그리고 마차가 다시 출발하자, 그녀는 바깥이 아니라 안을 향한 눈으로 실려 갔다.\n\n동승객 가운데 하나가, 그때까지 누가 했던 것보다 더 콕 집어 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. “어머, 댁은 아주 꽃다발이로구먼! 그것도 유월 초에 이런 장미라니!”\n\n그제야 그녀는 자기가 그들의 놀란 눈에 어떤 광경으로 비치는지를 알아차렸다. 가슴에는 장미가, 모자에도 장미가, 바구니에는 장미와 딸기가 가장자리까지 가득했다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며, 꽃은 누가 준 것이라고 어쩔 줄 모르고 말했다. 승객들이 보지 않을 때, 그녀는 모자에서 더 눈에 띄는 꽃들을 슬그머니 떼어 바구니에 넣고, 그 위를 손수건으로 덮었다. 그러고는 다시 생각에 잠겼는데, 아래를 내려다보다가 가슴에 남아 있던 장미 가시가 그만 그녀의 턱을 찔렀다. 블랙무어 골짜기의 모든 시골 사람들이 그렇듯, 테스도 공상과, 앞일을 점치는 미신에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 그녀는 이것을 흉조로 여겼으니—그날 처음으로 알아챈 흉조였다.\n\n합승마차는 섀스턴까지밖에 가지 않았고, 그 산악 마을에서 골짜기로, 말럿까지 걸어 내려가야 할 길이 몇 마일이었다. 어머니는, 혹 너무 지쳐 더 못 가겠거든 거기서, 그들이 아는 어느 시골 아낙의 집에서 하룻밤 묵으라고 일러 두었다. 그래서 테스는 그렇게 했고, 이튿날 오후에야 집으로 내려갔다. 집에 들어선 순간, 그녀는 어머니의 의기양양한 태도에서 그새 무슨 일이 있었음을 알아챘다.\n\n“오, 그래, 다 알고 있다! 잘될 거라고 했잖니, 이제 증명됐어!”\n\n“제가 없는 사이에요? 뭐가요?” 테스가 다소 지친 듯 말했다.\n\n어머니는 짓궂게 흐뭇해하며 소녀를 위아래로 훑어보고는, 놀려 대듯 말을 이었다. “그래, 네가 그 사람들 마음을 사로잡았구나!”\n\n“어떻게 아세요, 어머니?”\n\n“편지를 받았다.”\n\n그제야 테스는 그럴 만한 시간이 있었음을 떠올렸다.\n\n“그분들이—더버빌 마님이—말씀하시기로는, 당신 취미인 작은 닭 농장을 네가 돌봐 줬으면 한다는구나. 하지만 이건 네 기대를 부풀리지 않고 너를 거기로 데려가려는, 그분의 영리한 수법일 뿐이야. 너를 친척으로 인정하시려는 거다—그게 그 뜻이야.”\n“하지만 전 그분을 못 뵈었어요.”\n\n“그래도 누구는 봤겠지?”\n\n“아드님을 봤어요.”\n\n“그래서 그 사람이 너를 친척으로 인정하더냐?”\n\n“음—저더러 사촌 누이라고 불렀어요.”\n\n“그럴 줄 알았다니까! 재키—그 사람이 얘더러 사촌 누이라고 불렀대!” 조앤이 남편에게 외쳤다. “그러니 당연히 자기 어머니한테 말했을 거고, 그분이 너를 거기 두고 싶어 하시는 거야.”\n\n“하지만 제가 닭을 잘 칠 수 있을지 모르겠어요.” 미심쩍어하는 테스가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 누가 잘 친다는 거냐. 너는 그 일을 타고났고, 그 속에서 자랐어. 그 일을 타고난 사람은 어떤 도제보다도 늘 더 잘 아는 법이야. 게다가 그건 그저 네가 신세 진다는 느낌이 들지 않게, 뭔가 할 일이 있는 척 보이려는 것일 뿐이라니까.”\n\n“아무래도 가지 말아야 할 것 같아요.” 테스가 골똘히 말했다. “누가 편지를 썼대요? 제가 좀 봐도 돼요?”\n\n“더버빌 마님이 쓰셨지. 여기 있다.”\n\n편지는 삼인칭으로 쓰여 있었고, 더비필드 부인에게, 그 댁 따님이 그 마님의 닭 농장 운영에 보탬이 될 것이며, 와 줄 수 있다면 편안한 방을 마련해 두겠고, 마음에 들면 후한 급료를 주겠노라고 간략히 알리고 있었다.\n\n“오—그게 다예요!” 테스가 말했다.\n\n“그분이 한걸음에 너를 끌어안고 입 맞추고 어루만지기를 바랄 순 없잖니.”\n\n테스는 창밖을 내다보았다.\n\n“저는 차라리 아버지하고 어머니랑 여기 있고 싶어요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“근데 왜?”\n\n“왜인지는 말씀드리지 않겠어요, 어머니. 사실, 저도 왜인지 잘 모르겠어요.”\n\n일주일 뒤 어느 날 저녁, 그녀는 바로 근처에서 가벼운 일자리를 헛되이 찾아 헤매다 돌아왔다. 그녀의 생각은 여름 동안 말 한 마리를 또 살 만한 돈을 그러모으자는 것이었다. 문턱을 넘기가 무섭게 아이 하나가 방을 가로질러 춤추듯 달려 나오며 말했다. “그 신사분이 왔다 갔어!”\n\n어머니가 온몸의 구석구석에서 미소를 터뜨리며 서둘러 설명했다. 더버빌 마님의 아들이 말을 타고 찾아왔으니, 우연히 말럿 쪽으로 말을 달리던 참이었다는 것이다. 그는 어머니를 대신해, 테스가 그 노부인의 닭 농장을 정말로 맡으러 올 수 있는지 없는지 마침내 확실히 알고 싶어 했으니, 그때까지 새들을 돌보던 사내아이가 미덥지 못한 것으로 드러났던 것이다. “더버빌 씨 말이, 네가 보이는 그대로의 아이라면 분명 착한 처녀일 거고, 너는 분명 제 몸무게만큼의 황금만 한 값어치가 있다지 뭐냐. 그분이 너한테 아주 관심이 많으셔—솔직히 말하면.”\n\n테스는, 제 스스로의 평가로는 그토록 낮게 가라앉아 있던 차에, 낯선 이에게 그토록 높은 평을 얻었다는 말을 듣고는 한순간 정말로 기뻐하는 듯했다.\n\n“그렇게 생각해 주시다니 참 친절하시네요.” 그녀가 중얼거렸다. “거기서 사는 게 어떨지 확실히 알 수만 있다면, 언제라도 가겠어요.”\n\n“그분은 아주 잘생긴 사람이더라!”\n\n“전 그렇게 생각 안 해요.” 테스가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 어쨌든 너한테 온 기회야. 그리고 그 사람, 분명 아름다운 다이아몬드 반지를 끼고 있더라!”\n\n“네.” 어린 에이브러햄이 창가 의자에서 환하게 말했다. “나도 봤어! 그 사람이 콧스염한테 손을 올릴 때 그게 반짝거렸어. 엄마, 우리 대단한 친척은 왜 자꾸 콧스염한테 손을 올렸어?”\n\n“저 애 좀 보게!” 더비필드 부인이 곁들이듯 감탄하며 외쳤다.\n\n“아마 다이아몬드 반지를 자랑하려고 그랬겠지.” 존 경이 의자에서 꿈결처럼 중얼거렸다.\n\n“생각해 볼게요.” 테스가 방을 나서며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 얘가 우리 집안의 젊은 갈래를 단번에 정복해 버린 거예요.” 안주인이 남편에게 말을 이었다. “그러니 이걸 밀어붙이지 않으면 바보지요.”\n\n“내 자식들이 집을 떠나는 건 영 마뜩잖아.” 흥정꾼이 말했다. “이 집안의 가장으로서, 나머지가 나한테 와야 하는 거야.”\n\n“그래도 얘를 보내요, 재키.” 가엾은 어리석은 아내가 구슬렸다. “그 사람이 얘한테 푹 빠졌다니까요—척 보면 알아요. 얘더러 사촌 누이라고 불렀잖아요! 십중팔구 얘랑 결혼해서 귀부인으로 만들어 줄 거예요. 그러면 얘는 제 조상들이 그랬던 그 모습이 되는 거고요.”\n\n존 더비필드는 기력이나 건강보다 자만이 더 많은 사람이었던 터라, 이런 추측이 그에게는 흐뭇했다.\n\n“음, 어쩌면 젊은 더버빌 씨가 뜻하는 게 그것일지도 모르지.” 그가 인정했다. “그리고 정말이지, 옛 혈통에 줄을 대 제 핏줄을 끌어올리겠다는 진지한 생각이 그자한테 있을 법도 해. 테스, 그 깜찍한 것! 정말로 이런 결말을 보자고 그자들을 찾아갔단 말이야?”\n\n그동안 테스는 텃밭의 구스베리 덤불 사이를, 그리고 프린스의 무덤 위를 골똘히 거닐고 있었다. 그녀가 들어오자 어머니가 기세를 몰아붙였다.\n\n“그래, 어쩔 작정이냐?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“제가 더버빌 마님을 뵈었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.” 테스가 말했다.\n\n“이쯤 해서 결정하는 게 낫겠다. 그러면 곧 그분을 뵙게 될 테니.”\n\n아버지가 의자에서 기침을 했다.\n\n“뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠어요!” 소녀가 안절부절못하며 대답했다. “결정은 어머니가 하셔야죠. 제가 늙은 말을 죽였으니, 새 말을 구해 드리려면 뭐라도 해야 한다고는 생각해요. 하지만—하지만—더버빌 씨가 거기 있는 게 영 마뜩잖아요!”\n\n아이들은, 말이 죽은 뒤로 테스가 부유한 친척(그들은 저쪽 집안이 그러하다고 여겼다)에게 거두어지리라는 이 생각을 일종의 슬픔의 진통제로 삼아 왔던 터라, 테스가 내키지 않아 하자 울음을 터뜨리며, 그녀가 망설인다고 보채고 나무랐다.\n\n“테스 누나는 안 가아아아서 귀부우우인이 안 될 거래!—싫대, 안 가아아안대!” 그들이 입을 네모지게 벌리고 통곡했다. “그럼 우리는 멋진 새 말도 없고, 장난감 살 황금 돈도 잔뜩 없잖아! 그리고 테스 누나는 제일 좋은 옷 입고 예쁘게 안 보일 거야 이제 더는!”\n\n어머니도 같은 가락으로 거들었다. 게다가, 집안일을 한없이 질질 끌어 실제보다 더 고되게 보이게 하는 그녀 특유의 방식도 이 논쟁에 무게를 더했다. 아버지만이 홀로 중립의 태도를 지켰다.\n\n“갈게요.” 마침내 테스가 말했다.\n\n어머니는 딸의 승낙이 불러일으킨 혼례의 환상에 대한 자각을 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n“그래야지! 이렇게 예쁜 처녀한테, 이건 좋은 기회다!”\n\n테스가 못마땅한 듯 웃었다.\n\n“돈 버는 기회면 좋겠어요. 그 외에 다른 기회는 아니고요. 혼처니 하는 그 바보 같은 소리는 입 밖에 내지 않는 게 좋겠어요.”\n\n더비필드 부인은 약속하지 않았다. 그 방문객의 말을 듣고 난 뒤로, 자기가 자못 자랑스러워 할 말이 많지 않다고는 도저히 장담할 수 없었던 것이다. 이렇게 일은 정해졌다. 그리고 젊은 처녀는, 부르는 날이 언제든 떠날 채비를 하겠노라 동의하는 편지를 썼다. 그녀는, 더버빌 마님이 그녀의 결정을 기뻐하며, 모레 골짜기 꼭대기에서 그녀와 짐을 맞으러 용수철 달린 짐마차를 보낼 테니, 그날 출발할 준비를 해 두라는 통지를 정식으로 받았다. 더버빌 마님의 필체는 다소 남자 같아 보였다.\n\n“짐마차라고?” 조앤 더비필드가 미심쩍어하며 중얼거렸다. “제 친척한테라면 사륜마차쯤 보냈을 법한데!”\n\n마침내 길을 정하고 나자 테스는 한결 덜 안절부절못하고, 덜 멍해졌다. 그리 고되지 않을 일거리로 아버지께 말 한 마리를 마련해 드린다는 생각에 얼마간 자신감을 품고 제 일을 해 나갔다. 그녀는 학교 교사가 되기를 바랐으나, 운명은 달리 정한 듯했다. 마음으로는 어머니보다 어른스러웠던 터라, 자기를 향한 더비필드 부인의 혼인에 대한 희망 따위를 그녀는 한순간도 진지하게 여기지 않았다. 그 경박한 여인은 딸이 태어난 그해부터 줄곧 딸에게 좋은 혼처를 찾아 주고 있었던 것이다.\n\n# VII\n\n떠나기로 정해진 날 아침, 테스는 동트기 전에—어둠의 가장자리 그 한 찰나, 숲이 아직 고요한 가운데, 제 딴에는 적어도 자기가 하루 중 정확한 시각을 안다고 또렷한 목소리로 확신하며 노래하는 단 한 마리 예언자 같은 새만이 깨어 있고, 나머지는 그가 틀렸다고 똑같이 확신하기라도 하듯 침묵을 지키던 그때—깨어 있었다. 그녀는 아침 식사 때까지 위층에서 짐을 꾸리다가, 평소의 평일 옷차림으로 내려왔으니, 나들이옷은 상자 안에 곱게 개어 넣어 두었다.\n\n어머니가 나무랐다. “네 친척을 만나러 가면서, 그것보다 좀 더 멋쟁이로 차려입지 않고 나설 셈이냐?”\n\n“하지만 전 일하러 가는 거예요!” 테스가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 그렇긴 하다.” 더비필드 부인이 말하고는, 은밀한 어조로 “처음엔 좀 그런 척이 있을 수도 있지…. 하지만 네 가장 좋은 면을 바깥으로 내보이는 게 더 현명할 거다.” 하고 덧붙였다.\n\n“알았어요. 어머니가 제일 잘 아시겠죠.” 테스가 차분히 내맡기듯 대답했다.\n\n그리고 어버이를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 소녀는 제 몸을 온전히 조앤의 손에 맡기며 평온하게 말했다—“마음대로 하세요, 어머니.”\n\n더비필드 부인은 이 순순함에 더없이 기뻤다. 먼저 그녀는 커다란 대야를 가져와 테스의 머리를 어찌나 정성껏 감겼던지, 말려서 빗질하고 나니 다른 때의 갑절은 많아 보였다. 그녀는 평소보다 더 넓은 분홍 리본으로 그것을 묶었다. 그러고는, 테스가 클럽 행진 때 입었던 흰 원피스를 그녀에게 입혔으니, 그 산뜻하게 풍성한 자락이 부풀린 머리 모양과 어우러져, 한창 피어나는 그녀의 몸매에 나이를 무색케 하는 풍만함을 더해 주었고, 그녀가 아직 아이나 다름없는데도 여인으로 여겨지게 할 만했다.\n\n“어머, 내 스타킹 뒤꿈치에 구멍이 났네!” 테스가 말했다.\n\n“스타킹 구멍 따위는 신경 쓰지 마라—그건 말을 안 하니까! 내가 처녀였을 적엔, 예쁜 보닛만 있으면 뒤꿈치쯤 악마가 보든 말든 상관없었다.”\n\n소녀의 모습에 대한 어머니의 자랑스러움은, 화가가 화폭에서 물러서듯 그녀를 한 걸음 물러서서 제 작품을 전체로 살펴보게 했다.\n\n“네 모습을 봐야 한다!” 그녀가 외쳤다. “요전 날보다 훨씬 낫구나.”\n\n거울이 한 번에 테스 몸의 아주 작은 부분만 비출 만큼 작았던 터라, 더비필드 부인은 검은 망토를 여닫이창 바깥에 걸어, 차려입기 좋아하는 시골 사람들이 으레 하듯 유리창을 커다란 반사경으로 만들었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 아래층의 남편에게로 내려갔으니, 그는 아래 방에 앉아 있었다.\n\n“내 당신한테 말해 두는데, 더비필드.” 그녀가 의기양양하게 말했다. “그 사람이 얘를 사랑하지 않고는 못 배길 거예요. 하지만 무슨 일이 있어도, 그 사람이 얘를 마음에 둔 것이며 얘가 잡은 이 기회에 대해 테스한테 너무 많이 말하진 말아요. 얘는 워낙 별난 처녀라, 그 사람한테, 아니면 거기 가는 것 자체에, 지금이라도 마음이 틀어질 수 있으니까. 다 잘되면, 우리한테 알려 준 스태그풋 레인의 목사님께 꼭 뭔가 보답을 해야겠어요—참 친절하고 좋은 분이지!”\n\n그러나 소녀가 떠날 순간이 다가오면서, 차려입을 때의 첫 흥분이 가시자 조앤 더비필드의 마음에는 작은 불안이 자리 잡았다. 그것은 안주인으로 하여금, 조금 거기까지—골짜기에서 바깥세상으로 처음 가파르게 솟아오르는 오르막이 시작되는 지점까지—따라 걷겠노라 말하게 했다. 그 꼭대기에서 테스는 스토크더버빌가에서 보낸 용수철 짐마차를 만나기로 되어 있었고, 그녀의 상자는 손수레꾼 소년 편으로 이미 그 정상을 향해 미리 실려 가 채비를 갖추어 두었다.\n\n어머니가 보닛을 쓰는 것을 보고, 어린아이들이 따라가겠다고 떼를 썼다.\n\n“언니가 우리 신사 사촌이랑 결혼해서 멋진 옷 입으러 가는데, 나도 언니랑 조금만 같이 걸을래!”\n\n“자.” 테스가 얼굴을 붉히며 홱 돌아서서 말했다. “그런 소리는 더 듣지 않겠어! 어머니, 어쩌자고 애들 머릿속에 그런 헛것을 집어넣으셨어요?”\n\n“우리 부유한 친척한테 일하러 가는 거란다, 얘들아. 그래서 새 말 살 돈을 마련하는 걸 돕는 거고.” 더비필드 부인이 달래듯 말했다.\n\n“안녕히 계세요, 아버지.” 테스가 목이 메어 말했다.\n\n“잘 가거라, 우리 딸.” 존 경이 가슴에서 머리를 들며 말했으니, 그는 오늘 아침 이 일을 기려 약간 과음한 탓에 들었던 선잠을 멈춘 참이었다. “그래, 내 젊은 친구가 제 핏줄의 이렇게 어여쁜 본보기를 마음에 들어 했으면 좋겠구나. 그리고 테스, 그자한테 전해라. 우리가 옛 영화에서 깡그리 가라앉았으니, 내가 그 작위를 그자한테 팔겠다고—그래, 팔겠다고—그것도 터무니없는 값은 아닐 거라고.”\n\n“천 파운드 밑으로는 안 돼요!” 더비필드 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n“그자한테 전해라—천 파운드 받겠다고. 음, 곰곰 생각해 보면, 그보다 덜 받아도 되지. 그자가 나 같은 어수룩한 위인보다야 그걸 더 빛나게 할 테니. 그자한테 백 파운드면 가지라고 전해라. 하지만 자잘한 데 매달리진 않겠다—그자한테 오십 파운드면 가지라고 해—이십 파운드면! 그래, 이십 파운드—그게 제일 낮은 값이다. 빌어먹을, 가문의 명예는 가문의 명예니, 한 푼도 덜 받진 않겠다!”\n\n테스의 두 눈은 너무 그렁그렁했고 목소리는 너무 메어 있어, 가슴속에 있는 심정을 차마 입에 담을 수 없었다. 그녀는 홱 돌아서서 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n그리하여 소녀들과 어머니가 다 함께 걸었다. 테스의 양옆에 아이 하나씩이 그녀의 손을 잡고서, 마치 큰일을 하러 가는 이를 보듯 이따금 골똘한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 어머니는 가장 어린것을 데리고 바로 뒤를 따랐다. 그 무리는, 순진함이 양옆을 지키고 순박한 허영심이 뒤를 받친, 정직한 아름다움의 한 폭 그림을 이루었다. 그들은 길을 따라가다 오르막이 시작되는 지점에 이르렀는데, 그 마루에서 트랜트리지의 마차가 그녀를 태우기로 되어 있었으니, 마지막 비탈을 오르는 수고를 말에게 덜어 주려고 그 한계가 정해진 것이었다. 첫 언덕들 너머 저 멀리, 섀스턴의 절벽 같은 집들이 능선을 끊고 있었다. 오르막을 끼고 도는 높다란 길에는, 그들이 앞서 보낸 소년 말고는 아무도 보이지 않았으니, 그는 테스의 온 세간이 담긴 손수레 손잡이에 걸터앉아 있었다.\n\n“여기서 조금만 기다리면 곧 마차가 올 거다, 틀림없이.” 더비필드 부인이 말했다. “그래, 저기 보이는구나!”\n\n마차가 왔다—가장 가까운 고지의 이마 뒤에서 불쑥 나타나, 손수레를 둔 소년 곁에 멈춰 섰다. 그러자 어머니와 아이들은 더는 가지 않기로 했고, 부랴부랴 작별을 고하니, 테스는 언덕 위로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n그들은 그녀의 흰 형체가, 이미 상자가 실려 있는 용수철 짐마차로 다가가는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그녀가 그것에 채 닿기도 전에, 또 다른 마차 한 대가 정상의 나무 덤불에서 튀어나와, 거기 길의 굽이를 돌아 짐마차를 지나치고는, 테스 곁에 멈춰 섰다. 그녀는 몹시 놀란 듯 고개를 들었다.\n\n어머니는, 그 두 번째 마차가 첫 번째 같은 초라한 탈것이 아니라, 번쩍번쩍 옻칠을 하고 잘 꾸민, 말끔한 이륜마차임을 처음으로 알아보았다. 마부는 스물서넛쯤 된 젊은이로, 이 사이에 시가를 물고 있었으니, 멋쟁이 모자에, 황갈색 윗도리며, 같은 빛깔의 반바지, 흰 목수건, 빳빳이 세운 옷깃, 그리고 갈색 운전용 장갑을 끼고 있었다—한마디로, 한두 주 전에 테스에 대한 대답을 들으러 조앤을 찾아왔던, 그 잘생기고 말 잘 타는 젊은 한량이었다.\n\n더비필드 부인은 아이처럼 손뼉을 쳤다. 그러더니 아래를 내려다보았다가, 다시 빤히 쳐다보았다. 이것이 무슨 뜻인지 내가 잘못 본 걸까?\n\n“언니를 귀부인으로 만들어 줄 신사 친척이 저 사람이야?” 가장 어린아이가 물었다.\n\n그동안 모슬린을 두른 테스의 형체가, 이 마차 곁에 가만히, 어쩔 줄 모르고 서 있는 것이 보였으니, 그 마차 주인이 그녀에게 말을 걸고 있었다. 그녀의 우유부단해 보이는 태도는, 실은 우유부단함을 넘어선 것이었다. 그것은 불안이었다. 그녀는 초라한 짐마차 쪽이 차라리 나았을 것이다. 젊은이가 마차에서 내려, 그녀에게 어서 오르라고 재촉하는 듯했다. 그녀는 언덕 아래 친척들 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려, 그 작은 무리를 바라보았다. 무언가가 그녀를 결심으로 다그치는 듯했으니, 어쩌면 자기가 프린스를 죽였다는 생각이었을 것이다. 그녀는 불쑥 올라탔고, 그가 그녀 곁에 오르더니, 곧장 말에 채찍질을 했다. 순식간에 그들은 상자를 실은 느린 짐마차를 지나쳐, 언덕의 어깨 뒤로 사라졌다.\n\n테스가 시야에서 사라지고, 한 편의 극으로서 이 일의 흥미가 끝나자마자, 어린것들의 눈에 눈물이 가득 고였다. 가장 어린아이가 “가엾은, 불쌍한 테스 누나가 귀부인 되러 떠나지 않았으면 좋겠어!” 하고 말하더니, 입꼬리를 축 늘어뜨리며 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그 새로운 관점은 옮아 가는 것이어서, 그다음 아이도 똑같이 했고, 또 그다음 아이도 그러더니, 마침내 셋 모두가 큰 소리로 울부짖었다.\n\n조앤 더비필드의 눈에도 눈물이 고였으니, 집으로 돌아가려 돌아설 때였다. 그러나 마을로 돌아왔을 즈음엔, 그녀는 우연의 호의에 수동적으로 기대고 있었다. 그래도 그날 밤 잠자리에서 그녀가 한숨을 쉬자, 남편이 무슨 일이냐고 물었다.\n\n“오, 나도 잘은 모르겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “어쩌면 테스를 보내지 않는 편이 나았을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서요.”\n\n“그런 건 진작 생각했어야 하는 거 아냐?”\n\n“음, 얘한테는 기회잖아요—그래도, 다시 한다면, 그 신사가 정말로 마음씨 고운 젊은이고 얘를 친척으로 귀히 여기는지 알아내기 전엔 보내지 않았을 거예요.”\n\n“그래, 당신, 아마 그랬어야 했겠지.” 존 경이 코를 골며 말했다.\n\n조앤 더비필드는 늘 어디선가 위안을 찾아내곤 했다. “음, 진짜 혈통의 한 사람이니, 제 으뜸패만 잘 쓴다면 그 사람을 휘어잡을 만하지. 그리고 그 사람이 전에 결혼 안 해 주면 후에라도 해 줄 거야. 그자가 얘한테 사랑으로 온통 불타오르는 건 누구 눈에나 보이니까.”\n\n“얘 으뜸패가 뭔데? 더버빌 핏줄 말이야?”\n\n“아니, 멍청한 양반아. 얘 얼굴—내 얼굴이 그랬듯이.”\n\n# VIII\n\n그녀 곁에 올라탄 알렉 더버빌은 첫 언덕의 마루를 따라 빠르게 마차를 몰았고, 가는 내내 테스에게 듣기 좋은 말을 늘어놓았으며, 그녀의 상자를 실은 짐마차는 저 멀리 뒤처져 갔다. 점점 높이 오르자, 사방으로 광대한 풍경이 그들 둘레에 펼쳐졌다. 뒤로는 그녀가 태어난 푸른 골짜기가, 앞으로는 트랜트리지로의 첫 짧은 방문 말고는 그녀가 아무것도 모르는 잿빛 고장이 있었다. 그렇게 그들은 어느 내리막의 가장자리에 이르렀으니, 거기서 길은 거의 일 마일에 걸쳐 길고 곧게 뻗어 내려가고 있었다.\n\n아버지의 말이 당한 그 사고 이래로, 테스 더비필드는 천성이 용감한데도 바퀴 위에서는 몹시 겁이 많아졌으니, 조금만 움직임이 고르지 못해도 흠칫했다. 그녀는 모는 이의 어딘가 무모한 운전에 마음이 불안해지기 시작했다.\n\n“천천히 내려가시겠죠, 선생님?” 그녀가 짐짓 태연한 척하며 말했다.\n\n더버빌은 그녀를 돌아보더니, 큼직한 흰 앞니 끝으로 시가를 깨물고는, 입술이 저절로 천천히 미소 짓게 두었다.\n\n“원, 테스.” 그가 한두 모금 더 빨고 나서 대답했다. “당신처럼 씩씩하고 발랄한 처녀가 그런 걸 묻는 게 아니지? 원, 나는 늘 전속력으로 달려 내려가오. 기분을 띄우는 데는 그만한 게 없거든.”\n\n“하지만 지금은 그럴 필요 없잖아요?”\n\n“아.” 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “따져야 할 게 둘이오. 나 혼자가 아니란 말이지. 팁도 생각해 줘야 하는데, 이 녀석이 성깔이 아주 별나거든.”\n\n“누구요?”\n\n“원, 이 암말 말이오. 방금 아주 험상궂은 눈으로 나를 돌아본 것 같은데. 못 봤소?”\n\n“저를 겁주려 하지 마세요, 선생님.” 테스가 딱딱하게 말했다.\n\n“원, 그러는 거 아니오. 산 사람 중에 이 말을 다룰 수 있는 자가 있다면 그게 바로 나요. 산 사람 누구나 할 수 있다고는 안 하겠소—하지만 그럴 힘을 가진 자가 있다면, 그게 나란 말이오.”\n\n“그런 말을 왜 데리고 계세요?”\n\n“아, 물을 만하지! 그게 내 팔자였나 보오. 팁은 한 녀석을 죽였소. 그리고 내가 그 녀석을 사자마자 나도 거의 죽일 뻔했고. 그러고 나서, 내 장담하건대, 내가 그 녀석을 거의 죽일 뻔했지. 하지만 아직도 성미가 까칠해, 아주 까칠하단 말이오. 그래서 가끔은 그 뒤에 앉아 있으면 목숨이 위태롭다오.”\n\n그들은 막 내려가기 시작하던 참이었고, 말은—제 뜻에서든 그의 뜻에서든(후자일 공산이 컸다)—기대되는 그 무모한 짓을 어찌나 잘 알고 있던지, 뒤에서 굳이 일러 줄 것도 거의 없어 보였다.\n\n아래로, 아래로 그들은 내달렸으니, 바퀴는 팽이처럼 윙윙거렸고, 이륜마차는 좌우로 흔들렸으며, 그 굴대는 나아가는 선에 비스듬히 살짝 비틀려 갔고, 말의 형체는 그들 앞에서 물결처럼 솟구쳤다 가라앉았다. 어떨 땐 바퀴 하나가 여러 야드씩 땅에서 떠 있는 듯했고, 어떨 땐 돌멩이 하나가 산울타리 너머로 튕겨 나갔으며, 말발굽에서 튀는 부싯돌 불꽃이 대낮 빛보다 더 빛났다. 곧게 뻗은 길의 모습이 그들이 나아갈수록 점점 커졌고, 양쪽 둑이 쪼개지는 막대기처럼 갈라지며 하나씩 양쪽 어깨를 스쳐 휙휙 지나갔다.\n\n바람이 테스의 흰 모슬린을 뚫고 살갗까지 스며들었고, 감은 머리카락이 뒤로 휘날렸다. 그녀는 드러내 놓고 두려워하지 않으리라 작정했으나, 더버빌의 고삐 쥔 팔을 움켜잡았다.\n\n“내 팔 건드리지 마요! 그러다간 우리 다 나가떨어진단 말이오! 내 허리를 잡아요!”\n\n그녀는 그의 허리를 붙들었고, 그렇게 그들은 바닥에 다다랐다.\n\n“하느님 덕에 무사했네요, 당신 그 장난질에도 불구하고!” 그녀가 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채 말했다.\n\n“테스—저런! 그게 성깔이군!” 더버빌이 말했다.\n\n“사실인걸요.”\n\n“음, 위험을 벗어났다 싶기가 무섭게 그렇게 고마운 줄도 모르고 손을 놓아 버릴 건 없잖소.”\n\n그녀는 자기가 무얼 하고 있었는지, 저도 모르게 그를 붙들면서 그가 남자였는지 여자였는지, 막대기였는지 돌이었는지 따위는 생각지도 못했다. 도로 차분함을 되찾은 그녀는 대꾸도 없이 앉아 있었고, 그렇게 그들은 또 다른 내리막의 마루에 이르렀다.\n\n“자, 또 한 번!” 더버빌이 말했다.\n\n“안 돼요, 안 돼!” 테스가 말했다. “제발 좀 더 분별 있게 굴어요.”\n\n“하지만 사람이 이 주(州)에서 가장 높은 지점에 올라섰으면, 다시 내려가야 하는 법이지.” 그가 받아쳤다.\n\n그는 고삐를 늦췄고, 그들은 두 번째로 내달렸다. 더버빌은 마차가 흔들리는 가운데 그녀에게 얼굴을 돌리고, 장난기 어린 놀림조로 말했다. “자, 어서, 아까처럼 다시 내 허리에 팔을 둘러요, 어여쁜 아가씨.”\n\n“절대로!” 테스가 꿋꿋이 말하며, 그를 건드리지 않고 잡을 수 있는 한 잘 붙들었다.\n\n“저 호랑가시 열매 같은 입술에, 아니면 저 달아오른 뺨에라도, 작은 입맞춤 하나만 하게 해 주면 멈추겠소, 테스—내 명예를 걸고, 그러리다!”\n\n테스는 한없이 놀라, 자리에서 더 뒤로 미끄러졌고, 그러자 그는 말을 새로 다그쳐 그녀를 더욱 흔들어 댔다.\n\n“다른 걸로는 안 되겠어요?” 그녀가 마침내 절망에 빠져, 야생 짐승의 그것 같은 커다란 눈으로 그를 빤히 노려보며 외쳤다. 어머니가 그녀를 그토록 예쁘게 꾸며 준 것이, 보아하니 한탄스러운 결말을 보자는 것이었던 셈이다.\n\n“안 되오, 사랑스러운 테스.” 그가 대답했다.\n\n“오, 모르겠어요—좋아요. 상관없어요!” 그녀가 비참하게 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n그가 고삐를 당겼고, 마차가 느려지는 사이 그가 막 그 바라던 입맞춤을 찍으려는 찰나, 그녀는 마치 제 정숙함을 아직 미처 자각하지 못했던 양 옆으로 휙 피했다. 그의 두 팔이 고삐로 차 있던 터라, 그녀의 그 술책을 막을 힘이 그에게는 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n“이런, 빌어먹을—우리 둘 다 목을 부러뜨려 버리겠어!” 변덕스럽게 격정적인 그녀의 동행이 욕설을 퍼부었다. “그래, 그렇게 말을 손바닥 뒤집듯 어길 수 있나, 이 어린 마녀 같으니!”\n\n“좋아요.” 테스가 말했다. “당신이 그렇게 작정하셨다니 꼼짝 안 하겠어요! 하지만 저는—사촌인 당신이 저한테 친절하게 대해 주고, 저를 지켜 주실 줄 알았어요!”\n\n“사촌은 무슨 얼어 죽을! 자, 어서!”\n\n“하지만 저는 누가 입 맞추는 거 싫어요, 선생님!” 그녀가 애원했으니, 큰 눈물 한 방울이 얼굴을 타고 굴러 내리기 시작했고, 울지 않으려 애쓰는 통에 입꼬리가 떨렸다. “이럴 줄 알았으면 오지도 않았을 거예요!”\n\n그는 가차 없었고, 그녀는 가만히 앉아 있었으며, 더버빌은 그녀에게 정복의 입맞춤을 했다. 그가 그러기가 무섭게 그녀는 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉히며 손수건을 꺼내, 그의 입술이 닿았던 뺨의 그 자리를 닦아 냈다. 그 모습에 그의 정열이 부아가 치밀었으니, 그녀의 그 행동이 저도 모르게 한 짓이었기 때문이다.\n\n“시골 처녀치고는 어지간히 예민하군!” 젊은이가 말했다.\n\n테스는 이 말에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으니, 실은 그 속뜻을 잘 헤아리지도 못했고, 본능적으로 뺨을 문질러 그에게 가한 면박도 개의치 않았다. 사실 그녀는, 그런 것이 물리적으로 가능한 한도껏, 그 입맞춤을 도로 무른 셈이었다. 그가 언짢아한다는 어렴풋한 느낌과 함께, 그녀는 그들이 멜버리 다운과 윈그린 근처를 종종걸음으로 지나는 동안 줄곧 앞만 똑바로 바라보았는데, 이윽고 또 한 번 내려가야 할 내리막이 있음을 알아채고는 기겁했다.\n\n“그것 때문에 후회하게 해 주지!” 그가, 다친 듯한 어조를 여전히 띤 채, 다시 채찍을 휘두르며 말을 이었다. “그게 싫거든, 다시 하게 기꺼이 허락하든지—손수건은 없이 말이오.”\n\n그녀는 한숨을 쉬었다. “좋아요, 선생님!” 그녀가 말했다. “오—제 모자 좀 줍게 해 주세요!”\n\n말하는 그 순간, 그녀의 모자가 길로 날아가 떨어졌으니, 고지에서의 그들의 속도가 결코 느리지 않았던 것이다. 더버빌이 마차를 세우고는 자기가 주워 주겠다고 했으나, 테스는 어느새 반대쪽으로 내려가 있었다.\n\n그녀는 돌아가 그 물건을 집어 들었다.\n\n“모자를 벗은 게 더 예쁘군, 정말이지, 그게 가능하다면 말이오.” 그가 마차 뒤쪽 너머로 그녀를 찬찬히 바라보며 말했다. “자, 어서, 다시 올라타요! 뭐 하는 거요?”\n\n모자는 제자리에 놓여 매여 있었으나, 테스는 앞으로 나아오지 않았다.\n\n“싫어요, 선생님.” 그녀가, 도전하는 의기양양함으로 눈을 빛내며, 입속의 붉음과 상앗빛을 드러내며 말했다. “내가 정신을 차린 이상, 다시는 안 타요!”\n\n“뭐라고—내 곁에 안 오르겠다고?”\n\n“네, 걸어가겠어요.”\n\n“트랜트리지까지는 아직 오륙 마일이오.”\n\n“수십 마일이라도 상관없어요. 게다가 짐마차도 뒤에 오고요.”\n\n“이 교활한 계집 같으니! 자, 말해 봐—일부러 그 모자를 날려 버린 거 아니오? 내 장담하건대 그랬을 거요!”\n\n그녀의 전략적인 침묵이 그의 의심을 굳혔다.\n\n그러자 더버빌은 그 장난질을 두고 그녀에게 욕설과 악담을 퍼부으며, 떠오르는 온갖 말로 그녀를 불러 댔다. 그는 별안간 말을 돌려 그녀에게 도로 마차를 몰아붙여, 이륜마차와 산울타리 사이에 그녀를 가두려 했다. 그러나 그녀를 다치게 하지 않고서는 그럴 수 없었다.\n\n“그렇게 못된 말을 쓰다니 부끄러운 줄 아셔야죠!”\n테스가 기어 올라간 산울타리 꼭대기에서 기세 좋게 소리쳤다. \"당신 같은 건 조금도 좋아하지 않아! 밉고 끔찍해! 어머니한테 돌아갈 거야, 정말로!\"\n\n테스의 화난 얼굴을 보자 더버빌의 언짢음이 가셨다. 그는 호탕하게 웃었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래서 더 좋아.\" 그가 말했다. \"자, 화 풀어. 다시는 네 뜻을 거스르는 짓 안 할게. 내 목숨을 걸지!\"\n\n그래도 테스는 다시 마차에 타라고 구슬려질 기색이 아니었다. 다만 그가 마차를 자기 옆에 바짝 붙여 모는 것까지 마다하지는 않았다. 이런 식으로, 느린 걸음으로, 둘은 트랜트리지 마을을 향해 나아갔다. 이따금 더버빌은 자기 잘못 때문에 그녀를 걷게 만든 광경에 일종의 격한 괴로움을 드러냈다. 사실 이제는 그를 안심하고 믿어도 좋았을 것이다. 하지만 그는 당분간 그녀의 신뢰를 잃은 처지였고, 그녀는 집으로 돌아가는 편이 더 현명하지 않을까 생각하듯 골똘히 땅을 디디며 앞으로 나아갔다. 그러나 결심은 이미 굳혀진 터였고, 지금 와서 더 무거운 이유도 없이 그것을 저버리는 일은 어린애처럼 흔들리는 짓으로 여겨졌다. 어떻게 부모를 마주하고, 짐 상자를 도로 찾아오고, 그런 감상적인 이유로 집안을 다시 일으키려는 계획 전체를 뒤엎는단 말인가?\n\n몇 분 뒤 더 슬로프스의 굴뚝이 시야에 들어왔고, 오른편 아늑한 구석에 테스가 갈 목적지인 양계장과 오두막이 보였다.\n\n# IX\n\n테스가 감독자이자, 사료 공급자, 간호사, 외과의, 친구 노릇을 맡게 된 닭 무리는 한때 정원이었으나 지금은 짓밟혀 모래만 깔린 네모난 터에 선 낡은 초가 오두막을 본거지로 삼고 있었다. 집은 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮였고, 굴뚝은 이 기생식물의 가지들로 커져 폐허가 된 탑처럼 보였다. 아래층 방들은 온전히 새들 차지였다. 새들은 마치 이 집을 자기네가 지은 양, 지금은 교회 묘지에 동서로 누운 어느 먼지투성이 등본 보유자들이 지은 게 아닌 양, 주인 행세를 하며 그 안을 거닐었다. 이 지난날 주인들의 후손들은, 자기네가 그토록 아꼈고 조상의 돈이 그토록 많이 들었으며 더버빌 일가가 와서 여기 집을 짓기 전 여러 세대에 걸쳐 자기네 소유였던 그 집을, 스토크더버빌 부인이 법에 따라 재산을 손에 넣자마자 무심히 닭장으로 바꿔 버린 일을 거의 집안에 대한 모욕처럼 느꼈다. \"할아버지 시절엔 기독교인이 살기에도 충분히 좋은 집이었는데.\" 그들은 그렇게 말했다.\n\n수십 명의 갓난아이가 젖을 빨며 울어 대던 방들이 이제는 갓 깨어난 병아리들이 톡톡 쪼는 소리로 울렸다. 닭장 속 정신 사나운 암탉들이, 예전에 점잖은 농부들을 받치고 있던 의자가 놓였던 자리를 차지했다. 굴뚝 모퉁이, 한때 활활 타오르던 화덕에는 이제 거꾸로 엎은 벌통들이 가득 차 그 안에 암탉들이 알을 낳았다. 한편 바깥에서는 대대로 살림을 맡았던 사람들이 삽으로 정성껏 다듬어 놓은 텃밭이 수탉들에게 더없이 거칠게 헤집어졌다.\n\n오두막이 선 정원은 담으로 둘러싸여 있었고, 오직 문 하나로만 드나들 수 있었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 테스가 한 시간쯤, 어엿한 가금업자의 딸로서 익힌 솜씨에 따라 시설을 고치고 손보는 데 매달려 있을 때, 담의 문이 열리고 흰 모자에 앞치마를 두른 하녀 하나가 들어왔다. 저택에서 온 하녀였다.\n\n\"더버빌 마님이 늘 하던 대로 닭들을 보고 싶어 하세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러나 테스가 잘 이해하지 못하는 걸 알아채고 이렇게 설명했다. \"마님은 연세 드신 데다, 눈이 안 보이세요.\"\n\n\"눈이 안 보인다고요!\" 테스가 말했다.\n그 소식에 일어난 불안이 채 자리를 잡을 새도 없이, 그녀는 동행의 지시에 따라 함부르크종 가운데 가장 아름다운 두 마리를 품에 안고서, 마찬가지로 두 마리를 안은 하녀를 따라 이웃한 저택으로 갔다. 그 저택은 화려하고 위풍당당했으나, 이쪽 면에는 어딜 봐도 그 방들에 사는 누군가가 말 못 하는 짐승들을 향한 애정에 마음을 굽힐 줄 안다는 흔적이 있었다. 정면이 보이는 자리에 깃털이 날렸고, 잔디 위에는 닭장들이 놓여 있었다.\n\n일 층 거실에서, 빛을 등진 채 안락의자에 파묻혀 있는 이가 이 영지의 주인이자 안주인이었다. 예순 살, 어쩌면 그보다 덜한 백발의 부인이 큼직한 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 오래 눈이 멀었거나 태어날 때부터 못 보던 사람들에게서 보이는 그 멈춰 버린 표정이 아니라, 시력을 단계적으로 잃어 가며 애써 붙들려 했으나 마지못해 놓아 버린 이들에게 흔한 살아 움직이는 표정이 있었다. 테스는 한쪽 팔에 한 마리씩, 깃털 달린 짐을 안고 이 부인에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"아, 네가 내 새들을 돌보러 온 처녀로구나?\" 더버빌 부인이 낯선 발소리를 알아채고 말했다. \"이 아이들한테 잘해 주길 바란다. 내 집사가 그러는데 네가 아주 적임자라더구나. 그래, 어디 있느냐? 아, 이놈은 스트럿이로구나! 그런데 오늘은 영 기운이 없네, 그렇지? 낯선 손길에 안긴 게 겁이 난 모양이다. 페나도 그렇고—그래, 좀 무서워하는구나—그렇지, 얘들아? 하지만 곧 너한테 익숙해질 게다.\"\n\n부인이 말하는 동안 테스와 다른 하녀는 그녀의 손짓에 따라 닭을 한 마리씩 그녀 무릎에 놓았고, 그녀는 머리에서 꼬리까지 닭을 더듬으며 부리와 볏, 수탉의 목깃, 날개, 발톱을 살폈다. 손의 감촉만으로 그녀는 단번에 닭을 알아보았고, 깃털 한 가닥이라도 상하거나 후줄근하면 곧 찾아냈다. 그녀는 닭의 모이주머니를 만져 보고 무엇을 먹었는지, 너무 적게 혹은 너무 많이 먹지 않았는지 알아냈다. 그녀의 얼굴은 마음속을 스쳐 가는 평가들을 생생한 무언극으로 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n두 처녀가 들여온 새들은 차례로 마당에 돌려보내졌고, 이 과정은 아끼는 수탉과 암탉이 모두 노부인 앞에 바쳐질 때까지 되풀이되었다—함부르크종, 밴텀종, 코친종, 브라마종, 도킹종, 그리고 그 무렵 유행하던 그 밖의 온갖 종이었다—그녀가 새를 무릎에 받아 들며 손님 하나하나를 알아보는 데 틀리는 일은 거의 없었다.\n\n그것은 테스에게 견진성사를 떠올리게 했다. 더버빌 부인이 주교요, 닭들이 인도되어 온 젊은이들이며, 자기와 하녀가 그들을 데려온 본당 신부와 보좌신부였다. 의식이 끝날 무렵 더버빌 부인이 얼굴을 주름지게 일그러뜨려 잔물결을 일으키며 불쑥 테스에게 물었다. \"휘파람 불 줄 아느냐?\"\n\n\"휘파람이요, 마님?\"\n\n\"그래, 곡조를 부는 휘파람 말이다.\"\n테스는 여느 시골 처녀들처럼 휘파람을 불 줄 알았으나, 점잖은 자리에서 내세우고 싶은 재주는 아니었다. 그래도 그녀는 그게 사실이라고 무던히 인정했다.\n\n\"그럼 매일 연습해야 한다. 그걸 아주 잘 부는 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 그만뒀거든. 내 멋쟁이방울새들한테 휘파람을 불어 주었으면 한다. 눈으로 못 보니 듣기라도 하고 싶고, 그렇게 해서 새들한테 곡조를 가르치거든. 새장이 어디 있는지 일러 주거라, 엘리자베스. 내일부터 시작해야 한다. 안 그러면 새들 노래 솜씨가 도로 나빠진다. 며칠째 돌보지 않았으니.\"\n\n\"더버빌 도련님이 오늘 아침에 새들한테 휘파람을 불어 주셨어요, 마님.\" 엘리자베스가 말했다.\n\n\"그 녀석이! 흥!\"\n노부인의 얼굴이 혐오의 골을 깊이 파며 일그러졌고, 그녀는 더는 대꾸하지 않았다.\n\n이렇게 해서 친척이라 짐작되던 부인에게 테스가 맞아들여진 일이 끝났고, 새들은 우리로 돌려보내졌다. 더버빌 부인의 태도에 대한 처녀의 놀라움은 크지 않았다. 집의 규모를 본 뒤로 그 이상은 기대하지도 않았으니까. 그러나 노부인이 이른바 친척 관계라는 말은 한마디도 들은 적이 없다는 사실은 그녀로서는 알 길이 없었다. 그녀는 눈먼 부인과 그 아들 사이에 큰 애정이 흐르지 않는다고 짐작했다. 하지만 그 점에서도 그녀는 틀렸다. 더버빌 부인은 제 자식을 원망하면서 사랑하고, 쓰라리게 아낄 수밖에 없는 첫 어머니가 아니었다.\n\n전날의 언짢은 첫걸음에도 불구하고, 일단 자리를 잡고 보니 햇살이 비치는 아침이면 테스는 새 자리의 자유로움과 새로움에 마음이 기울었다. 그리고 뜻밖에 청받은 그 방면에서 자기 능력을 시험해, 자리를 지킬 수 있을지 가늠해 보고 싶은 호기심이 일었다. 담으로 둘러싸인 정원에 혼자 있게 되자마자 그녀는 닭장 위에 앉아 오랫동안 묵힌 연습을 위해 진지하게 입을 오므렸다. 예전의 솜씨가 입술 사이로 텅 빈 바람 소리만 새 나갈 뿐 또렷한 음 하나 못 내는 지경으로 퇴화했음을 알았다.\n\n타고난 재주가 어쩌다 이렇게까지 녹슬었나 의아해하며 그녀는 헛되이 불고 또 불었다. 그러다 오두막 못지않게 정원 담을 뒤덮은 담쟁이 가지들 사이에서 무언가 움직이는 기척을 알아챘다. 그쪽을 보니 담 꼭대기에서 텃밭으로 뛰어내리는 형체가 보였다. 알렉 더버빌이었다. 전날 그녀가 묵을 정원사 오두막 문까지 데려다준 뒤로 마주친 적 없는 그였다.\n\n\"맹세코!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"자연에도 예술에도 지금 네 모습처럼 아름다운 건 없었어, '사촌' 테스('사촌'이라는 말에는 희미한 조롱의 울림이 있었다). 담 너머로 너를 지켜봤지—기념비 위의 '인내'처럼 앉아서, 그 예쁜 붉은 입을 휘파람 모양으로 삐죽 내밀고, 후우 후우 불어 대고, 혼자 속으로 욕설을 내뱉으면서도, 음 하나 못 내고 있더군. 아니, 못 한다고 잔뜩 골이 났구나.\"\n\n\"골이야 났을지 몰라도, 욕설은 안 했어요.\"\n\n\"아! 네가 왜 애쓰는지 알겠다—그 멧새 녀석들! 어머니가 너더러 그 녀석들 음악 교육을 맡으라는 거지. 정말 이기적이지 뭐야! 여기 이 빌어먹을 닭들 돌보는 것만으로도 처녀 하나한테 충분한 일거리가 아니라는 듯이 말이야. 나라면 단칼에 거절하겠어.\"\n\n\"하지만 마님이 특별히 저더러 하라고 하셨고, 내일 아침까지 준비하라고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"그래? 그럼—내가 한두 번 가르쳐 주지.\"\n\n\"오 안 돼요, 그러지 마세요!\" 테스가 문 쪽으로 물러서며 말했다.\n\n\"별소릴 다 하네. 너를 건드리려는 게 아니야. 봐—난 이 철망 이쪽에 서 있고, 넌 저쪽에 있어. 그러면 아주 안심해도 돼. 자, 봐. 넌 입술을 너무 빡빡하게 오므려. 이렇게—봐.\"\n\n그는 말에 맞춰 행동하며 \"가져가오, 오 그 입술을 가져가오\"의 한 소절을 휘파람으로 불었다. 그러나 그 암시는 테스에게 전해지지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 해 봐.\" 더버빌이 말했다.\n\n그녀는 새침한 표정을 지으려 애썼다. 얼굴이 조각상처럼 굳었다. 그러나 그가 끈질기게 졸라 댔고, 마침내 그를 떼어 내려고 그녀는 시킨 대로 또렷한 음을 내려고 입술을 내밀었다. 그러면서도 괴롭게 웃었고, 이내 웃어 버린 게 분해 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n그는 \"다시 해 봐!\" 하며 그녀를 부추겼다.\n\n이제 테스는 자못 진지했다. 고통스러우리만치 진지했다. 그녀는 시도했고—마침내 뜻밖에도 진짜 둥근 음 하나를 내놓았다. 성공의 순간적 기쁨이 그녀를 사로잡았다. 눈이 커졌고, 그녀는 저도 모르게 그의 얼굴을 보며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"바로 그거야! 이제 내가 너를 출발시켰으니—넌 멋지게 해 나갈 거야. 자—내가 네 곁에 안 가겠다고 했지. 그리고 인간으로 태어나 일찍이 없던 그런 유혹에도 불구하고, 난 약속을 지키마…… 테스, 내 어머니를 별난 늙은이라고 생각하니?\"\n\n\"아직 마님을 잘 모르는걸요, 도련님.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하게 될 거야. 그럴 수밖에. 너더러 자기 멧새들한테 휘파람을 배우라고 시키는 분이니까. 난 지금 어머니한테 좀 미운털이 박혔지만, 넌 어머니 가축들만 잘 돌보면 단단히 총애받을 거야. 잘 있어. 여기서 무슨 어려움이 생기거나 도움이 필요하면, 집사한테 가지 말고, 나한테 와.\"\n\n테스 더버빌이 자리 하나를 채우겠다고 맡고 들어선 곳은 바로 이런 식으로 굴러가는 살림이었다. 그녀의 첫날 경험은 그 뒤 여러 날을 두고 이어진 나날들의 본보기라 할 만했다. 알렉 더버빌의 존재에 대한 익숙함—그 젊은이가 장난스러운 대화로, 그리고 둘만 있을 때면 농 삼아 그녀를 사촌이라 부르며 그녀 안에 조심스레 길러 낸 그것—은 그에 대한 처음의 수줍음을 많이 걷어 냈다. 그렇다고 새롭고 더 부드러운 종류의 수줍음을 자아낼 만한 감정을 심어 준 것은 아니었지만. 다만 그녀는 단순한 동무 사이였다면 그렇지 않았을 만큼 그의 손아귀에서 고분고분했다. 그의 어머니에게 어쩔 수 없이 의지해야 했고, 그 부인이 비교적 무력한 탓에 결국 그에게도 기대야 했기 때문이다.\n\n머지않아 그녀는, 일단 솜씨를 되찾고 나니 더버빌 부인의 방에서 멧새들에게 휘파람을 불어 주는 일이 그리 고된 일이 아님을 알게 되었다. 음악을 좋아하던 어머니에게서 그 가락새들에게 더없이 잘 어울리는 곡조를 여럿 익혀 둔 덕이었다. 매일 아침 새장 옆에서 부는 이 휘파람은 정원에서 연습할 때보다 훨씬 흡족한 시간이었다. 젊은이의 존재에 거리낄 일이 없으니 그녀는 입을 한껏 벌려, 입술을 창살에 가까이 대고, 귀 기울여 듣는 청중을 향해 느긋한 우아함으로 곡조를 불어 댔다.\n\n더버빌 부인은 묵직한 다마스크 휘장을 드리운 큼직한 사주식 침대에서 잤고, 멧새들도 같은 방을 차지해 정해진 시간이면 그 안을 자유로이 날아다니며 가구와 천갈이에 작은 흰 얼룩을 남겼다. 한번은 테스가 새장들이 늘어선 창가에서 여느 때처럼 가르치고 있는데, 침대 뒤에서 무언가 바스락거리는 소리가 들리는 것 같았다. 노부인은 그 자리에 없었고, 돌아본 처녀는 휘장 술 아래로 한 켤레 장화의 발끝이 보이는 것 같다는 인상을 받았다. 그러자 그녀의 휘파람이 어찌나 끊겼던지, 만약 듣는 자가 있었다면 자기 존재를 그녀가 의심한다는 걸 알아챘을 것이 분명했다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 아침마다 휘장을 살폈으나, 그 안에서 누구도 찾아내지 못했다. 알렉 더버빌은 그런 식의 매복으로 그녀를 겁주려던 변덕을 다시 생각해 그만둔 것이 분명했다.\n\n# X\n\n마을마다 저마다의 특이한 성질이, 저마다의 기질이, 흔히 저마다의 도덕률이 있다. 트랜트리지 안팎의 젊은 여자들 가운데 더러는 경박함이 두드러졌는데, 어쩌면 그 부근의 더 슬로프스를 다스리는 빼어난 인물을 보여 주는 징후였는지도 모른다. 그곳에는 더 뿌리 깊은 결함도 하나 있었다. 술이 셌다. 둘레의 농장들에서 흔한 대화는 돈을 모아 봐야 소용없다는 이야기였다. 작업복 차림의 산술가들이 쟁기나 괭이에 기대어, 평생 품삯에서 떼어 모은 저축이 어떤 식으로 불어나더라도 교구 구호금이 노년의 사내에게 더 넉넉한 살림 밑천이 됨을 증명하려고 자못 정교한 계산에 들어가곤 했다.\n\n이 철학자들의 으뜸가는 낙은 토요일 밤마다, 일이 끝나면, 두세 마일 떨어진 쇠락한 장터 마을 체이스버러로 가는 것이었다. 그리고 이튿날 새벽 시간에 돌아와, 한때 독립적이던 여관들을 독차지한 자들이 맥주랍시고 팔아먹는 야릇한 혼합물의 소화불량 후유증을 잠으로 떨쳐 내며 일요일을 보내는 것이었다.\n\n오랫동안 테스는 이 주간 순례에 끼지 않았다. 그러나 자기와 그리 나이 차이도 안 나는 아낙들의 성화에—들일꾼의 품삯은 스물한 살이나 마흔이나 똑같이 높은지라 이곳에서는 결혼이 일렀다—테스도 마침내 가기로 했다. 그 나들이의 첫 경험은 기대보다 많은 즐거움을 안겨 주었다. 일주일 내내 양계장에만 단조롭게 매달리던 끝이라 다른 이들의 흥겨움이 자못 전염성이 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 가고 또 갔다. 우아하고 호감 가는 데다, 게다가 여자가 되기 직전 그 한순간의 문턱에 선 까닭에, 그녀의 모습은 체이스버러 거리의 빈둥거리는 자들에게서 슬그머니 곁눈질을 끌었다. 그래서 때로는 장에 혼자 갔어도, 그녀는 늘 해 질 녘이면 동무들을 찾아, 집으로 돌아가는 길에 함께라는 보호를 받으려 했다.\n\n이런 일이 한두 달 이어지던 어느 구월 토요일, 장날과 시장이 겹쳤다. 그래서 트랜트리지에서 온 순례자들은 그날 여관에서 갑절의 즐거움을 찾았다. 테스는 일 때문에 늦게 출발해, 동무들이 그녀보다 훨씬 먼저 마을에 닿았다. 해 지기 직전의 화창한 구월 저녁이었다. 노란 빛과 푸른 그늘이 머리카락처럼 가는 선을 이루며 다투고, 그 안에서 춤추는 무수한 날벌레 말고는 더 단단한 사물의 도움 없이도 대기 그 자체가 하나의 풍경을 이루는 그런 때였다. 이 어슴푸레한 안개 속을 테스는 느긋이 걸어갔다.\n\n그녀는 마을에 닿고서야 장날과 시장이 겹쳤음을 알았는데, 그때는 이미 어스름이 짙어 가던 무렵이었다. 변변찮은 장보기는 곧 끝났다. 그러고는 여느 때처럼 트랜트리지 사람 몇몇을 찾아 둘러보기 시작했다.\n\n처음에는 그들을 찾지 못했는데, 대부분 자기네 농장과 거래하는 건초 다발 만드는 사람 겸 토탄 장수의 집에서 벌인다는, 이른바 끼리끼리의 작은 춤판에 갔다는 말을 들었다. 그는 읍내 외진 구석에 살았고, 그리로 가는 길을 찾으려다 그녀의 눈에 길모퉁이에 서 있는 더버빌 씨가 들어왔다.\n\n\"이런—나의 미녀? 이렇게 늦게 여기 있다니?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 그저 함께 돌아갈 일행을 기다리는 중이라고 말했다.\n\n\"또 보자고.\" 그녀가 뒷골목으로 걸어 내려가는 동안 그가 어깨 너머로 말했다.\n\n건초 다발 만드는 집에 다가서니 뒤편 어느 건물에서 흘러나오는 릴 가락의 바이올린 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 춤추는 소리는 들리지 않았다—발 구르는 소리가 으레 음악을 삼켜 버리는 이 고장에서는 보기 드문 일이었다. 앞문이 열려 있어 밤 그림자가 허락하는 데까지 집을 곧장 가로질러 뒤편 정원까지 들여다보였다. 두드려도 아무도 나오지 않자, 그녀는 집을 가로질러 소리가 그녀를 끌어당긴 헛간으로 가는 길을 올라갔다.\n\n그것은 저장용으로 쓰는 창문 없는 건물이었고, 열린 문에서 노란빛의 안개가 어둠 속으로 떠 나왔다. 처음에 테스는 빛을 받은 연기인 줄 알았다. 그러나 가까이 다가가 보니 그것은 헛간 안의 촛불에 비친 먼지구름이었다. 그 빛줄기가 자욱한 안개 위로 문간의 윤곽을 정원의 너른 밤 속까지 밀어 내고 있었다.\n\n바짝 다가가 안을 들여다보니, 춤사위에 맞춰 위아래로 내달리는 어렴풋한 형체들이 보였는데, 발소리가 잠잠한 것은 그들이 \"스크로프\"에 발목까지 빠져 있는 탓이었다. 곧 토탄을 비롯한 산물들을 저장하면서 생긴 가루 찌꺼기였는데, 어지러이 구르는 그들의 발이 이것을 일으켜 그 광경을 감싼 안개를 만들어 냈다. 토탄과 건초의 떠다니는 이 곰팡내 나는 부스러기에, 춤꾼들의 땀과 열기가 뒤섞여 일종의 식물 인간 화분을 이루었고, 그 사이로 음 죽인 바이올린들이 가락을 가냘프게 밀어냈다. 박자를 밟아 대는 그 기세와는 사뭇 대조를 이루었다. 그들은 춤추다 기침했고, 기침하다 웃었다. 내달리는 짝들에게서는 도드라진 빛점 말고는 거의 분간이 안 됐다—그 흐릿함이 그들을 요정을 끌어안은 사티로스로, 무수한 시링크스를 빙빙 돌리는 무수한 판으로 빚어냈다. 프리아포스를 피하려 애쓰다 번번이 실패하는 로티스로.\n\n이따금 한 쌍이 바람을 쐬러 문간으로 다가오면, 더는 그들의 이목구비를 가리지 못한 안개 탓에 반신반인들이 그녀의 바로 이웃들이라는 소박한 인격으로 풀려났다. 트랜트리지가 두세 시간 안에 이토록 미쳐 날뛰게 탈바꿈할 수 있었더란 말인가!\n\n무리 가운데 실레노스 같은 자들 몇몇이 벽가의 긴 의자와 건초 다발에 앉아 있었고, 그중 하나가 그녀를 알아보았다.\n\n\"처녀들은 플라워 드 루스에서 춤추는 걸 점잖지 못하다고들 여기지.\" 그가 설명했다. \"누가 제 정인情人인지 다들 보게 하기 싫은 거야. 게다가 그 집은 한창 흥이 올라 관절에 기름칠이 도는 판에 문을 닫아 버리기도 하거든. 그래서 우리는 여기 와서 술을 시켜 오는 거지.\"\n\n\"그런데 그쪽들 중에 집에 가는 사람은 언제 가나요?\" 테스가 다소 불안해하며 물었다.\n\n\"지금—거의 곧장. 이게 거의 마지막 춤이야.\"\n\n그녀는 기다렸다. 릴이 끝나 가자 일행 중 더러는 떠날 마음을 먹었다. 그러나 다른 이들은 가려 하지 않았고, 또 한 차례 춤판이 짜였다. 이것으로 분명 끝나겠지, 테스는 생각했다. 그러나 그것이 또 다른 춤으로 이어졌다. 그녀는 안절부절못하고 불안해졌다. 그러나 이토록 오래 기다린 마당이라 더 기다릴 수밖에 없었다. 장날인지라 길마다 어쩌면 흉계를 품었을 떠돌이들이 흩어져 있었다. 가늠할 수 있는 위험은 두렵지 않았지만, 그녀는 알 수 없는 것이 두려웠다. 말럿 가까이였다면 두려움이 덜했을 것이다.\n\n\"긴장 풀어, 마음씨 고운 아가씨.\" 얼굴이 땀에 젖고 밀짚모자를 어찌나 뒤로 젖혀 썼는지 챙이 성인의 후광처럼 머리를 두른 한 젊은이가 기침 사이사이 타일렀다. \"뭐가 그리 급해? 내일은 일요일이잖아, 고맙게도, 예배 시간 동안 자면서 술기운 풀면 되지. 자, 나랑 한 바퀴 출까?\"\n\n그녀는 춤을 싫어하지는 않았으나, 여기서 출 생각은 없었다. 춤사위가 더 격해졌다. 빛나는 구름 기둥 뒤의 바이올린 잡이들이 이따금 줄받침 엉뚱한 쪽을 켜거나 활 등으로 켜서 가락을 바꿔 놓았다. 그래도 상관없었다. 헐떡이는 형체들은 계속 빙빙 돌았다.\n\n이전 짝을 고수하고픈 마음이면 그들은 짝을 바꾸지 않았다. 짝을 바꾼다는 건 둘 중 누군가가 아직 흡족한 선택에 이르지 못했다는 뜻일 뿐이었고, 이쯤 되니 짝마다 알맞게 맞춰진 상태였다. 그제야 황홀경과 꿈이 시작되었다. 그 안에서는 감정이 우주의 본질이었고, 물질이란 네가 돌고 싶은 자리에서 도는 걸 방해할 법한 군더더기 침입에 지나지 않았다.\n\n별안간 바닥에서 둔탁하게 쿵 하는 소리가 났다. 한 쌍이 쓰러져 한 무더기로 뒤엉켜 누웠다. 다음 짝은 멈추지 못하고 그 장애물에 걸려 고꾸라졌다. 엎어진 형체들 둘레로, 방 안에 자욱한 먼지 속에서 또 안쪽의 먼지구름이 일었고, 그 속에 버둥대며 뒤엉킨 팔다리가 어렴풋이 분간되었다.\n\n\"집에 가면 이걸로 혼날 줄 알아, 이 양반아!\" 그 인간 무더기에서 여자 목소리가 터져 나왔다—제 짝의 서투름이 사달을 낸 그 불운한 여자의 목소리였다. 그녀는 마침 그의 갓 혼인한 아내이기도 했는데, 트랜트리지에서는 부부 사이에 얼마간 애정이 남아 있는 한 그런 짝짓기에 이상할 게 없었다. 게다가 그들의 후일에도, 따뜻한 정이 통할 법한 독신들 사이를 어긋난 짝으로 만들지 않으려고 그러는 일이 드물지 않았다.\n\n테스 등 뒤, 정원 그늘에서 큰 웃음소리가 터져 방 안의 키득거림과 어우러졌다. 그녀는 돌아보았고, 시가의 빨간 불씨를 보았다. 알렉 더버빌이 홀로 거기 서 있었다. 그가 그녀에게 손짓했고, 그녀는 마지못해 그쪽으로 물러났다.\n\n\"그래, 나의 미녀, 여기서 뭘 하고 있지?\"\n\n긴 하루와 걷기에 어찌나 지쳤던지 그녀는 그에게 고민을 털어놓았다—그가 그녀를 본 뒤로 줄곧, 함께 돌아갈 일행을 기다려 왔다고, 밤길이 낯선 까닭이라고. \"그런데 저 사람들은 도무지 그만둘 줄을 모르네요. 정말이지 더는 못 기다리겠어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 기다리지 마. 오늘은 안장 얹은 말 한 마리밖에 안 끌고 왔지만, 플라워 드 루스로 와. 마차 한 대 빌려서 내가 집에 데려다줄게.\"\n\n테스는 우쭐하긴 했어도 그에 대한 처음의 불신을 끝내 다 떨치지 못한 데다, 더디긴 해도 일꾼들과 함께 걸어가는 편이 나았다. 그래서 그녀는 무척 고맙지만 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않다고 답했다. \"저 사람들을 기다리겠다고 했으니, 이제 저를 기다릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 독립심 강한 아가씨. 마음대로 해…… 그럼 나도 서두르지 않겠어…… 어이쿠, 저기서 한바탕 야단법석이로구나!\"\n\n그는 빛 속으로 나서지 않았으나, 그들 중 몇몇이 그를 알아보았고, 그의 존재 탓에 잠시 멈칫하며 시간이 얼마나 흘러가는지 헤아리게 되었다. 그가 시가에 다시 불을 붙이고 걸어가 버리자마자, 트랜트리지 사람들은 다른 농장에서 온 이들 사이에서 자기들끼리 모이기 시작했고, 무리 지어 떠날 채비를 했다. 보따리와 바구니를 챙겨 들었고, 삼십 분 뒤 종소리가 열한 시 십오 분을 알릴 무렵, 그들은 집을 향해 언덕을 오르는 오솔길을 따라 흩어진 채 걸어가고 있었다.\n\n마른 흰 길을 따라 삼 마일 걷는 길이었는데, 오늘 밤은 달빛에 한층 더 희게 보였다.\n\n무리에 섞여, 이 사람과 저 사람을 오가며 걷던 테스는 곧 알아챘다. 서늘한 밤공기가 술을 너무 많이 마신 사내들 사이에 비틀거림과 뱀처럼 구불대는 갈지자 걸음을 만들어 내고 있음을. 더 무모한 여자들 몇몇도 걸음이 갈팡질팡했다—말하자면 검은 표독 여인, 스페이드 여왕이라 불리던 카 다치, 얼마 전까지 더버빌의 총애를 받던 여자였다. 그 동생 낸시는 다이아몬드 여왕이라는 별명이 붙었고, 아까 이미 나동그라졌던 갓 혼인한 여자도 있었다. 그러나 미혹의 빛이 벗겨진 천한 눈에 지금 그들이 아무리 땅에 매인 덩어리처럼 보일지언정, 그들 자신에게는 사정이 달랐다. 그들은 자기들을 떠받치는 어떤 매질媒質 속에서 떠가는 듯한 감각으로 길을 따랐고, 독창적이고 심오한 사상으로 가득 차, 자기 자신과 둘레의 자연이 모든 부분이 조화롭고 기쁘게 서로 스며드는 하나의 유기체를 이룬다고 느꼈다. 그들은 머리 위의 달과 별만큼 숭고했고, 달과 별은 그들만큼 열렬했다.\n\n그러나 테스는 아버지의 집에서 이런 종류의 고통스러운 경험을 숱하게 겪은 터라, 그들의 상태를 알아챈 일이 달빛 여정에서 막 느끼기 시작한 즐거움을 잡쳐 놓았다. 그래도 앞서 말한 이유로 그녀는 무리에 붙어 있었다.\n\n탁 트인 큰길에서는 흩어진 채 걸었으나, 이제 길이 밭 출입문을 지나게 되었고, 맨 앞에 선 이가 문을 여는 데 애를 먹자 다들 한데 모여들었다.\n\n이 선두에 선 보행자가 스페이드 여왕 카였는데, 그녀는 어머니의 식료품과 제 옷가지, 그리고 한 주 동안 사들인 다른 물건들이 든 고리버들 바구니를 이고 있었다. 바구니가 크고 무거워, 카는 나르기 편하라고 그것을 머리 꼭대기에 얹었는데, 그녀가 양손을 허리에 짚고 걷는 동안 바구니는 위태로운 균형으로 흔들거리며 실려 갔다.\n\n\"아니—카 다치, 네 등을 타고 내려오는 저게 도대체 뭐야?\" 무리 중 하나가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n다들 카를 보았다. 그녀의 옷은 얇은 무명 날염천이었는데, 뒤통수에서부터 일종의 밧줄 같은 것이 허리 한참 아래까지 내려오는 게 보였다, 마치 중국인의 변발처럼.\n\n\"머리카락이 흘러내린 거야.\" 또 한 사람이 말했다.\n\n아니다. 머리카락이 아니었다. 그것은 그녀의 바구니에서 새 나오는 검은 줄기였고, 차고 고요한 달빛 속에서 끈적한 뱀처럼 번들거렸다.\n\n\"당밀이야.\" 눈썰미 좋은 아낙이 말했다.\n\n당밀이었다. 카의 가엾은 노할머니는 단것을 무척 좋아했다. 제 벌통에서 꿀은 넘치게 났지만, 영혼이 갈망하는 건 당밀이었고, 카는 깜짝 선물로 그걸 대접하려던 참이었다. 검은 처녀가 황급히 바구니를 내려 보니, 시럽이 든 그릇이 안에서 박살나 있었다.\n\n이쯤 되자 카의 등의 괴이한 모양새에 폭소가 터졌고, 그것에 부아가 치민 검은 여왕은, 비웃는 자들의 도움 따위는 마다하고, 손에 잡히는 첫 수단으로 그 흉한 꼴을 없애려 했다. 그녀는 흥분해 일행이 막 건너려던 밭으로 뛰어들더니, 풀밭 위에 벌렁 자빠져, 풀밭 위에서 수평으로 빙글빙글 돌고 팔꿈치로 몸을 끌며 옷을 닦을 수 있는 대로 닦기 시작했다.\n\n웃음소리가 더 크게 울렸다. 그들은 카의 광경에 발작하듯 웃다가 기운이 빠져, 문에, 기둥에 매달리고 지팡이에 몸을 기댔다. 그때까지 잠자코 있던 우리의 여주인공도, 이 거친 순간에는 다른 이들과 어울려 웃지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그것은 불운이었다—여러모로. 검은 여왕은 다른 일꾼들의 목소리 가운데 섞인 테스의 더 차분하고 풍성한 음색을 듣자마자, 오래 묵어 있던 경쟁심에 불이 붙어 미칠 지경이 되었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 제가 싫어하는 대상을 정면으로 마주했다.\n\n\"어디서 감히 날 비웃어, 이 계집애야!\" 그녀가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"다들 웃으니까 정말 어쩔 수가 없었어요.\" 테스가 여전히 키득거리며 사과했다.\n\n\"흥, 네가 무슨 대단한 줄 알지, 안 그래, 지금 그 양반 첫째 총애 받는 몸이라고! 하지만 어디 두고 봐, 아가씨, 두고 봐! 난 너 같은 거 둘 몫은 거뜬해! 자 봐라—덤벼!\"\n\n테스가 질겁한 가운데 검은 여왕은 옷의 상의를 벗어 던지기 시작했다—비웃음거리가 된 그 꼴 때문에라도 어차피 벗어 버리고 싶던 차였다—마침내 통통한 목과 어깨, 팔을 달빛에 드러냈고, 그 아래 그것들은 건장한 시골 처녀가 지닌 흠 없이 둥근 살집으로, 프락시텔레스의 작품처럼 환하고 아름다워 보였다. 그녀는 주먹을 쥐고 테스를 향해 자세를 잡았다.\n\n\"그럼 정말이지, 난 싸우지 않겠어!\" 후자가 위엄 있게 말했다. \"당신이 그런 부류인 줄 알았더라면, 이런 잡것들과 어울려 올 만큼 내 몸을 낮추지도 않았을 거야!\"\n\n다소 싸잡아 한 그 말은, 다른 쪽들에서 고운 테스의 운 나쁜 머리 위로 욕설의 폭우를 쏟아지게 했다. 특히 다이아몬드 여왕이 그러했는데, 그녀 역시 카가 의심받던 것과 같은 관계로 더버빌과 얽혀 있던지라, 공동의 적에 맞서 카와 한편이 되었다. 다른 여자 몇몇도 끼어들어 적의를 드러냈는데, 흥겹게 보낸 저녁이 아니었다면 그토록 어리석게 굴 이들은 아무도 없었을 것이다. 그러자 테스가 부당하게 윽박질림을 당하는 걸 보고, 남편들과 정인들이 그녀를 두둔하며 화해를 붙이려 했다. 그러나 그 시도의 결과는 곧장 싸움을 키우는 것이었다.\n\n테스는 분하고 부끄러웠다. 이제는 길의 외로움도, 시간이 늦은 것도 개의치 않았다. 그녀의 유일한 목표는 되도록 빨리 이 무리 전체에서 벗어나는 것이었다. 그들 중 나은 자들은 이튿날이면 제 격분을 후회하리라는 걸 그녀는 잘 알았다. 이제 모두 밭 안에 들어와 있었고, 그녀가 혼자 내달으려 뒤로 슬금슬금 물러서던 차에, 길을 가린 산울타리 모퉁이에서 거의 소리 없이 말 탄 자 하나가 나타났고, 알렉 더버빌이 그들을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"이게 다 무슨 소란이야, 일꾼들?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n설명이 선뜻 나오지는 않았다. 사실 그는 설명이 필요하지도 않았다. 아직 멀리서부터 그들의 목소리를 들은 터라 살금살금 말을 몰아 다가왔고, 흡족할 만큼은 이미 알아챈 뒤였다.\n\n테스는 다른 이들과 떨어져, 문 가까이 서 있었다. 그가 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 굽혔다. \"내 뒤에 올라타.\" 그가 속삭였다. \"그러면 저 빽빽대는 고양이들한테서 눈 깜짝할 새에 벗어날 수 있어!\"\n\n위기감이 어찌나 생생했던지 그녀는 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다. 인생의 다른 거의 어느 순간이었다면, 앞서 여러 번 그랬듯 이렇게 내미는 도움과 동행을 거절했을 것이다. 그리고 지금도 외로움만으로는 달리 행동하게 만들지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그 청이 하필, 이 적들에 대한 두려움과 분노가 발을 한 번 굴러 그들에 대한 승리로 바뀔 수 있는 그 순간에 온지라, 그녀는 충동에 몸을 내맡겨, 문을 넘어, 그의 발등에 발끝을 디디고, 그의 뒤 안장으로 기어올랐다. 다투던 흥청대는 무리가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알아챘을 때, 두 사람은 이미 멀리 잿빛 속으로 내달리고 있었다.\n\n스페이드 여왕은 제 상의의 얼룩도 잊은 채, 다이아몬드 여왕과 갓 혼인한 비틀거리는 젊은 여자 곁에 섰다—모두 말발굽 소리가 길에서 잦아들어 침묵으로 사라져 가는 쪽을 못 박힌 듯 응시하면서.\n\n\"다들 뭘 보는 거야?\" 그 일을 못 본 사내 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"호—호—호!\" 검은 카가 웃었다.\n\n\"히—히—히!\" 술 취한 신부가 다정한 남편 팔에 의지해 몸을 가누며 웃었다.\n\n\"흐—흐—흐!\" 검은 카의 어머니가 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 짤막하게 설명하듯 웃었다. \"냄비에서 뛰쳐나와 불 속으로 들어갔구먼!\"\n\n그러고는 알코올의 과음으로도 좀처럼 영영 망가뜨릴 수 없는 이 한데서 자란 아이들은 밭길로 들어섰다. 그들이 걸어가는 동안, 저마다의 머리 그림자 둘레로, 이슬에 번들거리는 장막 위에 달빛이 만들어 낸 유백색 빛의 둥근 테가 함께 옮겨 갔다. 보행자 저마다 제 것 말고는 어떤 후광도 볼 수 없었는데, 그 후광은 천하게 비틀거리든 어떻든 머리 그림자를 결코 떠나지 않았고, 거기에 들러붙어 끈질기게 그것을 아름답게 꾸몄다. 마침내 그 갈팡질팡하는 움직임이 빛 발산의 타고난 한 부분처럼 보였고, 그들 숨결의 김이 밤안개의 한 성분처럼 보였다. 그리하여 그 광경의 정령과, 달빛의 정령과, 자연의 정령이, 술의 정령과 조화롭게 어우러지는 듯했다.\n\n# XI\n\n두 사람은 한동안 말없이 가볍게 말을 몰았다. 테스는 그에게 매달린 채 여전히 승리에 헐떡였으나, 다른 면에서는 미심쩍었다. 그녀는 그 말이 그가 이따금 올라타던 사나운 놈이 아님을 알아챘고, 그 점에서는 불안하지 않았다. 비록 그를 단단히 붙들었어도 그녀의 자리는 위태로웠지만. 그녀는 짐승을 걸음 속도로 늦춰 달라고 부탁했고, 알렉도 그에 따랐다.\n\n\"산뜻하게 해치웠지, 안 그래, 테스?\" 그가 이윽고 말했다.\n\n\"네!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"정말 고맙다고 인사해야겠죠.\"\n\n\"정말 그래?\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"테스, 넌 왜 늘 내가 입 맞추는 걸 싫어하지?\"\n\n\"아마—당신을 사랑하지 않아서겠죠.\"\n\n\"정말 확실해?\"\n\n\"가끔은 당신한테 화도 나요!\"\n\n\"아, 어쩐지 그럴까 봐 반쯤 두려웠어.\" 그렇지만 알렉은 그 고백에 토를 달지 않았다. 그는 무엇이든 냉담함보다는 낫다는 걸 알았다. \"내가 화나게 했을 때 왜 말하지 않았어?\"\n\n\"이유야 잘 알잖아요. 여기서는 어쩔 도리가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"내 구애로 너를 그렇게 자주 언짢게 한 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"가끔은 그랬어요.\"\n\n\"몇 번이나?\"\n\n\"당신도 나만큼 알잖아요—너무 여러 번.\"\n\n\"내가 시도할 때마다?\"\n\n그녀는 잠잠했고, 말은 한참을 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어, 저녁 내내 우묵한 곳에 깔려 있던 희미하게 빛나는 안개가 사방으로 번져 두 사람을 에워쌌다. 그것은 달빛을 머금어 띄워 놓은 듯해, 맑은 공기에서보다 한층 더 짙게 퍼지게 했다. 그 탓인지, 아니면 멍하니 있어서인지, 졸려서인지, 그녀는 트랜트리지로 가는 오솔길이 큰길에서 갈라지는 지점을 오래전에 지나쳤다는 것도, 길잡이가 트랜트리지 길로 들어서지 않았다는 것도 알아채지 못했다.\n\n그녀는 말할 수 없이 지쳐 있었다. 그 주 내내 아침 다섯 시에 일어났고, 날마다 온종일 서 있었으며, 오늘 저녁에는 거기에 더해 체이스버러까지 삼 마일을 걸었고, 먹지도 마시지도 못한 채—그들을 어서 출발시키고 싶은 조바심이 먹고 마실 짬을 막은 탓에—이웃들을 세 시간이나 기다렸다. 그러고 나서 집으로 가는 길을 일 마일 걸었고, 그 다툼의 흥분을 겪었으며, 말이 더디게 나아가는 통에 이제 거의 한 시가 다 되었다. 그러나 진짜 졸음에 휩싸인 것은 단 한 번뿐이었다. 그 망각의 순간에 그녀의 머리가 가만히 그에게 기울어 닿았다.\n\n더버빌은 말을 세우고, 등자에서 발을 빼고, 안장 위에서 옆으로 돌아앉아, 그녀를 받치려고 팔로 그녀의 허리를 감쌌다.\n\n이것은 곧장 그녀를 방어 태세로 돌려놓았고, 그녀에게 곧잘 일던 그 갑작스러운 보복 충동의 하나로 그녀는 그를 자기에게서 살짝 밀쳤다. 위태로운 자세에 있던 그는 거의 균형을 잃어, 길바닥으로 굴러떨어질 뻔한 것을 간신히 면했다. 다행히 말이 힘이 세긴 했어도 그가 타던 말 중 가장 얌전한 놈이었다.\n\n\"그건 정말 매정하군!\" 그가 말했다. \"해코지하려는 게 아니라—그저 네가 떨어지지 않게 하려는 거였어.\"\n\n그녀는 미심쩍게 곱씹다가, 어쩌면 결국 그 말이 사실일지도 모른다는 생각에 마음을 누그러뜨려, 자못 겸손하게 말했다. \"죄송해요, 도련님.\"\n\n\"네가 나한테 어느 정도 믿음을 보이지 않는 한 용서 못 해. 맙소사!\" 그가 터뜨렸다. \"내가 뭐길래 너 같은 풋내기한테 이토록 퇴짜를 맞아야 하지? 죽도록 긴 석 달 가까이 너는 내 감정을 가지고 놀고, 나를 피하고, 나를 쌀쌀맞게 굴었어. 더는 못 참아!\"\n\n\"내일이면 도련님 곁을 떠날게요.\"\n\n\"아니, 넌 내일 날 떠나지 않아! 한 번 더 묻겠는데, 내 팔로 너를 안게 해 줘서 네 믿음을 보여 주겠어? 자, 우리 둘 사이의 일, 다른 누구도 없이, 지금. 우린 서로를 잘 알잖아. 그리고 내가 너를 사랑하고, 너를 세상에서 가장 예쁜 처녀로 여긴다는 걸 너도 알잖아, 실제로 그러니까. 너를 연인처럼 대하면 안 돼?\"\n\n그녀는 거부하듯 짜증스레 숨을 빨리 들이쉬며, 자리에서 불편하게 몸을 비틀었고, 멀리 앞을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"모르겠어요—저는—언제 그렇다 아니다 말할 수 있겠어요, 그게—\"\n\n그는 바라던 대로 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안음으로써 그 문제를 매듭지었고, 테스는 더는 부정의 말을 내지 않았다. 이렇게 둘은 천천히 옆걸음으로 나아갔는데, 문득 그녀는 자기들이 터무니없이 오랫동안—체이스버러에서 오는 짧은 여정이 이런 걸음 속도라 해도 으레 걸리는 시간보다 훨씬 더 오래—나아가고 있었으며, 더는 단단한 길이 아니라 그저 바퀴 자국뿐인 길 위에 있다는 걸 깨달았다.\n\n\"아니, 우리 어디 있는 거예요?\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n\"숲을 지나가는 중이야.\"\n\n\"숲이라니—무슨 숲이요? 우리 분명 길에서 한참 벗어났잖아요?\"\n\n\"체이스의 한 자락—잉글랜드에서 가장 오래된 숲이지. 아름다운 밤인데, 좀 더 길게 달리면 안 될 게 뭐 있어?\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 음흉할 수가 있어요!\" 테스가 짐짓 토라진 듯하면서도 진짜로 당황해하며 말했고, 자칫 자기가 미끄러져 떨어질 위험을 무릅쓰면서도 그의 손가락을 하나하나 펴서 그 팔을 떼어 냈다. \"내가 그렇게 당신을 믿어 주고 있었는데, 그 밀친 일로 내가 당신한테 잘못했다고 생각해서 당신 비위 맞추려고 따라 주고 있었는데! 제발 내려 주고, 걸어서 집에 가게 해 줘요.\"\n\n\"걸어서는 집에 못 가, 그대, 공기가 맑다 해도 말이야. 굳이 말하자면 우린 트랜트리지에서 몇 마일이나 떨어져 있고, 이렇게 짙어 가는 안개 속에서 이 나무들 사이를 몇 시간이나 헤맬지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"그건 상관없어요.\" 그녀가 달래듯 말했다. \"내려 줘요, 제발. 어디든 상관없어요. 그저 내리게만 해 줘요, 도련님, 부탁이에요!\"\n\n\"그래, 좋아, 그럼—한 가지 조건이 있어. 너를 이런 외진 데까지 데려왔으니, 네 생각이야 어떻든 너를 집까지 안전하게 데려다줄 책임이 내게 있다고 느껴. 도움 없이 네가 트랜트리지에 가는 건 도무지 불가능해. 사실대로 말하자면, 그대, 이 안개가 모든 걸 워낙 가려 놓아서 나조차 우리가 어디 있는지 잘 모르겠거든. 자, 내가 덤불을 헤치고 걸어가 어떤 길이나 집을 찾아 우리 위치를 정확히 알아내는 동안 말 옆에서 기다리겠다고 약속하면, 기꺼이 너를 여기 내려 주마. 돌아오면 자세히 길을 일러 줄 테고, 걸어가겠다고 고집하면 걸어도 되고, 타고 가도 되고—네 마음대로 해.\"\n\n그녀는 이 조건을 받아들여, 가까운 쪽으로 미끄러져 내렸다—다만 그가 잽싸게 스치듯 입 맞추기 전은 아니었다. 그는 반대편으로 뛰어내렸다.\n\n\"말은 제가 붙잡고 있어야겠죠?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니, 그럴 필요 없어.\" 알렉이 헐떡이는 짐승을 토닥이며 대답했다. \"오늘 밤엔 이놈도 실컷 했으니까.\"\n\n그는 말 머리를 덤불 쪽으로 돌려 가지에 매어 놓고, 수북이 쌓인 낙엽 더미 속에 그녀를 위한 일종의 자리, 둥지 같은 것을 만들어 주었다.\n\n\"자, 거기 앉아.\" 그가 말했다. \"낙엽은 아직 안 젖었어. 말이나 한번 봐 주면—그걸로 충분해.\"\n\n그는 그녀에게서 몇 걸음 멀어졌다가, 돌아와 말했다. \"그런데 말이야, 테스, 오늘 네 아버지한테 새 조랑말이 한 마리 생겼어. 누가 줬지.\"\n\n\"누가요? 당신이!\"\n\n더버빌이 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"오 정말 친절하셔라!\" 그녀가 외쳤는데, 하필 그런 때에 그에게 고마워해야 한다는 게 거북하기 짝이 없었다.\n\n\"그리고 아이들한테는 장난감이 좀 있어.\"\n\n\"몰랐어요—당신이 그 애들한테 뭘 보내 주신 줄은!\" 그녀가 몹시 감동해 중얼거렸다. \"차라리 안 그러셨으면 싶을 정도예요—그래요, 차라리 그랬으면!\"\n\n\"왜, 그대?\"\n\n\"그게—절 너무 옭아매니까요.\"\n\n\"테시—이제 날 조금도 사랑하지 않니?\"\n\n\"고마워요.\" 그녀가 마지못해 인정했다. \"하지만 사랑하진 않는 것 같아요—\" 자기를 향한 그의 열정이 이 결과의 한 요인이라는 생각이 불현듯 떠올라 어찌나 괴로웠던지, 그녀는 느린 눈물 한 방울로 시작해, 이어 또 한 방울로, 끝내 엉엉 울고 말았다.\n\n\"울지 마, 그대, 사랑스러운 그대! 자 여기 앉아서, 내가 올 때까지 기다려.\"\n그녀는 순순히 그가 쌓아 둔 낙엽 사이에 앉아, 살짝 몸을 떨었다. \"추워?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"많이는 아니고—조금요.\"\n\n그는 손가락으로 그녀를 건드렸는데, 손가락이 솜털 속으로 파묻히듯 그녀 속으로 가라앉았다. \"그 부푼 모슬린 옷 하나밖에 안 입었구나—어쩌다가?\"\n\n\"이게 제 제일 좋은 여름옷이에요. 출발할 땐 무척 따뜻했고, 말을 타게 될 줄도, 밤이 될 줄도 몰랐거든요.\"\n\n\"구월엔 밤이 쌀쌀해지지. 어디 보자.\" 그는 입고 있던 얇은 외투를 벗어, 그것을 부드럽게 그녀에게 둘러 주었다. \"그래—이제 좀 따뜻할 거야.\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"자, 나의 예쁜이, 거기서 쉬어. 곧 돌아올게.\"\n\n외투의 단추를 그녀의 어깨에 채워 준 뒤, 그는 이쯤 되어 나무들 사이에 장막을 친 안개의 그물 속으로 뛰어들었다. 그녀는 그가 옆 비탈을 오르며 가지를 바스락거리는 소리를 들을 수 있었는데, 마침내 그의 움직임은 새가 폴짝거리는 소리보다 더 크지 않게 되었다가, 끝내 사라졌다. 달이 지면서 창백한 빛이 옅어졌고, 그가 두고 간 낙엽 위에서 몽상에 잠긴 테스는 보이지 않게 되었다.\n\n그동안 알렉 더버빌은, 자기들이 체이스의 어느 구역에 있는지에 대한 진짜 의문을 풀려고 비탈을 올라 나아갔다. 사실 그는 한 시간 넘게 그야말로 되는대로 말을 몰며, 그녀와의 동행을 늘리려고 손에 닿는 갈림길마다 아무 데로나 접어들었고, 길가의 어떤 사물보다 달빛에 비친 테스의 몸에 훨씬 더 마음을 쏟았다. 지친 짐승을 좀 쉬게 하는 편이 좋겠기에, 그는 지표를 찾는 일을 서두르지 않았다. 언덕을 기어 넘어 이웃 골짜기로 내려서니 윤곽이 눈에 익은 큰길의 울타리에 이르렀고, 그것으로 그들의 위치 문제가 풀렸다.\n이에 더버빌은 발길을 돌렸다. 그러나 이때쯤엔 달이 완전히 져 버린 데다, 부분적으로 안개 탓에 체이스는, 아침이 멀지 않았는데도, 짙은 어둠에 싸여 있었다. 그는 나뭇가지에 부딪치지 않으려 두 손을 앞으로 뻗고 나아가야 했고, 출발했던 바로 그 자리를 찾는 일이 처음에는 도무지 그의 능력 밖임을 알았다. 위아래로, 빙빙 돌며 헤맨 끝에, 마침내 바로 곁에서 말이 살짝 움직이는 소리를 들었다. 그리고 그의 외투 소매가 뜻밖에 그의 발에 걸렸다.\n\n\"테스!\" 더버빌이 말했다.\n\n대답이 없었다. 어둠이 이제 어찌나 짙던지 그는 발치의 창백한 안개 같은 형체 말고는 그야말로 아무것도 볼 수 없었는데, 그것이 그가 낙엽 위에 두고 간 흰 모슬린 형체를 나타내고 있었다. 그 밖의 모든 것은 한결같이 캄캄했다. 더버빌이 몸을 굽혔다. 그리고 가만하고 규칙적인 숨소리를 들었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇고 더 낮게 숙였고, 마침내 그녀의 숨결이 그의 얼굴을 따뜻하게 했으며, 한순간 그의 뺨이 그녀의 뺨에 닿았다. 그녀는 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 그녀의 속눈썹에는 눈물이 맺혀 있었다.\n\n어둠과 침묵이 사방을 다스렸다. 그들 위로 체이스의 태곳적 주목과 떡갈나무가 솟아 있었고, 그 안에는 마지막 선잠에 든 새들이 얌전히 깃들어 있었다. 그들 둘레로는 폴짝거리는 토끼와 산토끼가 살금살금 다녔다. 그러나 누군가는 물을지도 모른다, 테스의 수호천사는 어디 있었느냐고. 그녀의 소박한 믿음이 받드는 섭리는 어디 있었느냐고. 어쩌면 그는, 빈정대던 티스바 사람이 말한 또 다른 신처럼, 이야기를 나누고 있었거나, 사냥을 하고 있었거나, 여행 중이었거나, 잠들어 깨우지 못할 상태였는지도 모른다.\n\n거미줄처럼 예민하고 아직 눈처럼 사실상 새하얗던 이 아름다운 여성의 천 위에, 어찌하여 그것이 받기로 정해진 그토록 거친 무늬가 새겨져야 했는지. 어찌하여 그토록 자주 거친 것이 이렇듯 고운 것을 차지하는지, 그른 남자가 여자를, 그른 여자가 남자를 차지하는지, 수천 년에 걸친 분석 철학도 우리의 질서 감각에 설명해 내지 못했다. 물론, 지금의 이 파국 속에 도사린 어떤 응보의 가능성을 인정할 수는 있다. 분명 테스 더버빌의 갑옷 입은 조상들 가운데 누군가는 싸움터에서 흥청대며 돌아오는 길에 제 시대의 농가 처녀들에게 똑같은, 아니 그보다 더 무자비한 짓을 저질렀을 것이다. 그러나 아비의 죄를 자식에게 묻는 일이 신들에게는 그럭저럭 괜찮은 도덕일지언정, 보통의 인간 본성은 그것을 경멸한다. 그러므로 그렇게 한들 사정이 나아지지는 않는다.\n\n저 아래 그 외진 곳의 테스의 동족들이 운명론적인 투로 저희끼리 입버릇처럼 말하듯, \"그리될 일이었던\" 것이다. 거기에 그 안타까움이 놓여 있었다. 어머니의 문간을 나서 트랜트리지 양계장에서 제 운을 시험해 보러 가던 예전의 그 자신과, 그 뒤로 우리 여주인공의 인격을 가늠할 수 없는 사회적 심연이 갈라놓게 될 터였다.\n\n# XII\n\n바구니는 무거웠고 보따리는 컸지만, 그녀는 자기 특유의 짐이 물질에 있지 않다는 듯한 사람처럼 그것들을 끌고 갔다. 이따금 그녀는 어떤 문이나 기둥 곁에서 기계적으로 쉬려고 멈추었다. 그러고는 짐을 통통하고 둥근 팔 위로 다시 추슬러, 또박또박 다시 걸어갔다.\n\n시월 하순의 어느 일요일 아침이었다, 테스 더버빌이 트랜트리지에 온 지 넉 달쯤, 체이스의 밤 말타기로부터 몇 주쯤 지난 뒤였다. 동틀 무렵을 지난 지 얼마 안 된 때라, 등 뒤 지평선 위의 노란 빛이 그녀의 얼굴이 향한 능선을 비추었다—그녀가 요사이 낯선 이가 되어 있던 골짜기의 경계였고, 태어난 곳에 닿으려면 넘어가야 할 능선이었다. 이쪽에서는 오르막이 완만했고, 토양과 풍경이 블레이크모어 골짜기 안의 것과 사뭇 달랐다. 두 고장 사람들의 기질과 말씨조차, 빙 둘러 난 철도가 뒤섞어 놓는 효과에도 불구하고 차이의 그늘이 있었다. 그래서 트랜트리지에 머물던 곳에서 이십 마일도 채 안 되는데, 고향 마을은 아득히 먼 곳처럼 여겨졌다. 그곳에 갇혀 사는 들일꾼들은 북쪽과 서쪽으로 장사하고, 북쪽과 서쪽으로 다니고 연애하고 혼인했으며, 북쪽과 서쪽으로 생각했다. 이쪽 사람들은 주로 동쪽과 남쪽으로 그들의 기운과 관심을 돌렸다.\n\n그 비탈은 유월의 그날 더버빌이 그토록 사납게 그녀를 몰아 내려가던 바로 그 비탈이었다. 테스는 멈추지 않고 남은 길을 다 올라갔고, 가파른 비탈의 가장자리에 이르러 너머의 낯익은 푸른 세계를 굽어보았다, 이제 절반쯤 안개에 가려진 그곳을. 여기서 보면 그곳은 늘 아름다웠다. 오늘 테스에게는 무서우리만치 아름다웠다. 그 눈이 마지막으로 그곳에 닿은 뒤로, 그녀는 고운 새들이 노래하는 곳에서 뱀이 쉭쉭댄다는 것을 알게 되었고, 그 가르침으로 인생을 보는 눈이 송두리째 바뀌어 버린 까닭이었다. 참으로, 생각에 짓눌려 여기 가만히 서서 뒤돌아보는 그녀는, 집에서의 그 단순했던 처녀와는 다른 처녀였다. 그녀는 차마 골짜기 쪽을 내다볼 수 없었다.\n\n테스 자신이 방금 힘겹게 올라온 그 긴 흰 길을 따라 올라오는, 두 바퀴 달린 수레 한 대가 보였고, 그 곁에는 한 사내가 걸으며 손을 들어 그녀의 주의를 끌고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 별 헤아림 없이 잠잠한 마음으로 그를 기다리라는 신호에 따랐고, 몇 분 뒤 사내와 말이 그녀 곁에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"왜 이렇게 몰래 빠져나간 거야?\" 더버빌이 나무라듯 가쁜 숨으로 말했다. \"그것도 일요일 아침에, 다들 잠자리에 있을 때 말이야! 우연히 알게 돼서, 너를 따라잡으려고 미친 듯이 몰아왔어. 이 암말 좀 봐. 왜 이렇게 가 버린 거야? 아무도 네가 가는 걸 막으려 하지 않은 거 너도 알잖아. 그리고 걸어서 이 무거운 짐까지 짊어지고 고생할 필요가 어디 있었어! 미친놈처럼 따라온 건, 네가 돌아오지 않겠다면 남은 거리만이라도 태워 주려는 거야.\"\n\n\"안 돌아가요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"안 돌아갈 줄 알았어—내가 그랬잖아! 그럼, 바구니 올려놓고, 내가 태워 줄게.\"\n\n그녀는 맥없이 바구니와 보따리를 이륜마차 안에 놓고, 올라탔고, 둘은 나란히 앉았다. 이제 그녀는 그가 두렵지 않았는데, 그 마음 놓임의 까닭에 그녀의 슬픔이 놓여 있었다.\n\n더버빌은 기계적으로 시가에 불을 붙였고, 여정은 길가의 흔한 사물들에 관한 토막토막의 무덤덤한 이야기로 이어졌다. 그는 초여름에 같은 길을 반대 방향으로 달리며 그녀에게 입 맞추려 애쓰던 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 잊지 않았고, 이제 꼭두각시처럼 앉아, 그의 말에 외마디로 대답했다. 몇 마일을 가니 그 너머에 말럿 마을이 선 나무숲이 눈에 들어왔다. 그제야 그녀의 멈춰 있던 얼굴에 조금이나마 감정이 드러났고, 눈물 한두 방울이 흘러내리기 시작했다.\n\n\"뭐 때문에 우는 거야?\" 그가 차갑게 물었다.\n\n\"그냥 제가 저기서 태어났다는 생각이 들어서요.\" 테스가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"뭐—누구나 어딘가에서는 태어나기 마련이지.\"\n\n\"차라리 태어나지 않았더라면—저기서든 다른 어디서든!\"\n\n\"흥! 그럼, 트랜트리지에 오기 싫었으면 왜 온 거야?\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"날 사랑해서 온 게 아니라는 건, 내가 맹세하지.\"\n\n\"맞아요. 만약 당신을 사랑해서 갔다면, 만약 한 번이라도 진심으로 당신을 사랑했다면, 만약 지금도 당신을 사랑한다면, 지금처럼 제 나약함 때문에 저 자신을 이토록 혐오하고 증오하지는 않을 거예요!…… 잠깐 당신한테 눈이 멀었던 거예요, 그게 다였어요.\"\n\n그는 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그녀가 다시 말했다—\n\n\"당신 속셈을 알아챘을 땐 이미 너무 늦었어요.\"\n\n\"여자란 다 그렇게들 말하지.\"\n\n\"어떻게 감히 그런 말을!\" 그녀가 그를 향해 격하게 돌아서며 외쳤는데, 그 눈은 (언젠가 그가 더 보게 될) 잠재된 기개가 깨어나며 번득였다. \"맙소사! 당신을 마차에서 떨어뜨려 버릴 수도 있어! 여자들이 다 하는 말을 어떤 여자들은 진짜로 느낄지도 모른다는 생각은 한 번도 못 해 봤어요?\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 그가 웃으며 말했다. \"너를 아프게 해서 미안해. 내가 잘못했어—인정하지.\" 그는 이어 말하며 조금 쓰라림에 빠져들었다. \"다만 그걸 그렇게 끝도 없이 내 얼굴에 들이댈 필요는 없잖아. 난 마지막 한 푼까지 갚을 준비가 돼 있어. 네가 다시는 밭일이나 낙농장 일을 안 해도 된다는 거 너도 알잖아. 요새 네가 마치 버는 것보다 리본 한 가닥도 더 못 산다는 듯이 일부러 꾸민 그 휑하니 수수한 차림 대신, 최상품으로 몸을 두를 수 있다는 것도 알잖아.\"\n\n그녀의 입술이 살짝 들렸으나, 대체로 그녀의 크고 충동적인 천성에는 멸시라 할 게 거의 없었다.\n\n\"당신한테서 더는 아무것도 받지 않겠다고 했고, 안 받을 거예요—못 받아요! 그러면 그 짓을 계속하는 당신의 물건이 되어 버릴 텐데, 난 그러지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n\"네 태도를 보면 진짜배기 원조 더버빌인 데다 공주님인 줄 알겠어—하! 하! 그래, 테스, 그대, 더는 할 말이 없군. 나는 나쁜 놈인가 봐—지독하게 나쁜 놈. 나쁘게 태어나, 나쁘게 살아왔고, 십중팔구 나쁘게 죽겠지. 하지만, 내 잃어버린 영혼을 걸고, 너한테만은 다시 나쁘게 굴지 않겠어, 테스. 그리고 만에 하나 어떤 사정이 생긴다면—너도 알지—네가 조금이라도 도움이 필요하거나, 조금이라도 곤란해지면, 한 줄만 보내, 그러면 필요한 건 뭐든 곧장 받게 될 거야. 난 트랜트리지에 없을지도 몰라—한동안 런던에 갈 거거든—그 노인네는 도무지 견딜 수가 없어서. 하지만 편지는 다 전달될 거야.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 더 멀리 태워다 주는 걸 원치 않는다고 말했고, 둘은 바로 그 나무숲 아래에 멈췄다. 더버빌이 내려, 그녀를 두 팔로 번쩍 들어 내려놓은 뒤, 그녀의 물건들을 그녀 곁 땅에 놓아 주었다. 그녀는 그에게 살짝 고개를 숙였고, 그 눈길이 잠시 그의 눈에 머물렀다. 그러고는 떠나려고 짐 꾸러미를 집으려 돌아섰다.\n\n알렉 더버빌이 시가를 떼고, 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 굽혀 말했다—\n\n\"그렇게 돌아서 가 버리려는 건 아니지, 그대! 자!\"\n\n\"원하신다면요.\" 그녀가 무심히 대답했다. \"보세요, 당신이 절 얼마나 좌지우지했는지!\"\n\n이에 그녀는 돌아서서 그에게 얼굴을 들어 올린 채, 그가 그녀의 뺨에 입 맞추는 동안 대리석 경계비처럼 가만히 있었다—반쯤은 마지못한 듯이, 반쯤은 흥취가 아직 다 가시지 않은 듯이. 입맞춤이 오가는 동안 그녀의 눈은 오솔길의 가장 멀리 떨어진 나무들에 멍하니 머물렀는데, 마치 그가 무얼 하는지 거의 의식하지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n\"이제 반대쪽도, 옛정을 봐서.\"\n\n그녀는 마치 화가나 이발사의 청에 응하듯 똑같이 수동적으로 고개를 돌렸고, 그는 반대쪽에 입 맞췄는데, 그의 입술이 닿은 뺨은 둘레 밭의 버섯 껍질처럼 축축하고 매끄럽게 차가웠다.\n\n\"넌 입술을 내주지도, 마주 입 맞추지도 않는구나. 한 번도 기꺼이 그러질 않아—넌 결코 날 사랑하지 않겠지, 안타깝게도.\"\n\n\"여러 번 말했잖아요. 사실이에요. 한 번도 진심으로 진짜로 당신을 사랑한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 못 할 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 구슬프게 덧붙였다. \"어쩌면, 무엇보다, 이 일에 거짓말 하나 하는 게 지금 제게 가장 득이 될지도 모르죠. 하지만 비록 보잘것없을지언정, 그런 거짓말은 하지 않을 만큼의 명예는 제게 남아 있어요. 정말 당신을 사랑한다면야, 당신한테 알려 줄 더없이 좋은 까닭이 있겠죠. 하지만 사랑하지 않아요.\"\n\n그는, 그 광경이 그의 마음을, 혹은 양심을, 혹은 체면을 자못 짓누르기라도 하는 듯, 힘겹게 숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 우스꽝스러우리만치 우울하구나, 테스. 이제 와 너한테 아첨할 까닭도 없으니, 분명히 말하는데 그렇게 슬퍼할 것 없어. 너는 이 고장 어떤 여자와 견줘도, 양가집이든 평민이든, 미모만큼은 뒤지지 않아. 실속 있는 사내로서, 또 너를 위하는 사람으로서 하는 말이야. 현명하다면, 그게 시들기 전에 지금보다 더 세상에 내보일 거다…… 그래도, 테스, 내게 돌아오지 않겠어! 내 영혼을 걸고, 이렇게 너를 보내기는 정말 싫구나!\"\n\n\"절대로, 절대로요! 진작 봤어야 할 걸 본 순간 마음을 굳혔어요. 돌아가지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 잘 가, 넉 달짜리 내 사촌—잘 있어!\"\n\n그는 가볍게 뛰어올라 고삐를 추슬렀고, 붉은 열매가 달린 키 큰 산울타리 사이로 사라졌다.\n\n테스는 그의 뒷모습을 바라보지 않고, 굽이진 오솔길을 따라 천천히 걸어 나갔다. 아직 이른 시각이라, 해의 아래쪽 가장자리가 막 언덕을 벗어났을 뿐, 그 빛살은 미지근하니 엿보는 듯해 아직은 촉감보다 눈에 와닿았다. 가까이에 인기척 하나 없었다. 슬픈 시월과 더 슬픈 그녀 자신만이 그 오솔길을 떠도는 단 둘뿐인 존재인 듯했다.\n\n그러나 걷는 동안, 등 뒤에서 어떤 발소리가, 한 사내의 발소리가 다가왔다. 그가 어찌나 빠르게 다가왔던지, 그녀가 그 가까움을 채 알아채기도 전에 그는 바짝 뒤따라붙어 \"안녕하세요\" 하고 인사를 건넨 뒤였다. 그는 일종의 장인匠人처럼 보였고, 손에는 붉은 페인트 깡통을 들고 있었다. 그는 사무적인 태도로 바구니를 들어 줄까 물었고, 그녀가 허락하자 그녀 곁에서 걸었다.\n\n\"이 안식일 아침에 나다니기엔 이르네요!\" 그가 쾌활하게 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n\"다들 한 주 일에서 벗어나 쉬고 있을 때 말이오.\"\n\n이 말에도 그녀는 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"하기야 난 오늘 이 한 주 다 합친 것보다 더 진짜 일을 하지만.\"\n\n\"그러세요?\"\n\n\"한 주 내내 난 사람의 영광을 위해 일하고, 일요일엔 하느님의 영광을 위해 일하지요. 그게 다른 것보다 더 진짜 아니겠소—그렇지요? 여기 이 층계 디딤대에서 좀 할 일이 있소.\" 사내는 말하며, 길가의 목초지로 통하는 한 틈으로 몸을 돌렸다. \"잠깐만 기다려 주시면,\" 그가 덧붙였다, \"오래 걸리지 않을 거요.\"\n그가 그녀의 바구니를 들고 있는지라 그녀로서는 달리 어쩔 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 기다렸다,\n\n그가 그녀의 바구니를 가지고 있었으므로 그녀로서는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 그를 지켜보며 기다렸다. 그는 그녀의 바구니와 양철통을 내려놓고, 통 안에 든 붓으로 페인트를 휘저은 다음, 디딤대를 이루는 세 개의 널빤지 중 가운데 널빤지에 커다란 네모반듯한 글자를 칠하기 시작했다. 한 단어마다 쉼표를 찍었는데, 그 단어가 읽는 이의 마음에 단단히 박혀들도록 잠시 멈춤을 주려는 듯했다— 네, 멸망이, 자고, 있지, 않느니라.\n\n벧후 2장 3절.\n평온한 풍경, 잡목 숲의 창백하게 시들어가는 빛깔, 지평선의 푸른 대기, 이끼 낀 디딤대 널빤지를 배경으로, 이 노려보는 듯한 주홍빛 글자들이 빛을 뿜었다. 글자들은 제 스스로 외쳐대며 대기를 울리는 듯했다. 어떤 사람들은 이 흉측한 훼손을 보고 \"아아, 가엾은 신학이여!\" 하고 외쳤을지도 모른다—한때 인류에게 충실히 봉사했던 신조의 마지막 기괴한 단계라며. 그러나 그 글자들은 테스에게 고발하는 듯한 공포로 파고들었다. 마치 이 남자가 그녀의 최근 사정을 알고 있는 것 같았다. 그런데 그는 생판 모르는 타인이었다.\n\n성구를 다 칠한 그가 그녀의 바구니를 집어 들었고, 그녀는 기계적으로 그의 곁에서 다시 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"당신이 칠하는 걸 믿으세요?\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"이 성구를 믿느냐고요? 내가 내 존재를 믿느냐는 거나 마찬가지요!\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"만약 그 죄가 스스로 구한 게 아니라면요?\"\n\n그는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그런 불타는 물음에 대해선 내가 따져 가를 수 없소,\" 그가 말했다. \"지난여름 내내 나는 수백 마일을 걸으며 이 지역 곳곳의 벽이며 문이며 디딤대마다 이 성구들을 칠했소. 그걸 어떻게 받아들일지는 읽는 사람들 마음에 맡길 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 끔찍해요,\" 테스가 말했다. \"짓누르고! 죽이고!\"\n\n\"바로 그러라고 만든 거요!\" 그가 장사꾼 같은 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"하지만 내가 빈민가랑 항구를 위해 아껴 둔 가장 독한 것들을 봐야 하오. 그걸 보면 몸부림칠 거요! 하긴 이건 시골 지역에는 아주 좋은 성구지만.... 아—저기 헛간 옆에 버려진 빈 벽이 한 자락 좋게 있구먼. 저기에 하나 칠해야겠소—당신 같은 위험한 젊은 여자들이 새겨들으면 좋을 걸로 말이오. 좀 기다려 주겠소, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러고는 바구니를 들고 테스는 터벅터벅 계속 걸어갔다. 조금 앞으로 나아가 그녀는 고개를 돌렸다. 그 오래된 잿빛 벽이 처음 것과 비슷한 불꽃 같은 글자를 광고하기 시작했는데, 이전에는 한 번도 떠맡아 본 적 없는 임무에 괴로워하는 듯 낯설고 어색한 표정이었다. 그가 이제 절반쯤 칠해 가는 그 글귀가 무엇이 될지 읽고 깨달았을 때 그녀는 갑작스레 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다—\n너희는, 간음하지, 말지니라—\n\n# THOU, SHALT, NOT, COMMIT—\n\n그녀의 쾌활한 동행은 그녀가 바라보는 것을 보고 붓질을 멈추고 외쳤다—\n\n\"이런 중대한 일들에 대해 가르침을 구하고 싶거든, 오늘 당신이 가려는 교구에서 자선 설교를 할 아주 진지하고 훌륭한 분이 계시오—에민스터의 클레어 목사님이오. 난 이제 그분과 같은 신앙은 아니지만, 그분은 좋은 분이고, 내가 아는 어떤 목사보다도 잘 풀어 설명하실 거요. 내 안에서 그 일을 시작하게 하신 분이 바로 그분이오.\"\n\n그러나 테스는 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 가슴을 두근거리며 다시 걸음을 옮겼고, 눈은 땅에 박혀 있었다. \"흥—하느님이 그런 말씀을 하셨을 리 없어!\" 얼굴의 홍조가 가시자 그녀는 경멸하듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n아버지의 굴뚝에서 갑자기 연기 한 줄기가 솟아올랐는데, 그 광경에 그녀의 가슴이 아렸다. 집에 다다랐을 때 안의 모습은 그녀의 가슴을 더욱 아리게 했다. 막 아래층으로 내려온 어머니가 벽난로 앞에서 그녀를 맞이하려 돌아섰는데, 그곳에서 어머니는 아침 주전자 아래 껍질 벗긴 참나무 잔가지에 불을 붙이고 있었다. 어린 동생들은 아직 위층에 있었고, 아버지도 마찬가지였다. 일요일 아침이라 아버지는 삼십 분쯤 더 누워 있어도 마땅하다고 여기는 날이었다.\n\n\"이런!—우리 귀여운 테스!\" 놀란 어머니가 벌떡 일어나 딸에게 입을 맞추며 소리쳤다. \"어떻게 지냈니? 네가 내 코앞에 올 때까지 못 봤구나! 결혼하러 집에 온 거니?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그것 때문에 온 건 아니에요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그럼 휴가차 온 거니?\"\n\n\"네—휴가차요. 긴 휴가차요,\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고, 네 사촌이 점잖은 도리를 다하지 않겠다는 거니?\"\n\n\"그분은 제 사촌이 아니고, 저와 결혼하지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n어머니는 그녀를 유심히 살폈다.\n\n\"이리 와 봐라, 너 나한테 다 말하지 않았구나,\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n그러자 테스는 어머니에게 다가가 조앤의 목에 얼굴을 묻고 털어놓았다.\n\n\"그런데도 그자를 너랑 결혼시키지 못했단 말이냐!\" 어머니가 거듭 말했다. \"그런 일이 있고서야, 너 말고 어떤 여자라도 다 해냈을 거다!\"\n\n\"아마 저 말고는 어떤 여자라도 그랬겠죠.\"\n\n\"네가 해냈더라면 돌아와서 들려줄 그럴듯한 이야깃거리가 됐을 텐데!\" 더비필드 부인은 분해서 눈물을 터뜨릴 듯이 말을 이었다. \"너와 그자에 관한 소문이 죄다 여기까지 들려왔는데, 그게 이렇게 끝날 줄 누가 알았겠니! 너 자신만 생각하지 말고 식구들을 위해 뭔가 좋은 일을 할 생각은 왜 못 했니? 내가 어떻게 죽도록 일하고 종처럼 부려지는지 좀 봐라, 게다가 네 가엾고 약한 아버지는 기름받이처럼 심장이 막혀 있는데. 난 이 일에서 뭔가가 나오기를 정말 바랐다! 넉 달 전에 너랑 그자가 함께 마차를 타고 떠나던 날 얼마나 어여쁜 한 쌍이었던지! 그자가 우리한테 베푼 걸 봐라—우린 다 우리가 그의 친척이라서 그런 줄 알았다. 하지만 친척이 아니라면, 그건 분명 너를 사랑해서 한 일일 거야. 그런데도 넌 그자랑 결혼하지 못했다니!\"\n\n알렉 더버빌의 마음을 돌려 그녀와 결혼하게 만든다고! 그가 그녀와 결혼한다고! 결혼에 관해서 그는 단 한 마디도 한 적이 없었다. 또 설령 했다 한들? 사회적 구원을 향한 발작적인 움켜쥠이 그녀를 어떻게 그에게 대답하도록 내몰았을지 그녀는 알 수 없었다. 하지만 가엾고 어리석은 어머니는 이 남자를 향한 그녀의 지금 심정을 거의 알지 못했다. 어쩌면 그것은 이런 상황에서 흔치 않고, 불운하고, 설명할 수 없는 것이었는지도 모른다. 하지만 그것은 분명 거기 있었다. 그리고 이것이, 그녀가 말했듯이, 그녀로 하여금 스스로를 혐오하게 만드는 것이었다. 그녀는 한 번도 그를 온전히 마음에 둔 적이 없었다. 지금은 조금도 마음에 두지 않았다. 그녀는 그를 두려워했고, 그 앞에서 움찔했고, 그가 그녀의 무력함을 교묘히 이용하는 데 굴복했다. 그러다 그의 열렬한 태도에 잠시 눈이 멀어 한동안 혼란스러운 항복으로 마음이 흔들리기도 했다. 그러다 갑자기 그를 경멸하고 싫어하게 되어 달아났다. 그게 전부였다. 그를 미워하는 것은 아니었다. 하지만 그는 그녀에게 흙과 재 같은 존재였고, 자기 이름을 위해서라 해도 그와 결혼하고 싶은 마음은 거의 없었다.\n\n\"그자를 남편으로 삼을 작정이 아니었다면 좀 더 조심했어야지!\"\n\n\"아 어머니, 우리 어머니!\" 괴로워하는 처녀는 가엾은 가슴이 터질 것처럼 격정적으로 어머니에게 돌아서며 울부짖었다. \"제가 어떻게 알 수 있었겠어요? 넉 달 전 이 집을 떠날 때 전 어린애였어요. 남자들에게 위험이 있다고 왜 말해 주지 않으셨어요? 왜 경고해 주지 않으셨어요? 귀부인들은 소설을 읽어서 이런 술수들을 알기에 무엇을 막아야 할지 알지만, 전 그런 식으로 배울 기회가 한 번도 없었고, 어머니는 절 도와주지 않으셨어요!\"\n\n어머니는 풀이 죽었다.\n\n\"그자의 다정한 감정이랑 그게 어디로 이어질지 말하면, 네가 그자한테 도도하게 굴어서 기회를 놓칠까 봐 그랬다,\" 어머니는 앞치마로 눈을 닦으며 중얼거렸다. \"뭐, 이제 어쩌겠니, 받아들이는 수밖에. 어쨌든 다 자연의 이치고, 하느님이 기뻐하시는 일이지!\"\n\n# XIII\n\n테스 더비필드가 사이비 친척의 영지에서 돌아온 일은 소문으로 널리 퍼졌다—사방 일 마일쯤 되는 공간에 소문이라는 말이 과하지 않다면 말이다. 오후가 되자 마롯의 처녀 몇몇, 테스의 옛 학교 친구들과 아는 이들이 그녀를 보러 찾아왔다. 그들은 (그들이 생각하기에) 비범한 정복을 이뤄낸 사람을 방문하는 데 걸맞게 가장 좋은 옷을 풀 먹여 다림질해 차려입고 와서는, 방 안에 빙 둘러앉아 굉장한 호기심으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀에게 반한 이가 바로 그 서른한 번째 사촌인 더버빌 씨라는 사실—이 지역 토박이만은 아닌 신사로, 무모한 바람둥이이자 여자 마음을 깨뜨리는 자라는 평판이 트랜트리지 인근 경계 너머로 퍼지기 시작한 인물이었기에—이, 그 두려움으로 인해, 위험하지 않았더라면 발휘했을 것보다 훨씬 더 높은 매혹을 테스의 추정된 처지에 더해 주었다.\n\n그들의 관심이 어찌나 깊던지 어린 처녀들은 그녀가 등을 돌렸을 때 속닥였다—\n\n\"쟤 정말 예쁘다. 그리고 저 제일 좋은 옷이 쟤를 얼마나 돋보이게 하는지! 분명 엄청 비쌌을 거야, 그리고 그 사람이 준 선물일 거야.\"\n\n귀퉁이 찬장에서 찻그릇을 꺼내려고 손을 뻗고 있던 테스는 이런 평을 듣지 못했다. 들었더라면 그녀는 친구들의 오해를 곧장 바로잡았을지도 모른다. 그러나 어머니는 들었고, 화려한 결혼의 희망을 빼앗긴 조앤의 단순한 허영심은 화려한 연애 소동의 화젯거리로나마 한껏 자신을 채웠다. 대체로 그녀는 흡족했다. 비록 그런 제한적이고 덧없는 승리가 딸의 평판을 대가로 치를지라도 말이다. 어쩌면 결혼으로 끝날지도 모르는 일이었다. 그리고 그들의 찬탄에 응하는 따뜻함에 겨워, 그녀는 방문객들에게 차를 마시고 가라고 청했다.\n\n그들의 수다, 그들의 웃음, 그들의 짓궂은 농지거리, 무엇보다도 그들의 번득이고 가물거리는 시샘이 테스의 기운도 되살려 주었다. 그리고 저녁이 깊어 가면서, 그녀는 그들의 들뜬 흥분에 전염되어 거의 명랑해졌다. 그녀의 얼굴에서 대리석 같은 굳음이 사라졌고, 그녀는 예전의 통통 튀던 걸음새 비슷한 것으로 움직였으며, 젊은 아름다움을 온통 발그레 물들였다.\n\n이따금, 생각과는 달리, 그녀는 그들의 질문에 우월한 태도로 대답하기도 했다. 마치 구애의 장에서 자신이 겪은 경험이 정말이지 조금은 부러워할 만한 것이었음을 인정하듯이. 그러나 로버트 사우스의 말마따나 \"제 자신의 파멸과 사랑에 빠진\" 것과는 거리가 멀어서, 그 환상은 번개처럼 덧없었다. 차가운 이성이 돌아와 그녀의 발작적인 약함을 비웃었다. 한순간의 자만이 지녔던 섬뜩함이 그녀를 단죄하고, 다시 속을 닫은 무기력으로 그녀를 불러들이곤 했다.\n\n그리고 이튿날 아침 동틀 녘의 낙담이라니. 더 이상 일요일이 아니라 월요일이었고, 가장 좋은 옷도 없었고, 웃던 방문객들도 가 버렸고, 그녀는 순진한 어린 동생들이 그녀 주위에서 부드럽게 숨 쉬는 가운데 옛 침대에서 홀로 깨어났다. 그녀의 귀향이 주던 흥분과 그것이 불러일으킨 관심 대신, 그녀 앞에는 도움도 없이, 동정도 거의 없이 밟아 가야 할 길고 돌투성이인 큰길이 보였다. 그때 그녀의 우울은 끔찍했고, 그녀는 무덤 속에라도 숨어 버리고 싶었다.\n\n몇 주가 지나는 동안 테스는 어느 일요일 아침 교회에 갈 만큼은 모습을 드러낼 정도로 충분히 기운을 차렸다. 그녀는 성가를—그게 어떤 것이든—듣는 것과 오래된 시편을 듣는 것과 아침 찬송에 함께하는 것을 좋아했다. 발라드를 부르던 어머니에게서 물려받은 그 타고난 가락 사랑은, 가장 단순한 음악에도 그녀를 사로잡는 힘을 부여하여 때로는 그녀의 가슴을 몸 밖으로 끌어낼 지경이었다.\n\n그녀 나름의 이유로 되도록 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려고, 그리고 젊은 남자들의 수작을 피하려고, 그녀는 종이 울리기 전에 길을 나서서 회랑 아래 뒷자리, 잡동사니 가까이, 늙은 남녀들만 오는 곳, 교회 묘지 연장들 사이에 관 받침대가 세워져 있는 그곳에 앉았다.\n\n교구민들이 둘씩 셋씩 들어와서는 그녀 앞 줄줄이 자리 잡고, 기도하는 것도 아니면서 기도하는 양 사분의 삼 분쯤 이마를 손에 기댔다가, 몸을 일으켜 주위를 둘러보았다. 성가가 시작되자, 그녀가 좋아하는 곡 하나가 마침 그 가운데 골라졌다—오래된 이중창 성가 \"랭던\"이었다—그러나 그녀는 그 곡의 이름을 몰랐다. 무척 알고 싶었지만 말이다. 그녀는, 정확히 말로 옮기지는 못한 채로, 작곡가의 힘이 얼마나 기이하고 신과 같은 것인지를 생각했다. 무덤 속에서, 처음엔 자기 혼자만 느꼈던 감정의 흐름을 따라, 그의 이름을 들어 본 적도 없고 그의 인격에 대해 단서 하나 결코 얻지 못할 그녀 같은 처녀를 이끌어 갈 수 있다니.\n\n고개를 돌렸던 사람들은 예배가 진행되면서 다시 고개를 돌렸다. 그러다 마침내 그녀를 알아보고는 서로 속닥였다. 그녀는 그들의 속닥임이 무엇에 관한 것인지 알았고, 가슴이 메스꺼워졌으며, 더 이상 교회에 올 수 없겠다고 느꼈다.\n\n동생 몇과 함께 쓰는 침실이 그 어느 때보다도 더 끊임없이 그녀의 은신처가 되었다. 여기, 몇 평방 야드의 초가지붕 아래에서, 그녀는 바람과 눈과 비, 화려한 일몰, 그리고 잇따라 보름달이 차는 것을 지켜보았다. 어찌나 틀어박혀 지냈던지 마침내 거의 모든 사람이 그녀가 떠나간 줄로 여겼다.\n\n이 무렵 테스가 하는 유일한 운동은 어두워진 뒤였다. 그리고 숲속에 나가 있는 그때야말로 그녀가 가장 덜 외로워 보이는 때였다. 그녀는 빛과 어둠이 어찌나 고르게 균형을 이루는지 낮의 속박과 밤의 긴장이 서로를 상쇄하여 절대적인 정신의 자유만 남는, 저녁의 그 순간을 머리카락 한 올의 너비까지도 정확히 맞출 줄 알았다. 바로 그때야말로 살아 있다는 곤경이 가능한 한 가장 작은 차원으로 옅어지는 때였다. 그녀는 그림자를 두려워하지 않았다. 그녀의 유일한 생각은 인간을 피하는 것—아니 차라리 세상이라 불리는 저 차가운 응결물을 피하는 것뿐인 듯했는데, 그것은 무리로는 그토록 무섭지만, 낱낱으로는 그토록 두렵지 않고 심지어 가엾기까지 한 것이었다.\n\n이 외로운 언덕과 골짜기에서 그녀의 고요한 미끄러짐은 그녀가 움직이는 그 환경과 한 조각으로 어우러졌다. 그녀의 유연하고 은밀한 자태는 그 풍경의 본질적인 일부가 되었다. 때때로 그녀의 변덕스러운 공상은 주위의 자연스러운 과정들을 강렬하게 부풀려 그것들이 그녀 자신의 이야기의 일부인 듯 보이게 만들었다. 아니 차라리 그것들은 그 일부가 되었다. 세상이란 오직 심리적 현상일 뿐이고, 그것들이 그렇게 보이는 대로 그것들은 그러했으니까. 겨울 잔가지의 단단히 감긴 봉오리와 껍질 사이에서 신음하는 한밤의 미풍과 돌풍은 쓰라린 책망의 정식이었다. 비 오는 날은 어린 시절의 하느님으로 명확히 분류할 수도 없고 다른 어떤 것으로도 이해할 수 없는, 어떤 막연한 윤리적 존재의 마음속에서, 그녀의 약함에 대한 돌이킬 수 없는 슬픔의 표현이었다.\n\n그러나 관습의 누더기에 기초하고, 그녀에게 적대적인 환영과 목소리들이 들끓는, 그녀 자신을 이렇게 규정짓는 이 에워쌈은 테스의 공상이 빚어낸 가련하고 잘못된 창조물이었다—아무런 까닭도 없이 그녀를 겁먹게 한 도덕적 도깨비들의 구름이었다. 실제 세계와 조화를 이루지 못하는 것은 그녀가 아니라 바로 그것들이었다. 산울타리에서 잠든 새들 사이를 걷거나, 달빛 비친 토끼굴에서 깡충거리는 토끼들을 바라보거나, 꿩이 잔뜩 앉은 나뭇가지 아래 서 있을 때, 그녀는 자신을 죄의 형상이 순결의 안식처에 침입한 것으로 여겼다. 그러나 그러는 내내 그녀는 차이가 없는 곳에서 구별을 짓고 있었다. 자신이 적대 관계에 있다고 느꼈으나, 그녀는 더없이 조화를 이루고 있었다. 그녀는 받아들여진 어떤 사회적 법을 어기게 되었으나, 그녀가 자신을 그런 변칙이라고 상상한 그 환경에 알려진 어떤 법도 어긴 것이 아니었다.\n\n# XIV\n\n팔월의 어느 안개 낀 일출이었다. 더 짙은 밤의 수증기들이 따뜻한 햇살의 공격을 받아 갈라지고 오그라들어, 움푹한 곳과 은폐된 곳마다 외따로 떨어진 양털 뭉치들이 되어, 거기서 흔적도 없이 말라 사라질 때를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n안개 탓에 해는 묘하게도 지각이 있는 듯, 인격적인 모습을 띠고 있어, 그것을 제대로 표현하려면 남성 대명사를 요구했다. 그 지금의 모습은, 풍경 속에 인간의 형상이 일체 없는 것과 맞물려, 옛날의 태양 숭배를 단숨에 설명해 주었다. 하늘 아래 이보다 더 온전한 종교가 일찍이 없었음을 느낄 수 있었다. 그 발광체는 금발에 빛을 내뿜고 온화한 눈을 한 신과 같은 존재로, 젊음의 활기와 열중함으로 자기에게 흥미가 넘쳐흐르는 대지를 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n조금 뒤 그의 빛이 오두막 덧창 틈으로 새어들어, 안의 찬장과 서랍장과 다른 가구들 위에 시뻘겋게 단 부지깽이 같은 줄무늬를 던졌다. 그리고 아직 일어나지 않은 추수꾼들을 깨웠다.\n\n그러나 그날 아침 모든 붉은 것 가운데 가장 밝은 것은 칠을 한 나무로 된 두 개의 넓은 팔이었으니, 그것은 마롯 마을 바로 옆 노란 밀밭의 가장자리에서 솟아 있었다. 그것들은 아래쪽의 다른 두 개와 더불어 수확기의 회전하는 몰타 십자가를 이루었는데, 그 기계는 오늘 작업을 준비하려고 전날 저녁에 밭으로 옮겨져 와 있었다. 그것들에 발린 페인트는 햇빛에 빛깔이 더욱 짙어져, 마치 액체 불에 담갔다 꺼낸 듯한 모습이었다.\n\n밭은 이미 \"열려\" 있었다. 다시 말해, 말들과 기계가 처음 지나갈 수 있도록 밭의 둘레 전체를 따라 밀 사이로 몇 피트 너비의 길이 손으로 베어져 있었다.\n\n남자들과 사내아이들로 이루어진 무리 하나와 여자들로 이루어진 다른 무리 하나가, 마침 동쪽 산울타리 꼭대기의 그림자가 서쪽 산울타리 중간께를 때리는 그 시각에 길을 따라 내려와서, 무리들의 머리는 일출을 누리는 동안 발은 아직 새벽녘에 잠겨 있었다. 그들은 가장 가까운 밭문 양옆에 선 두 개의 돌기둥 사이로 길에서 사라졌다.\n\n이윽고 안쪽에서 메뚜기의 구애 같은 째깍거리는 소리가 일어났다. 기계가 시작된 것이었고, 말 세 마리와 앞서 말한 길고 삐걱대는 기계가 움직이며 한데 엮인 모습이 문 너머로 보였다. 끄는 말 한 마리에 마부가 올라타 있었고, 기구의 좌석에는 보조 한 사람이 앉아 있었다. 밭의 한쪽 가장자리를 따라 짐수레 전체가 나아갔고, 기계 수확기의 팔들이 천천히 회전하다가, 언덕 아래로 지나가 완전히 시야에서 사라졌다. 잠시 후 그것은 같은 한결같은 속도로 밭의 반대편에서 올라왔다. 앞선 말의 이마에 달린 반짝이는 놋쇠 별이 그루터기 위로 솟아오르며 먼저 눈에 들어왔고, 그다음 밝은 팔들이, 그다음 기계 전체가 보였다.\n\n밭을 에워싼 좁은 그루터기 길은 한 바퀴 돌 때마다 넓어졌고, 서 있는 밀은 아침이 깊어 갈수록 점점 작은 면적으로 줄어들었다. 토끼며 산토끼며 뱀이며 쥐며 생쥐가, 자기네 피난처의 덧없는 성질도, 그리고 그날 늦게 닥칠 운명도 모른 채, 요새로 들어가듯 안쪽으로 물러났다. 그날 늦게, 그들의 은신처가 점점 더 끔찍하게 좁아져 들면, 적과 동지가 한데 옹송그려 모이게 되고, 마침내 마지막 몇 야드 남은 곧추선 밀마저 빗나감 없는 수확기의 이빨 아래 쓰러지면, 추수꾼들의 막대기와 돌멩이에 그들은 하나도 빠짐없이 죽임을 당하게 될 터였다.\n\n수확기는 쓰러진 밀을 작은 더미로 뒤에 남겼는데, 더미마다 한 단이 될 만한 양이었다. 그리고 그 위에 뒤따르던 부지런한 결속꾼들이 손을 댔다—주로 여자들이었지만, 더러는 사라사 셔츠를 입고 허리께를 가죽끈으로 동여 바지를 받친 남자들도 있었으니, 그 바람에 뒤의 단추 두 개가 쓸모없어져, 움직일 때마다 햇살에 반짝이며 곤두서서, 마치 등허리에 박힌 한 쌍의 눈 같았다.\n\n그러나 이 결속꾼 무리 가운데 가장 흥미로운 것은 다른 성, 즉 여자들이었으니, 여자가 야외 자연의 한 부분이 되어, 보통 때처럼 그저 거기 놓인 사물에 그치지 않게 될 때 얻는 매력 때문이었다. 들판의 남자는 들판에서 하나의 인격이지만, 들판의 여자는 들판의 일부분이었다. 그녀는 어쩐지 제 자신의 윤곽을 잃고, 주위의 정수를 빨아들여, 그것과 자신을 동화시켜 버린 것이다.\n\n여자들은—아니 차라리 처녀들이라 해야겠다, 대부분 젊었으니까—햇볕을 막으려고 펄럭이는 커다란 차양이 달린, 끈으로 조인 무명 보닛을 쓰고, 손이 그루터기에 다치지 않게 장갑을 끼고 있었다. 한 사람은 연분홍 재킷을 입었고, 또 한 사람은 크림빛의 소매가 꼭 끼는 가운을 입었으며, 또 한 사람은 수확기의 팔처럼 새빨간 페티코트를 입었다. 그리고 더 나이 든 다른 이들은 갈색의 거친 \"통옷\" 즉 작업복을 입었으니—오래전부터 자리 잡은, 들판 여자에게 가장 알맞은 옷이건만, 젊은 처녀들은 그것을 버려 가고 있었다. 이날 아침 눈길은 절로 분홍 무명 재킷을 입은 처녀에게 되돌아간다. 그녀가 그들 가운데 가장 유연하고 곱게 빠진 자태였기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀의 보닛이 이마 위로 어찌나 깊이 당겨져 있던지 결속하는 동안에는 얼굴이 조금도 드러나지 않았다. 다만 보닛 차양 아래로 흘러나온 짙은 갈색 머리칼 한두 가닥으로 그녀의 살빛을 짐작할 수는 있었다. 어쩌면 그녀가 무심한 시선을 끄는 한 가지 이유는 다른 여자들은 자주 주위를 둘러보는데 그녀는 결코 그것을 구하지 않기 때문이리라.\n\n그녀의 결속은 시계 같은 단조로움으로 진행되었다. 마지막으로 끝낸 단에서 그녀는 한 움큼의 이삭을 뽑아, 왼쪽 손바닥으로 그 끝을 토닥여 고르게 했다. 그러고는 몸을 낮게 숙여 앞으로 나아가며, 두 손으로 밀을 무릎에 대고 그러모은 다음, 장갑 낀 왼손을 다발 밑으로 밀어 넣어 반대편의 오른손과 맞닿게 하여, 연인을 끌어안듯 밀을 품에 안았다. 그녀는 매듭끈의 양 끝을 한데 모으고, 단 위에 무릎을 꿇고 그것을 묶었으며, 미풍에 치마가 들릴 때마다 이따금 치마를 쳐서 내렸다. 손목을 감싼 담황색 가죽 토시와 가운 소매 사이로 맨팔 한 자락이 보였다. 그리고 하루가 깊어 갈수록 그 여성스러운 매끄러움은 그루터기에 긁혀 상처가 나고 피가 났다.\n\n이따금 그녀는 몸을 일으켜 쉬면서, 흐트러진 앞치마를 다시 매거나 보닛을 바로 당겼다. 그러면 깊고 검은 눈과 길고 묵직하게 들러붙는 머리채를 가진 어여쁜 젊은 여인의 갸름한 얼굴이 보였는데, 그 머리채는 무엇에든 닿으면 애원하듯 그것을 끌어안는 듯했다. 뺨은 시골에서 자란 처녀에게 흔한 것보다 더 창백했고, 이는 더 가지런했으며, 붉은 입술은 더 가늘었다.\n\n그것은 테스 더비필드, 달리 말하면 더버빌이었으니, 다소 달라져 있었다—같은 사람이면서도 같은 사람이 아니었다. 그녀가 있는 곳이 낯선 땅이 아니었음에도, 그녀의 지금 삶의 단계에서 그녀는 여기서 이방인이자 타지인으로 살고 있었다. 오랜 칩거 끝에 그녀는 고향 마을에서 야외 일을 떠맡기로 결심했으니, 농사 세계에서 일 년 중 가장 바쁜 철이 왔고, 그녀가 집 안에서 할 수 있는 어떤 일도 당장은 밭에서 추수하는 것만큼 벌이가 되지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n다른 여자들의 동작도 테스의 것과 어느 정도 비슷했으니, 한 사람 한 사람이 한 단을 끝낼 때마다 무리 전체가 사방춤을 추는 무용수들처럼 한데 모여들어, 저마다 제 단을 다른 이들의 단에 기대 세우다가, 마침내 여남은 개로 이루어진 한 무더기, 여기서 부르는 말로 \"낟가리\"가 만들어졌다.\n\n그들은 아침을 먹으러 갔다가 다시 왔고, 일은 전과 같이 진행되었다. 열한 시가 가까워지자, 그녀를 지켜보는 사람이라면 이따금 테스의 눈길이 단 묶는 일을 멈추지는 않으면서도 애틋하게 언덕 마루로 흘끔거리는 것을 알아챘을지도 모른다. 그 시각이 다 되어 갈 무렵, 여섯 살에서 열네 살에 이르는 한 무리의 아이들 머리가 그루터기 덮인 언덕의 볼록한 등성이 위로 솟아올랐다.\n\n테스의 얼굴이 살짝 붉어졌으나, 그래도 그녀는 멈추지 않았다.\n\n오는 이들 가운데 맏이인, 삼각형 숄을 두르고 그 모서리를 그루터기에 질질 끌고 오는 한 처녀가, 첫눈에는 인형처럼 보였으나 알고 보니 긴 배내옷을 입은 갓난아기인 것을 두 팔에 안고 있었다. 또 한 아이는 점심을 좀 가져왔다. 추수꾼들은 일손을 멈추고 음식을 챙겨, 낟가리 하나에 기대 앉았다. 여기서 그들은 먹기 시작했는데, 남자들은 돌 단지를 거리낌 없이 들이켜며 잔을 돌렸다.\n\n테스 더비필드는 일손을 멈춘 마지막 사람 중 하나였다. 그녀는 낟가리 끝에 앉았는데, 얼굴을 동료들에게서 다소 돌린 채였다. 그녀가 자리를 잡자, 토끼 가죽 모자를 쓰고 허리띠에 붉은 손수건을 찔러 넣은 한 남자가 그녀가 마시도록 에일 잔을 낟가리 너머로 내밀었다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 권유를 받아들이지 않았다. 점심을 펼쳐 놓자마자 그녀는 그 큰 처녀, 곧 여동생을 불러 아기를 받아 들었고, 짐을 덜게 되어 기뻐한 여동생은 다음 낟가리로 가서 거기서 놀고 있는 다른 아이들과 어울렸다. 테스는, 묘하게 은밀하면서도 용감한 동작으로, 그리고 여전히 붉어 오르는 낯빛으로, 옷을 풀고 아기에게 젖을 물리기 시작했다.\n\n가장 가까이 앉은 남자들은 사려 깊게 얼굴을 밭의 반대편 끝으로 돌렸고, 더러는 담배를 피우기 시작했다. 한 사람은 멍하니 애착을 담아, 더는 한 줄기도 내주지 않을 단지를 아쉬운 듯 쓰다듬었다. 테스를 제외한 모든 여자가 활기찬 이야기에 빠져들어, 흐트러진 머리 매듭을 다듬었다.\n\n아기가 배불리 먹자, 젊은 어머니는 무릎 위에 아기를 똑바로 앉히고, 먼 곳을 바라보며, 거의 싫어하는 것에 가까운 침울한 무심함으로 아기를 어르다가, 그러다 갑자기 결코 그칠 수 없다는 듯 격렬하게 수십 번 입을 맞추기 시작했고, 격정과 경멸이 기이하게 뒤섞인 그 맹렬함에 아기는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"쟤는 저 애를 미워하는 척하고, 저랑 아기랑 둘 다 교회 묘지에 있었으면 좋겠다고 말은 해도, 사실은 저 애를 끔찍이 아껴,\" 붉은 페티코트를 입은 여자가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"곧 그런 말 안 하게 될 거야,\" 담황색 옷을 입은 여자가 대꾸했다. \"원, 사람이 그런 걸 시간이 지나면 어떻게든 익숙해지는 게 참 신기하다니까!\"\n\n\"설득보다 좀 더한 게 그 일이 생기는 데 작용했지 싶어. 작년에 어느 날 밤 체이스 숲에서 흐느끼는 소리를 들은 이들이 있었거든. 그리고 사람들이 마침 지나갔더라면 어떤 자한테는 일이 험하게 됐을지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"뭐, 좀 더했든 좀 덜했든, 하필이면 다른 누구도 아닌 쟤한테 그런 일이 생기다니 천 번 만 번 안됐어. 하지만 늘 제일 고운 애한테 그런 일이 생긴다니까! 못생긴 애들은 교회처럼 안전하지—그렇지, 제니?\" 말한 여자는 무리 가운데 한 사람을 돌아보았는데, 그 사람은 분명 못생겼다고 해도 부족함이 없는 이였다.\n\n정말이지 천 번 만 번 안된 일이었다. 거기 앉아 있는 테스를 바라보면, 적이라 해도 달리 느끼기란 불가능했다. 꽃 같은 입과 크고 부드러운 눈—검지도 푸르지도 잿빛도 보랏빛도 아닌, 차라리 그 모든 빛깔이 한데 어우러진 데다, 그 홍채를 들여다보면 보이는 또 다른 백 가지 빛깔—빛깔 뒤에 빛깔, 색조 너머 색조, 바닥이 없는 동공을 둘러싼—거의 표준이라 할 만한 여인이었다. 그녀의 혈통에서 물려받은 성격의 사소한 경솔함만 아니었다면 말이다.\n\n그녀 자신도 놀란 어떤 결심이 그녀를 여러 달 만에 처음으로 이번 주에 밭으로 데려온 것이었다. 외로운 미숙함이 짜낼 수 있는 온갖 후회의 기관으로 두근거리는 가슴을 닳리고 소모한 끝에, 상식이 그녀를 밝혀 주었다. 그녀는 다시 쓸모 있는 사람이 되는 게—어떤 대가를 치르더라도 다시금 달콤한 독립을 맛보는 게 좋겠다고 느꼈다. 과거는 과거였다. 그것이 어떠했든, 더는 코앞에 있지 않았다. 그 결과가 무엇이든, 시간이 그 위를 덮어 버릴 터였다. 몇 년만 지나면 그것들은 다 처음부터 없었던 것처럼 될 테고, 그녀 자신도 풀 아래 묻혀 잊힐 터였다. 그러는 사이에도 나무들은 예전과 똑같이 푸르렀고, 새들은 노래했고, 해는 여전히 변함없이 환하게 빛났다. 낯익은 주위 풍경은 그녀의 슬픔 때문에 어두워지지도 않았고, 그녀의 고통 때문에 병들지도 않았다.\n\n그녀의 고개를 그토록 깊이 숙이게 한 것—제 처지에 대한 세상의 관심이라는 생각—이 환상에 기초한 것임을, 그녀는 깨달았어야 했다. 그녀는 자기 자신을 제외한 그 누구에게도 하나의 존재도, 하나의 경험도, 하나의 열정도, 하나의 감각의 구조물도 아니었다. 그 밖의 온 인류에게 테스는 그저 스쳐 가는 생각 하나일 뿐이었다. 친구들에게조차 그녀는 자주 스쳐 가는 생각 이상은 아니었다. 그녀가 밤낮 내내 자신을 비참하게 만든다 한들, 그들에게는 고작 이만큼이었다—\"아, 쟤가 자기를 불행하게 만드는구나.\" 그녀가 명랑하려 애쓰고, 온갖 근심을 떨치고, 햇빛과 꽃과 아기에서 기쁨을 누리려 한다 한들, 그들에게 그녀는 고작 이런 생각일 수 있을 뿐이었다—\"아, 쟤가 그걸 아주 잘 견디는구나.\" 게다가 무인도에 홀로 있었더라면 그녀에게 닥친 일로 인해 비참해졌을까? 그리 심하지는 않았을 것이다. 만약 그녀가 이제 막 창조되어, 이름 없는 아이의 어버이라는 것 말고는 삶에 대한 아무런 경험도 없이, 남편 없는 어머니인 자신을 발견하게 되었다면, 그 처지가 그녀를 절망케 했을까? 아니, 그녀는 그것을 차분히 받아들이고 거기서 기쁨을 찾았을 것이다. 비참함의 대부분은 그녀의 타고난 감각이 아니라 그녀의 관습적인 겉모습이 만들어 낸 것이었다.\n\n테스의 추론이 무엇이었든, 어떤 정신이 그녀를 부추겨 예전에 그랬던 것처럼 말끔하게 차려입고 밭으로 나오게 했으니, 마침 그때 추수 일손이 무척 모자라던 참이었다. 이것이 그녀가 위엄을 지니고 처신하며, 심지어 아기를 품에 안고 있을 때조차도 이따금 사람들의 얼굴을 차분히 마주 볼 수 있었던 까닭이다.\n\n추수꾼 남자들이 밀 낟가리에서 일어나 팔다리를 쭉 펴고 담뱃대를 껐다. 마구를 풀고 먹이를 주었던 말들이 다시 진홍빛 기계에 매였다. 테스는 제 식사를 얼른 마치고는 맏여동생에게 와서 아기를 데려가라고 손짓하고, 옷을 여미고, 담황색 장갑을 다시 끼고, 다음 단을 묶을 매듭끈을 마지막으로 끝낸 단에서 뽑으려고 다시 몸을 숙였다.\n\n오후와 저녁에도 아침의 일이 이어졌고, 테스는 추수꾼 무리와 함께 해 질 녘까지 남아 있었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 모두 가장 큰 짐마차 한 대에 올라타 집으로 향했는데, 동쪽 땅에서 떠오른 넓고 빛바랜 달이 동행했으니, 그 얼굴은 좀먹은 토스카나 성인의 닳아빠진 금박 후광을 닮아 있었다. 테스의 여자 동료들은 노래를 불렀고, 그녀가 다시 바깥에 모습을 드러낸 것을 무척 반기며 살갑게 굴었다. 다만 즐거운 푸른 숲에 갔다가 달라진 처지가 되어 돌아온 처녀에 관한 발라드의 몇 소절을 짓궂게 끼워 넣는 것만은 참지 못했다. 인생에는 균형추와 보상이 있는 법이다. 그녀를 사회적 경계의 본보기로 만든 그 사건은, 동시에 한순간 그녀를 많은 이에게 마을에서 가장 흥미로운 인물로 만들기도 했다. 그들의 다정함은 그녀를 자기 자신에게서 더욱 멀어지게 했고, 그들의 발랄한 기운은 전염성이 있어서, 그녀는 거의 명랑해졌다.\n\n그러나 이제 그녀의 도덕적 슬픔이 지나가자, 어떤 사회적 법도 알지 못하는 그녀의 자연스러운 쪽에서 새로운 슬픔이 일어났다. 집에 다다랐을 때 그녀는 슬프게도 아기가 오후부터 갑자기 병이 났다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그토록 여리고 자그마한 몸이었으니 그런 쇠약은 일어남 직한 일이었다. 그럼에도 그 일은 충격으로 다가왔다.\n\n아기가 세상에 나옴으로써 사회에 저지른 죄는 그 어린 어머니의 머릿속에서 잊혔다. 그녀 영혼의 바람은 아이의 목숨을 지킴으로써 그 죄를 이어 가는 것이었다. 그러나 곧 분명해졌으니, 그 작은 육체의 죄수에게 해방의 시각이 그녀의 가장 나쁜 걱정이 짐작했던 것보다 더 일찍 오리라는 것이었다. 그리고 이를 알아챘을 때 그녀는 아이를 잃는 단순한 슬픔을 넘어서는 비참함 속으로 빠져들었다. 그녀의 아기는 세례를 받지 못했던 것이다.\n\n테스는, 만약 자신이 저지른 일로 인해 불타야 한다면 불타야지, 그걸로 끝이라는 생각을 수동적으로 받아들이는 마음 상태로 흘러들어 가 있었다. 모든 시골 처녀와 마찬가지로 그녀는 성경에 단단히 기초가 잡혀 있어서, 아홀라와 아홀리바의 이야기를 충실히 공부했고, 거기서 끌어낼 교훈을 알고 있었다. 그러나 같은 물음이 아기와 관련해 일어나자, 그것은 매우 다른 빛깔을 띠었다. 그녀의 귀염둥이가 죽어 가려는데, 구원이 없었다.\n\n거의 잠자리에 들 시각이었으나, 그녀는 아래층으로 달려 내려가 목사님을 부르러 보내도 되겠냐고 물었다. 마침 그 순간은 아버지가 가문의 옛 귀족됨에 대한 자부심이 가장 드높고, 테스가 그 귀족됨에 묻혀 놓은 오점에 대한 예민함이 가장 두드러진 때였으니, 그가 막 롤리버 여관에서의 주간 술추렴을 마치고 돌아온 참이었기 때문이다. 어떤 목사도 자기 집 문 안에 들여 제 일을 캐고 다니게 할 수 없다고, 그는 선언했다. 그것도 하필 그녀의 수치로 인해 그 일을 숨기는 것이 그 어느 때보다 더 절실해진 지금에 말이다. 그는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n식구들은 잠자리에 들었고, 한없이 괴로운 테스도 물러나 누웠다. 그녀는 누워서 끊임없이 잠을 깼고, 한밤중에 아기가 더 나빠진 것을 알아챘다. 아기는 분명 죽어 가고 있었다—조용히, 고통 없이, 그러나 그렇다고 덜 확실하지도 않게.\n\n비참함에 빠진 그녀는 침대 위에서 몸을 흔들었다. 시계가 한 시라는 엄숙한 시각을 알렸으니, 공상이 이성 밖에서 활보하고 악의에 찬 가능성들이 사실인 양 바위처럼 굳건히 서 있는 그 시각이었다. 그녀는 세례를 받지 못하고 사생아라는 이중의 단죄로 인해 지옥의 가장 밑바닥 구석에 보내진 아이를 떠올렸다. 마왕이, 빵 굽는 날 화덕을 데울 때 쓰는 것 같은 세 갈래 쇠스랑으로 아이를 던져 대는 모습을 보았다. 이 그림에다 그녀는, 이 기독교 나라에서 어린아이들에게 더러 가르치는 갖가지 기괴하고 별스러운 고문의 세부 사항을 보태었다. 잠든 집안의 정적 속에서 그 섬뜩한 상상이 어찌나 강하게 그녀의 머릿속을 사로잡던지, 잠옷이 땀에 축축해지고, 심장이 고동칠 때마다 침대가 흔들렸다.\n\n아기의 숨이 점점 더 가빠졌고, 어머니의 정신적 긴장은 더해 갔다. 그 작은 것을 입맞춤으로 삼켜 버리려 해 봐야 소용없었다. 그녀는 더 이상 침대에 있을 수 없어 방 안을 열에 들떠 서성였다.\n\n\"오 자비로우신 하느님, 불쌍히 여기소서. 제 가엾은 아기를 불쌍히 여기소서!\" 그녀가 부르짖었다. \"저한테는 얼마든지 원하시는 만큼 노여움을 퍼부으셔도 좋습니다. 하지만 이 아이만은 불쌍히 여기소서!\"\n\n그녀는 서랍장에 기대어, 한참 동안 두서없는 간구를 중얼거리다가, 갑자기 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"아! 어쩌면 아기를 구할 수 있을지도 몰라! 어쩌면 똑같을지도 몰라!\"\n\n그녀는 어찌나 밝게 말했던지 그녀를 둘러싼 어둠 속에서 얼굴이 빛났을 듯도 싶었다. 그녀는 촛불을 켜고, 벽 아래 두 번째와 세 번째 침대로 가서, 어린 누이들과 동생들을 깨웠으니, 그들은 모두 같은 방을 쓰고 있었다. 세면대 뒤로 갈 수 있도록 그것을 끌어낸 다음, 그녀는 물병에서 물을 좀 따르고, 그들을 둘러 무릎 꿇리고는, 손가락을 똑바로 곧추세워 두 손을 한데 모으게 했다. 거의 잠도 깨지 못한 아이들이, 그녀의 태도에 경외감에 사로잡혀, 눈이 점점 더 커지면서, 이 자세 그대로 있는 동안, 그녀는 침대에서 아기를—아이가 낳은 아이를—데려왔으니, 너무도 미성숙하여 그 낳은 이에게 어머니라는 칭호를 부여하기에 충분한 인격으로조차 보이지 않을 정도였다. 그러고 나서 테스는 대야 옆에 아기를 팔에 안고 똑바로 섰다. 바로 아랫동생이, 교회의 서기가 목사 앞에 들고 있듯이, 기도서를 펼쳐 그녀 앞에 들었다. 이렇게 하여 그 처녀는 자기 아이에게 세례를 베풀기 시작했다.\n\n긴 흰 잠옷을 입고 선 그녀의 자태는 유난히 크고 위엄 있어 보였고, 비비 꼰 짙은 머리채의 굵은 밧줄이 등 뒤로 허리까지 곧장 늘어져 있었다. 약한 촛불의 다정한 어스름이 그녀의 몸과 이목구비에서, 햇빛이라면 드러냈을 자잘한 흠들을—손목의 그루터기 긁힌 자국과 눈의 피로를—덜어 내 주었다. 그녀의 드높은 열정이 그녀를 파멸시켰던 그 얼굴에 변모시키는 효과를 미쳐, 거의 왕에 어울릴 위엄의 기운을 띤, 티 없이 깨끗한 아름다움의 것으로 그것을 보여 주었다. 둘러 무릎 꿇은 어린것들이, 졸린 눈을 빨갛게 깜박이며, 그녀의 준비를 기다렸으니, 그 시각의 몸의 무거움이 활발해지도록 놔두지 않는 멈춰진 경이로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n그들 가운데 가장 감명받은 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"테스, 정말로 얘한테 세례를 줄 거야?\"\n\n그 처녀 어머니는 진지하게 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"얘 이름은 뭐가 될 거야?\"\n\n그녀는 그것을 생각해 두지 않았으나, 세례 의식을 진행하는 동안 창세기의 한 구절에서 떠오른 이름이 머리에 떠올랐고, 이제 그녀는 그것을 입에 올렸다.\n\n\"소로우, 나는 성부와 성자와 성령의 이름으로 네게 세례를 주노라.\"\n\n그녀가 물을 뿌렸고, 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"'아멘' 하렴, 얘들아.\"\n\n작은 목소리들이 순종하여 응답으로 가늘게 울렸다. \"아멘!\"\n\n테스는 계속했다.\n\n\"우리는 이 아이를 받아들이며\"—따위를 이어 가다가—\"십자가 표시로 그를 표하노라.\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 대야에 손을 담가, 집게손가락으로 아기에게 열렬히 커다란 십자가를 그렸고, 그가 죄와 세상과 악마에 맞서 사내답게 싸우며, 제 목숨 다하는 날까지 충실한 군사이자 종이 되리라는 관례적인 문구들을 이어 갔다. 그녀는 주기도문을 차례대로 진행했고, 아이들이 가는 모깃소리 같은 울음으로 그녀를 따라 더듬더듬 읊다가, 마지막에 이르러서는 목소리를 서기의 음높이로 높여, 다시 가늘게 침묵 속으로 울렸다. \"아멘!\"\n\n그러자 그들의 누나는, 그 성사의 효력에 대한 한껏 커진 확신으로, 그 뒤에 따르는 감사기도를 가슴 밑바닥에서부터 쏟아 냈으니, 마음이 말에 실릴 때 그녀의 목소리가 얻는 그 막힌 디아파종 음으로 담대하고 의기양양하게 그것을 읊었으니, 그녀를 아는 이들은 결코 잊지 못할 음이었다. 신앙의 황홀경이 그녀를 거의 신격화하여, 그녀의 얼굴에 빛나는 광채를 드리우고 양 뺨 한가운데에 붉은 점을 가져왔다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 눈동자에 거꾸로 비친 자그마한 촛불의 불꽃이 다이아몬드처럼 빛났다. 아이들은 점점 더 깊은 경외심으로 그녀를 올려다보았고, 더는 물을 마음이 없어졌다. 이제 그들에게 그녀는 누나처럼 보이지 않고, 크고 우뚝 솟은 두려운 존재로—그들과 아무 공통점도 없는 신성한 인물로 보였다.\n\n가엾은 소로우가 죄와 세상과 악마에 맞서 벌이는 싸움은 제한된 광휘에 그칠 운명이었다—어쩌면 그 시작을 생각하면 그 자신에게는 다행한 일이었으리라. 아침의 푸르름 속에서 그 연약한 군사이자 종은 마지막 숨을 거두었고, 다른 아이들이 깨어났을 때 그들은 비통하게 울며, 또 예쁜 아기를 낳아 달라고 누나를 졸랐다.\n\n세례를 베푼 뒤로 테스를 사로잡았던 평온함은 아기를 잃고 나서도 그녀에게 남아 있었다. 정말이지 대낮에는 그의 영혼에 대한 자신의 공포가 다소 과장되었던 것이라 느껴졌다. 근거가 있었든 없었든, 이제 그녀는 아무런 불안이 없었으니, 만약 섭리가 그런 비슷한 행위를 인준하지 않는다면, 적어도 그녀로서는 그 변칙으로 인해 잃은 그런 종류의 천국을—자기 자신을 위해서든 자기 아이를 위해서든—귀하게 여기지 않겠노라 따지면서였다.\n\n이렇게 하여 원치 않았던 소로우는 세상을 떠났다—그 침입자 같은 존재, 사회적 법을 존중하지 않는 파렴치한 자연이 준 사생아 선물, 영원의 시간이 고작 며칠에 지나지 않았던 떠돌이, 해니 세기니 하는 것들이 일찍이 있었다는 것조차 알지 못한 자, 그에게는 오두막 안이 우주요, 일주일의 날씨가 기후요, 갓 태어난 갓난아기 시절이 인간의 일생이요, 빨려는 본능이 인간의 지식이었던 자였다.\n\n세례에 대해 적잖이 골똘히 생각하던 테스는, 그것이 교리적으로 아이에게 기독교식 매장을 보장하기에 충분한지 궁금했다. 이것은 교구의 목사 말고는 아무도 말해 줄 수 없었는데, 그는 새로 온 사람이라 그녀를 알지 못했다. 그녀는 해 질 녘 그의 집에 가서 문 옆에 섰으나, 안으로 들어갈 용기를 낼 수 없었다. 돌아서던 차에 우연히 집으로 오던 그를 마주치지 않았더라면 이 일은 포기되었을 것이다. 어스름 속이라 그녀는 거리낌 없이 말하는 것이 꺼려지지 않았다.\n\n\"여쭤 보고 싶은 게 있어요, 목사님.\"\n\n그가 기꺼이 들어 주겠다고 하자, 그녀는 아기의 병과 즉석에서 치른 의식의 이야기를 들려주었다. \"그런데 목사님,\" 그녀가 간절하게 덧붙였다. \"이것 좀 말씀해 주세요—목사님이 세례를 주신 것과 그 애한테 똑같을까요?\"\n\n자기가 불려 갔어야 할 일을 손님들끼리 서투르게 그르쳐 놓은 것을 알게 된 장사꾼의 자연스러운 심정으로, 그는 아니라고 말하고 싶어졌다. 그러나 그 처녀의 위엄, 그 목소리에 담긴 묘한 부드러움이 어우러져 그의 더 고귀한 충동을—아니 차라리 실제의 회의 위에 형식적인 신앙을 접붙이려 십 년을 애쓴 끝에 그에게 남아 있던 그것을—움직였다. 그의 안에서 인간과 성직자가 다투었고, 승리는 인간에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 그가 말했다. \"똑같을 게다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 애한테 기독교식 매장을 해 주시겠어요?\" 그녀가 재빨리 물었다.\n\n목사는 자신이 궁지에 몰린 것을 느꼈다. 아기가 병들었다는 소식을 듣고, 그는 양심에 따라 밤이 깊은 뒤 그 의식을 행하러 집으로 갔었는데, 그를 들이지 않은 거절이 테스가 아니라 테스의 아버지에게서 나온 것임을 알지 못한 채라, 그 변칙적 거행에 대해 부득이했다는 변명을 받아들일 수 없었다.\n\n\"아—그건 다른 문제다,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"다른 문제라니요—왜요?\" 테스가 다소 발끈하여 물었다.\n\n\"음—우리 둘만의 일이라면 내 기꺼이 그리하겠다. 하지만 그래서는 안 된다—어떤 이유들 때문에.\"\n\n\"딱 한 번만요, 목사님!\"\n\n\"정말로 그래서는 안 된다.\"\n\n\"오 목사님!\" 그녀는 말하면서 그의 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n그가 고개를 저으며 손을 빼냈다.\n\n\"그럼 전 목사님이 싫어요!\" 그녀가 터뜨렸다. \"그리고 목사님 교회엔 다신 안 갈 거예요!\"\n\n\"그리 경솔하게 말하지 마라.\"\n\n\"목사님이 안 해 주셔도 그 애한테는 똑같지 않을까요?... 똑같을까요? 제발 성인이 죄인에게 말하듯 하지 마시고, 목사님 그 자신으로서 저 자신에게—이 가엾은 저에게 말씀해 주세요!\"\n\n자신이 이런 문제들에 관해 견지하고 있다고 여기는 엄격한 관념과 자신의 대답을 목사가 어떻게 조화시켰는지는 평신도의 능력으로 헤아릴 수 없는 일이다. 변명할 수 없는 일은 아니지만 말이다. 다소 마음이 움직인 그는, 이 경우에도 이렇게 말했다—\n\n\"똑같을 게다.\"\n\n그리하여 아기는 그날 밤 작은 전나무 상자에 담겨, 어느 늙은 여인의 숄에 싸여 교회 묘지로 옮겨졌고, 묘지기에게 치른 일 실링과 맥주 한 파인트의 값으로, 등불 빛에 의지해 묻혔으니, 하느님께서 쐐기풀이 자라도록 내버려 두시는 그분 땅의 그 초라한 구석, 세례받지 못한 모든 갓난아기와 소문난 술꾼들과 자살한 자들과 그 밖에 단죄받았으리라 짐작되는 이들이 묻히는 곳이었다. 그러나 그 불길한 주변에도 불구하고, 테스는 용감하게 욋가지 두 개와 노끈 한 가닥으로 작은 십자가를 만들었고, 그것을 꽃으로 묶은 다음, 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 교회 묘지에 들어갈 수 있었던 어느 저녁에 무덤 머리맡에 세웠으며, 발치에도 같은 꽃 한 다발을 물이 든 작은 단지에 담아, 꽃이 살아 있도록 두었다. 그 단지 바깥에 한낱 관찰의 눈이 \"킬웰스 마멀레이드\"라는 글자를 알아챈들 그게 무슨 상관이랴? 어머니의 사랑의 눈은 더 높은 것들을 바라보는 그 시야 속에서 그것들을 보지 못했다.\n\n# XV\n\n\"경험을 통해,\" 로저 애스컴은 말한다. \"우리는 긴 헤맴으로 지름길을 찾아낸다.\" 그 긴 헤맴이 우리를 더 이상의 여행에 부적합하게 만드는 일도 적지 않으니, 그렇다면 우리의 경험이 그때 우리에게 무슨 소용이랴? 테스 더비필드의 경험은 바로 이런 무력화시키는 종류의 것이었다. 마침내 그녀는 무엇을 해야 할지 배웠으나, 이제 누가 그녀가 하는 것을 받아 주려 하겠는가?\n\n더버빌가에 가기 전에, 만약 그녀가 자신과 세상 일반에 알려진 갖가지 격언 같은 성구와 문구의 인도 아래 힘차게 움직였더라면, 의심할 바 없이 그녀는 결코 속아 넘어가지 않았으리라. 그러나 황금 같은 견해의 온전한 진리를 그것으로부터 이득을 볼 수 있는 동안에 느끼는 것은 테스의 능력 밖이었으며—누구의 능력 밖이기도 하다. 그녀는—그리고 얼마나 더 많은 이가—성 아우구스티누스와 함께 하느님께 빈정대듯 이렇게 말했을지도 모른다. \"당신은 허락하신 것보다 더 나은 길을 권하셨나이다.\"\n\n그녀는 겨울 동안 아버지의 집에 머무르며, 닭의 깃털을 뽑거나, 칠면조와 거위를 살찌우거나, 더버빌이 그녀에게 준 어떤 고운 옷가지—경멸하며 따로 치워 두었던 것—로 동생들의 옷을 지었다. 그에게 부탁하는 일만은 하지 않으려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 열심히 일하는 줄로 여겨질 때 자주 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 끼고 골똘히 생각에 잠기곤 했다.\n\n그녀는 한 해의 순환 속에서 지나가는 날짜들을 철학적으로 헤아렸다. 체이스 숲의 어두운 배경을 거느린 트랜트리지에서의 파멸의 그 재앙적인 밤, 또 아기가 태어나고 죽은 날짜, 또 그녀 자신의 생일, 그리고 그녀가 얼마간 관여했던 사건들로 저마다 따로 떼어진 그 밖의 모든 날. 어느 날 오후, 거울에 비친 자신의 고움을 들여다보다가, 그녀는 문득 생각했으니, 그것들보다 그녀에게 더 중요한 또 하나의 날짜가 있다는 것을. 곧 이 모든 매력이 사라지고 없을, 자기 자신이 죽는 날을. 한 해의 다른 모든 날 사이에 음흉하게, 보이지 않게 숨어 있어, 그녀가 해마다 그 위를 지나갈 때 아무런 표시도 소리도 내지 않지만, 그렇다고 거기 분명히 있지 않은 것은 아닌 그날을. 그것은 언제일까? 그토록 차가운 친척과 해마다 마주치면서 왜 그녀는 그 한기를 느끼지 못하는 것일까? 그녀는 제러미 테일러의 생각을 품고 있었으니, 언젠가 미래에 그녀를 알던 이들이 이렇게 말하리라는 것이었다. \"○○일이지, 가엾은 테스 더비필드가 죽은 날이.\" 그리고 그들의 마음에는 그 말에 별스러운 데가 조금도 없으리라는 것이었다. 모든 세월을 통틀어 시간 속에서 그녀의 종착점이 될 운명인 그날에 대해, 그녀는 그것이 달과 주와 계절과 해의 어느 자리에 있는지 알지 못했다.\n\n이렇게 거의 단숨에 테스는 단순한 처녀에서 복잡한 여인으로 변했다. 사색의 표징들이 그녀의 얼굴로 스며들었고, 이따금 비극의 가락이 그녀의 목소리로 스며들었다. 그녀의 눈은 더 커지고 더 웅변적이 되었다. 그녀는 고운 사람이라 불릴 만한 무언가가 되었다. 그녀의 모습은 아름답고 사람을 사로잡았다. 그녀의 영혼은 지난 한두 해의 격동적인 경험들이 도무지 타락시키지 못한 한 여인의 영혼이었다. 세상의 견해만 아니었다면, 그 경험들은 그저 폭넓은 교육이었을 따름이다.\n\n근래 그녀가 워낙 동떨어져 지낸 탓에, 널리 알려진 적도 없던 그녀의 고난은 마롯에서 거의 잊혔다. 그러나 자기 가문이 부유한 더버빌가와 \"친척임을 주장하려는\"—그리고 그녀를 통해 한층 더 가까운 결합까지 꾀하려는—시도가 무너지는 것을 지켜본 곳에서 다시는 진정으로 편안할 수 없으리라는 것이 그녀에게 분명해졌다. 적어도 긴 세월이 그것에 대한 그녀의 날카로운 의식을 지워 버리기 전까지는 거기서 편안할 수 없었다. 그러나 지금도 테스는 자기 안에 여전히 따뜻한 희망찬 생명의 맥박이 뛰는 것을 느꼈다. 어쩌면 아무런 기억도 없는 어느 한구석에서 그녀는 행복할 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 과거와 그에 딸린 모든 것에서 벗어나는 것은 그것을 소멸시키는 것이었고, 그러려면 그녀는 멀리 떠나야 했다.\n\n한번 잃으면 영영 잃는다는 것이 정조에 관해 정말로 참일까? 그녀는 자문하곤 했다. 지난 일들을 가릴 수만 있다면 그것이 거짓임을 입증할 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 유기적 자연 곳곳에 스민 회복의 힘이 설마 처녀성에게만 거부되었을 리는 없었다.\n\n그녀는 새로운 출발의 기회를 찾지 못한 채 오랫동안 기다렸다. 유난히 좋은 봄이 돌아왔고, 봉오리 속에서 발아의 움직임이 거의 들릴 듯했다. 그것이, 야생 짐승들을 움직이듯 그녀를 움직여, 떠나고 싶어 못 견디게 만들었다. 마침내 오월 초의 어느 날, 오래전 문의를 보냈던 어머니의 옛 친구—한 번도 본 적 없는 사람—에게서 편지가 그녀에게 도착했으니, 남쪽으로 여러 마일 떨어진 어느 낙농장에서 솜씨 좋은 우유 짜는 여자가 필요하다는 것, 그리고 낙농장 주인이 여름철 동안 그녀를 기꺼이 두겠다는 것이었다.\n\n바랐던 만큼 그리 멀리 떨어진 곳은 아니었으나, 아마도 충분히 먼 곳이었으니, 그녀의 이동과 평판의 반경이 워낙 작았기 때문이다. 영역이 좁은 사람들에게는 몇 마일이 지리상의 도(度)와 같고, 교구가 주(州)와 같으며, 주가 지방이나 왕국과 같은 법이다.\n\n한 가지 점에 대해서 그녀는 결심이 굳었다. 새 삶의 꿈과 행동 속에 더 이상 더버빌가의 공중누각은 없으리라는 것. 그녀는 우유 짜는 여자 테스가 될 것이고, 그 이상은 아니었다. 이 점에 대한 테스의 심정을 어머니는 너무도 잘 알았기에—비록 그 문제에 관해 둘 사이에 오간 말은 없었지만—이제 기사 가문의 혈통에 대해서는 절대 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n그러나 인간이란 이렇게 모순된 존재라, 그 새 장소가 그녀에게 주는 흥미 가운데 하나는 그곳이 우연히도 그녀 조상들의 땅 가까이 자리해 있다는 점이었다(어머니는 뼛속까지 블레이크모어 사람이었으나, 그들은 블레이크모어 사람이 아니었기 때문이다). 그녀가 가기로 되어 있는 톨보세이즈라 불리는 낙농장은, 더버빌가의 옛 영지 일부에서 멀지 않은 곳, 그녀의 할머니들과 그 권세 있던 남편들이 묻힌 그 위대한 가문의 납골당 가까이에 서 있었다. 그녀는 그것들을 바라보며, 더버빌가가 바빌론처럼 무너졌다는 것뿐만 아니라, 한 비천한 후손의 개인적 순결도 그처럼 소리 없이 스러질 수 있다는 것을 생각할 수 있을 터였다. 그러는 내내 그녀는 자신이 조상의 땅에 있음으로 해서 어떤 기이하게 좋은 일이 생길지도 모른다고 궁금해했고, 그녀 안의 어떤 정신이 잔가지 속 수액처럼 절로 솟아올랐다. 그것은 일시적인 제동 후에 새로이 차오르는 뜻밖의 젊음이었으니, 희망과, 자기 기쁨을 향한 정복할 수 없는 본능을 함께 가져왔다.\n\n회복\n\n# XVI\n\n오월의, 백리향 향기 풍기고 새들이 알을 까는 어느 아침, 트랜트리지에서 돌아온 지 이삼 년 후에—테스 더비필드에게는 말없이 자신을 재건한 세월이었던—그녀는 두 번째로 집을 떠났다.\n\n나중에 부쳐 받을 수 있도록 짐을 꾸려 두고, 그녀는 세를 낸 이륜마차를 타고 스타캐슬이라는 작은 마을로 출발했으니, 여정 중에 그곳을 지나야 했기 때문이다. 이번에는 첫 모험 때와 거의 정반대 방향이었다. 가장 가까운 언덕의 굽이에서 그녀는, 그토록 떠나고 싶어 했으면서도, 마롯과 아버지의 집을 아쉬운 듯 돌아보았다.\n\n거기 사는 그녀의 친족들은 비록 그녀가 멀리 떠나가고 그녀의 미소를 잃을지라도, 의식 속의 즐거움이 크게 줄지 않은 채 전과 다름없이 일상을 이어 가리라. 며칠만 지나면 아이들은 그녀의 떠남이 남긴 어떤 빈자리도 느끼지 못한 채 여느 때처럼 즐겁게 놀이에 빠질 터였다. 어린 동생들을 두고 떠나는 이 일이 최선이라고 그녀는 판단했었다. 만약 그녀가 남는다면, 아이들은 그녀의 가르침으로 얻는 이로움보다 그녀의 본보기로 입는 해로움이 아마 더 클 테니까.\n\n그녀는 스타캐슬을 멈추지 않고 지나, 남서쪽으로 가는 운송 마차를 기다릴 수 있는 큰길의 교차점으로 나아갔다. 이 내륙 지대를 에워싼 철도들은 아직 이 일대를 가로질러 들어온 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 기다리는 사이에 어느 농부가 봄 짐수레를 몰고 지나왔는데, 그녀가 가려는 방향과 거의 같은 쪽으로 가고 있었다. 그가 그녀에게는 낯선 사람이었으나, 그 권유의 동기가 한낱 그녀의 용모에 바치는 찬사일 뿐임을 모른 체하고, 그녀는 그의 옆자리에 앉으라는 권유를 받아들였다. 그는 웨더베리로 가는 길이었고, 거기까지 그와 동행하면 캐스터브리지를 거쳐 운송 마차로 가는 대신 나머지 거리를 걸어갈 수 있었다.\n\n이 긴 마차 여정 후에 테스는 웨더베리에서, 그 농부가 일러 준 어느 오두막에서 정오에 가벼운 정체불명의 식사를 한 것 말고는 더 머무르지 않았다. 거기서부터 그녀는, 바구니를 손에 들고 걸어서 출발했으니, 이 지역과 더 너머 골짜기의 낮게 깔린 풀밭을 가르는 넓은 황야 고지대에 다다르기 위해서였다. 그 골짜기에 그날 순례의 목표이자 종착지인 낙농장이 서 있었다.\n\n테스는 전에 이 지방을 찾아온 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 그래도 그 풍경에 친근감을 느꼈다. 그녀의 왼편으로 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 풍경 속 검은 한 자락이 보였는데, 알아본바 그것은 킹스비어 인근을 표시하는 나무들이라는 그녀의 짐작이 맞다고 확인되었다—그 교구의 교회 안에 그녀 조상들의—그녀의 쓸모없는 조상들의—뼈가 묻혀 있었다.\n\n이제 그녀는 그들에 대해 아무런 흠모도 없었다. 그녀는 그들이 자신을 끌고 다닌 그 춤 때문에 그들을 거의 미워했다. 그들의 것이었던 모든 것 가운데 그녀가 간직한 것이라곤 오래된 인장과 숟가락뿐이었다. \"흥—내 안엔 아버지만큼이나 어머니도 들어 있어!\" 그녀는 말했다. \"내 어여쁨은 다 어머니한테서 왔고, 어머니는 한낱 우유 짜는 여자였을 뿐이야.\"\n\n에그던의 그 사이에 놓인 고지와 저지를 넘는 여정은, 그곳에 다다랐을 때, 그녀가 예상했던 것보다 더 성가신 걸음이었으니, 거리는 실상 몇 마일에 지나지 않았건만 말이다. 여러 차례 길을 잘못 든 탓에 두 시간이 지나서야 그녀는 그토록 찾던 골짜기를 굽어보는 마루에 다다랐으니, 그 위대한 낙농장들의 골짜기, 우유와 버터가 무성하게 자라나, 그녀의 고향에서보다, 덜 섬세할지언정 더 풍성하게 생산되는 골짜기—바르 강 혹은 프룸 강이 그토록 잘 물을 댄 푸르른 평원이었다.\n\n그것은 본질적으로 그녀가 지금까지—트랜트리지에서의 그 재앙적인 체류를 빼고는—오로지 알고 지내 온 작은 낙농장들의 골짜기, 곧 블랙무어 골짜기와는 달랐다. 여기서는 세계가 더 큰 본을 따라 그려져 있었다. 울타리 친 땅이 십 에이커가 아니라 오십 에이커였고, 농가들은 더 널찍했으며, 이 일대에서 소 떼는 종족을 이루었다—거기서는 그저 가족이었는데 말이다. 동쪽 끝에서 서쪽 끝까지 그녀의 눈 아래 펼쳐진 이 무수한 젖소들은, 그녀가 일찍이 한눈에 본 어떤 것보다도 그 수가 많았다. 푸른 풀밭은 반 알슬로트나 살라르트의 화폭이 시민들로 빽빽한 것만큼이나 소들로 빽빽하게 점점이 박혀 있었다. 붉고 거무튀튀한 소들의 무르익은 빛깔은 저녁 햇빛을 빨아들였고, 흰 털가죽 짐승들은 그 빛을, 그녀가 서 있는 그 먼 높이에서조차 거의 눈부실 만큼의 광선으로 눈에 되돌려 주었다.\n\n그녀 앞에 펼쳐진 조감(鳥瞰)의 광경은, 그녀가 그토록 잘 아는 저 다른 광경만큼 무성하게 아름답지는 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 더 마음을 북돋웠다. 거기에는 그 맞수 골짜기의 그 짙푸른 대기도, 그 무거운 토양과 냄새도 없었다. 새로운 공기는 맑고, 상쾌하고, 천상의 것 같았다. 이 이름난 낙농장들의 풀과 소를 길러 내는 그 강 자체도, 블랙무어의 시내들처럼 흐르지 않았다. 그것들은 느리고, 조용하고, 자주 흐릿했으니, 진흙 바닥 위로 흐르는 그곳에서는 조심성 없는 도섭자가 부지중에 가라앉아 사라질 수도 있었다. 프룸의 물은 복음서 기자에게 보여진 그 순수한 생명의 강처럼 맑았고, 구름 그림자처럼 빠르며, 자갈 깔린 여울이 온종일 하늘을 향해 재잘거렸다. 거기서 물에 핀 꽃은 백합이었으나, 여기서는 미나리아재비였다.\n\n무겁던 공기가 가벼워진 그 질의 변화 때문이었는지, 아니면 자신을 향한 짓궂은 눈길이 하나도 없는 새로운 풍경 속에 있다는 느낌 때문이었는지, 그녀의 기운이 놀랍도록 솟아올랐다. 그녀의 희망은, 부드러운 남풍을 맞받아 통통 튀듯 나아가는 그녀를 에워싼 어떤 이상적인 광구(光球) 속에서 햇살과 뒤섞였다. 그녀는 미풍마다 유쾌한 목소리를 들었고, 새소리마다 기쁨이 숨어 있는 듯했다.\n\n근래 그녀의 얼굴은 마음 상태가 변함에 따라 변해 왔으니, 생각이 즐거우냐 침울하냐에 따라 아름다움과 평범함 사이를 끊임없이 오갔다. 어느 날은 발그레하고 흠 하나 없다가, 또 어느 날은 창백하고 비극적이었다. 발그레할 때 그녀는 창백할 때보다 덜 느끼고 있었다. 그녀의 더 완전한 아름다움은 그녀의 덜 고양된 기분과 어울렸고, 그녀의 더 강렬한 기분은 그녀의 덜 완전한 아름다움과 어울렸다. 지금 남풍을 맞받고 있는 것은 육체적으로 그녀의 가장 좋은 얼굴이었다.\n\n가장 비천한 것에서 가장 고귀한 것에 이르기까지 모든 생명에 두루 스민, 어딘가에서 달콤한 기쁨을 찾으려는 그 저항할 수 없고 보편적이며 절로 일어나는 경향이, 마침내 테스를 사로잡았다. 지금도 그녀는 겨우 스무 살의 젊은 여인, 정신적으로도 정서적으로도 아직 자라기를 끝내지 못한 사람이었으니, 어떤 사건도 그녀에게 시간이 지나면 변형될 수 없는 인상을 남겼을 리 없었다.\n\n이리하여 그녀의 기운과 감사의 마음과 희망은 점점 더 높이 솟아올랐다. 그녀는 여러 발라드를 시험 삼아 불러 보았으나, 그것들이 부족하다 느꼈다. 그러다, 지혜의 나무 열매를 따 먹기 전 일요일 아침이면 그토록 자주 눈으로 훑어 내려가던 그 시편집을 떠올리고, 그녀는 노래했다. \"오 너희 해와 달이여... 오 너희 별들이여... 너희 땅 위의 푸른 것들이여... 너희 공중의 새들이여... 짐승과 가축들이여... 사람의 자녀들이여... 너희는 주님을 송축하고, 그분을 찬양하며 영원토록 높이 받들지어다!\"\n\n그녀는 갑자기 멈추고 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 어쩌면 난 아직 주님을 잘 알지 못하나 봐.\"\n\n그리고 아마도 그 반쯤 무의식적인 황홀가는 일신교의 무대 위에서 터져 나온 물신숭배적 발언이었으리라. 야외 자연의 형상과 힘을 주된 벗으로 삼는 여인들은, 훗날 그들의 핏줄에 가르쳐진 체계화된 종교보다, 먼 조상들의 이교적 환상을 그 영혼 속에 훨씬 더 많이 간직하고 있는 법이다. 그래도 테스는 어릴 적부터 더듬더듬 외워 온 오래된 「베네디키테」 속에서 자신의 감정에 적어도 비슷한 표현을 찾아냈다. 그것으로 충분했다. 독립적인 삶의 수단을 향해 첫발을 떼었을 뿐인 그런 사소한 출발에 그토록 큰 만족을 느끼는 것은 더비필드 집안 기질의 일부였다. 테스는 정말로 곧게 걷고 싶어 했고, 그녀의 아버지는 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 하지만 그녀는, 당장의 작은 성취에 만족하고, 한때 강성했던 더버빌 가문이 지금처럼 무겁게 짓눌린 처지의 집안만이 이룰 수 있는 그 보잘것없는 사회적 향상을 위해 고된 노력을 기울일 마음이 없다는 점에서는 아버지를 닮아 있었다.\n\n말하자면 거기에는, 테스의 나이가 지닌 자연스러운 활기뿐 아니라, 그녀를 한동안 그토록 압도했던 경험 이후에 다시 불붙은, 어머니 쪽 집안의 미처 다 쓰지 못한 기운도 있었다. 진실을 말하자면—여자들은 대체로 그런 굴욕을 견디고 살아가며, 기운을 되찾고, 다시 흥미로운 눈으로 주위를 둘러본다. 살아 있는 한 희망이 있다는 신념은, 어느 마음씨 좋은 이론가들이 우리에게 믿게 하려는 것만큼 \"배신당한\" 여인들에게 그토록 낯선 것이 아니다.\n\n그리하여 테스 더비필드는, 마음이 가뿐하고 삶에 대한 열의로 가득 차서, 순례의 목적지인 낙농장을 향해 에그던의 비탈을 점점 더 아래로 내려갔다.\n\n마지막 한 가지 점에서, 서로 겨루는 두 골짜기 사이의 뚜렷한 차이가 이제 드러났다. 블랙무어의 비밀은 둘러싼 높은 곳에서 가장 잘 발견되었다. 그러나 눈앞의 골짜기를 제대로 읽으려면 그 한가운데로 내려가야만 했다. 테스가 이 일을 해냈을 때, 그녀는 자신이 동서로 눈길 닿는 데까지 펼쳐진 양탄자 같은 평지 위에 서 있음을 알았다.\n\n강은 더 높은 지대에서 흙을 훔쳐와 이 골짜기에 입자를 날라다 이 수평의 땅을 모두 만들었다. 그리고 이제 지치고 늙고 가늘어진 채로, 한때 제가 약탈한 것들의 한복판을 구불구불 누비며 누워 있었다.\n\n방향을 그리 확신하지 못한 채, 테스는 둘러싸인 푸른 평지의 너른 공간 위에 가만히 서 있었다. 끝이 어딘지 모를 당구대 위의 파리 한 마리처럼, 그리고 그 주변에게는 그 파리만큼도 대수롭지 않은 존재로. 지금껏 이 고요한 골짜기에 그녀의 존재가 끼친 유일한 효과라면, 외로운 왜가리 한 마리의 마음을 동요시킨 것뿐이었다. 그 새는 그녀의 길에서 멀지 않은 땅으로 내려앉더니, 목을 곧추세우고 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n갑자기 저지대 사방에서 길게 늘어지며 거듭되는 부름이 솟아올랐다—\"와우! 와우! 와우!\"\n\n가장 먼 동쪽에서 가장 먼 서쪽까지 그 외침은 전염되듯 퍼져 나갔고, 더러는 개 짖는 소리가 곁들여졌다. 그것은 아름다운 테스가 당도했다는 골짜기의 의식이 드러난 것이 아니라, 그저 젖 짜는 시각을 알리는 평범한 신호였다—네 시 반, 낙농 일꾼들이 소들을 몰아들이기 시작하는 때였다.\n\n가장 가까이서 부름을 무덤덤하게 기다리고 있던 붉고 흰 소떼가, 이제 뒤편의 농막을 향해 무리 지어 나아갔다. 걸을 때마다 커다란 젖통이 아래에서 흔들거렸다. 테스는 천천히 그 뒤를 따라가, 소들이 앞서 들어간 열린 문을 지나 마당으로 들어섰다. 길게 늘어선 이엉 지붕 헛간들이 울안을 빙 둘렀고, 그 비탈에는 선명한 초록 이끼가 더께처럼 끼어 있었으며, 그 처마는 지난 세월의 수많은 소와 송아지들의 옆구리에 닳아 반들반들 매끄러워진 나무 기둥들로 떠받쳐져 있었다. 그 소들은 이제 그 깊이를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 망각 속으로 사라져 버렸다. 기둥들 사이에는 젖소들이 줄지어 있었는데, 지금 이 순간 뒤쪽의 별난 눈에는 저마다 두 개의 줄기 위에 얹힌 원으로 보였고, 그 한가운데로 꼬리 하나가 추처럼 흔들렸다. 한편 해는 이 참을성 많은 줄 뒤로 내려앉으며, 그들의 그림자를 벽 안쪽으로 정확히 던졌다. 그렇게 해는 이 어둑하고 수수한 형상들의 그림자를, 마치 궁전 벽에 비친 궁정 미녀의 옆모습이라도 되는 양 윤곽 하나하나에 정성을 다해 매일 저녁 던졌다. 오래전 대리석 정면에 올림포스의 형상들을, 혹은 알렉산드로스와 카이사르와 파라오들의 윤곽을 베껴 그렸던 만큼이나 부지런히 그것들을 베껴 냈다.\n\n칸막이 우리에 매인 것은 덜 얌전한 소들이었다. 제 의지로 가만히 서 있을 소들은 마당 한복판에서 젖을 짰고, 지금도 그렇게 더 점잖은 소들 여럿이 거기 서서 기다리고 있었다—모두 일급 젖소들로, 이 골짜기 밖에서는 좀처럼 볼 수 없고 골짜기 안에서도 늘 볼 수 있는 것은 아니었다. 일 년 중 한창인 이 철에 물가 풀밭이 대 주는 즙 많은 먹이로 살을 찌운 것들이었다. 그중 흰 점이 박힌 소들은 햇빛을 받아 눈부시게 반짝였고, 뿔에 박힌 윤나는 놋쇠 마디는 군대의 위용 같은 것을 띠며 번득였다. 굵은 핏줄이 선 젖통은 모래주머니처럼 묵직하게 늘어졌고, 젖꼭지는 집시 항아리의 다리처럼 삐죽 튀어나와 있었다. 저마다 차례가 오기를 기다리며 머뭇거리는 동안 젖이 새어 나와 방울져 땅에 떨어졌다.\n\n# XVII\n\nXVII\n풀밭에서 소들이 당도하자, 젖 짜는 처녀들과 사내들이 오두막에서, 또 낙농장 집에서 우르르 내려왔다. 처녀들은 나막신을 신고 걸었는데, 날씨 때문이 아니라 마당의 진창 위로 신발을 띄워 두려는 것이었다. 처녀들은 저마다 세 발 걸상에 앉아 얼굴을 옆으로 돌리고 오른뺨을 소에 기댄 채, 다가오는 테스를 소의 옆구리 너머로 골똘히 바라보았다. 모자챙을 내려 이마에 납작 붙이고 땅바닥을 바라보던 사내 일꾼들은 그녀를 알아채지 못했다.\n\n그중 하나는 다부진 중년 사내였다—그의 긴 흰 작업 앞치마는 다른 이들의 두름천보다 조금 더 곱고 깨끗했고, 그 아래 입은 윗도리는 장에 나갈 만한 번듯한 차림새였다—그가 바로 그녀가 찾던 주인 낙농가였다. 엿새 동안은 젖을 짜고 버터를 만드는 일꾼이었다가, 이렛날에는 번들거리는 고급 나사옷을 입고 교회의 가족석에 앉는 신사로 변하는 그의 이중 면모가 어찌나 두드러졌던지, 운까지 붙은 노래가 생겼을 정도였다.\n\n낙농가 딕 한 주 내내는 딕이지만 일요일엔 리처드 크릭 나리.\n\n테스가 멍하니 서 있는 것을 보고 그는 그녀에게 다가왔다.\n\n낙농가들은 대개 젖 짜는 시각에 퉁명스러운 태도를 보이지만, 마침 크릭 씨는 새 일손을 얻게 되어 기뻤다—요즘은 바쁜 나날이었던 것이다—그래서 그는 그녀를 따뜻이 맞으며 어머니와 나머지 식구들의 안부를 물었다(다만 이것은 그저 형식적인 것이었으니, 사실 그는 테스에 관한 짤막한 사무 편지로 알기 전까지는 더비필드 부인의 존재조차 모르고 있었다).\n\n\"오—그래, 어릴 적엔 자네 동네를 아주 잘 알았지.\" 그가 말을 맺듯 말했다. \"그 뒤로는 한 번도 가 본 적 없지만 말이야. 그리고 옛날에 이 근처에 살던, 지금은 죽어 사라진 지 오래된 아흔 살 노파가 나한테 그러더군. 블랙무어 골짜기에 자네하고 비슷한 성을 가진 집안이 원래 이쪽 출신이고, 이 땅에서 거의 다 스러져 버린 아주 오래된 옛 가문인데—새 세대는 그걸 모른다고 말이야. 하지만, 원 참, 나야 그 노파의 횡설수설을 귀담아듣지 않았지. 안 듣고말고.\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요—아무것도 아니에요.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서는 일 이야기뿐이었다.\n\n\"젖을 깨끗이 짤 줄은 아나, 아가씨? 이맘때 내 소들이 젖이 마르는 건 싫으니까.\"\n\n그녀가 그 점은 안심시키자, 그는 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았다. 그녀는 그동안 집 안에만 많이 틀어박혀 있었던 탓에 살결이 섬약해져 있었다.\n\n\"정말 견딜 수 있겠나? 거친 사람들한테는 여기가 그럭저럭 편하긴 하지만, 우리가 오이 모종틀 속에서 사는 건 아니라서 말이야.\"\n\n그녀가 견딜 수 있다고 잘라 말하자, 그녀의 열의와 의욕이 그의 마음을 산 듯했다.\n\n\"음, 차 한 그릇이나 뭐 먹을 거라도 좀 들겠지, 응? 아직은 아니라고? 음, 알아서 하게나. 하지만 정말이지, 나 같으면 그렇게 먼 길을 와서는 마른 풀줄기처럼 목이 탔을 거야.\"\n\n\"제 손에 익히게 지금부터 젖을 짜겠어요.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 잠시 기운을 차리려고 우유를 조금 마셨는데—낙농가 크릭은 놀랐다, 아니 사실 약간 경멸하는 기색이었다. 그의 머릿속에는 우유가 마실 것으로 좋다는 생각이 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었던 모양이다.\n\n\"오, 그걸 삼킬 수 있다면야 그러게나.\" 그가 그녀가 홀짝이는 들통을 들어 주며 무심히 말했다. \"난 몇 년째 입에도 안 댄 거지—안 대고말고. 빌어먹을 그거, 내 뱃속에 납덩이처럼 들어앉을 텐데. 저 녀석한테 손을 대 보게.\" 그가 가장 가까운 소를 향해 고갯짓하며 말을 이었다. \"그래도 저 녀석은 젖이 좀 빡빡하게 나오긴 해. 우리한테는 빡빡한 놈도 있고 수월한 놈도 있지, 사람들처럼 말이야. 어쨌든 곧 알게 될 거야.\"\n\n테스가 보닛을 두건으로 바꿔 쓰고, 정말로 소 밑 걸상에 앉아, 두 주먹에서 우유가 들통 속으로 쭉쭉 뿜어 나오자, 그녀는 정말로 자기 앞날의 새 토대를 놓았다는 느낌이 드는 듯했다. 그 확신이 평온을 낳았고, 맥박이 느려졌으며, 그녀는 주위를 둘러볼 여유가 생겼다.\n\n젖 짜는 일꾼들은 사내와 처녀로 이루어진 제법 작은 한 부대를 이루었는데, 사내들은 젖꼭지가 빡빡한 소들을 맡고, 처녀들은 더 순한 성질의 소들을 맡았다. 큰 낙농장이었다. 크릭의 관리 아래 젖소가 모두 백 마리 가까이 있었다. 그 소떼 가운데 여섯에서 여덟 마리는 주인 낙농가가 집을 비울 때가 아니면 제 손으로 직접 짰다. 이것들은 모든 소 가운데서도 젖이 가장 빡빡한 놈들이었다. 그의 임시 젖꾼들은 그때그때 다소 되는대로 고용한 자들이라, 그는 이 여섯 마리를 그들 손에 맡기려 하지 않았다—성의 없이 굴어 젖을 끝까지 다 짜지 않을까 봐서였다. 또 처녀들에게도 맡기지 않았다—손아귀 힘이 모자라 똑같은 식으로 일을 그르칠까 봐서였다. 그 결과 시간이 흐르면 소들은 \"젖이 마르게\", 곧 젖이 끊기게 되곤 했다. 젖을 게을리 짜는 것이 그토록 심각한 까닭은 당장의 손실 때문이 아니라, 수요가 줄면 공급도 줄고, 끝내는 공급이 멎어 버리기 때문이었다.\n\n테스가 제 소 앞에 자리를 잡은 뒤로 한동안 마당에는 아무 말이 없었고, 여러 들통 속으로 젖이 들어가는 그르렁 소리를 방해하는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 다만 이 짐승 저 짐승에게 돌아서라거나 가만히 서 있으라고 외치는 잠깐의 고함만 가끔 끼어들 뿐이었다. 움직이는 것이라곤 젖꾼들의 손이 위아래로 오르내리는 것과 소꼬리가 흔들리는 것뿐이었다. 그렇게 그들 모두는, 골짜기 양쪽 비탈까지 펼쳐진 광활하고 평평한 풀밭에 둘러싸여 일을 이어 갔다—오래전 잊힌 옛 풍경들이 한데 합쳐져 이루어진 평탄한 풍경이었고, 틀림없이 지금 그것들이 이루고 있는 풍경과는 성격이 크게 달랐을 것이다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이야,\" 방금 끝낸 소에서 갑자기 일어서며, 한 손에는 세 발 걸상을, 다른 손에는 들통을 낚아채 들고 가까이 있는 다음 빡빡한 놈에게로 옮겨 가며 낙농가가 말했다. \"내 생각엔, 오늘 소들이 젖을 여느 때처럼 내주질 않는단 말이지. 정말이지, 윙커가 이렇게 젖을 안 내주기 시작하면, 한여름쯤엔 밑에 들어가 짤 가치도 없어질 거야.\"\n\n\"새 일손이 우리 사이에 들어와서 그래요.\" 조너선 카일이 말했다. \"전에도 그런 걸 본 적이 있다고요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지. 그럴지도 몰라. 난 그 생각을 못 했군.\"\n\n\"그럴 때면 젖이 뿔 속으로 올라간다고들 하던데요.\" 한 젖 짜는 처녀가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 뿔 속으로 올라간다는 건 말이지,\" 낙농가 크릭이 미심쩍게 대꾸했다. 마치 마법조차 해부학적 가능성에 제약을 받기라도 한다는 듯이. \"글쎄, 모르겠는걸. 정말 모르겠어. 하지만 뿔 없는 소도 뿔 있는 놈만큼이나 젖을 안 내주니까, 난 그 말엔 영 동의가 안 돼. 자네 그 뿔 없는 소 수수께끼 아나, 조너선? 뿔 없는 소가 왜 뿔 있는 소보다 일 년에 젖을 덜 내줄까?\"\n\n\"몰라요!\" 젖 짜는 처녀가 끼어들었다. \"왜 그래요?\"\n\n\"뿔 없는 놈이 그렇게 많지가 않으니까.\" 낙농가가 말했다. \"어쨌거나, 이 장난꾼들이 오늘 젖을 안 내주는 건 분명해. 여보게들, 가락이나 한두 자락 뽑아야겠어—그게 유일한 처방이거든.\"\n\n이 근방 낙농장에서는 소들이 평소의 산출량을 내주지 않으려는 기색을 보이면, 소들을 꾀어내는 수단으로 노래를 곧잘 동원했다. 이 청에 따라 젖꾼들의 무리가 가락을 터뜨렸다—사실 순전히 사무적인 어조였고 그리 신명이 나는 것도 아니었다. 그래도 그들 나름의 믿음에 따르면, 노래가 이어지는 동안에는 뚜렷한 개선이 있었다. 어둠 속에서 잠자리에 들기를 무서워하는 어느 살인자에 관한—제 둘레에 유황 불길이 어른거리는 게 보여서 그랬다는—흥겨운 발라드를 열네댓 절쯤 다 부르고 났을 때, 사내 젖꾼 하나가 말했다—\n\n\"걸상에 앉아 노래 부르는 게 이렇게 사람 숨을 다 빼먹지만 않아도 좋으련만! 나리, 나리 하프를 가져오시지요. 그래도 바이올린이 제일 낫긴 합니다만.\"\n\n이 말에 귀를 기울이던 테스는 그 말이 낙농가에게 한 것인 줄 알았으나, 틀렸다. \"왜?\"라는 대꾸가, 말하자면 우리에 매인 암갈색 소의 뱃속에서 나오는 것처럼 들려왔다. 그것은 그 짐승 뒤에 있던, 그녀가 여태 알아채지 못한 젖꾼이 한 말이었다.\n\n\"오, 그렇지. 바이올린만 한 게 없지.\" 낙농가가 말했다. \"하긴 황소가 암소보다 가락에 더 마음이 움직이는 것 같긴 해—적어도 내 경험으론 그래. 한번은 멜스톡에 늙은 노인이 하나 있었는데—이름이 윌리엄 듀이라고—거기서 짐꾼으로 일을 꽤 하던 집안 사람이었지—조너선, 자네 기억나나?—내가 내 친형을 아는 것만큼이나, 말하자면, 그 사람 얼굴을 잘 알았거든. 그래, 이 사람이 어느 좋은 달밤에 결혼식에서 바이올린을 켜고 집으로 돌아오는 길이었는데, 지름길로 가려고 포티에이커스라는 밭을 가로질렀어. 거기에 황소 한 마리가 풀을 뜯으러 나와 있었지. 황소가 윌리엄을 보더니, 뿔을 땅에 들이대고 그를 쫓아오는 거야, 이런! 윌리엄이 죽을힘을 다해 뛰었지만—게다가 술도 그리 많이 마시지 않았는데도(결혼식이고 사람들도 넉넉했던 걸 생각하면 말이지)—제때 울타리에 닿아 넘어가 목숨을 구하기는 글렀다는 걸 깨달았어. 그래, 마지막 생각으로, 그는 뛰면서 바이올린을 꺼내 들고 지그를 켜기 시작했어. 황소 쪽으로 돌아서서 구석으로 뒷걸음치면서 말이야. 황소가 누그러지더니 가만히 서서 윌리엄 듀이를 빤히 쳐다봤어. 윌리엄은 계속 켜고 또 켰지. 그러다 황소 얼굴에 일종의 미소 같은 게 슬며시 번지더란 말이야. 그런데 윌리엄이 연주를 멈추고 울타리를 넘으려고 돌아서기만 하면, 황소는 미소를 거두고 윌리엄의 바지 엉덩이 쪽으로 뿔을 들이대는 거야. 그래, 윌리엄은 좋든 싫든 다시 돌아서서 계속 켤 수밖에 없었어. 게다가 세상에 겨우 새벽 세 시였고, 몇 시간이 지나도 아무도 그 길로 안 올 걸 그도 알았지. 어찌나 졸리고 지쳤던지 어쩔 줄을 몰랐어. 네 시쯤까지 켜고 났을 때 그는 곧 정말로 손을 놓을 수밖에 없겠다 싶어서 속으로 그랬어. '이제 이 마지막 한 곡이 나하고 영원한 안녕 사이에 놓인 전부로구나! 하느님, 절 구하소서, 안 그러면 전 끝장입니다.' 그래, 그러다 그는 크리스마스이브 한밤중에 가축들이 무릎 꿇는 걸 본 일이 떠올랐어. 그땐 크리스마스이브가 아니었지만, 황소한테 한번 속임수를 써 보자는 생각이 머리에 떠오른 거야. 그래서 그는, 크리스마스 캐럴을 부를 때처럼, 「강생 찬송가」를 켜기 시작했어. 그랬더니, 이런 세상에, 황소가 굽힌 두 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉는 거야. 그 무지함 속에서, 마치 정말 강생의 밤 그 시각이기라도 한 것처럼 말이지. 그 뿔 달린 친구가 주저앉기 무섭게 윌리엄은 돌아서서 사냥개처럼 후딱 달아나, 기도하던 황소가 다시 일어나 쫓아오기도 전에 무사히 울타리를 뛰어넘었어. 윌리엄은 늘 그러더군, 사람이 바보 같은 꼴을 하는 걸 여러 번 봤지만, 자기 경건한 감정을 가지고 장난친 거고 크리스마스이브도 아니었다는 걸 알았을 때 그 황소만큼 바보 같은 꼴은 본 적이 없다고. 그래, 윌리엄 듀이, 그게 그 사람 이름이야. 그리고 지금 이 순간 그가 멜스톡 교회 묘지 어디에 누워 있는지 한 발자국까지 짚어 줄 수 있지—바로 두 번째 주목과 북쪽 회랑 사이라고.\"\n\n\"기묘한 이야기로군요. 우리를 신앙이 살아 있던 중세로 데려가네요!\"\n\n낙농장 마당에서는 유별난 그 말이, 암갈색 소 뒤의 목소리로 중얼거려졌다. 하지만 그 언급을 알아듣는 이가 아무도 없어 누구도 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 다만 이야기를 들려준 사람만이 그것이 제 이야기를 미심쩍어하는 뜻일지도 모른다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n\"음, 그게 사실이고말고요, 나리. 사실이든 아니든 말입니다. 그 사람을 잘 알았는걸요.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요. 의심하지 않습니다.\" 암갈색 소 뒤의 사람이 말했다.\n\n그렇게 테스의 주의는 낙농가의 말상대에게 끌렸는데, 그가 어찌나 끈질기게 젖소의 옆구리에 머리를 파묻고 있던지 그녀에게는 그의 아주 작은 일부밖에 보이지 않았다. 어째서 낙농가 본인까지 그를 \"나리\"라고 부르는지 그녀는 이해할 수 없었다. 하지만 까닭을 알아낼 길이 없었다. 그는 소 세 마리는 짜고도 남을 만큼 오래 소 밑에 있으면서, 일이 잘 안 풀리는 듯 이따금 혼자 끙 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"살살 하세요, 나리. 살살요.\" 낙농가가 말했다. \"그건 힘이 아니라 요령으로 하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"나도 알겠소.\" 마침내 일어서서 두 팔을 뻗으며 상대가 말했다. \"하지만 이 녀석은 끝낸 것 같소, 손가락이 다 쑤시긴 하지만.\"\n\n그제야 테스는 그의 전신을 볼 수 있었다. 그는 젖 짤 때 낙농업자가 입는 흔한 흰 앞치마에 가죽 각반을 차고 있었고, 장화는 마당의 진창으로 더께가 앉아 있었다. 하지만 이것이 그의 이 고장 차림새 전부였다. 그 아래에는 무언가 교양 있고, 절제되고, 섬세하고, 슬프고, 남다른 것이 있었다.\n\n하지만 그 외양의 세세한 부분은, 그가 전에 본 적 있는 사람이라는 발견 앞에 잠시 밀려났다. 그때 이후로 테스가 워낙 많은 우여곡절을 겪은 터라, 그녀는 한순간 그를 어디서 만났는지 떠올리지 못했다. 그러다 그가 말롯의 클럽 춤판에 끼어들었던 그 도보 여행객임이 퍼뜩 떠올랐다—어디서 왔는지 모를, 지나가던 낯선 사람, 다른 처녀들과는 춤추었으나 그녀와는 추지 않았고, 그녀를 가벼이 두고 친구들과 제 갈 길로 가 버린 그 사람.\n\n자신의 곤경 이전에 있었던 한 사건이 이렇게 되살아나며 밀려든 기억의 물결은, 그가 그녀 또한 알아보고 어떤 식으로든 그녀의 사연을 알아낼까 하는 순간적인 당혹을 일으켰다. 하지만 그에게서 알아보는 기색이 전혀 없자 그 당혹은 가셨다. 그녀는 차츰, 처음이자 유일했던 그 만남 이후로 그의 활기 있는 얼굴이 더 사려 깊어졌고, 젊은 남자다운 맵시 있는 콧수염과 턱수염이 자랐음을 알아보았다—턱수염은 뺨에서 시작되는 부분은 가장 옅은 밀짚 빛깔이었다가, 뿌리에서 멀어질수록 따뜻한 갈색으로 짙어졌다. 아마포 젖 짜기 앞치마 아래로 그는 짙은 골덴 윗도리에 코듀로이 반바지와 각반을 차고, 풀 먹인 흰 셔츠를 입고 있었다. 젖 짜는 장비를 빼면 그가 무엇 하는 사람인지 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다. 그는 괴짜 지주일 수도 있었고 신사 같은 농부일 수도 있었으니, 어느 쪽이든 그럴듯했다. 그가 낙농 일에는 풋내기에 불과하다는 것을, 그녀는 그가 소 한 마리 젖을 짜는 데 들인 시간으로 단박에 알아챘다.\n\n그러는 동안 젖 짜는 처녀들 여럿이 새로 온 처녀를 두고 서로 이렇게 말했다. \"쟤 참 예쁘지 않니!\" 진심 어린 너그러움과 감탄이 담긴 말이었으나, 듣는 이가 그 단언을 조금 깎아 주기를 은근히 바라는 마음도 절반쯤 섞여 있었다—엄밀히 말하면 그렇게 깎을 수도 있었으니, 예쁘다는 말은 테스를 보고 눈에 와닿는 것에 대한 부정확한 정의였기 때문이다. 저녁 젖 짜기가 끝나자 그들은 줄줄이 집 안으로 들어갔다. 거기서는 낙농가의 아내인 크릭 부인이—그녀는 너무 점잖아서 직접 젖을 짜러 나가지 않았고, 젖 짜는 처녀들이 무명옷을 입으니 자기는 따뜻한 날씨에도 두꺼운 모직 가운을 입었다—통과 살림살이를 살피고 있었다.\n\n테스는 자기 말고 처녀 두셋만이 낙농장 집에서 잔다는 것을 알게 되었고, 일손 대부분은 제집으로 돌아갔다. 저녁 식사 때 그녀는 이야기에 한마디 거든 그 점잖은 젖꾼을 보지 못했고, 그에 대해 아무것도 묻지 않았다. 저녁 나머지 시간은 침실에서 자기 자리를 정리하는 데 보냈다. 그곳은 우유 저장실 위에 있는 넓은 방으로, 길이가 삼십 피트쯤 되었다. 집 안에서 자는 다른 세 젖 짜는 처녀의 잠자리도 같은 방에 있었다. 그들은 한창때의 젊은 여자들이었고, 하나만 빼고는 그녀보다 나이가 좀 위였다. 잠자리에 들 무렵 테스는 완전히 지쳐, 곧장 잠이 들었다.\n\n하지만 옆 침대를 쓰는 처녀 하나는 테스보다 잠이 덜 와서, 그녀가 막 발을 들인 이 집에 관한 이런저런 사정을 테스에게 기어이 들려주려 했다. 그 처녀의 속삭이는 말은 어둠과 뒤섞였고, 졸음에 겨운 테스의 마음에는 그 말들이 둥둥 떠다니는 그 어둠 속에서 생겨나는 것처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n\"엔젤 클레어 씨—젖 짜는 걸 배우는, 하프 켜는 그분 말이야—우리한텐 별로 말을 안 하셔. 목사님 아들인데, 자기 생각에 너무 빠져 있어서 여자애들은 거들떠보지도 않아. 낙농가 주인의 견습생이거든—농사 일을 갈래갈래 다 배우는 거야. 다른 데서 양 치는 걸 배웠고, 지금은 낙농 일을 익히는 중이고…… 그래, 아주 신사 집안 출신이야. 그분 아버지가 에민스터의 클레어 목사님인데—여기서 몇 마일이나 떨어진 곳이지.\"\n\n\"오—그분 얘긴 들어 봤어.\" 이제 잠이 깬 그녀의 동무가 말했다. \"아주 열성적인 목사님이라고, 그렇지 않니?\"\n\n\"맞아—그렇고말고—온 웨식스에서 가장 열성적인 분이래—옛날 저교회파의 마지막 사람이라더라—이 근방은 죄다 고교회파라고들 하는데 말이야. 그 댁 아들들은 우리 클레어 씨만 빼고 다 목사가 됐어.\"\n\n테스는 이 시각에는, 지금의 클레어 씨가 왜 형제들처럼 목사가 되지 않았는지 물어볼 호기심이 없었고, 점점 다시 잠이 들었다. 그녀에게 사정을 들려주던 처녀의 말은, 옆 치즈 다락에서 나는 치즈 냄새와, 아래층에서 압착틀로부터 일정하게 떨어지는 유장 소리와 함께 그녀에게 흘러들었다.\nXVIII\n\n# XVIII\n\n엔젤 클레어는 과거로부터, 또렷한 인물의 모습이라기보다 음미하듯 듣던 목소리로, 한곳에 멍하니 못 박힌 눈의 긴 응시로, 그리고 남자치고는 다소 작고 가는 선이 새겨진 입의 움직임으로 떠오른다. 그래도 그 입은 이따금 아랫입술을 뜻밖에 단단히 다물어, 우유부단하다는 어떤 추측도 지워 버릴 만했다. 그럼에도 그의 거동과 시선에는 무언가 안개 같고, 골똘하고, 어렴풋한 것이 있어, 그가 아마 제 물질적 앞날에 대해 그리 뚜렷한 목표나 관심이 없는 사람임을 드러냈다. 그래도 소년 시절 사람들은 그를 두고, 마음만 먹으면 무엇이든 해낼 사람이라고들 했다.\n\n그는 주의 반대편 끝에서 가난한 목사로 지내는 아버지의 막내아들로, 다른 농장 몇 군데를 두루 거친 뒤 여섯 달 견습생으로 탤보세이스 낙농장에 와 있었다. 그의 목적은 농사의 여러 과정에 실용적인 기술을 익히는 것이었고, 형편이 정해지는 대로 식민지로 가든지 본국에서 자작농장을 부치든지 할 작정이었다.\n\n농사꾼과 가축 사육자의 대열에 들어선 것은, 본인도 남들도 예상치 못한 이 젊은이의 행로였다.\n\n아버지 클레어 씨는 첫 아내가 죽으며 딸 하나를 남기자, 늘그막에 재혼했다. 이 부인은 다소 뜻밖에도 그에게 아들 셋을 안겨 주었고, 그래서 막내인 엔젤과 그의 아버지인 교구 목사 사이에는 거의 한 세대가 빠진 듯이 보였다. 이 사내아이들 가운데 앞서 말한 엔젤, 곧 그가 늘그막에 본 자식은, 학구적 훈련을 충분히 빛낼 만한 어릴 적 자질을 지닌 유일한 아들이었음에도, 대학 학위를 따지 않은 유일한 아들이었다.\n\n엔젤이 말롯의 춤판에 나타나기 두세 해쯤 전, 그가 학교를 마치고 집에서 학업을 이어 가던 어느 날, 동네 서점에서 제임스 클레어 목사 앞으로 소포 하나가 목사관에 도착했다. 목사가 그것을 뜯어 책 한 권이 든 것을 발견하고 몇 쪽을 읽더니, 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그 책을 옆구리에 끼고 곧장 가게로 갔다.\n\n\"왜 이게 우리 집으로 왔소?\" 그가 책을 치켜들며 단호히 물었다.\n\n\"주문이 들어온 겁니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"내가 한 것도 아니고, 우리 집안 누가 한 것도 아니오. 다행스럽게도 말이오.\"\n\n가게 주인이 주문 장부를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"오, 잘못 배달된 겁니다, 손님.\" 그가 말했다. \"엔젤 클레어 씨가 주문하신 거라, 그분께 보냈어야 했군요.\"\n\n클레어 씨는 한 대 얻어맞은 듯 움찔했다. 그는 창백하고 풀이 죽어 집으로 돌아와, 엔젤을 서재로 불렀다.\n\n\"이 책을 좀 들여다봐라, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"이게 어떤 책인지 너는 아느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 주문했어요.\" 엔젤이 담담하게 말했다.\n\n\"무엇 하러?\"\n\n\"읽으려고요.\"\n\n\"어찌 그런 걸 읽을 생각을 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"어찌라뇨? 그건—철학 체계인걸요. 이보다 더 도덕적인, 아니 종교적이기까지 한 저작은 출간된 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그래—도덕적이긴 하겠지, 그건 부정 않겠다. 하지만 종교적이라니!—게다가 복음의 사역자가 되려는 너에게 말이다!\"\n\n\"아버지께서 그 이야기를 꺼내셨으니,\" 아들이 얼굴에 근심 어린 생각을 띠며 말했다. \"이참에 한 번에 말씀드리고 싶어요. 저는 성직을 받지 않는 편이 좋겠어요. 양심상 그럴 수 없을 것 같아요. 저는 교회를 부모를 사랑하듯 사랑해요. 앞으로도 늘 교회에 더없이 따뜻한 애정을 품을 거예요. 제가 그 역사를 이보다 더 깊이 흠모하는 제도는 없어요. 하지만 교회가 지탱될 수 없는 구속(救贖)의 우상 신학에서 자기 정신을 풀어놓기를 거부하는 한, 저는 형들처럼 정직하게 교회의 사역자로 안수받을 수가 없어요.\"\n\n이 곧고 단순한 마음의 교구 목사는, 제 살붙이 가운데 하나가 이 지경에 이르리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없었다! 그는 어안이 벙벙해지고, 충격을 받고, 마비되었다. 게다가 엔젤이 교회에 들어가지 않을 거라면, 케임브리지에 보내 봐야 무슨 소용이란 말인가? 정해진 관념에 사로잡힌 이 사내에게는, 안수가 아닌 다른 무언가로 가는 발판으로서의 대학이란, 본문 없는 서문처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 단지 종교적인 데 그치지 않고 독실한 사람이었다. 확고한 신자였으되—요즘 교회 안팎의 신학적 야바위꾼들이 얼버무리며 풀이하는 식이 아니라, 복음주의 학파의 옛 열렬한 의미로 그러했다. 그는 능히\n\n참으로 믿을 수 있었으니 영원하시고 거룩하신 분이 십팔 세기 전에 참으로……\n\n엔젤의 아버지는 논증으로, 설득으로, 간청으로 애썼다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아버지. 저는 신앙고백서가 요구하는 대로 '문자 그대로의, 문법적인 의미로' 받아들여 제4조를 인정할 수가 없어요(나머지는 말할 것도 없고요). 그러니 지금 형편으로는 목사가 될 수 없어요.\" 엔젤이 말했다. \"종교 문제에서 제 본능은 온통 재건을 향해 있어요. 아버지께서 좋아하시는 「히브리서」를 인용하자면, '흔들리는 것들은 만들어진 것이므로 제거하사, 흔들리지 않는 것들이 남게 하려 하심이라.'\"\n\n그의 아버지가 어찌나 깊이 슬퍼하던지, 엔젤은 그 모습을 보는 것만으로도 속이 다 아팠다.\n\n\"네 어머니와 내가 아껴 쓰고 절약해 가며 너를 대학에 보내 가르치는 게, 그게 하느님의 영예와 영광에 쓰이지 않을 거라면 무슨 소용이란 말이냐?\" 그의 아버지가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그건 인간의 영예와 영광에 쓰일 수 있잖아요, 아버지.\"\n\n어쩌면 엔젤이 고집을 부렸다면 형들처럼 케임브리지에 갈 수 있었을지도 모른다. 하지만 그 배움의 전당을 오직 성직으로 가는 디딤돌로만 보는 교구 목사의 견해는 가문의 전통이나 다름없었다. 그리고 그 관념이 아버지의 마음에 어찌나 깊이 뿌리박혀 있던지, 감수성 예민한 아들에게는 고집을 부리는 것이 마치 신탁을 횡령하려는 의도처럼, 또 집안의 경건한 어른들을 욕보이는 일처럼 보이기 시작했다. 아버지가 넌지시 비쳤듯이, 그분들은 세 아들에게 한결같은 교육 방침을 실천하느라 큰 절약을 해 왔고 또 하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"케임브리지는 안 가도 돼요.\" 마침내 엔젤이 말했다. \"이런 형편에 거길 갈 자격이 제겐 없다고 느껴요.\"\n\n이 결정적인 논쟁의 결과는 머지않아 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 두서없는 공부와 사업과 사색으로 여러 해를 보냈다. 사회적 형식과 관례에 상당한 무관심을 드러내기 시작했다. 지위와 부의 물질적 구별을 점점 더 경멸했다. 심지어 \"유서 깊은 명문가\"라는 것조차(이 고장의 한 작고한 명사가 즐겨 쓰던 말투를 빌리자면) 그 후손들에게 훌륭한 새 각오가 없는 한 그에게는 아무 향기도 없었다. 이러한 금욕에 균형을 맞추려는 듯, 세상이 어떤 곳인지 보려고, 또 거기서 직업이나 사업을 해 볼 생각으로 런던에 가서 살았을 때, 그는 정신을 잃고 자기보다 훨씬 나이 많은 여자에게 거의 옭아매일 뻔했다. 다행히 그 경험으로 크게 상하지 않고 빠져나오긴 했지만.\n\n어릴 적 시골의 고적함과 친했던 탓에 그에게는 현대 도시 생활에 대한, 이겨 낼 수 없고 거의 분별없을 지경의 혐오가 자라났고, 그것은 영적인 소명을 따르기가 불가능해진 마당에 세속적인 직업을 좇아 그가 바랐을 법한 성공으로부터도 그를 가로막았다. 하지만 무언가는 해야 했다. 그는 귀중한 세월을 숱하게 허비했다. 그리고 식민지 농부로 번창하는 삶을 막 시작한 지인이 하나 있던 터라, 엔젤은 이것이 어쩌면 올바른 방향으로 가는 길잡이일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 농사—식민지에서든, 미국에서든, 본국에서든—어쨌든 세심한 견습을 거쳐 그 일에 충분히 능숙해진 뒤의 농사야말로, 그가 생계 능력보다도 더 귀히 여기는 것, 곧 지적 자유를 희생하지 않고도 자립을 안겨 줄 만한 천직이었다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 스물여섯 살의 엔젤 클레어가 이곳 탤보세이스에서 소를 배우는 학생으로, 그리고 가까이에 편히 묵을 집이 없는 터라 낙농가의 하숙인으로 있는 것을 보게 된다.\n\n그의 방은 낙농장 집의 전체 길이만큼 뻗은 거대한 다락이었다. 치즈 다락에서 사다리로만 올라갈 수 있었고, 오랫동안 닫혀 있다가 그가 와서 자기 은신처로 고른 방이었다. 여기서 클레어는 공간이 넉넉했고, 식구들이 잠자리에 든 뒤 그가 이리저리 거니는 소리가 낙농장 사람들에게 곧잘 들리곤 했다. 한쪽 끝에는 휘장으로 한 칸이 막혀 있었고, 그 뒤에는 그의 침대가 있었으며, 바깥쪽은 수수한 거실로 꾸며져 있었다.\n\n처음에 그는 줄곧 위층에서 지내며 책을 꽤 읽고, 경매에서 산 낡은 하프를 퉁기곤 했다. 심사가 사나울 때면 언젠가 이걸로 길거리에서 밥벌이를 해야 할지도 모르겠다고 말하기도 했다. 하지만 그는 곧, 낙농가 부부와 처녀들과 사내들이 다 함께 어울려 활기찬 모임을 이루는 공용 식당 부엌에서 아래층 식사를 하며 인간 본성을 읽는 편을 더 좋아하게 되었다. 집 안에서 자는 젖꾼은 몇 안 되었지만, 여럿이 식사 때면 식구들과 합류했기 때문이다. 클레어는 이곳에 오래 머물수록 그 무리가 덜 거슬렸고, 그들과 한자리를 함께 나누는 것이 점점 더 좋아졌다.\n\n놀랍게도 그는 정말로 그들과의 어울림에서 참된 기쁨을 누렸다. 그의 상상 속 판에 박힌 농촌 사람들—신문 지면에서 호지라는 가련한 허수아비로 인격화되던 그 형상—은 며칠 머무는 사이 지워져 버렸다. 가까이서 보니 호지 따위는 없었다. 사실 처음에는, 대조적인 사회에서 갓 빠져나온 클레어의 지성에 그가 이제 허물없이 어울리는 이 벗들이 좀 낯설어 보이기는 했다. 낙농가네 식구들과 대등한 일원으로 자리에 앉는 것이 처음에는 체면 깎이는 일처럼 여겨졌다. 그 관념도, 방식도, 주변 환경도 퇴보적이고 무의미해 보였다. 하지만 거기서 날마다 살아가면서, 예리한 이 체류자는 그 광경에서 새로운 면모를 알아차리게 되었다. 객관적인 변화는 전혀 없었는데도, 단조로움 자리에 다양함이 들어선 것이다. 그의 주인과 주인의 집안 사람들, 사내들과 처녀들이, 클레어에게 친밀히 알려지면서 마치 화학 변화처럼 저마다 분화되기 시작했다. 파스칼의 한 마디가 그에게 절절히 와닿았다. \"지성이 높아질수록, 독창적인 사람들이 더 많이 보인다. 평범한 자들은 사람과 사람 사이의 차이를 알아보지 못한다.\" 전형적이고 한결같던 호지는 더는 존재하지 않았다. 그는 갖가지로 제각각인 동료 인간 여럿으로 해체되었다—마음씨가 가지각색이고, 그 차이가 무한한 존재들로. 더러는 행복하고, 다수는 평온하며, 몇몇은 풀이 죽었고, 여기저기 천재라 할 만큼 빛나는 이가 있는가 하면, 더러는 미련하고, 더러는 방종하고, 더러는 엄격했다. 더러는 말없는 밀턴 같았고, 더러는 잠재된 크롬웰 같았다—그가 제 친구들을 두고 그러듯 서로에 대해 나름의 견해를 품고, 서로를 칭찬하거나 책망할 수 있고, 서로의 약점이나 악덕을 바라보며 즐거워하거나 서글퍼할 수 있는 사람들로, 저마다 제 나름의 길을 걸어 흙먼지 어린 죽음으로 향하는 사람들로.\n\n뜻밖에도 그는 야외 생활을, 그 자체로, 또 그것이 가져다주는 것 때문에, 제 앞날의 행로와는 별개로 좋아하기 시작했다. 그의 처지를 생각하면, 그는 자비로운 권능에 대한 믿음이 스러지면서 문명화된 종족들을 사로잡고 있는 그 만성적 우울로부터 놀랄 만큼 자유로워졌다. 근래 몇 해 만에 처음으로 그는, 직업을 위해 벼락치기 공부를 할 요량 따위는 없이, 사색이 이끄는 대로 책을 읽을 수 있었다. 익혀 둘 만하다고 여긴 몇 권 안 되는 농사 안내서는 그의 시간을 거의 잡아먹지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n그는 옛 인연에서 멀어졌고, 삶과 인간 속에서 무언가 새로운 것을 보았다. 부차적으로는, 전에는 어렴풋이만 알던 현상들과 가까이 사귀게 되었다—저마다의 기분을 띤 계절들, 아침과 저녁, 밤과 한낮, 제각기 다른 성미의 바람들, 나무와 물과 안개, 그늘과 침묵, 그리고 무생물들의 목소리를.\n\n이른 아침은 아직 충분히 쌀쌀해서, 그들이 아침을 먹는 넓은 방에서는 불기운이 반가웠다. 그리고 크릭 부인의 분부에 따라—그녀는 그가 너무 점잖아서 자기네 식탁에서 함께 먹기는 어렵다고 여겼다—식사 동안 엔젤 클레어는 입을 떡 벌린 굴뚝 모퉁이에 앉는 것이 관례였고, 그의 찻잔과 받침과 접시는 팔꿈치 옆 경첩 달린 받침판 위에 놓였다. 맞은편 길고 넓은 중간설주 창에서 들어오는 빛이 그의 구석을 비추었고, 굴뚝을 타고 내려오는 차고 푸른 빛깔의 보조 광선까지 더해져, 그가 마음 내킬 때면 거기서 책을 수월하게 읽을 수 있었다. 클레어와 창 사이에는 그의 동료들이 둘러앉은 식탁이 있었고, 우물우물 씹는 그들의 옆얼굴이 유리창을 배경으로 또렷이 솟아 있었다. 한쪽으로는 우유 저장실 문이 있어, 그 너머로 줄지어 놓인 직사각형 통들이 아침에 짠 우유로 가득 차 찰랑이는 것이 보였다. 저 끝에서는 커다란 교유기가 돌아가는 게 보였고, 그 철벅철벅 소리가 들렸다—그 동력은, 사내아이가 모는 가운데 원을 그리며 도는 기운 없는 말의 모습으로 창 너머에 보였다.\n\n테스가 온 뒤 며칠 동안, 클레어는 우편으로 막 도착한 책이나 정기간행물이나 악보를 멍하니 읽느라, 식탁에 그녀가 있다는 것을 거의 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 말수가 워낙 적고 다른 처녀들은 워낙 떠들어 대서, 그 재잘거림이 그에게 새로운 가락으로 와닿지 않았다. 게다가 그는 늘 바깥 풍경의 세세한 부분은 무시하고 전체 인상만 받아들이는 버릇이 있었다. 하지만 어느 날, 그가 악보 하나를 들여다보며 상상의 힘으로 머릿속에서 그 가락을 듣고 있다가 무기력하게 빠져들자, 악보가 화로 쪽으로 또르르 굴러떨어졌다. 그는 통나무 불을 바라보았다. 아침 요리와 끓이기가 끝난 뒤 그 꼭대기에서 한 가닥 불꽃이 사위어 가는 춤을 추듯 빙글거리며, 그의 마음속 가락에 맞춰 지그를 추는 듯했다. 또 가로대, 곧 십자 막대에 매달린 두 개의 굴뚝 갈고리에는 검댕이 깃털처럼 앉아 있었는데, 그것도 같은 가락에 떨리는 듯했다. 또 반쯤 빈 주전자가 끙끙대며 반주를 넣는 것 같았다. 식탁의 대화가 그의 환상의 관현악과 뒤섞이더니, 그는 문득 이런 생각이 들었다. \"저 젖 짜는 처녀들 가운데 하나는 목소리가 어쩜 저리 피리 소리 같담! 새로 온 처녀인가 보군.\"\n\n클레어는 다른 처녀들과 함께 앉아 있는 그녀에게로 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n그녀는 그를 보고 있지 않았다. 사실 그가 오래도록 입을 다물고 있던 터라, 그가 방에 있다는 것은 거의 잊힌 상태였다.\n\n\"유령에 대해선 저도 잘 몰라요.\" 그녀가 말하고 있었다. \"하지만 우리가 살아 있는 동안에도 우리 영혼이 몸 밖으로 나가게 만들 수 있다는 건 알아요.\"\n\n낙농가가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 그녀에게로 돌아섰다. 그의 눈에는 진지한 호기심이 가득했고, 그의 큼지막한 나이프와 포크는(여기서 아침 식사는 진짜 아침 식사였다) 마치 교수대의 첫머리처럼 식탁 위에 곧추 꽂혀 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고—정말로? 그게 사실인가, 아가씨?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"영혼이 나가는 걸 느끼는 아주 쉬운 방법은요,\" 테스가 이어 갔다. \"밤에 풀밭에 누워서 크고 밝은 별 하나를 똑바로 올려다보는 거예요. 그러고 거기에 마음을 붙박고 있으면, 곧 자기가 몸에서 수백, 수백 마일이나 떨어져 있다는 걸 알게 돼요. 그 몸뚱이는 도무지 필요 없는 것처럼 느껴지고요.\"\n\n낙농가는 테스에게서 그 빤한 시선을 거두어 제 아내에게로 못 박았다.\n\n\"거참 희한한 일이로군, 크리스티애너—응? 지난 삼십 년간 별빛 비치는 밤에 구애하러, 장사하러, 의사 부르러, 산파 부르러 그렇게 숱한 마일을 터덜터덜 걸었으면서도, 이제껏 그런 건 전혀 생각도 못 했고, 내 영혼이 셔츠 깃 위로 단 한 치도 떠오르는 걸 느껴 본 적도 없으니 말이야.\"\n\n낙농가의 견습생까지 포함해 모두의 주의가 자기에게 쏠리자, 테스는 얼굴을 붉히고, 그저 공상일 뿐이라고 얼버무리며 다시 아침 식사를 들었다.\n\n클레어는 계속 그녀를 살폈다. 그녀는 곧 식사를 마쳤고, 클레어가 자기를 바라보고 있다는 의식이 들자, 누가 보고 있다는 걸 알아챈 집짐승처럼 어색해하며 집게손가락으로 식탁보에 가상의 무늬를 그리기 시작했다.\n\n\"저 젖 짜는 처녀는 어쩌면 저리 싱그럽고 순결한 자연의 딸인가!\" 그는 속으로 말했다.\n\n그러다 그는 그녀에게서 무언가 낯익은 것을, 곧 깊이 생각해야 할 필요가 하늘을 잿빛으로 물들이기 전의, 즐겁고 앞날을 걱정 없던 과거로 그를 데려가는 무언가를 알아보는 듯했다. 그는 그녀를 전에 본 적이 있다고 결론지었다. 어디서였는지는 알 수 없었다. 어느 시골 산책길에서 우연히 마주친 것임은 분명했고, 그는 그 일에 그리 큰 호기심을 품지는 않았다. 하지만 그 정황만으로도, 곁에 있는 여인을 바라보고 싶을 때면 다른 예쁜 젖 짜는 처녀들보다 테스를 골라 보게 되기에 충분했다.\n\n# XIX\n\nXIX\n대개 소들은 나타나는 대로, 호불호나 선택 없이 젖을 짰다. 하지만 어떤 소들은 특정한 한 쌍의 손을 좋아하는 기색을 보였고, 그 편애가 때로는 어찌나 심한지 제가 좋아하는 사람한테가 아니면 아예 서 있으려고도 하지 않아서, 낯선 이의 들통은 사정없이 걷어차여 엎어지곤 했다.\n\n낙농가 크릭의 원칙은, 끊임없이 소들을 바꿔 가며 이런 편애와 반감을 깨뜨려야 한다고 고집하는 것이었다. 그러지 않으면 젖꾼 사내나 처녀 하나가 낙농장을 떠날 경우 그가 곤란해지기 때문이었다. 하지만 처녀들의 속셈은 낙농가의 원칙과는 정반대였다. 처녀마다 제 손에 익은 소 여덟에서 열 마리를 날마다 골라 잡으면, 그 순한 젖통을 다루는 일이 놀랄 만큼 수월하고 힘들지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n테스도 동료들처럼, 어떤 소들이 제 손놀림을 좋아하는지 곧 알아냈다. 게다가 지난 두세 해 동안 틈틈이 스스로를 가두었던 그 긴 칩거 생활로 손가락이 섬약해진 터라, 그녀는 이 점에서 젖소들의 뜻을 기꺼이 맞추고 싶었다. 아흔다섯 마리 전체 가운데 특히 여덟 마리—덤플링, 팬시, 로프티, 미스트, 올드 프리티, 영 프리티, 타이디, 라우드—는, 비록 한두 마리는 젖꼭지가 당근처럼 단단하긴 했어도, 그녀에게는 흔쾌히 젖을 내주어서 그 일이 손가락만 살짝 대는 정도에 그쳤다. 하지만 낙농가의 바람을 아는 터라, 그녀는 아직 다룰 수 없는 아주 빡빡한 놈들만 빼고는 나오는 대로 양심껏 소들을 맡으려 애썼다.\n\n그런데 그녀는 곧, 겉으로는 우연인 듯한 소들의 위치와 이 문제에 관한 자기 바람 사이에 묘한 일치가 있음을 알아챘고, 마침내 그 배열이 우연의 결과일 리 없다고 느꼈다. 낙농가의 견습생이 요즘 소들을 모아들이는 일을 거들어 왔던 것이다. 그래서 대여섯 번째쯤 되었을 때, 그녀는 소에 기대 쉬다가 짓궂은 의심이 가득한 눈길을 그에게로 돌렸다.\n\n\"클레어 씨, 소들을 줄 세우셨군요!\" 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 그렇게 따지면서도, 자기도 모르게 미소의 기미가 윗입술을 살며시 들어 올려 이 끝이 드러났고, 아랫입술은 짐짓 엄하게 가만히 있었다.\n\n\"뭐, 상관없어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 언제까지나 여기서 그 소들 젖을 짤 테니까.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요? 저도 그러길 바라요! 하지만 모르는 일이죠.\"\n\n그녀는 나중에 자기 자신에게 화가 났다. 그가, 이 은둔을 좋아하는 자신의 무거운 까닭을 알지 못한 채, 자기 말뜻을 오해했을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서였다. 마치 그가 곁에 있는 것이 어쩐지 자기 바람의 한 요소이기라도 한 양, 그녀가 그에게 너무 간곡히 말했던 것이다. 그 불안이 어찌나 컸던지, 해 질 녘 젖 짜기가 끝나자 그녀는 정원을 홀로 거닐며, 그의 사려 깊음을 알아챘다고 그에게 털어놓은 것을 두고두고 후회했다.\n\n유월의 전형적인 여름 저녁이었다. 대기가 어찌나 섬세한 평형을 이루고 어찌나 잘 전해 주는지, 무생물조차 다섯까지는 아니어도 두세 가지 감각을 지닌 듯했다. 가까운 것과 먼 것 사이에 구별이 없었고, 듣는 이는 지평선 안의 모든 것이 바로 곁에 있는 듯 느꼈다. 그 무성(無聲)은 그녀에게 단순한 소음의 부재라기보다 하나의 분명한 실체로 다가왔다. 그것은 현을 퉁기는 소리에 깨졌다.\n\n테스는 그 음들을 자기 머리 위 다락에서 들은 적이 있었다. 갇힌 채로 어둑하고 납작하게 짓눌려 있던 그 소리들이, 이제 고요한 대기 속을 헤매며 나신(裸身)과도 같은 적나라한 기운을 띠고 흐르자, 지금처럼 그녀에게 절절히 와닿은 적이 없었다. 솔직히 말하자면, 악기도 연주도 변변치 못했다. 하지만 모든 것은 상대적이었고, 귀를 기울이던 테스는 홀린 새처럼 그 자리를 떠날 수 없었다. 떠나기는커녕 그녀는 연주자 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그가 제 존재를 눈치채지 못하도록 산울타리 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 채로.\n\n테스가 와 있던 정원의 변두리는 몇 해째 가꾸지 않고 방치되어 있어서, 이제 축축하고 무성하게 즙 많은 풀이 우거져 손만 대도 꽃가루 안개를 피워 올렸다. 또 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 키 큰 잡초 꽃들이 피어 있었는데—그 붉고 노랗고 보라색인 빛깔이 가꾼 꽃들 못지않게 눈부신 색의 향연을 이루었다. 그녀는 고양이처럼 살금살금 이 무성한 풀숲을 헤치고 나아갔다. 치맛자락에 거품벌레 침을 묻히고, 발밑의 달팽이를 으스러뜨리고, 엉겅퀴 즙과 민달팽이 점액으로 손을 더럽히며, 사과나무 줄기에는 눈처럼 희지만 살갗에는 꼭두서니 같은 얼룩을 남기는 끈적한 깍지진디를 맨팔에 문질러 가며. 그렇게 그녀는, 여전히 그의 눈에 띄지 않은 채 클레어 아주 가까이까지 다가갔다.\n\n테스는 시간도 공간도 의식하지 못했다. 별을 응시하면 마음대로 끌어낼 수 있다고 그녀가 말했던 그 황홀이, 이제 그녀의 어떤 작정도 없이 찾아왔다. 그녀는 그 중고 하프의 가는 음들 위에 너울거렸고, 그 화음이 산들바람처럼 그녀를 통과하며 눈에 눈물을 고이게 했다. 떠도는 꽃가루는 그의 음들이 눈에 보이게 된 것 같았고, 정원의 축축함은 정원의 감수성이 흘리는 눈물 같았다. 거의 밤이 가까웠는데도, 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 잡초 꽃들은 마치 골똘함에 겨워 꽃잎을 닫지 않으려는 듯 환히 빛났고, 빛깔의 물결이 소리의 물결과 뒤섞였다.\n\n아직 비치고 있던 빛은 주로 서쪽 구름 둑에 난 커다란 구멍에서 나오는 것이었다. 다른 데는 이미 땅거미가 내린 가운데, 우연히 뒤에 남겨진 한 조각 대낮 같았다. 그는 구슬픈 가락을 끝맺었는데, 대단한 솜씨를 요하지 않는 아주 단순한 연주였다. 그녀는 또 한 곡이 시작될까 싶어 기다렸다. 하지만 연주에 싫증이 난 그는 무심코 울타리를 돌아 나와, 그녀 뒤로 어슬렁어슬렁 다가오고 있었다. 테스는, 뺨이 화끈 달아오른 채, 거의 움직이지도 않는 듯 슬그머니 자리를 떴다. 하지만 엔젤은 그녀의 밝은 여름 옷을 보았고, 입을 열었다. 그가 제법 멀리 떨어져 있었는데도 그 낮은 음성이 그녀에게 와닿았다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 슬그머니 비키는 거요, 테스?\" 그가 말했다. \"겁이 나요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 나리—바깥 것들이 무섭진 않아요. 특히나 사과꽃이 지고 온통 푸른 지금 같은 때는요.\"\n\n\"하지만 집 안의 두려움은 있는 거죠—응?\"\n\n\"음—네, 나리.\"\n\n\"무엇이?\"\n\n\"딱히 뭐라고 말씀드릴 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"우유가 시어지는 거?\"\n\n\"아니에요.\"\n\n\"인생 전반?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\"아—나도 그래요, 아주 자주. 살아 있다는 이 곤란한 처지가 꽤 심각한 일이지, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그래요—그렇게 말씀하시니 그렇네요.\"\n\n\"그래도, 당신 같은 어린 처녀가 벌써 그걸 그렇게 보리라곤 생각 못 했는데. 어쩌다 그러는 거요?\"\n\n그녀는 망설이며 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"자, 테스, 나한테만 살짝 말해 봐요.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 사물의 모습이 자기에게 어떻게 비치느냐고 묻는 줄 알고, 수줍게 대답했다—\n\n\"나무들은 캐묻는 눈을 가졌잖아요, 안 그래요?—그러니까, 가진 것처럼 보인다는 거예요. 그리고 강은 이렇게 말하죠—'왜 그런 눈초리로 나를 괴롭히느냐?' 그리고 수많은 내일이 죄다 한 줄로 늘어서 있는 게 보이는 것 같아요. 그중 첫째 것이 제일 크고 제일 또렷하고, 나머지는 멀찍이 설수록 점점 더 작아지고요. 하지만 죄다 어찌나 사납고 잔인한지, 마치 이렇게 말하는 것 같아요. '내가 간다! 나를 조심해! 나를 조심해!' …… 하지만 나리는, 음악으로 꿈을 불러일으켜서, 그런 끔찍한 공상을 죄다 쫓아 버릴 수 있잖아요!\"\n\n그는 이 젊은 여자가—한낱 젖 짜는 처녀에 불과해도 동료들의 부러움을 살 만한 그 희귀한 무언가를 지닌 그녀가—그토록 서글픈 상상을 빚어내는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그녀는 제 고장 말투로—여섯째 학년 교육의 도움을 조금 받아—거의 시대의 정서라 할 만한 감정을, 곧 현대성의 통증을 토로하고 있었다. 그런데 이른바 진보적 관념이라는 것이 실은 대부분 정의(定義)의 최신 유행에 지나지 않는다는 것을—'~학'이나 '~주의'로 끝나는 말로, 사람들이 수백 년 동안 어렴풋이 붙잡아 온 감각들을 더 정확히 표현한 것에 지나지 않는다는 것을—떠올리자, 그 깨달음이 그를 덜 사로잡았다.\n\n그래도, 그것이 아직 그토록 어린 그녀에게 찾아왔다는 것은 이상한 일이었다. 이상한 정도가 아니라, 깊은 인상을 주고, 흥미롭고, 가련했다. 그 원인을 짐작 못 하니, 강렬함은 경험의 길이가 아니라 강도(强度)에 달린 문제임을 그에게 일깨워 줄 만한 것이 아무것도 없었다. 테스의 한때 스쳐 간 육신의 화는, 그녀의 정신적 수확이었던 것이다.\n\n테스 쪽에서는, 성직자 집안 출신에 좋은 교육을 받고 육체적 궁핍도 모르는 사람이 어째서 살아 있는 것을 불운으로 여기는지 이해할 수 없었다. 불행한 순례자인 그녀 자신에게는 아주 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었다. 하지만 이 감탄스럽고 시적인 사람이 어떻게 굴욕의 골짜기로 내려간 적이 있을 수 있단 말인가, 우스 땅의 사람과 더불어—두세 해 전 그녀 자신이 느꼈던 것처럼—이렇게 느낀 적이 있을 수 있단 말인가. \"내 영혼은 차라리 질식하여 죽기를, 이 목숨보다 더 원하노라. 나는 그것이 역겹다. 나는 영영 살기를 원치 않노라.\"\n\n지금 그가 제 계급에서 벗어나 있는 것은 사실이었다. 하지만 그녀는 그것이 단지, 조선소의 표트르 대제처럼, 그가 알고 싶은 것을 배우고 있어서일 뿐임을 알았다. 그는 소젖을 짜야만 해서 짜는 게 아니라, 부유하고 번창한 낙농가, 지주, 농사꾼, 가축 사육자가 되는 법을 배우고 있어서 짜는 것이었다. 그는 미국이나 오스트레일리아의 아브라함이 되어, 군주처럼 제 양 떼와 소 떼를, 제 점박이와 얼룩이를, 제 종들과 하녀들을 호령할 터였다. 그래도 그녀에게는, 분명 책을 좋아하고 음악적이고 사색하는 젊은이가 아버지와 형들처럼 성직자가 아니라 굳이 농부가 되기로 작정했다는 게 이따금 도무지 이해되지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 둘 다 서로의 비밀에 대한 실마리를 쥐지 못한 채, 상대가 드러내는 것에 저마다 어리둥절해하며, 서로의 내력을 캐려 들지는 않은 채 서로의 성격과 기분에 대한 새 앎을 기다렸다.\n\n날마다, 시각마다, 그에게는 그녀 본성의 작은 한 획이, 그녀에게는 그의 한 획이 또 하나씩 다가왔다. 테스는 억눌린 삶을 살려 애썼지만, 제 안의 생명력이 얼마나 강한지는 거의 헤아리지 못했다.\n\n처음에 테스는 엔젤 클레어를 남자라기보다 하나의 지성으로 여기는 듯했다. 그런 지성으로서 그녀는 그를 자신과 견주었다. 그리고 그가 비추는 빛이 얼마나 풍성한지, 자신의 보잘것없는 지적 입지와 헤아릴 수 없는 안데스 같은 그의 높이 사이의 거리가 얼마나 먼지를 발견할 때마다, 그녀는 몹시 풀이 죽어, 자기 쪽에서 무엇이든 더 애써 볼 의욕마저 잃었다.\n\n어느 날 그가 무심코 그녀에게 고대 그리스의 목가적 삶에 대해 무언가 이야기했을 때, 그는 그녀의 낙담을 알아챘다. 그가 말하는 동안 그녀는 둑에서 \"도련님과 아가씨\"라 불리는 봉오리를 따고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 갑자기 그렇게 시름에 잠긴 얼굴이오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그저—제 자신에 대한 거예요.\" 그녀가 슬픔이 어린 가냘픈 웃음을 띠며 말했다. 그러면서 가다 말다 \"아가씨\" 하나의 껍질을 벗기기 시작했다. \"그냥, 저한테 어쩌면 있었을지도 모를 것에 대한 생각이요! 제 인생은 기회가 없어서 허비된 것만 같아요! 나리가 아시는 것, 읽으신 것, 보고 생각하신 것을 보면, 저는 제가 얼마나 아무것도 아닌 존재인지 느껴져요! 저는 성경 속에 살던 그 가련한 스바 여왕 같아요. 제 안엔 더는 기운이 없어요.\"\n\n\"이런, 그런 걸로 마음 쓰지 말아요! 아니,\" 그가 다소 열을 띠며 말했다. \"나야말로 기꺼이 도와주고말고요, 테스 양, 역사든 당신이 익혀 보고 싶은 어떤 방면의 독서든—\"\n\n\"또 아가씨네요.\" 그녀가 막 껍질 벗긴 봉오리를 내밀며 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?\"\n\n\"껍질을 벗겨 보면 늘 도련님보다 아가씨가 더 많다는 뜻이에요.\"\n\n\"도련님이니 아가씨니 하는 건 그만두고. 어떤 공부를 해 보고 싶지 않아요—이를테면 역사 같은 거?\"\n\n\"가끔은 제가 이미 아는 것 이상으론 그것에 대해 알고 싶지 않다는 생각이 들어요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"제가 그저 긴 줄의 하나일 뿐이라는 걸 배워서 무슨 소용이겠어요—어느 낡은 책에 저랑 꼭 같은 누군가가 적혀 있다는 걸 알아내고, 제가 그 사람 역할이나 되풀이하리라는 걸 아는 게 말이에요. 그건 절 슬프게 할 뿐이에요. 제일 좋은 건, 내 본성과 내 지난 행동이 수천수만의 그것과 꼭 같았다는 걸, 그리고 내 다가올 삶과 행동도 수천수만의 그것과 같으리라는 걸 기억하지 않는 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 정말로 아무것도 배우고 싶지 않다는 거요?\"\n\n\"왜—왜 해가 의로운 자에게나 불의한 자에게나 똑같이 비치는지, 그건 좀 알고 싶어요.\" 그녀가 목소리를 살짝 떨며 대답했다. \"하지만 그건 책이 가르쳐 주지 않는 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"테스, 그런 쓴소리는 그만!\" 물론 그는 관습적인 의무감에서 그렇게 말했을 뿐이었으니, 그런 의문은 지난날 그 자신에게도 낯설지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 그 익숙지 않은 입과 입술을 바라보며, 그는 흙의 딸인 이런 처녀가 그런 정서를 그저 어디서 주워들어 외운 것에 불과하리라 여겼다. 그녀는 도련님과 아가씨 껍질을 계속 벗겼다. 클레어는, 그녀가 부드러운 뺨 위로 시선을 떨굴 때 물결처럼 굽이지는 속눈썹을 잠시 바라보다가, 미적거리며 자리를 떴다. 그가 가고 나자 그녀는 한동안 서서, 마지막 봉오리를 생각에 잠겨 벗겼다. 그러다 몽상에서 깨어나, 그것과 그 꽃의 귀족 무리를 모조리 못마땅한 듯 땅바닥에 내던졌다. 제 어리석은 짓에 스스로 부아가 끓어오르면서, 동시에 마음 깊은 곳에서 따뜻함이 차오르는 가운데.\n\n그가 자기를 얼마나 멍청하다고 여길까! 그의 좋은 평가를 갈구하는 마음이 솟구치자, 그녀는 근래 잊으려 애써 온 것을, 그 결말이 하도 언짢았던 그것을 떠올렸다—제 집안이 기사 가문인 더버빌가와 동일하다는 사실을. 그것이 아무 쓸모없는 속성이고, 그 발견이 여러모로 그녀에게 재앙이었어도, 어쩌면 클레어 씨는, 신사이자 역사를 공부하는 사람으로서, 킹스비어 교회의 저 퍼벡 대리석과 설화석고로 된 사람들이 실은 그녀 자신의 직계 조상을 나타낸다는 것을, 또 그녀가 트랜트리지의 그자들처럼 돈과 야심으로 빚어진 가짜 더버빌이 아니라 뼛속까지 참된 더버빌이라는 것을 안다면, 도련님과 아가씨를 가지고 벌인 그 어린애 같은 짓은 잊을 만큼 그녀를 존중해 줄지도 몰랐다.\n\n하지만 그 사실을 털어놓는 모험을 감행하기 전에, 미심쩍은 마음의 테스는 낙농가에게, 클레어 씨가 돈과 땅을 다 잃은 옛 명문가를 크게 존중하는지 어떤지 물어, 그것이 클레어 씨에게 미칠 영향을 에둘러 떠보았다.\n\n\"클레어 씨로 말하자면,\" 낙농가가 힘주어 말했다. \"자네가 본 중에 가장 반골인 괴짜라네—제 집안 나머지하고는 영 딴판이지. 그리고 그가 무엇보다 더 싫어하는 게 하나 있다면, 바로 이른바 옛 명문가라는 관념이야. 그는 그러더군, 옛 가문이란 지난날 제 할 일을 다 해 버렸으니 이제 안에 남은 게 있을 리 없다고, 그게 이치에 맞는 말이라고 말이야. 이 골짜기 아래로 몇 마일이나 땅을 거느렸던 빌렛가니 드렝카드가니 그레이가니 세인트퀸틴가니 하디가니 굴드가니 하는 집안들이 있는데, 지금은 죄다 헐값에 사들일 수 있을 지경이라네. 아니, 여기 우리 어린 레티 프리들 알지, 걔가 패리델가의 하나야—킹스힝톡 너머로 땅을 잔뜩 가졌던 옛 가문 말일세. 지금은 웨식스 백작 소유가 됐지만, 그 백작이나 그 집안이 알려지기도 전부터 말이야. 그런데, 클레어 씨가 이걸 알아내고는 며칠 동안 그 가엾은 애한테 아주 비웃듯이 말하더군. '아!' 하고 그애한테 그래. '넌 결코 좋은 젖꾼이 못 될 거야! 네 솜씨는 죄다 옛날 옛적 팔레스타인에서 다 써 버렸으니, 더 많은 위업을 위해 기운을 차리려면 천 년은 묵정밭으로 쉬어야 한다고!' 며칠 전엔 어떤 사내애가 일자리를 구하러 와서 제 이름이 매트라고 하기에, 성을 물으니까 제가 성을 가졌단 얘긴 들어 본 적이 없다는 거야. 왜냐고 물으니, 제 집안이 자리 잡은 지 그리 오래되지 않아서 그런가 보다고 하더군. '아! 너야말로 내가 딱 원하는 애로구나!' 하고 클레어 씨가 벌떡 일어나 그애와 악수를 하면서 그래. '너한테 큰 기대를 거마.' 그러고는 그애한테 반 크라운을 줬지. 오 안 돼! 그는 옛 명문가라면 비위가 상한다니까!\"\n\n클레어의 견해를 이렇게 우스꽝스럽게 옮긴 말을 듣고 나니, 가엾은 테스는 약한 순간에라도 제 집안에 대해 한마디도 하지 않은 것이 다행스러웠다—비록 그 집안이, 워낙 오래되어 거의 한 바퀴를 돌아 새 가문이 되어 버렸다 할 만큼 유별나게 오래되긴 했어도. 게다가 그 점에서는, 다른 낙농장 처녀도 자기 못지않은 셈이었다. 그녀는 더버빌가의 묘소와, 자기가 그 이름을 물려받은 정복왕 시대의 그 기사에 대해 입을 다물었다. 클레어의 성격을 엿보고 나니, 그가 자기에게 흥미를 갖게 된 것이 주로 전통 없이 새롭다고 여겨지는 그녀의 면모 덕분이라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n# XX\n\nXX\n계절이 무르익어 갔다. 한 해 치의 꽃과 잎과 나이팅게일과 개똥지빠귀와 핀치와 그 밖의 덧없는 생명들이, 불과 일 년 전 다른 것들이—지금 이것들이 한낱 씨눈과 무기물 입자에 불과하던 그때—섰던 바로 그 자리를 차지했다. 동트는 빛의 광선이 봉오리를 끌어내 긴 줄기로 늘이고, 소리 없는 물줄기 속에 수액을 끌어 올리고, 꽃잎을 열고, 보이지 않는 분출과 숨결로 향기를 빨아 냈다.\n\n낙농가 크릭네의 처녀와 사내들의 살림은 편안하게, 평온하게, 더없이 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 그들의 처지는 어쩌면 사회적 등급에서 가장 행복한 위치였을 것이다. 궁핍이 끝나는 선 위에 있으면서도, 격식이 자연스러운 감정을 옥죄기 시작하고 닳아 빠진 유행의 압박이 넉넉함을 부족하게 여기게 만드는 선 아래에 있었으니 말이다.\n\n그렇게 잎이 무성한 시절이 지나갔다. 바깥에서는 나무가 우거지는 것만이 유일한 목표인 듯한 그 계절이. 테스와 클레어는 자기도 모르게 서로를 살폈고, 늘 정념의 가장자리에 아슬아슬 균형을 잡으면서도, 겉으로는 그 속에 빠지지 않으려는 듯했다. 그러는 내내 그들은, 한 골짜기의 두 시냇물이 만나는 것만큼이나 어김없이, 거역할 수 없는 법칙에 따라 서로를 향해 모여들고 있었다.\n테스는 근래의 삶에서 지금처럼 행복했던 적이 없었다.\n\n어쩌면 다시는 이만큼 행복할 수 없을지도 몰랐다. 우선 그녀는 이 새로운 환경 속에서 몸도 마음도 잘 들어맞았다. 씨가 뿌려진 자리에서 독성 지층까지 뿌리를 내렸던 어린 나무가 더 깊은 흙으로 옮겨 심긴 셈이었다. 게다가 그녀도, 클레어도, 아직은 호감과 사랑 사이의 모호한 땅 위에 서 있었다. 깊은 곳에는 아직 닿지 않았고, 어색하게 캐묻는 성찰도 아직 시작되지 않았다. \"이 새 물살은 나를 어디로 데려가려는 걸까? 내 미래에 이것은 무슨 뜻일까? 내 과거에 비추어 이것은 어떻게 서 있는가?\" 테스는 아직 엔젤 클레어에게 한낱 스쳐 가는 현상이었다. 그의 의식 속에서 겨우 막 지속성이라는 속성을 얻기 시작한, 장밋빛으로 마음을 데우는 환영이었다. 그래서 그는 자기 마음이 그녀에게 사로잡히도록 내버려 두었고, 그 몰두를 그저 지극히 새롭고 신선하며 흥미로운 여성이라는 표본을 향한 철학자의 관조쯤으로 여겼다.\n\n그들은 끊임없이 마주쳤다. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다. 그 기이하고 엄숙한 막간, 아침의 어스름 속에서, 보랏빛이나 분홍빛 새벽 속에서 그들은 날마다 마주쳤다. 이곳에서는 일찍 일어나야, 아주 일찍 일어나야 했기 때문이다. 젖 짜는 일은 일찌감치 이루어졌고, 그 전에는 크림 걷어 내는 일이 있었는데, 그것은 세 시가 조금 지나 시작되었다. 대개 누군가 한 사람이 나머지를 깨우는 몫을 맡았고, 첫 사람은 자명종으로 깼다. 테스가 가장 늦게 온 데다, 다른 이들처럼 자명종 소리를 듣고도 내처 자는 법이 없다는 것을 다들 곧 알아챘기에, 이 일은 가장 자주 그녀에게 떠맡겨졌다. 세 시를 알리는 종이 울리고 윙 하고 멎자마자, 그녀는 방을 나와 낙농장 주인의 방문으로 달려갔다. 그런 다음 사다리를 올라 엔젤의 방으로 가, 큰 속삭임으로 그를 불렀다. 그러고는 동료 젖 짜는 처녀들을 깨웠다. 테스가 옷을 다 입을 무렵이면 클레어는 아래층에 내려와 축축한 공기 속에 나가 있었다. 나머지 처녀들과 낙농장 주인은 대개 베개 위에서 한 번 더 몸을 뒤척이고, 십오 분쯤 지나서야 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n동틀 녘의 잿빛 중간 색조는, 그 음영의 정도가 같다 해도, 하루가 저무는 잿빛 중간 색조와 같지 않다. 아침의 어스름 속에서는 빛이 능동적이고 어둠이 수동적으로 보인다. 저녁의 어스름 속에서는 어둠이 능동적이고 차오르는 것이며, 빛은 졸린 듯 물러서는 쪽이다.\n\n그렇게 자주, 어쩌면 늘 우연만은 아니게, 낙농장에서 가장 먼저 일어나는 두 사람이었기에, 그들은 스스로를 온 세상에서 가장 먼저 깨어난 사람들로 여겼다. 이곳에 머문 초기의 그 나날들에 테스는 크림을 걷지 않고, 일어나자마자 곧장 바깥으로 나갔는데, 거기에는 대개 그가 그녀를 기다리고 있었다. 트인 풀밭에 가득 퍼진, 유령 같고 반쯤만 형체를 갖춘 물빛은 그들에게 고립감을 심어 주었다. 마치 그들이 아담과 이브인 것처럼. 하루가 아직 어둑하게 시작되는 이 단계에서 테스는 클레어에게 기질과 체구 양쪽으로 위엄 있는 큼직함을, 거의 군림하는 듯한 힘을 드러내 보이는 듯했다. 아마도 그 비현실적인 시각에, 그의 시야 안 트인 들판을 거닐 만한, 그녀처럼 몸이 그토록 잘 갖추어진 여인은 좀처럼 없으리라는 것을—온 잉글랜드를 통틀어도 극히 드물리라는 것을—그가 알았기 때문이리라. 아름다운 여인들은 보통 한여름 새벽에 잠들어 있다. 그녀는 바로 곁에 있었고, 나머지는 어디에도 없었다.\n\n소 떼가 누운 자리로 그들이 함께 걸어가던, 그 뒤섞이고 야릇하며 빛나는 어둠은 그에게 곧잘 부활의 시각을 떠올리게 했다. 막달레나가 자기 곁에 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 거의 하지 못했다. 온 풍경이 중립적인 음영 속에 잠겨 있을 때, 그의 시선이 모이는 초점인 그녀의 얼굴은 안개층 위로 떠올라 일종의 인광을 머금은 듯했다. 그녀는 유령 같았다. 마치 그저 풀려난 한 영혼인 것처럼. 사실은 그녀의 얼굴이, 그런 기색도 없이, 북동쪽에서 비치는 차가운 새벽빛을 받은 것이었다. 그 자신의 얼굴도, 그는 생각지 못했지만, 그녀에게는 똑같은 모습으로 비쳤다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 바로 그때 그녀는 그에게 가장 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 그녀는 더는 젖 짜는 처녀가 아니라, 여성의 환영 같은 정수였다. 하나의 전형적 형상으로 응축된 성性 전체였다. 그는 그녀를 아르테미스라고, 데메테르라고, 그 밖의 공상적인 이름들로 반쯤 놀리듯 불렀는데, 그녀는 그 뜻을 알지 못했기에 좋아하지 않았다.\n\n\"테스라고 불러 주세요.\" 그녀는 곁눈질하며 말하곤 했고, 그는 그렇게 했다.\n\n그러면 점점 날이 밝아 왔고, 그녀의 이목구비는 그저 여성다워졌다. 행복을 내려 줄 수 있는 여신의 모습에서, 그것을 갈망하는 한 존재의 모습으로 바뀌어 있었다.\n\n이 인간 아닌 시각에 그들은 물새들에게 아주 가까이 다가갈 수 있었다. 왜가리들이, 문과 덧창을 여는 듯한 크고 거침없는 소리를 내며, 풀밭 옆 자기들이 자주 찾는 인공림의 가지에서 날아 나왔다. 또는 이미 그 자리에 있을 때면, 두 사람이 지나가도 물속에 굳건히 버티고 서서, 시계태엽으로 도는 꼭두각시처럼 느리고 수평이며 무심한 회전으로 머리를 돌려 그들을 지켜보았다.\n\n그때 그들은 여름의 옅은 안개가 층층이, 양털처럼, 평평하게, 겉보기엔 침대보보다 두껍지 않게, 작게 떨어진 자투리로 풀밭 여기저기 깔린 것을 볼 수 있었다. 풀의 잿빛 물기 위에는 소들이 밤새 누워 있던 자국이 남아 있었다. 온통 이슬의 바다 속에, 소의 몸뚱이만 한 크기로 짙은 초록빛 마른 풀이 섬을 이룬 자국이었다. 그 섬마다에서 구불구불한 자취가 뻗어 나갔는데, 소가 일어난 뒤 풀을 뜯으러 어슬렁어슬렁 멀어져 간 길이었고, 그 자취 끝에서 그들은 소를 찾아냈다. 소가 그들을 알아보고 콧구멍으로 코를 골 듯 뿜는 숨결은, 자욱한 안개 한가운데 제 나름의 더 짙은 작은 안개를 만들어 냈다. 그러고 나서 그들은 짐승들을 가축마당으로 몰거나, 경우에 따라 그 자리에 앉아 젖을 짰다.\n\n또는 어쩌면 여름 안개가 더 자욱하게 퍼져, 풀밭이 하얀 바다처럼 누워 있고, 그 속에서 흩어진 나무들이 위험한 암초처럼 솟아 있기도 했다. 새들이 그 사이로 솟구쳐 위쪽 광휘 속으로 올라가, 날개를 편 채 매달려 햇볕을 쬐거나, 풀밭을 갈라놓은 젖은 난간—이제는 유리 막대처럼 반짝이는—에 내려앉았다. 안개에서 맺힌 자잘한 물기의 다이아몬드가 테스의 속눈썹에도 매달렸고, 머리카락 위에는 씨앗 진주 같은 물방울이 맺혔다. 날이 완전히 강해지고 평범해지면 이것들은 그녀에게서 말라 버렸다. 게다가 그때면 테스는 그 기이하고 천상적인 아름다움을 잃었다. 그녀의 이와 입술과 눈이 햇살 속에서 반짝였고, 그녀는 다시 세상의 다른 여인들을 상대로 제 자리를 지켜야 하는, 눈부시게 아름다운 젖 짜는 처녀일 뿐이었다.\n\n이 무렵이면 그들은 낙농장 주인 크릭의 목소리를 듣곤 했다. 늦게 온 비거주 젖 짜는 일꾼들을 나무라고, 손을 씻지 않았다고 늙은 데버라 파인더에게 날카롭게 호통치는 소리였다.\n\n\"제발 펌프 밑에 손 좀 쑤셔 넣어, 데브! 정말이지, 런던 사람들이 자네하고 자네 칠칠치 못한 꼴을 알기만 하면, 지금도 우유랑 버터를 입에 댈 때 까다롭게 구는 판에, 더 깨작거리며 삼킬 거야. 그것도 보통 일이 아니지.\"\n\n젖 짜기가 진행되었고, 거의 끝나 갈 무렵이면 테스와 클레어도 나머지 사람들과 함께, 크릭 부인이 부엌에서 묵직한 아침 식탁을 벽에서 끌어내는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 이것은 끼니마다 변함없이 앞서는 일이었고, 식탁을 치운 뒤 그것을 다시 제자리로 끌어다 놓을 때도 똑같은 끔찍한 긁는 소리가 따라붙었다.\n\n# XXI\n\n아침 식사 직후 우유 작업장에 큰 소동이 벌어졌다. 교유기는 여느 때처럼 돌아갔지만, 버터가 나오지 않았다. 이런 일이 벌어질 때마다 낙농장은 마비되었다. 큰 원통 속에서 우유가 철벅철벅 소리를 냈지만, 그들이 기다리는 그 소리는 끝내 나지 않았다.\n\n낙농장 주인 크릭과 그 아내, 젖 짜는 처녀들인 테스, 매리언, 레티 프리들, 이즈 휴엣, 그리고 오두막에서 온 기혼 여인들, 또 클레어 씨와 조너선 카일, 늙은 데버라, 그 밖의 사람들이 모두 절망스러운 얼굴로 교유기를 바라보며 서 있었다. 바깥에서 말을 돌리던 소년은 자기가 사태를 알고 있다는 표시로 달덩이 같은 눈을 떴다. 우울한 말조차도 한 바퀴 돌 때마다 캐묻는 듯한 절망으로 창문 안을 들여다보는 듯했다.\n\n\"에그던에 사는 마술사 트렌들의 아들한테 찾아간 게 벌써 몇 년 전이야—몇 년이라고!\" 낙농장 주인이 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"게다가 그 아들놈은 제 아비에 비하면 아무것도 아니었지. 한 번 말했으면 쉰 번은 말했을 거야, 나는 그자를 안 믿는다고. 사람들 오줌점은 아주 잘 치긴 하지만 말이야. 그래도 그자가 살아 있다면 가 봐야겠지. 아 그래, 가 봐야겠어, 이런 일이 계속된다면!\"\n\n클레어 씨조차도 낙농장 주인의 절망에 비극적인 기분이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 어렸을 적엔 캐스터브리지 건너편에 사는, '와이드오'라고들 부르던 마술사 폴이 아주 용한 사람이었지요.\" 조너선 카일이 말했다. \"한데 이제는 다 썩은 부싯깃처럼 됐어요.\"\n\n\"우리 할아버지는 아울스콤 너머의 마술사 민턴한테 다니셨는데, 아주 솜씨 좋은 사람이었다고, 할아버지 말씀을 들었지.\" 크릭 씨가 이었다. \"그런데 요즘엔 그런 진짜배기 사람이 없단 말이야!\"\n\n크릭 부인의 마음은 눈앞의 일에 더 가까이 머물러 있었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 이 집 누군가가 사랑에 빠진 건지도 모르죠.\" 그녀가 조심스레 말했다. \"젊었을 적에 들으니, 그게 이런 일을 일으킨다더군요. 아니, 크릭—몇 년 전에 우리 집에 있던 그 처녀 기억나요? 그때도 버터가 안 나왔잖아요—\"\n\n\"아 그래, 그래!—하지만 그게 그 까닭은 아니지. 연애하고는 아무 상관 없었어. 나는 그 일 다 기억하는데—그건 교유기가 망가져서 그랬던 거야.\"\n\n그가 클레어를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"한때 우리 집에서 젖 짜는 일을 하던 잭 돌럽이라는 화냥년의 자식 같은 놈이 있었지요, 선생. 그놈이 멜스톡 너머에 사는 한 처녀한테 구애하고는, 전에 여럿한테 그랬듯이 그 처녀도 속여 먹었어요. 그런데 이번에는 상대를 잘못 골랐지요. 처녀 본인이 아니었거든요. 달력에 박힌 날 중에서 하필 성목요일에, 우리는 지금처럼 여기 있었는데, 다만 그땐 교유 작업은 없었지요. 그때 그 처녀 어미가 문으로 올라오는 걸 봤는데, 손에는 황소도 때려눕힐 만한 커다란 놋쇠 장식 우산을 들고서 이러는 거예요. '잭 돌럽이 여기서 일하나요?—그놈한테 볼일이 있어서요! 그놈하고 크게 따질 게 있단 말입니다, 분명히요!' 그리고 어미 뒤로 좀 떨어져서 잭의 처녀가, 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 서럽게 울며 걸어왔지요. '오 맙소사, 큰일 났네!' 잭이 창밖으로 그들을 내다보며 말했어요. '저 여자가 날 죽이겠어! 어디로 가지—어디로—? 내가 어디 있는지 말하지 마!' 그러더니 그놈은 뚜껑 문으로 교유기 안에 기어들어 가서, 처녀 어미가 우유 작업장으로 막 들이닥칠 때 안에 틀어박혀 버렸지요. '그 악당이—어디 있어요?' 어미가 말했어요. '잡기만 하면 얼굴을 할퀴어 놓을 테니, 어디 한번 잡아 보게 해 줘요!' 자, 어미가 사방을 뒤지며, 잭을 이쪽저쪽 솔기까지 욕을 퍼붓는데, 잭은 교유기 안에서 거의 숨이 막혀 누워 있고, 가엾은 처녀는—아니 젊은 여인이라고 해야겠군요—문간에 서서 눈이 빠지게 울고 있었지요. 나는 그걸 결코 못 잊을 거예요, 결코! 대리석 돌도 녹였을 거라니까요! 한데 어미는 그놈을 아무 데서도 찾지 못했어요.\"\n\n낙농장 주인이 말을 멈추자, 듣던 이들에게서 한두 마디 평이 흘러나왔다.\n\n낙농장 주인 크릭의 이야기는 흔히 끝난 듯해도 정말로 끝난 게 아닐 때가 많았고, 낯선 이들은 섣불리 끝맺음의 감탄사를 내뱉도록 속아 넘어가곤 했다. 오랜 친구들은 사정을 더 잘 알았지만. 이야기꾼은 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자, 그 노파가 어떻게 그걸 알아챌 만한 머리가 돌아갔는지 나는 도무지 알 수 없지만, 어쨌든 그놈이 저 교유기 안에 들어 있다는 걸 알아냈지요. 노파는 한마디 말도 없이 손잡이를 잡고는 (그땐 손으로 돌렸거든요), 그놈을 빙빙 돌려 댔고, 잭은 안에서 펄떡거리기 시작했어요. '오 맙소사! 교유기 멈춰요! 나 좀 꺼내 줘요!' 그놈이 머리를 쑥 내밀며 말했지요. '내가 교유기에 갈려 푸딩이 되겠어요!' (그놈은 속으로는 비겁한 놈이었어요, 그런 자들이 대개 그렇듯이.) '그 애의 순결한 정조를 짓밟은 걸 보상하기 전까지는 안 돼!' 노파가 말했어요. '교유기 멈춰요, 이 늙은 마녀야!' 그놈이 비명을 질렀지요. '나더러 늙은 마녀라고, 이 사기꾼아!' 노파가 말했어요. '지난 다섯 달 동안 나를 장모님이라고 불렀어야 마땅한 주제에!' 그러고는 교유기가 계속 돌아갔고, 잭의 뼈가 다시 덜그럭덜그럭 굴렀지요. 자, 우리 중 누구도 감히 끼어들 엄두를 못 냈어요. 그러다 마침내 그놈이 그 애한테 일을 바로잡겠다고 약속했지요. '그래요—약속은 꼭 지키겠어요!' 그놈이 말했어요. 그래서 그날은 그렇게 끝났지요.\"\n\n듣던 이들이 미소 지으며 한마디씩 하고 있을 때, 그들 등 뒤에서 빠른 움직임이 있었고, 그들은 돌아보았다. 테스가 창백한 얼굴로 문 쪽으로 가 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 참 덥네요!\" 그녀가 거의 들리지 않게 말했다.\n\n날이 덥긴 했고, 그들 중 누구도 그녀가 물러난 것을 낙농장 주인의 회고담과 연결 짓지 않았다. 그가 다가가 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주며, 다정한 농담조로 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아가씨\" (그는 무의식적인 반어로 그녀에게 이 애칭을 자주 붙였다), \"내 낙농장에서 제일 예쁜 젖 짜는 처녀가 말이야, 여름 날씨 첫 기운에 이렇게 지쳐 버리면 안 되지. 안 그러면 한여름 복중에 자네가 없어서 우리가 톡톡히 곤란해질 거 아닌가, 안 그렇소, 클레어 씨?\"\n\n\"좀 어지러워서요—그리고—밖에 나가 있는 게 나을 것 같아요.\" 그녀는 기계적으로 말하고는 밖으로 사라졌다.\n\n다행스럽게도 그 순간 돌아가던 교유기 속 우유가 철벅거리던 소리를 바꿔 또렷한 찰싹찰싹 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"나온다!\" 크릭 부인이 외쳤고, 모두의 관심이 테스에게서 떨어져 나갔다.\n\n그 아름다운 고통받는 이는 곧 겉으로는 평정을 되찾았다. 그러나 오후 내내 몹시 우울한 채로 있었다. 저녁 젖 짜기가 끝나자 그녀는 다른 이들과 함께 있고 싶지 않았고, 밖으로 나가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 거닐었다. 그녀는 비참했다—오 너무도 비참했다—동료들에게는 낙농장 주인의 이야기가 그저 우스운 이야기쯤이었지 그 이상이 아니었다는 깨달음에. 그녀 말고는 누구도 그 슬픔을 보는 것 같지 않았다. 분명히 그 누구도 그것이 자기 경험의 여린 자리를 얼마나 잔인하게 건드렸는지 알지 못했다. 이제 저녁 해는 그녀에게 추악해 보였다. 하늘에 난 크게 곪은 상처 같았다. 다만 외로운, 목쉰 갈대참새 한 마리가 강가 덤불에서, 슬프고도 기계로 찍어 낸 듯한 가락으로 그녀에게 인사했다. 우정이 닳아 버린 지난날의 한 벗을 닮은 가락으로.\n\n이 긴 유월의 나날에 젖 짜는 처녀들은, 사실 집안 식구 대부분이, 해 질 무렵이나 그보다 일찍 잠자리에 들었다. 젖 짜기에 앞선 아침 일이 양동이가 가득 차는 철에는 너무도 일찍 시작되고 고됐기 때문이다. 테스는 보통 동료들을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 그러나 이날 밤에는 공동 침실로 가장 먼저 갔고, 다른 처녀들이 들어왔을 때 그녀는 이미 깜박 졸고 있었다. 그녀는 그들이 사라진 해의 주황빛 속에서 옷을 벗는 것을 보았다. 그 빛이 그들의 몸을 제 빛깔로 물들였다. 그녀는 다시 졸았지만, 그들의 목소리에 다시 깨어나 조용히 그들 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다.\n\n세 침실 동무 중 누구도 아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다. 그들은 잠옷 바람에 맨발로 창가에 한 무리로 서 있었고, 서쪽의 마지막 붉은 햇살이 여전히 그들의 얼굴과 목과 주위의 벽을 데우고 있었다. 모두 깊은 관심으로 정원의 누군가를 지켜보고 있었고, 세 얼굴이 바싹 붙어 있었다. 하나는 명랑하고 둥근 얼굴, 하나는 검은 머리에 창백한 얼굴, 그리고 하나는 적갈색 머리채의 아름다운 얼굴이었다.\n\n\"밀지 마! 나만큼 너도 잘 보이잖아.\" 적갈색 머리에 막내인 레티가 창문에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"네가 그분을 사랑해 봐야 나만큼이나 소용없어, 레티 프리들.\" 명랑한 얼굴의 맏이 매리언이 짓궂게 말했다. \"그분 마음은 네 뺨이 아닌 다른 뺨에 가 있단 말이야!\"\n\n레티 프리들은 여전히 바라보았고, 다른 이들도 다시 바라보았다.\n\n\"저기 또 나오신다!\" 검고 축축한 머리에 날카롭게 깎인 입술을 가진 창백한 처녀, 이즈 휴엣이 외쳤다.\n\n\"넌 아무 말도 할 처지 아냐, 이즈.\" 레티가 대꾸했다. \"네가 그분 그림자에 입 맞추는 거 내가 봤거든.\"\n\n\"걔가 뭘 하는 걸 봤다고?\" 매리언이 물었다.\n\n\"아니—그분이 유청 통 위에 서서 유청을 빼고 있었는데, 그 얼굴 그림자가 뒤쪽 벽에 비쳤거든. 마침 거기 통을 채우며 서 있던 이즈 바로 가까이에 말이야. 걔가 입을 벽에 대고 그분 입 그림자에 입을 맞추는 거야. 그분은 못 봤지만 나는 봤다고.\"\n\n\"오 이즈 휴엣!\" 매리언이 말했다.\n\n이즈 휴엣의 뺨 한가운데에 장밋빛 점이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"흥, 그게 뭐 나쁠 거 있어.\" 그녀가 짐짓 태연한 척하며 단언했다. \"그리고 내가 그분을 사랑한다면, 레티 너도 그렇고, 따지고 보면 매리언 너도 그래.\"\n\n매리언의 통통한 얼굴은 만성적인 분홍빛을 넘어 더 붉어질 수가 없었다.\n\n\"내가!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"무슨 소릴! 아, 저기 또 나오신다! 사랑스러운 눈—사랑스러운 얼굴—사랑스러운 클레어 씨!\"\n\n\"거봐—네가 인정했어!\"\n\n\"너도 했지—우리 다 했어.\" 매리언이 남들 생각 따위 완전히 무심한 데서 오는 무미건조한 솔직함으로 말했다. \"우리끼리 안 그런 척하는 건 어리석은 일이지, 다른 사람들한테야 인정할 필요 없지만. 나라면 내일이라도 당장 그분과 결혼하겠어!\"\n\n\"나도—그 이상이라도.\" 이즈 휴엣이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 더 소심한 레티가 속삭였다.\n\n엿듣던 이는 마음이 뜨거워졌다.\n\n\"우리 모두가 그분과 결혼할 순 없잖아.\" 이즈가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 중 누구도 못 할걸. 그게 더 나쁜 거지.\" 맏이가 말했다.\n\"저기 또 나오신다!\"\n\n세 사람 모두 그에게 소리 없이 입맞춤을 날렸다.\n\n\"왜?\" 레티가 재빨리 물었다.\n\n\"그분은 테스 더비필드를 제일 좋아하시니까.\" 매리언이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"내가 날마다 지켜봐서 알아냈어.\"\n\n생각에 잠긴 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"하지만 걔는 그분한테 아무 마음도 없잖아?\" 마침내 레티가 숨죽여 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄—나도 가끔은 그렇게 생각해.\"\n\n\"하지만 이게 다 얼마나 어리석은 짓이람!\" 이즈 휴엣이 못 참고 말했다. \"당연히 그분은 우리 중 누구하고도, 테스하고도 결혼 안 하실 거야—신사의 아들이고, 해외에서 큰 지주에 농장주가 될 분인데! 차라리 우리한테 1년에 얼마씩 받고 농장 일꾼으로 따라오라고 하실 가능성이 더 크지!\"\n\n한 사람이 한숨을 쉬고, 또 한 사람이 한숨을 쉬었으며, 매리언의 통통한 몸이 가장 크게 한숨지었다. 바로 곁 침대에 누운 누군가도 한숨을 쉬었다. 예쁘고 붉은 머리의 막내, 레티 프리들의 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그 고장 연대기에서 그토록 중요한 패리델 가문의 마지막 봉오리. 그들은 좀 더 말없이 지켜보았고, 세 얼굴은 여전히 아까처럼 바싹 붙어 있었으며, 세 가지 머리 빛깔이 뒤섞였다. 그러나 아무것도 모르는 클레어 씨가 안으로 들어가 버려, 그들은 더는 그를 볼 수 없었다. 그리고 어둠이 짙어지기 시작하자, 그들은 슬그머니 침대로 기어들었다. 몇 분 뒤 그들은 그가 사다리를 올라 자기 방으로 가는 소리를 들었다. 매리언은 곧 코를 골았지만, 이즈는 오랫동안 망각에 빠져들지 못했다. 레티 프리들은 울다가 잠들었다.\n\n더 깊은 정념을 지닌 테스는 그때조차도 잠과는 거리가 멀었다. 이 대화는 그날 그녀가 삼켜야만 했던 또 하나의 쓴 약이었다. 그녀의 가슴에는 질투의 감정이 거의 일지 않았다. 사실 그녀는 자기가 선택받았음을 알고 있었다. 더 곱게 빚어졌고, 더 잘 배웠으며, 레티를 빼면 가장 어렸지만 둘 중 누구보다 더 여인다웠기에, 이 솔직한 벗들을 상대로 엔젤 클레어의 마음속에서 제 자리를 지키는 데는 그저 아주 약간의 평범한 신경만 쓰면 된다는 것을 그녀는 알아챘다. 그러나 심각한 물음은 이것이었다. 과연 그래도 되는 걸까? 물론 진지한 의미에서는 셋 중 누구에게도 유령만 한 가능성조차 거의 없었다. 그러나 누군가 한 사람이 그에게 한때의 마음을 불러일으키고, 그가 여기 머무는 동안 그의 관심을 받는 기쁨을 누릴 가능성은 있었거나, 있어 왔다. 그런 어울리지 않는 인연이 결혼으로 이어진 적도 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 크릭 부인에게서, 클레어 씨가 어느 날 웃으며 이렇게 물었다는 이야기를 들었다. 만 에이커나 되는 식민지 목초지를 먹여 살리고 가축을 기르고 곡식을 거둬야 하는 마당에, 고상한 숙녀와 결혼해서 무슨 소용이 있겠느냐고. 농사일을 아는 여자만이 그에게 분별 있는 유일한 아내일 거라고. 그러나 클레어 씨가 진지하게 말한 것이든 아니든, 이제 어떤 남자에게도 양심상 결코 자기와 결혼하도록 허락할 수 없는 그녀가, 그리고 결코 그런 유혹에 넘어가지 않으리라 신앙심으로 굳게 다짐한 그녀가, 그가 탤보세이스에 머무는 동안 그의 눈길 속에서 햇볕을 쬐는 짧은 행복을 위해, 무엇 때문에 클레어 씨의 관심을 다른 여자들에게서 돌려놓아야 한단 말인가?\n\n# XXII\n\n이튿날 아침 그들은 하품을 하며 아래층으로 내려왔다. 그러나 크림 걷기와 젖 짜기는 여느 때처럼 진행되었고, 그들은 아침을 먹으러 안으로 들어갔다. 낙농장 주인 크릭이 집 안을 쿵쿵 돌아다니고 있는 게 보였다. 그는 편지를 한 통 받았는데, 한 고객이 버터에서 이상한 맛이 난다고 불평한 내용이었다.\n\n\"제기랄, 정말 그렇단 말이야!\" 낙농장 주인이 왼손에 버터 한 덩이가 붙은 나무 주걱을 들고 말했다. \"그래—직접 맛들 보라고!\"\n\n여럿이 그의 주위로 모여들었다. 클레어 씨가 맛보고, 테스가 맛보고, 다른 실내 젖 짜는 처녀들도, 젖 짜는 일꾼 한둘도, 그리고 마지막으로 기다리던 아침 식탁에서 나온 크릭 부인도 맛보았다. 정말로 이상한 맛이 났다.\n\n낙농장 주인은 그 맛을 더 잘 느껴 그것이 어떤 종류의 해로운 잡초에서 비롯된 것인지 알아내려고 골똘히 정신을 집중하더니, 갑자기 외쳤다.\n\n\"마늘이야! 게다가 나는 그 풀밭에 마늘잎이 한 가닥도 안 남은 줄 알았는데!\"\n\n그러자 오래된 일꾼들이 모두 떠올렸다. 최근에 소 몇 마리를 들여보낸 어떤 마른 풀밭이, 지난 세월에도 똑같은 식으로 버터를 망쳤다는 것을. 그때 낙농장 주인은 그 맛을 알아채지 못해 버터에 마법이 걸린 줄로만 알았었다.\n\n\"그 풀밭을 샅샅이 뒤져야겠어.\" 그가 다시 말했다. \"이런 일이 계속되면 안 돼!\"\n\n모두 끝이 뾰족한 헌 칼로 무장하고 함께 나갔다. 보통 눈에 띄지 않을 정도로 아주 미세한 크기로만 그 적대적인 풀이 있을 수 있었기에, 눈앞의 무성한 풀밭을 헤치며 그것을 찾아내는 일은 다소 가망 없는 시도로 보였다. 그래도 그들은 일렬로 늘어섰고, 수색의 중요성 때문에 모두가 거들었다. 위쪽 끝에는 낙농장 주인이, 돕겠다고 자청한 클레어 씨와 함께 섰다. 그다음에는 테스, 매리언, 이즈 휴엣, 레티가, 그다음에는 빌 루얼, 조너선, 그리고 기혼 낙농 여인들—양털 같은 검은 머리에 데굴거리는 눈을 가진 벡 닙스, 그리고 물 댄 풀밭의 겨울 습기로 폐병을 앓는 아마빛 머리의 프랜시스—이 섰는데, 이들은 각자 자기 오두막에서 사는 이들이었다.\n\n눈을 땅에 박은 채 그들은 밭의 한 줄기를 천천히 기어가듯 가로질렀고, 좀 더 아래쪽으로 되돌아오는 식이었다. 그렇게 하면 다 끝났을 때 풀밭의 단 한 치도 누군가의 눈에 들지 않은 데가 없게 될 터였다. 그것은 더없이 지루한 일이었다. 밭 전체에서 마늘 싹은 대여섯 개밖에 발견되지 않았다. 그래도 그 풀의 톡 쏘는 맛이 어찌나 강한지, 아마 소 한 마리가 한 입 베어 문 것만으로도 그날 낙농장 전체 산물에 맛을 입히기에 충분했을 것이다.\n\n그토록 천성과 기분이 서로 크게 다른 그들이었지만, 허리를 굽힌 채 묘하게도 한결같은 줄을 이루었다—기계 같고, 소리도 없이. 이웃 오솔길을 지나가는 낯선 구경꾼이 있었다면, 그들을 한 덩어리로 묶어 \"촌놈\"이라 부른다 해도 너끈히 용서받았을 것이다. 풀을 분간하느라 낮게 몸을 숙이고 기어가는 동안, 미나리아재비에서 반사된 부드러운 노란빛이 그늘진 그들의 얼굴에 비쳐, 정수리에는 한낮의 온 힘으로 햇볕이 쏟아지는데도 그들에게 요정 같은, 달빛 어린 모습을 입혔다.\n\n엔젤 클레어는 모든 일에서 나머지 사람들과 함께한다는 자기 원칙을 평등주의적으로 고수하며, 이따금 고개를 들어 올렸다. 그가 테스 옆에서 걸은 것은 물론 우연이 아니었다.\n\n\"음, 좀 어때요?\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아주 좋아요, 고맙습니다, 선생님.\" 그녀가 얌전하게 대답했다.\n\n겨우 반 시간 전에 그들이 숱한 사사로운 일들을 의논하던 터라, 이런 인사조 말투는 좀 군더더기처럼 들렸다. 그러나 그들은 그때 더는 말을 잇지 못했다. 그들은 기고 또 기었고, 그녀의 속치마 단이 그의 각반에 막 스쳤으며, 그의 팔꿈치가 때때로 그녀의 팔꿈치를 스쳤다. 마침내 그다음 자리에 있던 낙농장 주인이 더는 견디지 못했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 이렇게 굽히고 있자니 등이 영락없이 폈다 접혔다 하는구먼!\" 그가 더없이 괴로운 표정으로 천천히 몸을 펴 곧추서며 외쳤다. \"그리고 자네, 아가씨 테스, 자네는 하루 이틀 전에 몸이 안 좋았잖아—이러다간 머리가 단단히 아플 거야! 어지럽거든 더는 하지 말게. 나머지는 다른 사람들한테 끝내라고 맡겨.\"\n\n낙농장 주인 크릭이 물러섰고, 테스도 뒤로 처졌다. 클레어 씨도 줄에서 빠져나와 잡초를 찾아 따로 돌아다니기 시작했다. 그가 자기 가까이에 있는 것을 보자, 전날 밤 들은 이야기 탓에 잔뜩 긴장한 그녀가 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n\"저 애들 예쁘지 않아요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"누가요?\"\n\n\"이즈 휴엣하고 레티요.\"\n\n테스는 우울하게 결심한 터였다. 이 두 처녀 중 누구라도 좋은 농장주의 아내가 될 거라고, 그러니 자기는 그들을 추천하고 자신의 비참한 매력은 가려야 한다고.\n\n\"예쁘다고요? 음, 그래요—예쁜 처녀들이지요—생기 있고. 나도 자주 그렇게 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 가엾게도, 예쁜 건 오래가지 않잖아요!\"\n\n\"오 그래요, 안타깝게도.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 낙농 여인들이에요.\"\n\n\"그래요. 그래도 당신보다 낫진 않지요.\"\n\n\"저 애들이 저보다 크림을 더 잘 걷어요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n클레어는 계속 그들을 살펴보았다—그들 역시 그를 살피지 않은 건 아니었다.\n\n\"레티가 얼굴을 붉히고 있어요.\" 테스가 갸륵하게 이었다.\n\n\"누가요?\"\n\n\"레티 프리들이요.\"\n\n\"오! 왜죠?\"\n\"당신이 보고 있어서요.\"\n\n아무리 자기를 희생하려는 기분이었다 해도, 테스는 차마 더 나아가 이렇게 외칠 수는 없었다. \"정말로 숙녀 말고 낙농 여인을 원하신다면, 저 애들 중 하나와 결혼하세요. 그리고 저와 결혼할 생각은 마세요!\" 그녀는 낙농장 주인 크릭을 따라갔고, 클레어가 뒤에 남아 있는 것을 보며 서글픈 만족을 얻었다.\n\n이날부터 그녀는 그를 피하려고 애써 힘을 기울였다. 두 사람이 나란히 있게 된 것이 순전히 우연이라 해도, 예전처럼 그와 오래 함께 있도록 자신을 결코 내버려 두지 않았다. 그녀는 다른 세 처녀에게 온갖 기회를 주었다.\n\n테스는, 그들이 자기에게 털어놓은 고백을 통해, 엔젤 클레어가 모든 젖 짜는 처녀들의 명예를 제 손에 맡아 두고 있음을 깨달을 만큼 충분히 여인이었다. 그리고 그가 셋 중 누구의 행복도 조금이라도 위태롭게 하지 않으려 조심하는 것을 알아챘기에, 옳든 그르든 그녀가 그에게서 본 자제하는 의무감에 대해 다정한 존경심이 테스 안에 자라났다. 그것은 그녀가 이성異性 가운데 누구에게서도 찾으리라 기대한 적 없는 자질이었으며, 그것이 없었다면 그의 집 동무인 순박한 마음들 가운데 한 사람 이상이 울며 제 순례 길을 갔을지도 모르는 자질이었다.\n\n# XXIII\n\n칠월의 무더운 날씨가 그들도 모르는 새 살그머니 닥쳤고, 평평한 골짜기의 대기는 아편처럼 무겁게 낙농 사람들과 소들과 나무들 위에 드리웠다. 김이 무럭무럭 나는 더운 비가 자주 내려, 소들이 풀을 뜯는 풀밭을 더욱 무성하게 했고, 다른 풀밭의 늦은 건초 만들기를 방해했다.\n\n일요일 아침이었다. 젖 짜기는 끝났고, 바깥에서 온 젖 짜는 일꾼들은 집으로 돌아갔다. 테스와 다른 세 처녀는 재빨리 옷을 차려입고 있었다. 처녀들 모두가 낙농장에서 삼사 마일쯤 떨어진 멜스톡 교회에 함께 가기로 뜻을 모은 터였다. 그녀가 탤보세이스에 온 지 이제 두 달이 되었고, 이것이 그녀의 첫 나들이였다.\n\n전날 오후와 밤 내내 거센 뇌우가 풀밭에 쉭쉭 쏟아져 건초 일부를 강으로 쓸어 넣었다. 그러나 이날 아침에는 그 폭우 덕에 해가 더욱 눈부시게 빛났고, 공기는 향기롭고 맑았다.\n\n그들의 교구에서 멜스톡으로 이어지는 구불구불한 오솔길은, 그 일부 구간에서 가장 낮은 지대를 따라 나 있었다. 처녀들이 가장 움푹 꺼진 곳에 이르렀을 때, 비가 내린 결과 오솔길이 신발 위까지 약 오십 야드에 걸쳐 물에 잠겨 있는 것을 알게 되었다. 평일이라면 이것은 심각한 방해가 아니었을 것이다. 높은 나막신과 부츠로 아무 거리낌 없이 철벅철벅 지나갔을 테니까. 그러나 이 허영의 날, 이 주님의 날에는—영적인 일에 마음 쓰는 척 위선을 부리면서도 육신이 육신과 교태를 부리러 나서는 이 날에는—흰 양말과 얇은 신발과, 진흙 한 점만 묻어도 다 보이는 분홍색, 흰색, 연보라색 가운을 차려입는 이 날에는, 그 물웅덩이가 거북한 장애물이었다. 그들은 교회 종이 부르는 소리를 들을 수 있었다—아직 거의 1마일이나 떨어진 곳이었다.\n\n\"여름철에 강물이 이렇게 불어날 줄 누가 생각했겠어!\" 매리언이, 그들이 기어올라 가 물웅덩이를 지날 때까지 그 비탈을 따라 살금살금 가 보려는 심산으로 위태로운 발판을 디디고 있던, 길가 둑 꼭대기에서 말했다.\n\n\"아무래도 물을 곧장 가로질러 걷거나, 아니면 통행료 받는 큰길로 빙 돌아가지 않고는 거기까지 못 가겠어. 그러면 우리 너무너무 늦을 텐데!\" 레티가 가망 없다는 듯 멈춰 서며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 교회에 늦게 들어가면 얼굴이 어찌나 화끈 달아오르는지, 사람들이 다 돌아보고 쳐다보면, '주여 어여삐 여기소서' 하는 데까지 가서야 겨우 다시 식는단 말이야.\" 매리언이 말했다.\n\n그들이 둑에 매달려 서 있을 때, 길모퉁이를 돌아 첨벙거리는 소리가 들렸고, 이윽고 엔젤 클레어가 물을 헤치며 오솔길을 따라 그들 쪽으로 다가오는 게 보였다.\n\n네 사람의 가슴이 동시에 크게 두근거렸다.\n\n그의 차림새는, 교조적인 목사의 아들이 흔히 보이는 것 중에서도 아마 가장 안식일답지 않은 것이었으리라. 그의 옷은 낙농 작업복이었고, 긴 장화에, 머리를 시원하게 하려고 모자 안에 양배추 잎을 댔으며, 엉겅퀴 캐는 호미까지 들어 마무리를 지었다. \"교회에 가시는 게 아니야.\" 매리언이 말했다.\n\n\"안 가셔—가시면 좋을 텐데!\" 테스가 중얼거렸다.\n\n사실 엔젤은, 옳든 그르든 (얼버무리는 논쟁가들이 쓰는 안전한 표현을 빌리자면), 맑은 여름날에는 교회와 예배당의 설교보다 돌멩이 속의 설교를 더 좋아했다. 게다가 이날 아침에는 홍수로 인한 건초 피해가 큰지 어떤지 보러 나온 길이었다. 길을 걷다가 그는 멀리서 처녀들을 보았는데, 그들은 길을 건너기가 어려워 정신이 팔린 나머지 그를 알아채지 못했다. 그는 그곳에서 물이 불었다는 것을, 그래서 그들의 발길을 완전히 막아 버리리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는, 어떻게 그들을—특히 그중 한 사람을—도울 수 있을지 어렴풋이 생각하며 서둘러 왔다.\n\n장밋빛 뺨에 빛나는 눈을 한 네 사람은, 가벼운 여름 차림으로 지붕 비탈의 비둘기처럼 길가 둑에 매달려 어찌나 사랑스러워 보였던지, 그는 가까이 가기 전에 잠시 멈춰 그들을 바라보았다. 그들의 얇은 치마가 풀밭에서 수많은 파리와 나비를 쓸어 올렸는데, 빠져나가지 못한 그것들은 마치 새장 속 새처럼 그 투명한 천 속에 갇혀 있었다. 엔젤의 눈길은 마침내 넷 중 맨 뒤의 테스에게 떨어졌다. 그들의 곤경에 터질 듯한 웃음을 누르고 있던 그녀는, 환하게 빛나는 얼굴로 그의 눈길을 마주하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그는 자기 긴 장화 위로는 차오르지 않는 물속에서 그들 아래에 이르렀고, 갇힌 파리와 나비들을 바라보며 서 있었다.\n\n\"교회에 가려는 거예요?\" 그가 맨 앞에 있는 매리언에게 말했는데, 그다음 두 사람까지 그 말에 포함시키면서도 테스만은 피했다.\n\n\"네, 선생님. 그런데 늦어지고 있어요. 게다가 제 얼굴이 어찌나 달아오르는지—\"\n\n\"내가 물웅덩이를 건너게 해 드릴게요—한 사람도 빠짐없이 다요.\"\n\n네 사람 모두, 마치 한 가슴이 그들을 관통해 뛰는 듯 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"그건 안 되실 것 같은데요, 선생님.\" 매리언이 말했다.\n\n\"이게 당신들이 지나갈 유일한 방법이에요. 가만히 서요. 말도 안 되는 소리—당신은 그렇게 무겁지 않아요! 넷을 한꺼번에 다 안고도 갈 수 있어요. 자, 매리언, 잘 들어요.\" 그가 이었다. \"팔을 내 어깨에 이렇게 두르세요. 자! 꽉 잡아요. 잘했어요.\"\n\n매리언은 시키는 대로 그의 팔과 어깨에 몸을 실었고, 엔젤은 그녀를 안고 성큼성큼 걸어갔다. 뒤에서 보니 그의 호리호리한 몸은 그녀가 떠오르게 하는 커다란 꽃다발에 달린 한낱 줄기 같았다. 그들은 길굽이를 돌아 사라졌고, 다만 그의 첨벙거리는 발소리와 매리언의 보닛 맨 위 리본만이 그들이 어디 있는지 알려 주었다. 몇 분 뒤 그가 다시 나타났다. 둑 위에서 다음 차례는 이즈 휴엣이었다.\n\n\"저기 오신다.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸는데, 감정에 입술이 바싹 마른 것을 알 수 있었다. \"이제 나도 매리언처럼 그분 목에 팔을 두르고 그 얼굴을 들여다봐야 해.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐 별거라고.\" 테스가 재빨리 말했다.\n\n\"모든 일에는 다 때가 있는 법이지.\" 이즈가 아랑곳없이 이었다. \"껴안을 때가 있고, 껴안기를 삼갈 때가 있나니. 이제 첫 번째 것이 내 차례가 되겠네.\"\n\n\"어머—그건 성경 말씀이잖아, 이즈!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 이즈가 말했다. \"나는 교회에서 늘 예쁜 구절에는 귀가 솔깃하거든.\"\n\n이 일의 사분의 삼이 그저 평범한 친절 행위였던 엔젤 클레어가 이제 이즈에게 다가왔다. 그녀는 조용히, 꿈꾸듯 그의 팔에 몸을 실었고, 엔젤은 그녀를 안고 차근차근 걸어갔다. 그가 세 번째로 돌아오는 소리가 들렸을 때, 레티의 두근대는 심장은 그녀를 흔들어 대는 것이 거의 눈에 보일 지경이었다. 그가 붉은 머리 처녀에게 다가가, 그녀를 안아 올리면서 테스를 흘끗 보았다. 그의 입술은 이보다 더 또렷이 말할 수 없었으리라. \"이제 곧 당신과 나 차례요.\" 그녀의 얼굴에 알아들었다는 기색이 떠올랐다. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다. 두 사람 사이에는 어떤 이해가 자리 잡았다.\n\n가엾고 자그마한 레티는, 단연 가장 가벼운 무게였는데도, 클레어가 안은 짐 중에서 가장 성가신 짐이었다. 매리언은 곡식 자루 같았다. 통통함이 죽은 무게처럼 짓눌러, 그는 정말로 비틀거렸었다. 이즈는 분별 있게 차분히 안겨 갔다. 레티는 한 다발의 히스테리였다. 그래도 그는 그 안절부절못하는 가엾은 것을 데리고 무사히 건너 그녀를 내려놓고는 돌아왔다. 테스는 산울타리 너머로, 그가 다음 솟은 땅에 세워 둔 그대로 한 무리를 이루고 서 있는 멀리 있는 세 사람을 볼 수 있었다. 이제 그녀의 차례였다. 동료들에게서 보고는 경멸했던 것, 곧 클레어 씨의 숨결과 눈길이 가까이 다가올 때의 흥분이 자기 안에서는 한층 격해져 있음을 알아채고 그녀는 당황했다. 그리고 자기 비밀을 들킬까 두려운 듯, 마지막 순간에 그에게 머뭇거리며 핑계를 댔다.\n\n\"어쩌면 제가 둑을 따라 기어갈 수 있을지도 몰라요—제가 저 애들보다 더 잘 기어요. 분명 무척 피곤하실 텐데요, 클레어 씨!\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요, 테스.\" 그가 재빨리 말했다. 그리고 그녀가 채 알아차리기도 전에, 그녀는 그의 팔에 안겨 그의 어깨에 기대어 있었다.\n\n\"라헬 하나를 얻으려고 레아 셋을 거쳤군요.\" 그가 속삭였다.\n\n\"저 애들이 저보다 나은 여자들이에요.\" 그녀가 너그럽게 자기 결심을 지키며 대답했다.\n\n\"내겐 아니에요.\" 엔젤이 말했다.\n\n그가 이 말에 그녀가 얼굴 붉히는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그들은 말없이 몇 걸음을 갔다.\n\n\"제가 너무 무겁진 않죠?\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"오 아니에요. 매리언을 들어 봐야 해요! 그런 덩어리를. 당신은 햇볕에 데워진 굽이치는 물결 같아요. 그리고 당신을 감싼 이 모슬린의 보드라움은 다 그 물거품이고요.\"\n\n\"참 예쁜 말이네요—제가 당신께 그렇게 보인다면요.\"\n\n\"내가 이 수고의 사분의 삼을 오직 그 마지막 사분의 일을 위해 견뎌 냈다는 걸 아세요?\"\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"오늘 이런 일이 일어날 줄은 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"저도요... 물이 너무 갑자기 불어서요.\"\n\n물이 불어난 것이 그가 가리키는 바라고 그녀가 알아들었다는 듯한 그 말투를, 그녀의 숨결 상태가 배반하고 있었다. 클레어는 가만히 멈춰 서서 그녀에게로 얼굴을 기울였다.\n\n\"오 테시!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n처녀의 뺨이 산들바람에 화끈 달아올랐고, 그녀는 격한 감정에 차마 그의 눈을 들여다볼 수 없었다. 그것은 엔젤에게, 자기가 우연히 생긴 처지를 다소 부당하게 이용하고 있음을 일깨웠다. 그래서 그는 더는 나아가지 않았다. 사랑을 말하는 분명한 말이 아직 두 사람의 입술을 오간 적이 없었으니, 이 지점에서 멈추는 것이 지금은 바람직했다. 그래도 그는 남은 거리를 되도록 길게 늘이려고 천천히 걸었다. 그러나 마침내 그들은 길굽이에 이르렀고, 그 뒤로 나아가는 길은 다른 세 사람의 눈에 훤히 드러났다. 마른 땅에 닿았고, 그는 그녀를 내려놓았다.\n\n그녀의 벗들은 그녀와 그를 동그랗게 뜬, 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 바라보고 있었고, 그녀는 그들이 자기 이야기를 하고 있었음을 알 수 있었다. 그는 서둘러 그들에게 작별을 고하고는, 물에 잠긴 길을 따라 첨벙거리며 되돌아갔다.\n\n네 사람은 아까처럼 함께 걸어갔는데, 마침내 매리언이 침묵을 깨고 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼—정말이지, 우리는 쟤를 당해 낼 가망이 없어!\" 그녀는 기쁨 없는 눈으로 테스를 바라보았다.\n\n\"무슨 소리야?\" 테스가 물었다.\n\n\"그분은 너를 제일 좋아해—정말로 제일! 너를 안고 갈 때 우리도 다 봤어. 네가 조금이라도 부추겼더라면 그분은 너한테 입 맞췄을 거야.\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그들이 길을 나설 때의 명랑함은 어쩐지 사라져 버렸다. 그래도 그들 사이에 적의나 악의는 없었다. 그들은 너그러운 젊은 영혼들이었다. 운명론이 강한 정서로 자리 잡은 외딴 시골 구석에서 자랐기에, 그들은 그녀를 탓하지 않았다. 그렇게 밀려나는 일은 일어나기 마련이었다.\n\n테스의 마음은 아팠다. 자기가 엔젤 클레어를 사랑한다는 사실을 스스로에게 숨길 수 없었다. 어쩌면 다른 이들도 그에게 마음을 빼앗겼다는 것을 알기에 더욱 격정적으로 사랑하는 것인지도 몰랐다. 이런 정서에는 전염성이 있다, 특히 여자들 사이에서는. 그런데도 그 똑같이 굶주린 천성이 이에 맞서 싸웠으나, 너무도 미약하게 싸웠기에, 자연스러운 결과가 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"나는 결코 네 앞길을, 너희 누구의 앞길도 막지 않을 거야!\" 그날 밤 침실에서 그녀가 레티에게 단언했다 (그녀의 눈물이 흘러내렸다). \"나도 어쩔 수가 없는걸, 얘! 그분 마음에 결혼 생각 따위는 전혀 없는 것 같아. 하지만 설령 그분이 내게 청혼한다 해도 나는 거절할 거야, 어떤 남자라도 거절할 것처럼.\"\n\n\"오! 거절하겠다고? 왜?\" 레티가 의아해하며 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 수밖에 없어! 하지만 분명히 말할게. 나를 완전히 빼놓고 보면, 그분은 너희 중 누구도 고르지 않을 것 같아.\"\n\n\"난 한 번도 그걸 바란 적 없어—생각해 본 적도 없어!\" 레티가 흐느꼈다. \"하지만 오! 차라리 죽었으면 좋겠어!\"\n\n스스로도 거의 이해 못 할 감정에 시달리던 그 가엾은 아이는, 마침 그때 위층으로 올라온 다른 두 처녀에게로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"우린 쟤랑 다시 친구야.\" 그녀가 그들에게 말했다. \"쟤도 우리만큼이나 그분이 자기를 고르리라고는 생각 안 한대.\"\n\n그렇게 거리낌이 사라지고, 그들은 서로 마음을 터놓고 따뜻해졌다.\n\n\"나는 이제 내가 뭘 하든 상관없을 것 같아.\" 매리언이 말했는데, 그녀의 기분은 가장 낮은 저음으로 가라앉아 있었다. \"나한테 두 번이나 청혼한 스티클퍼드의 어느 낙농 일꾼하고 결혼하려던 참이었어. 하지만—맙소사—이제는 그 사람 아내가 되느니 차라리 내 목숨을 끊겠어! 너는 왜 말이 없니, 이즈?\"\n\n\"솔직히 말하자면,\" 이즈가 중얼거렸다. \"오늘 그분이 나를 안았을 때 나한테 입 맞추실 거라고 확신했어. 그래서 나는 그분 가슴에 가만히 기대어 바라고 또 바라며 조금도 움직이지 않았지. 하지만 그분은 안 그러셨어. 나는 더는 여기 탤보세이스에 머물고 싶지 않아! 집에 가 버릴 거야.\"\n\n침실의 공기는 처녀들의 가망 없는 정념으로 두근거리는 듯했다. 그들은 잔인한 자연의 법칙이 떠안긴 감정의 짓누름 아래 열에 들떠 몸부림쳤다—기대하지도 바라지도 않았던 감정이었다. 그날의 사건은 그들의 가슴속을 태우는 불길에 부채질을 했고, 그 고통은 거의 견딜 수 없을 지경이었다. 그들을 개인으로 구별 짓던 차이들은 이 정념에 의해 추상되어 사라졌고, 저마다 성性이라 불리는 한 유기체의 일부일 뿐이었다. 거기엔 그토록 솔직함이 많고 질투가 적었는데, 그것은 희망이 없었기 때문이다. 저마다 어지간히 분별 있는 처녀였기에, 헛된 자만으로 자신을 속이거나, 제 사랑을 부인하거나, 남들을 누르겠다는 생각으로 잘난 체하지 않았다. 사회적 관점에서 본 그들 도취의 부질없음을 온전히 깨달은 것, 그 목적 없는 시작, 제 안에 갇힌 전망, 문명의 눈에 그 존재를 정당화할 그 무엇도 없다는 것 (자연의 눈에는 모자란 것이 하나도 없으나), 그것이 존재한다는 단 하나의 사실, 그들을 죽을 듯한 기쁨으로 황홀케 하는 그 사실—이 모든 것이 그들에게 일종의 체념을, 일종의 위엄을 안겨 주었으니, 그것은 그를 남편으로 얻겠다는 현실적이고 속된 기대였다면 부수어 버렸을 것이었다.\n\n그들은 작은 침대에서 엎치락뒤치락했고, 아래층에서는 치즈 짜는 틀이 단조롭게 물을 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"테스, 너 깨어 있니?\" 반 시간쯤 뒤에 누군가 속삭였다.\n\n이즈 휴엣의 목소리였다.\n\n테스가 그렇다고 대답하자, 레티와 매리언도 갑자기 이불을 박차고 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"우리도 그래!\"\n\n\"어떤 여자일까—그분 집안에서 그분한테 점찍어 뒀다는 그 숙녀 말이야!\"\n\n\"궁금하네.\" 이즈가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 숙녀를 그분한테 점찍어 뒀다고?\" 테스가 흠칫 놀라 숨을 들이켰다. \"난 그런 얘기 한 번도 못 들었어!\"\n\n\"오 그래—다들 수군대던걸. 그분 집안에서 고른, 그분과 같은 신분의 젊은 숙녀래. 그분 아버지의 교구인 에민스터 근처에 사는 어느 신학박사의 딸이라더라. 그분은 그 숙녀를 그리 마음에 두지 않는다지만. 그래도 분명히 그 여자랑 결혼하실 거래.\"\n\n이 일에 대해 그들이 들은 것은 정말 얼마 안 되었다. 그래도 밤의 그늘 속에서 비참하고 애달픈 꿈을 쌓아 올리기엔 충분했다. 그들은 그가 마음을 돌려 승낙하게 되는 일의 온갖 세부를, 결혼 준비를, 신부의 행복을, 그 옷과 면사포를, 그와 함께할 그녀의 더없이 행복한 가정을 그려 보았다. 그가 그들의 사랑에 관한 한 그들 자신에게 망각이 내려앉을 그때를. 그렇게 그들은 이야기하고, 아파하고, 잠이 그 슬픔을 흘려 보내 줄 때까지 울었다.\n\n이 사실이 드러난 뒤로 테스는, 클레어가 자기에게 베푸는 관심에 무언가 진지하고 의도된 뜻이 숨어 있으리라는 어리석은 생각을 더는 품지 않았다. 그것은 사랑 그 자체의 한때를 위한, 그녀의 얼굴을 향한 스쳐 가는 여름 사랑일 뿐—그 이상은 아니었다. 그리고 이 슬픈 깨달음의 가시 면류관은 이런 것이었다. 그가 나머지 사람들보다 지나가듯이나마 정말로 더 좋아한 그녀, 자기 천성이 그들보다 더 격정적이고 더 영리하며 더 아름답다는 것을 스스로 아는 그녀가, 점잖음의 눈으로 보면 그가 외면하는 더 수수한 처녀들보다 그에게 훨씬 덜 어울린다는 것이었다.\n\n# XXIV\n\n프룸 골짜기의 줄줄 흐르는 비옥함과 따뜻한 발효 속에서, 수정受精의 쉭쉭거리는 소리 아래로 수액이 솟구치는 소리가 거의 들릴 듯한 이 계절에, 제아무리 공상적인 사랑이라도 격정으로 자라지 않기란 불가능했다. 거기 자리한 채비된 가슴들은 주위 환경에 의해 잉태되었다.\n\n칠월이 그들의 머리 위로 지나갔고, 그 뒤를 따라온 테르미도르 같은 무더위는 마치 자연이 탤보세이스 낙농장의 마음들 상태에 맞추려는 노력 같았다. 봄과 초여름에는 그토록 신선하던 이곳의 공기가 이제는 정체되고 맥 빠지게 했다. 그 무거운 향기가 그들을 짓눌렀고, 한낮이면 풍경이 혼절해 누운 듯했다. 에티오피아 같은 불볕더위가 목초지의 위쪽 비탈을 누렇게 태웠으나, 물줄기가 졸졸 흐르는 이곳에는 아직 밝은 초록빛 풀이 있었다. 그리고 클레어가 바깥의 더위에 짓눌렸듯이, 그는 안으로도 부드럽고 말 없는 테스를 향해 점점 커 가는 정념의 열기에 짓눌렸다.\n\n비가 그치니 고지대는 메말랐다. 낙농장 주인이 장에서 집으로 달려오는 용수철 마차의 바퀴는 큰길의 가루가 된 표면을 핥아 올렸고, 그 뒤로는 흰 먼지 띠가 따라붙어, 마치 얇은 화약 도화선에 불을 붙인 것 같았다. 소들은 등에에 미쳐 날뛰며 다섯 단짜리 가축마당 문을 사납게 뛰어넘었다. 낙농장 주인 크릭은 월요일부터 토요일까지 셔츠 소매를 줄곧 걷어붙이고 있었다. 열린 창문은 문이 열려 있지 않으면 환기에 아무 소용이 없었고, 낙농장 정원에서는 검은새와 개똥지빠귀가 까치밥나무 덤불 아래를 기어 다니는데, 날개 달린 짐승이라기보다는 네발짐승에 가까운 모양새였다. 부엌의 파리들은 게으르고 성가시고 친근하게, 익숙지 않은 곳들—바닥이며 서랍 속, 젖 짜는 처녀들의 손등 위—을 기어 다녔다. 대화는 일사병에 관한 것이었고, 버터 만들기는, 더더욱 버터 보관하기는, 절망이었다.\n\n그들은 시원함과 편리함을 위해 소를 몰아넣지 않고 풀밭에서 통째로 젖을 짰다. 낮 동안 짐승들은, 가장 작은 나무의 그림자가 하루의 운행을 따라 줄기를 빙 돌며 옮겨 가면 굽실거리듯 그 그림자를 따라다녔다. 그리고 젖 짜는 이들이 오면 짐승들은 파리 때문에 가만히 서 있지를 못했다.\n\n이런 어느 오후, 젖을 다 짜지 않은 소 네댓 마리가 마침 전체 무리에서 떨어져 산울타리 모퉁이 뒤에 서 있었는데, 그중에는 덤플링과 올드 프리티가 있었다. 이 둘은 다른 어느 처녀의 손보다 테스의 손을 좋아했다. 그녀가 젖 짜기를 끝낸 소 밑의 걸상에서 일어났을 때, 한동안 그녀를 지켜보던 엔젤 클레어가 앞서 말한 짐승들을 다음에 맡아 주겠느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 말없이 응낙하고는, 걸상을 팔 길이만큼 들고 무릎에 양동이를 받친 채, 그것들이 서 있는 곳으로 돌아갔다. 곧 올드 프리티의 젖이 양동이로 솨아 들어가는 소리가 산울타리 너머로 들려왔고, 그러자 엔젤도 모퉁이를 돌아가, 거기 길을 잃고 들어온, 젖이 잘 안 나오는 소 한 마리를 마저 짜고 싶어졌다. 그는 이제 낙농장 주인 못지않게 이 일을 해낼 수 있었다.\n\n남자들은 모두, 그리고 여자들 일부도, 젖을 짤 때 이마를 소에 처박고 양동이를 들여다보았다. 그러나 몇몇은—주로 더 젊은 이들은—머리를 옆으로 기댔다. 이것이 테스 더비필드의 버릇이었으니, 관자놀이를 소의 옆구리에 대고, 눈은 명상에 잠긴 이의 고요함으로 풀밭 저 끝에 둔 채였다. 그녀는 그렇게 올드 프리티의 젖을 짜고 있었고, 마침 해가 젖 짜는 쪽에 있어, 분홍 가운을 입은 그녀의 몸과 흰 커튼 같은 보닛 위로, 그리고 그녀의 옆얼굴 위로 평평하게 비쳐, 그 옆얼굴을 소의 거무스름한 배경에서 깎아 낸 카메오처럼 또렷하게 만들었다.\n\n그녀는 클레어가 자기를 따라 돌아왔다는 것도, 그가 자기 소 밑에 앉아 자기를 지켜보고 있다는 것도 알지 못했다. 그녀의 머리와 이목구비의 고요함은 놀라웠다. 그녀는 눈을 뜨고도 아무것도 보지 않은 채 황홀경에 든 듯했다. 이 그림에서 움직이는 것이라고는 올드 프리티의 꼬리와 테스의 분홍빛 손뿐이었는데, 그 손이 어찌나 부드럽게 움직이는지 그저 율동적인 고동일 뿐이었다. 마치 뛰는 심장처럼, 반사적인 자극에 따르는 것 같았다.\n\n그에게 그녀의 얼굴은 어찌나 사랑스러웠던가. 그러나 거기엔 천상적인 데라고는 전혀 없었다. 온통 진짜 생기, 진짜 온기, 진짜 육화肉化였다. 그리고 그것이 절정에 이른 곳이 바로 그녀의 입이었다. 거의 그만큼 깊고 말하는 듯한 눈을 그는 전에도 본 적이 있었고, 어쩌면 그만큼 고운 뺨도 본 적이 있었다. 그만큼 활처럼 휜 눈썹도, 거의 그만큼 맵시 있는 턱과 목도. 그러나 그녀의 입은 이 땅의 어떤 얼굴에서도 견줄 만한 것을 본 적이 없었다. 조금이라도 불씨를 지닌 젊은 남자에게, 그녀의 붉은 윗입술 한가운데 그 자그맣게 위로 올라간 곡선은 정신을 어지럽히고, 넋을 빼앗고, 미치게 했다. 그는 전에 어떤 여자의 입술과 이에서도, 눈雪으로 채운 장미라는 옛 엘리자베스 시대의 비유를 이토록 끈질긴 반복으로 마음에 떠올리게 한 것을 본 적이 없었다. 연인으로서 그는 즉석에서 그것을 완벽하다고 불렀을지도 모른다. 그러나 아니다—그것은 완벽하지 않았다. 그리고 바로 그 완벽해 보이는 것 위에 닿은 불완전함의 한 점이, 그 달콤함을 주었으니, 그것이야말로 인간다움을 주는 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n클레어는 그 입술의 곡선을 하도 여러 번 살펴본 터라 마음속에서 쉽게 재현해 낼 수 있었다. 그런데 이제 그것이 빛깔과 생명을 입은 채 다시 그를 마주하니, 그의 살갗에 일종의 기운을, 그의 신경에 한 줄기 산들바람을 보내 왔고, 그것은 거의 메스꺼움을 일으킬 뻔했다. 그리고 실제로, 어떤 알 수 없는 생리적 작용에 의해, 김빠지게도 재채기를 일으켰다.\n\n그러자 그녀는 그가 자기를 지켜보고 있음을 알아챘다. 그러나 그녀는 자세를 조금도 바꾸어 그것을 드러내지 않으려 했다. 다만 그 기이하게 꿈꾸는 듯한 고정됨은 사라졌고, 자세히 보는 눈이라면 그녀의 얼굴 장밋빛이 짙어졌다가, 이윽고 옅어져 그 기운이 살짝만 남는 것을 쉽게 알아챘을 것이다.\n\n하늘에서 내려온 자극처럼 클레어 속으로 스며든 그 영향력은 잦아들지 않았다. 결심도, 과묵함도, 신중함도, 두려움도, 패한 대대처럼 물러났다. 그는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나, 소가 그럴 마음이 있다면 차 버리든 말든 양동이를 내버려 둔 채, 자기 눈이 갈망하는 대상을 향해 빠르게 다가갔고, 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다.\n\n테스는 완전히 불시에 당했고, 미처 생각해 볼 겨를도 없는 필연성으로 그의 포옹에 몸을 맡겼다. 다가온 이가 다른 누구도 아닌 정말로 자기 연인임을 본 그녀는, 입술을 벌리고 한순간의 기쁨에 그에게 무너져 내렸으니, 황홀한 외침에 아주 가까운 무언가와 함께였다.\n\n그는 너무도 유혹적인 그 입에 막 입 맞추려던 참이었으나, 여린 양심을 위해 스스로를 억눌렀다.\n\n\"용서해 줘요, 테스, 내 사랑!\" 그가 속삭였다. \"물어봤어야 했는데. 나는—내가 뭘 하는지도 몰랐어요. 무례하게 굴 뜻은 아니에요. 나는 당신에게 헌신해요, 테시, 더없이 소중한 사람, 온 진심을 다해!\"\n\n이때쯤 올드 프리티가 어리둥절해하며 뒤를 돌아보았다. 그러더니 아득한 옛 관습에 따르면 한 사람만 있어야 할 자기 밑에 두 사람이 웅크리고 있는 것을 보고는, 못마땅한 듯 뒷다리를 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"화났어요—우리가 무슨 짓을 하는지 몰라서—젖을 차 엎을 거예요!\" 테스가, 짐승의 움직임에 신경을 쓰면서도 마음으로는 자기 자신과 클레어에게 더 깊이 사로잡힌 채, 부드럽게 몸을 빼내려 애쓰며 외쳤다.\n\n그녀가 걸상에서 빠져나와 일어섰고, 그들은 함께 섰는데, 그의 팔은 여전히 그녀를 감싸고 있었다. 먼 곳에 고정된 테스의 눈에 눈물이 차오르기 시작했다.\n\n\"왜 우는 거예요, 내 사랑?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"오—나도 모르겠어요!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n자기가 처한 처지를 더 또렷이 보고 느끼게 되자, 그녀는 동요하며 물러서려 했다.\n\n\"자, 마침내 내 감정을 드러내고 말았네요, 테스.\" 그가, 자기 마음이 판단을 앞질렀음을 무심결에 내비치는 야릇한 절망의 한숨과 함께 말했다. \"내가—당신을 진심으로 깊이 사랑한다는 건 말할 필요도 없어요. 하지만 나는—지금은 더 나아가지 않을게요—당신을 괴롭히고 있으니까요—나도 당신만큼이나 놀랐어요. 내가 당신이 무방비한 틈을 타 함부로 굴었다고는—너무 성급하고 분별없이 굴었다고는 생각하지 않겠죠?\"\n\n\"아니에요—저도 잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 빠져나가도록 놓아주었다. 그리고 일이 분 만에 두 사람은 각자 젖 짜기를 다시 시작했다. 둘이 하나로 끌려간 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그리고 몇 분 뒤 낙농장 주인이 그 가려진 모퉁이를 돌아왔을 때, 그 두드러지게 떨어져 있는 한 쌍이 서로에게 한낱 아는 사이 이상이라는 것을 드러낼 기미는 어디에도 없었다. 그러나 크릭이 그들을 마지막으로 본 뒤의 그 사이에, 그 둘의 천성에게 우주의 축을 바꿔 놓은 무언가가 일어난 것이었다. 만약 그 본질을 알았다면 낙농장 주인이 현실적인 사람으로서 경멸했을 만한 무언가였으나, 이른바 현실적이라는 것들을 한 무더기 쌓아 올린 것보다 더 끈질기고 거역할 수 없는 경향에 바탕을 둔 무언가였다. 한 장의 베일이 휙 걷혔다. 이제부터 두 사람 각자의 시야가 펼쳐지는 영역은 새로운 지평을 가지게 될 터였다—짧은 동안이든 오랜 동안이든.\n\n# XXV\n\n저녁이 다가올 무렵, 안절부절못하던 클레어는 어스름 속으로 나갔다. 그를 사로잡은 그녀는 자기 방으로 물러간 뒤였다.\n\n밤은 낮만큼이나 후텁지근했다. 어두워진 뒤에도 풀밭 위가 아니고는 시원함이라곤 없었다. 길도, 정원의 오솔길도, 집의 정면도, 가축마당 담도 화덕처럼 따뜻해서, 한낮의 온도를 밤 산책자의 얼굴에 되비쳤다.\n\n그는 낙농장 마당의 동쪽 문에 걸터앉아, 자기 자신을 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그날 감정이 정말로 판단을 질식시켜 버렸던 것이다.\n\n세 시간 전 그 갑작스러운 포옹 이후로 두 사람은 떨어져 지냈다. 그녀는 일어난 일에 정적에 잠긴, 거의 겁먹은 듯이 보였고, 한편 그 새로움과 계획에 없던 일과 상황을 통제할 수 없었다는 것이, 두근거리며 사색하는 존재인 그를 불안하게 했다. 그는 아직도 서로의 진정한 관계가 무엇인지, 이제부터 제삼자들 앞에서 둘이 어떻게 처신해야 할지 도무지 가늠할 수 없었다.\n\n엔젤은 이 낙농장에 견습생으로 올 때, 여기서의 자기 임시 생활이 인생에서 한낱 짧은 막간일 뿐 곧 지나가고 일찌감치 잊힐 것이라는 생각이었다. 그는 마치 가려진 골방에서처럼, 자기를 사로잡는 바깥 세상을 차분히 바라볼 수 있는 곳으로 이곳에 왔으며, 월트 휘트먼처럼 그 세상을 향해 이렇게 외칠 작정이었다—\n\n흔한 옷차림을 한 남자들과 여자들의 무리여, 너희는 내게 얼마나 신기한가!—\n\n그리하여 그 세상으로 새로이 뛰어들 계획을 세울 작정이었다. 그러나 보라, 그 사로잡는 광경이 이곳으로 들어와 버렸다. 그를 온통 사로잡던 세상은 흥미 없는 바깥의 말 없는 무언극으로 흩어져 버렸고, 한편 이곳, 겉보기엔 어둑하고 격정 없어 보이는 이곳에서, 그가 다른 어디서도 결코 겪어 본 적 없는 식으로 새로움이 화산처럼 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n집의 창문이란 창문이 다 열려 있어, 클레어는 마당 너머로 잠자리에 드는 식구들의 사소한 소리 하나하나를 들을 수 있었다. 그토록 보잘것없고, 그토록 하찮으며, 그에게는 그저 마지못해 머무는 곳일 뿐이어서 이제껏 풍경 속에서 무슨 가치 있는 대상이랍시고 눈여겨볼 만큼 중요하게 여긴 적도 없던 그 낙농장 집이, 이제는 무엇이 되었는가? 세월에 닳고 이끼 낀 벽돌 박공들이 \"머물러요!\" 하고 숨결을 토했다. 창문들은 미소 지었고, 문은 어르고 손짓했으며, 덩굴은 한패가 되어 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그 안의 한 인격이 미치는 영향이 어찌나 멀리까지 뻗어 나가는지, 벽돌과 회반죽과 머리 위로 드리운 하늘 전체에 스며들어 그것들을 불타는 감수성으로 두근거리게 했다. 이 강력한 인격은 누구의 것이었던가? 한 젖 짜는 처녀의 것이었다.\n\n이 외진 낙농장의 삶이 그에게 얼마나 큰일이 되었는지 알게 된 것은 정말 놀라운 일이었다. 그리고 새로운 사랑이 이에 일부 책임이 있긴 했으나, 그것만이 전부는 아니었다. 엔젤 말고도 많은 이가, 삶의 크기란 그 바깥의 이동에 달린 것이 아니라 그 주관적 경험에 달려 있음을 배웠다. 감수성 예민한 농부가, 가죽이 두꺼운 왕보다 더 크고 더 충만하며 더 극적인 삶을 산다. 그렇게 보니, 그는 삶이 다른 어디서와 마찬가지로 이곳에서도 똑같은 크기로 보인다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n자신의 이단성과 결함과 약점에도 불구하고, 클레어는 양심을 지닌 사람이었다. 테스는 가지고 놀다 내쳐 버릴 하찮은 존재가 아니라, 자기의 귀한 삶을 살아가는 한 여인이었다. 그 삶은, 그것을 견디거나 누리는 그녀 자신에게는, 가장 위대한 자의 삶이 그 자신에게 지니는 것만큼이나 큰 차원을 지니고 있었다. 테스에게는 온 세상이 그녀의 감각에 달려 있었다. 그녀의 존재를 통해서야 비로소 그녀의 모든 동료 피조물도 그녀에게 존재했다. 우주 자체도 테스가 태어난 바로 그해 그날에야 비로소 테스를 위해 생겨난 것이었다.\n\n그가 침범해 들어간 그 의식은, 무정한 제일원인第一原因이 테스에게 베푼 단 하나의 존재 기회였다—그녀의 전부요, 그녀의 처음이자 마지막인 단 하나의 기회였다. 그러니 어찌 그가 그녀를 자기보다 덜 중한 존재로, 쓰다듬다 싫증 날 예쁜 노리개쯤으로 여기겠는가. 그리고 그녀 안에 일깨워 놓았음을 그가 아는 그 애정을—그 과묵함 아래 그토록 열렬하고 그토록 감수성 예민한 그녀의 애정을—그것이 그녀를 괴롭히고 망가뜨리지 않도록 더없이 진지하게 다루지 않을 수 있겠는가?\n\n날마다 늘 하던 식으로 그녀를 마주친다는 것은, 이미 시작된 것을 키우는 일이 될 터였다. 그토록 가까운 관계로 살면서 마주친다는 것은 애정에 빠져드는 것을 뜻했다. 살과 피로는 그것을 거부할 수 없었다. 그리고 그런 경향이 어떻게 매듭지어질지 아무런 결론에도 이르지 못한 터라, 그는 둘이 함께 해야 하는 일들로부터 당분간 거리를 두기로 결심했다. 아직은 입은 해가 작았다.\n\n그러나 결코 그녀에게 다가가지 않겠다는 결심을 지키기란 쉽지 않았다. 맥박이 한 번 뛸 때마다 그는 그녀에게로 떠밀렸다.\n\n그는 친구들을 보러 가야겠다고 생각했다. 그들에게 이 일을 슬쩍 떠볼 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 다섯 달도 채 안 되어 이곳에서의 임기가 끝날 것이고, 다른 농장들에서 몇 달을 더 보내고 나면, 그는 농사 지식을 온전히 갖추어 제 힘으로 시작할 수 있는 처지가 될 터였다. 농장주라면 아내가 필요하지 않겠는가, 그리고 농장주의 아내가 응접실의 밀랍 인형이어야 할까, 아니면 농사를 아는 여자여야 할까? 침묵이 그에게 돌려준 흐뭇한 대답에도 불구하고, 그는 길을 떠나기로 결심했다.\n\n어느 날 아침 탤보세이스 낙농장에서 아침을 먹으려 앉았을 때, 어떤 처녀가 그날 클레어 씨를 통 보지 못했다고 말했다.\n\n\"오 그래,\" 낙농장 주인 크릭이 말했다. \"클레어 씨는 며칠 친척들하고 지내려고 에민스터 집에 가셨어.\"\n\n그 식탁에 둘러앉은 격정에 찬 네 사람에게는, 아침의 햇살이 한순간에 꺼졌고 새들도 노랫소리를 죽였다. 그러나 어느 처녀도 말로든 몸짓으로든 자기의 막막함을 드러내지 않았다. \"그분도 나하고 함께할 기한이 거의 끝나 가지,\" 낙농장 주인이, 무심결에 잔인한 무덤덤함으로 덧붙였다. \"그러니 다른 데서의 계획을 알아보기 시작한 모양이야.\"\n\n\"그분은 얼마나 더 여기 머무시나요?\" 이즈 휴엣이 물었다. 침울에 빠진 처녀들 가운데 그 질문에 목소리를 맡길 수 있는 유일한 이였다.\n\n다른 이들은 마치 제 목숨이 거기 달리기라도 한 듯 낙농장 주인의 대답을 기다렸다\n그것을 응시했다. 레티는 입술을 벌린 채 식탁보를 바라보았고, 마리안은 붉은 얼굴에 열기까지 더해졌고, 테스는 가슴이 두근거리며 풀밭을 내다보고 있었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 메모장을 들여다보지 않고는 정확한 날짜를 기억할 수가 없구먼.\" 크릭이 여전히 견딜 수 없으리만치 무심하게 대답했다. \"그것도 좀 바뀔 수 있고. 그 사람은 짚 마당에서 송아지 받는 연습을 좀 더 익히려고 분명 머무를 거야. 내가 보기엔 연말까지는 붙어 있을 게야.\"\n\n그와 함께하는 넉 달 남짓의, 고통스러우리만치 황홀한 시간—\"고통으로 둘러싸인 기쁨\". 그 후엔 입에 담을 수 없는 밤의 검은 어둠.\n\n이 아침의 바로 그 순간, 앤젤 클레어는 아침을 먹는 이들로부터 십 마일 떨어진 좁은 길을 따라, 에민스터에 있는 아버지의 목사관 쪽으로 말을 달리고 있었다. 그는 크릭 부인이 정중한 안부와 함께 자기 부모에게 보낸 검은 순대 몇 개와 벌꿀술 한 병이 든 작은 바구니를 되도록 잘 들고 가는 중이었다. 하얀 길이 그의 앞으로 뻗어 있었고, 그의 눈은 그 위에 머물러 있었다. 하지만 그 눈은 길이 아니라 내년을 응시하고 있었다. 그는 그녀를 사랑했다. 그녀와 결혼해야 하는가? 감히 결혼할 수 있는가? 어머니와 형들은 뭐라 할 것인가? 그 일이 있고 이삼 년 후에 자기 자신은 뭐라 말하게 될 것인가? 그것은 이 일시적 감정 아래에 굳건한 동지애의 싹이 깔려 있느냐, 아니면 영속성의 토대 하나 없이 오직 그녀의 형상에서 오는 관능적 기쁨일 뿐이냐에 달려 있었다.\n\n언덕에 둘러싸인 아버지의 작은 마을, 붉은 돌로 지은 튜더 양식의 교회탑, 목사관 가까이 늘어선 나무들이 마침내 그의 발치 아래로 모습을 드러냈고, 그는 잘 아는 대문을 향해 말을 몰아 내려갔다. 집으로 들어가기 전 교회 쪽으로 눈길을 던지자, 제의실 문 옆에 열두 살에서 열여섯 살 사이의 소녀들 한 무리가 서 있는 것이 보였다. 누군가 다른 사람이 오기를 기다리는 듯했는데, 그 사람이 곧 모습을 드러냈다. 여학생들보다 다소 나이가 든 사람으로, 챙 넓은 모자에 빳빳하게 풀 먹인 캠브릭 천 아침 가운을 입고, 손에는 책 두어 권을 들고 있었다.\n\n클레어는 그녀를 잘 알았다. 그녀가 자기를 보았는지 확신할 수 없었다. 그녀가 보지 못했기를 바랐는데, 그래야 가서 그녀에게 말을 걸 필요가 없을 터였다. 흠 하나 없는 사람이긴 했지만 말이다. 그녀에게 인사를 건네기가 너무도 꺼려져, 그는 그녀가 자기를 보지 못했다고 단정하기로 했다. 그 젊은 숙녀는 머시 챈트 양으로, 아버지의 이웃이자 친구의 외동딸이었고, 언젠가 그가 그녀와 결혼하기를 그의 부모가 은근히 바라는 상대였다. 그녀는 율법폐기론과 성서반에 정통했고, 지금도 분명 성서반을 열려는 참이었다. 클레어의 마음은 바르 골짜기의, 정열에 차고 여름에 흠뻑 젖은 이교도들에게로 날아갔다. 소똥을 애교점처럼 묻힌 그들의 발그레한 얼굴들에게로. 그리고 그 모두 중에 가장 정열적인 한 사람에게로.\n\n에민스터까지 말을 달려가기로 마음먹은 것은 순간의 충동이었고, 그래서 그는 어머니 아버지에게 미리 알리는 편지도 쓰지 못했다. 그래도 그들이 교구 일로 외출하기 전, 아침 식사 시간쯤에 도착하려고 했다. 그는 조금 늦었고, 그들은 이미 아침상에 둘러앉아 있었다. 식탁에 모인 무리가 그가 들어서자마자 벌떡 일어나 그를 맞았다. 그들은 그의 아버지와 어머니, 인근 카운티의 한 도시에서 부목사로 있다가 보름 안짝으로 집에 와 있는 형 펠릭스 목사, 그리고 또 다른 형으로 고전학자이자 자기 칼리지의 펠로 겸 학장이며 긴 방학을 맞아 케임브리지에서 내려와 있는 커스버트 목사였다. 그의 어머니는 모자를 쓰고 은테 안경을 낀 모습이었고, 아버지는 실제 그 사람 그대로의 모습—진지하고 신을 두려워하는, 다소 수척하고 예순다섯쯤 된, 창백한 얼굴에 사색과 결의의 주름이 새겨진 사람—이었다. 그들 머리 위에는 앤젤의 누나, 가족 중 맏이로 그보다 열여섯 살 위인, 선교사와 결혼해 아프리카로 떠난 누나의 그림이 걸려 있었다.\n\n늙은 클레어 씨는 지난 이십 년 사이 동시대의 삶에서 거의 사라져 버린 부류의 성직자였다. 위클리프, 후스, 루터, 칼뱅의 직계 영적 후손이요, 복음주의자 중의 복음주의자요, 회심주의자요, 삶과 사상에서 사도와 같은 단순함을 지닌 사람으로, 그는 거친 젊은 시절에 존재의 더 깊은 물음들에 대해 단번에 마음을 정해 버렸고, 그 후로는 그것들에 대한 어떤 추가적 논의도 받아들이지 않았다. 그는 자기와 같은 시대, 같은 사상 학파에 속한 이들에게조차 극단적이라 여겨졌다. 한편으로는, 그와 완전히 반대편에 선 자들마저도 그의 철저함에, 그리고 원칙을 적용하는 그 열정 안에서 원칙에 관한 모든 의문을 떨쳐 버리는 그 놀라운 힘에 마지못해 감탄을 보냈다. 그는 다소의 바울을 사랑했고, 성 요한을 좋아했으며, 성 야고보는 감히 그럴 수 있는 만큼 미워했고, 디모데와 디도와 빌레몬에 대해서는 복잡한 감정을 품었다. 그의 지성에 신약은 그리스도서라기보다 바울서였다—논증이라기보다 도취였다. 그의 결정론 신조는 거의 악덕에 이를 지경이었고, 그 부정적인 면에서는 쇼펜하우어와 레오파르디의 철학과 사촌뻘인 단념의 철학에 온전히 이르렀다. 그는 교회 법규와 예배 규정을 경멸했고, 신조 조항을 두고 맹세했으며, 그 전 범주에 걸쳐 자신이 일관되다고 여겼는데—어떤 면에서는 그랬을지도 모른다. 한 가지만큼은 그는 확실히 그러했다—진실했다.\n\n자연의 삶과 무르익은 여성성에서 오는, 그의 아들 앤젤이 근래 바르 골짜기에서 겪고 있던 미적이고 관능적이며 이교적인 즐거움에 대해, 만일 그가 캐묻거나 상상으로라도 그것을 파악할 수 있었다면 그의 기질은 지극히 반감을 느꼈을 것이다. 언젠가 앤젤은 짜증이 난 순간에 아버지에게, 현대 문명의 종교가 팔레스타인이 아니라 그리스에서 나왔더라면 인류에게 훨씬 더 나았을지도 모른다고 말하는 불운을 저지른 적이 있었다. 아버지의 슬픔은, 그런 명제 안에 진실의 천 분의 일이라도, 하물며 반쪽 진실이나 온전한 진실이 도사리고 있을 수 있음을 깨닫지 못하는, 그런 멍한 종류의 것이었다. 그는 그저 한동안 앤젤에게 엄숙하게 설교를 늘어놓았을 뿐이다. 하지만 그의 마음씨가 어찌나 너그러운지 그는 결코 무엇이든 오래 담아 두지 않았고, 오늘도 어린아이처럼 솔직하고 다정한 미소로 아들을 맞이했다.\n\n앤젤은 자리에 앉았고, 그곳은 집처럼 느껴졌다. 하지만 그는 예전만큼 자신이 거기 모인 가족의 일원이라고 느끼지는 못했다. 이곳으로 돌아올 때마다 그는 이 어긋남을 의식했고, 지난번 목사관 생활을 함께한 이래 그것은 평소보다도 더 뚜렷이 자기 자신에게 낯선 것으로 자라 있었다. 그 초월적 열망들은—여전히 무의식적으로 천동설적 세계관에, 곧 천정의 낙원과 천저의 지옥에 토대를 두고 있었는데—마치 다른 행성에 사는 사람들의 꿈이라도 되는 양 그 자신에게 낯설었다. 근래 그는 오직 삶만을 보았고, 오직 존재의 거대한 정열적 맥박만을 느꼈다. 지혜라면 기꺼이 규율하는 데 만족했을 것을 헛되이 억제하려 드는 그 신조들에 의해 비틀리지도, 일그러지지도, 옭아매이지도 않은 맥박을.\n\n그들 편에서는 그에게서 큰 차이를, 예전의 앤젤 클레어로부터 점점 멀어지는 어긋남을 보았다. 지금 그들이, 특히 형들이 알아챈 것은 주로 그의 태도의 차이였다. 그는 농부처럼 행동하게 되어 가고 있었다. 다리를 아무렇게나 내던졌고, 얼굴 근육은 더 표정이 풍부해졌으며, 눈은 혀가 말하는 만큼, 아니 그 이상을 말해 주었다. 학자의 태도는 거의 사라졌고, 응접실 청년의 태도는 더더욱 사라졌다. 잘난 체하는 자라면 그가 교양을 잃었다고 했을 테고, 까다로운 자라면 그가 거칠어졌다고 했을 것이다. 톨버세이즈의 요정들 및 목동들과 한집에 살며 어울린 결과의 전염이 그러했다.\n\n아침 식사 후 그는 두 형과 함께 걸었다. 비복음적이고 잘 교육받았으며 품질 보증이 찍힌 청년들로, 가장 먼 결까지 빈틈없이 올바른, 체계적 교육의 선반에서 해마다 깎아 내는 흠잡을 데 없는 모범들이었다. 둘 다 다소 근시였는데, 외알 안경에 줄을 다는 것이 관습일 때는 외알 안경에 줄을 달았고, 양알 안경을 쓰는 것이 관습일 때는 양알 안경을 썼고, 일반 안경을 쓰는 것이 관습일 때는 곧장 일반 안경을 썼다. 모두 자기네 시력 결함의 구체적 종류와는 무관하게 말이다. 워즈워스가 옥좌에 오를 때는 휴대용 시집을 가지고 다녔고, 셸리가 폄하될 때는 그가 책장에서 먼지를 뒤집어쓰도록 내버려 두었다. 코레조의 성가족화가 찬미받을 때는 코레조의 성가족화를 찬미했고, 그가 깎아내려지고 벨라스케스가 추켜세워질 때는 아무런 개인적 이의도 없이 부지런히 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n이 두 사람이 앤젤의 점점 커지는 사회적 부적응을 알아챘다면, 앤젤은 그들의 점점 좁아지는 정신적 한계를 알아챘다. 펠릭스는 그에게 온통 교회로만 보였고, 커스버트는 온통 칼리지로만 보였다. 한쪽에게는 교구 종교회의와 시찰이 세상의 태엽이었고, 다른 쪽에게는 케임브리지가 그러했다. 두 형은 문명사회에 대학인도 아니고 교회인도 아닌, 별로 중요치 않은 수천만의 외부인이 있다는 것을 솔직히 인정했다. 하지만 그들은 헤아리고 존중할 대상이라기보다 그저 용인할 대상이었다.\n\n둘 다 효성스럽고 자상한 아들이었고, 부모를 정기적으로 찾아뵈었다. 펠릭스는 비록 신학 계보에서 아버지보다 훨씬 더 최근의 지점에서 갈라져 나온 가지였지만, 자기희생적이고 사심 없는 면은 덜했다. 상반된 의견을 그것을 품은 자에게 위험으로 보는 측면에서는 아버지보다 더 관대했으나, 그것을 자기 가르침에 대한 무례로 보아 용서하는 데는 아버지보다 덜 선뜻했다. 커스버트는 대체로 더 자유로운 사고를 지녔지만, 더 큰 명민함과 더불어 마음은 그만큼 깊지 못했다.\n\n언덕길을 걸어가는 동안 앤젤의 예전 감정이 그의 안에서 되살아났다—그들이 자기에 비해 어떤 이점을 지녔든 간에, 어느 쪽도 삶을 실제로 살아지는 그대로 보거나 그려 내지는 못한다는 감정이었다. 어쩌면 많은 사람이 그렇듯, 그들의 관찰의 기회는 표현의 기회만큼 좋지 못했는지도 모른다. 어느 쪽도, 자기네와 자기네 동료들이 떠다니는 매끄럽고 잔잔한 물결 바깥에서 작동하는 복잡한 힘들에 대해 제대로 된 인식이 없었다. 어느 쪽도 국지적 진리와 보편적 진리의 차이를 보지 못했다. 성직자와 학자의 귀에 안쪽 세계가 들려주는 것과 바깥 세계가 생각하고 있는 것이 전혀 다른 것이라는 차이를 말이다.\n\n\"이제 자네한테는 농사 아니면 아무것도 없는 모양이군, 친애하는 아우야.\" 펠릭스가 슬프고 엄숙하게 안경 너머로 먼 들판을 바라보며 여러 말 가운데 막내에게 그렇게 말하고 있었다. \"그러니 우리는 그걸 최대한 살려야지. 하지만 부디 도덕적 이상과 되도록 많이 닿아 있도록 애써 주기를 간곡히 부탁하네. 농사란 물론 겉으로 거칠게 사는 걸 뜻하지. 하지만 그래도 검소한 삶에 고결한 사색이 함께할 수 있으니까.\"\n\n\"물론 그럴 수 있죠.\" 앤젤이 말했다. \"천구백 년 전에 이미 증명되지 않았나요—제가 형님 영역을 조금 침범해도 된다면요? 형님, 왜 제가 고결한 사색과 도덕적 이상을 내던질 것 같다고 생각하시죠?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 자네 편지의 어조와 우리 대화로 미루어—그저 내 짐작일 뿐일지도 모르지만—자네가 어쩐지 지적 파악력을 잃어 가는 것 같았어. 커스버트, 자네도 그런 생각 안 들던가?\"\n\n\"이보세요, 펠릭스 형님.\" 앤젤이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"우리는 아주 좋은 친구죠, 아시잖아요. 저마다 자기에게 할당된 원을 밟으며 지내는. 하지만 지적 파악력 얘기가 나온 김에 말하자면, 만족에 겨운 독단론자인 형님은 제 것은 가만 내버려 두고 형님 것이 어찌 되었는지나 알아보시는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n그들은 언덕을 도로 내려와 정찬을 들었는데, 정찬은 부모가 교구에서 보내는 오전 일과가 으레 끝나는 시각이면 언제든 그 시각으로 정해졌다. 오후의 방문객들을 고려한 편의 따위는 사심 없는 클레어 부부의 고려에 들어올 마지막 항목이었다. 비록 세 아들은 부모가 현대적 관념에 조금쯤 따라 주기를 바라는 점에서는 충분히 한마음이었지만 말이다.\n\n산책으로 그들은 시장기가 돌았는데, 특히 이제는 야외에서 일하는 사람이 되어 다소 거칠게 차려진 낙농가의 식탁에 흔하던 '값 치르지 않은 풍성한 음식dapes inemptae'에 익숙해진 앤젤이 그러했다. 하지만 두 노인 중 어느 쪽도 도착하지 않았고, 아들들이 기다리다 거의 지칠 무렵에야 그들의 부모가 들어왔다. 그 극기의 부부는 병든 교구민 몇몇의 식욕을 돋우는 일에 매달려 있었던 것인데, 다소 모순되게도 그들은 그 교구민들을 어떻게든 육신 안에 가두어 두려 애쓰면서 정작 자기네 식욕은 까맣게 잊고 있었다.\n\n가족은 식탁에 앉았고, 차가운 음식으로 차린 검소한 식사가 그들 앞에 놓였다. 앤젤은 크릭 부인의 검은 순대를 찾아 둘러보았는데, 낙농장에서 하던 그대로 먹음직하게 구워 달라고 일러둔 것이었고, 그가 직접 즐기는 만큼이나 그 놀라운 허브 풍미를 아버지와 어머니도 높이 음미해 주기를 바랐던 것이다.\n\n\"아! 검은 순대를 찾고 있구나, 얘야.\" 클레어의 어머니가 말했다. \"하지만 이유를 알면 너도 분명 그게 없어도 개의치 않을 게다. 네 아버지와 나도 분명 그럴 테니까. 내가 네 아버지께 제안했단다, 지금 진전섬망 발작 때문에 한 푼도 못 버는 사람의 아이들에게 크릭 부인의 친절한 선물을 가져다주자고. 네 아버지도 그게 그 아이들에게 큰 기쁨이 되리라고 동의하셨고. 그래서 그렇게 했단다.\"\n\n\"물론이죠.\" 앤젤이 명랑하게 말하며 벌꿀술을 찾아 둘러보았다.\n\n\"그 벌꿀술은 너무 알코올이 강해서,\" 그의 어머니가 말을 이었다. \"음료로 쓰기엔 영 부적합하더구나. 하지만 비상시엔 럼이나 브랜디만큼 값진 것이라, 약장에 넣어 두었단다.\"\n\n\"우리는 원칙상 이 식탁에서 결코 독주를 마시지 않는단다.\" 그의 아버지가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그럼 낙농가 부인에게는 뭐라고 말씀드려야 할까요?\" 앤젤이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 진실을 말해야지.\" 그의 아버지가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 차라리 벌꿀술과 검은 순대를 아주 맛있게 먹었다고 말하고 싶었어요. 그분은 친절하고 쾌활한 사람이라, 제가 돌아가자마자 분명 물어볼 테니까요.\"\n\n\"우리가 그러지 않았으니 너도 그럴 수 없지.\" 클레어 씨가 명료하게 대답했다.\n\n\"아—그렇네요. 하지만 그 벌꿀술은 꽤나 한잔 거나하게 걸칠 만한 술이었는데요.\"\n\n\"한잔 뭐라고?\" 커스버트와 펠릭스가 동시에 말했다.\n\n\"아—톨버세이즈에서들 쓰는 표현이에요.\" 앤젤이 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. 그는 부모가 정서가 결여된 점에서는 그르지만 그 실천에서는 옳다고 느꼈고, 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n# XXVI\n\n저녁이 되어, 가족 기도를 마친 후에야 앤젤은 마음에 둔 한두 가지 주제를 아버지에게 꺼낼 기회를 잡았다. 그는 형들 뒤로 양탄자 위에 무릎을 꿇고 그들의 산책화 뒤축에 박힌 작은 못들을 들여다보면서 그 목적을 위해 마음을 단단히 다잡았었다. 예배가 끝나자 형들은 어머니와 함께 방을 나갔고, 클레어 씨와 그만이 남았다.\n\n젊은이는 먼저 연장자와 함께, 영국에서든 식민지에서든 큰 규모의 농부로 자리 잡으려는 자기 계획을 논의했다. 그러자 그의 아버지가, 앤젤을 케임브리지에 보내는 비용을 들이지 않았으니, 그가 부당하게 푸대접받았다고 느끼지 않도록 언젠가 그를 위해 땅을 사거나 임차하는 데 보탤 돈을 해마다 따로 떼어 두는 것을 자기 의무로 느껴 왔다고 말했다.\n\n\"세속적 재산으로 따지자면,\" 그의 아버지가 말을 이었다. \"너는 몇 년 안에 의심할 바 없이 네 형들보다 훨씬 우월한 처지에 서게 될 게다.\"\n\n늙은 클레어 씨의 이 사려 깊음이 앤젤을 또 다른, 더 소중한 주제로 이끌었다. 그는 아버지에게, 자기가 이제 스물여섯 살이며, 농사 사업을 시작하게 되면 온갖 일을 살피느라 뒤통수에도 눈이 필요할 것이라고 말했다. 자기가 들판에 나가 있는 동안 살림의 노역을 감독할 누군가가 필요하리라고. 그러니 자기가 결혼하는 것이 좋지 않겠느냐고.\n\n그의 아버지는 이 생각을 무리한 것으로 여기지 않는 듯했다. 그러자 앤젤이 물음을 던졌다—\n\n\"검소하고 부지런히 일하는 농부인 저에게 어떤 아내가 가장 좋을 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"참된 기독교도 여인, 네가 나가고 들어올 때 너에게 도움과 위안이 되어 줄 여인이지. 그 너머는 사실 거의 중요치 않다. 그런 여인은 찾을 수 있어. 정말이지, 진지한 마음을 지닌 내 친구이자 이웃인 챈트 박사가—\"\n\n\"하지만 그녀는 무엇보다 먼저 젖소를 짤 줄 알고, 좋은 버터를 만들고, 거대한 치즈를 만들 줄 알아야 하지 않을까요? 암탉과 칠면조를 품게 하고 병아리를 길러 낼 줄 알고, 비상시에 일꾼들이 일하는 들판을 지휘할 줄 알고, 양과 송아지의 값어치를 가늠할 줄 알아야 하지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"그렇지, 농부의 아내라면 그렇지, 물론이고말고. 그게 바람직하겠지.\" 연장자 클레어 씨는 이런 점들을 분명 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었다. \"내가 덧붙이려던 건,\" 그가 말했다. \"순결하고 성스러운 여인으로 말하자면, 네가 한때 어느 정도 관심을 보였던 네 친구 머시보다 네 참된 이익에 더 맞는 사람을, 또 네 어머니와 내 마음에 더 드는 사람을 분명 찾지 못하리라는 거다. 사실 내 이웃 챈트의 딸이 근래 우리 주변 젊은 성직자들의 유행을 좇아 축일이면 성찬대를—어느 날 그 애가 제단이라 부르는 걸 듣고 충격을 받았다만—꽃이며 다른 잡것들로 꾸미게 되긴 했지. 하지만 그 애 아버지는 나만큼이나 그런 겉치레를 반대하는 사람이라, 그건 고칠 수 있다고 하더구나. 그저 한때의 소녀다운 객기일 뿐이니, 분명 오래가지 않을 게다.\"\n\n\"네, 네. 머시가 착하고 독실한 건 압니다. 하지만 아버지, 챈트 양만큼이나 순결하고 정숙하되, 그 숙녀의 교회적 소양 대신 농장 생활의 의무를 농부 자신만큼이나 잘 아는 젊은 여인이라면 저에게 무한히 더 잘 맞으리라고 생각지 않으세요?\"\n\n그의 아버지는 농부 아내의 의무에 대한 지식이 인류에 대한 바울적 관점에 비하면 두 번째라는 확신을 고수했다. 그러자 충동적인 앤젤은 아버지의 정서를 존중하는 동시에 자기 마음의 대의를 진척시키고 싶은 마음에 그럴듯해졌다. 그는 운명이, 혹은 섭리가 자기 앞에 한 여인을 던져 주었는데, 농부의 반려가 될 모든 자질을 갖추었으며 단연 진지한 성향의 사람이라고 말했다. 그녀가 아버지의 건전한 저교회파에 속해 있는지 아닌지는 말하지 않으려 했다. 하지만 그 점에 대해서는 아마 설득에 열려 있을 것이라고, 그녀는 소박한 신앙을 지닌 규칙적인 교회 출석자라고, 정직한 마음에 수용적이고 지적이며 더없이 우아하고 베스타 여사제처럼 순결하며, 외모로는 유난히 아름답다고 말했다.\n\n\"그 애는 네가 결혼하고 싶어 할 만한 집안 사람이냐—요컨대, 숙녀냐?\" 대화 도중 슬그머니 서재로 들어와 있던 그의 어머니가 놀라며 물었다.\n\n\"흔히 말하는 숙녀는 아니에요.\" 앤젤이 굽힘 없이 말했다. \"오두막살이꾼의 딸이니까요, 자랑스럽게 말씀드리지만요. 하지만 그래도 그녀는 숙녀예요—감정에서나 천성에서나요.\"\n\n\"머시 챈트는 아주 좋은 집안 출신이란다.\"\n\n\"체!—그게 무슨 이점이 있어요, 어머니?\" 앤젤이 재빨리 말했다. \"저처럼, 또 앞으로도 거칠게 살아가야 할 남자의 아내에게 집안이 무슨 도움이 되겠어요?\"\n\n\"머시는 소양이 있어. 그리고 소양에는 그 나름의 매력이 있단다.\" 그의 어머니가 은테 안경 너머로 그를 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"외적인 소양으로 말하자면, 제가 살아갈 삶에서 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있겠어요?—그리고 그녀의 학식으로 말하자면, 그건 제가 떠맡을 수 있어요. 그녀는 충분히 영리한 학생이에요, 어머니도 그녀를 아셨다면 그렇게 말씀하셨을 거예요. 그녀는 시로 넘쳐흘러요—실현된 시라고 할까요, 그렇게 표현해도 된다면요. 종이 위의 시인들이 글로만 쓰는 것을 그녀는 직접 살아요…… 그리고 분명 흠잡을 데 없는 기독교도예요. 어쩌면 어머니가 퍼뜨리고 싶어 하시는 바로 그 부족, 그 속屬, 그 종種의 사람일지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"오 앤젤, 너 비웃고 있구나!\"\n\n\"어머니, 죄송해요. 하지만 그녀가 정말로 거의 매주 일요일 아침마다 교회에 가고, 착한 기독교도 처녀이니, 어머니도 그 자질을 봐서 어떤 사회적 결함이든 너그러이 봐주실 거라고, 그녀를 택하느니보다 더 못한 선택을 할 수도 있다고 느끼실 거라고 확신해요.\" 앤젤은 사랑하는 테스의 그 다소 기계적인 정통 신앙에 대해 자못 진지해졌다. 그 신앙이 자기에게 이토록 큰 도움이 되리라곤 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채, 그는 그녀와 다른 젖 짜는 처녀들이 그것을 실천하는 것을 볼 때마다, 본질적으로 자연주의적인 믿음들 사이에서 그것이 명백히 비현실적이라는 이유로 그것을 가벼이 여기곤 했던 것이다.\n\n이 알지 못하는 젊은 여인에게 아들이 갖다 붙인 그 호칭에 대해 과연 아들 자신이 조금이라도 권리가 있는가 하는 슬픈 의심 속에서, 클레어 부부는 적어도 그녀가 신앙관에서만큼은 건전하다는 점이 간과해서는 안 될 이점이라고 느끼기 시작했다. 특히 이 두 사람의 결합이 섭리의 작용으로 이루어졌음이 분명한 까닭에 더욱 그러했다. 왜냐하면 앤젤은 결코 정통 신앙을 자기 선택의 조건으로 삼을 사람이 아니었기 때문이다. 그들은 마침내, 서두르지 않는 편이 낫겠지만 그녀를 만나 보는 데는 반대하지 않겠다고 말했다.\n\n그래서 앤젤은 지금 더 자세한 사정을 밝히기를 삼갔다. 그는 부모가 한결같고 자기희생적이긴 하지만, 그래도 중산층 사람들로서 어떤 잠재된 편견들이 그들 안에 존재한다고, 그것을 극복하려면 어느 정도 요령이 필요하리라고 느꼈다. 비록 법적으로는 자기 뜻대로 할 자유가 있고, 또 며느리가 자기네에게서 멀리 떨어져 살 가능성이 큰 만큼 그녀의 자질이 자기네 삶에 실질적 차이를 만들 수 없다 해도, 그는 애정을 위해 자기 인생의 가장 중요한 결정에서 그들의 정서를 다치게 하고 싶지 않았다.\n\n그는 테스의 삶에서 일어난 우연한 일들을, 마치 그것들이 본질적 특징이기라도 한 양 곱씹는 자신의 모순들을 알아챘다. 그가 테스를 사랑한 것은 그녀 자신을 위해서였다. 그녀의 영혼, 그녀의 마음, 그녀의 본질을. 낙농장에서의 솜씨나 그의 학생으로서의 재능 때문이 아니었고, 더더욱 그녀의 소박하고 형식적인 신앙 고백 때문은 아니었다. 그녀의 꾸밈없는 야외의 삶은 그가 받아들이기에 관습이라는 광택을 입힐 필요가 없었다. 그는 교육이 아직까지는 가정의 행복이 달려 있는 감정과 충동의 박동에 거의 영향을 미치지 못했다고 보았다. 아마 세월이 흐르면 개선된 도덕적·지적 훈련 체계가 인간 본성의 비자발적, 심지어 무의식적 본능까지도 상당히, 어쩌면 현저히 끌어올릴지도 모른다. 하지만 오늘날까지는, 그가 보기에 교양이란 그 영향 아래 놓인 삶들의 정신적 표피에만 영향을 미쳤다고 할 수 있었다. 이 믿음은 그가 여성들에 대해 겪은 경험으로 확인되었는데, 근래 그 경험이 교양 있는 중산층에서 시골 공동체로까지 넓어지면서, 한 사회 계층의 착하고 지혜로운 여인과 또 다른 사회 계층의 착하고 지혜로운 여인 사이의 본질적 차이가, 같은 계층이나 계급 안의 착한 자와 나쁜 자, 지혜로운 자와 어리석은 자 사이의 차이보다 얼마나 더 작은지를 그에게 가르쳐 주었다.\n\n그가 떠나는 아침이었다. 형들은 이미 목사관을 떠나 북쪽으로 도보 여행에 나섰는데, 거기서 한 사람은 자기 칼리지로, 다른 한 사람은 자기 부목사직으로 돌아갈 참이었다. 앤젤도 그들과 함께 갈 수 있었지만 톨버세이즈에 있는 연인에게 돌아가는 편을 택했다. 그는 그 일행 가운데 어색한 일원이 되었을 것이다. 왜냐하면 셋 중 가장 감상력이 뛰어난 인문주의자요, 가장 이상적인 종교인이요, 심지어 가장 정통한 그리스도학자였음에도, 그는 자기 모난 형태가 자기를 위해 마련된 둥근 구멍에 맞지 않으리라는 상존하는 의식 속에서 소외감을 느꼈기 때문이다. 펠릭스에게도 커스버트에게도 그는 감히 테스 이야기를 꺼내지 못했다.\n\n그의 어머니는 그에게 샌드위치를 만들어 주었고, 아버지는 자기 암말을 타고 길을 따라 얼마간 그를 배웅했다. 자기 일은 꽤 잘 진척시킨 터라, 앤젤은 그늘진 길을 함께 터덜터덜 가면서 아버지가 들려주는 이야기에 기꺼이 침묵하며 귀를 기울였다. 아버지가 신약을 엄격히 해석한다는 이유로—그것을 그들은 해로운 칼뱅주의 교리에 비추어 본 해석이라 여겼는데—아버지가 사랑하는 동료 성직자들이 보이는 냉랭함과 교구의 어려움에 관한 이야기였다.\n\n\"해롭다니!\" 클레어 씨가 너그러운 경멸을 담아 말했다. 그러고는 그 생각의 터무니없음을 보여 줄 경험들을 들려 나가기 시작했다. 그는 자기가 그 도구가 되어 이루어진, 악하게 살던 자들의 놀라운 회심들에 대해 이야기했는데, 가난한 자들 사이에서뿐 아니라 부유하고 넉넉한 자들 사이에서도 그러했다고 했다. 또한 그는 수많은 실패도 솔직히 인정했다.\n\n후자의 한 사례로, 그는 사십 마일쯤 떨어진 트랜트리지 근방에 사는, 더버빌이라는 이름의 한 젊은 벼락출세 지주의 경우를 언급했다.\n\n\"킹스비어를 비롯한 여러 곳의 그 옛 더버빌가 사람은 아니겠죠?\" 그의 아들이 물었다. \"사두마차 유령 전설을 지닌, 그 묘하게 유서 깊고 쇠락한 가문 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오 아니다. 원래의 더버빌가는 육칠십 년, 아니 팔십 년 전에 쇠락해 사라졌단다—적어도 내가 알기론 그래. 이건 그 이름을 가져다 쓴 새 가문인 듯하구나. 옛 기사 가문의 명예를 위해서라도, 이들이 가짜이기를 바란다, 정말이지. 하지만 네가 옛 가문에 관심을 표하는 걸 들으니 이상하구나. 너는 나보다도 그런 걸 덜 중히 여기는 줄 알았다.\"\n\n\"저를 오해하시는 거예요, 아버지. 자주 그러시죠.\" 앤젤이 약간 짜증스레 말했다. \"정치적으로 저는 가문이 오래되었다는 것의 미덕에 대해 회의적이에요. 그들 가운데 현명한 자들조차 어떤 이들은 '제 가문의 계승을 한탄한다'고, 햄릿의 말처럼요. 하지만 서정적으로, 극적으로, 심지어 역사적으로 저는 그들에게 다정한 애착을 느껴요.\"\n\n이 구별은, 결코 미묘한 것이 아니었음에도, 연장자 클레어 씨에게는 너무 미묘했다. 그는 막 들려주려던 이야기를 이어 갔는데, 그 내용인즉 이러했다. 이른바 더버빌이라는 노인이 죽은 후 그 젊은이는 가장 비난받아 마땅한 정욕을 키웠는데, 그에게는 앞 못 보는 어머니가 있어 그 처지를 보면 더 분별 있게 굴었어야 마땅했다는 것이다. 클레어 씨가 선교 설교를 하러 그 지방에 갔을 때 그 젊은이의 행적에 관한 이야기가 그의 귀에 들어왔고, 그는 그 죄인에게 그의 영적 상태에 관해 말할 기회를 대담하게 붙잡았다. 비록 그가 이방인으로서 남의 설교단을 차지하고 있는 처지였음에도, 그는 이것을 자기 의무로 느꼈고, 누가복음의 말씀을 본문으로 삼았다. \"어리석은 자여, 오늘 밤에 네 영혼을 도로 찾으리라!\" 그 젊은이는 이 직설적 공격에 몹시 분개했고, 둘이 마주쳐 벌어진 말다툼에서 그는 클레어 씨의 백발을 존중하지 않은 채 거리낌 없이 공공연히 그를 모욕했다.\n\n앤젤은 고통으로 얼굴이 붉어졌다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 그가 슬프게 말했다. \"악당들 때문에 그런 까닭 없는 고통에 스스로를 내맡기지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요!\"\n\n\"고통이라?\" 그의 아버지가 자기부정의 열정으로 거친 얼굴을 빛내며 말했다. \"나에게 유일한 고통이 있었다면 그 가엾고 어리석은 젊은이를 위한 고통이었지. 너는 그자의 격분한 말이 나에게 무슨 고통을 줄 수 있다고 생각하느냐, 아니 그자의 매질인들? '우리가 욕을 먹은즉 축복하고, 박해를 받은즉 참고, 비방을 받은즉 권면하니, 우리가 지금까지 세상의 더러운 것과 만물의 찌꺼기같이 되었도다.' 고린도인들에게 한 그 옛날의 고귀한 말씀이 바로 이 시간에도 엄연히 진실하구나.\"\n\n\"매질은 아니죠, 아버지? 매질까지 하진 않았겠죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 하진 않았다. 하지만 나는 술에 미친 상태의 사람들에게서 매질을 견딘 적이 있단다.\"\n\n\"설마요!\"\n\"열두 번도 더 그랬다, 얘야. 그래서 어떻단 말이냐? 나는 그렇게 함으로써 그들이 제 살붙이를 죽이는 죄에서 구해 주었고, 그들은 살아남아 나에게 감사하고 하느님을 찬양했단다.\"\n\n\"이 젊은이도 그러기를 바랍니다!\" 앤젤이 열렬히 말했다. \"하지만 아버지 말씀으로 미루어, 그렇지 못할까 봐 두렵네요.\"\n\n\"그래도 우리는 희망을 품자꾸나.\" 클레어 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 그를 위해 계속 기도하련다. 무덤 이편에서는 아마 다시는 만나지 못하겠지만 말이다. 하지만 어쨌든 그 가엾은 내 말 한마디가 언젠가 그의 마음속에서 좋은 씨앗으로 싹틀지도 모르지.\"\n\n지금도 늘 그렇듯, 클레어의 아버지는 어린아이처럼 낙천적이었다. 그리고 젊은이는 아버지의 편협한 교리를 받아들일 수는 없었지만, 그 실천을 우러러보았고 신앙가의 모습 아래 깃든 영웅을 알아보았다. 어쩌면 그는 지금 그 어느 때보다 더 아버지의 실천을 우러러보았는지도 모른다. 테스를 아내로 삼는 문제에서 아버지가 그녀가 살림이 넉넉한지 무일푼인지 단 한 번도 캐물어 볼 생각조차 하지 않았음을 보았기에 말이다. 바로 그 세속에 무심함이 앤젤로 하여금 농부로서 생계를 꾸리게 만든 까닭이었고, 또 그것이 아마 그의 형들을 평생 가난한 목사의 처지에 묶어 두게 될 터였다. 그럼에도 앤젤은 그것을 못지않게 찬미했다. 정말이지, 자기 자신의 이단적 성향에도 불구하고 앤젤은 인간적인 면에서는 자기가 두 형제 중 어느 쪽보다도 아버지에게 더 가깝다고 자주 느꼈다.\n\n# XXVII\n\n눈부신 한낮의 대기 속에서 이십 몇 마일의 오르막과 내리막 길을 말로 달린 끝에, 그는 오후에 톨버세이즈에서 서쪽으로 한두 마일 떨어진 외딴 둔덕에 이르렀고, 거기서 다시 그 푸르고 수액 가득하고 습한 골짜기, 바르 혹은 프룸 골짜기를 내려다보았다. 그가 고지대에서 아래쪽 기름진 충적토로 내려가기 시작하자마자 대기가 더 무거워졌다. 여름 과일의 나른한 향기, 안개, 건초, 꽃들이 그 안에서 거대한 향기의 못을 이루었고, 그것이 이 시각에는 짐승들을, 바로 그 벌과 나비까지도 졸리게 만드는 듯했다. 클레어는 이제 이 장소에 너무도 익숙해져서, 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 풀밭 여기저기에 점점이 흩어진 젖소들을 보아도 한 마리 한 마리 이름으로 알아보았다. 그는 여기서 삶을 그 안쪽 면에서, 학창 시절에는 전혀 낯설었던 방식으로 바라볼 수 있는 자기 능력을 사치스러운 기분으로 깨달았다. 그리고 부모를 그토록 사랑하면서도, 가정생활을 경험한 후에 지금처럼 이곳에 오는 것이 마치 부목과 붕대를 벗어 던지는 것 같은 느낌을 준다는 것을 깨닫지 않을 수 없었다. 영국 시골 사회의 기질에 가해지는 그 한 가지 관습적 굴레마저도 이곳에는 없었으니, 톨버세이즈에는 그곳에 거주하는 지주가 없었던 것이다.\n\n낙농장 밖에는 사람 하나 나와 있지 않았다. 거주민들은 모두 한 시간쯤 되는 평소의 오후 낮잠을 즐기고 있었는데, 여름철에 지키는 지독히 이른 시간 탓에 그 낮잠은 필요한 것이 되어 있었다. 문가에는 나무 테를 두른 들통들이, 무수한 문질러 닦기에 흠뻑 젖고 바래진 채로, 그 목적을 위해 거기 고정해 둔 껍질 벗긴 갈래진 떡갈나무 가지 위에 모자걸이의 모자처럼 걸려 있었다. 모두 저녁 젖 짜기를 위해 준비되어 마른 상태였다. 앤젤은 안으로 들어가 집의 고요한 통로들을 지나 뒤편 구역으로 갔고, 거기서 잠시 귀를 기울였다. 사내들 몇이 누워 있는 짐수레 헛간에서 끊임없는 코 고는 소리가 들려왔다. 더 멀리 떨어진 곳에서는 더위에 헐떡이는 돼지들의 꿀꿀거리고 꽥꽥거리는 소리가 일었다. 잎이 넓은 대황과 양배추 포기들도 잠들어 있었는데, 그 넓고 축 늘어진 표면들이 반쯤 접은 우산처럼 햇볕 속에 드리워져 있었다.\n\n그는 말의 굴레를 벗기고 먹이를 주었고, 다시 집으로 들어설 때 시계가 세 시를 쳤다. 세 시는 오후의 크림 걷어 내는 시각이었다. 그리고 그 종소리와 함께 클레어는 위층 마룻널이 삐걱대는 소리를, 이어서 계단을 내려오는 발길의 닿는 소리를 들었다. 그것은 테스의 것이었고, 그녀는 다음 순간 그의 눈앞으로 내려왔다.\n\n그녀는 그가 들어온 것을 듣지 못했고, 그가 거기 있다는 것을 거의 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 하품을 하고 있었고, 그는 마치 뱀의 입 같은 그녀의 붉은 입속을 보았다. 그녀가 한쪽 팔을 둘둘 감아 올린 머리 타래 위로 한껏 높이 뻗고 있어서, 그는 햇볕에 그을린 자국 위쪽으로 그 새틴 같은 섬세함을 볼 수 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 잠으로 발그레했고, 눈꺼풀은 동공 위로 무겁게 드리워져 있었다. 그녀의 천성에 가득 찬 충만함이 그녀에게서 숨결처럼 풍겨 나왔다. 그것은 여인의 영혼이 다른 어느 때보다 더 육화되는 순간이었다. 가장 영적인 아름다움이 스스로를 육체로 드러내는 순간, 그리고 성性이 그 드러남에서 바깥 자리를 차지하는 순간이었다.\n\n그러더니 얼굴의 나머지가 채 깨어나기도 전에 그 눈이 흐릿한 무거움을 뚫고 밝게 번뜩였다. 기쁨과 수줍음과 놀라움이 묘하게 뒤섞인 표정으로 그녀가 외쳤다—\"오 클레어 씨! 어쩌면 그렇게 절 놀라게 하세요—저—\"\n\n처음에는 그의 고백이 들여온 변화된 관계를 생각할 겨를이 없었다. 하지만 그가 계단 맨 아래로 걸음을 내디딜 때 그녀가 그의 다정한 눈길과 마주치자, 그 일의 온전한 의미가 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"사랑하는, 귀여운 테스!\" 그가 그녀에게 팔을 두르고 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 발그레한 뺨에 갖다 대며 속삭였다. \"제발, 부디 더는 나를 '씨'라고 부르지 말아요. 당신 때문에 이렇게 일찍 서둘러 돌아온 거예요!\"\n\n테스의 격하기 쉬운 심장이 대답 대신 그의 심장에 부딪쳐 뛰었다. 그렇게 그들은 현관의 붉은 벽돌 바닥에 서 있었고, 그가 그녀를 자기 가슴에 꼭 끌어안는 동안 해가 창문으로 비스듬히 들어와 그의 등에, 그녀의 기울인 얼굴에, 그녀의 관자놀이의 푸른 핏줄에, 그녀의 맨팔과 목에, 그리고 그녀의 머리칼 깊은 곳까지 내리쬐었다. 옷을 입은 채로 누워 있던 터라 그녀는 햇볕을 쬔 고양이처럼 따스했다. 처음에 그녀는 그를 똑바로 올려다보려 하지 않았으나, 곧 눈을 들었고, 그의 눈은 끊임없이 변하는 동공의 깊이를, 푸른빛과 검은빛과 잿빛과 보랏빛으로 뻗어 나가는 그 섬유들을 가늠하며 깊이 잠겼다. 그동안 그녀는 마치 두 번째로 깨어난 이브가 아담을 바라보았을 법한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저 크림 걷으러 가야 해요.\" 그녀가 사정하듯 말했다. \"오늘은 늙은 뎁밖에 도울 사람이 없어요. 크릭 부인은 크릭 씨랑 장에 가셨고, 레티는 몸이 안 좋고, 다른 애들은 어딘가로 나가서 젖 짤 때까진 안 돌아올 거예요.\"\n\n그들이 우유간으로 물러갈 때 데버라 파이앤더가 계단에 나타났다.\n\n\"내가 돌아왔어요, 데버라.\" 클레어 씨가 위쪽을 향해 말했다. \"그러니 내가 테스의 크림 걷는 일을 도울 수 있어요. 그리고 분명 무척 피곤할 테니, 젖 짤 때까진 내려오지 않아도 돼요.\"\n\n어쩌면 그날 오후 톨버세이즈의 우유는 크림이 그리 철저히 걷어지지 못했을 것이다. 테스는 익숙한 물건들이 빛과 그늘과 위치는 지녔으되 뚜렷한 윤곽은 없는 듯이 보이는 꿈속에 있었다. 일을 위해 크림 걷는 도구를 식히려고 펌프 아래 댈 때마다 그녀의 손은 떨렸다. 그의 애정의 열기가 너무도 또렷이 느껴져, 그녀는 너무 뜨거운 햇볕 속의 식물처럼 그 아래서 움츠러드는 듯했다.\n\n그러자 그가 다시 그녀를 자기 옆으로 끌어당겼고, 그녀가 크림 가장자리를 잘라 내려고 통 안쪽 둘레를 집게손가락으로 빙 두르고 나자, 그가 자연스러운 방식으로 그 손가락을 깨끗이 했다. 톨버세이즈 낙농장의 거리낌 없는 풍습이 지금은 편리했던 것이다.\n\n\"지금 말하나 나중에 말하나 마찬가지니, 사랑하는 사람.\" 그가 부드럽게 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"아주 실질적인 성격의 무언가를 당신에게 묻고 싶어요. 지난주 풀밭에서의 그날 이후로 줄곧 생각해 온 거예요. 나는 곧 결혼하고 싶고, 농부이니, 보다시피 내 아내로는 농장 운영에 관해 다 아는 여자가 필요해요. 당신이 그 여자가 되어 주겠어요, 테스?\"\n\n그는 자기 머리라면 못마땅해했을 충동에 그가 굴복했다고 그녀가 생각하지 않도록 그렇게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴이 온통 근심으로 물들었다. 그녀는 가까이 있음의 피할 수 없는 결과에, 그를 사랑할 수밖에 없음에 굴복했었다. 하지만 이 갑작스러운 귀결까지는 셈에 넣지 못했는데, 사실 클레어 자신도 이렇게 빨리 그것을 꺼낼 생각은 아니었으면서 그녀 앞에 내놓은 것이었다. 소멸의 쓰라림 같은 고통과 함께 그녀는, 명예로운 여자로서 반드시 해야 하고 또 맹세코 해야 할 대답의 말을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오 클레어 씨—저는 당신의 아내가 될 수 없어요—될 수 없어요!\"\n\n자기 자신의 결정이 내는 소리가 테스의 심장 자체를 부수는 듯했고, 그녀는 비탄에 잠겨 얼굴을 숙였다.\n\n\"하지만, 테스!\" 그가 그녀의 대답에 놀라며, 그녀를 더욱더 탐욕스레 끌어안으며 말했다. \"안 된다는 거예요? 분명 나를 사랑하잖아요?\"\n\n\"오 네, 네! 그리고 저는 세상 누구의 것이 되느니보다 당신의 것이 되고 싶어요.\" 괴로워하는 처녀의 다정하고 정직한 목소리가 대답했다. \"하지만 당신과 결혼할 수는 없어요!\"\n\n\"테스.\" 그가 그녀를 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓고 말했다. \"당신, 다른 누군가와 결혼하기로 약혼했군요!\"\n\"아니에요, 아니에요!\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 나를 거절하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"저는 결혼하고 싶지 않아요! 그럴 생각을 해 본 적이 없어요. 그럴 수 없어요! 저는 그저 당신을 사랑하고 싶을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜요?\"\n\n둘러댈 수밖에 없어, 그녀는 더듬더듬 말했다—\n\n\"당신 아버지는 목사님이고, 어머니는 저 같은 사람과 당신이 결혼하는 걸 좋아하지 않으실 거예요. 당신이 숙녀와 결혼하기를 바라실 거예요.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼요—내가 두 분 모두에게 말씀드렸어요. 그게 내가 집에 간 이유 중 하나였어요.\"\n\n\"저는 안 된다고 느껴요—절대로, 절대로요!\" 그녀가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"이렇게 묻는 게 너무 갑작스러운 거예요, 내 예쁜 사람?\"\n\n\"네—예상하지 못했어요.\"\n\n\"부디, 이 일을 넘겨 버려도 된다면, 테스, 내가 시간을 줄게요.\" 그가 말했다. \"집에 와서 당신 모두에게 한꺼번에 말하는 건 정말 갑작스러운 일이었어요. 한동안은 다시 이 얘기를 꺼내지 않을게요.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 반짝이는 크림 걷는 도구를 집어 들고 펌프 아래 댄 다음 새로 일을 시작했다. 하지만 다른 때처럼, 아무리 애를 써도 필요한 그 섬세한 솜씨로 크림 바로 아래 표면을 정확히 짚어 낼 수가 없었다. 어떤 때는 우유 속으로 파고들었고, 어떤 때는 허공을 갈랐다. 그녀는 거의 앞을 볼 수가 없었으니, 이 가장 좋은 벗이자 사랑하는 옹호자에게는 결코 설명할 수 없는 비탄이 자아낸 두 줄기 흐릿한 눈물이 두 눈에 가득 차 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"크림을 못 걷겠어요—못 하겠어요!\" 그녀가 그에게서 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n더는 그녀를 동요시키고 방해하지 않으려고, 사려 깊은 클레어는 더 일반적인 방향으로 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신은 우리 부모님을 아주 오해하고 있어요. 그분들은 세상에서 가장 소박한 분들이고, 전혀 야심이 없어요. 몇 안 남은 복음주의 학파의 두 분이죠. 테스, 당신은 복음주의자예요?\"\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 아주 규칙적으로 교회에 가고, 여기 우리 목사님은 그리 고교회파가 아니라고들 하더군요.\"\n\n테스가 매주 듣는 교구 성직자의 신앙관에 대해 갖고 있는 생각은, 그의 설교를 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 클레어의 것보다도 오히려 더 막연한 듯했다.\n\n\"거기서 듣는 걸 제 마음에 지금보다 더 단단히 새길 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\" 그녀가 안전한 일반론으로 말했다. \"그게 자주 저에겐 큰 슬픔이에요.\"\n\n그녀가 너무도 꾸밈없이 말했으므로, 앤젤은 그녀가 자기 신조가 고교회파인지 저교회파인지 광교회파인지 모른다 해도 아버지가 종교적 이유로 그녀를 반대할 수는 없으리라고 마음속으로 확신했다. 그는, 사실인즉 그녀가 품은 그 혼란스러운 믿음들이, 보아하니 어린 시절에 빨아들인 것들로서, 굳이 따지자면 어법에서는 트랙트파적이고 본질에서는 범신론적이라는 것을 알고 있었다. 혼란스럽든 어떻든, 그것을 어지럽히는 것은 그가 가장 바라지 않는 바였다.\n\n그대의 누이가 기도할 때, 그녀를 내버려 두라, 그녀의 이른 천국을, 그녀의 행복한 신앙관을. 그대는 그늘진 암시로, 가락 고운 나날을 이끌어 가는 한 생을 어지럽히지 말라.\n\n그는 이따금 이 충고가 가락만큼 정직하지는 못하다고 생각했었다. 하지만 지금은 기꺼이 그것을 따랐다.\n\n그는 자기 방문에서 있었던 일들에 대해, 아버지의 생활 방식에 대해, 자기 원칙을 향한 아버지의 열정에 대해 더 이야기했다. 그녀는 더 평온해졌고, 크림 걷는 손길에서 출렁임이 사라졌다. 그녀가 통 하나하나를 차례로 끝낼 때마다 그가 뒤를 따르며 우유를 흘려보낼 마개를 뽑았다.\n\n\"들어오실 때 좀 의기소침해 보이시던데요.\" 그녀가 자기 이야기에서 멀어지고 싶은 마음에 조심스레 말해 보았다.\n\n\"네—그게, 아버지가 자기 근심과 어려움에 대해 나에게 많은 얘기를 하셨는데, 그 주제는 늘 나를 우울하게 해요. 아버지가 어찌나 열성적인지 자기와 생각이 다른 사람들에게서 숱한 면박과 매질을 받으시는데, 그 연세의 분이 그런 굴욕을 당했다는 얘기를 듣는 게 나는 싫어요. 더욱이 열성이 그렇게까지 지나치면 아무 소용도 없다고 생각하니 더 그렇고요. 아주 최근에 아버지가 끼어든 무척 불쾌한 장면에 대해 얘기해 주셨어요. 아버지는 어느 선교 단체의 대리인으로 여기서 사십 마일 떨어진 트랜트리지 근방에 설교하러 가셨다가, 거기 어딘가에서 마주친 방탕한 젊은 냉소가에게—그 동네 어느 지주의 아들로, 앞 못 보는 어머니를 둔 자에게—훈계하는 걸 자기 일로 삼으셨대요. 아버지가 그 신사에게 정면으로 말을 거셨고, 큰 소동이 일었죠. 정말이지, 아무 소용없을 게 그토록 뻔한데도 낯선 사람에게 대화를 강요하다니 아버지가 참 어리석으셨어요. 하지만 무엇이든 자기 의무라고 생각하면 때를 가리지 않고 하시는 분이라, 물론 적을 많이 만드시죠. 철저히 악한 자들 사이에서뿐 아니라, 성가신 일을 싫어하는 태평한 자들 사이에서도요. 아버지는 그 일을 자랑스러워하신다고, 또 간접적으로 선이 이루어질 수도 있다고 하세요. 하지만 나는 이제 연로해지셨으니 아버지가 그렇게 자신을 소진하지 않으셨으면, 그런 돼지들은 진창에서 뒹굴게 내버려 두셨으면 해요.\"\n\n테스의 표정은 굳고 지쳐 보였고, 그 무르익은 입은 비극적이었다. 하지만 그녀는 더는 어떤 떨림도 보이지 않았다. 클레어는 되살아난 아버지에 대한 생각 때문에 그녀를 특별히 주목하지 못했다. 그렇게 그들은 액체의 직사각형들이 이룬 하얀 줄을 따라 계속 내려가며 그것들을 다 끝내고 흘려보냈고, 그러자 다른 처녀들이 돌아와 자기 들통을 집어 들었으며, 뎁이 새 우유를 받을 통들을 끓는 물로 씻으러 왔다. 테스가 들판의 젖소들에게로 가려고 물러설 때 그가 그녀에게 부드럽게 말했다—\n\n\"내 물음은요, 테스?\"\n\n\"오 안 돼요—안 돼요!\" 그녀가 무거운 절망으로, 알렉 더버빌에 대한 언급 속에서 자기 자신의 과거의 소용돌이를 다시금 들은 사람처럼 대답했다. \"그건 안 돼요!\"\n\n그녀는 풀밭을 향해 나갔고, 마치 탁 트인 공기로 자기 슬픈 속박을 몰아내려는 듯 펄쩍 뛰며 다른 젖 짜는 처녀들과 합류했다. 처녀들 모두가 저 멀리 풀밭에서 젖소들이 풀을 뜯고 있는 곳을 향해 나아갔다. 그 무리는 야생 동물의 대담한 우아함으로—무한한 공간에 익숙한 여인들의, 무모하고 길들지 않은 몸놀림으로—나아갔고, 그 안에서 그들은 헤엄치는 자가 물결에 자신을 내맡기듯 공기에 자신을 내맡겼다. 테스가 다시 눈앞에 들어온 지금, 그가 예술의 거처가 아니라 거리낌 없는 자연에서 짝을 고르는 것이 이제 그에게는 충분히 자연스러워 보였다.\n\n# XXVIII\n\n그녀의 거절은, 예상치 못한 것이긴 했으나, 클레어를 영영 낙담시키지는 못했다. 그의 여성에 대한 경험은, 부정이 흔히 긍정의 서두에 지나지 않음을 알 만큼은 충분했다. 그리고 지금의 이 부정의 태도 안에 수줍음의 희롱과는 크게 다른 예외가 깃들어 있음을 알지 못할 만큼은 미흡했다. 그녀가 이미 자기에게 사랑을 속삭이도록 허락했다는 것을 그는 추가적인 확신으로 읽었으니, 들판과 목초지에서 \"거저 한숨짓는 것\"이 결코 낭비로 여겨지지 않는다는 사실을 그는 온전히 믿지 못했던 것이다. 이곳에서는, 한 처녀의 안정된 가정에 대한 갈망이 정열을 그 자체로 목적이라 보는 건강한 생각을 마비시켜 버리는 야심가들의 근심 어린 불안한 가정에서보다, 사랑을 속삭이는 일이 깊이 따지지 않고 그저 그 자체의 달콤함을 위해 받아들여지는 경우가 더 많았다.\n\n\"테스, 왜 그렇게 단호하게 '안 된다'고 말한 거예요?\" 며칠 지나 그가 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"묻지 마세요. 왜인지 말씀드렸잖아요—얼마쯤은요. 저는 충분히 좋은 사람이 못 돼요—충분히 자격이 있는 사람이 못 돼요.\"\n\n\"어째서요? 충분히 훌륭한 숙녀가 못 된다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"네—그런 거 비슷한 거예요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"당신 친구들이 저를 경멸할 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 당신은 그분들을 오해하고 있어요—내 아버지와 어머니를요. 형들로 말하자면, 나는 신경 안 써요—\" 그가 그녀가 빠져나가지 못하도록 그녀의 등 뒤로 손가락을 깍지 꼈다. \"자—진심이 아니었죠, 그렇죠, 사랑스러운 사람?—분명 진심이 아니었어요! 당신 때문에 내가 너무 안절부절못해서 책도 못 읽고, 연주도 못 하고, 아무것도 못 하겠어요. 나는 서두르지 않아요, 테스. 하지만 알고 싶어요—당신의 따스한 입술로 직접 듣고 싶어요—언젠가 당신이 내 사람이 되리라는 걸요—당신이 고르는 어느 때라도 좋아요. 다만 언젠가는요?\"\n\n그녀는 그저 고개를 가로젓고 그에게서 눈길을 돌릴 뿐이었다.\n\n클레어는 그녀를 유심히 바라보며, 마치 상형문자라도 되는 양 그녀 얼굴의 글자들을 새겨 읽었다. 그 거절은 진심으로 보였다.\n\n\"그럼 나는 당신을 이렇게 안고 있어서는 안 되겠군요—그렇죠? 나는 당신에 대한 권리가 없어요—당신이 어디 있는지 찾아낼 권리도, 당신과 함께 걸을 권리도 없어요! 솔직히, 테스, 다른 남자를 사랑하나요?\"\n\n\"어떻게 그런 걸 물으세요?\" 그녀가 계속 자신을 억누르며 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 그렇지 않다는 걸 나는 거의 알아요. 하지만 그럼, 왜 나를 밀어내는 거예요?\"\n\n\"당신을 밀어내는 게 아니에요. 당신이—나를 사랑한다고 말해 주는 게 좋아요. 그리고 나와 함께 다니면서 언제든 그렇게 말해 줘도 돼요—절 화나게 하지 않으면서요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나를 남편으로는 받아 주지 않겠다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"아—그건 달라요—그건 정말, 당신을 위한 거예요, 내 사랑하는 사람! 오, 믿어 주세요, 그건 오로지 당신을 위해서예요! 저는 그런 식으로 당신의 사람이 되겠다고 약속하는 그 큰 행복을 제게 주고 싶지 않아요—왜냐하면—왜냐하면 저는 그래서는 안 된다고 확신하니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 나를 행복하게 해 줄 거예요!\"\n\n\"아—그렇게 생각하시지만, 당신은 몰라요!\"\n\n이런 때에, 그녀의 거절의 근거가 사회적이고 예의에 관한 일들에서 자기가 부족하다는 겸손한 자각이라고 짐작하고서, 그는 그녀가 놀랍도록 박식하고 재주가 많다고 말하곤 했다—그것은 확실히 사실이었으니, 타고난 영민함과 그를 향한 흠모 덕분에 그녀가 놀랄 만큼 그의 어휘와 억양, 그리고 그의 지식의 조각들을 주워 익혔던 것이다. 이런 다정한 다툼과 그녀의 승리가 있고 나면, 그녀는 젖 짜는 시간이면 가장 멀리 떨어진 젖소 아래로, 한가한 틈이면 골풀 사이로나 자기 방으로 홀로 가서 말없이 슬퍼하곤 했다. 겉보기엔 무덤덤하게 부정의 말을 내뱉은 지 채 일 분도 지나지 않아서 말이다.\n\n그 갈등은 너무도 무시무시했다. 그녀 자신의 마음이 너무도 강하게 그의 편에 서 있어서—가엾고 작은 양심 하나에 맞선 두 개의 열렬한 마음이었다—그녀는 가진 모든 수단을 다해 자기 결심을 굳히려 애썼다. 그녀는 마음을 정한 채로 톨버세이즈에 왔다. 어떤 일이 있어도 그녀는, 훗날 자기 남편이 눈먼 채로 자기와 결혼한 것을 두고 쓰라리게 후회하게 만들지도 모를 그런 걸음에는 동의할 수 없었다. 그리고 자기 마음이 치우치지 않았을 때 양심이 자기를 위해 내린 결정을 이제 와서 뒤엎어서는 안 된다고 그녀는 여겼다.\n\n\"왜 아무도 그에게 나에 대해 다 말해 주지 않을까?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"겨우 사십 마일 떨어진 곳이었는데—왜 그 소문이 여기까지 닿지 않았을까? 누군가는 분명 알 텐데!\"\n\n하지만 아무도 모르는 듯했다. 아무도 그에게 말해 주지 않았다.\n\n이삼 일 동안 더는 아무 말도 없었다. 그녀는 한방을 쓰는 동무들의 슬픈 얼굴빛으로 미루어, 그들이 자기를 단지 총애받는 사람으로만이 아니라 선택받은 사람으로 여기고 있음을 짐작했다. 하지만 그들은 그녀가 그의 앞길에 스스로를 들이밀지 않는다는 것을 제 눈으로 볼 수 있었다.\n\n테스는 자기 인생의 실이 이토록 또렷이 두 가닥으로, 곧 분명한 기쁨과 분명한 고통으로 꼬여 있는 시절을 일찍이 알지 못했다. 다음번 치즈 만들기 때 두 사람은 다시 단둘이 남겨졌다. 낙농장 주인 자신이 손을 거들고 있었는데, 크릭 씨도 그 아내와 마찬가지로 근래 이 두 사람 사이의 상호 관심을 눈치챈 듯했다. 비록 그들이 너무도 신중하게 처신하여 그 의심은 더없이 희미한 것에 지나지 않았지만 말이다. 어쨌든 낙농장 주인은 그들을 둘이 있게 내버려 두었다.\n\n그들은 응유 덩어리를 통에 넣기 전에 잘게 부수고 있었다. 그 작업은 빵을 큰 규모로 부수는 행위와 비슷했다. 그리고 그 티 없이 새하얀 응유 가운데서 테스 더비필드의 손은 장미의 분홍빛을 띠고 있었다. 한 줌씩 통을 채우고 있던 앤젤이 갑자기 멈추더니 그녀의 손 위에 자기 두 손을 납작하게 포갰다. 그녀의 소매는 팔꿈치 한참 위까지 걷어 올려져 있었고, 그가 더 낮게 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 부드러운 팔 안쪽 핏줄에 입을 맞추었다. 9월 초의 날씨가 무더웠음에도, 응유를 만지작거린 탓에 그녀의 팔은 갓 딴 버섯처럼 그의 입에 차갑고 축축했고, 유장의 맛이 났다. 하지만 그녀는 너무도 예민한 감수성의 다발이라, 그 닿음에 맥박이 빨라지고 피가 손끝으로 몰려들었으며, 차갑던 팔이 화끈 달아올랐다. 그러더니, 마치 그녀의 마음이 \"수줍음이 더 필요한가? 사람과 사람 사이에서 그렇듯, 남자와 여자 사이에서도 진실은 진실이거늘.\" 하고 말하기라도 한 듯이, 그녀가 눈을 들어 헌신적으로 그의 눈을 향해 빛냈고, 그 입술은 다정한 어렴풋한 미소로 올라갔다.\n\n\"내가 왜 그랬는지 알아요, 테스?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저를 무척 사랑하시니까요!\"\n\n\"그래요, 그리고 새로운 간청의 시작으로요.\"\n\n\"또 그러시면 안 돼요!\"\n\n그녀는 자기 자신의 욕망 아래서 자기 저항이 무너져 버릴지도 모른다는 갑작스러운 두려움을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"오, 테스!\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"나는 당신이 왜 이렇게 애를 태우는지 도무지 알 수가 없어요. 왜 나를 이렇게 실망시켜요? 정말이지 당신은 거의 요염한 여자 같아요—맹세코 그래요—도시물을 가장 잘 먹은 요염한 여자요! 그런 여자들은 당신처럼 꼭 이렇게 달아올랐다 식었다 하는데, 톨버세이즈 같은 은신처에서 마주치리라곤 가장 예상치 못할 부류죠…… 그래도, 사랑하는 사람,\" 그 말이 그녀를 얼마나 아프게 했는지 알아채고 그가 재빨리 덧붙였다. \"당신이 일찍이 살았던 그 누구보다도 정직하고 흠 하나 없는 사람이라는 걸 나는 알아요. 그러니 어떻게 당신을 바람둥이라 여길 수 있겠어요? 테스, 보아하니 당신이 나를 사랑하는데, 왜 내 아내가 된다는 생각을 싫어하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"저는 그 생각이 싫다고 말한 적 없어요. 그렇게 말할 수도 없고요. 왜냐하면—그건 사실이 아니니까요!\"\n\n이제 그 부담이 견딜 수 있는 한계를 넘어서자, 그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 그녀는 자리를 떠야 했다. 클레어는 너무도 아프고 당혹스러워, 뒤쫓아가 통로에서 그녀를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"말해 줘요, 말해 줘요!\" 그가 자기 손에 응유가 묻은 것도 잊은 채 격정적으로 그녀를 끌어안으며 말했다. \"나 말고는 어느 누구의 사람도 되지 않겠다고 말해 줘요!\"\n\n\"그럴게요, 그렇게 말할게요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그리고 지금 저를 보내 주시면 완전한 대답을 드릴게요. 제 지난 일들을 말씀드릴게요—저에 관한 모든 것을—전부요!\"\n\n\"당신의 지난 일들이라, 사랑하는 사람. 그래요, 물론이죠. 얼마든지요.\" 그가 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 사랑스러운 풍자로 동의를 표했다. \"내 테스는, 의심할 바 없이, 저기 정원 울타리에 핀 저 야생 나팔꽃, 오늘 아침 처음으로 제 몸을 활짝 연 저 꽃만큼이나 많은 지난 일이 있겠죠. 무엇이든 말해 줘요. 다만 나에게 어울리지 않는다는 그 가련한 표현만은 더 이상 쓰지 말아요.\"\n\n\"그러도록—노력할게요! 그리고 제 이유들을 내일—다음 주에 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n\"일요일에요?\"\n\n\"네, 일요일에요.\"\n\n마침내 그녀는 빠져나왔고, 농가 마당 아래쪽 끝의, 가지 잘린 버드나무 덤불에 이르러서야 물러서던 걸음을 멈추었다. 거기서라면 아무에게도 보이지 않을 수 있었다. 여기서 테스는 마치 침대 위에라도 누우듯 바스락거리는 창끝풀 덤불 위로 몸을 던졌고, 두근거리는 비참함 속에 웅크린 채 머물러 있었다. 그 비참함은, 결말에 대한 두려움으로도 다 억누를 수 없는 순간적인 기쁨의 분출들로 이따금 깨어졌다.\n\n사실인즉, 그녀는 묵인 쪽으로 떠밀려 가고 있었다. 그녀의 숨결의 매번의 오르내림, 피의 매번의 물결, 귓속에서 노래하는 매번의 맥박이, 그녀의 양심의 가책에 맞서 자연과 한목소리로 반기를 드는 목소리였다. 그를 무모하고 분별없이 받아들이는 것, 아무것도 밝히지 않은 채 제단에서 그와 맺어지고 발각을 운에 맡기는 것, 고통의 무쇠 이빨이 그녀를 물어 닫을 틈을 갖기 전에 무르익은 기쁨을 낚아채는 것, 그것이 사랑이 권하는 바였다. 그리고 거의 황홀의 공포 속에서 테스는, 여러 달에 걸친 외로운 자기 징벌과 씨름과 묵상과 엄격한 고립의 미래를 살아가려는 계획들에도 불구하고, 사랑의 권유가 이기리라는 것을 직감했다.\n\n오후가 깊어 갔고, 그녀는 여전히 버드나무들 사이에 머물러 있었다. 그녀는 갈래진 받침대에서 들통을 내리는 달그락 소리를, 젖소들을 한데 모으는 일에 따라붙는 \"워워!\" 하는 소리를 들었다. 하지만 그녀는 젖 짜러 가지 않았다. 그들이 그녀의 동요를 볼 테고, 그 까닭을 오직 사랑뿐이라고 여기는 낙농장 주인이 사람 좋게 그녀를 놀려 댈 텐데, 그 시달림은 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n그녀의 연인은 그녀의 지나치게 격앙된 상태를 짐작했음이 분명했고, 그녀가 나타나지 않은 데 대해 무언가 구실을 만들어 냈음이 분명했다. 아무도 묻지 않았고 부르지도 않았으니까. 여섯 시 반에 해가 하늘의 거대한 대장간 같은 모습으로 평평한 들판 위로 내려앉았다. 그리고 이내 다른 쪽에서 호박처럼 거대한 달이 떠올랐다. 끊임없는 가지치기로 본래 모습이 비틀린 그 가지 잘린 버드나무들이, 그 달을 배경으로 곤두선 채 가시털 돋친 괴물들이 되었다. 그녀는 불도 켜지 않고 안으로 들어가 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n이제 수요일이었다. 목요일이 왔고, 앤젤은 멀리서 생각에 잠긴 듯 그녀를 바라보았으나 어떤 식으로도 그녀를 방해하지 않았다. 마리안을 비롯한 집안의 젖 짜는 처녀들도 무언가 분명한 일이 진행되고 있음을 짐작한 듯, 침실에서 그녀에게 어떤 말도 강요하지 않았다. 금요일이 지나갔고, 토요일도. 내일이 그날이었다.\n\n\"나는 굴복하고 말 거야—나는 그러겠다고 말할 거야—나는 그와 결혼하게 내버려 둘 거야—나도 어쩔 수가 없어!\" 그날 밤 그녀는 다른 처녀 중 하나가 잠결에 그의 이름을 한숨처럼 내뱉는 것을 듣고는, 뜨거운 얼굴을 베개에 묻은 채 시샘하듯 헐떡였다. \"나 말고 다른 누가 그를 갖는 건 견딜 수가 없어! 하지만 그건 그에게 잘못된 일이고, 그가 알게 되면 그를 죽일지도 몰라! 오 내 가슴—오—오—오!\"\n\n# XXIX\n\n\"자, 내가 오늘 아침에 누구 소식을 들었을 것 같나?\" 이튿날 크릭 낙농장 주인이 아침상에 앉으며, 우물거리며 먹는 사내들과 처녀들을 수수께끼 내듯 빙 둘러보며 말했다. \"자, 대체 누구일 것 같아?\"\n\n한 사람이 알아맞혀 보았고, 또 한 사람이 알아맞혀 보았다. 크릭 부인은 알아맞혀 보지 않았으니, 이미 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"흠,\" 낙농장 주인이 말했다. \"바로 그 칠칠치 못한 잡놈 잭 돌럽이야. 얼마 전에 어떤 과부 여자랑 결혼했다는군.\"\n\n\"설마 잭 돌럽이? 그 악당이—그럴 수가!\" 한 젖 짜는 사내가 말했다.\n\n그 이름은 테스 더비필드의 의식 속으로 재빨리 파고들었으니, 그것은 제 연인을 욕보였다가 나중에 그 처녀의 어머니에게 버터 휘젓는 통 속에서 호되게 당한 그 사내의 이름이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그래서 약속대로 그 용감한 아주머니의 딸과 결혼한 건가요?\" 앤젤 클레어가 멍하니 물었다. 그는 크릭 부인이 그의 신사다움을 의식해 늘 그를 따로 앉히는 작은 탁자에서 읽고 있던 신문을 뒤적이고 있었다.\n\n\"아닙니다요, 나리. 그럴 마음이 애당초 없었죠.\" 낙농장 주인이 대답했다. \"말씀드렸듯이 과부 여자인데, 돈깨나 있었던 모양이에요—연 오십 파운드쯤요. 그놈이 노린 건 그게 다였죠. 둘은 부랴부랴 결혼했고, 그런 다음 여자가 그놈한테 말하길, 결혼하는 바람에 그 연 오십 파운드를 잃게 됐다지 뭡니까. 그 소식에 우리 신사 양반 심정이 어땠을지 한번 상상해 보세요! 그 후로 그렇게 개와 고양이처럼 으르렁대며 사는 꼴이 다시없을 정도라니까요! 그놈한테는 아주 싸지요. 하지만 불행히도 가엾은 여자가 제일 손해를 보는 거죠.\"\n\n\"흠, 그 어리석은 여편네가 첫 남편 귀신이 그놈을 괴롭히리란 걸 진작 말했어야지.\" 크릭 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지, 그렇지.\" 낙농장 주인이 머뭇거리며 대꾸했다. \"그래도 어찌 된 일인지는 빤히 보이잖나. 여자는 가정을 원했고, 그놈을 잃을 위험을 무릅쓰기 싫었던 거지. 처녀들, 그게 그런 거였을 것 같지 않나?\"\n\n그는 처녀들이 늘어선 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"교회에 들어가기 직전에, 그놈이 도저히 발을 뺄 수 없을 때 말했어야 해요.\" 마리안이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래요, 그랬어야죠.\" 이즈도 동의했다.\n\n\"여자는 그놈이 뭘 노리는지 봤을 텐데, 거절했어야 해요.\" 레티가 발작하듯 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 자네는 어떻게 생각하나, 아가씨?\" 낙농장 주인이 테스에게 물었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 여자가—사실대로 말했어야 해요—아니면 거절하든가요—잘 모르겠어요.\" 테스가 대답했는데, 버터 바른 빵이 목에 걸렸다.\n\n\"난 둘 다 안 했을 거야, 천만에.\" 오두막에서 온 기혼의 일손, 벡 닙스가 말했다. \"사랑과 전쟁에선 뭐든 공정한 법이야. 나라면 그 여자가 한 그대로 그놈이랑 결혼했을 테고, 만약 그놈이 나한테, 내가 말하고 싶지 않았던 내 첫 남자에 관한 무엇이든 미리 말 안 했다고 두 마디라도 지껄였다면, 밀방망이로 후려쳐 쓰러뜨렸을 거야—그런 비쩍 마른 좀팽이 같은 놈쯤은! 어떤 여자든 할 수 있지.\"\n\n이 익살에 뒤따른 웃음에 테스는 형식상 그저 서글픈 미소 하나를 보탰을 뿐이다. 그들에게 희극인 것이 그녀에게는 비극이었고, 그녀는 그들의 웃음을 견디기 어려웠다. 그녀는 곧 식탁에서 일어나, 클레어가 곧 자기를 뒤따라오리라는 느낌을 안고서, 꼬불꼬불한 작은 길을 따라 걸었다. 관개 수로의 이쪽으로 발을 디뎠다가 저쪽으로 디뎠다가 하면서, 마침내 바르강의 본류 곁에 섰다. 사람들이 강 위쪽에서 물풀을 베어 낸 터라, 그 풀 덩어리들이 그녀 곁을 떠다니고 있었다—초록빛 미나리아재비의 떠다니는 섬들이었는데, 그녀가 거의 올라타도 될 만했다. 그 풀의 긴 타래들이, 젖소들이 건너지 못하게 박아 놓은 말뚝들에 걸려 멈춰 있었다.\n\n그렇다, 거기에 아픔이 있었다. 여자가 자기 이야기를 털어놓는다는 이 문제는—그녀에게는 가장 무거운 십자가였건만—남들에게는 한낱 우스개로밖에 보이지 않는 듯했다. 마치 사람들이 순교를 비웃기라도 하는 것 같았다.\n\n\"테시!\" 등 뒤에서 소리가 들리더니, 클레어가 도랑을 훌쩍 건너 그녀의 발치에 내려섰다. \"내 아내가—곧!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요. 그럴 수 없어요. 당신을 위해서예요, 오 클레어 씨. 당신을 위해서, 그러니 아니라고 하는 거예요!\"\n\n\"테스!\"\n\n\"그래도 아니에요!\" 그녀가 거듭 말했다.\n\n이렇게 나올 줄 예상하지 못한 그는, 말을 마친 직후 늘어뜨린 머리채 아래로 그녀의 허리를 가볍게 감싸 안았다. (테스를 비롯한 젊은 우유 짜는 처녀들은 일요일 아침이면 머리를 풀어헤친 채로 아침을 먹은 뒤, 교회에 갈 채비로 한껏 높이 틀어 올렸다. 소에 머리를 기댄 채 젖을 짤 때는 도저히 할 수 없는 머리 모양이었다.) 만약 그녀가 \"아니에요\" 대신 \"네\"라고 했더라면 그는 입을 맞추었을 것이다. 분명 그럴 작정이었으니까. 그러나 그녀의 단호한 거절이 양심적인 그의 마음을 머뭇거리게 했다. 한집에 사는 동무라는 처지가, 어쩔 수 없이 부대껴야 하는 까닭에, 여자인 그녀를 너무도 불리하게 만드는지라, 그녀가 그를 더 잘 피할 수 있는 형편이었다면 떳떳하게 썼을 법한 어떤 달콤한 말의 압박도, 지금 그녀에게 행사하는 것은 부당하다고 그는 느꼈다. 그는 잠시 가두었던 그녀의 허리를 풀어 주었고, 입맞춤을 거두었다.\n\n모든 것이 그 풀어 줌에 달려 있었다. 이번에 그녀가 그를 거절할 힘을 준 것은 오로지 주인이 들려준 과부 이야기뿐이었다. 그것도 한순간만 더 지났더라면 무너졌을 것이다. 그러나 에인절은 더는 말하지 않았다. 그의 얼굴에는 당혹감이 어렸다. 그는 가 버렸다.\n\n날이면 날마다 두 사람은 만났다—전보다는 다소 뜸하게. 그렇게 두세 주가 지나갔다. 구월 말이 다가왔고, 그녀는 그의 눈빛에서 그가 다시 청해 올 수도 있겠다는 것을 읽을 수 있었다.\n\n이제 그의 방식은 달라졌다—마치 그녀의 거절이란 결국 수줍음일 뿐이며, 그런 청혼의 새로움에 놀란 젊음의 표현에 지나지 않는다고 마음을 굳힌 사람 같았다. 그 화제가 오를 때마다 변덕스럽게 발뺌하는 그녀의 태도가 그런 생각을 뒷받침했다. 그래서 그는 더 어르고 달래는 쪽으로 나갔다. 말 이상으로 넘어가거나 다시 애무를 시도하는 일은 결코 없이, 말로써 할 수 있는 최선을 다했다.\n\n이렇게 클레어는, 졸졸 흐르는 우유 소리 같은 낮은 목소리로 끈질기게 구애했다—소 곁에서, 우유 거품을 걷어 낼 때, 버터를 만들 때, 치즈를 만들 때, 알을 품은 닭들 사이에서, 새끼 낳는 돼지들 틈에서—일찍이 어떤 우유 짜는 처녀도 이런 남자에게 이토록 구애받은 적이 없었다.\n\n테스는 자신이 끝내 무너지고 말리라는 것을 알았다. 앞선 그 결합에 어떤 도덕적 유효성이 있다는 종교적 감각도, 솔직하고 싶다는 양심적 바람도, 이 앞에서는 더 오래 버틸 수 없었다. 그녀는 그를 너무도 뜨겁게 사랑했고, 그는 그녀의 눈에 너무도 신과 같았다. 그리고 비록 배운 것은 없어도 본능적으로 섬세했던 그녀의 천성은 그의 인도와 보살핌을 갈구했다. 그리하여 테스는 \"나는 결코 그의 아내가 될 수 없어\"라고 스스로 거듭 되뇌었으나, 그 말은 헛되었다. 차분한 강인함이라면 굳이 입에 올릴 수고조차 하지 않았을 것을 입 밖에 내고 있다는 사실 자체가 그녀의 나약함을 드러내는 증거였다. 그의 목소리가 그 해묵은 화제를 다시 꺼낼 때마다, 그 모든 소리는 두려운 환희로 그녀를 흔들었고, 그녀는 자신이 두려워하던 그 번복을 도리어 갈망했다.\n\n그의 태도는—어떤 남자인들 안 그렇겠는가?—어떤 처지에서든, 어떤 변화나 비난이나 폭로 앞에서든 그녀를 사랑하고 아끼고 지켜 줄 사람의 그것이어서, 그녀는 그 햇살을 쬐며 어둠이 옅어졌다. 그동안 계절은 춘분점을 향해 나아가고 있었고, 날씨는 여전히 맑았으나 해는 훨씬 짧아졌다. 낙농장은 다시 오랫동안 아침 촛불을 켜 놓고 일했다. 그리고 어느 날 아침 세시에서 네시 사이, 클레어의 새로운 간청이 다시 한번 터져 나왔다.\n\n그녀는 잠옷 차림으로 평소처럼 그의 방문 앞으로 뛰어올라 가 그를 불렀다. 그러고는 옷을 입고 다른 이들을 부르러 돌아갔다가, 십 분쯤 뒤 손에 촛불을 들고 계단 꼭대기로 걸어가던 참이었다. 바로 그 순간 그가 셔츠 바람으로 위층 계단을 내려와, 계단을 가로질러 팔을 뻗었다.\n\n\"자, 바람둥이 아가씨, 내려가기 전에.\" 그가 단호하게 말했다. \"내가 말을 꺼낸 지 보름이오. 이대로는 더는 안 되겠소. 무슨 뜻인지 말해 주시오. 안 그러면 나는 이 집을 떠나야겠소. 방금 내 방문이 빠끔히 열려 있어서 당신을 보았소. 당신의 안전을 위해서라도 나는 가야 하오. 당신은 모르오. 자? 마침내 그렇다는 거요?\"\n\n\"저는 이제 막 일어났어요, 클레어 씨. 이렇게 이른 시간에 추궁하다니 너무하세요!\" 그녀가 입을 삐죽였다. \"저를 바람둥이라고 부르실 건 없어요. 잔인하고 사실도 아니에요. 나중까지 기다려 주세요. 제발 나중까지 기다려 주세요! 지금부터 그때까지 정말로 진지하게 생각해 볼게요. 그러니 내려가게 해 주세요!\"\n\n촛불을 비스듬히 든 채, 자기 말의 진지함을 웃음으로 무마하려 애쓰는 그 모습이, 그가 말한 그런 여자처럼 조금은 보이기도 했다.\n\n\"그럼 클레어 씨 말고 에인절이라고 불러 주오.\"\n\n\"에인절.\"\n\n\"에인절, 가장 사랑하는 사람—안 될 게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 부르면 제가 동의한다는 뜻이 되잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그건 그저 당신이 나를 사랑한다는 뜻일 뿐이오, 설령 나와 결혼할 수 없더라도 말이오. 그리고 그건 당신이 오래전에 친절하게도 인정해 준 일이잖소.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋아요. '에인절, 가장 사랑하는 사람'이라고요, 정 그래야 한다면.\" 그녀가 촛불을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. 마음을 졸이면서도 입가에는 장난기 어린 곡선이 떠올랐다.\n\n클레어는 그녀의 약속을 받아 내기 전에는 결코 입 맞추지 않으리라 다짐했다. 그러나 어쩐 일인지, 테스가 예쁘게 걷어 올린 우유 짜는 작업복 차림으로, 거품 걷고 젖 짜는 일이 끝나 손질할 짬이 날 때까지 머리를 아무렇게나 머리 위에 틀어 올린 채 거기 서 있자, 그는 그 다짐을 깨고 한순간 그녀의 뺨에 입술을 가져갔다. 그녀는 그를 돌아보지도, 다른 말을 더하지도 않고 아주 빠르게 계단을 내려갔다. 다른 처녀들은 이미 내려와 있었고, 그 화제는 더 이어지지 않았다. 매리언만 빼고는, 모두가 바깥의 첫 차가운 새벽 기미와 대비되어 아침 촛불이 뿜어내는 서글픈 누런빛 속에서, 그 두 사람을 애틋하고도 의심쩍은 눈길로 바라보았다.\n\n거품 걷는 일이 끝나자—가을이 다가올수록 우유가 줄어들어 날마다 점점 줄어드는 일이었지만—레티와 나머지가 밖으로 나갔다. 연인들이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"우리의 떨리는 삶은 저들의 삶과는 사뭇 다르지, 안 그래?\" 그가 동트는 날의 싸늘한 창백함 속을 종종걸음으로 앞서가는 세 사람을 바라보며, 생각에 잠긴 듯 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 아주 다르진 않다고 생각해요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각하지?\"\n\n\"떨리지 않는 여자의 삶이란 거의 없으니까요.\" 테스가 그 새 낱말이 인상에 남는 듯 잠시 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"저 세 사람한테도 당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 많은 게 있어요.\"\n\n\"그들 안에 무엇이 있는데?\"\n\n\"저 세 사람 중 거의 누구라도,\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. \"저보다 더 어울리는—어쩌면 더 어울리는—아내가 될 거예요. 그리고 어쩌면 그들도 저만큼이나 당신을 사랑할지도—거의.\"\n\n\"오, 테시!\"\n\n그토록 의연하게 너그러움이 자신에게 불리한 입찰을 하나 던지도록 마음먹었으면서도, 그 조바심 어린 탄성을 듣는 것이 그녀에게는 더없는 안도였음을 보여 주는 기색이 있었다. 이제 그 일은 끝났고, 그녀에게는 그 순간 두 번째로 자기를 제물로 바치려 시도할 힘이 없었다. 오두막 가운데 하나에서 온 우유 짜는 사람이 그들과 합류했고, 그토록 그들에게 깊이 관계된 그 일에 대해서는 더 말이 오가지 않았다. 그러나 테스는 오늘이 그 일을 결정짓는 날이 되리라는 것을 알았다.\n\n오후에는 주인집 식구와 일꾼 가운데 여럿이 평소처럼 목초지로 내려갔다. 낙농장에서 한참 떨어진 곳이었는데, 거기서는 소를 집으로 몰아오지 않고 많은 소의 젖을 그 자리에서 짰다. 소들이 새끼를 배 가면서 산출량은 점점 줄었고, 무성한 푸른 철의 임시 우유 짜는 일꾼들은 이미 해고된 뒤였다.\n\n일은 느긋하게 진척되었다. 한 통 가득 찰 때마다 그 자리에 끌어다 놓은 커다란 용수철 짐마차에 실린 길쭉한 깡통에 부어졌다. 젖을 다 짠 소들은 어슬렁어슬렁 멀어져 갔다. 다른 이들과 함께 거기 있던 크릭 주인은, 그의 작업복이 납빛 저녁 하늘을 배경으로 기적처럼 새하얗게 빛나고 있었는데, 문득 자기의 묵직한 시계를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"이런, 생각보다 늦었군.\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 빌어먹을! 조심하지 않으면 이 우유를 제때 역에 못 대겠어. 오늘은 집에 가져가서 전체와 섞은 뒤 부칠 시간이 없네. 여기서 곧장 역으로 가야겠어. 누가 몰고 가지?\"\n\n자기 일도 아니었건만 클레어 씨가 자청하고 나섰고, 테스에게 같이 가자고 청했다. 저녁은 해가 없는데도 그 계절치고 따뜻하고 후텁지근했던 터라, 테스는 우유 짜는 모자만 쓴 채 팔뚝을 드러내고 윗옷도 걸치지 않고 나와 있었다. 마차를 탈 차림은 분명 아니었다. 그래서 그녀는 자기의 변변찮은 옷차림을 죽 훑어보는 것으로 대답을 대신했다. 그러나 클레어가 부드럽게 졸랐다. 그녀는 자기 우유통과 걸상을 주인에게 넘겨 집으로 가져가게 함으로써 응낙의 뜻을 표하고는, 클레어 곁에 올라 용수철 짐마차에 탔다.\n\n# XXX\n\n기우는 햇빛 속에서 두 사람은 목초지를 가로지르는 평탄한 길을 따라갔다. 목초지는 잿빛으로 몇 마일이나 뻗어 나가, 저 멀리 끝자락에서는 에그던 황야의 거뭇하고 가파른 비탈을 배경으로 두고 있었다. 그 꼭대기에는 전나무 무더기와 줄기가 서 있었는데, 톱니처럼 들쭉날쭉한 우듬지가 마치 마법에 걸린 검은 정면의 성을 머리에 인 흉벽 두른 탑들처럼 보였다.\n\n두 사람은 서로 가까이 있다는 느낌에 너무도 빠져든 나머지 오랫동안 말을 꺼내지 못했다. 등 뒤 길쭉한 깡통 속 우유가 출렁거리는 소리만이 그 침묵을 깰 뿐이었다. 그들이 따라가는 좁은 길은 어찌나 인적이 드문지, 개암 열매가 가지에 그대로 매달려 있다가 껍질에서 미끄러져 나올 지경이었고, 검은딸기는 묵직한 송이를 이루어 늘어져 있었다. 이따금 에인절은 채찍 끈을 그 송이 가운데 하나에 휘감아 따서는 길동무에게 건네주곤 했다.\n\n이내 흐린 하늘은 비의 전령 같은 빗방울을 떨구어 그 속뜻을 알리기 시작했고, 한낮의 정체된 공기는 두 사람의 얼굴 둘레를 노니는 변덕스러운 산들바람으로 바뀌었다. 강과 못 위에 어렸던 수은 빛 윤기가 사라졌다. 너른 빛의 거울이던 그것들이, 줄칼처럼 까슬까슬한 표면을 한 윤기 없는 납빛 판으로 변했다. 그러나 그 광경도 그녀의 골똘함을 흔들지는 못했다. 본디 살구빛이던 그녀의 얼굴은 철 따라 약간 그을려 있었는데, 빗방울이 두드리는 통에 그 빛깔이 더욱 짙어졌다. 그리고 소의 옆구리에 눌려 평소처럼 묶은 데서 흘러내려 무명 보닛의 장막 밖으로 빠져나온 머리카락은, 물기에 척척하게 젖어 들어 거의 해초나 다름없는 꼴이 되었다.\n\n\"제가 오지 말았어야 했나 봐요.\" 그녀가 하늘을 올려다보며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"비가 와서 안됐소.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 당신이 여기 있어 주니 얼마나 기쁜지!\"\n\n멀리 에그던이 차츰 흐물흐물한 비의 장막 뒤로 사라졌다. 저녁은 점점 어두워졌고, 길마다 문이 가로막고 있어 걷는 속도보다 빨리 모는 것은 안전하지 않았다. 공기는 제법 쌀쌀했다.\n\n\"팔도 어깨도 아무것도 안 걸쳐서, 당신이 감기 들까 봐 너무 걱정이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"내게 바싹 붙어요. 그러면 이슬비도 별로 못 괴롭힐 거요. 이 비가 나를 돕고 있을지 모른다는 생각이 들지 않았다면, 더더욱 안타까웠을 텐데.\"\n\n그녀가 알아차리지 못할 만큼 슬며시 더 가까이 붙자, 그는 큼직한 돛천 한 자락으로 두 사람을 함께 둘러 감쌌다. 우유통에 햇볕이 들지 않게 가끔 쓰던 천이었다. 클레어는 양손이 자유롭지 못했으므로, 테스가 그 천이 자기뿐 아니라 그에게서도 흘러내리지 않게 붙잡았다.\n\n\"자, 이제 다시 됐소. 아—아니, 안 됐군! 빗물이 내 목으로 조금 흘러드는데, 당신 쪽으로는 더 흐르겠소. 그래, 그게 낫군. 당신 팔은 젖은 대리석 같소, 테스. 천으로 닦아요. 자, 가만히 있으면 더는 한 방울도 안 맞을 거요. 그런데, 여보—내 그 질문 말이오—그 오래 묵은 질문 말이오?\"\n\n한동안 그가 들을 수 있는 대답이라곤 젖어 가는 길 위를 두드리는 말발굽 소리와, 등 뒤 깡통 속 우유가 출렁거리는 소리뿐이었다.\n\n\"당신이 한 말 기억하오?\"\n\n\"기억해요.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"집에 닿기 전에, 잊지 말고.\"\n\n\"애써 볼게요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 더 말하지 않았다. 마차가 나아가는 동안, 캐롤라인 시대에 지어진 어느 옛 장원 저택의 잔해가 하늘을 배경으로 솟아올랐고, 이윽고 지나쳐 뒤로 멀어졌다.\n\n\"저것은,\" 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주려고 그가 일러 주었다. \"흥미로운 옛집이오—한때 이 주에서 큰 세력을 떨치던 노르만계의 어느 오래된 가문, 더버빌 가에 속했던 여러 영지 가운데 하나지. 나는 그들의 저택을 지날 때마다 그 가문을 떠올리지 않을 수 없소. 이름난 가문이 끊긴다는 데에는 무언가 몹시 서글픈 데가 있어. 설령 그것이 사납고 거만하고 봉건적인 명성이었다 해도 말이오.\"\n\n\"네.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 바로 앞 그늘진 너른 들판의 한 지점을 향해 천천히 나아갔다. 거기서는 희미한 불빛 하나가 그 존재를 드러내기 시작하고 있었다. 낮이면 그곳에서, 짙은 초록 배경 위로 이따금 깜빡이는 하얀 증기 줄기가 일정한 간격을 두고 피어올라, 그들의 외딴 세계와 근대적 삶이 간헐적으로 맞닿는 순간을 알리던 자리였다. 근대적 삶은 하루에 서너 번 이 지점까지 증기의 더듬이를 뻗어, 토박이들의 존재를 건드렸다가, 마치 건드린 것이 영 마뜩잖다는 듯 이내 그 더듬이를 다시 거두어들이곤 했다.\n\n그들은 그 희미한 불빛에 다다랐다. 자그마한 철도역의 그을린 등불에서 나오는 빛이었다. 보잘것없는 지상의 별이었으나, 어떤 의미에서는 그것이 그토록 초라하게 대비되는 저 천상의 별들보다 탤보세이스 낙농장과 인류에게 더 중요한 별이었다. 갓 짠 우유 깡통들이 빗속에서 내려졌고, 테스는 곁에 선 호랑가시나무에서 약간의 비가림을 얻었다.\n\n이윽고 젖은 선로 위로 거의 소리도 없이 멈춰 서는 기차의 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 들렸고, 우유는 깡통 하나하나가 잽싸게 화물칸으로 휙휙 실려 들어갔다. 기관차의 불빛이 한순간 커다란 호랑가시나무 아래 꼼짝 않고 선 테스 더비필드의 모습 위로 번쩍였다. 번쩍이는 크랭크와 바퀴에 이 순박한 처녀보다 더 어울리지 않아 보이는 것은 없었으리라. 둥근 맨팔, 비에 젖은 얼굴과 머리, 잠시 멈춰 선 다정한 표범 같은 정지한 자세, 시대도 유행도 없는 무명 옷, 그리고 이마 위로 늘어진 무명 보닛.\n\n그녀는 격정적인 천성이 이따금 보이는 그 말 없는 순종으로 다시 연인 곁에 올라탔고, 두 사람이 다시 돛천을 머리끝까지 뒤집어쓰자, 이제 짙어진 밤 속으로 도로 잠겨 들었다. 테스는 감수성이 너무도 예민했던 까닭에, 물질적 진보의 소용돌이와 맞닿았던 그 몇 분이 그녀의 생각 속에 오래 남았다.\n\n\"런던 사람들이 내일 아침에 이걸 마시겠죠, 그렇죠?\" 그녀가 물었다. \"우리가 한 번도 본 적 없는 낯선 사람들이요.\"\n\n\"그래—아마 그러겠지. 우리가 부친 그대로는 아니고. 머리로 올라가지 않게끔 농도를 낮춘 뒤에 말이오.\"\n\n\"고귀한 남자와 고귀한 여자, 대사와 백인대장, 귀부인과 장사하는 여자, 그리고 소라고는 한 번도 본 적 없는 갓난아기들.\"\n\n\"음, 그래. 어쩌면. 특히 백인대장들이.\"\n\n\"우리에 대해서는, 이게 어디서 오는지는 아무것도 모르겠죠. 아니면 우리 둘이 오늘 밤 빗속에서, 이게 제때 닿도록 황야를 몇 마일이나 가로질러 몰고 왔다는 걸 생각이나 할까요?\"\n\n\"우리가 이 귀하신 런던 사람들만을 위해서 몬 건 아니오. 우리 자신을 위해서도 조금 몬 거지—당신이, 분명히, 가라앉혀 줄 그 마음 졸이던 문제 때문에 말이오, 사랑하는 테스. 자, 이렇게 말하게 해 주오. 당신은 이미 내 것이오, 알다시피. 당신 마음 말이오. 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"당신도 저만큼 잘 아시잖아요. 오 네—네!\"\n\n\"그럼, 당신 마음이 그렇다면, 어째서 당신 손은 아니란 말이오?\"\n\n\"제 유일한 이유는 당신 때문이었어요—어떤 문제 때문에요. 당신께 말씀드릴 게 있어요—\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 오롯이 내 행복을 위한 일이고, 게다가 내 세상살이의 편의를 위한 일이라고 쳐 봅시다?\"\n\n\"오 네. 그게 당신의 행복과 세상살이의 편의를 위한 거라면요. 하지만 제가 여기 오기 전의 제 삶이—저는 원해요—\"\n\n\"음, 그건 내 행복뿐 아니라 내 편의를 위한 일이기도 하오. 내가 아주 큰 농장을, 영국이든 식민지든, 갖게 된다면, 당신은 아내로서 더없이 값진 사람이 될 거요. 이 나라에서 가장 큰 저택에서 나온 여자보다 나을 거요. 그러니 제발—제발, 사랑하는 테시, 당신이 내 앞길을 막으리라는 그 마음을랑 떨쳐 버려요.\"\n\n\"하지만 제 내력이요. 당신이 그걸 알기를 바라요—말하게 해 주셔야 해요—그러면 당신은 저를 그렇게 좋아하지 않으실 거예요!\"\n\n\"하고 싶다면 하구려, 가장 사랑하는 사람. 그럼 그 귀한 내력을 들어 봅시다. 그래, 나는 어디어디에서, 주님의 해 몇 년에 태어났고—\"\n\n\"저는 말럿에서 태어났어요.\" 그녀가, 가벼이 던진 말이긴 했어도 그 말을 거들음 삼아 붙들며 말했다. \"그리고 거기서 자랐고요. 학교를 그만둘 때 육 학년이었는데, 사람들이 제가 무척 영민하니 좋은 선생이 될 거라고 했어요. 그래서 제가 선생이 되기로 정해졌고요. 그런데 우리 집에 어려움이 있었어요. 아버지가 그리 부지런하지 못하셨고, 술도 좀 드셨거든요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 가엾은 것! 새로울 것 없는 일이지.\" 그가 그녀를 자기 곁으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"그러고는—거기에 아주 별난 게 있어요—저에 대해서요. 저—저는—\"\n\n테스의 숨결이 가빠졌다.\n\n\"그래, 가장 사랑하는 사람. 괜찮소.\"\n\n\"저는—저는—더비필드가 아니라 더버빌이에요—우리가 지나온 그 옛집을 가졌던 그들과 같은 가문의 후손이요. 그리고—우리는 다 영락해서 아무것도 아니게 됐어요!\"\n\n\"더버빌이라니!—정말이오! 그런데 그게 그 어려움의 전부요, 사랑하는 테스?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 가냘프게 대답했다.\n\n\"그래—이걸 안다고 내가 당신을 덜 사랑할 까닭이 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n\"주인 어른이 당신은 오래된 가문을 싫어한다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n그가 웃었다.\n\n\"음, 한편으로는 사실이오. 나는 무엇보다 핏줄을 앞세우는 귀족주의 원리를 정말 싫어하고, 따져 보는 사람으로서 우리가 존중해야 할 유일한 혈통이란 육체적 부계 따위와는 상관없이 슬기롭고 덕 있는 이들의 그 정신적 혈통뿐이라고 생각하오. 하지만 이 소식에는 굉장히 흥미가 동하는걸—내가 얼마나 흥미로운지 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요! 당신 자신은 그 유명한 가계의 일원이라는 게 흥미롭지 않소?\"\n\n\"아뇨. 저는 그게 서글프다고 생각했어요—특히 여기 와서, 제 눈에 보이는 저 언덕과 들판 가운데 많은 곳이 한때 우리 아버지 집안 것이었음을 알고 나서는요. 하지만 다른 언덕과 들판은 레티네 집안 것이었고, 어쩌면 또 다른 데는 매리언네 것이었겠죠. 그러니 저는 그걸 별로 대단하게 여기지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그래—지금 땅을 일구는 이들 가운데 얼마나 많은 사람이 한때는 그 땅의 주인이었는지 놀라울 따름이오. 나는 가끔, 어떤 정파의 정치가들이 그런 사정을 밑천 삼지 않는 게 의아하다오. 하지만 그들은 그걸 모르는 모양이지... 당신 이름이 더버빌과 닮았다는 걸 내가 알아채지 못하고, 그 빤한 변형을 짚어 내지 못했다니 의아하군. 그런데 이게 당신을 괴롭히던 그 비밀이었던 거요!\"\n\n그녀는 말하지 않았다. 마지막 순간에 용기가 그녀를 저버렸다. 진작 말하지 않았다고 그가 나무랄까 봐 두려웠던 것이다. 그리고 자기를 지키려는 본능이 솔직함보다 더 강했다.\n\n\"물론,\" 아무것도 모르는 클레어가 말을 이었다. \"나야 당신이, 나머지를 희생시켜 제 힘을 키운 그 잇속 차리는 소수가 아니라, 오래도록 참고 견디며, 입 다물고, 기록되지도 못한 영국 민중의 그 대열에서만 나온 사람임을 알게 됐다면 기뻤을 거요. 하지만 당신을 사랑하는 마음에 나는 거기서 타락해 버렸소, 테스 (그는 말하며 웃었다). 그래서 마찬가지로 잇속을 차리게 됐지. 당신을 위해서, 나는 당신의 혈통을 기뻐하오. 세상은 구제 불능으로 속물이니, 당신의 그 출신이라는 사실은, 내가 마음먹은 대로 당신을 책 많이 읽은 여자로 만들고 나면, 세상이 당신을 내 아내로 받아들이는 데 제법 차이를 빚어낼지도 모르오. 우리 어머니도, 가엾은 양반, 그것 때문에 당신을 훨씬 더 좋게 보실 거요. 테스, 당신은 바로 오늘부터 당신 이름을 제대로 써야 하오—더버빌이라고.\"\n\n\"저는 다른 쪽이 차라리 더 좋은데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래야 하오, 가장 사랑하는 사람! 맙소사, 어디 졸부 백만장자 수십 명이 그런 가문을 차지하려고 달려들 텐데! 그러고 보니, 그런 부류 가운데 그 이름을 취한 자가 하나 있지—내가 어디서 들었더라?—체이스 근방이었던 것 같은데. 아니, 그자가 바로 내가 당신에게 말한, 우리 아버지와 한바탕했던 그 사람이오. 참 묘한 우연이군!\"\n\n\"에인절, 저는 차라리 그 이름을 안 쓰는 게 낫겠어요! 어쩌면 불길한 이름인지도 몰라요!\"\n\n그녀는 동요하고 있었다.\n\n\"자 그럼, 테레사 더버빌 양, 당신은 내 손안에 있소. 내 이름을 받으시오, 그러면 당신 이름에서 벗어나게 될 테니! 비밀도 드러났으니, 어째서 당신은 더 이상 나를 거절해야 한단 말이오?\"\n\n\"저를 아내로 두는 게 정말로 당신을 행복하게 할 게 확실하다면, 그리고 당신이 저와 결혼하기를 정말로, 정말로 간절히 원한다고 느끼신다면—\"\n\n\"그렇소, 가장 사랑하는 사람, 물론이오!\"\n\n\"제 말은, 제게 어떤 허물이 있든, 당신이 저를 너무도 간절히 원하고 저 없이는 도저히 살 수 없겠다고 하셔야만, 제가 그러겠다고 말해야 마땅하다는 마음이 들 거란 거예요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그럴 거요—그러겠다고 말하고 있잖소, 나는 알아! 당신은 영원히, 영원토록 내 것이 될 거요.\" 그가 그녀를 바싹 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"네!\"\n\n그 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 그녀는 메마르고 거친 흐느낌을 터뜨렸는데, 어찌나 격렬한지 몸이 찢어지는 듯했다. 테스는 결코 신경질적인 처녀가 아니었던지라, 그는 놀랐다.\n\n\"왜 우는 거요, 가장 사랑하는 사람?\"\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요—정말!—저는 너무 기뻐서요—당신 것이 되어, 당신을 행복하게 해 드린다고 생각하니!\"\n\n\"하지만 이건 그리 기쁜 것 같지가 않은데, 내 테시!\"\n\n\"제 말은—제 맹세를 무너뜨렸기에 우는 거예요! 저는 결혼하지 않은 채 죽겠다고 했었거든요!\"\n\n\"하지만, 나를 사랑한다면 내가 당신 남편이 되기를 바랄 거 아니오?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 네! 하지만 오, 저는 가끔 차라리 태어나지 말았더라면 싶어요!\"\n\n\"자, 사랑하는 테스, 당신이 무척 흥분해 있고 또 무척 경험이 없다는 걸 내가 모른다면, 그 말은 그리 듣기 좋은 말은 아니라고 했을 거요. 나를 마음에 둔다면서 어쩌다 그런 걸 바라게 됐소? 당신, 나를 마음에 두긴 하오? 어떤 식으로든 그걸 증명해 줬으면 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"제가 한 것보다 어떻게 더 증명하란 말이에요?\" 그녀가 애정에 정신을 잃고 외쳤다. \"이러면 더 증명이 될까요?\"\n\n그녀가 그의 목을 끌어안았고, 클레어는 처음으로, 테스가 그를 사랑하듯 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 사랑하는 사람의 입술에 닿는 격정적인 여자의 입맞춤이 어떤 것인지를 알게 되었다.\n\n\"자—이제 믿으시겠어요?\" 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며, 눈을 닦으며 물었다.\n\n\"믿소. 나는 정말로 의심한 적 없소—한 번도, 한 번도!\"\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 어둠을 뚫고 마차를 몰아갔다. 돛천 안에서 한 덩이를 이룬 채, 말은 제멋대로 가고, 비는 그들을 향해 들이쳤다. 그녀는 승낙했다. 처음부터 응낙했어도 마찬가지였을 것을. 만물에 깃든 그 \"기쁨을 향한 갈망\", 마치 조수가 무력한 해초를 흔들듯 인류를 제 목적 쪽으로 휘젓는 그 어마어마한 힘은, 사회의 규범을 두고 막연히 골머리를 앓는다고 해서 다스려질 것이 아니었다.\n\n\"어머니께 편지를 써야겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그래도 괜찮죠?\"\n\n\"물론이오, 사랑하는 아이. 당신은 내게 아이 같소, 테스. 이런 때에 어머니께 편지 쓰는 게 얼마나 마땅한 일인지, 또 내가 그걸 반대한다면 얼마나 잘못된 일인지조차 모를 만큼 말이오. 어머니는 어디 사시오?\"\n\n\"같은 곳에요—말럿. 블랙무어 골짜기 저편이요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 나는 이번 여름 전에도 당신을 본 적이 있는 거요—\"\n\n\"네. 그 풀밭 무도회에서요. 그런데 당신은 저와 춤춰 주지 않았죠. 오, 그게 지금 우리에게 무슨 불길한 징조가 아니었으면 좋겠어요!\"\n\n# XXXI\n\n테스는 바로 다음 날 어머니에게 더없이 절절하고 다급한 편지를 써 보냈고, 그 주가 끝나 갈 무렵 조앤 더비필드의 떠돌이 같은 지난 세기풍 필체로 적힌 답장이 그녀의 사연에 응답해 왔다.\n\n사랑하는 테스,\n\n이 몇 줄을 적으니 네가 잘 지내고 있기를 바란다. 나는 지금 잘 있다, 하느님 덕분이다. 사랑하는 테스, 네가 정말로 곧 결혼하게 됐다는 말을 들으니 우리 모두 기쁘다. 그런데 네 물음에 대해서는, 테스, 우리끼리만, 아주 은밀히, 그러나 아주 강하게 말하건대, 무슨 일이 있어도 그에게 네 지난 곤경에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 마라. 나도 네 아버지에게 다 말하지 않았다. 그이가 자기 체면을 어찌나 떠받드는지, 어쩌면 네 약혼자도 그 모양일지 모른다. 많은 여자가—이 나라에서 가장 높은 분들 가운데도 더러—한때 곤경을 겪었다. 그런데 남들도 제 일을 나발 불지 않는데 어째서 네가 네 일을 나발 불어야 하느냐? 그렇게 멍청한 계집은 없을 게다. 더구나 이미 그토록 오래전 일이고, 네 잘못도 전혀 아닌데 말이다. 네가 쉰 번을 물어도 나는 똑같이 대답하겠다. 게다가, 명심해라. 네가 마음에 있는 걸 다 털어놓는 어린애 같은 천성이라는 걸 알기에—어찌나 단순한지!—나는 네 앞날을 마음에 두고서, 절대로 말로든 행동으로든 그걸 새어 나가게 하지 않겠다고 네게 약속을 받았다. 그리고 너는 이 문을 나서며 더없이 엄숙하게 그러겠다고 약속했다. 나는 네 아버지에게 그 일도, 다가올 네 결혼 이야기도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그이가 가는 데마다 떠벌릴 테니까, 가엾고 단순한 사람. 사랑하는 테스, 기운을 내어라. 네가 사는 고장에는 변변한 게 별로 없고, 있어 봐야 묽고 시큼한 것뿐인 줄 아니, 우리가 결혼 선물로 사과술 한 통을 보내려 한다. 그럼 오늘은 이만 줄이고, 네 젊은이에게도 안부 전한다.—사랑을 담아, 네 어미가,\n\n제이 더비필드\n\n\"오 어머니, 어머니!\" 테스가 중얼거렸다.\n\n가장 짓누르는 사건들조차 더비필드 부인의 탄력 있는 마음에는 얼마나 가벼이 와닿는지를 그녀는 깨닫고 있었다. 어머니는 테스가 보는 대로 삶을 보지 않았다. 지난날의 그 잊히지 않는 사건도 어머니에게는 한낱 스쳐 가는 사고에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 따라야 할 길에 관해서는, 그 이유야 어떻든, 어쩌면 어머니가 옳은지도 몰랐다. 겉으로 보기에 침묵이야말로 자기가 떠받드는 그이의 행복을 위해 가장 나은 길 같았다. 침묵하기로 하자.\n\n이렇게, 세상에서 그녀의 행동을 좌우할 권리의 그림자나마 지닌 유일한 사람의 명령으로 마음을 다잡자, 테스는 한결 차분해졌다. 책임이 옮겨졌고, 그녀의 마음은 몇 주 만에 가장 가벼웠다. 그녀가 승낙한 뒤로 이어진, 시월부터 시작된 저무는 가을날들은, 그녀가 평생 어느 때보다 황홀경에 더 가까운 영적 높이에서 살아간 한철을 이루었다.\n\n클레어를 향한 그녀의 사랑에는 흙냄새라고는 거의 한 점도 없었다. 그녀의 숭고한 믿음에 그는 선함이 다다를 수 있는 모든 것이었다—인도자이자 철학자이자 벗이 알아야 할 모든 것을 아는 사람이었다. 그녀는 그의 몸매를 이루는 윤곽의 선 하나하나가 남성미의 완성이요, 그의 영혼은 성인의 영혼이며, 그의 지성은 선지자의 지성이라고 여겼다. 그를 향한 사랑의 슬기로움이, 사랑으로서, 그녀의 품위를 떠받쳐 주었다. 그녀는 마치 왕관을 쓴 듯했다. 그녀가 본 대로, 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑의 연민은 그녀로 하여금 헌신하는 마음으로 그에게 마음을 들어 올리게 했다. 그는 이따금, 깊은 바닥이라곤 없는 그녀의 커다랗고 경배하는 눈이 그 심연에서 자기를 바라보는 것을 마주치곤 했다. 마치 그녀가 눈앞에 무언가 불멸하는 것을 보고 있기라도 한 듯이.\n\n그녀는 과거를 떨쳐 버렸다—그것을 짓밟아 꺼 버렸다. 마치 연기를 내며 위험하게 타들어 가는 숯덩이를 밟아 끄듯이.\n\n그녀는 남자들이 여자를 향한 사랑에서 그처럼 사심 없고, 의협심 있고, 지켜 줄 줄 아는 존재일 수 있다는 것을 미처 몰랐었다. 이 점에서 에인절 클레어는 그녀가 생각하는 그런 사람과는 한참, 정말이지 터무니없이 멀었다. 그러나 그는 진실로, 동물적이라기보다 영적인 사람이었다. 자기 자신을 잘 다스렸고, 보기 드물게 천박함에서 자유로웠다. 차가운 천성은 아니었으나, 뜨겁다기보다 밝은 쪽이었다—바이런풍이라기보다 셸리풍이었다. 필사적으로 사랑할 수는 있었으되, 그 사랑은 유난히 상상적이고 영묘한 쪽으로 기울었다. 사랑하는 이를 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 자기 자신으로부터 시샘하듯 지켜 낼 수 있는, 까다로운 감정이었다. 이것이 테스를 놀라게 하고 황홀하게 했다. 지금껏 그녀의 짧은 경험이 너무도 불행했던 까닭이었다. 남성에 대한 분노에서 되돌아오는 반동으로, 그녀는 클레어를 지나치게 떠받드는 쪽으로 휩쓸려 갔다.\n\n두 사람은 꾸밈없이 서로의 곁을 찾았다. 그녀는 정직한 믿음으로, 그와 함께 있고 싶다는 바람을 감추지 않았다. 이 문제에 관한 그녀의 본능을 죄다 한데 모아 분명히 말하자면 이런 것이었다. 사랑을 고백한 마당에, 뭇 남자를 끌어당기는 여자라는 성의 그 종잡을 수 없는 속성이, 그토록 완벽한 남자에게는 도리어 거슬릴지도 모른다는 것. 왜냐하면 그것은 그 본성상 어쩔 수 없이 술수의 낌새를 띠고 있을 테니까.\n\n약혼 기간에 바깥에서 거리낌 없이 동무로 지내는 그 시골 풍습이 그녀가 아는 유일한 풍습이었고, 그녀에게는 그것이 조금도 이상하지 않았다. 다만 클레어에게는 그것이 묘하게 앞서 나가는 것처럼 보였는데, 그녀가 다른 모든 낙농장 사람과 마찬가지로 그것을 얼마나 당연한 일로 여기는지를 보고 나서야 그렇지 않게 되었다. 그리하여, 이 시월의 경이로운 오후들 동안 그들은 졸졸 흐르는 지류 개울가를 따라가는 좁은 오솔길을 따라 목초지를 거닐었고, 작은 나무다리를 폴짝 건너 건너편으로 갔다가 다시 돌아오곤 했다. 그들은 졸졸거리는 어느 둑의 소리에서 벗어나는 일이 없었으니, 그 잔잔한 울림이 두 사람의 도란거림에 동무가 되어 주었다. 한편 햇살은, 목초지 그 자체만큼이나 수평으로 누워, 풍경 위로 빛의 꽃가루를 흩뿌렸다. 그들은 나무와 산울타리의 그늘 속에 자그마한 푸른 안개가 어린 것을 보았다. 다른 데서는 여태 환한 햇살이 비치는 동안에도 말이다. 해가 어찌나 땅에 가깝고 풀밭이 어찌나 평평한지, 클레어와 테스의 그림자는 그들 앞으로 사분의 일 마일이나 길게 뻗어, 마치 두 개의 긴 손가락이 멀리, 푸른 충적 평지가 골짜기의 비탈진 옆구리에 맞닿는 곳을 가리키는 것 같았다.\n\n여기저기서 사람들이 일하고 있었다—목초지를 \"손보거나\", 겨울 관개를 위해 작은 물길을 파서 트고, 소에게 밟혀 무너진 둑을 손보는 철이었기 때문이다. 강이 골짜기 전체만큼 넓었던 시절에 그 강이 실어다 놓은, 흑옥처럼 새까만 양토 삽질거리는, 흙의 정수였다. 지난날의 들판을 빻고, 우려내고, 정제하고, 갈고닦아 비할 데 없는 기름짐에 이르게 한 것이었으며, 거기서 목초지의, 그리고 거기서 풀을 뜯는 가축의 그 모든 비옥함이 비롯되었다.\n\n클레어는 이 물일꾼들이 빤히 보는 앞에서도 대담하게 그녀의 허리를 감싼 채로 있었다. 마치 사람들 앞에서 시시덕거리는 데 익숙한 남자 같은 태도였으나, 사실은 입을 벌리고 일꾼들을 곁눈질하며 줄곧 경계하는 짐승 같은 표정을 짓고 있는 그녀만큼이나 수줍었다.\n\n\"당신은 저들 앞에서 저를 당신 사람이라고 인정하는 걸 부끄러워하지 않으시네요!\" 그녀가 기뻐하며 말했다.\n\n\"오 그렇소!\"\n\"하지만 당신이 저처럼 우유 짜는 처녀와 이렇게 거닐고 다닌다는 게 에민스터에 있는 당신 친구분들 귀에 들어가기라도 하면—\"\n\n\"여태껏 본 가장 매혹적인 우유 짜는 처녀지.\"\n\n\"그분들은 그게 자기네 체면에 상처라고 느낄지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 사람—더버빌 가가 클레어 가의 체면을 상하게 한다니! 그건 멋진 패요—당신이 그런 가문에 속한다는 것 말이오. 나는 우리가 결혼하고, 당신이 트링엄 목사에게서 나온 그 가계의 증거를 갖추게 됐을 때 멋지게 써먹으려고 그걸 아껴 두고 있다오. 그건 그렇다 치고, 내 앞날은 우리 집안과는 완전히 동떨어지게 될 거요—그들의 삶은 표면조차 건드리지 못할 거요. 우리는 영국의 이 지역을 떠날 거요—어쩌면 영국 자체를—그러니 여기 사람들이 우리를 어떻게 보든 그게 무슨 상관이겠소? 당신도 떠나는 게 좋겠지, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n그와 함께, 그의 막역한 벗으로서 세상을 헤쳐 나간다는 생각에 그녀 안에서 솟아오른 감정이 너무도 큰 나머지, 그녀는 그저 그렇다는 짤막한 긍정 외에는 더 대답할 수가 없었다. 그녀의 감정은 파도가 와글거리듯 그녀의 귀를 거의 가득 메우며 눈가로 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그녀는 자기 손을 그의 손에 얹었고, 그렇게 두 사람은 나아갔다. 다리 아래 강에서 비친 해가, 녹은 쇳물 같은 금속성 빛으로 두 사람의 눈을 부시게 하며 솟아오르는 곳까지. 정작 해 자체는 다리에 가려 보이지 않았건만. 두 사람이 가만히 멈춰 서자, 매끈한 물 표면에서 털 난 머리며 깃 난 머리가 빠끔 솟아올랐다. 그러나 그 거슬리는 존재들이 지나가지 않고 멈춰 서 있다는 걸 알아채자, 그것들은 다시 사라졌다. 이 강가에서 두 사람은 안개가 그들 둘레로 자욱이 내려앉기 시작할 때까지 머뭇거렸다—이 무렵이면 저녁 일찍 그러했다—안개는 그녀의 속눈썹에 내려앉아 수정처럼 거기 머물렀고, 그의 눈썹과 머리카락에도 내려앉았다.\n\n일요일이면 두 사람은 아주 캄캄해진 뒤 더 늦게까지 걸었다. 그들이 약혼한 뒤 첫 일요일 저녁에 역시 밖에 나와 있던 몇몇 낙농장 사람들은, 비록 주고받는 말까지 들리기엔 너무 멀리 있었으나, 황홀경에 부서진 그녀의 충동적인 말소리를 들었다. 그녀가 그의 팔에 기대어 걸으며, 가슴의 두근거림에 음절이 끊겨 발작하듯 걸리는 그녀의 말투며, 만족스러운 멈춤이며, 그녀의 영혼이 거기 올라탄 듯한 이따금의 가벼운 웃음을—자기가 사랑하고 다른 모든 여자에게서 쟁취해 낸 남자와 함께 있는 여자의 그 웃음, 자연의 그 무엇과도 같지 않은 웃음을 알아차렸다. 그들은 마치 아직 완전히 내려앉지 않은 새가 스치듯 나는 것 같은, 그녀의 발걸음의 가벼움을 눈여겨보았다.\n\n이제 그를 향한 애정은 테스라는 존재의 숨이요 생명이었다. 그것은 광구처럼 그녀를 에워쌌고, 그녀를 환히 비추어 지난날의 슬픔을 잊게 했으며, 끈질기게 그녀를 건드리려 드는 음울한 망령들을—의심, 두려움, 침울, 근심, 수치를—물리쳐 주었다. 그녀는 그것들이 그 에워싸는 빛의 바깥에서 늑대처럼 기다리고 있음을 알았으나, 한참씩 그것들을 굶주린 채 거기 굴복시켜 둘 힘이 있었다.\n\n영적인 망각이 지적인 기억과 나란히 공존했다. 그녀는 환한 빛 속을 걸었으나, 그 뒤편에 저 어둠의 형상들이 늘 펼쳐져 있음을 알았다. 그것들이 물러가고 있는지, 다가오고 있는지, 둘 중 하나로 날마다 조금씩 움직이고 있었다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 테스와 클레어는 집에 남은 다른 식구가 죄다 나가 있어 어쩔 수 없이 안에서 집을 지키며 앉아 있게 되었다. 이야기를 나누다가 그녀가 생각에 잠긴 채 그를 올려다보았고, 그를 흐뭇하게 바라보는 그의 두 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"저는 당신에게 어울리는 사람이 아니에요—아니에요, 아니라고요!\" 그녀가, 그의 떠받듦과 그로 인한 자기 자신의 그 벅찬 기쁨에 질겁한 듯, 낮은 걸상에서 벌떡 일어서며 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n클레어는 그녀의 흥분이 죄다 그 일에서 비롯되었다고 여겼는데, 그것은 그저 그 흥분의 작은 부분일 뿐이었다. 그가 말했다—\n\n\"그런 식으로 말하는 건 못 봐주겠소, 사랑하는 테스! 사람의 됨됨이는 경멸할 만한 한 무더기 인습을 손쉽게 부려 쓰는 데 있는 게 아니라, 참되고, 정직하고, 의롭고, 순결하고, 사랑스럽고, 평판 좋은 이들 가운데 헤아려지는 데 있는 거요—바로 당신처럼 말이오, 내 테스.\"\n\n그녀는 목구멍으로 치미는 흐느낌과 싸웠다. 그 미덕들의 나열이 요 몇 해 교회에서 얼마나 자주 그녀의 어린 가슴을 아프게 했던가. 그리고 하필 지금 그가 그것을 들먹이다니 얼마나 묘한 일인가.\n\n\"제가—열여섯 살이었을 때, 어린 동생들과 함께 살고, 당신이 풀밭에서 춤추던 그때, 왜 머물러서 저를 사랑해 주지 않았어요? 오, 왜 그러지 않았어요, 왜 그러지 않았어요!\" 그녀가 두 손을 격하게 맞잡으며 말했다.\n\n에인절은 그녀를 달래고 안심시키기 시작했다. 그러면서 속으로, 정말이지 그녀가 얼마나 변덕스러운 사람인지, 그리고 그녀가 행복을 오롯이 자기에게 의지하게 되면 그녀를 얼마나 조심스레 대해야 할지를 생각했다.\n\n\"아—내가 왜 머물지 않았던고!\" 그가 말했다. \"바로 그게 내가 느끼는 바요. 그때 내가 알기만 했더라면! 하지만 그렇게 쓰라리게 후회할 건 없소—왜 그래야 하오?\"\n\n감추려는 여자의 본능으로 그녀는 황급히 말머리를 돌렸다—\n\n\"저는 지금 가질 수 있는 것보다 사 년 더 당신의 마음을 가질 수 있었을 텐데요. 그랬다면 저는 지금처럼 시간을 허비하지 않았을 거고요—그만큼 훨씬 더 오래 행복했을 텐데요!\"\n\n이렇게 괴로워하는 이는, 뒤에 길고 어두운 음모의 행렬을 거느린 다 자란 여자가 아니라, 단순한 삶을 살아온, 아직 스물한 살도 안 된 처녀였다. 미숙하던 시절에 올가미에 걸린 새처럼 붙들린 처녀였다. 마음을 더 온전히 가라앉히려고 그녀는 작은 걸상에서 일어나 방을 나갔는데, 나가는 길에 치맛자락으로 걸상을 넘어뜨렸다.\n\n그는 장작 받침쇠에 걸쳐 놓은 푸른 물푸레나무 다발에서 솟는 명랑한 불빛 곁에 계속 앉아 있었다. 나뭇가지들이 기분 좋게 탁탁 튀며, 끝에서 수액 거품을 쉭쉭 뿜어냈다. 그녀가 돌아왔을 때는 다시 평소의 그녀로 돌아와 있었다.\n\n\"당신은 좀 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없는 데가 있다고 생각하지 않소, 테스?\" 그가, 걸상에 방석을 펴 주고 그녀 곁 긴 의자에 앉으며, 너그러이 말했다. \"당신한테 뭘 좀 물어보려던 참이었는데, 바로 그때 당신이 달아나 버렸잖소.\"\n\n\"네, 어쩌면 제가 변덕스러운지도 몰라요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. 그녀는 불쑥 그에게 다가가더니 그의 두 팔에 각각 한 손씩 얹었다. \"아니에요, 에인절, 저는 정말로 그렇진 않아요—천성이 그렇진 않다는 말이에요!\" 자기가 그렇지 않다는 걸 더더욱 확실히 보여 주려고, 그녀는 긴 의자에서 그에게 바싹 붙어 앉아, 머리를 클레어의 어깨에 기대어 쉬게 했다. \"뭘 물어보려고 하셨어요—분명히 제가 대답할게요.\" 그녀가 다소곳이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"음, 당신은 나를 사랑하고, 나와 결혼하기로 동의했소. 그러니 거기서 셋째가 따라 나오지. '그날을 언제로 할까?'\"\n\n\"저는 이렇게 사는 게 좋아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 새해부터, 아니면 조금 더 늦게라도, 내 힘으로 사업을 시작할 생각을 해야 하오. 그리고 내 새 처지의 갖가지 잡다한 일에 얽혀 들기 전에, 내 동반자부터 확실히 해 두고 싶소.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"아주 현실적으로 말하자면, 그게 다 끝날 때까지는 결혼하지 않는 편이 가장 낫지 않을까요?—당신이 떠나서 저를 여기 두고 가신다는 생각은 견딜 수가 없지만요!\"\n\n\"물론 당신은 견딜 수 없지—그리고 이 경우엔 그게 가장 나은 길도 아니오. 나는 여러모로 내 출발을 도와줄 당신이 필요하오. 언제로 할까? 지금부터 보름 뒤는 어떻소?\"\n\n\"안 돼요.\" 그녀가 정색하며 말했다. \"먼저 생각해야 할 게 너무 많아요.\"\n\"하지만—\"\n\n그가 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 부드럽게 더 끌어당겼다.\n\n결혼이라는 현실은 그토록 가까이 다가서자 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것이었다. 그 문제를 두고 더 이야기가 진척되기도 전에, 긴 의자 모퉁이를 돌아 방 안의 환한 불빛 속으로 크릭 주인과 크릭 부인, 그리고 우유 짜는 처녀 둘이 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n테스는 탄력 있는 공처럼 그의 곁에서 튕겨져 일어섰고, 그녀의 얼굴은 불빛 속에서 붉어지고 눈은 반짝였다.\n\n\"이렇게 그분 곁에 바싹 앉아 있으면 어찌 될지 알고 있었다니까요!\" 그녀가 분해하며 외쳤다. \"다들 분명히 와서 우릴 들킬 거라고 속으로 그랬는데! 하지만 저는 정말로 그분 무릎에 앉아 있었던 게 아니에요, 거의 그래 보였을지는 몰라도요!\"\n\n\"음—굳이 말 안 했더라면, 이 불빛으로는 당신이 대체 어디 앉아 있었는지 우린 영 알아채지도 못했을 거요.\" 주인이 대꾸했다. 그러고는 결혼에 얽힌 감정이라곤 도무지 모르는 사내의 무덤덤한 낯빛으로 아내에게 말을 이었다—\"자, 크리스티애너, 이게 다 사람이란 게 멀쩡한 남들을 두고 괜히 무슨 생각을 하겠거니 짐작해선 안 된다는 본보기지. 오 그렇고말고, 저 애가 말만 안 했더라면 어디 앉아 있었는지 한마디도 생각조차 안 했을 거야—나는 아니지.\"\n\n\"우리는 곧 결혼할 겁니다.\" 클레어가 짐짓 태연한 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"아—그러신다고요! 음, 정말 반가운 소식입니다, 나리. 한동안 그러실 거라고 생각하고 있었습죠. 저 애는 우유 짜는 처녀로 두기엔 너무 아까운 애예요—제가 처음 본 그날부터 그리 말했습죠—누구한테든 큰 상이고요. 게다가 신사 농장주의 아내로는 더할 나위 없는 여자예요. 저 애를 곁에 두면 나리께선 농장지기 손아귀에 휘둘리실 일이 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n어느새 테스는 사라지고 없었다. 그녀는 크릭의 무뚝뚝한 칭찬에 무안했다기보다, 크릭을 뒤따라 들어온 처녀들의 표정에 더 충격을 받았던 것이다. 저녁을 먹은 뒤 그녀가 침실에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 다 거기 있었다. 등불 하나가 켜져 있었고, 처녀들마다 하얗게 침대에서 몸을 일으킨 채 테스를 기다리고 있었다. 그 모습이 마치 복수하러 온 유령들이 한 줄로 늘어선 것 같았다.\n\n그러나 그들의 기분에 악의라곤 없다는 것을 그녀는 곧 알아차렸다. 그들은 애초에 가져 보리라 기대조차 하지 않았던 것을 잃었다고 느낄 수는 없었던 것이다. 그들의 상태는 객관적이고 관조적이었다.\n\n\"저이가 저 애랑 결혼한대!\" 레티가 테스에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 중얼거렸다. \"얼굴에 다 쓰여 있는걸!\"\n\n\"너 정말 그분이랑 결혼하는 거야?\" 매리언이 물었다.\n\n\"응.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n\"언제?\"\n\n\"언젠가.\"\n\n그들은 이것이 그저 둘러대는 말이라고 여겼다.\n\n\"그래—그분이랑 결혼한대—신사분이랑!\" 이즈 휴엣이 거듭 말했다.\n\n그리고 일종의 홀린 듯한 마음에 세 처녀는 하나씩 차례로 침대에서 기어 나와, 다가와서 맨발로 테스를 둘러싸고 섰다. 레티는 그런 기적이 일어난 뒤 친구의 몸뚱이가 진짜인지 확인이라도 하려는 듯 테스의 어깨에 두 손을 얹었고, 나머지 둘은 그녀의 허리에 팔을 둘렀다. 모두가 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"어쩜 이럴 수가! 내가 도무지 생각할 수 있는 것 이상이야!\" 이즈 휴엣이 말했다.\n\n매리언이 테스에게 입을 맞추었다. \"그래.\" 그녀가 입술을 떼며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그건 쟤를 사랑해서야, 아니면 이젠 다른 입술이 거기 닿았으니까야?\" 이즈가 매리언에게 시큰둥하게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"난 그런 생각 한 거 아냐.\" 매리언이 순순히 말했다. \"난 그저 이게 다 너무 야릇해서—쟤가 그분 아내가 되고, 다른 누구도 아니라는 게. 난 거기에 토 달지 않아, 우리 둘 다 그래, 우린 그걸 바라지도 않았으니까—그냥 그분을 사랑했을 뿐이지. 그래도, 세상 어떤 여자도 그분이랑 결혼하지 못할 거야—어떤 고운 귀부인도, 비단이며 공단을 두른 누구도 아니고, 우리처럼 사는 쟤가 하는 거니까.\"\n\n\"너희 정말로 이 일로 나를 미워하지 않는 거 맞아?\" 테스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n그들은 대답하기 전에 하얀 잠옷 차림으로 그녀 주위를 맴돌았다. 마치 그 대답이 그녀의 표정 속에 들어 있기라도 한 듯이.\n\n\"모르겠어—모르겠어.\" 레티 프리들이 중얼거렸다. \"널 미워하고 싶은데, 그게 안 돼!\"\n\n\"나도 그래.\" 이즈와 매리언이 맞장구쳤다. \"쟤를 미워할 수가 없어. 어쩐지 쟤가 나를 막아.\"\n\n\"그분은 너희 중 누군가와 결혼했어야 해.\" 테스가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"너희가 다 나보다 나으니까.\"\n\n\"우리가 너보다 낫다고?\" 처녀들이 낮고 느릿한 속삭임으로 말했다. \"아니야, 아니야, 사랑하는 테스!\"\n\n\"너희가 그래!\" 그녀가 격하게 반박했다. 그러고는 갑자기 매달린 그들의 팔을 뿌리치며 발작하듯 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 서랍장에 몸을 기댄 채 끊임없이 되뇌면서. \"오 그래, 그래, 그래!\"\n\n한번 무너지자 그녀는 울음을 멈출 수 없었다.\n\n\"그분은 너희 중 하나를 얻었어야 했어!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"지금이라도 내가 그렇게 만들어야 한다는 생각이 들어! 너희가 나보다 그분에게 나을 거야—내가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠어! 오! 오!\"\n\n그들이 그녀에게 다가가 끌어안았으나, 여전히 흐느낌이 그녀를 찢어 놓았다.\n\n\"물 좀 가져와.\" 매리언이 말했다. \"우리 때문에 마음이 상한 거야, 가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!\"\n\n그들은 그녀를 부드럽게 침대 곁으로 도로 데려가, 거기서 따뜻하게 입 맞추었다.\n\n\"네가 그분한테 가장 어울려.\" 매리언이 말했다. \"더 귀부인 같고, 우리보다 더 많이 배웠지, 특히 그분이 너한테 그토록 많은 걸 가르쳐 준 뒤로는. 하지만 너라도 자랑스러워해야 해. 너 자랑스럽지, 분명히!\"\n\n\"응, 자랑스러워.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 이렇게 무너져 버려서 부끄러워.\"\n\n다들 침대에 누워 불이 꺼졌을 때, 매리언이 그녀 쪽으로 속삭여 건넸다—\n\n\"네가 그분 아내가 되면 우릴 생각해 줘, 테스. 우리가 너한테 그분을 사랑했다고 말한 것도, 너를 미워하지 않으려 애썼고, 미워하지 않았고, 미워할 수도 없었다는 것도. 네가 그분의 선택이었고, 우린 그분에게 뽑히길 바란 적도 없었으니까.\"\n\n이 말에, 짜고 따가운 눈물이 다시 테스의 베개 위로 흘러내렸다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 터질 듯한 가슴으로, 어머니의 명령에도 불구하고 에인절 클레어에게 자기 내력을 죄다 털어놓기로 마음먹었다는 것을 그들은 알지 못했다—자기가 그를 위해 살고 숨 쉬는 그 사람으로 하여금, 원한다면 자기를 경멸하게 두고, 어머니로 하여금 자기를 바보로 여기게 두는 한이 있더라도, 그에 대한 배신으로 여겨질지 모를, 또 어쩐지 이 처녀들에게 잘못을 저지르는 것만 같은 침묵을 지키느니 그러는 편이 낫다고.\n\n# XXXII\n\n이 뉘우치는 마음 때문에 그녀는 결혼 날짜를 입에 올리지 못했다. 십일월이 시작될 때까지도 그 날짜는 여전히 미루어진 채였다. 그가 가장 마음 동할 만한 순간들에 물었는데도 그러했다. 그러나 테스의 바람은, 모든 것이 그때 그대로 머물러 있는 영원한 약혼 상태를 향한 것 같았다.\n\n이제 목초지는 변해 가고 있었다. 그러나 젖 짜기 전 이른 오후에는 거기서 한동안 빈둥거릴 만큼 여전히 따뜻했고, 이 무렵 낙농 일의 형편도 빈둥거릴 여유 시간을 한 시간쯤은 허락해 주었다. 해 쪽으로 축축한 잔디밭을 건너다보면, 광원체 아래로 반짝이는 잔물결 같은 거미줄이 그들의 눈에 보였다. 마치 바다 위 달빛이 지나간 자취 같았다. 자신들의 그 짧은 영광을 까맣게 모르는 각다귀들이 이 길의 어른거리는 빛을 가로질러 거닐다가, 마치 몸속에 불을 품기라도 한 듯 환히 빛나더니, 이내 그 빛줄기 밖으로 벗어나 아주 꺼져 버렸다. 이런 것들을 앞에 두고 그는 날짜가 여전히 미결의 문제라는 것을 그녀에게 일깨우곤 했다.\n\n아니면 밤에, 크릭 부인이 그에게 기회를 주려고 꾸며 낸 어떤 심부름에 그가 그녀를 따라나섰을 때 묻기도 했다. 그것은 대개 골짜기 위쪽 비탈에 있는 농가까지 가서, 따로 떼어 놓은 짚 외양간에서 새끼 밸 때가 다 된 소들이 어떻게 지내는지 알아보는 일이었다. 한 해 가운데 소들의 세계에 큰 변화가 닥치는 철이었기 때문이다. 짐승들이 날마다 무리 지어 이 산실 같은 곳으로 보내졌고, 거기서 새끼를 낳을 때까지 짚을 먹으며 지냈다. 그 일이 있은 뒤, 송아지가 걸을 수 있게 되자마자, 어미와 새끼는 낙농장으로 도로 몰려왔다. 송아지가 팔리기 전까지의 그 사이 기간에는 물론 짤 젖이 거의 없었지만, 송아지가 떼어지자마자 우유 짜는 처녀들은 다시 평소처럼 일에 매달려야 했다.\n\n이 어두운 산책 가운데 하나에서 돌아오던 길에 두 사람은 평지 바로 위로 솟은 커다란 자갈 벼랑에 이르렀고, 거기 가만히 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다. 이제 개울마다 물이 불어 둑으로 콸콸 솟구치고 암거 밑에서 졸졸 울렸다. 자그마한 도랑까지 죄다 가득 차서, 어디로도 지름길을 낼 수 없었고, 걷는 이들은 늘 다니는 길을 따라갈 수밖에 없었다. 보이지 않는 골짜기 전역에서 무수히 뒤섞인 울림이 밀려왔다. 그것은 그들의 상상에, 발아래 거대한 도시가 펼쳐져 있고 그 웅성거림이 도시 백성의 아우성이라는 느낌을 들이밀었다.\n\n\"꼭 수만 명 같아요.\" 테스가 말했다. \"장터에서 군중집회를 열어, 입씨름하고, 설교하고, 다투고, 흐느끼고, 신음하고, 기도하고, 저주하는 거예요.\"\n\n클레어는 딱히 귀 기울이고 있지 않았다.\n\n\"오늘 크릭이 당신한테 말했소, 여보? 겨울 동안엔 일손이 많이 필요하지 않다는 거 말이오.\"\n\n\"아뇨.\"\n\n\"소들이 빠르게 젖이 마르고 있소.\"\n\n\"네. 어제 여섯인가 일곱 마리가 짚 외양간으로 갔고, 그저께는 세 마리가 가서, 이미 스무 마리 가까이 짚 외양간에 있어요. 아—그게, 송아지 받는 데에 농장주가 제 도움을 원치 않는다는 건가요? 오, 저는 여기서 더는 필요 없는 거네요! 저는 그토록 애썼는데—\"\n\n\"크릭이 더는 당신이 필요 없다고 딱 잘라 말한 건 아니오. 다만, 우리 사이가 어떤지 알고 있으니, 더없이 호의적이고 정중한 태도로 이렇게 말하더군. 내가 성탄절에 떠날 때 당신도 데려갈 줄로 알고 있다고. 그래서 내가 당신 없이 어쩔 셈이냐고 물었더니, 그저 사실 이맘때면 여자 일손은 아주 조금만 있어도 된다고만 하더군. 그가 이런 식으로 당신을 몰아붙여 주는 게 도리어 좀 반가웠으니, 나도 어지간히 죄인인 셈이오.\"\n\n\"반가워하실 일이 아니었던 것 같은데요, 에인절. 필요 없다는 건, 비록 그게 동시에 편리한 일이라도, 언제나 서글픈 거니까요.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 편리하지—당신도 그건 인정했잖소.\" 그가 그녀의 뺨에 손가락을 댔다. \"아!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"들킨 게 부끄러워서 그녀의 뺨에 붉은빛이 올라오는 게 느껴지는걸! 하지만 내가 왜 이렇게 시시덕거리지! 우린 시시덕거리지 맙시다—삶은 너무 진지하니까.\"\n\n\"그래요. 어쩌면 당신보다 제가 그걸 먼저 봤는지도 몰라요.\"\n\n그녀는 그때 그것을 보고 있었다. 어젯밤의 그 감정에 따라—결국 그와 결혼하기를 거절하고 낙농장을 떠난다는 것은, 낙농장이 아닌 어떤 낯선 곳으로 간다는 뜻이었다. 송아지 받는 철이 다가오는 지금 우유 짜는 처녀는 찾는 데가 없었으니까. 에인절 클레어 같은 신과 같은 존재라곤 없는 어떤 경작 농장으로 간다는 뜻이었다. 그녀는 그 생각이 싫었고, 집으로 돌아간다는 생각은 더더욱 싫었다.\n\n\"그러니 진지하게 말해서, 가장 사랑하는 테스,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"당신이 아마 성탄절에 떠나야 할 테니, 그때 내가 당신을 내 것으로 데려가는 게 여러모로 바람직하고 편리한 일이오. 게다가, 당신이 세상에서 가장 셈할 줄 모르는 처녀가 아니라면, 우리가 이렇게 언제까지나 지낼 수는 없다는 걸 알 거요.\"\n\n\"저는 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 늘 여름이고 가을이고, 당신은 늘 제게 구애하고, 늘 지난 여름내 그래 주셨던 만큼 저를 많이 생각해 주신다면!\"\n\n\"나는 늘 그럴 거요.\"\n\n\"오, 당신이 그러시리란 걸 알아요!\" 그녀가 그를 향한 믿음에 갑자기 불타올라 외쳤다. \"에인절, 제가 영원히 당신 것이 될 그날을 정할게요!\"\n\n그리하여 마침내 그 집으로 돌아오는 어두운 산책길에, 좌우의 무수한 물의 목소리들 가운데서, 두 사람 사이에 그 일이 정해졌다.\n\n낙농장에 다다르자 크릭 부부에게 곧바로 알렸다—비밀로 해 달라는 당부와 함께. 두 연인은 저마다 결혼을 되도록 조용히 치르고 싶어 했기 때문이다. 주인은, 곧 그녀를 내보낼 생각을 하고 있었으면서도, 이제는 그녀를 잃는다고 야단스레 걱정했다. 거품 걷는 일은 어쩐단 말인가? 앵글베리와 샌드본의 귀부인들에게 보낼 그 장식 버터 덩이는 누가 만든단 말인가? 크릭 부인은 그 우유부단함이 마침내 끝장난 것을 테스에게 축하해 주며, 테스를 처음 본 순간 그녀가 어떤 평범한 바깥일꾼이 아닌 누군가의 점지된 사람이 되리라는 걸 알아봤다고 말했다. 도착하던 그날 오후 외양간 마당을 가로질러 걸어오던 테스가 어찌나 기품 있어 보이던지, 좋은 집안 출신임을 능히 장담할 수 있었다는 것이다. 사실 크릭 부인은 테스가 다가올 때 우아하고 잘생겼다고 생각했던 것을 기억하긴 했지만, 그 기품이란 나중에 알게 된 사실이 거든 상상의 산물이었을지도 몰랐다.\n\n이제 테스는 의지의 감각도 없이 시간의 날개에 실려 떠밀려 갔다. 말은 떨어졌고, 그날의 날짜는 적혔다. 본디 밝던 그녀의 지성도, 들에서 일하는 사람들과, 동료 인간보다 자연현상과 더 폭넓게 어울리는 이들에게 흔한 그 운명론적 확신을 받아들이기 시작했다. 그리하여 그녀는 그런 마음 상태 특유의, 연인이 권하는 모든 일에 수동적으로 순응하는 그 흐름 속으로 떠밀려 들어갔다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 어머니에게 다시 편지를 썼다. 표면상으로는 결혼 날짜를 알리기 위해서였으나, 실은 다시 한번 조언을 간청하기 위해서였다. 그녀를 택한 사람은 신사였고, 그 점을 어쩌면 어머니가 충분히 헤아리지 못했는지도 몰랐다. 결혼 뒤의 해명이라면, 더 거친 남자라면 가벼운 마음으로 받아들일지 몰라도, 그는 같은 마음으로 받아들이지 않을 수도 있었다. 그러나 이 편지에는 더비필드 부인에게서 아무 답장도 오지 않았다.\n\n당장 결혼해야 할 현실적 필요가 있다고 에인절 클레어가 자기 자신에게도 테스에게도 그럴듯하게 내세웠음에도, 사실 그 발걸음에는 성급한 구석이 있었다. 훗날 가서 그것이 드러난다. 그는 그녀를 끔찍이 사랑했으나, 어쩌면 그를 향한 그녀의 감정의 그 격정적인 철저함에 비하면, 다소 이상적이고 공상적인 사랑이었다. 멋없는 시골 생활에 묶일 운명이라고 여겼을 때만 해도, 그는 이 목가적인 존재에게서 본 그런 매력이 그 무대 뒤편에서 발견되리라고는 생각조차 못 했었다. 순박함이란 입에 올려 말할 거리였으나, 그것이 사람에게 실제로 어떻게 와닿는지는 여기 오기 전까지 몰랐던 것이다. 그러면서도 그는 자기 앞날의 행로를 또렷이 내다보는 것과는 거리가 멀었고, 자기가 인생에서 제대로 출발했다고 여길 수 있기까지는 한두 해가 걸릴지도 몰랐다. 그 비밀은, 자기가 집안의 편견 탓에 참된 운명을 놓치게 됐다는 느낌이 그의 행로와 성격에 입힌 그 무모함의 빛깔에 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 그 중부 지방 농장에 완전히 자리 잡을 때까지 우리가 기다리는 편이 낫지 않을까요?\" 그녀가 한번은 머뭇거리며 물었다. (그때는 중부 지방 농장이라는 생각이었다.)\n\n\"솔직히 말하면, 내 테스, 나는 당신을 내 보호와 따뜻한 마음에서 떨어진 어디에도 두고 싶지 않소.\"\n\n그 이유는, 그것대로는, 그럴듯한 것이었다. 그가 그녀에게 끼친 영향이 어찌나 뚜렷했던지, 그녀는 그의 태도와 습관, 그의 말투와 표현, 그의 좋아하는 것과 싫어하는 것까지 물려받아 있었다. 그러니 그녀를 농지에 남겨 둔다는 것은 그녀를 다시 그와 어긋나는 쪽으로 도로 미끄러지게 두는 일이 될 터였다. 그가 그녀를 자기 보살핌 아래 두고 싶어 한 데에는 또 다른 이유가 있었다. 그의 부모는 당연히, 그가 그녀를 영국이든 식민지든 먼 정착지로 데려가기 전에 적어도 한 번은 그녀를 보고 싶어 했다. 그리고 그들의 어떤 의견도 그의 뜻을 바꾸도록 허용되지 않을 것이었으므로, 그는 유리한 자리를 알아보는 동안 두어 달쯤 셋방에서 그와 함께 사는 것이, 그녀가 견디기 힘든 시련으로 느낄지 모를 일—목사관에서 그의 어머니에게 인사드리는 일—에 어느 정도 사교적 도움이 되리라고 판단했다.\n\n다음으로, 그는 제분소가 돌아가는 모습을 조금 보고 싶었다. 제분소 하나를 곡물 재배와 결합해 쓸 수도 있겠다는 생각이 있었기 때문이다. 웰브리지에 있는 크고 오래된 물방앗간의 주인이—한때 수도원의 방앗간이었던 곳인데—언제든 그가 오고 싶을 때 그 유서 깊은 작업 방식을 둘러보고 며칠간 그 일에 손을 보태게 해 주겠다고 제안한 터였다. 클레어는 이 무렵 어느 날, 몇 마일 떨어진 그곳을 찾아가 자세한 사정을 알아보고, 저녁에 탤보세이스로 돌아왔다. 그녀는 그가 웰브리지 제분소에서 한동안 지내기로 마음먹은 것을 알게 되었다. 그런데 그의 마음을 그렇게 정하게 한 것은 무엇이었던가? 빻고 체질하는 과정을 들여다볼 기회라기보다는, 다름 아니라 바로 그 농가에서—훼손되기 전에는 더버빌 가의 한 갈래의 저택이었던 그 농가에서—셋방을 얻을 수 있다는 우연한 사실이었다. 클레어가 현실적인 문제를 매듭짓는 방식은 늘 이런 식이었다. 그 문제와는 아무 상관도 없는 어떤 정서에 기대어. 두 사람은 도시와 여관을 찾아 여행하는 대신, 결혼한 직후 곧장 그리로 가서 보름간 머물기로 정했다.\n\n\"그런 다음 우리는 내가 들은, 런던 건너편에 있는 농장 몇 군데를 살펴보러 나설 거요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 삼월이나 사월쯤엔 우리 아버지와 어머니를 찾아뵙는 거지.\"\n\n이런 절차에 관한 문제들이 떠올랐다 지나갔고, 그녀가 그의 것이 될 그날, 그 믿기지 않는 날이 가까운 미래에 크게 어른거렸다. 십이월 삼십일일, 섣달그믐이 그 날짜였다. 그의 아내라니, 그녀는 속으로 되뇌었다. 정말 그럴 수 있을까? 두 사람이 함께, 가를 것이라곤 아무것도 없이, 모든 일을 둘이 나누다니, 안 될 게 뭐람? 그런데 그래도 왜?\n\n어느 일요일 아침 이즈 휴엣이 교회에서 돌아와 테스에게 따로 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 네 결혼 공고가 안 났더라.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\"\n\n\"오늘이 첫 번째 공고가 났어야 하는 날이잖아.\" 그녀가 테스를 가만히 바라보며 대답했다. \"섣달그믐에 결혼할 생각이지, 얘?\"\n\n테스는 얼른 그렇다고 답했다.\n\n\"공고는 세 번 나야 해. 그런데 이제 그사이에 일요일이 두 번밖에 안 남았어.\"\n\n테스는 자기 뺨이 핼쑥해지는 것을 느꼈다. 이즈의 말이 맞았다. 당연히 세 번 나야 했다. 어쩌면 그가 잊어버렸나 보다! 그렇다면 일주일을 미뤄야 하는데, 그건 불길했다. 어떻게 연인에게 일깨워 준담? 그토록 머뭇거리기만 하던 그녀가, 갑자기 그 소중한 상을 놓칠까 봐 조바심과 불안에 불타올랐다.\n\n자연스러운 사건 하나가 그녀의 근심을 덜어 주었다. 이즈가 결혼 공고가 빠진 일을 크릭 부인에게 말했고, 크릭 부인은 안주인의 권한이랍시고 그 점에 대해 에인절에게 말했다.\n\n\"그걸 잊으신 거예요, 클레어 씨? 결혼 공고 말이에요.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 잊지 않았습니다.\" 클레어가 말했다.\n\n그는 테스와 단둘이 있게 되자마자 그녀를 안심시켰다.\n\n\"공고 일로 저들이 당신을 놀려도 신경 쓰지 마요. 우리한테는 혼인 허가증이 더 조용할 거요. 그래서 당신과 상의하지 않고 허가증으로 정했소. 그러니 일요일 아침에 교회에 가더라도, 듣고 싶었다 한들, 당신 이름은 못 들을 거요.\"\n\n\"전 듣고 싶지 않았어요, 가장 사랑하는 사람.\" 그녀가 자랑스레 말했다.\n\n그래도 일이 착착 진행되고 있다는 것을 안 것은 테스에게 엄청난 안도였다. 그녀는 누군가가 자기 내력을 이유로 일어나 공고를 막아서지나 않을까 줄곧 적잖이 두려워했던 것이다. 사정이 어찌나 그녀에게 유리하게 돌아가는지!\n\n\"영 마음이 놓이질 않아.\" 그녀가 속으로 말했다. \"이 모든 행운이 나중에 한 무더기 불운으로 다 매질당해 쫓겨날지도 몰라. 하늘이란 으레 그러는 법이니까. 차라리 보통 공고로 했으면 좋았을걸!\"\n\n그러나 모든 것이 매끄럽게 흘러갔다. 그녀는 지금 가진 가장 좋은 흰 드레스를 입고 결혼하기를 그가 바랄지, 아니면 새것을 사야 할지 궁금했다. 그 문제는 그의 앞서 생각해 주는 마음으로 풀렸으니, 그녀 앞으로 부쳐진 커다란 꾸러미 몇 개가 도착하면서 그것이 드러났다. 그 안에서 그녀는 보닛부터 신발까지 옷가지 한 벌을 통째로 찾아냈는데, 두 사람이 계획한 그 소박한 결혼식에 썩 어울릴 만한, 완벽한 아침 예복도 들어 있었다. 그는 그 꾸러미들이 도착한 직후 집에 들어와, 그녀가 위층에서 그것들을 풀고 있는 소리를 들었다.\n\n잠시 뒤 그녀가, 얼굴에는 홍조를, 눈에는 눈물을\n눈을.\n\n\"정말 세심하게도 마음을 써 주셨네요!\" 그의 어깨에 뺨을 기댄 채 그녀가 속삭였다. \"장갑이며 손수건까지! 내 사랑—어쩜 이렇게 다정하고, 이렇게 친절하실까!\"\n\n\"아니야, 아니야, 테스. 그저 런던의 어느 여자 상인에게 주문한 것뿐이야—그뿐이라고.\" 그리고 그녀가 자기를 너무 높이 보지 않도록, 그는 위층으로 올라가서 서두르지 말고 전부 잘 맞는지 살펴보라고, 만약 맞지 않거든 마을 바느질꾼에게 조금 손을 보게 하라고 일렀다.\n\n그녀는 정말로 위층으로 올라가 가운을 입어 보았다. 혼자가 되자 그녀는 잠시 거울 앞에 서서 비단옷이 자아내는 모습을 들여다보았다. 그러자 어머니가 부르던, 신비한 옷에 관한 발라드 한 구절이 머릿속에 떠올랐다—\n\n한 번이라도 그릇된 짓을 저지른 아내에게는 결코 어울리지 않으리라,\n\n더비필드 부인이 어린 그녀에게 그토록 명랑하게, 그토록 짓궂게 불러 주던 노래였다. 발은 요람에 얹은 채, 그 가락에 맞추어 요람을 흔들면서. 만약 이 옷이, 기네비어 왕비의 옷이 왕비를 배신했던 것처럼, 색이 변하면서 자기를 배신해 버린다면. 낙농장에 온 이래로 그녀는 지금까지 단 한 번도 그 구절을 떠올린 적이 없었다.\n\n# XXXIII\n\n엔젤은 결혼식 전에, 낙농장에서 멀리 떨어진 어딘가에서, 그녀와 하루를 보내고 싶었다. 아직 한낱 연인과 애인일 뿐인 동안에 누리는 마지막 나들이로서. 다시는 되풀이되지 않을 상황 속에서 펼쳐지는 낭만적인 하루, 게다가 그보다 더 크고 더 빛나는 또 하나의 날이 바로 코앞에 환히 다가와 있는 그런 하루를. 그래서 그 전 주에 그는 가장 가까운 읍내에 가서 몇 가지를 사 오자고 제안했고, 둘은 함께 길을 나섰다.\n\n낙농장에서의 클레어의 생활은, 자기 계급의 세계에 관한 한 은둔자나 다름없었다. 여러 달 동안 그는 읍내 근처에도 가 본 적이 없었고, 탈것이 필요치 않았으니 한 대도 두지 않았으며, 말을 타거나 몰 일이 있으면 낙농장 주인의 조랑말이나 이륜마차를 빌렸다. 그날 둘은 이륜마차를 타고 갔다.\n\n그러고는 둘이 생전 처음으로 한 살림의 동반자로서 함께 장을 보았다. 크리스마스이브였고, 호랑가시나무와 겨우살이가 가득 실려 있었으며, 그날을 맞아 사방에서 몰려든 낯선 사람들로 읍내는 몹시 붐볐다. 테스는 미모에 더하여 행복까지 얼굴에 얹은 채 돌아다닌 대가를 치렀으니, 그의 팔짱을 끼고 사람들 사이를 지나는 동안 숱한 시선을 받았던 것이다.\n\n저녁이 되자 둘은 묵었던 여관으로 돌아왔고, 엔젤이 말과 이륜마차를 문 앞으로 끌어다 놓도록 보러 간 사이 테스는 입구에서 기다렸다. 공동 거실은 손님들로 가득했고, 그들은 끊임없이 드나들었다. 그 사람들이 지나갈 때마다 문이 열렸다 닫혔다 하면서, 안쪽 응접실의 불빛이 테스의 얼굴을 환히 비추었다. 사내 둘이 다른 사람들 틈에 섞여 나오면서 그녀 곁을 지나갔다. 그중 하나가 놀란 듯 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았는데, 그녀는 그가 트랜트리지 사람이 아닐까 싶었다. 비록 그 마을은 워낙 멀리 떨어져 있어 여기서 트랜트리지 사람을 보기란 드문 일이었지만.\n\n\"참한 처녀로군.\" 다른 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 참하긴 하지. 하지만 내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면—\" 그러더니 그는 그 평가의 나머지 부분을 곧바로 부정해 버렸다.\n\n마구간 마당에서 막 돌아온 클레어는 문턱에서 그 사내와 맞닥뜨리며 그 말을 들었고, 테스가 움츠러드는 것을 보았다. 그녀에게 가해진 모욕에 그는 가슴 깊은 곳까지 찔린 듯했고, 아무것도 미처 생각하기도 전에 주먹에 온 힘을 실어 사내의 턱을 후려쳐, 그를 비틀거리며 복도 안쪽으로 나가떨어지게 했다.\n\n사내는 정신을 차리고 덤벼들 기색을 보였고, 클레어는 문밖으로 한 발 나서며 방어 자세를 취했다. 그러나 상대는 다시 생각을 고쳐먹기 시작했다. 그는 테스 곁을 지나며 다시 한번 그녀를 보더니, 클레어에게 말했다—\n\n\"죄송합니다, 선생님. 완전히 사람을 잘못 봤습니다. 여기서 사십 마일 떨어진 데 사는 다른 여자인 줄 알았지 뭡니까.\"\n\n클레어는 그제야 자기가 너무 성급했고, 게다가 그녀를 여관 복도에 세워 둔 것도 잘못이었다고 느끼고는, 이런 경우에 늘 하던 대로 했다. 사내에게 오 실링을 주어 얻어맞은 자리를 달래라 했고, 그렇게 둘은 서로 화평하게 밤 인사를 건네며 헤어졌다. 클레어가 마부에게서 고삐를 넘겨받고 젊은 부부가 마차를 몰고 떠나자마자, 두 사내는 반대 방향으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"그래, 진짜 잘못 본 거였나?\" 두 번째 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 아니지. 다만 저 신사 양반 기분을 상하게 하고 싶지 않았던 거야—나야 그렇지.\"\n\n그러는 사이 연인들은 마차를 몰아 앞으로 나아가고 있었다.\n\n\"우리 결혼식을 조금 뒤로 미룰 수 있을까요?\" 테스가 메마르고 둔탁한 목소리로 물었다. \"그러니까, 우리가 원한다면 말이에요?\"\n\n\"안 돼, 내 사랑. 진정해. 그 작자가 나를 폭행죄로 고소할 틈이라도 줘야 한다는 말인가?\" 그가 유쾌하게 물었다.\n\n\"아니에요—그냥 말하고 싶었던 건—혹시 미뤄야 할 일이 생긴다면, 하는 거였어요.\"\n\n그녀가 무슨 말을 하려는 것인지는 그리 분명치 않았고, 그는 그런 망상은 마음속에서 떨쳐 버리라고 일렀다. 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 고분고분 그렇게 했다. 그러나 집으로 돌아오는 내내 그녀는 침울했다, 몹시 침울했다. 그러다 이런 생각이 들었다. \"우리는 떠날 거야, 아주 먼 곳으로, 이곳에서 수백 마일 떨어진 데로. 그러면 이런 일은 다시는 일어날 수 없고, 과거의 어떤 유령도 거기까지는 닿지 못할 거야.\"\n\n그날 밤 둘은 층계참에서 다정하게 헤어졌고, 클레어는 자기 다락방으로 올라갔다. 테스는 자지 않고 앉아, 남은 며칠로는 시간이 모자랄까 봐 자잘한 준비물들을 마저 손보았다. 그렇게 앉아 있는데 위층 엔젤의 방에서 소리가 들렸다, 쿵쿵거리며 몸부림치는 소리가. 집안의 다른 사람들은 모두 잠들어 있었고, 클레어가 어디 아픈 게 아닐까 하는 걱정에 그녀는 뛰어 올라가 그의 방문을 두드리며 무슨 일이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아무것도 아니야, 여보.\" 안에서 그가 대답했다. \"깨워서 정말 미안해! 그런데 까닭이 좀 우습거든. 잠이 들었다가 당신을 모욕한 그 작자랑 또 싸우는 꿈을 꿨지 뭐야. 당신이 들은 소리는 내가 짐 가방을 주먹으로 마구 두드린 거였어. 오늘 짐을 싸려고 꺼내 놓은 그 가방 말이야. 나는 가끔 잠결에 이런 별난 짓을 한다고. 가서 자고 더는 신경 쓰지 마.\"\n\n이것이 그녀의 망설임을 한쪽으로 기울게 하는 데 필요한 마지막 한 방울이었다. 입으로 직접 과거를 그에게 밝히기란 도저히 할 수 없었다. 그러나 다른 방법이 있었다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아 편지지 넉 장에 삼사 년 전의 그 일들을 간결하게 적어, 봉투에 넣고 클레어에게 보내는 것이라 적었다. 그런 다음, 마음이 또 약해질까 봐 신발도 신지 않은 채 살금살금 위층으로 올라가 그 쪽지를 그의 방문 밑으로 밀어 넣었다.\n\n그날 밤 그녀의 잠은 끊기고 또 끊겼으니, 그럴 만도 했다. 그녀는 위층에서 들려올 첫 희미한 기척에 귀를 기울였다. 기척은 여느 때처럼 들려왔고, 그는 여느 때처럼 내려왔다. 그녀도 내려갔다. 그는 계단 아래서 그녀를 맞으며 입을 맞추었다. 분명히 여느 때 못지않게 따뜻한 입맞춤이었다!\n\n그가 조금 동요하고 지쳐 보인다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 그는 그녀의 고백에 관해 한마디도 하지 않았다, 둘만 있을 때조차도. 그가 그걸 받아 보긴 한 걸까? 그가 먼저 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않는 한 자기로서는 아무 말도 할 수 없다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그렇게 하루가 지났고, 그가 무슨 생각을 하든 그것을 혼자 간직하기로 마음먹은 것이 분명해 보였다. 그러면서도 그는 전과 다름없이 솔직하고 다정했다. 자기 의심이 어린아이 같은 것이었을까? 그가 자기를 용서한 걸까? 있는 그대로의 자기를, 바로 그 모습 그대로 사랑하면서, 자기의 불안을 어리석은 악몽쯤으로 여기며 빙긋 웃은 걸까? 그가 정말로 쪽지를 받아 본 걸까? 그녀는 그의 방을 슬쩍 들여다보았지만 그것은 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 어쩌면 그가 자기를 용서한 것인지도 몰랐다. 그러나 설령 그가 그것을 받아 보지 못했다 해도, 그가 분명 자기를 용서해 주리라는 갑작스러운 열렬한 믿음이 그녀에게 솟아났다.\n\n아침저녁으로 그는 한결같았고, 그렇게 섣달그믐날이 밝았다—결혼식 날이었다.\n\n연인들은 우유 짜는 시각에 일어나지 않았다. 낙농장에 머무는 이 마지막 한 주 내내 그들은 손님이나 다름없는 대접을 받았고, 테스는 자기만의 방을 쓰는 영예를 누렸다. 아침 식사 시간에 아래층으로 내려온 둘은, 마지막으로 본 이래로 그 큰 부엌에 자기들을 위해 어떤 변화가 일어나 있는지를 보고 놀랐다. 아침의 어느 터무니없는 시각에 낙농장 주인은 입을 떡 벌린 듯한 굴뚝 모퉁이를 하얗게 칠하게 하고, 벽돌 화덕을 붉게 칠하게 했으며, 예전에 그 자리를 지키던 검은 잔가지 무늬의 낡고 거무튀튀한 푸른 무명 가리개 대신, 활활 타오르는 듯한 노란 다마스크 가리개를 아치 위에 걸쳐 두게 했다. 한겨울 아침이면 그야말로 부엌의 중심이 되는 그 자리가 이렇게 새롭게 단장되니, 방 전체에 환한 표정이 드리워졌다.\n\n\"기념으로 뭐라도 좀 해야겠다고 작정했지요.\" 낙농장 주인이 말했다. \"그런데 옛날 같으면 으레 그랬듯이 바이올린이며 베이스 비올까지 갖춘 떠들썩한 잔치를 벌이게 해 달라는데도 도무지 들으려 하질 않으시니, 소리 안 나는 일로 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 건 이게 다였습니다.\"\n\n테스의 식구들은 워낙 멀리 떨어져 살아서, 설령 누구라도 청했다 한들 예식에 편히 참석할 만한 사람이 없었다. 사실 말럿에서는 아무도 초대받지 않았다. 엔젤의 가족으로 말하자면, 그는 편지를 써서 날짜를 제대로 알렸고, 적어도 그날 식구 중 한 명이라도 오고 싶다면 와 주면 반갑겠다고 다짐해 두었다. 그의 형제들은 아예 답이 없었으니, 그에게 분개한 듯했다. 한편 부모는 다소 슬픈 편지를 보내, 그가 결혼으로 황급히 뛰어든 성급함을 한탄하면서도, 비록 낙농장 여인이 자기들이 바랄 수 있는 며느릿감으로는 가장 뜻밖이긴 하나 아들이 이제 스스로 가장 잘 판단할 만한 나이에 이르렀으리라는 말로 이 일을 좋게 받아들였다.\n\n이러한 가족의 냉랭함이 클레어를 괴롭힌 정도는, 머잖아 그들을 놀래 줄 작정으로 그가 쥐고 있던 그 멋진 으뜸 패가 없었더라면 받았을 괴로움보다 한결 덜했다. 낙농장에서 갓 데려온 테스를 더버빌가의 여인이요 숙녀로서 내보이는 일은 무모하고 위험하다고 그는 느꼈다. 그래서 그녀의 혈통을 감추어 두었던 것이니, 몇 달 동안 자기와 함께 여행하고 책을 읽으며 세상 물정에 익숙해진 뒤에야 그녀를 부모에게 데려가, 그 유서 깊은 가문에 걸맞은 여인임을 의기양양하게 내보이면서 그 사실을 알려 줄 작정이었다. 그저 연인다운 어여쁜 꿈이긴 했다. 어쩌면 테스의 혈통은 세상 누구보다도 그 자신에게 더 값진 것이었는지도 모른다.\n\n엔젤이 자기를 대하는 태도가 자기 고백으로 인해 조금도 달라지지 않았다는 것을 알아채자, 테스는 그가 정말 그것을 받아 보긴 한 걸까 하는 죄스러운 의심에 휩싸였다. 그가 식사를 마치기도 전에 그녀는 식탁에서 일어나 황급히 위층으로 올라갔다. 그토록 오랫동안 클레어의 굴, 아니 차라리 둥지였던 그 기이하고 황량한 방을 다시 한번 들여다봐야겠다는 생각이 들었던 것이다. 사다리를 올라가 그녀는 그 방의 열린 문 앞에 서서, 바라보며 곰곰이 생각했다. 그녀는 며칠 전 그토록 흥분한 채 쪽지를 밀어 넣었던 문턱으로 몸을 굽혔다. 카펫이 문지방 바로 앞까지 깔려 있었는데, 그 카펫 가장자리 밑에서 그녀는 그에게 보내는 편지가 든 봉투의 희미한 흰 모서리를 알아보았다. 그가 분명 한 번도 보지 못한 그 편지를. 그녀가 다급한 나머지 문 밑으로뿐 아니라 카펫 밑으로까지 밀어 넣어 버린 탓이었다.\n\n아찔한 기분으로 그녀는 그 편지를 빼냈다. 봉인된 채 그대로였다, 그녀의 손을 떠났을 때 모습 그대로. 산은 아직 옮겨지지 않은 것이다. 지금은 그에게 그것을 읽게 할 수 없었다. 집안이 온통 준비로 부산했으니. 그녀는 자기 방으로 내려가 거기서 편지를 없애 버렸다.\n\n그가 그녀를 다시 보았을 때 그녀의 안색이 어찌나 창백한지 그는 꽤 걱정스러웠다. 그녀는 잘못 끼워진 편지의 사연을, 마치 그것이 고백을 막아 준 것이기라도 하다는 듯 덥석 붙잡았다. 그러나 양심으로는 그럴 필요가 없음을 알고 있었다. 아직 시간이 있었다. 그러나 모든 것이 어수선했다. 오가는 사람이 끊이지 않았고, 모두가 옷을 차려입어야 했으며, 낙농장 주인과 크리크 부인은 증인으로 동행해 달라는 부탁을 받은 터였다. 그러니 차분히 생각하거나 마음먹고 이야기할 짬이라곤 거의 없었다. 테스가 클레어와 단둘이 있을 수 있는 유일한 짬은 둘이 층계참에서 마주쳤을 때였다.\n\n\"당신한테 꼭 이야기하고 싶은 게 있어요—제 모든 잘못과 실수를 고백하고 싶어요!\" 그녀가 짐짓 가벼운 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니—잘못 이야기 같은 건 하면 안 돼—적어도 오늘만큼은 당신을 완벽한 사람으로 여겨야지, 내 사랑!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"앞으로 우리 흠을 두고 이야기할 시간은 얼마든지 있을 거야, 그러길 바라. 그때 내 흠도 함께 고백하지.\"\n\n\"하지만 지금 하는 편이 나을 것 같아요, 그래야 나중에 당신이 이런 말을 못 할 테니까—\"\n\n\"그래, 내 돈키호테 같은 사람아, 당신은 뭐든 내게 말해도 돼—자, 우리가 숙소에 자리 잡자마자 말이야, 지금 말고. 그때 나도 내 흠을 당신에게 말하지. 하지만 그걸로 오늘을 망치진 말자. 그건 따분할 때 풀어놓기에 더없이 좋은 거리가 될 테니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금은 제가 말하기를 바라지 않으시는 거죠, 여보?\"\n\n\"바라지 않아, 테시, 정말로.\"\n\n옷을 차려입고 출발하느라 부산한 통에 이 정도 이상의 이야기를 할 짬은 없었다. 거듭 곱씹어 보니 그의 그 말이 그녀를 안심시켜 주는 듯했다. 그를 향한 헌신의 압도적인 물결이 그녀를 휩쓸어 다음 두어 시간의 위태로운 고비를 그대로 밀고 나갔고, 그 물결이 더 깊은 사색의 통로를 막아 버렸다. 그토록 오래 억눌러 온 그녀의 단 하나의 소망—자기를 그의 것으로 만들고, 그를 자기 주인이라, 자기만의 사람이라 부르고, 그런 다음 필요하다면 죽어도 좋다는 그 소망이—마침내 그녀를 터덜터덜 걷던 사색의 오솔길에서 들어 올렸다. 옷을 차려입으면서 그녀는 갖가지 빛깔의 이상들로 이루어진 마음의 구름 속을 거닐었고, 그 구름의 환함은 온갖 불길한 가능성을 가려 버렸다.\n\n교회는 멀리 떨어져 있었고, 더구나 겨울이라 마차를 타지 않을 수 없었다. 길가 여관에서 사륜 포장마차를 한 대 불렀는데, 역마차로 여행하던 옛 시절부터 거기 보관되어 온 탈것이었다. 굵직한 바큇살과 묵직한 바퀴 테, 크게 굽은 차체, 거대한 가죽끈과 용수철, 그리고 성벽을 부수는 망치 같은 끌채를 갖추고 있었다. 마부는 예순 살 먹은 점잖은 \"소년\"이었으니—젊어서 지나치게 비바람에 시달린 탓에, 독한 술로 그것을 가라앉히려다 류머티즘성 통풍에 시달리게 된 순교자였다—직업적으로 말을 몰 필요가 없어진 이래로 흘러간 스물다섯 해 내내, 마치 옛 시절이 다시 돌아오기를 기다리는 듯, 여관 문 앞에 서서 하릴없이 지냈다. 그의 오른쪽 다리 바깥쪽에는 늘 진물이 흐르는 상처가 있었는데, 캐스터브리지의 킹스암스 여관에서 정식으로 고용되어 일하던 여러 해 동안 귀족들 마차의 끌채에 끊임없이 부딪쳐 생긴 것이었다.\n\n이 둔중하고 삐걱거리는 구조물 안에, 이 쇠락한 마부 뒤에, 네 사람의 일행이 자리를 잡았다—신부와 신랑, 그리고 크리크 부부였다. 엔젤은 적어도 형제 중 하나라도 신랑 들러리로 와 주기를 바랐지만, 편지로 그 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤음에도 그들이 침묵을 지킨 것은 올 마음이 없다는 뜻이었다. 그들은 이 결혼을 못마땅해했고, 그것을 두둔해 주리라 기대할 수도 없었다. 어쩌면 그들이 참석할 수 없는 편이 차라리 나았다. 그들은 세속에 물든 젊은이들은 아니었으나, 이 혼사에 대한 견해를 떠나서도, 낙농장 사람들과 어울리는 일은 편향된 그들의 까다로운 취향에 불쾌하게 거슬렸을 터였다.\n\n그때의 추진력에 떠받쳐, 테스는 이 모든 것을 전혀 알지 못했고, 아무것도 보지 못했으며, 교회로 가는 길이 어떤 길인지도 몰랐다. 그녀가 아는 것은 엔젤이 자기 곁에 바짝 있다는 것뿐, 나머지는 모두 환한 안개였다. 그녀는 일종의 천상의 존재, 시에서 비롯된 존재였다—클레어가 둘이 함께 산책할 때면 그녀에게 이야기해 주곤 하던 그 고전 속 여신들 가운데 하나였다.\n\n혼인이 허가증에 의한 것이었으므로 교회 안에는 열 명 남짓의 사람밖에 없었다. 천 명이 있었다 한들 그녀에게는 더 이상의 영향을 미치지 못했을 것이다. 그들은 지금 그녀의 세계로부터 별처럼 아득히 떨어져 있었다. 그에게 신의를 맹세하던 그 황홀한 엄숙함 속에서, 보통의 남녀 간 감정 따위는 경박한 것으로만 여겨졌다. 예식 중 잠시 멈춤이 있을 때, 둘이 함께 무릎 꿇고 있는 동안, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 그를 향해 몸을 기울여 어깨가 그의 팔에 닿았다. 스쳐 가는 어떤 생각에 겁이 났던 것이고, 그 동작은 무의식적인 것이었다. 그가 정말로 거기 있음을 확인하려는, 그리고 그의 신의가 그 무엇에도 흔들리지 않으리라는 믿음을 다지려는 동작이었다.\n\n클레어는 그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 알았다—그녀 몸의 모든 곡선이 그것을 드러냈다—그러나 그때 그는 그녀 헌신의 온전한 깊이를, 그 한결같음을, 그 온순함을 알지 못했다. 그것이 얼마나 큰 오랜 인내를, 얼마나 큰 정직을, 얼마나 큰 견딤을, 얼마나 큰 신실함을 담보하는지를.\n\n둘이 교회 밖으로 나서자 종지기들이 종을 받침대에서 끌어 흔들었고, 세 가락으로 이루어진 조촐한 종소리가 울려 퍼졌다—이만한 작은 교구의 기쁨에는 이 정도의 표현으로 족하다고 교회를 지은 사람들이 여겼던 것이다. 남편과 함께 종탑을 지나 정문으로 가는 길에서, 그녀는 격자 살이 달린 종루로부터 둥글게 퍼지는 소리의 원 속에서 떨리는 공기가 그들 주위로 윙윙대는 것을 느낄 수 있었고, 그것은 그녀가 살고 있는 한껏 충전된 마음의 분위기와 잘 어울렸다.\n\n이러한 마음 상태, 곧 성 요한이 태양 안에서 본 천사처럼 자기 것이 아닌 광채로 영광스러워진 듯한 그 상태는, 교회 종소리가 잦아들고 결혼식의 격정이 가라앉을 때까지 이어졌다. 이제 그녀의 눈은 세세한 것들을 한층 또렷이 머물러 볼 수 있게 되었고, 크리크 부부가 자기들 이륜마차를 따로 보내 그것을 타고 가게 하고 사륜마차는 젊은 부부에게 넘겨주기로 한 터라, 그녀는 처음으로 그 탈것의 생김새와 특징을 눈여겨보았다. 말없이 앉은 채로 그녀는 그것을 오랫동안 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신, 뭔가 짓눌린 듯해 보이는군, 테시.\" 클레어가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 손을 이마에 갖다 대며 대답했다. \"여러 가지가 떨려요. 모든 게 너무나 엄숙해요, 엔젤. 그중에서도, 이 마차를 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같아요, 아주 잘 아는 것만 같아요. 정말 이상해요—꿈에서 본 게 틀림없어요.\"\n\n\"아—더버빌가의 마차 전설을 들은 게로군—그 가문이 이 고장에서 한창 잘나가던 시절에 관한, 이 고을에 잘 알려진 그 미신 말이야. 그래서 이 둔중한 낡은 물건이 그걸 떠올리게 한 거지.\"\n\n\"제가 알기로는 한 번도 들은 적이 없는데요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 전설이 뭐예요—제가 알아도 될까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄—지금은 자세히 말하고 싶지 않아. 십육 세기인가 십칠 세기인가의 어느 더버빌가 사람이 자기 집안의 마차 안에서 끔찍한 죄를 저질렀어. 그 이후로 그 가문 사람들은 언제든—— 하지만 다른 날에 이야기해 줄게—꽤 음울한 이야기거든. 분명 이 유서 깊은 마차를 본 것이 당신 마음속에 그 어렴풋한 기억을 되살린 모양이야.\"\n\n\"전에 그걸 들은 기억이 없어요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"우리 가문 사람들이 그걸 보는 건, 우리가 죽으려 할 때인가요, 엔젤, 아니면 우리가 죄를 저질렀을 때인가요?\"\n\n\"그만, 테스!\"\n\n그는 입맞춤으로 그녀의 말을 막았다.\n\n집에 다다를 무렵 그녀는 뉘우치며 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그녀는 정녕 엔젤 클레어 부인이었으나, 그 이름을 가질 도덕적 권리가 자기에게 있긴 한 것일까? 자기는 차라리 알렉산더 더버빌 부인이 더 맞는 게 아닐까? 사랑의 강렬함이, 올곧은 영혼들이 죄스러운 침묵이라 여길 만한 것을 정당화할 수 있을까? 이런 경우에 여자에게 무엇이 요구되는지 그녀는 알지 못했고, 의논할 상대도 없었다.\n\n그래도 잠깐이나마 자기 방에 혼자 있게 되자—이 방에 들어오는 것도 이날이 마지막이었다—그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 기도했다. 하느님께 기도하려 애썼지만, 정작 그녀의 간구를 받은 것은 남편이었다. 이 남자를 향한 그녀의 우상 숭배는 어찌나 깊은지, 그녀 자신마저 그것이 불길한 조짐이 아닐까 두려워할 지경이었다. 그녀는 로런스 수사가 한 말, \"이 격렬한 기쁨은 격렬한 끝을 맺느니라\"라는 말이 담은 뜻을 의식하고 있었다. 그것은 인간의 처지에 비해 너무 절박한 것일지도—너무 무성하고, 너무 거칠고, 너무 치명적인 것일지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"오 내 사랑, 나는 어찌하여 당신을 이토록 사랑하는 걸까!\" 그녀는 거기 홀로 속삭였다. \"당신이 사랑하는 그 여자는 진짜 내가 아니라, 내 모습을 한 다른 여자인 것을. 내가 될 수도 있었던 그 여자인 것을!\"\n\n오후가 왔고, 그와 함께 떠날 시각이 다가왔다. 둘은 며칠 동안 웰브리지 방앗간 근처 옛 농가의 숙소로 가기로 한 계획을 실행하기로 했다. 그가 밀가루 제조 공정을 조사하는 동안 거기 머물 작정이었던 곳이다. 두 시가 되자 이제 출발하는 것밖에는 남은 일이 없었다. 낙농장의 일꾼들이 모두 붉은 벽돌 입구에 서서 둘이 나가는 것을 지켜보았고, 낙농장 주인 내외도 문까지 따라 나왔다. 테스는 자기 방을 함께 쓰던 세 처녀가 벽을 등지고 나란히 서서 시름에 잠긴 듯 고개를 숙이고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀들이 헤어지는 순간에 모습을 보일지 못내 궁금했었는데, 거기 그녀들이 있었다, 끝까지 의연하고 한결같이. 그녀는 가녀린 레티가 어째서 그토록 연약해 보이는지, 이즈가 어째서 그토록 비극적으로 슬퍼 보이는지, 매리언이 어째서 그토록 멍해 보이는지 알고 있었다. 그리고 그녀들의 그림자를 바라보느라 잠시 자기 자신을 끈질기게 따라다니는 그림자를 잊었다.\n\n그녀는 충동적으로 그에게 속삭였다—\n\n\"저 가엾은 애들한테, 처음이자 마지막으로, 한 번씩만 입맞춰 줄래요?\"\n\n클레어는 그런 작별의 의례에—그에게는 그뿐인 일이었으니—조금도 거리낌이 없었다. 그래서 그는 그녀들 앞을 지나며 서 있는 그대로 차례차례 입을 맞추었고, 그러면서 한 사람 한 사람에게 \"안녕\"이라고 말했다. 문에 다다랐을 때 테스는 여자다운 마음으로 뒤를 돌아보며 그 자비로운 입맞춤의 효과를 살폈다. 그녀의 시선에는 으레 있을 법한 승리감 같은 것이 없었다. 설령 있었다 한들, 처녀들이 모두 얼마나 마음이 흔들렸는지를 보았더라면 사라져 버렸을 것이다. 그 입맞춤은 분명 그녀들이 가라앉히려 애쓰던 감정을 일깨워 해를 끼친 것이었다.\n\n이 모든 것을 클레어는 알아채지 못했다. 쪽문으로 나아가며 그는 낙농장 주인 내외와 악수하고, 그동안 베풀어 준 보살핌에 마지막 감사를 전했다. 그러고 나서 일행이 떠나기 전에 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 그 침묵을 깬 것은 수탉이 우는 소리였다. 장미 모양 볏을 가진 흰 수탉이 와서 집 앞 울타리 말뚝에, 그들에게서 몇 야드도 안 되는 거리에 내려앉아 있었고, 그 울음소리가 그들의 귀를 꿰뚫었다가 바위 골짜기를 따라가는 메아리처럼 차츰 잦아들었다.\n\n\"어머나?\" 크리크 부인이 말했다. \"오후에 우는 닭이라니!\"\n\n마당 문 옆에 사내 둘이 서서 그것을 열어 잡고 있었다.\n\n\"안 좋은 징조야.\" 한 사내가 다른 사내에게 중얼거렸다, 그 말이 쪽문 앞의 무리에게 들리리라고는 생각지도 못한 채.\n\n수탉이 또 울었다—곧장 클레어를 향해서.\n\n\"허허!\" 낙농장 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"저 소리 듣기 싫어요!\" 테스가 남편에게 말했다. \"마부더러 어서 출발하라고 하세요. 안녕히, 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n수탉이 또 울었다.\n\n\"훠이! 썩 꺼지지 못해, 이놈, 안 그러면 모가지를 비틀어 버릴 테다!\" 낙농장 주인이 다소 짜증을 내며 새를 향해 돌아서서 쫓아 버렸다. 그리고 안으로 들어가며 아내에게 말했다. \"하필 오늘 같은 날 저런 짓을 하다니! 한 해가 다 가도록 오후에 우는 소리는 들은 적이 없었는데 말이야.\"\n\n\"그저 날씨가 바뀐다는 뜻일 뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 생각하는 그런 게 아니라요. 그런 일은 있을 수 없어요!\"\n\n# XXXIV\n\n그들은 골짜기를 따라 평평한 길로 몇 마일을 달려, 웰브리지에 다다라서는 마을에서 왼쪽으로 벗어나, 그 지명의 절반을 이루는 거대한 엘리자베스 시대의 다리를 건넜다. 그 바로 뒤에, 그들이 숙소를 잡은 집이 서 있었으니, 프룸 골짜기를 지나는 여행자라면 누구나 그 외관을 잘 아는 집이었다. 한때는 어느 훌륭한 장원 저택의 일부였고, 더버빌가의 소유이자 거처였으나, 일부가 헐린 뒤로는 농가가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"당신 조상의 저택 중 하나에 잘 오셨소이다!\" 그녀를 마차에서 내려 주며 클레어가 말했다. 그러나 그는 그 농담을 후회했다. 풍자에 너무 가까웠던 것이다.\n\n안으로 들어서니, 둘은 방을 두 칸만 빌렸을 뿐인데도, 농부가 앞으로 며칠간 그들이 머문다는 것을 핑계 삼아 새해 인사차 친구들을 찾아간 터였고, 그들의 자잘한 시중을 들도록 이웃 오두막의 여인 하나만 남겨 두었음을 알게 되었다. 집을 통째로 차지하게 된 것이 둘은 마음에 들었고, 자기들만의 지붕 아래서 보내는 첫 순간임을 실감했다. 그러나 그는 곰팡내 나는 그 낡은 집이 신부의 기분을 다소 가라앉게 한다는 것을 알아차렸다. 마차가 떠나자 둘은 손을 씻으러 계단을 올랐고, 허드렛일하는 여인이 길을 안내했다. 층계참에서 테스가 걸음을 멈추며 흠칫했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"저 끔찍한 여자들요!\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"어찌나 놀라게 하던지요.\"\n\n그가 올려다보니, 벽체에 끼워 넣은 판자에 실물 크기의 초상화 두 점이 보였다. 이 저택을 찾는 이라면 누구나 알듯, 이 그림들은 약 이백 년 전 시대의 중년 여인들을 그린 것으로, 그 이목구비는 한번 보면 결코 잊을 수 없는 것이었다. 하나는 길쭉하고 뾰족한 얼굴 생김과 좁은 눈, 그리고 가차 없는 배신을 암시하는 듯한 능글맞은 웃음을 띠었고, 다른 하나는 낫 모양의 코와 큼직한 이, 그리고 잔인함에 이를 만큼의 오만을 풍기는 대담한 눈을 지녔으니, 그것을 본 사람을 두고두고 그 꿈속까지 따라다니는 얼굴들이었다.\n\n\"저건 누구의 초상화인가요?\" 클레어가 허드렛일하는 여인에게 물었다.\n\n\"나이 든 어른들한테 듣기로는 더버빌가의 부인들이라던데요, 이 장원의 옛 영주들 말이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"벽 속에 끼워 넣어 지은 거라서 떼어 낼 수가 없답니다.\"\n\n이 일의 언짢은 점은, 테스에게 끼치는 영향에 더하여, 그녀의 고운 이목구비가 이 과장된 형상들 속에서 의심할 여지 없이 더듬어 보였다는 데 있었다. 그러나 그는 이에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 신혼 시절을 보낼 집으로 굳이 이곳을 고른 것을 후회하며 옆방으로 들어갔다. 그들을 위해 다소 황급히 준비된 곳이라, 둘은 한 대야에 손을 씻었다. 클레어가 물속에서 그녀의 손을 건드렸다.\n\n\"어느 게 내 손가락이고 어느 게 당신 거지?\" 그가 올려다보며 말했다. \"아주 뒤섞여 버렸군.\"\n\n\"전부 당신 거예요.\" 그녀가 무척 어여쁘게 말했고, 실제 기분보다 더 명랑한 척하려 애썼다. 이런 때에 보이는 그녀의 세심한 마음씨를 그가 못마땅해한 것은 아니었다. 그것은 분별 있는 여자라면 누구나 보일 법한 것이었다. 그러나 테스는 자기가 지나치게 마음을 쓰고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그것에 맞서 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n그해의 짧디짧은 마지막 오후, 해가 어찌나 낮게 떴는지, 작은 틈으로 비쳐 들어와 황금빛 지팡이를 이루며 그녀의 치마까지 뻗어, 거기에 마치 그녀에게 찍힌 물감 자국 같은 반점을 만들었다. 둘은 차를 마시러 그 오래된 응접실로 들어갔고, 거기서 단둘이 첫 식사를 나누었다. 둘은—아니 차라리 그는—어찌나 어린아이 같던지, 그녀와 같은 버터빵 접시를 쓰는 것이, 또 자기 입술로 그녀 입가의 부스러기를 털어 주는 것이 재미있었다. 그녀가 이런 시시한 장난에 자기만큼 신이 나서 끼어들지 않는 것이 그는 조금 의아했다.\n\n오랫동안 말없이 그녀를 바라보며, 그는 마음속으로 생각했다. 어려운 구절의 참뜻을 가려내려는 사람처럼. \"그녀는 사랑스럽고 사랑스러운 테스야. 이 작은 여인 같은 존재가 나의 선의나 악의에, 나의 행운이나 불운에 얼마나 철저하고 돌이킬 수 없이 매여 있는지를, 나는 충분히 엄숙하게 깨닫고 있는 걸까? 그렇지 못한 것 같다. 내가 여자 자신이 아닌 한, 깨달을 수 없으리라 본다. 내가 세속적 처지에서 어떠한가, 그녀도 그러하다. 내가 무엇이 되든, 그녀도 그것이 되어야 한다. 내가 될 수 없는 것은, 그녀도 될 수 없다. 그런데 내가 그녀를 소홀히 하거나, 상처 입히거나, 헤아리기를 잊는 일이 있을까? 그런 죄악은 부디 없기를!\"\n\n둘은 짐을 기다리며 찻상 앞에 계속 앉아 있었다. 낙농장 주인이 날이 저물기 전에 보내 주마고 약속한 짐이었다. 그러나 저녁이 깃들기 시작했는데도 짐은 오지 않았고, 둘은 입은 옷 말고는 아무것도 가져온 것이 없었다. 해가 지면서 겨울 낮의 고요하던 기색이 변했다. 바깥에서는 비단을 세게 비비는 듯한 소리들이 일기 시작했고, 지난가을의 편히 잠들어 있던 죽은 잎들이 짜증스러운 부활로 깨어나 마지못해 이리저리 휘날리며 덧창에 부딪쳤다. 이윽고 비가 내리기 시작했다.\n\n\"저 수탉은 날씨가 바뀔 걸 알았던 거야.\" 클레어가 말했다.\n\n그들의 시중을 들던 여인은 밤이 되어 집으로 돌아갔지만, 식탁에 양초를 놓아두고 갔고, 이제 둘은 그것에 불을 붙였다. 촛불 하나하나가 벽난로 쪽으로 끌리듯 기울었다.\n\n\"이런 낡은 집들은 외풍이 너무 심하군.\" 불꽃을, 그리고 옆구리를 타고 흘러내리는 촛농을 바라보며 엔젤이 말을 이었다. \"짐이 대체 어디 있는 건지 모르겠어. 우린 빗과 솔조차 없잖아.\"\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 그녀가 멍하니 대답했다.\n\n\"테스, 당신 오늘 저녁엔 조금도 즐거워 보이질 않아—예전 모습과는 영 딴판이야. 위층 판자에 그려진 저 마귀할멈들이 당신 마음을 흔들어 놓은 거야. 당신을 이리로 데려온 게 미안해. 당신, 정말 나를 사랑하긴 하는 걸까, 어쩐지 궁금해지는군?\"\n\n그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 그 말에 진지한 뜻은 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 감정으로 가득 차 있던 터라, 상처 입은 짐승처럼 움찔했다. 눈물을 흘리지 않으려 애썼지만, 한두 방울 비치는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다.\n\n\"그런 뜻이 아니었어!\" 그가 미안해하며 말했다. \"당신 물건이 없어서 속상한 거지, 알아. 늙은 조너선이 왜 그걸 가지고 오지 않는지 모르겠군. 아니, 벌써 일곱 시잖아? 아, 저기 오는군!\"\n\n문을 두드리는 소리가 났고, 달리 응대할 사람이 없으니 클레어가 나갔다. 그는 작은 꾸러미 하나를 손에 들고 방으로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"결국 조너선이 아니었어.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그 꾸러미는 특별 심부름꾼이 가져온 것이었다. 그는 결혼한 부부가 떠난 직후 에민스터 목사관에서 톨버세이즈로 도착해, 그들을 좇아 여기까지 따라왔던 것이다. 그것을 오직 부부의 손에만 건네고 다른 누구에게도 넘기지 말라는 분부를 받은 터였다. 클레어가 그것을 불빛 가까이로 가져왔다. 한 자가 채 안 되는 길이에, 캔버스 천으로 꿰매여 있었고, 아버지의 인장이 찍힌 붉은 밀랍으로 봉인되었으며, 아버지의 필체로 \"엔젤 클레어 부인 앞\"이라 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"당신에게 보내는 자그마한 결혼 선물이야, 테스.\" 그것을 그녀에게 건네며 그가 말했다. \"참 마음을 써 주셨군!\"\n\n테스는 그것을 받아 들며 조금 당황한 기색이었다.\n\n\"당신이 열어 주면 좋겠어요, 여보.\" 꾸러미를 이리저리 뒤집으며 그녀가 말했다. \"저 큼직한 봉인을 깨뜨리고 싶지 않아요. 너무 엄숙해 보여서요. 부디 저 대신 열어 주세요!\"\n\n그가 꾸러미를 풀었다. 안에는 모로코가죽 상자가 들어 있었고, 그 위에 쪽지 한 장과 열쇠 하나가 놓여 있었다.\n\n쪽지는 클레어에게 보내는 것으로, 다음과 같은 내용이었다.\n\n사랑하는 아들에게,— 아마 너는 잊었겠지만, 네가 어린 소년이었을 때 네 대모이신 피트니 부인이 돌아가시며—허영심이 있긴 했으나 친절한 분이었지—그분의 보석함 내용물 중 일부를, 네가 언젠가 아내를 맞게 된다면 그 아내에게 주라며 나에게 신탁으로 남기셨단다. 너를, 그리고 네가 누구를 택하든 그 사람을 아끼는 마음의 표시로 말이다. 이 신탁을 나는 이행해 왔고, 그 다이아몬드는 그 이래로 내 거래 은행에 줄곧 보관되어 왔다. 이런 상황에서 이는 다소 어울리지 않는 처사로 여겨지긴 하나, 너도 알겠지만, 나로서는 이제 그것을 평생토록 사용할 권리가 정당하게 돌아갈 그 여인에게 넘겨주지 않을 수 없으니, 그리하여 곧바로 보낸다. 네 대모님 유언의 조건에 따르면, 엄밀히 말해 이것은 가보가 되는 것으로 안다. 이 일에 관한 조항의 정확한 문구를 동봉한다.\n\n\"기억나는군.\" 클레어가 말했다. \"하지만 까맣게 잊고 있었어.\"\n\n상자를 열어 보니, 펜던트가 달린 목걸이 하나와 팔찌, 귀고리, 그리고 그 밖의 자잘한 장신구 몇 점이 들어 있었다.\n\n테스는 처음엔 그것에 손대기를 두려워하는 듯했으나, 클레어가 그 한 벌을 펼쳐 놓자 잠시 그녀의 눈이 그 보석들 못지않게 반짝였다.\n\n\"이게 제 거예요?\" 그녀가 믿기지 않는다는 듯 물었다.\n\n\"그럼, 당연하지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그는 불을 들여다보았다. 열다섯 살 소년이었을 때, 자기 대모이자 그가 평생 접해 본 유일한 부자였던 향사의 부인이, 자기의 성공을 굳게 믿었던 일을 그는 떠올렸다. 그녀는 그에게 놀라운 출세를 예언했었다. 그렇게 짐작된 출세에, 그의 아내와 그 후손들의 아내를 위해 이런 화려한 장신구를 간직해 두는 일은 조금도 어긋날 게 없어 보였었다. 그것들이 지금은 어쩐지 빈정대듯 반짝였다. \"그렇지만 왜?\" 그는 자신에게 물었다. 그것은 처음부터 끝까지 그저 허영의 문제일 뿐이었다. 그리고 만약 허영이 등식의 한쪽에 허용된다면, 다른 쪽에도 허용되어 마땅했다. 그의 아내는 더버빌가의 여인이었다. 그것이 그녀보다 더 잘 어울릴 사람이 누가 있겠는가?\n\n별안간 그가 열에 들떠 말했다—\n\n\"테스, 그걸 차 봐—차 보라고!\" 그러고는 그녀를 돕고자 불에서 돌아섰다.\n\n그러나 마치 마법에라도 걸린 듯, 그녀는 이미 그것들을 다 차고 있었다—목걸이, 귀고리, 팔찌, 전부 다.\n\n\"그런데 가운이 맞질 않는걸, 테스.\" 클레어가 말했다. \"저런 보석 한 벌에는 가슴이 깊이 파인 옷이라야 하는데.\"\n\n\"그래야 하나요?\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀에게 보디스 윗단을 안쪽으로 접어 넣어, 저녁 예복의 재단에 대강 비슷해 보이게 하는 법을 일러 주었다. 그녀가 그렇게 하여, 목걸이의 펜던트가 본래 그러도록 만들어진 대로 그녀 목의 흰 살결 한가운데 외따로 드리워지자, 그는 한 걸음 물러나 그녀를 찬찬히 살폈다.\n\n\"세상에.\" 클레어가 말했다. \"당신, 어쩜 이렇게 아름다울까!\"\n\n누구나 알듯이, 고운 깃털이 고운 새를 만드는 법이다. 소박한 처지와 옷차림으로는 무심히 지나치는 이의 눈에 그저 그만한 미모로 보이던 시골 처녀도, 예술이 베푸는 도움으로 멋쟁이 여인처럼 차려입히면 놀라운 미인으로 활짝 피어난다. 반면 한밤의 무도회를 빛내던 미인도 흐린 날 단조롭게 펼쳐진 순무밭 위에서 들일하는 여인의 작업복을 입혀 놓으면 흔히 초라한 꼴이 되고 만다. 그는 지금까지 한 번도 테스의 팔다리와 이목구비가 지닌 예술적 빼어남을 헤아려 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"당신이 무도회장에 나서기만 한다면!\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 아니야—아니야, 여보. 나는 챙 달린 보닛에 무명 원피스 차림의 당신이 가장 좋은 것 같아—그래, 이 차림보다 그게 더 좋아, 이 위엄을 이토록 잘 받쳐 내긴 하지만 말이야.\"\n\n자기의 빼어난 모습을 의식하자 테스에게는 흥분의 홍조가 일었으나, 그것은 행복은 아니었다.\n\n\"이걸 벗을게요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"조너선이 저를 볼지도 모르니까요. 이건 저한테 어울리지 않아요, 그렇죠? 팔아 버려야겠죠, 아마?\"\n\n\"몇 분만 더 그대로 두구려. 팔다니? 결코 안 돼. 그건 신의를 저버리는 일이 될 거야.\"\n\n또 다른 생각에 마음이 움직여 그녀는 선뜻 따랐다. 그녀에게는 할 이야기가 있었고, 어쩌면 이것들이 도움이 될지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 보석을 몸에 두른 채 자리에 앉았고, 둘은 다시 조너선이 짐을 가지고 대체 어디쯤 있을까 이런저런 짐작을 늘어놓았다. 그가 오면 마시라고 따라 둔 맥주는 오래 놓여 있어 김이 다 빠져 있었다.\n\n이내 둘은 저녁을 먹기 시작했는데, 식사는 이미 곁상에 차려져 있었다. 다 먹기도 전에 굴뚝 연기가 한 차례 출렁하더니, 솟아오르던 연기 가닥이 방 안으로 불룩 밀려 나왔다. 마치 어떤 거인이 한순간 굴뚝 꼭대기에 손을 얹기라도 한 듯했다. 그것은 바깥문이 열린 탓이었다. 이제 복도에서 묵직한 발소리가 들렸고, 엔젤이 나갔다.\n\n\"문을 두드려도 도무지 아무도 들으시질 않아서요.\" 조너선 케일이 변명했으니, 마침내 그였던 것이다. \"게다가 밖에 비가 와서, 제가 문을 열었습니다. 짐을 가져왔습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"가져와 줘서 정말 반갑네. 한데 너무 늦었군.\"\n\n\"그게, 네, 선생님.\"\n\n조너선 케일의 어조에는 낮에는 없던 어떤 가라앉은 기색이 있었고, 세월의 주름에 더하여 근심의 주름이 그의 이마에 깊이 패어 있었다. 그가 말을 이었다—\n\n\"선생님하고 마님—이제는 그렇게 불러야겠지요—두 분이 오늘 오후에 우릴 떠나신 뒤로, 낙농장에선 다들 자칫 엄청난 변고가 될 뻔한 일에 혼이 다 빠졌답니다. 혹시 수탉이 오후에 울던 거, 잊지 않으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"아이고—뭐가—\"\n\n\"그게, 누구는 그게 이 뜻이라 하고 누구는 저 뜻이라 합니다만, 정작 일어난 일은, 가엾은 어린 레티 프리들이 물에 빠져 죽으려 했다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이런! 정말로! 아니, 그 애는 다른 애들이랑 우리에게 작별 인사를 했는데—\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 그게, 선생님, 선생님하고 마님—이제 법적으로 그러하신 분 말입니다만—두 분이 마차로 떠나신 뒤에, 말씀드린 대로, 레티와 매리언이 보닛을 쓰고 나갔답니다. 그런데 지금이야 섣달그믐이라 할 일도 많지 않고, 다들 속에 든 것 때문에 들떠 있던 터라, 아무도 별로 눈여겨보질 않았지요. 둘이 루에버라드까지 가서 뭘 좀 마시고는, 거기서 드리암드 크로스까지 터덜터덜 걸어갔는데, 거기서 갈라진 모양입니다. 레티는 집으로 가려는 듯 물가 풀밭을 가로질러 갔고, 매리언은 다음 마을로 갔는데, 거기 또 다른 술집이 있거든요. 레티에 대해선 그 뒤로 보이지도 들리지도 않다가, 뱃사공이 집으로 가던 길에 큰 못가에서 뭔가를 봤답니다. 그게 그 애의 보닛하고 숄을 챙겨 놓은 거였지요. 물속에서 그 애를 찾았답니다. 그 사람하고 다른 사람 하나가 죽은 줄 알고 그 애를 집으로 데려왔는데, 차츰 정신을 차리더랍니다.\"\n\n엔젤은 테스가 이 음울한 이야기를 엿듣고 있다는 것을 문득 떠올리고는, 그녀가 있는 안쪽 응접실로 통하는 대기실과 복도 사이의 문을 닫으러 갔다. 그러나 그의 아내는 어깨에 숄을 두른 채 바깥방으로 나와 사내의 이야기를 듣고 있었으니, 그녀의 눈은 짐과 그 위에서 반짝이는 빗방울에 멍하니 머물러 있었다.\n\n\"게다가 이뿐만이 아닙니다. 매리언도 말입니다, 버드나무 늪가에서 곤드레만드레 취해 쓰러진 채로 발견됐답니다—전에는 한 푼짜리 맥주 말고는 입에 댄 적이 없는 걸로 알려진 애가 말이지요. 하기야 얼굴만 봐도 알 만큼 늘 먹성은 좋았습니다만. 처녀들이 죄다 정신이 나가 버린 것만 같습니다요!\"\n\n\"그럼 이즈는요?\" 테스가 물었다.\n\n\"이즈는 여느 때처럼 집안일을 하고 있습니다. 다만 그 애 말이, 일이 어찌 된 영문인지 짐작이 간다고 하더군요. 그러면서 그 애는 그 일로 영 풀이 죽어 있습니다, 가엾은 것이, 그럴 만도 하지요. 그래서 보시다시피, 선생님, 선생님 짐 몇 가지하고 마님 잠옷이며 화장 도구를 수레에 싣던 바로 그때 이 모든 일이 터지는 바람에, 제가 이렇게 늦은 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇군. 자, 조너선, 짐 가방을 위층으로 올려 주고, 맥주 한 잔 마신 다음, 자네가 필요할지 모르니 되도록 빨리 돌아가게.\"\n\n테스는 안쪽 응접실로 돌아가 불가에 앉아 그것을 그윽이 들여다보고 있었다. 조너선 케일이 짐을 다 놓을 때까지 계단을 오르내리는 그 묵직한 발소리가 그녀에게 들려왔고, 남편이 내다 준 맥주에 대해, 또 받은 사례에 대해 그가 고맙다고 말하는 소리도 들렸다. 이윽고 조너선의 발소리가 문에서 잦아들었고, 그의 수레가 삐걱대며 멀어져 갔다.\n\n엔젤은 문을 잠그는 묵직한 참나무 빗장을 밀어 걸고는, 그녀가 화덕 위로 몸을 기울이고 앉아 있는 곳으로 들어와, 뒤에서 두 손으로 그녀의 두 뺨을 감쌌다. 그녀가 신나게 벌떡 일어나, 그토록 애태우던 화장 도구를 풀어헤치리라 그는 기대했지만, 그녀가 일어나지 않자 그도 그녀와 함께 난롯불 빛 속에 앉았다. 저녁상 위의 양초들은 너무 가느다랗고 가물거려 그 불빛을 방해하지 못했다.\n\n\"처녀들에 관한 이 슬픈 이야기를 당신이 듣게 되어 정말 안됐어.\" 그가 말했다. \"그래도, 그것 때문에 기죽지는 마. 레티는 원래 좀 병적인 데가 있었잖아, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"전혀 그럴 까닭이 없는데도요.\" 테스가 말했다. \"정작 그럴 까닭이 있는 사람들은 그걸 숨기고, 아닌 척하는데 말이에요.\"\n\n이 일이 그녀의 저울을 한쪽으로 기울게 했다. 그녀들은 짝사랑의 불행이 덮친 순박하고 죄 없는 처녀들이었다. 그녀들은 운명의 손에서 더 나은 것을 받을 만한 이들이었다. 자기는 더 못한 것을 받아 마땅했건만—그런데도 자기가 택함을 받은 자였다. 아무것도 치르지 않고 모든 것을 거머쥐는 것은 사악한 짓이었다. 그녀는 마지막 한 푼까지 치르리라. 바로 여기서, 바로 지금 털어놓으리라. 그가 자기 손을 잡은 채 불을 들여다보던 그때, 그녀는 이 마지막 결심에 이르렀다.\n\n이제 불꽃이 사라진 잉걸불에서 한결같은 불그레한 빛이 뿜어 나와 벽난로의 옆면과 안쪽을, 또 반들반들 윤이 나는 장작 받침쇠와 끝이 맞물리지 않는 낡은 놋쇠 부집게를 그 빛깔로 물들였다. 벽난로 선반의 아랫면도 그 짙은 빛으로 발그레해졌고, 불에 가장 가까운 식탁 다리들도 그러했다. 테스의 얼굴과 목도 같은 온기를 되비쳤고, 그 온기는 보석 하나하나를 알데바란이나 시리우스로 바꾸어 놓았다—흰빛, 붉은빛, 초록빛으로 번득이는 별자리가, 그녀의 맥박이 뛸 때마다 서로 빛깔을 바꾸었다.\n\n\"우리 잘못을 털어놓자고 오늘 아침에 서로 했던 말, 기억하지?\" 그녀가 여전히 꼼짝 않고 있는 것을 보고 그가 불쑥 물었다. \"우린 어쩌면 가볍게 말했고, 당신도 그랬을 법해. 하지만 나로서는 결코 가벼운 약속이 아니었어. 당신에게 고백하고 싶은 게 있어, 내 사랑.\"\n\n그에게서 나온 이 말이 너무도 뜻밖에 딱 들어맞아, 그것은 그녀에게 마치 하늘의 섭리가 끼어든 것 같은 작용을 했다.\n\n\"당신이 고백할 게 있다고요?\" 그녀가 재빨리, 심지어 기쁨과 안도마저 담아 말했다.\n\n\"안 그러리라 생각했지? 아—당신은 나를 너무 높이 봤던 거야. 자, 들어 봐. 머리를 여기에 기대, 당신이 나를 용서해 주길, 그리고 진작 말했어야 했을 일을 진작 말하지 않은 나를 노여워하지 말아 주길 바라니까.\"\n\n이 얼마나 기이한 일인가! 그가 마치 자기의 분신 같았다. 그녀는 말이 없었고, 클레어가 말을 이었다—\n\n\"내가 그걸 말하지 않은 건, 당신을 얻을 기회가 위태로워질까 두려웠기 때문이야, 여보, 내 인생의 가장 큰 상인 당신을 말이지—나는 당신을 내 펠로십이라 부르잖아. 내 형의 펠로십은 자기 대학에서 얻은 것이지만, 내 펠로십은 톨버세이즈 낙농장에서 얻은 거니까. 그래, 나는 그걸 걸 수 없었어. 한 달 전에 말하려 했지—당신이 내 사람이 되겠다고 했던 그때 말이야. 하지만 못 했어. 그게 당신을 놀라게 해서 멀어지게 할까 봐 두려웠거든. 그래서 미뤘지. 그러고는 어제 말해서, 적어도 당신에게 나를 벗어날 기회라도 주려고 했어. 하지만 못 했어. 그리고 오늘 아침에도, 당신이 층계참에서 우리 잘못을 고백하자고 했을 때도 못 했지—죄인 같으니라고! 하지만 이제 당신이 거기 그토록 엄숙하게 앉아 있는 걸 보니, 말하지 않을 수 없군. 당신이 나를 용서해 줄지 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"오 그럼요! 분명히—\"\n\n\"그래, 그러길 바라. 하지만 잠깐만. 당신은 아직 몰라. 처음부터 이야기하지. 내 가엾은 아버지는 내 교리 때문에 나를 영영 길 잃은 자 중 하나로 여기시며 두려워하시지만, 나도 물론 선한 도덕을 믿는 사람이야, 테스, 당신만큼이나 말이야. 나는 한때 사람들을 가르치는 사람이 되고 싶었고, 성직에 들어설 수 없다는 걸 알았을 때는 크게 실망했어. 나는 비록 흠 없음을 내 것이라 주장할 순 없었지만 흠 없음을 흠모했고, 부정함을 미워했어, 지금도 그러길 바라지. 축자영감설을 어떻게 생각하든, 바울의 이 말씀에는 진심으로 동의하지 않을 수 없어. '너는 본이 되라—말과 행실과 사랑과 영과 믿음과 정결로.' 그것이 우리 가엾은 인간들에게 유일한 안전장치니까. '온전한 삶을 사는 자는'이라고 한 로마 시인이 말했지, 성 바울과는 어울리지 않는 동무이긴 하지만—\n\n올곧게 살며 허물에서 벗어난 사람은 무어인의 창이나 활도 필요로 하지 않으리.\n\n\"그런데, 어떤 곳은 선한 의도로 포장되어 있다고들 하지. 나는 그 모든 것을 그토록 강하게 느꼈던 만큼, 다른 사람들을 위한 내 고매한 뜻 한가운데서 나 자신이 넘어졌을 때, 그것이 내 안에 얼마나 끔찍한 회한을 낳았는지 당신도 알게 될 거야.\"\n\n그러고는 그는 자기 삶의 그 시절을 그녀에게 이야기했다. 앞서 언급된 적 있는 그 시절, 런던에서 의혹과 곤경에 파도 위의 코르크 마개처럼 이리저리 휩쓸리던 끝에, 한 낯선 여자와 마흔여덟 시간의 방탕에 빠져들었던 그 일을.\n\n\"다행히 나는 거의 곧바로 내 어리석음을 깨달았어.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자와는 더 이상 상관하지 않으려고, 집으로 돌아왔지. 그 잘못을 다시 저지른 적은 없어. 하지만 나는 당신을 완전한 솔직함과 명예로 대하고 싶었고, 이걸 말하지 않고는 그럴 수가 없었어. 나를 용서하겠어?\"\n\n그녀는 대답 삼아 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"그럼 우린 이 일을 지금 당장, 그리고 영원히 떨쳐 버리는 거야!—이런 자리에 비하면 너무 괴로운 이야기니까—그리고 좀 더 가벼운 이야기를 하자고.\"\n\n\"오, 엔젤—저는 거의 기뻐요—이제 당신이 저를 용서해 줄 수 있을 테니까요! 저는 아직 고백을 안 했어요. 저도 고백할 게 있어요—기억하시죠, 제가 그렇게 말했잖아요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지! 자 그럼 어디 들어 보자, 못된 작은 사람아.\"\n\n\"비록 당신은 웃지만, 어쩌면 그건 당신 것만큼 심각하거나, 그보다 더 심각할지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"그게 더 심각하긴 어려울 거야, 여보.\"\n\n\"그럴 수 없겠죠—오 그래요, 그럴 수 없을 거예요!\" 그녀는 그 희망에 기뻐하며 벌떡 일어났다. \"그래요, 분명 그게 더 심각할 순 없겠죠.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"왜냐하면 그건 꼭 똑같은 거니까요! 이제 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n둘의 손은 여전히 맞잡혀 있었다. 쇠살대 아래의 재는 불빛에 수직으로 비쳐, 마치 타는 듯한 황무지 같았다. 상상력이라면 이 붉은 잉걸불의 빛 속에서 최후의 날 같은 무시무시함을 보았을 법했다. 그 빛은 그의 얼굴과 손에, 그리고 그녀의 그것에 떨어졌고, 그녀 이마 주위로 풀린 머리카락 속을 들여다보며 그 아래 고운 살결을 불태웠다. 그녀 형체의 커다란 그림자가 벽과 천장 위로 솟아올랐다. 그녀가 앞으로 몸을 숙이자, 목에 걸린 다이아몬드 하나하나가 두꺼비 눈처럼 불길하게 윙크했다. 그리고 그녀는 그의 관자놀이에 자기 이마를 갖다 댄 채, 알렉 더버빌과의 인연과 그 결과에 관한 자기 이야기를 시작했다. 움츠러듦 없이 그 말들을 중얼거리며, 눈꺼풀을 아래로 떨군 채로.\n\n# XXXV\n\n그녀의 이야기가 끝났다. 거듭한 확언도, 곁들인 부연 설명도 다 끝났다. 그동안 테스의 목소리는 처음 그 어조보다 좀처럼 높아진 적이 없었고, 어떤 변명의 말도 한마디 없었으며, 그녀는 울지도 않았다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 고백이 진행되는 동안 바깥 사물들의 빛깔조차 변모를 겪는 듯했다. 쇠살대 속의 불은 짓궂게—악마처럼 익살맞게 보였으니, 마치 그녀의 곤경 따위는 조금도 개의치 않는 듯했다. 난로 울도 하릴없이 히죽거렸으니, 마치 그 또한 개의치 않는 듯했다. 물병에서 나오는 빛은 그저 색채의 문제에만 골몰해 있었다. 주위의 모든 물질적 사물들이 무시무시한 되풀이로 자기들의 무책임함을 알렸다. 그런데도 그가 그녀에게 입맞추던 그 순간들 이래로 변한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 아니, 차라리 사물의 실체에서는 변한 것이 아무것도 없었다. 그러나 사물의 본질이 변해 버린 것이다.\n\n그녀가 말을 멈추자, 앞서 나누었던 애정 어린 말들의 청각적 인상이 그들의 뇌 한구석으로 황급히 밀려나, 더없이 눈먼 어리석음의 시절에서 들려오는 메아리처럼 스스로를 되풀이하는 듯했다.\n\n클레어는 불을 휘젓는 엉뚱한 행동을 했다. 그 깨달음은 아직 그의 밑바닥까지 가닿지도 못한 터였다. 잉걸불을 휘저은 뒤 그는 자리에서 일어섰다. 이제 그녀의 고백이 지닌 온 힘이 그에게 전해진 것이다. 그의 얼굴은 시들어 있었다. 골똘히 집중하느라 그는 발을 마룻바닥에 띄엄띄엄 굴렀다. 어떤 방도로도 그는 충분히 빈틈없이 생각해 낼 수가 없었다. 그것이 그 막연한 움직임의 뜻이었다. 입을 열었을 때 그것은 그녀가 그에게서 들어 온 그 갖가지 다양한 어조들 가운데 가장 어설프고 가장 평범한 목소리였다.\n\n\"테스!\"\n\n\"네, 여보.\"\n\n\"내가 이걸 믿어야 하나? 당신 태도로 보아 이걸 사실로 받아들여야겠군. 오 당신이 제정신이 아닐 리는 없잖아! 차라리 그래야 할 텐데! 그런데 당신은 그렇지 않아... 내 아내, 내 테스—당신 안의 그 어떤 것도 그런 가정을 뒷받침하지 않는데?\"\n\n\"저는 제정신이 아닌 게 아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그런데도—\" 그는 멍하니 그녀를 바라보다가, 얼떨떨한 감각으로 말을 이었다. \"왜 진작 내게 말하지 않았지? 아, 그래, 당신은 어떤 식으로든 말하려고 했었지—그런데 내가 막았어, 기억나는군!\"\n\n이 말들도, 그의 다른 말들도, 표면의 형식적인 횡설수설에 지나지 않았으니, 그 밑바닥은 마비된 채 그대로였다. 그는 돌아서서 의자 위로 몸을 숙였다. 테스는 그가 있는 방 한가운데까지 그를 뒤따라가, 거기 서서 눈물 흘리지 않는 눈으로 그를 빤히 바라보았다. 이윽고 그녀는 그의 발치로 무릎을 꿇으며 미끄러지듯 주저앉아, 그 자세 그대로 한 덩이로 웅크렸다.\n\n\"우리 사랑의 이름으로, 저를 용서해 주세요!\" 그녀가 바싹 마른 입으로 속삭였다. \"저는 똑같은 일로 당신을 용서했잖아요!\"\n\n그리고 그가 대답하지 않자, 그녀는 다시 말했다—\n\n\"당신이 용서받았듯 저를 용서해 주세요! 저는 당신을 용서해요, 엔젤.\" \"당신을—그래요, 당신은 용서하는군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 날 용서하지 않는군요?\"\n\n\"오 테스, 이 경우엔 용서가 통하질 않아! 당신은 한 사람이었는데, 이제 당신은 다른 사람이야. 맙소사—그런 기괴한—요술 같은 것에 어떻게 용서가 통할 수 있겠어!\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 이 정의를 곱씹었다. 그러다 별안간 끔찍한 웃음을 터뜨렸다—지옥에서 터지는 웃음처럼 부자연스럽고 섬뜩한 웃음을.\n\n\"그만—그만요! 그게 저를 아주 죽여요, 그 웃음이!\" 그녀가 비명을 질렀다. \"오 제게 자비를 베풀어 주세요—자비를요!\"\n\n그는 대답하지 않았다. 그러자 병자처럼 핏기 가신 얼굴로 그녀가 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"엔젤, 엔젤! 그 웃음은 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 그녀가 소리쳤다. \"이게 제게 어떤 건지 아세요?\"\n\n그는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"저는 당신을 행복하게 해 드리길 바라고, 갈망하고, 기도해 왔어요! 그렇게 하는 게 얼마나 큰 기쁨일지, 그렇게 못 하면 제가 얼마나 자격 없는 아내일지 생각해 왔다고요! 그게 제가 느껴 온 거예요, 엔젤!\"\n\n\"그건 알아.\"\n\n\"엔젤, 저는 당신이 저를 사랑한다고 생각했어요—저를, 바로 저 자신을요! 당신이 사랑하는 게 저라면, 오 어떻게 그렇게 바라보고 말할 수가 있나요? 무서워요! 한번 당신을 사랑하기 시작했으니, 저는 영원히 당신을 사랑해요—어떤 변화 속에서도, 어떤 치욕 속에서도요, 당신은 당신 자신이니까요. 저는 더 바라지 않아요. 그런데 어떻게 당신이, 오 나의 남편이, 저를 사랑하기를 멈출 수가 있나요?\"\n\n\"거듭 말하지만, 내가 사랑해 온 그 여자는 당신이 아니야.\"\n\n\"하지만 누구죠?\"\n\n\"당신 모습을 한 다른 여자야.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 말에서, 예전에 자기가 품었던 불안한 예감이 그대로 실현된 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 그녀를 일종의 사기꾼으로—순결한 여자의 탈을 쓴 죄 있는 여자로 보고 있었다. 그것을 깨닫는 순간 그녀의 창백한 얼굴에 공포가 어렸다. 그녀의 뺨은 축 늘어졌고, 입은 거의 동그란 작은 구멍 같은 모양이 되었다. 그가 자기를 그렇게 본다는 그 끔찍한 느낌이 어찌나 그녀를 마비시켰는지, 그녀가 비틀거리자 그는 그녀가 쓰러지려는 줄 알고 한 발 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"앉아, 앉아.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다. \"당신은 아파. 그러는 게 당연하지.\"\n\n그녀는 자기가 어디 있는지도 모른 채 정말로 앉았다. 그 긴장된 표정이 여전히 얼굴에 어린 채로, 그 눈은 그의 살갗에 소름이 끼치게 할 만한 것이었다.\n\n\"그럼 저는 이제 당신 사람이 아닌 거죠, 그렇죠, 엔젤?\" 그녀가 어쩔 줄 모르며 물었다. \"그가 사랑한 건 제가 아니라, 저를 닮은 다른 여자라고 하니까요.\"\n\n이렇게 떠오른 자기 모습에, 그녀는 부당하게 학대받은 사람으로서 자기 자신을 가엾이 여기게 되었다. 자기 처지를 더 깊이 헤아리자 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 가득 찼다. 그녀는 돌아앉아 자기를 가엾어하는 눈물을 봇물처럼 터뜨렸다.\n\n클레어는 이 변화에 안도했다. 일어난 일이 그녀에게 미친 영향이 그에게는 점차, 고백 그 자체의 비통함에 못지않은 근심거리가 되어 가던 참이었기 때문이다. 그는 그녀의 슬픔이 격렬함을 다 소진하고, 봇물 같던 울음이 이따금 숨이 막히는 흐느낌으로 잦아들 때까지, 참을성 있게, 무심하게 기다렸다.\n\n\"엔젤.\" 그녀가 별안간, 본래의 어조로 말했다. 공포에 질려 미친 듯 메마르던 목소리는 이제 사라지고 없었다. \"엔젤, 제가 너무 사악해서 당신과 제가 함께 살 수 없는 건가요?\"\n\n\"우리가 어떻게 해야 할지 나도 도무지 생각이 떠오르질 않아.\"\n\n\"저와 함께 살게 해 달라고 청하지 않을게요, 엔젤, 제겐 그럴 권리가 없으니까요! 하겠다고 했던 대로 어머니와 동생들에게 우리가 결혼했다는 편지를 쓰지도 않을게요. 그리고 우리가 숙소에 머무는 동안 만들려고 마름질해 둔 그 좋은 반짇고리도 마저 만들지 않을게요.\"\n\n\"안 만든다고?\"\n\n\"네, 아무것도 하지 않을게요, 당신이 시키지 않는 한요. 그리고 당신이 저를 떠나가더라도 저는 따라가지 않을게요. 당신이 다시는 제게 말을 걸지 않더라도 그 까닭을 묻지 않을게요, 당신이 물어도 된다고 하지 않는 한요.\"\n\n\"그러면 내가 너에게 무언가를 하라고 시킨다면?\"\n\n\"당신의 비참한 노예처럼 따를게요, 그게 설령 누워 죽으라는 것일지라도요.\"\n\n\"당신은 참 착하군. 하지만 내가 보기엔 지금의 그 자기희생적인 마음씨와, 지난날의 그 자기보존의 마음씨 사이엔 조화가 없는 것 같은데.\"\n\n이것이 적의가 담긴 첫마디였다. 그러나 테스에게 공들인 빈정거림을 던지는 것은 개나 고양이에게 던지는 것이나 매한가지였다. 그 교묘함의 묘미는 그녀가 음미하지 못한 채 그냥 지나가 버렸고, 그녀는 그것을 그저 노여움이 지배하고 있음을 뜻하는 적대적인 소리로만 받아들였다. 그녀는 입을 다물고 있었으니, 그가 자기를 향한 애정을 억누르고 있다는 것을 알지 못했다. 그녀는 눈물 한 방울이 그의 뺨을 타고 천천히 흘러내리는 것조차 거의 보지 못했다. 그 눈물이 어찌나 컸던지, 마치 현미경의 대물렌즈처럼, 굴러 내리는 그 살갗의 땀구멍들을 확대해 보였다. 그러는 사이, 그녀의 고백이 그의 삶에, 그의 우주에 일으킨 그 끔찍하고도 전면적인 변화에 대한 깨달음이 다시 그에게 돌아왔고, 그는 자기가 처한 그 새로운 상황 속을 헤쳐 나아가려 필사적으로 애썼다. 그에 따른 어떤 행동이 필요했다. 그런데 무엇을?\n\n\"테스.\" 그는 할 수 있는 한 부드럽게 말했다. \"나는—이 방에—지금은 있을 수가 없어. 잠깐 밖에 좀 나갔다 오겠어.\"\n\n그는 조용히 방을 나갔고, 저녁 식사를 위해 따라 두었던 포도주 두 잔은—하나는 그녀를 위해, 하나는 그를 위해—손도 대지 않은 채 식탁 위에 남았다. 둘의 애찬은 결국 이렇게 되고 만 것이다. 두어 시간 전 차를 마실 때만 해도 둘은 애정의 변덕스러운 장난기로 한 잔으로 나누어 마셨건만.\n\n아무리 살며시 닫혔다 한들, 그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히는 소리가 테스를 망연한 상태에서 깨워 냈다. 그가 가 버린 것이다. 그녀는 머물 수 없었다. 황급히 외투를 두르고 그녀는 문을 열고 그를 뒤따랐다. 다시는 돌아오지 않을 사람처럼 촛불을 꺼 버린 채로. 비는 그쳤고, 밤은 이제 맑게 개어 있었다.\n\n그녀는 이내 그의 바로 뒤까지 따라붙었으니, 클레어가 정처 없이 느릿느릿 걷고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀의 옅은 잿빛 형체 곁에서 그의 형체는 검고, 불길하고, 위압적으로 보였고, 한순간 그토록 자랑스러워했던 그 보석들의 감촉이 그녀에게는 비아냥처럼 느껴졌다. 클레어는 그녀의 발소리를 듣고 돌아섰지만, 그녀가 있다는 것을 알아챈 것이 그에게는 아무 차이도 없는 듯, 집 앞에 놓인 큰 다리의 입을 떡 벌린 다섯 아치를 넘어 계속 걸어갔다.\n\n길 위의 소와 말 발자국에는 물이 가득 차 있었으니, 비가 그것들을 채울 만큼은 내렸으나 씻어 낼 만큼은 아니었던 것이다. 그녀가 지나가자, 이 자그마한 웅덩이들 위로 비친 별들이 빠르게 스쳐 지나며 가물거렸다. 그것들이 거기 비친 것을 보지 않았더라면, 그녀는 머리 위에서 그것들이 빛나고 있다는 것조차 몰랐으리라—우주에서 가장 광대한 것들이 그토록 보잘것없는 것들 속에 비쳐 있었다.\n\n그들이 오늘 여행해 온 곳은 톨버세이즈와 같은 골짜기에 있었으나, 강을 따라 몇 마일 더 아래쪽이었다. 그리고 주위가 탁 트여 있어 그녀는 쉽사리 그를 시야에 둘 수 있었다. 집에서 멀어지자 길은 풀밭 사이로 굽이굽이 이어졌고, 그녀는 그 길을 따라 클레어를 뒤따랐다. 그를 따라잡으려는, 또는 그의 주의를 끌려는 어떤 시도도 없이, 다만 말없이 텅 빈 충실함으로.\n\n그러나 마침내 그녀의 무기력한 걸음이 그녀를 그의 곁에까지 데려갔는데도, 그는 여전히 아무 말이 없었다. 속아 넘어간 정직함의 잔인함은 진상을 알고 난 뒤에 흔히 큰 법인데, 지금 클레어의 경우 그것은 막강했다. 바깥 공기는 그에게서 충동적으로 행동하려는 경향을 죄다 앗아 가 버린 듯했다. 그가 자기를 아무런 광채도 없이—온전히 헐벗은 모습 그대로 보고 있다는 것을, 그리고 시간이 그때 자기를 향해 그 풍자의 시편을 읊조리고 있다는 것을 그녀는 알았다—\n\n보라, 그대의 얼굴이 헐벗을 때, 그대를 사랑하던 자가 미워하리라. 그대 운명이 기울 때 그대의 얼굴은 더 이상 곱지 않으리. 그대의 삶은 잎새처럼 떨어져 비처럼 흩날리리니, 그대 머리의 너울은 슬픔이 되고, 그 면류관은 고통이 되리라.\n\n그는 여전히 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 이제 그녀가 곁에 있다는 것도 그 생각의 흐름을 끊거나 돌릴 만한 힘이 모자랐다. 그에게는 그녀의 존재가 얼마나 미약한 것이 되어 버렸을까! 그녀는 클레어에게 말을 걸지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"제가 무얼 했나요—제가 대체 무얼 했나요! 저는 당신을 향한 제 사랑을 방해하거나 거짓으로 만드는 것은 하나도 말하지 않았어요. 제가 그걸 꾸미기라도 했다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠? 당신이 노여워하는 건 당신 자신의 마음속에 있는 거예요, 엔젤. 제 안에 있는 게 아니라요. 오, 그건 제 안에 있지 않아요, 그리고 저는 당신이 생각하는 그런 거짓된 여자가 아니에요!\"\n\n\"음—글쎄. 거짓되진 않지, 내 아내여. 하지만 같은 사람은 아니야. 아니, 같은 사람이 아니야. 하지만 나로 하여금 당신을 책망하게 만들진 마. 나는 그러지 않겠다고 맹세했고, 그러지 않으려고 무엇이든 하겠어.\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 정신없이 애원을 이어 갔다. 그리고 어쩌면 차라리 침묵에 남겨 두는 편이 나았을 말들을 했다.\n\n\"엔젤!—엔젤! 저는 어린아이였어요—그 일이 일어났을 때 어린아이였다고요! 저는 남자에 대해 아무것도 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 죄를 지었다기보다 죄를 당한 쪽이었어, 그건 인정해.\"\n\n\"그럼 저를 용서하지 않으시겠어요?\"\n\n\"용서는 해. 하지만 용서가 다는 아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 저를 사랑해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n이 물음에 그는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"오 엔젤—어머니 말씀이, 가끔 그런 일이 일어난다고 하셨어요!—어머니는 저보다 더한 경우도 여럿 아시는데, 남편이 그걸 그리 마음에 두지 않았다고요—적어도 극복했다고요. 게다가 그 여자들은 제가 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 남편을 사랑하지도 않았는데 말이에요!\"\n\n\"그만, 테스. 따지지 마. 사회가 다르면 풍습도 다른 거야. 당신은 거의 나로 하여금, 당신이 사회의 이치를 한 번도 깨쳐 본 적 없는 무지한 시골 여자라고 말하게 만드는군. 당신은 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 몰라.\"\n\n「난 처지가 농사꾼일 뿐이지, 천성까지 그런 건 아니에요!」\n\n테스는 발끈하려는 충동에 사로잡혀 말했지만, 그 충동은 일었던 만큼 빠르게 가라앉았다.\n\n「그럴수록 너한테는 더 안된 일이지. 네 가계를 파헤친 그 목사도 차라리 입을 다물고 있었으면 좋았을 거다. 나는 너의 집안이 몰락한 사실과 또 하나의 사실—네 의지가 굳세지 못하다는 사실을 연결 짓지 않을 수가 없어. 쇠락한 집안은 쇠락한 의지를, 쇠락한 행실을 뜻하는 법이지. 맙소사, 네 혈통을 굳이 알려서, 너를 더 경멸할 빌미를 내게 쥐여 주다니! 나는 너를 자연에서 갓 솟아난 아이로 여겼는데, 알고 보니 너는 무력해진 귀족의 뒤늦은 씨앗이었구나!」\n\n「그렇게 따지면 우리 집안만큼 형편없는 집안이 수두룩해요! 레티네도 한때는 대지주였고, 빌릿 낙농가도 그랬어요. 지금은 짐마차꾼인 데비하우스 집안도 옛날엔 드바이외 집안이었고요. 나 같은 사람은 어디에나 있어요. 이건 우리 고장의 특징인 걸요, 나로서도 어쩔 수 없는 일이에요.」\n\n「그럴수록 그 고장한테 더 안된 일이지.」\n\n테스는 이 비난들을 낱낱이 따지지 않고 그저 덩어리째로 받아들였다. 그가 더는 이제껏처럼 자신을 사랑하지 않는다는 것, 그 한 가지 외에 다른 모든 것에 그녀는 무심했다.\n\n두 사람은 다시 말없이 정처 없이 걸었다. 훗날 전해진 이야기로는, 그날 밤 늦게 의사를 부르러 나선 웰브리지의 한 오두막 사람이 목초지에서 두 연인을 만났는데, 둘은 아주 천천히 걷고 있었고, 말 한마디 없이, 마치 장례 행렬처럼 하나가 다른 하나의 뒤를 따르고 있었으며, 얼핏 본 그들의 얼굴은 근심에 차고 슬퍼 보였다고 한다. 나중에 돌아오는 길에 그는 같은 들판에서 또 그들을 지나쳤는데, 여전히 그처럼 느릿느릿 나아가고 있었고, 시각이나 음산한 밤은 아랑곳하지 않는 모습이 아까와 똑같았다. 오직 자기 일로 머릿속이 가득했던 데다 집안에 환자가 있었던 탓에, 그는 이 기묘한 광경을 마음에 담아 두지 못했지만, 한참 뒤에야 그것을 떠올렸다.\n\n그 오두막 사람이 오가는 사이에 테스는 남편에게 이렇게 말했다—\n\n「내가 평생 당신에게 큰 불행의 원인이 되는 걸 어떻게 막을 수 있을지 모르겠어요. 저기 아래 강이 있어요. 거기서 스스로 끝낼 수 있어요. 난 무섭지 않아요.」\n\n「나는 내 다른 어리석음에 살인까지 보태고 싶지는 않아.」 그가 말했다.\n\n「내가 스스로 한 일이라는 걸—내 수치 때문이었다는 걸—보여 주는 무언가를 남겨 둘게요. 그러면 사람들이 당신을 탓하지 않을 거예요.」\n\n「그런 터무니없는 소리 하지 마—듣고 싶지 않아. 이런 종류의 일에 그런 생각을 하다니 말도 안 되는 소리야. 이건 비극이라기보다 차라리 비웃을 거리에 가까운 일이라고. 너는 이 불행의 성질을 조금도 이해하지 못해. 만약 알려진다면 세상 사람 열에 아홉은 그저 농담거리로 여길 일이야. 제발 부탁이니 집으로 돌아가서 자도록 해.」\n\n「그럴게요.」 그녀가 고분고분 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 방앗간 뒤편, 잘 알려진 시토 수도원 폐허로 이어지는 길을 빙 돌아 걸어왔는데, 그 방앗간은 수백 년 전 그 수도원 시설에 딸려 있던 것이었다. 방앗간은 여전히 돌아가고 있었다. 먹을 것은 언제까지나 없어서는 안 될 것이므로. 수도원은 스러졌다. 신조는 덧없는 것이므로. 사람은 끊임없이 본다. 한때뿐인 것에 바쳐진 봉사가 영원한 것에 바쳐진 봉사보다 더 오래 살아남는 것을. 산책길이 빙 돌아온 터라 두 사람은 아직 집에서 그리 멀지 않았고, 그의 지시를 따르려면 그녀는 본류를 가로지르는 큰 돌다리에 닿아 몇 야드만 길을 따라가면 되었다. 그녀가 돌아왔을 때, 모든 것이 떠날 때 그대로였고, 불은 아직 타고 있었다. 그녀는 아래층에 일 분도 채 머물지 않고 곧장 자기 방으로 올라갔는데, 짐은 그리로 옮겨져 있었다. 그녀는 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉아 멍하니 주위를 둘러보다가, 이윽고 옷을 벗기 시작했다. 등불을 침대 쪽으로 가져가자 그 빛이 흰 무명 천개에 비쳤는데, 그 아래 무언가가 매달려 있어, 그녀는 그게 무엇인지 보려고 촛불을 들어 올렸다. 겨우살이 가지였다. 에인절이 거기 매달아 둔 것이었다. 그녀는 단번에 알아챘다. 꾸려서 가져오기가 그토록 어려웠던 그 수수께끼 같은 꾸러미의 정체가 바로 이것이었다. 그는 그 속에 든 것을 그녀에게 설명하려 하지 않으면서, 머지않아 시간이 그 용도를 알려 줄 거라고만 했었다. 들뜬 마음에, 흥겨운 기분에 그는 그것을 거기 매달아 둔 것이었다. 지금 그 겨우살이는 어쩌면 그리 어리석고 때아니게 보이던지.\n\n이제 더 두려워할 것도 없고, 바랄 것도 거의 없었으므로—그가 마음을 누그러뜨리리라는 약속은 어디에도 없는 듯했으므로—그녀는 맥없이 자리에 누웠다. 슬픔이 더는 골똘함이 아니게 될 때, 잠은 제 기회를 엿본다. 안식을 가로막는 그 숱한 행복한 마음결 가운데, 이것은 안식을 반기는 마음결이었으니, 몇 분 만에 외로운 테스는 존재를 잊었다. 어쩌면 한때 제 조상들의 신방이었을지도 모를 그 방의, 향내 어린 고요에 둘러싸인 채.\n\n그날 밤 늦게 클레어 역시 집으로 발길을 되돌렸다. 거실로 살그머니 들어가 불을 밝히고는, 제 갈 길을 곰곰이 따져 본 사람다운 태도로, 거기 놓인 낡은 말총 소파 위에 깔개를 펼쳐 대충 잠자리 모양으로 다듬었다. 눕기 전에 그는 신을 벗고 살금살금 위층으로 올라가, 그녀의 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였다. 고르게 들려오는 그녀의 숨소리는 그녀가 깊이 잠들었음을 알려 주었다.\n\n「고맙기도 하지!」 클레어가 중얼거렸다. 그러면서도 그는 한 가지 생각에 쓰라림이 욱신 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다—전적으로 옳지는 않으나 대체로는 옳은 그 생각, 곧 그녀가 제 삶의 짐을 그의 어깨로 옮겨 놓고서 이제 근심 없이 쉬고 있다는 생각에.\n\n그는 내려가려고 돌아섰다가, 마음을 정하지 못하고 다시 그녀의 방문 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그 순간 그는 더버빌 가문의 한 귀부인이 눈에 들어왔는데, 그 초상화는 테스의 침실 입구 바로 위에 걸려 있었다. 촛불 빛 아래에서 그 그림은 그저 불쾌한 정도를 넘어섰다. 그 여인의 이목구비에는 불길한 의도가 도사리고 있었으니, 이성異性을 향한 복수의 결연한 작심이었다—적어도 그때 그에게는 그렇게 보였다. 초상화의 캐롤라인 시대풍 보디스는 깊이 파여 있었다—테스가 목걸이를 보여 주려고 옷깃을 접어 넣었을 때와 꼭 같이. 그리하여 그는 다시금 두 사람이 닮았다는 괴로운 느낌을 받았다.\n\n그 제동만으로 충분했다. 그는 다시 물러나 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n그의 태도는 여전히 차분하고 차가웠으며, 작고 꾹 다문 입은 그의 자제력을 그대로 드러냈다. 그의 얼굴에는 그녀의 고백 이후로 번져 든 그 끔찍한 메마른 표정이 아직 어려 있었다. 그것은 이제 더는 정념의 노예가 아니면서도, 그 해방에서 아무런 득도 찾지 못한 사내의 얼굴이었다. 그는 그저 인간 경험의 가슴 쓰린 우연들을, 일들의 뜻밖임을 바라보고 있을 뿐이었다. 한 시간 전까지, 그가 그녀를 흠모해 온 그 길고 긴 세월 내내, 테스만큼 순결하고, 달콤하고, 처녀다운 존재가 있으리라고는 도무지 여겨지지 않았다. 그러나—\n\n조금만 모자라도, 얼마나 아득히 다른 세계인가!\n\n그녀의 마음이 그 정직하고 싱싱한 얼굴에 그대로 드러나 있지 않다고 혼잣말로 단정했을 때, 그는 잘못 생각한 것이었다. 그러나 테스에게는 그를 바로잡아 줄 변호인이 없었다. 그는 생각을 이어 갔다. 응시하는 동안 혀가 말하는 바와 조금도 어긋남을 내비친 적 없는 저 두 눈이, 그러면서도 줄곧 겉으로 드러난 세계 뒤편의 또 다른 세계를, 어긋나고 대조되는 세계를 내내 보고 있었다는 게 과연 있을 수 있는 일일까?\n\n그는 거실의 잠자리에 몸을 기대고 불을 껐다. 밤이 들어와 거기 제자리를 잡았다. 무심하고 아랑곳없이. 이미 그의 행복을 삼켜 버리고 이제 그것을 시큰둥하게 소화하고 있는 밤, 다른 수천 사람의 행복도 그처럼 거의 흐트러짐 없이, 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않고 삼켜 버릴 채비가 된 밤이었다.\n\n# XXXVI\n\n클레어는, 마치 범죄와 결부되기라도 한 듯 잿빛으로 음흉하게 트는 새벽빛 속에서 일어났다. 벽난로는 꺼져 버린 잉걸불을 안은 채 그를 마주했다. 차려진 저녁 식탁 위에는 입도 대지 않은 포도주가 두 잔 가득 채워진 채, 이제 김 빠지고 막이 낀 채로 놓여 있었다. 비어 버린 그녀의 자리와 그 자신의 자리, 그리고 다른 세간들은, 어쩔 수 없다는 그 영원한 표정을 띠고서, 어떻게 해야 하느냐는 그 견디기 힘든 물음을 던지고 있었다. 위층에서는 아무 소리도 없었다. 그러나 몇 분 뒤 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 그는 그것이 두 사람이 여기 머무는 동안 시중을 들어 주기로 한 이웃 오두막 아낙임을 떠올렸다.\n\n지금 같은 때에 집 안에 제삼자가 있다는 건 몹시 거북한 일이라, 그는 이미 옷을 차려입은 터라 창문을 열고 그녀에게 오늘 아침은 자기들끼리 어떻게든 해 보겠다고 일러 주었다. 그녀가 손에 우유통을 들고 있어, 그는 문간에 두고 가라고 했다. 아낙이 가 버린 뒤 그는 집 뒤편을 뒤져 땔감을 찾아 금세 불을 지폈다. 식료품 저장실에는 달걀과 버터, 빵 따위가 넉넉히 있어, 클레어는 이내 아침상을 차렸으니, 낙농장에서 쌓은 경험 덕에 집안일 채비에 손이 익숙해진 까닭이었다. 지펴진 장작 연기가 바깥 굴뚝에서 연잎 모양의 기둥처럼 피어올랐다. 지나가던 동네 사람들이 그것을 보고는 갓 결혼한 부부를 떠올리며 그들의 행복을 부러워했다.\n\n에인절은 마지막으로 방 안을 한 번 둘러보고는, 계단 아래로 가서 의례적인 목소리로 외쳤다—\n\n「아침 준비됐어요!」\n\n그는 현관문을 열고 아침 공기 속으로 몇 걸음 내디뎠다. 잠시 후 돌아왔을 때 그녀는 이미 거실에서 기계적으로 아침상을 다시 매만지고 있었다. 그녀가 옷을 빈틈없이 갖춰 입은 데다, 그가 부른 뒤로 흐른 시간이 고작 이삼 분에 지나지 않았으므로, 그가 부르러 가기 전에 이미 옷을 다 입었거나 거의 다 입은 상태였음이 분명했다. 그녀의 머리는 뒤통수에 크고 둥글게 틀어 올려져 있었고, 새 옷 가운데 하나를—목둘레에 흰 주름 장식이 달린 옅은 파란색 모직 옷을—입고 있었다. 그녀의 손과 얼굴은 차가워 보였으니, 어쩌면 불기 하나 없는 침실에서 옷을 입은 채로 오래도록 앉아 있었던 모양이었다. 그녀를 부르던 클레어의 두드러지게 예의 바른 말투가 그 순간 그녀에게 한 가닥 새로운 희망의 빛을 불어넣어 준 듯했다. 그러나 그를 바라보자 그 희망은 이내 스러졌다.\n\n사실 두 사람은 한때 타올랐던 불의 잿더미일 뿐이었다. 지난밤의 뜨거운 슬픔에 뒤이어 무거운 짓눌림이 찾아들었으니, 마치 이제 그 무엇도 두 사람 가운데 누구도 감각의 열기로 되살려 낼 수 없을 듯했다.\n\n그는 그녀에게 부드럽게 말했고, 그녀도 그처럼 담담하게 대답했다. 마침내 그녀가 그에게 다가와, 자신의 얼굴 또한 눈에 보이는 대상이라는 걸 의식하지 못하는 사람처럼, 또렷하게 윤곽 잡힌 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n「에인절!」 그녀는 말하다가 멈추고는, 한때 자신의 연인이었던 그 사내가 정말 여기 살아 있는 게 도무지 믿기지 않는다는 듯, 산들바람처럼 가볍게 손가락으로 그를 건드렸다. 그녀의 두 눈은 빛났고, 창백한 뺨은 여전히 여느 때의 둥그스름함을 띠고 있었으나, 반쯤 마른 눈물이 그 위에 반짝이는 자국을 남겨 두었다. 그리고 평소 같으면 무르익은 붉은빛이던 입술은 뺨만큼이나 핏기가 가셔 있었다. 그토록 생생히 살아 떨고 있으면서도, 마음의 슬픔에 짓눌린 탓에 그 생명의 박동이 어찌나 끊길 듯하던지, 거기에 조금만 더 힘을 가하면 정말로 병이 들어, 그 특유의 눈빛이 흐려지고 입술이 야위어질 것만 같았다.\n\n그녀는 더없이 순결해 보였다. 자연은, 그 변덕스러운 장난질로, 테스의 얼굴에 처녀성의 도장을 어찌나 또렷이 찍어 놓았던지, 그는 얼이 빠진 듯한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n「테스! 사실이 아니라고 말해 줘! 아니야, 사실일 리 없어!」\n\n「사실이에요.」\n\n「한마디 한마디 다?」\n\n「한마디 한마디 다요.」\n\n그는 애원하듯 그녀를 바라보았으니, 거짓인 줄 뻔히 알면서도 기꺼이 그녀의 입에서 거짓말을 받아 들고는, 어떤 궤변으로든 그것을 그럴듯한 부인否認으로 만들어 내고 싶다는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀는 그저 되풀이할 뿐이었다—\n\n「사실이에요.」\n\n「그자는 살아 있어?」 에인절이 그제야 물었다.\n\n「아기는 죽었어요.」\n\n「하지만 그 사내 말이야?」\n\n「살아 있어요.」\n\n마지막 절망이 클레어의 얼굴을 스쳐 갔다.\n\n「영국에 있어?」\n\n「네.」\n\n그는 정처 없이 몇 걸음 옮겼다.\n\n「내 처지가—이렇군.」 그가 불쑥 말했다. 「나는 생각했어—누구라도 그렇게 생각했을 거야—사회적 지위와 재산과 세상 물정에 밝은 여자를 얻겠다는 야심을 죄다 버리면, 발그레한 뺨을 손에 넣는 것만큼이나 틀림없이 시골의 순결함도 손에 넣으리라고. 그런데—그러나, 나는 너를 탓할 사람이 못 돼. 탓하지 않겠어.」\n\n테스는 그의 처지를 너무도 온전히 헤아린 터라 나머지 말은 필요치도 않았다. 바로 거기에 그 괴로움이 있었으니, 그녀는 그가 사방으로 모든 것을 잃었음을 알아본 것이다.\n\n「에인절—나도 결국 당신에게 마지막 빠져나갈 길이 하나 있다는 걸 몰랐다면 당신과의 결혼까지 일을 끌고 가지는 않았을 거예요. 다만 당신이 결코 그러지 않기를 바랐을 뿐이지—」\n\n그녀의 목소리가 잠겨 들었다.\n\n「마지막 길이라니?」\n\n「내 말은, 나를 떼어 버리는 길 말이에요. 당신은 나를 떼어 버릴 수 있어요.」\n\n「어떻게?」\n\n「나와 이혼하면 되죠.」\n\n「세상에—어쩌면 그리도 단순하니! 내가 어떻게 너와 이혼을 한단 말이냐?」\n\n「못 해요—이제 다 말했는데도? 난 내 고백이 당신에게 그럴 근거가 되어 줄 줄 알았어요.」\n\n「오 테스—너는 너무, 너무도—어린애 같구나—덜 여물고—풋내가 나, 그래. 네가 뭔지 도무지 모르겠다. 너는 법이라는 걸 몰라—너는 모른다고!」\n\n「뭐라고요—그게 안 된다고요?」\n\n「정말 안 돼.」\n\n이 말을 듣는 그녀의 얼굴에는 비참함에 다급한 수치가 뒤섞였다.\n\n「난 그런 줄—그런 줄 알았어요.」 그녀가 속삭였다. 「오, 이제야 내가 당신 눈에 얼마나 못된 여자로 보일지 알겠어요! 믿어 줘요—제발 믿어 줘요, 내 영혼을 걸고, 난 정말 당신이 그럴 수 있다고만 여겼어요! 당신이 그러지 않기를 바랐지만, 그래도, 당신이 마음만 굳게 먹고 나를 조금도—조금도 사랑하지 않는다면, 나를 내칠 수 있다고 한 점 의심 없이 믿었어요!」\n\n「네 생각이 틀렸어.」 그가 말했다.\n\n「오, 그렇다면 내가 했어야 했어요, 어젯밤에 해 버렸어야 했어요! 그런데 용기가 없었어요. 그게 꼭 나다워요!」\n\n「무슨 용기 말이냐?」\n\n그녀가 대답하지 않자 그는 그녀의 손을 잡았다.\n\n「무엇을 하려고 했던 거야?」 그가 캐물었다.\n\n「스스로 끝내 버리려고요.」\n\n「언제?」\n\n그녀는 추궁하는 듯한 그의 말투에 몸을 비틀었다. 「어젯밤에요.」 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n「어디서?」\n\n「당신이 매달아 둔 겨우살이 아래에서요.」\n\n「맙소사—! 어떻게?」 그가 준엄하게 물었다.\n\n「화내지 않겠다고 하면 말할게요!」 그녀가 움츠리며 말했다. 「내 가방의 끈으로 하려고 했어요. 그런데 못 했어요—그 마지막 짓을! 그게 당신 이름에 추문을 끼칠까 봐 무서웠어요.」\n\n자청해서가 아니라 쥐어짜듯 끌려 나온 이 고백의 뜻밖임에 그는 눈에 띄게 흔들렸다. 그러나 그는 여전히 그녀를 붙든 채, 그녀의 얼굴에서 아래로 시선을 떨구며 말했다. 「자, 잘 들어. 너는 절대로 그런 끔찍한 일을 생각해서는 안 돼! 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어! 네 남편으로서 약속을 받아 두겠다, 다시는 그런 시도를 하지 않겠다고.」\n\n「약속할게요. 그게 얼마나 못된 짓인지 알았어요.」\n\n「못된 짓이지! 그런 생각은 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 너답지 못한 거야.」\n\n「하지만, 에인절,」 그녀는 차분히 아랑곳없는 표정으로 두 눈을 크게 뜨고 그를 보며 호소했다. 「그건 오로지 당신을 위해 떠올린 거예요—당신이 받아야만 하리라 여겼던 그 이혼의 추문 없이 당신을 자유롭게 해 주려고요. 나 자신을 위해서라면 꿈에도 그럴 생각을 못 했을 거예요. 하지만, 따지고 보면 내 손으로 그러는 건 나한테 분에 넘치는 일이에요. 그 일격을 가해야 할 사람은 바로 당신이에요, 나로 인해 망가진 내 남편이. 만약 당신이 마음을 다잡고 그렇게 해 줄 수만 있다면, 그럴 수만 있다면, 나는 당신을 더—더 사랑하게 될 것 같아요. 당신한테 달리 빠져나갈 길이 없으니까요. 내가 너무도 쓸모없는 존재라는 게 느껴져요! 너무도 거치적거리기만 하는 존재라는 게요!」\n\n「쉿!」\n\n「그래요, 당신이 안 된다고 하니, 안 할게요. 난 당신 뜻에 어긋나는 바람은 없어요.」\n\n그는 이 말이 충분히 진실임을 알았다. 지난밤의 절망 이후로 그녀의 활기는 바닥까지 떨어졌고, 더 두려워할 무모함도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n테스는 다시 아침 식탁 일에 그런대로 분주히 매달려 보려 했고, 두 사람은 같은 쪽에 나란히 앉아 서로 시선이 마주치지 않게 했다. 처음에는 서로 먹고 마시는 소리를 듣는 것이 어딘가 거북했으나, 이건 피할 수 없는 노릇이었다. 게다가 두 사람 다 먹는 양은 얼마 되지 않았다. 아침을 마치자 그는 일어나, 저녁 식사 때쯤 돌아올 거라고 일러 두고는, 여기 온 단 하나의 실질적인 이유였던, 그 방앗간 일을 배운다는 계획을 기계적으로 좇아 방앗간 주인에게로 떠났다.\n\n그가 가 버린 뒤 테스는 창가에 서 있다가, 이윽고 방앗간 부지로 이어지는 큰 돌다리를 건너는 그의 모습을 보았다. 그는 다리 너머로 가라앉듯 사라졌고, 그 너머 철길을 건너 자취를 감추었다. 그러자 그녀는 한숨도 쉬지 않고 방 안으로 마음을 돌려, 식탁을 치우고 정돈하기 시작했다.\n\n품팔이 아낙이 곧 왔다. 그녀가 곁에 있는 것이 처음에는 테스에게 긴장이었으나, 나중에는 위안이 되었다. 열두 시 반에 테스는 부엌에 그 일손을 혼자 남겨 두고 거실로 돌아와, 다리 너머로 에인절의 모습이 다시 나타나기를 기다렸다.\n\n한 시쯤 그가 모습을 드러냈다. 그가 사 분의 일 마일이나 떨어져 있었는데도 그녀의 얼굴은 발그레해졌다. 그녀는 그가 들어올 때까지 식사를 차려 내려고 부엌으로 달려갔다. 그는 먼저 어제 둘이 함께 손을 씻었던 방으로 갔고, 거실로 들어서자 접시 뚜껑들이 마치 그 자신의 손짓에 따르듯 접시에서 들려 올라갔다.\n「어쩌면 이리 시간을 잘 맞췄을까!」 그가 말했다.\n\n「네. 다리를 건너오는 걸 봤거든요.」 그녀가 말했다.\n\n식사 시간은 그가 오전에 애비밀에서 한 일에 관한 평범한 이야기로 흘러갔다. 가루를 체로 치는 방법이며 구식 기계 이야기였는데, 그는 그것이 근래의 개량된 방식을 깨치는 데 별로 도움이 되지 않을까 걱정스러워했다. 그중 일부는 옆에 딸린 수도원 건물들에서—지금은 한 무더기 폐허가 된 그곳에서—수도사들을 위해 곡식을 빻던 시절부터 줄곧 쓰여 온 듯했다. 그는 한 시간쯤 지나 다시 집을 나섰다가 해 질 녘에 돌아와, 저녁 내내 자기 서류들을 들여다보며 시간을 보냈다. 그녀는 자신이 거치적거리는 게 아닐까 두려워, 늙은 아낙이 가 버리자 부엌으로 물러나, 한 시간 넘게 할 수 있는 한 분주히 몸을 놀렸다.\n\n클레어의 모습이 문간에 나타났다. 「이렇게 일하면 안 돼.」 그가 말했다. 「너는 내 하녀가 아니야. 내 아내라고.」\n\n그녀는 눈을 들었고, 다소 얼굴이 환해졌다. 「내가 그렇게 여겨도—정말요?」 그녀는 애처로운 비웃음을 담아 중얼거렸다. 「이름뿐이라는 거겠죠! 뭐, 나도 그 이상은 바라지 않아요.」\n\n「그렇게 여겨도 된다니까, 테스! 너는 그래. 무슨 소리를 하는 거야?」\n\n「모르겠어요.」 그녀는 목소리에 눈물을 담은 채 다급하게 말했다. 「난 내가—그러니까 내가 떳떳하지 못하니까 그런 줄 알았어요. 한참 전에 이미 말했잖아요, 내가 당신과 결혼할 만큼 떳떳하지 못한 것 같다고—그래서 당신과 결혼하고 싶지 않았던 거예요, 다만—다만 당신이 졸랐잖아요!」\n\n그녀는 흐느낌을 터뜨리며 그에게 등을 돌렸다. 에인절 클레어가 아닌 어떤 사내라도 그 모습에 마음이 거의 돌아섰을 것이다. 평소에는 그토록 부드럽고 다정한 그였지만, 그 본바탕의 아득한 깊은 곳에는, 물러진 양토壤土 속의 금속 광맥처럼, 단단한 논리의 침전물이 숨어 있어, 그것을 가로지르려는 모든 것의 날을 무디게 만들었다. 그것은 그가 교회를 받아들이는 것을 가로막았고, 테스를 받아들이는 것도 가로막았다. 게다가 그의 애정 자체가 불길이라기보다는 차라리 광채에 가까웠으니, 이성異性에 관해서라면, 믿기를 그치는 순간 그는 좇기도 그쳤다. 이 점에서 그는, 지성으로는 경멸하는 것에 관능적으로는 여전히 빠져 있는 그 숱한 마음 여린 천성들과 사뭇 대비되었다. 그는 그녀의 흐느낌이 그칠 때까지 기다렸다.\n\n「영국 여자의 절반이라도 너만큼 떳떳했으면 좋겠다.」 그는 여자라는 족속 전체를 향한 쓰라림이 부글거리듯 솟구쳐 말했다. 「이건 떳떳하냐 마냐의 문제가 아니라, 원칙의 문제야!」\n\n그는 이런 따위의 말들을, 그리고 이와 비슷한 부류의 말들을 더 그녀에게 했으니, 한번 그 시선이 겉모습에 우롱당했다 싶으면 그토록 끈질기게 곧은 영혼을 비틀어 놓는, 그 반감의 물결에 여전히 휩쓸려 있던 까닭이었다. 사실 그 밑바닥에는 동정의 역류逆流가 흐르고 있어, 세상 물정에 밝은 여자라면 그것을 타고 그를 정복할 수도 있었으리라. 그러나 테스는 이런 것을 생각하지 못했다. 그녀는 모든 것을 제가 받아 마땅한 벌로 여기며 거의 입을 열지 않았다. 그를 향한 그녀의 헌신은 그 한결같음이 가히 애처로울 지경이었다. 천성이 발끈하기 쉬운 그녀였건만, 그가 무슨 말을 해도 그녀는 추한 꼴을 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 제 잇속을 구하지 않았고, 노여워하지 않았으며, 그의 처사를 두고 악한 생각을 품지 않았다. 그녀는 마치 사도 바울이 말한 그 자애가, 제 잇속만 구하는 현대 세상으로 되돌아온 모습 그대로인 듯했다.\n\n이날 저녁과 밤, 그리고 아침은 앞선 날들이 지나간 그대로 똑같이 지나갔다. 단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 그녀는—한때 그토록 자유롭고 거리낌 없던 테스는—감히 먼저 다가가 보려 했다. 그가 식사를 마치고 밀가루 방앗간에 가려고 세 번째로 자리에서 일어설 때였다. 식탁을 떠나며 그가 「잘 있어」라고 하자, 그녀도 똑같은 말로 대답하면서 동시에 그가 입 맞춰 주기를 바라듯 입술을 그의 쪽으로 기울였다. 그러나 그는 그 청을 받아들이지 않고, 황급히 곁으로 비켜서며 말했다—\n\n「제때 들어올게.」\n\n테스는 매라도 맞은 듯 자기 안으로 움츠러들었다. 그는 그녀의 뜻을 거슬러서까지 그 입술에 닿으려 한 적이 숱하게 많았고, 그녀가 주로 먹고 사는 버터와 달걀과 우유와 꿀맛이 그녀의 입과 숨결에서 난다고, 그것에서 자기가 양분을 얻는다고, 그런 따위의 어리석은 말들을 흥겹게 늘어놓은 적도 숱하게 많았다. 그러나 이제 그는 그것에 마음이 없었다. 그는 그녀가 문득 움츠러드는 것을 보고는, 부드럽게 말했다—\n\n「알다시피, 나는 앞으로 어떻게 할지 궁리를 해야 해. 우리가 당장 갈라서면 너에게 추문이 일었을 테니, 그걸 피하려고 얼마간 함께 지내는 게 꼭 필요했던 거야. 하지만 이건 어디까지나 형식을 차리기 위한 것일 뿐임을 너도 알아야 해.」\n\n「네.」 테스가 멍하니 말했다.\n\n그는 밖으로 나갔다가, 방앗간으로 가는 길에 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 좀 더 다정하게 응해 줄 걸, 적어도 한 번쯤은 그녀에게 입을 맞춰 줄 걸 하고 한순간 바랐다.\n\n이렇게 두 사람은 이 절망스러운 하루 이틀을 견디며 지냈다. 한집에 있는 것은 참으로 사실이었으나, 연인이 되기 전보다도 더 멀리 떨어진 채였다. 그가 말한 그대로, 어떻게 일을 처리할지 궁리하느라 활기가 마비된 채 지내고 있다는 것이 그녀의 눈에도 뚜렷이 보였다. 그녀는 그토록 유연해 보이는 겉모습 아래 그런 단호함이 도사리고 있음을 알아채고는 두려움에 사로잡혔다. 그의 한결같음은 참으로 너무도 모질었다. 그녀는 이제 더는 용서를 기대하지 않았다. 그가 방앗간에 가 있는 동안 그를 떠나 버릴까 하고 한두 번 넘게 생각했으나, 만약 그것이 알려지면 그에게 득이 되기는커녕 오히려 그를 더 옭아매고 욕보이는 빌미가 될까 두려웠다.\n\n한편 클레어는 참으로, 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그의 생각은 한순간도 멎지 않았으니, 그는 생각하느라 병이 들어 갔다. 생각에 파먹히고, 생각에 시들어, 한때의 그 고동치고 나긋나긋하던 가정적인 정다움을 죄다 채찍질당해 잃었다. 그는 「어떻게 해야 하나—어떻게 해야 하나?」 하고 혼잣말하며 이리저리 거닐었는데, 우연히 그녀가 그 말을 엿들었다. 그것이 그녀로 하여금, 이제껏 둘 사이에 깔려 있던 미래에 관한 침묵을 깨뜨리게 만들었다.\n\n「설마—당신이 나와—오래 살지는 않을 거죠, 그렇죠, 에인절?」 그녀가 물었는데, 푹 꺼진 입가가, 그 얼굴에 단련된 평온의 표정을 붙잡아 두는 수단이 얼마나 순전히 기계적인 것인지를 그대로 드러냈다.\n\n「나는 그럴 수가 없어.」 그가 말했다. 「내 자신을 경멸하지 않고서는, 더 나쁘게는 어쩌면 너를 경멸하지 않고서는 말이야. 물론 내 말은, 흔한 의미에서 너와 함께 살 수가 없다는 거야. 지금은, 내가 무엇을 느끼든, 너를 경멸하지는 않아. 그리고, 솔직히 말하게 해 줘, 안 그러면 너는 내 어려움을 다 알지 못할 테니. 그 사내가 살아 있는 동안 우리가 어떻게 함께 살 수 있겠어?—천성으로 따지면 그자가 네 남편이지, 내가 아니야. 만약 그가 죽었다면 사정이 다를지도 몰라… 게다가 그게 어려움의 전부가 아니야. 또 다른 데에 어려움이 있어—우리 자신이 아닌 다른 사람들의 장래에 걸린 문제 말이야. 앞으로 닥쳐올 세월을 생각해 봐, 우리에게 아이들이 태어나고, 이 지난 일이 알려지는 것을—그건 알려지고야 말 테니까. 이 땅 끝 어느 구석이라도, 누군가 거기서 나오거나 다른 데서 거기로 들어가지 않는 곳은 없어. 자, 생각해 봐. 우리 살과 피를 나눈 가엾은 것들이, 비웃음을 받으며 자라나, 나이를 먹어 갈수록 그 비웃음의 온전한 무게를 차츰 절감하게 되는 것을. 그 애들에게 그게 얼마나 끔찍한 깨달음이겠어! 얼마나 막막한 앞날이겠어! 이런 일이 닥칠 수 있음을 곰곰이 생각해 보고도 너는 떳떳하게 ‘함께 있어요’라고 말할 수 있겠어? 우리가 이미 겪고 있는 괴로움을 견디는 편이, 다른 괴로움으로 달아나는 것보다 낫지 않겠어?」\n\n근심에 짓눌린 그녀의 눈꺼풀은 아까처럼 계속 내리깔린 채였다.\n\n「‘함께 있어요’라고는 말 못 하겠어요.」 그녀가 대답했다. 「못 하겠어요. 거기까지는 미처 생각하지 못했어요.」\n\n테스의 여자다운 희망은—고백하자면—어찌나 끈질기게 되살아나는 것이었던지, 한집에서 살을 맞대고 지내는 정다움이 오래도록 이어진다면, 그의 판단을 거슬러서라도 그 냉담함을 끝내 허물어뜨리리라는, 남몰래 품은 환상으로 그녀 안에서 다시 살아났다. 흔히 말하는 의미에서 닳지 않은 그녀였지만, 그렇다고 모자란 것은 아니었다. 가까이 붙어 있다는 것에 얼마나 큰 설득의 힘이 깃들어 있는지를 본능으로 알지 못했다면, 그건 오히려 여자다움의 결핍을 뜻했으리라. 만약 이것이 실패한다면 그녀를 구할 다른 길은 없음을 그녀는 알았다. 책략의 본성을 띤 것에 희망을 거는 건 그릇된 일이라고 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. 그러면서도 그런 종류의 희망을 그녀는 꺼 버릴 수가 없었다. 그의 마지막 진술은 이제 다 펼쳐졌고, 그것은 그녀의 말마따나 새로운 관점이었다. 그녀는 정말로 거기까지는 한 번도 생각해 보지 못했으니, 자신을 멸시할 자식들이 태어날 수 있다는 그의 또렷한 그림은, 그 속까지 인정에 넘치는 정직한 마음에 죽음 같은 확신을 안겨 주는 것이었다. 순전한 경험이 이미 그녀에게 가르쳐 준 바, 어떤 처지에서는 착하게 사는 것보다 더 나은 한 가지가 있었으니, 그것은 곧 어떤 삶이든 아예 살게 되는 일에서 구원받는 것이었다. 고통으로 앞일을 내다보게 된 모든 이가 그렇듯, 그녀도 쉴리프뤼돔 씨의 말마따나 「너는 태어나리라」라는 그 명령 속에서 형벌의 선고를 들을 수 있었다. 특히 그것이 제 몸에서 나올 수도 있는 자식을 향한 것이라면 더더욱.\n\n그러나 어머니 자연이 어찌나 여우같이 교활한지, 지금껏 테스는 클레어를 향한 사랑에 눈이 멀어, 그 사랑이 새 생명들을 낳을 수 있고, 그리하여 제가 스스로 불행이라 한탄했던 것을 다른 이들에게 떠안길 수 있다는 사실을 잊고 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러므로 그녀는 그의 논리를 당해 낼 수 없었다. 그러나 지나치게 예민한 자들 특유의, 스스로와 싸우는 성향 탓에, 그에 대한 반박이 클레어 자신의 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 그는 그것이 거의 두렵기까지 했다. 그것은 그녀의 남다른 신체적 천성에 근거한 것이었으니, 그녀는 그것을 가망 있게 써먹을 수도 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 이렇게 덧붙일 수도 있었으리라. 「오스트레일리아의 고원이나 텍사스의 평원에서라면, 누가 내 불행을 알거나 아랑곳하겠어요, 누가 나나 당신을 탓하겠어요?」 그러나 대다수 여자가 그렇듯, 그녀는 그 한순간의 모습을 마치 피할 수 없는 것인 양 받아들였다. 어쩌면 그녀가 옳았을지도 모른다. 여자의 직관적인 마음은 제 자신의 쓰라림뿐 아니라 제 남편의 쓰라림까지도 안다. 설령 이렇게 가정된 비난들이 낯선 이들의 입에서 그나 그의 자식들을 향해 던져질 가망이 없다 해도, 그것은 그 자신의 까다로운 머릿속에서 그의 귀에까지 닿았을 것이다.\n\n소원해진 지 사흘째 되는 날이었다. 더 짐승 같은 데가 있었더라면 그가 더 고결한 사내였으리라는 그 묘한 역설을 누군가는 무릅쓰고 내놓을지도 모른다. 우리는 그렇게 말하지는 않겠다. 그러나 클레어의 사랑이 흠이 될 만큼 너무 영적이고, 실행에 옮길 수 없을 만큼 너무 관념적이었음은 의심할 여지가 없다. 이런 천성을 지닌 이들에게는, 몸이 곁에 있는 것이 몸이 곁에 없는 것보다 어쩐지 덜 끌리는 법이다. 후자는 실제의 결점들을 편리하게 떨궈 낸 이상적인 현존을 빚어내는 까닭이다. 그녀는 자신의 인격이 예상했던 만큼 강하게 제 처지를 호소해 주지 못함을 깨달았다. 그 비유적인 표현은 사실이었다. 그녀는 한때 그의 욕망을 불러일으켰던 그 여자와는 다른 여자였다.\n\n「당신이 한 말을 곰곰이 생각해 봤어요.」 그녀가 그에게 말했는데, 한 손가락을 식탁보 위로 문지르면서, 두 사람 모두를 조롱하는 그 반지를 낀 다른 한 손으로는 이마를 받치고 있었다. 「다 옳은 말이에요, 전부 다. 틀림없이 그래요. 당신은 내게서 떠나야 해요.」\n\n「하지만 너는 어떻게 하려고?」\n\n「난 집으로 가면 돼요.」\n\n클레어는 거기까지는 생각하지 못했다.\n\n「정말이야?」 그가 물었다.\n\n「정말이에요. 우린 갈라서야 해요. 그러니 어서 끝내 버리는 게 나아요. 당신이 전에 그랬잖아요, 내가 남자들을 그들의 더 나은 판단을 거슬러 끌어당기는 재주가 있다고. 만약 내가 늘 당신 눈앞에 있으면, 당신의 이성과 바람을 거슬러 당신의 계획을 바꾸게 만들지도 몰라요. 그러면 나중에 당신의 뉘우침과 나의 슬픔은 끔찍할 거예요.」\n\n「그러니까 너는 집으로 가고 싶다는 거지?」 그가 물었다.\n\n「난 당신을 떠나서 집으로 가고 싶어요.」\n\n「그렇다면 그렇게 하자.」\n\n그녀는 그를 올려다보지는 않았으나, 흠칫했다. 제안과 약조 사이에는 차이가 있었고, 그녀는 그것을 너무도 빠르게 느낀 것이다.\n\n「이렇게 되고 말 줄 알았어요.」 그녀는 얼굴을 고분고분 굳힌 채 중얼거렸다. 「난 원망하지 않아요, 에인절, 나—나도 이게 최선이라고 생각해요. 당신이 한 말에 난 완전히 수긍했어요. 그래요, 우리가 함께 지낸다 해도 다른 누구도 나를 탓하지 않는다 칠지라도, 그래도 언젠가, 몇 해가 지난 뒤, 당신은 어떤 사소한 일로 내게 화를 낼 수도 있고, 내 지난 일을 잘 아는 당신이, 그만 모진 말을 내뱉고 싶어질지도 몰라요. 그리고 그 말을, 어쩌면 내 자식들이 엿들을 수도 있고요. 오, 지금은 그저 나를 아프게만 하는 일이 그때는 나를 모질게 괴롭히고 죽일 거예요! 갈게요—내일.」\n\n「나도 여기 머물지 않을 거야. 내가 먼저 꺼내고 싶지는 않았지만, 우리가 갈라서는 게 마땅하다는 걸 알게 됐어—적어도 한동안은, 일이 어떤 모양새로 흘러갔는지 내가 좀 더 잘 헤아릴 수 있고, 너에게 편지를 쓸 수 있게 될 때까지는 말이야.」\n\n테스는 남편을 몰래 곁눈질했다. 그는 핏기가 가시고, 심지어 떨고 있기까지 했다. 그러나 아까처럼, 그녀는 자신이 결혼한 이 온순한 존재의 깊은 곳에서 드러난 단호함에 소스라쳤다—더 거친 감정을 더 섬세한 감정에, 실체를 관념에, 육신을 정신에 굴복시키려는 의지였다. 성벽도, 성향도, 습관도, 그 상상력의 우위가 휘두르는 폭압적인 바람 앞에서는 마른 낙엽이나 다름없었다.\n\n그가 그녀의 시선을 알아챘는지, 그는 이렇게 설명했다—\n\n「난 사람들에게서 떨어져 있을 때 그들을 더 너그럽게 생각하게 돼.」 그러고는 냉소적으로 덧붙였다. 「누가 알겠어. 어쩌면 언젠가 우리도, 지친 나머지, 한데 어우러져 살게 될지도 모르지. 수천 명이 그렇게 해 왔으니까!」\n\n그날 그는 짐을 꾸리기 시작했고, 그녀도 위층으로 올라가 짐을 꾸리기 시작했다. 두 사람 다, 누그러뜨리는 추측의 겉치레를 자기네 행동 위에 덧입히긴 했어도, 마음속으로는 이튿날 아침 영영 갈라서게 될지도 모른다는 생각을 품고 있음을 서로 알았다. 두 사람은 무언가 마지막이라는 기미가 도는 이별이라면 그것이 곧 고문인, 그런 부류의 사람들이었던 까닭이다. 그도 알고 그녀도 알았다. 서로가 서로에게 행사해 온 그 매혹이—그녀의 경우 무슨 재주와는 무관한 매혹이—갈라선 첫 며칠 동안은 그 어느 때보다도 더 강력해지겠지만, 시간이 그 효력을 차츰 옅게 만들고야 말리라는 것을. 그녀를 한집 식구로 받아들이는 데 반대하는 그 현실적인 논거들이, 더 멀찍이 떨어진 시야의 차디찬 북극광 속에서 한층 더 강하게 제 목소리를 낼지도 모른다는 것을. 게다가 두 사람이 일단 갈라서서—함께하던 거처와 함께하던 환경을 버리고 나면—새로운 싹들이 어느새 돋아 올라 비워진 자리를 저마다 메우고, 예기치 못한 일들이 작정을 가로막으며, 옛 계획들은 잊히는 법이다.\n\n# XXXVII\n\n자정이 소리 없이 와서 지나갔으니, 프룸 골짜기에는 그것을 알릴 만한 것이 아무것도 없었다.\n\n한 시가 조금 지난 무렵, 한때 더버빌 가문의 저택이었던 어둑한 농가에서 가벼운 삐걱 소리가 났다. 위층 방을 쓰던 테스가 그 소리를 듣고 잠에서 깼다. 그것은 계단 모퉁이 디딤판에서 난 소리였는데, 그 디딤판은 늘 그렇듯 못이 헐겁게 박혀 있었다. 그녀는 침실 문이 열리고, 남편의 모습이 묘하게 조심스러운 발걸음으로 달빛 줄기를 가로지르는 것을 보았다. 그는 셔츠와 바지만 입은 차림이었는데, 그의 두 눈이 부자연스럽게 허공을 응시하고 있음을 알아채자 처음 솟구쳤던 기쁨은 스러졌다. 방 한가운데에 이르자 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 형언할 수 없이 슬픈 어조로 중얼거렸다—\n\n「죽었어! 죽었어! 죽었어!」\n\n무언가 강하게 마음을 뒤흔드는 힘이 작용하면, 클레어는 이따금 잠결에 걸어 다녔고, 심지어 기이한 짓까지 해 보이곤 했다. 결혼 직전, 장에 갔다 돌아오던 날 밤, 자기 침실에서 그녀를 모욕한 사내와 벌인 격투를 재연해 보였던 것처럼. 테스는 끊임없는 마음의 고통이 지금 그를 그런 몽유 상태로 몰아넣었음을 알아챘다.\n\n그를 향한 그녀의 변함없는 믿음은 마음속 깊은 곳에 자리 잡고 있어, 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든, 그는 그녀에게 일신상의 두려움 따위는 조금도 불러일으키지 않았다. 설령 그가 손에 권총을 들고 들어왔다 해도, 그의 보호하려는 마음에 대한 그녀의 믿음은 거의 흔들리지 않았으리라.\n\n클레어는 가까이 다가와 그녀 위로 몸을 숙였다. 「죽었어, 죽었어, 죽었어!」 그가 중얼거렸다. 가늠할 수 없는 비탄의 그 똑같은 눈길로 잠시 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라본 뒤, 그는 더 낮게 몸을 숙여 그녀를 두 팔로 감싸안고는, 마치 수의로 싸듯 침대보로 그녀를 둘둘 말았다. 그러고는 죽은 사람을 대하듯 더없이 정중하게 그녀를 침대에서 들어 올려 방을 가로질러 안고 가며, 이렇게 중얼거렸다—\n\n「가엾은, 가엾은 내 테스—내 가장 사랑스러운, 더없이 소중한 테스! 어쩌면 그리도 다정하고, 그리도 착하고, 그리도 진실한지!」\n\n깨어 있는 동안에는 그토록 모질게 거두어들였던 그 사랑의 말들이, 버림받아 굶주린 그녀의 마음에는 이루 말할 수 없이 달콤했다. 설령 지친 제 목숨을 구하기 위해서라 해도, 그녀는 몸을 뒤척이거나 발버둥 쳐서 지금 처한 이 자리를 끝장내려 하지는 않았으리라. 그리하여 그녀는 숨조차 거의 못 쉴 만큼 그저 가만히 누운 채, 그가 자신을 어떻게 하려는 걸까 궁금해하며, 층계참으로 들려 나가도록 자신을 내맡겼다.\n\n「내 아내가—죽었어, 죽었어!」 그가 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀를 안은 채 난간에 기대려고 잠시 하던 짓을 멈췄다. 그가 그녀를 아래로 내던지려는 걸까? 그녀 안에서 제 한 몸을 아끼는 마음은 거의 사그라들어 있었고, 그가 이튿날 떠날 작정임을—어쩌면 영영 떠날 작정임을—알고 있던 터라, 그녀는 이 위태로운 자세로 그의 두 팔에 안긴 채 차라리 공포보다는 황홀함에 가까운 느낌으로 누워 있었다. 둘이 함께 떨어져, 둘 다 산산조각 날 수만 있다면, 그 얼마나 어울리고, 그 얼마나 바랄 만한 일이랴.\n\n그러나 그는 그녀를 떨어뜨리지 않고, 난간의 받침을 이용해 그녀의 입술에 입을 맞췄다—낮 동안에는 멸시받던 그 입술에. 그러고는 새삼 단단히 고쳐 안고 계단을 내려갔다. 헐거운 디딤판이 삐걱거려도 그는 깨어나지 않았고, 두 사람은 무사히 아래층에 닿았다. 그는 한순간 그녀를 안은 한 손을 풀어 문빗장을 밀어 열고 밖으로 나갔는데, 양말만 신은 발가락이 문 모서리에 살짝 부딪혔다. 그러나 그는 이를 개의치 않는 듯했고, 탁 트인 바깥에서 몸을 펼 자리가 생기자, 한결 수월하게 안고 갈 수 있도록 그녀를 어깨 쪽으로 추켜올렸으니, 옷을 걸치지 않은 덕에 짐이 한결 가벼워졌다. 그리하여 그는 그녀를 집 밖으로 안고서, 몇 야드 떨어진 강 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n그의 궁극적인 속셈이—설령 무슨 속셈이 있었다 해도—무엇인지 그녀는 아직 가늠하지 못했고, 마치 제삼자라도 된 듯 그 문제를 두고 이리저리 짐작해 보는 자신을 발견했다. 그녀는 제 온 존재를 그에게 어찌나 편안히 내맡겼던지, 그가 자신을 마음대로 처분할 수 있는 절대적인 소유물로 여기고 있다고 생각하는 것이 그녀에게는 흐뭇했다. 내일의 이별이라는 공포가 어른거리는 가운데에서도, 그가 이제 정말로 자신을 아내 테스로 알아보고 있다는 것, 비록 그 알아봄이 자신을 해칠 권리를 스스로에게 부여하는 데까지 나아갔을지언정 자신을 내치지는 않는다는 것을 느끼는 일은 위안이 되었다.\n\n아! 이제 그녀는 그가 무슨 꿈을 꾸고 있는지 알았다—그가 다른 젖 짜는 처녀들과 함께 자신을 안고 물길을 헤쳐 데려가던 그 일요일 아침이었다. 그 처녀들도, 만약 그게 가능한 일이라면—테스로서는 좀처럼 인정하기 어려운 일이었지만—자신만큼이나 그를 사랑했었다. 클레어는 그녀를 안은 채 다리를 건너지 않고, 같은 쪽 강가를 따라 옆에 딸린 방앗간 쪽으로 몇 걸음 더 나아가더니, 이윽고 강기슭에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n이 몇 마일에 걸친 목초지를 기어 내려오는 그 강물은 이따금 갈라져, 정처 없는 굽이를 뱀처럼 그리며, 이름도 없는 작은 섬들을 고리처럼 휘감았다가, 다시 돌아와 한 몸을 이루어 저 아래에서 넓은 본류가 되었다. 그가 그녀를 데려온 자리 맞은편이 바로 그렇게 물줄기들이 한데 모이는 곳이라, 강은 그만큼 물이 불고 깊었다. 강 위에는 좁은 발판 다리가 놓여 있었으나, 이제 가을 홍수가 난간을 쓸어 가 버려 휑한 널빤지만 남았는데, 그것이 내달리는 물살 위로 몇 인치쯤 떠 있어, 침착한 사람조차 어질어질한 통로가 되었다. 테스는 낮 동안 집 창문에서 젊은이들이 균형 잡기 묘기 삼아 그 위를 걸어 건너는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 어쩌면 그녀의 남편도 그 똑같은 광경을 보았는지, 어쨌든 그는 이제 그 널빤지에 올라서더니, 한 발을 앞으로 미끄러뜨리며 그 위를 나아갔다.\n\n그가 그녀를 물에 빠뜨려 죽이려는 걸까? 아마 그런 것 같았다. 그 자리는 인적이 없었고, 강은 그런 속셈을 이루기에 충분할 만큼 깊고 넓었다. 그가 원한다면 그녀를 빠뜨려 죽일 수도 있으리라. 그건 내일 갈라서서 따로따로의 삶을 사는 것보다 차라리 나았다.\n\n빠른 물살이 두 사람 아래에서 내달리고 소용돌이치며, 물에 비친 달의 얼굴을 출렁이고, 일그러뜨리고, 쪼개 놓았다. 물거품 방울들이 떠내려가고, 말뚝에 걸린 물풀들이 그 뒤에서 너울거렸다. 만약 둘이 함께 지금 저 물살 속으로 떨어질 수만 있다면, 두 팔이 어찌나 단단히 서로를 얽어 안고 있는지 결코 구해지지 못할 터였다. 두 사람은 거의 고통 없이 세상을 떠날 것이고, 그러면 더는 그녀를 향한, 또 그녀와 결혼한 그를 향한 비난도 없으리라. 그가 그녀와 보낸 마지막 반시간은 사랑이 깃든 시간이었으련만, 만약 둘이 살아남아 그가 깨어난다면, 그의 낮 동안의 혐오가 되돌아올 것이고, 이 시간은 그저 한순간 지나간 꿈으로만 곱씹어질 터였다.\n\n둘 다를 저 심연 속으로 곤두박질치게 할 그런 움직임을 취하고픈 충동이 그녀 안에서 꿈틀거렸으나, 그녀는 감히 그 충동에 몸을 맡기지 못했다. 그녀가 제 목숨을 얼마나 하찮게 여기는지는 이미 증명된 바였다. 그러나 그의 목숨은—그녀에게는 그것을 함부로 건드릴 권리가 없었다. 그는 그녀를 안고 무사히 건너편에 다다랐다.\n\n거기서 두 사람은 수도원 경내를 이루는 식림지 안에 들어섰고, 그는 그녀를 새로 고쳐 안고 몇 걸음 더 나아가, 이윽고 수도원 성당의 허물어진 성가대석에 이르렀다. 북쪽 벽 옆에는 어느 수도원장의 텅 빈 돌관이 놓여 있었는데, 음산한 익살에 마음이 동한 관광객이라면 으레 그 안에 누워 보곤 하던 것이었다. 그 안에 클레어는 테스를 조심스레 눕혔다. 그녀의 입술에 두 번째로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 그는 간절히 바라던 목적이 이루어지기라도 한 듯 깊이 숨을 내쉬었다. 그러고 나서 클레어는 그 옆 바닥에 드러누웠고, 곧바로 기진맥진한 자의 깊고 죽은 듯한 잠에 빠져들어, 통나무처럼 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 그 행동을 빚어냈던 마음의 흥분은 이제 다 사위어 버린 것이었다.\n\n테스는 관 속에서 몸을 일으켜 앉았다. 그날 밤은 철에 비해 메마르고 포근하긴 했어도, 반쯤 헐벗은 차림의 그가 여기 오래 머물기에는 위태로울 만큼 충분히 추웠다. 그를 제멋대로 내버려 둔다면 그는 십중팔구 아침까지 거기 머물다가 얼어붙어 영락없이 죽고 말 것이었다. 그녀는 몽유 끝에 그렇게 죽은 이들의 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 그러나 어떻게 감히 그를 깨워, 그가 무슨 짓을 하고 있었는지 알게 한단 말인가? 그녀를 두고 저지른 어리석은 짓을 깨닫는 일이 그를 욕보일 텐데. 그래도 테스는 돌 감옥에서 걸어 나와 그를 살짝 흔들어 보았으나, 거칠게 굴지 않고서는 그를 깨울 수가 없었다. 무언가 하지 않으면 안 되었으니, 그녀가 몸을 떨기 시작한 까닭이었다. 침대보는 변변찮은 가리개일 뿐이었다. 그 몇 분간의 모험을 치르는 동안에는 흥분이 어느 정도 그녀의 몸을 데워 주었으나, 그 더없이 행복한 한때는 이제 끝나 버렸다.\n\n문득 그녀에게 구슬려 보자는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그리하여 그녀는 그의 귀에 대고, 끌어모을 수 있는 한 굳세고 단호하게 속삭였다—\n\n「우리 계속 걸어요, 여보.」 그러면서 동시에 넌지시 그의 팔을 잡았다. 다행스럽게도 그는 거역하지 않고 따랐다. 그녀의 말이 그를 다시 꿈속으로 돌려보낸 듯했는데, 그 꿈은 이제부터 새로운 국면으로 접어든 모양이었으니, 그 속에서 그는 그녀가 영혼이 되어 일어나 자신을 천국으로 이끌고 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 이렇게 그녀는 그의 팔을 잡고 그를 그들의 거처 앞 돌다리까지 인도했고, 그것을 건너 저택 문 앞에 두 사람은 섰다. 테스의 발은 아주 맨발이었고, 돌멩이들이 그 발을 아프게 하고 뼛속까지 시리게 했다. 그러나 클레어는 모직 양말을 신고 있어 아무런 불편도 느끼지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n더는 어려움이 없었다. 그녀는 그를 구슬려 그 자신의 소파 침상에 눕히고는, 따뜻하게 덮어 주었으며, 그의 몸에서 축축한 기운을 말려 내려고 임시로 장작불을 지폈다. 그녀는 이렇게 살뜰히 보살피느라 나는 소리에 그가 깨어날까 봐 걱정스러우면서도, 속으로는 그러기를 은근히 바랐다. 그러나 그의 마음과 몸이 어찌나 지쳐 있었던지 그는 흐트러짐 없이 잠들어 있었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 두 사람이 마주치자마자 테스는, 에인절이 자신이 그 밤의 나들이에 얼마나 깊이 얽혀 있었는지를 거의, 아니 전혀 알지 못함을 알아챘다. 비록 자기 자신에 관해서는, 그가 가만히 누워만 있지는 않았음을 어렴풋이 의식했을지도 모르지만. 사실 그는 그날 아침 소멸처럼 깊은 잠에서 깨어났고, 뇌가 마치 몸을 떨쳐 일으키는 삼손처럼 제 힘을 시험하는 그 처음 몇 순간 동안, 그는 간밤에 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 있었다는 어렴풋한 느낌을 품었다. 그러나 자신이 처한 현실이 이내 그 다른 일에 대한 짐작을 밀어내 버렸다.\n\n그는 무언가 마음의 방향을 알아챌 단서가 나타나기를 기다리며 지켜보았다. 그는 알았다. 만약 간밤에 내린 어떤 작정이 아침의 빛 속에서도 사라지지 않는다면, 그것은 비록 감정의 충동에서 비롯되었을지언정 순수한 이성에 가까운 토대 위에 서 있는 것이며, 따라서 그만큼 믿을 만한 것임을. 그리하여 그는 창백한 아침 빛 속에서, 그녀와 갈라서겠다는 결심을 보았다. 뜨겁고 분에 찬 본능으로서가 아니라, 그것을 들끓고 타오르게 했던 그 정념이 죄다 벗겨진 채, 앙상한 뼈대만 남아 서 있는 결심을. 한낱 해골에 지나지 않았으나, 그럼에도 엄연히 거기 있는 결심을. 클레어는 더는 망설이지 않았다.\n\n아침 식사 때, 그리고 두 사람이 남은 몇 가지 물건을 꾸리는 동안, 그는 간밤의 노고에서 비롯된 피로를 어찌나 역력히 드러냈던지, 테스는 일어났던 모든 일을 털어놓을 뻔했다. 그러나 그가 본능적으로 자신을 향한 애정을 드러냈음을—그의 상식이 인정하지 않는 그 애정을, 이성이 잠든 사이 그의 끌림이 그의 위엄을 더럽혔음을 알게 되면 그가 화내고, 괴로워하고, 멋쩍어할 것이라는 생각이 또다시 그녀를 멈칫하게 했다. 그것은 술이 깬 사내를, 취중에 저지른 종잡을 수 없는 행동을 두고 비웃는 것이나 다름없었다. 그가 자신의 다정했던 일탈을 어렴풋이나마 기억하고 있을지도 모르며, 그것이 그녀에게 떠나지 말라고 새삼 애원할 기회를 주는 셈이니 그녀가 그 기회를 틈타 애정 공세를 펼칠까 봐 그것을 입에 올리길 꺼리는 것일지도 모른다는 생각도 문득 그녀의 머리를 스쳤다.\n\n그는 편지로 가장 가까운 읍에서 탈것을 한 대 불러 두었는데, 아침을 마치고 얼마 안 되어 그것이 도착했다. 그녀는 그 안에서 끝의 시작을—적어도 한동안의 끝을 보았으니, 간밤의 그 일로 그의 다정함이 드러난 것이 그와 함께할 수 있을 어떤 미래에 대한 꿈을 불러일으켰던 까닭이다. 짐이 위에 실리고, 마부가 두 사람을 태우고 떠났다. 방앗간 주인과 늙은 시중 아낙은 두 사람이 부랴부랴 떠나는 데에 다소 놀라움을 내비쳤는데, 클레어는 그것을, 방앗간 일이 자신이 살펴보고자 했던 근래의 방식이 아님을 알게 된 탓으로 둘러댔으니, 그것은 그만큼은 사실인 이야기였다. 그 이상으로, 두 사람이 떠나는 모양새에는 무슨 낭패가 있었다거나, 둘이 함께 친구들을 방문하러 가는 게 아니라는 기미는 전혀 없었다.\n\n그들의 길은 며칠 전 서로에게 그토록 엄숙한 기쁨을 품고 떠나왔던 그 낙농장 근처를 지났다. 클레어가 크릭 씨와의 용무를 마무리 짓고자 했으므로, 테스로서는 자기네의 불행한 처지에 대한 의심을 사고 싶지 않은 한, 동시에 크릭 부인을 방문하지 않기란 좀처럼 어려운 일이었다.\n\n방문을 되도록 눈에 띄지 않게 하려고, 두 사람은 큰길에서 낙농장 집으로 내려가는 쪽문 곁에 마차를 세워 두고, 나란히 걸어서 그 길을 내려갔다. 버드나무 밭은 베어진 뒤라, 둘은 그루터기들 너머로, 클레어가 아내가 되어 달라고 조르며 그녀를 뒤따라왔던 그 자리를 볼 수 있었다. 왼쪽으로는 그녀가 그의 하프 소리에 홀렸던 울 안이 있었고, 멀찍이 외양간 뒤로는 두 사람의 첫 포옹의 무대였던 풀밭이 있었다. 여름 풍경의 금빛은 이제 잿빛으로 바랬고, 빛깔들은 초라했으며, 기름졌던 흙은 진창이었고, 강물은 차가웠다.\n\n낙농장 마당 문 너머로 낙농장 주인이 그들을 보고 다가오면서, 갓 결혼한 부부가 다시 나타날 때 탈보세이즈와 그 근방에서 으레 어울린다고 여겨지는 그런 농지거리를 얼굴에 띄웠다. 그러자 크릭 부인이 집에서 나왔고, 그들의 옛 지인 몇몇도 나왔으나, 메리언과 레티는 거기 없는 듯했다.\n\n테스는 그들의 짓궂은 공세와 정겨운 농지거리를 꿋꿋이 견뎌 냈는데, 그것은 그들이 생각하는 것과는 사뭇 다르게 그녀의 마음을 건드렸다. 남편과 아내가 서로의 소원해진 사이를 비밀로 하기로 한 말없는 약속에 따라, 두 사람은 여느 때와 다름없이 행동했다. 그러고 나서, 그녀로서는 차라리 그 이야기가 한마디도 나오지 않았더라면 싶었지만, 테스는 메리언과 레티의 사연을 낱낱이 들어야만 했다. 레티는 친정아버지 집으로 돌아갔고, 메리언은 다른 데서 일자리를 찾으러 떠났다고 했다. 사람들은 메리언이 변변치 못한 신세가 될까 걱정했다.\n\n이 이야기의 서글픔을 떨쳐 내려고 테스는 가서 자신이 아끼던 소들에게 일일이 손을 대며 작별을 고했다. 그리고 그녀와 클레어가 떠나려고 나란히 섰을 때, 마치 몸과 마음이 하나로 맺어진 듯한 그 모습은, 그것을 참되게 본 사람이라면 누구에게나 유난히 가엾게 비쳤으리라. 겉보기에는 한 생명의 두 팔다리처럼, 그의 팔이 그녀의 팔에 닿고, 그녀의 치맛자락이 그에게 닿은 채, 한쪽을 향해 서서—온 낙농장 사람들이 다른 쪽을 향한 것과는 반대로—작별 인사에서 「우리」라고 말하면서도, 두 사람은 양극처럼 멀리 갈라져 있었다. 어쩌면 그들의 태도에 어딘가 유난히 뻣뻣하고 멋쩍은 데가, 하나가 되었노라 표방하는 데 어울리게 굴려는 데서 오는 어떤 어색함이, 젊은 부부의 자연스러운 수줍음과는 다른 그것이 드러났던 모양이다. 두 사람이 떠난 뒤 크릭 부인이 남편에게 이렇게 말한 걸 보면 말이다—\n\n「저 색시 눈빛이 어찌 그리 부자연스럽게 반짝이던지, 또 둘이 어찌 그리 밀랍 인형처럼 서서 꿈결인 양 말을 하던지! 당신도 그렇게 느껴지지 않았수? 테스는 어딘가 늘 좀 별난 데가 있더니, 이제 보니 잘사는 사내의 자랑스러운 새색시 같지가 않구려.」\n\n두 사람은 다시 탈것에 올라, 웨더베리와 스택풋 레인 쪽으로 길을 달려, 마침내 레인 여관에 다다랐고, 거기서 클레어는 마차와 마부를 돌려보냈다. 두 사람은 여기서 잠시 쉬었다가, 골짜기로 들어서서, 두 사람의 관계를 알지 못하는 낯선 마부의 마차에 실려 그녀의 집 쪽으로 나아갔다. 너틀베리를 지나, 갈림길이 있는 중간쯤의 한 지점에 이르자, 클레어는 마차를 세우고 테스에게, 만약 그녀가 어머니 집으로 돌아갈 작정이라면 여기서 자신은 그녀와 헤어지겠다고 말했다. 마부 앞에서는 자유로이 이야기를 나눌 수 없었으므로, 그는 그녀에게 갈림길 가운데 하나를 따라 몇 걸음 함께 걸어 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 응했고, 마부에게 몇 분 기다리라고 이른 뒤, 두 사람은 거닐며 멀어졌다.\n\n「자, 이제 우리 서로의 뜻을 분명히 해 두자.」 그가 부드럽게 말했다. 「우리 사이에 노여움은 없어. 다만 지금으로서는 내가 견딜 수 없는 무언가가 있을 뿐이야. 그걸 견뎌 보도록 애써 볼게. 내가 어디로 가는지는 나 스스로 알게 되는 대로 너에게 알려 줄게. 그리고 만약 내가 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 있게 된다면—그게 바람직하고, 또 가능한 일이라면—너에게 가겠어. 하지만 내가 너에게 갈 때까지는, 네가 나에게 오려고 하지 않는 편이 나을 거야.」\n\n그 선고의 준엄함은 테스에게 죽음처럼 느껴졌다. 그가 자신을 어떻게 보고 있는지를 그녀는 너무도 또렷이 알았다. 그는 자신을, 그에게 엄청난 기만을 부린 여자 말고는 달리 어떤 모습으로도 볼 수 없는 것이었다. 그러나 자기가 한 그런 짓을 한 여자라 한들 이 모든 벌을 받아 마땅하단 말인가? 하지만 그녀는 그 점을 두고 그와 더 다툴 수 없었다. 그녀는 그저 그가 한 말을 그대로 따라 되뇌었을 뿐이다.\n\n「당신이 나에게 올 때까지 나는 당신에게 가려고 하면 안 된다는 거죠?」\n\n「바로 그래.」\n\n「편지는 써도 되나요?」\n\n「오 그럼—네가 아프거나, 무엇이든 필요한 게 있으면 말이야. 그런 일이 없기를 바라지만. 그러니 어쩌면 내가 너에게 먼저 편지를 쓰게 될지도 모르겠다.」\n\n「그 조건에 따를게요, 에인절. 내가 어떤 벌을 받아야 마땅한지는 당신이 가장 잘 아니까요. 다만—다만—내가 견딜 수 있는 것보다 더 무겁게는 하지 말아 줘요!」\n\n그 일에 관해 그녀가 한 말은 그게 다였다. 만약 테스가 약삭빨라서, 그 외딴 오솔길에서 한바탕 소란을 피우고, 까무러치고, 발작하듯 흐느껴 울었더라면, 그를 사로잡은 그 까다로움의 광기에도 불구하고, 그는 아마 그녀를 당해 내지 못했으리라. 그러나 그녀의 오래 참는 마음결이 그가 갈 길을 수월하게 만들어 주었으니, 그녀 자신이 그에게는 가장 좋은 변호인이었다. 자존심 또한 그녀의 순순한 복종에 한몫했는데—이는 어쩌면 더버빌 가문 전체에서 너무도 뚜렷한, 운명에 무모하게 몸을 내맡기는 그 성향의 한 징후였는지도 모른다—그리하여 그녀가 호소만 했더라면 울려 낼 수 있었을 그 숱한 효과적인 심금들은 끝내 건드려지지 않은 채 남았다.\n\n그들의 나머지 이야기는 오로지 실질적인 문제들뿐이었다. 그는 이제 그녀에게 제법 넉넉한 액수의 돈이 든 꾸러미를 건넸는데, 그것은 이 목적을 위해 거래 은행에서 찾아 둔 것이었다. 그 다이아몬드들은—유언장의 문구를 그가 제대로 이해했다면—테스가 살아 있는 동안에 한해 그녀의 것인 듯했으므로, 안전을 위해 자기가 은행에 맡기도록 해 달라고 그는 권했고, 그녀는 선뜻 이에 동의했다.\n\n이런 일들을 정해 두고 나서, 그는 테스와 함께 마차로 되돌아 걸어가, 그녀를 안에 태웠다. 마부에게는 삯을 치르고 그녀를 어디로 데려다줄지 일러 두었다. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 가방과 우산을—이곳으로 오면서 그가 챙겨 온 단 두 가지 물건을—집어 들고는, 그녀에게 작별을 고했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 거기서 그 자리로 헤어졌다.\n\n마차가 언덕을 기어오르듯 올라갔고, 클레어는, 테스가 한순간이라도 창밖을 내다봐 주었으면 하는, 미처 의도치 않은 바람을 품은 채 그것이 멀어져 가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러나 그녀는 그럴 생각을 도무지 하지 못했으니, 반쯤 죽은 듯 까무러친 채 안에 누워, 감히 그럴 엄두도 내지 못했으리라. 그리하여 그는 그녀가 멀어져 가는 것을 바라보았고, 마음의 비통함 속에서 한 시인의 시구 한 줄을, 그 나름으로 유난스럽게 고쳐서 읊조렸다—\n\n하느님은 하늘에 계시지 않다. 세상만사 다 글렀다!\n\n테스가 언덕마루를 넘어가 버리자 그는 제 갈 길을 가려고 돌아섰고, 자신이 여전히 그녀를 사랑하고 있음을 거의 알지 못했다.\n\n# XXXVIII\n\n블랙무어 골짜기를 가로질러 마차가 달려가고, 어린 시절의 풍경이 주위에 펼쳐지기 시작하자, 테스는 멍한 상태에서 정신을 차렸다. 그녀의 첫 번째 생각은, 어떻게 부모님을 마주할 수 있을까 하는 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 마을로 가는 큰길에 놓인 통행료 징수소 문에 다다랐다. 그 문을 열어 준 것은 낯선 사람이었지, 여러 해 그 문을 지켜 와 그녀를 알던 그 늙은이가 아니었다. 그 늙은이는 아마 설날에—그런 교체가 이루어지던 그날에—그만둔 모양이었다. 근래에 집에서 아무 소식도 받지 못한 터라, 그녀는 그 문지기에게 소식을 물었다.\n\n「오—아무 일도 없습니다요, 아가씨.」 그가 대답했다. 「말럿은 여전히 말럿이지요. 죽은 사람들도 있고 뭐 그렇고요. 존 더비필드 그 양반도, 이번 주에 따님 하나를 신사 농부한테 시집보냈답니다. 존 그 양반 집에서 치른 건 아니고요, 알다시피요. 혼례는 딴 데서 치렀지요. 신랑 되는 양반이 워낙 지체 높은 사람이라, 존 그 양반네 식구들은 거기 한몫 낄 만큼 잘사는 축으로 안 쳐 줬다나 봅디다. 신랑은, 존 그 양반이 핏줄로 따지면 자기가 오래되고 유서 깊은 귀족이고, 지금도 자기네 납골당에 집안 해골들이 떡하니 있는데, 로마 시절에 재산을 죄다 빼앗긴 거라는 걸, 그게 어찌 밝혀졌는지도 모르는 눈치더라고요. 어쨌든, 이제 우리가 존 경이라 부르는 그 양반은, 혼례 날을 할 수 있는 한 떡 벌어지게 차렸고, 교구 사람 모두한테 한턱을 쐈답니다. 그리고 존 그 양반 마누라는 퓨어 드롭에서 열한 시가 넘도록 노래를 불러 댔지요.」\n\n이 말을 듣자 테스는 가슴이 어찌나 메스껍던지, 짐이며 살림살이를 실은 마차를 타고 버젓이 집으로 들어갈 마음을 도무지 먹을 수가 없었다. 그녀는 문지기에게 자기 물건들을 잠시 그의 집에 맡겨 두어도 되겠느냐고 물었고, 그가 이의를 달지 않자, 마차를 돌려보내고는 뒷길로 혼자서 마을을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n아버지의 집 굴뚝이 눈에 들어오자, 그녀는 도대체 어떻게 저 집에 들어갈 수 있을까 자문했다. 저 오두막 안에서 그녀의 식구들은, 그녀가 비교적 부유한 사내와 함께 멀리 신혼여행을 떠나 있으리라고, 그 사내가 그녀를 펄펄 나는 번영으로 이끌어 주리라고 태평하게 짐작하고 있을 터였다. 그런데 정작 그녀는 여기, 의지할 데 하나 없이, 세상에 달리 갈 데도 없이, 홀로 그 낡은 문으로 살금살금 다가서고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 아무에게도 들키지 않고 집에 다다르지는 못했다. 정원 울타리 바로 곁에서 그녀는 자신을 아는 한 처녀와—학교 다닐 적 가깝게 지냈던 두세 명 가운데 하나와 마주쳤다. 테스가 어떻게 여기 왔는지 몇 마디 캐묻고 나서, 그 친구는 그녀의 비극적인 얼굴빛은 아랑곳없이 불쑥 끼어들어 물었다—\n\n「그런데 네 신랑은 어디 있어, 테스?」\n\n테스는 그가 볼일이 있어 불려 갔다고 황급히 둘러대고는, 그 말상대를 남겨 둔 채 정원 울타리를 기어 넘어, 그렇게 집으로 가는 길을 찾아들었다.\n\n정원 길을 올라가는데 어머니가 뒷문 곁에서 노래 부르는 소리가 들렸고, 그쪽이 눈에 들어오자, 더비필드 부인이 문간 디딤돌에서 침대보를 짜고 있는 것이 보였다. 그녀는 테스를 알아채지 못한 채 그 일을 마치고는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 딸이 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n빨래통은 늘 놓이던 그 자리, 늘 쓰던 그 사 분의 일짜리 큰 통 위에 그대로 있었고, 어머니는 침대보를 한쪽으로 던져 놓고 막 다시 두 팔을 담그려던 참이었다.\n\n「아니—테스!—얘야—난 네가 결혼한 줄로만—이번엔 정말로 제대로 식을 올린 줄 알았는데—우리가 사과주도 보냈잖니—」\n\n「네, 어머니. 결혼했어요.」\n\n「하게 됐다고?」\n\n「아뇨—결혼했어요.」\n\n「결혼을 해! 그럼 네 남편은 어디 있어?」\n\n「오, 잠시 멀리 떠났어요.」\n\n「멀리 떠나! 그럼 결혼은 언제 한 거냐? 네가 말한 그날?」\n\n「네, 화요일에요, 어머니.」\n\n「그런데 이제 겨우 토요일인데, 벌써 멀리 떠났다고?」\n\n「네, 떠났어요.」\n\n「그게 대체 무슨 소리냐? 네가 얻는다는 남편들은 죄다 이 모양이니, 망할 놈들 같으니, 내 말이!」\n\n「어머니!」 테스는 조운 더비필드에게로 다가가, 그 푸근한 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고는 흐느낌을 터뜨렸다. 「어떻게 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠어요, 어머니! 어머니가 저한테 말씀하셨고, 편지로도 쓰셨잖아요, 그이한테 말하지 말라고요. 그런데 저는 그이한테 말해 버렸어요—어쩔 수가 없었어요—그랬더니 그이가 떠나 버렸어요!」\n\n「오 이 어리석은 것아—이 어리석은 것아!」 더비필드 부인은 어찌나 흥분했는지 테스와 제 몸에 물을 튀겨 가며 버럭 소리쳤다. 「맙소사! 내가 이런 말을 하게 될 줄이야, 그래도 다시 한번 말하마, 이 어리석은 것아!」\n\n테스는 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨었으니, 그토록 여러 날의 긴장이 마침내 풀린 것이었다.\n\n「알아요—알아요—저도 알아요!」 그녀가 흐느낌 사이로 가쁘게 말했다. 「하지만, 오 어머니, 저는 어쩔 수가 없었어요! 그이는 너무도 착했어요—그래서 일어났던 일을 두고 그이의 눈을 가리려 한다는 게 너무도 못된 짓으로 느껴졌어요! 만약—만약—다시 그래야 한다 해도—저는 똑같이 할 거예요. 그이에게 그런 죄를—그토록 죄를 지을 수는—감히 그럴 수는 없었어요!」\n\n「그래도 처음에 그 사람이랑 결혼할 만큼은 죄를 지었잖니!」\n\n「네, 네. 바로 거기에 제 비참함이 있어요! 하지만 저는 그이가 마음만 굳게 먹고 그 일을 못 본 척 넘어가지 않을 작정이라면, 법으로 저를 떼어 버릴 수 있는 줄 알았어요. 그리고 오, 어머니가 아셨더라면—제가 그이를 얼마나 사랑했는지, 그이를 얼마나 간절히 갖고 싶어 했는지, 그리고 그이를 그토록 끔찍이 아끼는 마음과 그이에게 떳떳하고 싶은 바람 사이에서 제가 얼마나 갈가리 찢겼는지를 반만이라도 아셨더라면!」\n\n테스는 어찌나 몸이 떨렸던지 더는 말을 잇지 못하고, 맥없이 무너지듯 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n「그래, 그래. 이미 엎질러진 물은 도로 담을 수 없지! 내가 낳은 자식들이 어째서 죄다 남의 자식들보다 더 한심한 멍청이여야 하는지 나도 정말 모르겠다—그 사람이 너무 늦어서야 알게 될 텐데, 그런 일을 굳이 나불대다니, 그것보다 더 분별없는 짓이 어디 있냐!」 여기서 더비필드 부인은 가엾은 어미 신세가 되어 자기 몫의 눈물을 흘리기 시작했다. 「네 아버지가 뭐라 하실지 난 모르겠다.」 그녀가 말을 이었다. 「그 양반은 날마다 롤리버즈며 퓨어 드롭에 가서 이 혼사 자랑을 늘어놓고, 너를 통해 집안이 제자리를 되찾게 됐다고 떠벌리고 다녔는데—가엾고 어리석은 양반 같으니!—그런데 이제 네가 이렇게 다 망쳐 놨으니! 어이구 맙소사!」\n\n마치 일을 한 점으로 모으기라도 하려는 듯, 바로 그 순간 테스의 아버지가 다가오는 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 그는 곧바로 들어오지는 않았고, 더비필드 부인은 자기가 직접 그에게 이 나쁜 소식을 전하겠노라고, 테스는 당분간 눈에 띄지 않게 있으라고 말했다. 처음의 실망이 한바탕 터져 나온 뒤, 조운은 이 낭패를, 테스의 애초의 사달을 받아들였을 때처럼, 마치 비 오는 휴일이나 농사의 흉작을 받아들이듯 받아들이기 시작했다.\n감자 농사처럼, 자격이나 어리석음과는 상관없이 그들에게 닥쳐온 일로, 견뎌야 할 외부의 우연한 충돌로, 교훈이 아닌 것으로 받아들였다.\n\n테스는 위층으로 물러갔다가 침대가 옮겨지고 새로 자리가 잡힌 것을 무심코 보았다. 그녀의 옛 침대는 어린 동생 둘이 쓰도록 손질되어 있었다. 이제 여기에 그녀가 누울 자리는 없었다.\n\n아래층 방은 천장이 마감되어 있지 않아 그녀는 거기서 벌어지는 일을 거의 다 들을 수 있었다. 이윽고 아버지가 들어왔는데, 보아하니 살아 있는 암탉 한 마리를 안고 있었다. 그는 이제 두 번째 말마저 팔아야 했던 탓에 도보 행상꾼이 되어, 바구니를 팔에 걸고 다녔다. 그 암탉은 그가 일에 나서 있음을 사람들에게 보이려고 오늘 아침 내내 그렇게 자주 안고 다닌 것이었지만, 정작 다리가 묶인 채 롤리버 술집 탁자 밑에 한 시간 넘게 누워 있었다.\n\n\"방금 우리가—\" 더비필드가 입을 열더니, 그 자리에서 술집에서 일어났던 성직자에 관한 논의를 아내에게 소상히 늘어놓았다. 그것은 자기 딸이 성직자 집안에 시집갔다는 사실에서 비롯된 것이었다. \"옛날에는 그것들도 우리 조상처럼 '경'이라 불렸지.\" 그는 말했다. \"요즘이야 엄밀히 따지면 그저 '서기'에 지나지 않지만 말이야.\" 테스가 이 일이 크게 알려지지 않기를 바랐던 터라, 그는 자세한 내막은 입에 올리지 않았다. 그는 그녀가 곧 그 금지를 거두어 주기를 바랐다. 그는 그 부부가 변질되지 않은 테스 본래의 성, 더버빌을 쓰면 어떻겠느냐고 했다. 그게 남편 성보다 낫다는 것이었다. 그는 오늘 그녀한테서 편지가 오지 않았느냐고 물었다.\n\n그러자 더비필드 부인은 편지는 오지 않았으나 안타깝게도 테스가 직접 왔다고 일러 주었다.\n\n마침내 모든 게 무너졌다는 사정을 듣고 나자, 더비필드답지 않은 침울한 굴욕감이 흥을 돋우던 술기운의 작용을 짓눌렀다. 그러나 그 사건의 본질적인 무게보다도, 그것이 남들의 마음에 어떻게 비칠까 하는 짐작이 더 그의 예민한 신경을 건드렸다.\n\n\"생각해 보게나, 이게 그 끝이라니!\" 존 경이 말했다. \"킹스비어의 저 교회 밑에 졸라드 지주네 술 창고만 한 우리 집안 납골당이 있고, 우리 조상들이 거기 떼로 누워 있단 말이야, 역사에 기록된 누구 못지않게 진짜배기 지방 명문가 뼈와 골수로 말이지. 그런데 이제 롤리버랑 퓨어 드롭 패거리가 나한테 뭐라고 할지 뻔하다고! 그것들이 눈을 흘기고 노려보며 이러겠지. '이게 자네 그 대단한 혼사인가, 이게 노르만 왕 시절 자네 조상들 본래 신분으로 돌아간 꼴인가!' 이건 너무하다, 조앤. 작위고 뭐고 내 손으로 끝장을 내야겠다—더는 못 견디겠어!… 그래도 그자가 그애와 결혼했다면 그애를 부양하게 만들 수 있는 거 아닌가?\"\n\n\"그야, 그렇지요. 하지만 그애는 그럴 생각조차 안 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그자가 정말로 그애와 결혼했다고 생각해?—아니면 그 첫 번째 일처럼—\"\n\n여기까지 들은 가엾은 테스는 더는 들을 수가 없었다. 자기 친정집에서조차, 제 말이 의심받을 수 있다는 깨달음은 다른 무엇으로도 할 수 없을 만큼 그녀의 마음을 이 집에서 등 돌리게 했다. 운명의 공격은 어찌나 뜻밖인가! 아버지가 그녀를 조금이라도 의심한다면, 이웃과 지인 들은 얼마나 더 의심하겠는가? 아, 그녀는 집에서 오래 살 수 없었다!\n\n그리하여 그녀는 며칠만 자신에게 허락했고, 그 끝에 클레어에게서 짧은 편지를 받았다. 농장을 보러 잉글랜드 북부로 갔다는 내용이었다. 그의 아내라는 자신의 진짜 지위가 주는 광채를 갈망하던 마음에서, 또 둘 사이에 벌어진 엄청난 간극을 부모에게 감추려는 마음에서, 그녀는 이 편지를 다시 떠나는 구실로 삼았고, 부모에게는 자기가 남편과 합류하러 떠나는 것이라는 인상을 남겨 두었다. 더 나아가 남편이 자기에게 매정했다는 혐의를 받지 않도록 막으려고, 그녀는 클레어가 준 오십 파운드 가운데 이십오 파운드를 떼어 어머니에게 건넸다. 마치 앤젤 클레어 같은 사람의 아내라면 너끈히 그럴 수 있다는 듯이, 지난 세월 자기가 그들에게 끼친 수고와 굴욕에 대한 작은 보답이라며. 이렇게 자신의 위엄을 내세우며 그녀는 작별을 고했다. 그러고 나서 한동안 더비필드네 집안에서는 테스의 후한 인심 덕에 활기찬 일들이 벌어졌고, 그녀의 어머니는 젊은 부부 사이에 벌어졌던 균열이 서로 떨어져서는 살 수 없다는 강한 감정 아래 저절로 봉합되었노라고 말했고, 실은 그렇게 믿기도 했다.\n\n# XXXIX\n\n결혼한 지 삼 주 뒤, 클레어는 자기 아버지의 그 잘 알려진 목사관으로 이어지는 언덕을 내려가고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그가 내려가는 길에 교회 탑이 저녁 하늘로 솟아올라, 그가 왜 왔느냐고 묻는 듯한 모양새를 했다. 어스름이 깔린 마을의 살아 있는 사람 중 누구도 그를 알아채지 못했고, 더더욱 그를 기다린 이도 없는 듯했다. 그는 유령처럼 도착하고 있었고, 제 발소리마저 떨쳐 버려야 할 거추장스러운 것만 같았다.\n\n그에게 삶의 그림은 달라져 있었다. 이전까지 그는 삶을 그저 사변적으로만 알았다. 이제 그는 실제적인 인간으로서 삶을 안다고 여겼다. 어쩌면 여전히 그렇지 못했을지도 모르지만. 그럼에도 인류는 이제 이탈리아 미술의 애잔한 감미로움 속에서가 아니라, 비르츠 미술관의 부릅뜬 눈과 소름 끼치는 자세로, 또 판 베이르스의 습작에 담긴 음흉한 곁눈질을 띤 채 그의 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n이 첫 몇 주 동안 그의 행동은 이루 말할 수 없이 종잡을 데가 없었다. 마치 별일 없었다는 듯, 모든 시대의 위대하고 현명한 자들이 권하는 방식대로 기계적으로 농사 계획을 좇으려 애써 본 뒤, 그는 그 위대하고 현명한 자들 가운데 자기 충고가 실행 가능한지 시험해 볼 만큼 자기 자신 밖으로 멀리 나가 본 이는 거의 없으리라고 결론지었다. \"이것이 으뜸가는 것이다. 동요하지 말라\"고 이교도 도덕가는 말했다. 그것은 바로 클레어 자신의 견해이기도 했다. 그러나 그는 동요했다. \"너희 마음에 근심하지 말고 두려워하지도 말라\"고 나사렛 사람은 말했다. 클레어는 진심으로 맞장구쳤다. 그래도 그의 마음은 여전히 근심에 차 있었다. 그는 이 두 위대한 사상가와 얼굴을 맞대고, 사람 대 사람으로서 간곡히 호소하며, 그 방법을 일러 달라고 청하고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 간절했던가!\n\n그의 기분은 끈질긴 무관심으로 바뀌어 갔고, 마침내 그는 외부인의 무심한 흥미를 가지고 제 존재를 바라보고 있다는 착각에 빠졌다.\n\n이 모든 황폐가 그녀가 더버빌가 사람이라는 우연 탓에 빚어졌다는 확신이 그를 쓰라리게 했다. 테스가 자기가 곱게 꿈꾸어 온 대로 밑바닥에서 올라온 새로운 족속이 아니라, 그 쇠진한 옛 혈통에서 나온 사람임을 알았을 때, 어찌하여 그는 자기 원칙에 충실하게 그녀를 금욕적으로 버리지 못했던가? 이것이 변절의 대가였고, 그의 벌은 마땅한 것이었다.\n\n이윽고 그는 지치고 불안해졌으며, 그 불안은 점점 커져 갔다. 그는 자기가 그녀를 부당하게 대한 것은 아닌지 곱씹었다. 그는 먹는지도 모른 채 먹었고, 맛도 모른 채 마셨다. 시간이 한 시간 한 시간 흘러가면서, 지나간 그 긴 나날들의 행위 하나하나의 동기가 눈앞에 떠오르면서, 그는 테스를 소중한 소유물로 가진다는 관념이 자기의 온갖 계획과 말과 행실에 얼마나 긴밀히 얽혀 있었는지를 깨달았다.\n\n여기저기 다니던 중 그는 어느 작은 마을 외곽에서 빨강과 파랑의 벽보 하나를 보았다. 이민 가는 농부에게 브라질 제국이 펼쳐 주는 큰 이점을 늘어놓은 것이었다. 거기서는 유난히 유리한 조건으로 땅을 내준다고 했다. 브라질은 새로운 발상으로서 그를 다소 끌어당겼다. 테스도 언젠가는 거기서 합류할 수 있고, 어쩌면 풍경과 관념과 관습이 사뭇 다른 그 나라에서라면, 여기서는 그녀와의 삶을 불가능해 보이게 만드는 그 관습들이 그토록 위력을 떨치지는 않을 것이었다. 요컨대 그는 브라질을 시도해 보고픈 마음에 강하게 기울었고, 더욱이 그곳으로 떠날 철이 마침 코앞에 닥쳐 있었다.\n\n이런 생각으로 그는 부모에게 자기 계획을 알리고, 테스를 데려오지 않은 채 도착한 것에 대해 둘을 갈라놓은 진짜 사정을 드러내지 않는 선에서 할 수 있는 최선의 해명을 하려고 에민스터로 돌아오는 길이었다. 그가 문에 이르렀을 때 초승달이 그의 얼굴을 비추었다. 그가 아내를 품에 안고 강을 건너 수도사들의 묘지로 데려가던 그날 새벽녘에 그믐달이 비추었던 것과 꼭 같았다. 다만 이제 그의 얼굴은 더 야위어 있었다.\n\n클레어는 부모에게 자신의 방문을 미리 알리지 않았던 터라, 그의 도착은 마치 물총새가 잔잔한 못 속으로 자맥질하듯 목사관의 분위기를 휘저어 놓았다. 그의 아버지와 어머니는 둘 다 거실에 있었으나 두 형은 이제 집에 없었다. 앤젤은 들어와 등 뒤로 문을 조용히 닫았다.\n\n\"그런데—네 아내는 어디 있느냐, 앤젤?\" 그의 어머니가 외쳤다. \"너 정말 우리를 놀라게 하는구나!\"\n\n\"잠시—친정에 가 있습니다. 브라질로 가기로 결심해서 좀 서둘러 집에 왔어요.\"\n\n\"브라질이라니! 거기는 분명 죄다 로마 가톨릭교도들이 아니냐!\"\n\n\"그런가요? 그건 생각 못 했습니다.\"\n\n그러나 가톨릭 나라로 떠난다는 그 새삼스러움과 괴로움조차도, 아들의 결혼에 대한 클레어 부부의 자연스러운 관심을 오래 밀어내지는 못했다.\n\n\"삼 주 전에 결혼이 이루어졌다는 네 짧은 편지를 받았다.\" 클레어 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 네 아버지가, 너도 알다시피, 네 대모의 선물을 그애에게 보냈지. 물론 우리 중 아무도 참석하지 않는 편이 가장 좋았어, 특히 네가 그애 집이 어디든 거기서가 아니라 낙농장에서 결혼하기를 원했으니 말이다. 그랬더라면 너도 거북했을 테고, 우리한테도 기쁠 게 없었을 테지. 네 형들도 그 점을 아주 절실히 느꼈단다. 이제 다 끝난 일이니 우리는 불평하지 않으마, 특히 네가 복음의 사역 대신 택한 그 일에 그애가 어울리는 사람이라면 더욱. … 그래도 그애를 먼저 봤더라면, 앤젤, 아니면 그애에 대해 조금이라도 더 알았더라면 좋았을 것을. 우리는 무엇이 그애를 가장 기쁘게 할지 몰라 우리 나름의 선물을 보내지 않았다만, 그저 미뤄진 것뿐이라고 여겨 다오. 앤젤, 이 결혼 때문에 내 마음에도 네 아버지 마음에도 너에 대한 노여움은 없다. 다만 네 아내를 직접 볼 수 있을 때까지 그애에 대한 호의를 아껴 두는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 생각했을 뿐이야. 그런데 이제 너는 그애를 데려오지 않았구나. 이상한 일이다. 무슨 일이 있었던 게냐?\"\n\n그는 자기가 이리로 오는 동안 그녀는 당분간 친정에 가 있는 편이 가장 낫다고 둘이서 생각했다고 답했다.\n\n\"숨김없이 말씀드리지요, 어머니.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그녀가 어머니께 떳떳이 올 수 있다고 느껴질 때까지 이 집에서 떼어 두려고 늘 마음먹고 있었습니다. 하지만 이 브라질 생각은 아주 최근에 떠오른 것이고요. 만약 제가 정말 간다면, 이 첫 여정에 그녀를 데려가는 건 좋지 않을 겁니다. 제가 돌아올 때까지 그녀는 친정에 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 네가 떠나기 전에 나는 그애를 못 보는 게냐?\"\n\n그는 못 볼 것 같다고 했다. 그가 말한 대로 본래 그의 계획은, 어떤 식으로든 부모의 선입견을—감정을—다치게 하지 않으려고 한동안 그녀를 이리로 데려오지 않는 것이었고, 다른 이유들 때문에도 그는 그 계획을 고수해 왔다. 곧장 떠난다면 그는 일 년 안에 한 번 고향에 들러야 할 테고, 그가 두 번째로—그녀와 함께—떠나기 전에 부모가 그녀를 볼 수 있을 터였다.\n\n서둘러 차린 저녁상이 들어왔고, 클레어는 자기 계획을 더 풀어 설명했다. 신부를 보지 못한 어머니의 실망은 여전히 그녀에게 남아 있었다. 테스에 대한 클레어의 지난 열정이 모성의 공감을 타고 그녀에게도 옮아, 그녀는 나사렛에서도 좋은 것이 나올 수 있다고—탤버세이즈 낙농장에서도 매력적인 여인이 나올 수 있다고—거의 믿게 될 지경이었다. 그녀는 아들이 식사하는 모습을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"그애를 좀 그려 볼 수 없겠니? 분명 아주 예쁜 아이일 게다, 앤젤.\"\n\n\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지요!\" 그는 쓰라림을 덮어 가리는 열기를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그애가 정결하고 정숙하다는 건 두말할 것도 없겠지?\"\n\n\"정결하고 정숙하지요, 물론입니다.\"\n\n\"그애가 아주 또렷이 보이는구나. 며칠 전에 네가 그러지 않았니, 그애가 몸매가 곱고, 둥글게 잘 빠졌으며, 큐피드의 활처럼 짙은 붉은 입술에, 검은 속눈썹과 눈썹을 지녔고, 배의 밧줄처럼 굵은 머리채에, 보랏빛 도는 푸르스름하고 거뭇한 큰 눈을 가졌다고.\"\n\n\"그랬지요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"그애가 아주 잘 보인다. 그렇게 외진 곳에서 살았으니, 자연히 너를 보기 전까지는 바깥세상 젊은이를 거의 본 적이 없었겠구나.\"\n\n\"거의 그렇지요.\"\n\n\"네가 그애 첫사랑이었니?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\"\n\n\"이렇게 소박하고, 입술 발그레하고, 튼튼한 농가 처녀들보다 더 못한 아내들도 세상엔 많단다. 물론 나야 바랐을 테지만—그래, 내 아들이 농부가 될 바에야, 그 아내가 바깥일에 익숙한 사람이었던 것도 어쩌면 합당한 일이겠지.\"\n\n그의 아버지는 덜 캐물었다. 그러나 저녁 기도 전에 늘 읽던 성경 구절을 읽을 때가 되자, 목사는 클레어 부인에게 일렀다—\n\n\"앤젤이 왔으니, 평소 차례대로 읽었어야 할 장보다는 잠언 삼십일 장을 읽는 게 더 어울리겠소.\"\n\n\"네, 그러시고말고요.\" 클레어 부인이 말했다. \"르무엘 왕의 말씀이지요.\" (그녀도 남편 못지않게 장과 절을 인용할 수 있었다.) \"사랑하는 아들아, 네 아버지가 잠언에서 현숙한 아내를 칭송하는 장을 우리에게 읽어 주기로 하셨다. 그 말씀을 자리에 없는 그 아이에게 적용하라고 굳이 일러 줄 필요는 없겠지. 하늘이 그애의 모든 길을 지켜 주시기를!\"\n\n클레어의 목구멍에 무언가가 치밀어 올랐다. 들고 다닐 수 있는 성경대를 구석에서 꺼내어 벽난로 한가운데에 놓았고, 늙은 하인 둘이 들어왔으며, 앤젤의 아버지는 방금 말한 그 장의 십 절부터 읽기 시작했다—\n\n\"누가 현숙한 여인을 찾아 얻겠느냐. 그의 값은 진주보다 더 하니라. 그는 밤이 새기 전에 일어나서 그 집안사람에게 음식을 나누어 주며, 힘으로 허리를 묶고 그 팔을 강하게 하며, 자기의 무역하는 것이 이로운 줄을 깨닫고 밤에 등불을 끄지 아니하며, 자기 집안일을 보살피고 게으른 양식을 먹지 아니하나니, 그의 자식들은 일어나 감사하며, 그의 남편도 일어나 그를 칭찬하기를, 덕행 있는 여자가 많으나 그대는 모든 여자보다 뛰어나다 하느니라.\"\n\n기도가 끝나자, 그의 어머니가 말했다—\n\n\"네 아버지가 읽으신 그 장이, 그 몇몇 대목에서, 네가 택한 여인에게 어쩌면 그토록 꼭 들어맞는지 생각하지 않을 수 없었단다. 알겠니, 그 완벽한 여인은 일하는 여인이었어. 게으른 여자도 아니요, 고운 귀부인도 아니요, 제 손과 머리와 마음을 남을 위해 쓰는 여인이었지. '그의 자식들은 일어나 감사하며, 그의 남편도 일어나 그를 칭찬하기를, 덕행 있는 여자가 많으나 그대는 모든 여자보다 뛰어나다 하느니라.' 그래, 그애를 봤더라면 좋았을 것을, 앤젤. 그애가 정결하고 순결하다면, 나한테는 충분히 교양 있는 아이였을 텐데.\"\n\n클레어는 더는 이것을 견딜 수 없었다. 그의 눈은 눈물로 가득 찼는데, 그것은 마치 녹은 납방울 같았다. 그는 자신이 그토록 사랑하는 이 진실하고 소박한 영혼들에게 황급히 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다. 세상도, 육신도, 악마도 제 마음속에서는 알지 못하고, 그저 자기들 밖에 있는 무언가 막연한 것으로만 아는 이들에게. 그는 제 방으로 갔다.\n\n그의 어머니가 뒤따라와 방문을 두드렸다. 클레어가 문을 여니, 어머니가 근심 어린 눈으로 밖에 서 있었다.\n\n\"앤젤.\" 그녀가 물었다. \"이렇게 곧 떠나다니 무슨 잘못된 일이라도 있는 게냐? 네가 영 너답지 않은 게 분명하구나.\"\n\n\"네, 좀 그렇습니다, 어머니.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그애 때문이냐? 자, 아들아, 나는 그게 그 일이란 걸 안다—그애 때문이란 걸 알아! 이 삼 주 동안 둘이 다툰 게냐?\"\n\n\"꼭 다툰 건 아니에요.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 우리 사이에 견해 차이가—\"\n\n\"앤젤—그애가 내력을 캐물어도 떳떳할 만한 처녀냐?\"\n\n어머니의 본능으로 클레어 부인은, 아들을 그토록 들쑤셔 놓은 듯한 그 종류의 근심에 손가락을 정확히 짚었다.\n\n\"그녀는 한 점 흠이 없습니다!\" 그는 대답했다. 그러면서 그 거짓말 때문에 당장 영원한 지옥에 떨어진다 해도 그렇게 말했으리라고 느꼈다.\n\n\"그렇다면 나머지는 신경 쓰지 마라. 따지고 보면 자연에서 때 묻지 않은 시골 처녀만큼 정결한 것도 드물단다. 처음에 네 더 교양 있는 감각에 거슬릴 만한 거친 태도가 좀 있더라도, 네 곁에서 함께 지내며 가르침을 받는 동안 분명 사라질 게다.\"\n\n이 눈먼 너그러움의 끔찍한 빈정거림은, 그가 이 결혼으로 자기 앞길을 완전히 망쳐 버렸다는 부차적인 깨달음을 클레어에게 일깨워 주었다. 그 깨달음은 비밀이 밝혀진 뒤 그의 머릿속에 처음부터 떠오른 생각은 아니었다. 사실 그 자신을 두고 보자면 자기 앞길 따위는 그다지 마음 쓰지 않았다. 그러나 그는 부모와 형들을 생각해서 적어도 그것을 떳떳한 것으로 만들고 싶었다. 그리고 이제 촛불을 들여다보니, 그 불꽃이 그에게 말없이 일러 주었다. 자기는 분별 있는 사람들을 비추라고 만들어졌으며, 속아 넘어간 자, 실패한 자의 얼굴을 밝히는 일은 질색이라고.\n\n동요가 가라앉으면 그는 가엾은 아내에게 화가 치미는 순간이 있었다. 그녀가 자기로 하여금 부모를 속이지 않을 수 없는 처지에 빠뜨렸다는 것 때문에. 그는 화가 나서 마치 그녀가 방 안에 있기라도 한 듯 거의 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 그러면 그녀의 비둘기 같은 목소리가, 항변하듯 애처롭게, 어둠을 흔들었고, 그녀의 입술이 비단결처럼 그의 이마를 스쳤으며, 그는 공기 속에서 그녀의 따스한 숨결을 분간할 수 있었다.\n\n이날 밤, 그가 깎아내리며 헐뜯던 그 여인은 제 남편이 얼마나 위대하고 선한 사람인지를 생각하고 있었다. 그러나 두 사람 위에는 앤젤 클레어가 알아챈 것보다 더 깊은 그늘이 드리워 있었으니, 곧 그 자신의 한계라는 그늘이었다. 판단의 독립을 그토록 애써 시도했음에도, 지난 이십오 년의 한 표본적 산물인 이 진보적이고 선의에 찬 젊은이는, 불시에 어린 시절의 가르침으로 되돌아가게 되자 여전히 관습과 인습의 노예였다. 본질적으로 따지자면 이 젊은 아내가, 악을 똑같이 싫어하는 마음을 지닌 다른 어떤 여인 못지않게 르무엘 왕의 칭송을 받을 자격이 있다는 것을—그녀의 도덕적 가치는 성취가 아니라 성향으로 헤아려야 한다는 것을—어떤 예언자도 그에게 일러 주지 않았고, 그 자신도 스스로에게 일러 줄 만한 예언자가 못 되었다. 게다가 가까이 있는 형상은 그런 경우 손해를 보는데, 그늘 없이 제 초라함을 고스란히 드러내기 때문이다. 반면 멀리 있는 어렴풋한 형상은 떠받들어지니, 그 거리가 그 얼룩마저 예술적 미덕으로 만들어 주는 까닭이다. 테스가 무엇이 아닌지를 따지느라, 그는 그녀가 무엇인지를 놓쳤고, 결함 있는 것이 온전한 것보다 더 클 수 있음을 잊었다.\n\n# XL\n\n아침 식사 자리에서는 브라질이 화제였고, 모두가 그 나라 땅을 두고 클레어가 벌이려는 실험을 희망적으로 보려 애썼다. 거기로 이민 갔다가 일 년 안에 고향으로 돌아온 몇몇 농장 일꾼들의 의기를 꺾는 소식에도 불구하고. 식사 후 클레어는 그곳에서 자기가 관계된 자질구레한 일들을 마무리하고 지역 은행에서 가진 돈을 죄다 찾으려고 작은 읍내로 들어갔다. 돌아오는 길에 그는 교회 옆에서 머시 챈트 양과 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 마치 그 교회 벽에서 풍겨 나온 무슨 산물 같았다. 그녀는 자기 반 학생들을 위한 성경책을 한 아름 안고 있었고, 그녀의 인생관이란, 남들에게는 가슴앓이를 일으키는 일들이 그녀에게는 더없이 행복한 미소를 빚어내게 하는 것이었다—부러운 결과이긴 했으나, 앤젤이 보기에 그것은 인간성을 신비주의에 바치는 기이하고 부자연스러운 희생으로 얻은 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 그가 잉글랜드를 떠나려 한다는 것을 듣고는, 그게 참으로 훌륭하고 전도유망한 계획 같다고 했다.\n\n\"그래, 상업적인 의미에서야 그럴듯한 계획이지, 아마도.\" 그가 대답했다. \"하지만 머시, 그건 삶의 연속성을 뚝 끊어 버려. 차라리 수도원이 더 나을지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"수도원이라뇨! 어머, 앤젤 클레어!\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"아니, 못된 사람 같으니, 수도원이라면 수도사를 뜻하고, 수도사라면 로마 가톨릭을 뜻하잖아요.\"\n\n\"그리고 로마 가톨릭은 죄를, 죄는 지옥살이를 뜻하고. 그대는 위태로운 처지에 있구려, 앤젤 클레어.\"\n\n\"저는 제 신교 신앙을 자랑으로 여겨요!\" 그녀가 엄하게 말했다.\n\n그러자 클레어는, 순전한 비참함에 빠져, 사람이 제 참된 원칙을 거스르며 짓궂게 구는 그런 악마적인 기분 중 하나에 사로잡혀, 그녀를 가까이 불러서는 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 이단적인 생각들을 마귀처럼 그녀의 귀에 속삭였다. 그녀의 고운 얼굴에 떠오른 경악을 보며 잠깐 웃던 그의 웃음은, 그것이 그의 안위를 걱정하는 고통과 불안으로 바뀌자 멎었다.\n\n\"머시.\" 그가 말했다. \"용서해 주오. 내가 미쳐 가고 있나 봐!\"\n\n그녀는 정말로 그가 그렇다고 생각했다. 그렇게 대화는 끝났고, 클레어는 다시 목사관으로 들어갔다. 그는 지역 은행가에게 더 행복한 날이 올 때까지 보석들을 맡겨 두었다. 또한 그는 은행에 삼십 파운드를 넣어 두어, 몇 달 뒤 테스가 필요로 할 때 그녀에게 보내지도록 했고, 블랙무어 골짜기의 친정에 있는 그녀에게 편지를 써 자기가 한 일을 알렸다. 이 금액에다 이미 그녀의 손에 쥐여 준 액수—약 오십 파운드—를 더하면, 당장의 그녀의 필요에는 넉넉히 충분하리라고 그는 바랐다. 특히 비상시에는 자기 아버지에게 청하라고 일러 두었으니 더욱.\n\n그는 그녀의 주소를 알려 부모를 그녀와 연락이 닿게 하지 않는 편이 가장 낫다고 여겼다. 그리고 두 사람을 갈라놓은 진짜 사정을 모르는 터라, 그의 아버지도 어머니도 그가 그렇게 하라고 권하지 않았다. 그날 낮에 그는 목사관을 떠났는데, 마무리해야 할 일을 어서 끝내고 싶었기 때문이다.\n\n잉글랜드의 이 지역을 떠나기 전 마지막 의무로서, 그는 웰브리지의 농가에 들러야 했다. 결혼 후 첫 사흘을 테스와 함께 보낸 곳으로, 약간의 집세를 치르고, 그들이 쓰던 방들의 열쇠를 돌려주고, 두고 온 자질구레한 물건 두세 가지를 가져와야 했다. 그의 삶에 드리워진 가장 깊은 그림자가 그 음울함을 그에게 펼쳐 보인 곳이 바로 이 지붕 아래였다. 그러나 그가 거실 문을 열고 안을 들여다보았을 때, 가장 먼저 되살아난 기억은 어느 비슷한 오후에 둘이서 행복하게 도착했던 일이었다. 한 거처를 함께 나눈다는 그 첫 신선한 느낌, 둘이 함께한 첫 식사, 손을 맞잡고 난롯가에서 도란거리던 일이었다.\n\n그가 방문했을 때 농부와 그 아내는 마침 들에 나가 있어서, 클레어는 한동안 방에 혼자 있었다. 자기도 미처 헤아리지 못했던 감정의 되살아남에 속으로 부풀어 오른 채, 그는 위층 그녀의 방으로 올라갔다. 한 번도 그의 방이었던 적 없는 방이었다. 침대는 그녀가 떠나던 날 아침 제 손으로 정돈해 놓은 그대로 매끈했다. 겨우살이는 그가 걸어 둔 그대로 닫집 아래 매달려 있었다. 거기 걸린 지 서너 주가 되어 빛이 바래 가고 있었고, 잎과 열매는 쪼글쪼글했다. 앤젤은 그것을 떼어 내 벽난로에 짓이겨 넣었다. 거기 서서, 그는 처음으로 이 어림짐작에서 자기가 택한 길이 과연 현명한 것이었는지, 하물며 너그러운 것이었는지를 의심했다. 그러나 자기가 잔인하게 눈이 멀었던 게 아니었던가? 갈피 없이 뒤엉킨 감정의 무리 속에서 그는 눈물 젖은 눈으로 침대맡에 무릎을 꿇었다. \"아, 테스! 진작 말해 주었더라면, 나는 용서했을 텐데!\" 그가 애통해했다.\n\n아래층에서 발소리가 들리자 그는 일어나 계단 꼭대기로 갔다. 계단 아래에 한 여자가 서 있는 것이 보였고, 그녀가 얼굴을 드는 순간 그는 창백하고 검은 눈을 한 이즈 휴엣을 알아보았다.\n\n\"클레어 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신과 클레어 부인을 뵈러, 또 두 분이 잘 계신지 여쭤보려고 들렀어요. 여기 다시 돌아와 계실지도 모른다고 생각했거든요.\"\n\n이 처녀는 그가 비밀을 짐작했으나 아직 그의 비밀은 짐작하지 못한 처녀였다. 그를 사랑하는 정직한 처녀, 테스만큼, 아니 거의 테스만큼 훌륭한 실속 있는 농부의 아내가 되었을 처녀였다.\n\n\"여기 혼자 있어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리는 이제 여기 살지 않습니다.\" 자기가 왜 왔는지 설명한 뒤, 그가 물었다. \"집에는 어느 길로 가나요, 이즈?\"\n\n\"이제 저는 탤버세이즈 낙농장에 집이 없어요, 나리.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇죠?\"\n\n이즈는 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"거기가 너무 적적해서 떠났어요! 저는 이쪽에서 지내고 있고요.\" 그녀는 반대 방향을, 그가 가려는 방향을 가리켰다.\n\n\"그럼—지금 거기로 가는 길인가요? 태워 주기를 바란다면 데려다줄게요.\"\n\n그녀의 올리브빛 얼굴이 한층 짙은 빛을 띠었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 클레어 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그는 곧 농부를 찾아 집세며, 숙소를 갑작스레 비우는 바람에 따져야 했던 몇 가지 다른 항목의 셈을 치렀다. 클레어가 말과 마차로 돌아오자 이즈가 그 옆자리로 뛰어올랐다.\n\n\"나는 잉글랜드를 떠날 거예요, 이즈.\" 마차를 몰아 가며 그가 말했다. \"브라질로 갑니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 클레어 부인도 그런 여정을 좋아하나요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"그녀는 지금은 안 가요—한 일 년쯤이라고 해 두죠. 나는 정찰하러 가는 거예요—거기 사는 게 어떤지 보러요.\"\n\n그들은 동쪽으로 꽤 먼 거리를 달려 나갔고, 이즈는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"다들 어떻게 지내요?\" 그가 물었다. \"레티는 어때요?\"\n\n\"제가 마지막으로 봤을 땐 좀 신경 쇠약 같은 상태였어요. 어찌나 야위고 볼이 푹 꺼졌는지 폐병이라도 든 것 같더라고요. 이젠 아무도 그애한테 반하지 않을 거예요.\" 이즈가 멍하니 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 매리언은요?\"\n\n이즈는 목소리를 낮추었다.\n\n\"매리언은 술을 마셔요.\"\n\n\"저런!\"\n\n\"네. 낙농장 주인이 그애를 내보냈어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은요!\"\n\n\"저는 술도 안 마시고, 폐병도 안 들었어요. 하지만—이젠 아침 먹기 전에 노래 부르는 건 영 신통치 않아요!\"\n\n\"왜 그래요? 아침 젖 짤 때 '큐피드의 정원에서'랑 '재단사의 바지'를 얼마나 깔끔하게 뽑아내곤 했는지 기억나요?\"\n\n\"아, 네! 나리가 처음 오셨을 때였죠. 한참 계신 다음이 아니라요.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 시들해졌죠?\"\n\n그녀의 검은 눈이 대답 대신 한순간 그의 얼굴로 번득 치솟았다.\n\n\"이즈!—그건 너무 어리석은 일이에요—나 같은 사람한테!\" 그가 말하고는 상념에 잠겼다. \"그럼—만약 내가 당신한테 결혼하자고 청했더라면?\"\n\n\"그러셨다면 저는 '네'라고 했을 거고, 나리는 자기를 사랑하는 여자와 결혼하셨을 거예요!\"\n\n\"정말로요!\"\n\n\"바닥까지 송두리째요!\" 그녀가 격하게 속삭였다. \"아, 맙소사! 지금까지 한 번도 눈치채지 못하셨던 거예요!\"\n\n이윽고 그들은 어느 마을로 갈라지는 샛길에 다다랐다.\n\n\"여기서 내려야 해요. 저는 저쪽에 살아요.\" 이즈가 불쑥 말했다. 고백한 뒤로 한 번도 입을 열지 않았던 그녀였다.\n\n클레어는 말을 늦추었다. 그는 자기 운명에 분노했고, 사회의 규범들에 쓰라린 적의를 품었다. 그것들이 그를 한 구석에 가두어, 거기서는 떳떳한 길이라곤 빠져나갈 데가 없었기 때문이다. 이렇게 옭아매듯 인습의 회초리에 입을 맞추는 대신, 차라리 앞으로의 가정사를 헐겁게 꾸려 사회에 앙갚음을 하면 어떤가?\n\n\"나는 브라질로 혼자 가요, 이즈.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 아내와 헤어졌어요, 여행 때문이 아니라 개인적인 이유로. 다시는 그녀와 함께 살지 않을지도 몰라요. 당신을 사랑하지는 못할지도 모르지만—그녀 대신 나와 함께 가지 않겠어요?\"\n\n\"정말로 제가 가기를 바라세요?\"\n\n\"그래요. 나는 충분히 험하게 당했으니 위안을 바랄 만하잖아요. 그리고 적어도 당신은 사심 없이 나를 사랑하니까요.\"\n\n\"네—갈게요.\" 이즈가 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"가겠다고요? 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알아요, 이즈?\"\n\n\"나리가 거기 가 계신 동안 제가 나리와 함께 산다는 뜻이지요—저한테는 그걸로 충분해요.\"\n\n\"명심해요, 이제 당신은 도덕에 관해선 나를 믿어선 안 돼요. 하지만 일러 두건대, 이건 문명의—그러니까 서구 문명의 눈에는 나쁜 짓일 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런 건 상관없어요. 막다른 고통에 이르러 달리 길이 없을 땐 어느 여자도 상관 안 해요!\"\n\n\"그럼 내리지 말고, 그 자리에 그대로 앉아 있어요.\"\n\n그는 애정의 기색이라곤 조금도 보이지 않은 채 갈림길을 지나, 일 마일, 이 마일을 몰아갔다.\n\n\"당신은 나를 아주, 아주 많이 사랑해요, 이즈?\" 그가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"그래요—그렇다고 말했잖아요! 우리가 낙농장에 함께 있던 내내 나리를 사랑했어요!\"\n\n\"테스보다 더요?\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"그녀보다 더는 아니에요.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 아무도 테스만큼 나리를 사랑할 순 없을 테니까요!… 그애는 나리를 위해 목숨이라도 내놓았을 거예요. 저는 그 이상은 못 해요.\"\n\n브올 산꼭대기의 예언자처럼, 이즈 휴엣도 그런 순간 비뚜로 말하고 싶었을 테지만, 테스의 인품이 그녀의 거친 본성에 행사한 매혹이 그녀로 하여금 어쩔 수 없이 갸륵하게 굴게 했다.\n\n클레어는 잠자코 있었다. 이토록 뜻밖의, 흠잡을 데 없는 데서 나온 이 솔직한 말에 그의 마음이 북받쳤다. 그의 목구멍에는 마치 흐느낌이 굳어 버린 듯한 무언가가 걸려 있었다. 그의 귀에는 그 말이 거듭 울렸다. \"그애는 나리를 위해 목숨이라도 내놓았을 거예요. 저는 그 이상은 못 해요!\"\n\n\"우리가 부질없이 떠든 이야기는 잊어요, 이즈.\" 그가 별안간 말 머리를 돌리며 말했다. \"내가 무슨 말을 지껄였는지 모르겠어요! 이제 당신 샛길이 갈라지는 데까지 다시 데려다줄게요.\"\n\n\"솔직하게 군 대가가 이거로군요! 아—내가 이걸 어떻게 견뎌요—어떻게—어떻게!\"\n\n이즈 휴엣은 격한 울음을 터뜨리며, 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 깨닫고는 제 이마를 쳐 댔다.\n\n\"자리에 없는 사람을 위해 베푼 그 가엾고 작은 정의를 후회하는 거예요? 아, 이즈, 후회로 그걸 망치지 말아요!\"\n\n그녀는 차츰 마음을 가라앉혔다.\n\n\"알겠어요, 나리. 어쩌면 저도, 가겠다고 했을 때, 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 몰랐던 거겠지요! 저는 바라요—이루어질 수 없는 것을!\"\n\n\"내겐 이미 사랑하는 아내가 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"네, 네! 있으시지요!\"\n\n그들은 반 시간 전에 지나쳤던 그 샛길 모퉁이에 다다랐고, 그녀는 폴짝 내렸다.\n\n\"이즈—제발, 제발 내 한순간의 경솔함을 잊어 줘요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"정말 생각 없는 짓, 분별없는 짓이었어요!\"\n\n\"잊으라고요? 절대로, 절대로요! 아, 저한테는 경솔함이 아니었어요!\"\n\n그는 그 상처 입은 외침이 전하는 비난을 자신이 얼마나 톡톡히 받아 마땅한지를 느꼈고, 이루 말할 수 없는 슬픔에 잠겨 마차에서 뛰어내려 그녀의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"그래도, 이즈, 우리 어쨌든 친구로 헤어져요? 당신은 내가 무얼 견뎌야 했는지 모를 거예요!\"\n\n그녀는 참으로 너그러운 처녀였고, 둘의 작별을 더는 쓰라림으로 흐리게 하지 않았다.\n\n\"용서할게요, 나리!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 이즈.\" 그녀가 그 곁에 서 있는 동안, 그는 자기가 도무지 느끼지 못하는 스승의 역할을 억지로 떠맡으며 말했다. \"매리언을 만나거든 부디 일러 줘요, 착한 여자가 되어 어리석음에 빠지지 말라고. 그러겠다고 약속해 줘요. 그리고 레티에게는, 세상에 나보다 더 가치 있는 사내들이 많으니, 나를 위해서라도 슬기롭고 바르게 행동하라고 전해 줘요—이 말을 기억해요—슬기롭고 바르게—나를 위해서. 나는 죽어 가는 자가 죽어 가는 이들에게 보내듯 이 전갈을 그들에게 보내는 거예요. 다시는 그들을 못 볼 테니까. 그리고 당신, 이지, 당신은 내 아내에 관한 그 정직한 말로, 도무지 믿기지 않는 어리석음과 배신의 충동에서 나를 구해 주었어요. 여자도 나쁠 수 있지만, 이런 일에서 남자만큼 나쁘진 않아요! 그 한 가지만으로도 나는 당신을 결코 잊을 수 없어요. 지금껏 그래 왔듯 늘 착하고 진실한 처녀로 있어 줘요. 그리고 나를, 보잘것없는 연인이었으나 충실한 벗으로 여겨 줘요. 약속해 줘요.\"\n\n그녀는 약속했다.\n\n\"하늘이 당신을 축복하고 지켜 주시기를, 나리. 안녕히 가세요!\"\n\n그는 마차를 몰아갔다. 그러나 이즈가 샛길로 접어들어 클레어가 시야에서 사라지자마자, 그녀는 찢어지는 듯한 고통의 발작에 휩싸여 둑에 몸을 내던졌다. 그리고 그날 밤 늦게 어머니의 오두막에 들어설 때 그녀의 얼굴은 일그러진 부자연스러운 모습이었다. 앤젤 클레어와 헤어진 뒤부터 집에 다다르기까지 그 어두운 시간을 이즈가 어떻게 보냈는지는 아무도 끝내 듣지 못했다.\n\n클레어 역시 그 처녀에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 쑤시는 듯한 상념과 떨리는 입술에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 그의 슬픔은 이즈를 위한 것이 아니었다. 그날 저녁 그는 가장 가까운 역으로 가는 길을 버리고, 자기를 테스의 집과 갈라놓는 사우스웨식스의 그 드높은 등줄기 같은 산마루를 가로질러 마차를 몰고 갈 마음의 깃털 한 올 무게만큼의 차이로 아슬아슬한 지경에 있었다. 그를 멈춰 세운 것은 그녀의 천성에 대한 경멸도, 그녀의 마음이 어떤 상태일지에 대한 짐작도 아니었다.\n\n아니. 그것은, 이즈의 인정으로 입증되었듯 그녀의 사랑에도 불구하고, 사실은 변하지 않았다는 느낌이었다. 처음에 그가 옳았다면, 지금도 그는 옳았다. 그리고 그가 발을 들여놓은 그 길의 관성이, 이날 오후 그에게 작용한 것보다 더 강하고 더 지속적인 힘에 의해 방향이 틀어지지 않는 한, 그를 그 길로 계속 나아가게 했다. 그녀에게는 머지않아 돌아올 수 있을 터였다. 그는 그날 밤 런던행 기차에 올랐고, 닷새 뒤에는 승선하는 항구에서 형들과 작별의 악수를 나누었다.\n\n# XLI\n\n겨울철의 앞선 사건들로부터 시계를 앞당겨, 클레어와 테스가 헤어진 지 여덟 달이 넘은 어느 시월의 하루로 넘어가자. 우리는 후자가 달라진 처지에 놓인 것을 보게 된다. 남들이 짐 상자와 트렁크를 날라 주던 신부가 아니라, 예전 그녀가 신부가 아니었던 시절처럼 바구니와 보따리를 제 손으로 든 외로운 여인을. 이 시련의 기간 동안 그녀의 안락을 위해 남편이 마련해 둔 넉넉한 자금 대신, 그녀가 내놓을 수 있는 것은 납작해진 지갑 하나뿐이다.\n\n고향 말럿을 다시 떠난 뒤, 그녀는 몸을 크게 혹사하는 일 없이 봄과 여름을 견뎌 냈다. 그동안은 주로 블랙무어 골짜기 서쪽 포트브레디 인근에서 가벼운 낙농 일을 비정규로 하며 보냈는데, 그곳은 그녀의 고향에서도 탤버세이즈에서도 똑같이 먼 곳이었다. 그녀는 이쪽이 그가 준 생활비로 살아가는 것보다 나았다. 정신적으로 그녀는 완전한 정체 상태에 머물러 있었고, 그 기계적인 일은 이 상태를 막기보다 오히려 부추겼다. 그녀의 의식은 저 다른 낙농장에, 저 다른 계절에, 거기서 그녀와 마주했던 그 다정한 연인의 곁에 가 있었다—그녀가 제 것으로 붙들려고 움켜쥐는 순간 환영 속 형상처럼 사라져 버린 그 사람의 곁에.\n\n낙농 일은 젖이 줄어들기 시작할 때까지만 이어졌다. 탤버세이즈에서처럼 정규로 두 번째 고용된 게 아니라, 그저 보조 인력으로 일했기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 수확철이 시작되고 있던 터라, 그녀는 그저 목초지에서 그루터기 밭으로 자리를 옮기기만 하면 일거리는 얼마든지 있었고, 이 일은 수확이 끝날 때까지 이어졌다.\n\n클레어가 준 생활비 가운데 그녀에게 남았던 이십오 파운드는—오십 파운드의 나머지 절반을 자기가 부모에게 끼친 수고와 비용에 대한 보탬으로 떼어 준 뒤 남은 것이었는데—아직 거의 쓰지 않은 상태였다. 그러나 이제 궂은 날씨가 불운하게도 한동안 이어졌고, 그 기간에 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 그 금화들에 손을 대야 했다.\n\n그녀는 그것들을 떠나보내는 일을 견딜 수 없었다. 앤젤이 제 손에 쥐여 준 것이었고, 그녀를 위해 은행에서 반짝거리는 새것으로 받아 온 것이었다. 그의 손길이 그것들을 자신을 기리는 기념물로 신성하게 만들어 놓았던 것이다—그것들은 아직 그와 그녀 자신의 경험으로 빚어진 것 말고는 어떤 다른 내력도 없는 듯했다—그러니 그것들을 흩어 보내는 일은 성물을 내주는 것과 같았다. 그러나 그녀는 그래야만 했고, 하나씩 하나씩 그것들은 그녀의 손을 떠났다.\n\n그녀는 어머니에게 이따금 주소를 알려 줄 수밖에 없었지만, 자기 형편은 숨겼다. 돈이 거의 다 떨어졌을 무렵 어머니에게서 편지 한 통이 닿았다. 조앤은 자기들이 끔찍한 곤경에 처했다고 적었다. 가을비가 집의 이엉을 뚫고 새서 통째로 새로 이어야 하는데, 지난번 이엉 값을 한 번도 치르지 못한 탓에 그렇게 할 수가 없다는 것이었다. 새 서까래와 위층의 새 천장도 필요한데, 지난번 청구액까지 합하면 이십 파운드에 이르리라고 했다. 사위가 재력 있는 사람이고 지금쯤은 틀림없이 돌아왔을 테니, 그 돈을 보내 줄 수 없겠느냐는 것이었다.\n\n테스는 거의 곧바로 앤젤의 은행가에게서 받을 삼십 파운드가 있었고, 사정이 그토록 딱한지라 그 돈을 받자마자 청한 대로 이십 파운드를 보냈다. 나머지의 일부는 겨울옷에 쓸 수밖에 없었고, 코앞에 닥친 그 모진 계절 내내 쓸 돈으로는 명목뿐인 금액만 남았다. 마지막 한 파운드마저 떠나가자, 더 자금이 필요할 때면 언제든 자기 아버지에게 청하라던 앤젤의 말이 따져 볼 거리로 남았다.\n\n그러나 테스는 그 일을 생각하면 할수록 그렇게 하기가 더 꺼려졌다. 클레어를 생각하는 마음에서 비롯된 똑같은 섬세함, 자존심, 헛된 수치심—뭐라 부르든 간에—이 그녀로 하여금 친정 부모에게마저 이 소원함이 길어지고 있음을 숨기게 했던 것인데, 바로 그것이 그가 넉넉히 남겨 준 생활비를 받고도 자기가 궁핍하다는 것을 그의 부모에게 털어놓지 못하게 막았다. 그들은 아마 이미 그녀를 멸시하고 있을 텐데, 동냥하는 처지의 그녀를 얼마나 더 멸시하겠는가! 그 결과, 목사의 며느리는 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 자기 처지를 그에게 알릴 마음이 들지 않았다.\n\n남편의 부모와 연락하기를 꺼리는 마음은 시간이 흐르면 줄어들지도 모른다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 친정에 대해서는 정반대였다. 결혼 후 짧게 다녀간 그 방문 끝에 그 집을 떠날 때, 그들은 그녀가 결국 남편과 합류하러 가는 것이라는 인상을 갖고 있었다. 그리고 그때부터 지금까지 그녀는, 자기가 편안하게 그의 귀환을 기다리고 있다는 그들의 믿음을 깨뜨릴 일을 아무것도 하지 않았다. 그의 브라질 여정이 짧은 체류로만 끝나, 그 후에 그가 자기를 데리러 오거나, 아니면 합류하라고 편지를 써 보내거나—어쨌거나 머지않아 두 사람이 양가와 세상 앞에 하나 된 모습을 보이게 되리라고, 헛된 희망인 줄 알면서도 기대했다. 이 희망을 그녀는 여전히 품고 있었다. 첫 번째 시도의 좌절을 무위로 돌려 줄 그 떠들썩한 결혼을 한 뒤에, 자기가 버림받은 아내이며, 부모의 곤궁을 덜어 준 지금은 제 두 손으로 벌어 먹고살아야 하는 처지임을 부모에게 알린다는 것은, 정말이지 너무 과한 일이었다.\n\n그 한 벌의 휘황한 보석들이 그녀의 머릿속에 다시 떠올랐다. 클레어가 그것들을 어디에 맡겨 두었는지 그녀는 몰랐고, 자기가 그것들을 쓸 수만 있고 팔 수는 없다는 게 사실이라면 그건 별로 중요치도 않았다. 설령 그것들이 온전히 그녀의 것이라 하더라도, 본질적으로는 조금도 제 것이 아닌 그것들에 대한 법적 권리로 제 배를 불린다는 것은 더없이 비열한 짓일 터였다.\n\n그동안 그녀 남편의 나날도 결코 시련에서 자유롭지 않았다. 바로 이 순간 그는 브라질 쿠리치바 인근의 진흙땅에서 열병으로 앓아누워 있었다. 천둥 폭풍우에 흠뻑 젖고 갖은 고난에 시달리면서. 마침 이 무렵 브라질 정부의 약속에 속아, 또 영국의 고지대에서 쟁기질하고 씨 뿌리며 타고난 온갖 날씨를 다 견뎌 낸 그 몸뚱이들이 브라질 평원에서 불시에 들이닥치는 온갖 날씨도 똑같이 잘 견뎌 내리라는 근거 없는 억측에 속아 그리로 건너간 모든 영국 농부와 농장 일꾼들과 매한가지였다.\n\n다시 본론으로 돌아가자. 그리하여 테스의 마지막 금화마저 다 써 버렸을 때 그녀에게는 그것들을 대신할 다른 돈이 마련되어 있지 않았고, 게다가 철 탓에 일자리를 얻기가 점점 더 어려워졌다. 지성과 활력과 건강과 성실함이 삶의 어느 영역에서든 얼마나 드문 것인지 알지 못했던 까닭에, 그녀는 실내 일자리를 구하기를 삼갔다. 읍내며, 큰 저택이며, 재력 있고 사회적으로 세련된 사람들이며, 시골과는 다른 몸가짐의 사람들이 두려웠던 것이다. 그 점잖음의 방면에서 검은 근심이 찾아왔던 터였다. 사회는 자기가 얕은 경험으로 짐작하는 것보다 나을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그녀에게는 그것을 입증할 증거가 없었고, 그런 처지에서 그녀의 본능은 사회의 언저리를 피하는 것이었다.\n\n봄과 여름 동안 그녀가 보조 젖 짜는 처녀로 일했던 포트브레디 너머 서쪽의 작은 낙농장들은 더는 일손이 필요치 않았다. 탤버세이즈라면, 순전한 동정에서라도, 아마 그녀에게 자리를 내주었을 것이다. 그러나 그곳에서의 삶이 아무리 편안했어도 그녀는 돌아갈 수 없었다. 그 반전이 너무도 견디기 어려울 터였고, 그녀가 돌아가면 자기가 떠받드는 남편에게 비난이 쏟아질지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 그들의 연민을, 또 자기의 기이한 처지를 두고 서로 수군거리는 소리를 견딜 수 없었을 것이다. 다만 자기 사연이 사람들 각자의 마음속에 따로따로 고립되어 머무는 한, 거기 있는 모든 사람이 자기 형편을 다 알게 되는 것쯤은 거의 마주할 수 있었을 것이다. 그녀의 예민함을 움츠러들게 한 것은 그녀에 관한 생각들이 서로 오가는 일이었다. 테스는 이 구별을 설명할 수 없었다. 그저 그렇게 느낀다는 것만 알 뿐이었다.\n\n이제 그녀는 카운티 한복판의 고지대 농장으로 가는 길이었다. 매리언에게서 떠돌다 그녀에게 닿은 편지가 추천해 준 곳이었다. 매리언은 어찌어찌—아마 이즈 휴엣을 통해—테스가 남편과 헤어졌다는 소식을 들었고, 마음씨 좋되 이제는 술독에 빠진 그 처녀는 테스가 곤경에 처했으리라 여기고는, 자기가 낙농장을 떠난 뒤 이 고지대로 왔다는 것과, 정말로 그녀가 예전처럼 다시 일하고 있는 거라면 일손이 더 필요한 이곳에서 만나고 싶다는 것을 옛 친구에게 서둘러 알려 온 것이었다.\n\n날이 짧아져 감에 따라 남편의 용서를 얻으리라는 희망은 모두 그녀를 떠나기 시작했다. 그리고 그녀가 정처 없이 떠돌게 만든 그 무심한 본능에는 어딘가 야생 짐승의 습성 같은 데가 있었다—걸음마다 사건 많던 과거에서 자신을 조금씩 떼어 내며, 제 정체를 지워 가며, 자기가 어디 있는지를 남들이—제 행복에 중요한, 그들의 행복에는 아니더라도—재빨리 알아낼지도 모를 우연이나 변수 따위는 조금도 마음에 두지 않으면서.\n\n외로운 처지에서 겪는 어려움 가운데 적지 않은 것이, 그녀의 용모가 끄는 시선이었다. 그녀의 타고난 매력에다 클레어에게서 물려받은 어떤 기품 있는 태도까지 더해진 까닭이었다. 결혼을 위해 마련했던 옷들이 남아 있는 동안에는 이렇게 무심코 던져지는 관심 어린 눈길도 그녀에게 별 불편을 주지 않았다. 그러나 들일하는 여자의 작업복을 걸쳐야 하게 되자마자, 거친 말이 그녀에게 한두 번이 아니게 건네졌다. 그래도 몸에 대한 두려움을 일으킬 일은 어느 십일월의 한 오후가 되기까지는 일어나지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 지금 가려는 고지대 농장보다 브릿 강 서쪽의 시골을 더 좋아했었다. 한 가지 이유로는 그곳이 남편의 아버지 집에서 더 가까웠기 때문이다. 그리고 언젠가 목사관에 들러 보기로 마음먹을지도 모른다는 생각으로, 알아보는 이 없이 그 일대를 맴도는 일이 그녀에게는 즐거움을 주었다. 그러나 일단 더 높고 메마른 지대를 시도해 보기로 마음을 정하고 나자, 그녀는 다시 동쪽으로 발걸음을 돌려, 그날 밤을 묵으려는 초크뉴턴 마을을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n샛길은 길고 단조로웠고, 날이 부쩍 짧아진 탓에 그녀가 미처 알아채기도 전에 땅거미가 그녀를 덮쳤다. 샛길이 구불구불한 길이를 군데군데 드러내며 뻗어 내려가는 어느 언덕마루에 다다랐을 때, 그녀는 등 뒤에서 발소리를 들었고, 몇 순간 만에 한 남자가 그녀를 따라잡았다. 그는 테스 옆에 바짝 다가서더니 말했다—\n\n\"안녕하시오, 예쁜 아가씨.\" 그녀는 거기에 공손히 답했다.\n\n하늘에 아직 남은 빛이 그녀의 얼굴을 비추었다. 풍경은 거의 어두웠지만. 남자는 돌아서서 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"이런, 틀림없이, 한동안 트랜트리지에 있던 그 젊은 처자로구먼—젊은 더버빌 지주 나리의 정인 말이오. 나도 그때 거기 있었지, 지금은 거기 안 살지만.\"\n\n그녀는 그가, 자기에게 상스럽게 말을 걸었다가 술집에서 앤젤에게 얻어맞아 쓰러졌던 그 잘사는 촌뜨기임을 알아보았다. 고통의 경련이 그녀를 꿰뚫고 지나갔고, 그녀는 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"솔직하게 인정할 만큼은 솔직하구려, 내가 읍내에서 한 말이 사실이었다고 말이오. 당신 정부가 그렇게 펄펄 뛰긴 했어도—그렇잖소, 이 능청맞은 것아? 따지고 보면 그자가 날린 그 주먹질에 대해선 당신이 나한테 용서를 빌어야 할 거요.\"\n\n여전히 테스에게서는 아무 대답도 나오지 않았다. 쫓기는 그녀의 영혼에게는 빠져나갈 길이 오직 하나뿐인 듯했다. 그녀는 별안간 바람 같은 속도로 줄행랑을 쳤고, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 길을 따라 달려, 곧장 어느 조림지로 통하는 문에 이르렀다. 그녀는 그 안으로 뛰어들었고, 누가 발견할 가능성조차 없을 만큼 그 그늘 깊숙이 들어가서야 비로소 멈추었다.\n\n발밑의 낙엽은 메말라 있었고, 낙엽수들 사이에 자란 호랑가시나무 덤불 몇 그루의 잎이 바람을 막아 줄 만큼 빽빽했다. 그녀는 마른 잎을 한곳에 그러모아 큰 더미를 만들고, 그 한가운데에 일종의 둥지 같은 것을 빚었다. 테스는 그 안으로 기어들었다.\n\n그렇게 든 잠은 자연히 토막토막 끊기는 것이었다. 그녀는 이상한 소리가 들리는 것 같았지만, 그건 산들바람 탓이라고 스스로를 타일렀다. 그녀는 자기는 여기 추운 곳에 있는데 남편은 지구 반대편 어딘가 막연하게 따뜻한 땅에 있는 것을 생각했다. 세상에 자기만큼 비참한 존재가 또 있을까? 테스는 자문했고, 허비해 버린 제 삶을 생각하며 말했다. \"모든 것이 헛되다.\" 그녀는 그 말을 기계적으로 되뇌다가, 이것이 현대에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 생각이라는 데 생각이 미쳤다. 솔로몬은 이천 년도 더 전에 거기까지는 생각했다. 그녀 자신은, 비록 사상가들의 선두에 선 것은 아니어도, 훨씬 더 멀리 나아가 있었다. 모든 것이 그저 헛되기만 하다면, 누가 그걸 마음에 두겠는가? 모든 것은, 아아, 헛됨보다 더 나빴다—불의요, 형벌이요, 강요요, 죽음이었다. 앤젤 클레어의 아내는 제 이마에 손을 얹어, 그 곡선과, 부드러운 살갗 밑으로 만져지는 눈구멍의 가장자리를 느꼈고, 그러면서 언젠가 그 뼈가 앙상히 드러날 때가 오리라고 생각했다. \"지금이 그때라면 좋겠어.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n이런 변덕스러운 공상에 잠겨 있던 와중에 그녀는 낙엽 사이에서 새롭고 이상한 소리를 들었다. 바람일지도 몰랐다. 하지만 바람이라곤 거의 없었다. 어떤 때는 팔딱이는 소리였고, 어떤 때는 파닥이는 소리였으며, 어떤 때는 헐떡임이나 그렁거림 같은 소리였다. 머지않아 그녀는 그 소리가 무슨 야생 생물에게서 나는 것임을 확신하게 되었는데, 머리 위 나뭇가지에서 시작된 그 소리에 뒤이어 무거운 몸뚱이가 땅에 쿵 떨어지는 소리가 나자 더욱 그러했다. 다른, 더 즐거운 사정 아래 여기 몸을 숨기고 있었더라면 그녀는 겁에 질렸을 것이다. 그러나 인간 세계 바깥에 있는 지금, 그녀에게는 두려움이 없었다.\n\n마침내 하늘에서 날이 밝았다. 위쪽이 한동안 환해지고 나자 숲속에도 날이 밝았다.\n\n세상의 활동하는 시간을 알리는 안심되고 무미건조한 빛이 강해지자마자, 그녀는 낙엽 더미 밑에서 기어 나와 대담하게 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러고는 자기를 들쑤셔 놓은 일이 무엇이었는지를 알아챘다. 그녀가 몸을 피한 그 조림지는 이 지점에서 뾰족한 끄트머리로 좁아져 이쪽 끝에서 끝났고, 울타리 바깥은 경작지였다. 나무 아래에는 꿩 여러 마리가 흩어져 있었는데, 그 화려한 깃털이 피로 얼룩져 있었다. 더러는 죽어 있었고, 더러는 날개를 힘없이 파닥이고 있었으며, 더러는 하늘을 멀거니 올려다보고 있었고, 더러는 빠르게 헐떡이고 있었으며, 더러는 몸이 뒤틀려 있었고, 더러는 길게 뻗어 있었다—다들 고통 속에 몸부림치고 있었다. 다만 밤사이 더는 견디지 못한 본성 탓에 그 괴로움이 끝나 버린 운 좋은 것들만 빼고.\n\n테스는 이것이 무슨 뜻인지 단박에 짐작했다. 그 새들은 어제 어느 사냥 패거리에게 이 구석으로 몰려 내려온 것이었다. 그리고 총에 맞아 그 자리에서 죽어 떨어졌거나 밤이 되기 전에 죽은 것들은 사람들이 찾아내어 가져갔지만, 심하게 다친 새들 가운데 다수는 달아나 숨어 버렸거나, 빽빽한 나뭇가지 사이로 날아올라 거기에 자리를 지키고 있다가, 밤사이 피를 흘려 점점 기운이 빠지자 그녀가 들은 대로 하나둘씩 떨어져 내린 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 처녀 시절에 이런 사내들을 이따금 언뜻 본 적이 있었다. 울타리 너머를 살피거나 덤불 사이로 엿보며 총을 겨누던 모습, 야릇하게 차려입고 눈에는 피에 굶주린 빛을 띤 모습을. 그녀는 들어 알고 있었다. 그자들이 그때는 거칠고 잔인해 보여도 일 년 내내 그런 것은 아니며, 사실은 가을과 겨울의 몇 주를 빼면 자못 점잖은 사람들이라는 것을. 다만 그 몇 주 동안에는, 말레이반도의 주민들처럼, 광기에 사로잡혀 날뛰며 생명을 파괴하는 것을 제 목적으로 삼는다는 것을—이 경우에는 순전히 그자들의 그 성향을 충족시키려는 목적으로 인공적인 수단을 써서 태어나게 한, 자연의 그 우글거리는 한 가족 안에서 저보다 약한 형제들에게 그토록 무례하고 그토록 비겁한 짓을 저지르는, 죄 없는 깃털 달린 생물들을.\n\n제 일처럼 같은 처지의 고난받는 것들을 위해 아파할 수 있는 영혼의 충동으로, 테스의 첫 생각은 아직 살아 있는 새들을 그 고통에서 벗어나게 해 주는 것이었다. 이를 위해 그녀는 제 손으로, 찾을 수 있는 만큼의 새들 목을 비틀어 꺾고는, 사냥터지기들이—아마도 올 텐데—그것들을 다시 찾으러 올 때까지 찾은 자리에 그대로 놓아두었다.\n\n\"가엾은 것들아—너희들의 이런 비참함을 보면서도 나 자신을 세상에서 가장 비참한 존재로 여겼다니!\" 그녀는 새들을 부드럽게 죽이며 눈물을 흘리면서 외쳤다. \"내겐 몸이 쑤시는 아픔조차 한 점 없는데! 나는 갈가리 찢기지도 않았고, 피를 흘리지도 않으며, 나를 먹이고 입힐 두 손이 있는데.\" 그녀는 자연에 아무런 근거도 없는, 사회의 자의적인 법 아래 단죄당했다는 느낌, 그보다 더 만져질 것 없는 무언가에 바탕을 둔 간밤의 그 우울을 스스로 부끄러워했다.\n\n# XLII\n\n이제 날이 훤히 밝았고, 그녀는 다시 길을 나서, 큰길로 조심스레 나왔다. 그러나 조심할 필요는 없었다. 가까이에는 사람 그림자 하나 없었고, 테스는 꿋꿋이 앞으로 나아갔다. 밤새 고통을 말없이 견뎌 낸 새들의 기억이 그녀에게 슬픔이란 상대적인 것이며, 한번 견해 따위를 멸시할 만큼 충분히 높이 올라설 수만 있다면 제 슬픔쯤은 견딜 만한 것임을 일깨워 주었다. 그러나 그것이 클레어에게 견지되는 한 그녀는 그렇게 할 수 없었다.\n\n그녀는 초크뉴턴에 다다라 어느 여관에서 아침을 먹었는데, 거기서 젊은이 몇이 그녀의 고운 외모를 두고 성가실 만큼 추켜세웠다. 어쩐 일인지 그녀는 희망을 느꼈다. 남편 역시 아직도 그녀에게 이와 똑같은 말을 해 줄 수 있을지도 모르지 않는가? 그 가능성을 위해서라도 그녀는 자신을 잘 건사하고, 이렇게 무심코 다가오는 구애자들을 떨쳐 내야 했다. 이를 위해 테스는 제 용모로 인해 더는 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰지 않기로 결심했다. 마을을 벗어나자마자 그녀는 덤불숲으로 들어가 바구니에서 가장 낡은 들일용 가운 하나를 꺼냈다. 낙농장에서조차 한 번도 입은 적 없는, 말럿에서 그루터기 밭을 누비며 일하던 때 이후로는 입어 본 적 없는 옷이었다. 또 그녀는 기막힌 생각이 떠올라 보따리에서 손수건을 하나 꺼내, 마치 치통을 앓는 사람처럼 턱과 양 볼과 관자놀이 절반을 가리며 보닛 아래 얼굴에 둘러 묶었다. 그러고는 주머니 거울의 도움을 받아 작은 가위로 인정사정없이 제 눈썹을 잘라 냈고, 그렇게 공격적인 흠모를 막아 둔 채 울퉁불퉁한 제 길을 다시 갔다.\n\n\"무슨 허수아비 같은 처자람!\" 다음으로 그녀와 마주친 사내가 일행에게 말했다.\n\n그 말을 들으며 그녀는 순전히 제 신세가 가여워 눈물이 핑 돌았다. \"하지만 난 상관없어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아, 그래—난 상관없어! 이제 난 늘 못생긴 채로 있을 테야. 앤젤이 여기 없고, 나를 보살펴 줄 사람이 아무도 없으니까. 내 남편이었던 사람은 떠나 버렸고, 다시는 나를 사랑하지 않겠지. 하지만 나는 한결같이 그이를 사랑하고, 다른 모든 남자는 미워하며, 그자들이 나를 경멸스럽게 여기도록 만들고 싶어!\"\n\n이렇게 테스는 걸어간다. 풍경의 한 부분이 된 형상이요, 겨울 차림을 한 더없이 순박한 들일하는 여인이다. 회색 서지 망토에, 붉은 모직 목도리, 누르스름한 갈색의 거친 작업복에 덮인 거친 천 치마, 그리고 담황색 가죽 장갑. 그 낡은 옷의 실 한 올 한 올이 빗방울의 매질과 햇살의 그을림과 바람의 시달림 아래 바래고 닳아 버렸다. 이제 그녀에게서는 젊은 정열의 기색이라곤 찾아볼 수 없다—\n\n처녀의 입은 차갑고 . . . . . 겹겹이 단순한 주름이 그 머리를 감싼다.\n\n거의 지각도 없는, 거의 무기물에 가까운 무언가를 보듯 눈길이 그 위를 미끄러져 갔을 법한 이 겉모습 속에는, 그러나 나이에 비해 만물의 티끌과 재를, 욕정의 잔인함과 사랑의 덧없음을 너무도 잘 배워 버린 한 고동치는 생명의 기록이 들어 있었다.\n\n이튿날 날씨는 궂었지만 그녀는 터벅터벅 걸어 나아갔다. 원초적 적의의 정직함과 직선적임과 공평무사함은 그녀를 거의 당황시키지 못했다. 그녀의 목적은 겨울 한 철의 일거리와 겨울 한 철의 거처였으므로, 허비할 시간이 없었다. 짧은 고용에 대한 그녀의 경험은, 더는 그런 것을 받아들이지 않겠다고 결심하게 할 만큼이었다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는 매리언이 편지를 써 보낸 그 장소를 향해 농장에서 농장으로 나아갔다. 그곳은 소문난 고됨이 끌리기는커녕 그 반대인지라, 마지막 수단으로만 쓰기로 마음먹은 곳이었다. 먼저 그녀는 더 가벼운 종류의 일자리를 알아보았고, 그런 일이라면 어느 것을 구해도 받아들여지기가 가망 없어지자, 그다음으로 덜 가벼운 일을 청했다. 그렇게 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 낙농과 가금 돌보기로 시작했다가, 가장 싫어하는 무겁고 거친 일로—경작지 일로—끝났다. 정말이지 그녀가 스스로 자청해서는 결코 맡지 않았을 만큼 거친 일이었다.\n\n이튿날 저녁 무렵 그녀는 들쭉날쭉한 백악질 대지, 곧 반구형의 봉분들이 젖가슴처럼 부풀어 오른 그 고원에 다다랐다—마치 젖가슴이 여럿인 키벨레 여신이 거기 벌렁 누워 길게 뻗어 있기라도 한 듯한 그 고원은, 그녀가 태어난 골짜기와 그녀의 사랑이 깃든 골짜기 사이에 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n이곳은 공기가 메마르고 차가웠으며, 긴 수레길은 비가 온 지 몇 시간 만에 허옇게 먼지가 일며 바싹 말랐다. 나무는 거의 없거나 아예 없었다. 울타리에 자랐을 법한 나무들은 나무와 덤불과 수풀의 천적인 소작농들이 키 작은 산울타리와 함께 인정사정없이 후려쳐 눕혀 버린 까닭이었다. 앞쪽 중간쯤 거리에 그녀는 불바로와 네틀콤타우트의 꼭대기를 볼 수 있었는데, 그것들은 정답게 느껴졌다. 이 고지대에서 보면 그것들은 낮고 수수한 모양새였지만, 어린 시절 블랙무어 쪽 반대편에서 다가갈 때면 하늘을 향해 우뚝 솟은 높다란 보루 같았다. 남쪽으로, 여러 마일 떨어진 거리에, 해안 쪽으로 늘어선 언덕과 능선들 너머로, 그녀는 윤이 나는 강철 같은 표면을 분간할 수 있었다. 프랑스 쪽으로 멀찍이 뻗어 나간 한 지점의 영국 해협이었다.\n\n그녀 앞, 살짝 꺼진 분지에는 한 마을의 잔해가 있었다. 사실 그녀는 매리언이 머무는 곳, 플린트콤애시에 다다른 것이었다. 어쩔 도리가 없는 듯했다. 그녀는 이곳으로 오도록 운명 지어진 것이었다. 그녀 주위의 고집스러운 흙은, 이곳에서 필요로 하는 노동이 가장 거친 종류의 것임을 충분히 또렷이 드러내 보였다. 그러나 일자리 찾기를 그만두고 쉴 때가 되었고, 게다가 비가 내리기 시작한 터라, 그녀는 머물기로 마음먹었다.\n마을 어귀에는 박공이 길 쪽으로 비죽 나온 오두막 한 채가 있었다. 묵을 곳을 청하기 전에 그녀는 그 처마 밑에 서서 저녁이 저무는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"내가 앤젤 클레어 부인이라고 누가 생각이나 하겠어!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n벽은 그녀의 등과 어깨에 따뜻하게 느껴졌다. 그녀는 그 박공 바로 안쪽이 오두막의 벽난로 자리이며, 그 열기가 벽돌을 통해 전해 온다는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 거기에 손을 녹였고, 또 가랑비에 발갛게 젖은 볼을 그 위안을 주는 표면에 갖다 댔다. 그 벽이 그녀가 가진 유일한 벗인 것만 같았다. 거기를 떠나고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 없었던지, 밤새도록 거기 머물러 있을 수도 있을 것 같았다.\n\n테스는 하루 일을 마치고 한데 모인 오두막 식구들이 안에서 서로 이야기하는 소리를, 또 저녁 접시들이 달그락거리는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그러나 마을 거리에서 그녀는 아직 사람 그림자 하나 보지 못했다. 그 적막은 마침내 다가오는 한 여인의 형상으로 깨졌는데, 그녀는 저녁이 추운데도 여름철의 무명 가운에 차양 보닛을 쓰고 있었다. 테스는 본능적으로 그게 매리언일지도 모른다고 생각했고, 어스름 속에서 분간할 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오자, 아니나 다를까 그녀였다. 매리언은 예전보다 한층 살이 찌고 얼굴이 더 붉었으며, 차림새는 확연히 더 초라했다. 그녀가 살아온 어느 이전 시기였다면 테스는 이런 형편에서 옛정을 다시 잇고 싶은 마음이 거의 들지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 외로움은 너무도 컸기에, 그녀는 매리언의 인사에 선뜻 응했다.\n\n매리언은 안부를 물으면서 자못 깍듯했으나, 테스가 여전히 처음과 다를 바 없는 형편에 머물러 있다는 사실에 무척 마음이 동요한 듯했다. 그래도 그녀는 두 사람이 헤어졌다는 소식을 어렴풋이 듣기는 했던 터였다.\n\n\"테스—클레어 부인—사랑하는 그분의 사랑하는 아내! 그런데 정말 이렇게까지 딱하게 됐어, 얘? 어쩌자고 그 고운 얼굴을 그렇게 싸매고 있는 거야? 누가 널 때리기라도 했어? 그분이?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야! 그냥 추근거리거나 끌어안기지 않으려고 그런 것뿐이야, 매리언.\"\n\n* 추근거리거나 끌어안기지: 원문의 사투리 \"clipsed or colled\"로, 희롱당하거나 껴안기는 것을 뜻한다.\n\n그런 거친 상상을 불러일으킬 만한 붕대를, 그녀는 역겹다는 듯 풀어 던졌다.\n\"그리고 너 칼라도 안 했네.\" (테스는 낙농장에서 작은 흰 칼라를 두르는 데 익숙했다.)\n\n\"알아, 메리언.\"\n\n\"오다가 잃어버렸구나.\"\n\n\"잃어버린 게 아냐. 사실은, 내 겉모습 따위 아무래도 좋아서, 그래서 안 했어.\"\n\n\"그럼 결혼반지도 안 끼고?\"\n\n\"아니, 끼고 있어. 다만 남들 앞에선 안 해. 리본에 꿰어 목에 걸고 다녀. 사람들이 내가 결혼으로 어떤 신분이 됐는지, 아니 내가 결혼했다는 사실 자체를 알았으면 싶지 않아. 지금 같은 생활을 하면서는 그게 너무 거북하거든.\"\n\n메리언은 잠시 입을 다물었다.\n\"하지만 너는 신사의 아내잖아. 그런데 이런 식으로 사는 건 아무래도 옳지 않아 보여!\"\n\n\"아냐, 옳아, 더없이 옳아. 비록 내가 무척 불행하긴 해도.\"\n\n\"세상에, 세상에. 그분이 너랑 결혼했는데도—네가 불행할 수가 있다니!\"\n\n\"아내들도 가끔은 불행해. 남편의 잘못 때문이 아니라—자기 잘못 때문에.\"\n\n\"넌 잘못이 없어, 얘. 그건 내가 확신해. 그분도 잘못이 없고. 그러니 분명 너희 둘 바깥의 무언가일 거야.\"\n\n\"메리언, 사랑하는 메리언, 아무것도 묻지 않고 나한테 부탁 하나 들어줄래? 내 남편은 외국으로 갔는데, 어쩌다 보니 내가 받은 생활비를 다 써 버려서, 한동안 옛날 일거리로 돌아가야 해. 나를 클레어 부인이라 부르지 말고, 예전처럼 테스라고 불러 줘. 여기 일손 구하니?\"\n\n\"응, 그럼. 여긴 늘 일손을 받아. 오려는 사람이 드물거든. 굶주린 땅 같은 곳이야. 밀이랑 스웨덴순무밖에 안 키워. 나야 여기 있다지만, 너 같은 애가 오는 건 안됐다 싶어.\"\n\n\"하지만 너도 나만큼이나 솜씨 좋은 낙농 일꾼이었잖아.\"\n\n\"그랬지. 근데 술을 입에 대면서 그쪽 일에선 손을 놨어. 세상에, 그게 지금 내 유일한 위안이라니까! 네가 들어오면 스웨덴순무 캐는 일에 배정될 거야. 내가 하는 일이 그거거든. 근데 넌 마음에 안 들 거야.\"\n\n\"아, 뭐든 좋아! 나 대신 말 좀 해 줄래?\"\n\n\"네가 직접 말하는 게 나을 거야.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그럼, 메리언, 명심해—내가 자리를 얻게 되면 그분 얘기는 한마디도 하지 마. 그분 이름을 진흙탕에 끌어내리고 싶지 않아.\"\n\n메리언은 테스보다 결이 거칠긴 해도 정말로 믿을 만한 처녀였고, 그녀가 부탁하는 건 무엇이든 약속했다.\n\n\"오늘이 급료 받는 밤이야.\" 메리언이 말했다. \"나랑 같이 가면 바로 알게 될 거야. 네가 행복하지 않다니 정말 안됐어. 하지만 그건 그분이 멀리 있어서겠지, 나도 알아. 그분이 여기 있다면 넌 불행할 리가 없어, 그분이 너한테 돈 한 푼 안 줘도—심지어 너를 종처럼 부려먹어도 말이야.\"\n\n\"맞아. 그럴 리 없지!\"\n\n둘은 함께 걸어 곧 농가에 다다랐는데, 그 황량함이 거의 숭고할 지경이었다. 눈에 들어오는 나무 한 그루 없었고, 이 계절엔 푸른 목초지 하나 없이, 사방이 묵정밭과 순무뿐이었다. 단조롭고 평평하게 다듬어진 산울타리로 갈린 넓은 밭들만 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n테스는 일꾼 무리가 급료를 다 받을 때까지 농가 문밖에서 기다렸고, 그런 뒤 메리언이 그녀를 소개했다. 농장주 본인은 집에 없는 모양이었으나, 이날 저녁 그를 대신한 그의 아내는, 테스가 옛 성모영보일까지 머물기로 동의하자 그녀를 고용하는 데 반대하지 않았다. 이제 여자 들일은 좀처럼 제공되지 않았고, 그 값싼 품삯 덕에 여자가 남자만큼 쉽게 해낼 수 있는 일에는 오히려 이문이 남았다.\n\n계약서에 서명하고 나자 테스가 당장 할 일은 묵을 곳을 구하는 것뿐이었는데, 예전에 그 박공벽에 기대어 몸을 녹였던 바로 그 집에서 하나를 찾았다. 그녀가 확보한 것은 변변찮은 생계였지만, 어쨌든 겨울 동안 비바람을 피할 거처는 되어 주었다. 그날 밤 그녀는 부모에게 새 주소를 알리는 편지를 썼다. 혹여 남편에게서 편지가 말럿으로 올지 모르니까. 그러나 자기 처지의 비참함은 알리지 않았다. 그것이 그에게 비난을 불러올 수도 있었으므로.\n\n# XLIII\n\n메리언이 플린트콤애시 농장을 굶주린 땅 같은 곳이라 규정한 데에는 조금의 과장도 없었다. 그 땅에서 유일하게 살이 오른 것은 메리언 자신뿐이었고, 그녀조차 외지에서 들여온 존재였다. 마을에는 세 부류가 있다. 영주의 보살핌을 받는 마을, 제 손으로 제 살림을 꾸리는 마을, 그리고 제 손으로도 영주의 손으로도 보살핌을 못 받는 마을(달리 말하면, 현지에 사는 지주의 소작인들로 이루어진 마을, 자유 보유농이나 등본 보유농의 마을, 그리고 부재지주의 마을, 즉 땅과 함께 통째로 경작되는 마을이다). 이곳 플린트콤애시는 그 세 번째였다.\n\n그러나 테스는 일에 착수했다. 인내—도덕적 용기와 육체적 소심함이 한데 섞인 그것은, 이제 엔젤 클레어 부인에게서 더 이상 사소한 특징이 아니었고, 그것이 그녀를 버티게 했다.\n\n그녀와 동료가 캐기 일을 배정받은 스웨덴순무밭은 한 뙈기로 백 에이커 남짓 뻗어 있었고, 농장에서 가장 높은 지대에, 돌투성이 랜칫 혹은 린칫 위로 솟아 있었다. 그것은 백악층에 박힌 규질 광맥이 드러난 것으로, 둥글납작하고 뾰족하고 남근 같은 모양의 헐거운 흰 부싯돌 수만 개로 이루어져 있었다. 순무는 저마다 위쪽 절반을 가축이 뜯어 먹었고, 이제 두 여자가 할 일은 해커라 불리는 갈고리 쇠스랑으로 뿌리의 아래쪽, 흙에 묻힌 절반을 파내 그것마저 먹이로 쓰게 하는 것이었다. 그 채소의 잎은 이미 한 장도 남김없이 먹혀, 밭 전체가 빛깔로는 황량한 칙칙한 갈색이었다. 그것은 이목구비 없는 낯빛이었다. 마치 어떤 얼굴이 턱에서 이마까지 그저 살갗의 너른 면일 뿐인 것처럼. 하늘은 다른 빛깔로 똑같은 모습을 띠고 있었다. 윤곽이 사라진, 새하얗게 텅 빈 낯이었다. 그래서 이 위아래 두 얼굴은 온종일 서로를 마주했다. 흰 얼굴은 갈색 얼굴을 내려다보고, 갈색 얼굴은 흰 얼굴을 올려다보았으며, 그 사이엔 갈색 얼굴의 표면을 파리처럼 기어 다니는 두 처녀 말고는 아무것도 끼어 있지 않았다.\n\n아무도 그들 곁에 오지 않았고, 그들의 동작은 기계적인 규칙성을 보였다. 그들의 모습은 거친 삼베 '래퍼'를 둘러쓴 채였다—소매 달린 갈색 앞치마로, 치맛자락이 바람에 펄럭이지 않게 뒤쪽 아래에서 묶었다—짧은 치마 아래로는 발목 위까지 올라오는 장화가 드러났고, 손에는 토시 달린 노란 양가죽 장갑을 꼈다. 두건 자락이 그들의 숙인 머리에 드리운 그 사색적인 분위기는, 보는 이로 하여금 초기 이탈리아 화가가 그린 두 마리아의 어떤 구도를 떠올리게 했으리라.\n\n그들은 시간이 가고 또 가도 일을 계속했고, 풍경 속에서 자신들이 지닌 그 쓸쓸한 모습을 의식하지 못했으며, 제 처지가 공정한지 부당한지도 생각하지 않았다. 그들 같은 처지에서조차 꿈속에서 존재하기란 가능했다. 오후에 다시 비가 들이쳤고, 메리언은 더 일하지 않아도 된다고 말했다. 하지만 일하지 않으면 품삯도 못 받았다. 그래서 그들은 계속 일했다. 이 밭은 지대가 워낙 높아 비가 내릴 까닭도 없었으나, 울부짖는 바람을 타고 수평으로 내달려 유리 파편처럼 그들에게 박혀, 끝내 흠뻑 젖게 했다. 테스는 지금까지 그 말이 정말 무슨 뜻인지 몰랐다. 축축함에도 정도가 있고, 흔히들 아주 조금만 젖어도 흠뻑 젖었다고 말한다. 그러나 밭에 서서 더디게 일하며 빗물이 처음엔 다리와 어깨로, 다음엔 엉덩이와 머리로, 그다음엔 등과 앞과 옆구리로 스며드는 것을 느끼면서도, 납빛 광선이 스러져 해가 졌음을 알릴 때까지 계속 일하는 데에는, 뚜렷한 만큼의 극기, 아니 용기마저 요구된다.\n\n그래도 그들은 짐작만큼 젖은 것을 그리 절감하지는 않았다. 둘 다 젊었고, 탤보세이즈 낙농장에서 함께 살며 사랑했던 시절 이야기를 하고 있었으니까. 여름이 제 선물을 아낌없이 베풀던 그 행복한 푸른 땅을—모두에게는 물질로, 이들에게는 마음으로 베풀던 그곳을. 테스는 법적으로는, 비록 실제로는 아니어도, 제 남편인 그 남자 이야기를 메리언과 나누고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 그 화제의 거부할 수 없는 매혹이 그녀를 배신하여, 메리언의 말에 맞장구치게 만들었다. 그리하여 앞서 말했듯, 보닛의 축축한 자락이 그들의 얼굴을 매섭게 후려치고, 래퍼가 진력나도록 몸에 달라붙었음에도, 그들은 이 오후 내내 푸르고 햇살 가득하고 낭만적인 탤보세이즈의 추억 속에서 살았다.\n\n\"날 좋을 때는 여기서 프룸 골짜기 몇 마일 안쪽 언덕이 어렴풋이 보여.\" 메리언이 말했다.\n\n\"아! 그래?\" 테스가 이 고장의 새로운 가치에 눈뜨며 말했다.\n\n그리하여 여기서도 어디서나처럼 두 힘이 작용하고 있었다. 즐기려는 타고난 의지와, 즐김에 맞서는 상황의 의지가. 메리언의 의지는 오후가 깊어 가자 주머니에서 흰 헝겊으로 마개를 막은 한 파인트들이 병을 꺼내 스스로를 돕는 방법을 썼고, 거기서 한 모금 마시라고 테스에게 권했다. 그러나 테스는 남의 도움 없이 꿈꾸는 자기 힘만으로도 지금의 승화에는 충분했으므로, 그저 입만 살짝 축이는 정도 말고는 사양했고, 그러자 메리언이 그 독한 술을 한 모금 들이켰다.\n\n\"이제 익숙해졌어.\" 메리언이 말했다. \"이젠 끊을 수가 없어. 이게 내 유일한 위안이야—봐, 난 그분을 잃었잖아. 넌 안 잃었고. 넌 어쩌면 이거 없이도 견딜 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n테스는 자기 상실도 메리언의 것 못지않게 크다고 여겼지만, 적어도 글자상으로는 엔젤의 아내라는 위엄에 떠받쳐진 처지였기에, 메리언이 둘을 갈라 보는 것을 받아들였다.\n\n이런 풍경 속에서 테스는 아침 서리와 오후 빗속에서 노예처럼 일했다. 스웨덴순무를 캐지 않을 때는 다듬는 일이었는데, 이 과정에서는 빌훅으로 흙과 잔뿌리를 잘라 낸 뒤 뿌리를 갈무리해 두었다가 나중에 쓰게 했다. 이 일을 할 때는 비가 오면 이엉을 인 바자 옆에서 비를 피할 수 있었다. 그러나 서리가 내리면 두꺼운 가죽 장갑조차 손에 쥔 얼어붙은 덩이가 손가락을 에는 것을 막아 주지 못했다. 그래도 테스는 희망을 버리지 않았다. 그녀가 클레어 성품의 으뜸가는 성분이라고 줄곧 꼽아 온 그 너그러움이 머잖아 그를 다시 자기에게로 데려오리라는 확신이 있었다.\n\n익살스러운 기분이 한껏 오른 메리언은 앞서 말한 그 기묘한 모양의 부싯돌들을 찾아내며 깔깔 비명을 질러 댔지만, 테스는 더없이 무덤덤한 채로 있었다. 그들은 자주 들판 너머, 바르강이나 프룸강이 뻗어 있다고 알려진 쪽을 바라보았다. 눈으로 볼 수는 없을지언정 말이다. 그러고는 두루뭉술한 잿빛 안개에 눈을 박은 채, 저 너머에서 보낸 옛 시절을 그려 보았다.\n\n\"아.\" 메리언이 말했다. \"옛 패거리에서 한두 명만 더 여기 와 줬으면 얼마나 좋을까! 그럼 매일 여기 들판에서 탤보세이즈 얘기를 끄집어내고, 그분 얘기도 하고, 거기서 보낸 그 좋았던 시절 얘기도 하고, 우리가 알던 옛것들 얘기도 하면서, 거의 다, 보이는 것처럼, 되살려 낼 수 있을 텐데!\"\n메리언의 눈이 부드러워지고, 그 환영이 되살아나자 목소리도 아련해졌다. \"이즈 휴엣한테 편지를 써야겠어.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"지금 집에서 빈둥거리며 아무것도 안 하고 있을 거야, 내가 알지. 우리 여기 있다고 알리고, 오라고 해야지. 그리고 어쩌면 레티도 이젠 괜찮을 만큼 나았을지 몰라.\"\n\n테스는 그 제안에 반대할 말이 없었고, 옛 탤보세이즈의 기쁨을 들여오려는 이 계획에 대해 다음으로 들은 소식은 이삼 일 뒤였다. 메리언이, 이즈가 자기 물음에 답을 보내왔고 올 수 있으면 오겠다고 약속했다고 알려 준 것이다.\n\n몇 해 만에 처음 보는 혹독한 겨울이었다. 그것은 체스 두는 이의 수처럼 은밀하고 가늠된 미끄럼으로 다가왔다. 어느 아침, 외로이 서 있는 몇 그루 나무와 산울타리의 가시나무가 마치 식물의 껍질을 벗고 동물의 가죽을 걸친 듯 보였다. 잔가지 하나하나가 밤새 껍질에서 돋아난 털 같은 흰 보풀로 덮여 평소의 네 배쯤 굵어 보였고, 덤불이며 나무 전체가 하늘과 지평선의 구슬픈 잿빛 위에 흰 선으로 그려진 또렷한 소묘를 이루었다. 거미줄은 지금껏 누구도 본 적 없던 헛간과 담벼락 곳곳에 제 존재를 드러냈는데, 결정화하는 대기가 그것을 보이게 끄집어낸 것으로, 헛간과 기둥과 문의 도드라진 데마다 흰 털실 고리처럼 매달려 있었다.\n\n이 얼어붙은 축축함의 계절이 지나자 마른 서리의 시기가 왔고, 그때 북극 너머에서 온 낯선 새들이 플린트콤애시의 고지에 소리 없이 당도하기 시작했다. 여위고 유령 같은 새들로, 비극적인 눈을 지녔다—인간이라면 아무도 상상조차 못 한 규모로, 누구도 견딜 수 없는 피가 엉길 추위 속에서, 사람의 발길 닿지 않는 극지의 격변하는 공포의 광경을 목격한 눈이었다. 오로라의 쏘아 대는 빛 속에서 빙산이 무너지고 눈산이 미끄러지는 것을 보았고, 거대한 폭풍과 땅과 바다가 뒤틀리는 소용돌이에 반쯤 눈이 멀었으며, 그런 광경이 빚어낸 표정을 그대로 간직한 눈이었다. 이 이름 없는 새들은 테스와 메리언에게 꽤 가까이 다가왔으나, 인간은 결코 못 볼 그 모든 광경을 보고도 아무런 이야기도 전해 주지 않았다. 보고 온 것을 말하려는 여행자의 욕심 따위 그들에겐 없었고, 말없는 무심함으로, 자기들이 대수롭잖게 여기는 그 경험들을 떨쳐 버린 채, 이 수수한 고지의 눈앞 일들에만—두 처녀가 해커로 흙덩이를 헤집어, 이 방문객들이 먹이로 즐기는 무언가를 드러내 주는 그 하찮은 동작들에만 마음을 두었다.\n\n그러던 어느 날, 묘한 기운이 이 트인 고장의 공기에 스며들었다. 비도 아닌 습기와, 서리도 아닌 추위가 찾아왔다. 그것은 두 사람의 눈알을 시리게 하고, 이마를 쑤시게 했으며, 뼛속까지 파고들어, 몸의 겉보다 그 속을 더 헤집었다. 그들은 그것이 눈을 뜻함을 알았고, 밤이 되자 눈이 왔다. 옆을 지나다 멈춰 서는 외로운 길손이라면 누구든 위로해 주던 그 따뜻한 박공의 오두막에서 계속 살던 테스는 밤중에 깨어, 이엉 위에서 마치 지붕이 온갖 바람의 체육관으로 변해 버린 듯한 소리를 들었다. 아침에 일어나려고 등불을 켜 보니, 눈이 여닫이창 틈으로 불어 들어와 안쪽에 더없이 고운 가루로 흰 원뿔을 이루었고, 굴뚝으로도 내려와 마룻바닥에 발창 깊이만큼 쌓여, 그녀가 움직이자 신발 자국이 났다. 바깥에서는 폭풍이 어찌나 빠르게 몰아치는지 부엌 안에까지 눈안개가 일었다. 그러나 아직 문밖은 너무 어두워 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n테스는 스웨덴순무 일을 계속하기란 불가능함을 알았다. 그리고 외로운 작은 등불 옆에서 아침을 다 먹었을 무렵, 메리언이 와서, 날씨가 바뀔 때까지 헛간에서 나머지 여자들과 함께 갈대 추리는 일을 하게 되었다고 알려 주었다. 그래서 바깥의 한결같은 어둠의 망토가 어수선한 잿빛의 뒤범벅으로 바뀌기 시작하자마자, 그들은 등불을 끄고, 가장 두꺼운 앞치마로 몸을 감싸고, 모직 목도리를 목에 두르고 가슴께에 가로질러 묶은 뒤 헛간으로 출발했다. 눈은 극지의 분지에서 흰 구름기둥처럼 새들을 뒤따라 왔고, 눈송이 하나하나는 분간할 수 없었다. 돌풍에서는 빙산과 북극해와 고래와 흰곰의 냄새가 났고, 눈을 실어 와 땅을 핥기만 할 뿐 그 위에 쌓이지는 않게 했다. 그들은 솜털 같은 들판을 가로질러 몸을 비스듬히 기울인 채 터벅터벅 나아가며, 되도록 산울타리의 그늘에 붙어 가려 했으나, 그것은 가림막이라기보다 차라리 체 노릇을 했다. 공기는 그것을 들끓게 하는 허연 무리들로 핏기를 잃을 만큼 시달려, 두 사람을 기이하게 비틀고 빙빙 돌렸으니, 빛깔 없는 만물의 혼돈을 떠올리게 했다. 그래도 두 젊은 여자는 제법 명랑했다. 마른 고지에서 이런 날씨는 그 자체로 기를 꺾는 것은 아니었으니까.\n\n\"하하! 그 약삭빠른 북녘 새들은 이게 올 줄 알았던 거야.\" 메리언이 말했다. \"틀림없어, 그것들은 북극성에서부터 줄곧 이 날씨 바로 앞을 앞질러 다녀. 네 남편은, 얘, 분명히 지금 이때 내내 타는 듯한 날씨를 보내고 있을 거야. 세상에, 그분이 지금 자기 어여쁜 아내를 볼 수만 있다면! 이런 날씨가 네 미모를 조금도 해치진 않지만—사실, 오히려 더 살려 주거든.\"\n\n\"내 앞에서 그분 얘기는 하지 마, 메리언.\" 테스가 엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 근데—넌 분명 그분을 마음에 두고 있잖아! 안 그래?\"\n\n대답 대신, 테스는 눈물 어린 눈으로, 충동적으로 남아메리카가 있으리라 짐작되는 쪽을 향해 돌아서더니, 입술을 모아 눈 내리는 바람결에 격정의 입맞춤을 후 불어 보냈다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그런 줄 알아. 하지만 정말이지, 부부한테 이건 참 별난 삶이야! 자—더는 한마디도 안 할게! 그건 그렇고, 날씨로 말하자면, 밀 헛간 안에선 우리한테 해될 게 없어. 근데 갈대 추리기는 무지하게 고된 일이야—스웨덴순무 캐기보다 더해. 난 튼튼하니까 견디지만, 넌 나보다 가냘프잖아. 주인장이 대체 왜 너한테 그 일을 시켰는지 모르겠다.\"\n\n그들은 밀 헛간에 다다라 안으로 들어갔다. 그 길쭉한 건물의 한쪽 끝은 곡식으로 가득했고, 가운데가 갈대 추리는 일을 하는 곳이었으며, 그 전날 저녁에 이미 갈대 압착틀에 여자들이 하루 동안 뽑아낼 만한 밀단이 충분히 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"어머, 이즈가 왔네!\" 메리언이 말했다.\n\n이즈가 맞았고, 그녀가 앞으로 나왔다. 그녀는 전날 오후 어머니 집에서부터 줄곧 걸어왔는데, 거리가 그리 멀 줄 몰랐던 탓에 늦어졌으나, 눈이 내리기 직전에 도착해 술집에서 잤다. 농장주가 장에서 그녀 어머니와, 오늘 오면 받아 주겠다고 약조했던 터라, 그녀는 늑장으로 그를 실망시킬까 봐 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n테스와 메리언과 이즈 말고도, 이웃 마을에서 온 여자가 둘 있었다. 아마존 같은 자매로, 테스는 흠칫하며 그들이 검은 카, 곧 스페이드의 여왕과 그 아래 동생 다이아몬드의 여왕임을 떠올렸다—트랜트리지의 한밤 다툼에서 그녀와 싸우려 들었던 자들이었다. 그들은 그녀를 알아보는 기색이 없었고, 어쩌면 정말 못 알아봤을 수도 있었다. 그때 그들은 술기운에 취해 있었고, 이곳에서나 거기서나 잠시 머무는 떠돌이일 뿐이었으니까. 그들은 우물 파기, 산울타리 손질, 도랑 파기, 땅 파헤치기까지, 온갖 남자 일을 피로한 줄도 모르고 즐겨 했다. 갈대 추리기로도 이름난 솜씨꾼들이라, 다소 거만한 기색으로 나머지 셋을 둘러보았다.\n\n장갑을 끼고, 모두가 압착틀 앞에 한 줄로 늘어서서 일에 착수했다. 그것은 두 기둥을 가로대로 이은 구조물로, 그 아래에 뽑아낼 밀단을 이삭이 바깥으로 향하게 뉘어 놓고, 가로대는 기둥에 꽂은 핀으로 눌러 고정하되 밀단이 줄어드는 대로 낮춰 가는 식이었다.\n\n날빛은 빛깔이 짙어 갔는데, 빛이 하늘에서 아래로 들지 않고 눈에서 위로 헛간 문으로 들어왔기 때문이다. 처녀들은 압착틀에서 한 줌 한 줌 잡아 뽑았다. 그러나 추문을 늘어놓는 낯선 두 여자가 있는 탓에, 메리언과 이즈는 처음엔 바라던 대로 옛이야기를 나눌 수가 없었다. 이윽고 그들은 말발굽의 둔탁한 소리를 들었고, 농장주가 헛간 문 앞까지 말을 타고 왔다. 그는 말에서 내려 테스에게 바짝 다가와, 골똘히 그녀의 옆얼굴을 바라보며 서 있었다. 그녀는 처음엔 돌아보지 않았으나, 그의 붙박인 자세에 끝내 고개를 돌렸고, 그제야 자기 고용주가, 자기 내력을 들먹였던 탓에 그녀가 큰길에서 달아났던 바로 그 트랜트리지 토박이임을 알아챘다.\n\n그는 그녀가 뽑아낸 밀단 묶음을 바깥 더미로 날라 둘 때까지 기다렸다가 말했다. \"그래, 네가 내 호의를 그토록 고약하게 받아쳤던 그 젊은 계집이렷다? 네가 고용됐단 말을 듣자마자 그럴지도 모른다 싶었는데, 아니면 내 목을 매달아도 좋아! 흥, 넌 첫 번째 여관에서 네 정부랑 같이 있을 때 날 이겼다고 여겼고, 두 번째 길에서 줄행랑쳤을 때도 그랬겠지. 하지만 이번엔 내가 너를 이긴 것 같구나.\" 그는 차가운 웃음으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n테스는 아마존들과 농장주 사이에 끼여, 덮치기 그물에 걸린 새처럼 아무 대꾸도 않고 계속 짚을 뽑았다. 그녀는 이제 사람 됨됨이를 어지간히 읽을 줄 알아, 자기 고용주의 수작 따위는 두려워할 것 없음을 알았다. 그보다는 클레어가 자기를 대했던 처사에 대한 굴욕에서 비롯된 횡포였다. 통틀어 보면 그녀는 남자에게서 그런 감정을 차라리 더 낫게 여겼고, 그것은 견뎌 낼 만큼 용감하다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"내가 너한테 반했다고 여겼겠지? 어떤 여자들은 어찌나 어리석은지, 눈길 하나하나를 진지한 본심으로 받아들인다니까. 하지만 젊은 계집들 머릿속에서 그런 헛소리를 빼내는 데는 들판에서 겨울나기만 한 게 없지. 게다가 넌 성모영보일까지 서명하고 약조했어. 자, 이제 내게 용서를 빌 텐가?\"\n\n\"당신이 제게 비셔야 할 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"좋다—네 멋대로 해. 하지만 여기서 누가 주인인지 두고 보자. 오늘 네가 한 게 이 밀단 전부냐?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"형편없는 솜씨로군. 저쪽 사람들이 한 걸 좀 봐라\" (건장한 두 여자를 가리키며). \"나머지도 다 너보다 잘했어.\"\n\n\"저들은 다 전에 해 봤고, 저는 안 해 봤어요. 그리고 이건 도급 일이고 우리는 한 만큼만 삯을 받으니, 당신한테는 차이가 없을 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"천만에, 차이가 있지. 난 헛간을 비워야 한단 말이다.\"\n\n\"저는 다른 사람들이 두 시에 떠나는 대신, 오후 내내 일하겠어요.\"\n\n그는 시무룩하게 그녀를 노려보고는 가 버렸다. 테스는 이보다 훨씬 못한 곳에 올 수도 있었으리라 느꼈다. 그래도 무엇이든 수작질보다는 나았다. 두 시가 되자 직업적인 갈대 추리꾼들은 술병에 남은 마지막 반 파인트를 들이켜고, 갈고리를 내려놓고, 마지막 단을 묶고는 가 버렸다. 메리언과 이즈도 그러려 했으나, 테스가 솜씨 부족을 더 긴 시간으로 메우려 남겠다는 말을 듣고는 그녀를 두고 가려 하지 않았다. 여전히 내리는 눈을 내다보며 메리언이 외쳤다. \"자, 이제 우리끼리 다 차지했네.\" 그리하여 마침내 이야기는 낙농장에서의 옛 경험으로 돌아갔다. 그리고, 물론, 엔젤 클레어를 향한 그들의 애정에 얽힌 일들로도.\n\n\"이즈, 그리고 메리언.\" 엔젤 클레어 부인이, 그녀가 얼마나 아내답지 못한 처지인지를 생각하면 무척이나 가슴 아픈 위엄을 담아 말했다. \"이제 난 예전처럼 너희랑 클레어 씨 얘기에 끼어들 수가 없어. 알아주겠지, 그럴 수 없다는 걸. 왜냐하면, 비록 그분이 지금은 내 곁을 떠나 있어도, 그분은 내 남편이니까.\"\n\n이즈는 클레어를 사랑했던 네 처녀 가운데 천성이 가장 맹랑하고 가장 신랄했다. \"그분이 더없이 멋진 연인이었던 건 틀림없지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 너를 그렇게 빨리 떠나 버릴 만큼, 그분이 너무 다정한 남편은 아닌 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"가실 수밖에 없었어—저쪽 땅 일을 알아보러, 어쩔 수 없이 가셔야만 했어!\" 테스가 변호했다.\n\n\"겨울 동안은 너를 건사해 줄 수도 있었잖아.\"\n\n\"아—그건 어떤 사고 탓이야—오해 때문이지. 그건 따지지 말자.\" 테스가 눈물 어린 말로 대답했다. \"어쩌면 그분 편에서도 할 말이 많을 거야! 그분은 어떤 남편들처럼 나한테 말도 없이 떠나진 않았어. 그리고 난 그분이 어디 있는지 언제든 알아낼 수 있어.\"\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 그들은 오래도록 몽상에 잠긴 채로, 계속 밀 이삭을 거머쥐고, 짚을 뽑아내고, 그것을 팔 아래 그러모으고, 빌훅으로 이삭을 잘라 냈다. 헛간 안에는 짚이 사각거리는 소리와 갈고리가 사각 끊는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 나지 않았다. 그러다 테스가 별안간 기운이 빠져, 제 발치의 밀 이삭 더미 위로 풀썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"네가 못 버틸 줄 알았어!\" 메리언이 소리쳤다. \"이 일은 너 같은 살로는 안 돼. 더 단단한 살이 있어야 해.\"\n\n바로 그때 농장주가 들어왔다. \"오, 내가 없으니 이 모양이로군.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이건 제 손해예요.\" 그녀가 변호했다. \"당신 손해가 아니라.\"\n\n\"난 그걸 끝내 놓고 싶단 말이다.\" 그는 고집스레 말하고는, 헛간을 가로질러 다른 쪽 문으로 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"저 사람 신경 쓰지 마, 얘야.\" 메리언이 말했다. \"난 전에도 여기서 일해 봤어. 자, 넌 저기 가서 좀 누워 있어. 이즈랑 내가 네 몫을 채워 줄게.\"\n\n\"너희한테 그러게 하긴 싫어. 게다가 난 너희보다 키도 크잖아.\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 너무 기진한 나머지 잠시 눕는 데 동의했고, 헛간 저쪽 끝에 던져 쌓아 둔 풀테일—곧은 짚을 뽑아낸 뒤 남은 찌끼—더미 위에 몸을 기댔다. 그녀가 쓰러진 것은 고된 일 탓이기도 했지만, 남편과의 이별이라는 화제가 다시 들춰진 데서 온 동요 탓이 그에 못지않게 컸다. 그녀는 의지 없이 그저 지각만 하는 상태로 누워 있었고, 다른 이들이 짚을 바스락대고 이삭을 잘라 내는 소리가 마치 몸에 닿는 손길만큼의 무게로 다가왔다.\n\n그녀는 제 구석에서 그 소리들 말고도 그들의 두런거리는 목소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그녀는 그들이 이미 꺼낸 화제를 이어 가고 있음을 확신했지만, 목소리가 너무 낮아 말을 알아들을 수가 없었다. 마침내 테스는 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지 점점 더 알고 싶어졌고, 이제 좀 나아졌다고 스스로를 다독이며 일어나 다시 일을 시작했다.\n\n그러자 이번엔 이즈 휴엣이 뻗어 버렸다. 그녀는 전날 저녁 십수 마일을 넘게 걸었고, 자정에야 잠자리에 들었다가 다섯 시에 다시 일어났던 것이다. 오로지 메리언만이, 술병과 튼튼한 체구 덕에, 등과 팔에 가해지는 부담을 탈 없이 견뎌 냈다. 테스는 이즈에게 그만두라고 권하며, 자기가 좀 나아졌으니 그녀 없이도 하루를 마치겠다고, 그리고 밀단 수를 똑같이 나누겠다고 했다.\n\n이즈는 고마이 그 제안을 받아들이고, 커다란 문을 지나 눈 덮인 길로 나가 제 숙소로 사라졌다. 메리언은, 술병 탓에 이맘때 오후면 늘 그렇듯, 낭만적인 기분에 젖기 시작했다.\n\n\"난 그분이 그럴 줄 정말 몰랐어—절대로!\" 그녀가 꿈결 같은 어조로 말했다. \"난 그분을 그렇게 사랑했는데! 그분이 너를 가졌던 건 괜찮았어. 하지만 이즈 일은 너무 심하잖아!\"\n\n테스는 그 말에 화들짝 놀라, 하마터면 빌훅에 손가락을 자를 뻔했다.\n\n\"내 남편 얘기야?\" 그녀가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"음, 그래. 이즈가 '그 애한텐 말하지 마' 그랬는데, 도저히 못 참겠어! 그분이 이즈한테 시키려던 일이야. 그분이 이즈더러 자기랑 같이 브라질로 떠나자고 했대.\"\n\n테스의 얼굴이 바깥 풍경처럼 새하얗게 질리며, 그 곡선이 곧게 펴졌다. \"그래서 이즈가 안 가겠다고 했어?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"몰라. 어쨌든 그분이 마음을 바꿨대.\"\n\n\"흥—그럼 진심이 아니었던 거지! 그냥 남자의 농담이었어!\"\n\n\"아냐, 진심이었어. 정거장 쪽으로 한참이나 데려다줬다니까.\"\n\n\"데려가진 않았잖아!\"\n\n그들은 말없이 계속 짚을 뽑았고, 그러다 테스가 아무런 전조도 없이 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"이것 봐!\" 메리언이 말했다. \"괜히 말했나 봐!\"\n\n\"아냐. 네가 말해 준 건 정말 잘한 일이야! 난 여태 갈팡질팡 맥없이 살아오면서, 이게 어디로 흘러갈지 보지 못했어! 그분한테 더 자주 편지를 써 보냈어야 했는데. 그분은 내가 자기한테 갈 순 없다고 했지, 내가 쓰고 싶은 만큼 자주 편지 쓰면 안 된다고는 안 했어. 더는 이렇게 어물대지 않을 거야! 모든 걸 그분 손에 맡겨 두다니, 난 정말 잘못했고 너무 게을렀어!\"\n\n헛간 안의 어슴푸레한 빛이 점점 더 어두워져, 더는 일할 만큼 보이지가 않았다. 그날 저녁 테스는 집에 다다라, 회칠한 작은 제 방의 호젓함 속에 들어서자, 충동적으로 클레어에게 편지를 쓰기 시작했다. 그러나 의심에 빠져, 끝맺지를 못했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 심장께에 지니고 다니던 리본에서 반지를 빼내, 밤새 손가락에 끼고 있었다. 마치 자기가 정말로 이 붙잡을 길 없는 연인의 아내라는 느낌으로 스스로를 다잡으려는 듯이. 자기를 떠난 지 얼마 되지도 않아 이즈더러 함께 외국으로 가자고 제안할 수 있는 그 연인의. 그것을 알면서, 어떻게 그에게 애원의 편지를 쓰거나, 자기가 아직도 그를 마음에 두고 있음을 드러낼 수 있겠는가?\n\n# XLIV\n\n헛간에서의 그 폭로로 인해 그녀의 생각은 요사이 한두 번 향했던 그 방향으로—멀리 떨어진 에민스터 목사관으로—새로이 이끌렸다. 그녀가 바라기만 한다면 클레어에게 편지를 보내고, 어려운 일이 있으면 그들에게 직접 편지하라고 일러둔 것이 바로 남편의 부모를 통해서였다. 그러나 자기가 도덕적으로 그에게 아무런 권리도 없다는 그 의식이, 이런 쪽지를 보내려는 충동을 늘 거두게 했다. 그래서 목사관의 가족에게도, 결혼 이후 친정 부모에게 그랬듯, 그녀는 사실상 존재하지 않는 사람이었다. 양쪽 모두를 향한 이 자기 지움은, 제 분수를 공정히 따져 마땅히 받을 자격이 없는 호의나 동정 따위는 아무것도 바라지 않으려는 그녀의 독립적인 성품과 자못 잘 들어맞았다. 그녀는 제 자질로 서거나 무너지기로 마음먹었고, 한순간의 충동에 휩쓸린 그 가문의 한 사람이 교회 장부에 제 이름 옆에 그녀의 이름을 적었다는 하찮은 사실로 그녀를 위해 세워진, 그저 형식뿐인 그런 권리들은 내려놓기로 한 것이다.\n\n그러나 이제 이즈의 이야기에 찔려 열병에 들뜬 마당이라, 그녀의 단념하는 힘에도 한계가 있었다. 어째서 남편은 그녀에게 편지하지 않았을까? 그는 적어도 자기가 떠난 곳의 지명만은 알려 주겠다고 분명히 내비쳤다. 그런데 제 주소를 알리는 한 줄도 보내지 않았다. 그는 정말로 무심한 걸까? 아니면 병이 난 걸까? 자기가 먼저 다가서야 하는 걸까? 분명 그녀는 염려하는 마음의 용기를 그러모아, 소식을 구하러 목사관을 찾아가, 그의 침묵에 대한 제 슬픔을 토로할 수 있으리라. 엔젤의 아버지가 사람들이 말하던 대로 선한 분이라면, 그녀의 애정에 굶주린 처지를 헤아려 줄 수 있으리라. 사회적인 고초야 그녀가 감출 수 있었다.\n\n평일에 농장을 떠나기란 그녀의 힘으로 될 일이 아니었다. 일요일만이 유일하게 가능한 기회였다. 플린트콤애시가 아직 어떤 철도도 오르지 못한 백악질 대지 한복판에 있는 터라, 걸어가는 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 거리가 한쪽으로만 십오 마일이니, 일찍 일어나 그 일에 하루를 길게 잡아야 했다.\n\n보름 뒤, 눈이 사라지고 그 뒤로 매서운 검은 서리가 든 무렵, 그녀는 길 상태를 틈타 그 시도를 해 보기로 했다. 그 일요일 새벽 네 시에 그녀는 아래층으로 내려와 별빛 속으로 나섰다. 날씨는 여전히 좋았고, 땅은 그녀의 발밑에서 모루처럼 쟁쟁 울렸다.\n\n메리언과 이즈는 그 여정이 그녀의 남편과 관계된 일임을 알기에 그녀의 나들이에 무척 관심이 많았다. 그들의 숙소는 길을 따라 조금 더 간 오두막에 있었지만, 그들은 와서 테스의 출발을 거들어 주며, 시부모의 마음을 사로잡으려면 가장 어여쁜 차림으로 단장해야 한다고 주장했다. 그러나 그녀는 늙은 클레어 씨의 엄격한 칼뱅주의 신조를 아는 터라, 그런 데 무심했고, 심지어 미심쩍어하기까지 했다. 그 서글픈 결혼으로부터 이제 한 해가 흘렀지만, 그녀는 그때 가득했던 옷장의 잔해에서 충분한 옷가지를 간직해 두어, 최근 유행 따위는 내세우지 않는 수수한 시골 처녀로서 무척 사랑스럽게 차려입을 수 있었다. 부드러운 잿빛 모직 가운에, 얼굴과 목의 분홍빛 살갗에 닿는 흰 크레이프 주름 장식, 그리고 검은 벨벳 재킷과 모자였다.\n\n\"네 남편이 지금 너를 볼 수 없다니 천만 번 애석한 일이야—너 정말 미인으로 보여!\" 이즈 휴엣이, 바깥의 강철빛 별빛과 안의 노란 촛불빛 사이 문지방에 선 테스를 바라보며 말했다. 이즈는 그 처지에 자신을 너그러이 내맡긴 채 말한 것이었다. 그녀로서는—개암 한 알보다 큰 마음을 가진 여자라면 누구라도—테스가 곁에 있는데 그녀에게 적의를 품을 수는 없었으니, 테스가 제 동성들에게 미치는 영향력은 자못 보기 드문 따스함과 강함을 지녀, 시샘이니 경쟁이니 하는 더 못난 여성적 감정들을 묘하게 압도해 버렸다.\n\n여기는 마지막으로 한 번 잡아당기고 매만지고, 저기는 살짝 솔질해 주고 나서, 그들은 그녀를 떠나보냈다. 그리고 그녀는 동트기 전 진줏빛 대기 속으로 빨려 들어갔다. 그녀가 성큼성큼 제 걸음을 내디디며 단단한 길을 따라 또각또각 멀어지는 발소리를 그들은 들었다. 이즈조차 그녀가 이기기를 바랐고, 제 정조에 딱히 자부심이 있는 것도 아니었으나, 한순간 클레어에게 끌렸을 때 제 벗에게 못할 짓을 하려던 것이 막혔던 게 다행이라고 느꼈다.\n\n클레어가 테스와 결혼한 지 하루를 뺀 꼭 한 해 전이었고, 그가 그녀 곁을 떠나 있은 지도 한 해에서 며칠 모자랄 뿐이었다. 그래도, 메마르고 맑은 겨울 아침에, 이 백악질 등성이들의 희박한 공기를 가르며, 그녀 같은 그런 심부름을 안고 활기차게 걷기 시작하는 것은 우울한 일이 아니었다. 그리고 출발할 때 그녀의 꿈이, 시어머니의 마음을 얻어 그 부인에게 제 온 내력을 털어놓고, 그녀를 제 편으로 끌어들여, 그리하여 달아난 남편을 되찾는 것이었음은 의심할 여지가 없다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는, 그 아래로 흙기 많은 블랙무어 골짜기가 뻗어 있는 거대한 절벽 가장자리에 다다랐다. 골짜기는 이제 새벽녘 안개에 잠겨 고요히 누워 있었다. 고지의 빛깔 없는 공기 대신, 저 아래 대기는 짙은 푸른빛이었다. 그녀가 지금 일하는 데 익숙해진 백 에이커짜리 너른 울 대신, 그녀 아래에는 채 여섯 에이커도 안 되는 작은 밭들이 어찌나 많은지, 이 높이에서 보면 그물코처럼 보였다. 여기 위쪽 풍경은 희끄무레한 갈색이었고, 저 아래는 프룸 골짜기처럼 늘 푸르렀다. 그러나 그녀의 슬픔이 모양을 갖춘 곳이 바로 저 골짜기였고, 그래서 그녀는 예전만큼 그곳을 사랑하지 않았다. 무릇 느낄 줄 아는 모든 이에게 그렇듯, 그녀에게도 아름다움은 사물 그 자체가 아니라 그 사물이 상징하는 것에 있었다.\n\n골짜기를 오른편에 둔 채 그녀는 한결같이 서쪽으로 방향을 잡았다. 힌톡 마을들 위를 지나고, 셔턴애버스에서 캐스터브리지로 가는 큰길과 직각으로 가로지르며, 도그베리 언덕과 하이스토이 가장자리를 끼고 돌았는데, 그 사이의 골짜기는 \"악마의 부엌\"이라 불렸다. 여전히 그 높은 길을 따라가 그녀는 크로스인핸드에 다다랐고, 거기엔 돌기둥이 황량하고 말없이 서서, 기적이, 혹은 살인이, 아니면 둘 다가 일어난 자리를 표시하고 있었다. 거기서 삼 마일 더 가, 그녀는 롱애시 길이라 불리는 곧고 인적 없는 로마 가도를 가로질렀고, 그 길에 닿자마자 곧장 거기를 벗어나 가로지르는 샛길로 언덕을 내려가 에버셰드라는 작은 읍, 아니 마을로 접어들었으니, 이제 거리의 절반쯤을 온 셈이었다. 그녀는 여기서 한 번 멈춰, 자못 든든하게 두 번째 아침을 먹었다—여관은 피하는 터라 소앤에이콘이 아니라, 교회 옆 한 오두막에서였다.\n\n여정의 후반부는 벤빌 길을 거쳐 가는, 한결 부드러운 고장이었다. 그러나 자기와 순례의 목적지 사이의 거리가 줄어들수록, 테스의 자신감도 줄어들었고, 그녀의 일은 더 위압적으로 떠올랐다. 그녀의 목적은 그토록 또렷한 선으로 보이는데 풍경은 그토록 흐릿이 보여, 그녀는 이따금 길을 잃을 뻔하기도 했다. 그래도 정오 무렵 그녀는 에민스터와 그 목사관이 자리한 분지 가장자리의 한 대문 옆에서 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n지금 이 순간 목사와 그의 교인들이 그 아래 모여 있음을 그녀가 아는 그 네모진 탑은, 그녀의 눈에 준엄해 보였다. 그녀는 어떻게든 평일에 올 도리를 마련했더라면 싶었다. 그런 선한 분이라도 일요일을 골라 온 여자에 대해서는, 그녀의 사정이 어쩔 수 없었음은 도무지 헤아리지 못한 채, 편견을 품을지도 모를 일이었다. 그러나 이제는 계속 나아가는 것이 그녀의 의무였다. 그녀는 여기까지 걸어온 그 두꺼운 장화를 벗고, 어여쁜 에나멜가죽 얇은 신으로 갈아 신고는, 벗은 장화를 나중에 쉬이 찾을 수 있도록 대문 기둥 옆 산울타리 속에 쑤셔 넣고 언덕을 내려갔다. 매서운 공기에서 얻은 화사한 혈색은, 목사관에 가까워질수록 그녀의 뜻과는 달리 옅어져 갔다.\n\n테스는 자기에게 유리하게 작용할 무슨 우연이라도 있기를 바랐으나, 아무것도 그녀에게 유리하지 않았다. 목사관 잔디밭의 관목들이 서리 어린 산들바람에 거북하게 바스락거렸다. 그녀는 제아무리 한껏 차려입었어도, 아무리 상상력을 펼쳐 봐도, 그 집이 가까운 친척의 거처라고는 도무지 느껴지지 않았다. 그런데도 본질적으로는, 본성에서나 감정에서나, 그녀를 그들에게서 갈라놓는 것이 아무것도 없었다. 고통에서도, 기쁨에서도, 생각에서도, 태어남에서도, 죽음에서도, 죽음 이후에서도, 그들은 한가지였다.\n\n그녀는 안간힘을 써서 마음을 다잡고, 회전 대문 안으로 들어가 초인종을 울렸다. 일은 저질러졌고, 물러설 길은 없었다. 아니, 일은 저질러지지 않았다. 그녀가 울린 종에 아무도 응답하지 않았다. 안간힘은 다시 한번 그러모아 짜내야 했다. 그녀는 두 번째로 종을 울렸고, 그 행동의 동요에다 십오 마일을 걸은 뒤의 피로까지 겹쳐, 기다리는 동안 한 손을 엉덩이에 짚고 팔꿈치를 현관 벽에 댄 채 몸을 지탱했다. 바람이 어찌나 살을 에는지 담쟁이 잎들이 쪼그라들어 잿빛으로 변했고, 저마다 쉴 새 없이 옆 잎을 톡톡 두드려 그녀의 신경을 불안스레 건드렸다. 어느 고기 사는 사람의 쓰레기 더미에서 날려 온 피 묻은 종잇조각 하나가 대문 밖 길에서 오르내리며 펄럭였다. 가라앉기엔 너무 가볍고 날아가기엔 너무 무거운 그것을, 지푸라기 몇 가닥이 벗 삼아 따라다녔다.\n\n두 번째 종소리는 더 컸지만, 여전히 아무도 오지 않았다. 그러자 그녀는 현관에서 걸어 나와, 대문을 열고 빠져나왔다. 그리고 마치 되돌아갈까 망설이듯 집의 정면을 미심쩍게 올려다보면서도, 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 대문을 닫았다. 자기가 알아보였을지도 모른다는(어떻게 그랬을지는 알 수 없었지만) 느낌, 그리고 자기를 들이지 말라는 분부가 내려졌을지도 모른다는 느낌이 그녀를 떠나지 않았다.\n\n테스는 모퉁이까지 갔다. 그녀는 자기가 할 수 있는 건 다 했다. 그러나 지금의 떨림을 모면하려다 훗날의 괴로움을 치르지는 않겠다고 마음먹고, 그녀는 다시 걸음을 돌려 집을 한참 지나치도록 되짚어 가며, 창이란 창은 다 올려다보았다.\n\n아—설명인즉슨, 그들이 모두, 하나도 빠짐없이 교회에 가 있다는 것이었다. 그녀는 남편이, 자기 아버지는 늘 하인들까지 포함해 온 식구가 아침 예배에 가기를 고집했고, 그 결과 집에 오면 찬 음식을 먹는다고 했던 말을 떠올렸다. 그러니, 예배가 끝날 때까지 기다리기만 하면 될 일이었다. 그녀는 그 자리에서 기다리며 사람들 눈에 띄고 싶지 않아, 교회를 지나 샛길로 빠지려고 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그러나 교회 묘지 문에 다다랐을 때 사람들이 쏟아져 나오기 시작했고, 테스는 그들 한가운데에 끼고 말았다.\n\n에민스터의 교인들은 그녀를, 한가로이 집으로 걸어가는 자그마한 시골 읍내 사람들의 무리가, 제 눈에 낯선 이로 비치는 평범치 않은 여자를 쳐다볼 수 있는 딱 그만큼으로 쳐다보았다. 그녀는 걸음을 재촉해, 자기가 왔던 길을 거슬러 올라갔다. 목사의 가족이 점심을 들고 나서 그녀를 맞이하기 편할 때까지 산울타리 사이에서 몸을 피해 있을 작정이었다. 그녀는 곧 교회에 가던 이들을 멀찍이 따돌렸으나, 팔짱을 낀 채 빠른 걸음으로 그녀 뒤를 바짝 쫓아오는 두 젊은 사내만은 예외였다.\n\n그들이 가까워지자 그녀는 그들의 목소리가 진지한 이야기에 몰두해 있음을 들을 수 있었고, 그런 처지의 여자다운 타고난 민첩함으로, 그 소리에서 제 남편의 음색과 같은 결을 알아채지 못할 리 없었다. 그 보행자들은 그의 두 형제였다. 모든 계획을 잊은 채, 테스의 단 하나의 두려움은, 그들과 마주할 채비도 되기 전에, 이 흐트러진 몰골로 그들이 지금 자기를 따라잡을까 하는 것이었다. 그들이 자기를 알아볼 리는 없다고 느끼면서도, 그녀는 본능적으로 그들의 살핌을 두려워했다. 그들이 빨리 걸을수록, 그녀도 빨리 걸었다. 그들은 점심이나 저녁을 들러 안으로 들어가기 전에, 긴 예배 내내 앉아 있느라 시린 팔다리에 온기를 되돌리려고, 분명히 잠깐 빠르게 거니는 산책에 나선 참이었다.\n\n테스보다 앞서 언덕을 오른 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었다—제법 흥미로운, 그러나 어쩌면 조금은 새침하고 점잔 빼는 듯한 숙녀풍의 젊은 여자였다. 테스가 거의 그녀를 따라잡았을 무렵, 시형제들의 걸음이 빨라져 그들이 그녀의 등 바로 뒤까지 다가오는 바람에, 그녀는 그들의 대화를 한마디도 빠짐없이 들을 수 있었다. 그러나 그들은 저 앞쪽 한층 더 멀리 있는 그 젊은 숙녀를 보고 한 사람이 \"저기 머시 챈트가 있네. 따라잡자\"라고 말하기 전까지는, 그녀의 흥미를 특별히 끄는 말은 아무것도 하지 않았다.\n\n테스는 그 이름을 알았다. 그 여자는 엔젤의 부모와 그녀의 부모가 엔젤의 평생 반려로 점지해 두었던, 그리고 끼어든 그녀 자신만 아니었다면 그가 필시 결혼했을 그 여자였다. 미리 들은 바가 없었더라도 그녀는 잠깐만 기다렸으면 그만큼은 알았으리라. 형제 중 하나가 이어서 이렇게 말했으니까. \"아! 가엾은 엔젤, 가엾은 엔젤! 저 참한 아가씨를 볼 때마다, 낙농장 처녀인지 뭔지에게 제 몸을 던져 버린 그애의 경솔함이 점점 더 한스러워. 보아하니 묘한 일이야. 그애한테 그 여자가 합류했는지 어떤지 난 모르겠어. 하지만 몇 달 전 그애한테서 소식을 들었을 땐 아직 안 그랬더라.\"\n\n\"나도 모르지. 그앤 요즘 나한테 아무것도 얘기를 안 해. 그애의 그 경솔한 결혼이, 그애의 그 별난 견해로 비롯된 나와의 그 소원함을 아주 완성해 버린 것 같아.\"\n\n테스는 그 긴 언덕을 더욱 빠르게 치고 올라갔다. 그러나 눈에 띄지 않고서는 그들을 앞질러 걸을 수가 없었다. 마침내 그들은 그녀를 아주 앞질러, 그녀 곁을 지나쳐 갔다. 한층 더 앞서 있던 젊은 숙녀가 그들의 발소리를 듣고 돌아섰다. 그러자 인사가 오가고 악수가 오갔으며, 셋은 함께 걸어갔다.\n\n그들은 곧 언덕 꼭대기에 다다랐고, 분명히 이 지점을 산책의 끝으로 삼을 작정인 듯 걸음을 늦추더니, 셋 다 한쪽으로 비켜 한 대문 쪽으로 갔으니, 바로 그 한 시간 전 테스가 읍내로 내려가기에 앞서 그곳을 둘러보려 멈춰 섰던 그 대문이었다. 이야기를 나누는 동안 성직자 형제 중 하나가 우산으로 산울타리를 조심스레 쑤시더니 무언가를 끄집어내 밝은 데로 끌어냈다.\n\n\"여기 헌 장화 한 켤레가 있네.\" 그가 말했다. \"어느 떠돌이쯤 되는 자가 버린 거겠지.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 맨발로 읍내에 들어와 우리 동정을 사 보려던 어느 사기꾼이겠죠.\" 챈트 양이 말했다. \"맞아요, 분명 그럴 거예요. 멀쩡한 걷기 좋은 장화인걸요—조금도 닳지 않았어요. 어쩜 그리 못된 짓을! 제가 가난한 누군가에게 주려고 집으로 가져가야겠어요.\"\n\n장화를 찾아낸 사람은 커스버트 클레어였는데, 그가 지팡이 갈고리로 그것을 집어 그녀에게 건네주었다. 그리하여 테스의 장화는 임자가 바뀌고 말았다. 이 말을 들은 그녀는 제 모직 베일의 가림막 아래로 그 곁을 지나쳐 걸었고, 이윽고 돌아보니 교회에서 온 그 무리가 그녀의 장화를 들고 대문을 떠나 언덕 아래로 물러간 것이 보였다.\n\n그리하여 우리의 여주인공은 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. 눈물이, 앞을 가리는 눈물이 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸다. 이 모든 광경을 제 자신에 대한 단죄로 읽게 만든 것이 죄다 감상이요, 아무 근거 없는 예민함임을 그녀는 알았다. 그런데도 그녀는 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 무방비한 제 몸뚱이 하나로 그 모든 불길한 징조들을 거스를 수가 없었다. 목사관으로 되돌아간다는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 엔젤의 아내는, 그 ―그녀에게는― 지나치게 점잖은 성직자들에게 멸시받는 무엇처럼 그 언덕으로 쫓겨 올라간 듯한 기분마저 들었다. 그 모욕이 아무리 무심코 가해진 것이라 해도, 아버지가 아니라 아들들과 마주친 것은 다소 불운한 일이었다. 아버지는 비록 옹졸한 데가 있긴 해도 그들보다 훨씬 덜 빳빳하고 덜 깐깐했으며, 자비라는 천품을 온전히 지닌 분이었으니까. 먼지투성이 제 장화를 다시 떠올리자, 그녀는 그것이 당한 조롱이 가여워, 그 신발마저 거의 측은해졌고, 그 임자에게 삶이 얼마나 가망 없는 것인지를 절감했다.\n\n\"아!\" 그녀가 여전히 제 신세를 가여워하며 한숨지었다. \"저 사람들은 몰랐던 거야, 그분이 사 준 이 어여쁜 신을 아끼려고 제일 험한 길에선 그 헌 장화를 신었다는 걸—아니야—저 사람들은 그걸 몰랐어! 그리고 그분이 내 어여쁜 옷 색깔을 골라 줬다는 것도 생각 못 했어—아니야—어떻게 알았겠어? 알았더라도 어쩌면 신경도 안 썼겠지, 그 사람들은 그분도 그리 마음에 두지 않으니까, 가엾어라!\"\n\n그러고서 그녀는, 인습에 매인 판단의 잣대로 이 모든 뒤늦은 슬픔을 자기에게 안긴 그 사랑하는 남자를 위해 마음 아파했다. 그리고 제 일생의 가장 큰 불행이, 시아버지를 그 아들들로 미루어 가늠한 탓에 바로 이 마지막 결정적 순간에 일어난 이 여성다운 용기의 상실이었음을 알지 못한 채, 그녀는 제 길을 갔다. 그녀의 지금 형편이야말로, 늙은 클레어 부부의 동정을 불러일으켰을 바로 그런 것이었다. 그들의 마음은, 그토록 절박하지는 않은 사람들의 미묘한 정신적 고뇌가 그들의 관심이나 배려를 얻지 못할 때조차, 극단의 경우들을 향해서는 단숨에 뛰쳐나갔다. 세리와 죄인들에게 와락 달려드느라, 그들은 서기관과 바리새인들의 근심에도 한마디쯤 해 줄 말이 있을 수 있음을 잊곤 했다. 그리고 이 결함, 혹은 이 한계가, 바로 이 순간 그들에게 자기네 며느리를, 그들의 사랑을 받기에 자못 알맞은 부류의 길 잃은 사람으로 천거해 주었을지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n그리하여 그녀는, 희망에 온전히 부풀지는 않았으나, 제 삶에 한 고비가 다가오고 있다는 확신만은 가득한 채로, 자기가 왔던 길을 따라 터덜터덜 되짚어 가기 시작했다. 보아하니 어떤 고비도 닥치지는 않은 듯했다. 그리고 다시 목사관과 마주할 용기를 그러모을 수 있을 때까지 그 굶주린 땅 같은 농장에 계속 머무는 것 말고는, 그녀에게 남은 일이 없었다. 사실 그녀는, 적어도 머시 챈트가 보여 줄 수 없는 그런 얼굴만은 자기가 내보일 수 있음을 세상에 알리려는 듯, 이 돌아오는 길에서 자기 자신에게 충분한 관심을 두어 베일을 걷어 올리기는 했다. 그러나 그것은 서글피 고개를 흔들며 한 짓이었다. \"아무것도 아니야—아무것도 아니야!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아무도 이걸 사랑하지 않아. 아무도 이걸 보지 않아. 나 같은 버림받은 것의 겉모습 따위 누가 신경이나 쓰겠어!\"\n\n그녀의 돌아오는 길은 행군이라기보다 차라리 어슬렁거림이었다. 거기엔 활기도, 목적도 없었다. 그저 한 가닥 가는 방향만 있을 뿐이었다. 지루하게 긴 벤빌 길을 따라 그녀는 지치기 시작했고, 대문에 기대고 이정표 옆에서 걸음을 멈추곤 했다.\n\n그녀는 일곱 번째인가 여덟 번째 마일에 이르러, 그 아래로 에버셰드라는 마을, 아니 작은 읍이 자리한 가파르고 긴 언덕을 내려가서야 비로소 어느 집에 들어섰다. 아침에 그토록 대조적인 기대를 안고 아침을 먹었던 그곳이었다. 그녀가 다시 들어가 앉은 교회 옆 오두막은 그 마을 끝자락에서 거의 첫 번째 집이었는데, 여자가 그녀에게 식료품 저장실에서 우유를 좀 가져다주는 동안, 테스는 거리를 내려다보다가 그곳이 아주 인적이 끊긴 듯함을 알아챘다.\n\n\"사람들이 오후 예배에 갔나 보네요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 얘.\" 늙은 여자가 말했다. \"그러기엔 아직 일러. 종이 아직 안 울렸거든. 다들 저쪽 헛간에 설교 들으러 갔지. 예배와 예배 사이에 거기서 떠돌이 설교꾼 하나가 설교를 하는데—훌륭하고, 불같고, 신실한 사람이라더라. 하지만 세상에, 난 그 사람 설교 들으러 안 가! 정식으로 강단에서 나오는 것만으로도 나한텐 충분히 뜨거우니까.\"\n\n테스는 곧 마을 안쪽으로 걸어 들어갔고, 그녀의 발소리가 마치 죽은 자들의 고장인 양 집집마다 메아리쳤다. 중심부에 가까워지자 다른 소리들이 그녀의 메아리에 끼어들었다. 그리고 길에서 멀지 않은 곳에 헛간이 보이자, 그녀는 이것이 그 설교꾼의 말소리이리라 짐작했다.\n\n그의 목소리는 고요하고 맑은 공기 속에서 어찌나 또렷해졌던지, 그녀가 헛간의 닫힌 쪽에 있었는데도 곧 그 문장들을 알아들을 수 있었다. 설교는, 예상할 만하게도, 더없이 극단적인 율법폐기론 류의 것으로, 성 바울로의 신학에 풀이된 대로의, 믿음에 의한 의로움에 관한 것이었다. 이 광상시인의 굳어진 관념은 활기찬 열정으로, 전적으로 격앙된 웅변조로 전해졌으니, 그에게는 변증가로서의 솜씨가 분명 없었던 까닭이다. 테스는 설교의 첫머리는 듣지 못했으나, 끊임없이 되풀이되는 것에서 그 본문이 무엇이었는지를 알아챘다—\n\n\"오 어리석은 갈라디아 사람들아, 예수 그리스도께서 너희 눈앞에 십자가에 못 박히신 것이 밝히 보였거늘, 누가 너희를 홀려서 진리에 순종치 못하게 하더냐?\"\n\n뒤에 서서 듣던 그녀는, 그 설교꾼의 교리가 엔젤의 아버지가 지닌 견해의 격렬한 형태임을 알아채고는 더욱 흥미가 일었고, 그 설교자가 자기가 어떻게 그런 견해에 이르게 되었는지 제 영적인 체험을 상세히 풀어놓기 시작하자 그녀의 흥미는 한층 짙어졌다. 그는 말하기를, 자기는 죄인 중의 가장 큰 죄인이었노라 했다. 비웃었고, 분별없는 자들과 음탕한 자들과 함부로 어울렸노라 했다. 그러나 깨우침의 날이 왔고, 인간적으로 보자면 그것은 주로 어떤 목사의 영향으로 이루어진 일인데, 자기는 처음엔 그를 모질게 모욕했으나, 그의 이별의 말이 제 마음에 가라앉아 거기 남아 있다가, 마침내 하늘의 은총으로 그 말들이 자기 안에 이 변화를 일으켜, 지금 그들이 보는 모습으로 자기를 만들었노라 했다.\n\n그러나 테스에게 그 교리보다 더 놀라웠던 것은 그 목소리였으니, 도무지 있을 수 없는 일 같았지만, 그것은 다름 아닌 알렉 더버빌의 목소리였다. 괴로운 긴장 속에 얼굴이 굳어진 채, 그녀는 헛간 정면으로 돌아 나와 그 앞을 지났다. 낮게 깔린 겨울 해가 이쪽 면의 두 짝 큰 출입문 위로 곧장 비쳐 들었다. 문 한 짝이 열려 있어, 햇살이 타작마당을 가로질러 멀리, 설교꾼과 그의 청중에게까지 뻗어 들었고, 그들은 모두 북풍을 피해 아늑하게 들어앉아 있었다. 듣는 이들은 죄다 마을 사람들이었는데, 그 가운데에는 예전 어느 잊지 못할 때에 빨간 페인트통을 들고 다니던 것을 그녀가 보았던 그 남자도 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 주의는 중앙의 인물에게로 쏠렸으니, 그는 곡식 자루 몇 개 위에 올라서서 사람들과 문 쪽을 향하고 있었다. 세 시의 해가 그에게 가득 비쳤고, 자기를 유혹했던 자가 자기와 마주하고 있다는 그 묘하게 맥 빠지는 확신은—그의 말을 또렷이 들은 그 순간부터 테스 안에서 줄곧 자라 오던 그것은—마침내 정말로 사실로 굳어졌다.\n\n회심한 자\n\n# XLV\n\n이 순간에 이르기까지 그녀는 트랜트리지를 떠난 이래로 더버빌을 한 번도 보지도, 그에게서 소식을 듣지도 못했다.\n\n그 마주침은 무거운 순간에, 그 충격이 감정적으로 가장 적은 동요로 와닿게끔 짜인 온갖 순간들 가운데 하나에 찾아왔다. 그러나 도리를 따지지 않는 기억이란 그런 것이어서, 비록 그가 거기 공공연히 또렷하게, 제 지난날의 방종을 슬퍼하는, 회심한 사람으로 서 있었는데도, 한 가닥 두려움이 그녀를 사로잡아 그녀의 움직임을 마비시켰으니, 그녀는 물러서지도 나아가지도 못했다.\n\n그 얼굴을 마지막으로 보았을 때 거기서 뿜어 나오던 것을 생각하고, 그것을 이제 와 바라보다니!… 거기엔 똑같이 잘생기고도 불쾌한 면모가 있었으나, 이제 그는 말끔히 다듬은 구식 구레나룻을 길렀고, 검은 콧수염은 사라지고 없었다. 그리고 그의 옷차림은 절반은 성직자풍이었으니, 그 손질이 그의 표정을 충분히 바꿔 놓아 그의 이목구비에서 멋부림을 걷어 냈고, 한순간 그녀가 그가 그 사람임을 믿지 못하게 막았다.\n\n테스의 감각에는, 처음 한순간, 이런 입에서 이 엄숙한 성경 말씀이 줄지어 나온다는 것에, 섬뜩한 기괴함이, 음산한 부조화가 있었다. 채 사 년도 안 된 전에, 이 너무도 익숙한 억양은 그녀의 귀에 그토록 어긋난 목적의 말들을 실어다 주었던 터라, 그녀의 마음은 그 대조의 아이러니에 자못 메슥거렸다.\n\n그것은 개심이라기보다 변모였다. 예전의 관능의 곡선들은 이제 경건한 열정의 선들로 다듬어져 있었다. 유혹을 뜻하던 입술 모양은 이제 간구를 표현하게끔 만들어졌고, 어제는 방탕으로 풀이될 수 있었던 뺨의 홍조는 오늘은 경건한 수사의 광채로 복음화되었으며, 동물성은 광신이 되었고, 이교는 바울로주의가 되었으며, 옛적에 그토록 위세 좋게 그녀의 자태에 번득이던 그 대담히 굴리는 눈은 이제 거의 사납다 할 신숭배의 거친 기운으로 빛났다. 제 바람이 가로막힐 때면 그의 얼굴이 짓곤 하던 그 검은 모서리들은, 이제 진창 속 뒹굴기로 기어이 다시 돌아서려 드는 구제 불능의 타락자를 그려 내는 데 쓰이고 있었다.\n\n이목구비는, 그 자체로, 항변하는 듯 보였다. 그것들은 제 본래의 함의에서 비틀려, 자연이 그것들에 의도하지 않은 인상들을 나타내게끔 돌려지고 말았다. 묘한 일이었다, 바로 그 드높여짐이 오용이라는 것이, 들어 올리는 것이 거짓되이 꾸미는 듯 보인다는 것이.\n\n그러나 정말 그럴 수 있을까? 그녀는 그 옹졸한 마음을 더는 인정하지 않으려 했다. 더버빌이, 제 영혼을 살리려고 제 사악함에서 돌아선 첫 번째 사악한 사람도 아닐진대, 어째서 그녀가 그것을 그에게서 부자연스러운 일이라 여겨야 한단 말인가? 그것은 다만, 좋은 새 말을 나쁜 옛 가락으로 듣는 통에 그녀 안에서 어긋나 버린 생각의 버릇일 뿐이었다. 죄인이 클수록 성인도 크다는 것은, 기독교 역사를 멀리 파헤치지 않고도 알아낼 수 있는 일이었다.\n\n이런 따위의 인상들이 그녀를 어렴풋이, 또렷한 또렷함도 없이 흔들었다. 놀라움의 맥 빠진 멈춤이 그녀가 움직이도록 놓아주자마자, 그녀의 충동은 그의 시야 밖으로 지나쳐 가는 것이었다. 해를 등진 그녀의 위치에서, 그는 아직 분명히 그녀를 알아보지 못한 터였다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 다시 움직이는 순간 그는 그녀를 알아보았다. 그녀의 옛 연인에게 미친 영향은 전기 같았으니, 그의 존재가 그녀에게 미친 영향보다 훨씬 더 강했다. 그의 불길은, 그의 웅변이 들끓던 그 울림은, 그에게서 빠져나가 버린 듯했다. 그의 입술은 거기 얹힌 말들 아래에서 버둥거리며 떨렸으나, 그녀가 그를 마주하고 있는 한 그 말들을 도무지 내뱉지 못했다. 그의 눈은, 그녀의 얼굴에 처음 한 번 던져진 뒤로는, 그녀 쪽을 빼고 다른 모든 방향으로 어지러이 매달렸으나, 몇 초마다 한 번씩 절박한 도약으로 되돌아왔다. 그러나 이 마비는 잠깐밖에 이어지지 않았으니, 그의 기운이 위축되는 만큼 테스의 기운이 되돌아왔고, 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 빠르게 헛간을 지나 앞으로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n생각을 추스를 수 있게 되자마자, 그것이, 그들의 처지가 서로 뒤바뀐 이 변화가 그녀를 소스라치게 했다. 그녀의 파멸을 빚어낸 자가 이제는 성령의 편에 서 있고, 그녀는 거듭나지 못한 채로 남아 있었다. 그리고, 그 전설에서처럼, 결국 그의 제단 위에 그녀의 키프로스 여신상이 별안간 나타나, 그로 인해 사제의 불길이 거의 다 꺼져 버린 꼴이 되고 만 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 고개를 돌리지 않고 계속 걸어갔다. 그녀의 등은 시선의 빛줄기에 대한 민감함을 부여받은 듯했고—심지어 그녀의 옷가지마저도—그만큼 그녀는, 그 헛간 바깥에서 자기에게 머물고 있을지 모를 상상 속의 시선에 생생히 깨어 있었다. 여기까지 오는 내내 그녀의 마음은 무력한 슬픔으로 무거웠으나, 이제는 그 괴로움의 결이 달라졌다. 너무 오래 거부당한 애정에 대한 그 굶주림이, 한동안은, 여전히 그녀를 에워싸고 있는 가차 없는 과거에 대한 거의 육체적인 감각에 밀려나 버렸다. 그것은 자기 잘못에 대한 의식을 실질적인 절망으로까지 짙게 했다. 그녀가 그토록 바랐던, 자기의 옛 삶과 지금의 삶 사이의 단절은, 결국 일어나지 않았던 것이다. 지난 일들은, 그녀 자신이 지난 일이 되기 전에는, 결코 온전한 지난 일이 되지 못하리라.\n\n그렇게 골똘히 잠긴 채, 그녀는 롱애시 길의 북쪽 부분을 직각으로 다시 가로질렀고, 이윽고 제 앞에, 남은 여정이 그 가장자리를 따라 놓인 그 고지로 희끄무레하게 오르는 길이 보였다. 그 메마르고 창백한 노면은 준엄하게 앞으로 뻗어, 그 차가운 황량함 위에 여기저기 점점이 흩어진 이따금의 갈색 말똥 말고는, 단 하나의 사람도, 탈것도, 자국도 없이 끊김 없이 이어졌다. 이 오르막을 천천히 헤치고 오르던 테스는 등 뒤의 발소리를 알아채고 돌아섰다가, 그 너무도 낯익은 형체가—그토록 기이하게 감리교도로 차려입은 그 형체가—다가오는 것을 보았으니, 이 세상 누구보다도 무덤 이편에서 홀로 마주치고 싶지 않은 단 한 사람이었다.\n\n그러나 생각하거나 피할 시간은 그리 많지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 자기를 따라잡게 둘 수밖에 없는 필연에 되도록 차분히 따랐다. 그녀는 그가, 빠른 걸음 때문이라기보다 제 안의 감정 때문에 흥분해 있음을 보았다.\n\n\"테스!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 돌아보지 않은 채 걸음을 늦추었다.\n\n\"테스!\" 그가 되풀이했다. \"나야—알렉 더버빌.\"\n\n그제야 그녀는 그를 돌아보았고, 그가 다가왔다.\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 그녀가 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n\"흠—그게 다요? 하지만 난 그 이상 받을 자격도 없지! 물론,\" 그가 가벼운 웃음을 곁들이며 덧붙였다. \"나를 이런 꼴로 보는 게 당신 눈엔 우스꽝스러운 데가 있겠지. 하지만—그건 감수해야겠지…. 당신이 떠나 버렸다고, 어디로 갔는지 아무도 모른다고 들었소. 테스, 내가 왜 당신을 뒤쫓아 왔는지 궁금하겠지?\"\n\n\"좀 그러네요. 그리고 진심으로, 당신이 따라오지 않았더라면 싶어요!\"\n\n\"그래—그렇게 말할 만도 하지.\" 그가 침울하게 받았다. 둘은 함께 앞으로 걸어갔고, 그녀는 내키지 않는 걸음이었다. \"하지만 나를 오해하진 마시오. 내가 이렇게 청하는 건, 당신이 저 아래서 당신의 갑작스러운 등장에 내가 얼마나 넋이 나갔는지—혹시 그걸 알아챘다면—그걸 보고 그렇게 여겼을지 몰라서요. 그건 그저 한순간의 흔들림이었소. 그리고 당신이 내게 어떤 사람이었는지를 생각하면, 그건 충분히 자연스러운 일이었지. 하지만 의지가 나를 그것에서 벗어나게 도와주었소—내가 이런 말을 하니 당신은 나를 사기꾼이라 여길지 모르겠지만—그리고 곧바로 나는 느꼈소, 모든 것 가운데\"\n세상에서 다가올 진노로부터 구해 내는 것이 내 의무이자 소망인 사람들 가운데—비웃고 싶으면 비웃어도 좋다—내가 그토록 심하게 욕보인 그 여자가 바로 그런 사람이었소. 나는 오직 그 목적 하나만 품고 왔소—그 이상은 없소.\"\n\n그녀의 대꾸하는 말에는 아주 가느다란 경멸의 결이 섞여 있었다. \"당신은 당신 자신은 구원했나요? 자선은 집안에서 시작된다잖아요.\"\n\n\"나는 아무것도 하지 않았소!\" 그가 무심하게 말했다. \"내 청중들에게 줄곧 말해 왔듯이, 하늘이 다 하셨소. 테스, 당신이 내게 아무리 경멸을 퍼붓는다 해도, 내가 나 자신에게 퍼부은 것에는—내 지난 세월의 옛 아담에게 퍼부은 것에는 미치지 못할 거요! 그래, 참 이상한 이야기지. 믿든 말든 좋소. 하지만 내 회심이 어떻게 이루어졌는지 그 경위를 들려줄 수는 있고, 적어도 들어 줄 만큼은 당신이 흥미를 느꼈으면 좋겠소. 에민스터의 목사 이름을 들어 본 적 있소—틀림없이 들어 봤겠지?—노년의 클레어 씨 말이오. 그분은 그 학파에서 가장 진실한 분들 중 하나이고, 교회에 몇 남지 않은 열정적인 사람들 중 하나요. 내가 한패로 합류한 기독교 신자들의 극단 진영만큼 강렬하지는 않지만, 국교회 성직자들 중에서는 아주 예외적인 분이오. 그 젊은 축들은 궤변으로 참된 교리를 차츰 묽게 만들어, 끝내는 옛 모습의 그림자에 지나지 않게 되어 가지. 나는 그분과 오직 교회와 국가의 문제에서만 의견이 갈리오—'너희는 그들 가운데서 나와 따로 있으라, 주께서 말씀하시느니라' 하는 본문의 해석에서 말이오—그뿐이오. 나는 굳게 믿거니와, 그분은 이 나라에서 당신이 댈 수 있는 그 누구보다도 더 많은 영혼을 구원해 낸 겸손한 도구였소. 그분 얘기를 들어 봤소?\"\n\n\"들어 봤어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"이삼 년 전 그분이 어느 선교 단체를 위해 설교하러 트랜트리지에 왔었소. 그리고 한심한 작자였던 나는, 그분이 사심 없이 나를 타이르고 길을 보여 주려 했을 때 그분을 모욕했소. 그분은 내 행동에 노여워하지 않았소. 그저, 언젠가 내가 성령의 첫 열매를 받으리라고, 비웃으러 온 자가 때로는 기도하러 남기도 한다고 말씀하셨을 뿐이오. 그분의 말씀에는 이상한 마력이 있었소. 그 말이 내 마음속에 가라앉았소. 하지만 가장 크게 나를 친 것은 어머니를 잃은 일이었소. 그리고 차츰차츰 나는 빛을 보게 되었소. 그때 이후로 내 유일한 소망은 참된 견해를 다른 사람들에게 전하는 것이었고, 오늘도 내가 하려던 게 바로 그것이오. 다만 이 근방에서 설교하게 된 건 최근 들어서요. 목회의 첫 몇 달은 잉글랜드 북부에서 낯선 사람들 틈에서 보냈소. 거기서 어설픈 첫 시도들을 해 보는 편을 택했지, 사람의 진실성을 시험하는 가장 혹독한 그 일—다시 말해 자기를 알아 온 사람들, 어둠의 시절을 함께한 동무들 앞에서 말하는 일—을 겪기 전에 용기를 좀 얻으려고 말이오. 테스, 자기 자신을 한바탕 호되게 후려치는 그 기쁨을 당신이 알기만 한다면, 나는 분명—\"\n\n\"그만하세요!\" 그녀가 격하게 외치며, 그에게서 돌아서 길가의 디딤다리 쪽으로 가더니 그 위에 몸을 기댔다. \"나는 그런 갑작스러운 일은 믿을 수가 없어요! 당신이 나한테 이렇게 말하는 게 분해요. 당신도 알면서—당신이 나한테 무슨 해를 끼쳤는지 알면서! 당신 같은 사람들은 나 같은 사람의 삶을 슬픔으로 쓰디쓰고 새카맣게 만들면서 이 땅에서 실컷 쾌락을 누리잖아요. 그러고 나서 그게 물릴 만큼 누리고 나면, 회심해서 하늘의 쾌락까지 챙길 궁리를 하니 참 갸륵하기도 하지! 그런 건 꺼져 버려요—나는 당신을 믿지 않아요—그게 싫어요!\"\n\n\"테스,\" 그가 다그쳤다. \"그런 말 하지 마오! 그건 내게 유쾌한 새로운 생각처럼 다가왔단 말이오! 그래서 당신은 나를 안 믿는다? 뭘 안 믿는다는 거요?\"\n\n\"당신의 회심요. 당신의 종교 책략요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n그녀가 목소리를 낮췄다. \"당신보다 나은 사람도 그런 건 믿지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"참 여자다운 이유로군! 그 나은 사람이 누구요?\"\n\n\"말할 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"흠,\" 그가 단언했는데, 그 말 밑에 깔린 원망이 언제라도 튀어나올 듯했다. \"내가 좋은 사람이라고 말하다니 천만의 말씀이오—나는 그런 말은 하지도 않소, 당신도 알잖소. 정말이지 나는 선함에는 신참이오. 하지만 신참이 때로는 가장 멀리 보는 법이오.\"\n\n\"네,\" 그녀가 슬프게 대답했다. \"하지만 나는 당신이 새로운 영으로 회심했다는 걸 믿을 수가 없어요. 알렉, 당신이 느끼는 그런 번쩍임은, 오래가지 못할까 두려워요!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 기대고 있던 디딤다리에서 돌아서 그를 마주 보았다. 그러자 그의 눈이 무심결에 그 낯익은 얼굴과 자태에 가닿더니, 그대로 머무르며 그녀를 응시했다. 그의 안에 있던 저열한 인간은 지금 잠잠했다. 그러나 그것은 분명 뽑혀 나간 것도, 완전히 제압된 것도 아니었다.\n\n\"나를 그렇게 보지 마오!\" 그가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n자기 행동과 표정을 전혀 의식하지 못하고 있던 테스는, 즉시 그 크고 검은 눈길을 거두며 얼굴을 붉히고 더듬거렸다. \"죄송해요!\" 그러자 그녀 안에서, 전에도 종종 찾아오던 그 비참한 감정이 되살아났다. 자연이 자기에게 부여한 이 육신의 장막 속에 깃들어 사는 것 자체가 어쩐지 잘못을 저지르는 것만 같은 그 감정이.\n\n\"아니, 아니오! 사과할 것 없소. 그런데 미모를 가리려고 베일을 쓰는 거라면, 왜 내려 두지 않는 거요?\"\n\n그녀가 황급히 베일을 끌어 내리며 말했다. \"주로 바람을 막으려는 거였어요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 이래라저래라 하는 게 내가 야박해 보일지도 모르겠소.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 내가 당신을 너무 자주 보지 않는 편이 낫소. 위험할지도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 말이오, 여자들의 얼굴은 이미 내게 너무 큰 힘을 휘둘렀던 터라 그것을 두려워하지 않을 수가 없소! 복음 전도자는 그런 것들과 아무 상관도 없어야 하는데, 그게 내가 잊고 싶은 옛 시절을 떠올리게 한단 말이오!\"\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로 두 사람의 대화는, 그들이 계속 걸어가는 동안 이따금 한마디씩 무심히 던지는 정도로 줄어들었다. 테스는 속으로 그가 자기와 함께 어디까지 가려는 건지 궁금해하면서도, 단호히 명령조로 그를 돌려보내고 싶지는 않았다. 문이나 디딤다리에 이를 때마다 그 위에 빨간색이나 파란색 글자로 성경 구절이 적혀 있는 것을 종종 보았는데, 그녀는 그에게 누가 수고스럽게 이런 표어들을 써 붙여 놓았는지 아느냐고 물었다. 그는 그 사람이 자기와, 그 지역에서 자기와 함께 일하는 다른 이들에게 고용되어, 악한 세대의 마음을 움직일 수 있는 어떤 방법도 시도하지 않은 채 남겨 두지 않으려고 이런 경고문들을 그려 넣는 거라고 말해 주었다.\n\n이윽고 길은 \"크로스인핸드\"라 불리는 곳에 닿았다. 이 표백된 듯 황량한 고지의 모든 자리 가운데 이곳이 가장 쓸쓸했다. 이곳은 화가와 풍경 애호가들이 풍경에서 찾는 그 매력으로부터 워낙 멀리 떨어져 있어, 도리어 새로운 종류의 아름다움, 비극적 색조를 띤 부정적인 아름다움에 이를 정도였다. 그곳은 거기 서 있는 돌기둥에서 이름을 따왔는데, 어느 지역 채석장에서도 알려지지 않은 지층에서 나온, 기이하고 거친 단일 석재였고, 그 위에는 사람의 손이 투박하게 새겨져 있었다. 그 내력과 의미에 관해서는 서로 다른 이야기가 전해졌다. 어떤 권위자들은 한때 신앙의 십자가가 그 위에 온전히 세워져 있었고 지금 남은 유물은 그 밑동에 불과하다고 했다. 또 어떤 이들은 그 돌이 서 있는 그대로 온전한 것이며, 경계나 만남의 장소를 표시하려고 거기 박아 둔 것이라고 했다. 어쨌든, 그 유물의 기원이 무엇이든, 그것이 서 있는 그 풍경 한가운데에는 기분에 따라 불길하거나 엄숙한 무언가가 있었고 지금도 있다. 가장 무덤덤한 행인조차 깊은 인상을 받게 하는 무언가가.\n\n\"이제 당신과 헤어져야 할 것 같소.\" 그들이 이곳에 가까워졌을 때 그가 말했다. \"오늘 저녁 여섯 시에 애벗스서널에서 설교를 해야 하는데, 내 길은 여기서 오른쪽으로 갈라지오. 그리고 당신은 나를 좀 어지럽혔소, 테시—왜인지는 말할 수 없고, 말하지 않겠소. 가서 힘을 좀 추슬러야겠소…. 그런데 어떻게 지금은 그렇게 유창하게 말하는 거요? 누가 당신한테 그런 좋은 영어를 가르쳐 주었소?\"\n\n\"고생하면서 이것저것 배웠어요.\" 그녀가 얼버무리며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 고생을 했단 말이오?\"\n\n그녀는 첫 번째 고생 이야기를 그에게 했다—그와 관련된 단 하나의 고생을.\n\n더버빌은 말문이 막혔다. \"지금까지 이걸 전혀 몰랐소!\" 그가 이어 중얼거렸다. \"고생이 닥쳐오는 게 느껴졌을 때 왜 내게 편지하지 않았소?\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그가 침묵을 깨고 덧붙였다. \"그래—당신은 나를 다시 보게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"다시는 내 곁에 오지 마세요!\"\n\n\"생각해 보겠소. 하지만 헤어지기 전에 이리 와 보오.\" 그가 기둥으로 다가갔다. \"이것은 한때 거룩한 십자가였소. 유물 같은 건 내 신조에 없지만, 나는 이따금 당신이 두렵소—지금 당신이 나를 두려워할 필요보다 훨씬 더 말이오. 그러니 내 두려움을 덜어 주려거든, 저 돌로 된 손 위에 당신 손을 얹고 맹세하시오. 절대로 나를 유혹하지 않겠다고—당신의 매력으로든 행동으로든.\"\n\n\"세상에—어쩜 그렇게 쓸데없는 걸 요구하세요! 그런 건 내 생각에서 가장 멀리 있는 일이에요!\"\n\n\"그래도—맹세하시오.\"\n\n테스는 반쯤 겁에 질려 그의 끈질긴 요구에 굴복했다. 돌 위에 손을 얹고 맹세했다.\n\n\"당신이 신자가 아니라는 게 안타깝소.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"어떤 불신자가 당신을 사로잡아 마음을 흔들어 놓았다니 말이오. 하지만 이제 그만하겠소. 적어도 집에서는 당신을 위해 기도할 수 있고, 그렇게 하겠소. 그러면 또 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠소? 나는 가오. 잘 있으오!\"\n\n그는 산울타리의 사냥용 쪽문으로 돌아서더니, 다시는 그녀에게 눈길을 두지 않고 그것을 뛰어넘어 애벗스서널 방향으로 고원을 가로질러 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다. 걸어가는 그의 걸음걸이에는 동요가 비쳤고, 이윽고 어떤 앞선 생각에 떠밀린 듯 주머니에서 작은 책을 꺼냈는데, 그 책장 사이에는 여러 번 다시 읽은 듯 닳고 더러워진 편지 한 통이 접혀 있었다. 더버빌은 그 편지를 펼쳤다. 그것은 이 무렵보다 몇 달 전 날짜로 되어 있었고, 클레어 목사의 서명이 있었다.\n\n편지는 더버빌의 회심에 대한 글쓴이의 꾸밈없는 기쁨을 표하는 것으로 시작했고, 그 일에 관해 목사에게 연락해 준 친절에 감사했다. 거기에는 더버빌의 지난 행실에 대한 용서를 따뜻이 확언하는 클레어 씨의 말과, 그 젊은이의 장래 계획에 대한 관심이 담겨 있었다. 클레어 씨 자신은 자기 인생의 그토록 여러 해를 바쳐 온 그 교회의 목회에 더버빌이 들어오는 것을 무척 보고 싶었을 것이고, 그러기 위해 신학교에 들어가도록 도왔으리라 했다. 그러나 수신인이 그로 인해 생길 지체 때문에 어쩌면 그러기를 원치 않았을 수도 있으니, 자기는 그 일의 절대적 중요성을 고집할 사람은 아니라고 했다. 사람은 누구나 자기가 가장 잘할 수 있는 방식으로, 성령이 이끄는 방법을 따라 일해야 하는 법이라고.\n\n더버빌은 이 편지를 읽고 또 읽으며, 냉소적으로 자기 자신을 조롱하는 듯했다. 그는 또 걸으면서 비망록의 몇 구절도 읽었고, 마침내 그의 얼굴은 평온함을 띠었으며, 보아하니 테스의 모습은 더 이상 그의 마음을 어지럽히지 않는 듯했다.\n\n그동안 그녀는 집으로 가는 가장 가까운 길이 나 있는 언덕 가장자리를 따라 걸어왔다. 일 마일도 채 안 되는 거리에서 외로운 양치기 한 사람을 만났다.\n\n\"제가 지나온 저 오래된 돌은 무슨 의미예요?\" 그녀가 그에게 물었다. \"저게 한때 거룩한 십자가였나요?\"\n\n\"십자가—아니요, 그건 십자가가 아니었어요! 그건 불길한 물건이에요, 아가씨. 옛날에 어떤 죄인의 친척들이 세운 거지요. 그 죄인은 거기서 손이 기둥에 못 박히는 고문을 당하고 나중에 교수형을 당했답니다. 그 밑에 뼈가 묻혀 있어요. 사람들 말로는 그자가 악마에게 영혼을 팔았고, 때때로 돌아다닌다고 하지요.\"\n\n그녀는 이 뜻밖의 섬뜩한 이야기에 가벼운 죽음의 전율을 느끼며, 그 외로운 남자를 뒤로하고 떠났다. 플린트콤애시에 가까워졌을 때는 땅거미가 지고 있었고, 마을 어귀의 골목에서 그녀는 한 처녀와 그 연인 곁으로 다가갔으나 그들은 그녀를 알아채지 못했다. 그들은 비밀이랄 것도 없는 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 남자의 더 따뜻한 어조에 응답하는 젊은 여자의 맑고 무심한 목소리가, 다른 무엇도 끼어들지 않는 고인 듯한 어둠으로 가득한 이 어스름한 지평선 안에서 유일하게 마음을 달래 주는 것으로 차가운 공기 속에 퍼졌다. 한순간 그 목소리들은 테스의 마음을 북돋웠으나, 곧 그녀는 이 만남도 어느 한쪽에서든 자기 고난의 서곡이 되었던 바로 그 끌림에서 비롯된 것임을 헤아렸다. 그녀가 가까이 다가가자, 처녀가 차분히 돌아보며 그녀를 알아보았고, 젊은 남자는 멋쩍게 자리를 떴다. 그 여자는 이즈 휴엇이었는데, 테스의 외출에 대한 관심이 곧바로 자기 일을 제쳐 두게 했다. 테스는 그 결과를 그다지 명확하게 설명하지 않았고, 눈치 빠른 처녀였던 이즈는 테스가 방금 한 장면을 목격한 자기 자신의 소소한 일에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"저 사람은 앰비 시들링이에요. 톨버세이스에서 가끔 와서 일손을 거들던 그 친구요.\" 그녀가 무심하게 설명했다. \"글쎄 내가 여기 온 걸 수소문해서 알아내고는 나를 따라왔지 뭐예요. 이 년째 나를 사랑해 왔다네요. 하지만 난 대꾸도 거의 안 했어요.\"\nXLVI\n\n# XLVI\n\n그녀의 헛된 여정이 있은 지 며칠이 지났고, 테스는 들판에 나가 있었다. 메마른 겨울바람이 여전히 불었지만, 바람이 들이치는 길목에 세워 둔 이엉을 엮은 가림막이 그 힘을 그녀에게서 막아 주었다. 바람을 등진 쪽에는 순무 써는 기계가 있었는데, 새로 칠한 그 선명한 파란빛은, 그 밖으로는 가라앉은 듯한 풍경 속에서 거의 목소리를 내는 것처럼 보였다. 그 앞쪽 맞은편에는 길쭉한 흙더미, 곧 \"무덤\"이 있었는데, 그 안에 순무 뿌리가 초겨울부터 저장되어 있었다. 테스는 그 덮개를 벗긴 끝자락에 서서, 작은 낫으로 뿌리 하나하나에서 잔뿌리와 흙을 쳐 내고, 그 일이 끝나면 그것을 써는 기계 안으로 던져 넣고 있었다. 한 남자가 기계의 손잡이를 돌리고 있었고, 그 통에서는 갓 잘린 순무가 쏟아져 나왔다. 그 노란 조각들의 싱싱한 냄새에는 코를 훌쩍이는 듯한 바람 소리, 써는 칼날의 날카로운 휙휙 소리, 그리고 테스의 가죽 장갑 낀 손이 휘두르는 낫의 탕탕 소리가 함께 어우러졌다.\n\n순무를 뽑아낸 자리에 드러난, 휑한 농경지의 갈색이 펼쳐진 너른 평지는, 차츰 더 짙은 갈색의 이랑들로 줄무늬지기 시작해, 점점 넓은 띠로 퍼져 가고 있었다. 그 이랑들 가장자리를 따라 무언가가 열 개의 다리로 기어가고 있었는데, 서두르지도 쉬지도 않으며 밭의 끝에서 끝까지 오르내리고 있었다. 그것은 말 두 필과 사람 하나였고, 그 사이로 쟁기가 가며, 봄 파종을 위해 정리된 땅을 갈아엎고 있었다.\n\n몇 시간 동안 이 즐거움 없는 단조로움을 덜어 주는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그러다 쟁기질하는 무리 너머 저 멀리에 검은 점 하나가 보였다. 그것은 산울타리 모퉁이의 터진 틈에서 나타났고, 비탈을 올라 순무 써는 사람들 쪽으로 향하는 기색이었다. 한낱 점에 불과하던 비례에서 그것은 볼링 핀의 형태로 커졌고, 곧 검은 옷을 입은 남자임이 드러났는데, 플린트콤애시 방향에서 오고 있었다. 기계 앞의 남자는 눈으로 달리 할 일이 없던 터라 다가오는 자를 줄곧 지켜보았지만, 일에 매여 있던 테스는 동료가 그 접근을 일러 주기 전까지는 그를 알아채지 못했다.\n\n그것은 그녀의 가혹한 주인 그로비 농장주가 아니었다. 반쯤 성직자 차림을 한 사람이었는데, 한때 거리낌 없고 태평하던 알렉 더버빌이었던 자를 지금 대신하고 있었다. 설교에 열이 올라 있지 않은 터라 지금의 그에게는 열정이 덜했고, 기계를 돌리는 남자가 있는 게 그를 거북하게 하는 듯했다. 테스의 얼굴에는 이미 창백한 괴로움이 어렸고, 그녀는 베일 달린 두건을 얼굴 위로 더 깊이 끌어당겼다.\n\n더버빌이 다가와 조용히 말했다—\n\n\"당신에게 할 말이 있소, 테스.\"\n\n\"당신은 내 마지막 부탁을, 내 곁에 오지 말라는 부탁을 거절했군요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소. 하지만 그럴 만한 까닭이 있소.\"\n\n\"그럼 말해 보세요.\"\n\n\"당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 심각한 일이오.\"\n\n그는 누가 엿듣지 않나 둘레를 살폈다. 그들은 써는 기계를 돌리는 남자에게서 어느 정도 떨어져 있었고, 기계가 움직이는 소리 또한 알렉의 말이 다른 귀에 닿는 것을 충분히 막아 주었다. 더버빌은 그 일꾼에게 등을 돌려 테스를 가리도록 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"이런 거요.\" 그가 변덕스러운 뉘우침을 담아 말을 이었다. \"지난번에 만났을 때 당신 영혼과 내 영혼을 생각하느라, 나는 당신의 형편을 물어보는 걸 소홀히 했소. 당신은 옷을 잘 차려입고 있었고, 나는 그 생각을 못 했소. 하지만 이제 보니 형편이 고되구려—내가 당신을 알던 시절보다 더 고되고—당신이 마땅히 받아야 할 것보다도 더 고되군. 어쩌면 그중 상당 부분이 내 탓일 거요!\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았고, 그는 캐묻듯 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그녀는 고개를 숙인 채, 두건에 얼굴이 완전히 가려진 채로, 다시 순무를 다듬는 일로 돌아갔다. 그녀는 일을 계속함으로써 그를 자기 감정 밖에 두기가 한결 수월하다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"테스,\" 그가 못마땅한 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. \"당신 일은 내가 관여했던 일들 중에서도 가장 최악이었소! 당신이 말해 주기 전까지 나는 어떤 결과가 빚어졌는지 전혀 몰랐소. 그 순결한 생명을 더럽히다니 나는 얼마나 못된 놈이었던지! 죄는 온전히 내 것이었소—트랜트리지에서의 그 모든 관습에 어긋난 일이 말이오. 그리고 당신도, 내가 한낱 비천한 모방에 불과한 그 진짜 핏줄을 지닌 당신도, 닥칠 수 있는 일들에 대해 얼마나 눈먼 어린것이었던지! 진심으로 말하건대, 부모가 딸들을 그렇게, 악한 자들이 그들에게 놓을 덫과 그물에 대해 위험할 만큼 무지한 채로 키우는 건 부끄러운 일이오. 그 동기가 선한 것이든 단순한 무관심의 결과이든 말이오.\"\n\n테스는 여전히 듣기만 할 뿐, 한 알 한 알 둥근 뿌리를 던지고 또 집어 들기를 기계처럼 규칙적으로 되풀이했고, 한낱 들일하는 여자의 시름 어린 윤곽만이 그녀를 드러낼 뿐이었다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 하려고 온 말은 그게 아니오.\" 더버빌이 말을 이었다. \"내 사정은 이렇소. 당신이 트랜트리지에 있던 이후로 나는 어머니를 잃었고, 그 영지는 내 것이 되었소. 하지만 나는 그것을 팔고 아프리카에서 선교 일에 헌신할 작정이오. 의심할 바 없이 나는 그 일에 형편없이 서투른 손을 보이겠지. 어쨌든, 내가 당신에게 묻고 싶은 건 이거요. 내가 의무를 다할 수 있게—당신에게 부린 농간에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 유일한 보상을 할 수 있게 해 주겠소. 다시 말해, 내 아내가 되어 나와 함께 가 주겠소?… 나는 벌써 이 귀한 서류를 마련해 두었소. 그건 늙으신 어머니의 임종 소원이었소.\"\n\n그가 멋쩍은 듯 약간 더듬거리며 주머니에서 양피지 한 장을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"그게 뭐예요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"혼인 허가증이오.\"\n\n\"오 아니에요, 안 돼요!\" 그녀가 화들짝 물러서며 재빨리 말했다.\n\n\"안 하겠다고? 어째서요?\"\n\n그가 그 질문을 던질 때, 좌절된 의무의 실망만은 아닌 어떤 실망이 더버빌의 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그것은 그녀를 향한 옛 정념의 무언가가 되살아났다는 명백한 징후였다. 의무와 욕망이 손을 맞잡고 나아가고 있었다.\n\n\"분명히,\" 그가 더 격한 어조로 다시 입을 열다가, 써는 기계를 돌리는 일꾼을 돌아보았다.\n\n테스 역시 이 논쟁이 여기서 끝날 수 없음을 느꼈다. 그녀는 그 남자에게 한 신사분이 자기를 보러 와서 잠깐 같이 걷고 싶다고 알린 뒤, 더버빌과 함께 얼룩말 무늬처럼 줄진 밭을 가로질러 자리를 옮겼다. 처음으로 새로 갈아엎은 구역에 이르자 그가 손을 내밀어 그녀가 그곳을 건너도록 도우려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 보지 못한 척, 흙이 말린 이랑들의 마루를 밟으며 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n\"테스, 나와 결혼해서 나를 스스로를 존중할 줄 아는 사람으로 만들어 주지 않겠소?\" 고랑을 다 건너자마자 그가 되풀이했다.\n\"그럴 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜요?\"\n\n\"내가 당신에게 아무런 애정도 없다는 걸 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 시간이 지나면, 어쩌면 그런 감정이 생길지도 모르지—당신이 정말로 나를 용서할 수 있게 되는 순간 말이오?\"\n\n\"절대로요!\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 단정하오?\"\n\n\"나는 다른 사람을 사랑하니까요.\"\n\n그 말은 그를 놀라게 한 듯했다.\n\n\"당신이?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"다른 사람을? 하지만 도덕적으로 옳고 마땅한 것에 대한 분별이 당신에게는 조금도 무게가 없단 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니에요—그런 말 하지 마세요!\"\n\n\"어쨌든 그렇다면, 이 다른 남자에 대한 당신의 사랑은 당신이 극복하게 될 한때의 감정일 뿐일지도—\"\n\"아니요—아니에요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그렇소! 왜 아니란 말이오?\"\n\n\"말할 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고 말해야 하오!\"\n\n\"그럼 말할게요…. 나는 그와 결혼했어요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 외쳤다. 그리고 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그녀를 응시했다.\n\n\"말하고 싶지 않았어요—말할 생각이 아니었어요!\" 그녀가 간청했다. \"여기서는 비밀이에요, 아니면 적어도 어렴풋이만 알려져 있어요. 그러니 제발, 부디 나한테 캐묻는 건 그만둬 주시겠어요? 우리는 이제 남남이라는 걸 기억하셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"남남—우리가? 남남이라!\"\n\n한순간 그의 옛 빈정거림의 번뜩임이 그의 얼굴에 어렸다. 그러나 그는 단호히 그것을 눌러 가라앉혔다.\n\n\"저 남자가 당신 남편이오?\" 그가 써는 기계를 돌리는 일꾼을 손짓으로 가리키며 기계적으로 물었다.\n\n\"저 남자라뇨!\" 그녀가 자랑스럽게 말했다. \"천만에요!\"\n\n\"그럼 누구요?\"\n\n\"내가 말하고 싶지 않은 걸 묻지 마세요!\" 그녀가 애원했고, 치켜든 얼굴과 속눈썹 그늘진 눈에서 그에게 호소를 번뜩였다.\n\n더버빌은 동요했다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 오직 당신을 위해서 물은 거요!\" 그가 발끈해서 되받았다. \"하늘의 천사들이여!—이런 말을 한 걸 하느님이 용서하시기를—맹세컨대 나는 당신을 위한다고 생각해서 여기 온 거란 말이오. 테스—나를 그렇게 보지 마오—나는 당신의 그 눈길을 견딜 수가 없소! 기독교 이전에도 이후에도 분명 저런 눈은 없었을 거요! 자—나는 정신을 잃지 않겠소. 감히 그럴 수 없소. 인정하건대, 당신을 본 것이 당신을 향한 내 사랑을 일깨웠소. 나는 그 사랑이 그런 온갖 감정과 함께 다 꺼져 버린 줄 알았는데 말이오. 하지만 나는 우리의 결혼이 우리 둘 다에게 성화가 될 수 있으리라 생각했소. '믿지 아니하는 남편이 아내로 말미암아 거룩하게 되고, 믿지 아니하는 아내가 남편으로 말미암아 거룩하게 되나니' 하고 나는 속으로 말했소. 하지만 내 계획은 산산이 부서졌으니, 나는 이 실망을 견뎌야 하오!\"\n\n그는 침울하게 눈을 땅에 두고 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"결혼했다. 결혼했어!… 그래, 그렇다면,\" 그가 사뭇 차분하게 덧붙이며, 허가증을 천천히 둘로 찢어 주머니에 넣었다. \"그렇게 막혀 버린 거라면, 나는 당신과 당신 남편에게, 그가 누구든, 무언가 좋은 일을 해 주고 싶소. 묻고 싶은 게 여럿 있지만, 물론 당신 뜻을 거슬러서 그러지는 않겠소. 다만 내가 당신 남편을 알 수만 있다면, 그와 당신에게 더 수월하게 도움을 줄 수 있을 텐데. 그는 이 농장에 있소?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"멀리 있어요.\"\n\n\"멀리? 당신에게서? 그 사람은 대체 어떤 남편이란 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 그이를 헐뜯지 마세요! 그건 당신 때문이었어요! 그이가 알아내고는—\"\n\n\"아, 그렇게 된 거요!… 그것 참 안됐구려, 테스!\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신에게서 떨어져 있다니—당신을 이렇게 일하게 내버려 두다니!\"\n\n\"그이는 나를 일하라고 내버려 둔 게 아니에요!\" 그녀가 자리에 없는 이를 변호하느라 온 열정을 다해 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"그이는 이걸 몰라요! 이건 내가 스스로 정한 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 편지는 하오?\"\n\n\"나는—말할 수 없어요. 우리 둘만의 사사로운 일들이 있어요.\"\n\n\"물론 그건 그가 편지를 안 한다는 뜻이로군. 당신은 버림받은 아내요, 나의 어여쁜 테스—\"\n\n충동에 휩싸여 그가 갑자기 돌아서 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다. 그 손에는 담황색 장갑이 끼워져 있었고, 그는 그 안의 생명도 형태도 드러내 주지 않는 거친 가죽 손가락만을 붙들었다.\n\n\"안 돼요—안 돼요!\" 그녀가 겁에 질려 외치며, 주머니에서 손을 빼듯 장갑에서 손을 빼내어 그 장갑을 그의 손아귀에 남겨 두었다. \"오, 제발 가 주시겠어요—나와 내 남편을 위해서—당신 자신의 기독교의 이름으로, 가 주세요!\"\n\n\"그래요, 그러겠소. 가겠소.\" 그가 불쑥 말하더니, 장갑을 그녀에게 도로 내밀고 떠나려 돌아섰다. 그러나 다시 몸을 돌려 말했다. \"테스, 하느님이 나의 심판자이심을 두고 맹세하건대, 당신 손을 잡은 데에 나는 아무런 속임수도 품지 않았소!\"\n\n그들이 골몰해 있느라 알아채지 못했던, 밭의 흙바닥을 두드리는 말발굽 소리가 바로 그들 뒤에서 멎었다. 그리고 한 목소리가 그녀의 귀에 닿았다.\n\n\"이런 대낮에 일은 안 하고 어디서 뭣들 하고 있는 거야?\"\n\n그로비 농장주가 멀리서 두 사람의 모습을 발견하고, 그들이 자기 밭에서 무슨 일을 하는지 알아보려고 꼬치꼬치 따지듯 말을 달려 가로질러 온 것이었다.\n\n\"그녀에게 그런 식으로 말하지 마시오!\" 더버빌이 말했고, 그의 얼굴은 기독교가 아닌 무언가로 검게 일그러졌다.\n\n\"허, 그러쇼, 나리! 그래 감리교 목사 양반이 이 여자랑 무슨 상관이라도 있소?\"\n\n\"이자는 누구요?\" 더버빌이 테스에게 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n그녀가 그에게 바싹 다가갔다.\n\n\"가세요—제발 부탁이에요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 그래서 당신을 저 폭군한테 내맡겨 두라고? 저자 얼굴만 봐도 얼마나 막돼먹은 놈인지 알겠소.\"\n\n\"그는 나를 해치지 않아요. 그는 나를 사랑하지 않으니까요. 나는 성모영보 축일에 떠날 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 나로서는 따르는 것 말고는 도리가 없겠지. 하지만—그래, 잘 있으오!\"\n\n그녀가 가해자보다도 더 두려워하던 그녀의 보호자가 마지못해 사라지자, 농장주는 꾸지람을 계속했고, 테스는 그것을 더없이 냉정하게 받아들였으니, 그런 종류의 공격은 성별과 무관한 것이었다. 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 그녀를 후려쳤을 이 돌 같은 남자를 주인으로 두는 것은, 앞서 겪은 일들에 비하면 차라리 안도에 가까웠다. 그녀는 자기 노동의 무대였던 밭 마루 쪽으로 말없이 되돌아 걸었는데, 방금 일어난 그 만남에 어찌나 빠져 있었던지, 그로비의 말 코가 자기 어깨에 거의 닿을 듯한 것도 거의 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"성모영보 축일까지 날 위해 일하기로 약속을 했으면, 그걸 지키도록 내가 단단히 지켜보겠어.\" 그가 으르렁거렸다. \"여편네들이란, 빌어먹을—이랬다저랬다 변덕이야. 하지만 더는 못 참아!\"\n\n그가 다른 여자들은, 한때 자기가 나가떨어졌던 일에 앙심을 품고 그녀를 들볶듯이 들볶지 않는다는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었기에, 한순간 그녀는 만약 자기가 방금 받은 제안을, 곧 돈 많은 알렉의 아내가 되라는 제안을 자유로이 받아들일 수 있었다면 그 결과가 어떠했을지 그려 보았다. 그것은 그녀를 예속에서 완전히 들어 올려 주었을 것이다. 지금의 이 억압적인 고용주뿐 아니라, 그녀를 멸시하는 듯한 온 세상으로부터의 예속에서. \"하지만 안 돼, 안 돼!\" 그녀가 숨 가쁘게 말했다. \"지금 그와 결혼할 수는 없었어! 그는 나한테 너무나 거북한 사람이야.\"\n\n바로 그날 밤 그녀는 클레어에게 호소하는 편지를 쓰기 시작했는데, 자기 고생은 그에게 감추고, 자기의 변치 않는 애정을 다짐했다. 행간을 읽어 낼 위치에 있는 사람이라면 누구든, 그 깊은 사랑 뒤편에 어떤 끔찍한 두려움이—거의 절망에 가까운 것이—드러나지 않은 어떤 은밀한 사태에 대한 두려움이 도사리고 있음을 알아챘을 것이다. 그러나 이번에도 그녀는 자기 토로를 끝맺지 못했다. 그는 이즈에게 함께 가자고 했었고, 어쩌면 그는 그녀를 조금도 마음에 두지 않는지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 편지를 상자에 넣어 두고, 그것이 과연 언젠가 에인절의 손에 닿을지 궁금해했다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로 그녀의 매일의 일과는 제법 무겁게 흘러갔고, 마침내 농사꾼들에게 큰 의미가 있는 날—캔들마스 장날에 이르렀다. 바로 이 장에서 다가올 성모영보 축일 이후 열두 달치 새 고용 계약이 맺어졌고, 일자리를 옮길 생각이 있는 농촌 사람들은 장이 서는 군청 소재지에 으레 모여들었다. 플린트콤애시 농장의 일꾼들은 거의 다 떠날 작정이었고, 이른 아침부터 읍내 방향으로 일제히 빠져나가는 행렬이 이어졌는데, 그 읍내는 언덕진 지형을 넘어 십에서 십이 마일가량 떨어진 곳에 있었다. 테스 역시 분기일에 떠날 작정이었지만, 다른 노천 고용 계약을 맺을 필요가 없게 될 무슨 일이 일어나리라는 막연한 희망을 품고서, 장에 가지 않은 몇 안 되는 사람 중 하나였다.\n\n이 무렵치고는 놀랍도록 포근한, 평온한 이월의 어느 날이었고, 누구라도 겨울이 다 간 거라고 생각할 뻔했다. 그녀가 점심을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그녀가 하숙하고 있던 오두막의 창을 더버빌의 모습이 어둡게 가렸는데, 오늘은 그 오두막을 그녀 혼자 차지하고 있었다.\n\n테스는 벌떡 일어섰지만, 그녀의 방문객은 이미 문을 두드린 뒤였고, 이치상 도무지 달아날 수도 없었다. 더버빌이 문을 두드리는 모양, 문까지 걸어오는 걸음에는 그녀가 지난번에 보았을 때의 그 태도와는 뭐라 형언할 수 없이 다른 무언가가 있었다. 그것들은 행하는 자가 부끄러워하는 행동들 같았다. 그녀는 문을 열지 않으리라 생각했다. 그러나 그것도 별 의미가 없었으므로 일어나, 빗장을 들어 올리고는 재빨리 뒤로 물러섰다. 그가 들어와 그녀를 보더니, 말도 꺼내기 전에 의자에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n\"테스—어쩔 수가 없었소!\" 그가 절박하게 입을 떼며, 흥분의 홍조까지 겹쳐 달아오른 얼굴을 닦았다. \"적어도 잘 지내는지 안부라도 물으려고 들러야겠다고 느꼈소. 정말이지 그 일요일에 당신을 보기 전까지는 당신 생각을 전혀 하지 않고 있었소. 그런데 이제는 아무리 애를 써도 당신의 모습을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없소! 선한 여자가 악한 남자에게 해를 끼친다는 건 고약한 일이지만, 사실이 그렇소. 테스, 당신이 나를 위해 기도만 해 준다면!\"\n\n그의 태도에 억눌린 불만은 거의 가련할 지경이었으나, 그래도 테스는 그를 가엾이 여기지 않았다.\n\n\"내가 어떻게 당신을 위해 기도하겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"세상을 움직이는 그 위대한 힘이 나 때문에 자기 계획을 바꾸시리라고 믿는 것이 내겐 금지되어 있는데요?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 생각하오?\"\n\n\"네. 나는 달리 생각하는 그 주제넘음에서 고침을 받았어요.\"\n\n\"고침을? 누구에게?\"\n\n\"굳이 말하자면, 내 남편에게요.\"\n\n\"아—당신 남편—당신 남편이라! 참 이상하게 들리는구려! 일전에도 당신이 그 비슷한 걸 넌지시 비쳤던 게 기억나오. 테스, 당신은 이런 문제들에 대해 정말로 무엇을 믿소?\" 그가 물었다. \"당신은 아무 종교도 없는 것 같은데—어쩌면 나 때문이겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 종교가 있어요. 비록 초자연적인 건 아무것도 믿지 않지만요.\"\n\n더버빌이 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 보았다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 택한 길이 죄다 틀렸다고 생각하오?\"\n\n\"상당 부분은요.\"\n\n\"흠—그런데도 나는 그것에 대해 그토록 확신했는데.\" 그가 불안하게 말했다.\n\n\"나는 산상수훈의 정신은 믿어요. 내 사랑하는 남편도 그랬고요…. 하지만 나는 믿지 않아요—\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 자기가 부정하는 것들을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"실은 말이오,\" 더버빌이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"당신의 그 사랑하는 남편이 믿은 건 뭐든 당신이 받아들이고, 그가 물리친 건 뭐든 당신이 물리치는군. 당신 나름의 조사나 추론은 조금도 없이 말이오. 그게 딱 당신네 여자들이지. 당신 정신은 그에게 노예가 되어 있소.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 그이가 모든 걸 알았으니까요!\" 그녀가, 아무리 완전한 사람이라도 받기 힘들었을, 하물며 그녀의 남편으로서는 더더욱 받기 힘들었을, 에인절 클레어에 대한 의기양양한 단순한 믿음으로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지, 하지만 그렇게 남의 부정적인 견해를 통째로 받아들여서는 안 되오. 당신한테 그런 회의주의를 가르치다니 어지간한 작자였겠군!\"\n\n\"그이는 내 판단을 강요한 적이 없어요! 그이는 결코 그 문제로 나와 다투려 하지 않았어요! 하지만 나는 이렇게 보았어요. 교리를 깊이 파고들어 본 끝에 그이가 믿은 것이, 교리를 전혀 들여다본 적도 없는 내가 믿을 만한 것보다 옳을 가능성이 훨씬 크다고요.\"\n\n\"그가 뭐라고 말하곤 했소? 뭔가 말했을 것 아니오?\"\n\n그녀는 곰곰이 생각했다. 그리고 에인절 클레어의 말이라면 그 정신을 이해하지 못할 때조차 그 글자 하나하나를 예리하게 기억하는 능력으로, 이따금 그가 그녀를 곁에 두고 일종의 소리 내어 생각하기에 빠져들 때 그가 쓰던 어떤 가차 없는 논쟁적 삼단논법을 떠올렸다. 그것을 옮기면서 그녀는 클레어의 말투와 태도까지도 경건한 충실함으로 함께 흉내 냈다.\n\n\"그걸 다시 말해 보오.\" 더없이 주의 깊게 듣고 있던 더버빌이 청했다.\n\n그녀가 그 논증을 되풀이했고, 더버빌은 생각에 잠겨 그녀를 따라 그 말을 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"다른 건 또 없소?\" 그가 이윽고 물었다.\n\n\"또 언젠가는 이런 비슷한 말을 했어요.\" 그러고서 그녀는 다른 하나를 들려주었는데, 그것은 어쩌면 『철학 사전』에서부터 헉슬리의 『논문집』에 이르는 계보의 숱한 저작에서 그 짝을 찾을 수 있었을 법한 것이었다.\n\n\"아—하! 그걸 어떻게 다 기억하오?\"\n\n\"나는 그이가 믿는 걸 믿고 싶었어요. 그이는 내가 그러기를 바라지 않았는데도요. 그래서 그이를 구슬려서 그이 생각 몇 가지를 말하게 했어요. 그 말은 내가 다 이해한다고는 못 하겠어요. 하지만 그게 옳다는 건 알아요.\"\n\n\"흠. 당신 자신도 모르는 걸 나한테 가르칠 수 있다니, 참 기막히군!\"\n\n그는 생각에 빠졌다.\n\n\"그래서 나는 내 영적인 운명을 그이의 것에 걸었어요.\" 그녀가 다시 말했다. \"나는 그게 달라지기를 바라지 않았어요. 그이에게 충분히 좋은 거라면 나한테도 충분히 좋아요.\"\n\n\"그가, 당신도 자기만큼이나 대단한 이교도라는 걸 아오?\"\n\n\"아니요—나는 그이한테 말한 적 없어요—내가 이교도라면 말이지만요.\"\n\n\"그래—따지고 보면, 테스, 당신은 오늘 나보다 형편이 낫소! 당신은 내 교리를 설교해야 한다고 믿지 않으니, 그것을 삼간다 해서 양심에 거리낄 게 없소. 나는 그것을 설교해야 한다고 믿는데도, 마귀들처럼, 믿으면서 떠는 거요. 왜냐하면 나는 별안간 그 설교를 그만두고 당신을 향한 내 정념에 굴복해 버렸으니까.\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\"\n\"아니 글쎄,\" 그가 메마르게 말했다. \"오늘 당신을 보겠다고 이 먼 길을 다 왔단 말이오! 그런데 나는 캐스터브리지 장에 가려고 집을 나섰소. 거기서 오늘 오후 두 시 반에 짐마차 위에서 말씀을 설교하기로 약속이 되어 있고, 거기서 형제들 모두가 바로 이 순간 나를 기다리고 있소. 여기 그 공고문이 있소.\"\n\n그가 가슴주머니에서 벽보 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 모임의 날짜와 시간과 장소가, 그리고 그 자리에서 그, 더버빌이 앞서 말한 대로 복음을 설교하리라는 것이 인쇄되어 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 거기에 어떻게 가시려고요?\" 테스가 시계를 보며 말했다.\n\n\"갈 수가 없소! 나는 여기로 와 버렸으니까.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요, 정말로 설교하기로 약속을 해 놓고, 그런데—\"\n\n\"설교하기로 약속을 해 놓고도, 거기 가지 않을 거요—한때 내가 멸시했던 한 여자를 보고 싶다는 불타는 욕망 때문에!—아니, 내 말과 진실을 두고 맹세하건대, 나는 당신을 멸시한 적이 없소. 멸시했다면 지금 당신을 사랑하지도 않을 거요! 내가 당신을 멸시하지 않았던 건, 당신이 그 모든 일에도 더럽혀지지 않은 채로 남았기 때문이오. 당신은 상황을 알아채자 그토록 재빠르고 단호하게 내게서 물러섰고, 내 마음대로 곁에 머물러 있지 않았소. 그래서 세상에 내가 경멸하지 않은 치마 하나가 있었으니, 그게 바로 당신이오. 하지만 이제는 당신이 나를 경멸해도 마땅하오! 나는 산 위에서 경배한다고 여겼는데, 알고 보니 여전히 숲속 동산에서 섬기고 있구려! 하! 하!\"\n\n\"오 알렉 더버빌! 이게 무슨 뜻이에요? 내가 뭘 했다고요!\"\n\n\"했다고?\" 그가 그 말에 영혼 없는 비웃음을 담아 말했다. \"일부러 한 건 아무것도 없소. 하지만 당신은 내 타락의—그들 말로 '되돌아감'의—그 빌미가, 죄 없는 빌미가 된 거요. 나는 나 자신에게 묻소. 나는 정말로, '세상의 더러움을 피한 후에 다시 거기 얽매여 정복당하는'—그 나중 형편이 처음보다 더 나빠지는—저 '썩어짐의 종들' 중 하나인가?\" 그가 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹었다. \"테스, 내 아가씨, 나는 당신을 다시 보기 전까지는 적어도 사회적 구원에 이르는 길 위에 있었단 말이오!\" 그가 그녀를 마치 어린아이인 양 변덕스럽게 흔들며 말했다. \"그런데 어째서 당신은 나를 유혹한 거요? 나는 그 눈과 그 입을 다시 보기 전까지는 사람이 그럴 수 있는 한 굳건했소—분명 이브 이후로 그토록 사람을 미치게 하는 입은 없었을 거요!\" 그의 목소리가 잦아들었고, 그 자신의 검은 눈에서 뜨거운 교태가 쏘아져 나왔다. \"이 요부 같으니, 테스. 이 사랑스러운 빌어먹을 바빌론의 마녀 같으니—나는 당신을 다시 만나자마자 도무지 당신을 뿌리칠 수가 없었소!\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 다시 보게 된 걸 내가 어쩔 수는 없잖아요!\" 테스가 움찔 물러서며 말했다.\n\n\"알아—거듭 말하지만 나는 당신을 탓하지 않소. 하지만 사실은 사실이오. 그날 농장에서 당신이 함부로 부려지는 걸 보았을 때, 나는 당신을 보호할 법적 권리가 내게 없다는 생각에—그 권리를 가질 수 없다는 생각에 미칠 지경이었소. 한편 그 권리를 가진 자는 당신을 완전히 내버려 두는 것 같으니!\"\n\n\"그이를 헐뜯지 마세요—그이는 멀리 있단 말이에요!\" 그녀가 몹시 흥분해서 외쳤다. \"그이를 정정당당하게 대해 주세요—그이는 당신에게 잘못한 적이 없어요! 오, 그이의 정직한 이름에 해가 될 추문이 퍼지기 전에 그이의 아내에게서 떠나가 주세요!\"\n\n\"그러겠소—그러겠소.\" 그가 마치 유혹하는 꿈에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 말했다. \"나는 장에서 그 가엾고 술 취한 얼간이들에게 설교하기로 한 약속을 깨 버렸소—이런 짓궂은 장난을 친 건 처음이오. 한 달 전이었다면 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있다는 데에 소름이 끼쳤을 거요. 나는 떠나겠소—맹세하리다—그리고—아, 내가 할 수 있을까! 멀찍이 떨어져 있겠다고.\" 그러더니 별안간, \"한 번만 안아 봅시다, 테시—딱 한 번! 그저 옛정을 봐서—\"\n\n\"나는 막아 낼 힘이 없어요, 알렉! 한 선한 사람의 명예가 내 보살핌에 달려 있어요—생각해 봐요—부끄러운 줄 아세요!\"\n\n\"쳇! 그래, 알았소—알았소!\"\n\n그는 자신의 나약함에 스스로 분이 나서 입술을 앙다물었다. 그의 눈은 세속적 신앙도 종교적 신앙도 다 메말라 있었다. 그가 회개한 이래 그의 얼굴 주름 사이에 생기 없이 누워 있던 그 옛 변덕스러운 정념의 송장들이, 부활하듯 깨어나 한데 모여드는 듯했다. 그는 마음을 정하지 못한 채 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n오늘 자기 약속을 어긴 이 일이 신자의 단순한 되돌아감일 뿐이라고 더버빌은 단언했지만, 에인절 클레어의 말이 메아리쳐 나온 테스의 말은 그에게 깊은 인상을 남겼고, 그가 그녀를 떠난 뒤에도 계속 그러했다. 그는 말없이 걸어 나갔는데, 마치 자기 입지가 더는 지탱될 수 없다는, 여태껏 꿈에도 생각지 못한 가능성에 그의 기력이 마비된 듯했다. 이성은 그의 변덕스러운 회심과는 아무 상관이 없었으니, 그 회심은 어쩌면 새로운 자극을 찾던 한 무심한 남자의 한낱 변덕에 지나지 않았고, 어머니의 죽음에 일시적으로 감화된 것에 불과했다.\n\n테스가 그의 열정의 바다에 떨어뜨린 논리의 물방울들은 그 끓어오름을 식혀 고요한 정체로 만들어 버렸다. 그녀가 자기에게 건네준 그 결정처럼 굳은 문구들을 거듭 곱씹으며 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"그 영리한 친구는, 그녀에게 그런 것들을 가르쳐 줌으로써 자기가 그녀에게로 돌아갈 내 길을 닦아 주고 있는 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐겠지!\"\n\n# XLVII\n\nXLVII\n플린트콤애시 농장에서 마지막 밀 낟가리를 타작하는 날이다. 삼월 아침의 동틀 녘은 유난히 표정이 없어, 동쪽 지평선이 어디쯤 놓여 있는지조차 알 길이 없다. 그 어스름을 배경으로 사다리꼴 모양의 낟가리 꼭대기가 솟아 있는데, 그것은 겨울 날씨에 씻기고 바래며 이곳에 쓸쓸히 서 있었다.\n\n이즈 휴엇과 테스가 작업 현장에 다다랐을 때는, 바스락거리는 소리만이 다른 이들이 앞서 와 있음을 알려 줄 뿐이었다. 거기에, 빛이 밝아지면서 이윽고 꼭대기에 있는 두 남자의 실루엣이 더해졌다. 그들은 낟가리를 \"벗기느라\", 곧 낟단을 던져 내리기에 앞서 이엉을 걷어 내느라 분주했다. 그 일이 진행되는 동안 이즈와 테스는 다른 여자 일꾼들과 함께, 희끄무레한 갈색 앞치마 차림으로 서서 기다리며 떨고 있었으니, 그로비 농장주가 되도록이면 하루 안에 일을 끝마치려고 그들이 이렇게 이른 시각에 현장에 나와 있어야 한다고 우긴 탓이었다. 낟가리 처마 바로 밑에, 아직 거의 보이지 않게, 여자들이 와서 섬겨야 할 그 붉은 폭군이 있었다—가죽끈과 바퀴가 딸린 목제 틀의 구조물, 곧 타작기였는데, 그것은 돌아가는 동안 그들의 근육과 신경의 인내에 대해 폭군 같은 요구를 끊임없이 해 댔다.\n\n조금 떨어진 곳에 또 하나의 어렴풋한 형체가 있었다. 이쪽은 검었고, 한껏 비축된 힘을 말해 주는 듯한 끊임없는 쉭쉭 소리를 내고 있었다. 물푸레나무 곁으로 길게 솟은 굴뚝과 그 자리에서 뿜어 나오는 온기는, 많은 햇빛이 필요하지도 않게, 이곳이 이 작은 세계의 으뜸 동력 노릇을 할 기관임을 설명해 주었다. 그 기관 곁에는 검고 미동도 없는 한 존재가 서 있었는데, 그을음투성이에 때 낀, 큰 키를 한 화신이 일종의 무아지경에 빠진 채, 곁에 석탄 더미를 쌓아 두고 있었다. 그는 화부였다. 그의 태도와 빛깔의 고립감은 그에게 마치 토페트에서 온 피조물 같은 외양을 입혀 주었으니, 노란 곡식과 창백한 흙의 이 맑고 연기 없는 지역으로 잘못 흘러들어 와, 자기와는 아무런 공통점도 없는 이곳의 원주민들을 놀라게 하고 어지럽히는 것만 같았다.\n\n그가 보이는 그대로 그는 느꼈다. 그는 농경의 세계 안에 있었으나, 그 세계에 속한 자는 아니었다. 그는 불과 연기를 섬겼다. 들판의 이 거주민들은 초목과 날씨와 서리와 햇빛을 섬겼다. 그는 자기 기관과 함께 농장에서 농장으로, 군에서 군으로 떠돌았으니, 아직 이 웨식스 지방에서 증기 타작기는 떠돌이 신세였기 때문이다. 그는 낯선 북부 사투리로 말했고, 생각은 자기 자신에게로 향한 채, 눈은 자기 쇠붙이 피보호물에 두고서, 주위의 풍경은 거의 알아보지도 못했고 조금도 마음에 두지 않았다. 토착민들과는 오직 꼭 필요한 만큼만 교류했으니, 마치 어떤 오랜 운명의 저주가 그를 자기 뜻과 무관하게 이곳에서, 그의 명부의 주인을 섬기며 떠돌게 강요하는 것만 같았다. 그의 기관 구동 바퀴에서 낟가리 밑의 붉은 타작기로 이어진 그 긴 가죽끈만이 농경과 그를 잇는 유일한 연결선이었다.\n\n그들이 낟단을 벗기는 동안 그는 자기의 휴대용 동력 저장고 곁에 무덤덤하게 서 있었고, 그 뜨거운 검정 둘레로 아침 공기가 일렁였다. 그는 준비 작업과는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그의 불은 백열로 달궈진 채 대기하고 있었고, 그의 증기는 고압에 차 있었으니, 몇 초 안에 그는 그 긴 가죽끈을 보이지 않는 속도로 움직이게 할 수 있었다. 그 끈이 미치는 범위 너머의 환경이 곡식이든 짚이든 혼돈이든, 그에게는 다 매한가지였다. 토박이 게으름뱅이 중 누구라도 그에게 너는 자신을 뭐라 부르느냐고 물으면, 그는 짤막하게 대답했다. \"기관사요.\"\n\n날이 완전히 밝아 낟가리의 벗김이 끝났다. 그러자 남자들이 제 자리를 잡았고, 여자들이 올라갔으며, 일이 시작되었다. 그로비 농장주는—혹은 그들이 부르는 대로 \"그분\"은—이미 그 전에 도착해 있었고, 그의 지시로 테스는 기계의 발판 위, 낟단을 먹이는 남자 가까이에 배치되었다. 그녀가 할 일은 이즈 휴엇이 건네주는 곡식 낟단을 하나하나 푸는 것이었는데, 이즈는 그 옆에, 다만 낟가리 위에 서 있었다. 그래서 먹이는 사람이 그것을 움켜쥐어 돌아가는 통 위에 펼치면, 그 통이 한순간에 알곡을 모조리 털어 내는 것이었다.\n\n처음에 한두 번 걸리적거리는 일이 있어 기계를 싫어하는 자들의 마음을 흐뭇하게 한 뒤, 그들은 곧 한창 진행에 들어섰다. 일은 아침 식사 때까지 속도를 냈고, 그때 타작기가 반 시간 동안 멈췄다. 식사 후 다시 시작하면서, 농장의 보조 일손 전부가 짚가리를 쌓는 노동에 투입되었는데, 그 짚가리는 곡식 낟가리 곁에 자라나기 시작했다. 점심은 자리를 뜨지 않고 선 채로 서둘러 먹었고, 그러고서 또 두어 시간이 흘러 저녁 식사 때가 가까워졌다. 가차 없는 바퀴는 줄곧 돌아갔고, 타작기의 파고드는 듯한 윙윙거림은 돌아가는 철망 우리 가까이에 있는 모든 이의 골수까지 떨리게 했다.\n\n쌓여 가는 짚가리 위의 노인들은, 떡갈나무 헛간 바닥에서 도리깨로 타작하곤 하던 옛날을 이야기했다. 키질에 이르기까지 모든 것이 손노동으로 이루어지던 시절을 말이다. 그들이 생각하기에 그것은 비록 더디기는 해도 더 나은 결과를 냈다. 곡식 낟가리 위의 사람들도 조금은 이야기를 나누었다. 그러나 테스를 포함해 기계에 붙어 땀 흘리는 이들은 여러 마디 말을 주고받으며 자기 일을 가볍게 할 수 없었다. 그녀를 그토록 호되게 시험한 것은 바로 그 일의 끊임없음이었고, 그것은 그녀로 하여금 차라리 플린트콤애시에 오지 않았더라면 하고 바라게 만들기 시작했다. 곡식 낟가리 위의 여자들은—특히 그중 하나인 마리안은—이따금 멈춰 서서 큰 병에 든 에일이나 차가운 차를 마시거나, 얼굴을 닦고 옷에서 짚과 겨 부스러기를 떨어내며 한담 몇 마디를 나눌 수 있었다. 그러나 테스에게는 한숨 돌릴 틈이 없었다. 통이 결코 멈추지 않으니 그것에 낟단을 먹이는 남자도 멈출 수 없었고, 그 남자에게 푼 낟단을 대 주어야 하는 그녀 역시 멈출 수 없었기 때문이다. 다만 마리안이 그녀와 자리를 바꿔 줄 때만은 예외였는데, 마리안은 먹이는 일을 하기에는 손이 너무 굼뜨다는 그로비의 반대에도 불구하고 이따금 반 시간씩 그렇게 해 주었다.\n\n아마도 어떤 경제적인 이유에서인지, 이 특정한 일에는 으레 여자가 뽑혔는데, 그로비가 테스를 고른 동기로 댄 것은, 그녀가 낟단을 푸는 데 힘과 빠르기를, 그리고 그 둘에 더해 지구력까지 가장 잘 겸비한 사람 중 하나라는 것이었고, 이는 사실이었는지도 모른다. 말소리를 막아 버리는 타작기의 윙윙거림은, 곡식 공급이 정해진 양에 못 미칠 때마다 미친 듯한 포효로 커졌다. 테스와 먹이는 남자는 결코 고개를 돌릴 수 없었던 터라, 저녁 식사 시간 바로 직전에 한 사람이 문으로 소리 없이 밭에 들어와 두 번째 낟가리 아래 서서 그 광경을, 특히 테스를 지켜보고 있었다는 것을 그녀는 알지 못했다. 그는 유행하는 무늬의 트위드 양복을 입었고, 화려한 지팡이를 빙빙 돌리고 있었다.\n\n\"저 사람 누구야?\" 이즈 휴엇이 마리안에게 말했다. 그녀는 처음에는 그 물음을 테스에게 던졌으나, 테스는 그것을 들을 수 없었다.\n\n\"누구 정부겠지, 뭐.\" 마리안이 짤막하게 말했다.\n\n\"테스를 노리는 거라면 일 기니를 걸겠어.\"\n\n\"오 아니야. 요새 테스 꽁무니를 졸졸 따라다니던 그 떠벌이 목사잖아. 이런 멋쟁이가 아니라.\"\n\n\"글쎄—저 사람이 그 사람이야.\"\n\n\"설교하던 그 사람이라고? 하지만 전혀 딴판인걸!\"\n\n\"검은 외투랑 흰 목도리를 벗어 던지고, 구레나룻도 잘라 냈지만, 그래도 저 사람이 그 사람 맞아.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 생각해? 그럼 내가 테스한테 일러 줘야지.\" 마리안이 말했다.\n\n\"하지 마. 곧 알아서 보게 될 텐데 뭐.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 설교를 하면서 한편으로 유부녀한테 구애를 하다니, 그게 도무지 옳은 일 같지가 않아. 비록 그 남편이 외국에 가 있고, 어떤 의미로는 그녀가 과부 신세나 마찬가지라 해도 말이야.\"\n\n\"오—그가 그녀를 어쩌지는 못해.\" 이즈가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"수렁에 빠진 짐마차를 그 구덩이에서 끄집어낼 수 없는 것처럼, 그녀의 마음은 그것이 자리 잡은 그 한 곳에서 들어 올려질 수가 없어. 어머나 세상에, 구애도, 설교도, 일곱 천둥 그 자체도, 여자가 마음을 떼는 게 차라리 나을 그런 때에 그녀를 떼어 놓지는 못해.\"\n\n저녁 식사 시간이 되어 윙윙거림이 멎었다. 그러자 테스는 제 자리를 떠났는데, 기계의 진동으로 무릎이 어찌나 비참하게 떨리던지 거의 걸을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"나처럼 술을 한 쿼트쯤 들이켰어야지.\" 마리안이 말했다. \"그랬으면 그렇게 핼쑥해 보이진 않을 텐데. 어쩜, 맙소사, 네 얼굴이 마녀한테 짓눌리기라도 한 것 같구나!\"\n\n마음씨 착한 마리안은, 테스가 이렇게 지친 마당에 자기를 찾아온 방문객의 존재를 알아채면 입맛이 달아나는 나쁜 영향을 받을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤다. 그래서 마리안이 낟가리 저편의 사다리로 테스를 내려가게 할까 궁리하고 있을 때, 그 신사가 앞으로 나와 위를 올려다보았다.\n\n테스가 짧게 \"오!\" 하고 소리를 냈다. 그리고 잠시 후 재빨리 말했다. \"나는 여기서 점심을 먹을래—바로 이 낟가리 위에서.\"\n\n이따금 그들은 오두막에서 너무 멀리 떨어져 있을 때면 다들 이렇게 하곤 했다. 그러나 오늘은 제법 매서운 바람이 불고 있던 터라, 마리안과 나머지 사람들은 내려가서 짚가리 밑에 앉았다.\n\n새로 온 사람은 정말로 알렉 더버빌, 곧 한때의 복음 전도자였다. 바뀐 옷차림과 외양에도 불구하고 말이다. 한눈에 보아도 원래의 그 세속적 욕망이 되돌아왔음이 분명했다. 그는 서너 살 더 먹은 남자가 할 수 있는 한 그대로, 테스가 그 흠모자이자 이른바 사촌이라는 자를 처음 알았을 때의 그 옛 멋스럽고 거침없는 모습으로 자신을 되돌려 놓았던 것이다. 자기가 있던 자리에 그대로 있기로 정한 테스는, 땅에서 보이지 않게 낟단들 사이에 앉아 식사를 시작했다. 그러다 이윽고 사다리 위로 발소리가 들렸고, 곧이어 알렉이 낟가리 위에—이제 낟단들로 이루어진 길쭉하고 평평한 발판이 된 그곳에 나타났다. 그는 그것들을 성큼성큼 가로질러, 한마디 말도 없이 그녀 맞은편에 앉았다.\n\n테스는 자기가 가져온 두툼한 팬케이크 한 조각, 그 변변찮은 점심을 계속 먹었다. 다른 일꾼들은 이때쯤 모두 낟가리 밑에 모여 있었는데, 거기서는 흐트러진 짚이 아늑한 은신처를 이루고 있었다.\n\n\"보다시피 나 또 왔소.\" 더버빌이 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌자고 나를 이렇게 괴롭히는 거예요!\" 그녀가 외쳤고, 바로 그 손끝에서 비난이 번뜩였다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 괴롭힌다고? 도리어 내가 물어야 할 것 같소, 당신은 어쩌자고 나를 괴롭히는 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 언제 당신을 조금이라도 괴롭혔다고요!\"\n\n\"안 그런다고? 하지만 괴롭히고 있소! 당신은 나를 따라다니며 사로잡소. 방금 그렇게 쓰라린 번뜩임으로 나를 향해 돌렸던 바로 그 눈, 그것이 그때 당신이 보여 준 그대로, 밤에도 낮에도 내게 찾아온단 말이오! 테스, 당신이 우리의 그 아이 이야기를 내게 한 이후로, 마치 청교도적인 강한 물줄기로 흐르던 내 감정이 별안간 당신 쪽으로 트인 길을 찾아내어 단숨에 콸콸 쏟아져 버린 것만 같소. 종교의 물길은 그 즉시 메말라 버렸고, 그렇게 만든 건 바로 당신이오!\"\n\n그녀는 말없이 응시했다.\n\n\"뭐예요—설교를 아주 그만뒀단 말이에요?\" 그녀가 물었다. 그녀는 에인절에게서 현대 사상의 회의를 충분히 받아들인 터라 들뜬 열광을 멸시했지만, 한 여자로서 다소 섬뜩하기도 했다.\n\n더버빌이 짐짓 엄숙한 척 말을 이었다—\n\n\"아주 그만뒀소. 캐스터브리지 장에서 술꾼들에게 설교하기로 되어 있던 그날 오후 이후로 모든 약속을 깨 버렸소. 형제들이 나를 어떻게 생각하는지는 귀신이나 알겠지. 아하! 형제들이라! 틀림없이 그들은 나를 위해 기도하고—나를 위해 울 거요. 그들은 나름대로 친절한 사람들이니까. 하지만 내가 무슨 상관이오? 그것에 대한 믿음을 잃어버린 마당에 어떻게 그 일을 계속할 수 있겠소?—그건 가장 천한 종류의 위선이었을 거요! 그들 틈에서 나는 후메내오와 알렉산더처럼 서 있었을 테지. 신성모독하지 않는 법을 배우도록 사탄에게 넘겨진 그 자들 말이오. 당신은 참으로 멋진 복수를 한 거요! 나는 순결한 당신을 보았고, 당신을 속였소. 사 년 뒤, 당신은 나를 기독교 광신자로 만나, 그러고는 나를 움직여서, 어쩌면 나를 완전한 멸망으로 몰아넣었지! 하지만 테스, 내가 부르곤 하던 대로 내 사촌, 이건 그저 내 말버릇일 뿐이니, 그렇게 끔찍하게 걱정스러운 표정은 짓지 마오. 물론 당신은 그 예쁜 얼굴과 맵시 있는 몸매를 그대로 지니고 있은 것 말고는 아무것도 한 게 없소. 나는 당신이 나를 보기 전에 낟가리 위에 있는 당신을 보았소—그 꼭 끼는 앞치마 같은 것이 그 몸매를 돋보이게 하고, 그 날개 달린 듯한 보닛도 그렇고—당신네 들판 처녀들은 위험에서 벗어나 있고 싶거든 결코 그런 보닛을 써서는 안 되오.\" 그는 몇 순간 말없이 그녀를 바라보다가, 짧고 냉소적인 웃음과 함께 다시 입을 열었다. \"내가 그 대리인이라고 여겼던 그 독신의 사도조차도, 이런 예쁜 얼굴에 유혹당했더라면, 지금의 나처럼 그녀를 위해 쟁기를 놓아 버렸을 거라고 나는 믿소!\"\n\n테스가 항변하려 했으나, 바로 이 대목에서 그녀의 모든 유창함이 사라져 버렸고, 그는 아랑곳없이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"뭐, 당신이 베푸는 이 낙원도 따지고 보면 어쩌면 다른 어떤 것 못지않게 좋은 거겠지. 하지만 진지하게 말해서, 테스.\" 더버빌이 일어나 더 가까이 다가오더니, 낟단들 사이에 옆으로 비스듬히 기대 팔꿈치를 괴었다. \"지난번에 당신을 본 이후로, 나는 당신이 그가 한 말이라며 들려준 그 말을 곰곰이 생각해 봤소. 나는 이런 결론에 이르렀소. 이 진부하고 케케묵은 명제들에는 아무래도 상식이 좀 모자라는 것 같다고 말이오. 내가 어떻게 그 가엾은 클레어 목사의 열정에 그토록 불붙어서, 심지어 그분을 능가할 만큼 그렇게 미친 듯이 일에 매달릴 수 있었는지, 나는 도무지 알 수가 없소! 그리고 지난번에 당신이, 당신의 그 놀라운 남편의—이름은 한 번도 말해 주지 않은 그 남편의—지성을 근거 삼아 한 말, 아무 교리 없이도 이른바 윤리 체계라는 걸 가질 수 있다는 그 말에 대해서는, 나로서는 도무지 그 길이 보이질 않소.\"\n\n\"아니, 적어도 사랑과 친절과 순결의 종교는 가질 수 있잖아요. 그—뭐라고 부르셨죠—교리는 못 가진다 해도요.\"\n\n\"오 아니! 나는 그런 부류와는 다른 사람이오! 누군가가 '이걸 해라, 그러면 네가 죽은 뒤에 좋은 일이 있을 것이다. 저걸 해라, 그러면 네게 나쁜 일이 있을 것이다' 하고 말해 주는 이가 없다면, 나는 좀처럼 달아오르질 못하오. 빌어먹을, 책임을 물을 상대가 없는데 내 행실과 정념에 대해 책임을 느낄 마음은 없소. 그리고 내가 당신이라면, 내 사랑, 나도 그러지 않겠소!\"\n\n그녀는 그의 둔한 머릿속에서 그가 두 가지를, 곧 신학과 도덕을 뒤섞었다고, 그것들은 인류의 원시 시대에는 전혀 별개의 것이었다고 따져 말하려 애썼다. 그러나 에인절 클레어의 과묵함 탓에, 그녀 자신이 받은 교육이 전무한 탓에, 그리고 그녀가 이성보다는 감정의 그릇인 탓에, 그녀는 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n\"뭐, 괜찮소.\" 그가 다시 말했다. \"내가 여기 있잖소, 내 사랑, 옛 시절처럼 말이오!\"\n\n\"그때처럼은 아니에요—결코 그때처럼은 아니에요—이건 달라요!\" 그녀가 간청했다. \"그리고 나한테는 한 번도 온기 같은 건 없었어요! 오, 신앙을 잃은 게 당신을 나한테 이렇게 말하게 만든 거라면, 어째서 그 신앙을 지키지 않았어요!\"\n\n\"당신이 그걸 내게서 두들겨 빼 버렸으니까. 그러니 그 죄악이 당신의 그 예쁜 머리 위에 떨어지기를! 당신 남편은 자기 가르침이 어떻게 자기에게 되돌아와 부딪칠지 꿈에도 몰랐겠지! 하하—그래도 당신이 나를 배교자로 만들어 준 게 나는 끔찍이도 기쁘오! 테스, 나는 그 어느 때보다 당신에게 마음을 빼앗겼고, 또 당신이 가엾기도 하오. 당신이 아무리 입을 다물어도, 나는 당신이 곤경에 처해 있는 걸 알겠소—마땅히 당신을 아껴 줘야 할 자에게서 버림받은 채로 말이오.\"\n\n그녀는 음식 부스러기를 목구멍으로 넘길 수가 없었다. 입술이 바싹 말랐고, 숨이 막힐 지경이었다. 낟가리 밑에서 먹고 마시는 일꾼들의 목소리와 웃음소리가 마치 사분의 일 마일이나 떨어진 데서 들려오는 것처럼 그녀에게 닿았다.\n\n\"이건 나한테 잔인한 짓이에요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 나를 조금이라도 위한다면, 어떻게—어떻게 나한테 이런 이야기를 늘어놓을 수가 있어요?\"\n\n\"옳아, 옳은 말이오.\" 그가 조금 움찔하며 말했다. \"내 행실 때문에 당신을 나무라려고 온 게 아니오. 테스, 나는 당신이 이렇게 일하는 게 싫다는 걸 말하러 온 거요. 일부러 당신을 위해 온 거란 말이오. 당신은 내가 아닌 남편이 있다고 하지. 그래, 어쩌면 있겠지. 하지만 나는 그를 본 적이 없고, 당신은 그의 이름조차 말해 주지 않았소. 통틀어 보면 그는 아무래도 신화 속 인물 같단 말이오. 하지만 설령 당신에게 남편이 있다 해도, 나는 그보다 내가 당신에게 더 가깝다고 생각하오. 적어도 나는 당신을 곤경에서 구하려고 애쓰는데, 그는 그러지 않으니, 그 보이지도 않는 얼굴에 복이나 있기를! 내가 읽곤 하던 준엄한 예언자 호세아의 말이 떠오르는구려. 모르오, 테스?—'그가 그 연인들을 따라갈지라도 미치지 못하며, 그들을 찾을지라도 만나지 못하리니, 그제야 그가 이르기를, 내가 가서 내 본 남편에게로 돌아가리라, 그때의 내 형편이 지금보다 나았음이라!'… 테스, 내 마차가 바로 저 언덕 아래에서 기다리고 있고, 그리고—내 사랑이여, 그의 사랑이 아니라!—나머지는 당신도 알 거요.\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안 그녀의 얼굴은 둔탁한 진홍빛 불길로 달아올랐다. 그러나 그녀는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"당신은 내 타락의 원인이었소.\" 그가 팔을 그녀의 허리 쪽으로 뻗으며 말을 이었다. \"그러니 당신은 기꺼이 그 타락을 함께 나눠야 하고, 당신이 남편이라 부르는 그 노새 같은 자를 영영 떠나야 하오.\"\n\n스키머케이크를 먹으려고 벗어 두었던 그녀의 가죽 장갑 한 짝이 그녀의 무릎 위에 놓여 있었는데, 그녀는 아무런 예고도 없이 그 장갑의 손목 부분을 잡고 격하게 휘둘러 곧장 그의 얼굴에 내리쳤다. 그것은 전사의 장갑처럼 무겁고 두꺼웠고, 그의 입을 정통으로 후려쳤다. 상상력을 발휘한다면, 그 행동을 무장한 그녀의 조상들이 적잖이 익혔던 어떤 솜씨가 되살아난 것으로 볼 수도 있었을 것이다. 알렉이 기대 누운 자세에서 사납게 벌떡 일어났다. 그녀의 가격이 떨어진 자리에 진홍빛 핏방울이 배어 나왔고, 이내 피가 그의 입에서 짚 위로 떨어지기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 곧 자신을 다잡고, 차분히 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 피 흐르는 입술을 닦았다.\n\n그녀 역시 벌떡 일어섰으나, 다시 주저앉았다. \"자, 나를 벌하세요!\" 그녀가, 잡아 죽이려는 자에게 목이 비틀리기 직전 참새가 던지는 그 절망적인 반항의 눈길로, 그를 향해 눈을 치켜뜨며 말했다. \"나를 채찍질하든 짓뭉개든 하세요. 낟가리 밑에 있는 저 사람들 따위는 신경 쓸 것 없어요! 나는 비명도 지르지 않을 테니까. 한번 희생물이면 영영 희생물—그게 법칙이니까!\"\n\n\"오 아니, 아니오, 테스.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다. \"나는 이건 충분히 너그러이 봐줄 수 있소. 하지만 당신은 한 가지를 너무도 부당하게 잊고 있구려. 당신이 내가 그럴 수 있는 길을 막아 버리지만 않았더라면 나는 당신과 결혼했을 거라는 사실 말이오. 내가 대놓고 내 아내가 되어 달라고 청하지 않았소—응? 대답해 보오.\"\n\n\"청했죠.\"\n\n\"그런데 당신은 그럴 수 없다. 하지만 한 가지는 기억해 두오!\" 그가 그녀에게 청혼하던 자기 진심과 그녀의 지금의 배은망덕을 떠올리며 노여움이 그를 사로잡자 목소리가 굳어졌고, 그는 그녀 곁으로 성큼 다가가 어깨를 붙들었으니, 그의 손아귀 아래 그녀가 부르르 떨렸다. \"기억해 두오, 나의 마님, 한때 나는 당신의 주인이었소! 나는 다시 당신의 주인이 될 거요. 당신이 어떤 남자의 아내라면, 당신은 내 것이오!\"\n\n이제 아래에서 타작꾼들이 움직이기 시작했다.\n\n\"우리 다툼은 이쯤 해 두고.\" 그가 그녀를 놓아주며 말했다. \"이제 나는 당신을 떠나겠소. 그리고 오후 중에 당신 대답을 들으러 다시 오겠소. 당신은 아직 나를 몰라! 하지만 나는 당신을 알지.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 입을 열지 않은 채, 넋이 나간 듯 그대로 있었다. 더버빌은 낟단들을 가로질러 물러나 사다리를 내려갔고, 그동안 아래의 일꾼들은 일어나 팔을 쭉 뻗으며 마신 맥주를 흔들어 내렸다. 그러고서 타작기가 다시 돌기 시작했다. 그리고 새로이 바스락거리는 짚 소리 한가운데서 테스는 꿈속에 잠긴 사람처럼 윙윙거리는 통 곁 제 자리로 돌아가, 낟단을 하나하나 끝없이 이어 풀었다.\n\n# XLVIII\n\nXLVIII\n오후에 농장주는 그날 밤 안에 낟가리를 끝내야 한다고 알렸으니, 일하는 것을 볼 수 있을 만한 달이 떠 있는 데다, 기관을 다루는 남자가 이튿날 다른 농장에 고용되어 있었기 때문이다. 그리하여 윙윙거리고 웅웅거리고 바스락거리는 소리가 평소보다도 더 끊임없이 이어졌다.\n\n테스가 눈을 들어 잠시 둘레를 둘러본 것은 \"나밋\" 시간, 곧 세 시쯤이 되어서였다. 알렉 더버빌이 돌아와 문 옆 산울타리 아래 서 있는 것을 보고도 그녀는 거의 놀라지 않았다. 그는 그녀가 눈을 드는 것을 보고는, 정중하게 손을 흔들며 그녀에게 입맞춤을 날렸다. 그것은 둘의 다툼이 끝났다는 뜻이었다. 테스는 다시 눈을 내리깔고, 조심스럽게\n안 보려고 그 방향으로는 눈길을 주지 않았다.\n\n이렇게 오후는 더디게 흘러갔다. 밀 더미는 점점 낮아졌고, 짚 더미는 점점 높아졌으며, 곡식 자루들은 수레에 실려 옮겨졌다. 여섯 시에 밀 더미는 땅에서 어깨 높이쯤으로 내려앉았다. 그러나 손도 대지 않은 채 남은 타작 전의 밀단들은, 게걸스러운 삼키는 기계가 그토록 엄청난 양을 꿀꺽 삼켰는데도, 여전히 셀 수 없이 많아 보였다. 그 기계에 먹이를 댄 것은 그 남자와 테스였고, 대부분은 테스의 두 젊은 손을 거쳐 들어갔다. 그리고 아침에는 아무것도 없던 자리에 솟아오른 거대한 짚 더미는, 윙윙대는 그 붉은 탐식가가 싸 놓은 배설물처럼 보였다. 서쪽 하늘에서는 노한 듯한 빛살이—거친 삼월이 일몰로 내줄 수 있는 전부였다—구름 끼었던 하루 끝에 터져 나와, 타작꾼들의 지치고 끈적이는 얼굴 위로 쏟아져 그것을 구릿빛으로 물들였고, 여자들의 펄럭이는 옷자락도 마찬가지로 물들여 흐릿한 불꽃처럼 몸에 달라붙게 했다.\n\n헐떡이는 통증이 더미 전체로 번졌다. 기계에 먹이를 대던 남자는 지쳐 있었고, 테스는 그의 붉은 목덜미가 흙먼지와 겉껍질로 더께져 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 테스는 여전히 제자리를 지키고 섰고, 상기되어 땀에 젖은 얼굴은 곡식 먼지를 뒤집어썼으며, 흰 보닛은 그 먼지로 거뭇하게 물들었다. 기계 위에 자리를 잡고 그 회전에 온몸을 흔들리는 여자는 테스뿐이었고, 더미가 낮아진 탓에 이제 메리언과 이즈와는 떨어져, 전처럼 임무를 서로 바꿔 줄 수도 없게 되었다. 온몸의 섬유 하나하나까지 동참하는 끊임없는 떨림은 그녀를 멍한 공상 속으로 몰아넣었고, 그 안에서 두 팔은 의식과 따로 노는 듯 움직였다. 그녀는 자기가 어디 있는지조차 거의 알지 못했고, 아래에서 이즈 휴엣이 머리가 흘러내린다고 일러 주는 소리도 듣지 못했다.\n\n점점 그들 가운데 가장 생생하던 이들조차 송장처럼 핼쑥해지고 눈이 움푹 패기 시작했다. 테스가 고개를 들 때마다 보이는 것은 언제나 크게 솟아오른 짚 더미였고, 그 위에 셔츠 바람의 남자들이 잿빛 북쪽 하늘을 등지고 서 있었다. 그 앞에는 야곱의 사다리 같은 길고 붉은 컨베이어가 놓여, 타작된 짚이 끊임없이 흐르며 올라갔다. 언덕을 거꾸로 거슬러 오르는 노란 강이 더미 꼭대기에서 뿜어져 나왔다. 테스는 알렉 더버빌이 여전히 그 자리에 있어 어디선가 자기를 지켜보고 있음을 알았다. 다만 어디인지는 말할 수 없었다. 그가 머물 핑계는 있었다. 타작이 끝나가며 마지막 밀단들에 가까워지면 늘 쥐잡이가 조금 벌어졌고, 타작과는 상관없는 사람들이 그 구경을 하러 가끔 끼어들었기 때문이다. 온갖 부류의 놀이꾼들, 테리어와 익살스러운 파이프를 든 신사들, 막대기와 돌멩이를 든 거친 사내들이었다.\n\n그러나 더미 바닥에 살아 있는 쥐 층에 닿으려면 한 시간은 더 일해야 했다. 그리고 애벗스서널 옆 자이언츠힐 쪽의 저녁 빛이 스러져 가자, 반대편 미들턴 수도원과 쇼츠퍼드 쪽에 누운 지평선에서 그 계절의 하얀 얼굴을 한 달이 떠올랐다. 지난 한두 시간 동안 메리언은 테스가 마음에 걸렸으나, 가까이 다가가 말을 건넬 수가 없었다. 다른 여자들은 에일을 마셔 기운을 지탱했지만, 테스는 어린 시절 집안에서 본 그 결과 탓에 대대로 내려온 두려움 때문에 마시지 않고 버텼던 것이다. 그래도 테스는 여전히 일을 계속했다. 제 몫을 채우지 못하면 자리를 떠나야 했으니까. 한두 달 전이었다면 그 가능성을 담담하게, 아니 오히려 안도하며 바라봤겠지만, 더버빌이 그녀 주위를 맴돌기 시작한 뒤로는 그것이 공포가 되어 버렸다.\n\n밀단을 던져 올리는 이들과 먹이를 대는 이들이 이제 더미를 어찌나 낮춰 놓았던지, 땅에 선 사람들이 그들과 말을 주고받을 수 있게 되었다. 테스가 놀랍게도 농장주 그로비가 기계 위로 올라와 그녀에게 다가오더니, 친구와 합류하고 싶다면 더는 일을 계속할 필요가 없으며, 그녀 자리를 대신할 다른 사람을 보내 주겠다고 말했다. 그 \"친구\"란 더버빌임을 그녀는 알았고, 또 이 양보가 그 친구—아니 적—의 청에 따라 베풀어진 것임도 알았다. 그녀는 고개를 저으며 계속 고되게 일했다.\n\n마침내 쥐잡이 시간이 찾아왔고, 사냥이 시작됐다. 그 짐승들은 더미가 가라앉음에 따라 차차 아래로 기어 내려와 바닥에 다 모여 있다가, 이제 마지막 피난처가 걷히자 사방으로 빈 땅을 가로질러 내달렸다. 이쯤에는 반쯤 거나하게 취한 메리언이 날카로운 비명을 질러 동료들에게 쥐 한 마리가 제 몸으로 침입했음을 알렸다—나머지 여자들은 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고 몸을 들어 올리는 갖가지 방책으로 그 공포를 막아 두었던 터였다. 마침내 쥐가 떨려 나왔고, 개들이 짖고 사내들이 고함치고 여자들이 비명 지르고 욕설과 발구르는 소리와 아수라장 같은 아비규환 속에서, 테스는 마지막 밀단을 풀었다. 드럼이 느려지고 윙윙거림이 멎자, 그녀는 기계에서 내려와 땅을 디뎠다.\n\n쥐잡이를 구경만 하고 있던 그녀의 연인이 곧장 그녀 곁에 와 있었다.\n\n\"뭐예요—그래도—내가 모욕적으로 따귀까지 때렸는데도요!\" 그녀가 숨죽인 목소리로 말했다. 너무도 기진맥진해서 더 큰 소리로 말할 기운이 없었다.\n\n\"네가 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 거기에 기분 상한다면, 나야말로 정말 어리석은 거지.\" 그가 트랜트리지 시절의 그 유혹하는 목소리로 답했다. \"이 작은 팔다리가 어찌나 떨리는지! 너는 피 뽑힌 송아지처럼 약해졌어, 너도 알잖니. 게다가 내가 온 뒤로는 아무것도 안 해도 됐는데. 어쩜 그렇게 고집을 부려? 어쨌든 나는 농장주한테 증기 타작에 여자를 쓸 권리가 없다고 말해 뒀어. 여자한테 맞는 일이 아니야. 좀 나은 농장에서는 다 그만뒀다고, 그자도 잘 알면서. 너희 집까지 같이 걸어갈게.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요.\" 그녀가 지친 걸음으로 답했다. \"그러고 싶으면 같이 걸어요! 당신이 내 처지를 알기 전에 나와 결혼하러 왔던 건 잊지 않고 있어요. 어쩌면—어쩌면 당신은 내가 생각했던 것보다 조금은 더 낫고 친절한 사람인지도 몰라요. 친절로 한 일이라면 무엇이든 고맙게 받을게요. 다른 뜻으로 한 일이라면 무엇이든 화가 나요. 가끔은 당신 속뜻을 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"우리의 옛 관계를 정당하게 만들 수 없다면, 적어도 너를 도와줄 수는 있어. 그리고 예전에 보였던 것보다 훨씬 더 네 마음을 헤아리면서 그렇게 할 거야. 내 종교적 광기인지 뭔지는 이제 끝났어. 하지만 약간의 선한 마음은 남아 있지. 그러길 바라. 자, 테스, 남자와 여자 사이의 모든 다정하고 굳은 것을 걸고 말하건대, 나를 믿어! 나는 너를 걱정에서 벗어나게 해 줄 만큼, 아니 그 이상으로 가진 게 있어. 너 자신을 위해서도, 네 부모와 동생들을 위해서도. 네가 나를 믿어 주기만 한다면 그들 모두를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있어.\"\n\n\"요즘 그 애들을 봤어요?\" 그녀가 재빨리 물었다.\n\n\"응. 그들은 네가 어디 있는지 몰랐어. 내가 여기서 너를 찾은 건 순전히 우연이었지.\"\n\n차가운 달이 정원 울타리 잔가지 사이로 테스의 지친 얼굴을 비스듬히 비추었다. 그녀가 임시 거처인 오두막 밖에 멈춰 서자, 더버빌도 그 곁에 멈췄다.\n\n\"내 어린 동생들 얘긴 꺼내지 마요—나를 아주 무너지게 하지 마요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 애들을 돕고 싶다면—하느님은 그 애들이 도움이 필요한 걸 아시죠—나한테 말하지 말고 그냥 해 줘요. 아니, 아니에요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"그 애들을 위해서든 나를 위해서든, 당신한테서는 아무것도 받지 않을 거예요!\"\n\n그는 더는 그녀를 따라가지 않았다. 그녀가 한 집안 식구와 함께 사는 처지라, 집 안에서는 모든 게 다 드러나기 때문이었다. 그녀는 안으로 들어서서 빨래 통에 몸을 씻고 식구들과 저녁을 나눠 먹자마자 생각에 잠겼고, 벽 밑 탁자로 물러나 자기 작은 등불 빛에 의지해 격정에 휩싸인 마음으로 글을 써 내려갔다—\n\n나의 남편에게,\n\n당신을 이렇게 부르게 해 주세요—그래야만 해요—나 같은 보잘것없는 아내를 떠올리는 게 당신을 화나게 한다 해도요. 곤경에 빠진 나는 당신에게 부르짖을 수밖에 없어요—내겐 다른 사람이 없으니까요! 나는 너무도 유혹에 노출되어 있어요, 에인절. 그게 누구인지 말하기가 두렵고, 그것에 대해 글로 쓰는 것조차 내키지 않아요. 그래도 나는 당신이 상상도 못 할 만큼 당신에게 매달리고 있어요! 무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지기 전에, 지금 당장 나에게 와 줄 수는 없나요? 아, 당신이 그럴 수 없다는 걸 알아요, 그토록 멀리 있으니까요! 당신이 곧 오지 않거나 당신에게 오라고 말해 주지 않으면 나는 죽을 것만 같아요. 당신이 내게 내린 벌은 마땅한 거예요—나도 알아요—충분히 마땅하죠—나에게 화내는 당신은 옳고 정당해요. 하지만, 에인절, 제발, 제발 정당하기만 하지는 말아요—내가 그럴 자격이 없더라도, 그저 조금만 나에게 다정해 주고, 나에게 와 줘요! 당신이 와 준다면, 나는 당신 품에서 죽어도 좋아요! 당신이 나를 용서해 준 거라면, 그렇게 하는 것만으로 나는 흡족할 거예요! 에인절, 나는 오로지 당신만을 위해 살아요. 나는 당신을 너무도 사랑해서 당신이 떠난 걸 탓할 수도 없고, 당신이 농장을 찾아야 했던 게 어쩔 수 없는 일이었음을 알아요. 내가 따끔하거나 쓰디쓴 말을 한마디라도 하리라고는 생각하지 말아요. 그저 나에게 돌아와만 줘요. 당신이 없으니 나는 외로워요, 내 사랑, 아, 너무도 외로워요! 일을 해야 하는 건 괜찮아요. 하지만 당신이 짤막한 한 줄이라도 보내 \"곧 가겠소\"라고 말해 준다면, 나는 견디며 버틸 거예요, 에인절—아, 너무도 기쁘게요!\n\n우리가 결혼한 뒤로 줄곧, 생각으로도 눈길로도 당신에게 충실한 것이 내게는 그토록 깊은 신앙이 되어서, 누군가 나도 모르는 새에 나에게 칭찬 한마디를 건네도 그것이 당신에게 죄짓는 것처럼 느껴져요. 우리가 낙농장에 있을 때 느꼈던 그 마음을 당신은 조금도 느낀 적이 없나요? 만약 느낀 적이 있다면, 어떻게 나에게서 멀리 떨어져 있을 수 있나요? 나는 당신이 사랑에 빠졌던 바로 그 여자예요, 에인절. 그래요, 바로 그 여자예요!—당신이 싫어한, 하지만 본 적도 없는 그 여자가 아니에요. 당신을 만난 순간 과거가 내게 무슨 의미였겠어요? 그건 완전히 죽은 것이었어요. 나는 당신에게서 새 생명을 가득 채워 받은 다른 여자가 되었어요. 내가 어떻게 그 이전의 여자일 수 있겠어요? 왜 당신은 이걸 보지 못하나요? 여보, 당신이 조금만 더 자만해서, 내 안에 이런 변화를 일으킬 만큼 당신이 강했다는 걸 믿을 정도로 자신을 믿어 준다면, 어쩌면 당신은 나에게, 당신의 가엾은 아내에게 올 마음이 들지도 몰라요.\n\n당신이 언제까지나 나를 사랑하리라 믿을 수 있다고 여겼던 그 행복 속에서 나는 얼마나 어리석었던가요! 그런 것이 가엾은 나에게는 허락되지 않는다는 걸 진작 알았어야 했어요. 하지만 나는 마음이 아파요, 옛 시절 때문만이 아니라 지금 이 순간 때문에요. 생각해 봐요—당신을 영영, 영영 볼 수 없다는 게 내 마음을 얼마나 아프게 하는지! 아, 내가 매일같이, 하루 종일 느끼는 이 아픔을 단 일 분만이라도 당신의 다정한 마음에 매일 한 번씩 느끼게 할 수 있다면, 그것이 당신을 가엾고 외로운 당신의 사람에게 연민을 베풀게 할지도 모르는데.\n\n사람들은 아직도 내가 제법 예쁘다고들 해요, 에인절(솔직하고 싶으니 말하자면, 그들이 쓰는 말은 '아름답다'예요). 어쩌면 나는 그들이 말하는 그런 사람인지도 몰라요. 하지만 나는 내 고운 외모를 값지게 여기지 않아요. 그것이 당신의 것이기에 갖고 싶을 뿐이에요, 내 사랑. 그래서 적어도 나에게 당신이 가질 만한 한 가지는 있게 하려고요. 이걸 어찌나 절실히 느꼈는지, 그것 때문에 성가신 일을 겪었을 때, 사람들이 믿어 주는 한 오래도록 내 얼굴을 붕대로 동여매고 다녔어요. 아, 에인절, 내가 이 모든 걸 말하는 건 허영심에서가 아니에요—당신은 분명 내가 그렇지 않다는 걸 알 거예요—오직 당신이 나에게 와 주게 하려는 거예요!\n\n당신이 정말로 나에게 올 수 없다면, 내가 당신에게 가도 될까요? 나는, 말했듯이, 괴로워요, 하지 않으려는 일을 하라고 떠밀리고 있어요. 내가 한 치라도 굴복할 리는 없지만, 그래도 어떤 사고가 어떤 일로 이어질지 두려워요. 내 첫 번째 잘못 때문에 나는 이토록 무방비하니까요. 이것에 대해서는 더 말할 수가 없어요—너무 비참해지니까요. 하지만 내가 어떤 무서운 덫에 빠져 무너진다면, 나의 마지막 처지는 첫 번째 처지보다 더 나빠질 거예요. 아, 하느님, 그건 생각조차 할 수 없어요! 당장 가게 해 주든가, 당장 나에게 와 줘요!\n\n당신의 아내로는 안 된다면, 당신의 하인으로라도 당신과 함께 사는 것을, 나는 기꺼이, 아니 기쁘게 받아들일 거예요. 그저 당신 곁에 있을 수만 있다면, 당신을 잠깐씩이라도 볼 수만 있다면, 당신을 내 것이라고 여길 수만 있다면요.\n\n당신이 여기 없으니 햇빛도 나에게 보여 줄 게 아무것도 없고, 들판의 까마귀와 찌르레기를 보는 것도 싫어요. 예전에 그것들을 나와 함께 보던 당신이 그리워 슬프고 또 슬퍼지니까요. 하늘에서든 땅에서든 땅 밑에서든 내가 바라는 건 단 하나, 당신을 만나는 것뿐이에요, 내 사랑! 나에게 와 줘요—나에게 와서, 나를 위협하는 것에서 나를 구해 줘요!—\n\n당신의 충실하고 가슴 찢어진\n테스\n\n# XLIX\n\n서쪽에 있는 그 조용한 사제관의 아침 식탁으로 그 호소는 마땅히 흘러 들어갔다. 그 골짜기는 공기가 어찌나 부드럽고 흙이 어찌나 기름진지, 자라나는 데 드는 수고가 플린트콤애시의 경작에 비하면 겉핥기식 손길만으로도 충분했고, 테스에게는 인간 세상이 그토록 달라 보였던 곳이었다(실은 거의 다를 게 없었지만). 순전히 안전을 위해, 에인절은 테스에게 자신의 아버지를 통해 연락을 전하라고 부탁해 두었다. 무거운 마음으로 개척하러 떠난 그 나라에서 바뀌는 거처들을 그는 아버지에게 곧잘 알려 두고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"자,\" 늙은 클레어 씨가 봉투를 읽고 나서 아내에게 말했다. \"에인절이 우리에게 그러길 바란다고 했던 대로 다음 달 말에 리우를 떠나 집에 들르려 한다면, 이게 그 애 계획을 앞당길지도 모르겠소. 이건 그 애 아내한테서 온 것 같으니 말이오.\" 그는 그녀를 생각하며 깊이 숨을 들이쉬었다. 그리고 편지는 곧장 에인절에게 보내지도록 주소가 다시 적혔다.\n\n\"가엾은 애, 무사히 집에 돌아오길 바라요.\" 클레어 부인이 중얼거렸다. \"죽는 날까지 나는 그 애가 부당한 대우를 받았다고 느낄 거예요. 그 애가 믿음이 없었어도 케임브리지에 보냈어야 했어요, 다른 아이들이 누린 것과 똑같은 기회를 주었어야 했다고요. 제대로 된 영향을 받았으면 그 애도 거기서 벗어났을 거고, 어쩌면 결국 성직을 받았을지도 몰라요. 교회든 교회가 아니든, 그 애한테는 그게 더 공평했을 거예요.\"\n\n이것이 클레어 부인이 두 아들과 관련해 남편의 평온을 흩뜨린 유일한 비탄이었다. 그리고 그녀는 이런 말을 자주 토해 내지도 않았다. 그녀는 독실한 만큼 사려 깊었고, 이 문제에서 자신의 처사가 과연 정당했는가를 두고 남편의 마음 또한 의혹으로 괴로워하고 있음을 알았기 때문이다. 그녀는 그가 밤에 잠 못 이루며 에인절을 향한 한숨을 기도로 억누르는 소리를 너무도 자주 들었다. 그러나 타협을 모르는 그 복음주의자는, 지금까지도, 불신자인 아들에게 다른 두 아들에게 준 것과 똑같은 학문적 혜택을 주는 것이 정당했으리라고는 여기지 않았다. 바로 그 혜택이, 비록 개연성은 없을지언정 가능성으로는, 자신이 평생의 사명이자 소망으로 삼아 전파해 온 교리들을—그리고 성직에 든 아들들의 사명 또한—깎아내리는 데 쓰일 수도 있었으니 말이다. 한 손으로는 믿음 굳은 두 아들의 발밑에 받침대를 놓아 주고, 다른 손으로는 똑같은 인위적 수단으로 믿음 없는 아들을 떠받치는 것은, 자신의 신념과 처지와 희망 모두에 어긋나는 일이라고 그는 여겼다. 그럼에도 그는 잘못 이름 붙여진 자신의 에인절을 사랑했고, 아브라함이 운명 지어진 이삭과 함께 산을 오르며 슬퍼했을 법한 마음으로, 아들을 그렇게 대한 것을 남몰래 애통해했다. 그가 스스로 빚어낸 그 말 없는 회한은, 아내가 소리 내어 쏟아 내는 자책보다 훨씬 더 쓰라렸다.\n\n그들은 이 불운한 결혼을 두고 자신들을 탓했다. 에인절이 애초에 농부가 될 운명이 아니었다면 농촌 처녀들과 어울릴 일도 없었으리라. 그들은 무엇이 그와 그의 아내를 갈라놓았는지, 그 헤어짐이 언제 일어났는지도 분명히 알지 못했다. 처음에 그들은 무언가 심각한 반감 같은 것이리라 짐작했다. 그러나 후의 편지들에서 그는 이따금 그녀를 데리러 집에 올 생각을 내비쳤고, 그 표현들로 미루어 그들은 그 갈라섬이 그토록 절망적으로 영구한 무언가에서 비롯된 것은 아니기를 바랐다. 그는 그녀가 친정 식구들과 함께 있다고 말해 두었고, 의혹 속에서도 그들은 더 나아지게 할 방도를 알지 못하는 상황에 끼어들지 않기로 했다.\n\n테스의 편지가 향한 그 눈은 이때 노새 등에 실려, 남아메리카 대륙 내륙에서 해안 쪽으로 그를 실어 나르며, 끝없이 펼쳐진 광야를 바라보고 있었다. 이 낯선 땅에서의 그의 경험은 슬픈 것이었다. 도착한 직후 앓았던 심한 병은 끝내 완전히 그를 떠나지 않았고, 그는 차츰 이곳에서 농사짓겠다는 희망을 거의 접기로 마음을 굳혀 가고 있었다. 다만 머무를 가능성이 조금이라도 남아 있는 한, 이 마음의 변화를 부모에게는 비밀로 했다.\n\n쉬운 자립이라는 선전에 현혹되어 그의 뒤를 따라 이 나라로 몰려온 농업 노동자 무리는, 고생하고, 죽고, 시들어 갔다. 그는 영국 농장에서 온 어머니들이 아기를 품에 안고 터덜터덜 걷는 것을 보곤 했는데, 그 아이가 열병에 걸려 죽으면, 어머니는 걸음을 멈추고 맨손으로 푸석한 흙에 구덩이를 파고, 똑같은 천연의 무덤 연장으로 아기를 거기에 묻고는, 눈물 한 방울을 떨구고 다시 터덜터덜 걸어가는 것이었다.\n\n에인절이 애초 의도한 것은 브라질 이주가 아니라 제 나라의 북쪽이나 동쪽 농장이었다. 그가 이곳으로 온 것은 발작적인 절망 때문이었으니, 영국 농민들 사이의 브라질 열풍이 우연히도 자신의 과거에서 벗어나려는 그의 욕구와 맞아떨어졌던 것이다.\n\n이 부재의 시간 동안 그는 마음으로 열두 살은 더 늙었다. 이제 인생에서 그를 사로잡은 가치는 그 아름다움이라기보다 그 애잔함이었다. 오랫동안 옛 신비주의 체계들을 의심해 온 그는, 이제 도덕에 대한 옛 평가들마저 의심하기 시작했다. 그것들은 다시 조정될 필요가 있다고 그는 생각했다. 도덕적인 사람이란 누구인가? 더더욱 절실하게, 도덕적인 여자란 누구인가? 한 인격의 아름다움이나 추함은 그 성취에만이 아니라 그 목표와 충동에도 깃들어 있었다. 그 참된 역사는 행해진 일들 가운데가 아니라 의지로 품은 일들 가운데 놓여 있었다.\n\n그렇다면, 테스는 어떠한가?\n\n이런 빛 아래에서 그녀를 바라보자, 자신의 성급한 판단에 대한 후회가 그를 짓누르기 시작했다. 그는 그녀를 영원히 거부하는가, 아닌가? 그는 더 이상 자기가 그녀를 언제까지나 거부하겠노라 말할 수 없었고, 그렇게 말하지 못한다는 것은 마음으로는 지금 그녀를 받아들이는 셈이었다.\n\n그녀의 기억에 대한 이 자라나는 애틋함은 시기상으로 그녀가 플린트콤애시에 머물던 때와 겹쳤지만, 그것은 그녀가 자신의 처지나 심정에 대해 그에게 한마디 폐를 끼쳐도 좋다고 스스로 여기기 전이었다. 그는 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못했고, 그녀가 소식을 알리지 않는 동기를 두고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하면서도 묻지 않았다. 그리하여 그녀의 그 순종적인 침묵은 잘못 해석되었다. 그가 이해했더라면 그것이 실은 얼마나 많은 것을 말하고 있었던가!—그가 내렸다가 잊어버린 명령을 그녀가 글자 그대로 정확히 지키고 있었음을, 타고난 대담함에도 불구하고 그녀가 어떤 권리도 주장하지 않고, 그의 판단이 모든 면에서 옳은 것임을 인정하며, 거기에 말없이 고개를 숙이고 있었음을.\n\n앞서 말한 노새를 타고 나라 내륙을 가로지르는 여정에서, 또 다른 남자가 그의 곁에서 말을 몰고 있었다. 에인절의 동행자 역시 영국인으로, 같은 목적을 품고 있었으나, 그는 그 섬의 다른 지역 출신이었다. 둘 다 심란한 상태였고, 그들은 고향 일을 이야기했다. 속내가 속내를 불렀다. 남자들이, 특히 먼 타지에 있을 때, 친구들에게는 결코 입에 올리지 않을 자기 삶의 세부를 낯선 이에게 털어놓곤 하는 그 묘한 성향에 따라, 에인절은 말을 몰며 그 남자에게 자기 결혼의 슬픈 사실들을 고백했다.\n\n그 낯선 이는 에인절보다 훨씬 더 많은 나라를 떠돌았고 훨씬 더 많은 민족들 사이에서 지냈다. 세계를 두루 본 그의 마음에 그런 사회 규범에서의 일탈은, 가정사에는 그토록 거대해 보일지언정, 골짜기와 산맥의 들쭉날쭉함이 지구 전체의 곡선에 미치는 정도밖에 되지 않았다. 그는 그 문제를 에인절과는 사뭇 다른 빛으로 보았다. 테스가 어떠했는가는 그녀가 앞으로 어떠할 것인가에 비하면 아무 중요치 않다고 여겼고, 클레어가 그녀를 떠나온 것은 잘못이었다고 대놓고 말했다.\n\n이튿날 그들은 천둥 폭풍우에 흠뻑 젖었다. 에인절의 동행자는 열병으로 쓰러졌고, 그 주가 다 가기 전에 죽었다. 클레어는 그를 묻으려고 몇 시간을 기다린 뒤, 다시 제 길을 갔다.\n\n흔해 빠진 이름 말고는 도무지 아는 게 없었던 그 도량 넓은 낯선 이의 무심코 던진 말들은, 그의 죽음으로 한층 숭고해져, 철학자들의 그 어떤 논리적 윤리보다 더 클레어에게 깊은 영향을 미쳤다. 그 대비 앞에서 클레어는 자신의 편협함이 부끄러웠다. 자신의 모순들이 홍수처럼 그에게 밀려들었다. 그는 줄곧 기독교를 희생시켜 가며 헬레니즘 이교를 떠받들어 왔다. 그런데 그 문명에서는 불법적인 몸의 내어 줌이 반드시 멸시받는 것은 아니었다. 그렇다면 분명, 순결을 잃지 않은 상태에 대한 그 혐오를—신비주의 신조와 더불어 물려받은 그 혐오를—적어도 그 결과가 배신에서 비롯된 것일 때는 바로잡을 여지가 있는 것으로 여길 수 있었으리라. 회한이 그를 찔렀다. 그의 기억 속에서 결코 완전히 잠잠해진 적 없던 이즈 휴엣의 말이 그에게 되돌아왔다. 그는 이즈에게 자기를 사랑하느냐고 물었고, 그녀는 그렇다고 답했었다. 그녀가 테스보다 그를 더 사랑하느냐고? 아니라고 그녀는 답했었다. 테스는 그를 위해 목숨도 내놓을 것이고, 자기로서는 그 이상은 할 수 없다고.\n\n그는 결혼식 날 모습 그대로의 테스를 떠올렸다. 그녀의 두 눈이 어떻게 그에게 머물렀던가. 그녀가 어떻게 그의 말을 신의 말씀이라도 되는 양 한마디 한마디에 매달렸던가! 그리고 난롯가에서의 그 끔찍한 저녁, 그녀의 순박한 영혼이 그의 영혼 앞에 제 속을 드러냈을 때, 불빛 속에서 그녀의 얼굴은 얼마나 가여워 보였던가. 그의 사랑과 보호가 거두어질 수도 있다는 것을 도무지 받아들이지 못하던 그 얼굴이.\n\n그리하여 그는 그녀의 비판자에서 그녀의 변호인으로 자라났다. 그는 그녀를 두고 자기 자신에게 냉소적인 말들을 내뱉었었다. 그러나 그 누구도 늘 냉소가가 되어서는 살아갈 수 없는 법이니, 그는 그 말들을 거두어들였다. 그런 말들을 내뱉은 잘못은, 개별의 경우를 도외시한 채 일반 원리에 자신을 휘둘리게 내버려 둔 데서 비롯되었다.\n\n그러나 이런 추론은 다소 케케묵은 것이다. 오늘 이전에도 연인들과 남편들이 이미 거쳐 간 자리이니까. 클레어는 그녀에게 모질었다. 그건 의심할 여지가 없다. 남자들은 사랑하거나 사랑했던 여자들에게 너무도 자주 모질다. 여자들도 남자들에게 그렇다. 그런데도 이런 모짊은, 그것이 자라나는 근원인 저 보편적인 모짊에 비하면 다정함 그 자체다. 기질을 향한 처지의 모짊, 목표를 향한 수단의 모짊, 어제를 향한 오늘의 모짊, 오늘을 향한 앞날의 모짊에 비하면.\n\n그녀 집안의 그 역사적 흥미—그가 한물간 세력이라 멸시했던 그 위풍당당한 더버빌 가문의 핏줄—가 이제 그의 감정을 건드렸다. 어째서 그는 이런 것들의 정치적 가치와 상상적 가치의 차이를 알지 못했던가? 후자의 측면에서 그녀의 더버빌 혈통은 어마어마한 의미를 지닌 사실이었다. 경제에는 무가치할지언정, 몽상가에게는, 쇠락과 몰락을 두고 도덕을 논하는 자에게는, 더없이 유용한 재료였다. 그것은 머지않아 잊힐 사실이었다—가엾은 테스의 핏줄과 이름에 깃든 그 한 줌의 고귀함은. 그리고 킹스비어의 대리석 기념비와 납으로 싸인 해골들과 그녀를 잇는 그 세습의 끈 위로 망각이 내려앉으리라. 이렇게 시간은 제 손으로 빚은 낭만들을 무자비하게 파괴한다. 그녀의 얼굴을 거듭거듭 떠올리며, 그는 이제 그 안에서 그녀의 고귀한 할머니들을 빛냈을 그 위엄의 섬광을 볼 수 있을 것만 같았다. 그리고 그 환영은 예전에 그가 느꼈던 그 기운을 핏줄을 타고 흐르게 했으니, 그 기운 뒤에는 일종의 메스꺼움이 남았었다.\n\n침범당하지 않았다 할 수 없는 과거에도 불구하고, 테스 같은 여자 안에 여전히 깃들어 있는 것은 또래 여자들의 싱그러움보다 더 값졌다. 에브라임에서 거둔 포도 이삭이 아비에셀의 포도 수확보다 낫지 아니한가?\n\n새로이 되살아난 사랑은 이렇게 말하며, 테스의 헌신적인 토로가 들어설 길을 닦고 있었다. 그 토로는 바로 그때 그의 아버지를 통해 그에게 전달되고 있었으나, 그가 내륙 깊숙이 있는 탓에 그에게 닿기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸릴 터였다.\n\n한편 그 호소에 에인절이 응답하여 와 주리라는 편지를 쓴 이의 기대는 컸다 작았다 오락가락했다. 그 기대를 줄어들게 한 것은, 헤어짐으로 이끈 그녀 삶의 사실들이 바뀌지 않았다는—결코 바뀔 수 없다는—점, 그리고 그녀가 곁에 있어도 그 사실들을 옅게 만들지 못했으니 곁에 없다 한들 그럴 리 없다는 점이었다. 그럼에도 그녀는, 그가 만약 온다면 그를 가장 기쁘게 하기 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을까 하는 애틋한 물음에 마음을 쏟았다. 그가 하프로 연주하던 곡들에 좀 더 주의를 기울일걸, 농촌 처녀들이 부르던 노래들 가운데 어느 것이 그가 가장 좋아하는 발라드인지 좀 더 궁금해하며 그에게 물어볼걸, 하는 바람에 한숨이 새어 나왔다. 그녀는 탤보세이스에서 이즈를 따라온 앰비 시들링에게 에둘러 물었고, 마침 앰비는 기억해 냈다. 낙농장에서 소들이 젖을 내리게 하려고 그들이 흥얼대던 가락 토막들 가운데, 클레어가 \"큐피드의 정원\"과 \"내겐 사냥터가 있고, 사냥개가 있네\"와 \"동틀 무렵\"을 좋아하는 듯했고, \"재단사의 바지\"와 \"이렇게 고운 미인으로 자랐네\"는—썩 훌륭한 곡들이었는데도—별로 마음에 들어 하지 않는 듯했음을.\n\n이 발라드들을 완벽하게 익히는 것이 이제 그녀의 별난 소망이 되었다. 그녀는 틈날 때마다 남몰래 그것들을 연습했고, 특히 \"동틀 무렵\"을 그러했다.\n\n일어나라, 일어나라, 일어나라! 그대 임에게 꽃다발을 꺾어 주오, 정원에 피어나는 가장 향기로운 꽃들을 죄다. 멧비둘기와 작은 새들이 가지마다 둥지를 트네, 오월의 이른 무렵 동틀 무렵에!\n\n이 추운 마른 철에 그녀가 다른 처녀들과 떨어져 일할 때마다 이 노래들을 부르는 것을 들었다면, 돌의 마음이라도 녹았을 것이다. 그러는 내내 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸으니, 어쩌면 그가 끝내 그것들을 들으러 와 주지 않으리라는 생각 때문이었고, 노래의 단순하고 어리석은 가사가 부르는 이의 아파하는 마음을 가슴 아픈 조롱처럼 울리며 메아리쳤기 때문이었다.\n\n테스는 이 부질없는 꿈에 어찌나 빠져 있었는지, 계절이 어떻게 흘러가는지도 모르는 듯했다. 날이 길어진 것도, 성모영보대축일이 코앞이며 곧 옛 성모영보대축일이 뒤따라와 이곳에서의 그녀 고용 기한이 끝나리라는 것도.\n\n그러나 그 분기 결산일이 채 오기 전에, 테스로 하여금 사뭇 다른 일들을 생각하게 만든 무언가가 일어났다. 어느 날 저녁 그녀는 여느 때처럼 거처에서 나머지 식구들과 함께 아래층 방에 앉아 있었는데, 누군가 문을 두드리며 테스를 찾았다. 문간 너머로 그녀는 스러져 가는 빛을 등진 형체를 보았다. 키는 여인만 하고 너비는 어린아이만 한, 호리호리하고 키 큰 소녀 같은 존재였는데, 그 소녀가 \"테스 언니!\" 하고 부를 때까지 그녀는 황혼 속에서 누구인지 알아보지 못했다.\n\n\"뭐야—라이자루니?\" 테스가 깜짝 놀란 목소리로 물었다. 일 년 남짓 전에 어린애로 집에 두고 떠나온 동생이, 갑작스러운 한 차례 솟구침으로 이런 모습으로 훌쩍 자라 있었던 것이다. 정작 루 자신은 아직 그 의미를 거의 헤아리지 못하는 듯했다. 한때 길었으나 이제는 자라면서 짧아진 원피스 아래로 드러난 가느다란 다리와, 어색한 손과 팔이 그 어림과 미숙함을 드러냈다.\n\n\"응, 하루 종일 쏘다녔어, 언니.\" 루가 감정 없는 진지함으로 말했다. \"언니를 찾느라고. 나 너무 지쳤어.\"\n\n\"집에 무슨 일 있어?\"\n\n\"엄마가 많이 편찮으셔, 의사 선생님이 돌아가실 거래. 아빠도 별로 편찮으셔서, 자기처럼 그렇게 높은 집안의 사람이 막일하면서 뼈 빠지게 일하는 건 옳지 않다고 하셔. 우린 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠어.\"\n\n테스는 라이자루에게 들어와 앉으라고 할 생각조차 들지 않은 채 한참을 멍하니 서 있었다. 그러고 나서 그렇게 하고, 라이자루가 차를 마시고 있을 때, 그녀는 결심을 굳혔다. 반드시 집으로 가야 했다. 그녀의 고용 계약은 옛 성모영보대축일인 사월 엿새까지였지만, 거기까지 남은 기간이 길지 않았으므로 그녀는 위험을 무릅쓰고 당장 떠나기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그날 밤에 가면 열두 시간을 버는 셈이었다. 그러나 동생이 너무 지쳐서 그런 거리를 이튿날까지는 감당할 수 없었다. 테스는 메리언과 이즈가 사는 데로 달려 내려가 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알리고, 자기 사정을 농장주에게 잘 둘러대 달라고 부탁했다. 돌아와서는 루에게 저녁을 차려 주고, 그런 다음 어린 동생을 제 침대에 뉘어 재우고, 버들 바구니에 들어갈 만큼 제 소지품을 챙겨 넣고, 루에게 이튿날 아침 자기를 따라오라 일러 두고는 길을 나섰다.\n\n# L\n\n시계가 열 시를 칠 때 그녀는 십오 마일을 걸어야 하는 길을 위해, 강철빛 별들 아래 쌀쌀한 춘분의 어둠 속으로 뛰어들었다. 외진 고장에서 밤은 소리 없이 걷는 행인에게 위험이라기보다 보호막이다. 이를 아는 테스는, 낮이라면 거의 무서워했을 샛길들을 따라 가장 가까운 길을 잡아 나아갔다. 그러나 이제 약탈자는 없었고, 유령 같은 두려움도 어머니 생각에 마음에서 밀려났다. 그렇게 그녀는 마일을 거듭 나아가, 오르락내리락하다가 마침내 불배로에 이르렀고, 자정 무렵 그 높은 곳에서 어지러운 그늘의 심연을 내려다보았다. 그것이 그 골짜기에 대해 드러나 보이는 전부였으니, 그 골짜기 건너편에서 그녀는 태어났던 것이다. 이미 고지대에서 오 마일가량을 지나온 그녀에게는, 이제 여정이 끝나기까지 저지대에서 십 마일 내지 십일 마일이 남아 있었다. 굽이굽이 내려가는 길이 희미한 별빛 아래 겨우 보였고, 그녀가 그 길을 따라가자 이내 위쪽 땅과는 사뭇 다른 흙을 밟게 되었는데, 그 차이는 밟는 감촉으로도 냄새로도 느껴졌다. 그것은 블랙무어 골짜기의 무거운 점토 땅이었고, 그 골짜기 가운데서도 유료 도로가 한 번도 뚫린 적 없는 일부였다. 이런 무거운 흙 위에서는 미신이 가장 오래 머문다. 한때 숲이었던 이 땅은, 이 어슴푸레한 시각에 그 옛 성격을 어느 정도 내세우는 듯했고, 먼 것과 가까운 것이 한데 뒤섞이며 모든 나무와 키 큰 울타리가 제 존재를 한껏 부풀렸다. 이곳에서 사냥당하던 수사슴들, 바늘에 찔리고 물에 담가지던 마녀들, 지나갈 때 당신에게 \"키득\"거리던 초록빛 반짝이는 요정들—이곳은 여전히 그것들에 대한 믿음으로 들끓었고, 그것들은 이제 한 무리의 장난꾸러기 떼를 이루었다.\n\n너틀베리에서 그녀는 마을 주막을 지났는데, 그 간판은 그녀 발소리의 인사에 화답하듯 삐걱거렸으나, 그 소리를 들은 사람은 그녀 자신밖에 없었다. 초가지붕 아래로 그녀의 마음의 눈은 풀린 힘줄과 늘어진 근육들이 어둠 속에 펼쳐져, 작은 자줏빛 조각보 사각형들로 기운 이불 밑에서, 햄블던힐에 분홍빛 안개의 기색이 나타나는 즉시 이튿날의 새 노동을 위해 잠의 손길로 다시 기운을 차리는 과정을 거치는 모습을 떠올렸다.\n\n세 시에 그녀는 자신이 헤쳐 온 미로 같은 샛길들의 마지막 모퉁이를 돌아 말럿으로 들어섰고, 클럽 처녀였던 시절 에인절 클레어를 처음 보았던—그가 그녀와 춤추지 않았던—그 들판을 지나쳤다. 그 실망의 느낌은 아직도 그녀에게 남아 있었다. 어머니의 집 쪽에서 그녀는 불빛을 보았다. 그것은 침실 창문에서 흘러나왔고, 나뭇가지 하나가 그 앞에서 흔들리며 불빛을 그녀에게 깜빡이게 했다. 집의 윤곽을—그녀의 돈으로 새로 이엉을 인 그 집을—분간할 수 있게 되자마자, 그것은 테스의 상상에 여느 때처럼 그 옛 효력을 고스란히 미쳤다. 그것은 늘 그녀 몸과 삶의 일부인 듯싶었다. 그 지붕창들의 기울기, 박공들의 마무리, 굴뚝 꼭대기를 이룬 깨진 벽돌 켜들, 그 모두가 그녀의 인격과 무언가 통하는 데가 있었다. 그녀가 보기에 그 모습들에 멍한 마비가 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 어머니의 병을 뜻했다.\n\n그녀는 아무도 깨우지 않을 만큼 살며시 문을 열었다. 아래층 방은 비어 있었으나, 어머니 곁을 지키며 밤샘하던 이웃이 계단 꼭대기로 나와, 더비필드 부인이 마침 그때 잠들어 있긴 하나 차도가 없다고 속삭였다. 테스는 자기 아침을 차려 먹고, 그런 다음 어머니 방에서 간병인의 자리를 떠맡았다.\n\n아침에 아이들을 바라보니, 모두가 묘하게 길쭉해진 모습이었다. 그녀가 떠나 있던 것은 겨우 일 년 남짓이었는데도 그 자란 정도가 놀라웠다. 그리고 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 아이들의 필요에 매달려야 하는 형편이 그녀를 자기 자신의 근심에서 끌어내 주었다. 아버지의 병약함은 여전히 그 막연한 종류 그대로였고, 그는 여느 때처럼 의자에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 도착한 이튿날 그는 유난히 기분이 밝았다. 그에게는 살아갈 합리적인 계획이 하나 있었고, 테스가 그게 무엇이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"이 잉글랜드 일대의 옛 골동품 애호가들한테 죄다 사람을 보내 볼까 생각 중이다.\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 부양할 기금에 기부해 달라고 청하는 거지. 분명히 그들은 그걸 낭만적이고 예술적이고 마땅한 일로 여길 거야. 그자들은 옛 폐허를 보존하고, 무슨 뼈다귀 같은 걸 찾아내는 데 돈을 엄청 쓰잖니. 살아 있는 유물이라면, 나에 대해 알기만 한다면, 그들한테는 훨씬 더 흥미로울 게 틀림없지. 누군가 돌아다니며 그들 사이에 무엇이 살아 있는지 일러 줬으면 좋으련만, 그들은 그를 아무것도 아니라 여기고 있으니! 나를 발견한 트링엄 목사가 살아 있었다면, 분명 그가 해 줬을 텐데.\"\n\n테스는 이 거창한 계획에 대한 반론을, 당장 눈앞에 닥친 시급한 문제들과 씨름하고 난 뒤로 미뤘다. 그 문제들은 그녀가 부쳐 온 돈으로도 별로 나아진 것 같지 않았다. 집 안의 필요가 좀 누그러지자, 그녀는 바깥일로 눈을 돌렸다. 이제 심고 씨 뿌리는 철이었다. 마을 사람들의 정원과 분배지 상당수는 이미 봄갈이를 마쳤지만, 더비필드네의 정원과 분배지는 뒤처져 있었다. 그녀는 그것이 그들이 씨감자를 죄다 먹어 치운 탓임을 알고는 아연실색했다—앞날을 생각지 않는 자들의 마지막 실수였다. 그녀는 한시바삐 구할 수 있는 다른 씨감자를 구해 왔고, 며칠 지나자 아버지는 테스의 설득에 힘입어 정원을 돌볼 만큼은 기운을 차렸다. 한편 그녀 자신은 마을에서 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 들판에 세 들어 부치던 분배지 텃밭을 맡았다.\n\n병실의 갑갑함에서 벗어난 뒤라 그녀는 그 일이 좋았다. 어머니의 차도 덕에 이제 그녀가 병실에 매일 필요는 없었다. 격렬한 움직임이 생각을 덜어 주었다. 그 텃밭은 높고 메마른 트인 울안에 있었는데, 그런 땅뙈기가 마흔에서 쉰 개쯤 있었고, 낮 동안의 품팔이 노동이 끝난 뒤라야 일이 가장 활기를 띠는 곳이었다. 땅 파기는 보통 여섯 시에 시작되어 어스름이나 달빛 속으로 한없이 이어졌다. 마침 지금은 죽은 잡초와 쓰레기 더미가 여러 땅뙈기에서 타고 있었으니, 메마른 날씨가 그 불타는 것을 거들었다.\n\n어느 화창한 날, 테스와 라이자루는 해의 마지막 빛살이 땅뙈기들을 가르는 흰 말뚝들 위로 납작하게 내리쬘 때까지 이웃들과 함께 여기서 일했다. 일몰에 뒤이어 황혼이 깔리자, 우산잔디와 양배추 줄기를 태우는 불길이 분배지들을 띄엄띄엄 밝히기 시작했고, 그 윤곽은 바람에 실려 오는 짙은 연기 아래로 나타났다 사라졌다 했다. 불이 이글거릴 때면, 땅을 따라 납작하게 불려 가는 연기 둑들이 그 자체로 불투명한 윤기를 띠게 환히 밝혀져 일꾼들을 서로에게서 가려 주었다. 그리고 낮에는 벽이요 밤에는 빛이었던 저 \"구름 기둥\"의 뜻을 헤아릴 수 있었다.\n\n저녁이 짙어지자, 정원 일 하던 남녀 가운데 더러는 그날 일을 접었으나, 더 많은 수는 심을 것을 다 심으려고 남았고, 테스도 그중에 끼어 있었다. 다만 동생은 집으로 돌려보냈다. 그녀가 쇠스랑을 들고 일하던 곳은 우산잔디를 태우던 땅뙈기 가운데 하나였고, 쇠스랑의 빛나는 네 갈래 살이 돌과 마른 흙덩이에 부딪혀 작은 딸깍 소리를 냈다. 때로 그녀는 제 불의 연기에 완전히 휩싸였고, 그러면 연기가 그녀의 형체를 풀어 주어, 더미에서 뿜어 나오는 놋쇳빛 불꽃에 환히 비치게 했다. 오늘 밤 그녀는 묘한 옷차림을 하고 있어 다소 눈에 띄는 모양새였다. 여러 번 빨아 빛이 바랜 가운에 짧은 검정 재킷을 걸쳤는데, 전체적인 인상이 결혼식과 장례식 하객을 하나로 합쳐 놓은 듯했다. 더 뒤쪽의 여자들은 흰 앞치마를 둘렀는데, 그것이 그녀들의 창백한 얼굴과 더불어, 이따금 불길의 섬광을 받을 때 말고는, 어스름 속에서 보이는 전부였다.\n\n서쪽으로는 들판의 경계를 이룬 헐벗은 가시나무 울타리의 철사 같은 가지들이 아래쪽 하늘의 창백한 유백색을 배경으로 솟아 있었다. 그 위로는 목성이 활짝 핀 노랑수선화처럼 걸려, 어찌나 밝은지 거의 그림자를 드리울 정도였다. 정체 모를 작은 별 몇 개가 다른 곳에 나타나고 있었다. 멀리서 개 한 마리가 짖었고, 이따금 수레바퀴가 마른 길 위를 덜컹거리며 지나갔다.\n\n여전히 쇠스랑 살은 부지런히 딸깍거렸으니, 아직 늦은 시각이 아니었기 때문이다. 그리고 공기는 상쾌하고 매서웠지만 그 안에 봄의 속삭임이 깃들어 일꾼들의 기운을 북돋웠다. 그 장소, 그 시각, 타닥거리는 불길, 빛과 그림자의 환상적인 신비—그 무언가가 테스뿐 아니라 다른 이들도 거기 있는 것을 즐기게 만들었다. 겨울의 서리 속에서는 마귀처럼 오고 여름의 온기 속에서는 연인처럼 오는 해 질 녘이, 이 삼월의 날에는 마음을 가라앉히는 자처럼 찾아왔다.\n\n아무도 제 동료들을 쳐다보지 않았다. 모두의 눈은 불빛에 드러나는 흙의 뒤집힌 표면에 쏠려 있었다. 그리하여 테스가 흙덩이를 휘저으며, 클레어가 끝내 그것들을 들으리라는 희망은 이제 거의 없는 채로 제 어리석은 작은 노래들을 부르는 동안, 그녀는 자기 가장 가까이서 일하는 사람을 한참 동안 알아채지 못했다—긴 작업복을 입은 한 남자였는데, 알고 보니 자기와 같은 땅뙈기를 쇠스랑질하고 있었고, 그녀는 아버지가 일을 거들라고 그를 보냈으려니 짐작했다. 그가 파 들어가는 방향이 그를 더 가까이 데려왔을 때 그녀는 그를 좀 더 의식하게 되었다. 때로 연기가 둘을 갈라놓았고, 그러다 연기가 비껴가면, 둘은 서로에게는 보이되 나머지 모두에게서는 가려졌다.\n\n테스는 함께 일하는 그 사람에게 말을 걸지 않았고, 그도 그녀에게 말을 걸지 않았다. 그녀는 또한, 환한 대낮에는 그가 거기 없었다는 것, 그리고 자기가 그를 말럿 일꾼들 가운데 누구로도 알지 못한다는 것을 떠올린 것 말고는 더는 그를 생각지 않았다. 그것도 이상할 게 없었으니, 근래 몇 년 그녀의 부재가 그토록 잦고 길었던 까닭이다. 이윽고 그가 그녀에게 어찌나 바짝 붙어 파는지, 불꽃의 빛살이 그의 쇠스랑 강철 살에서도 그녀 자신의 것에서만큼이나 또렷이 반사되었다. 죽은 잡초 한 무더기를 던져 넣으려고 불 쪽으로 올라가다가, 그녀는 그가 반대편에서 똑같이 하는 것을 보았다. 불이 확 타올랐고, 그녀는 더버빌의 얼굴을 마주 보았다.\n\n그가 거기 있다는 뜻밖의 사실, 그리고 이제는 가장 구식인 일꾼들이나 입는 주름 잡힌 작업복 차림의 그 기괴한 몰골은, 그것이 뜻하는 바를 두고 그녀를 오싹하게 하는 섬뜩한 익살스러움을 띠었다. 더버빌이 낮고 긴 웃음을 흘렸다.\n\n\"농담할 마음이 든다면, 이거 참 낙원 같지 않으냐고 말하겠는데!\" 그가 고개를 기울여 그녀를 보며 익살맞게 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 그녀가 힘없이 물었다.\n\n\"익살꾼이라면 이게 꼭 낙원 같다고 하겠지. 너는 이브고, 나는 열등한 짐승으로 변장하고 너를 꾀러 온 그 옛 다른 자고 말이야. 내가 신학에 빠져 있던 시절엔 밀턴의 그 장면을 제법 꿰고 있었지. 이런 대목이 있어—\n\n'여왕이여, 길은 이미 닦여 있고, 멀지 않소, 도금양 한 줄 너머에… …그대 내 인도를 받아들인다면, 곧 그리로 데려다주리다.' '그럼 앞장서요.' 이브가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐 이런 식이지. 사랑하는 테스, 나는 그저 이걸 네가 짐작했거나 입에 올렸을 법한 것으로—전혀 사실이 아닌데도—말해 보는 거야. 네가 나를 그토록 나쁘게 여기니 말이지.\"\n\n\"나는 당신이 사탄이라고 한 적도 없고 그렇게 여긴 적도 없어요. 그런 식으로는 당신을 전혀 생각하지 않아요. 당신이 나를 모욕할 때 말고는, 당신에 대한 내 생각은 아주 차가워요. 뭐예요, 순전히 나 때문에 여기 와서 땅을 판 거예요?\"\n\n\"순전히 그래서지. 너를 보려고. 그 이상은 아니야. 이 작업복은, 오는 길에 팔려고 걸어 둔 걸 봤는데, 눈에 띄지 않으려고 나중에 생각해 낸 거야. 나는 네가 이렇게 일하는 것에 항의하러 왔어.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 이 일이 좋아요—아버지를 위한 거니까요.\"\n\n\"저쪽 데서 일하던 계약은 끝났느냐?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"다음엔 어디로 갈 거지? 네 사랑하는 남편한테 가려고?\"\n\n그녀는 그 굴욕적인 상기를 견딜 수 없었다.\n\n\"아—몰라요!\" 그녀가 쓰라리게 말했다. \"나한테는 남편이 없어요!\"\n\n\"그건 사실이지—네가 말하는 그런 뜻으로는. 하지만 너한테는 친구가 있어, 그리고 나는 네가 싫다 해도 너를 편안하게 해 주기로 마음먹었어. 네 집에 내려가 보면, 내가 너를 위해 거기로 보낸 게 뭔지 알게 될 거다.\"\n\n\"아, 알렉, 제발 나한테 아무것도 주지 말아 줘요! 나는 당신한테서 받을 수 없어요! 싫어요—옳지 않아요!\"\n\n\"옳은 일이야!\" 그가 가볍게 외쳤다. \"내가 너에게 느끼는 만큼 애틋하게 느끼는 여자가 곤경에 빠진 걸, 도우려 애써 보지도 않고 두고 보지는 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 아주 잘 지내요! 내가 곤경에 빠진 건 다만—다만—사는 것에 관한 게 전혀 아니에요!\"\n\n그녀는 돌아서서, 절박하게 다시 땅을 파기 시작했고, 눈물이 쇠스랑 자루 위로, 흙덩이 위로 뚝뚝 떨어졌다.\n\n\"그 애들 얘기야—네 동생들 말이다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 애들을 생각해 봤어.\"\n\n테스의 가슴이 떨렸다—그가 그녀의 약한 곳을 건드리고 있었다. 그는 그녀의 가장 큰 걱정거리를 알아맞혔다. 집에 돌아온 뒤로 그녀의 영혼은 격정적인 애정으로 그 아이들에게 쏠려 있었다.\n\n\"네 어머니가 회복되지 않으면, 누군가 그 애들을 위해 무언가 해야 할 텐데. 네 아버지는 많은 걸 할 수 없을 테니, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"내가 도우면 할 수 있어요. 하셔야 하고요!\"\n\n\"내 도움도 있고.\"\n\n\"안 돼요!\"\n\n\"이 얼마나 빌어먹게 어리석은 짓이냐!\" 더버빌이 터뜨렸다. \"이봐, 그자는 우리가 같은 집안이라 여기고, 아주 흡족해할 텐데!\"\n\n\"안 그래요. 내가 그 오해를 풀어 드렸어요.\"\n\n\"그런 바보가 어디 있어!\"\n\n더버빌은 화가 나서 그녀에게서 물러나 울타리 쪽으로 갔고, 거기서 자기를 변장시켰던 그 긴 작업복을 벗더니, 둘둘 말아 우산잔디 불 속에 밀어 넣고는 가 버렸다.\n\n테스는 이 일 뒤로 땅 파기를 계속할 수 없었다. 마음이 안절부절못했다. 그가 아버지 집으로 돌아갔을까 궁금했고, 쇠스랑을 손에 들고 집을 향해 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n집에서 이십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 동생 하나가 그녀를 맞았다.\n\n\"아, 테스 언니—어떡해! 라이자루 언니가 울고 있고, 집에 사람들이 잔뜩 와 있어. 엄마는 많이 좋아지셨는데, 아빠가 돌아가신 것 같대!\"\n\n그 아이는 소식의 엄청남은 알았으나 아직 그 슬픔까지는 알지 못한 채, 동그래진 눈으로 짐짓 중요한 표정을 지으며 테스를 바라보고 섰다가, 테스에게 이는 변화를 보고는 말했다—\n\n\"뭐야, 언니, 우리 이제 아빠랑 다시는 영영 말 못 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"하지만 아빠는 그저 조금 편찮으셨을 뿐인데!\" 테스가 정신없이 외쳤다.\n\n라이자루가 다가왔다.\n\n\"방금 픽 쓰러지셨어, 엄마 때문에 와 있던 의사 선생님이 가망이 없다고 했어. 심장이 안으로 자라 버렸대.\"\n\n그렇다. 더비필드 부부는 자리가 뒤바뀌었다. 죽어 가던 쪽은 위험에서 벗어났고, 가볍게 앓던 쪽이 죽은 것이다. 그 소식은 들리는 것보다 한층 더 큰 의미를 담고 있었다. 아버지의 목숨은 그 자신의 성취와는 별개의 가치를 지녔으니, 어쩌면 그렇지 않았다면 그리 큰 가치가 없었을 것이다. 그것은 그 집과 부지가 임차 계약으로 묶여 있던 세 사람의 수명 가운데 마지막이었고, 그것은 오래전부터 차지농이 정규 일꾼들을 위해 탐내 온 것이었다. 그 일꾼들은 오두막 거처가 부족했던 것이다. 게다가 마을에서 \"거주권자\"는 작은 자유보유농만큼이나 못마땅하게 여겨졌으니, 그들의 독립적인 태도 때문이었다. 그래서 임차 계약이 끝나면 결코 갱신되는 법이 없었다.\n* 거주권자: 한 사람 또는 몇 사람의 수명 동안 집을 보유하는 종신 임차인.\n\n이리하여 한때 더버빌이었던 더비필드 가문은, 자기들에게 운명이 내려앉는 것을 보았다. 그들이 이 고을의 올림포스 신들 가운데 있던 시절, 지금의 자기들처럼 땅 없는 자들의 머리 위로 그 운명을 분명 여러 번, 그것도 모질게 내려앉히게 했을 그 운명이었다. 이렇게 밀물과 썰물이—변화의 율동이—하늘 아래 모든 것 속에서 번갈아 들며 끈질기게 이어진다.\n\n# LI\n\n마침내 옛 성모영보대축일 전야가 되었고, 농촌 세계는 한 해의 바로 그 날에만 일어나는 이동의 열병에 들떠 있었다. 그날은 약속이 이행되는 날이다. 성촉절에 맺은, 이듬해 한 해 동안의 옥외 고용 계약이 이제 실행에 옮겨지는 것이다. 더 이상 옛 자리에 머물고 싶지 않은 노동자들이—혹은 바깥에서 다른 말이 들어오기 전까지 그들이 아득한 옛날부터 스스로를 일컫던 대로 \"일꾼들\"이—새 농장으로 옮겨 가고 있었다.\n\n이 농장에서 농장으로의 해마다의 이주는 이곳에서 점점 늘어나는 추세였다. 테스의 어머니가 어렸을 적에는 말럿 일대 들일하는 사람들 대다수가 한 농장에서 평생을 머물렀고, 그 농장은 그들 아버지와 할아버지의 집이기도 했다. 그러나 근래에는 해마다 옮기려는 욕구가 높이 치솟았다. 젊은 가족들에게 그것은 어쩌면 이득이 될 수도 있는 즐거운 흥분이었다. 한 가족에게 이집트인 곳이, 멀리서 그것을 바라보는 가족에게는 약속의 땅이었으니, 거기 살다 보면 그곳이 다시 그들의 이집트가 되었다. 그렇게 그들은 옮기고 또 옮겼다.\n\n그러나 마을 생활에서 점점 더 뚜렷이 드러나는 그 모든 변천이 전적으로 농촌의 불안에서 비롯된 것만은 아니었다. 인구 감소 또한 진행되고 있었다. 마을에는 예전에 농업 노동자들과 나란히, 그들보다 분명 윗줄에 드는, 흥미롭고 더 식견 있는 한 계층이—테스의 아버지와 어머니가 속했던 계층이—있었으니, 목수와 대장장이, 제화공, 행상, 그리고 농장 일꾼이 아닌 정체 모를 여러 일꾼들이 거기 들었다. 이들은 테스 아버지처럼 종신보유농이거나, 등본보유농이거나, 이따금 작은 자유보유농인 덕분에 목표와 처신에 어느 정도의 안정을 누리던 사람들이었다. 그러나 그 긴 보유 기한이 끝나면, 그 땅은 좀처럼 비슷한 임차인들에게 다시 빌려지지 않았고, 차지농이 제 일손을 위해 꼭 필요로 하지 않는 한 대개 헐려 버렸다. 땅에 직접 고용되지 않은 오두막 거주민들은 못마땅하게 여겨졌고, 일부가 쫓겨나면 그것이 다른 이들의 생계를 굶주리게 했으니, 그들 역시 어쩔 수 없이 뒤따라야 했다. 지난날 마을 생활의 등뼈를 이루었던, 마을 전통의 보관자였던 이 가족들은 큰 중심지로 피난처를 구해야 했다. 통계학자들이 익살스레 \"농촌 인구의 대도시 지향 경향\"이라 이름 붙인 그 과정은, 실은 기계의 힘에 떠밀린 물이 언덕 위로 흐르는 경향이었다.\n\n* 등본보유농: 영주 법정 등본에 기록된 권리로 토지를 보유하던 소작농.\n이런 식으로 말럿의 오두막 거처가 철거로 상당히 줄어들자, 남아 있는 집마다 농사꾼이 제 일꾼들을 위해 필요로 했다. 테스의 삶에 그토록 짙은 그늘을 드리운 그 사건이 일어난 이래로, 더비필드 가족은(그들의 혈통은 믿어지지 않았다) 오직 도덕의 이익을 위해서라도 임차 기한이 끝나면 떠나야 할 집안으로 암묵리에 여겨져 왔다. 사실 그 집안이 절제든, 진중함이든, 정숙함이든, 그 어느 면에서도 빛나는 본보기가 아니었던 것은 정말이지 사실이었다. 아버지는, 심지어 어머니까지도, 때때로 술에 취했고, 어린 자식들은 좀처럼 교회에 가지 않았으며, 맏딸은 괴이한 결합을 했다. 어떤 식으로든 마을은 순결하게 지켜져야 했다. 그래서 더비필드 가족이 쫓겨날 수 있게 된 이 첫 성모영보대축일에, 그 집은 널찍한지라 대가족을 거느린 짐마차꾼에게 필요했고, 과부 조앤과 그 딸들인 테스와 라이자루, 사내아이 에이브러햄, 그리고 더 어린 아이들은 다른 데로 가야 했다.\n\n그들이 떠나기 전날 저녁, 하늘을 흐릿하게 가린 이슬비 탓에 일찌감치 어두워지고 있었다. 그들의 집이자 출생지였던 마을에서 보내는 마지막 밤이었으므로, 더비필드 부인과 라이자루와 에이브러햄은 몇몇 벗들에게 작별을 고하러 나갔고, 테스는 그들이 돌아올 때까지 집을 지키고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 창가 의자에 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 창틀에 바짝 댄 채였는데, 거기서는 빗물의 바깥 막이 안쪽 유리창을 타고 미끄러져 내렸다. 그녀의 눈길은 거미줄에 머물렀다. 아마 오래전에 굶어 죽었을 그 거미는, 파리라곤 오는 법 없는 구석에 잘못 자리를 잡았던 모양으로, 창틀로 새어 드는 미미한 바람결에 떨고 있었다. 테스는 이 집안의 처지를 곱씹고 있었고, 거기서 자기 자신의 나쁜 영향을 알아보았다. 자기가 집에 오지 않았다면, 어머니와 아이들은 아마 주 단위 세입자로 계속 머물게 허락되었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 돌아오자마자 거의 곧바로, 깐깐한 성품에 큰 영향력을 지닌 어떤 사람들 눈에 띄고 말았다. 그들은 그녀가 교회 묘지에서 빈둥대며, 작은 모종삽으로 한 아기의 지워진 무덤을 제 딴엔 정성껏 손보는 것을 보았던 것이다. 이로써 그들은 그녀가 다시 여기서 살고 있음을 알아챘고, 그녀의 어머니는 딸을 \"거두어들였다\"고 꾸지람을 들었다. 조앤은 발끈하여 쏘아붙였고, 제멋대로 당장 떠나겠노라 내뱉었다. 그러자 그 말이 그대로 받아들여졌고, 그 결과가 바로 이것이었다.\n\n\"나는 절대 집에 오지 말았어야 했어.\" 테스가 쓰라리게 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n이런 생각에 어찌나 골몰했던지, 그녀는 거리를 말 타고 내려오는 흰 방수외투 차림의 한 남자를 처음에는 거의 알아채지 못했다. 아마 그녀의 얼굴이 유리창에 바짝 붙어 있던 탓에 그가 그녀를 그토록 빨리 보았을 것이고, 말을 오두막 정면에 어찌나 바싹 몰았던지 말굽이 거의 벽 밑 화초 자라는 좁은 화단 위에 닿을 듯했다. 그가 승마용 채찍으로 창을 두드리고서야 그녀는 그를 알아보았다. 비가 거의 그쳐 있었고, 그녀는 그의 손짓에 따라 창틀을 열었다.\n\n\"나 못 봤어?\" 더버빌이 물었다.\n\n\"정신을 딴 데 두고 있었어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"들은 것 같긴 한데, 마차와 말 소리인 줄 알았어요. 일종의 꿈에 잠겨 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"아! 어쩌면 더버빌의 마차 소리를 들었던 거겠지. 그 전설을 너도 알 테지?\"\n\n\"아니요. 누가—누군가 언젠가 그걸 나한테 들려주려다가 말았어요.\"\n\n\"네가 진짜 더버빌이라면 나도 너한테 들려줘선 안 되겠지. 하지만 나야 가짜니까 상관없어. 좀 음산한 얘기야. 존재하지도 않는 마차의 그 소리는 더버빌 핏줄을 타고난 자만이 들을 수 있는데, 그걸 듣는 자에게는 흉조라고 여겨지지. 여러 세기 전에 그 집안 사람 하나가 저지른 살인과 관련된 거야.\"\n\n\"이미 시작했으니, 끝까지 마저 해 봐요.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그 집안 사람 하나가 어떤 아름다운 여자를 납치했다는데, 그녀가 자기를 싣고 가던 마차에서 달아나려 하자, 몸싸움 중에 그가 그녀를 죽였다는 거야—아니면 그녀가 그를 죽였든가—어느 쪽인지는 잊어버렸군. 이게 그 이야기의 한 가지 판본이지… 보아하니 네 통이며 양동이가 다 꾸려져 있네. 떠나는 거지, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"네, 내일요—옛 성모영보대축일에요.\"\n\n\"떠난다는 말을 듣긴 했는데, 좀처럼 믿기지가 않았어. 너무 갑작스러워서 말이지. 왜 그런 거지?\"\n\n\"아버지가 그 집의 마지막 목숨이었는데, 그게 끊기자 우리는 더 머물 권리가 없어졌어요. 어쩌면 주 단위 세입자로는 머물 수 있었을지도 몰라요—나만 아니었다면요.\"\n\n\"네가 어떻다는 거지?\"\n\n\"나는—제대로 된 여자가 아니거든요.\"\n\n더버빌의 얼굴이 붉어졌다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을 수치스러운 노릇이 있나! 한심한 속물들 같으니! 그 더러운 영혼들이 새까맣게 타 재가 되어 버려라!\" 그가 비꼬는 듯한 분노의 어조로 외쳤다. \"그래서 떠나는 거란 말이지? 쫓겨나는 거야?\"\n\n\"꼭 쫓겨나는 건 아니에요. 하지만 곧 떠나야 할 거라고들 하니까, 다들 옮겨 가는 지금 떠나는 게 낫다고요. 더 나은 기회가 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"어디로 갈 건데?\"\n\n\"킹스비어요. 거기에 방을 얻어 뒀어요. 어머니가 아버지 집안 사람들 일이라면 어찌나 어리석으신지, 거기로 가시겠대요.\"\n\n\"하지만 네 어머니 집안은 셋방살이에는 맞지 않아, 그것도 그런 코딱지만 한 동네에서. 자, 그러지 말고 트랜트리지에 있는 내 정원 별채로 오는 게 어때? 어머니가 돌아가신 뒤로는 이제 가금류도 거의 없어. 하지만 집은 네가 알던 그대로 있고, 정원도 있어. 하루면 흰 칠을 새로 할 수 있고, 네 어머니는 거기서 아주 편안히 지낼 수 있어. 그리고 아이들은 내가 좋은 학교에 넣어 주지. 정말이지 나는 너를 위해 뭔가 해야만 해!\"\n\n\"하지만 우리는 이미 킹스비어에 방을 얻었는걸요!\" 그녀가 잘라 말했다. \"거기서 기다릴 수 있고요—\"\n\n\"기다려—뭘? 그 멋진 남편을 기다리는 거겠지, 틀림없이. 자, 이봐, 테스, 나는 남자가 어떤지 알아. 그리고 너희가 갈라선 사연을 염두에 두면, 그가 너와 화해하는 일은 결코 없으리라고 나는 아주 확신해. 자, 나는 네 적이었지만, 네가 믿지 않더라도 나는 네 친구야. 이 내 오두막으로 와. 우리가 제대로 된 닭 무리를 키워 보자고. 네 어머니가 그걸 아주 훌륭하게 돌볼 수 있을 거야. 그리고 아이들은 학교에 다니면 되고.\"\n\n테스의 숨이 점점 더 빨라졌고, 마침내 그녀가 말했다—\n\n\"당신이 이 모든 걸 해 주리란 걸 내가 어떻게 알아요? 당신 마음이 바뀔 수도 있고—그러면—우리는—내 어머니는—다시 집 없는 신세가 될 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아 아니야—아니야. 필요하다면 그런 일이 없도록 문서로 보장해 주마. 잘 생각해 봐.\"\n\n테스는 고개를 저었다. 그러나 더버빌은 끈질겼다. 그녀는 그가 그토록 단호한 것을 좀처럼 본 적이 없었다. 그는 거절을 받아들이려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"제발 네 어머니한테 말이라도 해 봐.\" 그가 힘주어 말했다. \"판단은 네가 아니라 어머니가 할 일이야. 나는 내일 아침 집을 쓸어 내고 흰 칠을 하고 불을 지펴 두게 할 거야. 그러면 저녁까지는 다 마를 테니, 너는 곧장 그리로 오면 돼. 자, 명심해, 나는 너를 기다리고 있을 거야.\"\n\n테스는 또 고개를 저었고, 그녀의 목은 복잡한 감정으로 메었다. 그녀는 더버빌을 올려다볼 수가 없었다.\n\n\"나는 지난 일로 너한테 빚진 게 있잖니, 너도 알다시피.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 너는 그 광기에서도 나를 낫게 해 줬고. 그러니 나는 기쁘구나—\"\n\n\"차라리 당신이 그 광기를 그대로 간직했더라면 좋았을걸요. 그래서 거기 딸려 있던 그 신앙생활도 함께 지켰더라면요!\"\n\n\"이 기회에 너에게 조금이나마 갚을 수 있어 기쁘다. 내일이면 네 어머니의 살림살이가 내려지는 소리를 들을 수 있겠지… 자, 그 약속의 표시로 이제 손을 다오—사랑스럽고 아름다운 테스!\"\n\n마지막 말에서 그는 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추었고, 반쯤 열린 창틀 안으로 손을 들이밀었다. 폭풍 같은 눈으로 그녀는 잽싸게 고정 막대를 잡아당겼는데, 그러는 바람에 그의 팔이 창틀과 돌 문설주 사이에 끼고 말았다.\n\n\"빌어먹을—너 참 잔인하구나!\" 그가 팔을 홱 빼내며 말했다. \"아니, 아니야!—일부러 그런 게 아니란 건 나도 알아. 좋아, 나는 너를, 아니 적어도 네 어머니와 아이들을 기다리고 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"나는 안 갈 거예요—나한테는 돈이 많이 있어요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디에?\"\n\n\"내 시아버지한테요, 청하기만 하면요.\"\n\n\"청하기만 하면이라. 하지만 너는 청하지 않을걸, 테스. 나는 너를 알아. 너는 결코 청하지 않을 거야—먼저 굶어 죽고 말겠지!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 말을 몰고 가 버렸다. 거리 모퉁이에서 바로 그는 페인트 통을 든 남자와 마주쳤는데, 그 남자가 그에게 형제들을 저버린 거냐고 물었다.\n\n\"꺼져 버려!\" 더버빌이 말했다.\n\n테스는 한참 동안 그 자리에 그대로 있었다. 그러다 문득 불의에 대한 반항의 감정이 솟구쳐, 뜨거운 눈물이 한꺼번에 밀려들며 두 눈 언저리가 부풀어 올랐다. 그녀의 남편, 다름 아닌 에인절 클레어조차도, 다른 이들처럼 그녀에게 가혹한 처사를 내렸던 것이다. 정말 그랬다! 그녀는 전에는 그런 생각을 결코 인정한 적이 없었다. 그러나 그는 정말로 그랬다! 평생에 단 한 번도—영혼 깊은 데서 그녀는 맹세할 수 있었다—그녀는 잘못을 저지르려 한 적이 없었다. 그런데도 이런 가혹한 심판들이 닥쳤다. 그녀의 죄가 무엇이었든, 그것은 고의의 죄가 아니라 부주의의 죄였는데, 어째서 그녀는 이토록 끈질기게 벌받아야 했단 말인가?\n\n그녀는 격정에 사로잡혀 손에 잡히는 첫 종잇조각을 움켜쥐고는, 다음과 같은 글을 휘갈겼다.\n\n아, 어째서 당신은 나를 그토록 끔찍하게 대했나요, 에인절! 나는 그럴 만하지 않아요. 나는 이 모든 걸 곰곰이 따져 봤고, 나는 결코, 결코 당신을 용서할 수 없어요! 내가 당신에게 잘못하려던 게 아니었음을 당신도 알면서—어째서 당신은 나에게 그토록 잘못을 저질렀나요? 당신은 잔인해요, 정말이지 잔인해요! 나는 당신을 잊으려 애쓸 거예요. 내가 당신 손에서 받은 건 죄다 불의예요! T.\n\n그녀는 우편배달부가 지나갈 때까지 지켜보다가, 편지를 들고 그에게 달려 나갔고, 그러고는 다시 창유리 안쪽 제자리에 맥없이 앉았다.\n\n그렇게 다정하게 쓰느니 차라리 이렇게 쓰는 편이 나았다. 어떻게\n그가 애원에 굴복할 것인가? 사실은 변하지 않았다. 그의 생각을 바꿔 놓을 새로운 사건은 없었다.\n\n점점 어두워졌고, 불빛이 방 안을 비추었다. 어린아이들 중 가장 큰 둘은 어머니와 함께 나갔고, 가장 어린 넷은, 세 살 반부터 열한 살까지인 그 아이들은, 모두 검은 옷을 입고 난롯가에 모여 저희끼리 사소한 이야기를 재잘거리고 있었다. 마침내 테스가 촛불도 켜지 않은 채 그들 곁에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"오늘이 우리가 여기서, 우리가 태어난 이 집에서 자는 마지막 밤이란다, 얘들아.\" 그녀가 빠르게 말했다. \"이걸 생각해 둬야 해, 그렇지 않니?\"\n\n아이들은 모두 입을 다물었다. 그 나이다운 감수성으로 그들은 그녀가 불러일으킨 종말의 그림에 금방이라도 울음을 터뜨릴 듯했다. 그날 내내 새로운 곳으로 간다는 생각에 들떠 있었으면서도. 테스는 화제를 바꾸었다.\n\n\"노래해 줘, 얘들아.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 노래 부를까?\"\n\n\"아무거나 아는 거. 난 상관없어.\"\n\n잠깐 멈춤이 있었다. 그것은 먼저 머뭇거리는 작은 한 음으로 깨졌다. 그러더니 두 번째 목소리가 그것을 받쳐 주었고, 세 번째와 네 번째가 한목소리로 어우러졌다. 주일학교에서 배운 가사로—\n\n여기서 우리는 슬픔과 고통을 겪고, 여기서 만나 다시 헤어지네. 천국에서는 더 이상 헤어지지 않으리.\n\n넷은 오래전에 그 문제를 매듭지어 버려, 거기에 틀림이 없으니 더 생각할 필요가 없다고 느끼는 사람들의 무덤덤한 수동성으로 계속 불렀다. 음절을 또박또박 내려고 얼굴을 잔뜩 긴장시킨 채 그들은 일렁이는 불의 한가운데를 계속 바라보았고, 가장 어린아이의 음이 나머지 아이들의 쉼표 사이로 흘러들었다.\n\n테스는 그들에게서 돌아서 다시 창가로 갔다. 이제 바깥은 어둠이 내렸지만, 그녀는 어둠 속을 들여다보려는 듯 유리창에 얼굴을 댔다. 사실은 눈물을 감추려는 것이었다. 아이들이 부르는 그 노래를 믿을 수만 있다면, 확신만 할 수 있다면, 지금 모든 것이 얼마나 다를까. 얼마나 마음 놓고 아이들을 신의 섭리와 그들의 장차 올 왕국에 맡길 수 있을까! 그러나 그러지 못하는 한, 그녀가 무언가를 해야 했다. 아이들의 섭리가 되어 주어야 했다. 적잖은 수백만의 다른 이들에게처럼 테스에게도, 시인의 시구에는 끔찍한 풍자가 깃들어 있었으니—\n\n완전히 헐벗은 채로가 아니라 영광의 구름을 끌며 우리는 온다.\n\n그녀와 그녀 같은 이들에게 태어남 자체가 굴욕적인 개인적 강제의 시련이었으며, 그 결과의 무엇으로도 정당화될 수 없는, 기껏해야 누그러뜨릴 수 있을 뿐인 까닭 없는 시련이었다.\n\n젖은 길의 그늘 속에서 그녀는 곧 키 큰 '라이자루와 에이브러햄과 함께 오는 어머니를 알아보았다. 더비필드 부인의 나막신이 또각거리며 문 앞까지 왔고, 테스가 문을 열었다.\n\n\"창밖에 말 발자국이 보이는구나.\" 조앤이 말했다. \"누가 다녀갔니?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n난롯가의 아이들이 그녀를 심각하게 쳐다보았고, 하나가 중얼거렸다—\n\n\"왜, 테스, 말 탄 신사분 말이야!\"\n\n\"다녀간 건 아니에요.\" 테스가 말했다. \"지나가다 제게 말을 걸었어요.\"\n\n\"그 신사가 누구였니?\" 어머니가 물었다. \"네 남편이냐?\"\n\n\"아니요. 그이는 절대로, 절대로 오지 않을 거예요.\" 테스가 돌처럼 굳은 절망 속에서 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 누구였느냐?\"\n\n\"아, 물으실 거 없어요. 어머니도 전에 본 적 있고, 저도 봤어요.\"\n\n\"아! 뭐라고 하더냐?\" 조앤이 궁금해하며 말했다.\n\n\"내일 킹스비어 숙소에 자리를 잡으면 말씀드릴게요—한마디 한마디 다요.\"\n\n그녀의 남편은 아니라고, 그녀는 말했었다. 그러나 육체적 의미에서는 오직 이 남자만이 자기 남편이라는 의식이 점점 더 무겁게 그녀를 짓누르는 듯했다.\n\n# LII\n\n다음 날 아침 아직 어두운 새벽녘에, 큰길 가까이 사는 이들은 덜컹거리는 소음에 밤잠이 깨지는 것을 느꼈다. 그 소리는 날이 밝을 때까지 띄엄띄엄 이어졌다—이달 첫째 주에는 어김없이 되풀이되는 소음으로, 같은 달 셋째 주의 뻐꾸기 소리만큼이나 확실한 것이었다. 그것은 대규모 이사의 전조였으니, 이주하는 가족들의 짐을 실으러 가는 빈 마차와 마부들이 지나가는 소리였다. 고용된 일꾼은 늘 자기 일손을 필요로 하는 농장주의 마차로 목적지까지 옮겨졌기 때문이다. 이 일을 하루 안에 끝내야 한다는 사실이, 자정 직후부터 그 울림이 시작되는 까닭을 설명해 주었다. 마부들의 목표는 여섯 시까지 이사 나가는 집들의 문 앞에 닿는 것이었고, 그때 곧장 짐 싣기가 시작되었다.\n\n그러나 테스와 그녀 어머니의 살림에는 그런 조바심 내는 농장주가 마차를 보내 주지 않았다. 그들은 여자뿐이었고, 정규 일꾼도 아니었으며, 어디서도 딱히 필요로 하지 않았다. 그래서 그들은 제 돈으로 마차를 빌려야 했고, 거저 보내 주는 것은 하나도 없었다.\n\n그날 아침 창밖을 내다보았을 때, 날씨가 바람 불고 잔뜩 흐리긴 해도 비가 내리지 않고 마차가 도착한 것을 알게 된 것은 테스에게 다행이었다. 비 내리는 성모영보 대축일은 이사하는 가족들이 결코 잊지 못하는 유령 같은 것이었다. 축축한 가구, 축축한 침구, 축축한 옷가지가 따라붙어 온갖 병폐의 행렬을 남겼다.\n\n그녀의 어머니와 '라이자루와 에이브러햄도 깨어 있었지만, 어린아이들은 그대로 자게 두었다. 넷은 희미한 빛 속에서 아침을 먹었고, \"집 비우기\"에 착수했다.\n\n그 일은 어느 정도 활기차게 진행되었으니, 친절한 이웃 한둘이 거들었다. 큰 가구들이 자리 잡아 실리고 나자, 침대와 침구로 둥근 둥지를 만들었고, 그 안에 조앤 더비필드와 어린아이들이 여정 내내 앉아 가기로 했다. 짐을 다 싣고 나서 말들을 데려오기까지 오랜 지체가 있었으니, 짐을 빼는 동안 말들의 마구를 풀어 놓았던 탓이었다. 하지만 마침내 두 시쯤 모든 것이 길에 올랐다. 냄비가 마차 차축에 매달려 흔들렸고, 더비필드 부인과 식구들이 맨 위에 앉았으며, 그 부인은 시계 부속이 상하지 않도록 시계 머리를 무릎에 안고 있었는데, 마차가 유난히 출렁일 때마다 그 시계가 한 시, 또는 한 시 반을 다친 듯한 소리로 쳤다. 테스와 그 다음으로 큰 딸은 마을을 벗어날 때까지 마차 옆에서 걸었다.\n\n그들은 그날 아침과 전날 저녁에 이웃 몇 집을 찾아갔고, 더러는 배웅을 나와 모두 잘되기를 빌어 주었다. 비록 속마음으로는 그런 가족에게 행복이 가능하리라곤 거의 기대하지 않았지만 말이다. 더비필드네는 저들 자신을 빼고는 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않는 이들이었으니. 곧 마차 행렬이 더 높은 지대로 오르기 시작했고, 지형과 토양이 바뀌면서 바람이 더 매서워졌다.\n\n그날이 사월 엿새였으므로, 더비필드네 마차는 짐 꼭대기에 식구들을 태운 다른 마차들을 많이 만났다. 그 짐들은 거의 변함없는 원칙에 따라 쌓여 있었는데, 아마도 벌에게 육각형이 그러하듯 시골 일꾼에게 고유한 방식이었을 것이다. 짐 쌓기의 바탕은 집안의 찬장으로, 번쩍이는 손잡이와 손때 자국과 살림의 흔적이 잔뜩 묻은 그것이, 끌채 말들의 꽁무니 위로 똑바로 자연스러운 자세로 앞쪽에 위풍당당하게 서 있었으니, 마치 경건히 모시고 가야 할 어떤 언약궤 같았다.\n\n어떤 살림들은 활기찼고 어떤 것은 침울했다. 더러는 길가 여관 문 앞에 멈춰 서 있었는데, 거기서 때가 되자 더비필드네 짐승 무리도 말에게 먹이를 주고 길손들을 쉬게 하려고 멈춰 섰다. 멈춰 선 동안 테스의 눈에 삼 파인트짜리 파란 머그잔이 들어왔는데, 그것이 같은 여관에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 역시 멈춰 선 어느 살림의 짐 꼭대기에 앉은 여자들 쪽으로 오르내리며 공중을 오가고 있었다. 그녀는 그 머그잔이 위로 올라가는 한 번의 여정을 눈으로 좇다가, 그것을 움켜쥔 손의 주인을 자기가 잘 안다는 걸 알아챘다. 테스는 그 마차로 다가갔다.\n\n\"마리안과 이즈!\" 그녀가 두 처녀에게 소리쳤다. 정말 그들이었으니, 자기들이 묵던 집의 이사 가는 가족과 함께 앉아 있었다. \"너희도 오늘 다들처럼 집 비우는 거야?\"\n\n그렇다고, 그들이 말했다. 플린트콤애시에서의 삶은 그들에게 너무 거칠었고, 그래서 거의 통지도 없이 떠나왔다고, 그로비가 원한다면 자기들을 고소하라고 내버려 둔 채 말이다. 그들이 테스에게 자기들 목적지를 말했고, 테스도 자기 목적지를 말해 주었다.\n\n마리안이 짐 너머로 몸을 기울이며 목소리를 낮추었다. \"너를 따라다니는 그 신사 말이야—누군지 짐작하겠지—네가 떠난 뒤에 플린트콤으로 너를 찾으러 왔던 거 알아? 우린 네가 그 사람 보기 싫어할 줄 알고 어디 있는지 말 안 했어.\"\n\n\"아—그런데 난 그 사람을 봤어!\" 테스가 중얼거렸다. \"그이가 나를 찾아냈어.\"\n\n\"그럼 네가 어디로 가는지도 알아?\"\n\n\"그럴 거야.\"\n\n\"남편은 돌아왔어?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n그녀는 아는 이들에게 작별을 고했다—각자의 마부들이 이제 여관에서 나왔으므로—그리고 두 마차는 서로 반대 방향으로 여정을 다시 시작했다. 마리안과 이즈와 그들이 운명을 함께하기로 한 농부의 가족이 탄 마차는 산뜻하게 칠해져 있었고, 마구에 번쩍이는 놋쇠 장식을 단 힘센 말 세 마리가 끌었다. 반면 더비필드 부인과 식구들이 탄 마차는 그 위에 얹힌 짐 무게를 겨우 견딜까 말까 한 삐걱대는 구조물이었으니, 만들어진 이래 한 번도 칠을 해 본 적 없고 말 두 마리만이 끄는 것이었다. 그 대조는 번창하는 농장주가 데리러 와 주는 것과, 고용주가 와 주기를 기다리지 않는 곳으로 제 힘으로 옮겨 가는 것 사이의 차이를 잘 드러냈다.\n\n거리는 멀었다—하루 여정으로는 너무 멀었다—말들은 그것을 더없이 힘겹게 해냈다. 그토록 일찍 떠났는데도, 그린힐이라 불리는 고지의 일부를 이루는 한 언덕의 옆구리를 돌아갈 무렵에는 이미 오후가 한참 깊어 있었다. 말들이 멈춰 서서 오줌을 누고 숨을 고르는 동안 테스는 주위를 둘러보았다. 언덕 아래, 바로 그들 앞쪽에 그들이 순례해 온 반쯤 죽은 작은 읍이 있었다. 킹스비어, 아버지가 아프도록 이야기하고 노래하던 그 조상들이 누워 있는 곳이었다. 킹스비어, 더버빌가가 꼬박 오백 년을 살았으니 세상의 온갖 장소 중에서도 더버빌가의 본가라 할 수 있을 바로 그곳이었다.\n\n한 남자가 변두리에서 그들 쪽으로 다가오는 것이 보였는데, 그들 마차에 실린 짐의 성격을 알아보고는 걸음을 재촉했다.\n\n\"댁이 더비필드 부인이라고 부르는 그 여자분이지요, 아마?\" 그가 테스의 어머니에게 말했다. 어머니는 남은 길을 걸으려고 마차에서 내려 있었다.\n\n그녀는 고개를 끄덕였다. \"내 권리를 따진다면야 작고하신 존 더버빌 경의, 가엾은 귀족 어른의 미망인이지요. 그리고 그분 선조들의 영지로 돌아가는 길이고요.\"\n\n\"오? 글쎄, 그건 난 아무것도 모르겠고요. 다만 댁이 더비필드 부인이라면, 원하시던 방들이 다 나갔다고 전하라는 심부름을 왔습니다. 오늘 아침 편지를 받기 전까진 오시는 줄도 몰랐어요—그땐 이미 너무 늦었고요. 하지만 어디 다른 숙소야 분명 구하실 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n남자는 그 소식에 잿빛으로 창백해진 테스의 얼굴을 알아챘다. 그녀의 어머니는 어쩔 줄 모르고 막막한 표정이었다. \"이제 어쩌면 좋니, 테스?\" 그녀가 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"네 조상들의 땅에서 받는 환영이 이 꼴이라니! 아무튼 더 알아보자꾸나.\"\n\n그들은 읍내로 들어가 온 힘을 다해 알아보았는데, 테스는 어머니와 '라이자루가 알아보러 다니는 동안 마차에 남아 아이들을 돌보았다. 한 시간쯤 뒤 조앤이 마지막으로 마차에 돌아왔을 때, 숙소를 구하려던 그녀의 노력은 여전히 헛수고였고, 마차 마부는 말들이 반쯤 죽어 가니 짐을 내려야 한다고, 적어도 오늘 밤 안에 길 일부는 되돌아가야 한다고 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요—여기에 내려요.\" 조앤이 자포자기하듯 말했다. \"어디든 잘 데를 구하지요.\"\n\n마차는 묘지 담장 아래, 사람 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에 멈춰 섰고, 마부는 마다하지 않고 곧 그 초라한 살림 무더기를 끌어내렸다. 그러고 나자 그녀는 삯을 치렀는데, 그 바람에 거의 마지막 한 실링까지 털어 버린 셈이었다. 그는 그런 가족과 더 엮이지 않게 되어 더없이 기뻐하며 마차를 몰아 그들을 두고 떠났다. 마른 밤이었고, 그는 그들이 별 탈 없으리라 짐작했다.\n\n테스는 가구 더미를 절망스레 바라보았다. 이 봄날 저녁의 차가운 햇빛이 항아리와 주전자 위로, 산들바람에 떨고 있는 마른 약초 다발 위로, 찬장의 놋쇠 손잡이 위로, 그들 모두가 흔들리며 잠들었던 버들가지 요람 위로, 잘 닦인 시계 상자 위로 심술궂게 들여다보았다. 그것들은 모두 지붕 없는 노출의 변덕에—본디 그러라고 만들어진 게 아닌 그 노출에—내버려진 실내 물건들이 내뿜는 원망스러운 빛을 발했다. 둘레에는 울타리를 걷어 낸 언덕과 비탈이—이제는 작은 목초지로 잘게 나뉜—펼쳐져 있었고, 더버빌 저택이 한때 서 있던 자리를 보여 주는 푸른 주춧돌도 있었다. 또 늘 그 영지에 속해 있던 에그던 황야의 외진 한 자락도 보였다. 바로 곁에는 더버빌 곁복도라 불리는 교회의 곁복도가 태연하게 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n\"네 집안 납골당은 우리 소유 아니냐?\" 테스의 어머니가 교회와 묘지를 둘러보고 돌아오며 말했다. \"그럼, 당연히 그렇지, 그러니 거기다 야영을 하자꾸나, 얘들아, 네 조상들의 땅이 우리에게 지붕을 마련해 줄 때까지! 자, 테스와 '라이자와 에이브러햄, 너희가 날 도와라. 이 아이들을 위해 둥지를 만들고, 그다음에 다시 한 바퀴 둘러보자.\"\n\n테스는 맥없이 거들었고, 십오 분 만에 그 낡은 네 기둥 침대가 짐 무더기에서 떼어져, 교회 남쪽 벽 아래에, 곧 더버빌 곁복도라 불리는 건물 부분 아래에 세워졌다. 그 아래에는 거대한 납골당이 자리하고 있었다. 침대의 닫집 위로는 여러 칸으로 된 아름다운 격자무늬 창이 있었는데, 만들어진 연대는 십오 세기였다. 그것은 더버빌 창이라 불렸고, 윗부분에는 더비필드네의 낡은 인장과 숟가락에 새겨진 것과 같은 문장(紋章)들을 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n조앤은 침대 둘레로 커튼을 쳐 그것을 훌륭한 천막으로 만들고, 더 어린아이들을 그 안에 넣었다. \"최악의 경우엔 우리도 거기서 자면 되지, 하룻밤쯤이야.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 더 가서 알아보자꾸나, 그리고 얘들 먹을 거라도 좀 구하고! 오, 테스, 네가 신사와 결혼하는 시늉을 한 게 우릴 이 꼴로 만들어 놓는다면 무슨 소용이 있니!\"\n\n'라이자루와 사내아이를 데리고 그녀는 교회를 작은 읍에서 갈라 놓는 그 작은 오솔길을 다시 올라갔다. 거리로 들어서자마자 그들은 말을 탄 채 위아래를 두리번거리는 한 남자를 보았다. \"아—내가 당신들을 찾고 있었소!\" 그가 말을 몰고 다가오며 말했다. \"이거야말로 유서 깊은 곳에서의 가족 상봉이로군!\"\n\n알렉 더버빌이었다. \"테스는 어디 있소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n개인적으로 조앤은 알렉을 좋아하지 않았다. 그녀는 건성으로 교회 방향을 가리키고는 가던 길을 갔고, 더버빌은 그들이 잘 곳을 찾는 데 여전히 실패했을 경우—방금 그 소식을 들었다며—다시 보러 오겠다고 했다. 그들이 가 버리자 더버빌은 말을 타고 여관으로 갔고, 곧이어 걸어서 나왔다.\n\n그동안 테스는 침대 안의 아이들과 함께 남아 잠시 그들과 이야기를 나누다가, 지금 당장은 아이들을 더 편하게 해 줄 수 없음을 알고는 이제 어스름의 그늘로 거뭇해지기 시작하는 묘지를 거닐었다. 교회 문은 잠겨 있지 않았고, 그녀는 난생처음 그 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n침대가 서 있는 그 창 아래에는 가문의 무덤들이 있었는데, 그 연대가 여러 세기에 걸쳐 있었다. 그것들은 닫집이 덮인, 제단 모양의 소박한 무덤들이었다. 조각은 훼손되고 부서졌으며, 놋쇠 장식은 틀에서 뜯겨 나가 리벳 구멍만 사질 절벽의 흰털발제비 둥지 구멍처럼 남아 있었다. 자기 집안사람들이 사회적으로 멸문되었음을 일깨워 준 그 모든 표지 가운데, 이 약탈만큼 강렬한 것은 없었다.\n\n그녀는 이렇게 새겨진 어두운 돌에 다가갔다—\n\n# OSTIUM SEPULCHRI ANTIQUAE FAMILIAE D’URBERVILLE\n\n더버빌 고가(古家)의 묘소 입구\n테스는 추기경처럼 교회 라틴어를 읽지는 못했지만, 이것이 자기 조상의 묘소 입구이며, 아버지가 술에 취해 읊조리던 그 키 큰 기사들이 그 안에 누워 있음은 알았다.\n\n그녀는 생각에 잠겨 물러나려 돌아서다가, 그 모든 것 중에서도 가장 오래된 제단 무덤 곁을 지나게 되었는데, 그 위에는 누운 형상이 하나 있었다. 어스름 속에서 그녀는 그것을 미처 알아보지 못했었고, 그 조각상이 움직인다는 묘한 착각이 아니었다면 지금도 거의 알아보지 못했을 것이다. 가까이 다가가자마자 그녀는 순식간에 그 형상이 살아 있는 사람임을 알아챘다. 그리고 혼자가 아니었다는 충격이 어찌나 격렬했던지 그녀는 완전히 압도되어 거의 기절할 지경으로 주저앉았는데, 다만 그 형상에서 알렉 더버빌을 알아본 다음의 일이었다.\n\n그는 석판에서 뛰어내려 그녀를 부축했다.\n\n\"당신이 들어오는 걸 봤소.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신의 명상을 방해하지 않으려고 저 위로 올라가 있었지. 가족 상봉 아니오, 여기 우리 발밑에 이 양반들까지 있으니? 들어 보시오.\"\n\n그가 뒤꿈치로 바닥을 묵직하게 굴렀더니, 아래에서 텅 빈 메아리가 울려 나왔다.\n\n\"저 양반들 좀 흔들어 놓았겠지, 장담하건대!\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 내가 그저 저들 중 하나의 돌로 만든 모형인 줄 알았겠지. 하지만 아니오. 옛 질서는 변하는 법. 가짜 더버빌의 새끼손가락 하나가 당신을 위해, 저 밑에 있는 진짜의 왕조 전체보다 더 많은 걸 해 줄 수 있소…… 자, 내게 명령하시오. 내가 무얼 하면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"가 버려요!\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그러지—당신 어머니를 찾으러 가겠소.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다. 그러나 그녀 곁을 지나며 그가 속삭였다. \"이걸 명심해. 당신은 결국 고분고분해질 거요!\"\n\n그가 가 버리자 그녀는 납골당 입구 위로 몸을 숙이고 말했다—\n\n\"왜 나는 이 문의 잘못된 쪽에 있는 걸까!\"\n\n그러는 동안 마리안과 이즈 휴엇은 농부의 세간을 싣고 자기들의 가나안 땅을 향해—바로 그날 아침에야 다른 어떤 가족이 떠나온 이집트를 향해—계속 나아가고 있었다. 하지만 두 처녀는 한참 동안 자기들이 어디로 가는지 생각하지 않았다. 그들의 이야기는 에인절 클레어와 테스, 그리고 테스를 끈질기게 따라다니는 연인에 관한 것이었으니, 그자가 테스의 지난 내력과 맺은 관계는 그들이 그전에 일부는 듣고 일부는 짐작하고 있던 터였다.\n\n\"그 여자가 그 사람을 전에 전혀 몰랐던 것도 아니잖아.\" 마리안이 말했다. \"한번 그 여자를 차지했던 적이 있다는 게 천지 차이를 만드는 거지. 그자가 그 여자를 또 꾀어내 간다면 그건 천 번이라도 안타까운 일일 거야. 클레어 씨는 우리에겐 절대 아무것도 될 수 없어, 이즈. 그런데 우리가 왜 그 사람을 그 여자한테 못 가게 시샘하고, 이 다툼을 고쳐 보려 하지 않는 거지? 그 여자가 어떤 궁지에 몰려 있는지, 무엇이 주위를 맴돌고 있는지 그 사람이 알기만 한다면, 자기 사람을 챙기러 올지도 몰라.\"\n\n\"우리가 그 사람한테 알려 줄 수 있을까?\"\n\n그들은 목적지에 닿을 때까지 줄곧 이것을 생각했다. 하지만 새 거처에 다시 자리 잡느라 분주한 통에 그때는 온통 거기에 정신이 팔렸다. 그러나 한 달 뒤 자리를 잡고 나서, 그들은 클레어가 곧 돌아온다는 소식을 들었다. 비록 테스에 관해서는 더 이상 아무것도 알아내지 못했지만 말이다. 그러자 그에 대한 애정으로 다시금 마음이 일렁이면서도, 그녀에게는 의리를 지키려는 마음에서, 마리안이 둘이 함께 쓰는 일 페니짜리 잉크병 마개를 뽑았고, 두 처녀 사이에서 몇 줄이 지어졌다.\n\n존경하는 선생님, 당신의 부인이 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 당신도 그분을 사랑하신다면, 부인을 살피세요. 그분은 친구의 탈을 쓴 한 원수에게 호되게 시달리고 있으니까요. 선생님, 그분 가까이에 마땅히 멀리 있어야 할 자가 하나 있어요. 여자는 제 힘에 부치도록 시험받아서는 안 되며, 끊임없이 떨어지는 물방울이 돌을 닳게 하지요—아니, 그 이상이지요—다이아몬드를요. 두 사람의 진심 어린 벗으로부터\n\n이것은 그들이 클레어와 연관이 있다고 들어 본 단 한 곳, 에민스터 사제관으로 에인절 클레어 앞으로 부쳐졌다. 그러고 나서 그들은 자기들의 너그러움에 감정이 한껏 고조된 상태에 빠져, 발작하듯 띄엄띄엄 노래하면서 동시에 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n# LIII\n\n에민스터 사제관의 저녁이었다. 늘 켜 두는 두 자루의 촛불이 사제의 서재에서 초록 갓 아래 타고 있었지만, 그는 거기 앉아 있지 않았다. 이따금 그는 들어와 점점 온화해지는 봄에 그만하면 족한 작은 불을 뒤적이고는 다시 나갔다. 때로는 현관문 앞에 멈춰 서고, 응접실로 갔다가, 다시 현관문으로 돌아오곤 했다.\n\n그것은 서쪽을 향하고 있었고, 안에는 어둠이 깔렸어도 바깥은 아직 또렷이 볼 만큼 빛이 남아 있었다. 응접실에 앉아 있던 클레어 부인이 그를 따라 이리로 왔다.\n\n\"아직 시간 많아요.\" 사제가 말했다. \"기차가 제시간에 와도 그 애가 초크뉴턴에 닿는 건 여섯 시고, 시골길 십 마일을, 그중 오 마일은 크리머크록 길인데, 우리 늙은 말이 서둘러 덜컹덜컹 달릴 수 있는 거리가 아니잖소.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 애는 우리랑 한 시간 만에 온 적도 있어요, 여보.\"\n\n\"몇 년 전이지.\"\n\n이렇게 그들은 시간을 흘려보냈는데, 둘 다 이것이 그저 숨만 낭비하는 일이며 꼭 해야 할 단 하나는 그저 기다리는 것뿐임을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n마침내 오솔길에서 가벼운 소리가 났고, 정말로 그 낡은 조랑말 마차가 난간 바깥에 나타났다. 그들은 거기서 내리는 한 형상을 보았는데, 그를 알아보는 척했지만, 마침 어떤 특정한 사람이 올 시각에 그가 자기들 마차에서 내리지 않았더라면 거리에서 마주쳐도 알아보지 못한 채 지나쳤을 것이다.\n\n클레어 부인이 어두운 통로를 지나 문으로 달려갔고, 그녀의 남편이 더 천천히 뒤따라왔다.\n\n막 들어서려던 그 새 도착자는 문간에 선 그들의 근심 어린 얼굴과, 그들이 저무는 햇살의 마지막 빛을 마주하고 있던 까닭에 그들의 안경에 어린 서쪽 하늘의 광채를 보았다. 하지만 그들은 빛을 등진 그의 형체만을 볼 수 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"오, 내 아들, 내 아들아—드디어 다시 집에 왔구나!\" 클레어 부인이 외쳤다. 그 순간 그녀는 이 모든 이별의 원인이 된 이단의 오점 따위는, 그의 옷에 묻은 먼지만큼도 마음에 두지 않았다. 진리의 가장 충실한 신봉자라 한들, 어떤 여인이 말씀의 약속과 위협을 제 자식을 믿는 그 의미만큼 믿으며, 제 자식의 행복과 견주어 저울질할 때 제 신학을 바람에 내던지지 않을 것인가? 촛불이 켜진 방에 들어서자마자 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"오, 이건 에인절이 아니야—내 아들이 아니야—떠나갔던 그 에인절이 아니야!\" 그녀가 슬픔의 온갖 역설을 담아 외치며 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n그의 아버지 역시 그를 보고 충격을 받았으니, 클레어가 겪은 근심과 험한 계절로 인해 그 모습이 예전의 윤곽에서 그토록 깎여 나가 있었다. 그가 집안일의 비웃음에 처음 진저리치며 그토록 무모하게 서둘러 떠난 그 기후 속에서 말이다. 사람 뒤에 해골이, 그리고 거의 해골 뒤에 유령이 비치는 듯했다. 그는 크리벨리가 그린 죽은 그리스도와 닮아 있었다. 움푹 꺼진 눈구멍은 병색을 띠었고, 눈 속의 빛은 사그라들어 있었다. 늙은 조상들의 모난 움푹함과 주름이 제 차례보다 이십 년 일찍 그의 얼굴에 군림하기 시작했다.\n\n\"제가 거기서 앓았어요, 아시잖아요.\" 그가 말했다. \"이젠 다 괜찮아요.\"\n\n하지만 그 말이 거짓임을 증명하기라도 하듯, 그의 다리가 풀리는 듯하더니 그는 쓰러지지 않으려고 갑자기 자리에 앉았다. 그저 가벼운 현기증 발작이었으니, 지루한 하루 여정과 도착의 흥분에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n\"최근에 제게 온 편지 없었나요?\" 그가 물었다. \"지난번에 보내 주신 건 순전히 우연으로, 내륙에 있던 탓에 한참 늦어서야 받았어요. 안 그랬으면 더 일찍 올 수도 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"네 아내한테서 온 거였지, 아마?\"\n\n\"맞아요.\"\n\n최근에 단 한 통이 더 와 있었다. 그들은 그가 곧 집으로 출발할 줄 알고 그것을 그에게 부쳐 보내지 않은 터였다.\n\n그는 꺼내 준 편지를 황급히 뜯었고, 테스의 필체로 적힌, 그녀가 마지막으로 급히 휘갈겨 보낸 그 심정을 읽고는 몹시 동요했다.\n\n오 왜 당신은 저를 그토록 끔찍하게 대하셨나요, 에인절! 저는 그럴 만한 짓을 하지 않았어요. 저는 이 모든 걸 곰곰이 생각해 봤고, 당신을 결코, 결코 용서할 수 없어요! 제가 당신을 해칠 뜻이 없었다는 걸 아시잖아요—그런데 왜 저를 그토록 해치셨나요? 당신은 잔인해요, 정말 잔인해요! 저는 당신을 잊으려 애쓰겠어요. 제가 당신 손에서 받은 건 온통 부당함뿐이에요! T.\n\n\"정말이지 맞는 말이야!\" 에인절이 편지를 내던지며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그 사람은 영영 나와 화해하지 않을지도 몰라!\"\n\n\"에인절, 한낱 흙의 자식 때문에 그렇게 애태우지 마라!\" 그의 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"흙의 자식이라뇨! 글쎄요, 우리 모두가 흙의 자식이지요. 어머니가 말씀하시는 그런 의미에서 그 사람이 흙의 자식이면 좋겠어요. 하지만 이제 제가 전에 한 번도 설명한 적 없는 걸 말씀드릴게요. 그 사람 아버지는 우리 마을에서 눈에 띄지 않는 농사꾼의 삶을 사는 적잖은 다른 이들처럼, 가장 오래된 노르만 가문 중 하나의 남계 후손이고, '흙의 자식'이라는 별명을 얻은 사람이라는 걸요.\"\n\n그는 곧 잠자리에 들었고, 다음 날 아침 몹시 몸이 좋지 않아 방에 남아 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다. 그가 테스를 두고 떠나온 정황은, 적도 남쪽에 있으면서 그녀의 다정한 편지를 막 받았을 때만 해도 마음만 먹으면 용서하는 그 순간 당장 그녀의 품으로 달려가는 것이 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일처럼 보였건만, 막상 도착하고 보니 보였던 만큼 그리 쉽지 않은 그런 것이었다. 그녀는 열정적인 사람이었고, 그의 지체로 인해 그에 대한 평가가 달라졌음을 보여 주는—너무도 마땅하게 달라졌다고 그는 슬프게 인정했다—그녀의 지금 편지는, 그녀의 부모가 보는 앞에서 그녀를 예고 없이 마주하는 것이 현명한 일일까 자문하게 만들었다. 이별의 마지막 몇 주 동안 그녀의 사랑이 정말로 혐오로 변했다면, 갑작스러운 만남은 모진 말로 이어질지도 몰랐다.\n\n그래서 클레어는 자기가 돌아왔음을 알리고, 자기가 영국을 떠날 때 그녀에게 그러기로 정해 두었던 대로 그녀가 여전히 그곳에서 식구들과 살고 있기를 바란다는 한 줄을 말럿으로 보내, 테스와 그 식구들에게 마음의 준비를 시키는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그는 바로 그날 그 문의를 부쳤고, 그 주가 다 가기 전에 더비필드 부인에게서 짤막한 답장이 왔는데, 그것은 그의 곤혹을 풀어 주지 못했다. 주소가 적혀 있지 않은 데다, 놀랍게도 말럿에서 쓴 것이 아니었기 때문이다.\n\n선생님, 이 몇 줄을 적어 알리자면 제 딸은 지금 저와 떨어져 있고, 언제 돌아올지도 확실치 않으나, 돌아오는 대로 알려 드리겠습니다. 그 애가 지금 어디 임시로 머무는지는 제가 말씀드릴 처지가 못 됩니다. 저와 제 식구는 한동안 말럿을 떠나 있다는 것만 말씀드립니다.— 이만 줄입니다, J. 더비필드\n\n테스가 적어도 겉으로는 잘 지내고 있다는 걸 알게 된 것이 클레어에게는 어찌나 큰 안도였던지, 딸의 행방에 대한 어머니의 딱딱한 함구가 그를 오래 괴롭히지는 않았다. 그들 모두가 그에게 화가 나 있는 게 분명했다. 더비필드 부인이 테스의 귀가를 알려 줄 수 있을 때까지 그는 기다리기로 했고, 그녀의 편지로 보아 그것은 곧일 듯했다. 그는 그 이상을 누릴 자격이 없었다. 그의 사랑은 \"변화를 발견하면 변하는\" 사랑이었던 것이다. 그는 부재중에 몇 가지 기이한 경험을 했다. 그는 문자 그대로의 코르넬리아 속에서 사실상의 파우스티나를, 육신의 프리네 속에서 영혼의 루크레티아를 보았다. 그는 사람들에게 붙들려 가운데 세워진, 돌로 쳐 죽임당해 마땅하다던 그 여인을 생각했고, 왕비가 된 우리아의 아내를 생각했다. 그리고 자신이 왜 테스를 행적에 따라가 아니라 의지에 따라, 행위에 따라가 아니라 그 뜻에 따라 헤아리지 못했는가를 자문했다.\n\n조앤 더비필드가 약속한 두 번째 쪽지를 기다리며, 또 간접적으로 기력을 조금 더 회복하며 아버지 집에서 지내는 동안 하루 이틀이 지났다. 기력은 돌아올 기미를 보였지만, 조앤의 편지는 올 기미가 없었다. 그러자 그는 브라질로 부쳐졌던 그 옛 편지를, 곧 테스가 플린트콤애시에서 써 보낸 그 편지를 찾아내 다시 읽었다. 그 문장들은 처음 정독했을 때만큼이나 지금도 그의 마음을 울렸다.\n\n……저는 제 괴로움 속에서 당신께 부르짖을 수밖에 없어요—제겐 달리 아무도 없으니까요!…… 당신이 곧 오시거나 제게 오라고 말씀해 주시지 않으면 저는 죽고 말 것 같아요…… 제발, 제발, 옳고 그름을 따지지만 마시고—그저 제게 조금만 다정해 주세요…… 당신이 와 주신다면, 저는 당신 품에서 죽을 수도 있어요! 만약 당신이 저를 용서해 주신 거라면 저는 기꺼이 그렇게 하겠어요!…… 당신이 제게 단 한 줄이라도 보내, 곧 가겠다, 라고 말씀해 주신다면, 저는 견디며 기다리겠어요, 에인절—오, 그토록 기쁘게요!…… 당신을 영영—영영 못 본다는 게 제 마음을 얼마나 아프게 하는지 생각해 주세요! 아, 제가 매일같이, 온종일 내내 느끼는 그 아픔을 당신의 소중한 가슴도 하루에 단 일 분만이라도 느끼게 할 수 있다면, 그게 당신으로 하여금 가엾고 외로운 당신의 사람에게 연민을 보이게 할지도 모르는데…… 저는 당신의 아내로는 못 되더라도 당신의 하인으로 함께 사는 거라면 만족하겠어요, 아니, 기쁘겠어요. 그저 당신 곁에 있을 수만 있다면, 당신을 언뜻언뜻 볼 수만 있다면, 당신을 제 사람이라 여길 수만 있다면요…… 저는 하늘에서도 땅에서도 땅 밑에서도 오직 한 가지만을 바라요, 당신을 만나는 것, 나의 소중한 사람이여! 제게 와 주세요—제게 와 주세요, 그리고 저를 위협하는 것에서 구해 주세요!\n\n클레어는 그를 향한 그녀의 더 최근의, 더 모진 마음을 더 이상 믿지 않고, 당장 가서 그녀를 찾기로 결심했다. 그는 아버지에게 자기가 없는 동안 그녀가 돈을 청한 적이 있는지 물었다. 아버지는 없다고 답했고, 그제야 에인절은 처음으로, 그녀의 자존심이 길을 가로막아 그녀가 궁핍을 겪었음을 깨달았다. 그의 말에서 그의 부모는 이제 이별의 진짜 까닭을 알아챘다. 그리고 그들의 기독교 신앙은 타락한 자들을 각별히 보살피는 것이었기에, 그녀의 혈통도, 그녀의 순박함도, 심지어 그녀의 가난도 불러일으키지 못했던 테스에 대한 따뜻함이 그녀의 죄로 인해 곧장 일깨워졌다.\n\n여정에 쓸 몇 가지를 황급히 챙기던 그는, 역시 최근에 손에 들어온 한 통의 초라하고 수수한 편지를—마리안과 이즈 휴엇에게서 온, 이렇게 시작하는 그 편지를 훑어보았다—\n\n\"존경하는 선생님, 당신의 부인이 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 당신도 그분을 사랑하신다면, 부인을 살피세요.\" 그리고 \"두 사람의 진심 어린 벗으로부터\"라고 서명된 편지였다.\n\n# LIV\n\n십오 분 만에 클레어는 집을 나섰고, 그의 어머니는 거리로 사라지는 그의 야윈 모습을 지켜보았다. 그는 아버지의 늙은 암말을 빌리기를 마다했으니, 그것이 집안에 꼭 필요함을 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그는 여관으로 가서 이륜마차를 빌렸는데, 마구를 채우는 동안 도무지 기다릴 수가 없을 지경이었다. 그러고 나서 몇 분 지나지 않아, 그는 그해 서너 달 전에 테스가 그토록 큰 희망을 안고 내려왔다가 그토록 산산조각 난 뜻을 품고 올라갔던 그 읍에서 언덕을 올라 마차를 몰고 있었다.\n\n곧 벤빌 길이 그 앞에 펼쳐졌는데, 그 산울타리와 나무들이 움튼 싹으로 자줏빛이었다. 하지만 그는 다른 것들을 바라보고 있었고, 그저 길을 잃지 않을 만큼만 정신을 그 풍경으로 되돌릴 뿐이었다. 한 시간 반이 채 안 되어 그는 킹스힌톡 영지의 남쪽을 끼고 돌아, 십자가 손 고개의 불길한 고독 속으로 올라섰다. 그것은 알렉 더버빌이 개심하던 변덕 속에서 테스에게, 다시는 일부러 자기를 유혹하지 않겠노라는 그 기묘한 맹세를 시켰던 그 부정한 돌이었다. 작년의 희끄무레하게 시든 쐐기풀 줄기들이 지금도 둑에 헐벗은 채 남아 있었고, 올봄의 어린 초록 쐐기풀이 그 뿌리에서 돋아나고 있었다.\n\n거기서 그는 다른 힌톡들을 굽어보는 고지의 가장자리를 따라가다가, 오른쪽으로 돌아, 플린트콤애시의 상쾌한 석회질 지대로 뛰어들었다. 그녀가 그에게 보낸 편지 중 하나에 적었던 그 주소요, 그가 그녀의 어머니가 말한 임시 거처라고 짐작했던 곳이었다. 물론 그는 여기서 그녀를 찾지 못했다. 그리고 그의 우울을 더한 것은, 마을 사람들도 농장주 자신도 \"클레어 부인\"이라는 이름은 들어 본 적이 없다는 사실을 알게 된 일이었다. 테스는 세례명으로는 충분히 잘 기억되고 있었는데도 말이다. 이별하는 동안 그녀가 그의 성을 분명 한 번도 쓰지 않은 것이었고, 둘이 완전히 갈라섰다는 그녀의 의연한 의식은, 이 절제로써도 못지않게 드러났지만, 그녀가 아버지에게 더 많은 돈을 청하느니 차라리 택한 그 고생으로써도(그것을 그는 이제야 처음 알게 되었다) 드러났다.\n\n그들은 그에게, 테스 더비필드가 제대로 알리지도 않고 블랙무어 건너편 부모의 집으로 가 버렸다고 일러 주었고, 그래서 더비필드 부인을 찾는 것이 필요해졌다. 그녀는 그에게 자기가 이제 말럿에 없다고 말했지만, 실제 주소에 대해서는 묘하게 함구했으니, 유일한 방도는 말럿으로 가서 그것을 물어보는 것이었다. 테스에게 그토록 무뚝뚝했던 그 농장주는 클레어에게는 아주 매끄럽게 굴며, 그를 말럿 쪽으로 태워다 줄 말과 일꾼을 빌려주었다. 그가 타고 온 이륜마차는 에민스터로 돌려보냈으니, 그 말로는 하루 여정의 한계에 다다른 것이었다.\n\n클레어는 골짜기 변두리까지 가는 것 이상으로는 그 농장주의 마차를 빌리려 하지 않았고, 그것을 자기를 태워다 준 일꾼과 함께 돌려보낸 뒤, 한 여관에 묵었다가, 이튿날 자기 사랑하는 테스가 태어난 곳이 있는 그 지방으로 걸어 들어갔다. 정원과 나뭇잎에 빛깔이 많이 돌기에는 아직 너무 이른 철이었다. 이른바 봄이라는 것은 얇은 초록빛 한 겹을 덧입은 겨울에 지나지 않았고, 그것은 그의 기대와 한통속이었다.\n\n테스가 어린 시절을 보낸 집에는 이제 그녀를 전혀 모르는 다른 가족이 살고 있었다. 새 주민들은 정원에 나와 있었는데, 마치 이 집터가 다른 이들의 내력과 얽혀 본 적이 결코 없다는 듯—그들의 내력에 견주면 이들의 내력이란 백치가 들려주는 이야기에 불과하건만—오로지 제 일에만 관심을 쏟고 있었다. 그들은 자기네 관심사 생각만을 온통 머릿속에 둔 채 정원 길을 거닐었으니, 순간순간 자기들의 행동을 등 뒤의 희미한 유령들과 거슬리게 부딪치게 하면서, 테스가 거기 살던 시절이 이야기로 따지면 지금보다 조금도 더 강렬하지 않다는 듯 이야기를 나누었다. 봄새들조차 머리 위에서 노래했으니, 마치 딱히 빠진 사람은 아무도 없다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n이 더없이 순진한 이들에게—그들에게는 선임자들의 이름조차 흐릿해져 가는 기억에 불과했는데—물어보고서 클레어는, 존 더비필드가 죽었으며, 그의 미망인과 아이들이 킹스비어에 가서 살겠노라 말하며 말럿을 떠났으나, 정작 그러지 않고 그들이 언급한 다른 곳으로 가 버렸음을 알게 되었다. 이쯤 되자 클레어는 테스를 더 이상 품지 않는 그 집이 끔찍해져, 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않고 그 미운 존재로부터 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n그의 길은 그가 무도회에서 그녀를 처음 보았던 그 들판 옆을 지났다. 그곳은 그 집만큼이나 괴로웠으니—아니, 더 심했다. 그는 묘지를 가로질러 갔는데, 거기 새 묘비들 사이에서 다른 것들보다 다소 우월한 디자인의 묘비 하나를 보았다. 그 비문은 이러했다—\n\n한때 그 이름으로 막강하던 가문의, 정복왕의 기사들 중 한 사람인 페이건 더버빌 경으로부터 빛나는 혈통을 통해 직계로 내려온 후손이자, 올바로 부르자면 더버빌인 존 더비필드를 기리며. 18—년 3월 10일 사망. 강하던 자들이 어찌 그리 쓰러졌는가.\n\n어떤 남자가, 보아하니 묘지기인 듯한 자가 거기 서 있는 클레어를 보고는 다가왔다. \"아, 선생님, 저 양반은 여기 묻히고 싶어 하지 않고, 자기 조상들이 있는 킹스비어로 옮겨지길 바랐답니다.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 그 양반 소원을 들어주지 않았소?\"\n\n\"오—돈이 없어서요. 정말이지 선생님, 글쎄—저, 아무 데서나 떠벌리고 싶진 않습니다만—이 묘비조차, 위에 그렇게 거창하게 새겨 놓았어도, 값을 치르지 못했답니다.\"\n\n\"아, 누가 세웠소?\"\n\n남자가 마을 석공의 이름을 일러 주었고, 묘지를 떠나면서 클레어는 그 석공의 집에 들렀다. 그는 그 말이 사실임을 알고 청구서 값을 치렀다. 그러고 나서 그는 이주한 이들 쪽으로 방향을 잡았다.\n\n걷기에는 거리가 너무 멀었지만, 클레어는 혼자 있고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 강했던지 처음에는 탈것을 빌리려고도, 결국 그곳에 닿을 수 있을 우회 철로를 타려고도 하지 않았다. 그러나 섀스턴에서는 빌려야 한다는 걸 알았다. 하지만 길이 어찌나 멀었던지, 말럿을 떠난 뒤로 이십 마일이 넘는 거리를 지나, 저녁 일곱 시쯤이 되어서야 조앤이 사는 곳에 들어섰다.\n\n마을이 작았으므로 그는 더비필드 부인의 셋집을 찾는 데 별 어려움이 없었다. 그것은 큰길에서 멀리 떨어진, 담장 두른 정원이 딸린 집이었는데, 그녀가 거기에 자기의 볼품없는 낡은 가구를 되도록 잘 욱여넣어 둔 것이었다. 무슨 까닭에선지 그녀가 그가 찾아오기를 바라지 않았던 것이 분명했고, 그는 자신의 방문이 다소 침입처럼 느껴졌다. 그녀가 직접 문으로 나왔고, 저녁 하늘의 빛이 그녀의 얼굴 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n클레어가 그녀를 만난 것은 이번이 처음이었지만, 그는 너무 골몰해 있던 터라, 그녀가 점잖은 미망인의 옷차림을 한 여전히 잘생긴 여인이라는 것 말고는 더 관찰하지 못했다. 그는 자기가 테스의 남편임을, 그리고 자기가 거기 온 목적을 설명해야 했고, 그 일을 꽤나 어색하게 해냈다. \"당장 그 사람을 보고 싶습니다.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"다시 편지하시겠다고 하셨는데, 안 하셨더군요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 집에 안 왔으니까요.\" 조앤이 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 잘 지내는지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"몰라요. 하지만 선생이 아셔야지요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"인정합니다. 그 사람은 어디 머물고 있습니까?\"\n\n면담이 시작될 때부터 조앤은 한 손을 뺨 옆에 댄 채 자신의 곤혹스러움을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"그 애가—정확히 어디 머무는지는 모르겠어요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"그 애는—하지만—\" \"그 애는 어디 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"그런데 그 애는 지금 거기 없어요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 지금은 거기 없어요.\"\n발뺌하느라 그녀는 다시 말을 멈추었고, 그사이 어린아이들이 문가로 기어 나와 있었는데, 어머니의 치맛자락을 잡아당기며 가장 어린아이가 중얼거렸다—\n\n\"이 사람이 테스 언니랑 결혼할 신사예요?\"\n\n\"이미 결혼했단다.\" 조앤이 속삭였다. \"안으로 들어가.\"\n\n클레어는 그녀가 함구하려 애쓰는 것을 보고는 물었다—\n\n\"테스가 제가 자기를 찾아 나서기를 바랄 거라고 생각하십니까? 만약 아니라면, 물론—\"\n\n\"바라지 않을 것 같네요.\"\n\n\"확실합니까?\"\n\n\"바라지 않을 게 확실해요.\"\n\n그는 돌아서려다가, 테스의 그 다정한 편지를 떠올렸다.\n\n\"분명 바랄 겁니다!\" 그가 격정적으로 받아쳤다. \"제가 어머니보다 그 사람을 더 잘 압니다.\"\n\n\"그건 그럴 법도 하지요, 선생. 난 그 애를 정말로 알아본 적이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"제발 그 사람 주소를 알려 주십시오, 더비필드 부인, 외롭고 비참한 한 사내를 가엾이 여기셔서!\" 테스의 어머니는 다시 수직으로 세운 손으로 안절부절못하며 뺨을 쓸었고, 그가 괴로워하는 것을 보고는 마침내 낮은 목소리로 말했다—\n\n\"그 애는 샌드본에 있어요.\"\n\n\"아—거기 어디요? 샌드본은 큰 곳이 되었다던데요.\"\n\n\"내가 말한 것보다 더 자세히는 몰라요—샌드본. 나는 거기 가 본 적도 없으니까요.\"\n\n이 점에서는 조앤이 진실을 말하고 있음이 분명했고, 그는 더 캐묻지 않았다.\n\n\"필요한 게 있으십니까?\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"없어요, 선생.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"우린 그럭저럭 살 만하게 갖춰져 있어요.\"\n\n집 안에 들어가지 않은 채 클레어는 돌아섰다. 삼 마일 앞에 역이 있었고, 마부의 삯을 치른 뒤 그는 그리로 걸어갔다. 샌드본으로 가는 마지막 기차가 얼마 뒤에 떠났고, 그 기차가 클레어를 바퀴에 싣고 갔다.\n\n# LV\n\n그날 밤 열한 시, 한 호텔에 묵을 자리를 잡고 도착하자마자 아버지에게 자기 주소를 전보로 친 뒤, 그는 샌드본의 거리로 걸어 나갔다. 누구를 찾아가거나 묻기에는 너무 늦은 시각이었고, 그는 마지못해 그 목적을 아침으로 미루었다. 하지만 아직 잠자리에 들 수는 없었다.\n\n이 멋들어진 휴양지는, 동역과 서역, 잔교들, 소나무 숲, 산책로, 지붕 덮인 정원을 갖춘 이곳은, 에인절 클레어에게는 마치 요술 지팡이 한 번 휘둘러 갑자기 만들어진 뒤 먼지가 약간 앉도록 내버려 둔 요정의 나라 같았다. 거대한 에그던 황무지의 외진 동쪽 한 자락이 바로 가까이에 있는데도, 그 황갈빛 태고의 땅 바로 가장자리에 이런 번쩍이는 새것이, 이 환락의 도시가 솟아나기를 택한 것이었다. 그 변두리에서 일 마일 안쪽으로는 토양의 온갖 굴곡이 선사 시대의 것이었고, 모든 골은 손대지 않은 고대 브리튼의 옛길이었으니, 카이사르들의 시절 이후로 거기서는 단 한 뙈기의 흙도 갈아엎어진 적이 없었다. 그런데도 이 이국적인 것이 여기서 자라났으니, 예언자의 박넝쿨처럼 갑작스레 자라나 테스를 이리로 끌어들인 것이었다.\n\n자정의 등불 아래 그는 옛것 속의 이 새 세계의 구불구불한 길을 오르내렸고, 나무들 사이로, 별들을 등지고서, 이곳을 이룬 수많은 기발한 저택들의 우뚝한 지붕과 굴뚝과 정자와 탑 들을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그것은 따로 떨어진 저택들의 도시였으니, 영국 해협 위의 지중해풍 휴식처였다. 그리고 지금 밤에 보니 실제보다도 한층 더 위풍당당해 보였다.\n\n바다가 가까이에 있었으나 거슬리지는 않았다. 바다가 속삭였는데, 그는 그것이 소나무인 줄 알았다. 소나무가 꼭 같은 가락으로 속삭였는데, 그는 그것이 바다인 줄 알았다.\n\n테스가 대체 어디에 있을 수 있을까, 시골 처녀인 그의 젊은 아내가, 이 온갖 부유함과 멋스러움 한가운데에? 곰곰이 생각할수록 그는 더 어리둥절해졌다. 여기에 젖 짤 소가 있기나 할까? 갈 밭이라곤 분명 없었다. 그녀는 십중팔구 이 큰 집들 중 하나에서 무슨 일을 하기로 되어 있을 터였다. 그는 어슬렁거리며 침실 창문들과 하나씩 꺼져 가는 그 불빛들을 바라보았고, 그중 어느 것이 그녀의 것일까 궁금해했다.\n\n추측은 부질없었고, 열두 시가 막 지난 뒤 그는 안으로 들어가 잠자리에 들었다. 불을 끄기 전에 그는 테스의 그 격정적인 편지를 다시 읽었다. 그러나 잠은—그녀에게 그토록 가까이 있으면서도, 그토록 멀리 있으니—이룰 수 없었고, 그는 자꾸만 창의 차양을 들어 올려 맞은편 집들의 뒤편을 바라보며, 그 순간 어느 창틀 뒤에서 그녀가 쉬고 있을까 궁금해했다.\n\n차라리 밤을 꼬박 새우는 편이 나을 뻔했다. 아침에 그는 일곱 시에 일어나, 곧이어 중앙 우체국 방향으로 길을 잡아 나섰다. 문 앞에서 그는 아침 배달용 편지들을 들고 나오는 영리해 보이는 우체부 한 명과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"클레어 부인이라는 분의 주소를 아십니까?\" 에인절이 물었다. 우체부는 고개를 저었다.\n\n그러다 그녀가 처녀 시절 이름을 계속 썼을 법함을 떠올리고, 클레어가 말했다—\n\n\"더비필드 양이라는 분은요?\"\n\n\"더비필드요?\"\n\n이것 역시 그 우체부에게는 생소했다.\n\n\"아시다시피 손님들이야 날마다 오가니까요, 선생님.\" 그가 말했다. \"그러니 집 이름을 모르고서야 찾기란 불가능합니다.\"\n\n마침 그때 그의 동료 하나가 서둘러 나오기에, 그에게 그 이름을 다시 일러 주었다.\n\n\"더비필드라는 이름은 모르겠는데, 헤런즈에 더버빌이라는 이름은 있어요.\" 두 번째 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그겁니다!\" 클레어가 외쳤다. 그녀가 본래의 발음으로 되돌아갔구나 싶어 기뻤다. \"헤런즈가 어떤 곳이지요?\"\n\n\"멋들어진 하숙집이에요. 여긴 온통 하숙집이지요, 정말이지.\"\n\n클레어는 그 집을 찾아가는 길을 안내받아 서둘러 그리로 갔고, 우유 배달부와 함께 도착했다. 헤런즈는 평범한 별장이긴 했으나 제 부지 안에 들어앉아 있었고, 외양이 어찌나 사사로워 보이는지 하숙을 구하리라곤 도무지 기대하기 어려운 곳이었다. 가엾은 테스가 그가 두려워하던 대로 여기서 하인 노릇을 하고 있다면, 그녀는 저 우유 배달부에게로 뒷문으로 갈 터였고, 그도 그리로 가고 싶은 마음이 들었다. 그러나 망설이던 끝에 그는 앞쪽으로 돌아가 초인종을 울렸다.\n\n이른 시각이라 안주인이 직접 문을 열었다. 클레어는 테레사 더버빌, 또는 더비필드를 찾았다.\n\n\"더버빌 부인요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n그렇다면 테스가 결혼한 여인으로 통하는구나, 싶어 그는, 비록 그녀가 자기 성을 쓰지는 않았지만, 기뻤다.\n\n\"친척이 그분을 몹시 보고 싶어 한다고 좀 전해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"좀 이른데요. 누구라고 전할까요, 선생님?\"\n\"에인절이라고요.\"\n\n\"에인절 씨요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 에인절. 제 세례명입니다. 그러면 알아들을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"깨어 있는지 보고 오지요.\"\n\n그는 앞쪽 방, 곧 식당으로 안내되었고, 봄철 커튼 너머로 작은 잔디밭과, 그 위의 진달래며 다른 관목들을 내다보았다. 그녀의 처지가 그가 두려워했던 것만큼 결코 나쁘지 않은 것이 분명했고, 그녀가 어떻게든 그 보석들을 자기 것으로 청구해 팔아서 이 처지에 이른 게 틀림없다는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤다. 그는 한순간도 그녀를 탓하지 않았다. 곧 그의 예민해진 귀가 계단 위의 발소리를 잡아냈고, 그 소리에 그의 심장이 어찌나 아프게 쿵쿵거렸던지 그는 거의 똑바로 서 있을 수가 없을 지경이었다. \"이런! 이렇게 변해 버린 나를 그 사람이 어찌 생각할까!\" 그는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그리고 문이 열렸다.\n\n테스가 문턱에 나타났다—그가 그녀를 보리라 예상했던 것과는 전혀 다른, 정말이지 어리둥절할 만큼 다른 모습으로. 그녀의 위대한 타고난 아름다움은, 한층 돋보였다고는 못해도, 그 차림새로 인해 더 또렷이 드러나 있었다. 그녀는 회백색의 캐시미어 실내복을 헐겁게 두르고 있었는데, 그것은 반쯤 상중인 색조로 수놓아져 있었고, 같은 빛깔의 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다. 그녀의 목은 솜털 주름 장식 위로 솟아 있었고, 잘 기억하던 그 짙은 갈색 머리채는 일부는 뒤통수에 한 덩이로 말려 올려져 있고 일부는 어깨에 흘러내려 있었으니—분명 서두른 탓이었다. 그는 두 팔을 내밀었으나, 그것은 다시 옆구리로 떨어졌다. 그녀가 앞으로 나오지 않고 문 어귀에 그대로 멈춰 서 있었기 때문이다. 이제 한낱 누런 해골이 된 그는 둘 사이의 대조를 느꼈고, 자기 모습이 그녀에게 역겨우리라 생각했다.\n\n\"테스!\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 떠나간 걸 용서해 줄 수 있겠소? 나에게—와 줄 수 없겠소? 어쩌다—이런 모습이 된 거요?\"\n\n\"너무 늦었어요.\" 그녀가 말했는데, 방 안에 울리는 그녀의 목소리는 딱딱했고, 두 눈은 부자연스럽게 빛났다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 옳게 생각하지 못했소—있는 그대로의 당신을 보지 못했던 거요!\" 그가 계속 호소했다. \"그 뒤로 그걸 깨달았소, 더없이 사랑하는 나의 테시!\"\n\n\"너무 늦었어요, 너무 늦었어요!\" 고통으로 인해 매 순간이 한 시간처럼 느껴지는 사람의 조급함으로 그녀가 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"제게 가까이 오지 마세요, 에인절! 안 돼요—그러시면 안 돼요. 떨어져 계세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 병으로 이렇게 망가졌다고 해서 날 사랑하지 않는 거요, 나의 사랑하는 아내여? 당신은 그렇게 변덕스러운 사람이 아니잖소—나는 오직 당신을 위해 온 거요—이제 내 어머니와 아버지가 당신을 반겨 주실 거요!\"\n\n\"네—오, 그래요, 그래요! 하지만 너무 늦었다고, 너무 늦었다고 말하고 있잖아요.\"\n\n그녀는 꿈속에서 도망치려 하지만 움직일 수 없는 도망자가 된 듯했다. \"당신은 다 모르세요—그걸 모르시는 거예요? 그런데 모르신다면 어떻게 여기 오셨어요?\"\n\n\"여기저기 물어물어 길을 찾아냈소.\"\n\n\"저는 당신을 기다리고 또 기다렸어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었는데, 그 어조가 갑자기 예전의 피리 같은 애처로움을 되찾았다. \"그런데 당신은 오지 않았어요! 그리고 제가 편지를 썼는데, 당신은 오지 않았어요! 저 사람이 자꾸만 당신은 다시는 절대 오지 않을 거라고, 제가 어리석은 여자라고 말했어요. 저 사람은 제게, 어머니에게, 우리 모두에게 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 아주 친절했어요. 저 사람이—\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"저 사람이 저를 자기에게로 도로 차지했어요.\"\n\n클레어는 그녀를 날카롭게 바라보다가, 그 뜻을 알아채고는 역병에 걸린 자처럼 풀썩 꺾였고, 그의 시선이 가라앉았다. 그것은 그녀의 두 손에 떨어졌는데, 한때 발그레하던 그 손이 이제는 희고 한결 더 가냘팠다.\n\n그녀가 말을 이었다—\n\n\"저 사람은 위층에 있어요. 저는 이제 저 사람이 미워요, 저한테 거짓말을 했으니까요—당신이 다시는 오지 않을 거라고요. 그런데 당신이 오셨잖아요! 이 옷들은 저 사람이 제게 입혀 놓은 거예요. 저는 저 사람이 저를 어떻게 하든 상관없었어요! 하지만—가 주시겠어요, 에인절, 제발, 그리고 다시는 오지 말아 주시겠어요?\"\n\n그들은 못 박힌 듯 서 있었고, 좌절한 두 마음이 보기 딱할 만큼 기쁨 없는 빛으로 두 눈을 통해 내다보고 있었다. 둘 다 무언가가 현실로부터 자기들을 지켜 주기를 애원하는 듯했다.\n\n\"아—내 잘못이오!\" 클레어가 말했다.\n\n하지만 그는 말을 이어 갈 수 없었다. 말은 침묵만큼이나 아무것도 표현해 주지 못했다. 그러나 그에게는 한 가지가 어렴풋이 의식되었으니, 비록 그것이 그에게 분명해진 것은 나중의 일이지만—그의 본래의 테스가 눈앞의 그 육신을 자기 것으로 알아보기를 영혼으로는 그만두었다는 것, 그 육신을, 마치 물살 위의 시신처럼, 그 살아 있는 의지와는 동떨어진 방향으로 떠가도록 내버려 두고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n몇 순간이 지났고, 그는 테스가 가 버린 것을 알았다. 그가 그 순간에 골몰한 채 서 있는 동안 그의 얼굴은 한층 차갑게, 한층 더 오그라들었고, 일이 분 뒤 그는 어디로 가는지도 모르는 채 거리를 걷고 있는 자신을 발견했다.\n\n# LVI\n\n헤런즈의 집주인이자 그 모든 멋진 가구의 주인인 브룩스 부인은 유난히 호기심이 많은 부류의 사람은 아니었다. 가엾게도 그녀는 손익(損益)이라는 그 산술의 마귀에게 오래도록 어쩔 수 없이 종살이한 탓에 너무도 철저히 물질화되어 있어서, 있을 법한 하숙인의 주머니와 관련된 경우 말고는 호기심 자체를 위한 호기심은 별로 남아 있지 않았다. 그렇지만 그녀가 후한 셋돈을 내는 세입자들인, 그녀가 더버빌 부부라 여기던 이들에게 에인절 클레어가 찾아온 일은, 그 시각과 태도로 보아 충분히 이례적이어서, 하숙업과 관련된 경우 말고는 쓸모없다고 짓눌러 두었던 여자다운 성향을 되살려 놓았다.\n\n테스는 식당에 들어가지 않은 채 문간에서 남편에게 말했고, 통로 안쪽 자기 거실의 반쯤 닫힌 문 안에 서 있던 브룩스 부인은 그 두 가련한 영혼 사이의 대화의—그것을 대화라 부를 수 있다면—조각들을 들을 수 있었다. 그녀는 테스가 일 층으로 다시 올라가는 소리와, 클레어가 떠나는 소리와, 그의 뒤로 현관문이 닫히는 소리를 들었다. 그러고 나서 위층 방의 문이 닫혔고, 브룩스 부인은 테스가 자기 방으로 다시 들어갔음을 알았다. 그 젊은 부인이 옷을 다 갖춰 입지 않은 상태였으므로, 브룩스 부인은 그녀가 한동안은 다시 나오지 않으리라는 것을 알았다.\n\n* 일 층: 영국식 표현으로 우리 식의 이 층에 해당함.\n그래서 그녀는 살그머니 계단을 올라가, 앞쪽 방—곧 응접실로, 흔한 방식대로 접이문으로 바로 뒤쪽 방(침실이었다)과 이어진—의 문 앞에 섰다. 브룩스 부인의 가장 좋은 방들로 이루어진 이 일 층은 더버빌 부부가 주(週) 단위로 빌린 것이었다. 뒤쪽 방은 지금 고요했지만, 응접실에서는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n처음에 그녀가 그 소리에서 알아들을 수 있었던 것이라곤, 낮은 신음의 가락으로 끊임없이 되풀이되는 한 음절뿐이었으니, 마치 익시온의 수레바퀴에 묶인 어떤 영혼에게서 나오는 것 같았다—\n\"오—오—오!\"\n\n그러더니 침묵, 그러더니 무거운 한숨, 그리고 다시—\n\n\"오—오—오!\"\n안주인은 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보았다. 안쪽 방의 작은 부분만이 보였지만, 그 보이는 범위 안으로 이미 식사를 위해 차려진 아침상 한 귀퉁이가, 그리고 그 옆의 의자 하나가 들어왔다. 그 의자의 앉는 자리 위로 테스의 얼굴이 숙여져 있었으니, 그녀의 자세는 그 앞에 무릎 꿇은 모습이었다. 두 손은 머리 위로 깍지 끼고 있었고, 실내복 자락과 잠옷의 수놓인 자락이 그녀 뒤로 바닥에 흘러내려 있었으며, 슬리퍼가 벗겨진 양말 신지 않은 두 발이 양탄자 위로 드러나 있었다. 형언할 수 없는 절망의 그 중얼거림은 그녀의 입술에서 나오는 것이었다.\n\n그러더니 옆 침실에서 남자의 목소리가 들려왔다—\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않고, 외침이라기보다 독백에, 독백이라기보다 만가(挽歌)에 가까운 어조로 말을 이어 갔다. 브룩스 부인이 알아들을 수 있었던 것은 일부뿐이었다.\n\n\"그러고 나서 제 사랑하는, 사랑하는 남편이 제게로 돌아왔는데…… 저는 그걸 몰랐어요!…… 그리고 당신은 제게 그 잔인한 설득을 써먹었지요…… 당신은 그걸 멈추지 않았어요—그래요—멈추지 않았어요! 제 어린 동생들과 어머니의 궁핍—그게 바로 당신이 저를 흔들어 댄 미끼였어요…… 그리고 당신은 제 남편이 절대 돌아오지 않을 거라고, 절대로, 라고 말했어요. 그리고 당신은 저를 비웃으며, 그이를 기다리다니 내가 얼마나 멍청한 여자냐고 했지요!…… 그리고 결국 저는 당신 말을 믿고 굴복하고 말았어요!…… 그런데 그이가 돌아왔어요! 이제 그이는 가 버렸어요. 두 번째로 가 버렸고, 이제 저는 그이를 영영 잃었어요…… 그리고 그이는 이제 다시는 저를 조금도 사랑하지 않을 거예요—저를 미워할 뿐이겠지요!…… 오 그래요, 저는 이제 그이를 잃었어요—또다시, 당신 때문에!\" 머리를 의자에 댄 채 몸부림치며 그녀는 얼굴을 문 쪽으로 돌렸고, 브룩스 부인은 그 위에 어린 고통을 볼 수 있었다. 그녀의 입술이 이를 악문 탓에 피를 흘리고 있는 것도, 감긴 두 눈의 긴 속눈썹이 젖은 채 갈래갈래 뺨에 들러붙어 있는 것도. 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그이는 죽어 가고 있어요—마치 죽어 가는 사람 같았어요!…… 그리고 제 죄가 저는 죽이지 않고 그이를 죽일 거예요!…… 오, 당신은 제 삶을 갈가리 찢어 놓았어요…… 제가 다시는 그렇게 되지 않게 해 달라고 가엾이 여겨 빌었던 그 꼴로 저를 만들어 놓았어요!…… 저의 진정한 남편은 결코, 결코—오 하느님—저는 이걸 견딜 수 없어요!—견딜 수가 없어요!\"\n\n남자에게서 더 많은, 더 날카로운 말들이 나왔다. 그러더니 갑작스러운 옷자락 소리—그녀가 벌떡 일어선 것이었다. 브룩스 부인은 말하던 사람이 문밖으로 뛰쳐나오려는 줄 알고 황급히 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n그러나 그럴 필요는 없었으니, 거실 문은 열리지 않았기 때문이다. 하지만 브룩스 부인은 다시 층계참에서 엿보는 것이 위험하다고 느껴, 아래층 자기 거실로 들어갔다.\n\n그녀는 가만히 귀를 기울였으나 바닥 너머로는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았고, 그리하여 부엌으로 가서 중단했던 아침 식사를 마저 했다. 이윽고 일 층의 앞쪽 방으로 올라온 그녀는 바느질감을 집어 들고, 하숙인들이 종을 울려 아침상을 치우라 하기를 기다렸다. 그 일을 직접 하여, 가능하면 무슨 일인지 알아낼 작정이었다. 앉아 있는 동안 머리 위로, 그녀는 이제 누군가가 걸어 다니는 듯 마룻장이 가볍게 삐걱대는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 이윽고 그 움직임은 난간에 옷자락이 스치는 소리와, 현관문이 열렸다 닫히는 소리와, 거리로 나가는 길에 대문 쪽으로 지나가는 테스의 모습으로 설명되었다. 그녀는 이제 도착할 때 입었던, 형편이 넉넉한 젊은 부인의 외출복을 완전히 갖춰 입고 있었는데, 다만 모자와 검은 깃털 위로 베일을 드리운 것만이 더해져 있었다.\n* 일 층: 영국식 표현으로 우리 식의 이 층에 해당함.\n\n브룩스 부인은 위층 문간에서 세입자들 사이에 오간, 일시적이든 아니든 어떤 작별 인사도 한마디 잡아내지 못했다. 그들이 다투었거나, 아니면 더버빌 씨가 아직 자고 있는지도 몰랐다. 그는 일찍 일어나는 사람이 아니었으니까.\n\n그녀는 더 각별히 자기만의 거처인 뒤쪽 방으로 들어가 거기서 바느질을 계속했다. 부인 하숙인은 돌아오지 않았고, 신사도 종을 울리지 않았다. 브룩스 부인은 그 지체에 대해, 그리고 그토록 이르게 찾아온 그 방문객이 위층의 부부와 어떤 관계일까에 대해 골똘히 생각했다. 생각에 잠긴 채 그녀는 의자에 등을 기댔다.\n\n그러다가 그녀의 눈이 무심코 천장을 훑었는데, 전에는 거기서 한 번도 본 적 없는, 그 흰 표면 한가운데의 한 점에 시선이 붙들렸다. 처음 보았을 때는 봉인용 풀떡지만 한 크기였으나 그것은 순식간에 손바닥만 하게 커졌고, 그제야 그녀는 그것이 붉은색임을 알아챌 수 있었다. 한가운데에 이 주홍빛 얼룩이 박힌 그 직사각형의 흰 천장은, 거대한 하트 에이스 카드 같은 모양새였다.\n\n브룩스 부인은 야릇한 불안의 욱신거림을 느꼈다. 그녀는 식탁 위로 올라가 손가락으로 천장의 그 점을 만져 보았다. 축축했고, 그녀는 그것이 핏자국이 아닐까 싶었다.\n\n식탁에서 내려와 그녀는 거실을 나와 위층으로 올라갔으니, 머리 위의 방, 곧 응접실 뒤쪽의 침실로 들어갈 작정이었다. 하지만 이제 넋이 나가 버린 여자가 된 그녀는 도무지 손잡이를 잡을 엄두를 낼 수 없었다. 그녀는 귀를 기울였다. 안의 죽은 듯한 고요는 오직 규칙적인 박동 소리로만 깨지고 있었다.\n\n뚝, 뚝, 뚝.\n\n브룩스 부인은 황급히 계단을 내려가 현관문을 열고 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다. 그녀가 아는 한 남자, 곧 이웃 별장에서 일하는 일꾼 하나가 지나가고 있기에, 그녀는 그에게 안으로 들어와 자기와 함께 위층으로 가 달라고 사정했다. 하숙인 중 누군가에게 무슨 일이 생긴 것 같아 두렵다고. 일꾼은 응낙하고 그녀를 따라 층계참으로 올라갔다.\n\n그녀는 응접실 문을 열고 그가 들어가도록 뒤로 물러섰다가, 자기도 그의 뒤를 따라 들어갔다. 방은 비어 있었다. 아침상은—커피와 달걀과 차가운 햄으로 이루어진 푸짐한 식사가—그녀가 들여놓았을 때 그대로, 손도 대지 않은 채 식탁 위에 차려져 있었는데, 다만 고기 써는 칼이 없어진 것만 달랐다. 그녀는 남자에게 접이문을 지나 옆방으로 들어가 달라고 청했다.\n\n그가 문을 열고 한두 걸음 들어가더니, 거의 곧바로 굳어진 얼굴로 돌아 나왔다. \"오 하느님 맙소사, 침대에 있는 신사가 죽었습니다! 칼에 찔린 것 같아요—피가 바닥으로 잔뜩 흘러내렸어요!\"\n\n곧 경보가 울렸고, 얼마 전까지만 해도 그토록 조용하던 그 집이 수많은 발소리로 울렸으니, 그중에는 외과의도 하나 끼어 있었다.\n상처는 작았으나, 칼끝이 희생자의 심장에 닿아 있었다. 그는 등을 대고 누운 채, 창백하고, 굳어, 죽어 있었다. 일격을 당한 뒤로 거의 몸을 움직이지 못한 듯했다. 십오 분 만에 이 도시를 잠시 찾은 손님인 한 신사가 침대에서 칼에 찔렸다는 소식이 이 인기 휴양지의 거리와 별장마다 퍼졌다.\n\n# LVII\n\n그 사이 에인절 클레어는 자기가 왔던 길을 따라 기계적으로 걸어, 호텔로 들어가 아침 식탁 앞에 앉아 허공을 멍하니 바라보았다. 그는 무의식중에 먹고 마시기를 계속하다가, 문득 계산서를 청했다. 값을 치른 뒤 그는 손에 화장 가방을 들었다. 들고 온 짐이라고는 그것뿐이었다. 그리고 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n그가 떠나려는 순간 전보 한 통이 그에게 전해졌다. 어머니가 보낸 몇 마디였다. 그의 주소를 알게 되어 다들 기쁘다는 내용과, 그의 형 커스버트가 머시 챈트에게 청혼했고 받아들여졌다는 소식이었다.\n\n클레어는 그 종이를 구겨 버리고 역으로 가는 길을 따라갔다. 역에 닿고 보니 한 시간 넘게 떠날 기차가 없었다. 그는 앉아서 기다렸고, 십오 분쯤 기다리고 나니 더는 거기서 기다릴 수 없을 것 같았다. 마음이 부서지고 무감각해진 그는 서두를 일이 아무것도 없었다. 다만 그런 일의 무대가 된 도시에서 벗어나고 싶었다. 그래서 다음 역까지 걸어가, 거기서 기차에 오르기로 하고 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n그가 따라간 큰길은 탁 트여 있었고, 조금 가다가 골짜기로 내려갔다. 골짜기를 가로지르며 한쪽 끝에서 다른 끝까지 길이 뻗은 것이 보였다. 그는 이 우묵한 곳의 거의 전부를 지나, 서쪽 비탈을 오르던 중이었다. 숨을 고르려고 멈춰 선 그는 무심코 뒤를 돌아보았다. 왜 그랬는지 그 자신도 알 수 없었으나, 무언가가 그를 그렇게 하도록 떠미는 듯했다. 끈처럼 길게 뻗은 길의 표면이 등 뒤로 보이는 데까지 점점 줄어들었고, 그가 바라보는 사이 움직이는 점 하나가 그 흰 텅 빈 원근 속으로 끼어들었다.\n\n달리는 사람의 모습이었다. 클레어는, 누군가 자기를 따라잡으려 한다는 어렴풋한 느낌을 가지고 기다렸다.\n\n비탈을 내려오는 형체는 여자의 것이었다. 그러나 그의 마음은 아내가 자기를 뒤쫓고 있으리라는 생각에는 전혀 닿지 못한 터라, 그녀가 가까이 왔을 때조차도 지금 그가 보는 완전히 달라진 옷차림 아래에서 그녀를 알아보지 못했다. 아주 가까이 다가오고 나서야 그는 그녀가 테스임을 믿을 수 있었다.\n\n\"역에서 당신이—돌아서는 걸—내가 거기 닿기 직전에 봤어요—그래서 여기까지 줄곧 당신을 따라왔어요!\"\n\n그녀는 어찌나 창백하고, 어찌나 숨이 가쁘고, 온몸의 근육이 어찌나 떨리던지, 그는 그녀에게 한마디 묻지도 못하고 그녀의 손을 붙잡아 자기 팔 안으로 끌어당기며 데리고 걸었다. 혹시 마주칠지도 모를 행인을 피하려고 그는 큰길을 벗어나 전나무 몇 그루 아래 오솔길로 들어섰다. 신음하는 나뭇가지들 사이로 깊이 들어가자 그는 걸음을 멈추고 캐묻듯 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"에인절,\" 마치 이 순간을 기다렸다는 듯 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 왜 당신을 뒤쫓아 달려왔는지 알아요? 내가 그를 죽였다고 말하려고요!\" 말하는 동안 가엾은 흰 미소가 그녀의 얼굴을 밝혔다.\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 그가 말했다. 그녀의 이상한 태도로 보아 무슨 헛소리를 하는 게 아닌가 싶었다.\n\n\"내가 해 버렸어요—어떻게 했는지 모르겠어요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그래도, 그건 당신을 위해, 또 나 자신을 위해 갚아야 할 빚이었어요, 에인절. 오래전에, 내 장갑으로 그의 입을 쳤을 때 두려웠어요. 순진했던 어린 시절 그가 나한테 놓은 덫과, 나를 통해 당신에게 저지른 잘못 때문에 언젠가 내가 이런 짓을 할지도 모른다고요. 그는 우리 사이에 끼어들어 우리를 망쳤고, 이제는 다시는 그러지 못할 거예요. 나는 당신을 사랑한 만큼 그를 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없었어요, 에인절. 알잖아요, 그렇죠? 믿죠? 당신은 내게 돌아오지 않았고, 나는 어쩔 수 없이 그에게로 돌아가야 했어요. 왜 가 버렸어요—왜 그랬어요—내가 당신을 그토록 사랑했는데? 왜 그랬는지 도무지 알 수가 없어요. 하지만 당신을 탓하진 않아요. 다만, 에인절, 이제 내가 그를 죽였으니, 당신에게 지은 내 죄를 용서해 주겠어요? 달려오면서 생각했어요, 이제 그 일을 했으니 당신이 틀림없이 나를 용서해 주리라고요. 그렇게 하면 당신을 되찾을 수 있으리라는 게 환한 빛처럼 떠올랐어요. 더는 당신을 잃은 채로 견딜 수가 없었어요—당신이 나를 사랑하지 않는다는 걸 내가 얼마나 도무지 견딜 수 없었는지 당신은 몰라요! 이제는 사랑한다고 말해 줘요, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 남편이여. 이제 내가 그를 죽였으니, 사랑한다고 말해 줘요!\"\n\n\"사랑해, 테스—오, 사랑하고말고—다 돌아왔어!\" 그가 뜨거운 힘으로 그녀를 두 팔에 더 꽉 끌어안으며 말했다. \"그런데 무슨 뜻이야—그를 죽였다니?\"\n\n\"그랬다는 뜻이에요.\" 그녀가 꿈결 같은 목소리로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고, 몸을? 그가 죽었어?\"\n\n\"네. 그가 내가 당신 때문에 우는 소리를 듣고는 모질게 비웃었어요. 그리고 당신을 더러운 이름으로 불렀어요. 그래서 내가 해 버렸어요. 내 마음이 그걸 견딜 수 없었어요. 그는 전에도 당신 일로 나를 들볶았어요. 그러고 나서 나는 옷을 차려입고 당신을 찾아 나왔어요.\"\n\n차츰 그는, 적어도 그녀가 했다고 말한 일을 어렴풋이나마 시도했으리라는 쪽으로 믿게 되었다. 그녀의 충동에 대한 그의 공포는, 자기를 향한 그녀의 애정의 강렬함에 대한 경악과, 그 애정의 기이한 성질에 대한 경악과 뒤섞였다. 그 애정은 보아하니 그녀의 도덕적 분별을 송두리째 꺼뜨려 버린 듯했다. 자기 행동의 무게를 깨닫지 못한 채 그녀는 마침내 만족한 듯 보였다. 그녀가 그의 어깨에 기대 행복에 겨워 울고 있는 모습을 바라보면서, 그는 더버빌 가문의 피 속 어떤 알 수 없는 줄기가 이 일탈로—그것이 일탈이라면—이끌었을까 의아했다. 마차와 살인에 얽힌 그 집안의 전설이, 더버빌 가가 그런 짓을 저지른 것으로 알려진 데서 비롯된 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 순간 그의 마음을 스쳤다. 어지럽고 흥분한 그의 머릿속이 추리할 수 있는 한, 그녀가 말한 미친 듯한 슬픔의 순간에 그녀의 정신이 균형을 잃고 그녀를 이 심연으로 밀어넣은 것이리라고 그는 짐작했다.\n\n만약 사실이라면 몹시 끔찍한 일이었고, 일시적 환각이라면 슬픈 일이었다. 하지만 어쨌든, 여기 그에게 버림받았던 이 아내가, 열정적으로 그를 사모하는 이 여인이, 그가 자기에게 보호자 외의 다른 무엇이 되리라고는 추호도 의심하지 않은 채 그에게 매달려 있었다. 자기가 그렇지 않은 무언가가 되는 일은 그녀의 마음속에서 가능성의 영역 안에 있지조차 않다는 것을 그는 알아차렸다. 마침내 클레어 안에서 다정함이 완전히 우세를 차지했다. 그는 자신의 흰 입술로 그녀에게 끝없이 입맞추고, 그녀의 손을 잡고, 말했다—\n\n\"당신을 버리지 않을 거야! 내 힘이 닿는 온갖 수단을 다해 당신을 지킬 거야, 가장 사랑하는 사람아, 당신이 무슨 일을 했든 안 했든!\"\n\n그러고 나서 그들은 나무 아래로 계속 걸었다. 테스는 이따금 고개를 돌려 그를 바라보았다. 그가 아무리 초췌하고 볼품없어졌다 해도, 그녀가 그의 외모에서 조금의 흠도 찾아내지 못한다는 것은 분명했다. 그녀에게 그는 예전처럼 완벽 그 자체였다, 인격으로도 정신으로도. 그는 여전히 그녀의 안티노오스였고, 그녀의 아폴론이기까지 했다. 그의 병색 짙은 얼굴은, 그녀가 그를 처음 보았을 때 못지않게 이날에도 그녀의 애정 어린 눈길에 아침처럼 아름다웠다. 그것이야말로 자기를 순결하게 사랑해 주었고 자기를 순결하다고 믿어 준, 이 세상에서 유일한 남자의 얼굴이 아니던가!\n\n여러 가능성에 대한 본능 때문에, 그는 이제 애초 작정했던 대로 도시 너머 첫 역으로 향하지 않고, 여기서 몇 마일에 걸쳐 우거진 전나무 숲속으로 더 깊이 파고들었다. 서로의 허리를 껴안은 채 그들은 마른 전나무 잎이 깔린 바닥 위를 거닐었다. 마침내 둘 사이에 살아 있는 사람 하나 없이 함께 있다는 의식에 어렴풋한 도취의 분위기로 빠져들면서, 시신 하나가 있다는 사실은 못 본 척하면서. 이렇게 그들은 여러 마일을 나아가다가, 테스가 정신을 차리고 둘레를 살피며 머뭇머뭇 말했다—\n\n\"우리 어디 정해 둔 데로 가는 거예요?\"\n\n\"모르겠어, 사랑하는 사람아. 왜?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"음, 우리 몇 마일 더 걸을 수도 있고, 저녁이 되면 어디든—외딴 오두막이라도—묵을 곳을 찾을 수 있겠지. 잘 걸을 수 있겠어, 테시?\"\n\n\"오 그럼요! 당신 팔이 나를 안고 있으면 영원히, 영원히라도 걸을 수 있어요!\"\n\n대체로 그렇게 하는 게 좋을 듯했다. 그리하여 그들은 걸음을 재촉했고, 큰길을 피해 대체로 북쪽으로 향하는 외진 길들을 따라갔다. 그러나 하루 종일 그들의 움직임에는 비현실적인 막연함이 있었다. 둘 중 누구도 효과적인 도주나 변장이나 장기간의 은신 같은 문제를 고려하는 것 같지 않았다. 그들의 모든 생각은 일시적이고 앞을 내다보지 못하는 것이었다, 마치 두 아이의 계획처럼.\n\n한낮에 그들은 길가의 여관 가까이 다가갔고, 테스는 그와 함께 들어가 뭔가 먹을 것을 구하려 했으나, 그가 그녀를 설득해 자기가 돌아올 때까지 절반은 숲이고 절반은 황야인 이 지역의 나무와 덤불 사이에 남아 있게 했다. 그녀의 옷은 최신 유행을 따른 것이었다. 심지어 그녀가 든 상아 손잡이 양산조차도, 그들이 지금 흘러들어온 이 외진 곳에서는 본 적 없는 모양이었다. 그런 물건들의 재단새는 선술집 긴 의자에 앉은 이들의 눈길을 끌 만했다. 그는 곧 돌아왔다, 여섯 사람분은 될 음식과 포도주 두 병을 들고서—혹시라도 무슨 비상사태가 생기면 하루나 그 이상 버틸 만큼이었다.\n\n그들은 죽은 나뭇가지 위에 앉아 식사를 나눴다. 한 시와 두 시 사이에 그들은 남은 것을 챙겨 다시 나아갔다.\n\n\"나 얼마든지 걸을 만큼 힘이 나요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 대체로 나라 안쪽으로 방향을 잡는 게 나을 것 같아. 거기서 한동안 숨어 있을 수 있고, 해안 근처 어디보다도 수색당할 가능성이 적으니까.\" 클레어가 말했다. \"나중에, 그들이 우리를 잊었을 때, 어느 항구로 향하면 돼.\"\n\n이 말에 그녀는 그를 더 꽉 붙잡는 것 말고는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 그들은 곧장 내륙으로 들어갔다. 영국의 오월이었지만 날씨는 잔잔하고 화창했고, 오후 동안은 제법 따뜻했다. 걷는 마지막 몇 마일을 지나는 동안 오솔길은 그들을 뉴 포레스트의 깊숙한 곳으로 데려갔고, 저녁 무렵 어느 좁은 길의 모퉁이를 돌다가, 그들은 개울과 다리 너머에 흰 글씨로 \"이 매력적인 저택을 가구 딸려 세놓음\"이라고 칠해진 커다란 간판을 보았다. 자세한 내용이 이어졌고, 런던의 어느 중개업소로 문의하라는 안내가 붙어 있었다. 문을 지나자 집이 보였다. 정연한 설계에 넉넉한 방을 갖춘 오래된 벽돌 건물이었다.\n\n\"나 저 집 알아.\" 클레어가 말했다. \"브램스허스트 코트야. 보다시피 문이 잠겨 있고, 진입로엔 풀이 자라고 있어.\"\n\n\"창문 몇 개는 열려 있는데요.\" 테스가 말했다.\n\n\"그냥 방을 환기하려는 거겠지.\"\n\n\"이 방들이 다 비어 있는데, 우리는 머리 위에 지붕 하나 없네요!\"\n\n\"지치는구나, 나의 테스!\" 그가 말했다. \"곧 멈출게.\" 그러고는 그녀의 슬픈 입에 입맞추고, 다시 그녀를 이끌고 나아갔다.\n\n그 역시 점점 지쳐 갔다. 그들은 열두어 마일에서 열다섯 마일을 헤맸으니, 어디서 쉴지를 궁리해야 할 때였다. 그들은 멀리서 외딴 오두막들과 작은 여관들을 바라보았고, 그중 여관 하나에 다가가려 마음먹다가도, 가슴이 졸아들어 비켜 갔다. 마침내 그들의 걸음은 질질 끌렸고, 그들은 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"우리 나무 아래서 자도 될까요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n그는 계절이 아직 그러기엔 충분히 무르익지 않았다고 생각했다.\n\n\"우리가 지나온 그 빈 저택을 생각하고 있었어.\" 그가 말했다. \"그쪽으로 다시 돌아가자.\"\n\n그들은 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔으나, 아까처럼 그 정문 밖에 서기까지 반 시간이 걸렸다. 그러고 나서 그는 안에 누가 있는지 보러 가는 동안 그녀에게 있던 자리에 있으라고 청했다.\n\n그녀는 문 안쪽 덤불 사이에 앉았고, 클레어는 집 쪽으로 살금살금 다가갔다. 그가 자리를 비운 시간은 제법 길었고, 그가 돌아왔을 때 테스는 자기 자신이 아니라 그를 위해 미친 듯이 애를 태우고 있었다. 그는 한 소년에게서 알아냈다, 관리인으로 늙은 여자 하나가 있을 뿐인데, 그 여자는 날씨 좋은 날에만 근처 마을에서 와서 창문을 여닫는다는 것을. 여자는 해 질 무렵 창문을 닫으러 올 터였다.\n\"이제 아래쪽 창문 하나로 들어가서 거기서 쉬면 돼.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그의 호위를 받으며 그녀는 머뭇머뭇 정면 본채로 나아갔다. 덧문을 닫은 창문들은 보지 못하는 눈알들처럼, 누가 지켜볼 가능성을 차단하고 있었다. 몇 걸음 더 가서 문에 닿았고, 그 옆 창문 하나가 열려 있었다. 클레어는 기어 올라 들어가, 테스를 뒤따라 끌어 올렸다.\n\n현관 홀을 빼면 방들은 모두 어둠 속에 있었고, 그들은 계단을 올랐다. 위층에서도 덧문은 단단히 닫혀 있었다. 환기는, 적어도 이날만큼은, 앞쪽 홀 창문 하나와 뒤쪽 위층 창문 하나를 여는 것으로 형식적으로만 되어 있었다. 클레어는 어느 커다란 방의 문 빗장을 풀고, 더듬어 가로질러 가서, 덧문을 두세 인치쯤 벌렸다. 눈부신 햇살 한 줄기가 방 안으로 비쳐 들어와, 묵직한 구식 가구와, 진홍색 다마스크 휘장과, 거대한 사주식 침대를 드러냈다. 침대 머리맡을 따라 달리는 형상들이 새겨져 있었는데, 보아하니 아탈란타의 경주인 듯했다.\n\n\"드디어 쉴 곳이다!\" 그가 가방과 음식 꾸러미를 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n그들은 관리인이 창문을 닫으러 올 때까지 깊은 정적 속에 머물렀다. 만일을 위해, 혹시 그 여자가 무슨 우연한 이유로든 자기네 방문을 열까 봐, 아까처럼 덧문을 가로질러 막아 완전한 어둠 속에 자기네 몸을 두었다. 여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이에 여자가 왔으나, 그들이 있는 별채 쪽으로는 다가오지 않았다. 그들은 여자가 창문을 닫고, 잠그고, 문을 잠그고, 떠나는 소리를 들었다. 그러자 클레어는 다시 창문에서 빛 한 줄기를 슬쩍 들였고, 그들은 또 한 끼를 나눴다. 그러다 이윽고 그들은 흩어 버릴 촛불 하나 없는 밤의 그늘에 휩싸였다.\n\n# LVIII\n\n밤은 이상하리만치 엄숙하고 고요했다. 깊은 밤 그녀는 그에게 모든 이야기를 속삭였다. 그가 자면서 그녀를 두 팔에 안고 프룸 개울을 건넜던 일, 둘 다의 목숨이 위태로운 위험 속에서, 그리고 폐허가 된 수도원의 돌 관 속에 그녀를 뉘었던 일을. 그는 이제껏 그 일을 전혀 모르고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 다음 날 말해 주지 않았어?\" 그가 말했다. \"그랬으면 많은 오해와 비통을 막을 수 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"지나간 일은 생각하지 말아요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 지금 이 순간 바깥의 것은 생각하지 않을 거예요. 왜 그래야 해요! 내일이 무엇을 품고 있는지 누가 알아요?\"\n\n하지만 내일에는 보아하니 아무런 슬픔도 없었다. 아침은 비가 내리고 안개가 끼었고, 관리인이 날씨 좋은 날에만 창문을 연다는 사실을 옳게 들어 알고 있던 클레어는, 테스를 자게 둔 채 과감히 방에서 기어 나와 집을 살펴보았다. 집 안에 음식은 없었으나 물은 있었고, 그는 안개를 틈타 저택에서 나가, 이 마일 너머 작은 마을의 가게에서 차와 빵과 버터를 사 왔다. 연기 없이 불을 피울 수 있도록 작은 양철 주전자와 알코올 램프도 함께. 그가 다시 들어오는 소리에 그녀가 깼고, 그들은 그가 사 온 것으로 아침을 먹었다.\n\n그들은 바깥을 나다닐 마음이 들지 않았고, 그렇게 하루가 지났고, 이어진 밤이, 그리고 다음 날이, 또 다음 날이 지났다. 마침내 그들 자신도 거의 의식하지 못한 채, 닷새가 완전한 은둔 속에 흘러갔다. 사람의 모습이나 소리 하나 그들의 평온을—그 나름의 평온을—깨뜨리지 않았다. 날씨의 변화가 그들의 유일한 사건이었고, 뉴 포레스트의 새들이 그들의 유일한 벗이었다. 말없는 합의에 따라, 그들은 결혼식 날 이후의 과거의 어떤 사건에 대해서도 단 한 번도 거의 입에 올리지 않았다. 그 음울한 사이의 시간은 혼돈 속으로 가라앉는 듯했고, 그 위로 현재와 그 이전의 시간들이, 마치 그런 일이 애초에 없었던 듯 맞닿아 닫혔다. 그가 그들이 이 은신처를 떠나 사우샘프턴이나 런던 쪽으로 나아가야 한다고 넌지시 비칠 때마다, 그녀는 움직이기를 이상하리만치 꺼렸다.\n\n\"왜 이 달콤하고 사랑스러운 모든 것에 끝을 내야 해요!\" 그녀가 못마땅한 듯 말했다. \"올 것은 오고 말 거예요.\" 그러고는 덧문 틈으로 내다보며, \"바깥은 죄다 괴로움이고, 이 안은 만족이에요.\"\n\n그도 밖을 엿보았다. 정말 그랬다. 안에는 애정과, 결합과, 용서받은 잘못이 있었고, 바깥에는 가차 없는 것이 있었다.\n\n\"그리고—그리고,\" 그녀가 자기 뺨을 그의 뺨에 비비며 말했다. \"당신이 지금 나를 생각하는 그 마음이 오래가지 못할까 봐 두려워요. 나는 당신이 지금 나에게 품은 감정보다 더 오래 살고 싶지 않아요. 차라리 그러지 않는 게 나아요. 당신이 나를 경멸하게 될 때가 오면, 차라리 나는 죽어 묻혀 있는 게 나아요. 그래서 당신이 나를 경멸했다는 걸 내가 영영 알지 못하도록요.\"\n\n\"나는 결코 당신을 경멸할 수 없어.\"\n\n\"나도 그러길 바라요. 하지만 내 삶이 어땠는지를 생각하면, 어째서 어떤 남자가 조만간 나를 경멸하지 않을 수 있을지 알 수가 없어요…. 내가 얼마나 사악하게 미쳤던지! 그래도 예전에 나는 파리 한 마리, 벌레 한 마리 다치게 하는 것도 차마 못 했고, 새장에 갇힌 새를 보면 자주 울곤 했어요.\"\n\n그들은 또 하루를 더 머물렀다. 밤사이 흐린 하늘이 개었고, 그 결과 오두막의 늙은 관리인이 일찍 깼다. 눈부신 일출에 그녀는 평소답지 않게 기운이 났다. 그녀는 곧바로 인접한 저택을 열고, 이런 날 철저히 환기를 시켜야겠다고 마음먹었다. 그리하여 여섯 시 전에 도착해 아래층 방들을 연 그녀는 위층 침실로 올라갔고, 그들이 누워 있는 방의 손잡이를 막 돌리려 했다. 그 순간 그녀는 안에서 사람들의 숨소리가 들리는 것 같았다. 슬리퍼와 그녀의 노령 덕분에 그녀의 발걸음은 거기까지 소리 없는 것이었고, 그녀는 곧장 물러나려 했다. 그러다, 자기 귀가 속았을지도 모른다 여기고, 다시 문 쪽으로 돌아서서 가만히 손잡이를 돌려 보았다. 자물쇠는 고장 나 있었으나, 안쪽에서 가구 하나가 앞으로 밀어붙여져 있어, 문을 한두 인치 이상 열 수 없었다. 덧문 틈으로 들어온 아침 빛줄기가 두 사람의 얼굴 위에 떨어졌다. 두 사람은 깊은 잠에 싸여 있었고, 테스의 입술은 그의 뺨 가까이에서 반쯤 핀 꽃처럼 벌어져 있었다. 관리인은 그들의 순진한 모습과, 의자 위에 걸쳐진 테스의 우아한 가운과, 그 옆의 비단 양말과, 예쁜 양산과, 다른 옷이 없어 그대로 입고 온 그 밖의 차림새에 어찌나 마음이 흔들렸던지, 떠돌이며 부랑자의 뻔뻔함에 대한 처음의 분노가, 이 점잖은 사랑의 도피로 보이는 것에 대한 잠깐의 감상으로 누그러졌다. 그녀는 문을 닫고, 왔을 때처럼 살며시 물러나, 이웃들에게 이 기이한 발견을 의논하러 갔다.\n\n그녀가 물러난 지 일 분도 채 지나지 않아 테스가 깼고, 이어 클레어가 깼다. 둘 다 무언가가 자기들을 깨웠다는 느낌이 들었으나, 그게 무엇인지는 말할 수 없었다. 그리고 그 느낌이 일으킨 불안은 점점 강해졌다. 옷을 입자마자 그는 두세 인치쯤 벌어진 덧문 틈으로 잔디밭을 유심히 살폈다.\n\n\"우리 당장 떠나는 게 좋겠어.\" 그가 말했다. \"날씨가 좋아. 그리고 누군가 집 근처에 있는 것 같은 생각이 자꾸 들어. 어쨌든 그 여자는 오늘 틀림없이 올 거야.\"\n\n그녀는 순순히 동의했고, 방을 정돈한 뒤, 그들은 자기네 것인 몇 안 되는 물건을 챙겨 소리 없이 떠났다. 숲속으로 들어섰을 때 그녀는 돌아서서 그 집을 마지막으로 한 번 바라보았다.\n\n\"아, 행복한 집—잘 있어!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내 삶은 이제 고작 몇 주의 문제일 뿐이에요. 우리가 거기 머물러 있으면 안 됐을까요?\"\n\n\"그런 말 하지 마, 테스! 우리는 곧 이 일대를 완전히 벗어날 거야. 시작한 대로 길을 이어 가서, 곧장 북쪽으로 가자. 아무도 거기서 우리를 찾을 생각은 못 할 거야. 만약 누가 우리를 찾는다 해도, 웨식스의 항구들에서 찾을 테니까. 북쪽에 닿으면 어느 항구로 가서 떠나는 거야.\"\n\n이렇게 그녀를 설득한 뒤 그 계획은 실행에 옮겨졌고, 그들은 일직선으로 북쪽을 향했다. 저택에서의 긴 휴식이 이제 그들에게 걷는 힘을 빌려주었다. 한낮 무렵 그들은 자기들이 첨탑이 솟은 도시 멜체스터에 가까워지고 있음을 알았다. 그 도시는 바로 그들의 길목에 놓여 있었다. 그는 오후 동안 그녀를 나무 숲에서 쉬게 하고, 어둠을 틈타 밀고 나아가기로 했다. 땅거미가 질 무렵 클레어는 여느 때처럼 음식을 샀고, 그들의 밤 행군이 시작되었다. 어퍼 웨식스와 미드 웨식스의 경계선을 넘은 것은 여덟 시쯤이었다.\n\n길에 그리 매이지 않고 들판을 가로질러 걷는 것은 테스에게 새로운 일이 아니었고, 그녀는 그 일에서 예전의 그 민첩함을 보였다. 길을 가로막은 도시, 고도 멜체스터는, 그들을 막아선 큰 강을 건너는 데 그 도시의 다리를 이용하기 위해 반드시 통과해야 했다. 그들이 인적 끊긴 거리를 걸어간 것은 자정쯤이었다. 거리는 몇 개 안 되는 가로등이 띄엄띄엄 비추고 있었고, 그들은 자기네 발소리가 울리지 않도록 포장도로를 피했다. 우아하게 솟은 대성당 건축물 더미가 그들의 왼편으로 어슴푸레 솟아 있었으나, 이제 그것은 그들의 안중에 없었다. 도시를 빠져나오자 그들은 유료 간선도로를 따라갔고, 그 길은 몇 마일 뒤 탁 트인 평원을 가로질러 내달았다. 하늘은 구름으로 빽빽했으나, 어느 조각달인지 모를 데서 퍼져 나온 빛이 지금껏 그들을 조금 도와주었다. 그러나 이제 달은 가라앉았고, 구름은 거의 그들 머리 위에 내려앉은 듯했으며, 밤은 동굴처럼 어두워졌다. 그래도 그들은 길을 더듬어 나아갔다. 발소리가 울리지 않도록 되도록 잔디 위로만 걸었는데, 어떤 울타리도 담장도 없었으므로 그러기는 쉬웠다. 사방은 탁 트인 외로움이요 검은 적막이었고, 그 위로 매서운 바람이 불었다.\n\n그들이 이렇게 더듬더듬 이삼 마일을 더 나아갔을 때, 문득 클레어는 자기 바로 앞에, 잔디에서 가파르게 솟아오른 어떤 거대한 구조물을 의식하게 되었다. 그들은 하마터면 거기에 부딪칠 뻔했다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 괴물 같은 곳이지?\" 에인절이 말했다.\n\n\"윙윙거려요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"들어 봐요!\"\n\n그가 귀를 기울였다. 바람이 그 건조물을 타고 놀며, 어떤 거대한 외줄 하프의 음 같은, 웅웅거리는 가락을 빚어냈다. 거기서 다른 소리는 나지 않았고, 손을 들어 한두 걸음 나아간 클레어는 그 구조물의 수직 표면을 만졌다. 그것은 이음매도 쇠시리 장식도 없는 단단한 돌인 듯했다. 손가락을 죽 더듬어 가다가 그는, 자기가 닿은 것이 거대한 직사각형 기둥임을 알았다. 왼손을 뻗자 바로 옆에 비슷한 기둥 하나가 만져졌다. 머리 위 가늠할 수 없는 높이에서 무언가가 검은 하늘을 더 검게 만들고 있었는데, 기둥들을 수평으로 잇는 거대한 들보의 모습을 하고 있었다. 그들은 조심스레 그 아래, 그 사이로 들어섰다. 그들의 부드러운 옷 스치는 소리가 표면에 메아리쳤다. 그러나 그들은 여전히 바깥에 있는 듯했다. 그곳에는 지붕이 없었다. 테스는 두려운 듯 숨을 들이켰고, 어리둥절한 에인절이 말했다—\n\n\"이게 뭘까?\"\n\n옆으로 더듬어 가다가 그들은 또 하나의 탑 같은 기둥과 마주쳤다, 첫 번째 것처럼 네모지고 단호한 기둥과. 그 너머에 또 하나, 또 하나가 있었다. 그곳은 온통 문이요 기둥이었고, 더러는 위쪽에서 끊임없이 이어진 들보로 연결되어 있었다.\n\n\"그야말로 바람의 신전이군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n다음 기둥은 외따로 서 있었고, 다른 것들은 삼석탑을 이루었다. 또 다른 것들은 쓰러져 누워, 그 옆구리가 마차 한 대가 지날 만큼 넓은 둑길을 이루었다. 그리고 그것들이 평원의 풀 펼쳐진 너른 터 위에 무리 지은 거대한 돌기둥 숲을 이루고 있음이 곧 분명해졌다. 두 사람은 이 밤의 정자 속으로 더 들어가, 마침내 그 한복판에 섰다.\n\n\"스톤헨지야!\" 클레어가 말했다.\n\n\"이교도의 신전 말이에요?\"\n\n\"응. 여러 세기보다 오래된, 더버빌 가보다도 오래된 곳이야! 자, 어쩌지, 사랑하는 사람아? 더 가면 숨을 곳을 찾을지도 몰라.\"\n\n그러나 이때쯤 정말로 지친 테스는, 바로 곁에 놓인 직사각형 석판 위에 몸을 던졌다. 그것은 기둥 하나가 바람을 막아 주는 자리였다. 전날 햇볕이 닿은 덕에, 그 돌은 따뜻하고 말라 있어, 그녀의 치마와 신발을 적셨던 거칠고 차가운 둘레의 풀과는 위안이 될 만큼 대조적이었다.\n\n\"더는 가고 싶지 않아요, 에인절.\" 그녀가 그에게 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"우리 여기 머무르면 안 돼요?\"\n\n\"안 될 것 같아. 이곳은 지금은 그렇게 보이지 않지만, 낮에는 몇 마일 밖에서도 보이거든.\"\n\n\"생각해 보니, 우리 어머니 쪽 사람 하나가 이 근방에서 양치기였대요. 그리고 당신은 탤보세이즈에서 내가 이교도라고 늘 말하곤 했죠. 그러니 이제 나는 집에 온 셈이네요.\"\n\n그는 길게 뻗은 그녀의 몸 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 자기 입술을 그녀의 입술에 댔다.\n\n\"졸려, 사랑하는 사람아? 당신 지금 제단 위에 누워 있는 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"여기 있는 게 아주 좋아요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"어찌나 엄숙하고 외로운지—내 크나큰 행복 뒤에—내 얼굴 위로 하늘밖에 아무것도 없으니. 이 세상에 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 없는 것 같아요. 그리고 정말 그랬으면 좋겠어요—'라이자-루만 빼고는.\"\n\n클레어는 좀 더 밝아질 때까지 그녀가 여기서 쉬어도 괜찮겠다고 생각했고, 그는 그녀에게 자기 외투를 덮어 주고 그 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"에인절, 만약 나한테 무슨 일이 생기면, 나를 봐서 '라이자-루를 보살펴 주겠어요?\" 그들이 기둥 사이로 부는 바람 소리를 오래 듣고 있던 끝에 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"그러마.\"\n\n\"그 애는 참 착하고 순박하고 순결해요. 오, 에인절—머지않아 그렇게 되겠지만, 나를 잃으면 그 애와 결혼해 주면 좋겠어요. 오, 그래 준다면!\"\n\n\"당신을 잃으면 나는 모든 걸 잃는 거야! 게다가 그 애는 내 처제잖아.\"\n\n\"그런 건 아무것도 아니에요, 사랑하는 사람아. 말럿 근방에서는 사람들이 늘 처제와 결혼하는걸요. 그리고 '라이자-루는 어찌나 상냥하고 다정한지, 게다가 점점 아름다워지고 있어요. 오, 우리가 영혼이 되면 나는 기꺼이 당신을 그 애와 나눌 수 있어요! 당신이 그 애를 가르치고 길러서, 당신 자신을 위해 키워 준다면, 에인절!… 그 애는 내 좋은 점은 다 가졌으면서 내 나쁜 점은 없어요. 그리고 그 애가 당신 사람이 된다면, 죽음이 우리를 갈라놓지 않은 것이나 거의 마찬가지일 거예요… 자, 다 말했어요. 다시는 이 얘기 꺼내지 않을게요.\"\n\n그녀가 말을 멈추자 그는 생각에 잠겼다. 저 먼 북동쪽 하늘, 기둥들 사이로 그는 평평한 한 줄기 빛을 볼 수 있었다. 검은 구름의 한결같은 오목한 천장이, 마치 솥뚜껑처럼 통째로 들리며, 땅의 가장자리로 다가오는 낮을 들여보내고 있었다. 그 빛을 배경으로 우뚝 솟은 돌기둥들과 삼석탑들이 검게 윤곽을 드러내기 시작했다.\n\n\"여기서 사람들이 하느님께 제물을 바쳤을까요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"누구에게요?\"\n\"태양에게였을 거야. 저 외따로 떨어져 세워진 높다란 돌이 태양의 방향에 있고, 곧 그 뒤로 해가 떠오를 거야.\"\n\n\"이걸 보니 생각나요, 여보.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"기억해요, 우리가 결혼하기 전에는 당신이 내 어떤 믿음에도 결코 간섭하려 들지 않았던 거? 하지만 나는 그래도 당신 마음을 알았고, 당신이 생각하는 대로 나도 생각했어요—내 나름의 어떤 이유에서가 아니라, 당신이 그렇게 생각했기 때문에요. 이제 말해 줘요, 에인절, 우리가 죽은 뒤에 다시 만날 거라고 생각해요? 알고 싶어요.\"\n\n그는 이런 때에 대답을 피하려고 그녀에게 입맞추었다.\n\n\"오, 에인절—그건 아니라는 뜻인가 봐요!\" 그녀가 흐느낌을 누르며 말했다. \"그리고 나는 당신을 다시 보고 싶었는데—그토록, 그토록! 뭐라고요—그렇게 서로 잘 사랑하는 우리, 당신과 나조차도 안 된다고요, 에인절?\"\n\n자기보다 더 위대한 분처럼, 결정적인 때의 결정적인 물음에 그는 대답하지 않았다. 그리고 그들은 다시 침묵에 잠겼다. 한두 분 만에 그녀의 숨결이 한결 고르게 되었고, 그의 손을 쥔 그녀의 손아귀가 풀렸으며, 그녀는 잠이 들었다. 동쪽 지평선을 따라 뻗은 은빛 창백함의 띠가, 그레이트 플레인의 먼 부분들마저 어둡고 가깝게 보이게 했다. 그리고 그 거대한 풍경 전체는, 동트기 직전에 흔한 그 절제와, 과묵함과, 머뭇거림의 인상을 띠고 있었다. 동쪽의 기둥들과 그 들보들이 빛을 배경으로 검게 솟아 있었고, 그 너머의 거대한 불꽃 모양 태양석도, 그리고 그 중간의 제물석도. 이윽고 밤바람이 잦아들었고, 돌들의 잔 같은 오목한 곳에 고인 작은 웅덩이들의 떨림도 멎었다.\n같은 때, 무언가가 동쪽 우묵한 곳의 가장자리에서 움직이는 듯했다—한낱 점 하나가. 태양석 너머 우묵한 데서 그들에게 다가오는 한 남자의 머리였다. 클레어는 자기들이 더 나아갔더라면 좋았으리라 생각했으나, 이 상황에서는 가만히 있기로 했다. 그 형체는 그들이 있는 기둥의 원을 향해 곧장 다가왔다.\n\n그는 등 뒤에서 무슨 소리를, 발이 풀을 스치는 소리를 들었다. 돌아보니, 쓰러진 기둥들 너머로 또 하나의 형체가 보였다. 그러더니 그가 미처 알아채기도 전에, 또 하나가 삼석탑 아래 오른쪽 가까이에 있었고, 또 하나가 왼쪽에 있었다. 동이 서쪽에 선 남자의 정면을 가득 비추었고, 클레어는 이로써 그가 키가 크고, 훈련받은 사람처럼 걷는다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그들은 모두 분명한 목적을 가지고 좁혀 들어왔다. 그렇다면 그녀의 이야기는 사실이었던 것이다! 벌떡 일어선 그는 무기든, 굴러다니는 돌이든, 달아날 방도든, 무엇이든 찾아 둘레를 둘러보았다. 그러는 사이 가장 가까운 남자가 그에게 다가와 있었다.\n\n\"소용없습니다, 선생.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리는 평원에 열여섯 명이 있고, 온 고장이 들고일어나 있습니다.\"\n\n\"이 사람 잠을 마저 자게 해 주시오!\" 그가 둘러 모여드는 사람들에게 속삭이며 애원했다.\n\n그제야 비로소 그녀가 누워 있는 자리를 본 그들은, 아무 이의도 보이지 않고, 둘레의 기둥들처럼 가만히 서서 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그는 돌로 가서 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히고, 가엾은 작은 손 하나를 잡았다. 그녀의 숨결은 이제 빠르고 작아서, 여자라기보다 더 작은 어떤 생물의 것 같았다. 모두가 점점 밝아 오는 빛 속에서 기다렸다. 그들의 얼굴과 손은 은빛으로 입혀진 듯했고, 나머지 몸은 어두웠으며, 돌들은 초록빛 도는 잿빛으로 반들거렸고, 평원은 여전히 그림자 덩어리였다. 곧 빛이 강해졌고, 한 줄기 빛살이 그녀의 의식 없는 몸 위에 비쳐, 그녀의 눈꺼풀 아래를 들여다보며 그녀를 깨웠다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요, 에인절?\" 그녀가 화들짝 일어나며 말했다. \"그들이 나를 데리러 왔어요?\"\n\n\"그래, 사랑하는 사람아.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들이 왔어.\"\n\n\"마땅히 그래야 하는 대로네요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"에인절, 나는 거의 기뻐요—그래요, 기뻐요! 이 행복은 오래갈 수 없었을 거예요. 너무 과분했어요. 나는 충분히 누렸어요. 그리고 이제 당신이 나를 경멸하도록 살아 있지 않아도 되니까요!\"\n\n그녀는 일어서서 몸을 한 번 추스르고 앞으로 나아갔다. 두 남자 모두 움직이지 않았다.\n\n\"준비됐어요.\" 그녀가 조용히 말했다.\n\n# LIX\n\n윈톤체스터, 그 아름다운 옛 도시, 한때 웨식스의 수도였던 그곳은, 칠월 아침의 온갖 밝음과 따스함 속에서, 볼록하고 오목한 구릉지 사이에 자리하고 있었다. 박공 달린 벽돌과 기와와 자유석으로 지은 집들은 그 철 동안 자기를 감싼 이끼의 외피를 거의 말려 떨군 참이었고, 풀밭의 개울들은 수위가 낮았으며, 서쪽 성문에서 중세의 십자가까지, 또 중세의 십자가에서 다리까지 비탈진 하이 스트리트에서는, 흔히 구식 장날을 여는 그 느긋한 먼지 떨기와 비질이 한창이었다.\n\n앞서 말한 서쪽 성문에서부터 큰길은, 윈톤체스터 사람이라면 누구나 알듯, 정확히 측량된 일 마일 길이의 길고 한결같은 비탈을 오르며, 차츰 집들을 뒤로 남겼다. 도시의 경내에서부터 이 길을 따라 두 사람이 빠르게 걷고 있었다. 마치 그 고된 오르막을 의식하지 못하는 듯—기운이 넘쳐서가 아니라 골똘한 생각에 잠겨 의식하지 못하는 것이었다. 그들은 조금 아래쪽 높은 담장의, 좁고 빗장 달린 쪽문을 통해 이 길로 나온 터였다. 그들은 집들과 자기 동족의 시야에서 벗어나기를 애타게 바라는 듯했고, 이 길이 그렇게 할 가장 빠른 수단을 내주는 것 같았다. 그들은 젊었으나, 고개를 숙인 채 걸었다. 그 비탄에 잠긴 걸음걸이 위로 햇살이 무자비하게 미소 지었다.\n\n두 사람 중 하나는 에인절 클레어였고, 다른 하나는 키 크고 한창 피어나는 존재였다—반은 소녀, 반은 여인인—테스의 영적인 영상, 그녀보다 가냘프지만 똑같은 아름다운 눈을 가진—클레어의 처제, '라이자-루였다. 그들의 창백한 얼굴은 본래 크기의 절반으로 쪼그라든 듯했다. 그들은 손을 맞잡고 나아갔고, 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 그 숙인 고개는 조토의 「두 사도」의 그것이었다.\n\n그들이 거대한 웨스트 힐 꼭대기에 거의 다다랐을 때, 도시의 시계들이 여덟 시를 쳤다. 그 종소리에 둘은 저마다 흠칫했고, 몇 걸음 더 나아가 첫 번째 이정표에 닿았다. 그것은 풀의 푸른 가장자리에 희게 서 있었고, 여기서 길로 트인 구릉을 등지고 있었다. 그들은 잔디로 들어섰고, 자기네 의지를 압도하는 듯한 어떤 힘에 떠밀려, 문득 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 돌아서서, 그 돌 곁에서 마비된 듯한 긴장 속에 기다렸다.\n\n이 꼭대기에서 바라보는 전망은 거의 끝이 없었다. 아래 골짜기에는 그들이 방금 떠나온 도시가 자리해 있었고, 그 도드라진 건물들은 등각 투상도 속에서처럼 보였다—그중에는 노르만식 창문과 어마어마하게 긴 측랑과 신랑을 가진 너른 대성당 탑이, 성 토머스 교회의 첨탑들이, 칼리지의 작은 첨탑 달린 탑이, 그리고 더 오른쪽으로는, 오늘날까지도 순례자가 빵과 맥주의 적선을 받을 수 있는 옛 구호소의 탑과 박공들이 있었다. 도시 뒤로는 성 캐서린 언덕의 둥그스름한 고지가 휩쓸듯 뻗어 있었고, 더 멀리에는 풍경 너머 풍경이 이어지다가, 마침내 지평선이 그 위에 걸린 태양의 광휘 속으로 사라졌다.\n\n이 먼 들녘의 펼쳐짐을 배경으로, 다른 도시 건물들 앞쪽에 커다란 붉은 벽돌 건물이 솟아 있었다. 평평한 잿빛 지붕과, 갇혀 있음을 말해 주는 짧은 빗장 창문들의 줄을 가진 그것은, 고딕 건축물들의 예스러운 불규칙함과는 그 형식주의로 크게 대조를 이루었다. 그곳은 지나치며 보면 주목나무와 상록 떡갈나무들에 가려 길에서는 다소 가려져 있었으나, 이 위에서는 충분히 보였다. 두 사람이 방금 나온 그 쪽문은 바로 이 건물의 담장에 있었다. 건물 한가운데서 흉한 평평한 꼭대기의 팔각 탑이 동쪽 지평선을 배경으로 솟아 있었는데, 이 자리에서, 그 그늘진 쪽에서 빛을 등지고 보니, 그것은 이 도시의 아름다움에 찍힌 단 하나의 오점인 듯했다. 그러나 두 응시자가 관심을 둔 것은, 그 아름다움이 아니라 바로 이 오점이었다.\n\n탑의 처마돌림띠 위에는 키 큰 깃대 하나가 세워져 있었다. 그들의 눈은 거기에 못 박혀 있었다. 정시를 친 지 몇 분 뒤, 무언가가 깃대를 따라 천천히 올라가, 바람결에 자기를 펼쳤다. 검은 깃발이었다.\n\n「정의」는 이루어졌고, 아이스킬로스적 표현으로 말하자면, 불멸자들의 주재자가 테스를 데리고 벌인 자기 놀음을 끝냈다. 그리고 더버빌 가의 기사들과 귀부인들은 자기들의 무덤 속에서 아무것도 모른 채 계속 잠들어 있었다. 말없는 두 응시자는, 마치 기도하듯, 몸을 땅으로 굽혔고, 그렇게 오랫동안, 미동도 없이 머물러 있었다. 깃발은 소리 없이 계속 나부꼈다. 기운을 차리자마자 그들은 일어나, 다시 손을 맞잡고, 길을 떠났다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-110"]=[[["The main portion of the following story appeared—with slight modifications—in the _Graphic_ newspaper; other chapters, more especially addressed to adult readers, in the _Fortnightly Review_ and the _National Observer_, as episodic sketches.", "다음 이야기의 주요 부분은—약간의 수정을 거쳐—『그래픽』 신문에 실렸고, 다른 장들은, 특히 성인 독자를 겨냥한 부분은, 삽화적 소품의 형태로 『포트나이틀리 리뷰』와 『내셔널 옵서버』에 실렸다."], ["My thanks are tendered to the editors and proprietors of those periodicals for enabling me now to piece the trunk and limbs of the novel together, and print it complete, as originally written two years ago.", "이제 소설의 몸통과 사지를 한데 이어붙여, 이 년 전 처음 쓴 그대로 완전한 형태로 인쇄할 수 있게 해 준 이 정기간행물들의 편집자와 발행인 들에게 감사를 전한다."]], [["I will just add that the story is sent out in all sincerity of purpose, as an attempt to give artistic form to a true sequence of things; and in respect of the book’s opinions and sentiments, I would ask any too genteel reader, who cannot endure to have said what everybody nowadays thinks and feels, to remember a well-worn sentence of St.", "다만 한마디 덧붙이자면, 이 이야기는 사물의 참된 흐름에 예술적 형식을 부여하려는 시도로서 온전히 진실한 목적으로 세상에 내놓는 것이다. 그리고 이 책의 견해와 정서에 관해서는, 요즘 누구나 생각하고 느끼는 바를 말로 옮긴 것을 견딜 수 없어 하는 지나치게 점잖은 독자가 있다면, 성 히에로니무스의 닳고 닳은 한 문장을 떠올려 주기를 청한다."], ["Jerome’s: If an offense come out of the truth, better it is that the offense come than that the truth be concealed.", "진리에서 어떤 거슬림이 나온다면, 진리를 감추는 것보다 그 거슬림이 나오는 편이 낫다. T.H."]], [["_November_ 1891.", "1891년 11월."]], [["Author’s Preface to the Fifth and Later Editions\nThis novel being one wherein the great campaign of the heroine begins after an event in her experience which has usually been treated as fatal to her part of protagonist, or at least as the virtual ending of her enterprises and hopes, it was quite contrary to avowed conventions that the public should welcome the book and agree with me in holding that there was something more to be said in fiction than had been said about the shaded side of a well-known catastrophe.", "이 소설은 여주인공의 위대한 분투가, 보통 그녀의 주인공 역할에 치명적이라고, 적어도 그녀의 기획과 희망의 사실상 종말이라고 여겨져 온 한 사건 이후에 비로소 시작되는 작품이다. 그러니 잘 알려진 어떤 파국의 그늘진 면에 대해 소설에서 일찍이 말해진 것보다 더 말할 거리가 남아 있다고 보는 나의 견해에 대중이 동의하고 이 책을 환영해 준 것은, 공언된 관습에는 자못 어긋나는 일이었다."], ["But the responsive spirit in which _Tess of the d’Urbervilles_ has been received by the readers of England and America would seem to prove that the plan of laying down a story on the lines of tacit opinion, instead of making it to square with the merely vocal formulae of society, is not altogether a wrong one, even when exemplified in so unequal and partial an achievement as the present.", "그러나 『더버빌가의 테스』가 영국과 미국의 독자들에게 받은 호응의 정신을 보면, 사회의 한낱 입에 발린 정식에 들어맞도록 이야기를 짓는 대신 암묵적 여론의 선을 따라 이야기를 펼치는 방식이, 이번처럼 고르지 못하고 불완전한 성취로 예시된 경우에조차, 아주 그릇된 것만은 아님이 입증되는 듯하다."], ["For this responsiveness I cannot refrain from expressing my thanks; and my regret is that, in a world where one so often hungers in vain for friendship, where even not to be wilfully misunderstood is felt as a kindness, I shall never meet in person these appreciative readers, male and female, and shake them by the hand.", "이 호응에 대해 나는 감사의 뜻을 표하지 않을 수 없다. 그리고 나의 유감은, 우정을 그토록 자주 헛되이 갈구하는 세상에서, 일부러 오해받지 않는 것만으로도 친절로 느껴지는 이 세상에서, 이 고마운 독자들을, 남자든 여자든, 직접 만나 손을 맞잡을 일은 결코 없으리라는 점이다."]], [["I include amongst them the reviewers—by far the majority—who have so generously welcomed the tale.", "그들 가운데 나는 비평가들도—압도적 다수를—포함시킨다. 이 이야기를 그토록 너그러이 환영해 준 이들 말이다."], ["Their words show that they, like the others, have only too largely repaired my defects of narration by their own imaginative intuition.", "그들의 말은, 그들 역시 다른 독자들처럼, 자신의 상상적 직관으로 나의 서술의 결함을 너무도 크게 메워 주었음을 보여 준다."]], [["Nevertheless, though the novel was intended to be neither didactic nor aggressive, but in the scenic parts to be representative simply and in the contemplative to be oftener charged with impressions than with convictions, there have been objectors both to the matter and to the rendering.", "그럼에도, 이 소설은 교훈적이지도 공격적이지도 않게, 다만 장면을 그린 부분에서는 단순히 재현적으로, 사색하는 부분에서는 확신보다 인상을 더 많이 담도록 의도되었으나, 내용에 대해서도 표현에 대해서도 반대하는 이들이 있었다."]], [["The more austere of these maintain a conscientious difference of opinion concerning, among other things, subjects fit for art, and reveal an inability to associate the idea of the sub-title adjective with any but the artificial and derivative meaning which has resulted to it from the ordinances of civilization.", "이들 가운데 더 준엄한 자들은, 여러 가지 중에서도 예술에 적합한 소재에 관해 양심에 따른 견해 차이를 고수하며, 부제에 붙은 형용사의 관념을 문명의 규범이 그 말에 부여한 인위적이고 파생적인 의미 말고는 어떤 것과도 결부시키지 못하는 무능을 드러낸다."], ["They ignore the meaning of the word in Nature, together with all aesthetic claims upon it, not to mention the spiritual interpretation afforded by the finest side of their own Christianity.", "그들은 자연 안에서 그 말이 지니는 의미를, 그에 대한 모든 미학적 권리와 더불어 무시하며, 자기네 기독교의 가장 고결한 측면이 제공하는 영적 해석은 말할 것도 없다."], ["Others dissent on grounds which are intrinsically no more than an assertion that the novel embodies the views of life prevalent at the end of the nineteenth century, and not those of an earlier and simpler generation—an assertion which I can only hope may be well founded.", "또 다른 이들은, 본질적으로 따지자면 이 소설이 더 이르고 단순한 세대의 인생관이 아니라 십구 세기 말에 만연한 인생관을 구현하고 있다는 주장에 지나지 않는 근거로 반대한다—나로서는 그 주장이 근거가 충분하기를 바랄 따름인 주장이다. 다시 한번 말하건대, 소설은 인상이지 논증이 아니다."], ["Let me repeat that a novel is an impression, not an argument; and there the matter must rest; as one is reminded by a passage which occurs in the letters of Schiller to Goethe on judges of this class: “They are those who seek only their own ideas in a representation, and prize that which should be as higher than what is.", "그러니 이 문제는 거기서 멈추어야 한다. 이 부류의 심판자들에 관해 실러가 괴테에게 보낸 편지에 나오는 한 구절이 떠오른다. \"그들은 재현 속에서 오직 자기 자신의 관념만을 찾는 자들이며, 있는 것보다 있어야 할 것을 더 높이 친다."], ["The cause of the dispute, therefore, lies in the very first principles, and it would be utterly impossible to come to an understanding with them.”", "그러므로 분쟁의 원인은 바로 그 첫 원리에 있으니, 그들과 합의에 이르기란 도무지 불가능하다.\" 그리고 또 이렇게 말한다."], ["And again: “As soon as I observe that any one, when judging of poetical representations, considers anything more important than the inner Necessity and Truth, I have done with him.”", "\"시적 재현을 판단하면서 누군가가 내적 필연성과 진리보다 무언가를 더 중하게 여기는 것을 보는 순간, 나는 그자와 끝이다.\""]], [["In the introductory words to the first edition I suggested the possible advent of the genteel person who would not be able to endure something or other in these pages.", "초판 서문의 머리말에서 나는 이 책장들 속의 무언가를 견디지 못할 점잖은 인물이 나타날 가능성을 시사한 바 있다."], ["That person duly appeared among the aforesaid objectors.", "그 인물은 앞서 말한 반대자들 가운데 어김없이 나타났다."], ["In one case he felt upset that it was not possible for him to read the book through three times, owing to my not having made that critical effort which “alone can prove the salvation of such an one.”", "한 경우에 그는, \"오직 그것만이 그런 자의 구원을 입증할 수 있는\" 비평적 노력을 내가 기울이지 않은 탓에 이 책을 세 번 통독하기란 불가능하다고 속상해했다."], ["In another, he objected to such vulgar articles as the Devil’s pitchfork, a lodging-house carving-knife, and a shame-bought parasol, appearing in a respectable story.", "또 다른 경우에는, 점잖은 이야기 속에 악마의 쇠스랑이라든가 하숙집 고기칼, 수치스럽게 사들인 양산 따위의 천박한 물건들이 등장하는 데 반대했다."], ["In another place he was a gentleman who turned Christian for half-an-hour the better to express his grief that a disrespectful phrase about the Immortals should have been used; though the same innate gentility compelled him to excuse the author in words of pity that one cannot be too thankful for: “He does but give us of his best.”", "또 다른 대목에서 그는, 불멸의 신들에 관해 불경한 표현이 쓰인 데 대한 슬픔을 더 잘 드러내려고 반시간 동안 기독교도가 된 신사였다. 다만 바로 그 타고난 점잖음이, 그로 하여금 아무리 고마워해도 지나치지 않을 연민의 말로 저자를 용서하게 만들었으니, \"그저 자기 최선을 우리에게 줄 뿐인 것을.\""], ["I can assure this great critic that to exclaim illogically against the gods, singular or plural, is not such an original sin of mine as he seems to imagine.", "이 위대한 비평가에게 나는 이렇게 장담할 수 있다. 신들을, 단수든 복수든, 논리에 맞지 않게 향해 외치는 것은 그가 상상하는 만큼 나만의 독창적인 원죄가 아니라고."], ["True, it may have some local originality; though if Shakespeare were an authority on history, which perhaps he is not, I could show that the sin was introduced into Wessex as early as the Heptarchy itself.", "사실 거기에 약간의 국지적 독창성은 있을지 모른다. 다만 셰익스피어가 역사의 권위자라면—아마 아닐 테지만—나는 그 죄가 칠왕국 시대만큼이나 일찍이 웨식스에 들어왔음을 보여 줄 수 있다."], ["Says Glo’ster in _Lear_, otherwise Ina, king of that country:", "『리어왕』에서 글로스터가, 달리 말하면 그 나라의 왕 이나가 이렇게 말한다."]], [["As flies to wanton boys are we to the gods; They kill us for their sport.", "장난꾸러기 소년들에게 파리가 그러하듯 우리는 신들에게 그러하니, 그들은 재미 삼아 우리를 죽인다."]], [["The remaining two or three manipulators of Tess were of the predetermined sort whom most writers and readers would gladly forget; professed literary boxers, who put on their convictions for the occasion; modern “Hammers of Heretics”; sworn Discouragers, ever on the watch to prevent the tentative half-success from becoming the whole success later on; who pervert plain meanings, and grow personal under the name of practising the great historical method.", "테스를 주물러 댄 나머지 두세 명은, 대다수 작가와 독자가 기꺼이 잊어버리고 싶어 할 미리 작정한 부류였다. 자칭 문학의 권투 선수들, 그때그때 필요에 따라 자기 신념을 걸치는 자들, 현대판 \"이단자를 두드리는 망치들\", 잠정적인 절반의 성공이 훗날 온전한 성공으로 자라지 못하게 늘 망을 보는 맹세한 낙담 조장자들이었다. 그들은 명백한 의미를 왜곡하고, 위대한 역사적 방법을 실천한다는 명목 아래 인신공격을 일삼는다."], ["However, they may have causes to advance, privileges to guard, traditions to keep going; some of which a mere tale-teller, who writes down how the things of the world strike him, without any ulterior intentions whatever, has overlooked, and may by pure inadvertence have run foul of when in the least aggressive mood.", "하지만 그들에게도 내세울 명분이, 지킬 특권이, 이어 갈 전통이 있을지 모른다. 세상의 일들이 자기에게 어떻게 비치는지를, 그 어떤 숨은 의도도 없이 받아 적을 뿐인 한낱 이야기꾼은 그중 더러를 못 보고 지나쳤을 수도 있고, 조금도 공격할 뜻이 없을 때조차 순전한 부주의로 거기에 부딪쳤을 수도 있다."], ["Perhaps some passing perception, the outcome of a dream hour, would, if generally acted on, cause such an assailant considerable inconvenience with respect to position, interests, family, servant, ox, ass, neighbour, or neighbour’s wife.", "어쩌면 어느 꿈결 같은 한때의 산물인 지나가는 한 가닥 통찰이, 널리 실행에 옮겨진다면, 그런 공격자에게 지위와 이해관계와 가족, 하인, 소, 나귀, 이웃, 또는 이웃의 아내와 관련해 적잖은 불편을 끼칠지도 모른다."], ["He therefore valiantly hides his personality behind a publisher’s shutters, and cries “Shame!”", "그래서 그는 출판사의 덧문 뒤에 자기 정체를 용감하게 숨긴 채 \"수치다!\" 하고 외치는 것이다."], ["So densely is the world with any shifting of positions, even the best warranted advance, galls somebody’s kibe.", "세상은 어찌나 빽빽한지, 지위가 조금이라도 옮겨지면, 아무리 정당한 진전이라도, 누군가의 발뒤꿈치 부르튼 자리를 쓰리게 한다."], ["Such shiftings often begin in sentiment, and such sentiment sometimes begins in a novel.", "그런 옮겨짐은 흔히 정서에서 비롯되고, 그런 정서는 때로 소설 한 편에서 비롯된다."]], [["_July_ 1892.", "1892년 7월."]], [["The foregoing remarks were written during the early career of this story, when a spirited public and private criticism of its points was still fresh to the feelings.", "앞의 말들은 이 이야기의 초기 행로에서, 그 논점들에 대한 공적·사적 비평이 아직 감정에 생생하던 무렵에 쓴 것이다."], ["The pages are allowed to stand for what they are worth, as something once said; but probably they would not have been written now.", "한때 했던 말로서, 그 나름의 값어치만큼 그대로 두기로 한다. 다만 아마 지금이라면 쓰지 않았을 것이다."], ["Even in the first short time which has elapsed since the book was first published, some of the critics who have provoked the reply have “gone down into silence,” as if to remind one of the infinite unimportance of both their say and mine.", "책이 처음 출간된 이래 흘러간 짧은 시간 동안에도, 그 응답을 불러온 비평가들 가운데 일부는 \"침묵 속으로 내려갔으니\", 마치 그들의 말이나 나의 말이나 무한히 하찮음을 일깨우려는 듯하다."]], [["_January_ 1895.", "1895년 1월."]], [["The present edition of this novel contains a few pages that have never appeared in any previous edition.", "이 소설의 이번 판에는 이전 어느 판에도 실린 적 없는 몇 쪽이 들어 있다."], ["When the detached episodes were collected as stated in the preface of 1891, these pages were overlooked, though they were in the original manuscript. They occur in Chapter X.", "1891년 서문에 적은 대로 따로 떨어진 삽화들을 한데 모을 때, 이 쪽들은 원래 원고에 있었음에도 빠뜨려졌다. 이 부분은 10장에 나온다."]], [["Respecting the sub-title, to which allusion was made above, I may add that it was appended at the last moment, after reading the final proofs, as being the estimate left in a candid mind of the heroine’s character—an estimate that nobody would be likely to dispute.", "부제에 관해서는, 앞서 언급한 그 부제 말인데, 한마디 덧붙이자면 그것은 최종 교정쇄를 읽은 뒤 마지막 순간에 붙인 것으로, 여주인공의 성격에 대해 공정한 마음이 남긴 평가, 그러니까 누구도 이의를 달지 않으리라 여겨질 평가였다. 그런데 그것이 이 책에서 무엇보다도 가장 큰 이의를 받았다."], ["It was disputed more than anything else in the book.", "차라리 쓰지 않았던 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["_Melius fuerat non scibere._ But there it stands.", "그러나 그것은 그대로 거기에 있다."]], [["The novel was first published complete, in three volumes, in November, 1891.", "이 소설은 1891년 11월, 세 권짜리 완전판으로 처음 출간되었다."]], [["_March_ 1912.", "T.H."]], [["Phase the First:", "1912년 3월."]], [["The Maiden", "처녀\nI"]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["On an evening in the latter part of May a middle-aged man was walking homeward from Shaston to the village of Marlott, in the adjoining Vale of Blakemore, or Blackmoor.", "오월 하순 어느 저녁, 한 중년 남자가 섀스턴에서 이웃한 블레이크모어, 또는 블랙무어 골짜기에 있는 말럿 마을로 걸어 돌아가고 있었다."], ["The pair of legs that carried him were rickety, and there was a bias in his gait which inclined him somewhat to the left of a straight line.", "그를 떠받친 두 다리는 후들거렸고, 걸음걸이에는 한쪽으로 쏠리는 버릇이 있어 그를 직선에서 다소 왼쪽으로 기울게 했다."], ["He occasionally gave a smart nod, as if in confirmation of some opinion, though he was not thinking of anything in particular.", "그는 무언가 딱히 생각하는 것도 아니면서, 마치 어떤 의견을 확인이라도 하듯 이따금 고개를 야무지게 끄덕였다."], ["An empty egg-basket was slung upon his arm, the nap of his hat was ruffled, a patch being quite worn away at its brim where his thumb came in taking it off.", "빈 달걀 바구니가 그의 팔에 걸려 있었고, 모자의 보풀은 헝클어졌으며, 챙에서 모자를 벗을 때 엄지손가락이 닿는 자리는 천이 완전히 닳아 떨어져 있었다."], ["Presently he was met by an elderly parson astride on a gray mare, who, as he rode, hummed a wandering tune.", "이윽고 회색 암말에 올라탄 나이 든 목사가 그를 마주쳤는데, 목사는 말을 타고 가며 두서없는 가락을 흥얼거리고 있었다."]], [["“Good night t’ee,”", "\"안녕하시오.\""], ["said the man with the basket.", "바구니를 든 남자가 말했다."]], [["“Good night, Sir John,”", "\"안녕하시오, 존 경.\""], ["said the parson.", "목사가 말했다."]], [["The pedestrian, after another pace or two, halted, and turned round.", "걸어가던 남자는 한두 걸음 더 가다가 멈춰 서서 돌아섰다."]], [["“Now, sir, begging your pardon; we met last market-day on this road about this time, and I said ‘Good night,’ and you made reply ‘_Good night, Sir John_,’ as now.”", "\"이보시오, 실례지만 말이오. 지난 장날에도 이 길에서 이맘때 만났을 때, 내가 '안녕하시오' 했더니, 당신이 지금처럼 '안녕하시오, 존 경' 하고 답했소.\""]], [["“I did,” said the parson.", "\"그랬지요.\" 목사가 말했다."]], [["“And once before that—near a month ago.”", "\"그 전에도 한 번, 한 달쯤 전에 그랬고.\""]], [["“I may have.”", "\"그랬을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“Then what might your meaning be in calling me ‘Sir John’ these different times, when I be plain Jack Durbeyfield, the haggler?”", "\"그렇다면 여러 번이나 나를 '존 경'이라 부르는 게 무슨 뜻이오? 나는 그저 평범한 잭 더비필드, 행상꾼일 뿐인데?\""]], [["The parson rode a step or two nearer.", "목사가 한두 걸음 더 가까이 다가왔다."]], [["“It was only my whim,”", "\"그저 내 변덕이었소.\""], ["he said; and, after a moment’s hesitation: “It was on account of a discovery I made some little time ago, whilst I was hunting up pedigrees for the new county history.", "그가 말하고는, 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말을 이었다. \"얼마 전에 새 주(州) 역사책에 쓸 가계도를 뒤지다가 알아낸 사실 때문이었소."], ["I am Parson Tringham, the antiquary, of Stagfoot Lane.", "나는 스태그풋 레인의 골동품 연구가, 트링엄 목사라오."], ["Don’t you really know, Durbeyfield, that you are the lineal representative of the ancient and knightly family of the d’Urbervilles, who derive their descent from Sir Pagan d’Urberville, that renowned knight who came from Normandy with William the Conqueror, as appears by Battle Abbey Roll?”", "더비필드, 당신이 정말 모른단 말이오? 당신이 더버빌가의 오래되고 기사다운 가문의 직계 후손이라는 것을. 그 가문은 정복왕 윌리엄과 함께 노르망디에서 건너온 그 이름난 기사, 페이건 더버빌 경에게서 그 혈통을 잇고 있으니, 배틀 수도원 명부에도 나와 있소.\""]], [["“Never heard it before, sir!”", "\"그런 얘긴 처음 듣소!\""]], [["“Well it’s true.", "\"음, 사실이오."], ["Throw up your chin a moment, so that I may catch the profile of your face better.", "잠깐 턱을 들어 보시오, 당신 옆얼굴 윤곽을 좀 더 잘 보게."], ["Yes, that’s the d’Urberville nose and chin—a little debased.", "그렇지, 그게 바로 더버빌가의 코와 턱이오."], ["Your ancestor was one of the twelve knights who assisted the Lord of Estremavilla in Normandy in his conquest of Glamorganshire.", "조금 격이 떨어지긴 했지만. 당신 선조는 노르망디의 에스트르마빌라 영주가 글래모건셔를 정복하는 것을 도운 열두 기사 중 하나였소."], ["Branches of your family held manors over all this part of England; their names appear in the Pipe Rolls in the time of King Stephen.", "당신 가문의 분가들은 잉글랜드의 이 지역 전역에 걸쳐 영지를 가지고 있었고, 그들의 이름은 스티븐 왕 시대의 회계 장부에도 등장하오."], ["In the reign of King John one of them was rich enough to give a manor to the Knights Hospitallers; and in Edward the Second’s time your forefather Brian was summoned to Westminster to attend the great Council there.", "존 왕의 치세에는 그중 하나가 성요한기사단에 영지를 내줄 만큼 부유했고, 에드워드 이세 시대에는 당신의 선조 브라이언이 웨스트민스터로 소환되어 대평의회에 참석했소."], ["You declined a little in Oliver Cromwell’s time, but to no serious extent, and in Charles the Second’s reign you were made Knights of the Royal Oak for your loyalty.", "올리버 크롬웰 시대에는 조금 기울었으나 심각한 정도는 아니었고, 찰스 이세의 치세에는 충성심으로 왕립 떡갈나무 기사 작위를 받았소."], ["Aye, there have been generations of Sir Johns among you, and if knighthood were hereditary, like a baronetcy, as it practically was in old times, when men were knighted from father to son, you would be Sir John now.”", "그래, 당신 가문에는 대대로 존 경이 이어져 왔으니, 옛 시절처럼 기사 작위가 준남작 작위처럼 세습된다면, 즉 아버지에게서 아들로 기사 작위가 내려진다면, 당신은 지금 존 경이 되었을 거요.\""]], [["“Ye don’t say so!”", "\"설마 그럴 리가!\""]], [["“In short,”", "\"한마디로,\""], ["concluded the parson, decisively smacking his leg with his switch, “there’s hardly such another family in England.”", "목사가 회초리로 자기 다리를 단호하게 탁 치며 결론지었다. \"잉글랜드에 이만한 가문은 또 없을 거요.\""]], [["“Daze my eyes, and isn’t there?”", "\"세상에, 그렇단 말이오?\""], ["said Durbeyfield.", "더비필드가 말했다."], ["“And here have I been knocking about, year after year, from pillar to post, as if I was no more than the commonest feller in the parish....", "\"그런데 이렇게 나는 해마다 이리저리 떠밀려 다니며, 마치 이 교구에서 제일 흔해 빠진 놈이라도 되는 양 굴러먹었구려……"], ["And how long hev this news about me been knowed, Pa’son Tringham?”", "그런데 나에 관한 이 소식이 알려진 지는 얼마나 됐소, 트링엄 목사?\""]], [["The clergyman explained that, as far as he was aware, it had quite died out of knowledge, and could hardly be said to be known at all.", "목사는, 자기가 아는 한, 그것은 세상의 기억에서 완전히 사라졌으며 알려져 있다고 하기도 어렵다고 설명했다."], ["His own investigations had begun on a day in the preceding spring when, having been engaged in tracing the vicissitudes of the d’Urberville family, he had observed Durbeyfield’s name on his waggon, and had thereupon been led to make inquiries about his father and grandfather till he had no doubt on the subject.", "그 자신의 조사는 지난봄 어느 날 시작되었는데, 더버빌가의 흥망성쇠를 추적하던 중 더비필드의 짐마차에 적힌 이름을 보고는, 거기서부터 그의 아버지와 할아버지에 관해 알아보게 되었고, 마침내 그 문제에 의심의 여지가 없게 되었다는 것이다."]], [["“At first I resolved not to disturb you with such a useless piece of information,”", "\"처음에는 이런 쓸데없는 이야기로 당신을 성가시게 하지 않으리라 마음먹었소.\""], ["said he. “However, our impulses are too strong for our judgement sometimes.", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 우리의 충동은 때로 판단보다 너무 강한 법이지요."], ["I thought you might perhaps know something of it all the while.”", "그래도 당신이 어쩌면 이미 그 일을 조금쯤 알고 있을지도 모른다고 생각했소.\""]], [["“Well, I have heard once or twice, ’tis true, that my family had seen better days afore they came to Blackmoor.", "\"음, 우리 집안이 블랙무어로 오기 전에 더 나은 시절을 보냈다는 얘긴 한두 번 들은 적이 있긴 하오."], ["But I took no notice o’t, thinking it to mean that we had once kept two horses where we now keep only one.", "하지만 나는 그게 우리가 지금은 말 한 마리만 두지만 한때는 두 마리를 두었다는 뜻쯤으로 여기고 신경 쓰지 않았소."], ["I’ve got a wold silver spoon, and a wold graven seal at home, too; but, Lord, what’s a spoon and seal?...", "집에 오래된 은수저 하나랑, 오래된 문장이 새겨진 인장도 하나 있긴 하오. 하지만 원, 수저랑 인장이 다 뭐란 말이오?……"], ["And to think that I and these noble d’Urbervilles were one flesh all the time.", "그런데 나하고 그 고귀한 더버빌가가 줄곧 한 핏줄이었다니."], ["’Twas said that my gr’t-granfer had secrets, and didn’t care to talk of where he came from....", "우리 증조부가 비밀을 가지고 있었고, 어디서 왔는지 말하기 싫어했다고 하더이다……"], ["And where do we raise our smoke, now, parson, if I may make so bold; I mean, where do we d’Urbervilles live?”", "그런데 목사 양반, 외람되지만 우리가 지금 어디서 연기를 피우고 사오? 그러니까, 우리 더버빌가가 어디서 사오?\""]], [["“You don’t live anywhere.", "\"당신들은 아무 데서도 살지 않소."], ["You are extinct—as a county family.”", "당신들은—주(州)의 명문 가문으로서는—멸절되었소.\""]], [["“That’s bad.”", "\"그거 안됐구려.\""]], [["“Yes—what the mendacious family chronicles call extinct in the male line—that is, gone down—gone under.”", "\"그렇소. 그 허울 좋은 가문 연대기에서 말하는 대로, 남계가 끊겼소. 그러니까 가라앉았고, 몰락한 것이오.\""]], [["“Then where do we lie?”", "\"그럼 우리는 어디 누워 있소?\""]], [["“At Kingsbere-sub-Greenhill: rows and rows of you in your vaults, with your effigies under Purbeck-marble canopies.”", "\"킹스비어서브그린힐에, 줄줄이 늘어선 당신네 납골당에, 퍼벡 대리석 닫집 아래 조각상들과 함께.\""]], [["“And where be our family mansions and estates?”", "\"그럼 우리 가문의 저택과 영지는 어디 있소?\""]], [["“You haven’t any.”", "\"하나도 없소.\""]], [["“Oh?", "\"오?"], ["No lands neither?”", "땅도 없단 말이오?\""]], [["“None; though you once had ’em in abundance, as I said, for your family consisted of numerous branches.", "\"하나도. 비록 앞서 말했듯 한때는 차고 넘치게 가지고 있었지만 말이오, 당신네 가문은 수많은 분가로 이루어져 있었으니까."], ["In this county there was a seat of yours at Kingsbere, and another at Sherton, and another in Millpond, and another at Lullstead, and another at Wellbridge.”", "이 주(州)에도 킹스비어에 당신네 영지가 하나 있었고, 셔턴에 또 하나, 밀폰드에 또 하나, 럴스테드에 또 하나, 웰브리지에 또 하나가 있었소.\""]], [["“And shall we ever come into our own again?”", "\"그럼 우리가 다시 우리 것을 되찾게 될까요?\""]], [["“Ah—that I can’t tell!”", "\"아, 그건 나도 모르오!\""]], [["“And what had I better do about it, sir?” asked Durbeyfield, after a pause.", "\"그럼 그 일에 대해 내가 뭘 하는 게 좋겠소, 목사 양반?\" 잠시 뒤에 더비필드가 물었다."]], [["“Oh—nothing, nothing; except chasten yourself with the thought of ‘how are the mighty fallen.’ It is a fact of some interest to the local historian and genealogist, nothing more.", "\"오, 아무것도, 아무것도 하지 마시오. '강하던 자들이 어찌하여 엎드러졌는고' 하는 생각으로 스스로를 다스리는 것 말고는."], ["There are several families among the cottagers of this county of almost equal lustre.", "그건 지방 사학자와 계보학자에게나 다소 흥미로운 사실일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니오. 이 주(州)의 오두막살이 농민들 중에도 거의 같은 광채를 지닌 집안이 여럿 있소."], ["Good night.”", "잘 가시오.\""]], [["“But you’ll turn back and have a quart of beer wi’ me on the strength o’t, Pa’son Tringham?", "\"하지만 그 기념으로 발길을 돌려 나와 함께 맥주나 한 쿼트 하지 않겠소, 트링엄 목사? '순수한 한 방울'"], ["There’s a very pretty brew in tap at The Pure Drop—though, to be sure, not so good as at Rolliver’s.”", "주막에 아주 맛 좋은 술이 한 통 들어와 있소. 물론, 정말이지 롤리버 주막만큼 좋진 않지만 말이오.\""]], [["“No, thank you—not this evening, Durbeyfield.", "\"아니, 고맙소. 오늘 저녁은 됐소, 더비필드."], ["You’ve had enough already.”", "당신은 이미 충분히 마셨소.\""], ["Concluding thus, the parson rode on his way, with doubts as to his discretion in retailing this curious bit of lore.", "그렇게 끝맺고는, 목사는 이 진기한 옛이야기 한 토막을 늘어놓은 것이 분별 있는 짓이었나 의심하며 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["When he was gone, Durbeyfield walked a few steps in a profound reverie, and then sat down upon the grassy bank by the roadside, depositing his basket before him.", "목사가 가버리자, 더비필드는 깊은 몽상에 잠겨 몇 걸음 걷다가, 길가 풀 둑에 앉아 바구니를 앞에 내려놓았다."], ["In a few minutes a youth appeared in the distance, walking in the same direction as that which had been pursued by Durbeyfield.", "몇 분 뒤 저 멀리서 한 젊은이가 더비필드가 걸어온 것과 같은 방향으로 걸어오는 것이 보였다."], ["The latter, on seeing him, held up his hand, and the lad quickened his pace and came near.", "더비필드는 그를 보고 손을 들어 올렸고, 소년은 걸음을 재촉하여 가까이 다가왔다."]], [["“Boy, take up that basket! I want ’ee to go on an errand for me.”", "\"얘야, 그 바구니 좀 들어라! 너한테 심부름을 시켜야겠다.\""]], [["The lath-like stripling frowned.", "가느다란 막대기 같은 풋내기는 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["“Who be you, then, John Durbeyfield, to order me about and call me ‘boy’?", "\"당신이 누군데, 존 더비필드, 나한테 이래라저래라 하고 '얘야' 따위로 부르는 거요?"], ["You know my name as well as I know yours!”", "내가 당신 이름 아는 만큼 당신도 내 이름 잘 알면서!\""]], [["“Do you, do you? That’s the secret—that’s the secret!", "\"안다고, 안다 이거지? 그게 바로 비밀이야, 그게 비밀이라고!"], ["Now obey my orders, and take the message I’m going to charge ’ee wi’...", "자, 내 명령대로 하고, 내가 시킬 전갈을 받아라……"], ["Well, Fred, I don’t mind telling you that the secret is that I’m one of a noble race—it has been just found out by me this present afternoon, p.m.”", "그래, 프레드, 너한테 말해 줘도 상관없겠지, 그 비밀이란 내가 고귀한 가문의 후손이라는 거다. 바로 오늘 오후에, 정오 지나서 내가 막 알아낸 일이지.\""], ["And as he made the announcement, Durbeyfield, declining from his sitting position, luxuriously stretched himself out upon the bank among the daisies.", "그렇게 선언하면서 더비필드는 앉은 자세에서 몸을 눕혀, 데이지꽃 사이 둑 위에 호사스럽게 길게 뻗었다."]], [["The lad stood before Durbeyfield, and contemplated his length from crown to toe.", "소년은 더비필드 앞에 서서, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 길쭉한 몸을 찬찬히 바라보았다."]], [["“Sir John d’Urberville—that’s who I am,”", "\"더버빌가의 존 경, 그게 바로 나다.\""], ["continued the prostrate man.", "드러누운 남자가 말을 이었다."], ["“That is if knights were baronets—which they be.", "\"기사가 곧 준남작이라면 말이지. 그리고 실제로 그렇거든."], ["’Tis recorded in history all about me.", "나에 관한 게 죄다 역사책에 적혀 있어."], ["Dost know of such a place, lad, as Kingsbere-sub-Greenhill?”", "얘야, 킹스비어서브그린힐이라는 데를 아느냐?\""]], [["“Ees.", "\"네."], ["I’ve been there to Greenhill Fair.”", "그린힐 장(場)에 가본 적 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, under the church of that city there lie—”", "\"그래, 그 도시의 교회 아래에 누워 있는 게……\""]], [["“’Tisn’t a city, the place I mean; leastwise ’twaddn’ when I was there—’twas a little one-eyed, blinking sort o’ place.”", "\"제가 말하는 데는 도시가 아니에요. 적어도 제가 갔을 땐 아니었어요. 외눈박이처럼 깜박거리는 자그마한 동네였는걸요.\""]], [["“Never you mind the place, boy, that’s not the question before us.", "\"그 동네야 어떻든 상관없다, 얘야, 그게 지금 우리가 따질 문제가 아니야."], ["Under the church of that there parish lie my ancestors—hundreds of ’em—in coats of mail and jewels, in gr’t lead coffins weighing tons and tons.", "바로 그 교구의 교회 아래에 내 선조들이 누워 있어, 수백 명이나, 갑옷을 입고 보석을 두른 채, 수 톤씩이나 나가는 커다란 납관 속에 말이야."], ["There’s not a man in the county o’ South-Wessex that’s got grander and nobler skillentons in his family than I.”", "사우스웨식스 주(州) 어디에도 나만큼 웅장하고 고귀한 해골을 집안에 둔 사람은 없어.\""]], [["“Oh?”", "\"오?\""]], [["“Now take up that basket, and goo on to Marlott, and when you’ve come to The Pure Drop Inn, tell ’em to send a horse and carriage to me immed’ately, to carry me hwome.", "\"자, 이제 그 바구니를 들고 말럿으로 가서, '순수한 한 방울' 주막에 도착하거든, 즉시 말과 마차를 보내 나를 집으로 데려가라고 해라."], ["And in the bottom o’ the carriage they be to put a noggin o’ rum in a small bottle, and chalk it up to my account.", "그리고 마차 바닥에는 작은 병에 럼주 한 모금 담아 넣고, 내 외상으로 달아 두라고 해."], ["And when you’ve done that goo on to my house with the basket, and tell my wife to put away that washing, because she needn’t finish it, and wait till I come hwome, as I’ve news to tell her.”", "그걸 마치거든 바구니를 들고 우리 집으로 가서, 내 아내한테 그 빨래는 치워 두라고 해라, 끝낼 필요 없다고, 내가 집에 올 때까지 기다리라고 해, 들려줄 소식이 있으니까.\""]], [["As the lad stood in a dubious attitude, Durbeyfield put his hand in his pocket, and produced a shilling, one of the chronically few that he possessed.", "소년이 망설이는 태도로 서 있자, 더비필드는 주머니에 손을 넣어 만성적으로 몇 개 안 되는 그의 동전 중 하나인 일 실링을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Here’s for your labour, lad.”", "\"수고비다, 얘야.\""]], [["This made a difference in the young man’s estimate of the position.", "이것이 그 상황에 대한 젊은이의 평가를 바꿔 놓았다."]], [["“Yes, Sir John.", "\"네, 존 경."], ["Thank ’ee.", "감사합니다."], ["Anything else I can do for ’ee, Sir John?”", "또 해드릴 일이 있나요, 존 경?\""]], [["“Tell ’em at hwome that I should like for supper,—well, lamb’s fry if they can get it; and if they can’t, black-pot; and if they can’t get that, well chitterlings will do.”", "\"집에 가서 내가 저녁으로—그래, 구할 수 있으면 양 내장 튀김이 좋겠다고 전해라. 그게 안 되면 선지순대, 그것도 안 되면 뭐, 곱창이라도 괜찮다고.\""]], [["“Yes, Sir John.”", "\"네, 존 경.\""]], [["The boy took up the basket, and as he set out the notes of a brass band were heard from the direction of the village.", "소년이 바구니를 들었고, 그가 출발할 때 마을 쪽에서 금관악대의 가락이 들려왔다."]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"저게 뭐냐?\""], ["said Durbeyfield.", "더비필드가 말했다."], ["“Not on account o’ I?”", "\"설마 나 때문은 아니겠지?\""]], [["“’Tis the women’s club-walking, Sir John.", "\"여자들 모임 행진이에요, 존 경."], ["Why, your da’ter is one o’ the members.”", "아니, 당신 따님도 회원 중 하나잖아요.\""]], [["“To be sure—I’d quite forgot it in my thoughts of greater things!", "\"아 그래, 더 큰 일들을 생각하느라 까맣게 잊고 있었구나!"], ["Well, vamp on to Marlott, will ye, and order that carriage, and maybe I’ll drive round and inspect the club.”", "그럼 어서 말럿으로 가서 그 마차를 시켜 두어라, 그러면 나도 마차를 몰고 가서 그 모임을 한번 둘러볼지도 모르겠다.\""]], [["The lad departed, and Durbeyfield lay waiting on the grass and daisies in the evening sun.", "소년은 떠났고, 더비필드는 저녁 햇살 속 풀과 데이지꽃 위에 누워 기다렸다."], ["Not a soul passed that way for a long while, and the faint notes of the band were the only human sounds audible within the rim of blue hills.", "한참 동안 그 길로는 사람 하나 지나가지 않았고, 푸른 언덕들이 두른 가장자리 안에서 들리는 사람 소리라곤 희미한 악대 가락뿐이었다. II"]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["The village of Marlott lay amid the north-eastern undulations of the beautiful Vale of Blakemore, or Blackmoor, aforesaid, an engirdled and secluded region, for the most part untrodden as yet by tourist or landscape-painter, though within a four hours’ journey from London.", "말럿 마을은, 앞서 말한 아름다운 블레이크모어, 또는 블랙무어 골짜기의 북동쪽 굽이굽이 사이에 자리 잡고 있었으니, 빙 둘러싸여 외진 고장으로, 런던에서 네 시간이면 닿는 거리인데도 대부분 아직 관광객이나 풍경화가의 발길이 닿지 않은 곳이었다."]], [["It is a vale whose acquaintance is best made by viewing it from the summits of the hills that surround it—except perhaps during the droughts of summer.", "이 골짜기는, 어쩌면 한여름 가뭄철을 빼고는, 그것을 둘러싼 언덕 꼭대기에서 내려다볼 때 가장 잘 알게 되는 골짜기다."], ["An unguided ramble into its recesses in bad weather is apt to engender dissatisfaction with its narrow, tortuous, and miry ways.", "궂은 날씨에 안내자도 없이 그 깊은 곳으로 무작정 거니노라면, 좁고 구불구불하고 진창인 길에 불만을 품기 십상이다."]], [["This fertile and sheltered tract of country, in which the fields are never brown and the springs never dry, is bounded on the south by the bold chalk ridge that embraces the prominences of Hambledon Hill, Bulbarrow, Nettlecombe-Tout, Dogbury, High Stoy, and Bubb Down.", "들판이 결코 시들지 않고 샘이 결코 마르지 않는 이 비옥하고 아늑한 고장은, 남쪽으로 햄블던 힐, 불배로, 네틀콤투트, 도그버리, 하이 스토이, 버브 다운의 봉우리들을 품은 험준한 백악질 산등성이로 경계 지어진다."], ["The traveller from the coast, who, after plodding northward for a score of miles over calcareous downs and corn-lands, suddenly reaches the verge of one of these escarpments, is surprised and delighted to behold, extended like a map beneath him, a country differing absolutely from that which he has passed through.", "해안에서부터 석회질 구릉지와 곡창지대를 따라 북쪽으로 스무 마일을 터벅터벅 걸어온 나그네가, 문득 이 가파른 절벽 가장자리 하나에 다다르면, 발아래 지도처럼 펼쳐진, 자기가 지나온 곳과는 완전히 다른 고장을 보고 놀라며 기뻐한다."], ["Behind him the hills are open, the sun blazes down upon fields so large as to give an unenclosed character to the landscape, the lanes are white, the hedges low and plashed, the atmosphere colourless.", "그의 등 뒤로는 언덕들이 탁 트여 있고, 햇볕은 들판이 어찌나 넓은지 울타리 없는 풍경처럼 보이는 그 위로 이글거리며 내리쬐며, 오솔길들은 희고, 산울타리는 낮고 가지가 엮여 있으며, 대기는 빛깔이 없다."], ["Here, in the valley, the world seems to be constructed upon a smaller and more delicate scale; the fields are mere paddocks, so reduced that from this height their hedgerows appear a network of dark green threads overspreading the paler green of the grass.", "그런데 여기 이 골짜기에서는, 세상이 더 작고 더 섬세한 규모로 지어진 듯하다. 들판은 자그마한 목초지에 지나지 않아, 어찌나 작은지 이 높이에서 보면 그 산울타리들이 더 옅은 초록빛 풀밭을 뒤덮은 짙은 초록 실그물처럼 보인다."], ["The atmosphere beneath is languorous, and is so tinged with azure that what artists call the middle distance partakes also of that hue, while the horizon beyond is of the deepest ultramarine.", "아래쪽 대기는 나른하고, 어찌나 쪽빛으로 물들었는지 화가들이 중경(中景)이라 부르는 부분조차 그 빛깔을 띠며, 그 너머 지평선은 가장 짙은 군청색이다."], ["Arable lands are few and limited; with but slight exceptions the prospect is a broad rich mass of grass and trees, mantling minor hills and dales within the major.", "경작지는 적고 좁으니, 약간의 예외를 빼면 눈앞에 펼쳐지는 것은 큰 언덕과 골짜기 안의 작은 언덕과 골짜기를 외투처럼 덮은, 풀과 나무의 넓고 풍요로운 덩어리다."], ["Such is the Vale of Blackmoor.", "이러한 곳이 바로 블랙무어 골짜기다."]], [["The district is of historic, no less than of topographical interest.", "이 고장은 지형적으로 못지않게 역사적으로도 흥미롭다."], ["The Vale was known in former times as the Forest of White Hart, from a curious legend of King Henry III’s reign, in which the killing by a certain Thomas de la Lynd of a beautiful white hart which the king had run down and spared, was made the occasion of a heavy fine.", "이 골짜기는 옛날에 백사슴 숲이라 불렸는데, 헨리 삼세 치세의 진기한 전설에서 비롯된 이름이다. 그 전설에서 토머스 드 라 린드라는 자가, 왕이 몰다가 살려 준 아름다운 백사슴을 죽인 것이 무거운 벌금의 빌미가 되었다."], ["In those days, and till comparatively recent times, the country was densely wooded.", "그 시절에, 그리고 비교적 최근까지도, 이 고장은 빽빽이 숲으로 덮여 있었다."], ["Even now, traces of its earlier condition are to be found in the old oak copses and irregular belts of timber that yet survive upon its slopes, and the hollow-trunked trees that shade so many of its pastures.", "지금도 그 비탈에 아직 살아남은 오래된 떡갈나무 숲과 불규칙한 수목 띠에서, 그리고 그 많은 목초지에 그늘을 드리우는 속 빈 나무들에서, 옛 모습의 자취를 찾아볼 수 있다."]], [["The forests have departed, but some old customs of their shades remain.", "숲은 사라졌으나, 그 그늘에서 비롯된 오랜 풍습은 더러 남아 있다."], ["Many, however, linger only in a metamorphosed or disguised form.", "하지만 많은 것이 모습을 바꾸거나 위장한 형태로만 남아 있을 뿐이다."], ["The May-Day dance, for instance, was to be discerned on the afternoon under notice, in the guise of the club revel, or “club-walking,” as it was there called.", "가령 오월제 춤은, 이날 오후에, 그곳에서 모임 잔치, 또는 '모임 행진'이라 불리는 모습으로 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["It was an interesting event to the younger inhabitants of Marlott, though its real interest was not observed by the participators in the ceremony.", "이는 말럿의 젊은 주민들에게는 흥미로운 행사였으나, 정작 의식에 참여하는 이들은 그 참된 의미를 알아채지 못했다."], ["Its singularity lay less in the retention of a custom of walking in procession and dancing on each anniversary than in the members being solely women.", "그 특이함은 해마다 줄지어 걷고 춤추는 풍습을 이어 온 데 있다기보다, 회원이 오로지 여자들뿐이라는 데 있었다."], ["In men’s clubs such celebrations were, though expiring, less uncommon; but either the natural shyness of the softer sex, or a sarcastic attitude on the part of male relatives, had denuded such women’s clubs as remained (if any other did) of this their glory and consummation.", "남자들의 모임에서는 그런 행사가, 비록 사그라드는 중이긴 해도, 그리 드물지 않았다. 그러나 연약한 성(性)의 타고난 수줍음 때문인지, 아니면 남자 친척들의 빈정대는 태도 때문인지, 아직 남아 있는 여자들의 모임은 (만일 다른 모임이 있었다 한들) 이 영광이자 절정을 다 빼앗기고 말았다."], ["The club of Marlott alone lived to uphold the local Cerealia.", "오로지 말럿의 모임만이 살아남아 그 고장의 곡물 축제를 떠받쳤다."], ["It had walked for hundreds of years, if not as benefit-club, as votive sisterhood of some sort; and it walked still.", "그것은 공제조합으로서는 아니더라도 어떤 종류의 봉헌 자매회로서, 수백 년 동안 행진해 왔다. 그리고 지금도 행진하고 있었다."]], [["The banded ones were all dressed in white gowns—a gay survival from Old Style days, when cheerfulness and May-time were synonyms—days before the habit of taking long views had reduced emotions to a monotonous average.", "이 무리는 모두 흰 가운을 입고 있었으니, 명랑함과 오월이 한뜻이던 옛 시절, 멀리 내다보는 습관이 감정을 단조로운 평균으로 깎아내리기 전의 시절에서 살아남은 흥겨운 유물이었다."], ["Their first exhibition of themselves was in a processional march of two and two round the parish.", "그들이 처음으로 제 모습을 드러낸 것은 둘씩 짝지어 교구를 도는 행렬 행진에서였다."], ["Ideal and real clashed slightly as the sun lit up their figures against the green hedges and creeper-laced house-fronts; for, though the whole troop wore white garments, no two whites were alike among them.", "햇빛이 초록빛 산울타리와 덩굴이 얽힌 집들의 정면을 배경으로 그들의 자태를 비추자, 이상과 현실이 살짝 부딪쳤다. 무리 전체가 흰옷을 입었다지만, 그들 가운데 똑같은 흰빛은 둘도 없었으니까."], ["Some approached pure blanching; some had a bluish pallor; some worn by the older characters (which had possibly lain by folded for many a year) inclined to a cadaverous tint, and to a Georgian style.", "어떤 것은 순백에 가까웠고, 어떤 것은 푸르스름하게 창백했으며, 나이 든 이들이 입은 어떤 것은 (아마도 여러 해 접힌 채 묵혀 두었던 것이리라) 송장 같은 빛깔에, 조지 왕조풍으로 기울어 있었다."]], [["In addition to the distinction of a white frock, every woman and girl carried in her right hand a peeled willow wand, and in her left a bunch of white flowers.", "흰 드레스라는 특징 말고도, 여자와 처녀 들은 저마다 오른손에는 껍질 벗긴 버드나무 가지를, 왼손에는 흰 꽃 한 다발을 들고 있었다."], ["The peeling of the former, and the selection of the latter, had been an operation of personal care.", "앞엣것의 껍질을 벗기는 일과 뒤엣것을 고르는 일에는 저마다의 정성이 들어 있었다."]], [["There were a few middle-aged and even elderly women in the train, their silver-wiry hair and wrinkled faces, scourged by time and trouble, having almost a grotesque, certainly a pathetic, appearance in such a jaunty situation.", "행렬 속에는 중년의, 심지어 노년의 여자도 몇 있었으니, 세월과 고생에 시달려 은백색으로 곱슬거리는 머리칼과 주름진 얼굴은, 이런 발랄한 자리에서 거의 기괴한, 분명 애처로운 모습이었다."], ["In a true view, perhaps, there was more to be gathered and told of each anxious and experienced one, to whom the years were drawing nigh when she should say, “I have no pleasure in them,” than of her juvenile comrades.", "어쩌면 참되게 보자면, \"내게는 그것들에서 아무런 즐거움이 없노라\" 하고 말해야 할 세월이 다가오는 이 근심 많고 산전수전 겪은 이들 하나하나에 대해, 그 젊은 동무들에 대해서보다 더 많은 것을 헤아리고 들려줄 수 있었으리라."], ["But let the elder be passed over here for those under whose bodices the life throbbed quick and warm.", "하지만 여기서는 나이 든 이들은 지나치고, 그 보디스 아래 생명이 빠르고 따뜻하게 고동치는 이들에게로 가자."]], [["The young girls formed, indeed, the majority of the band, and their heads of luxuriant hair reflected in the sunshine every tone of gold, and black, and brown. Some had beautiful eyes, others a beautiful nose, others a beautiful mouth and figure: few, if any, had all.", "과연 젊은 처녀들이 이 무리의 다수를 이루었으니, 무성한 머리칼은 햇빛 속에서 금빛과 검은빛과 갈색의 온갖 색조를 비추었다. 어떤 이는 아름다운 눈을, 어떤 이는 아름다운 코를, 어떤 이는 아름다운 입과 자태를 지녔으되, 그 모두를 지닌 이는 거의, 아니 아예 없었다."], ["A difficulty of arranging their lips in this crude exposure to public scrutiny, an inability to balance their heads, and to dissociate self-consciousness from their features, was apparent in them, and showed that they were genuine country girls, unaccustomed to many eyes.", "이렇게 거칠게 대중의 눈길에 드러난 자리에서 입술을 어찌 두어야 할지 난감해하는 모습, 고개를 가누지 못하고 얼굴에서 자의식을 떼어 내지 못하는 모습이 그들에게 뚜렷이 보였으니, 그들이 여러 눈길에 익숙지 않은 진짜 시골 처녀들임을 드러냈다."]], [["And as each and all of them were warmed without by the sun, so each had a private little sun for her soul to bask in; some dream, some affection, some hobby, at least some remote and distant hope which, though perhaps starving to nothing, still lived on, as hopes will.", "그리고 그들 하나하나가 바깥으로 햇볕에 데워졌듯, 저마다 제 영혼이 쪼일 작은 사사로운 해 하나씩을 품고 있었다. 어떤 꿈, 어떤 애정, 어떤 취미, 적어도 어떤 아득하고 먼 희망 하나를. 그 희망은 비록 굶주려 사그라들지언정, 희망이란 으레 그렇듯, 여전히 살아남아 있었다."], ["They were all cheerful, and many of them merry.", "그들은 모두 명랑했고, 그중 많은 이가 들떠 있었다."]], [["They came round by The Pure Drop Inn, and were turning out of the high road to pass through a wicket-gate into the meadows, when one of the women said—", "그들이 '순수한 한 방울' 주막을 돌아, 큰길에서 벗어나 작은 쪽문을 지나 목초지로 들어서려 할 때, 한 여자가 말했다."]], [["“The Load-a-Lord!", "\"하느님 맙소사!"], ["Why, Tess Durbeyfield, if there isn’t thy father riding hwome in a carriage!”", "아니, 테스 더비필드, 저기 네 아버지가 마차를 타고 집에 가시는 거 아냐!\""]], [["A young member of the band turned her head at the exclamation.", "이 외침에 무리 중 한 젊은 회원이 고개를 돌렸다."], ["She was a fine and handsome girl—not handsomer than some others, possibly—but her mobile peony mouth and large innocent eyes added eloquence to colour and shape.", "그녀는 곱고 아름다운 처녀였으니—어쩌면 다른 몇몇보다 더 아름답진 않았을지 모르나—작약 같은 도톰한 입과 크고 순진한 눈이 빛깔과 모양에 웅변을 더해 주었다."], ["She wore a red ribbon in her hair, and was the only one of the white company who could boast of such a pronounced adornment.", "그녀는 머리에 빨간 리본을 매고 있었으니, 그 흰옷 무리 중 그토록 두드러진 장식을 뽐낼 수 있는 이는 그녀뿐이었다."], ["As she looked round Durbeyfield was seen moving along the road in a chaise belonging to The Pure Drop, driven by a frizzle-headed brawny damsel with her gown-sleeves rolled above her elbows.", "그녀가 둘러보니, 더비필드가 '순수한 한 방울'의 이륜마차를 타고 길을 따라 가는 것이 보였는데, 마차는 가운 소매를 팔꿈치 위로 걷어붙인, 머리가 곱슬곱슬한 다부진 처녀가 몰고 있었다."], ["This was the cheerful servant of that establishment, who, in her part of factotum, turned groom and ostler at times.", "그녀는 그 주막의 명랑한 하녀로, 잡일꾼 노릇을 하다가 때때로 마부와 마구간지기 일도 맡았다."], ["Durbeyfield, leaning back, and with his eyes closed luxuriously, was waving his hand above his head, and singing in a slow recitative—", "더비필드는 등을 기댄 채 호사스레 눈을 감고, 머리 위로 손을 흔들며, 느릿한 영창조로 노래하고 있었다."]], [["“I’ve-got-a-gr’t-family-vault-at-Kingsbere—and knighted-forefathers-in-lead-coffins-there!”", "\"나는—킹스비어에—커다란—가문—납골당이—있다네—그리고—거기—납관—속에—기사—작위—받은—선조들이—있다네!\""]], [["The clubbists tittered, except the girl called Tess—in whom a slow heat seemed to rise at the sense that her father was making himself foolish in their eyes.", "모임 사람들이 키득거렸으나, 테스라 불린 처녀만은 아니었다. 아버지가 사람들 눈에 바보짓을 하고 있다는 생각에 그녀 안에서 뜨거운 기운이 서서히 차오르는 듯했다."]], [["“He’s tired, that’s all,”", "\"피곤하셔서 그래.\""], ["she said hastily, “and he has got a lift home, because our own horse has to rest to-day.”", "그녀가 황급히 말했다. \"오늘은 우리 집 말이 쉬어야 해서, 그래서 마차를 얻어 타고 집에 가시는 거야.\""]], [["“Bless thy simplicity, Tess,”", "\"테스, 네 순진함에 복이나 받아라.\""], ["said her companions.", "동무들이 말했다."], ["“He’s got his market-nitch. Haw-haw!”", "\"장 보고 거나하게 한잔 걸치신 거지. 하하하!\""]], [["“Look here; I won’t walk another inch with you, if you say any jokes about him!”", "\"이봐, 그이에 대해 농담을 한마디라도 하면, 나 너희랑 한 발짝도 더 안 걸을 거야!\""], ["Tess cried, and the colour upon her cheeks spread over her face and neck.", "테스가 외쳤고, 양 볼에 오른 핏기가 얼굴과 목까지 번졌다."], ["In a moment her eyes grew moist, and her glance drooped to the ground.", "이내 그녀의 눈에 물기가 차오르고, 시선이 땅으로 떨어졌다."], ["Perceiving that they had really pained her they said no more, and order again prevailed.", "자기들이 정말로 그녀를 아프게 했음을 알아챈 그들은 더는 말하지 않았고, 다시 질서가 잡혔다."], ["Tess’s pride would not allow her to turn her head again, to learn what her father’s meaning was, if he had any; and thus she moved on with the whole body to the enclosure where there was to be dancing on the green.", "테스의 자존심은 다시 고개를 돌려, 아버지에게 무슨 뜻이 있었다면 그게 무엇인지 알아보는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 그렇게 그녀는 무리 전체와 함께 풀밭에서 춤을 추기로 되어 있는 마당으로 나아갔다."], ["By the time the spot was reached she had recovered her equanimity, and tapped her neighbour with her wand and talked as usual.", "그곳에 다다를 무렵 그녀는 평정을 되찾아, 가지로 옆 사람을 톡 치며 여느 때처럼 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["Tess Durbeyfield at this time of her life was a mere vessel of emotion untinctured by experience.", "테스 더비필드는 이 시기 그녀의 삶에서, 경험에 물들지 않은 한낱 감정의 그릇이었다."], ["The dialect was on her tongue to some extent, despite the village school: the characteristic intonation of that dialect for this district being the voicing approximately rendered by the syllable UR, probably as rich an utterance as any to be found in human speech.", "마을 학교를 다녔는데도 그녀의 혀에는 어느 정도 사투리가 남아 있었으니, 이 지역 사투리의 특징적인 억양은 대략 UR이라는 음절로 옮길 수 있는 발성이었는데, 아마 인간의 언어에서 찾을 수 있는 그 어떤 발음 못지않게 풍부한 소리였다."], ["The pouted-up deep red mouth to which this syllable was native had hardly as yet settled into its definite shape, and her lower lip had a way of thrusting the middle of her top one upward, when they closed together after a word.", "이 음절을 타고난, 도톰하게 오므린 짙은 붉은 입은 아직 그 분명한 모양으로 자리 잡지 않았고, 그녀의 아랫입술은 한마디 한 뒤 두 입술이 맞닿을 때면 윗입술 가운데를 위로 밀어 올리는 버릇이 있었다."]], [["Phases of her childhood lurked in her aspect still.", "그녀의 모습에는 아직 어린 시절의 단계들이 어른거렸다."], ["As she walked along to-day, for all her bouncing handsome womanliness, you could sometimes see her twelfth year in her cheeks, or her ninth sparkling from her eyes; and even her fifth would flit over the curves of her mouth now and then.", "오늘 이렇게 걸어가는 동안에도, 그 발랄하고 아름다운 여인다움에도 불구하고, 때때로 그녀의 볼에서 열두 살의 그녀를, 눈에서 반짝이는 아홉 살의 그녀를 볼 수 있었고, 심지어 다섯 살의 그녀가 이따금 입가의 곡선 위로 스쳐 지나가기도 했다."]], [["Yet few knew, and still fewer considered this.", "그러나 이것을 아는 이는 적었고, 헤아리는 이는 더더욱 적었다."], ["A small minority, mainly strangers, would look long at her in casually passing by, and grow momentarily fascinated by her freshness, and wonder if they would ever see her again: but to almost everybody she was a fine and picturesque country girl, and no more.", "소수의, 주로 낯선 이들이 무심코 지나치며 그녀를 한참 바라보다가, 그 싱그러움에 잠시 매혹되어, 과연 그녀를 다시 볼 수 있을까 궁금해하곤 했다. 하지만 거의 모든 이에게 그녀는 곱고 그림 같은 시골 처녀, 그 이상은 아니었다."]], [["Nothing was seen or heard further of Durbeyfield in his triumphal chariot under the conduct of the ostleress, and the club having entered the allotted space, dancing began.", "마구간지기 처녀가 모는 개선마차에 탄 더비필드는 더 보이지도 들리지도 않았고, 모임이 정해진 자리로 들어서자 춤이 시작되었다."], ["As there were no men in the company, the girls danced at first with each other, but when the hour for the close of labour drew on, the masculine inhabitants of the village, together with other idlers and pedestrians, gathered round the spot, and appeared inclined to negotiate for a partner.", "무리에 남자가 없었으므로 처녀들은 처음에는 서로 짝지어 춤을 추었으나, 일을 마칠 시각이 다가오자 마을의 남자 주민들이, 다른 한량들과 길손들과 더불어 그 자리로 모여들어, 짝을 흥정해 보려는 기색을 보였다."]], [["Among these on-lookers were three young men of a superior class, carrying small knapsacks strapped to their shoulders, and stout sticks in their hands.", "이 구경꾼들 가운데 신분이 좀 더 높은 세 젊은이가 있었으니, 어깨에 작은 배낭을 멘 끈으로 묶고 손에는 튼튼한 지팡이를 들고 있었다."], ["Their general likeness to each other, and their consecutive ages, would almost have suggested that they might be, what in fact they were, brothers.", "서로 전반적으로 닮은 점과 연이은 나이로 보아, 그들이—실제로 그러했듯이—형제라는 것을 거의 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["The eldest wore the white tie, high waistcoat, and thin-brimmed hat of the regulation curate; the second was the normal undergraduate; the appearance of the third and youngest would hardly have been sufficient to characterize him; there was an uncribbed, uncabined aspect in his eyes and attire, implying that he had hardly as yet found the entrance to his professional groove.", "맏이는 정식 부목사의 흰 넥타이와 높은 조끼, 챙이 얇은 모자 차림이었고, 둘째는 평범한 대학생 차림이었다. 셋째이자 막내의 행색은 그를 무어라 규정하기에 좀처럼 충분치 않았다. 그의 눈과 옷차림에는 어디에도 매이지 않고 갇히지 않은 데가 있어, 그가 아직 제 직업의 홈으로 들어가는 입구를 찾지 못했음을 내비쳤다."], ["That he was a desultory tentative student of something and everything might only have been predicted of him.", "그에 대해서는 그가 무언가를, 그리고 모든 것을 두서없이 시험 삼아 공부하는 학생이라는 것 정도밖에 점칠 수 없었으리라."]], [["These three brethren told casual acquaintance that they were spending their Whitsun holidays in a walking tour through the Vale of Blackmoor, their course being south-westerly from the town of Shaston on the north-east.", "이 세 형제는 우연히 마주친 이들에게, 자기들이 성령강림절 휴가를 블랙무어 골짜기를 가로지르는 도보 여행으로 보내는 중이며, 북동쪽의 섀스턴 마을에서 남서쪽으로 길을 잡았다고 말했다."]], [["They leant over the gate by the highway, and inquired as to the meaning of the dance and the white-frocked maids.", "그들은 큰길가 문에 기대어, 이 춤과 흰 드레스를 입은 처녀들이 무슨 뜻인지 물었다."], ["The two elder of the brothers were plainly not intending to linger more than a moment, but the spectacle of a bevy of girls dancing without male partners seemed to amuse the third, and make him in no hurry to move on.", "형 둘은 잠깐 이상은 머무를 생각이 없는 게 분명했으나, 남자 짝도 없이 춤추는 한 무리의 처녀들이라는 광경이 셋째에게는 흥미로워 보였는지, 좀처럼 발걸음을 옮기려 들지 않았다."], ["He unstrapped his knapsack, put it, with his stick, on the hedge-bank, and opened the gate.", "그는 배낭의 끈을 풀어 지팡이와 함께 산울타리 둑에 내려놓고 문을 열었다."]], [["“What are you going to do, Angel?”", "\"뭐 하려고 그래, 에인절?\""], ["asked the eldest.", "맏이가 물었다."]], [["“I am inclined to go and have a fling with them.", "\"가서 저들이랑 한바탕 춤추고 싶은데."], ["Why not all of us—just for a minute or two—it will not detain us long?”", "우리 다 같이 추면 어때—일이 분만—그리 오래 붙들리진 않을 거야.\""]], [["“No—no; nonsense!” said the first.", "\"안 돼, 안 돼, 말도 안 되는 소리!\""], ["“Dancing in public with a troop of country hoydens—suppose we should be seen!", "첫째가 말했다. \"한 무리 촌뜨기 계집애들과 대놓고 춤을 춘다니—누가 보기라도 하면 어쩌려고!"], ["Come along, or it will be dark before we get to Stourcastle, and there’s no place we can sleep at nearer than that; besides, we must get through another chapter of _A Counterblast to Agnosticism_ before we turn in, now I have taken the trouble to bring the book.”", "어서 가자, 안 그러면 스타우어캐슬에 닿기도 전에 날이 저물 거야, 거기보다 더 가까운 데는 묵을 데도 없어. 게다가 내가 일부러 책까지 챙겨 왔으니, 자기 전에 『불가지론에 대한 반박』을 한 장은 더 읽어야 한다고.\""]], [["“All right—I’ll overtake you and Cuthbert in five minutes; don’t stop; I give my word that I will, Felix.”", "\"알았어—오 분 안에 너랑 커스버트를 따라잡을게, 멈추지 마. 약속할게, 꼭 그럴게, 펠릭스.\""]], [["The two elder reluctantly left him and walked on, taking their brother’s knapsack to relieve him in following, and the youngest entered the field.", "형 둘은 마지못해 그를 두고 걸어갔으니, 동생을 뒤따라오기 쉽게 그의 배낭까지 들어 주었고, 막내는 들판으로 들어섰다."]], [["“This is a thousand pities,”", "\"이거 천 번 아까운 일인걸요.\""], ["he said gallantly, to two or three of the girls nearest him, as soon as there was a pause in the dance. “Where are your partners, my dears?”", "춤이 잠시 멎자마자 그가 가장 가까이 있는 두세 처녀에게 점잖게 말했다. \"그대들의 짝은 어디 있나요, 아가씨들?\""]], [["“They’ve not left off work yet,”", "\"아직 일을 안 마쳤어요.\""], ["answered one of the boldest.", "그중 가장 대담한 처녀가 답했다."], ["“They’ll be here by and by.", "\"곧 여기로 올 거예요."], ["Till then, will you be one, sir?”", "그때까지, 나리께서 짝이 되어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Certainly. But what’s one among so many!”", "\"그럼요. 하지만 이 많은 분들 중 나 하나가 무슨 소용이겠소!\""]], [["“Better than none.", "\"없는 것보다야 낫지요."], ["’Tis melancholy work facing and footing it to one of your own sort, and no clipsing and colling at all.", "자기랑 같은 부류끼리 마주 보고 발이나 맞추면서, 껴안고 어우러지는 것도 하나 없으면 참 서글픈 일인걸요."], ["Now, pick and choose.”", "자, 골라 보세요.\""]], [["“’Ssh—don’t be so for’ard!”", "\"쉿, 그렇게 나서지 좀 마!\""], ["said a shyer girl.", "더 수줍은 처녀가 말했다."]], [["The young man, thus invited, glanced them over, and attempted some discrimination; but, as the group were all so new to him, he could not very well exercise it.", "이렇게 청을 받은 젊은이는 그들을 훑어보며 좀 가려 보려 했으나, 무리가 죄다 처음 보는 이들이라 좀처럼 제대로 가려낼 수가 없었다."], ["He took almost the first that came to hand, which was not the speaker, as she had expected; nor did it happen to be Tess Durbeyfield.", "그는 거의 손에 닿는 대로 첫 번째 처녀를 골랐는데, 그것은 말을 건넨 처녀가 기대한 것과 달리 그녀가 아니었고, 또한 공교롭게도 테스 더비필드도 아니었다."], ["Pedigree, ancestral skeletons, monumental record, the d’Urberville lineaments, did not help Tess in her life’s battle as yet, even to the extent of attracting to her a dancing-partner over the heads of the commonest peasantry.", "가문이며, 조상의 해골이며, 기념비의 기록이며, 더버빌가의 이목구비도, 아직은 테스의 삶의 싸움에서, 흔해 빠진 시골 처녀들을 제치고 춤 상대 하나 끌어당겨 줄 만큼조차 그녀에게 도움이 되지 못했다."], ["So much for Norman blood unaided by Victorian lucre.", "빅토리아 시대의 돈이 받쳐 주지 않는 노르만 혈통이란 그 정도였다."]], [["The name of the eclipsing girl, whatever it was, has not been handed down; but she was envied by all as the first who enjoyed the luxury of a masculine partner that evening.", "다른 처녀들을 무색하게 만든 그 처녀의 이름은, 그게 무엇이었든, 전해 내려오지 않는다. 그러나 그녀는 그날 저녁 남자 짝이라는 호사를 처음 누린 이로서 모두의 부러움을 샀다."], ["Yet such was the force of example that the village young men, who had not hastened to enter the gate while no intruder was in the way, now dropped in quickly, and soon the couples became leavened with rustic youth to a marked extent, till at length the plainest woman in the club was no longer compelled to foot it on the masculine side of the figure.", "그러나 본보기의 힘이 어찌나 컸던지, 훼방꾼이 끼어들지 않았을 때는 문 안으로 들어서기를 서두르지 않던 마을 젊은이들이, 이제는 재빨리 끼어들어, 이내 짝들 사이에 시골 청년들이 눈에 띄게 섞여 들었으니, 마침내 모임에서 가장 못생긴 여자조차 더는 짝춤에서 남자 쪽 자리에 서서 발을 맞출 필요가 없게 되었다."]], [["The church clock struck, when suddenly the student said that he must leave—he had been forgetting himself—he had to join his companions.", "교회 시계가 종을 쳤다. 그 순간 학생은 가봐야겠다고 말했다. 그동안 자기를 잊고 있었던 것이다."], ["As he fell out of the dance his eyes lighted on Tess Durbeyfield, whose own large orbs wore, to tell the truth, the faintest aspect of reproach that he had not chosen her.", "일행과 합류해야 했다. 그가 춤에서 빠져나올 때 그의 눈길이 테스 더비필드에게 닿았는데, 사실대로 말하자면 그녀의 그 큰 눈동자에는 그가 자기를 고르지 않은 데 대한 희미한 원망의 기색이 어려 있었다."], ["He, too, was sorry then that, owing to her backwardness, he had not observed her; and with that in his mind he left the pasture.", "그 역시 그제야, 그녀가 뒤로 물러서 있던 탓에 그녀를 알아보지 못한 것이 안타까웠다. 그런 생각을 품은 채 그는 목초지를 떠났다."]], [["On account of his long delay he started in a flying-run down the lane westward, and had soon passed the hollow and mounted the next rise.", "너무 오래 지체한 탓에 그는 서쪽으로 난 오솔길을 쏜살같이 달려 내려가, 이내 움푹한 곳을 지나 다음 언덕을 올랐다."], ["He had not yet overtaken his brothers, but he paused to get breath, and looked back.", "아직 형들을 따라잡지는 못했으나, 숨을 고르려고 잠시 멈춰 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["He could see the white figures of the girls in the green enclosure whirling about as they had whirled when he was among them.", "초록 마당에서 처녀들의 흰 자태가, 자기가 그들 틈에 있을 때 빙빙 돌던 그대로 빙빙 도는 것이 보였다."], ["They seemed to have quite forgotten him already.", "그들은 벌써 그를 까맣게 잊은 듯했다."]], [["All of them, except, perhaps, one.", "어쩌면 한 사람만 빼고, 모두가."], ["This white shape stood apart by the hedge alone.", "이 흰 형체는 산울타리 옆에 홀로 떨어져 서 있었다."], ["From her position he knew it to be the pretty maiden with whom he had not danced.", "그 자리로 보아 그는 그것이 자기가 춤추지 않은 예쁜 처녀임을 알았다."], ["Trifling as the matter was, he yet instinctively felt that she was hurt by his oversight.", "사소한 일이었으나, 그래도 그는 그녀가 자기의 무심함에 상처받았음을 본능적으로 느꼈다."], ["He wished that he had asked her; he wished that he had inquired her name.", "그녀에게 청했더라면 좋았을 것을, 그녀의 이름이라도 물어보았더라면 좋았을 것을, 하고 그는 바랐다."], ["She was so modest, so expressive, she had looked so soft in her thin white gown that he felt he had acted stupidly.", "그녀는 어찌나 다소곳하고, 어찌나 표정이 풍부하던지, 얇은 흰 가운 차림이 어찌나 부드러워 보이던지, 그는 자기가 어리석게 굴었다고 느꼈다."]], [["However, it could not be helped, and turning, and bending himself to a rapid walk, he dismissed the subject from his mind.", "그러나 어쩔 수 없는 일이었고, 그는 몸을 돌려 빠른 걸음으로 옮기며 그 일을 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버렸다. III"]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["As for Tess Durbeyfield, she did not so easily dislodge the incident from her consideration.", "테스 더비필드로 말하자면, 그녀는 그 일을 그렇게 쉽사리 마음에서 몰아내지 못했다."], ["She had no spirit to dance again for a long time, though she might have had plenty of partners; but ah!", "짝이야 얼마든지 있었을 테지만, 그녀는 오랫동안 다시 춤출 마음이 나지 않았다. 하지만 아아!"], ["they did not speak so nicely as the strange young man had done.", "그들은 그 낯선 젊은이만큼 곱게 말하지 못했다."], ["It was not till the rays of the sun had absorbed the young stranger’s retreating figure on the hill that she shook off her temporary sadness and answered her would-be partner in the affirmative.", "그 젊은 이방인의 멀어져 가는 모습이 언덕 위에서 햇살에 삼켜지고 나서야, 그녀는 잠시의 슬픔을 떨치고 짝이 되고자 하는 이에게 그러마고 답했다."]], [["She remained with her comrades till dusk, and participated with a certain zest in the dancing; though, being heart-whole as yet, she enjoyed treading a measure purely for its own sake; little divining when she saw “the soft torments, the bitter sweets, the pleasing pains, and the agreeable distresses” of those girls who had been wooed and won, what she herself was capable of in that kind.", "그녀는 땅거미가 질 때까지 동무들과 함께 남아, 어느 정도 흥겹게 춤에 어울렸다. 다만 아직 마음이 온전했던 터라, 그녀는 순전히 춤 그 자체를 위해 한 가락 밟는 것을 즐겼을 뿐, 구애를 받고 마음을 얻은 처녀들의 그 \"달콤한 괴로움, 쓰디쓴 단맛, 기분 좋은 아픔, 즐거운 시름\"을 보면서도, 정작 자기 자신이 그런 일에서 어떤 일을 겪을 수 있는지는 거의 헤아리지 못했다."], ["The struggles and wrangles of the lads for her hand in a jig were an amusement to her—no more; and when they became fierce she rebuked them.", "자기 손을 잡고 지그를 추려는 청년들의 다툼과 옥신각신은 그녀에게 그저 재밋거리였을 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었다. 그리고 그들이 사나워지면 그녀는 그들을 나무랐다."]], [["She might have stayed even later, but the incident of her father’s odd appearance and manner returned upon the girl’s mind to make her anxious, and wondering what had become of him she dropped away from the dancers and bent her steps towards the end of the village at which the parental cottage lay.", "그녀는 더 늦게까지 남아 있을 수도 있었겠지만, 아버지의 이상한 행색과 거동이 떠올라 처녀의 마음을 불안하게 했고, 아버지가 어찌 되었을까 궁금해하며 그녀는 춤추는 무리에서 슬며시 빠져나와, 부모의 오두막이 있는 마을 끝자락으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["While yet many score yards off, other rhythmic sounds than those she had quitted became audible to her; sounds that she knew well—so well.", "아직 수십 야드나 떨어져 있는데도, 그녀가 두고 온 것과는 다른 가락의 소리가 들려왔다. 그녀가 잘 아는, 너무도 잘 아는 소리였다."], ["They were a regular series of thumpings from the interior of the house, occasioned by the violent rocking of a cradle upon a stone floor, to which movement a feminine voice kept time by singing, in a vigorous gallopade, the favourite ditty of “The Spotted Cow”—", "그것은 집 안에서 들려오는 일정한 쿵쿵 소리였으니, 돌바닥 위에서 요람을 거칠게 흔들어 대는 데서 나는 것이었고, 그 움직임에 박자를 맞추어 여자 목소리 하나가 활기찬 갤로파드 가락으로 즐겨 부르는 노래 「점박이 암소」를 부르고 있었다."]], [["I saw her lie do′-own in yon′-der green gro′-ove; Come, love!′ and I'll tell′ you where!′", "나는 보았네 그녀가 저—기 푸—른 숲에 누—워 있는 걸, 오라, 내 사랑! 내가 어디인지 일러—줄 테니!"]], [["The cradle-rocking and the song would cease simultaneously for a moment, and an exclamation at highest vocal pitch would take the place of the melody.", "요람 흔드는 소리와 노래가 잠시 동시에 그치고, 그 가락 대신 가장 높은 목청의 외침이 들려오곤 했다."]], [["“God bless thy diment eyes! And thy waxen cheeks!", "\"하느님이 네 다이아몬드 같은 눈에 복을 주시기를! 네 밀랍 같은 볼에도!"], ["And thy cherry mouth!", "네 앵두 같은 입에도!"], ["And thy Cubit’s thighs!", "네 큐피드 같은 허벅지에도!"], ["And every bit o’ thy blessed body!”", "네 복된 몸뚱이 구석구석에도!\""]], [["After this invocation the rocking and the singing would recommence, and the “Spotted Cow” proceed as before.", "이 기원이 끝나면 다시 요람 흔드는 소리와 노래가 시작되고, 「점박이 암소」가 전처럼 이어졌다."], ["So matters stood when Tess opened the door and paused upon the mat within it, surveying the scene.", "테스가 문을 열고 안쪽 발판 위에 멈춰 서서 그 광경을 살필 때, 사정은 이러했다."]], [["The interior, in spite of the melody, struck upon the girl’s senses with an unspeakable dreariness.", "가락이 울려 퍼지는데도, 집 안은 처녀의 감각에 이루 말할 수 없는 황량함으로 다가왔다."], ["From the holiday gaieties of the field—the white gowns, the nosegays, the willow-wands, the whirling movements on the green, the flash of gentle sentiment towards the stranger—to the yellow melancholy of this one-candled spectacle, what a step!", "들판의 잔칫날 흥겨움에서—흰 가운, 꽃다발, 버드나무 가지, 풀밭 위에서 빙빙 돌던 움직임, 낯선 이를 향한 은은한 감정의 번뜩임에서—초 한 자루 켜진 이 누런 우울의 광경에 이르기까지, 이 무슨 낙차란 말인가!"], ["Besides the jar of contrast there came to her a chill self-reproach that she had not returned sooner, to help her mother in these domesticities, instead of indulging herself out-of-doors.", "이 대비의 충격에 더해, 그녀에게는 자기가 더 일찍 돌아와 이 집안일에서 어머니를 돕지 않고 바깥에서 제 흥에 겨워 놀았다는 차가운 자책이 밀려왔다."]], [["There stood her mother amid the group of children, as Tess had left her, hanging over the Monday washing-tub, which had now, as always, lingered on to the end of the week.", "거기, 테스가 두고 갔던 그대로, 어머니가 아이들 무리 한가운데서, 월요일에 시작한 빨래 통 위에 구부정히 서 있었으니, 그 빨래는 늘 그렇듯 이제 주말까지 질질 끌려와 있었다."], ["Out of that tub had come the day before—Tess felt it with a dreadful sting of remorse—the very white frock upon her back which she had so carelessly greened about the skirt on the damping grass—which had been wrung up and ironed by her mother’s own hands.", "바로 그 통에서, 어제—테스는 끔찍한 회한의 가책을 느끼며 깨달았다—지금 자기 등에 걸친 바로 그 흰 드레스가 나왔으니, 그녀가 축축한 풀밭에서 치마 자락에 풀물을 그토록 함부로 들인 그 옷이, 어머니가 손수 짜서 다림질해 준 것이었다."]], [["As usual, Mrs Durbeyfield was balanced on one foot beside the tub, the other being engaged in the aforesaid business of rocking her youngest child.", "여느 때처럼 더비필드 부인은 통 옆에서 한 발로 균형을 잡고 있었고, 다른 발은 앞서 말한 막내를 어르는 일에 매여 있었다."], ["The cradle-rockers had done hard duty for so many years, under the weight of so many children, on that flagstone floor, that they were worn nearly flat, in consequence of which a huge jerk accompanied each swing of the cot, flinging the baby from side to side like a weaver’s shuttle, as Mrs Durbeyfield, excited by her song, trod the rocker with all the spring that was left in her after a long day’s seething in the suds.", "요람의 흔들개는 그 돌바닥 위에서 그토록 많은 해 동안, 그토록 많은 아이들의 무게를 받아 가며 고된 임무를 다한 끝에 거의 평평하게 닳아 버렸고, 그 탓에 요람이 한 번 흔들릴 때마다 엄청난 덜컹임이 따라붙어, 아기를 베틀의 북처럼 이쪽저쪽으로 내던졌으니, 더비필드 부인이 제 노래에 흥이 올라, 비눗물에 종일 부대낀 끝에 남은 온 힘을 다해 흔들개를 밟아 댔기 때문이다."]], [["Nick-knock, nick-knock, went the cradle; the candle-flame stretched itself tall, and began jigging up and down; the water dribbled from the matron’s elbows, and the song galloped on to the end of the verse, Mrs Durbeyfield regarding her daughter the while.", "요람은 딸깍딸깍, 딸깍딸깍 흔들렸고, 촛불은 키를 한껏 늘이며 위아래로 너울너울 춤추기 시작했으며, 부인의 팔꿈치에서는 물이 뚝뚝 떨어졌고, 노래는 한 절의 끝을 향해 내달렸으니, 그동안 더비필드 부인은 딸을 바라보았다."], ["Even now, when burdened with a young family, Joan Durbeyfield was a passionate lover of tune.", "어린 자식들의 짐을 진 지금까지도, 조앤 더비필드는 가락을 열렬히 사랑하는 사람이었다."], ["No ditty floated into Blackmoor Vale from the outer world but Tess’s mother caught up its notation in a week.", "바깥세상에서 블랙무어 골짜기로 흘러 들어온 노래 한 가락도, 테스의 어머니는 일주일이면 그 곡조를 익혀 버렸다."]], [["There still faintly beamed from the woman’s features something of the freshness, and even the prettiness, of her youth; rendering it probable that the personal charms which Tess could boast of were in main part her mother’s gift, and therefore unknightly, unhistorical.", "그 여인의 얼굴에는 아직도 젊은 날의 싱그러움이, 심지어 예쁨까지도 희미하게 비쳐 나왔으니, 테스가 뽐낼 수 있는 그 미모도 대부분 어머니에게서 물려받은 것이며, 따라서 기사답지도 역사적이지도 않은 것임을 짐작케 했다."]], [["“I’ll rock the cradle for ’ee, mother,”", "\"제가 요람을 흔들게요, 엄마.\""], ["said the daughter gently.", "딸이 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“Or I’ll take off my best frock and help you wring up?", "\"아니면 좋은 옷 벗고 짜는 거 도와드릴까요?"], ["I thought you had finished long ago.”", "진작 다 끝내신 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["Her mother bore Tess no ill-will for leaving the housework to her single-handed efforts for so long; indeed, Joan seldom upbraided her thereon at any time, feeling but slightly the lack of Tess’s assistance whilst her instinctive plan for relieving herself of her labours lay in postponing them.", "어머니는 그토록 오래 집안일을 자기 혼자 떠맡게 했다고 테스를 원망하지 않았다. 실은 조앤은 어느 때든 그 일로 딸을 나무라는 일이 거의 없었으니, 자기 일의 짐을 더는 본능적인 방법이 그 일을 미루어 두는 데 있었던 터라, 테스의 도움이 없는 것을 별로 아쉬워하지 않았던 것이다."], ["To-night, however, she was even in a blither mood than usual.", "그런데 오늘 밤은 여느 때보다도 한결 들떠 있었다."], ["There was a dreaminess, a pre-occupation, an exaltation, in the maternal look which the girl could not understand.", "그 어머니의 눈빛에는 처녀로서는 알 수 없는 몽롱함과 골똘함과 들뜸이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Well, I’m glad you’ve come,”", "\"음, 네가 와서 다행이다.\""], ["her mother said, as soon as the last note had passed out of her.", "마지막 음이 입에서 빠져나가자마자 어머니가 말했다."], ["“I want to go and fetch your father; but what’s more’n that, I want to tell ’ee what have happened.", "\"네 아버지를 데리러 가야 하는데, 그보다 더한 건, 너한테 무슨 일이 있었는지 들려주고 싶구나."], ["Y’ll be fess enough, my poppet, when th’st know!”", "알고 나면 너도 어지간히 기뻐할 거다, 우리 귀염둥아!\""], ["(Mrs Durbeyfield habitually spoke the dialect; her daughter, who had passed the Sixth Standard in the National School under a London-trained mistress, spoke two languages: the dialect at home, more or less; ordinary English abroad and to persons of quality.)", "(더비필드 부인은 늘 사투리로 말했다. 런던에서 교육받은 여선생 밑에서 국립학교 육 학년 과정을 마친 그녀의 딸은 두 가지 언어를 썼으니, 집에서는 어느 정도 사투리를, 밖에서나 지체 높은 이들에게는 표준 영어를 썼다.)"]], [["“Since I’ve been away?”", "\"제가 나가 있던 사이에요?\""], ["Tess asked. “Ay!”", "테스가 물었다."]], [["“Had it anything to do with father’s making such a mommet of himself in thik carriage this afternoon?", "\"그래!\" \"오늘 오후에 아버지가 저 마차에서 그렇게 허수아비 꼴을 하신 거랑 무슨 상관이 있어요?"], ["Why did ’er? I felt inclined to sink into the ground with shame!”", "왜 그러신 거예요? 부끄러워서 땅속으로 꺼져 버리고 싶었다고요!\""]], [["“That wer all a part of the larry!", "\"그게 다 그 일의 한 부분이었지!"], ["We’ve been found to be the greatest gentlefolk in the whole county—reaching all back long before Oliver Grumble’s time—to the days of the Pagan Turks—with monuments, and vaults, and crests, and ’scutcheons, and the Lord knows what all.", "우리가 이 주(州) 전체에서 제일가는 양반 가문으로 밝혀졌단다—올리버 그럼블 시대보다도 한참 전, 이교도 투르크인들 시절까지 거슬러 올라가는—기념비며, 납골당이며, 가문(家紋)이며, 문장(紋章)이며, 그 밖에 별의별 것까지 다 있는 가문 말이다."], ["In Saint Charles’s days we was made Knights o’ the Royal Oak, our real name being d’Urberville!...", "성(聖) 찰스 시대에는 우리가 왕립 떡갈나무 기사 작위까지 받았고, 우리 진짜 이름은 더버빌이야!……"], ["Don’t that make your bosom plim?", "어때, 가슴이 부풀지 않니?"], ["’Twas on this account that your father rode home in the vlee; not because he’d been drinking, as people supposed.”", "네 아버지가 삯마차를 타고 집에 온 게 다 그 때문이지, 사람들 짐작처럼 술을 마셔서가 아니란다.\""]], [["“I’m glad of that.", "\"그건 다행이네요."], ["Will it do us any good, mother?”", "그게 우리한테 무슨 득이 될까요, 엄마?\""]], [["“O yes! ’Tis thoughted that great things may come o’t.", "\"오, 그럼! 거기서 큰일이 생길지도 모른다고들 하더라."], ["No doubt a mampus of volk of our own rank will be down here in their carriages as soon as ’tis known.", "이 일이 알려지면 우리와 같은 신분의 양반들이 한 무더기 마차를 타고 이리로 내려올 게 틀림없어."], ["Your father learnt it on his way hwome from Shaston, and he has been telling me the whole pedigree of the matter.”", "네 아버지가 섀스턴에서 집에 오는 길에 그걸 알게 됐고, 그 일의 가계도를 죄다 나한테 들려줬단다.\""]], [["“Where is father now?”", "\"아버지는 지금 어디 계세요?\""], ["asked Tess suddenly.", "테스가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["Her mother gave irrelevant information by way of answer: “He called to see the doctor to-day in Shaston.", "어머니는 동문서답으로 답했다. \"오늘 섀스턴에서 의사를 만나러 갔지."], ["It is not consumption at all, it seems.", "폐병 같은 건 전혀 아니라더라."], ["It is fat round his heart, ’a says.", "심장 둘레에 지방이 꼈다더구나."], ["There, it is like this.”", "자, 이런 모양이래.\""], ["Joan Durbeyfield, as she spoke, curved a sodden thumb and forefinger to the shape of the letter C, and used the other forefinger as a pointer.", "조앤 더비필드는 말하면서, 물에 분 엄지와 검지를 구부려 알파벳 C자 모양을 만들고, 다른 검지를 가리키는 막대처럼 썼다."], ["“‘At the present moment,’ he says to your father, ‘your heart is enclosed all round there, and all round there; this space is still open,’ ’a says.", "\"'지금으로서는,' 의사가 네 아버지한테 그러더래, '당신 심장이 여기랑 여기 둘레로 빙 둘러 막혀 있고, 이 틈만 아직 열려 있소,' 하더란다."], ["‘As soon as it do meet, so,’”—Mrs Durbeyfield closed her fingers into a circle complete—“‘off you will go like a shadder, Mr Durbeyfield,’ ’a says.", "'이게 다 맞물리면, 이렇게,'\"—더비필드 부인은 손가락을 오므려 완전한 동그라미를 만들었다—\"'당신은 그림자처럼 훅 가버리는 거요, 더비필드 씨,' 하더래."], ["‘You mid last ten years; you mid go off in ten months, or ten days.’”", "'십 년을 더 살 수도 있고, 십 개월 만에, 아니 열흘 만에 갈 수도 있소,' 하고 말이야.\""]], [["Tess looked alarmed.", "테스는 놀란 기색이었다."], ["Her father possibly to go behind the eternal cloud so soon, notwithstanding this sudden greatness!", "이렇게 갑작스러운 영광을 누리면서도, 아버지가 그토록 빨리 영원한 구름 너머로 가버릴지도 모른다니!"]], [["“But where _is_ father?”", "\"그런데 아버지는 어디 계시냐고요?\""], ["she asked again.", "그녀가 다시 물었다."]], [["Her mother put on a deprecating look.", "어머니는 변명하는 표정을 지었다."], ["“Now don’t you be bursting out angry!", "\"자, 화부터 버럭 내지는 말아라!"], ["The poor man—he felt so rafted after his uplifting by the pa’son’s news—that he went up to Rolliver’s half an hour ago.", "가엾은 양반이—목사님 소식에 잔뜩 들뜬 나머지 너무 기운이 솟구쳐서—삼십 분 전에 롤리버 주막으로 올라갔단다."], ["He do want to get up his strength for his journey to-morrow with that load of beehives, which must be delivered, family or no.", "내일 그 벌통 짐을 싣고 떠나야 하니, 가문이고 뭐고 그건 꼭 배달해야 하는 거라, 그 여정에 쓸 기운을 차려야 한다잖니."], ["He’ll have to start shortly after twelve to-night, as the distance is so long.”", "거리가 워낙 멀어서 오늘 밤 열두 시 조금 지나서는 출발해야 한다는구나.\""]], [["“Get up his strength!”", "\"기운을 차린다고요!\""], ["said Tess impetuously, the tears welling to her eyes. “O my God!", "테스가 발끈하며 말했고, 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. \"오, 하느님!"], ["Go to a public-house to get up his strength! And you as well agreed as he, mother!”", "기운을 차린다고 술집에 가다니! 그리고 엄마도 아버지랑 똑같이 그러자고 했다니요, 엄마!\""]], [["Her rebuke and her mood seemed to fill the whole room, and to impart a cowed look to the furniture, and candle, and children playing about, and to her mother’s face.", "그녀의 나무람과 그 기색이 온 방 안을 채우는 듯했고, 가구며, 촛불이며, 이리저리 뛰노는 아이들이며, 어머니의 얼굴에까지 주눅 든 표정을 입히는 듯했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니다.\""], ["said the latter touchily, “I be not agreed.", "후자가 발끈하며 말했다. \"나는 그러자고 안 했어."], ["I have been waiting for ’ee to bide and keep house while I go fetch him.”", "내가 그이를 데리러 가는 동안 네가 남아 집을 봐 주길 기다리고 있던 참이다.\""]], [["“I’ll go.”", "\"제가 갈게요.\""]], [["“O no, Tess.", "\"오, 안 돼, 테스."], ["You see, it would be no use.”", "너도 알다시피, 그래 봐야 소용없을 거다.\""]], [["Tess did not expostulate.", "테스는 따지지 않았다."], ["She knew what her mother’s objection meant.", "어머니의 반대가 무슨 뜻인지 알았던 것이다."], ["Mrs Durbeyfield’s jacket and bonnet were already hanging slily upon a chair by her side, in readiness for this contemplated jaunt, the reason for which the matron deplored more than its necessity.", "더비필드 부인의 윗옷과 보닛은 이미 그녀 옆 의자에 슬그머니 걸쳐져, 이 작정한 나들이를 위한 채비를 갖추고 있었으니, 그 이유를, 정작 부인은 그 필요성보다도 더 한탄하는 것이었다."]], [["“And take the _Compleat Fortune-Teller_ to the outhouse,”", "\"그리고 『완벽한 점술서』는 헛간에 갖다 두렴.\""], ["Joan continued, rapidly wiping her hands, and donning the garments.", "조앤이 손을 부랴부랴 닦고 옷을 걸치며 말을 이었다."]], [["The _Compleat Fortune-Teller_ was an old thick volume, which lay on a table at her elbow, so worn by pocketing that the margins had reached the edge of the type.", "『완벽한 점술서』는 그녀의 팔꿈치 옆 탁자에 놓인 오래되고 두툼한 책이었는데, 주머니에 어찌나 넣고 다녔던지 닳아서 여백이 활자 가장자리까지 줄어들어 있었다."], ["Tess took it up, and her mother started.", "테스가 그것을 집어 들자, 어머니가 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["This going to hunt up her shiftless husband at the inn was one of Mrs Durbeyfield’s still extant enjoyments in the muck and muddle of rearing children.", "이렇게 주막으로 게으른 남편을 찾으러 가는 일은, 아이들을 기르는 진창과 혼란 속에서도 더비필드 부인에게 아직 남아 있는 즐거움 가운데 하나였다."], ["To discover him at Rolliver’s, to sit there for an hour or two by his side and dismiss all thought and care of the children during the interval, made her happy.", "롤리버 주막에서 그를 찾아내, 한두 시간 그의 곁에 앉아, 그동안 아이들에 대한 온갖 생각과 근심을 떨쳐 버리는 일이 그녀를 행복하게 했다."], ["A sort of halo, an occidental glow, came over life then.", "그럴 때면 삶 위로 일종의 후광이, 서쪽 노을 같은 빛이 어렸다."], ["Troubles and other realities took on themselves a metaphysical impalpability, sinking to mere mental phenomena for serene contemplation, and no longer stood as pressing concretions which chafed body and soul.", "근심이며 다른 현실들이 형이상학적으로 잡히지 않는 것이 되어, 차분히 바라볼 한낱 마음속 현상으로 가라앉았고, 더는 몸과 마음을 쓸어 대는 절박한 응어리로 버티고 서 있지 않았다."], ["The youngsters, not immediately within sight, seemed rather bright and desirable appurtenances than otherwise; the incidents of daily life were not without humorousness and jollity in their aspect there.", "당장 눈앞에 보이지 않는 아이들은 차라리 밝고 바람직한 부속물처럼 여겨졌고, 일상의 자질구레한 일들도 그곳에서는 그 면모에 우스움과 즐거움이 없지 않았다."], ["She felt a little as she had used to feel when she sat by her now wedded husband in the same spot during his wooing, shutting her eyes to his defects of character, and regarding him only in his ideal presentation as lover.", "그녀는 지금은 혼인한 남편이 구애하던 시절, 바로 그 자리에서 그의 곁에 앉아, 그의 성격적 결함에는 눈을 감고 오로지 연인이라는 이상적인 모습으로만 그를 바라보던 때 느끼던 기분을 조금쯤 다시 느꼈다."]], [["Tess, being left alone with the younger children, went first to the outhouse with the fortune-telling book, and stuffed it into the thatch.", "테스는 어린 동생들과 남겨진 채, 먼저 점술서를 들고 헛간으로 가서 그것을 초가지붕 짚 속에 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["A curious fetishistic fear of this grimy volume on the part of her mother prevented her ever allowing it to stay in the house all night, and hither it was brought back whenever it had been consulted.", "이 꾀죄죄한 책에 대한 어머니의 묘한 미신적 두려움 때문에, 어머니는 그것을 결코 밤새 집 안에 두는 법이 없었고, 그래서 그것을 들춰 본 뒤에는 늘 이리로 도로 가져왔다."], ["Between the mother, with her fast-perishing lumber of superstitions, folk-lore, dialect, and orally transmitted ballads, and the daughter, with her trained National teachings and Standard knowledge under an infinitely Revised Code, there was a gap of two hundred years as ordinarily understood.", "빠르게 스러져 가는 미신과 민간전승과 사투리와 입에서 입으로 전해진 옛 노래의 잡동사니를 지닌 어머니와, 무한히 개정된 학습 규정 아래서 국립학교의 정규 교육과 표준 지식을 익힌 딸 사이에는, 흔히들 말하듯 이백 년의 간극이 있었다."], ["When they were together the Jacobean and the Victorian ages were juxtaposed.", "그 둘이 함께 있을 때면 제임스 왕조 시대와 빅토리아 시대가 나란히 놓이는 셈이었다."]], [["Returning along the garden path Tess mused on what the mother could have wished to ascertain from the book on this particular day.", "정원 길을 따라 돌아오면서 테스는 하필 오늘 같은 날 어머니가 그 책에서 무엇을 알아내고 싶어 했을까 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["She guessed the recent ancestral discovery to bear upon it, but did not divine that it solely concerned herself.", "그녀는 최근의 가문 발견이 그것과 관련 있으리라 짐작했으나, 그것이 오로지 자기 자신에 관한 일임은 알아채지 못했다."], ["Dismissing this, however, she busied herself with sprinkling the linen dried during the day-time, in company with her nine-year-old brother Abraham, and her sister Eliza-Louisa of twelve and a half, called “’Liza-Lu,” the youngest ones being put to bed.", "그러나 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리고, 그녀는 낮 동안 말려 둔 빨래에 물을 뿌리는 일에 분주했으니, 아홉 살배기 남동생 에이브러햄과, '리자루'라 불리는 열두 살 반의 여동생 일라이자루이자가 함께였고, 더 어린 아이들은 잠자리에 들었다."], ["There was an interval of four years and more between Tess and the next of the family, the two who had filled the gap having died in their infancy, and this lent her a deputy-maternal attitude when she was alone with her juniors.", "테스와 그다음 아이 사이에는 사 년 남짓의 간격이 있었으니, 그 틈을 메웠던 두 아이는 갓난 시절에 죽었고, 이 때문에 그녀는 동생들과 단둘이 있을 때면 어머니 대리 같은 태도를 띠게 되었다."], ["Next in juvenility to Abraham came two more girls, Hope and Modesty; then a boy of three, and then the baby, who had just completed his first year.", "에이브러햄 다음으로 어린 축에는 호프와 모디스티라는 두 여자아이가 더 있었고, 그다음에 세 살배기 사내아이, 그다음에 막 첫돌을 채운 갓난아기가 있었다."]], [["All these young souls were passengers in the Durbeyfield ship—entirely dependent on the judgement of the two Durbeyfield adults for their pleasures, their necessities, their health, even their existence.", "이 어린 영혼들은 모두 더비필드라는 배에 탄 승객이었으니—그들의 즐거움도, 필요도, 건강도, 심지어 그 목숨까지도 전적으로 더비필드 집안 두 어른의 판단에 매여 있었다."], ["If the heads of the Durbeyfield household chose to sail into difficulty, disaster, starvation, disease, degradation, death, thither were these half-dozen little captives under hatches compelled to sail with them—six helpless creatures, who had never been asked if they wished for life on any terms, much less if they wished for it on such hard conditions as were involved in being of the shiftless house of Durbeyfield.", "더비필드 집안의 가장들이 곤경으로, 재난으로, 굶주림으로, 질병으로, 타락으로, 죽음으로 배를 몰아가기로 한다면, 갑판 아래 갇힌 이 여섯 어린 포로들도 그들과 함께 그리로 떠밀려 갈 수밖에 없었다—여섯의 가엾은 것들, 어떤 조건으로든 삶을 원하느냐는 물음조차 받은 적 없었거늘, 하물며 게으른 더비필드 집안의 자식이 된다는 데 따르는 그토록 모진 조건으로 삶을 원하느냐는 물음이야 더더욱."], ["Some people would like to know whence the poet whose philosophy is in these days deemed as profound and trustworthy as his song is breezy and pure, gets his authority for speaking of “Nature’s holy plan.”", "요즘 들어 그 시(詩)가 산뜻하고 순결한 만큼이나 그 철학이 심오하고 미덥다고 여겨지는 어느 시인이, 무슨 권위로 \"자연의 거룩한 섭리\"를 운운하는지, 더러는 알고 싶어 할지도 모른다."]], [["It grew later, and neither father nor mother reappeared.", "밤이 깊어 갔으나, 아버지도 어머니도 다시 나타나지 않았다."], ["Tess looked out of the door, and took a mental journey through Marlott.", "테스는 문밖을 내다보며, 마음속으로 말럿을 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["The village was shutting its eyes.", "마을이 눈을 감고 있었다."], ["Candles and lamps were being put out everywhere: she could inwardly behold the extinguisher and the extended hand.", "사방에서 촛불과 등불이 꺼져 가고 있었다. 그녀는 불 끄는 도구와 뻗은 손길을 마음속으로 그려 볼 수 있었다."]], [["Her mother’s fetching simply meant one more to fetch. Tess began to perceive that a man in indifferent health, who proposed to start on a journey before one in the morning, ought not to be at an inn at this late hour celebrating his ancient blood.", "어머니가 데리러 간 것은 결국 데려와야 할 사람이 하나 더 늘었다는 뜻일 뿐이었다. 테스는, 새벽 한 시 전에 길을 떠나려는, 건강도 시원찮은 사람이 이런 늦은 시각에 주막에서 제 가문의 핏줄이나 기리고 있어서는 안 된다는 것을 차츰 깨닫기 시작했다."]], [["“Abraham,”", "\"에이브러햄,\""], ["she said to her little brother, “do you put on your hat—you bain’t afraid?—and go up to Rolliver’s, and see what has gone wi’ father and mother.”", "그녀가 어린 남동생에게 말했다. \"모자 쓰고—무섭진 않지?—롤리버 주막에 올라가서, 아버지랑 어머니가 어찌 됐는지 좀 보고 오렴.\""]], [["The boy jumped promptly from his seat, and opened the door, and the night swallowed him up.", "소년은 자리에서 냉큼 뛰어내려 문을 열었고, 밤이 그를 삼켜 버렸다."], ["Half an hour passed yet again; neither man, woman, nor child returned.", "또 삼십 분이 지났다. 남자도, 여자도, 아이도 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Abraham, like his parents, seemed to have been limed and caught by the ensnaring inn.", "에이브러햄도 제 부모처럼 그 덫 같은 주막에 끈끈이로 붙들려 잡힌 모양이었다."]], [["“I must go myself,”", "\"내가 직접 가봐야겠어.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["’Liza-Lu then went to bed, and Tess, locking them all in, started on her way up the dark and crooked lane or street not made for hasty progress; a street laid out before inches of land had value, and when one-handed clocks sufficiently subdivided the day.", "그래서 리자루가 잠자리에 들었고, 테스는 그들을 모두 안에 가두어 잠그고, 어둡고 구불구불한, 급히 나아가라고 난 것이 아닌 골목길, 아니 거리를 따라 길을 나섰다. 그 거리는 손바닥만 한 땅에도 값이 매겨지기 전에, 시침 하나짜리 시계가 하루를 충분히 잘게 나누던 시절에 닦인 길이었다."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["Rolliver’s inn, the single alehouse at this end of the long and broken village, could only boast of an off-licence; hence, as nobody could legally drink on the premises, the amount of overt accommodation for consumers was strictly limited to a little board about six inches wide and two yards long, fixed to the garden palings by pieces of wire, so as to form a ledge.", "IV\n롤리버 주막은, 길고 끊긴 이 마을 이쪽 끝의 유일한 술집으로, 점포 밖 판매 면허밖에 자랑할 수 없었다. 그래서 누구도 가게 안에서는 합법적으로 술을 마실 수 없었으므로, 손님들을 위해 드러내 놓고 마련된 자리라곤, 정원 울타리 말뚝에 철사 조각으로 붙들어 매어 선반처럼 만든, 폭 여섯 인치에 길이 두 야드쯤 되는 작은 판자 하나뿐이었다."], ["On this board thirsty strangers deposited their cups as they stood in the road and drank, and threw the dregs on the dusty ground to the pattern of Polynesia, and wished they could have a restful seat inside.", "이 판자 위에 목마른 길손들은 길에 선 채로 술을 마시며 잔을 내려놓았고, 찌꺼기를 폴리네시아 군도 모양으로 먼지투성이 땅에 내던지며, 안에 편안히 앉을 자리가 있었으면 하고 바랐다."]], [["Thus the strangers.", "길손들은 그러했다."], ["But there were also local customers who felt the same wish; and where there’s a will there’s a way.", "그러나 똑같은 바람을 품은 동네 단골들도 있었다. 그리고 뜻이 있는 곳에 길이 있는 법이다."]], [["In a large bedroom upstairs, the window of which was thickly curtained with a great woollen shawl lately discarded by the landlady, Mrs Rolliver, were gathered on this evening nearly a dozen persons, all seeking beatitude; all old inhabitants of the nearer end of Marlott, and frequenters of this retreat.", "이층의 어느 큰 침실에는, 그 창문이 여주인 롤리버 부인이 얼마 전에 내버린 커다란 모직 숄로 두껍게 가려져 있었는데, 이날 저녁 열 명 남짓한 사람들이 모여 있었으니, 죄다 더없는 행복을 좇는 이들이요, 죄다 말럿 이쪽 끝의 오랜 주민이자 이 은신처의 단골이었다."], ["Not only did the distance to the The Pure Drop, the fully-licensed tavern at the further part of the dispersed village, render its accommodation practically unavailable for dwellers at this end; but the far more serious question, the quality of the liquor, confirmed the prevalent opinion that it was better to drink with Rolliver in a corner of the housetop than with the other landlord in a wide house.", "흩어진 마을의 더 먼 쪽에 있는, 정식 면허를 갖춘 술집 '순수한 한 방울'까지의 거리가 이쪽 끝에 사는 이들에게는 그 시설을 사실상 이용할 수 없게 만든 데다, 그보다 훨씬 더 심각한 문제, 곧 술의 품질이, 다른 주막 주인과 함께 너른 집에서 마시느니 롤리버와 함께 지붕 밑 한구석에서 마시는 편이 낫다는 널리 퍼진 의견을 굳혔던 것이다."]], [["A gaunt four-post bedstead which stood in the room afforded sitting-space for several persons gathered round three of its sides; a couple more men had elevated themselves on a chest of drawers; another rested on the oak-carved “cwoffer”; two on the wash-stand; another on the stool; and thus all were, somehow, seated at their ease.", "방 안에 놓인 앙상한 네 기둥 침대는 그 세 면을 둘러앉은 여러 사람에게 앉을 자리를 내주었고, 두엇은 서랍장 위에 몸을 올렸으며, 또 한 사람은 떡갈나무를 깎아 만든 \"괴짝\" 위에, 둘은 세면대 위에, 또 한 사람은 걸상 위에 자리 잡았으니, 이렇게 해서 다들 어떻게든 편히 앉아 있었다."], ["The stage of mental comfort to which they had arrived at this hour was one wherein their souls expanded beyond their skins, and spread their personalities warmly through the room.", "이 시각에 이른 그들의 마음의 편안함이란, 그 영혼이 살갗 밖으로 부풀어 올라, 제 인격을 방 안 가득 따뜻하게 펼쳐 놓는 그런 경지였다."], ["In this process the chamber and its furniture grew more and more dignified and luxurious; the shawl hanging at the window took upon itself the richness of tapestry; the brass handles of the chest of drawers were as golden knockers; and the carved bedposts seemed to have some kinship with the magnificent pillars of Solomon’s temple.", "이 과정에서 방과 그 가구는 점점 더 위엄 있고 호사스러워졌으니, 창에 걸린 숄은 태피스트리의 풍요로움을 띠었고, 서랍장의 놋쇠 손잡이는 황금 문고리 같았으며, 깎아 만든 침대 기둥들은 솔로몬 성전의 장엄한 기둥들과 어딘가 친척 사이라도 되는 듯했다."]], [["Mrs Durbeyfield, having quickly walked hitherward after parting from Tess, opened the front door, crossed the downstairs room, which was in deep gloom, and then unfastened the stair-door like one whose fingers knew the tricks of the latches well.", "더비필드 부인은 테스와 헤어진 뒤 이리로 잰걸음으로 걸어와, 앞문을 열고, 짙은 어둠에 잠긴 아래층 방을 가로지른 다음, 빗장의 요령을 잘 아는 손가락을 가진 사람답게 계단 문을 열었다."], ["Her ascent of the crooked staircase was a slower process, and her face, as it rose into the light above the last stair, encountered the gaze of all the party assembled in the bedroom.", "구불구불한 계단을 오르는 것은 더 더딘 일이었고, 마지막 계단 위로 빛 속에 떠오른 그녀의 얼굴은 침실에 모인 일행 전부의 시선과 마주쳤다."]], [["“—Being a few private friends I’ve asked in to keep up club-walking at my own expense,”", "\"—제 사비를 털어 모임 행진 뒤풀이를 하려고 사사로이 청한 몇몇 벗들이 와 있는 거랍니다.\""], ["the landlady exclaimed at the sound of footsteps, as glibly as a child repeating the Catechism, while she peered over the stairs.", "여주인이 발소리에 외쳤으니, 계단 너머를 빠끔히 내다보며, 교리문답을 외는 아이처럼 술술 늘어놓았다."], ["“Oh, ’tis you, Mrs Durbeyfield—Lard—how you frightened me!—I thought it might be some gaffer sent by Gover’ment.”", "\"오, 더비필드 부인, 당신이로군요—원 세상에—깜짝 놀랐잖아요!—정부에서 보낸 무슨 영감님인 줄 알았네.\""]], [["Mrs Durbeyfield was welcomed with glances and nods by the remainder of the conclave, and turned to where her husband sat.", "더비필드 부인은 나머지 모인 이들에게 눈짓과 끄덕임으로 맞아들여졌고, 남편이 앉아 있는 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["He was humming absently to himself, in a low tone: “I be as good as some folks here and there!", "그는 멍하니 혼자 나직한 목소리로 흥얼거리고 있었다. \"나는 여기저기 어떤 사람들 못지않게 훌륭하다네!"], ["I’ve got a great family vault at Kingsbere-sub-Greenhill, and finer skillentons than any man in Wessex!”", "나는 킹스비어서브그린힐에 커다란 가문 납골당이 있고, 웨식스의 그 어떤 사람보다 훌륭한 해골들이 있다네!\""]], [["“I’ve something to tell ’ee that’s come into my head about that—a grand projick!”", "\"그 일에 대해 내 머릿속에 떠오른 게 있는데, 들려줄 게 있어요—굉장한 계획이라고요!\""], ["whispered his cheerful wife.", "그의 명랑한 아내가 속삭였다."], ["“Here, John, don’t ’ee see me?”", "\"여보, 존, 나 안 보여요?\""], ["She nudged him, while he, looking through her as through a window-pane, went on with his recitative.", "그녀가 그를 쿡 찔렀으나, 그는 그녀를 마치 유리창 너머를 보듯 꿰뚫어 보며 제 영창을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Don’t ’ee sing so loud, my good man,”", "그렇게 큰 소리로 노래하지 마세요, 양반님.\""], ["said the landlady; “in case any member of the Gover’ment should be passing, and take away my licends.”", "여주인이 말했다. \"정부 사람이라도 지나가다가 제 면허를 빼앗아 가면 어쩌려고요.\""]], [["“He’s told ’ee what’s happened to us, I suppose?”", "\"우리한테 무슨 일이 생겼는지, 이이가 당신한테 다 말했겠죠?\""], ["asked Mrs Durbeyfield.", "더비필드 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Yes—in a way. D’ye think there’s any money hanging by it?”", "\"네, 그런 셈이죠. 거기서 돈푼이라도 나올 것 같아요?\""]], [["“Ah, that’s the secret,”", "\"아, 그게 비밀이지요.\""], ["said Joan Durbeyfield sagely.", "조앤 더비필드가 슬기롭게 말했다."], ["“However, ’tis well to be kin to a coach, even if you don’t ride in ’en.”", "\"하지만 마차에 타진 못해도, 마차와 친척이라는 것만으로도 좋은 일 아니겠어요.\""], ["She dropped her public voice, and continued in a low tone to her husband: “I’ve been thinking since you brought the news that there’s a great rich lady out by Trantridge, on the edge o’ The Chase, of the name of d’Urberville.”", "그녀는 남들 들으라는 목소리를 거두고, 남편에게 나직이 말을 이었다. \"당신이 그 소식을 가져온 뒤로 줄곧 생각해 봤는데, 트랜트리지 바깥, 체이스 숲 가장자리에, 더버빌이라는 이름의 굉장한 부잣집 마님이 산대요.\""]], [["“Hey—what’s that?”", "\"어이—그게 무슨 소리야?\""], ["said Sir John.", "존 경이 말했다."]], [["She repeated the information.", "그녀가 그 소식을 되풀이했다."], ["“That lady must be our relation,”", "\"그 마님이 우리 친척이 틀림없어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“And my projick is to send Tess to claim kin.”", "\"그래서 내 계획은 테스를 보내 친척임을 내세우게 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“There _is_ a lady of the name, now you mention it,”", "\"이제 당신이 말하니 그런 이름의 마님이 정말 있긴 하구먼.\""], ["said Durbeyfield.", "더비필드가 말했다."], ["“Pa’son Tringham didn’t think of that.", "\"트링엄 목사는 그 생각을 못 했지."], ["But she’s nothing beside we—a junior branch of us, no doubt, hailing long since King Norman’s day.”", "하지만 그 마님이야 우리에 비하면 아무것도 아니지—틀림없이 우리 집안의 방계일 거고, 노르만 왕 시절 한참 뒤에야 갈라져 나온 가지일 테니까.\""]], [["While this question was being discussed neither of the pair noticed, in their preoccupation, that little Abraham had crept into the room, and was awaiting an opportunity of asking them to return.", "이 문제가 논의되는 동안, 두 사람 다 골똘한 나머지, 어린 에이브러햄이 방 안으로 슬그머니 기어들어 와, 자기들에게 돌아가자고 청할 틈을 엿보고 있는 것을 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“She is rich, and she’d be sure to take notice o’ the maid,”", "\"그 마님은 부자고, 우리 딸을 분명히 눈여겨봐 줄 거예요.\""], ["continued Mrs Durbeyfield; “and ’twill be a very good thing.", "더비필드 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그러면 아주 좋은 일이 될 테고."], ["I don’t see why two branches o’ one family should not be on visiting terms.”", "한 집안의 두 가지가 왕래하지 못할 까닭이 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes; and we’ll all claim kin!” said Abraham brightly from under the bedstead.", "\"네, 그러면 우리도 다 친척이라고 내세우는 거예요!\" 침대 밑에서 에이브러햄이 환하게 말했다."], ["“And we’ll all go and see her when Tess has gone to live with her; and we’ll ride in her coach and wear black clothes!”", "\"그리고 테스가 그 마님이랑 같이 살러 가면, 우리도 다 같이 그 마님을 보러 가는 거고, 그 마님 마차를 타고, 검은 옷을 입는 거예요!\""]], [["“How do you come here, child?", "\"얘야, 네가 어떻게 여기 왔니?"], ["What nonsense be ye talking!", "무슨 헛소리를 하는 거야!"], ["Go away, and play on the stairs till father and mother be ready!...", "저리 가서, 아버지랑 어머니가 채비될 때까지 계단에서 놀고 있어!……"], ["Well, Tess ought to go to this other member of our family.", "그래, 테스가 이 우리 집안의 다른 식구를 찾아가는 게 좋겠어."], ["She’d be sure to win the lady—Tess would; and likely enough ’twould lead to some noble gentleman marrying her.", "그 애라면 분명히 마님의 마음을 살 거야—테스라면 말이지. 그러면 아마 어느 고귀한 신사가 그 애와 혼인하는 데까지 이를 게 틀림없어."], ["In short, I know it.”", "한마디로, 난 그렇게 될 줄 알아.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“I tried her fate in the _Fortune-Teller_, and it brought out that very thing!...", "\"내가 『점술서』로 그 애 운수를 봤는데, 바로 그게 나왔다니까요!……"], ["You should ha’ seen how pretty she looked to-day; her skin is as sumple as a duchess’.”", "오늘 그 애가 얼마나 예뻐 보였는지 당신도 봤어야 하는데. 살결이 공작부인처럼 매끈하다니까.\""]], [["“What says the maid herself to going?”", "\"그래, 본인은 가겠다고 하던가?\""]], [["“I’ve not asked her. She don’t know there is any such lady-relation yet.", "\"아직 그 애한테 안 물어봤어요. 그런 친척 마님이 있다는 것도 아직 몰라요."], ["But it would certainly put her in the way of a grand marriage, and she won’t say nay to going.”", "하지만 그건 분명히 그 애를 굉장한 혼사 길로 이끌어 줄 테고, 그 애도 가는 걸 싫다곤 안 할 거예요.\""]], [["“Tess is queer.”", "\"테스는 별나단 말이야.\""]], [["“But she’s tractable at bottom. Leave her to me.”", "\"하지만 속은 고분고분해요. 그 애는 나한테 맡겨 둬요.\""]], [["Though this conversation had been private, sufficient of its import reached the understandings of those around to suggest to them that the Durbeyfields had weightier concerns to talk of now than common folks had, and that Tess, their pretty eldest daughter, had fine prospects in store.", "이 대화는 사사로운 것이었으나, 그 요지가 주위 사람들의 머리에 충분히 전해져, 더비필드 집안이 이제 여느 사람들보다 더 묵직한 일거리를 입에 올리고 있으며, 그들의 예쁜 맏딸 테스에게 좋은 앞날이 기다리고 있다는 것을 짐작케 했다."]], [["“Tess is a fine figure o’ fun, as I said to myself to-day when I zeed her vamping round parish with the rest,”", "\"테스는 참 보기 좋은 처녀라니까, 오늘 그 애가 다른 애들이랑 교구를 돌아다니는 걸 봤을 때 나도 속으로 그랬지.\""], ["observed one of the elderly boozers in an undertone. “But Joan Durbeyfield must mind that she don’t get green malt in floor.”", "술꾼 노인 중 하나가 나직이 말했다. \"하지만 조앤 더비필드는 마룻바닥에서 보리 싹이 트지 않도록 조심해야 할 거야.\""], ["It was a local phrase which had a peculiar meaning, and there was no reply.", "이것은 묘한 뜻이 담긴 동네 말이었는데, 아무도 대꾸하지 않았다."]], [["The conversation became inclusive, and presently other footsteps were heard crossing the room below.", "대화는 모두가 어울리는 것이 되었고, 이윽고 아래층 방을 가로지르는 또 다른 발소리가 들렸다."]], [["“—Being a few private friends asked in to-night to keep up club-walking at my own expense.”", "\"—오늘 밤 제 사비로 모임 행진 뒤풀이를 하려고 사사로이 청한 몇몇 벗들이…….\""], ["The landlady had rapidly re-used the formula she kept on hand for intruders before she recognized that the newcomer was Tess.", "여주인은, 새로 들어온 이가 테스임을 알아채기도 전에, 침입자들을 위해 늘 준비해 두는 그 상투적인 말을 잽싸게 다시 써먹었다."]], [["Even to her mother’s gaze the girl’s young features looked sadly out of place amid the alcoholic vapours which floated here as no unsuitable medium for wrinkled middle-age; and hardly was a reproachful flash from Tess’s dark eyes needed to make her father and mother rise from their seats, hastily finish their ale, and descend the stairs behind her, Mrs Rolliver’s caution following their footsteps.", "어머니의 눈으로 보기에도, 주름진 중년에게는 그리 어울리지 않을 것 없는 매개로서 이곳에 떠도는 술기운 속에서, 처녀의 앳된 얼굴은 슬프도록 어울리지 않아 보였다. 그리고 테스의 검은 눈에서 번뜩이는 나무람의 빛까지 갈 것도 없이, 그 아버지와 어머니는 자리에서 일어나, 황급히 맥주를 마저 비우고, 그녀 뒤를 따라 계단을 내려왔으니, 롤리버 부인의 당부가 그 발소리를 뒤쫓았다."]], [["“No noise, please, if ye’ll be so good, my dears; or I mid lose my licends, and be summons’d, and I don’t know what all!", "\"제발 소리 좀 내지 말아 주세요, 그래 주시면 고맙겠어요, 여러분. 안 그러면 면허를 잃고, 소환장을 받고, 그 밖에 무슨 일이 닥칠지 몰라요!"], ["’Night t’ye!”", "안녕히들 가세요!\""]], [["They went home together, Tess holding one arm of her father, and Mrs Durbeyfield the other.", "그들은 함께 집으로 갔으니, 테스가 아버지의 한쪽 팔을, 더비필드 부인이 다른 쪽 팔을 잡았다."], ["He had, in truth, drunk very little—not a fourth of the quantity which a systematic tippler could carry to church on a Sunday afternoon without a hitch in his eastings or genuflections; but the weakness of Sir John’s constitution made mountains of his petty sins in this kind.", "그는 사실 술을 거의 마시지 않았으니—습관적으로 술을 즐기는 사람이라면 일요일 오후에 동쪽을 향한 절이나 무릎 꿇는 예배에 한 치 흔들림 없이 교회까지 지고 갈 수 있을 양의 사분의 일도 안 되었다. 하지만 존 경의 허약한 체질은 이 방면의 사소한 죄를 산더미로 부풀려 놓았다."], ["On reaching the fresh air he was sufficiently unsteady to incline the row of three at one moment as if they were marching to London, and at another as if they were marching to Bath—which produced a comical effect, frequent enough in families on nocturnal homegoings; and, like most comical effects, not quite so comic after all.", "신선한 바깥 공기에 나서자 그는 꽤나 비틀거려, 세 사람의 줄을 어느 순간에는 마치 런던으로 행군하는 듯이, 또 어느 순간에는 마치 배스로 행군하는 듯이 기우뚱하게 만들었으니—이는 우스운 효과를 자아냈으니, 밤늦게 집으로 돌아가는 가족들에게는 흔한 일이었다. 그리고 대부분의 우스운 효과가 그렇듯, 따지고 보면 그리 우습지만은 않은 것이었다."], ["The two women valiantly disguised these forced excursions and countermarches as well as they could from Durbeyfield, their cause, and from Abraham, and from themselves; and so they approached by degrees their own door, the head of the family bursting suddenly into his former refrain as he drew near, as if to fortify his soul at sight of the smallness of his present residence—", "두 여자는 이 본의 아닌 옆걸음질과 갈지자 행군을 할 수 있는 한 용감히 감추었으니, 그 원인인 더비필드에게서도, 에이브러햄에게서도, 그리고 자기들 자신에게서도 숨겼다. 그리하여 그들은 차츰차츰 제 집 문에 다가갔으니, 집안의 가장은 가까워지자 문득 아까 그 후렴을 터뜨렸다. 마치 지금 사는 거처가 얼마나 작은지를 보고 제 영혼을 다잡으려는 듯이—"]], [["“I’ve got a fam—ily vault at Kingsbere!”", "\"나는 킹스비어에 가—문 납골당이 있다네!\""]], [["“Hush—don’t be so silly, Jacky,”", "\"쉿—그렇게 바보같이 굴지 마세요, 재키.\""], ["said his wife. “Yours is not the only family that was of ’count in wold days.", "그의 아내가 말했다. \"옛날에 이름깨나 날린 게 당신네 집안만은 아니라고요."], ["Look at the Anktells, and Horseys, and the Tringhams themselves—gone to seed a’most as much as you—though you was bigger folks than they, that’s true.", "앵크텔 집안이랑, 호시 집안이랑, 트링엄 집안 자기들만 봐도, 당신만큼이나 거의 다 영락했잖아요—하긴 당신네가 그들보다 더 대단한 집안이긴 했지요, 그건 사실이고."], ["Thank God, I was never of no family, and have nothing to be ashamed of in that way!”", "다행히도 나는 무슨 집안 출신도 아니라서, 그런 걸로 부끄러워할 게 하나도 없으니!\""]], [["“Don’t you be so sure o’ that.", "\"그건 그렇게 장담하지 마."], ["From your nater ’tis my belief you’ve disgraced yourselves more than any o’ us, and was kings and queens outright at one time.”", "당신 천성으로 보면, 내 생각엔 당신네야말로 우리 누구보다도 더 망신살이 뻗쳤을 게 분명하고, 한때는 어엿한 왕이며 왕비였을 거야.\""]], [["Tess turned the subject by saying what was far more prominent in her own mind at the moment than thoughts of her ancestry—“I am afraid father won’t be able to take the journey with the beehives to-morrow so early.”", "테스는, 자기 조상에 대한 생각보다 그 순간 자기 마음속에서 훨씬 더 두드러진 것을 말함으로써 화제를 돌렸다—\"아버지가 내일 그렇게 일찍 벌통을 싣고 길을 떠나지 못하실까 봐 걱정이에요.\""]], [["“I? I shall be all right in an hour or two,”", "\"내가? 나는 한두 시간이면 멀쩡해질 거다.\""], ["said Durbeyfield.", "더비필드가 말했다."]], [["It was eleven o’clock before the family were all in bed, and two o’clock next morning was the latest hour for starting with the beehives if they were to be delivered to the retailers in Casterbridge before the Saturday market began, the way thither lying by bad roads over a distance of between twenty and thirty miles, and the horse and waggon being of the slowest.", "가족이 모두 잠자리에 든 것은 열한 시였고, 토요일 장이 서기 전에 벌통을 캐스터브리지의 소매상들에게 배달하려면 다음 날 아침 두 시가 출발할 수 있는 가장 늦은 시각이었으니, 그리로 가는 길은 험한 길을 따라 이십 마일에서 삼십 마일이나 되는 데다, 말과 짐마차도 더없이 느렸다."], ["At half-past one Mrs Durbeyfield came into the large bedroom where Tess and all her little brothers and sisters slept.", "한 시 반에 더비필드 부인이 테스와 그 어린 동생들이 모두 자는 큰 침실로 들어왔다."]], [["“The poor man can’t go,” she said to her eldest daughter, whose great eyes had opened the moment her mother’s hand touched the door.", "\"가엾은 양반이 못 가겠구나.\" 그녀가 맏딸에게 말했고, 딸의 큰 눈은 어머니의 손이 문에 닿는 순간 떠졌다."]], [["Tess sat up in bed, lost in a vague interspace between a dream and this information.", "테스는 침대에서 일어나 앉았으니, 꿈과 이 소식 사이의 어렴풋한 틈새에 빠진 채였다."]], [["“But somebody must go,” she replied.", "\"하지만 누군가는 가야 해요.\""], ["“It is late for the hives already.", "그녀가 답했다. \"벌통도 이미 늦었어요."], ["Swarming will soon be over for the year; and it we put off taking ’em till next week’s market the call for ’em will be past, and they’ll be thrown on our hands.”", "올해 분봉도 곧 끝날 테고, 다음 주 장까지 미뤘다간 사겠다는 데도 없어져서, 우리한테 고스란히 떠안게 될 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs Durbeyfield looked unequal to the emergency.", "더비필드 부인은 이 위급한 상황을 감당할 만해 보이지 않았다."], ["“Some young feller, perhaps, would go?", "\"어쩌면 어느 젊은이가 가주려나?"], ["One of them who were so much after dancing with ’ee yesterday,”", "어제 너랑 그렇게 춤추고 싶어 안달하던 애들 중 하나가 말이다.\""], ["she presently suggested.", "그녀가 이윽고 넌지시 말했다."]], [["“O no—I wouldn’t have it for the world!”", "\"오, 안 돼요—절대로 그렇겐 못 해요!\""], ["declared Tess proudly. “And letting everybody know the reason—such a thing to be ashamed of!", "테스가 자존심 있게 잘라 말했다. \"그리고 그 까닭을 모두에게 알리다니요—그런 부끄러운 일이!"], ["I think _I_ could go if Abraham could go with me to kip me company.”", "에이브러햄이 같이 가서 말동무가 되어 준다면 저 혼자라도 갈 수 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["Her mother at length agreed to this arrangement.", "어머니는 마침내 이 안에 동의했다."], ["Little Abraham was aroused from his deep sleep in a corner of the same apartment, and made to put on his clothes while still mentally in the other world.", "어린 에이브러햄은 같은 방 한구석에서 깊은 잠에서 깨워져, 아직 마음은 저세상에 둔 채로 옷을 입혀졌다."], ["Meanwhile Tess had hastily dressed herself; and the twain, lighting a lantern, went out to the stable.", "그동안 테스는 황급히 옷을 차려입었다. 그렇게 둘은 등불을 켜고 마구간으로 나갔다."], ["The rickety little waggon was already laden, and the girl led out the horse, Prince, only a degree less rickety than the vehicle.", "후들거리는 작은 짐마차에는 이미 짐이 실려 있었고, 처녀는 말 프린스를 끌어냈으니, 그 말은 마차보다 딱 한 단계만 덜 후들거릴 뿐이었다."]], [["The poor creature looked wonderingly round at the night, at the lantern, at their two figures, as if he could not believe that at that hour, when every living thing was intended to be in shelter and at rest, he was called upon to go out and labour.", "그 가엾은 짐승은, 모든 살아 있는 것이 잠자리에 들어 쉬어야 할 이 시각에 자기가 밖에 나가 일하라는 부름을 받았다는 것을 도무지 믿을 수 없다는 듯, 밤을, 등불을, 그리고 둘의 모습을 의아하게 둘러보았다."], ["They put a stock of candle-ends into the lantern, hung the latter to the off-side of the load, and directed the horse onward, walking at his shoulder at first during the uphill parts of the way, in order not to overload an animal of so little vigour.", "그들은 등불에 양초 토막을 잔뜩 채워 넣고, 그것을 짐의 바깥쪽에 매단 다음, 말을 앞으로 몰았으니, 처음에는 오르막길에서 기운이라곤 거의 없는 짐승에게 짐을 너무 지우지 않으려고 말의 어깨 옆에서 걸었다."], ["To cheer themselves as well as they could, they made an artificial morning with the lantern, some bread and butter, and their own conversation, the real morning being far from come.", "진짜 아침은 아직 멀었으나, 그들은 할 수 있는 한 기운을 내려고, 등불과 약간의 빵과 버터, 그리고 그들 자신의 대화로 인공의 아침을 만들었다."], ["Abraham, as he more fully awoke (for he had moved in a sort of trance so far), began to talk of the strange shapes assumed by the various dark objects against the sky; of this tree that looked like a raging tiger springing from a lair; of that which resembled a giant’s head.", "에이브러햄은 (지금까지 일종의 비몽사몽 속에서 움직여 온 터라) 좀 더 온전히 잠에서 깨어나면서, 하늘을 배경으로 한 갖가지 어두운 물체들이 띠는 이상한 모양들에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다. 굴에서 뛰쳐나오는 사나운 호랑이처럼 보이는 이 나무에 대해, 거인의 머리를 닮은 저것에 대해."]], [["When they had passed the little town of Stourcastle, dumbly somnolent under its thick brown thatch, they reached higher ground.", "스타우어캐슬이라는 작은 마을을 지났을 때, 그곳은 두꺼운 갈색 초가지붕 아래 말없이 잠들어 있었는데, 그들은 더 높은 지대에 이르렀다."], ["Still higher, on their left, the elevation called Bulbarrow, or Bealbarrow, well-nigh the highest in South Wessex, swelled into the sky, engirdled by its earthen trenches.", "왼쪽으로 더 높이, 사우스웨식스에서 거의 가장 높다 할 불배로, 또는 빌배로라 불리는 솟아오른 땅이, 흙으로 판 도랑들에 둘러싸인 채 하늘로 부풀어 올라 있었다."], ["From hereabout the long road was fairly level for some distance onward.", "이 언저리부터 긴 길은 한동안 꽤 평탄하게 이어졌다."], ["They mounted in front of the waggon, and Abraham grew reflective.", "그들은 짐마차 앞쪽에 올라탔고, 에이브러햄은 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Tess!” he said in a preparatory tone, after a silence.", "\"테스 누나!\" 침묵 끝에 그가 무언가를 꺼내려는 듯한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Abraham.”", "\"응, 에이브러햄.\""]], [["“Bain’t you glad that we’ve become gentlefolk?”", "\"우리가 양반이 됐다니 기쁘지 않아?\""]], [["“Not particular glad.”", "\"딱히 기쁘진 않아.\""]], [["“But you be glad that you ’m going to marry a gentleman?”", "\"하지만 누나가 신사랑 혼인하게 된다니 기쁘지?\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐?\""], ["said Tess, lifting her face.", "테스가 얼굴을 들며 말했다."]], [["“That our great relation will help ’ee to marry a gentleman.”", "\"우리 굉장한 친척이 누나가 신사랑 혼인하게 도와줄 거라고.\""]], [["“I? Our great relation?", "\"내가? 우리 굉장한 친척?"], ["We have no such relation.", "우리한테 그런 친척은 없어."], ["What has put that into your head?”", "누가 그런 걸 네 머릿속에 넣었니?\""]], [["“I heard ’em talking about it up at Rolliver’s when I went to find father.", "\"아버지 찾으러 갔을 때 롤리버 주막에서 그 얘기 하는 걸 들었어."], ["There’s a rich lady of our family out at Trantridge, and mother said that if you claimed kin with the lady, she’d put ’ee in the way of marrying a gentleman.”", "트랜트리지 바깥에 우리 집안의 부잣집 마님이 산대, 그리고 누나가 그 마님이랑 친척임을 내세우면, 마님이 누나를 신사랑 혼인하는 길로 이끌어 줄 거라고 엄마가 그랬어.\""]], [["His sister became abruptly still, and lapsed into a pondering silence.", "누나는 문득 잠잠해지더니, 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 침묵 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["Abraham talked on, rather for the pleasure of utterance than for audition, so that his sister’s abstraction was of no account.", "에이브러햄은, 듣는 것보다는 말하는 즐거움 때문에 계속 떠들어 댔으므로, 누나가 넋을 놓고 있는 것은 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["He leant back against the hives, and with upturned face made observations on the stars, whose cold pulses were beating amid the black hollows above, in serene dissociation from these two wisps of human life.", "그는 벌통에 등을 기대고, 얼굴을 위로 쳐든 채 별들에 대해 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 그 차가운 맥박이 머리 위 검은 허공 속에서, 이 두 가닥 인간의 삶과는 차분히 무관하게 고동치고 있었다."], ["He asked how far away those twinklers were, and whether God was on the other side of them.", "그는 저 반짝이는 것들이 얼마나 멀리 있는지, 그리고 그 너머에 하느님이 계신지 물었다."], ["But ever and anon his childish prattle recurred to what impressed his imagination even more deeply than the wonders of creation.", "그러나 그의 어린애다운 재잘거림은 이따금, 천지창조의 경이보다도 그의 상상에 한층 더 깊이 새겨진 것으로 되돌아왔다."], ["If Tess were made rich by marrying a gentleman, would she have money enough to buy a spyglass so large that it would draw the stars as near to her as Nettlecombe-Tout?", "만일 테스가 신사와 혼인해서 부자가 된다면, 누나는 별들을 네틀콤투트만큼 가까이 끌어당겨 줄 만큼 커다란 망원경을 살 돈이 생기는 걸까?"]], [["The renewed subject, which seemed to have impregnated the whole family, filled Tess with impatience.", "온 집안에 스며든 듯한 이 거듭되는 화제가 테스를 조바심으로 가득 채웠다."]], [["“Never mind that now!”", "\"이제 그 얘긴 그만해!\""], ["she exclaimed.", "그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["“Did you say the stars were worlds, Tess?”", "\"누나가 별들이 세계라고 했지, 테스 누나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"응.\""]], [["“All like ours?”", "\"다 우리 세계 같은 거야?\""]], [["“I don’t know; but I think so.", "“잘 모르겠어. 그래도 그런 것 같아."], ["They sometimes seem to be like the apples on our stubbard-tree.", "더러는 우리 집 텃밭 사과나무에 달린 사과 같달까."], ["Most of them splendid and sound—a few blighted.”", "대부분은 멀쩡하고 튼실한데—몇 개는 병들어 있지.”"]], [["“Which do we live on—a splendid one or a blighted one?”", "“우리가 사는 건 어느 쪽이야—멀쩡한 거야, 병든 거야?”"]], [["“A blighted one.”", "“병든 거지.”"]], [["“’Tis very unlucky that we didn’t pitch on a sound one, when there were so many more of ’em!”", "“멀쩡한 게 그렇게나 많은데, 하필 멀쩡한 걸 못 고른 게 참 운도 없네!”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그래.”"]], [["“Is it like that _really_, Tess?”", "“정말 그런 거야, 테스 누나?”"], ["said Abraham, turning to her much impressed, on reconsideration of this rare information. “How would it have been if we had pitched on a sound one?”", "에이브러햄이 이 드문 이야기를 곱씹다가 잔뜩 감명받은 얼굴로 누나를 돌아보며 말했다. “멀쩡한 걸 골랐으면 어땠을까?”"]], [["“Well, father wouldn’t have coughed and creeped about as he does, and wouldn’t have got too tipsy to go on this journey; and mother wouldn’t have been always washing, and never getting finished.”", "“음, 아버지가 지금처럼 기침하고 비실대지도 않았을 거고, 이번 길에 나서지 못할 만큼 취하지도 않았겠지. 그리고 어머니도 늘 빨래만 하면서 도무지 끝을 못 보지는 않았을 거야.”"]], [["“And you would have been a rich lady ready-made, and not have had to be made rich by marrying a gentleman?”", "“그리고 누나는 처음부터 부잣집 마님이라, 신사하고 결혼해서 부자가 될 필요도 없었겠네?”"]], [["“O Aby, don’t—don’t talk of that any more!”", "“오, 에이비, 그만—그런 얘기는 더는 하지 마!”"]], [["Left to his reflections Abraham soon grew drowsy. Tess was not skilful in the management of a horse, but she thought that she could take upon herself the entire conduct of the load for the present and allow Abraham to go to sleep if he wished to do so.", "혼자 생각에 잠긴 에이브러햄은 곧 졸음에 겨워졌다. 테스는 말을 다루는 데 능숙하지 않았으나, 당분간은 짐수레 운전을 자기가 도맡고, 에이브러햄이 원한다면 잠들게 둘 수 있겠다고 생각했다."], ["She made him a sort of nest in front of the hives, in such a manner that he could not fall, and, taking the reins into her own hands, jogged on as before.", "그녀는 동생이 떨어지지 않도록 벌통 앞에 일종의 둥지 같은 자리를 만들어 주고, 고삐를 자기 손에 쥔 채 전처럼 천천히 나아갔다."]], [["Prince required but slight attention, lacking energy for superfluous movements of any sort.", "프린스는 손이 거의 가지 않아도 됐다. 군더더기 동작이라곤 할 기운조차 없었던 것이다."], ["With no longer a companion to distract her, Tess fell more deeply into reverie than ever, her back leaning against the hives.", "곁에서 정신을 흩어 놓는 동무가 없어지자, 테스는 벌통에 등을 기댄 채 그 어느 때보다 깊이 몽상에 빠져들었다."], ["The mute procession past her shoulders of trees and hedges became attached to fantastic scenes outside reality, and the occasional heave of the wind became the sigh of some immense sad soul, conterminous with the universe in space, and with history in time.", "어깨 너머로 말없이 지나가는 나무와 산울타리의 행렬은 현실 밖 환상의 장면들과 이어졌고, 이따금 일렁이는 바람은 어떤 거대하고 슬픈 영혼의 한숨이 되었으니, 공간으로는 우주와 맞닿고 시간으로는 역사와 맞닿은 영혼이었다."], ["Then, examining the mesh of events in her own life, she seemed to see the vanity of her father’s pride; the gentlemanly suitor awaiting herself in her mother’s fancy; to see him as a grimacing personage, laughing at her poverty and her shrouded knightly ancestry.", "그러다 그녀는 제 삶에 얽힌 사건의 그물을 살펴보며, 아버지의 자부심이 얼마나 헛된 것인지를, 어머니의 공상 속에서 자기를 기다리는 신사다운 구혼자가 어떤 자인지를 보는 듯했다—그자를 보았으니, 얼굴을 잔뜩 찡그린 인물이 그녀의 가난과, 수의에 싸인 기사 가문의 혈통을 비웃고 있었다."], ["Everything grew more and more extravagant, and she no longer knew how time passed.", "모든 것이 점점 더 터무니없어졌고, 그녀는 더 이상 시간이 어떻게 흐르는지 알 수 없었다."], ["A sudden jerk shook her in her seat, and Tess awoke from the sleep into which she, too, had fallen.", "갑작스러운 충격이 자리에 앉은 그녀를 흔들었고, 테스 역시 빠져들었던 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["They were a long way further on than when she had lost consciousness, and the waggon had stopped.", "정신을 잃었을 때보다 한참을 더 와 있었고, 짐수레는 멈춰 서 있었다."], ["A hollow groan, unlike anything she had ever heard in her life, came from the front, followed by a shout of “Hoi there!”", "평생 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 텅 빈 신음 소리가 앞쪽에서 들려왔고, 뒤이어 “이봐, 거기!” 하는 외침이 따랐다."]], [["The lantern hanging at her waggon had gone out, but another was shining in her face—much brighter than her own had been.", "짐수레에 걸려 있던 등불은 꺼져 있었으나, 다른 등불 하나가 그녀의 얼굴을 비추고 있었다—제 것보다 훨씬 밝은 빛이었다."], ["Something terrible had happened.", "끔찍한 일이 벌어진 것이다."], ["The harness was entangled with an object which blocked the way.", "마구가 길을 막은 무언가에 뒤엉켜 있었다."]], [["In consternation Tess jumped down, and discovered the dreadful truth.", "기겁한 테스는 뛰어내렸고, 무서운 진실을 발견했다."], ["The groan had proceeded from her father’s poor horse Prince.", "그 신음은 아버지의 가엾은 말 프린스에게서 난 것이었다."], ["The morning mail-cart, with its two noiseless wheels, speeding along these lanes like an arrow, as it always did, had driven into her slow and unlighted equipage.", "소리 없는 두 바퀴로 늘 그러하듯 화살처럼 이 좁은 길을 내달리던 아침 우편마차가, 느릿느릿 불도 켜지 않은 그녀의 수레를 들이받은 것이었다."], ["The pointed shaft of the cart had entered the breast of the unhappy Prince like a sword, and from the wound his life’s blood was spouting in a stream, and falling with a hiss into the road.", "마차의 뾰족한 끌채가 칼처럼 불행한 프린스의 가슴을 파고들었고, 그 상처에서 생명의 피가 줄기를 이루며 솟구쳐, 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 길바닥으로 떨어지고 있었다."]], [["In her despair Tess sprang forward and put her hand upon the hole, with the only result that she became splashed from face to skirt with the crimson drops. Then she stood helplessly looking on.", "절망에 빠진 테스는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 그 구멍에 손을 갖다 댔으나, 얻은 것이라곤 얼굴에서 치마까지 진홍빛 핏방울이 튀어 얼룩진 것뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 어쩔 줄 모르고 서서 지켜보았다."], ["Prince also stood firm and motionless as long as he could; till he suddenly sank down in a heap.", "프린스 역시 버틸 수 있는 한 굳건히, 움직임 없이 서 있었다. 그러다 갑자기 풀썩 주저앉아 버렸다."]], [["By this time the mail-cart man had joined her, and began dragging and unharnessing the hot form of Prince.", "이때쯤 우편마차꾼이 그녀에게 다가와, 아직 온기가 남은 프린스의 몸을 끌어내고 마구를 풀기 시작했다."], ["But he was already dead, and, seeing that nothing more could be done immediately, the mail-cart man returned to his own animal, which was uninjured.", "그러나 프린스는 이미 죽어 있었고, 당장은 더 어찌할 도리가 없음을 본 우편마차꾼은 다친 데 없는 제 짐승에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["“You was on the wrong side,”", "“댁이 길을 잘못 들었소.”"], ["he said. “I am bound to go on with the mail-bags, so that the best thing for you to do is bide here with your load.", "그가 말했다. “나는 우편 자루를 가지고 계속 가야 하니, 댁은 짐을 지키며 여기 있는 게 상책이오."], ["I’ll send somebody to help you as soon as I can.", "되도록 빨리 사람을 보내 도와주리다."], ["It is getting daylight, and you have nothing to fear.”", "날이 밝아 오니, 무서워할 것 없소.”"]], [["He mounted and sped on his way; while Tess stood and waited.", "그는 말에 올라 제 길을 서둘러 갔고, 테스는 우두커니 서서 기다렸다."], ["The atmosphere turned pale, the birds shook themselves in the hedges, arose, and twittered; the lane showed all its white features, and Tess showed hers, still whiter.", "대기는 창백해졌고, 새들은 산울타리에서 몸을 털고 날아올라 지저귀었다. 좁은 길이 제 흰 모습을 온통 드러냈고, 테스도 제 모습을 드러냈으니, 길보다 더 희었다."], ["The huge pool of blood in front of her was already assuming the iridescence of coagulation; and when the sun rose a hundred prismatic hues were reflected from it.", "그녀 앞에 고인 거대한 핏덩이는 이미 응고하며 무지갯빛을 띠기 시작했고, 해가 떠오르자 거기서 백 가지 프리즘의 색조가 반사되었다. 프린스는 그 곁에 뻣뻣하게 누워 꼼짝하지 않았다."], ["Prince lay alongside, still and stark; his eyes half open, the hole in his chest looking scarcely large enough to have let out all that had animated him.", "두 눈은 반쯤 뜨여 있었고, 가슴에 난 구멍은 그를 살아 있게 하던 모든 것이 거기서 빠져나갔다기에는 도무지 충분히 커 보이지 않았다."]], [["“’Tis all my doing—all mine!”", "“이게 다 내 탓이야—전부 내 탓!”"], ["the girl cried, gazing at the spectacle.", "소녀가 그 광경을 바라보며 울부짖었다."], ["“No excuse for me—none.", "“변명할 거리도 없어—하나도."], ["What will mother and father live on now? Aby, Aby!”", "이제 어머니랑 아버지는 뭘로 먹고살아? 에이비, 에이비!”"], ["She shook the child, who had slept soundly through the whole disaster.", "그녀는 이 참사가 다 벌어지는 동안 곤히 잠들어 있던 아이를 흔들었다."], ["“We can’t go on with our load—Prince is killed!”", "“이제 짐을 못 가지고 가—프린스가 죽었어!”"]], [["When Abraham realized all, the furrows of fifty years were extemporized on his young face.", "에이브러햄이 사태를 다 알아차리자, 오십 년 세월의 주름이 그 어린 얼굴 위에 즉석에서 패었다."]], [["“Why, I danced and laughed only yesterday!”", "“아니, 난 어제만 해도 춤추고 웃었는데!”"], ["she went on to herself. “To think that I was such a fool!”", "그녀는 혼잣말을 이어 갔다. “내가 그런 바보였다니!”"]], [["“’Tis because we be on a blighted star, and not a sound one, isn’t it, Tess?”", "“그건 우리가 멀쩡한 별이 아니라 병든 별에 살고 있어서 그래, 그렇지, 누나?”"], ["murmured Abraham through his tears.", "에이브러햄이 눈물 사이로 중얼거렸다."]], [["In silence they waited through an interval which seemed endless.", "그들은 끝없이 이어지는 듯한 시간 동안 말없이 기다렸다."], ["At length a sound, and an approaching object, proved to them that the driver of the mail-car had been as good as his word.", "마침내 어떤 소리와, 다가오는 어떤 물체가, 우편마차꾼이 약속을 지켰음을 그들에게 알려 주었다."], ["A farmer’s man from near Stourcastle came up, leading a strong cob. He was harnessed to the waggon of beehives in the place of Prince, and the load taken on towards Casterbridge.", "스토어캐슬 근방에서 온 어느 농부의 일꾼이 튼튼한 조랑말을 끌고 다가왔다. 조랑말은 프린스 대신 벌통 짐수레에 매였고, 짐은 캐스터브리지를 향해 옮겨졌다."]], [["The evening of the same day saw the empty waggon reach again the spot of the accident.", "같은 날 저녁, 빈 짐수레가 사고 장소에 다시 이르렀다."], ["Prince had lain there in the ditch since the morning; but the place of the blood-pool was still visible in the middle of the road, though scratched and scraped over by passing vehicles.", "프린스는 아침부터 그 도랑에 누워 있었다. 그러나 핏덩이가 고였던 자리는, 지나가는 수레들에 긁히고 쓸렸어도 길 한복판에 여전히 보였다."], ["All that was left of Prince was now hoisted into the waggon he had formerly hauled, and with his hoofs in the air, and his shoes shining in the setting sunlight, he retraced the eight or nine miles to Marlott.", "이제 프린스에게서 남은 전부가 그가 예전에 끌던 그 짐수레에 들어 올려졌고, 발굽을 허공에 든 채, 편자가 지는 햇빛에 번쩍이며, 말럿까지 팔구 마일 길을 되짚어 갔다."]], [["Tess had gone back earlier.", "테스는 더 일찍 돌아가 있었다."], ["How to break the news was more than she could think.", "어떻게 소식을 전해야 할지 도무지 떠오르지 않았다."], ["It was a relief to her tongue to find from the faces of her parents that they already knew of their loss, though this did not lessen the self-reproach which she continued to heap upon herself for her negligence.", "부모의 얼굴에서 이미 그들의 손실을 알고 있음을 알아챘을 때, 그녀의 혀는 한시름 놓았다. 그렇다고 그녀가 제 부주의를 두고 스스로에게 쌓아 올리던 자책이 덜어진 것은 아니었지만."]], [["But the very shiftlessness of the household rendered the misfortune a less terrifying one to them than it would have been to a thriving family, though in the present case it meant ruin, and in the other it would only have meant inconvenience.", "그러나 바로 그 무계획한 살림살이 덕분에, 이 불행은 잘나가는 집안에 닥쳤을 때보다 그들에게 덜 끔찍한 일이 되었다. 다만 이번 경우엔 그것이 파멸을 뜻했고, 저쪽 경우엔 그저 불편을 뜻했을 뿐이지만."], ["In the Durbeyfield countenances there was nothing of the red wrath that would have burnt upon the girl from parents more ambitious for her welfare. Nobody blamed Tess as she blamed herself.", "더비필드 집안의 얼굴에는, 딸의 안위를 더 욕심내는 부모라면 소녀에게 불사르듯 쏟아 냈을 시뻘건 분노 따위는 조금도 없었다. 테스가 스스로를 탓한 만큼 그녀를 탓하는 이는 아무도 없었다."]], [["When it was discovered that the knacker and tanner would give only a very few shillings for Prince’s carcase because of his decrepitude, Durbeyfield rose to the occasion.", "말 가죽 장수와 무두장이가 프린스의 사체를 두고 늙어 빠진 짐승이라며 고작 몇 실링밖에 쳐 주지 않으리란 게 밝혀지자, 더비필드가 그 자리에서 떨치고 일어섰다."]], [["“No,” said he stoically, “I won’t sell his old body.", "“안 돼.” 그는 의연하게 말했다."], ["When we d’Urbervilles was knights in the land, we didn’t sell our chargers for cat’s meat.", "“녀석의 늙은 몸뚱이를 팔지는 않겠어. 우리 더버빌가가 이 땅의 기사였을 적엔, 군마를 고양이 먹이로 팔아넘기지 않았다고."], ["Let ’em keep their shillings!", "그까짓 실링은 자기네나 가지라고 해!"], ["He’ve served me well in his lifetime, and I won’t part from him now.”", "녀석은 살아생전 나를 잘 섬겼으니, 이제 와서 헤어지진 않겠어.”"]], [["He worked harder the next day in digging a grave for Prince in the garden than he had worked for months to grow a crop for his family.", "이튿날 그는 텃밭에 프린스의 무덤을 파느라, 지난 몇 달간 가족 먹일 작물을 길러 낼 때보다 더 열심히 일했다."], ["When the hole was ready, Durbeyfield and his wife tied a rope round the horse and dragged him up the path towards it, the children following in funeral train.", "구덩이가 다 파이자, 더비필드와 그의 아내는 말에 밧줄을 묶어 그것을 향해 길을 따라 끌어 올렸고, 아이들이 장례 행렬처럼 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Abraham and ’Liza-Lu sobbed, Hope and Modesty discharged their griefs in loud blares which echoed from the walls; and when Prince was tumbled in they gathered round the grave.", "에이브러햄과 라이자루는 흐느꼈고, 호프와 모데스티는 벽에 메아리치는 큰 울음소리로 슬픔을 토해 냈다. 프린스가 굴러 떨어지듯 묻히자 아이들은 무덤가에 둘러섰다."], ["The bread-winner had been taken away from them; what would they do?", "밥벌이가 그들에게서 떠나갔으니, 이제 어찌하나?"]], [["“Is he gone to heaven?”", "“프린스는 천국에 갔어요?”"], ["asked Abraham, between the sobs.", "에이브러햄이 흐느낌 사이로 물었다."]], [["Then Durbeyfield began to shovel in the earth, and the children cried anew.", "이윽고 더비필드가 흙을 삽으로 퍼 넣기 시작했고, 아이들은 다시 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["All except Tess.", "테스만 빼고 모두가."], ["Her face was dry and pale, as though she regarded herself in the light of a murderess.", "그녀의 얼굴은 마르고 창백했으니, 마치 스스로를 살인자로 여기는 듯했다."]], [["# V", "# V"]], [["The haggling business, which had mainly depended on the horse, became disorganized forthwith.", "말에 주로 기대고 있던 흥정 장사가 곧장 엉망이 되었다."], ["Distress, if not penury, loomed in the distance.", "궁핍까지는 아니어도, 곤경이 멀리서 어른거렸다."], ["Durbeyfield was what was locally called a slack-twisted fellow; he had good strength to work at times; but the times could not be relied on to coincide with the hours of requirement; and, having been unaccustomed to the regular toil of the day-labourer, he was not particularly persistent when they did so coincide.", "더비필드는 이 고장 말로 ‘느슨하게 꼬인 사내’라 불릴 만한 위인이었다. 이따금은 일할 기운이 넉넉했으나, 그 ‘이따금’이 일손이 필요한 때와 맞아떨어지리라고는 믿을 수 없었다. 게다가 날품팔이의 규칙적인 노역에 익숙지 않았던 터라, 어쩌다 그 둘이 맞아떨어질 때조차 그리 끈기 있게 버티지 못했다."]], [["Tess, meanwhile, as the one who had dragged her parents into this quagmire, was silently wondering what she could do to help them out of it; and then her mother broached her scheme.", "한편 테스는, 제 부모를 이 수렁에 빠뜨린 장본인으로서, 그들을 거기서 건져 내려면 무엇을 할 수 있을지 말없이 궁리하고 있었다. 그러던 차에 어머니가 제 계획을 꺼냈다."]], [["“We must take the ups wi’ the downs, Tess,”", "“좋을 때가 있으면 나쁠 때도 있는 법이지, 테스.”"], ["said she; “and never could your high blood have been found out at a more called-for moment.", "그녀가 말했다. “네 고귀한 핏줄이 이보다 더 절실한 때에 드러난 적도 없었다."], ["You must try your friends.", "친척을 찾아가 봐야 해."], ["Do ye know that there is a very rich Mrs d’Urberville living on the outskirts o’ The Chase, who must be our relation?", "더 체이스 변두리에 사는, 틀림없이 우리 친척일 아주 부유한 더버빌 마님이 있단다."], ["You must go to her and claim kin, and ask for some help in our trouble.”", "너는 그분께 가서 친척이라 밝히고, 우리 곤경에 좀 도와 달라고 청해야 해.”"]], [["“I shouldn’t care to do that,” says Tess.", "“그러고 싶지 않아요.” 테스가 말했다."], ["“If there is such a lady, ’twould be enough for us if she were friendly—not to expect her to give us help.”", "“그런 마님이 정말 계신다 해도, 우리한테 친절하게 대해 주시는 것만으로도 충분하죠—도움을 주실 거라 기대할 게 아니라요.”"]], [["“You could win her round to do anything, my dear.", "“넌 그분을 잘 구슬려서 뭐든 하게 할 수 있어, 얘야."], ["Besides, perhaps there’s more in it than you know of.", "게다가, 네가 아는 것보다 더한 게 있을지도 모른다."], ["I’ve heard what I’ve heard, good-now.”", "나는 들을 만큼 들었다니까, 정말로.”"]], [["The oppressive sense of the harm she had done led Tess to be more deferential than she might otherwise have been to the maternal wish; but she could not understand why her mother should find such satisfaction in contemplating an enterprise of, to her, such doubtful profit.", "자기가 저지른 해악에 대한 짓누르는 듯한 자책감 탓에, 테스는 평소보다 어머니의 뜻을 더 고분고분 따르게 되었다. 그러나 그녀로서는 이득이 그토록 의심스러운 일을 떠올리며 어머니가 어찌 그런 흡족함을 느끼는지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["Her mother might have made inquiries, and have discovered that this Mrs d’Urberville was a lady of unequalled virtues and charity.", "어머니가 알아보았더라면, 이 더버빌 마님이 견줄 데 없는 미덕과 자비를 갖춘 분임을 알아냈을지도 모른다."], ["But Tess’s pride made the part of poor relation one of particular distaste to her.", "그러나 테스의 자존심은 가난한 친척 노릇을 유난히 꺼리게 만들었다."]], [["“I’d rather try to get work,”", "“차라리 일자리를 구하는 게 낫겠어요.”"], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Durbeyfield, you can settle it,”", "“더비필드, 당신이 결정해요.”"], ["said his wife, turning to where he sat in the background. “If you say she ought to go, she will go.”", "아내가 뒤편에 앉은 그를 돌아보며 말했다. “당신이 가야 한다고 하면, 얘는 갈 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t like my children going and making themselves beholden to strange kin,” murmured he.", "“내 자식들이 가서 낯선 친척한테 신세 지는 건 마뜩잖아.” 그가 중얼거렸다."], ["“I’m the head of the noblest branch o’ the family, and I ought to live up to it.”", "“나는 이 가문에서 가장 고귀한 갈래의 가장이니, 그에 걸맞게 살아야지.”"]], [["His reasons for staying away were worse to Tess than her own objections to going.", "가지 않으려는 그의 이유가, 테스 자신이 가기를 꺼리는 이유보다 그녀에게는 더 못마땅했다."], ["“Well, as I killed the horse, mother,”", "“음, 내가 말을 죽였으니, 어머니.”"], ["she said mournfully, “I suppose I ought to do something.", "그녀가 서글프게 말했다. “뭐든 해야 할 것 같긴 해요."], ["I don’t mind going and seeing her, but you must leave it to me about asking for help. And don’t go thinking about her making a match for me—it is silly.”", "가서 그분을 뵙는 거야 상관없지만, 도움을 청하는 일은 제게 맡겨 주세요. 그리고 그분이 저한테 혼처를 맺어 줄 거라느니 하는 생각은 마세요—그건 바보 같은 짓이에요.”"]], [["“Very well said, Tess!”", "“참 옳은 말이다, 테스!”"], ["observed her father sententiously.", "아버지가 점잖게 한마디 했다."]], [["“Who said I had such a thought?”", "“내가 언제 그런 생각을 했다고?”"], ["asked Joan.", "조앤이 물었다."]], [["“I fancy it is in your mind, mother.", "“어머니 마음속에 있는 것 같아서요."], ["But I’ll go.”", "어쨌든 갈게요.”"]], [["Rising early next day she walked to the hill-town called Shaston, and there took advantage of a van which twice in the week ran from Shaston eastward to Chaseborough, passing near Trantridge, the parish in which the vague and mysterious Mrs d’Urberville had her residence.", "이튿날 일찍 일어난 그녀는 섀스턴이라는 언덕 마을까지 걸어가, 일주일에 두 번 섀스턴에서 동쪽 체이스버러로 가는 합승마차를 이용했다. 그 마차는, 어렴풋하고 알 수 없는 더버빌 마님이 거처를 둔 교구인 트랜트리지 근처를 지나갔다."]], [["Tess Durbeyfield’s route on this memorable morning lay amid the north-eastern undulations of the Vale in which she had been born, and in which her life had unfolded.", "그 잊지 못할 아침, 테스 더비필드의 길은 그녀가 태어나, 그녀의 삶이 펼쳐져 온 골짜기의 북동쪽 굽이굽이 사이로 나 있었다."], ["The Vale of Blackmoor was to her the world, and its inhabitants the races thereof.", "블랙무어 골짜기는 그녀에게 세상이었고, 그 주민들은 그 세상의 종족이었다."], ["From the gates and stiles of Marlott she had looked down its length in the wondering days of infancy, and what had been mystery to her then was not much less than mystery to her now.", "말럿의 문과 층계 디딤대에서 그녀는 어린 시절의 경이로운 나날에 그 골짜기를 내려다보았고, 그때 그녀에게 신비였던 것은 지금도 그에 못지않게 신비였다."], ["She had seen daily from her chamber-window towers, villages, faint white mansions; above all, the town of Shaston standing majestically on its height; its windows shining like lamps in the evening sun.", "그녀는 침실 창에서 날마다 탑들과 마을들과, 어렴풋한 흰 저택들을 보았다. 무엇보다 그 높은 곳에 위풍당당하게 선 섀스턴 마을을 보았으니, 그 창들이 저녁 햇빛 속에서 등불처럼 빛났다."], ["She had hardly ever visited the place, only a small tract even of the Vale and its environs being known to her by close inspection.", "그녀는 그곳에 거의 가 본 적이 없었고, 골짜기와 그 주변 가운데서도 작은 구역만을 가까이 들여다보아 알고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Much less had she been far outside the valley.", "골짜기 바깥 먼 곳은 더더욱 가 본 적이 없었다."], ["Every contour of the surrounding hills was as personal to her as that of her relatives’ faces; but for what lay beyond, her judgment was dependent on the teaching of the village school, where she had held a leading place at the time of her leaving, a year or two before this date.", "둘러싼 언덕들의 윤곽 하나하나는 친척들의 얼굴만큼이나 그녀에게 친근했다. 그러나 그 너머에 있는 것에 대해서는, 그녀의 판단이 마을 학교에서 배운 가르침에 기댈 뿐이었으니, 그녀는 이 일이 있기 한두 해 전 그곳을 떠날 무렵 그 학교에서 으뜸가는 자리를 차지하고 있었다."]], [["In those early days she had been much loved by others of her own sex and age, and had used to be seen about the village as one of three—all nearly of the same year—walking home from school side by side; Tess the middle one—in a pink print pinafore, of a finely reticulated pattern, worn over a stuff frock that had lost its original colour for a nondescript tertiary—marching on upon long stalky legs, in tight stockings which had little ladder-like holes at the knees, torn by kneeling in the roads and banks in search of vegetable and mineral treasures; her then earth-coloured hair hanging like pot-hooks; the arms of the two outside girls resting round the waist of Tess; her arms on the shoulders of the two supporters.", "그 어린 시절 그녀는 또래의 같은 성별 아이들에게 무척 사랑받았고, 셋이 한 무리를 이루어—모두 나이가 거의 같았다—나란히 학교에서 집으로 걸어가는 모습으로 마을에서 종종 눈에 띄곤 했다. 테스가 한가운데였다—고운 그물코 무늬가 든 분홍 무명 앞치마를, 본래 빛깔을 잃고 정체 모를 삼차색이 되어 버린 두툼한 원피스 위에 걸치고서—길고 가느다란 다리로 성큼성큼 걸었으니, 채소와 광물 보물을 찾느라 길과 둑에 무릎 꿇다가 찢어진, 무릎께에 작은 사다리꼴 구멍들이 난 꽉 끼는 스타킹을 신고 있었다. 그때 그녀의 흙빛 머리카락은 솥걸이 갈고리처럼 늘어져 있었다. 양옆 두 소녀의 팔은 테스의 허리를 감았고, 그녀의 팔은 두 동무의 어깨에 얹혀 있었다."]], [["As Tess grew older, and began to see how matters stood, she felt quite a Malthusian towards her mother for thoughtlessly giving her so many little sisters and brothers, when it was such a trouble to nurse and provide for them.", "테스는 자라면서 형편이 어떤지를 알게 되자, 어머니에게 자못 맬서스주의자가 된 심정을 느꼈다. 어린 동생을 그토록 많이, 생각 없이 안겨 준 탓이었으니, 그들을 돌보고 먹여 살리는 일이 이만저만한 골칫거리가 아니었기 때문이다."], ["Her mother’s intelligence was that of a happy child: Joan Durbeyfield was simply an additional one, and that not the eldest, to her own long family of waiters on Providence.", "어머니의 지력은 행복한 아이의 그것이었다. 조앤 더비필드는, 섭리에 기대 사는 그녀 자신의 그 긴 가족 명단에 더해진 또 하나의 아이였을 뿐이며, 그것도 맏이는 아니었다."]], [["However, Tess became humanely beneficent towards the small ones, and to help them as much as possible she used, as soon as she left school, to lend a hand at haymaking or harvesting on neighbouring farms; or, by preference, at milking or butter-making processes, which she had learnt when her father had owned cows; and being deft-fingered it was a kind of work in which she excelled.", "그래도 테스는 어린 동생들에게 인정 많은 자선을 베풀었고, 그들을 되도록 돕고자, 학교를 그만두자마자 이웃 농장에서 건초 만들기나 추수 일을 거들곤 했다. 또는 더 즐겨, 우유 짜기나 버터 만드는 일을 거들었으니, 이는 아버지가 소를 가지고 있을 적에 배운 것이었다. 손재주가 야물었던 터라, 그것은 그녀가 솜씨를 발휘하는 종류의 일이었다."]], [["Every day seemed to throw upon her young shoulders more of the family burdens, and that Tess should be the representative of the Durbeyfields at the d’Urberville mansion came as a thing of course.", "날마다 그녀의 어린 어깨에 집안의 짐이 더 얹히는 듯했고, 테스가 더버빌 저택에서 더비필드 가문을 대표하는 일은 당연한 것처럼 다가왔다."], ["In this instance it must be admitted that the Durbeyfields were putting their fairest side outward.", "이번 경우만큼은, 더비필드 집안이 자기네 가장 그럴듯한 면을 바깥으로 내세우고 있었음을 인정해야겠다."]], [["She alighted from the van at Trantridge Cross, and ascended on foot a hill in the direction of the district known as The Chase, on the borders of which, as she had been informed, Mrs d’Urberville’s seat, The Slopes, would be found.", "그녀는 트랜트리지 크로스에서 합승마차를 내려, 더 체이스라 알려진 지역 쪽으로 난 언덕을 걸어 올랐으니, 들은 바로는 그 경계에 더버빌 마님의 저택 더 슬로프스가 있다고 했다."], ["It was not a manorial home in the ordinary sense, with fields, and pastures, and a grumbling farmer, out of whom the owner had to squeeze an income for himself and his family by hook or by crook.", "그것은 흔히 말하는 영주의 저택, 즉 밭과 목초지와, 주인이 제 식구를 먹여 살릴 수입을 어떻게든 쥐어짜 내야 하는 투덜대는 소작농이 딸린 그런 집이 아니었다."], ["It was more, far more; a country-house built for enjoyment pure and simple, with not an acre of troublesome land attached to it beyond what was required for residential purposes, and for a little fancy farm kept in hand by the owner, and tended by a bailiff.", "그보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더한 것이었으니, 오로지 순수한 향락을 위해 지은 시골 저택으로, 거주에 필요한 만큼과, 주인이 직접 손에 쥐고 마름에게 돌보게 하는 작은 취미 농장에 드는 만큼을 넘어서는, 골칫거리가 될 땅이라곤 한 에이커도 딸려 있지 않았다."]], [["The crimson brick lodge came first in sight, up to its eaves in dense evergreens.", "진홍빛 벽돌 수위실이 먼저 눈에 들어왔으니, 처마까지 빽빽한 상록수에 파묻혀 있었다."], ["Tess thought this was the mansion itself till, passing through the side wicket with some trepidation, and onward to a point at which the drive took a turn, the house proper stood in full view.", "테스는 이것이 저택 본채인 줄 알았으나, 약간 떨리는 마음으로 옆쪽 작은 문을 지나 안으로 나아가, 진입로가 굽어지는 지점에 이르자 본채가 온전히 시야에 들어왔다."], ["It was of recent erection—indeed almost new—and of the same rich red colour that formed such a contrast with the evergreens of the lodge.", "그것은 최근에 세운 것이었으니—실로 거의 새것이었다—수위실의 상록수와 그토록 대비를 이루던 바로 그 짙은 붉은빛이었다."], ["Far behind the corner of the house—which rose like a geranium bloom against the subdued colours around—stretched the soft azure landscape of The Chase—a truly venerable tract of forest land, one of the few remaining woodlands in England of undoubted primaeval date, wherein Druidical mistletoe was still found on aged oaks, and where enormous yew-trees, not planted by the hand of man grew as they had grown when they were pollarded for bows.", "집 모퉁이 너머 멀리로—둘레의 차분한 빛깔에 맞서 제라늄 꽃송이처럼 솟아오른 그 모퉁이 너머로—더 체이스의 부드러운 하늘빛 풍경이 펼쳐져 있었으니, 그것은 참으로 유서 깊은 삼림지로, 의심할 바 없는 태곳적 연원을 지닌, 영국에 몇 남지 않은 숲 가운데 하나였다. 거기서는 늙은 떡갈나무에 아직도 드루이드교의 겨우살이가 발견되었고, 사람 손으로 심지 않은 거대한 주목들이, 활을 만들려고 가지를 쳐 내던 그 옛날 자라던 그대로 자라고 있었다."], ["All this sylvan antiquity, however, though visible from The Slopes, was outside the immediate boundaries of the estate.", "그러나 이 모든 숲의 태고는, 더 슬로프스에서 보이기는 했어도, 영지의 코앞 경계 바깥이었다."]], [["Everything on this snug property was bright, thriving, and well kept; acres of glass-houses stretched down the inclines to the copses at their feet.", "이 아늑한 저택의 모든 것이 환하고, 번창하고, 잘 손질되어 있었다. 몇 에이커에 걸친 온실들이 비탈을 따라 그 기슭의 잡목숲까지 뻗어 내려갔다."], ["Everything looked like money—like the last coin issued from the Mint.", "모든 것이 돈처럼 보였으니—조폐국에서 막 찍어 낸 마지막 동전처럼."], ["The stables, partly screened by Austrian pines and evergreen oaks, and fitted with every late appliance, were as dignified as Chapels-of-Ease.", "오스트리아소나무와 상록 떡갈나무로 일부 가려진 마구간은 온갖 최신 설비를 갖추고서, 부속 예배당만큼이나 위엄 있어 보였다."], ["On the extensive lawn stood an ornamental tent, its door being towards her.", "드넓은 잔디밭 위에는 장식용 천막이 서 있었고, 그 문이 그녀 쪽을 향해 있었다."]], [["Simple Tess Durbeyfield stood at gaze, in a half-alarmed attitude, on the edge of the gravel sweep.", "순박한 테스 더비필드는 자갈 깔린 마당 가장자리에 서서, 반쯤 겁먹은 자세로 멍하니 바라보았다."], ["Her feet had brought her onward to this point before she had quite realized where she was; and now all was contrary to her expectation.", "자기가 어디 있는지 채 깨닫기도 전에 발이 그녀를 이 지점까지 데려왔던 것인데, 이제 모든 것이 그녀의 예상과는 딴판이었다."]], [["“I thought we were an old family; but this is all new!”", "“우리가 오래된 집안인 줄 알았는데, 여긴 죄다 새것이잖아!”"], ["she said, in her artlessness.", "그녀가 천진하게 말했다."], ["She wished that she had not fallen in so readily with her mother’s plans for “claiming kin,” and had endeavoured to gain assistance nearer home.", "그녀는 ‘친척 찾기’를 두고 그토록 선뜻 어머니의 계획에 응한 것이, 그러느니 집 가까이에서 도움을 얻으려 애써 보았어야 했던 것이 못내 후회스러웠다."]], [["The d’Urbervilles—or Stoke-d’Urbervilles, as they at first called themselves—who owned all this, were a somewhat unusual family to find in such an old-fashioned part of the country.", "이 모든 것을 소유한 더버빌가—처음엔 스스로를 스토크더버빌가라 불렀던 이들—는, 이런 고풍스러운 시골 지역에서 마주치기엔 다소 별난 집안이었다."], ["Parson Tringham had spoken truly when he said that our shambling John Durbeyfield was the only really lineal representative of the old d’Urberville family existing in the county, or near it; he might have added, what he knew very well, that the Stoke-d’Urbervilles were no more d’Urbervilles of the true tree then he was himself.", "트링엄 목사가, 우리의 어슬렁대는 존 더비필드가 이 주(州) 안이나 그 근처에 현존하는, 옛 더버빌 가문의 진정 유일한 직계 후손이라 말했을 때 그는 참된 말을 했던 것이다. 그는 거기에, 자기가 익히 알고 있던 한 가지를 덧붙일 수도 있었을 것이다. 곧 스토크더버빌가도 그 자신과 마찬가지로 진짜 혈통의 더버빌가가 아니라는 것을."], ["Yet it must be admitted that this family formed a very good stock whereon to regraft a name which sadly wanted such renovation.", "그래도 이 집안이, 그토록 쇄신이 절실했던 한 가문 이름을 다시 접붙이기에 자못 훌륭한 바탕이 되었음은 인정해야겠다."]], [["When old Mr Simon Stoke, latterly deceased, had made his fortune as an honest merchant (some said money-lender) in the North, he decided to settle as a county man in the South of England, out of hail of his business district; and in doing this he felt the necessity of recommencing with a name that would not too readily identify him with the smart tradesman of the past, and that would be less commonplace than the original bald, stark words.", "근래에 작고한 노(老) 사이먼 스토크 씨가 북부에서 정직한 상인으로(혹자는 돈놀이꾼이라 했다) 재산을 일구었을 때, 그는 자기 사업 구역의 부름이 닿지 않는 잉글랜드 남부에서 향사(鄕士)로 자리 잡기로 마음먹었다. 그러면서 그는, 과거의 약삭빠른 장사치와 자신을 너무 쉽게 결부 짓지 않을 이름, 본래의 밋밋하고 무뚝뚝한 글자보다 덜 흔해 빠진 이름으로 다시 시작할 필요를 느꼈다."], ["Conning for an hour in the British Museum the pages of works devoted to extinct, half-extinct, obscured, and ruined families appertaining to the quarter of England in which he proposed to settle, he considered that _d’Urberville_ looked and sounded as well as any of them: and d’Urberville accordingly was annexed to his own name for himself and his heirs eternally.", "그는 대영박물관에서 한 시간 동안, 자기가 자리 잡으려는 잉글랜드의 그 지역과 관련된 멸절했거나, 반쯤 멸절했거나, 가려졌거나, 몰락한 가문들을 다룬 책장을 들여다본 끝에, ‘더버빌d’Urberville’이라는 이름이 그 어느 것 못지않게 보기에도 듣기에도 좋다고 여겼다. 그리하여 더버빌은 그 자신과 그 영원한 후손을 위해 그의 이름에 덧붙여졌다."], ["Yet he was not an extravagant-minded man in this, and in constructing his family tree on the new basis was duly reasonable in framing his inter-marriages and aristocratic links, never inserting a single title above a rank of strict moderation.", "그래도 그는 이 일에서 분에 넘치게 구는 사람은 아니었으니, 새로운 바탕 위에 가계도를 짜면서도 혼인 관계와 귀족 연줄을 엮는 데 마땅히 분별이 있었고, 엄격히 절제된 지위 위로 작위를 단 하나도 끼워 넣지 않았다."]], [["Of this work of imagination poor Tess and her parents were naturally in ignorance—much to their discomfiture; indeed, the very possibility of such annexations was unknown to them; who supposed that, though to be well-favoured might be the gift of fortune, a family name came by nature.", "이 상상의 작업을 가엾은 테스와 그 부모는 당연히 몰랐다—그들로서는 자못 곤혹스럽게도. 실로 그런 덧붙임이 가능하다는 것조차 그들은 알지 못했으니, 잘 타고난 용모야 운수의 선물일지 몰라도 가문 이름은 천성으로 오는 것이라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Tess still stood hesitating like a bather about to make his plunge, hardly knowing whether to retreat or to persevere, when a figure came forth from the dark triangular door of the tent.", "테스가 물에 뛰어들려는 사람처럼, 물러설지 밀고 나갈지 좀체 가늠하지 못한 채 여전히 머뭇거리며 서 있을 때, 한 형체가 천막의 어두운 삼각형 문에서 걸어 나왔다."], ["It was that of a tall young man, smoking.", "담배를 피우는 키 큰 젊은이였다."]], [["He had an almost swarthy complexion, with full lips, badly moulded, though red and smooth, above which was a well-groomed black moustache with curled points, though his age could not be more than three- or four-and-twenty.", "그는 거의 거무스름한 안색에, 비록 붉고 매끄럽긴 했어도 모양새가 영 어설픈 도톰한 입술을 지녔고, 그 위로는 끝을 말아 올린, 잘 손질된 검은 콧수염이 있었다. 그러나 그의 나이는 스물서넛을 넘지 않았을 것이다."], ["Despite the touches of barbarism in his contours, there was a singular force in the gentleman’s face, and in his bold rolling eye.", "그 윤곽에 야만적인 데가 묻어 있긴 했어도, 그 신사의 얼굴에는, 그리고 대담하게 굴리는 눈에는 남다른 힘이 있었다."]], [["“Well, my Beauty, what can I do for you?”", "“자, 어여쁜 아가씨, 무얼 도와 드릴까요?”"], ["said he, coming forward.", "그가 앞으로 나오며 말했다."], ["And perceiving that she stood quite confounded: “Never mind me. I am Mr d’Urberville.", "그러다 그녀가 잔뜩 어쩔 줄 몰라 서 있는 것을 알아채고는, “나는 신경 쓰지 마요. 나는 더버빌이오."], ["Have you come to see me or my mother?”", "나를 보러 왔소, 아니면 우리 어머니를 보러?”"]], [["This embodiment of a d’Urberville and a namesake differed even more from what Tess had expected than the house and grounds had differed.", "이 더버빌가의 화신이자 같은 이름을 지닌 자는, 집과 정원이 그러했던 것보다도 더, 테스가 예상했던 바와 달랐다."], ["She had dreamed of an aged and dignified face, the sublimation of all the d’Urberville lineaments, furrowed with incarnate memories representing in hieroglyphic the centuries of her family’s and England’s history.", "그녀는 나이 들고 위엄 있는 얼굴을, 모든 더버빌가 용모의 정수를, 그녀 가문과 잉글랜드 역사의 여러 세기를 상형문자로 새겨 담은 듯 화신된 기억들로 깊이 주름진 얼굴을 꿈꾸어 왔다."], ["But she screwed herself up to the work in hand, since she could not get out of it, and answered—", "그러나 그녀는 빠져나갈 수 없는 일이었으므로, 눈앞의 용무에 마음을 다잡고 대답했다—"]], [["“I came to see your mother, sir.”", "“어머님을 뵈러 왔어요, 선생님.”"]], [["“I am afraid you cannot see her—she is an invalid,”", "“유감이지만 만나실 수 없을 거요—병약하신 분이라.”"], ["replied the present representative of the spurious house; for this was Mr Alec, the only son of the lately deceased gentleman.", "가짜 가문의 현 대표자가 대답했다. 이자가 곧 최근 작고한 신사의 외아들 알렉 씨였던 것이다."], ["“Cannot I answer your purpose?", "“내가 그 용무를 대신할 수는 없겠소?"], ["What is the business you wish to see her about?”", "어머니를 뵙고자 하는 용건이 무엇이오?”"]], [["“It isn’t business—it is—I can hardly say what!”", "“용건이라기보다—그게—뭐라 말해야 할지 모르겠어요!”"]], [["“Pleasure?”", "“기쁜 일이오?”"]], [["“Oh no. Why, sir, if I tell you, it will seem—”", "“오, 아뇨. 저, 선생님, 말씀드리면 좀—”"]], [["Tess’s sense of a certain ludicrousness in her errand was now so strong that, notwithstanding her awe of him, and her general discomfort at being here, her rosy lips curved towards a smile, much to the attraction of the swarthy Alexander.", "이제 자기 심부름이 어딘가 우스꽝스럽다는 느낌이 테스에게 어찌나 강했던지, 그에 대한 경외감과 이곳에 있는 데서 오는 전반적인 거북함에도 불구하고, 그녀의 발그레한 입술이 미소를 향해 휘었고, 이는 거무스름한 알렉산더의 마음을 자못 끌었다."]], [["“It is so very foolish,”", "“너무나 바보 같은 일이라.”"], ["she stammered; “I fear I can’t tell you!”", "그녀가 더듬거렸다. “말씀드릴 수 없을 것 같아요!”"]], [["“Never mind; I like foolish things.", "“상관없소. 나는 바보 같은 것을 좋아하니."], ["Try again, my dear,”", "다시 해 봐요, 아가씨.”"], ["said he kindly.", "그가 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Mother asked me to come,”", "“어머니가 가 보라고 하셨어요.”"], ["Tess continued; “and, indeed, I was in the mind to do so myself likewise.", "테스가 말을 이었다. “게다가, 정말이지, 저 스스로도 그러려던 참이었고요."], ["But I did not think it would be like this. I came, sir, to tell you that we are of the same family as you.”", "하지만 이럴 줄은 몰랐어요. 저는 선생님께, 우리가 선생님과 같은 가문이라는 걸 말씀드리러 왔어요.”"]], [["“Ho!", "“호!"], ["Poor relations?”", "가난한 친척이라?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“네.”"]], [["“Stokes?”", "“스토크가?”"]], [["“No; d’Urbervilles.”", "“아뇨, 더버빌가요.”"]], [["“Ay, ay; I mean d’Urbervilles.”", "“그래, 그래. 더버빌가 말이오.”"]], [["“Our names are worn away to Durbeyfield; but we have several proofs that we are d’Urbervilles.", "“우리 이름은 닳고 닳아 더비필드가 됐지만, 우리가 더버빌가라는 증거가 여럿 있어요."], ["Antiquarians hold we are,—and—and we have an old seal, marked with a ramping lion on a shield, and a castle over him.", "고고학자들이 그렇다고 하고요—그리고—그리고 우리한테는 오래된 인장이 있는데, 방패 위에 앞발을 치켜든 사자가, 그 위에 성이 새겨져 있어요."], ["And we have a very old silver spoon, round in the bowl like a little ladle, and marked with the same castle.", "그리고 아주 오래된 은수저도 있는데, 작은 국자처럼 우묵하고, 똑같은 성이 새겨져 있어요."], ["But it is so worn that mother uses it to stir the pea-soup.”", "다만 어찌나 닳았는지 어머니가 완두콩 수프 젓는 데 쓰지만요.”"]], [["“A castle argent is certainly my crest,”", "“은빛 성은 분명 내 문장(紋章)이오.”"], ["said he blandly.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“And my arms a lion rampant.”", "“그리고 내 문장(紋章)은 앞발을 치켜든 사자고.”"]], [["“And so mother said we ought to make ourselves beknown to you—as we’ve lost our horse by a bad accident, and are the oldest branch o’ the family.”", "“그래서 어머니가, 우리가 선생님께 알리는 게 좋겠다고 하셨어요—우리가 안 좋은 사고로 말을 잃었고, 또 이 가문에서 가장 오래된 갈래라서요.”"]], [["“Very kind of your mother, I’m sure. And I, for one, don’t regret her step.”", "“참으로 친절하신 어머님이로군. 그리고 나로서는, 그분이 그러신 걸 후회하지 않소.”"], ["Alec looked at Tess as he spoke, in a way that made her blush a little.", "알렉은 말하면서, 테스가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히게 하는 그런 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“And so, my pretty girl, you’ve come on a friendly visit to us, as relations?”", "“그래서, 어여쁜 아가씨, 친척으로서 우리에게 다정한 방문을 온 거요?”"]], [["“I suppose I have,”", "“그런 셈이에요.”"], ["faltered Tess, looking uncomfortable again.", "테스가, 다시금 불편한 기색으로 우물거렸다."]], [["“Well—there’s no harm in it.", "“음—나쁠 거 없지."], ["Where do you live? What are you?”", "어디 사오? 무얼 하는 사람이오?”"]], [["She gave him brief particulars; and responding to further inquiries told him that she was intending to go back by the same carrier who had brought her.", "그녀는 그에게 간략히 사정을 말해 주었다. 그리고 이어진 물음들에 답하며, 자기를 데려다준 바로 그 마부 편으로 돌아갈 작정이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["“It is a long while before he returns past Trantridge Cross.", "“그자가 트랜트리지 크로스를 다시 지나가려면 한참 멀었소."], ["Supposing we walk round the grounds to pass the time, my pretty Coz?”", "시간이나 보낼 겸 정원이나 한 바퀴 둘러볼까, 어여쁜 사촌 누이?”"]], [["Tess wished to abridge her visit as much as possible; but the young man was pressing, and she consented to accompany him.", "테스는 방문을 되도록 짧게 줄이고 싶었으나, 젊은이가 졸라 대는 통에 그를 따라가기로 했다."], ["He conducted her about the lawns, and flower-beds, and conservatories; and thence to the fruit-garden and greenhouses, where he asked her if she liked strawberries.", "그는 그녀를 잔디밭과 화단과 온실로 이끌었고, 거기서 다시 과수원과 유리 온실로 데려가, 딸기를 좋아하느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said Tess, “when they come.”", "테스가 말했다. “철이 되면요.”"]], [["“They are already here.”", "“벌써 났소.”"], ["D’Urberville began gathering specimens of the fruit for her, handing them back to her as he stooped; and, presently, selecting a specially fine product of the “British Queen” variety, he stood up and held it by the stem to her mouth.", "더버빌은 그녀를 위해 열매 표본을 따기 시작했고, 몸을 굽히며 그것을 그녀에게 건네주었다. 이윽고 ‘브리티시 퀸’ 품종 가운데 유난히 좋은 것을 하나 골라, 똑바로 서서 줄기를 잡고 그녀의 입가에 들이밀었다."]], [["“No—no!” she said quickly, putting her fingers between his hand and her lips.", "“아뇨—아니에요!” 그녀가 재빨리, 그의 손과 제 입술 사이에 손가락을 집어넣으며 말했다."], ["“I would rather take it in my own hand.”", "“제 손으로 받는 게 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“Nonsense!”", "“쓸데없는 소리!”"], ["he insisted; and in a slight distress she parted her lips and took it in.", "그가 우겼다. 그래서 그녀는 약간 곤혹스러운 채로 입술을 벌려 그것을 받아 물었다."]], [["They had spent some time wandering desultorily thus, Tess eating in a half-pleased, half-reluctant state whatever d’Urberville offered her.", "그들은 이런 식으로 한동안 정처 없이 거닐었고, 테스는 더버빌이 건네는 것은 무엇이든 반쯤 즐거운, 반쯤 내키지 않는 상태로 먹었다."], ["When she could consume no more of the strawberries he filled her little basket with them; and then the two passed round to the rose-trees, whence he gathered blossoms and gave her to put in her bosom.", "그녀가 더는 딸기를 먹지 못하게 되자 그는 그녀의 작은 바구니를 딸기로 가득 채웠다. 그러고 나서 둘은 장미나무 쪽으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 그는 꽃을 따 그녀에게 가슴에 꽂으라고 주었다."], ["She obeyed like one in a dream, and when she could affix no more he himself tucked a bud or two into her hat, and heaped her basket with others in the prodigality of his bounty.", "그녀는 꿈속에 있는 사람처럼 따랐고, 더는 꽂을 데가 없게 되자 그가 직접 그녀의 모자에 꽃봉오리 한두 개를 꽂아 주고, 넘쳐흐르는 인심으로 다른 꽃들을 그녀의 바구니에 수북이 쌓았다."], ["At last, looking at his watch, he said, “Now, by the time you have had something to eat, it will be time for you to leave, if you want to catch the carrier to Shaston.", "마침내 그는 시계를 보며 말했다. “자, 뭐 좀 먹고 나면, 섀스턴 가는 마부를 잡으려거든 떠날 시간이 될 거요."], ["Come here, and I’ll see what grub I can find.”", "이리 와요, 무슨 먹을거리가 있나 보고 올 테니.”"]], [["Stoke d’Urberville took her back to the lawn and into the tent, where he left her, soon reappearing with a basket of light luncheon, which he put before her himself.", "스토크더버빌은 그녀를 잔디밭으로, 그리고 천막 안으로 데려가, 거기 두고 갔다가 곧 가벼운 점심거리가 든 바구니를 들고 다시 나타나, 손수 그녀 앞에 놓아 주었다."], ["It was evidently the gentleman’s wish not to be disturbed in this pleasant _tête-à-tête_ by the servantry.", "이 즐거운 단둘만의 자리를 하인들에게 방해받고 싶지 않다는 것이 분명 그 신사의 바람이었다."]], [["“Do you mind my smoking?”", "“담배 좀 피워도 괜찮겠소?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, not at all, sir.”", "“오, 전혀요, 선생님.”"]], [["He watched her pretty and unconscious munching through the skeins of smoke that pervaded the tent, and Tess Durbeyfield did not divine, as she innocently looked down at the roses in her bosom, that there behind the blue narcotic haze was potentially the “tragic mischief” of her drama—one who stood fair to be the blood-red ray in the spectrum of her young life.", "그는 천막 안에 자욱이 퍼진 연기의 타래 사이로, 그녀가 예쁘게, 또 무심하게 우물거리는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그리고 테스 더비필드는, 제 가슴의 장미를 천진하게 내려다보면서도, 저 푸른 마취의 안개 뒤에 자기 극(劇)의 ‘비극적 화근’이 될 자가, 그녀의 젊은 삶의 스펙트럼에서 핏빛 붉은 광선이 되기 십상인 자가 잠재해 있음을 알아채지 못했다."], ["She had an attribute which amounted to a disadvantage just now; and it was this that caused Alec d’Urberville’s eyes to rivet themselves upon her.", "그녀는 바로 지금, 단점이나 다름없는 한 가지 속성을 지니고 있었다. 알렉 더버빌의 눈을 그녀에게 못 박히게 한 것이 바로 그것이었다."], ["It was a luxuriance of aspect, a fulness of growth, which made her appear more of a woman than she really was.", "그것은 외양의 무성함, 성숙의 충만함이었으니, 그것이 그녀를 실제보다 더 여인답게 보이게 했다."], ["She had inherited the feature from her mother without the quality it denoted.", "그녀는 그 이목구비를, 그것이 가리키는 성질 없이, 어머니에게서 물려받았다."], ["It had troubled her mind occasionally, till her companions had said that it was a fault which time would cure.", "그것은 이따금 그녀의 마음을 괴롭혀 왔으나, 동무들이 그건 세월이 고쳐 줄 흠이라고 말해 주곤 했다."]], [["She soon had finished her lunch.", "그녀는 곧 점심을 다 먹었다."], ["“Now I am going home, sir,”", "“이제 집에 갈게요, 선생님.”"], ["she said, rising.", "그녀가 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“And what do they call you?”", "“그래, 이름이 뭐라고 했소?”"], ["he asked, as he accompanied her along the drive till they were out of sight of the house.", "그가, 집이 보이지 않는 데까지 진입로를 따라 그녀를 배웅하며 물었다."]], [["“Tess Durbeyfield, down at Marlott.”", "“테스 더비필드예요, 저 아래 말럿의.”"]], [["“And you say your people have lost their horse?”", "“그래, 당신네 식구가 말을 잃었다고 했지?”"]], [["“I—killed him!” she answered, her eyes filling with tears as she gave particulars of Prince’s death.", "“제가—그 애를 죽였어요!” 그녀가 프린스의 죽음을 자세히 일러 주며, 두 눈에 눈물이 차올라 대답했다."], ["“And I don’t know what to do for father on account of it!”", "“그래서 그 일로 아버지께 무얼 어찌해 드려야 할지 모르겠어요!”"]], [["“I must think if I cannot do something.", "“내가 무어라도 할 수 있을지 생각해 봐야겠소."], ["My mother must find a berth for you.", "우리 어머니가 당신한테 일자리를 마련해 주셔야지."], ["But, Tess, no nonsense about ‘d’Urberville’;—‘Durbeyfield’ only, you know—quite another name.”", "하지만, 테스, ‘더버빌’이니 하는 헛소리는 마요—그냥 ‘더비필드’요, 알겠소—아주 다른 이름이지.”"]], [["“I wish for no better, sir,” said she with something of dignity.", "“저는 그보다 나은 걸 바라지 않아요, 선생님.” 그녀가 다소 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["For a moment—only for a moment—when they were in the turning of the drive, between the tall rhododendrons and conifers, before the lodge became visible, he inclined his face towards her as if—but, no: he thought better of it, and let her go.", "한순간—오직 한순간—그들이 진입로 굽이에서, 키 큰 진달래와 침엽수 사이, 수위실이 보이기 전에 이르렀을 때, 그는 마치 그러려는 듯 그녀에게 얼굴을 기울였다—그러나, 아니다. 그는 마음을 고쳐먹고 그녀를 보내 주었다."]], [["Thus the thing began.", "이렇게 그 일이 시작되었다."], ["Had she perceived this meeting’s import she might have asked why she was doomed to be seen and coveted that day by the wrong man, and not by some other man, the right and desired one in all respects—as nearly as humanity can supply the right and desired; yet to him who amongst her acquaintance might have approximated to this kind, she was but a transient impression, half forgotten.", "이 만남의 의미를 알아챘더라면, 그녀는 어찌하여 자기가 그날 엉뚱한 남자에게 눈에 띄고 탐내어질 운명이었던가를, 모든 면에서—인간이 옳고 바라는 것을 빚어낼 수 있는 한도껏 옳고 바라는—그 다른 남자에게가 아니었던가를 물었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀의 지인들 가운데 그런 부류에 가까웠을 자에게, 그녀는 반쯤 잊힌, 한때 스쳐 간 인상에 지나지 않았다."]], [["In the ill-judged execution of the well-judged plan of things the call seldom produces the comer, the man to love rarely coincides with the hour for loving. Nature does not often say “See!”", "사물의 잘 짜인 계획이 서툴게 실행되는 가운데, 부름이 정작 올 사람을 불러내는 일은 드물고, 사랑할 사람이 사랑할 때와 맞아떨어지는 일도 좀처럼 없다."], ["to her poor creature at a time when seeing can lead to happy doing; or reply “Here!”", "자연은 가엾은 제 피조물에게, 봄이 행복한 행함으로 이어질 수 있는 그때에 “보라!”"], ["to a body’s cry of “Where?”", "하고 일러 주는 일이 흔치 않다."], ["till the hide-and-seek has become an irksome, outworn game.", "또 “어디?” 하는 어떤 몸의 외침에 “여기!”"], ["We may wonder whether at the acme and summit of the human progress these anachronisms will be corrected by a finer intuition, a closer interaction of the social machinery than that which now jolts us round and along; but such completeness is not to be prophesied, or even conceived as possible.", "하고 응답해 주는 일도, 그 숨바꼭질이 지긋지긋하고 닳아 빠진 놀이가 되어 버린 뒤에야 비로소 있을 뿐이다. 우리는 인류 진보의 절정이자 정점에 이르면, 지금 우리를 이리저리 덜컹대며 굴리는 것보다 더 섬세한 직관과, 더 긴밀한 사회 기구의 상호작용에 의해 이 시대착오들이 바로잡힐지 궁금해할 법도 하다."], ["Enough that in the present case, as in millions, it was not the two halves of a perfect whole that confronted each other at the perfect moment; a missing counterpart wandered independently about the earth waiting in crass obtuseness till the late time came.", "그러나 그런 완전함은 예언할 수도, 가능하다고 떠올릴 수조차 없다. 이번 경우에도, 수백만의 경우와 마찬가지로, 완전한 전체의 두 반쪽이 완전한 순간에 마주한 것이 아니었음을 아는 것으로 족하다. 사라진 짝은 제멋대로 땅 위를 헤매며, 어리석을 만큼 둔하게, 그 뒤늦은 때가 올 때까지 기다리고 있었던 것이다."], ["Out of which maladroit delay sprang anxieties, disappointments, shocks, catastrophes, and passing-strange destinies.", "이 서투른 지체에서 불안과, 실망과, 충격과, 파국과, 야릇하기 그지없는 운명들이 솟아났다."]], [["When d’Urberville got back to the tent he sat down astride on a chair, reflecting, with a pleased gleam in his face. Then he broke into a loud laugh.", "더버빌은 천막으로 돌아와, 얼굴에 흡족한 빛을 띤 채 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 의자에 걸터앉았다. 그러더니 그는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Well, I’m damned!", "“이런, 빌어먹을!"], ["What a funny thing! Ha-ha-ha!", "거 참 우스운 일이군! 하하하!"], ["And what a crumby girl!”", "그리고 어찌나 풋풋한 계집인지!”"]], [["# VI", "# VI"]], [["Tess went down the hill to Trantridge Cross, and inattentively waited to take her seat in the van returning from Chaseborough to Shaston.", "테스는 언덕을 내려와 트랜트리지 크로스로 갔고, 체이스버러에서 섀스턴으로 돌아가는 합승마차에 자리를 잡으려 멍하니 기다렸다."], ["She did not know what the other occupants said to her as she entered, though she answered them; and when they had started anew she rode along with an inward and not an outward eye.", "그녀는 마차에 오르며 다른 승객들이 자기에게 무어라 했는지 알지 못했다. 그들에게 대답은 했어도 말이다. 그리고 마차가 다시 출발하자, 그녀는 바깥이 아니라 안을 향한 눈으로 실려 갔다."]], [["One among her fellow-travellers addressed her more pointedly than any had spoken before: “Why, you be quite a posy!", "동승객 가운데 하나가, 그때까지 누가 했던 것보다 더 콕 집어 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. “어머, 댁은 아주 꽃다발이로구먼!"], ["And such roses in early June!”", "그것도 유월 초에 이런 장미라니!”"]], [["Then she became aware of the spectacle she presented to their surprised vision: roses at her breasts; roses in her hat; roses and strawberries in her basket to the brim.", "그제야 그녀는 자기가 그들의 놀란 눈에 어떤 광경으로 비치는지를 알아차렸다. 가슴에는 장미가, 모자에도 장미가, 바구니에는 장미와 딸기가 가장자리까지 가득했다."], ["She blushed, and said confusedly that the flowers had been given to her.", "그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며, 꽃은 누가 준 것이라고 어쩔 줄 모르고 말했다."], ["When the passengers were not looking she stealthily removed the more prominent blooms from her hat and placed them in the basket, where she covered them with her handkerchief.", "승객들이 보지 않을 때, 그녀는 모자에서 더 눈에 띄는 꽃들을 슬그머니 떼어 바구니에 넣고, 그 위를 손수건으로 덮었다."], ["Then she fell to reflecting again, and in looking downwards a thorn of the rose remaining in her breast accidentally pricked her chin.", "그러고는 다시 생각에 잠겼는데, 아래를 내려다보다가 가슴에 남아 있던 장미 가시가 그만 그녀의 턱을 찔렀다."], ["Like all the cottagers in Blackmoor Vale, Tess was steeped in fancies and prefigurative superstitions; she thought this an ill omen—the first she had noticed that day.", "블랙무어 골짜기의 모든 시골 사람들이 그렇듯, 테스도 공상과, 앞일을 점치는 미신에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 그녀는 이것을 흉조로 여겼으니—그날 처음으로 알아챈 흉조였다."]], [["The van travelled only so far as Shaston, and there were several miles of pedestrian descent from that mountain-town into the vale to Marlott.", "합승마차는 섀스턴까지밖에 가지 않았고, 그 산악 마을에서 골짜기로, 말럿까지 걸어 내려가야 할 길이 몇 마일이었다."], ["Her mother had advised her to stay here for the night, at the house of a cottage-woman they knew, if she should feel too tired to come on; and this Tess did, not descending to her home till the following afternoon.", "어머니는, 혹 너무 지쳐 더 못 가겠거든 거기서, 그들이 아는 어느 시골 아낙의 집에서 하룻밤 묵으라고 일러 두었다. 그래서 테스는 그렇게 했고, 이튿날 오후에야 집으로 내려갔다."], ["When she entered the house she perceived in a moment from her mother’s triumphant manner that something had occurred in the interim.", "집에 들어선 순간, 그녀는 어머니의 의기양양한 태도에서 그새 무슨 일이 있었음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Oh yes; I know all about it!", "“오, 그래, 다 알고 있다!"], ["I told ’ee it would be all right, and now ’tis proved!”", "잘될 거라고 했잖니, 이제 증명됐어!”"]], [["“Since I’ve been away? What has?”", "“제가 없는 사이에요? 뭐가요?”"], ["said Tess rather wearily.", "테스가 다소 지친 듯 말했다."]], [["Her mother surveyed the girl up and down with arch approval, and went on banteringly: “So you’ve brought ’em round!”", "어머니는 짓궂게 흐뭇해하며 소녀를 위아래로 훑어보고는, 놀려 대듯 말을 이었다. “그래, 네가 그 사람들 마음을 사로잡았구나!”"]], [["“How do you know, mother?”", "“어떻게 아세요, 어머니?”"]], [["“I’ve had a letter.”", "“편지를 받았다.”"]], [["Tess then remembered that there would have been time for this.", "그제야 테스는 그럴 만한 시간이 있었음을 떠올렸다."]], [["“They say—Mrs d’Urberville says—that she wants you to look after a little fowl-farm which is her hobby.", "“그분들이—더버빌 마님이—말씀하시기로는, 당신 취미인 작은 닭 농장을 네가 돌봐 줬으면 한다는구나."], ["But this is only her artful way of getting ’ee there without raising your hopes.", "하지만 이건 네 기대를 부풀리지 않고 너를 거기로 데려가려는, 그분의 영리한 수법일 뿐이야."], ["She’s going to own ’ee as kin—that’s the meaning o’t.”", "너를 친척으로 인정하시려는 거다—그게 그 뜻이야.” “하지만 전 그분을 못 뵈었어요.”"]], [["“But I didn’t see her.”", "“그래도 누구는 봤겠지?”"]], [["“You zid somebody, I suppose?”", "“아드님을 봤어요.”"]], [["“I saw her son.” “And did he own ’ee?”", "“그래서 그 사람이 너를 친척으로 인정하더냐?”"]], [["“Well—he called me Coz.”", "“음—저더러 사촌 누이라고 불렀어요.”"]], [["“An’ I knew it!", "“그럴 줄 알았다니까!"], ["Jacky—he called her Coz!” cried Joan to her husband.", "재키—그 사람이 얘더러 사촌 누이라고 불렀대!” 조앤이 남편에게 외쳤다."], ["“Well, he spoke to his mother, of course, and she do want ’ee there.”", "“그러니 당연히 자기 어머니한테 말했을 거고, 그분이 너를 거기 두고 싶어 하시는 거야.”"]], [["“But I don’t know that I am apt at tending fowls,”", "“하지만 제가 닭을 잘 칠 수 있을지 모르겠어요.”"], ["said the dubious Tess.", "미심쩍어하는 테스가 말했다."]], [["“Then I don’t know who is apt.", "“그럼 누가 잘 친다는 거냐."], ["You’ve be’n born in the business, and brought up in it.", "너는 그 일을 타고났고, 그 속에서 자랐어."], ["They that be born in a business always know more about it than any ’prentice.", "그 일을 타고난 사람은 어떤 도제보다도 늘 더 잘 아는 법이야."], ["Besides, that’s only just a show of something for you to do, that you midn’t feel beholden.”", "게다가 그건 그저 네가 신세 진다는 느낌이 들지 않게, 뭔가 할 일이 있는 척 보이려는 것일 뿐이라니까.”"]], [["“I don’t altogether think I ought to go,”", "“아무래도 가지 말아야 할 것 같아요.”"], ["said Tess thoughtfully.", "테스가 골똘히 말했다."], ["“Who wrote the letter?", "“누가 편지를 썼대요?"], ["Will you let me look at it?”", "제가 좀 봐도 돼요?”"]], [["“Mrs d’Urberville wrote it.", "“더버빌 마님이 쓰셨지."], ["Here it is.”", "여기 있다.”"]], [["The letter was in the third person, and briefly informed Mrs Durbeyfield that her daughter’s services would be useful to that lady in the management of her poultry-farm, that a comfortable room would be provided for her if she could come, and that the wages would be on a liberal scale if they liked her.", "편지는 삼인칭으로 쓰여 있었고, 더비필드 부인에게, 그 댁 따님이 그 마님의 닭 농장 운영에 보탬이 될 것이며, 와 줄 수 있다면 편안한 방을 마련해 두겠고, 마음에 들면 후한 급료를 주겠노라고 간략히 알리고 있었다."]], [["“Oh—that’s all!”", "“오—그게 다예요!”"], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“You couldn’t expect her to throw her arms round ’ee, an’ to kiss and to coll ’ee all at once.”", "“그분이 한걸음에 너를 끌어안고 입 맞추고 어루만지기를 바랄 순 없잖니.”"]], [["Tess looked out of the window.", "테스는 창밖을 내다보았다."]], [["“I would rather stay here with father and you,”", "“저는 차라리 아버지하고 어머니랑 여기 있고 싶어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But why?”", "“근데 왜?”"]], [["“I’d rather not tell you why, mother; indeed, I don’t quite know why.”", "“왜인지는 말씀드리지 않겠어요, 어머니. 사실, 저도 왜인지 잘 모르겠어요.”"]], [["A week afterwards she came in one evening from an unavailing search for some light occupation in the immediate neighbourhood.", "일주일 뒤 어느 날 저녁, 그녀는 바로 근처에서 가벼운 일자리를 헛되이 찾아 헤매다 돌아왔다."], ["Her idea had been to get together sufficient money during the summer to purchase another horse.", "그녀의 생각은 여름 동안 말 한 마리를 또 살 만한 돈을 그러모으자는 것이었다."], ["Hardly had she crossed the threshold before one of the children danced across the room, saying, “The gentleman’s been here!”", "문턱을 넘기가 무섭게 아이 하나가 방을 가로질러 춤추듯 달려 나오며 말했다. “그 신사분이 왔다 갔어!”"]], [["Her mother hastened to explain, smiles breaking from every inch of her person.", "어머니가 온몸의 구석구석에서 미소를 터뜨리며 서둘러 설명했다."], ["Mrs d’Urberville’s son had called on horseback, having been riding by chance in the direction of Marlott.", "더버빌 마님의 아들이 말을 타고 찾아왔으니, 우연히 말럿 쪽으로 말을 달리던 참이었다는 것이다."], ["He had wished to know, finally, in the name of his mother, if Tess could really come to manage the old lady’s fowl-farm or not; the lad who had hitherto superintended the birds having proved untrustworthy.", "그는 어머니를 대신해, 테스가 그 노부인의 닭 농장을 정말로 맡으러 올 수 있는지 없는지 마침내 확실히 알고 싶어 했으니, 그때까지 새들을 돌보던 사내아이가 미덥지 못한 것으로 드러났던 것이다."], ["“Mr d’Urberville says you must be a good girl if you are at all as you appear; he knows you must be worth your weight in gold.", "“더버빌 씨 말이, 네가 보이는 그대로의 아이라면 분명 착한 처녀일 거고, 너는 분명 제 몸무게만큼의 황금만 한 값어치가 있다지 뭐냐."], ["He is very much interested in ’ee—truth to tell.”", "그분이 너한테 아주 관심이 많으셔—솔직히 말하면.”"]], [["Tess seemed for the moment really pleased to hear that she had won such high opinion from a stranger when, in her own esteem, she had sunk so low.", "테스는, 제 스스로의 평가로는 그토록 낮게 가라앉아 있던 차에, 낯선 이에게 그토록 높은 평을 얻었다는 말을 듣고는 한순간 정말로 기뻐하는 듯했다."]], [["“It is very good of him to think that,”", "“그렇게 생각해 주시다니 참 친절하시네요.”"], ["she murmured; “and if I was quite sure how it would be living there, I would go any-when.”", "그녀가 중얼거렸다. “거기서 사는 게 어떨지 확실히 알 수만 있다면, 언제라도 가겠어요.”"]], [["“He is a mighty handsome man!”", "“그분은 아주 잘생긴 사람이더라!”"]], [["“I don’t think so,”", "“전 그렇게 생각 안 해요.”"], ["said Tess coldly.", "테스가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다."]], [["“Well, there’s your chance, whether or no; and I’m sure he wears a beautiful diamond ring!”", "“글쎄, 어쨌든 너한테 온 기회야. 그리고 그 사람, 분명 아름다운 다이아몬드 반지를 끼고 있더라!”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said little Abraham, brightly, from the window-bench; “and I seed it!", "어린 에이브러햄이 창가 의자에서 환하게 말했다. “나도 봤어!"], ["and it did twinkle when he put his hand up to his mistarshers.", "그 사람이 콧스염한테 손을 올릴 때 그게 반짝거렸어."], ["Mother, why did our grand relation keep on putting his hand up to his mistarshers?”", "엄마, 우리 대단한 친척은 왜 자꾸 콧스염한테 손을 올렸어?”"]], [["“Hark at that child!”", "“저 애 좀 보게!”"], ["cried Mrs Durbeyfield, with parenthetic admiration.", "더비필드 부인이 곁들이듯 감탄하며 외쳤다."]], [["“Perhaps to show his diamond ring,” murmured Sir John, dreamily, from his chair.", "“아마 다이아몬드 반지를 자랑하려고 그랬겠지.” 존 경이 의자에서 꿈결처럼 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I’ll think it over,”", "“생각해 볼게요.”"], ["said Tess, leaving the room.", "테스가 방을 나서며 말했다."]], [["“Well, she’s made a conquest o’ the younger branch of us, straight off,”", "“그래, 얘가 우리 집안의 젊은 갈래를 단번에 정복해 버린 거예요.”"], ["continued the matron to her husband, “and she’s a fool if she don’t follow it up.”", "안주인이 남편에게 말을 이었다. “그러니 이걸 밀어붙이지 않으면 바보지요.”"]], [["“I don’t quite like my children going away from home,”", "“내 자식들이 집을 떠나는 건 영 마뜩잖아.”"], ["said the haggler.", "흥정꾼이 말했다."], ["“As the head of the family, the rest ought to come to me.”", "“이 집안의 가장으로서, 나머지가 나한테 와야 하는 거야.”"]], [["“But do let her go, Jacky,”", "“그래도 얘를 보내요, 재키.”"], ["coaxed his poor witless wife.", "가엾은 어리석은 아내가 구슬렸다."], ["“He’s struck wi’ her—you can see that.", "“그 사람이 얘한테 푹 빠졌다니까요—척 보면 알아요."], ["He called her Coz!", "얘더러 사촌 누이라고 불렀잖아요!"], ["He’ll marry her, most likely, and make a lady of her; and then she’ll be what her forefathers was.”", "십중팔구 얘랑 결혼해서 귀부인으로 만들어 줄 거예요. 그러면 얘는 제 조상들이 그랬던 그 모습이 되는 거고요.”"]], [["John Durbeyfield had more conceit than energy or health, and this supposition was pleasant to him.", "존 더비필드는 기력이나 건강보다 자만이 더 많은 사람이었던 터라, 이런 추측이 그에게는 흐뭇했다."]], [["“Well, perhaps that’s what young Mr d’Urberville means,”", "“음, 어쩌면 젊은 더버빌 씨가 뜻하는 게 그것일지도 모르지.”"], ["he admitted; “and sure enough he mid have serious thoughts about improving his blood by linking on to the old line.", "그가 인정했다. “그리고 정말이지, 옛 혈통에 줄을 대 제 핏줄을 끌어올리겠다는 진지한 생각이 그자한테 있을 법도 해."], ["Tess, the little rogue!", "테스, 그 깜찍한 것!"], ["And have she really paid ’em a visit to such an end as this?”", "정말로 이런 결말을 보자고 그자들을 찾아갔단 말이야?”"]], [["Meanwhile Tess was walking thoughtfully among the gooseberry-bushes in the garden, and over Prince’s grave.", "그동안 테스는 텃밭의 구스베리 덤불 사이를, 그리고 프린스의 무덤 위를 골똘히 거닐고 있었다."], ["When she came in her mother pursued her advantage.", "그녀가 들어오자 어머니가 기세를 몰아붙였다."]], [["“Well, what be you going to do?” she asked.", "“그래, 어쩔 작정이냐?” 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I wish I had seen Mrs d’Urberville,”", "“제가 더버빌 마님을 뵈었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.”"], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“I think you mid as well settle it.", "“이쯤 해서 결정하는 게 낫겠다."], ["Then you’ll see her soon enough.”", "그러면 곧 그분을 뵙게 될 테니.”"]], [["Her father coughed in his chair.", "아버지가 의자에서 기침을 했다."]], [["“I don’t know what to say!”", "“뭐라고 해야 할지 모르겠어요!”"], ["answered the girl restlessly.", "소녀가 안절부절못하며 대답했다."], ["“It is for you to decide.", "“결정은 어머니가 하셔야죠."], ["I killed the old horse, and I suppose I ought to do something to get ye a new one.", "제가 늙은 말을 죽였으니, 새 말을 구해 드리려면 뭐라도 해야 한다고는 생각해요."], ["But—but—I don’t quite like Mr d’Urberville being there!”", "하지만—하지만—더버빌 씨가 거기 있는 게 영 마뜩잖아요!”"]], [["The children, who had made use of this idea of Tess being taken up by their wealthy kinsfolk (which they imagined the other family to be) as a species of dolorifuge after the death of the horse, began to cry at Tess’s reluctance, and teased and reproached her for hesitating.", "아이들은, 말이 죽은 뒤로 테스가 부유한 친척(그들은 저쪽 집안이 그러하다고 여겼다)에게 거두어지리라는 이 생각을 일종의 슬픔의 진통제로 삼아 왔던 터라, 테스가 내키지 않아 하자 울음을 터뜨리며, 그녀가 망설인다고 보채고 나무랐다."]], [["“Tess won’t go-o-o and be made a la-a-dy of!—no, she says she wo-o-on’t!”", "“테스 누나는 안 가아아아서 귀부우우인이 안 될 거래!—싫대, 안 가아아안대!”"], ["they wailed, with square mouths.", "그들이 입을 네모지게 벌리고 통곡했다."], ["“And we shan’t have a nice new horse, and lots o’ golden money to buy fairlings!", "“그럼 우리는 멋진 새 말도 없고, 장난감 살 황금 돈도 잔뜩 없잖아!"], ["And Tess won’t look pretty in her best cloze no mo-o-ore!”", "그리고 테스 누나는 제일 좋은 옷 입고 예쁘게 안 보일 거야 이제 더는!”"]], [["Her mother chimed in to the same tune: a certain way she had of making her labours in the house seem heavier than they were by prolonging them indefinitely, also weighed in the argument.", "어머니도 같은 가락으로 거들었다. 게다가, 집안일을 한없이 질질 끌어 실제보다 더 고되게 보이게 하는 그녀 특유의 방식도 이 논쟁에 무게를 더했다."], ["Her father alone preserved an attitude of neutrality.", "아버지만이 홀로 중립의 태도를 지켰다."]], [["“I will go,”", "“갈게요.”"], ["said Tess at last.", "마침내 테스가 말했다."]], [["Her mother could not repress her consciousness of the nuptial vision conjured up by the girl’s consent.", "어머니는 딸의 승낙이 불러일으킨 혼례의 환상에 대한 자각을 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["“That’s right!", "“그래야지!"], ["For such a pretty maid as ’tis, this is a fine chance!”", "이렇게 예쁜 처녀한테, 이건 좋은 기회다!”"]], [["Tess smiled crossly.", "테스가 못마땅한 듯 웃었다."]], [["“I hope it is a chance for earning money.", "“돈 버는 기회면 좋겠어요."], ["It is no other kind of chance.", "그 외에 다른 기회는 아니고요."], ["You had better say nothing of that silly sort about parish.”", "혼처니 하는 그 바보 같은 소리는 입 밖에 내지 않는 게 좋겠어요.”"]], [["Mrs Durbeyfield did not promise.", "더비필드 부인은 약속하지 않았다."], ["She was not quite sure that she did not feel proud enough, after the visitor’s remarks, to say a good deal.", "그 방문객의 말을 듣고 난 뒤로, 자기가 자못 자랑스러워 할 말이 많지 않다고는 도저히 장담할 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Thus it was arranged; and the young girl wrote, agreeing to be ready to set out on any day on which she might be required.", "이렇게 일은 정해졌다. 그리고 젊은 처녀는, 부르는 날이 언제든 떠날 채비를 하겠노라 동의하는 편지를 썼다."], ["She was duly informed that Mrs d’Urberville was glad of her decision, and that a spring-cart should be sent to meet her and her luggage at the top of the Vale on the day after the morrow, when she must hold herself prepared to start.", "그녀는, 더버빌 마님이 그녀의 결정을 기뻐하며, 모레 골짜기 꼭대기에서 그녀와 짐을 맞으러 용수철 달린 짐마차를 보낼 테니, 그날 출발할 준비를 해 두라는 통지를 정식으로 받았다."], ["Mrs d’Urberville’s handwriting seemed rather masculine.", "더버빌 마님의 필체는 다소 남자 같아 보였다."]], [["“A cart?” murmured Joan Durbeyfield doubtingly.", "“짐마차라고?” 조앤 더비필드가 미심쩍어하며 중얼거렸다."], ["“It might have been a carriage for her own kin!”", "“제 친척한테라면 사륜마차쯤 보냈을 법한데!”"]], [["Having at last taken her course Tess was less restless and abstracted, going about her business with some self-assurance in the thought of acquiring another horse for her father by an occupation which would not be onerous.", "마침내 길을 정하고 나자 테스는 한결 덜 안절부절못하고, 덜 멍해졌다. 그리 고되지 않을 일거리로 아버지께 말 한 마리를 마련해 드린다는 생각에 얼마간 자신감을 품고 제 일을 해 나갔다."], ["She had hoped to be a teacher at the school, but the fates seemed to decide otherwise.", "그녀는 학교 교사가 되기를 바랐으나, 운명은 달리 정한 듯했다."], ["Being mentally older than her mother she did not regard Mrs Durbeyfield’s matrimonial hopes for her in a serious aspect for a moment.", "마음으로는 어머니보다 어른스러웠던 터라, 자기를 향한 더비필드 부인의 혼인에 대한 희망 따위를 그녀는 한순간도 진지하게 여기지 않았다."], ["The light-minded woman had been discovering good matches for her daughter almost from the year of her birth.", "그 경박한 여인은 딸이 태어난 그해부터 줄곧 딸에게 좋은 혼처를 찾아 주고 있었던 것이다."]], [["# VII", "# VII"]], [["On the morning appointed for her departure Tess was awake before dawn—at the marginal minute of the dark when the grove is still mute, save for one prophetic bird who sings with a clear-voiced conviction that he at least knows the correct time of day, the rest preserving silence as if equally convinced that he is mistaken.", "떠나기로 정해진 날 아침, 테스는 동트기 전에—어둠의 가장자리 그 한 찰나, 숲이 아직 고요한 가운데, 제 딴에는 적어도 자기가 하루 중 정확한 시각을 안다고 또렷한 목소리로 확신하며 노래하는 단 한 마리 예언자 같은 새만이 깨어 있고, 나머지는 그가 틀렸다고 똑같이 확신하기라도 하듯 침묵을 지키던 그때—깨어 있었다."], ["She remained upstairs packing till breakfast-time, and then came down in her ordinary week-day clothes, her Sunday apparel being carefully folded in her box.", "그녀는 아침 식사 때까지 위층에서 짐을 꾸리다가, 평소의 평일 옷차림으로 내려왔으니, 나들이옷은 상자 안에 곱게 개어 넣어 두었다."]], [["Her mother expostulated.", "어머니가 나무랐다."], ["“You will never set out to see your folks without dressing up more the dand than that?”", "“네 친척을 만나러 가면서, 그것보다 좀 더 멋쟁이로 차려입지 않고 나설 셈이냐?”"]], [["“But I am going to work!”", "“하지만 전 일하러 가는 거예요!”"], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“Well, yes,” said Mrs Durbeyfield; and in a private tone, “at first there mid be a little pretence o’t....", "“그래, 그렇긴 하다.” 더비필드 부인이 말하고는, 은밀한 어조로 “처음엔 좀 그런 척이 있을 수도 있지…."], ["But I think it will be wiser of ’ee to put your best side outward,” she added.", "하지만 네 가장 좋은 면을 바깥으로 내보이는 게 더 현명할 거다.” 하고 덧붙였다."]], [["“Very well; I suppose you know best,”", "“알았어요. 어머니가 제일 잘 아시겠죠.”"], ["replied Tess with calm abandonment.", "테스가 차분히 내맡기듯 대답했다."]], [["And to please her parent the girl put herself quite in Joan’s hands, saying serenely—“Do what you like with me, mother.”", "그리고 어버이를 기쁘게 해 드리려고 소녀는 제 몸을 온전히 조앤의 손에 맡기며 평온하게 말했다—“마음대로 하세요, 어머니.”"]], [["Mrs Durbeyfield was only too delighted at this tractability.", "더비필드 부인은 이 순순함에 더없이 기뻤다."], ["First she fetched a great basin, and washed Tess’s hair with such thoroughness that when dried and brushed it looked twice as much as at other times.", "먼저 그녀는 커다란 대야를 가져와 테스의 머리를 어찌나 정성껏 감겼던지, 말려서 빗질하고 나니 다른 때의 갑절은 많아 보였다."], ["She tied it with a broader pink ribbon than usual.", "그녀는 평소보다 더 넓은 분홍 리본으로 그것을 묶었다."], ["Then she put upon her the white frock that Tess had worn at the club-walking, the airy fulness of which, supplementing her enlarged _coiffure_, imparted to her developing figure an amplitude which belied her age, and might cause her to be estimated as a woman when she was not much more than a child.", "그러고는, 테스가 클럽 행진 때 입었던 흰 원피스를 그녀에게 입혔으니, 그 산뜻하게 풍성한 자락이 부풀린 머리 모양과 어우러져, 한창 피어나는 그녀의 몸매에 나이를 무색케 하는 풍만함을 더해 주었고, 그녀가 아직 아이나 다름없는데도 여인으로 여겨지게 할 만했다."]], [["“I declare there’s a hole in my stocking-heel!” said Tess.", "“어머, 내 스타킹 뒤꿈치에 구멍이 났네!” 테스가 말했다."]], [["“Never mind holes in your stockings—they don’t speak!", "“스타킹 구멍 따위는 신경 쓰지 마라—그건 말을 안 하니까!"], ["When I was a maid, so long as I had a pretty bonnet the devil might ha’ found me in heels.”", "내가 처녀였을 적엔, 예쁜 보닛만 있으면 뒤꿈치쯤 악마가 보든 말든 상관없었다.”"]], [["Her mother’s pride in the girl’s appearance led her to step back, like a painter from his easel, and survey her work as a whole.", "소녀의 모습에 대한 어머니의 자랑스러움은, 화가가 화폭에서 물러서듯 그녀를 한 걸음 물러서서 제 작품을 전체로 살펴보게 했다."]], [["“You must zee yourself!”", "“네 모습을 봐야 한다!”"], ["she cried. “It is much better than you was t’other day.”", "그녀가 외쳤다. “요전 날보다 훨씬 낫구나.”"]], [["As the looking-glass was only large enough to reflect a very small portion of Tess’s person at one time, Mrs Durbeyfield hung a black cloak outside the casement, and so made a large reflector of the panes, as it is the wont of bedecking cottagers to do.", "거울이 한 번에 테스 몸의 아주 작은 부분만 비출 만큼 작았던 터라, 더비필드 부인은 검은 망토를 여닫이창 바깥에 걸어, 차려입기 좋아하는 시골 사람들이 으레 하듯 유리창을 커다란 반사경으로 만들었다."], ["After this she went downstairs to her husband, who was sitting in the lower room.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 아래층의 남편에게로 내려갔으니, 그는 아래 방에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“I’ll tell ’ee what ’tis, Durbeyfield,”", "“내 당신한테 말해 두는데, 더비필드.”"], ["said she exultingly; “he’ll never have the heart not to love her.", "그녀가 의기양양하게 말했다. “그 사람이 얘를 사랑하지 않고는 못 배길 거예요."], ["But whatever you do, don’t zay too much to Tess of his fancy for her, and this chance she has got.", "하지만 무슨 일이 있어도, 그 사람이 얘를 마음에 둔 것이며 얘가 잡은 이 기회에 대해 테스한테 너무 많이 말하진 말아요."], ["She is such an odd maid that it mid zet her against him, or against going there, even now.", "얘는 워낙 별난 처녀라, 그 사람한테, 아니면 거기 가는 것 자체에, 지금이라도 마음이 틀어질 수 있으니까."], ["If all goes well, I shall certainly be for making some return to pa’son at Stagfoot Lane for telling us—dear, good man!”", "다 잘되면, 우리한테 알려 준 스태그풋 레인의 목사님께 꼭 뭔가 보답을 해야겠어요—참 친절하고 좋은 분이지!”"]], [["However, as the moment for the girl’s setting out drew nigh, when the first excitement of the dressing had passed off, a slight misgiving found place in Joan Durbeyfield’s mind.", "그러나 소녀가 떠날 순간이 다가오면서, 차려입을 때의 첫 흥분이 가시자 조앤 더비필드의 마음에는 작은 불안이 자리 잡았다."], ["It prompted the matron to say that she would walk a little way—as far as to the point where the acclivity from the valley began its first steep ascent to the outer world.", "그것은 안주인으로 하여금, 조금 거기까지—골짜기에서 바깥세상으로 처음 가파르게 솟아오르는 오르막이 시작되는 지점까지—따라 걷겠노라 말하게 했다."], ["At the top Tess was going to be met with the spring-cart sent by the Stoke-d’Urbervilles, and her box had already been wheeled ahead towards this summit by a lad with trucks, to be in readiness.", "그 꼭대기에서 테스는 스토크더버빌가에서 보낸 용수철 짐마차를 만나기로 되어 있었고, 그녀의 상자는 손수레꾼 소년 편으로 이미 그 정상을 향해 미리 실려 가 채비를 갖추어 두었다."]], [["Seeing their mother put on her bonnet, the younger children clamoured to go with her.", "어머니가 보닛을 쓰는 것을 보고, 어린아이들이 따라가겠다고 떼를 썼다."]], [["“I do want to walk a little-ways wi’ Sissy, now she’s going to marry our gentleman-cousin, and wear fine cloze!”", "“언니가 우리 신사 사촌이랑 결혼해서 멋진 옷 입으러 가는데, 나도 언니랑 조금만 같이 걸을래!”"]], [["“Now,”", "“자.”"], ["said Tess, flushing and turning quickly, “I’ll hear no more o’ that!", "테스가 얼굴을 붉히며 홱 돌아서서 말했다. “그런 소리는 더 듣지 않겠어!"], ["Mother, how could you ever put such stuff into their heads?”", "어머니, 어쩌자고 애들 머릿속에 그런 헛것을 집어넣으셨어요?”"]], [["“Going to work, my dears, for our rich relation, and help get enough money for a new horse,”", "“우리 부유한 친척한테 일하러 가는 거란다, 얘들아. 그래서 새 말 살 돈을 마련하는 걸 돕는 거고.”"], ["said Mrs Durbeyfield pacifically.", "더비필드 부인이 달래듯 말했다."]], [["“Goodbye, father,” said Tess, with a lumpy throat.", "“안녕히 계세요, 아버지.” 테스가 목이 메어 말했다."]], [["“Goodbye, my maid,”", "“잘 가거라, 우리 딸.”"], ["said Sir John, raising his head from his breast as he suspended his nap, induced by a slight excess this morning in honour of the occasion.", "존 경이 가슴에서 머리를 들며 말했으니, 그는 오늘 아침 이 일을 기려 약간 과음한 탓에 들었던 선잠을 멈춘 참이었다."], ["“Well, I hope my young friend will like such a comely sample of his own blood.", "“그래, 내 젊은 친구가 제 핏줄의 이렇게 어여쁜 본보기를 마음에 들어 했으면 좋겠구나."], ["And tell’n, Tess, that being sunk, quite, from our former grandeur, I’ll sell him the title—yes, sell it—and at no onreasonable figure.”", "그리고 테스, 그자한테 전해라. 우리가 옛 영화에서 깡그리 가라앉았으니, 내가 그 작위를 그자한테 팔겠다고—그래, 팔겠다고—그것도 터무니없는 값은 아닐 거라고.”"]], [["“Not for less than a thousand pound!”", "“천 파운드 밑으로는 안 돼요!”"], ["cried Lady Durbeyfield.", "더비필드 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["“Tell’n—I’ll take a thousand pound.", "“그자한테 전해라—천 파운드 받겠다고."], ["Well, I’ll take less, when I come to think o’t.", "음, 곰곰 생각해 보면, 그보다 덜 받아도 되지."], ["He’ll adorn it better than a poor lammicken feller like myself can.", "그자가 나 같은 어수룩한 위인보다야 그걸 더 빛나게 할 테니."], ["Tell’n he shall hae it for a hundred.", "그자한테 백 파운드면 가지라고 전해라."], ["But I won’t stand upon trifles—tell’n he shall hae it for fifty—for twenty pound!", "하지만 자잘한 데 매달리진 않겠다—그자한테 오십 파운드면 가지라고 해—이십 파운드면!"], ["Yes, twenty pound—that’s the lowest.", "그래, 이십 파운드—그게 제일 낮은 값이다."], ["Dammy, family honour is family honour, and I won’t take a penny less!”", "빌어먹을, 가문의 명예는 가문의 명예니, 한 푼도 덜 받진 않겠다!”"]], [["Tess’s eyes were too full and her voice too choked to utter the sentiments that were in her.", "테스의 두 눈은 너무 그렁그렁했고 목소리는 너무 메어 있어, 가슴속에 있는 심정을 차마 입에 담을 수 없었다."], ["She turned quickly, and went out.", "그녀는 홱 돌아서서 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["So the girls and their mother all walked together, a child on each side of Tess, holding her hand and looking at her meditatively from time to time, as at one who was about to do great things; her mother just behind with the smallest; the group forming a picture of honest beauty flanked by innocence, and backed by simple-souled vanity.", "그리하여 소녀들과 어머니가 다 함께 걸었다. 테스의 양옆에 아이 하나씩이 그녀의 손을 잡고서, 마치 큰일을 하러 가는 이를 보듯 이따금 골똘한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 어머니는 가장 어린것을 데리고 바로 뒤를 따랐다."], ["They followed the way till they reached the beginning of the ascent, on the crest of which the vehicle from Trantridge was to receive her, this limit having been fixed to save the horse the labour of the last slope. Far away behind the first hills the cliff-like dwellings of Shaston broke the line of the ridge.", "그 무리는, 순진함이 양옆을 지키고 순박한 허영심이 뒤를 받친, 정직한 아름다움의 한 폭 그림을 이루었다. 그들은 길을 따라가다 오르막이 시작되는 지점에 이르렀는데, 그 마루에서 트랜트리지의 마차가 그녀를 태우기로 되어 있었으니, 마지막 비탈을 오르는 수고를 말에게 덜어 주려고 그 한계가 정해진 것이었다."], ["Nobody was visible in the elevated road which skirted the ascent save the lad whom they had sent on before them, sitting on the handle of the barrow that contained all Tess’s worldly possessions.", "첫 언덕들 너머 저 멀리, 섀스턴의 절벽 같은 집들이 능선을 끊고 있었다. 오르막을 끼고 도는 높다란 길에는, 그들이 앞서 보낸 소년 말고는 아무도 보이지 않았으니, 그는 테스의 온 세간이 담긴 손수레 손잡이에 걸터앉아 있었다."]], [["“Bide here a bit, and the cart will soon come, no doubt,”", "“여기서 조금만 기다리면 곧 마차가 올 거다, 틀림없이.”"], ["said Mrs Durbeyfield.", "더비필드 부인이 말했다."], ["“Yes, I see it yonder!”", "“그래, 저기 보이는구나!”"]], [["It had come—appearing suddenly from behind the forehead of the nearest upland, and stopping beside the boy with the barrow.", "마차가 왔다—가장 가까운 고지의 이마 뒤에서 불쑥 나타나, 손수레를 둔 소년 곁에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Her mother and the children thereupon decided to go no farther, and bidding them a hasty goodbye, Tess bent her steps up the hill.", "그러자 어머니와 아이들은 더는 가지 않기로 했고, 부랴부랴 작별을 고하니, 테스는 언덕 위로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["They saw her white shape draw near to the spring-cart, on which her box was already placed.", "그들은 그녀의 흰 형체가, 이미 상자가 실려 있는 용수철 짐마차로 다가가는 것을 보았다."], ["But before she had quite reached it another vehicle shot out from a clump of trees on the summit, came round the bend of the road there, passed the luggage-cart, and halted beside Tess, who looked up as if in great surprise.", "그러나 그녀가 그것에 채 닿기도 전에, 또 다른 마차 한 대가 정상의 나무 덤불에서 튀어나와, 거기 길의 굽이를 돌아 짐마차를 지나치고는, 테스 곁에 멈춰 섰다. 그녀는 몹시 놀란 듯 고개를 들었다."]], [["Her mother perceived, for the first time, that the second vehicle was not a humble conveyance like the first, but a spick-and-span gig or dog-cart, highly varnished and equipped.", "어머니는, 그 두 번째 마차가 첫 번째 같은 초라한 탈것이 아니라, 번쩍번쩍 옻칠을 하고 잘 꾸민, 말끔한 이륜마차임을 처음으로 알아보았다."], ["The driver was a young man of three- or four-and-twenty, with a cigar between his teeth; wearing a dandy cap, drab jacket, breeches of the same hue, white neckcloth, stick-up collar, and brown driving-gloves—in short, he was the handsome, horsey young buck who had visited Joan a week or two before to get her answer about Tess.", "마부는 스물서넛쯤 된 젊은이로, 이 사이에 시가를 물고 있었으니, 멋쟁이 모자에, 황갈색 윗도리며, 같은 빛깔의 반바지, 흰 목수건, 빳빳이 세운 옷깃, 그리고 갈색 운전용 장갑을 끼고 있었다—한마디로, 한두 주 전에 테스에 대한 대답을 들으러 조앤을 찾아왔던, 그 잘생기고 말 잘 타는 젊은 한량이었다."]], [["Mrs Durbeyfield clapped her hands like a child. Then she looked down, then stared again.", "더비필드 부인은 아이처럼 손뼉을 쳤다. 그러더니 아래를 내려다보았다가, 다시 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["Could she be deceived as to the meaning of this?", "이것이 무슨 뜻인지 내가 잘못 본 걸까?"]], [["“Is dat the gentleman-kinsman who’ll make Sissy a lady?”", "“언니를 귀부인으로 만들어 줄 신사 친척이 저 사람이야?”"], ["asked the youngest child.", "가장 어린아이가 물었다."]], [["Meanwhile the muslined form of Tess could be seen standing still, undecided, beside this turn-out, whose owner was talking to her.", "그동안 모슬린을 두른 테스의 형체가, 이 마차 곁에 가만히, 어쩔 줄 모르고 서 있는 것이 보였으니, 그 마차 주인이 그녀에게 말을 걸고 있었다."], ["Her seeming indecision was, in fact, more than indecision: it was misgiving.", "그녀의 우유부단해 보이는 태도는, 실은 우유부단함을 넘어선 것이었다. 그것은 불안이었다."], ["She would have preferred the humble cart.", "그녀는 초라한 짐마차 쪽이 차라리 나았을 것이다."], ["The young man dismounted, and appeared to urge her to ascend.", "젊은이가 마차에서 내려, 그녀에게 어서 오르라고 재촉하는 듯했다."], ["She turned her face down the hill to her relatives, and regarded the little group.", "그녀는 언덕 아래 친척들 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려, 그 작은 무리를 바라보았다."], ["Something seemed to quicken her to a determination; possibly the thought that she had killed Prince.", "무언가가 그녀를 결심으로 다그치는 듯했으니, 어쩌면 자기가 프린스를 죽였다는 생각이었을 것이다."], ["She suddenly stepped up; he mounted beside her, and immediately whipped on the horse.", "그녀는 불쑥 올라탔고, 그가 그녀 곁에 오르더니, 곧장 말에 채찍질을 했다."], ["In a moment they had passed the slow cart with the box, and disappeared behind the shoulder of the hill.", "순식간에 그들은 상자를 실은 느린 짐마차를 지나쳐, 언덕의 어깨 뒤로 사라졌다."]], [["Directly Tess was out of sight, and the interest of the matter as a drama was at an end, the little ones’ eyes filled with tears.", "테스가 시야에서 사라지고, 한 편의 극으로서 이 일의 흥미가 끝나자마자, 어린것들의 눈에 눈물이 가득 고였다."], ["The youngest child said, “I wish poor, poor Tess wasn’t gone away to be a lady!”", "가장 어린아이가 “가엾은, 불쌍한 테스 누나가 귀부인 되러 떠나지 않았으면 좋겠어!”"], ["and, lowering the corners of his lips, burst out crying.", "하고 말하더니, 입꼬리를 축 늘어뜨리며 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The new point of view was infectious, and the next child did likewise, and then the next, till the whole three of them wailed loud.", "그 새로운 관점은 옮아 가는 것이어서, 그다음 아이도 똑같이 했고, 또 그다음 아이도 그러더니, 마침내 셋 모두가 큰 소리로 울부짖었다."]], [["There were tears also in Joan Durbeyfield’s eyes as she turned to go home.", "조앤 더비필드의 눈에도 눈물이 고였으니, 집으로 돌아가려 돌아설 때였다."], ["But by the time she had got back to the village she was passively trusting to the favour of accident.", "그러나 마을로 돌아왔을 즈음엔, 그녀는 우연의 호의에 수동적으로 기대고 있었다."], ["However, in bed that night she sighed, and her husband asked her what was the matter.", "그래도 그날 밤 잠자리에서 그녀가 한숨을 쉬자, 남편이 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."]], [["“Oh, I don’t know exactly,”", "“오, 나도 잘은 모르겠어요.”"], ["she said. “I was thinking that perhaps it would ha’ been better if Tess had not gone.”", "그녀가 말했다. “어쩌면 테스를 보내지 않는 편이 나았을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서요.”"]], [["“Oughtn’t ye to have thought of that before?”", "“그런 건 진작 생각했어야 하는 거 아냐?”"]], [["“Well, ’tis a chance for the maid—Still, if ’twere the doing again, I wouldn’t let her go till I had found out whether the gentleman is really a good-hearted young man and choice over her as his kinswoman.”", "“음, 얘한테는 기회잖아요—그래도, 다시 한다면, 그 신사가 정말로 마음씨 고운 젊은이고 얘를 친척으로 귀히 여기는지 알아내기 전엔 보내지 않았을 거예요.”"]], [["“Yes, you ought, perhaps, to ha’ done that,” snored Sir John.", "“그래, 당신, 아마 그랬어야 했겠지.” 존 경이 코를 골며 말했다."]], [["Joan Durbeyfield always managed to find consolation somewhere: “Well, as one of the genuine stock, she ought to make her way with ’en, if she plays her trump card aright.", "조앤 더비필드는 늘 어디선가 위안을 찾아내곤 했다. “음, 진짜 혈통의 한 사람이니, 제 으뜸패만 잘 쓴다면 그 사람을 휘어잡을 만하지."], ["And if he don’t marry her afore he will after.", "그리고 그 사람이 전에 결혼 안 해 주면 후에라도 해 줄 거야."], ["For that he’s all afire wi’ love for her any eye can see.”", "그자가 얘한테 사랑으로 온통 불타오르는 건 누구 눈에나 보이니까.”"]], [["“What’s her trump card?", "“얘 으뜸패가 뭔데?"], ["Her d’Urberville blood, you mean?”", "더버빌 핏줄 말이야?”"]], [["“No, stupid; her face—as ’twas mine.”", "“아니, 멍청한 양반아. 얘 얼굴—내 얼굴이 그랬듯이.”"]], [["# VIII", "# VIII"]], [["Having mounted beside her, Alec d’Urberville drove rapidly along the crest of the first hill, chatting compliments to Tess as they went, the cart with her box being left far behind.", "그녀 곁에 올라탄 알렉 더버빌은 첫 언덕의 마루를 따라 빠르게 마차를 몰았고, 가는 내내 테스에게 듣기 좋은 말을 늘어놓았으며, 그녀의 상자를 실은 짐마차는 저 멀리 뒤처져 갔다."], ["Rising still, an immense landscape stretched around them on every side; behind, the green valley of her birth, before, a gray country of which she knew nothing except from her first brief visit to Trantridge.", "점점 높이 오르자, 사방으로 광대한 풍경이 그들 둘레에 펼쳐졌다. 뒤로는 그녀가 태어난 푸른 골짜기가, 앞으로는 트랜트리지로의 첫 짧은 방문 말고는 그녀가 아무것도 모르는 잿빛 고장이 있었다."], ["Thus they reached the verge of an incline down which the road stretched in a long straight descent of nearly a mile.", "그렇게 그들은 어느 내리막의 가장자리에 이르렀으니, 거기서 길은 거의 일 마일에 걸쳐 길고 곧게 뻗어 내려가고 있었다."]], [["Ever since the accident with her father’s horse Tess Durbeyfield, courageous as she naturally was, had been exceedingly timid on wheels; the least irregularity of motion startled her.", "아버지의 말이 당한 그 사고 이래로, 테스 더비필드는 천성이 용감한데도 바퀴 위에서는 몹시 겁이 많아졌으니, 조금만 움직임이 고르지 못해도 흠칫했다."], ["She began to get uneasy at a certain recklessness in her conductor’s driving.", "그녀는 모는 이의 어딘가 무모한 운전에 마음이 불안해지기 시작했다."]], [["“You will go down slow, sir, I suppose?”", "“천천히 내려가시겠죠, 선생님?”"], ["she said with attempted unconcern.", "그녀가 짐짓 태연한 척하며 말했다."]], [["D’Urberville looked round upon her, nipped his cigar with the tips of his large white centre-teeth, and allowed his lips to smile slowly of themselves.", "더버빌은 그녀를 돌아보더니, 큼직한 흰 앞니 끝으로 시가를 깨물고는, 입술이 저절로 천천히 미소 짓게 두었다."]], [["“Why, Tess,”", "“원, 테스.”"], ["he answered, after another whiff or two, “it isn’t a brave bouncing girl like you who asks that?", "그가 한두 모금 더 빨고 나서 대답했다. “당신처럼 씩씩하고 발랄한 처녀가 그런 걸 묻는 게 아니지?"], ["Why, I always go down at full gallop.", "원, 나는 늘 전속력으로 달려 내려가오."], ["There’s nothing like it for raising your spirits.”", "기분을 띄우는 데는 그만한 게 없거든.”"]], [["“But perhaps you need not now?”", "“하지만 지금은 그럴 필요 없잖아요?”"]], [["“Ah,”", "“아.”"], ["he said, shaking his head, “there are two to be reckoned with.", "그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “따져야 할 게 둘이오."], ["It is not me alone.", "나 혼자가 아니란 말이지."], ["Tib has to be considered, and she has a very queer temper.”", "팁도 생각해 줘야 하는데, 이 녀석이 성깔이 아주 별나거든.”"]], [["“Who?”", "“누구요?”"]], [["“Why, this mare. I fancy she looked round at me in a very grim way just then.", "“원, 이 암말 말이오. 방금 아주 험상궂은 눈으로 나를 돌아본 것 같은데."], ["Didn’t you notice it?”", "못 봤소?”"]], [["“Don’t try to frighten me, sir,”", "“저를 겁주려 하지 마세요, 선생님.”"], ["said Tess stiffly.", "테스가 딱딱하게 말했다."]], [["“Well, I don’t.", "“원, 그러는 거 아니오."], ["If any living man can manage this horse I can: I won’t say any living man can do it—but if such has the power, I am he.”", "산 사람 중에 이 말을 다룰 수 있는 자가 있다면 그게 바로 나요. 산 사람 누구나 할 수 있다고는 안 하겠소—하지만 그럴 힘을 가진 자가 있다면, 그게 나란 말이오.”"]], [["“Why do you have such a horse?”", "“그런 말을 왜 데리고 계세요?”"]], [["“Ah, well may you ask it!", "“아, 물을 만하지!"], ["It was my fate, I suppose.", "그게 내 팔자였나 보오."], ["Tib has killed one chap; and just after I bought her she nearly killed me.", "팁은 한 녀석을 죽였소. 그리고 내가 그 녀석을 사자마자 나도 거의 죽일 뻔했고."], ["And then, take my word for it, I nearly killed her.", "그러고 나서, 내 장담하건대, 내가 그 녀석을 거의 죽일 뻔했지."], ["But she’s touchy still, very touchy; and one’s life is hardly safe behind her sometimes.”", "하지만 아직도 성미가 까칠해, 아주 까칠하단 말이오. 그래서 가끔은 그 뒤에 앉아 있으면 목숨이 위태롭다오.”"]], [["They were just beginning to descend; and it was evident that the horse, whether of her own will or of his (the latter being the more likely), knew so well the reckless performance expected of her that she hardly required a hint from behind.", "그들은 막 내려가기 시작하던 참이었고, 말은—제 뜻에서든 그의 뜻에서든(후자일 공산이 컸다)—기대되는 그 무모한 짓을 어찌나 잘 알고 있던지, 뒤에서 굳이 일러 줄 것도 거의 없어 보였다."]], [["Down, down, they sped, the wheels humming like a top, the dog-cart rocking right and left, its axis acquiring a slightly oblique set in relation to the line of progress; the figure of the horse rising and falling in undulations before them.", "아래로, 아래로 그들은 내달렸으니, 바퀴는 팽이처럼 윙윙거렸고, 이륜마차는 좌우로 흔들렸으며, 그 굴대는 나아가는 선에 비스듬히 살짝 비틀려 갔고, 말의 형체는 그들 앞에서 물결처럼 솟구쳤다 가라앉았다."], ["Sometimes a wheel was off the ground, it seemed, for many yards; sometimes a stone was sent spinning over the hedge, and flinty sparks from the horse’s hoofs outshone the daylight.", "어떨 땐 바퀴 하나가 여러 야드씩 땅에서 떠 있는 듯했고, 어떨 땐 돌멩이 하나가 산울타리 너머로 튕겨 나갔으며, 말발굽에서 튀는 부싯돌 불꽃이 대낮 빛보다 더 빛났다."], ["The aspect of the straight road enlarged with their advance, the two banks dividing like a splitting stick; one rushing past at each shoulder.", "곧게 뻗은 길의 모습이 그들이 나아갈수록 점점 커졌고, 양쪽 둑이 쪼개지는 막대기처럼 갈라지며 하나씩 양쪽 어깨를 스쳐 휙휙 지나갔다."]], [["The wind blew through Tess’s white muslin to her very skin, and her washed hair flew out behind.", "바람이 테스의 흰 모슬린을 뚫고 살갗까지 스며들었고, 감은 머리카락이 뒤로 휘날렸다."], ["She was determined to show no open fear, but she clutched d’Urberville’s rein-arm.", "그녀는 드러내 놓고 두려워하지 않으리라 작정했으나, 더버빌의 고삐 쥔 팔을 움켜잡았다."]], [["“Don’t touch my arm!", "“내 팔 건드리지 마요!"], ["We shall be thrown out if you do!", "그러다간 우리 다 나가떨어진단 말이오!"], ["Hold on round my waist!”", "내 허리를 잡아요!”"]], [["She grasped his waist, and so they reached the bottom.", "그녀는 그의 허리를 붙들었고, 그렇게 그들은 바닥에 다다랐다."]], [["“Safe, thank God, in spite of your fooling!”", "“하느님 덕에 무사했네요, 당신 그 장난질에도 불구하고!”"], ["said she, her face on fire.", "그녀가 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채 말했다."]], [["“Tess—fie!", "“테스—저런!"], ["that’s temper!”", "그게 성깔이군!”"], ["said d’Urberville.", "더버빌이 말했다."]], [["“’Tis truth.”", "“사실인걸요.”"]], [["“Well, you need not let go your hold of me so thanklessly the moment you feel yourself out of danger.”", "“음, 위험을 벗어났다 싶기가 무섭게 그렇게 고마운 줄도 모르고 손을 놓아 버릴 건 없잖소.”"]], [["She had not considered what she had been doing; whether he were man or woman, stick or stone, in her involuntary hold on him.", "그녀는 자기가 무얼 하고 있었는지, 저도 모르게 그를 붙들면서 그가 남자였는지 여자였는지, 막대기였는지 돌이었는지 따위는 생각지도 못했다."], ["Recovering her reserve, she sat without replying, and thus they reached the summit of another declivity.", "도로 차분함을 되찾은 그녀는 대꾸도 없이 앉아 있었고, 그렇게 그들은 또 다른 내리막의 마루에 이르렀다."]], [["“Now then, again!”", "“자, 또 한 번!”"], ["said d’Urberville.", "더버빌이 말했다."]], [["“No, no!”", "“안 돼요, 안 돼!”"], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."], ["“Show more sense, do, please.”", "“제발 좀 더 분별 있게 굴어요.”"]], [["“But when people find themselves on one of the highest points in the county, they must get down again,”", "“하지만 사람이 이 주(州)에서 가장 높은 지점에 올라섰으면, 다시 내려가야 하는 법이지.”"], ["he retorted.", "그가 받아쳤다."]], [["He loosened rein, and away they went a second time.", "그는 고삐를 늦췄고, 그들은 두 번째로 내달렸다."], ["D’Urberville turned his face to her as they rocked, and said, in playful raillery: “Now then, put your arms round my waist again, as you did before, my Beauty.”", "더버빌은 마차가 흔들리는 가운데 그녀에게 얼굴을 돌리고, 장난기 어린 놀림조로 말했다. “자, 어서, 아까처럼 다시 내 허리에 팔을 둘러요, 어여쁜 아가씨.”"]], [["“Never!” said Tess independently, holding on as well as she could without touching him.", "“절대로!” 테스가 꿋꿋이 말하며, 그를 건드리지 않고 잡을 수 있는 한 잘 붙들었다."]], [["“Let me put one little kiss on those holmberry lips, Tess, or even on that warmed cheek, and I’ll stop—on my honour, I will!”", "“저 호랑가시 열매 같은 입술에, 아니면 저 달아오른 뺨에라도, 작은 입맞춤 하나만 하게 해 주면 멈추겠소, 테스—내 명예를 걸고, 그러리다!”"]], [["Tess, surprised beyond measure, slid farther back still on her seat, at which he urged the horse anew, and rocked her the more.", "테스는 한없이 놀라, 자리에서 더 뒤로 미끄러졌고, 그러자 그는 말을 새로 다그쳐 그녀를 더욱 흔들어 댔다."]], [["“Will nothing else do?”", "“다른 걸로는 안 되겠어요?”"], ["she cried at length, in desperation, her large eyes staring at him like those of a wild animal.", "그녀가 마침내 절망에 빠져, 야생 짐승의 그것 같은 커다란 눈으로 그를 빤히 노려보며 외쳤다."], ["This dressing her up so prettily by her mother had apparently been to lamentable purpose.", "어머니가 그녀를 그토록 예쁘게 꾸며 준 것이, 보아하니 한탄스러운 결말을 보자는 것이었던 셈이다."]], [["“Nothing, dear Tess,”", "“안 되오, 사랑스러운 테스.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, I don’t know—very well; I don’t mind!”", "“오, 모르겠어요—좋아요. 상관없어요!”"], ["she panted miserably.", "그녀가 비참하게 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["He drew rein, and as they slowed he was on the point of imprinting the desired salute, when, as if hardly yet aware of her own modesty, she dodged aside.", "그가 고삐를 당겼고, 마차가 느려지는 사이 그가 막 그 바라던 입맞춤을 찍으려는 찰나, 그녀는 마치 제 정숙함을 아직 미처 자각하지 못했던 양 옆으로 휙 피했다."], ["His arms being occupied with the reins there was left him no power to prevent her manœuvre.", "그의 두 팔이 고삐로 차 있던 터라, 그녀의 그 술책을 막을 힘이 그에게는 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["“Now, damn it—I’ll break both our necks!”", "“이런, 빌어먹을—우리 둘 다 목을 부러뜨려 버리겠어!”"], ["swore her capriciously passionate companion.", "변덕스럽게 격정적인 그녀의 동행이 욕설을 퍼부었다."], ["“So you can go from your word like that, you young witch, can you?”", "“그래, 그렇게 말을 손바닥 뒤집듯 어길 수 있나, 이 어린 마녀 같으니!”"]], [["“Very well,”", "“좋아요.”"], ["said Tess, “I’ll not move since you be so determined!", "테스가 말했다. “당신이 그렇게 작정하셨다니 꼼짝 안 하겠어요!"], ["But I—thought you would be kind to me, and protect me, as my kinsman!”", "하지만 저는—사촌인 당신이 저한테 친절하게 대해 주고, 저를 지켜 주실 줄 알았어요!”"]], [["“Kinsman be hanged! Now!”", "“사촌은 무슨 얼어 죽을! 자, 어서!”"]], [["“But I don’t want anybody to kiss me, sir!”", "“하지만 저는 누가 입 맞추는 거 싫어요, 선생님!”"], ["she implored, a big tear beginning to roll down her face, and the corners of her mouth trembling in her attempts not to cry.", "그녀가 애원했으니, 큰 눈물 한 방울이 얼굴을 타고 굴러 내리기 시작했고, 울지 않으려 애쓰는 통에 입꼬리가 떨렸다."], ["“And I wouldn’t ha’ come if I had known!”", "“이럴 줄 알았으면 오지도 않았을 거예요!”"]], [["He was inexorable, and she sat still, and d’Urberville gave her the kiss of mastery.", "그는 가차 없었고, 그녀는 가만히 앉아 있었으며, 더버빌은 그녀에게 정복의 입맞춤을 했다."], ["No sooner had he done so than she flushed with shame, took out her handkerchief, and wiped the spot on her cheek that had been touched by his lips.", "그가 그러기가 무섭게 그녀는 부끄러움에 얼굴을 붉히며 손수건을 꺼내, 그의 입술이 닿았던 뺨의 그 자리를 닦아 냈다."], ["His ardour was nettled at the sight, for the act on her part had been unconsciously done.", "그 모습에 그의 정열이 부아가 치밀었으니, 그녀의 그 행동이 저도 모르게 한 짓이었기 때문이다."]], [["“You are mighty sensitive for a cottage girl!”", "“시골 처녀치고는 어지간히 예민하군!”"], ["said the young man.", "젊은이가 말했다."]], [["Tess made no reply to this remark, of which, indeed, she did not quite comprehend the drift, unheeding the snub she had administered by her instinctive rub upon her cheek.", "테스는 이 말에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으니, 실은 그 속뜻을 잘 헤아리지도 못했고, 본능적으로 뺨을 문질러 그에게 가한 면박도 개의치 않았다."], ["She had, in fact, undone the kiss, as far as such a thing was physically possible.", "사실 그녀는, 그런 것이 물리적으로 가능한 한도껏, 그 입맞춤을 도로 무른 셈이었다."], ["With a dim sense that he was vexed she looked steadily ahead as they trotted on near Melbury Down and Wingreen, till she saw, to her consternation, that there was yet another descent to be undergone.", "그가 언짢아한다는 어렴풋한 느낌과 함께, 그녀는 그들이 멜버리 다운과 윈그린 근처를 종종걸음으로 지나는 동안 줄곧 앞만 똑바로 바라보았는데, 이윽고 또 한 번 내려가야 할 내리막이 있음을 알아채고는 기겁했다."]], [["“You shall be made sorry for that!”", "“그것 때문에 후회하게 해 주지!”"], ["he resumed, his injured tone still remaining, as he flourished the whip anew.", "그가, 다친 듯한 어조를 여전히 띤 채, 다시 채찍을 휘두르며 말을 이었다."], ["“Unless, that is, you agree willingly to let me do it again, and no handkerchief.”", "“그게 싫거든, 다시 하게 기꺼이 허락하든지—손수건은 없이 말이오.”"]], [["She sighed. “Very well, sir!”", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬었다. “좋아요, 선생님!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Oh—let me get my hat!”", "“오—제 모자 좀 줍게 해 주세요!”"]], [["At the moment of speaking her hat had blown off into the road, their present speed on the upland being by no means slow.", "말하는 그 순간, 그녀의 모자가 길로 날아가 떨어졌으니, 고지에서의 그들의 속도가 결코 느리지 않았던 것이다."], ["D’Urberville pulled up, and said he would get it for her, but Tess was down on the other side.", "더버빌이 마차를 세우고는 자기가 주워 주겠다고 했으나, 테스는 어느새 반대쪽으로 내려가 있었다."]], [["She turned back and picked up the article.", "그녀는 돌아가 그 물건을 집어 들었다."]], [["“You look prettier with it off, upon my soul, if that’s possible,”", "“모자를 벗은 게 더 예쁘군, 정말이지, 그게 가능하다면 말이오.”"], ["he said, contemplating her over the back of the vehicle.", "그가 마차 뒤쪽 너머로 그녀를 찬찬히 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Now then, up again! What’s the matter?”", "“자, 어서, 다시 올라타요! 뭐 하는 거요?”"]], [["The hat was in place and tied, but Tess had not stepped forward.", "모자는 제자리에 놓여 매여 있었으나, 테스는 앞으로 나아오지 않았다."]], [["“No, sir,”", "“싫어요, 선생님.”"], ["she said, revealing the red and ivory of her mouth as her eye lit in defiant triumph; “not again, if I know it!”", "그녀가, 도전하는 의기양양함으로 눈을 빛내며, 입속의 붉음과 상앗빛을 드러내며 말했다. “내가 정신을 차린 이상, 다시는 안 타요!”"]], [["“What—you won’t get up beside me?”", "“뭐라고—내 곁에 안 오르겠다고?”"]], [["“No; I shall walk.”", "“네, 걸어가겠어요.”"]], [["“’Tis five or six miles yet to Trantridge.”", "“트랜트리지까지는 아직 오륙 마일이오.”"]], [["“I don’t care if ’tis dozens.", "“수십 마일이라도 상관없어요."], ["Besides, the cart is behind.”", "게다가 짐마차도 뒤에 오고요.”"]], [["“You artful hussy!", "“이 교활한 계집 같으니!"], ["Now, tell me—didn’t you make that hat blow off on purpose?", "자, 말해 봐—일부러 그 모자를 날려 버린 거 아니오?"], ["I’ll swear you did!”", "내 장담하건대 그랬을 거요!”"]], [["Her strategic silence confirmed his suspicion.", "그녀의 전략적인 침묵이 그의 의심을 굳혔다."]], [["Then d’Urberville cursed and swore at her, and called her everything he could think of for the trick.", "그러자 더버빌은 그 장난질을 두고 그녀에게 욕설과 악담을 퍼부으며, 떠오르는 온갖 말로 그녀를 불러 댔다."], ["Turning the horse suddenly he tried to drive back upon her, and so hem her in between the gig and the hedge.", "그는 별안간 말을 돌려 그녀에게 도로 마차를 몰아붙여, 이륜마차와 산울타리 사이에 그녀를 가두려 했다."], ["But he could not do this short of injuring her.", "그러나 그녀를 다치게 하지 않고서는 그럴 수 없었다."]], [["“You ought to be ashamed of yourself for using such wicked words!”", "“그렇게 못된 말을 쓰다니 부끄러운 줄 아셔야죠!”"], ["cried Tess with spirit, from the top of the hedge into which she had scrambled. “I don’t like ’ee at all!", "테스가 기어 올라간 산울타리 꼭대기에서 기세 좋게 소리쳤다. \"당신 같은 건 조금도 좋아하지 않아!"], ["I hate and detest you! I’ll go back to mother, I will!”", "밉고 끔찍해! 어머니한테 돌아갈 거야, 정말로!\""]], [["D’Urberville’s bad temper cleared up at sight of hers; and he laughed heartily.", "테스의 화난 얼굴을 보자 더버빌의 언짢음이 가셨다. 그는 호탕하게 웃었다."]], [["“Well, I like you all the better,”", "\"그래, 그래서 더 좋아.\""], ["he said. “Come, let there be peace.", "그가 말했다. \"자, 화 풀어."], ["I’ll never do it any more against your will.", "다시는 네 뜻을 거스르는 짓 안 할게."], ["My life upon it now!”", "내 목숨을 걸지!\""]], [["Still Tess could not be induced to remount.", "그래도 테스는 다시 마차에 타라고 구슬려질 기색이 아니었다."], ["She did not, however, object to his keeping his gig alongside her; and in this manner, at a slow pace, they advanced towards the village of Trantridge.", "다만 그가 마차를 자기 옆에 바짝 붙여 모는 것까지 마다하지는 않았다. 이런 식으로, 느린 걸음으로, 둘은 트랜트리지 마을을 향해 나아갔다."], ["From time to time d’Urberville exhibited a sort of fierce distress at the sight of the tramping he had driven her to undertake by his misdemeanour.", "이따금 더버빌은 자기 잘못 때문에 그녀를 걷게 만든 광경에 일종의 격한 괴로움을 드러냈다. 사실 이제는 그를 안심하고 믿어도 좋았을 것이다."], ["She might in truth have safely trusted him now; but he had forfeited her confidence for the time, and she kept on the ground progressing thoughtfully, as if wondering whether it would be wiser to return home.", "하지만 그는 당분간 그녀의 신뢰를 잃은 처지였고, 그녀는 집으로 돌아가는 편이 더 현명하지 않을까 생각하듯 골똘히 땅을 디디며 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Her resolve, however, had been taken, and it seemed vacillating even to childishness to abandon it now, unless for graver reasons.", "그러나 결심은 이미 굳혀진 터였고, 지금 와서 더 무거운 이유도 없이 그것을 저버리는 일은 어린애처럼 흔들리는 짓으로 여겨졌다."], ["How could she face her parents, get back her box, and disconcert the whole scheme for the rehabilitation of her family on such sentimental grounds?", "어떻게 부모를 마주하고, 짐 상자를 도로 찾아오고, 그런 감상적인 이유로 집안을 다시 일으키려는 계획 전체를 뒤엎는단 말인가?"]], [["A few minutes later the chimneys of The Slopes appeared in view, and in a snug nook to the right the poultry-farm and cottage of Tess’s destination.", "몇 분 뒤 더 슬로프스의 굴뚝이 시야에 들어왔고, 오른편 아늑한 구석에 테스가 갈 목적지인 양계장과 오두막이 보였다."]], [["# IX", "# IX"]], [["The community of fowls to which Tess had been appointed as supervisor, purveyor, nurse, surgeon, and friend made its headquarters in an old thatched cottage standing in an enclosure that had once been a garden, but was now a trampled and sanded square.", "테스가 감독자이자, 사료 공급자, 간호사, 외과의, 친구 노릇을 맡게 된 닭 무리는 한때 정원이었으나 지금은 짓밟혀 모래만 깔린 네모난 터에 선 낡은 초가 오두막을 본거지로 삼고 있었다."], ["The house was overrun with ivy, its chimney being enlarged by the boughs of the parasite to the aspect of a ruined tower.", "집은 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮였고, 굴뚝은 이 기생식물의 가지들로 커져 폐허가 된 탑처럼 보였다."], ["The lower rooms were entirely given over to the birds, who walked about them with a proprietary air, as though the place had been built by themselves, and not by certain dusty copyholders who now lay east and west in the churchyard.", "아래층 방들은 온전히 새들 차지였다. 새들은 마치 이 집을 자기네가 지은 양, 지금은 교회 묘지에 동서로 누운 어느 먼지투성이 등본 보유자들이 지은 게 아닌 양, 주인 행세를 하며 그 안을 거닐었다."], ["The descendants of these bygone owners felt it almost as a slight to their family when the house which had so much of their affection, had cost so much of their forefathers’ money, and had been in their possession for several generations before the d’Urbervilles came and built here, was indifferently turned into a fowl-house by Mrs Stoke-d’Urberville as soon as the property fell into hand according to law.", "이 지난날 주인들의 후손들은, 자기네가 그토록 아꼈고 조상의 돈이 그토록 많이 들었으며 더버빌 일가가 와서 여기 집을 짓기 전 여러 세대에 걸쳐 자기네 소유였던 그 집을, 스토크더버빌 부인이 법에 따라 재산을 손에 넣자마자 무심히 닭장으로 바꿔 버린 일을 거의 집안에 대한 모욕처럼 느꼈다."], ["“’Twas good enough for Christians in grandfather’s time,” they said.", "\"할아버지 시절엔 기독교인이 살기에도 충분히 좋은 집이었는데.\" 그들은 그렇게 말했다."]], [["The rooms wherein dozens of infants had wailed at their nursing now resounded with the tapping of nascent chicks.", "수십 명의 갓난아이가 젖을 빨며 울어 대던 방들이 이제는 갓 깨어난 병아리들이 톡톡 쪼는 소리로 울렸다."], ["Distracted hens in coops occupied spots where formerly stood chairs supporting sedate agriculturists.", "닭장 속 정신 사나운 암탉들이, 예전에 점잖은 농부들을 받치고 있던 의자가 놓였던 자리를 차지했다."], ["The chimney-corner and once-blazing hearth was now filled with inverted beehives, in which the hens laid their eggs; while out of doors the plots that each succeeding householder had carefully shaped with his spade were torn by the cocks in wildest fashion.", "굴뚝 모퉁이, 한때 활활 타오르던 화덕에는 이제 거꾸로 엎은 벌통들이 가득 차 그 안에 암탉들이 알을 낳았다. 한편 바깥에서는 대대로 살림을 맡았던 사람들이 삽으로 정성껏 다듬어 놓은 텃밭이 수탉들에게 더없이 거칠게 헤집어졌다."]], [["The garden in which the cottage stood was surrounded by a wall, and could only be entered through a door.", "오두막이 선 정원은 담으로 둘러싸여 있었고, 오직 문 하나로만 드나들 수 있었다."]], [["When Tess had occupied herself about an hour the next morning in altering and improving the arrangements, according to her skilled ideas as the daughter of a professed poulterer, the door in the wall opened and a servant in white cap and apron entered.", "이튿날 아침 테스가 한 시간쯤, 어엿한 가금업자의 딸로서 익힌 솜씨에 따라 시설을 고치고 손보는 데 매달려 있을 때, 담의 문이 열리고 흰 모자에 앞치마를 두른 하녀 하나가 들어왔다."], ["She had come from the manor-house.", "저택에서 온 하녀였다."]], [["“Mrs d’Urberville wants the fowls as usual,”", "\"더버빌 마님이 늘 하던 대로 닭들을 보고 싶어 하세요.\""], ["she said; but perceiving that Tess did not quite understand, she explained, “Mis’ess is a old lady, and blind.”", "그녀가 말했다. 그러나 테스가 잘 이해하지 못하는 걸 알아채고 이렇게 설명했다."], ["“Blind!” said Tess.", "\"마님은 연세 드신 데다, 눈이 안 보이세요.\""]], [["Almost before her misgiving at the news could find time to shape itself she took, under her companion’s direction, two of the most beautiful of the Hamburghs in her arms, and followed the maid-servant, who had likewise taken two, to the adjacent mansion, which, though ornate and imposing, showed traces everywhere on this side that some occupant of its chambers could bend to the love of dumb creatures—feathers floating within view of the front, and hen-coops standing on the grass.", "\"눈이 안 보인다고요!\" 테스가 말했다. 그 소식에 일어난 불안이 채 자리를 잡을 새도 없이, 그녀는 동행의 지시에 따라 함부르크종 가운데 가장 아름다운 두 마리를 품에 안고서, 마찬가지로 두 마리를 안은 하녀를 따라 이웃한 저택으로 갔다. 그 저택은 화려하고 위풍당당했으나, 이쪽 면에는 어딜 봐도 그 방들에 사는 누군가가 말 못 하는 짐승들을 향한 애정에 마음을 굽힐 줄 안다는 흔적이 있었다. 정면이 보이는 자리에 깃털이 날렸고, 잔디 위에는 닭장들이 놓여 있었다."]], [["In a sitting-room on the ground-floor, ensconced in an armchair with her back to the light, was the owner and mistress of the estate, a white-haired woman of not more than sixty, or even less, wearing a large cap.", "일 층 거실에서, 빛을 등진 채 안락의자에 파묻혀 있는 이가 이 영지의 주인이자 안주인이었다. 예순 살, 어쩌면 그보다 덜한 백발의 부인이 큼직한 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["She had the mobile face frequent in those whose sight has decayed by stages, has been laboriously striven after, and reluctantly let go, rather than the stagnant mien apparent in persons long sightless or born blind.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 오래 눈이 멀었거나 태어날 때부터 못 보던 사람들에게서 보이는 그 멈춰 버린 표정이 아니라, 시력을 단계적으로 잃어 가며 애써 붙들려 했으나 마지못해 놓아 버린 이들에게 흔한 살아 움직이는 표정이 있었다."], ["Tess walked up to this lady with her feathered charges—one sitting on each arm.", "테스는 한쪽 팔에 한 마리씩, 깃털 달린 짐을 안고 이 부인에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Ah, you are the young woman come to look after my birds?”", "\"아, 네가 내 새들을 돌보러 온 처녀로구나?\""], ["said Mrs d’Urberville, recognizing a new footstep.", "더버빌 부인이 낯선 발소리를 알아채고 말했다."], ["“I hope you will be kind to them.", "\"이 아이들한테 잘해 주길 바란다."], ["My bailiff tells me you are quite the proper person.", "내 집사가 그러는데 네가 아주 적임자라더구나."], ["Well, where are they?", "그래, 어디 있느냐?"], ["Ah, this is Strut! But he is hardly so lively to-day, is he?", "아, 이놈은 스트럿이로구나! 그런데 오늘은 영 기운이 없네, 그렇지?"], ["He is alarmed at being handled by a stranger, I suppose.", "낯선 손길에 안긴 게 겁이 난 모양이다."], ["And Phena too—yes, they are a little frightened—aren’t you, dears?", "페나도 그렇고—그래, 좀 무서워하는구나—그렇지, 얘들아?"], ["But they will soon get used to you.”", "하지만 곧 너한테 익숙해질 게다.\""]], [["While the old lady had been speaking Tess and the other maid, in obedience to her gestures, had placed the fowls severally in her lap, and she had felt them over from head to tail, examining their beaks, their combs, the manes of the cocks, their wings, and their claws.", "부인이 말하는 동안 테스와 다른 하녀는 그녀의 손짓에 따라 닭을 한 마리씩 그녀 무릎에 놓았고, 그녀는 머리에서 꼬리까지 닭을 더듬으며 부리와 볏, 수탉의 목깃, 날개, 발톱을 살폈다."], ["Her touch enabled her to recognize them in a moment, and to discover if a single feather were crippled or draggled.", "손의 감촉만으로 그녀는 단번에 닭을 알아보았고, 깃털 한 가닥이라도 상하거나 후줄근하면 곧 찾아냈다."], ["She handled their crops, and knew what they had eaten, and if too little or too much; her face enacting a vivid pantomime of the criticisms passing in her mind.", "그녀는 닭의 모이주머니를 만져 보고 무엇을 먹었는지, 너무 적게 혹은 너무 많이 먹지 않았는지 알아냈다. 그녀의 얼굴은 마음속을 스쳐 가는 평가들을 생생한 무언극으로 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["The birds that the two girls had brought in were duly returned to the yard, and the process was repeated till all the pet cocks and hens had been submitted to the old woman—Hamburghs, Bantams, Cochins, Brahmas, Dorkings, and such other sorts as were in fashion just then—her perception of each visitor being seldom at fault as she received the bird upon her knees.", "두 처녀가 들여온 새들은 차례로 마당에 돌려보내졌고, 이 과정은 아끼는 수탉과 암탉이 모두 노부인 앞에 바쳐질 때까지 되풀이되었다—함부르크종, 밴텀종, 코친종, 브라마종, 도킹종, 그리고 그 무렵 유행하던 그 밖의 온갖 종이었다—그녀가 새를 무릎에 받아 들며 손님 하나하나를 알아보는 데 틀리는 일은 거의 없었다."]], [["It reminded Tess of a Confirmation, in which Mrs d’Urberville was the bishop, the fowls the young people presented, and herself and the maid-servant the parson and curate of the parish bringing them up.", "그것은 테스에게 견진성사를 떠올리게 했다. 더버빌 부인이 주교요, 닭들이 인도되어 온 젊은이들이며, 자기와 하녀가 그들을 데려온 본당 신부와 보좌신부였다."], ["At the end of the ceremony Mrs d’Urberville abruptly asked Tess, wrinkling and twitching her face into undulations, “Can you whistle?” “Whistle, Ma’am?”", "의식이 끝날 무렵 더버빌 부인이 얼굴을 주름지게 일그러뜨려 잔물결을 일으키며 불쑥 테스에게 물었다. \"휘파람 불 줄 아느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, whistle tunes.”", "\"휘파람이요, 마님?\""]], [["Tess could whistle like most other country-girls, though the accomplishment was one which she did not care to profess in genteel company.", "\"그래, 곡조를 부는 휘파람 말이다.\" 테스는 여느 시골 처녀들처럼 휘파람을 불 줄 알았으나, 점잖은 자리에서 내세우고 싶은 재주는 아니었다."], ["However, she blandly admitted that such was the fact.", "그래도 그녀는 그게 사실이라고 무던히 인정했다."]], [["“Then you will have to practise it every day. I had a lad who did it very well, but he has left.", "\"그럼 매일 연습해야 한다. 그걸 아주 잘 부는 사내아이가 하나 있었는데, 그만뒀거든."], ["I want you to whistle to my bullfinches; as I cannot see them, I like to hear them, and we teach ’em airs that way.", "내 멋쟁이방울새들한테 휘파람을 불어 주었으면 한다. 눈으로 못 보니 듣기라도 하고 싶고, 그렇게 해서 새들한테 곡조를 가르치거든."], ["Tell her where the cages are, Elizabeth.", "새장이 어디 있는지 일러 주거라, 엘리자베스."], ["You must begin to-morrow, or they will go back in their piping.", "내일부터 시작해야 한다. 안 그러면 새들 노래 솜씨가 도로 나빠진다."], ["They have been neglected these several days.”", "며칠째 돌보지 않았으니.\""]], [["“Mr d’Urberville whistled to ’em this morning, ma’am,”", "\"더버빌 도련님이 오늘 아침에 새들한테 휘파람을 불어 주셨어요, 마님.\""], ["said Elizabeth. “He! Pooh!”", "엘리자베스가 말했다."]], [["The old lady’s face creased into furrows of repugnance, and she made no further reply.", "\"그 녀석이! 흥!\" 노부인의 얼굴이 혐오의 골을 깊이 파며 일그러졌고, 그녀는 더는 대꾸하지 않았다."]], [["Thus the reception of Tess by her fancied kinswoman terminated, and the birds were taken back to their quarters.", "이렇게 해서 친척이라 짐작되던 부인에게 테스가 맞아들여진 일이 끝났고, 새들은 우리로 돌려보내졌다."], ["The girl’s surprise at Mrs d’Urberville’s manner was not great; for since seeing the size of the house she had expected no more.", "더버빌 부인의 태도에 대한 처녀의 놀라움은 크지 않았다. 집의 규모를 본 뒤로 그 이상은 기대하지도 않았으니까."], ["But she was far from being aware that the old lady had never heard a word of the so-called kinship.", "그러나 노부인이 이른바 친척 관계라는 말은 한마디도 들은 적이 없다는 사실은 그녀로서는 알 길이 없었다."], ["She gathered that no great affection flowed between the blind woman and her son.", "그녀는 눈먼 부인과 그 아들 사이에 큰 애정이 흐르지 않는다고 짐작했다."], ["But in that, too, she was mistaken.", "하지만 그 점에서도 그녀는 틀렸다."], ["Mrs d’Urberville was not the first mother compelled to love her offspring resentfully, and to be bitterly fond.", "더버빌 부인은 제 자식을 원망하면서 사랑하고, 쓰라리게 아낄 수밖에 없는 첫 어머니가 아니었다."]], [["In spite of the unpleasant initiation of the day before, Tess inclined to the freedom and novelty of her new position in the morning when the sun shone, now that she was once installed there; and she was curious to test her powers in the unexpected direction asked of her, so as to ascertain her chance of retaining her post.", "전날의 언짢은 첫걸음에도 불구하고, 일단 자리를 잡고 보니 햇살이 비치는 아침이면 테스는 새 자리의 자유로움과 새로움에 마음이 기울었다. 그리고 뜻밖에 청받은 그 방면에서 자기 능력을 시험해, 자리를 지킬 수 있을지 가늠해 보고 싶은 호기심이 일었다."], ["As soon as she was alone within the walled garden she sat herself down on a coop, and seriously screwed up her mouth for the long-neglected practice.", "담으로 둘러싸인 정원에 혼자 있게 되자마자 그녀는 닭장 위에 앉아 오랫동안 묵힌 연습을 위해 진지하게 입을 오므렸다."], ["She found her former ability to have degenerated to the production of a hollow rush of wind through the lips, and no clear note at all.", "예전의 솜씨가 입술 사이로 텅 빈 바람 소리만 새 나갈 뿐 또렷한 음 하나 못 내는 지경으로 퇴화했음을 알았다."]], [["She remained fruitlessly blowing and blowing, wondering how she could have so grown out of the art which had come by nature, till she became aware of a movement among the ivy-boughs which cloaked the garden-wall no less then the cottage.", "타고난 재주가 어쩌다 이렇게까지 녹슬었나 의아해하며 그녀는 헛되이 불고 또 불었다. 그러다 오두막 못지않게 정원 담을 뒤덮은 담쟁이 가지들 사이에서 무언가 움직이는 기척을 알아챘다."], ["Looking that way she beheld a form springing from the coping to the plot.", "그쪽을 보니 담 꼭대기에서 텃밭으로 뛰어내리는 형체가 보였다."], ["It was Alec d’Urberville, whom she had not set eyes on since he had conducted her the day before to the door of the gardener’s cottage where she had lodgings.", "알렉 더버빌이었다. 전날 그녀가 묵을 정원사 오두막 문까지 데려다준 뒤로 마주친 적 없는 그였다."]], [["“Upon my honour!”", "\"맹세코!\""], ["cried he, “there was never before such a beautiful thing in Nature or Art as you look, ‘Cousin’ Tess (‘Cousin’ had a faint ring of mockery).", "그가 외쳤다. \"자연에도 예술에도 지금 네 모습처럼 아름다운 건 없었어, '사촌' 테스('사촌'이라는 말에는 희미한 조롱의 울림이 있었다)."], ["I have been watching you from over the wall—sitting like _Im_-patience on a monument, and pouting up that pretty red mouth to whistling shape, and whooing and whooing, and privately swearing, and never being able to produce a note.", "담 너머로 너를 지켜봤지—기념비 위의 '인내'처럼 앉아서, 그 예쁜 붉은 입을 휘파람 모양으로 삐죽 내밀고, 후우 후우 불어 대고, 혼자 속으로 욕설을 내뱉으면서도, 음 하나 못 내고 있더군."], ["Why, you are quite cross because you can’t do it.”", "아니, 못 한다고 잔뜩 골이 났구나.\""]], [["“I may be cross, but I didn’t swear.”", "\"골이야 났을지 몰라도, 욕설은 안 했어요.\""]], [["“Ah! I understand why you are trying—those bullies!", "\"아! 네가 왜 애쓰는지 알겠다—그 멧새 녀석들!"], ["My mother wants you to carry on their musical education.", "어머니가 너더러 그 녀석들 음악 교육을 맡으라는 거지."], ["How selfish of her!", "정말 이기적이지 뭐야!"], ["As if attending to these curst cocks and hens here were not enough work for any girl.", "여기 이 빌어먹을 닭들 돌보는 것만으로도 처녀 하나한테 충분한 일거리가 아니라는 듯이 말이야."], ["I would flatly refuse, if I were you.”", "나라면 단칼에 거절하겠어.\""]], [["“But she wants me particularly to do it, and to be ready by to-morrow morning.”", "\"하지만 마님이 특별히 저더러 하라고 하셨고, 내일 아침까지 준비하라고 하셨어요.\""]], [["“Does she?", "\"그래?"], ["Well then—I’ll give you a lesson or two.”", "그럼—내가 한두 번 가르쳐 주지.\""]], [["“Oh no, you won’t!” said Tess, withdrawing towards the door.", "\"오 안 돼요, 그러지 마세요!\" 테스가 문 쪽으로 물러서며 말했다."]], [["“Nonsense; I don’t want to touch you.", "\"별소릴 다 하네. 너를 건드리려는 게 아니야."], ["See—I’ll stand on this side of the wire-netting, and you can keep on the other; so you may feel quite safe.", "봐—난 이 철망 이쪽에 서 있고, 넌 저쪽에 있어. 그러면 아주 안심해도 돼. 자, 봐."], ["Now, look here; you screw up your lips too harshly.", "넌 입술을 너무 빡빡하게 오므려."], ["There ’tis—so.”", "이렇게—봐.\""]], [["He suited the action to the word, and whistled a line of “Take, O take those lips away.”", "그는 말에 맞춰 행동하며 \"가져가오, 오 그 입술을 가져가오\"의 한 소절을 휘파람으로 불었다."], ["But the allusion was lost upon Tess.", "그러나 그 암시는 테스에게 전해지지 않았다."]], [["“Now try,” said d’Urberville.", "\"자, 해 봐.\" 더버빌이 말했다."]], [["She attempted to look reserved; her face put on a sculptural severity.", "그녀는 새침한 표정을 지으려 애썼다. 얼굴이 조각상처럼 굳었다."], ["But he persisted in his demand, and at last, to get rid of him, she did put up her lips as directed for producing a clear note; laughing distressfully, however, and then blushing with vexation that she had laughed.", "그러나 그가 끈질기게 졸라 댔고, 마침내 그를 떼어 내려고 그녀는 시킨 대로 또렷한 음을 내려고 입술을 내밀었다. 그러면서도 괴롭게 웃었고, 이내 웃어 버린 게 분해 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["He encouraged her with “Try again!”", "그는 \"다시 해 봐!\" 하며 그녀를 부추겼다."]], [["Tess was quite serious, painfully serious by this time; and she tried—ultimately and unexpectedly emitting a real round sound.", "이제 테스는 자못 진지했다. 고통스러우리만치 진지했다. 그녀는 시도했고—마침내 뜻밖에도 진짜 둥근 음 하나를 내놓았다."], ["The momentary pleasure of success got the better of her; her eyes enlarged, and she involuntarily smiled in his face.", "성공의 순간적 기쁨이 그녀를 사로잡았다. 눈이 커졌고, 그녀는 저도 모르게 그의 얼굴을 보며 미소 지었다."]], [["“That’s it!", "\"바로 그거야!"], ["Now I have started you—you’ll go on beautifully.", "이제 내가 너를 출발시켰으니—넌 멋지게 해 나갈 거야."], ["There—I said I would not come near you; and, in spite of such temptation as never before fell to mortal man, I’ll keep my word....", "자—내가 네 곁에 안 가겠다고 했지. 그리고 인간으로 태어나 일찍이 없던 그런 유혹에도 불구하고, 난 약속을 지키마……"], ["Tess, do you think my mother a queer old soul?”", "테스, 내 어머니를 별난 늙은이라고 생각하니?\""]], [["“I don’t know much of her yet, sir.”", "\"아직 마님을 잘 모르는걸요, 도련님.\""]], [["“You’ll find her so; she must be, to make you learn to whistle to her bullfinches.", "\"그렇게 생각하게 될 거야. 그럴 수밖에. 너더러 자기 멧새들한테 휘파람을 배우라고 시키는 분이니까."], ["I am rather out of her books just now, but you will be quite in favour if you treat her live-stock well. Good morning.", "난 지금 어머니한테 좀 미운털이 박혔지만, 넌 어머니 가축들만 잘 돌보면 단단히 총애받을 거야. 잘 있어."], ["If you meet with any difficulties and want help here, don’t go to the bailiff, come to me.”", "여기서 무슨 어려움이 생기거나 도움이 필요하면, 집사한테 가지 말고, 나한테 와.\""]], [["It was in the economy of this _régime_ that Tess Durbeyfield had undertaken to fill a place.", "테스 더버빌이 자리 하나를 채우겠다고 맡고 들어선 곳은 바로 이런 식으로 굴러가는 살림이었다."], ["Her first day’s experiences were fairly typical of those which followed through many succeeding days.", "그녀의 첫날 경험은 그 뒤 여러 날을 두고 이어진 나날들의 본보기라 할 만했다."], ["A familiarity with Alec d’Urberville’s presence—which that young man carefully cultivated in her by playful dialogue, and by jestingly calling her his cousin when they were alone—removed much of her original shyness of him, without, however, implanting any feeling which could engender shyness of a new and tenderer kind.", "알렉 더버빌의 존재에 대한 익숙함—그 젊은이가 장난스러운 대화로, 그리고 둘만 있을 때면 농 삼아 그녀를 사촌이라 부르며 그녀 안에 조심스레 길러 낸 그것—은 그에 대한 처음의 수줍음을 많이 걷어 냈다. 그렇다고 새롭고 더 부드러운 종류의 수줍음을 자아낼 만한 감정을 심어 준 것은 아니었지만."], ["But she was more pliable under his hands than a mere companionship would have made her, owing to her unavoidable dependence upon his mother, and, through that lady’s comparative helplessness, upon him.", "다만 그녀는 단순한 동무 사이였다면 그렇지 않았을 만큼 그의 손아귀에서 고분고분했다. 그의 어머니에게 어쩔 수 없이 의지해야 했고, 그 부인이 비교적 무력한 탓에 결국 그에게도 기대야 했기 때문이다."]], [["She soon found that whistling to the bullfinches in Mrs d’Urberville’s room was no such onerous business when she had regained the art, for she had caught from her musical mother numerous airs that suited those songsters admirably.", "머지않아 그녀는, 일단 솜씨를 되찾고 나니 더버빌 부인의 방에서 멧새들에게 휘파람을 불어 주는 일이 그리 고된 일이 아님을 알게 되었다. 음악을 좋아하던 어머니에게서 그 가락새들에게 더없이 잘 어울리는 곡조를 여럿 익혀 둔 덕이었다."], ["A far more satisfactory time than when she practised in the garden was this whistling by the cages each morning.", "매일 아침 새장 옆에서 부는 이 휘파람은 정원에서 연습할 때보다 훨씬 흡족한 시간이었다."], ["Unrestrained by the young man’s presence she threw up her mouth, put her lips near the bars, and piped away in easeful grace to the attentive listeners.", "젊은이의 존재에 거리낄 일이 없으니 그녀는 입을 한껏 벌려, 입술을 창살에 가까이 대고, 귀 기울여 듣는 청중을 향해 느긋한 우아함으로 곡조를 불어 댔다."]], [["Mrs d’Urberville slept in a large four-post bedstead hung with heavy damask curtains, and the bullfinches occupied the same apartment, where they flitted about freely at certain hours, and made little white spots on the furniture and upholstery.", "더버빌 부인은 묵직한 다마스크 휘장을 드리운 큼직한 사주식 침대에서 잤고, 멧새들도 같은 방을 차지해 정해진 시간이면 그 안을 자유로이 날아다니며 가구와 천갈이에 작은 흰 얼룩을 남겼다."], ["Once while Tess was at the window where the cages were ranged, giving her lesson as usual, she thought she heard a rustling behind the bed.", "한번은 테스가 새장들이 늘어선 창가에서 여느 때처럼 가르치고 있는데, 침대 뒤에서 무언가 바스락거리는 소리가 들리는 것 같았다."], ["The old lady was not present, and turning round the girl had an impression that the toes of a pair of boots were visible below the fringe of the curtains.", "노부인은 그 자리에 없었고, 돌아본 처녀는 휘장 술 아래로 한 켤레 장화의 발끝이 보이는 것 같다는 인상을 받았다."], ["Thereupon her whistling became so disjointed that the listener, if such there were, must have discovered her suspicion of his presence.", "그러자 그녀의 휘파람이 어찌나 끊겼던지, 만약 듣는 자가 있었다면 자기 존재를 그녀가 의심한다는 걸 알아챘을 것이 분명했다."], ["She searched the curtains every morning after that, but never found anybody within them.", "그 뒤로 그녀는 아침마다 휘장을 살폈으나, 그 안에서 누구도 찾아내지 못했다."], ["Alec d’Urberville had evidently thought better of his freak to terrify her by an ambush of that kind.", "알렉 더버빌은 그런 식의 매복으로 그녀를 겁주려던 변덕을 다시 생각해 그만둔 것이 분명했다."]], [["# X", "# X"]], [["Every village has its idiosyncrasy, its constitution, often its own code of morality.", "마을마다 저마다의 특이한 성질이, 저마다의 기질이, 흔히 저마다의 도덕률이 있다."], ["The levity of some of the younger women in and about Trantridge was marked, and was perhaps symptomatic of the choice spirit who ruled The Slopes in that vicinity.", "트랜트리지 안팎의 젊은 여자들 가운데 더러는 경박함이 두드러졌는데, 어쩌면 그 부근의 더 슬로프스를 다스리는 빼어난 인물을 보여 주는 징후였는지도 모른다."], ["The place had also a more abiding defect; it drank hard.", "그곳에는 더 뿌리 깊은 결함도 하나 있었다."], ["The staple conversation on the farms around was on the uselessness of saving money; and smock-frocked arithmeticians, leaning on their ploughs or hoes, would enter into calculations of great nicety to prove that parish relief was a fuller provision for a man in his old age than any which could result from savings out of their wages during a whole lifetime.", "술이 셌다. 둘레의 농장들에서 흔한 대화는 돈을 모아 봐야 소용없다는 이야기였다. 작업복 차림의 산술가들이 쟁기나 괭이에 기대어, 평생 품삯에서 떼어 모은 저축이 어떤 식으로 불어나더라도 교구 구호금이 노년의 사내에게 더 넉넉한 살림 밑천이 됨을 증명하려고 자못 정교한 계산에 들어가곤 했다."]], [["The chief pleasure of these philosophers lay in going every Saturday night, when work was done, to Chaseborough, a decayed market-town two or three miles distant; and, returning in the small hours of the next morning, to spend Sunday in sleeping off the dyspeptic effects of the curious compounds sold to them as beer by the monopolizers of the once-independent inns.", "이 철학자들의 으뜸가는 낙은 토요일 밤마다, 일이 끝나면, 두세 마일 떨어진 쇠락한 장터 마을 체이스버러로 가는 것이었다. 그리고 이튿날 새벽 시간에 돌아와, 한때 독립적이던 여관들을 독차지한 자들이 맥주랍시고 팔아먹는 야릇한 혼합물의 소화불량 후유증을 잠으로 떨쳐 내며 일요일을 보내는 것이었다."]], [["For a long time Tess did not join in the weekly pilgrimages.", "오랫동안 테스는 이 주간 순례에 끼지 않았다."], ["But under pressure from matrons not much older than herself—for a field-man’s wages being as high at twenty-one as at forty, marriage was early here—Tess at length consented to go.", "그러나 자기와 그리 나이 차이도 안 나는 아낙들의 성화에—들일꾼의 품삯은 스물한 살이나 마흔이나 똑같이 높은지라 이곳에서는 결혼이 일렀다—테스도 마침내 가기로 했다."], ["Her first experience of the journey afforded her more enjoyment than she had expected, the hilariousness of the others being quite contagious after her monotonous attention to the poultry-farm all the week.", "그 나들이의 첫 경험은 기대보다 많은 즐거움을 안겨 주었다. 일주일 내내 양계장에만 단조롭게 매달리던 끝이라 다른 이들의 흥겨움이 자못 전염성이 있었던 것이다."], ["She went again and again.", "그녀는 가고 또 갔다."], ["Being graceful and interesting, standing moreover on the momentary threshold of womanhood, her appearance drew down upon her some sly regards from loungers in the streets of Chaseborough; hence, though sometimes her journey to the town was made independently, she always searched for her fellows at nightfall, to have the protection of their companionship homeward.", "우아하고 호감 가는 데다, 게다가 여자가 되기 직전 그 한순간의 문턱에 선 까닭에, 그녀의 모습은 체이스버러 거리의 빈둥거리는 자들에게서 슬그머니 곁눈질을 끌었다. 그래서 때로는 장에 혼자 갔어도, 그녀는 늘 해 질 녘이면 동무들을 찾아, 집으로 돌아가는 길에 함께라는 보호를 받으려 했다."]], [["This had gone on for a month or two when there came a Saturday in September, on which a fair and a market coincided; and the pilgrims from Trantridge sought double delights at the inns on that account.", "이런 일이 한두 달 이어지던 어느 구월 토요일, 장날과 시장이 겹쳤다. 그래서 트랜트리지에서 온 순례자들은 그날 여관에서 갑절의 즐거움을 찾았다."], ["Tess’s occupations made her late in setting out, so that her comrades reached the town long before her.", "테스는 일 때문에 늦게 출발해, 동무들이 그녀보다 훨씬 먼저 마을에 닿았다."], ["It was a fine September evening, just before sunset, when yellow lights struggle with blue shades in hairlike lines, and the atmosphere itself forms a prospect without aid from more solid objects, except the innumerable winged insects that dance in it.", "해 지기 직전의 화창한 구월 저녁이었다. 노란 빛과 푸른 그늘이 머리카락처럼 가는 선을 이루며 다투고, 그 안에서 춤추는 무수한 날벌레 말고는 더 단단한 사물의 도움 없이도 대기 그 자체가 하나의 풍경을 이루는 그런 때였다."], ["Through this low-lit mistiness Tess walked leisurely along.", "이 어슴푸레한 안개 속을 테스는 느긋이 걸어갔다."]], [["She did not discover the coincidence of the market with the fair till she had reached the place, by which time it was close upon dusk.", "그녀는 마을에 닿고서야 장날과 시장이 겹쳤음을 알았는데, 그때는 이미 어스름이 짙어 가던 무렵이었다."], ["Her limited marketing was soon completed; and then as usual she began to look about for some of the Trantridge cottagers.", "변변찮은 장보기는 곧 끝났다. 그러고는 여느 때처럼 트랜트리지 사람 몇몇을 찾아 둘러보기 시작했다."]], [["At first she could not find them, and she was informed that most of them had gone to what they called a private little jig at the house of a hay-trusser and peat-dealer who had transactions with their farm.", "처음에는 그들을 찾지 못했는데, 대부분 자기네 농장과 거래하는 건초 다발 만드는 사람 겸 토탄 장수의 집에서 벌인다는, 이른바 끼리끼리의 작은 춤판에 갔다는 말을 들었다."], ["He lived in an out-of-the-way nook of the townlet, and in trying to find her course thither her eyes fell upon Mr d’Urberville standing at a street corner.", "그는 읍내 외진 구석에 살았고, 그리로 가는 길을 찾으려다 그녀의 눈에 길모퉁이에 서 있는 더버빌 씨가 들어왔다."]], [["“What—my Beauty?", "\"이런—나의 미녀?"], ["You here so late?”", "이렇게 늦게 여기 있다니?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["She told him that she was simply waiting for company homeward.", "그녀는 그저 함께 돌아갈 일행을 기다리는 중이라고 말했다."]], [["“I’ll see you again,”", "\"또 보자고.\""], ["said he over her shoulder as she went on down the back lane.", "그녀가 뒷골목으로 걸어 내려가는 동안 그가 어깨 너머로 말했다."]], [["Approaching the hay-trussers, she could hear the fiddled notes of a reel proceeding from some building in the rear; but no sound of dancing was audible—an exceptional state of things for these parts, where as a rule the stamping drowned the music.", "건초 다발 만드는 집에 다가서니 뒤편 어느 건물에서 흘러나오는 릴 가락의 바이올린 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 춤추는 소리는 들리지 않았다—발 구르는 소리가 으레 음악을 삼켜 버리는 이 고장에서는 보기 드문 일이었다."], ["The front door being open she could see straight through the house into the garden at the back as far as the shades of night would allow; and nobody appearing to her knock, she traversed the dwelling and went up the path to the outhouse whence the sound had attracted her.", "앞문이 열려 있어 밤 그림자가 허락하는 데까지 집을 곧장 가로질러 뒤편 정원까지 들여다보였다. 두드려도 아무도 나오지 않자, 그녀는 집을 가로질러 소리가 그녀를 끌어당긴 헛간으로 가는 길을 올라갔다."]], [["It was a windowless erection used for storage, and from the open door there floated into the obscurity a mist of yellow radiance, which at first Tess thought to be illuminated smoke.", "그것은 저장용으로 쓰는 창문 없는 건물이었고, 열린 문에서 노란빛의 안개가 어둠 속으로 떠 나왔다. 처음에 테스는 빛을 받은 연기인 줄 알았다."], ["But on drawing nearer she perceived that it was a cloud of dust, lit by candles within the outhouse, whose beams upon the haze carried forward the outline of the doorway into the wide night of the garden.", "그러나 가까이 다가가 보니 그것은 헛간 안의 촛불에 비친 먼지구름이었다. 그 빛줄기가 자욱한 안개 위로 문간의 윤곽을 정원의 너른 밤 속까지 밀어 내고 있었다."]], [["When she came close and looked in she beheld indistinct forms racing up and down to the figure of the dance, the silence of their footfalls arising from their being overshoe in “scroff”—that is to say, the powdery residuum from the storage of peat and other products, the stirring of which by their turbulent feet created the nebulosity that involved the scene.", "바짝 다가가 안을 들여다보니, 춤사위에 맞춰 위아래로 내달리는 어렴풋한 형체들이 보였는데, 발소리가 잠잠한 것은 그들이 \"스크로프\"에 발목까지 빠져 있는 탓이었다. 곧 토탄을 비롯한 산물들을 저장하면서 생긴 가루 찌꺼기였는데, 어지러이 구르는 그들의 발이 이것을 일으켜 그 광경을 감싼 안개를 만들어 냈다."], ["Through this floating, fusty _débris_ of peat and hay, mixed with the perspirations and warmth of the dancers, and forming together a sort of vegeto-human pollen, the muted fiddles feebly pushed their notes, in marked contrast to the spirit with which the measure was trodden out.", "토탄과 건초의 떠다니는 이 곰팡내 나는 부스러기에, 춤꾼들의 땀과 열기가 뒤섞여 일종의 식물 인간 화분을 이루었고, 그 사이로 음 죽인 바이올린들이 가락을 가냘프게 밀어냈다. 박자를 밟아 대는 그 기세와는 사뭇 대조를 이루었다."], ["They coughed as they danced, and laughed as they coughed.", "그들은 춤추다 기침했고, 기침하다 웃었다."], ["Of the rushing couples there could barely be discerned more than the high lights—the indistinctness shaping them to satyrs clasping nymphs—a multiplicity of Pans whirling a multiplicity of Syrinxes; Lotis attempting to elude Priapus, and always failing.", "내달리는 짝들에게서는 도드라진 빛점 말고는 거의 분간이 안 됐다—그 흐릿함이 그들을 요정을 끌어안은 사티로스로, 무수한 시링크스를 빙빙 돌리는 무수한 판으로 빚어냈다. 프리아포스를 피하려 애쓰다 번번이 실패하는 로티스로."]], [["At intervals a couple would approach the doorway for air, and the haze no longer veiling their features, the demigods resolved themselves into the homely personalities of her own next-door neighbours.", "이따금 한 쌍이 바람을 쐬러 문간으로 다가오면, 더는 그들의 이목구비를 가리지 못한 안개 탓에 반신반인들이 그녀의 바로 이웃들이라는 소박한 인격으로 풀려났다."], ["Could Trantridge in two or three short hours have metamorphosed itself thus madly!", "트랜트리지가 두세 시간 안에 이토록 미쳐 날뛰게 탈바꿈할 수 있었더란 말인가!"]], [["Some Sileni of the throng sat on benches and hay-trusses by the wall; and one of them recognized her.", "무리 가운데 실레노스 같은 자들 몇몇이 벽가의 긴 의자와 건초 다발에 앉아 있었고, 그중 하나가 그녀를 알아보았다."]], [["“The maids don’t think it respectable to dance at The Flower-de-Luce,”", "\"처녀들은 플라워 드 루스에서 춤추는 걸 점잖지 못하다고들 여기지.\""], ["he explained.", "그가 설명했다."], ["“They don’t like to let everybody see which be their fancy-men.", "\"누가 제 정인情人인지 다들 보게 하기 싫은 거야."], ["Besides, the house sometimes shuts up just when their jints begin to get greased.", "게다가 그 집은 한창 흥이 올라 관절에 기름칠이 도는 판에 문을 닫아 버리기도 하거든."], ["So we come here and send out for liquor.”", "그래서 우리는 여기 와서 술을 시켜 오는 거지.\""]], [["“But when be any of you going home?”", "\"그런데 그쪽들 중에 집에 가는 사람은 언제 가나요?\""], ["asked Tess with some anxiety.", "테스가 다소 불안해하며 물었다."]], [["“Now—a’most directly.", "\"지금—거의 곧장."], ["This is all but the last jig.”", "이게 거의 마지막 춤이야.\""]], [["She waited.", "그녀는 기다렸다."], ["The reel drew to a close, and some of the party were in the mind of starting.", "릴이 끝나 가자 일행 중 더러는 떠날 마음을 먹었다. 그러나 다른 이들은 가려 하지 않았고, 또 한 차례 춤판이 짜였다."], ["But others would not, and another dance was formed.", "이것으로 분명 끝나겠지, 테스는 생각했다."], ["This surely would end it, thought Tess.", "그러나 그것이 또 다른 춤으로 이어졌다."], ["But it merged in yet another.", "그녀는 안절부절못하고 불안해졌다."], ["She became restless and uneasy; yet, having waited so long, it was necessary to wait longer; on account of the fair the roads were dotted with roving characters of possibly ill intent; and, though not fearful of measurable dangers, she feared the unknown.", "그러나 이토록 오래 기다린 마당이라 더 기다릴 수밖에 없었다. 장날인지라 길마다 어쩌면 흉계를 품었을 떠돌이들이 흩어져 있었다. 가늠할 수 있는 위험은 두렵지 않았지만, 그녀는 알 수 없는 것이 두려웠다."], ["Had she been near Marlott she would have had less dread.", "말럿 가까이였다면 두려움이 덜했을 것이다."]], [["“Don’t ye be nervous, my dear good soul,”", "\"긴장 풀어, 마음씨 고운 아가씨.\""], ["expostulated, between his coughs, a young man with a wet face and his straw hat so far back upon his head that the brim encircled it like the nimbus of a saint.", "얼굴이 땀에 젖고 밀짚모자를 어찌나 뒤로 젖혀 썼는지 챙이 성인의 후광처럼 머리를 두른 한 젊은이가 기침 사이사이 타일렀다."], ["“What’s yer hurry?", "\"뭐가 그리 급해?"], ["To-morrow is Sunday, thank God, and we can sleep it off in church-time.", "내일은 일요일이잖아, 고맙게도, 예배 시간 동안 자면서 술기운 풀면 되지."], ["Now, have a turn with me?”", "자, 나랑 한 바퀴 출까?\""]], [["She did not abhor dancing, but she was not going to dance here.", "그녀는 춤을 싫어하지는 않았으나, 여기서 출 생각은 없었다."], ["The movement grew more passionate: the fiddlers behind the luminous pillar of cloud now and then varied the air by playing on the wrong side of the bridge or with the back of the bow.", "춤사위가 더 격해졌다. 빛나는 구름 기둥 뒤의 바이올린 잡이들이 이따금 줄받침 엉뚱한 쪽을 켜거나 활 등으로 켜서 가락을 바꿔 놓았다. 그래도 상관없었다."], ["But it did not matter; the panting shapes spun onwards.", "헐떡이는 형체들은 계속 빙빙 돌았다."]], [["They did not vary their partners if their inclination were to stick to previous ones.", "이전 짝을 고수하고픈 마음이면 그들은 짝을 바꾸지 않았다."], ["Changing partners simply meant that a satisfactory choice had not as yet been arrived at by one or other of the pair, and by this time every couple had been suitably matched.", "짝을 바꾼다는 건 둘 중 누군가가 아직 흡족한 선택에 이르지 못했다는 뜻일 뿐이었고, 이쯤 되니 짝마다 알맞게 맞춰진 상태였다."], ["It was then that the ecstasy and the dream began, in which emotion was the matter of the universe, and matter but an adventitious intrusion likely to hinder you from spinning where you wanted to spin.", "그제야 황홀경과 꿈이 시작되었다. 그 안에서는 감정이 우주의 본질이었고, 물질이란 네가 돌고 싶은 자리에서 도는 걸 방해할 법한 군더더기 침입에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Suddenly there was a dull thump on the ground: a couple had fallen, and lay in a mixed heap.", "별안간 바닥에서 둔탁하게 쿵 하는 소리가 났다. 한 쌍이 쓰러져 한 무더기로 뒤엉켜 누웠다."], ["The next couple, unable to check its progress, came toppling over the obstacle.", "다음 짝은 멈추지 못하고 그 장애물에 걸려 고꾸라졌다."], ["An inner cloud of dust rose around the prostrate figures amid the general one of the room, in which a twitching entanglement of arms and legs was discernible.", "엎어진 형체들 둘레로, 방 안에 자욱한 먼지 속에서 또 안쪽의 먼지구름이 일었고, 그 속에 버둥대며 뒤엉킨 팔다리가 어렴풋이 분간되었다."]], [["“You shall catch it for this, my gentleman, when you get home!”", "\"집에 가면 이걸로 혼날 줄 알아, 이 양반아!\""], ["burst in female accents from the human heap—those of the unhappy partner of the man whose clumsiness had caused the mishap; she happened also to be his recently married wife, in which assortment there was nothing unusual at Trantridge as long as any affection remained between wedded couples; and, indeed, it was not uncustomary in their later lives, to avoid making odd lots of the single people between whom there might be a warm understanding.", "그 인간 무더기에서 여자 목소리가 터져 나왔다—제 짝의 서투름이 사달을 낸 그 불운한 여자의 목소리였다. 그녀는 마침 그의 갓 혼인한 아내이기도 했는데, 트랜트리지에서는 부부 사이에 얼마간 애정이 남아 있는 한 그런 짝짓기에 이상할 게 없었다. 게다가 그들의 후일에도, 따뜻한 정이 통할 법한 독신들 사이를 어긋난 짝으로 만들지 않으려고 그러는 일이 드물지 않았다."]], [["A loud laugh from behind Tess’s back, in the shade of the garden, united with the titter within the room.", "테스 등 뒤, 정원 그늘에서 큰 웃음소리가 터져 방 안의 키득거림과 어우러졌다."], ["She looked round, and saw the red coal of a cigar: Alec d’Urberville was standing there alone.", "그녀는 돌아보았고, 시가의 빨간 불씨를 보았다. 알렉 더버빌이 홀로 거기 서 있었다."], ["He beckoned to her, and she reluctantly retreated towards him.", "그가 그녀에게 손짓했고, 그녀는 마지못해 그쪽으로 물러났다."]], [["“Well, my Beauty, what are you doing here?”", "\"그래, 나의 미녀, 여기서 뭘 하고 있지?\""]], [["She was so tired after her long day and her walk that she confided her trouble to him—that she had been waiting ever since he saw her to have their company home, because the road at night was strange to her.", "긴 하루와 걷기에 어찌나 지쳤던지 그녀는 그에게 고민을 털어놓았다—그가 그녀를 본 뒤로 줄곧, 함께 돌아갈 일행을 기다려 왔다고, 밤길이 낯선 까닭이라고."], ["“But it seems they will never leave off, and I really think I will wait no longer.”", "\"그런데 저 사람들은 도무지 그만둘 줄을 모르네요. 정말이지 더는 못 기다리겠어요.\""]], [["“Certainly do not.", "\"그럼 기다리지 마."], ["I have only a saddle-horse here to-day; but come to The Flower-de-Luce, and I’ll hire a trap, and drive you home with me.”", "오늘은 안장 얹은 말 한 마리밖에 안 끌고 왔지만, 플라워 드 루스로 와. 마차 한 대 빌려서 내가 집에 데려다줄게.\""]], [["Tess, though flattered, had never quite got over her original mistrust of him, and, despite their tardiness, she preferred to walk home with the work-folk.", "테스는 우쭐하긴 했어도 그에 대한 처음의 불신을 끝내 다 떨치지 못한 데다, 더디긴 해도 일꾼들과 함께 걸어가는 편이 나았다."], ["So she answered that she was much obliged to him, but would not trouble him.", "그래서 그녀는 무척 고맙지만 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않다고 답했다."], ["“I have said that I will wait for ’em, and they will expect me to now.”", "\"저 사람들을 기다리겠다고 했으니, 이제 저를 기다릴 거예요.\""]], [["“Very well, Miss Independence.", "\"좋아, 독립심 강한 아가씨."], ["Please yourself...", "마음대로 해……"], ["Then I shall not hurry...", "그럼 나도 서두르지 않겠어……"], ["My good Lord, what a kick-up they are having there!”", "어이쿠, 저기서 한바탕 야단법석이로구나!\""]], [["He had not put himself forward into the light, but some of them had perceived him, and his presence led to a slight pause and a consideration of how the time was flying.", "그는 빛 속으로 나서지 않았으나, 그들 중 몇몇이 그를 알아보았고, 그의 존재 탓에 잠시 멈칫하며 시간이 얼마나 흘러가는지 헤아리게 되었다."], ["As soon as he had re-lit a cigar and walked away the Trantridge people began to collect themselves from amid those who had come in from other farms, and prepared to leave in a body.", "그가 시가에 다시 불을 붙이고 걸어가 버리자마자, 트랜트리지 사람들은 다른 농장에서 온 이들 사이에서 자기들끼리 모이기 시작했고, 무리 지어 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["Their bundles and baskets were gathered up, and half an hour later, when the clock-chime sounded a quarter past eleven, they were straggling along the lane which led up the hill towards their homes.", "보따리와 바구니를 챙겨 들었고, 삼십 분 뒤 종소리가 열한 시 십오 분을 알릴 무렵, 그들은 집을 향해 언덕을 오르는 오솔길을 따라 흩어진 채 걸어가고 있었다."]], [["It was a three-mile walk, along a dry white road, made whiter to-night by the light of the moon.", "마른 흰 길을 따라 삼 마일 걷는 길이었는데, 오늘 밤은 달빛에 한층 더 희게 보였다."]], [["Tess soon perceived as she walked in the flock, sometimes with this one, sometimes with that, that the fresh night air was producing staggerings and serpentine courses among the men who had partaken too freely; some of the more careless women also were wandering in their gait—to wit, a dark virago, Car Darch, dubbed Queen of Spades, till lately a favourite of d’Urberville’s; Nancy, her sister, nicknamed the Queen of Diamonds; and the young married woman who had already tumbled down.", "무리에 섞여, 이 사람과 저 사람을 오가며 걷던 테스는 곧 알아챘다. 서늘한 밤공기가 술을 너무 많이 마신 사내들 사이에 비틀거림과 뱀처럼 구불대는 갈지자 걸음을 만들어 내고 있음을. 더 무모한 여자들 몇몇도 걸음이 갈팡질팡했다—말하자면 검은 표독 여인, 스페이드 여왕이라 불리던 카 다치, 얼마 전까지 더버빌의 총애를 받던 여자였다."], ["Yet however terrestrial and lumpy their appearance just now to the mean unglamoured eye, to themselves the case was different.", "그 동생 낸시는 다이아몬드 여왕이라는 별명이 붙었고, 아까 이미 나동그라졌던 갓 혼인한 여자도 있었다."], ["They followed the road with a sensation that they were soaring along in a supporting medium, possessed of original and profound thoughts, themselves and surrounding nature forming an organism of which all the parts harmoniously and joyously interpenetrated each other.", "그러나 미혹의 빛이 벗겨진 천한 눈에 지금 그들이 아무리 땅에 매인 덩어리처럼 보일지언정, 그들 자신에게는 사정이 달랐다. 그들은 자기들을 떠받치는 어떤 매질媒質 속에서 떠가는 듯한 감각으로 길을 따랐고, 독창적이고 심오한 사상으로 가득 차, 자기 자신과 둘레의 자연이 모든 부분이 조화롭고 기쁘게 서로 스며드는 하나의 유기체를 이룬다고 느꼈다."], ["They were as sublime as the moon and stars above them, and the moon and stars were as ardent as they.", "그들은 머리 위의 달과 별만큼 숭고했고, 달과 별은 그들만큼 열렬했다."]], [["Tess, however, had undergone such painful experiences of this kind in her father’s house that the discovery of their condition spoilt the pleasure she was beginning to feel in the moonlight journey.", "그러나 테스는 아버지의 집에서 이런 종류의 고통스러운 경험을 숱하게 겪은 터라, 그들의 상태를 알아챈 일이 달빛 여정에서 막 느끼기 시작한 즐거움을 잡쳐 놓았다."], ["Yet she stuck to the party, for reasons above given.", "그래도 앞서 말한 이유로 그녀는 무리에 붙어 있었다."]], [["In the open highway they had progressed in scattered order; but now their route was through a field-gate, and the foremost finding a difficulty in opening it, they closed up together.", "탁 트인 큰길에서는 흩어진 채 걸었으나, 이제 길이 밭 출입문을 지나게 되었고, 맨 앞에 선 이가 문을 여는 데 애를 먹자 다들 한데 모여들었다."]], [["This leading pedestrian was Car the Queen of Spades, who carried a wicker-basket containing her mother’s groceries, her own draperies, and other purchases for the week.", "이 선두에 선 보행자가 스페이드 여왕 카였는데, 그녀는 어머니의 식료품과 제 옷가지, 그리고 한 주 동안 사들인 다른 물건들이 든 고리버들 바구니를 이고 있었다."], ["The basket being large and heavy, Car had placed it for convenience of porterage on the top of her head, where it rode on in jeopardized balance as she walked with arms akimbo.", "바구니가 크고 무거워, 카는 나르기 편하라고 그것을 머리 꼭대기에 얹었는데, 그녀가 양손을 허리에 짚고 걷는 동안 바구니는 위태로운 균형으로 흔들거리며 실려 갔다."]], [["“Well—whatever is that a-creeping down thy back, Car Darch?”", "\"아니—카 다치, 네 등을 타고 내려오는 저게 도대체 뭐야?\""], ["said one of the group suddenly.", "무리 중 하나가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["All looked at Car.", "다들 카를 보았다."], ["Her gown was a light cotton print, and from the back of her head a kind of rope could be seen descending to some distance below her waist, like a Chinaman’s queue.", "그녀의 옷은 얇은 무명 날염천이었는데, 뒤통수에서부터 일종의 밧줄 같은 것이 허리 한참 아래까지 내려오는 게 보였다, 마치 중국인의 변발처럼."]], [["“’Tis her hair falling down,” said another.", "\"머리카락이 흘러내린 거야.\" 또 한 사람이 말했다."]], [["No; it was not her hair: it was a black stream of something oozing from her basket, and it glistened like a slimy snake in the cold still rays of the moon.", "아니다. 머리카락이 아니었다. 그것은 그녀의 바구니에서 새 나오는 검은 줄기였고, 차고 고요한 달빛 속에서 끈적한 뱀처럼 번들거렸다."]], [["“’Tis treacle,”", "\"당밀이야.\""], ["said an observant matron.", "눈썰미 좋은 아낙이 말했다."]], [["Treacle it was.", "당밀이었다."], ["Car’s poor old grandmother had a weakness for the sweet stuff.", "카의 가엾은 노할머니는 단것을 무척 좋아했다."], ["Honey she had in plenty out of her own hives, but treacle was what her soul desired, and Car had been about to give her a treat of surprise.", "제 벌통에서 꿀은 넘치게 났지만, 영혼이 갈망하는 건 당밀이었고, 카는 깜짝 선물로 그걸 대접하려던 참이었다."], ["Hastily lowering the basket the dark girl found that the vessel containing the syrup had been smashed within.", "검은 처녀가 황급히 바구니를 내려 보니, 시럽이 든 그릇이 안에서 박살나 있었다."]], [["By this time there had arisen a shout of laughter at the extraordinary appearance of Car’s back, which irritated the dark queen into getting rid of the disfigurement by the first sudden means available, and independently of the help of the scoffers.", "이쯤 되자 카의 등의 괴이한 모양새에 폭소가 터졌고, 그것에 부아가 치민 검은 여왕은, 비웃는 자들의 도움 따위는 마다하고, 손에 잡히는 첫 수단으로 그 흉한 꼴을 없애려 했다."], ["She rushed excitedly into the field they were about to cross, and flinging herself flat on her back upon the grass, began to wipe her gown as well as she could by spinning horizontally on the herbage and dragging herself over it upon her elbows.", "그녀는 흥분해 일행이 막 건너려던 밭으로 뛰어들더니, 풀밭 위에 벌렁 자빠져, 풀밭 위에서 수평으로 빙글빙글 돌고 팔꿈치로 몸을 끌며 옷을 닦을 수 있는 대로 닦기 시작했다."]], [["The laughter rang louder; they clung to the gate, to the posts, rested on their staves, in the weakness engendered by their convulsions at the spectacle of Car.", "웃음소리가 더 크게 울렸다. 그들은 카의 광경에 발작하듯 웃다가 기운이 빠져, 문에, 기둥에 매달리고 지팡이에 몸을 기댔다."], ["Our heroine, who had hitherto held her peace, at this wild moment could not help joining in with the rest.", "그때까지 잠자코 있던 우리의 여주인공도, 이 거친 순간에는 다른 이들과 어울려 웃지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["It was a misfortune—in more ways than one.", "그것은 불운이었다—여러모로."], ["No sooner did the dark queen hear the soberer richer note of Tess among those of the other work-people than a long-smouldering sense of rivalry inflamed her to madness.", "검은 여왕은 다른 일꾼들의 목소리 가운데 섞인 테스의 더 차분하고 풍성한 음색을 듣자마자, 오래 묵어 있던 경쟁심에 불이 붙어 미칠 지경이 되었다."], ["She sprang to her feet and closely faced the object of her dislike.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 제가 싫어하는 대상을 정면으로 마주했다."]], [["“How darest th’ laugh at me, hussy!”", "\"어디서 감히 날 비웃어, 이 계집애야!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 소리쳤다."]], [["“I couldn’t really help it when t’others did,”", "\"다들 웃으니까 정말 어쩔 수가 없었어요.\""], ["apologized Tess, still tittering.", "테스가 여전히 키득거리며 사과했다."]], [["“Ah, th’st think th’ beest everybody, dostn’t, because th’ beest first favourite with He just now!", "\"흥, 네가 무슨 대단한 줄 알지, 안 그래, 지금 그 양반 첫째 총애 받는 몸이라고!"], ["But stop a bit, my lady, stop a bit!", "하지만 어디 두고 봐, 아가씨, 두고 봐!"], ["I’m as good as two of such! Look here—here’s at ’ee!”", "난 너 같은 거 둘 몫은 거뜬해! 자 봐라—덤벼!\""]], [["To Tess’s horror the dark queen began stripping off the bodice of her gown—which for the added reason of its ridiculed condition she was only too glad to be free of—till she had bared her plump neck, shoulders, and arms to the moonshine, under which they looked as luminous and beautiful as some Praxitelean creation, in their possession of the faultless rotundities of a lusty country-girl.", "테스가 질겁한 가운데 검은 여왕은 옷의 상의를 벗어 던지기 시작했다—비웃음거리가 된 그 꼴 때문에라도 어차피 벗어 버리고 싶던 차였다—마침내 통통한 목과 어깨, 팔을 달빛에 드러냈고, 그 아래 그것들은 건장한 시골 처녀가 지닌 흠 없이 둥근 살집으로, 프락시텔레스의 작품처럼 환하고 아름다워 보였다."], ["She closed her fists and squared up at Tess.", "그녀는 주먹을 쥐고 테스를 향해 자세를 잡았다."]], [["“Indeed, then, I shall not fight!”", "\"그럼 정말이지, 난 싸우지 않겠어!\""], ["said the latter majestically; “and if I had known you was of that sort, I wouldn’t have so let myself down as to come with such a whorage as this is!”", "후자가 위엄 있게 말했다. \"당신이 그런 부류인 줄 알았더라면, 이런 잡것들과 어울려 올 만큼 내 몸을 낮추지도 않았을 거야!\""]], [["The rather too inclusive speech brought down a torrent of vituperation from other quarters upon fair Tess’s unlucky head, particularly from the Queen of Diamonds, who having stood in the relations to d’Urberville that Car had also been suspected of, united with the latter against the common enemy.", "다소 싸잡아 한 그 말은, 다른 쪽들에서 고운 테스의 운 나쁜 머리 위로 욕설의 폭우를 쏟아지게 했다. 특히 다이아몬드 여왕이 그러했는데, 그녀 역시 카가 의심받던 것과 같은 관계로 더버빌과 얽혀 있던지라, 공동의 적에 맞서 카와 한편이 되었다."], ["Several other women also chimed in, with an animus which none of them would have been so fatuous as to show but for the rollicking evening they had passed.", "다른 여자 몇몇도 끼어들어 적의를 드러냈는데, 흥겹게 보낸 저녁이 아니었다면 그토록 어리석게 굴 이들은 아무도 없었을 것이다."], ["Thereupon, finding Tess unfairly browbeaten, the husbands and lovers tried to make peace by defending her; but the result of that attempt was directly to increase the war.", "그러자 테스가 부당하게 윽박질림을 당하는 걸 보고, 남편들과 정인들이 그녀를 두둔하며 화해를 붙이려 했다. 그러나 그 시도의 결과는 곧장 싸움을 키우는 것이었다."]], [["Tess was indignant and ashamed.", "테스는 분하고 부끄러웠다."], ["She no longer minded the loneliness of the way and the lateness of the hour; her one object was to get away from the whole crew as soon as possible.", "이제는 길의 외로움도, 시간이 늦은 것도 개의치 않았다. 그녀의 유일한 목표는 되도록 빨리 이 무리 전체에서 벗어나는 것이었다."], ["She knew well enough that the better among them would repent of their passion next day.", "그들 중 나은 자들은 이튿날이면 제 격분을 후회하리라는 걸 그녀는 잘 알았다."], ["They were all now inside the field, and she was edging back to rush off alone when a horseman emerged almost silently from the corner of the hedge that screened the road, and Alec d’Urberville looked round upon them.", "이제 모두 밭 안에 들어와 있었고, 그녀가 혼자 내달으려 뒤로 슬금슬금 물러서던 차에, 길을 가린 산울타리 모퉁이에서 거의 소리 없이 말 탄 자 하나가 나타났고, 알렉 더버빌이 그들을 둘러보았다."]], [["“What the devil is all this row about, work-folk?” he asked.", "\"이게 다 무슨 소란이야, 일꾼들?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["The explanation was not readily forthcoming; and, in truth, he did not require any.", "설명이 선뜻 나오지는 않았다. 사실 그는 설명이 필요하지도 않았다."], ["Having heard their voices while yet some way off he had ridden creepingly forward, and learnt enough to satisfy himself.", "아직 멀리서부터 그들의 목소리를 들은 터라 살금살금 말을 몰아 다가왔고, 흡족할 만큼은 이미 알아챈 뒤였다."]], [["Tess was standing apart from the rest, near the gate.", "테스는 다른 이들과 떨어져, 문 가까이 서 있었다."], ["He bent over towards her.", "그가 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["“Jump up behind me,”", "\"내 뒤에 올라타.\""], ["he whispered, “and we’ll get shot of the screaming cats in a jiffy!”", "그가 속삭였다. \"그러면 저 빽빽대는 고양이들한테서 눈 깜짝할 새에 벗어날 수 있어!\""]], [["She felt almost ready to faint, so vivid was her sense of the crisis.", "위기감이 어찌나 생생했던지 그녀는 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다."], ["At almost any other moment of her life she would have refused such proffered aid and company, as she had refused them several times before; and now the loneliness would not of itself have forced her to do otherwise.", "인생의 다른 거의 어느 순간이었다면, 앞서 여러 번 그랬듯 이렇게 내미는 도움과 동행을 거절했을 것이다. 그리고 지금도 외로움만으로는 달리 행동하게 만들지 못했을 것이다."], ["But coming as the invitation did at the particular juncture when fear and indignation at these adversaries could be transformed by a spring of the foot into a triumph over them, she abandoned herself to her impulse, climbed the gate, put her toe upon his instep, and scrambled into the saddle behind him.", "그러나 그 청이 하필, 이 적들에 대한 두려움과 분노가 발을 한 번 굴러 그들에 대한 승리로 바뀔 수 있는 그 순간에 온지라, 그녀는 충동에 몸을 내맡겨, 문을 넘어, 그의 발등에 발끝을 디디고, 그의 뒤 안장으로 기어올랐다."], ["The pair were speeding away into the distant gray by the time that the contentious revellers became aware of what had happened.", "다투던 흥청대는 무리가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알아챘을 때, 두 사람은 이미 멀리 잿빛 속으로 내달리고 있었다."]], [["The Queen of Spades forgot the stain on her bodice, and stood beside the Queen of Diamonds and the new-married, staggering young woman—all with a gaze of fixity in the direction in which the horse’s tramp was diminishing into silence on the road.", "스페이드 여왕은 제 상의의 얼룩도 잊은 채, 다이아몬드 여왕과 갓 혼인한 비틀거리는 젊은 여자 곁에 섰다—모두 말발굽 소리가 길에서 잦아들어 침묵으로 사라져 가는 쪽을 못 박힌 듯 응시하면서."]], [["“What be ye looking at?”", "\"다들 뭘 보는 거야?\""], ["asked a man who had not observed the incident.", "그 일을 못 본 사내 하나가 물었다."]], [["“Ho-ho-ho!”", "\"호—호—호!\""], ["laughed dark Car.", "검은 카가 웃었다."]], [["“Hee-hee-hee!”", "\"히—히—히!\""], ["laughed the tippling bride, as she steadied herself on the arm of her fond husband.", "술 취한 신부가 다정한 남편 팔에 의지해 몸을 가누며 웃었다."]], [["“Heu-heu-heu!”", "\"흐—흐—흐!\""], ["laughed dark Car’s mother, stroking her moustache as she explained laconically: “Out of the frying-pan into the fire!”", "검은 카의 어머니가 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 짤막하게 설명하듯 웃었다. \"냄비에서 뛰쳐나와 불 속으로 들어갔구먼!\""]], [["Then these children of the open air, whom even excess of alcohol could scarce injure permanently, betook themselves to the field-path; and as they went there moved onward with them, around the shadow of each one’s head, a circle of opalized light, formed by the moon’s rays upon the glistening sheet of dew.", "그러고는 알코올의 과음으로도 좀처럼 영영 망가뜨릴 수 없는 이 한데서 자란 아이들은 밭길로 들어섰다. 그들이 걸어가는 동안, 저마다의 머리 그림자 둘레로, 이슬에 번들거리는 장막 위에 달빛이 만들어 낸 유백색 빛의 둥근 테가 함께 옮겨 갔다."], ["Each pedestrian could see no halo but his or her own, which never deserted the head-shadow, whatever its vulgar unsteadiness might be; but adhered to it, and persistently beautified it; till the erratic motions seemed an inherent part of the irradiation, and the fumes of their breathing a component of the night’s mist; and the spirit of the scene, and of the moonlight, and of Nature, seemed harmoniously to mingle with the spirit of wine.", "보행자 저마다 제 것 말고는 어떤 후광도 볼 수 없었는데, 그 후광은 천하게 비틀거리든 어떻든 머리 그림자를 결코 떠나지 않았고, 거기에 들러붙어 끈질기게 그것을 아름답게 꾸몄다. 마침내 그 갈팡질팡하는 움직임이 빛 발산의 타고난 한 부분처럼 보였고, 그들 숨결의 김이 밤안개의 한 성분처럼 보였다. 그리하여 그 광경의 정령과, 달빛의 정령과, 자연의 정령이, 술의 정령과 조화롭게 어우러지는 듯했다."]], [["# XI", "# XI"]], [["The twain cantered along for some time without speech, Tess as she clung to him still panting in her triumph, yet in other respects dubious.", "두 사람은 한동안 말없이 가볍게 말을 몰았다. 테스는 그에게 매달린 채 여전히 승리에 헐떡였으나, 다른 면에서는 미심쩍었다."], ["She had perceived that the horse was not the spirited one he sometimes rose, and felt no alarm on that score, though her seat was precarious enough despite her tight hold of him.", "그녀는 그 말이 그가 이따금 올라타던 사나운 놈이 아님을 알아챘고, 그 점에서는 불안하지 않았다. 비록 그를 단단히 붙들었어도 그녀의 자리는 위태로웠지만."], ["She begged him to slow the animal to a walk, which Alec accordingly did.", "그녀는 짐승을 걸음 속도로 늦춰 달라고 부탁했고, 알렉도 그에 따랐다."]], [["“Neatly done, was it not, dear Tess?”", "\"산뜻하게 해치웠지, 안 그래, 테스?\""], ["he said by and by.", "그가 이윽고 말했다."]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["said she. “I am sure I ought to be much obliged to you.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"정말 고맙다고 인사해야겠죠.\""]], [["“And are you?”", "\"정말 그래?\""]], [["She did not reply.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Tess, why do you always dislike my kissing you?”", "\"테스, 넌 왜 늘 내가 입 맞추는 걸 싫어하지?\""]], [["“I suppose—because I don’t love you.”", "\"아마—당신을 사랑하지 않아서겠죠.\""]], [["“You are quite sure?”", "\"정말 확실해?\""]], [["“I am angry with you sometimes!”", "\"가끔은 당신한테 화도 나요!\""]], [["“Ah, I half feared as much.” Nevertheless, Alec did not object to that confession.", "\"아, 어쩐지 그럴까 봐 반쯤 두려웠어.\" 그렇지만 알렉은 그 고백에 토를 달지 않았다."], ["He knew that anything was better then frigidity.", "그는 무엇이든 냉담함보다는 낫다는 걸 알았다."], ["“Why haven’t you told me when I have made you angry?”", "\"내가 화나게 했을 때 왜 말하지 않았어?\""]], [["“You know very well why.", "\"이유야 잘 알잖아요."], ["Because I cannot help myself here.”", "여기서는 어쩔 도리가 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I haven’t offended you often by love-making?”", "\"내 구애로 너를 그렇게 자주 언짢게 한 건 아니지?\""]], [["“You have sometimes.”", "\"가끔은 그랬어요.\""]], [["“How many times?”", "\"몇 번이나?\""]], [["“You know as well as I—too many times.”", "\"당신도 나만큼 알잖아요—너무 여러 번.\""]], [["“Every time I have tried?”", "\"내가 시도할 때마다?\""]], [["She was silent, and the horse ambled along for a considerable distance, till a faint luminous fog, which had hung in the hollows all the evening, became general and enveloped them.", "그녀는 잠잠했고, 말은 한참을 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어, 저녁 내내 우묵한 곳에 깔려 있던 희미하게 빛나는 안개가 사방으로 번져 두 사람을 에워쌌다."], ["It seemed to hold the moonlight in suspension, rendering it more pervasive than in clear air.", "그것은 달빛을 머금어 띄워 놓은 듯해, 맑은 공기에서보다 한층 더 짙게 퍼지게 했다."], ["Whether on this account, or from absent-mindedness, or from sleepiness, she did not perceive that they had long ago passed the point at which the lane to Trantridge branched from the highway, and that her conductor had not taken the Trantridge track.", "그 탓인지, 아니면 멍하니 있어서인지, 졸려서인지, 그녀는 트랜트리지로 가는 오솔길이 큰길에서 갈라지는 지점을 오래전에 지나쳤다는 것도, 길잡이가 트랜트리지 길로 들어서지 않았다는 것도 알아채지 못했다."]], [["She was inexpressibly weary.", "그녀는 말할 수 없이 지쳐 있었다."], ["She had risen at five o’clock every morning of that week, had been on foot the whole of each day, and on this evening had in addition walked the three miles to Chaseborough, waited three hours for her neighbours without eating or drinking, her impatience to start them preventing either; she had then walked a mile of the way home, and had undergone the excitement of the quarrel, till, with the slow progress of their steed, it was now nearly one o’clock.", "그 주 내내 아침 다섯 시에 일어났고, 날마다 온종일 서 있었으며, 오늘 저녁에는 거기에 더해 체이스버러까지 삼 마일을 걸었고, 먹지도 마시지도 못한 채—그들을 어서 출발시키고 싶은 조바심이 먹고 마실 짬을 막은 탓에—이웃들을 세 시간이나 기다렸다. 그러고 나서 집으로 가는 길을 일 마일 걸었고, 그 다툼의 흥분을 겪었으며, 말이 더디게 나아가는 통에 이제 거의 한 시가 다 되었다."], ["Only once, however, was she overcome by actual drowsiness.", "그러나 진짜 졸음에 휩싸인 것은 단 한 번뿐이었다."], ["In that moment of oblivion her head sank gently against him.", "그 망각의 순간에 그녀의 머리가 가만히 그에게 기울어 닿았다."]], [["D’Urberville stopped the horse, withdrew his feet from the stirrups, turned sideways on the saddle, and enclosed her waist with his arm to support her.", "더버빌은 말을 세우고, 등자에서 발을 빼고, 안장 위에서 옆으로 돌아앉아, 그녀를 받치려고 팔로 그녀의 허리를 감쌌다."]], [["This immediately put her on the defensive, and with one of those sudden impulses of reprisal to which she was liable she gave him a little push from her.", "이것은 곧장 그녀를 방어 태세로 돌려놓았고, 그녀에게 곧잘 일던 그 갑작스러운 보복 충동의 하나로 그녀는 그를 자기에게서 살짝 밀쳤다."], ["In his ticklish position he nearly lost his balance and only just avoided rolling over into the road, the horse, though a powerful one, being fortunately the quietest he rode.", "위태로운 자세에 있던 그는 거의 균형을 잃어, 길바닥으로 굴러떨어질 뻔한 것을 간신히 면했다. 다행히 말이 힘이 세긴 했어도 그가 타던 말 중 가장 얌전한 놈이었다."]], [["“That is devilish unkind!”", "\"그건 정말 매정하군!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I mean no harm—only to keep you from falling.”", "\"해코지하려는 게 아니라—그저 네가 떨어지지 않게 하려는 거였어.\""]], [["She pondered suspiciously, till, thinking that this might after all be true, she relented, and said quite humbly, “I beg your pardon, sir.”", "그녀는 미심쩍게 곱씹다가, 어쩌면 결국 그 말이 사실일지도 모른다는 생각에 마음을 누그러뜨려, 자못 겸손하게 말했다. \"죄송해요, 도련님.\""]], [["“I won’t pardon you unless you show some confidence in me.", "\"네가 나한테 어느 정도 믿음을 보이지 않는 한 용서 못 해. 맙소사!\""], ["Good God!” he burst out, “what am I, to be repulsed so by a mere chit like you?", "그가 터뜨렸다. \"내가 뭐길래 너 같은 풋내기한테 이토록 퇴짜를 맞아야 하지?"], ["For near three mortal months have you trifled with my feelings, eluded me, and snubbed me; and I won’t stand it!”", "죽도록 긴 석 달 가까이 너는 내 감정을 가지고 놀고, 나를 피하고, 나를 쌀쌀맞게 굴었어. 더는 못 참아!\""]], [["“I’ll leave you to-morrow, sir.”", "\"내일이면 도련님 곁을 떠날게요.\""]], [["“No, you will not leave me to-morrow!", "\"아니, 넌 내일 날 떠나지 않아!"], ["Will you, I ask once more, show your belief in me by letting me clasp you with my arm?", "한 번 더 묻겠는데, 내 팔로 너를 안게 해 줘서 네 믿음을 보여 주겠어?"], ["Come, between us two and nobody else, now.", "자, 우리 둘 사이의 일, 다른 누구도 없이, 지금."], ["We know each other well; and you know that I love you, and think you the prettiest girl in the world, which you are.", "우린 서로를 잘 알잖아. 그리고 내가 너를 사랑하고, 너를 세상에서 가장 예쁜 처녀로 여긴다는 걸 너도 알잖아, 실제로 그러니까."], ["Mayn’t I treat you as a lover?”", "너를 연인처럼 대하면 안 돼?\""]], [["She drew a quick pettish breath of objection, writhing uneasily on her seat, looked far ahead, and murmured, “I don’t know—I wish—how can I say yes or no when—”", "그녀는 거부하듯 짜증스레 숨을 빨리 들이쉬며, 자리에서 불편하게 몸을 비틀었고, 멀리 앞을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"모르겠어요—저는—언제 그렇다 아니다 말할 수 있겠어요, 그게—\""]], [["He settled the matter by clasping his arm round her as he desired, and Tess expressed no further negative.", "그는 바라던 대로 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안음으로써 그 문제를 매듭지었고, 테스는 더는 부정의 말을 내지 않았다."], ["Thus they sidled slowly onward till it struck her they had been advancing for an unconscionable time—far longer than was usually occupied by the short journey from Chaseborough, even at this walking pace, and that they were no longer on hard road, but in a mere trackway.", "이렇게 둘은 천천히 옆걸음으로 나아갔는데, 문득 그녀는 자기들이 터무니없이 오랫동안—체이스버러에서 오는 짧은 여정이 이런 걸음 속도라 해도 으레 걸리는 시간보다 훨씬 더 오래—나아가고 있었으며, 더는 단단한 길이 아니라 그저 바퀴 자국뿐인 길 위에 있다는 걸 깨달았다."]], [["“Why, where be we?” she exclaimed.", "\"아니, 우리 어디 있는 거예요?\" 그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["“Passing by a wood.”", "\"숲을 지나가는 중이야.\""]], [["“A wood—what wood?", "\"숲이라니—무슨 숲이요?"], ["Surely we are quite out of the road?”", "우리 분명 길에서 한참 벗어났잖아요?\""]], [["“A bit of The Chase—the oldest wood in England.", "\"체이스의 한 자락—잉글랜드에서 가장 오래된 숲이지."], ["It is a lovely night, and why should we not prolong our ride a little?”", "아름다운 밤인데, 좀 더 길게 달리면 안 될 게 뭐 있어?\""]], [["“How could you be so treacherous!”", "\"어쩜 그렇게 음흉할 수가 있어요!\""], ["said Tess, between archness and real dismay, and getting rid of his arm by pulling open his fingers one by one, though at the risk of slipping off herself.", "테스가 짐짓 토라진 듯하면서도 진짜로 당황해하며 말했고, 자칫 자기가 미끄러져 떨어질 위험을 무릅쓰면서도 그의 손가락을 하나하나 펴서 그 팔을 떼어 냈다."], ["“Just when I’ve been putting such trust in you, and obliging you to please you, because I thought I had wronged you by that push!", "\"내가 그렇게 당신을 믿어 주고 있었는데, 그 밀친 일로 내가 당신한테 잘못했다고 생각해서 당신 비위 맞추려고 따라 주고 있었는데!"], ["Please set me down, and let me walk home.”", "제발 내려 주고, 걸어서 집에 가게 해 줘요.\""]], [["“You cannot walk home, darling, even if the air were clear.", "\"걸어서는 집에 못 가, 그대, 공기가 맑다 해도 말이야."], ["We are miles away from Trantridge, if I must tell you, and in this growing fog you might wander for hours among these trees.”", "굳이 말하자면 우린 트랜트리지에서 몇 마일이나 떨어져 있고, 이렇게 짙어 가는 안개 속에서 이 나무들 사이를 몇 시간이나 헤맬지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Never mind that,”", "\"그건 상관없어요.\""], ["she coaxed. “Put me down, I beg you.", "그녀가 달래듯 말했다. \"내려 줘요, 제발."], ["I don’t mind where it is; only let me get down, sir, please!”", "어디든 상관없어요. 그저 내리게만 해 줘요, 도련님, 부탁이에요!\""]], [["“Very well, then, I will—on one condition.", "\"그래, 좋아, 그럼—한 가지 조건이 있어."], ["Having brought you here to this out-of-the-way place, I feel myself responsible for your safe-conduct home, whatever you may yourself feel about it.", "너를 이런 외진 데까지 데려왔으니, 네 생각이야 어떻든 너를 집까지 안전하게 데려다줄 책임이 내게 있다고 느껴."], ["As to your getting to Trantridge without assistance, it is quite impossible; for, to tell the truth, dear, owing to this fog, which so disguises everything, I don’t quite know where we are myself.", "도움 없이 네가 트랜트리지에 가는 건 도무지 불가능해. 사실대로 말하자면, 그대, 이 안개가 모든 걸 워낙 가려 놓아서 나조차 우리가 어디 있는지 잘 모르겠거든."], ["Now, if you will promise to wait beside the horse while I walk through the bushes till I come to some road or house, and ascertain exactly our whereabouts, I’ll deposit you here willingly.", "자, 내가 덤불을 헤치고 걸어가 어떤 길이나 집을 찾아 우리 위치를 정확히 알아내는 동안 말 옆에서 기다리겠다고 약속하면, 기꺼이 너를 여기 내려 주마."], ["When I come back I’ll give you full directions, and if you insist upon walking you may; or you may ride—at your pleasure.”", "돌아오면 자세히 길을 일러 줄 테고, 걸어가겠다고 고집하면 걸어도 되고, 타고 가도 되고—네 마음대로 해.\""]], [["She accepted these terms, and slid off on the near side, though not till he had stolen a cursory kiss.", "그녀는 이 조건을 받아들여, 가까운 쪽으로 미끄러져 내렸다—다만 그가 잽싸게 스치듯 입 맞추기 전은 아니었다."], ["He sprang down on the other side.", "그는 반대편으로 뛰어내렸다."]], [["“I suppose I must hold the horse?” said she.", "\"말은 제가 붙잡고 있어야겠죠?\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Oh no; it’s not necessary,”", "\"오, 아니, 그럴 필요 없어.\""], ["replied Alec, patting the panting creature.", "알렉이 헐떡이는 짐승을 토닥이며 대답했다."], ["“He’s had enough of it for to-night.”", "\"오늘 밤엔 이놈도 실컷 했으니까.\""]], [["He turned the horse’s head into the bushes, hitched him on to a bough, and made a sort of couch or nest for her in the deep mass of dead leaves.", "그는 말 머리를 덤불 쪽으로 돌려 가지에 매어 놓고, 수북이 쌓인 낙엽 더미 속에 그녀를 위한 일종의 자리, 둥지 같은 것을 만들어 주었다."]], [["“Now, you sit there,”", "\"자, 거기 앉아.\""], ["he said. “The leaves have not got damp as yet.", "그가 말했다. \"낙엽은 아직 안 젖었어."], ["Just give an eye to the horse—it will be quite sufficient.”", "말이나 한번 봐 주면—그걸로 충분해.\""]], [["He took a few steps away from her, but, returning, said, “By the bye, Tess, your father has a new cob to-day.", "그는 그녀에게서 몇 걸음 멀어졌다가, 돌아와 말했다. \"그런데 말이야, 테스, 오늘 네 아버지한테 새 조랑말이 한 마리 생겼어."], ["Somebody gave it to him.”", "누가 줬지.\""]], [["“Somebody? You!”", "\"누가요? 당신이!\""]], [["D’Urberville nodded.", "더버빌이 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“O how very good of you that is!”", "\"오 정말 친절하셔라!\""], ["she exclaimed, with a painful sense of the awkwardness of having to thank him just then.", "그녀가 외쳤는데, 하필 그런 때에 그에게 고마워해야 한다는 게 거북하기 짝이 없었다."]], [["“And the children have some toys.”", "\"그리고 아이들한테는 장난감이 좀 있어.\""]], [["“I didn’t know—you ever sent them anything!”", "\"몰랐어요—당신이 그 애들한테 뭘 보내 주신 줄은!\""], ["she murmured, much moved.", "그녀가 몹시 감동해 중얼거렸다."], ["“I almost wish you had not—yes, I almost wish it!”", "\"차라리 안 그러셨으면 싶을 정도예요—그래요, 차라리 그랬으면!\""]], [["“Why, dear?”", "\"왜, 그대?\""]], [["“It—hampers me so.”", "\"그게—절 너무 옭아매니까요.\""]], [["“Tessy—don’t you love me ever so little now?”", "\"테시—이제 날 조금도 사랑하지 않니?\""]], [["“I’m grateful,”", "\"고마워요.\""], ["she reluctantly admitted.", "그녀가 마지못해 인정했다."], ["“But I fear I do not—” The sudden vision of his passion for herself as a factor in this result so distressed her that, beginning with one slow tear, and then following with another, she wept outright.", "\"하지만 사랑하진 않는 것 같아요—\" 자기를 향한 그의 열정이 이 결과의 한 요인이라는 생각이 불현듯 떠올라 어찌나 괴로웠던지, 그녀는 느린 눈물 한 방울로 시작해, 이어 또 한 방울로, 끝내 엉엉 울고 말았다."]], [["“Don’t cry, dear, dear one!", "\"울지 마, 그대, 사랑스러운 그대!"], ["Now sit down here, and wait till I come.”", "자 여기 앉아서, 내가 올 때까지 기다려.\""], ["She passively sat down amid the leaves he had heaped, and shivered slightly.", "그녀는 순순히 그가 쌓아 둔 낙엽 사이에 앉아, 살짝 몸을 떨었다. \"추워?\""], ["“Are you cold?” he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Not very—a little.”", "\"많이는 아니고—조금요.\""]], [["He touched her with his fingers, which sank into her as into down.", "그는 손가락으로 그녀를 건드렸는데, 손가락이 솜털 속으로 파묻히듯 그녀 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["“You have only that puffy muslin dress on—how’s that?”", "\"그 부푼 모슬린 옷 하나밖에 안 입었구나—어쩌다가?\""]], [["“It’s my best summer one. ’Twas very warm when I started, and I didn’t know I was going to ride, and that it would be night.”", "\"이게 제 제일 좋은 여름옷이에요. 출발할 땐 무척 따뜻했고, 말을 타게 될 줄도, 밤이 될 줄도 몰랐거든요.\""]], [["“Nights grow chilly in September.", "\"구월엔 밤이 쌀쌀해지지."], ["Let me see.”", "어디 보자.\""], ["He pulled off a light overcoat that he had worn, and put it round her tenderly.", "그는 입고 있던 얇은 외투를 벗어, 그것을 부드럽게 그녀에게 둘러 주었다."], ["“That’s it—now you’ll feel warmer,” he continued.", "\"그래—이제 좀 따뜻할 거야.\" 그가 이어 말했다."], ["“Now, my pretty, rest there; I shall soon be back again.”", "\"자, 나의 예쁜이, 거기서 쉬어. 곧 돌아올게.\""]], [["Having buttoned the overcoat round her shoulders he plunged into the webs of vapour which by this time formed veils between the trees.", "외투의 단추를 그녀의 어깨에 채워 준 뒤, 그는 이쯤 되어 나무들 사이에 장막을 친 안개의 그물 속으로 뛰어들었다."], ["She could hear the rustling of the branches as he ascended the adjoining slope, till his movements were no louder than the hopping of a bird, and finally died away.", "그녀는 그가 옆 비탈을 오르며 가지를 바스락거리는 소리를 들을 수 있었는데, 마침내 그의 움직임은 새가 폴짝거리는 소리보다 더 크지 않게 되었다가, 끝내 사라졌다."], ["With the setting of the moon the pale light lessened, and Tess became invisible as she fell into reverie upon the leaves where he had left her.", "달이 지면서 창백한 빛이 옅어졌고, 그가 두고 간 낙엽 위에서 몽상에 잠긴 테스는 보이지 않게 되었다."]], [["In the meantime Alec d’Urberville had pushed on up the slope to clear his genuine doubt as to the quarter of The Chase they were in.", "그동안 알렉 더버빌은, 자기들이 체이스의 어느 구역에 있는지에 대한 진짜 의문을 풀려고 비탈을 올라 나아갔다."], ["He had, in fact, ridden quite at random for over an hour, taking any turning that came to hand in order to prolong companionship with her, and giving far more attention to Tess’s moonlit person than to any wayside object.", "사실 그는 한 시간 넘게 그야말로 되는대로 말을 몰며, 그녀와의 동행을 늘리려고 손에 닿는 갈림길마다 아무 데로나 접어들었고, 길가의 어떤 사물보다 달빛에 비친 테스의 몸에 훨씬 더 마음을 쏟았다."], ["A little rest for the jaded animal being desirable, he did not hasten his search for landmarks.", "지친 짐승을 좀 쉬게 하는 편이 좋겠기에, 그는 지표를 찾는 일을 서두르지 않았다."], ["A clamber over the hill into the adjoining vale brought him to the fence of a highway whose contours he recognized, which settled the question of their whereabouts.", "언덕을 기어 넘어 이웃 골짜기로 내려서니 윤곽이 눈에 익은 큰길의 울타리에 이르렀고, 그것으로 그들의 위치 문제가 풀렸다."], ["D’Urberville thereupon turned back; but by this time the moon had quite gone down, and partly on account of the fog The Chase was wrapped in thick darkness, although morning was not far off.", "이에 더버빌은 발길을 돌렸다. 그러나 이때쯤엔 달이 완전히 져 버린 데다, 부분적으로 안개 탓에 체이스는, 아침이 멀지 않았는데도, 짙은 어둠에 싸여 있었다."], ["He was obliged to advance with outstretched hands to avoid contact with the boughs, and discovered that to hit the exact spot from which he had started was at first entirely beyond him.", "그는 나뭇가지에 부딪치지 않으려 두 손을 앞으로 뻗고 나아가야 했고, 출발했던 바로 그 자리를 찾는 일이 처음에는 도무지 그의 능력 밖임을 알았다."], ["Roaming up and down, round and round, he at length heard a slight movement of the horse close at hand; and the sleeve of his overcoat unexpectedly caught his foot.", "위아래로, 빙빙 돌며 헤맨 끝에, 마침내 바로 곁에서 말이 살짝 움직이는 소리를 들었다. 그리고 그의 외투 소매가 뜻밖에 그의 발에 걸렸다."]], [["“Tess!”", "\"테스!\""], ["said d’Urberville.", "더버빌이 말했다."]], [["There was no answer.", "대답이 없었다."], ["The obscurity was now so great that he could see absolutely nothing but a pale nebulousness at his feet, which represented the white muslin figure he had left upon the dead leaves.", "어둠이 이제 어찌나 짙던지 그는 발치의 창백한 안개 같은 형체 말고는 그야말로 아무것도 볼 수 없었는데, 그것이 그가 낙엽 위에 두고 간 흰 모슬린 형체를 나타내고 있었다."], ["Everything else was blackness alike.", "그 밖의 모든 것은 한결같이 캄캄했다."], ["D’Urberville stooped; and heard a gentle regular breathing.", "더버빌이 몸을 굽혔다. 그리고 가만하고 규칙적인 숨소리를 들었다."], ["He knelt and bent lower, till her breath warmed his face, and in a moment his cheek was in contact with hers.", "그는 무릎을 꿇고 더 낮게 숙였고, 마침내 그녀의 숨결이 그의 얼굴을 따뜻하게 했으며, 한순간 그의 뺨이 그녀의 뺨에 닿았다."], ["She was sleeping soundly, and upon her eyelashes there lingered tears.", "그녀는 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 그녀의 속눈썹에는 눈물이 맺혀 있었다."]], [["Darkness and silence ruled everywhere around.", "어둠과 침묵이 사방을 다스렸다."], ["Above them rose the primaeval yews and oaks of The Chase, in which there poised gentle roosting birds in their last nap; and about them stole the hopping rabbits and hares.", "그들 위로 체이스의 태곳적 주목과 떡갈나무가 솟아 있었고, 그 안에는 마지막 선잠에 든 새들이 얌전히 깃들어 있었다. 그들 둘레로는 폴짝거리는 토끼와 산토끼가 살금살금 다녔다."], ["But, might some say, where was Tess’s guardian angel?", "그러나 누군가는 물을지도 모른다, 테스의 수호천사는 어디 있었느냐고."], ["where was the providence of her simple faith?", "그녀의 소박한 믿음이 받드는 섭리는 어디 있었느냐고."], ["Perhaps, like that other god of whom the ironical Tishbite spoke, he was talking, or he was pursuing, or he was in a journey, or he was sleeping and not to be awaked.", "어쩌면 그는, 빈정대던 티스바 사람이 말한 또 다른 신처럼, 이야기를 나누고 있었거나, 사냥을 하고 있었거나, 여행 중이었거나, 잠들어 깨우지 못할 상태였는지도 모른다."]], [["Why it was that upon this beautiful feminine tissue, sensitive as gossamer, and practically blank as snow as yet, there should have been traced such a coarse pattern as it was doomed to receive; why so often the coarse appropriates the finer thus, the wrong man the woman, the wrong woman the man, many thousand years of analytical philosophy have failed to explain to our sense of order.", "거미줄처럼 예민하고 아직 눈처럼 사실상 새하얗던 이 아름다운 여성의 천 위에, 어찌하여 그것이 받기로 정해진 그토록 거친 무늬가 새겨져야 했는지. 어찌하여 그토록 자주 거친 것이 이렇듯 고운 것을 차지하는지, 그른 남자가 여자를, 그른 여자가 남자를 차지하는지, 수천 년에 걸친 분석 철학도 우리의 질서 감각에 설명해 내지 못했다."], ["One may, indeed, admit the possibility of a retribution lurking in the present catastrophe.", "물론, 지금의 이 파국 속에 도사린 어떤 응보의 가능성을 인정할 수는 있다."], ["Doubtless some of Tess d’Urberville’s mailed ancestors rollicking home from a fray had dealt the same measure even more ruthlessly towards peasant girls of their time.", "분명 테스 더버빌의 갑옷 입은 조상들 가운데 누군가는 싸움터에서 흥청대며 돌아오는 길에 제 시대의 농가 처녀들에게 똑같은, 아니 그보다 더 무자비한 짓을 저질렀을 것이다."], ["But though to visit the sins of the fathers upon the children may be a morality good enough for divinities, it is scorned by average human nature; and it therefore does not mend the matter.", "그러나 아비의 죄를 자식에게 묻는 일이 신들에게는 그럭저럭 괜찮은 도덕일지언정, 보통의 인간 본성은 그것을 경멸한다. 그러므로 그렇게 한들 사정이 나아지지는 않는다."]], [["As Tess’s own people down in those retreats are never tired of saying among each other in their fatalistic way: “It was to be.” There lay the pity of it.", "저 아래 그 외진 곳의 테스의 동족들이 운명론적인 투로 저희끼리 입버릇처럼 말하듯, \"그리될 일이었던\" 것이다. 거기에 그 안타까움이 놓여 있었다."], ["An immeasurable social chasm was to divide our heroine’s personality thereafter from that previous self of hers who stepped from her mother’s door to try her fortune at Trantridge poultry-farm. End of Phase the First", "어머니의 문간을 나서 트랜트리지 양계장에서 제 운을 시험해 보러 가던 예전의 그 자신과, 그 뒤로 우리 여주인공의 인격을 가늠할 수 없는 사회적 심연이 갈라놓게 될 터였다."]], [["# XII", "# XII"]], [["Maiden No More\nThe basket was heavy and the bundle was large, but she lugged them along like a person who did not find her especial burden in material things.", "바구니는 무거웠고 보따리는 컸지만, 그녀는 자기 특유의 짐이 물질에 있지 않다는 듯한 사람처럼 그것들을 끌고 갔다."], ["Occasionally she stopped to rest in a mechanical way by some gate or post; and then, giving the baggage another hitch upon her full round arm, went steadily on again.", "이따금 그녀는 어떤 문이나 기둥 곁에서 기계적으로 쉬려고 멈추었다. 그러고는 짐을 통통하고 둥근 팔 위로 다시 추슬러, 또박또박 다시 걸어갔다."]], [["It was a Sunday morning in late October, about four months after Tess Durbeyfield’s arrival at Trantridge, and some few weeks subsequent to the night ride in The Chase.", "시월 하순의 어느 일요일 아침이었다, 테스 더버빌이 트랜트리지에 온 지 넉 달쯤, 체이스의 밤 말타기로부터 몇 주쯤 지난 뒤였다."], ["The time was not long past daybreak, and the yellow luminosity upon the horizon behind her back lighted the ridge towards which her face was set—the barrier of the vale wherein she had of late been a stranger—which she would have to climb over to reach her birthplace.", "동틀 무렵을 지난 지 얼마 안 된 때라, 등 뒤 지평선 위의 노란 빛이 그녀의 얼굴이 향한 능선을 비추었다—그녀가 요사이 낯선 이가 되어 있던 골짜기의 경계였고, 태어난 곳에 닿으려면 넘어가야 할 능선이었다."], ["The ascent was gradual on this side, and the soil and scenery differed much from those within Blakemore Vale.", "이쪽에서는 오르막이 완만했고, 토양과 풍경이 블레이크모어 골짜기 안의 것과 사뭇 달랐다."], ["Even the character and accent of the two peoples had shades of difference, despite the amalgamating effects of a roundabout railway; so that, though less than twenty miles from the place of her sojourn at Trantridge, her native village had seemed a far-away spot.", "두 고장 사람들의 기질과 말씨조차, 빙 둘러 난 철도가 뒤섞어 놓는 효과에도 불구하고 차이의 그늘이 있었다. 그래서 트랜트리지에 머물던 곳에서 이십 마일도 채 안 되는데, 고향 마을은 아득히 먼 곳처럼 여겨졌다."], ["The field-folk shut in there traded northward and westward, travelled, courted, and married northward and westward, thought northward and westward; those on this side mainly directed their energies and attention to the east and south.", "그곳에 갇혀 사는 들일꾼들은 북쪽과 서쪽으로 장사하고, 북쪽과 서쪽으로 다니고 연애하고 혼인했으며, 북쪽과 서쪽으로 생각했다. 이쪽 사람들은 주로 동쪽과 남쪽으로 그들의 기운과 관심을 돌렸다."]], [["The incline was the same down which d’Urberville had driven her so wildly on that day in June.", "그 비탈은 유월의 그날 더버빌이 그토록 사납게 그녀를 몰아 내려가던 바로 그 비탈이었다."], ["Tess went up the remainder of its length without stopping, and on reaching the edge of the escarpment gazed over the familiar green world beyond, now half-veiled in mist.", "테스는 멈추지 않고 남은 길을 다 올라갔고, 가파른 비탈의 가장자리에 이르러 너머의 낯익은 푸른 세계를 굽어보았다, 이제 절반쯤 안개에 가려진 그곳을."], ["It was always beautiful from here; it was terribly beautiful to Tess to-day, for since her eyes last fell upon it she had learnt that the serpent hisses where the sweet birds sing, and her views of life had been totally changed for her by the lesson.", "여기서 보면 그곳은 늘 아름다웠다. 오늘 테스에게는 무서우리만치 아름다웠다. 그 눈이 마지막으로 그곳에 닿은 뒤로, 그녀는 고운 새들이 노래하는 곳에서 뱀이 쉭쉭댄다는 것을 알게 되었고, 그 가르침으로 인생을 보는 눈이 송두리째 바뀌어 버린 까닭이었다."], ["Verily another girl than the simple one she had been at home was she who, bowed by thought, stood still here, and turned to look behind her.", "참으로, 생각에 짓눌려 여기 가만히 서서 뒤돌아보는 그녀는, 집에서의 그 단순했던 처녀와는 다른 처녀였다."], ["She could not bear to look forward into the Vale.", "그녀는 차마 골짜기 쪽을 내다볼 수 없었다."]], [["Ascending by the long white road that Tess herself had just laboured up, she saw a two-wheeled vehicle, beside which walked a man, who held up his hand to attract her attention.", "테스 자신이 방금 힘겹게 올라온 그 긴 흰 길을 따라 올라오는, 두 바퀴 달린 수레 한 대가 보였고, 그 곁에는 한 사내가 걸으며 손을 들어 그녀의 주의를 끌고 있었다."]], [["She obeyed the signal to wait for him with unspeculative repose, and in a few minutes man and horse stopped beside her.", "그녀는 별 헤아림 없이 잠잠한 마음으로 그를 기다리라는 신호에 따랐고, 몇 분 뒤 사내와 말이 그녀 곁에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Why did you slip away by stealth like this?”", "\"왜 이렇게 몰래 빠져나간 거야?\""], ["said d’Urberville, with upbraiding breathlessness; “on a Sunday morning, too, when people were all in bed!", "더버빌이 나무라듯 가쁜 숨으로 말했다. \"그것도 일요일 아침에, 다들 잠자리에 있을 때 말이야!"], ["I only discovered it by accident, and I have been driving like the deuce to overtake you.", "우연히 알게 돼서, 너를 따라잡으려고 미친 듯이 몰아왔어."], ["Just look at the mare.", "이 암말 좀 봐."], ["Why go off like this?", "왜 이렇게 가 버린 거야?"], ["You know that nobody wished to hinder your going. And how unnecessary it has been for you to toil along on foot, and encumber yourself with this heavy load!", "아무도 네가 가는 걸 막으려 하지 않은 거 너도 알잖아. 그리고 걸어서 이 무거운 짐까지 짊어지고 고생할 필요가 어디 있었어!"], ["I have followed like a madman, simply to drive you the rest of the distance, if you won’t come back.”", "미친놈처럼 따라온 건, 네가 돌아오지 않겠다면 남은 거리만이라도 태워 주려는 거야.\""]], [["“I shan’t come back,” said she.", "\"안 돌아가요.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I thought you wouldn’t—I said so!", "\"안 돌아갈 줄 알았어—내가 그랬잖아!"], ["Well, then, put up your basket, and let me help you on.”", "그럼, 바구니 올려놓고, 내가 태워 줄게.\""]], [["She listlessly placed her basket and bundle within the dog-cart, and stepped up, and they sat side by side.", "그녀는 맥없이 바구니와 보따리를 이륜마차 안에 놓고, 올라탔고, 둘은 나란히 앉았다."], ["She had no fear of him now, and in the cause of her confidence her sorrow lay.", "이제 그녀는 그가 두렵지 않았는데, 그 마음 놓임의 까닭에 그녀의 슬픔이 놓여 있었다."]], [["D’Urberville mechanically lit a cigar, and the journey was continued with broken unemotional conversation on the commonplace objects by the wayside.", "더버빌은 기계적으로 시가에 불을 붙였고, 여정은 길가의 흔한 사물들에 관한 토막토막의 무덤덤한 이야기로 이어졌다."], ["He had quite forgotten his struggle to kiss her when, in the early summer, they had driven in the opposite direction along the same road.", "그는 초여름에 같은 길을 반대 방향으로 달리며 그녀에게 입 맞추려 애쓰던 일을 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["But she had not, and she sat now, like a puppet, replying to his remarks in monosyllables.", "그러나 그녀는 잊지 않았고, 이제 꼭두각시처럼 앉아, 그의 말에 외마디로 대답했다."], ["After some miles they came in view of the clump of trees beyond which the village of Marlott stood.", "몇 마일을 가니 그 너머에 말럿 마을이 선 나무숲이 눈에 들어왔다."], ["It was only then that her still face showed the least emotion, a tear or two beginning to trickle down.", "그제야 그녀의 멈춰 있던 얼굴에 조금이나마 감정이 드러났고, 눈물 한두 방울이 흘러내리기 시작했다."]], [["“What are you crying for?”", "\"뭐 때문에 우는 거야?\""], ["he coldly asked.", "그가 차갑게 물었다."]], [["“I was only thinking that I was born over there,”", "\"그냥 제가 저기서 태어났다는 생각이 들어서요.\""], ["murmured Tess.", "테스가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Well—we must all be born somewhere.”", "\"뭐—누구나 어딘가에서는 태어나기 마련이지.\""]], [["“I wish I had never been born—there or anywhere else!”", "\"차라리 태어나지 않았더라면—저기서든 다른 어디서든!\""]], [["“Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["Well, if you didn’t wish to come to Trantridge why did you come?”", "그럼, 트랜트리지에 오기 싫었으면 왜 온 거야?\""]], [["She did not reply.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“You didn’t come for love of me, that I’ll swear.”", "\"날 사랑해서 온 게 아니라는 건, 내가 맹세하지.\""]], [["“’Tis quite true. If I had gone for love o’ you, if I had ever sincerely loved you, if I loved you still, I should not so loathe and hate myself for my weakness as I do now!...", "\"맞아요. 만약 당신을 사랑해서 갔다면, 만약 한 번이라도 진심으로 당신을 사랑했다면, 만약 지금도 당신을 사랑한다면, 지금처럼 제 나약함 때문에 저 자신을 이토록 혐오하고 증오하지는 않을 거예요!……"], ["My eyes were dazed by you for a little, and that was all.”", "잠깐 당신한테 눈이 멀었던 거예요, 그게 다였어요.\""]], [["He shrugged his shoulders. She resumed—", "그는 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그녀가 다시 말했다—"]], [["“I didn’t understand your meaning till it was too late.”", "\"당신 속셈을 알아챘을 땐 이미 너무 늦었어요.\""]], [["“That’s what every woman says.”", "\"여자란 다 그렇게들 말하지.\""]], [["“How can you dare to use such words!”", "\"어떻게 감히 그런 말을!\""], ["she cried, turning impetuously upon him, her eyes flashing as the latent spirit (of which he was to see more some day) awoke in her.", "그녀가 그를 향해 격하게 돌아서며 외쳤는데, 그 눈은 (언젠가 그가 더 보게 될) 잠재된 기개가 깨어나며 번득였다. \"맙소사!"], ["“My God! I could knock you out of the gig!", "당신을 마차에서 떨어뜨려 버릴 수도 있어!"], ["Did it never strike your mind that what every woman says some women may feel?”", "여자들이 다 하는 말을 어떤 여자들은 진짜로 느낄지도 모른다는 생각은 한 번도 못 해 봤어요?\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["he said, laughing; “I am sorry to wound you.", "그가 웃으며 말했다. \"너를 아프게 해서 미안해."], ["I did wrong—I admit it.”", "내가 잘못했어—인정하지.\""], ["He dropped into some little bitterness as he continued: “Only you needn’t be so everlastingly flinging it in my face.", "그는 이어 말하며 조금 쓰라림에 빠져들었다. \"다만 그걸 그렇게 끝도 없이 내 얼굴에 들이댈 필요는 없잖아."], ["I am ready to pay to the uttermost farthing.", "난 마지막 한 푼까지 갚을 준비가 돼 있어."], ["You know you need not work in the fields or the dairies again.", "네가 다시는 밭일이나 낙농장 일을 안 해도 된다는 거 너도 알잖아."], ["You know you may clothe yourself with the best, instead of in the bald plain way you have lately affected, as if you couldn’t get a ribbon more than you earn.”", "요새 네가 마치 버는 것보다 리본 한 가닥도 더 못 산다는 듯이 일부러 꾸민 그 휑하니 수수한 차림 대신, 최상품으로 몸을 두를 수 있다는 것도 알잖아.\""]], [["Her lip lifted slightly, though there was little scorn, as a rule, in her large and impulsive nature.", "그녀의 입술이 살짝 들렸으나, 대체로 그녀의 크고 충동적인 천성에는 멸시라 할 게 거의 없었다."]], [["“I have said I will not take anything more from you, and I will not—I cannot!", "\"당신한테서 더는 아무것도 받지 않겠다고 했고, 안 받을 거예요—못 받아요!"], ["I _should_ be your creature to go on doing that, and I won’t!”", "그러면 그 짓을 계속하는 당신의 물건이 되어 버릴 텐데, 난 그러지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["“One would think you were a princess from your manner, in addition to a true and original d’Urberville—ha! ha!", "\"네 태도를 보면 진짜배기 원조 더버빌인 데다 공주님인 줄 알겠어—하! 하!"], ["Well, Tess, dear, I can say no more.", "그래, 테스, 그대, 더는 할 말이 없군."], ["I suppose I am a bad fellow—a damn bad fellow.", "나는 나쁜 놈인가 봐—지독하게 나쁜 놈."], ["I was born bad, and I have lived bad, and I shall die bad in all probability. But, upon my lost soul, I won’t be bad towards you again, Tess.", "나쁘게 태어나, 나쁘게 살아왔고, 십중팔구 나쁘게 죽겠지. 하지만, 내 잃어버린 영혼을 걸고, 너한테만은 다시 나쁘게 굴지 않겠어, 테스."], ["And if certain circumstances should arise—you understand—in which you are in the least need, the least difficulty, send me one line, and you shall have by return whatever you require.", "그리고 만에 하나 어떤 사정이 생긴다면—너도 알지—네가 조금이라도 도움이 필요하거나, 조금이라도 곤란해지면, 한 줄만 보내, 그러면 필요한 건 뭐든 곧장 받게 될 거야."], ["I may not be at Trantridge—I am going to London for a time—I can’t stand the old woman.", "난 트랜트리지에 없을지도 몰라—한동안 런던에 갈 거거든—그 노인네는 도무지 견딜 수가 없어서."], ["But all letters will be forwarded.”", "하지만 편지는 다 전달될 거야.\""]], [["She said that she did not wish him to drive her further, and they stopped just under the clump of trees.", "그녀는 그가 더 멀리 태워다 주는 걸 원치 않는다고 말했고, 둘은 바로 그 나무숲 아래에 멈췄다."], ["D’Urberville alighted, and lifted her down bodily in his arms, afterwards placing her articles on the ground beside her.", "더버빌이 내려, 그녀를 두 팔로 번쩍 들어 내려놓은 뒤, 그녀의 물건들을 그녀 곁 땅에 놓아 주었다."], ["She bowed to him slightly, her eye just lingering in his; and then she turned to take the parcels for departure.", "그녀는 그에게 살짝 고개를 숙였고, 그 눈길이 잠시 그의 눈에 머물렀다. 그러고는 떠나려고 짐 꾸러미를 집으려 돌아섰다."]], [["Alec d’Urberville removed his cigar, bent towards her, and said—", "알렉 더버빌이 시가를 떼고, 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 굽혀 말했다—"]], [["“You are not going to turn away like that, dear! Come!”", "\"그렇게 돌아서 가 버리려는 건 아니지, 그대! 자!\""]], [["“If you wish,”", "\"원하신다면요.\""], ["she answered indifferently. “See how you’ve mastered me!”", "그녀가 무심히 대답했다. \"보세요, 당신이 절 얼마나 좌지우지했는지!\""]], [["She thereupon turned round and lifted her face to his, and remained like a marble term while he imprinted a kiss upon her cheek—half perfunctorily, half as if zest had not yet quite died out.", "이에 그녀는 돌아서서 그에게 얼굴을 들어 올린 채, 그가 그녀의 뺨에 입 맞추는 동안 대리석 경계비처럼 가만히 있었다—반쯤은 마지못한 듯이, 반쯤은 흥취가 아직 다 가시지 않은 듯이."], ["Her eyes vaguely rested upon the remotest trees in the lane while the kiss was given, as though she were nearly unconscious of what he did.", "입맞춤이 오가는 동안 그녀의 눈은 오솔길의 가장 멀리 떨어진 나무들에 멍하니 머물렀는데, 마치 그가 무얼 하는지 거의 의식하지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["“Now the other side, for old acquaintance’ sake.”", "\"이제 반대쪽도, 옛정을 봐서.\""]], [["She turned her head in the same passive way, as one might turn at the request of a sketcher or hairdresser, and he kissed the other side, his lips touching cheeks that were damp and smoothly chill as the skin of the mushrooms in the fields around.", "그녀는 마치 화가나 이발사의 청에 응하듯 똑같이 수동적으로 고개를 돌렸고, 그는 반대쪽에 입 맞췄는데, 그의 입술이 닿은 뺨은 둘레 밭의 버섯 껍질처럼 축축하고 매끄럽게 차가웠다."]], [["“You don’t give me your mouth and kiss me back.", "\"넌 입술을 내주지도, 마주 입 맞추지도 않는구나."], ["You never willingly do that—you’ll never love me, I fear.”", "한 번도 기꺼이 그러질 않아—넌 결코 날 사랑하지 않겠지, 안타깝게도.\""]], [["“I have said so, often.", "\"여러 번 말했잖아요."], ["It is true.", "사실이에요."], ["I have never really and truly loved you, and I think I never can.”", "한 번도 진심으로 진짜로 당신을 사랑한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 못 할 것 같아요.\""], ["She added mournfully, “Perhaps, of all things, a lie on this thing would do the most good to me now; but I have honour enough left, little as ’tis, not to tell that lie.", "그녀가 구슬프게 덧붙였다. \"어쩌면, 무엇보다, 이 일에 거짓말 하나 하는 게 지금 제게 가장 득이 될지도 모르죠. 하지만 비록 보잘것없을지언정, 그런 거짓말은 하지 않을 만큼의 명예는 제게 남아 있어요."], ["If I did love you, I may have the best o’ causes for letting you know it. But I don’t.”", "정말 당신을 사랑한다면야, 당신한테 알려 줄 더없이 좋은 까닭이 있겠죠. 하지만 사랑하지 않아요.\""]], [["He emitted a laboured breath, as if the scene were getting rather oppressive to his heart, or to his conscience, or to his gentility.", "그는, 그 광경이 그의 마음을, 혹은 양심을, 혹은 체면을 자못 짓누르기라도 하는 듯, 힘겹게 숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Well, you are absurdly melancholy, Tess.", "\"그래, 너는 우스꽝스러우리만치 우울하구나, 테스."], ["I have no reason for flattering you now, and I can say plainly that you need not be so sad.", "이제 와 너한테 아첨할 까닭도 없으니, 분명히 말하는데 그렇게 슬퍼할 것 없어."], ["You can hold your own for beauty against any woman of these parts, gentle or simple; I say it to you as a practical man and well-wisher.", "너는 이 고장 어떤 여자와 견줘도, 양가집이든 평민이든, 미모만큼은 뒤지지 않아. 실속 있는 사내로서, 또 너를 위하는 사람으로서 하는 말이야."], ["If you are wise you will show it to the world more than you do before it fades...", "현명하다면, 그게 시들기 전에 지금보다 더 세상에 내보일 거다……"], ["And yet, Tess, will you come back to me!", "그래도, 테스, 내게 돌아오지 않겠어!"], ["Upon my soul, I don’t like to let you go like this!”", "내 영혼을 걸고, 이렇게 너를 보내기는 정말 싫구나!\""]], [["“Never, never!", "\"절대로, 절대로요!"], ["I made up my mind as soon as I saw—what I ought to have seen sooner; and I won’t come.”", "진작 봤어야 할 걸 본 순간 마음을 굳혔어요. 돌아가지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Then good morning, my four months’ cousin—good-bye!”", "\"그럼 잘 가, 넉 달짜리 내 사촌—잘 있어!\""]], [["He leapt up lightly, arranged the reins, and was gone between the tall red-berried hedges.", "그는 가볍게 뛰어올라 고삐를 추슬렀고, 붉은 열매가 달린 키 큰 산울타리 사이로 사라졌다."]], [["Tess did not look after him, but slowly wound along the crooked lane.", "테스는 그의 뒷모습을 바라보지 않고, 굽이진 오솔길을 따라 천천히 걸어 나갔다."], ["It was still early, and though the sun’s lower limb was just free of the hill, his rays, ungenial and peering, addressed the eye rather than the touch as yet.", "아직 이른 시각이라, 해의 아래쪽 가장자리가 막 언덕을 벗어났을 뿐, 그 빛살은 미지근하니 엿보는 듯해 아직은 촉감보다 눈에 와닿았다."], ["There was not a human soul near.", "가까이에 인기척 하나 없었다."], ["Sad October and her sadder self seemed the only two existences haunting that lane.", "슬픈 시월과 더 슬픈 그녀 자신만이 그 오솔길을 떠도는 단 둘뿐인 존재인 듯했다."]], [["As she walked, however, some footsteps approached behind her, the footsteps of a man; and owing to the briskness of his advance he was close at her heels and had said “Good morning”", "그러나 걷는 동안, 등 뒤에서 어떤 발소리가, 한 사내의 발소리가 다가왔다. 그가 어찌나 빠르게 다가왔던지, 그녀가 그 가까움을 채 알아채기도 전에 그는 바짝 뒤따라붙어 \"안녕하세요\""], ["before she had been long aware of his propinquity.", "하고 인사를 건넨 뒤였다."], ["He appeared to be an artisan of some sort, and carried a tin pot of red paint in his hand.", "그는 일종의 장인匠人처럼 보였고, 손에는 붉은 페인트 깡통을 들고 있었다."], ["He asked in a business-like manner if he should take her basket, which she permitted him to do, walking beside him.", "그는 사무적인 태도로 바구니를 들어 줄까 물었고, 그녀가 허락하자 그녀 곁에서 걸었다."]], [["“It is early to be astir this Sabbath morn!”", "\"이 안식일 아침에 나다니기엔 이르네요!\""], ["he said cheerfully.", "그가 쾌활하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“When most people are at rest from their week’s work.”", "\"다들 한 주 일에서 벗어나 쉬고 있을 때 말이오.\""]], [["She also assented to this.", "이 말에도 그녀는 그렇다고 했다."]], [["“Though I do more real work to-day than all the week besides.”", "\"하기야 난 오늘 이 한 주 다 합친 것보다 더 진짜 일을 하지만.\""]], [["“Do you?”", "\"그러세요?\""]], [["“All the week I work for the glory of man, and on Sunday for the glory of God.", "\"한 주 내내 난 사람의 영광을 위해 일하고, 일요일엔 하느님의 영광을 위해 일하지요."], ["That’s more real than the other—hey?", "그게 다른 것보다 더 진짜 아니겠소—그렇지요?"], ["I have a little to do here at this stile.”", "여기 이 층계 디딤대에서 좀 할 일이 있소.\""], ["The man turned, as he spoke, to an opening at the roadside leading into a pasture.", "사내는 말하며, 길가의 목초지로 통하는 한 틈으로 몸을 돌렸다. \"잠깐만 기다려 주시면,\" 그가 덧붙였다, \"오래 걸리지 않을 거요.\""], ["“If you’ll wait a moment,” he added, “I shall not be long.”", "그가 그녀의 바구니를 들고 있는지라 그녀로서는 달리 어쩔 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 기다렸다,"]], [["As he had her basket she could not well do otherwise; and she waited, observing him.", "그가 그녀의 바구니를 가지고 있었으므로 그녀로서는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 그를 지켜보며 기다렸다."], ["He set down her basket and the tin pot, and stirring the paint with the brush that was in it began painting large square letters on the middle board of the three composing the stile, placing a comma after each word, as if to give pause while that word was driven well home to the reader’s heart—", "그는 그녀의 바구니와 양철통을 내려놓고, 통 안에 든 붓으로 페인트를 휘저은 다음, 디딤대를 이루는 세 개의 널빤지 중 가운데 널빤지에 커다란 네모반듯한 글자를 칠하기 시작했다. 한 단어마다 쉼표를 찍었는데, 그 단어가 읽는 이의 마음에 단단히 박혀들도록 잠시 멈춤을 주려는 듯했다— 네, 멸망이, 자고, 있지, 않느니라."]], [["Against the peaceful landscape, the pale, decaying tints of the copses, the blue air of the horizon, and the lichened stile-boards, these staring vermilion words shone forth.", "벧후 2장 3절. 평온한 풍경, 잡목 숲의 창백하게 시들어가는 빛깔, 지평선의 푸른 대기, 이끼 낀 디딤대 널빤지를 배경으로, 이 노려보는 듯한 주홍빛 글자들이 빛을 뿜었다."], ["They seemed to shout themselves out and make the atmosphere ring. Some people might have cried “Alas, poor Theology!”", "글자들은 제 스스로 외쳐대며 대기를 울리는 듯했다. 어떤 사람들은 이 흉측한 훼손을 보고 \"아아, 가엾은 신학이여!\""], ["at the hideous defacement—the last grotesque phase of a creed which had served mankind well in its time.", "하고 외쳤을지도 모른다—한때 인류에게 충실히 봉사했던 신조의 마지막 기괴한 단계라며."], ["But the words entered Tess with accusatory horror.", "그러나 그 글자들은 테스에게 고발하는 듯한 공포로 파고들었다."], ["It was as if this man had known her recent history; yet he was a total stranger.", "마치 이 남자가 그녀의 최근 사정을 알고 있는 것 같았다. 그런데 그는 생판 모르는 타인이었다."]], [["Having finished his text he picked up her basket, and she mechanically resumed her walk beside him.", "성구를 다 칠한 그가 그녀의 바구니를 집어 들었고, 그녀는 기계적으로 그의 곁에서 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“Do you believe what you paint?”", "\"당신이 칠하는 걸 믿으세요?\""], ["she asked in low tones.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Believe that tex?", "\"이 성구를 믿느냐고요?"], ["Do I believe in my own existence!”", "내가 내 존재를 믿느냐는 거나 마찬가지요!\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said she tremulously, “suppose your sin was not of your own seeking?”", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"만약 그 죄가 스스로 구한 게 아니라면요?\""]], [["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“I cannot split hairs on that burning query,” he said.", "\"그런 불타는 물음에 대해선 내가 따져 가를 수 없소,\""], ["“I have walked hundreds of miles this past summer, painting these texes on every wall, gate, and stile the length and breadth of this district.", "그가 말했다. \"지난여름 내내 나는 수백 마일을 걸으며 이 지역 곳곳의 벽이며 문이며 디딤대마다 이 성구들을 칠했소."], ["I leave their application to the hearts of the people who read ’em.”", "그걸 어떻게 받아들일지는 읽는 사람들 마음에 맡길 뿐이오.\""]], [["“I think they are horrible,” said Tess.", "\"제 생각엔 끔찍해요,\" 테스가 말했다."], ["“Crushing! Killing!”", "\"짓누르고! 죽이고!\""]], [["“That’s what they are meant to be!” he replied in a trade voice.", "\"바로 그러라고 만든 거요!\" 그가 장사꾼 같은 목소리로 대꾸했다."], ["“But you should read my hottest ones—them I kips for slums and seaports.", "\"하지만 내가 빈민가랑 항구를 위해 아껴 둔 가장 독한 것들을 봐야 하오."], ["They’d make ye wriggle!", "그걸 보면 몸부림칠 거요!"], ["Not but what this is a very good tex for rural districts....", "하긴 이건 시골 지역에는 아주 좋은 성구지만...."], ["Ah—there’s a nice bit of blank wall up by that barn standing to waste.", "아—저기 헛간 옆에 버려진 빈 벽이 한 자락 좋게 있구먼."], ["I must put one there—one that it will be good for dangerous young females like yerself to heed. Will ye wait, missy?”", "저기에 하나 칠해야겠소—당신 같은 위험한 젊은 여자들이 새겨들으면 좋을 걸로 말이오. 좀 기다려 주겠소, 아가씨?\""]], [["“No,” said she; and taking her basket Tess trudged on.", "\"아니요,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["A little way forward she turned her head.", "그러고는 바구니를 들고 테스는 터벅터벅 계속 걸어갔다."], ["The old gray wall began to advertise a similar fiery lettering to the first, with a strange and unwonted mien, as if distressed at duties it had never before been called upon to perform.", "조금 앞으로 나아가 그녀는 고개를 돌렸다. 그 오래된 잿빛 벽이 처음 것과 비슷한 불꽃 같은 글자를 광고하기 시작했는데, 이전에는 한 번도 떠맡아 본 적 없는 임무에 괴로워하는 듯 낯설고 어색한 표정이었다."], ["It was with a sudden flush that she read and realized what was to be the inscription he was now halfway through—", "그가 이제 절반쯤 칠해 가는 그 글귀가 무엇이 될지 읽고 깨달았을 때 그녀는 갑작스레 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다—\n너희는, 간음하지, 말지니라—"]], [["# THOU, SHALT, NOT, COMMIT—", "# THOU, SHALT, NOT, COMMIT—"]], [["Her cheerful friend saw her looking, stopped his brush, and shouted—", "그녀의 쾌활한 동행은 그녀가 바라보는 것을 보고 붓질을 멈추고 외쳤다—"]], [["“If you want to ask for edification on these things of moment, there’s a very earnest good man going to preach a charity-sermon to-day in the parish you are going to—Mr Clare of Emminster.", "\"이런 중대한 일들에 대해 가르침을 구하고 싶거든, 오늘 당신이 가려는 교구에서 자선 설교를 할 아주 진지하고 훌륭한 분이 계시오—에민스터의 클레어 목사님이오."], ["I’m not of his persuasion now, but he’s a good man, and he’ll expound as well as any parson I know. ’Twas he began the work in me.”", "난 이제 그분과 같은 신앙은 아니지만, 그분은 좋은 분이고, 내가 아는 어떤 목사보다도 잘 풀어 설명하실 거요. 내 안에서 그 일을 시작하게 하신 분이 바로 그분이오.\""]], [["But Tess did not answer; she throbbingly resumed her walk, her eyes fixed on the ground.", "그러나 테스는 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 가슴을 두근거리며 다시 걸음을 옮겼고, 눈은 땅에 박혀 있었다."], ["“Pooh—I don’t believe God said such things!”", "\"흥—하느님이 그런 말씀을 하셨을 리 없어!\""], ["she murmured contemptuously when her flush had died away.", "얼굴의 홍조가 가시자 그녀는 경멸하듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["A plume of smoke soared up suddenly from her father’s chimney, the sight of which made her heart ache.", "아버지의 굴뚝에서 갑자기 연기 한 줄기가 솟아올랐는데, 그 광경에 그녀의 가슴이 아렸다."], ["The aspect of the interior, when she reached it, made her heart ache more.", "집에 다다랐을 때 안의 모습은 그녀의 가슴을 더욱 아리게 했다."], ["Her mother, who had just come downstairs, turned to greet her from the fireplace, where she was kindling barked-oak twigs under the breakfast kettle.", "막 아래층으로 내려온 어머니가 벽난로 앞에서 그녀를 맞이하려 돌아섰는데, 그곳에서 어머니는 아침 주전자 아래 껍질 벗긴 참나무 잔가지에 불을 붙이고 있었다."], ["The young children were still above, as was also her father, it being Sunday morning, when he felt justified in lying an additional half-hour.", "어린 동생들은 아직 위층에 있었고, 아버지도 마찬가지였다. 일요일 아침이라 아버지는 삼십 분쯤 더 누워 있어도 마땅하다고 여기는 날이었다."]], [["“Well!—my dear Tess!”", "\"이런!—우리 귀여운 테스!\""], ["exclaimed her surprised mother, jumping up and kissing the girl. “How be ye?", "놀란 어머니가 벌떡 일어나 딸에게 입을 맞추며 소리쳤다. \"어떻게 지냈니?"], ["I didn’t see you till you was in upon me!", "네가 내 코앞에 올 때까지 못 봤구나!"], ["Have you come home to be married?”", "결혼하러 집에 온 거니?\""]], [["“No, I have not come for that, mother.”", "\"아니요, 그것 때문에 온 건 아니에요, 어머니.\""]], [["“Then for a holiday?”", "\"그럼 휴가차 온 거니?\""]], [["“Yes—for a holiday; for a long holiday,”", "\"네—휴가차요. 긴 휴가차요,\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“What, isn’t your cousin going to do the handsome thing?”", "\"뭐라고, 네 사촌이 점잖은 도리를 다하지 않겠다는 거니?\""]], [["“He’s not my cousin, and he’s not going to marry me.”", "\"그분은 제 사촌이 아니고, 저와 결혼하지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["Her mother eyed her narrowly.", "어머니는 그녀를 유심히 살폈다."]], [["“Come, you have not told me all,”", "\"이리 와 봐라, 너 나한테 다 말하지 않았구나,\""], ["she said.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["Then Tess went up to her mother, put her face upon Joan’s neck, and told.", "그러자 테스는 어머니에게 다가가 조앤의 목에 얼굴을 묻고 털어놓았다."]], [["“And yet th’st not got him to marry ’ee!”", "\"그런데도 그자를 너랑 결혼시키지 못했단 말이냐!\""], ["reiterated her mother.", "어머니가 거듭 말했다."], ["“Any woman would have done it but you, after that!”", "\"그런 일이 있고서야, 너 말고 어떤 여자라도 다 해냈을 거다!\""]], [["“Perhaps any woman would except me.”", "\"아마 저 말고는 어떤 여자라도 그랬겠죠.\""]], [["“It would have been something like a story to come back with, if you had!”", "\"네가 해냈더라면 돌아와서 들려줄 그럴듯한 이야깃거리가 됐을 텐데!\""], ["continued Mrs Durbeyfield, ready to burst into tears of vexation.", "더비필드 부인은 분해서 눈물을 터뜨릴 듯이 말을 이었다."], ["“After all the talk about you and him which has reached us here, who would have expected it to end like this!", "\"너와 그자에 관한 소문이 죄다 여기까지 들려왔는데, 그게 이렇게 끝날 줄 누가 알았겠니!"], ["Why didn’t ye think of doing some good for your family instead o’ thinking only of yourself?", "너 자신만 생각하지 말고 식구들을 위해 뭔가 좋은 일을 할 생각은 왜 못 했니?"], ["See how I’ve got to teave and slave, and your poor weak father with his heart clogged like a dripping-pan.", "내가 어떻게 죽도록 일하고 종처럼 부려지는지 좀 봐라, 게다가 네 가엾고 약한 아버지는 기름받이처럼 심장이 막혀 있는데."], ["I did hope for something to come out o’ this!", "난 이 일에서 뭔가가 나오기를 정말 바랐다!"], ["To see what a pretty pair you and he made that day when you drove away together four months ago!", "넉 달 전에 너랑 그자가 함께 마차를 타고 떠나던 날 얼마나 어여쁜 한 쌍이었던지!"], ["See what he has given us—all, as we thought, because we were his kin.", "그자가 우리한테 베푼 걸 봐라—우린 다 우리가 그의 친척이라서 그런 줄 알았다."], ["But if he’s not, it must have been done because of his love for ’ee.", "하지만 친척이 아니라면, 그건 분명 너를 사랑해서 한 일일 거야."], ["And yet you’ve not got him to marry!”", "그런데도 넌 그자랑 결혼하지 못했다니!\""]], [["Get Alec d’Urberville in the mind to marry her!", "알렉 더버빌의 마음을 돌려 그녀와 결혼하게 만든다고!"], ["He marry _her_!", "그가 그녀와 결혼한다고!"], ["On matrimony he had never once said a word.", "결혼에 관해서 그는 단 한 마디도 한 적이 없었다."], ["And what if he had?", "또 설령 했다 한들?"], ["How a convulsive snatching at social salvation might have impelled her to answer him she could not say.", "사회적 구원을 향한 발작적인 움켜쥠이 그녀를 어떻게 그에게 대답하도록 내몰았을지 그녀는 알 수 없었다."], ["But her poor foolish mother little knew her present feeling towards this man.", "하지만 가엾고 어리석은 어머니는 이 남자를 향한 그녀의 지금 심정을 거의 알지 못했다."], ["Perhaps it was unusual in the circumstances, unlucky, unaccountable; but there it was; and this, as she had said, was what made her detest herself.", "어쩌면 그것은 이런 상황에서 흔치 않고, 불운하고, 설명할 수 없는 것이었는지도 모른다. 하지만 그것은 분명 거기 있었다. 그리고 이것이, 그녀가 말했듯이, 그녀로 하여금 스스로를 혐오하게 만드는 것이었다."], ["She had never wholly cared for him; she did not at all care for him now.", "그녀는 한 번도 그를 온전히 마음에 둔 적이 없었다. 지금은 조금도 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["She had dreaded him, winced before him, succumbed to adroit advantages he took of her helplessness; then, temporarily blinded by his ardent manners, had been stirred to confused surrender awhile: had suddenly despised and disliked him, and had run away.", "그녀는 그를 두려워했고, 그 앞에서 움찔했고, 그가 그녀의 무력함을 교묘히 이용하는 데 굴복했다. 그러다 그의 열렬한 태도에 잠시 눈이 멀어 한동안 혼란스러운 항복으로 마음이 흔들리기도 했다. 그러다 갑자기 그를 경멸하고 싫어하게 되어 달아났다."], ["That was all.", "그게 전부였다."], ["Hate him she did not quite; but he was dust and ashes to her, and even for her name’s sake she scarcely wished to marry him.", "그를 미워하는 것은 아니었다. 하지만 그는 그녀에게 흙과 재 같은 존재였고, 자기 이름을 위해서라 해도 그와 결혼하고 싶은 마음은 거의 없었다."]], [["“You ought to have been more careful if you didn’t mean to get him to make you his wife!”", "\"그자를 남편으로 삼을 작정이 아니었다면 좀 더 조심했어야지!\""]], [["“O mother, my mother!”", "\"아 어머니, 우리 어머니!\""], ["cried the agonized girl, turning passionately upon her parent as if her poor heart would break.", "괴로워하는 처녀는 가엾은 가슴이 터질 것처럼 격정적으로 어머니에게 돌아서며 울부짖었다."], ["“How could I be expected to know?", "\"제가 어떻게 알 수 있었겠어요?"], ["I was a child when I left this house four months ago.", "넉 달 전 이 집을 떠날 때 전 어린애였어요."], ["Why didn’t you tell me there was danger in men-folk?", "남자들에게 위험이 있다고 왜 말해 주지 않으셨어요?"], ["Why didn’t you warn me?", "왜 경고해 주지 않으셨어요?"], ["Ladies know what to fend hands against, because they read novels that tell them of these tricks; but I never had the chance o’ learning in that way, and you did not help me!”", "귀부인들은 소설을 읽어서 이런 술수들을 알기에 무엇을 막아야 할지 알지만, 전 그런 식으로 배울 기회가 한 번도 없었고, 어머니는 절 도와주지 않으셨어요!\""]], [["Her mother was subdued.", "어머니는 풀이 죽었다."]], [["“I thought if I spoke of his fond feelings and what they might lead to, you would be hontish wi’ him and lose your chance,”", "\"그자의 다정한 감정이랑 그게 어디로 이어질지 말하면, 네가 그자한테 도도하게 굴어서 기회를 놓칠까 봐 그랬다,\""], ["she murmured, wiping her eyes with her apron.", "어머니는 앞치마로 눈을 닦으며 중얼거렸다."], ["“Well, we must make the best of it, I suppose.", "\"뭐, 이제 어쩌겠니, 받아들이는 수밖에."], ["’Tis nater, after all, and what do please God!”", "어쨌든 다 자연의 이치고, 하느님이 기뻐하시는 일이지!\""]], [["# XIII", "# XIII"]], [["The event of Tess Durbeyfield’s return from the manor of her bogus kinsfolk was rumoured abroad, if rumour be not too large a word for a space of a square mile.", "테스 더비필드가 사이비 친척의 영지에서 돌아온 일은 소문으로 널리 퍼졌다—사방 일 마일쯤 되는 공간에 소문이라는 말이 과하지 않다면 말이다."], ["In the afternoon several young girls of Marlott, former schoolfellows and acquaintances of Tess, called to see her, arriving dressed in their best starched and ironed, as became visitors to a person who had made a transcendent conquest (as they supposed), and sat round the room looking at her with great curiosity.", "오후가 되자 마롯의 처녀 몇몇, 테스의 옛 학교 친구들과 아는 이들이 그녀를 보러 찾아왔다. 그들은 (그들이 생각하기에) 비범한 정복을 이뤄낸 사람을 방문하는 데 걸맞게 가장 좋은 옷을 풀 먹여 다림질해 차려입고 와서는, 방 안에 빙 둘러앉아 굉장한 호기심으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["For the fact that it was this said thirty-first cousin, Mr d’Urberville, who had fallen in love with her, a gentleman not altogether local, whose reputation as a reckless gallant and heartbreaker was beginning to spread beyond the immediate boundaries of Trantridge, lent Tess’s supposed position, by its fearsomeness, a far higher fascination that it would have exercised if unhazardous.", "그녀에게 반한 이가 바로 그 서른한 번째 사촌인 더버빌 씨라는 사실—이 지역 토박이만은 아닌 신사로, 무모한 바람둥이이자 여자 마음을 깨뜨리는 자라는 평판이 트랜트리지 인근 경계 너머로 퍼지기 시작한 인물이었기에—이, 그 두려움으로 인해, 위험하지 않았더라면 발휘했을 것보다 훨씬 더 높은 매혹을 테스의 추정된 처지에 더해 주었다."]], [["Their interest was so deep that the younger ones whispered when her back was turned—", "그들의 관심이 어찌나 깊던지 어린 처녀들은 그녀가 등을 돌렸을 때 속닥였다—"]], [["“How pretty she is; and how that best frock do set her off!", "\"쟤 정말 예쁘다. 그리고 저 제일 좋은 옷이 쟤를 얼마나 돋보이게 하는지!"], ["I believe it cost an immense deal, and that it was a gift from him.”", "분명 엄청 비쌌을 거야, 그리고 그 사람이 준 선물일 거야.\""]], [["Tess, who was reaching up to get the tea-things from the corner-cupboard, did not hear these commentaries.", "귀퉁이 찬장에서 찻그릇을 꺼내려고 손을 뻗고 있던 테스는 이런 평을 듣지 못했다."], ["If she had heard them, she might soon have set her friends right on the matter.", "들었더라면 그녀는 친구들의 오해를 곧장 바로잡았을지도 모른다."], ["But her mother heard, and Joan’s simple vanity, having been denied the hope of a dashing marriage, fed itself as well as it could upon the sensation of a dashing flirtation.", "그러나 어머니는 들었고, 화려한 결혼의 희망을 빼앗긴 조앤의 단순한 허영심은 화려한 연애 소동의 화젯거리로나마 한껏 자신을 채웠다. 대체로 그녀는 흡족했다."], ["Upon the whole she felt gratified, even though such a limited and evanescent triumph should involve her daughter’s reputation; it might end in marriage yet, and in the warmth of her responsiveness to their admiration she invited her visitors to stay to tea.", "비록 그런 제한적이고 덧없는 승리가 딸의 평판을 대가로 치를지라도 말이다. 어쩌면 결혼으로 끝날지도 모르는 일이었다. 그리고 그들의 찬탄에 응하는 따뜻함에 겨워, 그녀는 방문객들에게 차를 마시고 가라고 청했다."]], [["Their chatter, their laughter, their good-humoured innuendoes, above all, their flashes and flickerings of envy, revived Tess’s spirits also; and, as the evening wore on, she caught the infection of their excitement, and grew almost gay.", "그들의 수다, 그들의 웃음, 그들의 짓궂은 농지거리, 무엇보다도 그들의 번득이고 가물거리는 시샘이 테스의 기운도 되살려 주었다. 그리고 저녁이 깊어 가면서, 그녀는 그들의 들뜬 흥분에 전염되어 거의 명랑해졌다."], ["The marble hardness left her face, she moved with something of her old bounding step, and flushed in all her young beauty.", "그녀의 얼굴에서 대리석 같은 굳음이 사라졌고, 그녀는 예전의 통통 튀던 걸음새 비슷한 것으로 움직였으며, 젊은 아름다움을 온통 발그레 물들였다."]], [["At moments, in spite of thought, she would reply to their inquiries with a manner of superiority, as if recognizing that her experiences in the field of courtship had, indeed, been slightly enviable.", "이따금, 생각과는 달리, 그녀는 그들의 질문에 우월한 태도로 대답하기도 했다. 마치 구애의 장에서 자신이 겪은 경험이 정말이지 조금은 부러워할 만한 것이었음을 인정하듯이."], ["But so far was she from being, in the words of Robert South, “in love with her own ruin,”", "그러나 로버트 사우스의 말마따나 \"제 자신의 파멸과 사랑에 빠진\""], ["that the illusion was transient as lightning; cold reason came back to mock her spasmodic weakness; the ghastliness of her momentary pride would convict her, and recall her to reserved listlessness again.", "것과는 거리가 멀어서, 그 환상은 번개처럼 덧없었다. 차가운 이성이 돌아와 그녀의 발작적인 약함을 비웃었다. 한순간의 자만이 지녔던 섬뜩함이 그녀를 단죄하고, 다시 속을 닫은 무기력으로 그녀를 불러들이곤 했다."]], [["And the despondency of the next morning’s dawn, when it was no longer Sunday, but Monday; and no best clothes; and the laughing visitors were gone, and she awoke alone in her old bed, the innocent younger children breathing softly around her.", "그리고 이튿날 아침 동틀 녘의 낙담이라니. 더 이상 일요일이 아니라 월요일이었고, 가장 좋은 옷도 없었고, 웃던 방문객들도 가 버렸고, 그녀는 순진한 어린 동생들이 그녀 주위에서 부드럽게 숨 쉬는 가운데 옛 침대에서 홀로 깨어났다."], ["In place of the excitement of her return, and the interest it had inspired, she saw before her a long and stony highway which she had to tread, without aid, and with little sympathy.", "그녀의 귀향이 주던 흥분과 그것이 불러일으킨 관심 대신, 그녀 앞에는 도움도 없이, 동정도 거의 없이 밟아 가야 할 길고 돌투성이인 큰길이 보였다."], ["Her depression was then terrible, and she could have hidden herself in a tomb.", "그때 그녀의 우울은 끔찍했고, 그녀는 무덤 속에라도 숨어 버리고 싶었다."]], [["In the course of a few weeks Tess revived sufficiently to show herself so far as was necessary to get to church one Sunday morning.", "몇 주가 지나는 동안 테스는 어느 일요일 아침 교회에 갈 만큼은 모습을 드러낼 정도로 충분히 기운을 차렸다."], ["She liked to hear the chanting—such as it was—and the old Psalms, and to join in the Morning Hymn.", "그녀는 성가를—그게 어떤 것이든—듣는 것과 오래된 시편을 듣는 것과 아침 찬송에 함께하는 것을 좋아했다."], ["That innate love of melody, which she had inherited from her ballad-singing mother, gave the simplest music a power over her which could well-nigh drag her heart out of her bosom at times.", "발라드를 부르던 어머니에게서 물려받은 그 타고난 가락 사랑은, 가장 단순한 음악에도 그녀를 사로잡는 힘을 부여하여 때로는 그녀의 가슴을 몸 밖으로 끌어낼 지경이었다."]], [["To be as much out of observation as possible for reasons of her own, and to escape the gallantries of the young men, she set out before the chiming began, and took a back seat under the gallery, close to the lumber, where only old men and women came, and where the bier stood on end among the churchyard tools.", "그녀 나름의 이유로 되도록 남의 눈에 띄지 않으려고, 그리고 젊은 남자들의 수작을 피하려고, 그녀는 종이 울리기 전에 길을 나서서 회랑 아래 뒷자리, 잡동사니 가까이, 늙은 남녀들만 오는 곳, 교회 묘지 연장들 사이에 관 받침대가 세워져 있는 그곳에 앉았다."]], [["Parishioners dropped in by twos and threes, deposited themselves in rows before her, rested three-quarters of a minute on their foreheads as if they were praying, though they were not; then sat up, and looked around.", "교구민들이 둘씩 셋씩 들어와서는 그녀 앞 줄줄이 자리 잡고, 기도하는 것도 아니면서 기도하는 양 사분의 삼 분쯤 이마를 손에 기댔다가, 몸을 일으켜 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["When the chants came on, one of her favourites happened to be chosen among the rest—the old double chant “Langdon”—but she did not know what it was called, though she would much have liked to know.", "성가가 시작되자, 그녀가 좋아하는 곡 하나가 마침 그 가운데 골라졌다—오래된 이중창 성가 \"랭던\"이었다—그러나 그녀는 그 곡의 이름을 몰랐다. 무척 알고 싶었지만 말이다."], ["She thought, without exactly wording the thought, how strange and god-like was a composer’s power, who from the grave could lead through sequences of emotion, which he alone had felt at first, a girl like her who had never heard of his name, and never would have a clue to his personality.", "그녀는, 정확히 말로 옮기지는 못한 채로, 작곡가의 힘이 얼마나 기이하고 신과 같은 것인지를 생각했다. 무덤 속에서, 처음엔 자기 혼자만 느꼈던 감정의 흐름을 따라, 그의 이름을 들어 본 적도 없고 그의 인격에 대해 단서 하나 결코 얻지 못할 그녀 같은 처녀를 이끌어 갈 수 있다니."]], [["The people who had turned their heads turned them again as the service proceeded; and at last observing her, they whispered to each other.", "고개를 돌렸던 사람들은 예배가 진행되면서 다시 고개를 돌렸다. 그러다 마침내 그녀를 알아보고는 서로 속닥였다."], ["She knew what their whispers were about, grew sick at heart, and felt that she could come to church no more.", "그녀는 그들의 속닥임이 무엇에 관한 것인지 알았고, 가슴이 메스꺼워졌으며, 더 이상 교회에 올 수 없겠다고 느꼈다."]], [["The bedroom which she shared with some of the children formed her retreat more continually than ever.", "동생 몇과 함께 쓰는 침실이 그 어느 때보다도 더 끊임없이 그녀의 은신처가 되었다."], ["Here, under her few square yards of thatch, she watched winds, and snows, and rains, gorgeous sunsets, and successive moons at their full.", "여기, 몇 평방 야드의 초가지붕 아래에서, 그녀는 바람과 눈과 비, 화려한 일몰, 그리고 잇따라 보름달이 차는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["So close kept she that at length almost everybody thought she had gone away.", "어찌나 틀어박혀 지냈던지 마침내 거의 모든 사람이 그녀가 떠나간 줄로 여겼다."]], [["The only exercise that Tess took at this time was after dark; and it was then, when out in the woods, that she seemed least solitary.", "이 무렵 테스가 하는 유일한 운동은 어두워진 뒤였다. 그리고 숲속에 나가 있는 그때야말로 그녀가 가장 덜 외로워 보이는 때였다."], ["She knew how to hit to a hair’s-breadth that moment of evening when the light and the darkness are so evenly balanced that the constraint of day and the suspense of night neutralize each other, leaving absolute mental liberty.", "그녀는 빛과 어둠이 어찌나 고르게 균형을 이루는지 낮의 속박과 밤의 긴장이 서로를 상쇄하여 절대적인 정신의 자유만 남는, 저녁의 그 순간을 머리카락 한 올의 너비까지도 정확히 맞출 줄 알았다."], ["It is then that the plight of being alive becomes attenuated to its least possible dimensions.", "바로 그때야말로 살아 있다는 곤경이 가능한 한 가장 작은 차원으로 옅어지는 때였다. 그녀는 그림자를 두려워하지 않았다."], ["She had no fear of the shadows; her sole idea seemed to be to shun mankind—or rather that cold accretion called the world, which, so terrible in the mass, is so unformidable, even pitiable, in its units.", "그녀의 유일한 생각은 인간을 피하는 것—아니 차라리 세상이라 불리는 저 차가운 응결물을 피하는 것뿐인 듯했는데, 그것은 무리로는 그토록 무섭지만, 낱낱으로는 그토록 두렵지 않고 심지어 가엾기까지 한 것이었다."]], [["On these lonely hills and dales her quiescent glide was of a piece with the element she moved in.", "이 외로운 언덕과 골짜기에서 그녀의 고요한 미끄러짐은 그녀가 움직이는 그 환경과 한 조각으로 어우러졌다."], ["Her flexuous and stealthy figure became an integral part of the scene.", "그녀의 유연하고 은밀한 자태는 그 풍경의 본질적인 일부가 되었다."], ["At times her whimsical fancy would intensify natural processes around her till they seemed a part of her own story.", "때때로 그녀의 변덕스러운 공상은 주위의 자연스러운 과정들을 강렬하게 부풀려 그것들이 그녀 자신의 이야기의 일부인 듯 보이게 만들었다."], ["Rather they became a part of it; for the world is only a psychological phenomenon, and what they seemed they were.", "아니 차라리 그것들은 그 일부가 되었다. 세상이란 오직 심리적 현상일 뿐이고, 그것들이 그렇게 보이는 대로 그것들은 그러했으니까."], ["The midnight airs and gusts, moaning amongst the tightly-wrapped buds and bark of the winter twigs, were formulae of bitter reproach.", "겨울 잔가지의 단단히 감긴 봉오리와 껍질 사이에서 신음하는 한밤의 미풍과 돌풍은 쓰라린 책망의 정식이었다."], ["A wet day was the expression of irremediable grief at her weakness in the mind of some vague ethical being whom she could not class definitely as the God of her childhood, and could not comprehend as any other.", "비 오는 날은 어린 시절의 하느님으로 명확히 분류할 수도 없고 다른 어떤 것으로도 이해할 수 없는, 어떤 막연한 윤리적 존재의 마음속에서, 그녀의 약함에 대한 돌이킬 수 없는 슬픔의 표현이었다."]], [["But this encompassment of her own characterization, based on shreds of convention, peopled by phantoms and voices antipathetic to her, was a sorry and mistaken creation of Tess’s fancy—a cloud of moral hobgoblins by which she was terrified without reason.", "그러나 관습의 누더기에 기초하고, 그녀에게 적대적인 환영과 목소리들이 들끓는, 그녀 자신을 이렇게 규정짓는 이 에워쌈은 테스의 공상이 빚어낸 가련하고 잘못된 창조물이었다—아무런 까닭도 없이 그녀를 겁먹게 한 도덕적 도깨비들의 구름이었다."], ["It was they that were out of harmony with the actual world, not she.", "실제 세계와 조화를 이루지 못하는 것은 그녀가 아니라 바로 그것들이었다."], ["Walking among the sleeping birds in the hedges, watching the skipping rabbits on a moonlit warren, or standing under a pheasant-laden bough, she looked upon herself as a figure of Guilt intruding into the haunts of Innocence.", "산울타리에서 잠든 새들 사이를 걷거나, 달빛 비친 토끼굴에서 깡충거리는 토끼들을 바라보거나, 꿩이 잔뜩 앉은 나뭇가지 아래 서 있을 때, 그녀는 자신을 죄의 형상이 순결의 안식처에 침입한 것으로 여겼다."], ["But all the while she was making a distinction where there was no difference.", "그러나 그러는 내내 그녀는 차이가 없는 곳에서 구별을 짓고 있었다."], ["Feeling herself in antagonism, she was quite in accord.", "자신이 적대 관계에 있다고 느꼈으나, 그녀는 더없이 조화를 이루고 있었다."], ["She had been made to break an accepted social law, but no law known to the environment in which she fancied herself such an anomaly.", "그녀는 받아들여진 어떤 사회적 법을 어기게 되었으나, 그녀가 자신을 그런 변칙이라고 상상한 그 환경에 알려진 어떤 법도 어긴 것이 아니었다."]], [["# XIV", "# XIV"]], [["It was a hazy sunrise in August.", "팔월의 어느 안개 낀 일출이었다."], ["The denser nocturnal vapours, attacked by the warm beams, were dividing and shrinking into isolated fleeces within hollows and coverts, where they waited till they should be dried away to nothing.", "더 짙은 밤의 수증기들이 따뜻한 햇살의 공격을 받아 갈라지고 오그라들어, 움푹한 곳과 은폐된 곳마다 외따로 떨어진 양털 뭉치들이 되어, 거기서 흔적도 없이 말라 사라질 때를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["The sun, on account of the mist, had a curious sentient, personal look, demanding the masculine pronoun for its adequate expression.", "안개 탓에 해는 묘하게도 지각이 있는 듯, 인격적인 모습을 띠고 있어, 그것을 제대로 표현하려면 남성 대명사를 요구했다."], ["His present aspect, coupled with the lack of all human forms in the scene, explained the old-time heliolatries in a moment.", "그 지금의 모습은, 풍경 속에 인간의 형상이 일체 없는 것과 맞물려, 옛날의 태양 숭배를 단숨에 설명해 주었다."], ["One could feel that a saner religion had never prevailed under the sky.", "하늘 아래 이보다 더 온전한 종교가 일찍이 없었음을 느낄 수 있었다."], ["The luminary was a golden-haired, beaming, mild-eyed, God-like creature, gazing down in the vigour and intentness of youth upon an earth that was brimming with interest for him.", "그 발광체는 금발에 빛을 내뿜고 온화한 눈을 한 신과 같은 존재로, 젊음의 활기와 열중함으로 자기에게 흥미가 넘쳐흐르는 대지를 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["His light, a little later, broke though chinks of cottage shutters, throwing stripes like red-hot pokers upon cupboards, chests of drawers, and other furniture within; and awakening harvesters who were not already astir.", "조금 뒤 그의 빛이 오두막 덧창 틈으로 새어들어, 안의 찬장과 서랍장과 다른 가구들 위에 시뻘겋게 단 부지깽이 같은 줄무늬를 던졌다. 그리고 아직 일어나지 않은 추수꾼들을 깨웠다."]], [["But of all ruddy things that morning the brightest were two broad arms of painted wood, which rose from the margin of yellow cornfield hard by Marlott village.", "그러나 그날 아침 모든 붉은 것 가운데 가장 밝은 것은 칠을 한 나무로 된 두 개의 넓은 팔이었으니, 그것은 마롯 마을 바로 옆 노란 밀밭의 가장자리에서 솟아 있었다."], ["They, with two others below, formed the revolving Maltese cross of the reaping-machine, which had been brought to the field on the previous evening to be ready for operations this day.", "그것들은 아래쪽의 다른 두 개와 더불어 수확기의 회전하는 몰타 십자가를 이루었는데, 그 기계는 오늘 작업을 준비하려고 전날 저녁에 밭으로 옮겨져 와 있었다."], ["The paint with which they were smeared, intensified in hue by the sunlight, imparted to them a look of having been dipped in liquid fire.", "그것들에 발린 페인트는 햇빛에 빛깔이 더욱 짙어져, 마치 액체 불에 담갔다 꺼낸 듯한 모습이었다."]], [["The field had already been “opened”; that is to say, a lane a few feet wide had been hand-cut through the wheat along the whole circumference of the field for the first passage of the horses and machine.", "밭은 이미 \"열려\" 있었다. 다시 말해, 말들과 기계가 처음 지나갈 수 있도록 밭의 둘레 전체를 따라 밀 사이로 몇 피트 너비의 길이 손으로 베어져 있었다."]], [["Two groups, one of men and lads, the other of women, had come down the lane just at the hour when the shadows of the eastern hedge-top struck the west hedge midway, so that the heads of the groups were enjoying sunrise while their feet were still in the dawn.", "남자들과 사내아이들로 이루어진 무리 하나와 여자들로 이루어진 다른 무리 하나가, 마침 동쪽 산울타리 꼭대기의 그림자가 서쪽 산울타리 중간께를 때리는 그 시각에 길을 따라 내려와서, 무리들의 머리는 일출을 누리는 동안 발은 아직 새벽녘에 잠겨 있었다."], ["They disappeared from the lane between the two stone posts which flanked the nearest field-gate.", "그들은 가장 가까운 밭문 양옆에 선 두 개의 돌기둥 사이로 길에서 사라졌다."]], [["Presently there arose from within a ticking like the love-making of the grasshopper.", "이윽고 안쪽에서 메뚜기의 구애 같은 째깍거리는 소리가 일어났다."], ["The machine had begun, and a moving concatenation of three horses and the aforesaid long rickety machine was visible over the gate, a driver sitting upon one of the hauling horses, and an attendant on the seat of the implement.", "기계가 시작된 것이었고, 말 세 마리와 앞서 말한 길고 삐걱대는 기계가 움직이며 한데 엮인 모습이 문 너머로 보였다. 끄는 말 한 마리에 마부가 올라타 있었고, 기구의 좌석에는 보조 한 사람이 앉아 있었다."], ["Along one side of the field the whole wain went, the arms of the mechanical reaper revolving slowly, till it passed down the hill quite out of sight.", "밭의 한쪽 가장자리를 따라 짐수레 전체가 나아갔고, 기계 수확기의 팔들이 천천히 회전하다가, 언덕 아래로 지나가 완전히 시야에서 사라졌다."], ["In a minute it came up on the other side of the field at the same equable pace; the glistening brass star in the forehead of the fore horse first catching the eye as it rose into view over the stubble, then the bright arms, and then the whole machine.", "잠시 후 그것은 같은 한결같은 속도로 밭의 반대편에서 올라왔다. 앞선 말의 이마에 달린 반짝이는 놋쇠 별이 그루터기 위로 솟아오르며 먼저 눈에 들어왔고, 그다음 밝은 팔들이, 그다음 기계 전체가 보였다."]], [["The narrow lane of stubble encompassing the field grew wider with each circuit, and the standing corn was reduced to a smaller area as the morning wore on.", "밭을 에워싼 좁은 그루터기 길은 한 바퀴 돌 때마다 넓어졌고, 서 있는 밀은 아침이 깊어 갈수록 점점 작은 면적으로 줄어들었다."], ["Rabbits, hares, snakes, rats, mice, retreated inwards as into a fastness, unaware of the ephemeral nature of their refuge, and of the doom that awaited them later in the day when, their covert shrinking to a more and more horrible narrowness, they were huddled together, friends and foes, till the last few yards of upright wheat fell also under the teeth of the unerring reaper, and they were every one put to death by the sticks and stones of the harvesters.", "토끼며 산토끼며 뱀이며 쥐며 생쥐가, 자기네 피난처의 덧없는 성질도, 그리고 그날 늦게 닥칠 운명도 모른 채, 요새로 들어가듯 안쪽으로 물러났다. 그날 늦게, 그들의 은신처가 점점 더 끔찍하게 좁아져 들면, 적과 동지가 한데 옹송그려 모이게 되고, 마침내 마지막 몇 야드 남은 곧추선 밀마저 빗나감 없는 수확기의 이빨 아래 쓰러지면, 추수꾼들의 막대기와 돌멩이에 그들은 하나도 빠짐없이 죽임을 당하게 될 터였다."]], [["The reaping-machine left the fallen corn behind it in little heaps, each heap being of the quantity for a sheaf; and upon these the active binders in the rear laid their hands—mainly women, but some of them men in print shirts, and trousers supported round their waists by leather straps, rendering useless the two buttons behind, which twinkled and bristled with sunbeams at every movement of each wearer, as if they were a pair of eyes in the small of his back.", "수확기는 쓰러진 밀을 작은 더미로 뒤에 남겼는데, 더미마다 한 단이 될 만한 양이었다. 그리고 그 위에 뒤따르던 부지런한 결속꾼들이 손을 댔다—주로 여자들이었지만, 더러는 사라사 셔츠를 입고 허리께를 가죽끈으로 동여 바지를 받친 남자들도 있었으니, 그 바람에 뒤의 단추 두 개가 쓸모없어져, 움직일 때마다 햇살에 반짝이며 곤두서서, 마치 등허리에 박힌 한 쌍의 눈 같았다."]], [["But those of the other sex were the most interesting of this company of binders, by reason of the charm which is acquired by woman when she becomes part and parcel of outdoor nature, and is not merely an object set down therein as at ordinary times.", "그러나 이 결속꾼 무리 가운데 가장 흥미로운 것은 다른 성, 즉 여자들이었으니, 여자가 야외 자연의 한 부분이 되어, 보통 때처럼 그저 거기 놓인 사물에 그치지 않게 될 때 얻는 매력 때문이었다."], ["A field-man is a personality afield; a field-woman is a portion of the field; she had somehow lost her own margin, imbibed the essence of her surrounding, and assimilated herself with it.", "들판의 남자는 들판에서 하나의 인격이지만, 들판의 여자는 들판의 일부분이었다. 그녀는 어쩐지 제 자신의 윤곽을 잃고, 주위의 정수를 빨아들여, 그것과 자신을 동화시켜 버린 것이다."]], [["The women—or rather girls, for they were mostly young—wore drawn cotton bonnets with great flapping curtains to keep off the sun, and gloves to prevent their hands being wounded by the stubble.", "여자들은—아니 차라리 처녀들이라 해야겠다, 대부분 젊었으니까—햇볕을 막으려고 펄럭이는 커다란 차양이 달린, 끈으로 조인 무명 보닛을 쓰고, 손이 그루터기에 다치지 않게 장갑을 끼고 있었다."], ["There was one wearing a pale pink jacket, another in a cream-coloured tight-sleeved gown, another in a petticoat as red as the arms of the reaping-machine; and others, older, in the brown-rough “wropper”", "한 사람은 연분홍 재킷을 입었고, 또 한 사람은 크림빛의 소매가 꼭 끼는 가운을 입었으며, 또 한 사람은 수확기의 팔처럼 새빨간 페티코트를 입었다. 그리고 더 나이 든 다른 이들은 갈색의 거친 \"통옷\""], ["or over-all—the old-established and most appropriate dress of the field-woman, which the young ones were abandoning.", "즉 작업복을 입었으니—오래전부터 자리 잡은, 들판 여자에게 가장 알맞은 옷이건만, 젊은 처녀들은 그것을 버려 가고 있었다."], ["This morning the eye returns involuntarily to the girl in the pink cotton jacket, she being the most flexuous and finely-drawn figure of them all.", "이날 아침 눈길은 절로 분홍 무명 재킷을 입은 처녀에게 되돌아간다. 그녀가 그들 가운데 가장 유연하고 곱게 빠진 자태였기 때문이다."], ["But her bonnet is pulled so far over her brow that none of her face is disclosed while she binds, though her complexion may be guessed from a stray twine or two of dark brown hair which extends below the curtain of her bonnet.", "그러나 그녀의 보닛이 이마 위로 어찌나 깊이 당겨져 있던지 결속하는 동안에는 얼굴이 조금도 드러나지 않았다. 다만 보닛 차양 아래로 흘러나온 짙은 갈색 머리칼 한두 가닥으로 그녀의 살빛을 짐작할 수는 있었다."], ["Perhaps one reason why she seduces casual attention is that she never courts it, though the other women often gaze around them.", "어쩌면 그녀가 무심한 시선을 끄는 한 가지 이유는 다른 여자들은 자주 주위를 둘러보는데 그녀는 결코 그것을 구하지 않기 때문이리라."]], [["Her binding proceeds with clock-like monotony.", "그녀의 결속은 시계 같은 단조로움으로 진행되었다."], ["From the sheaf last finished she draws a handful of ears, patting their tips with her left palm to bring them even.", "마지막으로 끝낸 단에서 그녀는 한 움큼의 이삭을 뽑아, 왼쪽 손바닥으로 그 끝을 토닥여 고르게 했다."], ["Then, stooping low, she moves forward, gathering the corn with both hands against her knees, and pushing her left gloved hand under the bundle to meet the right on the other side, holding the corn in an embrace like that of a lover.", "그러고는 몸을 낮게 숙여 앞으로 나아가며, 두 손으로 밀을 무릎에 대고 그러모은 다음, 장갑 낀 왼손을 다발 밑으로 밀어 넣어 반대편의 오른손과 맞닿게 하여, 연인을 끌어안듯 밀을 품에 안았다."], ["She brings the ends of the bond together, and kneels on the sheaf while she ties it, beating back her skirts now and then when lifted by the breeze.", "그녀는 매듭끈의 양 끝을 한데 모으고, 단 위에 무릎을 꿇고 그것을 묶었으며, 미풍에 치마가 들릴 때마다 이따금 치마를 쳐서 내렸다."], ["A bit of her naked arm is visible between the buff leather of the gauntlet and the sleeve of her gown; and as the day wears on its feminine smoothness becomes scarified by the stubble and bleeds.", "손목을 감싼 담황색 가죽 토시와 가운 소매 사이로 맨팔 한 자락이 보였다. 그리고 하루가 깊어 갈수록 그 여성스러운 매끄러움은 그루터기에 긁혀 상처가 나고 피가 났다."]], [["At intervals she stands up to rest, and to retie her disarranged apron, or to pull her bonnet straight.", "이따금 그녀는 몸을 일으켜 쉬면서, 흐트러진 앞치마를 다시 매거나 보닛을 바로 당겼다."], ["Then one can see the oval face of a handsome young woman with deep dark eyes and long heavy clinging tresses, which seem to clasp in a beseeching way anything they fall against.", "그러면 깊고 검은 눈과 길고 묵직하게 들러붙는 머리채를 가진 어여쁜 젊은 여인의 갸름한 얼굴이 보였는데, 그 머리채는 무엇에든 닿으면 애원하듯 그것을 끌어안는 듯했다."], ["The cheeks are paler, the teeth more regular, the red lips thinner than is usual in a country-bred girl.", "뺨은 시골에서 자란 처녀에게 흔한 것보다 더 창백했고, 이는 더 가지런했으며, 붉은 입술은 더 가늘었다."]], [["It is Tess Durbeyfield, otherwise d’Urberville, somewhat changed—the same, but not the same; at the present stage of her existence living as a stranger and an alien here, though it was no strange land that she was in.", "그것은 테스 더비필드, 달리 말하면 더버빌이었으니, 다소 달라져 있었다—같은 사람이면서도 같은 사람이 아니었다. 그녀가 있는 곳이 낯선 땅이 아니었음에도, 그녀의 지금 삶의 단계에서 그녀는 여기서 이방인이자 타지인으로 살고 있었다."], ["After a long seclusion she had come to a resolve to undertake outdoor work in her native village, the busiest season of the year in the agricultural world having arrived, and nothing that she could do within the house being so remunerative for the time as harvesting in the fields.", "오랜 칩거 끝에 그녀는 고향 마을에서 야외 일을 떠맡기로 결심했으니, 농사 세계에서 일 년 중 가장 바쁜 철이 왔고, 그녀가 집 안에서 할 수 있는 어떤 일도 당장은 밭에서 추수하는 것만큼 벌이가 되지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["The movements of the other women were more or less similar to Tess’s, the whole bevy of them drawing together like dancers in a quadrille at the completion of a sheaf by each, every one placing her sheaf on end against those of the rest, till a shock, or “stitch” as it was here called, of ten or a dozen was formed.", "다른 여자들의 동작도 테스의 것과 어느 정도 비슷했으니, 한 사람 한 사람이 한 단을 끝낼 때마다 무리 전체가 사방춤을 추는 무용수들처럼 한데 모여들어, 저마다 제 단을 다른 이들의 단에 기대 세우다가, 마침내 여남은 개로 이루어진 한 무더기, 여기서 부르는 말로 \"낟가리\"가 만들어졌다."]], [["They went to breakfast, and came again, and the work proceeded as before.", "그들은 아침을 먹으러 갔다가 다시 왔고, 일은 전과 같이 진행되었다."], ["As the hour of eleven drew near a person watching her might have noticed that every now and then Tess’s glance flitted wistfully to the brow of the hill, though she did not pause in her sheafing.", "열한 시가 가까워지자, 그녀를 지켜보는 사람이라면 이따금 테스의 눈길이 단 묶는 일을 멈추지는 않으면서도 애틋하게 언덕 마루로 흘끔거리는 것을 알아챘을지도 모른다."], ["On the verge of the hour the heads of a group of children, of ages ranging from six to fourteen, rose over the stubbly convexity of the hill.", "그 시각이 다 되어 갈 무렵, 여섯 살에서 열네 살에 이르는 한 무리의 아이들 머리가 그루터기 덮인 언덕의 볼록한 등성이 위로 솟아올랐다."]], [["The face of Tess flushed slightly, but still she did not pause.", "테스의 얼굴이 살짝 붉어졌으나, 그래도 그녀는 멈추지 않았다."]], [["The eldest of the comers, a girl who wore a triangular shawl, its corner draggling on the stubble, carried in her arms what at first sight seemed to be a doll, but proved to be an infant in long clothes.", "오는 이들 가운데 맏이인, 삼각형 숄을 두르고 그 모서리를 그루터기에 질질 끌고 오는 한 처녀가, 첫눈에는 인형처럼 보였으나 알고 보니 긴 배내옷을 입은 갓난아기인 것을 두 팔에 안고 있었다."], ["Another brought some lunch. The harvesters ceased working, took their provisions, and sat down against one of the shocks.", "또 한 아이는 점심을 좀 가져왔다. 추수꾼들은 일손을 멈추고 음식을 챙겨, 낟가리 하나에 기대 앉았다."], ["Here they fell to, the men plying a stone jar freely, and passing round a cup.", "여기서 그들은 먹기 시작했는데, 남자들은 돌 단지를 거리낌 없이 들이켜며 잔을 돌렸다."]], [["Tess Durbeyfield had been one of the last to suspend her labours.", "테스 더비필드는 일손을 멈춘 마지막 사람 중 하나였다."], ["She sat down at the end of the shock, her face turned somewhat away from her companions.", "그녀는 낟가리 끝에 앉았는데, 얼굴을 동료들에게서 다소 돌린 채였다."], ["When she had deposited herself a man in a rabbit-skin cap, and with a red handkerchief tucked into his belt, held the cup of ale over the top of the shock for her to drink. But she did not accept his offer.", "그녀가 자리를 잡자, 토끼 가죽 모자를 쓰고 허리띠에 붉은 손수건을 찔러 넣은 한 남자가 그녀가 마시도록 에일 잔을 낟가리 너머로 내밀었다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 권유를 받아들이지 않았다."], ["As soon as her lunch was spread she called up the big girl, her sister, and took the baby of her, who, glad to be relieved of the burden, went away to the next shock and joined the other children playing there.", "점심을 펼쳐 놓자마자 그녀는 그 큰 처녀, 곧 여동생을 불러 아기를 받아 들었고, 짐을 덜게 되어 기뻐한 여동생은 다음 낟가리로 가서 거기서 놀고 있는 다른 아이들과 어울렸다."], ["Tess, with a curiously stealthy yet courageous movement, and with a still rising colour, unfastened her frock and began suckling the child.", "테스는, 묘하게 은밀하면서도 용감한 동작으로, 그리고 여전히 붉어 오르는 낯빛으로, 옷을 풀고 아기에게 젖을 물리기 시작했다."]], [["The men who sat nearest considerately turned their faces towards the other end of the field, some of them beginning to smoke; one, with absent-minded fondness, regretfully stroking the jar that would no longer yield a stream.", "가장 가까이 앉은 남자들은 사려 깊게 얼굴을 밭의 반대편 끝으로 돌렸고, 더러는 담배를 피우기 시작했다. 한 사람은 멍하니 애착을 담아, 더는 한 줄기도 내주지 않을 단지를 아쉬운 듯 쓰다듬었다."], ["All the women but Tess fell into animated talk, and adjusted the disarranged knots of their hair.", "테스를 제외한 모든 여자가 활기찬 이야기에 빠져들어, 흐트러진 머리 매듭을 다듬었다."]], [["When the infant had taken its fill, the young mother sat it upright in her lap, and looking into the far distance, dandled it with a gloomy indifference that was almost dislike; then all of a sudden she fell to violently kissing it some dozens of times, as if she could never leave off, the child crying at the vehemence of an onset which strangely combined passionateness with contempt.", "아기가 배불리 먹자, 젊은 어머니는 무릎 위에 아기를 똑바로 앉히고, 먼 곳을 바라보며, 거의 싫어하는 것에 가까운 침울한 무심함으로 아기를 어르다가, 그러다 갑자기 결코 그칠 수 없다는 듯 격렬하게 수십 번 입을 맞추기 시작했고, 격정과 경멸이 기이하게 뒤섞인 그 맹렬함에 아기는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“She’s fond of that there child, though she mid pretend to hate en, and say she wishes the baby and her too were in the churchyard,”", "\"쟤는 저 애를 미워하는 척하고, 저랑 아기랑 둘 다 교회 묘지에 있었으면 좋겠다고 말은 해도, 사실은 저 애를 끔찍이 아껴,\""], ["observed the woman in the red petticoat.", "붉은 페티코트를 입은 여자가 한마디 했다."]], [["“She’ll soon leave off saying that,” replied the one in buff.", "\"곧 그런 말 안 하게 될 거야,\" 담황색 옷을 입은 여자가 대꾸했다."], ["“Lord, ’tis wonderful what a body can get used to o’ that sort in time!”", "\"원, 사람이 그런 걸 시간이 지나면 어떻게든 익숙해지는 게 참 신기하다니까!\""]], [["“A little more than persuading had to do wi’ the coming o’t, I reckon.", "\"설득보다 좀 더한 게 그 일이 생기는 데 작용했지 싶어."], ["There were they that heard a sobbing one night last year in The Chase; and it mid ha’ gone hard wi’ a certain party if folks had come along.”", "작년에 어느 날 밤 체이스 숲에서 흐느끼는 소리를 들은 이들이 있었거든. 그리고 사람들이 마침 지나갔더라면 어떤 자한테는 일이 험하게 됐을지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Well, a little more, or a little less, ’twas a thousand pities that it should have happened to she, of all others. But ’tis always the comeliest!", "\"뭐, 좀 더했든 좀 덜했든, 하필이면 다른 누구도 아닌 쟤한테 그런 일이 생기다니 천 번 만 번 안됐어. 하지만 늘 제일 고운 애한테 그런 일이 생긴다니까!"], ["The plain ones be as safe as churches—hey, Jenny?”", "못생긴 애들은 교회처럼 안전하지—그렇지, 제니?\""], ["The speaker turned to one of the group who certainly was not ill-defined as plain.", "말한 여자는 무리 가운데 한 사람을 돌아보았는데, 그 사람은 분명 못생겼다고 해도 부족함이 없는 이였다."]], [["It was a thousand pities, indeed; it was impossible for even an enemy to feel otherwise on looking at Tess as she sat there, with her flower-like mouth and large tender eyes, neither black nor blue nor grey nor violet; rather all those shades together, and a hundred others, which could be seen if one looked into their irises—shade behind shade—tint beyond tint—around pupils that had no bottom; an almost standard woman, but for the slight incautiousness of character inherited from her race.", "정말이지 천 번 만 번 안된 일이었다. 거기 앉아 있는 테스를 바라보면, 적이라 해도 달리 느끼기란 불가능했다. 꽃 같은 입과 크고 부드러운 눈—검지도 푸르지도 잿빛도 보랏빛도 아닌, 차라리 그 모든 빛깔이 한데 어우러진 데다, 그 홍채를 들여다보면 보이는 또 다른 백 가지 빛깔—빛깔 뒤에 빛깔, 색조 너머 색조, 바닥이 없는 동공을 둘러싼—거의 표준이라 할 만한 여인이었다. 그녀의 혈통에서 물려받은 성격의 사소한 경솔함만 아니었다면 말이다."]], [["A resolution which had surprised herself had brought her into the fields this week for the first time during many months.", "그녀 자신도 놀란 어떤 결심이 그녀를 여러 달 만에 처음으로 이번 주에 밭으로 데려온 것이었다."], ["After wearing and wasting her palpitating heart with every engine of regret that lonely inexperience could devise, common sense had illuminated her.", "외로운 미숙함이 짜낼 수 있는 온갖 후회의 기관으로 두근거리는 가슴을 닳리고 소모한 끝에, 상식이 그녀를 밝혀 주었다."], ["She felt that she would do well to be useful again—to taste anew sweet independence at any price.", "그녀는 다시 쓸모 있는 사람이 되는 게—어떤 대가를 치르더라도 다시금 달콤한 독립을 맛보는 게 좋겠다고 느꼈다."], ["The past was past; whatever it had been, it was no more at hand.", "과거는 과거였다. 그것이 어떠했든, 더는 코앞에 있지 않았다."], ["Whatever its consequences, time would close over them; they would all in a few years be as if they had never been, and she herself grassed down and forgotten.", "그 결과가 무엇이든, 시간이 그 위를 덮어 버릴 터였다. 몇 년만 지나면 그것들은 다 처음부터 없었던 것처럼 될 테고, 그녀 자신도 풀 아래 묻혀 잊힐 터였다."], ["Meanwhile the trees were just as green as before; the birds sang and the sun shone as clearly now as ever.", "그러는 사이에도 나무들은 예전과 똑같이 푸르렀고, 새들은 노래했고, 해는 여전히 변함없이 환하게 빛났다."], ["The familiar surroundings had not darkened because of her grief, nor sickened because of her pain.", "낯익은 주위 풍경은 그녀의 슬픔 때문에 어두워지지도 않았고, 그녀의 고통 때문에 병들지도 않았다."]], [["She might have seen that what had bowed her head so profoundly—the thought of the world’s concern at her situation—was founded on an illusion.", "그녀의 고개를 그토록 깊이 숙이게 한 것—제 처지에 대한 세상의 관심이라는 생각—이 환상에 기초한 것임을, 그녀는 깨달았어야 했다."], ["She was not an existence, an experience, a passion, a structure of sensations, to anybody but herself.", "그녀는 자기 자신을 제외한 그 누구에게도 하나의 존재도, 하나의 경험도, 하나의 열정도, 하나의 감각의 구조물도 아니었다."], ["To all humankind besides, Tess was only a passing thought.", "그 밖의 온 인류에게 테스는 그저 스쳐 가는 생각 하나일 뿐이었다."], ["Even to friends she was no more than a frequently passing thought.", "친구들에게조차 그녀는 자주 스쳐 가는 생각 이상은 아니었다."], ["If she made herself miserable the livelong night and day it was only this much to them—“Ah, she makes herself unhappy.”", "그녀가 밤낮 내내 자신을 비참하게 만든다 한들, 그들에게는 고작 이만큼이었다—\"아, 쟤가 자기를 불행하게 만드는구나.\""], ["If she tried to be cheerful, to dismiss all care, to take pleasure in the daylight, the flowers, the baby, she could only be this idea to them—“Ah, she bears it very well.”", "그녀가 명랑하려 애쓰고, 온갖 근심을 떨치고, 햇빛과 꽃과 아기에서 기쁨을 누리려 한다 한들, 그들에게 그녀는 고작 이런 생각일 수 있을 뿐이었다—\"아, 쟤가 그걸 아주 잘 견디는구나.\""], ["Moreover, alone in a desert island would she have been wretched at what had happened to her? Not greatly.", "게다가 무인도에 홀로 있었더라면 그녀에게 닥친 일로 인해 비참해졌을까? 그리 심하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["If she could have been but just created, to discover herself as a spouseless mother, with no experience of life except as the parent of a nameless child, would the position have caused her to despair?", "만약 그녀가 이제 막 창조되어, 이름 없는 아이의 어버이라는 것 말고는 삶에 대한 아무런 경험도 없이, 남편 없는 어머니인 자신을 발견하게 되었다면, 그 처지가 그녀를 절망케 했을까?"], ["No, she would have taken it calmly, and found pleasure therein.", "아니, 그녀는 그것을 차분히 받아들이고 거기서 기쁨을 찾았을 것이다."], ["Most of the misery had been generated by her conventional aspect, and not by her innate sensations.", "비참함의 대부분은 그녀의 타고난 감각이 아니라 그녀의 관습적인 겉모습이 만들어 낸 것이었다."]], [["Whatever Tess’s reasoning, some spirit had induced her to dress herself up neatly as she had formerly done, and come out into the fields, harvest-hands being greatly in demand just then.", "테스의 추론이 무엇이었든, 어떤 정신이 그녀를 부추겨 예전에 그랬던 것처럼 말끔하게 차려입고 밭으로 나오게 했으니, 마침 그때 추수 일손이 무척 모자라던 참이었다."], ["This was why she had borne herself with dignity, and had looked people calmly in the face at times, even when holding the baby in her arms.", "이것이 그녀가 위엄을 지니고 처신하며, 심지어 아기를 품에 안고 있을 때조차도 이따금 사람들의 얼굴을 차분히 마주 볼 수 있었던 까닭이다."]], [["The harvest-men rose from the shock of corn, and stretched their limbs, and extinguished their pipes.", "추수꾼 남자들이 밀 낟가리에서 일어나 팔다리를 쭉 펴고 담뱃대를 껐다."], ["The horses, which had been unharnessed and fed, were again attached to the scarlet machine.", "마구를 풀고 먹이를 주었던 말들이 다시 진홍빛 기계에 매였다."], ["Tess, having quickly eaten her own meal, beckoned to her eldest sister to come and take away the baby, fastened her dress, put on the buff gloves again, and stooped anew to draw a bond from the last completed sheaf for the tying of the next.", "테스는 제 식사를 얼른 마치고는 맏여동생에게 와서 아기를 데려가라고 손짓하고, 옷을 여미고, 담황색 장갑을 다시 끼고, 다음 단을 묶을 매듭끈을 마지막으로 끝낸 단에서 뽑으려고 다시 몸을 숙였다."]], [["In the afternoon and evening the proceedings of the morning were continued, Tess staying on till dusk with the body of harvesters.", "오후와 저녁에도 아침의 일이 이어졌고, 테스는 추수꾼 무리와 함께 해 질 녘까지 남아 있었다."], ["Then they all rode home in one of the largest wagons, in the company of a broad tarnished moon that had risen from the ground to the eastwards, its face resembling the outworn gold-leaf halo of some worm-eaten Tuscan saint.", "그러고 나서 그들은 모두 가장 큰 짐마차 한 대에 올라타 집으로 향했는데, 동쪽 땅에서 떠오른 넓고 빛바랜 달이 동행했으니, 그 얼굴은 좀먹은 토스카나 성인의 닳아빠진 금박 후광을 닮아 있었다."], ["Tess’s female companions sang songs, and showed themselves very sympathetic and glad at her reappearance out of doors, though they could not refrain from mischievously throwing in a few verses of the ballad about the maid who went to the merry green wood and came back a changed state.", "테스의 여자 동료들은 노래를 불렀고, 그녀가 다시 바깥에 모습을 드러낸 것을 무척 반기며 살갑게 굴었다. 다만 즐거운 푸른 숲에 갔다가 달라진 처지가 되어 돌아온 처녀에 관한 발라드의 몇 소절을 짓궂게 끼워 넣는 것만은 참지 못했다."], ["There are counterpoises and compensations in life; and the event which had made of her a social warning had also for the moment made her the most interesting personage in the village to many.", "인생에는 균형추와 보상이 있는 법이다. 그녀를 사회적 경계의 본보기로 만든 그 사건은, 동시에 한순간 그녀를 많은 이에게 마을에서 가장 흥미로운 인물로 만들기도 했다."], ["Their friendliness won her still farther away from herself, their lively spirits were contagious, and she became almost gay.", "그들의 다정함은 그녀를 자기 자신에게서 더욱 멀어지게 했고, 그들의 발랄한 기운은 전염성이 있어서, 그녀는 거의 명랑해졌다."]], [["But now that her moral sorrows were passing away a fresh one arose on the natural side of her which knew no social law.", "그러나 이제 그녀의 도덕적 슬픔이 지나가자, 어떤 사회적 법도 알지 못하는 그녀의 자연스러운 쪽에서 새로운 슬픔이 일어났다."], ["When she reached home it was to learn to her grief that the baby had been suddenly taken ill since the afternoon.", "집에 다다랐을 때 그녀는 슬프게도 아기가 오후부터 갑자기 병이 났다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Some such collapse had been probable, so tender and puny was its frame; but the event came as a shock nevertheless.", "그토록 여리고 자그마한 몸이었으니 그런 쇠약은 일어남 직한 일이었다. 그럼에도 그 일은 충격으로 다가왔다."]], [["The baby’s offence against society in coming into the world was forgotten by the girl-mother; her soul’s desire was to continue that offence by preserving the life of the child.", "아기가 세상에 나옴으로써 사회에 저지른 죄는 그 어린 어머니의 머릿속에서 잊혔다. 그녀 영혼의 바람은 아이의 목숨을 지킴으로써 그 죄를 이어 가는 것이었다."], ["However, it soon grew clear that the hour of emancipation for that little prisoner of the flesh was to arrive earlier than her worst misgiving had conjectured.", "그러나 곧 분명해졌으니, 그 작은 육체의 죄수에게 해방의 시각이 그녀의 가장 나쁜 걱정이 짐작했던 것보다 더 일찍 오리라는 것이었다."], ["And when she had discovered this she was plunged into a misery which transcended that of the child’s simple loss. Her baby had not been baptized.", "그리고 이를 알아챘을 때 그녀는 아이를 잃는 단순한 슬픔을 넘어서는 비참함 속으로 빠져들었다. 그녀의 아기는 세례를 받지 못했던 것이다."]], [["Tess had drifted into a frame of mind which accepted passively the consideration that if she should have to burn for what she had done, burn she must, and there was an end of it.", "테스는, 만약 자신이 저지른 일로 인해 불타야 한다면 불타야지, 그걸로 끝이라는 생각을 수동적으로 받아들이는 마음 상태로 흘러들어 가 있었다."], ["Like all village girls, she was well grounded in the Holy Scriptures, and had dutifully studied the histories of Aholah and Aholibah, and knew the inferences to be drawn therefrom.", "모든 시골 처녀와 마찬가지로 그녀는 성경에 단단히 기초가 잡혀 있어서, 아홀라와 아홀리바의 이야기를 충실히 공부했고, 거기서 끌어낼 교훈을 알고 있었다."], ["But when the same question arose with regard to the baby, it had a very different colour.", "그러나 같은 물음이 아기와 관련해 일어나자, 그것은 매우 다른 빛깔을 띠었다."], ["Her darling was about to die, and no salvation.", "그녀의 귀염둥이가 죽어 가려는데, 구원이 없었다."]], [["It was nearly bedtime, but she rushed downstairs and asked if she might send for the parson.", "거의 잠자리에 들 시각이었으나, 그녀는 아래층으로 달려 내려가 목사님을 부르러 보내도 되겠냐고 물었다."], ["The moment happened to be one at which her father’s sense of the antique nobility of his family was highest, and his sensitiveness to the smudge which Tess had set upon that nobility most pronounced, for he had just returned from his weekly booze at Rolliver’s Inn.", "마침 그 순간은 아버지가 가문의 옛 귀족됨에 대한 자부심이 가장 드높고, 테스가 그 귀족됨에 묻혀 놓은 오점에 대한 예민함이 가장 두드러진 때였으니, 그가 막 롤리버 여관에서의 주간 술추렴을 마치고 돌아온 참이었기 때문이다."], ["No parson should come inside his door, he declared, prying into his affairs, just then, when, by her shame, it had become more necessary than ever to hide them.", "어떤 목사도 자기 집 문 안에 들여 제 일을 캐고 다니게 할 수 없다고, 그는 선언했다. 그것도 하필 그녀의 수치로 인해 그 일을 숨기는 것이 그 어느 때보다 더 절실해진 지금에 말이다."], ["He locked the door and put the key in his pocket.", "그는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["The household went to bed, and, distressed beyond measure, Tess retired also.", "식구들은 잠자리에 들었고, 한없이 괴로운 테스도 물러나 누웠다."], ["She was continually waking as she lay, and in the middle of the night found that the baby was still worse. It was obviously dying—quietly and painlessly, but none the less surely.", "그녀는 누워서 끊임없이 잠을 깼고, 한밤중에 아기가 더 나빠진 것을 알아챘다. 아기는 분명 죽어 가고 있었다—조용히, 고통 없이, 그러나 그렇다고 덜 확실하지도 않게."]], [["In her misery she rocked herself upon the bed.", "비참함에 빠진 그녀는 침대 위에서 몸을 흔들었다."], ["The clock struck the solemn hour of one, that hour when fancy stalks outside reason, and malignant possibilities stand rock-firm as facts.", "시계가 한 시라는 엄숙한 시각을 알렸으니, 공상이 이성 밖에서 활보하고 악의에 찬 가능성들이 사실인 양 바위처럼 굳건히 서 있는 그 시각이었다."], ["She thought of the child consigned to the nethermost corner of hell, as its double doom for lack of baptism and lack of legitimacy; saw the arch-fiend tossing it with his three-pronged fork, like the one they used for heating the oven on baking days; to which picture she added many other quaint and curious details of torment sometimes taught the young in this Christian country.", "그녀는 세례를 받지 못하고 사생아라는 이중의 단죄로 인해 지옥의 가장 밑바닥 구석에 보내진 아이를 떠올렸다. 마왕이, 빵 굽는 날 화덕을 데울 때 쓰는 것 같은 세 갈래 쇠스랑으로 아이를 던져 대는 모습을 보았다. 이 그림에다 그녀는, 이 기독교 나라에서 어린아이들에게 더러 가르치는 갖가지 기괴하고 별스러운 고문의 세부 사항을 보태었다."], ["The lurid presentment so powerfully affected her imagination in the silence of the sleeping house that her nightgown became damp with perspiration, and the bedstead shook with each throb of her heart.", "잠든 집안의 정적 속에서 그 섬뜩한 상상이 어찌나 강하게 그녀의 머릿속을 사로잡던지, 잠옷이 땀에 축축해지고, 심장이 고동칠 때마다 침대가 흔들렸다."]], [["The infant’s breathing grew more difficult, and the mother’s mental tension increased.", "아기의 숨이 점점 더 가빠졌고, 어머니의 정신적 긴장은 더해 갔다."], ["It was useless to devour the little thing with kisses; she could stay in bed no longer, and walked feverishly about the room.", "그 작은 것을 입맞춤으로 삼켜 버리려 해 봐야 소용없었다. 그녀는 더 이상 침대에 있을 수 없어 방 안을 열에 들떠 서성였다."]], [["“O merciful God, have pity; have pity upon my poor baby!”", "\"오 자비로우신 하느님, 불쌍히 여기소서. 제 가엾은 아기를 불쌍히 여기소서!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 부르짖었다."], ["“Heap as much anger as you want to upon me, and welcome; but pity the child!”", "\"저한테는 얼마든지 원하시는 만큼 노여움을 퍼부으셔도 좋습니다. 하지만 이 아이만은 불쌍히 여기소서!\""]], [["She leant against the chest of drawers, and murmured incoherent supplications for a long while, till she suddenly started up.", "그녀는 서랍장에 기대어, 한참 동안 두서없는 간구를 중얼거리다가, 갑자기 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["perhaps baby can be saved! Perhaps it will be just the same!”", "어쩌면 아기를 구할 수 있을지도 몰라! 어쩌면 똑같을지도 몰라!\""]], [["She spoke so brightly that it seemed as though her face might have shone in the gloom surrounding her.", "그녀는 어찌나 밝게 말했던지 그녀를 둘러싼 어둠 속에서 얼굴이 빛났을 듯도 싶었다."], ["She lit a candle, and went to a second and a third bed under the wall, where she awoke her young sisters and brothers, all of whom occupied the same room.", "그녀는 촛불을 켜고, 벽 아래 두 번째와 세 번째 침대로 가서, 어린 누이들과 동생들을 깨웠으니, 그들은 모두 같은 방을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Pulling out the washing-stand so that she could get behind it, she poured some water from a jug, and made them kneel around, putting their hands together with fingers exactly vertical.", "세면대 뒤로 갈 수 있도록 그것을 끌어낸 다음, 그녀는 물병에서 물을 좀 따르고, 그들을 둘러 무릎 꿇리고는, 손가락을 똑바로 곧추세워 두 손을 한데 모으게 했다."], ["While the children, scarcely awake, awe-stricken at her manner, their eyes growing larger and larger, remained in this position, she took the baby from her bed—a child’s child—so immature as scarce to seem a sufficient personality to endow its producer with the maternal title.", "거의 잠도 깨지 못한 아이들이, 그녀의 태도에 경외감에 사로잡혀, 눈이 점점 더 커지면서, 이 자세 그대로 있는 동안, 그녀는 침대에서 아기를—아이가 낳은 아이를—데려왔으니, 너무도 미성숙하여 그 낳은 이에게 어머니라는 칭호를 부여하기에 충분한 인격으로조차 보이지 않을 정도였다."], ["Tess then stood erect with the infant on her arm beside the basin; the next sister held the Prayer-Book open before her, as the clerk at church held it before the parson; and thus the girl set about baptizing her child.", "그러고 나서 테스는 대야 옆에 아기를 팔에 안고 똑바로 섰다. 바로 아랫동생이, 교회의 서기가 목사 앞에 들고 있듯이, 기도서를 펼쳐 그녀 앞에 들었다. 이렇게 하여 그 처녀는 자기 아이에게 세례를 베풀기 시작했다."]], [["Her figure looked singularly tall and imposing as she stood in her long white nightgown, a thick cable of twisted dark hair hanging straight down her back to her waist.", "긴 흰 잠옷을 입고 선 그녀의 자태는 유난히 크고 위엄 있어 보였고, 비비 꼰 짙은 머리채의 굵은 밧줄이 등 뒤로 허리까지 곧장 늘어져 있었다."], ["The kindly dimness of the weak candle abstracted from her form and features the little blemishes which sunlight might have revealed—the stubble scratches upon her wrists, and the weariness of her eyes—her high enthusiasm having a transfiguring effect upon the face which had been her undoing, showing it as a thing of immaculate beauty, with a touch of dignity which was almost regal.", "약한 촛불의 다정한 어스름이 그녀의 몸과 이목구비에서, 햇빛이라면 드러냈을 자잘한 흠들을—손목의 그루터기 긁힌 자국과 눈의 피로를—덜어 내 주었다. 그녀의 드높은 열정이 그녀를 파멸시켰던 그 얼굴에 변모시키는 효과를 미쳐, 거의 왕에 어울릴 위엄의 기운을 띤, 티 없이 깨끗한 아름다움의 것으로 그것을 보여 주었다."], ["The little ones kneeling round, their sleepy eyes blinking and red, awaited her preparations full of a suspended wonder which their physical heaviness at that hour would not allow to become active.", "둘러 무릎 꿇은 어린것들이, 졸린 눈을 빨갛게 깜박이며, 그녀의 준비를 기다렸으니, 그 시각의 몸의 무거움이 활발해지도록 놔두지 않는 멈춰진 경이로 가득 차 있었다."]], [["The most impressed of them said:", "그들 가운데 가장 감명받은 아이가 말했다."]], [["“Be you really going to christen him, Tess?”", "\"테스, 정말로 얘한테 세례를 줄 거야?\""]], [["The girl-mother replied in a grave affirmative.", "그 처녀 어머니는 진지하게 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["“What’s his name going to be?”", "\"얘 이름은 뭐가 될 거야?\""]], [["She had not thought of that, but a name suggested by a phrase in the book of Genesis came into her head as she proceeded with the baptismal service, and now she pronounced it:", "그녀는 그것을 생각해 두지 않았으나, 세례 의식을 진행하는 동안 창세기의 한 구절에서 떠오른 이름이 머리에 떠올랐고, 이제 그녀는 그것을 입에 올렸다."]], [["“SORROW, I baptize thee in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost.”", "\"소로우, 나는 성부와 성자와 성령의 이름으로 네게 세례를 주노라.\""]], [["She sprinkled the water, and there was silence.", "그녀가 물을 뿌렸고, 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“Say ‘Amen,’ children.”", "\"'아멘' 하렴, 얘들아.\""]], [["The tiny voices piped in obedient response, “Amen!”", "작은 목소리들이 순종하여 응답으로 가늘게 울렸다. \"아멘!\""]], [["Tess went on:", "테스는 계속했다."]], [["“We receive this child”—and so forth—“and do sign him with the sign of the Cross.”", "\"우리는 이 아이를 받아들이며\"—따위를 이어 가다가—\"십자가 표시로 그를 표하노라.\""]], [["Here she dipped her hand into the basin, and fervently drew an immense cross upon the baby with her forefinger, continuing with the customary sentences as to his manfully fighting against sin, the world, and the devil, and being a faithful soldier and servant unto his life’s end.", "여기서 그녀는 대야에 손을 담가, 집게손가락으로 아기에게 열렬히 커다란 십자가를 그렸고, 그가 죄와 세상과 악마에 맞서 사내답게 싸우며, 제 목숨 다하는 날까지 충실한 군사이자 종이 되리라는 관례적인 문구들을 이어 갔다."], ["She duly went on with the Lord’s Prayer, the children lisping it after her in a thin gnat-like wail, till, at the conclusion, raising their voices to clerk’s pitch, they again piped into silence, “Amen!”", "그녀는 주기도문을 차례대로 진행했고, 아이들이 가는 모깃소리 같은 울음으로 그녀를 따라 더듬더듬 읊다가, 마지막에 이르러서는 목소리를 서기의 음높이로 높여, 다시 가늘게 침묵 속으로 울렸다. \"아멘!\""]], [["Then their sister, with much augmented confidence in the efficacy of the sacrament, poured forth from the bottom of her heart the thanksgiving that follows, uttering it boldly and triumphantly in the stopt-diapason note which her voice acquired when her heart was in her speech, and which will never be forgotten by those who knew her.", "그러자 그들의 누나는, 그 성사의 효력에 대한 한껏 커진 확신으로, 그 뒤에 따르는 감사기도를 가슴 밑바닥에서부터 쏟아 냈으니, 마음이 말에 실릴 때 그녀의 목소리가 얻는 그 막힌 디아파종 음으로 담대하고 의기양양하게 그것을 읊었으니, 그녀를 아는 이들은 결코 잊지 못할 음이었다."], ["The ecstasy of faith almost apotheosized her; it set upon her face a glowing irradiation, and brought a red spot into the middle of each cheek; while the miniature candle-flame inverted in her eye-pupils shone like a diamond.", "신앙의 황홀경이 그녀를 거의 신격화하여, 그녀의 얼굴에 빛나는 광채를 드리우고 양 뺨 한가운데에 붉은 점을 가져왔다. 그러는 동안 그녀의 눈동자에 거꾸로 비친 자그마한 촛불의 불꽃이 다이아몬드처럼 빛났다."], ["The children gazed up at her with more and more reverence, and no longer had a will for questioning.", "아이들은 점점 더 깊은 경외심으로 그녀를 올려다보았고, 더는 물을 마음이 없어졌다."], ["She did not look like Sissy to them now, but as a being large, towering, and awful—a divine personage with whom they had nothing in common.", "이제 그들에게 그녀는 누나처럼 보이지 않고, 크고 우뚝 솟은 두려운 존재로—그들과 아무 공통점도 없는 신성한 인물로 보였다."]], [["Poor Sorrow’s campaign against sin, the world, and the devil was doomed to be of limited brilliancy—luckily perhaps for himself, considering his beginnings.", "가엾은 소로우가 죄와 세상과 악마에 맞서 벌이는 싸움은 제한된 광휘에 그칠 운명이었다—어쩌면 그 시작을 생각하면 그 자신에게는 다행한 일이었으리라."], ["In the blue of the morning that fragile soldier and servant breathed his last, and when the other children awoke they cried bitterly, and begged Sissy to have another pretty baby.", "아침의 푸르름 속에서 그 연약한 군사이자 종은 마지막 숨을 거두었고, 다른 아이들이 깨어났을 때 그들은 비통하게 울며, 또 예쁜 아기를 낳아 달라고 누나를 졸랐다."]], [["The calmness which had possessed Tess since the christening remained with her in the infant’s loss.", "세례를 베푼 뒤로 테스를 사로잡았던 평온함은 아기를 잃고 나서도 그녀에게 남아 있었다."], ["In the daylight, indeed, she felt her terrors about his soul to have been somewhat exaggerated; whether well founded or not, she had no uneasiness now, reasoning that if Providence would not ratify such an act of approximation she, for one, did not value the kind of heaven lost by the irregularity—either for herself or for her child.", "정말이지 대낮에는 그의 영혼에 대한 자신의 공포가 다소 과장되었던 것이라 느껴졌다. 근거가 있었든 없었든, 이제 그녀는 아무런 불안이 없었으니, 만약 섭리가 그런 비슷한 행위를 인준하지 않는다면, 적어도 그녀로서는 그 변칙으로 인해 잃은 그런 종류의 천국을—자기 자신을 위해서든 자기 아이를 위해서든—귀하게 여기지 않겠노라 따지면서였다."]], [["So passed away Sorrow the Undesired—that intrusive creature, that bastard gift of shameless Nature, who respects not the social law; a waif to whom eternal Time had been a matter of days merely, who knew not that such things as years and centuries ever were; to whom the cottage interior was the universe, the week’s weather climate, new-born babyhood human existence, and the instinct to suck human knowledge.", "이렇게 하여 원치 않았던 소로우는 세상을 떠났다—그 침입자 같은 존재, 사회적 법을 존중하지 않는 파렴치한 자연이 준 사생아 선물, 영원의 시간이 고작 며칠에 지나지 않았던 떠돌이, 해니 세기니 하는 것들이 일찍이 있었다는 것조차 알지 못한 자, 그에게는 오두막 안이 우주요, 일주일의 날씨가 기후요, 갓 태어난 갓난아기 시절이 인간의 일생이요, 빨려는 본능이 인간의 지식이었던 자였다."]], [["Tess, who mused on the christening a good deal, wondered if it were doctrinally sufficient to secure a Christian burial for the child.", "세례에 대해 적잖이 골똘히 생각하던 테스는, 그것이 교리적으로 아이에게 기독교식 매장을 보장하기에 충분한지 궁금했다."], ["Nobody could tell this but the parson of the parish, and he was a new-comer, and did not know her.", "이것은 교구의 목사 말고는 아무도 말해 줄 수 없었는데, 그는 새로 온 사람이라 그녀를 알지 못했다."], ["She went to his house after dusk, and stood by the gate, but could not summon courage to go in.", "그녀는 해 질 녘 그의 집에 가서 문 옆에 섰으나, 안으로 들어갈 용기를 낼 수 없었다."], ["The enterprise would have been abandoned if she had not by accident met him coming homeward as she turned away. In the gloom she did not mind speaking freely.", "돌아서던 차에 우연히 집으로 오던 그를 마주치지 않았더라면 이 일은 포기되었을 것이다. 어스름 속이라 그녀는 거리낌 없이 말하는 것이 꺼려지지 않았다."]], [["“I should like to ask you something, sir.”", "\"여쭤 보고 싶은 게 있어요, 목사님.\""]], [["He expressed his willingness to listen, and she told the story of the baby’s illness and the extemporized ordinance.", "그가 기꺼이 들어 주겠다고 하자, 그녀는 아기의 병과 즉석에서 치른 의식의 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["“And now, sir,”", "\"그런데 목사님,\""], ["she added earnestly, “can you tell me this—will it be just the same for him as if you had baptized him?”", "그녀가 간절하게 덧붙였다. \"이것 좀 말씀해 주세요—목사님이 세례를 주신 것과 그 애한테 똑같을까요?\""]], [["Having the natural feelings of a tradesman at finding that a job he should have been called in for had been unskilfully botched by his customers among themselves, he was disposed to say no.", "자기가 불려 갔어야 할 일을 손님들끼리 서투르게 그르쳐 놓은 것을 알게 된 장사꾼의 자연스러운 심정으로, 그는 아니라고 말하고 싶어졌다."], ["Yet the dignity of the girl, the strange tenderness in her voice, combined to affect his nobler impulses—or rather those that he had left in him after ten years of endeavour to graft technical belief on actual scepticism.", "그러나 그 처녀의 위엄, 그 목소리에 담긴 묘한 부드러움이 어우러져 그의 더 고귀한 충동을—아니 차라리 실제의 회의 위에 형식적인 신앙을 접붙이려 십 년을 애쓴 끝에 그에게 남아 있던 그것을—움직였다."], ["The man and the ecclesiastic fought within him, and the victory fell to the man.", "그의 안에서 인간과 성직자가 다투었고, 승리는 인간에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["“My dear girl,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["he said, “it will be just the same.”", "그가 말했다. \"똑같을 게다.\""]], [["“Then will you give him a Christian burial?”", "\"그럼 그 애한테 기독교식 매장을 해 주시겠어요?\""], ["she asked quickly.", "그녀가 재빨리 물었다."]], [["The Vicar felt himself cornered.", "목사는 자신이 궁지에 몰린 것을 느꼈다."], ["Hearing of the baby’s illness, he had conscientiously gone to the house after nightfall to perform the rite, and, unaware that the refusal to admit him had come from Tess’s father and not from Tess, he could not allow the plea of necessity for its irregular administration.", "아기가 병들었다는 소식을 듣고, 그는 양심에 따라 밤이 깊은 뒤 그 의식을 행하러 집으로 갔었는데, 그를 들이지 않은 거절이 테스가 아니라 테스의 아버지에게서 나온 것임을 알지 못한 채라, 그 변칙적 거행에 대해 부득이했다는 변명을 받아들일 수 없었다."]], [["“Ah—that’s another matter,” he said.", "\"아—그건 다른 문제다,\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Another matter—why?”", "\"다른 문제라니요—왜요?\""], ["asked Tess, rather warmly.", "테스가 다소 발끈하여 물었다."]], [["“Well—I would willingly do so if only we two were concerned. But I must not—for certain reasons.”", "\"음—우리 둘만의 일이라면 내 기꺼이 그리하겠다. 하지만 그래서는 안 된다—어떤 이유들 때문에.\""]], [["“Just for once, sir!”", "\"딱 한 번만요, 목사님!\""]], [["“Really I must not.”", "\"정말로 그래서는 안 된다.\""]], [["“O sir!” She seized his hand as she spoke.", "\"오 목사님!\" 그녀는 말하면서 그의 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["He withdrew it, shaking his head.", "그가 고개를 저으며 손을 빼냈다."]], [["“Then I don’t like you!”", "\"그럼 전 목사님이 싫어요!\""], ["she burst out, “and I’ll never come to your church no more!”", "그녀가 터뜨렸다. \"그리고 목사님 교회엔 다신 안 갈 거예요!\""]], [["“Don’t talk so rashly.”", "\"그리 경솔하게 말하지 마라.\""]], [["“Perhaps it will be just the same to him if you don’t?...", "\"목사님이 안 해 주셔도 그 애한테는 똑같지 않을까요?..."], ["Will it be just the same? Don’t for God’s sake speak as saint to sinner, but as you yourself to me myself—poor me!”", "똑같을까요? 제발 성인이 죄인에게 말하듯 하지 마시고, 목사님 그 자신으로서 저 자신에게—이 가엾은 저에게 말씀해 주세요!\""]], [["How the Vicar reconciled his answer with the strict notions he supposed himself to hold on these subjects it is beyond a layman’s power to tell, though not to excuse.", "자신이 이런 문제들에 관해 견지하고 있다고 여기는 엄격한 관념과 자신의 대답을 목사가 어떻게 조화시켰는지는 평신도의 능력으로 헤아릴 수 없는 일이다. 변명할 수 없는 일은 아니지만 말이다."], ["Somewhat moved, he said in this case also—", "다소 마음이 움직인 그는, 이 경우에도 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["“It will be just the same.”", "\"똑같을 게다.\""]], [["So the baby was carried in a small deal box, under an ancient woman’s shawl, to the churchyard that night, and buried by lantern-light, at the cost of a shilling and a pint of beer to the sexton, in that shabby corner of God’s allotment where He lets the nettles grow, and where all unbaptized infants, notorious drunkards, suicides, and others of the conjecturally damned are laid.", "그리하여 아기는 그날 밤 작은 전나무 상자에 담겨, 어느 늙은 여인의 숄에 싸여 교회 묘지로 옮겨졌고, 묘지기에게 치른 일 실링과 맥주 한 파인트의 값으로, 등불 빛에 의지해 묻혔으니, 하느님께서 쐐기풀이 자라도록 내버려 두시는 그분 땅의 그 초라한 구석, 세례받지 못한 모든 갓난아기와 소문난 술꾼들과 자살한 자들과 그 밖에 단죄받았으리라 짐작되는 이들이 묻히는 곳이었다."], ["In spite of the untoward surroundings, however, Tess bravely made a little cross of two laths and a piece of string, and having bound it with flowers, she stuck it up at the head of the grave one evening when she could enter the churchyard without being seen, putting at the foot also a bunch of the same flowers in a little jar of water to keep them alive.", "그러나 그 불길한 주변에도 불구하고, 테스는 용감하게 욋가지 두 개와 노끈 한 가닥으로 작은 십자가를 만들었고, 그것을 꽃으로 묶은 다음, 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 교회 묘지에 들어갈 수 있었던 어느 저녁에 무덤 머리맡에 세웠으며, 발치에도 같은 꽃 한 다발을 물이 든 작은 단지에 담아, 꽃이 살아 있도록 두었다."], ["What matter was it that on the outside of the jar the eye of mere observation noted the words “Keelwell’s Marmalade”?", "그 단지 바깥에 한낱 관찰의 눈이 \"킬웰스 마멀레이드\"라는 글자를 알아챈들 그게 무슨 상관이랴?"], ["The eye of maternal affection did not see them in its vision of higher things.", "어머니의 사랑의 눈은 더 높은 것들을 바라보는 그 시야 속에서 그것들을 보지 못했다."]], [["# XV", "# XV"]], [["“By experience,”", "\"경험을 통해,\""], ["says Roger Ascham, “we find out a short way by a long wandering.”", "로저 애스컴은 말한다. \"우리는 긴 헤맴으로 지름길을 찾아낸다.\""], ["Not seldom that long wandering unfits us for further travel, and of what use is our experience to us then?", "그 긴 헤맴이 우리를 더 이상의 여행에 부적합하게 만드는 일도 적지 않으니, 그렇다면 우리의 경험이 그때 우리에게 무슨 소용이랴?"], ["Tess Durbeyfield’s experience was of this incapacitating kind.", "테스 더비필드의 경험은 바로 이런 무력화시키는 종류의 것이었다."], ["At last she had learned what to do; but who would now accept her doing?", "마침내 그녀는 무엇을 해야 할지 배웠으나, 이제 누가 그녀가 하는 것을 받아 주려 하겠는가?"]], [["If before going to the d’Urbervilles’ she had vigorously moved under the guidance of sundry gnomic texts and phrases known to her and to the world in general, no doubt she would never have been imposed on.", "더버빌가에 가기 전에, 만약 그녀가 자신과 세상 일반에 알려진 갖가지 격언 같은 성구와 문구의 인도 아래 힘차게 움직였더라면, 의심할 바 없이 그녀는 결코 속아 넘어가지 않았으리라."], ["But it had not been in Tess’s power—nor is it in anybody’s power—to feel the whole truth of golden opinions while it is possible to profit by them.", "그러나 황금 같은 견해의 온전한 진리를 그것으로부터 이득을 볼 수 있는 동안에 느끼는 것은 테스의 능력 밖이었으며—누구의 능력 밖이기도 하다."], ["She—and how many more—might have ironically said to God with Saint Augustine: “Thou hast counselled a better course than Thou hast permitted.”", "그녀는—그리고 얼마나 더 많은 이가—성 아우구스티누스와 함께 하느님께 빈정대듯 이렇게 말했을지도 모른다. \"당신은 허락하신 것보다 더 나은 길을 권하셨나이다.\""]], [["She remained at her father’s house during the winter months, plucking fowls, or cramming turkeys and geese, or making clothes for her sisters and brothers out of some finery which d’Urberville had given her, and she had put by with contempt. Apply to him she would not.", "그녀는 겨울 동안 아버지의 집에 머무르며, 닭의 깃털을 뽑거나, 칠면조와 거위를 살찌우거나, 더버빌이 그녀에게 준 어떤 고운 옷가지—경멸하며 따로 치워 두었던 것—로 동생들의 옷을 지었다. 그에게 부탁하는 일만은 하지 않으려 했다."], ["But she would often clasp her hands behind her head and muse when she was supposed to be working hard.", "그러나 그녀는 열심히 일하는 줄로 여겨질 때 자주 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 끼고 골똘히 생각에 잠기곤 했다."]], [["She philosophically noted dates as they came past in the revolution of the year; the disastrous night of her undoing at Trantridge with its dark background of The Chase; also the dates of the baby’s birth and death; also her own birthday; and every other day individualized by incidents in which she had taken some share.", "그녀는 한 해의 순환 속에서 지나가는 날짜들을 철학적으로 헤아렸다. 체이스 숲의 어두운 배경을 거느린 트랜트리지에서의 파멸의 그 재앙적인 밤, 또 아기가 태어나고 죽은 날짜, 또 그녀 자신의 생일, 그리고 그녀가 얼마간 관여했던 사건들로 저마다 따로 떼어진 그 밖의 모든 날."], ["She suddenly thought one afternoon, when looking in the glass at her fairness, that there was yet another date, of greater importance to her than those; that of her own death, when all these charms would have disappeared; a day which lay sly and unseen among all the other days of the year, giving no sign or sound when she annually passed over it; but not the less surely there.", "어느 날 오후, 거울에 비친 자신의 고움을 들여다보다가, 그녀는 문득 생각했으니, 그것들보다 그녀에게 더 중요한 또 하나의 날짜가 있다는 것을. 곧 이 모든 매력이 사라지고 없을, 자기 자신이 죽는 날을. 한 해의 다른 모든 날 사이에 음흉하게, 보이지 않게 숨어 있어, 그녀가 해마다 그 위를 지나갈 때 아무런 표시도 소리도 내지 않지만, 그렇다고 거기 분명히 있지 않은 것은 아닌 그날을."], ["When was it?", "그것은 언제일까?"], ["Why did she not feel the chill of each yearly encounter with such a cold relation?", "그토록 차가운 친척과 해마다 마주치면서 왜 그녀는 그 한기를 느끼지 못하는 것일까?"], ["She had Jeremy Taylor’s thought that some time in the future those who had known her would say: “It is the ——th, the day that poor Tess Durbeyfield died”; and there would be nothing singular to their minds in the statement.", "그녀는 제러미 테일러의 생각을 품고 있었으니, 언젠가 미래에 그녀를 알던 이들이 이렇게 말하리라는 것이었다. \"○○일이지, 가엾은 테스 더비필드가 죽은 날이.\" 그리고 그들의 마음에는 그 말에 별스러운 데가 조금도 없으리라는 것이었다."], ["Of that day, doomed to be her terminus in time through all the ages, she did not know the place in month, week, season or year.", "모든 세월을 통틀어 시간 속에서 그녀의 종착점이 될 운명인 그날에 대해, 그녀는 그것이 달과 주와 계절과 해의 어느 자리에 있는지 알지 못했다."]], [["Almost at a leap Tess thus changed from simple girl to complex woman.", "이렇게 거의 단숨에 테스는 단순한 처녀에서 복잡한 여인으로 변했다."], ["Symbols of reflectiveness passed into her face, and a note of tragedy at times into her voice.", "사색의 표징들이 그녀의 얼굴로 스며들었고, 이따금 비극의 가락이 그녀의 목소리로 스며들었다."], ["Her eyes grew larger and more eloquent.", "그녀의 눈은 더 커지고 더 웅변적이 되었다."], ["She became what would have been called a fine creature; her aspect was fair and arresting; her soul that of a woman whom the turbulent experiences of the last year or two had quite failed to demoralize.", "그녀는 고운 사람이라 불릴 만한 무언가가 되었다. 그녀의 모습은 아름답고 사람을 사로잡았다. 그녀의 영혼은 지난 한두 해의 격동적인 경험들이 도무지 타락시키지 못한 한 여인의 영혼이었다."], ["But for the world’s opinion those experiences would have been simply a liberal education.", "세상의 견해만 아니었다면, 그 경험들은 그저 폭넓은 교육이었을 따름이다."]], [["She had held so aloof of late that her trouble, never generally known, was nearly forgotten in Marlott.", "근래 그녀가 워낙 동떨어져 지낸 탓에, 널리 알려진 적도 없던 그녀의 고난은 마롯에서 거의 잊혔다."], ["But it became evident to her that she could never be really comfortable again in a place which had seen the collapse of her family’s attempt to “claim kin”—and, through her, even closer union—with the rich d’Urbervilles.", "그러나 자기 가문이 부유한 더버빌가와 \"친척임을 주장하려는\"—그리고 그녀를 통해 한층 더 가까운 결합까지 꾀하려는—시도가 무너지는 것을 지켜본 곳에서 다시는 진정으로 편안할 수 없으리라는 것이 그녀에게 분명해졌다."], ["At least she could not be comfortable there till long years should have obliterated her keen consciousness of it.", "적어도 긴 세월이 그것에 대한 그녀의 날카로운 의식을 지워 버리기 전까지는 거기서 편안할 수 없었다."], ["Yet even now Tess felt the pulse of hopeful life still warm within her; she might be happy in some nook which had no memories.", "그러나 지금도 테스는 자기 안에 여전히 따뜻한 희망찬 생명의 맥박이 뛰는 것을 느꼈다. 어쩌면 아무런 기억도 없는 어느 한구석에서 그녀는 행복할 수 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["To escape the past and all that appertained thereto was to annihilate it, and to do that she would have to get away.", "과거와 그에 딸린 모든 것에서 벗어나는 것은 그것을 소멸시키는 것이었고, 그러려면 그녀는 멀리 떠나야 했다."]], [["Was once lost always lost really true of chastity?", "한번 잃으면 영영 잃는다는 것이 정조에 관해 정말로 참일까?"], ["she would ask herself.", "그녀는 자문하곤 했다."], ["She might prove it false if she could veil bygones. The recuperative power which pervaded organic nature was surely not denied to maidenhood alone.", "지난 일들을 가릴 수만 있다면 그것이 거짓임을 입증할 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 유기적 자연 곳곳에 스민 회복의 힘이 설마 처녀성에게만 거부되었을 리는 없었다."]], [["She waited a long time without finding opportunity for a new departure.", "그녀는 새로운 출발의 기회를 찾지 못한 채 오랫동안 기다렸다."], ["A particularly fine spring came round, and the stir of germination was almost audible in the buds; it moved her, as it moved the wild animals, and made her passionate to go.", "유난히 좋은 봄이 돌아왔고, 봉오리 속에서 발아의 움직임이 거의 들릴 듯했다. 그것이, 야생 짐승들을 움직이듯 그녀를 움직여, 떠나고 싶어 못 견디게 만들었다."], ["At last, one day in early May, a letter reached her from a former friend of her mother’s, to whom she had addressed inquiries long before—a person whom she had never seen—that a skilful milkmaid was required at a dairy-house many miles to the southward, and that the dairyman would be glad to have her for the summer months.", "마침내 오월 초의 어느 날, 오래전 문의를 보냈던 어머니의 옛 친구—한 번도 본 적 없는 사람—에게서 편지가 그녀에게 도착했으니, 남쪽으로 여러 마일 떨어진 어느 낙농장에서 솜씨 좋은 우유 짜는 여자가 필요하다는 것, 그리고 낙농장 주인이 여름철 동안 그녀를 기꺼이 두겠다는 것이었다."]], [["It was not quite so far off as could have been wished; but it was probably far enough, her radius of movement and repute having been so small.", "바랐던 만큼 그리 멀리 떨어진 곳은 아니었으나, 아마도 충분히 먼 곳이었으니, 그녀의 이동과 평판의 반경이 워낙 작았기 때문이다."], ["To persons of limited spheres, miles are as geographical degrees, parishes as counties, counties as provinces and kingdoms.", "영역이 좁은 사람들에게는 몇 마일이 지리상의 도(度)와 같고, 교구가 주(州)와 같으며, 주가 지방이나 왕국과 같은 법이다."]], [["On one point she was resolved: there should be no more d’Urberville air-castles in the dreams and deeds of her new life.", "한 가지 점에 대해서 그녀는 결심이 굳었다. 새 삶의 꿈과 행동 속에 더 이상 더버빌가의 공중누각은 없으리라는 것."], ["She would be the dairymaid Tess, and nothing more.", "그녀는 우유 짜는 여자 테스가 될 것이고, 그 이상은 아니었다."], ["Her mother knew Tess’s feeling on this point so well, though no words had passed between them on the subject, that she never alluded to the knightly ancestry now.", "이 점에 대한 테스의 심정을 어머니는 너무도 잘 알았기에—비록 그 문제에 관해 둘 사이에 오간 말은 없었지만—이제 기사 가문의 혈통에 대해서는 절대 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["Yet such is human inconsistency that one of the interests of the new place to her was the accidental virtues of its lying near her forefathers’ country (for they were not Blakemore men, though her mother was Blakemore to the bone).", "그러나 인간이란 이렇게 모순된 존재라, 그 새 장소가 그녀에게 주는 흥미 가운데 하나는 그곳이 우연히도 그녀 조상들의 땅 가까이 자리해 있다는 점이었다(어머니는 뼛속까지 블레이크모어 사람이었으나, 그들은 블레이크모어 사람이 아니었기 때문이다)."], ["The dairy called Talbothays, for which she was bound, stood not remotely from some of the former estates of the d’Urbervilles, near the great family vaults of her granddames and their powerful husbands.", "그녀가 가기로 되어 있는 톨보세이즈라 불리는 낙농장은, 더버빌가의 옛 영지 일부에서 멀지 않은 곳, 그녀의 할머니들과 그 권세 있던 남편들이 묻힌 그 위대한 가문의 납골당 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["She would be able to look at them, and think not only that d’Urberville, like Babylon, had fallen, but that the individual innocence of a humble descendant could lapse as silently.", "그녀는 그것들을 바라보며, 더버빌가가 바빌론처럼 무너졌다는 것뿐만 아니라, 한 비천한 후손의 개인적 순결도 그처럼 소리 없이 스러질 수 있다는 것을 생각할 수 있을 터였다."], ["All the while she wondered if any strange good thing might come of her being in her ancestral land; and some spirit within her rose automatically as the sap in the twigs.", "그러는 내내 그녀는 자신이 조상의 땅에 있음으로 해서 어떤 기이하게 좋은 일이 생길지도 모른다고 궁금해했고, 그녀 안의 어떤 정신이 잔가지 속 수액처럼 절로 솟아올랐다."], ["It was unexpected youth, surging up anew after its temporary check, and bringing with it hope, and the invincible instinct towards self-delight. End of Phase the Second", "그것은 일시적인 제동 후에 새로이 차오르는 뜻밖의 젊음이었으니, 희망과, 자기 기쁨을 향한 정복할 수 없는 본능을 함께 가져왔다."]], [["The Rally", "회복"]], [["# XVI", "# XVI"]], [["On a thyme-scented, bird-hatching morning in May, between two and three years after the return from Trantridge—silent, reconstructive years for Tess Durbeyfield—she left her home for the second time.", "오월의, 백리향 향기 풍기고 새들이 알을 까는 어느 아침, 트랜트리지에서 돌아온 지 이삼 년 후에—테스 더비필드에게는 말없이 자신을 재건한 세월이었던—그녀는 두 번째로 집을 떠났다."]], [["Having packed up her luggage so that it could be sent to her later, she started in a hired trap for the little town of Stourcastle, through which it was necessary to pass on her journey, now in a direction almost opposite to that of her first adventuring.", "나중에 부쳐 받을 수 있도록 짐을 꾸려 두고, 그녀는 세를 낸 이륜마차를 타고 스타캐슬이라는 작은 마을로 출발했으니, 여정 중에 그곳을 지나야 했기 때문이다. 이번에는 첫 모험 때와 거의 정반대 방향이었다."], ["On the curve of the nearest hill she looked back regretfully at Marlott and her father’s house, although she had been so anxious to get away.", "가장 가까운 언덕의 굽이에서 그녀는, 그토록 떠나고 싶어 했으면서도, 마롯과 아버지의 집을 아쉬운 듯 돌아보았다."]], [["Her kindred dwelling there would probably continue their daily lives as heretofore, with no great diminution of pleasure in their consciousness, although she would be far off, and they deprived of her smile.", "거기 사는 그녀의 친족들은 비록 그녀가 멀리 떠나가고 그녀의 미소를 잃을지라도, 의식 속의 즐거움이 크게 줄지 않은 채 전과 다름없이 일상을 이어 가리라."], ["In a few days the children would engage in their games as merrily as ever, without the sense of any gap left by her departure.", "며칠만 지나면 아이들은 그녀의 떠남이 남긴 어떤 빈자리도 느끼지 못한 채 여느 때처럼 즐겁게 놀이에 빠질 터였다."], ["This leaving of the younger children she had decided to be for the best; were she to remain they would probably gain less good by her precepts than harm by her example.", "어린 동생들을 두고 떠나는 이 일이 최선이라고 그녀는 판단했었다. 만약 그녀가 남는다면, 아이들은 그녀의 가르침으로 얻는 이로움보다 그녀의 본보기로 입는 해로움이 아마 더 클 테니까."]], [["She went through Stourcastle without pausing and onward to a junction of highways, where she could await a carrier’s van that ran to the south-west; for the railways which engirdled this interior tract of country had never yet struck across it.", "그녀는 스타캐슬을 멈추지 않고 지나, 남서쪽으로 가는 운송 마차를 기다릴 수 있는 큰길의 교차점으로 나아갔다. 이 내륙 지대를 에워싼 철도들은 아직 이 일대를 가로질러 들어온 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["While waiting, however, there came along a farmer in his spring cart, driving approximately in the direction that she wished to pursue.", "그러나 기다리는 사이에 어느 농부가 봄 짐수레를 몰고 지나왔는데, 그녀가 가려는 방향과 거의 같은 쪽으로 가고 있었다."], ["Though he was a stranger to her she accepted his offer of a seat beside him, ignoring that its motive was a mere tribute to her countenance.", "그가 그녀에게는 낯선 사람이었으나, 그 권유의 동기가 한낱 그녀의 용모에 바치는 찬사일 뿐임을 모른 체하고, 그녀는 그의 옆자리에 앉으라는 권유를 받아들였다."], ["He was going to Weatherbury, and by accompanying him thither she could walk the remainder of the distance instead of travelling in the van by way of Casterbridge.", "그는 웨더베리로 가는 길이었고, 거기까지 그와 동행하면 캐스터브리지를 거쳐 운송 마차로 가는 대신 나머지 거리를 걸어갈 수 있었다."]], [["Tess did not stop at Weatherbury, after this long drive, further than to make a slight nondescript meal at noon at a cottage to which the farmer recommended her.", "이 긴 마차 여정 후에 테스는 웨더베리에서, 그 농부가 일러 준 어느 오두막에서 정오에 가벼운 정체불명의 식사를 한 것 말고는 더 머무르지 않았다."], ["Thence she started on foot, basket in hand, to reach the wide upland of heath dividing this district from the low-lying meads of a further valley in which the dairy stood that was the aim and end of her day’s pilgrimage.", "거기서부터 그녀는, 바구니를 손에 들고 걸어서 출발했으니, 이 지역과 더 너머 골짜기의 낮게 깔린 풀밭을 가르는 넓은 황야 고지대에 다다르기 위해서였다. 그 골짜기에 그날 순례의 목표이자 종착지인 낙농장이 서 있었다."]], [["Tess had never before visited this part of the country, and yet she felt akin to the landscape.", "테스는 전에 이 지방을 찾아온 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 그래도 그 풍경에 친근감을 느꼈다."], ["Not so very far to the left of her she could discern a dark patch in the scenery, which inquiry confirmed her in supposing to be trees marking the environs of Kingsbere—in the church of which parish the bones of her ancestors—her useless ancestors—lay entombed.", "그녀의 왼편으로 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 풍경 속 검은 한 자락이 보였는데, 알아본바 그것은 킹스비어 인근을 표시하는 나무들이라는 그녀의 짐작이 맞다고 확인되었다—그 교구의 교회 안에 그녀 조상들의—그녀의 쓸모없는 조상들의—뼈가 묻혀 있었다."]], [["She had no admiration for them now; she almost hated them for the dance they had led her; not a thing of all that had been theirs did she retain but the old seal and spoon.", "이제 그녀는 그들에 대해 아무런 흠모도 없었다. 그녀는 그들이 자신을 끌고 다닌 그 춤 때문에 그들을 거의 미워했다. 그들의 것이었던 모든 것 가운데 그녀가 간직한 것이라곤 오래된 인장과 숟가락뿐이었다."], ["“Pooh—I have as much of mother as father in me!” she said.", "\"흥—내 안엔 아버지만큼이나 어머니도 들어 있어!\" 그녀는 말했다."], ["“All my prettiness comes from her, and she was only a dairymaid.”", "\"내 어여쁨은 다 어머니한테서 왔고, 어머니는 한낱 우유 짜는 여자였을 뿐이야.\""]], [["The journey over the intervening uplands and lowlands of Egdon, when she reached them, was a more troublesome walk than she had anticipated, the distance being actually but a few miles.", "에그던의 그 사이에 놓인 고지와 저지를 넘는 여정은, 그곳에 다다랐을 때, 그녀가 예상했던 것보다 더 성가신 걸음이었으니, 거리는 실상 몇 마일에 지나지 않았건만 말이다."], ["It was two hours, owing to sundry wrong turnings, ere she found herself on a summit commanding the long-sought-for vale, the Valley of the Great Dairies, the valley in which milk and butter grew to rankness, and were produced more profusely, if less delicately, than at her home—the verdant plain so well watered by the river Var or Froom.", "여러 차례 길을 잘못 든 탓에 두 시간이 지나서야 그녀는 그토록 찾던 골짜기를 굽어보는 마루에 다다랐으니, 그 위대한 낙농장들의 골짜기, 우유와 버터가 무성하게 자라나, 그녀의 고향에서보다, 덜 섬세할지언정 더 풍성하게 생산되는 골짜기—바르 강 혹은 프룸 강이 그토록 잘 물을 댄 푸르른 평원이었다."]], [["It was intrinsically different from the Vale of Little Dairies, Blackmoor Vale, which, save during her disastrous sojourn at Trantridge, she had exclusively known till now.", "그것은 본질적으로 그녀가 지금까지—트랜트리지에서의 그 재앙적인 체류를 빼고는—오로지 알고 지내 온 작은 낙농장들의 골짜기, 곧 블랙무어 골짜기와는 달랐다."], ["The world was drawn to a larger pattern here.", "여기서는 세계가 더 큰 본을 따라 그려져 있었다."], ["The enclosures numbered fifty acres instead of ten, the farmsteads were more extended, the groups of cattle formed tribes hereabout; there only families.", "울타리 친 땅이 십 에이커가 아니라 오십 에이커였고, 농가들은 더 널찍했으며, 이 일대에서 소 떼는 종족을 이루었다—거기서는 그저 가족이었는데 말이다."], ["These myriads of cows stretching under her eyes from the far east to the far west outnumbered any she had ever seen at one glance before.", "동쪽 끝에서 서쪽 끝까지 그녀의 눈 아래 펼쳐진 이 무수한 젖소들은, 그녀가 일찍이 한눈에 본 어떤 것보다도 그 수가 많았다."], ["The green lea was speckled as thickly with them as a canvas by Van Alsloot or Sallaert with burghers.", "푸른 풀밭은 반 알슬로트나 살라르트의 화폭이 시민들로 빽빽한 것만큼이나 소들로 빽빽하게 점점이 박혀 있었다."], ["The ripe hue of the red and dun kine absorbed the evening sunlight, which the white-coated animals returned to the eye in rays almost dazzling, even at the distant elevation on which she stood.", "붉고 거무튀튀한 소들의 무르익은 빛깔은 저녁 햇빛을 빨아들였고, 흰 털가죽 짐승들은 그 빛을, 그녀가 서 있는 그 먼 높이에서조차 거의 눈부실 만큼의 광선으로 눈에 되돌려 주었다."]], [["The bird’s-eye perspective before her was not so luxuriantly beautiful, perhaps, as that other one which she knew so well; yet it was more cheering.", "그녀 앞에 펼쳐진 조감(鳥瞰)의 광경은, 그녀가 그토록 잘 아는 저 다른 광경만큼 무성하게 아름답지는 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 그것은 더 마음을 북돋웠다."], ["It lacked the intensely blue atmosphere of the rival vale, and its heavy soils and scents; the new air was clear, bracing, ethereal.", "거기에는 그 맞수 골짜기의 그 짙푸른 대기도, 그 무거운 토양과 냄새도 없었다. 새로운 공기는 맑고, 상쾌하고, 천상의 것 같았다."], ["The river itself, which nourished the grass and cows of these renowned dairies, flowed not like the streams in Blackmoor.", "이 이름난 낙농장들의 풀과 소를 길러 내는 그 강 자체도, 블랙무어의 시내들처럼 흐르지 않았다."], ["Those were slow, silent, often turbid; flowing over beds of mud into which the incautious wader might sink and vanish unawares.", "그것들은 느리고, 조용하고, 자주 흐릿했으니, 진흙 바닥 위로 흐르는 그곳에서는 조심성 없는 도섭자가 부지중에 가라앉아 사라질 수도 있었다."], ["The Froom waters were clear as the pure River of Life shown to the Evangelist, rapid as the shadow of a cloud, with pebbly shallows that prattled to the sky all day long.", "프룸의 물은 복음서 기자에게 보여진 그 순수한 생명의 강처럼 맑았고, 구름 그림자처럼 빠르며, 자갈 깔린 여울이 온종일 하늘을 향해 재잘거렸다."], ["There the water-flower was the lily; the crow-foot here.", "거기서 물에 핀 꽃은 백합이었으나, 여기서는 미나리아재비였다."]], [["Either the change in the quality of the air from heavy to light, or the sense of being amid new scenes where there were no invidious eyes upon her, sent up her spirits wonderfully.", "무겁던 공기가 가벼워진 그 질의 변화 때문이었는지, 아니면 자신을 향한 짓궂은 눈길이 하나도 없는 새로운 풍경 속에 있다는 느낌 때문이었는지, 그녀의 기운이 놀랍도록 솟아올랐다."], ["Her hopes mingled with the sunshine in an ideal photosphere which surrounded her as she bounded along against the soft south wind.", "그녀의 희망은, 부드러운 남풍을 맞받아 통통 튀듯 나아가는 그녀를 에워싼 어떤 이상적인 광구(光球) 속에서 햇살과 뒤섞였다."], ["She heard a pleasant voice in every breeze, and in every bird’s note seemed to lurk a joy.", "그녀는 미풍마다 유쾌한 목소리를 들었고, 새소리마다 기쁨이 숨어 있는 듯했다."]], [["Her face had latterly changed with changing states of mind, continually fluctuating between beauty and ordinariness, according as the thoughts were gay or grave.", "근래 그녀의 얼굴은 마음 상태가 변함에 따라 변해 왔으니, 생각이 즐거우냐 침울하냐에 따라 아름다움과 평범함 사이를 끊임없이 오갔다."], ["One day she was pink and flawless; another pale and tragical.", "어느 날은 발그레하고 흠 하나 없다가, 또 어느 날은 창백하고 비극적이었다."], ["When she was pink she was feeling less than when pale; her more perfect beauty accorded with her less elevated mood; her more intense mood with her less perfect beauty.", "발그레할 때 그녀는 창백할 때보다 덜 느끼고 있었다. 그녀의 더 완전한 아름다움은 그녀의 덜 고양된 기분과 어울렸고, 그녀의 더 강렬한 기분은 그녀의 덜 완전한 아름다움과 어울렸다."], ["It was her best face physically that was now set against the south wind.", "지금 남풍을 맞받고 있는 것은 육체적으로 그녀의 가장 좋은 얼굴이었다."]], [["The irresistible, universal, automatic tendency to find sweet pleasure somewhere, which pervades all life, from the meanest to the highest, had at length mastered Tess.", "가장 비천한 것에서 가장 고귀한 것에 이르기까지 모든 생명에 두루 스민, 어딘가에서 달콤한 기쁨을 찾으려는 그 저항할 수 없고 보편적이며 절로 일어나는 경향이, 마침내 테스를 사로잡았다."], ["Being even now only a young woman of twenty, one who mentally and sentimentally had not finished growing, it was impossible that any event should have left upon her an impression that was not in time capable of transmutation.", "지금도 그녀는 겨우 스무 살의 젊은 여인, 정신적으로도 정서적으로도 아직 자라기를 끝내지 못한 사람이었으니, 어떤 사건도 그녀에게 시간이 지나면 변형될 수 없는 인상을 남겼을 리 없었다."]], [["And thus her spirits, and her thankfulness, and her hopes, rose higher and higher.", "이리하여 그녀의 기운과 감사의 마음과 희망은 점점 더 높이 솟아올랐다."], ["She tried several ballads, but found them inadequate; till, recollecting the psalter that her eyes had so often wandered over of a Sunday morning before she had eaten of the tree of knowledge, she chanted: “O ye Sun and Moon ...", "그녀는 여러 발라드를 시험 삼아 불러 보았으나, 그것들이 부족하다 느꼈다. 그러다, 지혜의 나무 열매를 따 먹기 전 일요일 아침이면 그토록 자주 눈으로 훑어 내려가던 그 시편집을 떠올리고, 그녀는 노래했다. \"오 너희 해와 달이여..."], ["O ye Stars ... ye Green Things upon the Earth ...", "오 너희 별들이여... 너희 땅 위의 푸른 것들이여..."], ["ye Fowls of the Air ...", "너희 공중의 새들이여..."], ["Beasts and Cattle ...", "짐승과 가축들이여..."], ["Children of Men ...", "사람의 자녀들이여..."], ["bless ye the Lord, praise Him and magnify Him forever!”", "너희는 주님을 송축하고, 그분을 찬양하며 영원토록 높이 받들지어다!\""]], [["She suddenly stopped and murmured: “But perhaps I don’t quite know the Lord as yet.”", "그녀는 갑자기 멈추고 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 어쩌면 난 아직 주님을 잘 알지 못하나 봐.\""]], [["And probably the half-unconscious rhapsody was a Fetishistic utterance in a Monotheistic setting; women whose chief companions are the forms and forces of outdoor Nature retain in their souls far more of the Pagan fantasy of their remote forefathers than of the systematized religion taught their race at later date.", "그리고 아마도 그 반쯤 무의식적인 황홀가는 일신교의 무대 위에서 터져 나온 물신숭배적 발언이었으리라. 야외 자연의 형상과 힘을 주된 벗으로 삼는 여인들은, 훗날 그들의 핏줄에 가르쳐진 체계화된 종교보다, 먼 조상들의 이교적 환상을 그 영혼 속에 훨씬 더 많이 간직하고 있는 법이다."], ["However, Tess found at least approximate expression for her feelings in the old _Benedicite_ that she had lisped from infancy; and it was enough.", "그래도 테스는 어릴 적부터 더듬더듬 외워 온 오래된 「베네디키테」 속에서 자신의 감정에 적어도 비슷한 표현을 찾아냈다."], ["Such high contentment with such a slight initial performance as that of having started towards a means of independent living was a part of the Durbeyfield temperament.", "그것으로 충분했다. 독립적인 삶의 수단을 향해 첫발을 떼었을 뿐인 그런 사소한 출발에 그토록 큰 만족을 느끼는 것은 더비필드 집안 기질의 일부였다."], ["Tess really wished to walk uprightly, while her father did nothing of the kind; but she resembled him in being content with immediate and small achievements, and in having no mind for laborious effort towards such petty social advancement as could alone be effected by a family so heavily handicapped as the once powerful d’Urbervilles were now.", "테스는 정말로 곧게 걷고 싶어 했고, 그녀의 아버지는 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 하지만 그녀는, 당장의 작은 성취에 만족하고, 한때 강성했던 더버빌 가문이 지금처럼 무겁게 짓눌린 처지의 집안만이 이룰 수 있는 그 보잘것없는 사회적 향상을 위해 고된 노력을 기울일 마음이 없다는 점에서는 아버지를 닮아 있었다."]], [["There was, it might be said, the energy of her mother’s unexpended family, as well as the natural energy of Tess’s years, rekindled after the experience which had so overwhelmed her for the time.", "말하자면 거기에는, 테스의 나이가 지닌 자연스러운 활기뿐 아니라, 그녀를 한동안 그토록 압도했던 경험 이후에 다시 불붙은, 어머니 쪽 집안의 미처 다 쓰지 못한 기운도 있었다."], ["Let the truth be told—women do as a rule live through such humiliations, and regain their spirits, and again look about them with an interested eye. While there’s life there’s hope is a conviction not so entirely unknown to the “betrayed”", "진실을 말하자면—여자들은 대체로 그런 굴욕을 견디고 살아가며, 기운을 되찾고, 다시 흥미로운 눈으로 주위를 둘러본다. 살아 있는 한 희망이 있다는 신념은, 어느 마음씨 좋은 이론가들이 우리에게 믿게 하려는 것만큼 \"배신당한\""], ["as some amiable theorists would have us believe.", "여인들에게 그토록 낯선 것이 아니다."]], [["Tess Durbeyfield, then, in good heart, and full of zest for life, descended the Egdon slopes lower and lower towards the dairy of her pilgrimage.", "그리하여 테스 더비필드는, 마음이 가뿐하고 삶에 대한 열의로 가득 차서, 순례의 목적지인 낙농장을 향해 에그던의 비탈을 점점 더 아래로 내려갔다."]], [["The marked difference, in the final particular, between the rival vales now showed itself.", "마지막 한 가지 점에서, 서로 겨루는 두 골짜기 사이의 뚜렷한 차이가 이제 드러났다."], ["The secret of Blackmoor was best discovered from the heights around; to read aright the valley before her it was necessary to descend into its midst.", "블랙무어의 비밀은 둘러싼 높은 곳에서 가장 잘 발견되었다. 그러나 눈앞의 골짜기를 제대로 읽으려면 그 한가운데로 내려가야만 했다."], ["When Tess had accomplished this feat she found herself to be standing on a carpeted level, which stretched to the east and west as far as the eye could reach.", "테스가 이 일을 해냈을 때, 그녀는 자신이 동서로 눈길 닿는 데까지 펼쳐진 양탄자 같은 평지 위에 서 있음을 알았다."]], [["The river had stolen from the higher tracts and brought in particles to the vale all this horizontal land; and now, exhausted, aged, and attenuated, lay serpentining along through the midst of its former spoils.", "강은 더 높은 지대에서 흙을 훔쳐와 이 골짜기에 입자를 날라다 이 수평의 땅을 모두 만들었다. 그리고 이제 지치고 늙고 가늘어진 채로, 한때 제가 약탈한 것들의 한복판을 구불구불 누비며 누워 있었다."]], [["Not quite sure of her direction, Tess stood still upon the hemmed expanse of verdant flatness, like a fly on a billiard-table of indefinite length, and of no more consequence to the surroundings than that fly.", "방향을 그리 확신하지 못한 채, 테스는 둘러싸인 푸른 평지의 너른 공간 위에 가만히 서 있었다. 끝이 어딘지 모를 당구대 위의 파리 한 마리처럼, 그리고 그 주변에게는 그 파리만큼도 대수롭지 않은 존재로."], ["The sole effect of her presence upon the placid valley so far had been to excite the mind of a solitary heron, which, after descending to the ground not far from her path, stood with neck erect, looking at her.", "지금껏 이 고요한 골짜기에 그녀의 존재가 끼친 유일한 효과라면, 외로운 왜가리 한 마리의 마음을 동요시킨 것뿐이었다. 그 새는 그녀의 길에서 멀지 않은 땅으로 내려앉더니, 목을 곧추세우고 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["Suddenly there arose from all parts of the lowland a prolonged and repeated call—“Waow! waow! waow!”", "갑자기 저지대 사방에서 길게 늘어지며 거듭되는 부름이 솟아올랐다—\"와우! 와우! 와우!\""]], [["From the furthest east to the furthest west the cries spread as if by contagion, accompanied in some cases by the barking of a dog.", "가장 먼 동쪽에서 가장 먼 서쪽까지 그 외침은 전염되듯 퍼져 나갔고, 더러는 개 짖는 소리가 곁들여졌다."], ["It was not the expression of the valley’s consciousness that beautiful Tess had arrived, but the ordinary announcement of milking-time—half-past four o’clock, when the dairymen set about getting in the cows.", "그것은 아름다운 테스가 당도했다는 골짜기의 의식이 드러난 것이 아니라, 그저 젖 짜는 시각을 알리는 평범한 신호였다—네 시 반, 낙농 일꾼들이 소들을 몰아들이기 시작하는 때였다."]], [["The red and white herd nearest at hand, which had been phlegmatically waiting for the call, now trooped towards the steading in the background, their great bags of milk swinging under them as they walked.", "가장 가까이서 부름을 무덤덤하게 기다리고 있던 붉고 흰 소떼가, 이제 뒤편의 농막을 향해 무리 지어 나아갔다. 걸을 때마다 커다란 젖통이 아래에서 흔들거렸다."], ["Tess followed slowly in their rear, and entered the barton by the open gate through which they had entered before her.", "테스는 천천히 그 뒤를 따라가, 소들이 앞서 들어간 열린 문을 지나 마당으로 들어섰다."], ["Long thatched sheds stretched round the enclosure, their slopes encrusted with vivid green moss, and their eaves supported by wooden posts rubbed to a glossy smoothness by the flanks of infinite cows and calves of bygone years, now passed to an oblivion almost inconceivable in its profundity.", "길게 늘어선 이엉 지붕 헛간들이 울안을 빙 둘렀고, 그 비탈에는 선명한 초록 이끼가 더께처럼 끼어 있었으며, 그 처마는 지난 세월의 수많은 소와 송아지들의 옆구리에 닳아 반들반들 매끄러워진 나무 기둥들로 떠받쳐져 있었다. 그 소들은 이제 그 깊이를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 망각 속으로 사라져 버렸다."], ["Between the post were ranged the milchers, each exhibiting herself at the present moment to a whimsical eye in the rear as a circle on two stalks, down the centre of which a switch moved pendulum-wise; while the sun, lowering itself behind this patient row, threw their shadows accurately inwards upon the wall.", "기둥들 사이에는 젖소들이 줄지어 있었는데, 지금 이 순간 뒤쪽의 별난 눈에는 저마다 두 개의 줄기 위에 얹힌 원으로 보였고, 그 한가운데로 꼬리 하나가 추처럼 흔들렸다. 한편 해는 이 참을성 많은 줄 뒤로 내려앉으며, 그들의 그림자를 벽 안쪽으로 정확히 던졌다."], ["Thus it threw shadows of these obscure and homely figures every evening with as much care over each contour as if it had been the profile of a court beauty on a palace wall; copied them as diligently as it had copied Olympian shapes on marble _façades_ long ago, or the outline of Alexander, Caesar, and the Pharaohs.", "그렇게 해는 이 어둑하고 수수한 형상들의 그림자를, 마치 궁전 벽에 비친 궁정 미녀의 옆모습이라도 되는 양 윤곽 하나하나에 정성을 다해 매일 저녁 던졌다. 오래전 대리석 정면에 올림포스의 형상들을, 혹은 알렉산드로스와 카이사르와 파라오들의 윤곽을 베껴 그렸던 만큼이나 부지런히 그것들을 베껴 냈다."]], [["They were the less restful cows that were stalled.", "칸막이 우리에 매인 것은 덜 얌전한 소들이었다."], ["Those that would stand still of their own will were milked in the middle of the yard, where many of such better behaved ones stood waiting now—all prime milchers, such as were seldom seen out of this valley, and not always within it; nourished by the succulent feed which the water-meads supplied at this prime season of the year.", "제 의지로 가만히 서 있을 소들은 마당 한복판에서 젖을 짰고, 지금도 그렇게 더 점잖은 소들 여럿이 거기 서서 기다리고 있었다—모두 일급 젖소들로, 이 골짜기 밖에서는 좀처럼 볼 수 없고 골짜기 안에서도 늘 볼 수 있는 것은 아니었다. 일 년 중 한창인 이 철에 물가 풀밭이 대 주는 즙 많은 먹이로 살을 찌운 것들이었다."], ["Those of them that were spotted with white reflected the sunshine in dazzling brilliancy, and the polished brass knobs of their horns glittered with something of military display.", "그중 흰 점이 박힌 소들은 햇빛을 받아 눈부시게 반짝였고, 뿔에 박힌 윤나는 놋쇠 마디는 군대의 위용 같은 것을 띠며 번득였다."], ["Their large-veined udders hung ponderous as sandbags, the teats sticking out like the legs of a gipsy’s crock; and as each animal lingered for her turn to arrive the milk oozed forth and fell in drops to the ground.", "굵은 핏줄이 선 젖통은 모래주머니처럼 묵직하게 늘어졌고, 젖꼭지는 집시 항아리의 다리처럼 삐죽 튀어나와 있었다. 저마다 차례가 오기를 기다리며 머뭇거리는 동안 젖이 새어 나와 방울져 땅에 떨어졌다."]], [["# XVII", "# XVII"]], [["The dairymaids and men had flocked down from their cottages and out of the dairy-house with the arrival of the cows from the meads; the maids walking in pattens, not on account of the weather, but to keep their shoes above the mulch of the barton.", "XVII\n풀밭에서 소들이 당도하자, 젖 짜는 처녀들과 사내들이 오두막에서, 또 낙농장 집에서 우르르 내려왔다. 처녀들은 나막신을 신고 걸었는데, 날씨 때문이 아니라 마당의 진창 위로 신발을 띄워 두려는 것이었다."], ["Each girl sat down on her three-legged stool, her face sideways, her right cheek resting against the cow, and looked musingly along the animal’s flank at Tess as she approached.", "처녀들은 저마다 세 발 걸상에 앉아 얼굴을 옆으로 돌리고 오른뺨을 소에 기댄 채, 다가오는 테스를 소의 옆구리 너머로 골똘히 바라보았다."], ["The male milkers, with hat-brims turned down, resting flat on their foreheads and gazing on the ground, did not observe her.", "모자챙을 내려 이마에 납작 붙이고 땅바닥을 바라보던 사내 일꾼들은 그녀를 알아채지 못했다."]], [["One of these was a sturdy middle-aged man—whose long white “pinner” was somewhat finer and cleaner than the wraps of the others, and whose jacket underneath had a presentable marketing aspect—the master-dairyman, of whom she was in quest, his double character as a working milker and butter maker here during six days, and on the seventh as a man in shining broad-cloth in his family pew at church, being so marked as to have inspired a rhyme:", "그중 하나는 다부진 중년 사내였다—그의 긴 흰 작업 앞치마는 다른 이들의 두름천보다 조금 더 곱고 깨끗했고, 그 아래 입은 윗도리는 장에 나갈 만한 번듯한 차림새였다—그가 바로 그녀가 찾던 주인 낙농가였다. 엿새 동안은 젖을 짜고 버터를 만드는 일꾼이었다가, 이렛날에는 번들거리는 고급 나사옷을 입고 교회의 가족석에 앉는 신사로 변하는 그의 이중 면모가 어찌나 두드러졌던지, 운까지 붙은 노래가 생겼을 정도였다."]], [["Dairyman Dick All the week: On Sundays Mister Richard Crick.", "낙농가 딕 한 주 내내는 딕이지만 일요일엔 리처드 크릭 나리."]], [["Seeing Tess standing at gaze he went across to her.", "테스가 멍하니 서 있는 것을 보고 그는 그녀에게 다가왔다."]], [["The majority of dairymen have a cross manner at milking time, but it happened that Mr Crick was glad to get a new hand—for the days were busy ones now—and he received her warmly; inquiring for her mother and the rest of the family—(though this as a matter of form merely, for in reality he had not been aware of Mrs Durbeyfield’s existence till apprised of the fact by a brief business-letter about Tess).", "낙농가들은 대개 젖 짜는 시각에 퉁명스러운 태도를 보이지만, 마침 크릭 씨는 새 일손을 얻게 되어 기뻤다—요즘은 바쁜 나날이었던 것이다—그래서 그는 그녀를 따뜻이 맞으며 어머니와 나머지 식구들의 안부를 물었다(다만 이것은 그저 형식적인 것이었으니, 사실 그는 테스에 관한 짤막한 사무 편지로 알기 전까지는 더비필드 부인의 존재조차 모르고 있었다)."]], [["“Oh—ay, as a lad I knowed your part o’ the country very well,”", "\"오—그래, 어릴 적엔 자네 동네를 아주 잘 알았지.\""], ["he said terminatively.", "그가 말을 맺듯 말했다."], ["“Though I’ve never been there since.", "\"그 뒤로는 한 번도 가 본 적 없지만 말이야."], ["And a aged woman of ninety that use to live nigh here, but is dead and gone long ago, told me that a family of some such name as yours in Blackmoor Vale came originally from these parts, and that ’twere a old ancient race that had all but perished off the earth—though the new generations didn’t know it.", "그리고 옛날에 이 근처에 살던, 지금은 죽어 사라진 지 오래된 아흔 살 노파가 나한테 그러더군. 블랙무어 골짜기에 자네하고 비슷한 성을 가진 집안이 원래 이쪽 출신이고, 이 땅에서 거의 다 스러져 버린 아주 오래된 옛 가문인데—새 세대는 그걸 모른다고 말이야."], ["But, Lord, I took no notice of the old woman’s ramblings, not I.”", "하지만, 원 참, 나야 그 노파의 횡설수설을 귀담아듣지 않았지. 안 듣고말고.\""]], [["“Oh no—it is nothing,”", "\"아, 아니에요—아무것도 아니에요.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["Then the talk was of business only.", "그러고 나서는 일 이야기뿐이었다."]], [["“You can milk ’em clean, my maidy?", "\"젖을 깨끗이 짤 줄은 아나, 아가씨?"], ["I don’t want my cows going azew at this time o’ year.”", "이맘때 내 소들이 젖이 마르는 건 싫으니까.\""]], [["She reassured him on that point, and he surveyed her up and down.", "그녀가 그 점은 안심시키자, 그는 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았다."], ["She had been staying indoors a good deal, and her complexion had grown delicate.", "그녀는 그동안 집 안에만 많이 틀어박혀 있었던 탓에 살결이 섬약해져 있었다."]], [["“Quite sure you can stand it?", "\"정말 견딜 수 있겠나?"], ["’Tis comfortable enough here for rough folk; but we don’t live in a cowcumber frame.”", "거친 사람들한테는 여기가 그럭저럭 편하긴 하지만, 우리가 오이 모종틀 속에서 사는 건 아니라서 말이야.\""]], [["She declared that she could stand it, and her zest and willingness seemed to win him over.", "그녀가 견딜 수 있다고 잘라 말하자, 그녀의 열의와 의욕이 그의 마음을 산 듯했다."]], [["“Well, I suppose you’ll want a dish o’ tay, or victuals of some sort, hey?", "\"음, 차 한 그릇이나 뭐 먹을 거라도 좀 들겠지, 응?"], ["Not yet? Well, do as ye like about it.", "아직은 아니라고? 음, 알아서 하게나."], ["But faith, if ’twas I, I should be as dry as a kex wi’ travelling so far.”", "하지만 정말이지, 나 같으면 그렇게 먼 길을 와서는 마른 풀줄기처럼 목이 탔을 거야.\""]], [["“I’ll begin milking now, to get my hand in,” said Tess.", "\"제 손에 익히게 지금부터 젖을 짜겠어요.\" 테스가 말했다."]], [["She drank a little milk as temporary refreshment—to the surprise—indeed, slight contempt—of Dairyman Crick, to whose mind it had apparently never occurred that milk was good as a beverage.", "그녀는 잠시 기운을 차리려고 우유를 조금 마셨는데—낙농가 크릭은 놀랐다, 아니 사실 약간 경멸하는 기색이었다. 그의 머릿속에는 우유가 마실 것으로 좋다는 생각이 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었던 모양이다."]], [["“Oh, if ye can swaller that, be it so,”", "\"오, 그걸 삼킬 수 있다면야 그러게나.\""], ["he said indifferently, while holding up the pail that she sipped from.", "그가 그녀가 홀짝이는 들통을 들어 주며 무심히 말했다."], ["“’Tis what I hain’t touched for years—not I.", "\"난 몇 년째 입에도 안 댄 거지—안 대고말고."], ["Rot the stuff; it would lie in my innerds like lead.", "빌어먹을 그거, 내 뱃속에 납덩이처럼 들어앉을 텐데."], ["You can try your hand upon she,”", "저 녀석한테 손을 대 보게.\""], ["he pursued, nodding to the nearest cow.", "그가 가장 가까운 소를 향해 고갯짓하며 말을 이었다."], ["“Not but what she do milk rather hard.", "\"그래도 저 녀석은 젖이 좀 빡빡하게 나오긴 해."], ["We’ve hard ones and we’ve easy ones, like other folks. However, you’ll find out that soon enough.”", "우리한테는 빡빡한 놈도 있고 수월한 놈도 있지, 사람들처럼 말이야. 어쨌든 곧 알게 될 거야.\""]], [["When Tess had changed her bonnet for a hood, and was really on her stool under the cow, and the milk was squirting from her fists into the pail, she appeared to feel that she really had laid a new foundation for her future.", "테스가 보닛을 두건으로 바꿔 쓰고, 정말로 소 밑 걸상에 앉아, 두 주먹에서 우유가 들통 속으로 쭉쭉 뿜어 나오자, 그녀는 정말로 자기 앞날의 새 토대를 놓았다는 느낌이 드는 듯했다."], ["The conviction bred serenity, her pulse slowed, and she was able to look about her.", "그 확신이 평온을 낳았고, 맥박이 느려졌으며, 그녀는 주위를 둘러볼 여유가 생겼다."]], [["The milkers formed quite a little battalion of men and maids, the men operating on the hard-teated animals, the maids on the kindlier natures.", "젖 짜는 일꾼들은 사내와 처녀로 이루어진 제법 작은 한 부대를 이루었는데, 사내들은 젖꼭지가 빡빡한 소들을 맡고, 처녀들은 더 순한 성질의 소들을 맡았다."], ["It was a large dairy.", "큰 낙농장이었다."], ["There were nearly a hundred milchers under Crick’s management, all told; and of the herd the master-dairyman milked six or eight with his own hands, unless away from home.", "크릭의 관리 아래 젖소가 모두 백 마리 가까이 있었다. 그 소떼 가운데 여섯에서 여덟 마리는 주인 낙농가가 집을 비울 때가 아니면 제 손으로 직접 짰다. 이것들은 모든 소 가운데서도 젖이 가장 빡빡한 놈들이었다."], ["These were the cows that milked hardest of all; for his journey-milkmen being more or less casually hired, he would not entrust this half-dozen to their treatment, lest, from indifference, they should not milk them fully; nor to the maids, lest they should fail in the same way for lack of finger-grip; with the result that in course of time the cows would “go azew”—that is, dry up.", "그의 임시 젖꾼들은 그때그때 다소 되는대로 고용한 자들이라, 그는 이 여섯 마리를 그들 손에 맡기려 하지 않았다—성의 없이 굴어 젖을 끝까지 다 짜지 않을까 봐서였다. 또 처녀들에게도 맡기지 않았다—손아귀 힘이 모자라 똑같은 식으로 일을 그르칠까 봐서였다. 그 결과 시간이 흐르면 소들은 \"젖이 마르게\", 곧 젖이 끊기게 되곤 했다."], ["It was not the loss for the moment that made slack milking so serious, but that with the decline of demand there came decline, and ultimately cessation, of supply.", "젖을 게을리 짜는 것이 그토록 심각한 까닭은 당장의 손실 때문이 아니라, 수요가 줄면 공급도 줄고, 끝내는 공급이 멎어 버리기 때문이었다."]], [["After Tess had settled down to her cow there was for a time no talk in the barton, and not a sound interfered with the purr of the milk-jets into the numerous pails, except a momentary exclamation to one or other of the beasts requesting her to turn round or stand still.", "테스가 제 소 앞에 자리를 잡은 뒤로 한동안 마당에는 아무 말이 없었고, 여러 들통 속으로 젖이 들어가는 그르렁 소리를 방해하는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 다만 이 짐승 저 짐승에게 돌아서라거나 가만히 서 있으라고 외치는 잠깐의 고함만 가끔 끼어들 뿐이었다."], ["The only movements were those of the milkers’ hands up and down, and the swing of the cows’ tails.", "움직이는 것이라곤 젖꾼들의 손이 위아래로 오르내리는 것과 소꼬리가 흔들리는 것뿐이었다."], ["Thus they all worked on, encompassed by the vast flat mead which extended to either slope of the valley—a level landscape compounded of old landscapes long forgotten, and, no doubt, differing in character very greatly from the landscape they composed now.", "그렇게 그들 모두는, 골짜기 양쪽 비탈까지 펼쳐진 광활하고 평평한 풀밭에 둘러싸여 일을 이어 갔다—오래전 잊힌 옛 풍경들이 한데 합쳐져 이루어진 평탄한 풍경이었고, 틀림없이 지금 그것들이 이루고 있는 풍경과는 성격이 크게 달랐을 것이다."]], [["“To my thinking,”", "\"내 생각엔 말이야,\""], ["said the dairyman, rising suddenly from a cow he had just finished off, snatching up his three-legged stool in one hand and the pail in the other, and moving on to the next hard-yielder in his vicinity, “to my thinking, the cows don’t gie down their milk to-day as usual.", "방금 끝낸 소에서 갑자기 일어서며, 한 손에는 세 발 걸상을, 다른 손에는 들통을 낚아채 들고 가까이 있는 다음 빡빡한 놈에게로 옮겨 가며 낙농가가 말했다. \"내 생각엔, 오늘 소들이 젖을 여느 때처럼 내주질 않는단 말이지."], ["Upon my life, if Winker do begin keeping back like this, she’ll not be worth going under by midsummer.”", "정말이지, 윙커가 이렇게 젖을 안 내주기 시작하면, 한여름쯤엔 밑에 들어가 짤 가치도 없어질 거야.\""]], [["“’Tis because there’s a new hand come among us,”", "\"새 일손이 우리 사이에 들어와서 그래요.\""], ["said Jonathan Kail.", "조너선 카일이 말했다."], ["“I’ve noticed such things afore.”", "\"전에도 그런 걸 본 적이 있다고요.\""]], [["“To be sure.", "\"그렇겠지."], ["It may be so.", "그럴지도 몰라."], ["I didn’t think o’t.”", "난 그 생각을 못 했군.\""]], [["“I’ve been told that it goes up into their horns at such times,” said a dairymaid.", "\"그럴 때면 젖이 뿔 속으로 올라간다고들 하던데요.\" 한 젖 짜는 처녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well, as to going up into their horns,”", "\"음, 뿔 속으로 올라간다는 건 말이지,\""], ["replied Dairyman Crick dubiously, as though even witchcraft might be limited by anatomical possibilities, “I couldn’t say; I certainly could not.", "낙농가 크릭이 미심쩍게 대꾸했다. 마치 마법조차 해부학적 가능성에 제약을 받기라도 한다는 듯이. \"글쎄, 모르겠는걸."], ["But as nott cows will keep it back as well as the horned ones, I don’t quite agree to it.", "정말 모르겠어. 하지만 뿔 없는 소도 뿔 있는 놈만큼이나 젖을 안 내주니까, 난 그 말엔 영 동의가 안 돼."], ["Do ye know that riddle about the nott cows, Jonathan?", "자네 그 뿔 없는 소 수수께끼 아나, 조너선?"], ["Why do nott cows give less milk in a year than horned?”", "뿔 없는 소가 왜 뿔 있는 소보다 일 년에 젖을 덜 내줄까?\""]], [["“I don’t!”", "\"몰라요!\""], ["interposed the milkmaid, “Why do they?”", "젖 짜는 처녀가 끼어들었다. \"왜 그래요?\""]], [["“Because there bain’t so many of ’em,”", "\"뿔 없는 놈이 그렇게 많지가 않으니까.\""], ["said the dairyman.", "낙농가가 말했다."], ["“Howsomever, these gam’sters do certainly keep back their milk to-day.", "\"어쨌거나, 이 장난꾼들이 오늘 젖을 안 내주는 건 분명해."], ["Folks, we must lift up a stave or two—that’s the only cure for’t.”", "여보게들, 가락이나 한두 자락 뽑아야겠어—그게 유일한 처방이거든.\""]], [["Songs were often resorted to in dairies hereabout as an enticement to the cows when they showed signs of withholding their usual yield; and the band of milkers at this request burst into melody—in purely business-like tones, it is true, and with no great spontaneity; the result, according to their own belief, being a decided improvement during the song’s continuance.", "이 근방 낙농장에서는 소들이 평소의 산출량을 내주지 않으려는 기색을 보이면, 소들을 꾀어내는 수단으로 노래를 곧잘 동원했다. 이 청에 따라 젖꾼들의 무리가 가락을 터뜨렸다—사실 순전히 사무적인 어조였고 그리 신명이 나는 것도 아니었다. 그래도 그들 나름의 믿음에 따르면, 노래가 이어지는 동안에는 뚜렷한 개선이 있었다."], ["When they had gone through fourteen or fifteen verses of a cheerful ballad about a murderer who was afraid to go to bed in the dark because he saw certain brimstone flames around him, one of the male milkers said—", "어둠 속에서 잠자리에 들기를 무서워하는 어느 살인자에 관한—제 둘레에 유황 불길이 어른거리는 게 보여서 그랬다는—흥겨운 발라드를 열네댓 절쯤 다 부르고 났을 때, 사내 젖꾼 하나가 말했다—"]], [["“I wish singing on the stoop didn’t use up so much of a man’s wind!", "\"걸상에 앉아 노래 부르는 게 이렇게 사람 숨을 다 빼먹지만 않아도 좋으련만!"], ["You should get your harp, sir; not but what a fiddle is best.”", "나리, 나리 하프를 가져오시지요. 그래도 바이올린이 제일 낫긴 합니다만.\""]], [["Tess, who had given ear to this, thought the words were addressed to the dairyman, but she was wrong.", "이 말에 귀를 기울이던 테스는 그 말이 낙농가에게 한 것인 줄 알았으나, 틀렸다."], ["A reply, in the shape of “Why?” came as it were out of the belly of a dun cow in the stalls; it had been spoken by a milker behind the animal, whom she had not hitherto perceived.", "\"왜?\"라는 대꾸가, 말하자면 우리에 매인 암갈색 소의 뱃속에서 나오는 것처럼 들려왔다. 그것은 그 짐승 뒤에 있던, 그녀가 여태 알아채지 못한 젖꾼이 한 말이었다."]], [["“Oh yes; there’s nothing like a fiddle,”", "\"오, 그렇지. 바이올린만 한 게 없지.\""], ["said the dairyman.", "낙농가가 말했다."], ["“Though I do think that bulls are more moved by a tune than cows—at least that’s my experience.", "\"하긴 황소가 암소보다 가락에 더 마음이 움직이는 것 같긴 해—적어도 내 경험으론 그래."], ["Once there was an old aged man over at Mellstock—William Dewy by name—one of the family that used to do a good deal of business as tranters over there—Jonathan, do ye mind?—I knowed the man by sight as well as I know my own brother, in a manner of speaking.", "한번은 멜스톡에 늙은 노인이 하나 있었는데—이름이 윌리엄 듀이라고—거기서 짐꾼으로 일을 꽤 하던 집안 사람이었지—조너선, 자네 기억나나?—내가 내 친형을 아는 것만큼이나, 말하자면, 그 사람 얼굴을 잘 알았거든."], ["Well, this man was a coming home along from a wedding, where he had been playing his fiddle, one fine moonlight night, and for shortness’ sake he took a cut across Forty-acres, a field lying that way, where a bull was out to grass.", "그래, 이 사람이 어느 좋은 달밤에 결혼식에서 바이올린을 켜고 집으로 돌아오는 길이었는데, 지름길로 가려고 포티에이커스라는 밭을 가로질렀어. 거기에 황소 한 마리가 풀을 뜯으러 나와 있었지."], ["The bull seed William, and took after him, horns aground, begad; and though William runned his best, and hadn’t _much_ drink in him (considering ’twas a wedding, and the folks well off), he found he’d never reach the fence and get over in time to save himself.", "황소가 윌리엄을 보더니, 뿔을 땅에 들이대고 그를 쫓아오는 거야, 이런! 윌리엄이 죽을힘을 다해 뛰었지만—게다가 술도 그리 많이 마시지 않았는데도(결혼식이고 사람들도 넉넉했던 걸 생각하면 말이지)—제때 울타리에 닿아 넘어가 목숨을 구하기는 글렀다는 걸 깨달았어."], ["Well, as a last thought, he pulled out his fiddle as he runned, and struck up a jig, turning to the bull, and backing towards the corner.", "그래, 마지막 생각으로, 그는 뛰면서 바이올린을 꺼내 들고 지그를 켜기 시작했어. 황소 쪽으로 돌아서서 구석으로 뒷걸음치면서 말이야."], ["The bull softened down, and stood still, looking hard at William Dewy, who fiddled on and on; till a sort of a smile stole over the bull’s face.", "황소가 누그러지더니 가만히 서서 윌리엄 듀이를 빤히 쳐다봤어. 윌리엄은 계속 켜고 또 켰지. 그러다 황소 얼굴에 일종의 미소 같은 게 슬며시 번지더란 말이야."], ["But no sooner did William stop his playing and turn to get over hedge than the bull would stop his smiling and lower his horns towards the seat of William’s breeches.", "그런데 윌리엄이 연주를 멈추고 울타리를 넘으려고 돌아서기만 하면, 황소는 미소를 거두고 윌리엄의 바지 엉덩이 쪽으로 뿔을 들이대는 거야."], ["Well, William had to turn about and play on, willy-nilly; and ’twas only three o’clock in the world, and ’a knowed that nobody would come that way for hours, and he so leery and tired that ’a didn’t know what to do.", "그래, 윌리엄은 좋든 싫든 다시 돌아서서 계속 켤 수밖에 없었어. 게다가 세상에 겨우 새벽 세 시였고, 몇 시간이 지나도 아무도 그 길로 안 올 걸 그도 알았지. 어찌나 졸리고 지쳤던지 어쩔 줄을 몰랐어."], ["When he had scraped till about four o’clock he felt that he verily would have to give over soon, and he said to himself, ‘There’s only this last tune between me and eternal welfare!", "네 시쯤까지 켜고 났을 때 그는 곧 정말로 손을 놓을 수밖에 없겠다 싶어서 속으로 그랬어. '이제 이 마지막 한 곡이 나하고 영원한 안녕 사이에 놓인 전부로구나!"], ["Heaven save me, or I’m a done man.’ Well, then he called to mind how he’d seen the cattle kneel o’ Christmas Eves in the dead o’ night.", "하느님, 절 구하소서, 안 그러면 전 끝장입니다.' 그래, 그러다 그는 크리스마스이브 한밤중에 가축들이 무릎 꿇는 걸 본 일이 떠올랐어."], ["It was not Christmas Eve then, but it came into his head to play a trick upon the bull.", "그땐 크리스마스이브가 아니었지만, 황소한테 한번 속임수를 써 보자는 생각이 머리에 떠오른 거야."], ["So he broke into the ’Tivity Hymm, just as at Christmas carol-singing; when, lo and behold, down went the bull on his bended knees, in his ignorance, just as if ’twere the true ’Tivity night and hour.", "그래서 그는, 크리스마스 캐럴을 부를 때처럼, 「강생 찬송가」를 켜기 시작했어. 그랬더니, 이런 세상에, 황소가 굽힌 두 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉는 거야. 그 무지함 속에서, 마치 정말 강생의 밤 그 시각이기라도 한 것처럼 말이지."], ["As soon as his horned friend were down, William turned, clinked off like a long-dog, and jumped safe over hedge, before the praying bull had got on his feet again to take after him.", "그 뿔 달린 친구가 주저앉기 무섭게 윌리엄은 돌아서서 사냥개처럼 후딱 달아나, 기도하던 황소가 다시 일어나 쫓아오기도 전에 무사히 울타리를 뛰어넘었어."], ["William used to say that he’d seen a man look a fool a good many times, but never such a fool as that bull looked when he found his pious feelings had been played upon, and ’twas not Christmas Eve.", "윌리엄은 늘 그러더군, 사람이 바보 같은 꼴을 하는 걸 여러 번 봤지만, 자기 경건한 감정을 가지고 장난친 거고 크리스마스이브도 아니었다는 걸 알았을 때 그 황소만큼 바보 같은 꼴은 본 적이 없다고."], ["Yes, William Dewy, that was the man’s name; and I can tell you to a foot where’s he a-lying in Mellstock Churchyard at this very moment—just between the second yew-tree and the north aisle.”", "그래, 윌리엄 듀이, 그게 그 사람 이름이야. 그리고 지금 이 순간 그가 멜스톡 교회 묘지 어디에 누워 있는지 한 발자국까지 짚어 줄 수 있지—바로 두 번째 주목과 북쪽 회랑 사이라고.\""]], [["“It’s a curious story; it carries us back to medieval times, when faith was a living thing!”", "\"기묘한 이야기로군요. 우리를 신앙이 살아 있던 중세로 데려가네요!\""]], [["The remark, singular for a dairy-yard, was murmured by the voice behind the dun cow; but as nobody understood the reference, no notice was taken, except that the narrator seemed to think it might imply scepticism as to his tale.", "낙농장 마당에서는 유별난 그 말이, 암갈색 소 뒤의 목소리로 중얼거려졌다. 하지만 그 언급을 알아듣는 이가 아무도 없어 누구도 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 다만 이야기를 들려준 사람만이 그것이 제 이야기를 미심쩍어하는 뜻일지도 모른다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["“Well, ’tis quite true, sir, whether or no.", "\"음, 그게 사실이고말고요, 나리. 사실이든 아니든 말입니다."], ["I knowed the man well.”", "그 사람을 잘 알았는걸요.\""]], [["“Oh yes; I have no doubt of it,”", "\"오, 그럼요. 의심하지 않습니다.\""], ["said the person behind the dun cow.", "암갈색 소 뒤의 사람이 말했다."]], [["Tess’s attention was thus attracted to the dairyman’s interlocutor, of whom she could see but the merest patch, owing to his burying his head so persistently in the flank of the milcher.", "그렇게 테스의 주의는 낙농가의 말상대에게 끌렸는데, 그가 어찌나 끈질기게 젖소의 옆구리에 머리를 파묻고 있던지 그녀에게는 그의 아주 작은 일부밖에 보이지 않았다."], ["She could not understand why he should be addressed as “sir” even by the dairyman himself.", "어째서 낙농가 본인까지 그를 \"나리\"라고 부르는지 그녀는 이해할 수 없었다."], ["But no explanation was discernible; he remained under the cow long enough to have milked three, uttering a private ejaculation now and then, as if he could not get on.", "하지만 까닭을 알아낼 길이 없었다. 그는 소 세 마리는 짜고도 남을 만큼 오래 소 밑에 있으면서, 일이 잘 안 풀리는 듯 이따금 혼자 끙 소리를 냈다."]], [["“Take it gentle, sir; take it gentle,”", "\"살살 하세요, 나리. 살살요.\""], ["said the dairyman.", "낙농가가 말했다."], ["“’Tis knack, not strength, that does it.”", "\"그건 힘이 아니라 요령으로 하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“So I find,” said the other, standing up at last and stretching his arms.", "\"나도 알겠소.\" 마침내 일어서서 두 팔을 뻗으며 상대가 말했다."], ["“I think I have finished her, however, though she made my fingers ache.”", "\"하지만 이 녀석은 끝낸 것 같소, 손가락이 다 쑤시긴 하지만.\""]], [["Tess could then see him at full length.", "그제야 테스는 그의 전신을 볼 수 있었다."], ["He wore the ordinary white pinner and leather leggings of a dairy-farmer when milking, and his boots were clogged with the mulch of the yard; but this was all his local livery.", "그는 젖 짤 때 낙농업자가 입는 흔한 흰 앞치마에 가죽 각반을 차고 있었고, 장화는 마당의 진창으로 더께가 앉아 있었다. 하지만 이것이 그의 이 고장 차림새 전부였다."], ["Beneath it was something educated, reserved, subtle, sad, differing.", "그 아래에는 무언가 교양 있고, 절제되고, 섬세하고, 슬프고, 남다른 것이 있었다."]], [["But the details of his aspect were temporarily thrust aside by the discovery that he was one whom she had seen before.", "하지만 그 외양의 세세한 부분은, 그가 전에 본 적 있는 사람이라는 발견 앞에 잠시 밀려났다."], ["Such vicissitudes had Tess passed through since that time that for a moment she could not remember where she had met him; and then it flashed upon her that he was the pedestrian who had joined in the club-dance at Marlott—the passing stranger who had come she knew not whence, had danced with others but not with her, and slightingly left her, and gone on his way with his friends.", "그때 이후로 테스가 워낙 많은 우여곡절을 겪은 터라, 그녀는 한순간 그를 어디서 만났는지 떠올리지 못했다. 그러다 그가 말롯의 클럽 춤판에 끼어들었던 그 도보 여행객임이 퍼뜩 떠올랐다—어디서 왔는지 모를, 지나가던 낯선 사람, 다른 처녀들과는 춤추었으나 그녀와는 추지 않았고, 그녀를 가벼이 두고 친구들과 제 갈 길로 가 버린 그 사람."]], [["The flood of memories brought back by this revival of an incident anterior to her troubles produced a momentary dismay lest, recognizing her also, he should by some means discover her story.", "자신의 곤경 이전에 있었던 한 사건이 이렇게 되살아나며 밀려든 기억의 물결은, 그가 그녀 또한 알아보고 어떤 식으로든 그녀의 사연을 알아낼까 하는 순간적인 당혹을 일으켰다."], ["But it passed away when she found no sign of remembrance in him.", "하지만 그에게서 알아보는 기색이 전혀 없자 그 당혹은 가셨다."], ["She saw by degrees that since their first and only encounter his mobile face had grown more thoughtful, and had acquired a young man’s shapely moustache and beard—the latter of the palest straw colour where it began upon his cheeks, and deepening to a warm brown farther from its root.", "그녀는 차츰, 처음이자 유일했던 그 만남 이후로 그의 활기 있는 얼굴이 더 사려 깊어졌고, 젊은 남자다운 맵시 있는 콧수염과 턱수염이 자랐음을 알아보았다—턱수염은 뺨에서 시작되는 부분은 가장 옅은 밀짚 빛깔이었다가, 뿌리에서 멀어질수록 따뜻한 갈색으로 짙어졌다."], ["Under his linen milking-pinner he wore a dark velveteen jacket, cord breeches and gaiters, and a starched white shirt.", "아마포 젖 짜기 앞치마 아래로 그는 짙은 골덴 윗도리에 코듀로이 반바지와 각반을 차고, 풀 먹인 흰 셔츠를 입고 있었다."], ["Without the milking-gear nobody could have guessed what he was.", "젖 짜는 장비를 빼면 그가 무엇 하는 사람인지 아무도 짐작하지 못했을 것이다."], ["He might with equal probability have been an eccentric landowner or a gentlemanly ploughman.", "그는 괴짜 지주일 수도 있었고 신사 같은 농부일 수도 있었으니, 어느 쪽이든 그럴듯했다."], ["That he was but a novice at dairy work she had realized in a moment, from the time he had spent upon the milking of one cow.", "그가 낙농 일에는 풋내기에 불과하다는 것을, 그녀는 그가 소 한 마리 젖을 짜는 데 들인 시간으로 단박에 알아챘다."]], [["Meanwhile many of the milkmaids had said to one another of the newcomer, “How pretty she is!”", "그러는 동안 젖 짜는 처녀들 여럿이 새로 온 처녀를 두고 서로 이렇게 말했다. \"쟤 참 예쁘지 않니!\""], ["with something of real generosity and admiration, though with a half hope that the auditors would qualify the assertion—which, strictly speaking, they might have done, prettiness being an inexact definition of what struck the eye in Tess.", "진심 어린 너그러움과 감탄이 담긴 말이었으나, 듣는 이가 그 단언을 조금 깎아 주기를 은근히 바라는 마음도 절반쯤 섞여 있었다—엄밀히 말하면 그렇게 깎을 수도 있었으니, 예쁘다는 말은 테스를 보고 눈에 와닿는 것에 대한 부정확한 정의였기 때문이다."], ["When the milking was finished for the evening they straggled indoors, where Mrs Crick, the dairyman’s wife—who was too respectable to go out milking herself, and wore a hot stuff gown in warm weather because the dairymaids wore prints—was giving an eye to the leads and things.", "저녁 젖 짜기가 끝나자 그들은 줄줄이 집 안으로 들어갔다. 거기서는 낙농가의 아내인 크릭 부인이—그녀는 너무 점잖아서 직접 젖을 짜러 나가지 않았고, 젖 짜는 처녀들이 무명옷을 입으니 자기는 따뜻한 날씨에도 두꺼운 모직 가운을 입었다—통과 살림살이를 살피고 있었다."]], [["Only two or three of the maids, Tess learnt, slept in the dairy-house besides herself, most of the helpers going to their homes.", "테스는 자기 말고 처녀 두셋만이 낙농장 집에서 잔다는 것을 알게 되었고, 일손 대부분은 제집으로 돌아갔다."], ["She saw nothing at supper-time of the superior milker who had commented on the story, and asked no questions about him, the remainder of the evening being occupied in arranging her place in the bed-chamber.", "저녁 식사 때 그녀는 이야기에 한마디 거든 그 점잖은 젖꾼을 보지 못했고, 그에 대해 아무것도 묻지 않았다. 저녁 나머지 시간은 침실에서 자기 자리를 정리하는 데 보냈다."], ["It was a large room over the milk-house, some thirty feet long; the sleeping-cots of the other three indoor milkmaids being in the same apartment.", "그곳은 우유 저장실 위에 있는 넓은 방으로, 길이가 삼십 피트쯤 되었다. 집 안에서 자는 다른 세 젖 짜는 처녀의 잠자리도 같은 방에 있었다."], ["They were blooming young women, and, except one, rather older than herself.", "그들은 한창때의 젊은 여자들이었고, 하나만 빼고는 그녀보다 나이가 좀 위였다."], ["By bedtime Tess was thoroughly tired, and fell asleep immediately.", "잠자리에 들 무렵 테스는 완전히 지쳐, 곧장 잠이 들었다."]], [["But one of the girls, who occupied an adjoining bed, was more wakeful than Tess, and would insist upon relating to the latter various particulars of the homestead into which she had just entered.", "하지만 옆 침대를 쓰는 처녀 하나는 테스보다 잠이 덜 와서, 그녀가 막 발을 들인 이 집에 관한 이런저런 사정을 테스에게 기어이 들려주려 했다."], ["The girl’s whispered words mingled with the shades, and, to Tess’s drowsy mind, they seemed to be generated by the darkness in which they floated.", "그 처녀의 속삭이는 말은 어둠과 뒤섞였고, 졸음에 겨운 테스의 마음에는 그 말들이 둥둥 떠다니는 그 어둠 속에서 생겨나는 것처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["“Mr Angel Clare—he that is learning milking, and that plays the harp—never says much to us.", "\"엔젤 클레어 씨—젖 짜는 걸 배우는, 하프 켜는 그분 말이야—우리한텐 별로 말을 안 하셔."], ["He is a pa’son’s son, and is too much taken up wi’ his own thoughts to notice girls.", "목사님 아들인데, 자기 생각에 너무 빠져 있어서 여자애들은 거들떠보지도 않아."], ["He is the dairyman’s pupil—learning farming in all its branches.", "낙농가 주인의 견습생이거든—농사 일을 갈래갈래 다 배우는 거야."], ["He has learnt sheep-farming at another place, and he’s now mastering dairy-work....", "다른 데서 양 치는 걸 배웠고, 지금은 낙농 일을 익히는 중이고……"], ["Yes, he is quite the gentleman-born.", "그래, 아주 신사 집안 출신이야."], ["His father is the Reverend Mr Clare at Emminster—a good many miles from here.”", "그분 아버지가 에민스터의 클레어 목사님인데—여기서 몇 마일이나 떨어진 곳이지.\""]], [["“Oh—I have heard of him,”", "\"오—그분 얘긴 들어 봤어.\""], ["said her companion, now awake.", "이제 잠이 깬 그녀의 동무가 말했다."], ["“A very earnest clergyman, is he not?”", "\"아주 열성적인 목사님이라고, 그렇지 않니?\""]], [["“Yes—that he is—the earnestest man in all Wessex, they say—the last of the old Low Church sort, they tell me—for all about here be what they call High.", "\"맞아—그렇고말고—온 웨식스에서 가장 열성적인 분이래—옛날 저교회파의 마지막 사람이라더라—이 근방은 죄다 고교회파라고들 하는데 말이야."], ["All his sons, except our Mr Clare, be made pa’sons too.”", "그 댁 아들들은 우리 클레어 씨만 빼고 다 목사가 됐어.\""]], [["Tess had not at this hour the curiosity to ask why the present Mr Clare was not made a parson like his brethren, and gradually fell asleep again, the words of her informant coming to her along with the smell of the cheeses in the adjoining cheeseloft, and the measured dripping of the whey from the wrings downstairs.", "테스는 이 시각에는, 지금의 클레어 씨가 왜 형제들처럼 목사가 되지 않았는지 물어볼 호기심이 없었고, 점점 다시 잠이 들었다. 그녀에게 사정을 들려주던 처녀의 말은, 옆 치즈 다락에서 나는 치즈 냄새와, 아래층에서 압착틀로부터 일정하게 떨어지는 유장 소리와 함께 그녀에게 흘러들었다. XVIII"]], [["# XVIII", "# XVIII"]], [["Angel Clare rises out of the past not altogether as a distinct figure, but as an appreciative voice, a long regard of fixed, abstracted eyes, and a mobility of mouth somewhat too small and delicately lined for a man’s, though with an unexpectedly firm close of the lower lip now and then; enough to do away with any inference of indecision.", "엔젤 클레어는 과거로부터, 또렷한 인물의 모습이라기보다 음미하듯 듣던 목소리로, 한곳에 멍하니 못 박힌 눈의 긴 응시로, 그리고 남자치고는 다소 작고 가는 선이 새겨진 입의 움직임으로 떠오른다. 그래도 그 입은 이따금 아랫입술을 뜻밖에 단단히 다물어, 우유부단하다는 어떤 추측도 지워 버릴 만했다."], ["Nevertheless, something nebulous, preoccupied, vague, in his bearing and regard, marked him as one who probably had no very definite aim or concern about his material future.", "그럼에도 그의 거동과 시선에는 무언가 안개 같고, 골똘하고, 어렴풋한 것이 있어, 그가 아마 제 물질적 앞날에 대해 그리 뚜렷한 목표나 관심이 없는 사람임을 드러냈다."], ["Yet as a lad people had said of him that he was one who might do anything if he tried.", "그래도 소년 시절 사람들은 그를 두고, 마음만 먹으면 무엇이든 해낼 사람이라고들 했다."]], [["He was the youngest son of his father, a poor parson at the other end of the county, and had arrived at Talbothays Dairy as a six months’ pupil, after going the round of some other farms, his object being to acquire a practical skill in the various processes of farming, with a view either to the Colonies or the tenure of a home-farm, as circumstances might decide.", "그는 주의 반대편 끝에서 가난한 목사로 지내는 아버지의 막내아들로, 다른 농장 몇 군데를 두루 거친 뒤 여섯 달 견습생으로 탤보세이스 낙농장에 와 있었다. 그의 목적은 농사의 여러 과정에 실용적인 기술을 익히는 것이었고, 형편이 정해지는 대로 식민지로 가든지 본국에서 자작농장을 부치든지 할 작정이었다."]], [["His entry into the ranks of the agriculturists and breeders was a step in the young man’s career which had been anticipated neither by himself nor by others.", "농사꾼과 가축 사육자의 대열에 들어선 것은, 본인도 남들도 예상치 못한 이 젊은이의 행로였다."]], [["Mr Clare the elder, whose first wife had died and left him a daughter, married a second late in life.", "아버지 클레어 씨는 첫 아내가 죽으며 딸 하나를 남기자, 늘그막에 재혼했다."], ["This lady had somewhat unexpectedly brought him three sons, so that between Angel, the youngest, and his father the Vicar there seemed to be almost a missing generation.", "이 부인은 다소 뜻밖에도 그에게 아들 셋을 안겨 주었고, 그래서 막내인 엔젤과 그의 아버지인 교구 목사 사이에는 거의 한 세대가 빠진 듯이 보였다."], ["Of these boys the aforesaid Angel, the child of his old age, was the only son who had not taken a University degree, though he was the single one of them whose early promise might have done full justice to an academical training.", "이 사내아이들 가운데 앞서 말한 엔젤, 곧 그가 늘그막에 본 자식은, 학구적 훈련을 충분히 빛낼 만한 어릴 적 자질을 지닌 유일한 아들이었음에도, 대학 학위를 따지 않은 유일한 아들이었다."]], [["Some two or three years before Angel’s appearance at the Marlott dance, on a day when he had left school and was pursuing his studies at home, a parcel came to the Vicarage from the local bookseller’s, directed to the Reverend James Clare.", "엔젤이 말롯의 춤판에 나타나기 두세 해쯤 전, 그가 학교를 마치고 집에서 학업을 이어 가던 어느 날, 동네 서점에서 제임스 클레어 목사 앞으로 소포 하나가 목사관에 도착했다."], ["The Vicar having opened it and found it to contain a book, read a few pages; whereupon he jumped up from his seat and went straight to the shop with the book under his arm.", "목사가 그것을 뜯어 책 한 권이 든 것을 발견하고 몇 쪽을 읽더니, 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그 책을 옆구리에 끼고 곧장 가게로 갔다."]], [["“Why has this been sent to my house?”", "\"왜 이게 우리 집으로 왔소?\""], ["he asked peremptorily, holding up the volume.", "그가 책을 치켜들며 단호히 물었다."]], [["“It was ordered, sir.”", "\"주문이 들어온 겁니다, 손님.\""]], [["“Not by me, or any one belonging to me, I am happy to say.”", "\"내가 한 것도 아니고, 우리 집안 누가 한 것도 아니오. 다행스럽게도 말이오.\""]], [["The shopkeeper looked into his order-book.", "가게 주인이 주문 장부를 들여다보았다."]], [["“Oh, it has been misdirected, sir,”", "\"오, 잘못 배달된 겁니다, 손님.\""], ["he said. “It was ordered by Mr Angel Clare, and should have been sent to him.”", "그가 말했다. \"엔젤 클레어 씨가 주문하신 거라, 그분께 보냈어야 했군요.\""]], [["Mr Clare winced as if he had been struck.", "클레어 씨는 한 대 얻어맞은 듯 움찔했다."], ["He went home pale and dejected, and called Angel into his study.", "그는 창백하고 풀이 죽어 집으로 돌아와, 엔젤을 서재로 불렀다."]], [["“Look into this book, my boy,”", "\"이 책을 좀 들여다봐라, 얘야.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“What do you know about it?”", "\"이게 어떤 책인지 너는 아느냐?\""]], [["“I ordered it,”", "\"제가 주문했어요.\""], ["said Angel simply.", "엔젤이 담담하게 말했다."]], [["“What for?”", "\"무엇 하러?\""]], [["“To read.”", "\"읽으려고요.\""]], [["“How can you think of reading it?”", "\"어찌 그런 걸 읽을 생각을 한단 말이냐?\""]], [["“How can I?", "\"어찌라뇨?"], ["Why—it is a system of philosophy. There is no more moral, or even religious, work published.”", "그건—철학 체계인걸요. 이보다 더 도덕적인, 아니 종교적이기까지 한 저작은 출간된 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Yes—moral enough; I don’t deny that.", "\"그래—도덕적이긴 하겠지, 그건 부정 않겠다."], ["But religious!—and for _you_, who intend to be a minister of the Gospel!”", "하지만 종교적이라니!—게다가 복음의 사역자가 되려는 너에게 말이다!\""]], [["“Since you have alluded to the matter, father,”", "\"아버지께서 그 이야기를 꺼내셨으니,\""], ["said the son, with anxious thought upon his face, “I should like to say, once for all, that I should prefer not to take Orders.", "아들이 얼굴에 근심 어린 생각을 띠며 말했다. \"이참에 한 번에 말씀드리고 싶어요. 저는 성직을 받지 않는 편이 좋겠어요."], ["I fear I could not conscientiously do so.", "양심상 그럴 수 없을 것 같아요."], ["I love the Church as one loves a parent.", "저는 교회를 부모를 사랑하듯 사랑해요."], ["I shall always have the warmest affection for her.", "앞으로도 늘 교회에 더없이 따뜻한 애정을 품을 거예요."], ["There is no institution for whose history I have a deeper admiration; but I cannot honestly be ordained her minister, as my brothers are, while she refuses to liberate her mind from an untenable redemptive theolatry.”", "제가 그 역사를 이보다 더 깊이 흠모하는 제도는 없어요. 하지만 교회가 지탱될 수 없는 구속(救贖)의 우상 신학에서 자기 정신을 풀어놓기를 거부하는 한, 저는 형들처럼 정직하게 교회의 사역자로 안수받을 수가 없어요.\""]], [["It had never occurred to the straightforward and simple-minded Vicar that one of his own flesh and blood could come to this! He was stultified, shocked, paralysed.", "이 곧고 단순한 마음의 교구 목사는, 제 살붙이 가운데 하나가 이 지경에 이르리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없었다! 그는 어안이 벙벙해지고, 충격을 받고, 마비되었다."], ["And if Angel were not going to enter the Church, what was the use of sending him to Cambridge?", "게다가 엔젤이 교회에 들어가지 않을 거라면, 케임브리지에 보내 봐야 무슨 소용이란 말인가?"], ["The University as a step to anything but ordination seemed, to this man of fixed ideas, a preface without a volume.", "정해진 관념에 사로잡힌 이 사내에게는, 안수가 아닌 다른 무언가로 가는 발판으로서의 대학이란, 본문 없는 서문처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He was a man not merely religious, but devout; a firm believer—not as the phrase is now elusively construed by theological thimble-riggers in the Church and out of it, but in the old and ardent sense of the Evangelical school: one who could", "그는 단지 종교적인 데 그치지 않고 독실한 사람이었다. 확고한 신자였으되—요즘 교회 안팎의 신학적 야바위꾼들이 얼버무리며 풀이하는 식이 아니라, 복음주의 학파의 옛 열렬한 의미로 그러했다. 그는 능히"]], [["Indeed opine That the Eternal and Divine Did, eighteen centuries ago In very truth...", "참으로 믿을 수 있었으니 영원하시고 거룩하신 분이 십팔 세기 전에 참으로……"]], [["Angel’s father tried argument, persuasion, entreaty.", "엔젤의 아버지는 논증으로, 설득으로, 간청으로 애썼다."]], [["“No, father; I cannot underwrite Article Four (leave alone the rest), taking it ‘in the literal and grammatical sense’ as required by the Declaration; and, therefore, I can’t be a parson in the present state of affairs,”", "\"아니에요, 아버지. 저는 신앙고백서가 요구하는 대로 '문자 그대로의, 문법적인 의미로' 받아들여 제4조를 인정할 수가 없어요(나머지는 말할 것도 없고요)."], ["said Angel.", "그러니 지금 형편으로는 목사가 될 수 없어요.\""], ["“My whole instinct in matters of religion is towards reconstruction; to quote your favorite Epistle to the Hebrews, ‘the removing of those things that are shaken, as of things that are made, that those things which cannot be shaken may remain.’”", "엔젤이 말했다. \"종교 문제에서 제 본능은 온통 재건을 향해 있어요. 아버지께서 좋아하시는 「히브리서」를 인용하자면, '흔들리는 것들은 만들어진 것이므로 제거하사, 흔들리지 않는 것들이 남게 하려 하심이라.'\""]], [["His father grieved so deeply that it made Angel quite ill to see him.", "그의 아버지가 어찌나 깊이 슬퍼하던지, 엔젤은 그 모습을 보는 것만으로도 속이 다 아팠다."]], [["“What is the good of your mother and me economizing and stinting ourselves to give you a University education, if it is not to be used for the honour and glory of God?” his father repeated.", "\"네 어머니와 내가 아껴 쓰고 절약해 가며 너를 대학에 보내 가르치는 게, 그게 하느님의 영예와 영광에 쓰이지 않을 거라면 무슨 소용이란 말이냐?\" 그의 아버지가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Why, that it may be used for the honour and glory of man, father.”", "\"아니, 그건 인간의 영예와 영광에 쓰일 수 있잖아요, 아버지.\""]], [["Perhaps if Angel had persevered he might have gone to Cambridge like his brothers.", "어쩌면 엔젤이 고집을 부렸다면 형들처럼 케임브리지에 갈 수 있었을지도 모른다."], ["But the Vicar’s view of that seat of learning as a stepping-stone to Orders alone was quite a family tradition; and so rooted was the idea in his mind that perseverance began to appear to the sensitive son akin to an intent to misappropriate a trust, and wrong the pious heads of the household, who had been and were, as his father had hinted, compelled to exercise much thrift to carry out this uniform plan of education for the three young men.", "하지만 그 배움의 전당을 오직 성직으로 가는 디딤돌로만 보는 교구 목사의 견해는 가문의 전통이나 다름없었다. 그리고 그 관념이 아버지의 마음에 어찌나 깊이 뿌리박혀 있던지, 감수성 예민한 아들에게는 고집을 부리는 것이 마치 신탁을 횡령하려는 의도처럼, 또 집안의 경건한 어른들을 욕보이는 일처럼 보이기 시작했다. 아버지가 넌지시 비쳤듯이, 그분들은 세 아들에게 한결같은 교육 방침을 실천하느라 큰 절약을 해 왔고 또 하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“I will do without Cambridge,”", "\"케임브리지는 안 가도 돼요.\""], ["said Angel at last.", "마침내 엔젤이 말했다."], ["“I feel that I have no right to go there in the circumstances.”", "\"이런 형편에 거길 갈 자격이 제겐 없다고 느껴요.\""]], [["The effects of this decisive debate were not long in showing themselves.", "이 결정적인 논쟁의 결과는 머지않아 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He spent years and years in desultory studies, undertakings, and meditations; he began to evince considerable indifference to social forms and observances.", "그는 두서없는 공부와 사업과 사색으로 여러 해를 보냈다. 사회적 형식과 관례에 상당한 무관심을 드러내기 시작했다."], ["The material distinctions of rank and wealth he increasingly despised.", "지위와 부의 물질적 구별을 점점 더 경멸했다."], ["Even the “good old family” (to use a favourite phrase of a late local worthy) had no aroma for him unless there were good new resolutions in its representatives.", "심지어 \"유서 깊은 명문가\"라는 것조차(이 고장의 한 작고한 명사가 즐겨 쓰던 말투를 빌리자면) 그 후손들에게 훌륭한 새 각오가 없는 한 그에게는 아무 향기도 없었다."], ["As a balance to these austerities, when he went to live in London to see what the world was like, and with a view to practising a profession or business there, he was carried off his head, and nearly entrapped by a woman much older than himself, though luckily he escaped not greatly the worse for the experience.", "이러한 금욕에 균형을 맞추려는 듯, 세상이 어떤 곳인지 보려고, 또 거기서 직업이나 사업을 해 볼 생각으로 런던에 가서 살았을 때, 그는 정신을 잃고 자기보다 훨씬 나이 많은 여자에게 거의 옭아매일 뻔했다. 다행히 그 경험으로 크게 상하지 않고 빠져나오긴 했지만."]], [["Early association with country solitudes had bred in him an unconquerable, and almost unreasonable, aversion to modern town life, and shut him out from such success as he might have aspired to by following a mundane calling in the impracticability of the spiritual one.", "어릴 적 시골의 고적함과 친했던 탓에 그에게는 현대 도시 생활에 대한, 이겨 낼 수 없고 거의 분별없을 지경의 혐오가 자라났고, 그것은 영적인 소명을 따르기가 불가능해진 마당에 세속적인 직업을 좇아 그가 바랐을 법한 성공으로부터도 그를 가로막았다."], ["But something had to be done; he had wasted many valuable years; and having an acquaintance who was starting on a thriving life as a Colonial farmer, it occurred to Angel that this might be a lead in the right direction.", "하지만 무언가는 해야 했다. 그는 귀중한 세월을 숱하게 허비했다. 그리고 식민지 농부로 번창하는 삶을 막 시작한 지인이 하나 있던 터라, 엔젤은 이것이 어쩌면 올바른 방향으로 가는 길잡이일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Farming, either in the Colonies, America, or at home—farming, at any rate, after becoming well qualified for the business by a careful apprenticeship—that was a vocation which would probably afford an independence without the sacrifice of what he valued even more than a competency—intellectual liberty.", "농사—식민지에서든, 미국에서든, 본국에서든—어쨌든 세심한 견습을 거쳐 그 일에 충분히 능숙해진 뒤의 농사야말로, 그가 생계 능력보다도 더 귀히 여기는 것, 곧 지적 자유를 희생하지 않고도 자립을 안겨 줄 만한 천직이었다."]], [["So we find Angel Clare at six-and-twenty here at Talbothays as a student of kine, and, as there were no houses near at hand in which he could get a comfortable lodging, a boarder at the dairyman’s.", "그리하여 우리는 스물여섯 살의 엔젤 클레어가 이곳 탤보세이스에서 소를 배우는 학생으로, 그리고 가까이에 편히 묵을 집이 없는 터라 낙농가의 하숙인으로 있는 것을 보게 된다."]], [["His room was an immense attic which ran the whole length of the dairy-house.", "그의 방은 낙농장 집의 전체 길이만큼 뻗은 거대한 다락이었다."], ["It could only be reached by a ladder from the cheese-loft, and had been closed up for a long time till he arrived and selected it as his retreat.", "치즈 다락에서 사다리로만 올라갈 수 있었고, 오랫동안 닫혀 있다가 그가 와서 자기 은신처로 고른 방이었다."], ["Here Clare had plenty of space, and could often be heard by the dairy-folk pacing up and down when the household had gone to rest.", "여기서 클레어는 공간이 넉넉했고, 식구들이 잠자리에 든 뒤 그가 이리저리 거니는 소리가 낙농장 사람들에게 곧잘 들리곤 했다."], ["A portion was divided off at one end by a curtain, behind which was his bed, the outer part being furnished as a homely sitting-room.", "한쪽 끝에는 휘장으로 한 칸이 막혀 있었고, 그 뒤에는 그의 침대가 있었으며, 바깥쪽은 수수한 거실로 꾸며져 있었다."]], [["At first he lived up above entirely, reading a good deal, and strumming upon an old harp which he had bought at a sale, saying when in a bitter humour that he might have to get his living by it in the streets some day.", "처음에 그는 줄곧 위층에서 지내며 책을 꽤 읽고, 경매에서 산 낡은 하프를 퉁기곤 했다. 심사가 사나울 때면 언젠가 이걸로 길거리에서 밥벌이를 해야 할지도 모르겠다고 말하기도 했다."], ["But he soon preferred to read human nature by taking his meals downstairs in the general dining-kitchen, with the dairyman and his wife, and the maids and men, who all together formed a lively assembly; for though but few milking hands slept in the house, several joined the family at meals.", "하지만 그는 곧, 낙농가 부부와 처녀들과 사내들이 다 함께 어울려 활기찬 모임을 이루는 공용 식당 부엌에서 아래층 식사를 하며 인간 본성을 읽는 편을 더 좋아하게 되었다. 집 안에서 자는 젖꾼은 몇 안 되었지만, 여럿이 식사 때면 식구들과 합류했기 때문이다."], ["The longer Clare resided here the less objection had he to his company, and the more did he like to share quarters with them in common.", "클레어는 이곳에 오래 머물수록 그 무리가 덜 거슬렸고, 그들과 한자리를 함께 나누는 것이 점점 더 좋아졌다."]], [["Much to his surprise he took, indeed, a real delight in their companionship.", "놀랍게도 그는 정말로 그들과의 어울림에서 참된 기쁨을 누렸다."], ["The conventional farm-folk of his imagination—personified in the newspaper-press by the pitiable dummy known as Hodge—were obliterated after a few days’ residence.", "그의 상상 속 판에 박힌 농촌 사람들—신문 지면에서 호지라는 가련한 허수아비로 인격화되던 그 형상—은 며칠 머무는 사이 지워져 버렸다."], ["At close quarters no Hodge was to be seen.", "가까이서 보니 호지 따위는 없었다."], ["At first, it is true, when Clare’s intelligence was fresh from a contrasting society, these friends with whom he now hobnobbed seemed a little strange.", "사실 처음에는, 대조적인 사회에서 갓 빠져나온 클레어의 지성에 그가 이제 허물없이 어울리는 이 벗들이 좀 낯설어 보이기는 했다."], ["Sitting down as a level member of the dairyman’s household seemed at the outset an undignified proceeding.", "낙농가네 식구들과 대등한 일원으로 자리에 앉는 것이 처음에는 체면 깎이는 일처럼 여겨졌다."], ["The ideas, the modes, the surroundings, appeared retrogressive and unmeaning.", "그 관념도, 방식도, 주변 환경도 퇴보적이고 무의미해 보였다."], ["But with living on there, day after day, the acute sojourner became conscious of a new aspect in the spectacle.", "하지만 거기서 날마다 살아가면서, 예리한 이 체류자는 그 광경에서 새로운 면모를 알아차리게 되었다."], ["Without any objective change whatever, variety had taken the place of monotonousness.", "객관적인 변화는 전혀 없었는데도, 단조로움 자리에 다양함이 들어선 것이다."], ["His host and his host’s household, his men and his maids, as they became intimately known to Clare, began to differentiate themselves as in a chemical process.", "그의 주인과 주인의 집안 사람들, 사내들과 처녀들이, 클레어에게 친밀히 알려지면서 마치 화학 변화처럼 저마다 분화되기 시작했다."], ["The thought of Pascal’s was brought home to him: “_A mesure qu’on a plus d’esprit, on trouve qu’il y a plus d’hommes originaux.", "파스칼의 한 마디가 그에게 절절히 와닿았다. \"지성이 높아질수록, 독창적인 사람들이 더 많이 보인다."], ["Les gens du commun ne trouvent pas de différence entre les hommes_.”", "평범한 자들은 사람과 사람 사이의 차이를 알아보지 못한다.\""], ["The typical and unvarying Hodge ceased to exist.", "전형적이고 한결같던 호지는 더는 존재하지 않았다."], ["He had been disintegrated into a number of varied fellow-creatures—beings of many minds, beings infinite in difference; some happy, many serene, a few depressed, one here and there bright even to genius, some stupid, others wanton, others austere; some mutely Miltonic, some potentially Cromwellian—into men who had private views of each other, as he had of his friends; who could applaud or condemn each other, amuse or sadden themselves by the contemplation of each other’s foibles or vices; men every one of whom walked in his own individual way the road to dusty death.", "그는 갖가지로 제각각인 동료 인간 여럿으로 해체되었다—마음씨가 가지각색이고, 그 차이가 무한한 존재들로. 더러는 행복하고, 다수는 평온하며, 몇몇은 풀이 죽었고, 여기저기 천재라 할 만큼 빛나는 이가 있는가 하면, 더러는 미련하고, 더러는 방종하고, 더러는 엄격했다. 더러는 말없는 밀턴 같았고, 더러는 잠재된 크롬웰 같았다—그가 제 친구들을 두고 그러듯 서로에 대해 나름의 견해를 품고, 서로를 칭찬하거나 책망할 수 있고, 서로의 약점이나 악덕을 바라보며 즐거워하거나 서글퍼할 수 있는 사람들로, 저마다 제 나름의 길을 걸어 흙먼지 어린 죽음으로 향하는 사람들로."]], [["Unexpectedly he began to like the outdoor life for its own sake, and for what it brought, apart from its bearing on his own proposed career.", "뜻밖에도 그는 야외 생활을, 그 자체로, 또 그것이 가져다주는 것 때문에, 제 앞날의 행로와는 별개로 좋아하기 시작했다."], ["Considering his position he became wonderfully free from the chronic melancholy which is taking hold of the civilized races with the decline of belief in a beneficent Power.", "그의 처지를 생각하면, 그는 자비로운 권능에 대한 믿음이 스러지면서 문명화된 종족들을 사로잡고 있는 그 만성적 우울로부터 놀랄 만큼 자유로워졌다."], ["For the first time of late years he could read as his musings inclined him, without any eye to cramming for a profession, since the few farming handbooks which he deemed it desirable to master occupied him but little time.", "근래 몇 해 만에 처음으로 그는, 직업을 위해 벼락치기 공부를 할 요량 따위는 없이, 사색이 이끄는 대로 책을 읽을 수 있었다. 익혀 둘 만하다고 여긴 몇 권 안 되는 농사 안내서는 그의 시간을 거의 잡아먹지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["He grew away from old associations, and saw something new in life and humanity.", "그는 옛 인연에서 멀어졌고, 삶과 인간 속에서 무언가 새로운 것을 보았다."], ["Secondarily, he made close acquaintance with phenomena which he had before known but darkly—the seasons in their moods, morning and evening, night and noon, winds in their different tempers, trees, waters and mists, shades and silences, and the voices of inanimate things.", "부차적으로는, 전에는 어렴풋이만 알던 현상들과 가까이 사귀게 되었다—저마다의 기분을 띤 계절들, 아침과 저녁, 밤과 한낮, 제각기 다른 성미의 바람들, 나무와 물과 안개, 그늘과 침묵, 그리고 무생물들의 목소리를."]], [["The early mornings were still sufficiently cool to render a fire acceptable in the large room wherein they breakfasted; and, by Mrs Crick’s orders, who held that he was too genteel to mess at their table, it was Angel Clare’s custom to sit in the yawning chimney-corner during the meal, his cup-and-saucer and plate being placed on a hinged flap at his elbow.", "이른 아침은 아직 충분히 쌀쌀해서, 그들이 아침을 먹는 넓은 방에서는 불기운이 반가웠다. 그리고 크릭 부인의 분부에 따라—그녀는 그가 너무 점잖아서 자기네 식탁에서 함께 먹기는 어렵다고 여겼다—식사 동안 엔젤 클레어는 입을 떡 벌린 굴뚝 모퉁이에 앉는 것이 관례였고, 그의 찻잔과 받침과 접시는 팔꿈치 옆 경첩 달린 받침판 위에 놓였다."], ["The light from the long, wide, mullioned window opposite shone in upon his nook, and, assisted by a secondary light of cold blue quality which shone down the chimney, enabled him to read there easily whenever disposed to do so.", "맞은편 길고 넓은 중간설주 창에서 들어오는 빛이 그의 구석을 비추었고, 굴뚝을 타고 내려오는 차고 푸른 빛깔의 보조 광선까지 더해져, 그가 마음 내킬 때면 거기서 책을 수월하게 읽을 수 있었다."], ["Between Clare and the window was the table at which his companions sat, their munching profiles rising sharp against the panes; while to the side was the milk-house door, through which were visible the rectangular leads in rows, full to the brim with the morning’s milk.", "클레어와 창 사이에는 그의 동료들이 둘러앉은 식탁이 있었고, 우물우물 씹는 그들의 옆얼굴이 유리창을 배경으로 또렷이 솟아 있었다. 한쪽으로는 우유 저장실 문이 있어, 그 너머로 줄지어 놓인 직사각형 통들이 아침에 짠 우유로 가득 차 찰랑이는 것이 보였다."], ["At the further end the great churn could be seen revolving, and its slip-slopping heard—the moving power being discernible through the window in the form of a spiritless horse walking in a circle and driven by a boy.", "저 끝에서는 커다란 교유기가 돌아가는 게 보였고, 그 철벅철벅 소리가 들렸다—그 동력은, 사내아이가 모는 가운데 원을 그리며 도는 기운 없는 말의 모습으로 창 너머에 보였다."]], [["For several days after Tess’s arrival Clare, sitting abstractedly reading from some book, periodical, or piece of music just come by post, hardly noticed that she was present at table.", "테스가 온 뒤 며칠 동안, 클레어는 우편으로 막 도착한 책이나 정기간행물이나 악보를 멍하니 읽느라, 식탁에 그녀가 있다는 것을 거의 알아채지 못했다."], ["She talked so little, and the other maids talked so much, that the babble did not strike him as possessing a new note, and he was ever in the habit of neglecting the particulars of an outward scene for the general impression.", "그녀는 말수가 워낙 적고 다른 처녀들은 워낙 떠들어 대서, 그 재잘거림이 그에게 새로운 가락으로 와닿지 않았다. 게다가 그는 늘 바깥 풍경의 세세한 부분은 무시하고 전체 인상만 받아들이는 버릇이 있었다."], ["One day, however, when he had been conning one of his music-scores, and by force of imagination was hearing the tune in his head, he lapsed into listlessness, and the music-sheet rolled to the hearth.", "하지만 어느 날, 그가 악보 하나를 들여다보며 상상의 힘으로 머릿속에서 그 가락을 듣고 있다가 무기력하게 빠져들자, 악보가 화로 쪽으로 또르르 굴러떨어졌다. 그는 통나무 불을 바라보았다."], ["He looked at the fire of logs, with its one flame pirouetting on the top in a dying dance after the breakfast-cooking and boiling, and it seemed to jig to his inward tune; also at the two chimney crooks dangling down from the cotterel, or cross-bar, plumed with soot, which quivered to the same melody; also at the half-empty kettle whining an accompaniment.", "아침 요리와 끓이기가 끝난 뒤 그 꼭대기에서 한 가닥 불꽃이 사위어 가는 춤을 추듯 빙글거리며, 그의 마음속 가락에 맞춰 지그를 추는 듯했다. 또 가로대, 곧 십자 막대에 매달린 두 개의 굴뚝 갈고리에는 검댕이 깃털처럼 앉아 있었는데, 그것도 같은 가락에 떨리는 듯했다. 또 반쯤 빈 주전자가 끙끙대며 반주를 넣는 것 같았다."], ["The conversation at the table mixed in with his phantasmal orchestra till he thought: “What a fluty voice one of those milkmaids has!", "식탁의 대화가 그의 환상의 관현악과 뒤섞이더니, 그는 문득 이런 생각이 들었다. \"저 젖 짜는 처녀들 가운데 하나는 목소리가 어쩜 저리 피리 소리 같담!"], ["I suppose it is the new one.”", "새로 온 처녀인가 보군.\""]], [["Clare looked round upon her, seated with the others.", "클레어는 다른 처녀들과 함께 앉아 있는 그녀에게로 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["She was not looking towards him.", "그녀는 그를 보고 있지 않았다."], ["Indeed, owing to his long silence, his presence in the room was almost forgotten.", "사실 그가 오래도록 입을 다물고 있던 터라, 그가 방에 있다는 것은 거의 잊힌 상태였다."]], [["“I don’t know about ghosts,”", "\"유령에 대해선 저도 잘 몰라요.\""], ["she was saying; “but I do know that our souls can be made to go outside our bodies when we are alive.”", "그녀가 말하고 있었다. \"하지만 우리가 살아 있는 동안에도 우리 영혼이 몸 밖으로 나가게 만들 수 있다는 건 알아요.\""]], [["The dairyman turned to her with his mouth full, his eyes charged with serious inquiry, and his great knife and fork (breakfasts were breakfasts here) planted erect on the table, like the beginning of a gallows.", "낙농가가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 그녀에게로 돌아섰다. 그의 눈에는 진지한 호기심이 가득했고, 그의 큼지막한 나이프와 포크는(여기서 아침 식사는 진짜 아침 식사였다) 마치 교수대의 첫머리처럼 식탁 위에 곧추 꽂혀 있었다."]], [["“What—really now?", "\"뭐라고—정말로?"], ["And is it so, maidy?”", "그게 사실인가, 아가씨?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“A very easy way to feel ’em go,”", "\"영혼이 나가는 걸 느끼는 아주 쉬운 방법은요,\" 테스가 이어 갔다."], ["continued Tess, “is to lie on the grass at night and look straight up at some big bright star; and, by fixing your mind upon it, you will soon find that you are hundreds and hundreds o’ miles away from your body, which you don’t seem to want at all.”", "\"밤에 풀밭에 누워서 크고 밝은 별 하나를 똑바로 올려다보는 거예요. 그러고 거기에 마음을 붙박고 있으면, 곧 자기가 몸에서 수백, 수백 마일이나 떨어져 있다는 걸 알게 돼요. 그 몸뚱이는 도무지 필요 없는 것처럼 느껴지고요.\""]], [["The dairyman removed his hard gaze from Tess, and fixed it on his wife.", "낙농가는 테스에게서 그 빤한 시선을 거두어 제 아내에게로 못 박았다."]], [["“Now that’s a rum thing, Christianer—hey?", "\"거참 희한한 일이로군, 크리스티애너—응?"], ["To think o’ the miles I’ve vamped o’ starlight nights these last thirty year, courting, or trading, or for doctor, or for nurse, and yet never had the least notion o’ that till now, or feeled my soul rise so much as an inch above my shirt-collar.”", "지난 삼십 년간 별빛 비치는 밤에 구애하러, 장사하러, 의사 부르러, 산파 부르러 그렇게 숱한 마일을 터덜터덜 걸었으면서도, 이제껏 그런 건 전혀 생각도 못 했고, 내 영혼이 셔츠 깃 위로 단 한 치도 떠오르는 걸 느껴 본 적도 없으니 말이야.\""]], [["The general attention being drawn to her, including that of the dairyman’s pupil, Tess flushed, and remarking evasively that it was only a fancy, resumed her breakfast.", "낙농가의 견습생까지 포함해 모두의 주의가 자기에게 쏠리자, 테스는 얼굴을 붉히고, 그저 공상일 뿐이라고 얼버무리며 다시 아침 식사를 들었다."]], [["Clare continued to observe her.", "클레어는 계속 그녀를 살폈다."], ["She soon finished her eating, and having a consciousness that Clare was regarding her, began to trace imaginary patterns on the tablecloth with her forefinger with the constraint of a domestic animal that perceives itself to be watched.", "그녀는 곧 식사를 마쳤고, 클레어가 자기를 바라보고 있다는 의식이 들자, 누가 보고 있다는 걸 알아챈 집짐승처럼 어색해하며 집게손가락으로 식탁보에 가상의 무늬를 그리기 시작했다."]], [["“What a fresh and virginal daughter of Nature that milkmaid is!”", "\"저 젖 짜는 처녀는 어쩌면 저리 싱그럽고 순결한 자연의 딸인가!\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 속으로 말했다."]], [["And then he seemed to discern in her something that was familiar, something which carried him back into a joyous and unforeseeing past, before the necessity of taking thought had made the heavens gray.", "그러다 그는 그녀에게서 무언가 낯익은 것을, 곧 깊이 생각해야 할 필요가 하늘을 잿빛으로 물들이기 전의, 즐겁고 앞날을 걱정 없던 과거로 그를 데려가는 무언가를 알아보는 듯했다."], ["He concluded that he had beheld her before; where he could not tell.", "그는 그녀를 전에 본 적이 있다고 결론지었다. 어디서였는지는 알 수 없었다."], ["A casual encounter during some country ramble it certainly had been, and he was not greatly curious about it.", "어느 시골 산책길에서 우연히 마주친 것임은 분명했고, 그는 그 일에 그리 큰 호기심을 품지는 않았다."], ["But the circumstance was sufficient to lead him to select Tess in preference to the other pretty milkmaids when he wished to contemplate contiguous womankind.", "하지만 그 정황만으로도, 곁에 있는 여인을 바라보고 싶을 때면 다른 예쁜 젖 짜는 처녀들보다 테스를 골라 보게 되기에 충분했다."]], [["# XIX", "# XIX"]], [["In general the cows were milked as they presented themselves, without fancy or choice.", "XIX\n대개 소들은 나타나는 대로, 호불호나 선택 없이 젖을 짰다."], ["But certain cows will show a fondness for a particular pair of hands, sometimes carrying this predilection so far as to refuse to stand at all except to their favourite, the pail of a stranger being unceremoniously kicked over.", "하지만 어떤 소들은 특정한 한 쌍의 손을 좋아하는 기색을 보였고, 그 편애가 때로는 어찌나 심한지 제가 좋아하는 사람한테가 아니면 아예 서 있으려고도 하지 않아서, 낯선 이의 들통은 사정없이 걷어차여 엎어지곤 했다."]], [["It was Dairyman Crick’s rule to insist on breaking down these partialities and aversions by constant interchange, since otherwise, in the event of a milkman or maid going away from the dairy, he was placed in a difficulty.", "낙농가 크릭의 원칙은, 끊임없이 소들을 바꿔 가며 이런 편애와 반감을 깨뜨려야 한다고 고집하는 것이었다. 그러지 않으면 젖꾼 사내나 처녀 하나가 낙농장을 떠날 경우 그가 곤란해지기 때문이었다."], ["The maids’ private aims, however, were the reverse of the dairyman’s rule, the daily selection by each damsel of the eight or ten cows to which she had grown accustomed rendering the operation on their willing udders surprisingly easy and effortless.", "하지만 처녀들의 속셈은 낙농가의 원칙과는 정반대였다. 처녀마다 제 손에 익은 소 여덟에서 열 마리를 날마다 골라 잡으면, 그 순한 젖통을 다루는 일이 놀랄 만큼 수월하고 힘들지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Tess, like her compeers, soon discovered which of the cows had a preference for her style of manipulation, and her fingers having become delicate from the long domiciliary imprisonments to which she had subjected herself at intervals during the last two or three years, she would have been glad to meet the milchers’ views in this respect.", "테스도 동료들처럼, 어떤 소들이 제 손놀림을 좋아하는지 곧 알아냈다. 게다가 지난 두세 해 동안 틈틈이 스스로를 가두었던 그 긴 칩거 생활로 손가락이 섬약해진 터라, 그녀는 이 점에서 젖소들의 뜻을 기꺼이 맞추고 싶었다."], ["Out of the whole ninety-five there were eight in particular—Dumpling, Fancy, Lofty, Mist, Old Pretty, Young Pretty, Tidy, and Loud—who, though the teats of one or two were as hard as carrots, gave down to her with a readiness that made her work on them a mere touch of the fingers.", "아흔다섯 마리 전체 가운데 특히 여덟 마리—덤플링, 팬시, 로프티, 미스트, 올드 프리티, 영 프리티, 타이디, 라우드—는, 비록 한두 마리는 젖꼭지가 당근처럼 단단하긴 했어도, 그녀에게는 흔쾌히 젖을 내주어서 그 일이 손가락만 살짝 대는 정도에 그쳤다."], ["Knowing, however, the dairyman’s wish, she endeavoured conscientiously to take the animals just as they came, excepting the very hard yielders which she could not yet manage.", "하지만 낙농가의 바람을 아는 터라, 그녀는 아직 다룰 수 없는 아주 빡빡한 놈들만 빼고는 나오는 대로 양심껏 소들을 맡으려 애썼다."]], [["But she soon found a curious correspondence between the ostensibly chance position of the cows and her wishes in this matter, till she felt that their order could not be the result of accident.", "그런데 그녀는 곧, 겉으로는 우연인 듯한 소들의 위치와 이 문제에 관한 자기 바람 사이에 묘한 일치가 있음을 알아챘고, 마침내 그 배열이 우연의 결과일 리 없다고 느꼈다."], ["The dairyman’s pupil had lent a hand in getting the cows together of late, and at the fifth or sixth time she turned her eyes, as she rested against the cow, full of sly inquiry upon him.", "낙농가의 견습생이 요즘 소들을 모아들이는 일을 거들어 왔던 것이다. 그래서 대여섯 번째쯤 되었을 때, 그녀는 소에 기대 쉬다가 짓궂은 의심이 가득한 눈길을 그에게로 돌렸다."]], [["“Mr Clare, you have ranged the cows!”", "\"클레어 씨, 소들을 줄 세우셨군요!\""], ["she said, blushing; and in making the accusation, symptoms of a smile gently lifted her upper lip in spite of her, so as to show the tips of her teeth, the lower lip remaining severely still.", "그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 그렇게 따지면서도, 자기도 모르게 미소의 기미가 윗입술을 살며시 들어 올려 이 끝이 드러났고, 아랫입술은 짐짓 엄하게 가만히 있었다."]], [["“Well, it makes no difference,”", "\"뭐, 상관없어요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “You will always be here to milk them.”", "\"당신은 언제까지나 여기서 그 소들 젖을 짤 테니까.\""]], [["“Do you think so?", "\"그렇게 생각하세요?"], ["I _hope_ I shall!", "저도 그러길 바라요!"], ["But I don’t _know_.”", "하지만 모르는 일이죠.\""]], [["She was angry with herself afterwards, thinking that he, unaware of her grave reasons for liking this seclusion, might have mistaken her meaning.", "그녀는 나중에 자기 자신에게 화가 났다. 그가, 이 은둔을 좋아하는 자신의 무거운 까닭을 알지 못한 채, 자기 말뜻을 오해했을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서였다."], ["She had spoken so earnestly to him, as if his presence were somehow a factor in her wish. Her misgiving was such that at dusk, when the milking was over, she walked in the garden alone, to continue her regrets that she had disclosed to him her discovery of his considerateness.", "마치 그가 곁에 있는 것이 어쩐지 자기 바람의 한 요소이기라도 한 양, 그녀가 그에게 너무 간곡히 말했던 것이다. 그 불안이 어찌나 컸던지, 해 질 녘 젖 짜기가 끝나자 그녀는 정원을 홀로 거닐며, 그의 사려 깊음을 알아챘다고 그에게 털어놓은 것을 두고두고 후회했다."]], [["It was a typical summer evening in June, the atmosphere being in such delicate equilibrium and so transmissive that inanimate objects seemed endowed with two or three senses, if not five.", "유월의 전형적인 여름 저녁이었다. 대기가 어찌나 섬세한 평형을 이루고 어찌나 잘 전해 주는지, 무생물조차 다섯까지는 아니어도 두세 가지 감각을 지닌 듯했다."], ["There was no distinction between the near and the far, and an auditor felt close to everything within the horizon.", "가까운 것과 먼 것 사이에 구별이 없었고, 듣는 이는 지평선 안의 모든 것이 바로 곁에 있는 듯 느꼈다."], ["The soundlessness impressed her as a positive entity rather than as the mere negation of noise.", "그 무성(無聲)은 그녀에게 단순한 소음의 부재라기보다 하나의 분명한 실체로 다가왔다."], ["It was broken by the strumming of strings.", "그것은 현을 퉁기는 소리에 깨졌다."]], [["Tess had heard those notes in the attic above her head.", "테스는 그 음들을 자기 머리 위 다락에서 들은 적이 있었다."], ["Dim, flattened, constrained by their confinement, they had never appealed to her as now, when they wandered in the still air with a stark quality like that of nudity.", "갇힌 채로 어둑하고 납작하게 짓눌려 있던 그 소리들이, 이제 고요한 대기 속을 헤매며 나신(裸身)과도 같은 적나라한 기운을 띠고 흐르자, 지금처럼 그녀에게 절절히 와닿은 적이 없었다."], ["To speak absolutely, both instrument and execution were poor; but the relative is all, and as she listened Tess, like a fascinated bird, could not leave the spot.", "솔직히 말하자면, 악기도 연주도 변변치 못했다. 하지만 모든 것은 상대적이었고, 귀를 기울이던 테스는 홀린 새처럼 그 자리를 떠날 수 없었다."], ["Far from leaving she drew up towards the performer, keeping behind the hedge that he might not guess her presence.", "떠나기는커녕 그녀는 연주자 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그가 제 존재를 눈치채지 못하도록 산울타리 뒤에 몸을 숨긴 채로."]], [["The outskirt of the garden in which Tess found herself had been left uncultivated for some years, and was now damp and rank with juicy grass which sent up mists of pollen at a touch; and with tall blooming weeds emitting offensive smells—weeds whose red and yellow and purple hues formed a polychrome as dazzling as that of cultivated flowers.", "테스가 와 있던 정원의 변두리는 몇 해째 가꾸지 않고 방치되어 있어서, 이제 축축하고 무성하게 즙 많은 풀이 우거져 손만 대도 꽃가루 안개를 피워 올렸다. 또 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 키 큰 잡초 꽃들이 피어 있었는데—그 붉고 노랗고 보라색인 빛깔이 가꾼 꽃들 못지않게 눈부신 색의 향연을 이루었다."], ["She went stealthily as a cat through this profusion of growth, gathering cuckoo-spittle on her skirts, cracking snails that were underfoot, staining her hands with thistle-milk and slug-slime, and rubbing off upon her naked arms sticky blights which, though snow-white on the apple-tree trunks, made madder stains on her skin; thus she drew quite near to Clare, still unobserved of him.", "그녀는 고양이처럼 살금살금 이 무성한 풀숲을 헤치고 나아갔다. 치맛자락에 거품벌레 침을 묻히고, 발밑의 달팽이를 으스러뜨리고, 엉겅퀴 즙과 민달팽이 점액으로 손을 더럽히며, 사과나무 줄기에는 눈처럼 희지만 살갗에는 꼭두서니 같은 얼룩을 남기는 끈적한 깍지진디를 맨팔에 문질러 가며. 그렇게 그녀는, 여전히 그의 눈에 띄지 않은 채 클레어 아주 가까이까지 다가갔다."]], [["Tess was conscious of neither time nor space.", "테스는 시간도 공간도 의식하지 못했다."], ["The exaltation which she had described as being producible at will by gazing at a star came now without any determination of hers; she undulated upon the thin notes of the second-hand harp, and their harmonies passed like breezes through her, bringing tears into her eyes.", "별을 응시하면 마음대로 끌어낼 수 있다고 그녀가 말했던 그 황홀이, 이제 그녀의 어떤 작정도 없이 찾아왔다. 그녀는 그 중고 하프의 가는 음들 위에 너울거렸고, 그 화음이 산들바람처럼 그녀를 통과하며 눈에 눈물을 고이게 했다."], ["The floating pollen seemed to be his notes made visible, and the dampness of the garden the weeping of the garden’s sensibility.", "떠도는 꽃가루는 그의 음들이 눈에 보이게 된 것 같았고, 정원의 축축함은 정원의 감수성이 흘리는 눈물 같았다."], ["Though near nightfall, the rank-smelling weed-flowers glowed as if they would not close for intentness, and the waves of colour mixed with the waves of sound.", "거의 밤이 가까웠는데도, 고약한 냄새를 풍기는 잡초 꽃들은 마치 골똘함에 겨워 꽃잎을 닫지 않으려는 듯 환히 빛났고, 빛깔의 물결이 소리의 물결과 뒤섞였다."]], [["The light which still shone was derived mainly from a large hole in the western bank of cloud; it was like a piece of day left behind by accident, dusk having closed in elsewhere.", "아직 비치고 있던 빛은 주로 서쪽 구름 둑에 난 커다란 구멍에서 나오는 것이었다. 다른 데는 이미 땅거미가 내린 가운데, 우연히 뒤에 남겨진 한 조각 대낮 같았다."], ["He concluded his plaintive melody, a very simple performance, demanding no great skill; and she waited, thinking another might be begun.", "그는 구슬픈 가락을 끝맺었는데, 대단한 솜씨를 요하지 않는 아주 단순한 연주였다. 그녀는 또 한 곡이 시작될까 싶어 기다렸다."], ["But, tired of playing, he had desultorily come round the fence, and was rambling up behind her.", "하지만 연주에 싫증이 난 그는 무심코 울타리를 돌아 나와, 그녀 뒤로 어슬렁어슬렁 다가오고 있었다."], ["Tess, her cheeks on fire, moved away furtively, as if hardly moving at all.", "테스는, 뺨이 화끈 달아오른 채, 거의 움직이지도 않는 듯 슬그머니 자리를 떴다."], ["Angel, however, saw her light summer gown, and he spoke; his low tones reaching her, though he was some distance off.", "하지만 엔젤은 그녀의 밝은 여름 옷을 보았고, 입을 열었다. 그가 제법 멀리 떨어져 있었는데도 그 낮은 음성이 그녀에게 와닿았다."]], [["“What makes you draw off in that way, Tess?”", "\"왜 그렇게 슬그머니 비키는 거요, 테스?\""], ["said he. “Are you afraid?”", "그가 말했다. \"겁이 나요?\""]], [["“Oh no, sir—not of outdoor things; especially just now when the apple-blooth is falling, and everything is so green.”", "\"오, 아니에요, 나리—바깥 것들이 무섭진 않아요. 특히나 사과꽃이 지고 온통 푸른 지금 같은 때는요.\""]], [["“But you have your indoor fears—eh?”", "\"하지만 집 안의 두려움은 있는 거죠—응?\""]], [["“Well—yes, sir.”", "\"음—네, 나리.\""]], [["“What of?”", "\"무엇이?\""]], [["“I couldn’t quite say.”", "\"딱히 뭐라고 말씀드릴 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“The milk turning sour?”", "\"우유가 시어지는 거?\""]], [["“No.” “Life in general?”", "\"아니에요.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"인생 전반?\""]], [["“Ah—so have I, very often.", "\"네, 나리.\" \"아—나도 그래요, 아주 자주."], ["This hobble of being alive is rather serious, don’t you think so?”", "살아 있다는 이 곤란한 처지가 꽤 심각한 일이지, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“It is—now you put it that way.”", "\"그래요—그렇게 말씀하시니 그렇네요.\""]], [["“All the same, I shouldn’t have expected a young girl like you to see it so just yet.", "\"그래도, 당신 같은 어린 처녀가 벌써 그걸 그렇게 보리라곤 생각 못 했는데."], ["How is it you do?”", "어쩌다 그러는 거요?\""]], [["She maintained a hesitating silence.", "그녀는 망설이며 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Come, Tess, tell me in confidence.”", "\"자, 테스, 나한테만 살짝 말해 봐요.\""]], [["She thought that he meant what were the aspects of things to her, and replied shyly—", "그녀는 그가 사물의 모습이 자기에게 어떻게 비치느냐고 묻는 줄 알고, 수줍게 대답했다—"]], [["“The trees have inquisitive eyes, haven’t they?—that is, seem as if they had.", "\"나무들은 캐묻는 눈을 가졌잖아요, 안 그래요?—그러니까, 가진 것처럼 보인다는 거예요. 그리고 강은 이렇게 말하죠—'왜 그런 눈초리로 나를 괴롭히느냐?'"], ["And the river says,—‘Why do ye trouble me with your looks?’ And you seem to see numbers of to-morrows just all in a line, the first of them the biggest and clearest, the others getting smaller and smaller as they stand farther away; but they all seem very fierce and cruel and as if they said, ‘I’m coming!", "그리고 수많은 내일이 죄다 한 줄로 늘어서 있는 게 보이는 것 같아요. 그중 첫째 것이 제일 크고 제일 또렷하고, 나머지는 멀찍이 설수록 점점 더 작아지고요. 하지만 죄다 어찌나 사납고 잔인한지, 마치 이렇게 말하는 것 같아요."], ["Beware of me!", "'내가 간다!"], ["Beware of me!’ ...", "나를 조심해!"], ["But _you_, sir, can raise up dreams with your music, and drive all such horrid fancies away!”", "나를 조심해!' …… 하지만 나리는, 음악으로 꿈을 불러일으켜서, 그런 끔찍한 공상을 죄다 쫓아 버릴 수 있잖아요!\""]], [["He was surprised to find this young woman—who though but a milkmaid had just that touch of rarity about her which might make her the envied of her housemates—shaping such sad imaginings.", "그는 이 젊은 여자가—한낱 젖 짜는 처녀에 불과해도 동료들의 부러움을 살 만한 그 희귀한 무언가를 지닌 그녀가—그토록 서글픈 상상을 빚어내는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["She was expressing in her own native phrases—assisted a little by her Sixth Standard training—feelings which might almost have been called those of the age—the ache of modernism.", "그녀는 제 고장 말투로—여섯째 학년 교육의 도움을 조금 받아—거의 시대의 정서라 할 만한 감정을, 곧 현대성의 통증을 토로하고 있었다."], ["The perception arrested him less when he reflected that what are called advanced ideas are really in great part but the latest fashion in definition—a more accurate expression, by words in _logy_ and _ism_, of sensations which men and women have vaguely grasped for centuries.", "그런데 이른바 진보적 관념이라는 것이 실은 대부분 정의(定義)의 최신 유행에 지나지 않는다는 것을—'~학'이나 '~주의'로 끝나는 말로, 사람들이 수백 년 동안 어렴풋이 붙잡아 온 감각들을 더 정확히 표현한 것에 지나지 않는다는 것을—떠올리자, 그 깨달음이 그를 덜 사로잡았다."]], [["Still, it was strange that they should have come to her while yet so young; more than strange; it was impressive, interesting, pathetic.", "그래도, 그것이 아직 그토록 어린 그녀에게 찾아왔다는 것은 이상한 일이었다. 이상한 정도가 아니라, 깊은 인상을 주고, 흥미롭고, 가련했다."], ["Not guessing the cause, there was nothing to remind him that experience is as to intensity, and not as to duration.", "그 원인을 짐작 못 하니, 강렬함은 경험의 길이가 아니라 강도(强度)에 달린 문제임을 그에게 일깨워 줄 만한 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["Tess’s passing corporeal blight had been her mental harvest.", "테스의 한때 스쳐 간 육신의 화는, 그녀의 정신적 수확이었던 것이다."]], [["Tess, on her part, could not understand why a man of clerical family and good education, and above physical want, should look upon it as a mishap to be alive.", "테스 쪽에서는, 성직자 집안 출신에 좋은 교육을 받고 육체적 궁핍도 모르는 사람이 어째서 살아 있는 것을 불운으로 여기는지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["For the unhappy pilgrim herself there was very good reason.", "불행한 순례자인 그녀 자신에게는 아주 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었다."], ["But how could this admirable and poetic man ever have descended into the Valley of Humiliation, have felt with the man of Uz—as she herself had felt two or three years ago—“My soul chooseth strangling and death rather than my life.", "하지만 이 감탄스럽고 시적인 사람이 어떻게 굴욕의 골짜기로 내려간 적이 있을 수 있단 말인가, 우스 땅의 사람과 더불어—두세 해 전 그녀 자신이 느꼈던 것처럼—이렇게 느낀 적이 있을 수 있단 말인가. \"내 영혼은 차라리 질식하여 죽기를, 이 목숨보다 더 원하노라. 나는 그것이 역겹다."], ["I loathe it; I would not live alway.”", "나는 영영 살기를 원치 않노라.\""]], [["It was true that he was at present out of his class.", "지금 그가 제 계급에서 벗어나 있는 것은 사실이었다."], ["But she knew that was only because, like Peter the Great in a shipwright’s yard, he was studying what he wanted to know.", "하지만 그녀는 그것이 단지, 조선소의 표트르 대제처럼, 그가 알고 싶은 것을 배우고 있어서일 뿐임을 알았다."], ["He did not milk cows because he was obliged to milk cows, but because he was learning to be a rich and prosperous dairyman, landowner, agriculturist, and breeder of cattle.", "그는 소젖을 짜야만 해서 짜는 게 아니라, 부유하고 번창한 낙농가, 지주, 농사꾼, 가축 사육자가 되는 법을 배우고 있어서 짜는 것이었다."], ["He would become an American or Australian Abraham, commanding like a monarch his flocks and his herds, his spotted and his ring-straked, his men-servants and his maids.", "그는 미국이나 오스트레일리아의 아브라함이 되어, 군주처럼 제 양 떼와 소 떼를, 제 점박이와 얼룩이를, 제 종들과 하녀들을 호령할 터였다."], ["At times, nevertheless, it did seem unaccountable to her that a decidedly bookish, musical, thinking young man should have chosen deliberately to be a farmer, and not a clergyman, like his father and brothers.", "그래도 그녀에게는, 분명 책을 좋아하고 음악적이고 사색하는 젊은이가 아버지와 형들처럼 성직자가 아니라 굳이 농부가 되기로 작정했다는 게 이따금 도무지 이해되지 않았다."]], [["Thus, neither having the clue to the other’s secret, they were respectively puzzled at what each revealed, and awaited new knowledge of each other’s character and mood without attempting to pry into each other’s history.", "그리하여 둘 다 서로의 비밀에 대한 실마리를 쥐지 못한 채, 상대가 드러내는 것에 저마다 어리둥절해하며, 서로의 내력을 캐려 들지는 않은 채 서로의 성격과 기분에 대한 새 앎을 기다렸다."]], [["Every day, every hour, brought to him one more little stroke of her nature, and to her one more of his.", "날마다, 시각마다, 그에게는 그녀 본성의 작은 한 획이, 그녀에게는 그의 한 획이 또 하나씩 다가왔다."], ["Tess was trying to lead a repressed life, but she little divined the strength of her own vitality.", "테스는 억눌린 삶을 살려 애썼지만, 제 안의 생명력이 얼마나 강한지는 거의 헤아리지 못했다."]], [["At first Tess seemed to regard Angel Clare as an intelligence rather than as a man.", "처음에 테스는 엔젤 클레어를 남자라기보다 하나의 지성으로 여기는 듯했다."], ["As such she compared him with herself; and at every discovery of the abundance of his illuminations, of the distance between her own modest mental standpoint and the unmeasurable, Andean altitude of his, she became quite dejected, disheartened from all further effort on her own part whatever.", "그런 지성으로서 그녀는 그를 자신과 견주었다. 그리고 그가 비추는 빛이 얼마나 풍성한지, 자신의 보잘것없는 지적 입지와 헤아릴 수 없는 안데스 같은 그의 높이 사이의 거리가 얼마나 먼지를 발견할 때마다, 그녀는 몹시 풀이 죽어, 자기 쪽에서 무엇이든 더 애써 볼 의욕마저 잃었다."]], [["He observed her dejection one day, when he had casually mentioned something to her about pastoral life in ancient Greece.", "어느 날 그가 무심코 그녀에게 고대 그리스의 목가적 삶에 대해 무언가 이야기했을 때, 그는 그녀의 낙담을 알아챘다."], ["She was gathering the buds called “lords and ladies” from the bank while he spoke.", "그가 말하는 동안 그녀는 둑에서 \"도련님과 아가씨\"라 불리는 봉오리를 따고 있었다."]], [["“Why do you look so woebegone all of a sudden?” he asked.", "\"왜 갑자기 그렇게 시름에 잠긴 얼굴이오?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, ’tis only—about my own self,”", "\"오, 그저—제 자신에 대한 거예요.\""], ["she said, with a frail laugh of sadness, fitfully beginning to peel “a lady”", "그녀가 슬픔이 어린 가냘픈 웃음을 띠며 말했다. 그러면서 가다 말다 \"아가씨\" 하나의 껍질을 벗기기 시작했다."], ["meanwhile. “Just a sense of what might have been with me!", "\"그냥, 저한테 어쩌면 있었을지도 모를 것에 대한 생각이요!"], ["My life looks as if it had been wasted for want of chances!", "제 인생은 기회가 없어서 허비된 것만 같아요!"], ["When I see what you know, what you have read, and seen, and thought, I feel what a nothing I am!", "나리가 아시는 것, 읽으신 것, 보고 생각하신 것을 보면, 저는 제가 얼마나 아무것도 아닌 존재인지 느껴져요!"], ["I’m like the poor Queen of Sheba who lived in the Bible.", "저는 성경 속에 살던 그 가련한 스바 여왕 같아요."], ["There is no more spirit in me.”", "제 안엔 더는 기운이 없어요.\""]], [["“Bless my soul, don’t go troubling about that! Why,”", "\"이런, 그런 걸로 마음 쓰지 말아요! 아니,\""], ["he said with some enthusiasm, “I should be only too glad, my dear Tess, to help you to anything in the way of history, or any line of reading you would like to take up—”", "그가 다소 열을 띠며 말했다. \"나야말로 기꺼이 도와주고말고요, 테스 양, 역사든 당신이 익혀 보고 싶은 어떤 방면의 독서든—\""]], [["“It is a lady again,”", "\"또 아가씨네요.\""], ["interrupted she, holding out the bud she had peeled.", "그녀가 막 껍질 벗긴 봉오리를 내밀며 말을 끊었다."]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""]], [["“I meant that there are always more ladies than lords when you come to peel them.”", "\"껍질을 벗겨 보면 늘 도련님보다 아가씨가 더 많다는 뜻이에요.\""]], [["“Never mind about the lords and ladies.", "\"도련님이니 아가씨니 하는 건 그만두고."], ["Would you like to take up any course of study—history, for example?”", "어떤 공부를 해 보고 싶지 않아요—이를테면 역사 같은 거?\""]], [["“Sometimes I feel I don’t want to know anything more about it than I know already.”", "\"가끔은 제가 이미 아는 것 이상으론 그것에 대해 알고 싶지 않다는 생각이 들어요.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because what’s the use of learning that I am one of a long row only—finding out that there is set down in some old book somebody just like me, and to know that I shall only act her part; making me sad, that’s all.", "\"제가 그저 긴 줄의 하나일 뿐이라는 걸 배워서 무슨 소용이겠어요—어느 낡은 책에 저랑 꼭 같은 누군가가 적혀 있다는 걸 알아내고, 제가 그 사람 역할이나 되풀이하리라는 걸 아는 게 말이에요."], ["The best is not to remember that your nature and your past doings have been just like thousands’ and thousands’, and that your coming life and doings ’ll be like thousands’ and thousands’.”", "그건 절 슬프게 할 뿐이에요. 제일 좋은 건, 내 본성과 내 지난 행동이 수천수만의 그것과 꼭 같았다는 걸, 그리고 내 다가올 삶과 행동도 수천수만의 그것과 같으리라는 걸 기억하지 않는 거예요.\""]], [["“What, really, then, you don’t want to learn anything?”", "\"아니, 그럼 정말로 아무것도 배우고 싶지 않다는 거요?\""]], [["“I shouldn’t mind learning why—why the sun do shine on the just and the unjust alike,”", "\"왜—왜 해가 의로운 자에게나 불의한 자에게나 똑같이 비치는지, 그건 좀 알고 싶어요.\""], ["she answered, with a slight quaver in her voice. “But that’s what books will not tell me.”", "그녀가 목소리를 살짝 떨며 대답했다. \"하지만 그건 책이 가르쳐 주지 않는 거잖아요.\""]], [["“Tess, fie for such bitterness!”", "\"테스, 그런 쓴소리는 그만!\""], ["Of course he spoke with a conventional sense of duty only, for that sort of wondering had not been unknown to himself in bygone days.", "물론 그는 관습적인 의무감에서 그렇게 말했을 뿐이었으니, 그런 의문은 지난날 그 자신에게도 낯설지 않았기 때문이다."], ["And as he looked at the unpracticed mouth and lips, he thought that such a daughter of the soil could only have caught up the sentiment by rote.", "그리고 그 익숙지 않은 입과 입술을 바라보며, 그는 흙의 딸인 이런 처녀가 그런 정서를 그저 어디서 주워들어 외운 것에 불과하리라 여겼다."], ["She went on peeling the lords and ladies till Clare, regarding for a moment the wave-like curl of her lashes as they dropped with her bent gaze on her soft cheek, lingeringly went away.", "그녀는 도련님과 아가씨 껍질을 계속 벗겼다. 클레어는, 그녀가 부드러운 뺨 위로 시선을 떨굴 때 물결처럼 굽이지는 속눈썹을 잠시 바라보다가, 미적거리며 자리를 떴다."], ["When he was gone she stood awhile, thoughtfully peeling the last bud; and then, awakening from her reverie, flung it and all the crowd of floral nobility impatiently on the ground, in an ebullition of displeasure with herself for her _niaiseries_, and with a quickening warmth in her heart of hearts.", "그가 가고 나자 그녀는 한동안 서서, 마지막 봉오리를 생각에 잠겨 벗겼다. 그러다 몽상에서 깨어나, 그것과 그 꽃의 귀족 무리를 모조리 못마땅한 듯 땅바닥에 내던졌다. 제 어리석은 짓에 스스로 부아가 끓어오르면서, 동시에 마음 깊은 곳에서 따뜻함이 차오르는 가운데."]], [["How stupid he must think her! In an access of hunger for his good opinion she bethought herself of what she had latterly endeavoured to forget, so unpleasant had been its issues—the identity of her family with that of the knightly d’Urbervilles.", "그가 자기를 얼마나 멍청하다고 여길까! 그의 좋은 평가를 갈구하는 마음이 솟구치자, 그녀는 근래 잊으려 애써 온 것을, 그 결말이 하도 언짢았던 그것을 떠올렸다—제 집안이 기사 가문인 더버빌가와 동일하다는 사실을."], ["Barren attribute as it was, disastrous as its discovery had been in many ways to her, perhaps Mr Clare, as a gentleman and a student of history, would respect her sufficiently to forget her childish conduct with the lords and ladies if he knew that those Purbeck-marble and alabaster people in Kingsbere Church really represented her own lineal forefathers; that she was no spurious d’Urberville, compounded of money and ambition like those at Trantridge, but true d’Urberville to the bone.", "그것이 아무 쓸모없는 속성이고, 그 발견이 여러모로 그녀에게 재앙이었어도, 어쩌면 클레어 씨는, 신사이자 역사를 공부하는 사람으로서, 킹스비어 교회의 저 퍼벡 대리석과 설화석고로 된 사람들이 실은 그녀 자신의 직계 조상을 나타낸다는 것을, 또 그녀가 트랜트리지의 그자들처럼 돈과 야심으로 빚어진 가짜 더버빌이 아니라 뼛속까지 참된 더버빌이라는 것을 안다면, 도련님과 아가씨를 가지고 벌인 그 어린애 같은 짓은 잊을 만큼 그녀를 존중해 줄지도 몰랐다."]], [["But, before venturing to make the revelation, dubious Tess indirectly sounded the dairyman as to its possible effect upon Mr Clare, by asking the former if Mr Clare had any great respect for old county families when they had lost all their money and land.", "하지만 그 사실을 털어놓는 모험을 감행하기 전에, 미심쩍은 마음의 테스는 낙농가에게, 클레어 씨가 돈과 땅을 다 잃은 옛 명문가를 크게 존중하는지 어떤지 물어, 그것이 클레어 씨에게 미칠 영향을 에둘러 떠보았다."]], [["“Mr Clare,”", "\"클레어 씨로 말하자면,\""], ["said the dairyman emphatically, “is one of the most rebellest rozums you ever knowed—not a bit like the rest of his family; and if there’s one thing that he do hate more than another ’tis the notion of what’s called a’ old family.", "낙농가가 힘주어 말했다. \"자네가 본 중에 가장 반골인 괴짜라네—제 집안 나머지하고는 영 딴판이지. 그리고 그가 무엇보다 더 싫어하는 게 하나 있다면, 바로 이른바 옛 명문가라는 관념이야."], ["He says that it stands to reason that old families have done their spurt of work in past days, and can’t have anything left in ’em now.", "그는 그러더군, 옛 가문이란 지난날 제 할 일을 다 해 버렸으니 이제 안에 남은 게 있을 리 없다고, 그게 이치에 맞는 말이라고 말이야."], ["There’s the Billets and the Drenkhards and the Greys and the St Quintins and the Hardys and the Goulds, who used to own the lands for miles down this valley; you could buy ’em all up now for an old song a’most.", "이 골짜기 아래로 몇 마일이나 땅을 거느렸던 빌렛가니 드렝카드가니 그레이가니 세인트퀸틴가니 하디가니 굴드가니 하는 집안들이 있는데, 지금은 죄다 헐값에 사들일 수 있을 지경이라네. 아니, 여기 우리 어린 레티 프리들 알지, 걔가 패리델가의 하나야—킹스힝톡 너머로 땅을 잔뜩 가졌던 옛 가문 말일세."], ["Why, our little Retty Priddle here, you know, is one of the Paridelles—the old family that used to own lots o’ the lands out by King’s Hintock, now owned by the Earl o’ Wessex, afore even he or his was heard of.", "지금은 웨식스 백작 소유가 됐지만, 그 백작이나 그 집안이 알려지기도 전부터 말이야. 그런데, 클레어 씨가 이걸 알아내고는 며칠 동안 그 가엾은 애한테 아주 비웃듯이 말하더군. '아!' 하고 그애한테 그래."], ["Well, Mr Clare found this out, and spoke quite scornful to the poor girl for days. ‘Ah!’ he says to her, ‘you’ll never make a good dairymaid!", "'넌 결코 좋은 젖꾼이 못 될 거야! 네 솜씨는 죄다 옛날 옛적 팔레스타인에서 다 써 버렸으니, 더 많은 위업을 위해 기운을 차리려면 천 년은 묵정밭으로 쉬어야 한다고!'"], ["All your skill was used up ages ago in Palestine, and you must lie fallow for a thousand years to git strength for more deeds!’ A boy came here t’other day asking for a job, and said his name was Matt, and when we asked him his surname he said he’d never heard that ’a had any surname, and when we asked why, he said he supposed his folks hadn’t been ’stablished long enough. ‘Ah!", "며칠 전엔 어떤 사내애가 일자리를 구하러 와서 제 이름이 매트라고 하기에, 성을 물으니까 제가 성을 가졌단 얘긴 들어 본 적이 없다는 거야. 왜냐고 물으니, 제 집안이 자리 잡은 지 그리 오래되지 않아서 그런가 보다고 하더군. '아! 너야말로 내가 딱 원하는 애로구나!'"], ["you’re the very boy I want!’ says Mr Clare, jumping up and shaking hands wi’en; ‘I’ve great hopes of you;’ and gave him half-a-crown. O no!", "하고 클레어 씨가 벌떡 일어나 그애와 악수를 하면서 그래. '너한테 큰 기대를 거마.' 그러고는 그애한테 반 크라운을 줬지."], ["he can’t stomach old families!”", "오 안 돼! 그는 옛 명문가라면 비위가 상한다니까!\""]], [["After hearing this caricature of Clare’s opinion poor Tess was glad that she had not said a word in a weak moment about her family—even though it was so unusually old almost to have gone round the circle and become a new one.", "클레어의 견해를 이렇게 우스꽝스럽게 옮긴 말을 듣고 나니, 가엾은 테스는 약한 순간에라도 제 집안에 대해 한마디도 하지 않은 것이 다행스러웠다—비록 그 집안이, 워낙 오래되어 거의 한 바퀴를 돌아 새 가문이 되어 버렸다 할 만큼 유별나게 오래되긴 했어도."], ["Besides, another diary-girl was as good as she, it seemed, in that respect.", "게다가 그 점에서는, 다른 낙농장 처녀도 자기 못지않은 셈이었다."], ["She held her tongue about the d’Urberville vault and the Knight of the Conqueror whose name she bore.", "그녀는 더버빌가의 묘소와, 자기가 그 이름을 물려받은 정복왕 시대의 그 기사에 대해 입을 다물었다."], ["The insight afforded into Clare’s character suggested to her that it was largely owing to her supposed untraditional newness that she had won interest in his eyes.", "클레어의 성격을 엿보고 나니, 그가 자기에게 흥미를 갖게 된 것이 주로 전통 없이 새롭다고 여겨지는 그녀의 면모 덕분이라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["# XX", "# XX"]], [["The season developed and matured.", "XX\n계절이 무르익어 갔다."], ["Another year’s instalment of flowers, leaves, nightingales, thrushes, finches, and such ephemeral creatures, took up their positions where only a year ago others had stood in their place when these were nothing more than germs and inorganic particles.", "한 해 치의 꽃과 잎과 나이팅게일과 개똥지빠귀와 핀치와 그 밖의 덧없는 생명들이, 불과 일 년 전 다른 것들이—지금 이것들이 한낱 씨눈과 무기물 입자에 불과하던 그때—섰던 바로 그 자리를 차지했다."], ["Rays from the sunrise drew forth the buds and stretched them into long stalks, lifted up sap in noiseless streams, opened petals, and sucked out scents in invisible jets and breathings.", "동트는 빛의 광선이 봉오리를 끌어내 긴 줄기로 늘이고, 소리 없는 물줄기 속에 수액을 끌어 올리고, 꽃잎을 열고, 보이지 않는 분출과 숨결로 향기를 빨아 냈다."]], [["Dairyman Crick’s household of maids and men lived on comfortably, placidly, even merrily.", "낙농가 크릭네의 처녀와 사내들의 살림은 편안하게, 평온하게, 더없이 즐겁게 흘러갔다."], ["Their position was perhaps the happiest of all positions in the social scale, being above the line at which neediness ends, and below the line at which the _convenances_ begin to cramp natural feelings, and the stress of threadbare modishness makes too little of enough.", "그들의 처지는 어쩌면 사회적 등급에서 가장 행복한 위치였을 것이다. 궁핍이 끝나는 선 위에 있으면서도, 격식이 자연스러운 감정을 옥죄기 시작하고 닳아 빠진 유행의 압박이 넉넉함을 부족하게 여기게 만드는 선 아래에 있었으니 말이다."]], [["Thus passed the leafy time when arborescence seems to be the one thing aimed at out of doors.", "그렇게 잎이 무성한 시절이 지나갔다. 바깥에서는 나무가 우거지는 것만이 유일한 목표인 듯한 그 계절이."], ["Tess and Clare unconsciously studied each other, ever balanced on the edge of a passion, yet apparently keeping out of it.", "테스와 클레어는 자기도 모르게 서로를 살폈고, 늘 정념의 가장자리에 아슬아슬 균형을 잡으면서도, 겉으로는 그 속에 빠지지 않으려는 듯했다."], ["All the while they were converging, under an irresistible law, as surely as two streams in one vale.", "그러는 내내 그들은, 한 골짜기의 두 시냇물이 만나는 것만큼이나 어김없이, 거역할 수 없는 법칙에 따라 서로를 향해 모여들고 있었다. 테스는 근래의 삶에서 지금처럼 행복했던 적이 없었다."]], [["Tess had never in her recent life been so happy as she was now, possibly never would be so happy again.", "어쩌면 다시는 이만큼 행복할 수 없을지도 몰랐다."], ["She was, for one thing, physically and mentally suited among these new surroundings.", "우선 그녀는 이 새로운 환경 속에서 몸도 마음도 잘 들어맞았다."], ["The sapling which had rooted down to a poisonous stratum on the spot of its sowing had been transplanted to a deeper soil.", "씨가 뿌려진 자리에서 독성 지층까지 뿌리를 내렸던 어린 나무가 더 깊은 흙으로 옮겨 심긴 셈이었다."], ["Moreover she, and Clare also, stood as yet on the debatable land between predilection and love; where no profundities have been reached; no reflections have set in, awkwardly inquiring, “Whither does this new current tend to carry me?", "게다가 그녀도, 클레어도, 아직은 호감과 사랑 사이의 모호한 땅 위에 서 있었다. 깊은 곳에는 아직 닿지 않았고, 어색하게 캐묻는 성찰도 아직 시작되지 않았다. \"이 새 물살은 나를 어디로 데려가려는 걸까?"], ["What does it mean to my future?", "내 미래에 이것은 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["How does it stand towards my past?”", "내 과거에 비추어 이것은 어떻게 서 있는가?\""], ["Tess was the merest stray phenomenon to Angel Clare as yet—a rosy, warming apparition which had only just acquired the attribute of persistence in his consciousness.", "테스는 아직 엔젤 클레어에게 한낱 스쳐 가는 현상이었다. 그의 의식 속에서 겨우 막 지속성이라는 속성을 얻기 시작한, 장밋빛으로 마음을 데우는 환영이었다."], ["So he allowed his mind to be occupied with her, deeming his preoccupation to be no more than a philosopher’s regard of an exceedingly novel, fresh, and interesting specimen of womankind.", "그래서 그는 자기 마음이 그녀에게 사로잡히도록 내버려 두었고, 그 몰두를 그저 지극히 새롭고 신선하며 흥미로운 여성이라는 표본을 향한 철학자의 관조쯤으로 여겼다."]], [["They met continually; they could not help it.", "그들은 끊임없이 마주쳤다. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다."], ["They met daily in that strange and solemn interval, the twilight of the morning, in the violet or pink dawn; for it was necessary to rise early, so very early, here.", "그 기이하고 엄숙한 막간, 아침의 어스름 속에서, 보랏빛이나 분홍빛 새벽 속에서 그들은 날마다 마주쳤다. 이곳에서는 일찍 일어나야, 아주 일찍 일어나야 했기 때문이다."], ["Milking was done betimes; and before the milking came the skimming, which began at a little past three.", "젖 짜는 일은 일찌감치 이루어졌고, 그 전에는 크림 걷어 내는 일이 있었는데, 그것은 세 시가 조금 지나 시작되었다."], ["It usually fell to the lot of some one or other of them to wake the rest, the first being aroused by an alarm-clock; and, as Tess was the latest arrival, and they soon discovered that she could be depended upon not to sleep through the alarm as others did, this task was thrust most frequently upon her.", "대개 누군가 한 사람이 나머지를 깨우는 몫을 맡았고, 첫 사람은 자명종으로 깼다. 테스가 가장 늦게 온 데다, 다른 이들처럼 자명종 소리를 듣고도 내처 자는 법이 없다는 것을 다들 곧 알아챘기에, 이 일은 가장 자주 그녀에게 떠맡겨졌다."], ["No sooner had the hour of three struck and whizzed, than she left her room and ran to the dairyman’s door; then up the ladder to Angel’s, calling him in a loud whisper; then woke her fellow-milkmaids.", "세 시를 알리는 종이 울리고 윙 하고 멎자마자, 그녀는 방을 나와 낙농장 주인의 방문으로 달려갔다. 그런 다음 사다리를 올라 엔젤의 방으로 가, 큰 속삭임으로 그를 불렀다. 그러고는 동료 젖 짜는 처녀들을 깨웠다."], ["By the time that Tess was dressed Clare was downstairs and out in the humid air.", "테스가 옷을 다 입을 무렵이면 클레어는 아래층에 내려와 축축한 공기 속에 나가 있었다."], ["The remaining maids and the dairyman usually gave themselves another turn on the pillow, and did not appear till a quarter of an hour later.", "나머지 처녀들과 낙농장 주인은 대개 베개 위에서 한 번 더 몸을 뒤척이고, 십오 분쯤 지나서야 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["The gray half-tones of daybreak are not the gray half-tones of the day’s close, though the degree of their shade may be the same.", "동틀 녘의 잿빛 중간 색조는, 그 음영의 정도가 같다 해도, 하루가 저무는 잿빛 중간 색조와 같지 않다."], ["In the twilight of the morning, light seems active, darkness passive; in the twilight of evening it is the darkness which is active and crescent, and the light which is the drowsy reverse.", "아침의 어스름 속에서는 빛이 능동적이고 어둠이 수동적으로 보인다. 저녁의 어스름 속에서는 어둠이 능동적이고 차오르는 것이며, 빛은 졸린 듯 물러서는 쪽이다."]], [["Being so often—possibly not always by chance—the first two persons to get up at the dairy-house, they seemed to themselves the first persons up of all the world.", "그렇게 자주, 어쩌면 늘 우연만은 아니게, 낙농장에서 가장 먼저 일어나는 두 사람이었기에, 그들은 스스로를 온 세상에서 가장 먼저 깨어난 사람들로 여겼다."], ["In these early days of her residence here Tess did not skim, but went out of doors at once after rising, where he was generally awaiting her.", "이곳에 머문 초기의 그 나날들에 테스는 크림을 걷지 않고, 일어나자마자 곧장 바깥으로 나갔는데, 거기에는 대개 그가 그녀를 기다리고 있었다."], ["The spectral, half-compounded, aqueous light which pervaded the open mead impressed them with a feeling of isolation, as if they were Adam and Eve.", "트인 풀밭에 가득 퍼진, 유령 같고 반쯤만 형체를 갖춘 물빛은 그들에게 고립감을 심어 주었다. 마치 그들이 아담과 이브인 것처럼."], ["At this dim inceptive stage of the day Tess seemed to Clare to exhibit a dignified largeness both of disposition and physique, an almost regnant power, possibly because he knew that at that preternatural time hardly any woman so well endowed in person as she was likely to be walking in the open air within the boundaries of his horizon; very few in all England.", "하루가 아직 어둑하게 시작되는 이 단계에서 테스는 클레어에게 기질과 체구 양쪽으로 위엄 있는 큼직함을, 거의 군림하는 듯한 힘을 드러내 보이는 듯했다. 아마도 그 비현실적인 시각에, 그의 시야 안 트인 들판을 거닐 만한, 그녀처럼 몸이 그토록 잘 갖추어진 여인은 좀처럼 없으리라는 것을—온 잉글랜드를 통틀어도 극히 드물리라는 것을—그가 알았기 때문이리라."], ["Fair women are usually asleep at mid-summer dawns.", "아름다운 여인들은 보통 한여름 새벽에 잠들어 있다."], ["She was close at hand, and the rest were nowhere.", "그녀는 바로 곁에 있었고, 나머지는 어디에도 없었다."]], [["The mixed, singular, luminous gloom in which they walked along together to the spot where the cows lay often made him think of the Resurrection hour.", "소 떼가 누운 자리로 그들이 함께 걸어가던, 그 뒤섞이고 야릇하며 빛나는 어둠은 그에게 곧잘 부활의 시각을 떠올리게 했다."], ["He little thought that the Magdalen might be at his side.", "막달레나가 자기 곁에 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 거의 하지 못했다."], ["Whilst all the landscape was in neutral shade his companion’s face, which was the focus of his eyes, rising above the mist stratum, seemed to have a sort of phosphorescence upon it.", "온 풍경이 중립적인 음영 속에 잠겨 있을 때, 그의 시선이 모이는 초점인 그녀의 얼굴은 안개층 위로 떠올라 일종의 인광을 머금은 듯했다."], ["She looked ghostly, as if she were merely a soul at large.", "그녀는 유령 같았다. 마치 그저 풀려난 한 영혼인 것처럼."], ["In reality her face, without appearing to do so, had caught the cold gleam of day from the north-east; his own face, though he did not think of it, wore the same aspect to her.", "사실은 그녀의 얼굴이, 그런 기색도 없이, 북동쪽에서 비치는 차가운 새벽빛을 받은 것이었다. 그 자신의 얼굴도, 그는 생각지 못했지만, 그녀에게는 똑같은 모습으로 비쳤다."]], [["It was then, as has been said, that she impressed him most deeply.", "앞서 말했듯이, 바로 그때 그녀는 그에게 가장 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["She was no longer the milkmaid, but a visionary essence of woman—a whole sex condensed into one typical form.", "그녀는 더는 젖 짜는 처녀가 아니라, 여성의 환영 같은 정수였다. 하나의 전형적 형상으로 응축된 성性 전체였다."], ["He called her Artemis, Demeter, and other fanciful names half teasingly, which she did not like because she did not understand them.", "그는 그녀를 아르테미스라고, 데메테르라고, 그 밖의 공상적인 이름들로 반쯤 놀리듯 불렀는데, 그녀는 그 뜻을 알지 못했기에 좋아하지 않았다."]], [["“Call me Tess,”", "\"테스라고 불러 주세요.\""], ["she would say askance; and he did.", "그녀는 곁눈질하며 말하곤 했고, 그는 그렇게 했다."]], [["Then it would grow lighter, and her features would become simply feminine; they had changed from those of a divinity who could confer bliss to those of a being who craved it.", "그러면 점점 날이 밝아 왔고, 그녀의 이목구비는 그저 여성다워졌다. 행복을 내려 줄 수 있는 여신의 모습에서, 그것을 갈망하는 한 존재의 모습으로 바뀌어 있었다."]], [["At these non-human hours they could get quite close to the waterfowl.", "이 인간 아닌 시각에 그들은 물새들에게 아주 가까이 다가갈 수 있었다."], ["Herons came, with a great bold noise as of opening doors and shutters, out of the boughs of a plantation which they frequented at the side of the mead; or, if already on the spot, hardily maintained their standing in the water as the pair walked by, watching them by moving their heads round in a slow, horizontal, passionless wheel, like the turn of puppets by clockwork.", "왜가리들이, 문과 덧창을 여는 듯한 크고 거침없는 소리를 내며, 풀밭 옆 자기들이 자주 찾는 인공림의 가지에서 날아 나왔다. 또는 이미 그 자리에 있을 때면, 두 사람이 지나가도 물속에 굳건히 버티고 서서, 시계태엽으로 도는 꼭두각시처럼 느리고 수평이며 무심한 회전으로 머리를 돌려 그들을 지켜보았다."]], [["They could then see the faint summer fogs in layers, woolly, level, and apparently no thicker than counterpanes, spread about the meadows in detached remnants of small extent.", "그때 그들은 여름의 옅은 안개가 층층이, 양털처럼, 평평하게, 겉보기엔 침대보보다 두껍지 않게, 작게 떨어진 자투리로 풀밭 여기저기 깔린 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["On the gray moisture of the grass were marks where the cows had lain through the night—dark-green islands of dry herbage the size of their carcasses, in the general sea of dew.", "풀의 잿빛 물기 위에는 소들이 밤새 누워 있던 자국이 남아 있었다. 온통 이슬의 바다 속에, 소의 몸뚱이만 한 크기로 짙은 초록빛 마른 풀이 섬을 이룬 자국이었다."], ["From each island proceeded a serpentine trail, by which the cow had rambled away to feed after getting up, at the end of which trail they found her; the snoring puff from her nostrils, when she recognized them, making an intenser little fog of her own amid the prevailing one.", "그 섬마다에서 구불구불한 자취가 뻗어 나갔는데, 소가 일어난 뒤 풀을 뜯으러 어슬렁어슬렁 멀어져 간 길이었고, 그 자취 끝에서 그들은 소를 찾아냈다. 소가 그들을 알아보고 콧구멍으로 코를 골 듯 뿜는 숨결은, 자욱한 안개 한가운데 제 나름의 더 짙은 작은 안개를 만들어 냈다."], ["Then they drove the animals back to the barton, or sat down to milk them on the spot, as the case might require.", "그러고 나서 그들은 짐승들을 가축마당으로 몰거나, 경우에 따라 그 자리에 앉아 젖을 짰다."]], [["Or perhaps the summer fog was more general, and the meadows lay like a white sea, out of which the scattered trees rose like dangerous rocks.", "또는 어쩌면 여름 안개가 더 자욱하게 퍼져, 풀밭이 하얀 바다처럼 누워 있고, 그 속에서 흩어진 나무들이 위험한 암초처럼 솟아 있기도 했다."], ["Birds would soar through it into the upper radiance, and hang on the wing sunning themselves, or alight on the wet rails subdividing the mead, which now shone like glass rods.", "새들이 그 사이로 솟구쳐 위쪽 광휘 속으로 올라가, 날개를 편 채 매달려 햇볕을 쬐거나, 풀밭을 갈라놓은 젖은 난간—이제는 유리 막대처럼 반짝이는—에 내려앉았다."], ["Minute diamonds of moisture from the mist hung, too, upon Tess’s eyelashes, and drops upon her hair, like seed pearls.", "안개에서 맺힌 자잘한 물기의 다이아몬드가 테스의 속눈썹에도 매달렸고, 머리카락 위에는 씨앗 진주 같은 물방울이 맺혔다."], ["When the day grew quite strong and commonplace these dried off her; moreover, Tess then lost her strange and ethereal beauty; her teeth, lips, and eyes scintillated in the sunbeams and she was again the dazzlingly fair dairymaid only, who had to hold her own against the other women of the world.", "날이 완전히 강해지고 평범해지면 이것들은 그녀에게서 말라 버렸다. 게다가 그때면 테스는 그 기이하고 천상적인 아름다움을 잃었다. 그녀의 이와 입술과 눈이 햇살 속에서 반짝였고, 그녀는 다시 세상의 다른 여인들을 상대로 제 자리를 지켜야 하는, 눈부시게 아름다운 젖 짜는 처녀일 뿐이었다."]], [["About this time they would hear Dairyman Crick’s voice, lecturing the non-resident milkers for arriving late, and speaking sharply to old Deborah Fyander for not washing her hands.", "이 무렵이면 그들은 낙농장 주인 크릭의 목소리를 듣곤 했다. 늦게 온 비거주 젖 짜는 일꾼들을 나무라고, 손을 씻지 않았다고 늙은 데버라 파인더에게 날카롭게 호통치는 소리였다."]], [["“For Heaven’s sake, pop thy hands under the pump, Deb!", "\"제발 펌프 밑에 손 좀 쑤셔 넣어, 데브!"], ["Upon my soul, if the London folk only knowed of thee and thy slovenly ways, they’d swaller their milk and butter more mincing than they do a’ready; and that’s saying a good deal.”", "정말이지, 런던 사람들이 자네하고 자네 칠칠치 못한 꼴을 알기만 하면, 지금도 우유랑 버터를 입에 댈 때 까다롭게 구는 판에, 더 깨작거리며 삼킬 거야. 그것도 보통 일이 아니지.\""]], [["The milking progressed, till towards the end Tess and Clare, in common with the rest, could hear the heavy breakfast table dragged out from the wall in the kitchen by Mrs Crick, this being the invariable preliminary to each meal; the same horrible scrape accompanying its return journey when the table had been cleared.", "젖 짜기가 진행되었고, 거의 끝나 갈 무렵이면 테스와 클레어도 나머지 사람들과 함께, 크릭 부인이 부엌에서 묵직한 아침 식탁을 벽에서 끌어내는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 이것은 끼니마다 변함없이 앞서는 일이었고, 식탁을 치운 뒤 그것을 다시 제자리로 끌어다 놓을 때도 똑같은 끔찍한 긁는 소리가 따라붙었다."]], [["# XXI", "# XXI"]], [["There was a great stir in the milk-house just after breakfast.", "아침 식사 직후 우유 작업장에 큰 소동이 벌어졌다."], ["The churn revolved as usual, but the butter would not come.", "교유기는 여느 때처럼 돌아갔지만, 버터가 나오지 않았다."], ["Whenever this happened the dairy was paralyzed.", "이런 일이 벌어질 때마다 낙농장은 마비되었다."], ["Squish, squash echoed the milk in the great cylinder, but never arose the sound they waited for.", "큰 원통 속에서 우유가 철벅철벅 소리를 냈지만, 그들이 기다리는 그 소리는 끝내 나지 않았다."]], [["Dairyman Crick and his wife, the milkmaids Tess, Marian, Retty Priddle, Izz Huett, and the married ones from the cottages; also Mr Clare, Jonathan Kail, old Deborah, and the rest, stood gazing hopelessly at the churn; and the boy who kept the horse going outside put on moon-like eyes to show his sense of the situation.", "낙농장 주인 크릭과 그 아내, 젖 짜는 처녀들인 테스, 매리언, 레티 프리들, 이즈 휴엣, 그리고 오두막에서 온 기혼 여인들, 또 클레어 씨와 조너선 카일, 늙은 데버라, 그 밖의 사람들이 모두 절망스러운 얼굴로 교유기를 바라보며 서 있었다. 바깥에서 말을 돌리던 소년은 자기가 사태를 알고 있다는 표시로 달덩이 같은 눈을 떴다."], ["Even the melancholy horse himself seemed to look in at the window in inquiring despair at each walk round.", "우울한 말조차도 한 바퀴 돌 때마다 캐묻는 듯한 절망으로 창문 안을 들여다보는 듯했다."]], [["“’Tis years since I went to Conjuror Trendle’s son in Egdon—years!”", "\"에그던에 사는 마술사 트렌들의 아들한테 찾아간 게 벌써 몇 년 전이야—몇 년이라고!\""], ["said the dairyman bitterly.", "낙농장 주인이 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["“And he was nothing to what his father had been.", "\"게다가 그 아들놈은 제 아비에 비하면 아무것도 아니었지."], ["I have said fifty times, if I have said once, that I _don’t_ believe in en; though ’a do cast folks’ waters very true.", "한 번 말했으면 쉰 번은 말했을 거야, 나는 그자를 안 믿는다고. 사람들 오줌점은 아주 잘 치긴 하지만 말이야."], ["But I shall have to go to ’n if he’s alive.", "그래도 그자가 살아 있다면 가 봐야겠지."], ["O yes, I shall have to go to ’n, if this sort of thing continnys!”", "아 그래, 가 봐야겠어, 이런 일이 계속된다면!\""]], [["Even Mr Clare began to feel tragical at the dairyman’s desperation.", "클레어 씨조차도 낙농장 주인의 절망에 비극적인 기분이 들기 시작했다."]], [["“Conjuror Fall, t’other side of Casterbridge, that they used to call ‘Wide-O’, was a very good man when I was a boy,”", "\"내가 어렸을 적엔 캐스터브리지 건너편에 사는, '와이드오'라고들 부르던 마술사 폴이 아주 용한 사람이었지요.\""], ["said Jonathan Kail.", "조너선 카일이 말했다."], ["“But he’s rotten as touchwood by now.”", "\"한데 이제는 다 썩은 부싯깃처럼 됐어요.\""]], [["“My grandfather used to go to Conjuror Mynterne, out at Owlscombe, and a clever man a’ were, so I’ve heard grandf’er say,”", "\"우리 할아버지는 아울스콤 너머의 마술사 민턴한테 다니셨는데, 아주 솜씨 좋은 사람이었다고, 할아버지 말씀을 들었지.\""], ["continued Mr Crick.", "크릭 씨가 이었다."], ["“But there’s no such genuine folk about nowadays!”", "\"그런데 요즘엔 그런 진짜배기 사람이 없단 말이야!\""]], [["Mrs Crick’s mind kept nearer to the matter in hand.", "크릭 부인의 마음은 눈앞의 일에 더 가까이 머물러 있었다."]], [["“Perhaps somebody in the house is in love,”", "\"어쩌면 이 집 누군가가 사랑에 빠진 건지도 모르죠.\""], ["she said tentatively.", "그녀가 조심스레 말했다."], ["“I’ve heard tell in my younger days that that will cause it.", "\"젊었을 적에 들으니, 그게 이런 일을 일으킨다더군요."], ["Why, Crick—that maid we had years ago, do ye mind, and how the butter didn’t come then—”", "아니, 크릭—몇 년 전에 우리 집에 있던 그 처녀 기억나요? 그때도 버터가 안 나왔잖아요—\""]], [["“Ah yes, yes!—but that isn’t the rights o’t.", "\"아 그래, 그래!—하지만 그게 그 까닭은 아니지."], ["It had nothing to do with the love-making. I can mind all about it—’twas the damage to the churn.”", "연애하고는 아무 상관 없었어. 나는 그 일 다 기억하는데—그건 교유기가 망가져서 그랬던 거야.\""]], [["He turned to Clare.", "그가 클레어를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Jack Dollop, a ’hore’s-bird of a fellow we had here as milker at one time, sir, courted a young woman over at Mellstock, and deceived her as he had deceived many afore.", "\"한때 우리 집에서 젖 짜는 일을 하던 잭 돌럽이라는 화냥년의 자식 같은 놈이 있었지요, 선생. 그놈이 멜스톡 너머에 사는 한 처녀한테 구애하고는, 전에 여럿한테 그랬듯이 그 처녀도 속여 먹었어요."], ["But he had another sort o’ woman to reckon wi’ this time, and it was not the girl herself.", "그런데 이번에는 상대를 잘못 골랐지요. 처녀 본인이 아니었거든요."], ["One Holy Thursday of all days in the almanack, we was here as we mid be now, only there was no churning in hand, when we zid the girl’s mother coming up to the door, wi’ a great brass-mounted umbrella in her hand that would ha’ felled an ox, and saying ‘Do Jack Dollop work here?—because I want him!", "달력에 박힌 날 중에서 하필 성목요일에, 우리는 지금처럼 여기 있었는데, 다만 그땐 교유 작업은 없었지요. 그때 그 처녀 어미가 문으로 올라오는 걸 봤는데, 손에는 황소도 때려눕힐 만한 커다란 놋쇠 장식 우산을 들고서 이러는 거예요. '잭 돌럽이 여기서 일하나요?—그놈한테 볼일이 있어서요!"], ["I have a big bone to pick with he, I can assure ’n!’ And some way behind her mother walked Jack’s young woman, crying bitterly into her handkercher.", "그놈하고 크게 따질 게 있단 말입니다, 분명히요!' 그리고 어미 뒤로 좀 떨어져서 잭의 처녀가, 손수건에 얼굴을 묻고 서럽게 울며 걸어왔지요. '오 맙소사, 큰일 났네!'"], ["‘O Lard, here’s a time!’ said Jack, looking out o’ winder at ’em.", "잭이 창밖으로 그들을 내다보며 말했어요. '저 여자가 날 죽이겠어!"], ["‘She’ll murder me!", "어디로 가지—어디로—?"], ["Where shall I get—where shall I—?", "내가 어디 있는지 말하지 마!'"], ["Don’t tell her where I be!’ And with that he scrambled into the churn through the trap-door, and shut himself inside, just as the young woman’s mother busted into the milk-house.", "그러더니 그놈은 뚜껑 문으로 교유기 안에 기어들어 가서, 처녀 어미가 우유 작업장으로 막 들이닥칠 때 안에 틀어박혀 버렸지요. '그 악당이—어디 있어요?' 어미가 말했어요."], ["‘The villain—where is he?’ says she. ‘I’ll claw his face for’n, let me only catch him!’ Well, she hunted about everywhere, ballyragging Jack by side and by seam, Jack lying a’most stifled inside the churn, and the poor maid—or young woman rather—standing at the door crying her eyes out.", "'잡기만 하면 얼굴을 할퀴어 놓을 테니, 어디 한번 잡아 보게 해 줘요!' 자, 어미가 사방을 뒤지며, 잭을 이쪽저쪽 솔기까지 욕을 퍼붓는데, 잭은 교유기 안에서 거의 숨이 막혀 누워 있고, 가엾은 처녀는—아니 젊은 여인이라고 해야겠군요—문간에 서서 눈이 빠지게 울고 있었지요."], ["I shall never forget it, never!", "나는 그걸 결코 못 잊을 거예요, 결코!"], ["’Twould have melted a marble stone!", "대리석 돌도 녹였을 거라니까요!"], ["But she couldn’t find him nowhere at all.”", "한데 어미는 그놈을 아무 데서도 찾지 못했어요.\""]], [["The dairyman paused, and one or two words of comment came from the listeners.", "낙농장 주인이 말을 멈추자, 듣던 이들에게서 한두 마디 평이 흘러나왔다."]], [["Dairyman Crick’s stories often seemed to be ended when they were not really so, and strangers were betrayed into premature interjections of finality; though old friends knew better.", "낙농장 주인 크릭의 이야기는 흔히 끝난 듯해도 정말로 끝난 게 아닐 때가 많았고, 낯선 이들은 섣불리 끝맺음의 감탄사를 내뱉도록 속아 넘어가곤 했다. 오랜 친구들은 사정을 더 잘 알았지만."], ["The narrator went on—", "이야기꾼은 말을 이었다."]], [["“Well, how the old woman should have had the wit to guess it I could never tell, but she found out that he was inside that there churn.", "\"자, 그 노파가 어떻게 그걸 알아챌 만한 머리가 돌아갔는지 나는 도무지 알 수 없지만, 어쨌든 그놈이 저 교유기 안에 들어 있다는 걸 알아냈지요."], ["Without saying a word she took hold of the winch (it was turned by handpower then), and round she swung him, and Jack began to flop about inside.", "노파는 한마디 말도 없이 손잡이를 잡고는 (그땐 손으로 돌렸거든요), 그놈을 빙빙 돌려 댔고, 잭은 안에서 펄떡거리기 시작했어요."], ["‘O Lard! stop the churn!", "'오 맙소사! 교유기 멈춰요!"], ["let me out!’ says he, popping out his head.", "나 좀 꺼내 줘요!' 그놈이 머리를 쑥 내밀며 말했지요."], ["‘I shall be churned into a pummy!’ (He was a cowardly chap in his heart, as such men mostly be).", "'내가 교유기에 갈려 푸딩이 되겠어요!' (그놈은 속으로는 비겁한 놈이었어요, 그런 자들이 대개 그렇듯이.) '그 애의 순결한 정조를 짓밟은 걸 보상하기 전까지는 안 돼!'"], ["‘Not till ye make amends for ravaging her virgin innocence!’ says the old woman.", "노파가 말했어요. '교유기 멈춰요, 이 늙은 마녀야!' 그놈이 비명을 질렀지요."], ["‘Stop the churn you old witch!’ screams he.", "'나더러 늙은 마녀라고, 이 사기꾼아!'"], ["‘You call me old witch, do ye, you deceiver!’ says she, ‘when ye ought to ha’ been calling me mother-law these last five months!’ And on went the churn, and Jack’s bones rattled round again.", "노파가 말했어요. '지난 다섯 달 동안 나를 장모님이라고 불렀어야 마땅한 주제에!' 그러고는 교유기가 계속 돌아갔고, 잭의 뼈가 다시 덜그럭덜그럭 굴렀지요."], ["Well, none of us ventured to interfere; and at last ’a promised to make it right wi’ her.", "자, 우리 중 누구도 감히 끼어들 엄두를 못 냈어요. 그러다 마침내 그놈이 그 애한테 일을 바로잡겠다고 약속했지요."], ["‘Yes—I’ll be as good as my word!’ he said.", "'그래요—약속은 꼭 지키겠어요!' 그놈이 말했어요."], ["And so it ended that day.”", "그래서 그날은 그렇게 끝났지요.\""]], [["While the listeners were smiling their comments there was a quick movement behind their backs, and they looked round.", "듣던 이들이 미소 지으며 한마디씩 하고 있을 때, 그들 등 뒤에서 빠른 움직임이 있었고, 그들은 돌아보았다."], ["Tess, pale-faced, had gone to the door.", "테스가 창백한 얼굴로 문 쪽으로 가 있었다."]], [["“How warm ’tis to-day!”", "\"오늘 참 덥네요!\""], ["she said, almost inaudibly.", "그녀가 거의 들리지 않게 말했다."]], [["It was warm, and none of them connected her withdrawal with the reminiscences of the dairyman.", "날이 덥긴 했고, 그들 중 누구도 그녀가 물러난 것을 낙농장 주인의 회고담과 연결 짓지 않았다."], ["He went forward and opened the door for her, saying with tender raillery—", "그가 다가가 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주며, 다정한 농담조로 말했다."]], [["“Why, maidy”", "\"아니, 아가씨\""], ["(he frequently, with unconscious irony, gave her this pet name), “the prettiest milker I’ve got in my dairy; you mustn’t get so fagged as this at the first breath of summer weather, or we shall be finely put to for want of ’ee by dog-days, shan’t we, Mr Clare?”", "(그는 무의식적인 반어로 그녀에게 이 애칭을 자주 붙였다), \"내 낙농장에서 제일 예쁜 젖 짜는 처녀가 말이야, 여름 날씨 첫 기운에 이렇게 지쳐 버리면 안 되지. 안 그러면 한여름 복중에 자네가 없어서 우리가 톡톡히 곤란해질 거 아닌가, 안 그렇소, 클레어 씨?\""]], [["“I was faint—and—I think I am better out o’ doors,”", "\"좀 어지러워서요—그리고—밖에 나가 있는 게 나을 것 같아요.\""], ["she said mechanically; and disappeared outside.", "그녀는 기계적으로 말하고는 밖으로 사라졌다."]], [["Fortunately for her the milk in the revolving churn at that moment changed its squashing for a decided flick-flack.", "다행스럽게도 그 순간 돌아가던 교유기 속 우유가 철벅거리던 소리를 바꿔 또렷한 찰싹찰싹 소리를 냈다."]], [["“’Tis coming!”", "\"나온다!\""], ["cried Mrs Crick, and the attention of all was called off from Tess.", "크릭 부인이 외쳤고, 모두의 관심이 테스에게서 떨어져 나갔다."]], [["That fair sufferer soon recovered herself externally; but she remained much depressed all the afternoon.", "그 아름다운 고통받는 이는 곧 겉으로는 평정을 되찾았다. 그러나 오후 내내 몹시 우울한 채로 있었다."], ["When the evening milking was done she did not care to be with the rest of them, and went out of doors, wandering along she knew not whither.", "저녁 젖 짜기가 끝나자 그녀는 다른 이들과 함께 있고 싶지 않았고, 밖으로 나가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 거닐었다."], ["She was wretched—O so wretched—at the perception that to her companions the dairyman’s story had been rather a humorous narration than otherwise; none of them but herself seemed to see the sorrow of it; to a certainty, not one knew how cruelly it touched the tender place in her experience.", "그녀는 비참했다—오 너무도 비참했다—동료들에게는 낙농장 주인의 이야기가 그저 우스운 이야기쯤이었지 그 이상이 아니었다는 깨달음에. 그녀 말고는 누구도 그 슬픔을 보는 것 같지 않았다. 분명히 그 누구도 그것이 자기 경험의 여린 자리를 얼마나 잔인하게 건드렸는지 알지 못했다."], ["The evening sun was now ugly to her, like a great inflamed wound in the sky.", "이제 저녁 해는 그녀에게 추악해 보였다. 하늘에 난 크게 곪은 상처 같았다."], ["Only a solitary cracked-voice reed-sparrow greeted her from the bushes by the river, in a sad, machine-made tone, resembling that of a past friend whose friendship she had outworn.", "다만 외로운, 목쉰 갈대참새 한 마리가 강가 덤불에서, 슬프고도 기계로 찍어 낸 듯한 가락으로 그녀에게 인사했다. 우정이 닳아 버린 지난날의 한 벗을 닮은 가락으로."]], [["In these long June days the milkmaids, and, indeed, most of the household, went to bed at sunset or sooner, the morning work before milking being so early and heavy at a time of full pails.", "이 긴 유월의 나날에 젖 짜는 처녀들은, 사실 집안 식구 대부분이, 해 질 무렵이나 그보다 일찍 잠자리에 들었다. 젖 짜기에 앞선 아침 일이 양동이가 가득 차는 철에는 너무도 일찍 시작되고 고됐기 때문이다."], ["Tess usually accompanied her fellows upstairs.", "테스는 보통 동료들을 따라 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["To-night, however, she was the first to go to their common chamber; and she had dozed when the other girls came in.", "그러나 이날 밤에는 공동 침실로 가장 먼저 갔고, 다른 처녀들이 들어왔을 때 그녀는 이미 깜박 졸고 있었다."], ["She saw them undressing in the orange light of the vanished sun, which flushed their forms with its colour; she dozed again, but she was reawakened by their voices, and quietly turned her eyes towards them.", "그녀는 그들이 사라진 해의 주황빛 속에서 옷을 벗는 것을 보았다. 그 빛이 그들의 몸을 제 빛깔로 물들였다. 그녀는 다시 졸았지만, 그들의 목소리에 다시 깨어나 조용히 그들 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸다."]], [["Neither of her three chamber-companions had got into bed.", "세 침실 동무 중 누구도 아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다."], ["They were standing in a group, in their nightgowns, barefooted, at the window, the last red rays of the west still warming their faces and necks and the walls around them.", "그들은 잠옷 바람에 맨발로 창가에 한 무리로 서 있었고, 서쪽의 마지막 붉은 햇살이 여전히 그들의 얼굴과 목과 주위의 벽을 데우고 있었다."], ["All were watching somebody in the garden with deep interest, their three faces close together: a jovial and round one, a pale one with dark hair, and a fair one whose tresses were auburn.", "모두 깊은 관심으로 정원의 누군가를 지켜보고 있었고, 세 얼굴이 바싹 붙어 있었다. 하나는 명랑하고 둥근 얼굴, 하나는 검은 머리에 창백한 얼굴, 그리고 하나는 적갈색 머리채의 아름다운 얼굴이었다."]], [["“Don’t push!", "\"밀지 마!"], ["You can see as well as I,”", "나만큼 너도 잘 보이잖아.\""], ["said Retty, the auburn-haired and youngest girl, without removing her eyes from the window.", "적갈색 머리에 막내인 레티가 창문에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["“’Tis no use for you to be in love with him any more than me, Retty Priddle,”", "\"네가 그분을 사랑해 봐야 나만큼이나 소용없어, 레티 프리들.\""], ["said jolly-faced Marian, the eldest, slily.", "명랑한 얼굴의 맏이 매리언이 짓궂게 말했다."], ["“His thoughts be of other cheeks than thine!”", "\"그분 마음은 네 뺨이 아닌 다른 뺨에 가 있단 말이야!\""]], [["Retty Priddle still looked, and the others looked again.", "레티 프리들은 여전히 바라보았고, 다른 이들도 다시 바라보았다."]], [["“There he is again!”", "\"저기 또 나오신다!\""], ["cried Izz Huett, the pale girl with dark damp hair and keenly cut lips.", "검고 축축한 머리에 날카롭게 깎인 입술을 가진 창백한 처녀, 이즈 휴엣이 외쳤다."]], [["“You needn’t say anything, Izz,”", "\"넌 아무 말도 할 처지 아냐, 이즈.\""], ["answered Retty.", "레티가 대꾸했다."], ["“For I zid you kissing his shade.”", "\"네가 그분 그림자에 입 맞추는 거 내가 봤거든.\""]], [["_”What_ did you see her doing?”", "\"걔가 뭘 하는 걸 봤다고?\""], ["asked Marian.", "매리언이 물었다."]], [["“Why—he was standing over the whey-tub to let off the whey, and the shade of his face came upon the wall behind, close to Izz, who was standing there filling a vat.", "\"아니—그분이 유청 통 위에 서서 유청을 빼고 있었는데, 그 얼굴 그림자가 뒤쪽 벽에 비쳤거든. 마침 거기 통을 채우며 서 있던 이즈 바로 가까이에 말이야."], ["She put her mouth against the wall and kissed the shade of his mouth; I zid her, though he didn’t.”", "걔가 입을 벽에 대고 그분 입 그림자에 입을 맞추는 거야. 그분은 못 봤지만 나는 봤다고.\""]], [["“O Izz Huett!”", "\"오 이즈 휴엣!\""], ["said Marian.", "매리언이 말했다."]], [["A rosy spot came into the middle of Izz Huett’s cheek.", "이즈 휴엣의 뺨 한가운데에 장밋빛 점이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Well, there was no harm in it,”", "\"흥, 그게 뭐 나쁠 거 있어.\""], ["she declared, with attempted coolness.", "그녀가 짐짓 태연한 척하며 단언했다."], ["“And if I be in love wi’en, so is Retty, too; and so be you, Marian, come to that.”", "\"그리고 내가 그분을 사랑한다면, 레티 너도 그렇고, 따지고 보면 매리언 너도 그래.\""]], [["Marian’s full face could not blush past its chronic pinkness.", "매리언의 통통한 얼굴은 만성적인 분홍빛을 넘어 더 붉어질 수가 없었다."]], [["“I!” she said.", "\"내가!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“What a tale!", "\"무슨 소릴!"], ["Ah, there he is again!", "아, 저기 또 나오신다!"], ["Dear eyes—dear face—dear Mr Clare!”", "사랑스러운 눈—사랑스러운 얼굴—사랑스러운 클레어 씨!\""]], [["“There—you’ve owned it!”", "\"거봐—네가 인정했어!\""]], [["“So have you—so have we all,”", "\"너도 했지—우리 다 했어.\""], ["said Marian, with the dry frankness of complete indifference to opinion.", "매리언이 남들 생각 따위 완전히 무심한 데서 오는 무미건조한 솔직함으로 말했다."], ["“It is silly to pretend otherwise amongst ourselves, though we need not own it to other folks.", "\"우리끼리 안 그런 척하는 건 어리석은 일이지, 다른 사람들한테야 인정할 필요 없지만."], ["I would just marry ’n to-morrow!”", "나라면 내일이라도 당장 그분과 결혼하겠어!\""]], [["“So would I—and more,”", "\"나도—그 이상이라도.\""], ["murmured Izz Huett.", "이즈 휴엣이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“And I too,”", "\"나도 그래.\""], ["whispered the more timid Retty.", "더 소심한 레티가 속삭였다."]], [["The listener grew warm.", "엿듣던 이는 마음이 뜨거워졌다."]], [["“We can’t all marry him,”", "\"우리 모두가 그분과 결혼할 순 없잖아.\""], ["said Izz.", "이즈가 말했다."]], [["“We shan’t, either of us; which is worse still,”", "\"우리 중 누구도 못 할걸. 그게 더 나쁜 거지.\""], ["said the eldest.", "맏이가 말했다."], ["“There he is again!”", "\"저기 또 나오신다!\""]], [["They all three blew him a silent kiss.", "세 사람 모두 그에게 소리 없이 입맞춤을 날렸다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""], ["asked Retty quickly.", "레티가 재빨리 물었다."]], [["“Because he likes Tess Durbeyfield best,”", "\"그분은 테스 더비필드를 제일 좋아하시니까.\""], ["said Marian, lowering her voice.", "매리언이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다."], ["“I have watched him every day, and have found it out.”", "\"내가 날마다 지켜봐서 알아냈어.\""]], [["There was a reflective silence.", "생각에 잠긴 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“But she don’t care anything for ’n?”", "\"하지만 걔는 그분한테 아무 마음도 없잖아?\""], ["at length breathed Retty.", "마침내 레티가 숨죽여 말했다."]], [["“Well—I sometimes think that too.”", "\"글쎄—나도 가끔은 그렇게 생각해.\""]], [["“But how silly all this is!”", "\"하지만 이게 다 얼마나 어리석은 짓이람!\""], ["said Izz Huett impatiently.", "이즈 휴엣이 못 참고 말했다."], ["“Of course he won’t marry any one of us, or Tess either—a gentleman’s son, who’s going to be a great landowner and farmer abroad!", "\"당연히 그분은 우리 중 누구하고도, 테스하고도 결혼 안 하실 거야—신사의 아들이고, 해외에서 큰 지주에 농장주가 될 분인데!"], ["More likely to ask us to come wi’en as farm-hands at so much a year!”", "차라리 우리한테 1년에 얼마씩 받고 농장 일꾼으로 따라오라고 하실 가능성이 더 크지!\""]], [["One sighed, and another sighed, and Marian’s plump figure sighed biggest of all.", "한 사람이 한숨을 쉬고, 또 한 사람이 한숨을 쉬었으며, 매리언의 통통한 몸이 가장 크게 한숨지었다."], ["Somebody in bed hard by sighed too.", "바로 곁 침대에 누운 누군가도 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["Tears came into the eyes of Retty Priddle, the pretty red-haired youngest—the last bud of the Paridelles, so important in the county annals.", "예쁘고 붉은 머리의 막내, 레티 프리들의 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그 고장 연대기에서 그토록 중요한 패리델 가문의 마지막 봉오리."], ["They watched silently a little longer, their three faces still close together as before, and the triple hues of their hair mingling.", "그들은 좀 더 말없이 지켜보았고, 세 얼굴은 여전히 아까처럼 바싹 붙어 있었으며, 세 가지 머리 빛깔이 뒤섞였다."], ["But the unconscious Mr Clare had gone indoors, and they saw him no more; and, the shades beginning to deepen, they crept into their beds.", "그러나 아무것도 모르는 클레어 씨가 안으로 들어가 버려, 그들은 더는 그를 볼 수 없었다. 그리고 어둠이 짙어지기 시작하자, 그들은 슬그머니 침대로 기어들었다."], ["In a few minutes they heard him ascend the ladder to his own room.", "몇 분 뒤 그들은 그가 사다리를 올라 자기 방으로 가는 소리를 들었다."], ["Marian was soon snoring, but Izz did not drop into forgetfulness for a long time.", "매리언은 곧 코를 골았지만, 이즈는 오랫동안 망각에 빠져들지 못했다."], ["Retty Priddle cried herself to sleep.", "레티 프리들은 울다가 잠들었다."]], [["The deeper-passioned Tess was very far from sleeping even then.", "더 깊은 정념을 지닌 테스는 그때조차도 잠과는 거리가 멀었다."], ["This conversation was another of the bitter pills she had been obliged to swallow that day.", "이 대화는 그날 그녀가 삼켜야만 했던 또 하나의 쓴 약이었다."], ["Scarce the least feeling of jealousy arose in her breast.", "그녀의 가슴에는 질투의 감정이 거의 일지 않았다."], ["For that matter she knew herself to have the preference.", "사실 그녀는 자기가 선택받았음을 알고 있었다."], ["Being more finely formed, better educated, and, though the youngest except Retty, more woman than either, she perceived that only the slightest ordinary care was necessary for holding her own in Angel Clare’s heart against these her candid friends.", "더 곱게 빚어졌고, 더 잘 배웠으며, 레티를 빼면 가장 어렸지만 둘 중 누구보다 더 여인다웠기에, 이 솔직한 벗들을 상대로 엔젤 클레어의 마음속에서 제 자리를 지키는 데는 그저 아주 약간의 평범한 신경만 쓰면 된다는 것을 그녀는 알아챘다."], ["But the grave question was, ought she to do this?", "그러나 심각한 물음은 이것이었다. 과연 그래도 되는 걸까?"], ["There was, to be sure, hardly a ghost of a chance for either of them, in a serious sense; but there was, or had been, a chance of one or the other inspiring him with a passing fancy for her, and enjoying the pleasure of his attentions while he stayed here.", "물론 진지한 의미에서는 셋 중 누구에게도 유령만 한 가능성조차 거의 없었다. 그러나 누군가 한 사람이 그에게 한때의 마음을 불러일으키고, 그가 여기 머무는 동안 그의 관심을 받는 기쁨을 누릴 가능성은 있었거나, 있어 왔다."], ["Such unequal attachments had led to marriage; and she had heard from Mrs Crick that Mr Clare had one day asked, in a laughing way, what would be the use of his marrying a fine lady, and all the while ten thousand acres of Colonial pasture to feed, and cattle to rear, and corn to reap.", "그런 어울리지 않는 인연이 결혼으로 이어진 적도 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 크릭 부인에게서, 클레어 씨가 어느 날 웃으며 이렇게 물었다는 이야기를 들었다. 만 에이커나 되는 식민지 목초지를 먹여 살리고 가축을 기르고 곡식을 거둬야 하는 마당에, 고상한 숙녀와 결혼해서 무슨 소용이 있겠느냐고."], ["A farm-woman would be the only sensible kind of wife for him.", "농사일을 아는 여자만이 그에게 분별 있는 유일한 아내일 거라고."], ["But whether Mr Clare had spoken seriously or not, why should she, who could never conscientiously allow any man to marry her now, and who had religiously determined that she never would be tempted to do so, draw off Mr Clare’s attention from other women, for the brief happiness of sunning herself in his eyes while he remained at Talbothays?", "그러나 클레어 씨가 진지하게 말한 것이든 아니든, 이제 어떤 남자에게도 양심상 결코 자기와 결혼하도록 허락할 수 없는 그녀가, 그리고 결코 그런 유혹에 넘어가지 않으리라 신앙심으로 굳게 다짐한 그녀가, 그가 탤보세이스에 머무는 동안 그의 눈길 속에서 햇볕을 쬐는 짧은 행복을 위해, 무엇 때문에 클레어 씨의 관심을 다른 여자들에게서 돌려놓아야 한단 말인가?"]], [["# XXII", "# XXII"]], [["They came downstairs yawning next morning; but skimming and milking were proceeded with as usual, and they went indoors to breakfast.", "이튿날 아침 그들은 하품을 하며 아래층으로 내려왔다. 그러나 크림 걷기와 젖 짜기는 여느 때처럼 진행되었고, 그들은 아침을 먹으러 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Dairyman Crick was discovered stamping about the house.", "낙농장 주인 크릭이 집 안을 쿵쿵 돌아다니고 있는 게 보였다."], ["He had received a letter, in which a customer had complained that the butter had a twang.", "그는 편지를 한 통 받았는데, 한 고객이 버터에서 이상한 맛이 난다고 불평한 내용이었다."]], [["“And begad, so ’t have!” said the dairyman, who held in his left hand a wooden slice on which a lump of butter was stuck.", "\"제기랄, 정말 그렇단 말이야!\" 낙농장 주인이 왼손에 버터 한 덩이가 붙은 나무 주걱을 들고 말했다."], ["“Yes—taste for yourself!”", "\"그래—직접 맛들 보라고!\""]], [["Several of them gathered round him; and Mr Clare tasted, Tess tasted, also the other indoor milkmaids, one or two of the milking-men, and last of all Mrs Crick, who came out from the waiting breakfast-table.", "여럿이 그의 주위로 모여들었다. 클레어 씨가 맛보고, 테스가 맛보고, 다른 실내 젖 짜는 처녀들도, 젖 짜는 일꾼 한둘도, 그리고 마지막으로 기다리던 아침 식탁에서 나온 크릭 부인도 맛보았다."], ["There certainly was a twang.", "정말로 이상한 맛이 났다."]], [["The dairyman, who had thrown himself into abstraction to better realize the taste, and so divine the particular species of noxious weed to which it appertained, suddenly exclaimed—", "낙농장 주인은 그 맛을 더 잘 느껴 그것이 어떤 종류의 해로운 잡초에서 비롯된 것인지 알아내려고 골똘히 정신을 집중하더니, 갑자기 외쳤다."]], [["“’Tis garlic!", "\"마늘이야!"], ["and I thought there wasn’t a blade left in that mead!”", "게다가 나는 그 풀밭에 마늘잎이 한 가닥도 안 남은 줄 알았는데!\""]], [["Then all the old hands remembered that a certain dry mead, into which a few of the cows had been admitted of late, had, in years gone by, spoilt the butter in the same way.", "그러자 오래된 일꾼들이 모두 떠올렸다. 최근에 소 몇 마리를 들여보낸 어떤 마른 풀밭이, 지난 세월에도 똑같은 식으로 버터를 망쳤다는 것을."], ["The dairyman had not recognized the taste at that time, and thought the butter bewitched.", "그때 낙농장 주인은 그 맛을 알아채지 못해 버터에 마법이 걸린 줄로만 알았었다."]], [["“We must overhaul that mead,”", "\"그 풀밭을 샅샅이 뒤져야겠어.\""], ["he resumed; “this mustn’t continny!”", "그가 다시 말했다. \"이런 일이 계속되면 안 돼!\""]], [["All having armed themselves with old pointed knives, they went out together.", "모두 끝이 뾰족한 헌 칼로 무장하고 함께 나갔다."], ["As the inimical plant could only be present in very microscopic dimensions to have escaped ordinary observation, to find it seemed rather a hopeless attempt in the stretch of rich grass before them.", "보통 눈에 띄지 않을 정도로 아주 미세한 크기로만 그 적대적인 풀이 있을 수 있었기에, 눈앞의 무성한 풀밭을 헤치며 그것을 찾아내는 일은 다소 가망 없는 시도로 보였다."], ["However, they formed themselves into line, all assisting, owing to the importance of the search; the dairyman at the upper end with Mr Clare, who had volunteered to help; then Tess, Marian, Izz Huett, and Retty; then Bill Lewell, Jonathan, and the married dairywomen—Beck Knibbs, with her wooly black hair and rolling eyes; and flaxen Frances, consumptive from the winter damps of the water-meads—who lived in their respective cottages.", "그래도 그들은 일렬로 늘어섰고, 수색의 중요성 때문에 모두가 거들었다. 위쪽 끝에는 낙농장 주인이, 돕겠다고 자청한 클레어 씨와 함께 섰다. 그다음에는 테스, 매리언, 이즈 휴엣, 레티가, 그다음에는 빌 루얼, 조너선, 그리고 기혼 낙농 여인들—양털 같은 검은 머리에 데굴거리는 눈을 가진 벡 닙스, 그리고 물 댄 풀밭의 겨울 습기로 폐병을 앓는 아마빛 머리의 프랜시스—이 섰는데, 이들은 각자 자기 오두막에서 사는 이들이었다."]], [["With eyes fixed upon the ground they crept slowly across a strip of the field, returning a little further down in such a manner that, when they should have finished, not a single inch of the pasture but would have fallen under the eye of some one of them.", "눈을 땅에 박은 채 그들은 밭의 한 줄기를 천천히 기어가듯 가로질렀고, 좀 더 아래쪽으로 되돌아오는 식이었다. 그렇게 하면 다 끝났을 때 풀밭의 단 한 치도 누군가의 눈에 들지 않은 데가 없게 될 터였다."], ["It was a most tedious business, not more than half a dozen shoots of garlic being discoverable in the whole field; yet such was the herb’s pungency that probably one bite of it by one cow had been sufficient to season the whole dairy’s produce for the day.", "그것은 더없이 지루한 일이었다. 밭 전체에서 마늘 싹은 대여섯 개밖에 발견되지 않았다. 그래도 그 풀의 톡 쏘는 맛이 어찌나 강한지, 아마 소 한 마리가 한 입 베어 문 것만으로도 그날 낙농장 전체 산물에 맛을 입히기에 충분했을 것이다."]], [["Differing one from another in natures and moods so greatly as they did, they yet formed, bending, a curiously uniform row—automatic, noiseless; and an alien observer passing down the neighbouring lane might well have been excused for massing them as “Hodge”.", "그토록 천성과 기분이 서로 크게 다른 그들이었지만, 허리를 굽힌 채 묘하게도 한결같은 줄을 이루었다—기계 같고, 소리도 없이. 이웃 오솔길을 지나가는 낯선 구경꾼이 있었다면, 그들을 한 덩어리로 묶어 \"촌놈\"이라 부른다 해도 너끈히 용서받았을 것이다."], ["As they crept along, stooping low to discern the plant, a soft yellow gleam was reflected from the buttercups into their shaded faces, giving them an elfish, moonlit aspect, though the sun was pouring upon their backs in all the strength of noon.", "풀을 분간하느라 낮게 몸을 숙이고 기어가는 동안, 미나리아재비에서 반사된 부드러운 노란빛이 그늘진 그들의 얼굴에 비쳐, 정수리에는 한낮의 온 힘으로 햇볕이 쏟아지는데도 그들에게 요정 같은, 달빛 어린 모습을 입혔다."]], [["Angel Clare, who communistically stuck to his rule of taking part with the rest in everything, glanced up now and then.", "엔젤 클레어는 모든 일에서 나머지 사람들과 함께한다는 자기 원칙을 평등주의적으로 고수하며, 이따금 고개를 들어 올렸다."], ["It was not, of course, by accident that he walked next to Tess.", "그가 테스 옆에서 걸은 것은 물론 우연이 아니었다."]], [["“Well, how are you?”", "\"음, 좀 어때요?\""], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Very well, thank you, sir,”", "\"아주 좋아요, 고맙습니다, 선생님.\""], ["she replied demurely.", "그녀가 얌전하게 대답했다."]], [["As they had been discussing a score of personal matters only half-an-hour before, the introductory style seemed a little superfluous.", "겨우 반 시간 전에 그들이 숱한 사사로운 일들을 의논하던 터라, 이런 인사조 말투는 좀 군더더기처럼 들렸다."], ["But they got no further in speech just then.", "그러나 그들은 그때 더는 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["They crept and crept, the hem of her petticoat just touching his gaiter, and his elbow sometimes brushing hers.", "그들은 기고 또 기었고, 그녀의 속치마 단이 그의 각반에 막 스쳤으며, 그의 팔꿈치가 때때로 그녀의 팔꿈치를 스쳤다."], ["At last the dairyman, who came next, could stand it no longer.", "마침내 그다음 자리에 있던 낙농장 주인이 더는 견디지 못했다."]], [["“Upon my soul and body, this here stooping do fairly make my back open and shut!”", "\"맙소사, 이렇게 굽히고 있자니 등이 영락없이 폈다 접혔다 하는구먼!\""], ["he exclaimed, straightening himself slowly with an excruciated look till quite upright. “And you, maidy Tess, you wasn’t well a day or two ago—this will make your head ache finely!", "그가 더없이 괴로운 표정으로 천천히 몸을 펴 곧추서며 외쳤다. \"그리고 자네, 아가씨 테스, 자네는 하루 이틀 전에 몸이 안 좋았잖아—이러다간 머리가 단단히 아플 거야!"], ["Don’t do any more, if you feel fainty; leave the rest to finish it.”", "어지럽거든 더는 하지 말게. 나머지는 다른 사람들한테 끝내라고 맡겨.\""]], [["Dairyman Crick withdrew, and Tess dropped behind.", "낙농장 주인 크릭이 물러섰고, 테스도 뒤로 처졌다."], ["Mr Clare also stepped out of line, and began privateering about for the weed.", "클레어 씨도 줄에서 빠져나와 잡초를 찾아 따로 돌아다니기 시작했다."], ["When she found him near her, her very tension at what she had heard the night before made her the first to speak.", "그가 자기 가까이에 있는 것을 보자, 전날 밤 들은 이야기 탓에 잔뜩 긴장한 그녀가 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“Don’t they look pretty?” she said.", "\"저 애들 예쁘지 않아요?\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Who?”", "\"누가요?\""]], [["“Izzy Huett and Retty.”", "\"이즈 휴엣하고 레티요.\""]], [["Tess had moodily decided that either of these maidens would make a good farmer’s wife, and that she ought to recommend them, and obscure her own wretched charms.", "테스는 우울하게 결심한 터였다. 이 두 처녀 중 누구라도 좋은 농장주의 아내가 될 거라고, 그러니 자기는 그들을 추천하고 자신의 비참한 매력은 가려야 한다고."]], [["“Pretty? Well, yes—they are pretty girls—fresh looking.", "\"예쁘다고요? 음, 그래요—예쁜 처녀들이지요—생기 있고."], ["I have often thought so.”", "나도 자주 그렇게 생각했어요.\""]], [["“Though, poor dears, prettiness won’t last long!”", "\"하지만, 가엾게도, 예쁜 건 오래가지 않잖아요!\""]], [["“O no, unfortunately.”", "\"오 그래요, 안타깝게도.\""]], [["“They are excellent dairywomen.”", "\"훌륭한 낙농 여인들이에요.\""]], [["“Yes: though not better than you.”", "\"그래요. 그래도 당신보다 낫진 않지요.\""]], [["“They skim better than I.”", "\"저 애들이 저보다 크림을 더 잘 걷어요.\""]], [["“Do they?”", "\"그래요?\""]], [["Clare remained observing them—not without their observing him.", "클레어는 계속 그들을 살펴보았다—그들 역시 그를 살피지 않은 건 아니었다."]], [["“She is colouring up,”", "\"레티가 얼굴을 붉히고 있어요.\""], ["continued Tess heroically. “Who?”", "테스가 갸륵하게 이었다."]], [["“Retty Priddle.”", "\"누가요?\""]], [["“Oh! Why it that?”", "\"레티 프리들이요.\""]], [["“Because you are looking at her.”", "\"오! 왜죠?\" \"당신이 보고 있어서요.\""]], [["Self-sacrificing as her mood might be, Tess could not well go further and cry, “Marry one of them, if you really do want a dairywoman and not a lady; and don’t think of marrying me!”", "아무리 자기를 희생하려는 기분이었다 해도, 테스는 차마 더 나아가 이렇게 외칠 수는 없었다. \"정말로 숙녀 말고 낙농 여인을 원하신다면, 저 애들 중 하나와 결혼하세요. 그리고 저와 결혼할 생각은 마세요!\""], ["She followed Dairyman Crick, and had the mournful satisfaction of seeing that Clare remained behind.", "그녀는 낙농장 주인 크릭을 따라갔고, 클레어가 뒤에 남아 있는 것을 보며 서글픈 만족을 얻었다."]], [["From this day she forced herself to take pains to avoid him—never allowing herself, as formerly, to remain long in his company, even if their juxtaposition were purely accidental.", "이날부터 그녀는 그를 피하려고 애써 힘을 기울였다. 두 사람이 나란히 있게 된 것이 순전히 우연이라 해도, 예전처럼 그와 오래 함께 있도록 자신을 결코 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["She gave the other three every chance.", "그녀는 다른 세 처녀에게 온갖 기회를 주었다."]], [["Tess was woman enough to realize from their avowals to herself that Angel Clare had the honour of all the dairymaids in his keeping, and her perception of his care to avoid compromising the happiness of either in the least degree bred a tender respect in Tess for what she deemed, rightly or wrongly, the self-controlling sense of duty shown by him, a quality which she had never expected to find in one of the opposite sex, and in the absence of which more than one of the simple hearts who were his house-mates might have gone weeping on her pilgrimage.", "테스는, 그들이 자기에게 털어놓은 고백을 통해, 엔젤 클레어가 모든 젖 짜는 처녀들의 명예를 제 손에 맡아 두고 있음을 깨달을 만큼 충분히 여인이었다. 그리고 그가 셋 중 누구의 행복도 조금이라도 위태롭게 하지 않으려 조심하는 것을 알아챘기에, 옳든 그르든 그녀가 그에게서 본 자제하는 의무감에 대해 다정한 존경심이 테스 안에 자라났다. 그것은 그녀가 이성異性 가운데 누구에게서도 찾으리라 기대한 적 없는 자질이었으며, 그것이 없었다면 그의 집 동무인 순박한 마음들 가운데 한 사람 이상이 울며 제 순례 길을 갔을지도 모르는 자질이었다."]], [["# XXIII", "# XXIII"]], [["The hot weather of July had crept upon them unawares, and the atmosphere of the flat vale hung heavy as an opiate over the dairy-folk, the cows, and the trees.", "칠월의 무더운 날씨가 그들도 모르는 새 살그머니 닥쳤고, 평평한 골짜기의 대기는 아편처럼 무겁게 낙농 사람들과 소들과 나무들 위에 드리웠다."], ["Hot steaming rains fell frequently, making the grass where the cows fed yet more rank, and hindering the late hay-making in the other meads.", "김이 무럭무럭 나는 더운 비가 자주 내려, 소들이 풀을 뜯는 풀밭을 더욱 무성하게 했고, 다른 풀밭의 늦은 건초 만들기를 방해했다."]], [["It was Sunday morning; the milking was done; the outdoor milkers had gone home.", "일요일 아침이었다. 젖 짜기는 끝났고, 바깥에서 온 젖 짜는 일꾼들은 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Tess and the other three were dressing themselves rapidly, the whole bevy having agreed to go together to Mellstock Church, which lay some three or four miles distant from the dairy-house.", "테스와 다른 세 처녀는 재빨리 옷을 차려입고 있었다. 처녀들 모두가 낙농장에서 삼사 마일쯤 떨어진 멜스톡 교회에 함께 가기로 뜻을 모은 터였다."], ["She had now been two months at Talbothays, and this was her first excursion.", "그녀가 탤보세이스에 온 지 이제 두 달이 되었고, 이것이 그녀의 첫 나들이였다."]], [["All the preceding afternoon and night heavy thunderstorms had hissed down upon the meads, and washed some of the hay into the river; but this morning the sun shone out all the more brilliantly for the deluge, and the air was balmy and clear.", "전날 오후와 밤 내내 거센 뇌우가 풀밭에 쉭쉭 쏟아져 건초 일부를 강으로 쓸어 넣었다. 그러나 이날 아침에는 그 폭우 덕에 해가 더욱 눈부시게 빛났고, 공기는 향기롭고 맑았다."]], [["The crooked lane leading from their own parish to Mellstock ran along the lowest levels in a portion of its length, and when the girls reached the most depressed spot they found that the result of the rain had been to flood the lane over-shoe to a distance of some fifty yards.", "그들의 교구에서 멜스톡으로 이어지는 구불구불한 오솔길은, 그 일부 구간에서 가장 낮은 지대를 따라 나 있었다. 처녀들이 가장 움푹 꺼진 곳에 이르렀을 때, 비가 내린 결과 오솔길이 신발 위까지 약 오십 야드에 걸쳐 물에 잠겨 있는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["This would have been no serious hindrance on a week-day; they would have clicked through it in their high pattens and boots quite unconcerned; but on this day of vanity, this Sun’s-day, when flesh went forth to coquet with flesh while hypocritically affecting business with spiritual things; on this occasion for wearing their white stockings and thin shoes, and their pink, white, and lilac gowns, on which every mud spot would be visible, the pool was an awkward impediment.", "평일이라면 이것은 심각한 방해가 아니었을 것이다. 높은 나막신과 부츠로 아무 거리낌 없이 철벅철벅 지나갔을 테니까. 그러나 이 허영의 날, 이 주님의 날에는—영적인 일에 마음 쓰는 척 위선을 부리면서도 육신이 육신과 교태를 부리러 나서는 이 날에는—흰 양말과 얇은 신발과, 진흙 한 점만 묻어도 다 보이는 분홍색, 흰색, 연보라색 가운을 차려입는 이 날에는, 그 물웅덩이가 거북한 장애물이었다."], ["They could hear the church-bell calling—as yet nearly a mile off.", "그들은 교회 종이 부르는 소리를 들을 수 있었다—아직 거의 1마일이나 떨어진 곳이었다."]], [["“Who would have expected such a rise in the river in summer-time!”", "\"여름철에 강물이 이렇게 불어날 줄 누가 생각했겠어!\""], ["said Marian, from the top of the roadside bank on which they had climbed, and were maintaining a precarious footing in the hope of creeping along its slope till they were past the pool.", "매리언이, 그들이 기어올라 가 물웅덩이를 지날 때까지 그 비탈을 따라 살금살금 가 보려는 심산으로 위태로운 발판을 디디고 있던, 길가 둑 꼭대기에서 말했다."]], [["“We can’t get there anyhow, without walking right through it, or else going round the Turnpike way; and that would make us so very late!”", "\"아무래도 물을 곧장 가로질러 걷거나, 아니면 통행료 받는 큰길로 빙 돌아가지 않고는 거기까지 못 가겠어. 그러면 우리 너무너무 늦을 텐데!\""], ["said Retty, pausing hopelessly.", "레티가 가망 없다는 듯 멈춰 서며 말했다."]], [["“And I do colour up so hot, walking into church late, and all the people staring round,”", "\"그리고 나는 교회에 늦게 들어가면 얼굴이 어찌나 화끈 달아오르는지, 사람들이 다 돌아보고 쳐다보면, '주여 어여삐 여기소서'"], ["said Marian, “that I hardly cool down again till we get into the That-it-may-please-Thees.”", "하는 데까지 가서야 겨우 다시 식는단 말이야.\" 매리언이 말했다."]], [["While they stood clinging to the bank they heard a splashing round the bend of the road, and presently appeared Angel Clare, advancing along the lane towards them through the water.", "그들이 둑에 매달려 서 있을 때, 길모퉁이를 돌아 첨벙거리는 소리가 들렸고, 이윽고 엔젤 클레어가 물을 헤치며 오솔길을 따라 그들 쪽으로 다가오는 게 보였다."]], [["Four hearts gave a big throb simultaneously.", "네 사람의 가슴이 동시에 크게 두근거렸다."]], [["His aspect was probably as un-Sabbatarian a one as a dogmatic parson’s son often presented; his attire being his dairy clothes, long wading boots, a cabbage-leaf inside his hat to keep his head cool, with a thistle-spud to finish him off.", "그의 차림새는, 교조적인 목사의 아들이 흔히 보이는 것 중에서도 아마 가장 안식일답지 않은 것이었으리라. 그의 옷은 낙농 작업복이었고, 긴 장화에, 머리를 시원하게 하려고 모자 안에 양배추 잎을 댔으며, 엉겅퀴 캐는 호미까지 들어 마무리를 지었다."], ["“He’s not going to church,”", "\"교회에 가시는 게 아니야.\""], ["said Marian.", "매리언이 말했다."]], [["“No—I wish he was!”", "\"안 가셔—가시면 좋을 텐데!\""], ["murmured Tess.", "테스가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Angel, in fact, rightly or wrongly (to adopt the safe phrase of evasive controversialists), preferred sermons in stones to sermons in churches and chapels on fine summer days.", "사실 엔젤은, 옳든 그르든 (얼버무리는 논쟁가들이 쓰는 안전한 표현을 빌리자면), 맑은 여름날에는 교회와 예배당의 설교보다 돌멩이 속의 설교를 더 좋아했다."], ["This morning, moreover, he had gone out to see if the damage to the hay by the flood was considerable or not.", "게다가 이날 아침에는 홍수로 인한 건초 피해가 큰지 어떤지 보러 나온 길이었다."], ["On his walk he observed the girls from a long distance, though they had been so occupied with their difficulties of passage as not to notice him.", "길을 걷다가 그는 멀리서 처녀들을 보았는데, 그들은 길을 건너기가 어려워 정신이 팔린 나머지 그를 알아채지 못했다."], ["He knew that the water had risen at that spot, and that it would quite check their progress.", "그는 그곳에서 물이 불었다는 것을, 그래서 그들의 발길을 완전히 막아 버리리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["So he had hastened on, with a dim idea of how he could help them—one of them in particular.", "그래서 그는, 어떻게 그들을—특히 그중 한 사람을—도울 수 있을지 어렴풋이 생각하며 서둘러 왔다."]], [["The rosy-cheeked, bright-eyed quartet looked so charming in their light summer attire, clinging to the roadside bank like pigeons on a roof-slope, that he stopped a moment to regard them before coming close.", "장밋빛 뺨에 빛나는 눈을 한 네 사람은, 가벼운 여름 차림으로 지붕 비탈의 비둘기처럼 길가 둑에 매달려 어찌나 사랑스러워 보였던지, 그는 가까이 가기 전에 잠시 멈춰 그들을 바라보았다."], ["Their gauzy skirts had brushed up from the grass innumerable flies and butterflies which, unable to escape, remained caged in the transparent tissue as in an aviary.", "그들의 얇은 치마가 풀밭에서 수많은 파리와 나비를 쓸어 올렸는데, 빠져나가지 못한 그것들은 마치 새장 속 새처럼 그 투명한 천 속에 갇혀 있었다."], ["Angel’s eye at last fell upon Tess, the hindmost of the four; she, being full of suppressed laughter at their dilemma, could not help meeting his glance radiantly.", "엔젤의 눈길은 마침내 넷 중 맨 뒤의 테스에게 떨어졌다. 그들의 곤경에 터질 듯한 웃음을 누르고 있던 그녀는, 환하게 빛나는 얼굴로 그의 눈길을 마주하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["He came beneath them in the water, which did not rise over his long boots; and stood looking at the entrapped flies and butterflies.", "그는 자기 긴 장화 위로는 차오르지 않는 물속에서 그들 아래에 이르렀고, 갇힌 파리와 나비들을 바라보며 서 있었다."]], [["“Are you trying to get to church?”", "\"교회에 가려는 거예요?\""], ["he said to Marian, who was in front, including the next two in his remark, but avoiding Tess.", "그가 맨 앞에 있는 매리언에게 말했는데, 그다음 두 사람까지 그 말에 포함시키면서도 테스만은 피했다."]], [["“Yes, sir; and ’tis getting late; and my colour do come up so—”", "\"네, 선생님. 그런데 늦어지고 있어요. 게다가 제 얼굴이 어찌나 달아오르는지—\""]], [["“I’ll carry you through the pool—every Jill of you.”", "\"내가 물웅덩이를 건너게 해 드릴게요—한 사람도 빠짐없이 다요.\""]], [["The whole four flushed as if one heart beat through them.", "네 사람 모두, 마치 한 가슴이 그들을 관통해 뛰는 듯 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“I think you can’t, sir,”", "\"그건 안 되실 것 같은데요, 선생님.\""], ["said Marian.", "매리언이 말했다."]], [["“It is the only way for you to get past.", "\"이게 당신들이 지나갈 유일한 방법이에요."], ["Stand still.", "가만히 서요."], ["Nonsense—you are not too heavy!", "말도 안 되는 소리—당신은 그렇게 무겁지 않아요!"], ["I’d carry you all four together.", "넷을 한꺼번에 다 안고도 갈 수 있어요."], ["Now, Marian, attend,”", "자, 매리언, 잘 들어요.\""], ["he continued, “and put your arms round my shoulders, so. Now! Hold on.", "그가 이었다. \"팔을 내 어깨에 이렇게 두르세요. 자!"], ["That’s well done.”", "꽉 잡아요. 잘했어요.\""]], [["Marian had lowered herself upon his arm and shoulder as directed, and Angel strode off with her, his slim figure, as viewed from behind, looking like the mere stem to the great nosegay suggested by hers.", "매리언은 시키는 대로 그의 팔과 어깨에 몸을 실었고, 엔젤은 그녀를 안고 성큼성큼 걸어갔다. 뒤에서 보니 그의 호리호리한 몸은 그녀가 떠오르게 하는 커다란 꽃다발에 달린 한낱 줄기 같았다."], ["They disappeared round the curve of the road, and only his sousing footsteps and the top ribbon of Marian’s bonnet told where they were.", "그들은 길굽이를 돌아 사라졌고, 다만 그의 첨벙거리는 발소리와 매리언의 보닛 맨 위 리본만이 그들이 어디 있는지 알려 주었다."], ["In a few minutes he reappeared.", "몇 분 뒤 그가 다시 나타났다."], ["Izz Huett was the next in order upon the bank.", "둑 위에서 다음 차례는 이즈 휴엣이었다."]], [["“Here he comes,”", "\"저기 오신다.\""], ["she murmured, and they could hear that her lips were dry with emotion.", "그녀가 중얼거렸는데, 감정에 입술이 바싹 마른 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["“And I have to put my arms round his neck and look into his face as Marian did.”", "\"이제 나도 매리언처럼 그분 목에 팔을 두르고 그 얼굴을 들여다봐야 해.\""]], [["“There’s nothing in that,”", "\"그게 뭐 별거라고.\""], ["said Tess quickly.", "테스가 재빨리 말했다."]], [["“There’s a time for everything,”", "\"모든 일에는 다 때가 있는 법이지.\""], ["continued Izz, unheeding.", "이즈가 아랑곳없이 이었다."], ["“A time to embrace, and a time to refrain from embracing; the first is now going to be mine.”", "\"껴안을 때가 있고, 껴안기를 삼갈 때가 있나니. 이제 첫 번째 것이 내 차례가 되겠네.\""]], [["“Fie—it is Scripture, Izz!”", "\"어머—그건 성경 말씀이잖아, 이즈!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Izz, “I’ve always a’ ear at church for pretty verses.”", "이즈가 말했다. \"나는 교회에서 늘 예쁜 구절에는 귀가 솔깃하거든.\""]], [["Angel Clare, to whom three-quarters of this performance was a commonplace act of kindness, now approached Izz.", "이 일의 사분의 삼이 그저 평범한 친절 행위였던 엔젤 클레어가 이제 이즈에게 다가왔다."], ["She quietly and dreamily lowered herself into his arms, and Angel methodically marched off with her.", "그녀는 조용히, 꿈꾸듯 그의 팔에 몸을 실었고, 엔젤은 그녀를 안고 차근차근 걸어갔다."], ["When he was heard returning for the third time Retty’s throbbing heart could be almost seen to shake her.", "그가 세 번째로 돌아오는 소리가 들렸을 때, 레티의 두근대는 심장은 그녀를 흔들어 대는 것이 거의 눈에 보일 지경이었다."], ["He went up to the red-haired girl, and while he was seizing her he glanced at Tess.", "그가 붉은 머리 처녀에게 다가가, 그녀를 안아 올리면서 테스를 흘끗 보았다."], ["His lips could not have pronounced more plainly, “It will soon be you and I.”", "그의 입술은 이보다 더 또렷이 말할 수 없었으리라. \"이제 곧 당신과 나 차례요.\""], ["Her comprehension appeared in her face; she could not help it.", "그녀의 얼굴에 알아들었다는 기색이 떠올랐다. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다."], ["There was an understanding between them.", "두 사람 사이에는 어떤 이해가 자리 잡았다."]], [["Poor little Retty, though by far the lightest weight, was the most troublesome of Clare’s burdens.", "가엾고 자그마한 레티는, 단연 가장 가벼운 무게였는데도, 클레어가 안은 짐 중에서 가장 성가신 짐이었다."], ["Marian had been like a sack of meal, a dead weight of plumpness under which he has literally staggered.", "매리언은 곡식 자루 같았다. 통통함이 죽은 무게처럼 짓눌러, 그는 정말로 비틀거렸었다."], ["Izz had ridden sensibly and calmly.", "이즈는 분별 있게 차분히 안겨 갔다."], ["Retty was a bunch of hysterics.", "레티는 한 다발의 히스테리였다."], ["However, he got through with the disquieted creature, deposited her, and returned.", "그래도 그는 그 안절부절못하는 가엾은 것을 데리고 무사히 건너 그녀를 내려놓고는 돌아왔다."], ["Tess could see over the hedge the distant three in a group, standing as he had placed them on the next rising ground.", "테스는 산울타리 너머로, 그가 다음 솟은 땅에 세워 둔 그대로 한 무리를 이루고 서 있는 멀리 있는 세 사람을 볼 수 있었다."], ["It was now her turn.", "이제 그녀의 차례였다."], ["She was embarrassed to discover that excitement at the proximity of Mr Clare’s breath and eyes, which she had contemned in her companions, was intensified in herself; and as if fearful of betraying her secret, she paltered with him at the last moment.", "동료들에게서 보고는 경멸했던 것, 곧 클레어 씨의 숨결과 눈길이 가까이 다가올 때의 흥분이 자기 안에서는 한층 격해져 있음을 알아채고 그녀는 당황했다. 그리고 자기 비밀을 들킬까 두려운 듯, 마지막 순간에 그에게 머뭇거리며 핑계를 댔다."]], [["“I may be able to clim’ along the bank perhaps—I can clim’ better than they.", "\"어쩌면 제가 둑을 따라 기어갈 수 있을지도 몰라요—제가 저 애들보다 더 잘 기어요."], ["You must be so tired, Mr Clare!”", "분명 무척 피곤하실 텐데요, 클레어 씨!\""]], [["“No, no, Tess,” said he quickly.", "\"아니, 아니에요, 테스.\""], ["And almost before she was aware, she was seated in his arms and resting against his shoulder.", "그가 재빨리 말했다. 그리고 그녀가 채 알아차리기도 전에, 그녀는 그의 팔에 안겨 그의 어깨에 기대어 있었다."]], [["“Three Leahs to get one Rachel,”", "\"라헬 하나를 얻으려고 레아 셋을 거쳤군요.\""], ["he whispered.", "그가 속삭였다."]], [["“They are better women than I,”", "\"저 애들이 저보다 나은 여자들이에요.\""], ["she replied, magnanimously sticking to her resolve.", "그녀가 너그럽게 자기 결심을 지키며 대답했다."]], [["“Not to me,”", "\"내겐 아니에요.\""], ["said Angel.", "엔젤이 말했다."]], [["He saw her grow warm at this; and they went some steps in silence.", "그가 이 말에 그녀가 얼굴 붉히는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그들은 말없이 몇 걸음을 갔다."]], [["“I hope I am not too heavy?” she said timidly.", "\"제가 너무 무겁진 않죠?\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“O no. You should lift Marian!", "\"오 아니에요. 매리언을 들어 봐야 해요!"], ["Such a lump.", "그런 덩어리를."], ["You are like an undulating billow warmed by the sun.", "당신은 햇볕에 데워진 굽이치는 물결 같아요."], ["And all this fluff of muslin about you is the froth.”", "그리고 당신을 감싼 이 모슬린의 보드라움은 다 그 물거품이고요.\""]], [["“It is very pretty—if I seem like that to you.”", "\"참 예쁜 말이네요—제가 당신께 그렇게 보인다면요.\""]], [["“Do you know that I have undergone three-quarters of this labour entirely for the sake of the fourth quarter?”", "\"내가 이 수고의 사분의 삼을 오직 그 마지막 사분의 일을 위해 견뎌 냈다는 걸 아세요?\" \"아니요.\""]], [["“No.” “I did not expect such an event to-day.”", "\"오늘 이런 일이 일어날 줄은 몰랐어요.\""]], [["“Nor I...", "\"저도요..."], ["The water came up so sudden.”", "물이 너무 갑자기 불어서요.\""]], [["That the rise in the water was what she understood him to refer to, the state of breathing belied.", "물이 불어난 것이 그가 가리키는 바라고 그녀가 알아들었다는 듯한 그 말투를, 그녀의 숨결 상태가 배반하고 있었다."], ["Clare stood still and inclinced his face towards hers.", "클레어는 가만히 멈춰 서서 그녀에게로 얼굴을 기울였다."]], [["“O Tessy!”", "\"오 테시!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["The girl’s cheeks burned to the breeze, and she could not look into his eyes for her emotion.", "처녀의 뺨이 산들바람에 화끈 달아올랐고, 그녀는 격한 감정에 차마 그의 눈을 들여다볼 수 없었다."], ["It reminded Angel that he was somewhat unfairly taking advantage of an accidental position; and he went no further with it.", "그것은 엔젤에게, 자기가 우연히 생긴 처지를 다소 부당하게 이용하고 있음을 일깨웠다. 그래서 그는 더는 나아가지 않았다."], ["No definite words of love had crossed their lips as yet, and suspension at this point was desirable now.", "사랑을 말하는 분명한 말이 아직 두 사람의 입술을 오간 적이 없었으니, 이 지점에서 멈추는 것이 지금은 바람직했다."], ["However, he walked slowly, to make the remainder of the distance as long as possible; but at last they came to the bend, and the rest of their progress was in full view of the other three.", "그래도 그는 남은 거리를 되도록 길게 늘이려고 천천히 걸었다. 그러나 마침내 그들은 길굽이에 이르렀고, 그 뒤로 나아가는 길은 다른 세 사람의 눈에 훤히 드러났다."], ["The dry land was reached, and he set her down.", "마른 땅에 닿았고, 그는 그녀를 내려놓았다."]], [["Her friends were looking with round thoughtful eyes at her and him, and she could see that they had been talking of her.", "그녀의 벗들은 그녀와 그를 동그랗게 뜬, 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 바라보고 있었고, 그녀는 그들이 자기 이야기를 하고 있었음을 알 수 있었다."], ["He hastily bade them farewell, and splashed back along the stretch of submerged road.", "그는 서둘러 그들에게 작별을 고하고는, 물에 잠긴 길을 따라 첨벙거리며 되돌아갔다."]], [["The four moved on together as before, till Marian broke the silence by saying—", "네 사람은 아까처럼 함께 걸어갔는데, 마침내 매리언이 침묵을 깨고 말했다."]], [["“No—in all truth; we have no chance against her!”", "\"안 돼—정말이지, 우리는 쟤를 당해 낼 가망이 없어!\""], ["She looked joylessly at Tess.", "그녀는 기쁨 없는 눈으로 테스를 바라보았다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소리야?\""], ["asked the latter.", "테스가 물었다."]], [["“He likes ’ee best—the very best!", "\"그분은 너를 제일 좋아해—정말로 제일!"], ["We could see it as he brought ’ee.", "너를 안고 갈 때 우리도 다 봤어."], ["He would have kissed ’ee, if you had encouraged him to do it, ever so little.”", "네가 조금이라도 부추겼더라면 그분은 너한테 입 맞췄을 거야.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아냐, 아냐.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The gaiety with which they had set out had somehow vanished; and yet there was no enmity or malice between them.", "그들이 길을 나설 때의 명랑함은 어쩐지 사라져 버렸다. 그래도 그들 사이에 적의나 악의는 없었다."], ["They were generous young souls; they had been reared in the lonely country nooks where fatalism is a strong sentiment, and they did not blame her.", "그들은 너그러운 젊은 영혼들이었다. 운명론이 강한 정서로 자리 잡은 외딴 시골 구석에서 자랐기에, 그들은 그녀를 탓하지 않았다."], ["Such supplanting was to be.", "그렇게 밀려나는 일은 일어나기 마련이었다."]], [["Tess’s heart ached.", "테스의 마음은 아팠다."], ["There was no concealing from herself the fact that she loved Angel Clare, perhaps all the more passionately from knowing that the others had also lost their hearts to him.", "자기가 엔젤 클레어를 사랑한다는 사실을 스스로에게 숨길 수 없었다. 어쩌면 다른 이들도 그에게 마음을 빼앗겼다는 것을 알기에 더욱 격정적으로 사랑하는 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["There is contagion in this sentiment, especially among women.", "이런 정서에는 전염성이 있다, 특히 여자들 사이에서는."], ["And yet that same hungry nature had fought against this, but too feebly, and the natural result had followed.", "그런데도 그 똑같이 굶주린 천성이 이에 맞서 싸웠으나, 너무도 미약하게 싸웠기에, 자연스러운 결과가 뒤따랐다."]], [["“I will never stand in your way, nor in the way of either of you!”", "\"나는 결코 네 앞길을, 너희 누구의 앞길도 막지 않을 거야!\""], ["she declared to Retty that night in the bedroom (her tears running down).", "그날 밤 침실에서 그녀가 레티에게 단언했다 (그녀의 눈물이 흘러내렸다)."], ["“I can’t help this, my dear!", "\"나도 어쩔 수가 없는걸, 얘!"], ["I don’t think marrying is in his mind at all; but if he were ever to ask me I should refuse him, as I should refuse any man.”", "그분 마음에 결혼 생각 따위는 전혀 없는 것 같아. 하지만 설령 그분이 내게 청혼한다 해도 나는 거절할 거야, 어떤 남자라도 거절할 것처럼.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["would you? Why?”", "거절하겠다고? 왜?\""], ["said wondering Retty.", "레티가 의아해하며 말했다."]], [["“It cannot be!", "\"그럴 수밖에 없어!"], ["But I will be plain.", "하지만 분명히 말할게."], ["Putting myself quite on one side, I don’t think he will choose either of you.”", "나를 완전히 빼놓고 보면, 그분은 너희 중 누구도 고르지 않을 것 같아.\""]], [["“I have never expected it—thought of it!”", "\"난 한 번도 그걸 바란 적 없어—생각해 본 적도 없어!\""], ["moaned Retty.", "레티가 흐느꼈다."], ["“But O!", "\"하지만 오!"], ["I wish I was dead!”", "차라리 죽었으면 좋겠어!\""]], [["The poor child, torn by a feeling which she hardly understood, turned to the other two girls who came upstairs just then.", "스스로도 거의 이해 못 할 감정에 시달리던 그 가엾은 아이는, 마침 그때 위층으로 올라온 다른 두 처녀에게로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“We be friends with her again,”", "\"우린 쟤랑 다시 친구야.\""], ["she said to them.", "그녀가 그들에게 말했다."], ["“She thinks no more of his choosing her than we do.”", "\"쟤도 우리만큼이나 그분이 자기를 고르리라고는 생각 안 한대.\""]], [["So the reserve went off, and they were confiding and warm.", "그렇게 거리낌이 사라지고, 그들은 서로 마음을 터놓고 따뜻해졌다."]], [["“I don’t seem to care what I do now,”", "\"나는 이제 내가 뭘 하든 상관없을 것 같아.\""], ["said Marian, whose mood was turned to its lowest bass.", "매리언이 말했는데, 그녀의 기분은 가장 낮은 저음으로 가라앉아 있었다."], ["“I was going to marry a dairyman at Stickleford, who’s asked me twice; but—my soul—I would put an end to myself rather’n be his wife now!", "\"나한테 두 번이나 청혼한 스티클퍼드의 어느 낙농 일꾼하고 결혼하려던 참이었어. 하지만—맙소사—이제는 그 사람 아내가 되느니 차라리 내 목숨을 끊겠어!"], ["Why don’t ye speak, Izz?”", "너는 왜 말이 없니, 이즈?\""]], [["“To confess, then,”", "\"솔직히 말하자면,\""], ["murmured Izz, “I made sure to-day that he was going to kiss me as he held me; and I lay still against his breast, hoping and hoping, and never moved at all.", "이즈가 중얼거렸다. \"오늘 그분이 나를 안았을 때 나한테 입 맞추실 거라고 확신했어. 그래서 나는 그분 가슴에 가만히 기대어 바라고 또 바라며 조금도 움직이지 않았지."], ["But he did not. I don’t like biding here at Talbothays any longer!", "하지만 그분은 안 그러셨어. 나는 더는 여기 탤보세이스에 머물고 싶지 않아!"], ["I shall go hwome.”", "집에 가 버릴 거야.\""]], [["The air of the sleeping-chamber seemed to palpitate with the hopeless passion of the girls.", "침실의 공기는 처녀들의 가망 없는 정념으로 두근거리는 듯했다."], ["They writhed feverishly under the oppressiveness of an emotion thrust on them by cruel Nature’s law—an emotion which they had neither expected nor desired.", "그들은 잔인한 자연의 법칙이 떠안긴 감정의 짓누름 아래 열에 들떠 몸부림쳤다—기대하지도 바라지도 않았던 감정이었다."], ["The incident of the day had fanned the flame that was burning the inside of their hearts out, and the torture was almost more than they could endure.", "그날의 사건은 그들의 가슴속을 태우는 불길에 부채질을 했고, 그 고통은 거의 견딜 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["The differences which distinguished them as individuals were abstracted by this passion, and each was but portion of one organism called sex.", "그들을 개인으로 구별 짓던 차이들은 이 정념에 의해 추상되어 사라졌고, 저마다 성性이라 불리는 한 유기체의 일부일 뿐이었다."], ["There was so much frankness and so little jealousy because there was no hope.", "거기엔 그토록 솔직함이 많고 질투가 적었는데, 그것은 희망이 없었기 때문이다."], ["Each one was a girl of fair common sense, and she did not delude herself with any vain conceits, or deny her love, or give herself airs, in the idea of outshining the others.", "저마다 어지간히 분별 있는 처녀였기에, 헛된 자만으로 자신을 속이거나, 제 사랑을 부인하거나, 남들을 누르겠다는 생각으로 잘난 체하지 않았다."], ["The full recognition of the futility of their infatuation, from a social point of view; its purposeless beginning; its self-bounded outlook; its lack of everything to justify its existence in the eye of civilization (while lacking nothing in the eye of Nature); the one fact that it did exist, ecstasizing them to a killing joy—all this imparted to them a resignation, a dignity, which a practical and sordid expectation of winning him as a husband would have destroyed.", "사회적 관점에서 본 그들 도취의 부질없음을 온전히 깨달은 것, 그 목적 없는 시작, 제 안에 갇힌 전망, 문명의 눈에 그 존재를 정당화할 그 무엇도 없다는 것 (자연의 눈에는 모자란 것이 하나도 없으나), 그것이 존재한다는 단 하나의 사실, 그들을 죽을 듯한 기쁨으로 황홀케 하는 그 사실—이 모든 것이 그들에게 일종의 체념을, 일종의 위엄을 안겨 주었으니, 그것은 그를 남편으로 얻겠다는 현실적이고 속된 기대였다면 부수어 버렸을 것이었다."]], [["They tossed and turned on their little beds, and the cheese-wring dripped monotonously downstairs.", "그들은 작은 침대에서 엎치락뒤치락했고, 아래층에서는 치즈 짜는 틀이 단조롭게 물을 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["“B’ you awake, Tess?”", "\"테스, 너 깨어 있니?\""], ["whispered one, half-an-hour later.", "반 시간쯤 뒤에 누군가 속삭였다."]], [["It was Izz Huett’s voice.", "이즈 휴엣의 목소리였다."]], [["Tess replied in the affirmative, whereupon also Retty and Marian suddenly flung the bedclothes off them, and sighed—", "테스가 그렇다고 대답하자, 레티와 매리언도 갑자기 이불을 박차고 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["“So be we!”", "\"우리도 그래!\""]], [["“I wonder what she is like—the lady they say his family have looked out for him!”", "\"어떤 여자일까—그분 집안에서 그분한테 점찍어 뒀다는 그 숙녀 말이야!\""]], [["“I wonder,”", "\"궁금하네.\""], ["said Izz.", "이즈가 말했다."]], [["“Some lady looked out for him?”", "\"무슨 숙녀를 그분한테 점찍어 뒀다고?\""], ["gasped Tess, starting.", "테스가 흠칫 놀라 숨을 들이켰다."], ["“I have never heard o’ that!”", "\"난 그런 얘기 한 번도 못 들었어!\""]], [["“O yes—’tis whispered; a young lady of his own rank, chosen by his family; a Doctor of Divinity’s daughter near his father’s parish of Emminster; he don’t much care for her, they say.", "\"오 그래—다들 수군대던걸. 그분 집안에서 고른, 그분과 같은 신분의 젊은 숙녀래. 그분 아버지의 교구인 에민스터 근처에 사는 어느 신학박사의 딸이라더라."], ["But he is sure to marry her.”", "그분은 그 숙녀를 그리 마음에 두지 않는다지만. 그래도 분명히 그 여자랑 결혼하실 거래.\""]], [["They had heard so very little of this; yet it was enough to build up wretched dolorous dreams upon, there in the shade of the night.", "이 일에 대해 그들이 들은 것은 정말 얼마 안 되었다. 그래도 밤의 그늘 속에서 비참하고 애달픈 꿈을 쌓아 올리기엔 충분했다."], ["They pictured all the details of his being won round to consent, of the wedding preparations, of the bride’s happiness, of her dress and veil, of her blissful home with him, when oblivion would have fallen upon themselves as far as he and their love were concerned.", "그들은 그가 마음을 돌려 승낙하게 되는 일의 온갖 세부를, 결혼 준비를, 신부의 행복을, 그 옷과 면사포를, 그와 함께할 그녀의 더없이 행복한 가정을 그려 보았다. 그가 그들의 사랑에 관한 한 그들 자신에게 망각이 내려앉을 그때를."], ["Thus they talked, and ached, and wept till sleep charmed their sorrow away.", "그렇게 그들은 이야기하고, 아파하고, 잠이 그 슬픔을 흘려 보내 줄 때까지 울었다."]], [["After this disclosure Tess nourished no further foolish thought that there lurked any grave and deliberate import in Clare’s attentions to her.", "이 사실이 드러난 뒤로 테스는, 클레어가 자기에게 베푸는 관심에 무언가 진지하고 의도된 뜻이 숨어 있으리라는 어리석은 생각을 더는 품지 않았다."], ["It was a passing summer love of her face, for love’s own temporary sake—nothing more.", "그것은 사랑 그 자체의 한때를 위한, 그녀의 얼굴을 향한 스쳐 가는 여름 사랑일 뿐—그 이상은 아니었다."], ["And the thorny crown of this sad conception was that she whom he really did prefer in a cursory way to the rest, she who knew herself to be more impassioned in nature, cleverer, more beautiful than they, was in the eyes of propriety far less worthy of him than the homelier ones whom he ignored.", "그리고 이 슬픈 깨달음의 가시 면류관은 이런 것이었다. 그가 나머지 사람들보다 지나가듯이나마 정말로 더 좋아한 그녀, 자기 천성이 그들보다 더 격정적이고 더 영리하며 더 아름답다는 것을 스스로 아는 그녀가, 점잖음의 눈으로 보면 그가 외면하는 더 수수한 처녀들보다 그에게 훨씬 덜 어울린다는 것이었다."]], [["# XXIV", "# XXIV"]], [["Amid the oozing fatness and warm ferments of the Froom Vale, at a season when the rush of juices could almost be heard below the hiss of fertilization, it was impossible that the most fanciful love should not grow passionate.", "프룸 골짜기의 줄줄 흐르는 비옥함과 따뜻한 발효 속에서, 수정受精의 쉭쉭거리는 소리 아래로 수액이 솟구치는 소리가 거의 들릴 듯한 이 계절에, 제아무리 공상적인 사랑이라도 격정으로 자라지 않기란 불가능했다."], ["The ready bosoms existing there were impregnated by their surroundings.", "거기 자리한 채비된 가슴들은 주위 환경에 의해 잉태되었다."]], [["July passed over their heads, and the Thermidorean weather which came in its wake seemed an effort on the part of Nature to match the state of hearts at Talbothays Dairy.", "칠월이 그들의 머리 위로 지나갔고, 그 뒤를 따라온 테르미도르 같은 무더위는 마치 자연이 탤보세이스 낙농장의 마음들 상태에 맞추려는 노력 같았다."], ["The air of the place, so fresh in the spring and early summer, was stagnant and enervating now.", "봄과 초여름에는 그토록 신선하던 이곳의 공기가 이제는 정체되고 맥 빠지게 했다."], ["Its heavy scents weighed upon them, and at mid-day the landscape seemed lying in a swoon.", "그 무거운 향기가 그들을 짓눌렀고, 한낮이면 풍경이 혼절해 누운 듯했다."], ["Ethiopic scorchings browned the upper slopes of the pastures, but there was still bright green herbage here where the watercourses purled.", "에티오피아 같은 불볕더위가 목초지의 위쪽 비탈을 누렇게 태웠으나, 물줄기가 졸졸 흐르는 이곳에는 아직 밝은 초록빛 풀이 있었다."], ["And as Clare was oppressed by the outward heats, so was he burdened inwardly by waxing fervour of passion for the soft and silent Tess.", "그리고 클레어가 바깥의 더위에 짓눌렸듯이, 그는 안으로도 부드럽고 말 없는 테스를 향해 점점 커 가는 정념의 열기에 짓눌렸다."]], [["The rains having passed, the uplands were dry.", "비가 그치니 고지대는 메말랐다."], ["The wheels of the dairyman’s spring-cart, as he sped home from market, licked up the pulverized surface of the highway, and were followed by white ribands of dust, as if they had set a thin powder-train on fire.", "낙농장 주인이 장에서 집으로 달려오는 용수철 마차의 바퀴는 큰길의 가루가 된 표면을 핥아 올렸고, 그 뒤로는 흰 먼지 띠가 따라붙어, 마치 얇은 화약 도화선에 불을 붙인 것 같았다."], ["The cows jumped wildly over the five-barred barton-gate, maddened by the gad-fly; Dairyman Crick kept his shirt-sleeves permanently rolled up from Monday to Saturday; open windows had no effect in ventilation without open doors, and in the dairy-garden the blackbirds and thrushes crept about under the currant-bushes, rather in the manner of quadrupeds than of winged creatures.", "소들은 등에에 미쳐 날뛰며 다섯 단짜리 가축마당 문을 사납게 뛰어넘었다. 낙농장 주인 크릭은 월요일부터 토요일까지 셔츠 소매를 줄곧 걷어붙이고 있었다. 열린 창문은 문이 열려 있지 않으면 환기에 아무 소용이 없었고, 낙농장 정원에서는 검은새와 개똥지빠귀가 까치밥나무 덤불 아래를 기어 다니는데, 날개 달린 짐승이라기보다는 네발짐승에 가까운 모양새였다."], ["The flies in the kitchen were lazy, teasing, and familiar, crawling about in the unwonted places, on the floors, into drawers, and over the backs of the milkmaids’ hands.", "부엌의 파리들은 게으르고 성가시고 친근하게, 익숙지 않은 곳들—바닥이며 서랍 속, 젖 짜는 처녀들의 손등 위—을 기어 다녔다."], ["Conversations were concerning sunstroke; while butter-making, and still more butter-keeping, was a despair.", "대화는 일사병에 관한 것이었고, 버터 만들기는, 더더욱 버터 보관하기는, 절망이었다."]], [["They milked entirely in the meads for coolness and convenience, without driving in the cows.", "그들은 시원함과 편리함을 위해 소를 몰아넣지 않고 풀밭에서 통째로 젖을 짰다."], ["During the day the animals obsequiously followed the shadow of the smallest tree as it moved round the stem with the diurnal roll; and when the milkers came they could hardly stand still for the flies.", "낮 동안 짐승들은, 가장 작은 나무의 그림자가 하루의 운행을 따라 줄기를 빙 돌며 옮겨 가면 굽실거리듯 그 그림자를 따라다녔다. 그리고 젖 짜는 이들이 오면 짐승들은 파리 때문에 가만히 서 있지를 못했다."]], [["On one of these afternoons four or five unmilked cows chanced to stand apart from the general herd, behind the corner of a hedge, among them being Dumpling and Old Pretty, who loved Tess’s hands above those of any other maid.", "이런 어느 오후, 젖을 다 짜지 않은 소 네댓 마리가 마침 전체 무리에서 떨어져 산울타리 모퉁이 뒤에 서 있었는데, 그중에는 덤플링과 올드 프리티가 있었다. 이 둘은 다른 어느 처녀의 손보다 테스의 손을 좋아했다."], ["When she rose from her stool under a finished cow, Angel Clare, who had been observing her for some time, asked her if she would take the aforesaid creatures next.", "그녀가 젖 짜기를 끝낸 소 밑의 걸상에서 일어났을 때, 한동안 그녀를 지켜보던 엔젤 클레어가 앞서 말한 짐승들을 다음에 맡아 주겠느냐고 물었다."], ["She silently assented, and with her stool at arm’s length, and the pail against her knee, went round to where they stood.", "그녀는 말없이 응낙하고는, 걸상을 팔 길이만큼 들고 무릎에 양동이를 받친 채, 그것들이 서 있는 곳으로 돌아갔다."], ["Soon the sound of Old Pretty’s milk fizzing into the pail came through the hedge, and then Angel felt inclined to go round the corner also, to finish off a hard-yielding milcher who had strayed there, he being now as capable of this as the dairyman himself.", "곧 올드 프리티의 젖이 양동이로 솨아 들어가는 소리가 산울타리 너머로 들려왔고, 그러자 엔젤도 모퉁이를 돌아가, 거기 길을 잃고 들어온, 젖이 잘 안 나오는 소 한 마리를 마저 짜고 싶어졌다. 그는 이제 낙농장 주인 못지않게 이 일을 해낼 수 있었다."]], [["All the men, and some of the women, when milking, dug their foreheads into the cows and gazed into the pail.", "남자들은 모두, 그리고 여자들 일부도, 젖을 짤 때 이마를 소에 처박고 양동이를 들여다보았다."], ["But a few—mainly the younger ones—rested their heads sideways.", "그러나 몇몇은—주로 더 젊은 이들은—머리를 옆으로 기댔다."], ["This was Tess Durbeyfield’s habit, her temple pressing the milcher’s flank, her eyes fixed on the far end of the meadow with the quiet of one lost in meditation.", "이것이 테스 더비필드의 버릇이었으니, 관자놀이를 소의 옆구리에 대고, 눈은 명상에 잠긴 이의 고요함으로 풀밭 저 끝에 둔 채였다."], ["She was milking Old Pretty thus, and the sun chancing to be on the milking-side, it shone flat upon her pink-gowned form and her white curtain-bonnet, and upon her profile, rendering it keen as a cameo cut from the dun background of the cow.", "그녀는 그렇게 올드 프리티의 젖을 짜고 있었고, 마침 해가 젖 짜는 쪽에 있어, 분홍 가운을 입은 그녀의 몸과 흰 커튼 같은 보닛 위로, 그리고 그녀의 옆얼굴 위로 평평하게 비쳐, 그 옆얼굴을 소의 거무스름한 배경에서 깎아 낸 카메오처럼 또렷하게 만들었다."]], [["She did not know that Clare had followed her round, and that he sat under his cow watching her.", "그녀는 클레어가 자기를 따라 돌아왔다는 것도, 그가 자기 소 밑에 앉아 자기를 지켜보고 있다는 것도 알지 못했다."], ["The stillness of her head and features was remarkable: she might have been in a trance, her eyes open, yet unseeing.", "그녀의 머리와 이목구비의 고요함은 놀라웠다. 그녀는 눈을 뜨고도 아무것도 보지 않은 채 황홀경에 든 듯했다."], ["Nothing in the picture moved but Old Pretty’s tail and Tess’s pink hands, the latter so gently as to be a rhythmic pulsation only, as if they were obeying a reflex stimulus, like a beating heart.", "이 그림에서 움직이는 것이라고는 올드 프리티의 꼬리와 테스의 분홍빛 손뿐이었는데, 그 손이 어찌나 부드럽게 움직이는지 그저 율동적인 고동일 뿐이었다. 마치 뛰는 심장처럼, 반사적인 자극에 따르는 것 같았다."]], [["How very lovable her face was to him.", "그에게 그녀의 얼굴은 어찌나 사랑스러웠던가."], ["Yet there was nothing ethereal about it; all was real vitality, real warmth, real incarnation.", "그러나 거기엔 천상적인 데라고는 전혀 없었다. 온통 진짜 생기, 진짜 온기, 진짜 육화肉化였다."], ["And it was in her mouth that this culminated.", "그리고 그것이 절정에 이른 곳이 바로 그녀의 입이었다."], ["Eyes almost as deep and speaking he had seen before, and cheeks perhaps as fair; brows as arched, a chin and throat almost as shapely; her mouth he had seen nothing to equal on the face of the earth.", "거의 그만큼 깊고 말하는 듯한 눈을 그는 전에도 본 적이 있었고, 어쩌면 그만큼 고운 뺨도 본 적이 있었다. 그만큼 활처럼 휜 눈썹도, 거의 그만큼 맵시 있는 턱과 목도. 그러나 그녀의 입은 이 땅의 어떤 얼굴에서도 견줄 만한 것을 본 적이 없었다."], ["To a young man with the least fire in him that little upward lift in the middle of her red top lip was distracting, infatuating, maddening.", "조금이라도 불씨를 지닌 젊은 남자에게, 그녀의 붉은 윗입술 한가운데 그 자그맣게 위로 올라간 곡선은 정신을 어지럽히고, 넋을 빼앗고, 미치게 했다."], ["He had never before seen a woman’s lips and teeth which forced upon his mind with such persistent iteration the old Elizabethan simile of roses filled with snow.", "그는 전에 어떤 여자의 입술과 이에서도, 눈雪으로 채운 장미라는 옛 엘리자베스 시대의 비유를 이토록 끈질긴 반복으로 마음에 떠올리게 한 것을 본 적이 없었다."], ["Perfect, he, as a lover, might have called them off-hand.", "연인으로서 그는 즉석에서 그것을 완벽하다고 불렀을지도 모른다."], ["But no—they were not perfect.", "그러나 아니다—그것은 완벽하지 않았다."], ["And it was the touch of the imperfect upon the would-be perfect that gave the sweetness, because it was that which gave the humanity.", "그리고 바로 그 완벽해 보이는 것 위에 닿은 불완전함의 한 점이, 그 달콤함을 주었으니, 그것이야말로 인간다움을 주는 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["Clare had studied the curves of those lips so many times that he could reproduce them mentally with ease: and now, as they again confronted him, clothed with colour and life, they sent an _aura_ over his flesh, a breeze through his nerves, which well nigh produced a qualm; and actually produced, by some mysterious physiological process, a prosaic sneeze.", "클레어는 그 입술의 곡선을 하도 여러 번 살펴본 터라 마음속에서 쉽게 재현해 낼 수 있었다. 그런데 이제 그것이 빛깔과 생명을 입은 채 다시 그를 마주하니, 그의 살갗에 일종의 기운을, 그의 신경에 한 줄기 산들바람을 보내 왔고, 그것은 거의 메스꺼움을 일으킬 뻔했다. 그리고 실제로, 어떤 알 수 없는 생리적 작용에 의해, 김빠지게도 재채기를 일으켰다."]], [["She then became conscious that he was observing her; but she would not show it by any change of position, though the curious dream-like fixity disappeared, and a close eye might easily have discerned that the rosiness of her face deepened, and then faded till only a tinge of it was left.", "그러자 그녀는 그가 자기를 지켜보고 있음을 알아챘다. 그러나 그녀는 자세를 조금도 바꾸어 그것을 드러내지 않으려 했다. 다만 그 기이하게 꿈꾸는 듯한 고정됨은 사라졌고, 자세히 보는 눈이라면 그녀의 얼굴 장밋빛이 짙어졌다가, 이윽고 옅어져 그 기운이 살짝만 남는 것을 쉽게 알아챘을 것이다."]], [["The influence that had passed into Clare like an excitation from the sky did not die down.", "하늘에서 내려온 자극처럼 클레어 속으로 스며든 그 영향력은 잦아들지 않았다."], ["Resolutions, reticences, prudences, fears, fell back like a defeated battalion.", "결심도, 과묵함도, 신중함도, 두려움도, 패한 대대처럼 물러났다."], ["He jumped up from his seat, and, leaving his pail to be kicked over if the milcher had such a mind, went quickly towards the desire of his eyes, and, kneeling down beside her, clasped her in his arms.", "그는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나, 소가 그럴 마음이 있다면 차 버리든 말든 양동이를 내버려 둔 채, 자기 눈이 갈망하는 대상을 향해 빠르게 다가갔고, 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다."]], [["Tess was taken completely by surprise, and she yielded to his embrace with unreflecting inevitableness.", "테스는 완전히 불시에 당했고, 미처 생각해 볼 겨를도 없는 필연성으로 그의 포옹에 몸을 맡겼다."], ["Having seen that it was really her lover who had advanced, and no one else, her lips parted, and she sank upon him in her momentary joy, with something very like an ecstatic cry.", "다가온 이가 다른 누구도 아닌 정말로 자기 연인임을 본 그녀는, 입술을 벌리고 한순간의 기쁨에 그에게 무너져 내렸으니, 황홀한 외침에 아주 가까운 무언가와 함께였다."]], [["He had been on the point of kissing that too tempting mouth, but he checked himself, for tender conscience’ sake.", "그는 너무도 유혹적인 그 입에 막 입 맞추려던 참이었으나, 여린 양심을 위해 스스로를 억눌렀다."]], [["“Forgive me, Tess dear!”", "\"용서해 줘요, 테스, 내 사랑!\""], ["he whispered.", "그가 속삭였다."], ["“I ought to have asked.", "\"물어봤어야 했는데."], ["I—did not know what I was doing.", "나는—내가 뭘 하는지도 몰랐어요."], ["I do not mean it as a liberty.", "무례하게 굴 뜻은 아니에요."], ["I am devoted to you, Tessy, dearest, in all sincerity!”", "나는 당신에게 헌신해요, 테시, 더없이 소중한 사람, 온 진심을 다해!\""]], [["Old Pretty by this time had looked round, puzzled; and seeing two people crouching under her where, by immemorial custom, there should have been only one, lifted her hind leg crossly.", "이때쯤 올드 프리티가 어리둥절해하며 뒤를 돌아보았다. 그러더니 아득한 옛 관습에 따르면 한 사람만 있어야 할 자기 밑에 두 사람이 웅크리고 있는 것을 보고는, 못마땅한 듯 뒷다리를 들어 올렸다."]], [["“She is angry—she doesn’t know what we mean—she’ll kick over the milk!”", "\"화났어요—우리가 무슨 짓을 하는지 몰라서—젖을 차 엎을 거예요!\""], ["exclaimed Tess, gently striving to free herself, her eyes concerned with the quadruped’s actions, her heart more deeply concerned with herself and Clare.", "테스가, 짐승의 움직임에 신경을 쓰면서도 마음으로는 자기 자신과 클레어에게 더 깊이 사로잡힌 채, 부드럽게 몸을 빼내려 애쓰며 외쳤다."]], [["She slipped up from her seat, and they stood together, his arm still encircling her.", "그녀가 걸상에서 빠져나와 일어섰고, 그들은 함께 섰는데, 그의 팔은 여전히 그녀를 감싸고 있었다."], ["Tess’s eyes, fixed on distance, began to fill.", "먼 곳에 고정된 테스의 눈에 눈물이 차오르기 시작했다."]], [["“Why do you cry, my darling?” he said.", "\"왜 우는 거예요, 내 사랑?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“O—I don’t know!”", "\"오—나도 모르겠어요!\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["As she saw and felt more clearly the position she was in she became agitated and tried to withdraw.", "자기가 처한 처지를 더 또렷이 보고 느끼게 되자, 그녀는 동요하며 물러서려 했다."]], [["“Well, I have betrayed my feeling, Tess, at last,”", "\"자, 마침내 내 감정을 드러내고 말았네요, 테스.\""], ["said he, with a curious sigh of desperation, signifying unconsciously that his heart had outrun his judgement.", "그가, 자기 마음이 판단을 앞질렀음을 무심결에 내비치는 야릇한 절망의 한숨과 함께 말했다."], ["“That I—love you dearly and truly I need not say.", "\"내가—당신을 진심으로 깊이 사랑한다는 건 말할 필요도 없어요."], ["But I—it shall go no further now—it distresses you—I am as surprised as you are.", "하지만 나는—지금은 더 나아가지 않을게요—당신을 괴롭히고 있으니까요—나도 당신만큼이나 놀랐어요."], ["You will not think I have presumed upon your defencelessness—been too quick and unreflecting, will you?”", "내가 당신이 무방비한 틈을 타 함부로 굴었다고는—너무 성급하고 분별없이 굴었다고는 생각하지 않겠죠?\""]], [["“N’—I can’t tell.”", "\"아니에요—저도 잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["He had allowed her to free herself; and in a minute or two the milking of each was resumed.", "그는 그녀가 빠져나가도록 놓아주었다. 그리고 일이 분 만에 두 사람은 각자 젖 짜기를 다시 시작했다."], ["Nobody had beheld the gravitation of the two into one; and when the dairyman came round by that screened nook a few minutes later, there was not a sign to reveal that the markedly sundered pair were more to each other than mere acquaintance.", "둘이 하나로 끌려간 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그리고 몇 분 뒤 낙농장 주인이 그 가려진 모퉁이를 돌아왔을 때, 그 두드러지게 떨어져 있는 한 쌍이 서로에게 한낱 아는 사이 이상이라는 것을 드러낼 기미는 어디에도 없었다."], ["Yet in the interval since Crick’s last view of them something had occurred which changed the pivot of the universe for their two natures; something which, had he known its quality, the dairyman would have despised, as a practical man; yet which was based upon a more stubborn and resistless tendency than a whole heap of so-called practicalities.", "그러나 크릭이 그들을 마지막으로 본 뒤의 그 사이에, 그 둘의 천성에게 우주의 축을 바꿔 놓은 무언가가 일어난 것이었다. 만약 그 본질을 알았다면 낙농장 주인이 현실적인 사람으로서 경멸했을 만한 무언가였으나, 이른바 현실적이라는 것들을 한 무더기 쌓아 올린 것보다 더 끈질기고 거역할 수 없는 경향에 바탕을 둔 무언가였다."], ["A veil had been whisked aside; the tract of each one’s outlook was to have a new horizon thenceforward—for a short time or for a long. End of Phase the Third", "한 장의 베일이 휙 걷혔다. 이제부터 두 사람 각자의 시야가 펼쳐지는 영역은 새로운 지평을 가지게 될 터였다—짧은 동안이든 오랜 동안이든."]], [["# XXV", "# XXV"]], [["The Consequence\nClare, restless, went out into the dusk when evening drew on, she who had won him having retired to her chamber.", "저녁이 다가올 무렵, 안절부절못하던 클레어는 어스름 속으로 나갔다. 그를 사로잡은 그녀는 자기 방으로 물러간 뒤였다."]], [["The night was as sultry as the day.", "밤은 낮만큼이나 후텁지근했다."], ["There was no coolness after dark unless on the grass.", "어두워진 뒤에도 풀밭 위가 아니고는 시원함이라곤 없었다."], ["Roads, garden-paths, the house-fronts, the barton-walls were warm as hearths, and reflected the noontime temperature into the noctambulist’s face.", "길도, 정원의 오솔길도, 집의 정면도, 가축마당 담도 화덕처럼 따뜻해서, 한낮의 온도를 밤 산책자의 얼굴에 되비쳤다."]], [["He sat on the east gate of the dairy-yard, and knew not what to think of himself.", "그는 낙농장 마당의 동쪽 문에 걸터앉아, 자기 자신을 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Feeling had indeed smothered judgement that day.", "그날 감정이 정말로 판단을 질식시켜 버렸던 것이다."]], [["Since the sudden embrace, three hours before, the twain had kept apart.", "세 시간 전 그 갑작스러운 포옹 이후로 두 사람은 떨어져 지냈다."], ["She seemed stilled, almost alarmed, at what had occurred, while the novelty, unpremeditation, mastery of circumstance disquieted him—palpitating, contemplative being that he was.", "그녀는 일어난 일에 정적에 잠긴, 거의 겁먹은 듯이 보였고, 한편 그 새로움과 계획에 없던 일과 상황을 통제할 수 없었다는 것이, 두근거리며 사색하는 존재인 그를 불안하게 했다."], ["He could hardly realize their true relations to each other as yet, and what their mutual bearing should be before third parties thenceforward.", "그는 아직도 서로의 진정한 관계가 무엇인지, 이제부터 제삼자들 앞에서 둘이 어떻게 처신해야 할지 도무지 가늠할 수 없었다."]], [["Angel had come as pupil to this dairy in the idea that his temporary existence here was to be the merest episode in his life, soon passed through and early forgotten; he had come as to a place from which as from a screened alcove he could calmly view the absorbing world without, and, apostrophizing it with Walt Whitman—", "엔젤은 이 낙농장에 견습생으로 올 때, 여기서의 자기 임시 생활이 인생에서 한낱 짧은 막간일 뿐 곧 지나가고 일찌감치 잊힐 것이라는 생각이었다. 그는 마치 가려진 골방에서처럼, 자기를 사로잡는 바깥 세상을 차분히 바라볼 수 있는 곳으로 이곳에 왔으며, 월트 휘트먼처럼 그 세상을 향해 이렇게 외칠 작정이었다—"]], [["Crowds of men and women attired in the usual costumes, How curious you are to me!—", "흔한 옷차림을 한 남자들과 여자들의 무리여, 너희는 내게 얼마나 신기한가!—"]], [["resolve upon a plan for plunging into that world anew.", "그리하여 그 세상으로 새로이 뛰어들 계획을 세울 작정이었다."], ["But behold, the absorbing scene had been imported hither.", "그러나 보라, 그 사로잡는 광경이 이곳으로 들어와 버렸다."], ["What had been the engrossing world had dissolved into an uninteresting outer dumb-show; while here, in this apparently dim and unimpassioned place, novelty had volcanically started up, as it had never, for him, started up elsewhere.", "그를 온통 사로잡던 세상은 흥미 없는 바깥의 말 없는 무언극으로 흩어져 버렸고, 한편 이곳, 겉보기엔 어둑하고 격정 없어 보이는 이곳에서, 그가 다른 어디서도 결코 겪어 본 적 없는 식으로 새로움이 화산처럼 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["Every window of the house being open, Clare could hear across the yard each trivial sound of the retiring household.", "집의 창문이란 창문이 다 열려 있어, 클레어는 마당 너머로 잠자리에 드는 식구들의 사소한 소리 하나하나를 들을 수 있었다."], ["The dairy-house, so humble, so insignificant, so purely to him a place of constrained sojourn that he had never hitherto deemed it of sufficient importance to be reconnoitred as an object of any quality whatever in the landscape; what was it now?", "그토록 보잘것없고, 그토록 하찮으며, 그에게는 그저 마지못해 머무는 곳일 뿐이어서 이제껏 풍경 속에서 무슨 가치 있는 대상이랍시고 눈여겨볼 만큼 중요하게 여긴 적도 없던 그 낙농장 집이, 이제는 무엇이 되었는가?"], ["The aged and lichened brick gables breathed forth “Stay!”", "세월에 닳고 이끼 낀 벽돌 박공들이 \"머물러요!\" 하고 숨결을 토했다."], ["The windows smiled, the door coaxed and beckoned, the creeper blushed confederacy.", "창문들은 미소 지었고, 문은 어르고 손짓했으며, 덩굴은 한패가 되어 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["A personality within it was so far-reaching in her influence as to spread into and make the bricks, mortar, and whole overhanging sky throb with a burning sensibility.", "그 안의 한 인격이 미치는 영향이 어찌나 멀리까지 뻗어 나가는지, 벽돌과 회반죽과 머리 위로 드리운 하늘 전체에 스며들어 그것들을 불타는 감수성으로 두근거리게 했다."], ["Whose was this mighty personality? A milkmaid’s.", "이 강력한 인격은 누구의 것이었던가? 한 젖 짜는 처녀의 것이었다."]], [["It was amazing, indeed, to find how great a matter the life of the obscure dairy had become to him.", "이 외진 낙농장의 삶이 그에게 얼마나 큰일이 되었는지 알게 된 것은 정말 놀라운 일이었다."], ["And though new love was to be held partly responsible for this, it was not solely so.", "그리고 새로운 사랑이 이에 일부 책임이 있긴 했으나, 그것만이 전부는 아니었다."], ["Many besides Angel have learnt that the magnitude of lives is not as to their external displacements, but as to their subjective experiences.", "엔젤 말고도 많은 이가, 삶의 크기란 그 바깥의 이동에 달린 것이 아니라 그 주관적 경험에 달려 있음을 배웠다."], ["The impressionable peasant leads a larger, fuller, more dramatic life than the pachydermatous king.", "감수성 예민한 농부가, 가죽이 두꺼운 왕보다 더 크고 더 충만하며 더 극적인 삶을 산다."], ["Looking at it thus, he found that life was to be seen of the same magnitude here as elsewhere.", "그렇게 보니, 그는 삶이 다른 어디서와 마찬가지로 이곳에서도 똑같은 크기로 보인다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["Despite his heterodoxy, faults, and weaknesses, Clare was a man with a conscience.", "자신의 이단성과 결함과 약점에도 불구하고, 클레어는 양심을 지닌 사람이었다."], ["Tess was no insignificant creature to toy with and dismiss; but a woman living her precious life—a life which, to herself who endured or enjoyed it, possessed as great a dimension as the life of the mightiest to himself.", "테스는 가지고 놀다 내쳐 버릴 하찮은 존재가 아니라, 자기의 귀한 삶을 살아가는 한 여인이었다. 그 삶은, 그것을 견디거나 누리는 그녀 자신에게는, 가장 위대한 자의 삶이 그 자신에게 지니는 것만큼이나 큰 차원을 지니고 있었다."], ["Upon her sensations the whole world depended to Tess; through her existence all her fellow-creatures existed, to her.", "테스에게는 온 세상이 그녀의 감각에 달려 있었다. 그녀의 존재를 통해서야 비로소 그녀의 모든 동료 피조물도 그녀에게 존재했다."], ["The universe itself only came into being for Tess on the particular day in the particular year in which she was born.", "우주 자체도 테스가 태어난 바로 그해 그날에야 비로소 테스를 위해 생겨난 것이었다."]], [["This consciousness upon which he had intruded was the single opportunity of existence ever vouchsafed to Tess by an unsympathetic First Cause—her all; her every and only chance.", "그가 침범해 들어간 그 의식은, 무정한 제일원인第一原因이 테스에게 베푼 단 하나의 존재 기회였다—그녀의 전부요, 그녀의 처음이자 마지막인 단 하나의 기회였다."], ["How then should he look upon her as of less consequence than himself; as a pretty trifle to caress and grow weary of; and not deal in the greatest seriousness with the affection which he knew that he had awakened in her—so fervid and so impressionable as she was under her reserve—in order that it might not agonize and wreck her?", "그러니 어찌 그가 그녀를 자기보다 덜 중한 존재로, 쓰다듬다 싫증 날 예쁜 노리개쯤으로 여기겠는가. 그리고 그녀 안에 일깨워 놓았음을 그가 아는 그 애정을—그 과묵함 아래 그토록 열렬하고 그토록 감수성 예민한 그녀의 애정을—그것이 그녀를 괴롭히고 망가뜨리지 않도록 더없이 진지하게 다루지 않을 수 있겠는가?"]], [["To encounter her daily in the accustomed manner would be to develop what had begun.", "날마다 늘 하던 식으로 그녀를 마주친다는 것은, 이미 시작된 것을 키우는 일이 될 터였다."], ["Living in such close relations, to meet meant to fall into endearment; flesh and blood could not resist it; and, having arrived at no conclusion as to the issue of such a tendency, he decided to hold aloof for the present from occupations in which they would be mutually engaged.", "그토록 가까운 관계로 살면서 마주친다는 것은 애정에 빠져드는 것을 뜻했다. 살과 피로는 그것을 거부할 수 없었다. 그리고 그런 경향이 어떻게 매듭지어질지 아무런 결론에도 이르지 못한 터라, 그는 둘이 함께 해야 하는 일들로부터 당분간 거리를 두기로 결심했다."], ["As yet the harm done was small.", "아직은 입은 해가 작았다."]], [["But it was not easy to carry out the resolution never to approach her.", "그러나 결코 그녀에게 다가가지 않겠다는 결심을 지키기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["He was driven towards her by every heave of his pulse.", "맥박이 한 번 뛸 때마다 그는 그녀에게로 떠밀렸다."]], [["He thought he would go and see his friends.", "그는 친구들을 보러 가야겠다고 생각했다."], ["It might be possible to sound them upon this.", "그들에게 이 일을 슬쩍 떠볼 수 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["In less than five months his term here would have ended, and after a few additional months spent upon other farms he would be fully equipped in agricultural knowledge and in a position to start on his own account.", "다섯 달도 채 안 되어 이곳에서의 임기가 끝날 것이고, 다른 농장들에서 몇 달을 더 보내고 나면, 그는 농사 지식을 온전히 갖추어 제 힘으로 시작할 수 있는 처지가 될 터였다."], ["Would not a farmer want a wife, and should a farmer’s wife be a drawing-room wax-figure, or a woman who understood farming?", "농장주라면 아내가 필요하지 않겠는가, 그리고 농장주의 아내가 응접실의 밀랍 인형이어야 할까, 아니면 농사를 아는 여자여야 할까?"], ["Notwithstanding the pleasing answer returned to him by the silence, he resolved to go his journey.", "침묵이 그에게 돌려준 흐뭇한 대답에도 불구하고, 그는 길을 떠나기로 결심했다."]], [["One morning when they sat down to breakfast at Talbothays Dairy some maid observed that she had not seen anything of Mr Clare that day.", "어느 날 아침 탤보세이스 낙농장에서 아침을 먹으려 앉았을 때, 어떤 처녀가 그날 클레어 씨를 통 보지 못했다고 말했다."]], [["“O no,” said Dairyman Crick.", "\"오 그래,\" 낙농장 주인 크릭이 말했다."], ["“Mr Clare has gone hwome to Emminster to spend a few days wi’ his kinsfolk.”", "\"클레어 씨는 며칠 친척들하고 지내려고 에민스터 집에 가셨어.\""]], [["For four impassioned ones around that table the sunshine of the morning went out at a stroke, and the birds muffled their song.", "그 식탁에 둘러앉은 격정에 찬 네 사람에게는, 아침의 햇살이 한순간에 꺼졌고 새들도 노랫소리를 죽였다."], ["But neither girl by word or gesture revealed her blankness.", "그러나 어느 처녀도 말로든 몸짓으로든 자기의 막막함을 드러내지 않았다."], ["“He’s getting on towards the end of his time wi’ me,”", "\"그분도 나하고 함께할 기한이 거의 끝나 가지,\""], ["added the dairyman, with a phlegm which unconsciously was brutal; “and so I suppose he is beginning to see about his plans elsewhere.”", "낙농장 주인이, 무심결에 잔인한 무덤덤함으로 덧붙였다. \"그러니 다른 데서의 계획을 알아보기 시작한 모양이야.\""]], [["“How much longer is he to bide here?”", "\"그분은 얼마나 더 여기 머무시나요?\""], ["asked Izz Huett, the only one of the gloom-stricken bevy who could trust her voice with the question.", "이즈 휴엣이 물었다. 침울에 빠진 처녀들 가운데 그 질문에 목소리를 맡길 수 있는 유일한 이였다."]], [["The others waited for the dairyman’s answer as if their lives hung upon it; Retty, with parted lips, gazing on the tablecloth, Marian with heat added to her redness, Tess throbbing and looking out at the meads.", "다른 이들은 마치 제 목숨이 거기 달리기라도 한 듯 낙농장 주인의 대답을 기다렸다\n그것을 응시했다. 레티는 입술을 벌린 채 식탁보를 바라보았고, 마리안은 붉은 얼굴에 열기까지 더해졌고, 테스는 가슴이 두근거리며 풀밭을 내다보고 있었다."]], [["“Well, I can’t mind the exact day without looking at my memorandum-book,”", "\"글쎄, 메모장을 들여다보지 않고는 정확한 날짜를 기억할 수가 없구먼.\""], ["replied Crick, with the same intolerable unconcern.", "크릭이 여전히 견딜 수 없으리만치 무심하게 대답했다."], ["“And even that may be altered a bit.", "\"그것도 좀 바뀔 수 있고."], ["He’ll bide to get a little practice in the calving out at the straw-yard, for certain.", "그 사람은 짚 마당에서 송아지 받는 연습을 좀 더 익히려고 분명 머무를 거야."], ["He’ll hang on till the end of the year I should say.”", "내가 보기엔 연말까지는 붙어 있을 게야.\""]], [["Four months or so of torturing ecstasy in his society—of “pleasure girdled about with pain”.", "그와 함께하는 넉 달 남짓의, 고통스러우리만치 황홀한 시간—\"고통으로 둘러싸인 기쁨\"."], ["After that the blackness of unutterable night.", "그 후엔 입에 담을 수 없는 밤의 검은 어둠."]], [["At this moment of the morning Angel Clare was riding along a narrow lane ten miles distant from the breakfasters, in the direction of his father’s Vicarage at Emminster, carrying, as well as he could, a little basket which contained some black-puddings and a bottle of mead, sent by Mrs Crick, with her kind respects, to his parents.", "이 아침의 바로 그 순간, 앤젤 클레어는 아침을 먹는 이들로부터 십 마일 떨어진 좁은 길을 따라, 에민스터에 있는 아버지의 목사관 쪽으로 말을 달리고 있었다. 그는 크릭 부인이 정중한 안부와 함께 자기 부모에게 보낸 검은 순대 몇 개와 벌꿀술 한 병이 든 작은 바구니를 되도록 잘 들고 가는 중이었다."], ["The white lane stretched before him, and his eyes were upon it; but they were staring into next year, and not at the lane.", "하얀 길이 그의 앞으로 뻗어 있었고, 그의 눈은 그 위에 머물러 있었다. 하지만 그 눈은 길이 아니라 내년을 응시하고 있었다."], ["He loved her; ought he to marry her?", "그는 그녀를 사랑했다. 그녀와 결혼해야 하는가?"], ["Dared he to marry her?", "감히 결혼할 수 있는가?"], ["What would his mother and his brothers say?", "어머니와 형들은 뭐라 할 것인가?"], ["What would he himself say a couple of years after the event?", "그 일이 있고 이삼 년 후에 자기 자신은 뭐라 말하게 될 것인가?"], ["That would depend upon whether the germs of staunch comradeship underlay the temporary emotion, or whether it were a sensuous joy in her form only, with no substratum of everlastingness.", "그것은 이 일시적 감정 아래에 굳건한 동지애의 싹이 깔려 있느냐, 아니면 영속성의 토대 하나 없이 오직 그녀의 형상에서 오는 관능적 기쁨일 뿐이냐에 달려 있었다."]], [["His father’s hill-surrounded little town, the Tudor church-tower of red stone, the clump of trees near the Vicarage, came at last into view beneath him, and he rode down towards the well-known gate.", "언덕에 둘러싸인 아버지의 작은 마을, 붉은 돌로 지은 튜더 양식의 교회탑, 목사관 가까이 늘어선 나무들이 마침내 그의 발치 아래로 모습을 드러냈고, 그는 잘 아는 대문을 향해 말을 몰아 내려갔다."], ["Casting a glance in the direction of the church before entering his home, he beheld standing by the vestry-door a group of girls, of ages between twelve and sixteen, apparently awaiting the arrival of some other one, who in a moment became visible; a figure somewhat older than the school-girls, wearing a broad-brimmed hat and highly-starched cambric morning-gown, with a couple of books in her hand.", "집으로 들어가기 전 교회 쪽으로 눈길을 던지자, 제의실 문 옆에 열두 살에서 열여섯 살 사이의 소녀들 한 무리가 서 있는 것이 보였다. 누군가 다른 사람이 오기를 기다리는 듯했는데, 그 사람이 곧 모습을 드러냈다. 여학생들보다 다소 나이가 든 사람으로, 챙 넓은 모자에 빳빳하게 풀 먹인 캠브릭 천 아침 가운을 입고, 손에는 책 두어 권을 들고 있었다."]], [["Clare knew her well.", "클레어는 그녀를 잘 알았다."], ["He could not be sure that she observed him; he hoped she did not, so as to render it unnecessary that he should go and speak to her, blameless creature that she was.", "그녀가 자기를 보았는지 확신할 수 없었다. 그녀가 보지 못했기를 바랐는데, 그래야 가서 그녀에게 말을 걸 필요가 없을 터였다. 흠 하나 없는 사람이긴 했지만 말이다."], ["An overpowering reluctance to greet her made him decide that she had not seen him.", "그녀에게 인사를 건네기가 너무도 꺼려져, 그는 그녀가 자기를 보지 못했다고 단정하기로 했다."], ["The young lady was Miss Mercy Chant, the only daughter of his father’s neighbour and friend, whom it was his parents’ quiet hope that he might wed some day.", "그 젊은 숙녀는 머시 챈트 양으로, 아버지의 이웃이자 친구의 외동딸이었고, 언젠가 그가 그녀와 결혼하기를 그의 부모가 은근히 바라는 상대였다."], ["She was great at Antinomianism and Bible-classes, and was plainly going to hold a class now.", "그녀는 율법폐기론과 성서반에 정통했고, 지금도 분명 성서반을 열려는 참이었다."], ["Clare’s mind flew to the impassioned, summer-steeped heathens in the Var Vale, their rosy faces court-patched with cow-droppings; and to one the most impassioned of them all.", "클레어의 마음은 바르 골짜기의, 정열에 차고 여름에 흠뻑 젖은 이교도들에게로 날아갔다. 소똥을 애교점처럼 묻힌 그들의 발그레한 얼굴들에게로. 그리고 그 모두 중에 가장 정열적인 한 사람에게로."]], [["It was on the impulse of the moment that he had resolved to trot over to Emminster, and hence had not written to apprise his mother and father, aiming, however, to arrive about the breakfast hour, before they should have gone out to their parish duties.", "에민스터까지 말을 달려가기로 마음먹은 것은 순간의 충동이었고, 그래서 그는 어머니 아버지에게 미리 알리는 편지도 쓰지 못했다. 그래도 그들이 교구 일로 외출하기 전, 아침 식사 시간쯤에 도착하려고 했다."], ["He was a little late, and they had already sat down to the morning meal.", "그는 조금 늦었고, 그들은 이미 아침상에 둘러앉아 있었다."], ["The group at the table jumped up to welcome him as soon as he entered.", "식탁에 모인 무리가 그가 들어서자마자 벌떡 일어나 그를 맞았다."], ["They were his father and mother, his brother the Reverend Felix—curate at a town in the adjoining county, home for the inside of a fortnight—and his other brother, the Reverend Cuthbert, the classical scholar, and Fellow and Dean of his College, down from Cambridge for the long vacation.", "그들은 그의 아버지와 어머니, 인근 카운티의 한 도시에서 부목사로 있다가 보름 안짝으로 집에 와 있는 형 펠릭스 목사, 그리고 또 다른 형으로 고전학자이자 자기 칼리지의 펠로 겸 학장이며 긴 방학을 맞아 케임브리지에서 내려와 있는 커스버트 목사였다."], ["His mother appeared in a cap and silver spectacles, and his father looked what in fact he was—an earnest, God-fearing man, somewhat gaunt, in years about sixty-five, his pale face lined with thought and purpose.", "그의 어머니는 모자를 쓰고 은테 안경을 낀 모습이었고, 아버지는 실제 그 사람 그대로의 모습—진지하고 신을 두려워하는, 다소 수척하고 예순다섯쯤 된, 창백한 얼굴에 사색과 결의의 주름이 새겨진 사람—이었다."], ["Over their heads hung the picture of Angel’s sister, the eldest of the family, sixteen years his senior, who had married a missionary and gone out to Africa.", "그들 머리 위에는 앤젤의 누나, 가족 중 맏이로 그보다 열여섯 살 위인, 선교사와 결혼해 아프리카로 떠난 누나의 그림이 걸려 있었다."]], [["Old Mr Clare was a clergyman of a type which, within the last twenty years, has well nigh dropped out of contemporary life.", "늙은 클레어 씨는 지난 이십 년 사이 동시대의 삶에서 거의 사라져 버린 부류의 성직자였다."], ["A spiritual descendant in the direct line from Wycliff, Huss, Luther, Calvin; an Evangelical of the Evangelicals, a Conversionist, a man of Apostolic simplicity in life and thought, he had in his raw youth made up his mind once for all in the deeper questions of existence, and admitted no further reasoning on them thenceforward.", "위클리프, 후스, 루터, 칼뱅의 직계 영적 후손이요, 복음주의자 중의 복음주의자요, 회심주의자요, 삶과 사상에서 사도와 같은 단순함을 지닌 사람으로, 그는 거친 젊은 시절에 존재의 더 깊은 물음들에 대해 단번에 마음을 정해 버렸고, 그 후로는 그것들에 대한 어떤 추가적 논의도 받아들이지 않았다."], ["He was regarded even by those of his own date and school of thinking as extreme; while, on the other hand, those totally opposed to him were unwillingly won to admiration for his thoroughness, and for the remarkable power he showed in dismissing all question as to principles in his energy for applying them.", "그는 자기와 같은 시대, 같은 사상 학파에 속한 이들에게조차 극단적이라 여겨졌다. 한편으로는, 그와 완전히 반대편에 선 자들마저도 그의 철저함에, 그리고 원칙을 적용하는 그 열정 안에서 원칙에 관한 모든 의문을 떨쳐 버리는 그 놀라운 힘에 마지못해 감탄을 보냈다."], ["He loved Paul of Tarsus, liked St John, hated St James as much as he dared, and regarded with mixed feelings Timothy, Titus, and Philemon. The New Testament was less a Christiad then a Pauliad to his intelligence—less an argument than an intoxication.", "그는 다소의 바울을 사랑했고, 성 요한을 좋아했으며, 성 야고보는 감히 그럴 수 있는 만큼 미워했고, 디모데와 디도와 빌레몬에 대해서는 복잡한 감정을 품었다. 그의 지성에 신약은 그리스도서라기보다 바울서였다—논증이라기보다 도취였다."], ["His creed of determinism was such that it almost amounted to a vice, and quite amounted, on its negative side, to a renunciative philosophy which had cousinship with that of Schopenhauer and Leopardi.", "그의 결정론 신조는 거의 악덕에 이를 지경이었고, 그 부정적인 면에서는 쇼펜하우어와 레오파르디의 철학과 사촌뻘인 단념의 철학에 온전히 이르렀다."], ["He despised the Canons and Rubric, swore by the Articles, and deemed himself consistent through the whole category—which in a way he might have been. One thing he certainly was—sincere.", "그는 교회 법규와 예배 규정을 경멸했고, 신조 조항을 두고 맹세했으며, 그 전 범주에 걸쳐 자신이 일관되다고 여겼는데—어떤 면에서는 그랬을지도 모른다. 한 가지만큼은 그는 확실히 그러했다—진실했다."]], [["To the aesthetic, sensuous, pagan pleasure in natural life and lush womanhood which his son Angel had lately been experiencing in Var Vale, his temper would have been antipathetic in a high degree, had he either by inquiry or imagination been able to apprehend it.", "자연의 삶과 무르익은 여성성에서 오는, 그의 아들 앤젤이 근래 바르 골짜기에서 겪고 있던 미적이고 관능적이며 이교적인 즐거움에 대해, 만일 그가 캐묻거나 상상으로라도 그것을 파악할 수 있었다면 그의 기질은 지극히 반감을 느꼈을 것이다."], ["Once upon a time Angel had been so unlucky as to say to his father, in a moment of irritation, that it might have resulted far better for mankind if Greece had been the source of the religion of modern civilization, and not Palestine; and his father’s grief was of that blank description which could not realize that there might lurk a thousandth part of a truth, much less a half truth or a whole truth, in such a proposition.", "언젠가 앤젤은 짜증이 난 순간에 아버지에게, 현대 문명의 종교가 팔레스타인이 아니라 그리스에서 나왔더라면 인류에게 훨씬 더 나았을지도 모른다고 말하는 불운을 저지른 적이 있었다. 아버지의 슬픔은, 그런 명제 안에 진실의 천 분의 일이라도, 하물며 반쪽 진실이나 온전한 진실이 도사리고 있을 수 있음을 깨닫지 못하는, 그런 멍한 종류의 것이었다."], ["He had simply preached austerely at Angel for some time after.", "그는 그저 한동안 앤젤에게 엄숙하게 설교를 늘어놓았을 뿐이다."], ["But the kindness of his heart was such that he never resented anything for long, and welcomed his son to-day with a smile which was as candidly sweet as a child’s.", "하지만 그의 마음씨가 어찌나 너그러운지 그는 결코 무엇이든 오래 담아 두지 않았고, 오늘도 어린아이처럼 솔직하고 다정한 미소로 아들을 맞이했다."]], [["Angel sat down, and the place felt like home; yet he did not so much as formerly feel himself one of the family gathered there.", "앤젤은 자리에 앉았고, 그곳은 집처럼 느껴졌다. 하지만 그는 예전만큼 자신이 거기 모인 가족의 일원이라고 느끼지는 못했다."], ["Every time that he returned hither he was conscious of this divergence, and since he had last shared in the Vicarage life it had grown even more distinctly foreign to his own than usual.", "이곳으로 돌아올 때마다 그는 이 어긋남을 의식했고, 지난번 목사관 생활을 함께한 이래 그것은 평소보다도 더 뚜렷이 자기 자신에게 낯선 것으로 자라 있었다."], ["Its transcendental aspirations—still unconsciously based on the geocentric view of things, a zenithal paradise, a nadiral hell—were as foreign to his own as if they had been the dreams of people on another planet.", "그 초월적 열망들은—여전히 무의식적으로 천동설적 세계관에, 곧 천정의 낙원과 천저의 지옥에 토대를 두고 있었는데—마치 다른 행성에 사는 사람들의 꿈이라도 되는 양 그 자신에게 낯설었다."], ["Latterly he had seen only Life, felt only the great passionate pulse of existence, unwarped, uncontorted, untrammelled by those creeds which futilely attempt to check what wisdom would be content to regulate.", "근래 그는 오직 삶만을 보았고, 오직 존재의 거대한 정열적 맥박만을 느꼈다. 지혜라면 기꺼이 규율하는 데 만족했을 것을 헛되이 억제하려 드는 그 신조들에 의해 비틀리지도, 일그러지지도, 옭아매이지도 않은 맥박을."]], [["On their part they saw a great difference in him, a growing divergence from the Angel Clare of former times.", "그들 편에서는 그에게서 큰 차이를, 예전의 앤젤 클레어로부터 점점 멀어지는 어긋남을 보았다."], ["It was chiefly a difference in his manner that they noticed just now, particularly his brothers.", "지금 그들이, 특히 형들이 알아챈 것은 주로 그의 태도의 차이였다."], ["He was getting to behave like a farmer; he flung his legs about; the muscles of his face had grown more expressive; his eyes looked as much information as his tongue spoke, and more.", "그는 농부처럼 행동하게 되어 가고 있었다. 다리를 아무렇게나 내던졌고, 얼굴 근육은 더 표정이 풍부해졌으며, 눈은 혀가 말하는 만큼, 아니 그 이상을 말해 주었다."], ["The manner of the scholar had nearly disappeared; still more the manner of the drawing-room young man. A prig would have said that he had lost culture, and a prude that he had become coarse.", "학자의 태도는 거의 사라졌고, 응접실 청년의 태도는 더더욱 사라졌다. 잘난 체하는 자라면 그가 교양을 잃었다고 했을 테고, 까다로운 자라면 그가 거칠어졌다고 했을 것이다."], ["Such was the contagion of domiciliary fellowship with the Talbothays nymphs and swains.", "톨버세이즈의 요정들 및 목동들과 한집에 살며 어울린 결과의 전염이 그러했다."]], [["After breakfast he walked with his two brothers, non-evangelical, well-educated, hall-marked young men, correct to their remotest fibre, such unimpeachable models as are turned out yearly by the lathe of a systematic tuition.", "아침 식사 후 그는 두 형과 함께 걸었다. 비복음적이고 잘 교육받았으며 품질 보증이 찍힌 청년들로, 가장 먼 결까지 빈틈없이 올바른, 체계적 교육의 선반에서 해마다 깎아 내는 흠잡을 데 없는 모범들이었다."], ["They were both somewhat short-sighted, and when it was the custom to wear a single eyeglass and string they wore a single eyeglass and string; when it was the custom to wear a double glass they wore a double glass; when it was the custom to wear spectacles they wore spectacles straightway, all without reference to the particular variety of defect in their own vision.", "둘 다 다소 근시였는데, 외알 안경에 줄을 다는 것이 관습일 때는 외알 안경에 줄을 달았고, 양알 안경을 쓰는 것이 관습일 때는 양알 안경을 썼고, 일반 안경을 쓰는 것이 관습일 때는 곧장 일반 안경을 썼다. 모두 자기네 시력 결함의 구체적 종류와는 무관하게 말이다."], ["When Wordsworth was enthroned they carried pocket copies; and when Shelley was belittled they allowed him to grow dusty on their shelves.", "워즈워스가 옥좌에 오를 때는 휴대용 시집을 가지고 다녔고, 셸리가 폄하될 때는 그가 책장에서 먼지를 뒤집어쓰도록 내버려 두었다."], ["When Correggio’s Holy Families were admired, they admired Correggio’s Holy Families; when he was decried in favour of Velasquez, they sedulously followed suit without any personal objection.", "코레조의 성가족화가 찬미받을 때는 코레조의 성가족화를 찬미했고, 그가 깎아내려지고 벨라스케스가 추켜세워질 때는 아무런 개인적 이의도 없이 부지런히 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["If these two noticed Angel’s growing social ineptness, he noticed their growing mental limitations.", "이 두 사람이 앤젤의 점점 커지는 사회적 부적응을 알아챘다면, 앤젤은 그들의 점점 좁아지는 정신적 한계를 알아챘다."], ["Felix seemed to him all Church; Cuthbert all College. His Diocesan Synod and Visitations were the mainsprings of the world to the one; Cambridge to the other.", "펠릭스는 그에게 온통 교회로만 보였고, 커스버트는 온통 칼리지로만 보였다. 한쪽에게는 교구 종교회의와 시찰이 세상의 태엽이었고, 다른 쪽에게는 케임브리지가 그러했다."], ["Each brother candidly recognized that there were a few unimportant score of millions of outsiders in civilized society, persons who were neither University men nor churchmen; but they were to be tolerated rather than reckoned with and respected.", "두 형은 문명사회에 대학인도 아니고 교회인도 아닌, 별로 중요치 않은 수천만의 외부인이 있다는 것을 솔직히 인정했다. 하지만 그들은 헤아리고 존중할 대상이라기보다 그저 용인할 대상이었다."]], [["They were both dutiful and attentive sons, and were regular in their visits to their parents.", "둘 다 효성스럽고 자상한 아들이었고, 부모를 정기적으로 찾아뵈었다."], ["Felix, though an offshoot from a far more recent point in the devolution of theology than his father, was less self-sacrificing and disinterested.", "펠릭스는 비록 신학 계보에서 아버지보다 훨씬 더 최근의 지점에서 갈라져 나온 가지였지만, 자기희생적이고 사심 없는 면은 덜했다."], ["More tolerant than his father of a contradictory opinion, in its aspect as a danger to its holder, he was less ready than his father to pardon it as a slight to his own teaching.", "상반된 의견을 그것을 품은 자에게 위험으로 보는 측면에서는 아버지보다 더 관대했으나, 그것을 자기 가르침에 대한 무례로 보아 용서하는 데는 아버지보다 덜 선뜻했다."], ["Cuthbert was, upon the whole, the more liberal-minded, though, with greater subtlety, he had not so much heart.", "커스버트는 대체로 더 자유로운 사고를 지녔지만, 더 큰 명민함과 더불어 마음은 그만큼 깊지 못했다."]], [["As they walked along the hillside Angel’s former feeling revived in him—that whatever their advantages by comparison with himself, neither saw or set forth life as it really was lived.", "언덕길을 걸어가는 동안 앤젤의 예전 감정이 그의 안에서 되살아났다—그들이 자기에 비해 어떤 이점을 지녔든 간에, 어느 쪽도 삶을 실제로 살아지는 그대로 보거나 그려 내지는 못한다는 감정이었다."], ["Perhaps, as with many men, their opportunities of observation were not so good as their opportunities of expression.", "어쩌면 많은 사람이 그렇듯, 그들의 관찰의 기회는 표현의 기회만큼 좋지 못했는지도 모른다."], ["Neither had an adequate conception of the complicated forces at work outside the smooth and gentle current in which they and their associates floated.", "어느 쪽도, 자기네와 자기네 동료들이 떠다니는 매끄럽고 잔잔한 물결 바깥에서 작동하는 복잡한 힘들에 대해 제대로 된 인식이 없었다."], ["Neither saw the difference between local truth and universal truth; that what the inner world said in their clerical and academic hearing was quite a different thing from what the outer world was thinking.", "어느 쪽도 국지적 진리와 보편적 진리의 차이를 보지 못했다. 성직자와 학자의 귀에 안쪽 세계가 들려주는 것과 바깥 세계가 생각하고 있는 것이 전혀 다른 것이라는 차이를 말이다."]], [["“I suppose it is farming or nothing for you now, my dear fellow,”", "\"이제 자네한테는 농사 아니면 아무것도 없는 모양이군, 친애하는 아우야.\""], ["Felix was saying, among other things, to his youngest brother, as he looked through his spectacles at the distant fields with sad austerity.", "펠릭스가 슬프고 엄숙하게 안경 너머로 먼 들판을 바라보며 여러 말 가운데 막내에게 그렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["“And, therefore, we must make the best of it.", "\"그러니 우리는 그걸 최대한 살려야지."], ["But I do entreat you to endeavour to keep as much as possible in touch with moral ideals.", "하지만 부디 도덕적 이상과 되도록 많이 닿아 있도록 애써 주기를 간곡히 부탁하네."], ["Farming, of course, means roughing it externally; but high thinking may go with plain living, nevertheless.”", "농사란 물론 겉으로 거칠게 사는 걸 뜻하지. 하지만 그래도 검소한 삶에 고결한 사색이 함께할 수 있으니까.\""]], [["“Of course it may,”", "\"물론 그럴 수 있죠.\""], ["said Angel.", "앤젤이 말했다."], ["“Was it not proved nineteen hundred years ago—if I may trespass upon your domain a little?", "\"천구백 년 전에 이미 증명되지 않았나요—제가 형님 영역을 조금 침범해도 된다면요?"], ["Why should you think, Felix, that I am likely to drop my high thinking and my moral ideals?”", "형님, 왜 제가 고결한 사색과 도덕적 이상을 내던질 것 같다고 생각하시죠?\""]], [["“Well, I fancied, from the tone of your letters and our conversation—it may be fancy only—that you were somehow losing intellectual grasp.", "\"글쎄, 자네 편지의 어조와 우리 대화로 미루어—그저 내 짐작일 뿐일지도 모르지만—자네가 어쩐지 지적 파악력을 잃어 가는 것 같았어."], ["Hasn’t it struck you, Cuthbert?”", "커스버트, 자네도 그런 생각 안 들던가?\""]], [["“Now, Felix,”", "\"이보세요, 펠릭스 형님.\" 앤젤이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["said Angel drily, “we are very good friends, you know; each of us treading our allotted circles; but if it comes to intellectual grasp, I think you, as a contented dogmatist, had better leave mine alone, and inquire what has become of yours.”", "\"우리는 아주 좋은 친구죠, 아시잖아요. 저마다 자기에게 할당된 원을 밟으며 지내는. 하지만 지적 파악력 얘기가 나온 김에 말하자면, 만족에 겨운 독단론자인 형님은 제 것은 가만 내버려 두고 형님 것이 어찌 되었는지나 알아보시는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["They returned down the hill to dinner, which was fixed at any time at which their father’s and mother’s morning work in the parish usually concluded.", "그들은 언덕을 도로 내려와 정찬을 들었는데, 정찬은 부모가 교구에서 보내는 오전 일과가 으레 끝나는 시각이면 언제든 그 시각으로 정해졌다."], ["Convenience as regarded afternoon callers was the last thing to enter into the consideration of unselfish Mr and Mrs Clare; though the three sons were sufficiently in unison on this matter to wish that their parents would conform a little to modern notions.", "오후의 방문객들을 고려한 편의 따위는 사심 없는 클레어 부부의 고려에 들어올 마지막 항목이었다. 비록 세 아들은 부모가 현대적 관념에 조금쯤 따라 주기를 바라는 점에서는 충분히 한마음이었지만 말이다."]], [["The walk had made them hungry, Angel in particular, who was now an outdoor man, accustomed to the profuse _dapes inemptae_ of the dairyman’s somewhat coarsely-laden table.", "산책으로 그들은 시장기가 돌았는데, 특히 이제는 야외에서 일하는 사람이 되어 다소 거칠게 차려진 낙농가의 식탁에 흔하던 '값 치르지 않은 풍성한 음식dapes inemptae'에 익숙해진 앤젤이 그러했다."], ["But neither of the old people had arrived, and it was not till the sons were almost tired of waiting that their parents entered.", "하지만 두 노인 중 어느 쪽도 도착하지 않았고, 아들들이 기다리다 거의 지칠 무렵에야 그들의 부모가 들어왔다."], ["The self-denying pair had been occupied in coaxing the appetites of some of their sick parishioners, whom they, somewhat inconsistently, tried to keep imprisoned in the flesh, their own appetites being quite forgotten.", "그 극기의 부부는 병든 교구민 몇몇의 식욕을 돋우는 일에 매달려 있었던 것인데, 다소 모순되게도 그들은 그 교구민들을 어떻게든 육신 안에 가두어 두려 애쓰면서 정작 자기네 식욕은 까맣게 잊고 있었다."]], [["The family sat down to table, and a frugal meal of cold viands was deposited before them.", "가족은 식탁에 앉았고, 차가운 음식으로 차린 검소한 식사가 그들 앞에 놓였다."], ["Angel looked round for Mrs Crick’s black-puddings, which he had directed to be nicely grilled as they did them at the dairy, and of which he wished his father and mother to appreciate the marvellous herbal savours as highly as he did himself.", "앤젤은 크릭 부인의 검은 순대를 찾아 둘러보았는데, 낙농장에서 하던 그대로 먹음직하게 구워 달라고 일러둔 것이었고, 그가 직접 즐기는 만큼이나 그 놀라운 허브 풍미를 아버지와 어머니도 높이 음미해 주기를 바랐던 것이다."]], [["“Ah! you are looking for the black-puddings, my dear boy,”", "\"아! 검은 순대를 찾고 있구나, 얘야.\""], ["observed Clare’s mother.", "클레어의 어머니가 말했다."], ["“But I am sure you will not mind doing without them as I am sure your father and I shall not, when you know the reason.", "\"하지만 이유를 알면 너도 분명 그게 없어도 개의치 않을 게다. 네 아버지와 나도 분명 그럴 테니까."], ["I suggested to him that we should take Mrs Crick’s kind present to the children of the man who can earn nothing just now because of his attacks of delirium tremens; and he agreed that it would be a great pleasure to them; so we did.”", "내가 네 아버지께 제안했단다, 지금 진전섬망 발작 때문에 한 푼도 못 버는 사람의 아이들에게 크릭 부인의 친절한 선물을 가져다주자고. 네 아버지도 그게 그 아이들에게 큰 기쁨이 되리라고 동의하셨고. 그래서 그렇게 했단다.\""]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이죠.\""], ["said Angel cheerfully, looking round for the mead.", "앤젤이 명랑하게 말하며 벌꿀술을 찾아 둘러보았다."]], [["“I found the mead so extremely alcoholic,”", "\"그 벌꿀술은 너무 알코올이 강해서,\""], ["continued his mother, “that it was quite unfit for use as a beverage, but as valuable as rum or brandy in an emergency; so I have put it in my medicine-closet.”", "그의 어머니가 말을 이었다. \"음료로 쓰기엔 영 부적합하더구나. 하지만 비상시엔 럼이나 브랜디만큼 값진 것이라, 약장에 넣어 두었단다.\""]], [["“We never drink spirits at this table, on principle,”", "\"우리는 원칙상 이 식탁에서 결코 독주를 마시지 않는단다.\""], ["added his father.", "그의 아버지가 덧붙였다."]], [["“But what shall I tell the dairyman’s wife?”", "\"그럼 낙농가 부인에게는 뭐라고 말씀드려야 할까요?\""], ["said Angel.", "앤젤이 말했다."]], [["“The truth, of course,”", "\"물론 진실을 말해야지.\""], ["said his father.", "그의 아버지가 말했다."]], [["“I rather wanted to say we enjoyed the mead and the black-puddings very much.", "\"저는 차라리 벌꿀술과 검은 순대를 아주 맛있게 먹었다고 말하고 싶었어요."], ["She is a kind, jolly sort of body, and is sure to ask me directly I return.”", "그분은 친절하고 쾌활한 사람이라, 제가 돌아가자마자 분명 물어볼 테니까요.\""]], [["“You cannot, if we did not,”", "\"우리가 그러지 않았으니 너도 그럴 수 없지.\""], ["Mr Clare answered lucidly.", "클레어 씨가 명료하게 대답했다."]], [["“Ah—no; though that mead was a drop of pretty tipple.”", "\"아—그렇네요. 하지만 그 벌꿀술은 꽤나 한잔 거나하게 걸칠 만한 술이었는데요.\""]], [["“A what?” said Cuthbert and Felix both.", "\"한잔 뭐라고?\" 커스버트와 펠릭스가 동시에 말했다."]], [["“Oh—’tis an expression they use down at Talbothays,” replied Angel, blushing.", "\"아—톨버세이즈에서들 쓰는 표현이에요.\" 앤젤이 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["He felt that his parents were right in their practice if wrong in their want of sentiment, and said no more.", "그는 부모가 정서가 결여된 점에서는 그르지만 그 실천에서는 옳다고 느꼈고, 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["# XXVI", "# XXVI"]], [["It was not till the evening, after family prayers, that Angel found opportunity of broaching to his father one or two subjects near his heart.", "저녁이 되어, 가족 기도를 마친 후에야 앤젤은 마음에 둔 한두 가지 주제를 아버지에게 꺼낼 기회를 잡았다."], ["He had strung himself up to the purpose while kneeling behind his brothers on the carpet, studying the little nails in the heels of their walking boots.", "그는 형들 뒤로 양탄자 위에 무릎을 꿇고 그들의 산책화 뒤축에 박힌 작은 못들을 들여다보면서 그 목적을 위해 마음을 단단히 다잡았었다."], ["When the service was over they went out of the room with their mother, and Mr Clare and himself were left alone.", "예배가 끝나자 형들은 어머니와 함께 방을 나갔고, 클레어 씨와 그만이 남았다."]], [["The young man first discussed with the elder his plans for the attainment of his position as a farmer on an extensive scale—either in England or in the Colonies.", "젊은이는 먼저 연장자와 함께, 영국에서든 식민지에서든 큰 규모의 농부로 자리 잡으려는 자기 계획을 논의했다."], ["His father then told him that, as he had not been put to the expense of sending Angel up to Cambridge, he had felt it his duty to set by a sum of money every year towards the purchase or lease of land for him some day, that he might not feel himself unduly slighted.", "그러자 그의 아버지가, 앤젤을 케임브리지에 보내는 비용을 들이지 않았으니, 그가 부당하게 푸대접받았다고 느끼지 않도록 언젠가 그를 위해 땅을 사거나 임차하는 데 보탤 돈을 해마다 따로 떼어 두는 것을 자기 의무로 느껴 왔다고 말했다."]], [["“As far as worldly wealth goes,”", "\"세속적 재산으로 따지자면,\""], ["continued his father, “you will no doubt stand far superior to your brothers in a few years.”", "그의 아버지가 말을 이었다. \"너는 몇 년 안에 의심할 바 없이 네 형들보다 훨씬 우월한 처지에 서게 될 게다.\""]], [["This considerateness on old Mr Clare’s part led Angel onward to the other and dearer subject.", "늙은 클레어 씨의 이 사려 깊음이 앤젤을 또 다른, 더 소중한 주제로 이끌었다."], ["He observed to his father that he was then six-and-twenty, and that when he should start in the farming business he would require eyes in the back of his head to see to all matters—some one would be necessary to superintend the domestic labours of his establishment whilst he was afield.", "그는 아버지에게, 자기가 이제 스물여섯 살이며, 농사 사업을 시작하게 되면 온갖 일을 살피느라 뒤통수에도 눈이 필요할 것이라고 말했다. 자기가 들판에 나가 있는 동안 살림의 노역을 감독할 누군가가 필요하리라고."], ["Would it not be well, therefore, for him to marry?", "그러니 자기가 결혼하는 것이 좋지 않겠느냐고."]], [["His father seemed to think this idea not unreasonable; and then Angel put the question—", "그의 아버지는 이 생각을 무리한 것으로 여기지 않는 듯했다. 그러자 앤젤이 물음을 던졌다—"]], [["“What kind of wife do you think would be best for me as a thrifty hard-working farmer?”", "\"검소하고 부지런히 일하는 농부인 저에게 어떤 아내가 가장 좋을 것 같으세요?\""]], [["“A truly Christian woman, who will be a help and a comfort to you in your goings-out and your comings-in.", "\"참된 기독교도 여인, 네가 나가고 들어올 때 너에게 도움과 위안이 되어 줄 여인이지."], ["Beyond that, it really matters little.", "그 너머는 사실 거의 중요치 않다."], ["Such an one can be found; indeed, my earnest-minded friend and neighbour, Dr Chant—”", "그런 여인은 찾을 수 있어. 정말이지, 진지한 마음을 지닌 내 친구이자 이웃인 챈트 박사가—\""]], [["“But ought she not primarily to be able to milk cows, churn good butter, make immense cheeses; know how to sit hens and turkeys and rear chickens, to direct a field of labourers in an emergency, and estimate the value of sheep and calves?”", "\"하지만 그녀는 무엇보다 먼저 젖소를 짤 줄 알고, 좋은 버터를 만들고, 거대한 치즈를 만들 줄 알아야 하지 않을까요? 암탉과 칠면조를 품게 하고 병아리를 길러 낼 줄 알고, 비상시에 일꾼들이 일하는 들판을 지휘할 줄 알고, 양과 송아지의 값어치를 가늠할 줄 알아야 하지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Yes; a farmer’s wife; yes, certainly.", "\"그렇지, 농부의 아내라면 그렇지, 물론이고말고."], ["It would be desirable.”", "그게 바람직하겠지.\""], ["Mr Clare, the elder, had plainly never thought of these points before.", "연장자 클레어 씨는 이런 점들을 분명 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["“I was going to add,”", "\"내가 덧붙이려던 건,\""], ["he said, “that for a pure and saintly woman you will not find one more to your true advantage, and certainly not more to your mother’s mind and my own, than your friend Mercy, whom you used to show a certain interest in.", "그가 말했다. \"순결하고 성스러운 여인으로 말하자면, 네가 한때 어느 정도 관심을 보였던 네 친구 머시보다 네 참된 이익에 더 맞는 사람을, 또 네 어머니와 내 마음에 더 드는 사람을 분명 찾지 못하리라는 거다."], ["It is true that my neighbour Chant’s daughter had lately caught up the fashion of the younger clergy round about us for decorating the Communion-table—alter, as I was shocked to hear her call it one day—with flowers and other stuff on festival occasions.", "사실 내 이웃 챈트의 딸이 근래 우리 주변 젊은 성직자들의 유행을 좇아 축일이면 성찬대를—어느 날 그 애가 제단이라 부르는 걸 듣고 충격을 받았다만—꽃이며 다른 잡것들로 꾸미게 되긴 했지."], ["But her father, who is quite as opposed to such flummery as I, says that can be cured.", "하지만 그 애 아버지는 나만큼이나 그런 겉치레를 반대하는 사람이라, 그건 고칠 수 있다고 하더구나."], ["It is a mere girlish outbreak which, I am sure, will not be permanent.”", "그저 한때의 소녀다운 객기일 뿐이니, 분명 오래가지 않을 게다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes; Mercy is good and devout, I know.", "\"네, 네. 머시가 착하고 독실한 건 압니다."], ["But, father, don’t you think that a young woman equally pure and virtuous as Miss Chant, but one who, in place of that lady’s ecclesiastical accomplishments, understands the duties of farm life as well as a farmer himself, would suit me infinitely better?”", "하지만 아버지, 챈트 양만큼이나 순결하고 정숙하되, 그 숙녀의 교회적 소양 대신 농장 생활의 의무를 농부 자신만큼이나 잘 아는 젊은 여인이라면 저에게 무한히 더 잘 맞으리라고 생각지 않으세요?\""]], [["His father persisted in his conviction that a knowledge of a farmer’s wife’s duties came second to a Pauline view of humanity; and the impulsive Angel, wishing to honour his father’s feelings and to advance the cause of his heart at the same time, grew specious.", "그의 아버지는 농부 아내의 의무에 대한 지식이 인류에 대한 바울적 관점에 비하면 두 번째라는 확신을 고수했다. 그러자 충동적인 앤젤은 아버지의 정서를 존중하는 동시에 자기 마음의 대의를 진척시키고 싶은 마음에 그럴듯해졌다."], ["He said that fate or Providence had thrown in his way a woman who possessed every qualification to be the helpmate of an agriculturist, and was decidedly of a serious turn of mind.", "그는 운명이, 혹은 섭리가 자기 앞에 한 여인을 던져 주었는데, 농부의 반려가 될 모든 자질을 갖추었으며 단연 진지한 성향의 사람이라고 말했다."], ["He would not say whether or not she had attached herself to the sound Low Church School of his father; but she would probably be open to conviction on that point; she was a regular church-goer of simple faith; honest-hearted, receptive, intelligent, graceful to a degree, chaste as a vestal, and, in personal appearance, exceptionally beautiful.", "그녀가 아버지의 건전한 저교회파에 속해 있는지 아닌지는 말하지 않으려 했다. 하지만 그 점에 대해서는 아마 설득에 열려 있을 것이라고, 그녀는 소박한 신앙을 지닌 규칙적인 교회 출석자라고, 정직한 마음에 수용적이고 지적이며 더없이 우아하고 베스타 여사제처럼 순결하며, 외모로는 유난히 아름답다고 말했다."]], [["“Is she of a family such as you would care to marry into—a lady, in short?”", "\"그 애는 네가 결혼하고 싶어 할 만한 집안 사람이냐—요컨대, 숙녀냐?\""], ["asked his startled mother, who had come softly into the study during the conversation.", "대화 도중 슬그머니 서재로 들어와 있던 그의 어머니가 놀라며 물었다."]], [["“She is not what in common parlance is called a lady,”", "\"흔히 말하는 숙녀는 아니에요.\""], ["said Angel, unflinchingly, “for she is a cottager’s daughter, as I am proud to say.", "앤젤이 굽힘 없이 말했다. \"오두막살이꾼의 딸이니까요, 자랑스럽게 말씀드리지만요."], ["But she _is_ a lady, nevertheless—in feeling and nature.”", "하지만 그래도 그녀는 숙녀예요—감정에서나 천성에서나요.\""]], [["“Mercy Chant is of a very good family.”", "\"머시 챈트는 아주 좋은 집안 출신이란다.\""]], [["“Pooh!—what’s the advantage of that, mother?”", "\"체!—그게 무슨 이점이 있어요, 어머니?\""], ["said Angel quickly.", "앤젤이 재빨리 말했다."], ["“How is family to avail the wife of a man who has to rough it as I have, and shall have to do?”", "\"저처럼, 또 앞으로도 거칠게 살아가야 할 남자의 아내에게 집안이 무슨 도움이 되겠어요?\""]], [["“Mercy is accomplished.", "\"머시는 소양이 있어."], ["And accomplishments have their charm,”", "그리고 소양에는 그 나름의 매력이 있단다.\""], ["returned his mother, looking at him through her silver spectacles.", "그의 어머니가 은테 안경 너머로 그를 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["“As to external accomplishments, what will be the use of them in the life I am going to lead?—while as to her reading, I can take that in hand.", "\"외적인 소양으로 말하자면, 제가 살아갈 삶에서 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있겠어요?—그리고 그녀의 학식으로 말하자면, 그건 제가 떠맡을 수 있어요."], ["She’ll be apt pupil enough, as you would say if you knew her.", "그녀는 충분히 영리한 학생이에요, 어머니도 그녀를 아셨다면 그렇게 말씀하셨을 거예요."], ["She’s brim full of poetry—actualized poetry, if I may use the expression.", "그녀는 시로 넘쳐흘러요—실현된 시라고 할까요, 그렇게 표현해도 된다면요."], ["She _lives_ what paper-poets only write...", "종이 위의 시인들이 글로만 쓰는 것을 그녀는 직접 살아요……"], ["And she is an unimpeachable Christian, I am sure; perhaps of the very tribe, genus, and species you desire to propagate.”", "그리고 분명 흠잡을 데 없는 기독교도예요. 어쩌면 어머니가 퍼뜨리고 싶어 하시는 바로 그 부족, 그 속屬, 그 종種의 사람일지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“O Angel, you are mocking!”", "\"오 앤젤, 너 비웃고 있구나!\""]], [["“Mother, I beg pardon.", "\"어머니, 죄송해요."], ["But as she really does attend Church almost every Sunday morning, and is a good Christian girl, I am sure you will tolerate any social shortcomings for the sake of that quality, and feel that I may do worse than choose her.”", "하지만 그녀가 정말로 거의 매주 일요일 아침마다 교회에 가고, 착한 기독교도 처녀이니, 어머니도 그 자질을 봐서 어떤 사회적 결함이든 너그러이 봐주실 거라고, 그녀를 택하느니보다 더 못한 선택을 할 수도 있다고 느끼실 거라고 확신해요.\""], ["Angel waxed quite earnest on that rather automatic orthodoxy in his beloved Tess which (never dreaming that it might stand him in such good stead) he had been prone to slight when observing it practised by her and the other milkmaids, because of its obvious unreality amid beliefs essentially naturalistic.", "앤젤은 사랑하는 테스의 그 다소 기계적인 정통 신앙에 대해 자못 진지해졌다. 그 신앙이 자기에게 이토록 큰 도움이 되리라곤 꿈에도 생각지 못한 채, 그는 그녀와 다른 젖 짜는 처녀들이 그것을 실천하는 것을 볼 때마다, 본질적으로 자연주의적인 믿음들 사이에서 그것이 명백히 비현실적이라는 이유로 그것을 가벼이 여기곤 했던 것이다."]], [["In their sad doubts as to whether their son had himself any right whatever to the title he claimed for the unknown young woman, Mr and Mrs Clare began to feel it as an advantage not to be overlooked that she at least was sound in her views; especially as the conjunction of the pair must have arisen by an act of Providence; for Angel never would have made orthodoxy a condition of his choice.", "이 알지 못하는 젊은 여인에게 아들이 갖다 붙인 그 호칭에 대해 과연 아들 자신이 조금이라도 권리가 있는가 하는 슬픈 의심 속에서, 클레어 부부는 적어도 그녀가 신앙관에서만큼은 건전하다는 점이 간과해서는 안 될 이점이라고 느끼기 시작했다. 특히 이 두 사람의 결합이 섭리의 작용으로 이루어졌음이 분명한 까닭에 더욱 그러했다. 왜냐하면 앤젤은 결코 정통 신앙을 자기 선택의 조건으로 삼을 사람이 아니었기 때문이다."], ["They said finally that it was better not to act in a hurry, but that they would not object to see her.", "그들은 마침내, 서두르지 않는 편이 낫겠지만 그녀를 만나 보는 데는 반대하지 않겠다고 말했다."]], [["Angel therefore refrained from declaring more particulars now.", "그래서 앤젤은 지금 더 자세한 사정을 밝히기를 삼갔다."], ["He felt that, single-minded and self-sacrificing as his parents were, there yet existed certain latent prejudices of theirs, as middle-class people, which it would require some tact to overcome.", "그는 부모가 한결같고 자기희생적이긴 하지만, 그래도 중산층 사람들로서 어떤 잠재된 편견들이 그들 안에 존재한다고, 그것을 극복하려면 어느 정도 요령이 필요하리라고 느꼈다."], ["For though legally at liberty to do as he chose, and though their daughter-in-law’s qualifications could make no practical difference to their lives, in the probability of her living far away from them, he wished for affection’s sake not to wound their sentiment in the most important decision of his life.", "비록 법적으로는 자기 뜻대로 할 자유가 있고, 또 며느리가 자기네에게서 멀리 떨어져 살 가능성이 큰 만큼 그녀의 자질이 자기네 삶에 실질적 차이를 만들 수 없다 해도, 그는 애정을 위해 자기 인생의 가장 중요한 결정에서 그들의 정서를 다치게 하고 싶지 않았다."]], [["He observed his own inconsistencies in dwelling upon accidents in Tess’s life as if they were vital features.", "그는 테스의 삶에서 일어난 우연한 일들을, 마치 그것들이 본질적 특징이기라도 한 양 곱씹는 자신의 모순들을 알아챘다."], ["It was for herself that he loved Tess; her soul, her heart, her substance—not for her skill in the dairy, her aptness as his scholar, and certainly not for her simple formal faith-professions.", "그가 테스를 사랑한 것은 그녀 자신을 위해서였다. 그녀의 영혼, 그녀의 마음, 그녀의 본질을. 낙농장에서의 솜씨나 그의 학생으로서의 재능 때문이 아니었고, 더더욱 그녀의 소박하고 형식적인 신앙 고백 때문은 아니었다."], ["Her unsophisticated open-air existence required no varnish of conventionality to make it palatable to him.", "그녀의 꾸밈없는 야외의 삶은 그가 받아들이기에 관습이라는 광택을 입힐 필요가 없었다."], ["He held that education had as yet but little affected the beats of emotion and impulse on which domestic happiness depends.", "그는 교육이 아직까지는 가정의 행복이 달려 있는 감정과 충동의 박동에 거의 영향을 미치지 못했다고 보았다."], ["It was probable that, in the lapse of ages, improved systems of moral and intellectual training would appreciably, perhaps considerably, elevate the involuntary and even the unconscious instincts of human nature; but up to the present day, culture, as far as he could see, might be said to have affected only the mental epiderm of those lives which had been brought under its influence.", "아마 세월이 흐르면 개선된 도덕적·지적 훈련 체계가 인간 본성의 비자발적, 심지어 무의식적 본능까지도 상당히, 어쩌면 현저히 끌어올릴지도 모른다. 하지만 오늘날까지는, 그가 보기에 교양이란 그 영향 아래 놓인 삶들의 정신적 표피에만 영향을 미쳤다고 할 수 있었다."], ["This belief was confirmed by his experience of women, which, having latterly been extended from the cultivated middle-class into the rural community, had taught him how much less was the intrinsic difference between the good and wise woman of one social stratum and the good and wise woman of another social stratum, than between the good and bad, the wise and the foolish, of the same stratum or class.", "이 믿음은 그가 여성들에 대해 겪은 경험으로 확인되었는데, 근래 그 경험이 교양 있는 중산층에서 시골 공동체로까지 넓어지면서, 한 사회 계층의 착하고 지혜로운 여인과 또 다른 사회 계층의 착하고 지혜로운 여인 사이의 본질적 차이가, 같은 계층이나 계급 안의 착한 자와 나쁜 자, 지혜로운 자와 어리석은 자 사이의 차이보다 얼마나 더 작은지를 그에게 가르쳐 주었다."]], [["It was the morning of his departure.", "그가 떠나는 아침이었다."], ["His brothers had already left the Vicarage to proceed on a walking tour in the north, whence one was to return to his college, and the other to his curacy.", "형들은 이미 목사관을 떠나 북쪽으로 도보 여행에 나섰는데, 거기서 한 사람은 자기 칼리지로, 다른 한 사람은 자기 부목사직으로 돌아갈 참이었다."], ["Angel might have accompanied them, but preferred to rejoin his sweetheart at Talbothays.", "앤젤도 그들과 함께 갈 수 있었지만 톨버세이즈에 있는 연인에게 돌아가는 편을 택했다."], ["He would have been an awkward member of the party; for, though the most appreciative humanist, the most ideal religionist, even the best-versed Christologist of the three, there was alienation in the standing consciousness that his squareness would not fit the round hole that had been prepared for him.", "그는 그 일행 가운데 어색한 일원이 되었을 것이다. 왜냐하면 셋 중 가장 감상력이 뛰어난 인문주의자요, 가장 이상적인 종교인이요, 심지어 가장 정통한 그리스도학자였음에도, 그는 자기 모난 형태가 자기를 위해 마련된 둥근 구멍에 맞지 않으리라는 상존하는 의식 속에서 소외감을 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["To neither Felix nor Cuthbert had he ventured to mention Tess.", "펠릭스에게도 커스버트에게도 그는 감히 테스 이야기를 꺼내지 못했다."]], [["His mother made him sandwiches, and his father accompanied him, on his own mare, a little way along the road.", "그의 어머니는 그에게 샌드위치를 만들어 주었고, 아버지는 자기 암말을 타고 길을 따라 얼마간 그를 배웅했다."], ["Having fairly well advanced his own affairs, Angel listened in a willing silence, as they jogged on together through the shady lanes, to his father’s account of his parish difficulties, and the coldness of brother clergymen whom he loved, because of his strict interpretations of the New Testament by the light of what they deemed a pernicious Calvinistic doctrine.", "자기 일은 꽤 잘 진척시킨 터라, 앤젤은 그늘진 길을 함께 터덜터덜 가면서 아버지가 들려주는 이야기에 기꺼이 침묵하며 귀를 기울였다. 아버지가 신약을 엄격히 해석한다는 이유로—그것을 그들은 해로운 칼뱅주의 교리에 비추어 본 해석이라 여겼는데—아버지가 사랑하는 동료 성직자들이 보이는 냉랭함과 교구의 어려움에 관한 이야기였다."]], [["“Pernicious!”", "\"해롭다니!\""], ["said Mr Clare, with genial scorn; and he proceeded to recount experiences which would show the absurdity of that idea.", "클레어 씨가 너그러운 경멸을 담아 말했다. 그러고는 그 생각의 터무니없음을 보여 줄 경험들을 들려 나가기 시작했다."], ["He told of wondrous conversions of evil livers of which he had been the instrument, not only amongst the poor, but amongst the rich and well-to-do; and he also candidly admitted many failures.", "그는 자기가 그 도구가 되어 이루어진, 악하게 살던 자들의 놀라운 회심들에 대해 이야기했는데, 가난한 자들 사이에서뿐 아니라 부유하고 넉넉한 자들 사이에서도 그러했다고 했다. 또한 그는 수많은 실패도 솔직히 인정했다."]], [["As an instance of the latter, he mentioned the case of a young upstart squire named d’Urberville, living some forty miles off, in the neighbourhood of Trantridge.", "후자의 한 사례로, 그는 사십 마일쯤 떨어진 트랜트리지 근방에 사는, 더버빌이라는 이름의 한 젊은 벼락출세 지주의 경우를 언급했다."]], [["“Not one of the ancient d’Urbervilles of Kingsbere and other places?”", "\"킹스비어를 비롯한 여러 곳의 그 옛 더버빌가 사람은 아니겠죠?\""], ["asked his son. “That curiously historic worn-out family with its ghostly legend of the coach-and-four?”", "그의 아들이 물었다. \"사두마차 유령 전설을 지닌, 그 묘하게 유서 깊고 쇠락한 가문 말이에요.\""]], [["“O no. The original d’Urbervilles decayed and disappeared sixty or eighty years ago—at least, I believe so.", "\"오 아니다. 원래의 더버빌가는 육칠십 년, 아니 팔십 년 전에 쇠락해 사라졌단다—적어도 내가 알기론 그래."], ["This seems to be a new family which had taken the name; for the credit of the former knightly line I hope they are spurious, I’m sure.", "이건 그 이름을 가져다 쓴 새 가문인 듯하구나. 옛 기사 가문의 명예를 위해서라도, 이들이 가짜이기를 바란다, 정말이지."], ["But it is odd to hear you express interest in old families.", "하지만 네가 옛 가문에 관심을 표하는 걸 들으니 이상하구나."], ["I thought you set less store by them even than I.”", "너는 나보다도 그런 걸 덜 중히 여기는 줄 알았다.\""]], [["“You misapprehend me, father; you often do,”", "\"저를 오해하시는 거예요, 아버지. 자주 그러시죠.\""], ["said Angel with a little impatience.", "앤젤이 약간 짜증스레 말했다."], ["“Politically I am sceptical as to the virtue of their being old.", "\"정치적으로 저는 가문이 오래되었다는 것의 미덕에 대해 회의적이에요."], ["Some of the wise even among themselves ‘exclaim against their own succession,’ as Hamlet puts it; but lyrically, dramatically, and even historically, I am tenderly attached to them.”", "그들 가운데 현명한 자들조차 어떤 이들은 '제 가문의 계승을 한탄한다'고, 햄릿의 말처럼요. 하지만 서정적으로, 극적으로, 심지어 역사적으로 저는 그들에게 다정한 애착을 느껴요.\""]], [["This distinction, though by no means a subtle one, was yet too subtle for Mr Clare the elder, and he went on with the story he had been about to relate; which was that after the death of the senior so-called d’Urberville, the young man developed the most culpable passions, though he had a blind mother, whose condition should have made him know better.", "이 구별은, 결코 미묘한 것이 아니었음에도, 연장자 클레어 씨에게는 너무 미묘했다. 그는 막 들려주려던 이야기를 이어 갔는데, 그 내용인즉 이러했다. 이른바 더버빌이라는 노인이 죽은 후 그 젊은이는 가장 비난받아 마땅한 정욕을 키웠는데, 그에게는 앞 못 보는 어머니가 있어 그 처지를 보면 더 분별 있게 굴었어야 마땅했다는 것이다."], ["A knowledge of his career having come to the ears of Mr Clare, when he was in that part of the country preaching missionary sermons, he boldly took occasion to speak to the delinquent on his spiritual state.", "클레어 씨가 선교 설교를 하러 그 지방에 갔을 때 그 젊은이의 행적에 관한 이야기가 그의 귀에 들어왔고, 그는 그 죄인에게 그의 영적 상태에 관해 말할 기회를 대담하게 붙잡았다."], ["Though he was a stranger, occupying another’s pulpit, he had felt this to be his duty, and took for his text the words from St Luke: “Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee!”", "비록 그가 이방인으로서 남의 설교단을 차지하고 있는 처지였음에도, 그는 이것을 자기 의무로 느꼈고, 누가복음의 말씀을 본문으로 삼았다. \"어리석은 자여, 오늘 밤에 네 영혼을 도로 찾으리라!\""], ["The young man much resented this directness of attack, and in the war of words which followed when they met he did not scruple publicly to insult Mr Clare, without respect for his gray hairs.", "그 젊은이는 이 직설적 공격에 몹시 분개했고, 둘이 마주쳐 벌어진 말다툼에서 그는 클레어 씨의 백발을 존중하지 않은 채 거리낌 없이 공공연히 그를 모욕했다."]], [["Angel flushed with distress.", "앤젤은 고통으로 얼굴이 붉어졌다."]], [["“Dear father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["he said sadly, “I wish you would not expose yourself to such gratuitous pain from scoundrels!”", "그가 슬프게 말했다. \"악당들 때문에 그런 까닭 없는 고통에 스스로를 내맡기지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요!\""]], [["“Pain?” said his father, his rugged face shining in the ardour of self-abnegation.", "\"고통이라?\" 그의 아버지가 자기부정의 열정으로 거친 얼굴을 빛내며 말했다."], ["“The only pain to me was pain on his account, poor, foolish young man.", "\"나에게 유일한 고통이 있었다면 그 가엾고 어리석은 젊은이를 위한 고통이었지."], ["Do you suppose his incensed words could give me any pain, or even his blows?", "너는 그자의 격분한 말이 나에게 무슨 고통을 줄 수 있다고 생각하느냐, 아니 그자의 매질인들?"], ["‘Being reviled we bless; being persecuted we suffer it; being defamed we entreat; we are made as the filth of the world, and as the offscouring of all things unto this day.’ Those ancient and noble words to the Corinthians are strictly true at this present hour.”", "'우리가 욕을 먹은즉 축복하고, 박해를 받은즉 참고, 비방을 받은즉 권면하니, 우리가 지금까지 세상의 더러운 것과 만물의 찌꺼기같이 되었도다.' 고린도인들에게 한 그 옛날의 고귀한 말씀이 바로 이 시간에도 엄연히 진실하구나.\""]], [["“Not blows, father? He did not proceed to blows?”", "\"매질은 아니죠, 아버지? 매질까지 하진 않았겠죠?\""]], [["“No, he did not.", "\"아니, 하진 않았다."], ["Though I have borne blows from men in a mad state of intoxication.” “No!”", "하지만 나는 술에 미친 상태의 사람들에게서 매질을 견딘 적이 있단다.\""]], [["“A dozen times, my boy. What then?", "\"설마요!\" \"열두 번도 더 그랬다, 얘야."], ["I have saved them from the guilt of murdering their own flesh and blood thereby; and they have lived to thank me, and praise God.”", "그래서 어떻단 말이냐? 나는 그렇게 함으로써 그들이 제 살붙이를 죽이는 죄에서 구해 주었고, 그들은 살아남아 나에게 감사하고 하느님을 찬양했단다.\""]], [["“May this young man do the same!”", "\"이 젊은이도 그러기를 바랍니다!\""], ["said Angel fervently.", "앤젤이 열렬히 말했다."], ["“But I fear otherwise, from what you say.”", "\"하지만 아버지 말씀으로 미루어, 그렇지 못할까 봐 두렵네요.\""]], [["“We’ll hope, nevertheless,”", "\"그래도 우리는 희망을 품자꾸나.\""], ["said Mr Clare.", "클레어 씨가 말했다."], ["“And I continue to pray for him, though on this side of the grave we shall probably never meet again.", "\"그리고 나는 그를 위해 계속 기도하련다. 무덤 이편에서는 아마 다시는 만나지 못하겠지만 말이다."], ["But, after all, one of those poor words of mine may spring up in his heart as a good seed some day.”", "하지만 어쨌든 그 가엾은 내 말 한마디가 언젠가 그의 마음속에서 좋은 씨앗으로 싹틀지도 모르지.\""]], [["Now, as always, Clare’s father was sanguine as a child; and though the younger could not accept his parent’s narrow dogma, he revered his practice and recognized the hero under the pietist.", "지금도 늘 그렇듯, 클레어의 아버지는 어린아이처럼 낙천적이었다. 그리고 젊은이는 아버지의 편협한 교리를 받아들일 수는 없었지만, 그 실천을 우러러보았고 신앙가의 모습 아래 깃든 영웅을 알아보았다."], ["Perhaps he revered his father’s practice even more now than ever, seeing that, in the question of making Tessy his wife, his father had not once thought of inquiring whether she were well provided or penniless.", "어쩌면 그는 지금 그 어느 때보다 더 아버지의 실천을 우러러보았는지도 모른다. 테스를 아내로 삼는 문제에서 아버지가 그녀가 살림이 넉넉한지 무일푼인지 단 한 번도 캐물어 볼 생각조차 하지 않았음을 보았기에 말이다."], ["The same unworldliness was what had necessitated Angel’s getting a living as a farmer, and would probably keep his brothers in the position of poor parsons for the term of their activities; yet Angel admired it none the less.", "바로 그 세속에 무심함이 앤젤로 하여금 농부로서 생계를 꾸리게 만든 까닭이었고, 또 그것이 아마 그의 형들을 평생 가난한 목사의 처지에 묶어 두게 될 터였다. 그럼에도 앤젤은 그것을 못지않게 찬미했다."], ["Indeed, despite his own heterodoxy, Angel often felt that he was nearer to his father on the human side than was either of his brethren.", "정말이지, 자기 자신의 이단적 성향에도 불구하고 앤젤은 인간적인 면에서는 자기가 두 형제 중 어느 쪽보다도 아버지에게 더 가깝다고 자주 느꼈다."]], [["# XXVII", "# XXVII"]], [["An up-hill and down-hill ride of twenty-odd miles through a garish mid-day atmosphere brought him in the afternoon to a detached knoll a mile or two west of Talbothays, whence he again looked into that green trough of sappiness and humidity, the valley of the Var or Froom.", "눈부신 한낮의 대기 속에서 이십 몇 마일의 오르막과 내리막 길을 말로 달린 끝에, 그는 오후에 톨버세이즈에서 서쪽으로 한두 마일 떨어진 외딴 둔덕에 이르렀고, 거기서 다시 그 푸르고 수액 가득하고 습한 골짜기, 바르 혹은 프룸 골짜기를 내려다보았다."], ["Immediately he began to descend from the upland to the fat alluvial soil below, the atmosphere grew heavier; the languid perfume of the summer fruits, the mists, the hay, the flowers, formed therein a vast pool of odour which at this hour seemed to make the animals, the very bees and butterflies drowsy.", "그가 고지대에서 아래쪽 기름진 충적토로 내려가기 시작하자마자 대기가 더 무거워졌다. 여름 과일의 나른한 향기, 안개, 건초, 꽃들이 그 안에서 거대한 향기의 못을 이루었고, 그것이 이 시각에는 짐승들을, 바로 그 벌과 나비까지도 졸리게 만드는 듯했다."], ["Clare was now so familiar with the spot that he knew the individual cows by their names when, a long distance off, he saw them dotted about the meads.", "클레어는 이제 이 장소에 너무도 익숙해져서, 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 풀밭 여기저기에 점점이 흩어진 젖소들을 보아도 한 마리 한 마리 이름으로 알아보았다."], ["It was with a sense of luxury that he recognized his power of viewing life here from its inner side, in a way that had been quite foreign to him in his student-days; and, much as he loved his parents, he could not help being aware that to come here, as now, after an experience of home-life, affected him like throwing off splints and bandages; even the one customary curb on the humours of English rural societies being absent in this place, Talbothays having no resident landlord.", "그는 여기서 삶을 그 안쪽 면에서, 학창 시절에는 전혀 낯설었던 방식으로 바라볼 수 있는 자기 능력을 사치스러운 기분으로 깨달았다. 그리고 부모를 그토록 사랑하면서도, 가정생활을 경험한 후에 지금처럼 이곳에 오는 것이 마치 부목과 붕대를 벗어 던지는 것 같은 느낌을 준다는 것을 깨닫지 않을 수 없었다. 영국 시골 사회의 기질에 가해지는 그 한 가지 관습적 굴레마저도 이곳에는 없었으니, 톨버세이즈에는 그곳에 거주하는 지주가 없었던 것이다."]], [["Not a human being was out of doors at the dairy.", "낙농장 밖에는 사람 하나 나와 있지 않았다."], ["The denizens were all enjoying the usual afternoon nap of an hour or so which the exceedingly early hours kept in summer-time rendered a necessity.", "거주민들은 모두 한 시간쯤 되는 평소의 오후 낮잠을 즐기고 있었는데, 여름철에 지키는 지독히 이른 시간 탓에 그 낮잠은 필요한 것이 되어 있었다."], ["At the door the wood-hooped pails, sodden and bleached by infinite scrubbings, hung like hats on a stand upon the forked and peeled limb of an oak fixed there for that purpose; all of them ready and dry for the evening milking.", "문가에는 나무 테를 두른 들통들이, 무수한 문질러 닦기에 흠뻑 젖고 바래진 채로, 그 목적을 위해 거기 고정해 둔 껍질 벗긴 갈래진 떡갈나무 가지 위에 모자걸이의 모자처럼 걸려 있었다. 모두 저녁 젖 짜기를 위해 준비되어 마른 상태였다."], ["Angel entered, and went through the silent passages of the house to the back quarters, where he listened for a moment.", "앤젤은 안으로 들어가 집의 고요한 통로들을 지나 뒤편 구역으로 갔고, 거기서 잠시 귀를 기울였다."], ["Sustained snores came from the cart-house, where some of the men were lying down; the grunt and squeal of sweltering pigs arose from the still further distance.", "사내들 몇이 누워 있는 짐수레 헛간에서 끊임없는 코 고는 소리가 들려왔다. 더 멀리 떨어진 곳에서는 더위에 헐떡이는 돼지들의 꿀꿀거리고 꽥꽥거리는 소리가 일었다."], ["The large-leaved rhubarb and cabbage plants slept too, their broad limp surfaces hanging in the sun like half-closed umbrellas.", "잎이 넓은 대황과 양배추 포기들도 잠들어 있었는데, 그 넓고 축 늘어진 표면들이 반쯤 접은 우산처럼 햇볕 속에 드리워져 있었다."]], [["He unbridled and fed his horse, and as he re-entered the house the clock struck three.", "그는 말의 굴레를 벗기고 먹이를 주었고, 다시 집으로 들어설 때 시계가 세 시를 쳤다."], ["Three was the afternoon skimming-hour; and, with the stroke, Clare heard the creaking of the floor-boards above, and then the touch of a descending foot on the stairs.", "세 시는 오후의 크림 걷어 내는 시각이었다. 그리고 그 종소리와 함께 클레어는 위층 마룻널이 삐걱대는 소리를, 이어서 계단을 내려오는 발길의 닿는 소리를 들었다."], ["It was Tess’s, who in another moment came down before his eyes.", "그것은 테스의 것이었고, 그녀는 다음 순간 그의 눈앞으로 내려왔다."]], [["She had not heard him enter, and hardly realized his presence there.", "그녀는 그가 들어온 것을 듣지 못했고, 그가 거기 있다는 것을 거의 알아채지 못했다."], ["She was yawning, and he saw the red interior of her mouth as if it had been a snake’s.", "그녀는 하품을 하고 있었고, 그는 마치 뱀의 입 같은 그녀의 붉은 입속을 보았다."], ["She had stretched one arm so high above her coiled-up cable of hair that he could see its satin delicacy above the sunburn; her face was flushed with sleep, and her eyelids hung heavy over their pupils.", "그녀가 한쪽 팔을 둘둘 감아 올린 머리 타래 위로 한껏 높이 뻗고 있어서, 그는 햇볕에 그을린 자국 위쪽으로 그 새틴 같은 섬세함을 볼 수 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 잠으로 발그레했고, 눈꺼풀은 동공 위로 무겁게 드리워져 있었다."], ["The brim-fulness of her nature breathed from her.", "그녀의 천성에 가득 찬 충만함이 그녀에게서 숨결처럼 풍겨 나왔다."], ["It was a moment when a woman’s soul is more incarnate than at any other time; when the most spiritual beauty bespeaks itself flesh; and sex takes the outside place in the presentation.", "그것은 여인의 영혼이 다른 어느 때보다 더 육화되는 순간이었다. 가장 영적인 아름다움이 스스로를 육체로 드러내는 순간, 그리고 성性이 그 드러남에서 바깥 자리를 차지하는 순간이었다."]], [["Then those eyes flashed brightly through their filmy heaviness, before the remainder of her face was well awake.", "그러더니 얼굴의 나머지가 채 깨어나기도 전에 그 눈이 흐릿한 무거움을 뚫고 밝게 번뜩였다."], ["With an oddly compounded look of gladness, shyness, and surprise, she exclaimed—“O Mr Clare! How you frightened me—I—”", "기쁨과 수줍음과 놀라움이 묘하게 뒤섞인 표정으로 그녀가 외쳤다—\"오 클레어 씨! 어쩌면 그렇게 절 놀라게 하세요—저—\""]], [["There had not at first been time for her to think of the changed relations which his declaration had introduced; but the full sense of the matter rose up in her face when she encountered Clare’s tender look as he stepped forward to the bottom stair.", "처음에는 그의 고백이 들여온 변화된 관계를 생각할 겨를이 없었다. 하지만 그가 계단 맨 아래로 걸음을 내디딜 때 그녀가 그의 다정한 눈길과 마주치자, 그 일의 온전한 의미가 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다."]], [["“Dear, darling Tessy!”", "\"사랑하는, 귀여운 테스!\""], ["he whispered, putting his arm round her, and his face to her flushed cheek.", "그가 그녀에게 팔을 두르고 자기 얼굴을 그녀의 발그레한 뺨에 갖다 대며 속삭였다."], ["“Don’t, for Heaven’s sake, Mister me any more.", "\"제발, 부디 더는 나를 '씨'라고 부르지 말아요."], ["I have hastened back so soon because of you!”", "당신 때문에 이렇게 일찍 서둘러 돌아온 거예요!\""]], [["Tess’s excitable heart beat against his by way of reply; and there they stood upon the red-brick floor of the entry, the sun slanting in by the window upon his back, as he held her tightly to his breast; upon her inclining face, upon the blue veins of her temple, upon her naked arm, and her neck, and into the depths of her hair.", "테스의 격하기 쉬운 심장이 대답 대신 그의 심장에 부딪쳐 뛰었다. 그렇게 그들은 현관의 붉은 벽돌 바닥에 서 있었고, 그가 그녀를 자기 가슴에 꼭 끌어안는 동안 해가 창문으로 비스듬히 들어와 그의 등에, 그녀의 기울인 얼굴에, 그녀의 관자놀이의 푸른 핏줄에, 그녀의 맨팔과 목에, 그리고 그녀의 머리칼 깊은 곳까지 내리쬐었다."], ["Having been lying down in her clothes she was warm as a sunned cat.", "옷을 입은 채로 누워 있던 터라 그녀는 햇볕을 쬔 고양이처럼 따스했다."], ["At first she would not look straight up at him, but her eyes soon lifted, and his plumbed the deepness of the ever-varying pupils, with their radiating fibrils of blue, and black, and gray, and violet, while she regarded him as Eve at her second waking might have regarded Adam.", "처음에 그녀는 그를 똑바로 올려다보려 하지 않았으나, 곧 눈을 들었고, 그의 눈은 끊임없이 변하는 동공의 깊이를, 푸른빛과 검은빛과 잿빛과 보랏빛으로 뻗어 나가는 그 섬유들을 가늠하며 깊이 잠겼다. 그동안 그녀는 마치 두 번째로 깨어난 이브가 아담을 바라보았을 법한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I’ve got to go a-skimming,”", "\"저 크림 걷으러 가야 해요.\""], ["she pleaded, “and I have on’y old Deb to help me to-day.", "그녀가 사정하듯 말했다. \"오늘은 늙은 뎁밖에 도울 사람이 없어요."], ["Mrs Crick is gone to market with Mr Crick, and Retty is not well, and the others are gone out somewhere, and won’t be home till milking.”", "크릭 부인은 크릭 씨랑 장에 가셨고, 레티는 몸이 안 좋고, 다른 애들은 어딘가로 나가서 젖 짤 때까진 안 돌아올 거예요.\""]], [["As they retreated to the milk-house Deborah Fyander appeared on the stairs.", "그들이 우유간으로 물러갈 때 데버라 파이앤더가 계단에 나타났다."]], [["“I have come back, Deborah,”", "\"내가 돌아왔어요, 데버라.\""], ["said Mr Clare, upwards.", "클레어 씨가 위쪽을 향해 말했다."], ["“So I can help Tess with the skimming; and, as you are very tired, I am sure, you needn’t come down till milking-time.”", "\"그러니 내가 테스의 크림 걷는 일을 도울 수 있어요. 그리고 분명 무척 피곤할 테니, 젖 짤 때까진 내려오지 않아도 돼요.\""]], [["Possibly the Talbothays milk was not very thoroughly skimmed that afternoon.", "어쩌면 그날 오후 톨버세이즈의 우유는 크림이 그리 철저히 걷어지지 못했을 것이다."], ["Tess was in a dream wherein familiar objects appeared as having light and shade and position, but no particular outline.", "테스는 익숙한 물건들이 빛과 그늘과 위치는 지녔으되 뚜렷한 윤곽은 없는 듯이 보이는 꿈속에 있었다."], ["Every time she held the skimmer under the pump to cool it for the work her hand trembled, the ardour of his affection being so palpable that she seemed to flinch under it like a plant in too burning a sun.", "일을 위해 크림 걷는 도구를 식히려고 펌프 아래 댈 때마다 그녀의 손은 떨렸다. 그의 애정의 열기가 너무도 또렷이 느껴져, 그녀는 너무 뜨거운 햇볕 속의 식물처럼 그 아래서 움츠러드는 듯했다."]], [["Then he pressed her again to his side, and when she had done running her forefinger round the leads to cut off the cream-edge, he cleaned it in nature’s way; for the unconstrained manners of Talbothays dairy came convenient now.", "그러자 그가 다시 그녀를 자기 옆으로 끌어당겼고, 그녀가 크림 가장자리를 잘라 내려고 통 안쪽 둘레를 집게손가락으로 빙 두르고 나자, 그가 자연스러운 방식으로 그 손가락을 깨끗이 했다. 톨버세이즈 낙농장의 거리낌 없는 풍습이 지금은 편리했던 것이다."]], [["“I may as well say it now as later, dearest,” he resumed gently.", "\"지금 말하나 나중에 말하나 마찬가지니, 사랑하는 사람.\""], ["“I wish to ask you something of a very practical nature, which I have been thinking of ever since that day last week in the meads.", "그가 부드럽게 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"아주 실질적인 성격의 무언가를 당신에게 묻고 싶어요. 지난주 풀밭에서의 그날 이후로 줄곧 생각해 온 거예요."], ["I shall soon want to marry, and, being a farmer, you see I shall require for my wife a woman who knows all about the management of farms. Will you be that woman, Tessy?”", "나는 곧 결혼하고 싶고, 농부이니, 보다시피 내 아내로는 농장 운영에 관해 다 아는 여자가 필요해요. 당신이 그 여자가 되어 주겠어요, 테스?\""]], [["He put it that way that she might not think he had yielded to an impulse of which his head would disapprove.", "그는 자기 머리라면 못마땅해했을 충동에 그가 굴복했다고 그녀가 생각하지 않도록 그렇게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["She turned quite careworn.", "그녀의 얼굴이 온통 근심으로 물들었다."], ["She had bowed to the inevitable result of proximity, the necessity of loving him; but she had not calculated upon this sudden corollary, which, indeed, Clare had put before her without quite meaning himself to do it so soon.", "그녀는 가까이 있음의 피할 수 없는 결과에, 그를 사랑할 수밖에 없음에 굴복했었다. 하지만 이 갑작스러운 귀결까지는 셈에 넣지 못했는데, 사실 클레어 자신도 이렇게 빨리 그것을 꺼낼 생각은 아니었으면서 그녀 앞에 내놓은 것이었다."], ["With pain that was like the bitterness of dissolution she murmured the words of her indispensable and sworn answer as an honourable woman.", "소멸의 쓰라림 같은 고통과 함께 그녀는, 명예로운 여자로서 반드시 해야 하고 또 맹세코 해야 할 대답의 말을 중얼거렸다."]], [["“O Mr Clare—I cannot be your wife—I cannot be!”", "\"오 클레어 씨—저는 당신의 아내가 될 수 없어요—될 수 없어요!\""]], [["The sound of her own decision seemed to break Tess’s very heart, and she bowed her face in her grief.", "자기 자신의 결정이 내는 소리가 테스의 심장 자체를 부수는 듯했고, 그녀는 비탄에 잠겨 얼굴을 숙였다."]], [["“But, Tess!” he said, amazed at her reply, and holding her still more greedily close.", "\"하지만, 테스!\" 그가 그녀의 대답에 놀라며, 그녀를 더욱더 탐욕스레 끌어안으며 말했다."], ["“Do you say no?", "\"안 된다는 거예요?"], ["Surely you love me?”", "분명 나를 사랑하잖아요?\""]], [["“O yes, yes!", "\"오 네, 네!"], ["And I would rather be yours than anybody’s in the world,”", "그리고 저는 세상 누구의 것이 되느니보다 당신의 것이 되고 싶어요.\""], ["returned the sweet and honest voice of the distressed girl. “But I _cannot_ marry you!”", "괴로워하는 처녀의 다정하고 정직한 목소리가 대답했다. \"하지만 당신과 결혼할 수는 없어요!\""]], [["“Tess,”", "\"테스.\""], ["he said, holding her at arm’s length, “you are engaged to marry some one else!”", "그가 그녀를 팔 길이만큼 떼어 놓고 말했다. \"당신, 다른 누군가와 결혼하기로 약혼했군요!\" \"아니에요, 아니에요!\""]], [["“No, no!” “Then why do you refuse me?”", "\"그럼 왜 나를 거절하는 거예요?\""]], [["“I don’t want to marry!", "\"저는 결혼하고 싶지 않아요!"], ["I have not thought of doing it. I cannot!", "그럴 생각을 해 본 적이 없어요. 그럴 수 없어요!"], ["I only want to love you.”", "저는 그저 당신을 사랑하고 싶을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“But why?”", "\"하지만 왜요?\""]], [["Driven to subterfuge, she stammered—", "둘러댈 수밖에 없어, 그녀는 더듬더듬 말했다—"]], [["“Your father is a parson, and your mother wouldn’ like you to marry such as me.", "\"당신 아버지는 목사님이고, 어머니는 저 같은 사람과 당신이 결혼하는 걸 좋아하지 않으실 거예요."], ["She will want you to marry a lady.”", "당신이 숙녀와 결혼하기를 바라실 거예요.\""]], [["“Nonsense—I have spoken to them both.", "\"말도 안 돼요—내가 두 분 모두에게 말씀드렸어요."], ["That was partly why I went home.”", "그게 내가 집에 간 이유 중 하나였어요.\""]], [["“I feel I cannot—never, never!”", "\"저는 안 된다고 느껴요—절대로, 절대로요!\""], ["she echoed.", "그녀가 되뇌었다."]], [["“Is it too sudden to be asked thus, my Pretty?”", "\"이렇게 묻는 게 너무 갑작스러운 거예요, 내 예쁜 사람?\""]], [["“Yes—I did not expect it.”", "\"네—예상하지 못했어요.\""]], [["“If you will let it pass, please, Tessy, I will give you time,” he said.", "\"부디, 이 일을 넘겨 버려도 된다면, 테스, 내가 시간을 줄게요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“It was very abrupt to come home and speak to you all at once.", "\"집에 와서 당신 모두에게 한꺼번에 말하는 건 정말 갑작스러운 일이었어요."], ["I’ll not allude to it again for a while.”", "한동안은 다시 이 얘기를 꺼내지 않을게요.\""]], [["She again took up the shining skimmer, held it beneath the pump, and began anew.", "그녀는 다시 반짝이는 크림 걷는 도구를 집어 들고 펌프 아래 댄 다음 새로 일을 시작했다."], ["But she could not, as at other times, hit the exact under-surface of the cream with the delicate dexterity required, try as she might; sometimes she was cutting down into the milk, sometimes in the air.", "하지만 다른 때처럼, 아무리 애를 써도 필요한 그 섬세한 솜씨로 크림 바로 아래 표면을 정확히 짚어 낼 수가 없었다. 어떤 때는 우유 속으로 파고들었고, 어떤 때는 허공을 갈랐다."], ["She could hardly see, her eyes having filled with two blurring tears drawn forth by a grief which, to this her best friend and dear advocate, she could never explain.", "그녀는 거의 앞을 볼 수가 없었으니, 이 가장 좋은 벗이자 사랑하는 옹호자에게는 결코 설명할 수 없는 비탄이 자아낸 두 줄기 흐릿한 눈물이 두 눈에 가득 차 있었던 것이다."]], [["“I can’t skim—I can’t!” she said, turning away from him.", "\"크림을 못 걷겠어요—못 하겠어요!\" 그녀가 그에게서 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["Not to agitate and hinder her longer, the considerate Clare began talking in a more general way:", "더는 그녀를 동요시키고 방해하지 않으려고, 사려 깊은 클레어는 더 일반적인 방향으로 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“You quite misapprehend my parents.", "\"당신은 우리 부모님을 아주 오해하고 있어요."], ["They are the most simple-mannered people alive, and quite unambitious.", "그분들은 세상에서 가장 소박한 분들이고, 전혀 야심이 없어요."], ["They are two of the few remaining Evangelical school.", "몇 안 남은 복음주의 학파의 두 분이죠."], ["Tessy, are you an Evangelical?”", "테스, 당신은 복음주의자예요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“You go to church very regularly, and our parson here is not very High, they tell me.”", "\"당신은 아주 규칙적으로 교회에 가고, 여기 우리 목사님은 그리 고교회파가 아니라고들 하더군요.\""]], [["Tess’s ideas on the views of the parish clergyman, whom she heard every week, seemed to be rather more vague than Clare’s, who had never heard him at all.", "테스가 매주 듣는 교구 성직자의 신앙관에 대해 갖고 있는 생각은, 그의 설교를 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 클레어의 것보다도 오히려 더 막연한 듯했다."]], [["“I wish I could fix my mind on what I hear there more firmly than I do,”", "\"거기서 듣는 걸 제 마음에 지금보다 더 단단히 새길 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["she remarked as a safe generality.", "그녀가 안전한 일반론으로 말했다."], ["“It is often a great sorrow to me.”", "\"그게 자주 저에겐 큰 슬픔이에요.\""]], [["She spoke so unaffectedly that Angel was sure in his heart that his father could not object to her on religious grounds, even though she did not know whether her principles were High, Low or Broad.", "그녀가 너무도 꾸밈없이 말했으므로, 앤젤은 그녀가 자기 신조가 고교회파인지 저교회파인지 광교회파인지 모른다 해도 아버지가 종교적 이유로 그녀를 반대할 수는 없으리라고 마음속으로 확신했다."], ["He himself knew that, in reality, the confused beliefs which she held, apparently imbibed in childhood, were, if anything, Tractarian as to phraseology, and Pantheistic as to essence.", "그는, 사실인즉 그녀가 품은 그 혼란스러운 믿음들이, 보아하니 어린 시절에 빨아들인 것들로서, 굳이 따지자면 어법에서는 트랙트파적이고 본질에서는 범신론적이라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Confused or otherwise, to disturb them was his last desire:", "혼란스럽든 어떻든, 그것을 어지럽히는 것은 그가 가장 바라지 않는 바였다."]], [["Leave thou thy sister, when she prays, Her early Heaven, her happy views; Nor thou with shadow’d hint confuse A life that leads melodious days.", "그대의 누이가 기도할 때, 그녀를 내버려 두라, 그녀의 이른 천국을, 그녀의 행복한 신앙관을. 그대는 그늘진 암시로, 가락 고운 나날을 이끌어 가는 한 생을 어지럽히지 말라."]], [["He had occasionally thought the counsel less honest than musical; but he gladly conformed to it now.", "그는 이따금 이 충고가 가락만큼 정직하지는 못하다고 생각했었다. 하지만 지금은 기꺼이 그것을 따랐다."]], [["He spoke further of the incidents of his visit, of his father’s mode of life, of his zeal for his principles; she grew serener, and the undulations disappeared from her skimming; as she finished one lead after another he followed her, and drew the plugs for letting down the milk.", "그는 자기 방문에서 있었던 일들에 대해, 아버지의 생활 방식에 대해, 자기 원칙을 향한 아버지의 열정에 대해 더 이야기했다. 그녀는 더 평온해졌고, 크림 걷는 손길에서 출렁임이 사라졌다. 그녀가 통 하나하나를 차례로 끝낼 때마다 그가 뒤를 따르며 우유를 흘려보낼 마개를 뽑았다."]], [["“I fancied you looked a little downcast when you came in,”", "\"들어오실 때 좀 의기소침해 보이시던데요.\""], ["she ventured to observe, anxious to keep away from the subject of herself.", "그녀가 자기 이야기에서 멀어지고 싶은 마음에 조심스레 말해 보았다."]], [["“Yes—well, my father had been talking a good deal to me of his troubles and difficulties, and the subject always tends to depress me.", "\"네—그게, 아버지가 자기 근심과 어려움에 대해 나에게 많은 얘기를 하셨는데, 그 주제는 늘 나를 우울하게 해요."], ["He is so zealous that he gets many snubs and buffetings from people of a different way of thinking from himself, and I don’t like to hear of such humiliations to a man of his age, the more particularly as I don’t think earnestness does any good when carried so far.", "아버지가 어찌나 열성적인지 자기와 생각이 다른 사람들에게서 숱한 면박과 매질을 받으시는데, 그 연세의 분이 그런 굴욕을 당했다는 얘기를 듣는 게 나는 싫어요. 더욱이 열성이 그렇게까지 지나치면 아무 소용도 없다고 생각하니 더 그렇고요."], ["He has been telling me of a very unpleasant scene in which he took part quite recently.", "아주 최근에 아버지가 끼어든 무척 불쾌한 장면에 대해 얘기해 주셨어요."], ["He went as the deputy of some missionary society to preach in the neighbourhood of Trantridge, a place forty miles from here, and made it his business to expostulate with a lax young cynic he met with somewhere about there—son of some landowner up that way—and who has a mother afflicted with blindness.", "아버지는 어느 선교 단체의 대리인으로 여기서 사십 마일 떨어진 트랜트리지 근방에 설교하러 가셨다가, 거기 어딘가에서 마주친 방탕한 젊은 냉소가에게—그 동네 어느 지주의 아들로, 앞 못 보는 어머니를 둔 자에게—훈계하는 걸 자기 일로 삼으셨대요. 아버지가 그 신사에게 정면으로 말을 거셨고, 큰 소동이 일었죠."], ["My father addressed himself to the gentleman point-blank, and there was quite a disturbance. It was very foolish of my father, I must say, to intrude his conversation upon a stranger when the probabilities were so obvious that it would be useless.", "정말이지, 아무 소용없을 게 그토록 뻔한데도 낯선 사람에게 대화를 강요하다니 아버지가 참 어리석으셨어요. 하지만 무엇이든 자기 의무라고 생각하면 때를 가리지 않고 하시는 분이라, 물론 적을 많이 만드시죠."], ["But whatever he thinks to be his duty, that he’ll do, in season or out of season; and, of course, he makes many enemies, not only among the absolutely vicious, but among the easy-going, who hate being bothered.", "철저히 악한 자들 사이에서뿐 아니라, 성가신 일을 싫어하는 태평한 자들 사이에서도요. 아버지는 그 일을 자랑스러워하신다고, 또 간접적으로 선이 이루어질 수도 있다고 하세요."], ["He says he glories in what happened, and that good may be done indirectly; but I wish he would not wear himself out now he is getting old, and would leave such pigs to their wallowing.”", "하지만 나는 이제 연로해지셨으니 아버지가 그렇게 자신을 소진하지 않으셨으면, 그런 돼지들은 진창에서 뒹굴게 내버려 두셨으면 해요.\""]], [["Tess’s look had grown hard and worn, and her ripe mouth tragical; but she no longer showed any tremulousness.", "테스의 표정은 굳고 지쳐 보였고, 그 무르익은 입은 비극적이었다. 하지만 그녀는 더는 어떤 떨림도 보이지 않았다."], ["Clare’s revived thoughts of his father prevented his noticing her particularly; and so they went on down the white row of liquid rectangles till they had finished and drained them off, when the other maids returned, and took their pails, and Deb came to scald out the leads for the new milk.", "클레어는 되살아난 아버지에 대한 생각 때문에 그녀를 특별히 주목하지 못했다. 그렇게 그들은 액체의 직사각형들이 이룬 하얀 줄을 따라 계속 내려가며 그것들을 다 끝내고 흘려보냈고, 그러자 다른 처녀들이 돌아와 자기 들통을 집어 들었으며, 뎁이 새 우유를 받을 통들을 끓는 물로 씻으러 왔다."], ["As Tess withdrew to go afield to the cows he said to her softly—", "테스가 들판의 젖소들에게로 가려고 물러설 때 그가 그녀에게 부드럽게 말했다—"]], [["“And my question, Tessy?”", "\"내 물음은요, 테스?\""]], [["“O no—no!” replied she with grave hopelessness, as one who had heard anew the turmoil of her own past in the allusion to Alec d’Urberville.", "\"오 안 돼요—안 돼요!\" 그녀가 무거운 절망으로, 알렉 더버빌에 대한 언급 속에서 자기 자신의 과거의 소용돌이를 다시금 들은 사람처럼 대답했다."], ["“It _can’t_ be!”", "\"그건 안 돼요!\""]], [["She went out towards the mead, joining the other milkmaids with a bound, as if trying to make the open air drive away her sad constraint.", "그녀는 풀밭을 향해 나갔고, 마치 탁 트인 공기로 자기 슬픈 속박을 몰아내려는 듯 펄쩍 뛰며 다른 젖 짜는 처녀들과 합류했다."], ["All the girls drew onward to the spot where the cows were grazing in the farther mead, the bevy advancing with the bold grace of wild animals—the reckless, unchastened motion of women accustomed to unlimited space—in which they abandoned themselves to the air as a swimmer to the wave.", "처녀들 모두가 저 멀리 풀밭에서 젖소들이 풀을 뜯고 있는 곳을 향해 나아갔다. 그 무리는 야생 동물의 대담한 우아함으로—무한한 공간에 익숙한 여인들의, 무모하고 길들지 않은 몸놀림으로—나아갔고, 그 안에서 그들은 헤엄치는 자가 물결에 자신을 내맡기듯 공기에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["It seemed natural enough to him now that Tess was again in sight to choose a mate from unconstrained Nature, and not from the abodes of Art.", "테스가 다시 눈앞에 들어온 지금, 그가 예술의 거처가 아니라 거리낌 없는 자연에서 짝을 고르는 것이 이제 그에게는 충분히 자연스러워 보였다."]], [["# XXVIII", "# XXVIII"]], [["Her refusal, though unexpected, did not permanently daunt Clare.", "그녀의 거절은, 예상치 못한 것이긴 했으나, 클레어를 영영 낙담시키지는 못했다."], ["His experience of women was great enough for him to be aware that the negative often meant nothing more than the preface to the affirmative; and it was little enough for him not to know that in the manner of the present negative there lay a great exception to the dallyings of coyness.", "그의 여성에 대한 경험은, 부정이 흔히 긍정의 서두에 지나지 않음을 알 만큼은 충분했다. 그리고 지금의 이 부정의 태도 안에 수줍음의 희롱과는 크게 다른 예외가 깃들어 있음을 알지 못할 만큼은 미흡했다."], ["That she had already permitted him to make love to her he read as an additional assurance, not fully trowing that in the fields and pastures to “sigh gratis”", "그녀가 이미 자기에게 사랑을 속삭이도록 허락했다는 것을 그는 추가적인 확신으로 읽었으니, 들판과 목초지에서 \"거저 한숨짓는 것\"이 결코 낭비로 여겨지지 않는다는 사실을 그는 온전히 믿지 못했던 것이다."], ["is by no means deemed waste; love-making being here more often accepted inconsiderately and for its own sweet sake than in the carking, anxious homes of the ambitious, where a girl’s craving for an establishment paralyzes her healthy thought of a passion as an end.", "이곳에서는, 한 처녀의 안정된 가정에 대한 갈망이 정열을 그 자체로 목적이라 보는 건강한 생각을 마비시켜 버리는 야심가들의 근심 어린 불안한 가정에서보다, 사랑을 속삭이는 일이 깊이 따지지 않고 그저 그 자체의 달콤함을 위해 받아들여지는 경우가 더 많았다."]], [["“Tess, why did you say ‘no’ in such a positive way?”", "\"테스, 왜 그렇게 단호하게 '안 된다'고 말한 거예요?\""], ["he asked her in the course of a few days.", "며칠 지나 그가 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["She started.", "그녀는 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["“Don’t ask me.", "\"묻지 마세요."], ["I told you why—partly.", "왜인지 말씀드렸잖아요—얼마쯤은요."], ["I am not good enough—not worthy enough.”", "저는 충분히 좋은 사람이 못 돼요—충분히 자격이 있는 사람이 못 돼요.\""]], [["“How?", "\"어째서요?"], ["Not fine lady enough?”", "충분히 훌륭한 숙녀가 못 된다는 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes—something like that,”", "\"네—그런 거 비슷한 거예요.\""], ["murmured she.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Your friends would scorn me.”", "\"당신 친구들이 저를 경멸할 거예요.\""]], [["“Indeed, you mistake them—my father and mother.", "\"정말이지, 당신은 그분들을 오해하고 있어요—내 아버지와 어머니를요."], ["As for my brothers, I don’t care—”", "형들로 말하자면, 나는 신경 안 써요—\""], ["He clasped his fingers behind her back to keep her from slipping away.", "그가 그녀가 빠져나가지 못하도록 그녀의 등 뒤로 손가락을 깍지 꼈다."], ["“Now—you did not mean it, sweet?—I am sure you did not!", "\"자—진심이 아니었죠, 그렇죠, 사랑스러운 사람?—분명 진심이 아니었어요!"], ["You have made me so restless that I cannot read, or play, or do anything.", "당신 때문에 내가 너무 안절부절못해서 책도 못 읽고, 연주도 못 하고, 아무것도 못 하겠어요."], ["I am in no hurry, Tess, but I want to know—to hear from your own warm lips—that you will some day be mine—any time you may choose; but some day?”", "나는 서두르지 않아요, 테스. 하지만 알고 싶어요—당신의 따스한 입술로 직접 듣고 싶어요—언젠가 당신이 내 사람이 되리라는 걸요—당신이 고르는 어느 때라도 좋아요. 다만 언젠가는요?\""]], [["She could only shake her head and look away from him.", "그녀는 그저 고개를 가로젓고 그에게서 눈길을 돌릴 뿐이었다."]], [["Clare regarded her attentively, conned the characters of her face as if they had been hieroglyphics.", "클레어는 그녀를 유심히 바라보며, 마치 상형문자라도 되는 양 그녀 얼굴의 글자들을 새겨 읽었다."], ["The denial seemed real.", "그 거절은 진심으로 보였다."]], [["“Then I ought not to hold you in this way—ought I?", "\"그럼 나는 당신을 이렇게 안고 있어서는 안 되겠군요—그렇죠?"], ["I have no right to you—no right to seek out where you are, or walk with you!", "나는 당신에 대한 권리가 없어요—당신이 어디 있는지 찾아낼 권리도, 당신과 함께 걸을 권리도 없어요!"], ["Honestly, Tess, do you love any other man?”", "솔직히, 테스, 다른 남자를 사랑하나요?\""]], [["“How can you ask?” she said, with continued self-suppression.", "\"어떻게 그런 걸 물으세요?\" 그녀가 계속 자신을 억누르며 말했다."]], [["“I almost know that you do not.", "\"당신이 그렇지 않다는 걸 나는 거의 알아요."], ["But then, why do you repulse me?”", "하지만 그럼, 왜 나를 밀어내는 거예요?\""]], [["“I don’t repulse you.", "\"당신을 밀어내는 게 아니에요."], ["I like you to—tell me you love me; and you may always tell me so as you go about with me—and never offend me.”", "당신이—나를 사랑한다고 말해 주는 게 좋아요. 그리고 나와 함께 다니면서 언제든 그렇게 말해 줘도 돼요—절 화나게 하지 않으면서요.\""]], [["“But you will not accept me as a husband?”", "\"하지만 나를 남편으로는 받아 주지 않겠다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Ah—that’s different—it is for your good, indeed, my dearest!", "\"아—그건 달라요—그건 정말, 당신을 위한 거예요, 내 사랑하는 사람!"], ["O, believe me, it is only for your sake!", "오, 믿어 주세요, 그건 오로지 당신을 위해서예요!"], ["I don’t like to give myself the great happiness o’ promising to be yours in that way—because—because I am _sure_ I ought not to do it.”", "저는 그런 식으로 당신의 사람이 되겠다고 약속하는 그 큰 행복을 제게 주고 싶지 않아요—왜냐하면—왜냐하면 저는 그래서는 안 된다고 확신하니까요.\""]], [["“But you will make me happy!”", "\"하지만 당신은 나를 행복하게 해 줄 거예요!\""]], [["“Ah—you think so, but you don’t know!”", "\"아—그렇게 생각하시지만, 당신은 몰라요!\""]], [["At such times as this, apprehending the grounds of her refusal to be her modest sense of incompetence in matters social and polite, he would say that she was wonderfully well-informed and versatile—which was certainly true, her natural quickness and her admiration for him having led her to pick up his vocabulary, his accent, and fragments of his knowledge, to a surprising extent.", "이런 때에, 그녀의 거절의 근거가 사회적이고 예의에 관한 일들에서 자기가 부족하다는 겸손한 자각이라고 짐작하고서, 그는 그녀가 놀랍도록 박식하고 재주가 많다고 말하곤 했다—그것은 확실히 사실이었으니, 타고난 영민함과 그를 향한 흠모 덕분에 그녀가 놀랄 만큼 그의 어휘와 억양, 그리고 그의 지식의 조각들을 주워 익혔던 것이다."], ["After these tender contests and her victory she would go away by herself under the remotest cow, if at milking-time, or into the sedge or into her room, if at a leisure interval, and mourn silently, not a minute after an apparently phlegmatic negative.", "이런 다정한 다툼과 그녀의 승리가 있고 나면, 그녀는 젖 짜는 시간이면 가장 멀리 떨어진 젖소 아래로, 한가한 틈이면 골풀 사이로나 자기 방으로 홀로 가서 말없이 슬퍼하곤 했다. 겉보기엔 무덤덤하게 부정의 말을 내뱉은 지 채 일 분도 지나지 않아서 말이다."]], [["The struggle was so fearful; her own heart was so strongly on the side of his—two ardent hearts against one poor little conscience—that she tried to fortify her resolution by every means in her power.", "그 갈등은 너무도 무시무시했다. 그녀 자신의 마음이 너무도 강하게 그의 편에 서 있어서—가엾고 작은 양심 하나에 맞선 두 개의 열렬한 마음이었다—그녀는 가진 모든 수단을 다해 자기 결심을 굳히려 애썼다."], ["She had come to Talbothays with a made-up mind.", "그녀는 마음을 정한 채로 톨버세이즈에 왔다."], ["On no account could she agree to a step which might afterwards cause bitter rueing to her husband for his blindness in wedding her.", "어떤 일이 있어도 그녀는, 훗날 자기 남편이 눈먼 채로 자기와 결혼한 것을 두고 쓰라리게 후회하게 만들지도 모를 그런 걸음에는 동의할 수 없었다."], ["And she held that what her conscience had decided for her when her mind was unbiassed ought not to be overruled now.", "그리고 자기 마음이 치우치지 않았을 때 양심이 자기를 위해 내린 결정을 이제 와서 뒤엎어서는 안 된다고 그녀는 여겼다."]], [["“Why don’t somebody tell him all about me?”", "\"왜 아무도 그에게 나에 대해 다 말해 주지 않을까?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It was only forty miles off—why hasn’t it reached here?", "\"겨우 사십 마일 떨어진 곳이었는데—왜 그 소문이 여기까지 닿지 않았을까?"], ["Somebody must know!”", "누군가는 분명 알 텐데!\""]], [["Yet nobody seemed to know; nobody told him.", "하지만 아무도 모르는 듯했다. 아무도 그에게 말해 주지 않았다."]], [["For two or three days no more was said.", "이삼 일 동안 더는 아무 말도 없었다."], ["She guessed from the sad countenances of her chamber companions that they regarded her not only as the favourite, but as the chosen; but they could see for themselves that she did not put herself in his way.", "그녀는 한방을 쓰는 동무들의 슬픈 얼굴빛으로 미루어, 그들이 자기를 단지 총애받는 사람으로만이 아니라 선택받은 사람으로 여기고 있음을 짐작했다. 하지만 그들은 그녀가 그의 앞길에 스스로를 들이밀지 않는다는 것을 제 눈으로 볼 수 있었다."]], [["Tess had never before known a time in which the thread of her life was so distinctly twisted of two strands, positive pleasure and positive pain.", "테스는 자기 인생의 실이 이토록 또렷이 두 가닥으로, 곧 분명한 기쁨과 분명한 고통으로 꼬여 있는 시절을 일찍이 알지 못했다."], ["At the next cheese-making the pair were again left alone together.", "다음번 치즈 만들기 때 두 사람은 다시 단둘이 남겨졌다."], ["The dairyman himself had been lending a hand; but Mr Crick, as well as his wife, seemed latterly to have acquired a suspicion of mutual interest between these two; though they walked so circumspectly that suspicion was but of the faintest.", "낙농장 주인 자신이 손을 거들고 있었는데, 크릭 씨도 그 아내와 마찬가지로 근래 이 두 사람 사이의 상호 관심을 눈치챈 듯했다. 비록 그들이 너무도 신중하게 처신하여 그 의심은 더없이 희미한 것에 지나지 않았지만 말이다."], ["Anyhow, the dairyman left them to themselves.", "어쨌든 낙농장 주인은 그들을 둘이 있게 내버려 두었다."]], [["They were breaking up the masses of curd before putting them into the vats.", "그들은 응유 덩어리를 통에 넣기 전에 잘게 부수고 있었다."], ["The operation resembled the act of crumbling bread on a large scale; and amid the immaculate whiteness of the curds Tess Durbeyfield’s hands showed themselves of the pinkness of the rose.", "그 작업은 빵을 큰 규모로 부수는 행위와 비슷했다. 그리고 그 티 없이 새하얀 응유 가운데서 테스 더비필드의 손은 장미의 분홍빛을 띠고 있었다."], ["Angel, who was filling the vats with his handful, suddenly ceased, and laid his hands flat upon hers.", "한 줌씩 통을 채우고 있던 앤젤이 갑자기 멈추더니 그녀의 손 위에 자기 두 손을 납작하게 포갰다."], ["Her sleeves were rolled far above the elbow, and bending lower he kissed the inside vein of her soft arm. Although the early September weather was sultry, her arm, from her dabbling in the curds, was as cold and damp to his mouth as a new-gathered mushroom, and tasted of the whey.", "그녀의 소매는 팔꿈치 한참 위까지 걷어 올려져 있었고, 그가 더 낮게 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 부드러운 팔 안쪽 핏줄에 입을 맞추었다. 9월 초의 날씨가 무더웠음에도, 응유를 만지작거린 탓에 그녀의 팔은 갓 딴 버섯처럼 그의 입에 차갑고 축축했고, 유장의 맛이 났다."], ["But she was such a sheaf of susceptibilities that her pulse was accelerated by the touch, her blood driven to her finger-ends, and the cool arms flushed hot.", "하지만 그녀는 너무도 예민한 감수성의 다발이라, 그 닿음에 맥박이 빨라지고 피가 손끝으로 몰려들었으며, 차갑던 팔이 화끈 달아올랐다."], ["Then, as though her heart had said, “Is coyness longer necessary?", "그러더니, 마치 그녀의 마음이 \"수줍음이 더 필요한가?"], ["Truth is truth between man and woman, as between man and man,”", "사람과 사람 사이에서 그렇듯, 남자와 여자 사이에서도 진실은 진실이거늘.\""], ["she lifted her eyes and they beamed devotedly into his, as her lip rose in a tender half-smile.", "하고 말하기라도 한 듯이, 그녀가 눈을 들어 헌신적으로 그의 눈을 향해 빛냈고, 그 입술은 다정한 어렴풋한 미소로 올라갔다."]], [["“Do you know why I did that, Tess?” he said.", "\"내가 왜 그랬는지 알아요, 테스?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Because you love me very much!”", "\"저를 무척 사랑하시니까요!\""]], [["“Yes, and as a preliminary to a new entreaty.”", "\"그래요, 그리고 새로운 간청의 시작으로요.\""]], [["“Not _again_!”", "\"또 그러시면 안 돼요!\""]], [["She looked a sudden fear that her resistance might break down under her own desire.", "그녀는 자기 자신의 욕망 아래서 자기 저항이 무너져 버릴지도 모른다는 갑작스러운 두려움을 내비쳤다."]], [["“O, Tessy!”", "\"오, 테스!\""], ["he went on, “I _cannot_ think why you are so tantalizing.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"나는 당신이 왜 이렇게 애를 태우는지 도무지 알 수가 없어요."], ["Why do you disappoint me so?", "왜 나를 이렇게 실망시켜요?"], ["You seem almost like a coquette, upon my life you do—a coquette of the first urban water!", "정말이지 당신은 거의 요염한 여자 같아요—맹세코 그래요—도시물을 가장 잘 먹은 요염한 여자요!"], ["They blow hot and blow cold, just as you do, and it is the very last sort of thing to expect to find in a retreat like Talbothays....", "그런 여자들은 당신처럼 꼭 이렇게 달아올랐다 식었다 하는데, 톨버세이즈 같은 은신처에서 마주치리라곤 가장 예상치 못할 부류죠…… 그래도, 사랑하는 사람,\""], ["And yet, dearest,” he quickly added, observing how the remark had cut her, “I know you to be the most honest, spotless creature that ever lived.", "그 말이 그녀를 얼마나 아프게 했는지 알아채고 그가 재빨리 덧붙였다. \"당신이 일찍이 살았던 그 누구보다도 정직하고 흠 하나 없는 사람이라는 걸 나는 알아요."], ["So how can I suppose you a flirt? Tess, why don’t you like the idea of being my wife, if you love me as you seem to do?”", "그러니 어떻게 당신을 바람둥이라 여길 수 있겠어요? 테스, 보아하니 당신이 나를 사랑하는데, 왜 내 아내가 된다는 생각을 싫어하는 거예요?\""]], [["“I have never said I don’t like the idea, and I never could say it; because—it isn’t true!”", "\"저는 그 생각이 싫다고 말한 적 없어요. 그렇게 말할 수도 없고요. 왜냐하면—그건 사실이 아니니까요!\""]], [["The stress now getting beyond endurance, her lip quivered, and she was obliged to go away.", "이제 그 부담이 견딜 수 있는 한계를 넘어서자, 그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 그녀는 자리를 떠야 했다."], ["Clare was so pained and perplexed that he ran after and caught her in the passage.", "클레어는 너무도 아프고 당혹스러워, 뒤쫓아가 통로에서 그녀를 붙잡았다."]], [["“Tell me, tell me!”", "\"말해 줘요, 말해 줘요!\""], ["he said, passionately clasping her, in forgetfulness of his curdy hands: “do tell me that you won’t belong to anybody but me!”", "그가 자기 손에 응유가 묻은 것도 잊은 채 격정적으로 그녀를 끌어안으며 말했다. \"나 말고는 어느 누구의 사람도 되지 않겠다고 말해 줘요!\""]], [["“I will, I will tell you!”", "\"그럴게요, 그렇게 말할게요!\""], ["she exclaimed.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“And I will give you a complete answer, if you will let me go now.", "\"그리고 지금 저를 보내 주시면 완전한 대답을 드릴게요."], ["I will tell you my experiences—all about myself—all!”", "제 지난 일들을 말씀드릴게요—저에 관한 모든 것을—전부요!\""]], [["“Your experiences, dear; yes, certainly; any number.”", "\"당신의 지난 일들이라, 사랑하는 사람. 그래요, 물론이죠."], ["He expressed assent in loving satire, looking into her face.", "얼마든지요.\" 그가 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 사랑스러운 풍자로 동의를 표했다."], ["“My Tess, no doubt, almost as many experiences as that wild convolvulus out there on the garden hedge, that opened itself this morning for the first time.", "\"내 테스는, 의심할 바 없이, 저기 정원 울타리에 핀 저 야생 나팔꽃, 오늘 아침 처음으로 제 몸을 활짝 연 저 꽃만큼이나 많은 지난 일이 있겠죠."], ["Tell me anything, but don’t use that wretched expression any more about not being worthy of me.”", "무엇이든 말해 줘요. 다만 나에게 어울리지 않는다는 그 가련한 표현만은 더 이상 쓰지 말아요.\""]], [["“I will try—not!", "\"그러도록—노력할게요!"], ["And I’ll give you my reasons to-morrow—next week.”", "그리고 제 이유들을 내일—다음 주에 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["“Say on Sunday?”", "\"일요일에요?\""]], [["“Yes, on Sunday.”", "\"네, 일요일에요.\""]], [["At last she got away, and did not stop in her retreat till she was in the thicket of pollard willows at the lower side of the barton, where she could be quite unseen.", "마침내 그녀는 빠져나왔고, 농가 마당 아래쪽 끝의, 가지 잘린 버드나무 덤불에 이르러서야 물러서던 걸음을 멈추었다. 거기서라면 아무에게도 보이지 않을 수 있었다."], ["Here Tess flung herself down upon the rustling undergrowth of spear-grass, as upon a bed, and remained crouching in palpitating misery broken by momentary shoots of joy, which her fears about the ending could not altogether suppress.", "여기서 테스는 마치 침대 위에라도 누우듯 바스락거리는 창끝풀 덤불 위로 몸을 던졌고, 두근거리는 비참함 속에 웅크린 채 머물러 있었다. 그 비참함은, 결말에 대한 두려움으로도 다 억누를 수 없는 순간적인 기쁨의 분출들로 이따금 깨어졌다."]], [["In reality, she was drifting into acquiescence.", "사실인즉, 그녀는 묵인 쪽으로 떠밀려 가고 있었다."], ["Every see-saw of her breath, every wave of her blood, every pulse singing in her ears, was a voice that joined with nature in revolt against her scrupulousness.", "그녀의 숨결의 매번의 오르내림, 피의 매번의 물결, 귓속에서 노래하는 매번의 맥박이, 그녀의 양심의 가책에 맞서 자연과 한목소리로 반기를 드는 목소리였다."], ["Reckless, inconsiderate acceptance of him; to close with him at the altar, revealing nothing, and chancing discovery; to snatch ripe pleasure before the iron teeth of pain could have time to shut upon her: that was what love counselled; and in almost a terror of ecstasy Tess divined that, despite her many months of lonely self-chastisement, wrestlings, communings, schemes to lead a future of austere isolation, love’s counsel would prevail.", "그를 무모하고 분별없이 받아들이는 것, 아무것도 밝히지 않은 채 제단에서 그와 맺어지고 발각을 운에 맡기는 것, 고통의 무쇠 이빨이 그녀를 물어 닫을 틈을 갖기 전에 무르익은 기쁨을 낚아채는 것, 그것이 사랑이 권하는 바였다. 그리고 거의 황홀의 공포 속에서 테스는, 여러 달에 걸친 외로운 자기 징벌과 씨름과 묵상과 엄격한 고립의 미래를 살아가려는 계획들에도 불구하고, 사랑의 권유가 이기리라는 것을 직감했다."]], [["The afternoon advanced, and still she remained among the willows.", "오후가 깊어 갔고, 그녀는 여전히 버드나무들 사이에 머물러 있었다."], ["She heard the rattle of taking down the pails from the forked stands; the “waow-waow!” which accompanied the getting together of the cows.", "그녀는 갈래진 받침대에서 들통을 내리는 달그락 소리를, 젖소들을 한데 모으는 일에 따라붙는 \"워워!\" 하는 소리를 들었다."], ["But she did not go to the milking.", "하지만 그녀는 젖 짜러 가지 않았다."], ["They would see her agitation; and the dairyman, thinking the cause to be love alone, would good-naturedly tease her; and that harassment could not be borne.", "그들이 그녀의 동요를 볼 테고, 그 까닭을 오직 사랑뿐이라고 여기는 낙농장 주인이 사람 좋게 그녀를 놀려 댈 텐데, 그 시달림은 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["Her lover must have guessed her overwrought state, and invented some excuse for her non-appearance, for no inquiries were made or calls given.", "그녀의 연인은 그녀의 지나치게 격앙된 상태를 짐작했음이 분명했고, 그녀가 나타나지 않은 데 대해 무언가 구실을 만들어 냈음이 분명했다."], ["At half-past six the sun settled down upon the levels with the aspect of a great forge in the heavens; and presently a monstrous pumpkin-like moon arose on the other hand.", "아무도 묻지 않았고 부르지도 않았으니까. 여섯 시 반에 해가 하늘의 거대한 대장간 같은 모습으로 평평한 들판 위로 내려앉았다. 그리고 이내 다른 쪽에서 호박처럼 거대한 달이 떠올랐다."], ["The pollard willows, tortured out of their natural shape by incessant choppings, became spiny-haired monsters as they stood up against it. She went in and upstairs without a light.", "끊임없는 가지치기로 본래 모습이 비틀린 그 가지 잘린 버드나무들이, 그 달을 배경으로 곤두선 채 가시털 돋친 괴물들이 되었다. 그녀는 불도 켜지 않고 안으로 들어가 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["It was now Wednesday.", "이제 수요일이었다."], ["Thursday came, and Angel looked thoughtfully at her from a distance, but intruded in no way upon her.", "목요일이 왔고, 앤젤은 멀리서 생각에 잠긴 듯 그녀를 바라보았으나 어떤 식으로도 그녀를 방해하지 않았다."], ["The indoor milkmaids, Marian and the rest, seemed to guess that something definite was afoot, for they did not force any remarks upon her in the bedchamber.", "마리안을 비롯한 집안의 젖 짜는 처녀들도 무언가 분명한 일이 진행되고 있음을 짐작한 듯, 침실에서 그녀에게 어떤 말도 강요하지 않았다."], ["Friday passed; Saturday.", "금요일이 지나갔고, 토요일도."], ["To-morrow was the day.", "내일이 그날이었다."]], [["“I shall give way—I shall say yes—I shall let myself marry him—I cannot help it!”", "\"나는 굴복하고 말 거야—나는 그러겠다고 말할 거야—나는 그와 결혼하게 내버려 둘 거야—나도 어쩔 수가 없어!\""], ["she jealously panted, with her hot face to the pillow that night, on hearing one of the other girls sigh his name in her sleep.", "그날 밤 그녀는 다른 처녀 중 하나가 잠결에 그의 이름을 한숨처럼 내뱉는 것을 듣고는, 뜨거운 얼굴을 베개에 묻은 채 시샘하듯 헐떡였다."], ["“I can’t bear to let anybody have him but me!", "\"나 말고 다른 누가 그를 갖는 건 견딜 수가 없어!"], ["Yet it is a wrong to him, and may kill him when he knows!", "하지만 그건 그에게 잘못된 일이고, 그가 알게 되면 그를 죽일지도 몰라!"], ["O my heart—O—O—O!”", "오 내 가슴—오—오—오!\""]], [["# XXIX", "# XXIX"]], [["“Now, who mid ye think I’ve heard news o’ this morning?”", "\"자, 내가 오늘 아침에 누구 소식을 들었을 것 같나?\""], ["said Dairyman Crick, as he sat down to breakfast next day, with a riddling gaze round upon the munching men and maids.", "이튿날 크릭 낙농장 주인이 아침상에 앉으며, 우물거리며 먹는 사내들과 처녀들을 수수께끼 내듯 빙 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“Now, just who mid ye think?”", "\"자, 대체 누구일 것 같아?\""]], [["One guessed, and another guessed.", "한 사람이 알아맞혀 보았고, 또 한 사람이 알아맞혀 보았다."], ["Mrs Crick did not guess, because she knew already.", "크릭 부인은 알아맞혀 보지 않았으니, 이미 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"흠,\""], ["said the dairyman, “’tis that slack-twisted ’hore’s-bird of a feller, Jack Dollop.", "낙농장 주인이 말했다. \"바로 그 칠칠치 못한 잡놈 잭 돌럽이야."], ["He’s lately got married to a widow-woman.”", "얼마 전에 어떤 과부 여자랑 결혼했다는군.\""]], [["“Not Jack Dollop?", "\"설마 잭 돌럽이?"], ["A villain—to think o’ that!” said a milker.", "그 악당이—그럴 수가!\" 한 젖 짜는 사내가 말했다."]], [["The name entered quickly into Tess Durbeyfield’s consciousness, for it was the name of the lover who had wronged his sweetheart, and had afterwards been so roughly used by the young woman’s mother in the butter-churn.", "그 이름은 테스 더비필드의 의식 속으로 재빨리 파고들었으니, 그것은 제 연인을 욕보였다가 나중에 그 처녀의 어머니에게 버터 휘젓는 통 속에서 호되게 당한 그 사내의 이름이었기 때문이다."]], [["“And had he married the valiant matron’s daughter, as he promised?”", "\"그래서 약속대로 그 용감한 아주머니의 딸과 결혼한 건가요?\""], ["asked Angel Clare absently, as he turned over the newspaper he was reading at the little table to which he was always banished by Mrs Crick, in her sense of his gentility.", "앤젤 클레어가 멍하니 물었다. 그는 크릭 부인이 그의 신사다움을 의식해 늘 그를 따로 앉히는 작은 탁자에서 읽고 있던 신문을 뒤적이고 있었다."]], [["“Not he, sir. Never meant to,”", "\"아닙니다요, 나리. 그럴 마음이 애당초 없었죠.\""], ["replied the dairyman.", "낙농장 주인이 대답했다."], ["“As I say, ’tis a widow-woman, and she had money, it seems—fifty poun’ a year or so; and that was all he was after.", "\"말씀드렸듯이 과부 여자인데, 돈깨나 있었던 모양이에요—연 오십 파운드쯤요. 그놈이 노린 건 그게 다였죠."], ["They were married in a great hurry; and then she told him that by marrying she had lost her fifty poun’ a year.", "둘은 부랴부랴 결혼했고, 그런 다음 여자가 그놈한테 말하길, 결혼하는 바람에 그 연 오십 파운드를 잃게 됐다지 뭡니까."], ["Just fancy the state o’ my gentleman’s mind at that news!", "그 소식에 우리 신사 양반 심정이 어땠을지 한번 상상해 보세요!"], ["Never such a cat-and-dog life as they’ve been leading ever since!", "그 후로 그렇게 개와 고양이처럼 으르렁대며 사는 꼴이 다시없을 정도라니까요!"], ["Serves him well beright.", "그놈한테는 아주 싸지요."], ["But onluckily the poor woman gets the worst o’t.”", "하지만 불행히도 가엾은 여자가 제일 손해를 보는 거죠.\""]], [["“Well, the silly body should have told en sooner that the ghost of her first man would trouble him,” said Mrs Crick.", "\"흠, 그 어리석은 여편네가 첫 남편 귀신이 그놈을 괴롭히리란 걸 진작 말했어야지.\" 크릭 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay,” responded the dairyman indecisively.", "\"그렇지, 그렇지.\" 낙농장 주인이 머뭇거리며 대꾸했다."], ["“Still, you can see exactly how ’twas.", "\"그래도 어찌 된 일인지는 빤히 보이잖나."], ["She wanted a home, and didn’t like to run the risk of losing him.", "여자는 가정을 원했고, 그놈을 잃을 위험을 무릅쓰기 싫었던 거지."], ["Don’t ye think that was something like it, maidens?”", "처녀들, 그게 그런 거였을 것 같지 않나?\""]], [["He glanced towards the row of girls.", "그는 처녀들이 늘어선 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“She ought to ha’ told him just before they went to church, when he could hardly have backed out,”", "\"교회에 들어가기 직전에, 그놈이 도저히 발을 뺄 수 없을 때 말했어야 해요.\""], ["exclaimed Marian.", "마리안이 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, she ought,”", "\"그래요, 그랬어야죠.\""], ["agreed Izz.", "이즈도 동의했다."]], [["“She must have seen what he was after, and should ha’ refused him,”", "\"여자는 그놈이 뭘 노리는지 봤을 텐데, 거절했어야 해요.\""], ["cried Retty spasmodically.", "레티가 발작하듯 소리쳤다."]], [["“And what do you say, my dear?”", "\"그럼 자네는 어떻게 생각하나, 아가씨?\""], ["asked the dairyman of Tess.", "낙농장 주인이 테스에게 물었다."]], [["“I think she ought—to have told him the true state of things—or else refused him—I don’t know,”", "\"제 생각엔 여자가—사실대로 말했어야 해요—아니면 거절하든가요—잘 모르겠어요.\""], ["replied Tess, the bread-and-butter choking her.", "테스가 대답했는데, 버터 바른 빵이 목에 걸렸다."]], [["“Be cust if I’d have done either o’t,”", "\"난 둘 다 안 했을 거야, 천만에.\""], ["said Beck Knibbs, a married helper from one of the cottages. “All’s fair in love and war.", "오두막에서 온 기혼의 일손, 벡 닙스가 말했다. \"사랑과 전쟁에선 뭐든 공정한 법이야."], ["I’d ha’ married en just as she did, and if he’d said two words to me about not telling him beforehand anything whatsomdever about my first chap that I hadn’t chose to tell, I’d ha’ knocked him down wi’ the rolling-pin—a scram little feller like he!", "나라면 그 여자가 한 그대로 그놈이랑 결혼했을 테고, 만약 그놈이 나한테, 내가 말하고 싶지 않았던 내 첫 남자에 관한 무엇이든 미리 말 안 했다고 두 마디라도 지껄였다면, 밀방망이로 후려쳐 쓰러뜨렸을 거야—그런 비쩍 마른 좀팽이 같은 놈쯤은!"], ["Any woman could do it.”", "어떤 여자든 할 수 있지.\""]], [["The laughter which followed this sally was supplemented only by a sorry smile, for form’s sake, from Tess.", "이 익살에 뒤따른 웃음에 테스는 형식상 그저 서글픈 미소 하나를 보탰을 뿐이다."], ["What was comedy to them was tragedy to her; and she could hardly bear their mirth.", "그들에게 희극인 것이 그녀에게는 비극이었고, 그녀는 그들의 웃음을 견디기 어려웠다."], ["She soon rose from table, and, with an impression that Clare would soon follow her, went along a little wriggling path, now stepping to one side of the irrigating channels, and now to the other, till she stood by the main stream of the Var.", "그녀는 곧 식탁에서 일어나, 클레어가 곧 자기를 뒤따라오리라는 느낌을 안고서, 꼬불꼬불한 작은 길을 따라 걸었다. 관개 수로의 이쪽으로 발을 디뎠다가 저쪽으로 디뎠다가 하면서, 마침내 바르강의 본류 곁에 섰다."], ["Men had been cutting the water-weeds higher up the river, and masses of them were floating past her—moving islands of green crow-foot, whereon she might almost have ridden; long locks of which weed had lodged against the piles driven to keep the cows from crossing.", "사람들이 강 위쪽에서 물풀을 베어 낸 터라, 그 풀 덩어리들이 그녀 곁을 떠다니고 있었다—초록빛 미나리아재비의 떠다니는 섬들이었는데, 그녀가 거의 올라타도 될 만했다. 그 풀의 긴 타래들이, 젖소들이 건너지 못하게 박아 놓은 말뚝들에 걸려 멈춰 있었다."]], [["Yes, there was the pain of it.", "그렇다, 거기에 아픔이 있었다."], ["This question of a woman telling her story—the heaviest of crosses to herself—seemed but amusement to others.", "여자가 자기 이야기를 털어놓는다는 이 문제는—그녀에게는 가장 무거운 십자가였건만—남들에게는 한낱 우스개로밖에 보이지 않는 듯했다."], ["It was as if people should laugh at martyrdom.", "마치 사람들이 순교를 비웃기라도 하는 것 같았다."]], [["“Tessy!”", "\"테시!\""], ["came from behind her, and Clare sprang across the gully, alighting beside her feet.", "등 뒤에서 소리가 들리더니, 클레어가 도랑을 훌쩍 건너 그녀의 발치에 내려섰다."], ["“My wife—soon!”", "\"내 아내가—곧!\""]], [["“No, no; I cannot.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요. 그럴 수 없어요."], ["For your sake, O Mr Clare; for your sake, I say no!”", "당신을 위해서예요, 오 클레어 씨. 당신을 위해서, 그러니 아니라고 하는 거예요!\""]], [["“Tess!”", "\"테스!\""]], [["“Still I say no!”", "\"그래도 아니에요!\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 거듭 말했다."]], [["Not expecting this, he had put his arm lightly round her waist the moment after speaking, beneath her hanging tail of hair.", "이렇게 나올 줄 예상하지 못한 그는, 말을 마친 직후 늘어뜨린 머리채 아래로 그녀의 허리를 가볍게 감싸 안았다."], ["(The younger dairymaids, including Tess, breakfasted with their hair loose on Sunday mornings before building it up extra high for attending church, a style they could not adopt when milking with their heads against the cows.) If she had said “Yes” instead of “No”", "(테스를 비롯한 젊은 우유 짜는 처녀들은 일요일 아침이면 머리를 풀어헤친 채로 아침을 먹은 뒤, 교회에 갈 채비로 한껏 높이 틀어 올렸다. 소에 머리를 기댄 채 젖을 짤 때는 도저히 할 수 없는 머리 모양이었다.) 만약 그녀가 \"아니에요\""], ["he would have kissed her; it had evidently been his intention; but her determined negative deterred his scrupulous heart.", "대신 \"네\"라고 했더라면 그는 입을 맞추었을 것이다. 분명 그럴 작정이었으니까. 그러나 그녀의 단호한 거절이 양심적인 그의 마음을 머뭇거리게 했다."], ["Their condition of domiciliary comradeship put her, as the woman, to such disadvantage by its enforced intercourse, that he felt it unfair to her to exercise any pressure of blandishment which he might have honestly employed had she been better able to avoid him.", "한집에 사는 동무라는 처지가, 어쩔 수 없이 부대껴야 하는 까닭에, 여자인 그녀를 너무도 불리하게 만드는지라, 그녀가 그를 더 잘 피할 수 있는 형편이었다면 떳떳하게 썼을 법한 어떤 달콤한 말의 압박도, 지금 그녀에게 행사하는 것은 부당하다고 그는 느꼈다."], ["He released her momentarily-imprisoned waist, and withheld the kiss.", "그는 잠시 가두었던 그녀의 허리를 풀어 주었고, 입맞춤을 거두었다."]], [["It all turned on that release.", "모든 것이 그 풀어 줌에 달려 있었다."], ["What had given her strength to refuse him this time was solely the tale of the widow told by the dairyman; and that would have been overcome in another moment.", "이번에 그녀가 그를 거절할 힘을 준 것은 오로지 주인이 들려준 과부 이야기뿐이었다. 그것도 한순간만 더 지났더라면 무너졌을 것이다. 그러나 에인절은 더는 말하지 않았다."], ["But Angel said no more; his face was perplexed; he went away.", "그의 얼굴에는 당혹감이 어렸다. 그는 가 버렸다."]], [["Day after day they met—somewhat less constantly than before; and thus two or three weeks went by.", "날이면 날마다 두 사람은 만났다—전보다는 다소 뜸하게. 그렇게 두세 주가 지나갔다."], ["The end of September drew near, and she could see in his eye that he might ask her again.", "구월 말이 다가왔고, 그녀는 그의 눈빛에서 그가 다시 청해 올 수도 있겠다는 것을 읽을 수 있었다."]], [["His plan of procedure was different now—as though he had made up his mind that her negatives were, after all, only coyness and youth startled by the novelty of the proposal.", "이제 그의 방식은 달라졌다—마치 그녀의 거절이란 결국 수줍음일 뿐이며, 그런 청혼의 새로움에 놀란 젊음의 표현에 지나지 않는다고 마음을 굳힌 사람 같았다."], ["The fitful evasiveness of her manner when the subject was under discussion countenanced the idea.", "그 화제가 오를 때마다 변덕스럽게 발뺌하는 그녀의 태도가 그런 생각을 뒷받침했다."], ["So he played a more coaxing game; and while never going beyond words, or attempting the renewal of caresses, he did his utmost orally.", "그래서 그는 더 어르고 달래는 쪽으로 나갔다. 말 이상으로 넘어가거나 다시 애무를 시도하는 일은 결코 없이, 말로써 할 수 있는 최선을 다했다."]], [["In this way Clare persistently wooed her in undertones like that of the purling milk—at the cow’s side, at skimmings, at butter-makings, at cheese-makings, among broody poultry, and among farrowing pigs—as no milkmaid was ever wooed before by such a man.", "이렇게 클레어는, 졸졸 흐르는 우유 소리 같은 낮은 목소리로 끈질기게 구애했다—소 곁에서, 우유 거품을 걷어 낼 때, 버터를 만들 때, 치즈를 만들 때, 알을 품은 닭들 사이에서, 새끼 낳는 돼지들 틈에서—일찍이 어떤 우유 짜는 처녀도 이런 남자에게 이토록 구애받은 적이 없었다."]], [["Tess knew that she must break down. Neither a religious sense of a certain moral validity in the previous union nor a conscientious wish for candour could hold out against it much longer.", "테스는 자신이 끝내 무너지고 말리라는 것을 알았다. 앞선 그 결합에 어떤 도덕적 유효성이 있다는 종교적 감각도, 솔직하고 싶다는 양심적 바람도, 이 앞에서는 더 오래 버틸 수 없었다."], ["She loved him so passionately, and he was so godlike in her eyes; and being, though untrained, instinctively refined, her nature cried for his tutelary guidance.", "그녀는 그를 너무도 뜨겁게 사랑했고, 그는 그녀의 눈에 너무도 신과 같았다. 그리고 비록 배운 것은 없어도 본능적으로 섬세했던 그녀의 천성은 그의 인도와 보살핌을 갈구했다."], ["And thus, though Tess kept repeating to herself, “I can never be his wife,” the words were vain.", "그리하여 테스는 \"나는 결코 그의 아내가 될 수 없어\"라고 스스로 거듭 되뇌었으나, 그 말은 헛되었다."], ["A proof of her weakness lay in the very utterance of what calm strength would not have taken the trouble to formulate.", "차분한 강인함이라면 굳이 입에 올릴 수고조차 하지 않았을 것을 입 밖에 내고 있다는 사실 자체가 그녀의 나약함을 드러내는 증거였다."], ["Every sound of his voice beginning on the old subject stirred her with a terrifying bliss, and she coveted the recantation she feared.", "그의 목소리가 그 해묵은 화제를 다시 꺼낼 때마다, 그 모든 소리는 두려운 환희로 그녀를 흔들었고, 그녀는 자신이 두려워하던 그 번복을 도리어 갈망했다."]], [["His manner was—what man’s is not?—so much that of one who would love and cherish and defend her under any conditions, changes, charges, or revelations, that her gloom lessened as she basked in it.", "그의 태도는—어떤 남자인들 안 그렇겠는가?—어떤 처지에서든, 어떤 변화나 비난이나 폭로 앞에서든 그녀를 사랑하고 아끼고 지켜 줄 사람의 그것이어서, 그녀는 그 햇살을 쬐며 어둠이 옅어졌다."], ["The season meanwhile was drawing onward to the equinox, and though it was still fine, the days were much shorter.", "그동안 계절은 춘분점을 향해 나아가고 있었고, 날씨는 여전히 맑았으나 해는 훨씬 짧아졌다."], ["The dairy had again worked by morning candlelight for a long time; and a fresh renewal of Clare’s pleading occurred one morning between three and four.", "낙농장은 다시 오랫동안 아침 촛불을 켜 놓고 일했다. 그리고 어느 날 아침 세시에서 네시 사이, 클레어의 새로운 간청이 다시 한번 터져 나왔다."]], [["She had run up in her bedgown to his door to call him as usual; then had gone back to dress and call the others; and in ten minutes was walking to the head of the stairs with the candle in her hand.", "그녀는 잠옷 차림으로 평소처럼 그의 방문 앞으로 뛰어올라 가 그를 불렀다. 그러고는 옷을 입고 다른 이들을 부르러 돌아갔다가, 십 분쯤 뒤 손에 촛불을 들고 계단 꼭대기로 걸어가던 참이었다."], ["At the same moment he came down his steps from above in his shirt-sleeves and put his arm across the stairway.", "바로 그 순간 그가 셔츠 바람으로 위층 계단을 내려와, 계단을 가로질러 팔을 뻗었다."]], [["“Now, Miss Flirt, before you go down,”", "\"자, 바람둥이 아가씨, 내려가기 전에.\""], ["he said peremptorily.", "그가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“It is a fortnight since I spoke, and this won’t do any longer.", "\"내가 말을 꺼낸 지 보름이오. 이대로는 더는 안 되겠소."], ["You _must_ tell me what you mean, or I shall have to leave this house.", "무슨 뜻인지 말해 주시오. 안 그러면 나는 이 집을 떠나야겠소."], ["My door was ajar just now, and I saw you.", "방금 내 방문이 빠끔히 열려 있어서 당신을 보았소."], ["For your own safety I must go.", "당신의 안전을 위해서라도 나는 가야 하오."], ["You don’t know. Well?", "당신은 모르오. 자?"], ["Is it to be yes at last?”", "마침내 그렇다는 거요?\""]], [["“I am only just up, Mr Clare, and it is too early to take me to task!”", "\"저는 이제 막 일어났어요, 클레어 씨. 이렇게 이른 시간에 추궁하다니 너무하세요!\""], ["she pouted. “You need not call me Flirt.", "그녀가 입을 삐죽였다. \"저를 바람둥이라고 부르실 건 없어요."], ["’Tis cruel and untrue.", "잔인하고 사실도 아니에요."], ["Wait till by and by.", "나중까지 기다려 주세요."], ["Please wait till by and by!", "제발 나중까지 기다려 주세요!"], ["I will really think seriously about it between now and then.", "지금부터 그때까지 정말로 진지하게 생각해 볼게요."], ["Let me go downstairs!”", "그러니 내려가게 해 주세요!\""]], [["She looked a little like what he said she was as, holding the candle sideways, she tried to smile away the seriousness of her words.", "촛불을 비스듬히 든 채, 자기 말의 진지함을 웃음으로 무마하려 애쓰는 그 모습이, 그가 말한 그런 여자처럼 조금은 보이기도 했다."]], [["“Call me Angel, then, and not Mr Clare.”", "\"그럼 클레어 씨 말고 에인절이라고 불러 주오.\""]], [["“Angel.”", "\"에인절.\""]], [["“Angel dearest—why not?”", "\"에인절, 가장 사랑하는 사람—안 될 게 뭐요?\""]], [["“’Twould mean that I agree, wouldn’t it?”", "\"그렇게 부르면 제가 동의한다는 뜻이 되잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“It would only mean that you love me, even if you cannot marry me; and you were so good as to own that long ago.”", "\"그건 그저 당신이 나를 사랑한다는 뜻일 뿐이오, 설령 나와 결혼할 수 없더라도 말이오. 그리고 그건 당신이 오래전에 친절하게도 인정해 준 일이잖소.\""]], [["“Very well, then, ‘Angel dearest’, if I _must_,”", "\"그럼 좋아요. '에인절, 가장 사랑하는 사람'이라고요, 정 그래야 한다면.\""], ["she murmured, looking at her candle, a roguish curl coming upon her mouth, notwithstanding her suspense.", "그녀가 촛불을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. 마음을 졸이면서도 입가에는 장난기 어린 곡선이 떠올랐다."]], [["Clare had resolved never to kiss her until he had obtained her promise; but somehow, as Tess stood there in her prettily tucked-up milking gown, her hair carelessly heaped upon her head till there should be leisure to arrange it when skimming and milking were done, he broke his resolve, and brought his lips to her cheek for one moment.", "클레어는 그녀의 약속을 받아 내기 전에는 결코 입 맞추지 않으리라 다짐했다. 그러나 어쩐 일인지, 테스가 예쁘게 걷어 올린 우유 짜는 작업복 차림으로, 거품 걷고 젖 짜는 일이 끝나 손질할 짬이 날 때까지 머리를 아무렇게나 머리 위에 틀어 올린 채 거기 서 있자, 그는 그 다짐을 깨고 한순간 그녀의 뺨에 입술을 가져갔다."], ["She passed downstairs very quickly, never looking back at him or saying another word.", "그녀는 그를 돌아보지도, 다른 말을 더하지도 않고 아주 빠르게 계단을 내려갔다."], ["The other maids were already down, and the subject was not pursued.", "다른 처녀들은 이미 내려와 있었고, 그 화제는 더 이어지지 않았다."], ["Except Marian, they all looked wistfully and suspiciously at the pair, in the sad yellow rays which the morning candles emitted in contrast with the first cold signals of the dawn without.", "매리언만 빼고는, 모두가 바깥의 첫 차가운 새벽 기미와 대비되어 아침 촛불이 뿜어내는 서글픈 누런빛 속에서, 그 두 사람을 애틋하고도 의심쩍은 눈길로 바라보았다."]], [["When skimming was done—which, as the milk diminished with the approach of autumn, was a lessening process day by day—Retty and the rest went out.", "거품 걷는 일이 끝나자—가을이 다가올수록 우유가 줄어들어 날마다 점점 줄어드는 일이었지만—레티와 나머지가 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The lovers followed them.", "연인들이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Our tremulous lives are so different from theirs, are they not?”", "\"우리의 떨리는 삶은 저들의 삶과는 사뭇 다르지, 안 그래?\""], ["he musingly observed to her, as he regarded the three figures tripping before him through the frigid pallor of opening day.", "그가 동트는 날의 싸늘한 창백함 속을 종종걸음으로 앞서가는 세 사람을 바라보며, 생각에 잠긴 듯 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“Not so very different, I think,”", "\"그렇게 아주 다르진 않다고 생각해요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Why do you think that?”", "\"왜 그렇게 생각하지?\""]], [["“There are very few women’s lives that are not—tremulous,”", "\"떨리지 않는 여자의 삶이란 거의 없으니까요.\""], ["Tess replied, pausing over the new word as if it impressed her.", "테스가 그 새 낱말이 인상에 남는 듯 잠시 머뭇거리며 대답했다."], ["“There’s more in those three than you think.”", "\"저 세 사람한테도 당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 많은 게 있어요.\""]], [["“What is in them?”", "\"그들 안에 무엇이 있는데?\""]], [["“Almost either of ’em,”", "\"저 세 사람 중 거의 누구라도,\""], ["she began, “would make—perhaps would make—a properer wife than I.", "그녀가 입을 열었다. \"저보다 더 어울리는—어쩌면 더 어울리는—아내가 될 거예요."], ["And perhaps they love you as well as I—almost.”", "그리고 어쩌면 그들도 저만큼이나 당신을 사랑할지도—거의.\""]], [["“O, Tessy!”", "\"오, 테시!\""]], [["There were signs that it was an exquisite relief to her to hear the impatient exclamation, though she had resolved so intrepidly to let generosity make one bid against herself.", "그토록 의연하게 너그러움이 자신에게 불리한 입찰을 하나 던지도록 마음먹었으면서도, 그 조바심 어린 탄성을 듣는 것이 그녀에게는 더없는 안도였음을 보여 주는 기색이 있었다."], ["That was now done, and she had not the power to attempt self-immolation a second time then.", "이제 그 일은 끝났고, 그녀에게는 그 순간 두 번째로 자기를 제물로 바치려 시도할 힘이 없었다."], ["They were joined by a milker from one of the cottages, and no more was said on that which concerned them so deeply.", "오두막 가운데 하나에서 온 우유 짜는 사람이 그들과 합류했고, 그토록 그들에게 깊이 관계된 그 일에 대해서는 더 말이 오가지 않았다."], ["But Tess knew that this day would decide it.", "그러나 테스는 오늘이 그 일을 결정짓는 날이 되리라는 것을 알았다."]], [["In the afternoon several of the dairyman’s household and assistants went down to the meads as usual, a long way from the dairy, where many of the cows were milked without being driven home.", "오후에는 주인집 식구와 일꾼 가운데 여럿이 평소처럼 목초지로 내려갔다. 낙농장에서 한참 떨어진 곳이었는데, 거기서는 소를 집으로 몰아오지 않고 많은 소의 젖을 그 자리에서 짰다."], ["The supply was getting less as the animals advanced in calf, and the supernumerary milkers of the lush green season had been dismissed.", "소들이 새끼를 배 가면서 산출량은 점점 줄었고, 무성한 푸른 철의 임시 우유 짜는 일꾼들은 이미 해고된 뒤였다."]], [["The work progressed leisurely.", "일은 느긋하게 진척되었다."], ["Each pailful was poured into tall cans that stood in a large spring-waggon which had been brought upon the scene; and when they were milked, the cows trailed away.", "한 통 가득 찰 때마다 그 자리에 끌어다 놓은 커다란 용수철 짐마차에 실린 길쭉한 깡통에 부어졌다. 젖을 다 짠 소들은 어슬렁어슬렁 멀어져 갔다."], ["Dairyman Crick, who was there with the rest, his wrapper gleaming miraculously white against a leaden evening sky, suddenly looked at his heavy watch.", "다른 이들과 함께 거기 있던 크릭 주인은, 그의 작업복이 납빛 저녁 하늘을 배경으로 기적처럼 새하얗게 빛나고 있었는데, 문득 자기의 묵직한 시계를 들여다보았다."]], [["“Why, ’tis later than I thought,”", "\"이런, 생각보다 늦었군.\""], ["he said. “Begad!", "그가 말했다."], ["We shan’t be soon enough with this milk at the station, if we don’t mind.", "\"이런 빌어먹을! 조심하지 않으면 이 우유를 제때 역에 못 대겠어."], ["There’s no time to-day to take it home and mix it with the bulk afore sending off.", "오늘은 집에 가져가서 전체와 섞은 뒤 부칠 시간이 없네."], ["It must go to station straight from here.", "여기서 곧장 역으로 가야겠어."], ["Who’ll drive it across?”", "누가 몰고 가지?\""]], [["Mr Clare volunteered to do so, though it was none of his business, asking Tess to accompany him.", "자기 일도 아니었건만 클레어 씨가 자청하고 나섰고, 테스에게 같이 가자고 청했다."], ["The evening, though sunless, had been warm and muggy for the season, and Tess had come out with her milking-hood only, naked-armed and jacketless; certainly not dressed for a drive.", "저녁은 해가 없는데도 그 계절치고 따뜻하고 후텁지근했던 터라, 테스는 우유 짜는 모자만 쓴 채 팔뚝을 드러내고 윗옷도 걸치지 않고 나와 있었다. 마차를 탈 차림은 분명 아니었다."], ["She therefore replied by glancing over her scant habiliments; but Clare gently urged her.", "그래서 그녀는 자기의 변변찮은 옷차림을 죽 훑어보는 것으로 대답을 대신했다. 그러나 클레어가 부드럽게 졸랐다."], ["She assented by relinquishing her pail and stool to the dairyman to take home, and mounted the spring-waggon beside Clare.", "그녀는 자기 우유통과 걸상을 주인에게 넘겨 집으로 가져가게 함으로써 응낙의 뜻을 표하고는, 클레어 곁에 올라 용수철 짐마차에 탔다."]], [["# XXX", "# XXX"]], [["In the diminishing daylight they went along the level roadway through the meads, which stretched away into gray miles, and were backed in the extreme edge of distance by the swarthy and abrupt slopes of Egdon Heath.", "기우는 햇빛 속에서 두 사람은 목초지를 가로지르는 평탄한 길을 따라갔다. 목초지는 잿빛으로 몇 마일이나 뻗어 나가, 저 멀리 끝자락에서는 에그던 황야의 거뭇하고 가파른 비탈을 배경으로 두고 있었다."], ["On its summit stood clumps and stretches of fir-trees, whose notched tips appeared like battlemented towers crowning black-fronted castles of enchantment.", "그 꼭대기에는 전나무 무더기와 줄기가 서 있었는데, 톱니처럼 들쭉날쭉한 우듬지가 마치 마법에 걸린 검은 정면의 성을 머리에 인 흉벽 두른 탑들처럼 보였다."]], [["They were so absorbed in the sense of being close to each other that they did not begin talking for a long while, the silence being broken only by the clucking of the milk in the tall cans behind them.", "두 사람은 서로 가까이 있다는 느낌에 너무도 빠져든 나머지 오랫동안 말을 꺼내지 못했다. 등 뒤 길쭉한 깡통 속 우유가 출렁거리는 소리만이 그 침묵을 깰 뿐이었다."], ["The lane they followed was so solitary that the hazel nuts had remained on the boughs till they slipped from their shells, and the blackberries hung in heavy clusters.", "그들이 따라가는 좁은 길은 어찌나 인적이 드문지, 개암 열매가 가지에 그대로 매달려 있다가 껍질에서 미끄러져 나올 지경이었고, 검은딸기는 묵직한 송이를 이루어 늘어져 있었다."], ["Every now and then Angel would fling the lash of his whip round one of these, pluck it off, and give it to his companion.", "이따금 에인절은 채찍 끈을 그 송이 가운데 하나에 휘감아 따서는 길동무에게 건네주곤 했다."]], [["The dull sky soon began to tell its meaning by sending down herald-drops of rain, and the stagnant air of the day changed into a fitful breeze which played about their faces.", "이내 흐린 하늘은 비의 전령 같은 빗방울을 떨구어 그 속뜻을 알리기 시작했고, 한낮의 정체된 공기는 두 사람의 얼굴 둘레를 노니는 변덕스러운 산들바람으로 바뀌었다."], ["The quick-silvery glaze on the rivers and pools vanished; from broad mirrors of light they changed to lustreless sheets of lead, with a surface like a rasp.", "강과 못 위에 어렸던 수은 빛 윤기가 사라졌다. 너른 빛의 거울이던 그것들이, 줄칼처럼 까슬까슬한 표면을 한 윤기 없는 납빛 판으로 변했다."], ["But that spectacle did not affect her preoccupation.", "그러나 그 광경도 그녀의 골똘함을 흔들지는 못했다."], ["Her countenance, a natural carnation slightly embrowned by the season, had deepened its tinge with the beating of the rain-drops; and her hair, which the pressure of the cows’ flanks had, as usual, caused to tumble down from its fastenings and stray beyond the curtain of her calico bonnet, was made clammy by the moisture till it hardly was better than seaweed.", "본디 살구빛이던 그녀의 얼굴은 철 따라 약간 그을려 있었는데, 빗방울이 두드리는 통에 그 빛깔이 더욱 짙어졌다. 그리고 소의 옆구리에 눌려 평소처럼 묶은 데서 흘러내려 무명 보닛의 장막 밖으로 빠져나온 머리카락은, 물기에 척척하게 젖어 들어 거의 해초나 다름없는 꼴이 되었다."]], [["“I ought not to have come, I suppose,”", "\"제가 오지 말았어야 했나 봐요.\""], ["she murmured, looking at the sky.", "그녀가 하늘을 올려다보며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I am sorry for the rain,” said he.", "\"비가 와서 안됐소.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“But how glad I am to have you here!”", "\"하지만 당신이 여기 있어 주니 얼마나 기쁜지!\""]], [["Remote Egdon disappeared by degree behind the liquid gauze.", "멀리 에그던이 차츰 흐물흐물한 비의 장막 뒤로 사라졌다."], ["The evening grew darker, and the roads being crossed by gates, it was not safe to drive faster than at a walking pace.", "저녁은 점점 어두워졌고, 길마다 문이 가로막고 있어 걷는 속도보다 빨리 모는 것은 안전하지 않았다."], ["The air was rather chill.", "공기는 제법 쌀쌀했다."]], [["“I am so afraid you will get cold, with nothing upon your arms and shoulders,” he said.", "\"팔도 어깨도 아무것도 안 걸쳐서, 당신이 감기 들까 봐 너무 걱정이오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Creep close to me, and perhaps the drizzle won’t hurt you much.", "\"내게 바싹 붙어요. 그러면 이슬비도 별로 못 괴롭힐 거요."], ["I should be sorrier still if I did not think that the rain might be helping me.”", "이 비가 나를 돕고 있을지 모른다는 생각이 들지 않았다면, 더더욱 안타까웠을 텐데.\""]], [["She imperceptibly crept closer, and he wrapped round them both a large piece of sail-cloth, which was sometimes used to keep the sun off the milk-cans.", "그녀가 알아차리지 못할 만큼 슬며시 더 가까이 붙자, 그는 큼직한 돛천 한 자락으로 두 사람을 함께 둘러 감쌌다. 우유통에 햇볕이 들지 않게 가끔 쓰던 천이었다."], ["Tess held it from slipping off him as well as herself, Clare’s hands being occupied.", "클레어는 양손이 자유롭지 못했으므로, 테스가 그 천이 자기뿐 아니라 그에게서도 흘러내리지 않게 붙잡았다."]], [["“Now we are all right again.", "\"자, 이제 다시 됐소."], ["Ah—no we are not!", "아—아니, 안 됐군!"], ["It runs down into my neck a little, and it must still more into yours.", "빗물이 내 목으로 조금 흘러드는데, 당신 쪽으로는 더 흐르겠소."], ["That’s better.", "그래, 그게 낫군."], ["Your arms are like wet marble, Tess.", "당신 팔은 젖은 대리석 같소, 테스."], ["Wipe them in the cloth.", "천으로 닦아요."], ["Now, if you stay quiet, you will not get another drop.", "자, 가만히 있으면 더는 한 방울도 안 맞을 거요."], ["Well, dear—about that question of mine—that long-standing question?”", "그런데, 여보—내 그 질문 말이오—그 오래 묵은 질문 말이오?\""]], [["The only reply that he could hear for a little while was the smack of the horse’s hoofs on the moistening road, and the cluck of the milk in the cans behind them.", "한동안 그가 들을 수 있는 대답이라곤 젖어 가는 길 위를 두드리는 말발굽 소리와, 등 뒤 깡통 속 우유가 출렁거리는 소리뿐이었다."]], [["“Do you remember what you said?”", "\"당신이 한 말 기억하오?\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"기억해요.\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Before we get home, mind.”", "\"집에 닿기 전에, 잊지 말고.\""]], [["“I’ll try.”", "\"애써 볼게요.\""]], [["He said no more then. As they drove on, the fragment of an old manor house of Caroline date rose against the sky, and was in due course passed and left behind.", "그러고 나서 그는 더 말하지 않았다. 마차가 나아가는 동안, 캐롤라인 시대에 지어진 어느 옛 장원 저택의 잔해가 하늘을 배경으로 솟아올랐고, 이윽고 지나쳐 뒤로 멀어졌다."]], [["“That,”", "\"저것은,\""], ["he observed, to entertain her, “is an interesting old place—one of the several seats which belonged to an ancient Norman family formerly of great influence in this county, the d’Urbervilles.", "그녀를 즐겁게 해 주려고 그가 일러 주었다. \"흥미로운 옛집이오—한때 이 주에서 큰 세력을 떨치던 노르만계의 어느 오래된 가문, 더버빌 가에 속했던 여러 영지 가운데 하나지."], ["I never pass one of their residences without thinking of them.", "나는 그들의 저택을 지날 때마다 그 가문을 떠올리지 않을 수 없소."], ["There is something very sad in the extinction of a family of renown, even if it was fierce, domineering, feudal renown.”", "이름난 가문이 끊긴다는 데에는 무언가 몹시 서글픈 데가 있어. 설령 그것이 사납고 거만하고 봉건적인 명성이었다 해도 말이오.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["They crept along towards a point in the expanse of shade just at hand at which a feeble light was beginning to assert its presence, a spot where, by day, a fitful white streak of steam at intervals upon the dark green background denoted intermittent moments of contact between their secluded world and modern life.", "두 사람은 바로 앞 그늘진 너른 들판의 한 지점을 향해 천천히 나아갔다. 거기서는 희미한 불빛 하나가 그 존재를 드러내기 시작하고 있었다. 낮이면 그곳에서, 짙은 초록 배경 위로 이따금 깜빡이는 하얀 증기 줄기가 일정한 간격을 두고 피어올라, 그들의 외딴 세계와 근대적 삶이 간헐적으로 맞닿는 순간을 알리던 자리였다."], ["Modern life stretched out its steam feeler to this point three or four times a day, touched the native existences, and quickly withdrew its feeler again, as if what it touched had been uncongenial.", "근대적 삶은 하루에 서너 번 이 지점까지 증기의 더듬이를 뻗어, 토박이들의 존재를 건드렸다가, 마치 건드린 것이 영 마뜩잖다는 듯 이내 그 더듬이를 다시 거두어들이곤 했다."]], [["They reached the feeble light, which came from the smoky lamp of a little railway station; a poor enough terrestrial star, yet in one sense of more importance to Talbothays Dairy and mankind than the celestial ones to which it stood in such humiliating contrast.", "그들은 그 희미한 불빛에 다다랐다. 자그마한 철도역의 그을린 등불에서 나오는 빛이었다. 보잘것없는 지상의 별이었으나, 어떤 의미에서는 그것이 그토록 초라하게 대비되는 저 천상의 별들보다 탤보세이스 낙농장과 인류에게 더 중요한 별이었다."], ["The cans of new milk were unladen in the rain, Tess getting a little shelter from a neighbouring holly tree.", "갓 짠 우유 깡통들이 빗속에서 내려졌고, 테스는 곁에 선 호랑가시나무에서 약간의 비가림을 얻었다."]], [["Then there was the hissing of a train, which drew up almost silently upon the wet rails, and the milk was rapidly swung can by can into the truck.", "이윽고 젖은 선로 위로 거의 소리도 없이 멈춰 서는 기차의 쉭쉭거리는 소리가 들렸고, 우유는 깡통 하나하나가 잽싸게 화물칸으로 휙휙 실려 들어갔다."], ["The light of the engine flashed for a second upon Tess Durbeyfield’s figure, motionless under the great holly tree.", "기관차의 불빛이 한순간 커다란 호랑가시나무 아래 꼼짝 않고 선 테스 더비필드의 모습 위로 번쩍였다."], ["No object could have looked more foreign to the gleaming cranks and wheels than this unsophisticated girl, with the round bare arms, the rainy face and hair, the suspended attitude of a friendly leopard at pause, the print gown of no date or fashion, and the cotton bonnet drooping on her brow.", "번쩍이는 크랭크와 바퀴에 이 순박한 처녀보다 더 어울리지 않아 보이는 것은 없었으리라. 둥근 맨팔, 비에 젖은 얼굴과 머리, 잠시 멈춰 선 다정한 표범 같은 정지한 자세, 시대도 유행도 없는 무명 옷, 그리고 이마 위로 늘어진 무명 보닛."]], [["She mounted again beside her lover, with a mute obedience characteristic of impassioned natures at times, and when they had wrapped themselves up over head and ears in the sailcloth again, they plunged back into the now thick night.", "그녀는 격정적인 천성이 이따금 보이는 그 말 없는 순종으로 다시 연인 곁에 올라탔고, 두 사람이 다시 돛천을 머리끝까지 뒤집어쓰자, 이제 짙어진 밤 속으로 도로 잠겨 들었다."], ["Tess was so receptive that the few minutes of contact with the whirl of material progress lingered in her thought.", "테스는 감수성이 너무도 예민했던 까닭에, 물질적 진보의 소용돌이와 맞닿았던 그 몇 분이 그녀의 생각 속에 오래 남았다."]], [["“Londoners will drink it at their breakfasts to-morrow, won’t they?”", "\"런던 사람들이 내일 아침에 이걸 마시겠죠, 그렇죠?\" 그녀가 물었다."], ["she asked. “Strange people that we have never seen.”", "\"우리가 한 번도 본 적 없는 낯선 사람들이요.\""]], [["“Yes—I suppose they will.", "\"그래—아마 그러겠지."], ["Though not as we send it. When its strength has been lowered, so that it may not get up into their heads.”", "우리가 부친 그대로는 아니고. 머리로 올라가지 않게끔 농도를 낮춘 뒤에 말이오.\""]], [["“Noble men and noble women, ambassadors and centurions, ladies and tradeswomen, and babies who have never seen a cow.”", "\"고귀한 남자와 고귀한 여자, 대사와 백인대장, 귀부인과 장사하는 여자, 그리고 소라고는 한 번도 본 적 없는 갓난아기들.\""]], [["“Well, yes; perhaps; particularly centurions.”", "\"음, 그래. 어쩌면. 특히 백인대장들이.\""]], [["“Who don’t know anything of us, and where it comes from; or think how we two drove miles across the moor to-night in the rain that it might reach ’em in time?”", "\"우리에 대해서는, 이게 어디서 오는지는 아무것도 모르겠죠. 아니면 우리 둘이 오늘 밤 빗속에서, 이게 제때 닿도록 황야를 몇 마일이나 가로질러 몰고 왔다는 걸 생각이나 할까요?\""]], [["“We did not drive entirely on account of these precious Londoners; we drove a little on our own—on account of that anxious matter which you will, I am sure, set at rest, dear Tess.", "\"우리가 이 귀하신 런던 사람들만을 위해서 몬 건 아니오. 우리 자신을 위해서도 조금 몬 거지—당신이, 분명히, 가라앉혀 줄 그 마음 졸이던 문제 때문에 말이오, 사랑하는 테스."], ["Now, permit me to put it in this way.", "자, 이렇게 말하게 해 주오."], ["You belong to me already, you know; your heart, I mean.", "당신은 이미 내 것이오, 알다시피. 당신 마음 말이오."], ["Does it not?”", "안 그렇소?\""]], [["“You know as well as I.", "\"당신도 저만큼 잘 아시잖아요."], ["O yes—yes!”", "오 네—네!\""]], [["“Then, if your heart does, why not your hand?”", "\"그럼, 당신 마음이 그렇다면, 어째서 당신 손은 아니란 말이오?\""]], [["“My only reason was on account of you—on account of a question.", "\"제 유일한 이유는 당신 때문이었어요—어떤 문제 때문에요."], ["I have something to tell you—”", "당신께 말씀드릴 게 있어요—\""]], [["“But suppose it to be entirely for my happiness, and my worldly convenience also?”", "\"하지만 그게 오롯이 내 행복을 위한 일이고, 게다가 내 세상살이의 편의를 위한 일이라고 쳐 봅시다?\""]], [["“O yes; if it is for your happiness and worldly convenience.", "\"오 네. 그게 당신의 행복과 세상살이의 편의를 위한 거라면요."], ["But my life before I came here—I want—”", "하지만 제가 여기 오기 전의 제 삶이—저는 원해요—\""]], [["“Well, it is for my convenience as well as my happiness.", "\"음, 그건 내 행복뿐 아니라 내 편의를 위한 일이기도 하오."], ["If I have a very large farm, either English or colonial, you will be invaluable as a wife to me; better than a woman out of the largest mansion in the country.", "내가 아주 큰 농장을, 영국이든 식민지든, 갖게 된다면, 당신은 아내로서 더없이 값진 사람이 될 거요. 이 나라에서 가장 큰 저택에서 나온 여자보다 나을 거요."], ["So please—please, dear Tessy, disabuse your mind of the feeling that you will stand in my way.”", "그러니 제발—제발, 사랑하는 테시, 당신이 내 앞길을 막으리라는 그 마음을랑 떨쳐 버려요.\""]], [["“But my history.", "\"하지만 제 내력이요."], ["I want you to know it—you must let me tell you—you will not like me so well!”", "당신이 그걸 알기를 바라요—말하게 해 주셔야 해요—그러면 당신은 저를 그렇게 좋아하지 않으실 거예요!\""]], [["“Tell it if you wish to, dearest.", "\"하고 싶다면 하구려, 가장 사랑하는 사람."], ["This precious history then.", "그럼 그 귀한 내력을 들어 봅시다."], ["Yes, I was born at so and so, Anno Domini—”", "그래, 나는 어디어디에서, 주님의 해 몇 년에 태어났고—\""]], [["“I was born at Marlott,”", "\"저는 말럿에서 태어났어요.\""], ["she said, catching at his words as a help, lightly as they were spoken.", "그녀가, 가벼이 던진 말이긴 했어도 그 말을 거들음 삼아 붙들며 말했다."], ["“And I grew up there.", "\"그리고 거기서 자랐고요."], ["And I was in the Sixth Standard when I left school, and they said I had great aptness, and should make a good teacher, so it was settled that I should be one.", "학교를 그만둘 때 육 학년이었는데, 사람들이 제가 무척 영민하니 좋은 선생이 될 거라고 했어요. 그래서 제가 선생이 되기로 정해졌고요."], ["But there was trouble in my family; father was not very industrious, and he drank a little.”", "그런데 우리 집에 어려움이 있었어요. 아버지가 그리 부지런하지 못하셨고, 술도 좀 드셨거든요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["Poor child!", "가엾은 것!"], ["Nothing new.” He pressed her more closely to his side.", "새로울 것 없는 일이지.\" 그가 그녀를 자기 곁으로 더 바싹 끌어당겼다."]], [["“And then—there is something very unusual about it—about me.", "\"그러고는—거기에 아주 별난 게 있어요—저에 대해서요."], ["I—I was—”", "저—저는—\""]], [["Tess’s breath quickened.", "테스의 숨결이 가빠졌다."]], [["“Yes, dearest. Never mind.”", "\"그래, 가장 사랑하는 사람. 괜찮소.\""]], [["“I—I—am not a Durbeyfield, but a d’Urberville—a descendant of the same family as those that owned the old house we passed. And—we are all gone to nothing!”", "\"저는—저는—더비필드가 아니라 더버빌이에요—우리가 지나온 그 옛집을 가졌던 그들과 같은 가문의 후손이요. 그리고—우리는 다 영락해서 아무것도 아니게 됐어요!\""]], [["“A d’Urberville!—Indeed!", "\"더버빌이라니!—정말이오!"], ["And is that all the trouble, dear Tess?”", "그런데 그게 그 어려움의 전부요, 사랑하는 테스?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered faintly.", "그녀가 가냘프게 대답했다."]], [["“Well—why should I love you less after knowing this?”", "\"그래—이걸 안다고 내가 당신을 덜 사랑할 까닭이 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["“I was told by the dairyman that you hated old families.”", "\"주인 어른이 당신은 오래된 가문을 싫어한다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["He laughed.", "그가 웃었다."]], [["“Well, it is true, in one sense.", "\"음, 한편으로는 사실이오."], ["I do hate the aristocratic principle of blood before everything, and do think that as reasoners the only pedigrees we ought to respect are those spiritual ones of the wise and virtuous, without regard to corporal paternity.", "나는 무엇보다 핏줄을 앞세우는 귀족주의 원리를 정말 싫어하고, 따져 보는 사람으로서 우리가 존중해야 할 유일한 혈통이란 육체적 부계 따위와는 상관없이 슬기롭고 덕 있는 이들의 그 정신적 혈통뿐이라고 생각하오."], ["But I am extremely interested in this news—you can have no idea how interested I am!", "하지만 이 소식에는 굉장히 흥미가 동하는걸—내가 얼마나 흥미로운지 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요!"], ["Are you not interested yourself in being one of that well-known line?”", "당신 자신은 그 유명한 가계의 일원이라는 게 흥미롭지 않소?\""]], [["“No. I have thought it sad—especially since coming here, and knowing that many of the hills and fields I see once belonged to my father’s people.", "\"아뇨. 저는 그게 서글프다고 생각했어요—특히 여기 와서, 제 눈에 보이는 저 언덕과 들판 가운데 많은 곳이 한때 우리 아버지 집안 것이었음을 알고 나서는요."], ["But other hills and fields belonged to Retty’s people, and perhaps others to Marian’s, so that I don’t value it particularly.”", "하지만 다른 언덕과 들판은 레티네 집안 것이었고, 어쩌면 또 다른 데는 매리언네 것이었겠죠. 그러니 저는 그걸 별로 대단하게 여기지 않아요.\""]], [["“Yes—it is surprising how many of the present tillers of the soil were once owners of it, and I sometimes wonder that a certain school of politicians don’t make capital of the circumstance; but they don’t seem to know it...", "\"그래—지금 땅을 일구는 이들 가운데 얼마나 많은 사람이 한때는 그 땅의 주인이었는지 놀라울 따름이오. 나는 가끔, 어떤 정파의 정치가들이 그런 사정을 밑천 삼지 않는 게 의아하다오. 하지만 그들은 그걸 모르는 모양이지..."], ["I wonder that I did not see the resemblance of your name to d’Urberville, and trace the manifest corruption. And this was the carking secret!”", "당신 이름이 더버빌과 닮았다는 걸 내가 알아채지 못하고, 그 빤한 변형을 짚어 내지 못했다니 의아하군. 그런데 이게 당신을 괴롭히던 그 비밀이었던 거요!\""]], [["She had not told.", "그녀는 말하지 않았다."], ["At the last moment her courage had failed her; she feared his blame for not telling him sooner; and her instinct of self-preservation was stronger than her candour.", "마지막 순간에 용기가 그녀를 저버렸다. 진작 말하지 않았다고 그가 나무랄까 봐 두려웠던 것이다. 그리고 자기를 지키려는 본능이 솔직함보다 더 강했다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론,\""], ["continued the unwitting Clare, “I should have been glad to know you to be descended exclusively from the long-suffering, dumb, unrecorded rank and file of the English nation, and not from the self-seeking few who made themselves powerful at the expense of the rest.", "아무것도 모르는 클레어가 말을 이었다. \"나야 당신이, 나머지를 희생시켜 제 힘을 키운 그 잇속 차리는 소수가 아니라, 오래도록 참고 견디며, 입 다물고, 기록되지도 못한 영국 민중의 그 대열에서만 나온 사람임을 알게 됐다면 기뻤을 거요."], ["But I am corrupted away from that by my affection for you, Tess (he laughed as he spoke), and made selfish likewise.", "하지만 당신을 사랑하는 마음에 나는 거기서 타락해 버렸소, 테스 (그는 말하며 웃었다). 그래서 마찬가지로 잇속을 차리게 됐지."], ["For your own sake I rejoice in your descent.", "당신을 위해서, 나는 당신의 혈통을 기뻐하오."], ["Society is hopelessly snobbish, and this fact of your extraction may make an appreciable difference to its acceptance of you as my wife, after I have made you the well-read woman that I mean to make you.", "세상은 구제 불능으로 속물이니, 당신의 그 출신이라는 사실은, 내가 마음먹은 대로 당신을 책 많이 읽은 여자로 만들고 나면, 세상이 당신을 내 아내로 받아들이는 데 제법 차이를 빚어낼지도 모르오."], ["My mother too, poor soul, will think so much better of you on account of it.", "우리 어머니도, 가엾은 양반, 그것 때문에 당신을 훨씬 더 좋게 보실 거요."], ["Tess, you must spell your name correctly—d’Urberville—from this very day.”", "테스, 당신은 바로 오늘부터 당신 이름을 제대로 써야 하오—더버빌이라고.\""]], [["“I like the other way rather best.”", "\"저는 다른 쪽이 차라리 더 좋은데요.\""]], [["“But you _must_, dearest! Good heavens, why dozens of mushroom millionaires would jump at such a possession!", "\"하지만 그래야 하오, 가장 사랑하는 사람! 맙소사, 어디 졸부 백만장자 수십 명이 그런 가문을 차지하려고 달려들 텐데!"], ["By the bye, there’s one of that kidney who has taken the name—where have I heard of him?—Up in the neighbourhood of The Chase, I think.", "그러고 보니, 그런 부류 가운데 그 이름을 취한 자가 하나 있지—내가 어디서 들었더라?—체이스 근방이었던 것 같은데."], ["Why, he is the very man who had that rumpus with my father I told you of.", "아니, 그자가 바로 내가 당신에게 말한, 우리 아버지와 한바탕했던 그 사람이오."], ["What an odd coincidence!”", "참 묘한 우연이군!\""]], [["“Angel, I think I would rather not take the name!", "\"에인절, 저는 차라리 그 이름을 안 쓰는 게 낫겠어요!"], ["It is unlucky, perhaps!”", "어쩌면 불길한 이름인지도 몰라요!\""]], [["She was agitated.", "그녀는 동요하고 있었다."]], [["“Now then, Mistress Teresa d’Urberville, I have you.", "\"자 그럼, 테레사 더버빌 양, 당신은 내 손안에 있소."], ["Take my name, and so you will escape yours!", "내 이름을 받으시오, 그러면 당신 이름에서 벗어나게 될 테니!"], ["The secret is out, so why should you any longer refuse me?”", "비밀도 드러났으니, 어째서 당신은 더 이상 나를 거절해야 한단 말이오?\""]], [["“If it is _sure_ to make you happy to have me as your wife, and you feel that you do wish to marry me, _very, very_ much—”", "\"저를 아내로 두는 게 정말로 당신을 행복하게 할 게 확실하다면, 그리고 당신이 저와 결혼하기를 정말로, 정말로 간절히 원한다고 느끼신다면—\""]], [["“I do, dearest, of course!”", "\"그렇소, 가장 사랑하는 사람, 물론이오!\""]], [["“I mean, that it is only your wanting me very much, and being hardly able to keep alive without me, whatever my offences, that would make me feel I ought to say I will.”", "\"제 말은, 제게 어떤 허물이 있든, 당신이 저를 너무도 간절히 원하고 저 없이는 도저히 살 수 없겠다고 하셔야만, 제가 그러겠다고 말해야 마땅하다는 마음이 들 거란 거예요.\""]], [["“You will—you do say it, I know!", "\"당신은 그럴 거요—그러겠다고 말하고 있잖소, 나는 알아!"], ["You will be mine for ever and ever.”", "당신은 영원히, 영원토록 내 것이 될 거요.\""], ["He clasped her close and kissed her.", "그가 그녀를 바싹 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""]], [["She had no sooner said it than she burst into a dry hard sobbing, so violent that it seemed to rend her.", "그 말을 내뱉기가 무섭게 그녀는 메마르고 거친 흐느낌을 터뜨렸는데, 어찌나 격렬한지 몸이 찢어지는 듯했다."], ["Tess was not a hysterical girl by any means, and he was surprised.", "테스는 결코 신경질적인 처녀가 아니었던지라, 그는 놀랐다."]], [["“Why do you cry, dearest?”", "\"왜 우는 거요, 가장 사랑하는 사람?\""]], [["“I can’t tell—quite!—I am so glad to think—of being yours, and making you happy!”", "\"잘 모르겠어요—정말!—저는 너무 기뻐서요—당신 것이 되어, 당신을 행복하게 해 드린다고 생각하니!\""]], [["“But this does not seem very much like gladness, my Tessy!”", "\"하지만 이건 그리 기쁜 것 같지가 않은데, 내 테시!\""]], [["“I mean—I cry because I have broken down in my vow! I said I would die unmarried!”", "\"제 말은—제 맹세를 무너뜨렸기에 우는 거예요! 저는 결혼하지 않은 채 죽겠다고 했었거든요!\""]], [["“But, if you love me you would like me to be your husband?”", "\"하지만, 나를 사랑한다면 내가 당신 남편이 되기를 바랄 거 아니오?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes!", "\"네, 네, 네!"], ["But O, I sometimes wish I had never been born!”", "하지만 오, 저는 가끔 차라리 태어나지 말았더라면 싶어요!\""]], [["“Now, my dear Tess, if I did not know that you are very much excited, and very inexperienced, I should say that remark was not very complimentary.", "\"자, 사랑하는 테스, 당신이 무척 흥분해 있고 또 무척 경험이 없다는 걸 내가 모른다면, 그 말은 그리 듣기 좋은 말은 아니라고 했을 거요."], ["How came you to wish that if you care for me?", "나를 마음에 둔다면서 어쩌다 그런 걸 바라게 됐소?"], ["Do you care for me?", "당신, 나를 마음에 두긴 하오?"], ["I wish you would prove it in some way.”", "어떤 식으로든 그걸 증명해 줬으면 좋겠소.\""]], [["“How can I prove it more than I have done?”", "\"제가 한 것보다 어떻게 더 증명하란 말이에요?\""], ["she cried, in a distraction of tenderness.", "그녀가 애정에 정신을 잃고 외쳤다."], ["“Will this prove it more?”", "\"이러면 더 증명이 될까요?\""]], [["She clasped his neck, and for the first time Clare learnt what an impassioned woman’s kisses were like upon the lips of one whom she loved with all her heart and soul, as Tess loved him.", "그녀가 그의 목을 끌어안았고, 클레어는 처음으로, 테스가 그를 사랑하듯 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 사랑하는 사람의 입술에 닿는 격정적인 여자의 입맞춤이 어떤 것인지를 알게 되었다."]], [["“There—now do you believe?”", "\"자—이제 믿으시겠어요?\""], ["she asked, flushed, and wiping her eyes.", "그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며, 눈을 닦으며 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"믿소."], ["I never really doubted—never, never!”", "나는 정말로 의심한 적 없소—한 번도, 한 번도!\""]], [["So they drove on through the gloom, forming one bundle inside the sail-cloth, the horse going as he would, and the rain driving against them.", "그리하여 두 사람은 어둠을 뚫고 마차를 몰아갔다. 돛천 안에서 한 덩이를 이룬 채, 말은 제멋대로 가고, 비는 그들을 향해 들이쳤다."], ["She had consented.", "그녀는 승낙했다."], ["She might as well have agreed at first.", "처음부터 응낙했어도 마찬가지였을 것을."], ["The “appetite for joy” which pervades all creation, that tremendous force which sways humanity to its purpose, as the tide sways the helpless weed, was not to be controlled by vague lucubrations over the social rubric.", "만물에 깃든 그 \"기쁨을 향한 갈망\", 마치 조수가 무력한 해초를 흔들듯 인류를 제 목적 쪽으로 휘젓는 그 어마어마한 힘은, 사회의 규범을 두고 막연히 골머리를 앓는다고 해서 다스려질 것이 아니었다."]], [["“I must write to my mother,”", "\"어머니께 편지를 써야겠어요.\""], ["she said. “You don’t mind my doing that?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그래도 괜찮죠?\""]], [["“Of course not, dear child.", "\"물론이오, 사랑하는 아이."], ["You are a child to me, Tess, not to know how very proper it is to write to your mother at such a time, and how wrong it would be in me to object.", "당신은 내게 아이 같소, 테스. 이런 때에 어머니께 편지 쓰는 게 얼마나 마땅한 일인지, 또 내가 그걸 반대한다면 얼마나 잘못된 일인지조차 모를 만큼 말이오."], ["Where does she live?”", "어머니는 어디 사시오?\""]], [["“At the same place—Marlott.", "\"같은 곳에요—말럿."], ["On the further side of Blackmoor Vale.”", "블랙무어 골짜기 저편이요.\""]], [["“Ah, then I _have_ seen you before this summer—”", "\"아, 그렇다면 나는 이번 여름 전에도 당신을 본 적이 있는 거요—\""]], [["“Yes; at that dance on the green; but you would not dance with me.", "\"네. 그 풀밭 무도회에서요. 그런데 당신은 저와 춤춰 주지 않았죠."], ["O, I hope that is of no ill-omen for us now!”", "오, 그게 지금 우리에게 무슨 불길한 징조가 아니었으면 좋겠어요!\""]], [["# XXXI", "# XXXI"]], [["Tess wrote a most touching and urgent letter to her mother the very next day, and by the end of the week a response to her communication arrived in Joan Durbeyfield’s wandering last-century hand.", "테스는 바로 다음 날 어머니에게 더없이 절절하고 다급한 편지를 써 보냈고, 그 주가 끝나 갈 무렵 조앤 더비필드의 떠돌이 같은 지난 세기풍 필체로 적힌 답장이 그녀의 사연에 응답해 왔다."]], [["Dear Tess,", "사랑하는 테스,"]], [["J write these few lines Hoping they will find you well, as they leave me at Present, thank God for it.", "이 몇 줄을 적으니 네가 잘 지내고 있기를 바란다. 나는 지금 잘 있다, 하느님 덕분이다."], ["Dear Tess, we are all glad to Hear that you are going really to be married soon.", "사랑하는 테스, 네가 정말로 곧 결혼하게 됐다는 말을 들으니 우리 모두 기쁘다."], ["But with respect to your question, Tess, J say between ourselves, quite private but very strong, that on no account do you say a word of your Bygone Trouble to him.", "그런데 네 물음에 대해서는, 테스, 우리끼리만, 아주 은밀히, 그러나 아주 강하게 말하건대, 무슨 일이 있어도 그에게 네 지난 곤경에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 마라."], ["J did not tell everything to your Father, he being so Proud on account of his Respectability, which, perhaps, your Intended is the same.", "나도 네 아버지에게 다 말하지 않았다. 그이가 자기 체면을 어찌나 떠받드는지, 어쩌면 네 약혼자도 그 모양일지 모른다."], ["Many a woman—some of the Highest in the Land—have had a Trouble in their time; and why should you Trumpet yours when others don’t Trumpet theirs?", "많은 여자가—이 나라에서 가장 높은 분들 가운데도 더러—한때 곤경을 겪었다. 그런데 남들도 제 일을 나발 불지 않는데 어째서 네가 네 일을 나발 불어야 하느냐?"], ["No girl would be such a Fool, specially as it is so long ago, and not your Fault at all.", "그렇게 멍청한 계집은 없을 게다. 더구나 이미 그토록 오래전 일이고, 네 잘못도 전혀 아닌데 말이다."], ["J shall answer the same if you ask me fifty times.", "네가 쉰 번을 물어도 나는 똑같이 대답하겠다."], ["Besides, you must bear in mind that, knowing it to be your Childish Nature to tell all that’s in your heart—so simple!—J made you promise me never to let it out by Word or Deed, having your Welfare in my Mind; and you most solemnly did promise it going from this Door.", "게다가, 명심해라. 네가 마음에 있는 걸 다 털어놓는 어린애 같은 천성이라는 걸 알기에—어찌나 단순한지!—나는 네 앞날을 마음에 두고서, 절대로 말로든 행동으로든 그걸 새어 나가게 하지 않겠다고 네게 약속을 받았다. 그리고 너는 이 문을 나서며 더없이 엄숙하게 그러겠다고 약속했다."], ["J have not named either that Question or your coming marriage to your Father, as he would blab it everywhere, poor Simple Man.", "나는 네 아버지에게 그 일도, 다가올 네 결혼 이야기도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그이가 가는 데마다 떠벌릴 테니까, 가엾고 단순한 사람."], ["Dear Tess, keep up your Spirits, and we mean to send you a Hogshead of Cyder for you Wedding, knowing there is not much in your parts, and thin Sour Stuff what there is.", "사랑하는 테스, 기운을 내어라. 네가 사는 고장에는 변변한 게 별로 없고, 있어 봐야 묽고 시큼한 것뿐인 줄 아니, 우리가 결혼 선물로 사과술 한 통을 보내려 한다."], ["So no more at present, and with kind love to your Young Man.—From your affectte. Mother,", "그럼 오늘은 이만 줄이고, 네 젊은이에게도 안부 전한다.—사랑을 담아, 네 어미가,"]], [["J. Durbeyfield", "제이 더비필드"]], [["“O mother, mother!”", "\"오 어머니, 어머니!\""], ["murmured Tess.", "테스가 중얼거렸다."]], [["She was recognizing how light was the touch of events the most oppressive upon Mrs Durbeyfield’s elastic spirit.", "가장 짓누르는 사건들조차 더비필드 부인의 탄력 있는 마음에는 얼마나 가벼이 와닿는지를 그녀는 깨닫고 있었다."], ["Her mother did not see life as Tess saw it.", "어머니는 테스가 보는 대로 삶을 보지 않았다."], ["That haunting episode of bygone days was to her mother but a passing accident.", "지난날의 그 잊히지 않는 사건도 어머니에게는 한낱 스쳐 가는 사고에 지나지 않았다."], ["But perhaps her mother was right as to the course to be followed, whatever she might be in her reasons.", "그러나 따라야 할 길에 관해서는, 그 이유야 어떻든, 어쩌면 어머니가 옳은지도 몰랐다."], ["Silence seemed, on the face of it, best for her adored one’s happiness: silence it should be.", "겉으로 보기에 침묵이야말로 자기가 떠받드는 그이의 행복을 위해 가장 나은 길 같았다. 침묵하기로 하자."]], [["Thus steadied by a command from the only person in the world who had any shadow of right to control her action, Tess grew calmer.", "이렇게, 세상에서 그녀의 행동을 좌우할 권리의 그림자나마 지닌 유일한 사람의 명령으로 마음을 다잡자, 테스는 한결 차분해졌다."], ["The responsibility was shifted, and her heart was lighter than it had been for weeks.", "책임이 옮겨졌고, 그녀의 마음은 몇 주 만에 가장 가벼웠다."], ["The days of declining autumn which followed her assent, beginning with the month of October, formed a season through which she lived in spiritual altitudes more nearly approaching ecstasy than any other period of her life.", "그녀가 승낙한 뒤로 이어진, 시월부터 시작된 저무는 가을날들은, 그녀가 평생 어느 때보다 황홀경에 더 가까운 영적 높이에서 살아간 한철을 이루었다."]], [["There was hardly a touch of earth in her love for Clare.", "클레어를 향한 그녀의 사랑에는 흙냄새라고는 거의 한 점도 없었다."], ["To her sublime trustfulness he was all that goodness could be—knew all that a guide, philosopher, and friend should know.", "그녀의 숭고한 믿음에 그는 선함이 다다를 수 있는 모든 것이었다—인도자이자 철학자이자 벗이 알아야 할 모든 것을 아는 사람이었다."], ["She thought every line in the contour of his person the perfection of masculine beauty, his soul the soul of a saint, his intellect that of a seer.", "그녀는 그의 몸매를 이루는 윤곽의 선 하나하나가 남성미의 완성이요, 그의 영혼은 성인의 영혼이며, 그의 지성은 선지자의 지성이라고 여겼다."], ["The wisdom of her love for him, as love, sustained her dignity; she seemed to be wearing a crown.", "그를 향한 사랑의 슬기로움이, 사랑으로서, 그녀의 품위를 떠받쳐 주었다. 그녀는 마치 왕관을 쓴 듯했다."], ["The compassion of his love for her, as she saw it, made her lift up her heart to him in devotion.", "그녀가 본 대로, 그녀를 향한 그의 사랑의 연민은 그녀로 하여금 헌신하는 마음으로 그에게 마음을 들어 올리게 했다."], ["He would sometimes catch her large, worshipful eyes, that had no bottom to them looking at him from their depths, as if she saw something immortal before her.", "그는 이따금, 깊은 바닥이라곤 없는 그녀의 커다랗고 경배하는 눈이 그 심연에서 자기를 바라보는 것을 마주치곤 했다. 마치 그녀가 눈앞에 무언가 불멸하는 것을 보고 있기라도 한 듯이."]], [["She dismissed the past—trod upon it and put it out, as one treads on a coal that is smouldering and dangerous.", "그녀는 과거를 떨쳐 버렸다—그것을 짓밟아 꺼 버렸다. 마치 연기를 내며 위험하게 타들어 가는 숯덩이를 밟아 끄듯이."]], [["She had not known that men could be so disinterested, chivalrous, protective, in their love for women as he.", "그녀는 남자들이 여자를 향한 사랑에서 그처럼 사심 없고, 의협심 있고, 지켜 줄 줄 아는 존재일 수 있다는 것을 미처 몰랐었다."], ["Angel Clare was far from all that she thought him in this respect; absurdly far, indeed; but he was, in truth, more spiritual than animal; he had himself well in hand, and was singularly free from grossness.", "이 점에서 에인절 클레어는 그녀가 생각하는 그런 사람과는 한참, 정말이지 터무니없이 멀었다. 그러나 그는 진실로, 동물적이라기보다 영적인 사람이었다. 자기 자신을 잘 다스렸고, 보기 드물게 천박함에서 자유로웠다."], ["Though not cold-natured, he was rather bright than hot—less Byronic than Shelleyan; could love desperately, but with a love more especially inclined to the imaginative and ethereal; it was a fastidious emotion which could jealously guard the loved one against his very self.", "차가운 천성은 아니었으나, 뜨겁다기보다 밝은 쪽이었다—바이런풍이라기보다 셸리풍이었다. 필사적으로 사랑할 수는 있었으되, 그 사랑은 유난히 상상적이고 영묘한 쪽으로 기울었다. 사랑하는 이를 다른 누구도 아닌 바로 자기 자신으로부터 시샘하듯 지켜 낼 수 있는, 까다로운 감정이었다."], ["This amazed and enraptured Tess, whose slight experiences had been so infelicitous till now; and in her reaction from indignation against the male sex she swerved to excess of honour for Clare.", "이것이 테스를 놀라게 하고 황홀하게 했다. 지금껏 그녀의 짧은 경험이 너무도 불행했던 까닭이었다. 남성에 대한 분노에서 되돌아오는 반동으로, 그녀는 클레어를 지나치게 떠받드는 쪽으로 휩쓸려 갔다."]], [["They unaffectedly sought each other’s company; in her honest faith she did not disguise her desire to be with him.", "두 사람은 꾸밈없이 서로의 곁을 찾았다. 그녀는 정직한 믿음으로, 그와 함께 있고 싶다는 바람을 감추지 않았다."], ["The sum of her instincts on this matter, if clearly stated, would have been that the elusive quality of her sex which attracts men in general might be distasteful to so perfect a man after an avowal of love, since it must in its very nature carry with it a suspicion of art.", "이 문제에 관한 그녀의 본능을 죄다 한데 모아 분명히 말하자면 이런 것이었다. 사랑을 고백한 마당에, 뭇 남자를 끌어당기는 여자라는 성의 그 종잡을 수 없는 속성이, 그토록 완벽한 남자에게는 도리어 거슬릴지도 모른다는 것. 왜냐하면 그것은 그 본성상 어쩔 수 없이 술수의 낌새를 띠고 있을 테니까."]], [["The country custom of unreserved comradeship out of doors during betrothal was the only custom she knew, and to her it had no strangeness; though it seemed oddly anticipative to Clare till he saw how normal a thing she, in common with all the other dairy-folk, regarded it.", "약혼 기간에 바깥에서 거리낌 없이 동무로 지내는 그 시골 풍습이 그녀가 아는 유일한 풍습이었고, 그녀에게는 그것이 조금도 이상하지 않았다. 다만 클레어에게는 그것이 묘하게 앞서 나가는 것처럼 보였는데, 그녀가 다른 모든 낙농장 사람과 마찬가지로 그것을 얼마나 당연한 일로 여기는지를 보고 나서야 그렇지 않게 되었다."], ["Thus, during this October month of wonderful afternoons they roved along the meads by creeping paths which followed the brinks of trickling tributary brooks, hopping across by little wooden bridges to the other side, and back again.", "그리하여, 이 시월의 경이로운 오후들 동안 그들은 졸졸 흐르는 지류 개울가를 따라가는 좁은 오솔길을 따라 목초지를 거닐었고, 작은 나무다리를 폴짝 건너 건너편으로 갔다가 다시 돌아오곤 했다."], ["They were never out of the sound of some purling weir, whose buzz accompanied their own murmuring, while the beams of the sun, almost as horizontal as the mead itself, formed a pollen of radiance over the landscape.", "그들은 졸졸거리는 어느 둑의 소리에서 벗어나는 일이 없었으니, 그 잔잔한 울림이 두 사람의 도란거림에 동무가 되어 주었다. 한편 햇살은, 목초지 그 자체만큼이나 수평으로 누워, 풍경 위로 빛의 꽃가루를 흩뿌렸다."], ["They saw tiny blue fogs in the shadows of trees and hedges, all the time that there was bright sunshine elsewhere.", "그들은 나무와 산울타리의 그늘 속에 자그마한 푸른 안개가 어린 것을 보았다. 다른 데서는 여태 환한 햇살이 비치는 동안에도 말이다."], ["The sun was so near the ground, and the sward so flat, that the shadows of Clare and Tess would stretch a quarter of a mile ahead of them, like two long fingers pointing afar to where the green alluvial reaches abutted against the sloping sides of the vale.", "해가 어찌나 땅에 가깝고 풀밭이 어찌나 평평한지, 클레어와 테스의 그림자는 그들 앞으로 사분의 일 마일이나 길게 뻗어, 마치 두 개의 긴 손가락이 멀리, 푸른 충적 평지가 골짜기의 비탈진 옆구리에 맞닿는 곳을 가리키는 것 같았다."]], [["Men were at work here and there—for it was the season for “taking up” the meadows, or digging the little waterways clear for the winter irrigation, and mending their banks where trodden down by the cows.", "여기저기서 사람들이 일하고 있었다—목초지를 \"손보거나\", 겨울 관개를 위해 작은 물길을 파서 트고, 소에게 밟혀 무너진 둑을 손보는 철이었기 때문이다."], ["The shovelfuls of loam, black as jet, brought there by the river when it was as wide as the whole valley, were an essence of soils, pounded champaigns of the past, steeped, refined, and subtilized to extraordinary richness, out of which came all the fertility of the mead, and of the cattle grazing there.", "강이 골짜기 전체만큼 넓었던 시절에 그 강이 실어다 놓은, 흑옥처럼 새까만 양토 삽질거리는, 흙의 정수였다. 지난날의 들판을 빻고, 우려내고, 정제하고, 갈고닦아 비할 데 없는 기름짐에 이르게 한 것이었으며, 거기서 목초지의, 그리고 거기서 풀을 뜯는 가축의 그 모든 비옥함이 비롯되었다."]], [["Clare hardily kept his arm round her waist in sight of these watermen, with the air of a man who was accustomed to public dalliance, though actually as shy as she who, with lips parted and eyes askance on the labourers, wore the look of a wary animal the while.", "클레어는 이 물일꾼들이 빤히 보는 앞에서도 대담하게 그녀의 허리를 감싼 채로 있었다. 마치 사람들 앞에서 시시덕거리는 데 익숙한 남자 같은 태도였으나, 사실은 입을 벌리고 일꾼들을 곁눈질하며 줄곧 경계하는 짐승 같은 표정을 짓고 있는 그녀만큼이나 수줍었다."]], [["“You are not ashamed of owning me as yours before them!”", "\"당신은 저들 앞에서 저를 당신 사람이라고 인정하는 걸 부끄러워하지 않으시네요!\""], ["she said gladly. “O no!”", "그녀가 기뻐하며 말했다."]], [["“But if it should reach the ears of your friends at Emminster that you are walking about like this with me, a milkmaid—”", "\"오 그렇소!\" \"하지만 당신이 저처럼 우유 짜는 처녀와 이렇게 거닐고 다닌다는 게 에민스터에 있는 당신 친구분들 귀에 들어가기라도 하면—\""]], [["“The most bewitching milkmaid ever seen.”", "\"여태껏 본 가장 매혹적인 우유 짜는 처녀지.\""]], [["“They might feel it a hurt to their dignity.”", "\"그분들은 그게 자기네 체면에 상처라고 느낄지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“My dear girl—a d’Urberville hurt the dignity of a Clare!", "\"내 사랑하는 사람—더버빌 가가 클레어 가의 체면을 상하게 한다니!"], ["It is a grand card to play—that of your belonging to such a family, and I am reserving it for a grand effect when we are married, and have the proofs of your descent from Parson Tringham.", "그건 멋진 패요—당신이 그런 가문에 속한다는 것 말이오. 나는 우리가 결혼하고, 당신이 트링엄 목사에게서 나온 그 가계의 증거를 갖추게 됐을 때 멋지게 써먹으려고 그걸 아껴 두고 있다오."], ["Apart from that, my future is to be totally foreign to my family—it will not affect even the surface of their lives.", "그건 그렇다 치고, 내 앞날은 우리 집안과는 완전히 동떨어지게 될 거요—그들의 삶은 표면조차 건드리지 못할 거요."], ["We shall leave this part of England—perhaps England itself—and what does it matter how people regard us here?", "우리는 영국의 이 지역을 떠날 거요—어쩌면 영국 자체를—그러니 여기 사람들이 우리를 어떻게 보든 그게 무슨 상관이겠소?"], ["You will like going, will you not?”", "당신도 떠나는 게 좋겠지, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["She could answer no more than a bare affirmative, so great was the emotion aroused in her at the thought of going through the world with him as his own familiar friend.", "그와 함께, 그의 막역한 벗으로서 세상을 헤쳐 나간다는 생각에 그녀 안에서 솟아오른 감정이 너무도 큰 나머지, 그녀는 그저 그렇다는 짤막한 긍정 외에는 더 대답할 수가 없었다."], ["Her feelings almost filled her ears like a babble of waves, and surged up to her eyes.", "그녀의 감정은 파도가 와글거리듯 그녀의 귀를 거의 가득 메우며 눈가로 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["She put her hand in his, and thus they went on, to a place where the reflected sun glared up from the river, under a bridge, with a molten-metallic glow that dazzled their eyes, though the sun itself was hidden by the bridge.", "그녀는 자기 손을 그의 손에 얹었고, 그렇게 두 사람은 나아갔다. 다리 아래 강에서 비친 해가, 녹은 쇳물 같은 금속성 빛으로 두 사람의 눈을 부시게 하며 솟아오르는 곳까지. 정작 해 자체는 다리에 가려 보이지 않았건만."], ["They stood still, whereupon little furred and feathered heads popped up from the smooth surface of the water; but, finding that the disturbing presences had paused, and not passed by, they disappeared again.", "두 사람이 가만히 멈춰 서자, 매끈한 물 표면에서 털 난 머리며 깃 난 머리가 빠끔 솟아올랐다. 그러나 그 거슬리는 존재들이 지나가지 않고 멈춰 서 있다는 걸 알아채자, 그것들은 다시 사라졌다."], ["Upon this river-brink they lingered till the fog began to close round them—which was very early in the evening at this time of the year—settling on the lashes of her eyes, where it rested like crystals, and on his brows and hair.", "이 강가에서 두 사람은 안개가 그들 둘레로 자욱이 내려앉기 시작할 때까지 머뭇거렸다—이 무렵이면 저녁 일찍 그러했다—안개는 그녀의 속눈썹에 내려앉아 수정처럼 거기 머물렀고, 그의 눈썹과 머리카락에도 내려앉았다."]], [["They walked later on Sundays, when it was quite dark.", "일요일이면 두 사람은 아주 캄캄해진 뒤 더 늦게까지 걸었다."], ["Some of the dairy-people, who were also out of doors on the first Sunday evening after their engagement, heard her impulsive speeches, ecstasized to fragments, though they were too far off to hear the words discoursed; noted the spasmodic catch in her remarks, broken into syllables by the leapings of her heart, as she walked leaning on his arm; her contented pauses, the occasional little laugh upon which her soul seemed to ride—the laugh of a woman in company with the man she loves and has won from all other women—unlike anything else in nature.", "그들이 약혼한 뒤 첫 일요일 저녁에 역시 밖에 나와 있던 몇몇 낙농장 사람들은, 비록 주고받는 말까지 들리기엔 너무 멀리 있었으나, 황홀경에 부서진 그녀의 충동적인 말소리를 들었다. 그녀가 그의 팔에 기대어 걸으며, 가슴의 두근거림에 음절이 끊겨 발작하듯 걸리는 그녀의 말투며, 만족스러운 멈춤이며, 그녀의 영혼이 거기 올라탄 듯한 이따금의 가벼운 웃음을—자기가 사랑하고 다른 모든 여자에게서 쟁취해 낸 남자와 함께 있는 여자의 그 웃음, 자연의 그 무엇과도 같지 않은 웃음을 알아차렸다."], ["They marked the buoyancy of her tread, like the skim of a bird which has not quite alighted.", "그들은 마치 아직 완전히 내려앉지 않은 새가 스치듯 나는 것 같은, 그녀의 발걸음의 가벼움을 눈여겨보았다."]], [["Her affection for him was now the breath and life of Tess’s being; it enveloped her as a photosphere, irradiated her into forgetfulness of her past sorrows, keeping back the gloomy spectres that would persist in their attempts to touch her—doubt, fear, moodiness, care, shame.", "이제 그를 향한 애정은 테스라는 존재의 숨이요 생명이었다. 그것은 광구처럼 그녀를 에워쌌고, 그녀를 환히 비추어 지난날의 슬픔을 잊게 했으며, 끈질기게 그녀를 건드리려 드는 음울한 망령들을—의심, 두려움, 침울, 근심, 수치를—물리쳐 주었다."], ["She knew that they were waiting like wolves just outside the circumscribing light, but she had long spells of power to keep them in hungry subjection there.", "그녀는 그것들이 그 에워싸는 빛의 바깥에서 늑대처럼 기다리고 있음을 알았으나, 한참씩 그것들을 굶주린 채 거기 굴복시켜 둘 힘이 있었다."]], [["A spiritual forgetfulness co-existed with an intellectual remembrance.", "영적인 망각이 지적인 기억과 나란히 공존했다."], ["She walked in brightness, but she knew that in the background those shapes of darkness were always spread.", "그녀는 환한 빛 속을 걸었으나, 그 뒤편에 저 어둠의 형상들이 늘 펼쳐져 있음을 알았다."], ["They might be receding, or they might be approaching, one or the other, a little every day.", "그것들이 물러가고 있는지, 다가오고 있는지, 둘 중 하나로 날마다 조금씩 움직이고 있었다."]], [["One evening Tess and Clare were obliged to sit indoors keeping house, all the other occupants of the domicile being away.", "어느 날 저녁 테스와 클레어는 집에 남은 다른 식구가 죄다 나가 있어 어쩔 수 없이 안에서 집을 지키며 앉아 있게 되었다."], ["As they talked she looked thoughtfully up at him, and met his two appreciative eyes.", "이야기를 나누다가 그녀가 생각에 잠긴 채 그를 올려다보았고, 그를 흐뭇하게 바라보는 그의 두 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["“I am not worthy of you—no, I am not!” she burst out, jumping up from her low stool as though appalled at his homage, and the fulness of her own joy thereat.", "\"저는 당신에게 어울리는 사람이 아니에요—아니에요, 아니라고요!\" 그녀가, 그의 떠받듦과 그로 인한 자기 자신의 그 벅찬 기쁨에 질겁한 듯, 낮은 걸상에서 벌떡 일어서며 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["Clare, deeming the whole basis of her excitement to be that which was only the smaller part of it, said—", "클레어는 그녀의 흥분이 죄다 그 일에서 비롯되었다고 여겼는데, 그것은 그저 그 흥분의 작은 부분일 뿐이었다. 그가 말했다—"]], [["“I won’t have you speak like it, dear Tess!", "\"그런 식으로 말하는 건 못 봐주겠소, 사랑하는 테스!"], ["Distinction does not consist in the facile use of a contemptible set of conventions, but in being numbered among those who are true, and honest, and just, and pure, and lovely, and of good report—as you are, my Tess.”", "사람의 됨됨이는 경멸할 만한 한 무더기 인습을 손쉽게 부려 쓰는 데 있는 게 아니라, 참되고, 정직하고, 의롭고, 순결하고, 사랑스럽고, 평판 좋은 이들 가운데 헤아려지는 데 있는 거요—바로 당신처럼 말이오, 내 테스.\""]], [["She struggled with the sob in her throat.", "그녀는 목구멍으로 치미는 흐느낌과 싸웠다."], ["How often had that string of excellences made her young heart ache in church of late years, and how strange that he should have cited them now.", "그 미덕들의 나열이 요 몇 해 교회에서 얼마나 자주 그녀의 어린 가슴을 아프게 했던가. 그리고 하필 지금 그가 그것을 들먹이다니 얼마나 묘한 일인가."]], [["“Why didn’t you stay and love me when I—was sixteen; living with my little sisters and brothers, and you danced on the green?", "\"제가—열여섯 살이었을 때, 어린 동생들과 함께 살고, 당신이 풀밭에서 춤추던 그때, 왜 머물러서 저를 사랑해 주지 않았어요?"], ["O, why didn’t you, why didn’t you!”", "오, 왜 그러지 않았어요, 왜 그러지 않았어요!\""], ["she said, impetuously clasping her hands.", "그녀가 두 손을 격하게 맞잡으며 말했다."]], [["Angel began to comfort and reassure her, thinking to himself, truly enough, what a creature of moods she was, and how careful he would have to be of her when she depended for her happiness entirely on him.", "에인절은 그녀를 달래고 안심시키기 시작했다. 그러면서 속으로, 정말이지 그녀가 얼마나 변덕스러운 사람인지, 그리고 그녀가 행복을 오롯이 자기에게 의지하게 되면 그녀를 얼마나 조심스레 대해야 할지를 생각했다."]], [["“Ah—why didn’t I stay!”", "\"아—내가 왜 머물지 않았던고!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“That is just what I feel.", "\"바로 그게 내가 느끼는 바요."], ["If I had only known!", "그때 내가 알기만 했더라면!"], ["But you must not be so bitter in your regret—why should you be?”", "하지만 그렇게 쓰라리게 후회할 건 없소—왜 그래야 하오?\""]], [["With the woman’s instinct to hide she diverged hastily—", "감추려는 여자의 본능으로 그녀는 황급히 말머리를 돌렸다—"]], [["“I should have had four years more of your heart than I can ever have now.", "\"저는 지금 가질 수 있는 것보다 사 년 더 당신의 마음을 가질 수 있었을 텐데요."], ["Then I should not have wasted my time as I have done—I should have had so much longer happiness!”", "그랬다면 저는 지금처럼 시간을 허비하지 않았을 거고요—그만큼 훨씬 더 오래 행복했을 텐데요!\""]], [["It was no mature woman with a long dark vista of intrigue behind her who was tormented thus, but a girl of simple life, not yet one-and twenty, who had been caught during her days of immaturity like a bird in a springe.", "이렇게 괴로워하는 이는, 뒤에 길고 어두운 음모의 행렬을 거느린 다 자란 여자가 아니라, 단순한 삶을 살아온, 아직 스물한 살도 안 된 처녀였다. 미숙하던 시절에 올가미에 걸린 새처럼 붙들린 처녀였다."], ["To calm herself the more completely, she rose from her little stool and left the room, overturning the stool with her skirts as she went.", "마음을 더 온전히 가라앉히려고 그녀는 작은 걸상에서 일어나 방을 나갔는데, 나가는 길에 치맛자락으로 걸상을 넘어뜨렸다."]], [["He sat on by the cheerful firelight thrown from a bundle of green ash-sticks laid across the dogs; the sticks snapped pleasantly, and hissed out bubbles of sap from their ends.", "그는 장작 받침쇠에 걸쳐 놓은 푸른 물푸레나무 다발에서 솟는 명랑한 불빛 곁에 계속 앉아 있었다. 나뭇가지들이 기분 좋게 탁탁 튀며, 끝에서 수액 거품을 쉭쉭 뿜어냈다."], ["When she came back she was herself again.", "그녀가 돌아왔을 때는 다시 평소의 그녀로 돌아와 있었다."]], [["“Do you not think you are just a wee bit capricious, fitful, Tess?”", "\"당신은 좀 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없는 데가 있다고 생각하지 않소, 테스?\""], ["he said, good-humouredly, as he spread a cushion for her on the stool, and seated himself in the settle beside her. “I wanted to ask you something, and just then you ran away.”", "그가, 걸상에 방석을 펴 주고 그녀 곁 긴 의자에 앉으며, 너그러이 말했다. \"당신한테 뭘 좀 물어보려던 참이었는데, 바로 그때 당신이 달아나 버렸잖소.\""]], [["“Yes, perhaps I am capricious,”", "\"네, 어쩌면 제가 변덕스러운지도 몰라요.\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["She suddenly approached him, and put a hand upon each of his arms.", "그녀는 불쑥 그에게 다가가더니 그의 두 팔에 각각 한 손씩 얹었다."], ["“No, Angel, I am not really so—by nature, I mean!” The more particularly to assure him that she was not, she placed herself close to him in the settle, and allowed her head to find a resting-place against Clare’s shoulder.", "\"아니에요, 에인절, 저는 정말로 그렇진 않아요—천성이 그렇진 않다는 말이에요!\" 자기가 그렇지 않다는 걸 더더욱 확실히 보여 주려고, 그녀는 긴 의자에서 그에게 바싹 붙어 앉아, 머리를 클레어의 어깨에 기대어 쉬게 했다."], ["“What did you want to ask me—I am sure I will answer it,” she continued humbly.", "\"뭘 물어보려고 하셨어요—분명히 제가 대답할게요.\" 그녀가 다소곳이 말을 이었다."]], [["“Well, you love me, and have agreed to marry me, and hence there follows a thirdly, ‘When shall the day be?’”", "\"음, 당신은 나를 사랑하고, 나와 결혼하기로 동의했소. 그러니 거기서 셋째가 따라 나오지. '그날을 언제로 할까?'\""]], [["“I like living like this.”", "\"저는 이렇게 사는 게 좋아요.\""]], [["“But I must think of starting in business on my own hook with the new year, or a little later.", "\"하지만 나는 새해부터, 아니면 조금 더 늦게라도, 내 힘으로 사업을 시작할 생각을 해야 하오."], ["And before I get involved in the multifarious details of my new position, I should like to have secured my partner.”", "그리고 내 새 처지의 갖가지 잡다한 일에 얽혀 들기 전에, 내 동반자부터 확실히 해 두고 싶소.\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["she timidly answered, “to talk quite practically, wouldn’t it be best not to marry till after all that?—Though I can’t bear the thought o’ your going away and leaving me here!”", "그녀가 머뭇거리며 대답했다. \"아주 현실적으로 말하자면, 그게 다 끝날 때까지는 결혼하지 않는 편이 가장 낫지 않을까요?—당신이 떠나서 저를 여기 두고 가신다는 생각은 견딜 수가 없지만요!\""]], [["“Of course you cannot—and it is not best in this case.", "\"물론 당신은 견딜 수 없지—그리고 이 경우엔 그게 가장 나은 길도 아니오."], ["I want you to help me in many ways in making my start.", "나는 여러모로 내 출발을 도와줄 당신이 필요하오."], ["When shall it be?", "언제로 할까?"], ["Why not a fortnight from now?”", "지금부터 보름 뒤는 어떻소?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"안 돼요.\""], ["she said, becoming grave: “I have so many things to think of first.”", "그녀가 정색하며 말했다. \"먼저 생각해야 할 게 너무 많아요.\" \"하지만—\""]], [["“But—” He drew her gently nearer to him.", "그가 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 부드럽게 더 끌어당겼다."]], [["The reality of marriage was startling when it loomed so near.", "결혼이라는 현실은 그토록 가까이 다가서자 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것이었다."], ["Before discussion of the question had proceeded further there walked round the corner of the settle into the full firelight of the apartment Mr Dairyman Crick, Mrs Crick, and two of the milkmaids.", "그 문제를 두고 더 이야기가 진척되기도 전에, 긴 의자 모퉁이를 돌아 방 안의 환한 불빛 속으로 크릭 주인과 크릭 부인, 그리고 우유 짜는 처녀 둘이 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["Tess sprang like an elastic ball from his side to her feet, while her face flushed and her eyes shone in the firelight.", "테스는 탄력 있는 공처럼 그의 곁에서 튕겨져 일어섰고, 그녀의 얼굴은 불빛 속에서 붉어지고 눈은 반짝였다."]], [["“I knew how it would be if I sat so close to him!”", "\"이렇게 그분 곁에 바싹 앉아 있으면 어찌 될지 알고 있었다니까요!\""], ["she cried, with vexation.", "그녀가 분해하며 외쳤다."], ["“I said to myself, they are sure to come and catch us!", "\"다들 분명히 와서 우릴 들킬 거라고 속으로 그랬는데!"], ["But I wasn’t really sitting on his knee, though it might ha’ seemed as if I was almost!”", "하지만 저는 정말로 그분 무릎에 앉아 있었던 게 아니에요, 거의 그래 보였을지는 몰라도요!\""]], [["“Well—if so be you hadn’t told us, I am sure we shouldn’t ha’ noticed that ye had been sitting anywhere at all in this light,”", "\"음—굳이 말 안 했더라면, 이 불빛으로는 당신이 대체 어디 앉아 있었는지 우린 영 알아채지도 못했을 거요.\""], ["replied the dairyman.", "주인이 대꾸했다."], ["He continued to his wife, with the stolid mien of a man who understood nothing of the emotions relating to matrimony—“Now, Christianer, that shows that folks should never fancy other folks be supposing things when they bain’t.", "그러고는 결혼에 얽힌 감정이라곤 도무지 모르는 사내의 무덤덤한 낯빛으로 아내에게 말을 이었다—\"자, 크리스티애너, 이게 다 사람이란 게 멀쩡한 남들을 두고 괜히 무슨 생각을 하겠거니 짐작해선 안 된다는 본보기지."], ["O no, I should never ha’ thought a word of where she was a sitting to, if she hadn’t told me—not I.”", "오 그렇고말고, 저 애가 말만 안 했더라면 어디 앉아 있었는지 한마디도 생각조차 안 했을 거야—나는 아니지.\""]], [["“We are going to be married soon,”", "\"우리는 곧 결혼할 겁니다.\""], ["said Clare, with improvised phlegm.", "클레어가 짐짓 태연한 척하며 말했다."]], [["“Ah—and be ye!", "\"아—그러신다고요!"], ["Well, I am truly glad to hear it, sir.", "음, 정말 반가운 소식입니다, 나리."], ["I’ve thought you mid do such a thing for some time.", "한동안 그러실 거라고 생각하고 있었습죠."], ["She’s too good for a dairymaid—I said so the very first day I zid her—and a prize for any man; and what’s more, a wonderful woman for a gentleman-farmer’s wife; he won’t be at the mercy of his baily wi’ her at his side.”", "저 애는 우유 짜는 처녀로 두기엔 너무 아까운 애예요—제가 처음 본 그날부터 그리 말했습죠—누구한테든 큰 상이고요. 게다가 신사 농장주의 아내로는 더할 나위 없는 여자예요. 저 애를 곁에 두면 나리께선 농장지기 손아귀에 휘둘리실 일이 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["Somehow Tess disappeared.", "어느새 테스는 사라지고 없었다."], ["She had been even more struck with the look of the girls who followed Crick than abashed by Crick’s blunt praise.", "그녀는 크릭의 무뚝뚝한 칭찬에 무안했다기보다, 크릭을 뒤따라 들어온 처녀들의 표정에 더 충격을 받았던 것이다."], ["After supper, when she reached her bedroom, they were all present.", "저녁을 먹은 뒤 그녀가 침실에 이르렀을 때, 그들은 다 거기 있었다."], ["A light was burning, and each damsel was sitting up whitely in her bed, awaiting Tess, the whole like a row of avenging ghosts.", "등불 하나가 켜져 있었고, 처녀들마다 하얗게 침대에서 몸을 일으킨 채 테스를 기다리고 있었다. 그 모습이 마치 복수하러 온 유령들이 한 줄로 늘어선 것 같았다."]], [["But she saw in a few moments that there was no malice in their mood.", "그러나 그들의 기분에 악의라곤 없다는 것을 그녀는 곧 알아차렸다."], ["They could scarcely feel as a loss what they had never expected to have.", "그들은 애초에 가져 보리라 기대조차 하지 않았던 것을 잃었다고 느낄 수는 없었던 것이다."], ["Their condition was objective, contemplative.", "그들의 상태는 객관적이고 관조적이었다."]], [["“He’s going to marry her!”", "\"저이가 저 애랑 결혼한대!\""], ["murmured Retty, never taking eyes off Tess.", "레티가 테스에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“How her face do show it!”", "\"얼굴에 다 쓰여 있는걸!\""]], [["“You _be_ going to marry him?”", "\"너 정말 그분이랑 결혼하는 거야?\""], ["asked Marian.", "매리언이 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"응.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“When?”", "\"언제?\""]], [["“Some day.”", "\"언젠가.\""]], [["They thought that this was evasiveness only.", "그들은 이것이 그저 둘러대는 말이라고 여겼다."]], [["“_Yes_—going to _marry_ him—a gentleman!”", "\"그래—그분이랑 결혼한대—신사분이랑!\""], ["repeated Izz Huett.", "이즈 휴엣이 거듭 말했다."]], [["And by a sort of fascination the three girls, one after another, crept out of their beds, and came and stood barefooted round Tess.", "그리고 일종의 홀린 듯한 마음에 세 처녀는 하나씩 차례로 침대에서 기어 나와, 다가와서 맨발로 테스를 둘러싸고 섰다."], ["Retty put her hands upon Tess’s shoulders, as if to realize her friend’s corporeality after such a miracle, and the other two laid their arms round her waist, all looking into her face.", "레티는 그런 기적이 일어난 뒤 친구의 몸뚱이가 진짜인지 확인이라도 하려는 듯 테스의 어깨에 두 손을 얹었고, 나머지 둘은 그녀의 허리에 팔을 둘렀다. 모두가 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“How it do seem!", "\"어쩜 이럴 수가!"], ["Almost more than I can think of!”", "내가 도무지 생각할 수 있는 것 이상이야!\""], ["said Izz Huett.", "이즈 휴엣이 말했다."]], [["Marian kissed Tess. “Yes,”", "매리언이 테스에게 입을 맞추었다. \"그래.\""], ["she murmured as she withdrew her lips.", "그녀가 입술을 떼며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Was that because of love for her, or because other lips have touched there by now?” continued Izz drily to Marian.", "\"그건 쟤를 사랑해서야, 아니면 이젠 다른 입술이 거기 닿았으니까야?\" 이즈가 매리언에게 시큰둥하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“I wasn’t thinking o’ that,”", "\"난 그런 생각 한 거 아냐.\""], ["said Marian simply.", "매리언이 순순히 말했다."], ["“I was on’y feeling all the strangeness o’t—that she is to be his wife, and nobody else.", "\"난 그저 이게 다 너무 야릇해서—쟤가 그분 아내가 되고, 다른 누구도 아니라는 게."], ["I don’t say nay to it, nor either of us, because we did not think of it—only loved him.", "난 거기에 토 달지 않아, 우리 둘 다 그래, 우린 그걸 바라지도 않았으니까—그냥 그분을 사랑했을 뿐이지."], ["Still, nobody else is to marry’n in the world—no fine lady, nobody in silks and satins; but she who do live like we.”", "그래도, 세상 어떤 여자도 그분이랑 결혼하지 못할 거야—어떤 고운 귀부인도, 비단이며 공단을 두른 누구도 아니고, 우리처럼 사는 쟤가 하는 거니까.\""]], [["“Are you sure you don’t dislike me for it?”", "\"너희 정말로 이 일로 나를 미워하지 않는 거 맞아?\""], ["said Tess in a low voice.", "테스가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["They hung about her in their white nightgowns before replying, as if they considered their answer might lie in her look.", "그들은 대답하기 전에 하얀 잠옷 차림으로 그녀 주위를 맴돌았다. 마치 그 대답이 그녀의 표정 속에 들어 있기라도 한 듯이."]], [["“I don’t know—I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠어—모르겠어.\""], ["murmured Retty Priddle.", "레티 프리들이 중얼거렸다."], ["“I want to hate ’ee; but I cannot!”", "\"널 미워하고 싶은데, 그게 안 돼!\""]], [["“That’s how I feel,”", "\"나도 그래.\""], ["echoed Izz and Marian.", "이즈와 매리언이 맞장구쳤다."], ["“I can’t hate her.", "\"쟤를 미워할 수가 없어."], ["Somehow she hinders me!”", "어쩐지 쟤가 나를 막아.\""]], [["“He ought to marry one of you,”", "\"그분은 너희 중 누군가와 결혼했어야 해.\""], ["murmured Tess.", "테스가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“You are all better than I.”", "\"너희가 다 나보다 나으니까.\""]], [["“We better than you?”", "\"우리가 너보다 낫다고?\""], ["said the girls in a low, slow whisper. “No, no, dear Tess!”", "처녀들이 낮고 느릿한 속삭임으로 말했다. \"아니야, 아니야, 사랑하는 테스!\""]], [["“You are!” she contradicted impetuously.", "\"너희가 그래!\" 그녀가 격하게 반박했다."], ["And suddenly tearing away from their clinging arms she burst into a hysterical fit of tears, bowing herself on the chest of drawers and repeating incessantly, “O yes, yes, yes!”", "그러고는 갑자기 매달린 그들의 팔을 뿌리치며 발작하듯 눈물을 터뜨렸다. 서랍장에 몸을 기댄 채 끊임없이 되뇌면서. \"오 그래, 그래, 그래!\""]], [["Having once given way she could not stop her weeping.", "한번 무너지자 그녀는 울음을 멈출 수 없었다."]], [["“He ought to have had one of you!”", "\"그분은 너희 중 하나를 얻었어야 했어!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“I think I ought to make him even now!", "\"지금이라도 내가 그렇게 만들어야 한다는 생각이 들어!"], ["You would be better for him than—I don’t know what I’m saying! O! O!”", "너희가 나보다 그분에게 나을 거야—내가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠어! 오! 오!\""]], [["They went up to her and clasped her round, but still her sobs tore her.", "그들이 그녀에게 다가가 끌어안았으나, 여전히 흐느낌이 그녀를 찢어 놓았다."]], [["“Get some water,”", "\"물 좀 가져와.\""], ["said Marian, “She’s upset by us, poor thing, poor thing!”", "매리언이 말했다. \"우리 때문에 마음이 상한 거야, 가엾은 것, 가엾은 것!\""]], [["They gently led her back to the side of her bed, where they kissed her warmly.", "그들은 그녀를 부드럽게 침대 곁으로 도로 데려가, 거기서 따뜻하게 입 맞추었다."]], [["“You are best for’n,”", "\"네가 그분한테 가장 어울려.\""], ["said Marian.", "매리언이 말했다."], ["“More ladylike, and a better scholar than we, especially since he had taught ’ee so much.", "\"더 귀부인 같고, 우리보다 더 많이 배웠지, 특히 그분이 너한테 그토록 많은 걸 가르쳐 준 뒤로는."], ["But even you ought to be proud.", "하지만 너라도 자랑스러워해야 해."], ["You _be_ proud, I’m sure!”", "너 자랑스럽지, 분명히!\""]], [["“Yes, I am,”", "\"응, 자랑스러워.\""], ["she said; “and I am ashamed at so breaking down.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 이렇게 무너져 버려서 부끄러워.\""]], [["When they were all in bed, and the light was out, Marian whispered across to her—", "다들 침대에 누워 불이 꺼졌을 때, 매리언이 그녀 쪽으로 속삭여 건넸다—"]], [["“You will think of us when you be his wife, Tess, and of how we told ’ee that we loved him, and how we tried not to hate you, and did not hate you, and could not hate you, because you were his choice, and we never hoped to be chose by him.”", "\"네가 그분 아내가 되면 우릴 생각해 줘, 테스. 우리가 너한테 그분을 사랑했다고 말한 것도, 너를 미워하지 않으려 애썼고, 미워하지 않았고, 미워할 수도 없었다는 것도. 네가 그분의 선택이었고, 우린 그분에게 뽑히길 바란 적도 없었으니까.\""]], [["They were not aware that, at these words, salt, stinging tears trickled down upon Tess’s pillow anew, and how she resolved, with a bursting heart, to tell all her history to Angel Clare, despite her mother’s command—to let him for whom she lived and breathed despise her if he would, and her mother regard her as a fool, rather then preserve a silence which might be deemed a treachery to him, and which somehow seemed a wrong to these.", "이 말에, 짜고 따가운 눈물이 다시 테스의 베개 위로 흘러내렸다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 터질 듯한 가슴으로, 어머니의 명령에도 불구하고 에인절 클레어에게 자기 내력을 죄다 털어놓기로 마음먹었다는 것을 그들은 알지 못했다—자기가 그를 위해 살고 숨 쉬는 그 사람으로 하여금, 원한다면 자기를 경멸하게 두고, 어머니로 하여금 자기를 바보로 여기게 두는 한이 있더라도, 그에 대한 배신으로 여겨질지 모를, 또 어쩐지 이 처녀들에게 잘못을 저지르는 것만 같은 침묵을 지키느니 그러는 편이 낫다고."]], [["# XXXII", "# XXXII"]], [["This penitential mood kept her from naming the wedding-day.", "이 뉘우치는 마음 때문에 그녀는 결혼 날짜를 입에 올리지 못했다."], ["The beginning of November found its date still in abeyance, though he asked her at the most tempting times.", "십일월이 시작될 때까지도 그 날짜는 여전히 미루어진 채였다. 그가 가장 마음 동할 만한 순간들에 물었는데도 그러했다."], ["But Tess’s desire seemed to be for a perpetual betrothal in which everything should remain as it was then.", "그러나 테스의 바람은, 모든 것이 그때 그대로 머물러 있는 영원한 약혼 상태를 향한 것 같았다."]], [["The meads were changing now; but it was still warm enough in early afternoons before milking to idle there awhile, and the state of dairy-work at this time of year allowed a spare hour for idling.", "이제 목초지는 변해 가고 있었다. 그러나 젖 짜기 전 이른 오후에는 거기서 한동안 빈둥거릴 만큼 여전히 따뜻했고, 이 무렵 낙농 일의 형편도 빈둥거릴 여유 시간을 한 시간쯤은 허락해 주었다."], ["Looking over the damp sod in the direction of the sun, a glistening ripple of gossamer webs was visible to their eyes under the luminary, like the track of moonlight on the sea.", "해 쪽으로 축축한 잔디밭을 건너다보면, 광원체 아래로 반짝이는 잔물결 같은 거미줄이 그들의 눈에 보였다. 마치 바다 위 달빛이 지나간 자취 같았다."], ["Gnats, knowing nothing of their brief glorification, wandered across the shimmer of this pathway, irradiated as if they bore fire within them, then passed out of its line, and were quite extinct.", "자신들의 그 짧은 영광을 까맣게 모르는 각다귀들이 이 길의 어른거리는 빛을 가로질러 거닐다가, 마치 몸속에 불을 품기라도 한 듯 환히 빛나더니, 이내 그 빛줄기 밖으로 벗어나 아주 꺼져 버렸다."], ["In the presence of these things he would remind her that the date was still the question.", "이런 것들을 앞에 두고 그는 날짜가 여전히 미결의 문제라는 것을 그녀에게 일깨우곤 했다."]], [["Or he would ask her at night, when he accompanied her on some mission invented by Mrs Crick to give him the opportunity.", "아니면 밤에, 크릭 부인이 그에게 기회를 주려고 꾸며 낸 어떤 심부름에 그가 그녀를 따라나섰을 때 묻기도 했다."], ["This was mostly a journey to the farmhouse on the slopes above the vale, to inquire how the advanced cows were getting on in the straw-barton to which they were relegated.", "그것은 대개 골짜기 위쪽 비탈에 있는 농가까지 가서, 따로 떼어 놓은 짚 외양간에서 새끼 밸 때가 다 된 소들이 어떻게 지내는지 알아보는 일이었다."], ["For it was a time of the year that brought great changes to the world of kine.", "한 해 가운데 소들의 세계에 큰 변화가 닥치는 철이었기 때문이다."], ["Batches of the animals were sent away daily to this lying-in hospital, where they lived on straw till their calves were born, after which event, and as soon as the calf could walk, mother and offspring were driven back to the dairy.", "짐승들이 날마다 무리 지어 이 산실 같은 곳으로 보내졌고, 거기서 새끼를 낳을 때까지 짚을 먹으며 지냈다. 그 일이 있은 뒤, 송아지가 걸을 수 있게 되자마자, 어미와 새끼는 낙농장으로 도로 몰려왔다."], ["In the interval which elapsed before the calves were sold there was, of course, little milking to be done, but as soon as the calf had been taken away the milkmaids would have to set to work as usual.", "송아지가 팔리기 전까지의 그 사이 기간에는 물론 짤 젖이 거의 없었지만, 송아지가 떼어지자마자 우유 짜는 처녀들은 다시 평소처럼 일에 매달려야 했다."]], [["Returning from one of these dark walks they reached a great gravel-cliff immediately over the levels, where they stood still and listened.", "이 어두운 산책 가운데 하나에서 돌아오던 길에 두 사람은 평지 바로 위로 솟은 커다란 자갈 벼랑에 이르렀고, 거기 가만히 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다."], ["The water was now high in the streams, squirting through the weirs, and tinkling under culverts; the smallest gullies were all full; there was no taking short cuts anywhere, and foot-passengers were compelled to follow the permanent ways.", "이제 개울마다 물이 불어 둑으로 콸콸 솟구치고 암거 밑에서 졸졸 울렸다. 자그마한 도랑까지 죄다 가득 차서, 어디로도 지름길을 낼 수 없었고, 걷는 이들은 늘 다니는 길을 따라갈 수밖에 없었다."], ["From the whole extent of the invisible vale came a multitudinous intonation; it forced upon their fancy that a great city lay below them, and that the murmur was the vociferation of its populace.", "보이지 않는 골짜기 전역에서 무수히 뒤섞인 울림이 밀려왔다. 그것은 그들의 상상에, 발아래 거대한 도시가 펼쳐져 있고 그 웅성거림이 도시 백성의 아우성이라는 느낌을 들이밀었다."]], [["“It seems like tens of thousands of them,”", "\"꼭 수만 명 같아요.\" 테스가 말했다."], ["said Tess; “holding public-meetings in their market-places, arguing, preaching, quarrelling, sobbing, groaning, praying, and cursing.”", "\"장터에서 군중집회를 열어, 입씨름하고, 설교하고, 다투고, 흐느끼고, 신음하고, 기도하고, 저주하는 거예요.\""]], [["Clare was not particularly heeding.", "클레어는 딱히 귀 기울이고 있지 않았다."]], [["“Did Crick speak to you to-day, dear, about his not wanting much assistance during the winter months?”", "\"오늘 크릭이 당신한테 말했소, 여보? 겨울 동안엔 일손이 많이 필요하지 않다는 거 말이오.\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아뇨.\""]], [["“The cows are going dry rapidly.”", "\"소들이 빠르게 젖이 마르고 있소.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Six or seven went to the straw-barton yesterday, and three the day before, making nearly twenty in the straw already.", "어제 여섯인가 일곱 마리가 짚 외양간으로 갔고, 그저께는 세 마리가 가서, 이미 스무 마리 가까이 짚 외양간에 있어요."], ["Ah—is it that the farmer don’t want my help for the calving?", "아—그게, 송아지 받는 데에 농장주가 제 도움을 원치 않는다는 건가요?"], ["O, I am not wanted here any more! And I have tried so hard to—”", "오, 저는 여기서 더는 필요 없는 거네요! 저는 그토록 애썼는데—\""]], [["“Crick didn’t exactly say that he would no longer require you.", "\"크릭이 더는 당신이 필요 없다고 딱 잘라 말한 건 아니오."], ["But, knowing what our relations were, he said in the most good-natured and respectful manner possible that he supposed on my leaving at Christmas I should take you with me, and on my asking what he would do without you he merely observed that, as a matter of fact, it was a time of year when he could do with a very little female help.", "다만, 우리 사이가 어떤지 알고 있으니, 더없이 호의적이고 정중한 태도로 이렇게 말하더군. 내가 성탄절에 떠날 때 당신도 데려갈 줄로 알고 있다고. 그래서 내가 당신 없이 어쩔 셈이냐고 물었더니, 그저 사실 이맘때면 여자 일손은 아주 조금만 있어도 된다고만 하더군."], ["I am afraid I was sinner enough to feel rather glad that he was in this way forcing your hand.”", "그가 이런 식으로 당신을 몰아붙여 주는 게 도리어 좀 반가웠으니, 나도 어지간히 죄인인 셈이오.\""]], [["“I don’t think you ought to have felt glad, Angel.", "\"반가워하실 일이 아니었던 것 같은데요, 에인절."], ["Because ’tis always mournful not to be wanted, even if at the same time ’tis convenient.”", "필요 없다는 건, 비록 그게 동시에 편리한 일이라도, 언제나 서글픈 거니까요.\""]], [["“Well, it is convenient—you have admitted that.”", "\"음, 그건 편리하지—당신도 그건 인정했잖소.\""], ["He put his finger upon her cheek. “Ah!” he said.", "그가 그녀의 뺨에 손가락을 댔다. \"아!\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“What?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“I feel the red rising up at her having been caught!", "\"들킨 게 부끄러워서 그녀의 뺨에 붉은빛이 올라오는 게 느껴지는걸!"], ["But why should I trifle so!", "하지만 내가 왜 이렇게 시시덕거리지!"], ["We will not trifle—life is too serious.”", "우린 시시덕거리지 맙시다—삶은 너무 진지하니까.\""]], [["“It is.", "\"그래요."], ["Perhaps I saw that before you did.”", "어쩌면 당신보다 제가 그걸 먼저 봤는지도 몰라요.\""]], [["She was seeing it then.", "그녀는 그때 그것을 보고 있었다."], ["To decline to marry him after all—in obedience to her emotion of last night—and leave the dairy, meant to go to some strange place, not a dairy; for milkmaids were not in request now calving-time was coming on; to go to some arable farm where no divine being like Angel Clare was.", "어젯밤의 그 감정에 따라—결국 그와 결혼하기를 거절하고 낙농장을 떠난다는 것은, 낙농장이 아닌 어떤 낯선 곳으로 간다는 뜻이었다. 송아지 받는 철이 다가오는 지금 우유 짜는 처녀는 찾는 데가 없었으니까. 에인절 클레어 같은 신과 같은 존재라곤 없는 어떤 경작 농장으로 간다는 뜻이었다."], ["She hated the thought, and she hated more the thought of going home.", "그녀는 그 생각이 싫었고, 집으로 돌아간다는 생각은 더더욱 싫었다."]], [["“So that, seriously, dearest Tess,”", "\"그러니 진지하게 말해서, 가장 사랑하는 테스,\""], ["he continued, “since you will probably have to leave at Christmas, it is in every way desirable and convenient that I should carry you off then as my property.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"당신이 아마 성탄절에 떠나야 할 테니, 그때 내가 당신을 내 것으로 데려가는 게 여러모로 바람직하고 편리한 일이오."], ["Besides, if you were not the most uncalculating girl in the world you would know that we could not go on like this for ever.”", "게다가, 당신이 세상에서 가장 셈할 줄 모르는 처녀가 아니라면, 우리가 이렇게 언제까지나 지낼 수는 없다는 걸 알 거요.\""]], [["“I wish we could. That it would always be summer and autumn, and you always courting me, and always thinking as much of me as you have done through the past summer-time!”", "\"저는 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 늘 여름이고 가을이고, 당신은 늘 제게 구애하고, 늘 지난 여름내 그래 주셨던 만큼 저를 많이 생각해 주신다면!\""]], [["“I always shall.”", "\"나는 늘 그럴 거요.\""]], [["“O, I know you will!” she cried, with a sudden fervour of faith in him.", "\"오, 당신이 그러시리란 걸 알아요!\" 그녀가 그를 향한 믿음에 갑자기 불타올라 외쳤다."], ["“Angel, I will fix the day when I will become yours for always!”", "\"에인절, 제가 영원히 당신 것이 될 그날을 정할게요!\""]], [["Thus at last it was arranged between them, during that dark walk home, amid the myriads of liquid voices on the right and left.", "그리하여 마침내 그 집으로 돌아오는 어두운 산책길에, 좌우의 무수한 물의 목소리들 가운데서, 두 사람 사이에 그 일이 정해졌다."]], [["When they reached the dairy Mr and Mrs Crick were promptly told—with injunctions of secrecy; for each of the lovers was desirous that the marriage should be kept as private as possible.", "낙농장에 다다르자 크릭 부부에게 곧바로 알렸다—비밀로 해 달라는 당부와 함께. 두 연인은 저마다 결혼을 되도록 조용히 치르고 싶어 했기 때문이다."], ["The dairyman, though he had thought of dismissing her soon, now made a great concern about losing her.", "주인은, 곧 그녀를 내보낼 생각을 하고 있었으면서도, 이제는 그녀를 잃는다고 야단스레 걱정했다."], ["What should he do about his skimming?", "거품 걷는 일은 어쩐단 말인가?"], ["Who would make the ornamental butter-pats for the Anglebury and Sandbourne ladies?", "앵글베리와 샌드본의 귀부인들에게 보낼 그 장식 버터 덩이는 누가 만든단 말인가?"], ["Mrs Crick congratulated Tess on the shilly-shallying having at last come to an end, and said that directly she set eyes on Tess she divined that she was to be the chosen one of somebody who was no common outdoor man; Tess had looked so superior as she walked across the barton on that afternoon of her arrival; that she was of a good family she could have sworn.", "크릭 부인은 그 우유부단함이 마침내 끝장난 것을 테스에게 축하해 주며, 테스를 처음 본 순간 그녀가 어떤 평범한 바깥일꾼이 아닌 누군가의 점지된 사람이 되리라는 걸 알아봤다고 말했다. 도착하던 그날 오후 외양간 마당을 가로질러 걸어오던 테스가 어찌나 기품 있어 보이던지, 좋은 집안 출신임을 능히 장담할 수 있었다는 것이다."], ["In point of fact Mrs Crick did remember thinking that Tess was graceful and good-looking as she approached; but the superiority might have been a growth of the imagination aided by subsequent knowledge.", "사실 크릭 부인은 테스가 다가올 때 우아하고 잘생겼다고 생각했던 것을 기억하긴 했지만, 그 기품이란 나중에 알게 된 사실이 거든 상상의 산물이었을지도 몰랐다."]], [["Tess was now carried along upon the wings of the hours, without the sense of a will.", "이제 테스는 의지의 감각도 없이 시간의 날개에 실려 떠밀려 갔다."], ["The word had been given; the number of the day written down.", "말은 떨어졌고, 그날의 날짜는 적혔다."], ["Her naturally bright intelligence had begun to admit the fatalistic convictions common to field-folk and those who associate more extensively with natural phenomena than with their fellow-creatures; and she accordingly drifted into that passive responsiveness to all things her lover suggested, characteristic of the frame of mind.", "본디 밝던 그녀의 지성도, 들에서 일하는 사람들과, 동료 인간보다 자연현상과 더 폭넓게 어울리는 이들에게 흔한 그 운명론적 확신을 받아들이기 시작했다. 그리하여 그녀는 그런 마음 상태 특유의, 연인이 권하는 모든 일에 수동적으로 순응하는 그 흐름 속으로 떠밀려 들어갔다."]], [["But she wrote anew to her mother, ostensibly to notify the wedding-day; really to again implore her advice.", "그러나 그녀는 어머니에게 다시 편지를 썼다. 표면상으로는 결혼 날짜를 알리기 위해서였으나, 실은 다시 한번 조언을 간청하기 위해서였다."], ["It was a gentleman who had chosen her, which perhaps her mother had not sufficiently considered.", "그녀를 택한 사람은 신사였고, 그 점을 어쩌면 어머니가 충분히 헤아리지 못했는지도 몰랐다."], ["A post-nuptial explanation, which might be accepted with a light heart by a rougher man, might not be received with the same feeling by him.", "결혼 뒤의 해명이라면, 더 거친 남자라면 가벼운 마음으로 받아들일지 몰라도, 그는 같은 마음으로 받아들이지 않을 수도 있었다."], ["But this communication brought no reply from Mrs Durbeyfield.", "그러나 이 편지에는 더비필드 부인에게서 아무 답장도 오지 않았다."]], [["Despite Angel Clare’s plausible representation to himself and to Tess of the practical need for their immediate marriage, there was in truth an element of precipitancy in the step, as became apparent at a later date.", "당장 결혼해야 할 현실적 필요가 있다고 에인절 클레어가 자기 자신에게도 테스에게도 그럴듯하게 내세웠음에도, 사실 그 발걸음에는 성급한 구석이 있었다. 훗날 가서 그것이 드러난다."], ["He loved her dearly, though perhaps rather ideally and fancifully than with the impassioned thoroughness of her feeling for him.", "그는 그녀를 끔찍이 사랑했으나, 어쩌면 그를 향한 그녀의 감정의 그 격정적인 철저함에 비하면, 다소 이상적이고 공상적인 사랑이었다."], ["He had entertained no notion, when doomed as he had thought to an unintellectual bucolic life, that such charms as he beheld in this idyllic creature would be found behind the scenes.", "멋없는 시골 생활에 묶일 운명이라고 여겼을 때만 해도, 그는 이 목가적인 존재에게서 본 그런 매력이 그 무대 뒤편에서 발견되리라고는 생각조차 못 했었다."], ["Unsophistication was a thing to talk of; but he had not known how it really struck one until he came here.", "순박함이란 입에 올려 말할 거리였으나, 그것이 사람에게 실제로 어떻게 와닿는지는 여기 오기 전까지 몰랐던 것이다."], ["Yet he was very far from seeing his future track clearly, and it might be a year or two before he would be able to consider himself fairly started in life.", "그러면서도 그는 자기 앞날의 행로를 또렷이 내다보는 것과는 거리가 멀었고, 자기가 인생에서 제대로 출발했다고 여길 수 있기까지는 한두 해가 걸릴지도 몰랐다."], ["The secret lay in the tinge of recklessness imparted to his career and character by the sense that he had been made to miss his true destiny through the prejudices of his family.", "그 비밀은, 자기가 집안의 편견 탓에 참된 운명을 놓치게 됐다는 느낌이 그의 행로와 성격에 입힌 그 무모함의 빛깔에 있었다."]], [["“Don’t you think ’twould have been better for us to wait till you were quite settled in your midland farm?” she once asked timidly.", "\"당신이 그 중부 지방 농장에 완전히 자리 잡을 때까지 우리가 기다리는 편이 낫지 않을까요?\" 그녀가 한번은 머뭇거리며 물었다."], ["(A midland farm was the idea just then.)", "(그때는 중부 지방 농장이라는 생각이었다.)"]], [["“To tell the truth, my Tess, I don’t like you to be left anywhere away from my protection and sympathy.”", "\"솔직히 말하면, 내 테스, 나는 당신을 내 보호와 따뜻한 마음에서 떨어진 어디에도 두고 싶지 않소.\""]], [["The reason was a good one, so far as it went.", "그 이유는, 그것대로는, 그럴듯한 것이었다."], ["His influence over her had been so marked that she had caught his manner and habits, his speech and phrases, his likings and his aversions.", "그가 그녀에게 끼친 영향이 어찌나 뚜렷했던지, 그녀는 그의 태도와 습관, 그의 말투와 표현, 그의 좋아하는 것과 싫어하는 것까지 물려받아 있었다."], ["And to leave her in farmland would be to let her slip back again out of accord with him.", "그러니 그녀를 농지에 남겨 둔다는 것은 그녀를 다시 그와 어긋나는 쪽으로 도로 미끄러지게 두는 일이 될 터였다."], ["He wished to have her under his charge for another reason.", "그가 그녀를 자기 보살핌 아래 두고 싶어 한 데에는 또 다른 이유가 있었다."], ["His parents had naturally desired to see her once at least before he carried her off to a distant settlement, English or colonial; and as no opinion of theirs was to be allowed to change his intention, he judged that a couple of months’ life with him in lodgings whilst seeking for an advantageous opening would be of some social assistance to her at what she might feel to be a trying ordeal—her presentation to his mother at the Vicarage.", "그의 부모는 당연히, 그가 그녀를 영국이든 식민지든 먼 정착지로 데려가기 전에 적어도 한 번은 그녀를 보고 싶어 했다. 그리고 그들의 어떤 의견도 그의 뜻을 바꾸도록 허용되지 않을 것이었으므로, 그는 유리한 자리를 알아보는 동안 두어 달쯤 셋방에서 그와 함께 사는 것이, 그녀가 견디기 힘든 시련으로 느낄지 모를 일—목사관에서 그의 어머니에게 인사드리는 일—에 어느 정도 사교적 도움이 되리라고 판단했다."]], [["Next, he wished to see a little of the working of a flour-mill, having an idea that he might combine the use of one with corn-growing.", "다음으로, 그는 제분소가 돌아가는 모습을 조금 보고 싶었다. 제분소 하나를 곡물 재배와 결합해 쓸 수도 있겠다는 생각이 있었기 때문이다."], ["The proprietor of a large old water-mill at Wellbridge—once the mill of an Abbey—had offered him the inspection of his time-honoured mode of procedure, and a hand in the operations for a few days, whenever he should choose to come.", "웰브리지에 있는 크고 오래된 물방앗간의 주인이—한때 수도원의 방앗간이었던 곳인데—언제든 그가 오고 싶을 때 그 유서 깊은 작업 방식을 둘러보고 며칠간 그 일에 손을 보태게 해 주겠다고 제안한 터였다."], ["Clare paid a visit to the place, some few miles distant, one day at this time, to inquire particulars, and returned to Talbothays in the evening.", "클레어는 이 무렵 어느 날, 몇 마일 떨어진 그곳을 찾아가 자세한 사정을 알아보고, 저녁에 탤보세이스로 돌아왔다."], ["She found him determined to spend a short time at the Wellbridge flour-mills.", "그녀는 그가 웰브리지 제분소에서 한동안 지내기로 마음먹은 것을 알게 되었다."], ["And what had determined him? Less the opportunity of an insight into grinding and bolting than the casual fact that lodgings were to be obtained in that very farmhouse which, before its mutilation, had been the mansion of a branch of the d’Urberville family.", "그런데 그의 마음을 그렇게 정하게 한 것은 무엇이었던가? 빻고 체질하는 과정을 들여다볼 기회라기보다는, 다름 아니라 바로 그 농가에서—훼손되기 전에는 더버빌 가의 한 갈래의 저택이었던 그 농가에서—셋방을 얻을 수 있다는 우연한 사실이었다."], ["This was always how Clare settled practical questions; by a sentiment which had nothing to do with them.", "클레어가 현실적인 문제를 매듭짓는 방식은 늘 이런 식이었다. 그 문제와는 아무 상관도 없는 어떤 정서에 기대어."], ["They decided to go immediately after the wedding, and remain for a fortnight, instead of journeying to towns and inns.", "두 사람은 도시와 여관을 찾아 여행하는 대신, 결혼한 직후 곧장 그리로 가서 보름간 머물기로 정했다."]], [["“Then we will start off to examine some farms on the other side of London that I have heard of,”", "\"그런 다음 우리는 내가 들은, 런던 건너편에 있는 농장 몇 군데를 살펴보러 나설 거요.\""], ["he said, “and by March or April we will pay a visit to my father and mother.”", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 삼월이나 사월쯤엔 우리 아버지와 어머니를 찾아뵙는 거지.\""]], [["Questions of procedure such as these arose and passed, and the day, the incredible day, on which she was to become his, loomed large in the near future.", "이런 절차에 관한 문제들이 떠올랐다 지나갔고, 그녀가 그의 것이 될 그날, 그 믿기지 않는 날이 가까운 미래에 크게 어른거렸다."], ["The thirty-first of December, New Year’s Eve, was the date. His wife, she said to herself.", "십이월 삼십일일, 섣달그믐이 그 날짜였다. 그의 아내라니, 그녀는 속으로 되뇌었다."], ["Could it ever be?", "정말 그럴 수 있을까?"], ["Their two selves together, nothing to divide them, every incident shared by them; why not? And yet why?", "두 사람이 함께, 가를 것이라곤 아무것도 없이, 모든 일을 둘이 나누다니, 안 될 게 뭐람? 그런데 그래도 왜?"]], [["One Sunday morning Izz Huett returned from church, and spoke privately to Tess.", "어느 일요일 아침 이즈 휴엣이 교회에서 돌아와 테스에게 따로 말을 건넸다."]], [["“You was not called home this morning.”", "\"오늘 아침 네 결혼 공고가 안 났더라.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""]], [["“It should ha’ been the first time of asking to-day,”", "\"오늘이 첫 번째 공고가 났어야 하는 날이잖아.\""], ["she answered, looking quietly at Tess.", "그녀가 테스를 가만히 바라보며 대답했다."], ["“You meant to be married New Year’s Eve, deary?”", "\"섣달그믐에 결혼할 생각이지, 얘?\""]], [["The other returned a quick affirmative.", "테스는 얼른 그렇다고 답했다."]], [["“And there must be three times of asking. And now there be only two Sundays left between.”", "\"공고는 세 번 나야 해. 그런데 이제 그사이에 일요일이 두 번밖에 안 남았어.\""]], [["Tess felt her cheek paling; Izz was right; of course there must be three.", "테스는 자기 뺨이 핼쑥해지는 것을 느꼈다. 이즈의 말이 맞았다."], ["Perhaps he had forgotten!", "당연히 세 번 나야 했다."], ["If so, there must be a week’s postponement, and that was unlucky.", "어쩌면 그가 잊어버렸나 보다! 그렇다면 일주일을 미뤄야 하는데, 그건 불길했다."], ["How could she remind her lover?", "어떻게 연인에게 일깨워 준담?"], ["She who had been so backward was suddenly fired with impatience and alarm lest she should lose her dear prize.", "그토록 머뭇거리기만 하던 그녀가, 갑자기 그 소중한 상을 놓칠까 봐 조바심과 불안에 불타올랐다."]], [["A natural incident relieved her anxiety.", "자연스러운 사건 하나가 그녀의 근심을 덜어 주었다."], ["Izz mentioned the omission of the banns to Mrs Crick, and Mrs Crick assumed a matron’s privilege of speaking to Angel on the point.", "이즈가 결혼 공고가 빠진 일을 크릭 부인에게 말했고, 크릭 부인은 안주인의 권한이랍시고 그 점에 대해 에인절에게 말했다."]], [["“Have ye forgot ’em, Mr Clare?", "\"그걸 잊으신 거예요, 클레어 씨?"], ["The banns, I mean.”", "결혼 공고 말이에요.\""]], [["“No, I have not forgot ’em,” says Clare.", "\"아뇨, 잊지 않았습니다.\" 클레어가 말했다."]], [["As soon as he caught Tess alone he assured her:", "그는 테스와 단둘이 있게 되자마자 그녀를 안심시켰다."]], [["“Don’t let them tease you about the banns.", "\"공고 일로 저들이 당신을 놀려도 신경 쓰지 마요."], ["A licence will be quieter for us, and I have decided on a licence without consulting you.", "우리한테는 혼인 허가증이 더 조용할 거요. 그래서 당신과 상의하지 않고 허가증으로 정했소."], ["So if you go to church on Sunday morning you will not hear your own name, if you wished to.”", "그러니 일요일 아침에 교회에 가더라도, 듣고 싶었다 한들, 당신 이름은 못 들을 거요.\""]], [["“I didn’t wish to hear it, dearest,”", "\"전 듣고 싶지 않았어요, 가장 사랑하는 사람.\""], ["she said proudly.", "그녀가 자랑스레 말했다."]], [["But to know that things were in train was an immense relief to Tess notwithstanding, who had well-nigh feared that somebody would stand up and forbid the banns on the ground of her history.", "그래도 일이 착착 진행되고 있다는 것을 안 것은 테스에게 엄청난 안도였다. 그녀는 누군가가 자기 내력을 이유로 일어나 공고를 막아서지나 않을까 줄곧 적잖이 두려워했던 것이다."], ["How events were favouring her!", "사정이 어찌나 그녀에게 유리하게 돌아가는지!"]], [["“I don’t quite feel easy,”", "\"영 마음이 놓이질 않아.\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀가 속으로 말했다."], ["“All this good fortune may be scourged out of me afterwards by a lot of ill.", "\"이 모든 행운이 나중에 한 무더기 불운으로 다 매질당해 쫓겨날지도 몰라."], ["That’s how Heaven mostly does.", "하늘이란 으레 그러는 법이니까."], ["I wish I could have had common banns!”", "차라리 보통 공고로 했으면 좋았을걸!\""]], [["But everything went smoothly.", "그러나 모든 것이 매끄럽게 흘러갔다."], ["She wondered whether he would like her to be married in her present best white frock, or if she ought to buy a new one.", "그녀는 지금 가진 가장 좋은 흰 드레스를 입고 결혼하기를 그가 바랄지, 아니면 새것을 사야 할지 궁금했다."], ["The question was set at rest by his forethought, disclosed by the arrival of some large packages addressed to her.", "그 문제는 그의 앞서 생각해 주는 마음으로 풀렸으니, 그녀 앞으로 부쳐진 커다란 꾸러미 몇 개가 도착하면서 그것이 드러났다."], ["Inside them she found a whole stock of clothing, from bonnet to shoes, including a perfect morning costume, such as would well suit the simple wedding they planned.", "그 안에서 그녀는 보닛부터 신발까지 옷가지 한 벌을 통째로 찾아냈는데, 두 사람이 계획한 그 소박한 결혼식에 썩 어울릴 만한, 완벽한 아침 예복도 들어 있었다."], ["He entered the house shortly after the arrival of the packages, and heard her upstairs undoing them.", "그는 그 꾸러미들이 도착한 직후 집에 들어와, 그녀가 위층에서 그것들을 풀고 있는 소리를 들었다."]], [["A minute later she came down with a flush on her face and tears in her eyes.", "잠시 뒤 그녀가, 얼굴에는 홍조를, 눈에는 눈물을\n눈을."]], [["“How thoughtful you’ve been!”", "\"정말 세심하게도 마음을 써 주셨네요!\""], ["she murmured, her cheek upon his shoulder.", "그의 어깨에 뺨을 기댄 채 그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“Even to the gloves and handkerchief! My own love—how good, how kind!”", "\"장갑이며 손수건까지! 내 사랑—어쩜 이렇게 다정하고, 이렇게 친절하실까!\""]], [["“No, no, Tess; just an order to a tradeswoman in London—nothing more.”", "\"아니야, 아니야, 테스. 그저 런던의 어느 여자 상인에게 주문한 것뿐이야—그뿐이라고.\""], ["And to divert her from thinking too highly of him, he told her to go upstairs, and take her time, and see if it all fitted; and, if not, to get the village sempstress to make a few alterations.", "그리고 그녀가 자기를 너무 높이 보지 않도록, 그는 위층으로 올라가서 서두르지 말고 전부 잘 맞는지 살펴보라고, 만약 맞지 않거든 마을 바느질꾼에게 조금 손을 보게 하라고 일렀다."]], [["She did return upstairs, and put on the gown.", "그녀는 정말로 위층으로 올라가 가운을 입어 보았다."], ["Alone, she stood for a moment before the glass looking at the effect of her silk attire; and then there came into her head her mother’s ballad of the mystic robe—", "혼자가 되자 그녀는 잠시 거울 앞에 서서 비단옷이 자아내는 모습을 들여다보았다. 그러자 어머니가 부르던, 신비한 옷에 관한 발라드 한 구절이 머릿속에 떠올랐다—"]], [["That never would become that wife That had once done amiss,", "한 번이라도 그릇된 짓을 저지른 아내에게는 결코 어울리지 않으리라,"]], [["which Mrs Durbeyfield had used to sing to her as a child, so blithely and so archly, her foot on the cradle, which she rocked to the tune.", "더비필드 부인이 어린 그녀에게 그토록 명랑하게, 그토록 짓궂게 불러 주던 노래였다. 발은 요람에 얹은 채, 그 가락에 맞추어 요람을 흔들면서."], ["Suppose this robe should betray her by changing colour, as her robe had betrayed Queen Guinevere.", "만약 이 옷이, 기네비어 왕비의 옷이 왕비를 배신했던 것처럼, 색이 변하면서 자기를 배신해 버린다면."], ["Since she had been at the dairy she had not once thought of the lines till now.", "낙농장에 온 이래로 그녀는 지금까지 단 한 번도 그 구절을 떠올린 적이 없었다."]], [["# XXXIII", "# XXXIII"]], [["Angel felt that he would like to spend a day with her before the wedding, somewhere away from the dairy, as a last jaunt in her company while there were yet mere lover and mistress; a romantic day, in circumstances that would never be repeated; with that other and greater day beaming close ahead of them.", "엔젤은 결혼식 전에, 낙농장에서 멀리 떨어진 어딘가에서, 그녀와 하루를 보내고 싶었다. 아직 한낱 연인과 애인일 뿐인 동안에 누리는 마지막 나들이로서. 다시는 되풀이되지 않을 상황 속에서 펼쳐지는 낭만적인 하루, 게다가 그보다 더 크고 더 빛나는 또 하나의 날이 바로 코앞에 환히 다가와 있는 그런 하루를."], ["During the preceding week, therefore, he suggested making a few purchases in the nearest town, and they started together.", "그래서 그 전 주에 그는 가장 가까운 읍내에 가서 몇 가지를 사 오자고 제안했고, 둘은 함께 길을 나섰다."]], [["Clare’s life at the dairy had been that of a recluse in respect the world of his own class.", "낙농장에서의 클레어의 생활은, 자기 계급의 세계에 관한 한 은둔자나 다름없었다."], ["For months he had never gone near a town, and, requiring no vehicle, had never kept one, hiring the dairyman’s cob or gig if he rode or drove.", "여러 달 동안 그는 읍내 근처에도 가 본 적이 없었고, 탈것이 필요치 않았으니 한 대도 두지 않았으며, 말을 타거나 몰 일이 있으면 낙농장 주인의 조랑말이나 이륜마차를 빌렸다."], ["They went in the gig that day.", "그날 둘은 이륜마차를 타고 갔다."]], [["And then for the first time in their lives they shopped as partners in one concern.", "그러고는 둘이 생전 처음으로 한 살림의 동반자로서 함께 장을 보았다."], ["It was Christmas Eve, with its loads a holly and mistletoe, and the town was very full of strangers who had come in from all parts of the country on account of the day.", "크리스마스이브였고, 호랑가시나무와 겨우살이가 가득 실려 있었으며, 그날을 맞아 사방에서 몰려든 낯선 사람들로 읍내는 몹시 붐볐다."], ["Tess paid the penalty of walking about with happiness superadded to beauty on her countenance by being much stared at as she moved amid them on his arm.", "테스는 미모에 더하여 행복까지 얼굴에 얹은 채 돌아다닌 대가를 치렀으니, 그의 팔짱을 끼고 사람들 사이를 지나는 동안 숱한 시선을 받았던 것이다."]], [["In the evening they returned to the inn at which they had put up, and Tess waited in the entry while Angel went to see the horse and gig brought to the door.", "저녁이 되자 둘은 묵었던 여관으로 돌아왔고, 엔젤이 말과 이륜마차를 문 앞으로 끌어다 놓도록 보러 간 사이 테스는 입구에서 기다렸다."], ["The general sitting-room was full of guests, who were continually going in and out.", "공동 거실은 손님들로 가득했고, 그들은 끊임없이 드나들었다."], ["As the door opened and shut each time for the passage of these, the light within the parlour fell full upon Tess’s face.", "그 사람들이 지나갈 때마다 문이 열렸다 닫혔다 하면서, 안쪽 응접실의 불빛이 테스의 얼굴을 환히 비추었다."], ["Two men came out and passed by her among the rest.", "사내 둘이 다른 사람들 틈에 섞여 나오면서 그녀 곁을 지나갔다."], ["One of them had stared her up and down in surprise, and she fancied he was a Trantridge man, though that village lay so many miles off that Trantridge folk were rarities here.", "그중 하나가 놀란 듯 그녀를 위아래로 훑어보았는데, 그녀는 그가 트랜트리지 사람이 아닐까 싶었다. 비록 그 마을은 워낙 멀리 떨어져 있어 여기서 트랜트리지 사람을 보기란 드문 일이었지만."]], [["“A comely maid that,” said the other.", "\"참한 처녀로군.\" 다른 사내가 말했다."]], [["“True, comely enough.", "\"그래, 참하긴 하지."], ["But unless I make a great mistake—”", "하지만 내가 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면—\""], ["And he negatived the remainder of the definition forthwith.", "그러더니 그는 그 평가의 나머지 부분을 곧바로 부정해 버렸다."]], [["Clare had just returned from the stable-yard, and, confronting the man on the threshold, heard the words, and saw the shrinking of Tess.", "마구간 마당에서 막 돌아온 클레어는 문턱에서 그 사내와 맞닥뜨리며 그 말을 들었고, 테스가 움츠러드는 것을 보았다."], ["The insult to her stung him to the quick, and before he had considered anything at all he struck the man on the chin with the full force of his fist, sending him staggering backwards into the passage.", "그녀에게 가해진 모욕에 그는 가슴 깊은 곳까지 찔린 듯했고, 아무것도 미처 생각하기도 전에 주먹에 온 힘을 실어 사내의 턱을 후려쳐, 그를 비틀거리며 복도 안쪽으로 나가떨어지게 했다."]], [["The man recovered himself, and seemed inclined to come on, and Clare, stepping outside the door, put himself in a posture of defence.", "사내는 정신을 차리고 덤벼들 기색을 보였고, 클레어는 문밖으로 한 발 나서며 방어 자세를 취했다."], ["But his opponent began to think better of the matter.", "그러나 상대는 다시 생각을 고쳐먹기 시작했다."], ["He looked anew at Tess as he passed her, and said to Clare—", "그는 테스 곁을 지나며 다시 한번 그녀를 보더니, 클레어에게 말했다—"]], [["“I beg pardon, sir; ’twas a complete mistake.", "\"죄송합니다, 선생님. 완전히 사람을 잘못 봤습니다."], ["I thought she was another woman, forty miles from here.”", "여기서 사십 마일 떨어진 데 사는 다른 여자인 줄 알았지 뭡니까.\""]], [["Clare, feeling then that he had been too hasty, and that he was, moreover, to blame for leaving her standing in an inn-passage, did what he usually did in such cases, gave the man five shillings to plaster the blow; and thus they parted, bidding each other a pacific good night.", "클레어는 그제야 자기가 너무 성급했고, 게다가 그녀를 여관 복도에 세워 둔 것도 잘못이었다고 느끼고는, 이런 경우에 늘 하던 대로 했다. 사내에게 오 실링을 주어 얻어맞은 자리를 달래라 했고, 그렇게 둘은 서로 화평하게 밤 인사를 건네며 헤어졌다."], ["As soon as Clare had taken the reins from the ostler, and the young couple had driven off, the two men went in the other direction.", "클레어가 마부에게서 고삐를 넘겨받고 젊은 부부가 마차를 몰고 떠나자마자, 두 사내는 반대 방향으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“And was it a mistake?”", "\"그래, 진짜 잘못 본 거였나?\""], ["said the second one.", "두 번째 사내가 물었다."]], [["“Not a bit of it.", "\"전혀 아니지."], ["But I didn’t want to hurt the gentleman’s feelings—not I.”", "다만 저 신사 양반 기분을 상하게 하고 싶지 않았던 거야—나야 그렇지.\""]], [["In the meantime the lovers were driving onward.", "그러는 사이 연인들은 마차를 몰아 앞으로 나아가고 있었다."]], [["“Could we put off our wedding till a little later?”", "\"우리 결혼식을 조금 뒤로 미룰 수 있을까요?\""], ["Tess asked in a dry dull voice.", "테스가 메마르고 둔탁한 목소리로 물었다."], ["“I mean if we wished?”", "\"그러니까, 우리가 원한다면 말이에요?\""]], [["“No, my love. Calm yourself.", "\"안 돼, 내 사랑. 진정해."], ["Do you mean that the fellow may have time to summon me for assault?”", "그 작자가 나를 폭행죄로 고소할 틈이라도 줘야 한다는 말인가?\""], ["he asked good-humouredly.", "그가 유쾌하게 물었다."]], [["“No—I only meant—if it should have to be put off.”", "\"아니에요—그냥 말하고 싶었던 건—혹시 미뤄야 할 일이 생긴다면, 하는 거였어요.\""]], [["What she meant was not very clear, and he directed her to dismiss such fancies from her mind, which she obediently did as well as she could.", "그녀가 무슨 말을 하려는 것인지는 그리 분명치 않았고, 그는 그런 망상은 마음속에서 떨쳐 버리라고 일렀다. 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 고분고분 그렇게 했다. 그러나 집으로 돌아오는 내내 그녀는 침울했다, 몹시 침울했다."], ["But she was grave, very grave, all the way home; till she thought, “We shall go away, a very long distance, hundreds of miles from these parts, and such as this can never happen again, and no ghost of the past reach there.”", "그러다 이런 생각이 들었다. \"우리는 떠날 거야, 아주 먼 곳으로, 이곳에서 수백 마일 떨어진 데로. 그러면 이런 일은 다시는 일어날 수 없고, 과거의 어떤 유령도 거기까지는 닿지 못할 거야.\""]], [["They parted tenderly that night on the landing, and Clare ascended to his attic.", "그날 밤 둘은 층계참에서 다정하게 헤어졌고, 클레어는 자기 다락방으로 올라갔다."], ["Tess sat up getting on with some little requisites, lest the few remaining days should not afford sufficient time.", "테스는 자지 않고 앉아, 남은 며칠로는 시간이 모자랄까 봐 자잘한 준비물들을 마저 손보았다."], ["While she sat she heard a noise in Angel’s room overhead, a sound of thumping and struggling.", "그렇게 앉아 있는데 위층 엔젤의 방에서 소리가 들렸다, 쿵쿵거리며 몸부림치는 소리가."], ["Everybody else in the house was asleep, and in her anxiety lest Clare should be ill she ran up and knocked at his door, and asked him what was the matter.", "집안의 다른 사람들은 모두 잠들어 있었고, 클레어가 어디 아픈 게 아닐까 하는 걱정에 그녀는 뛰어 올라가 그의 방문을 두드리며 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing, dear,” he said from within.", "\"아, 아무것도 아니야, 여보.\" 안에서 그가 대답했다."], ["“I am so sorry I disturbed you!", "\"깨워서 정말 미안해! 그런데 까닭이 좀 우습거든."], ["But the reason is rather an amusing one: I fell asleep and dreamt that I was fighting that fellow again who insulted you, and the noise you heard was my pummelling away with my fists at my portmanteau, which I pulled out to-day for packing.", "잠이 들었다가 당신을 모욕한 그 작자랑 또 싸우는 꿈을 꿨지 뭐야. 당신이 들은 소리는 내가 짐 가방을 주먹으로 마구 두드린 거였어. 오늘 짐을 싸려고 꺼내 놓은 그 가방 말이야."], ["I am occasionally liable to these freaks in my sleep.", "나는 가끔 잠결에 이런 별난 짓을 한다고."], ["Go to bed and think of it no more.”", "가서 자고 더는 신경 쓰지 마.\""]], [["This was the last drachm required to turn the scale of her indecision.", "이것이 그녀의 망설임을 한쪽으로 기울게 하는 데 필요한 마지막 한 방울이었다."], ["Declare the past to him by word of mouth she could not; but there was another way.", "입으로 직접 과거를 그에게 밝히기란 도저히 할 수 없었다."], ["She sat down and wrote on the four pages of a note-sheet a succinct narrative of those events of three or four years ago, put it into an envelope, and directed it to Clare.", "그러나 다른 방법이 있었다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아 편지지 넉 장에 삼사 년 전의 그 일들을 간결하게 적어, 봉투에 넣고 클레어에게 보내는 것이라 적었다."], ["Then, lest the flesh should again be weak, she crept upstairs without any shoes and slipped the note under his door.", "그런 다음, 마음이 또 약해질까 봐 신발도 신지 않은 채 살금살금 위층으로 올라가 그 쪽지를 그의 방문 밑으로 밀어 넣었다."]], [["Her night was a broken one, as it well might be, and she listened for the first faint noise overhead.", "그날 밤 그녀의 잠은 끊기고 또 끊겼으니, 그럴 만도 했다. 그녀는 위층에서 들려올 첫 희미한 기척에 귀를 기울였다."], ["It came, as usual; he descended, as usual.", "기척은 여느 때처럼 들려왔고, 그는 여느 때처럼 내려왔다."], ["She descended.", "그녀도 내려갔다."], ["He met her at the bottom of the stairs and kissed her.", "그는 계단 아래서 그녀를 맞으며 입을 맞추었다."], ["Surely it was as warmly as ever!", "분명히 여느 때 못지않게 따뜻한 입맞춤이었다!"]], [["He looked a little disturbed and worn, she thought.", "그가 조금 동요하고 지쳐 보인다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["But he said not a word to her about her revelation, even when they were alone.", "그러나 그는 그녀의 고백에 관해 한마디도 하지 않았다, 둘만 있을 때조차도."], ["Could he have had it?", "그가 그걸 받아 보긴 한 걸까?"], ["Unless he began the subject she felt that she could say nothing.", "그가 먼저 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않는 한 자기로서는 아무 말도 할 수 없다고 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["So the day passed, and it was evident that whatever he thought he meant to keep to himself.", "그렇게 하루가 지났고, 그가 무슨 생각을 하든 그것을 혼자 간직하기로 마음먹은 것이 분명해 보였다."], ["Yet he was frank and affectionate as before.", "그러면서도 그는 전과 다름없이 솔직하고 다정했다."], ["Could it be that her doubts were childish?", "자기 의심이 어린아이 같은 것이었을까?"], ["that he forgave her; that he loved her for what she was, just as she was, and smiled at her disquiet as at a foolish nightmare?", "그가 자기를 용서한 걸까? 있는 그대로의 자기를, 바로 그 모습 그대로 사랑하면서, 자기의 불안을 어리석은 악몽쯤으로 여기며 빙긋 웃은 걸까?"], ["Had he really received her note?", "그가 정말로 쪽지를 받아 본 걸까?"], ["She glanced into his room, and could see nothing of it.", "그녀는 그의 방을 슬쩍 들여다보았지만 그것은 어디에도 보이지 않았다."], ["It might be that he forgave her.", "어쩌면 그가 자기를 용서한 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["But even if he had not received it she had a sudden enthusiastic trust that he surely would forgive her.", "그러나 설령 그가 그것을 받아 보지 못했다 해도, 그가 분명 자기를 용서해 주리라는 갑작스러운 열렬한 믿음이 그녀에게 솟아났다."]], [["Every morning and night he was the same, and thus New Year’s Eve broke—the wedding day.", "아침저녁으로 그는 한결같았고, 그렇게 섣달그믐날이 밝았다—결혼식 날이었다."]], [["The lovers did not rise at milking-time, having through the whole of this last week of their sojourn at the dairy been accorded something of the position of guests, Tess being honoured with a room of her own.", "연인들은 우유 짜는 시각에 일어나지 않았다. 낙농장에 머무는 이 마지막 한 주 내내 그들은 손님이나 다름없는 대접을 받았고, 테스는 자기만의 방을 쓰는 영예를 누렸다."], ["When they arrived downstairs at breakfast-time they were surprised to see what effects had been produced in the large kitchen for their glory since they had last beheld it.", "아침 식사 시간에 아래층으로 내려온 둘은, 마지막으로 본 이래로 그 큰 부엌에 자기들을 위해 어떤 변화가 일어나 있는지를 보고 놀랐다."], ["At some unnatural hour of the morning the dairyman had caused the yawning chimney-corner to be whitened, and the brick hearth reddened, and a blazing yellow damask blower to be hung across the arch in place of the old grimy blue cotton one with a black sprig pattern which had formerly done duty there.", "아침의 어느 터무니없는 시각에 낙농장 주인은 입을 떡 벌린 듯한 굴뚝 모퉁이를 하얗게 칠하게 하고, 벽돌 화덕을 붉게 칠하게 했으며, 예전에 그 자리를 지키던 검은 잔가지 무늬의 낡고 거무튀튀한 푸른 무명 가리개 대신, 활활 타오르는 듯한 노란 다마스크 가리개를 아치 위에 걸쳐 두게 했다."], ["This renovated aspect of what was the focus indeed of the room on a full winter morning threw a smiling demeanour over the whole apartment.", "한겨울 아침이면 그야말로 부엌의 중심이 되는 그 자리가 이렇게 새롭게 단장되니, 방 전체에 환한 표정이 드리워졌다."]], [["“I was determined to do summat in honour o’t”, said the dairyman.", "\"기념으로 뭐라도 좀 해야겠다고 작정했지요.\" 낙농장 주인이 말했다."], ["“And as you wouldn’t hear of my gieing a rattling good randy wi’ fiddles and bass-viols complete, as we should ha’ done in old times, this was all I could think o’ as a noiseless thing.”", "\"그런데 옛날 같으면 으레 그랬듯이 바이올린이며 베이스 비올까지 갖춘 떠들썩한 잔치를 벌이게 해 달라는데도 도무지 들으려 하질 않으시니, 소리 안 나는 일로 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 건 이게 다였습니다.\""]], [["Tess’s friends lived so far off that none could conveniently have been present at the ceremony, even had any been asked; but as a fact nobody was invited from Marlott.", "테스의 식구들은 워낙 멀리 떨어져 살아서, 설령 누구라도 청했다 한들 예식에 편히 참석할 만한 사람이 없었다. 사실 말럿에서는 아무도 초대받지 않았다."], ["As for Angel’s family, he had written and duly informed them of the time, and assured them that he would be glad to see one at least of them there for the day if he would like to come.", "엔젤의 가족으로 말하자면, 그는 편지를 써서 날짜를 제대로 알렸고, 적어도 그날 식구 중 한 명이라도 오고 싶다면 와 주면 반갑겠다고 다짐해 두었다."], ["His brothers had not replied at all, seeming to be indignant with him; while his father and mother had written a rather sad letter, deploring his precipitancy in rushing into marriage, but making the best of the matter by saying that, though a dairywoman was the last daughter-in-law they could have expected, their son had arrived at an age which he might be supposed to be the best judge.", "그의 형제들은 아예 답이 없었으니, 그에게 분개한 듯했다. 한편 부모는 다소 슬픈 편지를 보내, 그가 결혼으로 황급히 뛰어든 성급함을 한탄하면서도, 비록 낙농장 여인이 자기들이 바랄 수 있는 며느릿감으로는 가장 뜻밖이긴 하나 아들이 이제 스스로 가장 잘 판단할 만한 나이에 이르렀으리라는 말로 이 일을 좋게 받아들였다."]], [["This coolness in his relations distressed Clare less than it would have done had he been without the grand card with which he meant to surprise them ere long.", "이러한 가족의 냉랭함이 클레어를 괴롭힌 정도는, 머잖아 그들을 놀래 줄 작정으로 그가 쥐고 있던 그 멋진 으뜸 패가 없었더라면 받았을 괴로움보다 한결 덜했다."], ["To produce Tess, fresh from the dairy, as a d’Urberville and a lady, he had felt to be temerarious and risky; hence he had concealed her lineage till such time as, familiarized with worldly ways by a few months’ travel and reading with him, he could take her on a visit to his parents and impart the knowledge while triumphantly producing her as worthy of such an ancient line.", "낙농장에서 갓 데려온 테스를 더버빌가의 여인이요 숙녀로서 내보이는 일은 무모하고 위험하다고 그는 느꼈다. 그래서 그녀의 혈통을 감추어 두었던 것이니, 몇 달 동안 자기와 함께 여행하고 책을 읽으며 세상 물정에 익숙해진 뒤에야 그녀를 부모에게 데려가, 그 유서 깊은 가문에 걸맞은 여인임을 의기양양하게 내보이면서 그 사실을 알려 줄 작정이었다."], ["It was a pretty lover’s dream, if no more.", "그저 연인다운 어여쁜 꿈이긴 했다."], ["Perhaps Tess’s lineage had more value for himself than for anybody in the world beside.", "어쩌면 테스의 혈통은 세상 누구보다도 그 자신에게 더 값진 것이었는지도 모른다."]], [["Her perception that Angel’s bearing towards her still remained in no whit altered by her own communication rendered Tess guiltily doubtful if he could have received it.", "엔젤이 자기를 대하는 태도가 자기 고백으로 인해 조금도 달라지지 않았다는 것을 알아채자, 테스는 그가 정말 그것을 받아 보긴 한 걸까 하는 죄스러운 의심에 휩싸였다."], ["She rose from breakfast before he had finished, and hastened upstairs.", "그가 식사를 마치기도 전에 그녀는 식탁에서 일어나 황급히 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["It had occurred to her to look once more into the queer gaunt room which had been Clare’s den, or rather eyrie, for so long, and climbing the ladder she stood at the open door of the apartment, regarding and pondering.", "그토록 오랫동안 클레어의 굴, 아니 차라리 둥지였던 그 기이하고 황량한 방을 다시 한번 들여다봐야겠다는 생각이 들었던 것이다. 사다리를 올라가 그녀는 그 방의 열린 문 앞에 서서, 바라보며 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["She stooped to the threshold of the doorway, where she had pushed in the note two or three days earlier in such excitement.", "그녀는 며칠 전 그토록 흥분한 채 쪽지를 밀어 넣었던 문턱으로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["The carpet reached close to the sill, and under the edge of the carpet she discerned the faint white margin of the envelope containing her letter to him, which he obviously had never seen, owing to her having in her haste thrust it beneath the carpet as well as beneath the door.", "카펫이 문지방 바로 앞까지 깔려 있었는데, 그 카펫 가장자리 밑에서 그녀는 그에게 보내는 편지가 든 봉투의 희미한 흰 모서리를 알아보았다. 그가 분명 한 번도 보지 못한 그 편지를. 그녀가 다급한 나머지 문 밑으로뿐 아니라 카펫 밑으로까지 밀어 넣어 버린 탓이었다."]], [["With a feeling of faintness she withdrew the letter.", "아찔한 기분으로 그녀는 그 편지를 빼냈다."], ["There it was—sealed up, just as it had left her hands.", "봉인된 채 그대로였다, 그녀의 손을 떠났을 때 모습 그대로."], ["The mountain had not yet been removed.", "산은 아직 옮겨지지 않은 것이다."], ["She could not let him read it now, the house being in full bustle of preparation; and descending to her own room she destroyed the letter there.", "지금은 그에게 그것을 읽게 할 수 없었다. 집안이 온통 준비로 부산했으니. 그녀는 자기 방으로 내려가 거기서 편지를 없애 버렸다."]], [["She was so pale when he saw her again that he felt quite anxious.", "그가 그녀를 다시 보았을 때 그녀의 안색이 어찌나 창백한지 그는 꽤 걱정스러웠다."], ["The incident of the misplaced letter she had jumped at as if it prevented a confession; but she knew in her conscience that it need not; there was still time.", "그녀는 잘못 끼워진 편지의 사연을, 마치 그것이 고백을 막아 준 것이기라도 하다는 듯 덥석 붙잡았다. 그러나 양심으로는 그럴 필요가 없음을 알고 있었다. 아직 시간이 있었다."], ["Yet everything was in a stir; there was coming and going; all had to dress, the dairyman and Mrs Crick having been asked to accompany them as witnesses; and reflection or deliberate talk was well-nigh impossible.", "그러나 모든 것이 어수선했다. 오가는 사람이 끊이지 않았고, 모두가 옷을 차려입어야 했으며, 낙농장 주인과 크리크 부인은 증인으로 동행해 달라는 부탁을 받은 터였다. 그러니 차분히 생각하거나 마음먹고 이야기할 짬이라곤 거의 없었다."], ["The only minute Tess could get to be alone with Clare was when they met upon the landing.", "테스가 클레어와 단둘이 있을 수 있는 유일한 짬은 둘이 층계참에서 마주쳤을 때였다."]], [["“I am so anxious to talk to you—I want to confess all my faults and blunders!”", "\"당신한테 꼭 이야기하고 싶은 게 있어요—제 모든 잘못과 실수를 고백하고 싶어요!\""], ["she said with attempted lightness.", "그녀가 짐짓 가벼운 척하며 말했다."]], [["“No, no—we can’t have faults talked of—you must be deemed perfect to-day at least, my Sweet!”", "\"아니, 아니—잘못 이야기 같은 건 하면 안 돼—적어도 오늘만큼은 당신을 완벽한 사람으로 여겨야지, 내 사랑!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“We shall have plenty of time, hereafter, I hope, to talk over our failings.", "\"앞으로 우리 흠을 두고 이야기할 시간은 얼마든지 있을 거야, 그러길 바라."], ["I will confess mine at the same time.”", "그때 내 흠도 함께 고백하지.\""]], [["“But it would be better for me to do it now, I think, so that you could not say—”", "\"하지만 지금 하는 편이 나을 것 같아요, 그래야 나중에 당신이 이런 말을 못 할 테니까—\""]], [["“Well, my quixotic one, you shall tell me anything—say, as soon as we are settled in our lodging; not now.", "\"그래, 내 돈키호테 같은 사람아, 당신은 뭐든 내게 말해도 돼—자, 우리가 숙소에 자리 잡자마자 말이야, 지금 말고."], ["I, too, will tell you my faults then.", "그때 나도 내 흠을 당신에게 말하지."], ["But do not let us spoil the day with them; they will be excellent matter for a dull time.”", "하지만 그걸로 오늘을 망치진 말자. 그건 따분할 때 풀어놓기에 더없이 좋은 거리가 될 테니까.\""]], [["“Then you don’t wish me to, dearest?”", "\"그럼 지금은 제가 말하기를 바라지 않으시는 거죠, 여보?\""]], [["“I do not, Tessy, really.”", "\"바라지 않아, 테시, 정말로.\""]], [["The hurry of dressing and starting left no time for more than this.", "옷을 차려입고 출발하느라 부산한 통에 이 정도 이상의 이야기를 할 짬은 없었다."], ["Those words of his seemed to reassure her on further reflection.", "거듭 곱씹어 보니 그의 그 말이 그녀를 안심시켜 주는 듯했다."], ["She was whirled onward through the next couple of critical hours by the mastering tide of her devotion to him, which closed up further meditation.", "그를 향한 헌신의 압도적인 물결이 그녀를 휩쓸어 다음 두어 시간의 위태로운 고비를 그대로 밀고 나갔고, 그 물결이 더 깊은 사색의 통로를 막아 버렸다."], ["Her one desire, so long resisted, to make herself his, to call him her lord, her own—then, if necessary, to die—had at last lifted her up from her plodding reflective pathway.", "그토록 오래 억눌러 온 그녀의 단 하나의 소망—자기를 그의 것으로 만들고, 그를 자기 주인이라, 자기만의 사람이라 부르고, 그런 다음 필요하다면 죽어도 좋다는 그 소망이—마침내 그녀를 터덜터덜 걷던 사색의 오솔길에서 들어 올렸다."], ["In dressing, she moved about in a mental cloud of many-coloured idealities, which eclipsed all sinister contingencies by its brightness.", "옷을 차려입으면서 그녀는 갖가지 빛깔의 이상들로 이루어진 마음의 구름 속을 거닐었고, 그 구름의 환함은 온갖 불길한 가능성을 가려 버렸다."]], [["The church was a long way off, and they were obliged to drive, particularly as it was winter.", "교회는 멀리 떨어져 있었고, 더구나 겨울이라 마차를 타지 않을 수 없었다."], ["A closed carriage was ordered from a roadside inn, a vehicle which had been kept there ever since the old days of post-chaise travelling.", "길가 여관에서 사륜 포장마차를 한 대 불렀는데, 역마차로 여행하던 옛 시절부터 거기 보관되어 온 탈것이었다."], ["It had stout wheel-spokes and heavy felloes, a great curved bed, immense straps and springs, and a pole like a battering-ram. The postilion was a venerable “boy”", "굵직한 바큇살과 묵직한 바퀴 테, 크게 굽은 차체, 거대한 가죽끈과 용수철, 그리고 성벽을 부수는 망치 같은 끌채를 갖추고 있었다."], ["of sixty—a martyr to rheumatic gout, the result of excessive exposure in youth, counter-acted by strong liquors—who had stood at inn-doors doing nothing for the whole five-and-twenty years that had elapsed since he had no longer been required to ride professionally, as if expecting the old times to come back again.", "마부는 예순 살 먹은 점잖은 \"소년\"이었으니—젊어서 지나치게 비바람에 시달린 탓에, 독한 술로 그것을 가라앉히려다 류머티즘성 통풍에 시달리게 된 순교자였다—직업적으로 말을 몰 필요가 없어진 이래로 흘러간 스물다섯 해 내내, 마치 옛 시절이 다시 돌아오기를 기다리는 듯, 여관 문 앞에 서서 하릴없이 지냈다."], ["He had a permanent running wound on the outside of his right leg, originated by the constant bruisings of aristocratic carriage-poles during the many years that he had been in regular employ at the King’s Arms, Casterbridge.", "그의 오른쪽 다리 바깥쪽에는 늘 진물이 흐르는 상처가 있었는데, 캐스터브리지의 킹스암스 여관에서 정식으로 고용되어 일하던 여러 해 동안 귀족들 마차의 끌채에 끊임없이 부딪쳐 생긴 것이었다."]], [["Inside this cumbrous and creaking structure, and behind this decayed conductor, the _partie carrée_ took their seats—the bride and bridegroom and Mr and Mrs Crick.", "이 둔중하고 삐걱거리는 구조물 안에, 이 쇠락한 마부 뒤에, 네 사람의 일행이 자리를 잡았다—신부와 신랑, 그리고 크리크 부부였다."], ["Angel would have liked one at least of his brothers to be present as groomsman, but their silence after his gentle hint to that effect by letter had signified that they did not care to come.", "엔젤은 적어도 형제 중 하나라도 신랑 들러리로 와 주기를 바랐지만, 편지로 그 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤음에도 그들이 침묵을 지킨 것은 올 마음이 없다는 뜻이었다."], ["They disapproved of the marriage, and could not be expected to countenance it.", "그들은 이 결혼을 못마땅해했고, 그것을 두둔해 주리라 기대할 수도 없었다."], ["Perhaps it was as well that they could not be present.", "어쩌면 그들이 참석할 수 없는 편이 차라리 나았다."], ["They were not worldly young fellows, but fraternizing with dairy-folk would have struck unpleasantly upon their biased niceness, apart from their views of the match.", "그들은 세속에 물든 젊은이들은 아니었으나, 이 혼사에 대한 견해를 떠나서도, 낙농장 사람들과 어울리는 일은 편향된 그들의 까다로운 취향에 불쾌하게 거슬렸을 터였다."]], [["Upheld by the momentum of the time, Tess knew nothing of this, did not see anything, did not know the road they were taking to the church.", "그때의 추진력에 떠받쳐, 테스는 이 모든 것을 전혀 알지 못했고, 아무것도 보지 못했으며, 교회로 가는 길이 어떤 길인지도 몰랐다."], ["She knew that Angel was close to her; all the rest was a luminous mist.", "그녀가 아는 것은 엔젤이 자기 곁에 바짝 있다는 것뿐, 나머지는 모두 환한 안개였다."], ["She was a sort of celestial person, who owed her being to poetry—one of those classical divinities Clare was accustomed to talk to her about when they took their walks together.", "그녀는 일종의 천상의 존재, 시에서 비롯된 존재였다—클레어가 둘이 함께 산책할 때면 그녀에게 이야기해 주곤 하던 그 고전 속 여신들 가운데 하나였다."]], [["The marriage being by licence there were only a dozen or so of people in the church; had there been a thousand they would have produced no more effect upon her.", "혼인이 허가증에 의한 것이었으므로 교회 안에는 열 명 남짓의 사람밖에 없었다. 천 명이 있었다 한들 그녀에게는 더 이상의 영향을 미치지 못했을 것이다."], ["They were at stellar distances from her present world.", "그들은 지금 그녀의 세계로부터 별처럼 아득히 떨어져 있었다."], ["In the ecstatic solemnity with which she swore her faith to him the ordinary sensibilities of sex seemed a flippancy.", "그에게 신의를 맹세하던 그 황홀한 엄숙함 속에서, 보통의 남녀 간 감정 따위는 경박한 것으로만 여겨졌다."], ["At a pause in the service, while they were kneeling together, she unconsciously inclined herself towards him, so that her shoulder touched his arm; she had been frightened by a passing thought, and the movement had been automatic, to assure herself that he was really there, and to fortify her belief that his fidelity would be proof against all things.", "예식 중 잠시 멈춤이 있을 때, 둘이 함께 무릎 꿇고 있는 동안, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 그를 향해 몸을 기울여 어깨가 그의 팔에 닿았다. 스쳐 가는 어떤 생각에 겁이 났던 것이고, 그 동작은 무의식적인 것이었다. 그가 정말로 거기 있음을 확인하려는, 그리고 그의 신의가 그 무엇에도 흔들리지 않으리라는 믿음을 다지려는 동작이었다."]], [["Clare knew that she loved him—every curve of her form showed that—but he did not know at that time the full depth of her devotion, its single-mindedness, its meekness; what long-suffering it guaranteed, what honesty, what endurance, what good faith.", "클레어는 그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 알았다—그녀 몸의 모든 곡선이 그것을 드러냈다—그러나 그때 그는 그녀 헌신의 온전한 깊이를, 그 한결같음을, 그 온순함을 알지 못했다. 그것이 얼마나 큰 오랜 인내를, 얼마나 큰 정직을, 얼마나 큰 견딤을, 얼마나 큰 신실함을 담보하는지를."]], [["As they came out of church the ringers swung the bells off their rests, and a modest peal of three notes broke forth—that limited amount of expression having been deemed sufficient by the church builders for the joys of such a small parish.", "둘이 교회 밖으로 나서자 종지기들이 종을 받침대에서 끌어 흔들었고, 세 가락으로 이루어진 조촐한 종소리가 울려 퍼졌다—이만한 작은 교구의 기쁨에는 이 정도의 표현으로 족하다고 교회를 지은 사람들이 여겼던 것이다."], ["Passing by the tower with her husband on the path to the gate she could feel the vibrant air humming round them from the louvred belfry in the circle of sound, and it matched the highly-charged mental atmosphere in which she was living.", "남편과 함께 종탑을 지나 정문으로 가는 길에서, 그녀는 격자 살이 달린 종루로부터 둥글게 퍼지는 소리의 원 속에서 떨리는 공기가 그들 주위로 윙윙대는 것을 느낄 수 있었고, 그것은 그녀가 살고 있는 한껏 충전된 마음의 분위기와 잘 어울렸다."]], [["This condition of mind, wherein she felt glorified by an irradiation not her own, like the angel whom St John saw in the sun, lasted till the sound of the church bells had died away, and the emotions of the wedding-service had calmed down.", "이러한 마음 상태, 곧 성 요한이 태양 안에서 본 천사처럼 자기 것이 아닌 광채로 영광스러워진 듯한 그 상태는, 교회 종소리가 잦아들고 결혼식의 격정이 가라앉을 때까지 이어졌다."], ["Her eyes could dwell upon details more clearly now, and Mr and Mrs Crick having directed their own gig to be sent for them, to leave the carriage to the young couple, she observed the build and character of that conveyance for the first time.", "이제 그녀의 눈은 세세한 것들을 한층 또렷이 머물러 볼 수 있게 되었고, 크리크 부부가 자기들 이륜마차를 따로 보내 그것을 타고 가게 하고 사륜마차는 젊은 부부에게 넘겨주기로 한 터라, 그녀는 처음으로 그 탈것의 생김새와 특징을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Sitting in silence she regarded it long.", "말없이 앉은 채로 그녀는 그것을 오랫동안 바라보았다."]], [["“I fancy you seem oppressed, Tessy,”", "\"당신, 뭔가 짓눌린 듯해 보이는군, 테시.\""], ["said Clare.", "클레어가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered, putting her hand to her brow.", "그녀가 손을 이마에 갖다 대며 대답했다."], ["“I tremble at many things.", "\"여러 가지가 떨려요."], ["It is all so serious, Angel.", "모든 게 너무나 엄숙해요, 엔젤."], ["Among other things I seem to have seen this carriage before, to be very well acquainted with it.", "그중에서도, 이 마차를 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같아요, 아주 잘 아는 것만 같아요."], ["It is very odd—I must have seen it in a dream.”", "정말 이상해요—꿈에서 본 게 틀림없어요.\""]], [["“Oh—you have heard the legend of the d’Urberville Coach—that well-known superstition of this county about your family when they were very popular here; and this lumbering old thing reminds you of it.”", "\"아—더버빌가의 마차 전설을 들은 게로군—그 가문이 이 고장에서 한창 잘나가던 시절에 관한, 이 고을에 잘 알려진 그 미신 말이야. 그래서 이 둔중한 낡은 물건이 그걸 떠올리게 한 거지.\""]], [["“I have never heard of it to my knowledge,” said she.", "\"제가 알기로는 한 번도 들은 적이 없는데요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“What is the legend—may I know it?”", "\"그 전설이 뭐예요—제가 알아도 될까요?\""]], [["“Well—I would rather not tell it in detail just now.", "\"글쎄—지금은 자세히 말하고 싶지 않아."], ["A certain d’Urberville of the sixteenth or seventeenth century committed a dreadful crime in his family coach; and since that time members of the family see or hear the old coach whenever—— But I’ll tell you another day—it is rather gloomy.", "십육 세기인가 십칠 세기인가의 어느 더버빌가 사람이 자기 집안의 마차 안에서 끔찍한 죄를 저질렀어. 그 이후로 그 가문 사람들은 언제든—— 하지만 다른 날에 이야기해 줄게—꽤 음울한 이야기거든."], ["Evidently some dim knowledge of it has been brought back to your mind by the sight of this venerable caravan.”", "분명 이 유서 깊은 마차를 본 것이 당신 마음속에 그 어렴풋한 기억을 되살린 모양이야.\""]], [["“I don’t remember hearing it before,” she murmured.", "\"전에 그걸 들은 기억이 없어요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Is it when we are going to die, Angel, that members of my family see it, or is it when we have committed a crime?”", "\"우리 가문 사람들이 그걸 보는 건, 우리가 죽으려 할 때인가요, 엔젤, 아니면 우리가 죄를 저질렀을 때인가요?\""]], [["“Now, Tess!”", "\"그만, 테스!\""]], [["He silenced her by a kiss.", "그는 입맞춤으로 그녀의 말을 막았다."]], [["By the time they reached home she was contrite and spiritless.", "집에 다다를 무렵 그녀는 뉘우치며 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["She was Mrs Angel Clare, indeed, but had she any moral right to the name?", "그녀는 정녕 엔젤 클레어 부인이었으나, 그 이름을 가질 도덕적 권리가 자기에게 있긴 한 것일까?"], ["Was she not more truly Mrs Alexander d’Urberville?", "자기는 차라리 알렉산더 더버빌 부인이 더 맞는 게 아닐까?"], ["Could intensity of love justify what might be considered in upright souls as culpable reticence?", "사랑의 강렬함이, 올곧은 영혼들이 죄스러운 침묵이라 여길 만한 것을 정당화할 수 있을까?"], ["She knew not what was expected of women in such cases; and she had no counsellor.", "이런 경우에 여자에게 무엇이 요구되는지 그녀는 알지 못했고, 의논할 상대도 없었다."]], [["However, when she found herself alone in her room for a few minutes—the last day this on which she was ever to enter it—she knelt down and prayed.", "그래도 잠깐이나마 자기 방에 혼자 있게 되자—이 방에 들어오는 것도 이날이 마지막이었다—그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 기도했다."], ["She tried to pray to God, but it was her husband who really had her supplication. Her idolatry of this man was such that she herself almost feared it to be ill-omened.", "하느님께 기도하려 애썼지만, 정작 그녀의 간구를 받은 것은 남편이었다. 이 남자를 향한 그녀의 우상 숭배는 어찌나 깊은지, 그녀 자신마저 그것이 불길한 조짐이 아닐까 두려워할 지경이었다."], ["She was conscious of the notion expressed by Friar Laurence: “These violent delights have violent ends.”", "그녀는 로런스 수사가 한 말, \"이 격렬한 기쁨은 격렬한 끝을 맺느니라\"라는 말이 담은 뜻을 의식하고 있었다."], ["It might be too desperate for human conditions—too rank, to wild, too deadly.", "그것은 인간의 처지에 비해 너무 절박한 것일지도—너무 무성하고, 너무 거칠고, 너무 치명적인 것일지도 몰랐다."]], [["“O my love, why do I love you so!”", "\"오 내 사랑, 나는 어찌하여 당신을 이토록 사랑하는 걸까!\""], ["she whispered there alone; “for she you love is not my real self, but one in my image; the one I might have been!”", "그녀는 거기 홀로 속삭였다. \"당신이 사랑하는 그 여자는 진짜 내가 아니라, 내 모습을 한 다른 여자인 것을. 내가 될 수도 있었던 그 여자인 것을!\""]], [["Afternoon came, and with it the hour for departure.", "오후가 왔고, 그와 함께 떠날 시각이 다가왔다."], ["They had decided to fulfil the plan of going for a few days to the lodgings in the old farmhouse near Wellbridge Mill, at which he meant to reside during his investigation of flour processes.", "둘은 며칠 동안 웰브리지 방앗간 근처 옛 농가의 숙소로 가기로 한 계획을 실행하기로 했다. 그가 밀가루 제조 공정을 조사하는 동안 거기 머물 작정이었던 곳이다."], ["At two o’clock there was nothing left to do but to start.", "두 시가 되자 이제 출발하는 것밖에는 남은 일이 없었다."], ["All the servantry of the dairy were standing in the red-brick entry to see them go out, the dairyman and his wife following to the door.", "낙농장의 일꾼들이 모두 붉은 벽돌 입구에 서서 둘이 나가는 것을 지켜보았고, 낙농장 주인 내외도 문까지 따라 나왔다."], ["Tess saw her three chamber-mates in a row against the wall, pensively inclining their heads.", "테스는 자기 방을 함께 쓰던 세 처녀가 벽을 등지고 나란히 서서 시름에 잠긴 듯 고개를 숙이고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She had much questioned if they would appear at the parting moment; but there they were, stoical and staunch to the last.", "그녀들이 헤어지는 순간에 모습을 보일지 못내 궁금했었는데, 거기 그녀들이 있었다, 끝까지 의연하고 한결같이."], ["She knew why the delicate Retty looked so fragile, and Izz so tragically sorrowful, and Marian so blank; and she forgot her own dogging shadow for a moment in contemplating theirs.", "그녀는 가녀린 레티가 어째서 그토록 연약해 보이는지, 이즈가 어째서 그토록 비극적으로 슬퍼 보이는지, 매리언이 어째서 그토록 멍해 보이는지 알고 있었다. 그리고 그녀들의 그림자를 바라보느라 잠시 자기 자신을 끈질기게 따라다니는 그림자를 잊었다."]], [["She impulsively whispered to him—", "그녀는 충동적으로 그에게 속삭였다—"]], [["“Will you kiss ’em all, once, poor things, for the first and last time?”", "\"저 가엾은 애들한테, 처음이자 마지막으로, 한 번씩만 입맞춰 줄래요?\""]], [["Clare had not the least objection to such a farewell formality—which was all that it was to him—and as he passed them he kissed them in succession where they stood, saying “Goodbye” to each as he did so.", "클레어는 그런 작별의 의례에—그에게는 그뿐인 일이었으니—조금도 거리낌이 없었다. 그래서 그는 그녀들 앞을 지나며 서 있는 그대로 차례차례 입을 맞추었고, 그러면서 한 사람 한 사람에게 \"안녕\"이라고 말했다."], ["When they reached the door Tess femininely glanced back to discern the effect of that kiss of charity; there was no triumph in her glance, as there might have been.", "문에 다다랐을 때 테스는 여자다운 마음으로 뒤를 돌아보며 그 자비로운 입맞춤의 효과를 살폈다. 그녀의 시선에는 으레 있을 법한 승리감 같은 것이 없었다."], ["If there had it would have disappeared when she saw how moved the girls all were.", "설령 있었다 한들, 처녀들이 모두 얼마나 마음이 흔들렸는지를 보았더라면 사라져 버렸을 것이다."], ["The kiss had obviously done harm by awakening feelings they were trying to subdue.", "그 입맞춤은 분명 그녀들이 가라앉히려 애쓰던 감정을 일깨워 해를 끼친 것이었다."]], [["Of all this Clare was unconscious.", "이 모든 것을 클레어는 알아채지 못했다."], ["Passing on to the wicket-gate he shook hands with the dairyman and his wife, and expressed his last thanks to them for their attentions; after which there was a moment of silence before they had moved off.", "쪽문으로 나아가며 그는 낙농장 주인 내외와 악수하고, 그동안 베풀어 준 보살핌에 마지막 감사를 전했다. 그러고 나서 일행이 떠나기 전에 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["It was interrupted by the crowing of a cock.", "그 침묵을 깬 것은 수탉이 우는 소리였다."], ["The white one with the rose comb had come and settled on the palings in front of the house, within a few yards of them, and his notes thrilled their ears through, dwindling away like echoes down a valley of rocks.", "장미 모양 볏을 가진 흰 수탉이 와서 집 앞 울타리 말뚝에, 그들에게서 몇 야드도 안 되는 거리에 내려앉아 있었고, 그 울음소리가 그들의 귀를 꿰뚫었다가 바위 골짜기를 따라가는 메아리처럼 차츰 잦아들었다."]], [["“Oh?”", "\"어머나?\""], ["said Mrs Crick.", "크리크 부인이 말했다."], ["“An afternoon crow!”", "\"오후에 우는 닭이라니!\""]], [["Two men were standing by the yard gate, holding it open.", "마당 문 옆에 사내 둘이 서서 그것을 열어 잡고 있었다."]], [["“That’s bad,” one murmured to the other, not thinking that the words could be heard by the group at the door-wicket.", "\"안 좋은 징조야.\" 한 사내가 다른 사내에게 중얼거렸다, 그 말이 쪽문 앞의 무리에게 들리리라고는 생각지도 못한 채."]], [["The cock crew again—straight towards Clare.", "수탉이 또 울었다—곧장 클레어를 향해서."]], [["“Well!”", "\"허허!\""], ["said the dairyman.", "낙농장 주인이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t like to hear him!”", "\"저 소리 듣기 싫어요!\""], ["said Tess to her husband.", "테스가 남편에게 말했다."], ["“Tell the man to drive on.", "\"마부더러 어서 출발하라고 하세요."], ["Goodbye, goodbye!”", "안녕히, 안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["The cock crew again.", "수탉이 또 울었다."]], [["“Hoosh!", "\"훠이!"], ["Just you be off, sir, or I’ll twist your neck!” said the dairyman with some irritation, turning to the bird and driving him away.", "썩 꺼지지 못해, 이놈, 안 그러면 모가지를 비틀어 버릴 테다!\" 낙농장 주인이 다소 짜증을 내며 새를 향해 돌아서서 쫓아 버렸다."], ["And to his wife as they went indoors: “Now, to think o’ that just to-day!", "그리고 안으로 들어가며 아내에게 말했다. \"하필 오늘 같은 날 저런 짓을 하다니!"], ["I’ve not heard his crow of an afternoon all the year afore.”", "한 해가 다 가도록 오후에 우는 소리는 들은 적이 없었는데 말이야.\""]], [["“It only means a change in the weather,”", "\"그저 날씨가 바뀐다는 뜻일 뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “not what you think: ’tis impossible!”", "\"당신이 생각하는 그런 게 아니라요. 그런 일은 있을 수 없어요!\""]], [["# XXXIV", "# XXXIV"]], [["They drove by the level road along the valley to a distance of a few miles, and, reaching Wellbridge, turned away from the village to the left, and over the great Elizabethan bridge which gives the place half its name.", "그들은 골짜기를 따라 평평한 길로 몇 마일을 달려, 웰브리지에 다다라서는 마을에서 왼쪽으로 벗어나, 그 지명의 절반을 이루는 거대한 엘리자베스 시대의 다리를 건넜다."], ["Immediately behind it stood the house wherein they had engaged lodgings, whose exterior features are so well known to all travellers through the Froom Valley; once portion of a fine manorial residence, and the property and seat of a d’Urberville, but since its partial demolition a farmhouse.", "그 바로 뒤에, 그들이 숙소를 잡은 집이 서 있었으니, 프룸 골짜기를 지나는 여행자라면 누구나 그 외관을 잘 아는 집이었다. 한때는 어느 훌륭한 장원 저택의 일부였고, 더버빌가의 소유이자 거처였으나, 일부가 헐린 뒤로는 농가가 되어 있었다."]], [["“Welcome to one of your ancestral mansions!”", "\"당신 조상의 저택 중 하나에 잘 오셨소이다!\""], ["said Clare as he handed her down.", "그녀를 마차에서 내려 주며 클레어가 말했다."], ["But he regretted the pleasantry; it was too near a satire.", "그러나 그는 그 농담을 후회했다. 풍자에 너무 가까웠던 것이다."]], [["On entering they found that, though they had only engaged a couple of rooms, the farmer had taken advantage of their proposed presence during the coming days to pay a New Year’s visit to some friends, leaving a woman from a neighbouring cottage to minister to their few wants.", "안으로 들어서니, 둘은 방을 두 칸만 빌렸을 뿐인데도, 농부가 앞으로 며칠간 그들이 머문다는 것을 핑계 삼아 새해 인사차 친구들을 찾아간 터였고, 그들의 자잘한 시중을 들도록 이웃 오두막의 여인 하나만 남겨 두었음을 알게 되었다."], ["The absoluteness of possession pleased them, and they realized it as the first moment of their experience under their own exclusive roof-tree. But he found that the mouldy old habitation somewhat depressed his bride.", "집을 통째로 차지하게 된 것이 둘은 마음에 들었고, 자기들만의 지붕 아래서 보내는 첫 순간임을 실감했다. 그러나 그는 곰팡내 나는 그 낡은 집이 신부의 기분을 다소 가라앉게 한다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["When the carriage was gone they ascended the stairs to wash their hands, the charwoman showing the way.", "마차가 떠나자 둘은 손을 씻으러 계단을 올랐고, 허드렛일하는 여인이 길을 안내했다."], ["On the landing Tess stopped and started.", "층계참에서 테스가 걸음을 멈추며 흠칫했다."]], [["“What’s the matter?” said he.", "\"무슨 일이야?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["“Those horrid women!”", "\"저 끔찍한 여자들요!\""], ["she answered with a smile.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["“How they frightened me.”", "\"어찌나 놀라게 하던지요.\""]], [["He looked up, and perceived two life-size portraits on panels built into the masonry.", "그가 올려다보니, 벽체에 끼워 넣은 판자에 실물 크기의 초상화 두 점이 보였다."], ["As all visitors to the mansion are aware, these paintings represent women of middle age, of a date some two hundred years ago, whose lineaments once seen can never be forgotten.", "이 저택을 찾는 이라면 누구나 알듯, 이 그림들은 약 이백 년 전 시대의 중년 여인들을 그린 것으로, 그 이목구비는 한번 보면 결코 잊을 수 없는 것이었다."], ["The long pointed features, narrow eye, and smirk of the one, so suggestive of merciless treachery; the bill-hook nose, large teeth, and bold eye of the other suggesting arrogance to the point of ferocity, haunt the beholder afterwards in his dreams.", "하나는 길쭉하고 뾰족한 얼굴 생김과 좁은 눈, 그리고 가차 없는 배신을 암시하는 듯한 능글맞은 웃음을 띠었고, 다른 하나는 낫 모양의 코와 큼직한 이, 그리고 잔인함에 이를 만큼의 오만을 풍기는 대담한 눈을 지녔으니, 그것을 본 사람을 두고두고 그 꿈속까지 따라다니는 얼굴들이었다."]], [["“Whose portraits are those?”", "\"저건 누구의 초상화인가요?\""], ["asked Clare of the charwoman.", "클레어가 허드렛일하는 여인에게 물었다."]], [["“I have been told by old folk that they were ladies of the d’Urberville family, the ancient lords of this manor,”", "\"나이 든 어른들한테 듣기로는 더버빌가의 부인들이라던데요, 이 장원의 옛 영주들 말이에요.\""], ["she said, “Owing to their being builded into the wall they can’t be moved away.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"벽 속에 끼워 넣어 지은 거라서 떼어 낼 수가 없답니다.\""]], [["The unpleasantness of the matter was that, in addition to their effect upon Tess, her fine features were unquestionably traceable in these exaggerated forms.", "이 일의 언짢은 점은, 테스에게 끼치는 영향에 더하여, 그녀의 고운 이목구비가 이 과장된 형상들 속에서 의심할 여지 없이 더듬어 보였다는 데 있었다."], ["He said nothing of this, however, and, regretting that he had gone out of his way to choose the house for their bridal time, went on into the adjoining room.", "그러나 그는 이에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 신혼 시절을 보낼 집으로 굳이 이곳을 고른 것을 후회하며 옆방으로 들어갔다."], ["The place having been rather hastily prepared for them, they washed their hands in one basin. Clare touched hers under the water.", "그들을 위해 다소 황급히 준비된 곳이라, 둘은 한 대야에 손을 씻었다. 클레어가 물속에서 그녀의 손을 건드렸다."]], [["“Which are my fingers and which are yours?”", "\"어느 게 내 손가락이고 어느 게 당신 거지?\""], ["he said, looking up.", "그가 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“They are very much mixed.”", "\"아주 뒤섞여 버렸군.\""]], [["“They are all yours,”", "\"전부 당신 거예요.\""], ["said she, very prettily, and endeavoured to be gayer than she was.", "그녀가 무척 어여쁘게 말했고, 실제 기분보다 더 명랑한 척하려 애썼다."], ["He had not been displeased with her thoughtfulness on such an occasion; it was what every sensible woman would show: but Tess knew that she had been thoughtful to excess, and struggled against it.", "이런 때에 보이는 그녀의 세심한 마음씨를 그가 못마땅해한 것은 아니었다. 그것은 분별 있는 여자라면 누구나 보일 법한 것이었다. 그러나 테스는 자기가 지나치게 마음을 쓰고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그것에 맞서 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["The sun was so low on that short last afternoon of the year that it shone in through a small opening and formed a golden staff which stretched across to her skirt, where it made a spot like a paint-mark set upon her.", "그해의 짧디짧은 마지막 오후, 해가 어찌나 낮게 떴는지, 작은 틈으로 비쳐 들어와 황금빛 지팡이를 이루며 그녀의 치마까지 뻗어, 거기에 마치 그녀에게 찍힌 물감 자국 같은 반점을 만들었다."], ["They went into the ancient parlour to tea, and here they shared their first common meal alone.", "둘은 차를 마시러 그 오래된 응접실로 들어갔고, 거기서 단둘이 첫 식사를 나누었다."], ["Such was their childishness, or rather his, that he found it interesting to use the same bread-and-butter plate as herself, and to brush crumbs from her lips with his own.", "둘은—아니 차라리 그는—어찌나 어린아이 같던지, 그녀와 같은 버터빵 접시를 쓰는 것이, 또 자기 입술로 그녀 입가의 부스러기를 털어 주는 것이 재미있었다."], ["He wondered a little that she did not enter into these frivolities with his own zest.", "그녀가 이런 시시한 장난에 자기만큼 신이 나서 끼어들지 않는 것이 그는 조금 의아했다."]], [["Looking at her silently for a long time; “She is a dear dear Tess,”", "오랫동안 말없이 그녀를 바라보며, 그는 마음속으로 생각했다."], ["he thought to himself, as one deciding on the true construction of a difficult passage.", "어려운 구절의 참뜻을 가려내려는 사람처럼. \"그녀는 사랑스럽고 사랑스러운 테스야."], ["“Do I realize solemnly enough how utterly and irretrievably this little womanly thing is the creature of my good or bad faith and fortune?", "이 작은 여인 같은 존재가 나의 선의나 악의에, 나의 행운이나 불운에 얼마나 철저하고 돌이킬 수 없이 매여 있는지를, 나는 충분히 엄숙하게 깨닫고 있는 걸까?"], ["I think not. I think I could not, unless I were a woman myself.", "그렇지 못한 것 같다. 내가 여자 자신이 아닌 한, 깨달을 수 없으리라 본다."], ["What I am in worldly estate, she is.", "내가 세속적 처지에서 어떠한가, 그녀도 그러하다."], ["What I become, she must become.", "내가 무엇이 되든, 그녀도 그것이 되어야 한다."], ["What I cannot be, she cannot be.", "내가 될 수 없는 것은, 그녀도 될 수 없다."], ["And shall I ever neglect her, or hurt her, or even forget to consider her?", "그런데 내가 그녀를 소홀히 하거나, 상처 입히거나, 헤아리기를 잊는 일이 있을까?"], ["God forbid such a crime!”", "그런 죄악은 부디 없기를!\""]], [["They sat on over the tea-table waiting for their luggage, which the dairyman had promised to send before it grew dark.", "둘은 짐을 기다리며 찻상 앞에 계속 앉아 있었다. 낙농장 주인이 날이 저물기 전에 보내 주마고 약속한 짐이었다."], ["But evening began to close in, and the luggage did not arrive, and they had brought nothing more than they stood in.", "그러나 저녁이 깃들기 시작했는데도 짐은 오지 않았고, 둘은 입은 옷 말고는 아무것도 가져온 것이 없었다."], ["With the departure of the sun the calm mood of the winter day changed.", "해가 지면서 겨울 낮의 고요하던 기색이 변했다."], ["Out of doors there began noises as of silk smartly rubbed; the restful dead leaves of the preceding autumn were stirred to irritated resurrection, and whirled about unwillingly, and tapped against the shutters. It soon began to rain.", "바깥에서는 비단을 세게 비비는 듯한 소리들이 일기 시작했고, 지난가을의 편히 잠들어 있던 죽은 잎들이 짜증스러운 부활로 깨어나 마지못해 이리저리 휘날리며 덧창에 부딪쳤다. 이윽고 비가 내리기 시작했다."]], [["“That cock knew the weather was going to change,” said Clare.", "\"저 수탉은 날씨가 바뀔 걸 알았던 거야.\" 클레어가 말했다."]], [["The woman who had attended upon them had gone home for the night, but she had placed candles upon the table, and now they lit them.", "그들의 시중을 들던 여인은 밤이 되어 집으로 돌아갔지만, 식탁에 양초를 놓아두고 갔고, 이제 둘은 그것에 불을 붙였다."], ["Each candle-flame drew towards the fireplace.", "촛불 하나하나가 벽난로 쪽으로 끌리듯 기울었다."]], [["“These old houses are so draughty,”", "\"이런 낡은 집들은 외풍이 너무 심하군.\""], ["continued Angel, looking at the flames, and at the grease guttering down the sides.", "불꽃을, 그리고 옆구리를 타고 흘러내리는 촛농을 바라보며 엔젤이 말을 이었다."], ["“I wonder where that luggage is.", "\"짐이 대체 어디 있는 건지 모르겠어."], ["We haven’t even a brush and comb.”", "우린 빗과 솔조차 없잖아.\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠어요.\""], ["she answered, absent-minded.", "그녀가 멍하니 대답했다."]], [["“Tess, you are not a bit cheerful this evening—not at all as you used to be.", "\"테스, 당신 오늘 저녁엔 조금도 즐거워 보이질 않아—예전 모습과는 영 딴판이야."], ["Those harridans on the panels upstairs have unsettled you.", "위층 판자에 그려진 저 마귀할멈들이 당신 마음을 흔들어 놓은 거야."], ["I am sorry I brought you here.", "당신을 이리로 데려온 게 미안해."], ["I wonder if you really love me, after all?”", "당신, 정말 나를 사랑하긴 하는 걸까, 어쩐지 궁금해지는군?\""]], [["He knew that she did, and the words had no serious intent; but she was surcharged with emotion, and winced like a wounded animal.", "그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 그 말에 진지한 뜻은 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 감정으로 가득 차 있던 터라, 상처 입은 짐승처럼 움찔했다."], ["Though she tried not to shed tears, she could not help showing one or two.", "눈물을 흘리지 않으려 애썼지만, 한두 방울 비치는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다."]], [["“I did not mean it!”", "\"그런 뜻이 아니었어!\""], ["said he, sorry. “You are worried at not having your things, I know.", "그가 미안해하며 말했다. \"당신 물건이 없어서 속상한 거지, 알아."], ["I cannot think why old Jonathan has not come with them.", "늙은 조너선이 왜 그걸 가지고 오지 않는지 모르겠군."], ["Why, it is seven o’clock?", "아니, 벌써 일곱 시잖아?"], ["Ah, there he is!”", "아, 저기 오는군!\""]], [["A knock had come to the door, and, there being nobody else to answer it, Clare went out.", "문을 두드리는 소리가 났고, 달리 응대할 사람이 없으니 클레어가 나갔다."], ["He returned to the room with a small package in his hand.", "그는 작은 꾸러미 하나를 손에 들고 방으로 돌아왔다."]], [["“It is not Jonathan, after all,” he said.", "\"결국 조너선이 아니었어.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“How vexing!” said Tess.", "그 꾸러미는 특별 심부름꾼이 가져온 것이었다."], ["The packet had been brought by a special messenger, who had arrived at Talbothays from Emminster Vicarage immediately after the departure of the married couple, and had followed them hither, being under injunction to deliver it into nobody’s hands but theirs.", "그는 결혼한 부부가 떠난 직후 에민스터 목사관에서 톨버세이즈로 도착해, 그들을 좇아 여기까지 따라왔던 것이다. 그것을 오직 부부의 손에만 건네고 다른 누구에게도 넘기지 말라는 분부를 받은 터였다. 클레어가 그것을 불빛 가까이로 가져왔다."], ["Clare brought it to the light. It was less than a foot long, sewed up in canvas, sealed in red wax with his father’s seal, and directed in his father’s hand to “Mrs Angel Clare.”", "한 자가 채 안 되는 길이에, 캔버스 천으로 꿰매여 있었고, 아버지의 인장이 찍힌 붉은 밀랍으로 봉인되었으며, 아버지의 필체로 \"엔젤 클레어 부인 앞\"이라 적혀 있었다."]], [["“It is a little wedding-present for you, Tess,”", "\"당신에게 보내는 자그마한 결혼 선물이야, 테스.\""], ["said he, handing it to her.", "그것을 그녀에게 건네며 그가 말했다."], ["“How thoughtful they are!”", "\"참 마음을 써 주셨군!\""]], [["Tess looked a little flustered as she took it.", "테스는 그것을 받아 들며 조금 당황한 기색이었다."]], [["“I think I would rather have you open it, dearest,” said she, turning over the parcel.", "\"당신이 열어 주면 좋겠어요, 여보.\" 꾸러미를 이리저리 뒤집으며 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I don’t like to break those great seals; they look so serious.", "\"저 큼직한 봉인을 깨뜨리고 싶지 않아요. 너무 엄숙해 보여서요."], ["Please open it for me!”", "부디 저 대신 열어 주세요!\""]], [["He undid the parcel.", "그가 꾸러미를 풀었다."], ["Inside was a case of morocco leather, on the top of which lay a note and a key.", "안에는 모로코가죽 상자가 들어 있었고, 그 위에 쪽지 한 장과 열쇠 하나가 놓여 있었다."]], [["The note was for Clare, in the following words:", "쪽지는 클레어에게 보내는 것으로, 다음과 같은 내용이었다."]], [["My dear son,—\nPossibly you have forgotten that on the death of your godmother, Mrs Pitney, when you were a lad, she—vain, kind woman that she was—left to me a portion of the contents of her jewel-case in trust for your wife, if you should ever have one, as a mark of her affection for you and whomsoever you should choose.", "사랑하는 아들에게,— 아마 너는 잊었겠지만, 네가 어린 소년이었을 때 네 대모이신 피트니 부인이 돌아가시며—허영심이 있긴 했으나 친절한 분이었지—그분의 보석함 내용물 중 일부를, 네가 언젠가 아내를 맞게 된다면 그 아내에게 주라며 나에게 신탁으로 남기셨단다. 너를, 그리고 네가 누구를 택하든 그 사람을 아끼는 마음의 표시로 말이다."], ["This trust I have fulfilled, and the diamonds have been locked up at my banker’s ever since.", "이 신탁을 나는 이행해 왔고, 그 다이아몬드는 그 이래로 내 거래 은행에 줄곧 보관되어 왔다."], ["Though I feel it to be a somewhat incongruous act in the circumstances, I am, as you will see, bound to hand over the articles to the woman to whom the use of them for her lifetime will now rightly belong, and they are therefore promptly sent.", "이런 상황에서 이는 다소 어울리지 않는 처사로 여겨지긴 하나, 너도 알겠지만, 나로서는 이제 그것을 평생토록 사용할 권리가 정당하게 돌아갈 그 여인에게 넘겨주지 않을 수 없으니, 그리하여 곧바로 보낸다."], ["They become, I believe, heirlooms, strictly speaking, according to the terms of your godmother’s will.", "네 대모님 유언의 조건에 따르면, 엄밀히 말해 이것은 가보가 되는 것으로 안다."], ["The precise words of the clause that refers to this matter are enclosed.", "이 일에 관한 조항의 정확한 문구를 동봉한다."]], [["“I do remember,”", "\"기억나는군.\""], ["said Clare; “but I had quite forgotten.”", "클레어가 말했다. \"하지만 까맣게 잊고 있었어.\""]], [["Unlocking the case, they found it to contain a necklace, with pendant, bracelets, and ear-rings; and also some other small ornaments.", "상자를 열어 보니, 펜던트가 달린 목걸이 하나와 팔찌, 귀고리, 그리고 그 밖의 자잘한 장신구 몇 점이 들어 있었다."]], [["Tess seemed afraid to touch them at first, but her eyes sparkled for a moment as much as the stones when Clare spread out the set.", "테스는 처음엔 그것에 손대기를 두려워하는 듯했으나, 클레어가 그 한 벌을 펼쳐 놓자 잠시 그녀의 눈이 그 보석들 못지않게 반짝였다."]], [["“Are they mine?”", "\"이게 제 거예요?\""], ["she asked incredulously.", "그녀가 믿기지 않는다는 듯 물었다."]], [["“They are, certainly,” said he.", "\"그럼, 당연하지.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["He looked into the fire.", "그는 불을 들여다보았다."], ["He remembered how, when he was a lad of fifteen, his godmother, the Squire’s wife—the only rich person with whom he had ever come in contact—had pinned her faith to his success; had prophesied a wondrous career for him.", "열다섯 살 소년이었을 때, 자기 대모이자 그가 평생 접해 본 유일한 부자였던 향사의 부인이, 자기의 성공을 굳게 믿었던 일을 그는 떠올렸다. 그녀는 그에게 놀라운 출세를 예언했었다."], ["There had seemed nothing at all out of keeping with such a conjectured career in the storing up of these showy ornaments for his wife and the wives of her descendants.", "그렇게 짐작된 출세에, 그의 아내와 그 후손들의 아내를 위해 이런 화려한 장신구를 간직해 두는 일은 조금도 어긋날 게 없어 보였었다."], ["They gleamed somewhat ironically now. “Yet why?”", "그것들이 지금은 어쩐지 빈정대듯 반짝였다."], ["he asked himself.", "\"그렇지만 왜?\""], ["It was but a question of vanity throughout; and if that were admitted into one side of the equation it should be admitted into the other.", "그는 자신에게 물었다. 그것은 처음부터 끝까지 그저 허영의 문제일 뿐이었다. 그리고 만약 허영이 등식의 한쪽에 허용된다면, 다른 쪽에도 허용되어 마땅했다."], ["His wife was a d’Urberville: whom could they become better than her?", "그의 아내는 더버빌가의 여인이었다. 그것이 그녀보다 더 잘 어울릴 사람이 누가 있겠는가?"]], [["Suddenly he said with enthusiasm—", "별안간 그가 열에 들떠 말했다—"]], [["“Tess, put them on—put them on!”", "\"테스, 그걸 차 봐—차 보라고!\""], ["And he turned from the fire to help her.", "그러고는 그녀를 돕고자 불에서 돌아섰다."]], [["But as if by magic she had already donned them—necklace, ear-rings, bracelets, and all.", "그러나 마치 마법에라도 걸린 듯, 그녀는 이미 그것들을 다 차고 있었다—목걸이, 귀고리, 팔찌, 전부 다."]], [["“But the gown isn’t right, Tess,”", "\"그런데 가운이 맞질 않는걸, 테스.\""], ["said Clare. “It ought to be a low one for a set of brilliants like that.”", "클레어가 말했다. \"저런 보석 한 벌에는 가슴이 깊이 파인 옷이라야 하는데.\""]], [["“Ought it?”", "\"그래야 하나요?\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그럼.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["He suggested to her how to tuck in the upper edge of her bodice, so as to make it roughly approximate to the cut for evening wear; and when she had done this, and the pendant to the necklace hung isolated amid the whiteness of her throat, as it was designed to do, he stepped back to survey her.", "그는 그녀에게 보디스 윗단을 안쪽으로 접어 넣어, 저녁 예복의 재단에 대강 비슷해 보이게 하는 법을 일러 주었다. 그녀가 그렇게 하여, 목걸이의 펜던트가 본래 그러도록 만들어진 대로 그녀 목의 흰 살결 한가운데 외따로 드리워지자, 그는 한 걸음 물러나 그녀를 찬찬히 살폈다."]], [["“My heavens,”", "\"세상에.\""], ["said Clare, “how beautiful you are!”", "클레어가 말했다. \"당신, 어쩜 이렇게 아름다울까!\""]], [["As everybody knows, fine feathers make fine birds; a peasant girl but very moderately prepossessing to the casual observer in her simple condition and attire will bloom as an amazing beauty if clothed as a woman of fashion with the aids that Art can render; while the beauty of the midnight crush would often cut but a sorry figure if placed inside the field-woman’s wrapper upon a monotonous acreage of turnips on a dull day.", "누구나 알듯이, 고운 깃털이 고운 새를 만드는 법이다. 소박한 처지와 옷차림으로는 무심히 지나치는 이의 눈에 그저 그만한 미모로 보이던 시골 처녀도, 예술이 베푸는 도움으로 멋쟁이 여인처럼 차려입히면 놀라운 미인으로 활짝 피어난다. 반면 한밤의 무도회를 빛내던 미인도 흐린 날 단조롭게 펼쳐진 순무밭 위에서 들일하는 여인의 작업복을 입혀 놓으면 흔히 초라한 꼴이 되고 만다."], ["He had never till now estimated the artistic excellence of Tess’s limbs and features.", "그는 지금까지 한 번도 테스의 팔다리와 이목구비가 지닌 예술적 빼어남을 헤아려 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“If you were only to appear in a ball-room!”", "\"당신이 무도회장에 나서기만 한다면!\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “But no—no, dearest; I think I love you best in the wing-bonnet and cotton-frock—yes, better than in this, well as you support these dignities.”", "\"하지만 아니야—아니야, 여보. 나는 챙 달린 보닛에 무명 원피스 차림의 당신이 가장 좋은 것 같아—그래, 이 차림보다 그게 더 좋아, 이 위엄을 이토록 잘 받쳐 내긴 하지만 말이야.\""]], [["Tess’s sense of her striking appearance had given her a flush of excitement, which was yet not happiness.", "자기의 빼어난 모습을 의식하자 테스에게는 흥분의 홍조가 일었으나, 그것은 행복은 아니었다."]], [["“I’ll take them off,”", "\"이걸 벗을게요.\""], ["she said, “in case Jonathan should see me.", "그녀가 말했다. \"조너선이 저를 볼지도 모르니까요."], ["They are not fit for me, are they?", "이건 저한테 어울리지 않아요, 그렇죠?"], ["They must be sold, I suppose?”", "팔아 버려야겠죠, 아마?\""]], [["“Let them stay a few minutes longer.", "\"몇 분만 더 그대로 두구려. 팔다니?"], ["Sell them?", "결코 안 돼."], ["Never. It would be a breach of faith.”", "그건 신의를 저버리는 일이 될 거야.\""]], [["Influenced by a second thought she readily obeyed.", "또 다른 생각에 마음이 움직여 그녀는 선뜻 따랐다."], ["She had something to tell, and there might be help in these.", "그녀에게는 할 이야기가 있었고, 어쩌면 이것들이 도움이 될지도 몰랐다."], ["She sat down with the jewels upon her; and they again indulged in conjectures as to where Jonathan could possibly be with their baggage.", "그녀는 보석을 몸에 두른 채 자리에 앉았고, 둘은 다시 조너선이 짐을 가지고 대체 어디쯤 있을까 이런저런 짐작을 늘어놓았다."], ["The ale they had poured out for his consumption when he came had gone flat with long standing.", "그가 오면 마시라고 따라 둔 맥주는 오래 놓여 있어 김이 다 빠져 있었다."]], [["Shortly after this they began supper, which was already laid on a side-table.", "이내 둘은 저녁을 먹기 시작했는데, 식사는 이미 곁상에 차려져 있었다."], ["Ere they had finished there was a jerk in the fire-smoke, the rising skein of which bulged out into the room, as if some giant had laid his hand on the chimney-top for a moment.", "다 먹기도 전에 굴뚝 연기가 한 차례 출렁하더니, 솟아오르던 연기 가닥이 방 안으로 불룩 밀려 나왔다. 마치 어떤 거인이 한순간 굴뚝 꼭대기에 손을 얹기라도 한 듯했다."], ["It had been caused by the opening of the outer door.", "그것은 바깥문이 열린 탓이었다."], ["A heavy step was now heard in the passage, and Angel went out.", "이제 복도에서 묵직한 발소리가 들렸고, 엔젤이 나갔다."]], [["“I couldn’ make nobody hear at all by knocking,”", "\"문을 두드려도 도무지 아무도 들으시질 않아서요.\""], ["apologized Jonathan Kail, for it was he at last; “and as’t was raining out I opened the door.", "조너선 케일이 변명했으니, 마침내 그였던 것이다. \"게다가 밖에 비가 와서, 제가 문을 열었습니다."], ["I’ve brought the things, sir.”", "짐을 가져왔습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“I am very glad to see them.", "\"가져와 줘서 정말 반갑네."], ["But you are very late.”", "한데 너무 늦었군.\""]], [["“Well, yes, sir.”", "\"그게, 네, 선생님.\""]], [["There was something subdued in Jonathan Kail’s tone which had not been there in the day, and lines of concern were ploughed upon his forehead in addition to the lines of years. He continued—", "조너선 케일의 어조에는 낮에는 없던 어떤 가라앉은 기색이 있었고, 세월의 주름에 더하여 근심의 주름이 그의 이마에 깊이 패어 있었다. 그가 말을 이었다—"]], [["“We’ve all been gallied at the dairy at what might ha’ been a most terrible affliction since you and your Mis’ess—so to name her now—left us this a’ternoon.", "\"선생님하고 마님—이제는 그렇게 불러야겠지요—두 분이 오늘 오후에 우릴 떠나신 뒤로, 낙농장에선 다들 자칫 엄청난 변고가 될 뻔한 일에 혼이 다 빠졌답니다."], ["Perhaps you ha’nt forgot the cock’s afternoon crow?”", "혹시 수탉이 오후에 울던 거, 잊지 않으셨겠지요?\""]], [["“Dear me;—what—”", "\"아이고—뭐가—\""]], [["“Well, some says it do mane one thing, and some another; but what’s happened is that poor little Retty Priddle hev tried to drown herself.”", "\"그게, 누구는 그게 이 뜻이라 하고 누구는 저 뜻이라 합니다만, 정작 일어난 일은, 가엾은 어린 레티 프리들이 물에 빠져 죽으려 했다는 겁니다.\""]], [["“No! Really!", "\"이런! 정말로!"], ["Why, she bade us goodbye with the rest—”", "아니, 그 애는 다른 애들이랑 우리에게 작별 인사를 했는데—\""]], [["“Yes. Well, sir, when you and your Mis’ess—so to name what she lawful is—when you two drove away, as I say, Retty and Marian put on their bonnets and went out; and as there is not much doing now, being New Year’s Eve, and folks mops and brooms from what’s inside ’em, nobody took much notice.", "\"그렇지요. 그게, 선생님, 선생님하고 마님—이제 법적으로 그러하신 분 말입니다만—두 분이 마차로 떠나신 뒤에, 말씀드린 대로, 레티와 매리언이 보닛을 쓰고 나갔답니다."], ["They went on to Lew-Everard, where they had summut to drink, and then on they vamped to Dree-armed Cross, and there they seemed to have parted, Retty striking across the water-meads as if for home, and Marian going on to the next village, where there’s another public-house.", "그런데 지금이야 섣달그믐이라 할 일도 많지 않고, 다들 속에 든 것 때문에 들떠 있던 터라, 아무도 별로 눈여겨보질 않았지요. 둘이 루에버라드까지 가서 뭘 좀 마시고는, 거기서 드리암드 크로스까지 터덜터덜 걸어갔는데, 거기서 갈라진 모양입니다. 레티는 집으로 가려는 듯 물가 풀밭을 가로질러 갔고, 매리언은 다음 마을로 갔는데, 거기 또 다른 술집이 있거든요."], ["Nothing more was zeed or heard o’ Retty till the waterman, on his way home, noticed something by the Great Pool; ’twas her bonnet and shawl packed up.", "레티에 대해선 그 뒤로 보이지도 들리지도 않다가, 뱃사공이 집으로 가던 길에 큰 못가에서 뭔가를 봤답니다. 그게 그 애의 보닛하고 숄을 챙겨 놓은 거였지요."], ["In the water he found her.", "물속에서 그 애를 찾았답니다."], ["He and another man brought her home, thinking ’a was dead; but she fetched round by degrees.”", "그 사람하고 다른 사람 하나가 죽은 줄 알고 그 애를 집으로 데려왔는데, 차츰 정신을 차리더랍니다.\""]], [["Angel, suddenly recollecting that Tess was overhearing this gloomy tale, went to shut the door between the passage and the ante-room to the inner parlour where she was; but his wife, flinging a shawl round her, had come to the outer room and was listening to the man’s narrative, her eyes resting absently on the luggage and the drops of rain glistening upon it.", "엔젤은 테스가 이 음울한 이야기를 엿듣고 있다는 것을 문득 떠올리고는, 그녀가 있는 안쪽 응접실로 통하는 대기실과 복도 사이의 문을 닫으러 갔다. 그러나 그의 아내는 어깨에 숄을 두른 채 바깥방으로 나와 사내의 이야기를 듣고 있었으니, 그녀의 눈은 짐과 그 위에서 반짝이는 빗방울에 멍하니 머물러 있었다."]], [["“And, more than this, there’s Marian; she’s been found dead drunk by the withy-bed—a girl who hev never been known to touch anything before except shilling ale; though, to be sure, ’a was always a good trencher-woman, as her face showed.", "\"게다가 이뿐만이 아닙니다. 매리언도 말입니다, 버드나무 늪가에서 곤드레만드레 취해 쓰러진 채로 발견됐답니다—전에는 한 푼짜리 맥주 말고는 입에 댄 적이 없는 걸로 알려진 애가 말이지요. 하기야 얼굴만 봐도 알 만큼 늘 먹성은 좋았습니다만."], ["It seems as if the maids had all gone out o’ their minds!”", "처녀들이 죄다 정신이 나가 버린 것만 같습니다요!\""]], [["“And Izz?”", "\"그럼 이즈는요?\""], ["asked Tess.", "테스가 물었다."]], [["“Izz is about house as usual; but ’a do say ’a can guess how it happened; and she seems to be very low in mind about it, poor maid, as well she mid be.", "\"이즈는 여느 때처럼 집안일을 하고 있습니다. 다만 그 애 말이, 일이 어찌 된 영문인지 짐작이 간다고 하더군요. 그러면서 그 애는 그 일로 영 풀이 죽어 있습니다, 가엾은 것이, 그럴 만도 하지요."], ["And so you see, sir, as all this happened just when we was packing your few traps and your Mis’ess’s night-rail and dressing things into the cart, why, it belated me.”", "그래서 보시다시피, 선생님, 선생님 짐 몇 가지하고 마님 잠옷이며 화장 도구를 수레에 싣던 바로 그때 이 모든 일이 터지는 바람에, 제가 이렇게 늦은 겁니다요.\""]], [["“Yes. Well, Jonathan, will you get the trunks upstairs, and drink a cup of ale, and hasten back as soon as you can, in case you should be wanted?”", "\"그렇군. 자, 조너선, 짐 가방을 위층으로 올려 주고, 맥주 한 잔 마신 다음, 자네가 필요할지 모르니 되도록 빨리 돌아가게.\""]], [["Tess had gone back to the inner parlour, and sat down by the fire, looking wistfully into it.", "테스는 안쪽 응접실로 돌아가 불가에 앉아 그것을 그윽이 들여다보고 있었다."], ["She heard Jonathan Kail’s heavy footsteps up and down the stairs till he had done placing the luggage, and heard him express his thanks for the ale her husband took out to him, and for the gratuity he received.", "조너선 케일이 짐을 다 놓을 때까지 계단을 오르내리는 그 묵직한 발소리가 그녀에게 들려왔고, 남편이 내다 준 맥주에 대해, 또 받은 사례에 대해 그가 고맙다고 말하는 소리도 들렸다."], ["Jonathan’s footsteps then died from the door, and his cart creaked away.", "이윽고 조너선의 발소리가 문에서 잦아들었고, 그의 수레가 삐걱대며 멀어져 갔다."]], [["Angel slid forward the massive oak bar which secured the door, and coming in to where she sat over the hearth, pressed her cheeks between his hands from behind.", "엔젤은 문을 잠그는 묵직한 참나무 빗장을 밀어 걸고는, 그녀가 화덕 위로 몸을 기울이고 앉아 있는 곳으로 들어와, 뒤에서 두 손으로 그녀의 두 뺨을 감쌌다."], ["He expected her to jump up gaily and unpack the toilet-gear that she had been so anxious about, but as she did not rise he sat down with her in the firelight, the candles on the supper-table being too thin and glimmering to interfere with its glow.", "그녀가 신나게 벌떡 일어나, 그토록 애태우던 화장 도구를 풀어헤치리라 그는 기대했지만, 그녀가 일어나지 않자 그도 그녀와 함께 난롯불 빛 속에 앉았다. 저녁상 위의 양초들은 너무 가느다랗고 가물거려 그 불빛을 방해하지 못했다."]], [["“I am so sorry you should have heard this sad story about the girls,” he said.", "\"처녀들에 관한 이 슬픈 이야기를 당신이 듣게 되어 정말 안됐어.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Still, don’t let it depress you.", "\"그래도, 그것 때문에 기죽지는 마."], ["Retty was naturally morbid, you know.”", "레티는 원래 좀 병적인 데가 있었잖아, 알다시피.\""]], [["“Without the least cause,”", "\"전혀 그럴 까닭이 없는데도요.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."], ["“While they who have cause to be, hide it, and pretend they are not.”", "\"정작 그럴 까닭이 있는 사람들은 그걸 숨기고, 아닌 척하는데 말이에요.\""]], [["This incident had turned the scale for her.", "이 일이 그녀의 저울을 한쪽으로 기울게 했다."], ["They were simple and innocent girls on whom the unhappiness of unrequited love had fallen; they had deserved better at the hands of Fate.", "그녀들은 짝사랑의 불행이 덮친 순박하고 죄 없는 처녀들이었다. 그녀들은 운명의 손에서 더 나은 것을 받을 만한 이들이었다."], ["She had deserved worse—yet she was the chosen one.", "자기는 더 못한 것을 받아 마땅했건만—그런데도 자기가 택함을 받은 자였다."], ["It was wicked of her to take all without paying.", "아무것도 치르지 않고 모든 것을 거머쥐는 것은 사악한 짓이었다."], ["She would pay to the uttermost farthing; she would tell, there and then.", "그녀는 마지막 한 푼까지 치르리라. 바로 여기서, 바로 지금 털어놓으리라."], ["This final determination she came to when she looked into the fire, he holding her hand.", "그가 자기 손을 잡은 채 불을 들여다보던 그때, 그녀는 이 마지막 결심에 이르렀다."]], [["A steady glare from the now flameless embers painted the sides and back of the fireplace with its colour, and the well-polished andirons, and the old brass tongs that would not meet.", "이제 불꽃이 사라진 잉걸불에서 한결같은 불그레한 빛이 뿜어 나와 벽난로의 옆면과 안쪽을, 또 반들반들 윤이 나는 장작 받침쇠와 끝이 맞물리지 않는 낡은 놋쇠 부집게를 그 빛깔로 물들였다."], ["The underside of the mantel-shelf was flushed with the high-coloured light, and the legs of the table nearest the fire.", "벽난로 선반의 아랫면도 그 짙은 빛으로 발그레해졌고, 불에 가장 가까운 식탁 다리들도 그러했다."], ["Tess’s face and neck reflected the same warmth, which each gem turned into an Aldebaran or a Sirius—a constellation of white, red, and green flashes, that interchanged their hues with her every pulsation.", "테스의 얼굴과 목도 같은 온기를 되비쳤고, 그 온기는 보석 하나하나를 알데바란이나 시리우스로 바꾸어 놓았다—흰빛, 붉은빛, 초록빛으로 번득이는 별자리가, 그녀의 맥박이 뛸 때마다 서로 빛깔을 바꾸었다."]], [["“Do you remember what we said to each other this morning about telling our faults?”", "\"우리 잘못을 털어놓자고 오늘 아침에 서로 했던 말, 기억하지?\""], ["he asked abruptly, finding that she still remained immovable.", "그녀가 여전히 꼼짝 않고 있는 것을 보고 그가 불쑥 물었다."], ["“We spoke lightly perhaps, and you may well have done so.", "\"우린 어쩌면 가볍게 말했고, 당신도 그랬을 법해."], ["But for me it was no light promise.", "하지만 나로서는 결코 가벼운 약속이 아니었어."], ["I want to make a confession to you, Love.”", "당신에게 고백하고 싶은 게 있어, 내 사랑.\""]], [["This, from him, so unexpectedly apposite, had the effect upon her of a Providential interposition.", "그에게서 나온 이 말이 너무도 뜻밖에 딱 들어맞아, 그것은 그녀에게 마치 하늘의 섭리가 끼어든 것 같은 작용을 했다."]], [["“You have to confess something?”", "\"당신이 고백할 게 있다고요?\""], ["she said quickly, and even with gladness and relief.", "그녀가 재빨리, 심지어 기쁨과 안도마저 담아 말했다."]], [["“You did not expect it?", "\"안 그러리라 생각했지?"], ["Ah—you thought too highly of me.", "아—당신은 나를 너무 높이 봤던 거야."], ["Now listen.", "자, 들어 봐."], ["Put your head there, because I want you to forgive me, and not to be indignant with me for not telling you before, as perhaps I ought to have done.”", "머리를 여기에 기대, 당신이 나를 용서해 주길, 그리고 진작 말했어야 했을 일을 진작 말하지 않은 나를 노여워하지 말아 주길 바라니까.\""]], [["How strange it was!", "이 얼마나 기이한 일인가!"], ["He seemed to be her double.", "그가 마치 자기의 분신 같았다."], ["She did not speak, and Clare went on—", "그녀는 말이 없었고, 클레어가 말을 이었다—"]], [["“I did not mention it because I was afraid of endangering my chance of you, darling, the great prize of my life—my Fellowship I call you.", "\"내가 그걸 말하지 않은 건, 당신을 얻을 기회가 위태로워질까 두려웠기 때문이야, 여보, 내 인생의 가장 큰 상인 당신을 말이지—나는 당신을 내 펠로십이라 부르잖아."], ["My brother’s Fellowship was won at his college, mine at Talbothays Dairy.", "내 형의 펠로십은 자기 대학에서 얻은 것이지만, 내 펠로십은 톨버세이즈 낙농장에서 얻은 거니까."], ["Well, I would not risk it.", "그래, 나는 그걸 걸 수 없었어."], ["I was going to tell you a month ago—at the time you agreed to be mine, but I could not; I thought it might frighten you away from me.", "한 달 전에 말하려 했지—당신이 내 사람이 되겠다고 했던 그때 말이야. 하지만 못 했어. 그게 당신을 놀라게 해서 멀어지게 할까 봐 두려웠거든."], ["I put it off; then I thought I would tell you yesterday, to give you a chance at least of escaping me.", "그래서 미뤘지. 그러고는 어제 말해서, 적어도 당신에게 나를 벗어날 기회라도 주려고 했어."], ["But I did not.", "하지만 못 했어."], ["And I did not this morning, when you proposed our confessing our faults on the landing—the sinner that I was! But I must, now I see you sitting there so solemnly.", "그리고 오늘 아침에도, 당신이 층계참에서 우리 잘못을 고백하자고 했을 때도 못 했지—죄인 같으니라고! 하지만 이제 당신이 거기 그토록 엄숙하게 앉아 있는 걸 보니, 말하지 않을 수 없군."], ["I wonder if you will forgive me?”", "당신이 나를 용서해 줄지 모르겠어.\""]], [["“O yes! I am sure that—”", "\"오 그럼요! 분명히—\""]], [["“Well, I hope so. But wait a minute.", "\"그래, 그러길 바라. 하지만 잠깐만."], ["You don’t know.", "당신은 아직 몰라."], ["To begin at the beginning.", "처음부터 이야기하지."], ["Though I imagine my poor father fears that I am one of the eternally lost for my doctrines, I am of course, a believer in good morals, Tess, as much as you.", "내 가엾은 아버지는 내 교리 때문에 나를 영영 길 잃은 자 중 하나로 여기시며 두려워하시지만, 나도 물론 선한 도덕을 믿는 사람이야, 테스, 당신만큼이나 말이야."], ["I used to wish to be a teacher of men, and it was a great disappointment to me when I found I could not enter the Church.", "나는 한때 사람들을 가르치는 사람이 되고 싶었고, 성직에 들어설 수 없다는 걸 알았을 때는 크게 실망했어."], ["I admired spotlessness, even though I could lay no claim to it, and hated impurity, as I hope I do now.", "나는 비록 흠 없음을 내 것이라 주장할 순 없었지만 흠 없음을 흠모했고, 부정함을 미워했어, 지금도 그러길 바라지."], ["Whatever one may think of plenary inspiration, one must heartily subscribe to these words of Paul: ‘Be thou an example—in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity.’ It is the only safeguard for us poor human beings.", "축자영감설을 어떻게 생각하든, 바울의 이 말씀에는 진심으로 동의하지 않을 수 없어. '너는 본이 되라—말과 행실과 사랑과 영과 믿음과 정결로.' 그것이 우리 가엾은 인간들에게 유일한 안전장치니까."], ["‘_Integer vitae_,’ says a Roman poet, who is strange company for St Paul—", "'온전한 삶을 사는 자는'이라고 한 로마 시인이 말했지, 성 바울과는 어울리지 않는 동무이긴 하지만—"]], [["The man of upright life, from frailties free, Stands not in need of Moorish spear or bow.", "올곧게 살며 허물에서 벗어난 사람은 무어인의 창이나 활도 필요로 하지 않으리."]], [["“Well, a certain place is paved with good intentions, and having felt all that so strongly, you will see what a terrible remorse it bred in me when, in the midst of my fine aims for other people, I myself fell.”", "\"그런데, 어떤 곳은 선한 의도로 포장되어 있다고들 하지. 나는 그 모든 것을 그토록 강하게 느꼈던 만큼, 다른 사람들을 위한 내 고매한 뜻 한가운데서 나 자신이 넘어졌을 때, 그것이 내 안에 얼마나 끔찍한 회한을 낳았는지 당신도 알게 될 거야.\""]], [["He then told her of that time of his life to which allusion has been made when, tossed about by doubts and difficulties in London, like a cork on the waves, he plunged into eight-and-forty hours’ dissipation with a stranger.", "그러고는 그는 자기 삶의 그 시절을 그녀에게 이야기했다. 앞서 언급된 적 있는 그 시절, 런던에서 의혹과 곤경에 파도 위의 코르크 마개처럼 이리저리 휩쓸리던 끝에, 한 낯선 여자와 마흔여덟 시간의 방탕에 빠져들었던 그 일을."]], [["“Happily I awoke almost immediately to a sense of my folly,” he continued.", "\"다행히 나는 거의 곧바로 내 어리석음을 깨달았어.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["“I would have no more to say to her, and I came home.", "\"그 여자와는 더 이상 상관하지 않으려고, 집으로 돌아왔지."], ["I have never repeated the offence.", "그 잘못을 다시 저지른 적은 없어."], ["But I felt I should like to treat you with perfect frankness and honour, and I could not do so without telling this.", "하지만 나는 당신을 완전한 솔직함과 명예로 대하고 싶었고, 이걸 말하지 않고는 그럴 수가 없었어."], ["Do you forgive me?”", "나를 용서하겠어?\""]], [["She pressed his hand tightly for an answer.", "그녀는 대답 삼아 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“Then we will dismiss it at once and for ever!—too painful as it is for the occasion—and talk of something lighter.”", "\"그럼 우린 이 일을 지금 당장, 그리고 영원히 떨쳐 버리는 거야!—이런 자리에 비하면 너무 괴로운 이야기니까—그리고 좀 더 가벼운 이야기를 하자고.\""]], [["“O, Angel—I am almost glad—because now _you_ can forgive _me_!", "\"오, 엔젤—저는 거의 기뻐요—이제 당신이 저를 용서해 줄 수 있을 테니까요!"], ["I have not made my confession.", "저는 아직 고백을 안 했어요."], ["I have a confession, too—remember, I said so.”", "저도 고백할 게 있어요—기억하시죠, 제가 그렇게 말했잖아요.\""]], [["“Ah, to be sure!", "\"아, 그렇지!"], ["Now then for it, wicked little one.”", "자 그럼 어디 들어 보자, 못된 작은 사람아.\""]], [["“Perhaps, although you smile, it is as serious as yours, or more so.”", "\"비록 당신은 웃지만, 어쩌면 그건 당신 것만큼 심각하거나, 그보다 더 심각할지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“It can hardly be more serious, dearest.”", "\"그게 더 심각하긴 어려울 거야, 여보.\""]], [["“It cannot—O no, it cannot!”", "\"그럴 수 없겠죠—오 그래요, 그럴 수 없을 거예요!\""], ["She jumped up joyfully at the hope.", "그녀는 그 희망에 기뻐하며 벌떡 일어났다."], ["“No, it cannot be more serious, certainly,”", "\"그래요, 분명 그게 더 심각할 순 없겠죠.\""], ["she cried, “because ’tis just the same!", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"왜냐하면 그건 꼭 똑같은 거니까요!"], ["I will tell you now.”", "이제 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["She sat down again.", "그녀는 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Their hands were still joined.", "둘의 손은 여전히 맞잡혀 있었다."], ["The ashes under the grate were lit by the fire vertically, like a torrid waste.", "쇠살대 아래의 재는 불빛에 수직으로 비쳐, 마치 타는 듯한 황무지 같았다."], ["Imagination might have beheld a Last Day luridness in this red-coaled glow, which fell on his face and hand, and on hers, peering into the loose hair about her brow, and firing the delicate skin underneath.", "상상력이라면 이 붉은 잉걸불의 빛 속에서 최후의 날 같은 무시무시함을 보았을 법했다. 그 빛은 그의 얼굴과 손에, 그리고 그녀의 그것에 떨어졌고, 그녀 이마 주위로 풀린 머리카락 속을 들여다보며 그 아래 고운 살결을 불태웠다. 그녀 형체의 커다란 그림자가 벽과 천장 위로 솟아올랐다."], ["A large shadow of her shape rose upon the wall and ceiling.", "그녀가 앞으로 몸을 숙이자, 목에 걸린 다이아몬드 하나하나가 두꺼비 눈처럼 불길하게 윙크했다."], ["She bent forward, at which each diamond on her neck gave a sinister wink like a toad’s; and pressing her forehead against his temple she entered on her story of her acquaintance with Alec d’Urberville and its results, murmuring the words without flinching, and with her eyelids drooping down. End of Phase the Fourth", "그리고 그녀는 그의 관자놀이에 자기 이마를 갖다 댄 채, 알렉 더버빌과의 인연과 그 결과에 관한 자기 이야기를 시작했다. 움츠러듦 없이 그 말들을 중얼거리며, 눈꺼풀을 아래로 떨군 채로."]], [["# XXXV", "# XXXV"]], [["The Woman Pays\nHer narrative ended; even its re-assertions and secondary explanations were done.", "그녀의 이야기가 끝났다. 거듭한 확언도, 곁들인 부연 설명도 다 끝났다."], ["Tess’s voice throughout had hardly risen higher than its opening tone; there had been no exculpatory phrase of any kind, and she had not wept.", "그동안 테스의 목소리는 처음 그 어조보다 좀처럼 높아진 적이 없었고, 어떤 변명의 말도 한마디 없었으며, 그녀는 울지도 않았다."]], [["But the complexion even of external things seemed to suffer transmutation as her announcement progressed.", "그러나 그녀의 고백이 진행되는 동안 바깥 사물들의 빛깔조차 변모를 겪는 듯했다."], ["The fire in the grate looked impish—demoniacally funny, as if it did not care in the least about her strait.", "쇠살대 속의 불은 짓궂게—악마처럼 익살맞게 보였으니, 마치 그녀의 곤경 따위는 조금도 개의치 않는 듯했다."], ["The fender grinned idly, as if it too did not care. The light from the water-bottle was merely engaged in a chromatic problem.", "난로 울도 하릴없이 히죽거렸으니, 마치 그 또한 개의치 않는 듯했다. 물병에서 나오는 빛은 그저 색채의 문제에만 골몰해 있었다."], ["All material objects around announced their irresponsibility with terrible iteration.", "주위의 모든 물질적 사물들이 무시무시한 되풀이로 자기들의 무책임함을 알렸다."], ["And yet nothing had changed since the moments when he had been kissing her; or rather, nothing in the substance of things.", "그런데도 그가 그녀에게 입맞추던 그 순간들 이래로 변한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 아니, 차라리 사물의 실체에서는 변한 것이 아무것도 없었다."], ["But the essence of things had changed.", "그러나 사물의 본질이 변해 버린 것이다."]], [["When she ceased, the auricular impressions from their previous endearments seemed to hustle away into the corner of their brains, repeating themselves as echoes from a time of supremely purblind foolishness.", "그녀가 말을 멈추자, 앞서 나누었던 애정 어린 말들의 청각적 인상이 그들의 뇌 한구석으로 황급히 밀려나, 더없이 눈먼 어리석음의 시절에서 들려오는 메아리처럼 스스로를 되풀이하는 듯했다."]], [["Clare performed the irrelevant act of stirring the fire; the intelligence had not even yet got to the bottom of him.", "클레어는 불을 휘젓는 엉뚱한 행동을 했다. 그 깨달음은 아직 그의 밑바닥까지 가닿지도 못한 터였다."], ["After stirring the embers he rose to his feet; all the force of her disclosure had imparted itself now.", "잉걸불을 휘저은 뒤 그는 자리에서 일어섰다. 이제 그녀의 고백이 지닌 온 힘이 그에게 전해진 것이다."], ["His face had withered. In the strenuousness of his concentration he treadled fitfully on the floor.", "그의 얼굴은 시들어 있었다. 골똘히 집중하느라 그는 발을 마룻바닥에 띄엄띄엄 굴렀다."], ["He could not, by any contrivance, think closely enough; that was the meaning of his vague movement.", "어떤 방도로도 그는 충분히 빈틈없이 생각해 낼 수가 없었다. 그것이 그 막연한 움직임의 뜻이었다."], ["When he spoke it was in the most inadequate, commonplace voice of the many varied tones she had heard from him.", "입을 열었을 때 그것은 그녀가 그에게서 들어 온 그 갖가지 다양한 어조들 가운데 가장 어설프고 가장 평범한 목소리였다."]], [["“Tess!”", "\"테스!\""]], [["“Yes, dearest.”", "\"네, 여보.\""]], [["“Am I to believe this?", "\"내가 이걸 믿어야 하나?"], ["From your manner I am to take it as true.", "당신 태도로 보아 이걸 사실로 받아들여야겠군."], ["O you cannot be out of your mind!", "오 당신이 제정신이 아닐 리는 없잖아!"], ["You ought to be!", "차라리 그래야 할 텐데!"], ["Yet you are not... My wife, my Tess—nothing in you warrants such a supposition as that?”", "그런데 당신은 그렇지 않아... 내 아내, 내 테스—당신 안의 그 어떤 것도 그런 가정을 뒷받침하지 않는데?\""]], [["“I am not out of my mind,”", "\"저는 제정신이 아닌 게 아니에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“And yet—”", "\"그런데도—\""], ["He looked vacantly at her, to resume with dazed senses: “Why didn’t you tell me before?", "그는 멍하니 그녀를 바라보다가, 얼떨떨한 감각으로 말을 이었다. \"왜 진작 내게 말하지 않았지?"], ["Ah, yes, you would have told me, in a way—but I hindered you, I remember!”", "아, 그래, 당신은 어떤 식으로든 말하려고 했었지—그런데 내가 막았어, 기억나는군!\""]], [["These and other of his words were nothing but the perfunctory babble of the surface while the depths remained paralyzed.", "이 말들도, 그의 다른 말들도, 표면의 형식적인 횡설수설에 지나지 않았으니, 그 밑바닥은 마비된 채 그대로였다."], ["He turned away, and bent over a chair.", "그는 돌아서서 의자 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["Tess followed him to the middle of the room, where he was, and stood there staring at him with eyes that did not weep.", "테스는 그가 있는 방 한가운데까지 그를 뒤따라가, 거기 서서 눈물 흘리지 않는 눈으로 그를 빤히 바라보았다."], ["Presently she slid down upon her knees beside his foot, and from this position she crouched in a heap.", "이윽고 그녀는 그의 발치로 무릎을 꿇으며 미끄러지듯 주저앉아, 그 자세 그대로 한 덩이로 웅크렸다."]], [["“In the name of our love, forgive me!”", "\"우리 사랑의 이름으로, 저를 용서해 주세요!\""], ["she whispered with a dry mouth.", "그녀가 바싹 마른 입으로 속삭였다."], ["“I have forgiven you for the same!”", "\"저는 똑같은 일로 당신을 용서했잖아요!\""]], [["And, as he did not answer, she said again—", "그리고 그가 대답하지 않자, 그녀는 다시 말했다—"]], [["“Forgive me as you are forgiven!", "\"당신이 용서받았듯 저를 용서해 주세요!"], ["_I_ forgive _you_, Angel.” “You—yes, you do.”", "저는 당신을 용서해요, 엔젤.\" \"당신을—그래요, 당신은 용서하는군요.\""]], [["“But you do not forgive me?”", "\"하지만 당신은 날 용서하지 않는군요?\""]], [["“O Tess, forgiveness does not apply to the case!", "\"오 테스, 이 경우엔 용서가 통하질 않아!"], ["You were one person; now you are another.", "당신은 한 사람이었는데, 이제 당신은 다른 사람이야."], ["My God—how can forgiveness meet such a grotesque—prestidigitation as that!”", "맙소사—그런 기괴한—요술 같은 것에 어떻게 용서가 통할 수 있겠어!\""]], [["He paused, contemplating this definition; then suddenly broke into horrible laughter—as unnatural and ghastly as a laugh in hell.", "그는 말을 멈추고 이 정의를 곱씹었다. 그러다 별안간 끔찍한 웃음을 터뜨렸다—지옥에서 터지는 웃음처럼 부자연스럽고 섬뜩한 웃음을."]], [["“Don’t—don’t!", "\"그만—그만요!"], ["It kills me quite, that!”", "그게 저를 아주 죽여요, 그 웃음이!\""], ["she shrieked.", "그녀가 비명을 질렀다."], ["“O have mercy upon me—have mercy!”", "\"오 제게 자비를 베풀어 주세요—자비를요!\""]], [["He did not answer; and, sickly white, she jumped up.", "그는 대답하지 않았다. 그러자 병자처럼 핏기 가신 얼굴로 그녀가 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Angel, Angel!", "\"엔젤, 엔젤!"], ["what do you mean by that laugh?”", "그 웃음은 무슨 뜻이에요?\""], ["she cried out.", "그녀가 소리쳤다."], ["“Do you know what this is to me?”", "\"이게 제게 어떤 건지 아세요?\""]], [["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“I have been hoping, longing, praying, to make you happy!", "\"저는 당신을 행복하게 해 드리길 바라고, 갈망하고, 기도해 왔어요!"], ["I have thought what joy it will be to do it, what an unworthy wife I shall be if I do not!", "그렇게 하는 게 얼마나 큰 기쁨일지, 그렇게 못 하면 제가 얼마나 자격 없는 아내일지 생각해 왔다고요!"], ["That’s what I have felt, Angel!”", "그게 제가 느껴 온 거예요, 엔젤!\""]], [["“I know that.”", "\"그건 알아.\""]], [["“I thought, Angel, that you loved me—me, my very self!", "\"엔젤, 저는 당신이 저를 사랑한다고 생각했어요—저를, 바로 저 자신을요!"], ["If it is I you do love, O how can it be that you look and speak so?", "당신이 사랑하는 게 저라면, 오 어떻게 그렇게 바라보고 말할 수가 있나요? 무서워요!"], ["It frightens me! Having begun to love you, I love you for ever—in all changes, in all disgraces, because you are yourself.", "한번 당신을 사랑하기 시작했으니, 저는 영원히 당신을 사랑해요—어떤 변화 속에서도, 어떤 치욕 속에서도요, 당신은 당신 자신이니까요. 저는 더 바라지 않아요."], ["I ask no more. Then how can you, O my own husband, stop loving me?”", "그런데 어떻게 당신이, 오 나의 남편이, 저를 사랑하기를 멈출 수가 있나요?\""]], [["“I repeat, the woman I have been loving is not you.”", "\"거듭 말하지만, 내가 사랑해 온 그 여자는 당신이 아니야.\""]], [["“But who?”", "\"하지만 누구죠?\""]], [["“Another woman in your shape.”", "\"당신 모습을 한 다른 여자야.\""]], [["She perceived in his words the realization of her own apprehensive foreboding in former times.", "그녀는 그의 말에서, 예전에 자기가 품었던 불안한 예감이 그대로 실현된 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He looked upon her as a species of imposter; a guilty woman in the guise of an innocent one.", "그는 그녀를 일종의 사기꾼으로—순결한 여자의 탈을 쓴 죄 있는 여자로 보고 있었다."], ["Terror was upon her white face as she saw it; her cheek was flaccid, and her mouth had almost the aspect of a round little hole.", "그것을 깨닫는 순간 그녀의 창백한 얼굴에 공포가 어렸다. 그녀의 뺨은 축 늘어졌고, 입은 거의 동그란 작은 구멍 같은 모양이 되었다."], ["The horrible sense of his view of her so deadened her that she staggered, and he stepped forward, thinking she was going to fall.", "그가 자기를 그렇게 본다는 그 끔찍한 느낌이 어찌나 그녀를 마비시켰는지, 그녀가 비틀거리자 그는 그녀가 쓰러지려는 줄 알고 한 발 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“Sit down, sit down,” he said gently.", "\"앉아, 앉아.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“You are ill; and it is natural that you should be.”", "\"당신은 아파. 그러는 게 당연하지.\""]], [["She did sit down, without knowing where she was, that strained look still upon her face, and her eyes such as to make his flesh creep.", "그녀는 자기가 어디 있는지도 모른 채 정말로 앉았다. 그 긴장된 표정이 여전히 얼굴에 어린 채로, 그 눈은 그의 살갗에 소름이 끼치게 할 만한 것이었다."]], [["“I don’t belong to you any more, then; do I, Angel?”", "\"그럼 저는 이제 당신 사람이 아닌 거죠, 그렇죠, 엔젤?\""], ["she asked helplessly.", "그녀가 어쩔 줄 모르며 물었다."], ["“It is not me, but another woman like me that he loved, he says.”", "\"그가 사랑한 건 제가 아니라, 저를 닮은 다른 여자라고 하니까요.\""]], [["The image raised caused her to take pity upon herself as one who was ill-used.", "이렇게 떠오른 자기 모습에, 그녀는 부당하게 학대받은 사람으로서 자기 자신을 가엾이 여기게 되었다."], ["Her eyes filled as she regarded her position further; she turned round and burst into a flood of self-sympathetic tears.", "자기 처지를 더 깊이 헤아리자 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 가득 찼다. 그녀는 돌아앉아 자기를 가엾어하는 눈물을 봇물처럼 터뜨렸다."]], [["Clare was relieved at this change, for the effect on her of what had happened was beginning to be a trouble to him only less than the woe of the disclosure itself.", "클레어는 이 변화에 안도했다. 일어난 일이 그녀에게 미친 영향이 그에게는 점차, 고백 그 자체의 비통함에 못지않은 근심거리가 되어 가던 참이었기 때문이다."], ["He waited patiently, apathetically, till the violence of her grief had worn itself out, and her rush of weeping had lessened to a catching gasp at intervals.", "그는 그녀의 슬픔이 격렬함을 다 소진하고, 봇물 같던 울음이 이따금 숨이 막히는 흐느낌으로 잦아들 때까지, 참을성 있게, 무심하게 기다렸다."]], [["“Angel,”", "\"엔젤.\""], ["she said suddenly, in her natural tones, the insane, dry voice of terror having left her now.", "그녀가 별안간, 본래의 어조로 말했다. 공포에 질려 미친 듯 메마르던 목소리는 이제 사라지고 없었다."], ["“Angel, am I too wicked for you and me to live together?”", "\"엔젤, 제가 너무 사악해서 당신과 제가 함께 살 수 없는 건가요?\""]], [["“I have not been able to think what we can do.”", "\"우리가 어떻게 해야 할지 나도 도무지 생각이 떠오르질 않아.\""]], [["“I shan’t ask you to let me live with you, Angel, because I have no right to!", "\"저와 함께 살게 해 달라고 청하지 않을게요, 엔젤, 제겐 그럴 권리가 없으니까요!"], ["I shall not write to mother and sisters to say we be married, as I said I would do; and I shan’t finish the good hussif I cut out and meant to make while we were in lodgings.”", "하겠다고 했던 대로 어머니와 동생들에게 우리가 결혼했다는 편지를 쓰지도 않을게요. 그리고 우리가 숙소에 머무는 동안 만들려고 마름질해 둔 그 좋은 반짇고리도 마저 만들지 않을게요.\""]], [["“Shan’t you?”", "\"안 만든다고?\""]], [["“No, I shan’t do anything, unless you order me to; and if you go away from me I shall not follow ’ee; and if you never speak to me any more I shall not ask why, unless you tell me I may.”", "\"네, 아무것도 하지 않을게요, 당신이 시키지 않는 한요. 그리고 당신이 저를 떠나가더라도 저는 따라가지 않을게요. 당신이 다시는 제게 말을 걸지 않더라도 그 까닭을 묻지 않을게요, 당신이 물어도 된다고 하지 않는 한요.\""]], [["“And if I order you to do anything?”", "\"그러면 내가 너에게 무언가를 하라고 시킨다면?\""]], [["“I will obey you like your wretched slave, even if it is to lie down and die.”", "\"당신의 비참한 노예처럼 따를게요, 그게 설령 누워 죽으라는 것일지라도요.\""]], [["“You are very good.", "\"당신은 참 착하군."], ["But it strikes me that there is a want of harmony between your present mood of self-sacrifice and your past mood of self-preservation.”", "하지만 내가 보기엔 지금의 그 자기희생적인 마음씨와, 지난날의 그 자기보존의 마음씨 사이엔 조화가 없는 것 같은데.\""]], [["These were the first words of antagonism.", "이것이 적의가 담긴 첫마디였다."], ["To fling elaborate sarcasms at Tess, however, was much like flinging them at a dog or cat.", "그러나 테스에게 공들인 빈정거림을 던지는 것은 개나 고양이에게 던지는 것이나 매한가지였다."], ["The charms of their subtlety passed by her unappreciated, and she only received them as inimical sounds which meant that anger ruled.", "그 교묘함의 묘미는 그녀가 음미하지 못한 채 그냥 지나가 버렸고, 그녀는 그것을 그저 노여움이 지배하고 있음을 뜻하는 적대적인 소리로만 받아들였다."], ["She remained mute, not knowing that he was smothering his affection for her.", "그녀는 입을 다물고 있었으니, 그가 자기를 향한 애정을 억누르고 있다는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["She hardly observed that a tear descended slowly upon his cheek, a tear so large that it magnified the pores of the skin over which it rolled, like the object lens of a microscope.", "그녀는 눈물 한 방울이 그의 뺨을 타고 천천히 흘러내리는 것조차 거의 보지 못했다. 그 눈물이 어찌나 컸던지, 마치 현미경의 대물렌즈처럼, 굴러 내리는 그 살갗의 땀구멍들을 확대해 보였다."], ["Meanwhile reillumination as to the terrible and total change that her confession had wrought in his life, in his universe, returned to him, and he tried desperately to advance among the new conditions in which he stood.", "그러는 사이, 그녀의 고백이 그의 삶에, 그의 우주에 일으킨 그 끔찍하고도 전면적인 변화에 대한 깨달음이 다시 그에게 돌아왔고, 그는 자기가 처한 그 새로운 상황 속을 헤쳐 나아가려 필사적으로 애썼다."], ["Some consequent action was necessary; yet what?", "그에 따른 어떤 행동이 필요했다. 그런데 무엇을?"]], [["“Tess,”", "\"테스.\""], ["he said, as gently as he could speak, “I cannot stay—in this room—just now.", "그는 할 수 있는 한 부드럽게 말했다. \"나는—이 방에—지금은 있을 수가 없어."], ["I will walk out a little way.”", "잠깐 밖에 좀 나갔다 오겠어.\""]], [["He quietly left the room, and the two glasses of wine that he had poured out for their supper—one for her, one for him—remained on the table untasted.", "그는 조용히 방을 나갔고, 저녁 식사를 위해 따라 두었던 포도주 두 잔은—하나는 그녀를 위해, 하나는 그를 위해—손도 대지 않은 채 식탁 위에 남았다."], ["This was what their _Agape_ had come to.", "둘의 애찬은 결국 이렇게 되고 만 것이다."], ["At tea, two or three hours earlier, they had, in the freakishness of affection, drunk from one cup.", "두어 시간 전 차를 마실 때만 해도 둘은 애정의 변덕스러운 장난기로 한 잔으로 나누어 마셨건만."]], [["The closing of the door behind him, gently as it had been pulled to, roused Tess from her stupor.", "아무리 살며시 닫혔다 한들, 그의 등 뒤로 문이 닫히는 소리가 테스를 망연한 상태에서 깨워 냈다."], ["He was gone; she could not stay.", "그가 가 버린 것이다."], ["Hastily flinging her cloak around her she opened the door and followed, putting out the candles as if she were never coming back.", "그녀는 머물 수 없었다. 황급히 외투를 두르고 그녀는 문을 열고 그를 뒤따랐다. 다시는 돌아오지 않을 사람처럼 촛불을 꺼 버린 채로."], ["The rain was over and the night was now clear.", "비는 그쳤고, 밤은 이제 맑게 개어 있었다."]], [["She was soon close at his heels, for Clare walked slowly and without purpose.", "그녀는 이내 그의 바로 뒤까지 따라붙었으니, 클레어가 정처 없이 느릿느릿 걷고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His form beside her light gray figure looked black, sinister, and forbidding, and she felt as sarcasm the touch of the jewels of which she had been momentarily so proud.", "그녀의 옅은 잿빛 형체 곁에서 그의 형체는 검고, 불길하고, 위압적으로 보였고, 한순간 그토록 자랑스러워했던 그 보석들의 감촉이 그녀에게는 비아냥처럼 느껴졌다."], ["Clare turned at hearing her footsteps, but his recognition of her presence seemed to make no difference to him, and he went on over the five yawning arches of the great bridge in front of the house.", "클레어는 그녀의 발소리를 듣고 돌아섰지만, 그녀가 있다는 것을 알아챈 것이 그에게는 아무 차이도 없는 듯, 집 앞에 놓인 큰 다리의 입을 떡 벌린 다섯 아치를 넘어 계속 걸어갔다."]], [["The cow and horse tracks in the road were full of water, the rain having been enough to charge them, but not enough to wash them away.", "길 위의 소와 말 발자국에는 물이 가득 차 있었으니, 비가 그것들을 채울 만큼은 내렸으나 씻어 낼 만큼은 아니었던 것이다."], ["Across these minute pools the reflected stars flitted in a quick transit as she passed; she would not have known they were shining overhead if she had not seen them there—the vastest things of the universe imaged in objects so mean.", "그녀가 지나가자, 이 자그마한 웅덩이들 위로 비친 별들이 빠르게 스쳐 지나며 가물거렸다. 그것들이 거기 비친 것을 보지 않았더라면, 그녀는 머리 위에서 그것들이 빛나고 있다는 것조차 몰랐으리라—우주에서 가장 광대한 것들이 그토록 보잘것없는 것들 속에 비쳐 있었다."]], [["The place to which they had travelled to-day was in the same valley as Talbothays, but some miles lower down the river; and the surroundings being open, she kept easily in sight of him.", "그들이 오늘 여행해 온 곳은 톨버세이즈와 같은 골짜기에 있었으나, 강을 따라 몇 마일 더 아래쪽이었다. 그리고 주위가 탁 트여 있어 그녀는 쉽사리 그를 시야에 둘 수 있었다."], ["Away from the house the road wound through the meads, and along these she followed Clare without any attempt to come up with him or to attract him, but with dumb and vacant fidelity.", "집에서 멀어지자 길은 풀밭 사이로 굽이굽이 이어졌고, 그녀는 그 길을 따라 클레어를 뒤따랐다. 그를 따라잡으려는, 또는 그의 주의를 끌려는 어떤 시도도 없이, 다만 말없이 텅 빈 충실함으로."]], [["At last, however, her listless walk brought her up alongside him, and still he said nothing.", "그러나 마침내 그녀의 무기력한 걸음이 그녀를 그의 곁에까지 데려갔는데도, 그는 여전히 아무 말이 없었다."], ["The cruelty of fooled honesty is often great after enlightenment, and it was mighty in Clare now.", "속아 넘어간 정직함의 잔인함은 진상을 알고 난 뒤에 흔히 큰 법인데, 지금 클레어의 경우 그것은 막강했다."], ["The outdoor air had apparently taken away from him all tendency to act on impulse; she knew that he saw her without irradiation—in all her bareness; that Time was chanting his satiric psalm at her then—", "바깥 공기는 그에게서 충동적으로 행동하려는 경향을 죄다 앗아 가 버린 듯했다. 그가 자기를 아무런 광채도 없이—온전히 헐벗은 모습 그대로 보고 있다는 것을, 그리고 시간이 그때 자기를 향해 그 풍자의 시편을 읊조리고 있다는 것을 그녀는 알았다—"]], [["Behold, when thy face is made bare, he that loved thee shall hate; Thy face shall be no more fair at the fall of thy fate.", "보라, 그대의 얼굴이 헐벗을 때, 그대를 사랑하던 자가 미워하리라. 그대 운명이 기울 때 그대의 얼굴은 더 이상 곱지 않으리."], ["For thy life shall fall as a leaf and be shed as the rain; And the veil of thine head shall be grief, and the crown shall be pain.", "그대의 삶은 잎새처럼 떨어져 비처럼 흩날리리니, 그대 머리의 너울은 슬픔이 되고, 그 면류관은 고통이 되리라."]], [["He was still intently thinking, and her companionship had now insufficient power to break or divert the strain of thought.", "그는 여전히 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 이제 그녀가 곁에 있다는 것도 그 생각의 흐름을 끊거나 돌릴 만한 힘이 모자랐다."], ["What a weak thing her presence must have become to him!", "그에게는 그녀의 존재가 얼마나 미약한 것이 되어 버렸을까!"], ["She could not help addressing Clare.", "그녀는 클레어에게 말을 걸지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“What have I done—what _have_ I done!", "\"제가 무얼 했나요—제가 대체 무얼 했나요!"], ["I have not told of anything that interferes with or belies my love for you.", "저는 당신을 향한 제 사랑을 방해하거나 거짓으로 만드는 것은 하나도 말하지 않았어요."], ["You don’t think I planned it, do you?", "제가 그걸 꾸미기라도 했다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?"], ["It is in your own mind what you are angry at, Angel; it is not in me.", "당신이 노여워하는 건 당신 자신의 마음속에 있는 거예요, 엔젤. 제 안에 있는 게 아니라요."], ["O, it is not in me, and I am not that deceitful woman you think me!”", "오, 그건 제 안에 있지 않아요, 그리고 저는 당신이 생각하는 그런 거짓된 여자가 아니에요!\""]], [["“H’m—well.", "\"음—글쎄."], ["Not deceitful, my wife; but not the same.", "거짓되진 않지, 내 아내여. 하지만 같은 사람은 아니야."], ["No, not the same.", "아니, 같은 사람이 아니야."], ["But do not make me reproach you.", "하지만 나로 하여금 당신을 책망하게 만들진 마."], ["I have sworn that I will not; and I will do everything to avoid it.”", "나는 그러지 않겠다고 맹세했고, 그러지 않으려고 무엇이든 하겠어.\""]], [["But she went on pleading in her distraction; and perhaps said things that would have been better left to silence.", "그러나 그녀는 정신없이 애원을 이어 갔다. 그리고 어쩌면 차라리 침묵에 남겨 두는 편이 나았을 말들을 했다."]], [["“Angel!—Angel!", "\"엔젤!—엔젤!"], ["I was a child—a child when it happened!", "저는 어린아이였어요—그 일이 일어났을 때 어린아이였다고요!"], ["I knew nothing of men.”", "저는 남자에 대해 아무것도 몰랐어요.\""]], [["“You were more sinned against than sinning, that I admit.”", "\"당신은 죄를 지었다기보다 죄를 당한 쪽이었어, 그건 인정해.\""]], [["“Then will you not forgive me?”", "\"그럼 저를 용서하지 않으시겠어요?\""]], [["“I do forgive you, but forgiveness is not all.”", "\"용서는 해. 하지만 용서가 다는 아니야.\""]], [["“And love me?”", "\"그럼 저를 사랑해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["To this question he did not answer.", "이 물음에 그는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“O Angel—my mother says that it sometimes happens so!—she knows several cases where they were worse than I, and the husband has not minded it much—has got over it at least. And yet the woman had not loved him as I do you!”", "\"오 엔젤—어머니 말씀이, 가끔 그런 일이 일어난다고 하셨어요!—어머니는 저보다 더한 경우도 여럿 아시는데, 남편이 그걸 그리 마음에 두지 않았다고요—적어도 극복했다고요. 게다가 그 여자들은 제가 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 남편을 사랑하지도 않았는데 말이에요!\""]], [["“Don’t, Tess; don’t argue.", "\"그만, 테스. 따지지 마."], ["Different societies, different manners.", "사회가 다르면 풍습도 다른 거야."], ["You almost make me say you are an unapprehending peasant woman, who have never been initiated into the proportions of social things. You don’t know what you say.”", "당신은 거의 나로 하여금, 당신이 사회의 이치를 한 번도 깨쳐 본 적 없는 무지한 시골 여자라고 말하게 만드는군. 당신은 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 몰라.\""]], [["“I am only a peasant by position, not by nature!”", "「난 처지가 농사꾼일 뿐이지, 천성까지 그런 건 아니에요!」"]], [["She spoke with an impulse to anger, but it went as it came.", "테스는 발끈하려는 충동에 사로잡혀 말했지만, 그 충동은 일었던 만큼 빠르게 가라앉았다."]], [["“So much the worse for you. I think that parson who unearthed your pedigree would have done better if he had held his tongue.", "「그럴수록 너한테는 더 안된 일이지. 네 가계를 파헤친 그 목사도 차라리 입을 다물고 있었으면 좋았을 거다."], ["I cannot help associating your decline as a family with this other fact—of your want of firmness.", "나는 너의 집안이 몰락한 사실과 또 하나의 사실—네 의지가 굳세지 못하다는 사실을 연결 짓지 않을 수가 없어."], ["Decrepit families imply decrepit wills, decrepit conduct.", "쇠락한 집안은 쇠락한 의지를, 쇠락한 행실을 뜻하는 법이지."], ["Heaven, why did you give me a handle for despising you more by informing me of your descent!", "맙소사, 네 혈통을 굳이 알려서, 너를 더 경멸할 빌미를 내게 쥐여 주다니!"], ["Here was I thinking you a new-sprung child of nature; there were you, the belated seedling of an effete aristocracy!”", "나는 너를 자연에서 갓 솟아난 아이로 여겼는데, 알고 보니 너는 무력해진 귀족의 뒤늦은 씨앗이었구나!」"]], [["“Lots of families are as bad as mine in that! Retty’s family were once large landowners, and so were Dairyman Billett’s.", "「그렇게 따지면 우리 집안만큼 형편없는 집안이 수두룩해요! 레티네도 한때는 대지주였고, 빌릿 낙농가도 그랬어요."], ["And the Debbyhouses, who now are carters, were once the De Bayeux family.", "지금은 짐마차꾼인 데비하우스 집안도 옛날엔 드바이외 집안이었고요."], ["You find such as I everywhere; ’tis a feature of our county, and I can’t help it.”", "나 같은 사람은 어디에나 있어요. 이건 우리 고장의 특징인 걸요, 나로서도 어쩔 수 없는 일이에요.」"]], [["“So much the worse for the county.”", "「그럴수록 그 고장한테 더 안된 일이지.」"]], [["She took these reproaches in their bulk simply, not in their particulars; he did not love her as he had loved her hitherto, and to all else she was indifferent.", "테스는 이 비난들을 낱낱이 따지지 않고 그저 덩어리째로 받아들였다. 그가 더는 이제껏처럼 자신을 사랑하지 않는다는 것, 그 한 가지 외에 다른 모든 것에 그녀는 무심했다."]], [["They wandered on again in silence.", "두 사람은 다시 말없이 정처 없이 걸었다."], ["It was said afterwards that a cottager of Wellbridge, who went out late that night for a doctor, met two lovers in the pastures, walking very slowly, without converse, one behind the other, as in a funeral procession, and the glimpse that he obtained of their faces seemed to denote that they were anxious and sad.", "훗날 전해진 이야기로는, 그날 밤 늦게 의사를 부르러 나선 웰브리지의 한 오두막 사람이 목초지에서 두 연인을 만났는데, 둘은 아주 천천히 걷고 있었고, 말 한마디 없이, 마치 장례 행렬처럼 하나가 다른 하나의 뒤를 따르고 있었으며, 얼핏 본 그들의 얼굴은 근심에 차고 슬퍼 보였다고 한다."], ["Returning later, he passed them again in the same field, progressing just as slowly, and as regardless of the hour and of the cheerless night as before.", "나중에 돌아오는 길에 그는 같은 들판에서 또 그들을 지나쳤는데, 여전히 그처럼 느릿느릿 나아가고 있었고, 시각이나 음산한 밤은 아랑곳하지 않는 모습이 아까와 똑같았다."], ["It was only on account of his preoccupation with his own affairs, and the illness in his house, that he did not bear in mind the curious incident, which, however, he recalled a long while after.", "오직 자기 일로 머릿속이 가득했던 데다 집안에 환자가 있었던 탓에, 그는 이 기묘한 광경을 마음에 담아 두지 못했지만, 한참 뒤에야 그것을 떠올렸다."]], [["During the interval of the cottager’s going and coming, she had said to her husband—", "그 오두막 사람이 오가는 사이에 테스는 남편에게 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["“I don’t see how I can help being the cause of much misery to you all your life.", "「내가 평생 당신에게 큰 불행의 원인이 되는 걸 어떻게 막을 수 있을지 모르겠어요."], ["The river is down there.", "저기 아래 강이 있어요."], ["I can put an end to myself in it.", "거기서 스스로 끝낼 수 있어요."], ["I am not afraid.”", "난 무섭지 않아요.」"]], [["“I don’t wish to add murder to my other follies,”", "「나는 내 다른 어리석음에 살인까지 보태고 싶지는 않아.」"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I will leave something to show that I did it myself—on account of my shame.", "「내가 스스로 한 일이라는 걸—내 수치 때문이었다는 걸—보여 주는 무언가를 남겨 둘게요."], ["They will not blame you then.”", "그러면 사람들이 당신을 탓하지 않을 거예요.」"]], [["“Don’t speak so absurdly—I wish not to hear it.", "「그런 터무니없는 소리 하지 마—듣고 싶지 않아."], ["It is nonsense to have such thoughts in this kind of case, which is rather one for satirical laughter than for tragedy.", "이런 종류의 일에 그런 생각을 하다니 말도 안 되는 소리야. 이건 비극이라기보다 차라리 비웃을 거리에 가까운 일이라고."], ["You don’t in the least understand the quality of the mishap.", "너는 이 불행의 성질을 조금도 이해하지 못해."], ["It would be viewed in the light of a joke by nine-tenths of the world if it were known.", "만약 알려진다면 세상 사람 열에 아홉은 그저 농담거리로 여길 일이야."], ["Please oblige me by returning to the house, and going to bed.”", "제발 부탁이니 집으로 돌아가서 자도록 해.」"]], [["“I will,”", "「그럴게요.」"], ["said she dutifully.", "그녀가 고분고분 말했다."]], [["They had rambled round by a road which led to the well-known ruins of the Cistercian abbey behind the mill, the latter having, in centuries past, been attached to the monastic establishment.", "두 사람은 방앗간 뒤편, 잘 알려진 시토 수도원 폐허로 이어지는 길을 빙 돌아 걸어왔는데, 그 방앗간은 수백 년 전 그 수도원 시설에 딸려 있던 것이었다. 방앗간은 여전히 돌아가고 있었다."], ["The mill still worked on, food being a perennial necessity; the abbey had perished, creeds being transient.", "먹을 것은 언제까지나 없어서는 안 될 것이므로. 수도원은 스러졌다. 신조는 덧없는 것이므로."], ["One continually sees the ministration of the temporary outlasting the ministration of the eternal.", "사람은 끊임없이 본다. 한때뿐인 것에 바쳐진 봉사가 영원한 것에 바쳐진 봉사보다 더 오래 살아남는 것을."], ["Their walk having been circuitous, they were still not far from the house, and in obeying his direction she only had to reach the large stone bridge across the main river and follow the road for a few yards.", "산책길이 빙 돌아온 터라 두 사람은 아직 집에서 그리 멀지 않았고, 그의 지시를 따르려면 그녀는 본류를 가로지르는 큰 돌다리에 닿아 몇 야드만 길을 따라가면 되었다."], ["When she got back, everything remained as she had left it, the fire being still burning.", "그녀가 돌아왔을 때, 모든 것이 떠날 때 그대로였고, 불은 아직 타고 있었다."], ["She did not stay downstairs for more than a minute, but proceeded to her chamber, whither the luggage had been taken.", "그녀는 아래층에 일 분도 채 머물지 않고 곧장 자기 방으로 올라갔는데, 짐은 그리로 옮겨져 있었다."], ["Here she sat down on the edge of the bed, looking blankly around, and presently began to undress.", "그녀는 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉아 멍하니 주위를 둘러보다가, 이윽고 옷을 벗기 시작했다."], ["In removing the light towards the bedstead its rays fell upon the tester of white dimity; something was hanging beneath it, and she lifted the candle to see what it was.", "등불을 침대 쪽으로 가져가자 그 빛이 흰 무명 천개에 비쳤는데, 그 아래 무언가가 매달려 있어, 그녀는 그게 무엇인지 보려고 촛불을 들어 올렸다."], ["A bough of mistletoe.", "겨우살이 가지였다."], ["Angel had put it there; she knew that in an instant.", "에인절이 거기 매달아 둔 것이었다. 그녀는 단번에 알아챘다."], ["This was the explanation of that mysterious parcel which it had been so difficult to pack and bring; whose contents he would not explain to her, saying that time would soon show her the purpose thereof.", "꾸려서 가져오기가 그토록 어려웠던 그 수수께끼 같은 꾸러미의 정체가 바로 이것이었다. 그는 그 속에 든 것을 그녀에게 설명하려 하지 않으면서, 머지않아 시간이 그 용도를 알려 줄 거라고만 했었다."], ["In his zest and his gaiety he had hung it there.", "들뜬 마음에, 흥겨운 기분에 그는 그것을 거기 매달아 둔 것이었다."], ["How foolish and inopportune that mistletoe looked now.", "지금 그 겨우살이는 어쩌면 그리 어리석고 때아니게 보이던지."]], [["Having nothing more to fear, having scarce anything to hope, for that he would relent there seemed no promise whatever, she lay down dully.", "이제 더 두려워할 것도 없고, 바랄 것도 거의 없었으므로—그가 마음을 누그러뜨리리라는 약속은 어디에도 없는 듯했으므로—그녀는 맥없이 자리에 누웠다."], ["When sorrow ceases to be speculative, sleep sees her opportunity.", "슬픔이 더는 골똘함이 아니게 될 때, 잠은 제 기회를 엿본다."], ["Among so many happier moods which forbid repose this was a mood which welcomed it, and in a few minutes the lonely Tess forgot existence, surrounded by the aromatic stillness of the chamber that had once, possibly, been the bride-chamber of her own ancestry.", "안식을 가로막는 그 숱한 행복한 마음결 가운데, 이것은 안식을 반기는 마음결이었으니, 몇 분 만에 외로운 테스는 존재를 잊었다. 어쩌면 한때 제 조상들의 신방이었을지도 모를 그 방의, 향내 어린 고요에 둘러싸인 채."]], [["Later on that night Clare also retraced his steps to the house.", "그날 밤 늦게 클레어 역시 집으로 발길을 되돌렸다."], ["Entering softly to the sitting-room he obtained a light, and with the manner of one who had considered his course he spread his rugs upon the old horse-hair sofa which stood there, and roughly shaped it to a sleeping-couch.", "거실로 살그머니 들어가 불을 밝히고는, 제 갈 길을 곰곰이 따져 본 사람다운 태도로, 거기 놓인 낡은 말총 소파 위에 깔개를 펼쳐 대충 잠자리 모양으로 다듬었다."], ["Before lying down he crept shoeless upstairs, and listened at the door of her apartment.", "눕기 전에 그는 신을 벗고 살금살금 위층으로 올라가, 그녀의 방문 앞에서 귀를 기울였다."], ["Her measured breathing told that she was sleeping profoundly.", "고르게 들려오는 그녀의 숨소리는 그녀가 깊이 잠들었음을 알려 주었다."]], [["“Thank God!”", "「고맙기도 하지!」"], ["murmured Clare; and yet he was conscious of a pang of bitterness at the thought—approximately true, though not wholly so—that having shifted the burden of her life to his shoulders, she was now reposing without care.", "클레어가 중얼거렸다. 그러면서도 그는 한 가지 생각에 쓰라림이 욱신 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다—전적으로 옳지는 않으나 대체로는 옳은 그 생각, 곧 그녀가 제 삶의 짐을 그의 어깨로 옮겨 놓고서 이제 근심 없이 쉬고 있다는 생각에."]], [["He turned away to descend; then, irresolute, faced round to her door again.", "그는 내려가려고 돌아섰다가, 마음을 정하지 못하고 다시 그녀의 방문 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["In the act he caught sight of one of the d’Urberville dames, whose portrait was immediately over the entrance to Tess’s bedchamber.", "그 순간 그는 더버빌 가문의 한 귀부인이 눈에 들어왔는데, 그 초상화는 테스의 침실 입구 바로 위에 걸려 있었다."], ["In the candlelight the painting was more than unpleasant.", "촛불 빛 아래에서 그 그림은 그저 불쾌한 정도를 넘어섰다."], ["Sinister design lurked in the woman’s features, a concentrated purpose of revenge on the other sex—so it seemed to him then.", "그 여인의 이목구비에는 불길한 의도가 도사리고 있었으니, 이성異性을 향한 복수의 결연한 작심이었다—적어도 그때 그에게는 그렇게 보였다."], ["The Caroline bodice of the portrait was low—precisely as Tess’s had been when he tucked it in to show the necklace; and again he experienced the distressing sensation of a resemblance between them.", "초상화의 캐롤라인 시대풍 보디스는 깊이 파여 있었다—테스가 목걸이를 보여 주려고 옷깃을 접어 넣었을 때와 꼭 같이. 그리하여 그는 다시금 두 사람이 닮았다는 괴로운 느낌을 받았다."]], [["The check was sufficient.", "그 제동만으로 충분했다."], ["He resumed his retreat and descended.", "그는 다시 물러나 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["His air remained calm and cold, his small compressed mouth indexing his powers of self-control; his face wearing still that terrible sterile expression which had spread thereon since her disclosure.", "그의 태도는 여전히 차분하고 차가웠으며, 작고 꾹 다문 입은 그의 자제력을 그대로 드러냈다. 그의 얼굴에는 그녀의 고백 이후로 번져 든 그 끔찍한 메마른 표정이 아직 어려 있었다."], ["It was the face of a man who was no longer passion’s slave, yet who found no advantage in his enfranchisement.", "그것은 이제 더는 정념의 노예가 아니면서도, 그 해방에서 아무런 득도 찾지 못한 사내의 얼굴이었다."], ["He was simply regarding the harrowing contingencies of human experience, the unexpectedness of things.", "그는 그저 인간 경험의 가슴 쓰린 우연들을, 일들의 뜻밖임을 바라보고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Nothing so pure, so sweet, so virginal as Tess had seemed possible all the long while that he had adored her, up to an hour ago; but", "한 시간 전까지, 그가 그녀를 흠모해 온 그 길고 긴 세월 내내, 테스만큼 순결하고, 달콤하고, 처녀다운 존재가 있으리라고는 도무지 여겨지지 않았다. 그러나—"]], [["The little less, and what worlds away!", "조금만 모자라도, 얼마나 아득히 다른 세계인가!"]], [["He argued erroneously when he said to himself that her heart was not indexed in the honest freshness of her face; but Tess had no advocate to set him right.", "그녀의 마음이 그 정직하고 싱싱한 얼굴에 그대로 드러나 있지 않다고 혼잣말로 단정했을 때, 그는 잘못 생각한 것이었다. 그러나 테스에게는 그를 바로잡아 줄 변호인이 없었다."], ["Could it be possible, he continued, that eyes which as they gazed never expressed any divergence from what the tongue was telling, were yet ever seeing another world behind her ostensible one, discordant and contrasting?", "그는 생각을 이어 갔다. 응시하는 동안 혀가 말하는 바와 조금도 어긋남을 내비친 적 없는 저 두 눈이, 그러면서도 줄곧 겉으로 드러난 세계 뒤편의 또 다른 세계를, 어긋나고 대조되는 세계를 내내 보고 있었다는 게 과연 있을 수 있는 일일까?"]], [["He reclined on his couch in the sitting-room, and extinguished the light.", "그는 거실의 잠자리에 몸을 기대고 불을 껐다."], ["The night came in, and took up its place there, unconcerned and indifferent; the night which had already swallowed up his happiness, and was now digesting it listlessly; and was ready to swallow up the happiness of a thousand other people with as little disturbance or change of mien.", "밤이 들어와 거기 제자리를 잡았다. 무심하고 아랑곳없이. 이미 그의 행복을 삼켜 버리고 이제 그것을 시큰둥하게 소화하고 있는 밤, 다른 수천 사람의 행복도 그처럼 거의 흐트러짐 없이, 낯빛 하나 바꾸지 않고 삼켜 버릴 채비가 된 밤이었다."]], [["# XXXVI", "# XXXVI"]], [["Clare arose in the light of a dawn that was ashy and furtive, as though associated with crime.", "클레어는, 마치 범죄와 결부되기라도 한 듯 잿빛으로 음흉하게 트는 새벽빛 속에서 일어났다."], ["The fireplace confronted him with its extinct embers; the spread supper-table, whereon stood the two full glasses of untasted wine, now flat and filmy; her vacated seat and his own; the other articles of furniture, with their eternal look of not being able to help it, their intolerable inquiry what was to be done?", "벽난로는 꺼져 버린 잉걸불을 안은 채 그를 마주했다. 차려진 저녁 식탁 위에는 입도 대지 않은 포도주가 두 잔 가득 채워진 채, 이제 김 빠지고 막이 낀 채로 놓여 있었다. 비어 버린 그녀의 자리와 그 자신의 자리, 그리고 다른 세간들은, 어쩔 수 없다는 그 영원한 표정을 띠고서, 어떻게 해야 하느냐는 그 견디기 힘든 물음을 던지고 있었다."], ["From above there was no sound; but in a few minutes there came a knock at the door.", "위층에서는 아무 소리도 없었다. 그러나 몇 분 뒤 문 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["He remembered that it would be the neighbouring cottager’s wife, who was to minister to their wants while they remained here.", "그는 그것이 두 사람이 여기 머무는 동안 시중을 들어 주기로 한 이웃 오두막 아낙임을 떠올렸다."]], [["The presence of a third person in the house would be extremely awkward just now, and, being already dressed, he opened the window and informed her that they could manage to shift for themselves that morning.", "지금 같은 때에 집 안에 제삼자가 있다는 건 몹시 거북한 일이라, 그는 이미 옷을 차려입은 터라 창문을 열고 그녀에게 오늘 아침은 자기들끼리 어떻게든 해 보겠다고 일러 주었다."], ["She had a milk-can in her hand, which he told her to leave at the door.", "그녀가 손에 우유통을 들고 있어, 그는 문간에 두고 가라고 했다."], ["When the dame had gone away he searched in the back quarters of the house for fuel, and speedily lit a fire.", "아낙이 가 버린 뒤 그는 집 뒤편을 뒤져 땔감을 찾아 금세 불을 지폈다."], ["There was plenty of eggs, butter, bread, and so on in the larder, and Clare soon had breakfast laid, his experiences at the dairy having rendered him facile in domestic preparations.", "식료품 저장실에는 달걀과 버터, 빵 따위가 넉넉히 있어, 클레어는 이내 아침상을 차렸으니, 낙농장에서 쌓은 경험 덕에 집안일 채비에 손이 익숙해진 까닭이었다."], ["The smoke of the kindled wood rose from the chimney without like a lotus-headed column; local people who were passing by saw it, and thought of the newly-married couple, and envied their happiness.", "지펴진 장작 연기가 바깥 굴뚝에서 연잎 모양의 기둥처럼 피어올랐다. 지나가던 동네 사람들이 그것을 보고는 갓 결혼한 부부를 떠올리며 그들의 행복을 부러워했다."]], [["Angel cast a final glance round, and then going to the foot of the stairs, called in a conventional voice—", "에인절은 마지막으로 방 안을 한 번 둘러보고는, 계단 아래로 가서 의례적인 목소리로 외쳤다—"]], [["“Breakfast is ready!”", "「아침 준비됐어요!」"]], [["He opened the front door, and took a few steps in the morning air.", "그는 현관문을 열고 아침 공기 속으로 몇 걸음 내디뎠다."], ["When, after a short space, he came back she was already in the sitting-room mechanically readjusting the breakfast things. As she was fully attired, and the interval since his calling her had been but two or three minutes, she must have been dressed or nearly so before he went to summon her.", "잠시 후 돌아왔을 때 그녀는 이미 거실에서 기계적으로 아침상을 다시 매만지고 있었다. 그녀가 옷을 빈틈없이 갖춰 입은 데다, 그가 부른 뒤로 흐른 시간이 고작 이삼 분에 지나지 않았으므로, 그가 부르러 가기 전에 이미 옷을 다 입었거나 거의 다 입은 상태였음이 분명했다."], ["Her hair was twisted up in a large round mass at the back of her head, and she had put on one of the new frocks—a pale blue woollen garment with neck-frillings of white.", "그녀의 머리는 뒤통수에 크고 둥글게 틀어 올려져 있었고, 새 옷 가운데 하나를—목둘레에 흰 주름 장식이 달린 옅은 파란색 모직 옷을—입고 있었다."], ["Her hands and face appeared to be cold, and she had possibly been sitting dressed in the bedroom a long time without any fire.", "그녀의 손과 얼굴은 차가워 보였으니, 어쩌면 불기 하나 없는 침실에서 옷을 입은 채로 오래도록 앉아 있었던 모양이었다."], ["The marked civility of Clare’s tone in calling her seemed to have inspired her, for the moment, with a new glimmer of hope.", "그녀를 부르던 클레어의 두드러지게 예의 바른 말투가 그 순간 그녀에게 한 가닥 새로운 희망의 빛을 불어넣어 준 듯했다."], ["But it soon died when she looked at him.", "그러나 그를 바라보자 그 희망은 이내 스러졌다."]], [["The pair were, in truth, but the ashes of their former fires.", "사실 두 사람은 한때 타올랐던 불의 잿더미일 뿐이었다."], ["To the hot sorrow of the previous night had succeeded heaviness; it seemed as if nothing could kindle either of them to fervour of sensation any more.", "지난밤의 뜨거운 슬픔에 뒤이어 무거운 짓눌림이 찾아들었으니, 마치 이제 그 무엇도 두 사람 가운데 누구도 감각의 열기로 되살려 낼 수 없을 듯했다."]], [["He spoke gently to her, and she replied with a like undemonstrativeness.", "그는 그녀에게 부드럽게 말했고, 그녀도 그처럼 담담하게 대답했다."], ["At last she came up to him, looking in his sharply-defined face as one who had no consciousness that her own formed a visible object also.", "마침내 그녀가 그에게 다가와, 자신의 얼굴 또한 눈에 보이는 대상이라는 걸 의식하지 못하는 사람처럼, 또렷하게 윤곽 잡힌 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Angel!”", "「에인절!」"], ["she said, and paused, touching him with her fingers lightly as a breeze, as though she could hardly believe to be there in the flesh the man who was once her lover.", "그녀는 말하다가 멈추고는, 한때 자신의 연인이었던 그 사내가 정말 여기 살아 있는 게 도무지 믿기지 않는다는 듯, 산들바람처럼 가볍게 손가락으로 그를 건드렸다."], ["Her eyes were bright, her pale cheek still showed its wonted roundness, though half-dried tears had left glistening traces thereon; and the usually ripe red mouth was almost as pale as her cheek.", "그녀의 두 눈은 빛났고, 창백한 뺨은 여전히 여느 때의 둥그스름함을 띠고 있었으나, 반쯤 마른 눈물이 그 위에 반짝이는 자국을 남겨 두었다. 그리고 평소 같으면 무르익은 붉은빛이던 입술은 뺨만큼이나 핏기가 가셔 있었다."], ["Throbbingly alive as she was still, under the stress of her mental grief the life beat so brokenly that a little further pull upon it would cause real illness, dull her characteristic eyes, and make her mouth thin.", "그토록 생생히 살아 떨고 있으면서도, 마음의 슬픔에 짓눌린 탓에 그 생명의 박동이 어찌나 끊길 듯하던지, 거기에 조금만 더 힘을 가하면 정말로 병이 들어, 그 특유의 눈빛이 흐려지고 입술이 야위어질 것만 같았다."]], [["She looked absolutely pure.", "그녀는 더없이 순결해 보였다."], ["Nature, in her fantastic trickery, had set such a seal of maidenhood upon Tess’s countenance that he gazed at her with a stupefied air.", "자연은, 그 변덕스러운 장난질로, 테스의 얼굴에 처녀성의 도장을 어찌나 또렷이 찍어 놓았던지, 그는 얼이 빠진 듯한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Tess!", "「테스!"], ["Say it is not true!", "사실이 아니라고 말해 줘!"], ["No, it is not true!”", "아니야, 사실일 리 없어!」"]], [["“It is true.”", "「사실이에요.」"]], [["“Every word?”", "「한마디 한마디 다?」"]], [["“Every word.”", "「한마디 한마디 다요.」"]], [["He looked at her imploringly, as if he would willingly have taken a lie from her lips, knowing it to be one, and have made of it, by some sort of sophistry, a valid denial.", "그는 애원하듯 그녀를 바라보았으니, 거짓인 줄 뻔히 알면서도 기꺼이 그녀의 입에서 거짓말을 받아 들고는, 어떤 궤변으로든 그것을 그럴듯한 부인否認으로 만들어 내고 싶다는 듯했다."], ["However, she only repeated—", "그러나 그녀는 그저 되풀이할 뿐이었다—"]], [["“It is true.”", "「사실이에요.」"]], [["“Is he living?”", "「그자는 살아 있어?」"], ["Angel then asked.", "에인절이 그제야 물었다."]], [["“The baby died.”", "「아기는 죽었어요.」"]], [["“But the man?”", "「하지만 그 사내 말이야?」"]], [["“He is alive.”", "「살아 있어요.」"]], [["A last despair passed over Clare’s face.", "마지막 절망이 클레어의 얼굴을 스쳐 갔다."]], [["“Is he in England?”", "「영국에 있어?」"]], [["“Yes.”", "「네.」"]], [["He took a few vague steps.", "그는 정처 없이 몇 걸음 옮겼다."]], [["“My position—is this,”", "「내 처지가—이렇군.」"], ["he said abruptly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“I thought—any man would have thought—that by giving up all ambition to win a wife with social standing, with fortune, with knowledge of the world, I should secure rustic innocence as surely as I should secure pink cheeks; but—However, I am no man to reproach you, and I will not.”", "「나는 생각했어—누구라도 그렇게 생각했을 거야—사회적 지위와 재산과 세상 물정에 밝은 여자를 얻겠다는 야심을 죄다 버리면, 발그레한 뺨을 손에 넣는 것만큼이나 틀림없이 시골의 순결함도 손에 넣으리라고. 그런데—그러나, 나는 너를 탓할 사람이 못 돼. 탓하지 않겠어.」"]], [["Tess felt his position so entirely that the remainder had not been needed.", "테스는 그의 처지를 너무도 온전히 헤아린 터라 나머지 말은 필요치도 않았다."], ["Therein lay just the distress of it; she saw that he had lost all round.", "바로 거기에 그 괴로움이 있었으니, 그녀는 그가 사방으로 모든 것을 잃었음을 알아본 것이다."]], [["“Angel—I should not have let it go on to marriage with you if I had not known that, after all, there was a last way out of it for you; though I hoped you would never—”", "「에인절—나도 결국 당신에게 마지막 빠져나갈 길이 하나 있다는 걸 몰랐다면 당신과의 결혼까지 일을 끌고 가지는 않았을 거예요. 다만 당신이 결코 그러지 않기를 바랐을 뿐이지—」"]], [["Her voice grew husky.", "그녀의 목소리가 잠겨 들었다."]], [["“A last way?”", "「마지막 길이라니?」"]], [["“I mean, to get rid of me.", "「내 말은, 나를 떼어 버리는 길 말이에요."], ["You _can_ get rid of me.”", "당신은 나를 떼어 버릴 수 있어요.」"]], [["“How?”", "「어떻게?」"]], [["“By divorcing me.”", "「나와 이혼하면 되죠.」"]], [["“Good heavens—how can you be so simple! How can I divorce you?”", "「세상에—어쩌면 그리도 단순하니! 내가 어떻게 너와 이혼을 한단 말이냐?」"]], [["“Can’t you—now I have told you?", "「못 해요—이제 다 말했는데도?"], ["I thought my confession would give you grounds for that.”", "난 내 고백이 당신에게 그럴 근거가 되어 줄 줄 알았어요.」"]], [["“O Tess—you are too, too—childish—unformed—crude, I suppose!", "「오 테스—너는 너무, 너무도—어린애 같구나—덜 여물고—풋내가 나, 그래."], ["I don’t know what you are.", "네가 뭔지 도무지 모르겠다."], ["You don’t understand the law—you don’t understand!”", "너는 법이라는 걸 몰라—너는 모른다고!」"]], [["“What—you cannot?”", "「뭐라고요—그게 안 된다고요?」"]], [["“Indeed I cannot.”", "「정말 안 돼.」"]], [["A quick shame mixed with the misery upon his listener’s face.", "이 말을 듣는 그녀의 얼굴에는 비참함에 다급한 수치가 뒤섞였다."]], [["“I thought—I thought,”", "「난 그런 줄—그런 줄 알았어요.」"], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“O, now I see how wicked I seem to you!", "「오, 이제야 내가 당신 눈에 얼마나 못된 여자로 보일지 알겠어요!"], ["Believe me—believe me, on my soul, I never thought but that you could!", "믿어 줘요—제발 믿어 줘요, 내 영혼을 걸고, 난 정말 당신이 그럴 수 있다고만 여겼어요!"], ["I hoped you would not; yet I believed, without a doubt, that you could cast me off if you were determined, and didn’t love me at—at—all!”", "당신이 그러지 않기를 바랐지만, 그래도, 당신이 마음만 굳게 먹고 나를 조금도—조금도 사랑하지 않는다면, 나를 내칠 수 있다고 한 점 의심 없이 믿었어요!」"]], [["“You were mistaken,” he said.", "「네 생각이 틀렸어.」 그가 말했다."]], [["“O, then I ought to have done it, to have done it last night!", "「오, 그렇다면 내가 했어야 했어요, 어젯밤에 해 버렸어야 했어요!"], ["But I hadn’t the courage.", "그런데 용기가 없었어요."], ["That’s just like me!”", "그게 꼭 나다워요!」"]], [["“The courage to do what?”", "「무슨 용기 말이냐?」"]], [["As she did not answer he took her by the hand.", "그녀가 대답하지 않자 그는 그녀의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“What were you thinking of doing?”", "「무엇을 하려고 했던 거야?」"], ["he inquired.", "그가 캐물었다."]], [["“Of putting an end to myself.”", "「스스로 끝내 버리려고요.」"]], [["“When?”", "「언제?」"]], [["She writhed under this inquisitorial manner of his.", "그녀는 추궁하는 듯한 그의 말투에 몸을 비틀었다."], ["“Last night,”", "「어젯밤에요.」"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Where?”", "「어디서?」"]], [["“Under your mistletoe.”", "「당신이 매달아 둔 겨우살이 아래에서요.」"]], [["“My good—! How?”", "「맙소사—! 어떻게?」"], ["he asked sternly.", "그가 준엄하게 물었다."]], [["“I’ll tell you, if you won’t be angry with me!” she said, shrinking.", "「화내지 않겠다고 하면 말할게요!」 그녀가 움츠리며 말했다."], ["“It was with the cord of my box.", "「내 가방의 끈으로 하려고 했어요."], ["But I could not—do the last thing!", "그런데 못 했어요—그 마지막 짓을!"], ["I was afraid that it might cause a scandal to your name.”", "그게 당신 이름에 추문을 끼칠까 봐 무서웠어요.」"]], [["The unexpected quality of this confession, wrung from her, and not volunteered, shook him perceptibly.", "자청해서가 아니라 쥐어짜듯 끌려 나온 이 고백의 뜻밖임에 그는 눈에 띄게 흔들렸다."], ["But he still held her, and, letting his glance fall from her face downwards, he said, “Now, listen to this.", "그러나 그는 여전히 그녀를 붙든 채, 그녀의 얼굴에서 아래로 시선을 떨구며 말했다. 「자, 잘 들어."], ["You must not dare to think of such a horrible thing! How could you!", "너는 절대로 그런 끔찍한 일을 생각해서는 안 돼! 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어!"], ["You will promise me as your husband to attempt that no more.”", "네 남편으로서 약속을 받아 두겠다, 다시는 그런 시도를 하지 않겠다고.」"]], [["“I am ready to promise. I saw how wicked it was.”", "「약속할게요. 그게 얼마나 못된 짓인지 알았어요.」"]], [["“Wicked! The idea was unworthy of you beyond description.”", "「못된 짓이지! 그런 생각은 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 너답지 못한 거야.」"]], [["“But, Angel,”", "「하지만, 에인절,」"], ["she pleaded, enlarging her eyes in calm unconcern upon him, “it was thought of entirely on your account—to set you free without the scandal of the divorce that I thought you would have to get.", "그녀는 차분히 아랑곳없는 표정으로 두 눈을 크게 뜨고 그를 보며 호소했다. 「그건 오로지 당신을 위해 떠올린 거예요—당신이 받아야만 하리라 여겼던 그 이혼의 추문 없이 당신을 자유롭게 해 주려고요."], ["I should never have dreamt of doing it on mine.", "나 자신을 위해서라면 꿈에도 그럴 생각을 못 했을 거예요."], ["However, to do it with my own hand is too good for me, after all.", "하지만, 따지고 보면 내 손으로 그러는 건 나한테 분에 넘치는 일이에요."], ["It is you, my ruined husband, who ought to strike the blow.", "그 일격을 가해야 할 사람은 바로 당신이에요, 나로 인해 망가진 내 남편이."], ["I think I should love you more, if that were possible, if you could bring yourself to do it, since there’s no other way of escape for ’ee.", "만약 당신이 마음을 다잡고 그렇게 해 줄 수만 있다면, 그럴 수만 있다면, 나는 당신을 더—더 사랑하게 될 것 같아요. 당신한테 달리 빠져나갈 길이 없으니까요."], ["I feel I am so utterly worthless!", "내가 너무도 쓸모없는 존재라는 게 느껴져요!"], ["So very greatly in the way!”", "너무도 거치적거리기만 하는 존재라는 게요!」"]], [["“Ssh!”", "「쉿!」"]], [["“Well, since you say no, I won’t.", "「그래요, 당신이 안 된다고 하니, 안 할게요."], ["I have no wish opposed to yours.”", "난 당신 뜻에 어긋나는 바람은 없어요.」"]], [["He knew this to be true enough. Since the desperation of the night her activities had dropped to zero, and there was no further rashness to be feared.", "그는 이 말이 충분히 진실임을 알았다. 지난밤의 절망 이후로 그녀의 활기는 바닥까지 떨어졌고, 더 두려워할 무모함도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["Tess tried to busy herself again over the breakfast-table with more or less success, and they sat down both on the same side, so that their glances did not meet.", "테스는 다시 아침 식탁 일에 그런대로 분주히 매달려 보려 했고, 두 사람은 같은 쪽에 나란히 앉아 서로 시선이 마주치지 않게 했다."], ["There was at first something awkward in hearing each other eat and drink, but this could not be escaped; moreover, the amount of eating done was small on both sides.", "처음에는 서로 먹고 마시는 소리를 듣는 것이 어딘가 거북했으나, 이건 피할 수 없는 노릇이었다. 게다가 두 사람 다 먹는 양은 얼마 되지 않았다."], ["Breakfast over, he rose, and telling her the hour at which he might be expected to dinner, went off to the miller’s in a mechanical pursuance of the plan of studying that business, which had been his only practical reason for coming here.", "아침을 마치자 그는 일어나, 저녁 식사 때쯤 돌아올 거라고 일러 두고는, 여기 온 단 하나의 실질적인 이유였던, 그 방앗간 일을 배운다는 계획을 기계적으로 좇아 방앗간 주인에게로 떠났다."]], [["When he was gone Tess stood at the window, and presently saw his form crossing the great stone bridge which conducted to the mill premises.", "그가 가 버린 뒤 테스는 창가에 서 있다가, 이윽고 방앗간 부지로 이어지는 큰 돌다리를 건너는 그의 모습을 보았다."], ["He sank behind it, crossed the railway beyond, and disappeared.", "그는 다리 너머로 가라앉듯 사라졌고, 그 너머 철길을 건너 자취를 감추었다."], ["Then, without a sigh, she turned her attention to the room, and began clearing the table and setting it in order.", "그러자 그녀는 한숨도 쉬지 않고 방 안으로 마음을 돌려, 식탁을 치우고 정돈하기 시작했다."]], [["The charwoman soon came.", "품팔이 아낙이 곧 왔다."], ["Her presence was at first a strain upon Tess, but afterwards an alleviation.", "그녀가 곁에 있는 것이 처음에는 테스에게 긴장이었으나, 나중에는 위안이 되었다."], ["At half-past twelve she left her assistant alone in the kitchen, and, returning to the sitting-room, waited for the reappearance of Angel’s form behind the bridge.", "열두 시 반에 테스는 부엌에 그 일손을 혼자 남겨 두고 거실로 돌아와, 다리 너머로 에인절의 모습이 다시 나타나기를 기다렸다."]], [["About one he showed himself.", "한 시쯤 그가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Her face flushed, although he was a quarter of a mile off.", "그가 사 분의 일 마일이나 떨어져 있었는데도 그녀의 얼굴은 발그레해졌다."], ["She ran to the kitchen to get the dinner served by the time he should enter.", "그녀는 그가 들어올 때까지 식사를 차려 내려고 부엌으로 달려갔다."], ["He went first to the room where they had washed their hands together the day before, and as he entered the sitting-room the dish-covers rose from the dishes as if by his own motion.", "그는 먼저 어제 둘이 함께 손을 씻었던 방으로 갔고, 거실로 들어서자 접시 뚜껑들이 마치 그 자신의 손짓에 따르듯 접시에서 들려 올라갔다. 「어쩌면 이리 시간을 잘 맞췄을까!」 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "「네."], ["I saw you coming over the bridge,” said she.", "다리를 건너오는 걸 봤거든요.」 그녀가 말했다."]], [["The meal was passed in commonplace talk of what he had been doing during the morning at the Abbey Mill, of the methods of bolting and the old-fashioned machinery, which he feared would not enlighten him greatly on modern improved methods, some of it seeming to have been in use ever since the days it ground for the monks in the adjoining conventual buildings—now a heap of ruins.", "식사 시간은 그가 오전에 애비밀에서 한 일에 관한 평범한 이야기로 흘러갔다. 가루를 체로 치는 방법이며 구식 기계 이야기였는데, 그는 그것이 근래의 개량된 방식을 깨치는 데 별로 도움이 되지 않을까 걱정스러워했다. 그중 일부는 옆에 딸린 수도원 건물들에서—지금은 한 무더기 폐허가 된 그곳에서—수도사들을 위해 곡식을 빻던 시절부터 줄곧 쓰여 온 듯했다."], ["He left the house again in the course of an hour, coming home at dusk, and occupying himself through the evening with his papers.", "그는 한 시간쯤 지나 다시 집을 나섰다가 해 질 녘에 돌아와, 저녁 내내 자기 서류들을 들여다보며 시간을 보냈다."], ["She feared she was in the way and, when the old woman was gone, retired to the kitchen, where she made herself busy as well as she could for more than an hour.", "그녀는 자신이 거치적거리는 게 아닐까 두려워, 늙은 아낙이 가 버리자 부엌으로 물러나, 한 시간 넘게 할 수 있는 한 분주히 몸을 놀렸다."]], [["Clare’s shape appeared at the door.", "클레어의 모습이 문간에 나타났다."], ["“You must not work like this,” he said.", "「이렇게 일하면 안 돼.」 그가 말했다."], ["“You are not my servant; you are my wife.”", "「너는 내 하녀가 아니야. 내 아내라고.」"]], [["She raised her eyes, and brightened somewhat.", "그녀는 눈을 들었고, 다소 얼굴이 환해졌다."], ["“I may think myself that—indeed?”", "「내가 그렇게 여겨도—정말요?」"], ["she murmured, in piteous raillery.", "그녀는 애처로운 비웃음을 담아 중얼거렸다."], ["“You mean in name!", "「이름뿐이라는 거겠죠!"], ["Well, I don’t want to be anything more.”", "뭐, 나도 그 이상은 바라지 않아요.」"]], [["“You _may_ think so, Tess!", "「그렇게 여겨도 된다니까, 테스!"], ["You are.", "너는 그래."], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 소리를 하는 거야?」"]], [["“I don’t know,”", "「모르겠어요.」"], ["she said hastily, with tears in her accents.", "그녀는 목소리에 눈물을 담은 채 다급하게 말했다."], ["“I thought I—because I am not respectable, I mean.", "「난 내가—그러니까 내가 떳떳하지 못하니까 그런 줄 알았어요."], ["I told you I thought I was not respectable enough long ago—and on that account I didn’t want to marry you, only—only you urged me!”", "한참 전에 이미 말했잖아요, 내가 당신과 결혼할 만큼 떳떳하지 못한 것 같다고—그래서 당신과 결혼하고 싶지 않았던 거예요, 다만—다만 당신이 졸랐잖아요!」"]], [["She broke into sobs, and turned her back to him.", "그녀는 흐느낌을 터뜨리며 그에게 등을 돌렸다."], ["It would almost have won round any man but Angel Clare. Within the remote depths of his constitution, so gentle and affectionate as he was in general, there lay hidden a hard logical deposit, like a vein of metal in a soft loam, which turned the edge of everything that attempted to traverse it.", "에인절 클레어가 아닌 어떤 사내라도 그 모습에 마음이 거의 돌아섰을 것이다. 평소에는 그토록 부드럽고 다정한 그였지만, 그 본바탕의 아득한 깊은 곳에는, 물러진 양토壤土 속의 금속 광맥처럼, 단단한 논리의 침전물이 숨어 있어, 그것을 가로지르려는 모든 것의 날을 무디게 만들었다."], ["It had blocked his acceptance of the Church; it blocked his acceptance of Tess.", "그것은 그가 교회를 받아들이는 것을 가로막았고, 테스를 받아들이는 것도 가로막았다."], ["Moreover, his affection itself was less fire than radiance, and, with regard to the other sex, when he ceased to believe he ceased to follow: contrasting in this with many impressionable natures, who remain sensuously infatuated with what they intellectually despise.", "게다가 그의 애정 자체가 불길이라기보다는 차라리 광채에 가까웠으니, 이성異性에 관해서라면, 믿기를 그치는 순간 그는 좇기도 그쳤다. 이 점에서 그는, 지성으로는 경멸하는 것에 관능적으로는 여전히 빠져 있는 그 숱한 마음 여린 천성들과 사뭇 대비되었다."], ["He waited till her sobbing ceased.", "그는 그녀의 흐느낌이 그칠 때까지 기다렸다."]], [["“I wish half the women in England were as respectable as you,”", "「영국 여자의 절반이라도 너만큼 떳떳했으면 좋겠다.」"], ["he said, in an ebullition of bitterness against womankind in general.", "그는 여자라는 족속 전체를 향한 쓰라림이 부글거리듯 솟구쳐 말했다."], ["“It isn’t a question of respectability, but one of principle!”", "「이건 떳떳하냐 마냐의 문제가 아니라, 원칙의 문제야!」"]], [["He spoke such things as these and more of a kindred sort to her, being still swayed by the antipathetic wave which warps direct souls with such persistence when once their vision finds itself mocked by appearances.", "그는 이런 따위의 말들을, 그리고 이와 비슷한 부류의 말들을 더 그녀에게 했으니, 한번 그 시선이 겉모습에 우롱당했다 싶으면 그토록 끈질기게 곧은 영혼을 비틀어 놓는, 그 반감의 물결에 여전히 휩쓸려 있던 까닭이었다."], ["There was, it is true, underneath, a back current of sympathy through which a woman of the world might have conquered him.", "사실 그 밑바닥에는 동정의 역류逆流가 흐르고 있어, 세상 물정에 밝은 여자라면 그것을 타고 그를 정복할 수도 있었으리라."], ["But Tess did not think of this; she took everything as her deserts, and hardly opened her mouth.", "그러나 테스는 이런 것을 생각하지 못했다. 그녀는 모든 것을 제가 받아 마땅한 벌로 여기며 거의 입을 열지 않았다."], ["The firmness of her devotion to him was indeed almost pitiful; quick-tempered as she naturally was, nothing that he could say made her unseemly; she sought not her own; was not provoked; thought no evil of his treatment of her.", "그를 향한 그녀의 헌신은 그 한결같음이 가히 애처로울 지경이었다. 천성이 발끈하기 쉬운 그녀였건만, 그가 무슨 말을 해도 그녀는 추한 꼴을 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 제 잇속을 구하지 않았고, 노여워하지 않았으며, 그의 처사를 두고 악한 생각을 품지 않았다."], ["She might just now have been Apostolic Charity herself returned to a self-seeking modern world.", "그녀는 마치 사도 바울이 말한 그 자애가, 제 잇속만 구하는 현대 세상으로 되돌아온 모습 그대로인 듯했다."]], [["This evening, night, and morning were passed precisely as the preceding ones had been passed.", "이날 저녁과 밤, 그리고 아침은 앞선 날들이 지나간 그대로 똑같이 지나갔다."], ["On one, and only one, occasion did she—the formerly free and independent Tess—venture to make any advances.", "단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 그녀는—한때 그토록 자유롭고 거리낌 없던 테스는—감히 먼저 다가가 보려 했다."], ["It was on the third occasion of his starting after a meal to go out to the flour-mill.", "그가 식사를 마치고 밀가루 방앗간에 가려고 세 번째로 자리에서 일어설 때였다."], ["As he was leaving the table he said “Goodbye,” and she replied in the same words, at the same time inclining her mouth in the way of his.", "식탁을 떠나며 그가 「잘 있어」라고 하자, 그녀도 똑같은 말로 대답하면서 동시에 그가 입 맞춰 주기를 바라듯 입술을 그의 쪽으로 기울였다."], ["He did not avail himself of the invitation, saying, as he turned hastily aside—", "그러나 그는 그 청을 받아들이지 않고, 황급히 곁으로 비켜서며 말했다—"]], [["“I shall be home punctually.”", "「제때 들어올게.」"]], [["Tess shrank into herself as if she had been struck.", "테스는 매라도 맞은 듯 자기 안으로 움츠러들었다."], ["Often enough had he tried to reach those lips against her consent—often had he said gaily that her mouth and breath tasted of the butter and eggs and milk and honey on which she mainly lived, that he drew sustenance from them, and other follies of that sort.", "그는 그녀의 뜻을 거슬러서까지 그 입술에 닿으려 한 적이 숱하게 많았고, 그녀가 주로 먹고 사는 버터와 달걀과 우유와 꿀맛이 그녀의 입과 숨결에서 난다고, 그것에서 자기가 양분을 얻는다고, 그런 따위의 어리석은 말들을 흥겹게 늘어놓은 적도 숱하게 많았다."], ["But he did not care for them now.", "그러나 이제 그는 그것에 마음이 없었다."], ["He observed her sudden shrinking, and said gently—", "그는 그녀가 문득 움츠러드는 것을 보고는, 부드럽게 말했다—"]], [["“You know, I have to think of a course.", "「알다시피, 나는 앞으로 어떻게 할지 궁리를 해야 해."], ["It was imperative that we should stay together a little while, to avoid the scandal to you that would have resulted from our immediate parting. But you must see it is only for form’s sake.”", "우리가 당장 갈라서면 너에게 추문이 일었을 테니, 그걸 피하려고 얼마간 함께 지내는 게 꼭 필요했던 거야. 하지만 이건 어디까지나 형식을 차리기 위한 것일 뿐임을 너도 알아야 해.」"]], [["“Yes,”", "「네.」"], ["said Tess absently.", "테스가 멍하니 말했다."]], [["He went out, and on his way to the mill stood still, and wished for a moment that he had responded yet more kindly, and kissed her once at least.", "그는 밖으로 나갔다가, 방앗간으로 가는 길에 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 좀 더 다정하게 응해 줄 걸, 적어도 한 번쯤은 그녀에게 입을 맞춰 줄 걸 하고 한순간 바랐다."]], [["Thus they lived through this despairing day or two; in the same house, truly; but more widely apart than before they were lovers.", "이렇게 두 사람은 이 절망스러운 하루 이틀을 견디며 지냈다. 한집에 있는 것은 참으로 사실이었으나, 연인이 되기 전보다도 더 멀리 떨어진 채였다."], ["It was evident to her that he was, as he had said, living with paralyzed activities in his endeavour to think of a plan of procedure.", "그가 말한 그대로, 어떻게 일을 처리할지 궁리하느라 활기가 마비된 채 지내고 있다는 것이 그녀의 눈에도 뚜렷이 보였다."], ["She was awe-stricken to discover such determination under such apparent flexibility.", "그녀는 그토록 유연해 보이는 겉모습 아래 그런 단호함이 도사리고 있음을 알아채고는 두려움에 사로잡혔다."], ["His consistency was, indeed, too cruel.", "그의 한결같음은 참으로 너무도 모질었다."], ["She no longer expected forgiveness now.", "그녀는 이제 더는 용서를 기대하지 않았다."], ["More than once she thought of going away from him during his absence at the mill; but she feared that this, instead of benefiting him, might be the means of hampering and humiliating him yet more if it should become known.", "그가 방앗간에 가 있는 동안 그를 떠나 버릴까 하고 한두 번 넘게 생각했으나, 만약 그것이 알려지면 그에게 득이 되기는커녕 오히려 그를 더 옭아매고 욕보이는 빌미가 될까 두려웠다."]], [["Meanwhile Clare was meditating, verily.", "한편 클레어는 참으로, 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["His thought had been unsuspended; he was becoming ill with thinking; eaten out with thinking, withered by thinking; scourged out of all his former pulsating, flexuous domesticity.", "그의 생각은 한순간도 멎지 않았으니, 그는 생각하느라 병이 들어 갔다. 생각에 파먹히고, 생각에 시들어, 한때의 그 고동치고 나긋나긋하던 가정적인 정다움을 죄다 채찍질당해 잃었다."], ["He walked about saying to himself, “What’s to be done—what’s to be done?” and by chance she overheard him.", "그는 「어떻게 해야 하나—어떻게 해야 하나?」 하고 혼잣말하며 이리저리 거닐었는데, 우연히 그녀가 그 말을 엿들었다."], ["It caused her to break the reserve about their future which had hitherto prevailed.", "그것이 그녀로 하여금, 이제껏 둘 사이에 깔려 있던 미래에 관한 침묵을 깨뜨리게 만들었다."]], [["“I suppose—you are not going to live with me—long, are you, Angel?”", "「설마—당신이 나와—오래 살지는 않을 거죠, 그렇죠, 에인절?」"], ["she asked, the sunk corners of her mouth betraying how purely mechanical were the means by which she retained that expression of chastened calm upon her face.", "그녀가 물었는데, 푹 꺼진 입가가, 그 얼굴에 단련된 평온의 표정을 붙잡아 두는 수단이 얼마나 순전히 기계적인 것인지를 그대로 드러냈다."]], [["“I cannot”", "「나는 그럴 수가 없어.」"], ["he said, “without despising myself, and what is worse, perhaps, despising you.", "그가 말했다. 「내 자신을 경멸하지 않고서는, 더 나쁘게는 어쩌면 너를 경멸하지 않고서는 말이야."], ["I mean, of course, cannot live with you in the ordinary sense.", "물론 내 말은, 흔한 의미에서 너와 함께 살 수가 없다는 거야."], ["At present, whatever I feel, I do not despise you.", "지금은, 내가 무엇을 느끼든, 너를 경멸하지는 않아."], ["And, let me speak plainly, or you may not see all my difficulties.", "그리고, 솔직히 말하게 해 줘, 안 그러면 너는 내 어려움을 다 알지 못할 테니."], ["How can we live together while that man lives?—he being your husband in nature, and not I.", "그 사내가 살아 있는 동안 우리가 어떻게 함께 살 수 있겠어?—천성으로 따지면 그자가 네 남편이지, 내가 아니야."], ["If he were dead it might be different...", "만약 그가 죽었다면 사정이 다를지도 몰라…"], ["Besides, that’s not all the difficulty; it lies in another consideration—one bearing upon the future of other people than ourselves.", "게다가 그게 어려움의 전부가 아니야. 또 다른 데에 어려움이 있어—우리 자신이 아닌 다른 사람들의 장래에 걸린 문제 말이야."], ["Think of years to come, and children being born to us, and this past matter getting known—for it must get known.", "앞으로 닥쳐올 세월을 생각해 봐, 우리에게 아이들이 태어나고, 이 지난 일이 알려지는 것을—그건 알려지고야 말 테니까."], ["There is not an uttermost part of the earth but somebody comes from it or goes to it from elsewhere.", "이 땅 끝 어느 구석이라도, 누군가 거기서 나오거나 다른 데서 거기로 들어가지 않는 곳은 없어. 자, 생각해 봐."], ["Well, think of wretches of our flesh and blood growing up under a taunt which they will gradually get to feel the full force of with their expanding years.", "우리 살과 피를 나눈 가엾은 것들이, 비웃음을 받으며 자라나, 나이를 먹어 갈수록 그 비웃음의 온전한 무게를 차츰 절감하게 되는 것을. 그 애들에게 그게 얼마나 끔찍한 깨달음이겠어!"], ["What an awakening for them!", "얼마나 막막한 앞날이겠어!"], ["What a prospect! Can you honestly say ‘Remain’ after contemplating this contingency?", "이런 일이 닥칠 수 있음을 곰곰이 생각해 보고도 너는 떳떳하게 ‘함께 있어요’라고 말할 수 있겠어?"], ["Don’t you think we had better endure the ills we have than fly to others?”", "우리가 이미 겪고 있는 괴로움을 견디는 편이, 다른 괴로움으로 달아나는 것보다 낫지 않겠어?」"]], [["Her eyelids, weighted with trouble, continued drooping as before.", "근심에 짓눌린 그녀의 눈꺼풀은 아까처럼 계속 내리깔린 채였다."]], [["“I cannot say ‘Remain,’”", "「‘함께 있어요’라고는 말 못 하겠어요.」"], ["she answered, “I cannot; I had not thought so far.”", "그녀가 대답했다. 「못 하겠어요. 거기까지는 미처 생각하지 못했어요.」"]], [["Tess’s feminine hope—shall we confess it?—had been so obstinately recuperative as to revive in her surreptitious visions of a domiciliary intimacy continued long enough to break down his coldness even against his judgement.", "테스의 여자다운 희망은—고백하자면—어찌나 끈질기게 되살아나는 것이었던지, 한집에서 살을 맞대고 지내는 정다움이 오래도록 이어진다면, 그의 판단을 거슬러서라도 그 냉담함을 끝내 허물어뜨리리라는, 남몰래 품은 환상으로 그녀 안에서 다시 살아났다."], ["Though unsophisticated in the usual sense, she was not incomplete; and it would have denoted deficiency of womanhood if she had not instinctively known what an argument lies in propinquity.", "흔히 말하는 의미에서 닳지 않은 그녀였지만, 그렇다고 모자란 것은 아니었다. 가까이 붙어 있다는 것에 얼마나 큰 설득의 힘이 깃들어 있는지를 본능으로 알지 못했다면, 그건 오히려 여자다움의 결핍을 뜻했으리라."], ["Nothing else would serve her, she knew, if this failed.", "만약 이것이 실패한다면 그녀를 구할 다른 길은 없음을 그녀는 알았다."], ["It was wrong to hope in what was of the nature of strategy, she said to herself: yet that sort of hope she could not extinguish.", "책략의 본성을 띤 것에 희망을 거는 건 그릇된 일이라고 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. 그러면서도 그런 종류의 희망을 그녀는 꺼 버릴 수가 없었다."], ["His last representation had now been made, and it was, as she said, a new view.", "그의 마지막 진술은 이제 다 펼쳐졌고, 그것은 그녀의 말마따나 새로운 관점이었다."], ["She had truly never thought so far as that, and his lucid picture of possible offspring who would scorn her was one that brought deadly convictions to an honest heart which was humanitarian to its centre.", "그녀는 정말로 거기까지는 한 번도 생각해 보지 못했으니, 자신을 멸시할 자식들이 태어날 수 있다는 그의 또렷한 그림은, 그 속까지 인정에 넘치는 정직한 마음에 죽음 같은 확신을 안겨 주는 것이었다."], ["Sheer experience had already taught her that in some circumstances there was one thing better than to lead a good life, and that was to be saved from leading any life whatever.", "순전한 경험이 이미 그녀에게 가르쳐 준 바, 어떤 처지에서는 착하게 사는 것보다 더 나은 한 가지가 있었으니, 그것은 곧 어떤 삶이든 아예 살게 되는 일에서 구원받는 것이었다."], ["Like all who have been previsioned by suffering, she could, in the words of M. Sully-Prudhomme, hear a penal sentence in the fiat, “You shall be born,”", "고통으로 앞일을 내다보게 된 모든 이가 그렇듯, 그녀도 쉴리프뤼돔 씨의 말마따나 「너는 태어나리라」라는 그 명령 속에서 형벌의 선고를 들을 수 있었다."], ["particularly if addressed to potential issue of hers.", "특히 그것이 제 몸에서 나올 수도 있는 자식을 향한 것이라면 더더욱."]], [["Yet such is the vulpine slyness of Dame Nature, that, till now, Tess had been hoodwinked by her love for Clare into forgetting it might result in vitalizations that would inflict upon others what she had bewailed as misfortune to herself.", "그러나 어머니 자연이 어찌나 여우같이 교활한지, 지금껏 테스는 클레어를 향한 사랑에 눈이 멀어, 그 사랑이 새 생명들을 낳을 수 있고, 그리하여 제가 스스로 불행이라 한탄했던 것을 다른 이들에게 떠안길 수 있다는 사실을 잊고 있었던 것이다."]], [["She therefore could not withstand his argument.", "그러므로 그녀는 그의 논리를 당해 낼 수 없었다."], ["But with the self-combating proclivity of the supersensitive, an answer thereto arose in Clare’s own mind, and he almost feared it.", "그러나 지나치게 예민한 자들 특유의, 스스로와 싸우는 성향 탓에, 그에 대한 반박이 클레어 자신의 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 그는 그것이 거의 두렵기까지 했다."], ["It was based on her exceptional physical nature; and she might have used it promisingly.", "그것은 그녀의 남다른 신체적 천성에 근거한 것이었으니, 그녀는 그것을 가망 있게 써먹을 수도 있었다."], ["She might have added besides: “On an Australian upland or Texan plain, who is to know or care about my misfortunes, or to reproach me or you?”", "게다가 그녀는 이렇게 덧붙일 수도 있었으리라. 「오스트레일리아의 고원이나 텍사스의 평원에서라면, 누가 내 불행을 알거나 아랑곳하겠어요, 누가 나나 당신을 탓하겠어요?」"], ["Yet, like the majority of women, she accepted the momentary presentment as if it were the inevitable.", "그러나 대다수 여자가 그렇듯, 그녀는 그 한순간의 모습을 마치 피할 수 없는 것인 양 받아들였다."], ["And she may have been right.", "어쩌면 그녀가 옳았을지도 모른다."], ["The intuitive heart of woman knoweth not only its own bitterness, but its husband’s, and even if these assumed reproaches were not likely to be addressed to him or to his by strangers, they might have reached his ears from his own fastidious brain.", "여자의 직관적인 마음은 제 자신의 쓰라림뿐 아니라 제 남편의 쓰라림까지도 안다. 설령 이렇게 가정된 비난들이 낯선 이들의 입에서 그나 그의 자식들을 향해 던져질 가망이 없다 해도, 그것은 그 자신의 까다로운 머릿속에서 그의 귀에까지 닿았을 것이다."]], [["It was the third day of the estrangement.", "소원해진 지 사흘째 되는 날이었다."], ["Some might risk the odd paradox that with more animalism he would have been the nobler man.", "더 짐승 같은 데가 있었더라면 그가 더 고결한 사내였으리라는 그 묘한 역설을 누군가는 무릅쓰고 내놓을지도 모른다."], ["We do not say it. Yet Clare’s love was doubtless ethereal to a fault, imaginative to impracticability.", "우리는 그렇게 말하지는 않겠다. 그러나 클레어의 사랑이 흠이 될 만큼 너무 영적이고, 실행에 옮길 수 없을 만큼 너무 관념적이었음은 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["With these natures, corporal presence is something less appealing than corporal absence; the latter creating an ideal presence that conveniently drops the defects of the real.", "이런 천성을 지닌 이들에게는, 몸이 곁에 있는 것이 몸이 곁에 없는 것보다 어쩐지 덜 끌리는 법이다. 후자는 실제의 결점들을 편리하게 떨궈 낸 이상적인 현존을 빚어내는 까닭이다."], ["She found that her personality did not plead her cause so forcibly as she had anticipated.", "그녀는 자신의 인격이 예상했던 만큼 강하게 제 처지를 호소해 주지 못함을 깨달았다."], ["The figurative phrase was true: she was another woman than the one who had excited his desire.", "그 비유적인 표현은 사실이었다. 그녀는 한때 그의 욕망을 불러일으켰던 그 여자와는 다른 여자였다."]], [["“I have thought over what you say,”", "「당신이 한 말을 곰곰이 생각해 봤어요.」"], ["she remarked to him, moving her forefinger over the tablecloth, her other hand, which bore the ring that mocked them both, supporting her forehead.", "그녀가 그에게 말했는데, 한 손가락을 식탁보 위로 문지르면서, 두 사람 모두를 조롱하는 그 반지를 낀 다른 한 손으로는 이마를 받치고 있었다."], ["“It is quite true, all of it; it must be.", "「다 옳은 말이에요, 전부 다. 틀림없이 그래요."], ["You must go away from me.”", "당신은 내게서 떠나야 해요.」"]], [["“But what can you do?”", "「하지만 너는 어떻게 하려고?」"]], [["“I can go home.”", "「난 집으로 가면 돼요.」"]], [["Clare had not thought of that.", "클레어는 거기까지는 생각하지 못했다."]], [["“Are you sure?”", "「정말이야?」"], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Quite sure.", "「정말이에요."], ["We ought to part, and we may as well get it past and done.", "우린 갈라서야 해요. 그러니 어서 끝내 버리는 게 나아요."], ["You once said that I was apt to win men against their better judgement; and if I am constantly before your eyes I may cause you to change your plans in opposition to your reason and wish; and afterwards your repentance and my sorrow will be terrible.”", "당신이 전에 그랬잖아요, 내가 남자들을 그들의 더 나은 판단을 거슬러 끌어당기는 재주가 있다고. 만약 내가 늘 당신 눈앞에 있으면, 당신의 이성과 바람을 거슬러 당신의 계획을 바꾸게 만들지도 몰라요. 그러면 나중에 당신의 뉘우침과 나의 슬픔은 끔찍할 거예요.」"]], [["“And you would like to go home?”", "「그러니까 너는 집으로 가고 싶다는 거지?」"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I want to leave you, and go home.”", "「난 당신을 떠나서 집으로 가고 싶어요.」"]], [["“Then it shall be so.”", "「그렇다면 그렇게 하자.」"]], [["Though she did not look up at him, she started.", "그녀는 그를 올려다보지는 않았으나, 흠칫했다."], ["There was a difference between the proposition and the covenant, which she had felt only too quickly.", "제안과 약조 사이에는 차이가 있었고, 그녀는 그것을 너무도 빠르게 느낀 것이다."]], [["“I feared it would come to this,”", "「이렇게 되고 말 줄 알았어요.」"], ["she murmured, her countenance meekly fixed.", "그녀는 얼굴을 고분고분 굳힌 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“I don’t complain, Angel, I—I think it best.", "「난 원망하지 않아요, 에인절, 나—나도 이게 최선이라고 생각해요."], ["What you said has quite convinced me.", "당신이 한 말에 난 완전히 수긍했어요."], ["Yes, though nobody else should reproach me if we should stay together, yet somewhen, years hence, you might get angry with me for any ordinary matter, and knowing what you do of my bygones, you yourself might be tempted to say words, and they might be overheard, perhaps by my own children.", "그래요, 우리가 함께 지낸다 해도 다른 누구도 나를 탓하지 않는다 칠지라도, 그래도 언젠가, 몇 해가 지난 뒤, 당신은 어떤 사소한 일로 내게 화를 낼 수도 있고, 내 지난 일을 잘 아는 당신이, 그만 모진 말을 내뱉고 싶어질지도 몰라요. 그리고 그 말을, 어쩌면 내 자식들이 엿들을 수도 있고요."], ["O, what only hurts me now would torture and kill me then! I will go—to-morrow.”", "오, 지금은 그저 나를 아프게만 하는 일이 그때는 나를 모질게 괴롭히고 죽일 거예요! 갈게요—내일.」"]], [["“And I shall not stay here.", "「나도 여기 머물지 않을 거야."], ["Though I didn’t like to initiate it, I have seen that it was advisable we should part—at least for a while, till I can better see the shape that things have taken, and can write to you.”", "내가 먼저 꺼내고 싶지는 않았지만, 우리가 갈라서는 게 마땅하다는 걸 알게 됐어—적어도 한동안은, 일이 어떤 모양새로 흘러갔는지 내가 좀 더 잘 헤아릴 수 있고, 너에게 편지를 쓸 수 있게 될 때까지는 말이야.」"]], [["Tess stole a glance at her husband.", "테스는 남편을 몰래 곁눈질했다."], ["He was pale, even tremulous; but, as before, she was appalled by the determination revealed in the depths of this gentle being she had married—the will to subdue the grosser to the subtler emotion, the substance to the conception, the flesh to the spirit.", "그는 핏기가 가시고, 심지어 떨고 있기까지 했다. 그러나 아까처럼, 그녀는 자신이 결혼한 이 온순한 존재의 깊은 곳에서 드러난 단호함에 소스라쳤다—더 거친 감정을 더 섬세한 감정에, 실체를 관념에, 육신을 정신에 굴복시키려는 의지였다."], ["Propensities, tendencies, habits, were as dead leaves upon the tyrannous wind of his imaginative ascendency.", "성벽도, 성향도, 습관도, 그 상상력의 우위가 휘두르는 폭압적인 바람 앞에서는 마른 낙엽이나 다름없었다."]], [["He may have observed her look, for he explained—", "그가 그녀의 시선을 알아챘는지, 그는 이렇게 설명했다—"]], [["“I think of people more kindly when I am away from them”; adding cynically, “God knows; perhaps we will shake down together some day, for weariness; thousands have done it!”", "「난 사람들에게서 떨어져 있을 때 그들을 더 너그럽게 생각하게 돼.」 그러고는 냉소적으로 덧붙였다. 「누가 알겠어. 어쩌면 언젠가 우리도, 지친 나머지, 한데 어우러져 살게 될지도 모르지. 수천 명이 그렇게 해 왔으니까!」"]], [["That day he began to pack up, and she went upstairs and began to pack also.", "그날 그는 짐을 꾸리기 시작했고, 그녀도 위층으로 올라가 짐을 꾸리기 시작했다."], ["Both knew that it was in their two minds that they might part the next morning for ever, despite the gloss of assuaging conjectures thrown over their proceeding because they were of the sort to whom any parting which has an air of finality is a torture.", "두 사람 다, 누그러뜨리는 추측의 겉치레를 자기네 행동 위에 덧입히긴 했어도, 마음속으로는 이튿날 아침 영영 갈라서게 될지도 모른다는 생각을 품고 있음을 서로 알았다. 두 사람은 무언가 마지막이라는 기미가 도는 이별이라면 그것이 곧 고문인, 그런 부류의 사람들이었던 까닭이다."], ["He knew, and she knew, that, though the fascination which each had exercised over the other—on her part independently of accomplishments—would probably in the first days of their separation be even more potent than ever, time must attenuate that effect; the practical arguments against accepting her as a housemate might pronounce themselves more strongly in the boreal light of a remoter view.", "그도 알고 그녀도 알았다. 서로가 서로에게 행사해 온 그 매혹이—그녀의 경우 무슨 재주와는 무관한 매혹이—갈라선 첫 며칠 동안은 그 어느 때보다도 더 강력해지겠지만, 시간이 그 효력을 차츰 옅게 만들고야 말리라는 것을. 그녀를 한집 식구로 받아들이는 데 반대하는 그 현실적인 논거들이, 더 멀찍이 떨어진 시야의 차디찬 북극광 속에서 한층 더 강하게 제 목소리를 낼지도 모른다는 것을."], ["Moreover, when two people are once parted—have abandoned a common domicile and a common environment—new growths insensibly bud upward to fill each vacated place; unforeseen accidents hinder intentions, and old plans are forgotten.", "게다가 두 사람이 일단 갈라서서—함께하던 거처와 함께하던 환경을 버리고 나면—새로운 싹들이 어느새 돋아 올라 비워진 자리를 저마다 메우고, 예기치 못한 일들이 작정을 가로막으며, 옛 계획들은 잊히는 법이다."]], [["# XXXVII", "# XXXVII"]], [["Midnight came and passed silently, for there was nothing to announce it in the Valley of the Froom.", "자정이 소리 없이 와서 지나갔으니, 프룸 골짜기에는 그것을 알릴 만한 것이 아무것도 없었다."]], [["Not long after one o’clock there was a slight creak in the darkened farmhouse once the mansion of the d’Urbervilles.", "한 시가 조금 지난 무렵, 한때 더버빌 가문의 저택이었던 어둑한 농가에서 가벼운 삐걱 소리가 났다."], ["Tess, who used the upper chamber, heard it and awoke.", "위층 방을 쓰던 테스가 그 소리를 듣고 잠에서 깼다."], ["It had come from the corner step of the staircase, which, as usual, was loosely nailed.", "그것은 계단 모퉁이 디딤판에서 난 소리였는데, 그 디딤판은 늘 그렇듯 못이 헐겁게 박혀 있었다."], ["She saw the door of her bedroom open, and the figure of her husband crossed the stream of moonlight with a curiously careful tread.", "그녀는 침실 문이 열리고, 남편의 모습이 묘하게 조심스러운 발걸음으로 달빛 줄기를 가로지르는 것을 보았다."], ["He was in his shirt and trousers only, and her first flush of joy died when she perceived that his eyes were fixed in an unnatural stare on vacancy.", "그는 셔츠와 바지만 입은 차림이었는데, 그의 두 눈이 부자연스럽게 허공을 응시하고 있음을 알아채자 처음 솟구쳤던 기쁨은 스러졌다."], ["When he reached the middle of the room he stood still and murmured in tones of indescribable sadness—", "방 한가운데에 이르자 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 형언할 수 없이 슬픈 어조로 중얼거렸다—"]], [["“Dead! dead! dead!”", "「죽었어! 죽었어! 죽었어!」"]], [["Under the influence of any strongly-disturbing force, Clare would occasionally walk in his sleep, and even perform strange feats, such as he had done on the night of their return from market just before their marriage, when he re-enacted in his bedroom his combat with the man who had insulted her.", "무언가 강하게 마음을 뒤흔드는 힘이 작용하면, 클레어는 이따금 잠결에 걸어 다녔고, 심지어 기이한 짓까지 해 보이곤 했다. 결혼 직전, 장에 갔다 돌아오던 날 밤, 자기 침실에서 그녀를 모욕한 사내와 벌인 격투를 재연해 보였던 것처럼."], ["Tess saw that continued mental distress had wrought him into that somnambulistic state now.", "테스는 끊임없는 마음의 고통이 지금 그를 그런 몽유 상태로 몰아넣었음을 알아챘다."]], [["Her loyal confidence in him lay so deep down in her heart, that, awake or asleep, he inspired her with no sort of personal fear.", "그를 향한 그녀의 변함없는 믿음은 마음속 깊은 곳에 자리 잡고 있어, 깨어 있든 잠들어 있든, 그는 그녀에게 일신상의 두려움 따위는 조금도 불러일으키지 않았다."], ["If he had entered with a pistol in his hand he would scarcely have disturbed her trust in his protectiveness.", "설령 그가 손에 권총을 들고 들어왔다 해도, 그의 보호하려는 마음에 대한 그녀의 믿음은 거의 흔들리지 않았으리라."]], [["Clare came close, and bent over her.", "클레어는 가까이 다가와 그녀 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["“Dead, dead, dead!”", "「죽었어, 죽었어, 죽었어!」"], ["he murmured.", "그가 중얼거렸다."], ["After fixedly regarding her for some moments with the same gaze of unmeasurable woe, he bent lower, enclosed her in his arms, and rolled her in the sheet as in a shroud.", "가늠할 수 없는 비탄의 그 똑같은 눈길로 잠시 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라본 뒤, 그는 더 낮게 몸을 숙여 그녀를 두 팔로 감싸안고는, 마치 수의로 싸듯 침대보로 그녀를 둘둘 말았다."], ["Then lifting her from the bed with as much respect as one would show to a dead body, he carried her across the room, murmuring—", "그러고는 죽은 사람을 대하듯 더없이 정중하게 그녀를 침대에서 들어 올려 방을 가로질러 안고 가며, 이렇게 중얼거렸다—"]], [["“My poor, poor Tess—my dearest, darling Tess!", "「가엾은, 가엾은 내 테스—내 가장 사랑스러운, 더없이 소중한 테스!"], ["So sweet, so good, so true!”", "어쩌면 그리도 다정하고, 그리도 착하고, 그리도 진실한지!」"]], [["The words of endearment, withheld so severely in his waking hours, were inexpressibly sweet to her forlorn and hungry heart.", "깨어 있는 동안에는 그토록 모질게 거두어들였던 그 사랑의 말들이, 버림받아 굶주린 그녀의 마음에는 이루 말할 수 없이 달콤했다."], ["If it had been to save her weary life she would not, by moving or struggling, have put an end to the position she found herself in.", "설령 지친 제 목숨을 구하기 위해서라 해도, 그녀는 몸을 뒤척이거나 발버둥 쳐서 지금 처한 이 자리를 끝장내려 하지는 않았으리라."], ["Thus she lay in absolute stillness, scarcely venturing to breathe, and, wondering what he was going to do with her, suffered herself to be borne out upon the landing.", "그리하여 그녀는 숨조차 거의 못 쉴 만큼 그저 가만히 누운 채, 그가 자신을 어떻게 하려는 걸까 궁금해하며, 층계참으로 들려 나가도록 자신을 내맡겼다."]], [["“My wife—dead, dead!”", "「내 아내가—죽었어, 죽었어!」"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["He paused in his labours for a moment to lean with her against the banister.", "그는 그녀를 안은 채 난간에 기대려고 잠시 하던 짓을 멈췄다."], ["Was he going to throw her down?", "그가 그녀를 아래로 내던지려는 걸까?"], ["Self-solicitude was near extinction in her, and in the knowledge that he had planned to depart on the morrow, possibly for always, she lay in his arms in this precarious position with a sense rather of luxury than of terror.", "그녀 안에서 제 한 몸을 아끼는 마음은 거의 사그라들어 있었고, 그가 이튿날 떠날 작정임을—어쩌면 영영 떠날 작정임을—알고 있던 터라, 그녀는 이 위태로운 자세로 그의 두 팔에 안긴 채 차라리 공포보다는 황홀함에 가까운 느낌으로 누워 있었다."], ["If they could only fall together, and both be dashed to pieces, how fit, how desirable.", "둘이 함께 떨어져, 둘 다 산산조각 날 수만 있다면, 그 얼마나 어울리고, 그 얼마나 바랄 만한 일이랴."]], [["However, he did not let her fall, but took advantage of the support of the handrail to imprint a kiss upon her lips—lips in the day-time scorned.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 떨어뜨리지 않고, 난간의 받침을 이용해 그녀의 입술에 입을 맞췄다—낮 동안에는 멸시받던 그 입술에."], ["Then he clasped her with a renewed firmness of hold, and descended the staircase.", "그러고는 새삼 단단히 고쳐 안고 계단을 내려갔다."], ["The creak of the loose stair did not awaken him, and they reached the ground-floor safely.", "헐거운 디딤판이 삐걱거려도 그는 깨어나지 않았고, 두 사람은 무사히 아래층에 닿았다."], ["Freeing one of his hands from his grasp of her for a moment, he slid back the door-bar and passed out, slightly striking his stockinged toe against the edge of the door.", "그는 한순간 그녀를 안은 한 손을 풀어 문빗장을 밀어 열고 밖으로 나갔는데, 양말만 신은 발가락이 문 모서리에 살짝 부딪혔다."], ["But this he seemed not to mind, and, having room for extension in the open air, he lifted her against his shoulder, so that he could carry her with ease, the absence of clothes taking much from his burden.", "그러나 그는 이를 개의치 않는 듯했고, 탁 트인 바깥에서 몸을 펼 자리가 생기자, 한결 수월하게 안고 갈 수 있도록 그녀를 어깨 쪽으로 추켜올렸으니, 옷을 걸치지 않은 덕에 짐이 한결 가벼워졌다."], ["Thus he bore her off the premises in the direction of the river a few yards distant.", "그리하여 그는 그녀를 집 밖으로 안고서, 몇 야드 떨어진 강 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["His ultimate intention, if he had any, she had not yet divined; and she found herself conjecturing on the matter as a third person might have done.", "그의 궁극적인 속셈이—설령 무슨 속셈이 있었다 해도—무엇인지 그녀는 아직 가늠하지 못했고, 마치 제삼자라도 된 듯 그 문제를 두고 이리저리 짐작해 보는 자신을 발견했다."], ["So easefully had she delivered her whole being up to him that it pleased her to think he was regarding her as his absolute possession, to dispose of as he should choose.", "그녀는 제 온 존재를 그에게 어찌나 편안히 내맡겼던지, 그가 자신을 마음대로 처분할 수 있는 절대적인 소유물로 여기고 있다고 생각하는 것이 그녀에게는 흐뭇했다."], ["It was consoling, under the hovering terror of to-morrow’s separation, to feel that he really recognized her now as his wife Tess, and did not cast her off, even if in that recognition he went so far as to arrogate to himself the right of harming her.", "내일의 이별이라는 공포가 어른거리는 가운데에서도, 그가 이제 정말로 자신을 아내 테스로 알아보고 있다는 것, 비록 그 알아봄이 자신을 해칠 권리를 스스로에게 부여하는 데까지 나아갔을지언정 자신을 내치지는 않는다는 것을 느끼는 일은 위안이 되었다."]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["now she knew what he was dreaming of—that Sunday morning when he had borne her along through the water with the other dairymaids, who had loved him nearly as much as she, if that were possible, which Tess could hardly admit.", "이제 그녀는 그가 무슨 꿈을 꾸고 있는지 알았다—그가 다른 젖 짜는 처녀들과 함께 자신을 안고 물길을 헤쳐 데려가던 그 일요일 아침이었다. 그 처녀들도, 만약 그게 가능한 일이라면—테스로서는 좀처럼 인정하기 어려운 일이었지만—자신만큼이나 그를 사랑했었다."], ["Clare did not cross the bridge with her, but proceeding several paces on the same side towards the adjoining mill, at length stood still on the brink of the river.", "클레어는 그녀를 안은 채 다리를 건너지 않고, 같은 쪽 강가를 따라 옆에 딸린 방앗간 쪽으로 몇 걸음 더 나아가더니, 이윽고 강기슭에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["Its waters, in creeping down these miles of meadowland, frequently divided, serpentining in purposeless curves, looping themselves around little islands that had no name, returning and re-embodying themselves as a broad main stream further on.", "이 몇 마일에 걸친 목초지를 기어 내려오는 그 강물은 이따금 갈라져, 정처 없는 굽이를 뱀처럼 그리며, 이름도 없는 작은 섬들을 고리처럼 휘감았다가, 다시 돌아와 한 몸을 이루어 저 아래에서 넓은 본류가 되었다."], ["Opposite the spot to which he had brought her was such a general confluence, and the river was proportionately voluminous and deep.", "그가 그녀를 데려온 자리 맞은편이 바로 그렇게 물줄기들이 한데 모이는 곳이라, 강은 그만큼 물이 불고 깊었다."], ["Across it was a narrow foot-bridge; but now the autumn flood had washed the handrail away, leaving the bare plank only, which, lying a few inches above the speeding current, formed a giddy pathway for even steady heads; and Tess had noticed from the window of the house in the day-time young men walking across upon it as a feat in balancing.", "강 위에는 좁은 발판 다리가 놓여 있었으나, 이제 가을 홍수가 난간을 쓸어 가 버려 휑한 널빤지만 남았는데, 그것이 내달리는 물살 위로 몇 인치쯤 떠 있어, 침착한 사람조차 어질어질한 통로가 되었다. 테스는 낮 동안 집 창문에서 젊은이들이 균형 잡기 묘기 삼아 그 위를 걸어 건너는 것을 본 적이 있었다."], ["Her husband had possibly observed the same performance; anyhow, he now mounted the plank, and, sliding one foot forward, advanced along it.", "어쩌면 그녀의 남편도 그 똑같은 광경을 보았는지, 어쨌든 그는 이제 그 널빤지에 올라서더니, 한 발을 앞으로 미끄러뜨리며 그 위를 나아갔다."]], [["Was he going to drown her? Probably he was.", "그가 그녀를 물에 빠뜨려 죽이려는 걸까?"], ["The spot was lonely, the river deep and wide enough to make such a purpose easy of accomplishment.", "아마 그런 것 같았다. 그 자리는 인적이 없었고, 강은 그런 속셈을 이루기에 충분할 만큼 깊고 넓었다."], ["He might drown her if he would; it would be better than parting to-morrow to lead severed lives.", "그가 원한다면 그녀를 빠뜨려 죽일 수도 있으리라. 그건 내일 갈라서서 따로따로의 삶을 사는 것보다 차라리 나았다."]], [["The swift stream raced and gyrated under them, tossing, distorting, and splitting the moon’s reflected face.", "빠른 물살이 두 사람 아래에서 내달리고 소용돌이치며, 물에 비친 달의 얼굴을 출렁이고, 일그러뜨리고, 쪼개 놓았다."], ["Spots of froth travelled past, and intercepted weeds waved behind the piles.", "물거품 방울들이 떠내려가고, 말뚝에 걸린 물풀들이 그 뒤에서 너울거렸다."], ["If they could both fall together into the current now, their arms would be so tightly clasped together that they could not be saved; they would go out of the world almost painlessly, and there would be no more reproach to her, or to him for marrying her.", "만약 둘이 함께 지금 저 물살 속으로 떨어질 수만 있다면, 두 팔이 어찌나 단단히 서로를 얽어 안고 있는지 결코 구해지지 못할 터였다. 두 사람은 거의 고통 없이 세상을 떠날 것이고, 그러면 더는 그녀를 향한, 또 그녀와 결혼한 그를 향한 비난도 없으리라."], ["His last half-hour with her would have been a loving one, while if they lived till he awoke, his day-time aversion would return, and this hour would remain to be contemplated only as a transient dream.", "그가 그녀와 보낸 마지막 반시간은 사랑이 깃든 시간이었으련만, 만약 둘이 살아남아 그가 깨어난다면, 그의 낮 동안의 혐오가 되돌아올 것이고, 이 시간은 그저 한순간 지나간 꿈으로만 곱씹어질 터였다."]], [["The impulse stirred in her, yet she dared not indulge it, to make a movement that would have precipitated them both into the gulf.", "둘 다를 저 심연 속으로 곤두박질치게 할 그런 움직임을 취하고픈 충동이 그녀 안에서 꿈틀거렸으나, 그녀는 감히 그 충동에 몸을 맡기지 못했다."], ["How she valued her own life had been proved; but his—she had no right to tamper with it.", "그녀가 제 목숨을 얼마나 하찮게 여기는지는 이미 증명된 바였다. 그러나 그의 목숨은—그녀에게는 그것을 함부로 건드릴 권리가 없었다."], ["He reached the other side with her in safety.", "그는 그녀를 안고 무사히 건너편에 다다랐다."]], [["Here they were within a plantation which formed the Abbey grounds, and taking a new hold of her he went onward a few steps till they reached the ruined choir of the Abbey-church.", "거기서 두 사람은 수도원 경내를 이루는 식림지 안에 들어섰고, 그는 그녀를 새로 고쳐 안고 몇 걸음 더 나아가, 이윽고 수도원 성당의 허물어진 성가대석에 이르렀다."], ["Against the north wall was the empty stone coffin of an abbot, in which every tourist with a turn for grim humour was accustomed to stretch himself.", "북쪽 벽 옆에는 어느 수도원장의 텅 빈 돌관이 놓여 있었는데, 음산한 익살에 마음이 동한 관광객이라면 으레 그 안에 누워 보곤 하던 것이었다."], ["In this Clare carefully laid Tess.", "그 안에 클레어는 테스를 조심스레 눕혔다."], ["Having kissed her lips a second time he breathed deeply, as if a greatly desired end were attained.", "그녀의 입술에 두 번째로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 그는 간절히 바라던 목적이 이루어지기라도 한 듯 깊이 숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Clare then lay down on the ground alongside, when he immediately fell into the deep dead slumber of exhaustion, and remained motionless as a log.", "그러고 나서 클레어는 그 옆 바닥에 드러누웠고, 곧바로 기진맥진한 자의 깊고 죽은 듯한 잠에 빠져들어, 통나무처럼 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["The spurt of mental excitement which had produced the effort was now over.", "그 행동을 빚어냈던 마음의 흥분은 이제 다 사위어 버린 것이었다."]], [["Tess sat up in the coffin. The night, though dry and mild for the season, was more than sufficiently cold to make it dangerous for him to remain here long, in his half-clothed state.", "테스는 관 속에서 몸을 일으켜 앉았다. 그날 밤은 철에 비해 메마르고 포근하긴 했어도, 반쯤 헐벗은 차림의 그가 여기 오래 머물기에는 위태로울 만큼 충분히 추웠다."], ["If he were left to himself he would in all probability stay there till the morning, and be chilled to certain death. She had heard of such deaths after sleep-walking.", "그를 제멋대로 내버려 둔다면 그는 십중팔구 아침까지 거기 머물다가 얼어붙어 영락없이 죽고 말 것이었다. 그녀는 몽유 끝에 그렇게 죽은 이들의 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다."], ["But how could she dare to awaken him, and let him know what he had been doing, when it would mortify him to discover his folly in respect of her?", "그러나 어떻게 감히 그를 깨워, 그가 무슨 짓을 하고 있었는지 알게 한단 말인가? 그녀를 두고 저지른 어리석은 짓을 깨닫는 일이 그를 욕보일 텐데."], ["Tess, however, stepping out of her stone confine, shook him slightly, but was unable to arouse him without being violent.", "그래도 테스는 돌 감옥에서 걸어 나와 그를 살짝 흔들어 보았으나, 거칠게 굴지 않고서는 그를 깨울 수가 없었다."], ["It was indispensable to do something, for she was beginning to shiver, the sheet being but a poor protection.", "무언가 하지 않으면 안 되었으니, 그녀가 몸을 떨기 시작한 까닭이었다. 침대보는 변변찮은 가리개일 뿐이었다."], ["Her excitement had in a measure kept her warm during the few minutes’ adventure; but that beatific interval was over.", "그 몇 분간의 모험을 치르는 동안에는 흥분이 어느 정도 그녀의 몸을 데워 주었으나, 그 더없이 행복한 한때는 이제 끝나 버렸다."]], [["It suddenly occurred to her to try persuasion; and accordingly she whispered in his ear, with as much firmness and decision as she could summon—", "문득 그녀에게 구슬려 보자는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그리하여 그녀는 그의 귀에 대고, 끌어모을 수 있는 한 굳세고 단호하게 속삭였다—"]], [["“Let us walk on, darling,”", "「우리 계속 걸어요, 여보.」"], ["at the same time taking him suggestively by the arm.", "그러면서 동시에 넌지시 그의 팔을 잡았다."], ["To her relief, he unresistingly acquiesced; her words had apparently thrown him back into his dream, which thenceforward seemed to enter on a new phase, wherein he fancied she had risen as a spirit, and was leading him to Heaven.", "다행스럽게도 그는 거역하지 않고 따랐다. 그녀의 말이 그를 다시 꿈속으로 돌려보낸 듯했는데, 그 꿈은 이제부터 새로운 국면으로 접어든 모양이었으니, 그 속에서 그는 그녀가 영혼이 되어 일어나 자신을 천국으로 이끌고 있다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["Thus she conducted him by the arm to the stone bridge in front of their residence, crossing which they stood at the manor-house door.", "이렇게 그녀는 그의 팔을 잡고 그를 그들의 거처 앞 돌다리까지 인도했고, 그것을 건너 저택 문 앞에 두 사람은 섰다."], ["Tess’s feet were quite bare, and the stones hurt her, and chilled her to the bone; but Clare was in his woollen stockings and appeared to feel no discomfort.", "테스의 발은 아주 맨발이었고, 돌멩이들이 그 발을 아프게 하고 뼛속까지 시리게 했다. 그러나 클레어는 모직 양말을 신고 있어 아무런 불편도 느끼지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["There was no further difficulty.", "더는 어려움이 없었다."], ["She induced him to lie down on his own sofa bed, and covered him up warmly, lighting a temporary fire of wood, to dry any dampness out of him.", "그녀는 그를 구슬려 그 자신의 소파 침상에 눕히고는, 따뜻하게 덮어 주었으며, 그의 몸에서 축축한 기운을 말려 내려고 임시로 장작불을 지폈다."], ["The noise of these attentions she thought might awaken him, and secretly wished that they might.", "그녀는 이렇게 살뜰히 보살피느라 나는 소리에 그가 깨어날까 봐 걱정스러우면서도, 속으로는 그러기를 은근히 바랐다."], ["But the exhaustion of his mind and body was such that he remained undisturbed.", "그러나 그의 마음과 몸이 어찌나 지쳐 있었던지 그는 흐트러짐 없이 잠들어 있었다."]], [["As soon as they met the next morning Tess divined that Angel knew little or nothing of how far she had been concerned in the night’s excursion, though, as regarded himself, he may have been aware that he had not lain still.", "이튿날 아침 두 사람이 마주치자마자 테스는, 에인절이 자신이 그 밤의 나들이에 얼마나 깊이 얽혀 있었는지를 거의, 아니 전혀 알지 못함을 알아챘다. 비록 자기 자신에 관해서는, 그가 가만히 누워만 있지는 않았음을 어렴풋이 의식했을지도 모르지만."], ["In truth, he had awakened that morning from a sleep deep as annihilation; and during those first few moments in which the brain, like a Samson shaking himself, is trying its strength, he had some dim notion of an unusual nocturnal proceeding.", "사실 그는 그날 아침 소멸처럼 깊은 잠에서 깨어났고, 뇌가 마치 몸을 떨쳐 일으키는 삼손처럼 제 힘을 시험하는 그 처음 몇 순간 동안, 그는 간밤에 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 있었다는 어렴풋한 느낌을 품었다."], ["But the realities of his situation soon displaced conjecture on the other subject.", "그러나 자신이 처한 현실이 이내 그 다른 일에 대한 짐작을 밀어내 버렸다."]], [["He waited in expectancy to discern some mental pointing; he knew that if any intention of his, concluded over-night, did not vanish in the light of morning, it stood on a basis approximating to one of pure reason, even if initiated by impulse of feeling; that it was so far, therefore, to be trusted.", "그는 무언가 마음의 방향을 알아챌 단서가 나타나기를 기다리며 지켜보았다. 그는 알았다. 만약 간밤에 내린 어떤 작정이 아침의 빛 속에서도 사라지지 않는다면, 그것은 비록 감정의 충동에서 비롯되었을지언정 순수한 이성에 가까운 토대 위에 서 있는 것이며, 따라서 그만큼 믿을 만한 것임을."], ["He thus beheld in the pale morning light the resolve to separate from her; not as a hot and indignant instinct, but denuded of the passionateness which had made it scorch and burn; standing in its bones; nothing but a skeleton, but none the less there.", "그리하여 그는 창백한 아침 빛 속에서, 그녀와 갈라서겠다는 결심을 보았다. 뜨겁고 분에 찬 본능으로서가 아니라, 그것을 들끓고 타오르게 했던 그 정념이 죄다 벗겨진 채, 앙상한 뼈대만 남아 서 있는 결심을. 한낱 해골에 지나지 않았으나, 그럼에도 엄연히 거기 있는 결심을."], ["Clare no longer hesitated.", "클레어는 더는 망설이지 않았다."]], [["At breakfast, and while they were packing the few remaining articles, he showed his weariness from the night’s effort so unmistakeably that Tess was on the point of revealing all that had happened; but the reflection that it would anger him, grieve him, stultify him, to know that he had instinctively manifested a fondness for her of which his common-sense did not approve, that his inclination had compromised his dignity when reason slept, again deterred her.", "아침 식사 때, 그리고 두 사람이 남은 몇 가지 물건을 꾸리는 동안, 그는 간밤의 노고에서 비롯된 피로를 어찌나 역력히 드러냈던지, 테스는 일어났던 모든 일을 털어놓을 뻔했다. 그러나 그가 본능적으로 자신을 향한 애정을 드러냈음을—그의 상식이 인정하지 않는 그 애정을, 이성이 잠든 사이 그의 끌림이 그의 위엄을 더럽혔음을 알게 되면 그가 화내고, 괴로워하고, 멋쩍어할 것이라는 생각이 또다시 그녀를 멈칫하게 했다."], ["It was too much like laughing at a man when sober for his erratic deeds during intoxication.", "그것은 술이 깬 사내를, 취중에 저지른 종잡을 수 없는 행동을 두고 비웃는 것이나 다름없었다."], ["It just crossed her mind, too, that he might have a faint recollection of his tender vagary, and was disinclined to allude to it from a conviction that she would take amatory advantage of the opportunity it gave her of appealing to him anew not to go.", "그가 자신의 다정했던 일탈을 어렴풋이나마 기억하고 있을지도 모르며, 그것이 그녀에게 떠나지 말라고 새삼 애원할 기회를 주는 셈이니 그녀가 그 기회를 틈타 애정 공세를 펼칠까 봐 그것을 입에 올리길 꺼리는 것일지도 모른다는 생각도 문득 그녀의 머리를 스쳤다."]], [["He had ordered by letter a vehicle from the nearest town, and soon after breakfast it arrived.", "그는 편지로 가장 가까운 읍에서 탈것을 한 대 불러 두었는데, 아침을 마치고 얼마 안 되어 그것이 도착했다."], ["She saw in it the beginning of the end—the temporary end, at least, for the revelation of his tenderness by the incident of the night raised dreams of a possible future with him.", "그녀는 그 안에서 끝의 시작을—적어도 한동안의 끝을 보았으니, 간밤의 그 일로 그의 다정함이 드러난 것이 그와 함께할 수 있을 어떤 미래에 대한 꿈을 불러일으켰던 까닭이다."], ["The luggage was put on the top, and the man drove them off, the miller and the old waiting-woman expressing some surprise at their precipitate departure, which Clare attributed to his discovery that the mill-work was not of the modern kind which he wished to investigate, a statement that was true so far as it went.", "짐이 위에 실리고, 마부가 두 사람을 태우고 떠났다. 방앗간 주인과 늙은 시중 아낙은 두 사람이 부랴부랴 떠나는 데에 다소 놀라움을 내비쳤는데, 클레어는 그것을, 방앗간 일이 자신이 살펴보고자 했던 근래의 방식이 아님을 알게 된 탓으로 둘러댔으니, 그것은 그만큼은 사실인 이야기였다."], ["Beyond this there was nothing in the manner of their leaving to suggest a _fiasco_, or that they were not going together to visit friends.", "그 이상으로, 두 사람이 떠나는 모양새에는 무슨 낭패가 있었다거나, 둘이 함께 친구들을 방문하러 가는 게 아니라는 기미는 전혀 없었다."]], [["Their route lay near the dairy from which they had started with such solemn joy in each other a few days back, and as Clare wished to wind up his business with Mr Crick, Tess could hardly avoid paying Mrs Crick a call at the same time, unless she would excite suspicion of their unhappy state.", "그들의 길은 며칠 전 서로에게 그토록 엄숙한 기쁨을 품고 떠나왔던 그 낙농장 근처를 지났다. 클레어가 크릭 씨와의 용무를 마무리 짓고자 했으므로, 테스로서는 자기네의 불행한 처지에 대한 의심을 사고 싶지 않은 한, 동시에 크릭 부인을 방문하지 않기란 좀처럼 어려운 일이었다."]], [["To make the call as unobtrusive as possible, they left the carriage by the wicket leading down from the high road to the dairy-house, and descended the track on foot, side by side.", "방문을 되도록 눈에 띄지 않게 하려고, 두 사람은 큰길에서 낙농장 집으로 내려가는 쪽문 곁에 마차를 세워 두고, 나란히 걸어서 그 길을 내려갔다."], ["The withy-bed had been cut, and they could see over the stumps the spot to which Clare had followed her when he pressed her to be his wife; to the left the enclosure in which she had been fascinated by his harp; and far away behind the cow-stalls the mead which had been the scene of their first embrace.", "버드나무 밭은 베어진 뒤라, 둘은 그루터기들 너머로, 클레어가 아내가 되어 달라고 조르며 그녀를 뒤따라왔던 그 자리를 볼 수 있었다. 왼쪽으로는 그녀가 그의 하프 소리에 홀렸던 울 안이 있었고, 멀찍이 외양간 뒤로는 두 사람의 첫 포옹의 무대였던 풀밭이 있었다."], ["The gold of the summer picture was now gray, the colours mean, the rich soil mud, and the river cold.", "여름 풍경의 금빛은 이제 잿빛으로 바랬고, 빛깔들은 초라했으며, 기름졌던 흙은 진창이었고, 강물은 차가웠다."]], [["Over the barton-gate the dairyman saw them, and came forward, throwing into his face the kind of jocularity deemed appropriate in Talbothays and its vicinity on the re-appearance of the newly-married.", "낙농장 마당 문 너머로 낙농장 주인이 그들을 보고 다가오면서, 갓 결혼한 부부가 다시 나타날 때 탈보세이즈와 그 근방에서 으레 어울린다고 여겨지는 그런 농지거리를 얼굴에 띄웠다."], ["Then Mrs Crick emerged from the house, and several others of their old acquaintance, though Marian and Retty did not seem to be there.", "그러자 크릭 부인이 집에서 나왔고, 그들의 옛 지인 몇몇도 나왔으나, 메리언과 레티는 거기 없는 듯했다."]], [["Tess valiantly bore their sly attacks and friendly humours, which affected her far otherwise than they supposed.", "테스는 그들의 짓궂은 공세와 정겨운 농지거리를 꿋꿋이 견뎌 냈는데, 그것은 그들이 생각하는 것과는 사뭇 다르게 그녀의 마음을 건드렸다."], ["In the tacit agreement of husband and wife to keep their estrangement a secret they behaved as would have been ordinary.", "남편과 아내가 서로의 소원해진 사이를 비밀로 하기로 한 말없는 약속에 따라, 두 사람은 여느 때와 다름없이 행동했다."], ["And then, although she would rather there had been no word spoken on the subject, Tess had to hear in detail the story of Marian and Retty.", "그러고 나서, 그녀로서는 차라리 그 이야기가 한마디도 나오지 않았더라면 싶었지만, 테스는 메리언과 레티의 사연을 낱낱이 들어야만 했다."], ["The later had gone home to her father’s, and Marian had left to look for employment elsewhere. They feared she would come to no good.", "레티는 친정아버지 집으로 돌아갔고, 메리언은 다른 데서 일자리를 찾으러 떠났다고 했다. 사람들은 메리언이 변변치 못한 신세가 될까 걱정했다."]], [["To dissipate the sadness of this recital Tess went and bade all her favourite cows goodbye, touching each of them with her hand, and as she and Clare stood side by side at leaving, as if united body and soul, there would have been something peculiarly sorry in their aspect to one who should have seen it truly; two limbs of one life, as they outwardly were, his arm touching hers, her skirts touching him, facing one way, as against all the dairy facing the other, speaking in their adieux as “we”, and yet sundered like the poles.", "이 이야기의 서글픔을 떨쳐 내려고 테스는 가서 자신이 아끼던 소들에게 일일이 손을 대며 작별을 고했다. 그리고 그녀와 클레어가 떠나려고 나란히 섰을 때, 마치 몸과 마음이 하나로 맺어진 듯한 그 모습은, 그것을 참되게 본 사람이라면 누구에게나 유난히 가엾게 비쳤으리라. 겉보기에는 한 생명의 두 팔다리처럼, 그의 팔이 그녀의 팔에 닿고, 그녀의 치맛자락이 그에게 닿은 채, 한쪽을 향해 서서—온 낙농장 사람들이 다른 쪽을 향한 것과는 반대로—작별 인사에서 「우리」라고 말하면서도, 두 사람은 양극처럼 멀리 갈라져 있었다."], ["Perhaps something unusually stiff and embarrassed in their attitude, some awkwardness in acting up to their profession of unity, different from the natural shyness of young couples, may have been apparent, for when they were gone Mrs Crick said to her husband—", "어쩌면 그들의 태도에 어딘가 유난히 뻣뻣하고 멋쩍은 데가, 하나가 되었노라 표방하는 데 어울리게 굴려는 데서 오는 어떤 어색함이, 젊은 부부의 자연스러운 수줍음과는 다른 그것이 드러났던 모양이다. 두 사람이 떠난 뒤 크릭 부인이 남편에게 이렇게 말한 걸 보면 말이다—"]], [["“How onnatural the brightness of her eyes did seem, and how they stood like waxen images and talked as if they were in a dream!", "「저 색시 눈빛이 어찌 그리 부자연스럽게 반짝이던지, 또 둘이 어찌 그리 밀랍 인형처럼 서서 꿈결인 양 말을 하던지!"], ["Didn’t it strike ’ee that ’twas so?", "당신도 그렇게 느껴지지 않았수?"], ["Tess had always sommat strange in her, and she’s not now quite like the proud young bride of a well-be-doing man.”", "테스는 어딘가 늘 좀 별난 데가 있더니, 이제 보니 잘사는 사내의 자랑스러운 새색시 같지가 않구려.」"]], [["They re-entered the vehicle, and were driven along the roads towards Weatherbury and Stagfoot Lane, till they reached the Lane inn, where Clare dismissed the fly and man.", "두 사람은 다시 탈것에 올라, 웨더베리와 스택풋 레인 쪽으로 길을 달려, 마침내 레인 여관에 다다랐고, 거기서 클레어는 마차와 마부를 돌려보냈다."], ["They rested here a while, and entering the Vale were next driven onward towards her home by a stranger who did not know their relations.", "두 사람은 여기서 잠시 쉬었다가, 골짜기로 들어서서, 두 사람의 관계를 알지 못하는 낯선 마부의 마차에 실려 그녀의 집 쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["At a midway point, when Nuttlebury had been passed, and where there were cross-roads, Clare stopped the conveyance and said to Tess that if she meant to return to her mother’s house it was here that he would leave her.", "너틀베리를 지나, 갈림길이 있는 중간쯤의 한 지점에 이르자, 클레어는 마차를 세우고 테스에게, 만약 그녀가 어머니 집으로 돌아갈 작정이라면 여기서 자신은 그녀와 헤어지겠다고 말했다."], ["As they could not talk with freedom in the driver’s presence he asked her to accompany him for a few steps on foot along one of the branch roads; she assented, and directing the man to wait a few minutes they strolled away.", "마부 앞에서는 자유로이 이야기를 나눌 수 없었으므로, 그는 그녀에게 갈림길 가운데 하나를 따라 몇 걸음 함께 걸어 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 응했고, 마부에게 몇 분 기다리라고 이른 뒤, 두 사람은 거닐며 멀어졌다."]], [["“Now, let us understand each other,”", "「자, 이제 우리 서로의 뜻을 분명히 해 두자.」"], ["he said gently.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“There is no anger between us, though there is that which I cannot endure at present.", "「우리 사이에 노여움은 없어. 다만 지금으로서는 내가 견딜 수 없는 무언가가 있을 뿐이야."], ["I will try to bring myself to endure it.", "그걸 견뎌 보도록 애써 볼게."], ["I will let you know where I go to as soon as I know myself.", "내가 어디로 가는지는 나 스스로 알게 되는 대로 너에게 알려 줄게."], ["And if I can bring myself to bear it—if it is desirable, possible—I will come to you.", "그리고 만약 내가 그것을 견뎌 낼 수 있게 된다면—그게 바람직하고, 또 가능한 일이라면—너에게 가겠어."], ["But until I come to you it will be better that you should not try to come to me.”", "하지만 내가 너에게 갈 때까지는, 네가 나에게 오려고 하지 않는 편이 나을 거야.」"]], [["The severity of the decree seemed deadly to Tess; she saw his view of her clearly enough; he could regard her in no other light than that of one who had practised gross deceit upon him.", "그 선고의 준엄함은 테스에게 죽음처럼 느껴졌다. 그가 자신을 어떻게 보고 있는지를 그녀는 너무도 또렷이 알았다. 그는 자신을, 그에게 엄청난 기만을 부린 여자 말고는 달리 어떤 모습으로도 볼 수 없는 것이었다."], ["Yet could a woman who had done even what she had done deserve all this?", "그러나 자기가 한 그런 짓을 한 여자라 한들 이 모든 벌을 받아 마땅하단 말인가?"], ["But she could contest the point with him no further.", "하지만 그녀는 그 점을 두고 그와 더 다툴 수 없었다."], ["She simply repeated after him his own words.", "그녀는 그저 그가 한 말을 그대로 따라 되뇌었을 뿐이다."]], [["“Until you come to me I must not try to come to you?”", "「당신이 나에게 올 때까지 나는 당신에게 가려고 하면 안 된다는 거죠?」"]], [["“Just so.”", "「바로 그래.」"]], [["“May I write to you?”", "「편지는 써도 되나요?」"]], [["“O yes—if you are ill, or want anything at all.", "「오 그럼—네가 아프거나, 무엇이든 필요한 게 있으면 말이야."], ["I hope that will not be the case; so that it may happen that I write first to you.”", "그런 일이 없기를 바라지만. 그러니 어쩌면 내가 너에게 먼저 편지를 쓰게 될지도 모르겠다.」"]], [["“I agree to the conditions, Angel; because you know best what my punishment ought to be; only—only—don’t make it more than I can bear!”", "「그 조건에 따를게요, 에인절. 내가 어떤 벌을 받아야 마땅한지는 당신이 가장 잘 아니까요. 다만—다만—내가 견딜 수 있는 것보다 더 무겁게는 하지 말아 줘요!」"]], [["That was all she said on the matter.", "그 일에 관해 그녀가 한 말은 그게 다였다."], ["If Tess had been artful, had she made a scene, fainted, wept hysterically, in that lonely lane, notwithstanding the fury of fastidiousness with which he was possessed, he would probably not have withstood her.", "만약 테스가 약삭빨라서, 그 외딴 오솔길에서 한바탕 소란을 피우고, 까무러치고, 발작하듯 흐느껴 울었더라면, 그를 사로잡은 그 까다로움의 광기에도 불구하고, 그는 아마 그녀를 당해 내지 못했으리라."], ["But her mood of long-suffering made his way easy for him, and she herself was his best advocate.", "그러나 그녀의 오래 참는 마음결이 그가 갈 길을 수월하게 만들어 주었으니, 그녀 자신이 그에게는 가장 좋은 변호인이었다."], ["Pride, too, entered into her submission—which perhaps was a symptom of that reckless acquiescence in chance too apparent in the whole d’Urberville family—and the many effective chords which she could have stirred by an appeal were left untouched.", "자존심 또한 그녀의 순순한 복종에 한몫했는데—이는 어쩌면 더버빌 가문 전체에서 너무도 뚜렷한, 운명에 무모하게 몸을 내맡기는 그 성향의 한 징후였는지도 모른다—그리하여 그녀가 호소만 했더라면 울려 낼 수 있었을 그 숱한 효과적인 심금들은 끝내 건드려지지 않은 채 남았다."]], [["The remainder of their discourse was on practical matters only.", "그들의 나머지 이야기는 오로지 실질적인 문제들뿐이었다."], ["He now handed her a packet containing a fairly good sum of money, which he had obtained from his bankers for the purpose.", "그는 이제 그녀에게 제법 넉넉한 액수의 돈이 든 꾸러미를 건넸는데, 그것은 이 목적을 위해 거래 은행에서 찾아 둔 것이었다."], ["The brilliants, the interest in which seemed to be Tess’s for her life only (if he understood the wording of the will), he advised her to let him send to a bank for safety; and to this she readily agreed.", "그 다이아몬드들은—유언장의 문구를 그가 제대로 이해했다면—테스가 살아 있는 동안에 한해 그녀의 것인 듯했으므로, 안전을 위해 자기가 은행에 맡기도록 해 달라고 그는 권했고, 그녀는 선뜻 이에 동의했다."]], [["These things arranged, he walked with Tess back to the carriage, and handed her in.", "이런 일들을 정해 두고 나서, 그는 테스와 함께 마차로 되돌아 걸어가, 그녀를 안에 태웠다."], ["The coachman was paid and told where to drive her.", "마부에게는 삯을 치르고 그녀를 어디로 데려다줄지 일러 두었다."], ["Taking next his own bag and umbrella—the sole articles he had brought with him hitherwards—he bade her goodbye; and they parted there and then.", "그러고 나서 그는 자기 가방과 우산을—이곳으로 오면서 그가 챙겨 온 단 두 가지 물건을—집어 들고는, 그녀에게 작별을 고했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 거기서 그 자리로 헤어졌다."]], [["The fly moved creepingly up a hill, and Clare watched it go with an unpremeditated hope that Tess would look out of the window for one moment.", "마차가 언덕을 기어오르듯 올라갔고, 클레어는, 테스가 한순간이라도 창밖을 내다봐 주었으면 하는, 미처 의도치 않은 바람을 품은 채 그것이 멀어져 가는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["But that she never thought of doing, would not have ventured to do, lying in a half-dead faint inside.", "그러나 그녀는 그럴 생각을 도무지 하지 못했으니, 반쯤 죽은 듯 까무러친 채 안에 누워, 감히 그럴 엄두도 내지 못했으리라."], ["Thus he beheld her recede, and in the anguish of his heart quoted a line from a poet, with peculiar emendations of his own—", "그리하여 그는 그녀가 멀어져 가는 것을 바라보았고, 마음의 비통함 속에서 한 시인의 시구 한 줄을, 그 나름으로 유난스럽게 고쳐서 읊조렸다—"]], [["God’s _not_ in his heaven: All’s _wrong_ with the world!", "하느님은 하늘에 계시지 않다. 세상만사 다 글렀다!"]], [["When Tess had passed over the crest of the hill he turned to go his own way, and hardly knew that he loved her still.", "테스가 언덕마루를 넘어가 버리자 그는 제 갈 길을 가려고 돌아섰고, 자신이 여전히 그녀를 사랑하고 있음을 거의 알지 못했다."]], [["# XXXVIII", "# XXXVIII"]], [["As she drove on through Blackmoor Vale, and the landscape of her youth began to open around her, Tess aroused herself from her stupor.", "블랙무어 골짜기를 가로질러 마차가 달려가고, 어린 시절의 풍경이 주위에 펼쳐지기 시작하자, 테스는 멍한 상태에서 정신을 차렸다."], ["Her first thought was how would she be able to face her parents?", "그녀의 첫 번째 생각은, 어떻게 부모님을 마주할 수 있을까 하는 것이었다."]], [["She reached a turnpike-gate which stood upon the highway to the village.", "그녀는 마을로 가는 큰길에 놓인 통행료 징수소 문에 다다랐다."], ["It was thrown open by a stranger, not by the old man who had kept it for many years, and to whom she had been known; he had probably left on New Year’s Day, the date when such changes were made.", "그 문을 열어 준 것은 낯선 사람이었지, 여러 해 그 문을 지켜 와 그녀를 알던 그 늙은이가 아니었다. 그 늙은이는 아마 설날에—그런 교체가 이루어지던 그날에—그만둔 모양이었다."], ["Having received no intelligence lately from her home, she asked the turnpike-keeper for news.", "근래에 집에서 아무 소식도 받지 못한 터라, 그녀는 그 문지기에게 소식을 물었다."]], [["“Oh—nothing, miss,” he answered.", "「오—아무 일도 없습니다요, 아가씨.」 그가 대답했다."], ["“Marlott is Marlott still.", "「말럿은 여전히 말럿이지요. 죽은 사람들도 있고 뭐 그렇고요."], ["Folks have died and that.", "존 더비필드 그 양반도, 이번 주에 따님 하나를 신사 농부한테 시집보냈답니다."], ["John Durbeyfield, too, hev had a daughter married this week to a gentleman-farmer; not from John’s own house, you know; they was married elsewhere; the gentleman being of that high standing that John’s own folk was not considered well-be-doing enough to have any part in it, the bridegroom seeming not to know how’t have been discovered that John is a old and ancient nobleman himself by blood, with family skillentons in their own vaults to this day, but done out of his property in the time o’ the Romans.", "존 그 양반 집에서 치른 건 아니고요, 알다시피요. 혼례는 딴 데서 치렀지요. 신랑 되는 양반이 워낙 지체 높은 사람이라, 존 그 양반네 식구들은 거기 한몫 낄 만큼 잘사는 축으로 안 쳐 줬다나 봅디다."], ["However, Sir John, as we call ’n now, kept up the wedding-day as well as he could, and stood treat to everybody in the parish; and John’s wife sung songs at The Pure Drop till past eleven o’clock.”", "신랑은, 존 그 양반이 핏줄로 따지면 자기가 오래되고 유서 깊은 귀족이고, 지금도 자기네 납골당에 집안 해골들이 떡하니 있는데, 로마 시절에 재산을 죄다 빼앗긴 거라는 걸, 그게 어찌 밝혀졌는지도 모르는 눈치더라고요. 어쨌든, 이제 우리가 존 경이라 부르는 그 양반은, 혼례 날을 할 수 있는 한 떡 벌어지게 차렸고, 교구 사람 모두한테 한턱을 쐈답니다. 그리고 존 그 양반 마누라는 퓨어 드롭에서 열한 시가 넘도록 노래를 불러 댔지요.」"]], [["Hearing this, Tess felt so sick at heart that she could not decide to go home publicly in the fly with her luggage and belongings.", "이 말을 듣자 테스는 가슴이 어찌나 메스껍던지, 짐이며 살림살이를 실은 마차를 타고 버젓이 집으로 들어갈 마음을 도무지 먹을 수가 없었다."], ["She asked the turnpike-keeper if she might deposit her things at his house for a while, and, on his offering no objection, she dismissed her carriage, and went on to the village alone by a back lane.", "그녀는 문지기에게 자기 물건들을 잠시 그의 집에 맡겨 두어도 되겠느냐고 물었고, 그가 이의를 달지 않자, 마차를 돌려보내고는 뒷길로 혼자서 마을을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["At sight of her father’s chimney she asked herself how she could possibly enter the house?", "아버지의 집 굴뚝이 눈에 들어오자, 그녀는 도대체 어떻게 저 집에 들어갈 수 있을까 자문했다."], ["Inside that cottage her relations were calmly supposing her far away on a wedding-tour with a comparatively rich man, who was to conduct her to bouncing prosperity; while here she was, friendless, creeping up to the old door quite by herself, with no better place to go to in the world.", "저 오두막 안에서 그녀의 식구들은, 그녀가 비교적 부유한 사내와 함께 멀리 신혼여행을 떠나 있으리라고, 그 사내가 그녀를 펄펄 나는 번영으로 이끌어 주리라고 태평하게 짐작하고 있을 터였다. 그런데 정작 그녀는 여기, 의지할 데 하나 없이, 세상에 달리 갈 데도 없이, 홀로 그 낡은 문으로 살금살금 다가서고 있었다."]], [["She did not reach the house unobserved. Just by the garden-hedge she was met by a girl who knew her—one of the two or three with whom she had been intimate at school.", "그녀는 아무에게도 들키지 않고 집에 다다르지는 못했다. 정원 울타리 바로 곁에서 그녀는 자신을 아는 한 처녀와—학교 다닐 적 가깝게 지냈던 두세 명 가운데 하나와 마주쳤다."], ["After making a few inquiries as to how Tess came there, her friend, unheeding her tragic look, interrupted with—", "테스가 어떻게 여기 왔는지 몇 마디 캐묻고 나서, 그 친구는 그녀의 비극적인 얼굴빛은 아랑곳없이 불쑥 끼어들어 물었다—"]], [["“But where’s thy gentleman, Tess?”", "「그런데 네 신랑은 어디 있어, 테스?」"]], [["Tess hastily explained that he had been called away on business, and, leaving her interlocutor, clambered over the garden-hedge, and thus made her way to the house.", "테스는 그가 볼일이 있어 불려 갔다고 황급히 둘러대고는, 그 말상대를 남겨 둔 채 정원 울타리를 기어 넘어, 그렇게 집으로 가는 길을 찾아들었다."]], [["As she went up the garden-path she heard her mother singing by the back door, coming in sight of which she perceived Mrs Durbeyfield on the doorstep in the act of wringing a sheet.", "정원 길을 올라가는데 어머니가 뒷문 곁에서 노래 부르는 소리가 들렸고, 그쪽이 눈에 들어오자, 더비필드 부인이 문간 디딤돌에서 침대보를 짜고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Having performed this without observing Tess, she went indoors, and her daughter followed her.", "그녀는 테스를 알아채지 못한 채 그 일을 마치고는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 딸이 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["The washing-tub stood in the same old place on the same old quarter-hogshead, and her mother, having thrown the sheet aside, was about to plunge her arms in anew.", "빨래통은 늘 놓이던 그 자리, 늘 쓰던 그 사 분의 일짜리 큰 통 위에 그대로 있었고, 어머니는 침대보를 한쪽으로 던져 놓고 막 다시 두 팔을 담그려던 참이었다."]], [["“Why—Tess!—my chil’—I thought you was married!—married really and truly this time—we sent the cider—”", "「아니—테스!—얘야—난 네가 결혼한 줄로만—이번엔 정말로 제대로 식을 올린 줄 알았는데—우리가 사과주도 보냈잖니—」"]], [["“Yes, mother; so I am.”", "「네, 어머니. 결혼했어요.」"]], [["“Going to be?”", "「하게 됐다고?」"]], [["“No—I am married.”", "「아뇨—결혼했어요.」"]], [["“Married!", "「결혼을 해!"], ["Then where’s thy husband?”", "그럼 네 남편은 어디 있어?」"]], [["“Oh, he’s gone away for a time.”", "「오, 잠시 멀리 떠났어요.」"]], [["“Gone away!", "「멀리 떠나!"], ["When was you married, then?", "그럼 결혼은 언제 한 거냐?"], ["The day you said?”", "네가 말한 그날?」"]], [["“Yes, Tuesday, mother.”", "「네, 화요일에요, 어머니.」"]], [["“And now ’tis on’y Saturday, and he gone away?”", "「그런데 이제 겨우 토요일인데, 벌써 멀리 떠났다고?」"]], [["“Yes, he’s gone.”", "「네, 떠났어요.」"]], [["“What’s the meaning o’ that?", "「그게 대체 무슨 소리냐?"], ["’Nation seize such husbands as you seem to get, say I!”", "네가 얻는다는 남편들은 죄다 이 모양이니, 망할 놈들 같으니, 내 말이!」"]], [["“Mother!”", "「어머니!」"], ["Tess went across to Joan Durbeyfield, laid her face upon the matron’s bosom, and burst into sobs.", "테스는 조운 더비필드에게로 다가가, 그 푸근한 가슴에 얼굴을 묻고는 흐느낌을 터뜨렸다."], ["“I don’t know how to tell ’ee, mother!", "「어떻게 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠어요, 어머니!"], ["You said to me, and wrote to me, that I was not to tell him.", "어머니가 저한테 말씀하셨고, 편지로도 쓰셨잖아요, 그이한테 말하지 말라고요."], ["But I did tell him—I couldn’t help it—and he went away!”", "그런데 저는 그이한테 말해 버렸어요—어쩔 수가 없었어요—그랬더니 그이가 떠나 버렸어요!」"]], [["“O you little fool—you little fool!”", "「오 이 어리석은 것아—이 어리석은 것아!」"], ["burst out Mrs Durbeyfield, splashing Tess and herself in her agitation. “My good God!", "더비필드 부인은 어찌나 흥분했는지 테스와 제 몸에 물을 튀겨 가며 버럭 소리쳤다. 「맙소사!"], ["that ever I should ha’ lived to say it, but I say it again, you little fool!”", "내가 이런 말을 하게 될 줄이야, 그래도 다시 한번 말하마, 이 어리석은 것아!」"]], [["Tess was convulsed with weeping, the tension of so many days having relaxed at last.", "테스는 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨었으니, 그토록 여러 날의 긴장이 마침내 풀린 것이었다."]], [["“I know it—I know—I know!”", "「알아요—알아요—저도 알아요!」"], ["she gasped through her sobs.", "그녀가 흐느낌 사이로 가쁘게 말했다."], ["“But, O my mother, I could not help it!", "「하지만, 오 어머니, 저는 어쩔 수가 없었어요!"], ["He was so good—and I felt the wickedness of trying to blind him as to what had happened!", "그이는 너무도 착했어요—그래서 일어났던 일을 두고 그이의 눈을 가리려 한다는 게 너무도 못된 짓으로 느껴졌어요!"], ["If—if—it were to be done again—I should do the same.", "만약—만약—다시 그래야 한다 해도—저는 똑같이 할 거예요."], ["I could not—I dared not—so sin—against him!”", "그이에게 그런 죄를—그토록 죄를 지을 수는—감히 그럴 수는 없었어요!」"]], [["“But you sinned enough to marry him first!”", "「그래도 처음에 그 사람이랑 결혼할 만큼은 죄를 지었잖니!」"]], [["“Yes, yes; that’s where my misery do lie!", "「네, 네. 바로 거기에 제 비참함이 있어요!"], ["But I thought he could get rid o’ me by law if he were determined not to overlook it.", "하지만 저는 그이가 마음만 굳게 먹고 그 일을 못 본 척 넘어가지 않을 작정이라면, 법으로 저를 떼어 버릴 수 있는 줄 알았어요."], ["And O, if you knew—if you could only half know how I loved him—how anxious I was to have him—and how wrung I was between caring so much for him and my wish to be fair to him!”", "그리고 오, 어머니가 아셨더라면—제가 그이를 얼마나 사랑했는지, 그이를 얼마나 간절히 갖고 싶어 했는지, 그리고 그이를 그토록 끔찍이 아끼는 마음과 그이에게 떳떳하고 싶은 바람 사이에서 제가 얼마나 갈가리 찢겼는지를 반만이라도 아셨더라면!」"]], [["Tess was so shaken that she could get no further, and sank, a helpless thing, into a chair.", "테스는 어찌나 몸이 떨렸던지 더는 말을 잇지 못하고, 맥없이 무너지듯 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["“Well, well; what’s done can’t be undone!", "「그래, 그래. 이미 엎질러진 물은 도로 담을 수 없지!"], ["I’m sure I don’t know why children o’ my bringing forth should all be bigger simpletons than other people’s—not to know better than to blab such a thing as that, when he couldn’t ha’ found it out till too late!”", "내가 낳은 자식들이 어째서 죄다 남의 자식들보다 더 한심한 멍청이여야 하는지 나도 정말 모르겠다—그 사람이 너무 늦어서야 알게 될 텐데, 그런 일을 굳이 나불대다니, 그것보다 더 분별없는 짓이 어디 있냐!」"], ["Here Mrs Durbeyfield began shedding tears on her own account as a mother to be pitied.", "여기서 더비필드 부인은 가엾은 어미 신세가 되어 자기 몫의 눈물을 흘리기 시작했다."], ["“What your father will say I don’t know,”", "「네 아버지가 뭐라 하실지 난 모르겠다.」"], ["she continued; “for he’s been talking about the wedding up at Rolliver’s and The Pure Drop every day since, and about his family getting back to their rightful position through you—poor silly man!—and now you’ve made this mess of it!", "그녀가 말을 이었다. 「그 양반은 날마다 롤리버즈며 퓨어 드롭에 가서 이 혼사 자랑을 늘어놓고, 너를 통해 집안이 제자리를 되찾게 됐다고 떠벌리고 다녔는데—가엾고 어리석은 양반 같으니!—그런데 이제 네가 이렇게 다 망쳐 놨으니!"], ["The Lord-a-Lord!”", "어이구 맙소사!」"]], [["As if to bring matters to a focus, Tess’s father was heard approaching at that moment.", "마치 일을 한 점으로 모으기라도 하려는 듯, 바로 그 순간 테스의 아버지가 다가오는 소리가 들렸다."], ["He did not, however, enter immediately, and Mrs Durbeyfield said that she would break the bad news to him herself, Tess keeping out of sight for the present.", "그러나 그는 곧바로 들어오지는 않았고, 더비필드 부인은 자기가 직접 그에게 이 나쁜 소식을 전하겠노라고, 테스는 당분간 눈에 띄지 않게 있으라고 말했다."], ["After her first burst of disappointment Joan began to take the mishap as she had taken Tess’s original trouble, as she would have taken a wet holiday or failure in the potato-crop; as a thing which had come upon them irrespective of desert or folly; a chance external impingement to be borne with; not a lesson.", "처음의 실망이 한바탕 터져 나온 뒤, 조운은 이 낭패를, 테스의 애초의 사달을 받아들였을 때처럼, 마치 비 오는 휴일이나 농사의 흉작을 받아들이듯 받아들이기 시작했다. 감자 농사처럼, 자격이나 어리석음과는 상관없이 그들에게 닥쳐온 일로, 견뎌야 할 외부의 우연한 충돌로, 교훈이 아닌 것으로 받아들였다."]], [["Tess retreated upstairs and beheld casually that the beds had been shifted, and new arrangements made.", "테스는 위층으로 물러갔다가 침대가 옮겨지고 새로 자리가 잡힌 것을 무심코 보았다."], ["Her old bed had been adapted for two younger children.", "그녀의 옛 침대는 어린 동생 둘이 쓰도록 손질되어 있었다."], ["There was no place here for her now.", "이제 여기에 그녀가 누울 자리는 없었다."]], [["The room below being unceiled she could hear most of what went on there.", "아래층 방은 천장이 마감되어 있지 않아 그녀는 거기서 벌어지는 일을 거의 다 들을 수 있었다."], ["Presently her father entered, apparently carrying in a live hen.", "이윽고 아버지가 들어왔는데, 보아하니 살아 있는 암탉 한 마리를 안고 있었다."], ["He was a foot-haggler now, having been obliged to sell his second horse, and he travelled with his basket on his arm.", "그는 이제 두 번째 말마저 팔아야 했던 탓에 도보 행상꾼이 되어, 바구니를 팔에 걸고 다녔다."], ["The hen had been carried about this morning as it was often carried, to show people that he was in his work, though it had lain, with its legs tied, under the table at Rolliver’s for more than an hour.", "그 암탉은 그가 일에 나서 있음을 사람들에게 보이려고 오늘 아침 내내 그렇게 자주 안고 다닌 것이었지만, 정작 다리가 묶인 채 롤리버 술집 탁자 밑에 한 시간 넘게 누워 있었다."]], [["“We’ve just had up a story about—”", "\"방금 우리가—\""], ["Durbeyfield began, and thereupon related in detail to his wife a discussion which had arisen at the inn about the clergy, originated by the fact of his daughter having married into a clerical family.", "더비필드가 입을 열더니, 그 자리에서 술집에서 일어났던 성직자에 관한 논의를 아내에게 소상히 늘어놓았다. 그것은 자기 딸이 성직자 집안에 시집갔다는 사실에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["“They was formerly styled ‘sir’, like my own ancestry,”", "\"옛날에는 그것들도 우리 조상처럼 '경'이라 불렸지.\""], ["he said, “though nowadays their true style, strictly speaking, is ‘clerk’ only.”", "그는 말했다. \"요즘이야 엄밀히 따지면 그저 '서기'에 지나지 않지만 말이야.\""], ["As Tess had wished that no great publicity should be given to the event, he had mentioned no particulars.", "테스가 이 일이 크게 알려지지 않기를 바랐던 터라, 그는 자세한 내막은 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["He hoped she would remove that prohibition soon.", "그는 그녀가 곧 그 금지를 거두어 주기를 바랐다."], ["He proposed that the couple should take Tess’s own name, d’Urberville, as uncorrupted.", "그는 그 부부가 변질되지 않은 테스 본래의 성, 더버빌을 쓰면 어떻겠느냐고 했다."], ["It was better than her husbands’s.", "그게 남편 성보다 낫다는 것이었다."], ["He asked if any letter had come from her that day.", "그는 오늘 그녀한테서 편지가 오지 않았느냐고 물었다."]], [["Then Mrs Durbeyfield informed him that no letter had come, but Tess unfortunately had come herself.", "그러자 더비필드 부인은 편지는 오지 않았으나 안타깝게도 테스가 직접 왔다고 일러 주었다."]], [["When at length the collapse was explained to him, a sullen mortification, not usual with Durbeyfield, overpowered the influence of the cheering glass.", "마침내 모든 게 무너졌다는 사정을 듣고 나자, 더비필드답지 않은 침울한 굴욕감이 흥을 돋우던 술기운의 작용을 짓눌렀다."], ["Yet the intrinsic quality of the event moved his touchy sensitiveness less than its conjectured effect upon the minds of others.", "그러나 그 사건의 본질적인 무게보다도, 그것이 남들의 마음에 어떻게 비칠까 하는 짐작이 더 그의 예민한 신경을 건드렸다."]], [["“To think, now, that this was to be the end o’t!”", "\"생각해 보게나, 이게 그 끝이라니!\""], ["said Sir John.", "존 경이 말했다."], ["“And I with a family vault under that there church of Kingsbere as big as Squire Jollard’s ale-cellar, and my folk lying there in sixes and sevens, as genuine county bones and marrow as any recorded in history.", "\"킹스비어의 저 교회 밑에 졸라드 지주네 술 창고만 한 우리 집안 납골당이 있고, 우리 조상들이 거기 떼로 누워 있단 말이야, 역사에 기록된 누구 못지않게 진짜배기 지방 명문가 뼈와 골수로 말이지."], ["And now to be sure what they fellers at Rolliver’s and The Pure Drop will say to me!", "그런데 이제 롤리버랑 퓨어 드롭 패거리가 나한테 뭐라고 할지 뻔하다고! 그것들이 눈을 흘기고 노려보며 이러겠지."], ["How they’ll squint and glane, and say, ‘This is yer mighty match is it; this is yer getting back to the true level of yer forefathers in King Norman’s time!’ I feel this is too much, Joan; I shall put an end to myself, title and all—I can bear it no longer!...", "'이게 자네 그 대단한 혼사인가, 이게 노르만 왕 시절 자네 조상들 본래 신분으로 돌아간 꼴인가!' 이건 너무하다, 조앤. 작위고 뭐고 내 손으로 끝장을 내야겠다—더는 못 견디겠어!…"], ["But she can make him keep her if he’s married her?”", "그래도 그자가 그애와 결혼했다면 그애를 부양하게 만들 수 있는 거 아닌가?\""]], [["“Why, yes.", "\"그야, 그렇지요."], ["But she won’t think o’ doing that.”", "하지만 그애는 그럴 생각조차 안 할 거예요.\""]], [["“D’ye think he really have married her?—or is it like the first—”", "\"그자가 정말로 그애와 결혼했다고 생각해?—아니면 그 첫 번째 일처럼—\""]], [["Poor Tess, who had heard as far as this, could not bear to hear more.", "여기까지 들은 가엾은 테스는 더는 들을 수가 없었다."], ["The perception that her word could be doubted even here, in her own parental house, set her mind against the spot as nothing else could have done.", "자기 친정집에서조차, 제 말이 의심받을 수 있다는 깨달음은 다른 무엇으로도 할 수 없을 만큼 그녀의 마음을 이 집에서 등 돌리게 했다."], ["How unexpected were the attacks of destiny!", "운명의 공격은 어찌나 뜻밖인가!"], ["And if her father doubted her a little, would not neighbours and acquaintance doubt her much?", "아버지가 그녀를 조금이라도 의심한다면, 이웃과 지인 들은 얼마나 더 의심하겠는가?"], ["O, she could not live long at home!", "아, 그녀는 집에서 오래 살 수 없었다!"]], [["A few days, accordingly, were all that she allowed herself here, at the end of which time she received a short note from Clare, informing her that he had gone to the North of England to look at a farm.", "그리하여 그녀는 며칠만 자신에게 허락했고, 그 끝에 클레어에게서 짧은 편지를 받았다. 농장을 보러 잉글랜드 북부로 갔다는 내용이었다."], ["In her craving for the lustre of her true position as his wife, and to hide from her parents the vast extent of the division between them, she made use of this letter as her reason for again departing, leaving them under the impression that she was setting out to join him.", "그의 아내라는 자신의 진짜 지위가 주는 광채를 갈망하던 마음에서, 또 둘 사이에 벌어진 엄청난 간극을 부모에게 감추려는 마음에서, 그녀는 이 편지를 다시 떠나는 구실로 삼았고, 부모에게는 자기가 남편과 합류하러 떠나는 것이라는 인상을 남겨 두었다."], ["Still further to screen her husband from any imputation of unkindness to her, she took twenty-five of the fifty pounds Clare had given her, and handed the sum over to her mother, as if the wife of a man like Angel Clare could well afford it, saying that it was a slight return for the trouble and humiliation she had brought upon them in years past.", "더 나아가 남편이 자기에게 매정했다는 혐의를 받지 않도록 막으려고, 그녀는 클레어가 준 오십 파운드 가운데 이십오 파운드를 떼어 어머니에게 건넸다. 마치 앤젤 클레어 같은 사람의 아내라면 너끈히 그럴 수 있다는 듯이, 지난 세월 자기가 그들에게 끼친 수고와 굴욕에 대한 작은 보답이라며."], ["With this assertion of her dignity she bade them farewell; and after that there were lively doings in the Durbeyfield household for some time on the strength of Tess’s bounty, her mother saying, and, indeed, believing, that the rupture which had arisen between the young husband and wife had adjusted itself under their strong feeling that they could not live apart from each other.", "이렇게 자신의 위엄을 내세우며 그녀는 작별을 고했다. 그러고 나서 한동안 더비필드네 집안에서는 테스의 후한 인심 덕에 활기찬 일들이 벌어졌고, 그녀의 어머니는 젊은 부부 사이에 벌어졌던 균열이 서로 떨어져서는 살 수 없다는 강한 감정 아래 저절로 봉합되었노라고 말했고, 실은 그렇게 믿기도 했다."]], [["# XXXIX", "# XXXIX"]], [["It was three weeks after the marriage that Clare found himself descending the hill which led to the well-known parsonage of his father.", "결혼한 지 삼 주 뒤, 클레어는 자기 아버지의 그 잘 알려진 목사관으로 이어지는 언덕을 내려가고 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["With his downward course the tower of the church rose into the evening sky in a manner of inquiry as to why he had come; and no living person in the twilighted town seemed to notice him, still less to expect him.", "그가 내려가는 길에 교회 탑이 저녁 하늘로 솟아올라, 그가 왜 왔느냐고 묻는 듯한 모양새를 했다. 어스름이 깔린 마을의 살아 있는 사람 중 누구도 그를 알아채지 못했고, 더더욱 그를 기다린 이도 없는 듯했다."], ["He was arriving like a ghost, and the sound of his own footsteps was almost an encumbrance to be got rid of.", "그는 유령처럼 도착하고 있었고, 제 발소리마저 떨쳐 버려야 할 거추장스러운 것만 같았다."]], [["The picture of life had changed for him.", "그에게 삶의 그림은 달라져 있었다."], ["Before this time he had known it but speculatively; now he thought he knew it as a practical man; though perhaps he did not, even yet.", "이전까지 그는 삶을 그저 사변적으로만 알았다. 이제 그는 실제적인 인간으로서 삶을 안다고 여겼다. 어쩌면 여전히 그렇지 못했을지도 모르지만."], ["Nevertheless humanity stood before him no longer in the pensive sweetness of Italian art, but in the staring and ghastly attitudes of a Wiertz Museum, and with the leer of a study by Van Beers.", "그럼에도 인류는 이제 이탈리아 미술의 애잔한 감미로움 속에서가 아니라, 비르츠 미술관의 부릅뜬 눈과 소름 끼치는 자세로, 또 판 베이르스의 습작에 담긴 음흉한 곁눈질을 띤 채 그의 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["His conduct during these first weeks had been desultory beyond description.", "이 첫 몇 주 동안 그의 행동은 이루 말할 수 없이 종잡을 데가 없었다."], ["After mechanically attempting to pursue his agricultural plans as though nothing unusual had happened, in the manner recommended by the great and wise men of all ages, he concluded that very few of those great and wise men had ever gone so far outside themselves as to test the feasibility of their counsel.", "마치 별일 없었다는 듯, 모든 시대의 위대하고 현명한 자들이 권하는 방식대로 기계적으로 농사 계획을 좇으려 애써 본 뒤, 그는 그 위대하고 현명한 자들 가운데 자기 충고가 실행 가능한지 시험해 볼 만큼 자기 자신 밖으로 멀리 나가 본 이는 거의 없으리라고 결론지었다. \"이것이 으뜸가는 것이다."], ["“This is the chief thing: be not perturbed,”", "동요하지 말라\"고 이교도 도덕가는 말했다."], ["said the Pagan moralist. That was just Clare’s own opinion.", "그것은 바로 클레어 자신의 견해이기도 했다."], ["But he was perturbed.", "그러나 그는 동요했다."], ["“Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid,”", "\"너희 마음에 근심하지 말고 두려워하지도 말라\"고 나사렛 사람은 말했다."], ["said the Nazarene. Clare chimed in cordially; but his heart was troubled all the same.", "클레어는 진심으로 맞장구쳤다. 그래도 그의 마음은 여전히 근심에 차 있었다."], ["How he would have liked to confront those two great thinkers, and earnestly appeal to them as fellow-man to fellow-men, and ask them to tell him their method!", "그는 이 두 위대한 사상가와 얼굴을 맞대고, 사람 대 사람으로서 간곡히 호소하며, 그 방법을 일러 달라고 청하고 싶은 마음이 얼마나 간절했던가!"]], [["His mood transmuted itself into a dogged indifference till at length he fancied he was looking on his own existence with the passive interest of an outsider.", "그의 기분은 끈질긴 무관심으로 바뀌어 갔고, 마침내 그는 외부인의 무심한 흥미를 가지고 제 존재를 바라보고 있다는 착각에 빠졌다."]], [["He was embittered by the conviction that all this desolation had been brought about by the accident of her being a d’Urberville.", "이 모든 황폐가 그녀가 더버빌가 사람이라는 우연 탓에 빚어졌다는 확신이 그를 쓰라리게 했다."], ["When he found that Tess came of that exhausted ancient line, and was not of the new tribes from below, as he had fondly dreamed, why had he not stoically abandoned her in fidelity to his principles?", "테스가 자기가 곱게 꿈꾸어 온 대로 밑바닥에서 올라온 새로운 족속이 아니라, 그 쇠진한 옛 혈통에서 나온 사람임을 알았을 때, 어찌하여 그는 자기 원칙에 충실하게 그녀를 금욕적으로 버리지 못했던가?"], ["This was what he had got by apostasy, and his punishment was deserved.", "이것이 변절의 대가였고, 그의 벌은 마땅한 것이었다."]], [["Then he became weary and anxious, and his anxiety increased.", "이윽고 그는 지치고 불안해졌으며, 그 불안은 점점 커져 갔다."], ["He wondered if he had treated her unfairly.", "그는 자기가 그녀를 부당하게 대한 것은 아닌지 곱씹었다."], ["He ate without knowing that he ate, and drank without tasting.", "그는 먹는지도 모른 채 먹었고, 맛도 모른 채 마셨다."], ["As the hours dropped past, as the motive of each act in the long series of bygone days presented itself to his view, he perceived how intimately the notion of having Tess as a dear possession was mixed up with all his schemes and words and ways.", "시간이 한 시간 한 시간 흘러가면서, 지나간 그 긴 나날들의 행위 하나하나의 동기가 눈앞에 떠오르면서, 그는 테스를 소중한 소유물로 가진다는 관념이 자기의 온갖 계획과 말과 행실에 얼마나 긴밀히 얽혀 있었는지를 깨달았다."]], [["In going hither and thither he observed in the outskirts of a small town a red-and-blue placard setting forth the great advantages of the Empire of Brazil as a field for the emigrating agriculturist.", "여기저기 다니던 중 그는 어느 작은 마을 외곽에서 빨강과 파랑의 벽보 하나를 보았다. 이민 가는 농부에게 브라질 제국이 펼쳐 주는 큰 이점을 늘어놓은 것이었다."], ["Land was offered there on exceptionally advantageous terms.", "거기서는 유난히 유리한 조건으로 땅을 내준다고 했다."], ["Brazil somewhat attracted him as a new idea.", "브라질은 새로운 발상으로서 그를 다소 끌어당겼다."], ["Tess could eventually join him there, and perhaps in that country of contrasting scenes and notions and habits the conventions would not be so operative which made life with her seem impracticable to him here.", "테스도 언젠가는 거기서 합류할 수 있고, 어쩌면 풍경과 관념과 관습이 사뭇 다른 그 나라에서라면, 여기서는 그녀와의 삶을 불가능해 보이게 만드는 그 관습들이 그토록 위력을 떨치지는 않을 것이었다."], ["In brief he was strongly inclined to try Brazil, especially as the season for going thither was just at hand.", "요컨대 그는 브라질을 시도해 보고픈 마음에 강하게 기울었고, 더욱이 그곳으로 떠날 철이 마침 코앞에 닥쳐 있었다."]], [["With this view he was returning to Emminster to disclose his plan to his parents, and to make the best explanation he could make of arriving without Tess, short of revealing what had actually separated them.", "이런 생각으로 그는 부모에게 자기 계획을 알리고, 테스를 데려오지 않은 채 도착한 것에 대해 둘을 갈라놓은 진짜 사정을 드러내지 않는 선에서 할 수 있는 최선의 해명을 하려고 에민스터로 돌아오는 길이었다."], ["As he reached the door the new moon shone upon his face, just as the old one had done in the small hours of that morning when he had carried his wife in his arms across the river to the graveyard of the monks; but his face was thinner now.", "그가 문에 이르렀을 때 초승달이 그의 얼굴을 비추었다. 그가 아내를 품에 안고 강을 건너 수도사들의 묘지로 데려가던 그날 새벽녘에 그믐달이 비추었던 것과 꼭 같았다. 다만 이제 그의 얼굴은 더 야위어 있었다."]], [["Clare had given his parents no warning of his visit, and his arrival stirred the atmosphere of the Vicarage as the dive of the kingfisher stirs a quiet pool.", "클레어는 부모에게 자신의 방문을 미리 알리지 않았던 터라, 그의 도착은 마치 물총새가 잔잔한 못 속으로 자맥질하듯 목사관의 분위기를 휘저어 놓았다."], ["His father and mother were both in the drawing-room, but neither of his brothers was now at home.", "그의 아버지와 어머니는 둘 다 거실에 있었으나 두 형은 이제 집에 없었다."], ["Angel entered, and closed the door quietly behind him.", "앤젤은 들어와 등 뒤로 문을 조용히 닫았다."]], [["“But—where’s your wife, dear Angel?”", "\"그런데—네 아내는 어디 있느냐, 앤젤?\""], ["cried his mother.", "그의 어머니가 외쳤다."], ["“How you surprise us!”", "\"너 정말 우리를 놀라게 하는구나!\""]], [["“She is at her mother’s—temporarily.", "\"잠시—친정에 가 있습니다."], ["I have come home rather in a hurry because I’ve decided to go to Brazil.”", "브라질로 가기로 결심해서 좀 서둘러 집에 왔어요.\""]], [["“Brazil! Why they are all Roman Catholics there surely!”", "\"브라질이라니! 거기는 분명 죄다 로마 가톨릭교도들이 아니냐!\""]], [["“Are they?", "\"그런가요?"], ["I hadn’t thought of that.”", "그건 생각 못 했습니다.\""]], [["But even the novelty and painfulness of his going to a Papistical land could not displace for long Mr and Mrs Clare’s natural interest in their son’s marriage.", "그러나 가톨릭 나라로 떠난다는 그 새삼스러움과 괴로움조차도, 아들의 결혼에 대한 클레어 부부의 자연스러운 관심을 오래 밀어내지는 못했다."]], [["“We had your brief note three weeks ago announcing that it had taken place,”", "\"삼 주 전에 결혼이 이루어졌다는 네 짧은 편지를 받았다.\" 클레어 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs Clare, “and your father sent your godmother’s gift to her, as you know.", "\"그리고 네 아버지가, 너도 알다시피, 네 대모의 선물을 그애에게 보냈지."], ["Of course it was best that none of us should be present, especially as you preferred to marry her from the dairy, and not at her home, wherever that may be.", "물론 우리 중 아무도 참석하지 않는 편이 가장 좋았어, 특히 네가 그애 집이 어디든 거기서가 아니라 낙농장에서 결혼하기를 원했으니 말이다."], ["It would have embarrassed you, and given us no pleasure.", "그랬더라면 너도 거북했을 테고, 우리한테도 기쁠 게 없었을 테지."], ["Your bothers felt that very strongly.", "네 형들도 그 점을 아주 절실히 느꼈단다."], ["Now it is done we do not complain, particularly if she suits you for the business you have chosen to follow instead of the ministry of the Gospel....", "이제 다 끝난 일이니 우리는 불평하지 않으마, 특히 네가 복음의 사역 대신 택한 그 일에 그애가 어울리는 사람이라면 더욱. …"], ["Yet I wish I could have seen her first, Angel, or have known a little more about her.", "그래도 그애를 먼저 봤더라면, 앤젤, 아니면 그애에 대해 조금이라도 더 알았더라면 좋았을 것을."], ["We sent her no present of our own, not knowing what would best give her pleasure, but you must suppose it only delayed.", "우리는 무엇이 그애를 가장 기쁘게 할지 몰라 우리 나름의 선물을 보내지 않았다만, 그저 미뤄진 것뿐이라고 여겨 다오."], ["Angel, there is no irritation in my mind or your father’s against you for this marriage; but we have thought it much better to reserve our liking for your wife till we could see her.", "앤젤, 이 결혼 때문에 내 마음에도 네 아버지 마음에도 너에 대한 노여움은 없다. 다만 네 아내를 직접 볼 수 있을 때까지 그애에 대한 호의를 아껴 두는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 생각했을 뿐이야."], ["And now you have not brought her.", "그런데 이제 너는 그애를 데려오지 않았구나."], ["It seems strange.", "이상한 일이다."], ["What has happened?”", "무슨 일이 있었던 게냐?\""]], [["He replied that it had been thought best by them that she should go to her parents’ home for the present, whilst he came there.", "그는 자기가 이리로 오는 동안 그녀는 당분간 친정에 가 있는 편이 가장 낫다고 둘이서 생각했다고 답했다."]], [["“I don’t mind telling you, dear mother,”", "\"숨김없이 말씀드리지요, 어머니.\""], ["he said, “that I always meant to keep her away from this house till I should feel she could come with credit to you.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 그녀가 어머니께 떳떳이 올 수 있다고 느껴질 때까지 이 집에서 떼어 두려고 늘 마음먹고 있었습니다."], ["But this idea of Brazil is quite a recent one.", "하지만 이 브라질 생각은 아주 최근에 떠오른 것이고요."], ["If I do go it will be unadvisable for me to take her on this my first journey.", "만약 제가 정말 간다면, 이 첫 여정에 그녀를 데려가는 건 좋지 않을 겁니다."], ["She will remain at her mother’s till I come back.”", "제가 돌아올 때까지 그녀는 친정에 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“And I shall not see her before you start?”", "\"그럼 네가 떠나기 전에 나는 그애를 못 보는 게냐?\""]], [["He was afraid they would not.", "그는 못 볼 것 같다고 했다."], ["His original plan had been, as he had said, to refrain from bringing her there for some little while—not to wound their prejudices—feelings—in any way; and for other reasons he had adhered to it.", "그가 말한 대로 본래 그의 계획은, 어떤 식으로든 부모의 선입견을—감정을—다치게 하지 않으려고 한동안 그녀를 이리로 데려오지 않는 것이었고, 다른 이유들 때문에도 그는 그 계획을 고수해 왔다."], ["He would have to visit home in the course of a year, if he went out at once; and it would be possible for them to see her before he started a second time—with her.", "곧장 떠난다면 그는 일 년 안에 한 번 고향에 들러야 할 테고, 그가 두 번째로—그녀와 함께—떠나기 전에 부모가 그녀를 볼 수 있을 터였다."]], [["A hastily prepared supper was brought in, and Clare made further exposition of his plans.", "서둘러 차린 저녁상이 들어왔고, 클레어는 자기 계획을 더 풀어 설명했다."], ["His mother’s disappointment at not seeing the bride still remained with her.", "신부를 보지 못한 어머니의 실망은 여전히 그녀에게 남아 있었다."], ["Clare’s late enthusiasm for Tess had infected her through her maternal sympathies, till she had almost fancied that a good thing could come out of Nazareth—a charming woman out of Talbothays Dairy.", "테스에 대한 클레어의 지난 열정이 모성의 공감을 타고 그녀에게도 옮아, 그녀는 나사렛에서도 좋은 것이 나올 수 있다고—탤버세이즈 낙농장에서도 매력적인 여인이 나올 수 있다고—거의 믿게 될 지경이었다."], ["She watched her son as he ate.", "그녀는 아들이 식사하는 모습을 지켜보았다."]], [["“Cannot you describe her?", "\"그애를 좀 그려 볼 수 없겠니?"], ["I am sure she is very pretty, Angel.”", "분명 아주 예쁜 아이일 게다, 앤젤.\""]], [["“Of that there can be no question!”", "\"그건 의심할 여지가 없지요!\""], ["he said, with a zest which covered its bitterness.", "그는 쓰라림을 덮어 가리는 열기를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“And that she is pure and virtuous goes without question?”", "\"그리고 그애가 정결하고 정숙하다는 건 두말할 것도 없겠지?\""]], [["“Pure and virtuous, of course, she is.”", "\"정결하고 정숙하지요, 물론입니다.\""]], [["“I can see her quite distinctly.", "\"그애가 아주 또렷이 보이는구나."], ["You said the other day that she was fine in figure; roundly built; had deep red lips like Cupid’s bow; dark eyelashes and brows, an immense rope of hair like a ship’s cable; and large eyes violety-bluey-blackish.”", "며칠 전에 네가 그러지 않았니, 그애가 몸매가 곱고, 둥글게 잘 빠졌으며, 큐피드의 활처럼 짙은 붉은 입술에, 검은 속눈썹과 눈썹을 지녔고, 배의 밧줄처럼 굵은 머리채에, 보랏빛 도는 푸르스름하고 거뭇한 큰 눈을 가졌다고.\""]], [["“I did, mother.”", "\"그랬지요, 어머니.\""]], [["“I quite see her. And living in such seclusion she naturally had scarce ever seen any young man from the world without till she saw you.”", "\"그애가 아주 잘 보인다. 그렇게 외진 곳에서 살았으니, 자연히 너를 보기 전까지는 바깥세상 젊은이를 거의 본 적이 없었겠구나.\""]], [["“Scarcely.”", "\"거의 그렇지요.\""]], [["“You were her first love?”", "\"네가 그애 첫사랑이었니?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론입니다.\""]], [["“There are worse wives than these simple, rosy-mouthed, robust girls of the farm.", "\"이렇게 소박하고, 입술 발그레하고, 튼튼한 농가 처녀들보다 더 못한 아내들도 세상엔 많단다."], ["Certainly I could have wished—well, since my son is to be an agriculturist, it is perhaps but proper that his wife should have been accustomed to an outdoor life.”", "물론 나야 바랐을 테지만—그래, 내 아들이 농부가 될 바에야, 그 아내가 바깥일에 익숙한 사람이었던 것도 어쩌면 합당한 일이겠지.\""]], [["His father was less inquisitive; but when the time came for the chapter from the Bible which was always read before evening prayers, the Vicar observed to Mrs Clare—", "그의 아버지는 덜 캐물었다. 그러나 저녁 기도 전에 늘 읽던 성경 구절을 읽을 때가 되자, 목사는 클레어 부인에게 일렀다—"]], [["“I think, since Angel has come, that it will be more appropriate to read the thirty-first of Proverbs than the chapter which we should have had in the usual course of our reading?”", "\"앤젤이 왔으니, 평소 차례대로 읽었어야 할 장보다는 잠언 삼십일 장을 읽는 게 더 어울리겠소.\""]], [["“Yes, certainly,”", "\"네, 그러시고말고요.\""], ["said Mrs Clare.", "클레어 부인이 말했다."], ["“The words of King Lemuel”", "\"르무엘 왕의 말씀이지요.\""], ["(she could cite chapter and verse as well as her husband). “My dear son, your father has decided to read us the chapter in Proverbs in praise of a virtuous wife.", "(그녀도 남편 못지않게 장과 절을 인용할 수 있었다.) \"사랑하는 아들아, 네 아버지가 잠언에서 현숙한 아내를 칭송하는 장을 우리에게 읽어 주기로 하셨다."], ["We shall not need to be reminded to apply the words to the absent one.", "그 말씀을 자리에 없는 그 아이에게 적용하라고 굳이 일러 줄 필요는 없겠지."], ["May Heaven shield her in all her ways!”", "하늘이 그애의 모든 길을 지켜 주시기를!\""]], [["A lump rose in Clare’s throat. The portable lectern was taken out from the corner and set in the middle of the fireplace, the two old servants came in, and Angel’s father began to read at the tenth verse of the aforesaid chapter—", "클레어의 목구멍에 무언가가 치밀어 올랐다. 들고 다닐 수 있는 성경대를 구석에서 꺼내어 벽난로 한가운데에 놓았고, 늙은 하인 둘이 들어왔으며, 앤젤의 아버지는 방금 말한 그 장의 십 절부터 읽기 시작했다—"]], [["“Who can find a virtuous woman? for her price is far above rubies.", "\"누가 현숙한 여인을 찾아 얻겠느냐."], ["She riseth while it is yet night, and giveth meat to her household. She girdeth her loins with strength and strengtheneth her arms. She perceiveth that her merchandise is good; her candle goeth not out by night.", "그의 값은 진주보다 더 하니라."], ["She looketh well to the ways of her household, and eateth not the bread of idleness. Her children arise up and call her blessed; her husband also, and he praiseth her. Many daughters have done virtuously, but thou excellest them all.”", "그는 밤이 새기 전에 일어나서 그 집안사람에게 음식을 나누어 주며, 힘으로 허리를 묶고 그 팔을 강하게 하며, 자기의 무역하는 것이 이로운 줄을 깨닫고 밤에 등불을 끄지 아니하며, 자기 집안일을 보살피고 게으른 양식을 먹지 아니하나니, 그의 자식들은 일어나 감사하며, 그의 남편도 일어나 그를 칭찬하기를, 덕행 있는 여자가 많으나 그대는 모든 여자보다 뛰어나다 하느니라.\""]], [["When prayers were over, his mother said—", "기도가 끝나자, 그의 어머니가 말했다—"]], [["“I could not help thinking how very aptly that chapter your dear father read applied, in some of its particulars, to the woman you have chosen.", "\"네 아버지가 읽으신 그 장이, 그 몇몇 대목에서, 네가 택한 여인에게 어쩌면 그토록 꼭 들어맞는지 생각하지 않을 수 없었단다."], ["The perfect woman, you see, was a working woman; not an idler; not a fine lady; but one who used her hands and her head and her heart for the good of others.", "알겠니, 그 완벽한 여인은 일하는 여인이었어. 게으른 여자도 아니요, 고운 귀부인도 아니요, 제 손과 머리와 마음을 남을 위해 쓰는 여인이었지."], ["‘Her children arise up and call her blessed; her husband also, and he praiseth her. Many daughters have done virtuously, but she excelleth them all.’ Well, I wish I could have seen her, Angel.", "'그의 자식들은 일어나 감사하며, 그의 남편도 일어나 그를 칭찬하기를, 덕행 있는 여자가 많으나 그대는 모든 여자보다 뛰어나다 하느니라.' 그래, 그애를 봤더라면 좋았을 것을, 앤젤."], ["Since she is pure and chaste, she would have been refined enough for me.”", "그애가 정결하고 순결하다면, 나한테는 충분히 교양 있는 아이였을 텐데.\""]], [["Clare could bear this no longer.", "클레어는 더는 이것을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["His eyes were full of tears, which seemed like drops of molten lead.", "그의 눈은 눈물로 가득 찼는데, 그것은 마치 녹은 납방울 같았다."], ["He bade a quick good night to these sincere and simple souls whom he loved so well; who knew neither the world, the flesh, nor the devil in their own hearts, only as something vague and external to themselves.", "그는 자신이 그토록 사랑하는 이 진실하고 소박한 영혼들에게 황급히 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다. 세상도, 육신도, 악마도 제 마음속에서는 알지 못하고, 그저 자기들 밖에 있는 무언가 막연한 것으로만 아는 이들에게."], ["He went to his own chamber.", "그는 제 방으로 갔다."]], [["His mother followed him, and tapped at his door.", "그의 어머니가 뒤따라와 방문을 두드렸다."], ["Clare opened it to discover her standing without, with anxious eyes.", "클레어가 문을 여니, 어머니가 근심 어린 눈으로 밖에 서 있었다."]], [["“Angel,”", "\"앤젤.\""], ["she asked, “is there something wrong that you go away so soon?", "그녀가 물었다. \"이렇게 곧 떠나다니 무슨 잘못된 일이라도 있는 게냐?"], ["I am quite sure you are not yourself.”", "네가 영 너답지 않은 게 분명하구나.\""]], [["“I am not, quite, mother,”", "\"네, 좀 그렇습니다, 어머니.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“About her?", "\"그애 때문이냐?"], ["Now, my son, I know it is that—I know it is about her!", "자, 아들아, 나는 그게 그 일이란 걸 안다—그애 때문이란 걸 알아!"], ["Have you quarrelled in these three weeks?”", "이 삼 주 동안 둘이 다툰 게냐?\""]], [["“We have not exactly quarrelled,” he said.", "\"꼭 다툰 건 아니에요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“But we have had a difference—”", "\"하지만 우리 사이에 견해 차이가—\""]], [["“Angel—is she a young woman whose history will bear investigation?”", "\"앤젤—그애가 내력을 캐물어도 떳떳할 만한 처녀냐?\""]], [["With a mother’s instinct Mrs Clare had put her finger on the kind of trouble that would cause such a disquiet as seemed to agitate her son.", "어머니의 본능으로 클레어 부인은, 아들을 그토록 들쑤셔 놓은 듯한 그 종류의 근심에 손가락을 정확히 짚었다."]], [["“She is spotless!”", "\"그녀는 한 점 흠이 없습니다!\""], ["he replied; and felt that if it had sent him to eternal hell there and then he would have told that lie.", "그는 대답했다. 그러면서 그 거짓말 때문에 당장 영원한 지옥에 떨어진다 해도 그렇게 말했으리라고 느꼈다."]], [["“Then never mind the rest.", "\"그렇다면 나머지는 신경 쓰지 마라."], ["After all, there are few purer things in nature then an unsullied country maid.", "따지고 보면 자연에서 때 묻지 않은 시골 처녀만큼 정결한 것도 드물단다."], ["Any crudeness of manner which may offend your more educated sense at first, will, I am sure, disappear under the influence or your companionship and tuition.”", "처음에 네 더 교양 있는 감각에 거슬릴 만한 거친 태도가 좀 있더라도, 네 곁에서 함께 지내며 가르침을 받는 동안 분명 사라질 게다.\""]], [["Such terrible sarcasm of blind magnanimity brought home to Clare the secondary perception that he had utterly wrecked his career by this marriage, which had not been among his early thoughts after the disclosure.", "이 눈먼 너그러움의 끔찍한 빈정거림은, 그가 이 결혼으로 자기 앞길을 완전히 망쳐 버렸다는 부차적인 깨달음을 클레어에게 일깨워 주었다. 그 깨달음은 비밀이 밝혀진 뒤 그의 머릿속에 처음부터 떠오른 생각은 아니었다."], ["True, on his own account he cared very little about his career; but he had wished to make it at least a respectable one on account of his parents and brothers.", "사실 그 자신을 두고 보자면 자기 앞길 따위는 그다지 마음 쓰지 않았다. 그러나 그는 부모와 형들을 생각해서 적어도 그것을 떳떳한 것으로 만들고 싶었다."], ["And now as he looked into the candle its flame dumbly expressed to him that it was made to shine on sensible people, and that it abhorred lighting the face of a dupe and a failure.", "그리고 이제 촛불을 들여다보니, 그 불꽃이 그에게 말없이 일러 주었다. 자기는 분별 있는 사람들을 비추라고 만들어졌으며, 속아 넘어간 자, 실패한 자의 얼굴을 밝히는 일은 질색이라고."]], [["When his agitation had cooled he would be at moments incensed with his poor wife for causing a situation in which he was obliged to practise deception on his parents.", "동요가 가라앉으면 그는 가엾은 아내에게 화가 치미는 순간이 있었다. 그녀가 자기로 하여금 부모를 속이지 않을 수 없는 처지에 빠뜨렸다는 것 때문에."], ["He almost talked to her in his anger, as if she had been in the room.", "그는 화가 나서 마치 그녀가 방 안에 있기라도 한 듯 거의 그녀에게 말을 걸었다."], ["And then her cooing voice, plaintive in expostulation, disturbed the darkness, the velvet touch of her lips passed over his brow, and he could distinguish in the air the warmth of her breath.", "그러면 그녀의 비둘기 같은 목소리가, 항변하듯 애처롭게, 어둠을 흔들었고, 그녀의 입술이 비단결처럼 그의 이마를 스쳤으며, 그는 공기 속에서 그녀의 따스한 숨결을 분간할 수 있었다."]], [["This night the woman of his belittling deprecations was thinking how great and good her husband was.", "이날 밤, 그가 깎아내리며 헐뜯던 그 여인은 제 남편이 얼마나 위대하고 선한 사람인지를 생각하고 있었다."], ["But over them both there hung a deeper shade than the shade which Angel Clare perceived, namely, the shade of his own limitations.", "그러나 두 사람 위에는 앤젤 클레어가 알아챈 것보다 더 깊은 그늘이 드리워 있었으니, 곧 그 자신의 한계라는 그늘이었다."], ["With all his attempted independence of judgement this advanced and well-meaning young man, a sample product of the last five-and-twenty years, was yet the slave to custom and conventionality when surprised back into his early teachings.", "판단의 독립을 그토록 애써 시도했음에도, 지난 이십오 년의 한 표본적 산물인 이 진보적이고 선의에 찬 젊은이는, 불시에 어린 시절의 가르침으로 되돌아가게 되자 여전히 관습과 인습의 노예였다."], ["No prophet had told him, and he was not prophet enough to tell himself, that essentially this young wife of his was as deserving of the praise of King Lemuel as any other woman endowed with the same dislike of evil, her moral value having to be reckoned not by achievement but by tendency.", "본질적으로 따지자면 이 젊은 아내가, 악을 똑같이 싫어하는 마음을 지닌 다른 어떤 여인 못지않게 르무엘 왕의 칭송을 받을 자격이 있다는 것을—그녀의 도덕적 가치는 성취가 아니라 성향으로 헤아려야 한다는 것을—어떤 예언자도 그에게 일러 주지 않았고, 그 자신도 스스로에게 일러 줄 만한 예언자가 못 되었다."], ["Moreover, the figure near at hand suffers on such occasion, because it shows up its sorriness without shade; while vague figures afar off are honoured, in that their distance makes artistic virtues of their stains.", "게다가 가까이 있는 형상은 그런 경우 손해를 보는데, 그늘 없이 제 초라함을 고스란히 드러내기 때문이다. 반면 멀리 있는 어렴풋한 형상은 떠받들어지니, 그 거리가 그 얼룩마저 예술적 미덕으로 만들어 주는 까닭이다."], ["In considering what Tess was not, he overlooked what she was, and forgot that the defective can be more than the entire.", "테스가 무엇이 아닌지를 따지느라, 그는 그녀가 무엇인지를 놓쳤고, 결함 있는 것이 온전한 것보다 더 클 수 있음을 잊었다."]], [["# XL", "# XL"]], [["At breakfast Brazil was the topic, and all endeavoured to take a hopeful view of Clare’s proposed experiment with that country’s soil, notwithstanding the discouraging reports of some farm-labourers who had emigrated thither and returned home within the twelve months.", "아침 식사 자리에서는 브라질이 화제였고, 모두가 그 나라 땅을 두고 클레어가 벌이려는 실험을 희망적으로 보려 애썼다. 거기로 이민 갔다가 일 년 안에 고향으로 돌아온 몇몇 농장 일꾼들의 의기를 꺾는 소식에도 불구하고."], ["After breakfast Clare went into the little town to wind up such trifling matters as he was concerned with there, and to get from the local bank all the money he possessed.", "식사 후 클레어는 그곳에서 자기가 관계된 자질구레한 일들을 마무리하고 지역 은행에서 가진 돈을 죄다 찾으려고 작은 읍내로 들어갔다."], ["On his way back he encountered Miss Mercy Chant by the church, from whose walls she seemed to be a sort of emanation.", "돌아오는 길에 그는 교회 옆에서 머시 챈트 양과 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 마치 그 교회 벽에서 풍겨 나온 무슨 산물 같았다."], ["She was carrying an armful of Bibles for her class, and such was her view of life that events which produced heartache in others wrought beatific smiles upon her—an enviable result, although, in the opinion of Angel, it was obtained by a curiously unnatural sacrifice of humanity to mysticism.", "그녀는 자기 반 학생들을 위한 성경책을 한 아름 안고 있었고, 그녀의 인생관이란, 남들에게는 가슴앓이를 일으키는 일들이 그녀에게는 더없이 행복한 미소를 빚어내게 하는 것이었다—부러운 결과이긴 했으나, 앤젤이 보기에 그것은 인간성을 신비주의에 바치는 기이하고 부자연스러운 희생으로 얻은 것이었다."]], [["She had learnt that he was about to leave England, and observed what an excellent and promising scheme it seemed to be.", "그녀는 그가 잉글랜드를 떠나려 한다는 것을 듣고는, 그게 참으로 훌륭하고 전도유망한 계획 같다고 했다."]], [["“Yes; it is a likely scheme enough in a commercial sense, no doubt,” he replied.", "\"그래, 상업적인 의미에서야 그럴듯한 계획이지, 아마도.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["“But, my dear Mercy, it snaps the continuity of existence.", "\"하지만 머시, 그건 삶의 연속성을 뚝 끊어 버려."], ["Perhaps a cloister would be preferable.”", "차라리 수도원이 더 나을지도 몰라.\""]], [["“A cloister!", "\"수도원이라뇨!"], ["O, Angel Clare!”", "어머, 앤젤 클레어!\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“Why, you wicked man, a cloister implies a monk, and a monk Roman Catholicism.”", "\"아니, 못된 사람 같으니, 수도원이라면 수도사를 뜻하고, 수도사라면 로마 가톨릭을 뜻하잖아요.\""]], [["“And Roman Catholicism sin, and sin damnation.", "\"그리고 로마 가톨릭은 죄를, 죄는 지옥살이를 뜻하고."], ["Thou art in a parlous state, Angel Clare.”", "그대는 위태로운 처지에 있구려, 앤젤 클레어.\""]], [["“_I_ glory in my Protestantism!”", "\"저는 제 신교 신앙을 자랑으로 여겨요!\""], ["she said severely.", "그녀가 엄하게 말했다."]], [["Then Clare, thrown by sheer misery into one of the demoniacal moods in which a man does despite to his true principles, called her close to him, and fiendishly whispered in her ear the most heterodox ideas he could think of.", "그러자 클레어는, 순전한 비참함에 빠져, 사람이 제 참된 원칙을 거스르며 짓궂게 구는 그런 악마적인 기분 중 하나에 사로잡혀, 그녀를 가까이 불러서는 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 이단적인 생각들을 마귀처럼 그녀의 귀에 속삭였다."], ["His momentary laughter at the horror which appeared on her fair face ceased when it merged in pain and anxiety for his welfare.", "그녀의 고운 얼굴에 떠오른 경악을 보며 잠깐 웃던 그의 웃음은, 그것이 그의 안위를 걱정하는 고통과 불안으로 바뀌자 멎었다."]], [["“Dear Mercy,”", "\"머시.\""], ["he said, “you must forgive me.", "그가 말했다. \"용서해 주오."], ["I think I am going crazy!”", "내가 미쳐 가고 있나 봐!\""]], [["She thought that he was; and thus the interview ended, and Clare re-entered the Vicarage.", "그녀는 정말로 그가 그렇다고 생각했다. 그렇게 대화는 끝났고, 클레어는 다시 목사관으로 들어갔다."], ["With the local banker he deposited the jewels till happier days should arise.", "그는 지역 은행가에게 더 행복한 날이 올 때까지 보석들을 맡겨 두었다."], ["He also paid into the bank thirty pounds—to be sent to Tess in a few months, as she might require; and wrote to her at her parents’ home in Blackmoor Vale to inform her of what he had done.", "또한 그는 은행에 삼십 파운드를 넣어 두어, 몇 달 뒤 테스가 필요로 할 때 그녀에게 보내지도록 했고, 블랙무어 골짜기의 친정에 있는 그녀에게 편지를 써 자기가 한 일을 알렸다."], ["This amount, with the sum he had already placed in her hands—about fifty pounds—he hoped would be amply sufficient for her wants just at present, particularly as in an emergency she had been directed to apply to his father.", "이 금액에다 이미 그녀의 손에 쥐여 준 액수—약 오십 파운드—를 더하면, 당장의 그녀의 필요에는 넉넉히 충분하리라고 그는 바랐다. 특히 비상시에는 자기 아버지에게 청하라고 일러 두었으니 더욱."]], [["He deemed it best not to put his parents into communication with her by informing them of her address; and, being unaware of what had really happened to estrange the two, neither his father nor his mother suggested that he should do so.", "그는 그녀의 주소를 알려 부모를 그녀와 연락이 닿게 하지 않는 편이 가장 낫다고 여겼다. 그리고 두 사람을 갈라놓은 진짜 사정을 모르는 터라, 그의 아버지도 어머니도 그가 그렇게 하라고 권하지 않았다."], ["During the day he left the parsonage, for what he had to complete he wished to get done quickly.", "그날 낮에 그는 목사관을 떠났는데, 마무리해야 할 일을 어서 끝내고 싶었기 때문이다."]], [["As the last duty before leaving this part of England it was necessary for him to call at the Wellbridge farmhouse, in which he had spent with Tess the first three days of their marriage, the trifle of rent having to be paid, the key given up of the rooms they had occupied, and two or three small articles fetched away that they had left behind.", "잉글랜드의 이 지역을 떠나기 전 마지막 의무로서, 그는 웰브리지의 농가에 들러야 했다. 결혼 후 첫 사흘을 테스와 함께 보낸 곳으로, 약간의 집세를 치르고, 그들이 쓰던 방들의 열쇠를 돌려주고, 두고 온 자질구레한 물건 두세 가지를 가져와야 했다."], ["It was under this roof that the deepest shadow ever thrown upon his life had stretched its gloom over him.", "그의 삶에 드리워진 가장 깊은 그림자가 그 음울함을 그에게 펼쳐 보인 곳이 바로 이 지붕 아래였다."], ["Yet when he had unlocked the door of the sitting-room and looked into it, the memory which returned first upon him was that of their happy arrival on a similar afternoon, the first fresh sense of sharing a habitation conjointly, the first meal together, the chatting by the fire with joined hands.", "그러나 그가 거실 문을 열고 안을 들여다보았을 때, 가장 먼저 되살아난 기억은 어느 비슷한 오후에 둘이서 행복하게 도착했던 일이었다. 한 거처를 함께 나눈다는 그 첫 신선한 느낌, 둘이 함께한 첫 식사, 손을 맞잡고 난롯가에서 도란거리던 일이었다."]], [["The farmer and his wife were in the field at the moment of his visit, and Clare was in the rooms alone for some time.", "그가 방문했을 때 농부와 그 아내는 마침 들에 나가 있어서, 클레어는 한동안 방에 혼자 있었다."], ["Inwardly swollen with a renewal of sentiment that he had not quite reckoned with, he went upstairs to her chamber, which had never been his.", "자기도 미처 헤아리지 못했던 감정의 되살아남에 속으로 부풀어 오른 채, 그는 위층 그녀의 방으로 올라갔다. 한 번도 그의 방이었던 적 없는 방이었다."], ["The bed was smooth as she had made it with her own hands on the morning of leaving.", "침대는 그녀가 떠나던 날 아침 제 손으로 정돈해 놓은 그대로 매끈했다."], ["The mistletoe hung under the tester just as he had placed it.", "겨우살이는 그가 걸어 둔 그대로 닫집 아래 매달려 있었다."], ["Having been there three or four weeks it was turning colour, and the leaves and berries were wrinkled.", "거기 걸린 지 서너 주가 되어 빛이 바래 가고 있었고, 잎과 열매는 쪼글쪼글했다."], ["Angel took it down and crushed it into the grate.", "앤젤은 그것을 떼어 내 벽난로에 짓이겨 넣었다."], ["Standing there, he for the first time doubted whether his course in this conjecture had been a wise, much less a generous, one.", "거기 서서, 그는 처음으로 이 어림짐작에서 자기가 택한 길이 과연 현명한 것이었는지, 하물며 너그러운 것이었는지를 의심했다."], ["But had he not been cruelly blinded?", "그러나 자기가 잔인하게 눈이 멀었던 게 아니었던가?"], ["In the incoherent multitude of his emotions he knelt down at the bedside wet-eyed. “O Tess!", "갈피 없이 뒤엉킨 감정의 무리 속에서 그는 눈물 젖은 눈으로 침대맡에 무릎을 꿇었다. \"아, 테스!"], ["If you had only told me sooner, I would have forgiven you!” he mourned.", "진작 말해 주었더라면, 나는 용서했을 텐데!\" 그가 애통해했다."]], [["Hearing a footstep below, he rose and went to the top of the stairs.", "아래층에서 발소리가 들리자 그는 일어나 계단 꼭대기로 갔다."], ["At the bottom of the flight he saw a woman standing, and on her turning up her face recognized the pale, dark-eyed Izz Huett.", "계단 아래에 한 여자가 서 있는 것이 보였고, 그녀가 얼굴을 드는 순간 그는 창백하고 검은 눈을 한 이즈 휴엣을 알아보았다."]], [["“Mr Clare,”", "\"클레어 씨.\""], ["she said, “I’ve called to see you and Mrs Clare, and to inquire if ye be well.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신과 클레어 부인을 뵈러, 또 두 분이 잘 계신지 여쭤보려고 들렀어요."], ["I thought you might be back here again.”", "여기 다시 돌아와 계실지도 모른다고 생각했거든요.\""]], [["This was a girl whose secret he had guessed, but who had not yet guessed his; an honest girl who loved him—one who would have made as good, or nearly as good, a practical farmer’s wife as Tess.", "이 처녀는 그가 비밀을 짐작했으나 아직 그의 비밀은 짐작하지 못한 처녀였다. 그를 사랑하는 정직한 처녀, 테스만큼, 아니 거의 테스만큼 훌륭한 실속 있는 농부의 아내가 되었을 처녀였다."]], [["“I am here alone,”", "\"여기 혼자 있어요.\""], ["he said; “we are not living here now.”", "그가 말했다. \"우리는 이제 여기 살지 않습니다.\""], ["Explaining why he had come, he asked, “Which way are you going home, Izz?”", "자기가 왜 왔는지 설명한 뒤, 그가 물었다. \"집에는 어느 길로 가나요, 이즈?\""]], [["“I have no home at Talbothays Dairy now, sir,”", "\"이제 저는 탤버세이즈 낙농장에 집이 없어요, 나리.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Why is that?”", "\"왜 그렇죠?\""]], [["Izz looked down.", "이즈는 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“It was so dismal there that I left!", "\"거기가 너무 적적해서 떠났어요!"], ["I am staying out this way.” She pointed in a contrary direction, the direction in which he was journeying.", "저는 이쪽에서 지내고 있고요.\" 그녀는 반대 방향을, 그가 가려는 방향을 가리켰다."]], [["“Well—are you going there now?", "\"그럼—지금 거기로 가는 길인가요?"], ["I can take you if you wish for a lift.”", "태워 주기를 바란다면 데려다줄게요.\""]], [["Her olive complexion grew richer in hue.", "그녀의 올리브빛 얼굴이 한층 짙은 빛을 띠었다."]], [["“Thank ’ee, Mr Clare,”", "\"고맙습니다, 클레어 씨.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He soon found the farmer, and settled the account for his rent and the few other items which had to be considered by reason of the sudden abandonment of the lodgings.", "그는 곧 농부를 찾아 집세며, 숙소를 갑작스레 비우는 바람에 따져야 했던 몇 가지 다른 항목의 셈을 치렀다."], ["On Clare’s return to his horse and gig, Izz jumped up beside him.", "클레어가 말과 마차로 돌아오자 이즈가 그 옆자리로 뛰어올랐다."]], [["“I am going to leave England, Izz,”", "\"나는 잉글랜드를 떠날 거예요, 이즈.\""], ["he said, as they drove on.", "마차를 몰아 가며 그가 말했다."], ["“Going to Brazil.”", "\"브라질로 갑니다.\""]], [["“And do Mrs Clare like the notion of such a journey?” she asked.", "\"그럼 클레어 부인도 그런 여정을 좋아하나요?\" 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“She is not going at present—say for a year or so.", "\"그녀는 지금은 안 가요—한 일 년쯤이라고 해 두죠."], ["I am going out to reconnoitre—to see what life there is like.”", "나는 정찰하러 가는 거예요—거기 사는 게 어떤지 보러요.\""]], [["They sped along eastward for some considerable distance, Izz making no observation.", "그들은 동쪽으로 꽤 먼 거리를 달려 나갔고, 이즈는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“How are the others?”", "\"다들 어떻게 지내요?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."], ["“How is Retty?”", "\"레티는 어때요?\""]], [["“She was in a sort of nervous state when I zid her last; and so thin and hollow-cheeked that ’a do seem in a decline.", "\"제가 마지막으로 봤을 땐 좀 신경 쇠약 같은 상태였어요. 어찌나 야위고 볼이 푹 꺼졌는지 폐병이라도 든 것 같더라고요."], ["Nobody will ever fall in love wi’ her any more,”", "이젠 아무도 그애한테 반하지 않을 거예요.\""], ["said Izz absently.", "이즈가 멍하니 말했다."]], [["“And Marian?”", "\"그럼 매리언은요?\""]], [["Izz lowered her voice.", "이즈는 목소리를 낮추었다."]], [["“Marian drinks.”", "\"매리언은 술을 마셔요.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"저런!\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["The dairyman has got rid of her.”", "낙농장 주인이 그애를 내보냈어요.\""]], [["“And you!”", "\"그럼 당신은요!\""]], [["“I don’t drink, and I bain’t in a decline.", "\"저는 술도 안 마시고, 폐병도 안 들었어요."], ["But—I am no great things at singing afore breakfast now!”", "하지만—이젠 아침 먹기 전에 노래 부르는 건 영 신통치 않아요!\""]], [["“How is that?", "\"왜 그래요?"], ["Do you remember how neatly you used to turn ‘’Twas down in Cupid’s Gardens’ and ‘The Tailor’s Breeches’ at morning milking?”", "아침 젖 짤 때 '큐피드의 정원에서'랑 '재단사의 바지'를 얼마나 깔끔하게 뽑아내곤 했는지 기억나요?\""]], [["“Ah, yes! When you first came, sir, that was.", "\"아, 네! 나리가 처음 오셨을 때였죠."], ["Not when you had been there a bit.”", "한참 계신 다음이 아니라요.\""]], [["“Why was that falling-off?”", "\"왜 그렇게 시들해졌죠?\""]], [["Her black eyes flashed up to his face for one moment by way of answer.", "그녀의 검은 눈이 대답 대신 한순간 그의 얼굴로 번득 치솟았다."]], [["“Izz!—how weak of you—for such as I!” he said, and fell into reverie.", "\"이즈!—그건 너무 어리석은 일이에요—나 같은 사람한테!\" 그가 말하고는 상념에 잠겼다."], ["“Then—suppose I had asked _you_ to marry me?”", "\"그럼—만약 내가 당신한테 결혼하자고 청했더라면?\""]], [["“If you had I should have said ‘Yes’, and you would have married a woman who loved ’ee!”", "\"그러셨다면 저는 '네'라고 했을 거고, 나리는 자기를 사랑하는 여자와 결혼하셨을 거예요!\""]], [["“Really!”", "\"정말로요!\""]], [["“Down to the ground!”", "\"바닥까지 송두리째요!\""], ["she whispered vehemently.", "그녀가 격하게 속삭였다."], ["“O my God!", "\"아, 맙소사!"], ["did you never guess it till now!”", "지금까지 한 번도 눈치채지 못하셨던 거예요!\""]], [["By-and-by they reached a branch road to a village.", "이윽고 그들은 어느 마을로 갈라지는 샛길에 다다랐다."]], [["“I must get down.", "\"여기서 내려야 해요."], ["I live out there,”", "저는 저쪽에 살아요.\""], ["said Izz abruptly, never having spoken since her avowal.", "이즈가 불쑥 말했다. 고백한 뒤로 한 번도 입을 열지 않았던 그녀였다."]], [["Clare slowed the horse.", "클레어는 말을 늦추었다."], ["He was incensed against his fate, bitterly disposed towards social ordinances; for they had cooped him up in a corner, out of which there was no legitimate pathway.", "그는 자기 운명에 분노했고, 사회의 규범들에 쓰라린 적의를 품었다. 그것들이 그를 한 구석에 가두어, 거기서는 떳떳한 길이라곤 빠져나갈 데가 없었기 때문이다."], ["Why not be revenged on society by shaping his future domesticities loosely, instead of kissing the pedagogic rod of convention in this ensnaring manner?", "이렇게 옭아매듯 인습의 회초리에 입을 맞추는 대신, 차라리 앞으로의 가정사를 헐겁게 꾸려 사회에 앙갚음을 하면 어떤가?"]], [["“I am going to Brazil alone, Izz,”", "\"나는 브라질로 혼자 가요, 이즈.\""], ["said he. “I have separated from my wife for personal, not voyaging, reasons.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 아내와 헤어졌어요, 여행 때문이 아니라 개인적인 이유로."], ["I may never live with her again.", "다시는 그녀와 함께 살지 않을지도 몰라요."], ["I may not be able to love you; but—will you go with me instead of her?”", "당신을 사랑하지는 못할지도 모르지만—그녀 대신 나와 함께 가지 않겠어요?\""]], [["“You truly wish me to go?”", "\"정말로 제가 가기를 바라세요?\""]], [["“I do.", "\"그래요."], ["I have been badly used enough to wish for relief.", "나는 충분히 험하게 당했으니 위안을 바랄 만하잖아요."], ["And you at least love me disinterestedly.”", "그리고 적어도 당신은 사심 없이 나를 사랑하니까요.\""]], [["“Yes—I will go,” said Izz, after a pause.", "\"네—갈게요.\" 이즈가 한참 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다."]], [["“You will?", "\"가겠다고요?"], ["You know what it means, Izz?”", "그게 무슨 뜻인지 알아요, 이즈?\""]], [["“It means that I shall live with you for the time you are over there—that’s good enough for me.”", "\"나리가 거기 가 계신 동안 제가 나리와 함께 산다는 뜻이지요—저한테는 그걸로 충분해요.\""]], [["“Remember, you are not to trust me in morals now. But I ought to remind you that it will be wrong-doing in the eyes of civilization—Western civilization, that is to say.”", "\"명심해요, 이제 당신은 도덕에 관해선 나를 믿어선 안 돼요. 하지만 일러 두건대, 이건 문명의—그러니까 서구 문명의 눈에는 나쁜 짓일 거예요.\""]], [["“I don’t mind that; no woman do when it comes to agony-point, and there’s no other way!”", "\"그런 건 상관없어요. 막다른 고통에 이르러 달리 길이 없을 땐 어느 여자도 상관 안 해요!\""]], [["“Then don’t get down, but sit where you are.”", "\"그럼 내리지 말고, 그 자리에 그대로 앉아 있어요.\""]], [["He drove past the cross-roads, one mile, two miles, without showing any signs of affection.", "그는 애정의 기색이라곤 조금도 보이지 않은 채 갈림길을 지나, 일 마일, 이 마일을 몰아갔다."]], [["“You love me very, very much, Izz?”", "\"당신은 나를 아주, 아주 많이 사랑해요, 이즈?\""], ["he suddenly asked.", "그가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“I do—I have said I do!", "\"그래요—그렇다고 말했잖아요!"], ["I loved you all the time we was at the dairy together!”", "우리가 낙농장에 함께 있던 내내 나리를 사랑했어요!\""]], [["“More than Tess?”", "\"테스보다 더요?\""]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["she murmured, “not more than she.”", "그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"그녀보다 더는 아니에요.\""]], [["“How’s that?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Because nobody could love ’ee more than Tess did!...", "\"왜냐하면 아무도 테스만큼 나리를 사랑할 순 없을 테니까요!…"], ["She would have laid down her life for ’ee.", "그애는 나리를 위해 목숨이라도 내놓았을 거예요."], ["I could do no more.”", "저는 그 이상은 못 해요.\""]], [["Like the prophet on the top of Peor, Izz Huett would fain have spoken perversely at such a moment, but the fascination exercised over her rougher nature by Tess’s character compelled her to grace.", "브올 산꼭대기의 예언자처럼, 이즈 휴엣도 그런 순간 비뚜로 말하고 싶었을 테지만, 테스의 인품이 그녀의 거친 본성에 행사한 매혹이 그녀로 하여금 어쩔 수 없이 갸륵하게 굴게 했다."]], [["Clare was silent; his heart had risen at these straightforward words from such an unexpected unimpeachable quarter.", "클레어는 잠자코 있었다. 이토록 뜻밖의, 흠잡을 데 없는 데서 나온 이 솔직한 말에 그의 마음이 북받쳤다."], ["In his throat was something as if a sob had solidified there.", "그의 목구멍에는 마치 흐느낌이 굳어 버린 듯한 무언가가 걸려 있었다."], ["His ears repeated, “_She would have laid down her life for ’ee.", "그의 귀에는 그 말이 거듭 울렸다. \"그애는 나리를 위해 목숨이라도 내놓았을 거예요."], ["I could do no more!_”", "저는 그 이상은 못 해요!\""]], [["“Forget our idle talk, Izz,” he said, turning the horse’s head suddenly.", "\"우리가 부질없이 떠든 이야기는 잊어요, 이즈.\" 그가 별안간 말 머리를 돌리며 말했다."], ["“I don’t know what I’ve been saying!", "\"내가 무슨 말을 지껄였는지 모르겠어요!"], ["I will now drive you back to where your lane branches off.”", "이제 당신 샛길이 갈라지는 데까지 다시 데려다줄게요.\""]], [["“So much for honesty towards ’ee!", "\"솔직하게 군 대가가 이거로군요!"], ["O—how can I bear it—how can I—how can I!”", "아—내가 이걸 어떻게 견뎌요—어떻게—어떻게!\""]], [["Izz Huett burst into wild tears, and beat her forehead as she saw what she had done.", "이즈 휴엣은 격한 울음을 터뜨리며, 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지 깨닫고는 제 이마를 쳐 댔다."]], [["“Do you regret that poor little act of justice to an absent one?", "\"자리에 없는 사람을 위해 베푼 그 가엾고 작은 정의를 후회하는 거예요?"], ["O, Izz, don’t spoil it by regret!”", "아, 이즈, 후회로 그걸 망치지 말아요!\""]], [["She stilled herself by degrees.", "그녀는 차츰 마음을 가라앉혔다."]], [["“Very well, sir.", "\"알겠어요, 나리."], ["Perhaps I didn’t know what I was saying, either, wh—when I agreed to go! I wish—what cannot be!”", "어쩌면 저도, 가겠다고 했을 때, 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 몰랐던 거겠지요! 저는 바라요—이루어질 수 없는 것을!\""]], [["“Because I have a loving wife already.”", "\"내겐 이미 사랑하는 아내가 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes!", "\"네, 네!"], ["You have!”", "있으시지요!\""]], [["They reached the corner of the lane which they had passed half an hour earlier, and she hopped down.", "그들은 반 시간 전에 지나쳤던 그 샛길 모퉁이에 다다랐고, 그녀는 폴짝 내렸다."]], [["“Izz—please, please forget my momentary levity!”", "\"이즈—제발, 제발 내 한순간의 경솔함을 잊어 줘요!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“It was so ill-considered, so ill-advised!”", "\"정말 생각 없는 짓, 분별없는 짓이었어요!\""]], [["“Forget it?", "\"잊으라고요?"], ["Never, never!", "절대로, 절대로요!"], ["O, it was no levity to me!”", "아, 저한테는 경솔함이 아니었어요!\""]], [["He felt how richly he deserved the reproach that the wounded cry conveyed, and, in a sorrow that was inexpressible, leapt down and took her hand.", "그는 그 상처 입은 외침이 전하는 비난을 자신이 얼마나 톡톡히 받아 마땅한지를 느꼈고, 이루 말할 수 없는 슬픔에 잠겨 마차에서 뛰어내려 그녀의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Well, but, Izz, we’ll part friends, anyhow?", "\"그래도, 이즈, 우리 어쨌든 친구로 헤어져요?"], ["You don’t know what I’ve had to bear!”", "당신은 내가 무얼 견뎌야 했는지 모를 거예요!\""]], [["She was a really generous girl, and allowed no further bitterness to mar their adieux.", "그녀는 참으로 너그러운 처녀였고, 둘의 작별을 더는 쓰라림으로 흐리게 하지 않았다."]], [["“I forgive ’ee, sir!” she said.", "\"용서할게요, 나리!\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Now, Izz,”", "\"자, 이즈.\""], ["he said, while she stood beside him there, forcing himself to the mentor’s part he was far from feeling; “I want you to tell Marian when you see her that she is to be a good woman, and not to give way to folly.", "그녀가 그 곁에 서 있는 동안, 그는 자기가 도무지 느끼지 못하는 스승의 역할을 억지로 떠맡으며 말했다. \"매리언을 만나거든 부디 일러 줘요, 착한 여자가 되어 어리석음에 빠지지 말라고. 그러겠다고 약속해 줘요."], ["Promise that, and tell Retty that there are more worthy men than I in the world, that for my sake she is to act wisely and well—remember the words—wisely and well—for my sake.", "그리고 레티에게는, 세상에 나보다 더 가치 있는 사내들이 많으니, 나를 위해서라도 슬기롭고 바르게 행동하라고 전해 줘요—이 말을 기억해요—슬기롭고 바르게—나를 위해서."], ["I send this message to them as a dying man to the dying; for I shall never see them again.", "나는 죽어 가는 자가 죽어 가는 이들에게 보내듯 이 전갈을 그들에게 보내는 거예요. 다시는 그들을 못 볼 테니까."], ["And you, Izzy, you have saved me by your honest words about my wife from an incredible impulse towards folly and treachery.", "그리고 당신, 이지, 당신은 내 아내에 관한 그 정직한 말로, 도무지 믿기지 않는 어리석음과 배신의 충동에서 나를 구해 주었어요."], ["Women may be bad, but they are not so bad as men in these things!", "여자도 나쁠 수 있지만, 이런 일에서 남자만큼 나쁘진 않아요!"], ["On that one account I can never forget you.", "그 한 가지만으로도 나는 당신을 결코 잊을 수 없어요."], ["Be always the good and sincere girl you have hitherto been; and think of me as a worthless lover, but a faithful friend.", "지금껏 그래 왔듯 늘 착하고 진실한 처녀로 있어 줘요. 그리고 나를, 보잘것없는 연인이었으나 충실한 벗으로 여겨 줘요."], ["Promise.”", "약속해 줘요.\""]], [["She gave the promise.", "그녀는 약속했다."]], [["“Heaven bless and keep you, sir.", "\"하늘이 당신을 축복하고 지켜 주시기를, 나리."], ["Goodbye!”", "안녕히 가세요!\""]], [["He drove on; but no sooner had Izz turned into the lane, and Clare was out of sight, than she flung herself down on the bank in a fit of racking anguish; and it was with a strained unnatural face that she entered her mother’s cottage late that night.", "그는 마차를 몰아갔다. 그러나 이즈가 샛길로 접어들어 클레어가 시야에서 사라지자마자, 그녀는 찢어지는 듯한 고통의 발작에 휩싸여 둑에 몸을 내던졌다. 그리고 그날 밤 늦게 어머니의 오두막에 들어설 때 그녀의 얼굴은 일그러진 부자연스러운 모습이었다."], ["Nobody ever was told how Izz spent the dark hours that intervened between Angel Clare’s parting from her and her arrival home.", "앤젤 클레어와 헤어진 뒤부터 집에 다다르기까지 그 어두운 시간을 이즈가 어떻게 보냈는지는 아무도 끝내 듣지 못했다."]], [["Clare, too, after bidding the girl farewell, was wrought to aching thoughts and quivering lips. But his sorrow was not for Izz.", "클레어 역시 그 처녀에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 쑤시는 듯한 상념과 떨리는 입술에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 그의 슬픔은 이즈를 위한 것이 아니었다."], ["That evening he was within a feather-weight’s turn of abandoning his road to the nearest station, and driving across that elevated dorsal line of South Wessex which divided him from his Tess’s home.", "그날 저녁 그는 가장 가까운 역으로 가는 길을 버리고, 자기를 테스의 집과 갈라놓는 사우스웨식스의 그 드높은 등줄기 같은 산마루를 가로질러 마차를 몰고 갈 마음의 깃털 한 올 무게만큼의 차이로 아슬아슬한 지경에 있었다."], ["It was neither a contempt for her nature, nor the probable state of her heart, which deterred him.", "그를 멈춰 세운 것은 그녀의 천성에 대한 경멸도, 그녀의 마음이 어떤 상태일지에 대한 짐작도 아니었다."]], [["No; it was a sense that, despite her love, as corroborated by Izz’s admission, the facts had not changed.", "아니. 그것은, 이즈의 인정으로 입증되었듯 그녀의 사랑에도 불구하고, 사실은 변하지 않았다는 느낌이었다."], ["If he was right at first, he was right now.", "처음에 그가 옳았다면, 지금도 그는 옳았다."], ["And the momentum of the course on which he had embarked tended to keep him going in it, unless diverted by a stronger, more sustained force than had played upon him this afternoon.", "그리고 그가 발을 들여놓은 그 길의 관성이, 이날 오후 그에게 작용한 것보다 더 강하고 더 지속적인 힘에 의해 방향이 틀어지지 않는 한, 그를 그 길로 계속 나아가게 했다."], ["He could soon come back to her. He took the train that night for London, and five days after shook hands in farewell of his brothers at the port of embarkation.", "그녀에게는 머지않아 돌아올 수 있을 터였다. 그는 그날 밤 런던행 기차에 올랐고, 닷새 뒤에는 승선하는 항구에서 형들과 작별의 악수를 나누었다."]], [["# XLI", "# XLI"]], [["From the foregoing events of the winter-time let us press on to an October day, more than eight months subsequent to the parting of Clare and Tess.", "겨울철의 앞선 사건들로부터 시계를 앞당겨, 클레어와 테스가 헤어진 지 여덟 달이 넘은 어느 시월의 하루로 넘어가자."], ["We discover the latter in changed conditions; instead of a bride with boxes and trunks which others bore, we see her a lonely woman with a basket and a bundle in her own porterage, as at an earlier time when she was no bride; instead of the ample means that were projected by her husband for her comfort through this probationary period, she can produce only a flattened purse.", "우리는 후자가 달라진 처지에 놓인 것을 보게 된다. 남들이 짐 상자와 트렁크를 날라 주던 신부가 아니라, 예전 그녀가 신부가 아니었던 시절처럼 바구니와 보따리를 제 손으로 든 외로운 여인을. 이 시련의 기간 동안 그녀의 안락을 위해 남편이 마련해 둔 넉넉한 자금 대신, 그녀가 내놓을 수 있는 것은 납작해진 지갑 하나뿐이다."]], [["After again leaving Marlott, her home, she had got through the spring and summer without any great stress upon her physical powers, the time being mainly spent in rendering light irregular service at dairy-work near Port-Bredy to the west of the Blackmoor Valley, equally remote from her native place and from Talbothays.", "고향 말럿을 다시 떠난 뒤, 그녀는 몸을 크게 혹사하는 일 없이 봄과 여름을 견뎌 냈다. 그동안은 주로 블랙무어 골짜기 서쪽 포트브레디 인근에서 가벼운 낙농 일을 비정규로 하며 보냈는데, 그곳은 그녀의 고향에서도 탤버세이즈에서도 똑같이 먼 곳이었다."], ["She preferred this to living on his allowance.", "그녀는 이쪽이 그가 준 생활비로 살아가는 것보다 나았다."], ["Mentally she remained in utter stagnation, a condition which the mechanical occupation rather fostered than checked.", "정신적으로 그녀는 완전한 정체 상태에 머물러 있었고, 그 기계적인 일은 이 상태를 막기보다 오히려 부추겼다."], ["Her consciousness was at that other dairy, at that other season, in the presence of the tender lover who had confronted her there—he who, the moment she had grasped him to keep for her own, had disappeared like a shape in a vision.", "그녀의 의식은 저 다른 낙농장에, 저 다른 계절에, 거기서 그녀와 마주했던 그 다정한 연인의 곁에 가 있었다—그녀가 제 것으로 붙들려고 움켜쥐는 순간 환영 속 형상처럼 사라져 버린 그 사람의 곁에."]], [["The dairy-work lasted only till the milk began to lessen, for she had not met with a second regular engagement as at Talbothays, but had done duty as a supernumerary only.", "낙농 일은 젖이 줄어들기 시작할 때까지만 이어졌다. 탤버세이즈에서처럼 정규로 두 번째 고용된 게 아니라, 그저 보조 인력으로 일했기 때문이다."], ["However, as harvest was now beginning, she had simply to remove from the pasture to the stubble to find plenty of further occupation, and this continued till harvest was done.", "그러나 이제 수확철이 시작되고 있던 터라, 그녀는 그저 목초지에서 그루터기 밭으로 자리를 옮기기만 하면 일거리는 얼마든지 있었고, 이 일은 수확이 끝날 때까지 이어졌다."]], [["Of the five-and-twenty pounds which had remained to her of Clare’s allowance, after deducting the other half of the fifty as a contribution to her parents for the trouble and expense to which she had put them, she had as yet spent but little.", "클레어가 준 생활비 가운데 그녀에게 남았던 이십오 파운드는—오십 파운드의 나머지 절반을 자기가 부모에게 끼친 수고와 비용에 대한 보탬으로 떼어 준 뒤 남은 것이었는데—아직 거의 쓰지 않은 상태였다."], ["But there now followed an unfortunate interval of wet weather, during which she was obliged to fall back upon her sovereigns.", "그러나 이제 궂은 날씨가 불운하게도 한동안 이어졌고, 그 기간에 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 그 금화들에 손을 대야 했다."]], [["She could not bear to let them go.", "그녀는 그것들을 떠나보내는 일을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Angel had put them into her hand, had obtained them bright and new from his bank for her; his touch had consecrated them to souvenirs of himself—they appeared to have had as yet no other history than such as was created by his and her own experiences—and to disperse them was like giving away relics.", "앤젤이 제 손에 쥐여 준 것이었고, 그녀를 위해 은행에서 반짝거리는 새것으로 받아 온 것이었다. 그의 손길이 그것들을 자신을 기리는 기념물로 신성하게 만들어 놓았던 것이다—그것들은 아직 그와 그녀 자신의 경험으로 빚어진 것 말고는 어떤 다른 내력도 없는 듯했다—그러니 그것들을 흩어 보내는 일은 성물을 내주는 것과 같았다."], ["But she had to do it, and one by one they left her hands.", "그러나 그녀는 그래야만 했고, 하나씩 하나씩 그것들은 그녀의 손을 떠났다."]], [["She had been compelled to send her mother her address from time to time, but she concealed her circumstances.", "그녀는 어머니에게 이따금 주소를 알려 줄 수밖에 없었지만, 자기 형편은 숨겼다."], ["When her money had almost gone a letter from her mother reached her.", "돈이 거의 다 떨어졌을 무렵 어머니에게서 편지 한 통이 닿았다."], ["Joan stated that they were in dreadful difficulty; the autumn rains had gone through the thatch of the house, which required entire renewal; but this could not be done because the previous thatching had never been paid for.", "조앤은 자기들이 끔찍한 곤경에 처했다고 적었다. 가을비가 집의 이엉을 뚫고 새서 통째로 새로 이어야 하는데, 지난번 이엉 값을 한 번도 치르지 못한 탓에 그렇게 할 수가 없다는 것이었다."], ["New rafters and a new ceiling upstairs also were required, which, with the previous bill, would amount to a sum of twenty pounds.", "새 서까래와 위층의 새 천장도 필요한데, 지난번 청구액까지 합하면 이십 파운드에 이르리라고 했다."], ["As her husband was a man of means, and had doubtless returned by this time, could she not send them the money?", "사위가 재력 있는 사람이고 지금쯤은 틀림없이 돌아왔을 테니, 그 돈을 보내 줄 수 없겠느냐는 것이었다."]], [["Tess had thirty pounds coming to her almost immediately from Angel’s bankers, and, the case being so deplorable, as soon as the sum was received she sent the twenty as requested.", "테스는 거의 곧바로 앤젤의 은행가에게서 받을 삼십 파운드가 있었고, 사정이 그토록 딱한지라 그 돈을 받자마자 청한 대로 이십 파운드를 보냈다."], ["Part of the remainder she was obliged to expend in winter clothing, leaving only a nominal sum for the whole inclement season at hand.", "나머지의 일부는 겨울옷에 쓸 수밖에 없었고, 코앞에 닥친 그 모진 계절 내내 쓸 돈으로는 명목뿐인 금액만 남았다."], ["When the last pound had gone, a remark of Angel’s that whenever she required further resources she was to apply to his father, remained to be considered.", "마지막 한 파운드마저 떠나가자, 더 자금이 필요할 때면 언제든 자기 아버지에게 청하라던 앤젤의 말이 따져 볼 거리로 남았다."]], [["But the more Tess thought of the step, the more reluctant was she to take it.", "그러나 테스는 그 일을 생각하면 할수록 그렇게 하기가 더 꺼려졌다."], ["The same delicacy, pride, false shame, whatever it may be called, on Clare’s account, which had led her to hide from her own parents the prolongation of the estrangement, hindered her owning to his that she was in want after the fair allowance he had left her.", "클레어를 생각하는 마음에서 비롯된 똑같은 섬세함, 자존심, 헛된 수치심—뭐라 부르든 간에—이 그녀로 하여금 친정 부모에게마저 이 소원함이 길어지고 있음을 숨기게 했던 것인데, 바로 그것이 그가 넉넉히 남겨 준 생활비를 받고도 자기가 궁핍하다는 것을 그의 부모에게 털어놓지 못하게 막았다."], ["They probably despised her already; how much more they would despise her in the character of a mendicant!", "그들은 아마 이미 그녀를 멸시하고 있을 텐데, 동냥하는 처지의 그녀를 얼마나 더 멸시하겠는가!"], ["The consequence was that by no effort could the parson’s daughter-in-law bring herself to let him know her state.", "그 결과, 목사의 며느리는 아무리 애를 써도 도무지 자기 처지를 그에게 알릴 마음이 들지 않았다."]], [["Her reluctance to communicate with her husband’s parents might, she thought, lessen with the lapse of time; but with her own the reverse obtained.", "남편의 부모와 연락하기를 꺼리는 마음은 시간이 흐르면 줄어들지도 모른다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 친정에 대해서는 정반대였다."], ["On her leaving their house after the short visit subsequent to her marriage they were under the impression that she was ultimately going to join her husband; and from that time to the present she had done nothing to disturb their belief that she was awaiting his return in comfort, hoping against hope that his journey to Brazil would result in a short stay only, after which he would come to fetch her, or that he would write for her to join him; in any case that they would soon present a united front to their families and the world.", "결혼 후 짧게 다녀간 그 방문 끝에 그 집을 떠날 때, 그들은 그녀가 결국 남편과 합류하러 가는 것이라는 인상을 갖고 있었다. 그리고 그때부터 지금까지 그녀는, 자기가 편안하게 그의 귀환을 기다리고 있다는 그들의 믿음을 깨뜨릴 일을 아무것도 하지 않았다. 그의 브라질 여정이 짧은 체류로만 끝나, 그 후에 그가 자기를 데리러 오거나, 아니면 합류하라고 편지를 써 보내거나—어쨌거나 머지않아 두 사람이 양가와 세상 앞에 하나 된 모습을 보이게 되리라고, 헛된 희망인 줄 알면서도 기대했다."], ["This hope she still fostered. To let her parents know that she was a deserted wife, dependent, now that she had relieved their necessities, on her own hands for a living, after the _éclat_ of a marriage which was to nullify the collapse of the first attempt, would be too much indeed.", "이 희망을 그녀는 여전히 품고 있었다. 첫 번째 시도의 좌절을 무위로 돌려 줄 그 떠들썩한 결혼을 한 뒤에, 자기가 버림받은 아내이며, 부모의 곤궁을 덜어 준 지금은 제 두 손으로 벌어 먹고살아야 하는 처지임을 부모에게 알린다는 것은, 정말이지 너무 과한 일이었다."]], [["The set of brilliants returned to her mind.", "그 한 벌의 휘황한 보석들이 그녀의 머릿속에 다시 떠올랐다."], ["Where Clare had deposited them she did not know, and it mattered little, if it were true that she could only use and not sell them.", "클레어가 그것들을 어디에 맡겨 두었는지 그녀는 몰랐고, 자기가 그것들을 쓸 수만 있고 팔 수는 없다는 게 사실이라면 그건 별로 중요치도 않았다."], ["Even were they absolutely hers it would be passing mean to enrich herself by a legal title to them which was not essentially hers at all.", "설령 그것들이 온전히 그녀의 것이라 하더라도, 본질적으로는 조금도 제 것이 아닌 그것들에 대한 법적 권리로 제 배를 불린다는 것은 더없이 비열한 짓일 터였다."]], [["Meanwhile her husband’s days had been by no means free from trial.", "그동안 그녀 남편의 나날도 결코 시련에서 자유롭지 않았다."], ["At this moment he was lying ill of fever in the clay lands near Curitiba in Brazil, having been drenched with thunder-storms and persecuted by other hardships, in common with all the English farmers and farm-labourers who, just at this time, were deluded into going thither by the promises of the Brazilian Government, and by the baseless assumption that those frames which, ploughing and sowing on English uplands, had resisted all the weathers to whose moods they had been born, could resist equally well all the weathers by which they were surprised on Brazilian plains.", "바로 이 순간 그는 브라질 쿠리치바 인근의 진흙땅에서 열병으로 앓아누워 있었다. 천둥 폭풍우에 흠뻑 젖고 갖은 고난에 시달리면서. 마침 이 무렵 브라질 정부의 약속에 속아, 또 영국의 고지대에서 쟁기질하고 씨 뿌리며 타고난 온갖 날씨를 다 견뎌 낸 그 몸뚱이들이 브라질 평원에서 불시에 들이닥치는 온갖 날씨도 똑같이 잘 견뎌 내리라는 근거 없는 억측에 속아 그리로 건너간 모든 영국 농부와 농장 일꾼들과 매한가지였다."]], [["To return. Thus it happened that when the last of Tess’s sovereigns had been spent she was unprovided with others to take their place, while on account of the season she found it increasingly difficult to get employment.", "다시 본론으로 돌아가자. 그리하여 테스의 마지막 금화마저 다 써 버렸을 때 그녀에게는 그것들을 대신할 다른 돈이 마련되어 있지 않았고, 게다가 철 탓에 일자리를 얻기가 점점 더 어려워졌다."], ["Not being aware of the rarity of intelligence, energy, health, and willingness in any sphere of life, she refrained from seeking an indoor occupation; fearing towns, large houses, people of means and social sophistication, and of manners other than rural.", "지성과 활력과 건강과 성실함이 삶의 어느 영역에서든 얼마나 드문 것인지 알지 못했던 까닭에, 그녀는 실내 일자리를 구하기를 삼갔다. 읍내며, 큰 저택이며, 재력 있고 사회적으로 세련된 사람들이며, 시골과는 다른 몸가짐의 사람들이 두려웠던 것이다."], ["From that direction of gentility Black Care had come.", "그 점잖음의 방면에서 검은 근심이 찾아왔던 터였다."], ["Society might be better than she supposed from her slight experience of it.", "사회는 자기가 얕은 경험으로 짐작하는 것보다 나을지도 몰랐다."], ["But she had no proof of this, and her instinct in the circumstances was to avoid its purlieus.", "그러나 그녀에게는 그것을 입증할 증거가 없었고, 그런 처지에서 그녀의 본능은 사회의 언저리를 피하는 것이었다."]], [["The small dairies to the west, beyond Port-Bredy, in which she had served as supernumerary milkmaid during the spring and summer required no further aid.", "봄과 여름 동안 그녀가 보조 젖 짜는 처녀로 일했던 포트브레디 너머 서쪽의 작은 낙농장들은 더는 일손이 필요치 않았다."], ["Room would probably have been made for her at Talbothays, if only out of sheer compassion; but comfortable as her life had been there, she could not go back.", "탤버세이즈라면, 순전한 동정에서라도, 아마 그녀에게 자리를 내주었을 것이다. 그러나 그곳에서의 삶이 아무리 편안했어도 그녀는 돌아갈 수 없었다."], ["The anti-climax would be too intolerable; and her return might bring reproach upon her idolized husband.", "그 반전이 너무도 견디기 어려울 터였고, 그녀가 돌아가면 자기가 떠받드는 남편에게 비난이 쏟아질지도 몰랐다."], ["She could not have borne their pity, and their whispered remarks to one another upon her strange situation; though she would almost have faced a knowledge of her circumstances by every individual there, so long as her story had remained isolated in the mind of each.", "그녀는 그들의 연민을, 또 자기의 기이한 처지를 두고 서로 수군거리는 소리를 견딜 수 없었을 것이다. 다만 자기 사연이 사람들 각자의 마음속에 따로따로 고립되어 머무는 한, 거기 있는 모든 사람이 자기 형편을 다 알게 되는 것쯤은 거의 마주할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["It was the interchange of ideas about her that made her sensitiveness wince.", "그녀의 예민함을 움츠러들게 한 것은 그녀에 관한 생각들이 서로 오가는 일이었다."], ["Tess could not account for this distinction; she simply knew that she felt it.", "테스는 이 구별을 설명할 수 없었다. 그저 그렇게 느낀다는 것만 알 뿐이었다."]], [["She was now on her way to an upland farm in the centre of the county, to which she had been recommended by a wandering letter which had reached her from Marian.", "이제 그녀는 카운티 한복판의 고지대 농장으로 가는 길이었다. 매리언에게서 떠돌다 그녀에게 닿은 편지가 추천해 준 곳이었다."], ["Marian had somehow heard that Tess was separated from her husband—probably through Izz Huett—and the good-natured and now tippling girl, deeming Tess in trouble, had hastened to notify to her former friend that she herself had gone to this upland spot after leaving the dairy, and would like to see her there, where there was room for other hands, if it was really true that she worked again as of old.", "매리언은 어찌어찌—아마 이즈 휴엣을 통해—테스가 남편과 헤어졌다는 소식을 들었고, 마음씨 좋되 이제는 술독에 빠진 그 처녀는 테스가 곤경에 처했으리라 여기고는, 자기가 낙농장을 떠난 뒤 이 고지대로 왔다는 것과, 정말로 그녀가 예전처럼 다시 일하고 있는 거라면 일손이 더 필요한 이곳에서 만나고 싶다는 것을 옛 친구에게 서둘러 알려 온 것이었다."]], [["With the shortening of the days all hope of obtaining her husband’s forgiveness began to leave her; and there was something of the habitude of the wild animal in the unreflecting instinct with which she rambled on—disconnecting herself by littles from her eventful past at every step, obliterating her identity, giving no thought to accidents or contingencies which might make a quick discovery of her whereabouts by others of importance to her own happiness, if not to theirs.", "날이 짧아져 감에 따라 남편의 용서를 얻으리라는 희망은 모두 그녀를 떠나기 시작했다. 그리고 그녀가 정처 없이 떠돌게 만든 그 무심한 본능에는 어딘가 야생 짐승의 습성 같은 데가 있었다—걸음마다 사건 많던 과거에서 자신을 조금씩 떼어 내며, 제 정체를 지워 가며, 자기가 어디 있는지를 남들이—제 행복에 중요한, 그들의 행복에는 아니더라도—재빨리 알아낼지도 모를 우연이나 변수 따위는 조금도 마음에 두지 않으면서."]], [["Among the difficulties of her lonely position not the least was the attention she excited by her appearance, a certain bearing of distinction, which she had caught from Clare, being superadded to her natural attractiveness.", "외로운 처지에서 겪는 어려움 가운데 적지 않은 것이, 그녀의 용모가 끄는 시선이었다. 그녀의 타고난 매력에다 클레어에게서 물려받은 어떤 기품 있는 태도까지 더해진 까닭이었다."], ["Whilst the clothes lasted which had been prepared for her marriage, these casual glances of interest caused her no inconvenience, but as soon as she was compelled to don the wrapper of a fieldwoman, rude words were addressed to her more than once; but nothing occurred to cause her bodily fear till a particular November afternoon.", "결혼을 위해 마련했던 옷들이 남아 있는 동안에는 이렇게 무심코 던져지는 관심 어린 눈길도 그녀에게 별 불편을 주지 않았다. 그러나 들일하는 여자의 작업복을 걸쳐야 하게 되자마자, 거친 말이 그녀에게 한두 번이 아니게 건네졌다. 그래도 몸에 대한 두려움을 일으킬 일은 어느 십일월의 한 오후가 되기까지는 일어나지 않았다."]], [["She had preferred the country west of the River Brit to the upland farm for which she was now bound, because, for one thing, it was nearer to the home of her husband’s father; and to hover about that region unrecognized, with the notion that she might decide to call at the Vicarage some day, gave her pleasure.", "그녀는 지금 가려는 고지대 농장보다 브릿 강 서쪽의 시골을 더 좋아했었다. 한 가지 이유로는 그곳이 남편의 아버지 집에서 더 가까웠기 때문이다. 그리고 언젠가 목사관에 들러 보기로 마음먹을지도 모른다는 생각으로, 알아보는 이 없이 그 일대를 맴도는 일이 그녀에게는 즐거움을 주었다."], ["But having once decided to try the higher and drier levels, she pressed back eastward, marching afoot towards the village of Chalk-Newton, where she meant to pass the night.", "그러나 일단 더 높고 메마른 지대를 시도해 보기로 마음을 정하고 나자, 그녀는 다시 동쪽으로 발걸음을 돌려, 그날 밤을 묵으려는 초크뉴턴 마을을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["The lane was long and unvaried, and, owing to the rapid shortening of the days, dusk came upon her before she was aware.", "샛길은 길고 단조로웠고, 날이 부쩍 짧아진 탓에 그녀가 미처 알아채기도 전에 땅거미가 그녀를 덮쳤다."], ["She had reached the top of a hill down which the lane stretched its serpentine length in glimpses, when she heard footsteps behind her back, and in a few moments she was overtaken by a man.", "샛길이 구불구불한 길이를 군데군데 드러내며 뻗어 내려가는 어느 언덕마루에 다다랐을 때, 그녀는 등 뒤에서 발소리를 들었고, 몇 순간 만에 한 남자가 그녀를 따라잡았다."], ["He stepped up alongside Tess and said—", "그는 테스 옆에 바짝 다가서더니 말했다—"]], [["“Good night, my pretty maid”: to which she civilly replied.", "\"안녕하시오, 예쁜 아가씨.\" 그녀는 거기에 공손히 답했다."]], [["The light still remaining in the sky lit up her face, though the landscape was nearly dark.", "하늘에 아직 남은 빛이 그녀의 얼굴을 비추었다. 풍경은 거의 어두웠지만."], ["The man turned and stared hard at her.", "남자는 돌아서서 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“Why, surely, it is the young wench who was at Trantridge awhile—young Squire d’Urberville’s friend?", "\"이런, 틀림없이, 한동안 트랜트리지에 있던 그 젊은 처자로구먼—젊은 더버빌 지주 나리의 정인 말이오."], ["I was there at that time, though I don’t live there now.”", "나도 그때 거기 있었지, 지금은 거기 안 살지만.\""]], [["She recognized in him the well-to-do boor whom Angel had knocked down at the inn for addressing her coarsely.", "그녀는 그가, 자기에게 상스럽게 말을 걸었다가 술집에서 앤젤에게 얻어맞아 쓰러졌던 그 잘사는 촌뜨기임을 알아보았다."], ["A spasm of anguish shot through her, and she returned him no answer.", "고통의 경련이 그녀를 꿰뚫고 지나갔고, 그녀는 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Be honest enough to own it, and that what I said in the town was true, though your fancy-man was so up about it—hey, my sly one?", "\"솔직하게 인정할 만큼은 솔직하구려, 내가 읍내에서 한 말이 사실이었다고 말이오. 당신 정부가 그렇게 펄펄 뛰긴 했어도—그렇잖소, 이 능청맞은 것아?"], ["You ought to beg my pardon for that blow of his, considering.”", "따지고 보면 그자가 날린 그 주먹질에 대해선 당신이 나한테 용서를 빌어야 할 거요.\""]], [["Still no answer came from Tess. There seemed only one escape for her hunted soul.", "여전히 테스에게서는 아무 대답도 나오지 않았다. 쫓기는 그녀의 영혼에게는 빠져나갈 길이 오직 하나뿐인 듯했다."], ["She suddenly took to her heels with the speed of the wind, and, without looking behind her, ran along the road till she came to a gate which opened directly into a plantation.", "그녀는 별안간 바람 같은 속도로 줄행랑을 쳤고, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 길을 따라 달려, 곧장 어느 조림지로 통하는 문에 이르렀다."], ["Into this she plunged, and did not pause till she was deep enough in its shade to be safe against any possibility of discovery.", "그녀는 그 안으로 뛰어들었고, 누가 발견할 가능성조차 없을 만큼 그 그늘 깊숙이 들어가서야 비로소 멈추었다."]], [["Under foot the leaves were dry, and the foliage of some holly bushes which grew among the deciduous trees was dense enough to keep off draughts.", "발밑의 낙엽은 메말라 있었고, 낙엽수들 사이에 자란 호랑가시나무 덤불 몇 그루의 잎이 바람을 막아 줄 만큼 빽빽했다."], ["She scraped together the dead leaves till she had formed them into a large heap, making a sort of nest in the middle. Into this Tess crept.", "그녀는 마른 잎을 한곳에 그러모아 큰 더미를 만들고, 그 한가운데에 일종의 둥지 같은 것을 빚었다. 테스는 그 안으로 기어들었다."]], [["Such sleep as she got was naturally fitful; she fancied she heard strange noises, but persuaded herself that they were caused by the breeze.", "그렇게 든 잠은 자연히 토막토막 끊기는 것이었다. 그녀는 이상한 소리가 들리는 것 같았지만, 그건 산들바람 탓이라고 스스로를 타일렀다."], ["She thought of her husband in some vague warm clime on the other side of the globe, while she was here in the cold.", "그녀는 자기는 여기 추운 곳에 있는데 남편은 지구 반대편 어딘가 막연하게 따뜻한 땅에 있는 것을 생각했다."], ["Was there another such a wretched being as she in the world?", "세상에 자기만큼 비참한 존재가 또 있을까?"], ["Tess asked herself; and, thinking of her wasted life, said, “All is vanity.”", "테스는 자문했고, 허비해 버린 제 삶을 생각하며 말했다. \"모든 것이 헛되다.\""], ["She repeated the words mechanically, till she reflected that this was a most inadequate thought for modern days.", "그녀는 그 말을 기계적으로 되뇌다가, 이것이 현대에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 생각이라는 데 생각이 미쳤다."], ["Solomon had thought as far as that more than two thousand years ago; she herself, though not in the van of thinkers, had got much further.", "솔로몬은 이천 년도 더 전에 거기까지는 생각했다. 그녀 자신은, 비록 사상가들의 선두에 선 것은 아니어도, 훨씬 더 멀리 나아가 있었다."], ["If all were only vanity, who would mind it?", "모든 것이 그저 헛되기만 하다면, 누가 그걸 마음에 두겠는가?"], ["All was, alas, worse than vanity—injustice, punishment, exaction, death.", "모든 것은, 아아, 헛됨보다 더 나빴다—불의요, 형벌이요, 강요요, 죽음이었다."], ["The wife of Angel Clare put her hand to her brow, and felt its curve, and the edges of her eye-sockets perceptible under the soft skin, and thought as she did so that a time would come when that bone would be bare.", "앤젤 클레어의 아내는 제 이마에 손을 얹어, 그 곡선과, 부드러운 살갗 밑으로 만져지는 눈구멍의 가장자리를 느꼈고, 그러면서 언젠가 그 뼈가 앙상히 드러날 때가 오리라고 생각했다."], ["“I wish it were now,” she said.", "\"지금이 그때라면 좋겠어.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["In the midst of these whimsical fancies she heard a new strange sound among the leaves.", "이런 변덕스러운 공상에 잠겨 있던 와중에 그녀는 낙엽 사이에서 새롭고 이상한 소리를 들었다."], ["It might be the wind; yet there was scarcely any wind.", "바람일지도 몰랐다. 하지만 바람이라곤 거의 없었다."], ["Sometimes it was a palpitation, sometimes a flutter; sometimes it was a sort of gasp or gurgle.", "어떤 때는 팔딱이는 소리였고, 어떤 때는 파닥이는 소리였으며, 어떤 때는 헐떡임이나 그렁거림 같은 소리였다."], ["Soon she was certain that the noises came from wild creatures of some kind, the more so when, originating in the boughs overhead, they were followed by the fall of a heavy body upon the ground.", "머지않아 그녀는 그 소리가 무슨 야생 생물에게서 나는 것임을 확신하게 되었는데, 머리 위 나뭇가지에서 시작된 그 소리에 뒤이어 무거운 몸뚱이가 땅에 쿵 떨어지는 소리가 나자 더욱 그러했다."], ["Had she been ensconced here under other and more pleasant conditions she would have become alarmed; but, outside humanity, she had at present no fear.", "다른, 더 즐거운 사정 아래 여기 몸을 숨기고 있었더라면 그녀는 겁에 질렸을 것이다. 그러나 인간 세계 바깥에 있는 지금, 그녀에게는 두려움이 없었다."]], [["Day at length broke in the sky.", "마침내 하늘에서 날이 밝았다."], ["When it had been day aloft for some little while it became day in the wood.", "위쪽이 한동안 환해지고 나자 숲속에도 날이 밝았다."]], [["Directly the assuring and prosaic light of the world’s active hours had grown strong, she crept from under her hillock of leaves, and looked around boldly.", "세상의 활동하는 시간을 알리는 안심되고 무미건조한 빛이 강해지자마자, 그녀는 낙엽 더미 밑에서 기어 나와 대담하게 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Then she perceived what had been going on to disturb her.", "그러고는 자기를 들쑤셔 놓은 일이 무엇이었는지를 알아챘다."], ["The plantation wherein she had taken shelter ran down at this spot into a peak, which ended it hitherward, outside the hedge being arable ground.", "그녀가 몸을 피한 그 조림지는 이 지점에서 뾰족한 끄트머리로 좁아져 이쪽 끝에서 끝났고, 울타리 바깥은 경작지였다."], ["Under the trees several pheasants lay about, their rich plumage dabbled with blood; some were dead, some feebly twitching a wing, some staring up at the sky, some pulsating quickly, some contorted, some stretched out—all of them writhing in agony, except the fortunate ones whose tortures had ended during the night by the inability of nature to bear more.", "나무 아래에는 꿩 여러 마리가 흩어져 있었는데, 그 화려한 깃털이 피로 얼룩져 있었다. 더러는 죽어 있었고, 더러는 날개를 힘없이 파닥이고 있었으며, 더러는 하늘을 멀거니 올려다보고 있었고, 더러는 빠르게 헐떡이고 있었으며, 더러는 몸이 뒤틀려 있었고, 더러는 길게 뻗어 있었다—다들 고통 속에 몸부림치고 있었다. 다만 밤사이 더는 견디지 못한 본성 탓에 그 괴로움이 끝나 버린 운 좋은 것들만 빼고."]], [["Tess guessed at once the meaning of this.", "테스는 이것이 무슨 뜻인지 단박에 짐작했다."], ["The birds had been driven down into this corner the day before by some shooting-party; and while those that had dropped dead under the shot, or had died before nightfall, had been searched for and carried off, many badly wounded birds had escaped and hidden themselves away, or risen among the thick boughs, where they had maintained their position till they grew weaker with loss of blood in the night-time, when they had fallen one by one as she had heard them.", "그 새들은 어제 어느 사냥 패거리에게 이 구석으로 몰려 내려온 것이었다. 그리고 총에 맞아 그 자리에서 죽어 떨어졌거나 밤이 되기 전에 죽은 것들은 사람들이 찾아내어 가져갔지만, 심하게 다친 새들 가운데 다수는 달아나 숨어 버렸거나, 빽빽한 나뭇가지 사이로 날아올라 거기에 자리를 지키고 있다가, 밤사이 피를 흘려 점점 기운이 빠지자 그녀가 들은 대로 하나둘씩 떨어져 내린 것이었다."]], [["She had occasionally caught glimpses of these men in girlhood, looking over hedges, or peeping through bushes, and pointing their guns, strangely accoutred, a bloodthirsty light in their eyes.", "그녀는 처녀 시절에 이런 사내들을 이따금 언뜻 본 적이 있었다. 울타리 너머를 살피거나 덤불 사이로 엿보며 총을 겨누던 모습, 야릇하게 차려입고 눈에는 피에 굶주린 빛을 띤 모습을."], ["She had been told that, rough and brutal as they seemed just then, they were not like this all the year round, but were, in fact, quite civil persons save during certain weeks of autumn and winter, when, like the inhabitants of the Malay Peninsula, they ran amuck, and made it their purpose to destroy life—in this case harmless feathered creatures, brought into being by artificial means solely to gratify these propensities—at once so unmannerly and so unchivalrous towards their weaker fellows in Nature’s teeming family.", "그녀는 들어 알고 있었다. 그자들이 그때는 거칠고 잔인해 보여도 일 년 내내 그런 것은 아니며, 사실은 가을과 겨울의 몇 주를 빼면 자못 점잖은 사람들이라는 것을. 다만 그 몇 주 동안에는, 말레이반도의 주민들처럼, 광기에 사로잡혀 날뛰며 생명을 파괴하는 것을 제 목적으로 삼는다는 것을—이 경우에는 순전히 그자들의 그 성향을 충족시키려는 목적으로 인공적인 수단을 써서 태어나게 한, 자연의 그 우글거리는 한 가족 안에서 저보다 약한 형제들에게 그토록 무례하고 그토록 비겁한 짓을 저지르는, 죄 없는 깃털 달린 생물들을."]], [["With the impulse of a soul who could feel for kindred sufferers as much as for herself, Tess’s first thought was to put the still living birds out of their torture, and to this end with her own hands she broke the necks of as many as she could find, leaving them to lie where she had found them till the game-keepers should come—as they probably would come—to look for them a second time.", "제 일처럼 같은 처지의 고난받는 것들을 위해 아파할 수 있는 영혼의 충동으로, 테스의 첫 생각은 아직 살아 있는 새들을 그 고통에서 벗어나게 해 주는 것이었다. 이를 위해 그녀는 제 손으로, 찾을 수 있는 만큼의 새들 목을 비틀어 꺾고는, 사냥터지기들이—아마도 올 텐데—그것들을 다시 찾으러 올 때까지 찾은 자리에 그대로 놓아두었다."]], [["“Poor darlings—to suppose myself the most miserable being on earth in the sight o’ such misery as yours!”", "\"가엾은 것들아—너희들의 이런 비참함을 보면서도 나 자신을 세상에서 가장 비참한 존재로 여겼다니!\""], ["she exclaimed, her tears running down as she killed the birds tenderly.", "그녀는 새들을 부드럽게 죽이며 눈물을 흘리면서 외쳤다."], ["“And not a twinge of bodily pain about me!", "\"내겐 몸이 쑤시는 아픔조차 한 점 없는데!"], ["I be not mangled, and I be not bleeding, and I have two hands to feed and clothe me.”", "나는 갈가리 찢기지도 않았고, 피를 흘리지도 않으며, 나를 먹이고 입힐 두 손이 있는데.\""], ["She was ashamed of herself for her gloom of the night, based on nothing more tangible than a sense of condemnation under an arbitrary law of society which had no foundation in Nature.", "그녀는 자연에 아무런 근거도 없는, 사회의 자의적인 법 아래 단죄당했다는 느낌, 그보다 더 만져질 것 없는 무언가에 바탕을 둔 간밤의 그 우울을 스스로 부끄러워했다."]], [["# XLII", "# XLII"]], [["It was now broad day, and she started again, emerging cautiously upon the highway.", "이제 날이 훤히 밝았고, 그녀는 다시 길을 나서, 큰길로 조심스레 나왔다."], ["But there was no need for caution; not a soul was at hand, and Tess went onward with fortitude, her recollection of the birds’ silent endurance of their night of agony impressing upon her the relativity of sorrows and the tolerable nature of her own, if she could once rise high enough to despise opinion.", "그러나 조심할 필요는 없었다. 가까이에는 사람 그림자 하나 없었고, 테스는 꿋꿋이 앞으로 나아갔다. 밤새 고통을 말없이 견뎌 낸 새들의 기억이 그녀에게 슬픔이란 상대적인 것이며, 한번 견해 따위를 멸시할 만큼 충분히 높이 올라설 수만 있다면 제 슬픔쯤은 견딜 만한 것임을 일깨워 주었다."], ["But that she could not do so long as it was held by Clare.", "그러나 그것이 클레어에게 견지되는 한 그녀는 그렇게 할 수 없었다."]], [["She reached Chalk-Newton, and breakfasted at an inn, where several young men were troublesomely complimentary to her good looks.", "그녀는 초크뉴턴에 다다라 어느 여관에서 아침을 먹었는데, 거기서 젊은이 몇이 그녀의 고운 외모를 두고 성가실 만큼 추켜세웠다."], ["Somehow she felt hopeful, for was it not possible that her husband also might say these same things to her even yet?", "어쩐 일인지 그녀는 희망을 느꼈다. 남편 역시 아직도 그녀에게 이와 똑같은 말을 해 줄 수 있을지도 모르지 않는가?"], ["She was bound to take care of herself on the chance of it, and keep off these casual lovers.", "그 가능성을 위해서라도 그녀는 자신을 잘 건사하고, 이렇게 무심코 다가오는 구애자들을 떨쳐 내야 했다."], ["To this end Tess resolved to run no further risks from her appearance.", "이를 위해 테스는 제 용모로 인해 더는 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰지 않기로 결심했다."], ["As soon as she got out of the village she entered a thicket and took from her basket one of the oldest field-gowns, which she had never put on even at the dairy—never since she had worked among the stubble at Marlott.", "마을을 벗어나자마자 그녀는 덤불숲으로 들어가 바구니에서 가장 낡은 들일용 가운 하나를 꺼냈다. 낙농장에서조차 한 번도 입은 적 없는, 말럿에서 그루터기 밭을 누비며 일하던 때 이후로는 입어 본 적 없는 옷이었다."], ["She also, by a felicitous thought, took a handkerchief from her bundle and tied it round her face under her bonnet, covering her chin and half her cheeks and temples, as if she were suffering from toothache.", "또 그녀는 기막힌 생각이 떠올라 보따리에서 손수건을 하나 꺼내, 마치 치통을 앓는 사람처럼 턱과 양 볼과 관자놀이 절반을 가리며 보닛 아래 얼굴에 둘러 묶었다."], ["Then with her little scissors, by the aid of a pocket looking-glass, she mercilessly nipped her eyebrows off, and thus insured against aggressive admiration, she went on her uneven way.", "그러고는 주머니 거울의 도움을 받아 작은 가위로 인정사정없이 제 눈썹을 잘라 냈고, 그렇게 공격적인 흠모를 막아 둔 채 울퉁불퉁한 제 길을 다시 갔다."]], [["“What a mommet of a maid!”", "\"무슨 허수아비 같은 처자람!\""], ["said the next man who met her to a companion.", "다음으로 그녀와 마주친 사내가 일행에게 말했다."]], [["Tears came into her eyes for very pity of herself as she heard him.", "그 말을 들으며 그녀는 순전히 제 신세가 가여워 눈물이 핑 돌았다."], ["“But I don’t care!”", "\"하지만 난 상관없어!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“O no—I don’t care!", "\"아, 그래—난 상관없어!"], ["I’ll always be ugly now, because Angel is not here, and I have nobody to take care of me.", "이제 난 늘 못생긴 채로 있을 테야. 앤젤이 여기 없고, 나를 보살펴 줄 사람이 아무도 없으니까."], ["My husband that was is gone away, and never will love me any more; but I love him just the same, and hate all other men, and like to make ’em think scornfully of me!”", "내 남편이었던 사람은 떠나 버렸고, 다시는 나를 사랑하지 않겠지. 하지만 나는 한결같이 그이를 사랑하고, 다른 모든 남자는 미워하며, 그자들이 나를 경멸스럽게 여기도록 만들고 싶어!\""]], [["Thus Tess walks on; a figure which is part of the landscape; a fieldwoman pure and simple, in winter guise; a gray serge cape, a red woollen cravat, a stuff skirt covered by a whitey-brown rough wrapper, and buff-leather gloves.", "이렇게 테스는 걸어간다. 풍경의 한 부분이 된 형상이요, 겨울 차림을 한 더없이 순박한 들일하는 여인이다. 회색 서지 망토에, 붉은 모직 목도리, 누르스름한 갈색의 거친 작업복에 덮인 거친 천 치마, 그리고 담황색 가죽 장갑."], ["Every thread of that old attire has become faded and thin under the stroke of raindrops, the burn of sunbeams, and the stress of winds. There is no sign of young passion in her now—", "그 낡은 옷의 실 한 올 한 올이 빗방울의 매질과 햇살의 그을림과 바람의 시달림 아래 바래고 닳아 버렸다. 이제 그녀에게서는 젊은 정열의 기색이라곤 찾아볼 수 없다—"]], [["The maiden’s mouth is cold . . . . .", "처녀의 입은 차갑고 . . . . ."], ["Fold over simple fold Binding her head.", "겹겹이 단순한 주름이 그 머리를 감싼다."]], [["Inside this exterior, over which the eye might have roved as over a thing scarcely percipient, almost inorganic, there was the record of a pulsing life which had learnt too well, for its years, of the dust and ashes of things, of the cruelty of lust and the fragility of love.", "거의 지각도 없는, 거의 무기물에 가까운 무언가를 보듯 눈길이 그 위를 미끄러져 갔을 법한 이 겉모습 속에는, 그러나 나이에 비해 만물의 티끌과 재를, 욕정의 잔인함과 사랑의 덧없음을 너무도 잘 배워 버린 한 고동치는 생명의 기록이 들어 있었다."]], [["Next day the weather was bad, but she trudged on, the honesty, directness, and impartiality of elemental enmity disconcerting her but little.", "이튿날 날씨는 궂었지만 그녀는 터벅터벅 걸어 나아갔다. 원초적 적의의 정직함과 직선적임과 공평무사함은 그녀를 거의 당황시키지 못했다."], ["Her object being a winter’s occupation and a winter’s home, there was no time to lose.", "그녀의 목적은 겨울 한 철의 일거리와 겨울 한 철의 거처였으므로, 허비할 시간이 없었다."], ["Her experience of short hirings had been such that she was determined to accept no more.", "짧은 고용에 대한 그녀의 경험은, 더는 그런 것을 받아들이지 않겠다고 결심하게 할 만큼이었다."]], [["Thus she went forward from farm to farm in the direction of the place whence Marian had written to her, which she determined to make use of as a last shift only, its rumoured stringencies being the reverse of tempting.", "그리하여 그녀는 매리언이 편지를 써 보낸 그 장소를 향해 농장에서 농장으로 나아갔다. 그곳은 소문난 고됨이 끌리기는커녕 그 반대인지라, 마지막 수단으로만 쓰기로 마음먹은 곳이었다."], ["First she inquired for the lighter kinds of employment, and, as acceptance in any variety of these grew hopeless, applied next for the less light, till, beginning with the dairy and poultry tendance that she liked best, she ended with the heavy and course pursuits which she liked least—work on arable land: work of such roughness, indeed, as she would never have deliberately voluteered for.", "먼저 그녀는 더 가벼운 종류의 일자리를 알아보았고, 그런 일이라면 어느 것을 구해도 받아들여지기가 가망 없어지자, 그다음으로 덜 가벼운 일을 청했다. 그렇게 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 낙농과 가금 돌보기로 시작했다가, 가장 싫어하는 무겁고 거친 일로—경작지 일로—끝났다. 정말이지 그녀가 스스로 자청해서는 결코 맡지 않았을 만큼 거친 일이었다."]], [["Towards the second evening she reached the irregular chalk table-land or plateau, bosomed with semi-globular tumuli—as if Cybele the Many-breasted were supinely extended there—which stretched between the valley of her birth and the valley of her love.", "이튿날 저녁 무렵 그녀는 들쭉날쭉한 백악질 대지, 곧 반구형의 봉분들이 젖가슴처럼 부풀어 오른 그 고원에 다다랐다—마치 젖가슴이 여럿인 키벨레 여신이 거기 벌렁 누워 길게 뻗어 있기라도 한 듯한 그 고원은, 그녀가 태어난 골짜기와 그녀의 사랑이 깃든 골짜기 사이에 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["Here the air was dry and cold, and the long cart-roads were blown white and dusty within a few hours after rain.", "이곳은 공기가 메마르고 차가웠으며, 긴 수레길은 비가 온 지 몇 시간 만에 허옇게 먼지가 일며 바싹 말랐다."], ["There were few trees, or none, those that would have grown in the hedges being mercilessly plashed down with the quickset by the tenant-farmers, the natural enemies of tree, bush, and brake.", "나무는 거의 없거나 아예 없었다. 울타리에 자랐을 법한 나무들은 나무와 덤불과 수풀의 천적인 소작농들이 키 작은 산울타리와 함께 인정사정없이 후려쳐 눕혀 버린 까닭이었다."], ["In the middle distance ahead of her she could see the summits of Bulbarrow and of Nettlecombe Tout, and they seemed friendly.", "앞쪽 중간쯤 거리에 그녀는 불바로와 네틀콤타우트의 꼭대기를 볼 수 있었는데, 그것들은 정답게 느껴졌다."], ["They had a low and unassuming aspect from this upland, though as approached on the other side from Blackmoor in her childhood they were as lofty bastions against the sky.", "이 고지대에서 보면 그것들은 낮고 수수한 모양새였지만, 어린 시절 블랙무어 쪽 반대편에서 다가갈 때면 하늘을 향해 우뚝 솟은 높다란 보루 같았다."], ["Southerly, at many miles’ distance, and over the hills and ridges coastward, she could discern a surface like polished steel: it was the English Channel at a point far out towards France.", "남쪽으로, 여러 마일 떨어진 거리에, 해안 쪽으로 늘어선 언덕과 능선들 너머로, 그녀는 윤이 나는 강철 같은 표면을 분간할 수 있었다. 프랑스 쪽으로 멀찍이 뻗어 나간 한 지점의 영국 해협이었다."]], [["Before her, in a slight depression, were the remains of a village.", "그녀 앞, 살짝 꺼진 분지에는 한 마을의 잔해가 있었다."], ["She had, in fact, reached Flintcomb-Ash, the place of Marian’s sojourn.", "사실 그녀는 매리언이 머무는 곳, 플린트콤애시에 다다른 것이었다."], ["There seemed to be no help for it; hither she was doomed to come.", "어쩔 도리가 없는 듯했다. 그녀는 이곳으로 오도록 운명 지어진 것이었다."], ["The stubborn soil around her showed plainly enough that the kind of labour in demand here was of the roughest kind; but it was time to rest from searching, and she resolved to stay, particularly as it began to rain.", "그녀 주위의 고집스러운 흙은, 이곳에서 필요로 하는 노동이 가장 거친 종류의 것임을 충분히 또렷이 드러내 보였다. 그러나 일자리 찾기를 그만두고 쉴 때가 되었고, 게다가 비가 내리기 시작한 터라, 그녀는 머물기로 마음먹었다."], ["At the entrance to the village was a cottage whose gable jutted into the road, and before applying for a lodging she stood under its shelter, and watched the evening close in.", "마을 어귀에는 박공이 길 쪽으로 비죽 나온 오두막 한 채가 있었다. 묵을 곳을 청하기 전에 그녀는 그 처마 밑에 서서 저녁이 저무는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["“Who would think I was Mrs Angel Clare!”", "\"내가 앤젤 클레어 부인이라고 누가 생각이나 하겠어!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The wall felt warm to her back and shoulders, and she found that immediately within the gable was the cottage fireplace, the heat of which came through the bricks.", "벽은 그녀의 등과 어깨에 따뜻하게 느껴졌다. 그녀는 그 박공 바로 안쪽이 오두막의 벽난로 자리이며, 그 열기가 벽돌을 통해 전해 온다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["She warmed her hands upon them, and also put her cheek—red and moist with the drizzle—against their comforting surface.", "그녀는 거기에 손을 녹였고, 또 가랑비에 발갛게 젖은 볼을 그 위안을 주는 표면에 갖다 댔다."], ["The wall seemed to be the only friend she had.", "그 벽이 그녀가 가진 유일한 벗인 것만 같았다."], ["She had so little wish to leave it that she could have stayed there all night.", "거기를 떠나고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 없었던지, 밤새도록 거기 머물러 있을 수도 있을 것 같았다."]], [["Tess could hear the occupants of the cottage—gathered together after their day’s labour—talking to each other within, and the rattle of their supper-plates was also audible.", "테스는 하루 일을 마치고 한데 모인 오두막 식구들이 안에서 서로 이야기하는 소리를, 또 저녁 접시들이 달그락거리는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["But in the village-street she had seen no soul as yet. The solitude was at last broken by the approach of one feminine figure, who, though the evening was cold, wore the print gown and the tilt-bonnet of summer time.", "그러나 마을 거리에서 그녀는 아직 사람 그림자 하나 보지 못했다. 그 적막은 마침내 다가오는 한 여인의 형상으로 깨졌는데, 그녀는 저녁이 추운데도 여름철의 무명 가운에 차양 보닛을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Tess instinctively thought it might be Marian, and when she came near enough to be distinguishable in the gloom, surely enough it was she.", "테스는 본능적으로 그게 매리언일지도 모른다고 생각했고, 어스름 속에서 분간할 수 있을 만큼 가까이 다가오자, 아니나 다를까 그녀였다."], ["Marian was even stouter and redder in the face than formerly, and decidedly shabbier in attire.", "매리언은 예전보다 한층 살이 찌고 얼굴이 더 붉었으며, 차림새는 확연히 더 초라했다."], ["At any previous period of her existence Tess would hardly have cared to renew the acquaintance in such conditions; but her loneliness was excessive, and she responded readily to Marian’s greeting.", "그녀가 살아온 어느 이전 시기였다면 테스는 이런 형편에서 옛정을 다시 잇고 싶은 마음이 거의 들지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 외로움은 너무도 컸기에, 그녀는 매리언의 인사에 선뜻 응했다."]], [["Marian was quite respectful in her inquiries, but seemed much moved by the fact that Tess should still continue in no better condition than at first; though she had dimly heard of the separation.", "매리언은 안부를 물으면서 자못 깍듯했으나, 테스가 여전히 처음과 다를 바 없는 형편에 머물러 있다는 사실에 무척 마음이 동요한 듯했다. 그래도 그녀는 두 사람이 헤어졌다는 소식을 어렴풋이 듣기는 했던 터였다."]], [["“Tess—Mrs Clare—the dear wife of dear he!", "\"테스—클레어 부인—사랑하는 그분의 사랑하는 아내!"], ["And is it really so bad as this, my child?", "그런데 정말 이렇게까지 딱하게 됐어, 얘?"], ["Why is your cwomely face tied up in such a way?", "어쩌자고 그 고운 얼굴을 그렇게 싸매고 있는 거야?"], ["Anybody been beating ’ee? Not _he_?”", "누가 널 때리기라도 했어? 그분이?\""]], [["“No, no, no! I merely did it not to be clipsed or colled, Marian.”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야! 그냥 추근거리거나 끌어안기지 않으려고 그런 것뿐이야, 매리언.\""]], [["She pulled off in disgust a bandage which could suggest such wild thoughts.", "* 추근거리거나 끌어안기지: 원문의 사투리 \"clipsed or colled\"로, 희롱당하거나 껴안기는 것을 뜻한다."]], [["“And you’ve got no collar on”", "그런 거친 상상을 불러일으킬 만한 붕대를, 그녀는 역겹다는 듯 풀어 던졌다."], ["(Tess had been accustomed to wear a little white collar at the dairy).", "\"그리고 너 칼라도 안 했네.\" (테스는 낙농장에서 작은 흰 칼라를 두르는 데 익숙했다.)"]], [["“I know it, Marian.”", "\"알아, 메리언.\""]], [["“You’ve lost it travelling.”", "\"오다가 잃어버렸구나.\""]], [["“I’ve not lost it.", "\"잃어버린 게 아냐."], ["The truth is, I don’t care anything about my looks; and so I didn’t put it on.”", "사실은, 내 겉모습 따위 아무래도 좋아서, 그래서 안 했어.\""]], [["“And you don’t wear your wedding-ring?”", "\"그럼 결혼반지도 안 끼고?\""]], [["“Yes, I do; but not in public.", "\"아니, 끼고 있어. 다만 남들 앞에선 안 해."], ["I wear it round my neck on a ribbon.", "리본에 꿰어 목에 걸고 다녀."], ["I don’t wish people to think who I am by marriage, or that I am married at all; it would be so awkward while I lead my present life.” Marian paused.", "사람들이 내가 결혼으로 어떤 신분이 됐는지, 아니 내가 결혼했다는 사실 자체를 알았으면 싶지 않아. 지금 같은 생활을 하면서는 그게 너무 거북하거든.\""]], [["“But you _be_ a gentleman’s wife; and it seems hardly fair that you should live like this!”", "메리언은 잠시 입을 다물었다. \"하지만 너는 신사의 아내잖아. 그런데 이런 식으로 사는 건 아무래도 옳지 않아 보여!\""]], [["“O yes it is, quite fair; though I am very unhappy.”", "\"아냐, 옳아, 더없이 옳아. 비록 내가 무척 불행하긴 해도.\""]], [["“Well, well.", "\"세상에, 세상에."], ["_He_ married you—and you can be unhappy!”", "그분이 너랑 결혼했는데도—네가 불행할 수가 있다니!\""]], [["“Wives are unhappy sometimes; from no fault of their husbands—from their own.”", "\"아내들도 가끔은 불행해. 남편의 잘못 때문이 아니라—자기 잘못 때문에.\""]], [["“You’ve no faults, deary; that I’m sure of.", "\"넌 잘못이 없어, 얘. 그건 내가 확신해."], ["And he’s none.", "그분도 잘못이 없고."], ["So it must be something outside ye both.”", "그러니 분명 너희 둘 바깥의 무언가일 거야.\""]], [["“Marian, dear Marian, will you do me a good turn without asking questions?", "\"메리언, 사랑하는 메리언, 아무것도 묻지 않고 나한테 부탁 하나 들어줄래?"], ["My husband has gone abroad, and somehow I have overrun my allowance, so that I have to fall back upon my old work for a time.", "내 남편은 외국으로 갔는데, 어쩌다 보니 내가 받은 생활비를 다 써 버려서, 한동안 옛날 일거리로 돌아가야 해."], ["Do not call me Mrs Clare, but Tess, as before. Do they want a hand here?”", "나를 클레어 부인이라 부르지 말고, 예전처럼 테스라고 불러 줘. 여기 일손 구하니?\""]], [["“O yes; they’ll take one always, because few care to come.", "\"응, 그럼. 여긴 늘 일손을 받아. 오려는 사람이 드물거든."], ["’Tis a starve-acre place.", "굶주린 땅 같은 곳이야."], ["Corn and swedes are all they grow.", "밀이랑 스웨덴순무밖에 안 키워."], ["Though I be here myself, I feel ’tis a pity for such as you to come.”", "나야 여기 있다지만, 너 같은 애가 오는 건 안됐다 싶어.\""]], [["“But you used to be as good a dairywoman as I.”", "\"하지만 너도 나만큼이나 솜씨 좋은 낙농 일꾼이었잖아.\""]], [["“Yes; but I’ve got out o’ that since I took to drink.", "\"그랬지. 근데 술을 입에 대면서 그쪽 일에선 손을 놨어."], ["Lord, that’s the only comfort I’ve got now!", "세상에, 그게 지금 내 유일한 위안이라니까!"], ["If you engage, you’ll be set swede-hacking.", "네가 들어오면 스웨덴순무 캐는 일에 배정될 거야."], ["That’s what I be doing; but you won’t like it.”", "내가 하는 일이 그거거든. 근데 넌 마음에 안 들 거야.\""]], [["“O—anything!", "\"아, 뭐든 좋아!"], ["Will you speak for me?”", "나 대신 말 좀 해 줄래?\""]], [["“You will do better by speaking for yourself.”", "\"네가 직접 말하는 게 나을 거야.\""]], [["“Very well. Now, Marian, remember—nothing about _him_ if I get the place.", "\"좋아. 그럼, 메리언, 명심해—내가 자리를 얻게 되면 그분 얘기는 한마디도 하지 마."], ["I don’t wish to bring his name down to the dirt.”", "그분 이름을 진흙탕에 끌어내리고 싶지 않아.\""]], [["Marian, who was really a trustworthy girl though of coarser grain than Tess, promised anything she asked.", "메리언은 테스보다 결이 거칠긴 해도 정말로 믿을 만한 처녀였고, 그녀가 부탁하는 건 무엇이든 약속했다."]], [["“This is pay-night,”", "\"오늘이 급료 받는 밤이야.\""], ["she said, “and if you were to come with me you would know at once.", "메리언이 말했다. \"나랑 같이 가면 바로 알게 될 거야."], ["I be real sorry that you are not happy; but ’tis because he’s away, I know.", "네가 행복하지 않다니 정말 안됐어. 하지만 그건 그분이 멀리 있어서겠지, 나도 알아."], ["You couldn’t be unhappy if he were here, even if he gie’d ye no money—even if he used you like a drudge.”", "그분이 여기 있다면 넌 불행할 리가 없어, 그분이 너한테 돈 한 푼 안 줘도—심지어 너를 종처럼 부려먹어도 말이야.\""]], [["“That’s true; I could not!”", "\"맞아. 그럴 리 없지!\""]], [["They walked on together and soon reached the farmhouse, which was almost sublime in its dreariness.", "둘은 함께 걸어 곧 농가에 다다랐는데, 그 황량함이 거의 숭고할 지경이었다."], ["There was not a tree within sight; there was not, at this season, a green pasture—nothing but fallow and turnips everywhere, in large fields divided by hedges plashed to unrelieved levels.", "눈에 들어오는 나무 한 그루 없었고, 이 계절엔 푸른 목초지 하나 없이, 사방이 묵정밭과 순무뿐이었다. 단조롭고 평평하게 다듬어진 산울타리로 갈린 넓은 밭들만 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["Tess waited outside the door of the farmhouse till the group of workfolk had received their wages, and then Marian introduced her.", "테스는 일꾼 무리가 급료를 다 받을 때까지 농가 문밖에서 기다렸고, 그런 뒤 메리언이 그녀를 소개했다."], ["The farmer himself, it appeared, was not at home, but his wife, who represented him this evening, made no objection to hiring Tess, on her agreeing to remain till Old Lady-Day.", "농장주 본인은 집에 없는 모양이었으나, 이날 저녁 그를 대신한 그의 아내는, 테스가 옛 성모영보일까지 머물기로 동의하자 그녀를 고용하는 데 반대하지 않았다."], ["Female field-labour was seldom offered now, and its cheapness made it profitable for tasks which women could perform as readily as men.", "이제 여자 들일은 좀처럼 제공되지 않았고, 그 값싼 품삯 덕에 여자가 남자만큼 쉽게 해낼 수 있는 일에는 오히려 이문이 남았다."]], [["Having signed the agreement, there was nothing more for Tess to do at present than to get a lodging, and she found one in the house at whose gable-wall she had warmed herself.", "계약서에 서명하고 나자 테스가 당장 할 일은 묵을 곳을 구하는 것뿐이었는데, 예전에 그 박공벽에 기대어 몸을 녹였던 바로 그 집에서 하나를 찾았다."], ["It was a poor subsistence that she had ensured, but it would afford a shelter for the winter at any rate.", "그녀가 확보한 것은 변변찮은 생계였지만, 어쨌든 겨울 동안 비바람을 피할 거처는 되어 주었다."], ["That night she wrote to inform her parents of her new address, in case a letter should arrive at Marlott from her husband.", "그날 밤 그녀는 부모에게 새 주소를 알리는 편지를 썼다. 혹여 남편에게서 편지가 말럿으로 올지 모르니까."], ["But she did not tell them of the sorriness of her situation: it might have brought reproach upon him.", "그러나 자기 처지의 비참함은 알리지 않았다. 그것이 그에게 비난을 불러올 수도 있었으므로."]], [["# XLIII", "# XLIII"]], [["There was no exaggeration in Marian’s definition of Flintcomb-Ash farm as a starve-acre place.", "메리언이 플린트콤애시 농장을 굶주린 땅 같은 곳이라 규정한 데에는 조금의 과장도 없었다."], ["The single fat thing on the soil was Marian herself; and she was an importation.", "그 땅에서 유일하게 살이 오른 것은 메리언 자신뿐이었고, 그녀조차 외지에서 들여온 존재였다."], ["Of the three classes of village, the village cared for by its lord, the village cared for by itself, and the village uncared for either by itself or by its lord (in other words, the village of a resident squire’s tenantry, the village of free or copy-holders, and the absentee-owner’s village, farmed with the land) this place, Flintcomb-Ash, was the third.", "마을에는 세 부류가 있다. 영주의 보살핌을 받는 마을, 제 손으로 제 살림을 꾸리는 마을, 그리고 제 손으로도 영주의 손으로도 보살핌을 못 받는 마을(달리 말하면, 현지에 사는 지주의 소작인들로 이루어진 마을, 자유 보유농이나 등본 보유농의 마을, 그리고 부재지주의 마을, 즉 땅과 함께 통째로 경작되는 마을이다). 이곳 플린트콤애시는 그 세 번째였다."]], [["But Tess set to work.", "그러나 테스는 일에 착수했다."], ["Patience, that blending of moral courage with physical timidity, was now no longer a minor feature in Mrs Angel Clare; and it sustained her.", "인내—도덕적 용기와 육체적 소심함이 한데 섞인 그것은, 이제 엔젤 클레어 부인에게서 더 이상 사소한 특징이 아니었고, 그것이 그녀를 버티게 했다."]], [["The swede-field in which she and her companion were set hacking was a stretch of a hundred odd acres in one patch, on the highest ground of the farm, rising above stony lanchets or lynchets—the outcrop of siliceous veins in the chalk formation, composed of myriads of loose white flints in bulbous, cusped, and phallic shapes.", "그녀와 동료가 캐기 일을 배정받은 스웨덴순무밭은 한 뙈기로 백 에이커 남짓 뻗어 있었고, 농장에서 가장 높은 지대에, 돌투성이 랜칫 혹은 린칫 위로 솟아 있었다. 그것은 백악층에 박힌 규질 광맥이 드러난 것으로, 둥글납작하고 뾰족하고 남근 같은 모양의 헐거운 흰 부싯돌 수만 개로 이루어져 있었다."], ["The upper half of each turnip had been eaten off by the live-stock, and it was the business of the two women to grub up the lower or earthy half of the root with a hooked fork called a hacker, that it might be eaten also.", "순무는 저마다 위쪽 절반을 가축이 뜯어 먹었고, 이제 두 여자가 할 일은 해커라 불리는 갈고리 쇠스랑으로 뿌리의 아래쪽, 흙에 묻힌 절반을 파내 그것마저 먹이로 쓰게 하는 것이었다."], ["Every leaf of the vegetable having already been consumed, the whole field was in colour a desolate drab; it was a complexion without features, as if a face, from chin to brow, should be only an expanse of skin.", "그 채소의 잎은 이미 한 장도 남김없이 먹혀, 밭 전체가 빛깔로는 황량한 칙칙한 갈색이었다. 그것은 이목구비 없는 낯빛이었다. 마치 어떤 얼굴이 턱에서 이마까지 그저 살갗의 너른 면일 뿐인 것처럼."], ["The sky wore, in another colour, the same likeness; a white vacuity of countenance with the lineaments gone.", "하늘은 다른 빛깔로 똑같은 모습을 띠고 있었다. 윤곽이 사라진, 새하얗게 텅 빈 낯이었다."], ["So these two upper and nether visages confronted each other all day long, the white face looking down on the brown face, and the brown face looking up at the white face, without anything standing between them but the two girls crawling over the surface of the former like flies.", "그래서 이 위아래 두 얼굴은 온종일 서로를 마주했다. 흰 얼굴은 갈색 얼굴을 내려다보고, 갈색 얼굴은 흰 얼굴을 올려다보았으며, 그 사이엔 갈색 얼굴의 표면을 파리처럼 기어 다니는 두 처녀 말고는 아무것도 끼어 있지 않았다."]], [["Nobody came near them, and their movements showed a mechanical regularity; their forms standing enshrouded in Hessian “wroppers”—sleeved brown pinafores, tied behind to the bottom, to keep their gowns from blowing about—scant skirts revealing boots that reached high up the ankles, and yellow sheepskin gloves with gauntlets.", "아무도 그들 곁에 오지 않았고, 그들의 동작은 기계적인 규칙성을 보였다. 그들의 모습은 거친 삼베 '래퍼'를 둘러쓴 채였다—소매 달린 갈색 앞치마로, 치맛자락이 바람에 펄럭이지 않게 뒤쪽 아래에서 묶었다—짧은 치마 아래로는 발목 위까지 올라오는 장화가 드러났고, 손에는 토시 달린 노란 양가죽 장갑을 꼈다."], ["The pensive character which the curtained hood lent to their bent heads would have reminded the observer of some early Italian conception of the two Marys.", "두건 자락이 그들의 숙인 머리에 드리운 그 사색적인 분위기는, 보는 이로 하여금 초기 이탈리아 화가가 그린 두 마리아의 어떤 구도를 떠올리게 했으리라."]], [["They worked on hour after hour, unconscious of the forlorn aspect they bore in the landscape, not thinking of the justice or injustice of their lot.", "그들은 시간이 가고 또 가도 일을 계속했고, 풍경 속에서 자신들이 지닌 그 쓸쓸한 모습을 의식하지 못했으며, 제 처지가 공정한지 부당한지도 생각하지 않았다."], ["Even in such a position as theirs it was possible to exist in a dream.", "그들 같은 처지에서조차 꿈속에서 존재하기란 가능했다."], ["In the afternoon the rain came on again, and Marian said that they need not work any more.", "오후에 다시 비가 들이쳤고, 메리언은 더 일하지 않아도 된다고 말했다."], ["But if they did not work they would not be paid; so they worked on.", "하지만 일하지 않으면 품삯도 못 받았다. 그래서 그들은 계속 일했다."], ["It was so high a situation, this field, that the rain had no occasion to fall, but raced along horizontally upon the yelling wind, sticking into them like glass splinters till they were wet through.", "이 밭은 지대가 워낙 높아 비가 내릴 까닭도 없었으나, 울부짖는 바람을 타고 수평으로 내달려 유리 파편처럼 그들에게 박혀, 끝내 흠뻑 젖게 했다."], ["Tess had not known till now what was really meant by that.", "테스는 지금까지 그 말이 정말 무슨 뜻인지 몰랐다."], ["There are degrees of dampness, and a very little is called being wet through in common talk.", "축축함에도 정도가 있고, 흔히들 아주 조금만 젖어도 흠뻑 젖었다고 말한다."], ["But to stand working slowly in a field, and feel the creep of rain-water, first in legs and shoulders, then on hips and head, then at back, front, and sides, and yet to work on till the leaden light diminishes and marks that the sun is down, demands a distinct modicum of stoicism, even of valour.", "그러나 밭에 서서 더디게 일하며 빗물이 처음엔 다리와 어깨로, 다음엔 엉덩이와 머리로, 그다음엔 등과 앞과 옆구리로 스며드는 것을 느끼면서도, 납빛 광선이 스러져 해가 졌음을 알릴 때까지 계속 일하는 데에는, 뚜렷한 만큼의 극기, 아니 용기마저 요구된다."]], [["Yet they did not feel the wetness so much as might be supposed.", "그래도 그들은 짐작만큼 젖은 것을 그리 절감하지는 않았다."], ["They were both young, and they were talking of the time when they lived and loved together at Talbothays Dairy, that happy green tract of land where summer had been liberal in her gifts; in substance to all, emotionally to these.", "둘 다 젊었고, 탤보세이즈 낙농장에서 함께 살며 사랑했던 시절 이야기를 하고 있었으니까. 여름이 제 선물을 아낌없이 베풀던 그 행복한 푸른 땅을—모두에게는 물질로, 이들에게는 마음으로 베풀던 그곳을."], ["Tess would fain not have conversed with Marian of the man who was legally, if not actually, her husband; but the irresistible fascination of the subject betrayed her into reciprocating Marian’s remarks.", "테스는 법적으로는, 비록 실제로는 아니어도, 제 남편인 그 남자 이야기를 메리언과 나누고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 그 화제의 거부할 수 없는 매혹이 그녀를 배신하여, 메리언의 말에 맞장구치게 만들었다."], ["And thus, as has been said, though the damp curtains of their bonnets flapped smartly into their faces, and their wrappers clung about them to wearisomeness, they lived all this afternoon in memories of green, sunny, romantic Talbothays.", "그리하여 앞서 말했듯, 보닛의 축축한 자락이 그들의 얼굴을 매섭게 후려치고, 래퍼가 진력나도록 몸에 달라붙었음에도, 그들은 이 오후 내내 푸르고 햇살 가득하고 낭만적인 탤보세이즈의 추억 속에서 살았다."]], [["“You can see a gleam of a hill within a few miles o’ Froom Valley from here when ’tis fine,” said Marian.", "\"날 좋을 때는 여기서 프룸 골짜기 몇 마일 안쪽 언덕이 어렴풋이 보여.\" 메리언이 말했다."]], [["“Ah! Can you?”", "\"아! 그래?\""], ["said Tess, awake to the new value of this locality.", "테스가 이 고장의 새로운 가치에 눈뜨며 말했다."]], [["So the two forces were at work here as everywhere, the inherent will to enjoy, and the circumstantial will against enjoyment.", "그리하여 여기서도 어디서나처럼 두 힘이 작용하고 있었다. 즐기려는 타고난 의지와, 즐김에 맞서는 상황의 의지가."], ["Marian’s will had a method of assisting itself by taking from her pocket as the afternoon wore on a pint bottle corked with white rag, from which she invited Tess to drink.", "메리언의 의지는 오후가 깊어 가자 주머니에서 흰 헝겊으로 마개를 막은 한 파인트들이 병을 꺼내 스스로를 돕는 방법을 썼고, 거기서 한 모금 마시라고 테스에게 권했다."], ["Tess’s unassisted power of dreaming, however, being enough for her sublimation at present, she declined except the merest sip, and then Marian took a pull from the spirits.", "그러나 테스는 남의 도움 없이 꿈꾸는 자기 힘만으로도 지금의 승화에는 충분했으므로, 그저 입만 살짝 축이는 정도 말고는 사양했고, 그러자 메리언이 그 독한 술을 한 모금 들이켰다."]], [["“I’ve got used to it,”", "\"이제 익숙해졌어.\""], ["she said, “and can’t leave it off now.", "메리언이 말했다. \"이젠 끊을 수가 없어."], ["’Tis my only comfort—You see I lost him: you didn’t; and you can do without it perhaps.”", "이게 내 유일한 위안이야—봐, 난 그분을 잃었잖아. 넌 안 잃었고. 넌 어쩌면 이거 없이도 견딜 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["Tess thought her loss as great as Marian’s, but upheld by the dignity of being Angel’s wife, in the letter at least, she accepted Marian’s differentiation.", "테스는 자기 상실도 메리언의 것 못지않게 크다고 여겼지만, 적어도 글자상으로는 엔젤의 아내라는 위엄에 떠받쳐진 처지였기에, 메리언이 둘을 갈라 보는 것을 받아들였다."]], [["Amid this scene Tess slaved in the morning frosts and in the afternoon rains.", "이런 풍경 속에서 테스는 아침 서리와 오후 빗속에서 노예처럼 일했다."], ["When it was not swede-grubbing it was swede-trimming, in which process they sliced off the earth and the fibres with a bill-hook before storing the roots for future use.", "스웨덴순무를 캐지 않을 때는 다듬는 일이었는데, 이 과정에서는 빌훅으로 흙과 잔뿌리를 잘라 낸 뒤 뿌리를 갈무리해 두었다가 나중에 쓰게 했다."], ["At this occupation they could shelter themselves by a thatched hurdle if it rained; but if it was frosty even their thick leather gloves could not prevent the frozen masses they handled from biting their fingers.", "이 일을 할 때는 비가 오면 이엉을 인 바자 옆에서 비를 피할 수 있었다. 그러나 서리가 내리면 두꺼운 가죽 장갑조차 손에 쥔 얼어붙은 덩이가 손가락을 에는 것을 막아 주지 못했다."], ["Still Tess hoped. She had a conviction that sooner or later the magnanimity which she persisted in reckoning as a chief ingredient of Clare’s character would lead him to rejoin her.", "그래도 테스는 희망을 버리지 않았다. 그녀가 클레어 성품의 으뜸가는 성분이라고 줄곧 꼽아 온 그 너그러움이 머잖아 그를 다시 자기에게로 데려오리라는 확신이 있었다."]], [["Marian, primed to a humorous mood, would discover the queer-shaped flints aforesaid, and shriek with laughter, Tess remaining severely obtuse.", "익살스러운 기분이 한껏 오른 메리언은 앞서 말한 그 기묘한 모양의 부싯돌들을 찾아내며 깔깔 비명을 질러 댔지만, 테스는 더없이 무덤덤한 채로 있었다."], ["They often looked across the country to where the Var or Froom was known to stretch, even though they might not be able to see it; and, fixing their eyes on the cloaking gray mist, imagined the old times they had spent out there.", "그들은 자주 들판 너머, 바르강이나 프룸강이 뻗어 있다고 알려진 쪽을 바라보았다. 눈으로 볼 수는 없을지언정 말이다. 그러고는 두루뭉술한 잿빛 안개에 눈을 박은 채, 저 너머에서 보낸 옛 시절을 그려 보았다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Marian, “how I should like another or two of our old set to come here!", "메리언이 말했다. \"옛 패거리에서 한두 명만 더 여기 와 줬으면 얼마나 좋을까!"], ["Then we could bring up Talbothays every day here afield, and talk of he, and of what nice times we had there, and o’ the old things we used to know, and make it all come back a’most, in seeming!”", "그럼 매일 여기 들판에서 탤보세이즈 얘기를 끄집어내고, 그분 얘기도 하고, 거기서 보낸 그 좋았던 시절 얘기도 하고, 우리가 알던 옛것들 얘기도 하면서, 거의 다, 보이는 것처럼, 되살려 낼 수 있을 텐데!\""], ["Marian’s eyes softened, and her voice grew vague as the visions returned.", "메리언의 눈이 부드러워지고, 그 환영이 되살아나자 목소리도 아련해졌다."], ["“I’ll write to Izz Huett,”", "\"이즈 휴엣한테 편지를 써야겠어.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“She’s biding at home doing nothing now, I know, and I’ll tell her we be here, and ask her to come; and perhaps Retty is well enough now.”", "\"지금 집에서 빈둥거리며 아무것도 안 하고 있을 거야, 내가 알지. 우리 여기 있다고 알리고, 오라고 해야지. 그리고 어쩌면 레티도 이젠 괜찮을 만큼 나았을지 몰라.\""]], [["Tess had nothing to say against the proposal, and the next she heard of this plan for importing old Talbothays’ joys was two or three days later, when Marian informed her that Izz had replied to her inquiry, and had promised to come if she could.", "테스는 그 제안에 반대할 말이 없었고, 옛 탤보세이즈의 기쁨을 들여오려는 이 계획에 대해 다음으로 들은 소식은 이삼 일 뒤였다. 메리언이, 이즈가 자기 물음에 답을 보내왔고 올 수 있으면 오겠다고 약속했다고 알려 준 것이다."]], [["There had not been such a winter for years.", "몇 해 만에 처음 보는 혹독한 겨울이었다."], ["It came on in stealthy and measured glides, like the moves of a chess-player.", "그것은 체스 두는 이의 수처럼 은밀하고 가늠된 미끄럼으로 다가왔다."], ["One morning the few lonely trees and the thorns of the hedgerows appeared as if they had put off a vegetable for an animal integument.", "어느 아침, 외로이 서 있는 몇 그루 나무와 산울타리의 가시나무가 마치 식물의 껍질을 벗고 동물의 가죽을 걸친 듯 보였다."], ["Every twig was covered with a white nap as of fur grown from the rind during the night, giving it four times its usual stoutness; the whole bush or tree forming a staring sketch in white lines on the mournful gray of the sky and horizon.", "잔가지 하나하나가 밤새 껍질에서 돋아난 털 같은 흰 보풀로 덮여 평소의 네 배쯤 굵어 보였고, 덤불이며 나무 전체가 하늘과 지평선의 구슬픈 잿빛 위에 흰 선으로 그려진 또렷한 소묘를 이루었다."], ["Cobwebs revealed their presence on sheds and walls where none had ever been observed till brought out into visibility by the crystallizing atmosphere, hanging like loops of white worsted from salient points of the out-houses, posts, and gates.", "거미줄은 지금껏 누구도 본 적 없던 헛간과 담벼락 곳곳에 제 존재를 드러냈는데, 결정화하는 대기가 그것을 보이게 끄집어낸 것으로, 헛간과 기둥과 문의 도드라진 데마다 흰 털실 고리처럼 매달려 있었다."]], [["After this season of congealed dampness came a spell of dry frost, when strange birds from behind the North Pole began to arrive silently on the upland of Flintcomb-Ash; gaunt spectral creatures with tragical eyes—eyes which had witnessed scenes of cataclysmal horror in inaccessible polar regions of a magnitude such as no human being had ever conceived, in curdling temperatures that no man could endure; which had beheld the crash of icebergs and the slide of snow-hills by the shooting light of the Aurora; been half blinded by the whirl of colossal storms and terraqueous distortions; and retained the expression of feature that such scenes had engendered.", "이 얼어붙은 축축함의 계절이 지나자 마른 서리의 시기가 왔고, 그때 북극 너머에서 온 낯선 새들이 플린트콤애시의 고지에 소리 없이 당도하기 시작했다. 여위고 유령 같은 새들로, 비극적인 눈을 지녔다—인간이라면 아무도 상상조차 못 한 규모로, 누구도 견딜 수 없는 피가 엉길 추위 속에서, 사람의 발길 닿지 않는 극지의 격변하는 공포의 광경을 목격한 눈이었다. 오로라의 쏘아 대는 빛 속에서 빙산이 무너지고 눈산이 미끄러지는 것을 보았고, 거대한 폭풍과 땅과 바다가 뒤틀리는 소용돌이에 반쯤 눈이 멀었으며, 그런 광경이 빚어낸 표정을 그대로 간직한 눈이었다."], ["These nameless birds came quite near to Tess and Marian, but of all they had seen which humanity would never see, they brought no account.", "이 이름 없는 새들은 테스와 메리언에게 꽤 가까이 다가왔으나, 인간은 결코 못 볼 그 모든 광경을 보고도 아무런 이야기도 전해 주지 않았다."], ["The traveller’s ambition to tell was not theirs, and, with dumb impassivity, they dismissed experiences which they did not value for the immediate incidents of this homely upland—the trivial movements of the two girls in disturbing the clods with their hackers so as to uncover something or other that these visitants relished as food.", "보고 온 것을 말하려는 여행자의 욕심 따위 그들에겐 없었고, 말없는 무심함으로, 자기들이 대수롭잖게 여기는 그 경험들을 떨쳐 버린 채, 이 수수한 고지의 눈앞 일들에만—두 처녀가 해커로 흙덩이를 헤집어, 이 방문객들이 먹이로 즐기는 무언가를 드러내 주는 그 하찮은 동작들에만 마음을 두었다."]], [["Then one day a peculiar quality invaded the air of this open country.", "그러던 어느 날, 묘한 기운이 이 트인 고장의 공기에 스며들었다."], ["There came a moisture which was not of rain, and a cold which was not of frost.", "비도 아닌 습기와, 서리도 아닌 추위가 찾아왔다."], ["It chilled the eyeballs of the twain, made their brows ache, penetrated to their skeletons, affecting the surface of the body less than its core.", "그것은 두 사람의 눈알을 시리게 하고, 이마를 쑤시게 했으며, 뼛속까지 파고들어, 몸의 겉보다 그 속을 더 헤집었다."], ["They knew that it meant snow, and in the night the snow came.", "그들은 그것이 눈을 뜻함을 알았고, 밤이 되자 눈이 왔다."], ["Tess, who continued to live at the cottage with the warm gable that cheered any lonely pedestrian who paused beside it, awoke in the night, and heard above the thatch noises which seemed to signify that the roof had turned itself into a gymnasium of all the winds.", "옆을 지나다 멈춰 서는 외로운 길손이라면 누구든 위로해 주던 그 따뜻한 박공의 오두막에서 계속 살던 테스는 밤중에 깨어, 이엉 위에서 마치 지붕이 온갖 바람의 체육관으로 변해 버린 듯한 소리를 들었다."], ["When she lit her lamp to get up in the morning she found that the snow had blown through a chink in the casement, forming a white cone of the finest powder against the inside, and had also come down the chimney, so that it lay sole-deep upon the floor, on which her shoes left tracks when she moved about.", "아침에 일어나려고 등불을 켜 보니, 눈이 여닫이창 틈으로 불어 들어와 안쪽에 더없이 고운 가루로 흰 원뿔을 이루었고, 굴뚝으로도 내려와 마룻바닥에 발창 깊이만큼 쌓여, 그녀가 움직이자 신발 자국이 났다."], ["Without, the storm drove so fast as to create a snow-mist in the kitchen; but as yet it was too dark out-of-doors to see anything.", "바깥에서는 폭풍이 어찌나 빠르게 몰아치는지 부엌 안에까지 눈안개가 일었다. 그러나 아직 문밖은 너무 어두워 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["Tess knew that it was impossible to go on with the swedes; and by the time she had finished breakfast beside the solitary little lamp, Marian arrived to tell her that they were to join the rest of the women at reed-drawing in the barn till the weather changed.", "테스는 스웨덴순무 일을 계속하기란 불가능함을 알았다. 그리고 외로운 작은 등불 옆에서 아침을 다 먹었을 무렵, 메리언이 와서, 날씨가 바뀔 때까지 헛간에서 나머지 여자들과 함께 갈대 추리는 일을 하게 되었다고 알려 주었다."], ["As soon, therefore, as the uniform cloak of darkness without began to turn to a disordered medley of grays, they blew out the lamp, wrapped themselves up in their thickest pinners, tied their woollen cravats round their necks and across their chests, and started for the barn.", "그래서 바깥의 한결같은 어둠의 망토가 어수선한 잿빛의 뒤범벅으로 바뀌기 시작하자마자, 그들은 등불을 끄고, 가장 두꺼운 앞치마로 몸을 감싸고, 모직 목도리를 목에 두르고 가슴께에 가로질러 묶은 뒤 헛간으로 출발했다."], ["The snow had followed the birds from the polar basin as a white pillar of a cloud, and individual flakes could not be seen.", "눈은 극지의 분지에서 흰 구름기둥처럼 새들을 뒤따라 왔고, 눈송이 하나하나는 분간할 수 없었다."], ["The blast smelt of icebergs, arctic seas, whales, and white bears, carrying the snow so that it licked the land but did not deepen on it.", "돌풍에서는 빙산과 북극해와 고래와 흰곰의 냄새가 났고, 눈을 실어 와 땅을 핥기만 할 뿐 그 위에 쌓이지는 않게 했다."], ["They trudged onwards with slanted bodies through the flossy fields, keeping as well as they could in the shelter of hedges, which, however, acted as strainers rather than screens.", "그들은 솜털 같은 들판을 가로질러 몸을 비스듬히 기울인 채 터벅터벅 나아가며, 되도록 산울타리의 그늘에 붙어 가려 했으나, 그것은 가림막이라기보다 차라리 체 노릇을 했다."], ["The air, afflicted to pallor with the hoary multitudes that infested it, twisted and spun them eccentrically, suggesting an achromatic chaos of things.", "공기는 그것을 들끓게 하는 허연 무리들로 핏기를 잃을 만큼 시달려, 두 사람을 기이하게 비틀고 빙빙 돌렸으니, 빛깔 없는 만물의 혼돈을 떠올리게 했다."], ["But both the young women were fairly cheerful; such weather on a dry upland is not in itself dispiriting.", "그래도 두 젊은 여자는 제법 명랑했다. 마른 고지에서 이런 날씨는 그 자체로 기를 꺾는 것은 아니었으니까."]], [["“Ha-ha!", "\"하하!"], ["the cunning northern birds knew this was coming,”", "그 약삭빠른 북녘 새들은 이게 올 줄 알았던 거야.\""], ["said Marian.", "메리언이 말했다."], ["“Depend upon’t, they keep just in front o’t all the way from the North Star.", "\"틀림없어, 그것들은 북극성에서부터 줄곧 이 날씨 바로 앞을 앞질러 다녀."], ["Your husband, my dear, is, I make no doubt, having scorching weather all this time.", "네 남편은, 얘, 분명히 지금 이때 내내 타는 듯한 날씨를 보내고 있을 거야."], ["Lord, if he could only see his pretty wife now!", "세상에, 그분이 지금 자기 어여쁜 아내를 볼 수만 있다면!"], ["Not that this weather hurts your beauty at all—in fact, it rather does it good.”", "이런 날씨가 네 미모를 조금도 해치진 않지만—사실, 오히려 더 살려 주거든.\""]], [["“You mustn’t talk about him to me, Marian,”", "\"내 앞에서 그분 얘기는 하지 마, 메리언.\""], ["said Tess severely.", "테스가 엄하게 말했다."]], [["“Well, but—surely you care for ’n!", "\"그래, 근데—넌 분명 그분을 마음에 두고 있잖아!"], ["Do you?”", "안 그래?\""]], [["Instead of answering, Tess, with tears in her eyes, impulsively faced in the direction in which she imagined South America to lie, and, putting up her lips, blew out a passionate kiss upon the snowy wind.", "대답 대신, 테스는 눈물 어린 눈으로, 충동적으로 남아메리카가 있으리라 짐작되는 쪽을 향해 돌아서더니, 입술을 모아 눈 내리는 바람결에 격정의 입맞춤을 후 불어 보냈다."]], [["“Well, well, I know you do.", "\"그래, 그래, 그런 줄 알아."], ["But ’pon my body, it is a rum life for a married couple!", "하지만 정말이지, 부부한테 이건 참 별난 삶이야!"], ["There—I won’t say another word!", "자—더는 한마디도 안 할게!"], ["Well, as for the weather, it won’t hurt us in the wheat-barn; but reed-drawing is fearful hard work—worse than swede-hacking.", "그건 그렇고, 날씨로 말하자면, 밀 헛간 안에선 우리한테 해될 게 없어. 근데 갈대 추리기는 무지하게 고된 일이야—스웨덴순무 캐기보다 더해."], ["I can stand it because I’m stout; but you be slimmer than I.", "난 튼튼하니까 견디지만, 넌 나보다 가냘프잖아."], ["I can’t think why maister should have set ’ee at it.”", "주인장이 대체 왜 너한테 그 일을 시켰는지 모르겠다.\""]], [["They reached the wheat-barn and entered it.", "그들은 밀 헛간에 다다라 안으로 들어갔다."], ["One end of the long structure was full of corn; the middle was where the reed-drawing was carried on, and there had already been placed in the reed-press the evening before as many sheaves of wheat as would be sufficient for the women to draw from during the day.", "그 길쭉한 건물의 한쪽 끝은 곡식으로 가득했고, 가운데가 갈대 추리는 일을 하는 곳이었으며, 그 전날 저녁에 이미 갈대 압착틀에 여자들이 하루 동안 뽑아낼 만한 밀단이 충분히 놓여 있었다."]], [["“Why, here’s Izz!”", "\"어머, 이즈가 왔네!\""], ["said Marian.", "메리언이 말했다."]], [["Izz it was, and she came forward. She had walked all the way from her mother’s home on the previous afternoon, and, not deeming the distance so great, had been belated, arriving, however, just before the snow began, and sleeping at the alehouse.", "이즈가 맞았고, 그녀가 앞으로 나왔다. 그녀는 전날 오후 어머니 집에서부터 줄곧 걸어왔는데, 거리가 그리 멀 줄 몰랐던 탓에 늦어졌으나, 눈이 내리기 직전에 도착해 술집에서 잤다."], ["The farmer had agreed with her mother at market to take her on if she came to-day, and she had been afraid to disappoint him by delay.", "농장주가 장에서 그녀 어머니와, 오늘 오면 받아 주겠다고 약조했던 터라, 그녀는 늑장으로 그를 실망시킬까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["In addition to Tess, Marian, and Izz, there were two women from a neighbouring village; two Amazonian sisters, whom Tess with a start remembered as Dark Car, the Queen of Spades, and her junior, the Queen of Diamonds—those who had tried to fight with her in the midnight quarrel at Trantridge.", "테스와 메리언과 이즈 말고도, 이웃 마을에서 온 여자가 둘 있었다. 아마존 같은 자매로, 테스는 흠칫하며 그들이 검은 카, 곧 스페이드의 여왕과 그 아래 동생 다이아몬드의 여왕임을 떠올렸다—트랜트리지의 한밤 다툼에서 그녀와 싸우려 들었던 자들이었다."], ["They showed no recognition of her, and possibly had none, for they had been under the influence of liquor on that occasion, and were only temporary sojourners there as here.", "그들은 그녀를 알아보는 기색이 없었고, 어쩌면 정말 못 알아봤을 수도 있었다. 그때 그들은 술기운에 취해 있었고, 이곳에서나 거기서나 잠시 머무는 떠돌이일 뿐이었으니까."], ["They did all kinds of men’s work by preference, including well-sinking, hedging, ditching, and excavating, without any sense of fatigue.", "그들은 우물 파기, 산울타리 손질, 도랑 파기, 땅 파헤치기까지, 온갖 남자 일을 피로한 줄도 모르고 즐겨 했다."], ["Noted reed-drawers were they too, and looked round upon the other three with some superciliousness.", "갈대 추리기로도 이름난 솜씨꾼들이라, 다소 거만한 기색으로 나머지 셋을 둘러보았다."]], [["Putting on their gloves, all set to work in a row in front of the press, an erection formed of two posts connected by a cross-beam, under which the sheaves to be drawn from were laid ears outward, the beam being pegged down by pins in the uprights, and lowered as the sheaves diminished.", "장갑을 끼고, 모두가 압착틀 앞에 한 줄로 늘어서서 일에 착수했다. 그것은 두 기둥을 가로대로 이은 구조물로, 그 아래에 뽑아낼 밀단을 이삭이 바깥으로 향하게 뉘어 놓고, 가로대는 기둥에 꽂은 핀으로 눌러 고정하되 밀단이 줄어드는 대로 낮춰 가는 식이었다."]], [["The day hardened in colour, the light coming in at the barndoors upwards from the snow instead of downwards from the sky.", "날빛은 빛깔이 짙어 갔는데, 빛이 하늘에서 아래로 들지 않고 눈에서 위로 헛간 문으로 들어왔기 때문이다."], ["The girls pulled handful after handful from the press; but by reason of the presence of the strange women, who were recounting scandals, Marian and Izz could not at first talk of old times as they wished to do.", "처녀들은 압착틀에서 한 줌 한 줌 잡아 뽑았다. 그러나 추문을 늘어놓는 낯선 두 여자가 있는 탓에, 메리언과 이즈는 처음엔 바라던 대로 옛이야기를 나눌 수가 없었다."], ["Presently they heard the muffled tread of a horse, and the farmer rode up to the barndoor.", "이윽고 그들은 말발굽의 둔탁한 소리를 들었고, 농장주가 헛간 문 앞까지 말을 타고 왔다."], ["When he had dismounted he came close to Tess, and remained looking musingly at the side of her face.", "그는 말에서 내려 테스에게 바짝 다가와, 골똘히 그녀의 옆얼굴을 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["She had not turned at first, but his fixed attitude led her to look round, when she perceived that her employer was the native of Trantridge from whom she had taken flight on the high-road because of his allusion to her history.", "그녀는 처음엔 돌아보지 않았으나, 그의 붙박인 자세에 끝내 고개를 돌렸고, 그제야 자기 고용주가, 자기 내력을 들먹였던 탓에 그녀가 큰길에서 달아났던 바로 그 트랜트리지 토박이임을 알아챘다."]], [["He waited till she had carried the drawn bundles to the pile outside, when he said, “So you be the young woman who took my civility in such ill part?", "그는 그녀가 뽑아낸 밀단 묶음을 바깥 더미로 날라 둘 때까지 기다렸다가 말했다. \"그래, 네가 내 호의를 그토록 고약하게 받아쳤던 그 젊은 계집이렷다?"], ["Be drowned if I didn’t think you might be as soon as I heard of your being hired!", "네가 고용됐단 말을 듣자마자 그럴지도 모른다 싶었는데, 아니면 내 목을 매달아도 좋아!"], ["Well, you thought you had got the better of me the first time at the inn with your fancy-man, and the second time on the road, when you bolted; but now I think I’ve got the better of you.”", "흥, 넌 첫 번째 여관에서 네 정부랑 같이 있을 때 날 이겼다고 여겼고, 두 번째 길에서 줄행랑쳤을 때도 그랬겠지. 하지만 이번엔 내가 너를 이긴 것 같구나.\""], ["He concluded with a hard laugh.", "그는 차가운 웃음으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["Tess, between the Amazons and the farmer, like a bird caught in a clap-net, returned no answer, continuing to pull the straw.", "테스는 아마존들과 농장주 사이에 끼여, 덮치기 그물에 걸린 새처럼 아무 대꾸도 않고 계속 짚을 뽑았다."], ["She could read character sufficiently well to know by this time that she had nothing to fear from her employer’s gallantry; it was rather the tyranny induced by his mortification at Clare’s treatment of him.", "그녀는 이제 사람 됨됨이를 어지간히 읽을 줄 알아, 자기 고용주의 수작 따위는 두려워할 것 없음을 알았다. 그보다는 클레어가 자기를 대했던 처사에 대한 굴욕에서 비롯된 횡포였다."], ["Upon the whole she preferred that sentiment in man and felt brave enough to endure it.", "통틀어 보면 그녀는 남자에게서 그런 감정을 차라리 더 낫게 여겼고, 그것은 견뎌 낼 만큼 용감하다고 느꼈다."]], [["“You thought I was in love with ’ee I suppose? Some women are such fools, to take every look as serious earnest.", "\"내가 너한테 반했다고 여겼겠지? 어떤 여자들은 어찌나 어리석은지, 눈길 하나하나를 진지한 본심으로 받아들인다니까."], ["But there’s nothing like a winter afield for taking that nonsense out o’ young wenches’ heads; and you’ve signed and agreed till Lady-Day.", "하지만 젊은 계집들 머릿속에서 그런 헛소리를 빼내는 데는 들판에서 겨울나기만 한 게 없지. 게다가 넌 성모영보일까지 서명하고 약조했어."], ["Now, are you going to beg my pardon?”", "자, 이제 내게 용서를 빌 텐가?\""]], [["“I think you ought to beg mine.”", "\"당신이 제게 비셔야 할 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Very well—as you like.", "\"좋다—네 멋대로 해."], ["But we’ll see which is master here.", "하지만 여기서 누가 주인인지 두고 보자."], ["Be they all the sheaves you’ve done to-day?”", "오늘 네가 한 게 이 밀단 전부냐?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“’Tis a very poor show.", "\"형편없는 솜씨로군."], ["Just see what they’ve done over there”", "저쪽 사람들이 한 걸 좀 봐라\""], ["(pointing to the two stalwart women).", "(건장한 두 여자를 가리키며)."], ["“The rest, too, have done better than you.”", "\"나머지도 다 너보다 잘했어.\""]], [["“They’ve all practised it before, and I have not.", "\"저들은 다 전에 해 봤고, 저는 안 해 봤어요."], ["And I thought it made no difference to you as it is task work, and we are only paid for what we do.”", "그리고 이건 도급 일이고 우리는 한 만큼만 삯을 받으니, 당신한테는 차이가 없을 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["“Oh, but it does.", "\"천만에, 차이가 있지."], ["I want the barn cleared.”", "난 헛간을 비워야 한단 말이다.\""]], [["“I am going to work all the afternoon instead of leaving at two as the others will do.”", "\"저는 다른 사람들이 두 시에 떠나는 대신, 오후 내내 일하겠어요.\""]], [["He looked sullenly at her and went away.", "그는 시무룩하게 그녀를 노려보고는 가 버렸다."], ["Tess felt that she could not have come to a much worse place; but anything was better than gallantry.", "테스는 이보다 훨씬 못한 곳에 올 수도 있었으리라 느꼈다. 그래도 무엇이든 수작질보다는 나았다."], ["When two o’clock arrived the professional reed-drawers tossed off the last half-pint in their flagon, put down their hooks, tied their last sheaves, and went away.", "두 시가 되자 직업적인 갈대 추리꾼들은 술병에 남은 마지막 반 파인트를 들이켜고, 갈고리를 내려놓고, 마지막 단을 묶고는 가 버렸다."], ["Marian and Izz would have done likewise, but on hearing that Tess meant to stay, to make up by longer hours for her lack of skill, they would not leave her.", "메리언과 이즈도 그러려 했으나, 테스가 솜씨 부족을 더 긴 시간으로 메우려 남겠다는 말을 듣고는 그녀를 두고 가려 하지 않았다."], ["Looking out at the snow, which still fell, Marian exclaimed, “Now, we’ve got it all to ourselves.”", "여전히 내리는 눈을 내다보며 메리언이 외쳤다. \"자, 이제 우리끼리 다 차지했네.\""], ["And so at last the conversation turned to their old experiences at the dairy; and, of course, the incidents of their affection for Angel Clare.", "그리하여 마침내 이야기는 낙농장에서의 옛 경험으로 돌아갔다. 그리고, 물론, 엔젤 클레어를 향한 그들의 애정에 얽힌 일들로도."]], [["“Izz and Marian,”", "\"이즈, 그리고 메리언.\" 엔젤 클레어 부인이, 그녀가 얼마나 아내답지 못한 처지인지를 생각하면 무척이나 가슴 아픈 위엄을 담아 말했다."], ["said Mrs Angel Clare, with a dignity which was extremely touching, seeing how very little of a wife she was: “I can’t join in talk with you now, as I used to do, about Mr Clare; you will see that I cannot; because, although he is gone away from me for the present, he is my husband.”", "\"이제 난 예전처럼 너희랑 클레어 씨 얘기에 끼어들 수가 없어. 알아주겠지, 그럴 수 없다는 걸. 왜냐하면, 비록 그분이 지금은 내 곁을 떠나 있어도, 그분은 내 남편이니까.\""]], [["Izz was by nature the sauciest and most caustic of all the four girls who had loved Clare.", "이즈는 클레어를 사랑했던 네 처녀 가운데 천성이 가장 맹랑하고 가장 신랄했다."], ["“He was a very splendid lover, no doubt,”", "\"그분이 더없이 멋진 연인이었던 건 틀림없지.\""], ["she said; “but I don’t think he is a too fond husband to go away from you so soon.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 너를 그렇게 빨리 떠나 버릴 만큼, 그분이 너무 다정한 남편은 아닌 것 같은데.\""]], [["“He had to go—he was obliged to go, to see about the land over there!”", "\"가실 수밖에 없었어—저쪽 땅 일을 알아보러, 어쩔 수 없이 가셔야만 했어!\""], ["pleaded Tess.", "테스가 변호했다."]], [["“He might have tided ’ee over the winter.”", "\"겨울 동안은 너를 건사해 줄 수도 있었잖아.\""]], [["“Ah—that’s owing to an accident—a misunderstanding; and we won’t argue it,”", "\"아—그건 어떤 사고 탓이야—오해 때문이지. 그건 따지지 말자.\""], ["Tess answered, with tearfulness in her words.", "테스가 눈물 어린 말로 대답했다."], ["“Perhaps there’s a good deal to be said for him!", "\"어쩌면 그분 편에서도 할 말이 많을 거야!"], ["He did not go away, like some husbands, without telling me; and I can always find out where he is.”", "그분은 어떤 남편들처럼 나한테 말도 없이 떠나진 않았어. 그리고 난 그분이 어디 있는지 언제든 알아낼 수 있어.\""]], [["After this they continued for some long time in a reverie, as they went on seizing the ears of corn, drawing out the straw, gathering it under their arms, and cutting off the ears with their bill-hooks, nothing sounding in the barn but the swish of the straw and the crunch of the hook.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 그들은 오래도록 몽상에 잠긴 채로, 계속 밀 이삭을 거머쥐고, 짚을 뽑아내고, 그것을 팔 아래 그러모으고, 빌훅으로 이삭을 잘라 냈다. 헛간 안에는 짚이 사각거리는 소리와 갈고리가 사각 끊는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 나지 않았다."], ["Then Tess suddenly flagged, and sank down upon the heap of wheat-ears at her feet.", "그러다 테스가 별안간 기운이 빠져, 제 발치의 밀 이삭 더미 위로 풀썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“I knew you wouldn’t be able to stand it!”", "\"네가 못 버틸 줄 알았어!\" 메리언이 소리쳤다."], ["cried Marian. “It wants harder flesh than yours for this work.”", "\"이 일은 너 같은 살로는 안 돼. 더 단단한 살이 있어야 해.\""]], [["Just then the farmer entered.", "바로 그때 농장주가 들어왔다."], ["“Oh, that’s how you get on when I am away,” he said to her.", "\"오, 내가 없으니 이 모양이로군.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“But it is my own loss,”", "\"하지만 이건 제 손해예요.\""], ["she pleaded.", "그녀가 변호했다."], ["“Not yours.”", "\"당신 손해가 아니라.\""]], [["“I want it finished,” he said doggedly, as he crossed the barn and went out at the other door.", "\"난 그걸 끝내 놓고 싶단 말이다.\" 그는 고집스레 말하고는, 헛간을 가로질러 다른 쪽 문으로 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Don’t ’ee mind him, there’s a dear,” said Marian.", "\"저 사람 신경 쓰지 마, 얘야.\" 메리언이 말했다."], ["“I’ve worked here before.", "\"난 전에도 여기서 일해 봤어."], ["Now you go and lie down there, and Izz and I will make up your number.”", "자, 넌 저기 가서 좀 누워 있어. 이즈랑 내가 네 몫을 채워 줄게.\""]], [["“I don’t like to let you do that. I’m taller than you, too.”", "\"너희한테 그러게 하긴 싫어. 게다가 난 너희보다 키도 크잖아.\""]], [["However, she was so overcome that she consented to lie down awhile, and reclined on a heap of pull-tails—the refuse after the straight straw had been drawn—thrown up at the further side of the barn.", "그러나 그녀는 너무 기진한 나머지 잠시 눕는 데 동의했고, 헛간 저쪽 끝에 던져 쌓아 둔 풀테일—곧은 짚을 뽑아낸 뒤 남은 찌끼—더미 위에 몸을 기댔다."], ["Her succumbing had been as largely owning to agitation at the re-opening the subject of her separation from her husband as to the hard work.", "그녀가 쓰러진 것은 고된 일 탓이기도 했지만, 남편과의 이별이라는 화제가 다시 들춰진 데서 온 동요 탓이 그에 못지않게 컸다."], ["She lay in a state of percipience without volition, and the rustle of the straw and the cutting of the ears by the others had the weight of bodily touches.", "그녀는 의지 없이 그저 지각만 하는 상태로 누워 있었고, 다른 이들이 짚을 바스락대고 이삭을 잘라 내는 소리가 마치 몸에 닿는 손길만큼의 무게로 다가왔다."]], [["She could hear from her corner, in addition to these noises, the murmur of their voices.", "그녀는 제 구석에서 그 소리들 말고도 그들의 두런거리는 목소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["She felt certain that they were continuing the subject already broached, but their voices were so low that she could not catch the words.", "그녀는 그들이 이미 꺼낸 화제를 이어 가고 있음을 확신했지만, 목소리가 너무 낮아 말을 알아들을 수가 없었다."], ["At last Tess grew more and more anxious to know what they were saying, and, persuading herself that she felt better, she got up and resumed work.", "마침내 테스는 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지 점점 더 알고 싶어졌고, 이제 좀 나아졌다고 스스로를 다독이며 일어나 다시 일을 시작했다."]], [["Then Izz Huett broke down. She had walked more than a dozen miles the previous evening, had gone to bed at midnight, and had risen again at five o’clock.", "그러자 이번엔 이즈 휴엣이 뻗어 버렸다. 그녀는 전날 저녁 십수 마일을 넘게 걸었고, 자정에야 잠자리에 들었다가 다섯 시에 다시 일어났던 것이다."], ["Marian alone, thanks to her bottle of liquor and her stoutness of build, stood the strain upon back and arms without suffering.", "오로지 메리언만이, 술병과 튼튼한 체구 덕에, 등과 팔에 가해지는 부담을 탈 없이 견뎌 냈다."], ["Tess urged Izz to leave off, agreeing, as she felt better, to finish the day without her, and make equal division of the number of sheaves.", "테스는 이즈에게 그만두라고 권하며, 자기가 좀 나아졌으니 그녀 없이도 하루를 마치겠다고, 그리고 밀단 수를 똑같이 나누겠다고 했다."]], [["Izz accepted the offer gratefully, and disappeared through the great door into the snowy track to her lodging.", "이즈는 고마이 그 제안을 받아들이고, 커다란 문을 지나 눈 덮인 길로 나가 제 숙소로 사라졌다."], ["Marian, as was the case every afternoon at this time on account of the bottle, began to feel in a romantic vein.", "메리언은, 술병 탓에 이맘때 오후면 늘 그렇듯, 낭만적인 기분에 젖기 시작했다."]], [["“I should not have thought it of him—never!”", "\"난 그분이 그럴 줄 정말 몰랐어—절대로!\""], ["she said in a dreamy tone.", "그녀가 꿈결 같은 어조로 말했다."], ["“And I loved him so!", "\"난 그분을 그렇게 사랑했는데!"], ["I didn’t mind his having _you_.", "그분이 너를 가졌던 건 괜찮았어."], ["But this about Izz is too bad!”", "하지만 이즈 일은 너무 심하잖아!\""]], [["Tess, in her start at the words, narrowly missed cutting off a finger with the bill-hook.", "테스는 그 말에 화들짝 놀라, 하마터면 빌훅에 손가락을 자를 뻔했다."]], [["“Is it about my husband?” she stammered.", "\"내 남편 얘기야?\" 그녀가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Well, yes.", "\"음, 그래."], ["Izz said, ‘Don’t ’ee tell her’; but I am sure I can’t help it!", "이즈가 '그 애한텐 말하지 마' 그랬는데, 도저히 못 참겠어!"], ["It was what he wanted Izz to do.", "그분이 이즈한테 시키려던 일이야."], ["He wanted her to go off to Brazil with him.”", "그분이 이즈더러 자기랑 같이 브라질로 떠나자고 했대.\""]], [["Tess’s face faded as white as the scene without, and its curves straightened.", "테스의 얼굴이 바깥 풍경처럼 새하얗게 질리며, 그 곡선이 곧게 펴졌다."], ["“And did Izz refuse to go?”", "\"그래서 이즈가 안 가겠다고 했어?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know. Anyhow he changed his mind.”", "\"몰라. 어쨌든 그분이 마음을 바꿨대.\""]], [["“Pooh—then he didn’t mean it!", "\"흥—그럼 진심이 아니었던 거지!"], ["’Twas just a man’s jest!”", "그냥 남자의 농담이었어!\""]], [["“Yes he did; for he drove her a good-ways towards the station.”", "\"아냐, 진심이었어. 정거장 쪽으로 한참이나 데려다줬다니까.\""]], [["“He didn’t take her!”", "\"데려가진 않았잖아!\""]], [["They pulled on in silence till Tess, without any premonitory symptoms, burst out crying.", "그들은 말없이 계속 짚을 뽑았고, 그러다 테스가 아무런 전조도 없이 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“There!” said Marian.", "\"이것 봐!\""], ["“Now I wish I hadn’t told ’ee!”", "메리언이 말했다. \"괜히 말했나 봐!\""]], [["“No. It is a very good thing that you have done!", "\"아냐. 네가 말해 준 건 정말 잘한 일이야!"], ["I have been living on in a thirtover, lackaday way, and have not seen what it may lead to!", "난 여태 갈팡질팡 맥없이 살아오면서, 이게 어디로 흘러갈지 보지 못했어!"], ["I ought to have sent him a letter oftener.", "그분한테 더 자주 편지를 써 보냈어야 했는데."], ["He said I could not go to him, but he didn’t say I was not to write as often as I liked.", "그분은 내가 자기한테 갈 순 없다고 했지, 내가 쓰고 싶은 만큼 자주 편지 쓰면 안 된다고는 안 했어."], ["I won’t dally like this any longer!", "더는 이렇게 어물대지 않을 거야!"], ["I have been very wrong and neglectful in leaving everything to be done by him!”", "모든 걸 그분 손에 맡겨 두다니, 난 정말 잘못했고 너무 게을렀어!\""]], [["The dim light in the barn grew dimmer, and they could see to work no longer.", "헛간 안의 어슴푸레한 빛이 점점 더 어두워져, 더는 일할 만큼 보이지가 않았다."], ["When Tess had reached home that evening, and had entered into the privacy of her little white-washed chamber, she began impetuously writing a letter to Clare.", "그날 저녁 테스는 집에 다다라, 회칠한 작은 제 방의 호젓함 속에 들어서자, 충동적으로 클레어에게 편지를 쓰기 시작했다."], ["But falling into doubt, she could not finish it.", "그러나 의심에 빠져, 끝맺지를 못했다."], ["Afterwards she took the ring from the ribbon on which she wore it next her heart, and retained it on her finger all night, as if to fortify herself in the sensation that she was really the wife of this elusive lover of hers, who could propose that Izz should go with him abroad, so shortly after he had left her.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 심장께에 지니고 다니던 리본에서 반지를 빼내, 밤새 손가락에 끼고 있었다. 마치 자기가 정말로 이 붙잡을 길 없는 연인의 아내라는 느낌으로 스스로를 다잡으려는 듯이. 자기를 떠난 지 얼마 되지도 않아 이즈더러 함께 외국으로 가자고 제안할 수 있는 그 연인의."], ["Knowing that, how could she write entreaties to him, or show that she cared for him any more?", "그것을 알면서, 어떻게 그에게 애원의 편지를 쓰거나, 자기가 아직도 그를 마음에 두고 있음을 드러낼 수 있겠는가?"]], [["# XLIV", "# XLIV"]], [["By the disclosure in the barn her thoughts were led anew in the direction which they had taken more than once of late—to the distant Emminster Vicarage.", "헛간에서의 그 폭로로 인해 그녀의 생각은 요사이 한두 번 향했던 그 방향으로—멀리 떨어진 에민스터 목사관으로—새로이 이끌렸다."], ["It was through her husband’s parents that she had been charged to send a letter to Clare if she desired; and to write to them direct if in difficulty.", "그녀가 바라기만 한다면 클레어에게 편지를 보내고, 어려운 일이 있으면 그들에게 직접 편지하라고 일러둔 것이 바로 남편의 부모를 통해서였다."], ["But that sense of her having morally no claim upon him had always led Tess to suspend her impulse to send these notes; and to the family at the Vicarage, therefore, as to her own parents since her marriage, she was virtually non-existent.", "그러나 자기가 도덕적으로 그에게 아무런 권리도 없다는 그 의식이, 이런 쪽지를 보내려는 충동을 늘 거두게 했다. 그래서 목사관의 가족에게도, 결혼 이후 친정 부모에게 그랬듯, 그녀는 사실상 존재하지 않는 사람이었다."], ["This self-effacement in both directions had been quite in consonance with her independent character of desiring nothing by way of favour or pity to which she was not entitled on a fair consideration of her deserts.", "양쪽 모두를 향한 이 자기 지움은, 제 분수를 공정히 따져 마땅히 받을 자격이 없는 호의나 동정 따위는 아무것도 바라지 않으려는 그녀의 독립적인 성품과 자못 잘 들어맞았다."], ["She had set herself to stand or fall by her qualities, and to waive such merely technical claims upon a strange family as had been established for her by the flimsy fact of a member of that family, in a season of impulse, writing his name in a church-book beside hers.", "그녀는 제 자질로 서거나 무너지기로 마음먹었고, 한순간의 충동에 휩쓸린 그 가문의 한 사람이 교회 장부에 제 이름 옆에 그녀의 이름을 적었다는 하찮은 사실로 그녀를 위해 세워진, 그저 형식뿐인 그런 권리들은 내려놓기로 한 것이다."]], [["But now that she was stung to a fever by Izz’s tale, there was a limit to her powers of renunciation.", "그러나 이제 이즈의 이야기에 찔려 열병에 들뜬 마당이라, 그녀의 단념하는 힘에도 한계가 있었다."], ["Why had her husband not written to her?", "어째서 남편은 그녀에게 편지하지 않았을까?"], ["He had distinctly implied that he would at least let her know of the locality to which he had journeyed; but he had not sent a line to notify his address.", "그는 적어도 자기가 떠난 곳의 지명만은 알려 주겠다고 분명히 내비쳤다. 그런데 제 주소를 알리는 한 줄도 보내지 않았다."], ["Was he really indifferent?", "그는 정말로 무심한 걸까?"], ["But was he ill? Was it for her to make some advance?", "아니면 병이 난 걸까? 자기가 먼저 다가서야 하는 걸까?"], ["Surely she might summon the courage of solicitude, call at the Vicarage for intelligence, and express her grief at his silence.", "분명 그녀는 염려하는 마음의 용기를 그러모아, 소식을 구하러 목사관을 찾아가, 그의 침묵에 대한 제 슬픔을 토로할 수 있으리라."], ["If Angel’s father were the good man she had heard him represented to be, he would be able to enter into her heart-starved situation.", "엔젤의 아버지가 사람들이 말하던 대로 선한 분이라면, 그녀의 애정에 굶주린 처지를 헤아려 줄 수 있으리라."], ["Her social hardships she could conceal.", "사회적인 고초야 그녀가 감출 수 있었다."]], [["To leave the farm on a week-day was not in her power; Sunday was the only possible opportunity.", "평일에 농장을 떠나기란 그녀의 힘으로 될 일이 아니었다. 일요일만이 유일하게 가능한 기회였다."], ["Flintcomb-Ash being in the middle of the cretaceous tableland over which no railway had climbed as yet, it would be necessary to walk.", "플린트콤애시가 아직 어떤 철도도 오르지 못한 백악질 대지 한복판에 있는 터라, 걸어가는 수밖에 없었다."], ["And the distance being fifteen miles each way she would have to allow herself a long day for the undertaking by rising early.", "그리고 거리가 한쪽으로만 십오 마일이니, 일찍 일어나 그 일에 하루를 길게 잡아야 했다."]], [["A fortnight later, when the snow had gone, and had been followed by a hard black frost, she took advantage of the state of the roads to try the experiment.", "보름 뒤, 눈이 사라지고 그 뒤로 매서운 검은 서리가 든 무렵, 그녀는 길 상태를 틈타 그 시도를 해 보기로 했다."], ["At four o’clock that Sunday morning she came downstairs and stepped out into the starlight.", "그 일요일 새벽 네 시에 그녀는 아래층으로 내려와 별빛 속으로 나섰다."], ["The weather was still favourable, the ground ringing under her feet like an anvil.", "날씨는 여전히 좋았고, 땅은 그녀의 발밑에서 모루처럼 쟁쟁 울렸다."]], [["Marian and Izz were much interested in her excursion, knowing that the journey concerned her husband.", "메리언과 이즈는 그 여정이 그녀의 남편과 관계된 일임을 알기에 그녀의 나들이에 무척 관심이 많았다."], ["Their lodgings were in a cottage a little further along the lane, but they came and assisted Tess in her departure, and argued that she should dress up in her very prettiest guise to captivate the hearts of her parents-in-law; though she, knowing of the austere and Calvinistic tenets of old Mr Clare, was indifferent, and even doubtful.", "그들의 숙소는 길을 따라 조금 더 간 오두막에 있었지만, 그들은 와서 테스의 출발을 거들어 주며, 시부모의 마음을 사로잡으려면 가장 어여쁜 차림으로 단장해야 한다고 주장했다. 그러나 그녀는 늙은 클레어 씨의 엄격한 칼뱅주의 신조를 아는 터라, 그런 데 무심했고, 심지어 미심쩍어하기까지 했다."], ["A year had now elapsed since her sad marriage, but she had preserved sufficient draperies from the wreck of her then full wardrobe to clothe her very charmingly as a simple country girl with no pretensions to recent fashion; a soft gray woollen gown, with white crape quilling against the pink skin of her face and neck, and a black velvet jacket and hat.", "그 서글픈 결혼으로부터 이제 한 해가 흘렀지만, 그녀는 그때 가득했던 옷장의 잔해에서 충분한 옷가지를 간직해 두어, 최근 유행 따위는 내세우지 않는 수수한 시골 처녀로서 무척 사랑스럽게 차려입을 수 있었다. 부드러운 잿빛 모직 가운에, 얼굴과 목의 분홍빛 살갗에 닿는 흰 크레이프 주름 장식, 그리고 검은 벨벳 재킷과 모자였다."]], [["“’Tis a thousand pities your husband can’t see ’ee now—you do look a real beauty!”", "\"네 남편이 지금 너를 볼 수 없다니 천만 번 애석한 일이야—너 정말 미인으로 보여!\""], ["said Izz Huett, regarding Tess as she stood on the threshold between the steely starlight without and the yellow candlelight within.", "이즈 휴엣이, 바깥의 강철빛 별빛과 안의 노란 촛불빛 사이 문지방에 선 테스를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Izz spoke with a magnanimous abandonment of herself to the situation; she could not be—no woman with a heart bigger than a hazel-nut could be—antagonistic to Tess in her presence, the influence which she exercised over those of her own sex being of a warmth and strength quite unusual, curiously overpowering the less worthy feminine feelings of spite and rivalry.", "이즈는 그 처지에 자신을 너그러이 내맡긴 채 말한 것이었다. 그녀로서는—개암 한 알보다 큰 마음을 가진 여자라면 누구라도—테스가 곁에 있는데 그녀에게 적의를 품을 수는 없었으니, 테스가 제 동성들에게 미치는 영향력은 자못 보기 드문 따스함과 강함을 지녀, 시샘이니 경쟁이니 하는 더 못난 여성적 감정들을 묘하게 압도해 버렸다."]], [["With a final tug and touch here, and a slight brush there, they let her go; and she was absorbed into the pearly air of the fore-dawn.", "여기는 마지막으로 한 번 잡아당기고 매만지고, 저기는 살짝 솔질해 주고 나서, 그들은 그녀를 떠나보냈다. 그리고 그녀는 동트기 전 진줏빛 대기 속으로 빨려 들어갔다."], ["They heard her footsteps tap along the hard road as she stepped out to her full pace.", "그녀가 성큼성큼 제 걸음을 내디디며 단단한 길을 따라 또각또각 멀어지는 발소리를 그들은 들었다."], ["Even Izz hoped she would win, and, though without any particular respect for her own virtue, felt glad that she had been prevented wronging her friend when momentarily tempted by Clare.", "이즈조차 그녀가 이기기를 바랐고, 제 정조에 딱히 자부심이 있는 것도 아니었으나, 한순간 클레어에게 끌렸을 때 제 벗에게 못할 짓을 하려던 것이 막혔던 게 다행이라고 느꼈다."]], [["It was a year ago, all but a day, that Clare had married Tess, and only a few days less than a year that he had been absent from her.", "클레어가 테스와 결혼한 지 하루를 뺀 꼭 한 해 전이었고, 그가 그녀 곁을 떠나 있은 지도 한 해에서 며칠 모자랄 뿐이었다."], ["Still, to start on a brisk walk, and on such an errand as hers, on a dry clear wintry morning, through the rarefied air of these chalky hogs’-backs, was not depressing; and there is no doubt that her dream at starting was to win the heart of her mother-in-law, tell her whole history to that lady, enlist her on her side, and so gain back the truant.", "그래도, 메마르고 맑은 겨울 아침에, 이 백악질 등성이들의 희박한 공기를 가르며, 그녀 같은 그런 심부름을 안고 활기차게 걷기 시작하는 것은 우울한 일이 아니었다. 그리고 출발할 때 그녀의 꿈이, 시어머니의 마음을 얻어 그 부인에게 제 온 내력을 털어놓고, 그녀를 제 편으로 끌어들여, 그리하여 달아난 남편을 되찾는 것이었음은 의심할 여지가 없다."]], [["In time she reached the edge of the vast escarpment below which stretched the loamy Vale of Blackmoor, now lying misty and still in the dawn.", "이윽고 그녀는, 그 아래로 흙기 많은 블랙무어 골짜기가 뻗어 있는 거대한 절벽 가장자리에 다다랐다. 골짜기는 이제 새벽녘 안개에 잠겨 고요히 누워 있었다."], ["Instead of the colourless air of the uplands, the atmosphere down there was a deep blue.", "고지의 빛깔 없는 공기 대신, 저 아래 대기는 짙은 푸른빛이었다."], ["Instead of the great enclosures of a hundred acres in which she was now accustomed to toil, there were little fields below her of less than half-a-dozen acres, so numerous that they looked from this height like the meshes of a net.", "그녀가 지금 일하는 데 익숙해진 백 에이커짜리 너른 울 대신, 그녀 아래에는 채 여섯 에이커도 안 되는 작은 밭들이 어찌나 많은지, 이 높이에서 보면 그물코처럼 보였다."], ["Here the landscape was whitey-brown; down there, as in Froom Valley, it was always green.", "여기 위쪽 풍경은 희끄무레한 갈색이었고, 저 아래는 프룸 골짜기처럼 늘 푸르렀다."], ["Yet it was in that vale that her sorrow had taken shape, and she did not love it as formerly.", "그러나 그녀의 슬픔이 모양을 갖춘 곳이 바로 저 골짜기였고, 그래서 그녀는 예전만큼 그곳을 사랑하지 않았다."], ["Beauty to her, as to all who have felt, lay not in the thing, but in what the thing symbolized.", "무릇 느낄 줄 아는 모든 이에게 그렇듯, 그녀에게도 아름다움은 사물 그 자체가 아니라 그 사물이 상징하는 것에 있었다."]], [["Keeping the Vale on her right, she steered steadily westward; passing above the Hintocks, crossing at right-angles the high-road from Sherton-Abbas to Casterbridge, and skirting Dogbury Hill and High-Stoy, with the dell between them called “The Devil’s Kitchen”.", "골짜기를 오른편에 둔 채 그녀는 한결같이 서쪽으로 방향을 잡았다. 힌톡 마을들 위를 지나고, 셔턴애버스에서 캐스터브리지로 가는 큰길과 직각으로 가로지르며, 도그베리 언덕과 하이스토이 가장자리를 끼고 돌았는데, 그 사이의 골짜기는 \"악마의 부엌\"이라 불렸다."], ["Still following the elevated way she reached Cross-in-Hand, where the stone pillar stands desolate and silent, to mark the site of a miracle, or murder, or both.", "여전히 그 높은 길을 따라가 그녀는 크로스인핸드에 다다랐고, 거기엔 돌기둥이 황량하고 말없이 서서, 기적이, 혹은 살인이, 아니면 둘 다가 일어난 자리를 표시하고 있었다."], ["Three miles further she cut across the straight and deserted Roman road called Long-Ash Lane; leaving which as soon as she reached it she dipped down a hill by a transverse lane into the small town or village of Evershead, being now about halfway over the distance.", "거기서 삼 마일 더 가, 그녀는 롱애시 길이라 불리는 곧고 인적 없는 로마 가도를 가로질렀고, 그 길에 닿자마자 곧장 거기를 벗어나 가로지르는 샛길로 언덕을 내려가 에버셰드라는 작은 읍, 아니 마을로 접어들었으니, 이제 거리의 절반쯤을 온 셈이었다."], ["She made a halt here, and breakfasted a second time, heartily enough—not at the Sow-and-Acorn, for she avoided inns, but at a cottage by the church.", "그녀는 여기서 한 번 멈춰, 자못 든든하게 두 번째 아침을 먹었다—여관은 피하는 터라 소앤에이콘이 아니라, 교회 옆 한 오두막에서였다."]], [["The second half of her journey was through a more gentle country, by way of Benvill Lane.", "여정의 후반부는 벤빌 길을 거쳐 가는, 한결 부드러운 고장이었다."], ["But as the mileage lessened between her and the spot of her pilgrimage, so did Tess’s confidence decrease, and her enterprise loom out more formidably.", "그러나 자기와 순례의 목적지 사이의 거리가 줄어들수록, 테스의 자신감도 줄어들었고, 그녀의 일은 더 위압적으로 떠올랐다."], ["She saw her purpose in such staring lines, and the landscape so faintly, that she was sometimes in danger of losing her way.", "그녀의 목적은 그토록 또렷한 선으로 보이는데 풍경은 그토록 흐릿이 보여, 그녀는 이따금 길을 잃을 뻔하기도 했다."], ["However, about noon she paused by a gate on the edge of the basin in which Emminster and its Vicarage lay.", "그래도 정오 무렵 그녀는 에민스터와 그 목사관이 자리한 분지 가장자리의 한 대문 옆에서 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["The square tower, beneath which she knew that at that moment the Vicar and his congregation were gathered, had a severe look in her eyes.", "지금 이 순간 목사와 그의 교인들이 그 아래 모여 있음을 그녀가 아는 그 네모진 탑은, 그녀의 눈에 준엄해 보였다."], ["She wished that she had somehow contrived to come on a week-day.", "그녀는 어떻게든 평일에 올 도리를 마련했더라면 싶었다."], ["Such a good man might be prejudiced against a woman who had chosen Sunday, never realizing the necessities of her case. But it was incumbent upon her to go on now.", "그런 선한 분이라도 일요일을 골라 온 여자에 대해서는, 그녀의 사정이 어쩔 수 없었음은 도무지 헤아리지 못한 채, 편견을 품을지도 모를 일이었다."], ["She took off the thick boots in which she had walked thus far, put on her pretty thin ones of patent leather, and, stuffing the former into the hedge by the gatepost where she might readily find them again, descended the hill; the freshness of colour she had derived from the keen air thinning away in spite of her as she drew near the parsonage.", "그러나 이제는 계속 나아가는 것이 그녀의 의무였다. 그녀는 여기까지 걸어온 그 두꺼운 장화를 벗고, 어여쁜 에나멜가죽 얇은 신으로 갈아 신고는, 벗은 장화를 나중에 쉬이 찾을 수 있도록 대문 기둥 옆 산울타리 속에 쑤셔 넣고 언덕을 내려갔다. 매서운 공기에서 얻은 화사한 혈색은, 목사관에 가까워질수록 그녀의 뜻과는 달리 옅어져 갔다."]], [["Tess hoped for some accident that might favour her, but nothing favoured her.", "테스는 자기에게 유리하게 작용할 무슨 우연이라도 있기를 바랐으나, 아무것도 그녀에게 유리하지 않았다. 목사관 잔디밭의 관목들이 서리 어린 산들바람에 거북하게 바스락거렸다."], ["The shrubs on the Vicarage lawn rustled uncomfortably in the frosty breeze; she could not feel by any stretch of imagination, dressed to her highest as she was, that the house was the residence of near relations; and yet nothing essential, in nature or emotion, divided her from them: in pains, pleasures, thoughts, birth, death, and after-death, they were the same.", "그녀는 제아무리 한껏 차려입었어도, 아무리 상상력을 펼쳐 봐도, 그 집이 가까운 친척의 거처라고는 도무지 느껴지지 않았다. 그런데도 본질적으로는, 본성에서나 감정에서나, 그녀를 그들에게서 갈라놓는 것이 아무것도 없었다. 고통에서도, 기쁨에서도, 생각에서도, 태어남에서도, 죽음에서도, 죽음 이후에서도, 그들은 한가지였다."]], [["She nerved herself by an effort, entered the swing-gate, and rang the door-bell.", "그녀는 안간힘을 써서 마음을 다잡고, 회전 대문 안으로 들어가 초인종을 울렸다."], ["The thing was done; there could be no retreat.", "일은 저질러졌고, 물러설 길은 없었다."], ["No; the thing was not done.", "아니, 일은 저질러지지 않았다."], ["Nobody answered to her ringing.", "그녀가 울린 종에 아무도 응답하지 않았다."], ["The effort had to be risen to and made again.", "안간힘은 다시 한번 그러모아 짜내야 했다."], ["She rang a second time, and the agitation of the act, coupled with her weariness after the fifteen miles’ walk, led her to support herself while she waited by resting her hand on her hip and her elbow against the wall of the porch.", "그녀는 두 번째로 종을 울렸고, 그 행동의 동요에다 십오 마일을 걸은 뒤의 피로까지 겹쳐, 기다리는 동안 한 손을 엉덩이에 짚고 팔꿈치를 현관 벽에 댄 채 몸을 지탱했다."], ["The wind was so nipping that the ivy-leaves had become wizened and gray, each tapping incessantly upon its neighbour with a disquieting stir of her nerves.", "바람이 어찌나 살을 에는지 담쟁이 잎들이 쪼그라들어 잿빛으로 변했고, 저마다 쉴 새 없이 옆 잎을 톡톡 두드려 그녀의 신경을 불안스레 건드렸다."], ["A piece of blood-stained paper, caught up from some meat-buyer’s dust-heap, beat up and down the road without the gate; too flimsy to rest, too heavy to fly away; and a few straws kept it company.", "어느 고기 사는 사람의 쓰레기 더미에서 날려 온 피 묻은 종잇조각 하나가 대문 밖 길에서 오르내리며 펄럭였다. 가라앉기엔 너무 가볍고 날아가기엔 너무 무거운 그것을, 지푸라기 몇 가닥이 벗 삼아 따라다녔다."]], [["The second peal had been louder, and still nobody came.", "두 번째 종소리는 더 컸지만, 여전히 아무도 오지 않았다."], ["Then she walked out of the porch, opened the gate, and passed through.", "그러자 그녀는 현관에서 걸어 나와, 대문을 열고 빠져나왔다."], ["And though she looked dubiously at the house-front as if inclined to return, it was with a breath of relief that she closed the gate. A feeling haunted her that she might have been recognized (though how she could not tell), and orders been given not to admit her.", "그리고 마치 되돌아갈까 망설이듯 집의 정면을 미심쩍게 올려다보면서도, 안도의 숨을 내쉬며 대문을 닫았다. 자기가 알아보였을지도 모른다는(어떻게 그랬을지는 알 수 없었지만) 느낌, 그리고 자기를 들이지 말라는 분부가 내려졌을지도 모른다는 느낌이 그녀를 떠나지 않았다."]], [["Tess went as far as the corner.", "테스는 모퉁이까지 갔다."], ["She had done all she could do; but determined not to escape present trepidation at the expense of future distress, she walked back again quite past the house, looking up at all the windows.", "그녀는 자기가 할 수 있는 건 다 했다. 그러나 지금의 떨림을 모면하려다 훗날의 괴로움을 치르지는 않겠다고 마음먹고, 그녀는 다시 걸음을 돌려 집을 한참 지나치도록 되짚어 가며, 창이란 창은 다 올려다보았다."]], [["Ah—the explanation was that they were all at church, every one. She remembered her husband saying that his father always insisted upon the household, servants included, going to morning-service, and, as a consequence, eating cold food when they came home.", "아—설명인즉슨, 그들이 모두, 하나도 빠짐없이 교회에 가 있다는 것이었다. 그녀는 남편이, 자기 아버지는 늘 하인들까지 포함해 온 식구가 아침 예배에 가기를 고집했고, 그 결과 집에 오면 찬 음식을 먹는다고 했던 말을 떠올렸다."], ["It was, therefore, only necessary to wait till the service was over.", "그러니, 예배가 끝날 때까지 기다리기만 하면 될 일이었다."], ["She would not make herself conspicuous by waiting on the spot, and she started to get past the church into the lane.", "그녀는 그 자리에서 기다리며 사람들 눈에 띄고 싶지 않아, 교회를 지나 샛길로 빠지려고 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["But as she reached the churchyard-gate the people began pouring out, and Tess found herself in the midst of them.", "그러나 교회 묘지 문에 다다랐을 때 사람들이 쏟아져 나오기 시작했고, 테스는 그들 한가운데에 끼고 말았다."]], [["The Emminster congregation looked at her as only a congregation of small country-townsfolk walking home at its leisure can look at a woman out of the common whom it perceives to be a stranger.", "에민스터의 교인들은 그녀를, 한가로이 집으로 걸어가는 자그마한 시골 읍내 사람들의 무리가, 제 눈에 낯선 이로 비치는 평범치 않은 여자를 쳐다볼 수 있는 딱 그만큼으로 쳐다보았다."], ["She quickened her pace, and ascended the road by which she had come, to find a retreat between its hedges till the Vicar’s family should have lunched, and it might be convenient for them to receive her.", "그녀는 걸음을 재촉해, 자기가 왔던 길을 거슬러 올라갔다. 목사의 가족이 점심을 들고 나서 그녀를 맞이하기 편할 때까지 산울타리 사이에서 몸을 피해 있을 작정이었다."], ["She soon distanced the churchgoers, except two youngish men, who, linked arm-in-arm, were beating up behind her at a quick step.", "그녀는 곧 교회에 가던 이들을 멀찍이 따돌렸으나, 팔짱을 낀 채 빠른 걸음으로 그녀 뒤를 바짝 쫓아오는 두 젊은 사내만은 예외였다."]], [["As they drew nearer she could hear their voices engaged in earnest discourse, and, with the natural quickness of a woman in her situation, did not fail to recognize in those noises the quality of her husband’s tones.", "그들이 가까워지자 그녀는 그들의 목소리가 진지한 이야기에 몰두해 있음을 들을 수 있었고, 그런 처지의 여자다운 타고난 민첩함으로, 그 소리에서 제 남편의 음색과 같은 결을 알아채지 못할 리 없었다."], ["The pedestrians were his two brothers.", "그 보행자들은 그의 두 형제였다."], ["Forgetting all her plans, Tess’s one dread was lest they should overtake her now, in her disorganized condition, before she was prepared to confront them; for though she felt that they could not identify her, she instinctively dreaded their scrutiny.", "모든 계획을 잊은 채, 테스의 단 하나의 두려움은, 그들과 마주할 채비도 되기 전에, 이 흐트러진 몰골로 그들이 지금 자기를 따라잡을까 하는 것이었다. 그들이 자기를 알아볼 리는 없다고 느끼면서도, 그녀는 본능적으로 그들의 살핌을 두려워했다."], ["The more briskly they walked, the more briskly walked she.", "그들이 빨리 걸을수록, 그녀도 빨리 걸었다."], ["They were plainly bent upon taking a short quick stroll before going indoors to lunch or dinner, to restore warmth to limbs chilled with sitting through a long service.", "그들은 점심이나 저녁을 들러 안으로 들어가기 전에, 긴 예배 내내 앉아 있느라 시린 팔다리에 온기를 되돌리려고, 분명히 잠깐 빠르게 거니는 산책에 나선 참이었다."]], [["Only one person had preceded Tess up the hill—a ladylike young woman, somewhat interesting, though, perhaps, a trifle _guindée_ and prudish.", "테스보다 앞서 언덕을 오른 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었다—제법 흥미로운, 그러나 어쩌면 조금은 새침하고 점잔 빼는 듯한 숙녀풍의 젊은 여자였다."], ["Tess had nearly overtaken her when the speed of her brothers-in-law brought them so nearly behind her back that she could hear every word of their conversation.", "테스가 거의 그녀를 따라잡았을 무렵, 시형제들의 걸음이 빨라져 그들이 그녀의 등 바로 뒤까지 다가오는 바람에, 그녀는 그들의 대화를 한마디도 빠짐없이 들을 수 있었다."], ["They said nothing, however, which particularly interested her till, observing the young lady still further in front, one of them remarked, “There is Mercy Chant. Let us overtake her.”", "그러나 그들은 저 앞쪽 한층 더 멀리 있는 그 젊은 숙녀를 보고 한 사람이 \"저기 머시 챈트가 있네. 따라잡자\"라고 말하기 전까지는, 그녀의 흥미를 특별히 끄는 말은 아무것도 하지 않았다."]], [["Tess knew the name. It was the woman who had been destined for Angel’s life-companion by his and her parents, and whom he probably would have married but for her intrusive self.", "테스는 그 이름을 알았다. 그 여자는 엔젤의 부모와 그녀의 부모가 엔젤의 평생 반려로 점지해 두었던, 그리고 끼어든 그녀 자신만 아니었다면 그가 필시 결혼했을 그 여자였다."], ["She would have known as much without previous information if she had waited a moment, for one of the brothers proceeded to say: “Ah!", "미리 들은 바가 없었더라도 그녀는 잠깐만 기다렸으면 그만큼은 알았으리라. 형제 중 하나가 이어서 이렇게 말했으니까. \"아!"], ["poor Angel, poor Angel!", "가엾은 엔젤, 가엾은 엔젤!"], ["I never see that nice girl without more and more regretting his precipitancy in throwing himself away upon a dairymaid, or whatever she may be.", "저 참한 아가씨를 볼 때마다, 낙농장 처녀인지 뭔지에게 제 몸을 던져 버린 그애의 경솔함이 점점 더 한스러워. 보아하니 묘한 일이야."], ["It is a queer business, apparently. Whether she has joined him yet or not I don’t know; but she had not done so some months ago when I heard from him.”", "그애한테 그 여자가 합류했는지 어떤지 난 모르겠어. 하지만 몇 달 전 그애한테서 소식을 들었을 땐 아직 안 그랬더라.\""]], [["“I can’t say.", "\"나도 모르지."], ["He never tells me anything nowadays.", "그앤 요즘 나한테 아무것도 얘기를 안 해."], ["His ill-considered marriage seems to have completed that estrangement from me which was begun by his extraordinary opinions.”", "그애의 그 경솔한 결혼이, 그애의 그 별난 견해로 비롯된 나와의 그 소원함을 아주 완성해 버린 것 같아.\""]], [["Tess beat up the long hill still faster; but she could not outwalk them without exciting notice.", "테스는 그 긴 언덕을 더욱 빠르게 치고 올라갔다. 그러나 눈에 띄지 않고서는 그들을 앞질러 걸을 수가 없었다."], ["At last they outsped her altogether, and passed her by.", "마침내 그들은 그녀를 아주 앞질러, 그녀 곁을 지나쳐 갔다."], ["The young lady still further ahead heard their footsteps and turned.", "한층 더 앞서 있던 젊은 숙녀가 그들의 발소리를 듣고 돌아섰다."], ["Then there was a greeting and a shaking of hands, and the three went on together.", "그러자 인사가 오가고 악수가 오갔으며, 셋은 함께 걸어갔다."]], [["They soon reached the summit of the hill, and, evidently intending this point to be the limit of their promenade, slackened pace and turned all three aside to the gate whereat Tess had paused an hour before that time to reconnoitre the town before descending into it.", "그들은 곧 언덕 꼭대기에 다다랐고, 분명히 이 지점을 산책의 끝으로 삼을 작정인 듯 걸음을 늦추더니, 셋 다 한쪽으로 비켜 한 대문 쪽으로 갔으니, 바로 그 한 시간 전 테스가 읍내로 내려가기에 앞서 그곳을 둘러보려 멈춰 섰던 그 대문이었다."], ["During their discourse one of the clerical brothers probed the hedge carefully with his umbrella, and dragged something to light.", "이야기를 나누는 동안 성직자 형제 중 하나가 우산으로 산울타리를 조심스레 쑤시더니 무언가를 끄집어내 밝은 데로 끌어냈다."]], [["“Here’s a pair of old boots,”", "\"여기 헌 장화 한 켤레가 있네.\""], ["he said. “Thrown away, I suppose, by some tramp or other.”", "그가 말했다. \"어느 떠돌이쯤 되는 자가 버린 거겠지.\""]], [["“Some imposter who wished to come into the town barefoot, perhaps, and so excite our sympathies,”", "\"어쩌면 맨발로 읍내에 들어와 우리 동정을 사 보려던 어느 사기꾼이겠죠.\""], ["said Miss Chant.", "챈트 양이 말했다."], ["“Yes, it must have been, for they are excellent walking-boots—by no means worn out.", "\"맞아요, 분명 그럴 거예요. 멀쩡한 걷기 좋은 장화인걸요—조금도 닳지 않았어요."], ["What a wicked thing to do!", "어쩜 그리 못된 짓을!"], ["I’ll carry them home for some poor person.”", "제가 가난한 누군가에게 주려고 집으로 가져가야겠어요.\""]], [["Cuthbert Clare, who had been the one to find them, picked them up for her with the crook of his stick; and Tess’s boots were appropriated.", "장화를 찾아낸 사람은 커스버트 클레어였는데, 그가 지팡이 갈고리로 그것을 집어 그녀에게 건네주었다. 그리하여 테스의 장화는 임자가 바뀌고 말았다."], ["She, who had heard this, walked past under the screen of her woollen veil till, presently looking back, she perceived that the church party had left the gate with her boots and retreated down the hill.", "이 말을 들은 그녀는 제 모직 베일의 가림막 아래로 그 곁을 지나쳐 걸었고, 이윽고 돌아보니 교회에서 온 그 무리가 그녀의 장화를 들고 대문을 떠나 언덕 아래로 물러간 것이 보였다."]], [["Thereupon our heroine resumed her walk.", "그리하여 우리의 여주인공은 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["Tears, blinding tears, were running down her face.", "눈물이, 앞을 가리는 눈물이 그녀의 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["She knew that it was all sentiment, all baseless impressibility, which had caused her to read the scene as her own condemnation; nevertheless she could not get over it; she could not contravene in her own defenceless person all those untoward omens.", "이 모든 광경을 제 자신에 대한 단죄로 읽게 만든 것이 죄다 감상이요, 아무 근거 없는 예민함임을 그녀는 알았다. 그런데도 그녀는 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 무방비한 제 몸뚱이 하나로 그 모든 불길한 징조들을 거스를 수가 없었다."], ["It was impossible to think of returning to the Vicarage.", "목사관으로 되돌아간다는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었다."], ["Angel’s wife felt almost as if she had been hounded up that hill like a scorned thing by those—to her—superfine clerics.", "엔젤의 아내는, 그 ―그녀에게는― 지나치게 점잖은 성직자들에게 멸시받는 무엇처럼 그 언덕으로 쫓겨 올라간 듯한 기분마저 들었다."], ["Innocently as the slight had been inflicted, it was somewhat unfortunate that she had encountered the sons and not the father, who, despite his narrowness, was far less starched and ironed than they, and had to the full the gift of charity.", "그 모욕이 아무리 무심코 가해진 것이라 해도, 아버지가 아니라 아들들과 마주친 것은 다소 불운한 일이었다. 아버지는 비록 옹졸한 데가 있긴 해도 그들보다 훨씬 덜 빳빳하고 덜 깐깐했으며, 자비라는 천품을 온전히 지닌 분이었으니까."], ["As she again thought of her dusty boots she almost pitied those habiliments for the quizzing to which they had been subjected, and felt how hopeless life was for their owner.", "먼지투성이 제 장화를 다시 떠올리자, 그녀는 그것이 당한 조롱이 가여워, 그 신발마저 거의 측은해졌고, 그 임자에게 삶이 얼마나 가망 없는 것인지를 절감했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["she said, still sighing in pity of herself, “_they_ didn’t know that I wore those over the roughest part of the road to save these pretty ones _he_ bought for me—no—they did not know it!", "그녀가 여전히 제 신세를 가여워하며 한숨지었다. \"저 사람들은 몰랐던 거야, 그분이 사 준 이 어여쁜 신을 아끼려고 제일 험한 길에선 그 헌 장화를 신었다는 걸—아니야—저 사람들은 그걸 몰랐어!"], ["And they didn’t think that _he_ chose the colour o’ my pretty frock—no—how could they?", "그리고 그분이 내 어여쁜 옷 색깔을 골라 줬다는 것도 생각 못 했어—아니야—어떻게 알았겠어?"], ["If they had known perhaps they would not have cared, for they don’t care much for him, poor thing!”", "알았더라도 어쩌면 신경도 안 썼겠지, 그 사람들은 그분도 그리 마음에 두지 않으니까, 가엾어라!\""]], [["Then she grieved for the beloved man whose conventional standard of judgement had caused her all these latter sorrows; and she went her way without knowing that the greatest misfortune of her life was this feminine loss of courage at the last and critical moment through her estimating her father-in-law by his sons.", "그러고서 그녀는, 인습에 매인 판단의 잣대로 이 모든 뒤늦은 슬픔을 자기에게 안긴 그 사랑하는 남자를 위해 마음 아파했다. 그리고 제 일생의 가장 큰 불행이, 시아버지를 그 아들들로 미루어 가늠한 탓에 바로 이 마지막 결정적 순간에 일어난 이 여성다운 용기의 상실이었음을 알지 못한 채, 그녀는 제 길을 갔다."], ["Her present condition was precisely one which would have enlisted the sympathies of old Mr and Mrs Clare.", "그녀의 지금 형편이야말로, 늙은 클레어 부부의 동정을 불러일으켰을 바로 그런 것이었다."], ["Their hearts went out of them at a bound towards extreme cases, when the subtle mental troubles of the less desperate among mankind failed to win their interest or regard.", "그들의 마음은, 그토록 절박하지는 않은 사람들의 미묘한 정신적 고뇌가 그들의 관심이나 배려를 얻지 못할 때조차, 극단의 경우들을 향해서는 단숨에 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["In jumping at Publicans and Sinners they would forget that a word might be said for the worries of Scribes and Pharisees; and this defect or limitation might have recommended their own daughter-in-law to them at this moment as a fairly choice sort of lost person for their love.", "세리와 죄인들에게 와락 달려드느라, 그들은 서기관과 바리새인들의 근심에도 한마디쯤 해 줄 말이 있을 수 있음을 잊곤 했다. 그리고 이 결함, 혹은 이 한계가, 바로 이 순간 그들에게 자기네 며느리를, 그들의 사랑을 받기에 자못 알맞은 부류의 길 잃은 사람으로 천거해 주었을지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["Thereupon she began to plod back along the road by which she had come not altogether full of hope, but full of a conviction that a crisis in her life was approaching.", "그리하여 그녀는, 희망에 온전히 부풀지는 않았으나, 제 삶에 한 고비가 다가오고 있다는 확신만은 가득한 채로, 자기가 왔던 길을 따라 터덜터덜 되짚어 가기 시작했다."], ["No crisis, apparently, had supervened; and there was nothing left for her to do but to continue upon that starve-acre farm till she could again summon courage to face the Vicarage.", "보아하니 어떤 고비도 닥치지는 않은 듯했다. 그리고 다시 목사관과 마주할 용기를 그러모을 수 있을 때까지 그 굶주린 땅 같은 농장에 계속 머무는 것 말고는, 그녀에게 남은 일이 없었다."], ["She did, indeed, take sufficient interest in herself to throw up her veil on this return journey, as if to let the world see that she could at least exhibit a face such as Mercy Chant could not show.", "사실 그녀는, 적어도 머시 챈트가 보여 줄 수 없는 그런 얼굴만은 자기가 내보일 수 있음을 세상에 알리려는 듯, 이 돌아오는 길에서 자기 자신에게 충분한 관심을 두어 베일을 걷어 올리기는 했다."], ["But it was done with a sorry shake of the head.", "그러나 그것은 서글피 고개를 흔들며 한 짓이었다."], ["“It is nothing—it is nothing!”", "\"아무것도 아니야—아무것도 아니야!\""], ["she said. “Nobody loves it; nobody sees it.", "그녀가 말했다. \"아무도 이걸 사랑하지 않아."], ["Who cares about the looks of a castaway like me!”", "아무도 이걸 보지 않아. 나 같은 버림받은 것의 겉모습 따위 누가 신경이나 쓰겠어!\""]], [["Her journey back was rather a meander than a march.", "그녀의 돌아오는 길은 행군이라기보다 차라리 어슬렁거림이었다."], ["It had no sprightliness, no purpose; only a tendency.", "거기엔 활기도, 목적도 없었다. 그저 한 가닥 가는 방향만 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Along the tedious length of Benvill Lane she began to grow tired, and she leant upon gates and paused by milestones.", "지루하게 긴 벤빌 길을 따라 그녀는 지치기 시작했고, 대문에 기대고 이정표 옆에서 걸음을 멈추곤 했다."]], [["She did not enter any house till, at the seventh or eighth mile, she descended the steep long hill below which lay the village or townlet of Evershead, where in the morning she had breakfasted with such contrasting expectations.", "그녀는 일곱 번째인가 여덟 번째 마일에 이르러, 그 아래로 에버셰드라는 마을, 아니 작은 읍이 자리한 가파르고 긴 언덕을 내려가서야 비로소 어느 집에 들어섰다. 아침에 그토록 대조적인 기대를 안고 아침을 먹었던 그곳이었다."], ["The cottage by the church, in which she again sat down, was almost the first at that end of the village, and while the woman fetched her some milk from the pantry, Tess, looking down the street, perceived that the place seemed quite deserted.", "그녀가 다시 들어가 앉은 교회 옆 오두막은 그 마을 끝자락에서 거의 첫 번째 집이었는데, 여자가 그녀에게 식료품 저장실에서 우유를 좀 가져다주는 동안, 테스는 거리를 내려다보다가 그곳이 아주 인적이 끊긴 듯함을 알아챘다."]], [["“The people are gone to afternoon service, I suppose?” she said.", "\"사람들이 오후 예배에 갔나 보네요?\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“No, my dear,”", "\"아니야, 얘.\""], ["said the old woman.", "늙은 여자가 말했다."], ["“’Tis too soon for that; the bells hain’t strook out yet.", "\"그러기엔 아직 일러. 종이 아직 안 울렸거든."], ["They be all gone to hear the preaching in yonder barn. A ranter preaches there between the services—an excellent, fiery, Christian man, they say.", "다들 저쪽 헛간에 설교 들으러 갔지. 예배와 예배 사이에 거기서 떠돌이 설교꾼 하나가 설교를 하는데—훌륭하고, 불같고, 신실한 사람이라더라."], ["But, Lord, I don’t go to hear’n! What comes in the regular way over the pulpit is hot enough for I.”", "하지만 세상에, 난 그 사람 설교 들으러 안 가! 정식으로 강단에서 나오는 것만으로도 나한텐 충분히 뜨거우니까.\""]], [["Tess soon went onward into the village, her footsteps echoing against the houses as though it were a place of the dead.", "테스는 곧 마을 안쪽으로 걸어 들어갔고, 그녀의 발소리가 마치 죽은 자들의 고장인 양 집집마다 메아리쳤다."], ["Nearing the central part, her echoes were intruded on by other sounds; and seeing the barn not far off the road, she guessed these to be the utterances of the preacher.", "중심부에 가까워지자 다른 소리들이 그녀의 메아리에 끼어들었다. 그리고 길에서 멀지 않은 곳에 헛간이 보이자, 그녀는 이것이 그 설교꾼의 말소리이리라 짐작했다."]], [["His voice became so distinct in the still clear air that she could soon catch his sentences, though she was on the closed side of the barn.", "그의 목소리는 고요하고 맑은 공기 속에서 어찌나 또렷해졌던지, 그녀가 헛간의 닫힌 쪽에 있었는데도 곧 그 문장들을 알아들을 수 있었다."], ["The sermon, as might be expected, was of the extremest antinomian type; on justification by faith, as expounded in the theology of St Paul.", "설교는, 예상할 만하게도, 더없이 극단적인 율법폐기론 류의 것으로, 성 바울로의 신학에 풀이된 대로의, 믿음에 의한 의로움에 관한 것이었다."], ["This fixed idea of the rhapsodist was delivered with animated enthusiasm, in a manner entirely declamatory, for he had plainly no skill as a dialectician.", "이 광상시인의 굳어진 관념은 활기찬 열정으로, 전적으로 격앙된 웅변조로 전해졌으니, 그에게는 변증가로서의 솜씨가 분명 없었던 까닭이다."], ["Although Tess had not heard the beginning of the address, she learnt what the text had been from its constant iteration—", "테스는 설교의 첫머리는 듣지 못했으나, 끊임없이 되풀이되는 것에서 그 본문이 무엇이었는지를 알아챘다—"]], [["“O foolish Galatians, who hath bewitched you, that ye should not obey the truth, before whose eyes Jesus Christ hath been evidently set forth, crucified among you?”", "\"오 어리석은 갈라디아 사람들아, 예수 그리스도께서 너희 눈앞에 십자가에 못 박히신 것이 밝히 보였거늘, 누가 너희를 홀려서 진리에 순종치 못하게 하더냐?\""]], [["Tess was all the more interested, as she stood listening behind, in finding that the preacher’s doctrine was a vehement form of the view of Angel’s father, and her interest intensified when the speaker began to detail his own spiritual experiences of how he had come by those views. He had, he said, been the greatest of sinners.", "뒤에 서서 듣던 그녀는, 그 설교꾼의 교리가 엔젤의 아버지가 지닌 견해의 격렬한 형태임을 알아채고는 더욱 흥미가 일었고, 그 설교자가 자기가 어떻게 그런 견해에 이르게 되었는지 제 영적인 체험을 상세히 풀어놓기 시작하자 그녀의 흥미는 한층 짙어졌다. 그는 말하기를, 자기는 죄인 중의 가장 큰 죄인이었노라 했다."], ["He had scoffed; he had wantonly associated with the reckless and the lewd.", "비웃었고, 분별없는 자들과 음탕한 자들과 함부로 어울렸노라 했다."], ["But a day of awakening had come, and, in a human sense, it had been brought about mainly by the influence of a certain clergyman, whom he had at first grossly insulted; but whose parting words had sunk into his heart, and had remained there, till by the grace of Heaven they had worked this change in him, and made him what they saw him.", "그러나 깨우침의 날이 왔고, 인간적으로 보자면 그것은 주로 어떤 목사의 영향으로 이루어진 일인데, 자기는 처음엔 그를 모질게 모욕했으나, 그의 이별의 말이 제 마음에 가라앉아 거기 남아 있다가, 마침내 하늘의 은총으로 그 말들이 자기 안에 이 변화를 일으켜, 지금 그들이 보는 모습으로 자기를 만들었노라 했다."]], [["But more startling to Tess than the doctrine had been the voice, which, impossible as it seemed, was precisely that of Alec d’Urberville.", "그러나 테스에게 그 교리보다 더 놀라웠던 것은 그 목소리였으니, 도무지 있을 수 없는 일 같았지만, 그것은 다름 아닌 알렉 더버빌의 목소리였다."], ["Her face fixed in painful suspense, she came round to the front of the barn, and passed before it.", "괴로운 긴장 속에 얼굴이 굳어진 채, 그녀는 헛간 정면으로 돌아 나와 그 앞을 지났다."], ["The low winter sun beamed directly upon the great double-doored entrance on this side; one of the doors being open, so that the rays stretched far in over the threshing-floor to the preacher and his audience, all snugly sheltered from the northern breeze.", "낮게 깔린 겨울 해가 이쪽 면의 두 짝 큰 출입문 위로 곧장 비쳐 들었다. 문 한 짝이 열려 있어, 햇살이 타작마당을 가로질러 멀리, 설교꾼과 그의 청중에게까지 뻗어 들었고, 그들은 모두 북풍을 피해 아늑하게 들어앉아 있었다."], ["The listeners were entirely villagers, among them being the man whom she had seen carrying the red paint-pot on a former memorable occasion.", "듣는 이들은 죄다 마을 사람들이었는데, 그 가운데에는 예전 어느 잊지 못할 때에 빨간 페인트통을 들고 다니던 것을 그녀가 보았던 그 남자도 있었다."], ["But her attention was given to the central figure, who stood upon some sacks of corn, facing the people and the door.", "그러나 그녀의 주의는 중앙의 인물에게로 쏠렸으니, 그는 곡식 자루 몇 개 위에 올라서서 사람들과 문 쪽을 향하고 있었다."], ["The three o’clock sun shone full upon him, and the strange enervating conviction that her seducer confronted her, which had been gaining ground in Tess ever since she had heard his words distinctly, was at last established as a fact indeed. End of Phase the Fifth", "세 시의 해가 그에게 가득 비쳤고, 자기를 유혹했던 자가 자기와 마주하고 있다는 그 묘하게 맥 빠지는 확신은—그의 말을 또렷이 들은 그 순간부터 테스 안에서 줄곧 자라 오던 그것은—마침내 정말로 사실로 굳어졌다."]], [["The Convert", "회심한 자"]], [["# XLV", "# XLV"]], [["Till this moment she had never seen or heard from d’Urberville since her departure from Trantridge.", "이 순간에 이르기까지 그녀는 트랜트리지를 떠난 이래로 더버빌을 한 번도 보지도, 그에게서 소식을 듣지도 못했다."]], [["The rencounter came at a heavy moment, one of all moments calculated to permit its impact with the least emotional shock.", "그 마주침은 무거운 순간에, 그 충격이 감정적으로 가장 적은 동요로 와닿게끔 짜인 온갖 순간들 가운데 하나에 찾아왔다."], ["But such was unreasoning memory that, though he stood there openly and palpably a converted man, who was sorrowing for his past irregularities, a fear overcame her, paralyzing her movement so that she neither retreated nor advanced.", "그러나 도리를 따지지 않는 기억이란 그런 것이어서, 비록 그가 거기 공공연히 또렷하게, 제 지난날의 방종을 슬퍼하는, 회심한 사람으로 서 있었는데도, 한 가닥 두려움이 그녀를 사로잡아 그녀의 움직임을 마비시켰으니, 그녀는 물러서지도 나아가지도 못했다."]], [["To think of what emanated from that countenance when she saw it last, and to behold it now!...", "그 얼굴을 마지막으로 보았을 때 거기서 뿜어 나오던 것을 생각하고, 그것을 이제 와 바라보다니!…"], ["There was the same handsome unpleasantness of mien, but now he wore neatly trimmed, old-fashioned whiskers, the sable moustache having disappeared; and his dress was half-clerical, a modification which had changed his expression sufficiently to abstract the dandyism from his features, and to hinder for a second her belief in his identity.", "거기엔 똑같이 잘생기고도 불쾌한 면모가 있었으나, 이제 그는 말끔히 다듬은 구식 구레나룻을 길렀고, 검은 콧수염은 사라지고 없었다. 그리고 그의 옷차림은 절반은 성직자풍이었으니, 그 손질이 그의 표정을 충분히 바꿔 놓아 그의 이목구비에서 멋부림을 걷어 냈고, 한순간 그녀가 그가 그 사람임을 믿지 못하게 막았다."]], [["To Tess’s sense there was, just at first, a ghastly _bizarrerie_, a grim incongruity, in the march of these solemn words of Scripture out of such a mouth.", "테스의 감각에는, 처음 한순간, 이런 입에서 이 엄숙한 성경 말씀이 줄지어 나온다는 것에, 섬뜩한 기괴함이, 음산한 부조화가 있었다."], ["This too familiar intonation, less than four years earlier, had brought to her ears expressions of such divergent purpose that her heart became quite sick at the irony of the contrast.", "채 사 년도 안 된 전에, 이 너무도 익숙한 억양은 그녀의 귀에 그토록 어긋난 목적의 말들을 실어다 주었던 터라, 그녀의 마음은 그 대조의 아이러니에 자못 메슥거렸다."]], [["It was less a reform than a transfiguration.", "그것은 개심이라기보다 변모였다."], ["The former curves of sensuousness were now modulated to lines of devotional passion.", "예전의 관능의 곡선들은 이제 경건한 열정의 선들로 다듬어져 있었다."], ["The lip-shapes that had meant seductiveness were now made to express supplication; the glow on the cheek that yesterday could be translated as riotousness was evangelized to-day into the splendour of pious rhetoric; animalism had become fanaticism; Paganism, Paulinism; the bold rolling eye that had flashed upon her form in the old time with such mastery now beamed with the rude energy of a theolatry that was almost ferocious.", "유혹을 뜻하던 입술 모양은 이제 간구를 표현하게끔 만들어졌고, 어제는 방탕으로 풀이될 수 있었던 뺨의 홍조는 오늘은 경건한 수사의 광채로 복음화되었으며, 동물성은 광신이 되었고, 이교는 바울로주의가 되었으며, 옛적에 그토록 위세 좋게 그녀의 자태에 번득이던 그 대담히 굴리는 눈은 이제 거의 사납다 할 신숭배의 거친 기운으로 빛났다."], ["Those black angularities which his face had used to put on when his wishes were thwarted now did duty in picturing the incorrigible backslider who would insist upon turning again to his wallowing in the mire.", "제 바람이 가로막힐 때면 그의 얼굴이 짓곤 하던 그 검은 모서리들은, 이제 진창 속 뒹굴기로 기어이 다시 돌아서려 드는 구제 불능의 타락자를 그려 내는 데 쓰이고 있었다."]], [["The lineaments, as such, seemed to complain.", "이목구비는, 그 자체로, 항변하는 듯 보였다."], ["They had been diverted from their hereditary connotation to signify impressions for which Nature did not intend them.", "그것들은 제 본래의 함의에서 비틀려, 자연이 그것들에 의도하지 않은 인상들을 나타내게끔 돌려지고 말았다."], ["Strange that their very elevation was a misapplication, that to raise seemed to falsify.", "묘한 일이었다, 바로 그 드높여짐이 오용이라는 것이, 들어 올리는 것이 거짓되이 꾸미는 듯 보인다는 것이."]], [["Yet could it be so?", "그러나 정말 그럴 수 있을까?"], ["She would admit the ungenerous sentiment no longer.", "그녀는 그 옹졸한 마음을 더는 인정하지 않으려 했다."], ["D’Urberville was not the first wicked man who had turned away from his wickedness to save his soul alive, and why should she deem it unnatural in him?", "더버빌이, 제 영혼을 살리려고 제 사악함에서 돌아선 첫 번째 사악한 사람도 아닐진대, 어째서 그녀가 그것을 그에게서 부자연스러운 일이라 여겨야 한단 말인가?"], ["It was but the usage of thought which had been jarred in her at hearing good new words in bad old notes.", "그것은 다만, 좋은 새 말을 나쁜 옛 가락으로 듣는 통에 그녀 안에서 어긋나 버린 생각의 버릇일 뿐이었다."], ["The greater the sinner, the greater the saint; it was not necessary to dive far into Christian history to discover that.", "죄인이 클수록 성인도 크다는 것은, 기독교 역사를 멀리 파헤치지 않고도 알아낼 수 있는 일이었다."]], [["Such impressions as these moved her vaguely, and without strict definiteness.", "이런 따위의 인상들이 그녀를 어렴풋이, 또렷한 또렷함도 없이 흔들었다."], ["As soon as the nerveless pause of her surprise would allow her to stir, her impulse was to pass on out of his sight.", "놀라움의 맥 빠진 멈춤이 그녀가 움직이도록 놓아주자마자, 그녀의 충동은 그의 시야 밖으로 지나쳐 가는 것이었다."], ["He had obviously not discerned her yet in her position against the sun.", "해를 등진 그녀의 위치에서, 그는 아직 분명히 그녀를 알아보지 못한 터였다."]], [["But the moment that she moved again he recognized her.", "그러나 그녀가 다시 움직이는 순간 그는 그녀를 알아보았다."], ["The effect upon her old lover was electric, far stronger than the effect of his presence upon her.", "그녀의 옛 연인에게 미친 영향은 전기 같았으니, 그의 존재가 그녀에게 미친 영향보다 훨씬 더 강했다."], ["His fire, the tumultuous ring of his eloquence, seemed to go out of him.", "그의 불길은, 그의 웅변이 들끓던 그 울림은, 그에게서 빠져나가 버린 듯했다."], ["His lip struggled and trembled under the words that lay upon it; but deliver them it could not as long as she faced him.", "그의 입술은 거기 얹힌 말들 아래에서 버둥거리며 떨렸으나, 그녀가 그를 마주하고 있는 한 그 말들을 도무지 내뱉지 못했다."], ["His eyes, after their first glance upon her face, hung confusedly in every other direction but hers, but came back in a desperate leap every few seconds.", "그의 눈은, 그녀의 얼굴에 처음 한 번 던져진 뒤로는, 그녀 쪽을 빼고 다른 모든 방향으로 어지러이 매달렸으나, 몇 초마다 한 번씩 절박한 도약으로 되돌아왔다."], ["This paralysis lasted, however, but a short time; for Tess’s energies returned with the atrophy of his, and she walked as fast as she was able past the barn and onward.", "그러나 이 마비는 잠깐밖에 이어지지 않았으니, 그의 기운이 위축되는 만큼 테스의 기운이 되돌아왔고, 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 빠르게 헛간을 지나 앞으로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["As soon as she could reflect, it appalled her, this change in their relative platforms.", "생각을 추스를 수 있게 되자마자, 그것이, 그들의 처지가 서로 뒤바뀐 이 변화가 그녀를 소스라치게 했다."], ["He who had wrought her undoing was now on the side of the Spirit, while she remained unregenerate.", "그녀의 파멸을 빚어낸 자가 이제는 성령의 편에 서 있고, 그녀는 거듭나지 못한 채로 남아 있었다."], ["And, as in the legend, it had resulted that her Cyprian image had suddenly appeared upon his altar, whereby the fire of the priest had been well nigh extinguished.", "그리고, 그 전설에서처럼, 결국 그의 제단 위에 그녀의 키프로스 여신상이 별안간 나타나, 그로 인해 사제의 불길이 거의 다 꺼져 버린 꼴이 되고 만 것이었다."]], [["She went on without turning her head.", "그녀는 고개를 돌리지 않고 계속 걸어갔다."], ["Her back seemed to be endowed with a sensitiveness to ocular beams—even her clothing—so alive was she to a fancied gaze which might be resting upon her from the outside of that barn.", "그녀의 등은 시선의 빛줄기에 대한 민감함을 부여받은 듯했고—심지어 그녀의 옷가지마저도—그만큼 그녀는, 그 헛간 바깥에서 자기에게 머물고 있을지 모를 상상 속의 시선에 생생히 깨어 있었다."], ["All the way along to this point her heart had been heavy with an inactive sorrow; now there was a change in the quality of its trouble.", "여기까지 오는 내내 그녀의 마음은 무력한 슬픔으로 무거웠으나, 이제는 그 괴로움의 결이 달라졌다."], ["That hunger for affection too long withheld was for the time displaced by an almost physical sense of an implacable past which still engirdled her.", "너무 오래 거부당한 애정에 대한 그 굶주림이, 한동안은, 여전히 그녀를 에워싸고 있는 가차 없는 과거에 대한 거의 육체적인 감각에 밀려나 버렸다."], ["It intensified her consciousness of error to a practical despair; the break of continuity between her earlier and present existence, which she had hoped for, had not, after all, taken place.", "그것은 자기 잘못에 대한 의식을 실질적인 절망으로까지 짙게 했다. 그녀가 그토록 바랐던, 자기의 옛 삶과 지금의 삶 사이의 단절은, 결국 일어나지 않았던 것이다."], ["Bygones would never be complete bygones till she was a bygone herself.", "지난 일들은, 그녀 자신이 지난 일이 되기 전에는, 결코 온전한 지난 일이 되지 못하리라."]], [["Thus absorbed, she recrossed the northern part of Long-Ash Lane at right angles, and presently saw before her the road ascending whitely to the upland along whose margin the remainder of her journey lay.", "그렇게 골똘히 잠긴 채, 그녀는 롱애시 길의 북쪽 부분을 직각으로 다시 가로질렀고, 이윽고 제 앞에, 남은 여정이 그 가장자리를 따라 놓인 그 고지로 희끄무레하게 오르는 길이 보였다."], ["Its dry pale surface stretched severely onward, unbroken by a single figure, vehicle, or mark, save some occasional brown horse-droppings which dotted its cold aridity here and there.", "그 메마르고 창백한 노면은 준엄하게 앞으로 뻗어, 그 차가운 황량함 위에 여기저기 점점이 흩어진 이따금의 갈색 말똥 말고는, 단 하나의 사람도, 탈것도, 자국도 없이 끊김 없이 이어졌다."], ["While slowly breasting this ascent Tess became conscious of footsteps behind her, and turning she saw approaching that well-known form—so strangely accoutred as the Methodist—the one personage in all the world she wished not to encounter alone on this side of the grave.", "이 오르막을 천천히 헤치고 오르던 테스는 등 뒤의 발소리를 알아채고 돌아섰다가, 그 너무도 낯익은 형체가—그토록 기이하게 감리교도로 차려입은 그 형체가—다가오는 것을 보았으니, 이 세상 누구보다도 무덤 이편에서 홀로 마주치고 싶지 않은 단 한 사람이었다."]], [["There was not much time, however, for thought or elusion, and she yielded as calmly as she could to the necessity of letting him overtake her.", "그러나 생각하거나 피할 시간은 그리 많지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 자기를 따라잡게 둘 수밖에 없는 필연에 되도록 차분히 따랐다."], ["She saw that he was excited, less by the speed of his walk than by the feelings within him.", "그녀는 그가, 빠른 걸음 때문이라기보다 제 안의 감정 때문에 흥분해 있음을 보았다."]], [["“Tess!”", "\"테스!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["She slackened speed without looking round.", "그녀는 돌아보지 않은 채 걸음을 늦추었다."]], [["“Tess!”", "\"테스!\""], ["he repeated. “It is I—Alec d’Urberville.”", "그가 되풀이했다. \"나야—알렉 더버빌.\""]], [["She then looked back at him, and he came up.", "그제야 그녀는 그를 돌아보았고, 그가 다가왔다."]], [["“I see it is,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["she answered coldly.", "그녀가 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“Well—is that all?", "\"흠—그게 다요?"], ["Yet I deserve no more! Of course,”", "하지만 난 그 이상 받을 자격도 없지! 물론,\""], ["he added, with a slight laugh, “there is something of the ridiculous to your eyes in seeing me like this.", "그가 가벼운 웃음을 곁들이며 덧붙였다. \"나를 이런 꼴로 보는 게 당신 눈엔 우스꽝스러운 데가 있겠지."], ["But—I must put up with that....", "하지만—그건 감수해야겠지…."], ["I heard you had gone away; nobody knew where.", "당신이 떠나 버렸다고, 어디로 갔는지 아무도 모른다고 들었소."], ["Tess, you wonder why I have followed you?”", "테스, 내가 왜 당신을 뒤쫓아 왔는지 궁금하겠지?\""]], [["“I do, rather; and I would that you had not, with all my heart!”", "\"좀 그러네요. 그리고 진심으로, 당신이 따라오지 않았더라면 싶어요!\""]], [["“Yes—you may well say it,”", "\"그래—그렇게 말할 만도 하지.\""], ["he returned grimly, as they moved onward together, she with unwilling tread.", "그가 침울하게 받았다. 둘은 함께 앞으로 걸어갔고, 그녀는 내키지 않는 걸음이었다."], ["“But don’t mistake me; I beg this because you may have been led to do so in noticing—if you did notice it—how your sudden appearance unnerved me down there.", "\"하지만 나를 오해하진 마시오. 내가 이렇게 청하는 건, 당신이 저 아래서 당신의 갑작스러운 등장에 내가 얼마나 넋이 나갔는지—혹시 그걸 알아챘다면—그걸 보고 그렇게 여겼을지 몰라서요."], ["It was but a momentary faltering; and considering what you have been to me, it was natural enough.", "그건 그저 한순간의 흔들림이었소. 그리고 당신이 내게 어떤 사람이었는지를 생각하면, 그건 충분히 자연스러운 일이었지."], ["But will helped me through it—though perhaps you think me a humbug for saying it—and immediately afterwards I felt that of all persons in the world whom it was my duty and desire to save from the wrath to come—sneer if you like—the woman whom I had so grievously wronged was that person.", "하지만 의지가 나를 그것에서 벗어나게 도와주었소—내가 이런 말을 하니 당신은 나를 사기꾼이라 여길지 모르겠지만—그리고 곧바로 나는 느꼈소, 모든 것 가운데\" 세상에서 다가올 진노로부터 구해 내는 것이 내 의무이자 소망인 사람들 가운데—비웃고 싶으면 비웃어도 좋다—내가 그토록 심하게 욕보인 그 여자가 바로 그런 사람이었소."], ["I have come with that sole purpose in view—nothing more.”", "나는 오직 그 목적 하나만 품고 왔소—그 이상은 없소.\""]], [["There was the smallest vein of scorn in her words of rejoinder: “Have you saved yourself?", "그녀의 대꾸하는 말에는 아주 가느다란 경멸의 결이 섞여 있었다. \"당신은 당신 자신은 구원했나요?"], ["Charity begins at home, they say.”", "자선은 집안에서 시작된다잖아요.\""]], [["“_I_ have done nothing!”", "\"나는 아무것도 하지 않았소!\""], ["said he indifferently.", "그가 무심하게 말했다."], ["“Heaven, as I have been telling my hearers, has done all.", "\"내 청중들에게 줄곧 말해 왔듯이, 하늘이 다 하셨소."], ["No amount of contempt that you can pour upon me, Tess, will equal what I have poured upon myself—the old Adam of my former years!", "테스, 당신이 내게 아무리 경멸을 퍼붓는다 해도, 내가 나 자신에게 퍼부은 것에는—내 지난 세월의 옛 아담에게 퍼부은 것에는 미치지 못할 거요! 그래, 참 이상한 이야기지."], ["Well, it is a strange story; believe it or not; but I can tell you the means by which my conversion was brought about, and I hope you will be interested enough at least to listen.", "믿든 말든 좋소. 하지만 내 회심이 어떻게 이루어졌는지 그 경위를 들려줄 수는 있고, 적어도 들어 줄 만큼은 당신이 흥미를 느꼈으면 좋겠소. 에민스터의 목사 이름을 들어 본 적 있소—틀림없이 들어 봤겠지?—노년의 클레어 씨 말이오."], ["Have you ever heard the name of the parson of Emminster—you must have done do?—old Mr Clare; one of the most earnest of his school; one of the few intense men left in the Church; not so intense as the extreme wing of Christian believers with which I have thrown in my lot, but quite an exception among the Established clergy, the younger of whom are gradually attenuating the true doctrines by their sophistries, till they are but the shadow of what they were.", "그분은 그 학파에서 가장 진실한 분들 중 하나이고, 교회에 몇 남지 않은 열정적인 사람들 중 하나요. 내가 한패로 합류한 기독교 신자들의 극단 진영만큼 강렬하지는 않지만, 국교회 성직자들 중에서는 아주 예외적인 분이오. 그 젊은 축들은 궤변으로 참된 교리를 차츰 묽게 만들어, 끝내는 옛 모습의 그림자에 지나지 않게 되어 가지."], ["I only differ from him on the question of Church and State—the interpretation of the text, ‘Come out from among them and be ye separate, saith the Lord’—that’s all.", "나는 그분과 오직 교회와 국가의 문제에서만 의견이 갈리오—'너희는 그들 가운데서 나와 따로 있으라, 주께서 말씀하시느니라' 하는 본문의 해석에서 말이오—그뿐이오."], ["He is one who, I firmly believe, has been the humble means of saving more souls in this country than any other man you can name.", "나는 굳게 믿거니와, 그분은 이 나라에서 당신이 댈 수 있는 그 누구보다도 더 많은 영혼을 구원해 낸 겸손한 도구였소."], ["You have heard of him?”", "그분 얘기를 들어 봤소?\""]], [["“I have,”", "\"들어 봤어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“He came to Trantridge two or three years ago to preach on behalf of some missionary society; and I, wretched fellow that I was, insulted him when, in his disinterestedness, he tried to reason with me and show me the way.", "\"이삼 년 전 그분이 어느 선교 단체를 위해 설교하러 트랜트리지에 왔었소. 그리고 한심한 작자였던 나는, 그분이 사심 없이 나를 타이르고 길을 보여 주려 했을 때 그분을 모욕했소. 그분은 내 행동에 노여워하지 않았소."], ["He did not resent my conduct, he simply said that some day I should receive the first-fruits of the Spirit—that those who came to scoff sometimes remained to pray.", "그저, 언젠가 내가 성령의 첫 열매를 받으리라고, 비웃으러 온 자가 때로는 기도하러 남기도 한다고 말씀하셨을 뿐이오. 그분의 말씀에는 이상한 마력이 있었소."], ["There was a strange magic in his words.", "그 말이 내 마음속에 가라앉았소."], ["They sank into my mind. But the loss of my mother hit me most; and by degrees I was brought to see daylight.", "하지만 가장 크게 나를 친 것은 어머니를 잃은 일이었소. 그리고 차츰차츰 나는 빛을 보게 되었소."], ["Since then my one desire has been to hand on the true view to others, and that is what I was trying to do to-day; though it is only lately that I have preached hereabout.", "그때 이후로 내 유일한 소망은 참된 견해를 다른 사람들에게 전하는 것이었고, 오늘도 내가 하려던 게 바로 그것이오. 다만 이 근방에서 설교하게 된 건 최근 들어서요."], ["The first months of my ministry have been spent in the North of England among strangers, where I preferred to make my earliest clumsy attempts, so as to acquire courage before undergoing that severest of all tests of one’s sincerity, addressing those who have known one, and have been one’s companions in the days of darkness.", "목회의 첫 몇 달은 잉글랜드 북부에서 낯선 사람들 틈에서 보냈소. 거기서 어설픈 첫 시도들을 해 보는 편을 택했지, 사람의 진실성을 시험하는 가장 혹독한 그 일—다시 말해 자기를 알아 온 사람들, 어둠의 시절을 함께한 동무들 앞에서 말하는 일—을 겪기 전에 용기를 좀 얻으려고 말이오."], ["If you could only know, Tess, the pleasure of having a good slap at yourself, I am sure—”", "테스, 자기 자신을 한바탕 호되게 후려치는 그 기쁨을 당신이 알기만 한다면, 나는 분명—\""]], [["“Don’t go on with it!”", "\"그만하세요!\""], ["she cried passionately, as she turned away from him to a stile by the wayside, on which she bent herself.", "그녀가 격하게 외치며, 그에게서 돌아서 길가의 디딤다리 쪽으로 가더니 그 위에 몸을 기댔다."], ["“I can’t believe in such sudden things!", "\"나는 그런 갑작스러운 일은 믿을 수가 없어요!"], ["I feel indignant with you for talking to me like this, when you know—when you know what harm you’ve done me!", "당신이 나한테 이렇게 말하는 게 분해요. 당신도 알면서—당신이 나한테 무슨 해를 끼쳤는지 알면서!"], ["You, and those like you, take your fill of pleasure on earth by making the life of such as me bitter and black with sorrow; and then it is a fine thing, when you have had enough of that, to think of securing your pleasure in heaven by becoming converted!", "당신 같은 사람들은 나 같은 사람의 삶을 슬픔으로 쓰디쓰고 새카맣게 만들면서 이 땅에서 실컷 쾌락을 누리잖아요. 그러고 나서 그게 물릴 만큼 누리고 나면, 회심해서 하늘의 쾌락까지 챙길 궁리를 하니 참 갸륵하기도 하지!"], ["Out upon such—I don’t believe in you—I hate it!”", "그런 건 꺼져 버려요—나는 당신을 믿지 않아요—그게 싫어요!\""]], [["“Tess,”", "\"테스,\""], ["he insisted; “don’t speak so!", "그가 다그쳤다. \"그런 말 하지 마오!"], ["It came to me like a jolly new idea!", "그건 내게 유쾌한 새로운 생각처럼 다가왔단 말이오!"], ["And you don’t believe me?", "그래서 당신은 나를 안 믿는다?"], ["What don’t you believe?”", "뭘 안 믿는다는 거요?\""]], [["“Your conversion.", "\"당신의 회심요."], ["Your scheme of religion.”", "당신의 종교 책략요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["She dropped her voice.", "그녀가 목소리를 낮췄다."], ["“Because a better man than you does not believe in such.”", "\"당신보다 나은 사람도 그런 건 믿지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“What a woman’s reason!", "\"참 여자다운 이유로군!"], ["Who is this better man?”", "그 나은 사람이 누구요?\""]], [["“I cannot tell you.”", "\"말할 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"흠,\""], ["he declared, a resentment beneath his words seeming ready to spring out at a moment’s notice, “God forbid that I should say I am a good man—and you know I don’t say any such thing.", "그가 단언했는데, 그 말 밑에 깔린 원망이 언제라도 튀어나올 듯했다. \"내가 좋은 사람이라고 말하다니 천만의 말씀이오—나는 그런 말은 하지도 않소, 당신도 알잖소."], ["I am new to goodness, truly; but newcomers see furthest sometimes.”", "정말이지 나는 선함에는 신참이오. 하지만 신참이 때로는 가장 멀리 보는 법이오.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["she replied sadly.", "그녀가 슬프게 대답했다."], ["“But I cannot believe in your conversion to a new spirit.", "\"하지만 나는 당신이 새로운 영으로 회심했다는 걸 믿을 수가 없어요."], ["Such flashes as you feel, Alec, I fear don’t last!”", "알렉, 당신이 느끼는 그런 번쩍임은, 오래가지 못할까 두려워요!\""]], [["Thus speaking she turned from the stile over which she had been leaning, and faced him; whereupon his eyes, falling casually upon the familiar countenance and form, remained contemplating her.", "이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 기대고 있던 디딤다리에서 돌아서 그를 마주 보았다. 그러자 그의 눈이 무심결에 그 낯익은 얼굴과 자태에 가닿더니, 그대로 머무르며 그녀를 응시했다."], ["The inferior man was quiet in him now; but it was surely not extracted, nor even entirely subdued.", "그의 안에 있던 저열한 인간은 지금 잠잠했다. 그러나 그것은 분명 뽑혀 나간 것도, 완전히 제압된 것도 아니었다."]], [["“Don’t look at me like that!”", "\"나를 그렇게 보지 마오!\""], ["he said abruptly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["Tess, who had been quite unconscious of her action and mien, instantly withdrew the large dark gaze of her eyes, stammering with a flush, “I beg your pardon!”", "자기 행동과 표정을 전혀 의식하지 못하고 있던 테스는, 즉시 그 크고 검은 눈길을 거두며 얼굴을 붉히고 더듬거렸다. \"죄송해요!\""], ["And there was revived in her the wretched sentiment which had often come to her before, that in inhabiting the fleshly tabernacle with which Nature had endowed her she was somehow doing wrong.", "그러자 그녀 안에서, 전에도 종종 찾아오던 그 비참한 감정이 되살아났다. 자연이 자기에게 부여한 이 육신의 장막 속에 깃들어 사는 것 자체가 어쩐지 잘못을 저지르는 것만 같은 그 감정이."]], [["“No, no! Don’t beg my pardon.", "\"아니, 아니오! 사과할 것 없소."], ["But since you wear a veil to hide your good looks, why don’t you keep it down?”", "그런데 미모를 가리려고 베일을 쓰는 거라면, 왜 내려 두지 않는 거요?\""]], [["She pulled down the veil, saying hastily, “It was mostly to keep off the wind.”", "그녀가 황급히 베일을 끌어 내리며 말했다. \"주로 바람을 막으려는 거였어요.\""]], [["“It may seem harsh of me to dictate like this,”", "\"이렇게 이래라저래라 하는 게 내가 야박해 보일지도 모르겠소.\""], ["he went on; “but it is better that I should not look too often on you.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 내가 당신을 너무 자주 보지 않는 편이 낫소."], ["It might be dangerous.”", "위험할지도 모르니까.\""]], [["“Ssh!”", "\"쉿!\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“Well, women’s faces have had too much power over me already for me not to fear them!", "\"그게 말이오, 여자들의 얼굴은 이미 내게 너무 큰 힘을 휘둘렀던 터라 그것을 두려워하지 않을 수가 없소!"], ["An evangelist has nothing to do with such as they; and it reminds me of the old times that I would forget!”", "복음 전도자는 그런 것들과 아무 상관도 없어야 하는데, 그게 내가 잊고 싶은 옛 시절을 떠올리게 한단 말이오!\""]], [["After this their conversation dwindled to a casual remark now and then as they rambled onward, Tess inwardly wondering how far he was going with her, and not liking to send him back by positive mandate.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로 두 사람의 대화는, 그들이 계속 걸어가는 동안 이따금 한마디씩 무심히 던지는 정도로 줄어들었다. 테스는 속으로 그가 자기와 함께 어디까지 가려는 건지 궁금해하면서도, 단호히 명령조로 그를 돌려보내고 싶지는 않았다."], ["Frequently when they came to a gate or stile they found painted thereon in red or blue letters some text of Scripture, and she asked him if he knew who had been at the pains to blazon these announcements.", "문이나 디딤다리에 이를 때마다 그 위에 빨간색이나 파란색 글자로 성경 구절이 적혀 있는 것을 종종 보았는데, 그녀는 그에게 누가 수고스럽게 이런 표어들을 써 붙여 놓았는지 아느냐고 물었다."], ["He told her that the man was employed by himself and others who were working with him in that district, to paint these reminders that no means might be left untried which might move the hearts of a wicked generation.", "그는 그 사람이 자기와, 그 지역에서 자기와 함께 일하는 다른 이들에게 고용되어, 악한 세대의 마음을 움직일 수 있는 어떤 방법도 시도하지 않은 채 남겨 두지 않으려고 이런 경고문들을 그려 넣는 거라고 말해 주었다."]], [["At length the road touched the spot called “Cross-in-Hand.”", "이윽고 길은 \"크로스인핸드\"라 불리는 곳에 닿았다."], ["Of all spots on the bleached and desolate upland this was the most forlorn.", "이 표백된 듯 황량한 고지의 모든 자리 가운데 이곳이 가장 쓸쓸했다."], ["It was so far removed from the charm which is sought in landscape by artists and view-lovers as to reach a new kind of beauty, a negative beauty of tragic tone.", "이곳은 화가와 풍경 애호가들이 풍경에서 찾는 그 매력으로부터 워낙 멀리 떨어져 있어, 도리어 새로운 종류의 아름다움, 비극적 색조를 띤 부정적인 아름다움에 이를 정도였다."], ["The place took its name from a stone pillar which stood there, a strange rude monolith, from a stratum unknown in any local quarry, on which was roughly carved a human hand.", "그곳은 거기 서 있는 돌기둥에서 이름을 따왔는데, 어느 지역 채석장에서도 알려지지 않은 지층에서 나온, 기이하고 거친 단일 석재였고, 그 위에는 사람의 손이 투박하게 새겨져 있었다."], ["Differing accounts were given of its history and purport.", "그 내력과 의미에 관해서는 서로 다른 이야기가 전해졌다."], ["Some authorities stated that a devotional cross had once formed the complete erection thereon, of which the present relic was but the stump; others that the stone as it stood was entire, and that it had been fixed there to mark a boundary or place of meeting.", "어떤 권위자들은 한때 신앙의 십자가가 그 위에 온전히 세워져 있었고 지금 남은 유물은 그 밑동에 불과하다고 했다. 또 어떤 이들은 그 돌이 서 있는 그대로 온전한 것이며, 경계나 만남의 장소를 표시하려고 거기 박아 둔 것이라고 했다."], ["Anyhow, whatever the origin of the relic, there was and is something sinister, or solemn, according to mood, in the scene amid which it stands; something tending to impress the most phlegmatic passer-by.", "어쨌든, 그 유물의 기원이 무엇이든, 그것이 서 있는 그 풍경 한가운데에는 기분에 따라 불길하거나 엄숙한 무언가가 있었고 지금도 있다. 가장 무덤덤한 행인조차 깊은 인상을 받게 하는 무언가가."]], [["“I think I must leave you now,”", "\"이제 당신과 헤어져야 할 것 같소.\""], ["he remarked, as they drew near to this spot.", "그들이 이곳에 가까워졌을 때 그가 말했다."], ["“I have to preach at Abbot’s-Cernel at six this evening, and my way lies across to the right from here.", "\"오늘 저녁 여섯 시에 애벗스서널에서 설교를 해야 하는데, 내 길은 여기서 오른쪽으로 갈라지오."], ["And you upset me somewhat too, Tessy—I cannot, will not, say why.", "그리고 당신은 나를 좀 어지럽혔소, 테시—왜인지는 말할 수 없고, 말하지 않겠소."], ["I must go away and get strength....", "가서 힘을 좀 추슬러야겠소…."], ["How is it that you speak so fluently now?", "그런데 어떻게 지금은 그렇게 유창하게 말하는 거요?"], ["Who has taught you such good English?”", "누가 당신한테 그런 좋은 영어를 가르쳐 주었소?\""]], [["“I have learnt things in my troubles,” she said evasively.", "\"고생하면서 이것저것 배웠어요.\" 그녀가 얼버무리며 말했다."]], [["“What troubles have you had?”", "\"무슨 고생을 했단 말이오?\""]], [["She told him of the first one—the only one that related to him.", "그녀는 첫 번째 고생 이야기를 그에게 했다—그와 관련된 단 하나의 고생을."]], [["D’Urberville was struck mute.", "더버빌은 말문이 막혔다."], ["“I knew nothing of this till now!”", "\"지금까지 이걸 전혀 몰랐소!\""], ["he next murmured.", "그가 이어 중얼거렸다."], ["“Why didn’t you write to me when you felt your trouble coming on?”", "\"고생이 닥쳐오는 게 느껴졌을 때 왜 내게 편지하지 않았소?\""]], [["She did not reply; and he broke the silence by adding: “Well—you will see me again.”", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그가 침묵을 깨고 덧붙였다. \"그래—당신은 나를 다시 보게 될 거요.\""]], [["“No,” she answered.", "\"아니요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["“Do not again come near me!”", "\"다시는 내 곁에 오지 마세요!\""]], [["“I will think.", "\"생각해 보겠소."], ["But before we part come here.”", "하지만 헤어지기 전에 이리 와 보오.\""], ["He stepped up to the pillar.", "그가 기둥으로 다가갔다."], ["“This was once a Holy Cross.", "\"이것은 한때 거룩한 십자가였소."], ["Relics are not in my creed; but I fear you at moments—far more than you need fear me at present; and to lessen my fear, put your hand upon that stone hand, and swear that you will never tempt me—by your charms or ways.”", "유물 같은 건 내 신조에 없지만, 나는 이따금 당신이 두렵소—지금 당신이 나를 두려워할 필요보다 훨씬 더 말이오. 그러니 내 두려움을 덜어 주려거든, 저 돌로 된 손 위에 당신 손을 얹고 맹세하시오. 절대로 나를 유혹하지 않겠다고—당신의 매력으로든 행동으로든.\""]], [["“Good God—how can you ask what is so unnecessary!", "\"세상에—어쩜 그렇게 쓸데없는 걸 요구하세요!"], ["All that is furthest from my thought!”", "그런 건 내 생각에서 가장 멀리 있는 일이에요!\""]], [["“Yes—but swear it.”", "\"그래도—맹세하시오.\""]], [["Tess, half frightened, gave way to his importunity; placed her hand upon the stone and swore.", "테스는 반쯤 겁에 질려 그의 끈질긴 요구에 굴복했다. 돌 위에 손을 얹고 맹세했다."]], [["“I am sorry you are not a believer,”", "\"당신이 신자가 아니라는 게 안타깝소.\""], ["he continued; “that some unbeliever should have got hold of you and unsettled your mind.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"어떤 불신자가 당신을 사로잡아 마음을 흔들어 놓았다니 말이오. 하지만 이제 그만하겠소."], ["But no more now. At home at least I can pray for you; and I will; and who knows what may not happen?", "적어도 집에서는 당신을 위해 기도할 수 있고, 그렇게 하겠소. 그러면 또 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠소?"], ["I’m off.", "나는 가오."], ["Goodbye!”", "잘 있으오!\""]], [["He turned to a hunting-gate in the hedge and, without letting his eyes again rest upon her, leapt over and struck out across the down in the direction of Abbot’s-Cernel.", "그는 산울타리의 사냥용 쪽문으로 돌아서더니, 다시는 그녀에게 눈길을 두지 않고 그것을 뛰어넘어 애벗스서널 방향으로 고원을 가로질러 성큼성큼 걸어 나갔다."], ["As he walked his pace showed perturbation, and by-and-by, as if instigated by a former thought, he drew from his pocket a small book, between the leaves of which was folded a letter, worn and soiled, as from much re-reading.", "걸어가는 그의 걸음걸이에는 동요가 비쳤고, 이윽고 어떤 앞선 생각에 떠밀린 듯 주머니에서 작은 책을 꺼냈는데, 그 책장 사이에는 여러 번 다시 읽은 듯 닳고 더러워진 편지 한 통이 접혀 있었다."], ["D’Urberville opened the letter.", "더버빌은 그 편지를 펼쳤다."], ["It was dated several months before this time, and was signed by Parson Clare.", "그것은 이 무렵보다 몇 달 전 날짜로 되어 있었고, 클레어 목사의 서명이 있었다."]], [["The letter began by expressing the writer’s unfeigned joy at d’Urberville’s conversion, and thanked him for his kindness in communicating with the parson on the subject.", "편지는 더버빌의 회심에 대한 글쓴이의 꾸밈없는 기쁨을 표하는 것으로 시작했고, 그 일에 관해 목사에게 연락해 준 친절에 감사했다."], ["It expressed Mr Clare’s warm assurance of forgiveness for d’Urberville’s former conduct and his interest in the young man’s plans for the future.", "거기에는 더버빌의 지난 행실에 대한 용서를 따뜻이 확언하는 클레어 씨의 말과, 그 젊은이의 장래 계획에 대한 관심이 담겨 있었다."], ["He, Mr Clare, would much have liked to see d’Urberville in the Church to whose ministry he had devoted so many years of his own life, and would have helped him to enter a theological college to that end; but since his correspondent had possibly not cared to do this on account of the delay it would have entailed, he was not the man to insist upon its paramount importance.", "클레어 씨 자신은 자기 인생의 그토록 여러 해를 바쳐 온 그 교회의 목회에 더버빌이 들어오는 것을 무척 보고 싶었을 것이고, 그러기 위해 신학교에 들어가도록 도왔으리라 했다. 그러나 수신인이 그로 인해 생길 지체 때문에 어쩌면 그러기를 원치 않았을 수도 있으니, 자기는 그 일의 절대적 중요성을 고집할 사람은 아니라고 했다."], ["Every man must work as he could best work, and in the method towards which he felt impelled by the Spirit.", "사람은 누구나 자기가 가장 잘할 수 있는 방식으로, 성령이 이끄는 방법을 따라 일해야 하는 법이라고."]], [["D’Urberville read and re-read this letter, and seemed to quiz himself cynically.", "더버빌은 이 편지를 읽고 또 읽으며, 냉소적으로 자기 자신을 조롱하는 듯했다."], ["He also read some passages from memoranda as he walked till his face assumed a calm, and apparently the image of Tess no longer troubled his mind.", "그는 또 걸으면서 비망록의 몇 구절도 읽었고, 마침내 그의 얼굴은 평온함을 띠었으며, 보아하니 테스의 모습은 더 이상 그의 마음을 어지럽히지 않는 듯했다."]], [["She meanwhile had kept along the edge of the hill by which lay her nearest way home.", "그동안 그녀는 집으로 가는 가장 가까운 길이 나 있는 언덕 가장자리를 따라 걸어왔다."], ["Within the distance of a mile she met a solitary shepherd.", "일 마일도 채 안 되는 거리에서 외로운 양치기 한 사람을 만났다."]], [["“What is the meaning of that old stone I have passed?”", "\"제가 지나온 저 오래된 돌은 무슨 의미예요?\""], ["she asked of him.", "그녀가 그에게 물었다."], ["“Was it ever a Holy Cross?”", "\"저게 한때 거룩한 십자가였나요?\""]], [["“Cross—no; ’twer not a cross!", "\"십자가—아니요, 그건 십자가가 아니었어요!"], ["’Tis a thing of ill-omen, Miss.", "그건 불길한 물건이에요, 아가씨."], ["It was put up in wuld times by the relations of a malefactor who was tortured there by nailing his hand to a post and afterwards hung.", "옛날에 어떤 죄인의 친척들이 세운 거지요. 그 죄인은 거기서 손이 기둥에 못 박히는 고문을 당하고 나중에 교수형을 당했답니다."], ["The bones lie underneath.", "그 밑에 뼈가 묻혀 있어요."], ["They say he sold his soul to the devil, and that he walks at times.”", "사람들 말로는 그자가 악마에게 영혼을 팔았고, 때때로 돌아다닌다고 하지요.\""]], [["She felt the _petite mort_ at this unexpectedly gruesome information, and left the solitary man behind her.", "그녀는 이 뜻밖의 섬뜩한 이야기에 가벼운 죽음의 전율을 느끼며, 그 외로운 남자를 뒤로하고 떠났다."], ["It was dusk when she drew near to Flintcomb-Ash, and in the lane at the entrance to the hamlet she approached a girl and her lover without their observing her.", "플린트콤애시에 가까워졌을 때는 땅거미가 지고 있었고, 마을 어귀의 골목에서 그녀는 한 처녀와 그 연인 곁으로 다가갔으나 그들은 그녀를 알아채지 못했다."], ["They were talking no secrets, and the clear unconcerned voice of the young woman, in response to the warmer accents of the man, spread into the chilly air as the one soothing thing within the dusky horizon, full of a stagnant obscurity upon which nothing else intruded.", "그들은 비밀이랄 것도 없는 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 남자의 더 따뜻한 어조에 응답하는 젊은 여자의 맑고 무심한 목소리가, 다른 무엇도 끼어들지 않는 고인 듯한 어둠으로 가득한 이 어스름한 지평선 안에서 유일하게 마음을 달래 주는 것으로 차가운 공기 속에 퍼졌다."], ["For a moment the voices cheered the heart of Tess, till she reasoned that this interview had its origin, on one side or the other, in the same attraction which had been the prelude to her own tribulation.", "한순간 그 목소리들은 테스의 마음을 북돋웠으나, 곧 그녀는 이 만남도 어느 한쪽에서든 자기 고난의 서곡이 되었던 바로 그 끌림에서 비롯된 것임을 헤아렸다."], ["When she came close, the girl turned serenely and recognized her, the young man walking off in embarrassment.", "그녀가 가까이 다가가자, 처녀가 차분히 돌아보며 그녀를 알아보았고, 젊은 남자는 멋쩍게 자리를 떴다."], ["The woman was Izz Huett, whose interest in Tess’s excursion immediately superseded her own proceedings.", "그 여자는 이즈 휴엇이었는데, 테스의 외출에 대한 관심이 곧바로 자기 일을 제쳐 두게 했다."], ["Tess did not explain very clearly its results, and Izz, who was a girl of tact, began to speak of her own little affair, a phase of which Tess had just witnessed.", "테스는 그 결과를 그다지 명확하게 설명하지 않았고, 눈치 빠른 처녀였던 이즈는 테스가 방금 한 장면을 목격한 자기 자신의 소소한 일에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“He is Amby Seedling, the chap who used to sometimes come and help at Talbothays,”", "\"저 사람은 앰비 시들링이에요. 톨버세이스에서 가끔 와서 일손을 거들던 그 친구요.\""], ["she explained indifferently.", "그녀가 무심하게 설명했다."], ["“He actually inquired and found out that I had come here, and has followed me.", "\"글쎄 내가 여기 온 걸 수소문해서 알아내고는 나를 따라왔지 뭐예요."], ["He says he’s been in love wi’ me these two years. But I’ve hardly answered him.”", "이 년째 나를 사랑해 왔다네요. 하지만 난 대꾸도 거의 안 했어요.\" XLVI"]], [["# XLVI", "# XLVI"]], [["Several days had passed since her futile journey, and Tess was afield.", "그녀의 헛된 여정이 있은 지 며칠이 지났고, 테스는 들판에 나가 있었다."], ["The dry winter wind still blew, but a screen of thatched hurdles erected in the eye of the blast kept its force away from her.", "메마른 겨울바람이 여전히 불었지만, 바람이 들이치는 길목에 세워 둔 이엉을 엮은 가림막이 그 힘을 그녀에게서 막아 주었다."], ["On the sheltered side was a turnip-slicing machine, whose bright blue hue of new paint seemed almost vocal in the otherwise subdued scene.", "바람을 등진 쪽에는 순무 써는 기계가 있었는데, 새로 칠한 그 선명한 파란빛은, 그 밖으로는 가라앉은 듯한 풍경 속에서 거의 목소리를 내는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Opposite its front was a long mound or “grave”, in which the roots had been preserved since early winter.", "그 앞쪽 맞은편에는 길쭉한 흙더미, 곧 \"무덤\"이 있었는데, 그 안에 순무 뿌리가 초겨울부터 저장되어 있었다."], ["Tess was standing at the uncovered end, chopping off with a bill-hook the fibres and earth from each root, and throwing it after the operation into the slicer.", "테스는 그 덮개를 벗긴 끝자락에 서서, 작은 낫으로 뿌리 하나하나에서 잔뿌리와 흙을 쳐 내고, 그 일이 끝나면 그것을 써는 기계 안으로 던져 넣고 있었다."], ["A man was turning the handle of the machine, and from its trough came the newly-cut swedes, the fresh smell of whose yellow chips was accompanied by the sounds of the snuffling wind, the smart swish of the slicing-blades, and the choppings of the hook in Tess’s leather-gloved hand.", "한 남자가 기계의 손잡이를 돌리고 있었고, 그 통에서는 갓 잘린 순무가 쏟아져 나왔다. 그 노란 조각들의 싱싱한 냄새에는 코를 훌쩍이는 듯한 바람 소리, 써는 칼날의 날카로운 휙휙 소리, 그리고 테스의 가죽 장갑 낀 손이 휘두르는 낫의 탕탕 소리가 함께 어우러졌다."]], [["The wide acreage of blank agricultural brownness, apparent where the swedes had been pulled, was beginning to be striped in wales of darker brown, gradually broadening to ribands.", "순무를 뽑아낸 자리에 드러난, 휑한 농경지의 갈색이 펼쳐진 너른 평지는, 차츰 더 짙은 갈색의 이랑들로 줄무늬지기 시작해, 점점 넓은 띠로 퍼져 가고 있었다."], ["Along the edge of each of these something crept upon ten legs, moving without haste and without rest up and down the whole length of the field; it was two horses and a man, the plough going between them, turning up the cleared ground for a spring sowing.", "그 이랑들 가장자리를 따라 무언가가 열 개의 다리로 기어가고 있었는데, 서두르지도 쉬지도 않으며 밭의 끝에서 끝까지 오르내리고 있었다. 그것은 말 두 필과 사람 하나였고, 그 사이로 쟁기가 가며, 봄 파종을 위해 정리된 땅을 갈아엎고 있었다."]], [["For hours nothing relieved the joyless monotony of things.", "몇 시간 동안 이 즐거움 없는 단조로움을 덜어 주는 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Then, far beyond the ploughing-teams, a black speck was seen.", "그러다 쟁기질하는 무리 너머 저 멀리에 검은 점 하나가 보였다."], ["It had come from the corner of a fence, where there was a gap, and its tendency was up the incline, towards the swede-cutters.", "그것은 산울타리 모퉁이의 터진 틈에서 나타났고, 비탈을 올라 순무 써는 사람들 쪽으로 향하는 기색이었다."], ["From the proportions of a mere point it advanced to the shape of a ninepin, and was soon perceived to be a man in black, arriving from the direction of Flintcomb-Ash.", "한낱 점에 불과하던 비례에서 그것은 볼링 핀의 형태로 커졌고, 곧 검은 옷을 입은 남자임이 드러났는데, 플린트콤애시 방향에서 오고 있었다."], ["The man at the slicer, having nothing else to do with his eyes, continually observed the comer, but Tess, who was occupied, did not perceive him till her companion directed her attention to his approach.", "기계 앞의 남자는 눈으로 달리 할 일이 없던 터라 다가오는 자를 줄곧 지켜보았지만, 일에 매여 있던 테스는 동료가 그 접근을 일러 주기 전까지는 그를 알아채지 못했다."]], [["It was not her hard taskmaster, Farmer Groby; it was one in a semi-clerical costume, who now represented what had once been the free-and-easy Alec d’Urberville.", "그것은 그녀의 가혹한 주인 그로비 농장주가 아니었다. 반쯤 성직자 차림을 한 사람이었는데, 한때 거리낌 없고 태평하던 알렉 더버빌이었던 자를 지금 대신하고 있었다."], ["Not being hot at his preaching there was less enthusiasm about him now, and the presence of the grinder seemed to embarrass him.", "설교에 열이 올라 있지 않은 터라 지금의 그에게는 열정이 덜했고, 기계를 돌리는 남자가 있는 게 그를 거북하게 하는 듯했다."], ["A pale distress was already on Tess’s face, and she pulled her curtained hood further over it.", "테스의 얼굴에는 이미 창백한 괴로움이 어렸고, 그녀는 베일 달린 두건을 얼굴 위로 더 깊이 끌어당겼다."]], [["D’Urberville came up and said quietly—", "더버빌이 다가와 조용히 말했다—"]], [["“I want to speak to you, Tess.”", "\"당신에게 할 말이 있소, 테스.\""]], [["“You have refused my last request, not to come near me!”", "\"당신은 내 마지막 부탁을, 내 곁에 오지 말라는 부탁을 거절했군요!\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but I have a good reason.”", "\"그렇소. 하지만 그럴 만한 까닭이 있소.\""]], [["“Well, tell it.”", "\"그럼 말해 보세요.\""]], [["“It is more serious than you may think.”", "\"당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 심각한 일이오.\""]], [["He glanced round to see if he were overheard.", "그는 누가 엿듣지 않나 둘레를 살폈다."], ["They were at some distance from the man who turned the slicer, and the movement of the machine, too, sufficiently prevented Alec’s words reaching other ears.", "그들은 써는 기계를 돌리는 남자에게서 어느 정도 떨어져 있었고, 기계가 움직이는 소리 또한 알렉의 말이 다른 귀에 닿는 것을 충분히 막아 주었다."], ["D’Urberville placed himself so as to screen Tess from the labourer, turning his back to the latter.", "더버빌은 그 일꾼에게 등을 돌려 테스를 가리도록 자리를 잡았다."]], [["“It is this,”", "\"이런 거요.\""], ["he continued, with capricious compunction.", "그가 변덕스러운 뉘우침을 담아 말을 이었다."], ["“In thinking of your soul and mine when we last met, I neglected to inquire as to your worldly condition.", "\"지난번에 만났을 때 당신 영혼과 내 영혼을 생각하느라, 나는 당신의 형편을 물어보는 걸 소홀히 했소."], ["You were well dressed, and I did not think of it.", "당신은 옷을 잘 차려입고 있었고, 나는 그 생각을 못 했소."], ["But I see now that it is hard—harder than it used to be when I—knew you—harder than you deserve.", "하지만 이제 보니 형편이 고되구려—내가 당신을 알던 시절보다 더 고되고—당신이 마땅히 받아야 할 것보다도 더 고되군."], ["Perhaps a good deal of it is owning to me!”", "어쩌면 그중 상당 부분이 내 탓일 거요!\""]], [["She did not answer, and he watched her inquiringly, as, with bent head, her face completely screened by the hood, she resumed her trimming of the swedes.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았고, 그는 캐묻듯 그녀를 지켜보았다. 그녀는 고개를 숙인 채, 두건에 얼굴이 완전히 가려진 채로, 다시 순무를 다듬는 일로 돌아갔다."], ["By going on with her work she felt better able to keep him outside her emotions.", "그녀는 일을 계속함으로써 그를 자기 감정 밖에 두기가 한결 수월하다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Tess,”", "\"테스,\""], ["he added, with a sigh of discontent,—“yours was the very worst case I ever was concerned in!", "그가 못마땅한 한숨을 쉬며 덧붙였다. \"당신 일은 내가 관여했던 일들 중에서도 가장 최악이었소!"], ["I had no idea of what had resulted till you told me.", "당신이 말해 주기 전까지 나는 어떤 결과가 빚어졌는지 전혀 몰랐소."], ["Scamp that I was to foul that innocent life!", "그 순결한 생명을 더럽히다니 나는 얼마나 못된 놈이었던지!"], ["The whole blame was mine—the whole unconventional business of our time at Trantridge.", "죄는 온전히 내 것이었소—트랜트리지에서의 그 모든 관습에 어긋난 일이 말이오."], ["You, too, the real blood of which I am but the base imitation, what a blind young thing you were as to possibilities!", "그리고 당신도, 내가 한낱 비천한 모방에 불과한 그 진짜 핏줄을 지닌 당신도, 닥칠 수 있는 일들에 대해 얼마나 눈먼 어린것이었던지!"], ["I say in all earnestness that it is a shame for parents to bring up their girls in such dangerous ignorance of the gins and nets that the wicked may set for them, whether their motive be a good one or the result of simple indifference.”", "진심으로 말하건대, 부모가 딸들을 그렇게, 악한 자들이 그들에게 놓을 덫과 그물에 대해 위험할 만큼 무지한 채로 키우는 건 부끄러운 일이오. 그 동기가 선한 것이든 단순한 무관심의 결과이든 말이오.\""]], [["Tess still did no more than listen, throwing down one globular root and taking up another with automatic regularity, the pensive contour of the mere fieldwoman alone marking her.", "테스는 여전히 듣기만 할 뿐, 한 알 한 알 둥근 뿌리를 던지고 또 집어 들기를 기계처럼 규칙적으로 되풀이했고, 한낱 들일하는 여자의 시름 어린 윤곽만이 그녀를 드러낼 뿐이었다."]], [["“But it is not that I came to say,”", "\"하지만 내가 하려고 온 말은 그게 아니오.\""], ["d’Urberville went on.", "더버빌이 말을 이었다."], ["“My circumstances are these.", "\"내 사정은 이렇소."], ["I have lost my mother since you were at Trantridge, and the place is my own.", "당신이 트랜트리지에 있던 이후로 나는 어머니를 잃었고, 그 영지는 내 것이 되었소."], ["But I intend to sell it, and devote myself to missionary work in Africa.", "하지만 나는 그것을 팔고 아프리카에서 선교 일에 헌신할 작정이오."], ["A devil of a poor hand I shall make at the trade, no doubt.", "의심할 바 없이 나는 그 일에 형편없이 서투른 손을 보이겠지."], ["However, what I want to ask you is, will you put it in my power to do my duty—to make the only reparation I can make for the trick played you: that is, will you be my wife, and go with me?...", "어쨌든, 내가 당신에게 묻고 싶은 건 이거요. 내가 의무를 다할 수 있게—당신에게 부린 농간에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 유일한 보상을 할 수 있게 해 주겠소. 다시 말해, 내 아내가 되어 나와 함께 가 주겠소?…"], ["I have already obtained this precious document.", "나는 벌써 이 귀한 서류를 마련해 두었소."], ["It was my old mother’s dying wish.”", "그건 늙으신 어머니의 임종 소원이었소.\""]], [["He drew a piece of parchment from his pocket, with a slight fumbling of embarrassment.", "그가 멋쩍은 듯 약간 더듬거리며 주머니에서 양피지 한 장을 꺼냈다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭐예요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“A marriage licence.”", "\"혼인 허가증이오.\""]], [["“O no, sir—no!” she said quickly, starting back.", "\"오 아니에요, 안 돼요!\" 그녀가 화들짝 물러서며 재빨리 말했다."]], [["“You will not?", "\"안 하겠다고?"], ["Why is that?”", "어째서요?\""]], [["And as he asked the question a disappointment which was not entirely the disappointment of thwarted duty crossed d’Urberville’s face.", "그가 그 질문을 던질 때, 좌절된 의무의 실망만은 아닌 어떤 실망이 더버빌의 얼굴을 스쳤다."], ["It was unmistakably a symptom that something of his old passion for her had been revived; duty and desire ran hand-in-hand.", "그것은 그녀를 향한 옛 정념의 무언가가 되살아났다는 명백한 징후였다. 의무와 욕망이 손을 맞잡고 나아가고 있었다."]], [["“Surely,” he began again, in more impetuous tones, and then looked round at the labourer who turned the slicer.", "\"분명히,\" 그가 더 격한 어조로 다시 입을 열다가, 써는 기계를 돌리는 일꾼을 돌아보았다."]], [["Tess, too, felt that the argument could not be ended there.", "테스 역시 이 논쟁이 여기서 끝날 수 없음을 느꼈다."], ["Informing the man that a gentleman had come to see her, with whom she wished to walk a little way, she moved off with d’Urberville across the zebra-striped field.", "그녀는 그 남자에게 한 신사분이 자기를 보러 와서 잠깐 같이 걷고 싶다고 알린 뒤, 더버빌과 함께 얼룩말 무늬처럼 줄진 밭을 가로질러 자리를 옮겼다."], ["When they reached the first newly-ploughed section he held out his hand to help her over it; but she stepped forward on the summits of the earth-rolls as if she did not see him.", "처음으로 새로 갈아엎은 구역에 이르자 그가 손을 내밀어 그녀가 그곳을 건너도록 도우려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 보지 못한 척, 흙이 말린 이랑들의 마루를 밟으며 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["“You will not marry me, Tess, and make me a self-respecting man?”", "\"테스, 나와 결혼해서 나를 스스로를 존중할 줄 아는 사람으로 만들어 주지 않겠소?\""], ["he repeated, as soon as they were over the furrows.", "고랑을 다 건너자마자 그가 되풀이했다. \"그럴 수 없어요.\""]], [["“I cannot.”", "\"하지만 왜요?\""]], [["“But why?” “You know I have no affection for you.”", "\"내가 당신에게 아무런 애정도 없다는 걸 알잖아요.\""]], [["“But you would get to feel that in time, perhaps—as soon as you really could forgive me?”", "\"하지만 시간이 지나면, 어쩌면 그런 감정이 생길지도 모르지—당신이 정말로 나를 용서할 수 있게 되는 순간 말이오?\""]], [["“Never!”", "\"절대로요!\""]], [["“Why so positive?”", "\"왜 그렇게 단정하오?\""]], [["“I love somebody else.”", "\"나는 다른 사람을 사랑하니까요.\""]], [["The words seemed to astonish him.", "그 말은 그를 놀라게 한 듯했다."]], [["“You do?”", "\"당신이?\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Somebody else?", "\"다른 사람을?"], ["But has not a sense of what is morally right and proper any weight with you?”", "하지만 도덕적으로 옳고 마땅한 것에 대한 분별이 당신에게는 조금도 무게가 없단 말이오?\""]], [["“No, no, no—don’t say that!”", "\"아니, 아니, 아니에요—그런 말 하지 마세요!\""]], [["“Anyhow, then, your love for this other man may be only a passing feeling which you will overcome—”", "\"어쨌든 그렇다면, 이 다른 남자에 대한 당신의 사랑은 당신이 극복하게 될 한때의 감정일 뿐일지도—\" \"아니요—아니에요.\""]], [["“No—no.” “Yes, yes!", "\"그래요, 그렇소!"], ["Why not?”", "왜 아니란 말이오?\""]], [["“I cannot tell you.”", "\"말할 수 없어요.\""]], [["“You must in honour!”", "\"명예를 걸고 말해야 하오!\""]], [["“Well then....", "\"그럼 말할게요…."], ["I have married him.”", "나는 그와 결혼했어요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he exclaimed; and he stopped dead and gazed at her.", "그가 외쳤다. 그리고 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그녀를 응시했다."]], [["“I did not wish to tell—I did not mean to!”", "\"말하고 싶지 않았어요—말할 생각이 아니었어요!\""], ["she pleaded.", "그녀가 간청했다."], ["“It is a secret here, or at any rate but dimly known.", "\"여기서는 비밀이에요, 아니면 적어도 어렴풋이만 알려져 있어요."], ["So will you, _please_ will you, keep from questioning me?", "그러니 제발, 부디 나한테 캐묻는 건 그만둬 주시겠어요?"], ["You must remember that we are now strangers.”", "우리는 이제 남남이라는 걸 기억하셔야 해요.\""]], [["“Strangers—are we?", "\"남남—우리가?"], ["Strangers!”", "남남이라!\""]], [["For a moment a flash of his old irony marked his face; but he determinedly chastened it down.", "한순간 그의 옛 빈정거림의 번뜩임이 그의 얼굴에 어렸다. 그러나 그는 단호히 그것을 눌러 가라앉혔다."]], [["“Is that man your husband?”", "\"저 남자가 당신 남편이오?\""], ["he asked mechanically, denoting by a sign the labourer who turned the machine.", "그가 써는 기계를 돌리는 일꾼을 손짓으로 가리키며 기계적으로 물었다."]], [["“That man!”", "\"저 남자라뇨!\""], ["she said proudly. “I should think not!”", "그녀가 자랑스럽게 말했다. \"천만에요!\""]], [["“Who, then?”", "\"그럼 누구요?\""]], [["“Do not ask what I do not wish to tell!”", "\"내가 말하고 싶지 않은 걸 묻지 마세요!\""], ["she begged, and flashed her appeal to him from her upturned face and lash-shadowed eyes.", "그녀가 애원했고, 치켜든 얼굴과 속눈썹 그늘진 눈에서 그에게 호소를 번뜩였다."]], [["D’Urberville was disturbed.", "더버빌은 동요했다."]], [["“But I only asked for your sake!”", "\"하지만 나는 오직 당신을 위해서 물은 거요!\""], ["he retorted hotly.", "그가 발끈해서 되받았다."], ["“Angels of heaven!—God forgive me for such an expression—I came here, I swear, as I thought for your good.", "\"하늘의 천사들이여!—이런 말을 한 걸 하느님이 용서하시기를—맹세컨대 나는 당신을 위한다고 생각해서 여기 온 거란 말이오."], ["Tess—don’t look at me so—I cannot stand your looks!", "테스—나를 그렇게 보지 마오—나는 당신의 그 눈길을 견딜 수가 없소!"], ["There never were such eyes, surely, before Christianity or since!", "기독교 이전에도 이후에도 분명 저런 눈은 없었을 거요!"], ["There—I won’t lose my head; I dare not.", "자—나는 정신을 잃지 않겠소. 감히 그럴 수 없소."], ["I own that the sight of you had waked up my love for you, which, I believed, was extinguished with all such feelings.", "인정하건대, 당신을 본 것이 당신을 향한 내 사랑을 일깨웠소. 나는 그 사랑이 그런 온갖 감정과 함께 다 꺼져 버린 줄 알았는데 말이오."], ["But I thought that our marriage might be a sanctification for us both.", "하지만 나는 우리의 결혼이 우리 둘 다에게 성화가 될 수 있으리라 생각했소."], ["‘The unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the husband,’ I said to myself.", "'믿지 아니하는 남편이 아내로 말미암아 거룩하게 되고, 믿지 아니하는 아내가 남편으로 말미암아 거룩하게 되나니' 하고 나는 속으로 말했소."], ["But my plan is dashed from me; and I must bear the disappointment!”", "하지만 내 계획은 산산이 부서졌으니, 나는 이 실망을 견뎌야 하오!\""]], [["He moodily reflected with his eyes on the ground.", "그는 침울하게 눈을 땅에 두고 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Married.", "\"결혼했다."], ["Married!...", "결혼했어!…"], ["Well, that being so,”", "그래, 그렇다면,\""], ["he added, quite calmly, tearing the licence slowly into halves and putting them in his pocket; “that being prevented, I should like to do some good to you and your husband, whoever he may be.", "그가 사뭇 차분하게 덧붙이며, 허가증을 천천히 둘로 찢어 주머니에 넣었다. \"그렇게 막혀 버린 거라면, 나는 당신과 당신 남편에게, 그가 누구든, 무언가 좋은 일을 해 주고 싶소."], ["There are many questions that I am tempted to ask, but I will not do so, of course, in opposition to your wishes. Though, if I could know your husband, I might more easily benefit him and you.", "묻고 싶은 게 여럿 있지만, 물론 당신 뜻을 거슬러서 그러지는 않겠소. 다만 내가 당신 남편을 알 수만 있다면, 그와 당신에게 더 수월하게 도움을 줄 수 있을 텐데."], ["Is he on this farm?”", "그는 이 농장에 있소?\""]], [["“No,” she murmured.", "\"아니요.\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“He is far away.”", "\"멀리 있어요.\""]], [["“Far away?", "\"멀리?"], ["From _you_?", "당신에게서?"], ["What sort of husband can he be?”", "그 사람은 대체 어떤 남편이란 말이오?\""]], [["“O, do not speak against him!", "\"오, 그이를 헐뜯지 마세요!"], ["It was through you!", "그건 당신 때문이었어요!"], ["He found out—”", "그이가 알아내고는—\""]], [["“Ah, is it so!...", "\"아, 그렇게 된 거요!…"], ["That’s sad, Tess!”", "그것 참 안됐구려, 테스!\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“But to stay away from you—to leave you to work like this!”", "\"하지만 당신에게서 떨어져 있다니—당신을 이렇게 일하게 내버려 두다니!\""]], [["“He does not leave me to work!” she cried, springing to the defence of the absent one with all her fervour.", "\"그이는 나를 일하라고 내버려 둔 게 아니에요!\" 그녀가 자리에 없는 이를 변호하느라 온 열정을 다해 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["“He don’t know it!", "\"그이는 이걸 몰라요!"], ["It is by my own arrangement.”", "이건 내가 스스로 정한 거예요.\""]], [["“Then, does he write?”", "\"그럼 편지는 하오?\""]], [["“I—I cannot tell you.", "\"나는—말할 수 없어요."], ["There are things which are private to ourselves.”", "우리 둘만의 사사로운 일들이 있어요.\""]], [["“Of course that means that he does not.", "\"물론 그건 그가 편지를 안 한다는 뜻이로군."], ["You are a deserted wife, my fair Tess—”", "당신은 버림받은 아내요, 나의 어여쁜 테스—\""]], [["In an impulse he turned suddenly to take her hand; the buff-glove was on it, and he seized only the rough leather fingers which did not express the life or shape of those within.", "충동에 휩싸여 그가 갑자기 돌아서 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다. 그 손에는 담황색 장갑이 끼워져 있었고, 그는 그 안의 생명도 형태도 드러내 주지 않는 거친 가죽 손가락만을 붙들었다."]], [["“You must not—you must not!”", "\"안 돼요—안 돼요!\""], ["she cried fearfully, slipping her hand from the glove as from a pocket, and leaving it in his grasp.", "그녀가 겁에 질려 외치며, 주머니에서 손을 빼듯 장갑에서 손을 빼내어 그 장갑을 그의 손아귀에 남겨 두었다."], ["“O, will you go away—for the sake of me and my husband—go, in the name of your own Christianity!”", "\"오, 제발 가 주시겠어요—나와 내 남편을 위해서—당신 자신의 기독교의 이름으로, 가 주세요!\""]], [["“Yes, yes; I will,”", "\"그래요, 그러겠소. 가겠소.\""], ["he said abruptly, and thrusting the glove back to her he turned to leave.", "그가 불쑥 말하더니, 장갑을 그녀에게 도로 내밀고 떠나려 돌아섰다. 그러나 다시 몸을 돌려 말했다."], ["Facing round, however, he said, “Tess, as God is my judge, I meant no humbug in taking your hand!”", "\"테스, 하느님이 나의 심판자이심을 두고 맹세하건대, 당신 손을 잡은 데에 나는 아무런 속임수도 품지 않았소!\""]], [["A pattering of hoofs on the soil of the field, which they had not noticed in their preoccupation, ceased close behind them; and a voice reached her ear:", "그들이 골몰해 있느라 알아채지 못했던, 밭의 흙바닥을 두드리는 말발굽 소리가 바로 그들 뒤에서 멎었다. 그리고 한 목소리가 그녀의 귀에 닿았다."]], [["“What the devil are you doing away from your work at this time o’ day?”", "\"이런 대낮에 일은 안 하고 어디서 뭣들 하고 있는 거야?\""]], [["Farmer Groby had espied the two figures from the distance, and had inquisitively ridden across, to learn what was their business in his field.", "그로비 농장주가 멀리서 두 사람의 모습을 발견하고, 그들이 자기 밭에서 무슨 일을 하는지 알아보려고 꼬치꼬치 따지듯 말을 달려 가로질러 온 것이었다."]], [["“Don’t speak like that to her!” said d’Urberville, his face blackening with something that was not Christianity.", "\"그녀에게 그런 식으로 말하지 마시오!\" 더버빌이 말했고, 그의 얼굴은 기독교가 아닌 무언가로 검게 일그러졌다."]], [["“Indeed, Mister!", "\"허, 그러쇼, 나리!"], ["And what mid Methodist pa’sons have to do with she?”", "그래 감리교 목사 양반이 이 여자랑 무슨 상관이라도 있소?\""]], [["“Who is the fellow?”", "\"이자는 누구요?\""], ["asked d’Urberville, turning to Tess.", "더버빌이 테스에게 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["She went close up to him.", "그녀가 그에게 바싹 다가갔다."]], [["“Go—I do beg you!”", "\"가세요—제발 부탁이에요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["And leave you to that tyrant?", "그래서 당신을 저 폭군한테 내맡겨 두라고?"], ["I can see in his face what a churl he is.”", "저자 얼굴만 봐도 얼마나 막돼먹은 놈인지 알겠소.\""]], [["“He won’t hurt me.", "\"그는 나를 해치지 않아요."], ["_He’s_ not in love with me.", "그는 나를 사랑하지 않으니까요."], ["I can leave at Lady-Day.”", "나는 성모영보 축일에 떠날 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, I have no right but to obey, I suppose.", "\"글쎄, 나로서는 따르는 것 말고는 도리가 없겠지."], ["But—well, goodbye!”", "하지만—그래, 잘 있으오!\""]], [["Her defender, whom she dreaded more than her assailant, having reluctantly disappeared, the farmer continued his reprimand, which Tess took with the greatest coolness, that sort of attack being independent of sex.", "그녀가 가해자보다도 더 두려워하던 그녀의 보호자가 마지못해 사라지자, 농장주는 꾸지람을 계속했고, 테스는 그것을 더없이 냉정하게 받아들였으니, 그런 종류의 공격은 성별과 무관한 것이었다."], ["To have as a master this man of stone, who would have cuffed her if he had dared, was almost a relief after her former experiences.", "감히 그럴 수 있었다면 그녀를 후려쳤을 이 돌 같은 남자를 주인으로 두는 것은, 앞서 겪은 일들에 비하면 차라리 안도에 가까웠다."], ["She silently walked back towards the summit of the field that was the scene of her labour, so absorbed in the interview which had just taken place that she was hardly aware that the nose of Groby’s horse almost touched her shoulders.", "그녀는 자기 노동의 무대였던 밭 마루 쪽으로 말없이 되돌아 걸었는데, 방금 일어난 그 만남에 어찌나 빠져 있었던지, 그로비의 말 코가 자기 어깨에 거의 닿을 듯한 것도 거의 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“If so be you make an agreement to work for me till Lady-Day, I’ll see that you carry it out,”", "\"성모영보 축일까지 날 위해 일하기로 약속을 했으면, 그걸 지키도록 내가 단단히 지켜보겠어.\""], ["he growled. “’Od rot the women—now ’tis one thing, and then ’tis another.", "그가 으르렁거렸다. \"여편네들이란, 빌어먹을—이랬다저랬다 변덕이야."], ["But I’ll put up with it no longer!”", "하지만 더는 못 참아!\""]], [["Knowing very well that he did not harass the other women of the farm as he harassed her out of spite for the flooring he had once received, she did for one moment picture what might have been the result if she had been free to accept the offer just made her of being the monied Alec’s wife.", "그가 다른 여자들은, 한때 자기가 나가떨어졌던 일에 앙심을 품고 그녀를 들볶듯이 들볶지 않는다는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었기에, 한순간 그녀는 만약 자기가 방금 받은 제안을, 곧 돈 많은 알렉의 아내가 되라는 제안을 자유로이 받아들일 수 있었다면 그 결과가 어떠했을지 그려 보았다."], ["It would have lifted her completely out of subjection, not only to her present oppressive employer, but to a whole world who seemed to despise her.", "그것은 그녀를 예속에서 완전히 들어 올려 주었을 것이다. 지금의 이 억압적인 고용주뿐 아니라, 그녀를 멸시하는 듯한 온 세상으로부터의 예속에서. \"하지만 안 돼, 안 돼!\""], ["“But no, no!” she said breathlessly; “I could not have married him now!", "그녀가 숨 가쁘게 말했다. \"지금 그와 결혼할 수는 없었어!"], ["He is so unpleasant to me.”", "그는 나한테 너무나 거북한 사람이야.\""]], [["That very night she began an appealing letter to Clare, concealing from him her hardships, and assuring him of her undying affection.", "바로 그날 밤 그녀는 클레어에게 호소하는 편지를 쓰기 시작했는데, 자기 고생은 그에게 감추고, 자기의 변치 않는 애정을 다짐했다."], ["Any one who had been in a position to read between the lines would have seen that at the back of her great love was some monstrous fear—almost a desperation—as to some secret contingencies which were not disclosed.", "행간을 읽어 낼 위치에 있는 사람이라면 누구든, 그 깊은 사랑 뒤편에 어떤 끔찍한 두려움이—거의 절망에 가까운 것이—드러나지 않은 어떤 은밀한 사태에 대한 두려움이 도사리고 있음을 알아챘을 것이다."], ["But again she did not finish her effusion; he had asked Izz to go with him, and perhaps he did not care for her at all.", "그러나 이번에도 그녀는 자기 토로를 끝맺지 못했다. 그는 이즈에게 함께 가자고 했었고, 어쩌면 그는 그녀를 조금도 마음에 두지 않는지도 몰랐다."], ["She put the letter in her box, and wondered if it would ever reach Angel’s hands.", "그녀는 편지를 상자에 넣어 두고, 그것이 과연 언젠가 에인절의 손에 닿을지 궁금해했다."]], [["After this her daily tasks were gone through heavily enough, and brought on the day which was of great import to agriculturists—the day of the Candlemas Fair.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로 그녀의 매일의 일과는 제법 무겁게 흘러갔고, 마침내 농사꾼들에게 큰 의미가 있는 날—캔들마스 장날에 이르렀다."], ["It was at this fair that new engagements were entered into for the twelve months following the ensuing Lady-Day, and those of the farming population who thought of changing their places duly attended at the county-town where the fair was held.", "바로 이 장에서 다가올 성모영보 축일 이후 열두 달치 새 고용 계약이 맺어졌고, 일자리를 옮길 생각이 있는 농촌 사람들은 장이 서는 군청 소재지에 으레 모여들었다."], ["Nearly all the labourers on Flintcomb-Ash farm intended flight, and early in the morning there was a general exodus in the direction of the town, which lay at a distance of from ten to a dozen miles over hilly country.", "플린트콤애시 농장의 일꾼들은 거의 다 떠날 작정이었고, 이른 아침부터 읍내 방향으로 일제히 빠져나가는 행렬이 이어졌는데, 그 읍내는 언덕진 지형을 넘어 십에서 십이 마일가량 떨어진 곳에 있었다."], ["Though Tess also meant to leave at the quarter-day, she was one of the few who did not go to the fair, having a vaguely-shaped hope that something would happen to render another outdoor engagement unnecessary.", "테스 역시 분기일에 떠날 작정이었지만, 다른 노천 고용 계약을 맺을 필요가 없게 될 무슨 일이 일어나리라는 막연한 희망을 품고서, 장에 가지 않은 몇 안 되는 사람 중 하나였다."]], [["It was a peaceful February day, of wonderful softness for the time, and one would almost have thought that winter was over.", "이 무렵치고는 놀랍도록 포근한, 평온한 이월의 어느 날이었고, 누구라도 겨울이 다 간 거라고 생각할 뻔했다."], ["She had hardly finished her dinner when d’Urberville’s figure darkened the window of the cottage wherein she was a lodger, which she had all to herself to-day.", "그녀가 점심을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그녀가 하숙하고 있던 오두막의 창을 더버빌의 모습이 어둡게 가렸는데, 오늘은 그 오두막을 그녀 혼자 차지하고 있었다."]], [["Tess jumped up, but her visitor had knocked at the door, and she could hardly in reason run away.", "테스는 벌떡 일어섰지만, 그녀의 방문객은 이미 문을 두드린 뒤였고, 이치상 도무지 달아날 수도 없었다."], ["D’Urberville’s knock, his walk up to the door, had some indescribable quality of difference from his air when she last saw him.", "더버빌이 문을 두드리는 모양, 문까지 걸어오는 걸음에는 그녀가 지난번에 보았을 때의 그 태도와는 뭐라 형언할 수 없이 다른 무언가가 있었다."], ["They seemed to be acts of which the doer was ashamed.", "그것들은 행하는 자가 부끄러워하는 행동들 같았다."], ["She thought that she would not open the door; but, as there was no sense in that either, she arose, and having lifted the latch stepped back quickly.", "그녀는 문을 열지 않으리라 생각했다. 그러나 그것도 별 의미가 없었으므로 일어나, 빗장을 들어 올리고는 재빨리 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["He came in, saw her, and flung himself down into a chair before speaking.", "그가 들어와 그녀를 보더니, 말도 꺼내기 전에 의자에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["“Tess—I couldn’t help it!”", "\"테스—어쩔 수가 없었소!\""], ["he began desperately, as he wiped his heated face, which had also a superimposed flush of excitement.", "그가 절박하게 입을 떼며, 흥분의 홍조까지 겹쳐 달아오른 얼굴을 닦았다."], ["“I felt that I must call at least to ask how you are.", "\"적어도 잘 지내는지 안부라도 물으려고 들러야겠다고 느꼈소."], ["I assure you I had not been thinking of you at all till I saw you that Sunday; now I cannot get rid of your image, try how I may!", "정말이지 그 일요일에 당신을 보기 전까지는 당신 생각을 전혀 하지 않고 있었소. 그런데 이제는 아무리 애를 써도 당신의 모습을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없소!"], ["It is hard that a good woman should do harm to a bad man; yet so it is.", "선한 여자가 악한 남자에게 해를 끼친다는 건 고약한 일이지만, 사실이 그렇소."], ["If you would only pray for me, Tess!”", "테스, 당신이 나를 위해 기도만 해 준다면!\""]], [["The suppressed discontent of his manner was almost pitiable, and yet Tess did not pity him.", "그의 태도에 억눌린 불만은 거의 가련할 지경이었으나, 그래도 테스는 그를 가엾이 여기지 않았다."]], [["“How can I pray for you,”", "\"내가 어떻게 당신을 위해 기도하겠어요.\""], ["she said, “when I am forbidden to believe that the great Power who moves the world would alter His plans on my account?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"세상을 움직이는 그 위대한 힘이 나 때문에 자기 계획을 바꾸시리라고 믿는 것이 내겐 금지되어 있는데요?\""]], [["“You really think that?”", "\"정말 그렇게 생각하오?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["I have been cured of the presumption of thinking otherwise.”", "나는 달리 생각하는 그 주제넘음에서 고침을 받았어요.\""]], [["“Cured?", "\"고침을?"], ["By whom?”", "누구에게?\""]], [["“By my husband, if I must tell.”", "\"굳이 말하자면, 내 남편에게요.\""]], [["“Ah—your husband—your husband!", "\"아—당신 남편—당신 남편이라!"], ["How strange it seems!", "참 이상하게 들리는구려!"], ["I remember you hinted something of the sort the other day.", "일전에도 당신이 그 비슷한 걸 넌지시 비쳤던 게 기억나오."], ["What do you really believe in these matters, Tess?”", "테스, 당신은 이런 문제들에 대해 정말로 무엇을 믿소?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“You seem to have no religion—perhaps owing to me.”", "\"당신은 아무 종교도 없는 것 같은데—어쩌면 나 때문이겠지.\""]], [["“But I have. Though I don’t believe in anything supernatural.”", "\"하지만 나는 종교가 있어요. 비록 초자연적인 건 아무것도 믿지 않지만요.\""]], [["D’Urberville looked at her with misgiving.", "더버빌이 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 보았다."]], [["“Then do you think that the line I take is all wrong?”", "\"그럼 내가 택한 길이 죄다 틀렸다고 생각하오?\""]], [["“A good deal of it.”", "\"상당 부분은요.\""]], [["“H’m—and yet I’ve felt so sure about it,”", "\"흠—그런데도 나는 그것에 대해 그토록 확신했는데.\""], ["he said uneasily.", "그가 불안하게 말했다."]], [["“I believe in the _spirit_ of the Sermon on the Mount, and so did my dear husband....", "\"나는 산상수훈의 정신은 믿어요. 내 사랑하는 남편도 그랬고요…."], ["But I don’t believe—”", "하지만 나는 믿지 않아요—\""]], [["Here she gave her negations.", "여기서 그녀는 자기가 부정하는 것들을 늘어놓았다."]], [["“The fact is,”", "\"실은 말이오,\""], ["said d’Urberville drily, “whatever your dear husband believed you accept, and whatever he rejected you reject, without the least inquiry or reasoning on your own part.", "더버빌이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"당신의 그 사랑하는 남편이 믿은 건 뭐든 당신이 받아들이고, 그가 물리친 건 뭐든 당신이 물리치는군. 당신 나름의 조사나 추론은 조금도 없이 말이오."], ["That’s just like you women.", "그게 딱 당신네 여자들이지."], ["Your mind is enslaved to his.”", "당신 정신은 그에게 노예가 되어 있소.\""]], [["“Ah, because he knew everything!”", "\"아, 그건 그이가 모든 걸 알았으니까요!\""], ["said she, with a triumphant simplicity of faith in Angel Clare that the most perfect man could hardly have deserved, much less her husband.", "그녀가, 아무리 완전한 사람이라도 받기 힘들었을, 하물며 그녀의 남편으로서는 더더욱 받기 힘들었을, 에인절 클레어에 대한 의기양양한 단순한 믿음으로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but you should not take negative opinions wholesale from another person like that.", "\"그렇지, 하지만 그렇게 남의 부정적인 견해를 통째로 받아들여서는 안 되오."], ["A pretty fellow he must be to teach you such scepticism!”", "당신한테 그런 회의주의를 가르치다니 어지간한 작자였겠군!\""]], [["“He never forced my judgement!", "\"그이는 내 판단을 강요한 적이 없어요!"], ["He would never argue on the subject with me!", "그이는 결코 그 문제로 나와 다투려 하지 않았어요!"], ["But I looked at it in this way; what he believed, after inquiring deep into doctrines, was much more likely to be right than what I might believe, who hadn’t looked into doctrines at all.”", "하지만 나는 이렇게 보았어요. 교리를 깊이 파고들어 본 끝에 그이가 믿은 것이, 교리를 전혀 들여다본 적도 없는 내가 믿을 만한 것보다 옳을 가능성이 훨씬 크다고요.\""]], [["“What used he to say?", "\"그가 뭐라고 말하곤 했소?"], ["He must have said something?”", "뭔가 말했을 것 아니오?\""]], [["She reflected; and with her acute memory for the letter of Angel Clare’s remarks, even when she did not comprehend their spirit, she recalled a merciless polemical syllogism that she had heard him use when, as it occasionally happened, he indulged in a species of thinking aloud with her at his side.", "그녀는 곰곰이 생각했다. 그리고 에인절 클레어의 말이라면 그 정신을 이해하지 못할 때조차 그 글자 하나하나를 예리하게 기억하는 능력으로, 이따금 그가 그녀를 곁에 두고 일종의 소리 내어 생각하기에 빠져들 때 그가 쓰던 어떤 가차 없는 논쟁적 삼단논법을 떠올렸다."], ["In delivering it she gave also Clare’s accent and manner with reverential faithfulness.", "그것을 옮기면서 그녀는 클레어의 말투와 태도까지도 경건한 충실함으로 함께 흉내 냈다."]], [["“Say that again,” asked d’Urberville, who had listened with the greatest attention.", "\"그걸 다시 말해 보오.\" 더없이 주의 깊게 듣고 있던 더버빌이 청했다."]], [["She repeated the argument, and d’Urberville thoughtfully murmured the words after her.", "그녀가 그 논증을 되풀이했고, 더버빌은 생각에 잠겨 그녀를 따라 그 말을 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Anything else?”", "\"다른 건 또 없소?\""], ["he presently asked.", "그가 이윽고 물었다."]], [["“He said at another time something like this”; and she gave another, which might possibly have been paralleled in many a work of the pedigree ranging from the _Dictionnaire Philosophique_ to Huxley’s _Essays_.", "\"또 언젠가는 이런 비슷한 말을 했어요.\" 그러고서 그녀는 다른 하나를 들려주었는데, 그것은 어쩌면 『철학 사전』에서부터 헉슬리의 『논문집』에 이르는 계보의 숱한 저작에서 그 짝을 찾을 수 있었을 법한 것이었다."]], [["“Ah—ha!", "\"아—하!"], ["How do you remember them?”", "그걸 어떻게 다 기억하오?\""]], [["“I wanted to believe what he believed, though he didn’t wish me to; and I managed to coax him to tell me a few of his thoughts.", "\"나는 그이가 믿는 걸 믿고 싶었어요. 그이는 내가 그러기를 바라지 않았는데도요. 그래서 그이를 구슬려서 그이 생각 몇 가지를 말하게 했어요."], ["I can’t say I quite understand that one; but I know it is right.”", "그 말은 내가 다 이해한다고는 못 하겠어요. 하지만 그게 옳다는 건 알아요.\""]], [["“H’m.", "\"흠."], ["Fancy your being able to teach me what you don’t know yourself!”", "당신 자신도 모르는 걸 나한테 가르칠 수 있다니, 참 기막히군!\""]], [["He fell into thought.", "그는 생각에 빠졌다."]], [["“And so I threw in my spiritual lot with his,”", "\"그래서 나는 내 영적인 운명을 그이의 것에 걸었어요.\""], ["she resumed.", "그녀가 다시 말했다."], ["“I didn’t wish it to be different.", "\"나는 그게 달라지기를 바라지 않았어요."], ["What’s good enough for him is good enough for me.”", "그이에게 충분히 좋은 거라면 나한테도 충분히 좋아요.\""]], [["“Does he know that you are as big an infidel as he?”", "\"그가, 당신도 자기만큼이나 대단한 이교도라는 걸 아오?\""]], [["“No—I never told him—if I am an infidel.”", "\"아니요—나는 그이한테 말한 적 없어요—내가 이교도라면 말이지만요.\""]], [["“Well—you are better off to-day that I am, Tess, after all!", "\"그래—따지고 보면, 테스, 당신은 오늘 나보다 형편이 낫소!"], ["You don’t believe that you ought to preach my doctrine, and, therefore, do no despite to your conscience in abstaining.", "당신은 내 교리를 설교해야 한다고 믿지 않으니, 그것을 삼간다 해서 양심에 거리낄 게 없소."], ["I do believe I ought to preach it, but, like the devils, I believe and tremble, for I suddenly leave off preaching it, and give way to my passion for you.” “How?”", "나는 그것을 설교해야 한다고 믿는데도, 마귀들처럼, 믿으면서 떠는 거요. 왜냐하면 나는 별안간 그 설교를 그만두고 당신을 향한 내 정념에 굴복해 버렸으니까.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"어떻게요?\""], ["he said aridly; “I have come all the way here to see you to-day!", "\"아니 글쎄,\" 그가 메마르게 말했다. \"오늘 당신을 보겠다고 이 먼 길을 다 왔단 말이오!"], ["But I started from home to go to Casterbridge Fair, where I have undertaken to preach the Word from a waggon at half-past two this afternoon, and where all the brethren are expecting me this minute.", "그런데 나는 캐스터브리지 장에 가려고 집을 나섰소. 거기서 오늘 오후 두 시 반에 짐마차 위에서 말씀을 설교하기로 약속이 되어 있고, 거기서 형제들 모두가 바로 이 순간 나를 기다리고 있소."], ["Here’s the announcement.”", "여기 그 공고문이 있소.\""]], [["He drew from his breast-pocket a poster whereon was printed the day, hour, and place of meeting, at which he, d’Urberville, would preach the Gospel as aforesaid.", "그가 가슴주머니에서 벽보 한 장을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 모임의 날짜와 시간과 장소가, 그리고 그 자리에서 그, 더버빌이 앞서 말한 대로 복음을 설교하리라는 것이 인쇄되어 있었다."]], [["“But how can you get there?” said Tess, looking at the clock.", "\"하지만 거기에 어떻게 가시려고요?\" 테스가 시계를 보며 말했다."]], [["“I cannot get there! I have come here.”", "\"갈 수가 없소! 나는 여기로 와 버렸으니까.\""]], [["“What, you have really arranged to preach, and—”", "\"뭐라고요, 정말로 설교하기로 약속을 해 놓고, 그런데—\""]], [["“I have arranged to preach, and I shall not be there—by reason of my burning desire to see a woman whom I once despised!—No, by my word and truth, I never despised you; if I had I should not love you now!", "\"설교하기로 약속을 해 놓고도, 거기 가지 않을 거요—한때 내가 멸시했던 한 여자를 보고 싶다는 불타는 욕망 때문에!—아니, 내 말과 진실을 두고 맹세하건대, 나는 당신을 멸시한 적이 없소. 멸시했다면 지금 당신을 사랑하지도 않을 거요!"], ["Why I did not despise you was on account of your being unsmirched in spite of all; you withdrew yourself from me so quickly and resolutely when you saw the situation; you did not remain at my pleasure; so there was one petticoat in the world for whom I had no contempt, and you are she.", "내가 당신을 멸시하지 않았던 건, 당신이 그 모든 일에도 더럽혀지지 않은 채로 남았기 때문이오. 당신은 상황을 알아채자 그토록 재빠르고 단호하게 내게서 물러섰고, 내 마음대로 곁에 머물러 있지 않았소. 그래서 세상에 내가 경멸하지 않은 치마 하나가 있었으니, 그게 바로 당신이오."], ["But you may well despise me now!", "하지만 이제는 당신이 나를 경멸해도 마땅하오!"], ["I thought I worshipped on the mountains, but I find I still serve in the groves! Ha! ha!”", "나는 산 위에서 경배한다고 여겼는데, 알고 보니 여전히 숲속 동산에서 섬기고 있구려! 하! 하!\""]], [["“O Alec d’Urberville!", "\"오 알렉 더버빌!"], ["what does this mean?", "이게 무슨 뜻이에요?"], ["What have I done!”", "내가 뭘 했다고요!\""]], [["“Done?”", "\"했다고?\""], ["he said, with a soulless sneer in the word.", "그가 그 말에 영혼 없는 비웃음을 담아 말했다."], ["“Nothing intentionally.", "\"일부러 한 건 아무것도 없소."], ["But you have been the means—the innocent means—of my backsliding, as they call it.", "하지만 당신은 내 타락의—그들 말로 '되돌아감'의—그 빌미가, 죄 없는 빌미가 된 거요."], ["I ask myself, am I, indeed, one of those ‘servants of corruption’ who, ‘after they have escaped the pollutions of the world, are again entangled therein and overcome’—whose latter end is worse than their beginning?”", "나는 나 자신에게 묻소. 나는 정말로, '세상의 더러움을 피한 후에 다시 거기 얽매여 정복당하는'—그 나중 형편이 처음보다 더 나빠지는—저 '썩어짐의 종들' 중 하나인가?\""], ["He laid his hand on her shoulder.", "그가 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹었다."], ["“Tess, my girl, I was on the way to, at least, social salvation till I saw you again!”", "\"테스, 내 아가씨, 나는 당신을 다시 보기 전까지는 적어도 사회적 구원에 이르는 길 위에 있었단 말이오!\""], ["he said freakishly shaking her, as if she were a child.", "그가 그녀를 마치 어린아이인 양 변덕스럽게 흔들며 말했다."], ["“And why then have you tempted me?", "\"그런데 어째서 당신은 나를 유혹한 거요?"], ["I was firm as a man could be till I saw those eyes and that mouth again—surely there never was such a maddening mouth since Eve’s!”", "나는 그 눈과 그 입을 다시 보기 전까지는 사람이 그럴 수 있는 한 굳건했소—분명 이브 이후로 그토록 사람을 미치게 하는 입은 없었을 거요!\""], ["His voice sank, and a hot archness shot from his own black eyes.", "그의 목소리가 잦아들었고, 그 자신의 검은 눈에서 뜨거운 교태가 쏘아져 나왔다."], ["“You temptress, Tess; you dear damned witch of Babylon—I could not resist you as soon as I met you again!”", "\"이 요부 같으니, 테스. 이 사랑스러운 빌어먹을 바빌론의 마녀 같으니—나는 당신을 다시 만나자마자 도무지 당신을 뿌리칠 수가 없었소!\""]], [["“I couldn’t help your seeing me again!”", "\"당신이 나를 다시 보게 된 걸 내가 어쩔 수는 없잖아요!\""], ["said Tess, recoiling.", "테스가 움찔 물러서며 말했다."]], [["“I know it—I repeat that I do not blame you.", "\"알아—거듭 말하지만 나는 당신을 탓하지 않소."], ["But the fact remains.", "하지만 사실은 사실이오."], ["When I saw you ill-used on the farm that day I was nearly mad to think that I had no legal right to protect you—that I could not have it; whilst he who has it seems to neglect you utterly!”", "그날 농장에서 당신이 함부로 부려지는 걸 보았을 때, 나는 당신을 보호할 법적 권리가 내게 없다는 생각에—그 권리를 가질 수 없다는 생각에 미칠 지경이었소. 한편 그 권리를 가진 자는 당신을 완전히 내버려 두는 것 같으니!\""]], [["“Don’t speak against him—he is absent!”", "\"그이를 헐뜯지 마세요—그이는 멀리 있단 말이에요!\""], ["she cried in much excitement.", "그녀가 몹시 흥분해서 외쳤다."], ["“Treat him honourably—he has never wronged you!", "\"그이를 정정당당하게 대해 주세요—그이는 당신에게 잘못한 적이 없어요!"], ["O leave his wife before any scandal spreads that may do harm to his honest name!”", "오, 그이의 정직한 이름에 해가 될 추문이 퍼지기 전에 그이의 아내에게서 떠나가 주세요!\""]], [["“I will—I will,”", "\"그러겠소—그러겠소.\""], ["he said, like a man awakening from a luring dream.", "그가 마치 유혹하는 꿈에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 말했다."], ["“I have broken my engagement to preach to those poor drunken boobies at the fair—it is the first time I have played such a practical joke.", "\"나는 장에서 그 가엾고 술 취한 얼간이들에게 설교하기로 한 약속을 깨 버렸소—이런 짓궂은 장난을 친 건 처음이오. 한 달 전이었다면 그런 일이 일어날 수도 있다는 데에 소름이 끼쳤을 거요."], ["A month ago I should have been horrified at such a possibility.", "나는 떠나겠소—맹세하리다—그리고—아, 내가 할 수 있을까!"], ["I’ll go away—to swear—and—ah, can I!", "멀찍이 떨어져 있겠다고.\""], ["to keep away.” Then, suddenly: “One clasp, Tessy—one!", "그러더니 별안간, \"한 번만 안아 봅시다, 테시—딱 한 번!"], ["Only for old friendship—”", "그저 옛정을 봐서—\""]], [["“I am without defence. Alec!", "\"나는 막아 낼 힘이 없어요, 알렉!"], ["A good man’s honour is in my keeping—think—be ashamed!”", "한 선한 사람의 명예가 내 보살핌에 달려 있어요—생각해 봐요—부끄러운 줄 아세요!\""]], [["“Pooh! Well, yes—yes!”", "\"쳇! 그래, 알았소—알았소!\""]], [["He clenched his lips, mortified with himself for his weakness.", "그는 자신의 나약함에 스스로 분이 나서 입술을 앙다물었다."], ["His eyes were equally barren of worldly and religious faith.", "그의 눈은 세속적 신앙도 종교적 신앙도 다 메말라 있었다."], ["The corpses of those old fitful passions which had lain inanimate amid the lines of his face ever since his reformation seemed to wake and come together as in a resurrection. He went out indeterminately.", "그가 회개한 이래 그의 얼굴 주름 사이에 생기 없이 누워 있던 그 옛 변덕스러운 정념의 송장들이, 부활하듯 깨어나 한데 모여드는 듯했다. 그는 마음을 정하지 못한 채 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Though d’Urberville had declared that this breach of his engagement to-day was the simple backsliding of a believer, Tess’s words, as echoed from Angel Clare, had made a deep impression upon him, and continued to do so after he had left her.", "오늘 자기 약속을 어긴 이 일이 신자의 단순한 되돌아감일 뿐이라고 더버빌은 단언했지만, 에인절 클레어의 말이 메아리쳐 나온 테스의 말은 그에게 깊은 인상을 남겼고, 그가 그녀를 떠난 뒤에도 계속 그러했다."], ["He moved on in silence, as if his energies were benumbed by the hitherto undreamt-of possibility that his position was untenable.", "그는 말없이 걸어 나갔는데, 마치 자기 입지가 더는 지탱될 수 없다는, 여태껏 꿈에도 생각지 못한 가능성에 그의 기력이 마비된 듯했다."], ["Reason had had nothing to do with his whimsical conversion, which was perhaps the mere freak of a careless man in search of a new sensation, and temporarily impressed by his mother’s death.", "이성은 그의 변덕스러운 회심과는 아무 상관이 없었으니, 그 회심은 어쩌면 새로운 자극을 찾던 한 무심한 남자의 한낱 변덕에 지나지 않았고, 어머니의 죽음에 일시적으로 감화된 것에 불과했다."]], [["The drops of logic Tess had let fall into the sea of his enthusiasm served to chill its effervescence to stagnation.", "테스가 그의 열정의 바다에 떨어뜨린 논리의 물방울들은 그 끓어오름을 식혀 고요한 정체로 만들어 버렸다."], ["He said to himself, as he pondered again and again over the crystallized phrases that she had handed on to him, “That clever fellow little thought that, by telling her those things, he might be paving my way back to her!”", "그녀가 자기에게 건네준 그 결정처럼 굳은 문구들을 거듭 곱씹으며 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"그 영리한 친구는, 그녀에게 그런 것들을 가르쳐 줌으로써 자기가 그녀에게로 돌아갈 내 길을 닦아 주고 있는 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐겠지!\""]], [["# XLVII", "# XLVII"]], [["It is the threshing of the last wheat-rick at Flintcomb-Ash farm.", "XLVII\n플린트콤애시 농장에서 마지막 밀 낟가리를 타작하는 날이다."], ["The dawn of the March morning is singularly inexpressive, and there is nothing to show where the eastern horizon lies.", "삼월 아침의 동틀 녘은 유난히 표정이 없어, 동쪽 지평선이 어디쯤 놓여 있는지조차 알 길이 없다."], ["Against the twilight rises the trapezoidal top of the stack, which has stood forlornly here through the washing and bleaching of the wintry weather.", "그 어스름을 배경으로 사다리꼴 모양의 낟가리 꼭대기가 솟아 있는데, 그것은 겨울 날씨에 씻기고 바래며 이곳에 쓸쓸히 서 있었다."]], [["When Izz Huett and Tess arrived at the scene of operations only a rustling denoted that others had preceded them; to which, as the light increased, there were presently added the silhouettes of two men on the summit.", "이즈 휴엇과 테스가 작업 현장에 다다랐을 때는, 바스락거리는 소리만이 다른 이들이 앞서 와 있음을 알려 줄 뿐이었다. 거기에, 빛이 밝아지면서 이윽고 꼭대기에 있는 두 남자의 실루엣이 더해졌다."], ["They were busily “unhaling” the rick, that is, stripping off the thatch before beginning to throw down the sheaves; and while this was in progress Izz and Tess, with the other women-workers, in their whitey-brown pinners, stood waiting and shivering, Farmer Groby having insisted upon their being on the spot thus early to get the job over if possible by the end of the day.", "그들은 낟가리를 \"벗기느라\", 곧 낟단을 던져 내리기에 앞서 이엉을 걷어 내느라 분주했다. 그 일이 진행되는 동안 이즈와 테스는 다른 여자 일꾼들과 함께, 희끄무레한 갈색 앞치마 차림으로 서서 기다리며 떨고 있었으니, 그로비 농장주가 되도록이면 하루 안에 일을 끝마치려고 그들이 이렇게 이른 시각에 현장에 나와 있어야 한다고 우긴 탓이었다."], ["Close under the eaves of the stack, and as yet barely visible, was the red tyrant that the women had come to serve—a timber-framed construction, with straps and wheels appertaining—the threshing-machine which, whilst it was going, kept up a despotic demand upon the endurance of their muscles and nerves.", "낟가리 처마 바로 밑에, 아직 거의 보이지 않게, 여자들이 와서 섬겨야 할 그 붉은 폭군이 있었다—가죽끈과 바퀴가 딸린 목제 틀의 구조물, 곧 타작기였는데, 그것은 돌아가는 동안 그들의 근육과 신경의 인내에 대해 폭군 같은 요구를 끊임없이 해 댔다."]], [["A little way off there was another indistinct figure; this one black, with a sustained hiss that spoke of strength very much in reserve.", "조금 떨어진 곳에 또 하나의 어렴풋한 형체가 있었다. 이쪽은 검었고, 한껏 비축된 힘을 말해 주는 듯한 끊임없는 쉭쉭 소리를 내고 있었다."], ["The long chimney running up beside an ash-tree, and the warmth which radiated from the spot, explained without the necessity of much daylight that here was the engine which was to act as the _primum mobile_ of this little world.", "물푸레나무 곁으로 길게 솟은 굴뚝과 그 자리에서 뿜어 나오는 온기는, 많은 햇빛이 필요하지도 않게, 이곳이 이 작은 세계의 으뜸 동력 노릇을 할 기관임을 설명해 주었다."], ["By the engine stood a dark, motionless being, a sooty and grimy embodiment of tallness, in a sort of trance, with a heap of coals by his side: it was the engine-man.", "그 기관 곁에는 검고 미동도 없는 한 존재가 서 있었는데, 그을음투성이에 때 낀, 큰 키를 한 화신이 일종의 무아지경에 빠진 채, 곁에 석탄 더미를 쌓아 두고 있었다."], ["The isolation of his manner and colour lent him the appearance of a creature from Tophet, who had strayed into the pellucid smokelessness of this region of yellow grain and pale soil, with which he had nothing in common, to amaze and to discompose its aborigines.", "그는 화부였다. 그의 태도와 빛깔의 고립감은 그에게 마치 토페트에서 온 피조물 같은 외양을 입혀 주었으니, 노란 곡식과 창백한 흙의 이 맑고 연기 없는 지역으로 잘못 흘러들어 와, 자기와는 아무런 공통점도 없는 이곳의 원주민들을 놀라게 하고 어지럽히는 것만 같았다."]], [["What he looked he felt. He was in the agricultural world, but not of it.", "그가 보이는 그대로 그는 느꼈다. 그는 농경의 세계 안에 있었으나, 그 세계에 속한 자는 아니었다."], ["He served fire and smoke; these denizens of the fields served vegetation, weather, frost, and sun.", "그는 불과 연기를 섬겼다. 들판의 이 거주민들은 초목과 날씨와 서리와 햇빛을 섬겼다."], ["He travelled with his engine from farm to farm, from county to county, for as yet the steam threshing-machine was itinerant in this part of Wessex.", "그는 자기 기관과 함께 농장에서 농장으로, 군에서 군으로 떠돌았으니, 아직 이 웨식스 지방에서 증기 타작기는 떠돌이 신세였기 때문이다."], ["He spoke in a strange northern accent; his thoughts being turned inwards upon himself, his eye on his iron charge, hardly perceiving the scenes around him, and caring for them not at all: holding only strictly necessary intercourse with the natives, as if some ancient doom compelled him to wander here against his will in the service of his Plutonic master.", "그는 낯선 북부 사투리로 말했고, 생각은 자기 자신에게로 향한 채, 눈은 자기 쇠붙이 피보호물에 두고서, 주위의 풍경은 거의 알아보지도 못했고 조금도 마음에 두지 않았다. 토착민들과는 오직 꼭 필요한 만큼만 교류했으니, 마치 어떤 오랜 운명의 저주가 그를 자기 뜻과 무관하게 이곳에서, 그의 명부의 주인을 섬기며 떠돌게 강요하는 것만 같았다."], ["The long strap which ran from the driving-wheel of his engine to the red thresher under the rick was the sole tie-line between agriculture and him.", "그의 기관 구동 바퀴에서 낟가리 밑의 붉은 타작기로 이어진 그 긴 가죽끈만이 농경과 그를 잇는 유일한 연결선이었다."]], [["While they uncovered the sheaves he stood apathetic beside his portable repository of force, round whose hot blackness the morning air quivered.", "그들이 낟단을 벗기는 동안 그는 자기의 휴대용 동력 저장고 곁에 무덤덤하게 서 있었고, 그 뜨거운 검정 둘레로 아침 공기가 일렁였다."], ["He had nothing to do with preparatory labour.", "그는 준비 작업과는 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["His fire was waiting incandescent, his steam was at high pressure, in a few seconds he could make the long strap move at an invisible velocity.", "그의 불은 백열로 달궈진 채 대기하고 있었고, 그의 증기는 고압에 차 있었으니, 몇 초 안에 그는 그 긴 가죽끈을 보이지 않는 속도로 움직이게 할 수 있었다."], ["Beyond its extent the environment might be corn, straw, or chaos; it was all the same to him.", "그 끈이 미치는 범위 너머의 환경이 곡식이든 짚이든 혼돈이든, 그에게는 다 매한가지였다."], ["If any of the autochthonous idlers asked him what he called himself, he replied shortly, “an engineer.”", "토박이 게으름뱅이 중 누구라도 그에게 너는 자신을 뭐라 부르느냐고 물으면, 그는 짤막하게 대답했다. \"기관사요.\""]], [["The rick was unhaled by full daylight; the men then took their places, the women mounted, and the work began.", "날이 완전히 밝아 낟가리의 벗김이 끝났다. 그러자 남자들이 제 자리를 잡았고, 여자들이 올라갔으며, 일이 시작되었다."], ["Farmer Groby—or, as they called him, “he”—had arrived ere this, and by his orders Tess was placed on the platform of the machine, close to the man who fed it, her business being to untie every sheaf of corn handed on to her by Izz Huett, who stood next, but on the rick; so that the feeder could seize it and spread it over the revolving drum, which whisked out every grain in one moment.", "그로비 농장주는—혹은 그들이 부르는 대로 \"그분\"은—이미 그 전에 도착해 있었고, 그의 지시로 테스는 기계의 발판 위, 낟단을 먹이는 남자 가까이에 배치되었다. 그녀가 할 일은 이즈 휴엇이 건네주는 곡식 낟단을 하나하나 푸는 것이었는데, 이즈는 그 옆에, 다만 낟가리 위에 서 있었다. 그래서 먹이는 사람이 그것을 움켜쥐어 돌아가는 통 위에 펼치면, 그 통이 한순간에 알곡을 모조리 털어 내는 것이었다."]], [["They were soon in full progress, after a preparatory hitch or two, which rejoiced the hearts of those who hated machinery.", "처음에 한두 번 걸리적거리는 일이 있어 기계를 싫어하는 자들의 마음을 흐뭇하게 한 뒤, 그들은 곧 한창 진행에 들어섰다."], ["The work sped on till breakfast time, when the thresher was stopped for half an hour; and on starting again after the meal the whole supplementary strength of the farm was thrown into the labour of constructing the straw-rick, which began to grow beside the stack of corn.", "일은 아침 식사 때까지 속도를 냈고, 그때 타작기가 반 시간 동안 멈췄다. 식사 후 다시 시작하면서, 농장의 보조 일손 전부가 짚가리를 쌓는 노동에 투입되었는데, 그 짚가리는 곡식 낟가리 곁에 자라나기 시작했다."], ["A hasty lunch was eaten as they stood, without leaving their positions, and then another couple of hours brought them near to dinner-time; the inexorable wheel continuing to spin, and the penetrating hum of the thresher to thrill to the very marrow all who were near the revolving wire-cage.", "점심은 자리를 뜨지 않고 선 채로 서둘러 먹었고, 그러고서 또 두어 시간이 흘러 저녁 식사 때가 가까워졌다. 가차 없는 바퀴는 줄곧 돌아갔고, 타작기의 파고드는 듯한 윙윙거림은 돌아가는 철망 우리 가까이에 있는 모든 이의 골수까지 떨리게 했다."]], [["The old men on the rising straw-rick talked of the past days when they had been accustomed to thresh with flails on the oaken barn-floor; when everything, even to winnowing, was effected by hand-labour, which, to their thinking, though slow, produced better results.", "쌓여 가는 짚가리 위의 노인들은, 떡갈나무 헛간 바닥에서 도리깨로 타작하곤 하던 옛날을 이야기했다. 키질에 이르기까지 모든 것이 손노동으로 이루어지던 시절을 말이다. 그들이 생각하기에 그것은 비록 더디기는 해도 더 나은 결과를 냈다."], ["Those, too, on the corn-rick talked a little; but the perspiring ones at the machine, including Tess, could not lighten their duties by the exchange of many words.", "곡식 낟가리 위의 사람들도 조금은 이야기를 나누었다. 그러나 테스를 포함해 기계에 붙어 땀 흘리는 이들은 여러 마디 말을 주고받으며 자기 일을 가볍게 할 수 없었다. 그녀를 그토록 호되게 시험한 것은 바로 그 일의 끊임없음이었고, 그것은 그녀로 하여금 차라리 플린트콤애시에 오지 않았더라면 하고 바라게 만들기 시작했다."], ["It was the ceaselessness of the work which tried her so severely, and began to make her wish that she had never come to Flintcomb-Ash.", "곡식 낟가리 위의 여자들은—특히 그중 하나인 마리안은—이따금 멈춰 서서 큰 병에 든 에일이나 차가운 차를 마시거나, 얼굴을 닦고 옷에서 짚과 겨 부스러기를 떨어내며 한담 몇 마디를 나눌 수 있었다."], ["The women on the corn-rick—Marian, who was one of them, in particular—could stop to drink ale or cold tea from the flagon now and then, or to exchange a few gossiping remarks while they wiped their faces or cleared the fragments of straw and husk from their clothing; but for Tess there was no respite; for, as the drum never stopped, the man who fed it could not stop, and she, who had to supply the man with untied sheaves, could not stop either, unless Marian changed places with her, which she sometimes did for half an hour in spite of Groby’s objections that she was too slow-handed for a feeder.", "그러나 테스에게는 한숨 돌릴 틈이 없었다. 통이 결코 멈추지 않으니 그것에 낟단을 먹이는 남자도 멈출 수 없었고, 그 남자에게 푼 낟단을 대 주어야 하는 그녀 역시 멈출 수 없었기 때문이다. 다만 마리안이 그녀와 자리를 바꿔 줄 때만은 예외였는데, 마리안은 먹이는 일을 하기에는 손이 너무 굼뜨다는 그로비의 반대에도 불구하고 이따금 반 시간씩 그렇게 해 주었다."]], [["For some probably economical reason it was usually a woman who was chosen for this particular duty, and Groby gave as his motive in selecting Tess that she was one of those who best combined strength with quickness in untying, and both with staying power, and this may have been true.", "아마도 어떤 경제적인 이유에서인지, 이 특정한 일에는 으레 여자가 뽑혔는데, 그로비가 테스를 고른 동기로 댄 것은, 그녀가 낟단을 푸는 데 힘과 빠르기를, 그리고 그 둘에 더해 지구력까지 가장 잘 겸비한 사람 중 하나라는 것이었고, 이는 사실이었는지도 모른다."], ["The hum of the thresher, which prevented speech, increased to a raving whenever the supply of corn fell short of the regular quantity.", "말소리를 막아 버리는 타작기의 윙윙거림은, 곡식 공급이 정해진 양에 못 미칠 때마다 미친 듯한 포효로 커졌다."], ["As Tess and the man who fed could never turn their heads she did not know that just before the dinner-hour a person had come silently into the field by the gate, and had been standing under a second rick watching the scene and Tess in particular.", "테스와 먹이는 남자는 결코 고개를 돌릴 수 없었던 터라, 저녁 식사 시간 바로 직전에 한 사람이 문으로 소리 없이 밭에 들어와 두 번째 낟가리 아래 서서 그 광경을, 특히 테스를 지켜보고 있었다는 것을 그녀는 알지 못했다."], ["He was dressed in a tweed suit of fashionable pattern, and he twirled a gay walking-cane.", "그는 유행하는 무늬의 트위드 양복을 입었고, 화려한 지팡이를 빙빙 돌리고 있었다."]], [["“Who is that?”", "\"저 사람 누구야?\""], ["said Izz Huett to Marian.", "이즈 휴엇이 마리안에게 말했다."], ["She had at first addressed the inquiry to Tess, but the latter could not hear it.", "그녀는 처음에는 그 물음을 테스에게 던졌으나, 테스는 그것을 들을 수 없었다."]], [["“Somebody’s fancy-man, I s’pose,”", "\"누구 정부겠지, 뭐.\""], ["said Marian laconically.", "마리안이 짤막하게 말했다."]], [["“I’ll lay a guinea he’s after Tess.”", "\"테스를 노리는 거라면 일 기니를 걸겠어.\""]], [["“O no.", "\"오 아니야."], ["’Tis a ranter pa’son who’s been sniffing after her lately; not a dandy like this.”", "요새 테스 꽁무니를 졸졸 따라다니던 그 떠벌이 목사잖아. 이런 멋쟁이가 아니라.\""]], [["“Well—this is the same man.”", "\"글쎄—저 사람이 그 사람이야.\""]], [["“The same man as the preacher?", "\"설교하던 그 사람이라고?"], ["But he’s quite different!”", "하지만 전혀 딴판인걸!\""]], [["“He hev left off his black coat and white neckercher, and hev cut off his whiskers; but he’s the same man for all that.”", "\"검은 외투랑 흰 목도리를 벗어 던지고, 구레나룻도 잘라 냈지만, 그래도 저 사람이 그 사람 맞아.\""]], [["“D’ye really think so? Then I’ll tell her,”", "\"정말 그렇게 생각해? 그럼 내가 테스한테 일러 줘야지.\""], ["said Marian.", "마리안이 말했다."]], [["“Don’t. She’ll see him soon enough, good-now.”", "\"하지 마. 곧 알아서 보게 될 텐데 뭐.\""]], [["“Well, I don’t think it at all right for him to join his preaching to courting a married woman, even though her husband mid be abroad, and she, in a sense, a widow.”", "\"글쎄, 설교를 하면서 한편으로 유부녀한테 구애를 하다니, 그게 도무지 옳은 일 같지가 않아. 비록 그 남편이 외국에 가 있고, 어떤 의미로는 그녀가 과부 신세나 마찬가지라 해도 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh—he can do her no harm,”", "\"오—그가 그녀를 어쩌지는 못해.\""], ["said Izz drily. “Her mind can no more be heaved from that one place where it do bide than a stooded waggon from the hole he’s in.", "이즈가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"수렁에 빠진 짐마차를 그 구덩이에서 끄집어낼 수 없는 것처럼, 그녀의 마음은 그것이 자리 잡은 그 한 곳에서 들어 올려질 수가 없어."], ["Lord love ’ee, neither court-paying, nor preaching, nor the seven thunders themselves, can wean a woman when ’twould be better for her that she should be weaned.”", "어머나 세상에, 구애도, 설교도, 일곱 천둥 그 자체도, 여자가 마음을 떼는 게 차라리 나을 그런 때에 그녀를 떼어 놓지는 못해.\""]], [["Dinner-time came, and the whirling ceased; whereupon Tess left her post, her knees trembling so wretchedly with the shaking of the machine that she could scarcely walk.", "저녁 식사 시간이 되어 윙윙거림이 멎었다. 그러자 테스는 제 자리를 떠났는데, 기계의 진동으로 무릎이 어찌나 비참하게 떨리던지 거의 걸을 수가 없었다."]], [["“You ought to het a quart o’ drink into ’ee, as I’ve done,”", "\"나처럼 술을 한 쿼트쯤 들이켰어야지.\" 마리안이 말했다."], ["said Marian. “You wouldn’t look so white then.", "\"그랬으면 그렇게 핼쑥해 보이진 않을 텐데."], ["Why, souls above us, your face is as if you’d been hagrode!”", "어쩜, 맙소사, 네 얼굴이 마녀한테 짓눌리기라도 한 것 같구나!\""]], [["It occurred to the good-natured Marian that, as Tess was so tired, her discovery of her visitor’s presence might have the bad effect of taking away her appetite; and Marian was thinking of inducing Tess to descend by a ladder on the further side of the stack when the gentleman came forward and looked up.", "마음씨 착한 마리안은, 테스가 이렇게 지친 마당에 자기를 찾아온 방문객의 존재를 알아채면 입맛이 달아나는 나쁜 영향을 받을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤다. 그래서 마리안이 낟가리 저편의 사다리로 테스를 내려가게 할까 궁리하고 있을 때, 그 신사가 앞으로 나와 위를 올려다보았다."]], [["Tess uttered a short little “Oh!”", "테스가 짧게 \"오!\" 하고 소리를 냈다."], ["And a moment after she said, quickly, “I shall eat my dinner here—right on the rick.”", "그리고 잠시 후 재빨리 말했다. \"나는 여기서 점심을 먹을래—바로 이 낟가리 위에서.\""]], [["Sometimes, when they were so far from their cottages, they all did this; but as there was rather a keen wind going to-day, Marian and the rest descended, and sat under the straw-stack.", "이따금 그들은 오두막에서 너무 멀리 떨어져 있을 때면 다들 이렇게 하곤 했다. 그러나 오늘은 제법 매서운 바람이 불고 있던 터라, 마리안과 나머지 사람들은 내려가서 짚가리 밑에 앉았다."]], [["The newcomer was, indeed, Alec d’Urberville, the late Evangelist, despite his changed attire and aspect.", "새로 온 사람은 정말로 알렉 더버빌, 곧 한때의 복음 전도자였다. 바뀐 옷차림과 외양에도 불구하고 말이다."], ["It was obvious at a glance that the original _Weltlust_ had come back; that he had restored himself, as nearly as a man could do who had grown three or four years older, to the old jaunty, slapdash guise under which Tess had first known her admirer, and cousin so-called.", "한눈에 보아도 원래의 그 세속적 욕망이 되돌아왔음이 분명했다. 그는 서너 살 더 먹은 남자가 할 수 있는 한 그대로, 테스가 그 흠모자이자 이른바 사촌이라는 자를 처음 알았을 때의 그 옛 멋스럽고 거침없는 모습으로 자신을 되돌려 놓았던 것이다."], ["Having decided to remain where she was, Tess sat down among the bundles, out of sight of the ground, and began her meal; till, by-and-by, she heard footsteps on the ladder, and immediately after Alec appeared upon the stack—now an oblong and level platform of sheaves.", "자기가 있던 자리에 그대로 있기로 정한 테스는, 땅에서 보이지 않게 낟단들 사이에 앉아 식사를 시작했다. 그러다 이윽고 사다리 위로 발소리가 들렸고, 곧이어 알렉이 낟가리 위에—이제 낟단들로 이루어진 길쭉하고 평평한 발판이 된 그곳에 나타났다."], ["He strode across them, and sat down opposite of her without a word.", "그는 그것들을 성큼성큼 가로질러, 한마디 말도 없이 그녀 맞은편에 앉았다."]], [["Tess continued to eat her modest dinner, a slice of thick pancake which she had brought with her.", "테스는 자기가 가져온 두툼한 팬케이크 한 조각, 그 변변찮은 점심을 계속 먹었다."], ["The other workfolk were by this time all gathered under the rick, where the loose straw formed a comfortable retreat.", "다른 일꾼들은 이때쯤 모두 낟가리 밑에 모여 있었는데, 거기서는 흐트러진 짚이 아늑한 은신처를 이루고 있었다."]], [["“I am here again, as you see,”", "\"보다시피 나 또 왔소.\""], ["said d’Urberville.", "더버빌이 말했다."]], [["“Why do you trouble me so!” she cried, reproach flashing from her very finger-ends.", "\"어쩌자고 나를 이렇게 괴롭히는 거예요!\" 그녀가 외쳤고, 바로 그 손끝에서 비난이 번뜩였다."]], [["“_I_ trouble _you_?", "\"내가 당신을 괴롭힌다고?"], ["I think I may ask, why do you trouble me?”", "도리어 내가 물어야 할 것 같소, 당신은 어쩌자고 나를 괴롭히는 거요?\""]], [["“Sure, I don’t trouble you any-when!”", "\"내가 언제 당신을 조금이라도 괴롭혔다고요!\""]], [["“You say you don’t?", "\"안 그런다고?"], ["But you do! You haunt me.", "하지만 괴롭히고 있소!"], ["Those very eyes that you turned upon me with such a bitter flash a moment ago, they come to me just as you showed them then, in the night and in the day!", "당신은 나를 따라다니며 사로잡소. 방금 그렇게 쓰라린 번뜩임으로 나를 향해 돌렸던 바로 그 눈, 그것이 그때 당신이 보여 준 그대로, 밤에도 낮에도 내게 찾아온단 말이오!"], ["Tess, ever since you told me of that child of ours, it is just as if my feelings, which have been flowing in a strong puritanical stream, had suddenly found a way open in the direction of you, and had all at once gushed through.", "테스, 당신이 우리의 그 아이 이야기를 내게 한 이후로, 마치 청교도적인 강한 물줄기로 흐르던 내 감정이 별안간 당신 쪽으로 트인 길을 찾아내어 단숨에 콸콸 쏟아져 버린 것만 같소."], ["The religious channel is left dry forthwith; and it is you who have done it!”", "종교의 물길은 그 즉시 메말라 버렸고, 그렇게 만든 건 바로 당신이오!\""]], [["She gazed in silence.", "그녀는 말없이 응시했다."]], [["“What—you have given up your preaching entirely?”", "\"뭐예요—설교를 아주 그만뒀단 말이에요?\""], ["she asked. She had gathered from Angel sufficient of the incredulity of modern thought to despise flash enthusiasm; but, as a woman, she was somewhat appalled.", "그녀가 물었다. 그녀는 에인절에게서 현대 사상의 회의를 충분히 받아들인 터라 들뜬 열광을 멸시했지만, 한 여자로서 다소 섬뜩하기도 했다."]], [["In affected severity d’Urberville continued—", "더버빌이 짐짓 엄숙한 척 말을 이었다—"]], [["“Entirely. I have broken every engagement since that afternoon I was to address the drunkards at Casterbridge Fair.", "\"아주 그만뒀소. 캐스터브리지 장에서 술꾼들에게 설교하기로 되어 있던 그날 오후 이후로 모든 약속을 깨 버렸소."], ["The deuce only knows what I am thought of by the brethren. Ah-ha!", "형제들이 나를 어떻게 생각하는지는 귀신이나 알겠지. 아하! 형제들이라!"], ["The brethren! No doubt they pray for me—weep for me; for they are kind people in their way.", "틀림없이 그들은 나를 위해 기도하고—나를 위해 울 거요. 그들은 나름대로 친절한 사람들이니까."], ["But what do I care?", "하지만 내가 무슨 상관이오?"], ["How could I go on with the thing when I had lost my faith in it?—it would have been hypocrisy of the basest kind!", "그것에 대한 믿음을 잃어버린 마당에 어떻게 그 일을 계속할 수 있겠소?—그건 가장 천한 종류의 위선이었을 거요!"], ["Among them I should have stood like Hymenaeus and Alexander, who were delivered over to Satan that they might learn not to blaspheme.", "그들 틈에서 나는 후메내오와 알렉산더처럼 서 있었을 테지. 신성모독하지 않는 법을 배우도록 사탄에게 넘겨진 그 자들 말이오."], ["What a grand revenge you have taken!", "당신은 참으로 멋진 복수를 한 거요!"], ["I saw you innocent, and I deceived you.", "나는 순결한 당신을 보았고, 당신을 속였소."], ["Four years after, you find me a Christian enthusiast; you then work upon me, perhaps to my complete perdition!", "사 년 뒤, 당신은 나를 기독교 광신자로 만나, 그러고는 나를 움직여서, 어쩌면 나를 완전한 멸망으로 몰아넣었지!"], ["But Tess, my coz, as I used to call you, this is only my way of talking, and you must not look so horribly concerned.", "하지만 테스, 내가 부르곤 하던 대로 내 사촌, 이건 그저 내 말버릇일 뿐이니, 그렇게 끔찍하게 걱정스러운 표정은 짓지 마오."], ["Of course you have done nothing except retain your pretty face and shapely figure.", "물론 당신은 그 예쁜 얼굴과 맵시 있는 몸매를 그대로 지니고 있은 것 말고는 아무것도 한 게 없소."], ["I saw it on the rick before you saw me—that tight pinafore-thing sets it off, and that wing-bonnet—you field-girls should never wear those bonnets if you wish to keep out of danger.”", "나는 당신이 나를 보기 전에 낟가리 위에 있는 당신을 보았소—그 꼭 끼는 앞치마 같은 것이 그 몸매를 돋보이게 하고, 그 날개 달린 듯한 보닛도 그렇고—당신네 들판 처녀들은 위험에서 벗어나 있고 싶거든 결코 그런 보닛을 써서는 안 되오.\""], ["He regarded her silently for a few moments, and with a short cynical laugh resumed: “I believe that if the bachelor-apostle, whose deputy I thought I was, had been tempted by such a pretty face, he would have let go the plough for her sake as I do!”", "그는 몇 순간 말없이 그녀를 바라보다가, 짧고 냉소적인 웃음과 함께 다시 입을 열었다. \"내가 그 대리인이라고 여겼던 그 독신의 사도조차도, 이런 예쁜 얼굴에 유혹당했더라면, 지금의 나처럼 그녀를 위해 쟁기를 놓아 버렸을 거라고 나는 믿소!\""]], [["Tess attempted to expostulate, but at this juncture all her fluency failed her, and without heeding he added:", "테스가 항변하려 했으나, 바로 이 대목에서 그녀의 모든 유창함이 사라져 버렸고, 그는 아랑곳없이 덧붙였다."]], [["“Well, this paradise that you supply is perhaps as good as any other, after all.", "\"뭐, 당신이 베푸는 이 낙원도 따지고 보면 어쩌면 다른 어떤 것 못지않게 좋은 거겠지."], ["But to speak seriously, Tess.”", "하지만 진지하게 말해서, 테스.\""], ["D’Urberville rose and came nearer, reclining sideways amid the sheaves, and resting upon his elbow.", "더버빌이 일어나 더 가까이 다가오더니, 낟단들 사이에 옆으로 비스듬히 기대 팔꿈치를 괴었다."], ["“Since I last saw you, I have been thinking of what you said that _he_ said.", "\"지난번에 당신을 본 이후로, 나는 당신이 그가 한 말이라며 들려준 그 말을 곰곰이 생각해 봤소."], ["I have come to the conclusion that there does seem rather a want of common-sense in these threadbare old propositions; how I could have been so fired by poor Parson Clare’s enthusiasm, and have gone so madly to work, transcending even him, I cannot make out!", "나는 이런 결론에 이르렀소. 이 진부하고 케케묵은 명제들에는 아무래도 상식이 좀 모자라는 것 같다고 말이오. 내가 어떻게 그 가엾은 클레어 목사의 열정에 그토록 불붙어서, 심지어 그분을 능가할 만큼 그렇게 미친 듯이 일에 매달릴 수 있었는지, 나는 도무지 알 수가 없소!"], ["As for what you said last time, on the strength of your wonderful husband’s intelligence—whose name you have never told me—about having what they call an ethical system without any dogma, I don’t see my way to that at all.”", "그리고 지난번에 당신이, 당신의 그 놀라운 남편의—이름은 한 번도 말해 주지 않은 그 남편의—지성을 근거 삼아 한 말, 아무 교리 없이도 이른바 윤리 체계라는 걸 가질 수 있다는 그 말에 대해서는, 나로서는 도무지 그 길이 보이질 않소.\""]], [["“Why, you can have the religion of loving-kindness and purity at least, if you can’t have—what do you call it—dogma.”", "\"아니, 적어도 사랑과 친절과 순결의 종교는 가질 수 있잖아요. 그—뭐라고 부르셨죠—교리는 못 가진다 해도요.\""]], [["“O no! I’m a different sort of fellow from that!", "\"오 아니! 나는 그런 부류와는 다른 사람이오!"], ["If there’s nobody to say, ‘Do this, and it will be a good thing for you after you are dead; do that, and it will be a bad thing for you,’ I can’t warm up.", "누군가가 '이걸 해라, 그러면 네가 죽은 뒤에 좋은 일이 있을 것이다. 저걸 해라, 그러면 네게 나쁜 일이 있을 것이다' 하고 말해 주는 이가 없다면, 나는 좀처럼 달아오르질 못하오."], ["Hang it, I am not going to feel responsible for my deeds and passions if there’s nobody to be responsible to; and if I were you, my dear, I wouldn’t either!”", "빌어먹을, 책임을 물을 상대가 없는데 내 행실과 정념에 대해 책임을 느낄 마음은 없소. 그리고 내가 당신이라면, 내 사랑, 나도 그러지 않겠소!\""]], [["She tried to argue, and tell him that he had mixed in his dull brain two matters, theology and morals, which in the primitive days of mankind had been quite distinct.", "그녀는 그의 둔한 머릿속에서 그가 두 가지를, 곧 신학과 도덕을 뒤섞었다고, 그것들은 인류의 원시 시대에는 전혀 별개의 것이었다고 따져 말하려 애썼다."], ["But owing to Angel Clare’s reticence, to her absolute want of training, and to her being a vessel of emotions rather than reasons, she could not get on.", "그러나 에인절 클레어의 과묵함 탓에, 그녀 자신이 받은 교육이 전무한 탓에, 그리고 그녀가 이성보다는 감정의 그릇인 탓에, 그녀는 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["“Well, never mind,” he resumed.", "\"뭐, 괜찮소.\" 그가 다시 말했다."], ["“Here I am, my love, as in the old times!”", "\"내가 여기 있잖소, 내 사랑, 옛 시절처럼 말이오!\""]], [["“Not as then—never as then—’tis different!”", "\"그때처럼은 아니에요—결코 그때처럼은 아니에요—이건 달라요!\""], ["she entreated.", "그녀가 간청했다."], ["“And there was never warmth with me!", "\"그리고 나한테는 한 번도 온기 같은 건 없었어요!"], ["O why didn’t you keep your faith, if the loss of it has brought you to speak to me like this!”", "오, 신앙을 잃은 게 당신을 나한테 이렇게 말하게 만든 거라면, 어째서 그 신앙을 지키지 않았어요!\""]], [["“Because you’ve knocked it out of me; so the evil be upon your sweet head!", "\"당신이 그걸 내게서 두들겨 빼 버렸으니까. 그러니 그 죄악이 당신의 그 예쁜 머리 위에 떨어지기를!"], ["Your husband little thought how his teaching would recoil upon him!", "당신 남편은 자기 가르침이 어떻게 자기에게 되돌아와 부딪칠지 꿈에도 몰랐겠지!"], ["Ha-ha—I’m awfully glad you have made an apostate of me all the same!", "하하—그래도 당신이 나를 배교자로 만들어 준 게 나는 끔찍이도 기쁘오!"], ["Tess, I am more taken with you than ever, and I pity you too.", "테스, 나는 그 어느 때보다 당신에게 마음을 빼앗겼고, 또 당신이 가엾기도 하오."], ["For all your closeness, I see you are in a bad way—neglected by one who ought to cherish you.”", "당신이 아무리 입을 다물어도, 나는 당신이 곤경에 처해 있는 걸 알겠소—마땅히 당신을 아껴 줘야 할 자에게서 버림받은 채로 말이오.\""]], [["She could not get her morsels of food down her throat; her lips were dry, and she was ready to choke.", "그녀는 음식 부스러기를 목구멍으로 넘길 수가 없었다. 입술이 바싹 말랐고, 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."], ["The voices and laughs of the workfolk eating and drinking under the rick came to her as if they were a quarter of a mile off.", "낟가리 밑에서 먹고 마시는 일꾼들의 목소리와 웃음소리가 마치 사분의 일 마일이나 떨어진 데서 들려오는 것처럼 그녀에게 닿았다."]], [["“It is cruelty to me!”", "\"이건 나한테 잔인한 짓이에요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“How—how can you treat me to this talk, if you care ever so little for me?”", "\"당신이 나를 조금이라도 위한다면, 어떻게—어떻게 나한테 이런 이야기를 늘어놓을 수가 있어요?\""]], [["“True, true,” he said, wincing a little.", "\"옳아, 옳은 말이오.\" 그가 조금 움찔하며 말했다."], ["“I did not come to reproach you for my deeds.", "\"내 행실 때문에 당신을 나무라려고 온 게 아니오."], ["I came Tess, to say that I don’t like you to be working like this, and I have come on purpose for you.", "테스, 나는 당신이 이렇게 일하는 게 싫다는 걸 말하러 온 거요. 일부러 당신을 위해 온 거란 말이오."], ["You say you have a husband who is not I.", "당신은 내가 아닌 남편이 있다고 하지."], ["Well, perhaps you have; but I’ve never seen him, and you’ve not told me his name; and altogether he seems rather a mythological personage.", "그래, 어쩌면 있겠지. 하지만 나는 그를 본 적이 없고, 당신은 그의 이름조차 말해 주지 않았소. 통틀어 보면 그는 아무래도 신화 속 인물 같단 말이오."], ["However, even if you have one, I think I am nearer to you than he is.", "하지만 설령 당신에게 남편이 있다 해도, 나는 그보다 내가 당신에게 더 가깝다고 생각하오."], ["I, at any rate, try to help you out of trouble, but he does not, bless his invisible face!", "적어도 나는 당신을 곤경에서 구하려고 애쓰는데, 그는 그러지 않으니, 그 보이지도 않는 얼굴에 복이나 있기를!"], ["The words of the stern prophet Hosea that I used to read come back to me.", "내가 읽곤 하던 준엄한 예언자 호세아의 말이 떠오르는구려."], ["Don’t you know them, Tess?—‘And she shall follow after her lover, but she shall not overtake him; and she shall seek him, but shall not find him; then shall she say, I will go and return to my first husband; for then was it better with me than now!’ ... Tess, my trap is waiting just under the hill, and—darling mine, not his!—you know the rest.”", "모르오, 테스?—'그가 그 연인들을 따라갈지라도 미치지 못하며, 그들을 찾을지라도 만나지 못하리니, 그제야 그가 이르기를, 내가 가서 내 본 남편에게로 돌아가리라, 그때의 내 형편이 지금보다 나았음이라!'… 테스, 내 마차가 바로 저 언덕 아래에서 기다리고 있고, 그리고—내 사랑이여, 그의 사랑이 아니라!—나머지는 당신도 알 거요.\""]], [["Her face had been rising to a dull crimson fire while he spoke; but she did not answer.", "그가 말하는 동안 그녀의 얼굴은 둔탁한 진홍빛 불길로 달아올랐다. 그러나 그녀는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“You have been the cause of my backsliding,”", "\"당신은 내 타락의 원인이었소.\""], ["he continued, stretching his arm towards her waist; “you should be willing to share it, and leave that mule you call husband for ever.”", "그가 팔을 그녀의 허리 쪽으로 뻗으며 말을 이었다. \"그러니 당신은 기꺼이 그 타락을 함께 나눠야 하고, 당신이 남편이라 부르는 그 노새 같은 자를 영영 떠나야 하오.\""]], [["One of her leather gloves, which she had taken off to eat her skimmer-cake, lay in her lap, and without the slightest warning she passionately swung the glove by the gauntlet directly in his face.", "스키머케이크를 먹으려고 벗어 두었던 그녀의 가죽 장갑 한 짝이 그녀의 무릎 위에 놓여 있었는데, 그녀는 아무런 예고도 없이 그 장갑의 손목 부분을 잡고 격하게 휘둘러 곧장 그의 얼굴에 내리쳤다."], ["It was heavy and thick as a warrior’s, and it struck him flat on the mouth.", "그것은 전사의 장갑처럼 무겁고 두꺼웠고, 그의 입을 정통으로 후려쳤다."], ["Fancy might have regarded the act as the recrudescence of a trick in which her armed progenitors were not unpractised.", "상상력을 발휘한다면, 그 행동을 무장한 그녀의 조상들이 적잖이 익혔던 어떤 솜씨가 되살아난 것으로 볼 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["Alec fiercely started up from his reclining position.", "알렉이 기대 누운 자세에서 사납게 벌떡 일어났다."], ["A scarlet oozing appeared where her blow had alighted, and in a moment the blood began dropping from his mouth upon the straw.", "그녀의 가격이 떨어진 자리에 진홍빛 핏방울이 배어 나왔고, 이내 피가 그의 입에서 짚 위로 떨어지기 시작했다."], ["But he soon controlled himself, calmly drew his handkerchief from his pocket, and mopped his bleeding lips.", "그러나 그는 곧 자신을 다잡고, 차분히 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 피 흐르는 입술을 닦았다."]], [["She too had sprung up, but she sank down again.", "그녀 역시 벌떡 일어섰으나, 다시 주저앉았다."], ["“Now, punish me!”", "\"자, 나를 벌하세요!\""], ["she said, turning up her eyes to him with the hopeless defiance of the sparrow’s gaze before its captor twists its neck.", "그녀가, 잡아 죽이려는 자에게 목이 비틀리기 직전 참새가 던지는 그 절망적인 반항의 눈길로, 그를 향해 눈을 치켜뜨며 말했다."], ["“Whip me, crush me; you need not mind those people under the rick!", "\"나를 채찍질하든 짓뭉개든 하세요. 낟가리 밑에 있는 저 사람들 따위는 신경 쓸 것 없어요!"], ["I shall not cry out.", "나는 비명도 지르지 않을 테니까."], ["Once victim, always victim—that’s the law!”", "한번 희생물이면 영영 희생물—그게 법칙이니까!\""]], [["“O no, no, Tess,”", "\"오 아니, 아니오, 테스.\""], ["he said blandly.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“I can make full allowance for this.", "\"나는 이건 충분히 너그러이 봐줄 수 있소."], ["Yet you most unjustly forget one thing, that I would have married you if you had not put it out of my power to do so.", "하지만 당신은 한 가지를 너무도 부당하게 잊고 있구려. 당신이 내가 그럴 수 있는 길을 막아 버리지만 않았더라면 나는 당신과 결혼했을 거라는 사실 말이오."], ["Did I not ask you flatly to be my wife—hey?", "내가 대놓고 내 아내가 되어 달라고 청하지 않았소—응?"], ["Answer me.”", "대답해 보오.\""]], [["“You did.”", "\"청했죠.\""]], [["“And you cannot be. But remember one thing!”", "\"그런데 당신은 그럴 수 없다."], ["His voice hardened as his temper got the better of him with the recollection of his sincerity in asking her and her present ingratitude, and he stepped across to her side and held her by the shoulders, so that she shook under his grasp.", "하지만 한 가지는 기억해 두오!\" 그가 그녀에게 청혼하던 자기 진심과 그녀의 지금의 배은망덕을 떠올리며 노여움이 그를 사로잡자 목소리가 굳어졌고, 그는 그녀 곁으로 성큼 다가가 어깨를 붙들었으니, 그의 손아귀 아래 그녀가 부르르 떨렸다."], ["“Remember, my lady, I was your master once!", "\"기억해 두오, 나의 마님, 한때 나는 당신의 주인이었소!"], ["I will be your master again.", "나는 다시 당신의 주인이 될 거요."], ["If you are any man’s wife you are mine!”", "당신이 어떤 남자의 아내라면, 당신은 내 것이오!\""]], [["The threshers now began to stir below.", "이제 아래에서 타작꾼들이 움직이기 시작했다."]], [["“So much for our quarrel,”", "\"우리 다툼은 이쯤 해 두고.\""], ["he said, letting her go.", "그가 그녀를 놓아주며 말했다."], ["“Now I shall leave you, and shall come again for your answer during the afternoon.", "\"이제 나는 당신을 떠나겠소. 그리고 오후 중에 당신 대답을 들으러 다시 오겠소."], ["You don’t know me yet!", "당신은 아직 나를 몰라!"], ["But I know you.”", "하지만 나는 당신을 알지.\""]], [["She had not spoken again, remaining as if stunned. D’Urberville retreated over the sheaves, and descended the ladder, while the workers below rose and stretched their arms, and shook down the beer they had drunk.", "그녀는 다시 입을 열지 않은 채, 넋이 나간 듯 그대로 있었다. 더버빌은 낟단들을 가로질러 물러나 사다리를 내려갔고, 그동안 아래의 일꾼들은 일어나 팔을 쭉 뻗으며 마신 맥주를 흔들어 내렸다."], ["Then the threshing-machine started afresh; and amid the renewed rustle of the straw Tess resumed her position by the buzzing drum as one in a dream, untying sheaf after sheaf in endless succession.", "그러고서 타작기가 다시 돌기 시작했다. 그리고 새로이 바스락거리는 짚 소리 한가운데서 테스는 꿈속에 잠긴 사람처럼 윙윙거리는 통 곁 제 자리로 돌아가, 낟단을 하나하나 끝없이 이어 풀었다."]], [["# XLVIII", "# XLVIII"]], [["In the afternoon the farmer made it known that the rick was to be finished that night, since there was a moon by which they could see to work, and the man with the engine was engaged for another farm on the morrow.", "XLVIII\n오후에 농장주는 그날 밤 안에 낟가리를 끝내야 한다고 알렸으니, 일하는 것을 볼 수 있을 만한 달이 떠 있는 데다, 기관을 다루는 남자가 이튿날 다른 농장에 고용되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Hence the twanging and humming and rustling proceeded with even less intermission than usual.", "그리하여 윙윙거리고 웅웅거리고 바스락거리는 소리가 평소보다도 더 끊임없이 이어졌다."]], [["It was not till “nammet”-time, about three o’clock, that Tess raised her eyes and gave a momentary glance round.", "테스가 눈을 들어 잠시 둘레를 둘러본 것은 \"나밋\" 시간, 곧 세 시쯤이 되어서였다."], ["She felt but little surprise at seeing that Alec d’Urberville had come back, and was standing under the hedge by the gate.", "알렉 더버빌이 돌아와 문 옆 산울타리 아래 서 있는 것을 보고도 그녀는 거의 놀라지 않았다."], ["He had seen her lift her eyes, and waved his hand urbanely to her, while he blew her a kiss.", "그는 그녀가 눈을 드는 것을 보고는, 정중하게 손을 흔들며 그녀에게 입맞춤을 날렸다."], ["It meant that their quarrel was over.", "그것은 둘의 다툼이 끝났다는 뜻이었다."], ["Tess looked down again, and carefully abstained from gazing in that direction.", "테스는 다시 눈을 내리깔고, 조심스럽게\n안 보려고 그 방향으로는 눈길을 주지 않았다."]], [["Thus the afternoon dragged on.", "이렇게 오후는 더디게 흘러갔다."], ["The wheat-rick shrank lower, and the straw-rick grew higher, and the corn-sacks were carted away.", "밀 더미는 점점 낮아졌고, 짚 더미는 점점 높아졌으며, 곡식 자루들은 수레에 실려 옮겨졌다."], ["At six o’clock the wheat-rick was about shoulder-high from the ground.", "여섯 시에 밀 더미는 땅에서 어깨 높이쯤으로 내려앉았다."], ["But the unthreshed sheaves remaining untouched seemed countless still, notwithstanding the enormous numbers that had been gulped down by the insatiable swallower, fed by the man and Tess, through whose two young hands the greater part of them had passed.", "그러나 손도 대지 않은 채 남은 타작 전의 밀단들은, 게걸스러운 삼키는 기계가 그토록 엄청난 양을 꿀꺽 삼켰는데도, 여전히 셀 수 없이 많아 보였다. 그 기계에 먹이를 댄 것은 그 남자와 테스였고, 대부분은 테스의 두 젊은 손을 거쳐 들어갔다."], ["And the immense stack of straw where in the morning there had been nothing, appeared as the _faeces_ of the same buzzing red glutton.", "그리고 아침에는 아무것도 없던 자리에 솟아오른 거대한 짚 더미는, 윙윙대는 그 붉은 탐식가가 싸 놓은 배설물처럼 보였다."], ["From the west sky a wrathful shine—all that wild March could afford in the way of sunset—had burst forth after the cloudy day, flooding the tired and sticky faces of the threshers, and dyeing them with a coppery light, as also the flapping garments of the women, which clung to them like dull flames.", "서쪽 하늘에서는 노한 듯한 빛살이—거친 삼월이 일몰로 내줄 수 있는 전부였다—구름 끼었던 하루 끝에 터져 나와, 타작꾼들의 지치고 끈적이는 얼굴 위로 쏟아져 그것을 구릿빛으로 물들였고, 여자들의 펄럭이는 옷자락도 마찬가지로 물들여 흐릿한 불꽃처럼 몸에 달라붙게 했다."]], [["A panting ache ran through the rick.", "헐떡이는 통증이 더미 전체로 번졌다."], ["The man who fed was weary, and Tess could see that the red nape of his neck was encrusted with dirt and husks.", "기계에 먹이를 대던 남자는 지쳐 있었고, 테스는 그의 붉은 목덜미가 흙먼지와 겉껍질로 더께져 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["She still stood at her post, her flushed and perspiring face coated with the corndust, and her white bonnet embrowned by it.", "테스는 여전히 제자리를 지키고 섰고, 상기되어 땀에 젖은 얼굴은 곡식 먼지를 뒤집어썼으며, 흰 보닛은 그 먼지로 거뭇하게 물들었다."], ["She was the only woman whose place was upon the machine so as to be shaken bodily by its spinning, and the decrease of the stack now separated her from Marian and Izz, and prevented their changing duties with her as they had done.", "기계 위에 자리를 잡고 그 회전에 온몸을 흔들리는 여자는 테스뿐이었고, 더미가 낮아진 탓에 이제 메리언과 이즈와는 떨어져, 전처럼 임무를 서로 바꿔 줄 수도 없게 되었다."], ["The incessant quivering, in which every fibre of her frame participated, had thrown her into a stupefied reverie in which her arms worked on independently of her consciousness.", "온몸의 섬유 하나하나까지 동참하는 끊임없는 떨림은 그녀를 멍한 공상 속으로 몰아넣었고, 그 안에서 두 팔은 의식과 따로 노는 듯 움직였다."], ["She hardly knew where she was, and did not hear Izz Huett tell her from below that her hair was tumbling down.", "그녀는 자기가 어디 있는지조차 거의 알지 못했고, 아래에서 이즈 휴엣이 머리가 흘러내린다고 일러 주는 소리도 듣지 못했다."]], [["By degrees the freshest among them began to grow cadaverous and saucer-eyed.", "점점 그들 가운데 가장 생생하던 이들조차 송장처럼 핼쑥해지고 눈이 움푹 패기 시작했다."], ["Whenever Tess lifted her head she beheld always the great upgrown straw-stack, with the men in shirt-sleeves upon it, against the gray north sky; in front of it the long red elevator like a Jacob’s ladder, on which a perpetual stream of threshed straw ascended, a yellow river running uphill, and spouting out on the top of the rick.", "테스가 고개를 들 때마다 보이는 것은 언제나 크게 솟아오른 짚 더미였고, 그 위에 셔츠 바람의 남자들이 잿빛 북쪽 하늘을 등지고 서 있었다. 그 앞에는 야곱의 사다리 같은 길고 붉은 컨베이어가 놓여, 타작된 짚이 끊임없이 흐르며 올라갔다. 언덕을 거꾸로 거슬러 오르는 노란 강이 더미 꼭대기에서 뿜어져 나왔다."], ["She knew that Alec d’Urberville was still on the scene, observing her from some point or other, though she could not say where.", "테스는 알렉 더버빌이 여전히 그 자리에 있어 어디선가 자기를 지켜보고 있음을 알았다. 다만 어디인지는 말할 수 없었다."], ["There was an excuse for his remaining, for when the threshed rick drew near its final sheaves a little ratting was always done, and men unconnected with the threshing sometimes dropped in for that performance—sporting characters of all descriptions, gents with terriers and facetious pipes, roughs with sticks and stones.", "그가 머물 핑계는 있었다. 타작이 끝나가며 마지막 밀단들에 가까워지면 늘 쥐잡이가 조금 벌어졌고, 타작과는 상관없는 사람들이 그 구경을 하러 가끔 끼어들었기 때문이다. 온갖 부류의 놀이꾼들, 테리어와 익살스러운 파이프를 든 신사들, 막대기와 돌멩이를 든 거친 사내들이었다."]], [["But there was another hour’s work before the layer of live rats at the base of the stack would be reached; and as the evening light in the direction of the Giant’s Hill by Abbot’s-Cernel dissolved away, the white-faced moon of the season arose from the horizon that lay towards Middleton Abbey and Shottsford on the other side.", "그러나 더미 바닥에 살아 있는 쥐 층에 닿으려면 한 시간은 더 일해야 했다. 그리고 애벗스서널 옆 자이언츠힐 쪽의 저녁 빛이 스러져 가자, 반대편 미들턴 수도원과 쇼츠퍼드 쪽에 누운 지평선에서 그 계절의 하얀 얼굴을 한 달이 떠올랐다."], ["For the last hour or two Marian had felt uneasy about Tess, whom she could not get near enough to speak to, the other women having kept up their strength by drinking ale, and Tess having done without it through traditionary dread, owing to its results at her home in childhood.", "지난 한두 시간 동안 메리언은 테스가 마음에 걸렸으나, 가까이 다가가 말을 건넬 수가 없었다. 다른 여자들은 에일을 마셔 기운을 지탱했지만, 테스는 어린 시절 집안에서 본 그 결과 탓에 대대로 내려온 두려움 때문에 마시지 않고 버텼던 것이다."], ["But Tess still kept going: if she could not fill her part she would have to leave; and this contingency, which she would have regarded with equanimity and even with relief a month or two earlier, had become a terror since d’Urberville had begun to hover round her.", "그래도 테스는 여전히 일을 계속했다. 제 몫을 채우지 못하면 자리를 떠나야 했으니까. 한두 달 전이었다면 그 가능성을 담담하게, 아니 오히려 안도하며 바라봤겠지만, 더버빌이 그녀 주위를 맴돌기 시작한 뒤로는 그것이 공포가 되어 버렸다."]], [["The sheaf-pitchers and feeders had now worked the rick so low that people on the ground could talk to them.", "밀단을 던져 올리는 이들과 먹이를 대는 이들이 이제 더미를 어찌나 낮춰 놓았던지, 땅에 선 사람들이 그들과 말을 주고받을 수 있게 되었다."], ["To Tess’s surprise Farmer Groby came up on the machine to her, and said that if she desired to join her friend he did not wish her to keep on any longer, and would send somebody else to take her place. The “friend”", "테스가 놀랍게도 농장주 그로비가 기계 위로 올라와 그녀에게 다가오더니, 친구와 합류하고 싶다면 더는 일을 계속할 필요가 없으며, 그녀 자리를 대신할 다른 사람을 보내 주겠다고 말했다."], ["was d’Urberville, she knew, and also that this concession had been granted in obedience to the request of that friend, or enemy.", "그 \"친구\"란 더버빌임을 그녀는 알았고, 또 이 양보가 그 친구—아니 적—의 청에 따라 베풀어진 것임도 알았다."], ["She shook her head and toiled on.", "그녀는 고개를 저으며 계속 고되게 일했다."]], [["The time for the rat-catching arrived at last, and the hunt began.", "마침내 쥐잡이 시간이 찾아왔고, 사냥이 시작됐다."], ["The creatures had crept downwards with the subsidence of the rick till they were all together at the bottom, and being now uncovered from their last refuge, they ran across the open ground in all directions, a loud shriek from the by-this-time half-tipsy Marian informing her companions that one of the rats had invaded her person—a terror which the rest of the women had guarded against by various schemes of skirt-tucking and self-elevation.", "그 짐승들은 더미가 가라앉음에 따라 차차 아래로 기어 내려와 바닥에 다 모여 있다가, 이제 마지막 피난처가 걷히자 사방으로 빈 땅을 가로질러 내달렸다. 이쯤에는 반쯤 거나하게 취한 메리언이 날카로운 비명을 질러 동료들에게 쥐 한 마리가 제 몸으로 침입했음을 알렸다—나머지 여자들은 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고 몸을 들어 올리는 갖가지 방책으로 그 공포를 막아 두었던 터였다."], ["The rat was at last dislodged, and, amid the barking of dogs, masculine shouts, feminine screams, oaths, stampings, and confusion as of Pandemonium, Tess untied her last sheaf; the drum slowed, the whizzing ceased, and she stepped from the machine to the ground.", "마침내 쥐가 떨려 나왔고, 개들이 짖고 사내들이 고함치고 여자들이 비명 지르고 욕설과 발구르는 소리와 아수라장 같은 아비규환 속에서, 테스는 마지막 밀단을 풀었다. 드럼이 느려지고 윙윙거림이 멎자, 그녀는 기계에서 내려와 땅을 디뎠다."]], [["Her lover, who had only looked on at the rat-catching, was promptly at her side.", "쥐잡이를 구경만 하고 있던 그녀의 연인이 곧장 그녀 곁에 와 있었다."]], [["“What—after all—my insulting slap, too!”", "\"뭐예요—그래도—내가 모욕적으로 따귀까지 때렸는데도요!\""], ["said she in an underbreath.", "그녀가 숨죽인 목소리로 말했다."], ["She was so utterly exhausted that she had not strength to speak louder.", "너무도 기진맥진해서 더 큰 소리로 말할 기운이 없었다."]], [["“I should indeed be foolish to feel offended at anything you say or do,”", "\"네가 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 거기에 기분 상한다면, 나야말로 정말 어리석은 거지.\""], ["he answered, in the seductive voice of the Trantridge time.", "그가 트랜트리지 시절의 그 유혹하는 목소리로 답했다."], ["“How the little limbs tremble!", "\"이 작은 팔다리가 어찌나 떨리는지!"], ["You are as weak as a bled calf, you know you are; and yet you need have done nothing since I arrived.", "너는 피 뽑힌 송아지처럼 약해졌어, 너도 알잖니. 게다가 내가 온 뒤로는 아무것도 안 해도 됐는데."], ["How could you be so obstinate?", "어쩜 그렇게 고집을 부려?"], ["However, I have told the farmer that he has no right to employ women at steam-threshing.", "어쨌든 나는 농장주한테 증기 타작에 여자를 쓸 권리가 없다고 말해 뒀어."], ["It is not proper work for them; and on all the better class of farms it has been given up, as he knows very well.", "여자한테 맞는 일이 아니야. 좀 나은 농장에서는 다 그만뒀다고, 그자도 잘 알면서."], ["I will walk with you as far as your home.”", "너희 집까지 같이 걸어갈게.\""]], [["“O yes,” she answered with a jaded gait.", "\"아, 그래요.\" 그녀가 지친 걸음으로 답했다."], ["“Walk wi’ me if you will!", "\"그러고 싶으면 같이 걸어요!"], ["I do bear in mind that you came to marry me before you knew o’ my state.", "당신이 내 처지를 알기 전에 나와 결혼하러 왔던 건 잊지 않고 있어요."], ["Perhaps—perhaps you are a little better and kinder than I have been thinking you were.", "어쩌면—어쩌면 당신은 내가 생각했던 것보다 조금은 더 낫고 친절한 사람인지도 몰라요."], ["Whatever is meant as kindness I am grateful for; whatever is meant in any other way I am angered at.", "친절로 한 일이라면 무엇이든 고맙게 받을게요. 다른 뜻으로 한 일이라면 무엇이든 화가 나요."], ["I cannot sense your meaning sometimes.”", "가끔은 당신 속뜻을 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“If I cannot legitimize our former relations at least I can assist you.", "\"우리의 옛 관계를 정당하게 만들 수 없다면, 적어도 너를 도와줄 수는 있어."], ["And I will do it with much more regard for your feelings than I formerly showed.", "그리고 예전에 보였던 것보다 훨씬 더 네 마음을 헤아리면서 그렇게 할 거야."], ["My religious mania, or whatever it was, is over.", "내 종교적 광기인지 뭔지는 이제 끝났어."], ["But I retain a little good nature; I hope I do.", "하지만 약간의 선한 마음은 남아 있지. 그러길 바라."], ["Now, Tess, by all that’s tender and strong between man and woman, trust me!", "자, 테스, 남자와 여자 사이의 모든 다정하고 굳은 것을 걸고 말하건대, 나를 믿어!"], ["I have enough and more than enough to put you out of anxiety, both for yourself and your parents and sisters.", "나는 너를 걱정에서 벗어나게 해 줄 만큼, 아니 그 이상으로 가진 게 있어. 너 자신을 위해서도, 네 부모와 동생들을 위해서도."], ["I can make them all comfortable if you will only show confidence in me.”", "네가 나를 믿어 주기만 한다면 그들 모두를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있어.\""]], [["“Have you seen ’em lately?”", "\"요즘 그 애들을 봤어요?\""], ["she quickly inquired.", "그녀가 재빨리 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"응."], ["They didn’t know where you were.", "그들은 네가 어디 있는지 몰랐어."], ["It was only by chance that I found you here.”", "내가 여기서 너를 찾은 건 순전히 우연이었지.\""]], [["The cold moon looked aslant upon Tess’s fagged face between the twigs of the garden-hedge as she paused outside the cottage which was her temporary home, d’Urberville pausing beside her.", "차가운 달이 정원 울타리 잔가지 사이로 테스의 지친 얼굴을 비스듬히 비추었다. 그녀가 임시 거처인 오두막 밖에 멈춰 서자, 더버빌도 그 곁에 멈췄다."]], [["“Don’t mention my little brothers and sisters—don’t make me break down quite!”", "\"내 어린 동생들 얘긴 꺼내지 마요—나를 아주 무너지게 하지 마요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“If you want to help them—God knows they need it—do it without telling me.", "\"그 애들을 돕고 싶다면—하느님은 그 애들이 도움이 필요한 걸 아시죠—나한테 말하지 말고 그냥 해 줘요."], ["But no, no!”", "아니, 아니에요!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“I will take nothing from you, either for them or for me!”", "\"그 애들을 위해서든 나를 위해서든, 당신한테서는 아무것도 받지 않을 거예요!\""]], [["He did not accompany her further, since, as she lived with the household, all was public indoors.", "그는 더는 그녀를 따라가지 않았다. 그녀가 한 집안 식구와 함께 사는 처지라, 집 안에서는 모든 게 다 드러나기 때문이었다."], ["No sooner had she herself entered, laved herself in a washing-tub, and shared supper with the family than she fell into thought, and withdrawing to the table under the wall, by the light of her own little lamp wrote in a passionate mood—", "그녀는 안으로 들어서서 빨래 통에 몸을 씻고 식구들과 저녁을 나눠 먹자마자 생각에 잠겼고, 벽 밑 탁자로 물러나 자기 작은 등불 빛에 의지해 격정에 휩싸인 마음으로 글을 써 내려갔다—"]], [["My own Husband,", "나의 남편에게,"]], [["Let me call you so—I must—even if it makes you angry to think of such an unworthy wife as I.", "당신을 이렇게 부르게 해 주세요—그래야만 해요—나 같은 보잘것없는 아내를 떠올리는 게 당신을 화나게 한다 해도요."], ["I must cry to you in my trouble—I have no one else!", "곤경에 빠진 나는 당신에게 부르짖을 수밖에 없어요—내겐 다른 사람이 없으니까요!"], ["I am so exposed to temptation, Angel.", "나는 너무도 유혹에 노출되어 있어요, 에인절."], ["I fear to say who it is, and I do not like to write about it at all.", "그게 누구인지 말하기가 두렵고, 그것에 대해 글로 쓰는 것조차 내키지 않아요."], ["But I cling to you in a way you cannot think!", "그래도 나는 당신이 상상도 못 할 만큼 당신에게 매달리고 있어요!"], ["Can you not come to me now, at once, before anything terrible happens?", "무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지기 전에, 지금 당장 나에게 와 줄 수는 없나요?"], ["O, I know you cannot, because you are so far away!", "아, 당신이 그럴 수 없다는 걸 알아요, 그토록 멀리 있으니까요!"], ["I think I must die if you do not come soon, or tell me to come to you.", "당신이 곧 오지 않거나 당신에게 오라고 말해 주지 않으면 나는 죽을 것만 같아요."], ["The punishment you have measured out to me is deserved—I do know that—well deserved—and you are right and just to be angry with me.", "당신이 내게 내린 벌은 마땅한 거예요—나도 알아요—충분히 마땅하죠—나에게 화내는 당신은 옳고 정당해요."], ["But, Angel, please, please, not to be just—only a little kind to me, even if I do not deserve it, and come to me!", "하지만, 에인절, 제발, 제발 정당하기만 하지는 말아요—내가 그럴 자격이 없더라도, 그저 조금만 나에게 다정해 주고, 나에게 와 줘요!"], ["If you would come, I could die in your arms!", "당신이 와 준다면, 나는 당신 품에서 죽어도 좋아요!"], ["I would be well content to do that if so be you had forgiven me!", "당신이 나를 용서해 준 거라면, 그렇게 하는 것만으로 나는 흡족할 거예요!"], ["Angel, I live entirely for you.", "에인절, 나는 오로지 당신만을 위해 살아요."], ["I love you too much to blame you for going away, and I know it was necessary you should find a farm.", "나는 당신을 너무도 사랑해서 당신이 떠난 걸 탓할 수도 없고, 당신이 농장을 찾아야 했던 게 어쩔 수 없는 일이었음을 알아요."], ["Do not think I shall say a word of sting or bitterness.", "내가 따끔하거나 쓰디쓴 말을 한마디라도 하리라고는 생각하지 말아요."], ["Only come back to me.", "그저 나에게 돌아와만 줘요."], ["I am desolate without you, my darling, O, so desolate!", "당신이 없으니 나는 외로워요, 내 사랑, 아, 너무도 외로워요!"], ["I do not mind having to work: but if you will send me one little line, and say, “_I am coming soon_”, I will bide on, Angel—O, so cheerfully!", "일을 해야 하는 건 괜찮아요. 하지만 당신이 짤막한 한 줄이라도 보내 \"곧 가겠소\"라고 말해 준다면, 나는 견디며 버틸 거예요, 에인절—아, 너무도 기쁘게요!"]], [["It has been so much my religion ever since we were married to be faithful to you in every thought and look, that even when a man speaks a compliment to me before I am aware, it seems wronging you.", "우리가 결혼한 뒤로 줄곧, 생각으로도 눈길로도 당신에게 충실한 것이 내게는 그토록 깊은 신앙이 되어서, 누군가 나도 모르는 새에 나에게 칭찬 한마디를 건네도 그것이 당신에게 죄짓는 것처럼 느껴져요."], ["Have you never felt one little bit of what you used to feel when we were at the dairy? If you have, how can you keep away from me?", "우리가 낙농장에 있을 때 느꼈던 그 마음을 당신은 조금도 느낀 적이 없나요? 만약 느낀 적이 있다면, 어떻게 나에게서 멀리 떨어져 있을 수 있나요?"], ["I am the same woman, Angel, as you fell in love with; yes, the very same!—not the one you disliked but never saw.", "나는 당신이 사랑에 빠졌던 바로 그 여자예요, 에인절. 그래요, 바로 그 여자예요!—당신이 싫어한, 하지만 본 적도 없는 그 여자가 아니에요."], ["What was the past to me as soon as I met you?", "당신을 만난 순간 과거가 내게 무슨 의미였겠어요?"], ["It was a dead thing altogether.", "그건 완전히 죽은 것이었어요."], ["I became another woman, filled full of new life from you.", "나는 당신에게서 새 생명을 가득 채워 받은 다른 여자가 되었어요."], ["How could I be the early one?", "내가 어떻게 그 이전의 여자일 수 있겠어요?"], ["Why do you not see this?", "왜 당신은 이걸 보지 못하나요?"], ["Dear, if you would only be a little more conceited, and believe in yourself so far as to see that you were strong enough to work this change in me, you would perhaps be in a mind to come to me, your poor wife.", "여보, 당신이 조금만 더 자만해서, 내 안에 이런 변화를 일으킬 만큼 당신이 강했다는 걸 믿을 정도로 자신을 믿어 준다면, 어쩌면 당신은 나에게, 당신의 가엾은 아내에게 올 마음이 들지도 몰라요."]], [["How silly I was in my happiness when I thought I could trust you always to love me!", "당신이 언제까지나 나를 사랑하리라 믿을 수 있다고 여겼던 그 행복 속에서 나는 얼마나 어리석었던가요!"], ["I ought to have known that such as that was not for poor me.", "그런 것이 가엾은 나에게는 허락되지 않는다는 걸 진작 알았어야 했어요."], ["But I am sick at heart, not only for old times, but for the present.", "하지만 나는 마음이 아파요, 옛 시절 때문만이 아니라 지금 이 순간 때문에요."], ["Think—think how it do hurt my heart not to see you ever—ever!", "생각해 봐요—당신을 영영, 영영 볼 수 없다는 게 내 마음을 얼마나 아프게 하는지!"], ["Ah, if I could only make your dear heart ache one little minute of each day as mine does every day and all day long, it might lead you to show pity to your poor lonely one.", "아, 내가 매일같이, 하루 종일 느끼는 이 아픔을 단 일 분만이라도 당신의 다정한 마음에 매일 한 번씩 느끼게 할 수 있다면, 그것이 당신을 가엾고 외로운 당신의 사람에게 연민을 베풀게 할지도 모르는데."]], [["People still say that I am rather pretty, Angel (handsome is the word they use, since I wish to be truthful). Perhaps I am what they say.", "사람들은 아직도 내가 제법 예쁘다고들 해요, 에인절(솔직하고 싶으니 말하자면, 그들이 쓰는 말은 '아름답다'예요). 어쩌면 나는 그들이 말하는 그런 사람인지도 몰라요."], ["But I do not value my good looks; I only like to have them because they belong to you, my dear, and that there may be at least one thing about me worth your having.", "하지만 나는 내 고운 외모를 값지게 여기지 않아요. 그것이 당신의 것이기에 갖고 싶을 뿐이에요, 내 사랑. 그래서 적어도 나에게 당신이 가질 만한 한 가지는 있게 하려고요."], ["So much have I felt this, that when I met with annoyance on account of the same, I tied up my face in a bandage as long as people would believe in it. O Angel, I tell you all this not from vanity—you will certainly know I do not—but only that you may come to me!", "이걸 어찌나 절실히 느꼈는지, 그것 때문에 성가신 일을 겪었을 때, 사람들이 믿어 주는 한 오래도록 내 얼굴을 붕대로 동여매고 다녔어요. 아, 에인절, 내가 이 모든 걸 말하는 건 허영심에서가 아니에요—당신은 분명 내가 그렇지 않다는 걸 알 거예요—오직 당신이 나에게 와 주게 하려는 거예요!"]], [["If you really cannot come to me, will you let me come to you?", "당신이 정말로 나에게 올 수 없다면, 내가 당신에게 가도 될까요?"], ["I am, as I say, worried, pressed to do what I will not do.", "나는, 말했듯이, 괴로워요, 하지 않으려는 일을 하라고 떠밀리고 있어요."], ["It cannot be that I shall yield one inch, yet I am in terror as to what an accident might lead to, and I so defenceless on account of my first error.", "내가 한 치라도 굴복할 리는 없지만, 그래도 어떤 사고가 어떤 일로 이어질지 두려워요. 내 첫 번째 잘못 때문에 나는 이토록 무방비하니까요."], ["I cannot say more about this—it makes me too miserable.", "이것에 대해서는 더 말할 수가 없어요—너무 비참해지니까요."], ["But if I break down by falling into some fearful snare, my last state will be worse than my first.", "하지만 내가 어떤 무서운 덫에 빠져 무너진다면, 나의 마지막 처지는 첫 번째 처지보다 더 나빠질 거예요."], ["O God, I cannot think of it!", "아, 하느님, 그건 생각조차 할 수 없어요!"], ["Let me come at once, or at once come to me!", "당장 가게 해 주든가, 당장 나에게 와 줘요!"]], [["I would be content, ay, glad, to live with you as your servant, if I may not as your wife; so that I could only be near you, and get glimpses of you, and think of you as mine.", "당신의 아내로는 안 된다면, 당신의 하인으로라도 당신과 함께 사는 것을, 나는 기꺼이, 아니 기쁘게 받아들일 거예요. 그저 당신 곁에 있을 수만 있다면, 당신을 잠깐씩이라도 볼 수만 있다면, 당신을 내 것이라고 여길 수만 있다면요."]], [["The daylight has nothing to show me, since you are not here, and I don’t like to see the rooks and starlings in the field, because I grieve and grieve to miss you who used to see them with me.", "당신이 여기 없으니 햇빛도 나에게 보여 줄 게 아무것도 없고, 들판의 까마귀와 찌르레기를 보는 것도 싫어요. 예전에 그것들을 나와 함께 보던 당신이 그리워 슬프고 또 슬퍼지니까요."], ["I long for only one thing in heaven or earth or under the earth, to meet you, my own dear!", "하늘에서든 땅에서든 땅 밑에서든 내가 바라는 건 단 하나, 당신을 만나는 것뿐이에요, 내 사랑!"], ["Come to me—come to me, and save me from what threatens me!—", "나에게 와 줘요—나에게 와서, 나를 위협하는 것에서 나를 구해 줘요!—"]], [["Your faithful heartbroken Tess", "당신의 충실하고 가슴 찢어진\n테스"]], [["# XLIX", "# XLIX"]], [["The appeal duly found its way to the breakfast-table of the quiet Vicarage to the westward, in that valley where the air is so soft and the soil so rich that the effort of growth requires but superficial aid by comparison with the tillage at Flintcomb-Ash, and where to Tess the human world seemed so different (though it was much the same).", "서쪽에 있는 그 조용한 사제관의 아침 식탁으로 그 호소는 마땅히 흘러 들어갔다. 그 골짜기는 공기가 어찌나 부드럽고 흙이 어찌나 기름진지, 자라나는 데 드는 수고가 플린트콤애시의 경작에 비하면 겉핥기식 손길만으로도 충분했고, 테스에게는 인간 세상이 그토록 달라 보였던 곳이었다(실은 거의 다를 게 없었지만)."], ["It was purely for security that she had been requested by Angel to send her communications through his father, whom he kept pretty well informed of his changing addresses in the country he had gone to exploit for himself with a heavy heart.", "순전히 안전을 위해, 에인절은 테스에게 자신의 아버지를 통해 연락을 전하라고 부탁해 두었다. 무거운 마음으로 개척하러 떠난 그 나라에서 바뀌는 거처들을 그는 아버지에게 곧잘 알려 두고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said old Mr Clare to his wife, when he had read the envelope, “if Angel proposes leaving Rio for a visit home at the end of next month, as he told us that he hoped to do, I think this may hasten his plans; for I believe it to be from his wife.”", "늙은 클레어 씨가 봉투를 읽고 나서 아내에게 말했다. \"에인절이 우리에게 그러길 바란다고 했던 대로 다음 달 말에 리우를 떠나 집에 들르려 한다면, 이게 그 애 계획을 앞당길지도 모르겠소. 이건 그 애 아내한테서 온 것 같으니 말이오.\""], ["He breathed deeply at the thought of her; and the letter was redirected to be promptly sent on to Angel.", "그는 그녀를 생각하며 깊이 숨을 들이쉬었다. 그리고 편지는 곧장 에인절에게 보내지도록 주소가 다시 적혔다."]], [["“Dear fellow, I hope he will get home safely,”", "\"가엾은 애, 무사히 집에 돌아오길 바라요.\""], ["murmured Mrs Clare.", "클레어 부인이 중얼거렸다."], ["“To my dying day I shall feel that he has been ill-used.", "\"죽는 날까지 나는 그 애가 부당한 대우를 받았다고 느낄 거예요."], ["You should have sent him to Cambridge in spite of his want of faith and given him the same chance as the other boys had.", "그 애가 믿음이 없었어도 케임브리지에 보냈어야 했어요, 다른 아이들이 누린 것과 똑같은 기회를 주었어야 했다고요."], ["He would have grown out of it under proper influence, and perhaps would have taken Orders after all.", "제대로 된 영향을 받았으면 그 애도 거기서 벗어났을 거고, 어쩌면 결국 성직을 받았을지도 몰라요."], ["Church or no Church, it would have been fairer to him.”", "교회든 교회가 아니든, 그 애한테는 그게 더 공평했을 거예요.\""]], [["This was the only wail with which Mrs Clare ever disturbed her husband’s peace in respect to their sons.", "이것이 클레어 부인이 두 아들과 관련해 남편의 평온을 흩뜨린 유일한 비탄이었다. 그리고 그녀는 이런 말을 자주 토해 내지도 않았다."], ["And she did not vent this often; for she was as considerate as she was devout, and knew that his mind too was troubled by doubts as to his justice in this matter.", "그녀는 독실한 만큼 사려 깊었고, 이 문제에서 자신의 처사가 과연 정당했는가를 두고 남편의 마음 또한 의혹으로 괴로워하고 있음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Only too often had she heard him lying awake at night, stifling sighs for Angel with prayers.", "그녀는 그가 밤에 잠 못 이루며 에인절을 향한 한숨을 기도로 억누르는 소리를 너무도 자주 들었다."], ["But the uncompromising Evangelical did not even now hold that he would have been justified in giving his son, an unbeliever, the same academic advantages that he had given to the two others, when it was possible, if not probable, that those very advantages might have been used to decry the doctrines which he had made it his life’s mission and desire to propagate, and the mission of his ordained sons likewise.", "그러나 타협을 모르는 그 복음주의자는, 지금까지도, 불신자인 아들에게 다른 두 아들에게 준 것과 똑같은 학문적 혜택을 주는 것이 정당했으리라고는 여기지 않았다. 바로 그 혜택이, 비록 개연성은 없을지언정 가능성으로는, 자신이 평생의 사명이자 소망으로 삼아 전파해 온 교리들을—그리고 성직에 든 아들들의 사명 또한—깎아내리는 데 쓰일 수도 있었으니 말이다."], ["To put with one hand a pedestal under the feet of the two faithful ones, and with the other to exalt the unfaithful by the same artificial means, he deemed to be alike inconsistent with his convictions, his position, and his hopes.", "한 손으로는 믿음 굳은 두 아들의 발밑에 받침대를 놓아 주고, 다른 손으로는 똑같은 인위적 수단으로 믿음 없는 아들을 떠받치는 것은, 자신의 신념과 처지와 희망 모두에 어긋나는 일이라고 그는 여겼다."], ["Nevertheless, he loved his misnamed Angel, and in secret mourned over this treatment of him as Abraham might have mourned over the doomed Isaac while they went up the hill together.", "그럼에도 그는 잘못 이름 붙여진 자신의 에인절을 사랑했고, 아브라함이 운명 지어진 이삭과 함께 산을 오르며 슬퍼했을 법한 마음으로, 아들을 그렇게 대한 것을 남몰래 애통해했다."], ["His silent self-generated regrets were far bitterer than the reproaches which his wife rendered audible.", "그가 스스로 빚어낸 그 말 없는 회한은, 아내가 소리 내어 쏟아 내는 자책보다 훨씬 더 쓰라렸다."]], [["They blamed themselves for this unlucky marriage.", "그들은 이 불운한 결혼을 두고 자신들을 탓했다."], ["If Angel had never been destined for a farmer he would never have been thrown with agricultural girls.", "에인절이 애초에 농부가 될 운명이 아니었다면 농촌 처녀들과 어울릴 일도 없었으리라."], ["They did not distinctly know what had separated him and his wife, nor the date on which the separation had taken place.", "그들은 무엇이 그와 그의 아내를 갈라놓았는지, 그 헤어짐이 언제 일어났는지도 분명히 알지 못했다."], ["At first they had supposed it must be something of the nature of a serious aversion.", "처음에 그들은 무언가 심각한 반감 같은 것이리라 짐작했다."], ["But in his later letters he occasionally alluded to the intention of coming home to fetch her; from which expressions they hoped the division might not owe its origin to anything so hopelessly permanent as that.", "그러나 후의 편지들에서 그는 이따금 그녀를 데리러 집에 올 생각을 내비쳤고, 그 표현들로 미루어 그들은 그 갈라섬이 그토록 절망적으로 영구한 무언가에서 비롯된 것은 아니기를 바랐다."], ["He had told them that she was with her relatives, and in their doubts they had decided not to intrude into a situation which they knew no way of bettering.", "그는 그녀가 친정 식구들과 함께 있다고 말해 두었고, 의혹 속에서도 그들은 더 나아지게 할 방도를 알지 못하는 상황에 끼어들지 않기로 했다."]], [["The eyes for which Tess’s letter was intended were gazing at this time on a limitless expanse of country from the back of a mule which was bearing him from the interior of the South-American Continent towards the coast.", "테스의 편지가 향한 그 눈은 이때 노새 등에 실려, 남아메리카 대륙 내륙에서 해안 쪽으로 그를 실어 나르며, 끝없이 펼쳐진 광야를 바라보고 있었다."], ["His experiences of this strange land had been sad.", "이 낯선 땅에서의 그의 경험은 슬픈 것이었다."], ["The severe illness from which he had suffered shortly after his arrival had never wholly left him, and he had by degrees almost decided to relinquish his hope of farming here, though, as long as the bare possibility existed of his remaining, he kept this change of view a secret from his parents.", "도착한 직후 앓았던 심한 병은 끝내 완전히 그를 떠나지 않았고, 그는 차츰 이곳에서 농사짓겠다는 희망을 거의 접기로 마음을 굳혀 가고 있었다. 다만 머무를 가능성이 조금이라도 남아 있는 한, 이 마음의 변화를 부모에게는 비밀로 했다."]], [["The crowds of agricultural labourers who had come out to the country in his wake, dazzled by representations of easy independence, had suffered, died, and wasted away.", "쉬운 자립이라는 선전에 현혹되어 그의 뒤를 따라 이 나라로 몰려온 농업 노동자 무리는, 고생하고, 죽고, 시들어 갔다."], ["He would see mothers from English farms trudging along with their infants in their arms, when the child would be stricken with fever and would die; the mother would pause to dig a hole in the loose earth with her bare hands, would bury the babe therein with the same natural grave-tools, shed one tear, and again trudge on.", "그는 영국 농장에서 온 어머니들이 아기를 품에 안고 터덜터덜 걷는 것을 보곤 했는데, 그 아이가 열병에 걸려 죽으면, 어머니는 걸음을 멈추고 맨손으로 푸석한 흙에 구덩이를 파고, 똑같은 천연의 무덤 연장으로 아기를 거기에 묻고는, 눈물 한 방울을 떨구고 다시 터덜터덜 걸어가는 것이었다."]], [["Angel’s original intention had not been emigration to Brazil but a northern or eastern farm in his own country.", "에인절이 애초 의도한 것은 브라질 이주가 아니라 제 나라의 북쪽이나 동쪽 농장이었다."], ["He had come to this place in a fit of desperation, the Brazil movement among the English agriculturists having by chance coincided with his desire to escape from his past existence.", "그가 이곳으로 온 것은 발작적인 절망 때문이었으니, 영국 농민들 사이의 브라질 열풍이 우연히도 자신의 과거에서 벗어나려는 그의 욕구와 맞아떨어졌던 것이다."]], [["During this time of absence he had mentally aged a dozen years.", "이 부재의 시간 동안 그는 마음으로 열두 살은 더 늙었다."], ["What arrested him now as of value in life was less its beauty than its pathos.", "이제 인생에서 그를 사로잡은 가치는 그 아름다움이라기보다 그 애잔함이었다."], ["Having long discredited the old systems of mysticism, he now began to discredit the old appraisements of morality. He thought they wanted readjusting.", "오랫동안 옛 신비주의 체계들을 의심해 온 그는, 이제 도덕에 대한 옛 평가들마저 의심하기 시작했다. 그것들은 다시 조정될 필요가 있다고 그는 생각했다."], ["Who was the moral man?", "도덕적인 사람이란 누구인가?"], ["Still more pertinently, who was the moral woman?", "더더욱 절실하게, 도덕적인 여자란 누구인가?"], ["The beauty or ugliness of a character lay not only in its achievements, but in its aims and impulses; its true history lay, not among things done, but among things willed.", "한 인격의 아름다움이나 추함은 그 성취에만이 아니라 그 목표와 충동에도 깃들어 있었다. 그 참된 역사는 행해진 일들 가운데가 아니라 의지로 품은 일들 가운데 놓여 있었다."]], [["How, then, about Tess?", "그렇다면, 테스는 어떠한가?"]], [["Viewing her in these lights, a regret for his hasty judgement began to oppress him.", "이런 빛 아래에서 그녀를 바라보자, 자신의 성급한 판단에 대한 후회가 그를 짓누르기 시작했다."], ["Did he reject her eternally, or did he not?", "그는 그녀를 영원히 거부하는가, 아닌가?"], ["He could no longer say that he would always reject her, and not to say that was in spirit to accept her now.", "그는 더 이상 자기가 그녀를 언제까지나 거부하겠노라 말할 수 없었고, 그렇게 말하지 못한다는 것은 마음으로는 지금 그녀를 받아들이는 셈이었다."]], [["This growing fondness for her memory coincided in point of time with her residence at Flintcomb-Ash, but it was before she had felt herself at liberty to trouble him with a word about her circumstances or her feelings.", "그녀의 기억에 대한 이 자라나는 애틋함은 시기상으로 그녀가 플린트콤애시에 머물던 때와 겹쳤지만, 그것은 그녀가 자신의 처지나 심정에 대해 그에게 한마디 폐를 끼쳐도 좋다고 스스로 여기기 전이었다."], ["He was greatly perplexed; and in his perplexity as to her motives in withholding intelligence, he did not inquire.", "그는 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못했고, 그녀가 소식을 알리지 않는 동기를 두고 어쩔 줄 몰라 하면서도 묻지 않았다."], ["Thus her silence of docility was misinterpreted.", "그리하여 그녀의 그 순종적인 침묵은 잘못 해석되었다."], ["How much it really said if he had understood!—that she adhered with literal exactness to orders which he had given and forgotten; that despite her natural fearlessness she asserted no rights, admitted his judgement to be in every respect the true one, and bent her head dumbly thereto.", "그가 이해했더라면 그것이 실은 얼마나 많은 것을 말하고 있었던가!—그가 내렸다가 잊어버린 명령을 그녀가 글자 그대로 정확히 지키고 있었음을, 타고난 대담함에도 불구하고 그녀가 어떤 권리도 주장하지 않고, 그의 판단이 모든 면에서 옳은 것임을 인정하며, 거기에 말없이 고개를 숙이고 있었음을."]], [["In the before-mentioned journey by mules through the interior of the country, another man rode beside him.", "앞서 말한 노새를 타고 나라 내륙을 가로지르는 여정에서, 또 다른 남자가 그의 곁에서 말을 몰고 있었다."], ["Angel’s companion was also an Englishman, bent on the same errand, though he came from another part of the island.", "에인절의 동행자 역시 영국인으로, 같은 목적을 품고 있었으나, 그는 그 섬의 다른 지역 출신이었다."], ["They were both in a state of mental depression, and they spoke of home affairs.", "둘 다 심란한 상태였고, 그들은 고향 일을 이야기했다."], ["Confidence begat confidence.", "속내가 속내를 불렀다."], ["With that curious tendency evinced by men, more especially when in distant lands, to entrust to strangers details of their lives which they would on no account mention to friends, Angel admitted to this man as they rode along the sorrowful facts of his marriage.", "남자들이, 특히 먼 타지에 있을 때, 친구들에게는 결코 입에 올리지 않을 자기 삶의 세부를 낯선 이에게 털어놓곤 하는 그 묘한 성향에 따라, 에인절은 말을 몰며 그 남자에게 자기 결혼의 슬픈 사실들을 고백했다."]], [["The stranger had sojourned in many more lands and among many more peoples than Angel; to his cosmopolitan mind such deviations from the social norm, so immense to domesticity, were no more than are the irregularities of vale and mountain-chain to the whole terrestrial curve.", "그 낯선 이는 에인절보다 훨씬 더 많은 나라를 떠돌았고 훨씬 더 많은 민족들 사이에서 지냈다. 세계를 두루 본 그의 마음에 그런 사회 규범에서의 일탈은, 가정사에는 그토록 거대해 보일지언정, 골짜기와 산맥의 들쭉날쭉함이 지구 전체의 곡선에 미치는 정도밖에 되지 않았다."], ["He viewed the matter in quite a different light from Angel; thought that what Tess had been was of no importance beside what she would be, and plainly told Clare that he was wrong in coming away from her.", "그는 그 문제를 에인절과는 사뭇 다른 빛으로 보았다. 테스가 어떠했는가는 그녀가 앞으로 어떠할 것인가에 비하면 아무 중요치 않다고 여겼고, 클레어가 그녀를 떠나온 것은 잘못이었다고 대놓고 말했다."]], [["The next day they were drenched in a thunder-storm.", "이튿날 그들은 천둥 폭풍우에 흠뻑 젖었다."], ["Angel’s companion was struck down with fever, and died by the week’s end.", "에인절의 동행자는 열병으로 쓰러졌고, 그 주가 다 가기 전에 죽었다."], ["Clare waited a few hours to bury him, and then went on his way.", "클레어는 그를 묻으려고 몇 시간을 기다린 뒤, 다시 제 길을 갔다."]], [["The cursory remarks of the large-minded stranger, of whom he knew absolutely nothing beyond a commonplace name, were sublimed by his death, and influenced Clare more than all the reasoned ethics of the philosophers.", "흔해 빠진 이름 말고는 도무지 아는 게 없었던 그 도량 넓은 낯선 이의 무심코 던진 말들은, 그의 죽음으로 한층 숭고해져, 철학자들의 그 어떤 논리적 윤리보다 더 클레어에게 깊은 영향을 미쳤다."], ["His own parochialism made him ashamed by its contrast.", "그 대비 앞에서 클레어는 자신의 편협함이 부끄러웠다."], ["His inconsistencies rushed upon him in a flood.", "자신의 모순들이 홍수처럼 그에게 밀려들었다."], ["He had persistently elevated Hellenic Paganism at the expense of Christianity; yet in that civilization an illegal surrender was not certain disesteem.", "그는 줄곧 기독교를 희생시켜 가며 헬레니즘 이교를 떠받들어 왔다. 그런데 그 문명에서는 불법적인 몸의 내어 줌이 반드시 멸시받는 것은 아니었다."], ["Surely then he might have regarded that abhorrence of the un-intact state, which he had inherited with the creed of mysticism, as at least open to correction when the result was due to treachery.", "그렇다면 분명, 순결을 잃지 않은 상태에 대한 그 혐오를—신비주의 신조와 더불어 물려받은 그 혐오를—적어도 그 결과가 배신에서 비롯된 것일 때는 바로잡을 여지가 있는 것으로 여길 수 있었으리라."], ["A remorse struck into him.", "회한이 그를 찔렀다."], ["The words of Izz Huett, never quite stilled in his memory, came back to him.", "그의 기억 속에서 결코 완전히 잠잠해진 적 없던 이즈 휴엣의 말이 그에게 되돌아왔다."], ["He had asked Izz if she loved him, and she had replied in the affirmative.", "그는 이즈에게 자기를 사랑하느냐고 물었고, 그녀는 그렇다고 답했었다."], ["Did she love him more than Tess did?", "그녀가 테스보다 그를 더 사랑하느냐고?"], ["No, she had replied; Tess would lay down her life for him, and she herself could do no more.", "아니라고 그녀는 답했었다. 테스는 그를 위해 목숨도 내놓을 것이고, 자기로서는 그 이상은 할 수 없다고."]], [["He thought of Tess as she had appeared on the day of the wedding.", "그는 결혼식 날 모습 그대로의 테스를 떠올렸다. 그녀의 두 눈이 어떻게 그에게 머물렀던가."], ["How her eyes had lingered upon him; how she had hung upon his words as if they were a god’s!", "그녀가 어떻게 그의 말을 신의 말씀이라도 되는 양 한마디 한마디에 매달렸던가!"], ["And during the terrible evening over the hearth, when her simple soul uncovered itself to his, how pitiful her face had looked by the rays of the fire, in her inability to realize that his love and protection could possibly be withdrawn.", "그리고 난롯가에서의 그 끔찍한 저녁, 그녀의 순박한 영혼이 그의 영혼 앞에 제 속을 드러냈을 때, 불빛 속에서 그녀의 얼굴은 얼마나 가여워 보였던가. 그의 사랑과 보호가 거두어질 수도 있다는 것을 도무지 받아들이지 못하던 그 얼굴이."]], [["Thus from being her critic he grew to be her advocate.", "그리하여 그는 그녀의 비판자에서 그녀의 변호인으로 자라났다."], ["Cynical things he had uttered to himself about her; but no man can be always a cynic and live; and he withdrew them.", "그는 그녀를 두고 자기 자신에게 냉소적인 말들을 내뱉었었다. 그러나 그 누구도 늘 냉소가가 되어서는 살아갈 수 없는 법이니, 그는 그 말들을 거두어들였다."], ["The mistake of expressing them had arisen from his allowing himself to be influenced by general principles to the disregard of the particular instance.", "그런 말들을 내뱉은 잘못은, 개별의 경우를 도외시한 채 일반 원리에 자신을 휘둘리게 내버려 둔 데서 비롯되었다."]], [["But the reasoning is somewhat musty; lovers and husbands have gone over the ground before to-day.", "그러나 이런 추론은 다소 케케묵은 것이다. 오늘 이전에도 연인들과 남편들이 이미 거쳐 간 자리이니까."], ["Clare had been harsh towards her; there is no doubt of it.", "클레어는 그녀에게 모질었다. 그건 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["Men are too often harsh with women they love or have loved; women with men.", "남자들은 사랑하거나 사랑했던 여자들에게 너무도 자주 모질다."], ["And yet these harshnesses are tenderness itself when compared with the universal harshness out of which they grow; the harshness of the position towards the temperament, of the means towards the aims, of to-day towards yesterday, of hereafter towards to-day.", "여자들도 남자들에게 그렇다. 그런데도 이런 모짊은, 그것이 자라나는 근원인 저 보편적인 모짊에 비하면 다정함 그 자체다. 기질을 향한 처지의 모짊, 목표를 향한 수단의 모짊, 어제를 향한 오늘의 모짊, 오늘을 향한 앞날의 모짊에 비하면."]], [["The historic interest of her family—that masterful line of d’Urbervilles—whom he had despised as a spent force, touched his sentiments now.", "그녀 집안의 그 역사적 흥미—그가 한물간 세력이라 멸시했던 그 위풍당당한 더버빌 가문의 핏줄—가 이제 그의 감정을 건드렸다."], ["Why had he not known the difference between the political value and the imaginative value of these things?", "어째서 그는 이런 것들의 정치적 가치와 상상적 가치의 차이를 알지 못했던가?"], ["In the latter aspect her d’Urberville descent was a fact of great dimensions; worthless to economics, it was a most useful ingredient to the dreamer, to the moralizer on declines and falls.", "후자의 측면에서 그녀의 더버빌 혈통은 어마어마한 의미를 지닌 사실이었다. 경제에는 무가치할지언정, 몽상가에게는, 쇠락과 몰락을 두고 도덕을 논하는 자에게는, 더없이 유용한 재료였다."], ["It was a fact that would soon be forgotten—that bit of distinction in poor Tess’s blood and name, and oblivion would fall upon her hereditary link with the marble monuments and leaded skeletons at Kingsbere.", "그것은 머지않아 잊힐 사실이었다—가엾은 테스의 핏줄과 이름에 깃든 그 한 줌의 고귀함은. 그리고 킹스비어의 대리석 기념비와 납으로 싸인 해골들과 그녀를 잇는 그 세습의 끈 위로 망각이 내려앉으리라."], ["So does Time ruthlessly destroy his own romances.", "이렇게 시간은 제 손으로 빚은 낭만들을 무자비하게 파괴한다."], ["In recalling her face again and again, he thought now that he could see therein a flash of the dignity which must have graced her grand-dames; and the vision sent that _aura_ through his veins which he had formerly felt, and which left behind it a sense of sickness.", "그녀의 얼굴을 거듭거듭 떠올리며, 그는 이제 그 안에서 그녀의 고귀한 할머니들을 빛냈을 그 위엄의 섬광을 볼 수 있을 것만 같았다. 그리고 그 환영은 예전에 그가 느꼈던 그 기운을 핏줄을 타고 흐르게 했으니, 그 기운 뒤에는 일종의 메스꺼움이 남았었다."]], [["Despite her not inviolate past, what still abode in such a woman as Tess outvalued the freshness of her fellows.", "침범당하지 않았다 할 수 없는 과거에도 불구하고, 테스 같은 여자 안에 여전히 깃들어 있는 것은 또래 여자들의 싱그러움보다 더 값졌다."], ["Was not the gleaning of the grapes of Ephraim better than the vintage of Abi-ezer?", "에브라임에서 거둔 포도 이삭이 아비에셀의 포도 수확보다 낫지 아니한가?"]], [["So spoke love renascent, preparing the way for Tess’s devoted outpouring, which was then just being forwarded to him by his father; though owing to his distance inland it was to be a long time in reaching him.", "새로이 되살아난 사랑은 이렇게 말하며, 테스의 헌신적인 토로가 들어설 길을 닦고 있었다. 그 토로는 바로 그때 그의 아버지를 통해 그에게 전달되고 있었으나, 그가 내륙 깊숙이 있는 탓에 그에게 닿기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸릴 터였다."]], [["Meanwhile the writer’s expectation that Angel would come in response to the entreaty was alternately great and small.", "한편 그 호소에 에인절이 응답하여 와 주리라는 편지를 쓴 이의 기대는 컸다 작았다 오락가락했다."], ["What lessened it was that the facts of her life which had led to the parting had not changed—could never change; and that, if her presence had not attenuated them, her absence could not.", "그 기대를 줄어들게 한 것은, 헤어짐으로 이끈 그녀 삶의 사실들이 바뀌지 않았다는—결코 바뀔 수 없다는—점, 그리고 그녀가 곁에 있어도 그 사실들을 옅게 만들지 못했으니 곁에 없다 한들 그럴 리 없다는 점이었다."], ["Nevertheless she addressed her mind to the tender question of what she could do to please him best if he should arrive.", "그럼에도 그녀는, 그가 만약 온다면 그를 가장 기쁘게 하기 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을까 하는 애틋한 물음에 마음을 쏟았다."], ["Sighs were expended on the wish that she had taken more notice of the tunes he played on his harp, that she had inquired more curiously of him which were his favourite ballads among those the country-girls sang.", "그가 하프로 연주하던 곡들에 좀 더 주의를 기울일걸, 농촌 처녀들이 부르던 노래들 가운데 어느 것이 그가 가장 좋아하는 발라드인지 좀 더 궁금해하며 그에게 물어볼걸, 하는 바람에 한숨이 새어 나왔다."], ["She indirectly inquired of Amby Seedling, who had followed Izz from Talbothays, and by chance Amby remembered that, amongst the snatches of melody in which they had indulged at the dairyman’s, to induce the cows to let down their milk, Clare had seemed to like “Cupid’s Gardens”, “I have parks, I have hounds”, and “The break o’ the day”; and had seemed not to care for “The Tailor’s Breeches” and “Such a beauty I did grow”, excellent ditties as they were.", "그녀는 탤보세이스에서 이즈를 따라온 앰비 시들링에게 에둘러 물었고, 마침 앰비는 기억해 냈다. 낙농장에서 소들이 젖을 내리게 하려고 그들이 흥얼대던 가락 토막들 가운데, 클레어가 \"큐피드의 정원\"과 \"내겐 사냥터가 있고, 사냥개가 있네\"와 \"동틀 무렵\"을 좋아하는 듯했고, \"재단사의 바지\"와 \"이렇게 고운 미인으로 자랐네\"는—썩 훌륭한 곡들이었는데도—별로 마음에 들어 하지 않는 듯했음을."]], [["To perfect the ballads was now her whimsical desire.", "이 발라드들을 완벽하게 익히는 것이 이제 그녀의 별난 소망이 되었다."], ["She practised them privately at odd moments, especially “The break o’ the day”:", "그녀는 틈날 때마다 남몰래 그것들을 연습했고, 특히 \"동틀 무렵\"을 그러했다."]], [["Arise, arise, arise! And pick your love a posy, All o’ the sweetest flowers That in the garden grow.", "일어나라, 일어나라, 일어나라! 그대 임에게 꽃다발을 꺾어 주오, 정원에 피어나는 가장 향기로운 꽃들을 죄다."], ["The turtle doves and sma’ birds In every bough a-building, So early in the May-time At the break o’ the day!", "멧비둘기와 작은 새들이 가지마다 둥지를 트네, 오월의 이른 무렵 동틀 무렵에!"]], [["It would have melted the heart of a stone to hear her singing these ditties whenever she worked apart from the rest of the girls in this cold dry time; the tears running down her cheeks all the while at the thought that perhaps he would not, after all, come to hear her, and the simple silly words of the songs resounding in painful mockery of the aching heart of the singer.", "이 추운 마른 철에 그녀가 다른 처녀들과 떨어져 일할 때마다 이 노래들을 부르는 것을 들었다면, 돌의 마음이라도 녹았을 것이다. 그러는 내내 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸으니, 어쩌면 그가 끝내 그것들을 들으러 와 주지 않으리라는 생각 때문이었고, 노래의 단순하고 어리석은 가사가 부르는 이의 아파하는 마음을 가슴 아픈 조롱처럼 울리며 메아리쳤기 때문이었다."]], [["Tess was so wrapt up in this fanciful dream that she seemed not to know how the season was advancing; that the days had lengthened, that Lady-Day was at hand, and would soon be followed by Old Lady-Day, the end of her term here.", "테스는 이 부질없는 꿈에 어찌나 빠져 있었는지, 계절이 어떻게 흘러가는지도 모르는 듯했다. 날이 길어진 것도, 성모영보대축일이 코앞이며 곧 옛 성모영보대축일이 뒤따라와 이곳에서의 그녀 고용 기한이 끝나리라는 것도."]], [["But before the quarter-day had quite come, something happened which made Tess think of far different matters.", "그러나 그 분기 결산일이 채 오기 전에, 테스로 하여금 사뭇 다른 일들을 생각하게 만든 무언가가 일어났다."], ["She was at her lodging as usual one evening, sitting in the downstairs room with the rest of the family, when somebody knocked at the door and inquired for Tess.", "어느 날 저녁 그녀는 여느 때처럼 거처에서 나머지 식구들과 함께 아래층 방에 앉아 있었는데, 누군가 문을 두드리며 테스를 찾았다."], ["Through the doorway she saw against the declining light a figure with the height of a woman and the breadth of a child, a tall, thin, girlish creature whom she did not recognize in the twilight till the girl said “Tess!”", "문간 너머로 그녀는 스러져 가는 빛을 등진 형체를 보았다. 키는 여인만 하고 너비는 어린아이만 한, 호리호리하고 키 큰 소녀 같은 존재였는데, 그 소녀가 \"테스 언니!\" 하고 부를 때까지 그녀는 황혼 속에서 누구인지 알아보지 못했다."]], [["“What—is it ’Liza-Lu?”", "\"뭐야—라이자루니?\""], ["asked Tess, in startled accents.", "테스가 깜짝 놀란 목소리로 물었다."], ["Her sister, whom a little over a year ago she had left at home as a child, had sprung up by a sudden shoot to a form of this presentation, of which as yet Lu seemed herself scarce able to understand the meaning.", "일 년 남짓 전에 어린애로 집에 두고 떠나온 동생이, 갑작스러운 한 차례 솟구침으로 이런 모습으로 훌쩍 자라 있었던 것이다. 정작 루 자신은 아직 그 의미를 거의 헤아리지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Her thin legs, visible below her once-long frock, now short by her growing, and her uncomfortable hands and arms revealed her youth and inexperience.", "한때 길었으나 이제는 자라면서 짧아진 원피스 아래로 드러난 가느다란 다리와, 어색한 손과 팔이 그 어림과 미숙함을 드러냈다."]], [["“Yes, I have been traipsing about all day, Tess,”", "\"응, 하루 종일 쏘다녔어, 언니.\""], ["said Lu, with unemotional gravity, “a-trying to find ’ee; and I’m very tired.”", "루가 감정 없는 진지함으로 말했다. \"언니를 찾느라고. 나 너무 지쳤어.\""]], [["“What is the matter at home?”", "\"집에 무슨 일 있어?\""]], [["“Mother is took very bad, and the doctor says she’s dying, and as father is not very well neither, and says ’tis wrong for a man of such a high family as his to slave and drave at common labouring work, we don’t know what to do.”", "\"엄마가 많이 편찮으셔, 의사 선생님이 돌아가실 거래. 아빠도 별로 편찮으셔서, 자기처럼 그렇게 높은 집안의 사람이 막일하면서 뼈 빠지게 일하는 건 옳지 않다고 하셔. 우린 어쩌면 좋을지 모르겠어.\""]], [["Tess stood in reverie a long time before she thought of asking ’Liza-Lu to come in and sit down.", "테스는 라이자루에게 들어와 앉으라고 할 생각조차 들지 않은 채 한참을 멍하니 서 있었다."], ["When she had done so, and ’Liza-Lu was having some tea, she came to a decision. It was imperative that she should go home.", "그러고 나서 그렇게 하고, 라이자루가 차를 마시고 있을 때, 그녀는 결심을 굳혔다. 반드시 집으로 가야 했다."], ["Her agreement did not end till Old Lady-Day, the sixth of April, but as the interval thereto was not a long one she resolved to run the risk of starting at once.", "그녀의 고용 계약은 옛 성모영보대축일인 사월 엿새까지였지만, 거기까지 남은 기간이 길지 않았으므로 그녀는 위험을 무릅쓰고 당장 떠나기로 마음먹었다."]], [["To go that night would be a gain of twelve-hours; but her sister was too tired to undertake such a distance till the morrow.", "그날 밤에 가면 열두 시간을 버는 셈이었다. 그러나 동생이 너무 지쳐서 그런 거리를 이튿날까지는 감당할 수 없었다."], ["Tess ran down to where Marian and Izz lived, informed them of what had happened, and begged them to make the best of her case to the farmer.", "테스는 메리언과 이즈가 사는 데로 달려 내려가 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알리고, 자기 사정을 농장주에게 잘 둘러대 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Returning, she got Lu a supper, and after that, having tucked the younger into her own bed, packed up as many of her belongings as would go into a withy basket, and started, directing Lu to follow her next morning.", "돌아와서는 루에게 저녁을 차려 주고, 그런 다음 어린 동생을 제 침대에 뉘어 재우고, 버들 바구니에 들어갈 만큼 제 소지품을 챙겨 넣고, 루에게 이튿날 아침 자기를 따라오라 일러 두고는 길을 나섰다."]], [["# L", "# L"]], [["She plunged into the chilly equinoctial darkness as the clock struck ten, for her fifteen miles’ walk under the steely stars.", "시계가 열 시를 칠 때 그녀는 십오 마일을 걸어야 하는 길을 위해, 강철빛 별들 아래 쌀쌀한 춘분의 어둠 속으로 뛰어들었다."], ["In lonely districts night is a protection rather than a danger to a noiseless pedestrian, and knowing this, Tess pursued the nearest course along by-lanes that she would almost have feared in the day-time; but marauders were wanting now, and spectral fears were driven out of her mind by thoughts of her mother.", "외진 고장에서 밤은 소리 없이 걷는 행인에게 위험이라기보다 보호막이다. 이를 아는 테스는, 낮이라면 거의 무서워했을 샛길들을 따라 가장 가까운 길을 잡아 나아갔다. 그러나 이제 약탈자는 없었고, 유령 같은 두려움도 어머니 생각에 마음에서 밀려났다."], ["Thus she proceeded mile after mile, ascending and descending till she came to Bulbarrow, and about midnight looked from that height into the abyss of chaotic shade which was all that revealed itself of the vale on whose further side she was born.", "그렇게 그녀는 마일을 거듭 나아가, 오르락내리락하다가 마침내 불배로에 이르렀고, 자정 무렵 그 높은 곳에서 어지러운 그늘의 심연을 내려다보았다. 그것이 그 골짜기에 대해 드러나 보이는 전부였으니, 그 골짜기 건너편에서 그녀는 태어났던 것이다."], ["Having already traversed about five miles on the upland, she had now some ten or eleven in the lowland before her journey would be finished.", "이미 고지대에서 오 마일가량을 지나온 그녀에게는, 이제 여정이 끝나기까지 저지대에서 십 마일 내지 십일 마일이 남아 있었다."], ["The winding road downwards became just visible to her under the wan starlight as she followed it, and soon she paced a soil so contrasting with that above it that the difference was perceptible to the tread and to the smell.", "굽이굽이 내려가는 길이 희미한 별빛 아래 겨우 보였고, 그녀가 그 길을 따라가자 이내 위쪽 땅과는 사뭇 다른 흙을 밟게 되었는데, 그 차이는 밟는 감촉으로도 냄새로도 느껴졌다."], ["It was the heavy clay land of Blackmoor Vale, and a part of the Vale to which turnpike-roads had never penetrated.", "그것은 블랙무어 골짜기의 무거운 점토 땅이었고, 그 골짜기 가운데서도 유료 도로가 한 번도 뚫린 적 없는 일부였다."], ["Superstitions linger longest on these heavy soils.", "이런 무거운 흙 위에서는 미신이 가장 오래 머문다."], ["Having once been forest, at this shadowy time it seemed to assert something of its old character, the far and the near being blended, and every tree and tall hedge making the most of its presence.", "한때 숲이었던 이 땅은, 이 어슴푸레한 시각에 그 옛 성격을 어느 정도 내세우는 듯했고, 먼 것과 가까운 것이 한데 뒤섞이며 모든 나무와 키 큰 울타리가 제 존재를 한껏 부풀렸다."], ["The harts that had been hunted here, the witches that had been pricked and ducked, the green-spangled fairies that “whickered” at you as you passed;—the place teemed with beliefs in them still, and they formed an impish multitude now.", "이곳에서 사냥당하던 수사슴들, 바늘에 찔리고 물에 담가지던 마녀들, 지나갈 때 당신에게 \"키득\"거리던 초록빛 반짝이는 요정들—이곳은 여전히 그것들에 대한 믿음으로 들끓었고, 그것들은 이제 한 무리의 장난꾸러기 떼를 이루었다."]], [["At Nuttlebury she passed the village inn, whose sign creaked in response to the greeting of her footsteps, which not a human soul heard but herself.", "너틀베리에서 그녀는 마을 주막을 지났는데, 그 간판은 그녀 발소리의 인사에 화답하듯 삐걱거렸으나, 그 소리를 들은 사람은 그녀 자신밖에 없었다."], ["Under the thatched roofs her mind’s eye beheld relaxed tendons and flaccid muscles, spread out in the darkness beneath coverlets made of little purple patchwork squares, and undergoing a bracing process at the hands of sleep for renewed labour on the morrow, as soon as a hint of pink nebulosity appeared on Hambledon Hill.", "초가지붕 아래로 그녀의 마음의 눈은 풀린 힘줄과 늘어진 근육들이 어둠 속에 펼쳐져, 작은 자줏빛 조각보 사각형들로 기운 이불 밑에서, 햄블던힐에 분홍빛 안개의 기색이 나타나는 즉시 이튿날의 새 노동을 위해 잠의 손길로 다시 기운을 차리는 과정을 거치는 모습을 떠올렸다."]], [["At three she turned the last corner of the maze of lanes she had threaded, and entered Marlott, passing the field in which as a club-girl she had first seen Angel Clare, when he had not danced with her; the sense of disappointment remained with her yet.", "세 시에 그녀는 자신이 헤쳐 온 미로 같은 샛길들의 마지막 모퉁이를 돌아 말럿으로 들어섰고, 클럽 처녀였던 시절 에인절 클레어를 처음 보았던—그가 그녀와 춤추지 않았던—그 들판을 지나쳤다. 그 실망의 느낌은 아직도 그녀에게 남아 있었다."], ["In the direction of her mother’s house she saw a light.", "어머니의 집 쪽에서 그녀는 불빛을 보았다."], ["It came from the bedroom window, and a branch waved in front of it and made it wink at her.", "그것은 침실 창문에서 흘러나왔고, 나뭇가지 하나가 그 앞에서 흔들리며 불빛을 그녀에게 깜빡이게 했다."], ["As soon as she could discern the outline of the house—newly thatched with her money—it had all its old effect upon Tess’s imagination.", "집의 윤곽을—그녀의 돈으로 새로 이엉을 인 그 집을—분간할 수 있게 되자마자, 그것은 테스의 상상에 여느 때처럼 그 옛 효력을 고스란히 미쳤다."], ["Part of her body and life it ever seemed to be; the slope of its dormers, the finish of its gables, the broken courses of brick which topped the chimney, all had something in common with her personal character.", "그것은 늘 그녀 몸과 삶의 일부인 듯싶었다. 그 지붕창들의 기울기, 박공들의 마무리, 굴뚝 꼭대기를 이룬 깨진 벽돌 켜들, 그 모두가 그녀의 인격과 무언가 통하는 데가 있었다."], ["A stupefaction had come into these features, to her regard; it meant the illness of her mother.", "그녀가 보기에 그 모습들에 멍한 마비가 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 어머니의 병을 뜻했다."]], [["She opened the door so softly as to disturb nobody; the lower room was vacant, but the neighbour who was sitting up with her mother came to the top of the stairs, and whispered that Mrs Durbeyfield was no better, though she was sleeping just then.", "그녀는 아무도 깨우지 않을 만큼 살며시 문을 열었다. 아래층 방은 비어 있었으나, 어머니 곁을 지키며 밤샘하던 이웃이 계단 꼭대기로 나와, 더비필드 부인이 마침 그때 잠들어 있긴 하나 차도가 없다고 속삭였다."], ["Tess prepared herself a breakfast, and then took her place as nurse in her mother’s chamber.", "테스는 자기 아침을 차려 먹고, 그런 다음 어머니 방에서 간병인의 자리를 떠맡았다."]], [["In the morning, when she contemplated the children, they had all a curiously elongated look; although she had been away little more than a year, their growth was astounding; and the necessity of applying herself heart and soul to their needs took her out of her own cares.", "아침에 아이들을 바라보니, 모두가 묘하게 길쭉해진 모습이었다. 그녀가 떠나 있던 것은 겨우 일 년 남짓이었는데도 그 자란 정도가 놀라웠다. 그리고 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 아이들의 필요에 매달려야 하는 형편이 그녀를 자기 자신의 근심에서 끌어내 주었다."], ["Her father’s ill-health was the same indefinite kind, and he sat in his chair as usual.", "아버지의 병약함은 여전히 그 막연한 종류 그대로였고, 그는 여느 때처럼 의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["But the day after her arrival he was unusually bright.", "그러나 그녀가 도착한 이튿날 그는 유난히 기분이 밝았다."], ["He had a rational scheme for living, and Tess asked him what it was.", "그에게는 살아갈 합리적인 계획이 하나 있었고, 테스가 그게 무엇이냐고 물었다."]], [["“I’m thinking of sending round to all the old antiqueerians in this part of England,”", "\"이 잉글랜드 일대의 옛 골동품 애호가들한테 죄다 사람을 보내 볼까 생각 중이다.\""], ["he said, “asking them to subscribe to a fund to maintain me.", "그가 말했다. \"나를 부양할 기금에 기부해 달라고 청하는 거지."], ["I’m sure they’d see it as a romantical, artistical, and proper thing to do.", "분명히 그들은 그걸 낭만적이고 예술적이고 마땅한 일로 여길 거야."], ["They spend lots o’ money in keeping up old ruins, and finding the bones o’ things, and such like; and living remains must be more interesting to ’em still, if they only knowed of me.", "그자들은 옛 폐허를 보존하고, 무슨 뼈다귀 같은 걸 찾아내는 데 돈을 엄청 쓰잖니. 살아 있는 유물이라면, 나에 대해 알기만 한다면, 그들한테는 훨씬 더 흥미로울 게 틀림없지."], ["Would that somebody would go round and tell ’em what there is living among ’em, and they thinking nothing of him!", "누군가 돌아다니며 그들 사이에 무엇이 살아 있는지 일러 줬으면 좋으련만, 그들은 그를 아무것도 아니라 여기고 있으니!"], ["If Pa’son Tringham, who discovered me, had lived, he’d ha’ done it, I’m sure.”", "나를 발견한 트링엄 목사가 살아 있었다면, 분명 그가 해 줬을 텐데.\""]], [["Tess postponed her arguments on this high project till she had grappled with pressing matters in hand, which seemed little improved by her remittances.", "테스는 이 거창한 계획에 대한 반론을, 당장 눈앞에 닥친 시급한 문제들과 씨름하고 난 뒤로 미뤘다."], ["When indoor necessities had been eased, she turned her attention to external things.", "그 문제들은 그녀가 부쳐 온 돈으로도 별로 나아진 것 같지 않았다."], ["It was now the season for planting and sowing; many gardens and allotments of the villagers had already received their spring tillage; but the garden and the allotment of the Durbeyfields were behindhand.", "집 안의 필요가 좀 누그러지자, 그녀는 바깥일로 눈을 돌렸다. 이제 심고 씨 뿌리는 철이었다. 마을 사람들의 정원과 분배지 상당수는 이미 봄갈이를 마쳤지만, 더비필드네의 정원과 분배지는 뒤처져 있었다."], ["She found, to her dismay, that this was owing to their having eaten all the seed potatoes,—that last lapse of the improvident.", "그녀는 그것이 그들이 씨감자를 죄다 먹어 치운 탓임을 알고는 아연실색했다—앞날을 생각지 않는 자들의 마지막 실수였다."], ["At the earliest moment she obtained what others she could procure, and in a few days her father was well enough to see to the garden, under Tess’s persuasive efforts: while she herself undertook the allotment-plot which they rented in a field a couple of hundred yards out of the village.", "그녀는 한시바삐 구할 수 있는 다른 씨감자를 구해 왔고, 며칠 지나자 아버지는 테스의 설득에 힘입어 정원을 돌볼 만큼은 기운을 차렸다. 한편 그녀 자신은 마을에서 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 들판에 세 들어 부치던 분배지 텃밭을 맡았다."]], [["She liked doing it after the confinement of the sick chamber, where she was not now required by reason of her mother’s improvement.", "병실의 갑갑함에서 벗어난 뒤라 그녀는 그 일이 좋았다. 어머니의 차도 덕에 이제 그녀가 병실에 매일 필요는 없었다."], ["Violent motion relieved thought.", "격렬한 움직임이 생각을 덜어 주었다."], ["The plot of ground was in a high, dry, open enclosure, where there were forty or fifty such pieces, and where labour was at its briskest when the hired labour of the day had ended.", "그 텃밭은 높고 메마른 트인 울안에 있었는데, 그런 땅뙈기가 마흔에서 쉰 개쯤 있었고, 낮 동안의 품팔이 노동이 끝난 뒤라야 일이 가장 활기를 띠는 곳이었다."], ["Digging began usually at six o’clock and extended indefinitely into the dusk or moonlight.", "땅 파기는 보통 여섯 시에 시작되어 어스름이나 달빛 속으로 한없이 이어졌다."], ["Just now heaps of dead weeds and refuse were burning on many of the plots, the dry weather favouring their combustion.", "마침 지금은 죽은 잡초와 쓰레기 더미가 여러 땅뙈기에서 타고 있었으니, 메마른 날씨가 그 불타는 것을 거들었다."]], [["One fine day Tess and ’Liza-Lu worked on here with their neighbours till the last rays of the sun smote flat upon the white pegs that divided the plots.", "어느 화창한 날, 테스와 라이자루는 해의 마지막 빛살이 땅뙈기들을 가르는 흰 말뚝들 위로 납작하게 내리쬘 때까지 이웃들과 함께 여기서 일했다."], ["As soon as twilight succeeded to sunset the flare of the couch-grass and cabbage-stalk fires began to light up the allotments fitfully, their outlines appearing and disappearing under the dense smoke as wafted by the wind.", "일몰에 뒤이어 황혼이 깔리자, 우산잔디와 양배추 줄기를 태우는 불길이 분배지들을 띄엄띄엄 밝히기 시작했고, 그 윤곽은 바람에 실려 오는 짙은 연기 아래로 나타났다 사라졌다 했다."], ["When a fire glowed, banks of smoke, blown level along the ground, would themselves become illuminated to an opaque lustre, screening the workpeople from one another; and the meaning of the “pillar of a cloud”, which was a wall by day and a light by night, could be understood.", "불이 이글거릴 때면, 땅을 따라 납작하게 불려 가는 연기 둑들이 그 자체로 불투명한 윤기를 띠게 환히 밝혀져 일꾼들을 서로에게서 가려 주었다. 그리고 낮에는 벽이요 밤에는 빛이었던 저 \"구름 기둥\"의 뜻을 헤아릴 수 있었다."]], [["As evening thickened, some of the gardening men and women gave over for the night, but the greater number remained to get their planting done, Tess being among them, though she sent her sister home.", "저녁이 짙어지자, 정원 일 하던 남녀 가운데 더러는 그날 일을 접었으나, 더 많은 수는 심을 것을 다 심으려고 남았고, 테스도 그중에 끼어 있었다. 다만 동생은 집으로 돌려보냈다."], ["It was on one of the couch-burning plots that she laboured with her fork, its four shining prongs resounding against the stones and dry clods in little clicks.", "그녀가 쇠스랑을 들고 일하던 곳은 우산잔디를 태우던 땅뙈기 가운데 하나였고, 쇠스랑의 빛나는 네 갈래 살이 돌과 마른 흙덩이에 부딪혀 작은 딸깍 소리를 냈다."], ["Sometimes she was completely involved in the smoke of her fire; then it would leave her figure free, irradiated by the brassy glare from the heap.", "때로 그녀는 제 불의 연기에 완전히 휩싸였고, 그러면 연기가 그녀의 형체를 풀어 주어, 더미에서 뿜어 나오는 놋쇳빛 불꽃에 환히 비치게 했다."], ["She was oddly dressed to-night, and presented a somewhat staring aspect, her attire being a gown bleached by many washings, with a short black jacket over it, the effect of the whole being that of a wedding and funeral guest in one.", "오늘 밤 그녀는 묘한 옷차림을 하고 있어 다소 눈에 띄는 모양새였다. 여러 번 빨아 빛이 바랜 가운에 짧은 검정 재킷을 걸쳤는데, 전체적인 인상이 결혼식과 장례식 하객을 하나로 합쳐 놓은 듯했다."], ["The women further back wore white aprons, which, with their pale faces, were all that could be seen of them in the gloom, except when at moments they caught a flash from the flames.", "더 뒤쪽의 여자들은 흰 앞치마를 둘렀는데, 그것이 그녀들의 창백한 얼굴과 더불어, 이따금 불길의 섬광을 받을 때 말고는, 어스름 속에서 보이는 전부였다."]], [["Westward, the wiry boughs of the bare thorn hedge which formed the boundary of the field rose against the pale opalescence of the lower sky.", "서쪽으로는 들판의 경계를 이룬 헐벗은 가시나무 울타리의 철사 같은 가지들이 아래쪽 하늘의 창백한 유백색을 배경으로 솟아 있었다."], ["Above, Jupiter hung like a full-blown jonquil, so bright as almost to throw a shade.", "그 위로는 목성이 활짝 핀 노랑수선화처럼 걸려, 어찌나 밝은지 거의 그림자를 드리울 정도였다."], ["A few small nondescript stars were appearing elsewhere.", "정체 모를 작은 별 몇 개가 다른 곳에 나타나고 있었다."], ["In the distance a dog barked, and wheels occasionally rattled along the dry road.", "멀리서 개 한 마리가 짖었고, 이따금 수레바퀴가 마른 길 위를 덜컹거리며 지나갔다."]], [["Still the prongs continued to click assiduously, for it was not late; and though the air was fresh and keen there was a whisper of spring in it that cheered the workers on.", "여전히 쇠스랑 살은 부지런히 딸깍거렸으니, 아직 늦은 시각이 아니었기 때문이다. 그리고 공기는 상쾌하고 매서웠지만 그 안에 봄의 속삭임이 깃들어 일꾼들의 기운을 북돋웠다."], ["Something in the place, the hours, the crackling fires, the fantastic mysteries of light and shade, made others as well as Tess enjoy being there.", "그 장소, 그 시각, 타닥거리는 불길, 빛과 그림자의 환상적인 신비—그 무언가가 테스뿐 아니라 다른 이들도 거기 있는 것을 즐기게 만들었다."], ["Nightfall, which in the frost of winter comes as a fiend and in the warmth of summer as a lover, came as a tranquillizer on this March day.", "겨울의 서리 속에서는 마귀처럼 오고 여름의 온기 속에서는 연인처럼 오는 해 질 녘이, 이 삼월의 날에는 마음을 가라앉히는 자처럼 찾아왔다."]], [["Nobody looked at his or her companions.", "아무도 제 동료들을 쳐다보지 않았다."], ["The eyes of all were on the soil as its turned surface was revealed by the fires.", "모두의 눈은 불빛에 드러나는 흙의 뒤집힌 표면에 쏠려 있었다."], ["Hence as Tess stirred the clods and sang her foolish little songs with scarce now a hope that Clare would ever hear them, she did not for a long time notice the person who worked nearest to her—a man in a long smockfrock who, she found, was forking the same plot as herself, and whom she supposed her father had sent there to advance the work.", "그리하여 테스가 흙덩이를 휘저으며, 클레어가 끝내 그것들을 들으리라는 희망은 이제 거의 없는 채로 제 어리석은 작은 노래들을 부르는 동안, 그녀는 자기 가장 가까이서 일하는 사람을 한참 동안 알아채지 못했다—긴 작업복을 입은 한 남자였는데, 알고 보니 자기와 같은 땅뙈기를 쇠스랑질하고 있었고, 그녀는 아버지가 일을 거들라고 그를 보냈으려니 짐작했다."], ["She became more conscious of him when the direction of his digging brought him closer.", "그가 파 들어가는 방향이 그를 더 가까이 데려왔을 때 그녀는 그를 좀 더 의식하게 되었다."], ["Sometimes the smoke divided them; then it swerved, and the two were visible to each other but divided from all the rest.", "때로 연기가 둘을 갈라놓았고, 그러다 연기가 비껴가면, 둘은 서로에게는 보이되 나머지 모두에게서는 가려졌다."]], [["Tess did not speak to her fellow-worker, nor did he speak to her.", "테스는 함께 일하는 그 사람에게 말을 걸지 않았고, 그도 그녀에게 말을 걸지 않았다."], ["Nor did she think of him further than to recollect that he had not been there when it was broad daylight, and that she did not know him as any one of the Marlott labourers, which was no wonder, her absences having been so long and frequent of late years.", "그녀는 또한, 환한 대낮에는 그가 거기 없었다는 것, 그리고 자기가 그를 말럿 일꾼들 가운데 누구로도 알지 못한다는 것을 떠올린 것 말고는 더는 그를 생각지 않았다. 그것도 이상할 게 없었으니, 근래 몇 년 그녀의 부재가 그토록 잦고 길었던 까닭이다."], ["By-and-by he dug so close to her that the fire-beams were reflected as distinctly from the steel prongs of his fork as from her own.", "이윽고 그가 그녀에게 어찌나 바짝 붙어 파는지, 불꽃의 빛살이 그의 쇠스랑 강철 살에서도 그녀 자신의 것에서만큼이나 또렷이 반사되었다."], ["On going up to the fire to throw a pitch of dead weeds upon it, she found that he did the same on the other side.", "죽은 잡초 한 무더기를 던져 넣으려고 불 쪽으로 올라가다가, 그녀는 그가 반대편에서 똑같이 하는 것을 보았다."], ["The fire flared up, and she beheld the face of d’Urberville.", "불이 확 타올랐고, 그녀는 더버빌의 얼굴을 마주 보았다."]], [["The unexpectedness of his presence, the grotesqueness of his appearance in a gathered smockfrock, such as was now worn only by the most old-fashioned of the labourers, had a ghastly comicality that chilled her as to its bearing.", "그가 거기 있다는 뜻밖의 사실, 그리고 이제는 가장 구식인 일꾼들이나 입는 주름 잡힌 작업복 차림의 그 기괴한 몰골은, 그것이 뜻하는 바를 두고 그녀를 오싹하게 하는 섬뜩한 익살스러움을 띠었다."], ["D’Urberville emitted a low, long laugh.", "더버빌이 낮고 긴 웃음을 흘렸다."]], [["“If I were inclined to joke, I should say, How much this seems like Paradise!”", "\"농담할 마음이 든다면, 이거 참 낙원 같지 않으냐고 말하겠는데!\""], ["he remarked whimsically, looking at her with an inclined head.", "그가 고개를 기울여 그녀를 보며 익살맞게 말했다."]], [["“What do you say?” she weakly asked.", "\"뭐라고요?\" 그녀가 힘없이 물었다."]], [["“A jester might say this is just like Paradise.", "\"익살꾼이라면 이게 꼭 낙원 같다고 하겠지."], ["You are Eve, and I am the old Other One come to tempt you in the disguise of an inferior animal.", "너는 이브고, 나는 열등한 짐승으로 변장하고 너를 꾀러 온 그 옛 다른 자고 말이야."], ["I used to be quite up in that scene of Milton’s when I was theological.", "내가 신학에 빠져 있던 시절엔 밀턴의 그 장면을 제법 꿰고 있었지."], ["Some of it goes—", "이런 대목이 있어—"]], [["‘Empress, the way is ready, and not long, Beyond a row of myrtles.... ...", "'여왕이여, 길은 이미 닦여 있고, 멀지 않소, 도금양 한 줄 너머에…"], ["If thou accept My conduct, I can bring thee thither soon.’ ‘Lead then,’ said Eve.", "…그대 내 인도를 받아들인다면, 곧 그리로 데려다주리다.' '그럼 앞장서요.' 이브가 말했다."]], [["“And so on.", "\"뭐 이런 식이지."], ["My dear Tess, I am only putting this to you as a thing that you might have supposed or said quite untruly, because you think so badly of me.”", "사랑하는 테스, 나는 그저 이걸 네가 짐작했거나 입에 올렸을 법한 것으로—전혀 사실이 아닌데도—말해 보는 거야. 네가 나를 그토록 나쁘게 여기니 말이지.\""]], [["“I never said you were Satan, or thought it.", "\"나는 당신이 사탄이라고 한 적도 없고 그렇게 여긴 적도 없어요."], ["I don’t think of you in that way at all.", "그런 식으로는 당신을 전혀 생각하지 않아요."], ["My thoughts of you are quite cold, except when you affront me.", "당신이 나를 모욕할 때 말고는, 당신에 대한 내 생각은 아주 차가워요."], ["What, did you come digging here entirely because of me?”", "뭐예요, 순전히 나 때문에 여기 와서 땅을 판 거예요?\""]], [["“Entirely. To see you; nothing more.", "\"순전히 그래서지. 너를 보려고."], ["The smockfrock, which I saw hanging for sale as I came along, was an afterthought, that I mightn’t be noticed.", "그 이상은 아니야. 이 작업복은, 오는 길에 팔려고 걸어 둔 걸 봤는데, 눈에 띄지 않으려고 나중에 생각해 낸 거야."], ["I come to protest against your working like this.”", "나는 네가 이렇게 일하는 것에 항의하러 왔어.\""]], [["“But I like doing it—it is for my father.”", "\"하지만 나는 이 일이 좋아요—아버지를 위한 거니까요.\""]], [["“Your engagement at the other place is ended?”", "\"저쪽 데서 일하던 계약은 끝났느냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Where are you going to next?", "\"다음엔 어디로 갈 거지?"], ["To join your dear husband?”", "네 사랑하는 남편한테 가려고?\""]], [["She could not bear the humiliating reminder.", "그녀는 그 굴욕적인 상기를 견딜 수 없었다."]], [["“O—I don’t know!”", "\"아—몰라요!\""], ["she said bitterly.", "그녀가 쓰라리게 말했다."], ["“I have no husband!”", "\"나한테는 남편이 없어요!\""]], [["“It is quite true—in the sense you mean.", "\"그건 사실이지—네가 말하는 그런 뜻으로는."], ["But you have a friend, and I have determined that you shall be comfortable in spite of yourself.", "하지만 너한테는 친구가 있어, 그리고 나는 네가 싫다 해도 너를 편안하게 해 주기로 마음먹었어."], ["When you get down to your house you will see what I have sent there for you.”", "네 집에 내려가 보면, 내가 너를 위해 거기로 보낸 게 뭔지 알게 될 거다.\""]], [["“O, Alec, I wish you wouldn’t give me anything at all!", "\"아, 알렉, 제발 나한테 아무것도 주지 말아 줘요!"], ["I cannot take it from you! I don’t like—it is not right!”", "나는 당신한테서 받을 수 없어요! 싫어요—옳지 않아요!\""]], [["“It _is_ right!”", "\"옳은 일이야!\""], ["he cried lightly.", "그가 가볍게 외쳤다."], ["“I am not going to see a woman whom I feel so tenderly for as I do for you in trouble without trying to help her.”", "\"내가 너에게 느끼는 만큼 애틋하게 느끼는 여자가 곤경에 빠진 걸, 도우려 애써 보지도 않고 두고 보지는 않을 거야.\""]], [["“But I am very well off!", "\"하지만 나는 아주 잘 지내요!"], ["I am only in trouble about—about—not about living at all!”", "내가 곤경에 빠진 건 다만—다만—사는 것에 관한 게 전혀 아니에요!\""]], [["She turned, and desperately resumed her digging, tears dripping upon the fork-handle and upon the clods.", "그녀는 돌아서서, 절박하게 다시 땅을 파기 시작했고, 눈물이 쇠스랑 자루 위로, 흙덩이 위로 뚝뚝 떨어졌다."]], [["“About the children—your brothers and sisters,” he resumed.", "\"그 애들 얘기야—네 동생들 말이다.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["“I’ve been thinking of them.”", "\"그 애들을 생각해 봤어.\""]], [["Tess’s heart quivered—he was touching her in a weak place.", "테스의 가슴이 떨렸다—그가 그녀의 약한 곳을 건드리고 있었다."], ["He had divined her chief anxiety. Since returning home her soul had gone out to those children with an affection that was passionate.", "그는 그녀의 가장 큰 걱정거리를 알아맞혔다. 집에 돌아온 뒤로 그녀의 영혼은 격정적인 애정으로 그 아이들에게 쏠려 있었다."]], [["“If your mother does not recover, somebody ought to do something for them; since your father will not be able to do much, I suppose?”", "\"네 어머니가 회복되지 않으면, 누군가 그 애들을 위해 무언가 해야 할 텐데. 네 아버지는 많은 걸 할 수 없을 테니, 안 그래?\""]], [["“He can with my assistance. He must!”", "\"내가 도우면 할 수 있어요. 하셔야 하고요!\""]], [["“And with mine.”", "\"내 도움도 있고.\""]], [["“No, sir!”", "\"안 돼요!\""]], [["“How damned foolish this is!” burst out d’Urberville.", "\"이 얼마나 빌어먹게 어리석은 짓이냐!\" 더버빌이 터뜨렸다."], ["“Why, he thinks we are the same family; and will be quite satisfied!”", "\"이봐, 그자는 우리가 같은 집안이라 여기고, 아주 흡족해할 텐데!\""]], [["“He don’t.", "\"안 그래요."], ["I’ve undeceived him.”", "내가 그 오해를 풀어 드렸어요.\""]], [["“The more fool you!”", "\"그런 바보가 어디 있어!\""]], [["D’Urberville in anger retreated from her to the hedge, where he pulled off the long smockfrock which had disguised him; and rolling it up and pushing it into the couch-fire, went away.", "더버빌은 화가 나서 그녀에게서 물러나 울타리 쪽으로 갔고, 거기서 자기를 변장시켰던 그 긴 작업복을 벗더니, 둘둘 말아 우산잔디 불 속에 밀어 넣고는 가 버렸다."]], [["Tess could not get on with her digging after this; she felt restless; she wondered if he had gone back to her father’s house; and taking the fork in her hand proceeded homewards.", "테스는 이 일 뒤로 땅 파기를 계속할 수 없었다. 마음이 안절부절못했다. 그가 아버지 집으로 돌아갔을까 궁금했고, 쇠스랑을 손에 들고 집을 향해 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["Some twenty yards from the house she was met by one of her sisters.", "집에서 이십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 동생 하나가 그녀를 맞았다."]], [["“O, Tessy—what do you think!", "\"아, 테스 언니—어떡해!"], ["’Liza-Lu is a-crying, and there’s a lot of folk in the house, and mother is a good deal better, but they think father is dead!”", "라이자루 언니가 울고 있고, 집에 사람들이 잔뜩 와 있어. 엄마는 많이 좋아지셨는데, 아빠가 돌아가신 것 같대!\""]], [["The child realized the grandeur of the news; but not as yet its sadness, and stood looking at Tess with round-eyed importance till, beholding the effect produced upon her, she said—", "그 아이는 소식의 엄청남은 알았으나 아직 그 슬픔까지는 알지 못한 채, 동그래진 눈으로 짐짓 중요한 표정을 지으며 테스를 바라보고 섰다가, 테스에게 이는 변화를 보고는 말했다—"]], [["“What, Tess, shan’t we talk to father never no more?”", "\"뭐야, 언니, 우리 이제 아빠랑 다시는 영영 말 못 하는 거야?\""]], [["“But father was only a little bit ill!”", "\"하지만 아빠는 그저 조금 편찮으셨을 뿐인데!\""], ["exclaimed Tess distractedly.", "테스가 정신없이 외쳤다."]], [["’Liza-Lu came up.", "라이자루가 다가왔다."]], [["“He dropped down just now, and the doctor who was there for mother said there was no chance for him, because his heart was growed in.”", "\"방금 픽 쓰러지셨어, 엄마 때문에 와 있던 의사 선생님이 가망이 없다고 했어. 심장이 안으로 자라 버렸대.\""]], [["Yes; the Durbeyfield couple had changed places; the dying one was out of danger, and the indisposed one was dead.", "그렇다. 더비필드 부부는 자리가 뒤바뀌었다. 죽어 가던 쪽은 위험에서 벗어났고, 가볍게 앓던 쪽이 죽은 것이다."], ["The news meant even more than it sounded.", "그 소식은 들리는 것보다 한층 더 큰 의미를 담고 있었다."], ["Her father’s life had a value apart from his personal achievements, or perhaps it would not have had much.", "아버지의 목숨은 그 자신의 성취와는 별개의 가치를 지녔으니, 어쩌면 그렇지 않았다면 그리 큰 가치가 없었을 것이다."], ["It was the last of the three lives for whose duration the house and premises were held under a lease; and it had long been coveted by the tenant-farmer for his regular labourers, who were stinted in cottage accommodation.", "그것은 그 집과 부지가 임차 계약으로 묶여 있던 세 사람의 수명 가운데 마지막이었고, 그것은 오래전부터 차지농이 정규 일꾼들을 위해 탐내 온 것이었다. 그 일꾼들은 오두막 거처가 부족했던 것이다. 게다가 마을에서 \"거주권자\"는 작은 자유보유농만큼이나 못마땅하게 여겨졌으니, 그들의 독립적인 태도 때문이었다."], ["Moreover, “liviers” were disapproved of in villages almost as much as little freeholders, because of their independence of manner, and when a lease determined it was never renewed.", "그래서 임차 계약이 끝나면 결코 갱신되는 법이 없었다. * 거주권자: 한 사람 또는 몇 사람의 수명 동안 집을 보유하는 종신 임차인."]], [["Thus the Durbeyfields, once d’Urbervilles, saw descending upon them the destiny which, no doubt, when they were among the Olympians of the county, they had caused to descend many a time, and severely enough, upon the heads of such landless ones as they themselves were now.", "이리하여 한때 더버빌이었던 더비필드 가문은, 자기들에게 운명이 내려앉는 것을 보았다. 그들이 이 고을의 올림포스 신들 가운데 있던 시절, 지금의 자기들처럼 땅 없는 자들의 머리 위로 그 운명을 분명 여러 번, 그것도 모질게 내려앉히게 했을 그 운명이었다."], ["So do flux and reflux—the rhythm of change—alternate and persist in everything under the sky.", "이렇게 밀물과 썰물이—변화의 율동이—하늘 아래 모든 것 속에서 번갈아 들며 끈질기게 이어진다."]], [["# LI", "# LI"]], [["At length it was the eve of Old Lady-Day, and the agricultural world was in a fever of mobility such as only occurs at that particular date of the year.", "마침내 옛 성모영보대축일 전야가 되었고, 농촌 세계는 한 해의 바로 그 날에만 일어나는 이동의 열병에 들떠 있었다."], ["It is a day of fulfilment; agreements for outdoor service during the ensuing year, entered into at Candlemas, are to be now carried out.", "그날은 약속이 이행되는 날이다. 성촉절에 맺은, 이듬해 한 해 동안의 옥외 고용 계약이 이제 실행에 옮겨지는 것이다."], ["The labourers—or “work-folk”, as they used to call themselves immemorially till the other word was introduced from without—who wish to remain no longer in old places are removing to the new farms.", "더 이상 옛 자리에 머물고 싶지 않은 노동자들이—혹은 바깥에서 다른 말이 들어오기 전까지 그들이 아득한 옛날부터 스스로를 일컫던 대로 \"일꾼들\"이—새 농장으로 옮겨 가고 있었다."]], [["These annual migrations from farm to farm were on the increase here.", "이 농장에서 농장으로의 해마다의 이주는 이곳에서 점점 늘어나는 추세였다."], ["When Tess’s mother was a child the majority of the field-folk about Marlott had remained all their lives on one farm, which had been the home also of their fathers and grandfathers; but latterly the desire for yearly removal had risen to a high pitch.", "테스의 어머니가 어렸을 적에는 말럿 일대 들일하는 사람들 대다수가 한 농장에서 평생을 머물렀고, 그 농장은 그들 아버지와 할아버지의 집이기도 했다. 그러나 근래에는 해마다 옮기려는 욕구가 높이 치솟았다."], ["With the younger families it was a pleasant excitement which might possibly be an advantage.", "젊은 가족들에게 그것은 어쩌면 이득이 될 수도 있는 즐거운 흥분이었다."], ["The Egypt of one family was the Land of Promise to the family who saw it from a distance, till by residence there it became in turn their Egypt also; and so they changed and changed.", "한 가족에게 이집트인 곳이, 멀리서 그것을 바라보는 가족에게는 약속의 땅이었으니, 거기 살다 보면 그곳이 다시 그들의 이집트가 되었다. 그렇게 그들은 옮기고 또 옮겼다."]], [["However, all the mutations so increasingly discernible in village life did not originate entirely in the agricultural unrest.", "그러나 마을 생활에서 점점 더 뚜렷이 드러나는 그 모든 변천이 전적으로 농촌의 불안에서 비롯된 것만은 아니었다."], ["A depopulation was also going on.", "인구 감소 또한 진행되고 있었다."], ["The village had formerly contained, side by side with the argicultural labourers, an interesting and better-informed class, ranking distinctly above the former—the class to which Tess’s father and mother had belonged—and including the carpenter, the smith, the shoemaker, the huckster, together with nondescript workers other than farm-labourers; a set of people who owed a certain stability of aim and conduct to the fact of their being lifeholders like Tess’s father, or copyholders, or occasionally, small freeholders.", "마을에는 예전에 농업 노동자들과 나란히, 그들보다 분명 윗줄에 드는, 흥미롭고 더 식견 있는 한 계층이—테스의 아버지와 어머니가 속했던 계층이—있었으니, 목수와 대장장이, 제화공, 행상, 그리고 농장 일꾼이 아닌 정체 모를 여러 일꾼들이 거기 들었다. 이들은 테스 아버지처럼 종신보유농이거나, 등본보유농이거나, 이따금 작은 자유보유농인 덕분에 목표와 처신에 어느 정도의 안정을 누리던 사람들이었다."], ["But as the long holdings fell in, they were seldom again let to similar tenants, and were mostly pulled down, if not absolutely required by the farmer for his hands.", "그러나 그 긴 보유 기한이 끝나면, 그 땅은 좀처럼 비슷한 임차인들에게 다시 빌려지지 않았고, 차지농이 제 일손을 위해 꼭 필요로 하지 않는 한 대개 헐려 버렸다."], ["Cottagers who were not directly employed on the land were looked upon with disfavour, and the banishment of some starved the trade of others, who were thus obliged to follow.", "땅에 직접 고용되지 않은 오두막 거주민들은 못마땅하게 여겨졌고, 일부가 쫓겨나면 그것이 다른 이들의 생계를 굶주리게 했으니, 그들 역시 어쩔 수 없이 뒤따라야 했다."], ["These families, who had formed the backbone of the village life in the past, who were the depositaries of the village traditions, had to seek refuge in the large centres; the process, humorously designated by statisticians as “the tendency of the rural population towards the large towns”, being really the tendency of water to flow uphill when forced by machinery.", "지난날 마을 생활의 등뼈를 이루었던, 마을 전통의 보관자였던 이 가족들은 큰 중심지로 피난처를 구해야 했다. 통계학자들이 익살스레 \"농촌 인구의 대도시 지향 경향\"이라 이름 붙인 그 과정은, 실은 기계의 힘에 떠밀린 물이 언덕 위로 흐르는 경향이었다."]], [["The cottage accommodation at Marlott having been in this manner considerably curtailed by demolitions, every house which remained standing was required by the agriculturist for his work-people.", "* 등본보유농: 영주 법정 등본에 기록된 권리로 토지를 보유하던 소작농. 이런 식으로 말럿의 오두막 거처가 철거로 상당히 줄어들자, 남아 있는 집마다 농사꾼이 제 일꾼들을 위해 필요로 했다."], ["Ever since the occurrence of the event which had cast such a shadow over Tess’s life, the Durbeyfield family (whose descent was not credited) had been tacitly looked on as one which would have to go when their lease ended, if only in the interests of morality.", "테스의 삶에 그토록 짙은 그늘을 드리운 그 사건이 일어난 이래로, 더비필드 가족은(그들의 혈통은 믿어지지 않았다) 오직 도덕의 이익을 위해서라도 임차 기한이 끝나면 떠나야 할 집안으로 암묵리에 여겨져 왔다."], ["It was, indeed, quite true that the household had not been shining examples either of temperance, soberness, or chastity.", "사실 그 집안이 절제든, 진중함이든, 정숙함이든, 그 어느 면에서도 빛나는 본보기가 아니었던 것은 정말이지 사실이었다."], ["The father, and even the mother, had got drunk at times, the younger children seldom had gone to church, and the eldest daughter had made queer unions.", "아버지는, 심지어 어머니까지도, 때때로 술에 취했고, 어린 자식들은 좀처럼 교회에 가지 않았으며, 맏딸은 괴이한 결합을 했다."], ["By some means the village had to be kept pure.", "어떤 식으로든 마을은 순결하게 지켜져야 했다."], ["So on this, the first Lady-Day on which the Durbeyfields were expellable, the house, being roomy, was required for a carter with a large family; and Widow Joan, her daughters Tess and ’Liza-Lu, the boy Abraham, and the younger children had to go elsewhere.", "그래서 더비필드 가족이 쫓겨날 수 있게 된 이 첫 성모영보대축일에, 그 집은 널찍한지라 대가족을 거느린 짐마차꾼에게 필요했고, 과부 조앤과 그 딸들인 테스와 라이자루, 사내아이 에이브러햄, 그리고 더 어린 아이들은 다른 데로 가야 했다."]], [["On the evening preceding their removal it was getting dark betimes by reason of a drizzling rain which blurred the sky.", "그들이 떠나기 전날 저녁, 하늘을 흐릿하게 가린 이슬비 탓에 일찌감치 어두워지고 있었다."], ["As it was the last night they would spend in the village which had been their home and birthplace, Mrs Durbeyfield, ’Liza-Lu, and Abraham had gone out to bid some friends goodbye, and Tess was keeping house till they should return.", "그들의 집이자 출생지였던 마을에서 보내는 마지막 밤이었으므로, 더비필드 부인과 라이자루와 에이브러햄은 몇몇 벗들에게 작별을 고하러 나갔고, 테스는 그들이 돌아올 때까지 집을 지키고 있었다."]], [["She was kneeling in the window-bench, her face close to the casement, where an outer pane of rain-water was sliding down the inner pane of glass.", "그녀는 창가 의자에 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 창틀에 바짝 댄 채였는데, 거기서는 빗물의 바깥 막이 안쪽 유리창을 타고 미끄러져 내렸다."], ["Her eyes rested on the web of a spider, probably starved long ago, which had been mistakenly placed in a corner where no flies ever came, and shivered in the slight draught through the casement.", "그녀의 눈길은 거미줄에 머물렀다. 아마 오래전에 굶어 죽었을 그 거미는, 파리라곤 오는 법 없는 구석에 잘못 자리를 잡았던 모양으로, 창틀로 새어 드는 미미한 바람결에 떨고 있었다."], ["Tess was reflecting on the position of the household, in which she perceived her own evil influence.", "테스는 이 집안의 처지를 곱씹고 있었고, 거기서 자기 자신의 나쁜 영향을 알아보았다."], ["Had she not come home, her mother and the children might probably have been allowed to stay on as weekly tenants.", "자기가 집에 오지 않았다면, 어머니와 아이들은 아마 주 단위 세입자로 계속 머물게 허락되었을 것이다."], ["But she had been observed almost immediately on her return by some people of scrupulous character and great influence: they had seen her idling in the churchyard, restoring as well as she could with a little trowel a baby’s obliterated grave.", "그러나 그녀는 돌아오자마자 거의 곧바로, 깐깐한 성품에 큰 영향력을 지닌 어떤 사람들 눈에 띄고 말았다. 그들은 그녀가 교회 묘지에서 빈둥대며, 작은 모종삽으로 한 아기의 지워진 무덤을 제 딴엔 정성껏 손보는 것을 보았던 것이다."], ["By this means they had found that she was living here again; her mother was scolded for “harbouring”", "이로써 그들은 그녀가 다시 여기서 살고 있음을 알아챘고, 그녀의 어머니는 딸을 \"거두어들였다\"고 꾸지람을 들었다."], ["her; sharp retorts had ensued from Joan, who had independently offered to leave at once; she had been taken at her word; and here was the result.", "조앤은 발끈하여 쏘아붙였고, 제멋대로 당장 떠나겠노라 내뱉었다. 그러자 그 말이 그대로 받아들여졌고, 그 결과가 바로 이것이었다."]], [["“I ought never to have come home,”", "\"나는 절대 집에 오지 말았어야 했어.\""], ["said Tess to herself, bitterly.", "테스가 쓰라리게 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["She was so intent upon these thoughts that she hardly at first took note of a man in a white mackintosh whom she saw riding down the street.", "이런 생각에 어찌나 골몰했던지, 그녀는 거리를 말 타고 내려오는 흰 방수외투 차림의 한 남자를 처음에는 거의 알아채지 못했다."], ["Possibly it was owing to her face being near to the pane that he saw her so quickly, and directed his horse so close to the cottage-front that his hoofs were almost upon the narrow border for plants growing under the wall.", "아마 그녀의 얼굴이 유리창에 바짝 붙어 있던 탓에 그가 그녀를 그토록 빨리 보았을 것이고, 말을 오두막 정면에 어찌나 바싹 몰았던지 말굽이 거의 벽 밑 화초 자라는 좁은 화단 위에 닿을 듯했다."], ["It was not till he touched the window with his riding-crop that she observed him.", "그가 승마용 채찍으로 창을 두드리고서야 그녀는 그를 알아보았다."], ["The rain had nearly ceased, and she opened the casement in obedience to his gesture.", "비가 거의 그쳐 있었고, 그녀는 그의 손짓에 따라 창틀을 열었다."]], [["“Didn’t you see me?”", "\"나 못 봤어?\""], ["asked d’Urberville.", "더버빌이 물었다."]], [["“I was not attending,” she said.", "\"정신을 딴 데 두고 있었어요.\""], ["“I heard you, I believe, though I fancied it was a carriage and horses.", "그녀가 말했다. \"들은 것 같긴 한데, 마차와 말 소리인 줄 알았어요."], ["I was in a sort of dream.”", "일종의 꿈에 잠겨 있었거든요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you heard the d’Urberville Coach, perhaps.", "어쩌면 더버빌의 마차 소리를 들었던 거겠지."], ["You know the legend, I suppose?”", "그 전설을 너도 알 테지?\""]], [["“No. My—somebody was going to tell it me once, but didn’t.”", "\"아니요. 누가—누군가 언젠가 그걸 나한테 들려주려다가 말았어요.\""]], [["“If you are a genuine d’Urberville I ought not to tell you either, I suppose.", "\"네가 진짜 더버빌이라면 나도 너한테 들려줘선 안 되겠지."], ["As for me, I’m a sham one, so it doesn’t matter.", "하지만 나야 가짜니까 상관없어."], ["It is rather dismal.", "좀 음산한 얘기야."], ["It is that this sound of a non-existent coach can only be heard by one of d’Urberville blood, and it is held to be of ill-omen to the one who hears it.", "존재하지도 않는 마차의 그 소리는 더버빌 핏줄을 타고난 자만이 들을 수 있는데, 그걸 듣는 자에게는 흉조라고 여겨지지."], ["It has to do with a murder, committed by one of the family, centuries ago.”", "여러 세기 전에 그 집안 사람 하나가 저지른 살인과 관련된 거야.\""]], [["“Now you have begun it, finish it.”", "\"이미 시작했으니, 끝까지 마저 해 봐요.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아."], ["One of the family is said to have abducted some beautiful woman, who tried to escape from the coach in which he was carrying her off, and in the struggle he killed her—or she killed him—I forget which.", "그 집안 사람 하나가 어떤 아름다운 여자를 납치했다는데, 그녀가 자기를 싣고 가던 마차에서 달아나려 하자, 몸싸움 중에 그가 그녀를 죽였다는 거야—아니면 그녀가 그를 죽였든가—어느 쪽인지는 잊어버렸군."], ["Such is one version of the tale...", "이게 그 이야기의 한 가지 판본이지…"], ["I see that your tubs and buckets are packed.", "보아하니 네 통이며 양동이가 다 꾸려져 있네."], ["Going away, aren’t you?”", "떠나는 거지, 안 그래?\""]], [["“Yes, to-morrow—Old Lady Day.”", "\"네, 내일요—옛 성모영보대축일에요.\""]], [["“I heard you were, but could hardly believe it; it seems so sudden.", "\"떠난다는 말을 듣긴 했는데, 좀처럼 믿기지가 않았어. 너무 갑작스러워서 말이지."], ["Why is it?”", "왜 그런 거지?\""]], [["“Father’s was the last life on the property, and when that dropped we had no further right to stay.", "\"아버지가 그 집의 마지막 목숨이었는데, 그게 끊기자 우리는 더 머물 권리가 없어졌어요."], ["Though we might, perhaps, have stayed as weekly tenants—if it had not been for me.”", "어쩌면 주 단위 세입자로는 머물 수 있었을지도 몰라요—나만 아니었다면요.\""]], [["“What about you?”", "\"네가 어떻다는 거지?\""]], [["“I am not a—proper woman.”", "\"나는—제대로 된 여자가 아니거든요.\""]], [["D’Urberville’s face flushed.", "더버빌의 얼굴이 붉어졌다."]], [["“What a blasted shame!", "\"이런 빌어먹을 수치스러운 노릇이 있나!"], ["Miserable snobs!", "한심한 속물들 같으니!"], ["May their dirty souls be burnt to cinders!”", "그 더러운 영혼들이 새까맣게 타 재가 되어 버려라!\""], ["he exclaimed in tones of ironic resentment.", "그가 비꼬는 듯한 분노의 어조로 외쳤다."], ["“That’s why you are going, is it?", "\"그래서 떠나는 거란 말이지?"], ["Turned out?”", "쫓겨나는 거야?\""]], [["“We are not turned out exactly; but as they said we should have to go soon, it was best to go now everybody was moving, because there are better chances.”", "\"꼭 쫓겨나는 건 아니에요. 하지만 곧 떠나야 할 거라고들 하니까, 다들 옮겨 가는 지금 떠나는 게 낫다고요. 더 나은 기회가 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Where are you going to?”", "\"어디로 갈 건데?\""]], [["“Kingsbere.", "\"킹스비어요."], ["We have taken rooms there.", "거기에 방을 얻어 뒀어요."], ["Mother is so foolish about father’s people that she will go there.”", "어머니가 아버지 집안 사람들 일이라면 어찌나 어리석으신지, 거기로 가시겠대요.\""]], [["“But your mother’s family are not fit for lodgings, and in a little hole of a town like that.", "\"하지만 네 어머니 집안은 셋방살이에는 맞지 않아, 그것도 그런 코딱지만 한 동네에서."], ["Now why not come to my garden-house at Trantridge?", "자, 그러지 말고 트랜트리지에 있는 내 정원 별채로 오는 게 어때?"], ["There are hardly any poultry now, since my mother’s death; but there’s the house, as you know it, and the garden.", "어머니가 돌아가신 뒤로는 이제 가금류도 거의 없어. 하지만 집은 네가 알던 그대로 있고, 정원도 있어."], ["It can be whitewashed in a day, and your mother can live there quite comfortably; and I will put the children to a good school.", "하루면 흰 칠을 새로 할 수 있고, 네 어머니는 거기서 아주 편안히 지낼 수 있어. 그리고 아이들은 내가 좋은 학교에 넣어 주지."], ["Really I ought to do something for you!”", "정말이지 나는 너를 위해 뭔가 해야만 해!\""]], [["“But we have already taken the rooms at Kingsbere!” she declared.", "\"하지만 우리는 이미 킹스비어에 방을 얻었는걸요!\" 그녀가 잘라 말했다."], ["“And we can wait there—”", "\"거기서 기다릴 수 있고요—\""]], [["“Wait—what for?", "\"기다려—뭘?"], ["For that nice husband, no doubt.", "그 멋진 남편을 기다리는 거겠지, 틀림없이."], ["Now look here, Tess, I know what men are, and, bearing in mind the _grounds_ of your separation, I am quite positive he will never make it up with you.", "자, 이봐, 테스, 나는 남자가 어떤지 알아. 그리고 너희가 갈라선 사연을 염두에 두면, 그가 너와 화해하는 일은 결코 없으리라고 나는 아주 확신해."], ["Now, though I have been your enemy, I am your friend, even if you won’t believe it.", "자, 나는 네 적이었지만, 네가 믿지 않더라도 나는 네 친구야."], ["Come to this cottage of mine.", "이 내 오두막으로 와."], ["We’ll get up a regular colony of fowls, and your mother can attend to them excellently; and the children can go to school.”", "우리가 제대로 된 닭 무리를 키워 보자고. 네 어머니가 그걸 아주 훌륭하게 돌볼 수 있을 거야. 그리고 아이들은 학교에 다니면 되고.\""]], [["Tess breathed more and more quickly, and at length she said—", "테스의 숨이 점점 더 빨라졌고, 마침내 그녀가 말했다—"]], [["“How do I know that you would do all this?", "\"당신이 이 모든 걸 해 주리란 걸 내가 어떻게 알아요?"], ["Your views may change—and then—we should be—my mother would be—homeless again.”", "당신 마음이 바뀔 수도 있고—그러면—우리는—내 어머니는—다시 집 없는 신세가 될 텐데요.\""]], [["“O no—no. I would guarantee you against such as that in writing, if necessary.", "\"아 아니야—아니야. 필요하다면 그런 일이 없도록 문서로 보장해 주마."], ["Think it over.”", "잘 생각해 봐.\""]], [["Tess shook her head.", "테스는 고개를 저었다."], ["But d’Urberville persisted; she had seldom seen him so determined; he would not take a negative.", "그러나 더버빌은 끈질겼다. 그녀는 그가 그토록 단호한 것을 좀처럼 본 적이 없었다. 그는 거절을 받아들이려 하지 않았다."]], [["“Please just tell your mother,” he said, in emphatic tones.", "\"제발 네 어머니한테 말이라도 해 봐.\" 그가 힘주어 말했다."], ["“It is her business to judge—not yours.", "\"판단은 네가 아니라 어머니가 할 일이야."], ["I shall get the house swept out and whitened to-morrow morning, and fires lit; and it will be dry by the evening, so that you can come straight there.", "나는 내일 아침 집을 쓸어 내고 흰 칠을 하고 불을 지펴 두게 할 거야. 그러면 저녁까지는 다 마를 테니, 너는 곧장 그리로 오면 돼."], ["Now mind, I shall expect you.”", "자, 명심해, 나는 너를 기다리고 있을 거야.\""]], [["Tess again shook her head, her throat swelling with complicated emotion.", "테스는 또 고개를 저었고, 그녀의 목은 복잡한 감정으로 메었다."], ["She could not look up at d’Urberville.", "그녀는 더버빌을 올려다볼 수가 없었다."]], [["“I owe you something for the past, you know,”", "\"나는 지난 일로 너한테 빚진 게 있잖니, 너도 알다시피.\""], ["he resumed.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“And you cured me, too, of that craze; so I am glad—”", "\"그리고 너는 그 광기에서도 나를 낫게 해 줬고. 그러니 나는 기쁘구나—\""]], [["“I would rather you had kept the craze, so that you had kept the practice which went with it!”", "\"차라리 당신이 그 광기를 그대로 간직했더라면 좋았을걸요. 그래서 거기 딸려 있던 그 신앙생활도 함께 지켰더라면요!\""]], [["“I am glad of this opportunity of repaying you a little.", "\"이 기회에 너에게 조금이나마 갚을 수 있어 기쁘다."], ["To-morrow I shall expect to hear your mother’s goods unloading...", "내일이면 네 어머니의 살림살이가 내려지는 소리를 들을 수 있겠지…"], ["Give me your hand on it now—dear, beautiful Tess!”", "자, 그 약속의 표시로 이제 손을 다오—사랑스럽고 아름다운 테스!\""]], [["With the last sentence he had dropped his voice to a murmur, and put his hand in at the half-open casement.", "마지막 말에서 그는 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추었고, 반쯤 열린 창틀 안으로 손을 들이밀었다."], ["With stormy eyes she pulled the stay-bar quickly, and, in doing so, caught his arm between the casement and the stone mullion.", "폭풍 같은 눈으로 그녀는 잽싸게 고정 막대를 잡아당겼는데, 그러는 바람에 그의 팔이 창틀과 돌 문설주 사이에 끼고 말았다."]], [["“Damnation—you are very cruel!”", "\"빌어먹을—너 참 잔인하구나!\""], ["he said, snatching out his arm.", "그가 팔을 홱 빼내며 말했다."], ["“No, no!—I know you didn’t do it on purpose.", "\"아니, 아니야!—일부러 그런 게 아니란 건 나도 알아."], ["Well, I shall expect you, or your mother and children at least.”", "좋아, 나는 너를, 아니 적어도 네 어머니와 아이들을 기다리고 있을 거야.\""]], [["“I shall not come—I have plenty of money!”", "\"나는 안 갈 거예요—나한테는 돈이 많이 있어요!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디에?\""]], [["“At my father-in-law’s, if I ask for it.”", "\"내 시아버지한테요, 청하기만 하면요.\""]], [["“_If_ you ask for it.", "\"청하기만 하면이라."], ["But you won’t, Tess; I know you; you’ll never ask for it—you’ll starve first!”", "하지만 너는 청하지 않을걸, 테스. 나는 너를 알아. 너는 결코 청하지 않을 거야—먼저 굶어 죽고 말겠지!\""]], [["With these words he rode off. Just at the corner of the street he met the man with the paint-pot, who asked him if he had deserted the brethren.", "이 말과 함께 그는 말을 몰고 가 버렸다. 거리 모퉁이에서 바로 그는 페인트 통을 든 남자와 마주쳤는데, 그 남자가 그에게 형제들을 저버린 거냐고 물었다."]], [["“You go to the devil!”", "\"꺼져 버려!\""], ["said d’Urberville.", "더버빌이 말했다."]], [["Tess remained where she was a long while, till a sudden rebellious sense of injustice caused the region of her eyes to swell with the rush of hot tears thither.", "테스는 한참 동안 그 자리에 그대로 있었다. 그러다 문득 불의에 대한 반항의 감정이 솟구쳐, 뜨거운 눈물이 한꺼번에 밀려들며 두 눈 언저리가 부풀어 올랐다."], ["Her husband, Angel Clare himself, had, like others, dealt out hard measure to her; surely he had!", "그녀의 남편, 다름 아닌 에인절 클레어조차도, 다른 이들처럼 그녀에게 가혹한 처사를 내렸던 것이다."], ["She had never before admitted such a thought; but he had surely!", "정말 그랬다! 그녀는 전에는 그런 생각을 결코 인정한 적이 없었다."], ["Never in her life—she could swear it from the bottom of her soul—had she ever intended to do wrong; yet these hard judgements had come.", "그러나 그는 정말로 그랬다! 평생에 단 한 번도—영혼 깊은 데서 그녀는 맹세할 수 있었다—그녀는 잘못을 저지르려 한 적이 없었다. 그런데도 이런 가혹한 심판들이 닥쳤다."], ["Whatever her sins, they were not sins of intention, but of inadvertence, and why should she have been punished so persistently?", "그녀의 죄가 무엇이었든, 그것은 고의의 죄가 아니라 부주의의 죄였는데, 어째서 그녀는 이토록 끈질기게 벌받아야 했단 말인가?"]], [["She passionately seized the first piece of paper that came to hand, and scribbled the following lines:", "그녀는 격정에 사로잡혀 손에 잡히는 첫 종잇조각을 움켜쥐고는, 다음과 같은 글을 휘갈겼다."]], [["O why have you treated me so monstrously, Angel!", "아, 어째서 당신은 나를 그토록 끔찍하게 대했나요, 에인절!"], ["I do not deserve it.", "나는 그럴 만하지 않아요."], ["I have thought it all over carefully, and I can never, never forgive you!", "나는 이 모든 걸 곰곰이 따져 봤고, 나는 결코, 결코 당신을 용서할 수 없어요!"], ["You know that I did not intend to wrong you—why have you so wronged me?", "내가 당신에게 잘못하려던 게 아니었음을 당신도 알면서—어째서 당신은 나에게 그토록 잘못을 저질렀나요?"], ["You are cruel, cruel indeed!", "당신은 잔인해요, 정말이지 잔인해요!"], ["I will try to forget you.", "나는 당신을 잊으려 애쓸 거예요."], ["It is all injustice I have received at your hands! T.", "내가 당신 손에서 받은 건 죄다 불의예요! T."]], [["She watched till the postman passed by, ran out to him with her epistle, and then again took her listless place inside the window-panes.", "그녀는 우편배달부가 지나갈 때까지 지켜보다가, 편지를 들고 그에게 달려 나갔고, 그러고는 다시 창유리 안쪽 제자리에 맥없이 앉았다."]], [["It was just as well to write like that as to write tenderly.", "그렇게 다정하게 쓰느니 차라리 이렇게 쓰는 편이 나았다."], ["How could he give way to entreaty?", "어떻게\n그가 애원에 굴복할 것인가?"], ["The facts had not changed: there was no new event to alter his opinion.", "사실은 변하지 않았다. 그의 생각을 바꿔 놓을 새로운 사건은 없었다."]], [["It grew darker, the fire-light shining over the room.", "점점 어두워졌고, 불빛이 방 안을 비추었다."], ["The two biggest of the younger children had gone out with their mother; the four smallest, their ages ranging from three-and-a-half years to eleven, all in black frocks, were gathered round the hearth babbling their own little subjects.", "어린아이들 중 가장 큰 둘은 어머니와 함께 나갔고, 가장 어린 넷은, 세 살 반부터 열한 살까지인 그 아이들은, 모두 검은 옷을 입고 난롯가에 모여 저희끼리 사소한 이야기를 재잘거리고 있었다."], ["Tess at length joined them, without lighting a candle.", "마침내 테스가 촛불도 켜지 않은 채 그들 곁에 끼어들었다."]], [["“This is the last night that we shall sleep here, dears, in the house where we were born,”", "\"오늘이 우리가 여기서, 우리가 태어난 이 집에서 자는 마지막 밤이란다, 얘들아.\""], ["she said quickly.", "그녀가 빠르게 말했다."], ["“We ought to think of it, oughtn’t we?”", "\"이걸 생각해 둬야 해, 그렇지 않니?\""]], [["They all became silent; with the impressibility of their age they were ready to burst into tears at the picture of finality she had conjured up, though all the day hitherto they had been rejoicing in the idea of a new place.", "아이들은 모두 입을 다물었다. 그 나이다운 감수성으로 그들은 그녀가 불러일으킨 종말의 그림에 금방이라도 울음을 터뜨릴 듯했다. 그날 내내 새로운 곳으로 간다는 생각에 들떠 있었으면서도."], ["Tess changed the subject.", "테스는 화제를 바꾸었다."]], [["“Sing to me, dears,”", "\"노래해 줘, 얘들아.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“What shall we sing?”", "\"무슨 노래 부를까?\""]], [["“Anything you know; I don’t mind.”", "\"아무거나 아는 거. 난 상관없어.\""]], [["There was a momentary pause; it was broken, first, in one little tentative note; then a second voice strengthened it, and a third and a fourth chimed in unison, with words they had learnt at the Sunday-school—", "잠깐 멈춤이 있었다. 그것은 먼저 머뭇거리는 작은 한 음으로 깨졌다. 그러더니 두 번째 목소리가 그것을 받쳐 주었고, 세 번째와 네 번째가 한목소리로 어우러졌다. 주일학교에서 배운 가사로—"]], [["Here we suffer grief and pain, Here we meet to part again; In Heaven we part no more.", "여기서 우리는 슬픔과 고통을 겪고, 여기서 만나 다시 헤어지네. 천국에서는 더 이상 헤어지지 않으리."]], [["The four sang on with the phlegmatic passivity of persons who had long ago settled the question, and there being no mistake about it, felt that further thought was not required.", "넷은 오래전에 그 문제를 매듭지어 버려, 거기에 틀림이 없으니 더 생각할 필요가 없다고 느끼는 사람들의 무덤덤한 수동성으로 계속 불렀다."], ["With features strained hard to enunciate the syllables they continued to regard the centre of the flickering fire, the notes of the youngest straying over into the pauses of the rest.", "음절을 또박또박 내려고 얼굴을 잔뜩 긴장시킨 채 그들은 일렁이는 불의 한가운데를 계속 바라보았고, 가장 어린아이의 음이 나머지 아이들의 쉼표 사이로 흘러들었다."]], [["Tess turned from them, and went to the window again.", "테스는 그들에게서 돌아서 다시 창가로 갔다."], ["Darkness had now fallen without, but she put her face to the pane as though to peer into the gloom.", "이제 바깥은 어둠이 내렸지만, 그녀는 어둠 속을 들여다보려는 듯 유리창에 얼굴을 댔다."], ["It was really to hide her tears.", "사실은 눈물을 감추려는 것이었다."], ["If she could only believe what the children were singing; if she were only sure, how different all would now be; how confidently she would leave them to Providence and their future kingdom!", "아이들이 부르는 그 노래를 믿을 수만 있다면, 확신만 할 수 있다면, 지금 모든 것이 얼마나 다를까. 얼마나 마음 놓고 아이들을 신의 섭리와 그들의 장차 올 왕국에 맡길 수 있을까!"], ["But, in default of that, it behoved her to do something; to be their Providence; for to Tess, as to not a few millions of others, there was ghastly satire in the poet’s lines—", "그러나 그러지 못하는 한, 그녀가 무언가를 해야 했다. 아이들의 섭리가 되어 주어야 했다. 적잖은 수백만의 다른 이들에게처럼 테스에게도, 시인의 시구에는 끔찍한 풍자가 깃들어 있었으니—"]], [["Not in utter nakedness But trailing clouds of glory do we come.", "완전히 헐벗은 채로가 아니라 영광의 구름을 끌며 우리는 온다."]], [["To her and her like, birth itself was an ordeal of degrading personal compulsion, whose gratuitousness nothing in the result seemed to justify, and at best could only palliate.", "그녀와 그녀 같은 이들에게 태어남 자체가 굴욕적인 개인적 강제의 시련이었으며, 그 결과의 무엇으로도 정당화될 수 없는, 기껏해야 누그러뜨릴 수 있을 뿐인 까닭 없는 시련이었다."]], [["In the shades of the wet road she soon discerned her mother with tall ’Liza-Lu and Abraham.", "젖은 길의 그늘 속에서 그녀는 곧 키 큰 '라이자루와 에이브러햄과 함께 오는 어머니를 알아보았다."], ["Mrs Durbeyfield’s pattens clicked up to the door, and Tess opened it.", "더비필드 부인의 나막신이 또각거리며 문 앞까지 왔고, 테스가 문을 열었다."]], [["“I see the tracks of a horse outside the window,” said Joan.", "\"창밖에 말 발자국이 보이는구나.\" 조앤이 말했다."], ["“Hev somebody called?”", "\"누가 다녀갔니?\""]], [["“No,” said Tess.", "\"아니요.\" 테스가 말했다."]], [["The children by the fire looked gravely at her, and one murmured—", "난롯가의 아이들이 그녀를 심각하게 쳐다보았고, 하나가 중얼거렸다—"]], [["“Why, Tess, the gentleman a-horseback!”", "\"왜, 테스, 말 탄 신사분 말이야!\""]], [["“He didn’t call,”", "\"다녀간 건 아니에요.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."], ["“He spoke to me in passing.”", "\"지나가다 제게 말을 걸었어요.\""]], [["“Who was the gentleman?”", "\"그 신사가 누구였니?\""], ["asked the mother.", "어머니가 물었다."], ["“Your husband?”", "\"네 남편이냐?\""]], [["“No. He’ll never, never come,”", "\"아니요. 그이는 절대로, 절대로 오지 않을 거예요.\""], ["answered Tess in stony hopelessness.", "테스가 돌처럼 굳은 절망 속에서 대답했다."]], [["“Then who was it?”", "\"그럼 누구였느냐?\""]], [["“Oh, you needn’t ask.", "\"아, 물으실 거 없어요."], ["You’ve seen him before, and so have I.”", "어머니도 전에 본 적 있고, 저도 봤어요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["What did he say?”", "뭐라고 하더냐?\""], ["said Joan curiously.", "조앤이 궁금해하며 말했다."]], [["“I will tell you when we are settled in our lodging at Kingsbere to-morrow—every word.”", "\"내일 킹스비어 숙소에 자리를 잡으면 말씀드릴게요—한마디 한마디 다요.\""]], [["It was not her husband, she had said.", "그녀의 남편은 아니라고, 그녀는 말했었다."], ["Yet a consciousness that in a physical sense this man alone was her husband seemed to weigh on her more and more.", "그러나 육체적 의미에서는 오직 이 남자만이 자기 남편이라는 의식이 점점 더 무겁게 그녀를 짓누르는 듯했다."]], [["# LII", "# LII"]], [["During the small hours of the next morning, while it was still dark, dwellers near the highways were conscious of a disturbance of their night’s rest by rumbling noises, intermittently continuing till daylight—noises as certain to recur in this particular first week of the month as the voice of the cuckoo in the third week of the same.", "다음 날 아침 아직 어두운 새벽녘에, 큰길 가까이 사는 이들은 덜컹거리는 소음에 밤잠이 깨지는 것을 느꼈다. 그 소리는 날이 밝을 때까지 띄엄띄엄 이어졌다—이달 첫째 주에는 어김없이 되풀이되는 소음으로, 같은 달 셋째 주의 뻐꾸기 소리만큼이나 확실한 것이었다."], ["They were the preliminaries of the general removal, the passing of the empty waggons and teams to fetch the goods of the migrating families; for it was always by the vehicle of the farmer who required his services that the hired man was conveyed to his destination.", "그것은 대규모 이사의 전조였으니, 이주하는 가족들의 짐을 실으러 가는 빈 마차와 마부들이 지나가는 소리였다. 고용된 일꾼은 늘 자기 일손을 필요로 하는 농장주의 마차로 목적지까지 옮겨졌기 때문이다."], ["That this might be accomplished within the day was the explanation of the reverberation occurring so soon after midnight, the aim of the carters being to reach the door of the outgoing households by six o’clock, when the loading of their movables at once began.", "이 일을 하루 안에 끝내야 한다는 사실이, 자정 직후부터 그 울림이 시작되는 까닭을 설명해 주었다. 마부들의 목표는 여섯 시까지 이사 나가는 집들의 문 앞에 닿는 것이었고, 그때 곧장 짐 싣기가 시작되었다."]], [["But to Tess and her mother’s household no such anxious farmer sent his team.", "그러나 테스와 그녀 어머니의 살림에는 그런 조바심 내는 농장주가 마차를 보내 주지 않았다."], ["They were only women; they were not regular labourers; they were not particularly required anywhere; hence they had to hire a waggon at their own expense, and got nothing sent gratuitously.", "그들은 여자뿐이었고, 정규 일꾼도 아니었으며, 어디서도 딱히 필요로 하지 않았다. 그래서 그들은 제 돈으로 마차를 빌려야 했고, 거저 보내 주는 것은 하나도 없었다."]], [["It was a relief to Tess, when she looked out of the window that morning, to find that though the weather was windy and louring, it did not rain, and that the waggon had come.", "그날 아침 창밖을 내다보았을 때, 날씨가 바람 불고 잔뜩 흐리긴 해도 비가 내리지 않고 마차가 도착한 것을 알게 된 것은 테스에게 다행이었다."], ["A wet Lady-Day was a spectre which removing families never forgot; damp furniture, damp bedding, damp clothing accompanied it, and left a train of ills.", "비 내리는 성모영보 대축일은 이사하는 가족들이 결코 잊지 못하는 유령 같은 것이었다. 축축한 가구, 축축한 침구, 축축한 옷가지가 따라붙어 온갖 병폐의 행렬을 남겼다."]], [["Her mother, ’Liza-Lu, and Abraham were also awake, but the younger children were let sleep on.", "그녀의 어머니와 '라이자루와 에이브러햄도 깨어 있었지만, 어린아이들은 그대로 자게 두었다."], ["The four breakfasted by the thin light, and the “house-ridding” was taken in hand.", "넷은 희미한 빛 속에서 아침을 먹었고, \"집 비우기\"에 착수했다."]], [["It proceeded with some cheerfulness, a friendly neighbour or two assisting.", "그 일은 어느 정도 활기차게 진행되었으니, 친절한 이웃 한둘이 거들었다."], ["When the large articles of furniture had been packed in position, a circular nest was made of the beds and bedding, in which Joan Durbeyfield and the young children were to sit through the journey.", "큰 가구들이 자리 잡아 실리고 나자, 침대와 침구로 둥근 둥지를 만들었고, 그 안에 조앤 더비필드와 어린아이들이 여정 내내 앉아 가기로 했다."], ["After loading there was a long delay before the horses were brought, these having been unharnessed during the ridding; but at length, about two o’clock, the whole was under way, the cooking-pot swinging from the axle of the waggon, Mrs Durbeyfield and family at the top, the matron having in her lap, to prevent injury to its works, the head of the clock, which, at any exceptional lurch of the waggon, struck one, or one-and-a-half, in hurt tones.", "짐을 다 싣고 나서 말들을 데려오기까지 오랜 지체가 있었으니, 짐을 빼는 동안 말들의 마구를 풀어 놓았던 탓이었다. 하지만 마침내 두 시쯤 모든 것이 길에 올랐다. 냄비가 마차 차축에 매달려 흔들렸고, 더비필드 부인과 식구들이 맨 위에 앉았으며, 그 부인은 시계 부속이 상하지 않도록 시계 머리를 무릎에 안고 있었는데, 마차가 유난히 출렁일 때마다 그 시계가 한 시, 또는 한 시 반을 다친 듯한 소리로 쳤다."], ["Tess and the next eldest girl walked alongside till they were out of the village.", "테스와 그 다음으로 큰 딸은 마을을 벗어날 때까지 마차 옆에서 걸었다."]], [["They had called on a few neighbours that morning and the previous evening, and some came to see them off, all wishing them well, though, in their secret hearts, hardly expecting welfare possible to such a family, harmless as the Durbeyfields were to all except themselves.", "그들은 그날 아침과 전날 저녁에 이웃 몇 집을 찾아갔고, 더러는 배웅을 나와 모두 잘되기를 빌어 주었다. 비록 속마음으로는 그런 가족에게 행복이 가능하리라곤 거의 기대하지 않았지만 말이다. 더비필드네는 저들 자신을 빼고는 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않는 이들이었으니."], ["Soon the equipage began to ascend to higher ground, and the wind grew keener with the change of level and soil.", "곧 마차 행렬이 더 높은 지대로 오르기 시작했고, 지형과 토양이 바뀌면서 바람이 더 매서워졌다."]], [["The day being the sixth of April, the Durbeyfield waggon met many other waggons with families on the summit of the load, which was built on a wellnigh unvarying principle, as peculiar, probably, to the rural labourer as the hexagon to the bee.", "그날이 사월 엿새였으므로, 더비필드네 마차는 짐 꼭대기에 식구들을 태운 다른 마차들을 많이 만났다. 그 짐들은 거의 변함없는 원칙에 따라 쌓여 있었는데, 아마도 벌에게 육각형이 그러하듯 시골 일꾼에게 고유한 방식이었을 것이다."], ["The groundwork of the arrangement was the family dresser, which, with its shining handles, and finger-marks, and domestic evidences thick upon it, stood importantly in front, over the tails of the shaft-horses, in its erect and natural position, like some Ark of the Covenant that they were bound to carry reverently.", "짐 쌓기의 바탕은 집안의 찬장으로, 번쩍이는 손잡이와 손때 자국과 살림의 흔적이 잔뜩 묻은 그것이, 끌채 말들의 꽁무니 위로 똑바로 자연스러운 자세로 앞쪽에 위풍당당하게 서 있었으니, 마치 경건히 모시고 가야 할 어떤 언약궤 같았다."]], [["Some of the households were lively, some mournful; some were stopping at the doors of wayside inns; where, in due time, the Durbeyfield menagerie also drew up to bait horses and refresh the travellers.", "어떤 살림들은 활기찼고 어떤 것은 침울했다. 더러는 길가 여관 문 앞에 멈춰 서 있었는데, 거기서 때가 되자 더비필드네 짐승 무리도 말에게 먹이를 주고 길손들을 쉬게 하려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["During the halt Tess’s eyes fell upon a three-pint blue mug, which was ascending and descending through the air to and from the feminine section of a household, sitting on the summit of a load that had also drawn up at a little distance from the same inn.", "멈춰 선 동안 테스의 눈에 삼 파인트짜리 파란 머그잔이 들어왔는데, 그것이 같은 여관에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 역시 멈춰 선 어느 살림의 짐 꼭대기에 앉은 여자들 쪽으로 오르내리며 공중을 오가고 있었다."], ["She followed one of the mug’s journeys upward, and perceived it to be clasped by hands whose owner she well knew.", "그녀는 그 머그잔이 위로 올라가는 한 번의 여정을 눈으로 좇다가, 그것을 움켜쥔 손의 주인을 자기가 잘 안다는 걸 알아챘다."], ["Tess went towards the waggon.", "테스는 그 마차로 다가갔다."]], [["“Marian and Izz!”", "\"마리안과 이즈!\""], ["she cried to the girls, for it was they, sitting with the moving family at whose house they had lodged.", "그녀가 두 처녀에게 소리쳤다. 정말 그들이었으니, 자기들이 묵던 집의 이사 가는 가족과 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["“Are you house-ridding to-day, like everybody else?”", "\"너희도 오늘 다들처럼 집 비우는 거야?\""]], [["They were, they said.", "그렇다고, 그들이 말했다."], ["It had been too rough a life for them at Flintcomb-Ash, and they had come away, almost without notice, leaving Groby to prosecute them if he chose.", "플린트콤애시에서의 삶은 그들에게 너무 거칠었고, 그래서 거의 통지도 없이 떠나왔다고, 그로비가 원한다면 자기들을 고소하라고 내버려 둔 채 말이다."], ["They told Tess their destination, and Tess told them hers.", "그들이 테스에게 자기들 목적지를 말했고, 테스도 자기 목적지를 말해 주었다."]], [["Marian leant over the load, and lowered her voice.", "마리안이 짐 너머로 몸을 기울이며 목소리를 낮추었다."], ["“Do you know that the gentleman who follows ’ee—you’ll guess who I mean—came to ask for ’ee at Flintcomb after you had gone?", "\"너를 따라다니는 그 신사 말이야—누군지 짐작하겠지—네가 떠난 뒤에 플린트콤으로 너를 찾으러 왔던 거 알아?"], ["We didn’t tell’n where you was, knowing you wouldn’t wish to see him.”", "우린 네가 그 사람 보기 싫어할 줄 알고 어디 있는지 말 안 했어.\""]], [["“Ah—but I did see him!”", "\"아—그런데 난 그 사람을 봤어!\""], ["Tess murmured.", "테스가 중얼거렸다."], ["“He found me.”", "\"그이가 나를 찾아냈어.\""]], [["“And do he know where you be going?”", "\"그럼 네가 어디로 가는지도 알아?\""]], [["“I think so.”", "\"그럴 거야.\""]], [["“Husband come back?”", "\"남편은 돌아왔어?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["She bade her acquaintance goodbye—for the respective carters had now come out from the inn—and the two waggons resumed their journey in opposite directions; the vehicle whereon sat Marian, Izz, and the ploughman’s family with whom they had thrown in their lot, being brightly painted, and drawn by three powerful horses with shining brass ornaments on their harness; while the waggon on which Mrs Durbeyfield and her family rode was a creaking erection that would scarcely bear the weight of the superincumbent load; one which had known no paint since it was made, and drawn by two horses only.", "그녀는 아는 이들에게 작별을 고했다—각자의 마부들이 이제 여관에서 나왔으므로—그리고 두 마차는 서로 반대 방향으로 여정을 다시 시작했다. 마리안과 이즈와 그들이 운명을 함께하기로 한 농부의 가족이 탄 마차는 산뜻하게 칠해져 있었고, 마구에 번쩍이는 놋쇠 장식을 단 힘센 말 세 마리가 끌었다. 반면 더비필드 부인과 식구들이 탄 마차는 그 위에 얹힌 짐 무게를 겨우 견딜까 말까 한 삐걱대는 구조물이었으니, 만들어진 이래 한 번도 칠을 해 본 적 없고 말 두 마리만이 끄는 것이었다."], ["The contrast well marked the difference between being fetched by a thriving farmer and conveying oneself whither no hirer waited one’s coming.", "그 대조는 번창하는 농장주가 데리러 와 주는 것과, 고용주가 와 주기를 기다리지 않는 곳으로 제 힘으로 옮겨 가는 것 사이의 차이를 잘 드러냈다."]], [["The distance was great—too great for a day’s journey—and it was with the utmost difficulty that the horses performed it.", "거리는 멀었다—하루 여정으로는 너무 멀었다—말들은 그것을 더없이 힘겹게 해냈다."], ["Though they had started so early, it was quite late in the afternoon when they turned the flank of an eminence which formed part of the upland called Greenhill.", "그토록 일찍 떠났는데도, 그린힐이라 불리는 고지의 일부를 이루는 한 언덕의 옆구리를 돌아갈 무렵에는 이미 오후가 한참 깊어 있었다."], ["While the horses stood to stale and breathe themselves Tess looked around.", "말들이 멈춰 서서 오줌을 누고 숨을 고르는 동안 테스는 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Under the hill, and just ahead of them, was the half-dead townlet of their pilgrimage, Kingsbere, where lay those ancestors of whom her father had spoken and sung to painfulness: Kingsbere, the spot of all spots in the world which could be considered the d’Urbervilles’ home, since they had resided there for full five hundred years.", "언덕 아래, 바로 그들 앞쪽에 그들이 순례해 온 반쯤 죽은 작은 읍이 있었다. 킹스비어, 아버지가 아프도록 이야기하고 노래하던 그 조상들이 누워 있는 곳이었다. 킹스비어, 더버빌가가 꼬박 오백 년을 살았으니 세상의 온갖 장소 중에서도 더버빌가의 본가라 할 수 있을 바로 그곳이었다."]], [["A man could be seen advancing from the outskirts towards them, and when he beheld the nature of their waggon-load he quickened his steps.", "한 남자가 변두리에서 그들 쪽으로 다가오는 것이 보였는데, 그들 마차에 실린 짐의 성격을 알아보고는 걸음을 재촉했다."]], [["“You be the woman they call Mrs Durbeyfield, I reckon?”", "\"댁이 더비필드 부인이라고 부르는 그 여자분이지요, 아마?\""], ["he said to Tess’s mother, who had descended to walk the remainder of the way.", "그가 테스의 어머니에게 말했다. 어머니는 남은 길을 걸으려고 마차에서 내려 있었다."]], [["She nodded.", "그녀는 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“Though widow of the late Sir John d’Urberville, poor nobleman, if I cared for my rights; and returning to the domain of his forefathers.”", "\"내 권리를 따진다면야 작고하신 존 더버빌 경의, 가엾은 귀족 어른의 미망인이지요. 그리고 그분 선조들의 영지로 돌아가는 길이고요.\""]], [["“Oh?", "\"오?"], ["Well, I know nothing about that; but if you be Mrs Durbeyfield, I am sent to tell ’ee that the rooms you wanted be let.", "글쎄, 그건 난 아무것도 모르겠고요. 다만 댁이 더비필드 부인이라면, 원하시던 방들이 다 나갔다고 전하라는 심부름을 왔습니다."], ["We didn’t know that you was coming till we got your letter this morning—when ’twas too late.", "오늘 아침 편지를 받기 전까진 오시는 줄도 몰랐어요—그땐 이미 너무 늦었고요."], ["But no doubt you can get other lodgings somewhere.”", "하지만 어디 다른 숙소야 분명 구하실 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["The man had noticed the face of Tess, which had become ash-pale at his intelligence. Her mother looked hopelessly at fault.", "남자는 그 소식에 잿빛으로 창백해진 테스의 얼굴을 알아챘다. 그녀의 어머니는 어쩔 줄 모르고 막막한 표정이었다."], ["“What shall we do now, Tess?”", "\"이제 어쩌면 좋니, 테스?\""], ["she said bitterly.", "그녀가 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["“Here’s a welcome to your ancestors’ lands!", "\"네 조상들의 땅에서 받는 환영이 이 꼴이라니!"], ["However, let’s try further.”", "아무튼 더 알아보자꾸나.\""]], [["They moved on into the town, and tried with all their might, Tess remaining with the waggon to take care of the children whilst her mother and ’Liza-Lu made inquiries.", "그들은 읍내로 들어가 온 힘을 다해 알아보았는데, 테스는 어머니와 '라이자루가 알아보러 다니는 동안 마차에 남아 아이들을 돌보았다."], ["At the last return of Joan to the vehicle, an hour later, when her search for accommodation had still been fruitless, the driver of the waggon said the goods must be unloaded, as the horses were half-dead, and he was bound to return part of the way at least that night.", "한 시간쯤 뒤 조앤이 마지막으로 마차에 돌아왔을 때, 숙소를 구하려던 그녀의 노력은 여전히 헛수고였고, 마차 마부는 말들이 반쯤 죽어 가니 짐을 내려야 한다고, 적어도 오늘 밤 안에 길 일부는 되돌아가야 한다고 말했다."]], [["“Very well—unload it here,”", "\"좋아요—여기에 내려요.\""], ["said Joan recklessly.", "조앤이 자포자기하듯 말했다."], ["“I’ll get shelter somewhere.”", "\"어디든 잘 데를 구하지요.\""]], [["The waggon had drawn up under the churchyard wall, in a spot screened from view, and the driver, nothing loth, soon hauled down the poor heap of household goods.", "마차는 묘지 담장 아래, 사람 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에 멈춰 섰고, 마부는 마다하지 않고 곧 그 초라한 살림 무더기를 끌어내렸다."], ["This done, she paid him, reducing herself to almost her last shilling thereby, and he moved off and left them, only too glad to get out of further dealings with such a family.", "그러고 나자 그녀는 삯을 치렀는데, 그 바람에 거의 마지막 한 실링까지 털어 버린 셈이었다. 그는 그런 가족과 더 엮이지 않게 되어 더없이 기뻐하며 마차를 몰아 그들을 두고 떠났다."], ["It was a dry night, and he guessed that they would come to no harm.", "마른 밤이었고, 그는 그들이 별 탈 없으리라 짐작했다."]], [["Tess gazed desperately at the pile of furniture.", "테스는 가구 더미를 절망스레 바라보았다."], ["The cold sunlight of this spring evening peered invidiously upon the crocks and kettles, upon the bunches of dried herbs shivering in the breeze, upon the brass handles of the dresser, upon the wicker-cradle they had all been rocked in, and upon the well-rubbed clock-case, all of which gave out the reproachful gleam of indoor articles abandoned to the vicissitudes of a roofless exposure for which they were never made.", "이 봄날 저녁의 차가운 햇빛이 항아리와 주전자 위로, 산들바람에 떨고 있는 마른 약초 다발 위로, 찬장의 놋쇠 손잡이 위로, 그들 모두가 흔들리며 잠들었던 버들가지 요람 위로, 잘 닦인 시계 상자 위로 심술궂게 들여다보았다. 그것들은 모두 지붕 없는 노출의 변덕에—본디 그러라고 만들어진 게 아닌 그 노출에—내버려진 실내 물건들이 내뿜는 원망스러운 빛을 발했다."], ["Round about were deparked hills and slopes—now cut up into little paddocks—and the green foundations that showed where the d’Urberville mansion once had stood; also an outlying stretch of Egdon Heath that had always belonged to the estate.", "둘레에는 울타리를 걷어 낸 언덕과 비탈이—이제는 작은 목초지로 잘게 나뉜—펼쳐져 있었고, 더버빌 저택이 한때 서 있던 자리를 보여 주는 푸른 주춧돌도 있었다. 또 늘 그 영지에 속해 있던 에그던 황야의 외진 한 자락도 보였다."], ["Hard by, the aisle of the church called the d’Urberville Aisle looked on imperturbably.", "바로 곁에는 더버빌 곁복도라 불리는 교회의 곁복도가 태연하게 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["“Isn’t your family vault your own freehold?”", "\"네 집안 납골당은 우리 소유 아니냐?\""], ["said Tess’s mother, as she returned from a reconnoitre of the church and graveyard. “Why, of course ’tis, and that’s where we will camp, girls, till the place of your ancestors finds us a roof!", "테스의 어머니가 교회와 묘지를 둘러보고 돌아오며 말했다. \"그럼, 당연히 그렇지, 그러니 거기다 야영을 하자꾸나, 얘들아, 네 조상들의 땅이 우리에게 지붕을 마련해 줄 때까지!"], ["Now, Tess and ’Liza and Abraham, you help me.", "자, 테스와 '라이자와 에이브러햄, 너희가 날 도와라."], ["We’ll make a nest for these children, and then we’ll have another look round.”", "이 아이들을 위해 둥지를 만들고, 그다음에 다시 한 바퀴 둘러보자.\""]], [["Tess listlessly lent a hand, and in a quarter of an hour the old four-post bedstead was dissociated from the heap of goods, and erected under the south wall of the church, the part of the building known as the d’Urberville Aisle, beneath which the huge vaults lay.", "테스는 맥없이 거들었고, 십오 분 만에 그 낡은 네 기둥 침대가 짐 무더기에서 떼어져, 교회 남쪽 벽 아래에, 곧 더버빌 곁복도라 불리는 건물 부분 아래에 세워졌다. 그 아래에는 거대한 납골당이 자리하고 있었다."], ["Over the tester of the bedstead was a beautiful traceried window, of many lights, its date being the fifteenth century.", "침대의 닫집 위로는 여러 칸으로 된 아름다운 격자무늬 창이 있었는데, 만들어진 연대는 십오 세기였다."], ["It was called the d’Urberville Window, and in the upper part could be discerned heraldic emblems like those on Durbeyfield’s old seal and spoon.", "그것은 더버빌 창이라 불렸고, 윗부분에는 더비필드네의 낡은 인장과 숟가락에 새겨진 것과 같은 문장(紋章)들을 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["Joan drew the curtains round the bed so as to make an excellent tent of it, and put the smaller children inside.", "조앤은 침대 둘레로 커튼을 쳐 그것을 훌륭한 천막으로 만들고, 더 어린아이들을 그 안에 넣었다."], ["“If it comes to the worst we can sleep there too, for one night,” she said.", "\"최악의 경우엔 우리도 거기서 자면 되지, 하룻밤쯤이야.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["“But let us try further on, and get something for the dears to eat!", "\"하지만 더 가서 알아보자꾸나, 그리고 얘들 먹을 거라도 좀 구하고!"], ["O, Tess, what’s the use of your playing at marrying gentlemen, if it leaves us like this!”", "오, 테스, 네가 신사와 결혼하는 시늉을 한 게 우릴 이 꼴로 만들어 놓는다면 무슨 소용이 있니!\""]], [["Accompanied by ’Liza-Lu and the boy, she again ascended the little lane which secluded the church from the townlet.", "'라이자루와 사내아이를 데리고 그녀는 교회를 작은 읍에서 갈라 놓는 그 작은 오솔길을 다시 올라갔다."], ["As soon as they got into the street they beheld a man on horseback gazing up and down. “Ah—I’m looking for you!”", "거리로 들어서자마자 그들은 말을 탄 채 위아래를 두리번거리는 한 남자를 보았다. \"아—내가 당신들을 찾고 있었소!\""], ["he said, riding up to them.", "그가 말을 몰고 다가오며 말했다."], ["“This is indeed a family gathering on the historic spot!”", "\"이거야말로 유서 깊은 곳에서의 가족 상봉이로군!\""]], [["It was Alec d’Urberville. “Where is Tess?”", "알렉 더버빌이었다. \"테스는 어디 있소?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["Personally Joan had no liking for Alec.", "개인적으로 조앤은 알렉을 좋아하지 않았다."], ["She cursorily signified the direction of the church, and went on, d’Urberville saying that he would see them again, in case they should be still unsuccessful in their search for shelter, of which he had just heard.", "그녀는 건성으로 교회 방향을 가리키고는 가던 길을 갔고, 더버빌은 그들이 잘 곳을 찾는 데 여전히 실패했을 경우—방금 그 소식을 들었다며—다시 보러 오겠다고 했다."], ["When they had gone, d’Urberville rode to the inn, and shortly after came out on foot.", "그들이 가 버리자 더버빌은 말을 타고 여관으로 갔고, 곧이어 걸어서 나왔다."]], [["In the interim Tess, left with the children inside the bedstead, remained talking with them awhile, till, seeing that no more could be done to make them comfortable just then, she walked about the churchyard, now beginning to be embrowned by the shades of nightfall.", "그동안 테스는 침대 안의 아이들과 함께 남아 잠시 그들과 이야기를 나누다가, 지금 당장은 아이들을 더 편하게 해 줄 수 없음을 알고는 이제 어스름의 그늘로 거뭇해지기 시작하는 묘지를 거닐었다."], ["The door of the church was unfastened, and she entered it for the first time in her life.", "교회 문은 잠겨 있지 않았고, 그녀는 난생처음 그 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["Within the window under which the bedstead stood were the tombs of the family, covering in their dates several centuries.", "침대가 서 있는 그 창 아래에는 가문의 무덤들이 있었는데, 그 연대가 여러 세기에 걸쳐 있었다."], ["They were canopied, altar-shaped, and plain; their carvings being defaced and broken; their brasses torn from the matrices, the rivet-holes remaining like martin-holes in a sandcliff.", "그것들은 닫집이 덮인, 제단 모양의 소박한 무덤들이었다. 조각은 훼손되고 부서졌으며, 놋쇠 장식은 틀에서 뜯겨 나가 리벳 구멍만 사질 절벽의 흰털발제비 둥지 구멍처럼 남아 있었다."], ["Of all the reminders that she had ever received that her people were socially extinct, there was none so forcible as this spoliation.", "자기 집안사람들이 사회적으로 멸문되었음을 일깨워 준 그 모든 표지 가운데, 이 약탈만큼 강렬한 것은 없었다."]], [["She drew near to a dark stone on which was inscribed:", "그녀는 이렇게 새겨진 어두운 돌에 다가갔다—"]], [["# OSTIUM SEPULCHRI ANTIQUAE FAMILIAE D’URBERVILLE", "# OSTIUM SEPULCHRI ANTIQUAE FAMILIAE D’URBERVILLE"]], [["Tess did not read Church-Latin like a Cardinal, but she knew that this was the door of her ancestral sepulchre, and that the tall knights of whom her father had chanted in his cups lay inside.", "더버빌 고가(古家)의 묘소 입구\n테스는 추기경처럼 교회 라틴어를 읽지는 못했지만, 이것이 자기 조상의 묘소 입구이며, 아버지가 술에 취해 읊조리던 그 키 큰 기사들이 그 안에 누워 있음은 알았다."]], [["She musingly turned to withdraw, passing near an altar-tomb, the oldest of them all, on which was a recumbent figure.", "그녀는 생각에 잠겨 물러나려 돌아서다가, 그 모든 것 중에서도 가장 오래된 제단 무덤 곁을 지나게 되었는데, 그 위에는 누운 형상이 하나 있었다."], ["In the dusk she had not noticed it before, and would hardly have noticed it now but for an odd fancy that the effigy moved.", "어스름 속에서 그녀는 그것을 미처 알아보지 못했었고, 그 조각상이 움직인다는 묘한 착각이 아니었다면 지금도 거의 알아보지 못했을 것이다."], ["As soon as she drew close to it she discovered all in a moment that the figure was a living person; and the shock to her sense of not having been alone was so violent that she was quite overcome, and sank down nigh to fainting, not, however, till she had recognized Alec d’Urberville in the form.", "가까이 다가가자마자 그녀는 순식간에 그 형상이 살아 있는 사람임을 알아챘다. 그리고 혼자가 아니었다는 충격이 어찌나 격렬했던지 그녀는 완전히 압도되어 거의 기절할 지경으로 주저앉았는데, 다만 그 형상에서 알렉 더버빌을 알아본 다음의 일이었다."]], [["He leapt off the slab and supported her.", "그는 석판에서 뛰어내려 그녀를 부축했다."]], [["“I saw you come in,”", "\"당신이 들어오는 걸 봤소.\""], ["he said smiling, “and got up there not to interrupt your meditations.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신의 명상을 방해하지 않으려고 저 위로 올라가 있었지."], ["A family gathering, is it not, with these old fellows under us here? Listen.”", "가족 상봉 아니오, 여기 우리 발밑에 이 양반들까지 있으니? 들어 보시오.\""]], [["He stamped with his heel heavily on the floor; whereupon there arose a hollow echo from below.", "그가 뒤꿈치로 바닥을 묵직하게 굴렀더니, 아래에서 텅 빈 메아리가 울려 나왔다."]], [["“That shook them a bit, I’ll warrant!”", "\"저 양반들 좀 흔들어 놓았겠지, 장담하건대!\""], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“And you thought I was the mere stone reproduction of one of them.", "\"당신은 내가 그저 저들 중 하나의 돌로 만든 모형인 줄 알았겠지."], ["But no. The old order changeth.", "하지만 아니오. 옛 질서는 변하는 법."], ["The little finger of the sham d’Urberville can do more for you than the whole dynasty of the real underneath....", "가짜 더버빌의 새끼손가락 하나가 당신을 위해, 저 밑에 있는 진짜의 왕조 전체보다 더 많은 걸 해 줄 수 있소……"], ["Now command me.", "자, 내게 명령하시오."], ["What shall I do?”", "내가 무얼 하면 되겠소?\""]], [["“Go away!”", "\"가 버려요!\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I will—I’ll look for your mother,”", "\"그러지—당신 어머니를 찾으러 가겠소.\""], ["said he blandly.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["But in passing her he whispered: “Mind this; you’ll be civil yet!”", "그러나 그녀 곁을 지나며 그가 속삭였다. \"이걸 명심해. 당신은 결국 고분고분해질 거요!\""]], [["When he was gone she bent down upon the entrance to the vaults, and said—", "그가 가 버리자 그녀는 납골당 입구 위로 몸을 숙이고 말했다—"]], [["“Why am I on the wrong side of this door!”", "\"왜 나는 이 문의 잘못된 쪽에 있는 걸까!\""]], [["In the meantime Marian and Izz Huett had journeyed onward with the chattels of the ploughman in the direction of their land of Canaan—the Egypt of some other family who had left it only that morning.", "그러는 동안 마리안과 이즈 휴엇은 농부의 세간을 싣고 자기들의 가나안 땅을 향해—바로 그날 아침에야 다른 어떤 가족이 떠나온 이집트를 향해—계속 나아가고 있었다."], ["But the girls did not for a long time think of where they were going.", "하지만 두 처녀는 한참 동안 자기들이 어디로 가는지 생각하지 않았다."], ["Their talk was of Angel Clare and Tess, and Tess’s persistent lover, whose connection with her previous history they had partly heard and partly guessed ere this.", "그들의 이야기는 에인절 클레어와 테스, 그리고 테스를 끈질기게 따라다니는 연인에 관한 것이었으니, 그자가 테스의 지난 내력과 맺은 관계는 그들이 그전에 일부는 듣고 일부는 짐작하고 있던 터였다."]], [["“’Tisn’t as though she had never known him afore,”", "\"그 여자가 그 사람을 전에 전혀 몰랐던 것도 아니잖아.\""], ["said Marian.", "마리안이 말했다."], ["“His having won her once makes all the difference in the world.", "\"한번 그 여자를 차지했던 적이 있다는 게 천지 차이를 만드는 거지."], ["’Twould be a thousand pities if he were to tole her away again.", "그자가 그 여자를 또 꾀어내 간다면 그건 천 번이라도 안타까운 일일 거야."], ["Mr Clare can never be anything to us, Izz; and why should we grudge him to her, and not try to mend this quarrel?", "클레어 씨는 우리에겐 절대 아무것도 될 수 없어, 이즈. 그런데 우리가 왜 그 사람을 그 여자한테 못 가게 시샘하고, 이 다툼을 고쳐 보려 하지 않는 거지?"], ["If he could on’y know what straits she’s put to, and what’s hovering round, he might come to take care of his own.”", "그 여자가 어떤 궁지에 몰려 있는지, 무엇이 주위를 맴돌고 있는지 그 사람이 알기만 한다면, 자기 사람을 챙기러 올지도 몰라.\""]], [["“Could we let him know?”", "\"우리가 그 사람한테 알려 줄 수 있을까?\""]], [["They thought of this all the way to their destination; but the bustle of re-establishment in their new place took up all their attention then.", "그들은 목적지에 닿을 때까지 줄곧 이것을 생각했다. 하지만 새 거처에 다시 자리 잡느라 분주한 통에 그때는 온통 거기에 정신이 팔렸다."], ["But when they were settled, a month later, they heard of Clare’s approaching return, though they had learnt nothing more of Tess.", "그러나 한 달 뒤 자리를 잡고 나서, 그들은 클레어가 곧 돌아온다는 소식을 들었다. 비록 테스에 관해서는 더 이상 아무것도 알아내지 못했지만 말이다."], ["Upon that, agitated anew by their attachment to him, yet honourably disposed to her, Marian uncorked the penny ink-bottle they shared, and a few lines were concocted between the two girls. Honour’d Sir,", "그러자 그에 대한 애정으로 다시금 마음이 일렁이면서도, 그녀에게는 의리를 지키려는 마음에서, 마리안이 둘이 함께 쓰는 일 페니짜리 잉크병 마개를 뽑았고, 두 처녀 사이에서 몇 줄이 지어졌다."]], [["Look to your Wife if you do love her as much as she do love you.", "존경하는 선생님, 당신의 부인이 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 당신도 그분을 사랑하신다면, 부인을 살피세요."], ["For she is sore put to by an Enemy in the shape of a Friend.", "그분은 친구의 탈을 쓴 한 원수에게 호되게 시달리고 있으니까요."], ["Sir, there is one near her who ought to be Away.", "선생님, 그분 가까이에 마땅히 멀리 있어야 할 자가 하나 있어요."], ["A woman should not be try’d beyond her Strength, and continual dropping will wear away a Stone—ay, more—a Diamond.", "여자는 제 힘에 부치도록 시험받아서는 안 되며, 끊임없이 떨어지는 물방울이 돌을 닳게 하지요—아니, 그 이상이지요—다이아몬드를요."], ["From Two Well-Wishers", "두 사람의 진심 어린 벗으로부터"]], [["This was addressed to Angel Clare at the only place they had ever heard him to be connected with, Emminster Vicarage; after which they continued in a mood of emotional exaltation at their own generosity, which made them sing in hysterical snatches and weep at the same time. End of Phase the Sixth", "이것은 그들이 클레어와 연관이 있다고 들어 본 단 한 곳, 에민스터 사제관으로 에인절 클레어 앞으로 부쳐졌다. 그러고 나서 그들은 자기들의 너그러움에 감정이 한껏 고조된 상태에 빠져, 발작하듯 띄엄띄엄 노래하면서 동시에 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["# LIII", "# LIII"]], [["Fulfilment\nIt was evening at Emminster Vicarage.", "에민스터 사제관의 저녁이었다."], ["The two customary candles were burning under their green shades in the Vicar’s study, but he had not been sitting there.", "늘 켜 두는 두 자루의 촛불이 사제의 서재에서 초록 갓 아래 타고 있었지만, 그는 거기 앉아 있지 않았다."], ["Occasionally he came in, stirred the small fire which sufficed for the increasing mildness of the spring, and went out again; sometimes pausing at the front door, going on to the drawing-room, then returning again to the front door.", "이따금 그는 들어와 점점 온화해지는 봄에 그만하면 족한 작은 불을 뒤적이고는 다시 나갔다. 때로는 현관문 앞에 멈춰 서고, 응접실로 갔다가, 다시 현관문으로 돌아오곤 했다."]], [["It faced westward, and though gloom prevailed inside, there was still light enough without to see with distinctness.", "그것은 서쪽을 향하고 있었고, 안에는 어둠이 깔렸어도 바깥은 아직 또렷이 볼 만큼 빛이 남아 있었다."], ["Mrs Clare, who had been sitting in the drawing-room, followed him hither.", "응접실에 앉아 있던 클레어 부인이 그를 따라 이리로 왔다."]], [["“Plenty of time yet,”", "\"아직 시간 많아요.\""], ["said the Vicar.", "사제가 말했다."], ["“He doesn’t reach Chalk-Newton till six, even if the train should be punctual, and ten miles of country-road, five of them in Crimmercrock Lane, are not jogged over in a hurry by our old horse.”", "\"기차가 제시간에 와도 그 애가 초크뉴턴에 닿는 건 여섯 시고, 시골길 십 마일을, 그중 오 마일은 크리머크록 길인데, 우리 늙은 말이 서둘러 덜컹덜컹 달릴 수 있는 거리가 아니잖소.\""]], [["“But he has done it in an hour with us, my dear.”", "\"하지만 그 애는 우리랑 한 시간 만에 온 적도 있어요, 여보.\""]], [["“Years ago.”", "\"몇 년 전이지.\""]], [["Thus they passed the minutes, each well knowing that this was only waste of breath, the one essential being simply to wait.", "이렇게 그들은 시간을 흘려보냈는데, 둘 다 이것이 그저 숨만 낭비하는 일이며 꼭 해야 할 단 하나는 그저 기다리는 것뿐임을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["At length there was a slight noise in the lane, and the old pony-chaise appeared indeed outside the railings.", "마침내 오솔길에서 가벼운 소리가 났고, 정말로 그 낡은 조랑말 마차가 난간 바깥에 나타났다."], ["They saw alight therefrom a form which they affected to recognize, but would actually have passed by in the street without identifying had he not got out of their carriage at the particular moment when a particular person was due.", "그들은 거기서 내리는 한 형상을 보았는데, 그를 알아보는 척했지만, 마침 어떤 특정한 사람이 올 시각에 그가 자기들 마차에서 내리지 않았더라면 거리에서 마주쳐도 알아보지 못한 채 지나쳤을 것이다."]], [["Mrs Clare rushed through the dark passage to the door, and her husband came more slowly after her.", "클레어 부인이 어두운 통로를 지나 문으로 달려갔고, 그녀의 남편이 더 천천히 뒤따라왔다."]], [["The new arrival, who was just about to enter, saw their anxious faces in the doorway and the gleam of the west in their spectacles because they confronted the last rays of day; but they could only see his shape against the light.", "막 들어서려던 그 새 도착자는 문간에 선 그들의 근심 어린 얼굴과, 그들이 저무는 햇살의 마지막 빛을 마주하고 있던 까닭에 그들의 안경에 어린 서쪽 하늘의 광채를 보았다. 하지만 그들은 빛을 등진 그의 형체만을 볼 수 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["“O, my boy, my boy—home again at last!”", "\"오, 내 아들, 내 아들아—드디어 다시 집에 왔구나!\""], ["cried Mrs Clare, who cared no more at that moment for the stains of heterodoxy which had caused all this separation than for the dust upon his clothes.", "클레어 부인이 외쳤다. 그 순간 그녀는 이 모든 이별의 원인이 된 이단의 오점 따위는, 그의 옷에 묻은 먼지만큼도 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["What woman, indeed, among the most faithful adherents of the truth, believes the promises and threats of the Word in the sense in which she believes in her own children, or would not throw her theology to the wind if weighed against their happiness?", "진리의 가장 충실한 신봉자라 한들, 어떤 여인이 말씀의 약속과 위협을 제 자식을 믿는 그 의미만큼 믿으며, 제 자식의 행복과 견주어 저울질할 때 제 신학을 바람에 내던지지 않을 것인가?"], ["As soon as they reached the room where the candles were lighted she looked at his face.", "촛불이 켜진 방에 들어서자마자 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“O, it is not Angel—not my son—the Angel who went away!” she cried in all the irony of sorrow, as she turned herself aside.", "\"오, 이건 에인절이 아니야—내 아들이 아니야—떠나갔던 그 에인절이 아니야!\" 그녀가 슬픔의 온갖 역설을 담아 외치며 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["His father, too, was shocked to see him, so reduced was that figure from its former contours by worry and the bad season that Clare had experienced, in the climate to which he had so rashly hurried in his first aversion to the mockery of events at home.", "그의 아버지 역시 그를 보고 충격을 받았으니, 클레어가 겪은 근심과 험한 계절로 인해 그 모습이 예전의 윤곽에서 그토록 깎여 나가 있었다. 그가 집안일의 비웃음에 처음 진저리치며 그토록 무모하게 서둘러 떠난 그 기후 속에서 말이다."], ["You could see the skeleton behind the man, and almost the ghost behind the skeleton. He matched Crivelli’s dead _Christus_.", "사람 뒤에 해골이, 그리고 거의 해골 뒤에 유령이 비치는 듯했다. 그는 크리벨리가 그린 죽은 그리스도와 닮아 있었다."], ["His sunken eye-pits were of morbid hue, and the light in his eyes had waned.", "움푹 꺼진 눈구멍은 병색을 띠었고, 눈 속의 빛은 사그라들어 있었다."], ["The angular hollows and lines of his aged ancestors had succeeded to their reign in his face twenty years before their time.", "늙은 조상들의 모난 움푹함과 주름이 제 차례보다 이십 년 일찍 그의 얼굴에 군림하기 시작했다."]], [["“I was ill over there, you know,”", "\"제가 거기서 앓았어요, 아시잖아요.\""], ["he said. “I am all right now.”", "그가 말했다. \"이젠 다 괜찮아요.\""]], [["As if, however, to falsify this assertion, his legs seemed to give way, and he suddenly sat down to save himself from falling.", "하지만 그 말이 거짓임을 증명하기라도 하듯, 그의 다리가 풀리는 듯하더니 그는 쓰러지지 않으려고 갑자기 자리에 앉았다."], ["It was only a slight attack of faintness, resulting from the tedious day’s journey, and the excitement of arrival.", "그저 가벼운 현기증 발작이었으니, 지루한 하루 여정과 도착의 흥분에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["“Has any letter come for me lately?”", "\"최근에 제게 온 편지 없었나요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I received the last you sent on by the merest chance, and after considerable delay through being inland; or I might have come sooner.”", "\"지난번에 보내 주신 건 순전히 우연으로, 내륙에 있던 탓에 한참 늦어서야 받았어요. 안 그랬으면 더 일찍 올 수도 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“It was from your wife, we supposed?”", "\"네 아내한테서 온 거였지, 아마?\""]], [["“It was.”", "\"맞아요.\""]], [["Only one other had recently come.", "최근에 단 한 통이 더 와 있었다."], ["They had not sent it on to him, knowing he would start for home so soon.", "그들은 그가 곧 집으로 출발할 줄 알고 그것을 그에게 부쳐 보내지 않은 터였다."]], [["He hastily opened the letter produced, and was much disturbed to read in Tess’s handwriting the sentiments expressed in her last hurried scrawl to him.", "그는 꺼내 준 편지를 황급히 뜯었고, 테스의 필체로 적힌, 그녀가 마지막으로 급히 휘갈겨 보낸 그 심정을 읽고는 몹시 동요했다."]], [["O why have you treated me so monstrously, Angel!", "오 왜 당신은 저를 그토록 끔찍하게 대하셨나요, 에인절!"], ["I do not deserve it. I have thought it all over carefully, and I can never, never forgive you!", "저는 그럴 만한 짓을 하지 않았어요. 저는 이 모든 걸 곰곰이 생각해 봤고, 당신을 결코, 결코 용서할 수 없어요!"], ["You know that I did not intend to wrong you—why have you so wronged me?", "제가 당신을 해칠 뜻이 없었다는 걸 아시잖아요—그런데 왜 저를 그토록 해치셨나요?"], ["You are cruel, cruel indeed!", "당신은 잔인해요, 정말 잔인해요!"], ["I will try to forget you.", "저는 당신을 잊으려 애쓰겠어요."], ["It is all injustice I have received at your hands!", "제가 당신 손에서 받은 건 온통 부당함뿐이에요! T."]], [["T. “It is quite true!”", "\"정말이지 맞는 말이야!\""], ["said Angel, throwing down the letter.", "에인절이 편지를 내던지며 말했다."], ["“Perhaps she will never be reconciled to me!”", "\"어쩌면 그 사람은 영영 나와 화해하지 않을지도 몰라!\""]], [["“Don’t, Angel, be so anxious about a mere child of the soil!”", "\"에인절, 한낱 흙의 자식 때문에 그렇게 애태우지 마라!\""], ["said his mother.", "그의 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“Child of the soil!", "\"흙의 자식이라뇨!"], ["Well, we all are children of the soil.", "글쎄요, 우리 모두가 흙의 자식이지요."], ["I wish she were so in the sense you mean; but let me now explain to you what I have never explained before, that her father is a descendant in the male line of one of the oldest Norman houses, like a good many others who lead obscure agricultural lives in our villages, and are dubbed ‘sons of the soil.’”", "어머니가 말씀하시는 그런 의미에서 그 사람이 흙의 자식이면 좋겠어요. 하지만 이제 제가 전에 한 번도 설명한 적 없는 걸 말씀드릴게요. 그 사람 아버지는 우리 마을에서 눈에 띄지 않는 농사꾼의 삶을 사는 적잖은 다른 이들처럼, 가장 오래된 노르만 가문 중 하나의 남계 후손이고, '흙의 자식'이라는 별명을 얻은 사람이라는 걸요.\""]], [["He soon retired to bed; and the next morning, feeling exceedingly unwell, he remained in his room pondering.", "그는 곧 잠자리에 들었고, 다음 날 아침 몹시 몸이 좋지 않아 방에 남아 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The circumstances amid which he had left Tess were such that though, while on the south of the Equator and just in receipt of her loving epistle, it had seemed the easiest thing in the world to rush back into her arms the moment he chose to forgive her, now that he had arrived it was not so easy as it had seemed.", "그가 테스를 두고 떠나온 정황은, 적도 남쪽에 있으면서 그녀의 다정한 편지를 막 받았을 때만 해도 마음만 먹으면 용서하는 그 순간 당장 그녀의 품으로 달려가는 것이 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일처럼 보였건만, 막상 도착하고 보니 보였던 만큼 그리 쉽지 않은 그런 것이었다."], ["She was passionate, and her present letter, showing that her estimate of him had changed under his delay—too justly changed, he sadly owned,—made him ask himself if it would be wise to confront her unannounced in the presence of her parents.", "그녀는 열정적인 사람이었고, 그의 지체로 인해 그에 대한 평가가 달라졌음을 보여 주는—너무도 마땅하게 달라졌다고 그는 슬프게 인정했다—그녀의 지금 편지는, 그녀의 부모가 보는 앞에서 그녀를 예고 없이 마주하는 것이 현명한 일일까 자문하게 만들었다."], ["Supposing that her love had indeed turned to dislike during the last weeks of separation, a sudden meeting might lead to bitter words.", "이별의 마지막 몇 주 동안 그녀의 사랑이 정말로 혐오로 변했다면, 갑작스러운 만남은 모진 말로 이어질지도 몰랐다."]], [["Clare therefore thought it would be best to prepare Tess and her family by sending a line to Marlott announcing his return, and his hope that she was still living with them there, as he had arranged for her to do when he left England.", "그래서 클레어는 자기가 돌아왔음을 알리고, 자기가 영국을 떠날 때 그녀에게 그러기로 정해 두었던 대로 그녀가 여전히 그곳에서 식구들과 살고 있기를 바란다는 한 줄을 말럿으로 보내, 테스와 그 식구들에게 마음의 준비를 시키는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각했다."], ["He despatched the inquiry that very day, and before the week was out there came a short reply from Mrs Durbeyfield which did not remove his embarrassment, for it bore no address, though to his surprise it was not written from Marlott. Sir,", "그는 바로 그날 그 문의를 부쳤고, 그 주가 다 가기 전에 더비필드 부인에게서 짤막한 답장이 왔는데, 그것은 그의 곤혹을 풀어 주지 못했다. 주소가 적혀 있지 않은 데다, 놀랍게도 말럿에서 쓴 것이 아니었기 때문이다."]], [["J write these few lines to say that my Daughter is away from me at present, and J am not sure when she will return, but J will let you know as Soon as she do.", "선생님, 이 몇 줄을 적어 알리자면 제 딸은 지금 저와 떨어져 있고, 언제 돌아올지도 확실치 않으나, 돌아오는 대로 알려 드리겠습니다."], ["J do not feel at liberty to tell you Where she is temperly biding.", "그 애가 지금 어디 임시로 머무는지는 제가 말씀드릴 처지가 못 됩니다."], ["J should say that me and my Family have left Marlott for some Time.—\nYours,", "저와 제 식구는 한동안 말럿을 떠나 있다는 것만 말씀드립니다.— 이만 줄입니다, J. 더비필드"]], [["J. Durbeyfield\nIt was such a relief to Clare to learn that Tess was at least apparently well that her mother’s stiff reticence as to her whereabouts did not long distress him.", "테스가 적어도 겉으로는 잘 지내고 있다는 걸 알게 된 것이 클레어에게는 어찌나 큰 안도였던지, 딸의 행방에 대한 어머니의 딱딱한 함구가 그를 오래 괴롭히지는 않았다. 그들 모두가 그에게 화가 나 있는 게 분명했다."], ["They were all angry with him, evidently. He would wait till Mrs Durbeyfield could inform him of Tess’s return, which her letter implied to be soon.", "더비필드 부인이 테스의 귀가를 알려 줄 수 있을 때까지 그는 기다리기로 했고, 그녀의 편지로 보아 그것은 곧일 듯했다. 그는 그 이상을 누릴 자격이 없었다."], ["He deserved no more.", "그의 사랑은 \"변화를 발견하면 변하는\""], ["His had been a love “which alters when it alteration finds”.", "사랑이었던 것이다. 그는 부재중에 몇 가지 기이한 경험을 했다."], ["He had undergone some strange experiences in his absence; he had seen the virtual Faustina in the literal Cornelia, a spiritual Lucretia in a corporeal Phryne; he had thought of the woman taken and set in the midst as one deserving to be stoned, and of the wife of Uriah being made a queen; and he had asked himself why he had not judged Tess constructively rather than biographically, by the will rather than by the deed?", "그는 문자 그대로의 코르넬리아 속에서 사실상의 파우스티나를, 육신의 프리네 속에서 영혼의 루크레티아를 보았다. 그는 사람들에게 붙들려 가운데 세워진, 돌로 쳐 죽임당해 마땅하다던 그 여인을 생각했고, 왕비가 된 우리아의 아내를 생각했다. 그리고 자신이 왜 테스를 행적에 따라가 아니라 의지에 따라, 행위에 따라가 아니라 그 뜻에 따라 헤아리지 못했는가를 자문했다."]], [["A day or two passed while he waited at his father’s house for the promised second note from Joan Durbeyfield, and indirectly to recover a little more strength.", "조앤 더비필드가 약속한 두 번째 쪽지를 기다리며, 또 간접적으로 기력을 조금 더 회복하며 아버지 집에서 지내는 동안 하루 이틀이 지났다."], ["The strength showed signs of coming back, but there was no sign of Joan’s letter.", "기력은 돌아올 기미를 보였지만, 조앤의 편지는 올 기미가 없었다."], ["Then he hunted up the old letter sent on to him in Brazil, which Tess had written from Flintcomb-Ash, and re-read it.", "그러자 그는 브라질로 부쳐졌던 그 옛 편지를, 곧 테스가 플린트콤애시에서 써 보낸 그 편지를 찾아내 다시 읽었다."], ["The sentences touched him now as much as when he had first perused them.", "그 문장들은 처음 정독했을 때만큼이나 지금도 그의 마음을 울렸다."]], [["...I must cry to you in my trouble—I have no one else!...", "……저는 제 괴로움 속에서 당신께 부르짖을 수밖에 없어요—제겐 달리 아무도 없으니까요!……"], ["I think I must die if you do not come soon, or tell me to come to you...", "당신이 곧 오시거나 제게 오라고 말씀해 주시지 않으면 저는 죽고 말 것 같아요……"], ["please, please, not to be just—only a little kind to me....", "제발, 제발, 옳고 그름을 따지지만 마시고—그저 제게 조금만 다정해 주세요……"], ["If you would come, I could die in your arms!", "당신이 와 주신다면, 저는 당신 품에서 죽을 수도 있어요!"], ["I would be well content to do that if so be you had forgiven me!...", "만약 당신이 저를 용서해 주신 거라면 저는 기꺼이 그렇게 하겠어요!……"], ["if you will send me one little line, and say, _I am coming soon_, I will bide on, Angel—O, so cheerfully!...", "당신이 제게 단 한 줄이라도 보내, 곧 가겠다, 라고 말씀해 주신다면, 저는 견디며 기다리겠어요, 에인절—오, 그토록 기쁘게요!……"], ["think how it do hurt my heart not to see you ever—ever!", "당신을 영영—영영 못 본다는 게 제 마음을 얼마나 아프게 하는지 생각해 주세요!"], ["Ah, if I could only make your dear heart ache one little minute of each day as mine does every day and all day long, it might lead you to show pity to your poor lonely one....", "아, 제가 매일같이, 온종일 내내 느끼는 그 아픔을 당신의 소중한 가슴도 하루에 단 일 분만이라도 느끼게 할 수 있다면, 그게 당신으로 하여금 가엾고 외로운 당신의 사람에게 연민을 보이게 할지도 모르는데……"], ["I would be content, ay, glad, to live with you as your servant, if I may not as your wife; so that I could only be near you, and get glimpses of you, and think of you as mine....", "저는 당신의 아내로는 못 되더라도 당신의 하인으로 함께 사는 거라면 만족하겠어요, 아니, 기쁘겠어요. 그저 당신 곁에 있을 수만 있다면, 당신을 언뜻언뜻 볼 수만 있다면, 당신을 제 사람이라 여길 수만 있다면요……"], ["I long for only one thing in heaven or earth or under the earth, to meet you, my own dear!", "저는 하늘에서도 땅에서도 땅 밑에서도 오직 한 가지만을 바라요, 당신을 만나는 것, 나의 소중한 사람이여!"], ["Come to me—come to me, and save me from what threatens me!", "제게 와 주세요—제게 와 주세요, 그리고 저를 위협하는 것에서 구해 주세요!"]], [["Clare determined that he would no longer believe in her more recent and severer regard of him, but would go and find her immediately.", "클레어는 그를 향한 그녀의 더 최근의, 더 모진 마음을 더 이상 믿지 않고, 당장 가서 그녀를 찾기로 결심했다."], ["He asked his father if she had applied for any money during his absence.", "그는 아버지에게 자기가 없는 동안 그녀가 돈을 청한 적이 있는지 물었다."], ["His father returned a negative, and then for the first time it occurred to Angel that her pride had stood in her way, and that she had suffered privation.", "아버지는 없다고 답했고, 그제야 에인절은 처음으로, 그녀의 자존심이 길을 가로막아 그녀가 궁핍을 겪었음을 깨달았다."], ["From his remarks his parents now gathered the real reason of the separation; and their Christianity was such that, reprobates being their especial care, the tenderness towards Tess which her blood, her simplicity, even her poverty, had not engendered, was instantly excited by her sin.", "그의 말에서 그의 부모는 이제 이별의 진짜 까닭을 알아챘다. 그리고 그들의 기독교 신앙은 타락한 자들을 각별히 보살피는 것이었기에, 그녀의 혈통도, 그녀의 순박함도, 심지어 그녀의 가난도 불러일으키지 못했던 테스에 대한 따뜻함이 그녀의 죄로 인해 곧장 일깨워졌다."]], [["Whilst he was hastily packing together a few articles for his journey he glanced over a poor plain missive also lately come to hand—the one from Marian and Izz Huett, beginning—", "여정에 쓸 몇 가지를 황급히 챙기던 그는, 역시 최근에 손에 들어온 한 통의 초라하고 수수한 편지를—마리안과 이즈 휴엇에게서 온, 이렇게 시작하는 그 편지를 훑어보았다—"]], [["“Honour’d Sir, Look to your Wife if you do love her as much as she do love you,”", "\"존경하는 선생님, 당신의 부인이 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 당신도 그분을 사랑하신다면, 부인을 살피세요.\""], ["and signed, “From Two Well-Wishers.”", "그리고 \"두 사람의 진심 어린 벗으로부터\"라고 서명된 편지였다."]], [["# LIV", "# LIV"]], [["In a quarter of an hour Clare was leaving the house, whence his mother watched his thin figure as it disappeared into the street.", "십오 분 만에 클레어는 집을 나섰고, 그의 어머니는 거리로 사라지는 그의 야윈 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["He had declined to borrow his father’s old mare, well knowing of its necessity to the household.", "그는 아버지의 늙은 암말을 빌리기를 마다했으니, 그것이 집안에 꼭 필요함을 잘 알았기 때문이다."], ["He went to the inn, where he hired a trap, and could hardly wait during the harnessing.", "그는 여관으로 가서 이륜마차를 빌렸는데, 마구를 채우는 동안 도무지 기다릴 수가 없을 지경이었다."], ["In a very few minutes after, he was driving up the hill out of the town which, three or four months earlier in the year, Tess had descended with such hopes and ascended with such shattered purposes.", "그러고 나서 몇 분 지나지 않아, 그는 그해 서너 달 전에 테스가 그토록 큰 희망을 안고 내려왔다가 그토록 산산조각 난 뜻을 품고 올라갔던 그 읍에서 언덕을 올라 마차를 몰고 있었다."]], [["Benvill Lane soon stretched before him, its hedges and trees purple with buds; but he was looking at other things, and only recalled himself to the scene sufficiently to enable him to keep the way.", "곧 벤빌 길이 그 앞에 펼쳐졌는데, 그 산울타리와 나무들이 움튼 싹으로 자줏빛이었다. 하지만 그는 다른 것들을 바라보고 있었고, 그저 길을 잃지 않을 만큼만 정신을 그 풍경으로 되돌릴 뿐이었다."], ["In something less than an hour-and-a-half he had skirted the south of the King’s Hintock estates and ascended to the untoward solitude of Cross-in-Hand, the unholy stone whereon Tess had been compelled by Alec d’Urberville, in his whim of reformation, to swear the strange oath that she would never wilfully tempt him again.", "한 시간 반이 채 안 되어 그는 킹스힌톡 영지의 남쪽을 끼고 돌아, 십자가 손 고개의 불길한 고독 속으로 올라섰다. 그것은 알렉 더버빌이 개심하던 변덕 속에서 테스에게, 다시는 일부러 자기를 유혹하지 않겠노라는 그 기묘한 맹세를 시켰던 그 부정한 돌이었다."], ["The pale and blasted nettle-stems of the preceding year even now lingered nakedly in the banks, young green nettles of the present spring growing from their roots.", "작년의 희끄무레하게 시든 쐐기풀 줄기들이 지금도 둑에 헐벗은 채 남아 있었고, 올봄의 어린 초록 쐐기풀이 그 뿌리에서 돋아나고 있었다."]], [["Thence he went along the verge of the upland overhanging the other Hintocks, and, turning to the right, plunged into the bracing calcareous region of Flintcomb-Ash, the address from which she had written to him in one of the letters, and which he supposed to be the place of sojourn referred to by her mother.", "거기서 그는 다른 힌톡들을 굽어보는 고지의 가장자리를 따라가다가, 오른쪽으로 돌아, 플린트콤애시의 상쾌한 석회질 지대로 뛰어들었다. 그녀가 그에게 보낸 편지 중 하나에 적었던 그 주소요, 그가 그녀의 어머니가 말한 임시 거처라고 짐작했던 곳이었다. 물론 그는 여기서 그녀를 찾지 못했다."], ["Here, of course, he did not find her; and what added to his depression was the discovery that no “Mrs Clare” had ever been heard of by the cottagers or by the farmer himself, though Tess was remembered well enough by her Christian name.", "그리고 그의 우울을 더한 것은, 마을 사람들도 농장주 자신도 \"클레어 부인\"이라는 이름은 들어 본 적이 없다는 사실을 알게 된 일이었다. 테스는 세례명으로는 충분히 잘 기억되고 있었는데도 말이다."], ["His name she had obviously never used during their separation, and her dignified sense of their total severance was shown not much less by this abstention than by the hardships she had chosen to undergo (of which he now learnt for the first time) rather than apply to his father for more funds.", "이별하는 동안 그녀가 그의 성을 분명 한 번도 쓰지 않은 것이었고, 둘이 완전히 갈라섰다는 그녀의 의연한 의식은, 이 절제로써도 못지않게 드러났지만, 그녀가 아버지에게 더 많은 돈을 청하느니 차라리 택한 그 고생으로써도(그것을 그는 이제야 처음 알게 되었다) 드러났다."]], [["From this place they told him Tess Durbeyfield had gone, without due notice, to the home of her parents on the other side of Blackmoor, and it therefore became necessary to find Mrs Durbeyfield.", "그들은 그에게, 테스 더비필드가 제대로 알리지도 않고 블랙무어 건너편 부모의 집으로 가 버렸다고 일러 주었고, 그래서 더비필드 부인을 찾는 것이 필요해졌다."], ["She had told him she was not now at Marlott, but had been curiously reticent as to her actual address, and the only course was to go to Marlott and inquire for it.", "그녀는 그에게 자기가 이제 말럿에 없다고 말했지만, 실제 주소에 대해서는 묘하게 함구했으니, 유일한 방도는 말럿으로 가서 그것을 물어보는 것이었다."], ["The farmer who had been so churlish with Tess was quite smooth-tongued to Clare, and lent him a horse and man to drive him towards Marlott, the gig he had arrived in being sent back to Emminster; for the limit of a day’s journey with that horse was reached.", "테스에게 그토록 무뚝뚝했던 그 농장주는 클레어에게는 아주 매끄럽게 굴며, 그를 말럿 쪽으로 태워다 줄 말과 일꾼을 빌려주었다. 그가 타고 온 이륜마차는 에민스터로 돌려보냈으니, 그 말로는 하루 여정의 한계에 다다른 것이었다."]], [["Clare would not accept the loan of the farmer’s vehicle for a further distance than to the outskirts of the Vale, and, sending it back with the man who had driven him, he put up at an inn, and next day entered on foot the region wherein was the spot of his dear Tess’s birth.", "클레어는 골짜기 변두리까지 가는 것 이상으로는 그 농장주의 마차를 빌리려 하지 않았고, 그것을 자기를 태워다 준 일꾼과 함께 돌려보낸 뒤, 한 여관에 묵었다가, 이튿날 자기 사랑하는 테스가 태어난 곳이 있는 그 지방으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["It was as yet too early in the year for much colour to appear in the gardens and foliage; the so-called spring was but winter overlaid with a thin coat of greenness, and it was of a parcel with his expectations.", "정원과 나뭇잎에 빛깔이 많이 돌기에는 아직 너무 이른 철이었다. 이른바 봄이라는 것은 얇은 초록빛 한 겹을 덧입은 겨울에 지나지 않았고, 그것은 그의 기대와 한통속이었다."]], [["The house in which Tess had passed the years of her childhood was now inhabited by another family who had never known her.", "테스가 어린 시절을 보낸 집에는 이제 그녀를 전혀 모르는 다른 가족이 살고 있었다."], ["The new residents were in the garden, taking as much interest in their own doings as if the homestead had never passed its primal time in conjunction with the histories of others, beside which the histories of these were but as a tale told by an idiot.", "새 주민들은 정원에 나와 있었는데, 마치 이 집터가 다른 이들의 내력과 얽혀 본 적이 결코 없다는 듯—그들의 내력에 견주면 이들의 내력이란 백치가 들려주는 이야기에 불과하건만—오로지 제 일에만 관심을 쏟고 있었다."], ["They walked about the garden paths with thoughts of their own concerns entirely uppermost, bringing their actions at every moment in jarring collision with the dim ghosts behind them, talking as though the time when Tess lived there were not one whit intenser in story than now.", "그들은 자기네 관심사 생각만을 온통 머릿속에 둔 채 정원 길을 거닐었으니, 순간순간 자기들의 행동을 등 뒤의 희미한 유령들과 거슬리게 부딪치게 하면서, 테스가 거기 살던 시절이 이야기로 따지면 지금보다 조금도 더 강렬하지 않다는 듯 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Even the spring birds sang over their heads as if they thought there was nobody missing in particular.", "봄새들조차 머리 위에서 노래했으니, 마치 딱히 빠진 사람은 아무도 없다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["On inquiry of these precious innocents, to whom even the name of their predecessors was a failing memory, Clare learned that John Durbeyfield was dead; that his widow and children had left Marlott, declaring that they were going to live at Kingsbere, but instead of doing so had gone on to another place they mentioned.", "이 더없이 순진한 이들에게—그들에게는 선임자들의 이름조차 흐릿해져 가는 기억에 불과했는데—물어보고서 클레어는, 존 더비필드가 죽었으며, 그의 미망인과 아이들이 킹스비어에 가서 살겠노라 말하며 말럿을 떠났으나, 정작 그러지 않고 그들이 언급한 다른 곳으로 가 버렸음을 알게 되었다."], ["By this time Clare abhorred the house for ceasing to contain Tess, and hastened away from its hated presence without once looking back.", "이쯤 되자 클레어는 테스를 더 이상 품지 않는 그 집이 끔찍해져, 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않고 그 미운 존재로부터 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["His way was by the field in which he had first beheld her at the dance.", "그의 길은 그가 무도회에서 그녀를 처음 보았던 그 들판 옆을 지났다."], ["It was as bad as the house—even worse. He passed on through the churchyard, where, amongst the new headstones, he saw one of a somewhat superior design to the rest.", "그곳은 그 집만큼이나 괴로웠으니—아니, 더 심했다. 그는 묘지를 가로질러 갔는데, 거기 새 묘비들 사이에서 다른 것들보다 다소 우월한 디자인의 묘비 하나를 보았다."], ["The inscription ran thus:", "그 비문은 이러했다—"]], [["In memory of John Durbeyfield, rightly d’Urberville, of the once powerful family of that Name, and Direct Descendant through an illustrious Line from Sir Pagan d’Urberville, one of the Knights of the Conqueror.", "한때 그 이름으로 막강하던 가문의, 정복왕의 기사들 중 한 사람인 페이건 더버빌 경으로부터 빛나는 혈통을 통해 직계로 내려온 후손이자, 올바로 부르자면 더버빌인 존 더비필드를 기리며. 18—년 3월 10일 사망."], ["Died March 10th, 18—\nHow are the Mighty Fallen.", "강하던 자들이 어찌 그리 쓰러졌는가."]], [["Some man, apparently the sexton, had observed Clare standing there, and drew nigh.", "어떤 남자가, 보아하니 묘지기인 듯한 자가 거기 서 있는 클레어를 보고는 다가왔다."], ["“Ah, sir, now that’s a man who didn’t want to lie here, but wished to be carried to Kingsbere, where his ancestors be.”", "\"아, 선생님, 저 양반은 여기 묻히고 싶어 하지 않고, 자기 조상들이 있는 킹스비어로 옮겨지길 바랐답니다.\""]], [["“And why didn’t they respect his wish?”", "\"그런데 왜 그 양반 소원을 들어주지 않았소?\""]], [["“Oh—no money. Bless your soul, sir, why—there, I wouldn’t wish to say it everywhere, but—even this headstone, for all the flourish wrote upon en, is not paid for.”", "\"오—돈이 없어서요. 정말이지 선생님, 글쎄—저, 아무 데서나 떠벌리고 싶진 않습니다만—이 묘비조차, 위에 그렇게 거창하게 새겨 놓았어도, 값을 치르지 못했답니다.\""]], [["“Ah, who put it up?”", "\"아, 누가 세웠소?\""]], [["The man told the name of a mason in the village, and, on leaving the churchyard, Clare called at the mason’s house.", "남자가 마을 석공의 이름을 일러 주었고, 묘지를 떠나면서 클레어는 그 석공의 집에 들렀다."], ["He found that the statement was true, and paid the bill.", "그는 그 말이 사실임을 알고 청구서 값을 치렀다."], ["This done, he turned in the direction of the migrants.", "그러고 나서 그는 이주한 이들 쪽으로 방향을 잡았다."]], [["The distance was too long for a walk, but Clare felt such a strong desire for isolation that at first he would neither hire a conveyance nor go to a circuitous line of railway by which he might eventually reach the place.", "걷기에는 거리가 너무 멀었지만, 클레어는 혼자 있고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 강했던지 처음에는 탈것을 빌리려고도, 결국 그곳에 닿을 수 있을 우회 철로를 타려고도 하지 않았다."], ["At Shaston, however, he found he must hire; but the way was such that he did not enter Joan’s place till about seven o’clock in the evening, having traversed a distance of over twenty miles since leaving Marlott.", "그러나 섀스턴에서는 빌려야 한다는 걸 알았다. 하지만 길이 어찌나 멀었던지, 말럿을 떠난 뒤로 이십 마일이 넘는 거리를 지나, 저녁 일곱 시쯤이 되어서야 조앤이 사는 곳에 들어섰다."]], [["The village being small he had little difficulty in finding Mrs Durbeyfield’s tenement, which was a house in a walled garden, remote from the main road, where she had stowed away her clumsy old furniture as best she could.", "마을이 작았으므로 그는 더비필드 부인의 셋집을 찾는 데 별 어려움이 없었다. 그것은 큰길에서 멀리 떨어진, 담장 두른 정원이 딸린 집이었는데, 그녀가 거기에 자기의 볼품없는 낡은 가구를 되도록 잘 욱여넣어 둔 것이었다."], ["It was plain that for some reason or other she had not wished him to visit her, and he felt his call to be somewhat of an intrusion.", "무슨 까닭에선지 그녀가 그가 찾아오기를 바라지 않았던 것이 분명했고, 그는 자신의 방문이 다소 침입처럼 느껴졌다."], ["She came to the door herself, and the light from the evening sky fell upon her face.", "그녀가 직접 문으로 나왔고, 저녁 하늘의 빛이 그녀의 얼굴 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["This was the first time that Clare had ever met her, but he was too preoccupied to observe more than that she was still a handsome woman, in the garb of a respectable widow.", "클레어가 그녀를 만난 것은 이번이 처음이었지만, 그는 너무 골몰해 있던 터라, 그녀가 점잖은 미망인의 옷차림을 한 여전히 잘생긴 여인이라는 것 말고는 더 관찰하지 못했다."], ["He was obliged to explain that he was Tess’s husband, and his object in coming there, and he did it awkwardly enough.", "그는 자기가 테스의 남편임을, 그리고 자기가 거기 온 목적을 설명해야 했고, 그 일을 꽤나 어색하게 해냈다."], ["“I want to see her at once,” he added.", "\"당장 그 사람을 보고 싶습니다.\""], ["“You said you would write to me again, but you have not done so.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"다시 편지하시겠다고 하셨는데, 안 하셨더군요.\""]], [["“Because she’ve not come home,”", "\"그 애가 집에 안 왔으니까요.\""], ["said Joan.", "조앤이 말했다."]], [["“Do you know if she is well?”", "\"그 사람이 잘 지내는지 아십니까?\""]], [["“I don’t.", "\"몰라요."], ["But you ought to, sir,”", "하지만 선생이 아셔야지요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I admit it.", "\"인정합니다."], ["Where is she staying?”", "그 사람은 어디 머물고 있습니까?\""]], [["From the beginning of the interview Joan had disclosed her embarrassment by keeping her hand to the side of her cheek.", "면담이 시작될 때부터 조앤은 한 손을 뺨 옆에 댄 채 자신의 곤혹스러움을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“I—don’t know exactly where she is staying,”", "\"그 애가—정확히 어디 머무는지는 모르겠어요.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“She was—but—”", "\"그 애는—하지만—\""], ["“Where was she?”", "\"그 애는 어디 있었습니까?\""]], [["“Well, she is not there now.”", "\"그런데 그 애는 지금 거기 없어요.\""]], [["In her evasiveness she paused again, and the younger children had by this time crept to the door, where, pulling at his mother’s skirts, the youngest murmured—", "\"글쎄, 지금은 거기 없어요.\" 발뺌하느라 그녀는 다시 말을 멈추었고, 그사이 어린아이들이 문가로 기어 나와 있었는데, 어머니의 치맛자락을 잡아당기며 가장 어린아이가 중얼거렸다—"]], [["“Is this the gentleman who is going to marry Tess?”", "\"이 사람이 테스 언니랑 결혼할 신사예요?\""]], [["“He has married her,”", "\"이미 결혼했단다.\""], ["Joan whispered.", "조앤이 속삭였다."], ["“Go inside.”", "\"안으로 들어가.\""]], [["Clare saw her efforts for reticence, and asked—", "클레어는 그녀가 함구하려 애쓰는 것을 보고는 물었다—"]], [["“Do you think Tess would wish me to try and find her?", "\"테스가 제가 자기를 찾아 나서기를 바랄 거라고 생각하십니까?"], ["If not, of course—”", "만약 아니라면, 물론—\""]], [["“I don’t think she would.”", "\"바라지 않을 것 같네요.\""]], [["“Are you sure?”", "\"확실합니까?\""]], [["“I am sure she wouldn’t.”", "\"바라지 않을 게 확실해요.\""]], [["He was turning away; and then he thought of Tess’s tender letter.", "그는 돌아서려다가, 테스의 그 다정한 편지를 떠올렸다."]], [["“I am sure she would!”", "\"분명 바랄 겁니다!\""], ["he retorted passionately.", "그가 격정적으로 받아쳤다."], ["“I know her better than you do.”", "\"제가 어머니보다 그 사람을 더 잘 압니다.\""]], [["“That’s very likely, sir; for I have never really known her.”", "\"그건 그럴 법도 하지요, 선생. 난 그 애를 정말로 알아본 적이 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Please tell me her address, Mrs Durbeyfield, in kindness to a lonely wretched man!”", "\"제발 그 사람 주소를 알려 주십시오, 더비필드 부인, 외롭고 비참한 한 사내를 가엾이 여기셔서!\""], ["Tess’s mother again restlessly swept her cheek with her vertical hand, and seeing that he suffered, she at last said, is a low voice—", "테스의 어머니는 다시 수직으로 세운 손으로 안절부절못하며 뺨을 쓸었고, 그가 괴로워하는 것을 보고는 마침내 낮은 목소리로 말했다—"]], [["“She is at Sandbourne.”", "\"그 애는 샌드본에 있어요.\""]], [["“Ah—where there? Sandbourne has become a large place, they say.”", "\"아—거기 어디요? 샌드본은 큰 곳이 되었다던데요.\""]], [["“I don’t know more particularly than I have said—Sandbourne. For myself, I was never there.”", "\"내가 말한 것보다 더 자세히는 몰라요—샌드본. 나는 거기 가 본 적도 없으니까요.\""]], [["It was apparent that Joan spoke the truth in this, and he pressed her no further.", "이 점에서는 조앤이 진실을 말하고 있음이 분명했고, 그는 더 캐묻지 않았다."]], [["“Are you in want of anything?” he said gently.", "\"필요한 게 있으십니까?\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"없어요, 선생.\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“We are fairly well provided for.”", "\"우린 그럭저럭 살 만하게 갖춰져 있어요.\""]], [["Without entering the house Clare turned away.", "집 안에 들어가지 않은 채 클레어는 돌아섰다."], ["There was a station three miles ahead, and paying off his coachman, he walked thither.", "삼 마일 앞에 역이 있었고, 마부의 삯을 치른 뒤 그는 그리로 걸어갔다."], ["The last train to Sandbourne left shortly after, and it bore Clare on its wheels.", "샌드본으로 가는 마지막 기차가 얼마 뒤에 떠났고, 그 기차가 클레어를 바퀴에 싣고 갔다."]], [["# LV", "# LV"]], [["At eleven o’clock that night, having secured a bed at one of the hotels and telegraphed his address to his father immediately on his arrival, he walked out into the streets of Sandbourne.", "그날 밤 열한 시, 한 호텔에 묵을 자리를 잡고 도착하자마자 아버지에게 자기 주소를 전보로 친 뒤, 그는 샌드본의 거리로 걸어 나갔다."], ["It was too late to call on or inquire for any one, and he reluctantly postponed his purpose till the morning.", "누구를 찾아가거나 묻기에는 너무 늦은 시각이었고, 그는 마지못해 그 목적을 아침으로 미루었다."], ["But he could not retire to rest just yet.", "하지만 아직 잠자리에 들 수는 없었다."]], [["This fashionable watering-place, with its eastern and its western stations, its piers, its groves of pines, its promenades, and its covered gardens, was, to Angel Clare, like a fairy place suddenly created by the stroke of a wand, and allowed to get a little dusty.", "이 멋들어진 휴양지는, 동역과 서역, 잔교들, 소나무 숲, 산책로, 지붕 덮인 정원을 갖춘 이곳은, 에인절 클레어에게는 마치 요술 지팡이 한 번 휘둘러 갑자기 만들어진 뒤 먼지가 약간 앉도록 내버려 둔 요정의 나라 같았다."], ["An outlying eastern tract of the enormous Egdon Waste was close at hand, yet on the very verge of that tawny piece of antiquity such a glittering novelty as this pleasure city had chosen to spring up.", "거대한 에그던 황무지의 외진 동쪽 한 자락이 바로 가까이에 있는데도, 그 황갈빛 태고의 땅 바로 가장자리에 이런 번쩍이는 새것이, 이 환락의 도시가 솟아나기를 택한 것이었다."], ["Within the space of a mile from its outskirts every irregularity of the soil was prehistoric, every channel an undisturbed British trackway; not a sod having been turned there since the days of the Cæsars.", "그 변두리에서 일 마일 안쪽으로는 토양의 온갖 굴곡이 선사 시대의 것이었고, 모든 골은 손대지 않은 고대 브리튼의 옛길이었으니, 카이사르들의 시절 이후로 거기서는 단 한 뙈기의 흙도 갈아엎어진 적이 없었다."], ["Yet the exotic had grown here, suddenly as the prophet’s gourd; and had drawn hither Tess.", "그런데도 이 이국적인 것이 여기서 자라났으니, 예언자의 박넝쿨처럼 갑작스레 자라나 테스를 이리로 끌어들인 것이었다."]], [["By the midnight lamps he went up and down the winding way of this new world in an old one, and could discern between the trees and against the stars the lofty roofs, chimneys, gazebos, and towers of the numerous fanciful residences of which the place was composed.", "자정의 등불 아래 그는 옛것 속의 이 새 세계의 구불구불한 길을 오르내렸고, 나무들 사이로, 별들을 등지고서, 이곳을 이룬 수많은 기발한 저택들의 우뚝한 지붕과 굴뚝과 정자와 탑 들을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["It was a city of detached mansions; a Mediterranean lounging-place on the English Channel; and as seen now by night it seemed even more imposing than it was.", "그것은 따로 떨어진 저택들의 도시였으니, 영국 해협 위의 지중해풍 휴식처였다. 그리고 지금 밤에 보니 실제보다도 한층 더 위풍당당해 보였다."]], [["The sea was near at hand, but not intrusive; it murmured, and he thought it was the pines; the pines murmured in precisely the same tones, and he thought they were the sea.", "바다가 가까이에 있었으나 거슬리지는 않았다. 바다가 속삭였는데, 그는 그것이 소나무인 줄 알았다. 소나무가 꼭 같은 가락으로 속삭였는데, 그는 그것이 바다인 줄 알았다."]], [["Where could Tess possibly be, a cottage-girl, his young wife, amidst all this wealth and fashion?", "테스가 대체 어디에 있을 수 있을까, 시골 처녀인 그의 젊은 아내가, 이 온갖 부유함과 멋스러움 한가운데에?"], ["The more he pondered, the more was he puzzled.", "곰곰이 생각할수록 그는 더 어리둥절해졌다."], ["Were there any cows to milk here?", "여기에 젖 짤 소가 있기나 할까?"], ["There certainly were no fields to till.", "갈 밭이라곤 분명 없었다."], ["She was most probably engaged to do something in one of these large houses; and he sauntered along, looking at the chamber-windows and their lights going out one by one, and wondered which of them might be hers.", "그녀는 십중팔구 이 큰 집들 중 하나에서 무슨 일을 하기로 되어 있을 터였다. 그는 어슬렁거리며 침실 창문들과 하나씩 꺼져 가는 그 불빛들을 바라보았고, 그중 어느 것이 그녀의 것일까 궁금해했다."]], [["Conjecture was useless, and just after twelve o’clock he entered and went to bed.", "추측은 부질없었고, 열두 시가 막 지난 뒤 그는 안으로 들어가 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Before putting out his light he re-read Tess’s impassioned letter.", "불을 끄기 전에 그는 테스의 그 격정적인 편지를 다시 읽었다."], ["Sleep, however, he could not—so near her, yet so far from her—and he continually lifted the window-blind and regarded the backs of the opposite houses, and wondered behind which of the sashes she reposed at that moment.", "그러나 잠은—그녀에게 그토록 가까이 있으면서도, 그토록 멀리 있으니—이룰 수 없었고, 그는 자꾸만 창의 차양을 들어 올려 맞은편 집들의 뒤편을 바라보며, 그 순간 어느 창틀 뒤에서 그녀가 쉬고 있을까 궁금해했다."]], [["He might almost as well have sat up all night.", "차라리 밤을 꼬박 새우는 편이 나을 뻔했다."], ["In the morning he arose at seven, and shortly after went out, taking the direction of the chief post-office.", "아침에 그는 일곱 시에 일어나, 곧이어 중앙 우체국 방향으로 길을 잡아 나섰다."], ["At the door he met an intelligent postman coming out with letters for the morning delivery.", "문 앞에서 그는 아침 배달용 편지들을 들고 나오는 영리해 보이는 우체부 한 명과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Do you know the address of a Mrs Clare?”", "\"클레어 부인이라는 분의 주소를 아십니까?\""], ["asked Angel. The postman shook his head.", "에인절이 물었다. 우체부는 고개를 저었다."]], [["Then, remembering that she would have been likely to continue the use of her maiden name, Clare said—", "그러다 그녀가 처녀 시절 이름을 계속 썼을 법함을 떠올리고, 클레어가 말했다—"]], [["“Of a Miss Durbeyfield?”", "\"더비필드 양이라는 분은요?\""]], [["“Durbeyfield?”", "\"더비필드요?\""]], [["This also was strange to the postman addressed.", "이것 역시 그 우체부에게는 생소했다."]], [["“There’s visitors coming and going every day, as you know, sir,”", "\"아시다시피 손님들이야 날마다 오가니까요, 선생님.\""], ["he said; “and without the name of the house ’tis impossible to find ’em.”", "그가 말했다. \"그러니 집 이름을 모르고서야 찾기란 불가능합니다.\""]], [["One of his comrades hastening out at that moment, the name was repeated to him.", "마침 그때 그의 동료 하나가 서둘러 나오기에, 그에게 그 이름을 다시 일러 주었다."]], [["“I know no name of Durbeyfield; but there is the name of d’Urberville at The Herons,” said the second.", "\"더비필드라는 이름은 모르겠는데, 헤런즈에 더버빌이라는 이름은 있어요.\" 두 번째 사람이 말했다."]], [["“That’s it!”", "\"바로 그겁니다!\""], ["cried Clare, pleased to think that she had reverted to the real pronunciation.", "클레어가 외쳤다. 그녀가 본래의 발음으로 되돌아갔구나 싶어 기뻤다."], ["“What place is The Herons?”", "\"헤런즈가 어떤 곳이지요?\""]], [["“A stylish lodging-house.", "\"멋들어진 하숙집이에요."], ["’Tis all lodging-houses here, bless ’ee.”", "여긴 온통 하숙집이지요, 정말이지.\""]], [["Clare received directions how to find the house, and hastened thither, arriving with the milkman.", "클레어는 그 집을 찾아가는 길을 안내받아 서둘러 그리로 갔고, 우유 배달부와 함께 도착했다."], ["The Herons, though an ordinary villa, stood in its own grounds, and was certainly the last place in which one would have expected to find lodgings, so private was its appearance.", "헤런즈는 평범한 별장이긴 했으나 제 부지 안에 들어앉아 있었고, 외양이 어찌나 사사로워 보이는지 하숙을 구하리라곤 도무지 기대하기 어려운 곳이었다."], ["If poor Tess was a servant here, as he feared, she would go to the back-door to that milkman, and he was inclined to go thither also.", "가엾은 테스가 그가 두려워하던 대로 여기서 하인 노릇을 하고 있다면, 그녀는 저 우유 배달부에게로 뒷문으로 갈 터였고, 그도 그리로 가고 싶은 마음이 들었다."], ["However, in his doubts he turned to the front, and rang.", "그러나 망설이던 끝에 그는 앞쪽으로 돌아가 초인종을 울렸다."]], [["The hour being early, the landlady herself opened the door.", "이른 시각이라 안주인이 직접 문을 열었다."], ["Clare inquired for Teresa d’Urberville or Durbeyfield.", "클레어는 테레사 더버빌, 또는 더비필드를 찾았다."]], [["“Mrs d’Urberville?”", "\"더버빌 부인요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["Tess, then, passed as a married woman, and he felt glad, even though she had not adopted his name.", "그렇다면 테스가 결혼한 여인으로 통하는구나, 싶어 그는, 비록 그녀가 자기 성을 쓰지는 않았지만, 기뻤다."]], [["“Will you kindly tell her that a relative is anxious to see her?”", "\"친척이 그분을 몹시 보고 싶어 한다고 좀 전해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“It is rather early.", "\"좀 이른데요."], ["What name shall I give, sir?”", "누구라고 전할까요, 선생님?\" \"에인절이라고요.\""]], [["“Angel.” “Mr Angel?”", "\"에인절 씨요?\""]], [["“No; Angel. It is my Christian name.", "\"아니요, 에인절. 제 세례명입니다."], ["She’ll understand.”", "그러면 알아들을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I’ll see if she is awake.”", "\"깨어 있는지 보고 오지요.\""]], [["He was shown into the front room—the dining-room—and looked out through the spring curtains at the little lawn, and the rhododendrons and other shrubs upon it.", "그는 앞쪽 방, 곧 식당으로 안내되었고, 봄철 커튼 너머로 작은 잔디밭과, 그 위의 진달래며 다른 관목들을 내다보았다."], ["Obviously her position was by no means so bad as he had feared, and it crossed his mind that she must somehow have claimed and sold the jewels to attain it.", "그녀의 처지가 그가 두려워했던 것만큼 결코 나쁘지 않은 것이 분명했고, 그녀가 어떻게든 그 보석들을 자기 것으로 청구해 팔아서 이 처지에 이른 게 틀림없다는 생각이 그의 머리를 스쳤다."], ["He did not blame her for one moment.", "그는 한순간도 그녀를 탓하지 않았다."], ["Soon his sharpened ear detected footsteps upon the stairs, at which his heart thumped so painfully that he could hardly stand firm. “Dear me!", "곧 그의 예민해진 귀가 계단 위의 발소리를 잡아냈고, 그 소리에 그의 심장이 어찌나 아프게 쿵쿵거렸던지 그는 거의 똑바로 서 있을 수가 없을 지경이었다. \"이런!"], ["what will she think of me, so altered as I am!”", "이렇게 변해 버린 나를 그 사람이 어찌 생각할까!\""], ["he said to himself; and the door opened.", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그리고 문이 열렸다."]], [["Tess appeared on the threshold—not at all as he had expected to see her—bewilderingly otherwise, indeed.", "테스가 문턱에 나타났다—그가 그녀를 보리라 예상했던 것과는 전혀 다른, 정말이지 어리둥절할 만큼 다른 모습으로."], ["Her great natural beauty was, if not heightened, rendered more obvious by her attire.", "그녀의 위대한 타고난 아름다움은, 한층 돋보였다고는 못해도, 그 차림새로 인해 더 또렷이 드러나 있었다."], ["She was loosely wrapped in a cashmere dressing-gown of gray-white, embroidered in half-mourning tints, and she wore slippers of the same hue.", "그녀는 회백색의 캐시미어 실내복을 헐겁게 두르고 있었는데, 그것은 반쯤 상중인 색조로 수놓아져 있었고, 같은 빛깔의 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다."], ["Her neck rose out of a frill of down, and her well-remembered cable of dark-brown hair was partially coiled up in a mass at the back of her head and partly hanging on her shoulder—the evident result of haste.", "그녀의 목은 솜털 주름 장식 위로 솟아 있었고, 잘 기억하던 그 짙은 갈색 머리채는 일부는 뒤통수에 한 덩이로 말려 올려져 있고 일부는 어깨에 흘러내려 있었으니—분명 서두른 탓이었다."], ["He had held out his arms, but they had fallen again to his side; for she had not come forward, remaining still in the opening of the doorway.", "그는 두 팔을 내밀었으나, 그것은 다시 옆구리로 떨어졌다. 그녀가 앞으로 나오지 않고 문 어귀에 그대로 멈춰 서 있었기 때문이다."], ["Mere yellow skeleton that he was now, he felt the contrast between them, and thought his appearance distasteful to her.", "이제 한낱 누런 해골이 된 그는 둘 사이의 대조를 느꼈고, 자기 모습이 그녀에게 역겨우리라 생각했다."]], [["“Tess!”", "\"테스!\""], ["he said huskily, “can you forgive me for going away?", "그가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 떠나간 걸 용서해 줄 수 있겠소?"], ["Can’t you—come to me?", "나에게—와 줄 수 없겠소?"], ["How do you get to be—like this?”", "어쩌다—이런 모습이 된 거요?\""]], [["“It is too late,”", "\"너무 늦었어요.\""], ["said she, her voice sounding hard through the room, her eyes shining unnaturally.", "그녀가 말했는데, 방 안에 울리는 그녀의 목소리는 딱딱했고, 두 눈은 부자연스럽게 빛났다."]], [["“I did not think rightly of you—I did not see you as you were!”", "\"내가 당신을 옳게 생각하지 못했소—있는 그대로의 당신을 보지 못했던 거요!\""], ["he continued to plead.", "그가 계속 호소했다."], ["“I have learnt to since, dearest Tessy mine!”", "\"그 뒤로 그걸 깨달았소, 더없이 사랑하는 나의 테시!\""]], [["“Too late, too late!” she said, waving her hand in the impatience of a person whose tortures cause every instant to seem an hour.", "\"너무 늦었어요, 너무 늦었어요!\" 고통으로 인해 매 순간이 한 시간처럼 느껴지는 사람의 조급함으로 그녀가 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["“Don’t come close to me, Angel!", "\"제게 가까이 오지 마세요, 에인절!"], ["No—you must not. Keep away.”", "안 돼요—그러시면 안 돼요. 떨어져 계세요.\""]], [["“But don’t you love me, my dear wife, because I have been so pulled down by illness?", "\"하지만 내가 병으로 이렇게 망가졌다고 해서 날 사랑하지 않는 거요, 나의 사랑하는 아내여?"], ["You are not so fickle—I am come on purpose for you—my mother and father will welcome you now!”", "당신은 그렇게 변덕스러운 사람이 아니잖소—나는 오직 당신을 위해 온 거요—이제 내 어머니와 아버지가 당신을 반겨 주실 거요!\""]], [["“Yes—O, yes, yes!", "\"네—오, 그래요, 그래요!"], ["But I say, I say it is too late.”", "하지만 너무 늦었다고, 너무 늦었다고 말하고 있잖아요.\""]], [["She seemed to feel like a fugitive in a dream, who tries to move away, but cannot.", "그녀는 꿈속에서 도망치려 하지만 움직일 수 없는 도망자가 된 듯했다."], ["“Don’t you know all—don’t you know it?", "\"당신은 다 모르세요—그걸 모르시는 거예요?"], ["Yet how do you come here if you do not know?”", "그런데 모르신다면 어떻게 여기 오셨어요?\""]], [["“I inquired here and there, and I found the way.”", "\"여기저기 물어물어 길을 찾아냈소.\""]], [["“I waited and waited for you,”", "\"저는 당신을 기다리고 또 기다렸어요.\""], ["she went on, her tones suddenly resuming their old fluty pathos.", "그녀가 말을 이었는데, 그 어조가 갑자기 예전의 피리 같은 애처로움을 되찾았다."], ["“But you did not come!", "\"그런데 당신은 오지 않았어요!"], ["And I wrote to you, and you did not come!", "그리고 제가 편지를 썼는데, 당신은 오지 않았어요!"], ["He kept on saying you would never come any more, and that I was a foolish woman.", "저 사람이 자꾸만 당신은 다시는 절대 오지 않을 거라고, 제가 어리석은 여자라고 말했어요."], ["He was very kind to me, and to mother, and to all of us after father’s death. He—”", "저 사람은 제게, 어머니에게, 우리 모두에게 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 아주 친절했어요. 저 사람이—\""]], [["“I don’t understand.”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\""]], [["“He has won me back to him.”", "\"저 사람이 저를 자기에게로 도로 차지했어요.\""]], [["Clare looked at her keenly, then, gathering her meaning, flagged like one plague-stricken, and his glance sank; it fell on her hands, which, once rosy, were now white and more delicate.", "클레어는 그녀를 날카롭게 바라보다가, 그 뜻을 알아채고는 역병에 걸린 자처럼 풀썩 꺾였고, 그의 시선이 가라앉았다. 그것은 그녀의 두 손에 떨어졌는데, 한때 발그레하던 그 손이 이제는 희고 한결 더 가냘팠다."]], [["She continued—", "그녀가 말을 이었다—"]], [["“He is upstairs.", "\"저 사람은 위층에 있어요."], ["I hate him now, because he told me a lie—that you would not come again; and you _have_ come!", "저는 이제 저 사람이 미워요, 저한테 거짓말을 했으니까요—당신이 다시는 오지 않을 거라고요. 그런데 당신이 오셨잖아요!"], ["These clothes are what he’s put upon me: I didn’t care what he did wi’ me!", "이 옷들은 저 사람이 제게 입혀 놓은 거예요. 저는 저 사람이 저를 어떻게 하든 상관없었어요!"], ["But—will you go away, Angel, please, and never come any more?”", "하지만—가 주시겠어요, 에인절, 제발, 그리고 다시는 오지 말아 주시겠어요?\""]], [["They stood fixed, their baffled hearts looking out of their eyes with a joylessness pitiful to see.", "그들은 못 박힌 듯 서 있었고, 좌절한 두 마음이 보기 딱할 만큼 기쁨 없는 빛으로 두 눈을 통해 내다보고 있었다."], ["Both seemed to implore something to shelter them from reality.", "둘 다 무언가가 현실로부터 자기들을 지켜 주기를 애원하는 듯했다."]], [["“Ah—it is my fault!” said Clare.", "\"아—내 잘못이오!\" 클레어가 말했다."]], [["But he could not get on.", "하지만 그는 말을 이어 갈 수 없었다."], ["Speech was as inexpressive as silence.", "말은 침묵만큼이나 아무것도 표현해 주지 못했다."], ["But he had a vague consciousness of one thing, though it was not clear to him till later; that his original Tess had spiritually ceased to recognize the body before him as hers—allowing it to drift, like a corpse upon the current, in a direction dissociated from its living will.", "그러나 그에게는 한 가지가 어렴풋이 의식되었으니, 비록 그것이 그에게 분명해진 것은 나중의 일이지만—그의 본래의 테스가 눈앞의 그 육신을 자기 것으로 알아보기를 영혼으로는 그만두었다는 것, 그 육신을, 마치 물살 위의 시신처럼, 그 살아 있는 의지와는 동떨어진 방향으로 떠가도록 내버려 두고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["A few instants passed, and he found that Tess was gone.", "몇 순간이 지났고, 그는 테스가 가 버린 것을 알았다."], ["His face grew colder and more shrunken as he stood concentrated on the moment, and a minute or two after, he found himself in the street, walking along he did not know whither.", "그가 그 순간에 골몰한 채 서 있는 동안 그의 얼굴은 한층 차갑게, 한층 더 오그라들었고, 일이 분 뒤 그는 어디로 가는지도 모르는 채 거리를 걷고 있는 자신을 발견했다."]], [["# LVI", "# LVI"]], [["Mrs Brooks, the lady who was the householder at The Herons and owner of all the handsome furniture, was not a person of an unusually curious turn of mind. She was too deeply materialized, poor woman, by her long and enforced bondage to that arithmetical demon Profit-and-Loss, to retain much curiousity for its own sake, and apart from possible lodgers’ pockets.", "헤런즈의 집주인이자 그 모든 멋진 가구의 주인인 브룩스 부인은 유난히 호기심이 많은 부류의 사람은 아니었다. 가엾게도 그녀는 손익(損益)이라는 그 산술의 마귀에게 오래도록 어쩔 수 없이 종살이한 탓에 너무도 철저히 물질화되어 있어서, 있을 법한 하숙인의 주머니와 관련된 경우 말고는 호기심 자체를 위한 호기심은 별로 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Nevertheless, the visit of Angel Clare to her well-paying tenants, Mr and Mrs d’Urberville, as she deemed them, was sufficiently exceptional in point of time and manner to reinvigorate the feminine proclivity which had been stifled down as useless save in its bearings to the letting trade.", "그렇지만 그녀가 후한 셋돈을 내는 세입자들인, 그녀가 더버빌 부부라 여기던 이들에게 에인절 클레어가 찾아온 일은, 그 시각과 태도로 보아 충분히 이례적이어서, 하숙업과 관련된 경우 말고는 쓸모없다고 짓눌러 두었던 여자다운 성향을 되살려 놓았다."]], [["Tess had spoken to her husband from the doorway, without entering the dining-room, and Mrs Brooks, who stood within the partly-closed door of her own sitting-room at the back of the passage, could hear fragments of the conversation—if conversation it could be called—between those two wretched souls.", "테스는 식당에 들어가지 않은 채 문간에서 남편에게 말했고, 통로 안쪽 자기 거실의 반쯤 닫힌 문 안에 서 있던 브룩스 부인은 그 두 가련한 영혼 사이의 대화의—그것을 대화라 부를 수 있다면—조각들을 들을 수 있었다."], ["She heard Tess re-ascend the stairs to the first floor, and the departure of Clare, and the closing of the front door behind him.", "그녀는 테스가 일 층으로 다시 올라가는 소리와, 클레어가 떠나는 소리와, 그의 뒤로 현관문이 닫히는 소리를 들었다."], ["Then the door of the room above was shut, and Mrs Brooks knew that Tess had re-entered her apartment.", "그러고 나서 위층 방의 문이 닫혔고, 브룩스 부인은 테스가 자기 방으로 다시 들어갔음을 알았다."], ["As the young lady was not fully dressed, Mrs Brooks knew that she would not emerge again for some time.", "그 젊은 부인이 옷을 다 갖춰 입지 않은 상태였으므로, 브룩스 부인은 그녀가 한동안은 다시 나오지 않으리라는 것을 알았다."]], [["She accordingly ascended the stairs softly, and stood at the door of the front room—a drawing-room, connected with the room immediately behind it (which was a bedroom) by folding-doors in the common manner.", "* 일 층: 영국식 표현으로 우리 식의 이 층에 해당함. 그래서 그녀는 살그머니 계단을 올라가, 앞쪽 방—곧 응접실로, 흔한 방식대로 접이문으로 바로 뒤쪽 방(침실이었다)과 이어진—의 문 앞에 섰다."], ["This first floor, containing Mrs Brooks’s best apartments, had been taken by the week by the d’Urbervilles.", "브룩스 부인의 가장 좋은 방들로 이루어진 이 일 층은 더버빌 부부가 주(週) 단위로 빌린 것이었다."], ["The back room was now in silence; but from the drawing-room there came sounds.", "뒤쪽 방은 지금 고요했지만, 응접실에서는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["All that she could at first distinguish of them was one syllable, continually repeated in a low note of moaning, as if it came from a soul bound to some Ixionian wheel—", "처음에 그녀가 그 소리에서 알아들을 수 있었던 것이라곤, 낮은 신음의 가락으로 끊임없이 되풀이되는 한 음절뿐이었으니, 마치 익시온의 수레바퀴에 묶인 어떤 영혼에게서 나오는 것 같았다—\n\"오—오—오!\""]], [["Then a silence, then a heavy sigh, and again—", "그러더니 침묵, 그러더니 무거운 한숨, 그리고 다시—"]], [["The landlady looked through the keyhole.", "\"오—오—오!\" 안주인은 열쇠 구멍으로 들여다보았다."], ["Only a small space of the room inside was visible, but within that space came a corner of the breakfast table, which was already spread for the meal, and also a chair beside.", "안쪽 방의 작은 부분만이 보였지만, 그 보이는 범위 안으로 이미 식사를 위해 차려진 아침상 한 귀퉁이가, 그리고 그 옆의 의자 하나가 들어왔다."], ["Over the seat of the chair Tess’s face was bowed, her posture being a kneeling one in front of it; her hands were clasped over her head, the skirts of her dressing-gown and the embroidery of her night-gown flowed upon the floor behind her, and her stockingless feet, from which the slippers had fallen, protruded upon the carpet.", "그 의자의 앉는 자리 위로 테스의 얼굴이 숙여져 있었으니, 그녀의 자세는 그 앞에 무릎 꿇은 모습이었다. 두 손은 머리 위로 깍지 끼고 있었고, 실내복 자락과 잠옷의 수놓인 자락이 그녀 뒤로 바닥에 흘러내려 있었으며, 슬리퍼가 벗겨진 양말 신지 않은 두 발이 양탄자 위로 드러나 있었다."], ["It was from her lips that came the murmur of unspeakable despair.", "형언할 수 없는 절망의 그 중얼거림은 그녀의 입술에서 나오는 것이었다."]], [["Then a man’s voice from the adjoining bedroom—", "그러더니 옆 침실에서 남자의 목소리가 들려왔다—"]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이야?\""]], [["She did not answer, but went on, in a tone which was a soliloquy rather than an exclamation, and a dirge rather than a soliloquy. Mrs Brooks could only catch a portion:", "그녀는 대답하지 않고, 외침이라기보다 독백에, 독백이라기보다 만가(挽歌)에 가까운 어조로 말을 이어 갔다. 브룩스 부인이 알아들을 수 있었던 것은 일부뿐이었다."]], [["“And then my dear, dear husband came home to me ...", "\"그러고 나서 제 사랑하는, 사랑하는 남편이 제게로 돌아왔는데……"], ["and I did not know it!...", "저는 그걸 몰랐어요!……"], ["And you had used your cruel persuasion upon me ...", "그리고 당신은 제게 그 잔인한 설득을 써먹었지요……"], ["you did not stop using it—no—you did not stop!", "당신은 그걸 멈추지 않았어요—그래요—멈추지 않았어요!"], ["My little sisters and brothers and my mother’s needs—they were the things you moved me by ...", "제 어린 동생들과 어머니의 궁핍—그게 바로 당신이 저를 흔들어 댄 미끼였어요……"], ["and you said my husband would never come back—never; and you taunted me, and said what a simpleton I was to expect him!...", "그리고 당신은 제 남편이 절대 돌아오지 않을 거라고, 절대로, 라고 말했어요. 그리고 당신은 저를 비웃으며, 그이를 기다리다니 내가 얼마나 멍청한 여자냐고 했지요!……"], ["And at last I believed you and gave way!...", "그리고 결국 저는 당신 말을 믿고 굴복하고 말았어요!……"], ["And then he came back!", "그런데 그이가 돌아왔어요!"], ["Now he is gone. Gone a second time, and I have lost him now for ever ...", "이제 그이는 가 버렸어요. 두 번째로 가 버렸고, 이제 저는 그이를 영영 잃었어요……"], ["and he will not love me the littlest bit ever any more—only hate me!...", "그리고 그이는 이제 다시는 저를 조금도 사랑하지 않을 거예요—저를 미워할 뿐이겠지요!……"], ["O yes, I have lost him now—again because of—you!”", "오 그래요, 저는 이제 그이를 잃었어요—또다시, 당신 때문에!\""], ["In writhing, with her head on the chair, she turned her face towards the door, and Mrs Brooks could see the pain upon it, and that her lips were bleeding from the clench of her teeth upon them, and that the long lashes of her closed eyes stuck in wet tags to her cheeks.", "머리를 의자에 댄 채 몸부림치며 그녀는 얼굴을 문 쪽으로 돌렸고, 브룩스 부인은 그 위에 어린 고통을 볼 수 있었다. 그녀의 입술이 이를 악문 탓에 피를 흘리고 있는 것도, 감긴 두 눈의 긴 속눈썹이 젖은 채 갈래갈래 뺨에 들러붙어 있는 것도. 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["She continued: “And he is dying—he looks as if he is dying!...", "\"그리고 그이는 죽어 가고 있어요—마치 죽어 가는 사람 같았어요!……"], ["And my sin will kill him and not kill me!...", "그리고 제 죄가 저는 죽이지 않고 그이를 죽일 거예요!……"], ["O, you have torn my life all to pieces ...", "오, 당신은 제 삶을 갈가리 찢어 놓았어요……"], ["made me be what I prayed you in pity not to make me be again!...", "제가 다시는 그렇게 되지 않게 해 달라고 가엾이 여겨 빌었던 그 꼴로 저를 만들어 놓았어요!……"], ["My own true husband will never, never—O God—I can’t bear this!—I cannot!”", "저의 진정한 남편은 결코, 결코—오 하느님—저는 이걸 견딜 수 없어요!—견딜 수가 없어요!\""]], [["There were more and sharper words from the man; then a sudden rustle; she had sprung to her feet.", "남자에게서 더 많은, 더 날카로운 말들이 나왔다. 그러더니 갑작스러운 옷자락 소리—그녀가 벌떡 일어선 것이었다."], ["Mrs Brooks, thinking that the speaker was coming to rush out of the door, hastily retreated down the stairs.", "브룩스 부인은 말하던 사람이 문밖으로 뛰쳐나오려는 줄 알고 황급히 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["She need not have done so, however, for the door of the sitting-room was not opened.", "그러나 그럴 필요는 없었으니, 거실 문은 열리지 않았기 때문이다."], ["But Mrs Brooks felt it unsafe to watch on the landing again, and entered her own parlour below.", "하지만 브룩스 부인은 다시 층계참에서 엿보는 것이 위험하다고 느껴, 아래층 자기 거실로 들어갔다."]], [["She could hear nothing through the floor, although she listened intently, and thereupon went to the kitchen to finish her interrupted breakfast.", "그녀는 가만히 귀를 기울였으나 바닥 너머로는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았고, 그리하여 부엌으로 가서 중단했던 아침 식사를 마저 했다."], ["Coming up presently to the front room on the ground floor she took up some sewing, waiting for her lodgers to ring that she might take away the breakfast, which she meant to do herself, to discover what was the matter if possible.", "이윽고 일 층의 앞쪽 방으로 올라온 그녀는 바느질감을 집어 들고, 하숙인들이 종을 울려 아침상을 치우라 하기를 기다렸다. 그 일을 직접 하여, 가능하면 무슨 일인지 알아낼 작정이었다."], ["Overhead, as she sat, she could now hear the floorboards slightly creak, as if some one were walking about, and presently the movement was explained by the rustle of garments against the banisters, the opening and the closing of the front door, and the form of Tess passing to the gate on her way into the street.", "앉아 있는 동안 머리 위로, 그녀는 이제 누군가가 걸어 다니는 듯 마룻장이 가볍게 삐걱대는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 이윽고 그 움직임은 난간에 옷자락이 스치는 소리와, 현관문이 열렸다 닫히는 소리와, 거리로 나가는 길에 대문 쪽으로 지나가는 테스의 모습으로 설명되었다."], ["She was fully dressed now in the walking costume of a well-to-do young lady in which she had arrived, with the sole addition that over her hat and black feathers a veil was drawn.", "그녀는 이제 도착할 때 입었던, 형편이 넉넉한 젊은 부인의 외출복을 완전히 갖춰 입고 있었는데, 다만 모자와 검은 깃털 위로 베일을 드리운 것만이 더해져 있었다. * 일 층: 영국식 표현으로 우리 식의 이 층에 해당함."]], [["Mrs Brooks had not been able to catch any word of farewell, temporary or otherwise, between her tenants at the door above.", "브룩스 부인은 위층 문간에서 세입자들 사이에 오간, 일시적이든 아니든 어떤 작별 인사도 한마디 잡아내지 못했다."], ["They might have quarrelled, or Mr d’Urberville might still be asleep, for he was not an early riser.", "그들이 다투었거나, 아니면 더버빌 씨가 아직 자고 있는지도 몰랐다. 그는 일찍 일어나는 사람이 아니었으니까."]], [["She went into the back room, which was more especially her own apartment, and continued her sewing there.", "그녀는 더 각별히 자기만의 거처인 뒤쪽 방으로 들어가 거기서 바느질을 계속했다."], ["The lady lodger did not return, nor did the gentleman ring his bell.", "부인 하숙인은 돌아오지 않았고, 신사도 종을 울리지 않았다."], ["Mrs Brooks pondered on the delay, and on what probable relation the visitor who had called so early bore to the couple upstairs.", "브룩스 부인은 그 지체에 대해, 그리고 그토록 이르게 찾아온 그 방문객이 위층의 부부와 어떤 관계일까에 대해 골똘히 생각했다."], ["In reflecting she leant back in her chair.", "생각에 잠긴 채 그녀는 의자에 등을 기댔다."]], [["As she did so her eyes glanced casually over the ceiling till they were arrested by a spot in the middle of its white surface which she had never noticed there before.", "그러다가 그녀의 눈이 무심코 천장을 훑었는데, 전에는 거기서 한 번도 본 적 없는, 그 흰 표면 한가운데의 한 점에 시선이 붙들렸다."], ["It was about the size of a wafer when she first observed it, but it speedily grew as large as the palm of her hand, and then she could perceive that it was red.", "처음 보았을 때는 봉인용 풀떡지만 한 크기였으나 그것은 순식간에 손바닥만 하게 커졌고, 그제야 그녀는 그것이 붉은색임을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["The oblong white ceiling, with this scarlet blot in the midst, had the appearance of a gigantic ace of hearts.", "한가운데에 이 주홍빛 얼룩이 박힌 그 직사각형의 흰 천장은, 거대한 하트 에이스 카드 같은 모양새였다."]], [["Mrs Brooks had strange qualms of misgiving.", "브룩스 부인은 야릇한 불안의 욱신거림을 느꼈다."], ["She got upon the table, and touched the spot in the ceiling with her fingers.", "그녀는 식탁 위로 올라가 손가락으로 천장의 그 점을 만져 보았다."], ["It was damp, and she fancied that it was a blood stain.", "축축했고, 그녀는 그것이 핏자국이 아닐까 싶었다."]], [["Descending from the table, she left the parlour, and went upstairs, intending to enter the room overhead, which was the bedchamber at the back of the drawing-room.", "식탁에서 내려와 그녀는 거실을 나와 위층으로 올라갔으니, 머리 위의 방, 곧 응접실 뒤쪽의 침실로 들어갈 작정이었다."], ["But, nerveless woman as she had now become, she could not bring herself to attempt the handle. She listened.", "하지만 이제 넋이 나가 버린 여자가 된 그녀는 도무지 손잡이를 잡을 엄두를 낼 수 없었다. 그녀는 귀를 기울였다."], ["The dead silence within was broken only by a regular beat.", "안의 죽은 듯한 고요는 오직 규칙적인 박동 소리로만 깨지고 있었다."]], [["Drip, drip, drip.", "뚝, 뚝, 뚝."]], [["Mrs Brooks hastened downstairs, opened the front door, and ran into the street.", "브룩스 부인은 황급히 계단을 내려가 현관문을 열고 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["A man she knew, one of the workmen employed at an adjoining villa, was passing by, and she begged him to come in and go upstairs with her; she feared something had happened to one of her lodgers.", "그녀가 아는 한 남자, 곧 이웃 별장에서 일하는 일꾼 하나가 지나가고 있기에, 그녀는 그에게 안으로 들어와 자기와 함께 위층으로 가 달라고 사정했다. 하숙인 중 누군가에게 무슨 일이 생긴 것 같아 두렵다고."], ["The workman assented, and followed her to the landing.", "일꾼은 응낙하고 그녀를 따라 층계참으로 올라갔다."]], [["She opened the door of the drawing-room, and stood back for him to pass in, entering herself behind him.", "그녀는 응접실 문을 열고 그가 들어가도록 뒤로 물러섰다가, 자기도 그의 뒤를 따라 들어갔다."], ["The room was empty; the breakfast—a substantial repast of coffee, eggs, and a cold ham—lay spread upon the table untouched, as when she had taken it up, excepting that the carving-knife was missing.", "방은 비어 있었다. 아침상은—커피와 달걀과 차가운 햄으로 이루어진 푸짐한 식사가—그녀가 들여놓았을 때 그대로, 손도 대지 않은 채 식탁 위에 차려져 있었는데, 다만 고기 써는 칼이 없어진 것만 달랐다."], ["She asked the man to go through the folding-doors into the adjoining room.", "그녀는 남자에게 접이문을 지나 옆방으로 들어가 달라고 청했다."]], [["He opened the doors, entered a step or two, and came back almost instantly with a rigid face.", "그가 문을 열고 한두 걸음 들어가더니, 거의 곧바로 굳어진 얼굴로 돌아 나왔다."], ["“My good God, the gentleman in bed is dead! I think he has been hurt with a knife—a lot of blood has run down upon the floor!”", "\"오 하느님 맙소사, 침대에 있는 신사가 죽었습니다! 칼에 찔린 것 같아요—피가 바닥으로 잔뜩 흘러내렸어요!\""]], [["The alarm was soon given, and the house which had lately been so quiet resounded with the tramp of many footsteps, a surgeon among the rest.", "곧 경보가 울렸고, 얼마 전까지만 해도 그토록 조용하던 그 집이 수많은 발소리로 울렸으니, 그중에는 외과의도 하나 끼어 있었다."], ["The wound was small, but the point of the blade had touched the heart of the victim, who lay on his back, pale, fixed, dead, as if he had scarcely moved after the infliction of the blow.", "상처는 작았으나, 칼끝이 희생자의 심장에 닿아 있었다. 그는 등을 대고 누운 채, 창백하고, 굳어, 죽어 있었다. 일격을 당한 뒤로 거의 몸을 움직이지 못한 듯했다."], ["In a quarter of an hour the news that a gentleman who was a temporary visitor to the town had been stabbed in his bed, spread through every street and villa of the popular watering-place.", "십오 분 만에 이 도시를 잠시 찾은 손님인 한 신사가 침대에서 칼에 찔렸다는 소식이 이 인기 휴양지의 거리와 별장마다 퍼졌다."]], [["# LVII", "# LVII"]], [["Meanwhile Angel Clare had walked automatically along the way by which he had come, and, entering his hotel, sat down over the breakfast, staring at nothingness.", "그 사이 에인절 클레어는 자기가 왔던 길을 따라 기계적으로 걸어, 호텔로 들어가 아침 식탁 앞에 앉아 허공을 멍하니 바라보았다. 그는 무의식중에 먹고 마시기를 계속하다가, 문득 계산서를 청했다."], ["He went on eating and drinking unconsciously till on a sudden he demanded his bill; having paid which, he took his dressing-bag in his hand, the only luggage he had brought with him, and went out.", "값을 치른 뒤 그는 손에 화장 가방을 들었다. 들고 온 짐이라고는 그것뿐이었다. 그리고 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["At the moment of his departure a telegram was handed to him—a few words from his mother, stating that they were glad to know his address, and informing him that his brother Cuthbert had proposed to and been accepted by Mercy Chant.", "그가 떠나려는 순간 전보 한 통이 그에게 전해졌다. 어머니가 보낸 몇 마디였다. 그의 주소를 알게 되어 다들 기쁘다는 내용과, 그의 형 커스버트가 머시 챈트에게 청혼했고 받아들여졌다는 소식이었다."]], [["Clare crumpled up the paper and followed the route to the station; reaching it, he found that there would be no train leaving for an hour and more.", "클레어는 그 종이를 구겨 버리고 역으로 가는 길을 따라갔다. 역에 닿고 보니 한 시간 넘게 떠날 기차가 없었다."], ["He sat down to wait, and having waited a quarter of an hour felt that he could wait there no longer.", "그는 앉아서 기다렸고, 십오 분쯤 기다리고 나니 더는 거기서 기다릴 수 없을 것 같았다."], ["Broken in heart and numbed, he had nothing to hurry for; but he wished to get out of a town which had been the scene of such an experience, and turned to walk to the first station onward, and let the train pick him up there.", "마음이 부서지고 무감각해진 그는 서두를 일이 아무것도 없었다. 다만 그런 일의 무대가 된 도시에서 벗어나고 싶었다. 그래서 다음 역까지 걸어가, 거기서 기차에 오르기로 하고 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["The highway that he followed was open, and at a little distance dipped into a valley, across which it could be seen running from edge to edge.", "그가 따라간 큰길은 탁 트여 있었고, 조금 가다가 골짜기로 내려갔다. 골짜기를 가로지르며 한쪽 끝에서 다른 끝까지 길이 뻗은 것이 보였다."], ["He had traversed the greater part of this depression, and was climbing the western acclivity when, pausing for breath, he unconsciously looked back.", "그는 이 우묵한 곳의 거의 전부를 지나, 서쪽 비탈을 오르던 중이었다. 숨을 고르려고 멈춰 선 그는 무심코 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["Why he did so he could not say, but something seemed to impel him to the act.", "왜 그랬는지 그 자신도 알 수 없었으나, 무언가가 그를 그렇게 하도록 떠미는 듯했다."], ["The tape-like surface of the road diminished in his rear as far as he could see, and as he gazed a moving spot intruded on the white vacuity of its perspective.", "끈처럼 길게 뻗은 길의 표면이 등 뒤로 보이는 데까지 점점 줄어들었고, 그가 바라보는 사이 움직이는 점 하나가 그 흰 텅 빈 원근 속으로 끼어들었다."]], [["It was a human figure running.", "달리는 사람의 모습이었다."], ["Clare waited, with a dim sense that somebody was trying to overtake him.", "클레어는, 누군가 자기를 따라잡으려 한다는 어렴풋한 느낌을 가지고 기다렸다."]], [["The form descending the incline was a woman’s, yet so entirely was his mind blinded to the idea of his wife’s following him that even when she came nearer he did not recognize her under the totally changed attire in which he now beheld her.", "비탈을 내려오는 형체는 여자의 것이었다. 그러나 그의 마음은 아내가 자기를 뒤쫓고 있으리라는 생각에는 전혀 닿지 못한 터라, 그녀가 가까이 왔을 때조차도 지금 그가 보는 완전히 달라진 옷차림 아래에서 그녀를 알아보지 못했다."], ["It was not till she was quite close that he could believe her to be Tess.", "아주 가까이 다가오고 나서야 그는 그녀가 테스임을 믿을 수 있었다."]], [["“I saw you—turn away from the station—just before I got there—and I have been following you all this way!”", "\"역에서 당신이—돌아서는 걸—내가 거기 닿기 직전에 봤어요—그래서 여기까지 줄곧 당신을 따라왔어요!\""]], [["She was so pale, so breathless, so quivering in every muscle, that he did not ask her a single question, but seizing her hand, and pulling it within his arm, he led her along.", "그녀는 어찌나 창백하고, 어찌나 숨이 가쁘고, 온몸의 근육이 어찌나 떨리던지, 그는 그녀에게 한마디 묻지도 못하고 그녀의 손을 붙잡아 자기 팔 안으로 끌어당기며 데리고 걸었다."], ["To avoid meeting any possible wayfarers he left the high road and took a footpath under some fir-trees.", "혹시 마주칠지도 모를 행인을 피하려고 그는 큰길을 벗어나 전나무 몇 그루 아래 오솔길로 들어섰다."], ["When they were deep among the moaning boughs he stopped and looked at her inquiringly.", "신음하는 나뭇가지들 사이로 깊이 들어가자 그는 걸음을 멈추고 캐묻듯 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Angel,”", "\"에인절,\""], ["she said, as if waiting for this, “do you know what I have been running after you for?", "마치 이 순간을 기다렸다는 듯 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 왜 당신을 뒤쫓아 달려왔는지 알아요?"], ["To tell you that I have killed him!”", "내가 그를 죽였다고 말하려고요!\""], ["A pitiful white smile lit her face as she spoke.", "말하는 동안 가엾은 흰 미소가 그녀의 얼굴을 밝혔다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["said he, thinking from the strangeness of her manner that she was in some delirium.", "그가 말했다. 그녀의 이상한 태도로 보아 무슨 헛소리를 하는 게 아닌가 싶었다."]], [["“I have done it—I don’t know how,”", "\"내가 해 버렸어요—어떻게 했는지 모르겠어요.\""], ["she continued.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“Still, I owed it to you, and to myself, Angel.", "\"그래도, 그건 당신을 위해, 또 나 자신을 위해 갚아야 할 빚이었어요, 에인절."], ["I feared long ago, when I struck him on the mouth with my glove, that I might do it some day for the trap he set for me in my simple youth, and his wrong to you through me.", "오래전에, 내 장갑으로 그의 입을 쳤을 때 두려웠어요. 순진했던 어린 시절 그가 나한테 놓은 덫과, 나를 통해 당신에게 저지른 잘못 때문에 언젠가 내가 이런 짓을 할지도 모른다고요."], ["He has come between us and ruined us, and now he can never do it any more.", "그는 우리 사이에 끼어들어 우리를 망쳤고, 이제는 다시는 그러지 못할 거예요."], ["I never loved him at all, Angel, as I loved you.", "나는 당신을 사랑한 만큼 그를 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없었어요, 에인절."], ["You know it, don’t you?", "알잖아요, 그렇죠? 믿죠?"], ["You believe it? You didn’t come back to me, and I was obliged to go back to him.", "당신은 내게 돌아오지 않았고, 나는 어쩔 수 없이 그에게로 돌아가야 했어요."], ["Why did you go away—why did you—when I loved you so?", "왜 가 버렸어요—왜 그랬어요—내가 당신을 그토록 사랑했는데?"], ["I can’t think why you did it.", "왜 그랬는지 도무지 알 수가 없어요."], ["But I don’t blame you; only, Angel, will you forgive me my sin against you, now I have killed him?", "하지만 당신을 탓하진 않아요. 다만, 에인절, 이제 내가 그를 죽였으니, 당신에게 지은 내 죄를 용서해 주겠어요?"], ["I thought as I ran along that you would be sure to forgive me now I have done that.", "달려오면서 생각했어요, 이제 그 일을 했으니 당신이 틀림없이 나를 용서해 주리라고요."], ["It came to me as a shining light that I should get you back that way.", "그렇게 하면 당신을 되찾을 수 있으리라는 게 환한 빛처럼 떠올랐어요."], ["I could not bear the loss of you any longer—you don’t know how entirely I was unable to bear your not loving me!", "더는 당신을 잃은 채로 견딜 수가 없었어요—당신이 나를 사랑하지 않는다는 걸 내가 얼마나 도무지 견딜 수 없었는지 당신은 몰라요!"], ["Say you do now, dear, dear husband; say you do, now I have killed him!”", "이제는 사랑한다고 말해 줘요, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 남편이여. 이제 내가 그를 죽였으니, 사랑한다고 말해 줘요!\""]], [["“I do love you, Tess—O, I do—it is all come back!”", "\"사랑해, 테스—오, 사랑하고말고—다 돌아왔어!\""], ["he said, tightening his arms round her with fervid pressure.", "그가 뜨거운 힘으로 그녀를 두 팔에 더 꽉 끌어안으며 말했다."], ["“But how do you mean—you have killed him?”", "\"그런데 무슨 뜻이야—그를 죽였다니?\""]], [["“I mean that I have,”", "\"그랬다는 뜻이에요.\""], ["she murmured in a reverie.", "그녀가 꿈결 같은 목소리로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“What, bodily?", "\"뭐라고, 몸을?"], ["Is he dead?”", "그가 죽었어?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["He heard me crying about you, and he bitterly taunted me; and called you by a foul name; and then I did it.", "그가 내가 당신 때문에 우는 소리를 듣고는 모질게 비웃었어요. 그리고 당신을 더러운 이름으로 불렀어요. 그래서 내가 해 버렸어요."], ["My heart could not bear it.", "내 마음이 그걸 견딜 수 없었어요."], ["He had nagged me about you before.", "그는 전에도 당신 일로 나를 들볶았어요."], ["And then I dressed myself and came away to find you.”", "그러고 나서 나는 옷을 차려입고 당신을 찾아 나왔어요.\""]], [["By degrees he was inclined to believe that she had faintly attempted, at least, what she said she had done; and his horror at her impulse was mixed with amazement at the strength of her affection for himself, and at the strangeness of its quality, which had apparently extinguished her moral sense altogether.", "차츰 그는, 적어도 그녀가 했다고 말한 일을 어렴풋이나마 시도했으리라는 쪽으로 믿게 되었다. 그녀의 충동에 대한 그의 공포는, 자기를 향한 그녀의 애정의 강렬함에 대한 경악과, 그 애정의 기이한 성질에 대한 경악과 뒤섞였다. 그 애정은 보아하니 그녀의 도덕적 분별을 송두리째 꺼뜨려 버린 듯했다."], ["Unable to realize the gravity of her conduct, she seemed at last content; and he looked at her as she lay upon his shoulder, weeping with happiness, and wondered what obscure strain in the d’Urberville blood had led to this aberration—if it were an aberration.", "자기 행동의 무게를 깨닫지 못한 채 그녀는 마침내 만족한 듯 보였다. 그녀가 그의 어깨에 기대 행복에 겨워 울고 있는 모습을 바라보면서, 그는 더버빌 가문의 피 속 어떤 알 수 없는 줄기가 이 일탈로—그것이 일탈이라면—이끌었을까 의아했다."], ["There momentarily flashed through his mind that the family tradition of the coach and murder might have arisen because the d’Urbervilles had been known to do these things.", "마차와 살인에 얽힌 그 집안의 전설이, 더버빌 가가 그런 짓을 저지른 것으로 알려진 데서 비롯된 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 순간 그의 마음을 스쳤다."], ["As well as his confused and excited ideas could reason, he supposed that in the moment of mad grief of which she spoke, her mind had lost its balance, and plunged her into this abyss.", "어지럽고 흥분한 그의 머릿속이 추리할 수 있는 한, 그녀가 말한 미친 듯한 슬픔의 순간에 그녀의 정신이 균형을 잃고 그녀를 이 심연으로 밀어넣은 것이리라고 그는 짐작했다."]], [["It was very terrible if true; if a temporary hallucination, sad.", "만약 사실이라면 몹시 끔찍한 일이었고, 일시적 환각이라면 슬픈 일이었다."], ["But, anyhow, here was this deserted wife of his, this passionately-fond woman, clinging to him without a suspicion that he would be anything to her but a protector.", "하지만 어쨌든, 여기 그에게 버림받았던 이 아내가, 열정적으로 그를 사모하는 이 여인이, 그가 자기에게 보호자 외의 다른 무엇이 되리라고는 추호도 의심하지 않은 채 그에게 매달려 있었다."], ["He saw that for him to be otherwise was not, in her mind, within the region of the possible.", "자기가 그렇지 않은 무언가가 되는 일은 그녀의 마음속에서 가능성의 영역 안에 있지조차 않다는 것을 그는 알아차렸다."], ["Tenderness was absolutely dominant in Clare at last.", "마침내 클레어 안에서 다정함이 완전히 우세를 차지했다."], ["He kissed her endlessly with his white lips, and held her hand, and said—", "그는 자신의 흰 입술로 그녀에게 끝없이 입맞추고, 그녀의 손을 잡고, 말했다—"]], [["“I will not desert you!", "\"당신을 버리지 않을 거야!"], ["I will protect you by every means in my power, dearest love, whatever you may have done or not have done!”", "내 힘이 닿는 온갖 수단을 다해 당신을 지킬 거야, 가장 사랑하는 사람아, 당신이 무슨 일을 했든 안 했든!\""]], [["They then walked on under the trees, Tess turning her head every now and then to look at him.", "그러고 나서 그들은 나무 아래로 계속 걸었다. 테스는 이따금 고개를 돌려 그를 바라보았다."], ["Worn and unhandsome as he had become, it was plain that she did not discern the least fault in his appearance.", "그가 아무리 초췌하고 볼품없어졌다 해도, 그녀가 그의 외모에서 조금의 흠도 찾아내지 못한다는 것은 분명했다."], ["To her he was, as of old, all that was perfection, personally and mentally.", "그녀에게 그는 예전처럼 완벽 그 자체였다, 인격으로도 정신으로도."], ["He was still her Antinous, her Apollo even; his sickly face was beautiful as the morning to her affectionate regard on this day no less than when she first beheld him; for was it not the face of the one man on earth who had loved her purely, and who had believed in her as pure!", "그는 여전히 그녀의 안티노오스였고, 그녀의 아폴론이기까지 했다. 그의 병색 짙은 얼굴은, 그녀가 그를 처음 보았을 때 못지않게 이날에도 그녀의 애정 어린 눈길에 아침처럼 아름다웠다. 그것이야말로 자기를 순결하게 사랑해 주었고 자기를 순결하다고 믿어 준, 이 세상에서 유일한 남자의 얼굴이 아니던가!"]], [["With an instinct as to possibilities, he did not now, as he had intended, make for the first station beyond the town, but plunged still farther under the firs, which here abounded for miles.", "여러 가능성에 대한 본능 때문에, 그는 이제 애초 작정했던 대로 도시 너머 첫 역으로 향하지 않고, 여기서 몇 마일에 걸쳐 우거진 전나무 숲속으로 더 깊이 파고들었다."], ["Each clasping the other round the waist they promenaded over the dry bed of fir-needles, thrown into a vague intoxicating atmosphere at the consciousness of being together at last, with no living soul between them; ignoring that there was a corpse.", "서로의 허리를 껴안은 채 그들은 마른 전나무 잎이 깔린 바닥 위를 거닐었다. 마침내 둘 사이에 살아 있는 사람 하나 없이 함께 있다는 의식에 어렴풋한 도취의 분위기로 빠져들면서, 시신 하나가 있다는 사실은 못 본 척하면서."], ["Thus they proceeded for several miles till Tess, arousing herself, looked about her, and said, timidly—", "이렇게 그들은 여러 마일을 나아가다가, 테스가 정신을 차리고 둘레를 살피며 머뭇머뭇 말했다—"]], [["“Are we going anywhere in particular?”", "\"우리 어디 정해 둔 데로 가는 거예요?\""]], [["“I don’t know, dearest. Why?”", "\"모르겠어, 사랑하는 사람아. 왜?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Well, we might walk a few miles further, and when it is evening find lodgings somewhere or other—in a lonely cottage, perhaps.", "\"음, 우리 몇 마일 더 걸을 수도 있고, 저녁이 되면 어디든—외딴 오두막이라도—묵을 곳을 찾을 수 있겠지."], ["Can you walk well, Tessy?”", "잘 걸을 수 있겠어, 테시?\""]], [["“O yes! I could walk for ever and ever with your arm round me!”", "\"오 그럼요! 당신 팔이 나를 안고 있으면 영원히, 영원히라도 걸을 수 있어요!\""]], [["Upon the whole it seemed a good thing to do.", "대체로 그렇게 하는 게 좋을 듯했다."], ["Thereupon they quickened their pace, avoiding high roads, and following obscure paths tending more or less northward.", "그리하여 그들은 걸음을 재촉했고, 큰길을 피해 대체로 북쪽으로 향하는 외진 길들을 따라갔다."], ["But there was an unpractical vagueness in their movements throughout the day; neither one of them seemed to consider any question of effectual escape, disguise, or long concealment.", "그러나 하루 종일 그들의 움직임에는 비현실적인 막연함이 있었다. 둘 중 누구도 효과적인 도주나 변장이나 장기간의 은신 같은 문제를 고려하는 것 같지 않았다."], ["Their every idea was temporary and unforefending, like the plans of two children.", "그들의 모든 생각은 일시적이고 앞을 내다보지 못하는 것이었다, 마치 두 아이의 계획처럼."]], [["At mid-day they drew near to a roadside inn, and Tess would have entered it with him to get something to eat, but he persuaded her to remain among the trees and bushes of this half-woodland, half-moorland part of the country till he should come back.", "한낮에 그들은 길가의 여관 가까이 다가갔고, 테스는 그와 함께 들어가 뭔가 먹을 것을 구하려 했으나, 그가 그녀를 설득해 자기가 돌아올 때까지 절반은 숲이고 절반은 황야인 이 지역의 나무와 덤불 사이에 남아 있게 했다."], ["Her clothes were of recent fashion; even the ivory-handled parasol that she carried was of a shape unknown in the retired spot to which they had now wandered; and the cut of such articles would have attracted attention in the settle of a tavern.", "그녀의 옷은 최신 유행을 따른 것이었다. 심지어 그녀가 든 상아 손잡이 양산조차도, 그들이 지금 흘러들어온 이 외진 곳에서는 본 적 없는 모양이었다. 그런 물건들의 재단새는 선술집 긴 의자에 앉은 이들의 눈길을 끌 만했다."], ["He soon returned, with food enough for half-a-dozen people and two bottles of wine—enough to last them for a day or more, should any emergency arise.", "그는 곧 돌아왔다, 여섯 사람분은 될 음식과 포도주 두 병을 들고서—혹시라도 무슨 비상사태가 생기면 하루나 그 이상 버틸 만큼이었다."]], [["They sat down upon some dead boughs and shared their meal.", "그들은 죽은 나뭇가지 위에 앉아 식사를 나눴다."], ["Between one and two o’clock they packed up the remainder and went on again.", "한 시와 두 시 사이에 그들은 남은 것을 챙겨 다시 나아갔다."]], [["“I feel strong enough to walk any distance,” said she.", "\"나 얼마든지 걸을 만큼 힘이 나요.\" 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I think we may as well steer in a general way towards the interior of the country, where we can hide for a time, and are less likely to be looked for than anywhere near the coast,”", "\"우리는 대체로 나라 안쪽으로 방향을 잡는 게 나을 것 같아. 거기서 한동안 숨어 있을 수 있고, 해안 근처 어디보다도 수색당할 가능성이 적으니까.\""], ["Clare remarked.", "클레어가 말했다."], ["“Later on, when they have forgotten us, we can make for some port.”", "\"나중에, 그들이 우리를 잊었을 때, 어느 항구로 향하면 돼.\""]], [["She made no reply to this beyond that of grasping him more tightly, and straight inland they went. Though the season was an English May, the weather was serenely bright, and during the afternoon it was quite warm.", "이 말에 그녀는 그를 더 꽉 붙잡는 것 말고는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 그들은 곧장 내륙으로 들어갔다. 영국의 오월이었지만 날씨는 잔잔하고 화창했고, 오후 동안은 제법 따뜻했다."], ["Through the latter miles of their walk their footpath had taken them into the depths of the New Forest, and towards evening, turning the corner of a lane, they perceived behind a brook and bridge a large board on which was painted in white letters, “This desirable Mansion to be Let Furnished”; particulars following, with directions to apply to some London agents.", "걷는 마지막 몇 마일을 지나는 동안 오솔길은 그들을 뉴 포레스트의 깊숙한 곳으로 데려갔고, 저녁 무렵 어느 좁은 길의 모퉁이를 돌다가, 그들은 개울과 다리 너머에 흰 글씨로 \"이 매력적인 저택을 가구 딸려 세놓음\"이라고 칠해진 커다란 간판을 보았다. 자세한 내용이 이어졌고, 런던의 어느 중개업소로 문의하라는 안내가 붙어 있었다."], ["Passing through the gate they could see the house, an old brick building of regular design and large accommodation.", "문을 지나자 집이 보였다. 정연한 설계에 넉넉한 방을 갖춘 오래된 벽돌 건물이었다."]], [["“I know it,” said Clare.", "\"나 저 집 알아.\""], ["“It is Bramshurst Court.", "클레어가 말했다."], ["You can see that it is shut up, and grass is growing on the drive.”", "\"브램스허스트 코트야. 보다시피 문이 잠겨 있고, 진입로엔 풀이 자라고 있어.\""]], [["“Some of the windows are open,”", "\"창문 몇 개는 열려 있는데요.\""], ["said Tess.", "테스가 말했다."]], [["“Just to air the rooms, I suppose.”", "\"그냥 방을 환기하려는 거겠지.\""]], [["“All these rooms empty, and we without a roof to our heads!”", "\"이 방들이 다 비어 있는데, 우리는 머리 위에 지붕 하나 없네요!\""]], [["“You are getting tired, my Tess!”", "\"지치는구나, 나의 테스!\""], ["he said. “We’ll stop soon.”", "그가 말했다. \"곧 멈출게.\""], ["And kissing her sad mouth, he again led her onwards.", "그러고는 그녀의 슬픈 입에 입맞추고, 다시 그녀를 이끌고 나아갔다."]], [["He was growing weary likewise, for they had wandered a dozen or fifteen miles, and it became necessary to consider what they should do for rest.", "그 역시 점점 지쳐 갔다. 그들은 열두어 마일에서 열다섯 마일을 헤맸으니, 어디서 쉴지를 궁리해야 할 때였다."], ["They looked from afar at isolated cottages and little inns, and were inclined to approach one of the latter, when their hearts failed them, and they sheered off. At length their gait dragged, and they stood still.", "그들은 멀리서 외딴 오두막들과 작은 여관들을 바라보았고, 그중 여관 하나에 다가가려 마음먹다가도, 가슴이 졸아들어 비켜 갔다. 마침내 그들의 걸음은 질질 끌렸고, 그들은 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Could we sleep under the trees?”", "\"우리 나무 아래서 자도 될까요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["He thought the season insufficiently advanced.", "그는 계절이 아직 그러기엔 충분히 무르익지 않았다고 생각했다."]], [["“I have been thinking of that empty mansion we passed,”", "\"우리가 지나온 그 빈 저택을 생각하고 있었어.\""], ["he said. “Let us go back towards it again.”", "그가 말했다. \"그쪽으로 다시 돌아가자.\""]], [["They retraced their steps, but it was half an hour before they stood without the entrance-gate as earlier.", "그들은 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔으나, 아까처럼 그 정문 밖에 서기까지 반 시간이 걸렸다."], ["He then requested her to stay where she was, whilst he went to see who was within.", "그러고 나서 그는 안에 누가 있는지 보러 가는 동안 그녀에게 있던 자리에 있으라고 청했다."]], [["She sat down among the bushes within the gate, and Clare crept towards the house.", "그녀는 문 안쪽 덤불 사이에 앉았고, 클레어는 집 쪽으로 살금살금 다가갔다."], ["His absence lasted some considerable time, and when he returned Tess was wildly anxious, not for herself, but for him.", "그가 자리를 비운 시간은 제법 길었고, 그가 돌아왔을 때 테스는 자기 자신이 아니라 그를 위해 미친 듯이 애를 태우고 있었다."], ["He had found out from a boy that there was only an old woman in charge as caretaker, and she only came there on fine days, from the hamlet near, to open and shut the windows.", "그는 한 소년에게서 알아냈다, 관리인으로 늙은 여자 하나가 있을 뿐인데, 그 여자는 날씨 좋은 날에만 근처 마을에서 와서 창문을 여닫는다는 것을."], ["She would come to shut them at sunset.", "여자는 해 질 무렵 창문을 닫으러 올 터였다."], ["“Now, we can get in through one of the lower windows, and rest there,” said he.", "\"이제 아래쪽 창문 하나로 들어가서 거기서 쉬면 돼.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["Under his escort she went tardily forward to the main front, whose shuttered windows, like sightless eyeballs, excluded the possibility of watchers.", "그의 호위를 받으며 그녀는 머뭇머뭇 정면 본채로 나아갔다. 덧문을 닫은 창문들은 보지 못하는 눈알들처럼, 누가 지켜볼 가능성을 차단하고 있었다."], ["The door was reached a few steps further, and one of the windows beside it was open.", "몇 걸음 더 가서 문에 닿았고, 그 옆 창문 하나가 열려 있었다."], ["Clare clambered in, and pulled Tess in after him.", "클레어는 기어 올라 들어가, 테스를 뒤따라 끌어 올렸다."]], [["Except the hall, the rooms were all in darkness, and they ascended the staircase.", "현관 홀을 빼면 방들은 모두 어둠 속에 있었고, 그들은 계단을 올랐다."], ["Up here also the shutters were tightly closed, the ventilation being perfunctorily done, for this day at least, by opening the hall-window in front and an upper window behind.", "위층에서도 덧문은 단단히 닫혀 있었다. 환기는, 적어도 이날만큼은, 앞쪽 홀 창문 하나와 뒤쪽 위층 창문 하나를 여는 것으로 형식적으로만 되어 있었다."], ["Clare unlatched the door of a large chamber, felt his way across it, and parted the shutters to the width of two or three inches.", "클레어는 어느 커다란 방의 문 빗장을 풀고, 더듬어 가로질러 가서, 덧문을 두세 인치쯤 벌렸다."], ["A shaft of dazzling sunlight glanced into the room, revealing heavy, old-fashioned furniture, crimson damask hangings, and an enormous four-post bedstead, along the head of which were carved running figures, apparently Atalanta’s race.", "눈부신 햇살 한 줄기가 방 안으로 비쳐 들어와, 묵직한 구식 가구와, 진홍색 다마스크 휘장과, 거대한 사주식 침대를 드러냈다. 침대 머리맡을 따라 달리는 형상들이 새겨져 있었는데, 보아하니 아탈란타의 경주인 듯했다."]], [["“Rest at last!” said he, setting down his bag and the parcel of viands.", "\"드디어 쉴 곳이다!\" 그가 가방과 음식 꾸러미를 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["They remained in great quietness till the caretaker should have come to shut the windows: as a precaution, putting themselves in total darkness by barring the shutters as before, lest the woman should open the door of their chamber for any casual reason.", "그들은 관리인이 창문을 닫으러 올 때까지 깊은 정적 속에 머물렀다. 만일을 위해, 혹시 그 여자가 무슨 우연한 이유로든 자기네 방문을 열까 봐, 아까처럼 덧문을 가로질러 막아 완전한 어둠 속에 자기네 몸을 두었다."], ["Between six and seven o’clock she came, but did not approach the wing they were in.", "여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이에 여자가 왔으나, 그들이 있는 별채 쪽으로는 다가오지 않았다."], ["They heard her close the windows, fasten them, lock the door, and go away.", "그들은 여자가 창문을 닫고, 잠그고, 문을 잠그고, 떠나는 소리를 들었다."], ["Then Clare again stole a chink of light from the window, and they shared another meal, till by-and-by they were enveloped in the shades of night which they had no candle to disperse.", "그러자 클레어는 다시 창문에서 빛 한 줄기를 슬쩍 들였고, 그들은 또 한 끼를 나눴다. 그러다 이윽고 그들은 흩어 버릴 촛불 하나 없는 밤의 그늘에 휩싸였다."]], [["# LVIII", "# LVIII"]], [["The night was strangely solemn and still.", "밤은 이상하리만치 엄숙하고 고요했다."], ["In the small hours she whispered to him the whole story of how he had walked in his sleep with her in his arms across the Froom stream, at the imminent risk of both their lives, and laid her down in the stone coffin at the ruined abbey.", "깊은 밤 그녀는 그에게 모든 이야기를 속삭였다. 그가 자면서 그녀를 두 팔에 안고 프룸 개울을 건넜던 일, 둘 다의 목숨이 위태로운 위험 속에서, 그리고 폐허가 된 수도원의 돌 관 속에 그녀를 뉘었던 일을."], ["He had never known of that till now.", "그는 이제껏 그 일을 전혀 모르고 있었다."]], [["“Why didn’t you tell me next day?”", "\"왜 다음 날 말해 주지 않았어?\""], ["he said. “It might have prevented much misunderstanding and woe.”", "그가 말했다. \"그랬으면 많은 오해와 비통을 막을 수 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“Don’t think of what’s past!”", "\"지나간 일은 생각하지 말아요!\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I am not going to think outside of now.", "\"나는 지금 이 순간 바깥의 것은 생각하지 않을 거예요."], ["Why should we!", "왜 그래야 해요!"], ["Who knows what to-morrow has in store?”", "내일이 무엇을 품고 있는지 누가 알아요?\""]], [["But it apparently had no sorrow. The morning was wet and foggy, and Clare, rightly informed that the caretaker only opened the windows on fine days, ventured to creep out of their chamber and explore the house, leaving Tess asleep.", "하지만 내일에는 보아하니 아무런 슬픔도 없었다. 아침은 비가 내리고 안개가 끼었고, 관리인이 날씨 좋은 날에만 창문을 연다는 사실을 옳게 들어 알고 있던 클레어는, 테스를 자게 둔 채 과감히 방에서 기어 나와 집을 살펴보았다."], ["There was no food on the premises, but there was water, and he took advantage of the fog to emerge from the mansion and fetch tea, bread, and butter from a shop in a little place two miles beyond, as also a small tin kettle and spirit-lamp, that they might get fire without smoke.", "집 안에 음식은 없었으나 물은 있었고, 그는 안개를 틈타 저택에서 나가, 이 마일 너머 작은 마을의 가게에서 차와 빵과 버터를 사 왔다. 연기 없이 불을 피울 수 있도록 작은 양철 주전자와 알코올 램프도 함께."], ["His re-entry awoke her; and they breakfasted on what he had brought.", "그가 다시 들어오는 소리에 그녀가 깼고, 그들은 그가 사 온 것으로 아침을 먹었다."]], [["They were indisposed to stir abroad, and the day passed, and the night following, and the next, and next; till, almost without their being aware, five days had slipped by in absolute seclusion, not a sight or sound of a human being disturbing their peacefulness, such as it was.", "그들은 바깥을 나다닐 마음이 들지 않았고, 그렇게 하루가 지났고, 이어진 밤이, 그리고 다음 날이, 또 다음 날이 지났다. 마침내 그들 자신도 거의 의식하지 못한 채, 닷새가 완전한 은둔 속에 흘러갔다. 사람의 모습이나 소리 하나 그들의 평온을—그 나름의 평온을—깨뜨리지 않았다."], ["The changes of the weather were their only events, the birds of the New Forest their only company.", "날씨의 변화가 그들의 유일한 사건이었고, 뉴 포레스트의 새들이 그들의 유일한 벗이었다."], ["By tacit consent they hardly once spoke of any incident of the past subsequent to their wedding-day.", "말없는 합의에 따라, 그들은 결혼식 날 이후의 과거의 어떤 사건에 대해서도 단 한 번도 거의 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["The gloomy intervening time seemed to sink into chaos, over which the present and prior times closed as if it never had been.", "그 음울한 사이의 시간은 혼돈 속으로 가라앉는 듯했고, 그 위로 현재와 그 이전의 시간들이, 마치 그런 일이 애초에 없었던 듯 맞닿아 닫혔다."], ["Whenever he suggested that they should leave their shelter, and go forwards towards Southampton or London, she showed a strange unwillingness to move.", "그가 그들이 이 은신처를 떠나 사우샘프턴이나 런던 쪽으로 나아가야 한다고 넌지시 비칠 때마다, 그녀는 움직이기를 이상하리만치 꺼렸다."]], [["“Why should we put an end to all that’s sweet and lovely!” she deprecated.", "\"왜 이 달콤하고 사랑스러운 모든 것에 끝을 내야 해요!\" 그녀가 못마땅한 듯 말했다."], ["“What must come will come.”", "\"올 것은 오고 말 거예요.\""], ["And, looking through the shutter-chink: “All is trouble outside there; inside here content.”", "그러고는 덧문 틈으로 내다보며, \"바깥은 죄다 괴로움이고, 이 안은 만족이에요.\""]], [["He peeped out also.", "그도 밖을 엿보았다."], ["It was quite true; within was affection, union, error forgiven: outside was the inexorable.", "정말 그랬다. 안에는 애정과, 결합과, 용서받은 잘못이 있었고, 바깥에는 가차 없는 것이 있었다."]], [["“And—and,”", "\"그리고—그리고,\""], ["she said, pressing her cheek against his, “I fear that what you think of me now may not last.", "그녀가 자기 뺨을 그의 뺨에 비비며 말했다. \"당신이 지금 나를 생각하는 그 마음이 오래가지 못할까 봐 두려워요."], ["I do not wish to outlive your present feeling for me.", "나는 당신이 지금 나에게 품은 감정보다 더 오래 살고 싶지 않아요."], ["I would rather not.", "차라리 그러지 않는 게 나아요."], ["I would rather be dead and buried when the time comes for you to despise me, so that it may never be known to me that you despised me.”", "당신이 나를 경멸하게 될 때가 오면, 차라리 나는 죽어 묻혀 있는 게 나아요. 그래서 당신이 나를 경멸했다는 걸 내가 영영 알지 못하도록요.\""]], [["“I cannot ever despise you.”", "\"나는 결코 당신을 경멸할 수 없어.\""]], [["“I also hope that.", "\"나도 그러길 바라요."], ["But considering what my life has been, I cannot see why any man should, sooner or later, be able to help despising me....", "하지만 내 삶이 어땠는지를 생각하면, 어째서 어떤 남자가 조만간 나를 경멸하지 않을 수 있을지 알 수가 없어요…."], ["How wickedly mad I was!", "내가 얼마나 사악하게 미쳤던지!"], ["Yet formerly I never could bear to hurt a fly or a worm, and the sight of a bird in a cage used often to make me cry.”", "그래도 예전에 나는 파리 한 마리, 벌레 한 마리 다치게 하는 것도 차마 못 했고, 새장에 갇힌 새를 보면 자주 울곤 했어요.\""]], [["They remained yet another day.", "그들은 또 하루를 더 머물렀다."], ["In the night the dull sky cleared, and the result was that the old caretaker at the cottage awoke early.", "밤사이 흐린 하늘이 개었고, 그 결과 오두막의 늙은 관리인이 일찍 깼다."], ["The brilliant sunrise made her unusually brisk; she decided to open the contiguous mansion immediately, and to air it thoroughly on such a day.", "눈부신 일출에 그녀는 평소답지 않게 기운이 났다. 그녀는 곧바로 인접한 저택을 열고, 이런 날 철저히 환기를 시켜야겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["Thus it occurred that, having arrived and opened the lower rooms before six o’clock, she ascended to the bedchambers, and was about to turn the handle of the one wherein they lay.", "그리하여 여섯 시 전에 도착해 아래층 방들을 연 그녀는 위층 침실로 올라갔고, 그들이 누워 있는 방의 손잡이를 막 돌리려 했다."], ["At that moment she fancied she could hear the breathing of persons within.", "그 순간 그녀는 안에서 사람들의 숨소리가 들리는 것 같았다."], ["Her slippers and her antiquity had rendered her progress a noiseless one so far, and she made for instant retreat; then, deeming that her hearing might have deceived her, she turned anew to the door and softly tried the handle.", "슬리퍼와 그녀의 노령 덕분에 그녀의 발걸음은 거기까지 소리 없는 것이었고, 그녀는 곧장 물러나려 했다. 그러다, 자기 귀가 속았을지도 모른다 여기고, 다시 문 쪽으로 돌아서서 가만히 손잡이를 돌려 보았다."], ["The lock was out of order, but a piece of furniture had been moved forward on the inside, which prevented her opening the door more than an inch or two.", "자물쇠는 고장 나 있었으나, 안쪽에서 가구 하나가 앞으로 밀어붙여져 있어, 문을 한두 인치 이상 열 수 없었다."], ["A stream of morning light through the shutter-chink fell upon the faces of the pair, wrapped in profound slumber, Tess’s lips being parted like a half-opened flower near his cheek.", "덧문 틈으로 들어온 아침 빛줄기가 두 사람의 얼굴 위에 떨어졌다. 두 사람은 깊은 잠에 싸여 있었고, 테스의 입술은 그의 뺨 가까이에서 반쯤 핀 꽃처럼 벌어져 있었다."], ["The caretaker was so struck with their innocent appearance, and with the elegance of Tess’s gown hanging across a chair, her silk stockings beside it, the pretty parasol, and the other habits in which she had arrived because she had none else, that her first indignation at the effrontery of tramps and vagabonds gave way to a momentary sentimentality over this genteel elopement, as it seemed.", "관리인은 그들의 순진한 모습과, 의자 위에 걸쳐진 테스의 우아한 가운과, 그 옆의 비단 양말과, 예쁜 양산과, 다른 옷이 없어 그대로 입고 온 그 밖의 차림새에 어찌나 마음이 흔들렸던지, 떠돌이며 부랑자의 뻔뻔함에 대한 처음의 분노가, 이 점잖은 사랑의 도피로 보이는 것에 대한 잠깐의 감상으로 누그러졌다."], ["She closed the door, and withdrew as softly as she had come, to go and consult with her neighbours on the odd discovery.", "그녀는 문을 닫고, 왔을 때처럼 살며시 물러나, 이웃들에게 이 기이한 발견을 의논하러 갔다."]], [["Not more than a minute had elapsed after her withdrawal when Tess woke, and then Clare.", "그녀가 물러난 지 일 분도 채 지나지 않아 테스가 깼고, 이어 클레어가 깼다."], ["Both had a sense that something had disturbed them, though they could not say what; and the uneasy feeling which it engendered grew stronger.", "둘 다 무언가가 자기들을 깨웠다는 느낌이 들었으나, 그게 무엇인지는 말할 수 없었다. 그리고 그 느낌이 일으킨 불안은 점점 강해졌다."], ["As soon as he was dressed he narrowly scanned the lawn through the two or three inches of shutter-chink.", "옷을 입자마자 그는 두세 인치쯤 벌어진 덧문 틈으로 잔디밭을 유심히 살폈다."]], [["“I think we will leave at once,”", "\"우리 당장 떠나는 게 좋겠어.\""], ["said he. “It is a fine day.", "그가 말했다. \"날씨가 좋아."], ["And I cannot help fancying somebody is about the house.", "그리고 누군가 집 근처에 있는 것 같은 생각이 자꾸 들어."], ["At any rate, the woman will be sure to come to-day.”", "어쨌든 그 여자는 오늘 틀림없이 올 거야.\""]], [["She passively assented, and putting the room in order, they took up the few articles that belonged to them, and departed noiselessly.", "그녀는 순순히 동의했고, 방을 정돈한 뒤, 그들은 자기네 것인 몇 안 되는 물건을 챙겨 소리 없이 떠났다."], ["When they had got into the Forest she turned to take a last look at the house.", "숲속으로 들어섰을 때 그녀는 돌아서서 그 집을 마지막으로 한 번 바라보았다."]], [["“Ah, happy house—goodbye!”", "\"아, 행복한 집—잘 있어!\""], ["she said. “My life can only be a question of a few weeks.", "그녀가 말했다. \"내 삶은 이제 고작 몇 주의 문제일 뿐이에요."], ["Why should we not have stayed there?”", "우리가 거기 머물러 있으면 안 됐을까요?\""]], [["“Don’t say it, Tess! We shall soon get out of this district altogether.", "\"그런 말 하지 마, 테스! 우리는 곧 이 일대를 완전히 벗어날 거야."], ["We’ll continue our course as we’ve begun it, and keep straight north.", "시작한 대로 길을 이어 가서, 곧장 북쪽으로 가자."], ["Nobody will think of looking for us there.", "아무도 거기서 우리를 찾을 생각은 못 할 거야."], ["We shall be looked for at the Wessex ports if we are sought at all.", "만약 누가 우리를 찾는다 해도, 웨식스의 항구들에서 찾을 테니까."], ["When we are in the north we will get to a port and away.”", "북쪽에 닿으면 어느 항구로 가서 떠나는 거야.\""]], [["Having thus persuaded her, the plan was pursued, and they kept a bee-line northward.", "이렇게 그녀를 설득한 뒤 그 계획은 실행에 옮겨졌고, 그들은 일직선으로 북쪽을 향했다."], ["Their long repose at the manor-house lent them walking power now; and towards mid-day they found that they were approaching the steepled city of Melchester, which lay directly in their way.", "저택에서의 긴 휴식이 이제 그들에게 걷는 힘을 빌려주었다. 한낮 무렵 그들은 자기들이 첨탑이 솟은 도시 멜체스터에 가까워지고 있음을 알았다. 그 도시는 바로 그들의 길목에 놓여 있었다."], ["He decided to rest her in a clump of trees during the afternoon, and push onward under cover of darkness.", "그는 오후 동안 그녀를 나무 숲에서 쉬게 하고, 어둠을 틈타 밀고 나아가기로 했다."], ["At dusk Clare purchased food as usual, and their night march began, the boundary between Upper and Mid-Wessex being crossed about eight o’clock.", "땅거미가 질 무렵 클레어는 여느 때처럼 음식을 샀고, 그들의 밤 행군이 시작되었다. 어퍼 웨식스와 미드 웨식스의 경계선을 넘은 것은 여덟 시쯤이었다."]], [["To walk across country without much regard to roads was not new to Tess, and she showed her old agility in the performance.", "길에 그리 매이지 않고 들판을 가로질러 걷는 것은 테스에게 새로운 일이 아니었고, 그녀는 그 일에서 예전의 그 민첩함을 보였다."], ["The intercepting city, ancient Melchester, they were obliged to pass through in order to take advantage of the town bridge for crossing a large river that obstructed them.", "길을 가로막은 도시, 고도 멜체스터는, 그들을 막아선 큰 강을 건너는 데 그 도시의 다리를 이용하기 위해 반드시 통과해야 했다."], ["It was about midnight when they went along the deserted streets, lighted fitfully by the few lamps, keeping off the pavement that it might not echo their footsteps.", "그들이 인적 끊긴 거리를 걸어간 것은 자정쯤이었다. 거리는 몇 개 안 되는 가로등이 띄엄띄엄 비추고 있었고, 그들은 자기네 발소리가 울리지 않도록 포장도로를 피했다."], ["The graceful pile of cathedral architecture rose dimly on their left hand, but it was lost upon them now.", "우아하게 솟은 대성당 건축물 더미가 그들의 왼편으로 어슴푸레 솟아 있었으나, 이제 그것은 그들의 안중에 없었다."], ["Once out of the town they followed the turnpike-road, which after a few miles plunged across an open plain.", "도시를 빠져나오자 그들은 유료 간선도로를 따라갔고, 그 길은 몇 마일 뒤 탁 트인 평원을 가로질러 내달았다."], ["Though the sky was dense with cloud, a diffused light from some fragment of a moon had hitherto helped them a little.", "하늘은 구름으로 빽빽했으나, 어느 조각달인지 모를 데서 퍼져 나온 빛이 지금껏 그들을 조금 도와주었다."], ["But the moon had now sunk, the clouds seemed to settle almost on their heads, and the night grew as dark as a cave.", "그러나 이제 달은 가라앉았고, 구름은 거의 그들 머리 위에 내려앉은 듯했으며, 밤은 동굴처럼 어두워졌다."], ["However, they found their way along, keeping as much on the turf as possible that their tread might not resound, which it was easy to do, there being no hedge or fence of any kind.", "그래도 그들은 길을 더듬어 나아갔다. 발소리가 울리지 않도록 되도록 잔디 위로만 걸었는데, 어떤 울타리도 담장도 없었으므로 그러기는 쉬웠다."], ["All around was open loneliness and black solitude, over which a stiff breeze blew.", "사방은 탁 트인 외로움이요 검은 적막이었고, 그 위로 매서운 바람이 불었다."]], [["They had proceeded thus gropingly two or three miles further when on a sudden Clare became conscious of some vast erection close in his front, rising sheer from the grass.", "그들이 이렇게 더듬더듬 이삼 마일을 더 나아갔을 때, 문득 클레어는 자기 바로 앞에, 잔디에서 가파르게 솟아오른 어떤 거대한 구조물을 의식하게 되었다."], ["They had almost struck themselves against it.", "그들은 하마터면 거기에 부딪칠 뻔했다."]], [["“What monstrous place is this?”", "\"이게 무슨 괴물 같은 곳이지?\""], ["said Angel.", "에인절이 말했다."]], [["“It hums,”", "\"윙윙거려요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Hearken!”", "\"들어 봐요!\""]], [["He listened. The wind, playing upon the edifice, produced a booming tune, like the note of some gigantic one-stringed harp.", "그가 귀를 기울였다. 바람이 그 건조물을 타고 놀며, 어떤 거대한 외줄 하프의 음 같은, 웅웅거리는 가락을 빚어냈다."], ["No other sound came from it, and lifting his hand and advancing a step or two, Clare felt the vertical surface of the structure.", "거기서 다른 소리는 나지 않았고, 손을 들어 한두 걸음 나아간 클레어는 그 구조물의 수직 표면을 만졌다."], ["It seemed to be of solid stone, without joint or moulding.", "그것은 이음매도 쇠시리 장식도 없는 단단한 돌인 듯했다."], ["Carrying his fingers onward he found that what he had come in contact with was a colossal rectangular pillar; by stretching out his left hand he could feel a similar one adjoining.", "손가락을 죽 더듬어 가다가 그는, 자기가 닿은 것이 거대한 직사각형 기둥임을 알았다. 왼손을 뻗자 바로 옆에 비슷한 기둥 하나가 만져졌다."], ["At an indefinite height overhead something made the black sky blacker, which had the semblance of a vast architrave uniting the pillars horizontally.", "머리 위 가늠할 수 없는 높이에서 무언가가 검은 하늘을 더 검게 만들고 있었는데, 기둥들을 수평으로 잇는 거대한 들보의 모습을 하고 있었다."], ["They carefully entered beneath and between; the surfaces echoed their soft rustle; but they seemed to be still out of doors.", "그들은 조심스레 그 아래, 그 사이로 들어섰다. 그들의 부드러운 옷 스치는 소리가 표면에 메아리쳤다. 그러나 그들은 여전히 바깥에 있는 듯했다."], ["The place was roofless.", "그곳에는 지붕이 없었다."], ["Tess drew her breath fearfully, and Angel, perplexed, said—", "테스는 두려운 듯 숨을 들이켰고, 어리둥절한 에인절이 말했다—"]], [["“What can it be?”", "\"이게 뭘까?\""]], [["Feeling sideways they encountered another tower-like pillar, square and uncompromising as the first; beyond it another and another.", "옆으로 더듬어 가다가 그들은 또 하나의 탑 같은 기둥과 마주쳤다, 첫 번째 것처럼 네모지고 단호한 기둥과. 그 너머에 또 하나, 또 하나가 있었다."], ["The place was all doors and pillars, some connected above by continuous architraves.", "그곳은 온통 문이요 기둥이었고, 더러는 위쪽에서 끊임없이 이어진 들보로 연결되어 있었다."]], [["“A very Temple of the Winds,” he said.", "\"그야말로 바람의 신전이군.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["The next pillar was isolated; others composed a trilithon; others were prostrate, their flanks forming a causeway wide enough for a carriage; and it was soon obvious that they made up a forest of monoliths grouped upon the grassy expanse of the plain.", "다음 기둥은 외따로 서 있었고, 다른 것들은 삼석탑을 이루었다. 또 다른 것들은 쓰러져 누워, 그 옆구리가 마차 한 대가 지날 만큼 넓은 둑길을 이루었다. 그리고 그것들이 평원의 풀 펼쳐진 너른 터 위에 무리 지은 거대한 돌기둥 숲을 이루고 있음이 곧 분명해졌다."], ["The couple advanced further into this pavilion of the night till they stood in its midst.", "두 사람은 이 밤의 정자 속으로 더 들어가, 마침내 그 한복판에 섰다."]], [["“It is Stonehenge!” said Clare.", "\"스톤헨지야!\" 클레어가 말했다."]], [["“The heathen temple, you mean?”", "\"이교도의 신전 말이에요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"응."], ["Older than the centuries; older than the d’Urbervilles!", "여러 세기보다 오래된, 더버빌 가보다도 오래된 곳이야!"], ["Well, what shall we do, darling?", "자, 어쩌지, 사랑하는 사람아?"], ["We may find shelter further on.”", "더 가면 숨을 곳을 찾을지도 몰라.\""]], [["But Tess, really tired by this time, flung herself upon an oblong slab that lay close at hand, and was sheltered from the wind by a pillar.", "그러나 이때쯤 정말로 지친 테스는, 바로 곁에 놓인 직사각형 석판 위에 몸을 던졌다. 그것은 기둥 하나가 바람을 막아 주는 자리였다."], ["Owing to the action of the sun during the preceding day, the stone was warm and dry, in comforting contrast to the rough and chill grass around, which had damped her skirts and shoes.", "전날 햇볕이 닿은 덕에, 그 돌은 따뜻하고 말라 있어, 그녀의 치마와 신발을 적셨던 거칠고 차가운 둘레의 풀과는 위안이 될 만큼 대조적이었다."]], [["“I don’t want to go any further, Angel,”", "\"더는 가고 싶지 않아요, 에인절.\""], ["she said, stretching out her hand for his. “Can’t we bide here?”", "그녀가 그에게 손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"우리 여기 머무르면 안 돼요?\""]], [["“I fear not. This spot is visible for miles by day, although it does not seem so now.”", "\"안 될 것 같아. 이곳은 지금은 그렇게 보이지 않지만, 낮에는 몇 마일 밖에서도 보이거든.\""]], [["“One of my mother’s people was a shepherd hereabouts, now I think of it.", "\"생각해 보니, 우리 어머니 쪽 사람 하나가 이 근방에서 양치기였대요."], ["And you used to say at Talbothays that I was a heathen. So now I am at home.”", "그리고 당신은 탤보세이즈에서 내가 이교도라고 늘 말하곤 했죠. 그러니 이제 나는 집에 온 셈이네요.\""]], [["He knelt down beside her outstretched form, and put his lips upon hers.", "그는 길게 뻗은 그녀의 몸 곁에 무릎을 꿇고, 자기 입술을 그녀의 입술에 댔다."]], [["“Sleepy are you, dear?", "\"졸려, 사랑하는 사람아?"], ["I think you are lying on an altar.”", "당신 지금 제단 위에 누워 있는 것 같은데.\""]], [["“I like very much to be here,”", "\"여기 있는 게 아주 좋아요.\""], ["she murmured. “It is so solemn and lonely—after my great happiness—with nothing but the sky above my face.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"어찌나 엄숙하고 외로운지—내 크나큰 행복 뒤에—내 얼굴 위로 하늘밖에 아무것도 없으니."], ["It seems as if there were no folk in the world but we two; and I wish there were not—except ’Liza-Lu.”", "이 세상에 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 없는 것 같아요. 그리고 정말 그랬으면 좋겠어요—'라이자-루만 빼고는.\""]], [["Clare thought she might as well rest here till it should get a little lighter, and he flung his overcoat upon her, and sat down by her side.", "클레어는 좀 더 밝아질 때까지 그녀가 여기서 쉬어도 괜찮겠다고 생각했고, 그는 그녀에게 자기 외투를 덮어 주고 그 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“Angel, if anything happens to me, will you watch over ’Liza-Lu for my sake?”", "\"에인절, 만약 나한테 무슨 일이 생기면, 나를 봐서 '라이자-루를 보살펴 주겠어요?\""], ["she asked, when they had listened a long time to the wind among the pillars.", "그들이 기둥 사이로 부는 바람 소리를 오래 듣고 있던 끝에 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I will.”", "\"그러마.\""]], [["“She is so good and simple and pure.", "\"그 애는 참 착하고 순박하고 순결해요."], ["O, Angel—I wish you would marry her if you lose me, as you will do shortly.", "오, 에인절—머지않아 그렇게 되겠지만, 나를 잃으면 그 애와 결혼해 주면 좋겠어요."], ["O, if you would!”", "오, 그래 준다면!\""]], [["“If I lose you I lose all! And she is my sister-in-law.”", "\"당신을 잃으면 나는 모든 걸 잃는 거야! 게다가 그 애는 내 처제잖아.\""]], [["“That’s nothing, dearest.", "\"그런 건 아무것도 아니에요, 사랑하는 사람아."], ["People marry sister-laws continually about Marlott; and ’Liza-Lu is so gentle and sweet, and she is growing so beautiful.", "말럿 근방에서는 사람들이 늘 처제와 결혼하는걸요. 그리고 '라이자-루는 어찌나 상냥하고 다정한지, 게다가 점점 아름다워지고 있어요."], ["O, I could share you with her willingly when we are spirits!", "오, 우리가 영혼이 되면 나는 기꺼이 당신을 그 애와 나눌 수 있어요!"], ["If you would train her and teach her, Angel, and bring her up for your own self!...", "당신이 그 애를 가르치고 길러서, 당신 자신을 위해 키워 준다면, 에인절!…"], ["She had all the best of me without the bad of me; and if she were to become yours it would almost seem as if death had not divided us...", "그 애는 내 좋은 점은 다 가졌으면서 내 나쁜 점은 없어요. 그리고 그 애가 당신 사람이 된다면, 죽음이 우리를 갈라놓지 않은 것이나 거의 마찬가지일 거예요…"], ["Well, I have said it.", "자, 다 말했어요."], ["I won’t mention it again.”", "다시는 이 얘기 꺼내지 않을게요.\""]], [["She ceased, and he fell into thought.", "그녀가 말을 멈추자 그는 생각에 잠겼다."], ["In the far north-east sky he could see between the pillars a level streak of light.", "저 먼 북동쪽 하늘, 기둥들 사이로 그는 평평한 한 줄기 빛을 볼 수 있었다."], ["The uniform concavity of black cloud was lifting bodily like the lid of a pot, letting in at the earth’s edge the coming day, against which the towering monoliths and trilithons began to be blackly defined.", "검은 구름의 한결같은 오목한 천장이, 마치 솥뚜껑처럼 통째로 들리며, 땅의 가장자리로 다가오는 낮을 들여보내고 있었다. 그 빛을 배경으로 우뚝 솟은 돌기둥들과 삼석탑들이 검게 윤곽을 드러내기 시작했다."]], [["“Did they sacrifice to God here?”", "\"여기서 사람들이 하느님께 제물을 바쳤을까요?\""], ["asked she.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“No,” said he.", "\"아니.\""], ["“Who to?”", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I believe to the sun.", "\"누구에게요?\""], ["That lofty stone set away by itself is in the direction of the sun, which will presently rise behind it.”", "\"태양에게였을 거야. 저 외따로 떨어져 세워진 높다란 돌이 태양의 방향에 있고, 곧 그 뒤로 해가 떠오를 거야.\""]], [["“This reminds me, dear,”", "\"이걸 보니 생각나요, 여보.\""], ["she said. “You remember you never would interfere with any belief of mine before we were married?", "그녀가 말했다. \"기억해요, 우리가 결혼하기 전에는 당신이 내 어떤 믿음에도 결코 간섭하려 들지 않았던 거?"], ["But I knew your mind all the same, and I thought as you thought—not from any reasons of my own, but because you thought so.", "하지만 나는 그래도 당신 마음을 알았고, 당신이 생각하는 대로 나도 생각했어요—내 나름의 어떤 이유에서가 아니라, 당신이 그렇게 생각했기 때문에요."], ["Tell me now, Angel, do you think we shall meet again after we are dead?", "이제 말해 줘요, 에인절, 우리가 죽은 뒤에 다시 만날 거라고 생각해요?"], ["I want to know.”", "알고 싶어요.\""]], [["He kissed her to avoid a reply at such a time.", "그는 이런 때에 대답을 피하려고 그녀에게 입맞추었다."]], [["“O, Angel—I fear that means no!”", "\"오, 에인절—그건 아니라는 뜻인가 봐요!\""], ["said she, with a suppressed sob.", "그녀가 흐느낌을 누르며 말했다."], ["“And I wanted so to see you again—so much, so much!", "\"그리고 나는 당신을 다시 보고 싶었는데—그토록, 그토록!"], ["What—not even you and I, Angel, who love each other so well?”", "뭐라고요—그렇게 서로 잘 사랑하는 우리, 당신과 나조차도 안 된다고요, 에인절?\""]], [["Like a greater than himself, to the critical question at the critical time he did not answer; and they were again silent.", "자기보다 더 위대한 분처럼, 결정적인 때의 결정적인 물음에 그는 대답하지 않았다. 그리고 그들은 다시 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["In a minute or two her breathing became more regular, her clasp of his hand relaxed, and she fell asleep.", "한두 분 만에 그녀의 숨결이 한결 고르게 되었고, 그의 손을 쥔 그녀의 손아귀가 풀렸으며, 그녀는 잠이 들었다."], ["The band of silver paleness along the east horizon made even the distant parts of the Great Plain appear dark and near; and the whole enormous landscape bore that impress of reserve, taciturnity, and hesitation which is usual just before day.", "동쪽 지평선을 따라 뻗은 은빛 창백함의 띠가, 그레이트 플레인의 먼 부분들마저 어둡고 가깝게 보이게 했다. 그리고 그 거대한 풍경 전체는, 동트기 직전에 흔한 그 절제와, 과묵함과, 머뭇거림의 인상을 띠고 있었다."], ["The eastward pillars and their architraves stood up blackly against the light, and the great flame-shaped Sun-stone beyond them; and the Stone of Sacrifice midway.", "동쪽의 기둥들과 그 들보들이 빛을 배경으로 검게 솟아 있었고, 그 너머의 거대한 불꽃 모양 태양석도, 그리고 그 중간의 제물석도."], ["Presently the night wind died out, and the quivering little pools in the cup-like hollows of the stones lay still.", "이윽고 밤바람이 잦아들었고, 돌들의 잔 같은 오목한 곳에 고인 작은 웅덩이들의 떨림도 멎었다."], ["At the same time something seemed to move on the verge of the dip eastward—a mere dot.", "같은 때, 무언가가 동쪽 우묵한 곳의 가장자리에서 움직이는 듯했다—한낱 점 하나가."], ["It was the head of a man approaching them from the hollow beyond the Sun-stone.", "태양석 너머 우묵한 데서 그들에게 다가오는 한 남자의 머리였다."], ["Clare wished they had gone onward, but in the circumstances decided to remain quiet.", "클레어는 자기들이 더 나아갔더라면 좋았으리라 생각했으나, 이 상황에서는 가만히 있기로 했다."], ["The figure came straight towards the circle of pillars in which they were.", "그 형체는 그들이 있는 기둥의 원을 향해 곧장 다가왔다."]], [["He heard something behind him, the brush of feet.", "그는 등 뒤에서 무슨 소리를, 발이 풀을 스치는 소리를 들었다."], ["Turning, he saw over the prostrate columns another figure; then before he was aware, another was at hand on the right, under a trilithon, and another on the left.", "돌아보니, 쓰러진 기둥들 너머로 또 하나의 형체가 보였다. 그러더니 그가 미처 알아채기도 전에, 또 하나가 삼석탑 아래 오른쪽 가까이에 있었고, 또 하나가 왼쪽에 있었다."], ["The dawn shone full on the front of the man westward, and Clare could discern from this that he was tall, and walked as if trained.", "동이 서쪽에 선 남자의 정면을 가득 비추었고, 클레어는 이로써 그가 키가 크고, 훈련받은 사람처럼 걷는다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["They all closed in with evident purpose.", "그들은 모두 분명한 목적을 가지고 좁혀 들어왔다."], ["Her story then was true! Springing to his feet, he looked around for a weapon, loose stone, means of escape, anything.", "그렇다면 그녀의 이야기는 사실이었던 것이다! 벌떡 일어선 그는 무기든, 굴러다니는 돌이든, 달아날 방도든, 무엇이든 찾아 둘레를 둘러보았다."], ["By this time the nearest man was upon him.", "그러는 사이 가장 가까운 남자가 그에게 다가와 있었다."]], [["“It is no use, sir,”", "\"소용없습니다, 선생.\""], ["he said. “There are sixteen of us on the Plain, and the whole country is reared.”", "그가 말했다. \"우리는 평원에 열여섯 명이 있고, 온 고장이 들고일어나 있습니다.\""]], [["“Let her finish her sleep!” he implored in a whisper of the men as they gathered round.", "\"이 사람 잠을 마저 자게 해 주시오!\" 그가 둘러 모여드는 사람들에게 속삭이며 애원했다."]], [["When they saw where she lay, which they had not done till then, they showed no objection, and stood watching her, as still as the pillars around.", "그제야 비로소 그녀가 누워 있는 자리를 본 그들은, 아무 이의도 보이지 않고, 둘레의 기둥들처럼 가만히 서서 그녀를 지켜보았다."], ["He went to the stone and bent over her, holding one poor little hand; her breathing now was quick and small, like that of a lesser creature than a woman.", "그는 돌로 가서 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히고, 가엾은 작은 손 하나를 잡았다. 그녀의 숨결은 이제 빠르고 작아서, 여자라기보다 더 작은 어떤 생물의 것 같았다."], ["All waited in the growing light, their faces and hands as if they were silvered, the remainder of their figures dark, the stones glistening green-gray, the Plain still a mass of shade.", "모두가 점점 밝아 오는 빛 속에서 기다렸다. 그들의 얼굴과 손은 은빛으로 입혀진 듯했고, 나머지 몸은 어두웠으며, 돌들은 초록빛 도는 잿빛으로 반들거렸고, 평원은 여전히 그림자 덩어리였다."], ["Soon the light was strong, and a ray shone upon her unconscious form, peering under her eyelids and waking her.", "곧 빛이 강해졌고, 한 줄기 빛살이 그녀의 의식 없는 몸 위에 비쳐, 그녀의 눈꺼풀 아래를 들여다보며 그녀를 깨웠다."]], [["“What is it, Angel?”", "\"무슨 일이에요, 에인절?\""], ["she said, starting up.", "그녀가 화들짝 일어나며 말했다."], ["“Have they come for me?”", "\"그들이 나를 데리러 왔어요?\""]], [["“Yes, dearest,”", "\"그래, 사랑하는 사람아.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“They have come.”", "\"그들이 왔어.\""]], [["“It is as it should be,”", "\"마땅히 그래야 하는 대로네요.\""], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Angel, I am almost glad—yes, glad!", "\"에인절, 나는 거의 기뻐요—그래요, 기뻐요!"], ["This happiness could not have lasted.", "이 행복은 오래갈 수 없었을 거예요."], ["It was too much.", "너무 과분했어요."], ["I have had enough; and now I shall not live for you to despise me!”", "나는 충분히 누렸어요. 그리고 이제 당신이 나를 경멸하도록 살아 있지 않아도 되니까요!\""]], [["She stood up, shook herself, and went forward, neither of the men having moved.", "그녀는 일어서서 몸을 한 번 추스르고 앞으로 나아갔다. 두 남자 모두 움직이지 않았다."]], [["“I am ready,”", "\"준비됐어요.\""], ["she said quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 말했다."]], [["# LIX", "# LIX"]], [["The city of Wintoncester, that fine old city, aforetime capital of Wessex, lay amidst its convex and concave downlands in all the brightness and warmth of a July morning.", "윈톤체스터, 그 아름다운 옛 도시, 한때 웨식스의 수도였던 그곳은, 칠월 아침의 온갖 밝음과 따스함 속에서, 볼록하고 오목한 구릉지 사이에 자리하고 있었다."], ["The gabled brick, tile, and freestone houses had almost dried off for the season their integument of lichen, the streams in the meadows were low, and in the sloping High Street, from the West Gateway to the mediæval cross, and from the mediæval cross to the bridge, that leisurely dusting and sweeping was in progress which usually ushers in an old-fashioned market-day.", "박공 달린 벽돌과 기와와 자유석으로 지은 집들은 그 철 동안 자기를 감싼 이끼의 외피를 거의 말려 떨군 참이었고, 풀밭의 개울들은 수위가 낮았으며, 서쪽 성문에서 중세의 십자가까지, 또 중세의 십자가에서 다리까지 비탈진 하이 스트리트에서는, 흔히 구식 장날을 여는 그 느긋한 먼지 떨기와 비질이 한창이었다."]], [["From the western gate aforesaid the highway, as every Wintoncestrian knows, ascends a long and regular incline of the exact length of a measured mile, leaving the houses gradually behind.", "앞서 말한 서쪽 성문에서부터 큰길은, 윈톤체스터 사람이라면 누구나 알듯, 정확히 측량된 일 마일 길이의 길고 한결같은 비탈을 오르며, 차츰 집들을 뒤로 남겼다."], ["Up this road from the precincts of the city two persons were walking rapidly, as if unconscious of the trying ascent—unconscious through preoccupation and not through buoyancy.", "도시의 경내에서부터 이 길을 따라 두 사람이 빠르게 걷고 있었다. 마치 그 고된 오르막을 의식하지 못하는 듯—기운이 넘쳐서가 아니라 골똘한 생각에 잠겨 의식하지 못하는 것이었다."], ["They had emerged upon this road through a narrow, barred wicket in a high wall a little lower down.", "그들은 조금 아래쪽 높은 담장의, 좁고 빗장 달린 쪽문을 통해 이 길로 나온 터였다."], ["They seemed anxious to get out of the sight of the houses and of their kind, and this road appeared to offer the quickest means of doing so.", "그들은 집들과 자기 동족의 시야에서 벗어나기를 애타게 바라는 듯했고, 이 길이 그렇게 할 가장 빠른 수단을 내주는 것 같았다."], ["Though they were young, they walked with bowed heads, which gait of grief the sun’s rays smiled on pitilessly.", "그들은 젊었으나, 고개를 숙인 채 걸었다. 그 비탄에 잠긴 걸음걸이 위로 햇살이 무자비하게 미소 지었다."]], [["One of the pair was Angel Clare, the other a tall budding creature—half girl, half woman—a spiritualized image of Tess, slighter than she, but with the same beautiful eyes—Clare’s sister-in-law, ’Liza-Lu.", "두 사람 중 하나는 에인절 클레어였고, 다른 하나는 키 크고 한창 피어나는 존재였다—반은 소녀, 반은 여인인—테스의 영적인 영상, 그녀보다 가냘프지만 똑같은 아름다운 눈을 가진—클레어의 처제, '라이자-루였다."], ["Their pale faces seemed to have shrunk to half their natural size.", "그들의 창백한 얼굴은 본래 크기의 절반으로 쪼그라든 듯했다."], ["They moved on hand in hand, and never spoke a word, the drooping of their heads being that of Giotto’s “Two Apostles”.", "그들은 손을 맞잡고 나아갔고, 한마디도 하지 않았으며, 그 숙인 고개는 조토의 「두 사도」의 그것이었다."]], [["When they had nearly reached the top of the great West Hill the clocks in the town struck eight.", "그들이 거대한 웨스트 힐 꼭대기에 거의 다다랐을 때, 도시의 시계들이 여덟 시를 쳤다."], ["Each gave a start at the notes, and, walking onward yet a few steps, they reached the first milestone, standing whitely on the green margin of the grass, and backed by the down, which here was open to the road.", "그 종소리에 둘은 저마다 흠칫했고, 몇 걸음 더 나아가 첫 번째 이정표에 닿았다. 그것은 풀의 푸른 가장자리에 희게 서 있었고, 여기서 길로 트인 구릉을 등지고 있었다."], ["They entered upon the turf, and, impelled by a force that seemed to overrule their will, suddenly stood still, turned, and waited in paralyzed suspense beside the stone.", "그들은 잔디로 들어섰고, 자기네 의지를 압도하는 듯한 어떤 힘에 떠밀려, 문득 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 돌아서서, 그 돌 곁에서 마비된 듯한 긴장 속에 기다렸다."]], [["The prospect from this summit was almost unlimited.", "이 꼭대기에서 바라보는 전망은 거의 끝이 없었다."], ["In the valley beneath lay the city they had just left, its more prominent buildings showing as in an isometric drawing—among them the broad cathedral tower, with its Norman windows and immense length of aisle and nave, the spires of St Thomas’s, the pinnacled tower of the College, and, more to the right, the tower and gables of the ancient hospice, where to this day the pilgrim may receive his dole of bread and ale.", "아래 골짜기에는 그들이 방금 떠나온 도시가 자리해 있었고, 그 도드라진 건물들은 등각 투상도 속에서처럼 보였다—그중에는 노르만식 창문과 어마어마하게 긴 측랑과 신랑을 가진 너른 대성당 탑이, 성 토머스 교회의 첨탑들이, 칼리지의 작은 첨탑 달린 탑이, 그리고 더 오른쪽으로는, 오늘날까지도 순례자가 빵과 맥주의 적선을 받을 수 있는 옛 구호소의 탑과 박공들이 있었다."], ["Behind the city swept the rotund upland of St Catherine’s Hill; further off, landscape beyond landscape, till the horizon was lost in the radiance of the sun hanging above it.", "도시 뒤로는 성 캐서린 언덕의 둥그스름한 고지가 휩쓸듯 뻗어 있었고, 더 멀리에는 풍경 너머 풍경이 이어지다가, 마침내 지평선이 그 위에 걸린 태양의 광휘 속으로 사라졌다."]], [["Against these far stretches of country rose, in front of the other city edifices, a large red-brick building, with level gray roofs, and rows of short barred windows bespeaking captivity, the whole contrasting greatly by its formalism with the quaint irregularities of the Gothic erections.", "이 먼 들녘의 펼쳐짐을 배경으로, 다른 도시 건물들 앞쪽에 커다란 붉은 벽돌 건물이 솟아 있었다. 평평한 잿빛 지붕과, 갇혀 있음을 말해 주는 짧은 빗장 창문들의 줄을 가진 그것은, 고딕 건축물들의 예스러운 불규칙함과는 그 형식주의로 크게 대조를 이루었다."], ["It was somewhat disguised from the road in passing it by yews and evergreen oaks, but it was visible enough up here.", "그곳은 지나치며 보면 주목나무와 상록 떡갈나무들에 가려 길에서는 다소 가려져 있었으나, 이 위에서는 충분히 보였다."], ["The wicket from which the pair had lately emerged was in the wall of this structure.", "두 사람이 방금 나온 그 쪽문은 바로 이 건물의 담장에 있었다."], ["From the middle of the building an ugly flat-topped octagonal tower ascended against the east horizon, and viewed from this spot, on its shady side and against the light, it seemed the one blot on the city’s beauty.", "건물 한가운데서 흉한 평평한 꼭대기의 팔각 탑이 동쪽 지평선을 배경으로 솟아 있었는데, 이 자리에서, 그 그늘진 쪽에서 빛을 등지고 보니, 그것은 이 도시의 아름다움에 찍힌 단 하나의 오점인 듯했다."], ["Yet it was with this blot, and not with the beauty, that the two gazers were concerned.", "그러나 두 응시자가 관심을 둔 것은, 그 아름다움이 아니라 바로 이 오점이었다."]], [["Upon the cornice of the tower a tall staff was fixed.", "탑의 처마돌림띠 위에는 키 큰 깃대 하나가 세워져 있었다."], ["Their eyes were riveted on it. A few minutes after the hour had struck something moved slowly up the staff, and extended itself upon the breeze.", "그들의 눈은 거기에 못 박혀 있었다. 정시를 친 지 몇 분 뒤, 무언가가 깃대를 따라 천천히 올라가, 바람결에 자기를 펼쳤다."], ["It was a black flag.", "검은 깃발이었다."]], [["“Justice” was done, and the President of the Immortals, in Æschylean phrase, had ended his sport with Tess.", "「정의」는 이루어졌고, 아이스킬로스적 표현으로 말하자면, 불멸자들의 주재자가 테스를 데리고 벌인 자기 놀음을 끝냈다."], ["And the d’Urberville knights and dames slept on in their tombs unknowing.", "그리고 더버빌 가의 기사들과 귀부인들은 자기들의 무덤 속에서 아무것도 모른 채 계속 잠들어 있었다."], ["The two speechless gazers bent themselves down to the earth, as if in prayer, and remained thus a long time, absolutely motionless: the flag continued to wave silently.", "말없는 두 응시자는, 마치 기도하듯, 몸을 땅으로 굽혔고, 그렇게 오랫동안, 미동도 없이 머물러 있었다. 깃발은 소리 없이 계속 나부꼈다."], ["As soon as they had strength, they arose, joined hands again, and went on.", "기운을 차리자마자 그들은 일어나, 다시 손을 맞잡고, 길을 떠났다."]]];
